and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343630\tx:\\Social Economic\\Office of National Statistics\\Built Up Area by Population Size.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17247\tEXT_DS123462\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.859Z\t13\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOffice of National Statistics (ONS): Medium super output areas 2011 (3rd Party Data)\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343632\tx:\\Social Economic\\Office of National Statistics\\Census 2011\\Medium super output areas 2011.lyr\tData Custodian: Geospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-26T15:27:58.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17248\tEXT_DS123552\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.807Z\t742\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironment Agency: Coastal Flood Boundaries (CFB) publication 2018 - England and Wales (3rd Party Data)\t© Environment Agency Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Environment Agency information © Environment Agency and database right.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233353532\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2018\\CFB Estuary boundaries.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2018\\CFB Exteam sea levels.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2018\\CFB Exteam sea levels estuary.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2018\\CFB Gauge data.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2018\\CFB Surge shapes.lyr\tBen Hext\tThis metadata record is for AfA product AfA 010. This is part of Coastal Design/Extreme Sea Levels, a GIS dataset and supporting information providing design / extreme sea level and typical surge information around the coastline of the UK, including England, Wales, Scotland, Northern Ireland, Isle of Man and Jersey. The information is relevant under present day (year 2018) conditions and does not account for future changes due to climate change, such as sea level rise. This is a specialist dataset which informs on work commenced around the coast ranging from coastal flood modelling, scheme design, strategic planning and flood risk assessments.\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T16:58:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17246\tEXT_DS123682\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.444Z\t646\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRSPB: UK and Eire 95% Utilisation Distributions in 5% Bands (3rd Party Data)\t© [RSPB] Data may be re-used under the terms of the 3rd Party Open Data licence. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233363832\t\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Matty Murphy  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Services Team\tThis is a spatial data collation wholly owned by RSPB. From 2010 to 2015, the RSPB and partners undertook a series of large-scale seabird tracking studies across the UK during the late incubation / early chick rearing period of the breeding season using cutting-edge GPS tracking technology. For four of the species tracked, there was sufficient data coverage to map their UK-wide at sea distributions using habitat selection models. These four species were European shag Phalacrocorax aristotelis, black-legged kittiwake Rissa tridactyla, common guillemot Uria aalge, and razorbill Alca torda. Habitat selection models were based upon all GPS locations and therefore included coverage of all behaviours (e.g. foraging, commuting, resting etc.). This dataset uses the UK distribution maps of these four species to identify important areas of high seabird density at sea, based on hotspot mapping techniques. Utilisation distributions are 2-dimensional probability distributions that represent the time spent in a specific area and thus the probability of encountering an animal in that location during a future observation period.\tRoyal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)\t2018-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:24:55.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17294\tEXT_DS124237\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.130Z\t746\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMet Office: Numerical Weather Prediction (NWP) System data (3rd Party Data)\t© Crown copyright: Met Office. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323337\t\tJiping Shi, Team Leader Air Quality Modelling and Risk Assessment Team, Integrated Evidence Group\tNumerical weather prediction (NWP) is the process of obtaining an objective forecast of the future state of the atmosphere by running a computer model.\tMet Office\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17297\tEXT_DS124279\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.350Z\t1035\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLocal Authorities: Conwy Wildlife Sites (archived 2019) (3rd Party Data)\t© Local Authority\u0027s NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323739\tArchived from x:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Conwy Wildlife Sites.lyr  in 2019, retained in geospatial store\tInternal contact; Geospatial team\tConwy Wildlife Sites, 3rd party data and property of local authority.  Archived 2019, the Sites of Importance for Nature Conservation (SINC) record 121617 is to be used instead.\tConwy County Borough Council\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:24:55.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17283\tEXT_DS124281\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.767Z\t378\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLocal Authorities: Denbigh Wildlife Sites (archived 2019) (3rd Party Data)\t© Local Authority\u0027s NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323831\tArchivd from x:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Denbigh Wildlife Sites.lyr , retained in geospatial store\tInternal contact: Geospatial team\tDenbigh Wildlife Sites, 3rd party data and property of local authority. Archived 2019, the Sites of Importance for Nature Conservation (SINC) record 121617 is to be used instead.\tDenbighshire County Council\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17292\tEXT_DS124282\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.937Z\t673\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLocal Authorities: Flint Wildlife Sites (archived 2019) (3rd Party Data)\t© Local Authority\u0027s NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323832\tArchived from x:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Flint Wildlife Sites.lyr , retained in geospatial store\tInternal contact; geospatial team\tFlint Wildlife Sites, 3rd party data and property of local authority. Archived 2019, the Sites of Importance for Nature Conservation (SINC) record 121617 is to be used instead.\tFflintshire County Council \u003d Cyngor Sir y Fflint\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17287\tEXT_DS124284\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.824Z\t542\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLocal Authorities: Wrexham Wildlife Sites (archived 2019) (3rd Party Data)\t© Local Authority\u0027s NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323834\tarchived from x:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Wrexham Wildlife Sites.lyr , retained in geospatial store\tInternal contact: Geospatial team\tWrexham Wildlife Sites, 3rd party data and property of local authority. Archived 2019, the Sites of Importance for Nature Conservation (SINC) record 121617 is to be used instead.\tWrexham County Borough\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17300\tEXT_DS124285\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.338Z\t326\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTree Health Phytophthora ramorum and Phytophthora kernoviae disease data for England \u0026amp; Wales (3rd Party Data)\t© Fera Science Limited.  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323835\tStored on the Y:/ drive for use by the tree health team only.\tData Custodian: Sam Milner/Andy Wright\tTree Health Phytophthora ramorum and Phytophthora kernoviae disease data for England \u0026amp; Wales, owned by Fera Science Limited; https://www.fera.co.uk/\tFera Science Limited\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17291\tEXT_DS124288\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.933Z\t703\tUnassessed\tEditable\tESTYN: Education data (3rd party data)\t© Estyn NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323838\tX:\\Social Economic\\Education\tData custodian ; Geospatial Team. Knowledge Information Management\tFour spatial layers covering the following;    1) ESTYN Higher Education; Higher Education establishments in Wales, as at January 2014.    2) ESTYN annual report 2011-14; Based on the Local Authorities dataset provided by NRW, this layer shows the Local Authorities ESTYN results for period 2012-2014. Fields Overall Performance and Overall Prospects have been updated based on the ESTYN results downloaded from http://www.estyn.gov.uk/english/annual-report/.   3) ESTYN Further Education; Further Education establishments in Wales, as at January 2014.   4) ESTYN Independent Schools; Independant Schools in Wales, as at January 2014.\tEstyn\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17284\tEXT_DS124289\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.767Z\t769\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPublic rights of way (PROW) (3rd party data)\tContains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and database right. All rights reserved. Contains OS data © Crown Copyright and database rights. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Data was digitised from data © all 22 Welsh Local Authorities.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323839\tx:\\Recreation and Access\\Public rights of way\\PROW.lyr\tData Custodian: Geospatial Team\tPublic rights of way (PROW) data. This data is related to the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Public Rights of Way (PROW) Condition Survey (98725) and covers the baseline PRoW dataset  INFORMATION WARNING this data is from 2007 and as such may be out of date.It should NOT be used for legal challenges. For anything legal related please contact the Local Authorities for the most up to date version of this dataset.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various Local Authorities\t2025-04-23\trevision\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-23T09:25:09.000Z\tGary Eccleston\t-\tPublic record\n17296\tEXT_DS124491\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.339Z\t286\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNHS health data (3rd party data)\t© NHS Wales  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234343931\tX:\\Social Economic\\Health\tInternal contact; Geospatial team\tHealth statistics by Upper / Lower Super Output Area for adult acticity, diabetes, heart conditions, weight, hospital clinics, hospitals and mental components.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17290\tEXT_DS124494\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.846Z\t895\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRSPB marine bird data (3rd Party data)\t© RSPB  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234343934\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\tInternal contact; geospatial team\tSpatial layers for marine bird 95% Utilisation Distributions in 5% Bands for; Black-legged kittiwake, Common Guillemot, European Shag and Razorbill.\tRoyal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17285\tEXT_DS124512\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.766Z\t564\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThreatened Lichen species (3rd party data)\t© British Lichen Society Data may be re-used under the terms of the 3rd Party Open Data licence (CC-BY). It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353132\tThe data is downloaded to a spreadsheet, stored in the DMS: teams\u0026gt;evidence\u0026gt;species\u0026gt;lich\u0026gt;Lichen Information\u0026gt;Lichen Statuses (EVID-357-7).  This data is for internal use only and is not to be disseminated. The data has been collated into a gdb along with other information Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Non-vascular Plants\\Non-vascularPlants.gdb\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tBritish Lichen society collation of the available records for rare and threatened lichens and lichenicolous fungi in Wales from 1700 to 2012, for purposes of conservation, future monitoring and research. Habitat and substrate information is included where available. Species are included if they are nationally rare or scarce, or have a conservation status other than least concern, data deficient or not evaluated.\tBritish Lichen Society\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17277\tEXT_DS124586\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.388Z\t535\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFlood Hazard Research Centre (FHRC): multi-coloured manual (MCM) codes (3rd party data)\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353836\tData is held on Flood team DMS  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/teams/kdim/Data-Management/dai/Third%20Party%20Data%20Licenses%20Agreements/Middlesex%20University%20(RE%20MCM%20codes%20for%20NRD%20data)/MCM%20licence%20to%2030%20April%202020.docx?d\u003dw8347a6146492437abf82c4b8117109a0\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\tCustodian: Mark Pugh\tMiddlesex University Flood Hazard Research Centre (FHRC): multi-coloured manual (MCM) codes for use in Natural Receptors Database 2019 only\tFlood Hazard Research Centre (FHRC)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:23.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17299\tEXT_DS124587\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.347Z\t735\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNational Oceanography Centre (NOC): Class A tide gauge data (3rd party data)\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353837\tData held in WISKI- Hydro module\tCustodian: Liam Perrot / Andy Wall\tNational Oceanography Centre (NOC) Class A tide gauge data\tNational Oceanography Centre (NOC)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:14:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17288\tEXT_DS124588\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.833Z\t429\tUnassessed\tEditable\tJNCC: 2013 Article 17 species distribution data (3rd party data)\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353838\tData held on DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/reporting/sr/Habitats%20Directive/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx  \"I am working with the Environment Portal team to publish the trends in a Power BI report on the NRW website. All data behind the overall results is already publically available on the JNCC website but in spreadsheet format per feature\".\tInternal Contact: Sheryl Davies, Specialist Advisor - Environmental Reporting, Environmental Reporting Team Internal Contact: Dylan Lloyd, Specialist Advisor - Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tHaitats regulations article 17 species distribution data by JNCC.\tJoint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17295\tEXT_DS124590\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.349Z\t673\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMet Office: Bala, Alwen, Ness Gardens climate data (historic) (3rd party data)\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353930\tCustodian: Andy Wall / Zhong Zhang\tCustodian: Andy Wall / Zhong Zhang\tMet Office: Bala, Alwen, Ness Gardens climate data (historic data held only; mutual exchange no longer taking place)\tMet Office\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17293\tEXT_DS124591\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.999Z\t843\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMet Office: Soil Moisture Index (MORECS) Weekly and monthly data (3rd party data)\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353931\tData held on DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/waterman/wrmr/ww/Morecs%20Data/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx\tCustodian: Andy Wall/Zhong Zhang\tMet Office Soil Moisture Index (MORECS) Weekly and monthly data\tMet Office\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17286\tEXT_DS124593\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.883Z\t526\tUnassessed\tEditable\tScottish Power: cable records (3rd party data)\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353933\tWe have an access only licence for one NRW user (John A Williams) to access this data online/as a service\tinternal contact: John A Williams\tOnline Access to GIS cable records database, refreshed every 24 hours\tScottish Power (SP Power Systems Ltd)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:24:54.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17298\tEXT_DS124594\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.348Z\t513\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNetwork Rail: Network Route data (3rd party data)\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353934\tonly available to Nick Pritchard through the W drive\tInternal contact: Nick Pritchard\tRail network route data from Network Rail.\tNetwork Rail\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17289\tEXT_DS124598\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.872Z\t43\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNational Flood Asset Dataset for Wales: Ordinary Water courses flood assets held in AMX–Flood (3rd Party Data)\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly. Re-Use rights are still to be fully deteremined.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353938\tAMX\tawaiting information from custodian Richard Kelland - now moved to new role\tmetadata to be provided by custodian.   AMX flood asset data store contains data that has been split into three types according to data owner (NRW, EA, others)\tVarious Local Authorities\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T17:00:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17279\tEXT_DS124611\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.481Z\t728\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironment Agency (EA): Integrated Height Model data (3rd Party Data)\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly. Attribution statement: Contains Environment Agency information © Environment Agency and database right. All rights reserved. Includes Natural Resources Wales LiDAR information © Natural Resources Wales and database right. All rights reserved. Includes APBG licenced DTM data © Getmapping Plc and Bluesky International Limited [2015].\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363131\tX:\\Basemapping\\Height\\EA Integrated Height Model 2016 DTM 02m.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Height\\EA Integrated Height Model 2016 DTM 02m Hillshade.lyr\tBen Anscombe\tData supplied by the Environment Agency.   Data was derived from 2016 NRW and EA LIDAR, with gaps derived from licenced APGB DTM data.\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-25T15:26:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17282\tEXT_DS124614\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.708Z\t590\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRSPB: Marine Mammal Data (Cetaceans and seals) Holy Island, Anglesey (3rd Party Data)\t© RSPB NRW does NOT grant permission to 3rd Parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the owner directly\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363134\tCetaceanSightingsRecords2019_RSPB.xlsx. held in DMS as document  EVID-446-321 Seal survey 2019_RSPB.xlsx held in DMS as document EVID-446-320\tTom Stringell, Marine Mammal Specialist\tAd-hoc sighting records of cetaceans from Ellins Tower, South Stack Anglesey. All records are taken from the window looking west.  Additional information, such as behaviour, direction of travel are also recorded and provided to SeaWatch Foundation. Ad-hoc sighting records of Grey Seals and total number of pups from North Stack to the far end of The Range also known as Penrhosfeilw.\tRoyal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:24:56.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17276\tEXT_DS124645\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.296Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironment Agency: NRW Alternate Class Points WGS84 (3rd Party Data)\t© Environment Agency Data may be re-used under the terms of the 3rd Party Suppliers Open Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363435\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh Areas\\NRW Alternate Class Points WGS84.lyr\tMarine Monitoring Officer\tWater Framework Directive saltmarsh monitoring. Saltmarsh points of uncertainty taken from 2018 aerial imagery. These points required ground-truthing to determine the species present. In coordinate system WGS84 for ease of use on hand held differential GPS device.  This data is Open, but owned by the Environment Agency\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\t2018-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17281\tEXT_DS124707\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.718Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMedian line to 12 Nautical Miles (3rd Party Data)\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373037\tx:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Median line to 12 NM.lyr\tGeospatial team, Knowledge Information Management Group\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-26T15:28:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17280\tEXT_DS124708\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.406Z\t10\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government (WG) Cardigan and Tremadog Bays Annex I boulder/cobble reef towed video, multibeam and sidescan Sept 2019 for Assessing Welsh Fishing Activity (AWFA) (3rd Party Data)\t© Welsh Government. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373038\tto be stored in Central Archive - to be confirmed by Stu Kay/Mark Diggle\tHarry Goudge  - Specialist (Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer)\t*************Data provided by WG under an OGL.  NRW intend to analyse the data and store for internal and external use.  Any products will belong entirely to NRW, however raw data remains under WG ownership *******************    Survey aim was to improve confidence and confirm the presence or absence of Annex I boulder and cobble reef (stony reef) within areas of Tremadog and Cardigan Bays where historically demersal fishing was undertaken within SACs. NRW has previous survey evidence of low or medium quality (10-40% cover) stony reef in some of these areas, although confidence in this data varies and therefore needs validation, and where found to be present, reef extent needs confirming and mapping.    Survey output included:   * Raw, processed \u0026amp; georeferenced multibeam (MBES) and sidescan (SS) survey data and metadata to include survey period start and end times, locations and any explanatory notes.  * Raw video and stills image files; ship or USBL GPS logs showing locations of all video transects.   Drop-down video (DDV) survey metadata to include:     DDV tow codes, start and end times (i.e. camera first on and last off seabed); Start and end times/coordinates for areas of boulder/cobble reef;    details of species and habitats visible whist viewing the onboard video feed;  and any explanatory notes that will assist with future use of the imagery data.\tWelsh Government\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:00:34.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17274\tEXT_DS124723\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.236Z\t687\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government: Multibeam, Drop-Down Video survey and Habitat Maps from St David’s Head, Pembrokeshire and West coast of Anglesey (3rd Party Data)\t© Crown Copyright: Welsh Government. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and WG\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373233\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\EMFF SMNR Spatial Approach Data from WG\tContact: Internal:  Lee Murrary Marine Mapping Analyst\tThis dataset is wholly owned by Welsh Government. Natural Resources Wales has access to this data through an Open Government Licence. This dataset is made up of three parts: Multibeam Survey data, detailing the topography of the seafloor; drop down video surveys taken at point of interest within the survey extent, and broad habitat maps detailing the extent and location of habitats, based on the findings from the analysis of the drop-down videos and multibeam data. This project has received funding through the European Maritime and Fisheries Fund, which is funded by the European Union and the Welsh Government.\tWelsh Government\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17278\tEXT_DS124752\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.366Z\t606\tUnassessed\tEditable\tJNCC: Marine Habitat EUNIS level 3 combined Map (3rd Party Data)\tData available to the public may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  You must acknowledge the source of the information in your product or application by including or linking to the following attribution statement: Contains JNCC data © copyright and database right 2019.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373532\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Broadscale habitats\\JNCC combined map EUNIS level 3.lyr   This is a third party dataset, the data is downloadable from the JNCC website.\tNatasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tIn some cases, a full-coverage map displaying the best available data everywhere at the expense of consistency is required. The production of such a product showing EUNIS level 3 habitats requires integrating EUNIS level 3 seabed habitat data from fine and broad-scale habitat maps. The product aims to create a complete map that presents the best available information on the distribution of EUNIS level 3 habitats at any locations in UK waters. This data product is required for, among other things, assessments of progress towards networks of marine protected areas (MPAs) and marine spatial planning in the UK. The aim was to produce a single map layer displaying the best quality EUNIS level 3 data for any given location. Furthermore, the process for producing the layer needed to be:  • Repeatable;  • Transparent; • Easy to explain and understand;  • Objective;  • Fully documented;  • Appropriate for EUNIS level 3 habitats; and,  • Appropriate for the UK intertidal and subtidal areas   Any location would show only a single value describing the habitat at EUNIS level 3, i.e. no overlapping polygons leading to multiple possible values at a location. The data at any location should be the best available to describe the EUNIS level 3 habitat type (i.e. the most likely to be correct). EUNIS level 3 habitats (Appendix 1) describe physical habitats classified using biologically meaningful parameters, substrate type, and additionally for rock: energy and biological zone. Therefore, a method was created to choose data based on their ability to describe these physical variables.\tJoint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)\t2019-09-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:24:55.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17275\tEXT_DS124754\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.316Z\t12\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNational Flood Asset Dataset for Wales: Environment Agency Flood Assets held in AMX–Flood (Historic legacy data) (3rd Party Data)\t© Environement Agency NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373534\tAMX\tRichard Kelland - now moved posts\tmetadata to be provided by custodian.   AMX flood asset data store contains data that has been split into three types according to data owner (NRW, EA, others)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-26T15:29:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17272\tEXT_DS124757\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.048Z\t719\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDepartment for Transport (DfT) Data (3rd Party Data)\t© Department for Transport. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373537\tx:\\Transport\\Dept of Transport car parks.lyr  X:\\Transport\\DfT Welsh ferry terminals.lyr  X:\\Transport\\Railway\\DfT Welsh railway stations.lyr  X:\\Transport\\Bus Stops.lyr\tGeospatial team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) holds Department for Transport data (DfT) for Wales. This includes DfT car parks and DfT National Public Transport Access Nodes (NaPTAN) data.  The static car parks dataset includes co-ordinates of each car park and entrance/exit, car park name, locality and operator, for Wales.   The NaPTAN data is a national dataset of public transport access points  - currently NRW have obtained data relating to Welsh bus stops, Welsh ferry terminals and Welsh railway stations.\tDepartment for Transport\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:24:57.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17271\tEXT_DS124768\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.047Z\t479\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironment Agency (EA): Area of Interest (3rd Party Data)\t© Environment Agency and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved.  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly. Those seeking commercial re-use may require a commercial licence available from the OS.  For internal use in NRW please use the following OS acknowledgement - © Environment Agency and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales OS Licence number: AC0000849444.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373638\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\EA Area of interest.lyr\tGeospatail Team\tThis dataset is owned by Enivronment Agency (EA). This is a dataset that is used to define the areas by which NRW extract Water Quality Sampling Data and Water Quality Permits to send to the Environment Agency as part of the Data Exchange Agreement.\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:16:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17273\tEXT_DS124785\t2024-05-28T12:19:15.952Z\t17\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government: Glastir Monitoring and Evaluation Programme (GMEP) data (3rd Party data)\t© Crown Copyright: Welsh Government Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373835\tTo be added - Data to be downloaded from CEH data portal and added to the X drive\tInternal Custodian: Claire Burrows, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team External  Custodian: James Skates - Welsh Government       NRW has just negotiated access to all the data 22/06/2022.  Matt is going to discuss with Barny if we need to keep the NRW land version or whether the full data will replace it or whether we will need metadata entries for all three Reduced resolution Open data downloaded from CEH (THis metadata entry_ - suggest renaming to incl Open in the title and adding to the abstract for each of the entries whether they are open data or restricted data) Full resolution data for NRW land Full resolution data for all Wales\tGlastir is the Welsh Government\u0027s sustainable land management scheme which pays for environmental goods and services aimed at: Combating climate change; Improving water quality and managing water resources; Improving soil quality and management; Halting biodiversity loss; Managing landscapes and historic environment and improving public access to the countryside; Woodland creation and management.  The Glastir Monitoring and Evaluation Programme (GMEP) has been commissioned by the Welsh Government to assess the performance of Glastir and ran from 2013 to 2016.   The data layers are as follows:  • Vegetation plot data  • Topsoil meso-fauna data  • Topsoil particle size distribution  • Landscape and habitat area data  • Landscape point data  • Landscape linear feature data  • Pond quality metrics  • Topsoil physio-chemical properties  • Headwater stream quality metrics  • Birds  • Pollinators\tWelsh Government\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:03:17.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17327\tEXT_DS124787\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.499Z\t334\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMet Office: Flood Forecasting Data (3rd Party Data)\t© Crown copyright [followed by year of first publication], Met Office NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373837\tNRWs Flood forecasting Service, currently licensed until 2023\tAndy Wall\tFlood forecasting data (observed and forecast), including Atmospheric Gridded Best Data. Data is provided to NRW to Inform the NRW Flood Warning service on decision making, including input into flood forecasting models. Also used for event planning, informing communications and scaling up of resources for operational resilience.\tMet Office\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17328\tEXT_DS124812\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.611Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCefas: WaveNet data (3rd Party Data)\tUse constraints to be added  **For Intrenal use only*** the licence lists a number of contributors, whose data is provided by CEFAS (each are to state their copyright notice in the finalised licence when provided).   providers 1) AFB,  2) UKCFF,  3) Channel Coast Observatory,  4) Met Office, Met Ireland/Marine Institute (MI), Shell UK (Sh), National Oceanography Centre (NOC), Oil and Gas UK Companies (OG). 5) European Marine Energy Centre (EMEC Ltd) - No copyright required\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383132\t\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tWaveNet is Cefas’ strategic wave monitoring network for the United Kingdom and provides a single source of real-time wave data from a network of wave buoys located in areas at risk from flooding.  The network has been in operation since 2002, WaveNet collects and processes data from the Cefas-operated Datawell Directional Waverider buoys, tethered at strategic locations around the UK coastline. The WaveNet system also gathers wave data from a variety of third party platforms and programmes (industry and public sector-funded).  Cefas has been contracted to make WaveNet data available  in XML format to UK Coastal Flood Forecasting Service (UKCFF) through the Environment Agency or through the Met Office. Such data are collated through a number of contracts with third parties.\tCentre for Environmental Fisheries and Aquatic Sciences (CEFAS)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:07:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17321\tEXT_DS124834\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.309Z\t1420\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales and West Utilities: Gas Data (3rd Party Data)\t© Wales and West Utilities. NRW does NOT grant permission to 3rd Parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the owner directly. Our licence permits sub-licencing the data to NRW contractors provided Wales and West Utilities receive a list of who these contractors are; NRW contractors are also still required to submit a LSBUD search.  Attribution Statement: © Wales and West Utilities.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383334\tX:\\Utilities\\Gas\\Wales and West Intermediate pressure gas main.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Gas\\Wales and West Low pressure gas main.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Gas\\Wales and West Medium pressure gas main.lyr\t\tWales and West Utilities gas data showing the location of Gas Pipes across Wales. NRW has access to the following:  Intermediate Pressure Gas Main Pipelines Low Pressure Gas Main Pipelines High Pressure Gas Main Pipelines\tWales and West Utilities Limited\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17329\tEXT_DS124837\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.617Z\t782\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales and West Utilities: Major Accident Hazard Pipeline (3rd party data)\t© Wales and West Utilities. NRW does NOT grant permission to 3rd Parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the owner directly. Our licence permits sub-licencing the data to NRW contractors provided Wales and West Utilities receive a list of who these contractors are; NRW contractors are also still required to submit a LSBUD search.  Attribution Statement: © Wales and West Utilities.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383337\tX:\\Utilities\\Gas\\Wales and West Major Accident Hazard Pipeline.lyr\t\tWales and West Utilities gas data showing the location of Major Accident Hazard Pipelines across Wales.\tWales and West Utilities Limited\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:24:57.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17323\tEXT_DS124854\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.328Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMet Office: Quality Controlled Rainfall Data (3rd party data)\t© The Met Office NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly. Attribution Statement: © Crown Copyright 2011, The Met Office\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383534\t\tH\u0026amp;T Team\tTo be completed\tMet Office\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17318\tEXT_DS124855\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.257Z\t1007\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Land Cover Map 2015 (3rd Party Data)\t© UKCEH NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly. Attribution Statement: : © UKCEH. Contains Ordnance Survey data © Crown copyright and database right [year].\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383535\tX:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2015.lyr\t\tLand Cover Map is a UK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH) spatial dataset, which NRW acquires under licence. NRW only has a segment of the data and not the whole of GB. This segment covers, the whole of Wales, cross border catchments and coastal areas around the Bristol Channel.  The dataset is an update from the Land Cover Map 2007 (LCM2007).\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:24:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17316\tEXT_DS124931\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.307Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMet Office Rainfall Amount Annual Average 1961-1990 (3rd Party Data)\t© The Meterological Office. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393331\tX:\\Physical\\Meteorological\\Met Office Rainfall Amount Annual Average 1961-1990.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a spatial datasets for annual average rainfall obtained from the Met Office. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) have obtained this dataset via a bespoke licence from the Met Office. The Met Office is the UK National Meteorological Service set up to research the possibilities of forecasting the weather, and now uses more than 10 million weather observation from across the UK to create advanced atmospheric models to predict upcoming forecasts and longer term climate change scenarios.\tMet Office\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T16:56:24.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17320\tEXT_DS124935\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.307Z\t876\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEarthTrack (Living Wales) Ground Validation Points for Land Cover and Land Cover Change (3rd Party Data)\t© Living Wales, 2021  Data may be re-used under the terms of the 3rd Party Open Data licence. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.   Attribution Statement: © Living Wales and Database Rights 2021.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393335\tX:\\Biological\\Living Wales\\Earth Track Points in Wales (LCCS data format).lyr\tHelena Sykes and Philip Jutson - Specialist Advisors, Monitoring and Analysis\tThe EARTHTRACK app has been specifically designed to allow a wide range of users to collect ground truth data that then supports a) spatial retrieval and validation of attributes with pre-defined units (e.g., m, %) or categories (e.g., species type) from EO data (by allowing measurement and recording of these on the ground) and b) validation of derived land cover and change classifications obtained from satellite and airborne data.   Earth Track points include near real time and historical records of observations of land cover and change on the ground that are geographically referenced (typically \u0026lt; 3-5 m positional accuracy) with well-defined categories that mirror those mapped at the national level through Living Wales (wales.livingearth.online).  These points have been collected by the EarthTrack mobile application, which has been developed using Open Data Kit software and functionality but is being translated (in 2021/22) to a professional version available to be made available on the app stores and allowing use across major operating systems.  All points are used to validate the land cover and land cover change maps from 2018 onwards and into the future, including in near real time.  The Living Wales 10 m national land cover maps for Wales are constructed from environmental descriptors with predefined units (e.g., m, %) or codes (e.g., for lifeform or leaf type) retrieved or classified respectively primarily from satellite sensor data and according to the according to the United Nations (UN) Food and Agriculture Organisation (FAO) Land Cover Classification System (LCCS) Version 2 taxonomy (Di Gregorio, 2005).  The EarthTrack mobile application exactly replicates how these land cover classes are constructed but at the ground level.    The land cover change information also replicates that of the land cover maps for Wales, which is based on evidence, and uses the Global Change Taxonomy (with an associated glossary). This describes change across global environments according to at least 230 classes, with these defined according to the notation ‘impacts (pressures)’.   These categories are derived from the Drivers-Pressures-States-Impacts-Response framework, where the states define the physical, chemical and/or biological characteristics of an environment.  Impacts are discerned from state changes (either one or many) and result from pressures associated with abiotic or biotic events or processes (e.g., fires, droughts) or anthropogenic activities (e.g., ploughing, timber harvesting) with these driven primarily by climate/weather and economy.  Di Gregorio, A.: Land Cover Classification System (LCCS). Classification concepts and user manual for software version 2. FAO Environment and Natural Resources Series, 8, 190 pp (2005).\tAberystwyth University\t2017-04-11\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:50:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17312\tEXT_DS124989\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.941Z\t25\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSmall Sewage Discharge Risk Zone Map England (3rd Party Data)\t© Natural England NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393839\t\tDave Jones\tSmall Sewage Discharge map of the UK is a risk map covering the whole of England so it can be used to inform the potential risk of Diffuse Water Pollution for new or existing small discharges to ground for all sensitive SSSIs  Please be aware that the dataset is no longer held within NRW and has not been replaced or superseded.  The Metadata entry is a record of the existence of the dataset in NRW\tNatural England\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-26T15:29:57.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17330\tEXT_DS125070\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.574Z\t974\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCardiff Bay Barrage Fisheries Monitoring Programme (3rd Party Data)\tData may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303730\tZ:\\Archive\\Access Recreation \u0026amp; Education\\Access \u0026amp; Recreation Programmes \u0026amp; Projs\\Cardiff Bay Fisheries Monitoring Project\t\tThe data sets include fish telemetry data, fish trapping data, rod and line caught fish data, broodstock and juvenile stocking data and returning fish data. All data was collected to assess the impact of Cardiff Barrage on the runs of sea trout and salmon on the rivers Taff and Ely. An Annual Report was produced on a yearly basis from 1990 to 2019. A range of supporting information and data was also produced such as weir information.\tCardiff Council\t2019-12-19\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:05:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17315\tEXT_DS125135\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.199Z\t645\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLocal Environmental Records Centres (LERCS) Wales: All Species Data (3rd Party Data)\t© Cofnod, WWBIC, BIS, SEWBReC NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originators directly. For NRW internal use only.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313335\tX:\\Biological\\Local Record Centres\\LRC undesignated species point.lyr   X:\\Biological\\Local Record Centres\\LRC undesignated species polygon.lyr\tBarnaby Letheren\tThe data are collated from species datasets held by the Welsh Local Environmental Records Centres.   All species are included in this dataset. If you want just priority and protected species please refer to metadata entry EXT_DS111486 or Invasive non-native species please refer to metadata entry EXT_DS122097.    The purpose of this data capture was to inform conservation management and decisions.  Data being received from LERC\u0027s does vary between centre. North Wales Environmental Information Centre (COFNOD) data only contains data that has been verified by trusted sources and specialists in the field. South East Wales Biodiversity Records Centre (SEWBReC), West Wales Biodiversity Information Centre (WWBIC) and Biodiversity Information Service for Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park (BIS) data contains all protected and priority records including records that have been unverified.\tCofnod (North Wales Local Record Centre)-WWBIC (West Wales Biodiversity Information Centre)-BIS (Biodiversity Information Service for Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park)-SEWBReC (South East Wales Biodiversity Records Centre)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:15:11.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17326\tEXT_DS125192\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.508Z\t650\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRWE Seahorse Hippocampus guttulatus records (3rd Party Data)\t© RWE The full dataset may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313932\tThe collated GIS dataset with the RWE records can be currently found here: Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Marine_Habitats_Species\\BAP_species\\Updated_records_20200701.gdb\\Hippocampus_guttalatus_20211207 . It is due to go on the X drive in this location: X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\Section 7 Species  . Spreadsheet: Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Marine_Habitats_Species\\BAP_species\\new_data\\RWE_hippocampus_data_GIS_table_confirmed_20211207.xlsx\tNatasha Lough\tExcel spreadsheet of hippocampus guttulatus Seahorse records from RWE and derived GIS.\tRWE Generation UK\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:19:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17322\tEXT_DS125220\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.247Z\t19\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government: Glastir Monitoring and Evaluation Programme (GMEP) data on NRW land (3rd Party data)\t© Welsh Government NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323230\t\tFull resolution data for NRW land only.  NRW has just negotiated access to all the data 22/06/2022.  Matt is going to discuss with Barny if we need to keep the NRW land version or whether the full data will replace it or whether we will need metadata entries for all three Reduced resolution data downloaded from CEH Full resolution data for NRW land Full resolution data for all Wales\tGlastir is the Welsh Government\u0027s sustainable land management scheme which pays for environmental goods and services aimed at:  Combating climate change Improving water quality and managing water resources Improving soil quality and management Halting biodiversity loss Managing landscapes and historic environment and improving public access to the countryside Woodland creation and management  The Glastir Monitoring and Evaluation Programme (GMEP) has been commissioned by the Welsh Government to assess the performance of Glastir. GMEP was launched at the same time as the Glastir scheme. This provides fast policy feedback allowing for the scheme to be modified to improve efficiency and effectiveness.\tWelsh Government\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:21:34.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17324\tEXT_DS125269\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.280Z\t8\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonmouthshire Public Rights of Way (3rd Party Data)\t© MMC. Data may be re-used under the terms of the 3rd Party Open Data licence. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323639\tNRWGDBTP.GISDATA.PROW_MONMOUTHSHIRE_22_09_2021\tThomas Blythe\tPublic footpaths, byways and bridleways in Monmouthshire. Provided by Monmouthshire council in 2022.\tMonmouthshire County Council\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-19T16:34:39.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17325\tEXT_DS125289\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.496Z\t303\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEMFF Protecting Our Seas Project (3rd Party Data)\tData may be re-used under the terms of the 3rd Party Open Data licence. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323839\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\EMFF Protecting our Seas project Envision contract outputs\tNatasha Lough\tIn 2020-21 seabed surveys were carried out by ENVISION for the Welsh Fishermen’s Association - Cymdeithas Pysgotwyr Cymru (WFA-CPC) in two Welsh Special Areas of Conservation (SACs), where interaction between mobile fishing gear and conservation features have been highlighted as a potential issue.   The study supported ongoing work by the Welsh Government and Natural Resources Wales to evaluate interactions between fishing activities and features of Welsh Marine Protected Areas (MPAs).  Surveys were completed in the Severn Estuary/Môr Hafren SAC to explore the location of Sabellaria alveolata reefs, and in Cardigan Bay/Bae Ceredigion SAC to establish the extent and ‘reefiness’ of cobble/boulder habitats.  The data products for the Severn Estuary include the processed geophysical data, processed ARIS (Sonar Camera) data, data logs, GIS files and reports. The data products for Cardigan Bay include processed video data, data logs, GIS files and reports.\tWelsh Fishermen\u0027s Association - Cymdeithas Pysgotwyr Cymru (WFA-CPC)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:22:56.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17314\tEXT_DS125322\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.117Z\t688\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government: Peatlands of Wales Maps (3rd Party Data)\t© Welsh Government. © Cranfield University. © OS. © NRW. © BGS. The data available via DataMapWales may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the underlying raw data. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333232\tX drive at: X:\\Physical\\Peat Peatlands of Wales  Peatlands of Wales Thickness Peatlands of Wales Evidence Score Peatlands of Wales Emissions Peatlands of Wales Carbon Stock\tEsther Clews\tThe Peatlands of Wales Map series provides an updated distribution of Welsh Peatlands (to 2022) based on current evidence sources. The data layers were created on a 50m grid whereby the presence and thickness of peat are elucidated from a range of sources for each 50m grid cell across Wales. A peatland evidence score defines the level of confidence in the presence of peat in any given grid cell, with those cells scoring more than 2 on this scale of 1-10, captured in the ‘Peatlands of Wales’ peat distribution map.    Full details of the sources of data used to build this map as well as the methodologies used to define the level of evidence for:    - the presence of peat - https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:peatlands_of_wales_scg8  - the derivation of the peat evidence score - https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:peatlands_of_wales_evidence_2nss  - estimates of peat thickness (depth) - https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:peatlands_of_wales_thickness   - carbon stock estimates - https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:peatlands_of_wales_carbon_stock  - greenhouse gas emissions - https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:peatlands_of_wales_emmisions.  These are presented in the Peatlands of Wales mapping methodology report.\tWelsh Government\t2022-04-04\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/maps/peatlands-of-wales-maps/\t2026-01-07T17:26:23.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17319\tEXT_DS125406\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.254Z\t615\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSevern Lower Seagrass Project - Seagrass Aerial Imagery Test 2022 (3rd Party Data)\t© Project Seagrass. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343036\t\tAdam Leyshon\tAerial imagery collected by Project Seagrass using the WingtraOne VTOL drone. Imagery gathered as a test to determine feasibility of gathering seagrass extent data.  Dataset – aerial imagery in the form of Raster files destined for ARC GIS.\tProject Seagrass\t2022-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:27:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17311\tEXT_DS125512\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.000Z\t707\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPackaging Regulations - Approved Reprocessors and Exporters\t© Environment Agency copyright and/or database right 2019. All rights reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353132\tN/A  (not stored by NRW – data held on a stand-alone ICT system - National Packaging Waste Database.)   https://npwd.environment-agency.gov.uk/\tKate Llewellyn - Officer 2 - Once in Ops Waste Regulation\tA public register, including filters such as by Regulator, that produces a pdf report in that shows the details of packaging reprocessors and/or exporters that are accredited by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under the Producer Responsibility (Packaging Wate) Obligation Regulations 1997.  The report is downloadable from the National Packaging Waste Database. This is  UK wide ICT system, dedicated to producer responsibility, that is managed by the Environment Agency (EA) on behalf of the regulators across the UK (NRW, SEPA and NIEA).\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\t2022-10-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:28:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17317\tEXT_DS125513\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.244Z\t342\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPackaging Regulations Approved Schemes (3rd Party Data)\t© Environment Agenc. Data may be re-used under the terms of the 3rd Party Open Data licence. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353133\tNot stored by NRW. EA Maintain this dataset https://npwd.environment-agency.gov.uk/PublicRegisterSchemes.aspx?ReturnUrl\u003d%2fPackagingPublicRegisterLinks.aspx%3fReturnUrl%3d%252fdefault.aspx\tKate Llewellyn -  Officer 2 - Once in Ops Waste Regulation\tProducers are manufacturers, converters, importers, pack-fillers and sellers of packaging, who supply to another part of the chain or to the end user. The Producer Responsibility Obligations (Packaging Waste) Regulations 2007 (as amended) oblige companies to recycle packaging waste to meet national targets. Producers\u0027 recycling obligations are based on packaging handled in the previous year.  Businesses must pass two threshold tests to become obligated producers. They must have a turnover of £2million or above and handle 50 tonnes or more of packaging in a calendar year.  Environment Agency store this data and Public Register of Compliance Schemes Reports are available via the National Packaging Waste Database. https://npwd.environment-agency.gov.uk/PublicRegisterSchemes.aspx?ReturnUrl\u003d%2fPackagingPublicRegisterLinks.aspx%3fReturnUrl%3d%252fdefault.aspx\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\t2022-10-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-26T15:30:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17313\tEXT_DS125568\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.136Z\t42\tInvalid\tEditable\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS): Depth to Groundwater (3rd Party Data) (Archived)\t© British Geological Society NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353638\tX:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS Depth to Groundwater.lyr\tMatthew Wareham, Data Services Team\tThis dataset is owned by the British Geological Survey (BGS) who is a provides UK geoscientific data, information and knowledge.  This dataset that displays probable maximum depth in metres to the phreatic water table.  This represents the likely lowest water level under natural conditions, in an open well or borehole drilled into the upper parts of the bedrock.  NRW no longer have valid re-use licence for this data from 30th April 2024 and any locally stored copied should be deleted.\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:52:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17306\tEXT_DS125585\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.639Z\t11\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Landcover Crops (3rd Party Data)\t© UKCEH NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353835\tArchived\tMatthew Wareham Data Services\tDeveloped in collaboration with Remote Sensing Applications Consultants Ltd (RSAC), the maps exploit satellite data - Copernicus Sentinel-1 C-band SAR (Synthetic Aperture Radar) and, from 2016 onwards, Sentinel-2 optical data.   The crops from 2016 onwards are winter wheat (including oats), spring wheat, winter barley, spring barley, oilseed rape, field beans, potatoes, sugar beet, maize, and improved grass. Other cereals, root crops, early potatoes, and vegetables are grouped in a class called ‘other’, together with a small number of parcels which could not be classified. From 2020, solar fields (sites with solar panels at ground level) are also included.   UKCEH Land Cover® plus: Crops is based on the Land Cover Map parcel framework. Every parcel which is larger than 2ha and categorised as agricultural land is coded with crop type information. Where parcels contain two or more crops, preliminary processing is applied to subdivide parcels into separate cropping units.\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:31:11.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17309\tEXT_DS125586\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.699Z\t12\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Land Cover Plus - Crops (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353836\t\t\tDeveloped in collaboration with Remote Sensing Applications Consultants Ltd (RSAC), the maps exploit satellite data - Copernicus Sentinel-1 C-band SAR (Synthetic Aperture Radar) and, from 2016 onwards, Sentinel-2 optical data.   The crops from 2016 onwards are winter wheat (including oats), spring wheat, winter barley, spring barley, oilseed rape, field beans, potatoes, sugar beet, maize, and improved grass. Other cereals, root crops, early potatoes, and vegetables are grouped in a class called ‘other’, together with a small number of parcels which could not be classified. From 2020, solar fields (sites with solar panels at ground level) are also included.  UKCEH Land Cover® plus: Crops is based on the Land Cover Map parcel framework. Every parcel which is larger than 2ha and categorised as agricultural land is coded with crop type information. Where parcels contain two or more crops, preliminary processing is applied to subdivide parcels into separate cropping units.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-26T15:31:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17310\tEXT_DS125587\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.756Z\t856\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTraws Eryri long distance cycle trail – Cycling UK (3rd Party Data)\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353837\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Cycling and walking routes\\Traws Eryri cycle trail.lyr  \"X:\\Recreation and Access\\Cycling and walking routes\\Traws Eryri cycle trail fallback route.lyr \"\tJohn H Taylor - Team Leader, Recreation - NW Wales\tLong distance off road cycle trail promoted by Cycling UK and supported by NRW. Route runs from Machynlleth to Conwy primarily on existing public rights of way - unclassified council roads, bridleways, tarmac roads and forest roads. Some short sections use private land where an agreement for access is in place with the landowner.\tCycling UK\t2023-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-19T16:42:04.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17304\tEXT_DS125633\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.564Z\t1086\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMet Office Wave Height - Hindcast Extract (3rd Party Data)\t© Met Office. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the owner directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363333\t\tNeil Counsell, Specialist Advisor - Flood Forecasting, Flood and Operational Risk Management\tMet Office have developed a wave modelling to develop and maintain wave forecasts of sea state.  These wave models have been used as a tool to predict the sea state and wave height which can then be applied to coastal overtopping and flooding.  The forecast model for Wales has been developed from using three locations around the coastline of Wales (North, South and West).  From these three locations, NRW have collected observed data and the following parameters are collected.  The data stored in the archive consists of the following information:  dir: Wave Mean Direction (degree) hs: Significant Wave Height (m) tm: Mean Period Tm01 (s)  NRW have stored this information for all of the offshore locations in Wales for the period 2006 – 2020.\tMet Office\t2020-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:32:04.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17305\tEXT_DS125653\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.534Z\t399\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWoodland Creation Planner Scheme (WCPS) Pre-applications (Third Party Data)\t© Welsh Government NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363533\tX:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\tCarol Owen Email: Carol.Owen@Naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales provides a verification service to Welsh Government for Government funding for new woodland creation government grants. Woodland Creation Planner Scheme (WCPS) has introduced a pre-application service to inform if land is suitable.\tWelsh Government\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-19T16:44:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17308\tEXT_DS125658\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.685Z\t606\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS): GeoScour Open Data (3rd Party Data)\t© British Geological Survey Data may be re-used under the terms of the 3rd Party Open Data licence. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363538\tX:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS Geoscour Tier 1 and BGS Geoscour Tier 2  (7 layers)\tCustodian - GIS Team  Internal Contact - Oliver Lowe\tThe British Geological Survey (BGS) is a UK provider of geoscientific data, information and knowledge. Natural Resources Wales has downloaded this open data for the Wales extent to use internally in our mapping system.  The BGS GeoScour Open dataset provide a generalised overview of the natural characteristics and properties of catchment and riverine environments for the assessment of river scour in Great Britain.  The dataset comprises two different tiers of geographical information system data containing seven different data layers. Each tier represents a different scale of assessment, from a high-level catchment to subcatchment data. The datasets are polygon (area) layers, which are described using straightforward classifications and enabling an indicative catchment susceptibility assessment.\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS)\t2022-07-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:33:56.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17307\tEXT_DS125713\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.653Z\t322\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Wales wide marine non-native species distribution data from iRecord (3rd Party Data)\t© UKCEH NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373133\tGIS data is on x drive (both full and redacted copies): X\u0026gt;Biological\u0026gt;Non-Native Species\u0026gt;iRecord  Spreadsheets with both full and redacted copies of the data are located here: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mnns/New%20records/Data%20from%20iRecord  Data licence stored: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mnns/New%20records/Data%20from%20iRecord/TERMS_NRW%20download%20from%20iRecord_2023_08_17.pdf\tLaura Grant, Specialist Advisor – Marine Ecology\tThis data contains marine non-native species distribution in Wales.  The data was downloaded from iRecord (https://irecord.org.uk/ which is administered by CEH) using a pre set up query. The list of 102 non-native species of interest was provided to CEH in advance. The boundary is set to include species up to 12nm and includes a small inland buffer. The downloaded data contains data input to iRecord at all verification levels, including species which have not yet been verified.\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\t2023-08-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:35:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17301\tEXT_DS125725\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.315Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCrown Estate offshore wind leasing round 5 PDA WGS84\t© The Crown Estate Data may be re-used under the terms of the 3rd Party Open Data licence. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373235\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Crown Estate offshore wind leasing round 5 PDA\tThe Crown Estate, 1 St James\u0027s Market, London, SW1Y 4AH 020 7851 5000 enquiries@thecrownestate.co.uk http://www.ccw.gov.uk\tIn support of The Crown Estate’s Offshore Leasing Round 5, this data describes a final scenario of Project Development Areas (PDAs) for the leasing round.\t\t2023-11-14\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-19T16:45:15.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19510\tEXT_DS161293\t2024-09-10T14:39:07.496Z\t16\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Land Cover Map 2022 (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323933\tX:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2022 10m Classified Pixels.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2022 25m Rasterised Parcels.lyr\tGIS Team\tLCM2022 is the tenth UK land cover map produced by UKCEH (others include LCM1990, 2000, 2007, 2015, 2017, 2018, 2019, 2020, 2021). Land cover is given as 21 UKCEH Land Cover Classes based on Biodiversity Broad Habitats (BAP, see Jackson 2000). LCM2022 was created by classifying Classification Scenes comprising Sentinel-2 Seasonal Composite Images combined with a series of Context Layers designed to reduce spectral confusion. The classification process is automatic, using a technique we call Bootstrap Training, combined with a Random Forest classifier.   NRW has access to the 10m Classified Pixel and the 25 m Pixel Rasterised Parcel Datasets\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\t2024-05-20\tcreation\tnull\thttps://catalogue.ceh.ac.uk/documents/a413d1c9-5c2a-4864-a3ff-2b9f764ec32d\t2025-03-21T10:38:25.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19624\tEXT_DS161304\t2024-10-01T10:46:28.807Z\t678\tInvalid\tEditable\tNational Disused Coal Tip (3rd Party Data)\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333034\tX:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\National Disused Coal Tips.lyr, X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Disused Coal Tips on NRW Land.lyr\tMary Lane, Specialist Advisor, Land Management \u0026amp; Liabilities, Land Management Team\tThis dataset has been compiled by Welsh Government and shows the locations and boundaries of disused coal tips across Wales.  The dataset includes information on the potential to impact public safety ratings from A to R as well as source of boundary data, local authority where the tip is located and additional information about the tip.\tWelsh Government\t2024-06-26-2025-10-16\tcreation-publication\tnull\t\t2025-10-16T13:41:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19629\tEXT_DS161308\t2024-10-04T10:27:37.541Z\t26\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Land Cover Map 2022 (3rd Party Data)\t© UKCEH. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333038\tX:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2022 10m Classified Pixels.lyr X:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2022 25m Rasterised Parcels.lyr\tGeospatial (GIS) Team\tLand Cover Map 2022 (LCM2022) is the tenth UK land cover map produced by UKCEH (others include LCM1990, 2000, 2007, 2015, 2017, 2018, 2019, 2020, 2021). Land cover is given as 21 UKCEH Land Cover Classes based on Biodiversity Broad Habitats (BAP, see Jackson 2000).   LCM2022 was created by classifying Classification Scenes comprising Sentinel-2 Seasonal Composite Images combined with a series of Context Layers designed to reduce spectral confusion. The classification process is automatic, using a technique we call Bootstrap Training, combined with a Random Forest classifier.     NRW has access to the 10m Classified Pixel and the 25 m Pixel Rasterised Parcel Datasets\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\t2024-06-24\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-27T09:10:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19630\tEXT_DS161309\t2024-10-04T10:41:54.105Z\t1244\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Land Cover Map 2023 (3rd Party Data)\tUKCEH. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\tX:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2023 10m Classified Pixels.lyr X:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2023 25m Rasterised Parcels.lyr\tGIS Team\tLand Cover Map 2023 (LCM2023) is the eleventh UK land cover map produced by UKCEH (others include LCM1990, 2000, 2007, 2015, 2017, 2018, 2019, 2020, 2021, 2022). Land cover is given as 21 UKCEH Land Cover Classes based on Biodiversity Broad Habitats (BAP, see Jackson 2000).   LCM2023 was created by classifying Classification Scenes comprising Sentinel-2 Seasonal Composite Images combined with a series of Context Layers designed to reduce spectral confusion. The classification process is automatic, using a technique we call Bootstrap Training, combined with a Random Forest classifier.    NRW has access to the 10m classified Pixel and the 25m Pixel Rasterised Parcel Datasets\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\t2024-10-04\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2024-10-17T08:41:15.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n20046\tEXT_DS161326\t2025-01-10T13:52:27.289Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHistoric Floodplain Meadows along the River Wye (3rd Party Date)\tData may be re-used under the terms of the 3rd Party Open Data licence. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333236\tWaiting for X:Drive load\tStuart Smith - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tA shapefile comprising a single layer containing polygons each representing a floodplain meadow (FPM) as spatial information with attributes. The shapefile covers a study area comprising part of the catchment of the River Wye. the selected stretch covers the main channel of the Wye from Builth Wells to Llangurig. The study area was defined by a 1km buffer along the River Wye, excluding minor tributaries.\tFloodplain Meadows Partnership\t2025-01-10\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-01-14T10:02:56.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20189\tEXT_DS161332\t2025-02-04T10:25:43.747Z\t9\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNational Soil Resources Institute (NSRI) : Anglesey 63K Soil Series Map (3rd Party Data)\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333332\tx:\\Physical\\Soils\\NSRI Anglesey 63K Soil Series Map.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.  National Soil Resources Institute has been incorporated into LandIS, or the Land Information System, which is one of the offerings of the Cranfield Environment Centre (CEC) the largest UK national and international centre for research and development, consultancy and training in soils and their interaction with the atmosphere, land use, geology and water resources.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20306\tEXT_DS161342\t2025-02-21T10:59:41.681Z\t27\tInvalid\tEditable\tGazetteer and Bibliography of the Mines and Quarries of Wales (3rd Party Data)\tData may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333432\tY:\\Metal Mines\\NWCRO data\\Mines data.gdb\tDave Johnston - Senior Specialist Advisor, Abandoned Metal Mines\tJeremy Wilkinson’s Gazetteer and Bibliography of these mines and quarries. The gazetteer currently has entries for more than 4,670 documented mine, quarry and other site names plus entries for more than 4,300 other sites (unnamed levels, pits, quarries, shafts etc.) identified from historic maps.\tHendre Coed\t2025-02-21\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T13:59:27.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n20472\tEXT_DS161351\t2025-03-25T11:43:47.919Z\t36\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGB1900 Filtered Mines and Quarries (3rd Party Data)\tData may be re-used under the terms of the 3rd Party Open Data licence. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333531\tY:\\Metal Mines\\NWCRO data\tDave Johnston - Senior Specialist Advisor, Abandoned Metal Mines\tThe GB1900 project computerised all the place names and other text on the Second Edition County Series six-inch-to-one-mile maps covering the whole of Great Britain, published by the Ordnance Survey between 1888 and 1914. This dataset is a filtered version containing elements identified as related to mining and quarrying\tNorth Wales Cave Rescue Operation\t2025-03-25\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-05-07T08:22:04.830Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n21545\tEXT_DS161391\t2025-10-08T14:55:56.453Z\t10\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAfonydd Cymru - All Wales River Restoration Opportunities 2017-2025 (3rd Party Data)\t© Afonydd Cymru [yyyy]. Data may be re-used under the terms of the 3rd Party Open Data licence. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. The dataset can not be used for commercial purposes nor can it be used to create derived datasets.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333931\tX:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Welsh River Trust Areas.lyr\tRhodri Powell - Specialist Advisor, River Restoration Programme\tHabitat restoration and barrier easement opportunities identified on Welsh rivers.  Data were collected by Welsh rivers trusts undertaking walkover surveys of watercourses across Wales.\tAfonydd Cymru\t2025-09-18\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-08T15:10:56.175Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n21886\tEXT_DS161405\t2025-11-06T11:42:07.921Z\t9\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBannau Brycheiniog National Park Public Rights of Way (3rd party data)\t© 2025 copyright Brecon Beacons National Park. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343035\tOne Drive\tGeminie Drinkwater, Senior Officer, Land Management, Waterfalls Country Project\tGIS data set showing the public rights of way across the national park footpaths, including route number, route length, route location - parish and ward.\tBrecon Beacons National Park Authority\t2025-11-03\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-12T14:38:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n22278\tEXT_DS161471\t2025-12-03T15:59:10.303Z\t130\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK CEH Stream Power (3rd Party data)\t©UKCEH. Data may be re-used under the terms of the 3rd Party Open Data licence. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343731\tX:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\CEH Stream Power.lyr\tSuzanne Hearn\tStream power is a measure of river energy based on specific stream power (W/m2).  Rivers with higher energy have a greater capacity to \u0027self-heal\u0027 and can be restored using passive restoration techniques. In contrast, lower energy rivers require more costly interventionist restoration approaches such as remeandering. Through understanding the energy regime of the river we can design restoration to minimize the work needed and maximize the length of river improved by working with and enhancing the natural processes of the river.\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\t2021-10-07\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-17T11:15:06.000Z\tMatthew Wareham\t-\tPublic record\n22529\tEXT_DS161475\t2026-01-08T13:26:02.318Z\t9\tUnassessed\tEditable\t4.0 EXT Test Record *** This is not a real dataset *** FULLFIELD EXAMPLE (3rd party dataset)\t© Welsh Government. NRW does NOT grant permission to 3rd Parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the owner directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343735\tX:\\Biological\\WelshGovernment\\speciesdata.lyr\tNRW contact is Geospatail Team (Keith Harri Jones) Data is sourced through the Welsh Government Environmental Modelling, Geospatial and Monitoring Branch (Oliver Massey)\tTHIS IS NOT REAL DATA.  • This is NRW\u0027s test dataset for our reduced metadata schema for third party dataset supplied to NRW for use.  We do not write fully standard compliant metadata, but adhere to a reduced NRW standard.  THis record is intended to ensure staff can refer back to a record that meets this NRW standard to help themselves solve validation errors • It is also to ensure all elements are displaying and exporting to multiple XML schemas. The Metadata is written to be compliant with the NRW EXT Standard • It may temporarily be in public view when checking view formats for the public but will be removed later.  Completing a record to the EXT standard using the template will mean it is not compliant with GEMINI or MEDIN standards and therefpre the internal validation routines will have errors.  If in rare cases where (NRW has been transferred custodianship of the data by the third party for purposes of publishing) and the record needs to be onward published to MEDIN or FindOPenData portaland therefore meet the Gemini or MEDIN standard, then the record will need to be edited and added to in Advanced View.  Example text: Geospatial biological datasets provided by Welsh Government.  It includes: - Welsh Goverment bird data - Welsh Government mammal data - Welsh Government insect data\tWelsh Government\t2022-02-01\tcreation\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:seabird_foraging_population_counts\t2026-01-19T16:46:41.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n22637\tEXT_DS161484\t2026-01-19T17:08:50.207Z\t17\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government: Habitat Wales Scheme habitat agreement classifications (3rd party data)\t© Welsh Government. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343834\tX:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Habitat Wales Scheme 2024.lyr, X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Habitat Wales Scheme 2025.lyr\t\tClassification of the habitats that make up the Habitat Wales Scheme (HWS) agreement information for all customers that entered in the 2024 and 2025 scheme years.\tWelsh Government\t2026-01-19\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-28T11:54:40.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22655\tEXT_DS161487\t2026-01-23T11:08:33.891Z\t9\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOS Boundary Line UK High Water polygon (3rd Party Data)\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343837\tX:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\UK High Water Boundary.lyr\tGeospatial Services\tOS Boundary Line High Water\t\t2026-01-23\tcreation\tnull\t\t2026-02-26T15:32:57.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n22820\tEXT_DS161495\t2026-02-25T13:40:45.541Z\t0\tUnassessed\tNon-editable\tCrown Estate Land Ownership (3rd Party Data)\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343935\tx:\\Ownership\\Crown Estate Land Ownership\\Crown Estate Land Ownership.lyr\tGIS Team\tThis is a GIS dataset to show the extent and location of land which is owned by the Crown Estate.\tThe Crown Estate\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2026-02-25T13:40:45.725Z\tMatthew Wareham\t-\tPrivate record\n17079\tEXT_DS24229\t2024-05-28T12:10:11.121Z\t331\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBiodiversity Species Audit for Wales (3rd Party Data)\t© RSPB, 2015. This material contains sensitive data which must be kept confidential. Not for release by NRW.  NRW does NOT grant permission to disseminate or re-use this data. Those seeking such permission must approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323239\tCD ROM in NRW Maes y Fynnon Library. 2 CD’s available: Library Number:  502.74(429)\tGIS Service Desk Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.\tA Species Audit produced by Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB) as part of the Biodiversity Action Plan process to inform Local Biodiversity Action Planners of the whereabouts and status of selected species considered of particular importance for Wales. It is hoped that, in the longer term, analysis of these data will help identify other measures to address Species Action Plan targets.  The purpose of this data capture is to create an audit of species data across Wales.\tRoyal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)\t2003-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:30:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17071\tEXT_DS24266\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.869Z\t778\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDatabase of Welsh Fens (Fenbase 6) (3rd Party Data)\t© University of Sheffield NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly. The database was written by and copyright of Bryan Wheeler, University of Sheffield.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323636\tCCW VMware: H:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\JonesP\\FENBASE\tGIS Service Desk Peter Jones Peatland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW Kathryn Birch Habitat Survey and Evaluation Ecologist Peatland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\tDatabase of Welsh Fens, which was collected as part of a larger inventory of UK Fens data (Fenbase) by the Department of Animal and Plant Science, University of Sheffield. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and therefore Natural Resources Wales (NRW) does not possesses the underlying access code and therefore the data base cannot be altered, except for adding, removing and editing data records.\tUniversity Of Sheffield. Dept.Of Animal And Plant Science\t1990-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:24.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17080\tEXT_DS24275\t2024-05-28T12:10:11.374Z\t327\tUnassessed\tEditable\tJNCC Conservation of River Macrophyte Database (3rd Party Data)\t© CNC/NRW, SNH, NE, DAERA and JNCC 2011 Publically available data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  You must acknowledge the source of the information in your product or application by including or linking to the following attribution statement: \u0027River Macrophytes Database published by JNCC. Contains Scottish Natural Heritage (SNH), Natural England (NE), Natural Resources Wales (NRW), and Department of Agriculture, Environment and Rural Affairs (DAERA) data © copyright and database right 2011.\u0027\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323735\tThe master version is available through the JNCC website http://jncc.defra.gov.uk/page-5815. NRW hold a copy in archive on server-based storage as project 293, media 1196.  This is the 2011 version, where restrictions were included and is an unreacted version. NRW have also downloaded a copy of the redacted version for general use\tSenior Freshwater Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Evidence Group, NRW.  Tristan Hatton-Ellis acts as the data(base) manager for the Freshwater Lead Co-ordination Network, which represents JNCC on freshwater issues.   This responsibility may have been passed to Sue Hearn       JNCC have requested permission from all agencies/contributors to make this database available as open data 2016-05-11 (Lynn Heeley is main contact).  In principal NRW have agreed providing the database does not contain personal data or sensitive species.  When confirmed we will need to amend the metadata to OGL licences, requests would be referred to JNCC and we can add the correct resource locator.   Data was released under an OGL in 2016.  Personal data (recorder names) has been redacted.  Information about the original restrictions is held in the ReadMe file in the archive media 1196\tA copy of a Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) database used in the conservation of river macrophytes containing a collation of data on: Site Information, Survey Information, Plants, Physical Data, Fish and Invertebrate data.  The database is managed by the Freshwater Lead Co-ordination Network, the data is owned by individual conservation agencies or even individuals and contains NRWs River Macrophyte data.   The purpose of this data capture was to collate data on a variety of river sites across Wales. The database is not fully populated: some invert and fish sections have few records, though most of the coverage is reasonably comprehensive.\tNatural England-Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)-Scottish Natural Heritage (SNH)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:12.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17070\tEXT_DS24320\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.588Z\t298\tInvalid\tEditable\tFuturecoast (3rd Party Data)\t© DEFRA 2002.  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333230\tArchived as Project No. 426, Media No. 1459, 1460, 1461, 1462.\tGIS Service Desk Terrestrial Science Group, NRW External Custodian: Beth Greenaway (020) 72381587 beth.greenaway@defra.gsi.gov.uk\tIn 2000, the Department for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs (DEFRA) and the National Assembly for Wales commissioned Halcrow to develop Futurecoast, a completely new way of predicting shoreline evolution. The analysis it contains gives a better understanding of coastal systems and their characteristics, and it is now being used to create the next generation of Shoreline Management Plans.  The main format is oblique aerial photos taken from helicopter of the entire GB coastline, linked to the Ordnance Survey map.  They were produced by DEFRA in conjunction with Halcro, expressly to assist in the shoreline management planning process.  A \u0027behavioural systems\u0027 approach, such as was adopted by the FUTURECOAST project, involves the identification of the different elements that make up the coastal structure and developing an understanding of how these elements interact on a range of both temporal and spatial scales. In this exercise it is the interaction between the units that is central to determining the behaviour. Feedback invariably plays an important role and changes in energy/sediment inputs that affect one unit can in turn affect other units, which themselves give rise to a change in the level of energy/sediment input.  Whilst the starting point for a behavioural system is the energy and sediment pathways, it is important to identify the causative mechanism as a basis for building a robust means of predicting the response to change. This must take account of variations in sediment supply and forcing parameters, such as tide and wave energy. However, it is also important to look for situations where the system response is to switch to a different state, for example, the catastrophic failure of a spit, or the switching of channels as a consequence of episodic storm events.\tDEFRA (Department for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs)\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:56.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17074\tEXT_DS47100\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.864Z\t326\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEffects of Air Pollution and Climate Change on Welsh Calcicolous Ecosystems (3rd Party Data)\t© DEFRA 1992.  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3437313030\tPaper reports: Room G4 shelves, Monitoring Team, Environmental Change Group.  Raw data: ECG R:drive.\tGIS Service Desk  Environmental Audit Group, NRW External Contact: Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)       Alternative Title: Effects of Air pollution and climate change on British Calcicolous ecosystems - Welsh sites    Alternative Title: Effeithiau llygredd aer a newid hinsawdd ar ecosystemau calchgar Cymru     Temporal Extent: 1992-12-31 ; 1990-01-01/1992-12-31    Frequency of Update: Not Planned Extent : Wales British National Grid No Spatial Element Data Format Type: Documents Site reports including maps and ground photographs in paper format.  Raw data in reports and on MS Excel file. Originator: Department of the Environment (DOE) (Air and Environmental Quality Division)    Originator: Lancaster University. Unit of Vegetation Science\tThis dataset is comprised of the welsh component of a larger dataset of 128 plots spread over 56 calcicolous grassland monitoring sites in the UK. In Wales there are 17 plots over 9 calcicolous grasslands. The sites surveyed were eight National Nature Reserves (NNR\u0027s): Cadair Idris, Craig Cerrig gleisiad, Craig y Cilau, Cwm Idwal, Ogof Ffynnon Ddu, Snowdon, South Gower, Stackpole, and one Site of Species Scientific Interest (SSSI): The Great Orme. The idea behind the work was to create a network of calcicolous grassland sites to look at the effects of air pollution and climate change on these sites.\tDEFRA (Department for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs)\t1992-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:55:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17078\tEXT_DS64566\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.911Z\t1004\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOrdnance Survey Basemaps (3rd Party Data)\t© Ordnance Survey NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Attribution Statement: Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right. All rights reserved. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353636\tX:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping   X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\Grids   X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS MasterMap   X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS Products  X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS Raster maps  X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS Strategi Vector 200k\tGIS Service Desk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) holds copies of Ordnance Survey (OS) basemaps for the whole of Wales, in paper and digital format. NRW obtain much of it\u0027s OS data, initially, as part of the Public Sector Mapping Agreement (PSMA) and from 2020 as part of the Public Sector Geospatial Agreement (PSGA), which is a collective agreement between OS and the government.\tOrdnance Survey (OS)\t2020-07-16\tpublication\tnull\thttps://www.ordnancesurvey.co.uk/customers/public-sector\t2025-07-08T08:29:38.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17076\tEXT_DS87452\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.880Z\t422\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Species and Species of Local Conservation Concern for the Dyfi Biosphere Catchment Area (3rd Party Data)\t© Biodiversity Information Service (BIS) 2004.  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837343532\tMaster copy held by BIS Local Record Centre.   Copy held by Mike Bailey (Aberystwyth/Ynys Las).\tGIS Service Desk  Mike Bailey, Dyfi Reserve, Senior Reserve Manager, NRW\tThe Dyfi Biosphere is the only Biosphere Reserve in Wales and includes the Dyfi Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and Cors Fochno SSSI. This dataset is a collation of species records. Just under 5000 species records within the Dyfi catchment.  Species include all UK Species of Conservation Concern, Red Data Book and Nationally Scarce species, Amber List Birds and other locally important species.  Also recorded is UK and EU legally protected species, Section 74 of the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000 (CRoW) species, UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Species and Red List Birds.  The data was collated on behalf of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to contribute to the Dyfi Biosphere project.  Biosphere Reserves are nominated by national governments and designated under the Man and the Biosphere Programme of UNESCO.\tBiodiversity Information Service (BIS)\t2006-05-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17075\tEXT_DS99376\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.916Z\t891\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBat Records from National Trust Properties in Wales (3rd Party Data)\t© National Trust.  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939333736\tA copy of the data is held in Recorder 6. This data in is Recorder 6 under the Third Party Data (copy) tag.\tGIS Service Desk Sam Dyer, Mammal Ecologist, bats and riparian mammals, Terrestrial Species Team, NRW       This data in is Recorder 6 under the Third Party Data (copy) tag.\tThis is a third party dataset containing records from the National Trust Recorder 2002 species database extracted in March 2007.  These datasets include records of all bat species commonly found in Wales and are split into 5 separate surveys.  The National Trust licenced these datasets for use by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and CCW staff only under their Access Agreement. This Access Agreement has now been outdated. The purpose of this data capture was to conduct bat surveys across National Trust sites in Wales.\tNational Trust\t2006-06-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:18.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17077\tEXT_DS99475\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.905Z\t804\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government: Tir Gofal Whole Farm Boundary (3rd Party Data)\t© Crown Copyright: Welsh Government  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly. Attribution Statement: © Crown copyright: Welsh Government\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343735\tX:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Whole Farm.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Whole Farm Features Line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Whole Farm Features Points.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Whole Farm Features Poly.lyr    Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\agrienvironment\\wag_depc\\tir_gofal\\wf_signed\\tg_whole_farm.tab\tGIS Service Desk\tThis is a spatial dataset owned by Welsh Government, detailing the extent and location of Tir Gofal which is a whole farm agri-environment scheme across Wales. This third party dataset is licensed to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for internal use only. Tir Gofal, a whole farm agri-environment scheme for Wales, encourages farmers throughout Wales to maintain and enhance the agricultural landscape and its wildlife and to provide new opportunities for people to visit the countryside. It replaces the previous schemes such as Environmentally Sensitive Areas and Tir Cymen, and takes forward the experience gained in a single whole farm scheme.\tWelsh Government\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:56:38.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17068\tEXT_DS99640\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.481Z\t427\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government: Tir Cymen Areas (3rd Party Data)\t© Crown Copyright: Welsh Government NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly. Attribution Statement: © Crown copyright: Welsh Government.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363430\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\agrienvironment\\wag_depc\\tir_cymen\\tircymen_area.tab\tGIS Service Desk\tThis dataset is wholly owned by the Welsh Government and licensed to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for internal use only. Tir Cymen Areas details the extent and location of areas that come under the Tir Cymen agri-environment schemes.\tWelsh Government\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:59:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17073\tEXT_DS99645\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.934Z\t258\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government: Tir Cymen Open Access Areas (3rd Party Data)\t© Crown Copyright: Welsh Government NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly. Attribution Statement: © Crown Copyright: Welsh Government.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363435\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\agrienvironment\\wag_depc\\tir_cymen\\tir_cymen_open_access_areas.tab\tGIS Service Desk\tThis spatial dataset is owned by the Welsh Government and licensed to NRW for internal use only. Tir Cymen is an agri-environment scheme.\tWelsh Government\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19274\tNRW_DS000001\t2024-06-26T11:14:56.464Z\t37\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTemplate for Dataset Metadata in NRW 1.0 format\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\t\t\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2024-09-25T15:18:56.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19187\tNRW_DS000002\t2024-05-28T13:15:20.379Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2024-07-31T07:58:26.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19184\tNRW_DS000003\t2024-05-28T13:12:50.305Z\t458\tUnassessed\tEditable\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2024-08-02T13:13:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19186\tNRW_DS000004\t2024-05-28T13:14:30.956Z\t183\tUnassessed\tEditable\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2024-08-02T13:13:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17504\tNRW_DS100031\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.712Z\t621\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLand Agency - Proposed Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Holdings\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444.  Crown Copyright and Database Right  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030303331\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex CCW land holdings proposed.lyr\tInternal Custodian and Metadata contact:  GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW ( Hannah Brazier or Pippa Jones )\tThis is a GIS dataset containing all land proposed to be owned (purchased) by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW).  The dataset includes information on site name, scale captured, and unique codes. The purpose of this data capture was to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements and provide a complete table showing all land proposed to be owned by NRW in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17505\tNRW_DS100107\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.762Z\t871\tValid\tEditable\tLakes Inventory\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313037\tX:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Lakes inventory.lyr  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\physical\\hydrology\\lakes\\welsh_lakes.tab\tTristan Hatton-Ellis\tThis is the Welsh subset of an inventory of standing waters (freshwater lakes and lochs) derived from Ordnance Survey digital map data at a scale of 1:50 000 . The inventory basic physical data such as location, surface area, perimeter and altitude. Catchment areas were computed for water bodies with a surface area larger than 1 ha from a digital terrain model (DTM) using customised routines in a geographical information system (GIS).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-03-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-24T16:03:42.302Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17503\tNRW_DS100124\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.911Z\t23\tValid\tEditable\tGrazing Marsh Phase 1 Habitat Data (Derived from Phase 1 Terrestrial Habitat Survey)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright, 2015. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313234\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Terrestial\\Original_Phase_1   derived data from at least 4 datasets in this folder). The grazing site boundary data is no longer available in this folder - contact GIST for further information.   Archived under Project 248, Media 900. This includes both Site Boundaries and Phase 1 Habitat Data.\tJon Rothwell?\t*** This data is no longer accessible,  therefore this metadata is under review 02/08/2024 ****  This is a spatial dataset based on the Habitats of Wales Phase 1 data (1979-1997) it shows updated phase 1 polygons for Grazing Marsh and Floodplain Grasslands for Wales extracted from the all Wales Phase 1 digitisation.   This was used to produce an inventory of the priority UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitat grazing marshes and flood plains for all Wales, see metadata entry NRW_DS99333.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:02:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17508\tNRW_DS100170\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.791Z\t411\tUnassessed\tEditable\tStoneworts in Wales\t© CNC/NRW, 2020  There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313730\tTop copy as a spreadsheet in the DMS (EVID-359161902-13) https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/stnew/Stonewort%20Information/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fstnew%2FStonewort%20Information%2FStonewort%20Statuses  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestial\\Lower_plants\\Stoneworts.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Stoneworts_Grid.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Snr Habitat Survey \u0026amp; Evaluation \u0026amp; Non-Vascular Plant Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW Internal Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW       The GIS data may need tp be updated from the sreadsheet\tThis is an excel spreadsheet that is also presented as a spatial dataset.  The datasets contains a collation of all known records of stoneworts in Wales from 1726 up till July 2018. Stoneworts (or charophytes) are a group of algae that grow in fresh and brackish water and have a relatively complex structure. They are sensitive to pollution, including nutrient enrichment, and are therefore good indicators of water quality. Thirty species have been recorded in the UK and seventeen of these occur in Wales. Of the latter, four are Red Data Book species, six are Nationally Scarce, and 9 are considered rare in Wales; three are Priority Species in the UK BAP and are included on the National Assembly of Wales\u0027 List of Species and Habitats of Principal Importance for the Conservation of Biological Diversity (the \\Section 74\\\" list). The purpose of this data capture is to inform understanding of the distribution and status of rare an/or threatened lichen species in Wales. This in turn will facilitate decision-making on SSSI notifications species status assessments etc.\"\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17492\tNRW_DS100178\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.378Z\t1383\tValid\tEditable\tOpen Access - Registered Common Land (CRoW Act)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313738\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Registered Common Land 2004.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Registered Common Land 2014.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Chris Heaps, Specialist Advisor: Trails and Open Access Gary Eccleston/Geospatial team are responsible for the GIS digitisation and updates.\tThe CRoW Act provides for public access on foot to certain types of land, amends the law relating to public rights of way, increases measures for the management and protection for Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and strengthens wildlife enforcement legislation, and provides for better management of Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONB). Under Section 4 of the CRoW Act it states the following: It shall be the duty of Natural Resources Wales (formerly Countryside Council for Wales) to prepare, in respect of Wales, maps which together show; (a)  all registered common land, and (b)  all open country.   It is a redacted version of the \u0027Common Land (CROW Act) Fully Attributed Dataset\u0027, see separate metadata entry for details.   Digitisation took place in 2014 for all Wales. This dataset does not include Urban Commons, Commons with Deeds for Access, 1899 Commons Act, Provisional Order Confirmation or Village Greens.   When using this data to understand the extent of Open Access , it should be used in conjunction with Open land, Open Country, Registered Common Land, Other Statutory Access Land and Other Dedicated Land from the same years. Contact your local authority for access to and information about Registers of Common Land.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-16\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/395/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_COMMON_LAND_2014\t2026-01-06T16:00:25.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17499\tNRW_DS100182\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.564Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLocal Access Forums\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313832\t\t\tThis is a spatial Dataset\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:59:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17510\tNRW_DS100196\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.901Z\t440\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Vegetation Layer\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313936\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase1 Vegetation.lyr .   Version 3 is on the cloud for use in ArcGIS and version 2 is on M:\\Data\\Live.   Full description can be found in M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\Lookup_tables.\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing polygon layers showing the boundaries and attributes of vegetation polygons.   In 2014 the data were cleaned by NRW\u0027s Terrestrial Mapping Specialist, to rectify the line work issues, and the resolution was set to 0.5 m. Polygon attributes include Phase 1 habitat code(s), information on scattered vegetation uniformly distributed across an entire polygon, acid flush present in patches too small to map individually, dominant species codes, habitats in re-vegetated quarry and spoil, whether it was apparent from the OS Landline maps at the time of digitising in 2000-2002 that habitat loss had occurred, name of Upland Vegetation Survey site (or site-complex), whether polygon is upland or lowland, polygon area.   The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. The all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales. About 30 percent of upland polygons are mixed habitat with no information on habitat proportions. A voronoi version of the vegetation layer has been created to help address this (see metadata for All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Vegetation Layer voronoi - GIS dataset).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17498\tNRW_DS100201\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.563Z\t658\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Scans\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030323031\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Scans.lyr\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.   Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset of raster images of the hand-coloured Phase 1 Survey 5x5 km square 1:10,000 Ordnance Survey maps. The original maps (\u0027master maps\u0027) are lodged in the National Library of Wales.   The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:02:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17501\tNRW_DS100202\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.735Z\t357\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - SurveyArea\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030323032\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Survey area.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Habitat Survey.lyr\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis spatial dataset shows the boundaries and survey dates of the 15 different survey areas adopted for reporting purposes during the lowland phase of the All Wales terrestrial Phase 1 habitat survey. The survey areas are based on the Nature Conservancy Council\u0027s 15 Areas of Search. The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level.   An all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-04-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17496\tNRW_DS100237\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.422Z\t656\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Upland Boundary\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030323337\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Upland boundary.lyr\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis spatial dataset shows the boundary between upland and lowland that was adopted during the terrestrial Phase 1 Survey and the WFU Upland Vegetation Survey of Wales.   The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-07-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:01:56.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17497\tNRW_DS100238\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.434Z\t537\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - 1Kmsq\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030323338\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 1kmsq V2.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 1kmsq V3.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 1kmsq V3 centre point.lyr ,  Legacy CCW Archive - M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\ph_1_database\\Version_3\\Ph1_1kmsq_v3_centroids, data and polygons\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset made up of point dataset of Phase 1 habitat areas in every 1 km square in Wales.   The data are derived from a programme of field recording that was begun by the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) in 1979 and continued by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) after restructuring in 1991. The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level.   This electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage (see metadata for All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - 1kmsq - Access database). The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.   Habitat areas are summed over regions and districts within 1 km squares. Version 2 is a point layer of 1 km square centroids; habitats are labelled with Phase 1 lettered and alphanumeric codes; a \u0027modbog\u0027 column identifies whether modified bog (E.1.7, E.1.8 and E.4) is derived from blanket bog or raised bog; habitat areas are given for upland, lowland and total. Version 3 was derived to facilitate data disaggregation and reporting. In it areas of heath-grass mosaics (D.5, D.6 and D.7) are split 50:50 between the appropriate heathland and grassland classes; modified blanket bog is labelled E.1.6.1.2 and modified raised bog is labelled E.1.6.2.2; the Phase 1 fen categories are combined as E.3 and modified fen as E.3.1; the saltmarsh categories are combined as H.2.6; limestone pavement is combined with basic rock; intertidal habitats are removed because they were mapped in greater detail by CCW\u0027s Phase 1 Intertidal Survey (1996-2005). Version 3 comprises a non-mappable browser table of habitat areas per 1 km square, a table of 1km square centroids and a table of 1 km square polygons. The three tables link on the grid square field.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-04-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:59:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17495\tNRW_DS100241\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.430Z\t458\tValid\tEditable\tBiodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Sabellaria alveolata Honeycomb Worm\t© CNC/NRW, 2015. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030323431\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan\\Marine\\Marine Bap Habitats Sabellaria Alveolata Reef.lyr\tContact: GIS and Spatial Team\tSabellaria alveolata reefs are formed by the honeycomb worm Sabellaria alveolata, a polychaete which constructs tubes in tightly packed masses with a distinctive honeycomb-like appearance. These reefs can be up to 30 or even 50 cm thick and take the form of hummocks, sheets or more massive formations. Reefs are mainly found on the bottom third of the shore, but may reach mean high water of neap tides and extend into the shallow subtidal in places. This is a spatial dataset made up of known locations of the Sabellaria alveolata Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitats across Wales. The data are derived from the Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCWs) intertidal Phase 1 survey, data held by the Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) from the Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) project and from ad-hoc records by individuals. The aim of this data capture digitally is to represent the distribution of the BAP habitat across wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-16T15:23:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17494\tNRW_DS100338\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.373Z\t375\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland_Sites (Archived)\t© CNC/NRW, 2015. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333338\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_grassland\\PH2_Lowland_Grassland_sites.lyr\tMetadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Services, NRW Custodian: Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing sets of survey site boundaries recorded during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales (1987-2004). The Phase 2 lowland Grassland Survey of Wales was a detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation. The chief aims of the Phase 2 survey were to characterise the semi natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed. Sites varied in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering. The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and 2013 by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  Please see NRW_DS46160 which is metadata for the whole Phase 2 Lowland Grass Surveys.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-10-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17491\tNRW_DS100358\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.452Z\t412\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Mosaic Polygons (Archived)\t© CNC/NRW, 2015. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333538\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_grassland\\PH2_Lowland_Grassland_mospolys.lyr\tCustodian - Terrestrial Ecosystem Group, NRW Jane Stevens, Habitat and Ecosystem Resource Analyst, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Team\tThis is a spatial dataset of grassland mosaic polygons. These polygons are a gridsquares, coloured in chequerboard fasion according to Vegetation_type, for display purposes only. The records where collected during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales ( 1987 - 2004 ). Digitising this datasets allowed the detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation to be accessible more widely. The purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales, and provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed. Sites vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.  The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). CCW now forms part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  The main limitation of the data are the age of records. Habitat composition may change over time due to successional trends and also through agricultural treatments or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.  Please see NRW_DS46160 which is metadata for the whole Phase 2 Lowland Grass Surveys.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-04-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:50:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17502\tNRW_DS100359\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.737Z\t611\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Photo (Archived)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2006. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333539\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_grassland\\PH2_Lowland_Grassland_photos.lyr   Images are on DVD in the Data Services Safe.\tMetadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW Custodian: Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed. This is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons. Photo locations recorded during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales (1987-2004). A detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation. This dataset has all Wales coverage. Sites vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.  The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW).  Please see NRW_DS46160 which is metadata for the whole Phase 2 Lowland Grass Surveys.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:50:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17489\tNRW_DS100389\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.724Z\t316\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Quadrat (Archived)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2006. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333839\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_grassland\\PH2_Lowland_Grassland_quadrats.lyr\tMetadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW Custodian: Terrestrial Ecosystem Group, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed.  This dataset consists of quadrat locations recorded during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland survey of Wales ( 1987 - 2004 ) and a detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation. Sites across Wales vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.  The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by CCW.  Please see NRW_DS46160 which is metadata for the whole Phase 2 Lowland Grass Surveys.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17500\tNRW_DS100390\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.664Z\t362\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Target Notes (Archived)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2006. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333930\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Terrestial\\Original_Phase_1\\Phase1 Target.lyr\tMetadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Services, NRW Custodian: Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed.  This is a GIS dataset containing target notes recorded during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales (1987 - 2004). The Welsh Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey was a detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation. Sites vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.  The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW).  Please see NRW_DS46160 which is metadata for the whole Phase 2 Lowland Grass Surveys.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:52:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17509\tNRW_DS100391\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.908Z\t366\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Vegetation (Archived)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2006. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333931\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Terrestial\\Original_Phase_1\\Phase1 Vegetation.lyr\tMetadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Services, NRW Custodian: Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed.  This is a GIS dataset containing vegetation types recorded during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland survey of Wales (1987 - 2004). A detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation.  Sites vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha. The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW).  Please see NRW_DS46160 which is metadata for the whole Phase 2 Lowland Grass Surveys.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:52:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17490\tNRW_DS100503\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.733Z\t356\tUnassessed\tEditable\tArea of Search\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030353033\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Areas of search.lyr\tGIS Service Desk\tThis is a spatial dataset consisting of the former Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) categories for surveying and following vice County boundaries.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Ordnance Survey\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:59:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17493\tNRW_DS100514\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.425Z\t684\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Zostera marina (Eelgrass) Monitoring\t© CNC/NRW 2007 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030353134\tSkomer MNR, West Region Drive  /shared documents/data/biological data/zostera\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Zostera marina is the only flowering plant within the British Isles that grows and produces seed entirely submerged by seawater.  Zostera populations are highly productive habitats and they provide an important stabilising function for the mobile marine sediments. The maintenance of Zostera populations directly influences the associated algal and invertebrate communities that it supports, which are an important source of food for birds. Zostera marina is one of three seagrass species which are listed as nationally scarce and are included as a key habitat for conservation in the UK Biodiversity Action Plan 1994. The bed at North Haven, Skomer is one of only 4 in Wales and is the deepest. It is completely sub tidal and has been well surveyed.  The purpose of this data capture was: 1.  To map the boundaries of the Zostera marina bed. 2.  To determine and identify changes in its distribution and abundance. 3.  Record conspicuous organisms associated with the Zostera population.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:35:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17487\tNRW_DS100675\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.636Z\t370\tValid\tEditable\tEC Bathing Waters Historic Compliance 1988 to 2013\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030363735\tX:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\EC Bathing Waters Historic Compliance 1988 to 2013.lyr\tMark Squire Reporting team\tThis dataset is an archive of data relevant to the old Bathing Water Directive (76/160/EEC) and won\u0027t be updated now the directive is no longer in force.   For the latest bathing water quality information please see https://environment.data.gov.uk/wales/bathing-waters/index.html.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-03-17\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/78/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:EUBathingWaterCompliance\t2025-12-01T16:21:58.364Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17486\tNRW_DS100749\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.579Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Enterprise Holdings\t© Forest Enterprise Holdings 2006. This dataset is owned by the Forest Enterprise Holdings, and licensed to NRW for internal use only. Please contact the Forest Enterprise Holdings before using the dataset for any other purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373439\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\external_agency\\forestry\\forest_enterprise\\fe_land.TAB\t\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Forest Enterprise Holdings and licensed to CCW for internal use only.  The dataset has been supplied by the Forest Enterprise Holdings and contains the legal boundaries of Forestry Enterprise land as at 11/07/2001.\tForest Enterprise\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:59:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17488\tNRW_DS100750\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.716Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHabitat Agri-Environment Scheme\t© WAG 2006. This dataset is owned by the Welsh Assembly Government, and licensed to NRW for internal use only. Please contact the Welsh Assembly Government before using the dataset for any other purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373530\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\agrienvironment\\wag_depc\\esa\\habitatbddis.TAB\t\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Welsh Assembly Government and licensed to CCW for internal use only. The dataset has been supplied by the Welsh Assembly Government and, similar to the ESA`s, the Habitat scheme is a part farm Agri-Environment scheme, run by the WAG Agricultural Dept.\tFarming And Rural Conservation Agency [FRCA]\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:06:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17485\tNRW_DS100751\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.454Z\t359\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntegrated Pollution Prevention Control (IPPC) Sites\t© CNC/NRW and Environment Agency NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373531\tx:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\Active IPPC Sites.lyr  x:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\IPPC Sites.lyr   Archived Dataset: Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\physical\\hydrology\\environment_agency\\industrial_sites\\ipc_sites.TAB\tGIS Service Desk Data Custodian: Susan Freeman\tIn 1996 the EU adopted a set of common rules for permitting and controlling industrial installations in the IPPC Directive. In essence, the IPPC Directive was about minimising pollution from various industrial sources throughout the EU. Operators of industrial installations whose operating activities covered by Annex I of the IPPC Directive were required to obtain an environmental permit from the authorities in the EU countries.  Integrated Pollution Prevention Control (IPPC) Sites is a spatial dataset detail the point location of industrial installations that require a permit under The IPPC Directive. Please note, these points do not indicate the size or area covered by the installations, they represent a general location only. The system of Integrated Pollution Prevention and Control (IPPC) set out in the IPPC Directive applies an integrated environmental approach to the regulation of certain industrial activities. This means that emissions to air, water (including discharges to sewer) and land, plus a range of other environmental effects, must be considered together as part of a bespoke mix of regulated pollution prevention. This bespoke regulation will depend on the type, location and size of the installation. IPPC aims to prevent emissions and waste production and where that is not practicable, reduce them to acceptable levels.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17484\tNRW_DS100779\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.536Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRAS Sites\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373739\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\physical\\hydrology\\environment_agency\\industrial_sites\\ras_authorisations_sites.TAB\t\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Environment Agency Wales and licensed to CCW for internal use only.  The dataset has been supplied by the Environment Agency Wales and contains Sites under Radioactive Substance control (point data)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:23.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17483\tNRW_DS100786\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.315Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) - River Biology Spatial Data\t© Enviroment Agency.   NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373836\tx:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\GQA Biology Network.lyr  X:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\GQA Biology Sample Points.lyr\tPlease contact Nick Davies ( Environment Agency)\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Environment Agency Wales and licensed to NRW for internal use only. The dataset has been supplied by the Environment Agency Wales and contains  River quality biology. GQA covering England \u0026amp; Wales displayed in varying thickness according to flow and colour according to Biology (year 2000).\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:49:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17482\tNRW_DS100791\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.291Z\t790\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSquirrel Survey of Aberhirnant Forest and Woodland surrounding Lake Vyrnwy (1999 - 2000)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders   NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373931\t[A] Species data held in Recorder 6\tLiz Hallewell - Mammal Ecologist\tThis dataset is made up of presence or absence records for red and grey squirrels in Aberhirnant Forest and the woodlands surrounding Lake Vyrnwy.   The purpose of this data capture was to report on the presence, distribution and likely status of red squirrels in Aberhirnant Forest and the Lake Vyrnwy area.  The data is will be used as a basis to assess whether red squirrels have a future in this area, that is, sufficient potential to justify further monitoring and sympathetic forest management. The target sites included conifer plantations of Aberhirnant Forest and the catchment area of Lake Vyrnwy.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17481\tNRW_DS100811\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.219Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Discharge ( Revoked )\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030383131\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\WIMS Revoked Consents.lyr\t\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons.    The dataset has been supplied by the NRW and contains Revoked Water Discharge Consent Licenses point data information.  This is likely to be a duplicate of \"WIMS Revoked Consents - Aug 2016.lyr\" (NRW_DS123341) as the dataset WIMS Revoked Consents.LYR no longer exists on the NRW X drive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:01:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17528\tNRW_DS100821\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.247Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWoodland Grant Scheme 1\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030383231\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 1.lyr\tKeith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team, NRW\tThe Woodland Grant Scheme (WGS) provides incentives for people to create and manage woodlands on sites all over Wales. WGS1 operated between April 1989 and April 1992. This is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects in the form of polygons. The dataset identifies areas approved by grant type within each WGS Scheme. The list below provides a summary of grant type captured: SCHEME BOUNDARY -The boundary of the area(s) included within the scheme. NEW PLANTING APPROVED - Areas within the contract where new planting has been approved under NP, NB, NS, NC, NK, NQ and/or NU. NEW NATURAL REGENERATION APPROVED - Areas within the contract where new natural regeneration has been approved under NN, NL and/or NZ. REPLANTING/REGENERATION/MANAGEMENT - Areas within the contract where replanting, restocking by natural regeneration or management has been approved under RP, RN, RC RG, RQ, EN, MG and/or SM. COMMUNITY WOODLAND CONTRIBUTION - Areas within the contract approved for Community Woodland Contribution. Abbreviations used above: NP New planting, NB New planting with better land supplement, NS New planting accepted into the 1988 set-aside scheme, NC New planting of coppice, NK New planting of coppice with better land supplement, NQ New planting short rotation coppice, NU New planting short rotation coppice with better land supplement, NN New planting by natural regeneration, NL New planting by natural regeneration with better land supp, NZ New planting by natural regeneration with set-aside, RP Restocking by replanting, RC Restocking with coppice (planting not regrowth), RQ Restocking with short rotation coppice (planting not regrowth), RN Restocking by natural regeneration, EN Restocking by existing natural regeneration, MG Management grant, SM Special management grant. This is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects in the form of polygons.   Purpose of data capture: The scheme provided incentives for people to manage woodlands on sites all over Great Britain.  Temporal coverage: June 1988 to June1991.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1992-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:05:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17536\tNRW_DS100822\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.373Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWoodland Grant Scheme 2\tTo be added\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030383232\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 2.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Services Team ..........Keith Harri Jones\tThe Woodland Grant Scheme (WGS) provides incentives for people to create and manage woodlands on sites all over Wales. WGS2 operated between April 1992 and September 1994. This is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects in the form of polygonsThe dataset identifies areas approved by grant type within each WGS Scheme. The list below provides a summary of grant type captured: SCHEME BOUNDARY -The boundary of the area(s) included within the scheme. NEW PLANTING APPROVED - Areas within the contract where new planting has been approved under NP, NB, NS, NC, NK, NQ and/or NU. NEW NATURAL REGENERATION APPROVED - Areas within the contract where new natural regeneration has been approved under NN, NL and/or NZ. REPLANTING/REGENERATION/MANAGEMENT - Areas within the contract where replanting, restocking by natural regeneration or management has been approved under RP, RN, RC RG, RQ, EN, MG and/or SM. COMMUNITY WOODLAND CONTRIBUTION - Areas within the contract approved for Community Woodland Contribution. Abbreviations used above: NP New planting, NB New planting with better land supplement, NS New planting accepted into the 1988 set-aside scheme, NC New planting of coppice, NK New planting of coppice with better land supplement, NQ New planting short rotation coppice, NU New planting short rotation coppice with better land supplement, NN New planting by natural regeneration, NL New planting by natural regeneration with better land supp, NZ New planting by natural regeneration with set-aside, RP Restocking by replanting, RC Restocking with coppice (planting not regrowth), RQ Restocking with short rotation coppice (planting not regrowth), RN Restocking by natural regeneration, EN Restocking by existing natural regeneration, MG Management grant, SM Special management grant.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1994-09-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T17:06:12.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17535\tNRW_DS100829\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.363Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWoodland Grant Scheme 3\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030383239\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 3.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThe Woodland Grant Scheme (WGS) provides incentives for people to create and manage woodlands on sites all over Wales. The dataset identifies areas approved by grant type within each WGS Scheme. The list below provides a summary of grant type captured:  - Scheme Boundary: The boundary of the area(s) included within the scheme.  - New Planting Approved: Areas within the contract where new planting has been approved under New planting (NP), New planting with better land supplement (NB), New planting accepted into the 1988 set-aside scheme (NS), New planting of coppice (NC), New planting of coppice with better land supplement (NK), New planting short rotation coppice (NQ) and/or New planting short rotation coppice with better land supplement (NU).  - New Natural Regeneration Approved: Areas within the contract where new natural regeneration has been approved under New planting by natural regeneration (NN), New planting by natural regeneration with better land sup (NL) and/or New planting by natural regeneration with set-aside (NZ).  - Replanting/Regeneration/Management: Areas within the contract where replanting, restocking by natural regeneration or management has been approved under Restocking by replanting (RP), Restocking by natural regeneration (RN), Restocking with coppice (planting not regrowth) (RC), RG, Restocking with short rotation coppice (planting not regrowth) (RQ), Restocking by existing natural regeneration (EN), Management grant (MG) and/or Special management grant (SM).  - Community Woodland Contribution - Areas within the contract approved for Community Woodland Contribution.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17538\tNRW_DS101725\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.436Z\t571\tValid\tEditable\tAge and Growth Analysis of Native Oyster Beds (Ostrea edulis) in Wales\t© CNC/NRW 2003. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031373235\tArchived at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon as Project No. 77, Media 104  (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 101to200\\Media 104) and also copies are held at Pembroke Dock.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe native flat oyster Ostrea edulis is a UK and Welsh Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species, for which a UK action plan exists. Native oysters have a long history of over-exploitation in Wales and elsewhere in the UK. Harvested beds in Wales used to be present off Porthcawl, Swansea Bay and Mumbles, Milford Haven, Cardigan, Pwllheli and the Menai Strait. All of these fisheries declined during the early 1900\u0027s due to intense exploitation, possibly combined with disease and poor recruitment. Significant oyster beds are now restricted to Milford Haven, Swansea Bay and Porthcawl, although at a much reduced scale. The remaining oyster populations in Wales are under threat through increased fishery effort in the existing oyster fishery and from diversification of fishing activities in the fisheries. Dredging activity used in harvesting sublittoral oysters causes physical damage to the seabed and associated marine biodiversity. Oyster beds in Milford Haven occur in the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and form part of the features for which the SAC is designated.    The present work reports on the morphometric characteristics, the age structure, growth and settlement of Ostrea edulis populations from seven sites in Milford Haven in Pembrokeshire (Wales, UK). Information gained from the project will contribute to an ecological understanding of native oyster beds and provide management advice for the remaining native oyster beds in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:37:09.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17526\tNRW_DS101779\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.238Z\t687\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Survey of Native Oyster Beds (Ostrea edulis) in Wales\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031373739\t[A] Data is archived as Project No. 285, media no\u0027s 104,111 and 112 (File paths: K:\\Archive\\Media 101-200\\Media 104 etc.) [B] Marine Recorder Survey (MRCCW30000000032)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe native flat oyster Ostrea edulis is a UK and Welsh Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species, for which a UK action plan exists. Native oysters have a long history of over-exploitation in Wales and elsewhere in the UK. Oyster fisheries around Wales declined during the early 1900\u0027s due to intense exploitation, possibly combined with disease and poor recruitment. Significant oyster beds are now restricted to only a few locations. The remaining oyster population in Wales are under threat through increased fishery effort in the existing oyster fishery and from diversification of fishing activities in the fisheries.  The purpose of this data capture was to gather data on the distribution and abundance of oysters in locations known to currently support native oyster beds; and, to collect samples of oysters for age analysis and to gather data on the marine biodiversity associated with oyster beds. This dataset also helps towards a further understanding of the ecology of native oysters as well as informing management decisions (part of the UK and Welsh Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-03T17:51:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17540\tNRW_DS101780\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.730Z\t316\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA study of the Milford Haven maerl bed (2005)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031373830\tData is archived under project no.145, media no.\u0027s 471-478 Paper MNCR forms stored in off-site storage (Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Biodiversity Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tMaerl beds are a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitat and are also included in the biodiversity duty of public bodies introduced by the NERC Act 2006. Maerl is a collective term for several species of red seaweed, with hard, chalky skeletons. It is rock hard and, unlike other seaweeds, it grows as unattached rounded nodules or short, branched shapes on the seabed. Maerl beds are an important habitat for many different types of marine life, which live amongst or are attached to the surface of Maerl, or burrow in the coarse gravel of dead maerl beneath the top living layer. Maerl beds can be of importance to sustainable fisheries, providing nursery grounds for commercial species of fish and shellfish. In Milford Haven the maerl species Phymatolithon calcareum forms the only living maerl bed in Wales. P. calcareum is listed in the EC Habitats Directive Annex Vb. The aim of this project was to collate high quality images of the Milford Haven maerl bed to create a base line for long term monitoring for Biodiversity Action Plan reporting. There is high confidence in this dataset as the surveyors are amongst the most experienced marine biologists in the UK.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T10:28:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17521\tNRW_DS101782\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.140Z\t1238\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Wales\tThird parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031373832\tSpecies data stored in Recorder 6 all surveys are tagged as being Phase 2 Woodland Survey. a)  Woodland Ecologist\u0027s G:drive  b)  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\EIT\\Staff\\Cyprian Payne\\2009\\P2only.zipc)  M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\phase_2\\Woodland    c)  Spatial data available to internal NRW staff on the corporate spatial data store.  GIS layers are stored on the X Drive: X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Woodland  d) copy of the scanned paper maps are available on legacy CCW M drive at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Woodland\\Raster Data  Species data is also stored in Recorder 6 under the Phase 2 Woodland Survey Tag.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas The species part of the dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThe primary aim of the project was to complete woodland community descriptions of all Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSls) in Wales that have semi-natural woodland as a qualifying feature. There was also a need for some survey of woodland on SSSls where woodland is not the outright qualifying feature and some areas of non-SSSI woodland (primarily pSSSI and areas of woodland adjacent to existing SSSls). Sites and areas of woodland to be included in the survey were selected by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). National Vegetation Classification (NVC) field survey methodology followed procedures.    This dataset contains detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 800 woodlands of conservation interest in Wales, predominantly SSSI and pSSSI woodlands.   The purpose of this data capture was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities, to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland SSSI series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites. Phase 2 woodland surveys have been carried out periodically since the late 1980s but a concentrated period of survey on SSSI and pSSSI woodland occurred from 1995 and 2000.  Data was collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by CCW Knowledge Management Group, corrections made by contractors, validated again by CCW Knowledge Management Group, corrections made in-house in conjunction with Woodland Ecologist.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2000-09-11-2024-01-19\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6158/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_ph2_wood\t2025-12-01T16:34:57.271Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17534\tNRW_DS101803\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.369Z\t716\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater Image Library\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383033\tFreshwater Ecosystems Team Leader. N: and G: drive These images may have been transferred to the NRW Image Store\tA collection of freshwater images compiled and managed by Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader. If this data has been transferred to the image store the internal contact is Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis is a catalogue of images sourced from both slides and digital images. Approximately 75 percent are CCW, 10 percent are Head of the Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group or Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader within NRW. Approximately 15 percent of the images are sources externally from volunteers or organisation - information about this is held within an excel document within the image catalogue. These images were captured for numerous purposes including, training, interpretation, monitoring, and stock images for publication.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1994-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:17:59.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17532\tNRW_DS101806\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.252Z\t328\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring of King Scallop Pecten maximus\t© CNC/NRW 2009. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383036\tSkomer MNR, West Region Drive /shared documents/data/scallops\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  The scallop population in the Reserve is of conservation value because of its small size and its remoteness from any substantial population.  Scallops were collected commercially and recreationally in the area of the Reserve until 1990.  In 1990 the South Wales Sea Fisheries Committee (SWSFC) introduced a byelaw prohibiting scallop collecting by any means. The population of scallops needs to be monitored in order to assess the effectiveness of this byelaw. Surveys have been carried out since 1979 looking at scallop density and size.  The purpose of this data capture was to estimate the density of scallops within suitable habitats in the Reserve and to assess the age structure in the population.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:37:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17531\tNRW_DS101833\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.196Z\t867\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFluvial Audits of Welsh River Systems\tThere are use restrictions on this data. Please refer to individual metadata entries in the \u0027contains\u0027 field for specific restrictions. Fluvial Audit of the Upper Usk Tributaries (URI: 110182) Fluvial Audit of the River Dee (110183) Fluvial Audit of the Afon Tywi (URI: 111562) Fluvial audit of the River Wye and tributaries (URI: 111860)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383333\tArchived as Project 257, Media 951:   See the separate metadata entries Fluvial Audit of the Upper Usk Tributaries (URI: 110182)  Fluvial Audit of the River Dee (110183)  Fluvial Audit of the Afon Tywi (URI: 111562)  Fluvial audit of the River Wye and tributaries (URI: 111860).   Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media901to1000\\Media951\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW Sue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, Knowledge Strategy and Planning, NRW (Formerly Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, CCW)\tGeomorphological assessments that utilised historical information, contemporary field survey and scientific literature resources, have been undertaken for various Welsh river systems of high conservation importance including the Usk, Wye, Dee and Tywi. The purpose of these fluvial audits it to record the morphological features and to identify, and understand the geomorphological processes that influence and control the channel activity, and the morphological quality of the river systems.   Specific aims of each project include (but are not limited to): - Developing an understanding of the dynamic geomorphology of the key Welsh river systems. - Examining changes to the channel planform over time using historical maps and aerial photographs. - Investigating channel and catchment changes using historical records and consultation with Environment Agency (EA) staff, and to develop a time chart which identifies Potentially Destabilising Phenomena (PDP\u0027s). - Identifying the major geomorphological features and processes occurring by undertaking a field survey of present erosion, deposition, bank protection, flow types and sediment sources. - Identifying the key areas of channel instability (geomorphological activity) and determine the possible factors affecting this instability, in particular those contributing to any severe bank erosion evident. - Determining the presence and absence of geomorphological features associated with the SAC species habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-09-03T09:56:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17533\tNRW_DS101834\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.432Z\t584\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLlyn Tegid Hydroacoustic Surveys and Gwyniad Translocations 2004-2009\t© CNC/NRW 2008 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383334\t[A]  Data is Archived under Project Number: 29, 154, 194 ,226, 227, 200 Media Numbers 169, 468, 539, 595, 232, 952   [B]  Species Data is also stored in Recorder 6: CCW000800000000D Gwyniad Hydroacoustic and Gill netting survey of Llyn Arenig Fawr 2009 - CCW Science Report 904   [C]  Hardcopies are archived in CCW HQ fireproof safe: stack 05.\t\tThe gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) population of Llyn Tegid has been considered for a number of years to be threatened by deteriorating environmental conditions, especially those associated with eutrophication. This dataset contains the results of a project established in 2004 to carry out translocation, hydroacoustic surveys and measurement of environmental variables. The purpose of this data capture was to consider the implications for the status of gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) and its habitat in Llyn Tegid. The objectives of the project include both conservation actions and further research, including the establishment of a refuge population in Llyn Arenig Fawr. Surveys were carried out annually from 2004 - 2009.  Following a first unsuccessful translocation attempt in 2003, three successful translocations have taken place- in early 2005, 2006 and 2007. A full fish population survey of the recipient site of Llyn Arenig Fawr was undertaken in 2009.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17537\tNRW_DS101837\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.442Z\t838\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLlyn Tegid automatic monitoring station\t© CNC/NRW, SNPA, Aberystwyth University Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383337\tStored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon on optical media.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage.  Archived as Project 195 (Media 540, 541, 596, 953 and 1212),  project 347 (media1324), project 446 (media1487), project 462 (media 1523)\tInternal Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tYear-to-year changes in the weather have a major effect on the water quality in many freshwater lakes. For example, periods of heavy rain transport more nutrients from the surrounding land while periods of warm, calm weather during the summer promote the growth of cyanobacteria. Llyn Tegid, in Snowdonia, is a deep lake that periodically develops short-lived blooms of cyanobacteria. An automatic station was installed in Llyn Tegid in October 2006 to monitor its response to extreme variations in the weather. This station is fitted with a range of meteorological sensors, a chain of water temperature sensors to record the mixing characteristics of the lake and optical sensors to monitor the spatial distribution of the algae. This dataset includes raw data on parameters recorded by the station at hourly intervals such as conductivity, barometric pressure, mean chlorophyll a, temperature at varying depths, wind speed and direction, solar radiation etc. The reports include an analysis of this data and a review of technical performance of the station. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the response of Llyn Tegid to variations in the weather and support more detailed studies on the climatic sensitivity of the lake.\tSnowdonia National Park Authority-University of Wales, Aberystwyth-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:42.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17539\tNRW_DS101839\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.684Z\t848\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGeological Conservation Review (GCR) Site Management Reports\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383339\tThis data is stored on the DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Geodiversity/gcr/Data%20%20Statistics/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Geodiversity/gcr/SitePages/Home.aspx  reports are held here: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/kdim/rl/CCW%20GCR%20Site%20Management%20Reports/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx\tRaymond Roberts, North Region Earth Science Officer, CCW\tMulti-page reports produced for each Geological Conservation Review (GCR) site. The reports contain geological and conservation information, comprehensive site photography and local information. The Geological Conservation Review looked at identifying geological sites across the UK, that make up a unique contribution to Britain’s Earth Heritage. The Geological Conservation Review started in 1997 when a more systematic approach to site selection occurred. The objective of the review was to identify those sites needed to show all the key scientific elements of the Earth heritage of Britain.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:23.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17525\tNRW_DS101840\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.195Z\t609\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring of Nudibranch Species\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383430\t[A] Skomer MNR, West Region Drive /shared documents/data/biological data/nudibranch ,   [B] Species data in Marine Recorder (survey numbers MRCCW30000000052,MRCCW30000000055, MRCCW3000000005C)  NB this data set appears to have been extended to include nudibranch data from 2014 and 2018. There are surveys in Marine Recorder. Metadata may need updating to reflect this.\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Nudibranchs are predators on a wide range of epibenthic species. Most are seasonal and reliant on their prey for food, shelter and a place to lay their eggs. Being near the top of the food chain they can act as an indicator of the health of the communities they rely on.  Nudibranchs are a feature of the Skomer marine Nature Reserve (MNR) for which species diversity and the presence of reare or scarce species are attributes used to assess conservation status.  As top predators they can act as an indicator of the health of the communities they rely on.   Between 1975 and 1991 surveys of nudibranch diversity in the Skomer MNR were conducted as a series of identification courses/workshops at Dale Fort Field Centre and these showed species diversity to be high.  61 species of nudibranchs were recorded (Bunker Picton and Morrow 1993).  The Skomer MNR team complete nudibranch species surveys every four years, these have been completed in 2002, 2006 and 2010. The Skomer MNRs nudibranch population have been identified as being rich and diverse with over 62 species including one nationally rare species and four nationally scarce species. The purposes for this data capture were to: - to produce a species list for the entire Reserve on a 4 yearly basis.  - to record a minimum of 16 common species annually in the Skomer MNR from a check list.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-06-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:38:25.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17524\tNRW_DS101841\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.235Z\t377\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Echinus esculentus and other echinoderm species surveys\t© CNC/NRW 2008 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383431\tSkomer MNR, West Region Drive /shared documents/data/biological data/urchins.   This dataset is updated 4-yearly.  2003 data also available in Marine Recorder survey number MRCCW16800000005.\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ).  The purpose of this data capture was to assess the abundance of an important habitat grazer. The dataset includes data related to the abundance data for common sea urchin Echinus esculantus, Spiny starfish Martasterias glacialis, Seven armed starfish Luidia ciliaris and Common sunstar Crossaster papposus.  The data also contains the density of each species and depth band at which recorded and the presence of \\bald urchin disease\\\".  From 2007 the data includes a qualitative assessment of the larvae in plankton samples.\"\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:39:07.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17530\tNRW_DS101842\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.208Z\t360\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Condition Monitoring of Eunicella verrucosa\t© CNC/NRW 2008 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383432\tSkomer MNR office, V drive/ shared documents/ data/ biological/seafans.    Data also available in HQ Marine Recorder (survey number MRCCW16800000004)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Eunicella verrucosa is a feature of the marine nature reserve, it is at the edge of it\u0027s distribution range and is a potential indicator of climate change. Eunicella verrucosa colonies around the Skomer MNR have been photographed and assessed for condition since 1992. Colonies are revisited every year and assessed for: Presence, damage, epi-foulants, entanglement, growth, necrotic tissue and the presence of Tritonia nilsodherni and Simnia patula. Eunicella verrucosa is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species and information is used for BAP reporting.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:39:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17522\tNRW_DS101843\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.131Z\t713\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring of Red Sea Fingers Alcyonium glomeratum\t© CNC/NRW 2006. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383433\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/alglom ,  Images - Dell1590 C/docs and settings/Monitoring/alglom\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the red sea fingers Alcyonium glomeratum colony area and to look for damage and disease. Alcyonium glomeratum (red sea fingers) is a Lusitanian species near to its northern limit of distribution. Colonies are long-lived and possible indicators of climate change. Several colonies have been monitored since 1982. Some colonies are very stable over time and others are variable.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:40:32.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17529\tNRW_DS101844\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.248Z\t563\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Territorial Fish Species Monitoring\t© CNC/NRW 2008. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383434\tSkomer MNR office, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological/FISH.     Dataset is updated every 5 years  2005 and 2009 data also held in Marine Recorder (surveys MRCCW16800000002, MRMCS00500000036)  Data for 2013 held by Assistant Marine Conservation Officer, Pembrokeshire Team.\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Monitoring of territorial fish is part of an on-going survey was carried out in the Skomer Marine Conservation Zone using volunteer divers to assess the abundance of territorial fish species and the habitat characteristics of these populated areas.  The survey methods were developed through trials in 1997 and the first survey was completed in 2001 this was affected by heavy plankton bloom and therefore repeat survey was completed in 2002.  In 2005 survey reviewed the methods in order to allow improved statistical analysis but allowed comparison of the 2002 data.  The methods were also developed to be suitable for volunteer dive teams.  Seabed and Habitat data were collected for each site using Seasearch methods to support the fish data. The fish data entered is an average for two habitats (10m and 15m) and represents the number of fish per 90 m2.  In 2009 Habitat Descriptions were created using Phase 1 SeaSearch habitat description methods. These surveys were done by NRW and volunteers as in previous year. 2013 data is currently held by the Skomer Island Marine Team (Assistant Marine Conservation Officer, Pembrokeshire Team) and is to be entered into Marine Recorder.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:41:12.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17523\tNRW_DS101852\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.162Z\t355\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring of Parazoanthus axinellae\t© CNC/NRW 2006 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383532\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/parazoo\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Internal Custodian: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. The population of Parazoanthus axinellae (yellow trumpet anemone) is an important feature of Skomer MNR.  P. axinellae is a Lusitanian (south-western) species near to the edge of its range and may act as an indicator of climatic change. Colonies have been photographed from fixed locations since 2001 and colony growth has been monitored. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor P. axinellae colonies for changes in polyp density and colony area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:41:52.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17527\tNRW_DS101853\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.238Z\t330\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Ross Coral Pentapora foliacea\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383533\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/pentapora ,  Images - dell1590 C/doc \u0026amp; settings/MONITORING/pentapora   CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000057)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Colonies of the bryozoan Pentapora foliacea are fragile structures thought to be moderately slow growing, and long lived. They are important microhabitats for mobile species and are regarded as useful indicators of anthropogenic activity such as mobile fishing gear and anchoring. Stereo photographs of colonies have been taken from 1984. Image analysis methods have been tested and currently a morphological class system is used to classify and monitor the colonies. Alongside the basic monitoring various research projects are in progress looking at; the genetics of the species, Anti-microbial zone assays and palaeoclimate using two different techniques: Variation in zooid size and isotopic signature of the carbonate skeleton. The purpose of this data capture was to: - monitor the numbers and growth rate of Ross coral colonies.   - monitor the amount of damage occurring to the colonies.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:42:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17519\tNRW_DS101856\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.701Z\t659\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Subtidal Algal Assemblages\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383536\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/algae.    2005 Data also available in HQ Marine Recorder (Survey number MRCCW16800000008)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. Skomer MNR\u0027s algal communities have been identified as being rich and diverse with 241 species of red, green and brown algae recorded. This represents 34% of the British marine flora and 21% of North Atlantic marine flora including two nationally scarce species, five near their limit of distribution, four species with specialised habitat preferences and five deep water algal species. Skomer MNR has been identified as a Criteria B European IPA (important plant area) for marine algae. The area has a wide range of habitats, including excellent examples of algal communities on bedrock, boulders and cobbles. It has a very high diversity of algae with over 240 species recorded. Rare or threatened species include: Atractophora hypnoides, Sphacelaria mirabilis, Hydrolithon cruciatum and Hinksia ovata. In situ recording and collection is used to survey the algae at various habitats around the Reserve. A herbarium is kept for all notable species found. Methods have varied from year to year. The whole project was reviewed in 2007. There were numerous aims for this project and data capture.  1. To monitor temporal change in algal communities; 2. To monitor algal species of recognised importance; 3. To investigate species diversity using taxonomic distinctness analysis; 4. To establish a monitoring protocol for the future that is both achievable and worthwhile.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:43:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17520\tNRW_DS101857\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.745Z\t431\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring of sponge assemblages\t© CNC/NRW 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383537\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/sponges ,  Digital images - Dell1590 C/Documents and settings/monitoring/sponges ,   Species data held in Marine Recorder Surveys (MRCCW30900000005 / MRCCW16600000002)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  The sponge communities at Skomer MNR have been identified as rich and diverse with over 80 species. Six are nationally scarce species and eight near the limit of their distribution. Sponges are filter feeders and therefore reliant on water quality which makes them susceptible to changes in sediment deposition. They are therefore useful biotic indicators of changes in suspended sediment and surface sedimentation rates, the cause of which might include dredge spoil dumping. The purpose of this data capture was to: - monitor the sponge assemblages in the MNR;  - identify natural and anthropogenic fluctuations,  - identify the presence of rare, scarce and edge of range species in the MNR.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:43:41.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17515\tNRW_DS101860\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.331Z\t474\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Sediment infauna community data\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383630\tSkomer MNR. West Region drive shared document/data/biological/sediment/infauna.     Data updated every 4 years    Data is stored in NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (survey numbers MRCCW10000000058, MRCCW10000000080, MRCCW10000000095, MRCCW30900000002, MRCCW30900000003, MRCCW3000000005F)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Species abundance data from grab samples within Skomer MNR from 1993 onwards. Complimented by sediment grain size analysis and various oil content measures. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the sediment infauna community within Skomer Marine Nature Reserve.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-11-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:44:24.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17518\tNRW_DS101862\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.549Z\t606\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Sediment Surface Epifauna Community Surveys\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383632\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/sediment/ epifauna.    2001 data also available in Marine Recorder (MRCCW16800000009).   2004 data is also available in Marine Recorder (MRCCW30900000004).\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference data team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  The aim of this data capture was to describe and monitor the sediment surface community within the Skomer Marine Reserve. The project was an investigation into the sediment surface communities from two sites at Skomer MNR.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:45:10.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17516\tNRW_DS101864\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.485Z\t620\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Littoral communities Meso scale\t© CNC/NRW 2009. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383634\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared docs/data/biological/littoral ,   Images - Dell1590 C/docs and settings/MONITORING/littoral.  MarClim data is also available on the NBN Gateway.\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. Investigation into the intertidal littoral communities around Skomer MNR.  The purpose of this data capture was assess the \"health\" and changes occurring in the intertidal communities and investigating climate change effects.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:45:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17517\tNRW_DS101866\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.484Z\t333\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Stereo photographic monitoring of rocky reef communities\t© CNC/NRW 2009 Data collected from 1990 onwards. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source. Data collected from 1982 - 1990. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383636\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/stereo , Skomer MNR office fire proof cabinet 1 draw 4 (bottom)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Investigating rocky reef communities at Skomer MNR. Very little is known about the community dynamics on sublittoral habitats. The epibenthic communities are one of the most abundant and important features in the Reserve. In order to make decisions about the conservation status of these communities it is vital to have some understanding of how these communities change over time. Therefore the aim of the stereo photographic monitoring is to monitor how species and communities vary over time. Possible measurements include species richness / diversity, growth rates, longevity and competitive interactions over time.\tBullimore-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:46:25.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17513\tNRW_DS101867\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.160Z\t805\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Meteorological Records Wooltack Point\t© CNC/NRW 2008. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383637\tData also available for download through the following link; http://environmental-change.ccw.gov.uk/?page_id\u003d513 Data is held locally at the SKomer Office (DELL 709) A data backup is held one the Skomer mnr West Region drive/shared docs/data/weather\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Weather data collected from the Coastguard lookout station on Wooltack point, Pembrokeshire from May 1993 onwards. The continuous weather data can be used for monitoring climate change and factors effecting species within the MNR.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-04-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T13:25:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17512\tNRW_DS101869\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.134Z\t984\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSand dune survey of Great Britain\t© JNCC/SNH 1990.  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383639\tSpreadsheet stored on personal drive of NRW\u0027s Coastal Ecologist (Peter Rhind, retired). However, the data is on the X: drive as a GIS layer, so the \u0027spreadsheet\u0027 could be derived from that layer:- X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Sand Dunes.  JNCC report available in CCW Ffynnon: NRW-14-059263\tInternal Contact: Julie Creer, Lead Specialist Advisor – Coastal Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tSand dunes are one of a series of terrestrial habitats that in Britain are almost entirely restricted to the coast. The others are saltmarshes, maritime cliffs and grasslands, vegetated shingle and strandlines. This dataset is made up of the results from vegetation surveys covering 49 sand dune systems in Wales. The specific objectives of the sand dune survey are:  - To review existing knowledge of British dune vegetation; - To compile an inventory of the range and extent of dune vegetation throughout Britain; - To allow the national and regional importance of each individual site to be assessed; - To provide vegetation maps and descriptions for each site in sufficient detail to support site specific casework and conservation management, and to act as a baseline for future monitoring.\tScottish Natural Heritage (SNH)-Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)\t1995-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:00:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17514\tNRW_DS101870\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.098Z\t307\tValid\tEditable\tMaritime Cliff and Slope Inventory for Wales (2005)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2005. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383730\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Maritime_Cliff_and_Slope.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis, Julie Creer, Coastal Ecologist\tMaritime cliff and slopes are a feature of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) listed under annex 1 of the EC Habitats Directive, form part of some Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) across Wales, and are a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitat.   This habitat encompasses a variety of vegetation types, ranging from saxicolous lichen communities in the spray zone through to sparsely vegetated cliff-faces and ledges to coastal grassland, heath and scrub on the deeper soils of the cliff-top.   This is a spatial datasets which outlines 1 km squares where maritime cliff or slope habitat is found across Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T16:43:47.766Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17511\tNRW_DS101871\t2024-05-28T12:25:24.980Z\t621\tValid\tEditable\tCollated Saltmarsh Survey of Wales (1994 - 2004) (archived data)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2004. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383731\tData is archived under Project No. 252, media No.\u0027s 905 and 1099\tInternal Custodian: Coastal Ecologist, NRW\tThis, now static, dataset was collected to monitoring the progress of saltmarsh development across Wales, between 1994 and 2002.   The dataset comprises of a collation of biological data recorded across different saltmarsh environments.   This dataset is now archived, as more up-to-date information is available about saltmarsh habitats across Wales. However this still forms an important baseline dataset in which to monitor the health and species richness across saltmarshes and overtime. This will help inform conservation management requirements for the important saltmarsh habitats.   The more up-to-date surveys can be found in the following metadata entry: 114725 Priority Marine Habitats of Wales (coastal saltmarsh).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T16:56:07.304Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17551\tNRW_DS101896\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.444Z\t381\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Muddy gravels (LMX.Psyllid) in Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t© CCGC/CCW 2004. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and CCW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383936\tData is archived under project No. 199, media 362\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tMuddy gravels consist of shores of mixed sediments ranging from muds with gravel and sand components to mixed sediments with pebbles, gravels, sands and mud in even proportions. Mixed sediments which are predominantly muddy tend to support infauna similar to mud and sandy mud shores.  Although sheltered muddy gravel habitats occur principally in estuaries, rias and sea lochs in areas protected from wave action and strong tidal streams, the environmental conditions of the Menai Strait are unusual, in that the water is relatively turbid and although the area is largely sheltered from wave action it experiences extremely strong tidal currents, reaching up to 8 knots in places during spring tides. The muddy gravel LMX.Psyllid biotope has been mapped along the west side of the Menai Straits, with the mixed sediment shores between Beaumaris and Lleiniog being highly productive, rich in animal and plant species. The muddy gravel LMX.Psyllid contains an unusually high number of syllid polychaetes and form a nationally important biotope, uncommon within the UK. The aim of this data capture was to identify the boundary of muddy gravel and muddy gravel with canopy of tideswept Fucus serratus.  - To determine what exactly constitutes the boundary of muddy gravel - To measure the extent of the muddy gravel biotope - To determine a physical damage index for muddy gravels - To take samples at old and recently impacted areas of muddy gravel.  - To characterise the infaunal community and identify the degree and type of change using previous data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T13:26:12.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17553\tNRW_DS101897\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.527Z\t349\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales Wetland Survey 1977-1982\t© CNC/NRW Redacted data (personal data removed) may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383937\tAlso see entry for FenBase (holds the quadrat/species data).  Metadata entry: 24288 * Field maps, Quadrat cards and slides stored in MyF filing cabinet (see Kathryn Birch) * Scanned versions of field maps in DMS:  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/ps/WLPS19771982/Wales%20Wetland%20Survey%201977-1982%20GIS%20Table%20and%20Document%20Tracker.xlsx?d\u003dw890221785d3940d6ad3b6958d613eda5\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1 * Quadrat species data held in FenBase but extraction to accessible system expected by Autumn 2023 * Tracker in DMS:: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/ps/WLPS19771982/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx * GIS table:  X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales\\Wales Lowland Peatland Survey Sites 1977-1987.lyr\tKathryn Birch Or Peter Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  (Formerly, Peter Jones as Peatland Ecologist)\tThis project was a major survey of wetlands in Wales. It was a joint venture between NCC Wales HQ and CST to describe, classify and evaluate the lowland wetlands of Wales on a floristic basis. In all, around 270 wetland sites were surveyed across Wales. The dataset is comprised of sketch maps, quadrat records, proformas  and a report (Ratcliffe \u0026amp; Hattey, 1982). In part, to inform Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) notification.  This dataset is being mobilised in 2023.  Only a redacted version of this data will be available under the Open Governance Licence and available for public use, see Limitation on Public Access and Use Constraints\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1982-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-22T16:35:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17555\tNRW_DS101913\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.587Z\t427\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCollation of Images for Use in the Promotion of Marine Biodiversity in Wales\t© CNC/NRW 2004. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393133\tNRW Pembroke Dock Office, Archived as Project No. 172, Media Nos 502-504. Report is located in the NRW Reports Archive on the DMS.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Team Leader, Pembroke Dock Office, NRW. ( Ex-CCW Aethne Cooke)  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tA significant remit of the Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) process is to raise awareness of biodiversity, both with the public and other stakeholders. Raised awareness leads to better understanding, appreciation and ultimately, the public taking greater responsibility for biodiversity. Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) partnerships in particular, are well placed for this role as they operate on a local scale, able to engage local communities, partners and visitors to an area. Inability to easily access high quality marine images is often a hindrance in this process. The purpose of this project was to collate high quality images of marine BAP habitats and species (both Welsh and local priorities), and also images of general Welsh marine biodiversity.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17554\tNRW_DS101925\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.526Z\t277\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales: Digital Photographs\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393235\tMost of the slides are in a filing cabinet in in the office of CCW\u0027s Grassland Ecologist. The digital photos are stored in the restricted access GRASSURV directory on the N:drive of Terrestrial Science Group. H:\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV\\PHASE II PHOTO AND IMAGE ARCHIVE The DVDs are stored in the Marine Fireproof Safe (project T007, Media T_Media007- Media T_Media019)\tStuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, TEG, NRW Jonathon Rothwell, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThis metadata entry relates to the photographs taken during the grassland surveys of Wales. This links with the The metadata entry \u0027Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Photo _ GIS Dataset\u0027 which details a GIS dataset containing notes about the photo locations.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:08:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17552\tNRW_DS101928\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.528Z\t335\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales: Site packs paper maps and field notes\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393238\tMost of the files are kept in filing cabinets in the office of CCW\u0027s Grassland Ecologist - Stuart Smith, who refers to these on a daily basis.  The electronic copies are held on the restricted access GRASSURV directory on the N:drive of Terrestrial Science Group. Copies of the site packs have also been distributed to the relevant regional offices.  The paper copies are held together in TSG files. The site files and other info on Ffynnon has not been superseded by the Recorder database as the latter only contains the quadrat data and site logistical info (e.g. not maps, descriptions, evaluations). Some files relating to sites in south Glamorgan and Gwent are kept in CCW\u0027s Cardiff office.\tData Custodian: Stuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains site packs from over 1070 sites surveyed or resurveyed during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales 1987-2004 and since. The site packs contain, for each site, site summary sheets (general site information and plant community area summations), site descriptions, vegetation maps and plant community (quadrat and stand) data, recorded using the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). The data was collected to provide detailed information for each site, to supplement published summary information of the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:08:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17547\tNRW_DS101931\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.146Z\t367\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Infauna of the Usk and Wye and Severn Estuaries 2004\t© CNC/NRW 2004 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393331\tThe data are archived as Project No 406, Media No 1424.  A copy is also stored at H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Severn\\2004 Usk and Wye Infauna 2004\tIntertidal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tAs part of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) intertidal Phase 1 mapping survey. This collection of sediment samples were completed to aid the identification of the biotope of the mud sediments in the two sheltered estuaries of the Usk and Wye, and the more dynamic environment of the Severn estuary.  The data were used to inform the biotope mapping and identification of these estuaries for the CCW intertidal phase 1 mapping survey.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-11-11\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:48:05.071Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17549\tNRW_DS101965\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.181Z\t477\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Designated Site Assessment Documents and Data\t© CNC/NRW 2010. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropraite NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393635\tThese reports and data are archived as Project No 270, Media No 569 and 981\tInternal Custodian; Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team\tData derived from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) intertidal Phase 1 mapping survey have been used to evaluate the relative value of each survey site according to the JNCC intertidal Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) guidelines. The purpose of this data capture was to make recommendations and objectively evaluate the nature conservation value of stretches of the intertidal zone throughout Wales, in order to create new designations for SSSI and provide evidence and identify additional of intertidal features of interest to identify features to existing SSSIs. This in turn helps to develop Site Management Statements and Management Plans for existing SSSI and Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:48:26.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17550\tNRW_DS101977\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.250Z\t798\tValid\tEditable\tHABMAP Predictive Habitat Maps of the Irish Sea\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders, 2010. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393737\tData is archived as project number 274, media number 1014 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1001to1100).   Available on legacy drive: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\HABMAP\\Maps2).   Paper report available from NRW library (see library catalogue/related title field above).    Also available as PDF files on legacy drive: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\HABMAP\\Interreg Report3) HABMAP final report as above;  tif registered files on legacy shared drive: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Scienc\\MFSG\\Users\\HABMAP4).  MapInfo files Intended for CCW internal use only: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Users\\HABMAP\\ .   Image files available to download from HABMAP website (see online resource below).\tInternal Contact: Karen Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe HABMAP project was set up in response to the need for better spatial awareness of habitat distributions in the Southern Irish Sea. This work produced habitat maps of the seabed using novel predictive modelling techniques. Two main components of the project were the mapping of seabed habitats using data collation and predictive modelling, and the collection of new survey data. This was used to increase the knowledge of Irish Sea habitats and validate the maps produced by predictive modelling. There have be subsequent project developed from this HABMAP project, which includes the re-modelling and larger project area is being covered to include all Welsh waters. The purpose of this data capture is to provide seabed habitat maps that could be used for conservation and management. Project outputs might be used in strategic planning, decision making for offshore developments, sensitivity mapping and mapping essential fish habitats. Confidence of predicted biotope maps was assessed and rated as explained on individual maps produced. The area covered in this project related to the southern Irish Sea.\tCardiff University-National Museums and Galleries of Wales-Marine Institute-University College Cork-Trinity College Dublin-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:49:31.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17548\tNRW_DS101978\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.151Z\t995\tValid\tEditable\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey of Selected Alder Woods on Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales (JA1)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393738\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Woodland\\PH2_Woodland_Sites.lyr  Archived as Project 209 Media Number 574\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of alder woods at eleven sites on ten Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) throughout Wales.    The aim of this survey was to provide a better understanding of the plant communities of Welsh alder woods, a rather neglected habitat.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1996-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-03T13:52:18.693Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n17544\tNRW_DS101979\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.109Z\t719\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Sites (Polygon)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393739\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Map Sites.lyr .    Archived as project 204, media 569.\tCustodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a spatial dataset showing the mapped location of sites. Includes information exported form the Phase 1 Intertidal database and includes selection unit, area, length, dates of visits, digitiser and biotope richness. The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005. All work has been undertaken by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team. Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:51:09.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17546\tNRW_DS101980\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.141Z\t873\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Marine Mud and Muddy Gravel Characterisation in the Menai Strait\t© CNC/NRW 2003. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393830\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 3, Media No 100  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media100\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSeveral rich marine mud and muddy gravel habitats were identified within the Menai Strait by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Marine Intertidal Phase 1 survey team and a sampling programme initiated in order to better characterise the intertidal communities of these habitats.  Marine mud and muddy gravel habitats are an important feature of several candidate Special Area of Conservation (SACs), including the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay cSACs. Species lists for these habitats within the Menai Strait have not been compiled since 1982. This report details sampling work and laboratory analysis undertaken in order to characterise the intertidal communities of these habitats. Several rich marine mud and muddy gravel habitats were identified within the Menai Strait by the CCW Marine Intertidal Phase 1 survey team and a sampling programme initiated in order to better characterise the intertidal communities of these habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:53:40.038Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17545\tNRW_DS101981\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.110Z\t281\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Sites (Line)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393831\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Sites.lyr .   Archived as project 204, media 569.\tInternal Custodian:  Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environment Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tThis is a spatial dataset comprising of data for the mean high water mark around the coast of Wales divided into line lengths reflecting the phase 1 survey sites. Information provided includes site ID, site name, name of Special Area of Conservation (SAC) the site is part of, selection unit and length. The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005.   All work has been undertaken by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team. Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T10:06:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17543\tNRW_DS101993\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.018Z\t560\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Underboulder Communities in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004/2005\t© CNC/NRW 2013. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393933\tThe report and data collected is archived as Project No 199, Media No 550 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  A specific feature of the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC are the underboulder communities, which are established in the extensive boulder areas within the SAC.  Rich underboulder communities have been recorded and mapped on the rocky intertidal areas of the Menai Strait and an area west of Llandudno.  Underboulder communities are also present where there are tide-swept communities that happen to lie on boulder habitats throughout the Menai Strait.  Sponge dominated communities have been recorded from boulders and cobbles that are nationally uncommon, hosting a rich faunal turf on tide-swept substrata. The associated report combines the surveys and findings undertaking during 2004 (Boyes and Allen, 2004) and 2005 (Mazik et al. 2005). The report combines both datasets and additional analaysis has been completed using both sets of data.  The purpose of this data capture was to  - Provide a reference condition on species present at a number of sites within the SAC, - Evaluate the variability between boulders and between sites and attempt to correlate the variability with physical factors,  - Identify the best methods for measuring the condition of the underboulder features in the SAC in the future and to evaluate the possible methods by which the impact of boulder turning can be assessed and monitored.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T13:28:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17542\tNRW_DS101994\t2024-05-28T12:26:24.885Z\t510\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Pectenogammarus planicrurus in Cardigan Bay and Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SACs) 2004\t© CNC/NRW 2009.There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393934\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 554 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\PectenogammarusH:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2004 IECS\\Pectenogammarus\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThere were numerous aims of this data capture. - To establish the presence and extent of Pectenogammarus planicrurus at two sites where it had previously been found.  - To establish whether the amphipod was restricted to a localised habitat at the sites or whether it was present along the entire length of the shoreline, identify and map its presence and extent along the surf zone, estimate its abundance.  - To take representative core samples for enumeration of P. planicrurus and Particle Size Analysis across the full extent of the biotope.  The results of a Pectenogammarus planicrurus monitoring programme carried out at two sites- Pwllheli, Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SACs) and Gilfach yr Halen, Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SACs). P. planicrurus had previously been found at these sites during Phase 1 surveys undertaken by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) so the data collected contributed to an investigation into their continuing existence. P. planicrurus is the only amphipod which is a permanent resident of shingle beaches and is found in the intertidal zone of clean shingle beaches, often in the lee of obstacles such as rocky outcrops and groynes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:55:09.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17541\tNRW_DS101995\t2024-05-28T12:26:24.817Z\t635\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria alveolata in Cardigan Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2004\t© CNC/NRW 2008. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393935\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 548 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2004 IECS\\Sabellaria\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact:  Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary, tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats).  Honeycomb worms build tubes from sand and shell fragments.  They are found on the lower part of rocky seashores but they also need a sand supply for tube-building. Dense congregations of Sabellaria alveolata tubes form large biogenic reef structures, which can be up to a metre thick and take the form of hummocks, sheets or more massive formations.  The purpose of this data capture was to identify specific survey locations and to measure the extent of Sabellaria alveolata reef along agreed transects, and assess the quality of Sabellaria alveolata reef present and map and make notes on any sightings of wireweed Sargassum muticum.   Confidence in this dataset is high as surveys were undertaken by experienced staff.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T13:34:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17585\tNRW_DS101997\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.809Z\t382\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rocky Reefs in Milford Haven, Pembrokeshire Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2005\t© CNC/NRW 2008. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393937\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 556 (K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters (Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2005 IECS\\BedrockQuadrats Marine Recorder file: MRCCW30200000018 Arcview layers available in Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Pembrokeshire Marine SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now forming part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. The aim of this data capture was to establish a set of permanent monitoring sites representative of the rocky shore intertidal habitats within the Haven Waterway in order to implement a long-term monitoring programme. The purpose of this data capture and project was to collect and present baseline data on the current status of six sites within the Milford Haven waterway for future comparisons of distribution and abundance of rocky shore organisms.  As part of the Pembrokeshire Marine SAC, monitoring of the Haven waterway is required to provide regular reporting on the condition of the Habitats Directive features within the SAC.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T13:35:30.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17572\tNRW_DS102001\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.421Z\t366\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Ruppia maritima in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Spatial Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t© CCGC/CCW 2008.There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and CCW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303031\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 551 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\Ruppia maritime\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Ruppia maritima is a species of aquatic plant known by the common names beaked tasselweed, widgeongrass, ditch-grass and tassel pondweed. Ruppia maritima (beaked tassle weed) is found in suitable habitats all around the British coast and grows in soft sediments in sheltered shallow coastal waters from full salinity to nearly fresh water.  The aim of this survey was to return to an area where Ruppia maritima had previously been identified during a Phase 1 survey carried out by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to determine whether or not the species is still present. Ruppia maritima has previously been identified by CCW in 1999 during a Phase 1 survey of the Afon Tywi. It was found at grid reference SN371122 in a pool on a track in the higher saltmarsh in the middle reaches of the river. This area was revisited and the results of the survey can be found in this dataset.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T14:33:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17582\tNRW_DS102003\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.751Z\t878\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Biotope SLR.FserX.T in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2004\t© CNC/NRW 2008. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303033\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 552 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities on optical media and is maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Fynnon. Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer,  Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  SLR.FserX.T is a tide-swept algal biotope typically found on sheltered lower shore boulder, cobble and pebbles on muddy sediments that are subject to enhanced tidal water movement. It is considered nationally uncommon. This dataset contains the presence/absence of the biotope SLR.FserX.T (Fucus serratus with sponges, ascidians and red seaweeds on tide-swept eulittoral mixed substrata).  The purpose of this data capture was to determine the presence/absence of SLR.FserX.T biotope previously identified at Whiteford Point during a CCW Phase 1 survey and to ascertain the condition of the habitat.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T14:47:17.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17584\tNRW_DS102004\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.748Z\t358\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Hydroid-dominated Rockpools in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t© CNC/NRW 2008 . There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303034\tThe report and data are archived as Project No 199, Media No 555 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 555) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW headquarters on optical media.   This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters (File path: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05  IECS\\LRH Rockpools.  Marine Recorder file:MRCCW30200000017, MRCCW31600000007  GIS data is here:- Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Carmarthen Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata Point of Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tHydroid rockpools (LR.H) are, by definition, species poor in comparison to other rockpool biotopes within the UK. LR.H rockpools are shallow mixed substrata pools characterised by the hydroids Obelia longissima and Kirchenpaueria pinnata, ephemeral seaweeds such as Ulva sp. and Enteromorpha spp. and the common periwinkle Littorina littorea. The casts of the lugworm Arenicola marina and the sand mason worm Lanice conchilega can be found in sandier pools. Hydroid rockpools are also associated with mussel beds (SLR.MytX), with Mytilus edulis present in the pools. Barnacles (Semibalanus balanoides and Elminius modestus) and the keel worm Pomatoceros sp. may also be attached to the harder substrata. On the south side of the Burry Inlet, hydroid rockpools have been identified at Whiteford Point during Phase 1 biotope surveys carried out by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in 2000. The rockpools are located at the tip of Whiteford Point, within the vicinity of a cast iron Victorian lighthouse, currently listed as an ancient monument. The purpose of this data capture was to map and assess the current extent and distribution of Hydroid rockpools within the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and evaluate the possible ephemeral nature of Hydroid rockpools by assessing the species richness of the rockpools at Whiteford Point.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T14:33:39.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17575\tNRW_DS102006\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.504Z\t380\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Ophelia bicornis in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2004\t© CNC/NRW 2008. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303036\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199,Media No 553 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Fynnon, on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Fynnon. H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\Ophelia bicornis\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tThe purpose of this data capture was to determine whether Ophelia bicornis found at two sites within the lower Loughor estuary during Phase 1 surveys carried out by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) were still present.  The polychaete Ophelia bicornis is known from only a few locations in the UK and is therefore of national importance. It is particularly vulnerable to changes in the granulometric composition of the sediment, pollution and trampling. The purpose of this data capture to determine whether O. bicornis found at two sites within the lower Loughor estuary during Phase 1 surveys carried out by CCW were still present.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T14:34:18.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17583\tNRW_DS102007\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.739Z\t400\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera noltii) Beds in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2004 and 2005\t© CNC/NRW 2009. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303037\tThe report and data collected is archived as Project No 199, Media No 546 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon, on optical media.   This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\MarineMonitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2004_05 IECS\tIntertidal Ecologist, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Seagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas, such as lagoons and channels, which are sheltered from significant wave action.  Zostera noltii generally occurs higher on the shore than the other two Zostera species found in the UK and occurs in low/variable salinity areas. Seagrass beds have important biological functions and are of conservation and economic importance; however human activity in the coastal zone (pollution, dredging boat mooring etc) can affect its distribution or even destroy the beds completely.  The work expressed here is both to trial the methodology and to establish a reference condition for the eelgrass beds at Foryd Bay and Traeth Lafan in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. The purpose of this data capture was to determine and map the extent, density and condition of Zostera noltii identified at Traeth Lafan and Foryd bay in previous years, record the presence of epiphytes and ephemeral algal species on the beds, record other conspicuous biota within the Zostera beds, record any damage or disease of the Zostera and develop and test an index of damage.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T14:34:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17577\tNRW_DS102008\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.533Z\t285\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria alveolata Reefs in Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) (2004/2005)\t© CNC/NRW 2008. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303038\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 548 (File Path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 548) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maesy Ffynnon on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maesy Ffynnon.(File paths:Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\Sabellaria2004 and Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\Sabellaria2005 Marine Recorder Survey numbers: MRCCW30200000010, MRCCW30200000019\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary, tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats). Dense congregations of S. alveolata tubes form large biogenic reef structures, which can be up to a metre thick and take the form of hummocks, sheets or more massive formations. This dataset combines the results of two monitoring events carried out on Sabellaria alveolata reef features within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC during 2004 and 2005.   In 2004 the purpose of this data capture was to identify specific survey locations, measure the extent of S. alveolata reef along agreed transects, assess the quality of S. alveolata reef present.  Map and make notes on any sightings of wireweed Sargassum muticum.   In 2005 the purpose of this data capture was more detailed monitoring of selected reef areas to thoroughly characterise small patches of reef and make an assessment of the impacts of anthropogenic disturbance on reef diversity.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T14:49:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17576\tNRW_DS102009\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.512Z\t434\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Mawddach Estuary Biotopes in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2004\t© CNC/NRW 2008. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303039\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 549 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon on optical media.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\MawddachEstuary\tCustodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. The Afon Mawddach (Mawddach estuary) is a small, drying, bar-built estuary, and it is one of the best examples of its type in the UK. With its mountainous catchment area, it has some of the lowest nutrient inputs of any estuary in Wales.  For this reason, the Mawddach estuary has been included in the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The Mawddach estuary is predominantly a sandy/sandy mud estuary and contains communities characteristic of these conditions. The Institute of Estuarine and Coastal Studies (IECS) as part of the 2004 intertidal monitoring programme undertook a Phase 1 biotope survey of the Mawddach estuary following previous monitoring at the site. Specific aims of the survey included to carry out a Phase 1 biotope survey along predefined transects noting infaunal species and sediment characteristics, to digitally record photographic evidence along each transect and to assign biotopes to each of the sampling locations. This in turn would allow information to be provided on conservation objectives of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC estuary feature.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T15:33:41.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17568\tNRW_DS102010\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.300Z\t811\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera marina) Beds in Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in 2004/2005\t© CNC/NRW 2008. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303130\tThe report and data is archived as Project No199 Media No 547 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\Z_marina2004H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\Z_marina2005\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Seagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas, such as lagoons and channels, which are sheltered from significant wave action. Eelgrass (Zostera marina), is considered to be scarce (present in 16-100 ten km squares) is protected through the EC Habitats Directive and is classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitat.  In particular, seagrass meadows are used as a refuge area by several juvenile commercially important fish species, and similarly provide an important food source for wildfowl such as Brent geese. As a result of their dense root networks, together with a certain degree of wave attenuation and the trapping of sediment particles which further encourages sedimentation, seagrass beds help to stabilise the underlying substratum, thereby playing an important role in terms of coastal defence and the reduction of coastal erosion (Davison and Hughes, 1998). As such, in addition to their biological function and conservation importance, they are also of considerable economic importance. Human activity in the coastal zone (pollution, dredging boat mooring etc) can affect seagrass distribution or even destroy the beds completely.  The purpose of this data capture was to map the current known intertidal extent of the Zostera biotope, develop a survey methodology to determine grass density, record damage to the beds caused by physical impacts, develop an index of disease and record species (epibiota) on the Zostera and adjacent sediment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T09:27:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17578\tNRW_DS102011\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.509Z\t310\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Muddy Gravel Community (IMX.VsenMtru) in the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t© CNC/NRW 2008. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303131\tThe report and data collected under CCW contract FC 73-02-291 is archived as Project No 199,Media No 557 (K:\\Archive\\Media 501-600\\Media 557) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon on optical media.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon. (New file path: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\MuddyGravels Marine Recorder file: MRCCW3030000000B\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tThe results of monitoring the extent and species richness of a distinct area of mixed sediment that lies east of Llanbedrog beach. The muddy gravel community is - IMX.VsenMtru (Venerupis senegalensis and Mya truncata in lower shore or infralittoral muddy gravel), and has a high biodiversity of epifauna, flora and infauna, due to the admixture of cobbles, pebbles, gravel, sand and mud. This provides many different habitat niches. The habitat is located within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) on the lower shore at Llanbedrog. In fully marine conditions on the lower shore, this habitat can be particularly species rich due to the complex nature of the substratum supporting a high diversity of infauna and epifauna, however many factors affect the habitat, physical disturbance, bait digging, organic enrichment, introduction of non-native species etc. The purpose of this data capture was to map the current extent of muddy gravel, define the character of the muddy gravel for in situ recognition, identify current known species composition and identify location for representative core sampling and Particle Size Analysis.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T09:28:31.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17580\tNRW_DS102026\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.539Z\t439\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Number and Extent of Sea Caves, Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t© CNC/NRW 2009. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303236\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 559 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\SeaCavesFriog\tCustodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tSubmerged or partially submerged sea caves are included as an Annex I type within the EU Habitats Directive (92/43/EEC). These caves may vary in size and exposure, and thus the communities associated with them vary considerably depending on the structure and extent of the cave system. Sea caves generally provide habitat for colonies of encrusting animal species, but may also have shade-tolerant algal communities at their mouth. Caves on the shore and in the shallow sublittoral zone are frequently subject to conditions of strong wave surge and tend to have floors of coarse sediment, cobbles and boulders. These materials are often highly mobile and scour the cave walls. Caves that are subject to strong wave surge are characterised by communities of mussels Mytilus edulis, barnacles Balanus crenatus, cushion sponges, encrusting bryozoans and colonial ascidians, depending on the degree of water movement and scour at particular points in the cave system. The UK has the most varied and extensive inshore sea caves on the Atlantic coast of Europe. In Wales, sea caves are important within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC and are therefore a qualifying feature, but not a primary reason for the site selection. As such there is a requirement to assess the number and extent of intertidal sea caves within this SAC. Further background information on the surveying of sea caves in Welsh SACs can be found in Bunker and Holt (2003).  Sea caves are important within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC and are a qualifying feature. As such there is a requirement to assess the number and extent of intertidal sea caves within this SAC.  The specific aims of the survey were to identify and map the location of sea caves along the coastline, use digital photography to record their extent and to measure the threat from in-fill by the railway company.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T09:29:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17574\tNRW_DS102032\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.492Z\t552\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of LR.Rkp.SwSed Pools in the Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t© CNC/NRW 2009. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303332\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 555 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW headquarters on optical media.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2004 IECS\\Cei Bach rockpools\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tRockpools are an important sub-feature of the \u0027Reef\u0027 feature of the Cardigan Bay SAC, in that they provide habitats of high species richness, often with species that are restricted to rockpool habitats only. The nature of rockpools, particularly those with sediment in the bottom, provides additional challenges to developing a repeatable and reliable method for monitoring of these features. This dataset contains the results of a survey investigating the extent, distribution and species richness of LR.Rkp.SwSed (sand scoured) rockpools at Llanina Point, Cei Bach. Rockpools are an important sub-feature of the \u0027Reef\u0027 feature of the Cardigan Bay SAC, in that they provide habitats of high species richness, often with species that are restricted to rockpool habitats only. Taxonomic identification carried out by experienced intertidal surveyors.  Positional accuracy using the dGPS is expected to be 1-5 metres. The aims for the survey of the rockpools at Llanina Point, Cei Bach were to: - map and assess the current extent and distribution of LR.Rkp.SwSed pools within this area - assess the species richness of these rockpools - record any presence of Japanese wireweed Sargassum muticum at the site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:09:31.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17573\tNRW_DS102033\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.500Z\t468\tValid\tEditable\tCarmarthen Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Intertidal Sediment Flats Monitoring 2005\t© CNC/NRW 2008. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303333\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 549 (File path:- K:\\Archive\\Media 501 to 600\\Media 549) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW headquarters on optical media.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. Z:\\Arcive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\LSIB transects Marine Recorder file (Survey No. MRCCW3030000001E) GIS data is here:- Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Carmarthen Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to gain an understanding of the variety and species composition of intertidal communities and their macro-scale distribution within the interest features of the Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The present study aims to revisit the area to undertake representative sampling to be compared to the phase 1 mapping survey undertaken in 1999 and 2001 in order to assess shifts and changes in intertidal habitats within the SAC. As such, the specific objectives of the current survey were therefore to:   - Undertake in-situ biotope recording along pre-defined transects to establish the extent and distribution of intertidal soft sediment biotopes  - Verify field observations with quantitative sampling of the intertidal habitats using core sampling for benthic infauna (and particle size analysis for sedimentary characterisation)  - Compare 2005 findings with baseline phase 1 mapping survey data with the view to detect changes or shifts in biotope distribution  - Assign the 2004 biotope classification.    Between 1999 and 2001, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) undertook a phase 1 Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) biotope mapping survey  of the intertidal areas of the Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The study identified over 80 littoral biotopes covering an area of approximately 130,000 hectares and included several important sub-features such as seagrass Zostera noltii beds, saltmarsh, piddocks in clay, hydroid rockpools, tide-swept algal biotopes, the presence of the polychaete worm Ophelia bicornis and the presence of the tasselweed Ruppia maritima. These habitats were resurveyed in 2004 in order to assess their current distribution and status. In 2005 a further survey was commissioned to assess the macro-scale distribution and community composition of the littoral biotopes in the SAC and determine any significant changes from the phase 1 biotope mosaic.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T16:59:27.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17570\tNRW_DS102034\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.380Z\t875\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCarmarthen Bay Intertidal Attributes and Monitoring Techniques - Saltmarsh Boundaries\t© CNC/NRW 2008. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303334\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 549.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\Saltmarsh Edge\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW\tThe Burry Inlet is the large estuary of the River Loughor, which separates the Gower Peninsula from the mainland. The Burry Inlet is a Special Protection Areas (SPA) and Ramsar site and its Atlantic and Mediterranean Saltmeadows have contributed to its designation as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The saltmarsh habitats here correspondingly determine the border of important mudflat and sandflats within the SAC. The area of saltmarsh within the Burry Inlet is regarded the most extensive site of such vegetation in Wales. In 1998, a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey was carried out by Prosser and Wallace (1999) for the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), to provide more detailed information on the distribution of key elements of saltmarsh within the estuary. They mapped an area of 1879 ha of saltmarsh vegetation. As part of the ongoing monitoring program for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), the Institute of Estuarine and Coastal Studies (IECS) were commissioned to carry out further monitoring at key sites in order to provide an understanding of the dynamics of the saltmarsh and mudflat/sandflat habitats. The purpose of the data capture was to map the seaward boundary of saltmarsh at Llanrhidian Marshes between Great Pill and Salthouse Point; map the seaward boundary of saltmarsh between Penrhyn Gwyn and Loughor Bridge and record positions of outlying Spartina anglica tufts at both locations.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:12:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17579\tNRW_DS102035\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.542Z\t663\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCarmarthen Bay Intertidal Attributes and Monitoring Techniques - Monitoring of Piddocks\t© CNC/NRW 2009. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303335\tThe data and report are archived as Project No 199, Media No 558.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\Piddocks\tData Custodian - Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata point of contact - Master Reference Data Team\tUnder the 1992 EC Habitats and Species Directive, the UK submitted a list of candidate  Special Areas of Conservation (cSACs) to the European Commission (EC). Each cSAC is  selected for one or more of the habitat types and species listed in Annexes I and II of the  Directive. This process was undertaken largely in 1995-1996. The piddock Pholas dactylus occurs in Britain from Kent along the south and south-west coasts including south Wales, Anglesey and Solway (Hill 2004). It has also been recorded from several sites on the east coasts of Yorkshire and Northumbria and southwest Ireland. The surface of the peat or clay bed is characterised by dense algal mats and pools of standing water (JNCC 2004). The presence of piddocks is instantly identified by holes in the substratum.  The aim of this data capture was to map the current known extent and density of boring molluscs, piddocks, across the biotope at Marros Sands and Whiteford Point, and record any mussel spat or seed on the biotope.  Piddocks are bivalves that bore clay and peat exposures. The survey work is largely a trial to determine the best means by which to record the extent of the exposures as well as the populations of piddocks. Sand levels are found to change over time, seasonally, annually and as a result of one off weather events (storms), and thus the areas of populated peat and clay also vary. A non-destructive means of assessing the piddock population is considered.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:13:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17581\tNRW_DS102036\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.743Z\t406\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera noltii) Beds in Carmarthen Bay 2004\t© CNC/NRW 2009. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303336\tThe report and data collected under CCW contract FC 73-02-291 is archived as Project No 199, Media No 546 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 501 to 600\\Media 546) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\Zostera Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Carmarthen Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe seagrass Zostera is a flowering plant typically found in sheltered areas with a muddy / sandy substratum. Zostera beds are of significant ecological importance in that they support a diverse community of infaunal and epibenthic species, algal and epiphytic species and provide refuge and feeding sites for fish and crustaceans (particularly juveniles). Furthermore, Zostera beds are an important food source for wildfowl (e.g. Brent geese). Zostera beds can be an important factor in coastal defence. The root networks stabilise the sediment, while the plants encourage sedimentation through sediment particle trapping and a certain degree of wave attenuation. This together with their role in supporting commercially valuable fish and crustacean species means that Zostera beds are also of economic importance. However human activity in the coastal zone (pollution, dredging boat mooring etc) can affect its distribution or even destroy the beds completely. The purpose of this data capture was to determine and map the extent, density and condition of Zostera noltii identified in previous years at Llanrhidian Sands and Penrhyn Gwyn in the Burry Inlet, record any damage or disease of the Zostera, record the presence of epiphytes and ephemeral algal species on the beds, record the presence of mussel crumble, and develop an index of damage.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:15:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17569\tNRW_DS102058\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.214Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) - River Chemistry Spatial Data\t© NRW and Enivronment Agency. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303538\tX:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\GQA Chem Network.lyr\tPlease contact Nick Davies ( Environment Agency)\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Environment Agency Wales and licensed to NRW for internal use only.   The dataset has been supplied by the Environment Agency Wales and contains River quality chemistry. GQA covering England \u0026amp; Wales displayed in varying thickness according to flow and colour according to (year 2000) and chemistry.\tEnvironment Agency Wales (EA Wales)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:14:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17566\tNRW_DS102082\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.169Z\t518\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSquirrel Collation for Wales\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303832\t[A]  Stored in Recorder 6. -  Squirrel Data - CCW - Central Wales, Report 475  CCW0003800000009  (See Metadata Entry:  102089) -  Squirrel Data - CCW - Central Wales, West area report (56)  CCW000380000000A   (See Metadata Entry:  102112) -  Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Aberhirnant \u0026amp; Lake Vyrnwy, Report 241  CCW0003800000005   (See Metadata Entry:  100791) -  Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Aberhirnant, Report 589  CCW0003800000007   (See Metadata Entry:  102086) -  Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Clocaenog  CCW000380000000B   (See Metadata Entry:  102121) -  Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Cwm Mynach, Report 540  CCW0003800000008   (See Metadata Entry: 102126)  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).\tLiz Halliwell, Mammal Ecologist Woodland and Farmland Mammals, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tRecords presence and absence of red and grey squirrels in Wales.  The collation was created to give a picture of squirrels distributions in Wales and to collect all relevant data in one place 1951 to 2006. The majority of the data is fully validated and verified.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17567\tNRW_DS102086\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.094Z\t414\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSquirrel survey of Aberhirnant Forest and woodland surrounding Lake Vyrnwy (2001)\t© CNC/NRW 2001 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303836\t[A] Species Data held in Recorder 6   - Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Aberhirnant, Report 589 CCW0003800000007\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tPresence or absence or red and grey squirrels in Aberhirnant Forest and the woodlands surrounding Lake Vyrnwy. This is a follow-up to the \"Squirrel survey of Aberhirnant Forest and woodland surrounding Lake Vyrnwy 1999 - 2000\". The purpose of this data capture is to inform work to remove grey squirrels in the area as well as gather further information about red squirrel populations. The survey locations were conifer plantations of Aberhirnant Forest and the catchment area of Lake Vyrnwy.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17571\tNRW_DS102089\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.381Z\t430\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSquirrels of Central Wales  (2001)\t© CNC/NRW 2001 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303839\t[A]  Recorder 6  Squirrel Data - CCW - Central Wales, Report 475  CCW0003800000009.\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tThis dataset contains the records of the presence or absence of red and grey squirrels and associated tree species in four forests, Tywi, Irfon, Crychan and Brechfa, of Central Wales.  The aim of this data capture was to determine the presence and distribution of grey and red squirrel and to make some preliminary conclusions on population viability and recommendations for conservation actions for red squirrel.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17561\tNRW_DS102112\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.486Z\t704\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSquirrels of Central Wales - Collation of historical records (1950 - 1999)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2000 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032313132\tSpecies data held in Recorder 6  Squirrel Data - CCW - Central Wales, West area report (56)   CCW000380000000A\tLiz Halliwell, Mammal ecologist, NRW.  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe red squirrel (Sciurus vulgaris) is Britain\u0027s only native squirrel and has experienced a severe decline in England and Wales over the last 50 years. This decline is linked to the expansion of its larger relative the grey squirrel S. carolinensis, which was introduced into Britain from North America in the late nineteenth century. The exact mechanisms of displacement are not fully understood, but it appears that grey squirrels are better able to utilise food resources in broadleaved woodland, with the result that red squirrels generally disappear from such habitats within about 15 years of the arrival of greys. This dataset is a collation of red squirrel sightings for central Wales.  The purpose of this data capture was to collate all records of red squirrels from central Wales in order to identify remaining areas of refuge for this species. The data was also collated to provide the initial recommendations for conservation actions. Brief habitat assessments of the remaining red squirrels sites were provided, based on forest area, tree species composition and age structure. Forest configuration, adjoining habitat and the presence of large−seeded broadleaves were also considered, to assess the potential threat from grey squirrels. The primary aim of the present study was to collate all recent (post−1990) red squirrel records from central Wales, to identify the remaining red squirrel refugia. Other key objectives were to provide a preliminary habitat assessment of the main sites where red squirrels still occur, and to make initial recommendations for conservation action, as appropriate.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2000-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17562\tNRW_DS102121\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.517Z\t371\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSquirrels of Clocaenog Conifer Plantation in North Wales (1992-2006)\t© CNC/NRW 2006 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032313231\t[A]  Species data held in Recorder 6 Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Clocaenog CCW000380000000B\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tPresence of red and grey squirrels in Clocaenog Forest, based on joint contract run by Forestry Commission Research Agency (FCRA) and Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) (1992-1999) , with subsequent records supplied by Sarah Cartmel (1999-2006).  The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the population ecology of red and grey squirrels and to identify their habitat requirements, and to investigate the relative populations of red and grey squirrels in parts of the forest and whether they are stable or changing.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:18:01.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17563\tNRW_DS102126\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.459Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSquirrel Survey of Cwm-Mynach (2001 - 2002)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032313236\t[A]  Species data held in Recorder 6 Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Cwm Mynach, Report 540 CCW0003800000008\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tPresence or absence or red and grey squirrels in Cwm-Mynach woods in the Snowdonia National Park.  Also a records of tree species from which the records were taken.  Red squirrels were reported in the Cwm-Mynach woods in 1998 and 1999 and the work was commissioned to establish whether there is a continued presence of red squirrels in this area. Survey took place on conifer plantations to the east of Mynydd Cwm-mynach within the Snowdonia National Park between November 2001 and January 2002. Squirrel activity was low at the time of survey, which may account for the presence of no red squirrels.  Further surveys at a different time of year could be considered.  Records where a positive identification could not be made have been tagged as unidentified.  In some cases the hair was only identified to family level.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:14.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17564\tNRW_DS102150\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.802Z\t634\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Biodiversity Action Plan Invertebrate Species Spatial Distribution (archive dataset)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release. It is also advised to use this data as reference only, and a newer more accurate dataset should be used e.g. Section 7 invertebrate species list.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032313530\tM:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Species\\all_wales\\Terrestrial\\BAPs\\invertebrates\tDr Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW Formerly: Adrian Fowles in CCW\tThis is an archived reference spatial dataset. It is advised that users gain a more up to date version of this datasets. UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority species were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The original list of UK BAP priority species was created between 1995 and 1999.  In 2007, however, a revised list was produced, following a 2-year review of UK BAP processes and priorities, which included a review of the priority species and habitats lists. The UK list of priority species, however, remains an important reference source and has been used to help draw up statutory lists of priority species in  England,  Scotland, Wales and  Northern Ireland, as required under Section 41 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities Act 2006 (England), Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2016, Section 2(4) of the Nature Conservation (Scotland) Act 2004, and Section 3(1) of the Wildlife and Natural Environment Act (Northern Ireland) 2011. Invertebrate BAP lists of priority species were produced with several important aspects such as the following: - most invertebrates have an annual life cycle and require their specific habitat conditions to be present each year;  - the ecological niches occupied by invertebrates can be highly specialised; - many insect species require different conditions for each stage of their life cycle (egg, larva, pupa, adult); and - many species have limited powers of dispersal.  This invertebrate BAP dataset was originally created in 2004 as a spatial layer for use within Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). This data should be used as reference only as it is out of date.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:48.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17560\tNRW_DS102203\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.434Z\t1374\tValid\tEditable\tWelsh Lakes Bathymetry Data\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032323033\tArchived in project 297 Media 1207 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1201to1300\\Media1207),  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Welsh lakes bathymetry.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Wales lakes bathymetry points.lyr  Tristan has a copy held on the Y drive Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Monitoring\\Tristan_Hatton_Ellis\\Corporate_data\\CCW_Final_Data\tInternal Custodian:   Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, NRW\tBathymetric data from Welsh lakes collated from documentary and electronic sources and re-formatted into a consistent and accessible form and structure.    The data includes transformed raw coordinate and depth data to bathymetric surface rasters at 0.5 m interval polygon shapefiles that can be placed into GIS software with volume/depth and area/depth data for the lakes at the same 0.5 m interval.    The data supplied collates existing bathymetric data to produce a series of GIS layers that display detailed bathymetric maps of Welsh lakes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-04-02\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6562/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_wales_lakes_bathy\t2025-12-03T10:46:52.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17565\tNRW_DS102205\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.773Z\t365\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of the Nature Conservation Value of Mussel Crumble in the Burry Inlet and the Effects of Dredging/Hand Gathering Operations\t© CNC/NRW 2002 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032323035\t[A] Report available from NRW library (Maes Y Ffynnon) and in Ffynnon.  [B] Data is available in Marine Recorder (survey number MRCCW30000000032) [C] Archived in project 307, media1222 (K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe aim of this data capture was to identify and quantify the epifauna and infauna of the mussel crumble beds, quantify the impacts of a simulated \u0027dredging\u0027 operation on the fauna of the beds and assess the stability of the beds in relation to dredging activities. Mussel Crumble are beds of mussels, barnacles, mussel and cockle shells, bound together by the mussel byssus thread. This dataset includes sediment particle size data, macrobenthic infaunal data tables, raw data matrices for the epifaunal quadrats. The purpose of this data capture was to identify and quantify the epifauna and infauna of the mussel crumble beds, quantify the impacts of a simulated \u0027dredging\u0027 operation on the fauna of the beds and assess the stability of the beds in relation to dredging activities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:15:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17558\tNRW_DS102209\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.379Z\t1115\tValid\tEditable\tLANDMAP Geological Landscape\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032323039\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap Download\\LandMap Geological Landscape (Boundaries).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap Download\\LandMap Geological Landscape (Classification).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap Download\\LandMap Geological Landscape (Evaluation).lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jill Bullen, Senior Landscape Specialist, Strategic Planning Group Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tLANDMAP is a unique national information system, devised by the former Countryside Council for Wales, allowing information about landscape in Wales to be collected and organised into a nationally consistent dataset. The LANDMAP database includes both objective and subjective information and is designed to enable landscape quality to be taken into account in decision making.    This is a geographic dataset showing geological landscape classification for Wales in the form of polygons (Aspect Areas) with large amounts of associated textual information, including General, Description, Evaluation, Recommendations, Tolerance to Change, Area Boundary, Evaluation and Bibliography.    Information is gathered by LANDMAP in order to inform sustainable decision making and to enable landscape to be taken into account during decision making processes. The Geological Landscape dataset is important as geology is often the strongest influence on the landscape, affecting drainage patterns, vegetation cover, the human environment and so on. These effects in turn determine the possibilities for agriculture, water sources, transport networks etc.  2003 to 2008 then updated at regular intervals.   This dataset maps geological, geomorphological and hydrological features of an area. The geographical and topographical characters of areas are noted as are significant active geological and geomorphological processes, and landscape - forming pedological processes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-07-22\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/2476/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/business-sectors/planning-and-development/evidence-to-inform-development-planning/landmap-the-welsh-landscape-baseline/?lang\u003den\t2025-12-01T17:02:45.299Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17559\tNRW_DS102271\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.330Z\t459\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales: Area of Search (AoS) Reports\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032323731\tDigital data and reports available on the restricted access GRASSURV directory on the H:\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV  of Terrestrial Science Group.  Paper versions in libraries and held by CCW HQ and district staff.  Reports are found on legacy CCW\u0027s document archive, Report Numbers in Fynnon as follows: NRWF-15-000303      Brecknock 09/27617      Dinefwr and Carmarthen (2 reports) 09/27627      Ceredigion 09/27641      Clwyd NRWF-15-000451      Gwent 09/27672      MId \u0026amp; South Glamorgan 09/89979      Pembrokeshire 09/89950      Radnor 09/89971      West Glamorgan NRWF-15-000271      Anglesey and West Gwynedd (2 reports)\tStuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW Formerly Jane Stevens, Habitat and Ecosystem Resource Analyst, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tAreas of Search (AoS) are geographical areas used in the selection of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs). There are a total of 12 AoS in Wales. For each AoS there are one or two phase 2 lowland grassland AoS reports. This dataset contains the series of these reports, each of which includes a detailed description of the survey undertaken within the area, survey methodology and conservation assessments of each survey site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:56:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17556\tNRW_DS102568\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.158Z\t646\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLakes ( 1:50,000 )\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032353638\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\hydrology\\Lakes\\os_lakes.TAB\t\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons. Lakes and large ponds in Wales digitised at 1:50,000. The primary objective of this project is to amalgamate  Quattro spreadsheet data extracted from the OS 1:50,000 by the Ordnance Survey and the OS Lakes GIS layer available as a standard GIS dataset to produce a comprehensive GIS Welsh Lakes layer for use within NRW.\tOrdnance Survey-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-04-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17605\tNRW_DS102597\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.078Z\t296\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGreat Crested Newt (Triturus cristatus) Sites in Wales\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2014  NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW / third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032353937\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\\Great Crested Newts.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\\Great Crested Newts font point.lyr\tMetadata and data custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons. Listing of great crested newt (Triturus cristatus) sites-extracted from recorder. The purpose of this data capture was to provide information on the distribution and abundance of an Annex II species. Records for the whole of Wales although the majority of records occur in Ynys Mon and on the borders of East Wales. The collection of records began in 1974 and is on-going.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:58.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17610\tNRW_DS107789\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.080Z\t347\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDormouse Licence Return Data (1992 - 2006)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. The IPR of individual records rests with the originator.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037373839\t[A] Species data is held in Recorder 6 Dormouse - CCW - HQ Terr - Licence Returns CCW000380000000E\tInternal Custodian: - TEG, NRW -  Master Reference Data Team, NRW  Metadata Contact:  - Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset comprises of a collation of records from Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) and Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW) licence returns for work carried out on dormouse sites across Wales. The collation was created to add to the dormouse distribution in Wales, to collect relevant data in once place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available. The dataset does not represent a systematic coverage of survey of Wales. The location of records is generally random, depending on the requirements of the licence holders.\tVarious including volunteer recorders\t2006-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17606\tNRW_DS107792\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.107Z\t290\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Shad Monitoring Data for Rivers Wye, Usk and Tywi (2006)\t© CNC/NRW 2006 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037373932\t[A]  Data is archived as project 377, media 1387.   [B]  Species data also available in Recorder 6 - Shad Monitoring, Usk, Wye \u0026amp; Tywi Catchments 2006 - CCW -  Report no.40 CCW0002800000005.\t\tThe purpose of this data capture was to monitoring of the Rivers Wye, Usk and Tywi for the presence of Shad.  These Rivers are designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) partly because they support internationally important populations of twait shad.  Allis Shad is also a feature of the sites and a secondary reason for selection as a SAC.  Records of other species of freshwater and estuarine fish are also recorded as part of this dataset. This provides a collation of existing knowledge, habitat and shad data, and provides evidence to design and implement a suitable juvenile monitoring programme for their condition assessment.  There were 5 survey sites along the River Usk, 5 sites along the River Wye and 5 sites along the River Tywi.  Further data is available in paper format in the associated report with historical records of eggs surveys extending back to 1980.  This historical data is collated from the Environment Agency.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17607\tNRW_DS107795\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.107Z\t422\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater Bullhead Survey Data in Wales\t© CNC/NRW and Environment Agency 2007 You may reproduce this dataset free of charge for non-commercial and internal business purposes in any format or medium, provided that you do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s and Environment Agency\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. To avoid using old information, we recommend that you obtain the latest version from the original source. NRW owns all intellectual property rights (including copyrights and database) to the collation and data originated by NRW. The Environment Agency (EA) holds the copyright to data originated by EA. NRW has permission to release this data on behalf of both parties.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037373935\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the 17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A] Data is stored in Recorder 6:  - Bullhead Monitoring, Wye \u0026amp; Usk Catchments 2007 - CCW- Report no. 818 CCW000280000000B  - Bullhead Monitoring, Dee Catchment 2001-2004- CCW - RoC Report no. 19 CCW0002800000006\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tBullhead is listed under Annex II of the Habitat and Species Directive as a species of community interest and is a feature on the River Wye Afon Gwy and River Usk Afon Wysg Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales. Bullhead numbers have been declining across Europe in recent decades as a result of human activities. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor bullhead populations to assess whether SAC management adequately protects that species.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17614\tNRW_DS107797\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.106Z\t314\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBullhead Monitoring for the Dee Catchment, 2004\t© CNC/NRW 2004 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037373937\t[A]  Data Archived under Project Number 295 Media Number 1198    [B]  Data is also held in  Recorder 6 CCW0002800000006 Bullhead Monitoring [C]  Dee Catchment 2001-2004- CCW - RoC Report no. 19\t\tBullhead is listed under Annex II of the Habitat and Species Directive as a \"species of community interest\".  Bullhead numbers have been declining across Europe in recent decades as a result of human activities. Bullhead is a designated feature on the River Dee and Bala Lake / Afon Dyfrdwy a Llyn Tegid  Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in Wales.  The purpose of this data capture was to assess bullhead abundances and population structure throughout the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) area. Population data for the fish bullhead (Cottus gobio) collected in the River Dee Catchment since 2001 and additional quantitative and semi-quantitative survey data to assess bullhead abundance from 2004.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:30:59.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17612\tNRW_DS107825\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.067Z\t683\tValid\tEditable\tInvertebrate Data of Selected Coastal Soft Cliff Sites in Wales (2002 - 2004)\t© CNC/NRW and National Museums for Liverpool 2006 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ National Museums for Liverpool and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037383235\t[A] Recorder 6 - Welsh Invertebrate Database Liverpool Museum coastal soft cliff survey CCW000660000000X.\tMike Howe Invertebrate Ecologist Terrestrial Science Group NRW.\tCoastal soft cliffs have been highlighted as a priority habitat under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan as a component of the Maritime Cliff and Slope.  A total of 6 Grade 1, 7 Grade 2, and 47 Grade 3 species of invertebrates that are restricted to coastal soft cliffs have been identified for Wales.     A 3 year survey, jointly funded by Countryside Council for Wales, National Museums of Liverpool and with logistical support from the National Trust was undertaken between 2002 and 2004 to provide more detailed information on invertebrate fauna of coastal soft cliffs at selected sites throughout Wales.   The purpose of this data capture was to identify current threats and management requirements for soft cliff habitats, and to attempt to rank soft cliff sites on the basis of their cliff fauna across selected sites in Wales.\tNational Museums Liverpool-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T17:09:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17608\tNRW_DS107902\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.106Z\t469\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring Data for the Rivers Wye and Usk (2007)\t© CNC/NRW 2006 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037393032\t[A]  Data is archived under Project 358 Media Number 1339.   [B]  Data is also held in Recorder 6 Lamprey Monitoring, Wye \u0026amp; Usk Catchments 2005 - CCW -  Report no. 41 CCW0002800000007.\t\tRiver, Brook and Sea Lamprey are designated features of the River Usk and Wye Special Areas of Conservation (SAC).  Assessments of species composition, distribution, abundance and size composition of larval lamprey (ammocoetes and transformers) populations in these river are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment.  The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of Lampreys in the Usk and Wye Catchments.  Monitoring of the status of lampreys is required to comply with the reporting requirements of the Habitats Directive. 54 Sites on the River Wye (of which 30 contained positive records) and 35 sites in the River Usk (of which 10 contained positive records) were sampled.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:59.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17609\tNRW_DS107907\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.106Z\t655\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring for the Rivers Tywi, Teifi and Cleddau (2004-2005)\t© CNC/NRW 2004.  There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037393037\t[A] Associated data is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 61, Media 356.  [B] Species data available in database called Recorder 6 under the Freshwater. CCW0002800000008 Lamprey Monitoring, Tywi, Teifi \u0026amp; Cleddau Catchments 2004 - CCW - RoC Report no. 7.\t\tRiver, Brook and Sea Lamprey are designated (Annex II) features of the Tywi, Teifi and Cleddau River Special Areas of Conservation.  Assessments of species composition, distribution, abundance and size  of these species in these river are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment. The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of Lampreys in the Tywi, Teifi and Cleddau Special Areas of Conservation.  Monitoring of the status of lampreys is required to comply with the reporting requirements of the Habitats Directive.  A total of 112 sampling sites across the River Tywi, Teifi, Eastern and Western Cleddau were sampled of which 77 have positive records for either River or Brook Lamprey.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:30:56.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17603\tNRW_DS107949\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.082Z\t418\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Lamprey in the River Dee (2004)\t© CNC/NRW 2004. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037393439\t[A]  Data is stored as in the data Archive under Project Number 174, 294  Media Number 508, 1197.  [B]  Data is stored in Recorder Lamprey Monitoring, Dee Catchment 2004 - CCW - RoC Report No. 18 CCW0002800000009.   [C]  GIS and Spreadsheet data available.\t\tRiver, Brook and Sea Lamprey are a designated (Annex II) feature of the River Dee SAC.  Assessments of species composition, distribution, abundance and size class composition are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment.  The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of Lampreys in the Dee Catchment.  Monitoring of the status of Lampreys is required to comply with the reporting requirements of the Habitats Directive. The dataset is made up of a collation of historical data, recorded during the Environment Agency Wales juvenile salmon surveys from 1985 - 2004 and surveys conducted as part of the Countryside Council for Wales project 2002 - 2004.  Within the historical data, information has not been broken down to a species or genus level and is only recorded at the family level. Historical records collected 1985 - 2002 were sampled by electro-fishing according to protocols laid out by the \\Environment Agency Electric Fishing - Theory and Practice Manual\\\" (Environment Agency 1998). Sampling was either Quantitative or Semi-quantitative or Timed surveys; precise dates are not given and records are assigned a year only.\"\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17600\tNRW_DS108557\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.993Z\t584\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBullhead Monitoring for the Usk and Wye Catchments 2007\t© CNC/NRW 2007 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence.  No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038353537\t[A]  Data is Archived as Project No. 201, Media No. 566.  [B]  CRF Hard Drive.  [C]  Species data available in Recorder 6 CCW000280000000B Bullhead Monitoring, Wye \u0026amp; Usk Catchments 2007 - CCW- Report no. 818.\tCustodian - Master Reference Data Team - species Data is held in Recorder 6. Project Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis / Heather Garret. Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team.  Rivers Ecologist, NRW Area Officer, Llandrindod Wells, NRW\tThe bullhead, Cottus gobio, is declining throughout Europe (although still apparently common over its native range in the British Isles) and is listed in Annex II of the EU Habitats Directive as a \\species of community interest\\\". Member states have the duty to designate Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) for such species and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within the SACs. There are six Welsh rivers designated as SACs for bullhead including the Wye and Usk. The Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) (now part of Natural Resources Wales) is responsible for monitoring the condition of bullhead populations within them and this study represents the first systematic quantitative sampling for bullhead in Welsh SACs for this purpose. The bullhead is a feature on the River Wye / Afon Gwy SACs and River Usk / Afon Wysg  SACs in Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor bullhead populations to assess whether Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) management adequately protects that species. This project assessed the population attribute only. No judgement can be made as to whether bullhead is in favourable or unfavourable condition on the Rivers Wye and Usk until the three other mandatory attributes of water quality flow and river morphology have been assessed.  The monitoring was of populations of the freshwater fish Bullhead (Cottus gobio) on the River Usk and Wye SAC\u0027s in south-east Wales.\"\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17598\tNRW_DS108573\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.912Z\t455\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Distribution of Salix herbacea (tiny creeping willow) on the Glydeiriau and evaluation of habitat quality\t© CNC/NRW 2008 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038353733\tNRW Upland Ecologist Officer and TSG archive\tThe contractor was Richard Leishman and the contract manager was Barbara Jones Internal Custodian - There is no longer an Upland Ecologist in NRW, so the main contact is Jane Stevens or Andy Jones (in the address book at Robert A Jones). Peter Jones or Stuart Smith could also know where the data is.        Sent an email to Jane Stevens about the location of the data.\tEryri is the most southerly mountain range in Britain with summits of over 914 metres  (3,000 feet). As a consequence, it is also the southern-most station for a number of  Britain\u0027s montane vegetation communities, and also a range of arctic alpine plants. The importance of the montane vegetation in this area has been recognised both nationally through the notification of the three highest mountain ranges as the Eryri Site of Special Interest (SSSI), but also internationally through the designation of this site as the Eryri/Snowdonia Special Area of Conservation (SAC). In addition many of the upland habitats that are present are listed in the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP).  One of the arctic alpine plants which characterises some of Eryri\u0027s most exposed and inhospitable summits and crags is Salix herbacea, the smallest of the British willows. Salix herbacea is a component species of  Carex bigelowii-Racomitrium lanuginosum moss-heath (National Vegetation Classification (NVC) code U10), chasmophytic vegetation of rock crevices and rocky tors, and very rarely a component of flush/spring vegetation including (NVC code M3I) Anthelia julacea-Sphagnum auriculatum spring. The habitat that supports the largest stands of S. herbacea is the moss-heath and this occupies plateaux and ridges from 730-970 metres. Less frequently, it also occurs as a component of chasmophytic communities, usually with very few individuals at each site, and with an altitudinal range of 670 -998 metres.   This dataset is a collation of the results from a survey of the distribution of stands of Salix herbacea on the Glydeiriau, and comparisons between this data-set, and a similar data-set of 1986. The purpose of this data capture was to: -  Assess the condition of those plant communities which support Salix herbacea and where these communities are found to be in unfavourable condition,  -  Assess the causes and impacts that have lead to this condition;   -  Identify any changes in distribution that may have occurred during the period between the capture of data sets in 1986 and 2007 and which might be attributed to climate change;  -  Identify those stands of vegetation which might be expected to be most vulnerable to climate change, and which might be monitored in future years.  All suitable habitat on the Glydeiriau above 600 metres which supported Salix herbacea, included H6150: Siliceous alpine and boreal grasslands on summits, summit ridges, and bwlchs. H8220:  Siliceous rocky slopes with chasmophytic vegetation, particularly on exposed ridges and bwlchs, and high level.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17611\tNRW_DS108651\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.110Z\t1060\tValid\tEditable\tLANDMAP Landscape Habitats\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363531\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Landscape Habitats (Boundaries).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Landscape Habitats (Classification).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Landscape Habitats (Evaluation).lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jill Bullen, Senior Landscape Specialist, Strategic Planning Group\tLANDMAP is a unique national information system, devised by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), allowing information about landscape in Wales to be collected and organised into a nationally consistent dataset.   The LANDMAP database includes both objective and subjective information and is designed to enable landscape quality to be taken into account in decision making.    Geographic dataset showing habitat landscape classification for Wales in the form of polygons (Aspect Areas) with large amounts of associated textual information, including General, Description, Evaluation, Recommendations, Area Boundary, Evaluation and Bibliography. A LANDMAP Quality Assurance Procedure is in place to ensure that LANDMAP Information meets an agreed standard of good practice and that data is accurate and consistent across Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-09-17\tpublication\tnull\thttps://naturalresources.wales/evidence-and-data/accessing-our-data/beta-wales-environmental-information-portal/?lang\u003den\t2025-12-19T10:50:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17613\tNRW_DS108652\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.083Z\t662\tValid\tEditable\tLANDMAP Visual Sensory\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363532\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Visual Sensory (Boundaries).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Visual Sensory (Classification).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Visual Sensory (Evaluation).lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jill Bullen, Senior Landscape Specialist, Strategic Planning Group\tLANDMAP is a unique national information system, devised by the former Countryside Council for Wales, allowing information about landscape in Wales to be collected and organised into a nationally consistent dataset. The LANDMAP database includes both objective and subjective information and is designed to enable landscape quality to be taken into account in decision making.    Geographic dataset showing visual and sensory landscape classification for Wales in the form of polygons (Aspect Areas) with large amounts of associated textual information, including General, Description, Evaluation, Recommendations, Tolerance to Change, Area Boundary, Evaluation and Assessment.    This dataset maps the landscape as perceived through our senses based on the physical attributes of landform and land cover. The perceived characteristics of the landscape are primarily determined visually, but the senses of hearing, smell and touch are also considered.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-09-20\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/2479/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://naturalresources.wales/evidence-and-data/accessing-our-data/beta-wales-environmental-information-portal/?lang\u003den\t2025-12-19T10:52:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17597\tNRW_DS108653\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.904Z\t1665\tValid\tEditable\tLANDMAP Historic Landscape\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363533\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap2017\\LANDMAP Historic Landscape.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Historic Landscape.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jill Bullen, Senior Landscape Specialist, Strategic Planning Group\tLANDMAP is a unique national information system, devised by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), allowing information about landscape in Wales to be collected and organised into a nationally consistent dataset. The LANDMAP database includes both objective and subjective information and is designed to enable landscape quality to be taken into account in decision making.    This is a geographic dataset showing historical landscape classification for Wales in the form of polygons (Aspect Areas) with large amounts of associated textual information, including General, Description, Evaluation, Recommendations, Area Boundary, Evaluation and Bibliography. This dataset focuses on archaeological and historical sites and how they relate to each other and to the surrounding landscape. The features mapped include those resulting from past human activity and structurally prominent patterns and features that contribute to the historic character of the present landscape.   Information is gathered by LANDMAP in order to inform sustainable decision making and to enable landscape to be taken into account during decision making processes. Historic Landscape data can be used to assess the speed, direction and effect of change and to provide an understanding of how the landscape has evolved. A LANDMAP Quality Assurance Procedure is in place to ensure that LANDMAP Information meets an agreed standard of good practice and that data is accurate and consistent across Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-09-17\tpublication\tnull\thttps://naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/business-sectors/planning-and-development/evidence-to-inform-development-planning/landmap-the-welsh-landscape-baseline/?lang\u003den\t2025-12-19T10:47:30.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17601\tNRW_DS108654\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.946Z\t1115\tValid\tEditable\tLANDMAP Cultural Landscape (archived data)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363534\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap2017\\LANDMAP Cultural Landscape.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LANDMAP Cultural Landscape.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jill Bullen, Senior Landscape Specialist, Strategic Planning Group\tThis dataset has been superseded by Landmap Cultural Landscape Services (metadata number:125279).  Landmap is a unique national information system, devised by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), allowing information about landscape in Wales to be collected and organised into a nationally consistent dataset.   The Landmap database includes both objective and subjective information and is designed to enable landscape quality to be taken into account in decision making.    It is a geographic dataset showing cultural landscape classification for Wales in the form of polygons (Aspect Areas) with large amounts of associated textual information, including General, Description, Evaluation, Recommendations, Tolerance to Change, Area Boundary, Evaluation and Bibliography.    The dataset records human activity that can be related to landscape, including major influences in art or literature which depicted or described the landscape, as well as more commonplace expressions of culture like farming type, or choice of building materials which creates a distinctive character in an area.   Information is gathered by LANDMAP in order to inform sustainable decision making and to enable landscape to be taken into account during decision making processes. This dataset is important as landscape not only shapes, but is shaped by culture. The dataset will raise awareness and enable people to engage in landscape decision making in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-09-01\tpublication\tnull\thttps://naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/business-sectors/planning-and-development/evidence-to-inform-development-planning/landmap-the-welsh-landscape-baseline/?lang\u003den\t2025-05-02T15:16:21.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17596\tNRW_DS108658\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.849Z\t831\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAn Assessment of Juniperus communis (Juniper) Sub-species Hemisphaerica in Britain using Molecular Markers\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363538\tCCW library, TSG archive and Welsh national libraries\tAndy Jones (Robert A Jones) Flowering Plant and Fern Specialist, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of genetic analysis aimed at ascertaining the distinction of subspecies of Juniper comminis (juniper) a morphologically variable dioecious species native to the British Isle. Within Britain the extremes of this variation are recognised by two sub-species ssp. communis and ssp. nana but many intermediate forms are known and two further sub-species are also recognised by some- ssp. depressa and ssp. hemisphaerica. It has been suggested that these sub-species should be considered as varieties of J. communis, however because of the rarity of the ssp. hemisphaerica in Britain further clarification of its taxonomic status was desirable. The ssp. hemisphaerica, while found in mountainous areas of southern Europe and north-west Africa, is confined to two main small populations on low maritime cliffs in the UK. Other population, also consisting of less than 10 individuals, is found on Ramsay Island on the Pembrokeshire coast, south Wales. In addition, 4 other individuals from Wales have been considered to show morphological characters that are indicative of ssp. hemisphaerica, the populations being at Skrinkle Haven (Pembrokeshire) and Holyhead Mountain (Anglesey). The aims of the project were: - to ascertain whether plants named as Juniperus communis ssp. hemisphaerica form a cohesive genetic group distinct from Juniperus communis ssp. communis;  - to assess the extent of clonal growth in British populations of ssp. hemisphaerica. The dataset also confirms the status of a UK BAP species to determine future conservation effort.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:26.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17599\tNRW_DS108672\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.917Z\t477\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCanal sediment and peat chemistry at Crymlyn Bog National Nature Reserve (NNR) (2005)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363732\tThe results are held in the Report located in the NRW library: [exCCW location Ffynnon Reference DCT-10-037447]  Located in Reports Archive: NRW DMS KNOW-1375319549-766\tInternal Custodian:  Peter Jones, Peatland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW Jamie Bevan, Senior Reserves Manager, NNRs/Swansea Neath Port Talbot / Operations SW, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tAn investigation found that Crymlyn Brook was a major threat to the integrity of the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) qualifying habitats at Crymlyn Bog as it was a source of nitrogen and phosphorous flowing directly in to the area of low-nutrient status transition mire and quaking bog habitats. It was suggested that the course of the brook should be altered to what it was prior to changes and siltation during the latter half of the twentieth century. This would result in it avoiding the sensitive habitats, instead connecting to Glan-y-Wern canal the main route by which water leaves the mire.  This dataset contains the results of a survey of the possible route for the reinstatement of the Crymlyn Brook relating to the extant vegetation along the route and the degree and depth to which the substratum is contaminated with mineral oil. Also examined were the depth of the Glan-y-Wern canal and the degree to which the sediments in the canal are contaminated with heavy metals and mineral oil. The purpose of this data capture was to provide information on the depth of standing water in the canal, the chemistry of it\u0027s sediments, and various aspects relating to the extent of hydrocarbon pollution within peat lying along the course of a proposed drainage feature.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17604\tNRW_DS108704\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.073Z\t570\tValid\tEditable\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey of Woodland Site of Special Scentific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales - (E3)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 1998. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038373034\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Woodland\\PH2_Woodland_Sites.lyr  Archived as Project No. 206, Media No. 571\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of woodland at 171 sites (E3) on 132 Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) throughout Wales, covering a total of 3806 ha.    The purpose of this data capture was to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland SSSI series as fundamental site-support information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1998-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T09:36:47.074Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n17615\tNRW_DS108730\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.105Z\t908\tValid\tEditable\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey of Selected Woodland Proposed Sites of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSIs) in Wales (E4)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2015. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038373330\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Woodland\\PH2_Woodland_Quadrats.lyr  Archived as Project No.  207, Media No. 572\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThe Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) selection criteria for woodlands are based on the National Vegetation Classification (NVC), such that the best areas of each community should be represented within each Area of Search (AoS). NVC information is therefore required for pSSSls.   This dataset comprises of a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of woodland at 13 sites on 12 proposed Sites of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSIs) throughout Wales, covering a total of 305 ha. The purpose of this data capture was to provide consistent survey information to aid assessment of SSSI status and subsequent site-support work.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T09:41:28.352Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n17602\tNRW_DS108734\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.995Z\t421\tValid\tEditable\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey of 5 Woodland Proposed Sites of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSIs) in North East Wales (E5)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038373334\tArchived under Project No. 208, Media No. 573\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset consists of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of woodlands on five proposed Sites of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSIs) in North East Wales.   The purpose of this data capture was to provide consistent survey information to aid assessment of SSSI status and subsequent site-support work.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-03T14:07:05.327Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n17595\tNRW_DS108800\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.080Z\t977\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Saltmarsh Survey of Wales\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 [2016]. © Hawlfraint y Goron. Cedwir pob hawl. Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru AC0000849444 [2016] This dataset contains \u0027simple derived data\u0027 which is jointly owned by NRW and the Ordnance Survey in the form of polygon vector data- vegetation, site boundary and vegetation mosaic MapInfo tables. Third parties seeking to re-use this dataset require a NRW licence. It allows reproduction, dissemination and re-use in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383030\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Saltmarsh\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM  (was originally managed by Peter Rhind, Coastal Ecologist, ex-CCW).\tThis National Vegetation Classification (NVC) Phase 2 project and corresponding datasets are a collation of mapped saltmarsh habitats across Wales.  This data was collected to provide an overview of saltmarshes across Wales.  The surveys were conducted between 1990 and 2003. The dataset has been extensively validated and verified.   However, habitat composition may change over time due to successional trends, land management or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type or species in the digital data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.  Coastal saltmarshes in the UK comprise of the upper, vegetated portions of intertidal mudflats, lying approximately between mean high water neap tides and mean high water spring tides.  Saltmarshes are usually restricted to comparatively sheltered locations in five main physiographic situations: in estuaries, in saline lagoons, behind barrier islands, at the heads of sea lochs, and on beach plains.  The development of saltmarsh vegetation is dependent on the presence of intertidal mudflats.  Communities are additionally affected by differences in climate, the particle size of the sediment and, within estuaries, by decreasing salinity in the upper reaches.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-08-04\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3605/?access_token\u003di61nd1X0RwdwAgQrGW0sqAsWxBMiwx\u0026amp;ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_phase_2_salt_marsh\t2025-12-19T10:12:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17590\tNRW_DS108805\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.063Z\t389\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Monitoring Sites\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383035\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Wales monitoring points.lyr  x:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Wales monitoring areas.lyr  x:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Wales monitoring points.lyr  x:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Wales monitoring transects.lyr   Archived as project 345, media 1321.\tMarine Monitoring Team, NRW\tSite location of Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRWs) Marine Monitoring Sites within Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).  This is general location of the sites where marine monitoring takes place and does not show the individual sample locations. It includes the location of monitoring sites for subtidal rock, subtidal sediment, intertidal rock, intertidal sediment, lagoons, marine climate monitoring sites, marine birds and bird food sites, seals and cetacean monitoring sites as well as areas where acoustic and drop down video monitoring takes place.   Information is also available for potential sea cave monitoring sites, however this is not currently a NRW monitoring programme.   The purpose of this data capture was to provide an overview of Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) Marine SAC Monitoring Sites. This is a Wales-wide but concentrated in the SAC and Special Protection Areas (SPAs). However additional sites will be added as they are identified.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T17:11:34.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17591\tNRW_DS108822\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.054Z\t622\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring invertebrates on Sites of Scientific Interest (SSSIs): Toadflax Leaf Beetle (Chrysolina sanguinolenta) on Poppit Sands, Pembrokeshire\t© CNC/NRW 2008 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383232\tLibrary and Area Libraries:  H:Drive \\TSG\\ Technical Research Reports\\Fowles_A\tMike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW\tPoppit Sands actually fall within two Sites of Scientific Interest (SSSIs), Afon Teifi SSSI in the east and Aberarth − Carreg Wylan SSSI to the west, and the situation is further complicated by the boundaries of these two SSSIs overlapping. The line where the two SSSIs join almost follows a major path running from the car park to the beach 50 metres east of the RNLI buildings. The purpose of this data capture was to map the distribution of common toadflax (Linaria vulgaris) on and around poppit Sands, including number of plants, size of plants and numbers of leaves per plant. Count (zero) of Chrysolina sanguinolenta on and around these plants.  The purpose of this data capture was to determine a method of monitoring the Nationally Notable leaf beetle Chrysolina sanguinolenta, a qualifying SSSI feature here, and carry out Common Standards Monitoring.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-10-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:14.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17589\tNRW_DS108873\t2024-05-28T12:27:23.989Z\t418\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCockle and Mussel Stock (bird food) Surveys in the Burry Inlet 2005 - 2009\t© CNC/NRW 2009. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383733\tThe data is archived under project 212, media no. 578.\tData custodian Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPAs) are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive), and for regularly occurring migratory species. The Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) supports nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders.  It also supports an important cockle fishery. The removal of cockles and the associated disturbance caused by fishing, needs to be balanced with bird food requirements. The aim of the project is to assess the food requirements of wading birds that over-winter in the Burry Inlet Special Protection Areas (SPAs).  Data Collection is repeated annually.  In the future this data may be combined with data gathered on bird foraging behaviour, together with site specific data on bird numbers and used in an individual behaviour based model to predict the food requirements of a bird population.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:22:50.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17592\tNRW_DS108879\t2024-05-28T12:27:23.995Z\t31\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTerrestrial Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitats of Wales (archived 2022)\t© CNC/NRW 2003 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383739\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Archive\\\tPeter Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader\tThis dataset is the geographic data for Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats. The purpose of data capture was to show the distribution of BAP priority habitats across Wales and allow statistics to be derived.  Survey data initially collected between 1979 - 1997, however this dataset was compiled in February 2003. Derived from Phase 1 Terrestrial Habitat Survey and Other Datasets as required.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-11-16\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:17:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17594\tNRW_DS108883\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.091Z\t665\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInvestigation into the Role of the Ancient Woodland Inventory, Historical Maps and Indicator Species in Identifying Ancient Woodland in Wales (2007)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383833\tPaper reports - held in CCW libraries (Regional and HQ- see related titles field) [Ffynnon Reference DCT-12-243383]\tJim Latham, Woodland Ecologist, NRW\tThe results of an experimental study involving desk study, field survey and statistical analysis, which investigated whether a more robust basis for identifying ancient woodland in Wales could be developed. Dataset includes species records from 20 study sites, a provisional list of vascular plant ancient woodland indicator species for Wales, broken down by region, results of statistical tests etc. Overall aim was to investigate whether a more robust basis for identifying ancient woodland in Wales can be developed. This included a comparison of the AWI and Epoc 1 County Series OS maps in terms of the identification of possible ancient woodland and a review of the use and reliability of Ancient Woodland Indicator Species in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:02:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17587\tNRW_DS108947\t2024-05-28T12:27:23.978Z\t709\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHydrological Investigations to Underpin Mire Restoration Work at Cleddon Bog Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), Monmouthshire, SE Wales (2006)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038393437\tReport held in Ffynnon reference (DCT-09-011958)\tPete Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group  Senior Conservation Officer and Team Leader Monmouthshire CCW and Peatland Ecologist CCW\tCleddon Bog Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) is located around 9 km south of Monmouth, Monmouthshire SE Wales. The site has a valley-head mire which has developed within two connected valley-head troughs.  The small catchment of which the site forms the upper reaches is a hanging valley system to the River Wye which runs north-south around 1 km to the east. There is a comprehensive body of evidence which shows that the condition of the mire feature at the site is deteriorating. Over the past few decades this has been marked by a decline in the abundance of key mire species at the expense of purple moor-grass and scrub. It is thought that the causes of this deterioration include drying of the mire surface, cause by catchment afforestation and a range of peripheral and internal drainage measures, coupled with the cessation of historical management practices such as grazing and burning. This dataset was collected to assess the current hydrological regime of the site and provide specific detailed recommendations to support a future programme of hydrological restoration.  The objectives of this project were to: - Develop an initial conceptual understanding of the principle water supply mechanisms to the site, with particular reference to valley-flank communities influence by lateral seepage. - Based on the initial conceptual model, undertake a limited programme of field investigation to secure a better understanding of water flow pathways into and within the site.  - Identify a range of hydrological restoration options for the site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-03-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17593\tNRW_DS109024\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.061Z\t344\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAmphibians and Reptiles Licence Return Data  (2001 - 2008)\t© CNC / NRW and various third party originator. All Intellectual Property Rights (including copyright and database) to the collation are owned by NRW. The IPR of individual records rests with the originator.  You may reproduce this dataset free of charge for non-commercial and internal business purposes in any format or medium, provided that you do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. To avoid using old information, we recommend that you obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039303234\tRecorder 6 CCW0007200000002 Amphibian \u0026amp; Reptile Licence Return Data\tInternal Custodian:  Species Protection Team, Maes y Ffynnon/Ffynnon DRMS.  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW / Data Services Team, NRW\tThe collation of amphibian and reptile data from licence returns was created in order to store all relevant data in one location, and mobilise records which may not have otherwise been available to the public. This dataset comprises of records of amphibian and reptile distributions across Wales obtained from former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 and Natural Resources Wales\u0027 licence returns for scheduled amphibian and reptile species. This dataset does not represent a systematic coverage or survey of Wales, but enhances species distribution surveys. The geographic location of individual records is generally random, depending on the requirements of the licence holders. The dataset has been subject to some basic validation to eradicate erroneous outlying records and inputting errors but has not been systematically verified.\tVarious including volunteer recorders-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17588\tNRW_DS109025\t2024-05-28T12:27:23.796Z\t242\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOther Mammals Licence Return Data (1905 - 2007)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2008 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039303235\tSpecies Data held in Recorder 6: Other Mammal Licence Returns CCW0008000000006    Paper Records: Dylan Lloyd, Operational Support Group, Maes y Ffynnon.\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tCollation of records from CCW licence returns for work carried out on scheduled mammal species sites in Wales. The collation was created to add to the picture of protected mammal distributions in Wales, to collect relevant data in one place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available. This dataset includes sightings of Bank Vole, Eurasian Common Shrew, Eurasian Pygmy Shrew, Eurasian Red Squirrel, Eurasian Water Shrew, European Water Vole, Field Vole, Harvest Mouse, Polecat, Wood Mouse and Yellow-necked Mouse.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various including volunteer recorders\t2007-01-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:27.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17586\tNRW_DS109396\t2024-05-28T12:27:23.491Z\t673\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Long-term Viability of Restored Lowland Heathland in Pembrokeshire\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039333936\tCCW library (Ffynnon Reference: DCT-09-049345) - this just gives the location of the report.\tJane Stevens, TEG (Formerly Jan Sherry CCW)\tThis dataset contains the results of questionnaires and interviews conducted with farmers involved with the management of six restored lowland heathland sites in Pembrokeshire. It includes an assessment of the agricultural value of restored sites versus un-restored sites i.e. had the restoration scheme added value; an assessment of the ecological, environmental and financial implications of a range of long-term management options, and an assessment of the various mechanisms available to support long-term heathland management. The purpose of this data capture was to determine whether the heathland restoration scheme was seen by the agricultural community to have added value to the sites; to determine what levels of support, including financial support and other resources, graziers deemed they required in order to continue to manage the sites in line with nature conservation objectives within the schemes, and to identify any potential future developments which may support or impede the management of heathland.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17625\tNRW_DS109473\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.548Z\t619\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSite Dossier for Lichen Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) Features at Pen y Gogarth/Great Orme\u0027s Head SSSI\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039343733\t[A] CCW library and Ffynnon reference DCT-09-049763 [B] Report copy on DMs\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tData collected as part of a data search and lichen survey of Pen Y Gogarth/Great Ormes Head Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). The survey was divided into two parts: Part 1 dealt with the taxa that comprise the lichen flora, the origin of the Orme\u0027s older records and with the people who collected on the Great Orme prior to the current survey. Outputs include information on lichen communities and habitats and tables showing  the conservation status and rarity of each of the records, including those of special importance such as Red listed species.   Part 2 involved developing proformas and establishing Common Standards Monitoring for lichens through trials at the site. Outputs include two indirect monitoring site accounts with a summary of lichen interest, table of notable lichen species and management suggestions; site attribute tables for the two sites, and a map showing the extent of the sites. Also included are direct monitoring proformas for 6 sites with details of location, habitat, abundance, species present etc. The 6 sites monitored in this way are mapped to show their locations.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-03-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17635\tNRW_DS109566\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.064Z\t456\tValid\tEditable\tNon Native Species data - Distribution of Carpet Sea Squirt (Didemnum vexillum) in Holyhead Marina\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright [2011]. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039353636\tGIS Dataset: X:\\Biological\\Non_Native_Species\\Invasive Didemnum Vexillum.lyr  All data is archived under media no 580, 593 \u0026amp; 592 (Project 214). Working copies of the data is held on MFSG shared Drive S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Didemnum vexillum Photos also archived on MMT DVD 17 in the fireproof safe and video archived on the I drive I:\\MMT video Archive\\Handheld Diver video\\2008\\Didemnum vexillum.\tData Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata Custodian: Data Services\tDidemnum vexillum is an invasive colonial ascidian that has become established world-wide having been identified in Japan, North America, northern Europe, and New Zealand, following a global expansion since the 1970’s. It forms sheet like colonies on natural and artificial hard substrata as well as benthic organisms including other ascidians and algae. The serious ecological and economic damage experienced in New Zealand and other temperate regions has led to a large investment in on-going research into the biological tolerances and spread of D. vexillum as well as rapid response, monitoring and management following introductions.  In summer 2008 an MSc student from Bangor University carrying out a survey of non native species in marinas in North Wales found a sea squirt in Holyhead Harbour that looked like the invasive non native species D. vexillum. A specimen was sent to a UK expert, who confirmed in November 2008 that it was very likely to be D. vexillum. Specimens were confirmed by Gretchen Lambert (pers. com. University of Washington, USA) as being D. vexillum.   The purpose of this data capture was to assess the distribution and spread of the non-native species and possible threat to native species and biodiversity of the area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T17:12:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17642\tNRW_DS109614\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.022Z\t575\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Sea Temperature Recording\t© CNC/NRW 2008 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039363134\tSkomer MNR West Region drive/shared documents/data/oceanographic/temperature\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. Monitoring of the seabed and temperature at Skomer MNR.  The purpose of this data capture was to:  Provide accurate seawater temperature records for the seabed and the water column.  Record temperature as continuously as possible.  Produce an ongoing long-term data set for the site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T10:29:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17639\tNRW_DS109654\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.049Z\t411\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Sea Water Turbidity Recording\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039363534\tSkomer MNR West Region drive/shared documents/data/oceanographic/sediment\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Sechi disc readings have been taken since 1992. Samples of suspended sediment have been collected from 2 sites since 1994 and turbidity probes / loggers have been used (with limited success) since 2002. The purpose of this data capture was to provide a long-term record of sediment load in the water column and water clarity for the site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:23:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17643\tNRW_DS109658\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.057Z\t490\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCollated Zostera Seagrass Bed Habitat Boundaries for Wales\t© CNC/NRW 2006 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039363538\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC OSPAR Seagrass Beds Point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC OSPAR Seagrass Beds Polygon.lyr  Master copy stored on S:\\Users\\lough_n\\OSPAR species and habitats\\Zostera Beds\\Currentlayer_2008 Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\Zostera beds\tMetadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team Custodian: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group\tSeagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas, such as lagoons and channels, which are sheltered from significant wave action. The three species of Zostera that occur in the UK, Dwarf eelgrass (Zostera noltii), narrow-leaved eelgrass (Zostera angustifolia), and  eelgrass (Zostera marina),  are all considered to be scarce (present in 16-100 ten km squares) and have some protection through the EC Habitats Directive and is classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitat. Zostera beds is also on the OSPAR List of threatened and/or declining species and habitats. The OSPAR Convention is the Convention for the Protection of the Marine Environment of the North-East Atlantic. This is a collation of Zostera habitats mapped by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Phase 1 Intertidal Survey, Intertidal Monitoring Contracts, Fisheries Sensitivity maps as well as point sources derived from casework site visits, CCWs data holdings in Marine Recorder and Environment Agency Wales (EAW) Water Framework Directive (WFD) Monitoring Results. The purpose of this data capture was to create a spatial representation for Zostera habitat across Wales. This collation was carried out for reporting on the distribution of OSPAR Habitats in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:25:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17632\tNRW_DS109736\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.056Z\t431\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey of Wales\t© CCGC/CCW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Countryside Council for Wales, 100018813 [2002].© Hawlfraint y Goron. Cedwir pob hawl. Cyngor Cefn Gwlad Cymru, 100018813 [2002]. This dataset contains \u0027simple derived data\u0027 which is jointly owned by CCW and the Ordnance Survey. Third parties seeking to re-use this dataset require a CCW licence. It allows reproduction, dissemination and re-use in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373336\t[A] Paper reports for each survey available from CCW library (see CCW library catalogue/related titles field for more information). Gower 2001 survey available as hardcopy in CCW heathland ecologist\u0027s office.  [B] Species data held in Recorder 6 - surveys tagged as Phase 2 Lowland Heathland  [C] GIS data held on X Drive: X-Layer_Files\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_heathland - see GIS metadata in the contains field [D] H:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\SherryJ\\Lheathland\\Survey\\LHS original quadrat data  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tIn the absence of a Heathland Ecologist Liz Howe has suggested that Peter Jones is the best contact; Peter Jones, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW (The old custodian was ex-CCW Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist)\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) is the basis used for the selection of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and is used to define Annex 1 habitats for the selection of Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) under the Habitats Directive (EU Directive 92/43).  This project comprises of detailed data related to the Phase 2 plant community survey for Lowland Heath across Wales. The project covers dry and maritime heath covered by the heathland section of the NVC and wet heath covered by the section of the NVC. Two non-NVC communities the calcareous grass and heath, and shingle heath are also described.  This project had three main purposes: - To provide an overview of the Welsh lowland heathland resource allowing local conservation programs particularly local Biodiversity Action Plans (BAP) partnerships, to assess and interpret their resource in a Welsh context.  - To identify heathland types which are underrepresented in the SSSIs series.  - To identified gaps in knowledge and highlight areas for further study.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:25.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17645\tNRW_DS109743\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.077Z\t551\tValid\tEditable\tAcross Wales Diving Monitoring Project (2004)\t© CNC/NRW 2006. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373433\tData is stored in Marine Recorder. Data is archived  (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\) as project 54 media 335-353; and projects 215-225, 229, media numbers 581-591 and 598 and in the fireproof safe but currently the data is held by contractors and on CD.  Video data on the Z marine archive drive (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Video), still images on DVD.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Biologist, NRW.  Contact is probably Harry Goudge or Lucie Haines.\tThe initial 2 year project was a developmental trial for establishing  (i) Methods,  (ii) Recording protocols.  (iii) Deployment strategies,  (iv) Repeatability,  (v) Site marking and relocation,  (vi) Quality Control for site monitoring.   Following on from the initial 2 year project a longer-term project has been established to gather data in order to assess the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) sites for their \u0027favourable conservation status\u0027.  The concept of favourable conservation status is central to the EC Habitats Directive. These SAC sites included five marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) around the Welsh coast though only four of these (Menai Strait and Conwy Bay; Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau; Cardigan Bay; and Pembrokeshire Marine) have \u0027reefs\u0027 listed as Annex I \u0027features\u0027. An ongoing programme of monitoring the reefs in each of these SACs follows the establishment programme.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T10:30:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17630\tNRW_DS109749\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.049Z\t761\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCockle and Mussel Stock (bird food) Surveys in the Dee, Traeth Lafan and Burry Inlet 2004\t© CNC/NRW 2004. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373439\tAssociated data is held in the Marine Archive as Project No. 212, Media 151,577,578\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPAs) are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive), and for regularly occurring migratory species. The Dee Estuary, Traeth Lafan and Burry Inlet SPA support nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders as well as important cockle fisheries. The removal of cockles and the associated disturbance caused by fishing them needs to be balanced with bird food requirements. The aim of the project is to assess the food requirements of wading birds, such as oystercatcher and knot that over-winter in these areas in order to set monitoring targets and to assess the implications of cockle and mussel fishing scenarios to the bird populations. The purpose of this data capture was to  provide data on stock abundance and distribution across the whole of the  survey areas. The data collected and its results will primarily be used for site monitoring and also for casework. Longer term work on the Burry Inlet may combine this data with data gathered on bird foraging behaviour, together with site specific data on bird numbers and used in an individual behaviour based model to predict the food requirements of a bird population.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:26:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17641\tNRW_DS109752\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.065Z\t664\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGround and Aerial Monitoring of Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra) in Carmarthen Bay Spatial Protection Area (SPA) (2002 - 2008)\t© CNC/NRW 2008 . There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373532\tData is archived under project no.40, media no. 219 (data for 2002-2004), project No. 162, media 491 (data for 2004-2007) Project No. 165, media no. 494 and Project No. 205,media 570 (data for 2007-2008).\tInternal Custodians:  - Marine Ornithologist, NRW - Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW\tCarmarthen Bay Spatial Protection Area (SPA) was classified as the UK\u0027s first SPA in 2003 on the basis of holding at least 1% (16,000 individuals) of the biogeographic population of Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra). The Common Scoter is strongly migratory and often travels considerable distances over land making brief stop-overs on inland waters. Its diet consists predominantly of molluscs, especially during the winter, although it occasionally takes other aquatic invertebrates such as crustaceans (e.g. barnacles and shrimps), worms, echinoderms, isopods, amphipods and insects (e.g. midges and caddis flies) as well as small fish and fish eggs which are found in abundance in the shallow water in Carmarthen Bay.  Common scoter are also listed as a Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) has supported ground based counts since 1996, following the Sea Empress oil spill.  Aerial surveys have also been employed as scoter often occur out of sight of land.  This dataset is compiled from data collected by The British Trust for Ornithology with support from CCW from 2002 onwards. The purpose of this data capture was to maintain a monitoring program using a combination of ground-based and aerial surveys to assess numbers and distribution of common scoter in Carmarthen Bay and to investigate whether ground-based counts provide an appropriate index of overall numbers of scoter within Carmarthen Bay as well as update the current index.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:27:34.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17644\tNRW_DS109756\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.081Z\t365\tUnassessed\tEditable\tExtent and Distribution of Saline Lagoons in Wales (2009)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2009. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373536\tH:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working\\Saline Lagoons. H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\XMarine Habitats GIS working\\Saline Lagoons.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services\tCoastal saline lagoons are an unusual and rare habitat in the UK, where they are recognized as a \\key\\\" habitat under the Biodiversity Action Plan. The EC Council Habitats Directive on the conservation of natural habitats and of wild fauna and flora identified coastal lagoons as a priority habitat. Work carried out identified 14 saline or potential saline lagoons and ponds in Wales four of which are identified as of high conservation merit.  The extent of these lagoons have been mapped . A secondary layer showing the extent of other pools that were investigated is also available. The purpose of this digitisation was to provide one collated layer showing the extent and distribution of saline lagoons in Wales. This layer may also be used as the basis for monitoring the extent of saline lagoons and therefore as a contribution to an assessment of their conservation status and to provide one collated layer showing the extent and distribution of saline lagoons in Wales. This layer may also be used as the basis for monitoring the extent of saline lagoons and therefore as a contribution to an assessment of their conservation status. This layer was created in January 2009 based on information provided by CCW Contract Science Report No. 464 (see related titles).\"\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:28:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17629\tNRW_DS109760\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.035Z\t827\tValid\tEditable\tAcross Wales Sea Bottom Temperature Time Series Data\t© CNC/NRW 2013. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373630\t[A]  Data between 2000 and 2015 is archived in media no. 590 and the master spreadsheet held on Ffynnon.    [B]  Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG shared drive at H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Temperature \u0026amp; salinity loggers  The archived data may not be complete, contact the internal contact for more information on the most up-to-date data.\tCharlie Lindenbaum, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tTemperature loggers have been placed at sites across Special Areas of Conservations (SACs) in Wales to measure the bottom sea temperature.  The data is collected continuously but is processed to give the daily mean temperature as well as yearly averages. This data is collected as part of the Across Wales Diving Monitoring Survey.   Additional sea temperature recordings are taken at Skomer Marine Conservation Zone but these are covered in a separate metadata entry.  Water temperature is also recorded in saline lagoons at Cemlyn and Pickleridge.   The data are captured as part of the Across-Wales Diving Survey, designed to gather information on Reef features across the Marine SACs in Wales.  Gathering physical data such as temperature, provides context and background information for the condition assessment of each of the reef features monitored.  For example, biogenic reefs and many of the species that occur around the reefs in Wales may be sensitive to fluctuations in temperature.    The data collected may provide supporting evidence for any recorded changes in species composition on sites, particularly where species are known to be at the edge of their biogeographical range.  All information about the sites is in kept the Temperature loggers site location document.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T17:29:12.897Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17638\tNRW_DS109800\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.076Z\t338\tValid\tEditable\tNon Native Species Data - Common Slipper Shell (Crepidula fornicata) around Wales\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2009 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383030\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Invasive Crepidula Fornicata.lyr  Most Current Data: S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Species Records - New\\Non-native records\\AlienCrepidula\\MostCurrentData.\tData Custodian: Intertidal Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe dataset contains a series of records of the non-native slipper limpet Crepidula fornicata, around Wales.   The records have been extracted from the Phase 1 Intertidal survey from target notes and site species lists and collated with ad-hoc records recorded by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff or supplied by third parties, along with records extracted from CCW\u0027s Marine Recorder data holdings. The purpose of this collation is to monitor the spread of non-native marine species across Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T10:32:19.130Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17640\tNRW_DS109802\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.943Z\t690\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Species\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383032\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Species.lyr .   Archived as project 204, media 569.\tCustodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a GIS dataset containing Phase 1 level intertidal species index (point data). The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005. All work has been undertaken by Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team.   During the survey species were recorded in one of three ways:  1) in target notes,   2) in species lists for certain (mainly specialised and nationally important) biotopes found on a site, or  3) in species list for a whole site therefore precision is very variable.   Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:38:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17633\tNRW_DS109803\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.036Z\t565\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Access Points\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383033\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Access points.lyr    Archived as project 204, media 569 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 501-600\\Media 569).\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a static spatial dataset. The dataset contains details on access points used during the phase 1 intertidal survey, including site ID, OS grid references, access type, status e.g. public/private, contact details of landowners and notes.  Phase 1 intertidal surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.  This particular dataset provides information on access points used by the surveyors to access the various sites across Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:39:42.365Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17637\tNRW_DS109804\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.049Z\t279\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Intertidal Area of Wales\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383034\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal Data is archived as project 204, media 569 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 569)\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  Internal Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a static spatial dataset.  The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005. All work has been undertaken by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team. Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications. This particular dataset maps the whole intertidal area of Wales as a polygon to calculate total area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:15:32.124Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17636\tNRW_DS109814\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.051Z\t353\tValid\tEditable\tLower Limit of Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Survey\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383134\tLegacy CCW Archive (File path: Z\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Hydrographic\\LAT-revised.TAB\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis is a GIS dataset line dataset depicting an approximation of the Lowest Astronomical Tide, through digitising along a combination of the OS 1:50,000 intertidal boundary and the lowest survey points of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) marine intertidal phase 1 mapping survey. The purpose of this data capture was to define the lowest boundary of the intertidal zone, which has relevance when defining Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) boundaries for intertidal features. All SSSI\u0027s designated after the CCW Phase 1 mapping survey use this as their lowest limit.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:42:14.311Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17628\tNRW_DS109815\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.862Z\t656\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Climate Change Monitoring (MarClim)\t© CNC/NRW 2013 and MBA 2013. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ MBA and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source. All requests for the data held in Marine Recorder will be referred back to DASSH.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383135\tThe reports and data are archived as Project No 262 to 267 and 303, Media No 958 to 962, 964, 1215 and 1409 at CCW headquarters.   A working copy of the data is held at: H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intertidal Climate Change. Since 2013, data and reports are held on the NRW Document Management System\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe MarClim project was established in 2001 to investigate changes that had occurred in rocky intertidal systems within the last 50 years around the UK. MarClim established a low-cost network of sites covering England, Wales and Scotland which provided subsequent annual updates to track how climate influences the marine biodiversity of the British Isles.   This dataset consists of the Welsh coverage of MarClim surveys only. Since 2005, Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has continued working with the MBA, through a Partnership Agreement. The annual survey in Wales forms part of a longer, thirteen-year continuous UK survey. Geographical coverage includes sites throughout north, mid and southwest Wales for which historical data dating back to the 1950s exist, and additional sites where range extensions have been predicted to occur. Thirty seven sites were surveyed in north Wales and seven sites in south Wales. MarClim has been highlighted in a global scientific assessment of the need for scientific research to be appropriately designed to provide fit-for-purpose information at relevant spatial and temporal scales useful to managers of protected areas, statutory bodies and policymakers.    The main aims at the outset of the MarClim project in 2001 remain as follows: - To use existing historical information and collect new data on intertidal indicator species from the last 50-100 years to develop and test hypotheses on the impact of climatic change on marine biodiversity in Britain and Ireland.  - To forecast future marine community changes on the basis of the Met Office’s Hadley Centre climate change models and the United Kingdom Climate Impacts Partnership’s climate change scenarios. The broad range of species known or likely to be temperature sensitive was covered.  - To establish low-cost, fit-for-purpose, methodologies and networks to provide subsequent regular updates and track how climate influences the marine biodiversity of Britain and Ireland.  - To provide general contextual time series data to support reporting on the success or otherwise of the Marine Strategy Framework Directive, marine aspects of Biodiversity Action Plans, European initiatives including the Habitats, Birds and Water Framework Directives (through appropriate Regulations), and management and monitoring of marine activities and resources, including fisheries and Special Areas of Conservation.  - To evaluate whether the climate indicator species used in this work have a wider contribution to make as part of the sustainability indicators that are needed to underpin the UK sustainable development strategy.  - To disseminate the results widely, and accordingly elucidate the known impact climate has had on marine biodiversity over the last 100 years, and may have in the future.  - To provide a basis for the development of a proposal for European Commission funding to establish a pan-European network with related aims.  - To assess and report on the likely consequences of the predicted changes in response to climate for society, for commercial and non-commercial users of the marine environment and the policies and frameworks that conserve, manage and protect marine biodiversity.\tMarine Biological Association (MBA)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-03-31-2014-03-31\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-31T09:42:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17627\tNRW_DS109818\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.789Z\t715\tUnassessed\tEditable\tShellfish Stocks, Oystercatcher and Knot (bird) Modelling Data for the Burry Inlet and Three Rivers (2004-2007)\t© CNC/NRW 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383138\tAll the data is archived in project 158 and media no. 594. A copy is held on the MFSG shared drive with the report.\tInternal Contact: Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) supports nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders which feed on the mudflats and sandflats over the winter period. Oystercatcher feed predominantly on cockles, and knot feed on a range of prey including cockle spat.  CCW commissioned a series of studies to determine the food requirements of oystercatcher and knot, to develop conservation objectives, monitoring targets and to assess the implications of cockle and mussel fishing scenarios on the bird populations. The latest of which is published as CCW Marine Monitoring Report no. 65. The purpose of this project was to extend previous modelling to incorporate both the Burry Inlet and Three Rivers in order to determine the inter-reliance of the sites and how environmental change (e.g. changes in shellfishing, habitat loss, changes in human disturbance, climate change and changes in population size) affects foraging animal populations.  This information will be used to help inform management of the SPA.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-02-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:41:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17626\tNRW_DS109822\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.573Z\t540\tUnassessed\tEditable\tShellfish Stocks, Oystercatcher and Knot (bird) Modelling Data for the Dee Estuary, Traeth Lafan and the Burry Inlet and Three Rivers, Phase 2 (2003-2004)\t© CNC/NRW 2004 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383232\tThe data associated with this project is archived as Project 158 (Media number 486).  A working copy is also held on the MFSG shared drive.\tInternal Custodian: Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Dee, Traeth Lafan and Burry Inlet are designated Special Protection Areas (SPAs) for nationally and internationally important populations of wintering wildfowl and waders.  To set conservation objections and assess the sites for Favourable Conservation Status, information is required on numbers of birds using the sites and the food supplies they require to survive the winter and attain adequate body condition for Spring Migration  This is phase 2 of a project that was started in 2002-03 and uses existing and new data to parameterise an individual behaviour-based model to answer key questions relating to target setting, condition monitoring and management of each of the three sites. The purpose of this data capture was to inform the setting of conservation objectives for the SPAs and assess the favourable conservation status of bird populations.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:42:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17631\tNRW_DS109823\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.038Z\t646\tUnassessed\tEditable\tShellfish Stocks, Oystercatcher and Knot (bird) Modelling Data for the Dee Estuary, Traeth Lafan and the Burry Inlet and Three Rivers, Phase 1 (1993-2001)\t© CNC/NRW 2003 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383233\tThe data associated with this project is archived as Project 158 (Media number 103)\tInternal Custodian:  Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThe Dee, Traeth Lafan and Burry Inlet are designated Special Protection Areas (SPAs) for nationally and internationally important populations of wintering wildfowl and waders. In order to set conservation objectives and assess the sites for Favourable Conservation Status, information is required on numbers of birds using the sites and the food supplies they require to survive the winter and attain adequate body condition for Spring Migration. The three SPAs also support economically important cockle fisheries that may affect oystercatchers directly, by removing some of the food available to them and, indirectly, by displacing them from the best cockle beds to less suitable feeding areas.  This is phase 1 of the project that will assess and use existing data to run an Individual Behaviour-based Model (IBM) to answer key questions relating to target setting, condition monitoring and management of each of the three sites.  There were two main objectives to the project.  The first to assess the power of existing surveys to detect change in the cockle populations at each site and recommend possible improvements.  The second objective was to determine how each site was used by the populations of oystercatchers that roosted there.  The models can be used to establish the target baseline quality habitats in terms of invertebrate populations but also in terms of the ability of the site to support the bird populations for which it was designated and therefore establish parameters for longer term monitoring and to establish whether it is in favourable conservation status.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:42:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17634\tNRW_DS109830\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.053Z\t327\tValid\tEditable\tMarine intertidal Phase 1 Final Report and Analysis Data\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383330\tData is archived as project 204, media 1423 (File path \u003d K:\\Archive\\Media 1401-1500\\Media 1423)\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tThis dataset contains analysis data in the form of excel spreadsheets (survey dates, contacts, recreational popular areas, details of sea defences etc) and MapInfo tables (biotope distribution- including rare, scarce, nationally important and specialised biotopes, biotope richness, mapping of northern and southern species, non native species, LBAP areas, bait digging and boulder turning locations, angling, man made substrata, intertidal designated sites etc). It also contains many images taken during surveying which are stored as JPEGs. These are made up of the results of a 10 year survey of the intertidal zone of Wales including detailed information on a wide variety of features and activities e.g. the distribution of habitats and species, non-native species, man-made substrata, bait collection. It provides comprehensive baseline data for monitoring change or for the identification of locations sensitive to certain anthropogenic impacts - a critical capacity in the context of climate change.    The data also provides information to support a notification programme for intertidal Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), thereby providing a series of intertidal designated sites across Wales.  It also underpins the selection of the internationally important Special Area of Conservation (SAC) site series.  Intertidal mapped data provides an integrated information resource for Local Authority structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications. The maps give important regional information about habitat and biotope distribution. They are used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments, assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), as a basis for the development of sensitivity mapping, and as a baseline for developing monitoring of intertidal features in marine Special Areas of Conservation (SAC).  Sites are mapped once and therefore are a \u0027snapshot\u0027 in time.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:43:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17623\tNRW_DS109831\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.232Z\t384\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Database\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383331\tArchived at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\Phase1_Database\\Phase1Acc2k.mdb.   The database is available through the intranet.   Data is also archived as project 204, media 569 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to500\\Media 569 Also under construction on NRW intranet: https://apex.cyfoethnaturiolcymru.internal/apex/f?p\u003d116:1::::::  (see online resource)\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tThis is a database of Marine intertidal Phase 1 survey data, which can be queried to provide information on biotope, species distribution, site details, uses and impacts, target notes and conservation interests. Following the completion of the intertidal project in 2007 the database was transferred to Apex software to be made available internally on the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) intranet. The intertidal phase 1 survey was undertaken to provide an integrated information resource for Local Authority structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications. The data collected is also used to provide regional information about habitat and biotope distribution, assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSIs), a basis of sensitivity mapping and in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIAs).    The database itself aims to provide a storage system to effectively store the survey results whilst providing a convenient way to view and manipulate the data collected. Data validated to ensure it\u0027s entered onto the database correctly. The database has been designed to keep data entry errors to a minimum e.g. majority of data entry are taken from drop down menus, percentage values must add up to 100%, many fields are mandatory, and duplicate entries are not allowed.   Each record in the database is checked against the data collection form by the data imputer and by a member of the survey team. A further validation is carried out when the data is cross checked with the digital map to ensure that the database and maps agree.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:44:21.204Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17624\tNRW_DS109832\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.276Z\t315\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcross Wales Bays and Banks Subtidal Soft Sediment Surveys\t© CCGC/CCW and Environment Agency There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of CCW/Environment Agency and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383332\t[A]  All data is archived under project 240 (media no 763, 910, 1285, 1286, 1330, 1388).   [B]  A working copy is held on H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Subtidal\\Bays and Banks benthic invert data [C]  Associated Spatial Data for sampling locations are held on the X Drive: X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Wales monitoring points.lyr  [D]  Data is currently being entered into Marine Recorder by a contractor – Marine Ecosol\tThe internal data custodian is: Mike Camplin, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist \u0026amp; Marine Monitoring Team Lead, Evidence, Knowledge \u0026amp; Advice The metadata point of contact is: Data Services, NRW\tThe aim of this data capture was to aid in the monitoring and condition assessment of subtidal soft sediment Annex I features in Wales.  Subtidal benthic soft sediment repeat sampling is carried out with Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) across Wales to monitor Large Shallow Inlets and Bays, and Estuaries and Sandbank features. Some of these data are collected in conjunction with the  Water Framework Directive invertebrate monitoring programme so other data from these locations or the surrounding area may be available upon request from the EA. Surveys took place in large shallow inlets and bays (Conwy Bay, Red Wharf Bay,  Cardigan Bay, St Brides Bay, Carmarthen Bay) and designated subtidal sandbank features in Wales. Benthic invertebrate samples are processed by laboratories that are members of the National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control (NMBAQC) scheme and random samples from each survey are also selected for audit by the NMBAQC contractor. Data entry into Marine Recorder database has not been validated.\tEnvironment Agency (EA)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:44:03.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17622\tNRW_DS109834\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.199Z\t791\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcross Wales Intertidal Monitoring Survey\t© CNC/NRW 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383334\tData is updated every 6 years  [A]  All data is archived under project 199.  [B]  Data for each feature is archived under separate Media numbers (Please refer to individual metadata entries).   [C]  Repeat sampling station locations is available on the UKDMOS website.   [D]  Species data is available through Marine Recorder.\tInternal custodian: Intertidal Survey Team Leader, MFSG, NRW.   Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset comprises of results from intertidal monitoring of Special Area of Conservations (SACs) that contain Wales is undertaken. Work is undertaken to enable reporting on a range of external obligations under Habitats Directive, Water Framework Directive and the inter-agency agreement on Common Standards Monitoring; to harmonise with WFD monitoring in coastal and transitional waters. The work will also be used to inform management decisions on individual sites.  Each sample station will be monitored for one of the following SAC sub-features;   1) Under-boulders   2) Sabellaria reef  (created by the honeycomb worm)  3) Rockpools   4) Characterizing rock or sediment habitat    5) Zostera beds  (seagrass meadows) 6) Nationally rare or scarce species. Work has been carried out from 05/07/2004 and is on-going.    The majority of sample stations are visited every 6 years, although some sites where there are particular pressures are visited annually, for example reef features in Pembrokeshire Marine SAC and Zostera beds at Traeth Lafan in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. Confidence in certain methods and their data is high, whilst the objectives of some surveys are to develop confidence in the method and data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:45:34.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17620\tNRW_DS109835\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.059Z\t313\tValid\tEditable\tTremadog Bay Subtidal Macrobenthic Study\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383335\t[A]  The data is archived in Project no. 143 (media 469)- Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media401to500\\Media469. [B]  Species data is also held in Marine Recorder.   [C]  Specimens are stored at EMU.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Habitats Biologist, MFSG, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Area of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the \u0027Favourable Conservation Status\u0027 (FCS) of habitats and species as defined in the Directive. In the case of SACs, the features to which FCS applies are the habitats and/or species listed in Annex I and Annex II of the Habitat Directive for which the individual site has been selected. Infaunal monitoring work is required to provide management feedback and enable reporting on the FCS of infaunal aspects of SACs, Special Protection Areas (SPAs: Birds Directive) and Special Sites of Scientific Interest (SSSI).    The aim of this data capture was to gather repeatable data to provide reference conditions and to support the identification of the two characteristics of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau large shallow inlet and bays feature.  Tremadog Bay a large shallow Inlet and Bay within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC.  Two important characteristics of this feature have been identified.  These are:   1. The variety of subtidal sediment communities within the large shallow inlet and bay  2. The species richness and diversity of the sublittoral mixed sediment south west of Pwllheli\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:45:56.057Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17621\tNRW_DS109859\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.080Z\t1214\tValid\tEditable\tNational Landscape Character Areas (NLCA)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383539\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\National Landscape Character Areas.lyr  Written reports held in paper form in NRW libraries.\tMetadata and Data Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset comprises of boundaries for the 48 regional landscape character areas for Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to geographically distinguish different regions in Wales in terms of landscape identity and what characteristics and qualities make one region different and distinct from another.    These distinctions and policy drivers for this project are described, together with an illustrated narrative, and published on Natural Resources Wales (NRW\u0027s) website. This dataset covers 100% of terrestrial Wales including inter-tidal areas.  Boundary data derived from source data 2007-2008 with the final version being amended October 2008. Some area names were amended in August 2023 to match written profile documents, and to reflect official status change for the names Eryri and Bannau Brycheiniog.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-09-18\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/415/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_LANDSCAPE_CHARACTER_AREAS\t2026-01-12T15:33:54.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17619\tNRW_DS109860\t2024-05-28T12:28:15.984Z\t379\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features of the Braint Estuary 1996\t© CNC/NRW 1997. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383630\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales    Z:\\Archive\\MFSG Data archive\\Media301to400\\Media388 Archived as Project No. 131, media 388\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of the results of a National Vegetation Classification as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the Braint Estuary. The purpose of this data capture was to use the National Vegetation Classification to describe and map areas of saltmarsh on the Braint Estuary- put forward as a pSAC as part of the Glannau Mon: Cors Heli/ Anglesey Coast saltmarsh.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:47:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17617\tNRW_DS109861\t2024-05-28T12:28:15.881Z\t564\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features at Cefni and Aberffraw 1998\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright [year document is published]. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383631\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media201to300\\Media234 Archived as Project 53, media 234\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of a survey which used the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Anglesey (Cefni and Aberffraw). The purpose of this data capture is to use the National Vegetation Classification to describe and map areas of saltmarsh on the southern shore of Anglesey that were put forward as a potential Special Area of Conservation (pSAC) as part of the Glannau Mon: Cors Heli/ Anglesey Coast saltmarsh.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:51:21.180Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17618\tNRW_DS109879\t2024-05-28T12:28:15.893Z\t732\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features at Burry Inlet and Loughor Estuary (1998)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 1999. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383739\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media201to300\\Media217 Data is held as Project No.52, media 217.\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Burry Inlet and Loughor Estuary. The area comprises the most extensive site of saltmarsh vegetation in Wales. The purpose of this data capture is to provide detailed information on the distribution of the key elements for which the estuary was submitted for selection as a candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) including the extent of transitional zones, extent of saltmarsh communities and the distribution of the nationally scarce and locally distributed Marsh Mallow, Althaea officinalis. Another aim of the survey was to provide floristic data to enable the separation of the Mediterranean Saltmeadows from their more generally distributed counterparts.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:54:02.395Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17616\tNRW_DS109880\t2024-05-28T12:28:15.804Z\t305\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features of the Taf, Tywi and Gwendraeth Estuaries 1997\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 1998. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383830\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media101to200\\Media178 Data is held as Project No. 32, media 178\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThis dataset comprises of the results from a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the Taf, Twyi and Gwendraeth Estuaries- the \u0027three rivers complex\u0027. The work was carried out in preparation for notification of the saltmarshes in the complex as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1998-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:56:19.334Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17675\tNRW_DS109881\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.690Z\t695\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features at Milford Haven 2002\t© Milford Haven Waterway Environmental Survellance Group 2003 NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383831\tArchived as Project 132 Media 389.  X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh  Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW.          Although this dataset is 3rd party, we have decided not to include it as strictly 3rd party, because we were heavily involved in the validation process and we are a partner of the owner.\tThis dataset is the results of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats at Milford Haven. The purpose of this data capture was to survey and map the saltmarsh units within the Milford Haven Waterway within the context of the National Vegetation Classification and to repeat the recording of the permanent quadrats established in 1996 to assess change in the floristics of these areas.    Other purposes include the erection of a series of new permanent quadrats within the eastern section of the estuary as a baseline for the monitoring of future changes, and to record and map the distribution of notable species of the saltmarshes and their associated vegetation. This dataset has been amalgamate with saltmarsh data from across Wales to form the Phase 2 Saltmarsh Survey data for Wales.\tMilford Haven Waterway Environmental Surveillance Group [MHWESG]\t2003-03-14\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:16:21.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17673\tNRW_DS109882\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.673Z\t421\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features of the Severn Estuary 1998\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 1999. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383832\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media201to300\\Media214 Archived as project 130 media 214\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the Severn Estuary. The purpose of this data capture was to provide a detailed NVC description of Severn Estuary saltmarsh and an account of distribution and NVC associations of several nationally scarce and one locally scarce species. The survey also provided a preliminary conservation assessment for the Severn Estuary potential Special Area of Conservation (pSAC) and line transects as baseline data for future remote sensing studies.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:20:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17660\tNRW_DS109883\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.417Z\t452\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features of the Dee and Clwyd Estuaries 2000\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2001. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383833\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media101to200\\Media180 Data held as Project No. 129, media 180\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results from a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey used as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the Dee and Clwyd Estuaries. The purpose of this dataset was to provide systematic descriptions at the plant community level of saltmarsh and associated habitats in the Dee and Clwyd estuaries. The objective was to provide an essential basis for monitoring, facilitate management planning and improve our ability to evaluate development proposals.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:21:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17664\tNRW_DS109884\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.372Z\t651\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features at Milford Haven 1996 - Sea Empress Survey\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright [1999]. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383834\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Milford Haven 1996 Data also archived as project 47 media numbers 42, 54, 215 at  H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media001to100\\Media042 (report)   , H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media001to100\\Media054  ,    H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media201to300\\Media215\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThe Sea Empress released approximately 72,000 tonnes of crude oil and about 480 tonnes of heavy fuel oil in the period 15 to 21 Feb 1996 when grounded off Milford Haven and later while at the jetty.   The coastline within Milford Haven was heavily oiled and a major clean-up operation was mounted.  However, to avoid further damage, oil was not cleaned from the saltmarsh. Many of the affected saltmarshes in Milford Haven lie within Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and also form components of candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) under the Habitats Directive.  As part of the response to the oil spill, the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) commissioned a damage assessment survey of impacted saltmarshes in Milford Haven. Carry out an assessment of the possible damage to saltmarsh habitat and the associated scarce species Salicornia pusilla and Limonium humile affected by the Sea Empress oil spillage and to establish a base-line against which future changes may be kept under surveillance - this included mapping the saltmarshes using National Vegetation Classification (NVC) methodology. The survey also aimed to assess the levels of hydrocarbon pollution within the sediments of the affected saltmarshes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:26:32.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17663\tNRW_DS109885\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.385Z\t707\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features at Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) - Glaslyn 1993\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright [1994]. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383835\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Glaslyn 1993 Field Studies journal available at CCW Headquarters Library, Bangor. (see library catalogue) H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media533 Archived as project 190 media 533\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau - Glaslyn 1993.  The main objective of the survey was to use the National Vegetation Classification to describe and map the unusual brackish water saltmarsh communities on the seaward edge of the marsh as well as characterising some of the transitional vegetation types and the glycophytic communities on the middle and upper marsh.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1994-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:36:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17668\tNRW_DS109886\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.548Z\t340\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaltmarsh features at Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) - Dyfi 1996\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright [1996]. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383836\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Dyfi 1996 H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media901to1000\\Media902 Archived as project 249 media 902\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau - Dyfi 1996. The main objective of the survey was to use the National Vegetation Classification to describe and map the unusual brackish water saltmarsh communities on the seaward edge of the marsh as well as characterising some of the transitional vegetation types and the glycophytic communities on the middle and upper marsh.  Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by NRW, corrections made by contractors, validated again by NRW, corrections made in-house in conjunction with relevant salt marsh specialist.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1996-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:58.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17662\tNRW_DS109887\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.397Z\t398\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features at Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2003\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2004. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383837\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media201to300\\Media216 Archived as project 39 media number 216\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau.  The purpose of the data capture was to map the location and extent of saltmarsh and associated vegetation types, to classify the stands in relation to the National Vegetation Classification, to apprise the quality of the vegetation and to produce information relevant to the completion of a conservation assessment of the constituent sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:40:22.219Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17658\tNRW_DS109888\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.170Z\t423\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features of the Conwy Estuary 2001\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright [2002]. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383838\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Conwy 2001    H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media532 Archived as project 189 media 532\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of the results from a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey used as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the North Conwy Estuary.  The principal objective of the survey was to provide a suitable base for future monitoring and also as part of CCW\u0027s commitment to reporting on the suitability of the estuary description as a Site of Special Scientific Importance (SSSI) and inclusion into the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay possible Special Area of Conservation (pSAC).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:41:32.116Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17657\tNRW_DS109889\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.074Z\t271\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features at Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) - Dyfi 1993\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright [1993]. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383839\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Dyfi 1993 H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media901to1000\\Media903 Archived as project 250 media 903\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau - Dyfi 1993. Dyfi Junction saltmarsh survey.   The main objective of the survey was to use the National Vegetation Classification to describe and map the unusual brackish water saltmarsh communities as well as characterising some of the transitional vegetation types and the glycophytic communities present.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1993-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:44:10.924Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17672\tNRW_DS109891\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.582Z\t446\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcoustic Monitoring of the Extent of the North Llyn Modiolus Reef\t© CNC/NRW and Bangor University 2013 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW / Bangor University and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383931\tData from monitoring trial and subsequent 1999 surveys archived under media no. 004 and 005 Annual date collection from 2006 archived in project no. 233 (media 602). Analysis of the data from 2006 archived in project no. 234 (media 306)\tInternal Custodian is: Marine Monitoring Officer, MFSG, NRW. Metadata point of contact is: Data Services\tBiogenic horse mussel (Modiolus Modiolus) reef is an important feature of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC).  The reef has high biodiversity and is vulnerable to anthropogenic activities, such as benthic fishing.  The mussel reef forms an undulating surface that is significantly different to the surrounding seabed and therefore acoustic monitoring methods can be used to monitor changes in extent and identity areas of potential damage. Work is carried out on the north Llyn horse mussel reef  in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau jointly by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and University of Wales Bangor.  The purpose of this data capture was to: -  Measure extent and temporal changes within the of the Modiolus reef in the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC.   -  Investigate the health of the reef and for signs of anthropogenic activity within the area.   An area approximately 10km by 6km, adjacent to the Pen Llyn coast, south-west of Porth Dinllaen, approximately 1.5km off shore was surveyed.  All data is processed to reduce errors (e.g. positional corrections).  Acoustic data is subject to ground-truthing using drop down videos captured as part of a separate project.  As of April 1st, 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tUniversity of Wales Bangor-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:46:44.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17674\tNRW_DS109893\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.688Z\t597\tValid\tEditable\tReview of Side-Scan Sonar Results from North Llyn Modiolus Reef\t© CNC/NRW 2007 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383933\tAll data is archived in project no.150 (DV tapes are archived in media 482 \u0026amp; 483, project data is archived in media 484).  The original side-scan sonar data is archived under project 233 (media 602).\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Officer, MFSG, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tAn emergency review of side-scan sonar data collected by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in 2007 was required as the images showed marks that were potentially derived from trawling gear.  The area was approximately 10 km by 6 km and approximately 1.5 km offshore, within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and is also protected under Sea Fisheries byelaws that prevent the use of trawls and dredges for the harvesting of molluscan shellfish, except as authorised by the local sea fisheries committee.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:45:16.014Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17659\tNRW_DS109898\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.354Z\t406\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of the Recreational Use and Conservation Value of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 © Hawlfraint y Goron. Cedwir pob hawl. Cyngor Cefn Gwlad Cymru, AC0000849444. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383938\ta) MapInfo tables on CD held by CCW HQ library (copy held by Jan Sherry) b) Report and data files held as paper copy and CD by CCW HQ library (copy held by Jan Sherry) c) Excel files on CD held by CCW HQ library (copy held by Jan Sherry)\tLiz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW Formerly Jan Sherry Heathland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW\tThe coal extraction industry has had a massive impact on the natural environment at a landscape level, the most obvious legacy of the operations being the extensive coal spoil tips apparent across the region. In recent years millions of pounds have been spent on the stabilisation and eventual reclamation of these spoil areas and their redevelopment for housing, new industry or local open spaces. In the past the potential ecological value of the tips has generally been overlooked or understated. However, old coal spoil sites are now recognised as an important habitat for a range of species and the few remaining sites in the Valleys also have cultural and community value, however general awareness of the value of these sites is low. This has resulted in a loss of site value in term of heritage, ecology, landscape and amenity and there was a need to put individual sites into a context. The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), conducted an integrated strategic assessment of the recreational use and conservation value of heathland and associated habitats on the coal spoils of South Wales.  This dataset is a collation of all biological data associated with 25 coal spoil sites in the South Wales valleys. The purpose of this data capture was to conduct a strategic assessment of conservation, recreation and education use and potential of these sites.  Specific aims included initial classification of coal spoil types, collation of species and habitat data to allow an initial assessment of conservation value, assessment of recreational and amenity use and assessment of the potential for enhancements.  The area covered by this work included the South Wales valleys. The current dataset covers Torfaen, Rhondda Cynon Taf, Caerphilly, Blaenau Gwent and Merthyr County Borough Council (CBCs).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17661\tNRW_DS109899\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.467Z\t356\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383939\ta) Paper report held by CCW HQ library, digital version on CD in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) b) MapInfo layers on CD in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) c) Excel files on CD  in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) d) Photographs on CD  in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry)\tLiz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW (Formerly Jan Sherry Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems/ Heathland Ecologist, NRW)\tThe coal extraction industry has had a massive impact on the natural environment at a landscape level, the most obvious legacy of the operations being the extensive coal spoil tips apparent across the region. In recent years millions of pounds have been spent on the stabilisation and eventual reclamation of these spoil areas and their redevelopment for housing, new industry or local open spaces. In the past the potential ecological value of the tips has generally been overlooked or understated. However, old coal spoil sites are now recognised as an important habitat for a range of species and the few remaining sites in the Valleys also have cultural and community value, however general awareness of the value of these sites is low. This has resulted in a loss of site value in term of heritage, ecology, landscape and amenity and there was a need to put individual sites into a context. The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), conducted an integrated strategic assessment of the recreational use and conservation value of heathland and associated habitats on the coal spoils of South Wales.  This dataset compiles of the results of an National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of coal spoil habitats on 15 sites in the South Wales valleys. It contains quadrat and species data for heath, grassland, mire and associated habitats and mapped habitat polygons.  The purpose of this data capture was to conduct a strategic assessment of the conservation value of coal spoil in the South Wales valleys to be used to identify important sites which require protection through the planning process and as possible use for designation of sites. It has also been used to aid in identifying action by Local Biodiversity Action Plans (LBAPs).  The area covered includes South Wales valley; Torfaen, Rhondda Cynon Taf, Caerphilly and Merthyr County Borough Council (CBCs).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17671\tNRW_DS109900\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.473Z\t402\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales. Phase II: Assessment of Environmental Parameters\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393030\ta) Paper report held by CCW HQ library, digital version on CD in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) b) MapInfo layer on CD in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) c) Excel files on CD  in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) d) Photographs on CD  in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry)\tContact: Liz Howe (used to be Jan Sherry Heathland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW)\tThe coal extraction industry has had a massive impact on the natural environment at a landscape level, the most obvious legacy of the operations being the extensive coal spoil tips apparent across the region. In recent years millions of pounds have been spent on the stabilisation and eventual reclamation of these spoil areas and their redevelopment for housing, new industry or local open spaces. In the past the potential ecological value of the tips has generally been overlooked or understated. However, old coal spoil sites are now recognised as an important habitat for a range of species and the few remaining sites in the Valleys also have cultural and community value, however general awareness of the value of these sites is low. This has resulted in a loss of site value in term of heritage, ecology, landscape and amenity and there was a need to put individual sites into a context. The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), conducted an integrated strategic assessment of the recreational use and conservation value of heathland and associated habitats on the coal spoils of South Wales.  This datasets consists of the soil chemical analysis, soil physical analysis and soil biological analysis on 8 sites chosen from the 15 sites surveyed in the Assessment of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales 2006 survey.  The purpose of this data capture was to look at edaphic factors underlying the development of coal spoil communities of high conservation value. Edaphic factor can be defines as an abiotic factor relating to the physical or chemical composition of the soil found in a particular area. For example, very alkaline soil may be an edaphic factor limiting the variety of plants growing in a region. South Wales valley; current dataset covers Torfaen, Rhondda Cynon Taf, Caerphilly and Merthyr County Borough Councils (CBCs).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:38:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17666\tNRW_DS109916\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.462Z\t611\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCoal Spoil Conservation Project\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393136\tSee individual survey metadata entries in \u0027contains\u0027 field. 109899 Assessment of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales. 109898 Assessment of the recreational use and conservation value of heathland and associated habitats on the coal spoils of South Wales 109900 Assessment of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales. Phase II: Assessment of Environmental Parameters\tLiz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW (Formerly Jan Sherry Heathland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW)\tThe coal extraction industry has had a massive impact on the natural environment at a landscape level, the most obvious legacy of the operations being the extensive coal spoil tips apparent across the region. In recent years millions of pounds have been spent on the stabilisation and eventual reclamation of these spoil areas and their redevelopment for housing, new industry or local open spaces. In the past the potential ecological value of the tips has generally been overlooked or understated. However, old coal spoil sites are now recognised as an important habitat for a range of species and the few remaining sites in the Valleys also have cultural and community value, however general awareness of the value of these sites is low. This has resulted in a loss of site value in term of heritage, ecology, landscape and amenity and there was a need to put individual sites into a context.  The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), conducted an integrated strategic assessment of the recreational use and conservation value of heathland and associated habitats on the coal spoils of South Wales. This was intended as the first phase of a coal spoil conservation strategy which incorporated the results of additional archaeological and community assessment. The study was limited to the area covered by Torfaen, Merthyr Tydfil, Caerphilly, Rhondda Cynon Taf and Blaenau Gwent County Borough Councils. A Coal Spoil Conservation Strategy was initiated and this dataset includes the results from different phases of the project (see \u0027contains\u0027 field). The aim of this data capture was to provide background data for the delivery of a consistent strategy for local biodiversity action and amenity enhancement. The results of an archaeological and community assessment were also incorporated. The study suggests a classification for types of coal spoil and assesses the ecological value of a large number of sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-SEWBReC (South East Wales Biodiversity Records Centre)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17656\tNRW_DS109921\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.103Z\t547\tValid\tEditable\tCoastal Sensitivity Atlas\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2009. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393231\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Emergencies_Data\\oil_spill\\WalesMarineResourceAtlas. Maes y Fynnon and regional offices. MESG Oil Spill Contigency Plan - Fynnon numbers DCT-09-103877, DCT-09-103876.\tCustodians - GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW Jennie Jones was involve din updating the data, Mike Thomson will be taking over as business lead as hold  role of Duty dispersant officer Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW       This metadata entry need re-writing as the product has been updated and renamed to reflect NRW current role to provide dispersant advice only and that the data has been transferred to ArcGIS from MapInfo and may have been updated in some cases.  The new name is Coastal Sensitivity Atlas (previously known as Atlas of Coastal and Marine Resources Sensitive to Oil Pollution (Wales) - GIS Dataset Jennie Jones has taken a lead on the updating the Atlas, working with Phil Jutson. Mike Thomson (Incidents and Emergencies Training and Systems Manager) will become the business lead as the Dispersant Duty Officer\tThis spatial dataset is an Atlas of Coastal and Marine Resources Sensitive to Oil Pollution in Wales. This data capture aims to highlight sensitive and vulnerable areas across Wales which maybe sensitive to oil pollution. It contains, but is not limited to, information on seabirds, scoter, wetland birds, grey seals, cetaceans, booming plans, protected sites, sand dunes, saline lagoons, sea grass beds and biotopes.  This is intended for use by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) staff, but is a legacy Countryside Council for Wales’ dataset. It should be used when providing environmental advice during an oil spill response operation. It\u0027s primarily intended as a memory jogger during the early stages of operation. It provides information on the location of environmental resources only, background knowledge of the relative sensitivity and vulnerability of the resources is required when providing advice during marine pollution incidence. Covers the coast of Wales and adjacent coasts of England (east coast of Dee estuary to Hoylake and south coast of Bristol Channel to Porlock Bay). 1st edition published March 2003; currently being updated and 2nd edition to be published early 2009.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-01-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:46:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17669\tNRW_DS109980\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.551Z\t964\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCoastal Saline Lagoons and Pools in Wales\t© CNC/NRW 2000. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393830\tData is archived as project 12, media 22 and 23 (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 22 and K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 23) Species data held in Marine Recorder (survey number JNCCMNCR40000799) Original survey forms are stored in NRW off-site storage. Fireproof safe - Stack 01, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe Natural History Museum was asked by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to undertake a survey of all potential lagoonal or coastal brackish pond sites in Wales. This dataset presents the results of that survey, conducted during December 1998 as well as the results of the review and proposals for the monitoring and conservation of the coastal saline lagoons in Wales. Lagoons are an unusual and rare habitat, both in the UK (total resource ca 5,000 ha) and elsewhere. The characteristic lagoon community includes species almost or entirely restricted to lagoonal habitats in the United Kingdom, where they represent some of our rarest plants and animals; nine lagoonal species are currently protected under Schedules 5 and 8 of the Wildlife and Countryside Act (1981).  Survey of lagoonal habitats in Wales to identify lagoons and pools and carry out an assessment of condition and monitoring.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2000-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:47:41.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17665\tNRW_DS109983\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.434Z\t351\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Turbidity Data and Macroalgal Depth Distributions on a Transect in the Menai Strait\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393833\tData is archived in the NRW Marine archive as Project No 13, Media numbers 24,31 and 83 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\24 etc etc)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW.\tPresentation of data and analysis of patterns of water turbidity in the Menai Strait between 1961 and 1999 as well as depth distribution patterns of macroalgae (Telegraph Cable transect) at intervals from 1956 to 1999. Analysis of any relationship between the depth distribution of macroalgae and water turbidity patterns. The purpose of this data capture was to ascertain changes in turbidity and depth distribution patterns of macroalgae.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:48:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17667\tNRW_DS109992\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.510Z\t423\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRe-examination of distribution data for the harbour porpoise (Phocoena phocoena) on the North West European Continental Shelf\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393932\tData is within the Marine archive as Project No. 28 and 169, and Media163 Fireproof safe - Stack 05, Hafod Elfyn, CCW Headquarters, Bangor, Wales.\tMarine and freshwater Evidence Manager, NRW\tThe Joint Cetacean Database, contributed to by Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC), Sea Watch Foundation (SWF) and Sea Mammal Research Unit (SMRU) contained most, but not all, effort-related cetacean data for North-west European waters up to 1998. Effort-related data from this database have been used to produce maps of cetacean distributions for north-west European waters for a Cetacean Atlas at the scale of 1/4 ICES (International Council for the Exploration of the Sea) rectangles (c. 25 x 25 km - 625 km2) due to be published by the JNCC. Some initial modelling of distribution data for harbour porpoise had not indicated any discrete areas of concentration. However, the spatial analyses conducted to derive species distributions were at too coarse a scale to detect concentrations. The UK has undertaken to re-examine distribution data for harbour porpoise with a view to site selection for this species. During 2001, a number of new effort-related surveys of harbour porpoise distribution were conducted in Welsh waters.  These included:  1) surveys of southern Cardigan Bay with emphasis upon the Special Area of Conservation established for bottlenose dolphins, conducted by Sea Watch Foundation between May and October and funded by EU INTERREG Programme with support from the Countryside Council for Wales (Baines et al., 2002);  2) a parallel acoustic and visual survey of harbour porpoises in South-west Welsh waters with emphasis west of Pembrokeshire, conducted by Chris Pierpoint with support from the International Fund for Animal Welfare (Pierpoint, 2001); surveys of the waters around Bardsey Island and the Llyn Peninsula, North Wales, conducted by Friends of Cardigan Bay and the Whale and Dolphin Conservation Society (WDCS), with support from CCW; and land-based and offshore surveys off the coast of Anglesey, undertaken by Marine Awareness North Wales, also with support from CCW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17670\tNRW_DS109993\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.547Z\t706\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey of Lakes in the Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt Proposed Site of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSI) Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Special Protection Area (SPA)\t© CNC/NRW 2003 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393933\tArchived as Project No. 30, Media 176 Fireproof safe - Stack 05, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Ffynnon,  Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian: Head Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt Proposed Site of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSI) and Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is situated in North Wales in the Snowdonia National Park. The pSSSI/SAC boundary marks the limit of a large upland block located along the eastern fringe of the National Park. The primary reasons for the site qualifying for SAC status are the large areas of blanket bog, a priority habitat under the Habitats Directive, and the upland European dry heath.  Other qualifying features include two Annex I habitat lake types: (1) oligotrophic to mesotrophic standing waters with vegetation of the Littorelletea uniflorae and/or of the Isoeto-Nanojuncetea, and (2), natural dystrophic lakes and ponds. The limnological surveys (surveys of inland water) focus on the collation of site and catchment data and the collection of new data on lake bathymetry, water quality and macrophyte assemblages. A particular focus is an assessment of the current acidification and conservation status of the four lake sites.  A preliminary assessment of whether the lakes are in favourable condition, as defined in the Habitats Directive, is also outlined. The primary aim of this project was to survey four lakes in the Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt pSSSI/SAC in order to build up CCWs datasets on Welsh lakes.  The information collected is required for conservation site assessments, ecological quality judgements for site condition reporting, contributions to site management plans and assistance with casework issues.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:32:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17653\tNRW_DS109994\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.429Z\t683\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRestoring the Afon Teifi at Cors Caron National Nature Reserve\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393934\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media101to200\\Media179 Fireproof safe - Stack 05, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Ffynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian:  Senior Freshwater Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Afon Teifi is one of the largest rivers in Wales, and is of considerable national and international importance; designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). Approximately 15km from its source, the Teifi runs through the Cors Caron National Nature Reserve (NNR). Within the NNR approximately 1.5km of the Afon Teifi has been altered by straightening and deepening of the river. The northernmost straightening is present on Ordnance Survey maps dating from 1889 whilst the remaining alterations were undertaken in the period 1891 to 1905. The present day aerial photographs show the original meandering channel as sections of open water or vegetated channel. The presence of the original channel has lead to a proposal to restore the river to its former course. A scoping study by the River Restoration Centre has indicated that restoration is a viable option for the Afon Teifi within the NNR. The scheme proposed by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) incorporated an environmental assessment, stakeholder consultation, pre- and post-project monitoring programmes and technical design. This dataset contains the results of the work undertaken to address the scheme\u0027s requirements (Assessment of the current baseline information relating to the existing river morphology and hydrology, the in-channel vegetation and invertebrate fauna, the floodplain and the stakeholder interest). It includes an environmental impact assessment of the proposed restoration and presents a detailed plan of work for the restoration of the Afon Teifi in the NNR with guidance for further research and monitoring.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17651\tNRW_DS110015\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.424Z\t889\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh coastal habitats as calving and nursery grounds for the harbour porpoise (Phocoena phocoena)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303135\tData is held as Project No.65, media 33 Fireproof safe - Stack 01, Hafod Elfyn, CCW Headquarters, Bangor, Wales.\tRob Penrose (CSIP)\tThe results of an investigation into strandings data for evidence that harbour porpoises breed and calve in Welsh coastal waters. Harbour porpoise calves are recorded annually off the Welsh coast from mid-May onwards.  However, published survey data is lacking for the region and the distribution and relative abundance of calves have not been reported. This project present data from harbour porpoise strandings on the Welsh coast collected through the collaborative UK and Celtic Marine Mammal Strandings Project, examines the incidence and seasonality of calf and neonate strandings and compare stranding rates in Wales with England, Scotland and Ireland. The only data associated with this project is held within the report. Raw data is held by CSIP.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17652\tNRW_DS110016\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.394Z\t738\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBreeding Seabird Surveys of South-west Wales Colonies 1996-1998\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303136\tArchived as Project 66, Media 39.\tGIS Service Desk  Senior Reserve Manager, NRW\tSouth-west Wales has nationally and internationally important populations of nine species of seabirds and a total of thirteen regular breeding species. Following the Sea Empress oil spill at Milford Haven in February 1996, a census of seabird colonies in south-west Wales was initiated in order to assess the impact of the oil spill on population of seabirds breeding in the affected area. This dataset is compiled of the results of breeding seabird monitoring at colonies in south-west Wales in 1996, 1997 and 1998, the three seabird breeding seasons following the Sea Empress oil spill of February 1996.  Data collected in all three years of the project have been compared with data collected in previous years, in order to provide an assessment of the impact of the oil spill on local populations of ten species of breeding seabirds (Fulmar, Cormorant, Shag, Lesser Black-backed Gull, Herring Gull, Great Black-backed Gull, Kittiwake, Guillemot, Razorbill and Puffin).  Data collected for monitoring and comparison purposes to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on local populations of breeding seabirds.\tWildlife Trust (West Wales)\t1996-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17655\tNRW_DS110017\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.492Z\t432\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTemporal variation and monitoring of important lagoonal communities and species in Wales\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303137\tData is archived as Project No. 9 and Media No. 153\tInternal Contact: Marine Monitoring Ecologist, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of a synthesis, collation and review of knowledge pertaining to coastal saline lagoon sites in Wales and the important species recorded at these lagoons, undertaken by the Natural History Museum on behalf of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The scope was to understand:  - The habitat resource requirements of the species,  - The scales of natural variation in the species and in their habitat resource requirements, - The scales of natural variation in the habitats themselves, and   - To review monitoring and surveillance work in similar habitats and species. This work will allow the development of Standard Operating Procedures for condition monitoring the lagoons.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17654\tNRW_DS110018\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.577Z\t744\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGeomorphological Response of Welsh Sand Dunes to Sea Level Rise over the Next 100 Years\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303138\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media101to200\\Media177 Fireproof safe - Stack 05, Hafod Elfyn, NRW - Maes y Ffynnon Data is archived Project Number 31 Media Number 177\tInternal Custodian: Senior Coastal Scientist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tResults of an assessment into the impact of predicted of sea level rise and climate change on Welsh dune systems over the next 100 years, with particular emphasis on Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and certain other dune sites of key nature conservation importance. The main purpose was to assess the impact of predictions of sea level rise and climate change on Welsh dune systems over the next 100 years, with particular emphasis on cSAC sites, certain Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and other important nature conservation sites. The specific objectives of the study were to: -  Estimate future erosion/accretion rates and evolution of the dune systems -  Define the extent of sand dune habitat loss -  Assess the natural and anthropogenic constraints that may limit the ability of the dune systems to readjust -  Identify future management requirements and practices that will ensure the maintenance of the sand dune cSAC resource in Wales in terms of both quantity and quality\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:29:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17647\tNRW_DS110019\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.255Z\t875\tUnassessed\tEditable\tField observations on the disturbance of grey seals in North Wales\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303139\tReport and associated data are archived as Project No. 34, Media No\u0027s 76,182-185\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Officer, NRW.\tMany studies quantify the impacts of non-lethal human disturbance on animal behaviour to which animals respond by deploying what are interpreted as anti-predator strategies. These strategies are recognised as diverting time and energy from behaviours that would have occurred in the absence of human influences. In this report, levels of disturbance to grey seals are measured and these take into account the variety of seal vigilance levels as the prelude to / trigger of gross disturbance. Gross disturbance here denotes the stampeding of a seal or seals into the sea, where they were assembled on land, or where seals \u0027crash dive\u0027 when already in the sea.    The purposes of this data capture were to: -  Record the haul-out patterns of grey seals at Henllwyn Bay on Ynys Enlli/Bardsey when subject to no disturbance caused by people, in order to show examples of normal behaviour. To record examples of disturbance caused by human activity on land, in the sea and in boats, at Henllwyn for comparison with \u0027normal\u0027 / undisturbed behaviour.  -  Describe variables in seal behaviour observed over several tide cycles.   -  Outline some aspects of survey design to best measure disturbance.   -  Outline some site-specific management measures designed to reduce the frequency of disturbance events while also respecting the desires of people to have good views of and insights into the behaviour of grey seals at haul-out sites.   -  Provide a short review of papers and reports on seal disturbance at haul-out sites on the shores of the northeast Atlantic. This will examine management measures that have been or are about to be deployed elsewhere and the ways that such measures are being, or will be, monitored.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:47:27.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17649\tNRW_DS110021\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.375Z\t385\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBaseline Survey of Natura 2000 Standing Water Habitats in Wales\t© CNC/NRW 2006 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303231\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media201to300\\Media205 Fireproof safe - Stack 06, Hafod Elyfn, NRW Maes y Ffynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Senior Freshwater Ecologist, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Environment Agency coordinated macrophyte and physico-chemical surveys on over 50 lake sites in England and Wales during 2003, including a number of lake sites within Welsh Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs). In an effort to update existing information on the site condition of these lakes and provide additional information on other Welsh lakes in SACs, former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned surveys on a further 19 lake sites in 2004. This dataset brings together data collected in 2003 and 2004 from 28 Welsh lakes in 11 SACs.  Limnological data collected for the purpose of site condition assessment and to provide a series of lake data reports on each of the 11 specified SACs.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17650\tNRW_DS110022\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.429Z\t857\tValid\tEditable\tZooplankton analysis\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303232\tData is held as Project No. 41, media 94 (File path Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 94) Fireproof safe - Stack 03, Hafod Elfyn, CCW Headquarters, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tPlankton samples were collected from during a sandbank survey at nine sites in four Candidate Special Areas of Conservation (cSAC) using a plankton net positioned above a beam trawl. The zooplankton was analysed my Emu Ltd and found to contain common Irish Sea species.   Classification analysis revealed that four of the sites (Red Wharf Bay, Four Fathom, Helwick and Cardigan) each supported distinct communities based on their faunal composition, and the distinguishing features of each were identified. Samples from the remaining sites did not fall into site-specific groups indicating that the fauna in these areas was less distinct.   Data used to sort and identify the planktonic animals from samples taken as part of the sandbank survey in four SACs along the Welsh coastline.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:48:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17648\tNRW_DS110023\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.346Z\t601\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Sandbank Survey: Fish Stomach Content, Fish Epibenthic Survey, and Data Analysis (2001)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.y.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303233\t[A] Data is archived as project no. 234, media no. 96 and 745 (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 96 and K:\\Archive\\Media 701to800\\Media 745) [B] A copy is stored on the I drive:I:\\MMT video Archive\\Handheld surface video\\20010000 Sandbanks BS media 745.avi   [C] A clip has been extracted from the video, edited and made available on CCW\u0027s YouTube channel. See online resource below. [D] Species data on Marine Recorder (Survey numbers MRCCW30000000008, MRCCW30000000037, MRCCW30000000038).\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tSandbanks are listed as an Annex 1 habitat that requires conservation measures under the European Union Habitats Council of the European Communities (1992). Sandbanks can be categorised either by sediment type or by topography. The different sediments Annex I sandbanks can consist of are sands, gravelly sands and muddy sands. This study was designed to examine the fish and epifaunal biota of sandbanks and formed part of a larger collaborative survey in which the hyperbenthos and infauna were also sampled.  As a result of their unique physical regime, sandbanks may hold their own distinct flora and fauna that is particularly well adapted to such conditions or that uses these habitats in an opportunistic manner (e.g. at slack water). However, the extent to which sandbanks are characterised by a distinct set of taxa remains unknown.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:50:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17646\tNRW_DS110024\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.048Z\t652\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW) Acoustic Habitat Survey (multibeam) of Habitat and Feature Distribution in Pembrokeshire Marine Candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC)\t© CNC/NRW 2002 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303234\tAll data is archived at 102, 392, 515, 516, 517, 518, 519, 520, 954.  Copies are also available in the Pembroke Dock and Skomer offices.  A copy of the georeferenced tifs are also available on the MFSG drive as seamless layers at: S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Pembs Multibeam.   Fireproof safe - Stack 3, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian -  MFSG (NRW) Metadata point of contact - Data Services\tPembrokeshire Marine candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) is a large area including open coast and the Milford Haven Waterway that has been identified as being of European marine conservation importance.  Features of this site include marine species and seabed habitats.  The purpose of the Acoustic Habitat Survey was to provide information on the nature and characteristics of the seabed within defined areas of the Pembrokeshire coastline.  The dataset contains the results of a project to gather topographical and seabed characterisation information to improve the knowledge of the site and to aid in future site management and feature monitoring.  Primary focus- areas of topographical distinction such as reefs, other hard substrata and sandbanks. Areas covered in the Pembrokeshire cSAC survey include; Skomer, Grassholm, Hats \u0026amp; Barrels, Welshmans Bay, East side of Skokholm, St Ann\u0027s Head, Milford Approaches and the majority of the Milford Haven estuary upstream as far as Picton Point.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:50:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17703\tNRW_DS110026\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.576Z\t617\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDevelopment of a Methodology for the Assessment of Cumulative Effects of Marine Activities Using Liverpool Bay as a Case Study (2002)\t© CNC/NRW 2002. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303236\tData held as Project No. 75, media 80\tData Custodian - Maritime Policy Section Head, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tFormer Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and now Natural Resources Wales (NRW), encourages good practice in undertaking Environmental Impact Assessments (EIAs) for developments and activities, including those located in Welsh Territorial waters (up to 12 nm offshore). Experience has shown that EIAs carried out in the marine environment often address impacts of the development in isolation from other similar developments or other developments having similar impacts. A review of environmental statements produced for offshore oil and gas developments found that none addressed cumulative impacts over the projects lifetime or with regards to adjacent existing or proposed developments.  It can therefore be said that cumulative impacts from marine activities have seldom been adequately addressed in EIAs for offshore projects.  The purpose of this data capture is to:  -  Review current understandings of potential cumulative effects from offshore industries and develop a proposed methodology for cumulative effects assessment of offshore industries from a nature conservation and landscape / seascape perspective;   -  Produce a GIS database and analysis tool for offshore Cumulative Effects Assessment (CEA);   -  Identify current and predict likely future cumulative effects from offshore industries in Liverpool Bay on habitats and species of importance; and   -  Identify data gaps, and recommend further data and research requirements for undertaking CEA for offshore activities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:52:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17699\tNRW_DS110027\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.567Z\t620\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCarmarthen Bay: Evolution of Estuarine Morphology and Consequences for Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Management\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2003. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence.  Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303237\t[A] Report is held as Project No. 76, media 93 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 93) [B] Also available on OLIB\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tCarmarthen Bay has a total of four important estuaries comprising the Loughor Estuary and the Taf, Towy and Gwendraeth. Between 16th and 19th century these estuaries lost around 2400 Ha of saltmarsh due to land claim. Between the end of the 19th century and end of the 20th the estuaries gained 1136 Ha. The changes in the saltmarsh environment may impact shellfish, invertebrates and wading birds. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the history of evolution and current trends in the geomorphological development of the estuaries of the then Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC). Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries is now designated fully as an Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for numerous species and habitats including estuaries, mudflats, sand flats and lagoons, which make up 13.7% of the site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:14.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17705\tNRW_DS110029\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.680Z\t421\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSite Condition Assessments of Welsh Standing Water Features (2003 - 2008)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303239\tArchived as Project No. 84, Media No\u0027s 361 and 604.\tInternal Custodian: Freshwater Ecologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe UK Conservation Agencies have devised a Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) protocol (JNCC, 2005) for the routine monitoring and assessment of protected site condition.  The CSM lake monitoring method is based on previous survey protocols used by the conservation agencies. It aims to gather the maximum amount of information relating to the ecology of each water body in the most efficient way possible.  The purpose of this data capture was to provide an assessment of the current conservation status of water features in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:34:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17704\tNRW_DS110035\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.631Z\t579\tUnassessed\tEditable\tResults of a field survey following the Menai Strait oil spill\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303335\tArchived as project number 138 and Media No. 397 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 397)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tFormer Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) were informed at 09:13 on Sunday the 25th June 2006 that there was an oil spill in the Menai Strait. Oil had been reported to the Coastguard at around 08:15.  At the time the information available was that there was an oil/diesel slick, of unknown origin, spreading from Caernarfon up to the Swellies. The tide was flooding at this time with high water around 11:00. The level of the incident was thought to be minor. At 10:25 on 25th June CCW regional officers were on the shore close to the Friction Dynamics factory.  The strandline was reported to be contaminated: cobbles splattered with oil. The level of the incident was considered minor/medium at this time. The purpose of this data capture was to assess contamination of species and habitats following the oil spill on the 25th of June 2006.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17694\tNRW_DS110036\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.565Z\t318\tValid\tEditable\tVegetation Survey of Maritime Cliff and Slopes of the Llyn Peninsula\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright [2000]. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303336\tArchived as project number 139, media number 236.\tInternal Custodian:  Julie Creer, Coastal Ecology Specialist, Evidence and Analysis, NRW Heather Lewis, Coastal Ecology Specialist, Evidence \u0026amp; Analysis, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tMaritime cliff and slopes is a priority habitat within the UK Biodiversity Action Plan. Selected maritime sites on the Llyn Peninsula were surveyed, mapped and evaluated in September/October 2000 and the range of National Vegetation Classification (NVC) vegetation types present characterized.  A number of sections of the UK coastline have been nominated as \u0027Vegetated Sea Cliffs of the Atlantic and Baltic Coasts\u0027 candidate Special Areas of Conservation under the EC Habitats Directive. This survey was undertaken as part of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCWs) commitment to undertake research and survey in order to increase knowledge of the extent and quality of this resource. Selected maritime sites on the Llyn Peninsula:  -  Porth Neigwl (Hell\u0027s Mouth) -  Porth Dinllaen (Borth Wen to Penrhyn Nefyn) -  Porth Nefyn (Penrhyn Nefyn to Penrhyn Bodeilias) -  Porth Pistyll (Penrhyn Bodeilias to the lower slopes of Carreg y Llam)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2000-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T14:06:15.077Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17701\tNRW_DS110037\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.591Z\t454\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBroadscale Remote Survey and Mapping of Sublittoral Habitats and Biota of Milford Haven, Pembrokeshire and South Cardigan Bay\t© CNC/NRW 1999. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303337\t[A]  Data is Archived as Project 141, Media 77, (related Media numbers include 79, 505 and 506) [B]  Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elyfn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tData Custodians - Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group. Metadata Point of Contact - Data Services\tThe Broadscale Mapping Project has been devised and funded by a consortium consisting of the Crown Estate, the Countryside Council for Wales, English Nature, Scottish Natural Heritage and the SeaMap research group based at Newcastle University and undertaken by SeaMap. Three trial areas were selected, around Milford Haven, Pembrokeshire and South Cardigan Bay, for developing and testing the methodology, although experience from many other surveys conducted by SeaMap have also contributed to the development of the methodology. The aim of this data collection was to map the seafloor sediments and biota, for habitats and biotope mapping. This was supplemented by maps and supporting data showing the location of ground truth samples and the positions of the acoustic tracks.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:11:27.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17695\tNRW_DS110038\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.632Z\t921\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey of the Tentacled Lagoon Worm (Alkmaria romijni) at Carew Castle Mill Pond (2006)\t© CNC/NRW 2006 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303338\tThe report and data collected under this project is archived as Project 142, Media 467. Data is also available in Marine Recorder\tInternal Custodian: Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe tentacled lagoon worm (Alkmaria romijni) is a nationally scarce protected species found in lagoonal habitats in the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation. In Wales it has only been recorded from two saline lagoon sites in Pembrokeshire: Neyland Weir Pool (Westfield Pill) and Carew Castle Mill Pond.  Elsewhere in the UK it had been recorded from only a handful of sites in lagoons and estuaries along the English Channel and southern North Sea and internationally between Morocco and the Baltic where it is not uncommon. A. romijni is limited to brackish water habitats and threats to these habitats include agricultural run-off and sewage, coastal development and coastal defence as well as pollution from industrial discharges.  There were three main purposes of this data capture:   -  To establish a repeatable baseline survey of the tentacled lagoon worm (Alkmaria romijni) at Carew Castle Mill Pond.  -  To undertake a spatial survey of salinity at the sediment-water interface  -  To note field observations on the integrity of the inflow/outflow system or any activities at the site that might be considered to have an adverse effect on the integrity of the site as a saline lagoonal habitat.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:13:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17688\tNRW_DS110039\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.511Z\t376\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Rare Red Alga Anotrichium barbatum: UK Species Action Plan and its Implementation (1998 - 1999)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303339\t[A] Archived as project 153, media 251 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 251) [B] Marine Recorder survey (JNCCMNCR40000738).   [C] Paper MNCR forms are held in NRW off-site storage.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe marine red alga Anotrichium barbatum is one of two rare seaweeds in the UK with a Species Action Plan. The alga was detected in Tremadog Bay in 1998. The site was resurveyed in 1999 and Anotrichium barbatum was again recorded. The dataset contains information on the geographical distribution of the alga and density estimates. In 1997 an Action Plan was prepared for English Nature, but downgraded to a Species Statement because there were no known extant UK populations. The discovery of Anotrichium barbatum in Cardigan Bay in 1998 permitted the completion in 1998 of the Action Plan with target. The minimum objective is to stabilize the species\u0027 status i.e. to maintain the net population size and distribution against baseline figures set at the time of Action Plan publication.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2000-02-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:15:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17690\tNRW_DS110042\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.445Z\t497\tUnassessed\tEditable\tModelled biomass, production and species richness of benthic invertebrate communities in Welsh coastal waters\t© CNC/NRW 1999. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303432\tArchived at K:\\Archive\\Media 401to493\\Media493\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW  The PhD was being undertaken by Liz Richardson - former Bangor University\tSoft-sediment benthic invertebrate communities in the coastal zone of Wales were modelled using a size-based model. Biomass, production and species richness were modelled as a function of local environmental conditions and bottom trawling disturbance. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) contributed to a PhD at the School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University entitled. The socio-economic and ecological implications of inshore fisheries management (project FC 73-02-740). The final part of this PhD includes an evaluation of the economic impacts of different types of fisheries management, including No Take Zones (NTZs) and areas where no fishing with towed gears is permitted.  In order to base this economic analysis on realistic scenarios, data was needed concerning the locations which would most benefit from the protection described.  The state of the benthic community was modelled using bottom trawl data up to 2003 and therefore represents the state in 2003. Predictions for a situation without trawling are not time specific. Values represent a yearly average.  The model that was used was validated for the North Sea and explained 39% of the observed variation there. No data was available for validation of model predictions in the Irish Sea.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17698\tNRW_DS110058\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.582Z\t490\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Commercial Shipping Occurring in and around Skomer\t© CNC/NRW 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303538\tSkomer MNR office,West Region drive/shared docs/data/commercial/Tankers\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Data Services Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. This dataset consists of records of all the Tankers using St brides bay including details of record numbers, names and movements of vessels. The purpose of this data capture was to document the usage of St Brides Bay as a Tanker anchoring area.  Information may also provide context for any changes in the habitat and species composition in the area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:15:48.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17700\tNRW_DS110059\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.589Z\t716\tValid\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Marine Monitoring Programme Overview\t© CNC/NRW 2013. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale. Non senstive data and published data on the NBN Atlas is released under and OGL.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303539\t[A]  Species data held in Marine Recorder  [B]  Data held at Skomer Office on   \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\shared documents\\data\\biological data\\ [C]  \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\docs and settings\\Monitoring\\ [D] SOPs held in the DMS   This data is now available on the NBN Atlas  back ups of Video library \u0026amp; CDs in off-site storage: CCW/WRPB/025\tPhil Newman, Senior Marine Conservation Officer, Skomer Marine Conservation Zone\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ).  Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.   The Skomer Marine Monitoring Team has focused on developing a range of projects to monitor the health of the reserve and investigate the relevant aspects of the species life histories for baseline information.  Large scale surveys developed in the 1970\u0027s and 1980\u0027s provided the foundation for the monitoring.     This metadata details general monitoring on Skomer, each datasets has its own metadata entry.  Monitoring is undertaken to provide an assessment of the condition of features against set targets and will contribute to reporting on the MNR as well as Pembrokeshire Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) within which the reserve lies.  Data is collected around Skomer Island and on the mainland between Musselwick Sands and Gateholm Island within the Marine Nature Reserve.  Data has been collected from 1975 onwards. An extensive review of images has been completed to determine the quantity and quality of information that could be extracted from these images. Species data has been collated from a number of surveys entered into Marine Recorder which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-11-12T14:15:39.017Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17702\tNRW_DS110060\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.542Z\t425\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Commercial Fishing Activities Occurring in and around Skomer\t© CNC/NRW 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303630\tSkomer MNR office,West Drive drive/shared docs/data/commercial/potting\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. The purpose of this data capture was to document the usage of the MNR and to create GIS maps of fishing effort and types of gear used in the reserve. The dataset contains records of all the fishing activity in the reserve.  This includes potting effort, position and length of strings of pots, position of nets, number of boats using the area and number of dredgers in the St Brides area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-03-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:10:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17691\tNRW_DS110061\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.533Z\t561\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Scarlet and Gold Cup Coral Balanophyllia regia\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303631\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/cup corals ,  Images - dell1590 C/doc and settings/MONITORING/balanophyllia\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Cup corals are slow growing filter feeders, which are susceptible to changes in water quality and planktonic food supply.  Scarlet and gold star-coral, Balanophyllia regia, is a Lusitanian species and Skomer MNR is close to the northern edge of its range in the UK. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor Scarlet and Gold cup coral.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:04:04.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17697\tNRW_DS110062\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.566Z\t805\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Macrophyte Condition Assessment for the Afon Gwyrfai SAC (2007)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2008. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence.  Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303632\tThe report and data collected are archived as Project 198 (Media 545) and are stored in fireproof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Fynnon.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage.  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media545\tInternal Custodian: Rivers Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Afon Gwyrfai Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Llyn Cwellyn Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) was notified in 2003 and is considered to be one of the best examples in the United Kingdom of an oligotrophic to mesotrophic river with submerged beds of aquatic plants, especially its Callitricho-Batrachion vegetation community.  The purpose of this data capture was to undertake a Condition Assessment of the river macrophyte and river macrophyte-related attributes of the former Countryside Council for Wales CCW\u0027s Management Units 2, 5, 6 and 7 of the Afon Gwyrfai.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-05-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:11.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17696\tNRW_DS110063\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.589Z\t372\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Devonshire Cup Coral Caryophyllia smithii\t© CNC/NRW 2009. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303633\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/cup corals ,  Images - dell1590 C/doc and settings/MONITORING/cup corals\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. Cup corals are slow growing filter feeders, which are susceptible to changes in water quality and planktonic food supply. Caryophyllia smithii is a common feature of the sub-littoral benthic community of south-western Britain. Counts of cup corals have been done since 1993 using the images collected from the sponge assemblage project.  The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the population for changes in densities and to look for evidence of recruitment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:04:44.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17689\tNRW_DS110064\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.523Z\t648\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInitial hydroacoustic mapping of macrophyte communities in the southern end of Llyn Tegid\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303634\tThe report and data collected under CCW contract FC 73-02-304 and is archived as Project 196 (Media 542) and is stored in fireproof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Ffynnon on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage. H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media542\tInternal Custodian:  Rhian Thomas, Team Leader WFD, Water Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset is made up of the results of a hydroacoustic survey to map the extent of submerged macrophyte communities and major substrate types in the southern end of Llyn Tegid.  The objectives of the present part of the project were to use hydroacoustics to map the current extent of submerged macrophyte communities and major substrate types in the southern end of Llyn Tegid and to present the findings of such work at the Countryside Council for Wales Marine and Freshwater Workshop 2007. This works was undertaken as a small work package added during the summer of 2007 to a much larger project concerned with the hydroacoustic survey and translocation of the gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus).\tSnowdonia National Park Authority-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-02-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17687\tNRW_DS110067\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.359Z\t321\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Modelling for Llangorse Lake\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303637\tArchived as project number 202, media number 567 H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media567\tInternal Custodian: Senior Freshwater Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tLlangorse Lake is the largest natural eutrophic lake in Wales and has been designated as a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The lake has a history of eutrophication and so it was decided to undertake a nutrient modelling and nutrient budget exercise to determine the size and sources of nutrient loads entering the lake. Llangorse Lake has a history of eutrophication problems believed to have been caused by high nutrient loads entering the lake from the surrounding catchment. This study aimed to determine the size and main sources of those loads. Specific objectives include: -  To review existing data and information on the water quality of Llangorse Lake. -  To conduct a field study to investigate the nutrient budget of the lake. -  To model the nutrient delivery from the catchment to the lake, with particular focus on phosphorus delivery, to give an indication of source apportionment. -  To prepare a draft catchment management plan.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:17:59.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17693\tNRW_DS110068\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.553Z\t379\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Dee Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Comparative Macrophyte Surveys (2008)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303638\tThe report and data collected are archived as Project 211 as Media 576 and are stored in fireproof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Ffynnon. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage.\tInternal Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group. NRw  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned ECUS Ltd. to undertake macrophyte survey work on the River Dee Special Area of Conservation (SAC) to inform a larger project aiming to determine the main variability components for macrophyte communities in rivers. The objective of the larger project, a partnership between CCW, Natural England, the Environment Agency (EA), Scottish Environmental Protection Agency (SEPA) and Scotland and Northern Ireland Forum For Environmental Research (SNIFFER), is to develop and test a statistically robust, standardised field sampling method for river macrophytes, to be used by the agencies for assessment of ecological and conservation status of rivers.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17684\tNRW_DS110069\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.936Z\t281\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of the Impact of Hydraulic Cockle Dredging on the Macroinvertebrate Faunas of Traeth Lafan (1993-1994)\t© CCGC/CCW 1995. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and CCW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303639\t[A] Data is held as Report  (project?) No.184, media 523 (File path: K\\:Archive\\Media 501-600\\Media 523 [B] Survey data in Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000693)\tMetadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW Internal Contact: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis dataset contains the results of a macroinvertebrate and sediment survey conducted post hydraulic cockle dredging around Traeth Lafan, North Wales. The purpose of this data collection was to study the effects of hydraulic suction dredging on benthic invertebrate macrofaunas, substrate conformation and sediment composition on Traeth Lafan. The survey was located at Traeth Lafan, the northern entrance to the Menai Strait (Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC)).  A survey of the macroinvertebrates and sediments of Traeth Lafan, North Wales, was undertaken following extensive hydraulic cockle dredging in autumn 1993. The survey covered the site allocated for dredging and part of the hand gathering area, which was used as \u0027control\u0027, against which changes on the dredged ground could be compared. A follow up survey, using the same sampling grid and rationale, was carried out twelve months later, in November 1994, to ascertain the recovery state of the area after a full season\u0027s recruitment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1995-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:06:17.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17692\tNRW_DS110070\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.551Z\t435\tValid\tEditable\tAcross Wales Drop-Down Video Monitoring 2007 - 2010\t© CNC/NRW 2010. Non-sensitive data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303730\t[A] Data archived as project no. 35, media 1328.   (K:\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1328) - older data is held in media no. 175. [B]  Fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon on optical media.  [C]  Videos are maintained in the archive within multiple Media folders (see Archive inventory) but an index can be accessed through GIS layers X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine habitat video points\tCharles Lindenbaum, Specialist (Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer), Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Marine Archive: Monica Jones, Data Services Team Leader\tThe aim of this data capture was to report on the conservation status of the habitats and species within Wales\u0027 marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and enable reporting of their favourable conservation status as well as to inform site management.  Drop-down video surveys of SACs across Wales. Works is carried out by Royal Haskoning under contract to CCW and focuses on Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC and Pembrokeshire Marine SAC.    This project also aims to improve design and implementation of the project, harmonise the work in SACs with Water Framework Directive monitoring in overlapping areas and develop and refine Quality Control and Quality Assurance for drop-down video techniques.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:07:13.188Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17683\tNRW_DS110072\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.970Z\t419\tValid\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Littoral Communities Macro scale\t© CNC/NRW 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303732\tImages -  \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\docs and settings\\Monitoring\\littoral view points ,   SOP - DMS:  (Location to be provided by Helen Carby)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.   This data documents gross changes in shores and shore communities by means of viewpoint photography; identify shore or parameters requiring further or specific monitoring. The purpose of this data capture was to document gross scale changes in zonation and beach profiles.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:07:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17686\tNRW_DS110073\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.316Z\t594\tValid\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Recreational Activities Occurring in and around Skomer\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303733\tImages -  \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\docs and settings\\Monitoring\\littoral view points ,   SOP - DMS:  (Location to be provided by Helen Carby)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.    Records of all the recreational activities occurring in and around Skomer MNR. Including; diving, angling, canoe trips, boating activity and any incidents. The purpose of this data capture was to document the usage of the MNR.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:08:51.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17682\tNRW_DS110074\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.859Z\t715\tValid\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Grey Seals Halichoerus grypus\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303734\tData - Skomer MNR, West Region drive \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\shared documents\\data\\biological data\\seals ,  Images -  \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\docs and settings\\Monitoring\\Seals ,   SOP - DMS:  (Location to be provided by Helen Carby) Off-site boxes of the original paper data CCW/WRPB/003 \u0026amp; 004 (1991-1994 data)\tInternal Custodian: Kate Lock, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Internal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.   Grey seals are a protected species of conservation importance, which live and breed in the Skomer MNR. The West Wales population is the largest in SW Britain and is a feature of the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC).   The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the number and survival rate of seal pups born in the MNR.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:09:27.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17685\tNRW_DS110082\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.971Z\t694\tValid\tEditable\tSpatial Analysis of Intertidal and Subtidal Biotopes in Welsh Waters\t© CNC/NRW 2008. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303832\tArchived as project 203, media number 568 on backed up server-based storage at the Countryside Council for Wales (File path is Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media501to600\\Media568)\tInternal Custodian: NRW Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tMaps depicting the distribution of marine biotopes are now available for the whole of the Welsh intertidal and most of the seabed in Welsh Territorial Waters.  The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Phase 1 intertidal database provides the former; and the CCW HABMAP database provides the latter.    Using those databases, while taking account of their limitations, CCW wish to know the proportions of each major biotope group that lies within protected areas  - i.e. Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSIs) and Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).  This report provides a spatial analysis of those biotope data. Various data issues mean that these are only very rough calculations; the percentages are not definitive values.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-09-11\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:10:23.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17680\tNRW_DS110095\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.693Z\t663\tValid\tEditable\tSkomer Island National Nature Reserve (NNR) - Resurvey of Lichen Quadrats\t© CNC/NRW 1997. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303935\tArchived at Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media044.    Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW MyF Maes y Fynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset is made up of the results from a survey of lichen distribution and cover on Skomer Island following the Sea Empress oil spill. Pembrokeshire is of the highest significance for coastal saxicolous and terricolous lichens and Skomer is a key site in Countryside Council for Wales’ (CCW\u0027s) lichen monitoring programme.   After the Sea Empress oil spill SEEEC decided the Skomer lichens should be re-surveyed. Record type, distribution and extent of lichens within the 40 quadrats established on Skomer Island. Compare these records with those from earlier surveys and determine the nature of any changes that have taken place. Interpret any changes as far as possible, with special consideration given to those that may be attributable to oil pollution. Re-mark quadrats by indicating their precise position.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:11:18.114Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17679\tNRW_DS110096\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.675Z\t672\tValid\tEditable\tDevelopment and Field Trials of a Drop-Down Video System (2003)\t© CNC/NRW 2004. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303936\tZ:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media101to200\\Media162\tNRW Marine Monitoring Team, NRW. (original project commissioned by Rohan Holt - Marine and Freshwater Ecosystem Group)\tEnvision Mapping was contracted by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to develop a purpose built drop-down video system suitable for marine monitoring work. Detailed specifications were prepared and the system was manufactured by C-Technics, a specialist underwater video systems manufacturer. The system was trialled during the manufacturing process and modifications made prior to the system being completed. Upon completion the system was commissioned at three locations in Wales. This tested the system in field conditions for both operational suitability and also enabled the system to be modified to best suit the requirements of CCW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:13:12.078Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17681\tNRW_DS110097\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.782Z\t480\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSediment shore impact assessment- monitoring: strandline fauna\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303937\t[A] Report is archived as Project No. 14, Media No. 40 (K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 040) [B] Fireproof safe - Stack 01, Hafod Elfyn, NRW-Maes y Fynnon, Bangor, Wales. [C] CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (Survey number:MRCCW10000000078)\tInternal Contact: Master Reference Data Team Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe present study investigates the effects of the Sea Empress oil spill on the invertebrate populations of the strandline on two selected soft sediment shores, Pembrey and Pendine, in Carmarthen Bay, South Wales.  It represents one of four sampling protocols initially proposed by University of Wales, Swansea (March, 1996) and is the protocol selected by CCW (September, 1996).  A comparison can be made between the findings for one of the areas studied (Pembrey) with the results of a previous study on the strandline fauna of the same area (Llewellyn, 1996). Data was used to investigate the effects of the Sea Empress oil spill on the invertebrate populations of strandlines.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17678\tNRW_DS110098\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.695Z\t348\tValid\tEditable\tAppraisal of Environmental Impact and Recovery Using Laminaria Holdfast Faunas\t© CNC/NRW 1999.There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303938\tThe report and data are held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 15, media No.41 (pathway: Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media041).   Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW MyF Headquarters, Bangor, Wales.  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder. [MRCCW10000000093]\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team, NRW.   Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Sea Empress spill provided an opportunity to test the applicability of Laminaria holdfast faunal communities for detecting the pollution effects of a putative oil contamination gradient, and to assess the immediate response and consequent recovery of components of the holdfast fauna to oil pollution.   Data captured to test the applicability of Laminaria holdfast faunal communities for detecting the pollution effects of a putative oil contamination gradient, and to assess the immediate response and consequent recovery of components of the holdfast fauna to oil pollution.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:12:44.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17677\tNRW_DS110099\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.567Z\t350\tValid\tEditable\tStatus and Breeding of Cormorants Phalacrocorax carbo in Wales (1996-1997): The Effect of the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303939\tReports are archived as Project No\u0027s. 17 and 115, and Media No\u0027s. 49 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media049) and 383 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media301to400\\Media383).   Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW- Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.\tInternal Contact: Marine Monitoring Team Leader (was Bill Sanderson at the point of collection, now Lucie Haines), NRW Marine Ornithologist, NRW  (Matty Murphy)  Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tThe Sea Empress Environmental Evaluation Committee (SEEEC) and the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned various studies of the effects of the oil spill on the wildlife of Pembrokeshire. This dataset contains the results of work carried out to investigate the effects of the spill on Cormorants Phalacrocorax carbo.    In 1996 the project aimed to carry out a survey of the numbers, distribution and breeding performance of cormorants breeding in Wales, including detailed case studies at colonies in the area contaminated by oil in south and west Pembrokeshire.  Monitoring of numbers and breeding performance was continued in 1997 with detailed studies of breeding phenology and breeding success at two South Pembrokeshire colonies (St Margaret\u0027s Island and Green Scar).   Data was used to assess the numbers and distribution of Cormorants breeding in Wales; and to investigate the breeding performance of Cormorants in Wales, including detailed case studies at colonies in the area contaminated by oil in south and west Pembrokeshire.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:14:08.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17676\tNRW_DS110100\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.409Z\t314\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOrigins, population structure, pathology and diet samples of diver and auk casualties of the Sea Empress oil spill\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313030\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media001to100\\Media055 Data is stored within the report. Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elfyn, CCW Headquarters, Bangor\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy, MFSG, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset provides data on the examination of dead oiled seabirds from southwest Wales collected after the February 1996 Sea Empress oil spill in order to assess impact and damage done by the spill. Data was collected in order to determine origins, pathology and diet of casualties (seabirds affected by the Sea Empress oil spill).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17733\tNRW_DS110101\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.783Z\t332\tValid\tEditable\tThe Impact of the Sea Empress Oil Spill on the Prosobranch Mollusc Paludinella littorina\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313031\tReports are held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 21 and Media No. 56 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media056).   Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.  NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder [MRCCW10000000089]\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tIn March 1996 the Sea Empress Environmental Evaluation Committee (SEEEC) was set up by the government to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on the environment.  This contract was issued as part of the SEEEC monitoring programme designed to evaluate the impact of oil from the Sea Empress on local marine life.   Data collected in order to ascertain whether Paludinella littorina still lived at its known sites at Broad Haven and on Caldey Island after the Sea Empress oil spill and to obtain more information on the extent and density of the populations. The objectives were to:   1.  Ascertain whether Paludinella littorina is still living at its known sites at Broad Haven and on Caldey Island and to obtain more information on the extent and density of the populations.  2.  Attempt an assessment of the impact of oil damage, if any and subsequent recovery, to habitats at known Paludinella littorina sites.  3.  Locate and investigate other sites for Paludinella littorina on Caldey Island.  4.  Evaluate methods for monitoring and recommend a protocol for future monitoring work.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:15:47.384Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17726\tNRW_DS110102\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.485Z\t478\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAutecological study of Thick Top Shell, Osilinus lineatus (Monodonta lineata), in the area affected by the Sea Empress oil spill (1996 and 1997).\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313032\tThe report is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 23 and Media No. 62 (File path K:\\Media 001to100\\Media 62) Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor,\tMetadata Point of Contact: Data Services Team, NRW Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tAfter the oil spill from the Sea Empress investigations were initiated into the biota of many of the habitats affected. One of the species known to be susceptible to oil pollution, that had been studied previously and that would not be adequately investigated by the proposed investigations was the toothed or thick top shell Osilinus lineatus, an animal commonly found on shores in western Britain. Data collected in order to establish the population age and size structure at each study site and allow an assessment of the impact the Sea Empress oil spill has had on these populations of Osilinus linaetus.  As of 1 April 2013, the Countryside Council for Wales, Environment Agency Wales and Forestry Commission Wales became Natural Resources Wales / Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17723\tNRW_DS110103\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.495Z\t338\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommon scoter Melanitta nigra monitoring in Carmarthen Bay following the Sea Empress oil spill.\t© CNC/NRW 1997. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313033\tReport is held as Project No. 24, media 70.\tInternal Custodian: Patrick Lindley, Maritime Ornithologist, Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tFollowing the Sea Empress oil spill, a monitoring programme was immediately established by the Countryside Council for Wales to record numbers and distribution of Common Scoters in Carmarthen Bay. Scoters generally prefer feeding in waters less than 10 m deep and previous aerial surveys in Carmarthen Bay have shown that most birds feed inshore of the 10 m depth contour which extends some 6-10 km offshore.  The purpose of this data capture was to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on Common Scoter numbers and distribution in Camarthen Bay.  There is high confidence in this dataset as surveys undertaken by experienced staff members. However no spatial reference were provided with data for statistical analysis. It is assumed for the purpose of the metadata that samples were collected using British National Grid.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17734\tNRW_DS110113\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.788Z\t423\tValid\tEditable\tStatus of Breeding Common Scoter Melanitta nigra nigra in Britain and Ireland in 1996: The Effect of the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313133\tReport is held as Project No. 25, media 71 (File path: Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media071),   Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW MyF Headquarters, Bangor\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW (At the point of collection was Bill Sanderson, now Lucie Haines) Metadata Contact: Data services Team\tFollowing concerns regarding potential effects of the Sea Empress oil spill in Carmarthen Bay, February 1996, on the British and Irish populations of breeding Common Scoters Melanitta nigra nigra, their status and distribution were determined from a survey in May - June 1996.  The survey was undertaken in five areas (Shetland, the Flow country, West and South Scotland, Islay and Ireland), using methods devised during the first complete British and Irish census of this species in 1995.  Survey effort was concentrated on sites that held Common Scoters in 1995, and on sites with historical records.    The survey visited 223 sites and located 376 birds.  This was 79 fewer birds than the previous year, a 17% decline in numbers (16% in Ireland and 19% in Britain), a 19% decrease in occupied 10-km squares and a 31% decrease in occupied sites, compared to visits made at a similar time in 1995.  It is possible that a decline of this magnitude may be within the range of natural population fluctuations or it could be due to the late arrival of birds after the late spring in 1996.  However, over the last eight years, the number of Common Scoters in the Flow country has declined by 42% from an estimated 48 pairs in 1988 to only 28 in 1996.  Given the Common Scoter\u0027s threatened status in the UK, that the dynamics of the population are unknown, and the potential decline, annual monitoring of both numbers and productivity is a high priority. Data used to assess the potential effects of the Sea Empress oil spill in Carmarthen Bay, February 1996, on the British and Irish populations of breeding Common Scoters Melanitta nigra nigra.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1996-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:16:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17722\tNRW_DS110118\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.533Z\t396\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommon scoter (Melanitta nigra) Monitoring in Carmarthen Bay following the Sea Empress Oil Spill. April 1997 to March 1998.\t© CNC/NRW  1998. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313138\tReport is held as Project No. 26, media 72\tInternal Custodian: - Patrick Lindley Maritime Ornithologist, NRW - Mike Camplin Team Leader, Specialist Monitoring, NRW.  Metadata contact: - Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Common Scoter Melanitta nigra is listed in Annex II/2 of the EC Directive on the Conservation of Wild Birds (79/409/EEC), and occurs on the red list of the UK\u0027s Species of Conservation Concern. Carmarthen Bay, southwest Wales, is by far the most important wintering site in Wales. On 24 February 1996, large amounts of oil from the stricken Sea Empress tanker entered Carmarthen Bay.  Following the oil spill, a monitoring programme was immediately established by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to record numbers and distribution of Common Scoters in Carmarthen Bay. Data collected to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on common scoter populations in Carmarthen Bay, Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1998-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:14:03.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17731\tNRW_DS110119\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.717Z\t356\tValid\tEditable\tSandy Shore Meiofauna of South West Wales-Impact of the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t© CNC/NRW 1997. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313139\tReport and data is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 67, media No. 43 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media043),  Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.   NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder [MRCCW10000000072]\tNRW Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Seems more likely that Kirsten Ramsay or Matt Green would be a suitable contact\tThe high species diversity of meiofaunal communities imply a complex and intricate ecological web. However, relatively little information has been recorded on the ecological interactions of meiofauna. This is largely due to the practical difficulties involved in the study of these organisms. These difficulties arise from the small body size of meiofauna, their cryptic habitat and the taxonomic difficulties caused by their high species diversity. Various authors have suggested that meiofauna are potentially useful indicators of anthropogenic perturbations of the marine environment (Heip, 1980; Moore and Bett, 1989; Platt and Warwick, 1980; Vincx and Heip, 1991). This data was captured with the aim of assessing the impact of pollution from the Sea Empress oil spill on the structure and composition of meiofaunal communities of intertidal sandy beaches.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-07-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:17:51.308Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17719\tNRW_DS110120\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.536Z\t732\tValid\tEditable\tThe Abundance and Distribution of Waterfowl Within Milford Haven After the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t© CNC/NRW 1997. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313230\tReport and data are held within the Marine Arcive as Project No. 69, Media No\u0027s. 45-47  (File Path: Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media045 ,  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media046 ,  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media047)  Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW MyF Headquarters, Bangor\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy Marine Ornithologist, NRW. /  Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW (now Lucie Haines).  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis study was put in place to assess whether the oil spill was to impact the nationally important waterfowl populations found in the area of Milford Haven.   Two polluted (Angle Bay and Pembroke River) and two relatively unpolluted \u0027control\u0027 sites (the Carew/Cresswell and Cleddau Rivers) were monitored intensively over the three winters after the oil spill. The four sites have been monitored following standard Wetland Bird Survey (WeBS) methodology to assess the numbers of waterfowl present, and following standard British Trust for Ornithology all-day (across the tidal cycle) count methodology to assess the feeding areas most used.   The data was captured to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on waterfowl populations in the Milford Haven area.   Counts during winter took place between November and March as the estuary is most important for birds during the winter period.  The migration and early autumn periods have been excluded because numbers of birds can fluctuate widely from year to year, for reasons not associated with the estuary.  Each site was counted twice a month with one count on a spring tide and one on a neap tide where possible.  In the first year, only one count was carried out at each site in November. Historical data was used from 1982-1999.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2000-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:19:31.020Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17729\tNRW_DS110121\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.547Z\t486\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOil contamination of gannets and their nests on Grassholm subsequent to the sea Empress oil spill.\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313231\tReport is held in the Marine Archive as Project No. 70 and Media No. 48.  Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy, Marine Ornithologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tGrassholm, situated 23 km WNW of the entrance to Milford Haven, supports the worlds third largest gannetry with an estimated population of 30,000 pairs.  Gannet nests are perennial constructions 30-60 cm high, built out of seaweed, grass and items of floating debris collected from the sea.  Almost all gannet nests, therefore, contain man-made materials, often in substantial quantities. Dataset contains results of investigations to assess whether nest material contaminated with oil from the Sea Empress was incorporated into gannet nests on Grassholm and, if so, whether there was any effect on fertility or productivity in contaminated nests. At the time of the Sea Empress incident it is estimated that 80 percent of the adult breeding population of gannets on Grassholm had returned to the colony.  On a ship based survey between the 25-29/2/96, immediately after the spill, only 0.39 percent of 1,279 gannets recorded showed some degree of oiling, and a similar percentage were observed carrying nest material.  The small proportion carrying nest material suggests that at that time they had not started nest building.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1998-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17730\tNRW_DS110124\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.570Z\t589\tValid\tEditable\tCondition of Crustose Coralline Red Algae in Pembrokeshire After the Sea Empress Disaster\t© CNC/NRW 1997. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313234\tData are held within the Marine Archive as Project No.71, and Media No. 60 (File Path: Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media060) Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.   NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (Survey Number MRCCW10000000074)\tMetadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW  Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tFollowing the Sea Empress oil spill observations on the shores of West Angle Bay and Bluck\u0027s Pool and The Pole at Castlemartin showed that some populations of crustose coralline algae had become bleached and white. Collections were made at this time and during the following year to determine the nature of the bleaching and to follow the recovery, or otherwise, of these plants.   The purpose of this data capture was to determine the nature of the bleaching in crustose coralline algae and to follow the recovery, or otherwise, of these plants.    Two Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) were chosen for regular monitoring, the north shore of West Angle Bay 510 41.8\u0027 N 50  06.5\u0027 W and Bluck\u0027s Pool and The Pole, Castlemartin at 510 38\u0027 N  50 03\u0027 W. Further observations and specimens were obtained from Blaise Bullimore on Skomer (510 44\u0027 N 50 17\u0027 W), Manorbier (510  38.5\u0027 N 40 48.35\u0027 W), Worm\u0027s Head, Gower (510 33.5\u0027 N 40 19\u0027 W), Porth Trecastell, Anglesey (53 0 12\u0027 N 40 25.9\u0027 W)  and Bardsey Island (520 45\u0027 N 40 45\u0027W).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:21:18.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17725\tNRW_DS110125\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.488Z\t906\tValid\tEditable\tSea Empress Rocky Shore Impact Assessment/Monitoring: Permanent Transects and Rock Pool Studies\t© CNC/NRW 1998. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313235\tData are held with the Marine Archive as Project No. 72 and Media No. 63 and 64 Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.. Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media063 ,  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media064\tContact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThree permanent intertidal transects in the vicinity of the Dale Fort Field Centre were established immediately following the Sea Empress oil spill. Marine communities at these sites and in rock pools at a further site were surveyed at intervals up to fourteen months post spill. Data was collected in order to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on the rocky shore.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1998-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:22:25.408Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17720\tNRW_DS110126\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.536Z\t740\tValid\tEditable\tStudies and Biometric Gut Content Analyses of Common Scoter Melanitta nigra nigra Killed During the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313236\tDraft Reports are held as archive Project No. 73, media 68 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media068).    Final Reports are held in the DMS\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Ornothologist (Matty Murphy?) or NRW. Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW (Mike Camplin or Lucie Haines?).  Work was originally commissioned by Bill Sanderson. Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tCarmarthen Bay is the most important wintering site for Common Scoters in the UK. It is estimated that the Sea Empress oil spill in February 1996 resulted in the deaths of at least 4,500 Common Scoter. This dataset contains the results of a study to describe the population structure, body condition, level of oiling, biometrics, moult, diet and cause of death of Common Scoters killed by the Sea Empress oil spill, and to investigate factors affecting body condition, diet and cause of death. Data captured in order to describe the biometrics and moult of Common Scoters killed in the Sea Empress oil spill, to determine the age structure of killed birds, and to determine the diet of birds through gut contents analysis.  All data is held within the reports.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:23:20.041Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17732\tNRW_DS110140\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.757Z\t737\tValid\tEditable\tAssessment of Sublittoral Epibenthic Rock Communities and Species Following the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t© CNC/NRW 1997 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313430\tArchived at Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media052 .   Marine Recorder survey JNCCMNCR40000777\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning\tThe Sea Empress oil spill in February 1996 affected the water column, seabed and shores of south Pembrokeshire.  Several rocky subtidal sites in and around the entrance to Milford Haven were studied at the time of the spill. This datasets is made up of the results obtained during a subtidal survey of South Pembrokeshire 16 months after the Sea Empress oil spill. The survey aimed to examine the open coast of South Pembrokeshire sites close the entrances of Milford Haven, as far eastwards as Caldey Island.  The objective of this work was to revisit previously surveyed bedrock areas which may be have been subjected to elevated water column hydrocarbon load in order to investigate their community diversity, richness and condition.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:24:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17728\tNRW_DS110145\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.554Z\t584\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGreater Horseshoe Bat (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum) survey of the Castlemartin bat cave following the grounding of the Sea Empress\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313435\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media369\tWest Region Senior Reserve Manager, NRW (The Senior Reserve Managers in NRW include Graham Williams, Hywel Roberts, Emyr Humphreys, Huw Green, Rhodri Dafydd)\tThe Castlemartin coast holds the most important hibernacula for Greater Horseshoe Bats, Rhinolophus ferrumequinum in Wales. Following the oil spill from the Sea Empress, oil was dispersed along and deposited on the Castlemartin coast and concerns were raised about the contamination of Castlemartin bat cave. This dataset contains the results of surveys carried out to check for the presence of oil and bats at the cave. The purpose of this data capture was to determine whether oil pollution from the Sea Empress was adversely affecting the bats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17724\tNRW_DS110159\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.450Z\t388\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of Lichen Communities at Stackpole National Nature Reserve (NNR)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313539\tArchived at K:\\archive\\Media301to400\\Media372\tInternal Custodian 1: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Internal Custodian 2: Paul Culyer, Senior Officer Land Management, Conservation Land Management Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tStackpole Warren is noted for its communities of calcicolous terricolous lichens which occupy heavily rabbit-grazed ground with a poor cover of vascular plants. However, available evidence suggests that the amount of open ground and short grazed turf at Stackpole Warren has been decreasing for at least 3 decades. The purpose of this data capture was to examine the terricolous lichen sites at Stackpole Warren for signs of damage which might be attributable to oil pollution from the Sea Empress; to extend monitoring coverage of the terricolous lichen sites at Stackpole National Nature Reserve (NNR); and to assess the success of management in maintaining the notable species and communities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:14:04.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17727\tNRW_DS110174\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.500Z\t375\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Habitat Video Clips Available Through YouTube\t© CNC/NRW 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313734\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine_Habitat_videos ,  GIS subset data for video published on YouTube is only available in the Legacy CCW Archive for MapInfo format data: M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\Marine_Habitat_Videos ,  Video files held on NRW You Tube page.\tContact Charles Lindenbaum, Specialist (Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer), Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting for information about dropdown video surveys\tDrop down video and hand held video clips were produced to illustrate the seabed environment and its flora and fauna across Special Areas of Conservations (SACs).   Edited clips have been produced in order to provide access for Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRWs) staff to relevant underwater video.  By disseminating the data through YouTube, it becomes available to external audiences and provides an illustration of NRWs work and to increase awareness of the marine environment in Wales.    These videos were produced and filmed by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) which is now part of NRW. The GIS Dataset shows the location of these video surveys.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-03-01\tpublication\tnull\thttps://www.youtube.com/@countrysidecouncil/videos\t2025-03-07T16:40:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17721\tNRW_DS110179\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.534Z\t381\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBaseline survey for the Afon Teifi restoration project\t© CNC/NRW 2008 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313739\tArchived as project number 197 (media number 544) H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media544\tInternal Custodian:  Rivers Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Afon Teifi is the longest river to have its course entirely within Wales. Its conservation value is high, the entire river having Special Area of Conservation (SAC) status. Not far below its source in Teifi Pools, the river traverses the Cors Caron National Nature Reserve, meandering its way between the raised bogs for which the reserve is renowned. Parts of this sinuous course were canalized about a century ago, creating sections of unusually straight and deep channel between steep clay banks. It is proposed to restore these reaches of the river to their original course, turning the canalized sections into new lentic water bodies. This dataset includes the results of a baseline survey conducted in 2006-2007 which focussed on sediments, invertebrates and fish. Sediment sampling was conducted to determine the extent of metal contamination of the material that would be excavated in the course of the restoration. The purpose of this data capture is to contribute to the evidence base that would be essential to completing a fully documented restoration project. Sediment sampling was conducted to determine the extent of metal contamination of the material that would be excavated in the course of the restoration. The risk of metal contamination exists due to historic mining in the upper part of the catchment. Invertebrate sampling aimed to establish the potential for improvement of the river\u0027s conservation value by comparing the invertebrate assemblages of the uncanalized reaches with the canalized sections. It also aimed to establish the conservation value of the existing pond in the original channel, providing both a measure of the potential loss and a benchmark for the development of the replacement ponds in the canalized sections post-restoration. Survey of fish in an upstream uncanalized reach and in two canalized sections aimed to provide a baseline for the assessment of the effect of restoration on the suitability of the river for fish.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:39:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17735\tNRW_DS110182\t2024-05-28T12:30:03.120Z\t897\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFluvial Audit of the Upper Usk Tributaries\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313832\tArchived as Project 59, Media 354.   Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media301to400\\Media354 Also archived in CCW legacy GIS drive (M drive) Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\hydrology\\Hydrology_Reports\\fluvial_morphology\tSue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, NRW (Formerly Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, NRW)\tThe results of a Fluvial Geomorphical Audit of the Upper Usk tributaries. The Usk system, comprising the River Usk and its upper tributaries, has been designated as a Special Area for Conservation (SAC) and notified as a Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) for its geomorphological and physiographic features and the associated plant and animal communities. It supports internationally important populations of otter, Atlantic salmon, bullhead, brook lamprey, and river lamprey. This survey provides a baseline source for assessing potential habitat suitability by relating the presence or absence of morphological features to the condition parameters that are identified as habitat preferences for the SAC species. The purpose of this data capture is to develop an understanding of the dynamic geomorphology of the Upper Usk system.  The areas surveyed included the Upper Usk system (upstream of Abergavenny), and it\u0027s tributaries which included Hydfer, Crai, Senni, Treweryn, Camlais, Tarell, Cynrig, Menascin, Caerfanell, Cilieni, Eithrim, Bran, Ysgir, Ysgir Fechan, Ysgir Fawr, Honddu, Rhiangoll, Grwyne, Grwyne Fechan, Banw, Grwyne Fawr.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-09-03T10:05:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17717\tNRW_DS110183\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.422Z\t373\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFluvial Audit of the River Dee\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313833\tArchived as Project 161, Media 490.    Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media401to500\\Media490  Also archived in CCW legacy GIS drive (M drive) Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\hydrology\\Hydrology_Reports\\fluvial_morphology\tRhian Thomas, Env Mon Officer, Dadansoddi a Hysbysu, NRW ? Formerly Rhian Thomas in CCW\tThe results of a CCW commissioned Fluvial Geomorphological Audit of the River Dee to record the morphological features and to identify and understand the geomorphological processes which influence and control the channel activity and the morphological quality of the river system.  The River Dee is a dynamic gravel-bed river with evidence of active channel migration in specific locations. The river is of high conservation status being designated a Special Area for Conservation by virtue of its Ranunculus community and in its lower reaches the river channel and its floodplain are designated as a Site of Special Scientific Interest for its morphological and physiographic features.  This survey provides a baseline source for assessing site suitability by relating the presence or absence of morphological features to the condition parameters that are identified as habitat preferences for the components of the Ranunculus community. The purpose of this data capture was to develop an understanding of the dynamic geomorphology of the River Dee. The area used for this study included A 130 km length of the Main River of the River Dee, between the Bala Lake (Llyn Tegid) outflow and the Normal Tide Limit (NTL) at the Chester Weir (SJ 408659).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-09-03T10:06:25.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17716\tNRW_DS110192\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.357Z\t589\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Salinity Records\t© CNC/NRW 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313932\tSkomer MNR, V drive/shared documents/data/oceanographic data/sea water/ salinity\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Salinity is recorded from the sea bed and in a water column profile from a single site in the MNR. Records began in Jul 1992 and have continued to date. The purpose of this data capture was to record the oceanographic qualities of the surrounding sea water.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:32:07.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17718\tNRW_DS110193\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.461Z\t616\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Passive Sediment (Collector) Data\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313933\tSkomer MNR, V drive/shared documents/data/oceanographic data/sediment\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton Assistant Marine Conservation Officer - Skomer MNR Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Passive sediment traps have been deployed at 2 sites intermittently since 1994. Sediment dropping out of the water column is collected into a pot. The sample pots are changed every 2 weeks during the field season and the sediment samples are frozen. The purpose of this data capture was to record the depositional material occurring in the MNR.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:33:10.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17712\tNRW_DS110194\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.753Z\t653\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCondition monitoring of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313934\tIn summary DMS file and data are stored under the relevant SAC protected site under ‘monitoring’. GIS data is stored under the relevant GIS drive under ‘Evidence_Monitoring’ and split into either North or South Wales depending on location. Other than the top level summary data all field data and records are held and managed by the relevant NRW area.  This has largely been replaced by the baseline dashboard.\tInternal Contact/Custodian: Dylan Lloyd Specialist Advisor - Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team        Dylan Lloyd is supplying some more detailed information on Terrestrial SAC monitoring data to update the entry.  This entry is specifically about terrestrial SAC monitoring for habs directive.  Marine and Freshwater programmes should probably have a separate entry.  Dylan will consider if when data and programme reviewed additional metadata entries need to be added for each Location/feature that is monitored.  This metadata entry is the one that will be linked to from Terrestrial Monitoring SAC reports (reduced to one summary report due to staff workloads)\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs) are strictly protected sites designated under the EC Habitats Directive. The Habitats Directive has a list of habitat types and species that are considered to be most in need of conservation at a European level (excluding birds, which are covered under the EC Birds Directive). Once sites have been designated, the country agencies work with owners, occupiers and others to secure their management and protection regime. One element of this work is a programme of habitat and species monitoring across the site. Habitat monitoring includes woodland, uplands, wetlands, lowland grasslands and coastal margins. For species, monitoring covers a range of taxa from invertebrates through to mammals (including bat species) and both higher and lower plant.  This dataset combines the results of SAC monitoring from across Wales. The purpose of this collation is to assess the condition of features (the reason sites are protected) across these specific sites. The condition of features is monitored using site-specific performance indicators. The scope of performance indicators includes habitats and species across the terrestrial, freshwater, marine and coastal environments. This data is also used as an important record for aspects of their management, both as an aid to management decisions and for reporting at a variety of scales, including UK through JNCC, and for contributing to UK reports on Conservation Status under the EC Habitats Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17713\tNRW_DS110203\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.661Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) - River Nutrients Spatial Data\t© NRW and Enivronment Agency. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323033\tx:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\GQA Phosphate Network.lyr  x:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\GQA Nitrate Network.lyr\tPlease contact Jane Roberts ( Environment Agency)\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons. It is an external third party dataset owned by the Environment Agency Wales and licensed to NRW for internal use only.  The dataset has been supplied by the Environment Agency Wales and contains River Nutrient data (nitrate and phosphate) - GQA nitrate and phosphate grades achieved for each stretch in the 10k network.\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:23.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17711\tNRW_DS110209\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.657Z\t685\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEstablishment of a Permanent Monitoring Site at Mid Channel Rocks, Milford Haven\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323039\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media001to100\\Media053 Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elyfn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Metadata contact and Custodian: Data Services Team, NRW\tData from a study undertaken to establish a permanent monitoring site on Mid Channel Rocks, Milford Haven. The work was commissioned following the grounding of the Sea Empress oil tanker on the Mid Channel Rocks, Milford Haven on February 15th 1996.  On the four days following the grounding, the tanker remained in the immediate vicinity of these rocks, grounding and floating free several times.  An estimated 70,000 tonnes of oil were spilled within one nautical mile of the Mid-Channel Rocks.  A considerable volume of dispersant was applied from the air in the nearby area.  This is expected to have led to significantly elevated hydrocarbon levels in the water column, possibly including the more toxic oil fractions where fresh oil was dispersed. The objectives of this project were to establish a permanent sublittoral monitoring site at a rock substrate location close to the source of the spilled oil and which was amongst the most likely to have been subjected to elevated water column hydrocarbon load and to undertake a baseline survey to document epibenthic rock community and species composition, diversity, richness and condition. Data was captured in order to establish a permanent sublittoral monitoring site at a rock substrate location close to the source of the spilled oil and to undertake a baseline survey to document epibenthic rock community and species composition, diversity, richness and condition.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1998-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:14.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17710\tNRW_DS110210\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.571Z\t307\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEffectiveness of the rehabilitation of oiled birds with reference to the Sea Empress oil spill\t© CNC/NRW. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323130\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media001to100\\Media073 Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor\tInternal Custodian: Patrick Lindley or Matty Murphy, Marine Ornithologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis datasets comprises of the results from a review of the rescue and rehabilitation of oiled birds following the Sea Empress oil spill. This data was used in the evaluation of the effectiveness of rehabilitation efforts and suggested management procedures.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:38.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17714\tNRW_DS110213\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.656Z\t307\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPolycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbon (PAH) and N-Alkane Residues in Seabird Eggs after the Grounding of the Sea Empress.\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323133\tArchived as project no 93 (media no 368) - File path K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 368 NB The report won\u0027t open in Adobe acrobat or Word.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe grounding of the Sea Empress in February 1996 resulted in large-scale oil pollution of the water around the Pembrokeshire coast and its associated island seabird colonies. The accident occurred at about the time that seabirds were returning to their breeding colonies. Therefore, it was possible that breeding birds might transfer oil directly to their eggs through contact with oiled nesting material, feathers and feet, or oral ingestion of sub-lethal amounts of hydrocarbons and subsequent transfer to yolk. Exposure of adult birds and eggs to crude oil or polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs), which are present in crude oil, can have adverse effects on egg structure and quality, embryo development and hatching success. This dataset contains the results of an investigation into the PAH and n-alkane content of seabird eggs on the coast of Pembrokeshire following the Sea Empress oil spill. The aim of the study was to investigate whether there was any evidence of hydrocarbon contamination of seabird eggs resulting from the Seam Empress spill.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-10-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17709\tNRW_DS110217\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.588Z\t322\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPolycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbon composition of herbage samples following the Sea Empress oil spill\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323137\tArchived as project no 98 (media no 373) H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media373\t\tDataset contains the results of an investigation into the Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbon (PAH) composition of grass collected from the South Pembrokeshire coast following the Sea Empress oil spill. Data includes site location and grid references, herbage type, PAH concentrations and n-alkane content. Purpose of this data capture was to determine the concentration of PAH and aliphatic hydrocarbon in herbage samples following the oil spill and detect any patterns/trends.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17707\tNRW_DS110218\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.412Z\t376\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEpipelic and epilithic microalgal distribution, density and relative abundance following the Sea Empress oil spill.\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323138\tArchived as project no 111 - media no 385 (File path Z:\\Archive\\Media301to400\\Media385)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThe ecological impact of a pollution event such as the Sea Empress oil spill is readily observable on macroscopic plants and animals. Although microscopic organisms are not as easily observed and studied they may be a crucial part of the ecosystem structure and function. This dataset contains the result of a study into the effects of oil contamination on epipelic and epilithic microalgal communities in coastal habitats of South West Wales. Sites contaminated by the Sea Empress oil spill were compared to a nearby adjacent uncontaminated site. Purpose of this data capture was to study the effects of oil contamination on the distribution, density and abundance of epipelic and epilithic microscopic algal communities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1998-12-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17715\tNRW_DS110219\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.660Z\t358\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEffects of the Sea Empress oil spillage on sensitive sessile marine invertebrate communities\t© CNC/NRW 1998  There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323139\t[A] Archived as project no 107 (media no 380). File path K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 380. [B] Marine Recorder survey MRCCW10000000090\tInternal Custodian and Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe grounding of the Sea Empress at the mouth of Milford Haven in February 1996 resulted in oil polluted shorelines from the south of St Brides Bay to Pendine Sands. This dataset contains the results of an investigation into the effects of the oiling and its clean-up on sensitive sessile intertidal communities of marine invertebrates and the results of an investigation into possible effects of the oil/clean up on the biology of Alcyonidium species at Dale and Angle. The purpose of these investigations were to survey the lower shore sessile fauna on the north side of St Catherine\u0027s Island, Tenby documenting the present composition of the sessile fauna, to assess what changes had occurred since the 1950s and whether any of these might be attributable to the oiling an clean up following the Sea Empress grounding; and investigate the effect of the oil on the reproduction and recruitment of Alcyonidium species (Bryozoa) on Fucus serratus at Dale and Angle in Milford Haven and at control sites in Solva Harbour.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1998-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17708\tNRW_DS110223\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.518Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGeneral Quality Assessment (GQA) - River Invertebrate Data\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323233\tRhian Thomas, CCW\u0027s Rivers Ecologist holds a copy.\tSue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, NRW (Formerly Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, CCW) Nick Davies, Environment Agency\tThe GQA River Biology dataset (see separate metadata entry) gives stretches of rivers a grade from A-F depending on the number of invertebrate species caught. This dataset is a more extensive version containing the names of the invertebrates caught for the Rivers Usk and Wye.  This data was captured as part of the Routine Environment Agency England and Wales General Quality Assessment monitoring programme.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17706\tNRW_DS110240\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.199Z\t548\tUnassessed\tEditable\tJuvenile salmonid survey data for Special Area of Conservation (SAC) rivers.\t© Environment Agency  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323430\tLegacy CCW M:drive: M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Species\\all_wales\\Freshwater\\Freshwater_Fish Rhian Thomas, CCW\u0027s Rivers Ecologist holds copies of the spreadsheets.\tInternal custodian: Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, CCW  Metadata contact: Data services Team, NRW\tThe Environment Agency monitors juvenile salmon and trout populations nationally by fishing a limited number of sites on an annual basis to obtain meaningful temporal data for trends in fish populations. A more comprehensive spatial monitoring survey programme is carried out every 5 years to investigate differences between groups of sites within particular river catchments. Other species caught in the surveys are also recorded e.g. bullhead, lampreys, eels etc. This dataset contains juvenile salmon data for the 8 rivers designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) in Wales.  The purpose of this dataset is to obtain temporal data for trends in fish populations and to investigate differences between groups of sites within river catchments.\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\t2007-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17765\tNRW_DS110241\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.653Z\t668\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMean Trophic Rank Data for rivers in Wales\t© CNC/NRW and Environment Agency Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323431\tThe most up-to-date version of the data can be extracted from the BIOSYS database.\tInternal Custodian:  For this extract contact Rhian Thomas, Team Leader WFD, Water Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains Mean Trophic Rank data for a selection of rivers in Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the trophic status and eutrophication impact on rivers and streams using biotic indices. This is an old methodology and has now been superseded by LEAFPACKS. This dataset used to be supplied to former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) by former Environment Agency Wales (EAW), both of whom now for part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW). This data is now currently held in the BIOSYS database where it can be extracted for internal use.\tEnvironment Agency (EA)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17758\tNRW_DS110245\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.507Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFish Data for Transitional Waters\t© CNC/NRW 2008  There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323435\tA static archive copy is held on  Z:\\Archive \\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\WFD  Live data is held in the fisheries component of Wiski-Kieco.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNB FULL DATA FOR WALES IS NOW HELD ON WISKI-KIECO  Abundance data of fish caught in Welsh estuaries during Environment Agency Wales Water Framework Directive monitoring for the Transitional Fish biological quality element in 2007 onwards. For reporting the ecological quality status of Welsh estuaries under the EU Water Framework Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-11-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17762\tNRW_DS110271\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.472Z\t436\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring the Health of the North Llyn Modiolus Reef\t© CNC/NRW 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323731\tAll data is archived under project no.218, media no. 584. Working copies of the data is held on shared MFSG drive. Video tapes are archived in the fireproof safe.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG Metadata contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tModiolus Reefs are a feature of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC).  Information is gathered about the health of the reef through the recording of species present in fixed quadrats.  Live horse mussels are hard to distinguish within the quadrat, however it is known that each live horse mussel supports one colony of the soft coral dead man\u0027s fingers Alcyonium digitatum and therefore these are used to count the number of live horse mussels present. This survey work is carried out as part of the Across Wales Diving Survey. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the health of the reef. This contributes to reporting of favourable conservation status of the SAC feature.  Data will be used in conjunction with acoustic data that looks at the extent of the feature (reef) and potential areas of damage.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:34:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17760\tNRW_DS110284\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.506Z\t768\tUnassessed\tEditable\tVolunteer Diver Seagrass Zostera marina Surveys, Llyn Peninsula and Milford Haven (2008)\t© CNC/NRW and Marine Conservation Society © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323834\t[A] Data is archived as project 229, media no. 598 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501-600\\Media 598) [B ] A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG shared drive - [NB this may be out of date information]  [C] Marine Recorder Survey (MRMCS01200000011)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team. Internal Custodian: Data Services Team, NRW\tIn areas where conditions are suitable seagrass beds form and these are considered nationally important in the UK. Seagrasses are thought to help stabilising the seabed in addition to providing organic matter, shelter and food for a variety of fish, birds and invertebrates. Seagrass beds are vital nursery grounds for flatfish, and in some areas also for cephalopods. They are also known to be sensitive to changes in water quality and human disturbance. Seagrass beds have been designated as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan Habitat (underpinned by the Convention of Biological Diversity, Rio Earth Summit 1992), and as a Habitat of Principal Importance for the Conservation of Biological Diversity in Wales (NERC Act 2006: Section 42).   The survey was due to be expanded in 2009 to other areas within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and methodologies refined. 1. Develop methods to look at damage to seagrass beds caused by anchor chains compared to permanent moorings. 2. \u0027Ground-truth\u0027 aerial photographs of Porth Dinllaen and Milford Haven subtidal seagrass beds. 3. Test methods of measuring extent of large seagrass beds using volunteers. 4. Estimate density and measure depth of live seagrass in different parts of the bed. 5. Make note of any unusual feature of the seagrass bed.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Marine Conservation Society (MCS)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:34:55.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17754\tNRW_DS110289\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.369Z\t263\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNewborough Warren and Newborough Forest maps\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323839\tArchived as Project 159, Media 488 and Media 396\tMike Camplin, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist \u0026amp; Marine Monitoring Team Lead, Evidence, Knowledge and Advice - NRW (Used to be Gordon Bell / James Dargie)\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC) commissioned a survey of Newborough Warren and forest culminating in the production of high quality maps.\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC)\t1990-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17749\tNRW_DS110319\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.339Z\t461\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWest Wales Grey Seal Census 1991 to 1994\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333139\tDataset is archived as Project 137 (media number 393) H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media393\\Report Paper report is available from CCW library (see related titles field) 2 boxes off-site of original paper data: CCW/WRPB/003 - Seal Census ( 1992 - 1994 ), CCW/WRPB/004 - Seal Census ( 1992 - 1994 )\tInternal Custodian: Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW\tThe large breeding colonies of grey seals in northern Britain have been censused annually in recent years by the Sea Mammal Research Unit, but the relatively small population in Wales had not been the subject of a population estimate since 1974 and there had never been a comprehensive census of all mainland and island sites sustained through a pupping season.  The purpose of this data capture was to locate and document all grey seal breeding sites during the main pupping season from September to December 1991 and to estimate pup production at these sites. To estimate the total number of cows in the West Wales population, identify \u0027areas\u0027 where seals are subject to disturbance by man and assess the quality of the breeding haul-out environment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1995-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:35:42.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17755\tNRW_DS110320\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.370Z\t338\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAnalysis of seabird blood samples following the Sea Empress Oil Spill.\t© CNC/NRW 1997. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333230\tArchived as project no 123 (media no 364) H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media364\tInternal Contacts: Matty Murphy, Marine an Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe results of a study that used haematological techniques to investigate the subtle sub-lethal effects of oil on seabirds (razorbills, guillemots and shags) following the Sea Empress oil spill. Data includes summary statistics for the measured blood parameters.  The purpose of this data capture was to investigate subtle sub-lethal effects of oil on seabirds. Blood samples were collected from birds at two sites in Wales- Skomer Island and Middleholm, Pembrokeshire, and one site in Scotland- Isle of May, Firth of Forth.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:32.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17763\tNRW_DS110321\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.497Z\t430\tValid\tEditable\tLittoral Monitoring in Permanent Quadrats Established at West Angle Bay and Manorbier 1996-1997\t© CNC/NRW 1997. There are no use restrictions on this data. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333231\tArchived as project no 126 (media no 367). File path: -Z:|\\Archive\\Media 401to500\\dia301 to 400\\Media367.  Data is also held within Marine Recorder as survey numberMRCCW10000000075.\tInternal Contact: Mike Camplin, Team Leader, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tThe Sea Empress oil tanker ran aground at the entrance to Milford Haven on the 15th of February 1996 and proceeded to spill over 70,000 tonnes of crude oil into the sea. The purpose of data capture of this data capture was to investigate the changes in community structure following the oil spill and identify any differences between the two sites monitored. Two permanent quadrats established prior to the spill at West Angle Bay and Manorbier Bay were both severely impacted however neither were subjected to cleaning. This provided an ideal opportunity for pre and post spill monitoring, the results of which are presented in this dataset.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:25:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17756\tNRW_DS110332\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.461Z\t570\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRocky Shore Transect Monitoring in Milford Haven following the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333332\tArchived as project no 101 (media no 376) and project no 108 (media no 381 )  H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media376 H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media381 CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder.\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Marine Vertebrates, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThree months before the Sea Empress oil spill, baseline surveys of rocky shore fauna and flora were undertaken at fixed transect sites throughout Milford Haven and the Daugleddau Estuary. Following the spill Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned a full re-survey of the fixed transects in 1996 and 1997. The purpose of this data capture was to identify the effects of the oil spill on the flora and fauna of the rocky shore at Milford Haven through comparisons with baseline surveys and to provide quantitative data on important species for future analysis of recovery. This dataset contains the results of the survey work.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1998-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:14.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17750\tNRW_DS110333\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.330Z\t556\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBiotope studies on rocky shores following the Sea Empress oil spill\t© CNC/NRW 1998  There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333333\t[A] Archived as project no 105, media no 379 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 379) [B] Report available from NRW library\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of the results of a series of broadscale studies on rocky sea shores around the open coast of South Pembrokeshire following the Sea Empress oil spill in February 1996. The dataset includes maps and diagrams of biotopes, list of regional biotopes referred to in survey and target notes. The purpose of this data capture was to help assess the effects of oil on shore biotopes, communities and species over whole shore areas, to provide information on biotopes, habitat, communities or species which will assist in the development of future monitoring or surveillance strategies, and to investigate the role of Phase 1 studies in surveillance and monitoring of rocky intertidal areas. Surveys were concentrated on the open coast of South Pembrokeshire, from West Angle Bay to Manorbier. All survey carried out by experienced surveyors.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1998-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:53.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17753\tNRW_DS110334\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.354Z\t281\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEffects of oil and clean-up of oil from the Sea Empress on the nationally rare and scarce vascular plants of Pembrokeshire\t© CNC/NRW  There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333334\tArchived as project no 110 (media no 384). Report No. 289 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 384. Media 379 also mentioned originally in this entry but seems to have no connection.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset is made up of the results of an investigation into the impact of the Sea Empress oil spillage and its clean-up on populations of some of the rare and scarce vascular plants that were well recorded in Pembrokeshire prior to the pollution incident. The purpose of this data capture was to reassess the population size of selected nationally rare and nationally scarce vascular plants of the Milford Haven waterway and the South Pembrokeshire coast.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:53.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17747\tNRW_DS110336\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.312Z\t918\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of golden hair lichen (Teloschistes flavicans) in Pembrokeshire following the Sea Empress oil spill\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333336\tArchived as project no 99 (media no 374). Report No. 237 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media301 to 400\\Media374)\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tTeloschistes flavicans, the golden haired lichen, is a species formally widespread in the UK, but now mainly found only in South-West England and Wales. It is included as \u0027vulnerable\u0027 in the Red Data Book for British Lichens and is included on schedule 8 of the Wildlife and Countryside Act. It is a species particularly sensitive to sulphur dioxide air pollution. This dataset includes the results of a resurvey of Teloschistes flavicans in South Pembrokeshire following the Sea Empress oil spill in February 1996. A comprehensive survey of all known Teloschistes flavicans sites was undertaken in Pembrokeshire in 1994. It was considered likely that the lichen might be detrimentally affected by air-borne pollutants arising from the Sea Empress oil spill and consequently a resurvey was commissioned.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17759\tNRW_DS110337\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.458Z\t467\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInfauna of heavily oiled shores in Milford Haven and Carmarthen Bay following the Sea Empress oil spill\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333337\tArchived as project no 102 (media no 377). Report No. 244 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 377) Report available in CCW  library, see library catalogue/ \u0027related titles\u0027 field. CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (Survey no.  MRCCW10000000083)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team   Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe sediment shores of Pembrokeshire are of many types, from coarse sand, storm beaches to the soft fine muds of the Milford Haven Estuary. Following the grounding of the Sea Empress it became obvious that several sediment shores, particularly in the lower reaches off the Milford Haven, were vulnerable to serious damage as a results of large quantities of crude oil spilt. The physical characteristics of beaches determine to a large extent the types and range of biota present. This dataset includes the results of an assessment of the effects of the Sea Empress oil spill on the infauna of sediment shores in Milford Haven and Carmarthen Bay. The purpose of this data capture was to carry out biological and hydrocarbon analyses to assess the impact of the oil spill incident on sediment infauna.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1998-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17748\tNRW_DS110342\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.330Z\t723\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEcological impacts of shoreline clean-up during the Sea Empress Oil spill\t© CNC/NRW 2000 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333432\tArchived as project no 122 (media no 363) H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media363 Report available from CCW library (see library catalogue/\u0027related titles\u0027 field)\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of an assessment of the effects of shoreline clean-up activity following the Sea Empress oil spill, concentrating on conspicuous species of rocky shore algae and animals, coastal vegetation and signs of physical disturbance. The Sea Empress ran aground on the 15th of February 1996. Shoreline clean-up activities at over 100 sites around south-west Wales began on the 16th of February and were scaled down gradually from July 1996 through to summer 1997.  The purpose of this data capture was to carry out: -  An assessment of the environmental impacts of each individual clean up technique;  - Compare the impacts with those described from previous spills and experimental studies;  - Make recommendations on the suitability and application of techniques in different situations; - Assess the overall impact of the clean-up activity, in relation to the impacts of the oil pollution itself; and, - Make recommendations for future monitoring and studies of the effects of clean-up activities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17764\tNRW_DS110343\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.509Z\t677\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvival Rate of Rehabilitated Guillemots\t© CNC/NRW and BTO 1997. There are no use restrictions on this data.  There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ (e.g.) Forestry Commission and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333433\tArchived as project no 96 (media no 371) H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media371 Report available from CCW library (see library catalogue/\u0027related titles\u0027 field)\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy, Marine Ornithologist  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe results of a study on the post-release life expectancy and oiled and cleaned guillemots has caused concern for seabird rehabilitation. This lead to considerable concern that seabird rehabilitation programmes are of low conservation value and may even prolong the suffering of the rescued birds. The study aimed to: - Calculate the survival rates of oiled, cleaned and released guillemots, - Compare these with the natural survival rates of guillemots which have not been oil, cleaned and released, - Assess whether the survival of rehabilitated birds has increased in recent years as cleaning techniques have improved, - Critically examine the quality of the available data and make recommendations for future improvements.\tBritish Trust for Ornithology (BTO)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17751\tNRW_DS110344\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.343Z\t442\tValid\tEditable\tBroadleaved Woodland in North Wales\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333434\t1) Raw data- original copies held in CCW North Wales regional offices, photocopies of parts of the dataset held by Jim Latham at CCW Headquarters. 2) Paper reports- held in CCW libraries (Regional and HQ- see related titles field).\tJim Latham, Woodland and Spatial Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group, NRW\tA historically important dataset underpinning woodland site designation in North Wales. 260 woodland sites surveyed across North Wales.  The purpose of this data capture was to survey and evaluate in terms of their nature conservation interest all predominantly broadleaved woods in North Wales using nationally developed standard methods and evaluation criteria. The data collected helped to identify the \u0027best\u0027 examples and assisted in management and protection of the woods by making it possible to view them in context within an overall classification.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1985-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-16T15:26:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17746\tNRW_DS110345\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.173Z\t349\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWoodland Recording Cards\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333435\tHeld in box files in Jim Latham\u0027s (woodland ecologist) office.  Relocated to Woodland Tambour Unit in Old Post Room F12.\tInternal Custodian: Jim Latham, Woodland Ecologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW       Includes recently found box for Carmarthen.  Long terms plan is to digitise the habitat maps into GIS, scan the field notes and attach them to the GIS polygon via a hotlink.  Additionally species information should be transcribed to spread sheet for import to corporate biological recording database.  Could be let under data digitising \u0026amp; scanning  call-off contract when one is put in place\tApproximately 3,000 recording cards from woodland surveys in Wales. Some are from structured surveys e.g. the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) surveys of woodland Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), whilst others are ad-hoc recording events. The records can include site name, grid references, aspect, slope, altitude, area, species lists, height, cover, age class abundance etc. The purpose of this data capture was to gather compositional and structural information about woodlands; to gain an understanding of their species diversity and conservation importance; to influence site designation and to identify future research and survey needs.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17761\tNRW_DS110347\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.475Z\t927\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironmental Impact Assessment of Borden Chemicals Long Sea Outfall (March 2001)\t© CNC/NRW 2002 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333437\t[A] A copy of the raw data is archived as part of the Marine Sites: Data collation project as project 27, media 81 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100) [B] Species and habitat data is available in Marine Recorder [MRCCW90000000007]\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tBorden Chemicals (formerly B.P. Chemicals) is a large chemical works located to the east of Barry, south Wales. Effluent is released into the Bristol Channel via a long sea outfall which discharges approximately 1 km offshore at 51degrees 23.41 N 03degrees 14.49 W. This replaced an old outfall in 1993 that discharged into the intertidal zone to the east of Barry docks at 51degrees 23.67 N 03degrees 14.88 W. The effluent is a complex mix of mostly organic compounds including phenol, toluene, 1,2-dichloroethane, methanol, acrylonitrile and vinyl chloride.  However, the discharge generally meet the levels set by their consent for materials discharged into the waters of the Bristol Channel.   This dataset contains the results of a survey of the benthic environment in the vicinity of the Borden Chemicals long sea outfall, east of Barry in the Bristol Channel. The purpose of this data capture was to collect data to conduct biological analysis as well as sediment samples and physico-chemical analyses to provide information for an Environmental Impact Assessment. As of the 1st April 2013, Natural Resources Wales has taken over the functions currently carried out by the Countryside Council for Wales, Environment Agency Wales and Forestry Commission Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:26:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17752\tNRW_DS110433\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.315Z\t354\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW) Upland Framework\t© CNC/NRW NRW data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence NRW does NOT grant permission to disseminate or re-use any data that is not labelled as NRW ownership. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343333\tX:\\Biological\\Upland Framework Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\Upland_Framework\tCustodian: Liz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW (ex-CCW custodian Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist)  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tA framework developed to improve the quality and diversity of habitats on Wales\u0027 statutory upland sites.  The essence of the framework is a map based overview of statutory sites in the Welsh uplands, outlining the future, long-term conservation ideals for each upland statutory site. This dataset is made up of two layers, Upland Framework and the Upland Framework font point. The purpose of this data capture was to develop national and regional conservation priorities by highlighting important features and prioritising their conservation within particular area; and, provide guidance for management planning, including setting long-term objectives and steering management to achieve these objectives on individual sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:53.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17757\tNRW_DS110463\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.472Z\t542\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveys of Sea Caves in Welsh Special Areas of Conservation (SAC)\t© CNC/NRW 2002 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343633\tAll data is archived under project 243, media no. 777 including species spreadsheets reformatted for analysis in PRIMER.   Site locations are also available on the m drive and report is available in Ffynnon.  Photographs are available on the Marine Monitoring Team catalogue (CD 21, 22, 24, 44), video grabs available on the Marine Monitoring Team catalogue (CD 40).  Specimens are held by Francis Bunker and will be transferred to the National Museum of Wales in Cardiff. Original paper MNCR forms are held in CCW off-site storage.  (Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tInventorial survey as a first step for former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), to develop sensitivity management and monitoring for submerged or partially submerged caves.  Submerged or partially submerged caves are an Annex 1 habitat listed under the Habitats directive. Caves vary in size, from only a few metres to more extensive systems, which may extend hundreds of metres into the rock. There may be tunnels or caverns with one or more entrances, in which vertical and overhanging rock faces provide the principal marine habitat. Sea cave communities vary considerably depending on the structure and extent of the cave system, their degree of submergence and of exposure to sand scour and wave-surge, and their geology. Caves are typically colonised by encrusting animal species but may also support shade-tolerant seaweeds near their entrances. Physical conditions, such as inclination, wave surge, scour and shade, change rapidly from cave entrance to the inner parts of a cave, and this often leads to a marked gradation in the communities present.  Surveys were set up to identify a baseline and trial survey methodologies in order to establish surveillance and monitoring programmes for the feature.  24 caves (and tunnels) were described. They include caves that are wholly Intertidal, wholly subtidal and ones with both littoral and sublittoral elements. They range from caves that extend 1-3 meters into the cliff to ones that extend up to 100 meters into the cliff.  Inventorial survey as a first step for CCW to develop sensitivity management and monitoring for submerged or partially submerged caves.  24 caves across Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC), Cardigan Bay SAC, Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC and Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay SAC.  All measurements were taken to a standard protocol as described in the CCW Marine Monitoring Report no. 6.  Specimens were fixed and preserved.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:37:18.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17742\tNRW_DS110464\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.702Z\t352\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCemlyn Lagoon Soft Sediment Infauna Survey (2006 - 2012)\t© CNC/NRW 2008 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343634\tAll data is archived under project no. 241 and media no. 766.   A working copy is held on S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Lagoons\\Cemlyn Lagoon\tInternal Custodians - Marine monitoring Team, NRW  - Bill Sanderson, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG  Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services\tThe aim of the EC Habitats Directive 92/43/EEC is to promote the maintenance of biodiversity by requiring Member States to take measures to maintain or restore natural habitats and wild species listed on the Annexes to the Directive at a favourable conservation status, introducing robust protection for those habitats and species of European importance. Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) are strictly protected sites designated under the Habitats Directive.   In the UK coastal lagoons show a wide range of geographical and ecological variation; five main sub-types have been identified in the UK, on the basis of their physiography, as meeting the definition of the Annex I habitat type. These are graded from A to D.   - A Outstanding examples of the feature in a European context.  - B Excellent examples of the feature, significantly above the threshold for Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) notification but of somewhat lower value than grade A sites.  - C Examples of the feature which are of at least national importance (i.e. usually above the threshold for SSSI notification on terrestrial sites) but not significantly above this. These features are not the primary reason for SACs being selected.  - D Features of below SSSI quality occurring on SACs. These are non-qualifying features (“non-significant presence”), indicated by a letter D, but this is not a formal global grade.     Cemlyn lagoon on the north coast of Anglesey, north Wales, is considered to be the best example of a saline coastal lagoon in Wales (Graded A) and forms part of the Cemlyn Bay SAC. The lagoon is separated from the sea by a shingle bank with a narrow channel at the western end, across which a sluice system was built in the 1930s. Seawater exchange occurs mainly through the sluice and by percolation through the shingle bank, although in extreme storms coinciding with spring tides waves break over the top of the shingle bank. Cemlyn lagoon supports a relatively diverse set of species, several of which are specific to lagoons, including the bryozoan Conopeum seurati, the lagoon cockle Cerastoderma glaucum and the lagoonal mud-snail Ventrosia ventrosa. Cemlyn lagoon is also the only site in Wales where the lagoonal isopod Idotea chelipes has been recorded. A number of uncommon plant species are found within the lagoon, including the brackish water-crowfoot Ranunculus baudotii and beaked tasselweed Ruppia maritima. The purpose of this data capture is to aid in the monitoring and condition assessment of this Annex I feature. This dataset is a collation of data from subtidal benthic soft sediment sampling carried out within Cemlyn lagoon.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:29:00.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17744\tNRW_DS110466\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.702Z\t663\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPickleridge Lagoon Soft Sediment Infauna Survey (2006 - 2013)\t© CNC/NRW 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343636\t[A]  All data is archived under project no. 241,  media no 764.   [B]  A working copy is held on S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Lagoons\\Pickleridge\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Technical Specialist, MFSG. Assisstant Marine Conservation Officer, Skomer. Bill Sanderson, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG.  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset is a collation of data from subtidal benthic soft sediment sampling carried out within Pickleridge lagoon, which is a feature of the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The purpose of this data capture was to aid in the monitoring and condition assessment of this Annex I feature.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:29:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17741\tNRW_DS110467\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.655Z\t809\tValid\tEditable\tMorfa Gwyllt Lagoon Soft Sediment Infauna Survey (2006 - 2012)\t© CNC/NRW 2008. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343637\tAll data is archived under project no. 241 and  media no 765.   A working copy is held on Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Lagoons\\Morfa Gwyllt\tInternal Custodians:  Marine Technical Specialist, MFSG Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tCoastal lagoons are areas of shallow, coastal salt water, wholly or partially separated from the sea by sandbanks, shingle or, less frequently, rocks. Lagoons show a wide range of geographical and ecological variation; five main sub-types have been identified in the UK, on the basis of their physiography, as meeting the definition of the Annex I habitat type. Annex 1 habitat types are those habitats which are listed under the EC Habitats Directive and form part of Special Area of Conservation (SAC) interest features. Morfa Gwyllt lagoon in classed as one of these features within the Pen Llyn a`r Sarnau/ Lleyn Peninsula and the Sarnau SAC. Morfa Gwyllt lagoon is a small percolation lagoon that consists of a depression in a shingle bar across the mouth of the Afon Dysynni in mid Wales. This is the only example of a percolation lagoon in Wales. The substrate is a mosaic of medium sand over/amongst shingle, with muddier patches within the deeper pockets, and scattered larger pebbles.   Three lagoonal specialists have been found at this site: the amphipod Sphaeroma hookeri, the bryozoan Conopeum seurati and the alga Chaetomorpha linum. This dataset is a collation of data from subtidal benthic soft sediment sampling carried out within Morfa Gwyllt lagoon collected to aid the monitoring and condition assessment of this Annex 1 feature.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:35:12.399Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17745\tNRW_DS110469\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.701Z\t551\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe condition and conservation of montane heath and summit heath vegetation in Wales\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343639\tHardcopy of report is available from CCW libraries (see related titles field). PDF is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  (ex-CCW, Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist.) Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of an investigation into the relationship between species composition, grazing impacts, nitrogen pollution and nitrogen status of montane heath communities of the Carneddau massif in Snowdonia.  The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the links between species composition, grazing impacts, nitrogen pollution and nitrogen status of montane heath communities on the Cardeddau massif. The main objective was to provide information on the current status of the communities in order to indicate whether overgrazing is likely to be the main factor in their decline and whether a reduction in grazing levels might result in the restoration of these communities in North Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T13:30:33.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17743\tNRW_DS110470\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.686Z\t676\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCwm Idwal Surveillance data\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343730\t[A]  Hardcopy of report is available from CCW libraries (see related titles field). Word version is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist [B]  Raw data is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist [C]  Digital images on CCWs upland ecologist hardrive and on CD in TSG Archives\tPete Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, NRW  Jane Stevens, Habitat and Ecosystem Resource Analyst, NRW (Formerly: Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist)\tThis dataset currently contains the first year\u0027s recorded data following the establishment of a long-term vegetation surveillance system at Cwm Idwal National Nature Reserve (NNR). An earlier programme of intensive surveillance was undertaken at Cwm Idwal during 1999-2004 in response to the long term exclusion of grazing. This current surveillance project involves the establishment of a new, less intensive surveillance programme based on just eleven of the original plots, which will be repeated at three year intervals. The dataset includes a summary of plot frequency data, summary statistics for each species/feature, digital photographs, general observations on the eleven plots and plot identification sketches. The purpose of this data capture was to inform large-scale restoration projects in other parts of the Welsh uplands and provide information on the effects of such management decisions for the restoration of upland sites.\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:53.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17738\tNRW_DS110473\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.523Z\t1318\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCwm Idwal Vegetation Change Surveillance Project 1999-2004\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343733\ta) Hardcopy of final report is available from CCW libraries (see related titles field). Word version is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist. Annual interim reports also exist.  b) Raw data is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist c) Photographs available from CCW\u0027s upland ecologist\tLiz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW (exCCW Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist)\tThe results of a five year surveillance project carried out in Cwm Idwal National Nature Reserve (NNR), from which grazing has been excluded on a long term experimental basis.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish a detailed monitoring system to determine the long term impact of grazing removal on a range of vegetation communities; and, to determine the nature and direction of vegetation recovery in terms of its structure and composition.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:25.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17739\tNRW_DS110474\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.527Z\t566\tUnassessed\tEditable\tImpact of Grazing and Nitrogen Deposition on the Condition of Racomitrium lanuginosum on the Carneddau Mountains\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343734\ta) Hardcopy of report is available from CCW libraries (see related titles field). Word version is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist b) Raw data is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist\tPete Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader Jane Stevens, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW Formerly, Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tResults of work conducted on the moss heath habitat of the Carneddau massif to establish whether the overall condition of the dominant woolly fragile moss Racomitrium lanuginosum was associated with grazing pressure. The purpose of this data capture was to: - Determine whether the condition and growth of R. lanuginosum (on the basis of tissue chemistry, physiology, and growth rate) differs in areas of differing grazing intensity.  - Investigate the recovery potential of R. lanuginosum under the current pollution climate if grazers were removed.  - Find out whether membrane potassium leakage from R. lanuginosum shoots increases under increased levels of trampling damage and whether the depth of R. lanuginosum is affected by increased levels of trampling damage.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17740\tNRW_DS110476\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.647Z\t644\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Upland Vegetation Surveys of Wales (1999 - 2008)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343736\tNRW\u0027s Upland Ecologist holds a copy of each of the reports; they are also available from the HQ and regional NRW libraries MapInfo tables are stored on former CCW\u0027s m:drive: M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\upland_survey  X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Upland  The NVC data is stored in Recorder under one survey CCW Upland Survey CCW0007400000001. There are separate events for each geographic location.\tInternal Custodian: Jane Stevens, TEG, NRW Formerly Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tThis dataset contains both the non-spatial and spatial (GIS) outputs from a vegetation surveys of upland Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales to National Vegetation Classification (NVC) level.   The purpose of this data capture was to provide data on the extent and distribution of vegetation communities in the Welsh uplands for planning, notification and management purposes. Phase 1 level vegetation surveys of around 48 upland sites in Wales.   Each site has a dataset which includes a published report, quadrat data tables, maps (most paper maps have been digitised and are available as MapInfo tables and ESRI Feature Classes) and site descriptions.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-02-07\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3608/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_phase_2_upland\t2025-12-19T10:54:03.393Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17737\tNRW_DS110477\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.528Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey of Bannau Brycheiniog/Brecon Beacons Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS. Attribution Statement: © CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343737\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist\tPete Jones - Terrestrial Ecosystems Team Leader... Used to be Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tGeographic coverage: Brecon Beacons SSSI\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-09-05T12:36:20.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n17736\tNRW_DS110478\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.229Z\t752\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey of Mynydd Llangatwg and Mynydd Llangyndir\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343738\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist.  Raw data and map images are also held within the report.\tPeter Jones, Terrestrial and Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader - NRW (used to be Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist)\tThis dataset comprised of the results from a survey of an upland site in and adjacent to the Brecon Beacons National Park, carried out during May 2002 by the Central Environmental Surveys on behalf of the former Countryside Council for Wales. Within the report the survey is divided into two sites. One site includes the area within the boundary of Mynydd Llangatwg Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). The other site includes land to the south-west of the Mynydd Llangatwg SSSI.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17795\tNRW_DS110479\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.561Z\t386\tValid\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey of Cadair Idris National Nature Reserve (NNR)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343739\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive \u0026amp; with CCWs upland ecologist Some of this data is also held in Recorder 6 under: CCW Upland Survey CCW0007400000001\tinternal Custodian: Pete Jones, Terrestrial and Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader  Jane Stevens (TEG) Formerly Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tThis dataset contains the survey results from the Cadair Idris National Nature Reserve between August and September 1999. The survey was commissioned by former Countryside Council for Wales, in order to map and describe the vegetation of Cadair Idris. The reserve comprises of approximately 450 hectares of the Cadair Idris massif some 6 km south of Dolgellau.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2000-02-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-28T16:41:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17792\tNRW_DS110481\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.505Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey of Mynydd Eglwyseg, Denbighshire\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343831\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive \u0026amp; with CCWs upland ecologist\tBarbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tTemporal coverage: 2000-2001\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:09.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17787\tNRW_DS110482\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.297Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey of the Elenydd Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343832\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive \u0026amp; with CCWs upland ecologist\tBarbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tThe purpose of this survey was to map and describe the vegetation of twelve holdings in Elenydd Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) in mid Wales. These surveys were commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in order to map and describe the vegetation of two areas within Elenydd SSSI. Survey were carried out in August 1998, August 1999 and May 2002.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17781\tNRW_DS110493\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.233Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Surveys of Eryri\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343933\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist\tBarbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17793\tNRW_DS110494\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.527Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey of parts of the Migneint-Dduallt Area\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343934\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist\t(Formerly: Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist)\tThis survey was commissioned by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW). The work was done to map and describe the vegetation of several blocks of upland in the  Migneint-Dduallt area of north-west Wales and to help define the nature and boundaries of the Migneint-Dduallt Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and Special Area for Conservation (SAC). The whole survey area measures 58 square kilometres in area, and has an altitudinal range of 170-854 m. It consists of various types of habitat including level valley floors, steep valley sides, irregularly undulating hills and plateaux, and the high, steep-sided mountains of Arenig Fawr and Moel Llyfnant. The bedrock is a mixture of sedimentary and igneous rocks of Ordovician and Cambrian age, though much of the land is overlain by peat.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:56:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17794\tNRW_DS110495\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.529Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey of the Pumlumon Sites of Scientific Interest (SSSI)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS. Attribute Statement: © CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343935\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist\tBarbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tThis dataset consists of the results from a vegetation surveys at Pumlumon Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) on the borders of Powys and Ceredigion. In September 2004 a survey of the National Vegetation Classification communities present, together with an assessment of the vegetation condition of four habitat types. The work was carried out by Rigby Jerram Ecological Consultants on behalf of Countryside Council for Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:56:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17791\tNRW_DS110496\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.403Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Surveys of Rhinog Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and Adjacent Land\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS. Attribution Statement: © CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343936\tHard copies in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copies \u0026amp; electronic copies of reports with CCWs upland ecologist .  Digitised versions of maps for Rhinog SSSI survey also with CCWs upland ecologist\tBarbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tThis data collection was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in order to map, describe and assess the vegetation of the Rhinog Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) in north Wales. Ninety-five types of vegetation and habitat were records, the most extensive being lightly grazed Calluna vulgaris heaths.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:56:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17789\tNRW_DS110497\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.338Z\t713\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSea Fishing Atlas of Wales\t© CNC/NRW 2009  There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343937\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries_Habitat_and_sensitivity\\Fishing_activity\\ Archived as project 332, media 1291  (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1291\tInternal Custodians: Sea Fisheries Liaison Officer, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tWARNING: This data is outdated due to changes in fishery management and is not recommended for re-use. It could be misleading.  An atlas of sea fishing activities around the coast of Wales. The atlas includes sections on fishing activities, regulations and competent authorities (see \u0027contains\u0027 field for metadata entries for the three different sections). The purpose of this data capture was to outline general fishing patterns, regulations and competent authorities around Wales in order to present a general picture of fishing activity which has previously not been publicly available.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:58:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17786\tNRW_DS110498\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.232Z\t125\tValid\tEditable\tSea Fishing Activity in Welsh Waters\t© CNC/NRW 2009. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343938\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries_Habitat_and_sensitivity\\Fishing_activity\\ Archived as project 332, media 1291 (old version) at K:\\Archive\\Media 1291to1300\\Media 1291 or Project 376, Media 1396 (new version) at K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1396\tInternal Custodian:  Previous custodian who have now left or moved roles: Sea Fisheries Liaison Officer, NRW   Clare Eno and Rowland Sharp, NRW Environmental Policy Group  Current custodian: Internal Contact: Rowland Sharp, Senior Advisor, SW Area Operations (Marine)  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW       Removed from public view on the advice of marine fisheries experts as information is dated and could be misleading.\tWARNING: This data is outdated due to changes in fishery management and is not recommended for re-use. It could be misleading.    Information has been collated to produce a series of maps of sea fishing activities in Wales complete with regulatory boundaries (12nm, 6nm and 3nm). The purpose of this dataset was to provide spatial information for a range of fishing activities occurring in Welsh waters.   Activities mapped are:   - Beam trawling and scallop dredging  - Mussel, oyster and hydraulic dredging  - Otter trawling  - Light demersal trawls and seines  - Pelagic trawling, drift nets and hook and line fishing  - Static gear fishing - nets and long line  - Static gear fishing - pots  - Hand gathering  - Aquaculture   The dataset includes maps and a text description of each activity.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T10:28:39.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17778\tNRW_DS110499\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.114Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRegulations Governing Sea Fisheries in Welsh Waters\tSea Fisheries Committee owned data: NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.  NRW owned data:  © CNC/NRW  2009. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343939\tArchived as project 332, media 1291 (K:\\Archive\\Media1201to1300\\Media 1291 Available from NRW library  Available on the hard drives of Clare Eno and Rowland Sharp, NRW Environmental Policy Group\tInternal Contact: Rowland Sharp, Senior Advisor, SW Area Operations (Marine)       This is part of a larger dataset the Sea Fishing Atlas of Wales. (which consisted of three parts) The regulations bit is just held in the marine archive and is not available on the X drive.  This is because no-one has ever asked for it and it was a snapshot and I imagine completely out of date so as long as the project and media number are there I don’t think we need a X drive location.    Removed from public view on the advice of marine fisheries experts as information is dated and could be misleading.\tWARNING: This data is outdated due to changes in fishery management and is not recommended for re-use. It could be misleading.  This dataset contains a collation of all relevant regulations pertaining to Welsh water fisheries. The fisheries regulations are split according to whether they are general to Welsh waters (including relevant EU and UK regulations), Sea Fisheries Committee (SFC) districts or relate to specific locations around the coast. The relevant sections of the byelaws are quoted and correct to the end of 2008. Those fishing activities currently unregulated are also identified and mapped. Another component of the dataset is the Rainbow chart. The purpose of this data capture was to collate all relevant regulations relating to sea fishing activity in Welsh waters and to present the information spatially in the form of maps (most of the regulation information was previously available, primarily within SFC byelaws, but had not previously been mapped). The regulations documented are those that were in force up to December 2008, there may have been changes to some regulations or new regulations created since. For information on the current regulations the relevant organisations should be contacted.\tAssociation Of Sea Fisheries Committees-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17780\tNRW_DS110500\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.132Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCompetent Authorities for Fisheries in Welsh Waters\t© CNC/NRW 2009  There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353030\ta) Report available from NRW library  b) Available on the hard drives of Clare Eno and Rowland Sharp, NRW Environmental Policy Group c) Archived as project 332, media 1291 (File path: K\\Archive\\Media 1200to1300\\Media 1291\tInternal Contact: Colin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine Fisheries, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW       Removed from public view on the advice of marine fisheries experts as information is dated and could be misleading.\tWARNING: This data is outdated due to changes in fishery management and is not recommended for re-use. It could be misleading  This dataset contains a collation of tables containing information on the competent authorities i.e. the regulatory bodies with current competence for managing fisheries (towed gears, scallop fishery, static gear- potting, mussel fishery, cockle fishery) within each marine Natura 2000 site in Welsh waters. The purpose of this data capture was to collate all relevant regulations relating to sea fishing activity in Welsh waters. Information including name of European marine site, competent authority, relevant legislation, \u0027permit\u0027 controls and comments collated by contractor. The regulations documented are those that were in force up to December 2008, there may have been changes to some regulations or new regulations created since. For information on the current regulations the relevant organisations should be contacted.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17788\tNRW_DS110501\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.297Z\t997\tValid\tEditable\tModelling of Habitat Networks for Welsh Woodlands, Grasslands, Heathlands, Bogs and Fens\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353031\tX:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Habitat Networks all Wales\\Lowland ,  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Habitat Networks all Wales\\Upland\tJim Latham, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team Leader\tThere is a growing emphasis on ecological connectivity in planning for effective biodiversity conservation and building ecosystem resilience through Resilient Ecological Networks (RENs). A major part of this is to counteract the negative impacts of habitat fragmentation (reduction in are and increase in isolation).  Modelled habitat networks can provide insights into how landscapes are likely to be functioning and inform the location of actions to improve the connectivity and resilience of protected sites.  This is a spatial dataset consisting of maps of habitat networks originally developed by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in collaboration with Forestry Commission Wales and Forest Research using a functional networks approach, and now managed and progressively developed by NRW.  Patches of habitat and other intervening habitats through which many of their species are able to move are mapped as habitat networks.   Networks have been mapped for habitats including woodland, unimproved grassland, calcareous grassland, marshy grassland, heathland, fens and bogs; in most cases these are divided into upland and lowland versions and in some cases networks that support the highest quality areas of habitat have been selected out as priority layers.   Results are available for the whole of Wales as GIS layers and include three levels of habitat networks; core networks (areas within which species that require extensive habitat and disperse poorly are able to move), focal networks (areas within which species tolerant of smaller habitat patches and with greater dispersal ability are able to move), and local networks (areas within species that can persist within small habitat patches and have very limited dispersal abilities can move).    These habitat networks  also form the basis of ‘Priority Ecological Networks’ (PENs), a subset of the ‘focal’ networks described above which have been selected against our suite of Protected Sites.  Purpose of data capture was to allow the scope and range of potential networks to be rapidly explored. Predicted habitat networks can be used to guide large-scale planning for nature conservation, provide insight into how the landscape is likely to be functioning and prioritise action to improve the connectivity and viability of protected sites.  Layers are arranged in two folders: Level 1, containing all outputs across Wales, and Level 2 which are selected, priority networks, within which action may be targeted to enhance functional networks of the best habitat areas.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-06-27\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3592/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_habitat_network\t2025-05-07T08:59:24.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17779\tNRW_DS110502\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.139Z\t500\tValid\tEditable\tA collation of National Vegetation Classification (NVC) data for Woodlands in Wales\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353032\tRaw data spreadsheet and excel spreadsheet on the DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/wood/Woodland%20Types%20and%20Classification/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FTerrestrial%2DHabitats%2Fwood%2FWoodland%20Types%20and%20Classification%2FNVC%2FSurvey%20data%2FSURVEY\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000238FEB7B97F86443BBC09DF6F9B378DE\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BC1284E03%2DE26B%2D453C%2DB036%2DB0055ED6D8F8%7D  MapInfo table located at M:\\GIS_Users\\jim_l\\NVC\\nvc report - this needs to be translated before it is put on the X Drive. Paper reports held in CCW libraries (Regional and HQ - see related titles field).\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThe National Vegetation Classification (NVC) became established as the standard classification scheme for semi-natural woodlands in Britain, and has wide applications to nature conservation programmes. The NVC is the basis for selection of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and has been adopted as a fundamental tool for planning and creating new native woodlands.   This dataset is a collation of Woodland NVC data from former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) strategic surveys, individual site surveys, records from site visits, interpretation of pre-NVC surveys, and contributions from other bodies.   This purpose of this data capture was to provide a useful platform from which to review current understanding of woodland NVC in Wales, and give a snapshot of the woodland resource at the end of the 20th Century.  The total area surveyed is around 11,500 ha from 802 sites across Wales.   This data collation was also used to identify future research and survey needs. Areas requiring additional survey and any gaps in the understanding of communities could also be highlighted.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T13:46:17.561Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n17784\tNRW_DS110509\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.233Z\t382\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring and Surveillance of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353039\tMarine Monitoring is stored W: Drive  Terrestrial SAC Monitoring Paper Records: A filing cabinet of paper records which have not been digitised and is current stored with Tom Harrison.    Electronic Data: Our electronic data is stored in the following locations: Pre 2013 - On Ffynnon, SAC monitoring data is stored under the Monitoring folder for each SAC, e.g. Protected Sites - North Region - North West - Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn SAC ( SH55.11.100 ) – Monitoring Within each monitoring folder there will be a folder for each monitoring cycle of each feature, e.g. for the Gwyrfai there are folders for “Luronium natans 99 to 06”, “Luronium natans 07 to 12”, “Lutra lutra 99 to 06”, “Lutra Lutra 07 to 12”, etc.  There will also be a folder called “SAC Monitoring reports” which should contain the final SAC monitoring reports for each feature.  Post 2013 where projects were started once the DMS had been set up. This folder system is recreated on DMS for monitoring undertaken in the last 18 months or so.  It is all stored under the Land Management part of DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landman/SitePages/Home.aspx\tInternal Custodians: North Region: Tom Harrison, Environmental Assessment Officer, NE EAAT. South Region: Karen Wilkinson, Environmental Assessment Officer, SE EAAT South Region: Dan Guest, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, SW EAAT South Region: Alex Harding, Team Leader, SW EAAT Used to be: David Allen, Environmental Monitoring Manager, CCW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results from on going monitoring of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) across Wales. One of the key responsibilities of the statutory nature conservation agencies, such as Natural Resources Wales (NRW), in the UK is the identification and protection of a series of sites intended to conserve important wildlife and earth science features. These sites include SACs which are designated as part of the EC Habitats Directive.  Every six years, Member States of the European Union are required to report on implementation of the Habitats Directive, under Article 17 of the Directive. This reporting provides information on: the general implementation of the Directive; and the conservation status of individual habitats and species listed under the Annexes of the Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:54.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17782\tNRW_DS110510\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.138Z\t896\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCondition Monitoring of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353130\tHigh level data are held centrally by CCW Environmental Change Group.  Supporting data are held in Regional CCW offices or by Science Groups.  Published reports are available from CCWs libraries.\tInternal Custodian: Dylan Lloyd, Specialist Advisor - Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team  (Formerly: David Allen, Team Leader, Monitoring, Strategy and Planning, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW)\tThe Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) series has developed since 1949 as the suite of sites providing statutory protection for the best examples of the UK\u0027s flora, fauna, or geological or physiographical features. Originally notified under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949, SSSIs were re-notified under the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981. Improved provisions for the protection and management of SSSIs were introduced by the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000. These sites are also used to underpin other national and international nature conservation designations. The SSSIs designation may extend into intertidal areas out to the jurisdictional limit of local authorities. In Wales, the limit is Mean Low Water for SSSIs notified before 2002, and, for more recent notifications, the limit of Lowest Astronomical Tides (LAT), where the features of interest extend down to LAT.  Condition monitoring of SSSIs is essential to determine whether or not they are in satisfactory condition. This dataset contains the results of a single rapid review exercise and a programme of Common Standards Monitoring carried out by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to assess the condition of SSSI features in Wales.  The data from the rapid review monitoring was collected to provide an indicative picture of the condition of a sample of SSSI features. Common Standards Monitoring data is collected and assessed against a set of agreed criteria to provide an indication of the condition in each case. The first cycle of common standards monitoring took place between 2001 and 2006. The first rapid review exercise was carried out in 2003 and was updated in 2006.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:45.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17790\tNRW_DS110511\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.357Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring and Surveillance of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353131\t\tInternal Custodian: Dylan Lloyd, Specialist Advisor - Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tA variety of monitoring and surveillance projects are carried out on SSSIs across Wales.  Geographic coverage: SSSIs throughout Wales\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17785\tNRW_DS110512\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.231Z\t465\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring and Surveillance of National Nature Reserves (NNRs) in Wales\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353132\tIndividual NRW NNR warden offices. Data is fed into Countryside Monitoring System (CMS)  which is held on an external server accessed through a network connection. Contact Tom Hellewell about this.   The reserve staff and wardens hold a lot of the data - and also use a lot of the SAC and SSSI monitoring data to inform their site management plans.  September 2021 update - We still ask the NNR managers to update the data in CMSi. This was last done in November 2019. In the past we’ve tried to get it updated every 2 or 3 years – but looking to extend this to every 5 or 6 years as things really weren’t changing much. CMSi is good for at least another 2 years. There are plans to migrate the data to another system and the provisional plan was to do some development work on AMX to allow for this. However, this didn’t make the cut for this years IT development and no guarantee it will for next year either. Also not sure how this is affected by the AMX monitoring module – which I understand has been put on hold.\tInternal Custodian: Thomas Hellawell, Lead Specialist Advisor – Estates, Estate Planning Team Formerly David Allen, Environmental Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team\tNational Nature Reserves (NNRs) contain examples of some of the most important natural and semi-natural terrestrial and coastal ecosystems in Great Britain. They are managed to conserve their habitats or to provide special opportunities for scientific study of the habitats communities and species represented within them. In addition they may be managed to provide public recreation that is compatible with their natural heritage interests. NNRs are declared by the statutory country conservation agencies under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949 and the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981.  This dataset contains the monitoring and surveillance of work carried out on National Nature Reserves (NNRs) in areas such as water quality, water level, water chemistry, evapotranspiration, water chemistry, meteorology, soil temperature, atmospheric chemistry, habitats and species. Examples of particular site monitoring include the Coppicing Surveillance at Coedydd Aber or surveillance of vegetation changes following the removal of grazing from Cwm Idwal  (see \u0027contains\u0027 field for more information).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1970-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:26.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17773\tNRW_DS110513\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.657Z\t339\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCondition monitoring of Special Protection Areas (SPAs) in Wales\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353133\tVarious locations.\tInternal Custodian: Dylan Lloyd, Specialist Advisor - Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team  Matty Murphy  - NRW\u0027s main contact for Bird Data\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPAs) are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive, which came into force in April 1979. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive), and for regularly occurring migratory species. Once sites have been designated, the country agencies work with owners, occupiers and others to secure their management and protection regime. One element of this work was a programme of species monitoring across the site. This is a collation of existing monitoring of bird species across SPAs. This work is often undertaken by contractors or partners such as the British Trust for Ornithology (BTO) and the RSPB. The RSPB have previously collated all the data on behalf of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The first round of assessments occurred between 2001-2006. Second round undertaken by funded partnership post within RSPB for assessment period 2007 - 2013.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:42.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17783\tNRW_DS110514\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.231Z\t599\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring and Surveillance of Special Protection Areas (SPAs) in Wales\t© CNC/NRW and various Third Party recorders Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353134\tContact - Patrick Lindley for information about where NRW store their bird monitoring data.\tMatty Murphy - Maritime Ornithologist/ Marine vertebrate team leader, Natural resources planning/Marine and freshwater ecosytem group Patrick Lindley - Maritime Ornithologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW (Formerly David Allen, Environmental Monitoring Manager, CCW)\tThis dataset contains the results from on going monitoring of Special Protection Areas (SPAs) across Wales. SPAs are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive, which came into force in April 1979. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive), and for regularly occurring migratory species. One of the key responsibilities of the statutory nature conservation agencies, such as Natural Resources Wales (NRW), in the UK is the identification and protection of a series of sites intended to conserve important birds. Article 12 under the EC Birds Directive deals with the general reporting obligations of the Member States and Commission. Every three to four years, Member States of the European Union are required to report on implementation of the Birds Directive. This reporting provides information on: the general implementation of the Directive; and the conservation status of individual species listed under the Annexes of the Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)-British Trust for Ornithology (BTO)\t1983-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:06:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17777\tNRW_DS110515\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.057Z\t286\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLicencing Data for Welsh Assembly Government (WAG) and Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Licensing Authorities.\t© CNC/NRW / WG NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353135\ta) Archives and Dylan Lloyds many filing cabinets (\u0026gt;10) in OSG b) Tracking of activity spreadsheet is currently stored on CCW\u0027s Species Protection forum. Other electronic data is currently stored on Dylan Lloyds user drive, in copies of Recorder or in ISIS.\tDylan Lloyd, Senior Species Protection Officer, Operational Support Group, NRW\tThe relevant licensing authority depends on the legal purpose of each licence. For licences for which former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is the licensing authority, data includes electronic or hardcopy licence information e.g. historic archive of licence applications, copies of licences, licence return forms and correspondence, and electronic data such as copies of licences.  Where WAG is the licensing authority consultation with CCW occurs and a tracking of activity is kept on an electronic spreadsheet.\tWelsh Government-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17776\tNRW_DS110516\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.984Z\t622\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBat Licence Return Data (1996 - 2009)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353136\tExcel spreadsheet available from Environmental Information Team, CCW.  Paper licence returns stored with Operational Support Group, CCW Species data is held in Recorder 6: Bat Licence Returns CCW0008000000005.\tSenior Species Protection Officer, Operational Support Group, CCW\tThis former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), who are now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) dataset is a collation of licence return data for work carried out on bat sites in Wales. The collation was created to add to the picture of bat distributions in Wales, to collect relevant data in one place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available. The dataset does not represent a systematic coverage/survey of Wales. Location of records is generally random, depending on the requirements of the licence holders. Records are provided by licence holders, who have very variable recording ability. The dataset has been subject to some basic validation to eradicate erroneous outlying records and inputting errors.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various including volunteer recorders\t2009-07-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:30:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17770\tNRW_DS110517\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.519Z\t329\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBadger Licence Return Data (1997 - 2008)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders   NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353137\tExcel spreadsheet available from Environmental Information Team, NRW.  Paper licence returns stored with Operational Support Group, NRW. Some data is stored in Recorder 6\tSenior Species Protection Officer, Operational Support Group, NRW (Adam Cole-King)\tThis dataset consists of former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCWs\u0027) collation of licence return data for work carried out affecting badgers and their setts across Wales.  The purpose of this data capture was to: -  add to the picture of  badger distributions in Wales - collect relevant data in one place, and  - mobilise records which may not otherwise be available.   The dataset does not represent a systematic survey coverage  of Wales, and is instead based upon ad hoc records collected from licence returns. Location of records is generally random, depending on the requirements of the licence holders.  Records are provided by licence holders, who have very variable recording ability. The dataset has been subject to some basic validation to eradicate erroneous outlying records and inputting errors.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various including volunteer recorders\t2008-08-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:43.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17775\tNRW_DS110518\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.614Z\t363\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOtter Licence Return Data (1905 - 2008).\t© CNC/NRW  PAPER RECORDS: NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.   SPECIES DATA: Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353138\tExcel spreadsheet available from Environmental Information Team, CCW. Paper licence returns stored with Operational Support Group, CCW.  Species data held in Recorder 6: Otter Licence Returns CCW0008000000007\tInternal Custodian, Senior Species Protection Officer, Operational Support Group, CCW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset is a former Countryside Council for Wales collation of licence return data for work carried out affecting otters in Wales.The collation was created to add to the picture of otter distributions in Wales, to collect relevant data in one place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available.\tVarious including volunteer recorders\t2008-03-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:18:00.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17771\tNRW_DS110519\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.510Z\t696\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWild Bird Licence Return Data\t© CNC/NRW, Various Recorders NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353139\tData is currently held and custodianship rests with the RSPB\tDylan Lloyd, Environmental Surveillance Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW (Formerly Sian Whitehead, Senior Ornithologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW)\tA collation of licence return data for bird species listed under the EC Directive on the conservation of wild birds and schedule 1 of the Wildlife and Countryside Act. Excludes ringing licences, which are handled by British Trust for Ornithology (BTO). The collation was created to add to the picture of wild birds distributions in Wales, to collect relevant data in one place, and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various including volunteer recorders\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17769\tNRW_DS110520\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.310Z\t469\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOther Protected Animals Licence Return Data\t© CNC/NRW and various third party data recorders.  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353230\tPaper licence returns stored with Operational Support Group, CCW\tDylan Lloyd, Environmental Surveillance Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) collation of licence return data for work carried out in Wales affecting protected animal species. This collation includes invertebrates such as, but not limited to, marsh fritillary, crayfish and fairy shrimp. The collation was created to add to the picture of protected animals distributions in Wales, to collect relevant data in one place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available. The dataset does not represent a systematic coverage/survey of Wales. Location of records is generally random, depending on the requirements of the licence holders.\tVarious including volunteer recorders-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:32:23.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17774\tNRW_DS110521\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.656Z\t315\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPlant Licence Return Data (2003 - 2006)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353231\tPaper licence returns stored with Operational Support Group and Andy Jones, CCW.   Species data held on Recorder 6 Plant Licence Returns CCW0008000000003.\tSenior Species Officer, Casework Team, NRW\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW) collation of licence return data for work carried out in Wales affecting protected plant species. The collation was created to add to the picture of protected plant distributions in Wales, to collect relevant data in one place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various including volunteer recorders\t2006-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17772\tNRW_DS110688\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.649Z\t585\tValid\tEditable\tCountryside Council for Wales\u0027 Marine Monitoring Sample Stations for UKDMOS\t© CNC/NRW 2009. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130363838\tThis data is archived under Project No. 230, Media No. 599. Raw data provided to BODC for upload to UKDMOS. This data can be accessed via the UKDMOS website.  http://www.ukdmos.org/welcome.asp.\tInternal Custodian - Marine Monitoring Data Officer, MFSG.\tThe exact location of sampling stations that occur at each of the Marine Monitoring Sites.  The purpose of this data capture is to provide an overview of legacy Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) Marine Monitoring sample stations to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and make these available to a wider audience through a central location.  This will help facilitate a more coordinated approach to Marine Monitoring across the UK and promote the sharing of resources.   Sample stations for a variety of monitoring programmes are available including: Benthic rock and sediment, intertidal rock and sediment, saline lagoons, scoter aerial monitoring, bottlenose dolphins, grey seals, shellfish (bird food) stocks, video and acoustic monitoring. At some locations this is the same as the Marine Monitoring Site location as listed in the separate metadata entry (Marine Monitoring Sites - GIS Dataset) for others it may be a series of samples stations within the sites and for others it may be a larger area that is surveyed remotely.    The Marine Monitoring Sample locations are available online through UK Directory of Marine Observing Systems (UKDMOS).  Included are the following legacy CCW Marine Monitoring programmes;   - Intertidal Monitoring Surveys  - Benthic Subtidal Sediment Monitoring Survey  - Aerial scoter monitoring  - Acoustic Seabed Monitoring of the North Llyn Modiolus reef  - Benthic Rock Monitoring  - Bottlenose Dolphin Monitoring  - Shellfish Stock (bird food) Monitoring  - Skomer Marine Nature Reserve Monitoring  - Across Wales drop-down video monitoring   - Saline lagoon soft sediment survey  - Grey seal monitoring\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\thttp://ukdmos.org/search-directory\t2025-12-01T13:57:09.866Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17768\tNRW_DS110689\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.190Z\t361\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of Yellow Trumpet Anemone Parazoanthus axinellae at Pen Cristen, Bardsey Island\t© CNC/NRW 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130363839\tElectronic data (including maipulated images) is archived under project 219, media no. 800.  Copies of the video data is held on the Imaging drive, the tapes are held in the fireproof safe.  Images are held on the imaging computer and a copy on the MFSG drive.\tMarine Monitoring Officer, MFSG. Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG.\tYellow trumpet anemone Parazoanthus axinellae is primarily a south-western species, found at Bardsey Island and is close to the northern boundary of its known distribution at this location.  This species has been selected as an attribute for monitoring the condition of reefs in the Special Area of Conservation (SACs).  In 1999 an assessment of appropriate methods was made to look at monitoring the abundance and population structure of Parazoanthus axinellae and the erect branching sponge Axinella dissimilis. The trials focused on comparing different techniques to find the one that was most repeatable and therefore sensitive to population change.  Trials took place at Pen Cristen, on Bardsey Island.  Subsequent monitoring of the Parazoanthus axinellae assemblage has occurred annually at this location when weather conditions allow. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the numerical abundance, percentage cover and distribution of individuals within the dominant Parazoanthus axinellae assemblage using a quantitative photographic approach.  This will contribute to condition assessment of the reef feature in the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:44:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17767\tNRW_DS110872\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.130Z\t670\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcoustic Monitoring of South Llyn (East of the Tudwels) and Subsequent Infauna Sampling\t© CNC/NRW 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source. All intellectual property rights (including copyright and database rights) are owned by CCW, except video footage taken by Paul Kay. Applicants seeking to re-use this material should approach Paul Kay directly to obtain permission.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383732\t[A]  Electronic data is also archived under project 236, media no 802: (K:\\archive\\Media 801-900\\Media 802)  Copies of edited material are also archived under media 802. [B]  A working copy of data is held on  S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Sidescan\\tudwals 2009.   [C]  Images are held on DVD19 and videos are on the I drive.  [D]  Samples collected were lost: samples that got lost by the couriers (citylink)  then found and destroyed by them. which could be why [E] has not been completed [E]  Species data to be supplied as spreadsheet and entered to Marine Recorder\tInternal Custodian: Charlie Lindenbaum, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer,  Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team    Metadata Contact: Data Services Team       18/01/2016 - HR has contacted Matt Green and Charlie Lindenbaum about the location of the infaunal sampling data as it currently states that this data is held by Hebog. Once located this data should be entered into Marine Recorder, or at least be added to the import list. Reply stated that the samples were lost in the post.\tA side-scan sonar survey was carried out in Tremadog Bay following reports of possible scallop dredging within the Sea Fisheries Scallop Exclusion Zone.  The acoustic survey was followed up by infaunal sampling to look at the effect of disturbance of the substrata within the area. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate reports of possible scallop dredging within the Exclusion Zone and its effects on biodiversity within the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Exclusion Zone.  Confidence in this dataset is high. The side-scan data is processed to reduce errors (e.g. positional corrections).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:45:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17766\tNRW_DS110885\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.095Z\t612\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring Survey of the Effect of Parys Mountain Discharge around Amlwch, North Anglesey (2003)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2003. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383835\t[A]  This project is archived under project no. 253, media no 907 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 907) [B]  Survey data held in Marine recorder survey: MRCCW3000000002 [C]  Undergraduate project on copper concentrations in mussels available from Kirsten Ramsay (on paper, now send to off sire storage) [D] Paper MNCR forms in off-site storage (Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Contact: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe purpose of this data capture was to detect impacts of pumping discharge from the disused copper mines in Parys Mountain.  Intertidal surveys conducted around Amlwch, North Anglesey, during the early part of 2003 to detect impacts of pumping discharge from the disused copper mines in Parys Mountain into the Afon Goch (North) that flows into the sea underneath the Great Lakes plant in Amlwch. In addition there is a undergraduate project that looked at concentrations of copper in Mytilus edulis samples. These projects are both unpublished. The purpose of this data capture was to detect  impacts of pumping discharge from the disused copper mines in Parys Mountain.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-06-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:02:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17818\tNRW_DS110886\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.140Z\t756\tValid\tEditable\tEpiflora Diversity Within Eelgrass (Zostera marina) Beds on the Welsh Coast\t© CNC/NRW 2003. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383836\tHeld as a Marine Recorder survey: MRCCW30000000036\tCustodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tSeagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas, such as lagoons and channels, which are sheltered from significant wave action. Eelgrass (Zostera marina), is considered to be scarce (present in 16-100 ten km squares) is protected through the EC Habitats Directive and is classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitat. Zostera beds is also on the OSPAR List of threatened and/or declining species and habitats. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor Zostera marine habitat and condition, and this dataset is a collation results of a survey investigating the epifloral diversity within eelgrass beds around the coast of Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:35:41.225Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17812\tNRW_DS110890\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.909Z\t622\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring the Dynamics of the North Llyn Modiolus Reef\t© CNC/NRW 2010 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383930\tAll data is archived under project no.218, media no. 584. Working copies of the data is held on shared MFSG drive\tInternal custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG.  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tModiolus Reefs are a feature of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Information is gathered about the dynamics of the reef through the recording of spat/larval settlement.  This is used as an indicator of current/recent influences on the reef and can provide an early warning of potential problems. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the dynamics of the reef. This contributes to reporting of favourable conservation status of the SAC feature. Data will be used in conjunction with acoustic methods which look at the extent of the feature (reef) and photo quadrat methods that look at the health of the feature.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:46:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17816\tNRW_DS110891\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.943Z\t290\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBritannia Bridge Mussel Survey (1997)\t© CNC/NRW 1997 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383931\tMarine Recorder survey: MRCCW3000000002F\t\tThe purpose of this data capture was to provide information and data towards the casework requirement relating to the sublittoral mussel bed. Surveys took place around Britannia Bridge in the Menai Straits.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-08-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:32:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17824\tNRW_DS110893\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.294Z\t368\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRoxAnn Ground Truthing Video Drops 1997\t© CNC/NRW 1997. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383933\tMarine Recorder Survey: JNCCMNCR40000960.  Original MNCR forms are held in CCW off-site storage. Data is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 237, Media No\u0027s. 1, 75 and 109\tInternal Custodian: Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to increase the available information of species and habitats, thus extending the area covered by survey within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Results of a survey carried out from the Prince Madog Research Vessel as a joint operation by the University of Wales, Bangor and Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). Using RoxAnn, ground truthing video transects within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC were recorded.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:47:08.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17809\tNRW_DS110894\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.939Z\t461\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Sites: Data Collation\t© CNC/NRW 2002 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383934\ta) archived as Project 27, media no 81 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 81)   b) Marine Recorder - SURVEY NUMBERS NOT RECORDED IN METADATA\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tBetween October 2001 and June 2002, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) contracted Sea-Scope to source and collate any Welsh marine biological data which were not in CCW\u0027s possession. The main aim of the Project was to collate extant data, enabling them to be accessible to CCW data management systems and linking to other data collation projects nationally. Priority was to be given to data acquired from Special Areas of Conservation (SACs), particularly those relating to SAC moderation features. A higher priority was to be given to sourcing datasets from areas which had limited representation on the MNCR database, in terms of data already held. Also, datasets collected before 1980, with restricted phyla, or with suspected inaccuracies in species identification were to be given a lower priority.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:48:03.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17822\tNRW_DS110926\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.141Z\t461\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of Carbonate Reef Extent and Fish Population at Holden\u0027s Reef (2003 - 2010)\t© CNC/NRW 2010. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393236\t[A]  Data is archived under project 222, media no. 829.  [B]  Copies of the video data is held on the Imaging drive, the tapes are held in the fireproof safe.  [C[  Images are held on the imaging computer and a copy on the MFSG drive.   [D]  A working file is held on the MFSG drive.\tInternal Custodian: Mike Camplin - Marine Monitoring Team, NRW  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tHolden\u0027s Reef is a small (approximately 40 m diameter), roughly circular reef situated in the northern sector of Cardigan Bay. It is unusual in being formed as a result of a chemical reaction between seawater and the escape of natural gas from the seabed, a phenomenon generally found in much deeper waters.  Holden\u0027s Reef is one of an assemblage of methanogen-precipitated calcium carbonate reefs and was the first of the group to be discovered. This dataset contains the results of a programme of monitoring established to study fish populations and reef extent. It also includes data from an in-house survey carried out at Holden\u0027s Reef in 2003, initiated following the reef\u0027s discovery, and Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) phase 2 surveys carried out in 2005 of \\Little\\\" and \\\"Big\\\" Reef.  Holden\u0027s Reef is monitored for fish abundance and diversity extent and rugosity (the link between rugosity and fish abundance has been seen in several studies). This will contribute to condition assessment of the reef feature in the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC).  Holden\u0027s Reef is situated more than 10 km off shore north west of Barmouth and is approximately 40 m in diameter.  \"\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:33:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17814\tNRW_DS110931\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.895Z\t282\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDatasets from the South-West Britain Sublittoral Survey\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393331\tCCW\u0027s copy of marine recorder (see survey titles and keys in \u0027abstract\u0027) .Paper reports are available from CCW library (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe South-west Britain Sublittoral Survey was a three-year project carried out between 1976 and 1979 by the Field Studies Council for the Nature Conservancy Council. Former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), holds the data from a selection of surveys that make up the project .These are listed under Alternative Title which also includes: - 1978 SWBSS Gower sublittoral survey (JNCCMNCR10000069) - 1978 SWBSS Skokholm survey (JNCCMNCR10000076) The purpose of this data capture was to provide information on the nearshore sublittoral ecosystems present from Pembrokeshire around to Dorest and to assess the conservation interest of these sites. The area included in the survey is the old county of Pembrokeshire, the south coast of Wales, and the coast of Avon, Somerset, Devon, Cornwall and Dorset.\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC)\t1981-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:49:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17813\tNRW_DS110947\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.891Z\t631\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of Algal Diversity and Richness on the Glacial Moraine of Sarn Badrig\t© CNC/NRW 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393437\tAll electronic data is archived under project 221, media no. 587.   Specimens are held in the Marine Monitoring Team Herbarium and scanned versions of these are held on the MFSG drive.   Incidental video is stored on the imaging machine.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSarn Badrig (St Patrick\u0027s Causeway) is a belt reef running in a south-westerly direction approximately 18 km out from Mochras Point near Harlech. This reef forms part of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) protected under the EC Habitats Directive. The reef is composed of glacial moraine deposits and is exposed in patches at low water along most of its length.  The reef structure is very mobile and subject to tidal currents, wave action and sand scour and therefore many of the seaweeds found on the reef are annual or perennial species able to survive the scoured conditions.  These include a number of rare species that would be overgrown by species that would normally establish on more stable reef environment. The purpose of this data capture was to map the distribution of substratum types and select biological indices.  Also to assess and determine a suitable methodology to monitor algal richness within a benthic habitat of dynamic substrata.  The site will only be assessed for its algae richness and diversity every 2 years.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-08-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:50:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17808\tNRW_DS110954\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.927Z\t601\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLowland Grassland Areas of Search Re-visit Files\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393534\tStored on the restricted access GRASSURV directory on the N:drive of Terrestrial Science Group. H:\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV H:\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV\\PHASE II PHOTO AND IMAGE ARCHIVE\\Areas of Search\tStuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\tThe phase 2 lowland grassland survey of Wales came to an end in 2004; since then various lowland grassland sites within the 12 Welsh Areas of Search have been re-visited on an ad hoc basis. This dataset contains the notes from the re-visits including details of any ecological changes to have taken place since the original survey. The purpose of which was to provide a record of ecological change. Some of the revisit file notes also include information on management and ownership history, records of uncommon species present and redrafted vegetation maps. The dataset includes the photographs taken during the re-visits.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17807\tNRW_DS110956\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.765Z\t400\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales: Species Datasets\t© CNC/NRW, 2005.     There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393536\tThis dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  Biological Species Data is also held in Recorder 6. LGSW Brecknock CCW0002600000009    LGSW Carmarthen \u0026amp; Dinefwr CCW0002600000008    LGSW Ceredigion CCW0002600000005    LGSW Clwyd CCW0002600000003    LGSW East Gwynedd CCW0002600000002    LGSW Gwent CCW0002600000012    LGSW Mid \u0026amp; South Glamorgan CCW0002600000011    LGSW Montgomery CCW0002600000004    LGSW Preseli \u0026amp; South Pembroke CCW0002600000007    LGSW Radnor CCW0002600000006    LGSW West Glamorgan \u0026amp; Llanelli CCW0002600000010    LGSW West Gwynedd CCW0002600000001\tCustodians: Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW.  Data Manager, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW. Excel spreadsheets inputted into Recorder 6 by contractors (Charles Copp and Mike Weideli).  The restricted access GRASSURV directory on the N:drive of Terrestrial Science Group.    Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tA detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation. Community definition follows the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). At each site, homogeneous stands of vegetation were identified, sampled with 2 x 2 m quadrats and mapped onto Ordnance Survey base maps or aerial photographs at 1:5000 scale.  This dataset has all Wales coverage with sites varying in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha. The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and with Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and is now managed by Natural Resources Wales. This dataset contains all the floristic quadrat data and site information from the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales.  The dataset has been extensively validated and verified.  This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:15.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17823\tNRW_DS110957\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.088Z\t340\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSublittoral Surveys of Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) Formally a Marine Nature Reserve ( MNR ) Boundaries 1982\t© CNC/NRW 1991 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393537\tPaper report available in NRW\u0027s library collection, see \u0027related titles\u0027 above. Data is stored within JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder as JNCCMNCR10000184. NRW only hold a snapshot of this data.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW External Contact; JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. This dataset consists of the results of a survey carried out to describe the types of sublittoral habitats and communities adjacent to the coast in the region of the eastern boundaries of the Skomer Marine Nature Reserve. The purpose of this data capture was provide a basis for assessing the adequacy of the eastern boundaries in relation to sublittoral areas and their relevance in terms of the presence or absence of unusual communities or communities not well represented within other regions of the reserve.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1983-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:50:52.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17815\tNRW_DS110958\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.924Z\t594\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLittoral Survey of Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) Formally a Marine Nature Reserve ( MNR ) 1982\t© CNC/NRW 1991 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393538\tPaper report available in NRW\u0027s library collection at Maes-y-Fynnon, see \u0027related titles\u0027 above. Data is stored in JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder as - JNCCMNCR10000160. NRW only hold a snapshot of this data.\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team External Custodian: JNCC  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. The results of a survey aimed at describing the major littoral habitats and selected special sites, together with their communities, found around Skomer Island and the Marloes peninsula. The purpose of this data capture was to provide information on the range and distribution of littoral habitats and communities to aid in the drafting of the management plan necessary for the establishment of a Statutory Marine Nature Reserve.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1983-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:51:32.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17819\tNRW_DS110960\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.998Z\t405\tValid\tEditable\tSublittoral Survey of Bardsey and the Llyn Peninsula 1983\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393630\tPaper report available in NRW\u0027s library collection, see \u0027related titles\u0027 above. Data is stored within JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder as JNCCMNCR10000186. NRW only holds a snapshot of this data - contained in All Wales snapshot.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tBardsey Island and the adjacent coast of the Llyn Peninsula was one of seven areas proposed by the Nature Conservancy Council for establishment as a statutory marine nature reserve. This dataset contains the results of a survey carried out in 1983 to describe the range of sublittoral habitats and communities present in nearshore sublittoral areas of Bardsey and the Llyn Peninsula. The purpose of this data capture was to survey and describe the range of habitats and communities present in nearshore sublittoral areas and to assess the scientific interest and make recommendations on the conservation importance of the areas surveyed.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1984-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:34:50.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17811\tNRW_DS110962\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.866Z\t321\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLittoral Survey of Bardsey and the Llyn Peninsula 1983\t© CNC/NRW 1991 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393632\tPaper report available in NRW\u0027s library collection, see \u0027related titles\u0027 above. Data is stored in JNCC\u0027s copy of  Marine Recorder as - JNCCMNCR10000205\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, NRW External Custodian: JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tBardsey Island and the adjacent coast of the Llyn Peninsula was one of seven area proposed by the Nature Conservancy Council for establishment as a statutory marine nature reserve. The purpose of data capture was to describe the range of habitats, communities and species present on littoral rock within the proposed marine nature reserve and to assess the scientific interest and conservation importance of the areas visited. This dataset contains the results of a survey carried out in 1983 to describe the range of habitats, communities and species present on littoral rock on Bardsey and the Llyn Peninsula.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1984-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:54:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17821\tNRW_DS111009\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.122Z\t260\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Biological Survey of Bardsey Island 1977\t© CNC/NRW 1991 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131303039\tData held within JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000228) Paper report available from CCW\u0027s library (see \u0027related titles\u0027 section above)\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist, NRW External Contact: JNCC  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe results of a survey of the littoral and sub-littoral regions around Bardsey Island carried out in 1977. Bardsey Island was being offered for sale in 1977 therefore this survey was initiated in order to gain knowledge of the littoral and sub-littoral surroundings of the island in case public access to the areas was denied by subsequent owners. The data captured was also used to detect any changes since previous surveys and to investigate whether the shores of Bardsey offered a suitable site for a Marine Nature Reserve.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1982-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:41:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17810\tNRW_DS111011\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.861Z\t405\tValid\tEditable\tMonitoring of Limestone Communities and Sponge Morphology at Ynys Moelfre\t© CNC/NRW 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131303131\tAll electronic data is archived under project 226, media no. 898. Quadrat video and incidental video is stored on the imaging machine. A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG shared drive\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSponges are considered an important component of benthic communities in temperate waters and the community composition of subtidal limestone biotopes has been identified as a priority feature for the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC).   Monitoring sponge morphology diversity is used as a qualitative estimate of sponge species diversity and changes in morphological composition of sponge assemblages may also provide an indication of changes in environmental conditions.    The purpose of this data capture was to:  -  Identify community composition of subtidal limestone biotopes and their favourable conservation status.   -  Establish a baseline for the community and monitor changes in the diversity of subtidal sponge morphologies.    Changes in sponge species composition may indicate a change within the sessile community as a whole.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-06-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:43:09.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17820\tNRW_DS111013\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.007Z\t305\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Photographic Collection\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131303133\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1 Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Map Photos.lyr  for GIS layer showing  where location photos were taken Digital and scanned images stored on MFSG shared drive Z:\\Archive\\legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\Phase1_Database\\IntertidalPhotographs\\Validated\u0027. Pre-digital images stored in the Hafod Elfyn fireproof room.  Digital data is also archived as project 204, media 569 (File path K:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science.\tPaul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tA collection of photographs taken during the Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Mapping Survey of Wales and associated Microsoft Access database for viewing and displaying the images.  Supplementary visual information for the Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Mapping Survey of Wales. The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005.   All work has been undertaken by former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team. Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:44:06.261Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17817\tNRW_DS111077\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.924Z\t597\tValid\tEditable\tResults of Studies into the Impact of Hydraulic Cockle Dredging on Intertidal Sediment Flat Communities\t© NRW 1990. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131303737\tData is held within JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder. NRW only hold a snapshot of this data.  Paper report available from NRW library (see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above)\tCustodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW JNCC  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of experimental studies investigating the impact of hydraulic cockle dredging on intertidal sediment flat communities at Lafan Sands, North Wales, and Blackshaw Flats, Solway Firth during 1989 and 1990. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the implications of hydraulic dredging for marine nature conservation.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1990-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:46:05.318Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17806\tNRW_DS111134\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.511Z\t771\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring Tide-Swept Sponge Communities in the Menai Strait, Anglesey and Warrior, Pembrokeshire\t© CNC/NRW 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313334\tElectronic data archived under project 217, media number 801.  A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG drive. Streamed video footage is held on the imaging drive.  Mini DV tapes are stored in the fireproof safe.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSponges are an important component of benthic communities in temperate waters and can become dominant in suitable conditions. Monitoring takes place at 2 sites within the Menai Strait; Coleg Normal and Nelson\u0027s Column and at one site in the Milford Haven (Warrior), Pembrokeshire. Sponge communities in the Menai Strait are dominated by large, relatively fast-growing species, such as Halichondria panicea, Esperiopsis fucorum and Haliclona oculata which thrive in rapid flowing turbid waters. The purpose of this data capture was to record the abundance and \u0027luxuiousness\u0027 of the sponge communities to in order to give an indication of the favourable conservation status of benthic communities within the Special Areas of Conservation (SAC\u0027s).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T10:40:55.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17825\tNRW_DS111139\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.294Z\t355\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of Kelp Depth as an Indication of Turbidity in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Saranac Special Area of Conservation (SAC)\t© CNC/NRW 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313339\tElectronic data archived under project 220, media number 586. A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG drive. Streamed video footage is held on the imaging drive. Mini DV tapes are stored in the fireproof safe\tInternal Contact: Mike Camplin, Marine Monitoring Team Leader, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tTurbidity provides an indication of water clarity and water quality.  The depth to which light can penetrate is reduced with increased turbidity, which may be the result of natural processes in coastal and estuarine waters but can also be caused by anthropogenic causes such as coastal construction and dredge spoil dumping.  Curvie (Laminaria hyperborea) is known to be susceptible to the impacts of elevated turbidity levels and therefore changes in L. hyperborea can be an indicator of changes in turbidity.  Grazing of L. hyperborea by the common sea urchin Echinus esculentus may also have an impact of numbers of kelp plant and therefore numbers of this species present must also be taken into consideration. The purpose of this data capture was to use depth measurements for the distribution of kelp Laminaria hyperborea as a monitoring indicator for medium/long-term changes in turbidity level.  Also to measure the overall \u0027luxuriousness\u0027 (biomass) of the algal turf of the rocky reef.  This information will assist with reporting on the favourable conservation status of reef features in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T10:42:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17803\tNRW_DS111141\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.952Z\t672\tValid\tEditable\tBenthic Ecology of Carmarthen Bay (1998)\t© CNC/NRW and A.P. Woolmer. 2003. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/A.P. Woolmer. and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313431\t[A]  Paper report is available from CCW library (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above).  [B]  Report and data are held within the Marine Archive as Media No. 221 [File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 221) [C]  Sediment analysis data on CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW30000000004).\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe 1979 European Commission Directive (79/409 EEC) on the Conservation of Wild Birds requires Member States to notify the Commission of Special Protection Areas (SPAs) that are of particular importance to the conservation of wild birds.   For some time there had been an increasing body of evidence that Carmarthen Bay was an important site in Britain for wintering common scoters (Melanitta nigra), one of the species protected by the ‘Birds Directive’. This project was stimulated by concern for the Carmarthen Bay macrofaunal communities, on which the wintering population of the Common scoter depend, following the Sea Empress oil spill of February 1996.   This dataset contains the results of a project on the benthic ecology of Carmarthen Bay undertaken by Andrew Woolmer as part of his PhD to investigate the benthos and Scoter populations of Carmarthen Bay.   The purpose of this data capture was to describe quantitatively the distribution of the macrofaunal communities in Carmarthen Bay to provide accurate baseline data, and to assess the distribution and density of potential prey species of Common scoter in an internationally important over- wintering site.   The project comprised of four main areas of study:  1) Macrofaunal communities of Carmarthen Bay  2) Growth, abundance and spatial models of the Tellinacean bivalve Fabulina fibula  3) Macrofaunal response groups: a multivariate method for isolating indicator species along environmental gradients  4) Temporal variability, structural redundance, and the long term persistence of the Carmarthen Bay Tellina Community\tWoolmer-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:47:31.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17802\tNRW_DS111142\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.810Z\t663\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSublittoral Monitoring Trials of North Pen Llyn 1998\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313432\tPaper report available from NRW library.  Data on NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000772).  MNCR survey forms are held in NRW off-site storage.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe data was captured in order to assess the suitability of the biotope classification monitoring method trialled. A measure biotope richness as an attribute of north Pen Llyn reefs, was measured over three surveys transects between July and August 1998.  Surveyors were experienced in Phase 2 survey methodology and each record was carefully re-examined by an experienced operator to ensure that data were comparable to those assigned to biotopes in the national classification, therefore were correctly assigned.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:40:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17800\tNRW_DS111144\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.708Z\t318\tValid\tEditable\tResults of a Study into the Use of Transects to Identify the Broad-Scale Attributes of the Mawddach Estuary. (1999 CCW Marine Monitoring Trials)\t© CNC/NRW 2001 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313434\t[A]  Data held in NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000795).   [B]  Paper MNCR forms are held in NRW off-site storage.   [C]  Archived as project 316, media 1240.\tContact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to investigate the feasibility of using a transect based methodology to determine the proportion of muddy communities and the zonation of muddy communities from upper to lower shore.  The Mawddach is one of the best examples of a small, drying, bar-built estuary in Britain and, with its mountainous catchment area it has some of the lowest nutrient inputs in Wales.   In order to be able to report on the condition of the estuary a number of attributes have been identified which require monitoring.   The aims of this project was to investigate the feasibility of using a transect based methodology to determine the proportion of muddy communities and the zonation of muddy communities from upper to lower shore.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:50:04.301Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17804\tNRW_DS111145\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.953Z\t408\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of Subtidal Rocky Reef in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC)\t© CNC/NRW 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313435\tElectronic data archived under project 223, media number 899. A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG drive. Mini DV tape is stored in the fireproof safe and streamed material on the Imaging PC.  Still images are stored on the MFSG drive and copies are available on DVD12 and DVD21 in the fireproof safe (until room available in the electronic archive).  Specimen samples are stored in the NRW lab and will be archived with The National Museum of Wales in due course.\tInternal Custodian: Mike Camplin, Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of the results from an integrated monitoring strategy on a site level carried out to provide an overview of the status of reef feature within the Pembrokeshire MarineSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) as a whole.  Work is concentration on tide-swept infralittoral and circalittoral rock. 7 sites across the SAC selected and permanent monitoring quadrat established within each of the biotope complexes.  Within the upper parts of the Milford Haven sites were established at Beggars Reach; Warrior and Dockyard Bank.  Near to the mouth of the Haven; sites were established at Chapel Reef; Thorn Island; and Junkos Reef.  A site was also established within the off-shore islands of The Smalls.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-09-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T10:44:21.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17805\tNRW_DS111146\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.946Z\t313\tValid\tEditable\tResults of a Study into the Use of a Phase 2 Sampling Grid as a Monitoring Strategy for the Mawddach Estuary. (1999 CCW Marine Monitoring trials)\t© CNC/NRW 2001 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313436\tData held in NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000739). MNCR paper forms are held in NRW off-site storage.Report available from NRW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above) Archived as project 314, media 1238.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe aim of this survey was to investigate the feasibility of using a phase 2 sampling grid methodology to determine the proportion of muddy and sandy communities and the distribution of major communities within the estuary.  The Mawddach is one of the best examples of a small, drying, bar-built estuary in Britain and, with its mountainous catchment area it has some of the lowest nutrient inputs in Wales. In order to be able to report on the condition of the estuary a number of attributes have been identified which require monitoring.   This dataset contains the results of a survey carried out to trial a sampling strategy and methodology that addresses two of these attributes: - The relative proportion of muddy and sandy communities. - The distribution of major communities within the estuary.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:51:06.176Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17801\tNRW_DS111147\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.800Z\t483\tValid\tEditable\tMacroinvertebrates in the Glaslyn / Dwyryd Estuary\t© CNC/NRW 1991 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313437\tA copy of the report is available in NRW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above).   The survey data has been entered into JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000633). NRW only has a snapshot of this data.\tInternal Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External Contact: JNCC\tThis dataset contains the results of a macro-invertebrate survey of the Glaslyn/Dwyryd estuary undertaken in 1978 in order to better understand the ability of the estuary to receive wading birds that may be displaced by barrage construction. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the capacity of the estuary to receive wading birds that might be displaced from other estuaries on the Irish Sea flyway by barrage construction. Data capture also helped to obtain data relevant to the management of the adjacent Morfa Harlech National Nature Reserve and enabled collection of data on the marine fauna of the area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1978-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:51:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17799\tNRW_DS111148\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.721Z\t405\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMenai Strait Sublittoral Survey 1982 (Paper Data)\t© CNC/NRW 1991 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313438\tA copy of the report is available in NRW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above). The survey data has been entered into JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000293). NRW only hold a snapshot of this data.  Paper Data and associated additional survey cards stored in off site storage – box no. CCW/NRB/118\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Paper Data: contact Records Team or Data Services Team Electronic Data: Marine Recorder Snapshot distributed by JNCC\tThis dataset consists of the result of a sublittoral survey carried out in the Menai Strait during 1982 to investigate the habitats and communities present. The dataset contains site descriptions (name, location, depths surveyed, site features etc), lists of all algae and animal species observed and habitat data. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the sublittoral ecosystems of the Menai Strait and to determine the range and distribution of habitats and communities present within the Menai Strait and on adjacent open coasts.  Original survey forms are held by NRW, mobilised data is available through the JNCC distributed Marine Recorder snapshot\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1983-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T17:25:50.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17798\tNRW_DS111149\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.613Z\t368\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Subtidal Monitoring\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313439\tA copy of the report is available in NRW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above).  The survey data has been entered into JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000156).  NRW only have a snapshot of this data.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Data collected during the development and implementation of low budget stereo-photographic monitoring of rocky subtidal epibenthic communities in within Skomer marine reserve. The data includes descriptions of communities present and subsequent changes in these communities over a one year period (1982 - 1983). The Skomer subtidal monitoring project was established as an ongoing project however this dataset includes the results from the first year only. Purpose of this data capture was to locate and permanently mark suitable monitoring stations and to observe and record the topography and epibenthic communities present in order to enable the assessment of the natural fluctuations in subtidal communities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1983-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:52:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17797\tNRW_DS111150\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.547Z\t353\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh saxicolous lichen and bryophyte community data from a survey of upland Britain\t© CNC/NRW, NE and SNH 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ NE / SNH and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313530\tData is stored on NRW\u0027s upland ecologist\u0027s g:drive. Paper report is available from NRW library (see related titles section above).\tInternal Custodian: Jane Stevens  (used to be: Upland Ecologist, NRW)  Internal Custodian: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe British uplands have two groups of lichen species of outstanding conservation interest: 1) species of western oceanic areas and 2) species of calcareous rock at high altitudes. Phytosociological data were collected to describe the terrestrial lichen and bryophyte vegetation of rock or saxicolous habitats within 14 Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) within upland Britain, with special reference to four saxicolous habitats listed in Annex I of the EU Habitats Directive. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds the Welsh data as well as copies of the project report. The work aimed to improve our knowledge of the lichen and bryophyte communities of rock habitats in upland Great Britain, with particular regard to certain habitats, which have importance in nature conservation legislation. The work had the following main objectives: 1. To describe the lower plant assemblages of Annex I rock habitats in selected sites, which are Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). 2. To provide a preliminary classification of the lower plant communities of rock habitats in upland Britain (excluding freshwater habitats).  These objectives were achieved by extensive sampling of rock habitats, using phytosociological methods, within selected SACs. Those visited were, in Wales: Brecon Beacons, Cadair Idris, Eryri and Berwyn and South Clwyd Mountains.\tScottish Natural Heritage (SNH)-Natural England-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17796\tNRW_DS111152\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.489Z\t749\tValid\tEditable\tPembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Sea Salinity Time Series Data\t© CNC/NRW 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313532\tAll the salinity data is archived under project 225, media 591.  A working copy of the files is stored at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Temperature and salinity loggers\\Salinity Loggers (starr oddi) by year and location.  Metadata about the loggers is stored in Ffynnon.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tWithin Pembrokeshire Special Area of Conservation (SAC) an integrated monitoring strategy at a site level is employed to produce a view of the status of each feature within the SAC as a whole. The Data is captured as part of the Across-Wales Diving Survey, designed to gather information on Reef features across the Marine SAC\u0027s in Wales.  In order to ascertain whether observed changes are due to natural factors or anthropogenically created factors, a suite of environmental factors also need to be considered, such as turbidity, temperature, salinity and water chemistry. Salinity loggers have been placed at sites across Pembrokeshire Marine SAC.    The data is collected continuously, although this has not been processed to produce daily averages due to the low confidence in the data. Additional salinity recordings are taken at Skomer MNR but these are covered in a separate metadata entry. Salinity is also recorded in saline lagoons within Wales. Metadata about this data is held in a separate entry. Gathering physical data such as temperature provides context and background information for the condition assessment of each of the reef features monitored.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:54:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17848\tNRW_DS111154\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.661Z\t8\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Habitats and Species Spatial Layers for Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP), NERC Act, OSPAR (Archived)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2009 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale. If these conditions are abided by, recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313534\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP Species Archived X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\tInternal Custodian: - Natasha Lough - Marine Habitats Biologist, Knowledge Evidence and Advice,  Kirsten Ramsay - Subtidal Team Leader, Knowledge Evidence and Advice.   NRW  Metadata Contact: - Spatial Data and GIS Team, NRW\tThis archived collation of datasets has been superseded by Environment (Wales) Act Section 7: Marine Habitats and Species as part of the newer legislative requirements.    These datasets are associated with the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) was published back in 1994, and was the UK Government\u0027s response to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD), which the UK signed up to in 1992 in Rio de Janeiro. The Convention for the Protection of the Marine Environment of the North-East Atlantic (the \u0027OSPAR Convention\u0027) was open for signature at the Ministerial Meeting of the Oslo and Paris Commissions in Paris on 22 September 1992. The new annex on biodiversity and ecosystems was adopted in 1998 to cover non-polluting human activities that can adversely affect the sea. These two conventions have been highly important for identifying statutory lists of priority species and habitats for Wales. Under Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act, 2006 it is required that the a \u0027list of the living organisms and types of habitat which in the Assembly\u0027s opinion are of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\u0027. The UK BAP is one of the main focuses of this work. As part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 CBD, OSPAR Convention and NERC Act there have been a number of initiatives to identify those species and habitats whose UK populations may be under threat and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity.   The list includes all marine UK BAP priorities as well as additional habitats and species to reflect those in need of conservation action in Wales. For each species and habitat data provided it should include a map of the distribution around the Welsh coast and supporting text with information on the ecology, rarity, legislative protection, occurrence in statutory sites and known threats. This dataset is made up of a collation of datasets which have been updated throughout the years. All of these are listed separately under the \u0027Contains\u0027 field below. These datasets related to the marine species and habitats which are of conservation importance for Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17850\tNRW_DS111157\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.821Z\t6126\tValid\tEditable\tRegional Seascape Units\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2009. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313537\tX:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes regional units.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes coast hight points.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes coast hight regions.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes index value line.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes merged land units single polys.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes merged land units.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes merged sea units single polys.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes merged sea units.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes regional units.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes unit numbers.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes value index points.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes value weightedscore land units.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes value weightedscore line.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Landscape Architect, Environmental Policy Group, NRW.\tA \u0027seascape\u0027 is an area of sea, coastline and land as perceived by people, whose character results from the actions and interaction of land and sea, by natural and/or human factors.   This dataset is the product of the first detailed assessment of the character of Wales\u0027s seascapes. The purpose of this data capture was to characterise and define the geographical extent of the seascape units of Wales and to assess the sensitivity of each seascape to offshore developments.   The dataset contains a map, images, and descriptive text for each of the 50 seascapes units in Wales. Also included are maps illustrating the national and regional divisions of seascape units, land with sea views, relative visibility of the sea surface from land and tranquillity. There are also maps presenting the results of sensitivity assessments carried out to model the sensitivity of seascape units to offshore wind farm developments, wave farm developments and tidal stream developments. Included in the dataset is a pdf file outlining what exactly the MapInfo files in the dataset show.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:55:39.144Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17855\tNRW_DS111158\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.917Z\t477\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Nature Conservation Review Intertidal Survey of Rhos Point to New Brighton\t© CNC/NRW 1991 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313538\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on a copy of JNCC\u0027s Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000240). NRW only hold a snapshot of this data - part of All Wales snapshot)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe results of intertidal surveys carried out on the flora and fauna of sixteen sites within a survey area along the southern coast of Liverpool Bay. The shores within the survey area were mainly sediment shores however at some sites rocky substratum was present.  The purpose of this data capture was to describe a representative range of intertidal habitats and their associated flora and fauna from locations on the open coast distributed throughout the survey area. The survey was part of the Marine Nature Conservation Review which had the aims of extending our knowledge of British marine ecosystems, identifying sites of nature conservation importance and providing information to support more general measures required to minimise adverse effects of development and pollution.   The sampling area extends from Rhos-on-Sea in the west to New Brighton in the east, (approximately 50 km) and comprises of the majority of the southern coast of Liverpool Bay. All sites were surveyed between the 23rd - 25th of May 1990, and some sites were subject to additional visits on the 17th and 18th of September 1990.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1991-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T10:54:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17839\tNRW_DS111159\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.464Z\t348\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Reserve Sublittoral Habitats and Communities 1983\t© CNC/NRW 1991. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313539\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above.  Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR60000117).  NRW only hold a snapshot of this data\tInternal Custodian:  Assistant Marine Conservation Officer - Skomer MNR Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ).  This dataset contains the results of the survey of 44 sublittoral survey sites around the Skomer Marine Reserve during 1983. 111 species of plant and 276 animal species were noted (27 species of algae and 27 species of animal were previously unrecorded from the area). The purpose of this data capture was to collect information necessary to provide revised descriptions of habitats and communities for the Skomer Marine Reserve (SMR) and to continue the SMR seaweed survey (first started in 1982) in order to provide information on algal communities around the reserve and to provide quantitative information on seaweed communities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1984-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T09:26:23.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17841\tNRW_DS111160\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.502Z\t262\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey of Mynydd Preseli\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313630\tH: drive, under Science/TSG/PEATSURV/Upland Survey Preseli H:\\Science\\TSG\\PEATSURV\\Upland Survey Preseli  Legacy information: Draft report is currently held by Alex Turner CCWs ECN Project Officer, once finalised this will be from CCW libraries. The quadrat data is held in an excel spreadsheet, also stored by Alex Turner, whilst the vegetation maps are available as MapInfo tables on CCW Upland Ecologists user drive.\tDataset collected by CCW Staff including Alex Turner, Sam Bosanquet DK Reed AM Averis        Sam Bosanquet said that Alex never finished the Preseli write-up - with the excel spread sheets being in Ffynnon, and the GIS data being somewhere on ArcGIS. However it could still be in MapInfo format.\tThis dataset is realated to a draft Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) report entitled \u0027A vegetation survey of Mynydd Preseli, Pembrokeshire 2003-2005\u0027. The quadrat data, which details the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) habitat codes, has not yet been validated.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:53.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17847\tNRW_DS111161\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.557Z\t660\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNant Teyrn Freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site\t© CNC/NRW and Welsh Government, 2014 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ WG and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313631\tSee individual metadata entries in the \u0027contains\u0027 field above for more details.\tCustodian: Victoria Bowmaker and Alex Turner, ECN Project Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tEnvironmental Change Network (ECN) is network of sites spanning the UK where we conduct environmental monitoring using standard methods. There are currently 12 terrestrial sites and 45 freshwater sites, covering a wide range of upland and lowland habitats including moorland, chalk grassland, woods and forests, farmland, small ponds and streams, large rivers and lakes. ECN is the UK member of the International Long-Term Ecological Research Network (ILTER) and of LTER-Europe. The ECN\u0027s objectives are to establish and maintain a network of sites from which to obtain comparable long-term datasets through the monitoring of a range of variables; to integrate and analyse these data to identify natural and man-induced environmental changes, improve understanding of the causes of change, distinguish short-term fluctuations from long-term trends, and predict future changes.  Nant Teyrn is a small upland stream on the out flow from Llyn Teyrn in Yr Wyddfa National Nature Reserve. This freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) site is co-located with Yr Wyddfa/Snowdon terrestrial ECN site.  As part of the ECN project long term monitoring of physical, chemical and biological variables occurs at Nant Teyrn. The monitoring is designed to collect, store, analyse and interpret long-term datasets for physical, chemical and biological variables, which drive or respond to environmental change. The data is subject to a variety of quality control procedures (QA). Each monitoring protocol has a corresponding paper form. The data is entered into an Excel spreadsheet which has background calibration graphs in order to flag any outliers within the data. These outliers can then be double checked against the paper records. Every year the spreadsheet is checked against the paper records, and each line of data within the spreadsheet is assigned a quality code which helps to flag up any problems during data collation e.g. snow in the gauges. The CEH CCU also has its own QA procedures which they follow on receipt of the data.\tWelsh Government-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:53.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17846\tNRW_DS111162\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.534Z\t754\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features of Morfa Uchaf Dyffryn Conwy Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) 1990-1994\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 1994. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313632\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media901to1000\\Media904 Archived as Project 251, Media 904. Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Conwy 1990 to 1994 Original paper maps, quadrat cards, and floristic tables are in North Region files (Mold Office)\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNational Vegetation Classification has been used as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the southern Conwy Estuary.  The principal objective of the survey was to provide a suitable basis for Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) designation. Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors and CCW staff.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1994-08-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T09:27:14.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17852\tNRW_DS111164\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.918Z\t798\tUnassessed\tEditable\tYr Wyddfa/Snowdon Terrestrial Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site - Physical Data\t© CNC/NRW and WG 2014 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ WG and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313634\ta) The excel spreadsheet is stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff. The ECN data is also available from CEH.  b) Paper forms are filed in NRW\u0027s ECN team office c) Paper copies of reports are held in NRW\u0027s library, electronic versions are held by NRW\u0027s ECN team. d) ECN team members m:drives\tInternal Custodian:  Victoria Bowmaker, and Alex Turner, Environmental Change Network Project Officer, NRW  Metadata Contact:  Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tPhysical data collected at the Environmental Change Network (ECN) terrestrial site - Yr Wyddfa.  The 700ha site incorporates the summit of Yr Wyddfa and includes three additional summits over 800m, it also includes 5 lakes. It is part of Yr Wyddfa National Nature Reserve managed by NRW. Measurements are taken of various meteorological, surface water and soil variables. The purpose of this data capture was to use the observed changes in physical variables to indicate the possible causes and consequences of environmental change in the terrestrial environment. See overarching Yr Wyddfa ECN metadata entry (\u0027part of\u0027 field below) for general ECN objectives.\tWelsh Government-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:29:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17844\tNRW_DS111165\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.536Z\t574\tUnassessed\tEditable\tYr Wyddfa/Snowdon Terrestrial Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site - Chemical Data\t© CNC/NRW  and WG 2014 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ WG and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313635\ta) The excel spreadsheet is stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff.  The ECN data is also available from CEH. b) Paper forms are filed in NRW\u0027s ECN team office c) Paper copies of reports are held in NRW\u0027s library, electronic versions are held by NRW\u0027s ECN team. d) ECN team members m:drives\tVictoria Bowmaker, and Alex Turner, Environmental Change Network Project Officer, NRW.\tThis dataset is a collation of the chemical data collected at the Environmental Change Network (ECN) terrestrial site - Yr Wyddfa. The 700ha site incorporates the summit of Yr Wyddfa and includes three additional summits over 800 m, it also includes 5 lakes. Measurements taken for chemistry include atmospheric chemistry and precipitation chemistry. As well as the ECN monitoring protocols, other data collected at the site includes the results of ammonia monitoring taking place at Dyffryn Mymbyr, and monitoring of Ozone carried out at Marchlyn Mawr.  The purpose of this data capture is to use the observed changes in chemical variables to indicate the possible causes and consequences of environmental change in the terrestrial environment.\tWelsh Government-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17836\tNRW_DS111166\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.439Z\t669\tUnassessed\tEditable\tYr Wyddfa/Snowdon Terrestrial Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site - Biological Data\t© CNC/NRW and Welsh Government 2014 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ Welsh Government and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313636\ta) The excel spreadsheet is stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff. The ECN data is also available from CEH. b) Paper forms are filed in NRW\u0027s ECN team office c) Paper copies of reports are held in NRW\u0027s library, electronic versions are held by NRW\u0027s ECN team. d) ECN team members m:drives\tInternal Contact: Victoria Bowmaker, or Alex Turner, Environmental Change Network Project Officer, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of biological data collected at the Environmental Change Network (ECN) terrestrial site - Yr Wyddfa / Snowdon North Wales.The 700ha site incorporates the summit of Yr Wyddfa and includes three additional summits over 800 m, it also includes 5 lakes.  Aspects of the vegetation, vertebrates and invertebrates at the site are monitored following ECN protocols. As well as the ECN monitoring protocols, other data collected purely for Natural Resources Wales (NRW) use and include data on the arctic alpine flora and fungi. Phenological information is also collected (information on the timing if things e.g. tadpole hatching). The purpose of this data capture is to collect data on the biodiversity of the site and how it changes over time in order to indicate the possible causes and consequences of environmental change in the terrestrial environment.\tWelsh Government-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17851\tNRW_DS111167\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.798Z\t629\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNant Teyrn Freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site - Physical and Chemical data\t© CNC/NRW and Welsh Government 2014 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ WG and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313637\ta) The excel spreadsheet is stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff. The data also gets sent to CEH.  b) Paper forms are filed in NRW\u0027s ECN team office c) Paper copies of reports are held in NRW\u0027s library, electronic versions are held by NRW\u0027s ECN team d) ECN team members m:drives\tCustodian: Victoria Bowmarker, Alex Turner ECN Project Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tEnvironmental Change Network (ECN) is network of sites spanning the UK where we conduct environmental monitoring using standard methods. There are currently 12 terrestrial sites and 45 freshwater sites, covering a wide range of upland and lowland habitats including moorland, chalk grassland, woods and forests, farmland, small ponds and streams, large rivers and lakes. ECN is the UK member of the International Long-Term Ecological Research Network (ILTER) and of LTER-Europe. Nant Teyrn is a small upland stream on the out flow from Llyn Teyrn in Yr Wyddfa National Nature Reserve. This freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) site is co-located with Yr Wyddfa/Snowdon terrestrial ECN site. This dataset consists of the physical and chemical data collected at the ECN freshwater site at Nant Teyrn, Snowdonia. Measurements taken include surface water discharge and surface water chemistry. The physical and chemical variables measured are expected to indicate the possible causes and consequences of environmental change in the aquatic environment.\tWelsh Government-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:29:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17840\tNRW_DS111168\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.513Z\t669\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNant Teyrn Freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site - Biological data\t© CNC/NRW and Welsh Government 2014 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ Welsh Government and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313638\ta) The excel spreadsheet is stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff. The data also gets sent to CEH.  b) Paper forms are filed in NRWs ECN team office c) Paper copies of reports are held in NRW\u0027s library, electronic versions held by NRW\u0027s ECN team. d) ECN team members m:drives\tData Custodians: Victoria Bowmaker, or Alex Turner - ECN Project Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tEnvironmental Change Network (ECN) is network of sites spanning the UK where we conduct environmental monitoring using standard methods. There are currently 12 terrestrial sites and 45 freshwater sites, covering a wide range of upland and lowland habitats including moorland, chalk grassland, woods and forests, farmland, small ponds and streams, large rivers and lakes. ECN is the UK member of the International Long-Term Ecological Research Network (ILTER) and of LTER-Europe.  Nant Teyrn is a small upland stream on the out flow from Llyn Teyrn in Yr Wyddfa National Nature Reserve. This freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) site is co-located with Yr Wyddfa/Snowdon terrestrial ECN site. This dataset is made up of the biological data collected at the Environmental Change Network (ECN) freshwater site at Nant Teyrn, Snowdonia. Measurements and observations are made on phytoplankton, aquatic macrophytes, epithilic diatoms and macro-invertebrates.  The biological variables measured are expected to indicate the possible causes and consequences of environmental change in the aquatic environment.\tWelsh Government-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17854\tNRW_DS111170\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.895Z\t651\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Sublittoral Habitats and Communities 1984\t© CNC/NRW 1991 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313730\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above.   Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000161). NRW only hold a snapshot of this data.\tInternal Custodian:  Assistant Marine Conservation Officer - Skomer MNR Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ).  This dataset contains the results of surveys of habitats and communities from 90 previously unsurveyed or poorly described sites around Skomer Marine Reserve. The dataset includes site locations, grid references, site descriptions and site maps; habitat information; community categories and species distributions.  The purpose of this data capture was to add to the results of past surveys in collecting the information necessary to provide revised descriptions of habitats and communities for the whole reserve. A major aim of the survey was to produce more photographs of species and habitats in the reserve.  The survey spanned the period between the 7th of March and the 6th of September 1984, with the majority of survey work undertaken in the summer months, specifically August.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1985-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T09:27:55.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17843\tNRW_DS111172\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.542Z\t432\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland vegetation survey of Bwlch Corog, Ceredigion (2005)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444.  Crown Copyright and Database Right   Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313732\tQuadrat data is held in an excel spreadsheet, stored by Alex Turner, whilst the vegetation maps are available as MapInfo tables on CCW Upland Ecologists user drive. A paper copy of the report is available from CCW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above)\tInternal Custodian:  (used to be Alex Turner) Sam Bosenquet, Snr Habitat Survey \u0026amp; Evaluation \u0026amp; Non-Vascular Plant Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset comprises of the results from vegetation survey work carried out at Bwlch Corog. Bwlch Corog is located between two Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs): Pengarreg−gopa a Moel Hyrddod SSSI and Pencreigiau\u0027r Llan SSSI, which are notified for their blanket mire, dry and wet heath vegetation. The survey area can be divided into three main sub−areas: the enclosed fields of Pemprys and Cefn Coch separated by the more upland area of Bwlch Corog. The vegetation of this central area can be regarded as the most semi−natural and the principal vegetation types are M25 Molinia−Potentilla mire, M17 Scirpus−Eriophorum blanket mire, M15 Scirpus− Erica wet heath, H12 Calluna− Vaccinium heath, M6 Carex−Sphagnum mire U4 Festuca− Agrostis−Galium grassland, U5 Nardus−Galium grassland and U6 Juncus−Festuca grassland. There are large expanses of species−poor Molinia−dominated vegetation pointing to a prior history of frequent burning and heavy grazing which probably ceased when the adjacent forestry was planted.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17838\tNRW_DS111173\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.455Z\t364\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSublittoral Monitoring Trials of Sarn Badrig Reef (1997 - 1998)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313733\tData held in NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (1998 survey- JNCCMNCR40000773, 1999 quadrat survey MRCCW30000000006, 1999 transect survey JNCCMNCR40000776 ). Report available from NRW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above) MNCR paper forms are held in NRW off-site storage.\tMarine Monitoring Biologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW\tThe data was captured in order to assess the suitability of the biotope classification monitoring method trialled. Sublittoral monitoring trials were done to investigate the extent of biotopes on Sarn Badrig reef- the most extensive of three Sarnau reefs within Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC. In total three surveys were been carried out to investigate the biotopes present.  1) A 1998 survey to evaluate the deployment of divers for measuring the extent of the Sarnau reef biotopes.  2) A 1999 survey of Sarn Badrig reef using quadrat methodology.  3) A 1999 transect survey of the reef.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:13:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17853\tNRW_DS111174\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.917Z\t337\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sediment Flats in Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2007\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2009. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313734\t[A]  Data is held within the marine archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 549 [B]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 56 - MS\\ampersand\\\"CB intertidal monitoring 2007 ASML [C]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\Data [D]  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder [E]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\GIS [F]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\Images\"\t\tThe purpose of this data capture was to establish the reference condition from which to establish conservation objectives; and to be able to take account of natural changes in the appropriate management of the features of the Special Area of Conservation (SAC). In addition to this the data collected to establish sites for long term monitoring and collect baseline/reference data using appropriate methods, in accordance with the conservation objectives.  Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC is designated for five Annex 1 habitats: Sandbanks which are slightly covered by sea water all the time, Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide, Reefs, Large shallow inlets and bays and Submerged or partially submerged sea caves. This dataset includes the results of an intertidal monitoring survey carried out on the sediment flats at Traeth Lafan during September 2007. The intertidal sediment communities across Traeth Lafan are considered to be of particular nature conservation importance and their good condition (Favourable status) is listed as a Conservation Objective of the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. Performance Indicators were still being developed for this feature, so this survey focuses on the richness, distribution and abundance of sediment biotopes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T10:55:39.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17837\tNRW_DS111175\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.431Z\t862\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Tide-Swept Boulder Habitat in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2009. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313735\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 552. [A] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 56 - MS\\ampersand\\\"CB intertidal monitoring 2007 ASML [B] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\Data [C] NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder - MRCCW16300000005 [D] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\GIS [E] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\Images\"\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThere were two purposes for this data capture. Firstly, to establish the reference condition from which to establish conservation objectives; and to be able to take account of natural changes in the appropriate management of the features of the Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Secondly, to repeat survey of sites surveyed in 2005 to further test and develop monitoring methodology and collect monitoring data. Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC is designated for five Annex 1 habitats; Sandbanks which are slightly covered by sea water all the time, Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide, reefs, large shallow inlets and bays; and submerged or partially submerged sea caves. Intertidal mudflats and sandflats in this location include a variety of biotopes, many of which are present on the large sediment flat of Traeth Lafan. Intertidal reefs include a large variety of biotopes, with tide-swept bedrock, boulder and cobble habitats particularly well represented. This dataset includes the results of an intertidal monitoring survey carried out on the tide-swept boulder habitat just south of Britannia Bridge during September 2007.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T10:57:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17845\tNRW_DS111176\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.530Z\t358\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rocky Reefs in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t© CNC/NRW 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313736\t[A] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 60 - Pembroke Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs [C] NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (Survey No. MRCCW1630000000C) [D] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs\\GIS [E] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs\\Images\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tPembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is considered to be one of the best areas in the UK for marine reef habitats, a category within annex 1 of the Habitats Directive. This dataset comprises of the results of re-surveys of the six Milford Haven rocky intertidal transects first established in 2005, and the additional establishment of a similar transect at Nolton Haven in the north of Pembrokeshire.  The purpose of this data capture was to repeat a set of six permanent monitoring sites representative of the rocky shore intertidal habitats within the Haven Waterway and last surveyed in 2005 and to present data on the current status of the sites. This data was also used to establish another site outside the Haven to the north at Nolton Haven.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:03:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17842\tNRW_DS111177\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.525Z\t622\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t© CNC/NRW 2009. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313737\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 1221. [A]H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 60 - Pembroke Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML[B]H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs[C]  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder[D]H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs\\GIS[E]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs\\Images\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) was therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.   Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) was selected for eight Habitats Directive Annex I habitat types and five Annex II species. The SAC is considered to be one of the best areas in the UK for marine reef habitats, a category within the Annex I list. High habitat and biological diversity is of great importance throughout the site, particularly the best documented reefs habitat in the Milford Haven ria-estuary.   This dataset includes the results of a survey of intertidal rockpools at Pen-y-Holt on the Castlemartin peninsula.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T09:28:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17849\tNRW_DS111178\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.709Z\t828\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria Reef in Cardigan Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t© CNC/NRW 2009. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313738\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No, 199, Media No. 548. [A] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 57 - Cardigan Bay Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\Data [C] CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder [D] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\GIS [E] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\Images\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) was therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.   Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary, tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats).    Cardigan SAC is designated for a number of features, including Bottlenosed dolphins, Sandbanks, Reefs and Sea Caves, and some intertidal features including areas of bedrock and boulder/cobble habitats and many areas are characterised by biogenic reef formed by the honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata.   This dataset includes the results of a survey of the biogenic reef formed by the honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata at two sites within Cardigan Bay SAC. The purpose of this data capture was to establish sites for long term monitoring and collect baseline/reference data using appropriate methods. This is a high quality survey carried out by an experienced team of marine biologists.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-06-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T13:56:02.365Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17832\tNRW_DS111179\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.970Z\t621\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools in Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313739\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 555 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 555) [A] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 57 - Cardigan Bay Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\Data [C] NRWs copy of Marine Recorder - MRCCW16300000004 [D] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\GIS [E] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\Images\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tThe purpose of this data capture was to establish sites for long-term monitoring and collect baseline/reference data using appropriate methods.   Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated primarily for Bottlenose dolphins, but is also designated for Sandbanks, Reefs and Sea Caves. These Reefs and Sea Caves are present within intertidal areas of Cardigan Bay SAC.   Intertidal reefs in Cardigan Bay SAC include areas of bedrock and boulder/cobble habitats and many areas are characterised by biogenic reef formed by the honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria. Many intertidal rockpool biotopes are considered to be important for their biodiversity (JNCC 1996).   Rockpools are also a notable feature of some intertidal reef areas in the SAC. This dataset includes the results of a survey of intertidal rockpools at two sites within Cardigan Bay SAC.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T13:57:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17834\tNRW_DS111180\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.976Z\t373\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools, Caves and Overhangs in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2007\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313830\tData is held within the marine archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 555 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 555) [A]  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\MarineMonitoring\\MarineMonitoring Reports\\No 58 - Carm Bay Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B]  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\Data [C]  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder - MRCCW16300000008 [D]  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\GIS [E]   Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\Images\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) was therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features were in favourable conservation status.    There were two purposes for this data capture:    - To provide an inventory and distribution map of selected biotopes present to use as a reference condition for the rocky shore habitat communities in the SAC.  - To describe gross changes in these features since the Phase 1 survey (1999-2001) to give some indication of their stability.    The rocky shore areas that lies within the SAC includes a number of rockpool, overhang and cave features that are considered to be nationally important biotopes and/or Rare or Scarce in Wales and others that are considered to be important for their biodiversity. These potentially species rich rocky habitats were therefore surveyed to provide an inventory of these sub features for future survey work.    The confidence in this data is high as surveys were carried out by a team of experienced marine biologists.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T13:58:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17831\tNRW_DS111181\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.824Z\t303\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sediment Transects in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2009. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313831\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 549 [A]H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\MarineMonitoring\\MarineMonitoring Reports\\No 58 - Carm Bay Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\Data [C] CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder  [D] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\GIS [E] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\Images\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) was therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.    Between 1999 and 2001, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) undertook a Phase 1 biotope mapping survey of the intertidal areas of the Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC).   This dataset includes the results of surveys of the biotopes present along eight transects around Carmarthen Bay. Thirteen transects had been surveyed with a very similar methodology in 2005.  This survey repeated one of those transects and established seven new transects. The purpose of this data capture was to provide sufficient information to identify the biotopes present and describe the composition of the communities; and to establish sites for long-term monitoring and collect baseline/reference data using appropriate methods.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:16:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17835\tNRW_DS111182\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.969Z\t451\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Pectenogammarus planicrurus in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007 - Onwards\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2009. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313832\tData is archived within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 554 and Project 410. Media No. 1430.  Marine Monitoring data for each year is also organised in legacy drives  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tPectenogammarus planicrurus is the only amphipod which is a permanent resident of shingle beaches and it was found on the steep gravel or pebble beach fronting the promenade at Pwllheli during Phase 1 surveys undertaken by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) during June 1996.  This dataset contains the results of a survey carried out in 2007 to investigate whether P. planicrurus was still present in the area.  Monitoring of this feature is ongoing and is repeated on a yearly basis.   The purpose of this data capture is to confirm the continued presence and monitor the population of this scarce amphipod species at Pwllheli, repeating work carried out in 2004.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:40:02.163Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17833\tNRW_DS111183\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.977Z\t776\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Muddy Gravel in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2009. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313833\tData is held within Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 557 [A] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 59 - PLAS Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\Data [C] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\GIS [D]  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder [E] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\Images  The report and data collected under NRW contract FC 73-02-342 is archived as Project No 199, Media 549 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Fynnon on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist\tThe purpose of this data capture was to carry out a repeat survey of sites previously surveyed to further test and develop monitoring methodology and collect monitoring data.    In fully marine conditions on the lower shore, sheltered muddy gravel habitats can be particularly species rich due to the complex nature of the substratum supporting a high diversity of infauna and epifauna. Analysis of survey records held on the Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) database suggest that fully saline sheltered muddy gravel communities are scarce in their British distribution, with available descriptions lacking detail and comprehensive data. This has been attributed to the habitats being difficult to survey and monitor, due to the large quantities of coarse material which require sampling and subsequent analysis. Similarly, historical data on the distribution of muddy gravel beds appears limited.   This data set includes results of sediment coring and sampling undertaken at one site in Porthmadog during intertidal monitoring of the muddy gravel in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:41:03.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17828\tNRW_DS111184\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.582Z\t401\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Dyfi Estuary in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t© CNC/NRW 2009. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313834\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media 549 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\ Media 549. [A] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 59 - PLAS Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\Data [C] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\GIS [D]  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (Survey No. MRCCW16300000009) [E] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\Images\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tThe purpose of this data capture was to establish sites for long-term monitoring and collect baseline and reference data using appropriate methods.    Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary. Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau has representative examples of bar-built estuaries in north-west Wales, and includes the Glaslyn/Dwyryd, Mawddach and Dyfi estuaries.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T17:06:37.527Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17829\tNRW_DS111186\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.581Z\t461\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveys of Harbours, Rias and Estuaries in Southern Britain - Loughor Estuary and the Burry Inlet\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313836\tPaper report (volume 1) is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000256). NRW only has a snapshot of this data\tData contacts: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Nature Conservancy Council commissioned the Field Studies Council to undertake studies of the marine inlets of southern Britain, including harbours, rias and estuaries, in order to gain information on the ecosystems present. The study took place between 1985 and 1988 and covered 24 inlets. This dataset contains the results of a survey of Loughor Estuary (incorporating the Burry Inlet). The purpose of this data capture was to describe areas in terms of their physical attributes; to review the results of previous studies; to review marine resource usage e.g. fisheries, diving activities etc; to catalogue available information and to collect information and photographs on the habitats and species present.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1989-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:42:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17830\tNRW_DS111187\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.581Z\t639\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Sediment Survey of Cosheston Pill with Reference to Shelduck Distribution\t© CNC/NRW 1991 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313837\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000658)\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW  External Custodian: JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tCosheston Pill provides an important sanctuary for a number of wildfowl and wader species including redshanks, curlew and shelduck. Previous studies had shown that in Cosheston Pill shelduck appeared to frequent certain areas of the Pill preferentially- this dataset contains the results of an intertidal sediment survey designed to investigate this preference further. The purpose of data capture was to assess whether or not shelduck feeding area preference in Cosheston Pill were related to variation in the distribution of their prey species and therefore related to spatial variations in the characteristics of the sediments.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1990-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T15:56:15.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17827\tNRW_DS111189\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.409Z\t458\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveys of Harbours, Rias and Estuaries in Southern Britain - Milford Haven and Daugleddau\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313839\t[A] Paper reports (volumes 1 and 2) are available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. [B] Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000246). Available on All Wales Snapshot.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Custodian: JNCC    Metadata Contact:Master reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Nature Conservancy Council commissioned the Field Studies Council to undertake studies of the marine inlets of southern Britain, including harbours, rias and estuaries, in order to gain information on the ecosystems present. The study took place between 1985 and 1988 and covered 24 inlets. This dataset contains the results of a survey of Milford Haven and the estuary of the Rivers Cleddau (Daugleddau estuary). The purpose of this dataset was to describe areas in terms of their physical attributes; to review the results of previous studies; to review marine resource usage e.g. fisheries, diving activites etc; to catalogue available information and, to collect information and photographs on the habitats and species present. The field survey specifically aimed to collect further information on habitats and species where little or no previous work had been carried out, in areas of known or likely nature conservation importance, or where rare or unusual species may be present.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1987-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:43:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17826\tNRW_DS111190\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.367Z\t432\tValid\tEditable\tSublittoral Survey of Sarn Badrig, Sarn-y-Bwch and Cynfelin Patches 1986\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313930\tPaper reports are available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000125). NRW only hold a snapshot of this data\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe results of a sublittoral survey of the mid Wales sarnau (reefs) carried out in 1986 to more fully describe the sublittoral communities, habitats and species present on Sarn Badrig and the two smaller reefs to the south.  The purpose of this dataset was to describe the sublittoral and sublittoral fringe communities on and around the mid-Wales sarnau (reefs), and to assess their scientific interest and nature conservation importance.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1986-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:45:41.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17870\tNRW_DS111195\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.792Z\t566\tValid\tEditable\tGallows Point Sublittoral Survey 1998\t© CNC/NRW 2006 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313935\tThe data is available on NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000698).  MNCR forms are held in NRW off-site storage.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe aim of this data collection was to collect baseline data on the habitats and species present in the area adjacent to the proposed marine development. Prior to this site visit there were no survey data from the site. This dataset contains the results of a site visit undertaken by CCW to record baseline data on the habitats and species present at Gallows Point in view of a proposed marina development in the area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1998-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:44:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17885\tNRW_DS111196\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.905Z\t606\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRemote Sensing Image Catalogue\t© CNC/NRW  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313936\tMost images are kept in locked storage units in CCW\u0027s Maes y Ffynnon and the Environmental Policy group\u0027s work area.  Backups are held on hard drives and stored in a safe. The metadata is stored with the images.\tHelena Skyes  Used to be Alan Brown, Remote Sensing Officer, Environmental Policy Group, NRW\tRemote sensing is any method of collecting information about objects such as lakes, trees, fields and habitats \u0027from a distance\u0027. This dataset contains a collection of around 300 archival images purchased by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to build up a resource of satellite remote sensing images of the Welsh countryside. The images have not all been collected for specific projects, however some tasking has taken place i.e. images requested from a particular satellite or from a particular date. Each image has metadata detailing image identification number, date and time, type of image, location etc.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:02:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17876\tNRW_DS111197\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.871Z\t887\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRemote Sensing Phase 1 Habitat Survey Update\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2014. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313937\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Remote sensed Phase 1\\Phase 1 remote sensed.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Senior Remote Sensing Manager, Sustainable Communities, NRW Metadata Custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUp-to-date habitat survey of Wales produced by analysis of remote sensing data. The dataset is comprised of contiguous habitat polygons classified into phase 1 categories. It is the product of a large scale remote sensing project aimed at updating the Phase 1 habitat survey carried out in Wales between 1979 and 1999. It was produced in order to have key dataset for Wales which can be used to inform the core work of former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and its partners, and map specialist habitats or interpretations of habitats.  The maps will be checked through field visits and feedback from CCW staff with local knowledge.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-11-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:11.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17884\tNRW_DS111205\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.890Z\t448\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveys of Harbours, Rias and Estuaries in Southern Britain - Taf, Tywi and Gwendraeth Estuaries.\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323035\tPaper report (volume 1) is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000258)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Nature Conservancy Council commissioned the Field Studies Council to undertake studies of the marine inlets of southern Britain, including harbours, rias and estuaries, in order to gain information on the ecosystems present. The study took place between 1985 and 1988 and covered 24 inlets. This dataset contains the results of a survey of the Taf, Tywi and Gwendraeth estuaries. The purpose of this data capture was to: - Describe areas in terms of their physical attributes;  - Review the results of previous studies;  - Review marine resource usage e.g. fisheries, diving activities etc; and, - Catalogue available information and to collect information and photographs on the habitats and species present.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1989-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T15:56:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17875\tNRW_DS111212\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.865Z\t409\tValid\tEditable\tSurvey of the Littoral Zone of Great Britain\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323132\t[A] Paper reports are available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. [B] Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000265). NRW only have a snapshot of this data\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External Contact: JNCC  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tBetween 1970 and 1980 NCC contracted a survey on the littoral zone of the coast of Great Britain. Former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds a series of reports prepared under this contract as well as data in Marine Recorder.   The purpose of this data capture was:  -  To record the distribution of habitats and the associated organisms in the littoral zone of Great Britain in order to verify existing information, fill gaps in knowledge, and ensure uniformity in the description.   -  To make a photographic record of the main types of littoral habitats and the associated plant and animals.    -  To develop suitable criteria for the assessment and selection of key areas in terms of their scientific and conservation value and assess the information collected by the criteria and so select key areas, and to zone the rest of the coast in relation to the key areas.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1980-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:46:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17882\tNRW_DS111220\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.899Z\t346\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCollation of Information on Species and Habitats in the Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) 1975-1991\t© CNC/NRW 1991 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323230\t[A] Paper report is available from NRW Reports Archive on the DMS. [B] Species Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (Survey number: MRCCW10000000033). Snap\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. This dataset is a collation of previously unpublished information concerning species and habitat records from Skomer MNR and review and assess the conservation importance of faunal species (emphasis on difficult groups of marine infauna e.g. sponges and sea squirts). Some of the information dates from 1975 however most has come from an expedition to Skomer in July 1987 and a series of diving workshops at Dale Fort Field Centre between 1988 and 1991. The data is chiefly from the seas around Skomer but records from nearby areas of the Pembrokeshire coast are also included.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1992-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T15:57:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17872\tNRW_DS111292\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.739Z\t364\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ) Sediment Interface Study 1994-1995\t© CNC/NRW 1996 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323932\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above.   Data available on NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000059)\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. A detailed survey of the shores of Skomer Marine Nature Reserve was carried out in 1982. This dataset comprises of the results of surveys at two sites within Skomer MNR during 1994 and 1995 designed to establish monitoring sites and collect baseline survey data on the macrofauna and megafauna of the sediment/water interface in shallow subtidal regions. The purpose of this data capture was to initiate monitoring of the sediment/water interface in shallow subtidal regions taking into account both macrofauna obtained from samples and visible megafauna.\tSubsea Survey-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1996-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:46:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17883\tNRW_DS111294\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.904Z\t381\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools Following the Sea Empress Oil Spill 1996-97\t© CNC/NRW 1997 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323934\t[A] Paper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. [B] Data available on NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000076)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tFollowing the Sea Empress grounding in Milford Haven on February the 15th 1996, it was decided that a programme of monitoring was required to assess the impact of the oil on the coasts of Pembrokeshire and Carmarthenshire. This dataset contains the results of rockpool monitoring carried out for a year following the spillage to assess community changed caused by the oil spill. The data collected was used to assess community changes caused by the oil spillage. It was thought rockpool monitoring was particular suitable for this purpose because rockpools are diverse in physical characteristics, occur at different height on shores, contain a range of biota, and are relatively easy to re-locate.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T15:58:31.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17877\tNRW_DS111295\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.876Z\t946\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternational Biological Programme Data\t© Centre of Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)   NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323935\tThe hardcopy raw data is to be kept in the monitoring team store room at NRW\u0027s Plas Penrhos campus. The excel spreadsheets are stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff.\tAlex Turner, Environmental Change Network Project Officer, Operational Support Group, CCW.\tThe International Biological Programme (IBP) was an international research programme set up in the 1960s which aimed to understand the dynamics of whole ecosystems e.g. the energy flow across an ecosystem and the productivity of biological resources. Many sites were set up across the globe as part of the IBP, one of which was in Snowdonia. This dataset contains raw data collected at a site in Snowdonia as part of the IBP. Data collected includes meteorological data, vegetation data flora and fauna data etc. The dataset also contains some earlier data on sheep numbers. Purpose of data capture: The IBP was set up to coordinate large-scale ecological and environmental studies. The main purpose of data capture was to gain a greater understanding of whole ecosystem dynamics and how ecosystem processes may be affected by environmental change. Methods of data capture: Extensive field research was carried out at the site throughout the duration of the IBP. Soil collection, vegetation sampling, meteorological studies and weighing of animals were some of the methods used. Geographic coverage: The main experimental site was above Llyn Llydaw on Snowdon. Temporal coverage: The IBP ran from approximately 1964 to 1974. The main work on Snowdon took place during the late 60s; however there is also data on sheep numbers from the 1950s. Confidence in data: High. Survey work was undertaken by expert scientists.\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\t1978-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:02:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17879\tNRW_DS111296\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.884Z\t677\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhotographic collection from Yr Wyddfa Environmental Change Network (ECN) site\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323936\tDigital images are kept on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff, whilst the slides are housed in filing cabinets in NRW\u0027s ECN team office.\tInternal Custodian: Alex Turner, Environmental Change Network Project Officer, Operational Support Group, CCW. Victoria Bowmaker, Environmental Change Network Data Officer, Operational Support Group, CCW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW / Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tA collection of slides, scans and digital photographs taken at the Environmental Change Network sites on Snowdon. The purpose of this data capture was to keep a photographic record of the site in order to study changes over time.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1995-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:50:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17881\tNRW_DS111297\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.886Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Surveys of the Carneddau (2001-2002)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323937\tHard copy reports are available in CCW libraries and TSG archive.\tBarbara Jones, Upland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW\tThis survey was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales in order to map, describe and assess the vegetation of the western part of the Carneddau. The study forms part of the much larger Eryri Site of Special Scientific Interest Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:13:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17871\tNRW_DS111298\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.709Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Surveys of Proposed Extensions to the Eryri SAC\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323938\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist.\tBarbara Jones, Upland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW\tThis data collation is the results from two studies researching a proposed extension to Eryri Special Area of Conservation (SAC) conducted in 2001 and 2002. The first survey was commissioned by Countryside Council for Wales in order to map and describe the vegetation of six upload areas adjacent to the existing Carneddau and Clydeiriau Site of Special Scientific Interest. The second survey built on the results from the 2001 studies and evaluated these against the requirements for SPA’s.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Averis\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:13:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17880\tNRW_DS111299\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.900Z\t863\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHedgerow Database for Angelsey North Wales\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323939\tNeeds secure location. Currently on a cd with Claire Burrows\tInternal Custodian: Claire Burrows, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tThe Hedgerow Database contains data from hedgerow surveys undertaken on Anglesey looking at the structure and composition of the hedgerows.  In 2002 a Hedgerow Survey Handbook was produced setting a standard procedure for local hedgerow surveys in the UK. It was proposed that a series of hedgerow surveys were to be undertaken in Wales using this standard procedure however as yet only the Anglesey survey has taken place due to funding constraints and therefore the database only currently contains data from this one survey. The purpose of this data capture was to build up a detailed body of information on the current status of hedgerows across the UK thus enabling appropriate action to be taken to safeguard the most important hedgerows, and to monitor any changes in the quality and quantity of the hedgerow network over time.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17874\tNRW_DS111300\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.792Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTir Gofal Species Package Maps\tThere are no use restrictions on NRW owned data.   Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate parts of the dataset owned by third parties. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131333030\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Users/clare/TG targeting/final versions\tInternal Custodian: Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset contains maps of key areas for species that are targeted in Tir Gofal (the Welsh Government\u0027s agri-environmental programme) and information sheets describing the prescriptions for each species.  The maps are designed to be used by Tir Gofal project officers in order to inform them of the distribution of species targeted by Tir Gofal. Data collation and map production exercise carried out during 2007.\tButterfly Conservation-Plantlife-Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17868\tNRW_DS111301\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.669Z\t398\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGrazing Marsh Species Data\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131333031\t? This data may have been lost\tInternal Custodian: Claire Burrows, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset is a collation of flora and fauna species records from grazing marshes in Wales. The purpose of this dataset was to collate information on the range of species found in Welsh grazing marshes in order to provide an inventory of the species and their distribution.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:01:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17873\tNRW_DS111311\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.791Z\t392\tValid\tEditable\tEstablishment of Permanent Quadrats at Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ )\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131333131\tPaper report is available from CCW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on CCWs copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000068)\tInternal Custodian and Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  A detailed survey of the shores of Skomer Marine Nature Reserve was carried out in 1982.   This dataset contains the results of an exercise carried out in 1992 to repeat some of the littoral transects surveyed in 1982 and establish permanent quadrats at six sites.   The purpose of this data capture was to:   - to establish six permanent littoral monitoring sites at locations surveyed in the 1982 littoral survey.   - survey the sites and where appropriate compare with the results of the 1982 littoral survey.   - photograph the general appearance of the site for relocation purposes and photograph the quadrats at specified intervals along the transects at each site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1993-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T15:08:05.976Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17878\tNRW_DS111397\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.815Z\t333\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) Subtidal Monitoring Project - Animal Communities on Stones\t© CNC/NRW 1991 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131333937\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above.   Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000056)\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe results of subtidal monitoring undertaken on animal communities found on stones and pebbles at a site within Skomer Marine Nature Reserve. The purpose of this data capture was to observe and record seasonal settlement and colonisation of pebble habitat by marine fauna and record the changes in abundance and where possible the condition of the animals collected over a year.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1988-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:49:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17869\tNRW_DS111406\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.670Z\t436\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Island Eunicella verrucosa Survey 1985\t© CNC/NRW 1991 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343036\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000029)\tContact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset is a collation of the results of a survey aimed at studying the population of the broad sea fan Eunicella verrucosa at Skomer Marine Reserve. The dataset also contains data on a number of other species of interest and previously unsurveyed areas of the reserve. The purpose of this data capture was: -  Provide information on the distribution and abundance of E. verrucosa around Skomer and determine habitat preferences.  -  Investigate the size of individual E. verrucosa and the population age structure. -  Study the epibionts of E. verrucosa.  -  Study orientation and growth form of E. verrucosa colonies in a variety of environmental conditions. The survey also aimed to tag and photograph E. verrucosa for future monitoring, record the occurrence and abundance of various species of interest and survey some additional areas around Skomer Marine Reserve for which there was little or no information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1986-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:00:08.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17866\tNRW_DS111435\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.230Z\t392\tValid\tEditable\tResurvey of Permanent Quadrats at Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) Formally a Marine Nature Reserve ( MNR ) Following the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t© CNC/NRW 1996. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343335\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above.Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000092)\tMetadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. The results of a survey carried out to re-examine permanent quadrats and transects established in Skomer Marine Nature Reserve in 1992 (see separate metadata \"Establishment of permanent quadrats at Skomer Marine Nature Reserve\") following the Sea Empress oil spill in February 1996.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1996-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:46:40.492Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17865\tNRW_DS111468\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.917Z\t432\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEffects of the Sea Empress oil spill on seaweed animals of the West Angle tide pools (1996-1997).\t© CNC/NRW 1997 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343638\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above BarCode 14750 Maes y Ffynnon, Bangor. Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000085)\tInternal Custodian and metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe results of a survey investigating the effects of oil pollution on the species richness of mobile fauna living in association with the algal turf in the West Angle Tide Pools. The study compares data collected from the polluted pools at West Angle with data collected from an unpolluted tide pool system in South Devon. The purpose of this data capture was to discover the effects of the Sea Empress oil spill on the fauna of the Coralline alga turf fringing the tide pool system at West Angle Bay, Pembrokeshire.  Samples were collected over a 14 month period beginning in April 1996. Samples were taken in April, June, July and October 1996 and in March and June 1997. Samples were taken from the Devon site for April, June and July 1996.\tSea Empress Environmental Evaluation Committee (SEEEC)\t1997-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:30.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17867\tNRW_DS111487\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.158Z\t631\tValid\tEditable\tVolunteer Diver Seagrass Zostera marina Surveys Porth Dinllaen (2009)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2009. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343837\tData is archived as project 229, media no. 598.  A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG shared drive.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSeagrass is the only marine flowering plant found subtidally in the UK and seagrass beds are considered of National importance.  In 2009 Zostera marina was the focus of former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), volunteer dive surveys at Porth Dinllaen in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) to estimate the extent of the bed. The survey is a continuation of a 2008 pilot, and is due to be re-surveyed before 2012 to start to build up a picture of variation in the extent of the seagrass bed at the site. The main objectives of this project were to: 1. Test methods to measure extent of large seagrass beds using volunteers 2. Estimate density and measure depth of live seagrass in different parts of the bed 3. Make note of any unusual features of the seagrass bed 4. Compare with results and success of 2008 surveys\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:50:53.286Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17864\tNRW_DS111488\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.799Z\t421\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Regional Data: West Wales (West Wales Biodiversity Information Centre - WWBIC)\t© CNC/NRW  - NRW Regional Data: all taxa (sensitive species only), West Wales The Intellectual Property Rights (including copyright and database rights) to the collation and all records created by NRW staff or contractors are owned by NRW. Where individual record were created by a third party, the copyright to that record rests with the original recorder/surveyor.  You may re-use non-sensitive data free of charge in any format or medium, provided that you do so accurately, acknowledging both the NBN Gateway as the source and NRW/third party copyright. It must not be used it in a misleading context. To avoid using old information, we recommend that you always obtain the latest version from the NBN Gateway.    - NRW Regional Data: all taxa (excluding sensitive species), West Wales The Intellectual Property Rights (including copyright and database rights) to the collation and all records created by NRW staff or contractors are owned by NRW. Where individual record were created by a third party, the copyright to that record rests with the original recorder/surveyor.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343838\tWest Wales Biodiversity Information Centre  LRC hold the physical data set on their server. If you want extra information about the LERCs contact: Enterprise Data Management Specialist Advisor - Barny Letheren.   X:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to WWBIC.lyr\tWWBIC are the custodians on behalf of NRW Ruth Harding is the main lead with WWBIC  This data will be incoprorated in the GIS layers that NRW also received\tThis dataset is made up of a collation of data (sensitive species only) derived from Natural Resources Wales (formerly Countryside Council for Wales) regional offices in West Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to mobilise a valuable data resource in order to provide baseline data to inform conservation work, research, site management and decision-making within NRW and externally. Records are for NRW\u0027s West region which includes Carmarthenshire, Pembrokeshire and Ceredigion. Many records are for designated sites and a proportion are from the wider countryside. The majority of records have six figure grid references, some are site centroids.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:38.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17863\tNRW_DS111489\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.802Z\t517\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Regional Data: North Wales (North Wales Environmental Information Services - Cofnod)\t© CNC/NRW - NRW Regional Data: North Wales (Sensitive Records) Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence or equivalent. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale. At a spatial scale at which the records are considered non-sensitive then there are no use restrictions on this data, including for commercial and research purposes. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.    - NRW Regional Data: North Wales (Non-Sensitive Records) There are no use restrictions on this data, including for commercial and research purposes. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343839\tCofnod LRC hold the physical collation on their server. If you want extra information about the LERCs contact: Enterprise Data Management Specialist Advisor - Barny Letheren.   X:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to COFNOD point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to COFNOD region.lyr\tCofnod is the cutsodian Huw Jones is the main lead with Cofnod This data will be incoprorated in the GIS layers that NRW also received\tThis dataset is a collation of records derived from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) (formerly Countryside Council for Wales) North Region paper files and entered by staff of Natural Resources Wales onto the Cofnod (North Wales Environmental Information Service) Online Recording System. Records are primarily for the NRW\u0027s North Region which includes the following Local Authority areas: Anglesey, Gwynedd, Conwy, Denbighshire, Flintshire and Wrexham. However there are some records for other areas. Many records are for designated sites (including Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), Special Area of Conservation (SACs), National Nature Reserves (NNRs) etc), however, there are also large numbers of records from the wider countryside. The majority of the records have grid references of at least 100m accuracy (6 figure), however some of these may be derived from site centroids rather than refer to the actual location of the species. The majority of the records were originally created by NRW staff or qualified contractors. They have been entered onto computer by trained Cofnod staff or volunteers using Cofnod\u0027s custom built Data Entry Systems which use standard lists where possible to ensure consistent terminology. Randomised Quality Assurance checks have been carried out on much of the data (apart from that entered directly by NRW staff). All records have been screened to ensure that they have valid dates, species names and grid references. The majority of these records are subject to systematic verification by relevant local experts, and are labelled accordingly (within an additional Attribute field). Individual records have a default verification level of \u0027Assigned\u0027 or \u0027Unassessed\u0027 until they have been looked at by a Verifier, after which they may be re-labelled as \u0027Unconfirmed\u0027 or \u0027Considered Correct by Expert\u0027. Some records known to be from a trusted source are labelled as \u0027Considered Correct by Cofnod\u0027. Records identified by Verifiers as \u0027Known/Probably Incorrect\u0027 will be removed from the NBN Gateway. Further details are given within relevant Cofnod policies. Data should be checked against original sources before use in contentious circumstances.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17862\tNRW_DS111508\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.793Z\t712\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAccess Maps - Promoted Local Routes\t© CNC/NRW Recipients may re-use, reproduce and disseminate data accessed via the interactive map free of charge, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context.  The GIS layer may not be released for re-use, reproduction or dissemination as it is would result in the use of out of date data. The data held on this map layer is provided by our annual agreement partners and as such is an accumulation of different partner records.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131353038\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Access\\Access_Land\\WNAR\tJill Meyer, Access Information Development Officer, Access Recreation and Communication Group, NRW\tThis dataset is one of a number of MapInfo tables that are used to deliver the Welsh National Access Register (WNAR) project.  The collation and presentation of data on a number of routes and trails throughout Wales in an easy-to-view format enables people to find out more about routes of interest and their accessibility.  The dataset was first created in 2005. It is maintained and added to regularly during the year due to changing partnership information as well as in response to annual agreement partnership checks.   This project helps to deliver the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) \u0027PE04 Enjoying the Outdoors\u0027  programme output \u0027More people from all sectors of society will regularly be using and benefiting from widespread and equitable access to countryside, coast and urban green-space\u0027.  The overall aim of the project was to get more people (1% annual increase) from all sectors of society, regularly participating in open-air recreation and exercise. The objectives from this program output are:  1. Increase availability of high quality recreation resource, including public and other urban green-space, without compromising environmental and cultural interests  2. Provide readily available, targeted information about recreation opportunities and encourage greater awareness of rights and responsibility that will lead to positive behaviour changes  3. Provide a means of finding accommodation alongside the recreational opportunities in discussion with our partners.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17860\tNRW_DS111509\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.692Z\t656\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAccess Maps - Sites and Reserves\t© CNC/NRW and annual agreement partners  Recipients may re-use, reproduce and disseminate data accessed via the interactive map free of charge, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and CCW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context.  Printing of maps from the Countryside Access interactive map: You may print one extract from this website for your personal use if you hold a legal interest in CROW access land to enable you to exercise any of your powers under CROW. The user shall not alter or remove any copyright notice or proprietary legend contained in or on the information. This is to meet copyright requirements of the Ordnance Survey data and ensures\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131353039\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Access\\Access_Land\\WNAR\tInternal Custodian:  Jill Meyer, Access Information Development Officer, Access Recreation and Communication Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset is one of a number of GIS tables that are used to deliver the Welsh National Access Register (WNAR) project. The collation and presentation of data on a number of sites throughout Wales in an easy-to-view format enables people to find out more about places of interest and their accessibility. The dataset was first created in 2005. It is maintained and added to regularly during the year due to changing partnership information as well as in response to annual agreement partnership checks.  This ensures we display the information that our partners want to promote as well as keeping broken URL links to a minimum.  This project helps to deliver the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) \u0027PE04 Enjoying the Outdoors\u0027  programme output \u0027More people from all sectors of society will regularly be using and benefiting from widespread and equitable access to countryside, coast and urban green-space\u0027.  The overall aim of the project was to get more people (1% annual increase) from all sectors of society, regularly participating in open-air recreation and exercise. The objectives from this program output are:  1. Increase availability of high quality recreation resource, including public and other urban green-space, without compromising environmental and cultural interests  2. Provide readily available, targeted information about recreation opportunities and encourage greater awareness of rights and responsibility that will lead to positive behaviour changes  3. Provide a means of finding accommodation alongside the recreational opportunities in discussion with our partners.\tVarious\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:02:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17861\tNRW_DS111511\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.681Z\t772\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAccess Maps - Permissive Access in Wales\t© CNC/NRW  Recipients may re-use, reproduce and disseminate data accessed via the interactive map free of charge, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context.  The GIS layer may not be released for re-use, reproduction or dissemination as it is would result in the use of out of date data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131353131\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Access\\Access_Land\\Permissive Access\tInternal Custodian:  Jill Meyer, Access Information Development Officer, Access Recreation and Communication Group, NRW  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains permissive access land in Wales. This includes land under Tir Gofal agreement, which give access to specified parts of the farm, National Trust land and National Park access agreements and means that the landowner or tenant of the land, has agreed to allow public access for open-air recreation, normally on foot. The purpose of this data capture was to enable people to be confident of their access rights when visiting the Welsh countryside.\t\t2014-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17858\tNRW_DS111562\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.661Z\t806\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFluvial Audit of the Afon Tywi\tThird parties seeking to re-use this data require a OS Derived Data Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131353632\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\hydrology\\Hydrology_Reports\\fluvial_morphology\\Tywi Fluvial AuditData    Archived under Project 257 Media 951.  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media901to1000\\Media951\tInternal Custodians: Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Senior Freshwater Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group Rhian Thomas, Freshwater Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group  Metadata Contact: Data Services for Archived Data or GIS and Spatial Data Team for GIS data locations\tThe Afon Tywi in Carmarthenshire is a large actively eroding river, designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for its migratory fish, and as a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) for various features including river habitat, river shingle invertebrates and little ringed plover. The river is unusually active in the middle reaches, sometimes moving as much as 50 m in a single year, and the various species for which the river is designated depend on the river remaining active. In order to inform current and future river management, a fluvial audit of the past and present geomorophology of the Afon Tywi was carried out. This consists of a detailed technical report including identification of the most active river sections based on historic maps, a timeline of the river indicating key events such as major floods, and maps showing the geomorphology and sediment character of the river. Advice is also provided on the future sustainable management of the Tywi as an active shingle river. This dataset consists of the results from the Fluvial Audit of the Afon Tywi which describes and spatially maps the past and present fluvial geomorphology of the Tywi catchment. The aim of this data capture was to:  - Develop an understanding of the dynamic geomorphology of the Afon Tywi. Specific aims include: - Describe and map the fluvial geomorphology of the Tywi catchment past and present using a GIS system. - Interpret the data gathered in the context of past and present human activity, including a risk assessment of consented activities. - Provide advice on the future management of the Tywi SAC for use as a management and casework decision-making tool with regards to future bank reinforcement work, flood defence activities, aggregate extractions etc.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1953-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-09-03T10:00:58.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17859\tNRW_DS111625\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.693Z\t480\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Regional Data: Mid-Wales ( Biodiversity Information Service for Powys and Brecon Beacons - BIS )\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131363235\tX:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to BIS point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to BIS region.lyr   BIS LERC hold and manage the top copy of some NRW owned species data. Some of this data has its own metadata field (Contains Field) and other data doesn\u0027t. All NRW owned data which is managed by BIS is available to download on the NBN Gateway.   116719 - CCW Montgomeryshire General Bat Files WA0001500000001O\tInternal Custodian: Monica Jones, Team Leader, Data Services External Custodian: BIS  Additional information is available from BIS www.b-i-s.org  Janet Imlach janet@bis.org.uk Unit 4, 6 The Bulwark, Brecon, Powys www.bis.org.uk   Andrew Lucas is the NRW contact with BIS\tAll records collated from species records mobilised from former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) regional files, held at Newtown, Abergavenny and Llandrindod Wells offices. This dataset was created from species records extracted, computerised and collated from paper records held at former CCW\u0027s Regional office files covering the Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park area. The work was carried out under contract by ten Biodiversity Information Service for Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park (BIS) staff members within the NRW offices between 2003 and 2009, as part of CCW Data Mobilisation Project. However, coverage was not complete, as follows:- Newtown: completed 10Km square files and partial completed Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) files, Llandrindod Wells: General files complete. All SSSI files complete Abergavenny: 10km square files complete. SSSI complete. Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and National Nature Reserve (NNR) files not completed Records were entered into Recorder 2002 or Recorder 6 databases.  The majority of records are from Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park or within a 5km buffer. Many records are for designated sites (including SSSIs, SACs, National Nature Reserves etc), however, there are also large numbers of records from the wider countryside.  The grid references entered will be at resolution of original record and could be 2 or 4 figures, however, the majority of the records have 6 figure grid-references. Some grid references may be from site centroids rather than actual location of the species in question or may have been estimated from map. The purpose of this data capture was to create an electronic archive of species data held on CCW regional files and mobilise this data through BIS.  Confidence in data: The source of the original data has not been checked however much of the data originates from CCW staff or other trusted sources. Most of this data has not gone through external verification process but BIS will continue to work with VC recorders to verify records. Recorder has its own validation checks and will not accept data without who, what, where, when present. Some of the grid references entered could be \u0027estimated from map\u0027 if they were not provided. The grid references entered will be at resolution of original record and could be 2 or 4 figures. To avoid duplication each record was checked to see if already in BIS database using MapInfo GIS layer. At this time the operator could check that the grid reference matches location name and is within designated site boundary if appropriate. Operators checked their own entries for accuracy, as resources did not allow for a second operator check. CCW staffs were on hand to help with handwriting or taxonomic issues. Most of the operators on this project originally trained in BIS entering data, and had biological or ecological backgrounds. BIS has recently worked through the surveys to remove outlier records and resolve grid reference issues where possible and will continue to work to remove any duplication.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:46.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17857\tNRW_DS111647\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.488Z\t359\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAccess Maps - Promoted Regional Routes\t© CNC/NRW Recipients may re-use, reproduce and disseminate data accessed via the interactive map free of charge, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context.  The GIS layer may not be released for re-use, reproduction or dissemination as it is a dynamic dataset and could result in the use of out of date data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131363437\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Access\\Access_Land\\WNAR\tInternal Custodian: Jill Meyer, Access Information Development Officer, Access Recreation and Communication Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis data set is one of a number of MapInfo tables that are used to deliver the Welsh National Access Register (WNAR) project.  The collation and presentation of data on a number of routes and trails throughout Wales in an easy-to-view format enables people to find out more about places of interest and their accessibility. The dataset was first created in 2005. It is maintained and added to regularly during the year due to changing partnership information as well as in response to annual agreement partnership checks. This project helps to deliver the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) \u0027PE04 Enjoying the Outdoors\u0027  programme output \u0027More people from all sectors of society will regularly be using and benefiting from widespread and equitable access to countryside, coast and urban green-space\u0027.  The overall aim of the project was to get more people (1% annual increase) from all sectors of society, regularly participating in open-air recreation and exercise. 1. Increase availability of high quality recreation resource, including public and other urban green-space, without compromising environmental and cultural interests  2. Provide readily available, targeted information about recreation opportunities and encourage greater awareness of rights and responsibility that will lead to positive behaviour changes  3. Provide a means of finding accommodation alongside the recreational opportunities in discussion with our partners.\tVarious-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:01:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17856\tNRW_DS111769\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.413Z\t439\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSite Actions held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131373639\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.  Can be accessed via extranet by third parties who are responsible for actions. This is a legacy CCW Database and therefore Access maybe limited to a certain number of people.\tChristine Edwards, Strategic Project Manager, Operational Support Group, NRW Access to the database is provided under licence only through Gill Walker, Data Manager Sites       All requests for this data should be referred to Gill Walker.   Metadata will not be set to export to data.gov due to number of restriction son the data and access to the database\tThe Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database contains the actions that need to be carried out on sites to achieve appropriate conservation management The purpose of this data capture was to document and share information on who needs to do what, where, in order to get special sites into favourable condition. There are numerous management units involved (7,000 plus), there are many organisations whose work needs to be coordinated, and there are many different types of issues and actions needed. In practice a database is the most efficient way of collection and organising this information. The Actions Database project was initiated in May 2008. Data entry began in November 2008 and is ongoing.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:16:51.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17912\tNRW_DS111788\t2024-05-28T12:34:44.071Z\t688\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPredicting the displacement of common scoter Melanitta nigra from benthic feeding areas due to offshore windfarms\t© Collaborative Offshore Windfarm Research Into the Environment (COWRIE) Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131373838\tThis dataset is maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW HQ as Project 188 media numbers 530 \u0026amp; 531.\tInternal Custodian: Matt Murphy, Ornithologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis study used field observations and surveys combined with an individual-based modelling approach to predict the change in over-wintering mortality rates of common scoter that would result from the displacement of birds from potential feeding habitat through the avoidance of windfarms in Liverpool Bay. The purposes of this data capture were to: -  Develop a model to assist in predicting the effect of offshore windfarms on common scoter due to habitat loss and change. -  Link the non-breeding distribution of common scoter with environmental variables at selected sites -  Identify the characteristics of preferred feeding areas for common scoter within these sites.\tCollaborative Offshore Windfarm Research Into the Environment (COWRIE)\t2006-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:02.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17907\tNRW_DS111793\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.663Z\t501\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommon Scoter (Melanitta nigra) Infaunal Data Collected During Academic Research Projects\t© CCGC/CCW and various third party recorders 2004. CCW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of CCW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131373933\tData is stored as Project No.45 media number 221, and Project 2 media number 3.  The MSc project is archived as project 4 media number 227.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader Metadata Contact: Data Services Team\tCarmarthen Bay, an extensive, shallow embayment off the south coast of Wales, UK, encompasses a wide range of rich and diverse coastal and marine habitats. Carmarthen Bay is an internationally important wintering ground for the Common Scoter duck (Melanitta nigra) and is widely regarded as the most important such site in the UK causing the site to be proposed as a Special Protection Area (pSPA) under the EC Birds Directive. The Common Scoter is strongly migratory and often travels considerable distances over land making brief stop-overs on inland waters. Its diet consists predominantly of molluscs, especially during the winter, although it occasionally takes other aquatic invertebrates such as crustaceans (e.g. barnacles and shrimps), worms, echinoderms, isopods, amphipods and insects (e.g. midges and caddis flies) as well as small fish and fish eggs. The large concentrations of this species that occur during the moulting period and in winter are highly vulnerable to oil spills, human disturbance and the degradation of food resources as a result of oil exploration. The need for a comprehensive baseline survey of the macro fauna communities of Carmarthen Bay became a priority for government agencies such as the Countryside Council for Wales in the wake of the Sea Empress oil spill February 1996. The long-term presence of certain infaunal species and the stability of the areas populations of the species may be a factor in the importance of the Carmarthen Bay is an internationally important overwintering site for the common scoter.  This dataset consists of an extensive series of surveys of the macrofauna around Carmarthen Bay and was collated to identify environmental and / or biotic conditions relating to areas where scoter number were high or low. The majority of this dataset is data generated as part of a Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) funded PhD for the University of Wales Swansea known as \\Carmarthen Bay infauna scoter project\\\". It also includes data related to a University of Wales Swansea MSc Environmental biology dissertation entitled \u0027Common Scoter Melanitta nigra in their wintering habitats; an investigation into the factors influencing their finer scale distribution\u0027. Data was collected by students with CCW involvement or supervision.\"\tUniversity of Wales Swansea. Marine and Environmental Research Group-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:01:23.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17908\tNRW_DS111800\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.619Z\t662\tValid\tEditable\tCommon Scoter Survey (Melanitta nigra) in Liverpool Bay (2000-2001)\t© CNC/NRW 2001. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383030\tData is held as Project 44, media number 91 Further work in Liverpool Bay has included the Development of a methodology for the assessment of cumulative effects of marine activities using Liverpool Bay as a case study, held as Media No.80.\tData Custodian: Marine Ornathologist, NRW or Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services\tThe Common Scoter is strongly migratory and often travels considerable distances over land making brief stop-overs on inland waters. It is currently listed under the Birds Directive (Annex 2) Convention on Migratory Species (Bonn Convention Appendix 2) and is featured on the UK red-listed bird of conservation concern (for the breeding population) and a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Species for conservation and is a Schedule 1 species under the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981.    The majority of wintering common scoter recorded around the Welsh coast around Carmarthen Bay, North Cardigan Bay and the North Wales coast, which in combination are thought to hold 30-36,000 birds. A further 10 sites regularly support 1000 or more birds.   These are no known major concentrations away from these 13 sites, although this may be a consequence of the lack of appropriate survey. Large numbers of birds are recorded at several sites in April and May and there are significant post-breeding gatherings at three sites, including Carmarthen Bay, from August onwards. Casual observations suggest that these sites are not moulting grounds for the majority of birds. However there maybe as yet undetected moulting sites further offshore, as is the case in Denmark.    The purpose of this data capture was to record the numbers and distribution of common scoter in the Liverpool Bay; to determine the accuracy of the two survey methods (aerial and land-based) and the correlation between the two; and to provide recommendations on future monitoring of common scoter in Liverpool bay.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:51:43.435Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17911\tNRW_DS111813\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.875Z\t1341\tValid\tEditable\tAtlas of the Marine Mammals of Wales\t© CNC/NRW 2012 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383133\t[A]  This dataset is archived as Project 200, Media 565 (edition 1) and Media 1317 (edition 2) on backed-up server based storage at NRW, Maes y Fynnon. [B]  The report is available from NRW libraries (see related titles field above) and within the NRW Reports Archive located on the DMS.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tWelsh water support nationally and internationally important populations of marine mammals (whales, dolphins and seals) which use the area at various stages of their life histories such as feeding and breeding. Since the early 1990s the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) has been a major supporter of studies that have recorded the distribution and abundance of marine mammals in Wales.   These studies, often undertaken using dedicated networks of volunteers, have helped to inform conservation objectives for Special Areas of Conservation (SAC), monitoring targets and the assessment of proposed developments and impacts.  This dataset brings together the best available effort-related survey data on marine mammals in Welsh waters and adjacent parts of the Irish Sea, to provide the highest resolution analysis carried out to date of the spatial and temporal variation in their distribution. The Atlas contains distribution maps, including variations both seasonally and over the long terms of the five most common species (harbour porpoise, bottlenose dolphin, short-beaked common dolphin, Risso\u0027s dolphin and minke whale).  The purpose of this data capture is to describe the temporal distribution, spatial distribution and relative abundance of all marine mammal species sighted, detected or stranded in Welsh waters.   This dataset is concentrated on species regularly sighted and is designed to help meet obligations under the EU Habitats Directive. In addition is help former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now Natural Resources Wales (NRW)  advise on marine activities including renewable energy exploitation, fisheries, waterborne recreation and oil and gas exploitation. The study area consists primarily of Welsh territorial seas out to the median line with Ireland and the Isle of Man, and English territorial seas.  It was assumed that data contributed were reliable with respect to species identification and had been checked for errors in date, time and position fields; however factors affecting sight ability, survey characteristics and ecological factors may have affected sighting rates. Survey effort remains inadequate in all but a few small areas.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-18T14:33:47.000Z\tnobody nobody\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17896\tNRW_DS111815\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.448Z\t374\tUnassessed\tEditable\tConnectivity of Marine Habitats - A Review of Vulnerable Species (1960 - 2008)\t© CNC/NRW 2010 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383135\tArchived as project number 259, media number 955 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 955)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services\tThe ‘connectivity’ of marine benthic organisms is important as it has potential implications for designation and management of MPAs and is also likely to feed into work on the management of the wider environment through Marine Planning, which will be developed in Welsh waters in the near future. Connectivity is defined here as the exchange of sufficient organisms via dispersal and movement to sustain populations.    Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) contracted Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd. (Marine EcoSol) to undertake the first stages of analysis, primarily to review species that may be vulnerable to low connectivity, or fragmentation, in Welsh waters. The vulnerability to connectivity project (presented in the current report) aimed to identify marine invertebrates, macroalgae, plants and small territorial fish which are theoretically vulnerable to low connectivity and fragmentation, and flag those that might need to be carefully considered when managing the marine environment around Wales. This dataset contains the results of  the \u0027vulnerability to connectivity project\u0027,  a five staged review of existing information which highlighted species that may be vulnerable to poor connectivity, or fragmentation in Welsh Waters based on information easily available in 2009. Connectivity is now generally recognised as an important criterion in the design of networks of Marine Protected Areas. Historical data was reviewed up to November 2008, with the earliest data point recorded in 1960.\tJoint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:52:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17898\tNRW_DS111816\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.567Z\t654\tValid\tEditable\tMapping Marine Benthic Biodiversity in Wales\t© CNC/NRW 2010. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383136\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services, NRW\tThe UK is committed through international agreements and European obligations to the establishment of an ecologically coherent network of Marine Protected Areas (MPAs) to conserve marine ecosystems and biodiversity. The identification of areas of high biodiversity may be helpful for planning both Marine Protected Areas and for Marine Spatial Planning and can contribute to an ecosystem-based approach to the management of our seas. Furthermore, identifying which areas are most important for biodiversity not only yields benefits for the maintenance of ecosystem structure and functioning but can also enable cost effective prioritisation of areas for marine protection.   Each data layer produced in relation to this project has its own metadata entry. The main objective of the project is to provide maps indicating different levels of biodiversity in Welsh waters (including the intertidal). The data covers the Welsh coastline from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:54:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17904\tNRW_DS111834\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.587Z\t389\tValid\tEditable\tSt Bride\u0027s Bay Sublittoral Sediment Benthic Survey (2000)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383334\tData is held as Project No. 288, Media No. 1191.  Marine Recorder survey MRCCW30000000002.\tInternal Custodian: West Region Senior Marine Conservation Officer, NRW All other data held by Anne Bunker, West Region.  Metadata custodian: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to gain an understanding of the benthic sediment ecology of St Brides Bay in order to contribute to the development of conservation objectives for the Pembrokeshire Marine candidate Special Area of Conservation (SAC). During the summer of 2000 Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) investigated St Bride\u0027s Bay using a variety of survey techniques. This dataset contains the results of the analyses of biological data from grab and anchor dredge samples, and examines the community types present by comparison with those listed in the Marine Biotope Classification of Britain and Ireland.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2000-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T09:09:11.318Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17915\tNRW_DS111860\t2024-05-28T12:34:44.002Z\t941\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFluvial Audit of the River Wye and Tributaries\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383630\tArchived as Project 149, Media 481.   Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media401to500\\Media481  Also archived in CCW legacy GIS drive (M drive) Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\hydrology\\Hydrology_Reports\\fluvial_morphology\tSue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, Knowledge Strategy and Planning, NRW (Formerly Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, CCW)\tThis dataset consists of the results of a Fluvial Geomorphological Audit of the River Wye and it\u0027s Tributaries. The river Wye is one of the largest rivers in England and Wales, and supports a range of rare and threatened habitats and species. The river includes several Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). It is also designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC).   The purpose of this data capture was to characterise the geomorphology of the watercourses surveyed and to identify key areas for monitoring, management and restoration, particularly in relation to the desired physical habitats for the SAC species.   The area covered included 305 km of the River Wye and selected tributaries between Pant Mawr and Hay-on-Wye. Tributaries included Aran, Bachawy, Bachell Brook, Camddwr, Cletwr Brook, Clywedog Brook, Colwyn Brook, Duhonw, Dulas, Dulas Brook, Dulas Llynfi, Edw, Gwenlas Brook, Howey Brook, Ithon, Llaethydy Brook, Llymwynt Brook, Migrams Brook, Mithil Brook, Nant Bwch, Nant yr Offeiriad, rhiwiau Brook, Sgithwen Brook, and Triffrwd.\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:30:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17903\tNRW_DS111878\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.715Z\t346\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommunity Composition and Spatial Variation of Epifauna on the Horse Mussel Modiolus Reef (1999)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383738\t[A]  Marine Recorder survey JNCCMNCR40000775.  [B]  MNCR forms are held in CCW off-site storage.(Box CCW/MNCR/3) [C]  Report is available from CCW library (see related titles above) this survey relates to section 5.4 of the report.\tNRW Custodian Mike Camplin  Originator: Marine Monitoring Team CCW Bill Sanderson, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, CCW Rohan Holt, Marine Monitoring Biologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, CCW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to examine spatial variation in epifaunal community composition in order to inform the development of a sensitive and cost-effective future monitoring strategy.  Modiolus modiolus reef communities are comparatively rich in species and because they are also scarce in the UK it was considered important to monitor elements of community composition as an attribute that indicates the condition of these features. This dataset contains the results of in-situ sampling carried out by divers to try to determine the spatial variation in epifaunal community composition on the horse mussel reef off the coast of North Pen Llyn.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:54.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17906\tNRW_DS111924\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.627Z\t631\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSublittoral Survey of North Pen Llyn 1998\t© CNC/NRW 1999 Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131393234\tMarine Recorder survey JNCCMNCR40000771. Report is available from NRW library - Maes y Fynnon. Original MNCR forms are maintained at Maes y Fynnon off-site storage.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe aim of this survey was to gather detailed information on reef habitats and their associated communities in the northern part of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau cSAC and to provide a photographic and video record of the habitats and communities surveyed.  The geographic coverage included The North Pen Llyn coast, and concentrated on the section between Penrhyn Mawr and Porth Dinllaen. Surveys took place over a period between the 18th and the 22nd of July 1998.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:37:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17909\tNRW_DS111945\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.715Z\t396\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBenthos of Cardigan Bay Candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) 1998\t© CNC/NRW 1998. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131393435\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000774)\tThe Custodians are in MFSG, Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW Contact Master Reference Data Team for more information.\tThis survey was intended as a follow up excercise to the RoxAnn ground truthing video drops carried out in 1997.  This dataset comprises of the results of a survey carried out by Ivor Rees to ground truth RoxAnn data collected in Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1998-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:02:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17902\tNRW_DS112020\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.586Z\t881\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommon Land Registers Fully Attributed Dataset\t© Local Authorities and CNC/NRW  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Attribution Statement: © Local Authorities \u0026amp; © CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132303230\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Common Land Registers 2014.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Common Land Register 2001.lyr\t\tRegistered common land element of Section 4 Map as required by Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000. This dataset contains the Common Land areas of Wales. It is a fully attributed dataset, therefore due to sensitivities in the data cannot be released. Any line shown as not matching the original boundary has been copied from what is shown on the original registers and is not a digitising error.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:46:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17913\tNRW_DS112114\t2024-05-28T12:34:44.094Z\t626\tValid\tEditable\tOpen Access - Dedicated Forests (CRoW Act)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132313134\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Dedicated Forests 2014.lyr X:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Dedicated Forests 2004.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Gwen Thomas, Advisor (2) Estates Business Systems   Gary Eccleston/Geospatial team are responsible for the GIS digitisation and updates.\tDataset showing forests that comprised the freehold public forest estate dedicated by former Forestry Commission Wales (FCW), now Natural Resources Wales (NRW), under section 16 of the Countryside and Rights of Way (CRoW) Act in 2005. Some forest that was dedicated has since been sold and is now in private ownership.   When using this data to understand the extent of Open Access , it should be used in conjunction with Open land, Open Country, Registered Common Land, Other Statutory Access Land, and Other Dedicated Land from the same years.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-16\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_PUBLIC_FOREST_2014\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_PUBLIC_FOREST_2014\t2026-01-12T15:53:26.361Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17899\tNRW_DS112214\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.591Z\t1052\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPalaeoecological Study of Llyn Padarn (2009)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132323134\tElectronic data is archived as project 269, media no. 980.   The report is available on CCW\u0027s Electronic Documents Management System (Ffynnon: DCT-10-088698)\tSue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, NRW (Formerly Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, CCW)\tLlyn Padarn is designated as a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) for several reasons including its populations of Arctic charr and floating water-plantain. However, there have been several concerns of the water quality, owing to the decline in adult charr population and the occurrence of the blue-green algal bloom in 2009. In light of this, it was necessary to assess the recent environmental change that has occurred in the lake.  The aim of this project is to sample and analyse a lake sediment core from Llyn Padarn and relate the findings to chemical and nutrient changes over time and known events in the lake\u0027s history. Palaeolimnological methods offer a means of reconstructing the environmental history of standing waters by studying subfossil fragments of the biota preserved in the lake sediments. The project aims to reconstruct the environmental history of Llyn Padarn over approximately the last century using diatoms, with particular attention to the eutrophication history of the site. The study focuses on diatom remains in sediment cores, and also includes the application of an existing diatom-total phosphorus (TP) model to reconstruct the nutrient history of the lake. In addition to this, X-ray fluorescence analysis was carried out to assess down core changes in chemical composition, with a particular focus on iron, copper and P. The analysis of a dated sediment core collected from Llyn Padarn in 2009 (PADA2) was used to assess environmental change over the last 100-150 years.  The data was also used to reconstruct the environmental history of Llyn Padarn over approximately the last century with particular attention to the eutrophication history of the site. Diatom analysis of only three samples from an undated core taken from the lake in August 2004 showed that the diatom assemblage shifted from species typically associated with nutrient-poor waters towards those associated with more productive systems. This study builds on this earlier work by analysing a new sediment core at a higher resolution and providing a chronology to determine the timing and rates of change. The study feeds into ongoing monitoring at Llyn Padarn to determine the trophic status of the lake and the health of the Arctic charr population.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-05-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:56:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17900\tNRW_DS112225\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.566Z\t716\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBenthic Habitats: Maps of Sensitivity to Fishing Gears (Intertidal and Subtidal)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of a NRW Restricted Release Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132323235\tLegacy CCW Archive (File path is Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Users\\Sue Hearn\\\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team (Was Dr Clare Eno, Senior Fisheries Policy Officer, Envrionmental Policy Group, NRW)\tThis dataset consists of intertidal and subtidal maps of the sensitivity of benthic fisheries habitats to fishing gear types within the Welsh Territorial Seas (high water to 12 nm offshore).  Intertidal biotope mapping was carried out in order to determine the distribution and extent of seashore habitats, biological communities and species on the shores of Wales. Subtidal biotopes were predicted in HABMAP in order to act as a guide for marine scientists. The biotopes were converted to fisheries habitats in order to provide a means of assessing the sensitivity of benthic habitats to the effects of fishing gear types. The sensitivity of each fisheries habitat to each fishing gear and access was determined in order to provide a basis for management advice and fisheries regulation.  The intertidal area consists of the whole of the Welsh coastline between the limits of the tidal rise and fall. The subtidal area consists of Welsh water out to 12 nm offshore which are within the Southern Irish Sea i.e. sections around the Severn in the south and the Dee in the north are not included. HABMAP modelling has been re-run to include these areas in the south and north and these biotopes are due to be included in the fisheries habitats maps in 2010.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:47:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17910\tNRW_DS112227\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.746Z\t437\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal and Subtidal Predicted Benthic Fisheries Habitats Maps  (2009)\t© CNC/NRW 2010 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of a NRW Restricted Release Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132323237\tStated as Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Users\\Sue_Hr\\Benthic Fisheries habitats Maps but should have been moved to Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS Users\\Sue_Hr BUT THIS LOCATION DOES NOT APPEAR TO EXIST.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team (Formerly Claire Eno)\tThis dataset is consists of intertidal and subtidal maps of benthic fisheries habitats within the Welsh Territorial Seas (high water to 12 nm offshore).  Intertidal biotope mapping was carried out in order to determine the distribution and extent of seashore habitats, biological communities and species on the shores of Wales. Subtidal biotopes were predicted in HABMAP in order to act as a guide for marine scientists. The biotopes were converted to fisheries habitats in order to provide a means of assessing the sensitivity of benthic habitats to the effects of fishing gear types. The intertidal area consists of the whole of the welsh coastline between the limits of the tidal rise and fall.  The subtidal area consists of Welsh water out to 12 nm  offshore which are within the Southern Irish Sea  i.e. sections around the Severn in the south and the Dee in the north are not included. HABMAP modelling has been re-run to include these areas in the south and north and these biotopes are due to be included in the fisheries habitats maps in 2010.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:43:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17891\tNRW_DS112289\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.941Z\t653\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Tide-swept Boulder Habitat in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2008\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132323839\tThe data are archived as Project No 199, Media No 550 and is stored on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters.   H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MSCB\\2008 ASML MSCB\tIntertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Rocky reefs (hard substrata) are of considerable conservation importance due the high diversity of the communities they support and, as such, are included under Annex 1 of the EU Habitats Directive (92/43/EEC) (Hill et al. 1998). They are an abundant feature of the UK coastline, especially on western coasts where exposed bedrock predominates. In some areas, the substratum may be composed entirely of bedrock whilst on less steeply shelving / gently shelving shores, large boulders, cobbles, gravel, sand and mud may be present (Hill et al., 1998). Rocky reefs are extremely variable in terms of their structure and the communities they support with reef communities being influenced by the type of rock and the microhabitats available, sediment availability, degree of shelter, shore height, and temperature regime. Communities in exposed areas are usually dominated by sponges, anemones and foliose red algae with ascidians, delicate filamentous algae and fan worms being more common in sheltered areas (JNCC, 2005).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-06-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:02:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17914\tNRW_DS112290\t2024-05-28T12:34:44.014Z\t448\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sediment Flats in Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2008\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132323930\tThe data are archived as Project No 199, Media No 549 and is stored on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters.  [A]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MSCB\\2008 ASML MSCB\tIntertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW\tMenai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC)  is designated for five Annex 1 habitats: Sandbanks which are slightly covered by sea water all the time, Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide, Reefs, Large shallow inlets and bays and Submerged or partially submerged sea caves. Of these mudflats, sandflats and reefs are the main habitats of interest within intertidal areas of Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. Conservation objectives for these habitats are given in the Regulation 33 advice for the SAC.  The overriding objective of the survey was to establish the reference condition for the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC and develop monitoring stations for recording the level of natural change. This would enable the development of conservation objectives for the appropriate management of the features of the SAC.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:03:27.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17897\tNRW_DS112379\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.536Z\t299\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGround Monitoring of Common Scoter Melinitta nigra in Carmarthen Bay 2008 - 2009\t© CNC/NRW, 2010. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132333739\tData is held in the Marine Archive as project no 272 (media 989).\tInternal Custodian: - Marine Ornithologist, NRW. - Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tCarmarthen Bay Special Protection Area (SPA) was classified as the UK\u0027s first SPA in 2003 on the basis of holding at least 1% (16,000 individuals: Wetlands International 2006) of the biogeographic population of common scoter Melanitta nigra. Common Scoter feed on bivalves and other invertebrates such as polychaetes and amphipods which are found in abundance in the shallow waters (less than 10m) in Carmarthen Bay. Common scoter are also listed as a Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species. Former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), has supported ground based counts since 1996, following the Sea Empress oil spill. The purpose of this data capture was to carry out one scoter count of Carmarthen Bay in January and February 2009. This work feeds in to the condition monitoring and surveillance of Carmarthen Bay SPA and contributes to Common Standards Monitoring.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:57:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17905\tNRW_DS112384\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.567Z\t315\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGround Monitoring of Common Scoter Melinitta nigra in Carmarthen Bay 2009 - 2010\t© CNC/NRW 2010. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132333834\tData is archived under project no 273 (media 990).\tInternal Custodian: - Marine Ornithologist, NRW. - Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tCarmarthen Bay Special Protection Area (SPA) was classified as the UK\u0027s first SPA in 2003 on the basis of holding at least 1% (16,000 individuals: Wetlands International 2006) of the biogeographic population of common scoter Melanitta nigra. Common Scoter feed on bivalves and other invertebrates such as polychaetes and amphipods which are found in abundance in the shallow waters ( less than10m) in Carmarthen Bay. Common scoter are also listed as a BAP species. Purpose of data capture was to collate data to feed into the condition monitoring and surveillance of Carmarthen Bay SPA and contributes to Common Standards Monitoring.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:57:55.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17894\tNRW_DS112400\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.059Z\t237\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal habitat diversity (neighbourhood analysis)\t© CNC/NRW 2010 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343030\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Habitat diversity in the intertidal range is measured using biotope richness, biotope distinctness and the number of Biodiversity Action Plan priority features metrics. Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach that analyses data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 1 km is utilised to present these metrics. Biotope information for the Welsh intertidal zone was collated from CCW and is full coverage collected using standardised methods.  Establish intertidal diversity from known data.  The Welsh coastline, from the mean high-water datum to the mean low-water datum covering the intertidal zone.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:01:59.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17901\tNRW_DS112401\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.482Z\t301\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal habitat diversity\t© CNC/NRW 2010  There are no use restrictions on this data.   Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343031\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Habitat diversity in the intertidal range is measured using biotope richness, biotope distinctness and the number of Biodiversity Action Plan priority features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 1 km is utilised to present these metrics. Biotope information for the Welsh intertidal zone was collated from CCW and is full coverage collected using standardised methods.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish intertidal habitat diversity from known data. The Welsh coastline from the mean high water datum to the mean low water datum covering the intertidal zone.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:56:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17893\tNRW_DS112402\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.951Z\t334\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal Species Diversity Confidence Layer\t© CNC/NRW 2010  There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343032\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tContact Data Services for metadata information. Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Confidence ratings, based on the quality and quantity of data used in the final species diversity analysis, was calculated for each hexagonal unit to provide users with a view of the underlying data when examining hotspot occurrence. The purpose of this data capture was to establish species diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline from the mean high water datum to the mean low water datum covering the intertidal zone.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:08.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17895\tNRW_DS112403\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.160Z\t493\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal species diversity layer (chao2 analysis)\t© CNC/NRW 2010 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343033\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956.\tSubtidal Team Leader, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. The Chao2 estimator was applied to intertidal species data collated from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot), the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH). Chao2 is a technique for extrapolating species richness from limited numbers of samples based on the concept that rare species carry most information about the number of missing species; this was employed to check for artifacts in analyses. The purpose of the data capture was to establish species diversity from known data of the Welsh coastline from the mean high water datum to the mean low water datum covering the intertidal zone.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17890\tNRW_DS112404\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.013Z\t283\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal species diversity layer (neighbourhood analysis)\t© CNC/NRW 2010 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343034\tThe data is archived as project 260, Media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team The metadata point of contact is the Data Service Team.\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity in the intertidal range is measured using species richness, average taxonomic distinctness and number of Welsh Nationally Important Marine Features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 1 km is used to present these metrics. Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach that analyses data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one. The purpose of this data capture was to establish intertidal species diversity from known data along the Welsh coastline from the mean high water datum to the mean low water datum covering the intertidal zone.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T16:06:55.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17888\tNRW_DS112405\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.597Z\t243\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal species diversity layer\t© CNC/NRW 2010 There are no use restrictions on this data.  There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343035\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity in the intertidal range is measured using species richness, average taxonomic distinctness and number of Welsh Nationally Important Marine Features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 1 km is used to present these metrics. The purpose of this data capture was to establish intertidal diversity from known data of the Welsh coastline from the mean high water datum to the mean low water datum covering the intertidal zone.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:34:04.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17892\tNRW_DS112406\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.940Z\t309\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Species and Habitat Diversity Confidence Layer\t© CNC/NRW 2010.  There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343036\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Contact Data Services for metadata information.\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Confidence ratings, based on the quality and quantity of data used in the final species diversity analysis, was calculated for each hexagonal unit to provide users with a view of the underlying data when examining hotspot occurrence. The purpose of this data capture was to establish species diversity from known data for the Welsh coastline taken from the mean high-water datum to 12 nautical miles offshore.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:11.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17889\tNRW_DS112407\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.013Z\t641\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Species Diversity Layer (Chao2 Analysis)\t© CNC/NRW 2010. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343037\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of contact is Data Services\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. The Chao2 estimator was applied to species data collated from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot), the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH). Chao2 is a technique for extrapolating species richness from limited numbers of samples based on the concept that rare species carry most information about the number of missing species; this was employed to check for artifacts in analyses. The purpose of this data capture was to establish species diversity from known data for the Welsh coastline taken from the mean high water datum to 12 nautical miles offshore.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:07:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17887\tNRW_DS112408\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.578Z\t593\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Species Diversity Layer (Neighbourhood Analysis)\t© CNC/NRW 2010. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343038\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of Contact: Master Reference Data Team.\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity in the range is measured using species richness, average taxonomic distinctness and number of Welsh Nationally Important Marine Features metrics. Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach that analyses data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 10 km is utilised to present these metrics. The purpose of this data capture was to establish diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline, between the mean high-water mark up to 12 nautical miles offshore.   An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17886\tNRW_DS112409\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.475Z\t336\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Species Diversity Layer\t© CNC/NRW. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343039\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity is measured using species richness, average taxonomic distinctness and number of Welsh Nationally Important Marine Features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 10 km is used to present these metrics.    An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses. The purpose of this data capture was to establish species diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline from mean high water datum to 12 nautical miles offshore.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:57.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17936\tNRW_DS112410\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.870Z\t304\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Subtidal Habitat Diversity Layer (Neighbourhood Analysis)\t© CNC/NRW 2010 . There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343130\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tThe metadata point of contact is: Data Services Team Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Habitat diversity in the range is measured using biotope richness, biotope distinctness and the number of Biodiversity Action Plan priority features metrics. Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach that analyses data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 10 km is utilised to present these metrics. The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline, from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:09.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17940\tNRW_DS112412\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.963Z\t307\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSubtidal Habitat Diversity Layer (2010)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343132\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Habitat diversity in the range is measured using biotope richness, biotope distinctness and the number of Biodiversity Action Plan priority features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 1 km is used to present these metrics.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline, from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:24:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17930\tNRW_DS112413\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.946Z\t514\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Subtidal Species and Habitat Diversity Confidence Layer\t© CNC/NRW 2010. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343133\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Point of contact: Data Services Team\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Confidence ratings, based on the quality and quantity of data used in the final species diversity analysis, was calculated for each hexagonal unit to provide users with a view of the underlying data when examining hotspot occurrence.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline, from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.    An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:10.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17935\tNRW_DS112415\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.871Z\t372\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Subtidal Species Diversity Layer (Chao2 Analysis)\t© CNC/NRW 2010. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343135\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. The Chao2 estimator was applied to species data collated from the CCW and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot), the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH). Chao2 is a technique for extrapolating species richness from limited numbers of samples based on the concept that rare species carry most information about the number of missing species; this was employed to check for artifacts in analyses. The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline, from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17926\tNRW_DS112416\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.825Z\t342\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Subtidal species diversity layer (neighbourhood analysis)\t© CNC/NRW 2010. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343136\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity in the range is measured using biotope richness, biotope distinctness and the number of Biodiversity Action Plan priority features metrics.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline, from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.   An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17941\tNRW_DS112417\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.962Z\t269\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Subtidal species diversity layer\t© CNC/NRW 2010. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343137\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of Contact: Data Services, NRW\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity is measured using species richness, average taxonomic distinctness and number of Welsh Nationally Important Marine Features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 20 km is used to present these metrics.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline, from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17929\tNRW_DS112484\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.898Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGeological Site Condition Reports\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343834\tKept by Area Earth Science Officers. Digital photographs stored on the personal computers of the Area Earth Science Officers For instance, Bob Mathews works from home and stores his records on a work laptop that is backed-up on an external hard-drive. Paper records are also available.\tRaymond Roberts, North Region Earth Science Officer, NRW.\tShort paper forms used to record condition of geological features/ Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), accompanied by digital photographs. The forms form the basis of the geological site monitoring condition reports on the Monitoring Module of the Features Database.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:56:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17942\tNRW_DS112503\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.970Z\t612\tValid\tEditable\tHabitat Mapping for Conservation and Management of the Southern Irish Sea (HABMAP)\tPredicted habitat maps and model outputs: © CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. Input data parameter maps: The datasets are wholly owned by third parties (BGS, Met Office, SeaZone, ABPmer). The datasets are restricted. NRW is not licenced to release this data. Those seeking a copy of this data must approach the third parties directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353033\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\HABMAP\\   or z:\\Archive\\legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\HABMAP   or Archived as Project 275 and Media 1029 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1001to1100\\Media 1029)\tInternal Contact: Karen Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe HABMAP project was set up in response to the need for better spatial awareness of habitat distributions in the Southern Irish Sea. This work produced habitat maps of the seabed using novel predictive modelling techniques. This dataset is related to the predictive modelling only.    The HABMAP Extension Project has built on the methods developed during the original project, and has repeated the modelling work using higher resolution / improved input datasets to help increase the accuracy of the predictive map outputs. The modelling work has also been extended to cover all of Welsh waters (previously cut-off at the Interreg funding boundary), notably including the Dee and Severn estuaries.    The purpose of this data capture was to provide seabed habitat maps that could be used for conservation and management. Project outputs might be used in strategic planning, decision making for offshore developments, Marine Protected Area selection, sensitivity mapping and mapping essential fish habitats. However, because of the way the  has been produced, and the fact that some data has been modelled and derived, the maps are not appropriate to act as the sole evidence for any specific planning or regulatory decision or assessment without further supporting studies or evidence.    The project boundaries were as follows: Southern Irish Sea- land-based boundaries include the whole Welsh coast to the English border on the east side of the Dee Estuary in the north, and the whole Severn Estuary and Bristol Channel coastline in the south, extending as far as Morte Point (east of Ifracombe) in England. The southern project boundary then extends offshore (skirting the northern tip of Lundy) across to a point approximate 60km west of Waterford on the Irish coast, including the whole SE Ireland coastline and offshore banks as well as parts of the Celtic Sea. The boundary then extends northwards along the Irish coast to a point approximately 40 km north of Dublin.  Only the predicted habitat maps and model outputs is available under the Open Governance Licence and available for public use, see the Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-ABP Marine Environmental Research Ltd-British Geological Survey (BGS)-Met Office-SeaZone\t2017-08-11\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2618\t2026-01-12T16:23:22.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17944\tNRW_DS112540\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.970Z\t744\tValid\tEditable\tUnitisation Boundaries\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353430\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Unitization Boundaries.lyr\tChristine Edwards, Lead Specialist Advisor: Protected Sites Programme, Protected Sites\tDesignated sites in Wales include, but is not limited to, Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), Special Protection Areas (SPA), Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) and Ramsar sites. These sites have been divided into management units. This dataset contains the boundaries of these units and includes information such as designation type, unit name, unit area, national grid reference and former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) regions.   Digitising began in 2007 and is on going as and when new sites are created or existing sites are altered. The purpose of these management units is to have a coherent plan in order to manage sites in a practical manner to ensure that work is done to get sites and their corresponding features into a favourable condition, as per the requirements of the protected sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-10-16\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/406/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_UNITIZATION\t2025-12-19T10:49:29.330Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17931\tNRW_DS112544\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.943Z\t437\tValid\tEditable\tDrop-Down Video Survey Location Inventory (1999 - 2010)\t© CNC/NRW 2010. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353434\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine habitat video points ,  Archived under project 277, media 1057 (pathway: Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1001to1100\\Media1057) Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\Marine_Habitat_Videos\tCharles Lindenbaum, Specialist (Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer), Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting,   Monica Jones, Data Services Team Leader.\tThis is a spatial dataset to show the location  of drop-down video surveys. This dataset acts as an inventory of dropdown video survey which enables staff to navigate drop-down video archive using and identifying specific geographic location of each video clip. The purpose of this data capture was to allow staff to use and access relevant spatial information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:08:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17945\tNRW_DS112565\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.920Z\t522\tValid\tEditable\tOpen Access - Open Country (CRoW Act)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353635\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Open Country 2014.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Open County 2004.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Chris Heaps, Specialist Advisor: Trails and Open Access Gary Eccleston/Geospatial team are responsible for the GIS digitisation and updates.\tThe CRoW Act provides for public access on foot to certain types of land, amends the law relating to public rights of way, increases measures for the management and protection for Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and strengthens wildlife enforcement legislation, whilst providing for better management of Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONB).   Open Country 2014 is digitised to OS MasterMap. This data was part of the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000 (CroW Act).   When using this data to understand the extent of Open Access , it should be used in conjunction with Open land, Open Country, Registered Common Land, Other Statutory Access Land and Other Dedicated Land from the same years. Registered Common Land is a faithful copy of Local Authority commons.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-16\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/393/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_OPEN_COUNTRY_2014\t2026-01-13T17:32:51.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17938\tNRW_DS112566\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.889Z\t658\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSarn Badrig Reef Sublittoral Survey 1993\t© CNC/NRW 1994 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353636\tThe MNCR survey results have been entered into Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000498) Paper copies of the report are available in NRW\u0027s library and the original report is held by Lucy Kay in NRW\u0027s North Region.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW North Region Marine Specialist, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of a survey aiming to trial the three main methods used to describe, biotope recognition, biotope mapping, determining species presence, determining species abundance and cost effectiveness.  The three methods trialled were the BIOMAR/Roxanne methodology, the MNCR method and the Seasearch method.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1994-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:19:51.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17939\tNRW_DS112571\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.966Z\t734\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLamprey Habitat Assessement in the River Usk 1999\t© CNC/NRW 1999 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353731\t[A]  Associated is data is held within the Marine data Archive as project 280, media 1076.  [B]  Biological Data is held in Recorder 6 Lamprey Habitat Assessment in the River Usk 1998 - CCW - Report No. 365 CCW000280000000C\t\tThe River Usk has been notified as a site of Special Site of Scientific Interest (SSSI) and a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The three lamprey species which occur there are all listed in Annex II of the EU Species and Habitat Directive. The aim of this study was to investigate the status of lampreys in the Usk and to consider matters relevant to their conservation. Specific objectives of the study have been.  -  To define the characteristics of lamprey spawning and nursery habitat -  To obtain and summarise information on the distribution of lampreys and lamprey spawning and nursery habitat in the Usk -  To investigate the possibility of using information from RHS to predict the occurrence or lampreys in the Usk.  -  To make recommendations to assist with the monitoring and conservation of lampreys and their habitat. -  To monitoring the status of lampreys required to comply with the reporting requirements of  the Habitats Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:44:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17932\tNRW_DS112682\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.908Z\t388\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommon Scoter (Melanitta nigra) Summary Statistics and Distribution Analysis for Carmarthen Bay 1998 - 2010\t© CNC/NRW 2010. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132363832\tSummary data is archived under project 213, media 579 within the Marine Data Archive (W drive).   Source raw data is not archived with this project as the data is already in the archive with the project it was collected under\tData Custodians Marine Ornithologist, NRW or Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tCarmarthen Bay, an extensive, shallow embayment off the south coast of Wales, UK, encompasses a wide range of rich and diverse coastal and marine habitats. Carmarthen Bay is an internationally important wintering ground for the Common Scoter duck (Melanitta nigra) and is widely regarded as the most important such site in the UK causing the site to be proposed as a Special Protection Area (pSPA) under the EC Birds Directive. Former Countryside Council for Wales (now part of Natural Resources Wales) have supported ground and aerial based surveys for a 10 year period since the Sea Empress oil spill in 1996. The purpose of this data capture was to collate and synthesise records on common scoter populations and distributions for a 10 year period. Data was collated from old CCW reports. A summary of the data  has been produced to cover the period 1998 to 2010.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:09:21.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17943\tNRW_DS112723\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.968Z\t822\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTir Cymen Farm Management Plans for Wales (Paper Records)\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Maps are derived from Ordnance Survey base maps and may only be issued under licence available from NRW.  © CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132373233\tOff-site storage (contact library team)\tBuddug Jones, Common Land and Agriculture Advisor, Ecosystem Services Team/ Natural Resources Planning, NRW\tManagement plans for a number of holdings throughout Wales that are part of the Tir Cymen Farm Scheme. Each plan consists of personal details including the names and address of the owner and tenant of the holding; the type of farm enterprise; the general management practice of the holding including livestock type, use of common land and the growth of crops; the changes in management to conform to Tir Cymen guidelines; schedules of annual management payments and standard capital payments; paper maps showing the extent of the holding and surrounding area, the different habitat types surrounding the holding and the capital works being completed around the holding. The plans also list the whole farm management code which includes listing the archaeological features, land forms and water and wetlands found around the holding. The purpose of data capture was to ensure selected land holdings meet the Tir Cymen Farm Scheme.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1993-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:10.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17927\tNRW_DS112726\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.694Z\t780\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTir Cymen Farms - Raw Data\t© CNC/NRW  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.   Maps are derived from Ordnance Survey base maps and may only be issued under licence available from NRW. © CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132373236\tOff-site storage (contact library team)\tInternal Custodian: Buddug Jones, Common Land and Agriculture Advisor, Ecosystem Services Team/ Natural Resources Planning.  Metadata contact: Information Services Officer, Records and Information Services Team\tRaw data to support the management plans for a number of holdings throughout Wales that are part of the Tir Cymen Farm Scheme. The raw data includes macro landscape assessment records taken in each of the areas over a five year period; characterisation records; species plot records; cover plot records; boundary plot records, management maps. The purpose of this data capture was to ensure selected land holdings meet and continue to meet the Tir Cymen Farm Scheme.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1996-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:07:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17937\tNRW_DS112727\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.940Z\t488\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Twaite (Alosa fallax) and Allis (Alosa alosa) Shad egg survey of the River Usk (2010-2011)\t© CNC/NRW 2010 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132373237\t[A]  2010 survey data is held in the Marine Data Archive as Project 281 and Media 1096.  [B]  2011 survey data is held in the Marine Data Archive as Project 326, Media 1283.  [C]  Biological Data is held in Recorder 6: -  Shad Egg Survey River Usk 2010 - CCW - Report no.10/8/1 CCW000280000000E   -  Shad Egg Survey River Usk 2011 - CCW - Report no.11/8/3  CCW0008000000009\t\tThe River Usk rises in the Black Mountain range in the west of the Brecon Beacons National Park and flows east and then south, to enter the Severn Estuary at Newport.  The river is designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for several features, including salmon, lampreys and shads.  Twaite (Alosa fallax) and allis (Alosa alosa) shad are anadromous members of the Family Clupeidae (Herring).  In Britain, twaite shad is known to spawn in the Rivers Severn, Wye, Usk and Tywi.  However, it is not certain whether a breeding population of allis shad still exists.  The fomer Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), and now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) is responsible for monitoring the condition of shad populations within SACs and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within these designated sites. The JNCC \u0027Common Standards Methodology Guidance for Freshwater Fauna\u0027 (2005) gives a target for the spawning distribution of shad.  It states there should be \u0027no decline in spawning distribution\u0027 assessed by kick sampling during May and June.  There are two main data sources of shad spawning in the River Usk.  The Environment Agency Wales (EAW), now Natural Resources Wales recorded the presence or absence of shad eggs at a number of sites, from 1990 to 2005, although surveys have only been conducted annually since 2003.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T15:40:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17934\tNRW_DS112739\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.829Z\t13\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Species in Wales (Archived 2008)\t© CNC/NRW 2007. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132373339\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP Species Archived.     Data is archived under Project No. 231, Media 600.   This dataset has now been updated and superseded by a more up-to-date version of this dataset known as Section 7 species.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Biodiversity Liaison Officer (Ben Wray?), NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tThe UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) was published back in 1994, and was the UK Government’s response to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD), which the UK signed up to in 1992 in Rio de Janeiro. Similarly under Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act, 2006 it is required that the a \u0027list of the living organisms and types of habitat which in the Assembly\u0027s opinion are of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\u0027. The UK BAP is one of the main focuses of this work.  As part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 CBD and NERC Act there have been a number of initiatives to identify those species and habitats whose UK populations may be under threat and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity.  The list includes all marine UK BAP priorities as well as additional habitats and species to reflect those in need of conservation action in Wales. For each species and habitat data provided it should include a map of the distribution around the Welsh coast and supporting text with information on the ecology, rarity, legislative protection, occurrence in statutory sites and known threats. This data was captured to contribute towards providing all public bodies and Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) partnerships with information on the marine habitats and species included in the Section 42 list. This work included intertidal habitat searches for biotopes in the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Phase 1 dataset, done using 1997 biotope classification codes. Biotopes associated with \u0027Section 42\u0027 habitats were compiled using the 2004 biotope codes. Subtidal habitats were plotted using records of biotopes and/or species from Marine Recorder (Snapshot data dated November 2007).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17933\tNRW_DS112903\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.834Z\t365\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools in Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2008\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2010. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132393033\tThe data and the report are archived as Project No 199, Media no. 548 and 555.  [A] This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters and stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW headquarters on optical media. [B]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Cardigan Bay\\2008 ASML Cardigan BayData\tCustodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata point of contact:  Master Reference Data Team.\tIntertidal surveys provide comprehensive baseline data for monitoring change or for the identification of locations sensitive to certain anthropogenic impacts. The data also provides information to support a notification programme or intertidal Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) thereby providing a series of intertidal designated sites across Wales.  Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation  Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated primarily for Bottlenose dolphins, but is also designated for Sandbanks, Reefs and Sea Caves. Of these Reefs and Sea Caves are present within intertidal areas of Cardigan Bay SAC. Rockpools are also a notable feature of some intertidal reef areas in the SAC. Phase 1 surveys of intertidal habitats in the SAC were carried out in the SAC in 1996 and 2003 (Brazier et al. 2007), providing detailed mapping of the intertidal biotopes with some information on characterizing species. The data represents the results of a survey of intertidal rockpools at Aberporth, one of two sites surveyed during a similar survey undertaken in 2007, within the Cardigan Bay SAC.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:09:57.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17928\tNRW_DS113087\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.618Z\t657\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Survey Mapping (phase 1) from Pen-ychain to Criccieth, Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) 2010\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2011. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313133303837\tThe data are archived as Project No 289, Media No 1192 [K:\\Archive\\Media 1101-1200\\Media 1192]. Arc LayerY:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Pen Llyn SAC Monitoring.gdb\tPaul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team. Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. The purpose of this data capture was to conduct a habitat survey of the intertidal area from Pen-ychain to Criccieth Castle on the south coast of the Llyn Peninsula. This work supported the detailed SAC monitoring program by providing a more up to date baseline survey of the intertidal area. It provided CCW with information to help deal with current and future casework in relation to coastal defence, and reporting particularly regarding BAP habitats and species of conservation concern. One such Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitat are the Sabellaria alveolata reefs which are formed by the honeycomb worm Sabellaria alveolata, a polychaete which constructs tubes in tightly packed masses with a distinctive honeycomb-like appearance. These reefs can be up to 30 or even 50 cm thick and take the form of hummocks, sheets or more massive formations. Reefs are mainly found on the bottom third of the shore, but may reach mean high water of neap tides and extend into the shallow subtidal in places. The current report aims to fill these knowledge gaps for the survey area.  The purpose of this data capture is to update previous intertidal mapping in the area and to specifically provide an updated extent of honeycomb worm reef Sabellaria alveolata. The specific project aims were to identify natural temporal changes that have been witnessed since the original Phase 1 Intertidal Mapping Project in 1996-1997.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T10:25:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17923\tNRW_DS113445\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.989Z\t428\tValid\tEditable\tModelling the Predicted Spread of a Carpet Sea Squirt ( Didemnum vexillum ) around the Welsh Coast (2011)\t© CNC/NRW 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313133343435\tData is archived as Project No. 298, media No. 1208 (File path is Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 1208)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of contact: Data Services, NRW\tDidemnum vexillum is an invasive sea squirt that is not native to UK shores. It was first detected in Europe in 1991 and has since spread to several countries (including France, Ireland and the UK). The species has been located in Wales, Scotland and England and there is concern D. vexillum may have negative impacts on biodiversity and shellfish interests.    Predicting the spread of an invasive species is crucial when assessing possible management actions. The potential impacts of the species on both biodiversity and commercial interests need to be studied and a cost-benefit approach taken to decide on the best course of management for that species.    Geographic Information System (GIS) offers a fast, efficient way to map this predicted spread. The results of this mapping can then be used to focus on areas where D. vexillum may conflict with conservation and commercial interests.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-01-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:10:58.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17925\tNRW_DS113460\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.071Z\t618\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2008 CCW / ASML Carmarthen Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Cockle Bed Survey\t© CNC/NRW 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313133343630\tThe data are archived as Project No. 199, Media No. 549 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 549) whilst the report is held within Media No. 1218 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1201-1300\\Media 1218.This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW Maes y Fynnon. [A]  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2008 ASML Carm [B]  Stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Fynnon on optical media.  [C]  GIS data Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Carmarthen Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb [D] Marine Recorder file (survey number MRCCW16300000012)\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Internal Metadata point of contact - Master Reference Data Team  or Data Services\tThe Habitats Directive requires that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II.  For SACs in Wales, the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) was therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  To do this the former CCW developed a programme of feature condition monitoring.  Aquatic Survey and Monitoring Ltd. (ASML) have been contracted by CCW to develop and manage the monitoring programme for the intertidal features in marine SACs for the period 2006 to 2011; working as a team with CCW HQ and CCW Regional staff.  In Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are Large shallow inlets and bays, estuaries and Mudflats and sandflats not covered by sea water at low tide.  Specific areas of interest include the extensive sediment flats of the 3 rivers (Taf, Tywi and Gwendraeth), Loughor estuary (Burry inlet), open coast sands and notable rocky habitats, particularly in west Carmarthen Bay.  This report and dataset contains the results of the surveys carried out in those areas in June 2007.   The forms part of the monitoring that CCW also developed on intertidal monitoring across Wales during 2004 and 2005.  These surveys were managed and implemented for CCW by the Institute of Estuarine and Coastal Studies (IECS, University of Hull).  These projects focused on a wide range of sensitive habitats such as Zostera, muddy gravels, caves, rockpools, algal-dominated rocky shores, Sabellaria reefs, under-boulders, and various rare habitats and species.  In Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC, IECS surveyed Ruppia maritima (Boyes 2008a), Zostera noltii beds (Mazik \u0026amp; Boyes 2009), the biotope SLR.FserX.T (Boyes 2008b), the polychaete Ophelia bicornis (Mazik 2008), hydroid dominated rockpools (Boyes et al. 2004), saltmarsh boundary (Boyes \u0026amp; Brazier 2008) and the sediment flats across the SAC (Allen et al. 2008).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:11:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17920\tNRW_DS113480\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.783Z\t292\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Non-Native Atlas for Wales 2010\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2010. NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313133343830\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species  Archived under project 286, media no. 1184. Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Species\\all_wales\\Marine\\NonNatives\tCustodian: Marine and Freshwater Group or GIS and Spatial Data Team. Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThe dataset contains a series of records for each of the non-native marine species currently identified as occurring in Wales. The records have been extracted from Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCWs) Phase 1 Intertidal surveys, Marine Recorder data holdings, National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway web source, MarLIN/DASSH web source, DEFRA Marine Protected Area (MPA) non-native GIS layers, Local Environmental Record Centres (LERC\u0027s ), and other ad-hoc records collated by CCW staff or supplied by third parties. The purpose of this data collation is to monitor the spread of non-native marine species in Wales by creating a current and meaningful atlas using GIS layers for individual layers.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T10:26:10.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17924\tNRW_DS113767\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.078Z\t513\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFeasibility Study on the Use of Dispersants in the Bristol Channel in the Event of an Oil Pollution Incident (2002)\t© CNC/NRW and English Nature 2002. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ English Nature and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313133373637\tData is archived in the NRW data archive as project 299, media no.1209\tContact the Data Services Team Internal Custodian: Marine and Freshwater Evidence Manager, NRW\tThe approval and use of oil spill dispersants is regulated in UK waters. The Food and Environment Protection Act 1985 requires that a licence be issued for the deposit of any substance or article in the sea or that an exemption be granted under the Deposits in the Sea (Exemptions) Order 1985. The licensing authority for England and Wales is DEFRA (Department of Environment, Food and Rural Affairs (formerly MAFF - Ministry of Agriculture, Fisheries and Food).   Specific permission to use oil spill dispersants in shallow water (defined as areas of sea where the water depth is less than 20 metres, as marked on Admiralty charts, or within 1 nautical mile of such area) must be granted by DEFRA. The role of the EG will be to provide information about the sensitivities of resources in the area to oil pollution, so that DEFRA can make a reasoned decision on dispersant use.   The BCSEG considered many different relevant factors including the oil spill risks, likely spilled oil behaviour, characteristics of the Bristol Channel and the shoreline and marine resources that could be affected by spilled oil. The consequences of dispersing or not dispersing spilled oil were also considered. As a result of these deliberations, the areas of the Bristol Channel and Severn Estuary have been designated as dispersant use or non-use zones.  These zones are marked on maps for ease of reference. These zones are:  - Zone A Where dispersants can be used at any time of the tide  - Zone B Where dispersants can be used (but only with DEFRA\u0027s specific permission) on an outgoing tide from High Water until High Water + 5 hours  - Zone C Where dispersants can be used (but only with DEFRA\u0027s specific permission) on an outgoing tide from High Water until High Water + 2 hours  - Zone D Where dispersants are not to be used at any time .  In the event of oil pollution occurring in the Bristol Channel an Environment Group (EG) will be activated to consider the environmental consequences of the spill and the most suitable ways in which damage can be minimised. The use of oil spill dispersants is a response method that might be used. The Bristol Channel Standing Environment Group (BCSEG) commissioned this report to describe the factors in the Bristol Channel that need to be considered when deciding to use, or not to use, oil spill dispersants.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:14:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17922\tNRW_DS114189\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.811Z\t421\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGround Monitoring of Common Scoter Melinitta nigra in Carmarthen Bay (2010 - 2011)\t© CNC/NRW 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134313839\tData is archived under project no 301 (media 1211).\tInternal Custodians - Marine Ornithologist, NRW - Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata Contact: - Data Services\tCarmarthen Bay Spatial Protection Area (SPA) was classified as the UK\u0027s first SPA in 2003 on the basis of holding at least 1% (16,000 individuals) of the biogeographic population of Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra). The Common Scoter is strongly migratory and often travels considerable distances over land making brief stop-overs on inland waters. Its diet consists predominantly of molluscs, especially during the winter, although it occasionally takes other aquatic invertebrates such as crustaceans (e.g. barnacles and shrimps), worms, echinoderms, isopods, amphipods and insects (e.g. midges and caddis flies) as well as small fish and fish eggs which are found in abundance in the shallow water in Carmarthen Bay. Common scoter are also listed as a Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) has supported ground based counts since 1996, following the Sea Empress oil spill. This work feeds in to the condition monitoring and surveillance of Carmarthen Bay SPA and contributes to Common Standards Monitoring.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T10:26:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17921\tNRW_DS114338\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.812Z\t356\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sediment Transects in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2008\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2011. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134333338\tArchive project 199, media 1218. Data is held within Marine Recorder as No. MRCCW16300000012\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. The purpose of this data capture was to conduct Intertidal Special Area of Conservation monitoring.  Transect survey of sediment flats in Camarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC in June 2007. In Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are Large shallow inlets and bays, estuaries and Mudflats and sandflats not covered by sea water at low tide. Rockpools, overhangs and caves have also been identified as important elements of the large shallow inlets and bays feature and of Sites of Special Scientific Interest and metadata for these datasets are documented elsewhere.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T10:27:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17919\tNRW_DS114339\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.749Z\t339\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools, Caves and Overhangs in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2008\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2011. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134333339\tArchive project 199, media no. 1218 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1218) Species data available in Marine Recorder [MRCCW1630000001A] Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Carmarthen Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  Rockpools, overhangs and caves have been identified as important elements of the \u0027large shallow inlets and bays\u0027 feature and of Sites of Special Scientific Interest. These potentially species rich rocky habitats were surveyed in Carmarthen Bay to provide an inventory and to fully characterise these sub-features.  The purpose of this data capture was to provide an inventory and distribution map of selected biotopes and species inventory across South Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T10:28:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17918\tNRW_DS114387\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.626Z\t432\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Open Coast Sediment (Sandflats and Mudflats) in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2009\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2011. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134333837\tThe data and report are archived as Project No 109, Media No 1219 and stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Fynnon, on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage.H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2009 ASML PLAS\\\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe aim of this data capture was to proceed with the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) intertidal monitoring.  Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) developed a programme of intertidal monitoring work across Wales during 2004 and 2005. These surveys were managed and implemented for CCW by the Institute of Estuarine and Coastal Studies (IECS, University of Hull). These projects focused on a wide range of sensitive habitats such as Zostera, muddy gravels, caves, rock pools, algal dominated rocky shores, Sabellaria reefs, under-boulders, and various rare habitats and species.  Aquatic Survey and Monitoring Ltd. (ASML) have been contracted by CCW to continue development and management of the intertidal monitoring programme for each marine SAC for the period 2006 to 2011, working as a team with CCW HQ and CCW Regional staff.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T10:29:03.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17917\tNRW_DS114388\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.566Z\t588\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria reef in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2009\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2011. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134333838\tData is archived under: Project 190, media 1219\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary, tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats). CCW developed a programme of intertidal monitoring work across Wales during 2004 and 2005. These surveys were managed and implemented for CCW by the Institute of Estuarine and Coastal Studies (IECS, University of Hull). These projects focused on a wide range of sensitive habitats such as Zostera, muddy gravels, caves, rock pools, algal dominated rocky shores, Sabellaria reefs, under-boulders, and various rare habitats and species.   Aquatic Survey and Monitoring Ltd. (ASML) have been contracted by CCW to continue development and management of the intertidal monitoring programme for each marine SAC for the period 2006 to 2011, working as a team with CCW HQ and CCW Regional staff.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:08.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17916\tNRW_DS114436\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.538Z\t483\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2007 to 2013\t© CNC/NRW 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343336\tThe report and data collected under CCW contract FC 73-02-342 is archived as Project No 199, Media Nos 1221 and is maintained on a backed-up server based storage at NRW . H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2010 ASML Pembs\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. In Pembrokeshire Marine SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features include Reefs. The specific area sub-feature of interest covered here are the rockpools of Pen-y-holt on the wave exposed intertidal limestone reefs of South Pembrokeshire.  The aim of this data capture was to support a report of the Favourable conservation status of SAC features through intertidal monitoring. This data capture is related to studies carried out in these rockpools between 2007 and 2010.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-10-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T10:29:41.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17962\tNRW_DS114451\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.674Z\t773\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDiver Observations on Arctic Charr (Salvelinus alpinus) Spawning Grounds in Llyn Padarn Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) - December 2010\t© CNC/NRW 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343531\tData is archived within the MFSG data archive as project no. 302 and media no\u0027s 1214 and 1227.   Video is held on the imaging archive at V:\\MMT video Archive\\Tristan Freshwater\\2010 Llyn Padarn Arctic Charr Media1214 Additional footage is archived as  project 335, media 1301 and currently held on:  V:\\MMT video Archive\\Tristan Freshwater\\2011 Llyn Padan (Arctic Char) Media 1301 V:\\MMT video Archive\\Tristan Freshwater\\2011 1221 Llyn Padarn Arctic Charr RH Media 1301\tInternal Custodian: Rivers Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Contact:  Data Services for metadata information\tLlyn Padarn is a large, natural lake located at the foot of Snowdon alongside the village of Llanberis, North Wales. The lake is designated as a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) for its rare, genetically distinct Arctic charr (Salvelinus alpinus) population, the presence of the nationally scarce Annex II aquatic macrophyte, Luronium natans L. (Floating Water-plantain) and the exposure of geologically important Cambrian rock sequences. The Arctic charr population is one of only three naturally occurring populations in Wales and one of the southernmost in the UK. There has been concern in relation to the declining Arctic charr population in Llyn Padarn and the potential impact of the water quality of the lake on the population. This study was undertaken to investigate potential spawning areas in the lake for Arctic charr by a SCUBA diving survey. Knowledge of the location of the spawning grounds of charr in Llyn Padarn is important to inform management of the site for conservation of the species. The purpose of this data capture was to collected information so that any additional spawning grounds (to the one location known) could be identified along with any potentially important spawning sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:15:29.383Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17973\tNRW_DS114452\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.709Z\t808\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRestoration of Native Oyster, Ostrea edulis, in South Wales\t© CNC/NRW, 2011. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343532\tData is archived under project no. 306, media no. 1220 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1220)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team [previously  -Aethne Cooke (ex CCW), Marine Biodiversity Liaison Officer, NRW] Metadata Contact: Data Services, Evidence Knowledge and Information Management, KSP, NRW\tNative oyster populations throughout Europe suffered a serious decline in the late 19th and early 20th centuries due predominantly to over fishing and associated habitat loss, but exacerbated by anthropogenic and environmental pressures of pollution, disease and a series of cold winters. Historic accounts place Ostrea edulis at the centre of important local fisheries in South Wales back to the Roman occupation of Britain and describe a fishery supporting 200 vessels landing over 9 million oysters at its peak in the 19th century. Ostrea edulis are found on a wide variety of firm and stable seabed habitats where clean hard surfaces exist for larval settlement but it is likely that more complex habitat forms have historically existed including oyster banks and reefs. The aim of this study was to identify the scope and potential approaches for restoration of native oyster populations and habitats in South Wales. The associated report describes the options for practical approaches for restoration of the native oyster in its natural habitat in South Wales to meet UK and Welsh Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) targets for recovery.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-03-16\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:18:21.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17966\tNRW_DS114453\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.647Z\t433\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Mapping Project (Updating Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Biotope Mapping Survey)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343533\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1 Intertidal H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media1201to1300\\Media1217 Archived under project 305, media 1217. The data is available on the CCW corporate GIS (M) drive.\tInternal Custodians: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Spatial Data and GIS Team\tThe Phase 1 Intertidal project was completed in 2005 and maps finalised (see metadata record 24333), however since 2005 selected of areas have been re-mapped as up-to-date information was required for casework and other needs. There is a need to incorporate these newly mapped areas with the original Intertidal data layer and make it available as the most up-to-date data source.  A decision was made to only incorporate newly mapped areas or sites and not individual habitats, such as, Sabellaria reefs.   The purpose of this data capture was to update the Phase 1 Intertidal biotope and target note layers.   From 2005 onwards for updates, but the original sites were mapped from April 1996.  The methodology and the maps have undergone quality control independently, but the combined final output has not.  The data available to the public under an Open Government Licence has some information redacted. An un-redacted version can be requested and may be provided under NRW licence.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-10-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:16:26.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17964\tNRW_DS114458\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.645Z\t440\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria alveolata Reefs in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2010\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343538\tThe data and report are archived as Project No 199, Media No 1228. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW-Maes y Fynnon\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata contact: Master Data Reference Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary, tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats). Honeycomb worms build tubes from sand and shell fragments. They are found on the lower part of rocky seashores but they also need a sand supply for tube-building. They are found on the lower part of rocky seashores but they also need a sand supply for tube-building.  Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary ones. Of these, Estuaries and their constituent mudflats, sandflats and reefs, large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds, populations of the shingle-dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus, piddocks in clay and lower shore boulder communities and Reefs, particularly biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata are the main habitats of interest within the intertidal areas of the SAC. The purpose of this data capture was to monitoring the extent of Sabellaria alveolata reefs. Field work was carried out during five days of spring tides, 6th to 10th July 2009. Data quality and confidence considered high as all surveys were completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:19:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17959\tNRW_DS114459\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.627Z\t315\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera marina) Beds in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2010\t© CNC/NRW 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343539\tThe data and report are archived as Project No 199, Media No 1228. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW, Maes y Fynnon\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Seagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas, such as lagoons and channels, which are sheltered from significant wave action. Eelgrass (Zostera marina), is considered to be scarce (present in 16-100 ten km squares) is protected through the EC Habitats Directive and is classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitat. Data is captured to support reporting on conservation status of the Pen Llyn air Sarnau SAC.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:19:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17957\tNRW_DS114460\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.325Z\t979\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Heritage Evidence to Support Strategic Planning for Marine Renewable Energy\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2011. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343630\tThe data is archived as project No.309 and media No.1230\tInternal Custodian: Marine Spatial Planning, Energy and Infrastructure Team (Strategic Planning Group),\tThe Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is committed to working with regulators and developers to support the development of marine renewable energy in locations, and using technologies, that avoid significant adverse environmental impacts. The UK government is committed to producing 15 percent of all energy requirements from renewable sources by 2020. With approximately 1.5 percent of UK energy currently sourced from renewables, significant expansion will be needed to meet this target. As far as marine sources of renewable energy are concerned, UK government has to date relied on deployment of offshore wind to contribute to UK renewable energy targets and indications suggest that commercial scale (10 to 100MW) arrays of wave and tidal technologies are expected to commence largely post 2015 (DECC, 2010). CCW have undertaken to produce and share key synthesised evidence relating to Welsh natural heritage interests, in the form of GIS-based data layers, to be taken into account by strategic planning process for marine energy devices (wave and tidal stream).    The baseline layers highlight the breadth of natural heritage interests that need to be taken into account in strategic planning processes.  The interpreted layers communicate the relative levels of risks to Wales\u0027 marine natural heritage from device deployment.  Evidence layers have been produced for Welsh inshore territorial waters, from Mean High Water Spring baseline out to 12nm. The metadata describes the collation exercise, which was undertaken between Mar 2010 and Feb 2011.  Temporal coverage of each of the baseline datasets is documented elsewhere. The environmental effects of marine renewable energy devices are not well understood and information about the marine environment is often limited, so this work is based on the best available information at the time of the study.  This information is considered a snapshot in time and may be kept for archive puposes only.  Newer data products may be available though the WG Marine Planning Portal.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-06-30-2011-06-30\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:20:32.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17960\tNRW_DS114466\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.664Z\t372\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTy Gwyn Marsh - National Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey Saltmarsh Survey (2000)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2011. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence.  Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343636\tAssociated data is held at; M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Ty_Gwyn_marsh_2000\tInternal Custodian: NR Conservation Officer-Sites Team, NRW. Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tA standard Phase 2 NVC survey was undertaken at Ty Gywn Marsh as part of the Morfa Harlech Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) designation process.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:05:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17968\tNRW_DS114499\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.599Z\t830\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcross Wales Drop-Down Video Monitoring: North Llyn Ground Truthing (2011)\t© CNC/NRW 2011. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale. wn only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343939\tMaintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW Maes y Fynnon as project no. 313 media no. 1236. Videos are maintained in the archive but can be accessed through GIS layers on the M drive.\tInternal custodian: Marine Monitoring Officer, MFSG, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tA drop-down video survey carried out in North Wales. This work was carried out by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and focused on the North Llyn area.  Subsequent analysis of the video taken was undertaken by Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd on behalf of CCW. This work was conducted as part of a ground truthing exercise through which the extent and quality of Modiolus mussel beds could be assessed.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-06-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:39:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17975\tNRW_DS114500\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.639Z\t329\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcross Wales Drop-Down Video Monitoring: Puffin Island Survey (2011)\t© CNC/NRW 2011 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353030\tMaintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW Maes y Fynnon as project no. 313 media no. 1237. Videos are maintained in the archive but can be accessed through GIS layers on the M drive.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of drop-down video surveys of sites around Puffin Island, Anglesey. Work was carried out by former Countryside Counicl for Wales (CCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) within the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC).  Subsequent analysis of the video taken was undertaken by Marine Ecological Soultions Ltd on behalf of CCW. The project was designed to validate existing HABMAP data of the area surrounding Puffin Island.   Methods of data capture: Drop-down video is useful for gathering large volumes of data across wide areas of seabed and often to depths beyond which divers\u0027 observations can be safely made.  Detailed methods and site locations are described in CCW Marine Monitoring Report 24a.  Amendments to these methods include the addition of a HD video camera system manufactured by C-Tecnics in place of the SD camera previously used. A Canon EOS 400D digital SLR was also used in a housing manufactured by Rovtech Systems.  Photographic stills were taken every 11 seconds along the tows to assist with species and habitat validation.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:43:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17963\tNRW_DS114501\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.610Z\t1265\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Marine Benthic Species and Habitat Collation Held in Marine Recorder\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2011 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale. NB. NRW data are merged with snapshots of datasets from across Wales supplied from other data providers (e.g. JNCC, NE, DASSH and Seasearch) for access to these datasets use restrictions may differ and therefore dataset requestors are advised to contact the owner / originator of the specific dataset or data required.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353031\tMarine Recorder is currently available through the legacy CCW VMWare.  A back up is held on the legacy CCW MFSG drive along with copies of the most recent snapshot (H:\\Science\\MFSG\\MarineRecorder)  Copies of the NRW snapshot is also held at JNCC and the NBNDate file is archived at DASSH.  Some paper MNCR forms are held in off site storage  (Box CCW/MNCR/1, Box CCW/MNCR/2. Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Contact / Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW  Internal Data Contact: Marine and Freshwater Evidence Manager, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) currently holds and collates current and former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) marine benthic species, biotope, habitat, and particle size analyses data in Marine Recorder.  A snapshot of the data may be made available through the use of the Marine Wizard and is also available on the NBN Gateway. NRW data are merged with snapshots of datasets from across Wales supplied from other data providers (e.g. JNCC, NE, DASSH and Seasearch) using the suite of tools provided with the program and made available for internal use. The purpose of marine data capture through the use of Marine Recorder is to provide access to all marine benthic species and habitats data at NRW through one program.  From 2024, the data will be migrated to the new Marine Recorder Online tool.  A subset of data that is suitable for public use is published through the NBN Atlas.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:23:47.092Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17974\tNRW_DS114540\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.679Z\t718\tValid\tEditable\tDrop-Down Video of Historical Native Oyster (Ostrea edulis) Habitat in Swansea Bay, South Wales (2010)\t© CNC/NRW 2011. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353430\tData is archived at project no. 317, media nos. 1252 and 1397 (File paths (K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1252 and K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1397) Species data in NRW copy of Marine Recorder (Survey Number: MRCCW31000000003)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team (used to be Aethne Cooke)   Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe native or flat oyster (Ostrea edulis) is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority species, and it is also listed for Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006 as being \u0027of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity in Wales.\u0027   The native oyster has a long history as an important commercial species in Wales and elsewhere in the UK. Significant oyster beds are now restricted to Milford Haven, Swansea Bay/Mumbles and Porthcawl, although at a much reduced scale. The remaining oyster population in Wales is under threat through the spread of the protozoan parasite Bonamia ostreae, continued poor recruitment, and competition from the alien invasive slipper limpet Crepidula fornicata. The situation is exacerbated by the continued operation of a small oyster fishery in Milford Haven and Swansea Bay.   The aim of this project was to gather information on the status of historical native oyster beds off the south Pembrokeshire coast. This information will contribute to our understanding of the status of historical native oyster beds off the south Wales coast and will inform projects to reinstate oyster habitat and stock enhancement. This dataset collates the examination of drop down video of oyster beds. This project used video data was examined to identify broad seabed type and evidence of oysters.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:36:11.418Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17972\tNRW_DS114561\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.683Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecies Names Translation Lists (Vertebrates)\t© Cymdeithas Edward Llwyd 2011 Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353631\tCCW intranet (Ffenest), NBN Gateway and Species Dictionary (Natural History Museum)\t\tThis is a collated list of terrestrial vertebrate species names in English, Welsh and scientific Latin.  Purpose of data capture: To provide terminology lists for the Species Dictionary hosted by the Natural History Museum which feeds into the National Biodiversity Network Gateway and for the use of CCW staff.  Methods of data capture: Most of the translations have been created and/or verified by Cymdeithas Edward Llwyd for CCW the records have been extensively cleaned by the Natural History Museum  Confidence in data: All translations have been carefully checked and approved however with the English and Welsh names, as in any language, there will be multiple derivations and regional variations. Also the scientific terminology changes periodically as work on taxonomy progresses.\tCymdeithas Edward Llwyd\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:12.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17965\tNRW_DS114562\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.562Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecies Names Translation Lists (Moths and Butterflies)\t© Cymdeithas Edward Llwyd 2011 Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353632\tCCW intranet (Ffenest), NBN Gateway and Species Dictionary (Natural History Museum)\t\tThis is a collated list of moth and butterfly species names in English, Welsh and scientific Latin. Purpose of data capture: To provide terminology lists for the Species Dictionary hosted by the Natural History Museum which feeds into the National Biodiversity Network Gateway and for the use of CCW staff. Methods of data capture: Most of the translations have been created and/or verified by Cymdeithas Edward Llwyd for CCW the records have been extensively cleaned by the Natural History Museum Confidence in data: All translations have been carefully checked and approved however with the English and Welsh names, as in any language, there will be multiple derivations and regional variations. Also the scientific terminology changes periodically as work on taxonomy progresses.\tCymdeithas Edward Llwyd\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:13:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17970\tNRW_DS114563\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.671Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecies Names Translation Lists (Plants)\t© CNC/NRW and Cymdeithas Edward Llwyd 2011 Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353633\tCCW intranet (Ffenest), NBN Gateway and Species Dictionary (Natural History Museum)\t\tThis is a collated list of terrestrial plant species names in English, Welsh and scientific Latin.  Purpose of data capture: To provide terminology lists for the Species Dictionary hosted by the Natural History Museum which feeds into the National Biodiversity Network Gateway and for the use of CCW staff.  Methods of data capture: Most of the translations have been created and/or verified by Cymdeithas Edward Llwyd for CCW the records have been extensively cleaned by the Natural History Museum  Confidence in data: All translations have been carefully checked and approved however with the English and Welsh names, as in any language, there will be multiple derivations and regional variations. Also the scientific terminology changes periodically as work on taxonomy progresses.\tCymdeithas Edward Llwyd\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17967\tNRW_DS114564\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.585Z\t824\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMorphodynamic Modelled Outputs for the Mawddach Estuary\t© CNC/NRW and CAMS 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/CAMS and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353634\tData outputs associated with this project are archived as Project No. 318, Media No\u0027s 1253 and 1262 to 1266  (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1253 etc.)\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis report summarises the third stage of a modelling research study undertaken by Bangor University, investigating the hydrodynamic and morphodynamic response of representative Welsh estuaries (which include both past and potential future changes to the estuaries, as well as their present form) to a range of mean and extreme climatic conditions. In particular, coastal flood risk and potential for erosion and accretion has been assessed through the medium of high resolution computational models. The Mawddach estuary lies within the Snowdonia National Park, north Wales, and its landscape and environment represents one of the key natural assets which Wales possesses. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the response of the estuary to future tidal and atmospheric changes. The model has also been used to investigate the response of the estuarine dynamics to potential management strategies such as embankment re-alignment and re-location.\tCentre for Applied Marine Sciences (CAMS)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:20:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17956\tNRW_DS114565\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.256Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecies Names Translation Lists (Dragonflies)\t© Cymdeithas Edward Llwyd 2011 Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353635\tCCW intranet (Ffenest), NBN Gateway and Species Dictionary (Natural History Museum)\t\tThis is a collated list of dragonfly species names in English, Welsh and scientific Latin. Purpose of data capture: To provide terminology lists for the Species Dictionary hosted by the Natural History Museum which feeds into the National Biodiversity Network Gateway and for the use of CCW staff.\tCymdeithas Edward Llwyd\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:13:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17958\tNRW_DS114567\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.553Z\t312\tValid\tEditable\tDrop-Down Video of Historical Native Oyster (Ostrea edulis) Habitat in Pembrokeshire, South Wales (2010)\t© CNC/NRW 2011 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353637\t[A] MFSG Archive Project no. 320 and Media No. 1268 and 1397 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1268 and K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400) [B] Species data held in Marine Recorder survey number: MRCCW31000000002\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe native or flat oyster (Ostrea edulis) is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority species, and it is also listed for Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006 as being \u0027of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity in Wales.\u0027   The native oyster has a long history as an important commercial species in Wales and elsewhere in the UK. Significant oyster beds are now restricted to Milford Haven, Swansea Bay/Mumbles and Porthcawl, although at a much reduced scale. The remaining oyster population in Wales is under threat through the spread of the protozoan parasite Bonamia ostreae, continued poor recruitment, and competition from the alien invasive slipper limpet Crepidula fornicata. The situation is exacerbated by the continued operation of a small oyster fishery in Milford Haven and Swansea Bay.   The aim of this project was to gather information on the status of historical native oyster beds off the south Pembrokeshire coast. This information will contribute to our understanding of the status of historical native oyster beds off the south Wales coast and will inform projects to reinstate oyster habitat and stock enhancement.  This dataset collates the examination of drop down video of oyster beds.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:29:48.971Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17971\tNRW_DS114573\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.650Z\t515\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Algae Sites in Wales: Survey and Inventory of Important and Rare Algae\t© CNC/NRW 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source. Some images remain the IPR of Francis Bunker and should be acknowledged appropriately.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353733\tData is archived as project no. 325, media 1282 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1200to1300\\Media 1282)  Species data is also available through Marine Recorder (survey no. MRCCW31200000002)   The herbarium is stored at the National Museum of Wales, Cardiff.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services\tSeveral sites around Wales have been highlighted as being of interest for their marine seaweeds, either as species rich sites or for supporting rare, scarce or Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species. Many of these sites were designated in 2007 as provisional Important Plant Areas for Algae and all the sites are situated within marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) or Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSIs).  This study was designed to provide an inventory of species at selected sites, which were highlighted as the result of previous surveys, and also to recommend methodologies for future monitoring.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:37:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17961\tNRW_DS114587\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.563Z\t738\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBlack grouse (Tetrao tetrix) National Surveys (Welsh data)\t© CNC/NRW and RSPB Cymru, 2011. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353837\tHeld by RSPB Cymru and available by the NBN Gateway\tInternal Custodian: Sian Whitehead, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ornithologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team NRW\tThe male black grouse (Tetrao tetrix) population in Britain was surveyed in 1995/96 and 2005, in order to estimate population size.   The surveys were based on counts of males displaying in the spring. In Wales and Staffordshire, the entire known population was surveyed in 1995. In northern England and Scotland in 1995/96 and 2005, and in Wales in 2005, a stratified random sample of 5 km squares from the breeding range was surveyed. As such the dataset covers around 10% of the UK population overall and does NOT contain all black grouse leks in Britain in the survey years; its aim was to produce a reliable population estimate. Please note that, apart from the 1995 Welsh data, the dataset does NOT contain all leks of black grouse, because only a random subsample of the breeding range was surveyed.  Data have been thoroughly checked for geographical and other errors.\tRoyal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17969\tNRW_DS114591\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.634Z\t529\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFarmland Bird Survey North East Wales (2008)\t© CNC/NRW and RSPB Cymru, 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW / RSPB and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353931\tHeld by RSPB Cymru and available throught the NBN Gateway\tInternal Custodian: Sian Whitehead, Terrestrial and Freshwater Ornithologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tA systematic regional survey of farmland birds within and around the Bettisfield and Dee Estuary (Welsh side) areas undertaken in 2008. The survey aimed to establish whether corn bunting were still breeding in Wales and to gather information on farmland bird populations in this area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)\t2011-06-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:10.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17954\tNRW_DS114594\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.709Z\t626\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRing ouzel (Turdus torquata) national sample survey in the UK in 1999\t© CNC/NRW and RSPB Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353934\tHeld by RSPB Cymru and available on the NBN Gateway\tInternal Contact: Sian Whitehead, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ornithologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis is the first co-ordinated census of breeding ring ouzel Turdus torquata across the UK. A random subsample of the UK breeding range was surveyed to arrive at a population estimate, and as such the dataset does NOT contain all breeding locations of ring ouzel in the UK in 1999. Of the tetrads surveyed that were known to be occupied during the 1988-91 breeding bird atlas, 39-43% were unoccupied in 1999. The survey highlights a continuing contraction in range. The survey was run as part of the Statutory Conservation Agencies and RSPB Annual Breeding Birds Scheme (SCARABBS) and was funded jointly by the Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB), the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), English Nature (now Natural England) and Scottish Natural Heritage (SNH). The Ring Ouzel Study Group took part in the survey, and many landowners and site managers allowed access to their land.  The purpose of this data capture was to set a baseline for the future monitoring of breeding ring ouzels and to provide a systematically based estimate of the population size.\tRoyal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-08-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17955\tNRW_DS114595\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.809Z\t422\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTwite (Carduelis flavirostris) Breeding Season Survey, Wales 2008\t© CNC/NRW and RSPB Cymru 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW / RSPB and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353935\tHeld by RSPB Cymru and the NBN Gateway\tInternal Contacts: Data Services Team, NRW  Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ornithologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThis survey updated a survey undertaken in 2002 by Snowdonia National Park and the National Trust. In Wales, twite are restricted primarily to northern Snowdonia around the valley of Nant Ffrancon and Llyn Ogwen, bounded by the Carneddau to the north and east, by Elidir Fawr and Y Garn to the west and by the Glyder range to the south, where breeding has been recorded since 1944, with post breeding flocks of over 150 birds in the recent past (1999 - 2000). This dataset also contains records other species in addition to twite which were recorded during the survey. This survey updated a survey undertaken in 2002 by Snowdonia National Park and the National Trust to assess current status and provide reliable indication of trend in range and abundance between the reference years (2002 - 2008).\tRoyal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-06-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17952\tNRW_DS114596\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.576Z\t333\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTwite (Carduelis flavirostris) national sample survey in the UK in 1999 (Welsh data)\t© CNC/NRW and RSPB Cymru 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ RSPB and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353936\tHeld by RSPB Cymru and the NBN Gateway\tInternal Contacts:  Data Services Team, NRW Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ornithologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThis dataset contains results from the first comprehensive survey of breeding twite in the UK. The aim of this survey was to provide a baseline population estimate of breeding twite in the UK. A random subsample of the UK breeding range was surveyed to arrive at a population estimate, and as such the dataset does NOT contain all breeding locations of twite in the UK in 1999. There were an estimated 10 000 pairs of breeding twite in the UK in 1999, with the range lying between 6000 and 15 000 pairs. The majority, 9948 (95 percent confidence intervals 6293-14 586) pairs, was recorded in Scotland and England. Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) and English Nature (now Natural England) helped fund this survey as part of the Statutory Conservation Agencies/RSPB Annual Breeding Birds Scheme (SCARABBS).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)\t2008-10-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17953\tNRW_DS114631\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.632Z\t325\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommon Standards Macrophyte Surveys: Welsh Standing Waters 2007 - 2009\t© CCGC/NRW and Environment Agency 2010. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW / Environment Agency and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363331\tData is archied as project no. 329, media no. 1288\tInternal custodian: Heather Garrett, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tData collected during the survey of 41 standing water sites in Wales. With the exception of Llyn Bodlyn, all the sites are designated Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), with the majority also being within Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).  These surveys were undertaken as part of a wider project commissioned by the Environment Agency to meet obligations under the Water Framework Directive and provide a measure of site condition assessment for SSSI\u0027s.  The work was commissioned by the Environment Agency and funded in part by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW).  Work was conducted by ENSIS- ECRC. For further information please see \u0027Common Standards Macrophyte Surveys: Welsh Standing Waters 2007 - 2009. Data Summary Report to CCW as part of: Lake Macrophyte \u0026amp; Habitat Surveys for the Water Framework Directive, 2007-10. Contract No: 20457 The Environment Agency\u0027  Work carried out over the course of 3 summers, during 2006 - 2009. Palaeoecological and surface sediment analysis of  these sites incorporating chemical and bathymetric surveys was also conducted and this information is available in an associated report.  Please see \u0027Goldsmith, B., Burgess, A., Bennion, H., Turner, S.D., Appleby, P.G. \u0026amp; Piliposian, G.T. (2010). Palaeoecological and surface.\tEnvironment Agency (EA)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17951\tNRW_DS114632\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.445Z\t782\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBeach Sediment Particle Size for 10 Welsh Beaches (Collected 2010/2011)\t© CNC/NRW and EAW, Welsh Government, Crown Estates, BMAPA 2010 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ (e.g.) Forestry Commission and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363332\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 319, Media No. 1267 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1267) HQ library and electronic version held in DMS.\tInternal Custodian: Emmer Litt, Specialist Advisor - Marine and Coastal Physical Processes, All Wales Advice Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tBeaches in Wales are important for several reasons, including as a major \u0027attractor\u0027 of relevance to the tourist industry and local, regional and national economies. The Welsh Coast is vitally important to the tourism industry in Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to provide details of the types and properties of sediment on Welsh beaches to inform the Beach Nourishment in Wales Phase 2 project. 10 beaches were chosen during the Beach Nourishment in Wales Pilot Study project which included both sand and mixed sand/shingle systems located around the coasts of North, West and South Wales and which reflect varying relative importance of touristic / recreational, flood and erosion risk management and nature conservation interests.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:37:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17950\tNRW_DS114672\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.354Z\t816\tUnassessed\tEditable\tApplication of Phase 1 Intertidal Survey Techniques to Monitoring\t© CNC/NRW 2001. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363732\tArchived as project 315, media 1239. The report associated with this data is available in the Reports Archive - NRW\u0027s DMS.\tInternal Custodian : Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tThis dataset is part of the 1999 monitoring trials that became the basis for Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) monitoring in Wales; Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW\u0027s) Marine Monitoring Report No1. \u0027The establishment of a programme of surveillance and monitoring for judging the condition of the features of Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau\". Relating to section 3.4.2  This was important for the notification of protected sites and also for planning conservation management. The purpose of this data capture was to support the designation and conservation planning of protected sites.  The purpose of condition monitoring on the site is essential to provide information which enables judgements to be made about whether the favourable condition of the features of interest for which this site was designated is is being achieved and so unable judgements to be made about the appropriateness of site management. This programme of survey has been stimulated by the EC habitats directive SAC designation requirements because the implementation of the directive requires that appropriate adjacent intertidal land must be notified as a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) before it can be included as part of an SAC. Historically intertidal marine SSSIs have been notified on an ad hoc basis in Wales. However it was recognised a large scale notification of SSSIs linked to the habitats directive would need to be part of a series of sites covered a range of intertidal communities in Wales. In addition to this it was recognised that map data for the intertidal within the SACs would be invaluable for site management including sensitivity mapping and as a basis from which to develop future monitoring.  This dataset forms part of the Intertidal SAC monitoring program.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:23:15.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17948\tNRW_DS114673\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.346Z\t282\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Shad Egg Survey on the River Wye (2011)\t© CNC/NRW 2011. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363733\t[A]  Data is archived on NRW Marine Archive server as project 330, media 1289. [B]  Species data is available in Recorder 6: Shad Egg Survey River Wye 2011 - CCW - Report no. 11/8/4  CCW0008000000008\tInternal Custodian: Rivers Ecologist, MFEG, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThe River Wye rises on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains and flows in a generally south-easterly direction to enter the Severn Estuary at Chepstow.  The river is designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the Habitats Directive for several features, including salmon, lampreys and shads.  Twaite (Alosa fallax) and allis (Alosa alosa) shad. In Britain, significant populations of twaite shad are known to spawn in the Rivers Severn, Wye, Usk and Tywi.  However, it is not certain whether a breeding population of allis shad still exists. The population of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and water quality issues. The purpose of this data capture was to make an assessment of Conservation Status by summarising previous data held at the site, conducting a baseline survey and compare these with results from previous datasets.  Along the length of the welsh portion of the River Wye from Builth Wells to Monmouth. The survey was conducted in May, June and July of 2011.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:38:06.806Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17949\tNRW_DS114674\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.368Z\t20\tValid\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. The redacted data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363734\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived.   Data is archived as project 282, media 1098 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1001to1100)\tInternal Custodian: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) was published back in 1994, and was the UK Government’s response to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD), which the UK signed up to in 1992 in Rio de Janeiro.  Similarly under Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act, 2006 it is required that the a \u0027list of the living organisms and types of habitat which in the Assembly\u0027s opinion are of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\u0027.   Priority marine habitats in Wales are those identified as BAP habitats, Section 42 habitats or OSPAR habitats.  These habitats include: blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.  These datasets have been superseded.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-20T13:48:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17947\tNRW_DS114688\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.215Z\t706\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBarbastelle (Barbastella barbastellus) Bat Distribution Model (2011)\t© CNC/NRW and Various Contractors/Volunteers, 2015. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363838\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Species\\all_wales\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Bats\\Matt_Zeale_Barbastelle_Model\tInternal Custodian: Jean Matthews or Liz Halliwell - Mammal Ecologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group External Custodian: Bristol University / Matt Zeale  Ownership could be split between Bristol Uni, Matt Zeale and NRW at the moment it says various  Metadata point of Contact: GIS and Spatial Team.\tThe purpose of this data capture was to use a multidisciplinary approach to fill current knowledge gaps in the ecology and conservation requirements of a rare bat, the barbastelle (Barbastella barbastellus).  Spatial modelling was used to evaluate the environmental parameters that dictate where the species is able to persist in the UK and to identify areas of high conservation value. The purpose of this data capture for Countryside Council for Wales was to assess and predict the distribution of the species around Wales. The models were created and used my Matthew Zeale, a PhD student at the time. The PhD findings from the models indicated that B. barbastellus is highly dependent on large areas of native mature woodland and is limited in its distribution predominantly by summer climate, although a number of other environmental factors also appear to be important. The data collation and survey undertaken by experienced bat biologist. The results were later submitted as part of a PhD Thesis.\tVarious-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T15:36:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17946\tNRW_DS114691\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.170Z\t598\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMerthyr Mawr Warren: Potential for Dune Reactivation\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright, 2011. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS. Those seeking commercial re-use for OS derived data require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363931\tData is archived as project no. 331, media 1290\tMarine and Coastal Physical Scientist, NRW.\tThis dataset is a compilation of work carried out to assess the geomorphological feasibility of sand dune reactivation at Merthyr Mawr Warren, based on a review of background environmental data, air photographs LiDAR data, a site visit and laboratory analysis of a limited number of sediment samples.   The Merthyr Mawr dunefield is a sediment-starved system which is undergoing re-distribution of sandy sediment from the north-western part to the south-eastern part of the system. Photographic evidence indicates that bare sand and active dunes covered a large part of the area between the late 19th century and the late 1960’s, after which time the dunes became progressively stabilized by vegetation. The period of high dune mobility corresponded with a period of coastal erosion. No long-term wind or wave data are available for the area, but it is hypothesized that coastal erosion, frontal dune blowout development and inland dune mobility were encouraged by high average wind and wave energy conditions, and perhaps a higher frequency of severe storms. Conversely, the period of inland dune stability between the 1970’s and the present day was associated with beach accretion and foredune progradation. It is hypothesized that these changes were driven by lower average wind / wave energy conditions and lower frequency of severe storms.   Bare sand and active parabolic dunes have only been maintained in areas where there is high visitor pressure or other anthropogenic disturbance. The purpose of this data capture was to help inform decisions that may result in potential re-mobilisation work, including impact on existing habitats and stakeholder interests.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:38:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17996\tNRW_DS114713\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.021Z\t398\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessing the Vulnerability of Marine Habitats in Wales to the Impacts of Climate Changes (2011)\t© CNC/NRW, 2011. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373133\tData is archived as project 328, media 1287.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Ecologist, MFEG, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tClimate change is recognised as a major environmental issue and the process of global change and climate will impact upon many aspects of everyday life, presenting a serious threat to the natural environment. In response to the scientific and political consensus of a need to tackle climate change the UK government bought into law the climate change act 2008. This requires a Strategic Climate Change Risk Assessment (SCCRA) to be carried out every five years, the first of which will need to be completed by November 2011.   This project was carried out to develop a methodology to assess the vulnerability of Welsh marine habitats to the impact of climate change and to use this method to undertake an assessment of welsh marine habitats. This assessment helped enable the identification of those habitats most at risk from climate change and it is envisaged that this information will be used to inform management plans and government policy and decision making.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:39:32.573Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18002\tNRW_DS114722\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.052Z\t935\tValid\tEditable\tSeabirds at Sea Evidence Base\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373232\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Seabird coverage 3KM WGS84.lyr,  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Seabird WWT ESAS merged flying 3KM WGS84.lyr,  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Seabird WWT ESAS merged sitting 3KM WGS84.lyr,   The source data used to create the grid cell maps is also stored at: X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Seabird WWT SAS OBS merged WGS84.lyr   however this is not covered by this metadata entry as restrictions may apply and only available internally.    Archived data in original MapInfo format is held within the Marine archive as project 333, media no. 1292\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy Marine Ornithologist, MFEG, NRW Metadata contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tGIS layers showing the abundance and distribution of seabirds in welsh waters.  The datasets consists of the raw data showing observations of all seabirds and derived grids showing the density of flying and sitting species on a 3km grid scale and the survey coverage. The purpose of this data capture was to collate data from ESAS and WWT Consulting databases to produce combined database of birds recorded from surveys of Welsh waters.  Other sources identified were also consulted to produce the most complete representation of  the observed distribution of seabirds.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-07-01\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/882/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:SeabirdsAtSea\t2025-05-07T09:03:33.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18001\tNRW_DS114723\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.129Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Blue Mussel Beds (Mytilus edulis) (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW 2011 and various third party recorders NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373233\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC OSPAR Blue Mussel Beds Point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC OSPAR Blue Mussel Beds Polygon.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at H:\\Science\\MSFG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Additional Word documents are available as project 282, media 1098.  GIS layers are both point and polygon.\tInternal Custodian:  Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Data Services Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Blue Mussel (Mytilus edulis) beds on sediments only. Blue mussel (Mytilus edulis) beds are small, blue mussels which can form extensive beds, with living and dead mussels, sand and mud bound together by the mussel’s sticky byssus threads. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:16:42.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17997\tNRW_DS114724\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.072Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Carbonate Reefs (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2011 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373234\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Carbonate Reefs.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working. Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tCustodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Spatial Data and GIS Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.  The data is related to carbonate reefs only. The reef structure that was identified is known as \u0027Holden\u0027s Reef\u0027 – it is composed of a particular type of calcium carbonate formed by methane gas leaking from the seabed. This, and the associated reefs are the first known example of this habitat in UK inshore waters.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17998\tNRW_DS114725\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.070Z\t659\tValid\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Coastal Saltmarsh\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2011 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373235\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Seagrass Beds.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.    The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.     The data is related to coastal saltmarsh only. Coastal saltmarshes in the UK comprise the upper, vegetated portions of intertidal mudflats, lying approximately between mean high water neap tides and mean high water spring tides. Saltmarshes are usually restricted to comparatively sheltered locations in five main physiographic situations: in estuaries, in saline lagoons, behind barrier islands, at the heads of sea lochs, and on beach plains. The development of saltmarsh vegetation is dependent on the presence of intertidal mudflats.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:40:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17994\tNRW_DS114727\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.023Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Estuarine Rock (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373237\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC Estuarine Rock Point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC Estuarine Rock Polygon.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Data Services Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to estuarine rock only. Estuaries are usually soft, muddy places, so rock and stable boulders in estuaries are rare, and occur mostly in drowned river valleys and fjords.  They form a small proportion of estuarine seascapes but are important because they contribute a lot to the richness of life within estuaries.  The rich and sheltered waters of estuaries provide nursery grounds for fish, and the rocky areas are an important part of these. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17995\tNRW_DS114728\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.017Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Fragile Sponge and Anthozoan (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2011 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373238\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Fragile Sponges Anthozoan.lyr    x:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Fragile Sponges Biotopes.lyr    Data is archived as project 282, media 1098 Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.   Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (X drive).  .\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to fragile sponge and anthozoan only. Anthozoans are a group of soft animals with feathery tentacles, which includes soft corals, sea-fans, cup corals and anemones.  Members of this group can be found together with branching sponges on steeply sloping bedrock or large boulders in depths from about 50m to just below low tide. These sponge and anthozoan communities require sufficient water movement to bring a ready supply of food but not too much, as they are vulnerable to breakage.  As a result, they are found on shores which are exposed to moderate or strong waves and possibly quite strong tidal currents, but only in areas of those shores where there is some local shelter from the full force of the waves and tides.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:30.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17993\tNRW_DS114729\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.862Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Honeycomb Worm (Sabellaria) Reefs (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2011 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373239\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Sabellaria Alveolata Reef.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive). Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian:  Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Honeycomb worm (Sabellaria) reefs only. Sabellaria spinulosa reefs comprise of dense subtidal aggregations of this small, tube-building polychaete worm. Sabellaria spinulosa can act to stabilise cobble, pebble and gravel habitats, providing a consolidated habitat for epibenthic species. They are solid (albeit fragile), massive structures at least several centimetres thick, raised above the surrounding seabed, and persisting for many years. As such, they provide a biogenic habitat that allows many other associated species to become established. The S. spinulosa reef habitats of greatest nature conservation significance are those which occur on predominantly sediment or mixed sediment areas. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17992\tNRW_DS114730\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.877Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Intertidal Mudflats (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2004 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373330\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Intertidal Mudflats.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian:  Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to intertidal mudflats only. Mudflats are sedimentary intertidal habitats created by deposition in low energy coastal environments, particularly estuaries and other sheltered areas. Their sediment consists mostly of silts and clays with a high organic content. Towards the mouths of estuaries where salinity and wave energy are higher the proportion of sand increases. Mudflats are intimately linked by physical processes to, and may be dependent on, other coastal habitats such as soft cliffs and saltmarshes. They commonly appear in the natural sequence of habitats between subtidal channels and vegetated saltmarshes. In large estuaries they may be several kilometres wide and commonly form the largest part of the intertidal area of estuaries. However, in many places they have been much reduced by land claim. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18003\tNRW_DS114731\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.220Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Intertidal Underboulder (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2008 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373331\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Intertidal Underboulder Points.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Intertidal Underboulder Polygons.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.    The data is related to Intertidal underboulder communities only. Intertidal underboulder communities are unique environments from the rest of the shoreline due to residing in damp and shaded areas which provide refuge on an otherwise hostile beach. These environments are characterised by sea mats, sponges, sea squirts, barnacles, coat-of-mail shells, tube worms and tufts of pink coralline seaweed encrust the undersurfaces.  Sea slugs, such as the sea-lemon, feed on encrusting sponges and the European cowrie eats sea squirts.  Brittlestars, porcelain crabs (which are actually lobsters) and squat lobsters also cling to the undersides of boulders. The undersurfaces are an important refuge for the eggs of fish, dog whelks and sea slugs. Crabs and fish hide amongst the boulders, and they may be a particularly important shelter for young edible lobsters.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:16:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17987\tNRW_DS114732\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.648Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Maerl (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2011 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373332\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Maerl Beds.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to maerl only. Maerl is a collective term for several species of red seaweed, with hard, chalky skeletons. It is rock hard and, unlike other seaweeds, it grows as unattached rounded nodules or short, branched shapes on the seabed. Maerl beds are an important habitat for many different types of marine life, which live amongst or are attached to the surface of Maerl, or burrow in the coarse gravel of dead maerl beneath the top living layer. Maerl beds can be of importance to sustainable fisheries, providing nursery grounds for commercial species of fish and shellfish. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17990\tNRW_DS114733\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.750Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Horse Mussel (Modiolus modiolus) Beds (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2011 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373333\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Horse Mussel Beds.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to horse mussel Modiolus modiolus only and was originally collated to assist in the identification of Highly Protected Marine Conservation Zones (HPMCZ\u0027s) in Wales, however it was recognized that the data would have wide uses for conservation in Wales and therefore information about the habitats was as encompassing as possible. The horse mussel Modiolus modiolus forms dense beds at depths of 5-70 m in fully saline, often moderately tide-swept areas off northern and western parts of the British Isles. M. modiolus can occur as relatively small, dense beds of epifaunal mussels carpeting steep rocky surfaces, but is more frequently recessed at least partly into mixed or muddy sediments in a variety of tidal regimes. Extensive expanses of seabed are covered in scattered clumps of semi-recessed M. modiolus on muddy gravels.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18004\tNRW_DS114734\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.352Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Mud Habitats in Deep Water (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2011 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373334\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Mud in Deep Water WGS84.lyr      Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tData Custodian:  Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Mud habitats only. Mud habitats in deep water are fairly stable environments typically found below 20 meters where currents are slow and they are not affected by waves. This can be found offshore and in sheltered inshore environments such as sea lochs.  Most of the animals that live here burrow below the surface.  The animal communities vary according to the levels of silt, clay, sand and nutrients found in the mud. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:16:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17989\tNRW_DS114735\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.694Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Musculus discors Habitats (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2011 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373335\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Musculus Discors Beds.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Musculus discors habitats only. This biotope typically occurs on the upper faces of moderately exposed, moderately tide-swept bedrock, boulders and cobbles in slightly silty conditions. The mussel Musculus discors occurs in dense mats and occasionally completely coats all available surfaces.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18000\tNRW_DS114736\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.145Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Oyster Beds (Ostrea edulis) (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 1998 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373336\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\OSPAR Oyster Beds Point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\OSPAR Oyster Beds Polygon.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian:  Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Oyster (Ostrea edulis) beds only. Native Oyster (Ostrea edulis) beds occur in shallow water (usually less than about 10 m deep) on fine, muddy sand, native oysters can be found in large numbers.  Where this happens, the oysters themselves and the dead shells amongst them form extensive beds, which become home to many other marine creatures, including crabs, worms, sea snails, sponges, sea urchins, and seaweed.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1998-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18005\tNRW_DS114737\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.363Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Peat and Clay (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2011 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373337\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Peat and Clay Points.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Peat and Clay Polygons.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Peat and Clay only. Seabeds formed of exposed peat or clay, or in some cases both, are uncommon.  Where they do occur, they have been found between the tides as well as fully underwater.  They can be buried by sand or other sediments and then exposed again on a regular basis. These unique and fragile habitats are irreplaceable, as they were formed millions of years ago from ancient lakebeds and forested peatland.  They are threatened by coastal infrastructure development, cable laying, dredging and other activities that disturb the seabed, mussel fisheries and the collection of the piddocks for bait.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:17:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17999\tNRW_DS114738\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.128Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Saline Lagoons (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2001. © CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2001. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS. NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. The source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders must be acknowledged and not used it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373338\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Saline Lagoons.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Saline Lagoons 2009.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Saline Lagoons only. Lagoons in the UK are essentially bodies, natural or artificial, of salinewater partially separated from the adjacent sea. They retain a proportion of their seawater at low tide and may develop as brackish, full saline or hyper-saline water bodies.  Lagoons can contain a variety of substrata, often soft sediments which in turn may support tasselweeds and stoneworts aswell as filamentous green and brown algae. In addition lagoons contain invertebrates rarely found elsewhere. They also provide important habitat for waterfowl, marshland birdsand seabirds. The flora and invertebrate fauna present can be divided into three main components: those that are essentially freshwater in origin, those that are marine/brackish species and those that are more specialist lagoonal species. The presence of certain indigenous and specialist plants and animals make this habitat important to the UK\u0027s overall biodiversity. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:46.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17986\tNRW_DS114739\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.596Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Seagrass Beds (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2009 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373339\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Seagrass Beds.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to seagrass beds only. Seagrasses (also known, for their long thin leaves, as eel grass) are grass-like flowering plants with dark green, long, narrow, ribbon-shaped leaves. They are one of the very few groups of flowering plants that live in the sea. Common eel grass is the only species that occurs below low water mark, and it can form dense underwater lawns.  It grows mainly on sand, but also fine gravel, typically down to a depth of 4 m. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:48.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17991\tNRW_DS114740\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.876Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Sheltered Muddy Gravel (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2004 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373430\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Sheltered Muddy Gravels.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working. Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Sheltered Muddy Gravel only. Muddy gravels occur mainly in estuaries, drowned river valleys and sea lochs, in areas protected from wave action and strong tidal streams. They can be found both on the shore and in the shallows.  Which plants and animals occupy the gravels depends on the composition of the seabed and the influence of freshwater. Where the seawater is undiluted, muddy gravel seascapes tend to be dominated by worms, such as delicate peacock worms, with their fan of feathery tentacles, which is used for trapping food from the passing seawater.  Burrowing anemones are found in muddy gravels, as are burrowing bivalves (with their paired, hinged shells), including ridged carpet shells and blunt gapers. Coarse gravel and stones on surface of the muddy gravel provide a hard substrate for seaweeds to attach to.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-09-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:32.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17988\tNRW_DS114741\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.604Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Subtidal Mixed Muddy Sediments (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2011 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373431\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Subtidal Mixed Muddy Sediment WGS84.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to subtidal mixed sediments only. Subtidal mixed seabeds can have a range of different types of sediment from muddy, gravely sands to mosaics of cobbles and pebbles in or on a sand, gravel or mud seabed. Mixed areas also include seabeds where waves or ribbons of sand form on the surface of a gravel bed.  They may support a wide range of animals, both on and in the sediment.  Animals found here include worms, bivalves (with their paired, hinged shells), starfish and urchins, anemones, sea firs and sea mats.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17981\tNRW_DS114742\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.162Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Subtidal Sand and Gravels (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW 2011 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373432\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\Marine Bap Habitats Subtidal Sands and Gravels BGS.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Subtidal sand and gravels only. Sand and gravel seabeds are widespread.  Those found to the west of the UK include a high proportion of shell fragments, whereas those from the North Sea are mainly formed from rock.  They occur in a range of environmental conditions, from wave-sheltered, enclosed bays and estuaries to highly exposed open coasts. The mix of sand or gravel, and any sand waves or ripples present on the surface of the seabed, depend on factors such as the strength of the waves and tides. The communities of animals that live in this habitat are also determined by the environmental conditions. There tend to be fewer animals where waves and tides are strong, and the communities there will be characterised by rapid burrowers such as bristleworms, tube worms, bivalve shells and shrimp-like creatures, and perhaps by crabs, hermit crabs and sea snails on the surface of the seabed.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17979\tNRW_DS114743\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.060Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Tideswept Channels (Archived 2012)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2011 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373433\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Tidal Swept Channels.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working. Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Tide-swept channels only. Tide-swept channels occur where the constricted coastline acts as a funnel.  They are found at the entrances to fjords, lochs and lagoons, between individual islands, and between islands and the mainland.  The plentiful supply of food brought in on each tide supports rich and varied communities of marine life. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:14:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17980\tNRW_DS114744\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.159Z\t390\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Marine Species Sightings and Miscellaneous Data (Extracted from Marine Recorder)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373434\t[A]  Data is held in Natural Resources Wales copy of Marine Recorder.  Data from the old Marine Recorder (SurveyID MRCCW30000000080, MRCCW3000000005E) is available on the NBN Atlas.  New datasets stored in Marine Recorder Online (MRO) (SurveyID: ba528f7b-3b7f-4bfe-837a-3961dc0b0be6, 8f9ccf45-384e-48ce-9d1a-cb2f62875e4a) is due to be published.\tPaul Brazier Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting.  NRW.\tThe dataset was set up to collate ad-hoc records of species around Wales as seen by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) staff or provided specifically to NRW for its business use.  The purpose of this collation was to record incidental records. These tend to be species of special interest, such as, rare or scarce species or species not considered native in Welsh waters.    Data may include incidental records collected while conducting specific scientific research that are outside of the remit of the research or methodology used. This dataset is a work in progress and is continually updated.  Sightings date from 2007 onwards however older records may be added from files and notes as required. The majority of the recorded by experienced staff or are verified with known experts and staff through the use of photographs, video or specimens.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-11-14T11:45:13.658Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17985\tNRW_DS114753\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.243Z\t1447\tValid\tEditable\tMonitoring of a Maerl Bed in the Milford Haven Waterway, Pembrokeshire 2010\t© CNC/NRW 2012 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373533\tData is archived as project 337, media 1303.   Voucher specimens are to be held by the National Museums of Wales, Cardiff.\tMarine Biodiversity Ecologist, NRW. Team Leader - Marine Monitoring, NRW.\tMaerl is a collective term for several species of red seaweed, with hard, chalky skeletons. It is rock hard and, unlike other seaweeds, it grows as unattached rounded nodules or short, branched shapes on the seabed.  Like all seaweeds, maerl needs sunlight to grow, and it only occurs to a depth of about 20 m. Maerl can form large beds, when the conditions are right – a fast tidal flow or sufficient wave action to remove fine sediments, but not strong enough to break the brittle maerl branches.  Within these beds, layers of dead maerl build up with a thin layer of pink, living maerl on the top. Maerl beds are classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitat, listed under the Annex 1 of the Habitats Directive, is on the list of OSPAR\u0027s List of Threatened and/or Declining Species and Habitats (Region 3 - Celtic Sea), and the habitat supports a number of UKBAP priority species, and species of principal important for the purpose of conservation of biodiversity under the Natural Environment and Rural Communities Act 2006. This data contains the results of a monitoring exercise undertaken to assess the status of the maerl bed in the Haven for the reporting purposes, and also to compare with baseline data gathered in 2005 to identify any trends. The information contributes to UK BAP monitoring and reporting obligations and to the management of the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC). This dataset forms part of repeated monitoring of 6 sites within the maerl bed in the Milford Haven waterway. The initial surveys were carried out in 2005.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-12-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:24:00.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17983\tNRW_DS114765\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.152Z\t386\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCockle and Mussel Stock (Bird Food) Surveys in the Burry Inlet 2011\t© CNC/NRW  2011. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373635\tData is archived as project 341,media 1312\tInternal Custodians:  Maritime Ornithologist, NRW  and   Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPAs) are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive), and for regularly occurring migratory species. The Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) supports nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders. It also supports an important cockle fishery. The removal of cockles and the associated disturbance caused by fishing them needs to be balanced with bird food requirements. The aim of the project is to assess the food requirements of wading birds that over-winter in the Burry Inlet Special Protection Areas (SPAs). Data Collection is repeated annually. The purpose of this data capture was to provide data on stock abundance and distribution across the whole of the Burry Inlet. The data collected and its results will primarily be used for site monitoring and also for casework. In the future this data may be combined with data gathered on bird foraging behaviour, together with site specific data on bird numbers and used in an individual behaviour based model to predict the food requirements of a bird population.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17982\tNRW_DS114766\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.163Z\t418\tValid\tEditable\tSurveys of Potential Oystercatcher Prey, Pembrey Sands (2011)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373636\tData is archived as project 342 and media 1313.\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy, Maritime Ornithologist, NRW. Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata contact:  Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) supports nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders. The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), are studying the food requirements of oystercatcher and knot, to develop monitoring targets and to assess the implications of shellfish fisheries on the bird populations.  This data was captured to provide data on abundance and distribution of potential oystercatcher prey on Pembrey Sands. The data collected and its results will primarily be used for site monitoring and also for casework. As part of that study, surveys of the cockle and mussel stocks in the Burry Inlet have been carried out every autumn since 2004, using a standard methodology.  Significant populations of oystercatcher also feed in the Three Rivers and along Pembrey Sands and it is known that they are part of the same population that feed in the Burry Inlet SPA.    Data from the British Trust for Ornithology (BTO) on oystercatcher distribution along the Pembrey coast showed that they concentrate in the central section of the Sands and confirmed that they are feeding at low tide.  Sediment fauna that are found along the Pembrey coast include a variety of shellfish (but not cockles and mussels) and a number of large bodied polychaete worms that may be potential prey for oystercatcher.  A survey of potential oystercatcher prey along the Pembrey Sands was carried out in late September 2009.  It showed high densities of the bivalves Donax vittatus and Angulus vittatus and the polychaetes Lanice conchilega and Owenia fusiformis, distributed across the lower and middle shore.  A repeat survey was commissioned by CCW in autumn 2011. In the future this data may be combined with data gathered on bird foraging behaviour, together with site specific data on bird numbers and used in an individual behaviour based model to predict the food requirements of a bird population.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:47:15.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17984\tNRW_DS114771\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.169Z\t549\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcross Wales Marine Non-Native Species Survey around Welsh Marinas\t© CNC/NRW and University of Wales, Bangor. 2011. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ University of Wales, Bangor and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373731\t[A]  Data is archived as project 321 and media 1269.   [B]  Species data is stored in Marine Recorder 2011 CCW/SOS Across Wales Non-Native Species Survey MRCCW31300000002.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Ecologist,  NRW. External Custodian:  Stuart Jenkins, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe dataset is part of an Ongoing rapid assessments for presence/absence of marine Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) in Welsh marinas. A sub-section of probable invasive marine species was determined using a mixture of Welsh high-priority species and species previously found by the Marine Aliens 2 Group. The purpose of this data capture was to look at the spread of invasive non-natives in Wales and provide early warnings for new introductions with a possible view to employing eradication techniques.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-07-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:25:15.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17977\tNRW_DS114775\t2024-05-28T12:37:06.807Z\t686\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Marine Education Framework in Wales\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373735\t[A] Data is archived as project 344, media 1320 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1301 to 1400) [B] Reports and documents are also available in Ffynnon.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsty Lindenbaum, Senior Specialist Advisor Marine, Sustainable Places Land \u0026amp; Sea  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis project is the first step in the development of a Marine Education Framework in Wales to provide a strategic approach for integrating marine experiences, both inside and outside the classroom, with the Welsh curriculum and subject specifications. The purpose of this data capture was to identify links between Welsh educational policies, subject specifications and curricula and the marine environment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:04:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17978\tNRW_DS114776\t2024-05-28T12:37:06.830Z\t414\tUnassessed\tEditable\tVolunteer Resource Packs for Marine Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Species and Habitats\t© CCGC/CCW 2010  There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and CCW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373736\tData is archived as project 346, media 1322.   The report is available in Ffynnon. Contract Science Report No 987.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Biodiversity Ecologist, NRW. (Footage taken by Damon-stanwell Smith)  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tA project designed to develop a series of \"volunteer packs\" to provide guidance for volunteers to take measurements on the \u0027extent\u0027 and \u0027condition\u0027 of 8 Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitats and species as follows; Subtidal seagrass beds;  Mat mussel beds; Crayfish; crawfish or spiny Lobster; Fragile sponge and anthozoans communities on subtidal rock habitats; Intertidal seagrass beds; Honeycomb worm reefs; Peat and clay exposures; Natural blue mussel beds on sediment. This project was create and data captured to allow volunteers to contribute to BAP habiat and species recording by providing robust and scientific data to a described standard.  The project includes a report on the scope and discussion of best methods for volunteers for each habitat and species existing distribution maps and GIS layers for the 8 habitat and species images of each habitat and species draft resource packs and recording forms.\tCountryside Council for Wales\t\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17976\tNRW_DS114807\t2024-05-28T12:37:06.701Z\t621\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Lamprey in the River Dee (2011)\t© CNC/NRW 2012 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383037\t[A]  Data is archived as project 348, media 1325.   [B]  Species data in Recorder 6 [CCW000800000000A]\tInternal Custodian:  Tristan Hatton Ellis - Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tRiver, Brook and Sea Lamprey are a designated (Annex II) feature of the River Dee Special Area of Conservation. The purpose of this data collection was to assess the conservation status of the Annex II features, including species composition, distribution, abundance and size class composition are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment.  Monitoring was undertaken on the Dee Catchment in 2011.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18031\tNRW_DS114817\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.707Z\t367\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Preliminary Description of the Sublittoral Habitats and Associated Biota within the Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ )\t© CNC/NRW 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383137\t[A] Data is archived as project 352, media 1331 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1331) [B] Species and habitat data is available in Marine Recorder [MRCCW31700000003]\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team, NRW.\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  This dataset consists of the survey results from sublittoral work carried out between 1973 and 1975.  This work was the first baseline sublittoral dive survey of the Skomer Marine Nature Reserve and there is only one copy of the report currently available to NRW.  As a result the digitisation and archiving of this report in a format utilisable to ongoing monitoring work and decision makers was deemed essential. The survey work focussed on the sublittoral environment around Skomer Island Marine Nature Reserve. The locations of the stations were fixed at regular distances throughout the then proposed area of the reserve, at regular depth intervals; forming a series of discontinuously sampled transects of the seabed cliff or slope. This formation of sample stations was chosen to ensure information could be obtained on substrate types and topography; water movement (exposure); and macro-flora and fauna.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1976-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:37:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18035\tNRW_DS114818\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.718Z\t598\tUnassessed\tEditable\tManagement Measures for Self-Propagated Future Recovery of Crawfish, Palinurus elephas, in Welsh Waters.\t© CNC/NRW 2012 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383138\tReport archived as project 353, media 1332 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1332)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External Contact: NAFC Marine Centre  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe crawfish, Palinurus elephas, is a large benthic crustacean found mainly on rocky seabeds from the Azores in the south to Norway in the North and throughout the Mediterranean.  This species is commercially valuable and has been targeted by fisheries for over a century; however, fisheries in Wales and beyond have seen a steep decline in landings since the 1960s which coincided with the introduction of net fisheries for this species.   The crawfish is a priority species for the UK Biodiversity Action Plan and is also an important component of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) designated for rocky reefs.  It has been identified by the National Assembly for Wales as one of the species of \"principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\" in Wales and is listed in the Natural Environment Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  This act requires that steps be taken that are \u0027reasonably practicable to further the conservation of the living organisms\u0027 listed and to \u0027promote the taking by others of such steps\u0027.  It is therefore important to understand the potential impact of fisheries management measures and other management measures on the recovery of this species both in designated areas and within the wider context of Welsh waters as a whole including the potential impact of regulations or management measures in adjacent waters.   The purpose of this data capture was to investigate management measures for the self-propagated future recovery of crawfish Palinurus elephas in Welsh waters. The overall aim of this survey and reporting work was to establish the potential effectiveness of existing and possible management measures (both fishery and nature conservation based) to enable self-propagated recovery of P. elephas populations in Welsh waters within the context of their declined status.  This was to be carried out through a review of relevant literature on this and other crustacean species to determine using the best available data and information the potential benefit of the different management measures.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:49:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18033\tNRW_DS114819\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.688Z\t767\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros) and feeding habitat within Coedydd Derw a Safleoedd Ystlumod Meirion / Merionnydd Oak Woods Bat Sites SAC (2006)\t© CNC/NRW 2006 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence.  No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383139\tAssociated data and reports are held at Maes-y-Ffynnon at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\33 Monitoring selected colonies of LHB and feeding habitat within Meirionnydd Oak Woods SAC.\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tCoedydd Derw a Safloedd Ystlumod Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is a large composite site of international importance for lesser horseshoe bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros) containing more than 15 known maternity roosts and hibernacula. Although lesser horseshoe bat annual count data are collected from roosts within the SAC by the Gwynedd Bat Group and other volunteers, additional information is required regarding the condition of foraging habitat and flight lines surrounding the roosts. For the purposes of this contract, an area covering a radius of two kilometres around each of the ten target roosts was selected for further study. The purpose of this data capture was to establish the condition of the lesser horseshoe bat within the Meirionydd Oakwoods and Bat Sites Special Area of Conservation (SAC).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:09.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18029\tNRW_DS114836\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.693Z\t956\tValid\tEditable\tCollection Activity in the Menai Strait and Pilot Methods to Assess Frequency of Boulder Turning Activity (2011)\t© CNC/NRW 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383336\tArchived as project 355, media 1334. - Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1334\tData Custodian:  Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs) are strictly protected sites designated under the EC Habitats Directive. Reporting on the condition of the intertidal features of the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) depends upon an understanding of the features and the threats of damage by regulated and unregulated activities.The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), run an intertidal SAC monitoring program, which is undertaken to evaluate the quality of the biological communities associated with under-boulder and muddy gravel habitats. The biological data must be placed in context with the levels of collecting activity on the shore, specifically bait and winkle collecting.   CCW contracted Marine EcoSol to better assess the intensity of collecting activity in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC, in order to establish the levels that might be considered  acceptable, in terms of their impact on the intertidal (Reef) features. Both muddy gravel and underboulder communities are Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitats, and the 2011 field surveys are within or adjacent to intertidal Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and marine SAC.  The project was to investigate the feasibility of developing rapid visual assessment methods for use by non-specialists and to provide contextual information for status reporting of reef features within the Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay SAC.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:51:08.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18018\tNRW_DS114840\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.542Z\t860\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCountryside Survey (Survey data)\tRestrictions depend on resolution of data required. For more information see the Countryside Survey website (http://www.countrysidesurvey.org.uk/) All use of CS data must be acknowledged.  The following acknowledgement and copyright notice (where applicable), shall be used on all copies of the data, publications and reports, including use in presentations to any audience. - Acknowledgement: Countryside Survey data owned by NERC – Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology - Countryside Survey © Database Right/Copyright NERC– Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology. All rights reserved\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383430\tData held by the Countryside Survey on their website (http://www.countrysidesurvey.org.uk/home).\tMetadata point of contact: Data Services Custodians: CEH and NERC Contact: Countryside Survey Project Office, Centre for Ecology and Hydrology, Lancaster  Environment Centre, Library Avenue, Bailrigg, Lancaster LA1 4AP\tCountryside Survey in 2007 was funded by a partnership of government-funded bodies led by the Natural Environment Research Council (NERC) and the Department for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs (Defra).  Countryside Survey is a unique study or ‘audit’ of the natural resources of the UK’s countryside. The Survey has been carried out at discrete intervals since 1978 (2007, 1998, 1990, 1984 and 1978). The Countryside Survey provides scientifically reliable evidence about the state or health of the UK\u0027s countryside today. By comparing present findings with previous surveys it is possible to identify change (and the relative rate of change) in the countryside. This evidence is used to help form policies that influence management of the countryside, both now and in the future.  The purpose of this data capture is to provide a \u0027snap-shot\u0027 of the health of the countryside in the UK and a comparable dataset to track changes.\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:36.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18028\tNRW_DS114859\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.711Z\t401\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Regional Data: South East Wales (South East Wales Biological Records Centre - SEWBReC)\t© CNC/NRW  - NRW Regional Data: South East Wales Sensitive Species Records NRW is legally obliged to give public access to the data and information which it holds unless specific legal exceptions apply, for example, if the data is personal or if release of the data would result in environmental harm. This dataset does not contain any sensitive species.   - NRW Regional Data: South East Wales Non-sensitive Species Records The Intellectual Property Rights (including copyright and database rights) to the collation and all records created by NRW staff or contractors are owned by NRW. Where individual records were created by a third party, the copyright to that record rests with the original recorder/surveyor. You may reproduce non-sensitive data free of charge for non-commercial and internal business purposes in any format or medium, provided that you do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. To avoid using old information, we recommend that you obtain the latest version from the NBN Gateway.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383539\tX:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to SEWBREC.lyr   SEWBREC hold and manage data on behalf of NRW. NRW still hold ownership over these dataset. All data that SEWBREC hold as a top copy on behalf of NRW is published on the NBN Gateway. In Addition to this NRW hold a copy of the data in Recorder 6 - however this can be out of date. Some of these are listed in the \u0027Contains\u0027 Field and others aren\u0027t.  NRW hold an out of date copy of some of this data in Recorder 6: These are as follows. LC00021500000049  CCW Cosmeston Park SSSI LC0002150000003Z. CCW Craig Pont Rhondda SSSI LC00021500000040   CCW Cwm Taf Fechan Woodland SSSI LC00021500000030   CCW Lesser Horseshoe Bats LC0002150000002U  CCW The River Wye Valley LC0002150000002E  CCW - MOD Caerwent    Also contains the following specific metadata \u003d 116724 - CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework Files 2001 to 2005\tInternal Custodian and Metadata Contact: Karen Wilkinson is the main lead with SewBrec SewBrec are the Custodians This data will be incoprorated in the GIS layers that NRW also received\tThis dataset is made up of a collation of species records (excluding sensitive records) derived from Natural Resources Wales (formerly Countryside Council for Wales), South and East Region paper files. The purpose of this data capture was to mobilise a valuable data resource in order to provide baseline data to inform conservation work, research, site management and decision-making within NRW and externally. Records are for the NRW\u0027s South and East Region which includes Blaenau-Gwent, Bridgend, Caerphilly, Cardiff, Merthyr Tydfil, Monmouthshire, Neath-Port Talbot, Newport, Rhondda-Cynon-Taff, Swansea, Torfaen, and The Vale of Glamorgan. There are also Records from other parts of Wales. Many records are for designated sites (including Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), Special Area of Conservation (SACs), National Nature Reserves etc), however, there are also large numbers of records from the wider countryside. The majority of the records have six figure grid-references, but some of these may be from site centroids rather than actual location of the species in question.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:08.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18024\tNRW_DS114860\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.608Z\t734\tValid\tEditable\tColonisation, Growth and Interactions of Carpet Sea Squirt (Didemnum vexillum)\t© CNC/NRW and Bangor University 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ Bangor University and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383630\tData is archived as projects 323 and 324 and media 1340 and 1341 (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1340 and K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1341)\tStuart Jenkins, Bangor University, School of Ocean Sciences. Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe Carpet Sea Squirt (Didemnum vexillum) is a Invasive Non Native (INN) filter feeding marine invertebrate that forms large colonies carpetting hard structures on which it grows.    Carpet Sea Squirt (Didemnum vexillum) was only confirmed in British waters in 2008. However, there are strong concerns that it will quickly colonise a much wider area as it has been found to do in other countries such as New Zealand and the USA.    After an initial survey to identify the extent of colonisation, an eradication programme was undertaken by the Countryside Council for Wales. A programme of monitoring was initiated to measure the success of the eradication experiment and subsequent recolonisation and a research project undertaken in conjunction with Bangor University.    The purpose of this data capture was to assess patterns of settlement, growth, colonisation and species interactions of Didemnum vexillum within Holyhead Marina from season to season.  Also to look at how different disturbance magnitudes have negative or positive effects on Didemnum vexillum settlement and recruitment.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:35:58.235Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18027\tNRW_DS114892\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.693Z\t526\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEcological Effects of Re-Building Beaches in Wales\t©CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright (2012). All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383932\tData is archived the marine archive as project 354, media 1333 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1333)\tInternal Custodian: Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Process \u0026amp; Coastal Management, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tBeach re-building - also known as beach nourishment, is the process of adding sediment to a beach to make it higher and wider. In an era of sea level rise, beaches are under serious threat - many are already adversely affected by manmade defences that have reduced the natural sediment supply. Beaches are a key natural, social and economic resource for Wales - they play a vital role in sustaining coastal tourism and underpin important nature conservation and landscape sites and acts as \u0027natural\u0027 coastal defences, dissipating wave energy, helping to protect both natural and manmade structures. During 2009/2010 a Steering Group comprising The Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), The Crown Estate, British Marine Aggregates Producers Association (BMAPA), Welsh Government, and the Environment Agency Wales (EAW) commissioned a Pilot Study to begin investigating the issues relating to the use of aggregates (sand, shingle, gravel, etc.) for beach nourishment in Wales, specifically in relation to the potential requirements for the quantity of material that may be needed to maintain Welsh beaches at current and future risk. The study is, in essence, a short data and literature review to establish the current level of knowledge regarding the ecological impacts (both positive and negative) of beach nourishment on the intertidal ecology of Welsh beaches.  The potential impacts of more \u0027traditional\u0027 hard engineering and the use beach control structures are relatively well understood by coastal engineers, while the potential impacts of beach nourishment are less well known by those responsible for all aspects of beach management.  The aim of the study is to look at the ecological effects (including potential benefits or adverse effects) of re-building beaches. This considers the most appropriate places for beach re-building, the methods used (including timing of works) and the suitability of different types of material.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:26:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18021\tNRW_DS114930\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.611Z\t880\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcoustic Multibeam Echosounder Survey of Turbot Bank Pembrokeshire (March 2012)\t© CNC/NRW 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393330\tData is archived as Project 362, media 1347.   Data is also available through the UK Hydrographic Office.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Mapping Scientist, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tA multibeam echosounder survey was commissioned by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in March 2012 over Turbot Bank, Pembrokeshire. This was completed by EMU Ltd at the end of March 2012 to International Hydrographic Organisation (IHO) Order 1a standards. The data have been verified by the United Kingdom Hydrographic Office (UKHO) and fully tidally corrected to chart datum. The dataset shows the dominant bedform over the bank to be large to very large dunes, with bedrock visible on the eastern edge of the study area. Further data use and analysis by CCW should help to delineate features for mapping purposes, and monitor change over time.  The purpose of this data capture is to obtain bathymetry and backscatter datasets of the study area with 100% coverage.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-06-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:26:55.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18022\tNRW_DS114942\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.642Z\t367\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Piddock Beds in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2009\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2012. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393432\tArchived as project 199 media 1350.  Working copy available as H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm\\2009 ASML Carm\\Data and species data available in Marine Recorder.  Species data also available in Marine Recorder.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. This data was captured to support a number of reporting requirements. These were namely reporting on conservation status of the piddock feature; mapping the boundary of the clay and peat exposures; to record the density of the piddock holes; and evaluate the best sampling methods.  Carmarthen Bay SAC was designated for five Annex I intertidal habitat features: Estuaries; Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide; Large shallow inlets and bays; Salicornia and other annuals colonising mud and sand; Atlantic salt meadows (Glauco- Puccinellietalia maritimae). Specific areas of interest include the peat and clay beds burrowed by piddocks and the monitoring aimed to map the boundary of the clay/peat exposures, record the density of the piddock holes and evaluate the best sampling methods.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:52:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18025\tNRW_DS114943\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.642Z\t451\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera) Beds in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2009\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2012. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393433\tArchived as project 199 media 1350.   Working copy available as H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm\\2009 ASML Carm\\Data.  Species data also available through Marine Recorder.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Seagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas, such as lagoons and channels, which are sheltered from significant wave action. Eelgrass (Zostera marina), is considered to be scarce (present in 16-100 ten km squares) is protected through the EC Habitats Directive and is classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitat. Data is captured to support reporting on conservation status of the piddock feature. Carmarthen Bay SAC was designated for five Annex I intertidal habitat features: Estuaries; Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide; Large shallow inlets and bays; Salicornia and other annuals colonising mud and sand; Atlantic salt meadows (Glauco- Puccinellietalia maritimae). Specific areas of interest include areas of eelgrass Zostera noltii in the Burry Inlet and the monitoring aimed to map the area covered by Zostera at the two sites; sample points on a grid across those areas identified to have Zostera to estimate the cover of surface species.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:53:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18023\tNRW_DS114944\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.643Z\t697\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sediment Communities in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2009\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2012. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393434\tArchived as project 199 media 1350. Working copy available as H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm\\2009 ASML Carm\\Data. Species data also available in Marine Recorder.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  Carmarthen Bay SAC was designated for five Annex I intertidal habitat features: Estuaries; Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide; Large shallow inlets and bays; Salicornia and other annuals colonising mud and sand; Atlantic salt meadows (Glauco- Puccinellietalia maritimae). Specific areas of interest include the range of intertidal sediment communities in the bay and the monitoring aimed to collect core samples along transects established in previous years.  Data is captured to support reporting on conservation status of the piddock feature. All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  Data quality and confidence considered high. Piddocks can be described as a biotope that occurs on circalittoral soft rock, such as soft chalk or clay, most often in moderately exposed tide-swept conditions. As soft chalk and firm clay are often too soft for sessile filter-feeding animals to attach and thrive in large numbers, an extremely impoverished epifauna results on upward-facing surfaces, although vertical faces may be somewhat richer. The rock is sufficiently soft to be bored by bivalves.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:54:14.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18020\tNRW_DS114945\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.590Z\t421\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Saltmarsh Extent in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) 2009\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2012. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393435\tArchived as project 199 media 1350.   Working copy available as H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm\\2009 ASML Carm\\Data\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Carmarthen Bay SAC was designated for five Annex I intertidal habitat features: Estuaries; Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide; Large shallow inlets and bays; Salicornia and other annuals colonising mud and sand; Atlantic salt meadows (Glauco- Puccinellietalia maritimae).   Specific areas of interest include saltmarsh fringing the Burry Inlet.  The Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. Data was captured to, map the 2009 boundary for comparison with previous boundaries; record patches away from the main saltmarsh; take viewpoint photographs to enable a quick comparison of boundaries on future visits.  All surveys completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  Data quality and confidence considered high.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:54:48.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18032\tNRW_DS114948\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.690Z\t344\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Boulders Habitat in the Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2010\t© CNC/NRW 2010. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393438\tData is archived as project 199, media 1352 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400) A working copy of the data is available at  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy_CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MSandCB\\2010 ASML MSandCB\\Data Marine Recorder Survey: MRCCW1630000002F ArcGIS layers: Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. To do this CCW has developed a programme of feature condition monitoring. In Menai Strait \u0026amp; Conwy Bay SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide and Reefs.   The purpose of this data capture was to assist with the reporting on the favourable conservation status of Reef features in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation. This dataset comprises of the results from an intertidal monitoring survey carried out on the tide-swept boulder habitat in the Menai Strait in 2008. Boulders are a reef feature of the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. Boulders at both Britannia Bridge and Felinheli were surveyed. The purpose of this data capture was to assist in the reporting on the favourable conservation status of Reef features in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors so data quality and confidence is considered high.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:55:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18034\tNRW_DS114949\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.710Z\t401\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Biotope SLR.FserX.T in the Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2010\t© CNC/NRW 2010 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393439\tData is archived as project 199, media 1352.  A working copy of the data is available at  H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MSandCB\\2010 ASML MSandCB\\Data\tInternal custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. This dataset is the results of an intertidal monitoring survey carried out on the tide-swept boulder habitat in the Menai Strait in 2008.  Tide-swept Fucus serratus habitats is a nationally important habitat and although the habitat extent was known, no detailed studies of the communities had been carried out.  Tide-swept habitats were surveyed at two locations to provide a baseline for current condition and to allow a study for potential future impacts of mussel farming in the vicinity.  All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  Data quality and confidence considered high.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:56:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18019\tNRW_DS114970\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.522Z\t616\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey in the River Tywi (2011)\t© CNC/NRW 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393730\t[A]  Associated data is held within the Marine Archive as Project 356, Media 1335.  [B]  Biological Data is held in Recorder 6 Lamprey Monitoring, Tywi Catchment 2011 - CCW - Staff Science Report 11/8/5 CCW000800000000B.\t\tRiver lamprey, Brook lamprey and Sea lamprey are listed in Annex II of the EU Habitats Directive (EU Directive on the Conservation of Natural Habitats of Wild Fauna and Flora 92/43/EEC). There are six rivers in Wales designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) for lamprey, including the Afon Tywi in West Wales. Natural Resources Wales is responsible for monitoring the condition of lamprey species populations within SACs and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within designated sites.  The data, collected and contracted out by former Countryside Council for Wales, is used to assess the population attribute for all three lamprey species.  Sampling was carried out between the 5th October and 22nd November 2011.  A total of 30 sites distributed within the Tywi catchments were selected between Llandovery and Carmarthen.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:09.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18016\tNRW_DS114978\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.151Z\t800\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDormouse Core Areas\tThere are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.     Note to staff: Please attach the above statement to any data being issued.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393738\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Dormouse_Core_Areas.lyr\tCustodians and metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team Also contact the PTES for the most up to date version of the dataset.\tThis is a spatial dataset held by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) which holds records from the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) dormouse data sourced from areas of work, such as licence returns and dormouse survey work and also The Vincent Wildlife Trust (VWT) dormouse survey data. Data collation completed by PTES under data exchange agreement with PTES. Map has been used as basis for Glastir Targeted Element - core (or mandatory) sites.  Majority of records collected by experienced surveyors, or verified by experienced surveyors (e.g. nut hunt records). Licence returns have not been independently verified.  This dataset is a collation of individual records of dormouse sightings and/or evidence for Wales mostly between 1988 and 2014, although there are a few earlier records. Records originate from a variety of sources, with the collation held and maintained by People\u0027s Trust for Endangered Species (PTES). It is an incomplete directory of sites due to the ad-hoc nature of data collection. Some sites are regularly monitored using nestboxes as part of the National Dormouse Monitoring Programme (NDMP) and these sites are indicated in the attributes and more detailed biometric data are held by PTES who administer the monitoring programme.   Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds a copy of the GIS dataset on their internal GIS data store - however, requestors are advised to obtain the most up-to-date version of the data from PTES. This dataset is also held under the National Dormouse Database (NDD) available to download from the NBN Gateway.\tPeoples Trust for Endangered Species (PTES)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:15.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18011\tNRW_DS114979\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.865Z\t430\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDormouse Outer Areas\tThere are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.    Note to staff: Please attach the above statement to any data being issued.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393739\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Dormouse_Outer_Areas.lyr\tMetadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Team  Custodian: GIS and Spatial Team / PTES\tThis is a spatial dataset held by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) which holds records from the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) dormouse data sourced from areas of work, such as licence returns and dormouse survey work and also The Vincent Wildlife Trust (VWT) dormouse survey data. This dataset is a collation of dormouse records for Wales from the People\u0027s Trust for Endangered Species (PTES) National Dormouse Database, with a spatial resolution of a 2 km buffer zone. Map has been used as basis for Glastir Targeted Element - core (or mandatory) sites. Majority of records collected by experienced surveyors, or verified by experienced surveyors (e.g. nut hunt records). Licence returns have not been independently verified.  Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds a copy of the GIS dataset on their internal GIS data store - however, requestors are advised to obtain the most up-to-date version of the data from PTES. This dataset is also held under the National Dormouse Database (NDD) available to download from the NBN Gateway.\tPeoples Trust for Endangered Species (PTES)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:08.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18030\tNRW_DS115034\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.712Z\t567\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCoastal Vegetated Shingle Structures of Wales\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2012. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303334\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1348 Archived as Project 363 and media 1348.   Archived on the Z drive  (not available on x drive) Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_ M_DRIVE:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\VegetatedShingle\\\tCustodians Coastal Scientist, NRW. Marine and Freshwater Data Manager, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tA survey of Coastal Vegetated Shingle Ridges of Great Britain was commissioned in 1987 by the Nature Conservancy Council.  The project provides a classification of the main shingle plant communities found on stable or semi-stable structures in Great Britain and was published by Joint Nature Conservation Council (JNCC) in four parts.  The data was captured in an attempt to describe the size, location and quality of the Vegetated Shingle Ridges.  The surveys focused on the vegetation of stony banks and did not attempt to map strandline communities; therefore the site boundary is not a true representation of the extent of the Shingle Ridge.  An overall report which combines all data to determine which plant communities are found on shingle at a national level and how these related to existing National Vegetation Classification (NVC) communities, a map and a description of the major plant communities recorded at each of the sites.\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1993-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:56:58.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18026\tNRW_DS115035\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.608Z\t510\tUnassessed\tEditable\tVegetated Shingle Habitats\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303335\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping  Archived as Project 363 and media 1348. (File path) K:\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1348 Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_ M_DRIVE:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\VegetatedShingle\\\tInternal Contact: Julie Creer, Lead Specialist Advisor – Coastal Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Internal Custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset showing the boundary of shingle habitats and the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) equivalent code.  A GIS layer was generated in 2012 from paper maps from the 1993 JNCC report by Sneddon and Randall.  The purpose of this data capture is to generate an electronic version of the data and make it easily available for reporting purposes.\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-08-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:27:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18013\tNRW_DS115036\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.897Z\t284\tUnassessed\tEditable\tVegetated Shingle Site Boundary\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303336\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1348 Archived as Project 363 and media 1348.  Needs to be transferred over from Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\VegetatedShingle\\\tInternal Custodian: Monica Jones, Data Services, NRW (Contract Support Officer) Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tA polygon layer showing the boundary of vegetated shingle sites.  A GIS layer was generated in 2012 from paper maps from the 1993 JNCC report by Sneddon and Randall.  The purpose of this data capture is to generate an electronic version of the data and make it easily available for reporting purposes.  The surveys focused on the vegetation of stony banks and did not attempt to map strandline communities; therefore the site boundary is not a true representation of the extent of the Shingle Ridge.\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-08-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:28:27.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18012\tNRW_DS115048\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.864Z\t678\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLlyn Tegid Hydroacoustic Surveys and Gwyniad Translocation 2003\t© CNC/NRW 2003 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303438\t[A]  Data is archived as Project 369, Media 1364.   [B]  Species data is available in Recorder 6 Gwyniad Gill netting survey of Llyn Tegid 2003 - CCW Science Report 597  [CCW000800000000E]\t\tThe purpose of this project was to develop a generic Whitefish sampling protocol suitable for Llyn Tegid and produce a condition assessment of the population.  A population survey of Gwyniad was recommended to be undertaken after failed attempts to establish a refuge population in 2003. Project to develop a generic protocol for the assessment of whitefish (Coregonus albula and Coregonus lavaretus) populations developed by Scottish Natural Heritage (SNH) to the Gwyniad of Llyn Tegid during July 2003 with the addition of the determination of oxygen and temperature profiles and Secchi depths.  The protocol involved hydroacoustics and limited gill netting.  This datasets contains the results of the gill netting survey only.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:14.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18017\tNRW_DS115062\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.432Z\t333\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPredicting the Impacts of Objective 1 Funding on Selected Aquatic Natura 2000 Sites in Wales\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright [2002]. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303632\tData is archived as project 359, media 84.   Data is also available on the CCW M drive at the following location M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\marine_reports\\natura_2000\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Spatial Plan Energy and Infrastructure Team, NRW Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe aim of this project was to provide Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) with a clear understanding of where developments funded by the Structural Funds under Objective 1, may potentially impact sites along the Welsh coastline with aquatic Natura 2000 designations under the EU Habitats Directive (92/43/EEC). The project has involved a number of steps including; the identification of objective one structural funding projects likely to impact on the Natura 2000 sites in Wales; the identification of Welsh aquatic maturity thousand sites, and the identification of potential impacts and sensitivities based on the available information contained within Regional Action Plans (RAPS) and Local Action Plans (LAPS) and the list of priorities, measures and projects in each plan. The Objective 1 funded projects have been analysed and grouped into the following categories; fisheries, industrial developments, infrastructure developments, tourism initiatives, farming initiatives, forestry, biodiversity and conservation initiatives, and regional development. As part of this project an interactive GIS tool has been developed which identifies the Welsh aquatic maturity thousand sites within the Objective 1 funded areas and the nature conservation features for which the sites are designated. The GIS tool can be manipulated to indicate the generic impacts resulting from the likely types of Objective 1 funded projects to the features of interest at the site.  This study develops a GIS tool that aims to identify where developments funded under the Structural Funds from Objective 1 may impact Welsh coastlines that occur within Natura 2000 sites.  Although the data was based on the best information available at the time of capture, the base maps (Special Area of Conservation (SAC) features and boundaries) are now considered out-of-date.  Newer sources of data are currently available.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:39:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18015\tNRW_DS115064\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.033Z\t547\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCoastal Saltmarsh Monitoring\t© CNC/NRW 2007. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303634\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A]  Data is archived as project 370, media 1365.   [B]  Species data is also held in Recorder 6 [LC0003850000006Q]\tBianca Bacchi, Senior Env Mon Officer, Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R, NRW\tCoastal saltmarshes in the UK comprise the upper, vegetated portions of intertidal mudflats, lying approximately between mean high water neap tides and mean high water spring tides. The characteristic vegetation consists of a limited number of halophytic (salt tolerant) species adapted to regular immersion by the tides. This project was commissioned by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) primarily to cover the condition monitoring of the Annex I saltmarsh habitats across four Welsh Special Areas of Conservation (SAC). The two habitats concerned are:   - Atlantic salt meadows (referred to as ASM) - Annexe I code 1330. Atlantic salt meadows develop when halophytic vegetation colonises soft intertidal sediments of mud and sand in areas protected from strong wave action. This vegetation forms the middle and upper reaches of saltmarshes, where tidal inundation still occurs but with decreasing frequency and duration. A wide range of community types is represented and the saltmarshes can cover large areas, especially where there has been little or no enclosure on the landward side. The vegetation varies with climate and the frequency and duration of tidal inundation. - Salicornia and other annuals colonising mud (referred to as \u0027Salicornia\u0027) - Annexe I code 1310. This pioneer saltmarsh vegetation colonises intertidal mud and sandflats in areas protected from strong wave action and is an important precursor to the development of more stable saltmarsh vegetation. It develops at the lower reaches of saltmarshes where the vegetation is frequently flooded by the tide, and can also colonise open creek sides, depressions or pans within saltmarshes, as well as disturbed areas of upper saltmarshes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:58:30.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18014\tNRW_DS115065\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.018Z\t392\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Muddy Gravel, Sediments and Rocky Shore in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2008\t© CNC/NRW 2010 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303635\tData is archived a project 199, media 249.  Species data is also held in Marine Recorder and on the NBN Gateway.  A working copy of the data is stored at H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2008 ASML PLAS\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tPen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary ones. The major features of intertidal interest are Estuaries and their constituent mudflats, sandflats and reefs, Large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds, populations of the shingle dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus, piddocks in clay and lower shore boulder communities and Reefs, particularly biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata. Specific areas of interest for are the intertidal sediments and rocky areas in the Glaslyn and Dwyryd Estuaries.  There were two purposes for this data capture. The first was related to sediments. Sediment data was collected to test monitoring methodology, establish sites for long term monitoring and collect baseline and reference data. The second was related to the Data collected on the rocky shore to establish sand levels at key rocky areas - Port Meirion, Ynys Gifftan and Cei Ballast.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:59:23.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18010\tNRW_DS115072\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.792Z\t322\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria Reef in Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2008\t© CNC/NRW 2010. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303732\tArchived as Project No 199, Media No 548. Species data is also available in Marine Recorder and on the NBN Gateway.  A working copy of the data us stored at H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Cardigan Bay\\2008 ASML Cardigan BayData\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary, tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats).  In Cardigan Bay SAC specific areas of interest include the biogenic Sabellaria reef.  Sabellaria reef is considered vulnerable to coastal development, sea defence construction and heavy trampling. The purpose of this data capture was to re-survey the quadrat monitoring sites established in 2007. The descriptive transect surveys are not repeated in 2008. Data quality and confidence is considered high as all survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:00:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18008\tNRW_DS115074\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.623Z\t520\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Boulders Habitat in the Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2009\t© CNC/NRW 2010 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303734\tData is archived as project 199, media 550.  Species data is also available in Marine Recorder and the NBN Gateway.  A copy of the working data is available at H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2009 ASML MS\u0026amp;CB\\Data\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata: Master Reference Data Team\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. To do this CCW has developed a programme of feature condition monitoring.In Menai Strait \u0026amp; Conwy Bay SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide and Reefs. Specific areas of interest include areas of tide-swept bedrock and boulders and areas of tide-swept Fucus serratus on mixed sediment along the Menai Strait.  The overriding objective of the survey was to establish the reference condition for the Menai Strait \u0026amp; Conwy Bay SAC, using established monitoring stations (and establishing additional monitoring stations) to describe natural and unnatural changes in the communities. This will enable the development of conservation objectives for the appropriate management of the features of the SAC. This data was collected to build on the surveys conducted in 2005, 2007 and 2008 to further test and develop monitoring methodology and collect monitoring data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:01:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18007\tNRW_DS115077\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.556Z\t312\tValid\tEditable\tCollection Activity and Crab Survey in the Menai Strait (2009)\t© CNC/NRW 2010. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303737\tData is archived as project 199, media 550.  Species data is also available in Marine Recorder and the NBN Gateway.   A copy of the working data is available at H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2009 ASML MS\u0026amp;CB\\Data\tData Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services NRW\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs) are strictly protected sites designated under the EC Habitats Directive.  In the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide and Reefs. Specific areas of interest include areas of tide-swept bedrock and boulders and areas of tide-swept Fucus serratus on mixed sediment along the Menai Strait. In previous studies it was noted that a portion of the boulders had been turned by bait collectors. Surveys to assess bait collection activity and crab counts were carried out along the length of the shoreline of the Menai Strait using student volunteers. Data quality and confidence considered high.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:53:10.222Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18009\tNRW_DS115081\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.624Z\t639\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Biotope SLR.FserX.T in the Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) 2009\t© CNC/NRW 2010. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303831\tData is archived as project 199 and media 552.  Species data is also available in Marine Recorder.  A working copy of the data is available at  :\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2009 ASML MS\u0026amp;CB\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  In the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide, and reefs. Specific areas of interest include areas of tide-swept bedrock and boulders and areas of tide-swept Fucus serratus on mixed sediment along the Menai Strait. The purpose of this in-situ survey was to locate specific tide-swept Fucus serratus zones and measure the width of the zone, percentage of Fucus serratus within the zone and information on epibionts. This in turn provided a baseline for future condition monitoring and helped establish a methodology for intertidal surveying.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:02:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18006\tNRW_DS115123\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.421Z\t296\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveys of Potential Oystercatcher Prey, Pembrey Sands (2009)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313233\tData is archived as Project 371, media 1368\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy, Maritime Ornithologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) supports nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) are studying the food requirements of oystercatcher and knot, to develop monitoring targets and to assess the implications of shellfish fisheries on the bird populations. As part of that study, surveys of the cockle and mussel stocks in the Burry Inlet have been carried out every autumn since 2004, using a standard methodology. Significant populations of oystercatcher also feed in the Three Rivers and along Pembrey Sands and it is known that they are part of the same population that feed in the Burry Inlet SPA, but the information is limited. Data from the British Trust for Ornithology (BTO) on oystercatcher distribution along the Pembrey coast showed that they concentrate in the central section of the Sands and confirmed that they are feeding at low tide. It is known from previous survey work that the sediment fauna found along the Pembrey coast are likely to include a variety of shellfish (but not cockles and mussels), however data is limited. The purpose of this data capture was to provide data on the abundance and distribution of the potential prey species for the oystercatcher along the Pembrey Sands due to limited data availability. This data aimed to determine the potential prey of the oystercatcher in Pembrey Sands, examining the abundance and distribution of the sediment fauna.  Previous findings showed that high numbers of the main prey species of the oyster catchers (cockles and mussels) were not found in the area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-10-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:03:48.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18048\tNRW_DS115145\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.442Z\t530\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSeasearch Diver and Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Snorkel Seagrass Zostera Marina Surveys Rhoscolyn 2012\t© CNC/NRW and SeaSearch 2012 NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313435\tData is archived as project 375, media 1385 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1385) A working copy is stored at Z:\\Archive\\CCW_H_Drive\\Science\\MFSG\\Users\\Ramsay_K\\rhoscolyn seagrass\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSeagrass is the only marine flowering plant found subtidally in the UK and seagrass beds are considered of National Importance. In 2012 Seasearch volunteers undertook a one day survey to verify the presence of seagrass beds at Rhoscolyn, Anglesey, North Wales.  Following the successful Seasearch search for seagrass, CCW undertook a snorkel survey to establish the size of the larger bed to the west of the bay. 1. Confirm the presence of seagrass beds at Rhoscolyn. 2. Note estimates of density and depth of live seagrass in different parts of the bed 3. Make note of any unusual features of the seagrass bed\tMarine Conservation Society (MCS)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-09-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:53:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18047\tNRW_DS115165\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.345Z\t487\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Habitat Vulnerability Assessment of the Welsh Coast - Phase 1\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313635\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine habitat vulnerability.lyr   Archived as project 378, media 1389 (K:\\Archive\\Media\\Media 1301-1400\\Media 1389. The database is held in Z:\\Archive\\CCW_H_Drive\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\Ph1sites\\Intertidal Site Assessments\\Intertidal Site Assessment Contract\\ExeGesIS Products\\Assessment Database V10 The database is called: IntertidalPhase1Analysisv10.mde In this folder on the Z:\\ drive are also ArcGIS format maps covering the assessments of biotopes and units, and also summary report tables.\tPaul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tThe coastline of Wales supports a wide variety of important marine ecological systems, habitats and species. A range of human activities, such as coastal developments and defences can result in the loss of these important coastal and marine habitats. These human activities coupled with future climate change, in particular sea level rise, could have significant impacts upon the natural coastal and marine environments.  Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) require a way to model the vulnerability of coastal habitats to these impacts.  The following metadata describes phase 1 of this project. The purpose of this data capture was to produce a marine habitat vulnerability assessment of the Welsh Coast to consider those habitats/biotopes that are vulnerable to coastal squeeze as a result of coastal management and other natural constraints for the coast of Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:04:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18055\tNRW_DS115166\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.560Z\t646\tValid\tEditable\tHABMAP Acoustic Multibeam Echosounder Survey of Selected Areas of the Southern Irish Sea (Summer 2005)\t© CNC/NRW 2009 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313636\tData is stored in the CCW / NRW marine data archive as Project 274, Media 1353\tInternal Custodians: - Karen Robinson, Subtidal Marine Ecologist, NRW. - Subtidal Team, NRW.\tThe HABMAP project was set up in response to the need for better spatial awareness of habitat distributions in the Southern Irish Sea. It was a partnership project between partners in Wales and Ireland, and part funded by INTERREG IIIa European regional development fund.   Data collection included acoustic data, sediment data and seabed imagery. These data were used to classify the seabed and feed into a predictive modelling process to predict benthic habitat.      The multibeam data was collected in five locations throughout the Southern Irish Sea by the R/V Celtic Voyager to Irish National Seabed Survey (INSS) standard. They were then processed, cleaned and classified based on acoustic classes, which were then related to seabed sediment types. Bedforms were also identified using the MBES data.   The Coordinate System used for the multibeam data was UTM Zone 30N. The xyz data was usually processed to a 5 m resolution.   The purpose of this data capture was to obtain multibeam coverage of the five study areas identified to cover a variety of different environments with different physical properties and biological communities. This would be used to link backscatter intensity to physical properties of the seabed. Data depth corrected for tide using POLPRED software which utilises one or more of Proudman Oceanographic Laboratory\u0027s hydrodyamic models, and reduced to chart datum. CARIS HIPS / SIPS software used to create surfaces and gridded datasets by experienced surveyors.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-05-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:59:29.386Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18051\tNRW_DS115168\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.536Z\t681\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey in the River Usk (2011)\t© CNC/NRW 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and CCW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313638\t[A]  Data is archived as project 357, media 1336.   [B]  Species data is held within Recorder 6 as Lamprey Monitoring, River Usk 2011 - CCW – Science Report No. 11/8/6 CCW000800000000M.\t\tRiver lamprey, Brook lamprey and Sea lamprey are listed in Annex II of the EU Habitats Directive (EU Directive on the Conservation of Natural Habitats of Wild Fauna and Flora 92/43/EEC). There are six rivers in Wales designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) for lamprey, including the River Usk in South Wales. The Countryside Council for Wales and now Natural Resources Wales is responsible for monitoring the condition of lamprey species populations within SACs and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within designated sites.  The main aim of this project is to undertake a condition assessment for the population attribute only for brook, river and sea lamprey designated features for the River Usk SAC for the second Habitats Directive monitoring reporting cycle (2007-2012).  A total of 35 sites distributed within the Usk catchment.  Only a few sites had optimal habitats and sub-optimal habitats were dominant throughout.  Sampling was carried out between the 10th October and 16th November 2011.  The data is used to assess the population attribute for all three lamprey species. The results of the four attributes outlined in the Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) Guidance for Freshwater Fauna give an indication of the overall condition assessment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:09.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18053\tNRW_DS115169\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.565Z\t454\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBottlenose Dolphin and Harbour Porpoise Monitoring in Cardigan Bay and Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SAC)\t© CNC/NRW, 2014. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. © Sea Watch Foundation, 2014. Project Number 441, Media Number 1481 folder SWFOwnedData_Confidential, contains confidential data and information which must be kept confidential. Not for release by NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to disseminate or re-use this data. Those seeking such permission must approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313639\tData is archived under Project No 382  Media No 1393, 1410 (2005-2012) Subsequent data for 2013 Archived as project 441, media 1481 Data for 2014-2016 archived as project 479, media 1556\tInternal Custodian is: Marine Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tA population of bottlenose dolphins forms a primary interest of Cardigan Bay and it was for this that Cardigan Bay was first selected as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Cardigan Bay is known to host the largest population of semi-resident bottlenose dolphins in the UK. The data collected as part of this research is being used to monitor the population dynamics and behaviour of bottlenose dolphins and harbour porpoises in Cardigan Bay.  Cardigan Bay is known to host the largest population of semi-resident bottlenose dolphins in the UK. This data covers monitoring carried out by the Sea Watch Foundation between 2011-2013 on behalf of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and subsequently Natural Resources Wales (NRW), to monitor bottlenose dolphin and harbour porpoise populations in Cardigan Bay.  The goal of this data collection was to monitor bottlenose dolphin and harbour porpoise populations in Cardigan Bay. The aims were to: - Provide information on the condition of bottlenose dolphins and harbour porpoises in Cardigan Bay including Cardigan Bay and Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation;   - To use photo-identification techniques to evaluate dolphin movements distribution and abundance; - To assess supporting habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:05:20.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18049\tNRW_DS115170\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.429Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSea Watch Foundation Welsh Bottlenose Dolphin Photo-Identification Catalogue (1980-onwards)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444   Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313730\tArchived as project 382, media 1394. All data is held in separate media number #1410 (2005-2012) This data encompasses the catalogued report used for identification held as Media 1394, and the photo-identification catalogue itself including left and right photographs of individuals is held as Media 1410 subsequent data for 2013 Archived as project 441, media 1481 Data for 2014-2016 archived as project 479, media 1556\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW.\tLong term studies of individuals through photo-identification (frequently termed photo - ID) of naturally marked cetaceans can reveal much needed information using a technique that is non invasive. It provides information on group structure, site fidelity, movement patterns and population size. In conjunction with other studies, long term photo ID can also enhance descriptions of life history parameters such as age at sexual maturity, calving intervals, reproductive, and total life spans. In order to provide a photo identification catalogue of individual dolphins seen in Welsh Waters to provide further information of dolphin pods in Wales.\tSea Watch Foundation\t2012-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18054\tNRW_DS115176\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.483Z\t681\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera nolitii) Beds at Angle Bay in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2008\t© CCGC/CCW 2012 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and CCW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313736\tData is archived as project 199, media 546.   A working copy of the data is held at H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2008 ASML Pembs\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Spatial Data Team, NRW.\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. For SACs in Wales Zostera noliti is a sub-feature of the Annex II habitat, mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at high tide. Seagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas, such as lagoons and channels, which are sheltered from significant wave action.   This data was captured to support reporting on conservation status of the Welsh Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) sub-feature eelgrass, zostera noltii.  Pembrokeshire Marine SAC was selected for eight Habitats Directive Annex I habitat types and five Annex II species. The aim of this survey was to initiate eelgrass Zostera noltii monitoring at Angle Bay in Milford Haven.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T12:00:25.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18052\tNRW_DS115180\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.475Z\t299\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSite Condition Assessment of Welsh Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) Standing Water features (2007 - 2012)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313830\tData is archived as project 385, media 1399.\tInternal Custodian: Dr Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Freshwater team leader, CCW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tA report and supporting data was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in 2011 to provide an assessment of the current conservation status of 23, mostly oligotrophic Welsh standing waterbodies. These lie within six different Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). The data have been used to generate site-specific condition assessments. The collection of survey data and preparation of the site condition assessments broadly follows the methods detailed in the Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) Guidance (JNCC, 2005). The lakes are assessed every 6 years.  The data contributes the the report \\Site condition assessments of Welsh SAC and SSSI standing water features 2007-2012\\\".\"\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW)\t2013-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18043\tNRW_DS115186\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.933Z\t364\tUnassessed\tEditable\tReporting of Marine Protected Areas (MPAs) Contribution to the UK Network\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 2012.   NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313836\tAssociated data is held in the Marine Archive as project 383, media 1395\tInternal Custodian:  Deputy Group Head/Team Leader Marine Ecosystems, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSection 124 of the Marine and Coastal Access Act (MCA Act) requires Welsh Government to report on the extent to which they have achieved the objectives in the Act to use conservation sites to contribute to a UK network of Marine Protected Areas (MPAs).  Initial reporting takes place in 2012 and subsequently every 6 years.   The purpose of this data capture was to provide supporting information to the Welsh Government Marine Protected Areas of Wales in both the offshore and inshore marine environment. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) have provided a report and supporting information to WG which provides an assessment of the representative and replication of the suite of Welsh MPAs, as well as a discussion of management and condition evidence to date.  A copy of the data is archived at CCW as a record of the evidence provided for the initial reporting.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18050\tNRW_DS115188\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.475Z\t335\tValid\tEditable\tInvestigation into Musculus discors Beds in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau (2011)\t© Lisa Hopkinson 2011 There are no access restrictions to this data. NRW have received permission to release, publish or disseminate it freely. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313838\tData is archived a project 372, media 1369  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1369  Species data is available in NRW Marine Recorder [MRCCW31600000008].\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis survey was carried out as part of a MSc thesis in September 2011. To provide baseline data on M. discors beds around the LlPeninsula, with four specific aims: Establish the population structure for sample beds from around the LlPeninsula in order to investigate population structure and determine bed longevity. Identify the in-fauna/epi-fauna associated with M. discors beds. Investigate the sedimentation that occurs upon M. discors beds. Provide advice to CCW on whether M. discors should be included within a Local Biodiversity Action Plan for Gwynedd.   To determine M. discors population structure and influence on assemblage structure, twenty six, ten centimetre (cm) diameter cores of M. discors, associated fauna and sediment were collected by Seasearch volunteers on SCUBA from four randomly chosen beds in North Wales.were rinsed through a 63 micrometre (sieve with the sediment, M. discors and in-fauna retained. All liquid was retained for later filtration. M. discors were sorted from the sample and length measured to the nearest 0.1 millimetre (mm) using Vernier callipers with epifauna identified and enumerated (Rees et al., 2008). If present, new recruits were sorted from the sample and measured using a calibrated microscope slide (to the nearest 0.1mm).   In cases where there were high numbers of recruits, sub-sampling occurred with the abundance and size of a quarter of the total recruit population determined. All associated fauna were identified to the lowest taxonomic level possible.\tHopkinson\t2011-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T09:05:44.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18044\tNRW_DS115216\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.953Z\t498\tValid\tEditable\tAerial Monitoring of Common Scoter Melinitta nigra in Carmarthen Bay 2010 - 2011\t© CNC/NRW, 2012. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323136\tData is archived under Project No 301, media 1404\tInternal Custodian:  Maritime Ornithologist, Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tCarmarthen Bay Special Protection Area (SPA) was classified as the UK\u0027s first SPA in 2003 on the basis of holding at least 1% (16,000 individuals: Wetlands International 2006) of the biogeographic population of common scoter Melanitta nigra. Common Scoter feed on bivalves and other invertebrates such as polychaetes and amphipods which are found in abundance in the shallow waters (less than 10 m) in Carmarthen Bay.   Common scoter are also listed as a UK BAP species. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) has supported ground based counts since 1996, following the Sea Empress oil spill. The purpose of this data capture was to collect information regarding the abundance and distribution of common scoters in the Carmarthen Bay SPA.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T12:02:17.496Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18046\tNRW_DS115217\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.266Z\t318\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLlyn Tegid and Llyn Arenig Fawr hydroacoustic and Gwyniad surveys 2012\t© CNC/NRW 2012.  There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323137\t[A]  Data is archived as project 389, media 1405.  [B]  Species data is held in Recorder 6: - Gwyniad Hydroaccoustic Survey of Llyn Tegid 2012 - CCW Science Report 1012  CCW000800000000G  - Gwyniad Hydroacoustic and Gill Netting Survey of Llyn Arenig Fawr 2012- CCW Science Report 1013 CCW000800000000I.\t\tThe gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) population of Llyn Tegid has been considered for a number of years to be threatened by deteriorating environmental conditions, especially those associated with eutrophication. This dataset contains the results of a project investigating the environmental variables. Attemps to translocate a population to Llyn Arenig Fawr have been attempted since 2003. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the status of gwyniad and its habitat in Llyn Tegid (native population) and Llyn Arenig Fawr (introduced population).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-08-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18045\tNRW_DS115243\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.979Z\t337\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMilford Haven Waterway: Benthic and Intertidal Sediment Macrofauna Data Collation 2006\t© Milford Haven Waterway Environmental Surveillance Group 2006. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323433\tData is archived as project 391, media 1407 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1407)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe Milford Haven Waterway is arguably the most intensively studied coastal region of the UK with respect to the soft sediment macrobenthos. These studies have largely been motivated by concern for the environmental effects of the oil industry, since the diversity and composition of the macrobenthos have become one of the mainstays of marine biological effects monitoring. The purpose of this data capture was to: -  Evaluate the existing macrobenthic and environmental data.  -  Undertake new statistical analyses of the data and to interpret the findings of these analyses.  -  Make recommendations for a meaningful and cost effective monitoring programme that might best be employed for regular wide-scale evaluation of biological quality in relation to a range of anthropogenic impacts.\tMilford Haven Waterway Environmental Surveillance Group [MHWESG]\t2006-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18042\tNRW_DS115254\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.893Z\t936\tValid\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) / Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Sidescan Data Collation and Archiving Project\tSide-scan Sonar Raw data and tiles: © CNC/NRW and © Bangor University. NRW may only disseminate the data that they own. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate any of the raw data or tiles that are owned by Bangor University. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly. Side-scan sonar index: © CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323534\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Hydrographic\\Marine_Acoustic_data\\sidescan_data M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Hydrographic\\Marine_Acoustic_data\\sidescan_data\\Sidescan_Index.tab Archived as Project 392, Media 1408.\tGIS Service Desk / Dave Tavner - Marine Mapping Scientist, Evidence Analysis Subtidal Team Leader, MFEG, NRW External Custodian:  Jim Burnell, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University\tAcoustic survey data for Welsh waters is collected by a variety of different organisations including Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the Bangor University School of Ocean Sciences (SOS).   The survey location encompasses the Irish Sea, including Liverpool Bay, Isle of Man and Northern Irish coast.   The majority of data collected from January 1998 onwards is stored in disk format at SOS, and requires a specialised disk reader to be used. In order to make these data more accessible to SOS, CCW and subsequently Natural Resources Wales (NRW), this project was carried out in two parts. The first part translated all the data into a new digital format and also produced GeoTiff images in both low and high resolution. The second part catalogued all the data available in a spreadsheet and mapped it spatially into a GIS based spatial index for easy visualisation and query. The data is now more readily available for use on projects such as for marine monitoring work or habitat mapping. This will give staff an easy to use spatial index of data available to them, as well as additional information such as linked student dissertations or other projects.  Please see the limitations on public access and use constraints section for further information on this dataset.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-University of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences\t2013-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T17:45:31.939Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18041\tNRW_DS115268\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.870Z\t336\tValid\tEditable\tDistribution of Fisheries Habitats and Fishing Activity Project - Revised Fishing Atlas Activity Layers (Task 2)\t© CNC/NRW 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323638\tAssociated data is held within the Marine Archive as; Project 376, Media 1396\tData Custodian - Senior Fisheries Advisor, NRW\tThe objective of the associated project was to summarise how the fisheries habitats developed during the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) habitat sensitivity project intersected with Special Area of Conservation (SACs), SAC features and fishing activity. This sets the fisheries sensitivity work in the context of the Habitats Directive.   As part of the project, the Fishing Atlas activity layers were processed by the toolkit to remove areas where the activity was unlikely to occur due to the habitats present.  The outputs were to be used by CCW to determine whether sensitive habitats were subject to fishing activity.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T10:15:35.307Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18039\tNRW_DS115270\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.776Z\t406\tValid\tEditable\tDistribution of Fisheries Habitats and Fishing Activity Project - Output Layers by Gear Category (Task 4)\t© CNC/NRW 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323730\tAssociated data is held in the Marine Archive as Project 376, Media 1396.\tData Custodian: Senior fisheries Advisor, NRW\tThe objective of the associated project was to summarise how the fisheries habitats developed during the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) habitat sensitivity project intersected with Special Area of Conservation (SACs), SAC features and fishing activity. This sets the fisheries sensitivity work in the context of the Habitats Directive.   The outputs for Task 4 included one layer per gear category for each SAC feature, area outside SAC features and 12 nm zone outside SACs over which the activity occurred. The outputs were to be used by CCW to determine whether sensitive habitats were subject to fishing activity and whether these situations occurred within SACs and SAC features. The data could also be used as a baseline for discussions with fishermen.      As of April 1st, 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T10:21:51.058Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18040\tNRW_DS115271\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.793Z\t810\tValid\tEditable\tDistribution of Fisheries Habitats and Fishing Activity Project Outputs\t© CNC/NRW 2012 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323731\tAssociated data is held in the Marine Archive at Project 376, Media 1396   Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1396\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe objective of the associated project was to summarise how the fisheries habitats developed during the countryside Council for Wales (CCW) habitat sensitivity project intersected with Special Area of Conservation (SACs), SAC features and fishing activity. This sets the fisheries sensitivity work in the context of the Habitats Directive. The data used for this project was in the process of being revised therefore the aim of the project was to produce a toolkit which would allow the analysis to be repeated in future.   The outputs from this project included:  1. MapBasic scripts  2. Set of processed output layers  3. Set of Excel spreadsheet summarising the data  4. Overall report, archive appendix and technical appendix.   The outputs were to be used by CCW to determine whether sensitive habitats were subject to fishing activity and whether these situations occurred within SACs and SAC features. The data could also be used as a baseline for discussions with fishermen.  As of April 1st, 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T10:31:02.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18038\tNRW_DS115272\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.815Z\t599\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSensitive Marine Habitats Data Derived from Stereo Baited Remote Underwater Video System (2012)\t© CNC/ NRW and Welsh Biodiversity Partnership, Ecosystem Resilience and Diversity, and the Welsh Government, 2014 Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s and Welsh Biodiversity Partnership, Ecosystem Resilience and Diversity, and the Welsh Government copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323732\tData is Archived under project 398 media 1415 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1415)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset was collected using Baited Remote Underwater Video Systems (BRUV). This was the first known study of its kind in temperate waters and involved extensive method development. The BRUV surveys collected information on fish assemblages over three sensitive habitats (kelp, seagrass meadows and horse mussels). Surveys took places in the coastal waters off North Wales (Llyn Peninsula) and South Wales (Pembrokeshire), within Special Areas of Conservation as designated under the EU Habitats Directive. BRUVs were used as a non-destructive and non-invasive method to assess fish communities within Wales. This project was aimed as investigating the fish species assemblages present in each habitat type and determine the age structure of these.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:00.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18037\tNRW_DS115274\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.621Z\t662\tValid\tEditable\tSediment Core Sampling of Seagrass (Zostera) Bed and Sediment Areas in Porth Dinllaen\t© CNC/NRW 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323734\t[A] Archived as project 386, media 1401 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1401) [B] Marine Recorder (MRCCW31700000008) NBN file and Snapshot  This project follows on from volunteer P. Dinllaen work - carried out under Across Wales Diving, Project 229, media 598, 878 (File paths: K\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 598 and K:\\Archive\\Media 801to900\\Media 878) Also additional work carried out by Gwynedd Country Council (Lucy to send details) that will be archived separately\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tAs part of the management of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC), the SAC Officer has been working with the National Trust, local fishermen, boat owners and others to look at options to reduce the impact of moorings on the seagrass (Zostera marina) bed at Porth Dinllaen, North Wales.  This is a joint project between Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Gwynedd County Council as part of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC. As part of the Porth Dinllaen seagrass project a variety of studies are being undertaken to secure an appropriate baseline to establish a detailed picture of the current condition of the seagrass bed that can be used as a reference point to monitor recovery in the future as the project proceeds.  These were conducted to aid in the management of the seagrass bed (Zostera marina) present, listed as an important feature under Regulation 35 for the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation. This project was a component of a much larger venture which aims to better understand the local ecology and biology of Porth Dinllaen seagrass bed, with an ultimate goal of developing more seagrass friendly moorings and management techniques.   The project has been let in 4 stages: 1) The core and sediment samples and field notes were collected by MES on the 10th October  2) The cores were sent to Thomson Unicomarine for infauna and sediment analysis (By CCW)  3) The species data was sent to MES analysed in PRIMER  (Paid by GCC)  4) The analysed data was entered into Marine Recorder under the PKC data capture contract\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T10:34:16.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18036\tNRW_DS115275\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.514Z\t635\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Towed Video Seabed Surveys (2009 - 2011)\t© Bangor University 2011 NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323735\tArchived as Project 334, Media 1293 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1293)  Species data held in Marine Recorder [MRCCW3160000000A, MRCCW3160000000C, MRCCW3160000000D, MRCCW3160000000E]\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe primary aim of the survey work and reports by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats, and primarily whether \u0027stony reef\u0027 habitat was present and then to assess the effects of scallop dredging on those habitats. The secondary aim of this data capture was to improve seabed habitat mapping for Cardigan Bay and assign biotopes. Surveys of seabed in Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Bangor University in 2009, 2010 and 2011. Work included side-scan surveys (December 2009) grab sampling of sediments for particle size analysis (December 2009 and June 2010) and drop-down video and stills camera tows (all four surveys). The drop-down imagery dataset comprises: Survey 1 (December 2009): 72 tows (4743 stills); Survey 2 (June 2010): 59 tows (4720 stills); Survey 3 (December 2010): 13 tows (1364 stills); Survey 4 (April 2011): 47 tows (5347 stills). Video tows were approximately 10 minutes in length with still images taken every 10 seconds along the length of the tow. The videos and still images from the first two surveys were analysed by SOS and subsequently converted by Coastal Assessment Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to a format suitable for entry into Marine Recorder including assignment of biotopes. The videos and still images from the last two surveys were analysed by CALM to describe habitats species and biotopes. Grab samples were taken from each site during the first two surveys but only the sediment particle size analyses data from the first survey are included in this dataset. Sidescan survey data are not included with this dataset. The four surveys described were not directly associated with any other programme and no further work is currently planned.  However it is important to note that staff in the SOS are still working on the data collected during this survey and are planning to produce publications from it.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences\t2011-04-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:01.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18085\tNRW_DS115276\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.974Z\t288\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: European Dry Heath, Northern Atlantic Wet Heath and Blanket Bog monitoring at Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SAC\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2012. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence.  Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323736\tAssociated data for the 2006 monitoring report is held at NRW at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\29 European Dry Heath at Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SAC  Electrinic copies: Held on exCCW Document Store - Fynnon: Word Report  DCT-12-519992 Habitat Condition Assessment of the European Dry Heath, Northern Atlantic Wet Heath and Blanket Bog within the Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SAC 2012. Excel Spread Sheet of results: DCT-12-318185 SAC Monitoring 2012 Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt Dry Heath Wet Heath Blanket Bog Results\tData Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) monitoring is undertaken at the Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SAC in order to assess the condition of the three designated Annex I habitats present. Data for monitoring cycles 2006 and 2012 is available.  The purpose of this data capture is to assess the condition of European Dry Heath, Northern Atlantic Wet Heath and Blanket Bog habitats within the Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SAC.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:27:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18081\tNRW_DS115277\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.811Z\t834\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater Macrophyte Survey Data\t© CNC/NRW 2013. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323737\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the 17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A]  Data is archived as project 401, media no. 1418.  [B]  Species and abundance data is held within Recorder 6 as Macrophyte Survey of Mid-Wales Rivers 2013- CCW Science Report 1034 No. CCW000800000000K.\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW Data came from the core survey team of Sarah Clarke, Dianne Dobson and Rob Harrison.\tThe habitat \u0027H3260 Water Courses of Plain to Submontane Levels with the Ranunculion fluitantis and Callitricho-batrachion Vegetation\u0027 (henceforth \u0027Ranunculion habitat\u0027) is listed in Annexe I of the EU Habitat Directive as a habitat of European Importance. Four rivers in Wales, the Afon Gwyrfai, Afon Teifi, River Dee and River Wye are considered of European importance for this habitat type. A gap analysis of available data has indicated that there is a shortage of recent information on macrophyte community composition and extent in the Teifi and Upper Wye catchments. Therefore, these catchments have formed the focus of this study and data has been collected and analysed from these areas to determine whether the performance indicators for macrophyte community composition and extent for these rivers pass the required standard for extent and quality. The primary aim of this project is to produce an assessment of the status of the plant community component of the Ranunculion habitat on the Rivers Wye and Teifi. This dataset comprises of a survey entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway. Confidence in this dataset is high, all survey work was undertaken by a team of two experienced macrophyte surveyors.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18078\tNRW_DS115294\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.763Z\t567\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Shad Egg Survey River Usk and River Wye (2012)\t© CNC/NRW 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323934\t[A]  Data is archived as project 403, media 1420 [B]  Species data is held within Recorder 6 as Shad Egg Survey, River Usk and River Wye, 2012 - CCW - Report No. 13/8/3 CCW000800000000Q.\t\tThe River Usk and Wye are designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the EC 92/43/EEC Habitats Directive for several features including twaite shad (Alosa fallax) and allis shad (Alosa alosa). Shads are migratory members of the Clupeidae (herring family) that ascend rivers to spawn in late spring. The populations of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and issues surrounding water quality. In the UK, significant populations of shad spawn in the Rivers Severn, Usk, Wye and Tywi.  The River Usk and Wye have been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for shads and other features including salmon, lampreys and bullhead.  The purpose of this data capture was to assist with the assessment of  Favourable Conservation  Status along the Usk and Wye to supplement the main shad spawning distribution condiftion assessment for the Usk (previous surveys conducted in 2010 and 2011) and the Wye (previous surveys were conducted in 2011). 7 sites were chosen along the length of the River Usk and 10 along the length of the River Wye. Both surveys were conducted between high river flows events, and work was carried out by qualified Countryside Council for Wales monitoring staff.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18077\tNRW_DS115295\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.791Z\t327\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Function and Restoration of Biogenic Reefs Formed by Sabellaria alveolata\t© Natural Resources Wales (NRW) 2013.  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323935\tArchived as Project 404, Media 1421, 1425, 1426 and 1427\tGIS Service Desk Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist, NRW\tThe honeycomb worm Sabellaria alveolata is an example of a species that forms large well-consolidated reef structures. However, as the known area of S. alveolata covers less than 0.5 % of the coastline of the United Kingdom it is considered a fairly rare species. Individuals and reefs are typically found on the low to mid-shore of the intertidal zone, but have also been found in subtidal areas. In sufficient densities, individual tubes of S. alveolata coalesce to form biogenic structures that may reach several kilometres in length. These reefs can provide an important habitat for other benthic vertebrates and invertebrates (La Porta and Nicoletti 2009), as well as offering lesser studied benefits, including sediment entrainment/consolidation and potentially reducing wave energy transfer to the top of the shore. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the role S. alveolata has on coastal dynamics along with providing new baseline data directed towards the monitoring of the biogenic reefs.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences\t2013-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18083\tNRW_DS115296\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.803Z\t342\tValid\tEditable\tDrop-Down Video North Wales Survey 2012\t© CNC/NRW 2012. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323936\t[A] Associated data is held within NRW\u0027s Marine Archive as; Project 367, Media 1362 (File path is Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1362).    [B] Species data in Marine Recorder survey: MRCCW31600000009\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning\tDrop down video survey of seabed off the north coast of Pen Llyn and in Tremadog Bay, North Wales, carried out by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in 2012.    The dataset comprises of 26 video tows each of approximately 10 minutes in length with a set of still images that are taken approximately every 10 seconds along the length of the tow.    Videos and stills subsequently analysed by Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring, under contract to CCW, to describe habitats, species and biotopes.   The purpose of this data capture was to improve CCW\u0027s seabed habitat maps in those areas.  4 sites off the north coast of Pen Llyn, between Porth Dinllaen and Porth Colman, and 22 sites distributed around the northern half of Tremadog Bay.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T10:38:09.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18071\tNRW_DS115304\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.696Z\t1118\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHarvest Mice (Micromys minutus) in Wales - Where Might They Be?\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2013. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence.  Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333034\tAssociated data is held at; NRW corporate GIS (M:) drive (File path Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\ )  NB This data location is too imprecise. More detail is required.\tMammal Ecologist, NRW\tThe Harvest mouse (Micromys minutus), Britain’s smallest rodent, is sparsely distributed in Wales. The harvest mouse is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan priority species and is also on the list of Species of Principal Importance in Wales (Section 42, Natural Environment and Rural Communities Act 2006). Thus the species is afforded a high conservation status, yet very little is known about its true distribution across the UK and especially Wales.  This dataset is a collation of records obtained from a report written to question \u0027Harvest mice in Wales - where might they be?\u0027. The work carried out and demonstrated in the associated report (see related titles) was motivated by the lack of data for the harvest mouse in Wales, in an attempt to try and understand whether the species was as sparsely spread as the data would suggest. The results from analysis of the data would act to target harvest mouse survey to areas most likely to discover new populations and confirm existing ones. This dataset is made up of harvest mouse data obtained from the Welsh Local Records Centres, comprising 139 records dating back to 1895, with fewer than 30 records since 2000. Records are scattered across lowland areas in north, west and south Wales, forming obvious clusters in areas such as North Pembrokeshire, Bridgend and Eastern Glamorgan. The purpose of this data capture was to try gain an understanding of the distribution of harvest mouse around Wales. Various data were analysed alongside the harvest mouse records to highlight areas where potential harvest mouse surveys could be targeted. Maps were produced, to highlight sites with the highest habitat ranking around the most recent harvest mouse records.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18084\tNRW_DS115311\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.810Z\t631\tValid\tEditable\tMorphodynamic Modelled Outputs for the Dyfi Estuary\t© CNC/NRW and CAMS 2011. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/CAMS and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333131\tData is archived as project 407, 1428 (File path Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1401to1500\\Media1428)\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tA 4-month study, conducted by Bangor University for the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), to develop a high-resolution morphodynamic model of the Dyfi Estuary and surrounding area.   The purpose of this data capture was to collect data for modelling used to investigate the response of the estuary to future tidal and atmospheric changes. The model has also been used to investigate the response of the estuarine dynamics to potential management strategies. The work leads on from a previous study which developed an initial version of the Dyfi Model (Robins 2009, hereafter denoted the 2009 Dyfi Model). It was possible to improve the model resolution due to ever-increasing computer capabilities and data storage at Bangor University. There is a need for this Dyfi Estuary study to keep track with technological advances in modelling and to improve the accuracy of the simulations, broadening the possible applications of the model to coastal processes and coastal management scenarios.    Further information can be found in CCW Contract science report 1033.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Centre for Applied Marine Sciences (CAMS)\t2013-06-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T11:00:15.364Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18082\tNRW_DS115312\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.831Z\t646\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Afon Tywi Shad Egg Survey (2012)\t© CNC/NRW 2013 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333132\t[A]  Data is archived as project 408, media 1429.    [B]  Field notes are held in the Llandeilo Office and MFEG Office.   [C]  Species data is held within Recorder 6 as Shad egg Survey Afon Tywi 2012 - CCW - Report No. 12/8/4  CCW000800000000P\t\tThe Afon Tywi is designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the EC 92/43/EEC Habitats Directive for several features including twaite shad (Alosa fallax) and allis shad (Alosa alosa). Shads are migratory members of the Clupeidae (herring family) that ascend rivers to spawn in late spring. The populations of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and issues surrounding water quality. In the UK, significant populations of shad spawn in the Rivers Severn, Usk, Wye and Tywi.   Former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), Environment Agency Wales (EAW) and now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) is responsible for monitoring the condition of shad populations within SACs and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within these designated sites. The Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) published generic guidance on \u0027Common Standards Monitoring Guidance for Freshwater Fauna\u0027 (JNCC, 2005) outlining the attributes (mandatory and discretionary) and targets for monitoring freshwater fish. These include targets for shad population, river flow, water quality and river morphology.  The purpose of this data collection was to facilitate the determination of Favourable Conservation status of Shad in the Afon Tywi. Confidence in this dataset is high, as survey were undertaken by experienced staff members.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18079\tNRW_DS115314\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.804Z\t334\tValid\tEditable\tInfaunal Sampling of Sediment around the North Llyn Modiolus Bed (Additional Area) 2012\t© CNC/NRW 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333134\t[A] Data is archived as project 402, media 1419.   [B] Species data held in Marine Recorder [survey number:MRCCW3000000006C]\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tInfaunal and granulometery samples collected from sediment around the North Llyn modiolus beds.  Sampling was completed to inform decisions on the location of a potential fishing exclusions zone to prevent accidental incursions by fishing gear and smothering by resettlement of sediments suspended by fishing gear operating close to the bed. The work was mainly carried out to collect information about the sediment type in the area surrounding the \u0027new\u0027 Modiolus bed.  The data was also collected to inform discussions over the size of buffer area needed around the Modiolus bed for the towed fishing gears (trawls and dredges) exclusion area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-05-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T11:02:10.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18080\tNRW_DS115332\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.796Z\t417\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria alveolata Reefs in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2012\t© CNC/NRW 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333332\tData is archived as project 410, media 1430 and forms part of archive Project No 199.   This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2012 ASML PLAS.   Marine Recorder Survey MRCCW16300000038\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tPen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary ones, listed in Table 1.  Of these, Estuaries and their constituent mudflats, sandflats and reefs, large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds, populations of the shingle-dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus, piddocks in clay and lower shore boulder communities and Reefs, particularly biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata; are the main habitats of interest within the intertidal areas of the SAC.  Conservation objectives for these habitats are given in the Regulation 33 advice for the SAC (CCW 2009). The purpose of this data capture was to continue monitoring the intertidal area within the SAC.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T11:03:39.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18076\tNRW_DS115333\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.799Z\t621\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Mawddach Estuary Sediments in Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2012\t© CNC/NRW 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333333\t1) Data is archived as project 410, media 1430 (File path Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring) and forms part of archive Project No 199. 2) Marine Recorder Survey Number (MRCCW16300000044). 3) This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2012 ASML PLAS.   4) Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Pen Llyn SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tPen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC)  is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary ones.  Of these, Estuaries and their constituent mudflats, sandflats and reefs, large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds, populations of the shingle-dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus, piddocks in clay and lower shore boulder communities and Reefs, particularly biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata are the main habitats of interest within the intertidal areas of the SAC.  Conservation objectives for these habitats are given in the Regulation 33 advice for the SAC (CCW 2009).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T11:04:58.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18073\tNRW_DS115334\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.743Z\t386\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Bedrock at Porth Oer in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2012\t© CNC/NRW 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333334\tData is archived as project 410, media 1430 and forms part of archive Project No 199. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2012 ASML PLAS\tPaul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tPen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary ones. Of these, Estuaries and their constituent mudflats, sandflats and reefs, large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds, populations of the shingle-dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus, piddocks in clay and lower shore boulder communities and Reefs, particularly biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata are the main habitats of interest within the intertidal areas of the SAC.  Conservation objectives for these habitats are given in the Regulation 33 advice for the SAC (CCW 2009).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T11:07:04.061Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18068\tNRW_DS115378\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.672Z\t528\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring for the Afon Cleddau (2012)\t© CNC/NRW 2012 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333738\t[A]  Data is archived as project 415, media 1433. [B]  Species data is held within Recorder 6 as Survey Number CCW000800000000O.\t\tRiver lamprey (Lampetra fluviatilis), Brook lamprey (Lampetra planeri), and Sea lamprey (Petromyzon marinus) are listed in the Annex II of EU Habitats Directive (EU Directive on the Conservation of Natural Habitats of Wild Fauna and Flora 92/43/EEC) and 6 rivers in Wales are designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) for this feature.  The River Cleddau is one of the westernmost rivers in Britain and can be broadly divided into Eastern and Western arms. Assessments of species composition, distribution, abundance and size class composition are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment. The sampling strategy for the monitoring programme for sea, river and brook lamprey followed the protocols developed for the LIFE in UK Rivers project, was aimed at undertaking a condition assessment for the population of brook, river and sea lamprey designated features for the second Habitats Directive monitoring reporting cycle (2007-2012). 12 sites in the Eastern Cleddau and 13 sites in the Western Cleddau, Pembrokeshire.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18069\tNRW_DS115379\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.629Z\t766\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAll Wales Marsh Fritillary ( Euphydryas aurinia ) Habitat Quality Master Map\t© CNC/NRW and various Local Authorities and Contractors,  2013. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ and other contributors and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333739\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Users\\Adrian_f\\MapInfo Data\\Marsh Fritillary\\Habitat Quality Surveys\\All Wales\\Surveys\\All Wales\\All_Wales_surveys_Jun2013.TAB\tInternal Contact: Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW  ( Mike Howe ) Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team or Spatial Team.\tCompiled MapInfo layer of the condition of  habitat polygons for marsh fritillaries Euphydryas aurinia. The purpose of this data capture is to ascertain the extent and condition of habitat capable of supporting populations of marsh fritillaries across Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:57:58.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18070\tNRW_DS115409\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.673Z\t838\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rocky Reefs in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007-2010\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135343039\tProject 199, media 1436 Species data is held in Marine Recorder: 2007 CCW / ASML Pembrokeshire Marine SAC Rocky Reef Monitoring  MRCCW1630000000C 2008 CCW / ASML Pembrokeshire Marine SAC Rocky Reef Monitoring  MRCCW16300000013 2009 CCW / ASML Pembrokeshire Marine SAC Rocky Reef Monitoring  MRCCW16300000020 2010 CCW / ASML Pembrokeshire Marine SAC Rocky Reef Monitoring  MRCCW1630000002D  The file path for the report: H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\ No 101 - Pembrokeshire Marine 2007-2010 bedrock  The file path for the data and analyses: H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2010 ASML Pembs\\Data H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Analyses\\Pemb bedrock 2005-\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  In Pembrokeshire Marine SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features include Reefs. The specific area sub-feature of interest covered here are intertidal rocky reefs at six sites in Milford Haven together with one site at Nolton Haven in St Bride’s Bay and another site at Peny-Holt on the Castlemartin peninsula. The purpose of this data capture was to undertake quantitative monitor of the biota colonising sloping emersed bedrock and colonising rockpools.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:15.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18074\tNRW_DS115460\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.689Z\t427\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring Data for River Teifi 2012/2013\t© CNC/NRW, 2013.   There are no use restrictions on non landowner data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135343630\tData is archived as project 422, media 1440\tInternal Custodian: Terrestrial Environmental Evidence Officer, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe River Teifi has been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the EU Habitats Directive. The three species of lamprey for which the data was collected are listed under Annex II of the Habitats Directive. Data was collect to determine the habitat condition and suitability of habitats for the lamprey, as well as make recommendations for future surveys.  Data was collected to recognized standards and data presented using a GIS data. The data was collected to determine habitat conditions for lamprey in the Afon Teifi catchment. It involved collating information on age structure, distribution within the catchment, ammocoete density, spawning activity, and barriers to migration.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18067\tNRW_DS115512\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.662Z\t351\tValid\tEditable\tHistoric Aerial Imagery to Monitor Temporal Change in Intertidal Habitats\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2013. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353132\tData archived as project 430, media 1467\tPaul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tLong-term and broad-scale datasets are essential to any investigation of change in natural communities. This dataset includes the collation of aerial imagery for 44 sites around the Welsh coastline, selected to contain rocky shore, saltmarsh or Sabellaria alveolata across temporal scales providing suitable data for comparisons of change through time, both anthropogenic and biological.   In order for comparisons to present day imagery, current aerial imagery was also obtained in this study. Photographic aerial images containing features of conservation value (specifically rocky shores, Sabellaria alveolata inhabited shores and saltmarsh areas) within Wales were obtained. Historic photographic aerial images that contained features of conservation value (specifically rocky shores, Sabellaria alveolata inhabited shores and saltmarsh areas) within Wales were selected from expert knowledge and Countryside Council for Wales\u0027s collection of habitat maps.    The aim of this project was to collate, digitise and georeference aerial photographic imagery for sites that had multi-year overlap. Accuracies of less than 5 m RMS was obtained (with the exception of Treath Lafan, 1995, and the Dyfi Estuary, 1975). At these saltmarsh locations, there were too few ground control points to achieve this accuracy.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T11:08:59.308Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18075\tNRW_DS115518\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.744Z\t742\tValid\tEditable\tBardsey Seal Counts\t© CNC/NRW and RSPB. NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the RSPB. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353138\tArchived within the Marine Archive as Project No. 396, Media No. 1413\tInternal Custodian: Tom Stringell, Marine and Coastal Advice Team, NRW.   RSPB Custodian: Jo Porter\tGrey seals Halichoerus grypus are among the rarest seals in the world: the UK population represents about 40% of the world population and 95% of the EU population. Globally, there are three reproductively-isolated stocks of grey seal: a west Atlantic (northern North American) stock; a Baltic stock; and an East Atlantic stock. The latter extends from Iceland and northern Norway southwards to northern France, with the majority breeding around Great Britain and Ireland.    The extent of connectivity within the Irish Sea grey seal (Halichoerus grypus) population has been investigated every year since 2006 using  photo-identification and bio-logging techniques.  The purpose of this data capture was to survey Bardsey seal population fluctuations. Surveys were undertaken by both trained staff and volunteers.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)\t2013-01-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T12:16:22.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18066\tNRW_DS115520\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.452Z\t424\tValid\tEditable\tAcross Wales Lagoon Monitoring - Carew Mill Pond (2012)\t© CNC/NRW 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353230\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 241 and Media No\u0027s 1337.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting (Matt Green?),  NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset is a collation of data from subtidal benthic soft sediment sampling carried out within Carew Mill Pond lagoon, which is a feature of the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The data is unpublished. The purpose of this data capture is to aid in the monitoring and condition assessment of this Annex I feature.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T12:18:10.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18072\tNRW_DS115521\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.738Z\t820\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSwansea Bay and Carmarthen Bay Beach profiles data (Lavernock to St Anns Head Shoreline Management Plan (SMP2))\t© NRW, 2015 Third party data Some of this data maybe restricted. Contact Data Services for more information.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353231\tData is archived as project 432, media 1469.\tCeri Seaton       need to understabd the T\u0026amp;C under which this data was provided.  May be for internal use only\tThe purpose of this data capture was to provide a review of the background geological factors and physical processes which is intended to inform the assessment of policy options in the second generation Shoreline Management Plan for the area between St. Ann\u0027s Head and Lavernock Point.   This dataset describes the geological and physical processes occurring at Swansea and Carmarthen Bay. Processes investigated include beach profile, beach topographic survey, LiDAR cross sections, summary characteristics, tide data and weather data. Two related reports were written within the same project Annex A and Annex B, Annex B builds on the earlier review and presents further analysis of data. Particular attention has been given to the estuaries and intervening shorelines in Swansea Bay and Carmarthen Bay. Three sections of open coast associated with the estuaries were also considered:  - The Pendine to Ginst Point barrier complex (including Pendine Burrows and Laugharne Burrows) - The Tywyn Point to Pembrey Burrows barrier complex - The Hills Tor to Whiteford Point barrier complex (including Whiteford Burrows).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T10:30:17.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18064\tNRW_DS115523\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.022Z\t465\tValid\tEditable\tAcross Wales Lagoon Monitoring - Neyland Wier (Westfield Pill) Pool 2012\t© CNC/NRW 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353233\t[A] Data is archived as Project No. 241, Media No. 1338.    [B] For further details of analyses see: Analysis of macrofaunal and sediment samples. Analytical Report Report Number: J36221-1 available in NRW Marine Archive: Project Number 241 Media Number 1338. [C] Species and PSA data is also available in Marine Recorder [MRCCW3000000007A]\tInternal Custodian: NRW Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team (Matt Green?)  NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset is a collation of data from subtidal benthic soft sediment sampling carried out within Neyland Wier lagoon, which is a feature of the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC).   The purpose of this data capture was to aid in the monitoring and condition assessment of this Annex I feature\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T12:20:01.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18065\tNRW_DS115525\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.016Z\t426\tValid\tEditable\tAcross Wales Drop-Down Video Monitoring: Additional North Llyn Ground Truthing (2012)\t© CNC/NRW 2012. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353235\t[A]  Data is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 366, Media No. 1361 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400),   [B]  Videos are maintained in the archive  [C]  Marine Recorder Survey  - MRCCW3170000000A\tInternal Contact: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.     NRW.Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tDrop down video ground-truthing of additional Modiolus bed discovered partially outside the Special Area of Conservation (SAC).  This  follows on from previous ground-truthing carried out in 2011 (Marine Archive project 313). Analysis and Marine Recorder entry to be undertaken later in the year under Data Capture call-off contract.    The purpose of this data capture was to conduct a ground truthing exercise through which the extent and quality of Modiolus mussel beds could be assessed in an area of the coast of the Llyn Peninsula, North Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-06-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T12:21:27.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18063\tNRW_DS115526\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.002Z\t471\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Monitoring Drop-Down Video around North Llyn Special Area of Conservation ( SAC ) 2012\t© CNC/NRW 2013. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353236\tData is Archived as Project No. 365, Media 1360.  (Full pathway: K:\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1360) All species and habitat data is available in Marine Recorder [ MRCCW31700000005 \u0026amp; MRCCW3170000000B].   This monitoring is a follow on from data collected under archive project No. 35.\tInternal Custodian - CCW Marine Monitoring Ecologist.  Suggested NRW contact is Charles Lindenbaum, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team.   NRW Metadata contact - Data Services\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs) are strictly protected sites designated under the EC Habitats Directive. The Habitats Directive includes a list of habitat types and species that are considered to be most in need of conservation at a European level (excluding birds, which are covered under the EC Birds Directive). Once sites have been designated, the country agencies work with owners, occupiers and others to secure their management and protection regime.    This dataset provides a continuation of data collected from Drop Down Video Monitoring in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC.  The purpose of this data capture was to inform management decisions within the SACs. QA was undertaken including 5% of tows being re-analysed by a different surveyor and QA of at least 10% of data entry. Analysis and QA undertaken by Marine EcoSol.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-03-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:33:04.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18060\tNRW_DS115528\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.822Z\t795\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDrop Down Video Data Caernarfon Bay, Tremadog Bay, Cardigan Bay and west Anglesey (2012)\tFor datasets the dataset owned by NRW (MRCCW3170000000E - 2005 CCW Tremadog Bay sublittoral sediment survey)  the following applies.   © CNC/NRW, 2014.  For all other datasets listed below, the following applies:  © Bangor University 2012 and © Natural Resources Wales NRW have received permission to release, publish or disseminate the data freely, NRW does NOT grant permission to disseminate or re-use this data. Those seeking such permission must approach the originator directly.  Listed Datasets MRCCW31700000010 - 2012 Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Scallop Survey. Cardigan Bay, June. MRCCW31700000012 - 2012 Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Habitat Survey: West Anglesey, May.  MRCCW31700000013 - 2012 Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Habitat Survey: Caernarfon Bay, October. MRCCW3170000000D - 2012 Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Scallop Survey. Tremadog Bay, June.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353238\tData is archived as Project No. 387, Media 1402 Working area H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\drop down video\\2012\\SOS data\tData Custodian: Marine Monitoring Ecologist, NRW. Marine Recorder system custodian: Master Reference Data Team Metadata Point of contact: Master Reference Data Team\tBangor University School of Ocean Science undertook video surveys during a research programme. This data was provided to former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) so CCW could analyse the video and still images for species and habitat information. This data was then collated and entered into Marine Recorder. The purpose of this data capture and collation was to help build an evidence base on which to create informed management decisions within the marine environment. This data allows for a better Bangor University has given former CCW permission to hold, reuse and release this data as appropriate.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-University of Wales Bangor\t2012-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:02:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18062\tNRW_DS115537\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.006Z\t383\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring for the River Wye (2012)\t© CNC/NRW and Environment Agency and Dwr Cymr 2014 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ EA and Dwr Cymru/Welsh Water do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353337\tArchived as project number 434 Media Number: 1471\tInternal Custodian: Freshwater Monitoring Coordinator, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW.\tThe River Wye has been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The data supports a condition assessment for the population of brook, river and sea lamprey (protected under Annex II of the EU Habitats Directive 92/43/EEC) for the second Habitats Directive monitoring report cycle (2007-2012). This dataset combines the survey results from our lamprey surveys conducted by the former Environment Agency Wales (now part of Natural Resources Wales) and Dwr Cymru/Welsh Water. This data collection was required as part of the second Habitats Directive monitoring report cycle (2007-2012). 48 sites were surveyed along the following water courses; Afon Elan, Aran, Bidno, Camddwr-Irfon, Cammarch, Chwerfri, Cledan, Cnyffiad, Duhonw, Dulas Brook, Edw, Garth Dulas - Irfon, Gwenwst, Hafrena, Irfon, Llunfi, Marteg, Nant Gwesyn, Nant y Dernol, Sgithwen bridge, South Dulas - Irfon, Wye.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:14:00.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18061\tNRW_DS115547\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.833Z\t910\tValid\tEditable\tSevern Estuary Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Special Protection Area (SPA): Intertidal Mudflats and Sandflats Condition Assessment 2012\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2012. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353437\tData archived as Project 435, Media 1472 (File path:  K:\\Archive\\Media1401to1500\\Media1472).   Species and habitat data is available in Marine Recorder through Natural England Snapshot  [MRNE013700000005]\tPaul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer), Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting.\tThe Severn Estuary is the largest example of a coastal plain estuary in the United Kingdom. It has been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the EU Habitats Directive, a Special Protection Area (SPA) under the Birds Directive and as a Ramsar site under the Convention on Wetlands of International Importance.    The primary focus of this study relates to monitoring the intertidal mudflats and sandflats feature of the Severn Estuary SAC by assessing the relevant attributes for the purpose of condition assessment. However, monitoring of prey availability within the Severn Estuary SPA was also a secondary objective.    The survey was undertaken in three blocks between the 13th October and 14th November 2012.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Natural England\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T12:26:43.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18059\tNRW_DS115548\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.820Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSeal pelage and Photo ID Monitoring Wales (2005-2013)\t© CNC/NRW, 2014. The output data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353438\tData is archived as Project No. 395, Media No. 1412  Other information: The majority of the images are created by CCW.  Some images are suppplied to CCW for use within the project by volunteers and other third parties/partners. Images are also supplied to CCW through Flicker.  The ExtractCompare software used as part of this project is held on DELL1407.\tInternal Custodian: Tom Stringell, Senior Marine Mammal ecologist, Natural resources planning/Marine and freshwater ecosystem group,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW       This entry will be taken out of public view and a new metadata entry will be created for the latest version of the database.  This entry is retained internally as the data is retained as a snapshot in time.  New metadata entry 122202.\tThis dataset is a catalogue of seal images were collected as part of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW) seal monitoring work. Specific seal pelage surveys and images provided by volunteers and members of the public were also collected through NRWs Flicker account. These images were used to monitor behaviour and populations of seals around north Wales.  Photo-ID allowed these pelages to be captured and analysed, which allows a catalogue of individuals to be constructed. This was then used to estimate the population size and movements. By repeating the photo-ID on an annual basis matches can be re-made giving an indication of a seals fidelity to a particular site and associations between individuals.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-23T09:38:20.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18058\tNRW_DS115549\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.673Z\t361\tValid\tEditable\tFishMap Môn Detailed Fishing Intensity Levels\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2013. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353439\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Intensity\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists).   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW), recreational and commercial fishing sectors, and the Welsh Government. The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and combining it with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activity.   Fishing Intensity is described as the effort of a fishing activity in a given area. For example, this could be the number of pots hauled per day per hectare. It is also described as one of three levels, but the actual intensity value is derived differently for each fishing type.  The purpose of this data capture was to examine the spatial extent of fishing activities and the determine the habitats affected by different fishing gear types in an area around Anglesey.   The data consists of output generated for nine different gear types - King Scallop Dredging, Queen Scallop Dredging, Mussel Seed Dredging, Light Otter Trawling, Bottom Set Nets, Potting, Sea Angling and Casual and Professional Hand Gathering. There are multiple outputs for Potting and Sea Angling for commercial and recreational sectors. From the attribute \\_overlaps\\\"  it is possible to determine the number of vessels/ fishers operating in a particular area.   Due to the sensitivity of fishing intensity related data two versions of the FishMap Môn intensity level data were created both a detailed and generalised version. This detailed version of the dataset shows the precise locations of the fishing activities and the data also contains specific information about the fishing activities and so is restricted to the FishMap Môn Team and certain partners - contact the dataset owner for more information. \"\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-05-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T13:44:53.201Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18057\tNRW_DS115551\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.627Z\t656\tValid\tEditable\tFishMap Môn Habitat Sensitivity Confidence\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2013. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353531\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Sensitivity ,   Legacy CCW Archive:  M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\FishMapMon\\FMM_Data\\MapInfo_Tab\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists).  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team,\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW), the Welsh Government, and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors. Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited, Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers.   The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities.   Habitat Sensitivity Confidence represents the confidence NRW has had in the expert judgement of the Habitat Sensitivity Assessment from a fishing gear type.  The data consists of output generated for nine different gear types - King Scallop Dredging, Queen Scallop Dredging, Mussel Seed Dredging, Light Otter Trawling, Bottom Set Nets, Potting, Sea Angling and Casual and Professional Hand Gathering.  Due to it\u0027s nature as a pilot project NRW no longer makes this data routinely available, alternative fisheries related data may be available on the Welsh Government Marine Planning Portal.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:23:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18056\tNRW_DS115553\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.551Z\t830\tValid\tEditable\tFishMap Môn Generalised Fishing Intensity Levels\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2013. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353533\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\FishMapMon\\FMM_Data\\MapInfo_Tab\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists). Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRWCrown Copyright and Database Right.\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW), the Welsh Government, and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors. Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited, Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers.   The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities. The purpose of this data capture was to examine the spatial extent of fishing activities and the determine the habitats affected by different fishing gear types in an area around Anglesey.   Fishing Intensity is described as the effort of a fishing activity in a given area. For example, this could be the number of pots hauled per day per hectare. Fishing intensity is described as one of three levels, but the actual intensity value is derived differently for each fishing type. The data consists of output generated for nine different gear types - King Scallop Dredging, Queen Scallop Dredging, Mussel Seed Dredging, Light Otter Trawling, Bottom Set Nets, Potting, Sea Angling and Casual and Professional Hand Gathering.   There are multiple outputs for Potting and Sea Angling for commercial and recreational sectors From the attribute \\_overlaps\\\"  it is possible to determine the number of vessels/ fishers operating in a particular area. Due to the sensitivity of fishing intensity related data two versions of the FishMap Môn intensity level data were created both a detailed and generalised version. The detailed version is sensitive and therefore has restrictions accompanying the dataset however this generalised version does not.\"  Due to it\u0027s nature as a pilot project NRW no longer makes this data routinely available, alternative fisheries related data may be available on the Welsh Government Marine Planning\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:09:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18103\tNRW_DS115554\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.045Z\t435\tValid\tEditable\tFishMap Môn Detailed Habitat Sensitivity Levels\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2013. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353534\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Habitat\\\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists). Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRWCrown Copyright and Database Right.\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW), the Welsh Government, and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors. Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited, Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers.   The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities.   This data is related to habitat sensitivity. Habitat Sensitivity is based on the resistance and resilience of a habitat to a particular fishing intensity from a fishing gear type. Sensitivity is recorded as Low, Medium or High. The data consists of output generated for nine different gear types - King Scallop Dredging, Queen Scallop Dredging, Mussel Seed Dredging, Light Otter Trawling, Bottom Set Nets, Potting, Sea Angling and Casual and Professional Hand Gathering. There are multiple outputs for Potting and Sea Angling for commercial and recreational sectors.    The purpose of this data capture was to examine the spatial extent of fishing activities and the determine the habitats affected by different fishing gear types in an area around Anglesey. A 12 nautical mile buffered zone around the Isle of Anglesey / Ynys Mon, extending from Nefyn in the West, to Colwyn Bay in the East.    Due to the sensitivity of fishing intensity related data two versions of the Fishmap Môn habitat sensitivity level data were created, both a detailed and generalised version. This detailed version of the dataset shows the precise locations of the fishing activities, and the data also contains specific information about the fishing activities, and so is restricted to the FishMap Môn Team and certain partners - contact the dataset owner for more information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-05-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18101\tNRW_DS115555\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.911Z\t407\tValid\tEditable\tFishMap Môn Generalised Habitat Sensitivity Levels\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2013. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353535\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\FishMapMon\\FMM_Data\\MapInfo_Tab\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists). Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRWCrown Copyright and Database Right.   Metadata Custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW), the Welsh Government, and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors. Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited, Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers.   The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities.    Habitat Sensitivity is based on the resistance and resilience of a habitat to a particular fishing intensity from a fishing gear type. Sensitivity is recorded as Low, Medium or High. The data consists of output generated for nine different gear types - King Scallop Dredging, Queen Scallop Dredging, Mussel Seed Dredging, Light Otter Trawling, Bottom Set Nets, Potting, Sea Angling and Casual and Professional Hand Gathering. There are multiple outputs for Potting and Sea Angling for commercial and recreational sectors.   Due to the sensitivity of fishing intensity related data two versions of the FishMap Môn habitat sensitivity level data were created, both a detailed and generalised version. The detailed version of the dataset shows the precise locations of the fishing activities, and the data also contains specific information about the fishing activities, and so is restricted to the FishMap Môn Team and certain partners. However this generalised version is shown at a coarser scale and is available for release.  Due to it\u0027s nature as a pilot project NRW no longer makes this data routinely available, alternative fisheries related data may be available on the Welsh Government Marine Planning\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18112\tNRW_DS115556\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.064Z\t754\tValid\tEditable\tFishMap Môn Fishing Activity Combined with Seabed Habitat Sensitivity\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2013. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353536\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Combined Seabed Habitat Lines.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Combined Seabed Habitat Regions.lyr ,  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\FishMapMon\\FMM_Data\\MapInfo_Tab\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists).  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW.\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW), the Welsh Government, and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors.   Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited, Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers. The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities.    The purpose of this data capture was to examine the spatial extent of fishing activities and the determine the habitats affected by different fishing gear types in an area around Anglesey. Confidence in the data used for habitat types varied depending on the source of the data. The confidence in the Phase 1 habitat data is good, however confidence in subtidal data from the HABMAP program was assigned a confidence level of Very Poor, Poor, Fair and Good.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:59.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18104\tNRW_DS115560\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.035Z\t661\tValid\tEditable\tA Survey of Moorings and their Impact on Seagrass Beds at Porth Dinllaen (2012)\t© CNC/NRW and Gwynedd Council 2012. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/Gwynedd Council and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353630\tData is located as Project 386, media 1475 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1475)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tPorth Dinllaen has one of the largest and densest seagrass beds within Wales. As with the majority of the United Kingdom the seagrass bed at Porth Dinllaen is typified by one species, Zostera marina. The seagrass beds at Porth Dinllaen spans over 1 km in length and up to 650 m wide in places, throughout the inner and outer harbour areas. Previous work has found that boat moorings create distinct radial scour patterns within the Z. marina.   As part of the management of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation(SAC), the SAC Officer has been working with the National Trust, Relevant Authorities, local fishermen, boat owners and other stakeholders to look at options to reduce the impact of the moorings on Z. marina at Porth Dinllaen. In 2012 surveys were conducted within the outer harbour using volunteer SCUBA divers.   The aims of the 2012 surveys were to record the mooring number and designs used within Porth Dinllaen outer harbour, and assess how these moorings relate to the density of Z. marina, adjacent Z. marina canopy height, and presence of wasting. The work was carried out under the Ecosystem Resilience and Diversity Fund.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Gwynedd Council - Cyngor Gwynedd\t2013-05-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:42:08.141Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18110\tNRW_DS115569\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.044Z\t297\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFisher Towed Survey in Cardigan Bay 2012\tVideo Footage and Images: © School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Species Data: © CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353639\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466.\tGIS Service Desk  External Custodian: Michael Kaiser, School of Ocean Science, Bangor University Formerly: Dr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University\tSurveys of seabed in Cardigan Bay, carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS), Bangor University, in October 2012, as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  The main aim of the survey work was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats, and primarily whether cobble reef habitat was present, as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:45.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18107\tNRW_DS115570\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.039Z\t573\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Liverpool Bay (2012)\tVideo Footage and Images: © School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Species Data: © CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353730\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500) Species data held in Marine Recorder (Survey numbers  MRCCW31600000012 and MRCCW31600000013.\tGIS Service Desk  Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Contact: Mike Kaiser or Dr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University.\tThis Natural Resources Wales (NRW) owned dataset contains the species data associated with drop down video survey conducted by Bangor University. Surveys of seabed in Liverpool Bay, carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS), Bangor University, in June and October 2012, as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (9 tows) and associated still images collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog, using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was between 8 and 22 km offshore, north east of  Anglesey.  The tow lengths ranged from 100m to 500m and seabed consisted primarily of sands, gravels and pebbles with some areas of cobbles.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes. The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats, as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging.  The aim of the subsequent data capture was to improve Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) seabed habitat mapping for Liverpool Bay and assign biotopes. CCW now forms part of NRW.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:29:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18109\tNRW_DS115571\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.961Z\t389\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Cardigan Bay, West of Dyfi (2012)\tVideo Footage and Images: © School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Species Data: © CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353731\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1466) Species data held in Marine Recorder (Survey key:MRCCW31600000015)\tGIS Service Desk  Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis dataset consists of the results of a surveys of seabed in Cardigan Bay, carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS), Bangor University, in June 2012, as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (3 tows) and associated still images collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog, using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was approximately 25 km offshore, west of Dyfi.  The tow lengths ranged from 500m to 750m and seabed consisted primarily of sands, gravels and cobble/boulder reef.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes. The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats, as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging. The aim of the subsequent data capture was to improve CCW\u0027s seabed habitat mapping for Cardigan Bay and assign biotopes.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:23:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18111\tNRW_DS115572\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.038Z\t432\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Cardigan Bay (2013)\tVideo Footage and Images: © School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Species Data: © CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353732\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500) Data is stored in Marine Recorder (survey numbers MRCCW31600000010 and MRCCW31600000011)\tGIS Service Desk  Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team        Part of this dataset is sensitive and restricted. The sensitive data is as follows: Pecten maximus and Aequipecten opercularis. Sensitive data must be kept confidential; general release is not permitted due to risk of harm to the environment or third parties.\tSurveys of seabed in Cardigan Bay, carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS), Bangor University, in July 2013, as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (42 tows (24 in Work Package 1b and 18 in Work Package 2b) and associated still images collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog, using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was between 2 and 22 km offshore of the coast between Cardigan and New Quay.  The tow lengths ranged from 160m to 1800m and seabed consisted primarily of sands, gravels and pebbles with some areas of cobbles and boulders.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes.  The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats, as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging. The aim of the subsequent data capture was to improve CCW\u0027s seabed habitat mapping for Cardigan Bay and assign biotopes.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:29:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18099\tNRW_DS115574\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.894Z\t396\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Liverpool Bay (2013)\tVideo Footage and Images: © School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Species Data: © CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353734\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1466) Marine Recorder Database:  2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey Liverpool Bay, August MRCCW31600000014\tGIS Service Desk  Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Custodian: (Mike Kaiser) Dr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University.\tSurveys of seabed in Liverpool Bay, carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS), Bangor University, in August 2013, as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (9 tows) collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog, using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was between 8 and 27 km offshore, north of Bangor to Colwyn Bay. The tow lengths ranged from 216 m to 611 m and seabed consisted primarily of sands and gravels with some areas of pebbes.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes.  The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats, as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:29:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18106\tNRW_DS115575\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.030Z\t370\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Caernarfon Bay (2013)\tVideo Footage and Images: © School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Species Data: © CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353735\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1466) Marine Recorder survey: 2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Caernarfon Bay, July MRCCW31600000016\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Custodian: (Mike Kaiser) Dr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences,  Bangor University.\tSurveys of seabed in Caernarfon Bay, carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS), Bangor University, in July 2013, as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (11 tows) and associated still images collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog, using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was between 8 and 25 km offshore, in the centre of Caernarfon Bay.  The tow lengths ranged from 150m to 500m and seabed consisted primarily of sands and gravels with some pebbles and cobbles.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes. The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats, as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging.  The aim of the subsequent data capture was to improve CCW\u0027s seabed habitat mapping for Caernarfon Bay and assign biotopes.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:11.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18115\tNRW_DS115576\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.103Z\t615\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Tremadog Bay (2013)\tVideo Footage and Images: © School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Species Data: © CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353736\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1466. Marine Recorder: 2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Tremadog Bay, July MRCCW31600000017\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Custodian: (Mike Kaiser)  Dr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences,  Bangor University.\tSurveys of seabed in Tremadog Bay, carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS), Bangor University, in July 2013, as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (1 tow) and associated still images collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog, using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was about 6 km offshore of Abersoch.  The tow length was 430m and seabed consisted primarily of coarse sand.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes. The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats, as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging.  The aim of the subsequent data capture was to improve CCW\u0027s seabed habitat mapping for Tremadog Bay and assign biotopes. High - experienced surveyors followed by experienced video and stills analysis.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:29:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18105\tNRW_DS115577\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.031Z\t699\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: White Clawed Crayfish in River Wye SAC (2003)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353737\tHeld at H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports and Datasets\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW.  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe inclusion of the white-clawed crayfish on Annex II of the EU Habitats and Species Directive requires Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to identify Special Areas of Conservation for the species and to ensure that Favourable Conservation Status (FCS) applies. The purpose of this data capture was to undertake monitoring of the white-clawed crayfish and its habitat on the River Wye candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) in order to report on condition as part of the assessment of Favourable Conservation Status for Natura 2000 features.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18097\tNRW_DS115578\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.846Z\t442\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: European Dry Heath in Cwm Doethie-Mynydd Mallaen candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2003)\t© CNC/NRW 2004 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353738\tAssociated data is held on the H:/ at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\03 Cwm Doethie European Dry heath\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Cwm Doethie-Mynydd Mallaen candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) supports dry heath and sessile oak woodland, both of which are features of international importance. This dataset includes data collected during a survey and monitoring programme of the dry heath feature at Cwm Doethie-Mynydd Mallaen cSAC, undertaken in 2003 and 2004.  The purpose of the contract was to assess the condition, extent and distribution of the dry heath or potential dry heath within the boundary of the cSAC and to monitor the condition of selected plots against a set of performance indicators. The monitoring result would establish the present condition of the feature.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:32:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18108\tNRW_DS115580\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.968Z\t482\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Northern Atlantic Wet Heath, European Dry Heath and Raised Bog Habitats within Cernydd Carmel candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC)\t© CNC/NRW 2004. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353830\tAssociated data is held on the H:/ at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\04 Erica Tetralix Cernydd Carmel cSAC\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW.\tThis dataset includes data collected from an assessment of the extent and condition of three Annex I habitats of the EC Habitats Directive at the Cernydd Carmel cSAC; namely North Atlantic Wet Heaths with Erica tetralix, European Dry Heaths (all subtypes) and Active Raised Bogs. Two principle objectives were defined: 1. To carry out an initial condition survey of the Annex I listed habitats using condition classes provided by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff 2. To monitor the condition of the Annex I listed habitats against performance indicator criteria provided by former CCW staff\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:04.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18098\tNRW_DS115581\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.909Z\t637\tValid\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the Afon Teifi Catchment Area (2004)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353831\tData is held at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\05 Otter in the Afon Teifi\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales (Aberystwyth) to investigate the current and potential breeding success of the otter (Lutra lutra) in the Afon Teifi Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and catchment; report on the present condition and conservation status of the species; define conservation objectives; and to design an efficient monitoring methodology for future reporting. The aims of the project are: a) To report on the current and potential distribution and breeding success of the otter in the Afon Teifi cSAC and catchment; b) To produce conservation objectives using data obtained from the project; c) To design an efficient monitoring methodology for future reporting.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T07:45:44.054Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18102\tNRW_DS115583\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.025Z\t777\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDistribution of the Invasive Non Native Species (INNS) American slipper limpet Crepidula fornicata in Conwy Estuary (2012)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353833\tData is archived as project 442, media 1482 The file path for the report: H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Non-native species\\Alien Crepidula   The file path for the data and analyses: H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Non-native species\\Alien Crepidula\\Data and GIS  The file path for the archive: W:\\Media1401to1500\\Media1482\tInternal Custodian: Ben Wray, Marine Monitoring Officer, MFSG.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Menai Strait and Conwy estuary area is a designated Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in Wales. The Habitats Directive establishes that the management of SACs should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I habitats and Annex II species. Crepidula fornicata, the American slipper limpet is an invasive non native species in Wales. The slipper limpet occurs on a variety of substrates, it is often found attached to the shells of mussels, Mytilus edulis as well as oysters, stones and other shells around the low water mark. This species is found in south Wales, especially the Milford Haven area, with few records north of the Haven. The Menai Strait mussel fishery in north Wales is the largest in the UK. The mussel ‘seed’ is imported from outside the area. The problem with importing the seed from elsewhere is that there is the risk of accidentally introducing the invasive slipper limpet. This was the case in 2006 when C. fornicata escaped into the Menai Strait. The purpose of this data capture was to assess spread of Crepidula fornicate in North Wales by assessing the Conwy estuary.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-08-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18114\tNRW_DS115584\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.979Z\t285\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Monitoring Raised Mire Vegetation at Cors Caron candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2004)\t© CNC/NRW 2004. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353834\tAssociated data is held within the folloring location within Maes-y-Ffynnon\u0027s IT Sytem; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\06 Raised mire vegetation Cors Caron cSAC\tInternal custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset was collected at Cors Caron candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC), in partial fulfilment of former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Contract FC 73-05-35 \\Monitoring of Raised Mire Vegetation at Cors Fochno and Cors Caron cSAC\\\". A second report outlines the results of monitoring work undertaken at Cors Fochno. The contract was commissioned to aid the assessment of the extent and condition of three Annex 1 habitats listed in the EC \\\"Habitats Directive\\\" (Council of the European Communities 1992): Degraded raised bogs still capable of natural regeneration; Transition mires and quaking bogs; Depressions on peat substrates of the Rhynchosporion;\"\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:46.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18113\tNRW_DS115587\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.984Z\t677\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Vertigo geyeri in Waun Eurad and Cors Erddreiniog SACs (2003-2007)\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353837\tAssociated data for 2003 is held within Maes-y-Ffynnon\u0027s IT systems as H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\08 Vertigo Geyeri Cors Waun Eurad \u0027ampisand\u0027 Cors Erddreiniog  Associated data for 2007 monitoring is held at Maes-y-Ffynnon at - H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\42 Vertigo Geyeri Cors Waun Eurad \u0026amp; Cors Erddreiniog\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tGeyers whorl Snail, Vertigo geyeri is listed on Annex II of the European Union Habitats and Species Directive, classified in the British Red Data Book as RDB1 (Endangered), and is on the list of priority species of the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP). The purpose of this study was to undertake monitoring of the Vertigo geyeri population at Waun Eurad and Cors Erddreiniog, in order to carry out a condition assessment and to determine the attributes that will be assessed by future monitoring, which is a requirement of the EU Habitats and Species Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:03.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18096\tNRW_DS115594\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.845Z\t361\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Vertigo moulinsiana in Cors Geirch, Corsydd Llyn/Lleyn Fens cSAC (2004)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353934\tThe associated data is held on the H:/ at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\09 Vertigo moulinsiana  in Cors Geirch, Corsydd Llyn\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tDesmoulin\u0027s whorl snail, Vertigo moulinsiana, is listed in the British Red Data Book as an RDB3 (Rare) species, is scheduled on Annex II of the European Habitats and Species Directive and is included on the UK Biodiversity Action Plan short-list of priority species. The purpose of this study was to undertake monitoring of the Vertigo moulinsiana population at Cors Geirch in order to determine the thresholds necessary to carry out a condition assessment, which is a requirement of the EU Habitats and Species Directive. The only data held aside from the associated report is photographic images that are also held within the report itself.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:23:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18092\tNRW_DS115595\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.157Z\t725\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Freshwater Pearl Mussel on the Afon Eden cSAC (2003 and 2011)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353935\tMonitoring data for 2003 and 2011 cycles are held in the Marine Archive as Project No. 336, Media No. 1302.  Paper survey forms for 2011 monitoring are stored in regional registered files (Meirionnydd SH73.5.24)\tFreshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, NRW. Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW.\tFreshwater Pearl mussels are protected under national and international legislations. Afon Eden Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is the only site in Wales where pearl mussel forms the primary reason for selection of the Special Area of Conservation. The data forms part of ongoing monitoring of this site. The Afon Eden was surveyed to assess the current population status of Freshwater Pearl Mussel compared to that of a previous survey in 1997. The aim of this project was to undertake monitoring of the freshwater pearl mussel on the Afon Eden candidate (SAC) in order to be able to report on the condition as a requirement of the EC Habitats and Species Directive. There have been two monitoring cycles since the work in 1997, both in 2003 and 2011. The only data held for monitoring undertaken in 2003 are photographs taken of the site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T13:30:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18100\tNRW_DS115601\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.917Z\t500\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: \u0027European Dry Heath\u0027, \u0027Northern Atlantic Wet Heath with Erica tetralix\u0027 and \u0027Blanket Bog\u0027 at the Cadair Idris candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2004)\t© CNC/NRW 2003. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363031\tThe associated data is on the H Drive at;  H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\11 European dry heath, N. Atlantic wet heath at Cadair Idris\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW.\tThis dataset details the findings of a two part project to assess the condition of the European dry heath, wet heath and blanket bog habitats within the Cadair Idris candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) . The work was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), and carried out by Central Environmental Surveys (CES) during November 2003.  The purpose of this data capture was to map the dry heath, wet heath and blanket bog habitats, including condition classes of these, within the Cadair Idris cSAC to a uniform specification.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18091\tNRW_DS115607\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.092Z\t691\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Monitoring Raised Mire Vegetation at Cors Fochno candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2004)\t© CNC/NRW 2004. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363037\tData is held on the H:/ at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tThis dataset has been obtained through monitoring of Raised Mire Vegetation at Cors Fochno candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC).  The primary objectives were to map the extent and then assess the condition of three Annex 1 habitats (of the EC Habitats and Species Directive), namely \u0027Active Raised Bog\u0027 and the \u0027Depressions on peat substrates of the Rhynchosporion\u0027 (Rhynchosporion habitat) that forms part of it, together with \u0027Degraded Raised Bog\u0027. Assumed to be high as undertaken by experienced Surveyors.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18093\tNRW_DS115608\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.174Z\t442\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Petalwort Petalophyllum ralfsii at Y Twyni o Abermenai i Aberffraw cSAC (2004)\t© CNC/NRW 2004 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363038\tAssociated data is held on the Z Drive at: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\12 Petallophyllum ralfsii at Tywyni o Abermenai i Aberffraw\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tPetalwort Petalophyllum ralfsii is a pale green thalloid liverwort with erect lamellae on its upper surface, that grows in open, damp, calcareous dune slacks, often on low hummocks rather than on the very wet ground, on compacted sandy/muddy bryophyte-rich turf.  The inclusion of the Petallophyllum ralfsii on Annex II of the EU Habitats and Species Directive requires NRW to identify Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) for the species and to ensure that Favourable Conservation Status (FCS) applies. Whilst some background work has enabled the identification of  key areas for Petallophyllum ralfsii on the candidate SAC, further information on its current extent, distribution and habitat associates are required. The purpose of this data capture was to map the distribution, density and habitat characteristics of Petalophyllum ralfsii at Twyni o Abermenai i Aberffraw cSAC.  This will enable the targets within the conservation objective to be confirmed and to report on the condition of the Petalophyllum on the site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18094\tNRW_DS115610\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.174Z\t476\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the Afon Tywi Catchment Area (2005)\t© CNC/NRW 2005. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence.  No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363130\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\17 Otter in the Afon Tywi\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned this monitoring to assess the condition and conservation status of the European otter Lutra lutra, within the Afon Tywi Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and catchment. The otter, which has made a significant recovery in numbers from a rapid decline in the 1950\u0027s though to the early 1970\u0027s, is still considered to be rare, and across most of Europe is endangered. Indeed in many countries it is now considered extinct, making the populations in Wales all the more significant.  The results of this project, combined with those of similar projects instigated by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) on other SAC river catchments over the past two years, and in future years, will provide a valuable and unique picture of the otter in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:02:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18095\tNRW_DS115614\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.141Z\t332\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Southern Damselfly (Coenagrion mercuriale) on candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (cSAC\u0027s) in Wales (2005)\t© CNC/NRW 2005 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363134\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\18 Southern damselfly on cSAC in Wales (part 2)\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tFollowing Part 1 of the project to carry out an assessment of favourable condition for Southern Damselfly Coenagrion mercuriale (Boyce, 2004) the author was contracted by the former Countryside Council for Wales to continue with the condition assessment at further sites in Pembrokeshire and elsewhere in Wales on Candidate Special Areas of Conservation. Each site was assessed to examine whether Favourable Condition had been attained. Sites covered were as follows;  -  Pembrokeshire:  Mynydd Preseli, Gweunydd Blaencleddau (SN13), and Waun Fawr (SN03) -  Glamorgan:  Rhossili Down and Sluxton Farm (SS4290) and Cefn Bryn (SS5090) -  Anglesey:  Cors Erddreiniog (SH4683).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18090\tNRW_DS115615\t2024-05-28T12:39:52.932Z\t310\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the River Usk Catchment Area (2005)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363135\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at:- Z\\:Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027AMPISAND\u0027 Datasets\\19 Otter in the River Usk\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned this work to assess the condition and conservation status of the European otter Lutra lutra, within the River Usk Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and catchment. The results of this project, combined with those of similar projects instigated by CCW on other SAC river catchments over the past two years, and in future years, will provide a valuable and unique picture of the otter in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:47.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18089\tNRW_DS115616\t2024-05-28T12:39:52.926Z\t922\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Greater Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum) in the Wye Valley (2005)\t© CNC/NRW 2005 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence.  No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363136\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\20 Greater horseshoe bats in the Wye Valley\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Wye Valley and Forest of Dean Bats Sites Special Area of Conservation (SAC), is considered one of the best areas in the UK for both Greater Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum) and Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros). The activity patterns of greater horseshoe bats roosting at Newton Court Stable Block Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) were investigated over a ten-day period during August 2004 in order to ascertain key foraging areas, flight paths and night roosting sites. Ten bats, eight lactating females and two juvenile males, were radio-tagged and followed for the duration of the study period. Greena Ecological Consultancy was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales to identify and assess the condition of principal flight routes and key foraging areas used by the colony, in order to develop Performance Indicators. A condition survey of the actual and potential flight lines within 500 m of the Newton Court Stable Block was carried out, a paper map and MapInfo file has been compiled allowing future condition monitoring by the lengths of flight features present.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T09:13:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18088\tNRW_DS115617\t2024-05-28T12:39:52.808Z\t750\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: European Dry Heath, North Atlantic Wet Heath and Rhynchosporion Habitats in Preseli SAC (2005)\t© CNC/NRW 2005 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363137\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027AMPISAND\u0027 Datasets\\21 European dry heath within the Preseli SAC\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe collection of this dataset was commissioned by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to monitor the condition of the Northern Atlantic wet heath with Erica tetralix, European dry heath and Rhynchosporion habitats within the Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The original aim of the project was to monitor the condition of some of the Annex I habitats for which the site (Preseli SAC) has been designated against performance indicators provided by CCW. These habitats are Northern Atlantic wet heaths with Erica tetralix (H4010), European dry heaths (H4030) and Depressions on peat substrates of the Rhynchosporion (H7150). The monitoring follows on from a detailed habitat survey of the site undertaken jointly by CCW\u0027s Phase II survey team and under contract by earlier in 2005.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:25:48.987Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18087\tNRW_DS115618\t2024-05-28T12:39:52.783Z\t309\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Protection Area (SPA) Monitoring: Bird features within Welsh SPAs (2005)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363138\tThe Report, which contains the data, is held on the NRW DMS Under the Reports Archive \u0026gt; CCW Marine Environmental Monitoring Reports: -  KNOW-911039132-34 - CCW EMR 22 - A preliminary assessment of the condition of bird features within Welsh Special Protection Areas (2005)\tAdditional Information Contact: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis datasets presents a preliminary exploration of the potential use of existing data sets to inform assessments of the condition of Special Protection Area (SPA) features across Wales. The approach adopted for this study was to assess each species/assemblage in the individual SPAs. The most recent list of SPAs in Wales (UK SPA review) contains inconsistencies in the qualifying species/assemblage features when comparisons are made between the Natura 2000 datasheets, the SPA review, site documentation and the SPA review - species documentation.  The aim of this data capture was to: 1 To produce draft performance targets for bird features within Welsh Special Protection Areas, in accordance with the JNCC Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance for birds. 2 To make a preliminary assessment of whether performance targets have been met for the 6 year JNCC report. 3 To identify where existing data are insufficient to report on the performance targets.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18086\tNRW_DS115619\t2024-05-28T12:39:52.710Z\t508\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: European Dry Heath and Blanket Bog Habitat at the Berwyn a Mynyddoedd De Clwyd / Berwyn and South Clwyd Mountains candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2005)\t© CNC/NRW 2005.  There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363139\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027AMPISAND\u0027 Datasets\\23 European dry heath at Berwyn \u0027AMPISAND\u0027 S Clwyd mountains\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset presents the findings of an investigation into the condition of key habitats in selected management units within the Berwyn and South Clwyd Mountains candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC). The purpose of this data capture was to determine the extent, distribution and condition of the dry heath and blanket bog habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:33.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18145\tNRW_DS115625\t2024-05-28T12:40:37.374Z\t626\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGrey Seal Census for North Wales 2001\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363235\tArchived as Project 417, Media 1484.\tTom Stringell, Senior Marine Mammal Ecologist, Natural Resources Planning/Marine and Freshwater Ecosystem Group, NRW\tThe grey seal, Halichoerus grypus, is relatively uncommon globally with estimates of 300,000 individuals across the north Atlantic in 2000. In the British Isles, the grey seal is more common that other species of seal breeding. This dataset contains the results of a full census of grey seal pup production in North Wales for the 2001 season. The aim of this project was to undertake an appropriate level of survey of grey seals in North Wales, from Aberystwyth to the Dee Estuary, in order to lead to a clearer understanding of the abundance and distribution of grey seals and pup production, and thereby to guide management. The objectives were to: - Locate and document all major sites where grey seal haul out for moulting and breeding,  - Confirm the timing and extent of the breeding season - Locate and document all breeding sites in North Wales - Count the number of pups at each of these sites at regular intervals throughout that breeding season in 2001, in order to estimate pup production - Continue the daily pupping census of Bardsey Island - Take photographs of profiles to identify pelage markings of individuals for possible computer-aided matching in the future - Identify areas where seals are subject to disturbance by humans - Assess the quality of breeding haul-out environment - Advise on appropriate levels of monitoring / surveillance.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18134\tNRW_DS115626\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.845Z\t430\tValid\tEditable\tModelling the Sensitivity of Seabed Habitats to Combined Fishing Effects around Anglesey, North Wales (2011-2013).\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2013. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363236\tArchived as project 444, media 1485\tCustodian:  Was Clare Eno, potentially Colin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries,  Marine and Fisheries Welsh Government or Karen Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Habitats, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group may be able to provide information.   Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tA pilot study has been conducted to determine whether the theoretical combination of habitat sensitivity to fishing activity of different gear types. This theory was converted into a model, and run using combined fishing activity data, collected through the FishMap Mon pilot project, to predict the sensitivity of habitats.    The objective of this data capture was to determine whether the data on resistance to fishing activity data for each fisheries habitat developed during the NRW habitat sensitivity project could be applied to the impact of cumulative fishing activity on each habitat.   Specific aims of the modelling project were to:  - Calculate footprint versus resistance curves for each fisheries habitat and fishing activity using the revised activity footprints calculated for the FishMap Mon project  - Develop a methodology to model combined fishing effects  - Test the methodology on Red Wharf Bay and Conwy Bay  - Produce an output layer at 1 km grid resolution  - Produce thematic maps    The source GIS data was collated during the FishMap Mon project using a standard questionnaire and post-processing method. The resistance and intensity definitions were supplied by the FishMap Mon project team. The curves were calculated using an automated process to ensure processing was identical for all habitats and visually verified to ensure a good fit.   This project was a pilot to determine feasibility of the use of such models, therefore the output data is suggestive of the habitat sensitivity.  Due to it\u0027s nature as a pilot project NRW no longer makes this data routinely available, alternative fisheries related data may be available on the Welsh Government Marine Planning Portal.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-03-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:32.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18130\tNRW_DS115627\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.776Z\t433\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring Invertebrate Features on Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs): Tetragnatha striata on Newport Wetlands National Nature Reserve (NNR) Gwent (2013)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363237\tData held at the Gwent Levels Reserve - location to be confirmed\tKevin Dupe, Reserve Manager Newport Wetlands Team Leader, South East Region, NRW Mike Howe, Invertebrate Specialist, MFSG, NRW\tThe spider genus Tetragnatha is represented in Britain by six species: Tetragnatha extensa, T. montana, T. nigrita, T. obtusa, T. pinicola and T. striata. This dataset concerns Tetragnatha striata, a species which is listed as Nationally Notable B. The Spider Recording Scheme holds 322 British records of Tetragnatha striata. However, in Wales, it has only been recorded from five locations: Cors Goch National Nature Reserves (NNR); Conwy RSPB Reserve; Parc Menai, Bangor; Kenfig NNR; Newport Wetlands NNR. It was first discovered at the Newport Wetlands NNR in 2001, when a population was found inhabiting emergent Phragmites at what is now known as Reedbed-6. The spider constructs a small orb-web on reeds, but is highly cryptic, folding itself along a reed stem when at rest.  The data is used to detail the distribution of this feature across the NNR and proposes a Common Standards Monitoring protocol to enable its condition to be monitored and assessed in the future.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18135\tNRW_DS115628\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.799Z\t729\tUnassessed\tEditable\tConservation Management of Hay Meadows in the Elan Valley\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363238\tData outputs associated with this project are archived at HP TRIM Record Number: 09/90051 on server–based storage at Natural Resources Wales. (Ffynnon record number (folder) 09/9005) Relevant NRW Evidence Report: No 8: Conservation Management of species-rich grasslands in the Elan Valley, Radnorshire Hayes, M., Lowther. R   Located on the DMS: KNOW-101-8\tContact: Stuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW [M.J. Hayes \u0026amp; R.A. Lowther - originally collected the data.]  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Elan Valley, in mid-Powys, includes some of the most important and richest examples of unimproved mesotrophic grasslands in Wales. The majority of these are highly species-rich upland-fringe meadows that are now mostly designated as Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) due to their national significance. In order to help safeguard the future of these important sites the Elan Valley Meadow Project was initially set up in 2004 to address both the management of the existing species-rich grasslands and their potential expansion. The project was managed and implemented by a partnership between the Elan Valley Trust, Natural Resources Wales (Natural Resources Wales) (formerly Countryside Council for Wales; CCW) and independent ecological consultants. The primary objective of the project was to provide site-specific and ecologically sustainable management advice to help conserve these important and regionally distinct upland-fringe meadows. Specifically there was a need to determine what levels of nutrient inputs are required to enable reinstatement of traditional hay making practices while still conserving existing high levels of floristic diversity. To this end a long-term replicated field trial was undertaken to determine how a representative group of the Elan Valley meadows respond to different inputs of farmyard manure (FYM) and/or lime in terms of soil chemistry, herbage productivity and changes in species diversity.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:38:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18128\tNRW_DS115630\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.698Z\t877\tValid\tEditable\tFishMap Môn Project - Anglesey\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 . NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset in its entirety. For details on General Use Restrictions please refer to metadata for each of the individual output layers. Some Generalised data may be released for re-use.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363330\tAssociated data is held at:   X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fishmap_Mon  ,  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Analysis .   Generalised Data may only be available on the legacy CCW GIS archive (M:\\ drive).   For further details please refer to metadata for each of the individual output layers.\tInternal Custodian:  Colin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists).   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW), the Welsh Government, and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors. Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited, Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers.   The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities. It was jointly funded 75% / 25% between the European Fisheries Fund and the Welsh Government.   The pilot project focuses on an area around Anglesey in North Wales covering the waters out to 12 nautical miles. The Project area is located between Nefyn to the west and the Great Orme to the east and extends out to the Welsh 12 nautical mile limit. It encompasses an area of approximately 265 kmsq, of which approximately 3% are Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) and 3% are Special Protection Areas (SPAs).   The main aim of the project is to survey commercial, recreational and charter boat fishermen, map the distribution and intensity of fishing activity around Anglesey and combine it with seabed habitat maps and their sensitivities to fishing activity.  The project aimed to deliver the following outputs:  - Mapped fishing activity for a range of commercial and recreational fishing activity in the Project area. Maps have been created following surveys of approximately 50 commercial fishers and 600 recreational fishers.  - The FishMap Môn Guidance Tool; an easy to use web based mapping application allowing stakeholders to view fishing activity data or hypothetical fishing scenarios in combination with seabed habitats and their sensitivity.  - An evaluation of how learning from FishMap Môn could be applied to managing the marine environment in Wales.  - A literature review to explore global case studies where an Ecosystem Approach has been applied to managing the marine environment. The review will identify lessons learnt and how they relate to Wales  - Lessons in stakeholder engagement and collaborative working with the fishing industry The project also aimed to develop a trial method for gathering fishing activity data from recreational and commercial fishing communities; and, to develop and test an indicative, web based guidance tool for the pilot area using GIS Techniques.  Due to it\u0027s nature as a pilot project NRW no longer makes this data routinely available, alternative fisheries related data may be available on the Welsh Government Marine Planning Portal.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:14:04.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18138\tNRW_DS115631\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.780Z\t301\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFine Grain Vegetation Monitoring of Yr Wyddfa / Snowdon ECN Site\t© CNC/NRW 2005 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363331\tAssociated data is held at H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\28 Fine-Grain Vegetation at Snowdon ECN Site at NRW, Maes y Ffynnon.\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, Maes y Ffynnon, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Environmental Change Network (ECN) is the UK\u0027s long-term, integrated environmental monitoring and research programme. It gathers information on the responses to environmental change in physical, chemical and biological systems. This is done through a network of UK monitoring sites from which long-term datasets can be used to identify environmental changes and improve understanding of the causes of change. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate environmental change across the ECN site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18141\tNRW_DS115633\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.851Z\t835\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the River Wye SAC and catchment area (2006)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2006. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence.  Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363333\tAssociated data is held at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\30 Current \u0026amp; pot distribution, cond \u0026amp; breeding success of the otter in the R Wye SAC\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) monitoring provides an assessment of the current and potential distribution and breeding success of the otter within the Wye SAC and to identify features outside the SAC boundary whose condition may influence the viability of the otter meta-population associated with the River Wye.  Results from the most recent Otter Survey of Wales (Jones, 2004) show that otter signs were found within the Welsh part of the Wye catchment at 92% of sites searched. Whilst these signs are a useful indicator of otter distribution, they shed little light on otter breeding. The ability to monitor breeding activity and breeding sites in the catchment is crucial if the species and its habitats are to be maintained in a Favourable Conservation Status. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the current and potential breeding success of the otter within the Wye catchment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18140\tNRW_DS115634\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.845Z\t762\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros) in the Wye Valley and Forest of Dean Bat Sites SAC\u0027s (2006)\t© CNC/NRW 2006. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363334\tAssociated data is held at the following location at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon- H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\31 Monitoring the Welsh colonies of Lesser horseshoe Bats in the Wye Valley.\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Custodian: Data Services Team, NRW\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) monitoring of Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros) in the Wye Valley, commuting route and foraging areas were investigated. The study considered five nursery roosts and one hibernation roost on the Welsh side of the Wales-England border. The roosts are all subject to regular annual monitoring and it was not a requirement of this study to reproduce the monitoring effort. Primary focus of the project was to identify and verify important foraging habitats for lesser horseshoe bats and to assess the condition of commuting routes between roosts and forage areas.  The purpose of this data capture was to assist in the survey each surveyor was issued with a bat detector and each team had one Pettersson D-240x and one Skye Instruments SBR2100 bat detectors available to them. Detectors were set to a frequency of 108 kHz, the \u0027peak\u0027 echolocation frequency for lesser horseshoe bats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18127\tNRW_DS115635\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.710Z\t738\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the Afon Mawddach catchment area (2006)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2006. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence.  Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363335\tAssociated data is held at Maes y Ffynnon at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\32 Current \u0026amp; Pot dist, condition of the otter in the Afon Mawddach catchment\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Afon Mawwdach catchment forms part of two Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) the Afon Eden SAC and the Pen Llyn a Sarnau SAC. Current and potential distribution, and breeding success of the otter were investigated, as well as identifying and mapping potential and actual breeding habitat and feeding ponds and ditches. A major focus of this project is an assessment of the current and potential breeding success of the otter within the catchment, so that conservation objectives can be set, and future trends can be identified.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18137\tNRW_DS115636\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.844Z\t600\tValid\tEditable\tThe Feasibility of the Eradication of the Non Native Species Devil\u0027s Tongue Weed (Grateloupia turuturu) in Wales - Milford Haven Survey July 2013\t© CNC/NRW, 2014. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363336\tArchived as project 445, media 1486. Marine Recorder,  Survey_ID: MRCCW30000000092\tCustodian Contact: Ben Wray Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecology, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Data ServicesTeam\tGrateloupia turuturu, Devil\u0027s tongue weed is a Non Native Species (NNS) now found in the UK. Devil’s tongue weed grows in sheltered areas including harbours and bays, attached to pontoons, harbour walls, shells and stones. It occurs on the lower shore in pools, and subtidally to 7 m.  Due to its tolerance of low salinities it may be found in estuarine areas as well as fully marine waters.    A survey was undertaken to search for and record the presence of G. turuturu within Neyland marina and the surrounding Milford Haven area.  The results showed that 38% of the pontoons and other floating structures surveyed in Neyland marine and wider Milford Haven waterway had a presence of G. turuturu. It is postulated that Neyland marina provides a suitable environment for reproduction of G. turuturu and introduction may have occurred through ship ballast water and/or the import of live shellfish associated with local aquaculture.   The purpose of this survey was to assess the extent of dispersion of G. turuturu in Neyland marina and the wider Milford Haven waterway with the purpose of establishing whether eradication of this species was feasible. Information on the extent, density and dispersion of G. turuturu in the Milford Haven waterway may also influence future management responses to this and other marine non-native species.  Recording the extent of G. turuturu informs future management response and helps decide the feasibility of eradicating the species.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T12:29:51.035Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18132\tNRW_DS115638\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.829Z\t301\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater Macrophytes in Nant Teyrn - Freshwater Environmental Change Protocol (2006)\t© CNC/NRW 2006 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363338\tAssociated report and data is held at Maes-Y-Ffynnon at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\34 Freshwater Macrophytes in Nant Teyrn - Freshwater Environmental Change Protocol\tInternal Custodian:  Victoria Bowmaker, ECN Project Officer, NRW Alex Turner, ECN Project Officer, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tScott Wilson was commissioned to undertake a macrophyte survey of Nant Teyrn in Snowdonia National Park. The aim of this survey was to provide macrophyte monitoring data on a 100m stretch of Nant Teyrn within the Snowdonia Environmental Change Network (ECN) terrestrial site. The following elements were recorded: ·  Macrophyte species present and their relative abundance with reference to the check list of aquatic macrophytes used in ECN. Macrophytes include any plant observable by the naked eye and nearly always identifiable when observed.This includes all aquatic vascular plants and bryophytes, together with groups of algae which can be seen to be composed predominantly of a single species ·  Species distribution along a river length and zonation at selected sample points. ·  Physical characteristics of the sampling location\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:34:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18139\tNRW_DS115639\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.762Z\t839\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the Afon Gwyrfai and the Dyfi Estuary (2006)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2006. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence.  Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363339\tAssociated data and reports are held electronically at Maes-y-Ffynnon at;  H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\36 Otters in the Afon Gwyrfai \u0026amp; Dyfi estuary\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tThe Afon Gwyrfai and Dyfi Estuary forms part of two Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) the Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn SAC, and the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau / Llyn Peninsula and the Sarnau SAC. The purpose of this data capture was to report on the current and potential distribution, and breeding success of the otter within the Gwyrfai catchment and Dyfi Estuary area; identify and map actual and potential breeding habitat and feeding ponds and ditches; and, to produce performance indicators to contribute to former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) reporting of the condition and conservation status of the otter.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18133\tNRW_DS115640\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.821Z\t335\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Woodland Habitat at four SACs in North and Mid Wales (2007)\t© CNC/NRW 2007 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363430\tAssociated reports and data are held at Maes-y-Ffynnon at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\38 Woodland habitats at 4 SACs in North Wales\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tThe four Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) surveyed were Coedydd Derw a Safleoedd Ystlumod Meirion/Meirionnydd Oak woods and Bat Sites; Rhinog; Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt and Cwm Doethie Mynydd Mallaen. The Annex 1 habitats surveyed were old sessile oak woods with Ilex and Blechnum (Habitat 91AO); alluvial forests with Alnus glutinosa and Fraxinus excelsior (Alno-Padion, Alnion incanae, Salicion albae) (Habitat 91EO); Tilio-Acerion forests of slopes, screes and ravines (Habitat 9180), and bog woodland (Habitat 91DO). The project was completed in two phases, a survey phase and a monitoring phase. The survey phase of the contract had two main objectives: first, to assess the condition of Annex I woodland habitat within individual management compartments and second, to identify and map hot-spots for lichens and bryophytes. The monitoring phase had a single principal objective: to establish, permanently mark and record 28 sampling plots in areas identified by CCW; these plots will form the basis of long-term monitoring on the sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18131\tNRW_DS115641\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.831Z\t467\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Assessing Water Courses of Plain to Montane Levels with the Ranunculion Fluitantis and Callitricho-Batrachion Vegetation of the River Wye SAC\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363431\tAssociated data and reports are held in the NRW DMS Reports Archive \u0026gt; CCW Environmental Monitoring Reports KNOW-911039132-53  CCW EMR 39 - Assessment of the condition of water courses of plain to montane levels with the ranunculion fluitantis and callitricho batrachion vegetation of the River Wye SAC  On the DMS:  KNOW-911039132-53  CCW EMR 39 - Assessment of the condition of water courses of plain to montane levels with the ranunculion fluitantis and callitricho batrachion vegetation of the River Wye SAC Held on the CCW H Drive:  H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\39 Ranunculion of the River Wye SAC\\Report\tInternal custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe single habitat listed in Annex I of the Habitats Directive which is a primary reason for selection of the River Wye as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is of Water courses of plain to montane levels with submerged or floating vegetation of the Ranunculion fluitantis and Callitricho-Batrachion (Habitat 3260). The purpose of this data capture was to assess the condition of the Water Courses of Plain to Montane Levels with the Ranunculion Fluitantis and Callitricho-Batrachion Vegetation. The aim of the work was to assess the conservation condition of this habitat. This dataset presents the results of the Common Standards Monitoring Assessment (CSM) of the River Wye and selected tributaries. The assessment was performed based on data collected in 1995 and in 2005. The CSM assessment was based purely on macrophyte survey data as these were the only comparable data available from the 1995 surveys.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18142\tNRW_DS115651\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.848Z\t294\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEuropean Union (EU) Habitats Directive (Council Directive 92/43/EEC) reporting under Article 17 reporting round 2013\t© CNC/NRW 2014. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363531\tAssociated data is held in the Terrestrial Archive: at W:\\Terrestrial Archive\\T_Media001to100\\T_Media002\tInternal Custodian, contact relevant specialists or the Enterprise Data Management Specialist Advisor\tEvery six years, Member States of the European Union are required (by Article 17 of the Directive) to report on implementation of the Habitats Directive (Council Directive 92/43/EEC). The reporting format set by the European Commission requires a separate analysis for each species and each habitat in each biogeographic region which that country covers. This is the archive version of the databases and supporting evidence which was sent to JNCC added to the archive 15/04/2014. A live version of the databases are stored on a corporate drive and are in use by the relevant specialists. The purpose of this data capture was to meet the Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW\u0027s) obligations under Article 17 of the Habitats Directive. This dataset covers all of Wales and covers the six year reporting period to 2013.  All data was entered by specialists based on expert opinion and supporting data sources.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18129\tNRW_DS115656\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.826Z\t665\tUnassessed\tEditable\tApproaches for Monitoring the Effectiveness of Tir Gofal in Delivering Benefits to Farmland Birds\t© CNC/NRW 2004 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363536\tAssociated data is held at ;  H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\16 Scoping study Tir Gofal delivering benefits to farmland birds\tInternal Custodian:  Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tTir Gofal is the main scheme in Wales, within which some habitat management prescriptions are designed to provide benefits to breeding and wintering farmland birds. The aim of this project was to identify and evaluate methods of monitoring the extent to which this is achieved.  The purpose of this data capture was to assess possible approaches to monitor the effect of Tir Gofal on farmland birds.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18143\tNRW_DS115678\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.810Z\t380\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLow Tide Wetland Birds Surveys (WeBS) Counts and Feeding activity of Oystercatch and Knot in the Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) and Carmarthen Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) (2013-2014).\t© CNC/NRW and BTO, 2014 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ BTO and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363738\tData archived as project 447, media 1488\tInternal Custodian: Marine Ornithologist Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPAs) are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive 2009/147/EC, which came into force in April 1979. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive), and for regularly occurring migratory species.  This dataset consists of spatial information detailing Wetland Birds Surveys (WeBS) Low Tide Counts carried out various SPAs in the winter of 2013/2014. These count data details feeding and non-feeding birds. The focus of the study was on Oystercatcher and Knot, however other wetland birds were also included.  The aims of this study were to support further WeBS Low Tide Counts and provide additional information on the feeding behaviour of Oystercatchers and Knots in the Burry Inlet, Pembrey Coast and Three Rivers Estuary during the low tide period.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18126\tNRW_DS115679\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.466Z\t653\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInsect Survey Results for Two Brown Field Sand Quarry Sites, Borras Quarry and Marford Quarry, in North-East Wales (2013)\t© CNC/NRW, 2014.   There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363739\t[A]  Recorder 6 database.\t\tThe dataset contains the results from insect surveys conducted on two sand quarries in north-east Wales, Borras Quarry (162 species recorded) and Marford Quarry (145 species recorded). The data collected from Borras Quarry includes 14 psammophilic, 70 aculeate species. The data collected from Marford quarry includes 8 psammophilic and 69 aculeate species. The aim of this data collection was to identify whether the sand quarries supported suitable conditions for invertebrate species, in order to make recommendations for securing long-term sympathetic management of key sites. The diversity of aculeate species recorded from both quarries to date (135 species in total) ranks these areas as the most species-rich sites in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18136\tNRW_DS115702\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.851Z\t691\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring invertebrates by pitfall trapping after excavation of the surface vegetation in two slacks at Newborough Warren, Ynys Llanddwyn Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) in 2013.\t© CNC/NRW, 2014.   There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373032\t[A]  All data is currently held in the report, and is being transferred onto Recorder 6. [B]  The final report Located on the DMS: Reports Archive . NRW Evidence Reports KNOW-1206538853-14  NRW Evidence Report No. 022 - Monitoring~ace vegetation in two slacks at Newborough Warren - Ynys Llanddwyn SSSI in 2013\t\tThis dataset holds information regarding the monitoring of excavated dune slacks in Newborough Warren, north Wales. The monitoring consisted on invertebrate surveys focussing on the two excavations sites further inland, as well as an adjacent control site. The dataset hold 12,838 individual invertebrates records made up of 298 species. The aim of the current study is to monitor by pitfall trapping the effects upon selected dune invertebrates of the excavation of the surface vegetation of two slacks and the dunes bordering their NE ends.  Some voucher specimens have been retained and are in the authors\u0027 private collections.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18144\tNRW_DS115704\t2024-05-28T12:40:37.344Z\t825\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCondition assessment of 8 standing water Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and 24 unprotected lakes across Wales (2012-2014).\t© CNC/NRW, 2014.   There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373034\tData outputs associated with this project are archived as Media Number: 1493 Project Number: 449 (Condition Assessment of eight standing waters in Welsh SSSIs) 450 (The BAP lake surveys) 451 (Lake Ecological Surveys)\tInternal Contact: Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader - or Tristan Hatton-Ellis Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tLake ecosystems are threatened both nationally and globally. Commitments under European legislation and international treaties require the UK to protect and enhance these in a conservation conscience way taking account of the culturally important habitats. This data collection forms part of an ecological and limnological survey. Limnology is the study of inland waters as ecological systems interact with their drainage basins and the atmosphere. The lakes surveyed were as follows:  - Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Lake Surveys: Llynnau Cregennen, Llyn Dinas, Llyn Mair, Llyn Cerig-y-Myllt, Llyn Gwernan, Llyn Gwyddior and Llyn Coch-hwyad.   - Lake Ecological Surveys: Llyn Cadarn, Llyn yr Wyth-Eidion, Llyn Coron, Llyn Goddionduon, Llyn Anafon, Gloyw Lyn, Llyn Cwm Bychan, Llyn Eiddew-mawr, Llandgorse Lake, Llyn Mawr, Cosmoeston Lake, Llyn Eiddwen, Llyn Fanod, Upper Talley Lake, Lower Talley Lake, Llyn Penrhyn.    - Condition Assessment of Eight standing waters in Welsh SSSIs: Pysgodlyn Mawr, Llyn Llech Owain, Llyn Pencarreg, Witchett Pool, Llyn Treflesg, Llyn Cerrig-bach, Llyn Traffwll and Llyn Glasfryn.  Data from this network of lakes, supports an integrated monitoring programme for protected sites, the Water Framework Directive and other legislative and policy driven initiatives. The Welsh Environment Strategy has imposed a target of 95% of all Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) to be in favourable condition by 2015. All the SSSIs assessed in this dataset were classes as being in unfavourable condition. Therefore another objective of this dataset is to provide initial monitor the condition of a series of SSSIs lakes in order to assess the need for improvement or increased monitoring in the future to reach the targets set by the Welsh Environmental Strategy. The data was also collected to fill the gaps in the quality and condition of unprotected sites in Wales, by targeting select lakes of potential biodiversity importance in Wales. This forms part of an on-going strategic survey programme designed by NRW to improve the understanding of the distribution and quality of different lake types in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:30.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18122\tNRW_DS115773\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.871Z\t2075\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status and distribution of the shrill carder bee Bombus sylvarum in the Kenfig National Nature Reserve (NNR) - Port Talbot (2013).\t© CNC/NRW, 2014.   There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373733\t[A]  The data will become available in Recorder 6  [B]  The report located in the DMS Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports  NRW Evidence Report No. 023 - The status~ distribution of the shrill carder bee in the Kenfig - Port Talbot area in 2013.  KNOW-1206538853-15\t\tThe shrill carder bee (Bombus sylarum) was once relatively widespread across southern England and lowlands Wales, favouring flower-rich habitats such as sand dunes and established grasslands and heathlands. However, there has been a recent decline in the population. The main purpose of this survey was to provide an up-to-date account of the current status of the species, with an emphasis placed on determining the distribution limits, by re-visiting sites with previous records and surveying sites supporting flower-rich dunes, grasslands and other habitats.  The surveying took place in the Kenfig-Port Talbot area in Glamorgan between late July and early September 2013.  This dataset provides an up-to-date account of the distribution of shrill carder bee (Bombus sylarum) across these sites around South Wales. It is a collection of sightings of shrill carder bee and botanical surveys. The botanical surveys had a particular emphasis on plants associated with the foraging behaviour of shrill carder bees. A total of 17 species of bumblebee, including shrill carder bee, were recorded during the survey with a total of 936 recorded observations of 2122 individual bumblebees.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18120\tNRW_DS115774\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.849Z\t820\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpatial Conservation Status Modelling of the Great Crested Newt (Triturus cristatus) in South Wales (2013).\t© Amphibian and Reptile Conservation Trust, 2014. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the raw dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.  However the following applies to the model outputs and screen shots held within the report Spatial Conservation Status Modelling of the Great Crested Newt in South Wales. © CNC/NRW, 2014.   There are no use restrictions on these screen shots.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373734\tMaxEnt Model outputs are located within the accompaning report: NRW-14-105023 Spatial Conservation Status Modelling of the Great Crested Newt in South Wales. NRW Science Report Series. Report 30.   Raw Data is held and distributed by ARC Trust.  Associated Report Title: Spatial Conservation Status Modelling of the Great Crested Newt in South Wales (2013) Fletcher DH, Arnell AP, French GCA and Wilkinson JW.  NRW Science Report No.30  Ffynnon Number: NRW-14-105023\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset was compiled to build on previous related work conducted in North Wales and Powys. 12 Unitary Authorities in south Wales were assessed in terms of their Favourable Conservation Status for great crested newts (Triturus cristatus) using a habitat modelling approach.  Project specific aims included assessing: i) known distribution, ii) suitable habitat, iii) survey target areas, iv) important populations for metapopulation connectivity, v) pond and habitat creation areas, and, vi) Local Population Targets (LPTs) for each unitary authority in South Wales, to develop a tool kit which can be used by local planners as an indication as to the presence of great crested newts. This tool kit includes spatially-explicit information in the form of GIS-based mapping (layers) and spreadsheets that will inform conservation priorities and action, land-use planning and other things such as derogation licensing.  This project follows on from other recent attempts to describe the status of great crested newts (Triturus cristatus) in the UK and to apply the concept of Favourable Conservation Status (FCS). This is the third and final regional modelling exercise in Wales covering the country\u0027s core great crested newt range (Anglesey, NE Wales, Powys and South Wales between Gower and Monmouthshire). The integrated results will enable the first objective Wales-wide assessment of great crested newt status for the country. MaxEnt model testing produced acceptably high values (bigger than 0.7) for the area under the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve. Due to limited record numbers in individual Unitary Authorities, NRW built and tested the MaxEnt model with records from all 12 Unitary Authorities.\tAmphibian and Reptile Conservation-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:30:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18124\tNRW_DS115775\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.870Z\t658\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA geomorphological appraisal and options for dune rejuvenation at Pembrey Burrows (2014).\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373735\tReport location, with datasets included is located on Ffynnon NRW-14-1090223\tInternal Custodian: Invertebrate Ecologist, TEG, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of the results from a geomorphological assessment of Pembrey Burrows, south Wales. The datasets holds a combination of environmental data, published and unpublished literature, air photography, LiDAR interpretation, site visits and discussions with relevant personnel. The purpose of this data capture and analyses was to provide a geomorphological assessment of Pembrey Burrows sand dunes system, with the aim of creating an informed management policy in the future which could include options for dune rejuvenation.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:32:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18123\tNRW_DS115776\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.900Z\t694\tUnassessed\tEditable\tKenfig Dune Rejuvenation Trials Topographic Survey Data (2012 - 2015)\t© CNC/NRW, 2015 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373736\tArchive Project Number: 420  Media number:1438  [Data from 2012 /  2013 surveys]  1490  [Data from 2014 surveys]  1534 [data from 2015 surveys]\tInternal Contact: Marine and Coastal Physical Scientist, Evidence Analysis Group.   Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to assess the topographic mobility of the Kenfig National Nature Reserve sand dunes and to monitor the sand dune rejuvenation.  Kenfig sand dune rejuvenation trails began in December 2012. The dataset consists of points were topographic elevations were determined, many of which were on profile lines which were profile lines. Key features were defined based on the limits. These features included ridge crests, standing water, new vegetation growth and the position of the frontal sand dunes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-10-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18125\tNRW_DS115828\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.041Z\t630\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University SOS Prince Madog Video and Photographic Survey Data for Habitat and Scallop Assessments (2012)\t© Bangor University, 2014 and © Natural Resources Wales NRW have received permission to release, publish or disseminate the data freely. However data may only be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383238\tArchived as Project 387 Media 1402 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1402) Species Data is held in Marine Recorder surveys [MRCCW3170000000D, MRCCW3170000000E, MRCCW31700000010, MRCCW31700000012, MRCCW31700000013].\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Contact: School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University\tSurveys of the seabed in Cardigan Bay, Caernarfon Bay, West Anglesey and Tremadog Bay, were carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS), Bangor University in 2012. The datasets  comprises of a collation of video tows, photographs and subsequent species and habitat photographic analyses. The main aim of this survey work was to collect information on the characteristics and status of the offshore benthic environment, with specific reference to scallop beds and habitat types, at various locations around Wales.  Confidence in this dataset is high.\tUniversity of Wales Bangor\t2012-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:48.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18119\tNRW_DS115829\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.790Z\t405\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUnderwater photographs and video of aquatic flora, habitat structure and water transparency from Llynnau Cregennen.\t© CNC/NRW, 2014 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383239\tProject Number 455 Media 1516\tInternal Custodian: Freshwater Team Leader  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe data supports a condition assessment of Llynnau Cregennen on the northern slope of Cadair Idris above Dolgellau. The dataset supports an assessment of both the water clarity and diversity of aquatic plants in the lake. The dataset is made up of a number of underwater video and photography surveys which were conducted by Marine Ecosol. This data was collected in order to have representative samples, images and video footage of macrophyte and faunal community assemblages within Llynnau Cregennenn.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-08-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18121\tNRW_DS115830\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.809Z\t387\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Quality of Riverine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) across Wales (2006-2012).\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383330\tArchive project number: 448 Archive number 2006-2012\tInternal Contacts: Freshwater Team Leader, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis data collection was required as part of the water quality assessment of various Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) for the monitoring round and report cycle 2006 to 2012. Water quality targets for designated riverine features are specified under the JNCC CSM Guidance. The targets are based on routine water quality monitoring and subsequent reporting. Water quality targets are assessed based on the chemistry, biology, phosphate, suspended solids and un-ionized ammonia. The dataset details the results of water quality target testing for the following rivers and associated SACs: - Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn SAC - Afon Eden and Cors Goch Trawsfynydd SAC - River Dee and Bala Lake / Afon Dyfrdwy a Llyn Tegid SAC - Afon Tywi / River Tywi SAC - Afon Teifi / Afon Teifi - Afonydd Cleddau SAC - River Usk SAC - River Wye SAC - Afon Glaslyn (Meirionnydd Oakwoods SAC)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18118\tNRW_DS115834\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.791Z\t342\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAllis (Alosa alosa) and Twaite (Alosa fallax) shad monitoring data sampled from the rivers Wye, Usk and Tywi using Mitochondrial DNA techniques (2013).\t© CNC/NRW, 2014 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383334\tProject Number: 436 Media number: 1473\tInternal Contact: Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe river Wye, Usk and Tywi are, or form part of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs), designed under the EU Habitats Directive (92/43/EEC) for several features including the Twaite and Allis shad. The dataset contains the results from DNA analyses conducted on 226 fish eggs sampled from the river Wye, Usk and Tywi. The aim of the project was to use genetic techniques to address the weaknesses identified in the methods already adopted by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for sampling shad eggs.  The main objectives of analyses were to: - Determine whether the collected eggs belonged to Alosa spp. - Determine the proportion of Twaite and Allis shad using three different genetic markers.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-11-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18117\tNRW_DS115838\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.555Z\t634\tValid\tEditable\tTowed Video Surveys off the Coastline of the Llyn Peninsula North Wales (2013)\t© CNC/NRW. Non-sensitive data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383338\tMarine Recorder,  Survey Code: MRCCW31700000014.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tSurveys of the seabed were carried out using towed dropdown video surveys were taken aboard Lara B and William Stanley, both fishing vessels operating around Llyn Peninsula, Gwynedd, North Wales.   A total of 38 video tows were collected from infralittoral and circalittoral habitats at the following five locations offshore from the Llyn Peninsular North Wales; Llyn-Hells Mouth, Ynys Gwlan, Porth Meudwy, North Llyn Modiolus and North Llyn sea Grass. Where available HD GoPro video footage was used in the analyses, however if this was not possible a lower quality standard definition footage was used. The locations of the video tows were made into GIS files as a spatial representation of the location of the tow surveys. Video surveys were conduct to conduct ground-truthing surveys through the seabed around the Llyn Peninsula, Gwynedd, North Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:44:46.033Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18116\tNRW_DS115839\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.464Z\t429\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Towed Video Survey in Liverpool Bay; Caernarfon Bay and Cardigan Bay (2012 - 2013)\tVideo Footage and Images: © School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Species Data: © CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383339\tArchived as Project 439 Media 1479 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1479)   [C] Marine Recorder data to be entered (data  received 2014-09-31 - to be merged with the main database) MRCCW31700000015 - 2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Liverpool Bay, August. MRCCW31700000016 - 2012 DRAFT Fisher Survey Towed Survey in Cardigan Bay, October. MRCCW31700000017 - 2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Cardigan Bay, July. MRCCW31700000018 - 2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Caernarfon Bay, July. MRCCW31700000019 - 2012 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Liverpool Bay, October.\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Data Custodian: School of Ocean Science - Bangor University\tIn 2012 and 2013 video tows and photographic surveys were undertaken to ground truth the seabed around Cardigan Bay, Caernarfon Bay and Liverpool Bay. The Bangor University School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) surveyed five sites, two using fishing vessels and three using the Bangor University Research Vessel (RV) Prince Madog. In total sixty video tows were undertaken within circalittoral habitats; six of these had associated photographic stills, of which there were 570.  The purpose of this data capture was to ground truth areas of the seabed around Cardigan Bay, Caernarfon Bay and Liverpool Bay.  Confidence in this dataset is high as data was collected by scientists. Imagery was analysed using standard methods by competent and experienced surveyors.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:20.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18169\tNRW_DS115840\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.718Z\t987\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNewborough Topographic Surveys conducted as part of Sand Dune Rejuvenation Trials (1950-2015)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383430\tArchive Project Number: 421  Media number: 1439  [Data from 2013 surveys January and May]  1474  [Data from 2013 surveys August]  1489  [Data from 2014 surveys]   1535 [Data from 2015 Surveys]\tMarine and Coastal Physical Scientist, Evidence Analysis Group. Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group NRW.\tThere were three main purposes for this dataset:  -  To provide information on the development of remobilising and monitoring the dune system;  -  Conduct a baseline topographic survey to provide local pre-works and height information as part of the remobilisation and monitoring;  -  Provide temporal comparisons of topographic profiles and mapping the limits of define features  -  Temporal comparison of topographic profiles and mapping limits of define features.  Newborough sand dune system forms part of the Newborough Warren Ynys Llanddwyn Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and the Abermenai to Aberffraw Dunes Special Area of Conservation (SAC). This dataset holds a variety of physical, chemical and historic data related to rejuvenation and monitoring works completed on the dune system. The data includes topographic surveys, sediment sampling, LiDAR surveys, drone aerial photography surveys, historic aerial photos and maps.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-10-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:27.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18167\tNRW_DS115841\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.797Z\t315\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDefining and mapping calaminarian grassland in mid-Wales (2013)\t© CNC/NRW, 2014  There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383431\t[A]  The data is available on the DMS. Reports Archive \u0026gt;  NRW Evidence Reports.  NRW Evidence Report No. 024 - Defining and mapping calaminarian grassland in mid-Wales 2014   KNOW-1206538853-16 [B]  The data is stored in Ffynnon - Folder NRWF-14-003465 Document NRW14-118779 [C]  The data is also stored on the DMS: Evidence \u0026gt; Terrestrial Habitats \u0026gt; Lowland Grassland \u0026gt; Projects \u0026gt; Calaminarian - definition 2013 Document ID - EVID-271-338\tGrassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThe 2012/13 reporting for the status of habitats under the EU Habitats Directive indicated a lack of essential data for H6130 Calaminarian grasslands of the Violetalia, calaminariae  calaminarian grassland. This instigated the collection of data to identify areas of this type of habitat in mid-Wales. This dataset consists of botanical surveys conducted as a preliminary study associated with calaminarian grasslands in mid-Wales. The aim of this data collection was to gather information in order to identify lichen-rich forms of calaminaria grassland in mid-Wales with a view to obtaining information on extent of these areas in the future.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:40:04.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18174\tNRW_DS115843\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.756Z\t470\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMerthyr Mawr Topographic Surveys Conducted as part of the Sand Dune Rejuvenation Trials (2012 - 2015).\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383433\tArchive Project Number: 419  Media number: 1437 [Data from 2013 surveys]  1491 [Data from 2014 surveys]  1533 [data from 2015 surveys]\tInternal Contact: Marine and Coastal Physical Scientist, Evidence Analysis Group.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to assess the topographic mobility of the Merthyr Mawr National Nature Reserve sand dunes and to monitor the sand dune rejuvenation.  Merthyr Mawr Warren sand dune rejuvenation trails began in December 2012. The dataset consists of points were topographic elevations were determined, many of which were on profile lines which were profile lines. Key features were defined based on the limits. These features included ridge crests, standing water, new vegetation growth and the position of the frontal sand dunes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-10-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18162\tNRW_DS115844\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.663Z\t19\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSOS N Llyn and Tremadog Bay Fishers Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383434\tArchive Project: 419  Media: 1437 and 1491 (File paths: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1437 and K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1491)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18173\tNRW_DS115862\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.909Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBeach Profile Data Swansea Bay \u0026amp; Carmarthen Bay Coastal Autumn 2013\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383632\tProject 437 Media 1476 (File path: K\\Archive\\Media  1401to1500\\Media 1476)\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18172\tNRW_DS115863\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.949Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBeach Profile Data - Swansea and Carmarthen Bay 1998- 2013\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383633\tArchived as Project 438 Media 1477 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media1401to1500\\Media 1477)  The data can now be accessed externally via the Wales Coastal Monitoring Centre https://www.wcmc.wales/\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tThis data  was collected by the Swansea Bay \u0026amp; Carmarthen Bay coastal group, part funded by NRW/legacy bodies.    It can now be accessed externally via the Wales Coastal Monitoring Centre https://www.wcmc.wales/ We should direct customers to this resource.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18165\tNRW_DS115866\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.746Z\t367\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLlyn Tegid Hydroacoustic Surveys (2014)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383636\tData is archived as project 458, media 1519\tInternal Contact: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) population of Llyn Tegid has been considered for a number of years to be threatened by deteriorating environmental conditions, especially those associated with eutrophication. This dataset contains the results of a project undertaken in 2014 to carry out a hydroacoustic survey and measurement of environmental variables. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the status of gwyniad and its habitat in Llyn Tegid.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18159\tNRW_DS115875\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.739Z\t406\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Vegetation Layer - Voronoi\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383735\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Vegetation voronoi.lyr . Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\allwales_ph1 \\phase1_veg_voronoi.tab\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial polygons showing the boundaries and attributes of vegetation polygons.   A voronoi procedure was applied to the All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Vegetation Layer - GIS dataset to subdivide upland mixed habitat polygons into their component habitats as identified by the All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Habitat Layer - GIS dataset. New polygons derived by the voronoi procedure have Voronoi_UID\u0027s prefixed \u0027v_\u0027. New polygons created from Habitat Layer points mapped with a string of habitats, e.g. D.1.1 + D.2/E.2.1, are attributed \u0027mosaic\u0027 in the Phase 1 Code field, and the component habitats and their proportions are listed in the Mosaic browser table (see metadata for All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Mosaic Layer voronoi - GIS dataset). The two tables link on the Voronoi_UID field. The Phase 2 Tools were used to create a mospolys (mosaic polygons) layer to which the Phase 1 legend can be applied for better visualisation in GIS.    The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales 1km square dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage, and the maps were digitised as part of the mapping of Open Access land. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.   Voronoi was carried out to enable full representation and visualisation of the components of complex mixed-habitat upland polygons in the vector data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-11-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18160\tNRW_DS115876\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.723Z\t694\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Mosaic Layer Voronoi\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383736\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Mosnotes voronoi.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Mospolys voronoi.lyr ,  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\allwales_ph1 \\phase1_mosaic.tab\tInternal Custodian: Terrestrial mapping Scientist Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset is a GIS browser table that contains information on the habitats present and their proportions and areas in mosaic polygons. This method of dealing with mosaics is based on the approach used for mosaics in Phase 2 vegetation survey GIS datasets.    The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales 1km square dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage, and the maps were digitised as part of the project to map Open Access land.  The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales. Voronoi was carried out to enable full representation and visualisation of the components of complex mixed-habitat upland polygons and the mosaic browser lists habitats in mosaic polygons and gives proportions and area data.   Habitat mixtures were treated as equal splits, e.g. D.1.1 and E.2.1 in the string \u0027D.1.1 + E.2.1\u0027 were allocated proportions 1 : 1, and mosaics were treated as subsidiary divisions, e.g. the components of the string \u0027D.1.1 + D.2/E.2.1\u0027 were allocated proportions 2 : 1 : 1. Habitats in brackets were allocated proportions of 2 percent. The Phase 2 Tools were used to create a mospolys (mosaic polygons) layer to which the Phase 1 legend can be applied for better visualisation in GIS.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-11-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18161\tNRW_DS115877\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.740Z\t549\tUnassessed\tEditable\tQuantifying the Status of Great Crested Newts (Triturus cristatus) in Wales (2014)\t© Amphibian and Reptile Conservation Trust, 2014. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the raw dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.   However the following applies to the model outputs and screen shots held within the report Quantifying the Status of Great Crested Newts in Wales.French GCA, Wilkinson JW, Fletcher DH, and Arnell AP. 2014. NRW Science Report 31. © CNC/NRW, 2014.  There are no use restrictions on these screen shots.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383737\tA pdf of the report is available in Ffynnon: NRW-15-014399\tContact: Species Team Leader and Herpetologist Associated Report Title:  Quantifying the Status of Great Crested Newts in Wales.French GCA, Wilkinson JW, Fletcher DH, and Arnell AP. 2014. NRW Science Report 31  Ffynnon Number: NRW-15-014399\tThe Great Crested Newt (Triturus cristatus) is declining in the United Kingdom despite full European-level protection. Reporting on the species status to the EU is carried out at UK level but nature conservation is a devolved function. Knowledge and quantification of the Great Crested Newts status in Wales will therefore forms part of future UK wide reporting.  The purpose of this GIS based dataset was to: (i) Demonstrate the applicability of GIS techniques to quantification of conservation status parameters and predictive mapping; and, (ii) Produce regional, fine-scale spatial outputs (at 25 m resolution) which could be aggregated to create an objective quantification of spatial metrics for the Great Crested Newt across Wales.  Combined model outputs were good to excellent (AUC 0.71 to 0.86) and successfully quantified range (7,312 km squared), population (3,271 occupied ponds) and habitat (2,2170 km squared) parameters, as well as suggesting a proxy quantification for habitat quality (810 high quality ponds). Article 17 reporting is based on quantification of four parameters: range, population, habitat for the species, and future prospects.   Range: The defined range for the great crested newt in Wales is calculated using a convex hull algorithm in the R statistical software. The input data consisted of a collection of all known presence locations. The alpha value used was 8,000, arrived at by trialling several values and selecting the one most representative of the observed core distribution in Wales.  Population: Population is number of populations, equating to number of occupied ponds. To determine this number for Wales, the EQSS threshold for each model was used to determine the areas in which suitable ponds might be found. The suitable habitat was then clipped to the known range of the species in Wales and subsequently used to clip a UK wide pond dataset, extracted from Mastermap.   Habitat for the species: The EQSS outputs were converted into polygons, from which total area in km squared was derived. This area was then clipped to the known range of Great Crested Newts in Wales to provide an estimate of the suitable habitat available to the species within their range.   Future Prospects: It theoretically includes all factors potentially impacting great crested newts at sites and/or larger scales including (but not limited to) legislation, climate or site-specific factors. The UKs latest Article 17 report for the great crested newt lists Future Prospects as unknown. With application of the modelling processes described in the present report to Scotland and regions of England, confidence in habitat and population metrics could be improved to allow a UK-wide assessment of Future Prospects.\tAmphibian and Reptile Conservation-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18158\tNRW_DS115880\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.615Z\t9\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 1 Intertidal Data from Dee\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383830\tData is archived under project 204 media 1185 (File path; K:\\Archive\\Media 1101to1200\\Media 1185)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18175\tNRW_DS115886\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.892Z\t301\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMenai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) muddy gravel survey (2013)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383836\tCurrently stored at: he data are archived as Project No 411, Media No xxx and is stored on backed-up server based storage at NRW Maes y Ffynnon, Bangor office.  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MSCB\\2013 ASML MSCB  H:\\Science\\MFSG\\MarineRecorder\\MarineRecorderData\\MRCCW163_JenJones-original\\From Jen Jones 2014\\NBNData CCW163 JJ Pembs \u0026amp; Menai muddy gravel.mdb    Marine Recorder Survey MRCCW16300000031 2013 ASML /NRW Menai Strait \u0026amp; Conwy Bay SAC muddy gravel survey  H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2013 ASML MS\u0026amp;CB\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tSeveral rich marine mud and muddy gravel habitats were identified within the Menai Strait by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Marine Intertidal Phase 1 survey team and a sampling programme initiated in order to better characterise the intertidal communities of these habitats. Marine mud and muddy gravel habitats are an important feature of several Special Area of Conservation (SACs), including the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SACs. The purpose of this data capture was to carry out a repeat survey of sites previously surveyed to further test and develop monitoring methodology and collect monitoring data. In fully marine conditions on the lower shore, sheltered muddy gravel habitats can be particularly species rich due to the complex nature of the substratum supporting a high diversity of infauna and epifauna.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-06-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18168\tNRW_DS115887\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.868Z\t403\tUnassessed\tEditable\tComprehensive reassessment of Non Native Species in Welsh marinas (Marine Recorder dataset)\t© CNC/NRW and MBA 2014 Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383837\t[A] Species data is held in Marine Recorder: 2014 NRW Comprehensive reassessment of NNS in Welsh marinas MRCCW32900000002 [B] Copy of the source NBN file currently stored at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\MarineRecorder\\MarineRecorderData\\MRCCW329_BishopsGroup_MBA\\MRCCW329_NBNDATA.mdb  [C] A copy of the Marine Recorder species data is included on the NBN Gateway  Archived as project 457 Media 1518 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1501to1600\\Media 1518) A copy of the MEDIN format dataset is lodged at DASSH (likely to be condition of the WG funding).  NRW do not hold a copy in this format.  We are not publishing the metadata to MEDIN as we assume DASSH are.  DASSH identifier DASSHDT00000083\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team       We have not set this metadata entry to harvest to MEDIN portal because NRW only hold a copy of MR data, DASSH have the rest of the data and we assume that they are publishing metadata for the full data outputs. DASSH identifier DASSHDT00000083\tThe dataset is part of an ongoing rapid assessments for presence/absence of marine Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) in Welsh marinas.  Survey undertaken to assess the presence of non-native species in 15 marinas throughout Wales. Funding from obtained by Welsh Government Resilient Ecosystems Fund, which is administered by Natural Resources Wales (NRW). Comprehensive reassessment of non-native species on floating pontoons in Welsh marinas. The purpose of this data capture was to look at the spread of invasive non-natives in Wales and provide early warnings for new introductions with a possible view to employing eradication techniques.  This metadata only covers the data held in NRWs copy of marine recorder.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-07-03\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T17:26:24.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18156\tNRW_DS115889\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.255Z\t393\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Afon Tywi Shad Egg Survey (2013 and 2014)\t© CNC/NRW, 2015.   There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383839\t[A] Data is archived under Project Number: 459 Media Number: 1520.   [B] Field notes are held in Maes Newydd by the Technical Biodiversity Officer and River Officer, in Maes Ffynnon, under the Marine and Freshwater Ecosystem Group - Evidence and Analysis Team. [C] PDF of the report is held on the DMS Report Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports NRW Evidence Report No. 048 - Afon Tywi SAC shad egg surveys 2013 \u0026amp; 2014  KNOW-1206538853-57\t\tThe Afon Tywi is designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for several features including twaite shad (Alosa fallax) and allis shad (Alosa alosa). Shads are migratory members of the Clupeidae (herring family) that ascend rivers to spawn in late spring. In Britain, significant populations of shad spawn in the Rivers Severn, Usk, Wye and Tywi. The populations of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and issues surrounding water quality. The aim of this data collection was to contribute towards the assessment the condition of the shad population in the Afon Tywi SAC based on 2013 and 2014 egg surveys. The objectives of this study are to: 1)  Conduct a shad egg survey on the Afon Tywi in 2013 and 2014.   2)  Analyse and present the results for spawning distribution of the population attribute only.   3)  Use the data obtained from the genetic analysis of shad egg tissue to give additional context to the above analysis.  2013 Shad eggs were present at seven of 13 survey sites (54 percent). Each of the 12 survey sites were visited once. The egg sample size ranged from 1 to 100 plus eggs. The downstream limit of spawning was Tidal limit (site 16) and the upstream limit was Manordeilo (site 11). A total of 177 plus eggs were recorded. The identification of shad eggs were confirmed through genetic analysis at two of the three selected sample sites.  2014  Shad eggs were recorded at eight of the 14 survey sites (57 percent). Seven of the sample sites were visited twice (54 percent) and four sites were visited three times (29 percent). The 14 sites were surveyed between 16 May and 11 Junes over 6 days by 13 surveyors.   Purpose of data capture:\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18157\tNRW_DS115890\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.604Z\t663\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe reproduction and connectivity of Sabellaria alveolata reefs in Wales - MAR4REF (2014)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383930\tData and Report are Archived as project 460, media 1521 See project 404, media 1421, 1425, 1426, 1427\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe reef building tube worm Sabellaria alveolata is an important ecosystem engineer on UK shorelines and is subsequently protected as a result of its limited spatial distribution in reef form. To effectively manage and reduce impacts on populations resulting from environmental change, more information is required to understand the distribution and development of S. alveolata reefs. The purpose of this data capture was it collect information to help understand the distribution and development of Sabellaria alveolata reefs in order to effectively manage and reduce impacts of populations resulting from environmental change. The associated report presents the findings from a Welsh Government Resilient Ecosystems Fund funded project lead by Bangor University investigating the reproduction and connectivity of S. alveolata reefs along Welsh coastlines.  Three key sites for data collation, Llanddulas (north Wales), Aberarth (mid-Wales) and Dunraven (south Wales), but expanded to the regional scale for modelling larval movements between major reef sites throughout the Irish Sea. The main research areas included:  1) Laboratory-based experiments to describe larval behaviour in relation to phototaxis and quantification of swimming speed throughout different larval development stages.  2) We assessed adult fecundity and egg sizes from worms collected from all three study sites every month from February-Sept 2014.  3) We measured larval densities in the water column at all three sites during the theorised reproductive period.  4) We produced estimates of population connectivity for different reef sites within Wales and determined major source and sink sites by using a biophysical model to create estimates of larval movement.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-02-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:35:59.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18153\tNRW_DS115891\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.928Z\t395\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAllis (Alosa alosa) and Twaite (Alosa fallax) shad monitoring data sampled from the rivers Wye, Usk and Tywi, using Mitochondrial DNA techniques (2014).\t© CNC/NRW, 2015.   There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383931\tData is archived under project number 461 and media number 1522. Report is held on the DMS Report Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports.  NRW Evidence Report No. 053 - Monitoring Allis and Twaite Shad - quality assurance and species identification using molecular techniques  KNOW-1206538853-44\t\tPopulations of allis shad (Alosa alosa Lacépède) and twaite shad (Alosa fallax L.) have declined across their geographic distribution over the last century and despite being widespread around the coast of the British Isles, UK spawning populations are largely restricted to the Rivers Severn, Wye, Usk and Tywi which drain into the Bristol Channel. As a result, both species are protected by UK and international legislation. In response to a need to monitor the population status to inform conservation objectives for these species, the former Countryside Council for Wales and Environment Agency Wales, now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) developed a semi-quantitative and cost-effective method to assess recruitment potential by sampling shad eggs. However, this method is unable to distinguish between the two Alosa species, and there is a risk that eggs of other fish species may erroneously be recorded as shad eggs.   The aim of this data collection was to use genetic techniques to address the weaknesses related to the semi-quantitative and cost-effective methods adopted being unable to distinguish between the two Alosa species, and the risks that other eggs related to other fish species being erroneously recorded.  The two objectives were to: (a) Determine whether the collected eggs belonged to Alosa spp.  (b) Determine the proportion of A. fallax and A. alosa using three different genetic markers – mitochondrial DNA and two types of nuclear DNA: nif1-nDNA and six microsatellite loci.  A total of 226 eggs were successfully genotyped. 85% belonged to Alosa spp. demonstrating that a good level of egg identification in the collection team. Non-Alosa eggs were identified as belonging to minnow (Phoxinus phoxinus) and chub (Squalius cephalus). As a result, the extent of putative shad spawning on the Tywi is slightly less than suggested by the egg surveys alone. However, the upper extent of shad spawning on the Wye was confirmed as being Brynwern Bridge, about 9km further upstream than the previous known upper limit at Builth Wells.    Methods of data capture:\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:29:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18171\tNRW_DS115894\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.775Z\t271\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey of the spider Agroeca dentigera on Dyfi National Nature Reserve, with a particular focus on Ynylas Dunes (2013 to 2014).\t© CNC/NRW, 2015.   There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383934\tData being transferred to Recorder 6.  Report is available through the library. Reports Archive \u0026gt;  NRW Evidence Reports  KNOW-1206538853-47 NRW Evidence Report No. 054 - A survey of the spider Agroeca dentigera on Dyfi NNR, with a particular focus on Ynyslas Dunes in 2013 and 2014\tContact the Invertebrate Ecologist in NRW for additional information.   Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe sand dune system at Ynyslas, part of Dyfi National Nature Reserve (NNR), is the only known UK locality for the IUCN Vulnerable liocranid spider Agroeca dentigera. The main purposes of the survey work in 2013 and 2014 was to re-locate the spider, last recorded in 2002 and attempt to define its habitat preferences and ecological requirements by searching the dunes and other potentially suitable habitats within the NNR.  The main purposes of the survey work in 2013 and 2014 was to re-locate the spider, last recorded in 2002 and attempt to define its habitat preferences and ecological requirements by searching the dunes and other potentially suitable habitats within the NNR. The aim of this survey was to determine the current status and distribution of the spider Agroeca dentigera.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18164\tNRW_DS115896\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.775Z\t384\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring invertebrates by finger searching and pitfall trapping after excavation of the surface vegetation in three slacks at Newborough Warren - Ynys Llanddwyn SSSI (2014).\t© CNC/NRW, 2015. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383936\tData to be provided and added to Recorder 6.\t\tThe purpose of this data capture was to monitor the colonisation of excavated inland dune slacks by specialist pioneer invertebrates on Newborough Warren National Nature Reserve (NNR).  This monitoring data was a continuation of the work undertaken in 2013. All invertebrates captured in the taxa Orthoptera, Heteroptera, Auchenorrhyncha, Coleoptera, Aculeata, Diplopoda, Chilopoda, Araneae and Opiliones were identified and counted. The numbers of the individual species are recorded in tables after being corrected for trapping effort. There was little evidence of any change from 2013 in the fauna on the exposed dune habitat. In the slack habitat exposed by excavation, a marked increase in the abundance of the rove beetle Bledius subniger was revealed by finger searching and mapping the distribution of its casts. Pitfall trapping in Slack 4 also revealed the abundance of the ground beetles Dyschirius impunctipennis and D. politus that are known predators of B. subniger. Another ground beetle, Bembidion pallidipenne, characteristic of embryonic slack habitat, was also abundant. It is tentatively concluded that this fauna has greatly increased, probably from breeding in 2013. Some observations are given to illustrate the bias inherent in pitfall trapping and the danger of too easily comparing abundance between species that are caught in pitfalls. There seemed to be no detectable increase in species of Heterocerus and Dryops. Slack 4 had two distinct areas of slack habitat that had been created by the excavation. In the southern part, there was a higher organic content in the soil and greater plant cover and diversity. This contrasted with the northern part which had little plant cover and a low organic content in the soil. These differences were reflected in a marked difference in the fauna of the two areas. The southern half contained species found in the unexcavated slacks that appear to require some shade but were probably eliminated from the other excavated slacks.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18163\tNRW_DS115898\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.714Z\t383\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMacrophyte Survey of Welsh Lakes 2014\t© CNC/NRW, 2015.    There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383938\tData is archived under Project number 463 Media Number 1524\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) commissioned ECUS Ltd to collect and present ecological data from twenty-five lakes, across eight local authority areas in Wales. The data was needed to support NRW’s integrated monitoring programme for; the Water Framework Directive (WFD); protected sites including Special Areas of Conservation SACs, Special Protection Areas (SPAs) and Site of Special Scientific Importance (SSSIs), as well as other legislative and policy drivers. The lakes surveyed were selected for a number of reasons:  - SAC: Monitoring of a SAC feature (Natura 2000 Site) - SSSI: Monitoring of a Site of Special Scientific Interest feature; - WFD: Water Framework Directive surveillance network site - ND: Nitrates Directive Two notable species that were recorded during the surveys. Floating water-plantain Luronium natans, a European Protected Species, was recorded at seven lakes (Llyn Egnant, Llyn Gynon, Llyn Teifi, Llyn Cerrigllwydion Isaf, Gwnllyn, Llyn Padarn and Llyn Tegid). All of these are known locations for the species. Starry stonewort, Nitellopsis obtusa, is listed as Vulnerable on the UK Red List (JNCC, 2014). Starry stonewort was recorded at Llangorse Lake, making it only the second recorded site in Wales and a new record for this well-studied lake.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:35:58.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18166\tNRW_DS115954\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.795Z\t399\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of the condition of the white-clawed crayfish Austropotamobius pallipes in the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC) (2014).\t© CNC/NRW, 2015.    There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393534\tData is held within the following report: Rogers D \u0026amp; Watson E 2015. Assessment of the condition of the white-clawed crayfish Austropotamobius pallipes in the River Wye Special Area of Conservation in 2014. NRW Evidence Report No: 74. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\t\tIn October and November 2014, parts of the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC) on the Afon Edw, Nant yr Offeiriad and Sgithwen Brook were surveyed for white-clawed crayfish to assess the condition of both the crayfish populations and crayfish habitat. The work was undertaken to provide some of the data required to assess whether the white-clawed crayfish in the River Wye SAC was in favourable condition.   Whilst further data will need to be collected before the full assessment can be made, the apparent lack of white-clawed crayfish in the Afon Edw, following a mortality in 2006, is worrying given that this has been a very important waterway for this species in the recent past. Whilst crayfish numbers were relatively robust in Nant yr Offeiriad and Sgithwen Brook, populations were mostly confined to the headwaters.  It is important to complete survey work for white-clawed crayfish on the River Wye SAC in 2015 so that the 2014 results can be amalgamated into an overall assessment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:04.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18154\tNRW_DS115976\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.921Z\t421\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntegrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t© CNC/NRW, 2015. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393736\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tGillian Walker, Data Manager Sites, Terrestrial Management Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW. Christine Edwards, Protected Sites Team Leader, NRW.  Support Desk Team Leader, Information Group, NRW.\tThe Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) is updated continually and consists of several modules, each of which relate to protected sites across Wales. These modules include:  Casework - records of all consultations about development proposals and plans received by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and CCW\u0027s responses;  Consents and Assents - records for all Section 28 and Regulation 48 Consent and Assent requests received by CCW and CCW\u0027s responses;  Contacts - information of individuals and organisations who did business with CCW;   Features - qualifying and non-qualifying features (species and habitats) on Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs);    Land Agency - manages award and payment process for management agreements on NNRs and SSSIs, including land purchases, and buildings leases;  Licencing - details of the species licences granted by CCW to individuals and organisations;  Monitoring data - details about which SSSI Features have been monitored;  Open Access -  records of consultations in relation to the Countryside and Rights of Way (CRoW) Act 2000, in relation to parcels of land known as mountain, moor, heath or down;  Protected Sites - information on protected sites in Wales, and provides historical data on designation dates aiding the reporting process for designations in Wales; Site Actions - documents and shares information on who needs to do what, where, in order to get special conservation sites into favourable condition;  Wales Survey Inventory (WALSIN) - Index to all environmental surveys of NC/NCC since 1949 and bibliography of all files held by NCC, including archives from approximately 1900 until March 1992 (No Actual Data); (See Contains for more information)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-23T09:53:09.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18170\tNRW_DS115992\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.735Z\t479\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Sand Lizard  (Lacerta agilis) and Natterjack Toad (Epidalea calamita) Recovery Project 2011-2014\t© Amphibian and Reptile Conservation Trust, 2015. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393932\tRaw Data is held and distributed by ARC Trust.\tContact: Liz Howe Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset comprises of field observations of adults, spawn strings and habitat conditions for Natterjack Toads (Epidalea calamita) and Sand Lizards (Lacerta agilis). The data was collected for an assessment of current populations at re-introduction sites.   Natterjack Toad (Epidalea calamita) Natterjack toad breeding activity was recorded in all three sections of Gronant Dunes and Talacre Warren Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) in 2013. The breeding pools were monitored and the data presented to the annual recorders meetings.  A project to establish natterjacks at the Old Colliery site was started in 2009, but the initial translocation was unsuccessful. In 2010, Environment Agency Wales created about 30 pools at the site and further modified them for natterjacks in 2012. Their hydro-periodism was observed during 2013 and the translocation programme will start afresh in 2014.  In 2011 a total of only 168 spawn strings were recorded in Wales, whereas in 2012 was a record year was observed with 454 spawn strings counted. The relatively low spawn count in 2011 can be attributable to the dry weather that shortened the breeding season. In stark contrast to a dry start, 2012 became very wet and the natterjacks had a long and successful breeding season.  The total recorded in 2012 was the highest ever, a reflection of the strengthening population, good weather conditions and an increase in the number of pools at Talacre through the Million Ponds Project funding. The population and breeding range is likely to be at its highest level since the re-introduction project began in 1995.   Sand Lizard (Lacerta agilis) The sand lizard (Lacerta agilis) was formerly known from dune systems in north and west Wales. These known populations were lost due to erosion, development and increased public pressure. Natural Resources Wales (formerly CCW) has continued to provide funds to ascertain the status of the species via site monitoring and provided funding for captive breeding and translocation programmes, to restore the species former range. Monitoring at all of the translocation sites continued though was constrained by poor spring weather in 2013. Positive results of both adults and juveniles have been obtained at all established translocation sites.\tAmphibian and Reptile Conservation\t2015-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:28.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18155\tNRW_DS115995\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.127Z\t496\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: East Gwynedd, Gower and Glamorgan 1998\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393935\tData stored in Recorder 6 Additional sites 1998 (E Gwynedd, Gower, Glamorgan). Prosser \u0026amp; Wallace CCW0007300000001\tMetadata contact: Spatial Data Team, Master Reference Data Team, and Data Services all of which are in Evidence Knowledge and Information Management, NRW  Data Custodian:  Liz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW (The old custodian was ex-CCW Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist)\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in East Gwynedd, Gower and Glamorgan. The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around East Gwynedd, Gower and Glamorgan. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1998-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18152\tNRW_DS115996\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.927Z\t339\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Anglesey 1994\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393936\tData is stored in Recorder 6 under CCW0007300000002\tAJ Turner from the North Wales Wildlife Trust was the original contractor.  Metadata contact: Spatial Data Team, Master Reference Data Team, and Data Services all of which are in Evidence Knowledge and Information Management, NRW  Data Custodian:  Liz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW (The old custodian was ex-CCW Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist)\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Anglesey. The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathl