 improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities, to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland SSSI series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-06-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T09:47:28.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18192\tNRW_DS116011\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.646Z\t726\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Dinefwr Woodlands 1994\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303131\tData stored in Recorder 6 database CCW0007500000002.\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 1145 observations in woodlands of conservation interest in and around Carmarthen and Dinefwr woodlands. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales.    The standard woodland NVC survey methodology was used.    The purpose of this work was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities, and to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland SSSI series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1994-09-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T09:55:18.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18195\tNRW_DS116012\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.602Z\t597\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Dinefwr Woodlands (1993 - 1994)\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303132\tData stored in Recorder 6 database CCW0007500000005 ET Dinefwr Woodlands.\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 7140 observations in woodlands of conservation interest across Dinefwr woodlands. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales.     The purpose of this work was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities, to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1994-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T09:49:54.012Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18196\tNRW_DS116013\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.664Z\t280\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Welsh Wet Woodlands 1995\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303133\tData stored in Recorder 6 database CCW0007500000004 Welsh Wet Woodlands\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 3247 observations in woodlands of conservation interest in across welsh wet woodlands. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales.    The standard woodland NVC survey methodology was used.    The purpose of this work was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities, and to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1995-09-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T13:25:13.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18188\tNRW_DS116014\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.510Z\t361\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Welsh Woodlands (1996 - 1997)\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303134\tData stored in Recorder 6 database CCW0007500000001 Welsh Woodlands.\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 11833 observations in woodlands of conservation interest in across Wales.   The purpose of this work was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities, and to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-09-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T13:38:57.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18194\tNRW_DS116137\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.662Z\t639\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcoustic multibeam survey of St Bride\u0027s Bay (MBES data)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136313337\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 399, Media No 1416 (K\\:archive\\Media 1401-1500\\Media 1556) project 399 EA (Environment Agency) Acoustic multibeam survey  of St Bride\u0027s Bay [A] xyz data [B] multibeam tiffs [C] Surpporting information about any processing and source of data in form of emails saved as txt files\tInternal Custodian: Charlie Lindenbaum, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer,  Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW       Data provided to Pete Walker from Mike Camplin (Pete thinks that it came straight from Alison Miles at the EA in 2007). There is no metadata or additional information other than that stored with the data.  Just emails from Charlie Lindenbaum and Lucie Skates.  Don\u0027t think any internal CCW processing was done\tThis dataset shows the results of a bathymetric survey of St Brides Bay, Pembrokeshire. This dataset was owned by Environment Agency, however with the formation of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) in 2013, ownership was transferred to NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:14.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18200\tNRW_DS116210\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.714Z\t304\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRare and Protected Species Records across Wales (1975 to 2012)\t© CNC/NRW, 2015.    There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323130\t[A] This data is now available on the NBN Gateway [B] Original data on Wiski-Kieco\tInternal Custodian: Melissa Lacan, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, SE Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tThe aim of this data collection was to monitor the distribution of rare and protected species across Wales.  This dataset is primarily made up of aquatic and riparian species (marine and freshwater) including invertebrates, plants, algae and fish.  The data includes ad-hoc records from surveys at local offices.  Data collection started in the 1970s onwards.  This dataset does not include records of Freshwater Pearl Mussel, but otherwise includes all Rare and Protected species surveyed by the former Environment Agency Wales, now Natural Resources Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:46.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18186\tNRW_DS116211\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.434Z\t602\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNon Native Species Across Wales (1989 to 2012) (Extracted from BIOSYS)\t© CNC/NRW, 2015.   There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323131\t[A] This data is now available on the NBN Atlas [B] X:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\BIOSYS Invasive non native species.lyr  [C] X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Crayfish Non Native Species.lyr  [D] Original data is on Wiski -Kieco database\tInternal Custodian: Melissa Lacan, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, SE Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tThe aim of this data capture was to monitor the distribution of non-native species across Wales. Any non-native species present in a sample were recorded when they were of the target group, for example Signal Crayfish (Pacifastacus lenisculus) in a benthic invertebrate sample or Canadian pondweed (Elodea Canadensis) in a plant survey. Other non-native species are recorded when seen at or around monitoring sites. At the time of surveying between 1970s and 2012, non-native species did not have a specific monitoring programme.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18193\tNRW_DS116212\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.634Z\t318\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNative Freshwater Species Surveys Across Wales (1978 to 2019)\t© CNC/NRW, 2015.   There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323132\t[A] This data is available on the NBN Atlas.  [B] Original data held on Wiski-Kieco\tInternal Custodian: Melissa Lacan, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, SE Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tThe purpose of this data capture was to primarily monitor the distribution of freshwater invertebrates.  The data comprises primarily of the Welsh abstract of freshwater invertebrates. It comes from BIOSYS, the national ecology database for freshwater species and is related to the aquatic stages of the invertebrates. Most data have been collected from main rivers as part of an extensive routine monitoring network. The data are restricted to a selection of families which contain one species only.  The records cover the whole of Wales. Sites are recorded at a minimum precision of 100m. The distribution of records is biased towards main rivers and areas with historical water quality issues. This is especially true of older records. The sampling methodologies and taxa identified are the same at all sites.    NB This data set has been expanded to include all freshwater data from 1978 to 2019 at all taxonomic levels. Includes river \u0026amp; lake macroinvertabrates, river \u0026amp; lake diatoms and river macrophytes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-03-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:27:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18187\tNRW_DS116215\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.554Z\t383\tUnassessed\tEditable\tConsented Discharges to Controlled Waters (Archived)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323135\tExtracted post 26/06/2016 from PALS previously extracted from WIMS in Environment Agency Universe. WIMS data is stored in a static dataset on Y:DRive and Sharefile\tHenry Forrest, Permitting Officer, Wales Permitting Centre Cardiff (Water Resources), NRW\tThis data provides details of permit details as required under the Environmental Permit Regulations. Information is held for all permit holders and covers all substances that are controlled. This data is taken from the Permit and Licencing System (PALS)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:48.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18198\tNRW_DS116223\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.670Z\t831\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAd hoc North Wales Moth Records 2013 to 2014\t© CNC/NRW and Ian Sims, 2015.  NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323233\tData is stored in Recorder 6 under the Welsh Invertebrate Database. Recorder survey: Ian Sims sabbatical moth records 2013 - 2014 CCW000800000000Z.\tGIS Service Desk Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist MFSG, NRW External Custodian and Originator: Ian Sims, RSPB North Wales.\tThis is a third party dataset held by Natural Resources Wales. This dataset was collected as part of a sabbatical taken by Ian Sims. It consists of moth records and sightings obtained between 2013 and 2014 in North Wales. In all over 3113 records of over 11535 moths of 408 species were recorded, including 11 Nationally Notable species, Sandhill Rustic (Luperina nickerlii gueneei), (Na/RDB), Ashworth’s Rustic (Xestia ashworthii) (Na), Sand Dart (Agrotis ripae) (Nb), Anerastia lotella (Nb), Blomer’s Rivulet (Discoloxia blomeri) (Nb), Scarce Water-veneer (Donacaula mucronellus) (Nb), Homoeosoma nebulella (Nb), Marsh Oblique-striped (Hypenodes humidalis) (Nb), Shore Wainscot (Mythimna litoralis) (Nb), Pediasia aridella (Nb) and Pseudopostega crepusculella (Nb). Of the remaining species 69 were classified as  \u0027Local\u0027, 327 as \u0027common\u0027 with one \u0027Immigrant\u0027. 42 BAP species were identified, along with 7 Endangered and 32 Vulnerable species. At least an additional four species were identified to family level only.  This data is now part of Natural Resources Wales\u0027 Welsh Invertebrate Dataset.  This datasets is already available on the NBN Gateway and therefore is not required to be published by Natural Resources Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-12-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18199\tNRW_DS116225\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.649Z\t897\tValid\tEditable\tSpatial Flood Defences with Standardised Attributes (archived data)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323235\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Defences with standardised attributes.lyr  Stored in AMX but made available on the Y drive\tData Custodian: Mark Pugh- Reservoir Safety Team Metadata Contact - Data Services Team\tThis dataset has been superseded by the dataset \"Flood Defence Structures\" (NRW_DS125266).  This dataset shows flood defences that have been built to protect against flooding from rivers and the sea. The defences shown provide different levels of flood protection and this is recognised in the risk classification shown in the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) map.   Flood defences that are not yet shown, and the areas that benefit from them, will be gradually added.    Flood defences do not remove the risk of flooding however and can be overtopped or fail in extreme weather conditions.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:28.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18185\tNRW_DS116226\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.200Z\t1597\tValid\tEditable\tRecorded Flood Outlines (archived data)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323236\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\ Recorded flood outlines.lyr\tCustodian: Nick Steel, Flood Risk Analysis/FCRM / (Previously) Mark Pugh, Flood \u0026amp; Operational Risk Management, NRW\tThis dataset has been archived and has been superceded by  NRW_DS116228 Historic Flood Outlines.  Recorded Flood Outlines contains the individual location outline and approved attributes for records of historic flooding. Please note that these records show flooding to the land and do not necessarily indicate that properties within the historic flood extents were flooded internally.   It is also possible that the pattern of flooding in this area has changed and that this area would now flood under different circumstances. In addition absence of a historic flood event for an area does not mean that the area has never flooded only that we do not currently have records of flooding in this area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-04-29\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:28.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18184\tNRW_DS116227\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.180Z\t1437\tValid\tEditable\tBathing Water Quality at Designated Beaches\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323237\tExtracted from the WISKI KwiQm database and available on Sharefile.  Originally this data was extracted from WIMS Following transformation from WIMS to WISKI, future data will be extracted from WISKI as follows (2017 - jointly extracted from WIMS \u0026amp; WISKI, 2018 onwards - WISKI only)    There are also 4 GIS layers that are created from the same data in WISKI KwiQM.  These have their own metadata entries:   EC sites: NRW_DS124755 X:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\EC Bathing Waters.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\EC Bathing Waters Classification.lyr  Non-EC sites:  NRW_DS123239 X:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\Non EC Bathing Waters.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\Non EC Bathing Waters Classification.lyr\tCustodian: Helena Sykes Officer, Environmental Information, Environmental Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) monitors bathing water sites identified under EC Bathing Waters Directive 2006/7/EC in Wales. Samples are taken throughout the bathing season from May to the end of September and analysed for two parameters: Escherichia coli and intestinal enterococci. A classification of bathing water quality, generally based on 4 years of sample results, is produced for each bathing water at the end of each season.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-04\tpublication\tnull\thttps://environment.data.gov.uk/wales/bathing-waters/profiles/\t2026-02-05T09:34:12.186Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18182\tNRW_DS116228\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.945Z\t1557\tValid\tEditable\tRecorded Flood Extents\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323238\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Historic Floodmap.lyr\tInternal custodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group\tRecorded Flood Extents shows areas that have been recorded to have flooded in the past from rivers, the sea or surface water. The records come from a number of evidence sources including Natural Resources Wales, its predecessors or other Risk Management Authorities.    It is possible that the pattern of flooding in an area may have changed and would now flood under different circumstances. In addition, the absence of a recorded flood extent does not mean that the area has never flooded, only that we do not currently have records of flooding in this area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-11-27\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/346/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_HISTORIC_FLOODMAP\t2025-11-27T15:21:29.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18180\tNRW_DS116229\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.822Z\t1054\tValid\tEditable\tEmissions Inventory\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323239\tZ:\\Archive\\Sampling Monitoring \u0026amp; Analysis Reporting\\Reporting\\Statutory Reporting\\Industrial Emissions\tCustodian: Amy Healey, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting\tThe Emissions Inventory (EI) dataset collects data from regulated industrial activities and includes annual emissions to air and controlled waters, off-site transfers of waste and transfers of pollutants to wastewater.   The NRW EI includes all of the data from Wales that is published on the UK Pollutant Release and Transfer Register (UK PRTR) but also includes further emissions that are under the UK PRTR threshold.   The published dataset has been redacted to not include confidential permits.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-11-17\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/5879\t2025-12-01T17:12:03.061Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18183\tNRW_DS116230\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.199Z\t1959\tValid\tEditable\tSource Protection Zones (SPZ) Merged\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323330\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SPZ Source Protection Zones Merged.lyr\tDave Jones- Technical Specialist GW\tSource Protection Zones [Merged] have been created as public facing boundaries where individual groundwater bodies within Source Protection Zones (SPZ) have been dissolved so only the outer boundaries are shown. SPZs are defined around large and public potable groundwater abstraction sites. The purpose of SPZs is to provide additional protection to safeguard drinking water quality through constraining the proximity of an activity that may impact upon a drinking water abstraction.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/400/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_Source_Protection_Zones\t2026-01-12T14:39:55.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18181\tNRW_DS116231\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.931Z\t915\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Storage Areas\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323331\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Floodmap Flood Storage Areas.lyr  Data is extracted from Asset Management eXpert (AMX) software.\tInternal Custodian: Wyn Davies, Flood Risk Manager (Asset Management), Flood and Incident Management Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tFlood Storage Areas show those areas that act as a balancing reservoir, storage basin or balancing pond. Their purpose is to attenuate an incoming flood peak to a flow level that can be accepted by the downstream channel. It may also delay the timing of a flood peak so that its volume is discharged over a longer time interval.   Flood Storage Areas that are not yet shown will be gradually added as information becomes available.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-12-17\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/347/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_FLOODMAP_FLOOD_STORAGE\t2025-05-07T11:03:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18179\tNRW_DS116232\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.602Z\t663\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Map: Flood Zone 3 (archived data)\t© CNC/NRW and Centre for Ecology and Hydrology © NERC (CEH) Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the Centre for Ecology and Hydrology © NERC (CEH). Defra, Met Office and DARD Rivers Agency © Crown copyright. © Cranfield University. © James Hutton Institute. Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right 2015. Land and Property Services © Crown copyright and database right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323332\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Floodmap Flood Zone 3.lyr\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FRIM, NRW.\tThis dataset has been archived and has been superseded by Flood Map for Planning (NRW_DS125076).   The Flood Map shows the areas across Wales that could be affected by flooding from rivers or the sea. It also shows flood defences and the areas that benefit from them.   Flood Map is designed to raise awareness among the public local authorities and other organisations of the likelihood of flooding and to encourage people living and working in areas prone to flooding to find out more and take appropriate action.   The Flood Map includes several layers of information, this layer is: Flood Zone 3, which is NRWs best estimate of the areas of land with a 100 to 1 (or greater) chance of flooding each year from rivers or with a 200 to 1 chance (or greater) of flooding each year from the sea.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18178\tNRW_DS116233\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.601Z\t681\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Defences without Standardised Attributes (archived data)\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323333\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Floodmap Flood Defences without attributes.lyr Stored in AMX but made available On the Y drive\tMark Pugh, Principal Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group, FIRM\tThis dataset has been superseded by the dataset \"Flood Defence Structures\" (NRW_DS125266).  This dataset shows flood defences that have been built to protect against flooding from rivers and the sea. The defences shown provide different levels of flood protection and this is recognised in the risk classification shown in the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) map. Flood defences that are not yet shown, and the areas that benefit from them, will be gradually added.   Flood defences do not remove the risk of flooding however and can be overtopped or fail in extreme weather conditions.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-05-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-24T09:55:52.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18177\tNRW_DS116234\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.459Z\t1013\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Map: Areas Benefiting from Flood Defences (archived data)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323334\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Floodmap Areas Benefitting from Flood Defence.lyr  Extracted from the FloodGeodatabase\tCustodian: Wyn Davies, Flood Risk Manager (Asset Management), Flood and Incident Management, NRW\tThis dataset has been superceded by the \"Areas Benefitting from Flood Defences\" dataset (NRW_DS124843) which is displayed within the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) map.   The Flood Map is a spatial dataset that shows the areas across Wales that could be affected by flooding from rivers or the sea. It also shows flood defences and the areas that benefit from them.   Flood Map is designed to raise awareness among the public, local authorities and other organisations of the likelihood of flooding and to encourage people living and working in areas prone to flooding to find out more and take appropriate action.   The Flood Map includes several layers of information, this layer is: Areas Benefiting from Flood Defences, which shows those areas that would benefit from the presence of defences in a 1 percent fluvial / 0.5 percent tidal flood event.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-12-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T10:55:21.351Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18176\tNRW_DS116235\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.383Z\t914\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Map: Flood Zone 2 (archived data)\t© CNC/NRW and Centre for Ecology and Hydrology © NERC (CEH) Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the Centre for Ecology and Hydrology © NERC (CEH). Defra, Met Office and DARD Rivers Agency © Crown copyright. © Cranfield University. © James Hutton Institute. Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right 2015. Land and Property Services © Crown copyright and database right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323335\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Floodmap Flood Zone 2.lyr\tCustodian:  Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM, NRW\tThis dataset has been archived and has been superseded by Flood Map for Planning (NRW_DS125076).  Flood Map shows the areas across Wales that could be affected by flooding from rivers or the sea. It also shows flood defences and the areas that benefit from them.   Flood Map is designed to raise awareness among the public local authorities and other organisations of the likelihood of flooding and to encourage people living and working in areas prone to flooding to find out more and take appropriate action.   The Flood Map includes several layers of information, this layer is: Flood Zone 2, which is NRWs best estimate of the areas of land between Zone 3 and the extent of the flood from rivers or the sea with a 1000 to 1 chance of flooding in any year. It includes those areas defined in Flood Zone 3.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18234\tNRW_DS116236\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.640Z\t1276\tValid\tEditable\tHistoric Landfill Sites\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323336\tX:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\Historic Landfill Sites.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Gareth E Lewis - SUW Advisor Wales, Knowledge Strategy and Planning.  Also could contact: Tyrone (Ty) Ward - PPC/RSR Officer 1, PPC Team\tThis dataset is now Static and won’t be updated. However, this dataset should still be used for information on permits that were historic before came under EPR regulations.   This dataset should be used in conjunction with the Historic Landfill Sites points dataset (NRW_DS125453), which is the most comprehensive dataset for recently expired, surrendered, or revoked landfill sites.    Historic Landfill Sites is a spatial (polygon) dataset. These are landfill sites which have been taken off the Authorised Landfill Sites when the waste licence status changes to either:   - Licence expired: Some licences issued under the Control of Pollution Act 1974 were time limited and expired on the date specified in the licence.  - Licence Revoked: Whereby the licence has been entirely revoked and is no longer in force; or   - Licence Surrendered: Operator has successfully surrendered the licence which is no longer in force.    Under the Town and Country Planning (General Development Procedure) Order 1995 Local Planning Authorities have to consult with the former Environment Agency Wales, now part of Natural Resources Wales, about all applications they receive to develop land within 250 metres of landfill sites (including any land that has been used as a landfill site within the past 30 years or is likely to be used as one in the near future). The Historic Landfill dataset was created to help fulfil our statutory responsibility to Local Planning Authorities by supplying information on the risks posed by landfill sites for development within 250m.    The data is the most comprehensive and consistent national historic landfill dataset and defines the location of, and provides specific attributes for, known historic (closed) landfill sites, i.e. sites where there is no PPC permit or waste management licence currently in force. This includes sites that existed before the waste licensing regime and sites that have been licensed in the past but where this licence has been revoked, ceased to exist or surrendered and a certificate of completion has been issued.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-08-27\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/401/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_Historic_Landfill_Sites\t2025-12-19T11:54:20.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18232\tNRW_DS116237\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.529Z\t1731\tValid\tEditable\tDetailed River Network\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323337\tX:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Detailed River Network.lyr   DO NOT SET TO EXPORT or publish this data - As this data is old and has been superseded by an OS product (see metadata details)\tKeith Jones Spatial Service Team       DO NOT SET TO EXPORT - As this data is old and has been superseded by an OS product (see metadata details)\tThe Detailed River Network (DRN) is a large-scale, accurate and fully attributed digital river centreline covering Wales. The DRN is captured from the water features theme of the OS MasterMap topographic layer and built into a network using automated rules. Other input datasets and extensive local staff knowledge has been used to augment the core geometry to incorporate critical spatial detail and attribution, such as flow direction and path, not available from the OS mapping and to verify the accuracy of the centreline itself. The dataset has full-feature network geometry cross-referenced with OS MasterMap following Digital National Framework principles.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-07-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-05-15T09:09:49.234Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18225\tNRW_DS116238\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.309Z\t1317\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Warning Areas\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323338\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Warning Areas.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Andy Wall, Flood Risk Manager, NRW  Originators: EAW, NRW (via EA flood warning system as part of managed service)\tThis spatial Flood Warning Areas dataset are geographical areas where NRW expect flooding to occur and where NRW provide a Flood Warning Service.   Flood Warning Areas generally contain properties that are expected to flood from rivers or the sea. Specifically, Flood Warning Areas define locations within the Flood Warning Service Limit that represent a discrete community at risk of flooding. A discrete community is a recognised and named geographical community, which can be an urban area, a significant suburb of a large city or a village or a hamlet.   The purpose of Flood Warnings is to alert people that flooding is expected and they should take action to protect themselves and their property.   An online viewable version of this dataset is available here https://naturalresources.wales/our-evidence-and-reports/maps/flood-risk-map/?lang\u003den\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-25\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/349/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_FLOOD_WARNING\t2026-02-26T10:19:22.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18230\tNRW_DS116239\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.339Z\t1590\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Alert Areas\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323339\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Alert Areas.lyr\tCustodian: Andy Wall, Flood \u0026amp; Operational Risk Management\tFlood Alert Areas are geographical areas where it is possible for flooding to occur from rivers or the sea. A single Flood Alert Area may cover the floodplain within the Flood Warning Service Limit of multiple catchments of similar characteristics containing a number of Flood Warning Areas. A Flood Alert Area may also match that of a corresponding Flood Warning Area and warn for the possibility of flooding in that area. In some coastal locations a Flood Alert may be issued for spray or overtopping and be defined by a stretch of coastline. Practical and administrative factors may also influence the exact extent of a Flood Alert Area.    An online viewable version of the dataset is available here: https://naturalresources.wales/flooding/check-your-flood-risk-on-a-map-flood-risk-assessment-wales-map/?lang\u003den\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-25\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/348/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_FLOOD_WATCH_AREAS\t2026-02-26T10:20:15.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18235\tNRW_DS116240\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.617Z\t1774\tValid\tEditable\tNitrate Vulnerable Zones (NVZ)\tData may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution Statement: © CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323430\tx:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\NVZ Nitrate Vulnerable Zones 2013.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\NVZ Nitrate Vulnerable Zones 2010.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\NVZ Nitrate Vulnerable Zones 2008.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor, Water Quality Policy\tNitrate Vulnerable Zones (NVZs) were areas within Wales that contain surface water or groundwater that is susceptible to nitrate pollution from agricultural activities. They were designated in 2013 accordance with the requirements of the European Commission’s Nitrates Directive 91/676/EEC, which aimed to protect water quality across Europe by preventing nitrates from agricultural sources polluting ground and surface waters and by promoting the use of good farming practices. In April 2021 the designated NVZs in Wales were revoked by the introduction of the Water Resources (Control of Agricultural Pollution)(Wales) Regulations with some measures transitioning into law over a period of time. The transition periods do not apply to those farms located in previously designated NVZ  where all the measures within CoAPR apply  immediately. This dataset is relevant until all measures transition into law.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-01-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/404/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_Nitrate_Vulnerable_Zones_2013\t2025-05-02T15:16:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18218\tNRW_DS116241\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.176Z\t706\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts Cycle 1\t© CNC/NRW. Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology © NERC (CEH). Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right 2015. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323431\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD River Basin Districts Cycle 1\tInternal Contacts: Internal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team.\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts Cycle 1 is a spatial (polygon) dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD).  Article 2, clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...the area of land and sea, made up of one or more neighbouring river basins together with their associated ground waters and coastal waters\u0027.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/562/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_RIVERBASINDISTRICT_C1\t2025-03-07T15:52:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18226\tNRW_DS116242\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.357Z\t514\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 1\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323432\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Lake Waterbodies Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 1 (2008 -2013) is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing Water Framework Directive (WFD) attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Article 2, clause 5 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...a body of standing inland surface water\u0027. This dataset contains data related to the physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives that can be linked to water bodies by their unique identifiers.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/339/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_LAKES\t2025-12-19T11:52:56.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18223\tNRW_DS116243\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.294Z\t1147\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 1\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323433\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the WFD. Article 2, clause 7 of the WFD defines coastal waterbodies as \u0027...a surface water on the landward side of a line, every point of which is at a distance of one nautical mile on the seaward side from the nearest point of the baseline from which the breadth of territorial waters is measured, extending where appropriate up to the outer limit of transitional waters\u0027. Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives.   This dataset covers the layer for Cycle 1 of the Water framework Directive (2008 - 2013). The equivalent layer for Cycle 2 is covered under WFD Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 2.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/338/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_COASTAL\t2025-05-02T14:44:50.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18231\tNRW_DS116244\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.331Z\t302\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 1\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323434\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional (Estuarine) Waterbodies Cycle 1 (2008-2013) is a polygon dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 6 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...bodies of surface water in the vicinity of river mouths which are partly saline in character as a result of their proximity to coastal waters but are substantially influenced by freshwater flows\u0027. Transitional waterbodies were defined from Mean High Water.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-04-02\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/337/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_TRANSITIONAL\t2025-05-02T14:50:58.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18222\tNRW_DS116245\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.310Z\t465\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwater bodies Cycle 1\t© CNC/NRW Derived in part from 1:50,000 and 1:250,000 scale digital data under permission from British Geological Survey. ©NERC. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323435\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Groundwaterbodies Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwater bodies Cycle 1 is a spatial (polygon) dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD).  Article 2, clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...all water which is below the surface of the ground in the saturation zone and in direct contact with the ground or subsoil\u0027.  For the purposes of reporting under the WFD a groundwater body represent a distinct body of groundwater flow with a coherent flow unit including recharge and discharge areas with little flow across the boundaries.  These reflect hydrogeological characteristics containing information on flow and stage properties, recharge and vulnerability to pollution.  This has been undertaken through defining aquifers into different types and broken into catchment units at Catchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) scale. Since water bodies are attributed with a unique identifier (EA_WB_ID) this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-10-14\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3582/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_groundwater_bodies_c1\t2025-05-08T07:45:32.522Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18227\tNRW_DS116246\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.353Z\t535\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Monitoring Network Cycle 1\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323436\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Monitoring Network Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) Monitoring Network Cycle 1 (2008-2013) is a point dataset that contains the location of monitoring sites used by Natural Resources Wales in the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). The network consists of numerous monitoring sites used to record various parameters. These data are intended to show the location and extent of the network and does not hold any actual monitored data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18233\tNRW_DS116247\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.565Z\t832\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Management Catchments Cycle 1\t© CNC/NRW and CEH ©NERC (Centre for Ecology and Hydrology). Contains National Statistics data © Crown copyright and database right 2011. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323437\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Management Catchments Cycle 1.lyr\tRob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Management Catchments Cycle 1 is a spatial (polygon) dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the WFD)  Management catchments are the unit of geography for which action plans are drafted in implementing the WFD. WFD Management Catchments have an action plan published that relates to all waterbodies that fall within its boundaries.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/559/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_MANAGEMENTCATCHMENT_C1\t2025-05-07T16:01:07.783Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18217\tNRW_DS116248\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.171Z\t1103\tValid\tEditable\tSource Protection Zones (SPZ) Individual\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323438\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SPZ Source Protection Zones Individual.lyr\tDave Jones, Senior Specialist Advisor Geoscience\tSPZs are defined around large and public potable groundwater abstraction sites. The purpose of SPZs is to provide additional protection to safeguard drinking water quality through constraining the proximity of an activity that may impact upon a drinking water abstraction. This is part of an initial screening process in assessing impacts to groundwater resources.   Zones around location sites are defined by groundwater travel time to an abstraction. This is determined through applying groundwater flow models run at the location of abstractions, inputting parameters such as flow direction, geology type, rainfall and hydrological boundaries. SPZs provide a visual representation of the increased risks as you get closer to the abstraction.   The following subdivisions are defined within SPZs:   Zone 1: (Inner Protection Zone) - This zone is defined by a travel time of 50-days or less from any point within the zone at, or below, the water table. Additionally, the zone has as a minimum a 50-metre radius. It is based principally on biological decay criteria and is designed to protect against the transmission of toxic chemicals and water-borne disease.   Zone 2: (Outer Protection Zone) - This zone is defined by the 400-day travel time from a point below the water table. Additionally this zone has a minimum radius of 250 or 500 metres, depending on the size of the abstraction. The travel time is derived from consideration of the minimum time required to provide delay, dilution and attenuation of slowly degrading pollutants.   Zone 3: (Total catchment) - This zone is defined as the total area needed to support the abstraction or discharge from the protected groundwater source.   A further Zone 4, or ‘Zone of Special Interest’ was previously defined for some groundwater sources. These zones highlighted areas (mainly on non-aquifers) where known local conditions meant that potentially polluting activities could impact on a groundwater source even though the area is outside the normal catchment of that source. In future this zone will be incorporated into one of the other zones (1, 2 or 3), whichever is appropriate in the particular case.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-01-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T13:46:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18228\tNRW_DS116249\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.368Z\t985\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAuthorised Landfill Sites\t© CNC/NRW  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323439\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Waste\\Active Authorised Landfill Boundary.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Gareth E Lewis - SUW Advisor Wales, Knowledge Strategy and Planning  Also could contact: Tyrone (Ty) Ward - PPC/RSR Officer 1, PPC Team  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW       The publish data is a redacted version of the whole dataset - as the non redacted version includes names and addresses.\tThis dataset has now been discontinued and won’t be updated. The permit information may no longer be correct and the boundary may no longer reflect the actual site anymore, as sites may have expanded their perimeter to accommodate additional capacity. Also, this dataset will not show landfills that have been created after 2013 or may contain landfill sites that are now expired, surrendered or revoked.  The authorised landfill site is a spatial (polygon) dataset containing the boundaries of landfill sites which are currently authorised by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under The Environmental Permitting (England and Wales) Regulations 2010. The Environmental Permitting (England and Wales) Regulations provide industry, regulators and others with a single extended permitting and compliance system. Landfill permits were previously authorised by a Waste Management Licence,  or a Pollution Prevention and Control (PCC) Permit, which have subsequently become Environmental  (EPR) Permit, and are recorded within a NRW database. Pre 1996 landfill sites data was managed and collated by Local Authorities, this duty was then transferred to Environment Agency, and was taken over by NRW in 2013.  INFORMATION WARNING: This dataset has now been discontinued and won’t be updated. The permit information may no longer be correct and the and boundary may no longer not reflect the actual site anymore, as sites may have expanded their perimeter to accommodate additional capacity. Also this dataset will not show landfills that have been created after 2013 or may contain landfill sites that are now expired, surrendered or revoked.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-04-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:43:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18221\tNRW_DS116250\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.177Z\t818\tValid\tEditable\tReservoir Flood Map Maximum Flood Outline Extent\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323530\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Reservoir Maximum Flood Extent.lyr\tData custodian - Phillip Morgan, Team Leader, Reservoir Regulation\tData layer showing individual reservoir flood maps for 225 Large Raised Reservoirs including attributed data. Individual reservoirs may have up to 5 flood maps associated with them, based on separate breach locations.   The data shows the maximum extent of flood should the reservoir be breached, although the location of the reservoir can be inferred it is not explicitly shown on the maps.     Reservoir flooding is extremely unlikely to happen. There has been no loss of life in the UK from reservoir flooding since 1925.   All large reservoirs must be inspected and supervised by reservoir panel engineers. As the enforcement authority for the Reservoirs Act 1975 in Wales, we ensure that reservoirs are inspected regularly and essential safety work is carried out.    An online viewable version of this dataset is available here: https://flood-risk-maps.naturalresources.wales/?locale\u003den\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T11:49:02.307Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18229\tNRW_DS116251\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.377Z\t1311\tValid\tEditable\tLive Flood Warnings and Alerts Application Programming Interface (API)\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323531\tLive feed only: https://api-portal.naturalresources.wales/products/\tAndrew Wall Flood  Flood \u0026amp; Operational Risk Management, National Services\tFlood Warnings are provided by the constant monitoring of stations and forecasting flooding from rivers or the sea:    - Severe Flood Warning: Severe flooding. Danger to life.   - Flood Warning: Flooding is expected. Immediate action required.   - Flood Alert: Flooding is possible. Be prepared.   - Warning no longer in force: Flood warnings and flood alerts that have been removed in the last 24 hours.     Flood Warnings are available on our website and as a Live Feed. The live feed provides a summary of flood warnings (Alert, Warning and Severe) that are sent externally. The Flood Warnings Live Feed provides current status update of every Flood Warning Area in England and Wales. This is updated on the Flood Warning Information System (FWIS) service every minute although it is presented for external users every 15 minutes via the Data Distribution Hub where the XML can be securely downloaded (sFTP feed).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-06-13\tpublication\tnull\thttps://api-portal.naturalresources.wales/products\t2025-10-24T08:43:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18224\tNRW_DS116252\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.353Z\t984\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Extents\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323532\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Saltmarsh extents.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Bianca Bacchi- Rhymney and Ebbw A\u0026amp;R Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Services Team\tPolygon data layer showing the extent of Saltmarsh in Coastal and Transitional waters for use by both Flood and Coastal Risk Management and the implementation of Water Framework Directive. At the seaward end of the transect, the final demarcation will be where the saltmarsh vegetation cover has become so sparse it only covers 5% whether it is Upper, Mid, Lower or Pioneer saltmarsh.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-03-01\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/330/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_SALTMARSH_EXTENTS\t2025-12-19T10:08:33.089Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18219\tNRW_DS116253\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.241Z\t1183\tValid\tEditable\tEnvironmental Pollution Incidents\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323533\tNIRS up to 2016-02-29 WIRS from 2016-03-01 til present\tSean Moore, Incident Communications Centre Team Leader\tThis dataset contains details pollution incidents reported to Natural Resources Wales (NRW). Only substantiated and closed environmental protection incidents are included. Where these data indicate an incident occurred on a particular site or property no inference should be drawn that the site or property owner necessarily was responsible.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-23\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2642\t2026-02-24T15:29:40.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18216\tNRW_DS116254\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.174Z\t401\tValid\tEditable\tNational Receptor Dataset (NRD) 2011\t© Natural Resources Wales. All rights reserved. © Ordnance Survey AC0000849444 © Middlesex University, Flood Hazard Research Centre. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking re-use also require a licence from Ordnance Survey for the derived OS data and from Middlesex University for the derived Multi-Coloured Manual (MCM) data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323534\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Receptors Database (NRD) 2011.lyr   Reports - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/kdim/Data-Management/da/DataArchivingTNA/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?id\u003d%2Fteams%2Fkdim%2FData%2DManagement%2Fda%2FDataArchivingTNA%2FNRD%202011%20reports%2Ezip\u0026amp;parent\u003d%2Fteams%2Fkdim%2FData%2DManagement%2Fda%2FDataArchivingTNA\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group\tThe National Receptor Dataset (NRD) is a collection of risk receptors, typically residential and commercial property, primarily intended for use in flood and coastal erosion risk management.   NRD is a spatial dataset which contains a number of GIS layers categorised into themes of information including; buildings, environment, heritage, transport and utilities.   The NRD includes property points that are Copy Derived from OSMM AddressLayer2 with information on property type, floor area and Flood Hazard Research Centres Multi-Coloured Manual codes attributed.  It was designed to meet the needs of Preliminary Flood Risk Assessments and the Environment Agency\u0027s National Flood Risk Assessment although it can be used for other purposes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-11-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18220\tNRW_DS116255\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.179Z\t586\tValid\tEditable\tAreas Affecting Bathing Waters\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323535\tx:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\Areas Affecting Bathing Waters.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Helena Sykes, Lead Specialist Advisor, Monitoring Analysis, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning\tThis spatial dataset comprises of a polygon relating to each site identified under the Bathing Water Directive (76/160/EEC). These sites are drawn as a simple guide to aid the work of Natural Resources Wales and therefore these polygons have no formal status under the Bathing Water Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-04-20\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/326/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:Areas_Affecting_Bathing_Waters\t2025-11-06T15:54:34.258Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18215\tNRW_DS116256\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.766Z\t110\tValid\tEditable\tPotential Sites of Hydropower Opportunity\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323536\tX:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\River Obstructions.lyr\tGideon Carpenter, Hydrology and Water Resources Management, NRW\tThis data shows the location of opportunities for hydropower and the basic environmental sensitivity associated with exploiting them in Wales.   Barriers are identified and assessed in this project. The term barriers is used to describe sites with sufficient drop to provide a hydropower opportunity. They are mostly weirs, but could also be other man-made structures or natural features, such as waterfalls. The average maximum power generation capacity on a barrier was 45Kw, with a total potential capacity of 1178Mw.   Environmental sensitivities were assessed. This assessment considered the presence of fish species and whether the site has been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Almost half (46%) of these barriers are classified as highly sensitive, mostly because of the presence of migratory fish species such as salmon and eel. 27% are medium and high sensitivity, and the remainder are unclassified due to a lack of data.   When it is assumed that a new scheme has a fish pass built into it, the environmentally compatible opportunities increase considerably. Given the scale of the project and the data used, the results are not intended to replace any part of an individual site assessment. Instead, the dataset gives national and regional level overviews of the potential opportunities available, their locations, and their relative environmental sensitivity to exploitation.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-03T14:14:07.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18211\tNRW_DS116257\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.465Z\t1266\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRisk of Flooding from Reservoirs\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323537\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Reservoir Maximum Flood Extent.lyr\tData custodian - Phillip Morgan, Team Leader, Reservoir Regulation\tNRW is responsible for assuring the safety of the Welsh reservoirs by enforcing the Reservoirs Act 1975. Simplified inundation mapping was carried out nationally by the Environment Agency to provide a baseline assessment of all reservoirs falling within the Act. This work is now carried out by Natural Resources Wales (NRW). Reservoirs Flood Maps – Spatial Data are referred to externally as Risk of Flooding from Reservoirs.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:33.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18212\tNRW_DS116258\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.494Z\t931\tValid\tEditable\tCatchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) Boundaries\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323538\tX:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Catchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) Boundaries.lyr\tNRW Internal Custodian: Ben Anscombe, Principal Officer, Geographic Information Systems\tCatchment Abstraction Management Strategies (CAMS) assess the amount of water available in each river catchment.   The CAMS Boundaries are an external reference dataset giving an indication of where technical assessments of water abstraction have been undertaken.   CAMS helps to look at the balance between society, the economy and the environment. The technical assessment helps identify where water may be available for future use but also where water resource demands may be impacting the water balance.   The CAMS boundaries should be used on a national (Wales) scale to show the geographical distribution of CAMS and are not suitable for detailed technical assessments.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-11-22\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/410/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_CAMS_TECH_ASSESSMENT\t2025-12-19T11:36:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18213\tNRW_DS116259\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.531Z\t1065\tValid\tEditable\tCoastal Design - Extreme Sea Levels (archived data)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323539\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB confidence intervals.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB Estuary boundaries.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB extreme sea levels.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB Gauge data.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB Surge shapes.lyr\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM.\tThis dataset has now been superseded by data provided by the Environment Agency for England and Wales, please see metadata number EXT_DS123552 - \"Environment Agency: Coastal Flood Boundaries (CFB) publication 2018 - England and Wales (3rd Party Data)\".  This is a GIS dataset and supporting information providing design / extreme sea level and typical surge information around the coastline of Wales under present day conditions. This is a specialist dataset which informs on work commenced around the coast ranging from coastal flood modelling, scheme design, strategic planning and flood risk assessments.   The dataset contains the following layer components:  -  Extreme Sea Level Values: Extreme sea levels for a range of return periods  -  Extreme Sea Level Confidence Level: Confidence intervals providing allowances for uncertainty  -  Estuary Boundaries: Inland estuarine boundaries of the extreme sea levels  -  Surge Shape Locations: Reference to surge shape appropriate for each Class A gauge site and where to apply it  -  Tide Gauge Locations: Location of gauging stations\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-11-02\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18210\tNRW_DS116261\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.438Z\t1645\tValid\tEditable\tShoreline Management Plan Policies\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323631\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Management plan (SMP) 2.lyr\tInternal Custodians  Mark Pugh ,  FCRM Louise Pennington FCRM Nich Pritchard, FCRM\tA Shoreline Management Plan (SMP) is a large-scale assessment of the risks associated with coastal processes and helps reduce these risks to people and the developed, historic and natural environments. Coastal processes include tidal patterns, wave height, wave direction and the movement of beach and seabed materials.   The SMPs set the strategic policy direction for coastal management and identify the most sustainable approaches to managing the risks to the coast in the short term (Epoch 1 0-20 years), medium term (Epoch 2 20-50 years) and long term (Epoch 3 50-100 years).   A set of preferred policies are identified which are assigned to \u0027policy units\u0027 for each SMP epoch. A \u0027policy unit\u0027 (PU) is a length of shoreline where a separate shoreline management policy applies. PU\u0027s are defined by coastal areas that have similar characteristics in terms of coastal processes and assets at risk, that can be managed efficiently.   Four policy options are available for SMPs:  - Hold the Line (HTL): an aspiration to build or maintain artificial defences so that the current position of the shoreline remains. This can involve maintaining or changing the standard of protection.   - Advance the Line (ATL): by building new defences on the seaward side of the original defences. This is rarely used and is limited to policy units where there is significant land reclamation is considered. There are no policy units in Wales assigned ATL.  - Managed Realignment (MR): by allowing the shoreline to move backwards or forwards naturally but managing the process to direct it in certain areas.  - No Active Intervention (NAI): where there is no planned investment in coastal defences or operations, regardless of whether or not an artificial defence has existed previously.     The coastal management approach for a certain section of coast may change from the status quo where a policy may no longer be practical or acceptable over 100 years. Therefore, a combination of policies may be proposed over the length of the SMP.   The dataset identifies which of the second-generation Shoreline Management Plans are applicable to a particular stretch of the Welsh coastline and the policies assigned to policy units related to that particular area. This dataset is a polyline, spatial data layer for the Welsh coast only.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-12-17\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/5165/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_shoreline_management_plan_policies\t2025-11-06T15:56:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18214\tNRW_DS116262\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.491Z\t704\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGroundwater Vulnerability (1990 - 2008)\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. In some cases the dataset may be made available for non-commercial purposes . For commercial use please contact the British Geological Survey.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323632\tThis dataset is not available on the X drive.  But is stored in NRW\u0027s Geodatabases as a reference or archived dataset. this data can be put on the X Drive if needed, but more appropriate 2018 data is available on the X Drive. Feature Dataset: NRWGDBV.GISDATA.Designations Feature Class: NRWGDBV.GISDATA.GW_VULNERABILITY_ZONE Feature Class: NRWGDBV.GISDATA.GW_VULNERABILITY_ZONE_DRIFT\tCustodian: Dave Jones, Technical Specialist (Groundwater), Geoscience/Knowledge Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, NRW  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset of Groundwater Vulnerability between 1990 and 2008 residing as a reference or archived dataset in Natural Resources Wales\u0027 geodatabases. A newer version of this data is now available. This older 1990 - 2008 data consists of two polygon spatial layers available at a scale of 1:100,000.  These are Groundwater Vulnerability Zones, and Groundwater Vulnerability Zones Drift. The data broadly define areas relevant to the protection of groundwater.  The approach considers the vulnerability of the groundwater resources as a whole; and the specific importance of areas which for the catchments main sources of supply. Groundwater resources are assigned a vulnerability class [Groundwater Vulnerability 100K], based on soil type and the underlying geology only (e.g. depth to groundwater is not considered): -  Variably permeable groundwater with low leaching potential -  Variably permeable groundwater with intermediate leaching potential -  Variably permeable groundwater high leaching potential -  Highly permeable groundwater with intermediate leaching potential -  Highly permeable groundwater with high leaching potential -  Highly permeable groundwater with low leaching potential The Groundwater Vulnerability data is intended to be used to indicate where groundwater resources may be vulnerable from activities carried out on the surface land.  Other information, such as depth of groundwater and thickness and type of overlying cover will always be required for a site-specific assessment. An assessment of the vulnerability of groundwaters to diffuse pollution is also included as the Groundwater Vulnerability Drift 100K spatial layer (\u0027Drift\u0027 is transported rock debris overlying the solid bedrock) - it shows the distribution of low permeability drift deposits and should be used in conjunction with Groundwater Vulnerability 100K.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-07-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:39:04.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18207\tNRW_DS116263\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.229Z\t1523\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) Sensitive Areas - Eutrophic\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323633\tX:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\UWWTD Sensitive Areas - Eutrophic.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Geraint Weber Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tThe Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017 (WFD Regulations 2017) requires specification of protected areas for those areas designated as nutrient sensitive areas.   The Urban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) Sensitive Areas Eutrophic layer is a replacement layer of the Welsh only sites previously designated under the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive. It shows the extent of Urban Wastewater Treatment Regulation  (UWWTR) sensitive areas (eutrophic).     The UWWTR describes eutrophication as \u0027the enrichment of water by nutrients, especially compounds of nitrogen and/or phosphorous, causing an accelerated growth of algae and higher forms of plant life to produce an undesirable disturbance to the balance of organisms present in the water and to the quality of the water concerned\u0027.   The UWWTR regulates the collection and treatment of waste water from homes and from industry. In the UK, the Directive is implemented through the Urban Wastewater Treatment Regulations 1994. Under these Regulations, water bodies that are (or may soon become) eutrophic should be designated as sensitive areas by Defra. This applies to still fresh waters, rivers, estuaries and coastal waters.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-08-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/5375/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_uwwtr\t2025-12-19T11:38:55.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18208\tNRW_DS116264\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.297Z\t1202\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) Sensitive Areas - Bathing Waters\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323634\tUpdated every 4 years  X:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\UWWTR Sensitive Areas Bathing Waters.lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team Internal Custodian - Geraint Weber\tThe Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017 (WFD Regulations 2017) requires specification of protected areas for those areas designated as nutrient sensitive areas.     The Urban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) Sensitive Areas - Bathing Waters layer is a replacement layer of the Welsh only sites previously designated under the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive. It shows the extent of Urban Wastewater Treatment Regulation  (UWWTR) sensitive areas Bathing Waters.     The Urban Wastewater Treatment Regulations regulates the collection and treatment of waste water from homes and from industry. In the UK, the directive is implemented through the Urban Wastewater Treatment regulations 1994. Under these regulations, water bodies where treatment more stringent than secondary is necessary to fulfil the requirements of the Bathing Waters Directive should be designated as sensitive areas by Defra.   This dataset shows areas currently designated as UWWTD bathing water sensitive areas.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-08-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/5375/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_uwwtr\t2025-05-07T08:46:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18209\tNRW_DS116265\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.307Z\t669\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD) Sensitive Areas - Nitrates\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323635\tX:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\UWWTD Sensitive Areas - Nitrates.lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team Internal Custodian - Julie Boswell, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tThis dataset is a shapefile showing the extent of Urban Wastewater Treatment Directive (91/271/EEC) (UWWTD) sensitive areas (nitrate).   The Urban Wastewater Treatment Directive (91/271/EEC) regulates the collection and treatment of waste water from homes and from industry. In the UK, the directive is implemented through the Urban Wastewater Treatment regulations 1994. Under these regulations, water bodies that are used as sources for drinking water and which have high nitrate concentrations (as defined by Council Directive 75/440/EEC of 16th June 1975) should be designated as sensitive areas by Defra.   This dataset consists of rivers currently designated as UWWTD nitrate sensitive areas.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T11:35:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18206\tNRW_DS116266\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.175Z\t940\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Risk Areas (Cycle 1)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323636\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Risk Areas.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM.\tThe Cycle 1 Flood Risk Areas are those communities in Wales where most significant flood risks exist from surface water flooding only (from surface water run-off, groundwater and ordinary watercourses). These areas were identified for the purpose of the first Preliminary Flood Risk Assessment under the Flood Risk Regulations 2009 using guidance produced by the Welsh Government to identify areas where flood risk is an issue for greater than 5000 people.   The Flood Risk Areas were first identified in 2011 until being replaced in 2018 by the Cycle 2 Flood Risk Areas.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-04-21\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/435/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_FLOOD_RISK_AREAS\t2025-12-19T10:57:08.084Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18256\tNRW_DS116267\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.980Z\t417\tValid\tEditable\tInventory of Closed Mining Waste Facilities\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323637\tX:\\Industrial\\Mines\\Inventory of Closed Mining Waste Facilities.lyr\tRebecca Favager, Regulation Policy and Approach Manager Philip Goodman, Environment officer (Metal mines project manager), Environment management, NRW\tThe European Mining Waste Directive (2006/21/EC) requires Member States to create an inventory of closed or abandoned mine waste facilities causing serious environmental impacts, and to make this inventory available to the public. A waste facility means any area designated for the accumulation or deposit of extractive waste.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-03-18\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/420/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_MINING_WASTE_CLOSED_SITES\t2025-12-19T11:50:14.107Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18249\tNRW_DS116268\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.806Z\t682\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 2\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323638\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD River Waterbodies Catchments Cycle2 2015.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 2 2018 (Interim).lyr\tRob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 2 are a polygon dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive.   Catchments are defined as an area of land from which all surface run-off flows through a series of streams, rivers and, possibly, lakes to a particular point in the water course such as a river confluence. Since rivers are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to the WFD river waterbody to which it relates.  Updated to 2018 version.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-08-01\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3463/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_river_waterbody_catchments_c2\t2025-04-02T16:23:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18260\tNRW_DS116269\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.035Z\t916\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbodies Cycle 2\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323639\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Rivers Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 2 2018 (Interim).lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team  Internal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbodies Cycle 2 are a spatial (polygon) dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. \u0027WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 2\u0027 is a subset extracted from the NRW\u0027s Detailed River Network (DRN).   Updated to 2018 version\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-02-14\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/6196/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_river_waterbodies_c2\t2025-05-02T14:50:56.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18253\tNRW_DS116270\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.982Z\t799\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 2\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323730\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2 X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Lakes Cycle 2 2018 (Interim).lyr\tRob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS.\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 2 is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing Water Framework Directive (WFD) attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive.   Article 2, clause 5 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...a body of standing inland surface water\u0027. There is data on the physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives that can be linked to waterbodies by their unique identifiers.   Artificial and modified lake waterbodies are included within this dataset, however, generally only lakes above \u0026gt; 50 hectares were assessed under the WFD except for lakes in protected areas, where a minimum of 5.0 ha was used. Lakes below this threshold are not included within this dataset unless allocated as Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI).   Each waterbody has been assigned \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027, which is a unique identifier that enables a link to WFD attributes. These data apply to Cycle 2 of the Water Framework Directive.   Updated to 2018 version.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/331/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_LAKES_C2\t2025-12-19T11:51:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18265\tNRW_DS116271\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.066Z\t1043\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 2\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323731\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team Internal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 7 of the WFD defines coastal waterbodies as \u0027...a surface water on the landward side of a line, every point of which is at a distance of one nautical mile on the seaward side from the nearest point of the baseline from which the breadth of territorial waters is measured, extending where appropriate up to the outer limit of transitional waters\u0027. Coastal waters were defined by territorial waters 1 nautical mile from the Mean High Water coastline taken directly from OS 1:50K MeridianTM 2. The delineation between coastal and estuarine waters was delineated by the Environment Agency defined transitional waterbodies. Waterbodies are also split and assigned to River Basin Districts. Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives. This dataset covers the layer for Cycle 2 of the Water framework Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://mapdata.llyw.cymru/capabilities/layer/431/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://mapdata.llyw.cymru/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_COASTAL_C2\t2025-03-07T15:58:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18250\tNRW_DS116272\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.904Z\t760\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 2\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323732\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tInternal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\t\u0027Water Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional Waterbodies\u0027 is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 6 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...bodies of surface water in the vicinity of river mouths which are partly saline in character as a result of their proximity to coastal waters but are substantially influenced by freshwater flows\u0027.   Transitional waterbodies were defined from Mean High Water boundaries, taken directly from OS 1:50K MeridianTM 2, and NRW estuarine boundaries defined for the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD). Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives. These data apply to Cycle 2 of the Water Framework Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/430/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_TRANSITIONAL_C2\t2025-03-07T15:58:48.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18248\tNRW_DS116273\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.789Z\t1144\tValid\tEditable\tRisk of Flooding from Surface Water\t© CNC/NRW and Centre for Ecology and Hydrology © NERC (CEH) Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the Centre for Ecology and Hydrology © NERC (CEH). Defra, Met Office and DARD Rivers Agency © Crown copyright. © Cranfield University. © James Hutton Institute. Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right 2015. Land and Property Services © Crown copyright and database right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323733\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Surface Water\\Surface Water Flood Risk 30.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Surface Water\\Surface Water Flood Risk 100.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Surface Water\\Surface Water Flood Risk 1000.lyr\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM, NRW Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW       Data will not be added to Lle as the packaged data is too large and complex, will remain as a ShareFile download\tThe surface water flood maps give an indication of the broad areas likely to be at risk of surface water flooding.  This includes flooding that takes place from the surface runoff generated by rainwater (including snow and other precipitation) that: (a) is on the surface of the ground (whether or not it is moving), and (b) has not yet entered a watercourse, drainage system or public sewer.  The Flood Map for Surface Water pick out natural drainage channels, rivers, low areas in floodplains, and flow paths between buildings. But it does not indicate flooding caused by local rainfall.  It does not show flooding that occurs from overflowing watercourses, drainage systems or public sewers caused by catchment-wide rainfall events or river flow.  A national model has been run for 1 in 30, 1 in 100 and 1 in 1000 year rainfall events.  It has been modelled on a 2 metre square grid.  Lead Local Flood Authorities were consulted and where available locally held model outputs have been incorporated into the maps.  This dataset has been superseded by the new National Flood Hazard Maps 2019 - NRW_DS124790\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-10-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18259\tNRW_DS116274\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.924Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFlood Map for Surface Water Complex Package\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323734\t\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:52:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18257\tNRW_DS116275\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.982Z\t506\tValid\tEditable\tRisk of Flooding from Rivers and Seas (RoFRS) Property Flood Likelihood Category (archived data)\tContains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology © NERC (CEH). Defra, Met Office and DARD Rivers Agency © Crown copyright. © Cranfield University. © James Hutton Institute. Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right 2015. Land \u0026amp; Property Services © Crown copyright and database right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323735\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116275\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Flood Risk Manager\tThis dataset is now static. It is therefore no longer updated and is out of date.  This dataset is a product of a national assessment of flood risk for Wales produced using local expertise. This dataset is produced using [Risk of Flooding from Rivers and Sea] which shows the chance of flooding from rivers and/or the sea, based on cells of 50m. Each cell is allocated one of four flood risk categories, taking into account flood defences and their condition.   This dataset uses OS data to assign one of four flood risk categories to each property, based simply on the category allocated to the cell that the property is in. Individual addresses are not provided, but OS referencing is included to enable the data to be linked to address databases.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-06-23T14:56:16.595Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18262\tNRW_DS116276\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.060Z\t1154\tValid\tEditable\tRisk of Flooding from Rivers and Seas (RoFRS) (archived data)\t© CNC/NRW and CEH. Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the UK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology © UKCEH. Defra, Met Office and DARD Rivers Agency © Crown copyright. © Cranfield University. © James Hutton Institute. Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right 2015. Land and Property Services © Crown copyright and database right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323736\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Risk of Flooding from Rivers and Sea (RoFRS).lyr\tDatasets custodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM, NRW\tThis dataset has been superceded by the Flood Risk Assessment Wales dataset. It is therefore no longer updated. You can still view your risk of flooding by postcode on our website https://naturalresources.wales/flooding/check-your-flood-risk-by-postcode/?lang\u003den   This is a national assessment of flood risk for Wales produced using local expertise. The dataset shows the chance of flooding from rivers and/or the sea, based on cells of 50 m. Each cell is allocated one of four flood risk categories, taking into account flood defences and their condition. No more detailed resolution is provided.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-09-20\tpublication\tnull\thttps://naturalresources.wales/evidence-and-data/maps/long-term-flood-risk/?lang\u003den\t2025-06-23T14:47:41.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18251\tNRW_DS116277\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.983Z\t1043\tValid\tEditable\tRisk of Flooding from Rivers and Seas (RoFRS) Postcode Flood Likelihood Category (archived data)\tContains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology © NERC (CEH). Defra, Met Office and DARD Rivers Agency © Crown copyright. © Cranfield University. © James Hutton Institute. Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right 2015. Land \u0026amp; Property Services © Crown copyright and database right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323737\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116277\tDataset custodian: Mark Pugh, Flood Risk Manager\tThis dataset is now static. It is therefore no longer updated and is out of date.  This dataset is a product of a national assessment of flood risk for England produced using local expertise. This dataset is produced using [Risk of Flooding from Rivers and Sea] which shows the chance of flooding from rivers and/or the sea, based on cells of 50m. Each cell is allocated one of four flood risk categories, taking into account flood defences and their condition.   This dataset uses OS address data and Royal Mail postcode data to show how many properties are in each of four flood risk categories in each postcode, based simply on the category allocated to the cell that each property is in.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-06-23T15:00:03.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18255\tNRW_DS116278\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.983Z\t1168\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Operational Catchments Cycle 2\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323738\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Operational Catchments Cycle2 Draft.lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team Internal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tOperational catchments are a way of grouping Water Framework Directive (WFD) waterbodies together at a meaningful scale smaller than the management catchment or River Basin District. The river waterbodies are not always hydrologically linked as they may have been grouped based on pressures and measures. This data set is based on the surface water boundaries of operational catchments and excludes the underlying Groundwater data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/432/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_OPERATIONAL_CATCHMENTS_C2\t2025-03-07T15:52:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18264\tNRW_DS116279\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.061Z\t1473\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts Cycle 2\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323739\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD River Basin Districts Cycle2 Draft.lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team Internal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts (RBD) is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...the area of land and sea, made up of one or more neighbouring river basins together with their associated groundwaters and coastal waters…\u0027.   River Basin Districts have been delineated by using River Catchments as \\building blocks\\\" that have been aggregated together within a GIS ensuring that WFD rivers do not intersect boundaries. Coastal and transitional waterbodies are also merged and assigned to a river basin district. River Catchments were delineated through use of flow data and a digital terrain model run through a hydrological model. These data apply to Cycle 2 of the Water Framework Directive.\"\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/334/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_RIVER_BASIN_DISTRICTS_C2\t2025-03-07T15:58:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18263\tNRW_DS116280\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.059Z\t919\tValid\tEditable\tNational Receptor Dataset (NRD) 2014\t© CNC/NRW, © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. © Middlesex University, Flood Hazard Research Centre. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking re-use also require a licence from OS and Middlesex University.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323830\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Receptors Database (NRD) 2014.lyr\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group\tThe National Receptor Dataset (NRD) is a collection of risk receptors primarily intended for use in flood and coastal erosion risk management. The NRD includes property points that are Copy Derived from Ordnance Survey (OS) AddressBase Premium with information on property type, floor area and Flood Hazard Research Centres Multi-Coloured Manual codes attributed.  It is designed as the consequence information (particular properties) for risk assessments with flood hazards.   The version of the NRD described here is the NRD2014. This version of the NRD supersedes NRD2011, which was released in November 2011.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18254\tNRW_DS116281\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.972Z\t779\tValid\tEditable\tBetter Woodlands for Wales Case Boundaries\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323831\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Better Woods For Wales Boundary.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Better Woods for Wales SCDB.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tBetter Woodlands for Wales (BBW) is the Forestry Commission Wales (FCW) grant scheme designed to meet the Woodland Strategy vision. The vision of the strategy for Wales to be known for its high-quality woodlands that enhance the landscape, are appropriate to local conditions and have a diverse mixture of species and habitats. The BBW scheme has been developed in consultation with woodland owners and their agents as well as our partner organisations. As a result, the new scheme places greater emphasis on good quality woodland management and offers grants specially designed for Welsh woodlands. Grant aid is now based upon an approved long term management plan. This plan must meet the minimum standards under the UK Woodland Assurance Scheme. BWW was closed to new applications in April 2012.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-11-30\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/328/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_BWW_CASE_BOUNDARIES\t2025-03-07T15:59:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18247\tNRW_DS116283\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.703Z\t453\tValid\tEditable\tWoodland Dedications\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323833\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Dedications.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThe Dedication woodland Scheme (Basis I and II) was introduced in 1947 in order to encourage landowners to retain their land in forestry and to introduce good forestry practice. Basis III was introduced in 1974, providing grants for new planting and additional supplements for broadleaves. The Dedication Scheme was closed to new applicants in 1981. Land still under Dedication could continue to be within the scheme but Dedication would terminate on a change of ownership. Dedication schemes without a Plan of Operations and therefore receiving no grant, are deemed to be under Negative Covenant.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-09-26\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/419/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_DEDICATED_WOODLANDS\t2025-03-07T16:51:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18252\tNRW_DS116284\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.981Z\t1349\tValid\tEditable\tFelling Licence Applications\t© CNC/NRW. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Publicly available data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323834\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Felling Licences\\Felling Licence Applications.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team\tAnyone wishing to fell trees must ensure that a licence or permission under a grant scheme has been issued by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) before any felling is carried out or that one of the exceptions apply.  The applicant would normally need to get permission from the NRW to fell growing trees. This is usually given in a Felling Licence or an approval under a Dedication Scheme.   This dataset provides a record of all approved Felling License Applications in Wales and type of license approved.  The data available to the public under an Open Government Licence has personal information redacted.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/397/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_FELLING_LICENCE_APPLICATIONS\t2026-02-23T11:18:56.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18246\tNRW_DS116285\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.638Z\t769\tValid\tEditable\tNational Inventory of Woodland and Trees\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323835\tX:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Inventory of Woodland \u0026amp; Trees (GB) - 2002.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset was created by Forestry Commission Wales (FCW) and now remains as a static archive of the data. This file contains Forestry Commission\u0027s previous survey, the National Inventory of Woodland and Trees, and includes spatial data and associated metadata for the whole of Great Britain. This dataset shows all areas of woodland over 2ha in Great Britain and the Interpreted Forest Type (IFT) of those woodlands. The dataset includes FC new planting and new Woodland Grant Schemes as of 31st March 2002.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-10-29\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/436/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_NIWT\t2025-02-04T10:45:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18261\tNRW_DS116287\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.049Z\t542\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Recreation Routes\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323837\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\NRW Forest Recreation Routes.lyr\tCustodian: Geraint Morgan and Arpana Chunihal, National Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThe usage, name and grade of the series of recreation segments that link together to form linear recreation features, for example, mountain bike trails.  -  Walking:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment e.g. Nature trail, heritage trail, education trail, forest walk.  -  Cycling:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment e.g. Mountain bike route, road bike, downhill, cross-country, family, rough route.  -  Equestrian:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment e.g. Carriage route, standard. -  Forest Drive:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment e.g. Toll, Toll free.  -  Running:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment.  -  Huskies:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment.  -  Emergency Services:  Must be an agreed access route for emergency services (Fire, ambulance, mountain rescue).  -  Rally:  Must be an approved rally route.  Other Route Types are further split into Route Subtype; Nature Trail, Sculpture Trail, Heritage Trail, Sensory Trail, Education Trail, Play Trail, Downhill, Cross Country, Family, Carriage Route, Toll, Toll Free, Fire, Ambulance, Mountain Rescue, Forest Walk, Trim Trail, All ability Access Trail, Mountain Bike, Road Bike, Standard, Cross Country Ski, Segway, Other. Where appropriate routes are graded; Easy, Moderate, Difficult; Green - Easy, Blue - Moderate, Red - Difficult, Black - Severe, Orange - Bike Park, Forest road or similar.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-01-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/343/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_GB_RECREATION_ROUTES\t2025-10-10T07:51:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18258\tNRW_DS116288\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.980Z\t883\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Recreation Areas\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323838\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\NRW Forest Recreation Areas.lyr\tCustodian: Geraint Morgan and Arpana Chunihal, National Forest Planning Team     Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tA recreation area feature which is managed and has a physical boundary (e.g. a car park, play area or building). There are a number of Asset Types: -  Vehicle:  Purpose built and installed NRW asset, or regularly used natural feature, which has management activity and investment e.g. Car park, layby.  -  Visitor area:  Purpose built and installed NRW asset, which has management activity and investment e.g. BBQ area, picnic, education, events, arboretum.  -  Sports:  Purpose built and installed NRW asset (or sub-let), which has management activity and investment e.g. Archery, bike park, ropes course, GoApe.  -  Building:  Purpose built and installed NRW asset, which has management activity and investment e.g. Visitor centre, toilet, cycle hire, catering, shelter.  -  Play:  NRW area designated for play activities, which has management activity and investment e.g. Deb building, play area, paddling.  -  Accommodation:  Purpose built and installed NRW asset, which has management activity and investment e.g. Camp site, cabin site, caravan site, wild camp.  - Ponds/Lakes:  Natural or man-made feature, which has management activity and investment (Natural, fishing, model boats, duck flighting). -  Concert:  NRW area designated for concert activities, which has management activity and investment (Stage, backstage, viewing area).   Additionally these asset types are expanded on using a range of Asset Subtypes;  Car Park, Layby, BBQ Area, Picnic, Archery, Bike Park, Water Sports, Visitor Centre, Toilet, Cycle Hire, Information Office, Wildlife Hide, Heritage building, Shelter, Den Building, Play Area - Formal, Play Area - Wild, Paddling, Camp Site, Cabin Site, Caravan site, Natural, Fishing, Model Boars, Duck Flighting, Stage, Back stage, Viewing Area, Woodland Bays, Events, Go Ape, Climbing, Education, Ropes courses, Catering, Forest Classroom, Retail Outlet, Arboretum, Memorial Trees, Wild Camp Area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-01-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/345/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_GB_RECREATION_AREAS\t2025-10-10T07:51:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18242\tNRW_DS116289\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.181Z\t691\tValid\tEditable\tShoreline Management Plan and Coastal Erosion (archived data)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323839\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk/Coastal Erosion.lyr\tLouise Pennington / Mark Pugh: Flood Risk Team.\tThis dataset has been archived and is no longer maintained, please see dataset NRW_DS121852 \"National Coastal Erosion Risk Management (NCERM)\" for more up to date information.  The datasets shows the spatial coastal baseline which is split into \u0027frontages\u0027. These are defined as lengths of coast with consistent characteristics based on the cliff behaviour characteristics and the defence characteristics. It is intended as a benchmark dataset showing erosion extents and rates for three periods:    -  Short Term (0 - 20 yr)  -  Medium Term (20 - 50 yr) and  -  Long Term (50 - 100 yr).    Defence type and Shoreline Management Plan (SMP) policies for each of the three periods described above are included.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-12-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:08:18.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18244\tNRW_DS116290\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.349Z\t855\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Legal Boundary\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323930\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\NRW Forest Legal Boundary.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Ian Medcalf Secondary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan\tThis layer shows the external legal boundaries of land within Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Forestry ownership. It does not show the internal deed boundaries.  It has been designed to:   - enable the mapping and Geographic Information Units to interrogate and maintain NRW land holdings records,   - provide land ownership data to  staff with GIS,   - produce updated polygons to maintain legal boundary information in the \u0027Forester\u0027 GIS system.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/503/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_FOREST_LEGAL_BOUND\t2026-02-23T11:24:03.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18245\tNRW_DS116291\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.414Z\t874\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Ownership\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323931\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\NRW Forest Ownership.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team\tThis dataset illustrates the acquisitions that have created the NRW Forestry Estate as we know it today. This information is obtained from the Deed Management System which has been designed for the maintenance and reporting of the NRW land holdings.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/329/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_FORESTRY_OWNERSHIP_GB\t2026-02-23T11:36:39.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18243\tNRW_DS116292\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.261Z\t924\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Roads and Planned Forest Roads\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323932\tX:\\Transport\\Forest Roads\\NRW Forest Roads.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan   Secondary Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tForest Roads is compiled from Forest road network data managed by Forestry Civil Engineering. The data relates to forest road Classification.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/424/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_FOREST_ROADS\t2026-02-23T11:26:27.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18238\tNRW_DS116294\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.110Z\t1396\tValid\tEditable\tTraditional Orchards\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323934\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116294 X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Traditional orchards.lyr   (no longer exists - has it been replaced by X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Priority Habitat Traditional Orchards.lyr )\tInternal Custodian: Claire Burrows, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  [Former Internal Custodians: Hillary Miller (VES) / Jim Latham, Terrestrial Ecosystem Services, NRW]          (I think this dataset is now part of the Section 7 OSPAR habitats, for some reason it has also been published on DMW in its own right and therefore has its own metadata entry. Also see NRW_DS122418).\tThis is a spatial dataset contain the location of Traditional Orchards sites around Wales. Traditional orchards are listed as a UK BAP priority habitats, which are those habitats that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).   Traditional orchards are structurally and ecologically similar to wood-pasture and parkland, with open-grown trees set in herbaceous vegetation, but are generally distinguished from these priority habitat complexes by the following characteristics:   - the species composition of the trees, these being primarily in the family Rosaceae;  - the usually denser arrangement of the trees;  - the small scale of individual habitat patches;  - the wider dispersion and greater frequency of occurrence of habitat patches in the countryside.   Traditional orchards include plantings for nuts, principally hazel nuts, but also walnuts. Management of the trees is the other main feature distinguishing traditional orchards and wood-pasture and parkland. Trees in traditional orchards are, or were, grown for fruit and nut production, usually achieved through activities such as grafting and pruning; whereas timber has been the main product from trees in wood-pastures and parkland, mostly derived from pollarding or selective felling. Grazing or cutting of herbaceous vegetation are integral to orchard management, as they are in wood-pastures and parkland.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-04-01\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/350/?ows_service\u003dwms\tnull\t2025-05-07T10:40:26.036Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18239\tNRW_DS116295\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.097Z\t1055\tValid\tEditable\tWales Coast Path\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323935\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Wales Coast Path.lyr\tData Custodian: Quinten Grimley, Coastal Access Project Officer, Access Delivery Team, Sustainable Communities, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of the Wales Coast Path (WCP). The 870 mile long Wales Coast Path was officially opened in May 2012. It runs from the outskirts of Chester to Chepstow and, together with Offa\u0027s Dyke Path, forms a route of over 1000 miles, roughly around the edge of Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-07-31-2025-05-22\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/333/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WALES_COASTAL_PATH\t2025-11-06T15:59:14.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18241\tNRW_DS116296\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.114Z\t255\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Tree Cover 2006\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323936\tX:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2006\\Urban Tree Point 2006.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2006\\Urban Tree Poly 2006.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2006\\Urban Trees Land Categories 2006.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\Urban Extent.lyr\tCustodian:  John Briggs, Landscape Architect,   PPG Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team  Used to be Dafydd Fryer, Urban Forest Architect, Knowledge, Strategy and Planning, NRW. No longer works for NRW  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team, NRW\tThis spatial dataset relates to Phase 1 (2006) and consists of 4 individual layers, being Points (individual trees), polygons (groups of trees), urban extents (the extent of the study areas) and land use classifications. The technical report (see Related Titles and lineage) shows what is covered in each Phase (page 25), and provides more detail on the methodology behind the different layers in the dataset in Appendix A2.1 (page 132).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-01-13\tpublication\thttps://mapdata.llyw.cymru/capabilities/layergroup/877/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://mapdata.llyw.cymru/layergroups/inspire-nrw:UrbanTreeCover2006\t2025-02-04T10:17:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18240\tNRW_DS116297\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.109Z\t902\tValid\tEditable\tDee Water Protection Zone\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323937\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Dee water Protection Zone.lyr\tAngela Roberts-Custodian    *Consents relating to the DWPZ are Public Register and currently stored in the DMS and Carole Williams Environment Assistant is looking after these*\tThis dataset shows the location of the Dee Water Protection  Zone (DWPZ). A Water Protection Zone is an area where certain activities (storing or using controlled substances) are banned or restricted to reduce the risk of polluting drinking water. Under the Water Protection Zone (River Dee Catchment) (Procedural and Other Provisions) Regulations 1999, you need consent (permission) to carry out a controlled activity in the DWPZ. The aim is to prevent water pollution arising from activities that cannot be controlled using other permits. This map can be used to whether your site is within the DWPZ.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-05-01\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/409/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_DEE_PZ\t2025-11-10T16:45:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18237\tNRW_DS116298\t2024-05-28T12:43:54.927Z\t949\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Operational Areas\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323938\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Operational Areas.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Gary Ecclestone, Open Access GIS Officer, GIS and Spatial Information Services Team\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Operational Areas.    This dataset shows the six main operational areas:   - North East Wales  - North West Wales  - Mid Wales  - South East Wales  - South West Wales  - South Wales Central    These are also known as the same areas used for Area Statements.    It is the post OD (Organisational Design) catchment boundaries for the six NRW Operational Areas (or places).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-04-20\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/341/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_AREAS\t2025-12-19T11:37:44.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18236\tNRW_DS116299\t2024-05-28T12:43:54.738Z\t361\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Team Boundaries (Archived 2019)\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323939\tThis layerfile has been removed from the X: drive. The following dataset supersedes the Team Boundaries layer X:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Environment Teams.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Gary Ecclestone.       This layerfile has been removed from the X: drive. The metadata for Team Boundaries can be archived. The following dataset supersedes the Team Boundaries layer X:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Environment Teams.lyr (metadata 123441)  For this reason this record has been removed from public view.\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales team boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for operational managed teams.    This dataset defines the operational team boundaries and the 18 team locations within those areas.    Information warning: This dataset has now been archived as it has been superseded by \u0027Environment Teams, post Organisational Design, July 2019\u0027 NRW_DS123441.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-05-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-05-22T14:23:04.681Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18292\tNRW_DS116300\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.536Z\t856\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Conservation Zones (MCZ)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333030\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\MCZ Marine Conservation Zones.lyr\tKirsten Ramsay, Team Leader Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Marine Conservation Zones (MCZs) former Marine Nature Reserves in Wales.   MCZs are a way of conserving marine habitats and wildlife and other features, of special importance, along the shore or on the seabed. The Marine and Coastal Access Act 2009 allows for the creation of MCZs. MCZs protect a range of nationally important marine wildlife, habitats, geology and geomorphology, and can be designated anywhere in English and Welsh territorial and UK offshore water.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/389/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_MNR\t2025-11-13T12:33:47.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18286\tNRW_DS116301\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.543Z\t471\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Tree Cover 2009\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333031\tX:\\Biological\\Wales Urban Canopy Cover\\2009\\Urban Trees Point 2009 .lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2009\\Urban Trees Poly 2009.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2009\\Urban Trees Land Categories 2009.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\Urban Extent.lyr\tCustodian:  John Briggs, Landscape Architect, PPG Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team Used to be Dafydd Fryer, Urban Forest Architect, Knowledge, Strategy and Planning, NRW. No longer works for NRW    Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis spatial dataset relates to Phase 2 (2009) and consists of 4 individual layers, being Points (individual trees), polygons (groups of trees), urban extents (the extent of the study areas) and land use classifications. The technical report (see Related Titles and lineage) shows what is covered in each Phase (page 25), and provides more detail on the methodology behind the different layers in the dataset in Appendix A2.1 (page 132).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-01-13\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3606/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_urban_tree_cover_2009\t2025-11-19T10:50:32.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18283\tNRW_DS116303\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.405Z\t3000\tValid\tEditable\tNational River Flow Archive (NRFA)\t© CNC/NRW, Environment Agency, Scottish Environment Protection Agency, Rivers Agency. NRW owned data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of the licence. NRW does NOT grant permission to disseminate or re-use any data that is not labelled as NRW ownership. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333033\tWelsh NRW owned data is currently held in WISKI - and sent to CEH who collate this and put in NRFA  https://nrfa.ceh.ac.uk/data/search\tInternal Custodian of the NRW Welsh Data:  Alison Hanson, Hydrometry and Telemetry.  CEH hold the custodianship over the archived UK data that they publish on the agencies behalf.\tThe National River Flow Archive (NRFA) is the UK\u0027s focal point for river flow data and is the primary archive of daily and peak river flows for the United Kingdom. The archive incorporates daily, monthly and flood peak data from over 1500 gauging stations across the UK.   The NRFA holds a wide range of hydrological information to assist in the understanding and interpretation of measured river flows. In addition to time series of gauged river flow, the data centre maintains hydrometric information relating to the gauging stations and the catchments they command and data, quantifying other parts of the hydrological cycle.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-10-31\tpublication\tnull\thttps://nrfa.ceh.ac.uk/data/search\t2025-05-15T11:22:29.984Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18284\tNRW_DS116304\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.478Z\t478\tValid\tEditable\tEnforcement Action against Corporate Entities\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333034\tData is extracted from information stored in the National Enforcement Database (NEDS) from 01/01/2001 to 31/12/2015 and from 01/01/2016 to present in the Collins Database.\tCustodian: Helen White Governance / Stuart Kay Master Reference data\tConvictions (excluding those under appeal and, for avoidance of doubt, not including acquittals) and formal cautions data, filtered to limit information to corporate entities only. Data available commences from 1/1/2000. Updates available on a quarterly basis.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-05T09:26:03.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18293\tNRW_DS116305\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.536Z\t971\tValid\tEditable\tWaste Management Licence Current Exemptions\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333035\tData extracted from CRM and is then stored in Sharefile\tCustodian: Helen Florek Oughton - Customer Care Centre Stephen Badman\tCurrent Waste Management Licence Exemption data as required by Schedule 3 of the Waste Management Licensing (WML) Regulations 1994 (as amended) lists the activities which are normally exempt from WML in Wales. Circular 26/94 in Wales provides additional information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-04\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2635\t2026-02-05T09:28:57.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18289\tNRW_DS116306\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.533Z\t400\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHistorical Dangerous Substances List I and List II\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333036\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116306\tJulie Boswell / Jane Roberts: Environmental reporting team\tData was collated annually until the end of 2011 for the Dangerous Substances Directive (76/464/EEC) on the discharge of dangerous substances to surface waters. The Dangerous Substances Directive was repealed in 2012 following the introduction of the Water Framework Directive (2000/60/EC), which includes monitoring and assessment of a wide range of chemicals in surface waters.  List I covers substances that are particularly toxic or persistent, or may tend to accumulate in the environment. List II covers substances whose effects are less serious. List I includes 20 substances and List II has 67 substances. Every listed dangerous substance has a concentration limit known as an Environmental Quality Standard (EQS). The EQS is usually an upper concentration, with the exception of pH, which has both upper and lower limits. It is set for the receiving watercourse and not the discharge itself. The dangerous substance is not believed to be detrimental to aquatic life at any concentration below its EQS limit. The EQS varies for each substance and can be different for fresh, estuarine or coastal waters.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-12-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18288\tNRW_DS116307\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.563Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDangerous Substances List II/ Annex 10 Substances under WFD\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333037\t\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18294\tNRW_DS116308\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.483Z\t1385\tValid\tEditable\tEnvironmental Permitting Regulations - Industrial Sites\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333038\tPermitting and Licencing  System (PALS)\tData Custodian: Henry Forrest - Permitting Centre Wales, Cardiff\tThe Environmental Permitting Regulations, amongst other things, implement the IPPC (Integrated Pollution Prevention and Control) Directive (EC/61/96) in England and Wales.  Facilities covered by this legislation are known as Installations and generally have significant releases to air, land or water, or carry out certain, larger scale, waste management activities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3494/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_install_permits\t2026-02-23T11:05:22.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18280\tNRW_DS116309\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.202Z\t961\tValid\tEditable\tOSPAR River Monitoring Programme\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333039\tData is stored in WISKI Database\tCustodian: Claire Popp Environmental Reporting Team\tThe Convention for the Protection of the Marine Environment of the North-East Atlantic know as the OSPAR Convention 1998 (Oslo Convention 1972 and Paris Convention 1974), is an agreement signed by a number of European countries (including the United Kingdom) to protect the quality of the North East Atlantic.   The objective of the OSPAR Convention is to take all possible steps to prevent and eliminate pollution entering the sea. The OSPAR Convention provides standard methods for estimating the inputs of selected pollutants to the sea by using fixed sampling schedule and a standard input calculation.     In Wales, all principal rivers are sampled monthly (12 times a year) just upstream of their tidal limits. For those rivers carrying the heaviest contaminant loads the sampling frequency maybe increased beyond the minimum of 12. Major trade effluents and sewage effluents to estuaries or coastal waters are also sampled monthly to assess direct discharges to marine waters. Flow values of discharges direct to estuary or sea are sometimes provided by the operators themselves. This is usually part of statutory monitoring arrangements. The aim of these programmes is to assess the level of contamination entering the sea from Wales (the \u0027load\u0027) and to chart the progress in the reduction of this load.   This dataset contains the results and data obtained from these programmes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-11-01\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2629\t2025-12-19T11:58:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18285\tNRW_DS116310\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.550Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRadioactive Substances Register\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333130\t\tThis data is no longer available for external users; the old EA legacy data is outdated and product is discontinued; no new data is produced due to re-use concerns by Holly Noble’s team.\t\t\t2021-07-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:52:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18281\tNRW_DS116311\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.075Z\t516\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDischarges of Consented Red List Substances\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333131\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116311\tSimon Bonwick, Data Services Team, NRW\tThe UK has a list (known as the Red List) of 23 of the most dangerous substances which were selected for priority control under the Integrated Pollution Control legislation (subsequently superseded by the Pollution Prevention and Control and then Environmental Permitting Regulations). This list of substances includes EC List I substances defined under the Dangerous Substances Directive, as well as certain substances listed on EC List 2. There are statutory Environmental Quality Standards (EQSs) in place for their discharge into surface waters. Statutory EQSs for a further 25 substances came into force on 1 April 1998. These deal with substances produced by manufacturing industry, as well as a number of pesticides applied to crops. Dangerous Substances are toxic, do not or are very slow to degrade in water, and are likely to accumulate in living organisms.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18290\tNRW_DS116312\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.546Z\t866\tValid\tEditable\tHazardous Waste Registrations with Standard Industrial Classification (SIC) Code\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333132\tData is held in the NRW Customer Relationship Management Database.\tCustodian: Stephen Badman Customer Contact Centre\tThe Hazardous Waste Regulations 2005 require that anyone who produces or holds hazardous waste at any premises in Wales must register the premises with Natural Resources Wales each year, unless the total quantity of hazardous waste is less than 500 kg each year.    There is an online public register that allows searches to be run on registrations which shows the business name, registration reference, address,  postcode, registration start date and registration end date. The dataset contains information about live registrations.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-04\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2622\t2026-02-05T09:37:42.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18287\tNRW_DS116313\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.562Z\t709\tUnassessed\tEditable\tReferrals of Red List Discharges to Sewers (Corporate Entities)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333133\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116313\tChris Hall (SUW Advisor - Water quality)\tThe Referrals of Red List Discharges to Sewers dataset (extracted from IPCIS) records those companies (and potentially individuals) who have applied to water undertakers for permission to discharge a Red List substance into sewers. The Water Industry Act 1991 (as amended) section 120 (Applications for the discharge of special category effluent) directs sewerage undertakers who have received a notice containing an application for consent to discharge trade effluent from a trade premise into a public sewer (section 119) to refer to the following questions: - whether the discharges to which the notice relates should be prohibited; and - whether, if they are not prohibited, any requirements should be imposed as to the conditions on which they are made. It is this information, stored within IPCIS, that makes up the Referrals of Red List Discharges to Sewers dataset.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-12-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18295\tNRW_DS116314\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.565Z\t1034\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGroundwater Level Measurements\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333134\t\tCustodian: Liam Perrott       This is a large WISKI database dataset; requests are dealt with ad hoc only with usually high extraction costs regarding staff resources. We do not routinely supply this dataset as a whole. Please therefore always ask the customer for the  geographic extent they require and include this in the licence.  Due to national security reason, you must send any raw data extracts you receive from the data custodian password protected. (Please ask custodian or Simon Bonwick/Andrew Jeffery for help with this if required). Please send password and data in a separate email. Otherwise the NGRs must be abbreviated to 4 digits.\tThis dataset comprises groundwater level time series data taken at borehole monitoring stations located across Wales. Discrete station information is stored for each site including identifier, spatial reference, parameter type and time series type. This dataset contains sites for operational and closed monitoring stations.  Data is collected from NRW borehole monitoring stations that are collated by Area staff normally by either downloading the station \u0027Logger Data\u0027 or manually \u0027Dipping\u0027 to determine borehole water level.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18278\tNRW_DS116315\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.939Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSummary Shellfish Directive Assessments\t© CNC/NRW, 2015. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333135\t[A]  Data available in Biosys [B]  Packaged published version available on Sharefile.\tThere is a small risk that this data can be considered commercially confidential but is difficult to assess. However, these data would be made available under an EIR request and as such these would inevitably lead to disclosure where the public interest test has been applied.  For this reason we consider it unnecessary that this slight risk should prevent full access.\tShellfish waters are coastal and brackish waters used for commercial shell fishing. These areas have been designated under the EC Shellfish Waters Directive.  This dataset contains sample results taken within the Designated Shellfish Waters that are assessed for compliance under the standards as defined under Directive (79/923/EEC).  These sites are sampled for water quality and for faecal coliform amongst others and contain both the raw sample results as well as the summary compliance data.  Samples are taken monthly with summary data collated for compliance annually and reported to Defra and the Welsh Government.  Since these data are reported annually the dataset is available for each complete year reported. It is of note that shellfisheries no longer used on a commercial basis are still maintained.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-10-25\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T10:36:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18279\tNRW_DS116316\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.947Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSeagrass Taxa and Abundance\t© CNC/NRW 2013. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333136\t[A]  Data extracted from Biosys [B] X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seagrass Areas [C] X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seagrass Quadrats\t\tInformation regarding the presence, and percentage cover, of seagrass species at specific marine monitoring points held within the legacy Environment Agency Wales BIOSYS database. This is now NRW\u0027s store for legacy freshwater EAW data. EAW used this as the main database for storing, manipulating and reporting data from freshwater and marine biological surveys at any taxonomic level. These data represent ground-truthing monitoring for the ecological assessment of seagrasses within transitional and coastal waters of Wales. Seagrass data on BIOSYS are updated as new monitoring data are made available. Monitoring for seagrass occurs June to September with records being updated after the sampling season. The extracted data is a subset of the full dataset and only includes data managed and / or owned by Natural Resources Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-06-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T10:29:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18276\tNRW_DS116317\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.866Z\t581\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Rocky Shore Macroalgal Species\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333137\tBioSys WISKI KiEco\t\tInformation regarding the presence of macroalgal species on rocky shores monitored for the Water Framework Directive ecological assessment of transitional and coastal waters of Wales. Monitoring follows the Reduced Species List method outlined in the UK Technical Advisory Group method statement. Data are from intertidal rocky shores. The extracted data is a subset of the full dataset and only includes data collected and owned by the Natural Resources Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-06-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18291\tNRW_DS116318\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.549Z\t698\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Benthic Invertebrate Species (extracted from BIOSYS)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333138\t[A]  Extracted from BIOSYS Packaged data available: Y:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116318\tBianca Bacchi / Richard West - Senior Env Mon Officer, Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R\tInformation regarding the presence, and abundance, of benthic invertebrate species at specific marine monitoring points held within the Biological Systems (BIOSYS). BIOSYS is a former Environment Agency Wales\u0027 (EAW)) database. This welsh extract of the data is now stored and managed by Natural Resources Wales. BIOSYS was EAW main database for storing, manipulating and reporting data from freshwater and marine biological surveys at any taxonomic level, and Water Framework Directive marine benthic invertebrate database. Data are laboratory assessed and quality assured following the National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control (NMBAQC) scheme. National databases are continually being updated as a result of on-going benthic invertebrate monitoring programmes. The extracted data is a subset of the full dataset and only includes data collected/owned/managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-01-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:33.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18282\tNRW_DS116319\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.126Z\t439\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Species\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333139\t[A]  Extracted from BIOSYS\t\tInformation regarding the presence, and percentage cover, of saltmarsh angiosperms (flowering plants) at specific marine monitoring points held within the former Environment Agency Wales\u0027 welsh abstract of the BIOSYS database (our main database for storing, manipulating and reporting data from freshwater and marine biological surveys at any taxonomic level). This data is now managed and held by Natural Resources Wales. These data represent ground-truthing monitoring for the ecological assessment of saltmarsh within transitional and coastal waters of Wales. Welsh saltmarsh data originally held on BIOSYS are now updated as new monitoring data are made available through Natural Resources Wales. Monitoring for saltmarsh occurs June to September with records being updated after the sampling season. The extracted data is a subset of the full dataset and only includes data collected/owned by Natural Resources Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-06-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:02.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18277\tNRW_DS116320\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.964Z\t688\tValid\tEditable\tLarge Raised Reservoirs (archived data)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333230\tData extracted from Cronfeydd Cymru database  Y:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116320\tCustodian: Matthew O\u0027Brien, Reservoir Safety Team\tThis dataset has been discontinued. Please see the datasets which have replaced it:  - Large Raised Reservoir register (Locations and undertakers) NRW_DS125215 and;   - Large Raised Reservoir (Registered information) NRW_DS125216  This dataset contains details of Large Raised Reservoirs. Natural Resource Wales (NRW) collects and maintains data on all reservoirs designed or capable of holding more than 25,000 cubic metres of water above the natural level of any part of the land adjoining them defined as \u0027large raised reservoirs\u0027 under the Reservoirs Act 1975. Two types of reservoir are maintained within the register:   - Impounding (Dammed) or; - Non-Impounding (Pumped/unimpeded)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-05-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T11:55:30.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18274\tNRW_DS116321\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.710Z\t1515\tValid\tEditable\tLicenced Water Abstractions\t© CNC/NRW The redacted data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. A full version of the data will only be provided on request, on a case-by-case basis and under provision of a separate NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333231\tPermitting and Licencing System (PALS) - also know as Dynamix 365 Data stored from 2013-04-01 to 2016-02-26 in WISKI and from 2016-02-27 to present in PALS (Also know as Dynamix 365)\tData Custodian: Ashley Lansdown, Team Leader, Water Resources Permitting, Future Regulation\tThe water abstractions dataset details all sites in Wales covered under the Water Act 2003 where all abstractions of 20 cubic metres or more require an abstraction licence\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4295/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_water_resource_permits\t2026-02-23T11:13:22.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18273\tNRW_DS116322\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.722Z\t2064\tUnassessed\tEditable\tQuality Controlled Daily Rain Gauge Data from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Gauges\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333232\tHydrometry and Telemetry syste\tAlison Hanson- Hydrometry and telemetry.  Data is provided on request. Metadata- Data services Team       Currently data is only on request as has to be extracted by the H\u0026amp;T team\tThis dataset contains daily rainfall totals, given in mm to 1 decimal place, for all rainfall stations included within this dataset. The list of stations to be defined by the file contents with no separate index of stations. The period for counting rainfall will be in line with the water day which is 24 hour period between 9am and 9am. Stations which do not meet quality control specification, eg missing or invalid data, will be omitted from the published file.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:26:30.720Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18272\tNRW_DS116323\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.636Z\t1107\tValid\tEditable\tWaste Carriers, Brokers and Dealers\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333233\tData taken from the Customer Relationship Management (CRM) database  Y:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116323\tCustodian: Stephen Badman, Customer Contact Centre\tThis dataset contains details of currently permitted waste carriers, brokers and dealers.    Carrier: A person who transports controlled waste in the course of a business or otherwise with a view to profit.   Broker: Waste brokers are people who make arrangements, on behalf of others, to recover or dispose of waste, regardless of whether or not they handle the waste themselves.   Dealer: Waste dealers are people who use an agent to buy then sell wastes, regardless of whether they handle the waste themselves or not.   Historical details are not included.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-04\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2639\t2026-02-05T09:42:12.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18269\tNRW_DS116324\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.326Z\t454\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicators of Water Courses - Biology\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333234\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116324\tMetadata point of Contact: Data Services Custodian: Jane Roberts-  Environmental Reporting Team\tThe General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicator scheme or GQAHI (previously known as GQA) was the national indicator for water quality in rivers and canals. It was designed to provide an accurate and consistent assessment of the state of water quality and how it changes over time. These assessments were made for Biological, Chemical and Nutrients and undertaken for discrete river stretches. This datasets relates to the biological assessments. The Biology assessment gave an indicator of the overall \u0027health\u0027 of rivers. It describes water quality in terms of 83 groups of macroinvertebrates. Some of these are pollution sensitive so their presence suggested better water quality. A category was assigned to the river stretch where the monitoring point was located:  - A - Very Good: Biology similar to that expected for an average and unpolluted river of this size, type and location. High diversity of groups, usually with several species in each. Rare to find dominance of any one group.  - B - Good: Biology falls a little short of that expected for an unpolluted river. Small reduction in the number of groups that are sensitive to pollution. Moderate increase in the number of individuals in the groups that tolerate pollution. - C - Fairly Good: Biology worse than expected for an unpolluted river. Many sensitive groups absent or number of individuals reduced. Marked rise in numbers of individuals in groups that tolerate pollution.  - D - Fair: Sensitive groups scarce and contains only small numbers of individuals. A range of pollution tolerant groups present, some with high numbers of individuals.  - E - Poor: Biology restricted to pollution tolerant species with some groups dominant in terms of the numbers of individuals. Sensitive groups rare or absent.  - F - Bad: Biology limited to a small number of very tolerant groups (such as worms, midge larvae and leeches), present in very high numbers. In the worst case, there may be no life present. Sample points represent one or many stretches.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18275\tNRW_DS116325\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.723Z\t715\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicators of Water Courses - Chemistry\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333235\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116327\tMetadata point of contact: Data Services Custodian: Jane Roberts\tThe General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicator scheme or GQAHI (previously known as GQA) was the national indicator for water quality in rivers and canals. It was designed to provide an accurate and consistent assessment of the state of water quality and how it changes over time. These assessments were made for Biological, Chemical and Nutrients and undertaken for discrete river stretches. This datasets relates to the chemical assessments. In Wales we maintained the full GQA network until 2010 based on 800 sampling sites which provided information for approximately 4700km. Grades of river quality for the chemical GQA, Chemical grade Likely uses and characteristics:en - A: Very good, All abstractions, Very good Salmonid fisheries, Cyprinid fisheries, Natural ecosystems. - B: Good, All abstractions, Salmonid fisheries, Cyprinid fisheries, Ecosystems at or close to natural.  - C: Fairly good, Potable supply after advanced treatment, Other abstractions, Good Cyprinid fisheries, Natural ecosystems, or those corresponding to good Cyprinid fisheries  - D: Fair, Potable supply after advanced treatment, Other abstractions, Fair Cyprinid fisheries, Impacted ecosystems.  - E: Poor Low grade abstraction for industry, Fish absent or sporadically present, vulnerable to pollution, Impoverished ecosystems  - F: Bad, Very polluted rivers which may cause nuisance, Severely restricted ecosystems *Provided other standards are met where the grade is caused by discharges of organic pollution.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-07-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18270\tNRW_DS116326\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.228Z\t334\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicators of Water Courses - Nutrients\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333236\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116326\tMetadata point of contact: Data Service team Custodian: Jane Roberts\tThe General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicator scheme or GQAHI (previously known as GQA) was the national indicator for water quality in rivers and canals. It was designed to provide an accurate and consistent assessment of the state of water quality and how it changes over time. These assessments were made for Biological, Chemical and Nutrients and undertaken for discrete river stretches. This datasets relates to the nutrient assessments. In Wales we maintained the full GQA network until 2010 based on 800 sampling sites which provided information for approximately 4700km. The GQAHI/GQA scheme was designed to provide an accurate and consistent assessment of the state of water quality and how it changes over time. The Nutrients GQA described quality in terms of two nutrients: nitrates (mg NO3 /l) and phosphates (mg P/l) and graded from 1 to 6.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Environment Agency Wales\t2016-07-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18271\tNRW_DS116327\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.307Z\t903\tValid\tEditable\tHistoric River Quality Objectives\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333237\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116327\tInternal Contact Dave Johnson (was Jane Roberts)  in the Evidence, Monitoring \u0026amp; Reporting.\tThe River Quality Objectives (RQO) classification was used for planning water quality improvements until 2006 when the scheme ended. The national RQO classification scheme ceased at the end of 2006, it is now a fixed table which will not be updated. RQO compliance figures are available for 1997-2006.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-12-20\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2623\t2025-05-02T14:48:18.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18267\tNRW_DS116328\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.187Z\t791\tValid\tEditable\tUK Water Quality Sampling Harmonised Monitoring Scheme Summary Data\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333238\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116328\tInternal Custodian: Julie Boswell- Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS  Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tThe Historic UK Water Quality Sampling Harmonised Monitoring Scheme (HMS) data contains statistics for a series of water quality sampling sites including annual means, maximum and minimum values for each region for specified determinands from 1975 onwards. The Harmonised Monitoring Scheme (HMS) was established to provide an archive of water quality data for the UK. It is used to provide information for international obligations, including the long-term trends of some determinands and the estimation of riverborne input of selected determinands to the sea.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-10-25\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-05-02T14:48:16.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18268\tNRW_DS116329\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.294Z\t930\tValid\tEditable\tConsented Discharges to Controlled Waters with Conditions\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333239\tData is extracted from the Permitting and Licencing System (PALS)\tData Custodian: Henry Forrest, Permitting Centre Wales Cardiff\tThis dataset provide details of permit details as required under the Environmental Permit Regulations. Information is held for all permit holders and covers all substances that are controlled. The data come from Natural Resources Wales public Register.   The data set contains 3 tiers of information:    Tier 1 - Site and General Information relating to the permit operator, discharge address and type. The date of permit issue, effective and revocation. Information where the effluent enters the environment (such as river, coastal, groundwater) for each permit.     Tier 2 - Outlet and release Data on the effluent type e.g. Sewage effluent, Storm Overflow, Trade. The location is supplied for the effluent and the outlet in OS Nation Grid Reference format. Further detail is provided on the permit type and treatment type.    Tier 3 - Determinand /release detail Limits  Further detail is provided on the amount that can be discharged and in which time period in months. Release details/determinands are the substances and numerical limits that make up the effluent. This could include chemical, biological, and physical limits. The permitted limits are included for each determinand /release detail. Data is provided for each effluent and may contain one or more determinands depending on the complexity of the discharge.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3544/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_water_quality_permits\t2026-02-23T11:50:25.173Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18266\tNRW_DS116330\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.074Z\t298\tValid\tEditable\tMonitoring of Pesticides and Trace Organics in Water 1992 - 2008\t© CNC/NRW © Environment Agency Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333330\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\tDataset Custodian: Martin Stephens        This dataset is not to be published to data.gov.uk as already published through data.gov.uk by the EA.\tA pesticide is defined under the Food and Environment Protection Act (1985) as \u0027any substance, preparation or organism prepared or used for destroying any pest\u0027. Pesticides include herbicides, fungicides, insecticides, molluscicides, rodenticides, growth regulators and masonry and timber preservatives. They are not confined to agriculture, but are also used on roads and rail tracks, in homes and gardens, as sheep dips, for the protection of public health, and for many other purposes.   Monitoring for pesticides is guided by statutory requirements to monitor concentrations of specific pesticides listed in certain Directives. We also undertake non-statutory monitoring of pesticides, when investigating known or predicted local problems and pollution incidents. Trace Organics are organic compounds (including pesticides) detectable at low levels.  As with pesticides, our monitoring of these substances is guided by statutory requirements.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-10-24\tpublication\tnull\thttps://naturalresourceswales.sharefile.eu/d-sd5506c031384a3bb\t2025-12-23T09:46:33.743Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18307\tNRW_DS116331\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.914Z\t870\tValid\tEditable\tEnvironmental Permitting Regulations - Waste Sites\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333331\tThe data is stored from 2013-04-01 to 2015-12-31 in REGIS and  from 2016-01-01 to 2016-07-01 in Permitting And Licencing System.\tData Custodian: Henry Forrest/ Tim Morris - Permitting Centre Wales, Cardiff\tA waste management licence is a legal document issued under the Environmental Protection Act 1990. A licence authorises the treatment, keeping or disposal of waste in or on the land. Once we have issued a licence, neither the activities nor the area of land may be changed unless the licence is modified.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3577/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_waste_permits\t2026-02-23T11:08:55.244Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18310\tNRW_DS116332\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.929Z\t320\tUnassessed\tEditable\tExtractive Materials Management Statement Summaries – Corporate Entities Only\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333332\t[A] Extracted from Extractive Materials Management Statements.\tTim Morris: Waste Permiting       This entry is not added to Lle or Data.Gov to proactively publish it because the data is old and not something we recommend using, unless specifically requested, although it will be available to download from ShareFile as Open Data from the metadata\tExtractive Materials Management Statements are sent in by operators of mines and quarries to demonstrate that extractive materials produced on site are not extractive waste and therefore do not require a permit under the Environmental Permitting Regulations (EPR) to manage this material as would otherwise be required under the Mining Waste Directive. EMMS are not submitted under any legislative framework, but are submitted as a result of an agreement between ourselves and the mines and quarries sector as a way of stating what materials are not extractive waste due to European Court of Justice rulings that define extractive waste in a more restrictive way that the Directive. The EMMS spreadsheet holds summary details of all EMMS submitted to us since September 2010. Subsequent requests for individual EMM Statements themselves will be treated on an individual basis, and will be checked for relevant confidentialities, in accordance with normal procedures.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-01-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:02:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18313\tNRW_DS116333\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.950Z\t937\tValid\tEditable\tSurface Water Temperature Archive up to 2007\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333333\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116333\tInternal Contact: Alison Hanson\tWater temperature data is collected  for different reasons and in different locations. Time series of surface water temperatures can provide indicators of climate change and associated ecological responses. An archive was created in 2007 as part of a research project, and is a unique collation of Natural Resource Wales and The Environment Agencies water temperature data from more than 30,000 sites across England \u0026amp; Wales. The archive contains water temperature data (up to 2007) and site metadata. The archive contains water temperature data (up to 2007) and site metadata. Most sites have records starting from the 1980s.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-10-25\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2630\t2025-05-02T14:44:45.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18306\tNRW_DS116334\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.876Z\t874\tValid\tEditable\tOtter Survey Data 1991-2010\t© CNC/NRW, 2015. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333334\tThis data is available through Sharefile as part of a packaged licence product.\tHuw Jones, Team Leader, Natural Resource Management, NRW Janet Buckles, Technical Assistant Biodiversity, Biodiversity, NRW\tThe European otter (Lutra lutra) is widely recognised as an emblem for nature conservation in the UK because it is a top predator and an important biological indicator of the health of our rivers and wetlands. Monitoring the status of the otter therefore gives us a valuable measure of the state of our water and wetland ecosystems. In Wales as in much of the UK, it is a largely nocturnal animal and is rarely observed in the wild. It is however possible to detect its presence by searching for its distinctive droppings (spraints) and footprints.   As part of a scheme to monitor the distribution of otters throughout the UK, five Welsh otter survey were carried out as follows:    - 1st survey: 1977-78 run and funded by NCC.  - 2nd survey: 1984-85 run and funded by Vincent Wildlife Trust  - 3rd survey: 1991 run and funded by Vincent Wildlife Trust  - 4th survey: 2002 run by EAW and the Wildlife Trusts and funded by EAW, CCW and Welsh Water.  - 5th survey: 2009-2010 run by EAW and the Wildlife Trusts, IBAP officers and the Snowdonia Mammal Group.    The aim of these surveys was to monitor the distribution of otters throughout Wales. Sites were selected by accessibility and likelihood of finding signs of otter activity, typically road bridges and the confluences of watercourses. The number of sites visited per hydrometric region varied. No attempt was made to randomise the sampling effort, but there was some site variability due different issues when the surveys were undertaken.     This dataset is made up of two parts; the 3rd and 4th surveys (1991 and 2002), and the 5th survey 2009-2010. The full Welsh for the 1st and 2nd surveys, for 1997-78, 1984-85 is not currently available. The 1991 and 2002 data are currently available on the NBN Gateway (published by JNCC) as redacted open data.   This raw data is currently not available, however more detailed information about each surveying year can be found in the reports available through the library (see related titles). The 2009-2010 otter surveys are available though NRW but will be issued under licence.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-27\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr3416\t2025-12-01T17:16:41.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18317\tNRW_DS116335\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.977Z\t839\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHazardous Waste Interrogator\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333335\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\tTim Morris, Senior Waste Advisor (Hazardous Waste), Waste \u0026amp; Resources Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales is legally required to monitor all movements of hazardous waste in Wales. Hazardous waste producers are required to register with us and the site where the waste is disposed or recovered is required to inform us of the details of the wastes they receive.  Hazardous waste producer data is commercially confidential. However a summary of the movements is provided in this Hazardous Waste Interrogator. Only high-level waste classification geographical locations (where the waste was produced and where the waste management facility is located) and tonnage is included. Individual site names and producers details are not included.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-07-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18316\tNRW_DS116336\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.977Z\t2588\tValid\tEditable\tWaste Permit Returns Data Interrogator\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333336\tWaste Returns database and PALs database\tDataset point of contact: John Fry, Lead Specialist Advisor, Waste Policy\tAll operators of regulated waste management facilities have to provide us with details of the quantities and types of waste they deal with i.e. waste received into site and waste sent on from site to other facilities or processes. This data is used to monitor compliance but has historically been used by the EC, DEFRA and local authorities to assist in planning for new waste facilities and for monitoring against statutory targets. We have provided this data in an interrogatable format.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-07-07\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2619\t2025-12-19T10:43:00.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18318\tNRW_DS116337\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.024Z\t577\tValid\tEditable\tHydropower Permits\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333337\tData Extracted from Permitting And Licencing System (PALS) Data Stored in NALD from 2013-04-01 to 2016-02-26 and then Permitting And Licencing System from 2016-07-01\tData Custodian: Henry Forrest - Permitting Centre Wales, Cardiff\tPermits issued for Hydropower Generation sites. NRW assesses and licences hydropower schemes for the water they abstract and to protect the local and wider environment; this may require issuing one or a combination of Abstraction, Impoundment and Transfer licences dependant on the scheme/site. This dataset details the permits issued for new, varied or renewed hydropower schemes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3512/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_hydropower_permits\t2026-02-23T11:16:00.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18322\tNRW_DS116338\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.321Z\t1123\tValid\tEditable\tUK Water Quality Sampling Harmonised Monitoring Scheme Detailed Data\t© Environment Agency copyright and/or database right 2013. All rights reserved © Scottish Environmental Protection Agency 2013 © Northern Ireland Agri-Food and Biosciences Institute 2013 © Natural Resources Wales Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333338\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\tInternal custodian: Mark Squire / (previously) Julie Boswell\tThe Historic UK Water Quality Sampling Harmonised Monitoring Scheme (HMS) dataset contains individual determinand results for all sites in the UK Harmonised Monitoring Scheme network from 1974 until 2013. The Harmonised Monitoring Scheme (HMS) was established to provide an archive of water quality data for the UK. It is used to provide information for international obligations, including the long-term trends of some determinands and the estimation of riverborne input of selected determinands to the sea.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-06-22\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2633\t2025-05-02T15:16:18.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18311\tNRW_DS116339\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.920Z\t931\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333339\tThis dataset sits within Kieco - WISKI module.   [A]  This data is available on Sharefile and is classed as Open Data and static at the moment, until we can get an update out of WISKI.\tNicola Wiltshire, Environmental Monitoring Officer 1, Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R, NRW Mel Lacan, Senior Environmental Monitoring Officer, Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R, NRW       Notes from 17/07/2018 The RHS data only currently goes up to 2014. When EAW split from EA we got an excel spreadsheet which contains the 1994 - 2004 data, extracted from EA\u0027s Ecosys module. This RHS acts as summary dataset, which is adequate for most people to use, and it includes indices of habitat type. This data is now stored in Keico (WISKI) which contains a lot of data from 2003 onwards. Pre 2003 the methodology was different so it has been hard to get the data into keico, Mel Lacan is hoping that by the end of the year, the data will be entered and they can start reporting again, therefore updating this dataset.\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS) is Natural Resources Wales standard for collecting data on the physical character and quality of river habitats across the Wales. This dataset provides survey results carried out from 1994 to 2014.  The bulk of surveys were carried out between 1994 to 1997 and 2006 to 2008. Surveys are still carried out for specific drivers, for example assessing habitat availability and Water Framework Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:15.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18323\tNRW_DS116340\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.320Z\t898\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Risk Assessments for Cycle 2\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333430\tDMS spreadsheets with the WFD team\tRobert Blacklidge Environmental reporting team\tThis dataset contains risk assessment data produced to support the cycle 2 river basin management plans. It contains -   Lake water bodies  -   River water bodies  -   Transitional water bodies (estuaries) -   Groundwater bodies -   Coastal water bodies  that are assessed to be at risk of failing WFD Objectives or at risk of deteriorating from current status for a number of pressures. It also contains details of protected areas (Shellfish Waters and Bathing Waters) that are assessed to be at risk of failing their objectives or at risk of deteriorating in the future.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-10-25\tpublication\tnull\thttps://naturalresources.wales/evidence-and-data/research-and-reports/water-reports/river-basin-management-plans-published/?lang\u003den\t2025-05-07T15:34:01.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18320\tNRW_DS116342\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.220Z\t1702\tValid\tEditable\tHistoric Flood Warnings and Flood Alerts\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333432\tEmailed every quarter from EA, but in 2016 the data will come via our own service. Y:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116342\tAndrew Wall - Flood Forecasting Metadata Contact - Data Services Team\tListing of Severe Flood Warnings, Flood Warnings, Update Flood Warnings and Flood Alerts issued since the Floodline Warnings Direct service went live on January 26th 2006 to the present.   This dataset includes flood warnings issued by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) from 1 April 2013.   Flood Alerts and Warnings are issued for flooding from rivers and the sea.  For Flood Alerts this is generally river catchments or stretches of coastline. For Flood Warnings and Severe Flood Warnings this is specific communities or parts of communities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-12-18\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2644\t2025-12-22T10:38:17.061Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18302\tNRW_DS116343\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.362Z\t1105\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Risk Forecast Application Programming Interface (API)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333433\tHost by the EA and Made available through our API manager\tMartin Linforth: Flood and coastal risk management\tThe flood risk forecast is produced by the Flood Forecasting Centre (FFC) on a daily basis. It is issued more frequently when serious flooding is forecast. It provides the indication of the potential for flooding for five days: the day on which it is issued and the subsequent four days ahead.  The forecast highlights flood risk on a county by county basis and includes a short commentary on the situation. It covers flooding from rivers, the sea, surface water and groundwater for Wales and England.  This dataset is produced in partnership with the Met Office Flood Forecasting Centre and the Environment Agency and is intended to complement the Flood Warning Service provided by Natural Resources Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-10-12\tpublication\tnull\thttps://api-portal.naturalresources.wales/products\t2025-05-13T11:22:03.208Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18321\tNRW_DS116344\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.220Z\t862\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater Gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) Species Survey Data\t© CNC/NRW 2008  There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333434\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the 17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A] Species data is held in Recorder 6. -  Gwyniad Hydroacoustic and Gill netting survey of Llyn Arenig Fawr 2009 - CCW Science Report 904 CCW000800000000D    -  Gwyniad Hydroacoustic Survey 2003 - 2009 - CCW Science Report 903 - Countryside Council for Wales CCW000800000000C    -  Gwyniad Hydroacoustic and Gill Netting Survey of Llyn Arenig Fawr 2012- CCW Science Report 1013 CCW000800000000I     -  Gwyniad Gill netting survey of Llyn Tegid 2003 - CCW Science Report 597 CCW000800000000E\tTristan Hatton-Ellis, Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThe gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) population of Llyn Tegid has been considered for a number of years to be threatened by deteriorating environmental conditions, especially those associated with eutrophication. This dataset contains species data from the results of projects established to carry out translocation, hydroacoustic surveys and measurement of environmental variables. This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Atlas. The purpose of this survey work and collation was to develop a generic whitefish sampling protocol suitable for Llyn Tegid and to produce a condition assessment of the population. Data is collected in Llyn Tegid and Llyn Arenig Fawr. All hydroacoustic records are available for site centroid only. Gill netting results are recorded at the site of the cast where information was available.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18315\tNRW_DS116345\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.966Z\t951\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater River lamprey (Lampetra fluviatilis), Brook lamprey (Lampetra planeri) and Sea lamprey (Petromyzon marinus) Survey Data\t© CNC/NRW 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333435\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the 17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A] Species data held in Recorder 6: - Lamprey Monitoring, Wye and Usk Catchments 2005 - CCW - Report no. 41 CCW0002800000007 - Lamprey Monitoring, Tywi, Teifi and Cleddau Catchments 2004 - CCW - RoC Report no. 7 CCW0002800000008 - Lamprey Monitoring, Dee Catchment 2004 - CCW - RoC Report No. 18 CCW0002800000009 - Lamprey Habitat Assessment in the River Usk 1998 - CCW - Report No. 365 CCW000280000000C - Lamprey Monitoring, River Usk 2011 - CCW – Science Report No. 11/8/6 CCW000800000000M - Lamprey Monitoring, Cleddau Catchments 2012 - CCW - CCW Staff Science Report No. 13/8/1 CCW000800000000O - Lamprey Monitoring, Dee Catchment 2011 - CCW - Contract Science Report 975 CCW0008000000008 - Lamprey Monitoring, Tywi Catchment 2011 - CCW - Staff Science Report 11/8/5 CCW000800000000B\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader of Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tRiver lamprey (Lampetra fluviatilis), Brook lamprey (Lampetra planeri), and Sea lamprey (Petromyzon marinus) are designated features of the River Usk and Wye Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). Assessments of species composition, distribution, abundance and size composition of larval lamprey (ammocoetes and transformers) populations in these river are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment. The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of Lampreys in the Usk and Wye Catchments. This collation of lamprey data is made up of data collected by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the Environment Agency Wales for CCW SAC monitoring reports or CCW Review of Consents reports. The collation includes information about species distribution, composition and abundance for river, brook and sea lampreys which are a designated (Annex II) features within SAC rivers in Wales. The majority of the data was recorded at the genus level and no distinction was made between river and brook lampreys. However for some historical data no distinction was made between sea and river/brook lampreys and these were recorded at the family level. This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Atlas.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:35:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18324\tNRW_DS116346\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.355Z\t1003\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater Shad Monitoring Data\t© CNC/NRW 2011 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333436\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A] Species data is held in Recorder 6. -  Shad Monitoring, Usk, Wye \u0026amp; Tywi Catchments 2006 - CCW - Report no.40 CCW0002800000005. -  Shad Egg Survey River Usk 2010 - CCW - Report no.10/8/1 CCW000280000000E. -  Shad Egg Survey River Usk 2011 - CCW - Report no.11/8/3 CCW0008000000009. -  Shad Egg Survey River Wye 2011 - CCW - Report no. 11/8/4 CCW0008000000008.  -  Shad egg Survey Afon Tywi 2012 - CCW - Report No. 12/8/4 CCW000800000000P. -  Shad Egg Survey, River Usk and River Wye, 2012 - CCW - Report No. 13/8/3 CCW000800000000Q.\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThe Rivers Wye, Usk and Tywi are designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) under the EC 92/43/EEC Habitats Directive for several features including twaite shad (Alosa fallax) and allis shad (Alosa alosa). Shads are migratory members of the Clupeidae (herring family) that ascend rivers to spawn in late spring. The populations of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and issues surrounding water quality. In the UK, significant populations of shad spawn in the Rivers Severn, Usk, Wye and Tywi. The River Wye, Usk and Tywi have been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for shads and other features including salmon, lampreys and bullhead. However, it is not certain whether a breeding population of allis shad still exists. The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), and now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) is responsible for monitoring the condition of shad populations within SACs and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within these designated sites. The purpose of this data capture was to assist with the assessment of Favourable Conservation Status along the Wye, Usk and Tywi to supplement the main shad spawning distribution condition assessment for these rivers. This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Atlas.  Detailed information about each of the survey data can be found under their individual metadata entries.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:35:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18309\tNRW_DS116347\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.913Z\t352\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGreat Crested Newt Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring Data\t© CNC/NRW 2005 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333437\t[A]  Species Data held in Recorder 6: - CCW - HQ Terr - Main Assessment of Great Crested Newts at Johnston and Deeside \u0026amp; Buckley SAC CCW000630000000E    - CCW - HQ Terr - Initial Newt Surveys at Johnstown SAC and Deeside \u0026amp; Buckley SAC CCW0004800000001    - CCW - HQ Main Assessment of Great Crested Newts at Halkyn Mountain SAC CCW000480000000E      The following datasets are sensitive and restricted from public access. - Various - Historical Newt Surveys at Johnstown SAC and Deeside \u0026amp; Buckley SAC - Various - Historical Newt Surveys at Halkyn Mountain SAC  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tLiz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW  Johnstown, and Deeside and Buckley Special Areas of Conservation. - Sites were monitored by contractor Jan Wilkinson. At Halkyn Mountain Special Area of Conservation sites were monitored by contractor Young Associates.\tThis dataset comprises of Natural Resources Wales (NRWs) monitoring data for Great Crested Newts on Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales, where Great Crested Newts are a special feature. It also includes records of other amphibians. This dataset forms part of the Wales-wide Common Standards Monitoring. Currently the monitoring covers three sites in North East Wales: -  Johnstown Newt Sites SAC -  Deeside and Buckley Newt Sites SAC -  Halkyn Mountain/Mynydd Helygain SAC The aim of this data capture was to assess the condition of designated SAC features based on site-specific performance indicators, and to record aspects of their management, both as an aid to management decisions and for reporting at a variety of scales.  The reports also include collations of historical records however, these are not included here as they are not owned by NRW and are therefore omitted from this published dataset.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:27:08.849Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18325\tNRW_DS116350\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.561Z\t390\tValid\tEditable\tMarine data from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Technical Support (Research and Monitoring) Contracts in Wales\t© CNC/NRW 2013 recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence or equivalent. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333530\t[A] All species data is stored in Marine Recorder   This data is now available on the NBN Gateway\tMonica Jones, Data Services Team Leader, EPP, NRW\tThis dataset is comprised of a collection of marine species sightings collated since 1993 from marine surveys, technical support contracts and research, and monitoring contracts. This dataset is currently maintained and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW). The surveys focus on benthic habitats (often referred to as \u0027biotopes\u0027) in intertidal, inshore (typically within 3 nm) and subtidal areas. Original copies of all the surveys are now held in NRW\u0027s Marine Recorder database. The purpose of this data capture was to aid coastal zone and sea-use management and to contribute to the identification of areas of important marine natural heritage sites. This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Marine Recorder which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway.  Only a redacted version of this data is published and available under the Open Governance Licence and available for public use, see Limitation on Public Access and Use Constraints\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-12-23T11:16:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18319\tNRW_DS116351\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.261Z\t301\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSevern Estuary additional samples taken from Phase 1 intertidal surveys (2002)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333531\t[A] All species data is stored in Marine Recorder  2002 CCW Severn Estuary intertidal Phase 1 survey  MRCCW30000000045\tInternal Custodian:  Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environment Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tThis datasets contains the results of additional sampling taken place during phase 1 intertidal surveys around the Severn Estuary. Marine Nature conservation Review (MNCR) littoral habitat forms were completed, and infauna and Particle Size Analysis (PSA) were collected but never worked up due to resource constraints.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-05-16\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:35.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18312\tNRW_DS116352\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.925Z\t887\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Non Native Species (NNS) records from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Monitoring Research and Ad-hoc Sightings (extracted from Marine Recorder)\t© CNC/NRW and MBA 2017. NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333532\tNon Native Species Data - Sargassum muticum around Wales [A] Data is archived under Project No. 151, media no\u0027s. 230 and 485 (File paths: K:\\Archive\\Media201to300\\Media 230 and K:\\Archive\\Media 401to500\\Media 485) [B] Species Data is held in Marine Recorder: 1998-ongoing CCW Sargassum muticum records for Wales MRCCW30000000047  Across Wales Marine Non-Native Species Survey around Welsh Marinas  [C] Data is archived as project 321 and media 1269 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1269) [D] Species data is stored in Marine Recorder 2011 CCW/SOS Across Wales Non-Native Species Survey MRCCW31300000002.  Comprehensive reassessment of Non Native Species in Welsh marinas [E]  Species data is held in Marine Recorder: 2014 NRW Comprehensive reassessment of NNS in Welsh marinas MRCCW32900000002 [F]  Currently stored at Z:\\Achive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_Drive\\Science\\MFSG\\MarineRecorder\\MarineRecorderData\\MRCCW329_BishopsGroup_MBA\\MRCCW329_NBNDATA.mdb   [G] This data is now available on the NBN Atlas [H] Also available as a GSI layers: X:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\tInternal Custodian: Jennie Jones, Officer 2 (Marine Environmental Assessment Officer), Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tThis dataset comprises of marine surveys undertaken by the Natural Resources Wales (NRW) (and former Countryside Council for Wales) for Marine non-native species, currently held in Marine Recorder. These datasets have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway. The purpose of this data capture was to collate and monitor the spread of non-native species in Wales. The surveys represent on-going non-native monitoring, sightings and ad-hoc records of invasive and non-native species supplied to NRW or noted outside of other research and monitoring work. This published dataset is kept separate from other species survey data provided by NRW, to allow it to be updated more often. This data will contribute to the information already available in the UK non native species portal.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-06-20\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-11-12T16:22:08.148Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18308\tNRW_DS116441\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.930Z\t754\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Boundaries\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343431\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Boundaries.lyr\tGIS Service Desk South IDDs - Louise Harris, GIS/Engineering Technician, Spatial Data, KSP Directorate, NRW North IDDs - James West, Advisor- IDB Transition, Flood Risk Strategy, NRW       19/09/2019 Latest info from James West to Data Distribution is that NRW are still waiting for WG to draft an Order confirming the Scheme submitted to them in Feb 2018 to alter the boundaries of 8 (out of 10) IDDs. Until WG do this data will remain unchanged. Metadata will need to be updated when the new boundaries are submitted Contacted James West July 2022.The data on the IDD boundaries Dataset (on the X Drive) is still current. NRW is still awaiting the authorisation of our proposed (very minor) boundary changes we submitted to WG in Feb 2018\tContains the boundaries of all 14 Internal Drainage District\u0027s within wales. Mapped for the purposes of the management of Flood Defences, Water Levels and Flood Risk. Identifies the area which the Board is responsible for preventing flooding, managing water levels and flood defences. Digitised as polygons.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-23T11:18:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18314\tNRW_DS116442\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.909Z\t2245\tValid\tEditable\tAncient Woodland Inventory\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343432\tX:\\Physical\\Forestry\\Ancient Woodland Inventory 2011.lyr   X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\Ancient Woodland Inventory 2021.lyr\tCustodian: Chris Tucker, Specialist Advisor: Technical Forestry (Resilience)\tThis dataset comprises of the boundaries of Ancient Woodland sites in Wales. Each site is categorised as either Ancient Semi Natural Woodland (ASNW), Restored Ancient Woodland Site (RAWS), Plantation on Ancient Woodland Site (PAWS) or Ancient Woodland Site of Unknown Category (AWSU).   Initially all woods greater than 2 ha shown on the Ordnance Survey (OS) 1:25 000 1st Series maps surveyed between 1880 and 1960 were considered. Evidence as to which of these were ancient was provided by presence on the earlier 19th century OS 1st Edition Maps (surveyed 1805 - 1873; scale 1:63 360). The general presumption was that woodland on 1800s maps was ancient, unless there was other evidence that the wood originated between 1600AD and 1800. Supportive indications of ancient status included the wood\u0027s name, its situation in the landscape, and the nature of both the surrounding pattern of enclosure and the pattern of boundaries within the wood. Where available, field survey data such as the presence of indicator species, or other historical maps and documents were also used. In mountainous areas such as Wales the first edition Ordnance Survey 1 inch maps were hard to interpret because the heavy hatching used to indicate steep slopes tended to obscure tree symbols.  The 2021 update to the ancient woodland inventory includes changes to the 2011 version resulting from the provision of additional evidence presented to NRW through public enquiries. This includes private estate maps, tithe maps, management records and photographs. The evidence has been considered by a panel of NRW experts and the inventory amended if the evidence indicates that a site has had another land use for a significant period, i.e. woodland is absent from the site in two consecutive epochs of OS historic maps, or there has been significant ground disturbance to the site, e.g. quarrying, mining or development. Amendments include removal of sites from the inventory, adjustments to boundaries or changes to the ancient woodland category of sites.     The 2011 version remained static until it was replaced by the 2021 version. The 2021 version is being updated at least annually as new evidence becomes available.  An older 2004 version of the dataset also exists - please see metadata for dataset number NRW_DS125363 - Ancient Woodland Inventory for Wales 2004 (AWI2004) https://metadata.naturalresources.wales/geonetwork/srv/api/records/NRW_DS125363?language\u003deng\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-04-09\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/1105/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_ANCIENT_WOODLAND_INVENTORY_2021\t2025-12-19T12:02:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18305\tNRW_DS116443\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.467Z\t459\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPearl Mussel Distribution for Habitats Directive (Council Directive 92/43/EEC) Reporting under Article 17\t© CNC/NRW 2008  Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence or equivalent. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale. At a spatial scale at which the records are considered non-sensitive then there are no use restrictions on this data, including for commercial and research purposes. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343433\t[A]  This data is available on NBN Gateway [B] Collation of records extract from numerous databases and ad hoc records.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tMike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\tThis dataset comprises of a collation of records of pearl mussels from Welsh rivers.  This dataset is a compilation of numerous records from different sources complied for Habitats Directive Article 17 reporting, related to freshwater pearl mussels Margaritifera margaritifera. Every six years, Member States of the European Union are required (by Article 17 of the Directive) to report on implementation of the Habitats Directive. The reporting format set by the European Commission requires a separate analysis for each species and each habitat in each biogeographic region which that country covers.  This dataset comprises of Environment Agency and Countryside Council for Wales surveys.  The aim of this collation has been to ensure that the 10 km distribution maps on the NBN accurately reflect the known position of this species. Records have been compiled with this in mind and hence no attempt has been made to collate every single record for each 10km square within the designated time period.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18299\tNRW_DS116444\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.363Z\t339\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater Fish ad hoc Records for Habitats Directive (Council Directive 92/43/EEC) reporting under Article 17\t© CNC/NRW 2008  There are no use restrictions on this data, including for commercial and research purposes. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343434\t[A]  This data is available on NBN Atlas [B]  Collation of records extract from numerous databases and ad hoc records.\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tFreshwater fish species records collected by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff, contractors or members of the public. N.B. this dataset is an interim upload to allow Article 17 reporting to be completed. The data are being entered into Marine Recorder and a regular export will be carried out once this is complete. This export will replace this dataset. There are numerous reasons for this data capture. This dataset is a compilation of numerous records from different sources compiled for Habitats Directive Article 17 reporting, related to freshwater fish species. Every six years, Member States of the European Union are required (by Article 17 of the Directive) to report on implementation of the Habitats Directive. The reporting format set by the European Commission requires a separate analysis for each species and each habitat in each biogeographic region which that country covers.  This dataset is a collation of records that constitutes a gap filling exercise rather than a coherent dataset in itself. Consequently it should not be viewed as being spatially or temporally representative. However, it may be used in combination with other data to make assessments of the range and / or occurrence of relevant species.  We have high confidence in the taxonomic quality of the records.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:28.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18303\tNRW_DS116445\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.375Z\t870\tValid\tEditable\tEnd of life vehicles - Authorised Treatment Facilities - Public Register\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343435\tData is extracted from spread sheets stored in Local Offices\tData Custodian: Lisa McDonald SE Ops / National Waste Audit Team\tPermitted End-of-Life Vehicle (ELV) sites that can meet the required standards in the ELV Directive for de-polluting vehicles. Authorised sites can have access to the DVLA Certificate of Destruction (CoD) scheme.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-04\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2638\t2026-02-05T09:39:37.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18304\tNRW_DS116446\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.469Z\t1829\tValid\tEditable\tRiver Levels Application Programming Interface (API)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343436\tTelemetry system River levels online database\tCustodian: Alison Hanson Hydromentry and Telemetry\tThis dataset covers monitoring data that is updated on our systems on a minimum of a daily update cycle. This is increased during times of flooding etc. Measurements of the height (m) of water in a river, lake or coastal site taken using automatic field devices, are given usually every 15 minutes. Information is available for NRW river monitoring sites throughout Wales, as well as some reservoirs and coastal sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-06-30\tpublication\tnull\thttps://api-portal.naturalresources.wales/products\t2025-05-13T11:29:24.436Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18301\tNRW_DS116456\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.375Z\t567\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Watercourses\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343536\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Watercoureses.lyr\tCustodian: Spatial Data Team  South IDDs - Louise Harris North IDDs - James West\tAll former Internal Drainage Board(IDB)/Internal Drainage District(IDD) maintained watercourses also known as Reens, Ditches, Brooks and Drains are mapped in linear form in this vector dataset. Data collected and mapped for the purpose of water level management, flood defence maintenance and planning operations/work programming. Identifies watercourses managed for water voles, priority/critical watercourses which must be maintained and watercourses designated as \\urban\\\". Urban watercourses are those in urban areas which are given priority for Health \u0026amp; Safety and Flood Risk reasons.\"\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18298\tNRW_DS116457\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.375Z\t299\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Sluices\tTBC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343537\tX;\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Sluices.lyr\tCustodian: Spatial Data Team  South IDDs - Louise Harris North IDDs - James West\tSluices within the Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge, Lower Wye and Powysland Internal Drainage Districts digitised as point features and mapped for the purpose of flood defence, water level management and asset management. Information recorded includes Top, Base, Winter and Summer water levels, status of the sluice e.g. used or disused, if and when its due for conversion to a modern style sluice, sluice type and material and the watercourses the sluices are located on.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:31:04.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18300\tNRW_DS116458\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.375Z\t327\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Powerlines\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343538\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Powerlines.LYR\tLouise Harris, GIS/Engineering Technician, Spatial Data, KSP Directorate, NRW\tPowerlines within Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge IDD mapped in linear form in this vector dataset. Data collected and mapped for health and safety reasons and planning of operations on the Gwent Levels. Contains information on the height of powerlines.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:33.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18297\tNRW_DS116459\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.066Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Pipelines\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343539\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Pipelines.lyr\tCustodian: Spatial Data Team Louise Harris\tPipelines within Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge IDD mapped in point form in this vector dataset. Data collected and mapped for health and safety reasons and for planning operations. Enables operators to identify the location of pipelines and ensure their safety when maintaining watercourses and flood defences.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18296\tNRW_DS116460\t2024-05-28T12:45:26.883Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Dangerous Corners\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343630\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL dangerous corners.lyr\tLouise Harris, Spatial Data Team, NRW\tDangerous corners of watercourses within Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge mapped in linear form in this vector dataset. Shows dangerous corners for flailing works. Data collected and mapped for planning operations and health and safety reasons.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18343\tNRW_DS116461\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.249Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Safe Locations for Plant\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343631\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Safe Locations for Plant.lyr\tCustodian: Spatial Data Team, Louise Harris\tSafe locations for plant machinery to be left overnight or longer periods across the Gwent levels. Machines have been known to have fuel siphoned from them and faced other vandalism so to reduce this risk of this  safe locations where machinery can be safely left when unattended and not in use have been mapped.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:47:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18353\tNRW_DS116462\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.472Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNVC Terrestrial Plant Surveys Coastal Ecologist\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343632\t\tHeather Lewis, Coastal Ecologist\tHeather Lewis is filling in the metadata for this survey- Evidence Report 143.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:49:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18348\tNRW_DS116463\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.340Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Trash Screens\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343633\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Trash Screens.lyr\tLouise Harris, Spatial Data Team, NRW\tTrash Screens on watercourses within CWL IDD digitised as point features and mapped for the purpose of flood defence and asset management. Trash screens are a type of fencing used to filter out debris in the path of a waterway. They help keep watercourses free of debris and unwanted elements.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:06:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18346\tNRW_DS116464\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.350Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Lower Wye English Watercourses\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343634\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\LowerWye\\IDD Lower Wye English Watercourses.lyr\tSpatial Data Team, Louise Harris\tWatercourses within the English section of Lower Wye Internal Drainage Board now administered by Hoople. These watercourses drain into Lower Wye IDD watercourses within Wales. Mapped in linear form in this vector dataset. Data collected and mapped for the purpose of water level management, flood defence maintenance and planning operations/work programming.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:49:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18351\tNRW_DS116465\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.411Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Lower Wye English Boundary\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343635\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\LowerWye\\Lower Wye English Boundary.lyr\tSpatial Data Team - Louise Harris\tBoundary of the English section of the Lower Wye IDD. Mapped for the purposes of the management of Flood Defences, Water Levels and Flood Risk. Identifies the area which the Board is responsible for preventing flooding, managing water levels and flood defences.\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:49:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18355\tNRW_DS116466\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.540Z\t407\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Pumping Stations\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343636\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Pumping Stations.lyr\tLouise Harris, Spatial Data Team, NRW\tPumping Stations within Powysland and Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge IDD\u0027s mapped as point features\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:34:57.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18354\tNRW_DS116467\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.548Z\t314\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Land Drainage Consents\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343637\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Land Drainage Consents.lyr\tLouise Harris, Spatial Data Team, NRW\tLand Drainage Consents within Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge, Lower Wye and Powysland IDDs mapped in the form of polygons in this vector dataset. Mapped for the purpose of Flood Risk Management. Enables the identification of previous Land Drainage Consents and aids in assessing new Land Drainage Consents, in particular producing the site specific conditions.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18347\tNRW_DS116471\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.332Z\t20\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Species in Wales (archived 2018)\t© CNC/NRW 2007. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343731\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP Species Archived (Consists of 32 layer files)\tInternal Custodian: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis archived dataset consists of 32 datasets, and has been superseded by Environment (Wales) Act Section 7: Marine Species as part of the newer legislative requirements. This dataset is associated with the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) which was published back in 1994, and was the UK Governments response to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD), which the UK signed up to in 1992 in Rio de Janeiro. Similarly under Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act (applicable to England and Wales) 2006, it is required that a \u0027list of the living organisms and types of habitat which in the Assembly\u0027s opinion are of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\u0027. The NERC Act in Wales, is being superseded by the Environment Act (Wales) 2016. Under Section 7 of the Environment Act (Wales) These datasets will provide a basis on which to create a biodiversity list to meet the requirements of the Environment Act (Wales) set out under Section 6 and 7, which require biodiversity and resilience of ecosystem duty, and biodiversity lists and duty to take steps to maintain and enhance biodiversity are required, respectively. This includes the publishing of a list of living organisms of principal importance in Wales, for the purpose of maintaining and enhancing biodiversity in relation to Wales. These datasets will contribute towards the identification of marine organisms of principal importance in Wales, and will be kept under review in order to keep the list up-to-date as per Section 7 of the Environment Act (Wales).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-25\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T13:47:18.166Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18350\tNRW_DS116473\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.349Z\t31\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Habitats in Wales (archived 2018)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Derived in part from data provided under licence by the British Geological Survey © NERC Sensitive data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release and may be requested. Sensitive data can be requested and may be provided under licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343733\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived  Consists of 37 datasets\tInternal Custodian: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis archived dataset consists of 37 datasets, and has been superseded by \u0027Environment (Wales) Act Section 7: Marine Habitats\u0027 as part of the newer legislative requirements.   This dataset is associated with the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) which was published back in 1994, and was the UK Government\u0027s response to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD), which the UK signed up to in 1992 in Rio de Janeiro. Similarly under Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act, 2006 a \u0027list of the living organisms and types of habitat which in the Assembly\u0027s opinion are of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\u0027 is required.   The NERC Act in Wales, is being superseded by the Environment Act (Wales) 2016. This dataset will provided a basis on which to create a biodiversity list to meet the requirements of the Environment Act (Wales) set out under Section 6 and 7, which require biodiversity and resilience of ecosystem duty, and biodiversity lists and duty to take steps to maintain and enhance biodiversity are required, respectively. This includes the publishing of a list of types of habitats of principal importance in Wales, for the purpose of maintaining and enhancing biodiversity in relation to Wales. This dataset captured will contribute towards the identification of marine habitats of principal importance in Wales, and will be kept under review in order to keep the list up-to-date as per Section 7 of the Environment Act (Wales).   These habitats include: blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Native oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh, Sabellaria spinulosa (Ross worm) reefs, Seapens and burrowing megafauna communities and Subtidal sands and gravels.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-12-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-17T13:11:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18352\tNRW_DS116480\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.344Z\t766\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMacrophyte Survey of Welsh Lakes 2015\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343830\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Leafpacs calculator files within folder [Evidence \u0026gt; Freshwater Ecology \u0026gt; Internal Research \u0026gt; Lake Leafpacs data and spreadsheets \u0026gt; ENSIS Surveys 2015] in Microsoft Excel format [Lake_name_LEAFPACS 2 0_metriccalculator_Month_Year] and summary information in MS Excel format [Site_metrics_Summary_2015.xls].\tInternal: Tristan Hatton-Ellis,  Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, NRW\tThis project set out to collect, process and supply to Natural Resources Wales (NRW), ecological and environmental data from a network of 19 lake sites across Wales, in support of NRW\u0027s integrated monitoring programme for protected sites (Special Areas of Conservation and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)), the Water Framework Directive, Nitrates Directive, Biodiversity Action Plans and other legislative and policy drivers. In particular the surveys are aimed at informing management and restoration of protected sites and facilitating delivery of River Basin Management Plans.  Using standard methods, lakes were surveyed to assess the species and abundance of aquatic plants growing within and directly around the lake and to measure water clarity, dissolved oxygen and temperature within the lakes.  The results of the aquatic plant surveys are suitable for the purposes of assessing site condition for Habitats Directive standing water features and SSSI status. The lake metrics are applicable for the production of ecological quality ratios from which the lakes may be classified in accordance with the requirements of Water Framework Directive (2000/60/EC).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-02-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18349\tNRW_DS116492\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.334Z\t319\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) Habitat Quality on Castlemartin Range Pembrokeshire (2015)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343932\tSpecies data is stored in Recorder 6 (attached to the citation of the report) Report is available in PDF Format through the Reports Archive on the DMS Spatial Habitat Maps are being produced by Johnathon Rothwell.\tData Custodian: Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, TEG, NRW Jonathon Rothwell, Terrestrial Mapping Scientist  Metadata Contact: Data services Team, NRW\tThe marsh fritillary Euphydryas aurinia is an Annex II European protected species. The butterfly and its habitats are primary features of the Castlemartin Range Special Site of Scientific Interest (SSSI) in south Pembrokeshire. The metapopulation was discovered in 2003, too late to be included in the Limestone Coast of South West Wales Special Area of Conservation (SAC), but it is included as a key SSSI feature in the SAC Core Management Plan (CMP). Marsh fritillaries exhibit metapopulation dynamics, experiencing regular extinctions and colonisations of suitable patches over time. A large network of suitable sites is essential to maintain their populations in the long term. This survey was commissioned to examine potential marsh fritillary habitat on the Range and to determine the total amount and condition of habitat available for the metapopulation. All survey sites were on MOD Castlemartin Training Area, which in turn lies within the Pembrokeshire Coast National Park. The entire survey area is within the Castlemartin Range SSSI, and most of it is included in the Limestone Cliffs of Southwest Wales SAC.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-10-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18342\tNRW_DS116494\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.170Z\t894\tValid\tEditable\tOpen Access - Other Dedicated Land (CRoW Act)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343934\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Other Dedicated Land 2014.lyr\tChris Heaps, Specialist Advisor: Trails and Open Access, Open Access Team / Recreation \u0026amp; Partnerships Team\tThis dataset shows the area of land that has been dedicated under Section 16 of the CRoW Act by organisations/individuals other than Natural Resources Wales (NRW).   When using this data to understand the extent of Open Access, it should be used in conjunction with Open Land, Open Country, Registered Common Land, Other Statutory Access Land and Other Dedicated Land from the same years.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-16\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/421/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_OTHER_DEDICATED_LAND\t2025-12-19T12:00:41.215Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18340\tNRW_DS116495\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.130Z\t679\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWoodland Grant Scheme (WGS) Walkers Welcome\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343935\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\WGS Walkers Welcome.lyr\tCustodian: GIS and Spatial Services Team..........Keith Harris Jones\tThe former Forestry Commission now Natural Resources Wales, provide the following grants under the Woodland Grant Scheme for woodland owners who welcome the public into their woods: i) Community Woodland Contribution (CWC) ii) Annual Management Grant (AMG) iii) Woodland Improvement Grant (WIG). This dataset shows the locations of the Walkers Welcome areas which are intended to help inform the public of the woodlands where the owner has agreed to general access.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T13:30:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18345\tNRW_DS116496\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.145Z\t693\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of Invasive Non Native Species (INNS) Chinese Mitten Crabs (Eriocheir sinensis) on the River Dee 2007 - 2016\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343936\tData is held in Marine Recorder - MRCCW3000000009C 2015 NRW Monitoring of Chinese Mitten Crabs (Eriocheir sinensis) on the River Dee (NNS)  Data and associated project details e.g. images also need to be pulled together and put into Project Number is 467 Media number 1540.\tInternal Custodian: Ben Wray, Marine Monitoring Group NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Chinese mitten crab (Eriocheir sinensis) is registered by the Invasive Species Specialist Group (ISSG) of the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) as one of the top 100 invasive species worldwide. Mitten crabs are a catadromous species, migrating between fresh water and marine environments to reproduce. They spend the majority of their adult life in fresh water habitats, where they mature to reproductive size. Depending on mitten crab population density and habitat suitability, juvenile mitten crabs will migrate large distances up river to mature. The Dee Chinese mitten crab project set out to investigate the population of Chinese mitten crab in the Dee catchment and achieve a number of objectives including awareness raising, trialling and evaluating potential methods to monitor and manage the population and make inferences about the current and future status of the species in the Dee catchment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-02-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18344\tNRW_DS116497\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.211Z\t521\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAd Hoc sightings of Invasive Non Native Species (INNS) chinese mitten crab (Eriocheir sinensis) 2013 to 2014\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343937\tData is held in Marine Recorder  MRCCW3000000009C 2015  NRW Monitoring of Chinese Mitten Crabs (Eriocheir sinensis) on the River Dee (NNS)   Data and associated project details e.g. images also need to be pulled together and put into Project Number is 467 Media number 1540.\tInternal Custodian: Ben Wray, Marine Monitoring Group NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe Chinese mitten crab (Eriocheir sinensis) is registered by the Invasive Species Specialist Group (ISSG) of the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) as one of the top 100 invasive species worldwide. Mitten crabs are a catadromous species, migrating between fresh water and marine environments to reproduce. They spend the majority of their adult life in fresh water habitats, where they mature to reproductive size. Depending on mitten crab population density and habitat suitability, juvenile mitten crabs will migrate large distances up river to mature. The Dee Chinese mitten crab project set out to investigate the population of Chinese mitten crab in the Dee catchment and achieve a number of objectives including awareness raising, trialling and evaluating potential methods to monitor and manage the population and make inferences about the current and future status of the species in the Dee catchment. This dataset contains a subset of the monitoring data comprising of ad hoc sighting records of Chinese mitten crab surveyed between 2013 and 2014.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-02-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:34.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18337\tNRW_DS116498\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.090Z\t715\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Dee and Bala lake Special Area of Conservation (SAC) population attribute condition assessment for brook, river and sea lamprey (2014)\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343938\t[A] Report Word \u0026amp; pdf format:  Reports and field note are held internally on the DMS using the following pathway Located: Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting (SMAR) \u0026gt; Freshwater Monitoring \u0026gt;  - Running Waters Habitats Directive Data Cycle 1  - Running Waters Habitats Directive Data Cycle 2 - Running Waters Habitats Directive Data Cycle 3. [B]  Field record sheets:  -Dee lamprey annual survey results 2013 – 2018.xlsx SAMP-753-222  [C]  Data:GIS data: Arc GIS Shape files 10.2.2 Spatial data is stored on NRW Y Drive. Location TBC. [D] Species Data in BIOSYS and Recorder 6\tInternal Custodian: Heather Garrett, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning team \u0026amp; Robert Evans, Senior Environmental Monitoring Officer, (A\u0026amp;R), NRW North Ops area team. Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) is responsible for monitoring the condition of  lamprey populations within Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within these designated sites. This is in line with Habitats Directive monitoring and reporting on a 5 yearly cycle. The River Dee and Bala lake is designated under the Habitats Directive as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for several feature including brook, river and sea lamprey (Lampetra planeri, L. fluviatilis and Petromyzon marinus) The populations of all three species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and issues surrounding water quality. The River Dee and Bala lake SAC holds a significant population of these three lamprey species for Wales and Great Britain.   The aim of this data collection was to contribute towards the assessment of the condition of the shad population in the River Dee \u0026amp; Bala lake SAC as part of the Habitats Directive Report Cycles.  This dataset contains data from the following reporting cycles:  Cycle 1: 1999 – 2005,  Cycle 2: 2006 – 2012,  Cycle 3: 2013 – 2018 (data collection is on-going).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:20:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18338\tNRW_DS116504\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.112Z\t579\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status of Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail (Vertigo moulinsiana) at Rhos Goch National Nature Reserve (NNR) (2015)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353034\tA] The final report in Microsoft Word and Adobe PDF formats. [B] Species records, which are held on the NRW Recorder 6 database CCW000800000001J NRW Contracted Invertebrate Research.\tData Custodian: Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystem Services, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW  This survey was done by a contactor: MJ Willings on behalf of NRW.\tThis dataset is made up of the results of a survey for Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail (Vertigo moulinsiana) in 2015, confirming it\u0027s presences at Rhos Goch National Nature Reserve (NNR) following it discovery at this location in 2007, making this one of only three sites in Wales for the snail. Desmoulin’s whorl snail Vertigo moulinsiana, despite being a small snail, is the largest Vertigo species, with a shell height up to about 2.6 mm. V. moulinsiana is only known from three localities in Wales; Cors Geirch and Afon Penrhos near Pwllheli on the Llŷn peninsula, and Rhos Goch National Nature Reserve near Hay-on-Wye in Radnorshire. All of the Welsh V. moulinsiana sites are isolated and do not closely resemble the lake and river-side base-rich fens typically associated with the snail at most of its English sites.   The conservation importance of the species has meant its inclusion in various schedules and Red Data lists. It was categorised as Rare (category 3) in the UK Red Data Books, and more recently has been classed as Vulnerable in the recent IUCN-based UK Red List status review. The species is listed on Annex IIa of the European Community Habitats and Species Directive (92/43/EEC) and is also a Welsh Section 42 Species of Principal Importance (replacing the UK BAP priority species in 2006). The purpose of this data capture was to survey for V. moulinsiana, focussing on areas where there was known presence in 2007, and later a wider search for potentially suitable habitat within the NNR and adjoining Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18341\tNRW_DS116505\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.145Z\t394\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver habitat condition assessment using 2013 and 2014 River Habitat Survey (RHS)  data and Common Standards Monitoring guidance for River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC).\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353035\t[A] Reports and database exports held internally on the DMS using the following pathway: Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting (SMAR) \u0026gt; freshwater monitoring Running waters Habitats Directive data Cycle 3.  [B] Spatial data currently on W drive Heather Garrett Wye RHS but waiting for guidance to move to Y drive. [C] Species data held on BIOSYS \u0026amp; Environment Agency RHS database.\tInternal Custodian: Heather Garret, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team, NRW Melissa Lacan, Senior Env Monitoring Officer (Biology), Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R   Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of a condition assessment of the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC) physical river habitat quality using River Habitat Survey (RHS) and LEAFPAC (river macrophyte) survey data 2012 to 2016. Former Environment Agency RHS database contains welsh data which is now held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  NRW is responsible for monitoring the condition of the river habitat quality on SAC rivers designated for the Ranunculion feature; more formally known as \"Water courses of plain to montane levels with the Ranunculion fluitantis and Callitricho-Batrachion vegetation” (EU habitat code 3260). The RHS \u0026amp; LEAFPAC surveys are conducted as part of the Water Framework Directive (WFD) water body classifications. In addition NRW is required under the Habitats Directive to evaluate the conservation status of this river habitat. Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance recommends that some of this data can be used to assess the following physical habitat attributes: Habitat Modification Score; Riparian zone vegetation naturalness; Large woody debris; Fine sediment; Negative indicators: alien / locally absent species. The Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance sets out eight river habitat quality assessment attributes for the condition of this SAC feature. Specific details for RHS survey methods are detailed in The River Habitat Survey manual produced by the Environment Agency in 2003. Details for the LEAFPAC macrophyte survey method are found in Surveying freshwater macrophytes in rivers Operational instruction 131-07 produced by the Environment Agency in 2008.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-03-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18339\tNRW_DS116508\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.134Z\t452\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring Pen Llyn a’r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2013\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353038\tThe data are archived as The Project Number is 411, Media number 1525   This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters.   H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2013 ASML PLAS\tInternal Custodian - Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tPen Llyn a’r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated for its estuaries and their constituent mudflats, sandflats and reefs; large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds; populations of the shingle-dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus, piddocks in intertidal clay and lower shore boulder communities and reefs, particularly the biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata.  These features are monitored by ASML on behalf of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), which has a statutory duty to produce advice under Regulation 35 (formerly Regulation 33) of the Habitats Regulations 1994. This dataset is a compilation of a number of intertidal research and monitoring programmes conducted in 2013.  This included, intertidal surveying of the honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata at West Afon Dwyfor and Llandanwg; the surveying of the population status of Pectenogammarus populations (LGS.Pec), a scarce, shingle shore specific amphipod found near Pwllheli; sediment analyses of biotopes of the Dyfi and Glaslyn/Dwyryd estuaries were conducted, and subsequently the Glaslyn/Dwyryd infaunal samples were analysed; and finally a species list was created using fixed monitoring quadrats in the upper, mid and lower shore at Porth Oer, the results of which were compared to 2012 survey results.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:36.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18336\tNRW_DS116511\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.016Z\t585\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMacrofaunal Community Monitoring on Saline Lagoons across Wales\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose .\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353131\tData is currently being entered into Marine Recorder by a contractor – Marine Ecosol\tInternal Custodian: Mike Camplin, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist \u0026amp; Marine Monitoring Team Lead, Evidence, Knowledge \u0026amp; Advice  The data was entered into Marine Recorder by Harry Godge from Marine Ecosol.  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team - NRW\tShallow coastal lagoons are designated as a priority Annex I Habitat under the EC Habitats Directive. This dataset includes the infaunal community and species populations and sediment grain distribution data for five Welsh lagoons. Infaunal communities of three lagoons (Cemlyn, Anglesey; Pickleridge, Pembrokeshire; and Morfa Gwyllt, Gwynedd) have been annually monitored since 2006 and two lagoons (Neyland and Carew, Pembrokeshire) have been monitored since 2011. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the ecological health of invertebrate communities and population dynamics across lagoon environments, in order to infer the health and functionality of the ecosystems at each lagoon.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18334\tNRW_DS116512\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.680Z\t798\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMacrofaunal Community Monitoring on Inlets across Wales\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353132\tData is currently being entered into Marine Recorder by a contractor – Marine Ecosol\tInternal Custodian:  Mike Camplin, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist \u0026amp; Marine Monitoring Team Lead, Evidence, Knowledge \u0026amp; Advice  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tLarge shallow inlets and bays are habitat complexes which comprise an interdependent mosaic of subtidal and intertidal habitats. Several of these habitat types (1140 Mudflats and sandflats not covered by sea water at low tide, 1110 Sandbanks which are slightly covered by sea water all the time and 1170 Reefs) are listed as Annex I habitats in their own right.   In Wales inlets occur in a number of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). This includes Carmarthen Bay and Estuary SAC which is located off the south Wales, Pembrokeshire Marine SAC which is located around Pembrokeshire, south Wales; and Pen Llyn a’r Sarnau SAC which is located on the south side of the Llyn Peninsula north-west Wales.  The purpose of this data capture was to examine the indication of anthropogenic change on macro-faunal communities around shallow inlets and bays. This dataset comprises of infaunal species counts, sediment grain size and chemical compositions.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:27.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18335\tNRW_DS116513\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.685Z\t848\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Status of the Snowdon Beetle Chrysolina cerealis on Y Wyddfa (2015)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353133\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database in the Recorder 6\tDr Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Services, NRW\tThe Snowdon Beetle Chrysolina cerealis is restricted in the United Kingdom to Snowdon (Yr Wyddfa), although it was also found in Cwm Idwal until 1980. As recent records of the beetle have been few, this survey was undertaken in an attempt to determine its status and distribution. A total of five beetles (four larvae and one adult) were found following 63.5 hours of hand searching on and around thyme plants (the only recorded foodplant of the beetle in the UK) and stone-turning near patches of thyme from June to September 2015, with all specimens found in Clogwyn Coch in September. No beetles were found in Cwm du\u0027r Arddu, its traditional hotspot, despite exhaustive searches.   Further surveys were also conducted in 2016 please see -  National Museums Liverpool 2017. The status of the Snowdon Beetle Chrysolina cerealis on Yr Wyddfa in 2016. NRW Evidence Report No: 193, 18pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.  - Metadata for 2016 Survey here -  NRW_DS117097\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-23T11:04:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18333\tNRW_DS116518\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.499Z\t702\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring Menai Strait and Conwy Bay (2012): Evaluating Intertidal Seagrass Beds – Field Survey Versus Remote Sensing\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose .\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353138\tArchived as project 410, media 1541.  Locally held version of data at H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2012 CCW (to be cleared up)  H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2012 CCW Zostera\\ Archive: Data stored as Project 410, media 1541 W:\\Media1501to1600\\Media1541\\2012ZosteraProject\\Data\\\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW       Paul Brazier is completing a metadata proforma.  unlikely that the data will have any restrictions.  Contracted ortho-rectified images\tSeagrass beds are of special interest in Wales as features under the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 (as amended), a Section 42 habitat under the NERC Act (2006), a subfeature of Special Areas of Conservation under the Habitats Regulations and is a biological quality element (marine angiosperms) under the Water Framework Directive.  As such, it is crucial that a reliable method of evaluating the quality (extent, density and species richness) of the seagrass beds is used.  Traditionally, field observations are made, and records of the presence and extent derived from mapping using a GPS.  Until recent years, the resolution of aerial photographs, taken from aircraft, has not been high enough to differentiate seagrass beds, unless they are particularly dense, with clearly defined edges.  Many of the intertidal seagrass beds in Wales are patchy and of low density (5-50% cover) and cannot be identified reliably from 1:5,000 aerial photographs. This study uses an unmanned aerial vehicle to take higher resolution images, by flying lower, along with GPS positions, allowing for the many images to be stitched together and be orthorectified.  The two methods are compared, each one proving to have certain benefits and short-comings.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18332\tNRW_DS116519\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.526Z\t768\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Tidal Flaps\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353139\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Tidal Flaps\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset shows the location of tidal flaps across the Gwent Levels from Cardiff to Caldicot. Tidal Flaps are sea doors that allow water out of the reen/watercourse network when the tide is out and close when the tide is in to stop sea water from coming in to the reen/watercourse network.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-01-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18331\tNRW_DS116527\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.412Z\t728\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey of calaminarian grassland in Mid-Wales\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353237\tThe Excel sheets are on the DMS in Evidence\u0026gt;Terrestrial Habitats\u0026gt;Lowland Grassland\u0026gt;Calaminarian – survey 2014 (marked FINAL).  The ARC data is more difficult as they are currently on Stuart Smiths Y drive but I assume will need moving to a more permanent and accessible location (ie X Drive).\tInternal Custodian:  Stuart Smith Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe aims of this project were to refine guidance on calaminarian habitat definition for mid-Wales, and to provide data and information on the extent of the different forms. The purpose of this data capture was to assess calaminarian grassland habitat types, condition and extent for each site, and to gather information on the distribution and frequency of metallophytes and other notable species. This was done by detailed survey of the plants and lichens of the calaminarian grasslands on 27 abandoned mine sites in mid-Wales, 26 in Ceredigion and 1 in Montgomeryshire, with mapping of the compartments in GIS. A wide variation in the character of calaminarian habitat was observed, including grass-dominated examples, metallophyte-rich heathland and largely-bare highly-contaminated land.  Some of the vegetation was considered distinctive in both a British and European context. A total of 90 vascular plants, 38 bryophytes, 227 lichens and 15 lichenicolous fungi were recorded, including many species of conservation concern. Categories of calaminarian habitat types in mid Wales are presented, to facilitate future mapping of the habitat.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-03-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18328\tNRW_DS116536\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.291Z\t558\tValid\tEditable\tOpen Access - Other Statutory Access Land (CRoW Act)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353336\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Other Statutory Access Land 2014.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Other Statutory Access Land 2004.lyr\tChris Heaps, Specialist Advisor: Trails and Open Access, Open Access Team / Recreation \u0026amp; Partnerships Team\tThe CRoW Act provides for public access on foot to certain types of land, amends the law relating to public rights of way, increases measures for the management and protection for Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and strengthens wildlife enforcement legislation, and provides for better management of Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONB).   Under Section 4 of the CRoW Act it states the following: It shall be the duty of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW now forms part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW))  to prepare, in respect of Wales, maps which together show; (a) all registered common land, and (b) all open country.    This dataset contains all Common Land with a higher right of access (Urban Commons, Commons with Deeds for Access, 1899 Commons Act) for areas of Wales, and Elan Valley Estate. This dataset does not include the Registered Common Land dataset.   Digitisation took place in 2014 for all Wales.   When using this data for Open Access, it should be used in conjunction with Open Country, Registered Common Land and Public Forests from the same years. Contact your local authority for access to and information about Registers of Common Land.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-16\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/394/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_OTHER_STATUTORY_LAND_2014\t2025-12-19T11:59:32.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18329\tNRW_DS116538\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.399Z\t692\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Usk Special Area of Conservation (SAC) habitat structure condition assessment 2013 - 2015\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353338\t[A] Reports and database exports held internally on the DMS using the following pathway: Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting (SMAR) \u0026gt; freshwater monitoring Running waters Habitats Directive data Cycle 3.  [B] Spatial data currently on W drive Heather Garrett Wye RHS but waiting for guidance to move to Y drive. [C] Species data held on BIOSYS \u0026amp; Environment Agency RHS database.\tData Custodian:  Heather Garrett, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team Melissa Lacan, Senior Env Monitoring Officer (Biology) Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R   Internal Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of the condition assessment of the River Usk Special Area of Conservation (SAC) physical river habitat quality assessed using River Habitat Survey (RHS) and LEAFPAC (river macrophyte) survey data between 2012 and 2016. NRW is responsible for monitoring the condition of the river habitat quality on SAC rivers designated for the Ranunculion feature; more formally known as “Water courses of plain to montane levels with the Ranunculion fluitantis and Callitricho-Batrachion vegetation” (EU habitat code 3260). Habitats Directive and NRW is  required to evaluate the conservation status of this river habitat. The Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance (JNCC, 2014) sets out eight river habitat quality assessment attributes for the condition of this SAC feature. Specific details for RHS survey methods are detailed in Environment Agency. 2003. The River Habitat Survey manual. Details for the LEAFPAC macrophyte survey method are found in Environment Agency. 2008. Surveying freshwater macrophytes in rivers. Operational instruction 131-07.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-03-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:15.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18330\tNRW_DS116580\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.411Z\t627\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPotential Accessible Natural Greenspaces for Wales\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353830\tX:\\Social Economic\\Greenspaces\\Potential Greenspaces.lyr    Pete Frost’s Greenspaces Toolkit CD (https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/are/commu/gi/_layouts/15/WopiFrame.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d{71B1EAFE-DA54-4340-980B-6E3AE44FCA6C}\u0026amp;file\u003dGreenspace%20Toolkit%20version%202%202011.DOC\u0026amp;action\u003ddefault\u0026amp;DefaultItemOpen\u003d1)\tInternal Contacts: Pete Frost, Senior Urban Advisor, NRW  Metadata; Pippa Jones, GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of polygon areas showing the potential accessible natural greenspaces for the entire of Wales, input by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) with the assistance of Unitary Authorities and Planning areas and their original Urban Greenspace data. This was mapped against Mastermap 2009 mostly but amendments will be ongoing and will be mapped against the latest version of Mastermap that NRW holds at the time of digitisation.  This data will be used by Unitary Authorities and Planning Areas, and NRW staff to help make informed decisions on how best to change/create urban and rural areas, incorporating greenspaces for the health and wellbeing of the community they are surrounded by. This will also impact on the Welsh Index of Multiple Depravation and the Good for People toolkit.  Warning: The Column titled \u0027Status\u0027 is to be used when running any form of statistics as this is the column that says whether the polygon has been checked by the Local Authority, \u0027Draft\u0027 means it has not been checked and should be ignored for any statistical analysis, \u0027No Ver LA\u0027 Is to signify that the Local Authorities don\u0027t know anything about that parcel of land and should be used cautiously in any statistical analysis. \u0027Completed\u0027 are the polygons the Local Authorities have checked and are happy that the information is correct to the best of their knowledge.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18327\tNRW_DS116698\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.298Z\t2007\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAquifer Designation\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. In some cases the dataset maybe made available for non-commercial purposes . For commercial use please contact the British Geological Survey.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136363938\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Aquifer topology superficial.lyr  X:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Aquifer topology bedrock.lyr\tDave Jones-  Groundwater Team\tAquifer designation consists of two datasets.  These are NRW Aquifer topology superficial [Aquifer Designation Superficial Deposits] and NRW Aquifer topology bedrock [Aquifer Designation Bedrock Geology]. Aquifer designations identify the potential of the geological strata to provide water that can be abstract and have been defined through the assessment of the underlying geology. The designations are attributed directly to British Geological Society (BGS) 1:50K. Bedrock geology (formerly known as \u0027solid\u0027 geology by BGS) is a term used for the main mass of rocks forming the Earth and present everywhere, whether exposed at the surface in outcrops or concealed beneath superficial deposits or water. Geology less than 1.8 million years in age are referred to as Superficial Deposits. Superficial deposits are the youngest geological deposits formed during the most recent period of geological time, the Quaternary, which extends back about 1.8 million years from the present. They rest on older deposits or rocks referred to as Bedrock.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-British Geological Survey (BGS)\t2021-07-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18326\tNRW_DS116720\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.115Z\t611\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) North Wales Bat Roosts\t© CNC/NRW  Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence or equivalent. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale. At a spatial scale at which the records are considered non-sensitive then there are no use restrictions on this data, including for commercial and research purposes. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373230\tThis dataset is managed by Cofnod on behalf of NRW - it is published on the NBN Gateway.\tMetadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW  External Contacts and Data Custodian: Aisling May  - Rheolwr Data/Data Manager       aisling.may@cofnod.org.uk Cofnod, Intec, Ffordd y Parc, Parc Menai, Bangor, Gwynedd. LL57 4FG.  (01248) 672603 www.cofnod.org.uk\tThis dataset comprises of bat records from North Wales, including monitoring data from known bat roosts and some incidental bat rescue records. This dataset is now held and managed by Cofnod, North Wale Environmental Information Service.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18379\tNRW_DS116722\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.507Z\t797\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Mospolys Layer voronoi\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373232\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Mospolys voronoi.lyr\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset of Phase 1 mosaic polygons. The polygons are a grid of squares coloured in chequerboard fashion according to Phase1_Code and in proportion to their extent to enable the visualisation of mosaics in a GIS. The mosaic polygons were created by the NRW Phase 2 Tools from the All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Vegetation Layer - Voronoi - GIS dataset and the All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Mosaic Layer Voronoi - GIS dataset.   The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales 1km square dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage, and the maps were digitised as part of the project to map Open Access land. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales. Voronoi was carried out to enable full representation and visualisation of the components of complex mixed-habitat upland polygons.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T13:48:12.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18384\tNRW_DS116723\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.471Z\t752\tValid\tEditable\tWelsh Permitted Waste Operations\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373233\tCustomer Relationship Management (CRM)  Permitting and Licencing (PALS).\tData point of Contact: Tim Morris Waste Advisor\tThe EU Waste Framework Directive (2008/98EC) provides the legislative framework for the collection, transport, recovery and disposal of waste. The directive requires all Member States to take the necessary measures to ensure waste is recovered or disposed of without endangering human health or causing harm to the environment and includes permitting, registration and inspection requirement.   Article 28 of the Waste Framework Directive specifically requires Member States to publicly make available any data relating to waste facilities.   Natural Resources Wales (NRW) issue waste permits across Wales to anyone who deals with waste of any kind. This dataset contains details, obtained from the returned permits, of the waste facilities located across Wales. These waste sites are categorised into groups with each sub-type of waste site being linked to the overarching category.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-31\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2637\t2025-05-16T11:27:58.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18382\tNRW_DS116724\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.533Z\t309\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) (Cardiff) Bat Casework Files 2001 to 2011\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373234\tThis data is held and managed by SEWBREC - NRW own the data but SEWBREC manage it on our behalf as a top copy.  A copy of the data is also available in Recorder 6 but currently only from 2001 to 2005 (12/08/2016) We have received a whole update including all years - however this has not yet been put into Recorder 6. Instead at the moment this is on the NBN Gateway as part of the sensitive data that SEWBREC hold (see parent ID). LC0002150000001R CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework File 2001 LC0002150000001Z CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework File 2002 LC00021500000021 CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework File 2003 LC00021500000028 CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework File 2004 LC0002150000002L CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework File 2005\tContact: Data Services Team, NRW   External Contact:  David Slade Senior IT and Records Officer,  South East Wales Biodiversity Records Centre,  13 St Andrews Crescent, Cardiff CF10 3DB Tel: 029 2064 1110 Fax: 0843 2652714  Email: david.slade@sewbrec.org.uk www.sewbrec.org.uk\tThis dataset comprises of bat records extracted from casework files related to the Cardiff area. The top copy of this dataset is held and managed by South East Wales Biodiversity Records Centre (SEWBReC), who manage the data on behalf of Natural Resources Wales who continue to own the data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:23.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18369\tNRW_DS116725\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.399Z\t663\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater pearl mussel (Margaritifera margaritifera) larval encystment of host fish species on the Afon Eden – Cors Goch Trawsfynydd Special Area of Conservation (SAC)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373235\tData held on a site by site basis on word and excel documents on DMS.\tInternal Contact; Heather Garret, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, Monitoring Strategy and Planning Team, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe freshwater pearl mussel (Margaritifera margaritifera) is protected under several tiers of national and international legislation; EU Habitats and Species Directive Annexes II and V, UK Biodiversity Action Plan list of priority species, UK Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 Schedule 5, Bern Convention Appendix 3, IUCN Red Data List as Critically Endangered. The Afon Eden - Cors Goch Trawsfynydd is the only Special Area of Conservation (SAC) site in Wales where the freshwater pearl mussel Margaritifera margaritifera forms the primary reason for selection of a site. Each year during early summer and when the water temperature is suitable, the female adult mussel releases larvae (glochidia) into the river where they are either ingested or inhaled by host salmonid fish. The species of host fish is specific to each pearl mussel population and prior to these encystment surveys it had been assumed that Atlantic salmon Salmo salar were the host for the Afon Eden population. The results of four annual surveys for gill encystment has proven that brown trout (including sea trout) Salmo trutta is the host species for the Afon Eden population. This dataset contains the results of extent of encystment during electrofishing surveys for salmonid species.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18370\tNRW_DS116726\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.473Z\t670\tUnassessed\tEditable\tStackpole National Nature Reserve Species Inventory and Ad Hoc sightings from around Pembrokeshire (extracted from Recorder 6)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373236\tThis data is held in Recorder 6 under the survey tag Stackpole. In addition to this there is also a lot of data held in the Stackpole Office. These are kept with the NNR managers on hard drives and have not been entered into Recorder 6 due to technical difficulties. They are held in spreadsheets, word documents as individual records and ad hoc sightings.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas.   This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).\tPaul Culyer, Senior Reserve Manager - Pembrokeshire, Operational Services, South, NRW\tThis dataset contains species data from across Pembrokeshire and specifically Stackpole National Nature Reserve (NNR). The data was collected as part of Special Area of Conservation and Sites of Special Scientific Interest monitoring. It includes data from Stackpole, Castlemartin, Broomhill Burrows, Corsydd Llangloffan and Freshwater East.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18373\tNRW_DS116727\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.508Z\t457\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTycanol National Nature Reserve (NNR) Species Inventory\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373237\tAll this data is available in Recorder 6 under the Tycanol Tag.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tPaul Culyer, Senior Reserve Manager - Pembrokeshire, Operational Services, South, NRW\tThis dataset is made up of species sightings from Tycanol National Nature Reserve (NNR). The purpose of this data capture was to collate together species sightings for Ty Canol in order to create a species inventory for the site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18385\tNRW_DS116742\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.487Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCoastwatch Habitat Maps\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373432\tArchived as Project 470, Media 1547 (File path: K\\:Archive\\Media1501to1600\\Media1547)\tInternal Custodian: Gabrielle Wyn, Team Leader, All Wales Marine Advice Team (Gabe Wyn advised that should be kept as permanent record)  Gabe Wyn advised that should be kept as permanent record\tThis dataset consists of paper records which have been boxed and sent to offsite storage. The records relate to the Coastwatch project started during the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) days. The surveys were related to how people use the coast in terms of resources and recreation. The leaflet details some information related to the surveys as follows: Take part in Coastwatch the largest coastal survey of Great Britain ever planned. If you are interested in the coast or concerned about its protection you can help increase our knowledge of our coastal heritage.... to do the job properly we first need to know the location and size of all the different types of coastal habitat such as rocky and sandy shores saltmarshes and sand dunes. We also want to find out which human pressures are affecting these habitats and where they are concentrated.  This data is currently not known to be digitised. It has been superseded by the Phase 1 Intertidal work but still provides and important historic record of the coastline around Wales. The majority of records cover Wales however there are some ad hoc records which are located elsewhere.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:01:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18377\tNRW_DS116744\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.519Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIndexing Paper Record Cards for ad hoc Marine Species Sightings around Wales (Paper Records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373434\tIn Data Services Room in cardboard box labelled EIT Biol Data - Do No Throw Archived as Project 471, Media 1548\t\tThis is a paper records dataset, which is currently stored with the Data Services Team. These paper recording cards were put together by W.E Jones and are made up of numerous records collated from various reports related to the flora and fauna around Wales. Some of these records have been taken from reports and put into the Marine Recorder, but the paper records contain further information and records which have not be digitised. Some of these data may already be available in JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Records, for example 1982 Littoral hard substrata of Menai Strait (see Parent ID for further information).\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:09:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18372\tNRW_DS116767\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.370Z\t473\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Wales: Site packs, paper maps and field notes\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373637\tThese paper records are currently held in Maes y Ffynnon - contact James (Jim) Latham for more information.  Likely to be held in Woodland Tambour Unit in Old Post Room F12 Scans of the maps are held as tifs on Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Woodland\\Raster Data\tJames (Jim) Latham - Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team / David Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset contains site packs for sites surveyed or resurveyed during the Phase 2 Woodland Survey of Wales. The site packs contain site summary sheets, description maps, quadrat data for each site and standard data from National Vegetation Classification (NVC). The summary sheets include general site information, site descriptions, vegetation maps and plant community data.   The data was collected to provide supplementary information for the Phase 2 Woodland Survey of Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-08-25\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T13:37:45.929Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18383\tNRW_DS116782\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.568Z\t955\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAd Hoc Bat Records from around Wales\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373832\tData is stored in Recorder 6.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tLiz Halliwell, Mammal Ecologist Woodland and Farmland Mammals, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset combines the bat records Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds as a top copy within their copy of Recorder 6. It includes Important Welsh Bat Roosts Database (BATSITES-WALES), bat roosts data from Pembrokeshire hibernacula and day / night roost records, Welsh Lesser Horseshoe Bat summer roost surveillance database, and some regional records from Pembrokeshire and south Wales (BATDATA-WALES). This dataset gives an indication of the abundance of each species at each location, and gives the annual summer surveillance records for selected nursery roost sites.  The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts, to assist NRW in conservation work. In particular, to provide information when dealing with development proposals, to support the monitoring and notification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-09-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18380\tNRW_DS116788\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.434Z\t653\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Acid Sensitive Waterbodies Cycle 2\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373838\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 2 2016.lyr\tRob Blacklidge- Reporting Team WFD Specialist\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Acid Sensitive Waterbodies Cycle 2 (2014 - 2019) is a spatial (polygon shapefile) dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined, for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). To implement the guidance on managing forests in order to minimise adverse impacts in acid-sensitive areas, two categories of acid sensitivity have been identified. These are defined as \u0027failing due to acidification\u0027 and \u0027at risk of failing due to acidification\u0027.   Water bodies identified as currently \u0027failing\u0027 have been classified through the Water Framework Directive monitoring as being of less than \u0027good\u0027 status and the cause of that failure has been attributed to acidification. The \u0027at risk of failing\u0027 water bodies have been identified, via the findings of the Welsh acidification risk assessment, as being at risk of failing to achieve \u0027good\u0027 status by 2027, due to acid deposition. The risk assessment combines chemical and biological data and an assessment of the environment\u0027s ability to neutralise the predicted acid pollutants.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-11-01\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/396/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_ACID_SENSITIVE_WB_CYCLE2_2016\t2025-05-08T08:00:56.214Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18368\tNRW_DS116790\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.363Z\t403\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Artificial Waterbodies Canals Cycle 1\t© CNC/NRW © Canals and Rivers Trust and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373930\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Artificial Waterbodies Canals Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal Contacts: Internal custodian - Rob Blackridge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Artificial Waterbodies Canals Cycle 1 (2008 - 2013) is a spatial (polyline) dataset containing Water Framework Directive (WFD) attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive.  As stated within Article 2.8 of the WFD \u0027artificial water body\u0027 is defined as a body of surface water created by human activity.  Canals are therefore reported as a discrete, artificial waterbody dataset. Each waterbody has been assigned \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027, which is a unique identifier that enables a link to WFD attributes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-09-02\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18378\tNRW_DS116792\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.310Z\t814\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwaterbodies Cycle 2\t© CNC/NRW Derived in part from 1:50,000 and 1:250,000 scale digital data under permission from British Geological Survey. ©NERC. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373932\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Groundwater Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tRob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS.\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwaterbodies Cycle 2 (2014-2019) is a spatial (polygon) dataset which has been created for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027…all water which is below the surface of the ground in the saturation zone and in direct contact with the ground or subsoil\u0027.   For the purposes of reporting under the WFD a groundwater body represent a distinct body of groundwater flow with a coherent flow unit including recharge and discharge areas with little flow across the boundaries. These reflect hydrogeological characteristics containing information on flow and stage properties, recharge and vulnerability to pollution. This has been undertaken through defining aquifers into different types and broken into catchment units at Catchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) scale.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-09-09\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/416/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_GROUNDWATER_C2\t2025-12-19T12:03:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18381\tNRW_DS116796\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.510Z\t465\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInvertebrates Recorded on Exposed Riverine Sediments at Llanelltyd on the Afon Mawddach in 2016.\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373936\tData is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database in Recorder 6 Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports\tOriginator: NRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (contractor: Adrian Fowles)       The contact (address) details of the Originator, Adrian Fowles, are withheld as personal data.\tIn order to alleviate flooding problems on the Afon Mawddach at Llanelltyd, Natural Resources Wales is considering the removal of gravel from shingle bars immediately upstream and downstream of the bridge. Data was collected to evaluate the current significance of these bars for Exposed Riverine Sediments invertebrates and to indicate the possible impact of gravel removal on the Exposed Riverine Sediments fauna.    This dataset holds specialist invertebrate species of Exposed Riverine Sediments recorded on three dates in July and September 2016. Comparisons are made with a similar survey carried out on the Tan-lan bar in 2003 and Exposed Riverine Sediment Quality Indices are presented.   Unseasonal floods during August and September may have limited the range of species, but it would also appear that changes to the topography of the Tan-lan (upstream) sediment bar may also be responsible for limiting the area of habitat. The current survey did not yield sufficient species to allow a reliable index to be calculated but there is no reason to suppose that the Llanelltyd shingle bars are no longer important to the Exposed Riverine Sediments assemblage on the river.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-12-31\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-12-24T09:27:30.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18374\tNRW_DS116797\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.393Z\t238\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Status of the Gipsywort Weevil Datonychus Arquatus on Pembrey Air Weapons Range in 2016.\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373937\tReport is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports Data is stored in the Welsh Invertebrate Database in Recorder 6\tOriginator: NRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (contractor: Adrian Fowles)       The contact (address) details of the Originator, Adrian Fowles, are withheld as personal data.\tThe gipsywort weevil Datonychus arquatus, currently classified in Britain as Red Data Book: Indeterminate, is known in the British Isles only from Pembrey Air Weapons Range (SN3604) where it was first discovered in 1997. It is an Independently Qualifying Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) Feature of the Arfordir Pen-bre/ Pembrey Coast SSSI and a Conservation Objective has been developed (Boyce 2007). This contract sought to repeat the monitoring of 2006 to assess the current Condition of the SSSI Feature.   Three Stations were identified in 2006 as supporting an abundance of the foodplant, gipsywort Lycopus europaeus, and the targets for Favourable Condition require assessment of each of these Stations. However, all of the fen vegetation in Stations 1 \u0026amp; 2 had been mown in the week prior to survey, which would have caused the loss of any sub-populations of D. arquatus in these areas had they been present. Survey of the adjacent grazing enclosure revealed an additional area of suitable habitat and this was assessed in addition to Station 3. A total of seven adult arquatus was recorded - five in Station 3 and two in the newly-discovered area, here designated as Station 4. Ten large patches of foodplant were recorded in total, but in four of these the plants were either too short or the area of the patch was insufficient. The loss of Stations 1 \u0026amp; 2 meant that an outcome of Unfavourable: Declining was inevitable and Station 4 is not big enough to compensate for the loss of habitat in those Stations.   Management recommendations are given for each Station, which include the need to allow fen vegetation to recover in Stations 1 \u0026amp; 2 by stopping the current practice of mowing. In Stations 3 \u0026amp; 4 regular small-scale management of scrub encroachment will be needed to ensure that gipsywort patches are not lost to succession. Light grazing should be continued in the enclosure alongside Station 2 to help maintain open fen conditions. A visit was also made to the nearby Ffrwd Farm reserve of the Wildlife Trust of South \u0026amp; West Wales to sample gipsywort populations there for D. arquatus. Here cattle grazing is sufficiently high to restrict gipsywort to protected situations underneath established willow carr etc., although other parts of the reserve without grazing stock, which weren\u0027t surveyed on this visit, may have healthier populations of the foodplant. No examples of D. arquatus were found but further surveys are recommended.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18366\tNRW_DS116802\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.016Z\t1007\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Canals Cycle 2\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383032\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle 2\\WFD Canals Cycle 2 2015.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Canals Cycle 2 2018 (Interim).lyr\tRob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS.      N.B. The cycle 1 dataset is not available on DataMapWales as unlike Cycle 2 data, cycle 1 data is derived from Third Party Data (Canals trust)\tThis dataset is a GIS layer identifying the canal waterbodies managed under the Water Framework Directive and any related programmes. This includes canals which are reported to Europe as artificial rivers. These waterbodies are represented by their centreline, and can be linked to other WFD data using the unique water body ID (WB_ID).   \u0027WFD River, canal and SWT Waterbodies Cycle 2\u0027 is a subset extracted from the Natural Resources Wales Detailed River Network, with attributes removed and merged on the WB_ID.   Updated to 2018 version.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/429/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_CANALS_C2_LIVE\t2025-12-19T12:04:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18376\tNRW_DS116803\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.413Z\t982\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Surface Water Cycle 2\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383033\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Surface Water Cycle 2 2015.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Surface Water Transfers Cycle 2 2018 (Interim).lyr\tRobert Blacklidge, Principal Officer IEA, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tThis dataset is a GIS layer identifying the Surface Water Transfer (SWT) waterbodies managed under the Water Framework Directive and any related programmes. This includes SWT which are reported to Europe as artificial rivers. These waterbodies are represented by their centreline, and can be linked to other Water Framework Directive (WFD) data using the unique water body ID (WB_ID). WFD River, canal and SWT Waterbodies Cycle 2, is a subset extracted from the Natural Resources Wales Detailed River Network, with attributes removed and merged on the WB_ID.  Updated to 2018 version\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/427/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_SURFACE_WATER_C2_LIVE\t2025-05-07T15:28:32.313Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18375\tNRW_DS116805\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.389Z\t1328\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Species Data on the NBN Atlas\t© CNC/NRW Records set to public view may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Data made available via the NBN Request process may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383035\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas  Top copies of data held within corporate store e.g. Recorder 6, Marine Recorder, BioSys, WISKI.   Extracted version in NBN Exchange format held Y:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\NBN Data.  Uploads from legacy CCW held on the H drive (to me moved to the NRW archive storage area)\tJohn Beattie Advisor 2 - Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) species data held on the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Atlas. This includes marine, terrestrial and freshwater species records for a variety of taxonomic groups. Data was collected to support core NRW functions including statutory and non-statutory reporting, condition monitoring and decision making. The NBN data portal is the main route for the publishing NRW species data. Details of each NRW species data can be viewed through the NBN Atlas.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-01-2025-02-06\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-03-03T16:48:00.459Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18371\tNRW_DS116807\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.499Z\t1026\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Licences\t© CNC/NRW Publicly available data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383037\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licence applications line WGS84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licence applications points WGS84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licence applications polys WGS84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licence disposal sites WGS84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Grab Sample Applications.lyr    The restricted munitions data is stored on restricted editing area of the X drive.  X:\\Editing Restricted\tEmmer Litt\tThe records contain coordinate data associated with current marine licence applications in Wales. The location of disposal sites is also available. This data allows the locations of licence applications to be mapped to assess their impacts on designated sites and combined effects with other proposals.   Data related to historic and expired marine licence applications are held as a separate dataset (NRW_DS119099).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-25\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/874/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:MarineLicences\t2026-02-26T10:16:47.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18367\tNRW_DS116814\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.360Z\t2494\tValid\tEditable\tLIDAR terrain and surfaces models Wales\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383134\tX:\\Basemapping\\Height\\LiDAR\\Current LiDAR\tBen Anscombe / Louise Harris (Spatial Services Team).\tThis dataset shows the extent of data in each individual tile that went into the merging process to create the LIDAR Composite DSM \u0026amp; DTM.   Light Detection and Ranging (LIDAR) is an airborne mapping technique, which uses a laser to measure the distance between the aircraft and the ground. Up to 100,000 measurements per second are made of the ground, allowing highly detailed terrain models to be generated at spatial resolutions of between 25cm and 2 metres.   The Natural Resources Wales composite dataset contains digital elevation data derived from surveys carried out over several years and covers approximately 70% of Wales. We have made available 25cm, 50cm, 1m and 2m datasets, supplied as terrain models (a representation of the ground level) or surface models (a representation of object heights such as vehicles, buildings and vegetation).   In addition to the height information, georeferenced, coloured, shaded relief images at the same resolution as the input LiDAR data grids are available.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-01-01\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/6988/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_lidar_tile_catalogue_archive\t2025-05-22T14:15:37.390Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18364\tNRW_DS116819\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.849Z\t483\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAfon Teifi Special Area of Conservation (SAC) population attribute condition assessment for brook, river and sea lamprey population 2014 . Habitats Directive third reporting cycle 2013 – 2018\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383139\t- Data is stored in WISKI / KiECO  - Analysis: Workbook Teifi lamprey 2014.xlsx DMS ref: SAMP-753-136  - Report: Afon Teifi SAC population attribute condition assessment for brook, river and sea lamprey population 2014. Habitats Directive third reporting cycle 2013 – 2018. Report No. 106.\tData Custodian(s): Emma Keenan, Environmental Monitoring Officer Fisheries  Metadata Point of Contact:  Heather Garrett, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator.\tRiver lamprey (Lampetra fluviatilis), Brook lamprey (Lampetra planeri), and Sea lamprey (Petromyzon marinus) are designated features of the Afon Teifi Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Assessments of species composition, distribution, abundance and size composition of larval lamprey (ammocoetes and transformers) populations in these river are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment. The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of Lampreys in the Teifi catchments. This collation of lamprey data is made up of data collected by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW),  Environment Agency Wales and Natural Resources Wales. The collation includes information about species distribution, composition and abundance for river, brook and sea lampreys which are a designated (Annex II) features within SAC rivers in Wales. The majority of the data was recorded at the genus level and no distinction was made between river and brook lampreys. However for some historical data no distinction was made between sea and river/brook lampreys and these were recorded at the family level.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18365\tNRW_DS116820\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.848Z\t525\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBullhead (Cottus gobio) population condition assessment for River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Habitats Directive reporting cycle 3 2013 – 2018.\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383230\tOriginal data is stored in KiECO / WISKI with the exception of the shad data Analysis (when completed)  for each bullhead SAC river is held in a separate spreadsheet on DMS.  Wye: SAMP-753-344\tData Custodian(s): Emma Keenan, Environmental Monitoring Officer Fisheries  Metadata Point of Contact:  Heather Garrett, Freshwater Monitoring Coordinator\tBullhead (Cottus gobio) is a designated feature of the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Assessments of bullhead distribution, population demographic and density in this river is of particular interest as an indication of condition assessment. The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of bullhead in the River Wye catchments. This collation of bullhead data is made up of by-catch records collected by Natural Resources Wales during salmonids, eel and coarse fish surveys. In addition a small number of by-catch records are also made during river macroinvertebrate and shad egg kick sampling surveys. The collation includes information about species distribution, size and abundance for bullhead which are a designated (Annex II) features within SAC rivers in Wales. The first 50 fish are measured and the remainder of the catch at a survey point are only counted.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:12:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18359\tNRW_DS116821\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.613Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine and Coastal Recreation Audit 2009\t*****Too be confirmed/may need the OS acknowledgement***** © CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383231\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\MCRA H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media1501to1600\\Media1552 Archived as project 475, media 1552.\tData: Kirsty Lindenbaum, Marine Resource Management Advisor or  Mary Lewis, Marine Strategic Plan Energy and Advice Team Leader Metadata point of contact: Monica Jones.  Original data i.e.  Natural Facilities database - contact is Sue Williams, Coastal Access Database is within Jont Bullbeck\u0027s team, Richard Deering is relevant contact, although database no longer exists other than what is available as GIS layers       The datasets have not gone through the Open Data Process and therefore should possible not be classed as open data as yet.  However it is unlikely  that there are any restriction.  The Coastal Access database does not appear to have used an OS admin boundary dataset as the background as it is too generalised for high water.\tIn 2009 an audit was undertaken of existing information and knowledge on marine and coastal recreation by Land Use Consultants on behalf of the Countryside Council for Wales.  The study was undertaken in two stages: - Identify, analyse and map existing data on recreational activities, including an validation with key bodies and robustness test - Review existing promotional material and guidance on recreational activities in relation to their compatibility with marine protected areas. The study looked water-based activities, Intertidal activities, and land-based activities.  The data described here is a series of spatial datasets.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-10-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:50:59.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18361\tNRW_DS116826\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.699Z\t1544\tValid\tEditable\tHistoric LIDAR Archive\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383236\tx:\\Basemapping\\LiDAR\\Historic LiDAR\tBen Anscombe / Louise Harris (Spatial services team)\tLight Detection and Ranging (LIDAR) is an airborne mapping technique, which uses a laser to measure the distance between the aircraft and the ground. Up to 100,000 measurements per second are made of the ground, allowing highly detailed terrain models to be generated at spatial resolutions of between 25cm and 2 metres. The Natural Resources Wales composite dataset contains digital elevation data derived from surveys carried out over several years and covers approximately 70% of Wales. We are making available 25cm, 50cm, 1m and 2m datasets, supplied as terrain models (a representation of the ground level) or surface models (a representation of object heights such as vehicles, buildings and vegetation). In addition to the height information, geo-referenced, coloured, shaded relief images at the same resolution as the input LiDAR data grids are available.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-01-01-2024-06-27\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/6988/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_lidar_tile_catalogue_archive\t2025-03-03T16:38:02.306Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18362\tNRW_DS116829\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.705Z\t524\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of the Condition of the White-Clawed Crayfish Austropotamobius pallipes in the River Wye Special Area of Conservation in 2014-2016\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383239\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database in Recorder 6\tOriginator: NRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (contractor: David Rogers Associates)\tBetween October 2014 and September 2016 work was undertaken to provide the data required to assess whether the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC) was in favourable condition for the white-clawed crayfish Austropotamobius pallipes. The Afon Edw, Nant yr Offeiriad, Sgithwen Brook, Dulas Brook (Builth Road), Clyro Brook and Afon Llynfi were surveyed to assess the condition of both crayfish populations and crayfish habitat. A further two rivers (Afon Duhonw and Afon Irfon) make up the full complement of the Wye SAC tributaries but no crayfish were found in these two rivers in 2003 and there are no subsequent records, so these were not surveyed during the current survey and it is assumed that no white-clawed crayfish are present in these two rivers for this assessment.  Sampling followed the methodology developed during the 2003 monitoring programme (Rogers \u0026amp; Watson 2004) and included a combination of manual searching and trapping. During the survey, white-clawed crayfish were only found in three of the eight tributaries of the River Wye SAC: Nant yr Offeiriad, Sgithwen Brook and Clyro Brook (although the latter is not included as part of the SAC, it is included within the condition assessment). Whilst crayfish numbers were relatively robust in Nant yr Offeiriad and Sgithwen Brook, populations have now become confined to the headwaters. In 2016, Clyro Brook still supports a thriving population as it did in 2003, and this now appears to be one of the most important populations in the Wye because there is no diminution in numbers or extent as in the Sgithwen and Offeiriad. In fact, both numbers and extent have increased in the Clyro. The lack of white-clawed crayfish in the Afon Edw, following a mortality in 2006, is worrying given that this has been a very important waterway for this species in the recent past. Also, there appeared to be no crayfish left in Dulas Brook (Builth Road) in 2015 despite supporting a good population in downstream reaches in 2003.  Using the provisional conservation objectives as part of the assessment of Favourable Conservation Status for Natura 2000 features, the 2014 - 2016 survey shows the River Wye SAC does not achieve Favourable Conservation Status. Despite abundant suitable habitat (Attribute 3) and a lack of disease and the absence of aliens (Attribute 4), the density of crayfish throughout is not high enough (Attribute 1) and the distribution throughout the monitoring units is not sufficient (Attribute 2) for the River Wye SAC to achieve Favourable Status. In 2013-14, the Natural Resources Wales\u0027 Cynrig Fish Culture Unit implanted approximately 3000 crayfish into Afon Chwefru, a tributary of the Irfon. As part of the present work, an additional survey of a 1km stretch in two 500 metre sections at the introduction sites was included but no crayfish were found. Recommendations include a review of protection for surviving white-clawed crayfish populations and further survey work to verify the presence/absence of native and alien crayfish throughout the main River Wye and all tributaries and sub-tributaries. Given the importance of Clyro Brook for crayfish, it is recommended that this tributary is notified as part of the Wye Site of Special Scientific Interest and Wye SAC. [This report provides an amalgamation of the 2014, 2015 and 2016 crayfish survey results, and supersedes the 2014 and 2015 survey reports (Rogers \u0026amp; Watson, 2015 and 2016)].\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:36.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18363\tNRW_DS116830\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.762Z\t1096\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Status and Distribution of the Strandline Beetle Eurynebria complanata on Whiteford Burrows, Cefn Sidan, Laugharne \u0026amp; Pendine Burrows and Frainslake Sands, Castlemartin in 2016.\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383330\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database in Recorder 6\tOriginator: NRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (contractor: Barry Stewart Associates)\tThe Strandline Beetle Eurynebria complanata was recently classified as Endangered following a decline that has led to an apparent retreat in its Great Britain range to a few beaches in Carmarthen Bay. Even here, recent counts have indicated that the population might be at a critically low level. The beetle is an indicator of the health of strandline communities, which include a range of rare and scarce species likely to be similarly threatened.   The main aim of this study was to survey sites in South Wales in 2016 in order to determine the current status of E. complanata and inform management of strandline habitats. Repeat visits to key sites were undertaken to assess spatial and temporal changes.   Strandline Beetle was found at just four sites, all in Carmarthen Bay, with peak counts in June of 328 adults on Whiteford Burrows, 211 adults on Cefn Sidan, 288 adults on Laugharne-Pendine Burrows and one adult on Broughton Burrows, providing a total Welsh count of 828 beetles.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-23T11:23:30.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18360\tNRW_DS116836\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.640Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOtter Distribution Maps of Wales (1977-1980)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383336\tPaper format only - Held in in Evidence \u0026amp; Analysis files.   Maps are to be scanned and stored in DMS\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team       The paper maps will be scanned in-house (Worked carried out by Rhonwen Williams for the Terrestrial Mammal Group).  Long-term it would be useful to digitise the data into GIS either in-house or by offering to a third party (e.g. otter group, LERC).  The metadata is to remain as internal view only until available in an electronic format.  To check that this is not the same as the data described by EA as AFA224\tHand drawn maps showing the extent of rivers where otters are distributed.  Appears to be repeat surveys of 15 Rivers (with some additional sub-sites/tributaries).  Survey carried out 1977-1978, 1979-1980, 1980.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18358\tNRW_DS116837\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.324Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPaper Data: NCC Topographical/ Terrain Survey of Sites around Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383337\tCurrently in the Shelves in Old post Room F12.  To be sent to off-site storage, Hannah/Jill to supply the archive reference\tKeith Jones, Spatial Information Team Hannah Brazier, Spatial Information Team       Long-term the data could be scanned and disposed off subject to custodian agreement. Entry should not be included in WebView as data is not suitable for public supply\tto be supplied  3 boxes of paper surveys. Contains hand annotated maps of transect lines, and print out of information such as feature (trig point, fence etc..)grid ref, bearing, distance (3 boxes).  This was field surveys carried out by the Cartography team in the early 1990\u0027s to validated and identify features and reference points that were used to accurately map the boundary of designated sites.  These are to be stored in off-site storage long-term in case the boundaries are queried\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:06:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18357\tNRW_DS116847\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.273Z\t789\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBiodiversity Action Reporting System (BARS) Archived Action Data\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383437\tAn archive of the full data is stored as project T008, T_Media020. Copies of relevant spreadsheet will be stored in the DMS\tData and metadata: Terrestrial Mapping Specialist, Jonathan Rothewell Data and System: Specialist Advisor Enterprise Data Management, Barnaby Letheren       The GIS data should be rated as poor quality and will need cleaning up if it is to be made available as a corporate layer\tAn archive of NRWs (and legacy bodies) actions, boundaries and project data, downloaded December 2016, following the closure of the Biodiversity Action Reporting System.  Biodiversity actions are recorded as GIS point and polygon data with related information held in spreadsheets.  BARS was originally created to support the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UKBAP) and allowed organisations, companies and partnerships to manage and share their own Biodiversity Action Plans.  The UKBAP closed in 2011 and the last version of BARS was launched in April 2012 to collate biodiversity actions in a standardised form and capture geographic locations, enabling spatial analysis and reporting of activity.  The UK BARS partnership (DEFRA, Natural England, Northern Ireland Environment Agency, Scottish Natural Heritage, Wales Biodiversity Partnership, and Joint Nature Conservation Committee) took the difficult decision to discontinue support for BARS in November 2016.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:36.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18356\tNRW_DS117097\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.195Z\t360\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Status of the Snowdon Beetle Chrysolina cerealis on Yr Wyddfa (2016)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137303937\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database in the Recorder 6\tOriginator: NRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe\tThe Snowdon Beetle Chrysolina cerealis is restricted in the United Kingdom to Snowdon (Yr Wyddfa), although it was also found in Cwm Idwal until 1980. As recent records of the beetle have been few, a survey was undertaken in 2015 (NRW_DS116513) in an attempt to determine its status and distribution. A total of five beetles (four larvae and one adult) was found following 63.5 hours of hand searching on and around thyme plants (the only recorded foodplant of the beetle in the UK) and stone-turning near patches of thyme from June to September 2015, with all specimens found in Clogwyn Coch in September. No beetles were found in Cwm du\u0027r Arddu, its traditional hotspot, despite exhaustive searches.   A continuation of the survey from May to October 2016 located ten adults and one larva following 69 hours of search, with all adults recorded in Clogwyn Coch and the larva found under a stone in Cwm Glas Bach. The 2016 survey reinforces the suggestion that the beetle may be retreating to higher altitudes, perhaps as a consequence of climate change or other factors. Over the two year period, 16 beetles (11 adults and 5 larvae) have been found after more than 130 hours of search. As in 2015, no beetles were found in Cwm Idwal.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-12-24T09:27:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18403\tNRW_DS117501\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.189Z\t1050\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Woodland sub-compartment data\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137353031\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest SubCompartments.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan Secondary Metadata contact: Keith Harri Jones\tThe NRW Woodland Sub-compartment Database (SCDB) is to be our authoritative data source, giving us information for recording, monitoring, analysis and reporting which supports decision-making on the whole of the NRW woodland estate.   Information from the SCDB is used by NRW, wider government, industry and the public for economic, environmental and social forest-related decision-making. Furthermore, it supports forest related national policy development and government initiatives, and helps us meet our national and international forest-related reporting responsibilities.     The Inventory SCDB, will help provide a definitive measure of trends in extent, structure, composition, health, status, use, and management of NRW land holdings. We require this to meet national and international commitments, to report on the sustainable management of forests as well as to help us through the process of business and Forest Design Planning. As well as helping with the above, the SCDB helps us address detailed requests from industry, government, non-government organisations and the public for information on our estate.   NRW\u0027s growing national and international responsibilities and the requirements for monitoring and reporting on a range of forest statistics have highlighted the technical challenges we face in providing consistent, national level data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/504/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_PRODUCTISED_SCDB_LLE\t2026-02-23T11:21:52.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18405\tNRW_DS117519\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.325Z\t694\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Tree Cover 2013\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137353139\tX:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2013\\Urban Trees Point 2013.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2013\\Urban Trees Poly 2013.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2009\\Urban Trees Land Categories 2009.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\Urban Extent 2013.lyr\tCustodian:  John Briggs, Landscape Architect, PPG Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW.\tThis spatial dataset relates to Phase 3 (2013) and consists of 3 individual layers, being Points (individual trees), polygons (groups of trees) and urban extents (the extent of the study areas). The technical report (see Related Titles and lineage) shows what is covered in each Phase (page 25), and provides more detail on the methodology behind the different layers in the dataset in Appendix A2.1 (page 132).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-01-17\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/875/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:UrbanTreeCover2013Polygons\t2025-05-02T15:21:26.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18406\tNRW_DS117601\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.327Z\t428\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpatial Action Plan for Great Crested Newts in Wrexham, A Manual for Achieving Favourable Conservation Status.\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137363031\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr E.A.Howe (Contractor: Amphibian and Reptile Conservation Trust)\tThis report draws on a series of previous research reports commissioned by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and its predecessor body on determining the conservation status of great crested newts, Triturus cristatus, in north Wales. The Welsh Government is obligated to maintain or restore the favourable conservation status of great crested newts within Wales and this report aims to provide practical guidance on how this can be achieved within the County Borough of Wrexham. It is one of a series of three such reports, the other two covering the counties of Flintshire and Anglesey. Due to its legal status, the great crested newt is the focus of considerable conservation effort from a variety of bodies. This document aims to provide an overarching strategy to guide developers, conservation NGO\u0027s and local and national government in their efforts in order to achieve the greatest conservation benefit for great crested newts and ensure a unified approach to achieving Favourable Conservation Status (FCS) across the county. To this end, the report is accompanied by a number of GIS layers which will be of particular practical use to those engaged in great crested newt conservation across Wrexham.  The report presents a scoring method by which the Current Conservation Status (CCS) of great crested newts can be determined at multiple geographical scales from a single pond to the entire county. It then uses predictive modelling based on geographical, distributional, climatic and historical data to produce habitat suitability maps and to derive survey targets and pond creation targets to enable FCS to be achieved within the county. These techniques can be applied in various land use and planning scenarios and the report provides practical guidance on identifying areas of particularly high or low value for great crested newts, assessing the impacts of proposed developments, designing effective mitigation and the implications of agri-environment schemes for great crested newts.   During the modelling process, a total of 2,593 extant ponds were identified within the County Borough of Wrexham. With a view to redressing historic loss (37% of ponds having been lost since the 1843 mapping data), FCS was considered in the light of the level of occupancy that would have occurred at the time of the original mapping relative to current levels of occupancy and Habitat Suitability of ponds to the species (Habitat Suitability Index (HSI) scores). Historically we estimate there would have been 4,116 ponds in Wrexham and a corresponding 640 ponds occupied by great crested newts and 988 ponds with an HSI score \u0026gt;0.7. We propose that the 640 occupied ponds should be used to provide a target level for great crested newts (Favourable Reference Value for Range and Habitat sufficiency). This is further qualified by requiring these ponds have an HSI \u0026gt;0.7 to ensure habitat quality, has at least a medium population, are found at a density of at least 4 per km2 (to ensure population viability) and has a minor (or lower) level of threat (to address the species\u0027 future prospects). The Current Status of the species in Wrexham is estimated as having 403 ponds occupied by great crested newts.   The approach to identifying CCS and FCS laid out within this document is one of a number which are in the process of being developed across the UK and Europe. Whilst there is currently no single standard approach which can be applied uniformly across a range of species and habitats, one may emerge in the future. With the recent increase in studies looking at European Protected Species and with developing technologies (such as remote sensing and environmental DNA analysis) we may expect that new approaches to status assessment of great crested newts will develop in the near future. We would encourage efforts to learn from this work to further develop practical tools and necessary policy measures to aid the conservation of both for this and other species. However, this d ...\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:04.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18408\tNRW_DS117602\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.342Z\t1259\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironmental Incident data held in Wales Incident Recording System (WIRS)\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137363032\tData is stored in WIRS. WIRS is stored on its own server.\tInternal Data Custodian(s): John Farnhill, Data Quality Officer, NRW  Metadata Point of Contact:  Data Services Team\tDetails of all environmental incidents that are reported to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) via the environmental incident hotline or by internal NRW staff via the online reporting form are held on the Wales Incident Recording System (WIRS). WIRS in one of NRW\u0027s centralised storage databases. It hold information related to but not restricted too, incidents such as; environmental pollution (land, water and air); illegal dumping of waste (including fly-tipping); damage, destruction and removal of sensitive habitats and species; and incidents relating to the increased risk of flooding. Approximately 9000 calls reporting incidents in Wales are received and processed each year. A risk based approach is used to assess response and is proportionate to our ability to take action to prevent or mitigate impact to the environment; NRW\u0027s role within the incident: and NRW\u0027s professional reputation in influencing the greater public interest. Response time to all major and significant incidents is within 2 hours during office hours and 4 hours outside office hours. Other incidents are assessed based on the risk based approach.  As of 2016-03-01, data is recorded and stored on the Wales Incident Recording System (WIRS). Prior to this, between 2013-04-01 to 2016-02-29 data was stored on the National Incident Recording System (NIRS). The system allows user to manage and track the progress of incidents.  All pertinent details relating to an incident should be recorded as soon as possible. The data recorded over time relating to incidents can be used to provide management information, determine trends and highlight areas of concern, amongst other uses.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:00:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18414\tNRW_DS117691\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.328Z\t816\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey for the Blue Ground Beetle Carabus intricatus at Coed Maesmelin and other Woodlands in the Neath Valley, Glamorgan (2016)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137363931\t[A] Report is held in the DMS report Archive [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (David Boyce)\tThe chance finding of several adult Blue Ground Beetles Carabus intricatus in a garden on the edge of Neath in 2012 and 2013 led to the discovery of a population in the nearby Coed Maesmelin Woodland Trust reserve in January 2015 during a NRW-funded survey. Three adults were recorded, and recommendations were made to improve the quality of the woodland habitat for the beetle by re-introducing grazing and cutting back bramble to provide more open conditions.  Survey work in February 2016 focussed on finding additional populations of the beetle in woodlands within close proximity to Coed Maesmelin. A total of eight sites were visited during this second phase of survey work but no beetles were found, although some areas in the upper Mellte and Nedd valleys and at Craig Gwladys Country Park appeared to have highly suitable habitat. Coed Maesmelin was also re-visited, with just a single adult being found here on 4th February 2016. Bramble growth appeared to be even stronger than in the previous year and given the absence of other records from the Neath area, implementation of a programme of remedial management that aims to improve the habitat quality here is required urgently.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:26:15.397Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18412\tNRW_DS118108\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.345Z\t484\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInvestigating the Behaviour and Ecology of the Glutinous Snail Myxas glutinosa in Field Conditions in Llyn Tegid (2016)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138313038\t[A] Report is held in the DMS report Archive  [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Ian Hughes)\tThe Glutinous Snail Myxas glutinosa is an aquatic species restricted to a single known population in Britain located in Llyn Tegid.  Following on from successful captive breeding experiments and their revelations, proposals were made to use the knowledge acquired to begin field experiments that might lead to improved understanding of Myxas natural ecology and appropriate management procedures.  The data and associat report comprises the instigation of that work.   The experiments proposed are a tentative method to probe into the life of Myxas and answer the following questions:   Does Myxas migrate up and down the shore as lake levels recede and advance?   Do changes in water level benefit or harm the snail population or are they benign?   What substrates favour Myxas?  What physicochemical differences occur across Llyn Tegid and what effect do they have on Myxas?  Would Myxas benefit from the placement of refuges at particular sites around the lake and if so, where and of what material?   Is it possible to manage habitats with simple methods that benefit Myxas and could be employed to return Myxas to its former range?    The first stage has been completed with the installation of bespoke concrete refuges and a brief second inspection of these has already begun to provide some insight into the life of wild Myxas in the lake and an opportunity to begin mapping areas of the lake bed where Myxas occurs.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr1212\t2025-12-23T14:30:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18413\tNRW_DS118342\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.321Z\t417\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Status of the Caddis Adicella filicornis in Wales (2016)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138333432\t[A] Report is held in the DMS report Archive [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Dr Ian Wallace)\tA survey for the Endangered caddis Adicella filicornis (Trichoptera) in 2016 found pupal cases at all four Welsh sites with modern records, and a single adult. Populations at Coed Dol-goch, Halton Wood, Prisk Wood and Wilderness Wood appear to be small and the habitat used by the caddis - very small permanent streams on quite steep slopes close to their origin from groundwater springs - is also highly localised and very fragile. Only Coed Dol-goch supports a reasonable area of habitat with several stations being occupied.   None of the sites has any statutory protection, although Prisk Wood is owned by the Gwent Wildlife Trust and Coed Dol-goch is a community woodland. Consideration should be given to notifying one, if not all, sites as Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) for Adicella filicornis.   Prisk Wood and Wilderness Wood also support the only Welsh populations of the rare cranefly Ellipteroides alboscutellatus, a species associated with tufa-depositing seepages. Further surveys for Adicella filicornis at all four occupied sites are required to determine its status and distribution and to identify threats to its habitat.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr1212\t2025-12-24T09:23:56.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18407\tNRW_DS118344\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.317Z\t683\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Survey for the Caddis Limnephilus tauricus on Cors Erddreiniog, Anglesey (2016)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138333434\t[A] Report is held in the DMS report Archive  [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Dr Ian Wallace)\tA survey of Cors Erddreiniog National Nature Reserve (NNR) from July to September 2016 failed to find the caddis Limnephilus tauricus (classified as Vulnerable in the UK) at its only Welsh locality. First found on the reserve in 2006 in a small area of permanently wet, gently-flowing, spring-fed fen supporting an extensive bed of Yellow Flag Iris pseudacorus, small numbers of adults were also found at the same station in 2007 and 2008. No larvae have been found to date. Wider searches of apparently suitable habitat on Cors Erddreiniog in 2008 and 2016 proved fruitless. Whilst the vegetation at the original station looked very similar to the situation in 2006-08, the water flowing through the site was less clear and appeared to contain more iron flocculence. Further searches of Cors Erddreiniog NNR are required to determine the current status of Limnephilus tauricus.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr1212\t2025-12-24T09:26:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18410\tNRW_DS118345\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.344Z\t400\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Survey for the Sandhill Rustic Luperina nickerlii gueneei on Newborough Warren National Nature Reserve (2016)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138333435\t[A] Report is held in the DMS report Archive  [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Dr Ian Wallace)\tA total of 149 adults of the Sandhill Rustic Luperina nickerlii gueneei were recorded on Newborough Warren between 29th August and 2nd September 2016. This included 121 adults on saltmarsh and dune edge at Penllyn Pysgod and Braich Abermenai, and 28 adults on Cefni Dunes \u0026amp; saltmarsh. Numbers indicate robust populations at these two sites. They have survived the 2013-14 winter storms which eroded large areas of the fore dunes on Newborough Warren and may have responded to developing Sand Couch habitat following the event. The total count contrasts sharply with the previous survey in 2010 which recorded just twelve adults. However, seaward-facing populations in front of Newborough Forest at Ro Bach and Traeth Llanddwyn, and at Gwddw Llanddwyn on Ynys Llanddwyn, where the moth was recorded in small numbers in 2010, seem to have been lost. It is suggested that areas of suitable breeding habitat are mapped as they develop to determine their extent, and that the Sandhill Rustic population is monitored at regular intervals, depending upon the frequency of severe winter storms.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:23:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18415\tNRW_DS118482\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.435Z\t722\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAfon Teifi and Afon Eden Condition Assessment (2016)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138343832\tData is stored in WISKI\tDr Sue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, NRW (Ecus Ltd) Melanie Lacan, Senior Environmental Monitoring Officer, Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R, NRW, NRW\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS) was carried out in 2016 at 27 sample sites on the Afon Teifi and six sample sites on the Afon Eden. The data was collected to feed into condition assessment of the Afon Teifi and Afon Eden according to Common Standards Monitoring guidance for Rivers for the following physical river habitat quality attributes: Channel planform, Habitat Modification Score, Bank vegetation naturalness, Riparian zone vegetation naturalness, Large woody debris, In-channel structures, Fine sediment, Siltation, Negative indicators: alien/locally absent. species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:59:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18411\tNRW_DS118494\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.333Z\t577\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAfon Eden - Cors Goch Trawsfynydd Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Freshwater Pearl Mussel Population Condition Assessment Report - Habitats Directive Reporting Cycle 3 (2013 - 2018)\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138343934\tReport is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports\tNRW Rivers Ecologist: Sue Hearn (Heather Garrett, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, NRW)\tFreshwater pearl mussel counts were carried out in specific monitoring sections and transects on the Afon Eden. The methodology followed protocol devised for the Afon Eden in 2003. The data was collected to feed into condition assessment of the pearl mussel population on the Afon Eden according to Common Standards Monitoring guidance for Freshwater fauna for the following attributes: Spatial extent, Population density, Age structure, Dead shells, Suitable habitat\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:23:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18404\tNRW_DS118585\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.223Z\t533\tValid\tEditable\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicators of Water Courses\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138353835\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS118585\tMetadata point of Contact: Data Services Custodian: Jane Roberts-  Environmental Reporting Team\tThe General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicator scheme or GQAHI (previously known as GQA) was the national indicator for water quality in rivers and canals. It was designed to provide an accurate and consistent assessment of the state of water quality and how it changes over time. These assessments were made for Biological, Chemical and Nutrients and undertaken for discrete river stretches.  This is now a static dataset and 2009 was the final year of the scheme.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-03-21\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2640\t2025-05-02T14:50:59.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18397\tNRW_DS118643\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.059Z\t932\tValid\tEditable\tTranquil Areas Wales\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. The Collation data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. The input layers data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138363433\tPublished Dataset:  X:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas 2009.lyr   Other data related to the published data can be found in the following Folder Level: x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas  e.g. x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas 1997.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas A roads zone B.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas A roads zone C.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas active mineral zone B.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas active mineral zone C.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas airports zone B.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas airports zone C.lyr\tLead Specialist Advisor: Landscape - Jill Bullen Planning Landscape Energy and Climate Change Team, Environmental Policy and Permitting Department.\tThis data collation is made up of two datasets showing tranquil areas across Wales for 1997 and 2009. A map of the land of Wales, graded to show levels of Tranquillity.  It results from combining several underlying layers of data, covering different factors that either contribute to tranquillity or detract from tranquillity. Produced to provide a strategic baseline for monitoring change in tranquillity in Wales to inform strategic planning and policy-making.   Tranquillity is an aspect of the Welsh Government\u0027s Noise Action Plan (in response to the European Noise Directive) and is one of the qualitative measures of natural resources benefits provided by landscapes, as used in SoNaRR 2016.  An accompanying technical report details the methodology used and provides map images of underlying data layers and the resulting Tranquil Areas Map.   Caveats:  1. The resolution of some layers that informed the map were coarse, meaning that those parts of the data are unsuitable for using at a detailed scale.   2. Tranquillity was not formally defined in the accompanying technical report.  Welsh Government has more recently defined the term and Natural Resources Wales adopted this definition for SoNaRR 2016.    Tranquillity is is an untroubled state, which is peaceful, calm and free from unwanted disturbances. This can refer to a state of mind or a particular environment.  The 2009 dataset repeats more or less the same methodology used for a similar study in 1997.  This allows change over time to be reported. Other projects in other parts of the UK using data at better resolution and with more sophisticated analysis means that the Wales 1997 and 2009 studies are regarded as crude.  However to date the 2009 study is the most recent Tranquil Areas map available for Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-05-04\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/878/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:TranquilAreasWales\t2025-05-08T10:17:24.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18409\tNRW_DS118712\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.306Z\t453\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpatial Action Plan for Great Crested Newts in Anglesey, A Manual for Achieving Favourable Conservation Status.\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138373132\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr E.A.Howe (Contractor: Amphibian and Reptile Conservation Trust)\tThis report draws on a series of previous research reports commissioned by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and its predecessor body on determining the conservation status of great crested newts, Triturus cristatus, in north Wales. The Welsh Government is obligated to maintain or restore the favourable conservation status of great crested newts within Wales and this report aims to provide practical guidance on how this can be achieved within the County of Anglesey. It is one of a series of three such reports, the other two covering the counties of Flintshire and Wrexham. Due to its legal status, the great crested newt is the focus of considerable conservation effort from a variety of bodies. This document aims to provide an overarching strategy to guide developers, conservation NGO\u0027s and local and national government in their efforts in order to achieve the greatest conservation benefit for great crested newts and ensure a unified approach to achieving Favourable Conservation Status (FCS) across the county. To this end, the report is accompanied by a number of GIS layers which will be of particular practical use to those engaged in great crested newt conservation across Anglesey.  The report presents a scoring method by which the Current Conservation Status (CCS) of great crested newts can be determined at multiple geographical scales from a single pond to the entire county. It then uses predictive modelling based on geographical, distributional, climatic and historical data to produce habitat suitability maps and to derive survey targets and pond creation targets to enable FCS to be achieved within the county. These techniques can be applied in various land use and planning scenarios and the report provides practical guidance on identifying areas of particularly high or low value for great crested newts, assessing the impacts of proposed developments, designing effective mitigation and the implications of agri-environment schemes for great crested newts.    During the modelling process, a total of 2,146 extant ponds were identified within the County of Anglesey.  With a view to redressing historic loss (37% of ponds having been lost since the 1843 mapping data), FCS was considered in the light of the level of occupancy that would have occurred at the time of the original mapping relative to current levels of occupancy and Habitat Suitability of ponds to the species (Habitat Suitability Index (HSI) scores). Historically we estimate there would have been 2,983 ponds in Anglesey and a corresponding 464 ponds occupied by great crested newts and 716 ponds with an HSI score \u0026gt;0.7.  We propose that the 464 occupied ponds should be used to provide a target level for great crested newts (Favourable Reference Value for Range and Habitat sufficiency).  This is further qualified by requiring these ponds have an HSI \u0026gt;0.7 to ensure habitat quality, has at least a medium population, are found at a density of at least 4 per km2 (to ensure population viability) and has a minor (or lower) level of threat (to address the species\u0027 future prospects). The Current Status of the species in Anglesey is estimated as having 334 ponds occupied by great crested newts. Using the proposed scoring system, FCS in Anglesey requires 464 occupied ponds which form part of medium populations, in high pond density areas and that have an HSI of \u0026gt;0.7: the minimum score to achieve these criteria will be 16 for each pond.  To achieve FCS the score would need to exceed is 464 x 16 \u003d 8,352. With an estimated 334 ponds being occupied and if all of the ponds are in favourable condition (i.e. would score 16), then the Current Conservation Status score \u003d 5,344.  The approach to identifying CCS and FCS laid out within this document is one of a number which are in the process of being developed across the UK and Europe. Whilst there is currently no single standard approach which can be applied uniformly across a range of species and habitats, one may emerge in the ...\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18401\tNRW_DS118724\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.093Z\t446\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Survey for Geyer’s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Cors Erddreiniog SSSI and Cors Geirch SSSI (2016)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138373234\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information \u0026gt; Report Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Dr Martin Willing)\tA survey for Geyer\u0027s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Cors Erddreiniog Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)/ National Nature Reserve (NNR) and Cors Geirch SSSI/NNR in October 2016 failed to locate the snail. Survey locations were based upon previous records dating from 1985 to 2007 on Cors Erddreiniog and 1996 and 2008 on Cors Geirch. Losses from some former strongholds on Cors Erddreiniog are probably due to a lack of grazing, leading to the development of blanketing tussocks of Black Bog-rush Schoenus nigricans and Blunt-flowered Rush Juncus subnodulosus which have shaded out the open \u0027Carex-lawn\u0027 habitat required by the snail. This is most pronounced on Nant Isaf spring field (Compartment 13c) which has not been grazed for over 20 months. In other compartments, snail loss may be a consequence of over-grazing, or periods of under-grazing followed by cattle poaching of the remaining open areas. Similarly, Vertigo geyeri losses from known locations on Cors Geirch appear to be due to the development of a rank \u0027tussocky\u0027 sward that has reduced or eliminated the lightly-grazed \u0027Carex-lawn\u0027 habitat as a result of under-grazing. Voucher specimens from 2005 have been confirmed by the author of this report but no vouchers of the four specimens recorded in 2008 have been seen.       As small populations of Vertigo geyeri can be easily overlooked, it is premature to declare the snail as extinct on Cors Erddreiniog and Cors Geirch. Additionally, areas which previously supported very small numbers of snails and not sampled in 2016 need to be surveyed soon. More regular surveys are required in order to advise on appropriate grazing regimes if Vertigo geyeri is found to be \u0027hanging on\u0027 on either site. If this is the case, then the development of simplified and affordable surveillance routines is suggested.   Even in the apparent absence of Vertigo geyeri, it is vital that areas which previously supported the snail are managed appropriately. Grazing should be re-instated on Nant Isaf spring field as a matter of great urgency, and the current over-grazing of Compartment 9 on Cors Erddreiniog should be relaxed. On Cors Geirch, the area where Vertigo geyeri was first found in 1996 and again in 1998 and 2005 is too rank and grazing levels need to be increased to open up the seepages and the general sward.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:19:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18399\tNRW_DS118733\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.065Z\t1411\tValid\tEditable\tDevelopment Advice Maps (DAM)\tData may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138373333\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\DAM Zone A.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\DAM Zone B.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\DAM Zone C1.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\DAM Zone C2.lyr\tMark Pugh\tThe Development Advice Map (DAM) shows areas at risk of flooding from rivers and the sea for the purposes of land-use planning. The DAM supports Planning Policy Wales and Technical Advice Note (TAN) 15 to guide new development away from areas at risk of flooding wherever possible. Together, they form a precautionary framework to guide planning decisions. The DAM should be considered as a trigger for identifying which development proposals may need to undertake a more detailed assessment of flooding risks and consequences in line with the policy advice set out in TAN15.    Zone C (the 1000yr extreme flood outline) was last updated in January 2020. No further updates are planned to the DAM and you are advised to contact Natural Resources Wales (NRW) about the availability of more up-to-date information.    Zone B (areas known to have flooded in the past) was originally published in 2004 and revised in 2017.  When using the DAM the preview defaults to Zone B only.  You will need to use the legend control to switch on the other zones.    NRW has developed a new Flood Map for Planning (FMfP) which was published in September 2021.  This indicates undefended flood extents over the next 100yrs taking into account the impacts of climate change. This will replace the DAM in June 2023.  Although the Flood Map for Planning is not referred to in current planning policy, it can be considered as the most up to date information on flooding risks.     Due to technical Issues the Web Services and Map Browser for the Development Advice Map are currently not available. If you wish to browse the data see the NRW Flood Risk Viewer https://maps.cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk/Html5Viewer/Index.html?configBase\u003dhttps://maps.cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk/Geocortex/Essentials/REST/sites/Flood_Risk/viewers/Flood_Risk/virtualdirectory/Resources/Config/Default\u0026amp;layerTheme\u003d2\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-06-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3603/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_development_advice_map\t2025-05-07T15:07:37.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18396\tNRW_DS118757\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.812Z\t740\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpatial Action Plan for Great Crested Newts in Flintshire, A Manual for Achieving Favourable Conservation Status.\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138373537\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr E.A.Howe (Contractor: Amphibian and Reptile Conservation Trust)\tThis report draws on a series of previous research reports commissioned by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and its predecessor body on determining the conservation status of great crested newts, Triturus cristatus, in north Wales. The Welsh Government is obligated to maintain or restore the favourable conservation status of great crested newts within Wales and this report aims to provide practical guidance on how this can be achieved within the County of Flintshire. It is one of a series of three such reports, the other two covering the counties of Anglesey and Wrexham. Due to its legal status, the great crested newt is the focus of considerable conservation effort from a variety of bodies. This document aims to provide an overarching strategy to guide developers, conservation NGO\u0027s and local and national government in their efforts in order to achieve the greatest conservation benefit for great crested newts and ensure a unified approach to achieving Favourable Conservation Status (FCS) across the county. To this end, the report is accompanied by a number of GIS layers which will be of particular practical use to those engaged in great crested newt conservation across Flintshire.  The report presents a scoring method by which the Current Conservation Status (CCS) of great crested newts can be determined at multiple geographical scales from a single pond to the entire county. It then uses predictive modelling based on geographical, distributional, climatic and historical data to produce habitat suitability maps and to derive survey targets and pond creation targets to enable FCS to be achieved within the county. These techniques can be applied in various land use and planning scenarios and the report provides practical guidance on identifying areas of particularly high or low value for great crested newts, assessing the impacts of proposed developments, designing effective mitigation and the implications of agri-environment schemes for great crested newts.   During the modelling process, a total of 1,116 extant ponds were identified within the County of Flintshire.  With a view to redressing historic loss (37% of ponds having been lost since the 1843 mapping data), FCS was considered in the light of the level of occupancy that would have occurred at the time of the original mapping relative to current levels of occupancy and Habitat Suitability of ponds to the species (HSI scores). Historically we estimate there would have been 1,659 ponds in Flintshire and a corresponding 258 ponds occupied by great crested newts and 398 ponds with an HSI score \u0026gt;0.7.  We propose that the 258 occupied ponds should be used to provide a target level for great crested newts (FRV for Range and Habitat sufficiency).  This is further qualified by requiring these ponds have an HSI \u0026gt;0.7 to ensure habitat quality, has at least a medium population, are found at a density of at least 4 per km2 (to ensure population viability) and has a minor (or lower) level of threat (to address the species’ future prospects). The Current Status of the species in Flintshire is estimated as having 174 ponds occupied by great crested newts. Using the propose scoring system, FCS in Flintshire requires 258 occupied ponds which form part of medium populations, in high pond density areas and that have an HSI of \u0026gt;0.7: the minimum score to achieve these criteria will be 16 for each pond.  To achieve FCS the score would need to exceed is 258 x 16 \u003d 4,128. With an estimated 174 ponds being occupied and if all of the ponds are in favourable condition (i.e. would score 16), then the Current Conservation Status score \u003d 2,748.  The approach to identifying CCS and FCS laid out within this document is one of a number which are in the process of being developed across the UK and Europe. Whilst there is currently no single standard approach which can be applied uniformly across a range of species and habitats, one may emerge in the future. With the recent increase in stud ...\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18400\tNRW_DS118898\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.054Z\t391\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Survey for Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch National Nature Reserve / Site of Special Scientific Interest and the Afon Penrhos Floodplain (2016)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138383938\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information \u0026gt; Report Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Dr Martin Willing)\tA survey for Desmoulin\u0027s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch National Nature Reserve (NNR)/ Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and the Afon Penrhos floodplain was undertaken in October 2016 to determine its current status and distribution. Sampled locations were based upon the results of previous surveys at Cors Geirch (1999-2008) and Afon Penrhos (2002-2008).   The population at Cors Geirch was found to have declined considerably, with a complete loss of the snail from former stronghold areas and just 17 shells recorded from two small areas at the southern end of the site. Assessments in 2003 and 2008 had considered the population to be in favourable condition. With an eight-year monitoring hiatus, the causes of the sharp decline are unclear but are likely to include the spread of uncut and un-grazed rank, fen vegetation combined with encroaching birch and sallow. Dense vegetation growth at the site was such that the former ditch-lines, used as features for site monitoring, were almost completely obscured and difficult to reach.  At Afon Penrhos, the snail was found in large numbers in short, mechanically-cut and lightly horse-grazed \u0027fen-meadow\u0027; tall uncut fen supported significantly lower numbers. Elsewhere in the UK, the snail is typically associated with tall fen. Vertigo moulinsiana was recorded on about 19 acres (8 hectares) of the site, but population boundaries were not established on the eastern and south-east borders of the surveyed area. It is estimated that a further 75 acres (30 hectares) of potentially-suitable habitat exists which should be surveyed to determine if it supports the snail. The Afon Penrhos population is not only the largest in Wales, but also probably one of the largest in mainland Britain. The significance of the population here is further enhanced by recent considerable population losses at several Special Areas of Conservation(SAC) in southern England. Once the full extent of the population on Afon Penrhos is determined, it is recommended that the site should be notified as a SSSI to reduce any risk of loss or damage due to changes in site management.  In light of the snail\u0027s association with short \u0027fen-meadow\u0027 at Afon Penrhos, it is recommended that rank areas which previously supported Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch are opened up to provide short fen habitat to assist the recovery of the remnant population.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:13:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18402\tNRW_DS118975\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.113Z\t625\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMapping the Extent of Calaminarian Grassland at Mwyngloddfa Cwmystwyth SSSI 2016/2017\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138393735\tY drive:  Shared_Projects\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Phase 2 Grassland\\Phase 2 Grassland.gdb\\Cwmystwyth_veg GIS data stored at above location, rest of the information is stored DMS\u0026gt;Evidence\u0026gt;Terrestrial Habitats\u0026gt;Lowlands Grassland\u0026gt;Projects\u0026gt;Cwm Ystwyth calaminarian survey 2017\tStuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems, NRW.\tThe project aims to map the extent of the Annex 1 type \u0027H6130 Calaminarian grasslands of the Violetalia calaminariae\u0027 (Calaminarian grassland) on Mwyngloddfa Cwmystwyth Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and the adjacent area of Elenydd SSSI in Ceredigion.  The habitat here includes examples where Calaminarian grassland is characterised largely by the presence of metallophyte lichen species, although metallophyte bryophytes and vascular plants also indicate some forms of the community here. This survey built on previous work (Simkin, 2014) that sought to produce a more detailed categorisation of the habitat in mid Wales.  This established that different forms of the habitat existed which were variously typified by metallophyte-rich spoil, grassland and heathland, as well as areas that were largely denuded of vegetation (bare, highly contaminated spoil).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18398\tNRW_DS118981\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.054Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Rhiannon Modiolus Survey 2015: Video and Still Image Analysis\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138393831\tArchived as Project 476, Media 1553 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1501-1600\\Media 1553) and as Marine Recorder Survey MRCCW31600000018 Currently all project data stored at V:\\MMT video Archive\\Drop video\\2015\\Rhiannon Modiolus DDV until moved to archive (this appears to have happened).\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tModiolus modiolus (Horse Mussel) reef is an OSPAR threatened and declining habitat and a component of Annex I Reef.  In Wales two Modiolus reefs have been well described to date off the North Lleyn Peninsula. In 2008 the EIR Grid East West Interconnector Project conducted a survey approximately 10nm North of Anglesey and identified an area of seabed with an acoustic signature indicative of biogenic Modiolus modiolus reef. The presence of reef was later confirmed by drop down video survey. In 2012 surveys for the proposed Rhiannon Offshore Windfarm in the same location also identified several areas with an acoustic signal indicative of Modiolus reef.  NRW undertook some survey work in October 2015 to collect video and still images from areas identified as potential Modiolus reef by the two previous commercial projects in welsh waters. This report provides a detailed description of the method, a summary of the results, and some initial conclusions from the study\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:56:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18392\tNRW_DS118982\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.413Z\t783\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAnglesey Invasive Species, Non-native Species and Sabellaria spinulosa Survey 2016\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138393832\tStores as Project 477, Media 1554 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1501-1600\\Media 1554) [A] Report in PDF Format - held in the DMS (DMS: Evidence\u0026gt;Marine\u0026gt;projects\u0026gt;Contracts\u0026gt;HD INNS and Sabellaria survey October 2016 [B] Raw video files in .ASF format - held in Media 1554 [C] Still images in .JPG format - held in Media 1554  [D] Survey logs as .XLSM  and .TXT format - held in Media 1554  [E] Track GPS data in Arc .SHP and MapInfo .TAB format - held in Media 1554  [G] Marine Recorder Survey [MRCCW32600000002] - held in Media 1554 [H] Field Notes, scanned and stored as .PDF format - held in Media 1554 [H] Field Notes, scanned and stored as .PDF format - held in Media 1554\tInternal Custodian: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor -Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tOne of the aims of this survey was to collect data on Invasive Non-Native Species particularly in relation to Holyhead Deep.  This disposal site receives dredged disposal material mainly from Holyhead Port, which is in close proximity to Holyhead Marina where there are known records of non-native species. Sabellaria spinulosa (Ross Worm) reef is designated by the Oslo/Paris Convention (for the Protection of the Marine Environment of the North-East Atlantic) as an threatened and declining habitat and a component of Annex I Reef.  Individuals and crusts of Sabellaria spinulosa are recorded commonly around Wales but the presence of reef has rarely been confirmed.  Previous commercial surveys off north and west Anglesey have indicated the presence of Sabellaria spinulosa reef.  This survey, therefore, also aimed to verify these records and give an indication of extent of the habitat.  The principal objective was to undertake a taxonomic analysis of video and still images from survey work undertaken around north and west Anglesey in October 2016.  The survey area extended from Point Lynas west to Church Bay and out to sites on the Skerries, West Anglesey Reef and Holyhead Deep. The collection of underwater video using a high definition digital drop-down camera and stills for this survey was undertaken by the coastal survey vessel RV Mersey Guardian in October 2016.  The video dataset encompassed 25 tows, comprising over eight hours of video in total.  A total of 3759 still images taken at approximately seven second intervals were collected simultaneously with the video tows. The entire video/ set of stills from each tow were reviewed prior to detailed assessment and recording, and the boundaries between habitats (biotopes) or habitat complexes noted.  The species and substratum in each tow section or \u0027sample\u0027 was then recorded, and habitats assigned. A total of 35 samples were derived across the 25 survey stations, with Ross Worm reef recorded in 34 of the 35 samples.  Substantial reef over 10cm high was found at nine survey stations with the most diverse Ross worm reef habitat occurring on a stony reef of cobbles among pebbles off Wylfa.   No non-native species were recorded during the survey.  In particular there was concern that the Carpet Squirt (Didemnum vexillum) might occur at some of the sites receiving dredge material from Holyhead Port but it was not identified in any of the imagery. This species has recently been reported in natural habitats off the north Kent coast. Relatively little litter was seen in the video, mostly fragments of monofilament , pieces of plastic and cloth.  Some toppled Ross worm reef was noted at single sites off Cemlyn and Church Bay. Two of the three sites in Holyhead Deep receiving dredge material from Holyhead Port appeared to be siltier than the near shore survey locations.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18395\tNRW_DS119005\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.514Z\t708\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Licensing: Band 1 Sensitive Areas\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Non-sensitive published data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release. An un-redacted version can be requested and may be provided under licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303035\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licensing Band 1 access sensitivity habitats.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licensing Band 1 construction.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licensing Band 1 SPA sensitivity.lyr  for the coprporate versions  Working Version - updated regularly by the Marine Team Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Laura_Grant\\Low_impact_activities\\Final shapefile copies for MLT use\\\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor -Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.                                                              Data will be updated 6 monthly.  The subtidal team will retain an archive, dated version as previous version as we may need to go back to any layers which were issued to marine licence applicants in the past in case there is a dispute.               Update from Laura Grant April 2025: The published dataset hasn\u0027t been updated since 2018. The schedule for 6 month updates was probably overoptimistic when this was said.  However they do intend to update it sometime this year (2025) and they are also hoping eventually to set up something in FME to improve the update process as it is taking a long time to do this manually.\tNRW agreed, identified and documented which habitats and SAC features are sensitive to a range of Marine Licencing Band 1 activities.   This dataset consists of two layers detailing information about sensitive habitats.  These datasets constitute polygons derived from NRW held BAP (Section 7) / OSPAR layers, Regulation 35 SAC habitat layers and Phase 1 intertidal survey.  All the habitats included in these layers were deemed to be sensitive to Marine Licencing Band 1 activities.     The data includes records of protected or sensitive habitats, collected as part of baseline survey, monitoring programmes and ad-hoc records.  A third layer is derived from the Special Protection Area (SPA) designated sites dataset and identifies SPA sensitive to Marine Licencing Band 1 Activities.    The publicly available dataset has some environmentally sensitive information redacted.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitation on Public Access and Use Constraints. An un-redacted version can be requested and may be provided under a NRW licence.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-01-03\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/881/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:MarineLicensingBand1SensitiveAreas\t2025-12-23T10:26:48.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18393\tNRW_DS119019\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.418Z\t478\tValid\tEditable\tThe Wales Outdoor Recreation Survey\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303139\tNRW website and folder in the DMS  https://naturalresources.wales/media/4757/wales-outdoor-recreation-survey-2014-final-report.pdf\tSue Williams: Head of social Sciences.\tThe Welsh Outdoor Recreation Survey (WORS) provides information on participation in a wide range of outdoor activities, from climbing to picnics, which take place in all areas from local parks to mountains and the sea. It also covers people\u0027s attitudes towards biodiversity and details any actions they might have taken to help protect the environment. It is the largest survey of public engagement with the environment by people living in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-05-19\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2641\t2025-05-02T14:48:17.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18394\tNRW_DS119026\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.452Z\t710\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironment Agency Wales (EAW) Landholdings\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303236\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex EAW Landholdings.lyr\tIan Medcalf, GIS Officer, NRW William Patterson, Assistant Surveyor National Services, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset for internal use only, containing information on areas of land in which former Environment Agency Wales (EAW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) have an interest or a right to manage. This includes freehold and leasehold land, EAW land that is let out and land which has been disposed of, access agreements, agreements, deeds of grants, easements, fishing rights, honoraria, lettings, licenses, sub leases, and wayleaves. This provides an indicative representation land managed by EAW in Wales. The  dataset contains only polygons which includes information on site name and unique codes. Content varies for each parcel of land but often includes personal details (which are regarded as sensitive) and basic details of agreements.  The purpose of the data is to provide location and boundary information primarily for use by the estates department.  This is not an exhaustive list of all of the assets that ex-EAW own/maintain or have an interest in.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:34.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18391\tNRW_DS119037\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.412Z\t502\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Landholdings\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right   NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate restricted portions of this dataset.  Unrestricted data may be re-used under the terms of a NRW issued licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303337\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex CCW Land holdings current.lyr  X:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex EAW Landholdings.lyr  X:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex FCW Landholdings.lyr\tKeith Jones, Natural Resources Wales (NRW) GIS TEAM Jonathan Rothwell, Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tThis dataset is made up of separate spatial layers from all three legacy bodies, Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), Environment Agency Wales (EAW) and Forestry Commissions Wales (FCW), all of whom now make up Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  Dataset contains only polygons and includes information on site name, scale captured, unique codes. This provides accurate legal maps for management agreements, and provides a complete table showing all land owned by NRW in Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to provide an accurate digital legal maps for management agreements and provide a complete compilation of land owned or managed by NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:50.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18390\tNRW_DS119078\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.411Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFull-coverage European Nature Information System (EUNIS) level 3 layer integrating maps from surveys and broad-scale models\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303738\tLocation no longer exists (changed since entry creation) Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Marine_Habitats_Species\\Broadscale habitats\\JNCC combined map\\Version 20160427\\MPA_Network_Output_Welsh.gdb\\CombinedEUNIS_CP2_Welsh_all_attr_WGS84  Data available via Lle Portal\tInternal Custodian: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External Contact: Graeme.Duncan@jncc.gov.uk\tThe European Environment Agency (EEA) developed a classification scheme for habitats as part of its European Nature information System (EUNIS) system for managing species, site and habitat information. As part of the classification for EUNIS data there are different levels. Level 1 splits off marine habitats from coastal and terrestrial habitats. Level 2 uses the biological zone and the presence / absence of rock as classification criteria so A1 is littoral rock and other hard substrata while A5 is Sublittoral sediment.  Level 3 introduces depth into the classification and energy for hard substrata, and splits the softer substrata by different sediment types, so A1.1 \u003d High-energy littoral rock while A5.4 \u003d Sublittoral mixed sediments. Some divisions in the classification are determined by biology here, such as biogenic reef and large seaweed or plant dominated sediments. References to specific taxa, rather than those above are first introduced at Level 4, where major epifaunal/epifloral taxa are used to discriminate rocky habitats. At Level 5, discrimination is based on both physical and biological characteristics of the habitats. Level 6, the highest discriminant level in EUNIS, frequently describes notable variations in community structure of level 5 habitats. The purpose of this data capture was to create a spatial layer that contains the best available information on European Nature Information System (EUNIS) Level 3 habitats at any position in UK waters. This dataset combines field surveys, polygon habitat data and board-scale predictive maps from the EUSeaMap model.\tVarious\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:29:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18389\tNRW_DS119099\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.342Z\t765\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Licence Archive\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303939\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine licence archive lines WGS84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine licence archive points WSG84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine licence archive poly WGS84.lyr  Corporate Version Y:\\Marine_Marine Licence apps_Marine Licence apps.gdb Working Version - updated regularly by Marine Team\tEmmer Litt\tThe records contain coordinate data associated with historic and expired marine licence applications in Wales. The data allows the location of applications to be mapped to assess any in combination effects with other proposals.   Data related to current marine licence applications are held as a separate dataset (NRW_DS116807)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-25\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/879/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:MarineLicenceArchive\t2026-02-26T10:17:22.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18387\tNRW_DS119100\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.263Z\t549\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Article 17 Reporting Habitat Features\tData may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313030\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Marine  Consists of 18 layers held within the same location.  These three datasets have been updated in 2018. X:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Marine\\Art17 Subtidal Reef Point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Marine\\Art17 Subtidal Reef Polygon.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Marine\\Art17 Welsh Sandbanks.lyr  Data is also available via the WG web mapping portal \"DataMapWales\". JNCC also have a UK version of the composite Article 17 datasets that is available from their website.\tInternal Contact: Karen Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tReporting under Article 17 of the EU Habitats Directive requires member states to report on progress towards achieving favourable conservation status for habitats and species of Community Importance. The assessment of conservation status does not only relate to that component of the habitat area or species population to be found in Special Areas of Conservation, but to the totality of the habitats and species throughout the United Kingdom.   The results of NRW\u0027s feature monitoring work and other evidence collected (survey) or collated (other data sources) on feature extent feed in to the process of mapping and reporting under Article 17.   JNCC coordinate and provide UK-level feature reports under Article 17; England, Wales, Northern Ireland and Scotland submit country-level data and information to inform these reports.   This dataset only consists of the Welsh component of the Article 17 mapping.  This dataset is due to be replaced by the Marine Annex 1 Habitat Maps (Reg9a 2024) data (NRW_DS161407). The new data will be published on DataMapWales once it has gone through an Open Data Risk Assessment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-02-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/883/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:MarineArt17Features\t2025-11-19T15:10:50.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18388\tNRW_DS119101\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.272Z\t955\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Features\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Derived in part from data provided under licence by the British Geological Survey © NERC Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313031\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine SAC features\\SAC features X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine SAC features\\SAC Features Archive   There are 43 datasets\tKaren Robinson- Marine Ecologist - EVIAN       This metadata entry covers both the live data and the archive. Although current regulation is Reg 37, marine team have not updated any of the exiting layers.  Therefore the latest data is Reg 35 in the X drive data and is supplied in response to request for this dataset.  It is classed as Reg 37 data and a copy may be renamed to separate between the two.  Each layer will be updated as new evidence becomes available. The published data is technically the dataset generated for Reg35 but is the most recent information to inform Reg 37\tThe Conservation of Habitats and Species Regulations 2010 provide for the designation and protection of \u0027European sites\u0027, the protection of \u0027European protected species\u0027, and the adaptation of planning and other controls for the protection of European Sites.   This dataset is made up of 43 datasets showing the extent of features for which our marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) are designated and forms part of the advice packages Natural Resources Wales (NRW) prepares for our marine SACs under Regulation 37 (formerly Regulation 33 and 35) of the Conservation of Habitats and Species Regulations. They show the presence of (extent) of features within our marine SACs at the time of site designation against which statutory advice can be given. They also act as a baseline for feature extent against which changes can be measured during reporting under Article 17 of the Habitats Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-04-17\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/886/?ows_service\u003dwms\tnull\t2025-11-13T16:40:53.389Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18386\tNRW_DS119124\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.049Z\t1285\tValid\tEditable\tNational Marine Character Areas (NMCA)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313234\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\National Marine Character Areas.lyr\tJohn Briggs- Landscape Architect, PPG.\tSeascapes, like landscapes, reflect the relationship between people and place and the part it plays in forming the setting to our everyday lives. Marine Character Areas highlight the key natural, cultural and perceptual influences that make the character of each seascape distinct and unique. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) were commissioned on behalf of Welsh Government to identify the character of Wales’ seascapes at a broad-scale. We divided our inshore waters into 29 Marine Character Areas. This work provides the strategic spatial evidence on seascapes to inform the Wales National Marine Plan, which is being prepared by Welsh Government. Each area profile includes a description of the key characteristics, including their natural, cultural and perceptual influences, as well as land - sea inter-visibility maps.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-01-30\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/390/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_MARINE_CHARACTER_AREAS\t2025-11-13T16:44:13.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18436\tNRW_DS119125\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.190Z\t287\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Shad Egg Survey on the River Wye (2017)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313235\tDMS \u0026gt; Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting \u0026gt; Freshwater Monitoring \u0026gt; Running Waters Habitat Directive 3rd reporting cycle \u0026gt; Shad Fish Species - 3rd Reporting Cycle \u0026gt; Wye DMS \u0026gt; Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting \u0026gt; Freshwater Monitoring \u0026gt; Running Waters Habitats Directive Data Cycle 3 \u0026gt; Wye Shad \u0026gt; Workbook Wye shad egg surveys 2013 onwards.xlsx\tNRW Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator: Heather Garrett.\tThe River Wye rises on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains and flows in a generally south-easterly direction to enter the Severn Estuary at Chepstow. The river is designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the Habitats Directive for several features, including salmon, lampreys and shads: Twaite (Alosa fallax) and Allis (Alosa alosa) shad. In Britain, significant populations of Twaite shad are known to spawn in the Rivers Severn, Wye, Usk and Tywi. The population of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and water quality issues. The purpose of this data capture was to make an assessment of Conservation Status by conducting egg deposition surveys to assess spawning extent and compare these with results from previous datasets. Surveys carried out along the length of the Welsh portion of the River Wye from Builth Wells to Monmouth. The survey was conducted in May and June of 2013, 2015 \u0026amp; 2016.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:16.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18441\tNRW_DS119129\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.315Z\t676\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGrey Seal Halichoerus grypus Phenology at Skomer and Marloes Peninsula\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313239\tData is held in DMS: Evidence \u0026gt; Marine \u0026gt;Marine Mammals \u0026gt; Evidence Base / Contracts \u0026gt; Seal Monitoring and Research \u0026gt; Grey Seal phenology reports Referenced in Marine Archive as project 478, media 1555. • Marloes Peninsula census data.xlsx (EVID-307-372) • phenology basic analysis marloes.R (EDIV-307-371) • Data_Skomer.xls (EVID-307-375)  and metadata Data_Skomer_Notes.xls (EVID-307-377) • Skomer_daily_births.csv (EVID-307-376) • Skomer analysis (EVID-307-373)\tOriginator: NRW Senior Marine Mammal Ecologist: Tom Stringell (contractor: James Bull, Dept. Biosciences, Swansea University)\tThe grey seal (Halichoerus grypus) is listed in Annex II of the EU Habitats Directive and is a qualifying feature of three Special Areas of Conservation in Wales. Pembrokeshire colonies represent the largest breeding population in southwest Britain. The aim of this study was to provide evidence on the status of grey seal pup numbers around the Island of Skomer and the Marloes Peninsula, Pembrokeshire, based on over 30 years of survey data collected from 1983-2015. This report quantifies recent population growth, analyses the timing of the pupping season and makes recommendations for the future of this survey.  Logistical constraints at the end of the observing season required data modelling to estimate both the mid-point and length of the pupping season.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18433\tNRW_DS119132\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.201Z\t1030\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Management Catchments Cycle 2\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313332\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Management Catchment Cycle2.lyr\tCustodian: Robert Blacklidge, Environmental Reporting Team\tWFD Management Catchments Cycle 2 is a dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD).   Management catchments are the unit of geography for which action plans are drafted in implementing the WFD. This process was conducted by using expert judgement in consultation.   WFD Management Catchments have an action plan published that relates to all waterbodies that fall within its boundaries. These data apply to Cycle 2 of the Water Framework Directive\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/428/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_MGT_CATCHMENTS_C2\t2025-12-19T12:05:33.437Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18444\tNRW_DS119133\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.398Z\t1322\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 1\t© CNC/NRW. Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology © NERC (CEH). Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313333\tX:\\EU directives\\WFD\\Cycle 1\\river catchments.lyr\tData custodian: Rob Blacklidge, Environmental reporting team\tThe \u0027WFD River Waterbody Catchments\u0027 are a polygon Shapefile dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Catchments are defined as an area of land from which all surface run-off flows through a series of streams, rivers and, possibly, lakes to a particular point in the water course such as a river confluence. Delineation of the river catchment boundaries primarily utilised the CEH Flow Grid hydrological model run with CEH Integrated Hydrological Digital Terrain Model (IHDTM) data so as to determine water drainage into river stretches. Upstream catchment size was calculated within an ESRI GIS environment using the extension Arc Hydro.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/560/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_RIVERCATCHMENT_C1\t2025-03-07T15:52:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18445\tNRW_DS119134\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.456Z\t1247\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbodies Cycle 1\t© CNC/NRW. Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology © NERC (CEH). Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right 2015. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313334\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Rivers Waterbodies Cycle 1.lyr\tData Custodian: Rob Blacklidge, Environmental Reporting Team\tWFD River Waterbodies is a polyline Shapefile dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). The river polylines were defined by using of the Environment Agency - General Quality Assessment (GQA) River Stretches dataset this is copied directly from the CEH 1:50K River Network with some additional stretches added in by the Environment Agency. Additional stretches were added to cover all stretches designated under the Fresh Water Fish Directive, all stretches designated within riverine SSSIs, and ensuring that all riverine abstractions which contributed to the designation of a Drinking Water Protected Area were sited on a designated river waterbody. In addition to this, some stretches were added where the upstream catchment size was \u0026gt; 10km2, but no other river stretch was designated (10km2 was the original cut-off catchment size form defining a waterbody under the WFD).   The resultant WFD river waterbody datasets is a sub-set of the CEH network, including only stretches that meet any of the criteria outlined above.WFD River waterbodies share the same \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 as their river waterbody catchment allowing these 2 datasets to be linked. All river waterbodies are associated with a waterbody catchment. Not all catchments have a designated WFD river waterbody within them. These data apply to Cycle 1 of the Water Framework Directive. The equivalent layer for Cycle 2 is covered by WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 2 AfA292.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/561/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_RIVERWATERBODIES_C1\t2025-02-04T10:16:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18439\tNRW_DS119140\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.160Z\t385\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMacrophyte Survey of Welsh Lakes 2016\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313430\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Leafpacs calculator files within folder [Evidence \u0026gt; Freshwater Ecology \u0026gt; Internal Research \u0026gt; Lake Leafpacs data and spreadsheets \u0026gt; ENSIS Surveys 2016] in Microsoft Excel format [Lake_name_LEAFPACS 2 0_metriccalculator_Month_Year] and summary information in MS Excel format [Site_metrics_Summary_2015.xls]\tNRW Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems Tristan Hatton-Ellis\tThis project set out to collect, process and supply to Natural Resources Wales (NRW), ecological and environmental data from a network of 14 lake sites across Wales, in support of NRW\u0027s integrated monitoring programme for protected sites (Special Areas of Conservation and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)), the Water Framework Directive, Nitrates Directive, Biodiversity Action Plans and other legislative and policy drivers. In particular the surveys are aimed at informing management and restoration of protected sites and facilitating delivery of River Basin Management Plans.  Using standard methods, lakes were surveyed to assess the species and abundance of aquatic plants growing within and directly around the lake and to measure water clarity, dissolved oxygen and temperature within the lakes.  The results of the aquatic plant surveys are suitable for the purposes of assessing site condition for Habitats Directive standing water features and SSSI status. The lake metrics are applicable for the production of ecological quality ratios from which the lakes may be classified in accordance with the requirements of Water Framework Directive (2000/60/EC).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18438\tNRW_DS119141\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.220Z\t433\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Recreation points\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313431\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Designated Areas\\NRW Forest Recreation Points.lyr\tCustodian: Geraint Morgan and Arpana Chunihal National Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis is a dataset of physical recreation feature on the ground best depicted as point features. These features a divided into eight Categories.   - Signage - NRW branded signs, information and navigational aids, which have management activity and investment (e.g. trailhead, waymarker, information board)  - Built feature - Purpose built and installed NRW asset, which has management activity and investment (e.g. Gate, stile, bin, barrier)  - Forest furniture - Purpose built and installed NRW asset, which has management activity and investment (e.g. Bench, seat, picnic table). Exercise - Purpose built and installed FC asset, which has management activity and investment (e.g. Activity point).  - Play equipment - Purpose built and installed NRW asset, which has management activity and investment (e.g. Play component, structure).  - Viewpoint - A feature which has management activity and investment. Art feature - Purpose built and installed NRW asset or agreed feature, which has management activity and investment (e.g. Art work, sculpture).  - Forest entrance - Regularly used feature to gain access to the forest/NRW estate (e.g. Car park or layby access point).  - Miscellaneous - Features which do not fall into one of the existing feature types, but required to be captured for management purposes.  Within each Category there are a number of Asset types and Asset Subtypes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-01-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/344/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_GB_RECREATION_POINTS\t2025-10-10T07:51:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18426\tNRW_DS119153\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.002Z\t1063\tValid\tEditable\tTransfrontier Shipment (TFS) of Waste and Refuse Derived Fuel (RDF) (Wales)\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313533\tDMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/Regulatory/Permitting/International%20Waste%20Shipments/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2FRegulatory%2FPermitting%2FInternational%20Waste%20Shipments%2FTFS%20DATA%20%28external%20customers%20only%29%2FRDF%20DATA%2FRDF%20data%20%2DPublished\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000C2B903A34CC62A40BBDD00FD337BE4EC\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B6DF30B29%2D753D%2D47A5%2DA1DF%2D4DEAC78C0A06%7D\tMark Rhodes: Senior Environment Officer: Waste\tTransfrontier Shipment (TFS) of waste and Refuse Derived Fuel (RDF) waste export data (monthly tonnage weights) from Wales to non-UK countries, since November 2014. TFS or International Waste Shipments are terms used for exporting and importing waste. The Transfrontier Shipment of Waste regulations govern the movement of recyclable materials across borders either by road, rail, ait or sea. The European Community first introduced a directive on control procedures for the shipment of hazardous waste in 1984, when it became apparent that hazardous waste from developed countries was being exported and dumped in developing countries. RDF is produced from domestic and business waste, which includes biodegradable material as well as plastics.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-04\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2643\t2026-02-05T09:35:44.161Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18443\tNRW_DS119155\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.371Z\t531\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales Waste Information\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313535\tData is stored in WISKI and also on the DMS - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/reporting/was/SWMA/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Irina Nicholas, Environmental Reporting team, NRW John Fry, SUW Advisor Wales, NRW  Metadata Point of Contact:  Irina Nicholas, Environmental Reporting team, NRW\tThis is a summary of the types and quantities of waste handled by permitted waste management facilities in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:14:02.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18428\tNRW_DS119161\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.083Z\t1473\tValid\tEditable\tMain Rivers\t© CNC/NRW. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Rights. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313631\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Main Rivers.lyr\tRoss Akers/ Mark Pugh       Have based start date on the date that EA have recorded in their metadata on data.gov\tThe statutory main river map is a 1:10,000 scale spatial (polyline) dataset that defines statutory watercourses designated by Natural Resources Wales as main rivers. The data is for Wales only. \u0027Main rivers\u0027 are usually larger streams and rivers, but some of them are small watercourses of significance. They include certain structures that control or regulate the flow of water in, into or out of the channel.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-01-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/414/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_MAIN_RIVERS\t2025-05-15T09:24:04.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18430\tNRW_DS119162\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.194Z\t1154\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDrinking Water Protected Areas Safeguard Zones - Groundwater\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and database right. All rights reserved.  Derived from BGS digital data under licence from British Geological Survey  © NERC.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313632\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Non-statutory\\NRW Drinking Water Safeguard Zones (Groundwater).lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Jones - Technical Specialist Groundwater and Contaminated Land dave.jones@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tDrinking water safeguard zones are designated areas in which the use of certain substances must be carefully managed to prevent the pollution of raw water sources that are used to provide drinking water.   For at risk drinking water protected areas we may establish Safeguard Zones. These non-statutory Safeguard Zones are areas where activities can impact adversely on the quality of water abstracted in the drinking water protected area. Action to address pollution is targeted in these zones so that extra treatment of raw water can be avoided.   Safeguard Zones are a joint initiative between Natural Resources Wales and water companies. Safeguard Zones are one of the main tools for delivering the Drinking Water Protected Area objectives of the Water Framework Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:34:59.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18431\tNRW_DS119163\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.236Z\t438\tValid\tEditable\tForestry Commission Wales (FCW) Landholdings\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313633\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex FCW Landholdings.lyr\tData Custodian: Ian Medcalf GIS Officer, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset for internal use only, containing information on areas of land in which former Forestry Commission Wales (FCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) have a right to manage. This provides an indicative representation of land owned by the former FCW in Wales.   The dataset contains only polygons which includes information on site name and unique codes. Content varies for each parcel of land but often includes personal details which are regarded as sensitive.    The purpose of the data is to provide location and boundary information primarily for use by the estates department.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2000-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:44:34.385Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18432\tNRW_DS119168\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.191Z\t368\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhytophthora Working Target Sites\t© CNC/NRW  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Please note with redactions the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright.  Please contact Data Services for further information\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313638\tX:\\Incidents\\Forest Health\\Phytophthora Working Target Sites.lyr   Also available on the Y Drive: Y:\\Tree Health\\P ramorum\\FRWTS\\   where the tree health officers access the most up to date data. They also archive the older versions of this data in this folder as well.\tInternal Custodian: Callum Stone       Contains personal information in the comments fields which is why we cannot just give it out.\tPhytophthora ramorum (P. ramorum) is a fungus-like pathogen called a water mould. This is working data for the management of Phytophthora ramorum surveillance.  This is a spatial dataset which is generally updated weekly from information held by Forest Research on Natural Resources Wales (NRWs) behalf, as part of their Service Level Agreement. The data is very rough and is to be used internally, primarily by the Tree Health Team. This is not to be released externally and the use of internally without understanding of the terminology may result in misinformation.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-07-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:59:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18440\tNRW_DS119169\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.317Z\t595\tUnassessed\tEditable\tConstruction and Demolition waste survey Wales 2012\tInternal version:  © CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  External Version: © CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313639\tData:  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/Regulatory/wasters/waar/CD%202012%20Survey/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx   Report: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/Regulatory/wasters/waar/CD%202012%20Survey/Wales%20Waste%20Survey%20Report%20-%20CD%20Final.pdf\tJohn Fry, Waste Advisor John.Fry@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk       John Fry: john.fry@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThe purpose of this data collection was to produce information on the quantities, origins (by industry sector and geographic region), and fate (management method) of Construction and Demolition (C and D) wastes generated by businesses in Wales in 2012. This information is required by Welsh Government for a variety of reasons including: reporting on waste generation in compliance with the EU Waste Statistics Regulations 2002; informing the development of national waste policy; monitoring progress against national waste prevention and recycling targets; informing waste planners and the regulator; and providing data to the waste management industry to inform investment decisions.  The precision for the total waste generated was +/- 33.2% at 90% confidence.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:29:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18442\tNRW_DS119170\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.239Z\t270\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIndustrial and Commercial waste survey Wales 2012\tInternal version:  © CNC/NRW  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.   External Version: © CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313730\tData and Report Link:  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/Regulatory/wasters/waar/IC%202012%20Survey/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx\tJohn Fry, Waste Advisor john.frycyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThe purpose of this study was to produce information on the quantities, origins (by industry sector and geographic region), and fate (management method) of industrial and commercial (I and C) wastes generated by businesses in Wales in 2012. This information is required by Welsh Government for a variety of reasons including: reporting on waste generation in compliance with the EU Waste Statistics Regulations 2002; informing the development of national waste policy; monitoring progress against national waste prevention and recycling targets; informing waste planners and the regulator; and providing data to the waste management industry to inform investment decisions.  The precision for the total waste generated was +/- 7.9% at 90% confidence.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18434\tNRW_DS119181\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.225Z\t362\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Shad Egg Survey on the River Usk (2013 - 2018)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313831\tDMS \u0026gt; Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting \u0026gt; Freshwater Monitoring \u0026gt; Running Waters Habitat Directive 3rd reporting cycle \u0026gt; Shad Fish Species - 3rd Reporting Cycle \u0026gt; Usk. DMS \u0026gt; Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting \u0026gt; Freshwater Monitoring \u0026gt; Running Waters Habitats Directive Data Cycle 3 \u0026gt; Usk Shad \u0026gt; Workbook Usk shad cycle 3 2013 onwards.xlsx\tNRW Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator: Heather Garrett.\tThis report assesses the spawning extent population attribute of Allis and Twaite shad on the River Usk Special Area of Conservation (SAC), using a kick sampling monitoring method for shad eggs at routine sampling points using an Natural Resources Wales (NRW) standard protocol (Garrett, 2015) during the months of May - June in 2013, 2015 and 2016. This is one of the population attributes for condition assessment from the Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance for freshwater features (Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC), 2015). The two remaining population attributes are never assessed by NRW because of the invasive techniques for sampling juvenile and adult life stages of these two species. The environmental attribute targets for water quality, flow, river habitat etc will be assessed when sufficient data has been collated and is available.   Overall the shad spawning distribution target was not met on the River Usk SAC in the third reporting cycle. This preliminary assessment is classed as a Fail. For freshwater species and habitats the overall assessment of the feature as \u0027Favourable\u0027 or \u0027Unfavourable\u0027 (JNCC, 2017) incorporates both abiotic and biotic attributes. This overall assessment will be made when in addition to the population attributes, the environmental attributes (water quality, flow, river habitat, barriers to migration etc.) for the SAC river have also been assessed. This analysis will be documented in a summary table for each SAC river and each of its features.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18437\tNRW_DS119221\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.209Z\t687\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAfforested Deep Peat\t© CNC/NRW and BGS,OS, CEH \u0026amp; CU. NRW will only release this data in some circumstances and does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset unless they have a valid data use licence from the originators of the raw data used to derive these products.  Attribution Statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.   Contains Ordnance Survey Data. © Crown copyright and database right Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444 Mapping derived from 1:50,000 scale, BGS DiGMapGB ©NERC National Soils Map © Cranfield University (NSRI) LC0023/004-3 [2011] Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology information ©NERC (CEH) 2011. © Crown copyright and database right [2011]\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323231\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\Afforested Deep Peat.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team, NRW\tAreas in Wales where deep peat sites have been restored to forest. This dataset comprises the total afforested deep peat, including modified deep peat, in Wales and is categorised as Broadleaved or predominantly broadleaved, Conifer or Predominantly Conifer and Transition. The datasets gives information on soil type along with the species (broadleaved and conifer) replanted or whether the transformation of the site is still in transition.  Peatland in pristine or good condition provides a range of critical ecosystem services, including biodiversity, carbon storage and sequestration, regulation of stream base flows, water runoff and downstream flood peaks and nutrient regulation and retention. Peatlands are also sinks and sources of several natural greenhouse gases, particularly carbon dioxide (CO2) and methane (CH4). More than 75% of deep peat soils in Wales are covered in semi-natural vegetation. Most of this is upland blanket bog, with significant amounts of fen and flush and, locally, lowland raised bog. These are all UK Biodiversity Action Plan priority habitats with UK and Welsh targets for habitat management and restoration.  In order to deliver the habitat restoration targets set out in the Welsh Environmental Strategy, there is a need to progressively restore semi-natural habitat in afforested areas. Restoration measures should seek to better integrate the key objectives of nature conservation, soil carbon protection, carbon sequestration and water quality.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:46:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18429\tNRW_DS119234\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.069Z\t1031\tUnassessed\tEditable\tKnow Your River - Salmon and Sea Trout Catchment Summary\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323334\tMost up to date reports should be on the NRW website - https://naturalresources.wales/evidence-and-data/research-and-reports/terrestrial-and-freshwater-species-reports/know-your-rivers-salmon-and-sea-trout-catchment-summaries/?lang\u003den  Internal NE/NW docs  are held - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/anass/fr/Salmon%20action%20plans/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx  SW/MID KYR - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/anass/fr/Salmon%20action%20plans/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Fanass%2Ffr%2FSalmon%20action%20plans%2FSW%20Know%20Your%20Rivers\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120006992F85D1E299A439F90D9D45A521C69\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B8EF66B5A%2DB381%2D4FD9%2DA72E%2D8B9041DFF0D5%7D  SC/SE - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/anass/fr/Salmon%20action%20plans/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Fanass%2Ffr%2FSalmon%20action%20plans%2FSouth%20East%20Know%20Your%20Rivers\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120006992F85D1E299A439F90D9D45A521C69\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B8EF66B5A%2DB381%2D4FD9%2DA72E%2D8B9041DFF0D5%7D\tInternal Contact: Robert Evans, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, NW Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team   Metadata Point of Contact:  Data Services Team\tThe Know Your River documents give a brief overview of Salmon and Sea trout stocks on major rivers throughout Wales. Documents contain the following data: Rod Catches - data is graphed. Rod catch data is collected through anglers. All salmon and sea trout rod catch should be recorded by rod license holders. This data is used to estimate overall fish runs on catchments. Conservation objectives - data is graphed. This uses the rod catch data to estimate the spawning stock on a catchment. This is then compared against what we believe is the stock level needed for a healthy population on that catchment. This data influences byelaws for catchments e.g rivers not reaching conservation limit can have 100% catch and release imposed on them. Juvenile salmonid data - Any electro-fishing surveys carried out on a catchment are recorded on a map using the National Fisheries Classification Scheme to highlight the quality of the site. This data is collected due to the National Fisheries Monitoring Programme.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2009-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18427\tNRW_DS119251\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.039Z\t1075\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Water Quality Data Archive held in WISKI (KiWQM)\t© CNC/NRW and various third parties  NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator.  Data owned by NRW or data that forms part of the public register may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. However, NRW does NOT grant permission to re-use or disseminate restricted third party dataset held within the collation. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323531\tWISKI Database - Kister Water Quality Module\tNRW Water Quality Data Analyst: Martin Stephens       In the near future refineries and steelworks data (under PPC regime) will be added. Simon Webb will clearly mark these as third party on KiWQM as these must not be shared with customers; they are classed as commercially confidential/sensitive. Samples provided by welsh water as part of legislative requirements.\tWater quality data is held in the WISKI Kisters Water Quality Module (KiWQM). The Water Quality Archive provides a central repository for all data relating to water quality measurements that have since the 1960\u0027s been collected from over 22,000 sampling points, with 2,500 active today. Samples are taken from coastal or estuarine waters, rivers, lakes, ponds, canals or groundwater. Additional data has been provided by third parties as public record. The data measures a wide range of water quality parameters, such as nutrients, metals and physio-chemical determinants for both archived and processed data and these are used to assess discharge monitoring against discharge permits, investigation of pollution incidents or environmental monitoring. Whilst this data is used to meet the requirements of The Water Framework Directive and Bathing Water Quality assessments, the system is currently unable to record anything about compliance.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18435\tNRW_DS119255\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.248Z\t448\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBottlenose Dolphin Monitoring in Cardigan Bay 2014-2016\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323535\tData is archived as Project 479, Media 1556:  K:\\archive\\Media 1501 -1600\\Media 1556\tCharlie Lindenbaum, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer,  Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tThis dataset contains the results of bottlenose dolphin monitoring work conducted by the Sea Watch Foundation between 2014 and 2016 on behalf of Natural Resources Wales. The semi-resident population of bottlenose dolphins found in Cardigan Bay is one of two major coastal populations in the UK. The species is the primary feature of Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC), and also a qualifying feature of Pen Llŷn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC in northern Cardigan Bay, designated under the EC Habitats Directive. The primary objective of this contract was to monitor the bottlenose dolphin population of Cardigan Bay including both Cardigan Bay SAC and the wider Cardigan Bay area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-12-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T10:33:42.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18425\tNRW_DS119263\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.611Z\t504\tValid\tEditable\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SAC) and Special Protection Areas (SPA) Monitoring Programme Results 2013-2018\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323633\tDMS\tNRW Monitoring and Surveillance Advisor: Daniel Guest. NRW Environmental Surveillance Officer: Dylan Lloyd.\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs) are protected and designated sites under the EC Habitats and Birds Directives. The Directives list habitat types and species that are considered to be most in need of conservation at a European level. An obligation for this is a programme of species and habitat monitoring across the site.   This dataset shows the results of NRWs\u0027 feature condition monitoring programme on the SACs and SPAs in Wales and contains the base data used to populate one of NRWs\u0027 corporate performance indicators. The data is updated from the active condition monitoring programme on an annual basis.    The publication of indicative condition assessments for marine features in Wales in January 2018 supersedes the information for marine features currently in this data layer. The indicative condition assessments are NRWs formal advice on condition of its European marine sites and should be used. Information on the assessments and the reports themselves can be downloaded from the following link:  https://naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/environmental-topics/wildlife-and-biodiversity/find-protected-areas-of-land-and-seas/indicative-feature-condition-assessments-for-european-marine-sites-ems/?lang\u003den  Subsequent updates of the SAC and SPA table may provide updated assessments for a few marine features and as such any marine feature assessment dated after May 2017 should be considered to take precedence.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-05-15\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2645\t2025-05-15T09:31:41.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18420\tNRW_DS119266\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.431Z\t1473\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) Sensitive Area - Shellfish Waters\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323636\tX:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\UWWTR Sensitive Areas Shellfish Waters.lyr\tGeraint Weber - Team Leader Water Quality\tThe Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017 (WFD Regulations 2017) requires specification of protected areas for those areas designated as nutrient sensitive areas.     UWWTR Sensitive Areas Shellfish Waters layer has been renamed from the UWWTD Sensitive Areas Shellfish Waters and updated to only show areas which are designated as Shellfish Waters under the WFD Regulations following Welsh Government review.    This is a spatial dataset showing the extent of the Urban Wastewater Treatment Directive (UWWTD) sensitive areas (Shellfish Water) in Wales.   The Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD) (91/271/EEC) is designed to reduce the pollution of freshwater, estuarine and coastal waters by domestic sewage and industrial wastewater collectively known as urban waste water. These water have been identified as a sensitive because further treatment than secondary or equivalent treatment is necessary to meet other EC Directives such as the Water Framework Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-08-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/5375/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_uwwtr\t2026-01-09T16:51:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18417\tNRW_DS119267\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.447Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSeal Sites in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323637\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seal sites.lyr  X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seal haul out sites.lyr   The above are now considered old data and should be archived in favour of data published by WG on DataMapWales\tTom Stringell       This base layer was created to identify all know seal sites, a derived layer was then created to illustrate just those sites where monitoring was ongoing or that was currently suspended  X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seal Monitoring sites.lyr.  As seals were previously on the CCW sensitive features list the information was then redacted to 5km square resolution and provided to UKDMOS.  This redacted layer was also made available to WG Marine Monitoring Portal  ***to be removed when/if metadata is made publicly available**** This base layer was created to identify all known seal sites, a derived layer was then created to illustrate just those sites where monitoring was ongoing or that was currently suspended. As seals were previously on the CCW sensitive features list the information was then redacted to 5km square resolution and provided to UKDMOS. This redacted layer was also made available to WG Marine Monitoring Portal\tThis dataset shows the location of seal sites in West Wales. The original version was based upon those identified in the West Wales Grey Seal Census 1991-1994 and Grey Seal census for North Wales 2004. See the base reports for further information. This data has subsequently been updated to include sightings in 2006 (see reports listed in the lineage for further information).  The original data was derived from the two reports (from 1995 and 2004 as detailed in the lineage) to meet a need to provide information on the location of seal sites that were included in CCW\u0027s marine monitoring program.  The purpose of this was to provide information to the UKDMOS catalogue.  NRWs collated data has been superseded by a collation undertaken by Welsh Government and published in 2022 on DataMapWales\t\t2021-07-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18418\tNRW_DS119270\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.360Z\t1071\tValid\tEditable\tForest Management Coupes\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323730\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Management Coupes.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan  Secondary Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset shows management coupes. These are areas of intended management and may not be implemented when indicated or at all. The feature description field indicates the probable silvicultural system in use and the year field when that action will take place or be completed by.  A related layer is restocking coupes which show what happens to the area after management has taken place\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-02-29\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3515/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_forest_management_coupes\t2025-11-14T13:44:51.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18424\tNRW_DS119273\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.514Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Bridges\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323733\tX:\\Transport\\Bridges\\NRW Forest Bridges.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan    Secondary Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNational Forest Estate Bridges are managed by Forestry Civil Engineering in one of NRW\u0027s Forester GIS modules. This data set comprises location and category of construction.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-06-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:00:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18422\tNRW_DS119281\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.448Z\t1292\tValid\tEditable\tForest Restock Coupes\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323831\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Restock Coupes.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Restock 2nd rotation.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan Secondary Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset shows restocking coupes. These are areas of intended management and may not be implemented when indicated or at all. The attribution indicates the probable habitat or species and the year field when those will take place or be completed by. A related layer is management coupes which show what management is intended prior to restocking\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-06-30-2024-02-29\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3503/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_forest_restock_coupes\t2025-03-07T15:59:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18419\tNRW_DS119284\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.413Z\t687\tValid\tEditable\tGood for People Toolkit\t© CNC/NRW Raw 3rd Party Data: NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Modelled Outputs: Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323834\tV:\\Good For People GIS Toolkit Documentation: V:\\Good For People GIS Toolkit\\Documentation Project Files: V:\\Good For People GIS Toolkit\\project_files Info also available on the DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/are/commu/sp/Guidance/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?InitialTabId\u003dRibbon%2EDocument\u0026amp;VisibilityContext\u003dWSSTabPersistence\tMetadata / Custodian Contact: Ben Anscombe GIS and Spatial Data Team. KIM have the technical responsibility for the tool.  Ros Owen Recreation, Health and Wellbeing Team Leader  is also a key contact as she worked on gathering organisational requirements.\tThis is not a specific dataset, but rather the Good for People Toolkit is a spatial modelling framework developed to aid the bringing together and combining of data on a modelling platform producing key evidence for use by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).   This metadata entry can be supplied alongside data outputs although more specific metadata may be written if required for specific outputs.   Outputs from the model could be in the form of spreadsheets, spatial layers or maps, but all require some form of interpretation and knowledge for them to be understood by users.     About the Model: In 2014, NRW commissioned AECOM to develop a GIS based toolkit that would help to inform decisions around the use and targeting of NRW resources in order to best deliver priorities and outcomes that are \"Good for People\".   The Good for People GIS Toolkit is a spatial tool designed to help the Good for People programmes and the associated Enabling Plans: Outdoor Access and Recreation; Communities and Regeneration; and Education and Skills.   The toolkit addresses 18 research questions specified by NRW in relation to these plans by allowing data to be viewed in combination. These cover:  Q01 - Deprivation;  Q02 - Physical and Mental Ill-health;  Q03 - Unemployment;  Q04 - Education;  Q05 - Community Facilities;  Q06 - ESTYN (annual inspection report for individual education settings and for educational authorities);  Q07 - NEET and Pre-NEET (Not in Education Employment or Training);  Q08 - Access to Natural Green Space;  Q09 - Urban Tree Canopy Cover;  Q10 - Air Quality;  Q11 - Flooding (criteria not mapped at time);  Q12 - Access to NRW Land;  Q13 - Recreation Provision;  Q14 - Crime and Anti-Social Behaviour;  Q15 - Tourism;  Q16 - Active Travel;  Q17 - Slope;  Q18 - NRW and Partner Managed Land.   The toolkit can be used in a number of different ways. It has been designed to allow users to select the data layers that are most relevant to their purposes. Users can be confident that as an NRW-wide tool all NRW managers will be using the same evidence in a visual format so that plans for different areas can be compared fairly. The Good for People GIS Toolkit should be used where possible to support strategic and operational decision making within NRW in particular to strengthen decisions involving the needs of local communities and the benefits they derive from using and engaging with the natural environment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T13:30:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18421\tNRW_DS119289\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.414Z\t1036\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD) Sewage Treatment Works\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323839\tX:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\UWWTD Sewage Treatment Works.lyr\tMark Squire, Team Leader Water Policy \u0026amp; Directives Implementation\tSpatial dataset containing wastewater treatment plants monitored and reported under the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD) in Wales. These plants serve population equivalent (PE) greater than 2000 if discharging to freshwater or greater than 10000 if discharging to coastal/transitional waters. Details include load, treatment types and performance.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:17:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18416\tNRW_DS119291\t2024-05-28T12:48:34.967Z\t438\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTotal Peatland\t© CNC/NRW and BGS,OS \u0026amp; CU. NRW will only release this data in some circumstances and does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset unless they have a valid data use licence from the originators of the raw data used to derive these products.  Attribution Statement: Mapping derived from 1:50,000 scale, BGS DiGMapGB ©NERC National Soils Map © Cranfield University (NSRI) LC0023/004-3 [2011] Phase 1 habitat mapping, Habitats of Wales © CCW, 100018813 [2011] © Crown copyright and database right [2011] Ordnance Survey Licence number [100025498]\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323931\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\Total Peatland.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset comprises the total peatland for Wales. The peat is categorised as Deep Peaty Soil, Modified Deep Peaty Soil, Shallow Peaty Soil and Soil with Peaty Pockets.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18460\tNRW_DS119292\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.088Z\t309\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPeat Top Ten Sites\t© CNC/NRW and BGS,OS \u0026amp; CU. NRW will only release this data in some circumstances and does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset unless they have a valid data use licence from the originators of the raw data used to derive these products.  Attribution Statement: Mapping derived from 1:50,000 scale, BGS DiGMapGB ©NERC National Soils Map © Cranfield University (NSRI) LC0023/004-3 [2011] Phase 1 habitat mapping, Habitats of Wales © CCW, 100018813 [2011] © Crown copyright and database right [2011] Ordnance Survey Licence number [100025498]\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323932\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\Peat Top Ten Sites.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset comprises the National Assessment top 10 sites for potential peat restoration.  Peatland in pristine or good condition provides a range of critical ecosystem services, including biodiversity, carbon storage and sequestration, regulation of stream base flows, water runoff and downstream flood peaks and nutrient regulation and retention. Peatlands are also sinks and sources of several natural greenhouse gases, particularly carbon dioxide (CO2) and methane (CH4). More than 75% of deep peat soils in Wales are covered in semi-natural vegetation. Most of this is upland blanket bog, with significant amounts of fen and flush and, locally, lowland raised bog. These are all UK Biodiversity Action Plan priority habitats with UK and Welsh targets for habitat management and restoration.   In order to deliver the habitat restoration targets set out in the Welsh Environmental Strategy, there is a need to progressively restore semi-natural habitat in afforested areas. Restoration measures should seek to better integrate the key objectives of nature conservation, soil carbon protection, carbon sequestration and water quality.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:50.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18470\tNRW_DS119295\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.356Z\t890\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Pipelines on NRW Land\tUnredacted Data © CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Redacted Data © CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323935\tX:\\Utilities\\Water\\Water Pipelines on NRW Land.lyr\tGIS Technicians in 4 Forest Districts, National Forest Planning Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains water pipelines on NRW land. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any utility data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured water pipeline information\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:50.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18467\tNRW_DS119296\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.323Z\t581\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUnderground Telephone Lines on Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Land\tUnredacted Data © CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Redacted Data © CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323936\tX:\\Utilities\\Telecoms\\UG Telephone Lines on NRW Land.lyr\tCustodian: GIS Technicians in 4 Forest Districts, National Forest Planning Team Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains Underground Telephone Lines on NRW land. Currently the data covers forestry areas but in future will expand to all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any utility data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured underground telephone line information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:13:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18463\tNRW_DS119297\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.083Z\t318\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOver Head Telephone Lines on Natural Resource Wales (NRW) Land\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Redacted Data  © CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323937\tX:\\Utilities\\Telecoms\\OH Telephone Lines on NRW Land.lyr\tGIS Technicians in 4 Forest Districts, National Forest Planning Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains Overhead Telephone Lines on Natural Resource Wales (NRW) land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any utility data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured overhead telephone line information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T13:30:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18464\tNRW_DS119299\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.196Z\t387\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSection 42 Bryophytes Register\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323939\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Bryophytes S42 points.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tA set of records of bryophytes listed on Section 42 of the NERC Act, localised as accurately as possible and with the most recent record for a site listed rather than a full set of records from each site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-11-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:02:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18468\tNRW_DS119300\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.325Z\t789\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThreatened Bryophytes Species\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333030\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Bryophytes Threatened Species 2015.lyr   The spreadsheet for this dataset is available on the DMS: Evidence \u0026gt; Species \u0026gt; Bryophytes \u0026gt; Bryophyte Statuses https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Information/Bryophyte%20Statuses\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tA set of records of bryophytes listed as threatened in GB and/or Wales, localised as accurately as possible, with records of less than 100m resolution excluded (these are in another layer on Y:).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-11-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18461\tNRW_DS119301\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.090Z\t616\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSection 42 Fungi Register\t© CNC/NRW and © Various Other Organisations For 3rd party owned data requestors are advised to visit the NBN Atlas and / or contact the originators directly.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. For NRW Owned Data. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333031\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Fungi S42 Inventory 2014.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Fungi S42 point.lyr   These excluded records are on the Y Drive  The spatial data have been created from this excel document: Evidence \u0026gt; Species \u0026gt; Fungi \u0026gt; Fungus Information \u0026gt; Fungus Statuses  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/fun/Fungus%20Information/Fungus%20Statuses/Section%2042%20draft%20sites%20inventory%20fungi%20ex%20UWA.XLS\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tA set of records of fungi listed on Section 42 of the NERC Act, localised as accurately as possible, with imprecise records excluded.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-11-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:34:58.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18462\tNRW_DS119302\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.190Z\t435\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSection 42 Lichens Register\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333032\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Lichens S42 Inventory 2014.lyr   Excluded records these are on another layer on Y:\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tA set of records of lichens listed on Section 42 of the NERC Act, localised as accurately as possible, with imprecise records excluded.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-11-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18474\tNRW_DS119303\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.389Z\t475\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThreatened Lichen Species\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333033\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Lichens Threatened Species 2015.lyr   The excel spreadsheet associated with this file is saved in the following location on the DMS: Evidence \u0026gt; Species \u0026gt; Lichens \u0026gt; Lichen Information \u0026gt; Lichen Statuses \u0026gt; Wales threatened lichens 2015 all records https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Information/Lichen%20Statuses/Wales%20Threatened%20Lichens%202015%20all%20records.xlsx\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tA set of records of lichens listed as threatened in GB and/or Wales, localised as accurately as possible, with records of less than 100m resolution excluded.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-11-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:46.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18475\tNRW_DS119362\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.490Z\t621\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPowys Intensive Livestock Units 2004 2017\t© CNC/NRW  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333632\tX:\\Physical\\Agricultural\\Powys intensive livestock units 2004 2017.lyr\tRachel Jarvis - conservation officer in the Radnorshire NRM team\tA comprehensive dataset of the locations of intensive livestock units (poultry and pigs) in Powys, operational since 2004.  This is an ongoing, working dataset and will be updated periodically to reflect any new developments across Powys. For internal staff use only.  See also Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2008 2017 - NRW_DS119363 and Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2017 only - NRW_DS119364.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-11-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:47:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18466\tNRW_DS119363\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.324Z\t377\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPowys Intensive Livestock Units 2008 2017\t© CNC/NRW  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333633\tX:\\Physical\\Agricultural\\Powys intensive livestock units 2008 2017.lyr\tRachel Jarvis - conservation officer in the Radnorshire NRM team\tA comprehensive dataset of the locations of intensive livestock units (poultry and pigs) in Powys, operational since 2004.  This is an ongoing, working dataset and will be updated periodically to reflect any new developments across Powys. For internal staff use only.  See also Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2004 2017 - NRW_DS119362 and Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2017 only - NRW_DS119364.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-11-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:47:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18472\tNRW_DS119364\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.388Z\t522\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPowys Intensive Livestock Units 2017 only\t© CNC/NRW  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333634\tX:\\Physical\\Agricultural\\Powys intensive livestock units 2017 onwards.lyr\tRachel Jarvis - conservation officer in the Radnorshire NRM team\tA comprehensive dataset of the locations of intensive livestock units (poultry and pigs) in Powys, operational since 2004.  This is an ongoing, working dataset and will be updated periodically to reflect any new developments across Powys. For internal staff use only.  See also Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2004 2017 - NRW_DS119362 and Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2008 2017 - NRW_DS119363.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-07-02\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:47:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18469\tNRW_DS119974\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.200Z\t574\tValid\tEditable\tForest Priority Catchments 2016\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139393734\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW Forestry Priority Catchments 2016.lyr\tCustodian: Dave Johnston, Team Leader Environmental Reporting Metadata Point of Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Services Team\tThe dataset comprises a number of river catchments based on Water Framework Directive water body boundaries which have been identified by NRW as areas within which forestry environmental improvement works will be prioritised to reduce the impact of forestry on the water environment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-08-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3484/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_forest_priority_catchments_2016\t2025-03-07T15:58:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18473\tNRW_DS120006\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.321Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tKiWQM Sampling Stations\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230303036\tData is stored in WISKI New layer on GIS X:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\KiWQM NRW Stations.lyr\tData custodian is Martin Stephens  Simon Webb, Stuart Kay       OLLY - i can confirm that this relates to X:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\KiWQM NRW Stations.lyr GARY needs to confirm if this entry, it didn\u0027t seem to be related to a specific dataset, but potentially does now.  X:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\KiWQM NRW Stations.lyr???? MJ  - I don\u0027t think the the access and use is correct in this entry as only third party data may be restricted, should look at the access and use constraint statements for the KiWQM overarching dataset  - GIS team to check\tThe KiWQM Sampling Stations GIS layer provides a route of discovery for the spatial element of all data relating to water quality measurements that have taken place in and around Wales. It is a record of locations where water sampling has taken place.  Formerly this data was held as 2 layers; Open stations (Metadata # 120746) and Closed stations (Metadata # 120747). Information for sampling locations for stations in England are documented in a separate metadata entry: 120732 (Environment Agency (EA): KiWQM English Stations (3rd Party Data))\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-03-03\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18459\tNRW_DS120044\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.909Z\t457\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCondition assessment of the Glutinous Snail Myxas glutinosa in Llyn Tegid in 2014\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230303434\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive  Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database - Recorder 6\tCustodian: Mike Howe, invertebrate ecologist NRW.  External Contact / Originator - was put here because it looks as if this might be his home address as well - therefore in Responsible Organisation Martin Willing is C/O NRW. Dr Martin J Willing 14 Goodwood Close MIDHURST West Sussex GU29 9JG\tAs part of the Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Common Standards Monitoring programme, this report documents the results of monitoring for the Glutinous Snail Myxas glutinosa during 2014 at Llyn Tegid using the Conservation Objective produced for this snail (Willing, 2004, 2006, 2010). Llyn Tegid was visited in late October 2014 and, over two days, eight survey-stations situated around the lake were monitored (those used on nine previous occasions since 1999) for the presence of the snail (and associated molluscs) using the sampling procedures as detailed in the Conservation Objective. M. glutinosa was found at all 7 sites where it had previously been recorded in numbers that, using Condition Assessment standards, allows the status of the snail to be judged as in a Favourable Condition in the lake in 2014. The population has been in Favourable Condition since standardised monitoring first took place in 2002. The habitat at all of the sites was also judged to be in ‘good condition’ in 2014. Results from surveys undertaken since April 2000 are also included in the report. Following status monitoring work a live sample of M. glutinosa was taken under licence from NRW from the lake survey to allow captive observation of the snails to be undertaken and to investigate the possibility of captive breeding of the snail. Initial observation of the captive Myxas has revealed that the snails emerge from beneath stones in darkness and feed upon encrusting algae both on stones and glass tank sides. Myxas bred in captivity at the end of the experiment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:56.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18458\tNRW_DS120063\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.849Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNorth West Anglesey Modiolus – Drop Down Video Survey Sept 2009 \u0026amp; 2010\t© CCGC/CCW 2010  You may reproduce this dataset free of charge for non-commercial and internal business purposes in any format or medium, provided that you do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and CCW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. To avoid using old information, we recommend that you obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230303633\t[A] The data is stored in the marine archive  as project 268, media 965-979 (videos), media 1307 \u0026amp; 1308 (CMACS video and image grabs), 1309 (analysis and outputs). File path \u003d K:\\Archive\\ Media 900 -1000\\Media 965 etc. etc. [B] The report is still in draft but a copy is in the DMS  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/proj/_layouts/15/DocIdRedir.aspx?ID\u003dEVID-201-24 [C] Marine Recorder surveys : [see MRCCW31900000002 and JNCC survey MRCON02100000003 in the snapshot only)\tInternal Contact: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team        Distributor contact details: Miss Liz Morris Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd, 41 High Street, Menai Bridge, Anglesey, LL59 5EF 07868 705017 or 01248 712277 liz@marine-ecosol.com www.marine-ecosol.com  Analysis was undertaken on the following CCW-held video files: 20090925Tape1NWAngleseyModiolusSurface.avi 20090925Tape2NWAngleseyModiolusSurface.avi 20090928Tape3NWAngleseyModiolusSurface.avi  Data supplied by the contractor, Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd. are: 2 Modiolus_CCW_Metadata.xls \u003d the metadata taken on the day of survey, together with summary details of ALL habitat and subsection divisions 3 Modiolus Data Entry.xls \u003d ALL raw results / data scored by the contractor. Columns highlighted in red are definite Modiolus reef; highlighted in cream are areas of potential reef; text in red are QA duplicate scores 4 Summary of NW Anglesey Modiolus Reef only Sept09.xls \u003d this excel table summarises the areas of confirmed and potential Modiolus reef only.\tThe area to the North and West of the Isle of Anglesey (North Wales, UK) is one where horse mussel Modiolus modiolus beds are thought to occur. In the past, several surveys have recorded aggregations of Modiolus in this area but in general the density and extent of Modiolus has not been accurately mapped. Modiolus beds are important because they are part of the Habitats Directive Annex I Reef habitat, are on the OSPAR list of threatened and declining habitats and are a Biodiversity Action Plan habitat. Modiolus beds appear to be extremely sensitive to physical disturbance and are thought to have declined in extent and quality. A collaborative project between the Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) and the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) (now Natural Resources Wales) began in 2009 to investigate the possible presence of Modiolus modiolus beds and other reef habitat at two locations to the north and west of Anglesey. These locations were chosen based on historical evidence of the presence of the horse mussel Modiolus modiolus. Area NWA1-North is between 32 and 38km north of Holyhead and has an area of almost 14km2. It is characterised by a deep trench running ENE through the study area, which is approximately 105m at its deepest point, which shallows towards the north and south to depths of approximately 45 and 60m respectively. Area NWA2-West is located between 23 and 28km WNW of Holyhead and is almost 20km2 in area. The shallowest point is in the SE of the study area where there is a plateau at approximately 50m depth, which then drops off towards the NW to the base of a slope at approximately 90m deep.   The two areas were surveyed using a Multibeam Echosounder and drop down video. The Centre for Environment, Fisheries and Aquaculture Science (CEFAS) carried out a Multibeam Echosounder survey in July 2009 and the resulting data was interpreted for bedrock, quaternary bedforms, sediments and habitat features by CEFAS. Four sites in water deeper than 100m were surveyed using a stills camera by the Centre for Marine and Coastal Studies in June 2010. CCW then surveyed selected areas of interest using a drop down video during September 2009 and January 2010. The video footage was collected to identify Annex 1 Horse Mussel (Modiolus) reef habitat by Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd, who developed methods by which to score areas of potential reef to confirm presence of live Modiolus reef, and where possible quantify live Modiolus. 9 video transects were analysed. First they were split into rough sections of different habitat. Areas of potential Modiolus reef were identified, scored for Modiolus and Alcyonium (a known proxy for live Modiolus in the right habitat), and then JNCC’s Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) style habitat surveys were conducted. MNCR style habitat surveys were also undertaken on sections of habitat which were either flat, unconsolidated reefs of dead Modiolus shell, or habitats where no Modiolus were present. All habitats longer than 1 minute in length were further divided into 1 minute subsections (to keep the surveyors’ attention and increase the accuracy of scoring large numbers of Alcyonium).    In total 158 subsections of habitat were analysed (92 scored for Modiolus; 66 subsections for MNCR phase 2 style habitat survey only). 2 areas of certain live Modiolus reef were identified and confirmed, whilst another 7 subsections of habitat showed signs of live Modiolus that may be within areas of potential reef.  This could be due to the ‘banded’ habitats identified, where unconsolidated Modiolus shell gravel, embedded lag cobbles and pebbles, and live Modiolus reef were identified in ‘bands’ across a possibly gradually undulating seabed. 10% of the one minute sections were resurveyed by different surveyors for Quality Assurance (QA of counts and MNCR). As much of the video scoring could be considered ‘semi-quantitative’ and partially subjective, QA did not aim to fi ...\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-06-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:32.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18471\tNRW_DS120116\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.307Z\t628\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Lamprey Species Survey 2013 – 2015\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230313136\tKiECO database (WISKI)  Some data is also available on the DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/fm/Running%20Waters%20Habitats%20Directive%20Data%20Cycle%203/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Ffm%2FRunning%20Waters%20Habitats%20Directive%20Data%20Cycle%203%2FWye%20Lamprey\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000EE02D3CE2ED1AE4085983FEDF88CC2E5\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B90B85581%2DA33A%2D4F0D%2DB9D4%2DBCB28FC39C08%7D\tSophie Gott, Fisheries Technical Specialist, NRW Heather Garrett, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, NRW\tRiver Wye SAC lamprey species population condition assessment using revised Common Standards Monitoring attribute targets (JNCC, 2015). Analysis of lamprey larvae surveys conducted using electrofishing technique and an analysis of population spatial extent, density and age structure for the sea lamprey (Petromyzon marinus), river lamprey (Lampetra fluviatilis) \u0026amp; brook lamprey (Lampetra planeri).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:46.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18465\tNRW_DS120146\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.289Z\t551\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Priority Catchments 2013\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230313436\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\NRW Forestry Priority Catchments 2013.lyr\tCustodian: Dave Johnston, Team Leader Environmental Reporting Metadata Point of Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Services Team\tThe dataset comprises a number of river catchments based on Water Framework Directive water body boundaries which have been identified by NRW as areas within which forestry environmental improvement works will be prioritised to reduce the impact of forestry on the water environment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18456\tNRW_DS120294\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.482Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBadger Licences\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230323934\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Species and habitat\\Badger licences.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Iwan G. Hughes, Team Leader, Species \u0026amp; Carbon Permitting\tThe dataset includes details of Badger licences issued from 2002 to date.  Information includes the licence number, applicant, location, activity and dates of validity.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-06-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18457\tNRW_DS120494\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.799Z\t1134\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGroundwater Vulnerability (2018)\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. In some cases the dataset maybe made available for non-commercial purposes . For commercial use please contact the British Geological Survey.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230343934\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Simplified Groundwater Vulnerability.lyr  X:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Combined Groundwater Vulnerability.lyr   Associated layerfiles with this metadata are: Groundwater Vulnerability Groundwater Vulnerability Local Information Groundwater Vulnerability Solid Rock Risk\tDave Jones\tGroundwater vulnerability maps reflect improvements in data mapping and understanding of the factors affecting vulnerability. The maps show the vulnerability of groundwater at a location based on the hydrological, geological, hydrogeological and soil properties within a 1 kilometre square grid. The maps provide a separate assessment of the vulnerability of groundwater in overlying \u0027superficial\u0027, rocks, and those that comprise the underlying \u0027bedrock\u0027. The main aim of the maps is as a high level screening tool to give NRW, planners, consultants and other users an indication of whether a proposed development or activity is likely to be acceptable (e.g. located in an area of low vulnerability or over unproductive strata) or of potential concern (e.g. located in an area of high vulnerability). The maps can also be used to inform and target environmental management and incident response so that preventative and/or remedial actions can be taken as early as possible to protect groundwater.  Two main rock types are recognised: - Superficial (drift): permeable unconsolidated (loose) deposits, such as sands and gravels.  - Bedrock: solid permeable formations such as sandstone, chalk and limestone.  The vulnerability of these two rock types may differ due to their structure and location. Where both types are present, the maps display the most vulnerable category of the two.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-British Geological Survey (BGS)\t2021-07-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18454\tNRW_DS120507\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.228Z\t1138\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) A Felling Plan\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230353037\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA A Felling Plan.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of felling proposed by the Developer for the wind farm development. As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:19:58.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18449\tNRW_DS120730\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.086Z\t668\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) F Felling Plan\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230373330\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA F Felling Plan.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of felling proposed by the Developer for the wind farm development.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:20:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18452\tNRW_DS120746\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.164Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tKiWQM Open Stations\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230373436\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\KiWQM NRW Open Stations.lyr  Source data is stored in WISKI\tSimon Webb, Stuart Kay - now Martin Stephens?       MJ  - I don\u0027t think the the access and use is correct in this entry as only third party data may be restricted, should look at the access and use constraint statements for the KiWQM overarching dataset  - GIS team to check\tThe KiWQM Sampling Stations GIS layer provides a route of discovery for the spatial element of all data relating to water quality measurements that have taken place in and around Wales. It is a record of locations where water sampling has taken place.  ***Replaced by metadata entry 120006**** which has created a combined open and closed station dataset\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-01-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18455\tNRW_DS120747\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.208Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tKiWQM Closed Stations\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230373437\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\KiWQM NRW Closed Stations.lyr  Source data is stored in WISKI\tSimon Webb, Stuart Kay       MJ  - I don\u0027t think the the access and use is correct in this entry as only third party data may be restricted, should look at the access and use constraint statements for the KiWQM overarching dataset  - GIS team to check\tThe KiWQM Sampling Stations GIS layer provides a route of discovery for the spatial element of all data relating to water quality measurements that have taken place in and around Wales. It is a record of locations where water sampling has taken place.  ***Replaced by metadata entry 120006**** which has created a combined open and closed station dataset\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-01-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18453\tNRW_DS121120\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.203Z\t406\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring Invertebrate features on Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs): assessing and reviewing the status of the troglobitic spider Porrhomma rosenhaueri in Ogof y Ci and Lesser Garth Cave, South Wales in 2017\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231313230\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tData Custodian: Mike Howe Invertebrate Ecologist Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tThis dataset was collected to assess whether Porrhomma rosenhaueri was still present in the Ogof y Ci cave system and if so, to consider the viability of establishing a Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) protocol for monitoring the population within the cave; and to revisit the CSM established for the Lesser Garth Cave to collate further data on the condition of both the Porrhomma rosenhaueri population and the ecological condition of the cave.     The cave-dwelling spider Porrhomma rosenhaueri (L. Koch) is unique to the British fauna as it is considered to be the only species of paleotroglobitic spider present, and whilst the cavernicolous fauna of the UK has undoubtedly been under-recorded, sufficient work has occurred to suggest that Porrhomma rosenhaueri is genuinely rare.The spider has a very limited distribution in the UK and is only known from two sites in South Wales, Ogof y Ci near Merthyr Tydfil and Lesser Garth Cave near Cardiff.   The Ogof y Ci population was first discovered in 1971 but since this period no recorded survey work on the spider has taken place and the status of the spider within the cave was unknown. The Lesser Garth Cave population was discovered in 1979 during an investigation of the cave for the cave dwelling isopod Proasellus cavaticus. The cave has since been surveyed for the spider on a number of occasions, initially in 1997 as part of an environmental impact assessment, and more recently in 2009 in order to establish a Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) protocol for the longer-term monitoring of the spider population in the cave.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T09:32:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18451\tNRW_DS121121\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.095Z\t369\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status and distribution of the ground beetle Harpalus melancholicus at Stackpole Warren in 2017\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231313231\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database (Recorder 6)\tData Custodian: Mike Howe Invertebrate Ecologist\tStackpole Warren supports the only known British population of the Endangered ground beetle Harpalus melancholicus, the Stackpole Seed-eater. This data collection was commissioned to attempt to determine its current status and distribution on Stackpole Warren.    This survey has done much to characterise the habitat occupied by H. melancholicus, with adults recorded only from two small patches of habitat where the toe of a vegetated sand-dune encroaches over outcropping limestone. The survey has also confirmed that H. melancholicus is a seed-eater, with Lesser Hawkbit Leontodon saxatilis and probably thyme Thymus sp. as food items. It is hypothesised that H. melancholicus is ecologically highly specialised, with a patchy occurrence at Stackpole Warren, but that additional occupied habitat patches remain to be discovered. Alternative hypotheses are also presented.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T09:30:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18450\tNRW_DS121231\t2024-05-28T12:49:34.919Z\t935\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales\t© CNC/NRW. Each of the individual layers contains derived data.   Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution Statement: Please see metadata for each individual data layer for appropriate third party acknowledgement.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323331\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tThe project created a toolbox of mapped data and methods which enable operational staff in Wales to identify potential locations for Working with Natural Processes (WWNP).    Data has been produced for each intervention covered by the project. The final outputs include the following datasets:    • Floodplain Woodland Planting Potential:   • Riparian Woodland Planting Potential   • Wider Catchment Woodland  • Floodplain Reconnection Potential   • Runoff Attenuation Features 3.3% AEP   • Runoff Attenuation Features 1% AEP  • Woodland Constraints\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-03-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18448\tNRW_DS121238\t2024-05-28T12:49:34.832Z\t860\tValid\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Floodplain Reconnection Potential\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323338\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Floodplain Reconnection Potential .lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) Floodplain Reconnection Potential is our best estimate of locations where it may be possible to establish reconnection between a watercourse and its natural floodplain, especially during high flows. The dataset is designed to support signposting of areas where there is currently poor connectivity such that flood waters are constrained to the channel and flood waves may therefore propagate downstream rapidly.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-03-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/510/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WWNP_FLOODPLAIN_WOODLAND_POTENTIAL\t2025-05-02T15:08:21.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18447\tNRW_DS121241\t2024-05-28T12:49:34.811Z\t952\tValid\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Floodplain Woodland Planting Potential\t© CNC/NRW and Environment Agency Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution Statement: © Environment Agency and © Natural Resources Wales copyright and database rights. All rights reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323431\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Floodplain Woodland Planting Potential.lyr X:\\Resource Management\\Flood Mitigation Woodland Opportunities\\EA Potential Floodplain Woodland.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) Floodplain Woodland Planting Potential is our best estimate of locations where tree planting on the floodplain may be possible, and effective to attenuate flooding.   The dataset is designed to support signposting of areas of floodplain not already wooded. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) also has access to some Environment Agency data which we are licenced to have as part of our data exchange agreement.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Environment Agency (EA)\t2018-03-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/510/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WWNP_FLOODPLAIN_WOODLAND_POTENTIAL\t2025-03-07T16:51:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18446\tNRW_DS121294\t2024-05-28T12:49:34.763Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEcological Surveys of Welsh Lakes 2017\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323934\tAsk Tristan Hatton Ellis, or see report as alot of the data is in there: Ecological Surveys of Welsh Lakes 2017 NRW Evidence Report No. 257\tTristan Hatton Ellis\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) is repsonsible for the management and monitoring of the freshwater environment in Wales including protecting sites designated under UK and European legislation and enivronmental montoring for the Water Framework (WFD) and Nitrates Directive. This includes montioring of lakes. This project set out to collect, process and supply to NRW ecological and environmental data from a network of 16 lake sites across Wales, in support of NRW\u0027s integrated monitoring programme for protected sites (Special Area of Conservation and Sites of Special Scientific Interest), the Water Framework Directive, Nitrates Directive, Biodiversity Action Plans and other legislative and policy drivers. In particular, the surveys are aimed at informing management and restoration of protected sites and facilitating delivery of River Basin Management Plans.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18502\tNRW_DS121295\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.259Z\t295\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2017\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323935\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tData Custodian: Mike Howe\tThis dataset was collected to add to our current knowledge of the saproxylic invertebrate fauna found across the Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Despite the designation remarkably little is known about the saproxylic invertebrate fauna of the SAC. It is known to support significant populations of a number of nationally rare and threatened species, but until now only limited investigation of the fauna had been carried out.   The two outstanding species known from the SAC are the lime bark beetle Ernoporus tiliae and Cosnard\u0027s net-winged beetle Erotides cosnardi, both Species of Principal Importance in England but not included on Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2016.     These works will be the first phase of an intended larger body of work planned for the coming years aimed at producing a comprehensive inventory of invertebrate interest across the Wye Valley Woodlands. This inventory in the longer term will aid the process of drawing up management guidelines as a basis for the sustainable management of the woodlands within the Wye Valley Woodlands SAC and potentially the wider landscape.   These preliminary surveys have demonstrated the great potential interest of the Wye Valley Woodlands SAC for saproxylic invertebrates and provide ample justification for continued survey, as resources permit. They suggest that it is the extent of the woodland in the SAC that is a key factor, although individual areas may have more localised special interest for particular rare and threatened species. The importance of veteran trees is emphasised as well as the value of individual open-grown trees. These are significant as they suggest a modified approach to saproxylic conservation within the SAC.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:07:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18500\tNRW_DS121298\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.352Z\t330\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status and distribution of the ground beetle Leistus montanus in 2017 at its historic sites in Wales.\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323938\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tData Custodian: Mike Howe  Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tThe aim of the 2017 data collection was to determine the current status and distribution of Leistus montanus in Wales by visiting all known locations and searching under stones on scree slopes. Once regularly recorded in the montane areas of Scotland and the Lake District, recent records of the IUCN Endangered ground beetle Leistus montanus, a species associated with high altitude scree, are confined to the Langdale Pikes in 2008 and Skiddaw in 2016, both in Cumbria. In Wales, it was historically recorded on Cadair Idris in 1829 and 1968, from Y Lliwedd on Snowdon (Yr Wyddfa) in 1965 and 1968, and from Carnedd y Ddelw on the Carneddau in 1971. The only recent record was thought to be from Eglwyseg Rocks in 2007.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T10:03:34.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18499\tNRW_DS121347\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.340Z\t599\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Development Boundary\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333437\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy Development Boundary.lyr\t\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of the proposed windfarm development area as defined by the Developer. As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:47:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18503\tNRW_DS121357\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.183Z\t468\tValid\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Riparian Woodland Potential\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333537\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Riparian Woodland Planting Potential.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) Riparian Woodland Potential is our best estimate of locations where tree planting may be possible on smaller floodplains close to flow pathways, and effective to attenuate flooding. The dataset is designed to support signposting of riparian areas not already wooded. The dataset is based upon a 50m buffer of available OS Open Data river networks.   A set of open access constraints data was used to erase areas which contained existing woodland, watercourses, peat, roads, rail and urban locations.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-03-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/509/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WWNP_RIPERIAN_WOODLAND_POTENTIAL\t2025-03-07T16:51:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18501\tNRW_DS121359\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.326Z\t425\tValid\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Woodland Constraints\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333539\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Natural Processes woodland.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tThis dataset comprises a range of land cover types where the woodland potential layers have been masked, owing to existing woodland, watercourses, peat, roads, rail and urban areas. Other constraints data, such as protected habitats and high grade agricultural land, should also be considered.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-06-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3581/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wwnp_woodland_constraints\t2025-05-14T08:23:00.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18494\tNRW_DS121361\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.263Z\t430\tValid\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Wider Catchment Woodland Potential\t© CNC/NRW. Derived in part from data provided under licence by the British Geological Survey © NERC Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333631\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Wider Catchment Woodland Potential.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) Wider Catchment Woodland Potential is our best estimate of locations where there are slowly permeable soils, where scrub and tree planting may be most effective to increase infiltration and hydrological losses. The dataset is designed to support signposting of areas not already wooded.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-03-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/508/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WWNP_WIDER_CATCHMENT_POTENTIAL\t2025-05-02T15:16:23.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18504\tNRW_DS121362\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.242Z\t614\tValid\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Runoff Attenuation Features 1% AEP\t© CNC/NRW. Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology © NERC (CEH). Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333632\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Runoff Attenuation Features 1% AEP.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tRunoff Attenuation Features Potential is our best estimate of locations of high flow accumulation across the land surface or in smaller channels, where it may be possible to temporarily store water and attenuate flooding during high flows. The dataset is designed to support signposting of areas where to target enhanced storage. It is based upon the Risk of Flooding from Surface Water datasets and identifies areas of high flow accumulations for the 1% Annual Exceedance Probability surface water maps. The areas of ponding or accumulation are between 100 and 5000 metres squared, and have been tagged where they fall on an area of slope steeper than 6% as gully blocking opportunities. All the potential areas have been constrained so that they are not in urban areas or on roads, rails or canals.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-03-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/507/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WWWP_RUNOFF_ANTEN_1PC\t2025-05-08T07:47:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18497\tNRW_DS121363\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.305Z\t665\tValid\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Runoff Attenuation Features 3.3% AEP\t© CNC/NRW. Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology © NERC (CEH). Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333633\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Runoff Attenuation Features 3% AEP.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tRunoff Attenuation Features Potential is our best estimate of locations of high flow accumulation across the land surface or in smaller channels, where it may be possible to temporarily store water and attenuate flooding during high flows.  The dataset is designed to support signposting of areas to target enhanced storage. It is based upon the Risk of Flooding from Surface Water datasets and identifies areas of high flow accumulations for the 3.3% Annual Exceedance Probability surface water maps. The areas of ponding or accumulation are between 100 and 5000 metres squared, and have been tagged where they fall on an area of slope steeper than 6% as gully blocking opportunities.   All the potential areas have been constrained so that they are not in urban areas or on roads, rails or canals.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-03-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/506/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WWWP_RUNOFF_ANTEN_3PC\t2025-05-07T08:50:04.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18505\tNRW_DS121391\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.350Z\t412\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessing Sensitivity of Welsh Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs) Features to Unregulated and Partially Regulated Activities\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333931\tDMS Evidence\u0026gt;Marine\u0026gt;Projects\u0026gt;Contracts\u0026gt;Assessing sensitivities of Welsh MPA features to unregulated activities\tInternal Data Custodian: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor -Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team   Metadata Contact: Data Services Team        Extra info from Laura Grant: There are a couple of reasons why we don’t want to let the public to get hold of this data at the moment. It is part of an interim project and it needs further work before publication.  If the spreadsheets were released now then they could be taken out of context.  Some of the spreadsheets contain data supplied by Natural England.  NE state ‘: We are currently exploring charging for certain advice on operations products, particularly where we have used contracts to conduct some of the assessments as a way of recouping some of this cost. With this in mind, it is very important that this information is not shared any wider than yourselves.  Some of the spreadsheets contain data supplied by MarLIN.  There is ongoing discussion as to who owns the data and MarLIN currently don’t want the information released in the form it appears in these spreadsheets.  DO NOT SET FOR EXPORT.\tThis dataset is associated with the outputs from \u0027Assessing Sensitivity of Welsh Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Special Protection Areas (SPA) Features to Unregulated and Partially Regulated Activities\u0027 contract which is a pilot project part of a wider programme of work. The data outputs are a series of spreadsheets which accompany a report and are split to mirror the outputs of 3 Work Packages.  The data which constitutes these spreadsheets is composed of work by consultants ABPmer alongside existing data obtained from a Natural England led contract and the Marine Biological Association. WP1_Activity_Pressure matrix_070318: Contains definitions of activities used in the study, whether there is evidence to support a link between each activity and pressure, whether the pressures associated with an activity and a level that needs to be considered further, a review of the current pressure benchmarks and an assessment of the activity-pressure against the benchmarks to determine the likelihood of an activity exceeding the benchmark pressure. WP2_Benthic_Activity_Pressure_Feature_070318: Contains the pressure-feature relationship for each activity (for biotopes) and the accompanying sensitivity scores. WP2_Benthic_Sensitivity_Summary_070318: Gives the maximum sensitivity of the activity - feature (biotope) combination. WP2_Mobile_Activity_Pressure_Feature_070318: Contains the pressure-feature relationship for each activity (for mobile species) and the accompanying sensitivity scores. WP2_Mobile_Sensitivity_Summary_070318: Gives the maximum sensitivity of the activity - feature (mobile species) combination. WP3_All_Sites_Features_Max_Sensitivity_070318: Contains the sensitivity scores for each activity vs annex 1 habitat / mobile species (within each relevant Welsh SAC /SPA)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-03-16\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18498\tNRW_DS121392\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.327Z\t627\tValid\tEditable\tWoodland Grant Scheme\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333932\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 1.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 2.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 3.lyr\tKeith Harri Jones- Spatial team Leader\tThe Woodland Grant Scheme (WGS) provided incentives for people to create and manage woodlands on sites all over Wales. The scheme was run in 3 phases with the last phase ending in 2006.   Woodland Grant Scheme 1 operated between April 1989 and April 1992.  Woodland Grant Scheme 2 operated between April 1992 and September 1994 Woodland Grant Scheme 3 operated between September 1994 and April 2006  Spatial data has been digitised against OS raster backdrops  by the former Forestry Commission wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:WoodlandGrantScheme\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:WoodlandGrantScheme\t2025-03-04T08:46:29.431Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18496\tNRW_DS121398\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.252Z\t1307\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Coastal Flood Forecasting (UKCFF) Tide Gauge Network\t© Natural Resources Wales (NRW), Environment Agency (EA) Scottish Environment Protection Agency (SEPA) and Department for Agriculture and Rivers Agency Northern Ireland (RANI). Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333938\tAlthough NRW is supplied the whole dataset, the Mumbles head data is the only data held in WISKI 7 Hydro and telemetry.\tAndy Wall Alison Hanson Liam Perrott, Technical Specialist Hydrometry       Not to publish to data.gov.uk\tThe UK Coastal Flood Forecasting (UKCFF) is a partnership formed by the Environment Agency (EA), Scottish Environment Protection Agency (SEPA) Department for Agriculture and Rivers Agency Northern Ireland (RAN) and Natural Resources Wales (NRW). Through the partnership the authorities commission and deliver the capability to monitoring and forecasting of coastal conditions that underpin coastal flood forecasting and flood risk modelling throughout the UK.   The network includes 43 gauges most of which are related through the national levelling network to Ordnance Datum Newlyn. The whole network is owned by the Environment Agency who operates the network on behalf of the UKCFF partners. Data are collected, processed and archived centrally to provide long time series of reliable and accurate sea levels. The British Oceanographic Data Centre (BODC) act as Data Archive Centre for this data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Scottish Environment Protection Agency (SEPA)-Environment Agency (EA)-Department of Agriculture, Environment and Rural Affairs\t2018-03-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:56.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18491\tNRW_DS121508\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.935Z\t309\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive Cycle 2Target Catchments Rivers\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353038\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Target Rivers Catchments Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tRhian Thomas, Water Framework Directive Team Leader, Water Team\tDataset shows the targeted waterbodies in cycle 2 (2015-2021) for the Water Framework Directive.  These are the waterbodies that NRW has committed to improve a WFD boundary class for this second reporting cycle.  The data is in 4 layers;  Target Catchments Rivers - NRW_DS121508 – this is the targeted river waterbodies catchments Target Waterbodies Groundwater - NRW_DS121509 – this is the targeted groundwater bodies Target Waterbodies Rivers - NRW_DS121510 – this is the targeted rivers as a line dataset Target Waterbodies Lakes - NRW_DS121511 – this is the targeted lakes waterbodies\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18495\tNRW_DS121509\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.255Z\t349\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive Cycle 2 - Target Waterbodies Groundwater\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353039\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Target Groundwater Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tRhian Thomas, Water Framework Directive Team Leader, Water Team\tDataset shows the targeted waterbodies in cycle 2 (2015-2021) for the Water Framework Directive.  These are the waterbodies that NRW has committed to improve a WFD boundary class for this second reporting cycle.  The data is in 4 layers; Target Catchments Rivers - NRW_DS121508 – this is the targeted river waterbodies catchments Target Waterbodies Groundwater - NRW_DS121509 – this is the targeted groundwater bodies Target Waterbodies Rivers - NRW_DS121510 – this is the targeted rivers as a line dataset Target Waterbodies Lakes - NRW_DS121511 – this is the targeted lakes wabter bodies\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:05.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18493\tNRW_DS121510\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.037Z\t369\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive Cycle 2 - Target Waterbodies Rivers\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353130\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Target River Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tRhian Thomas, Water Framework Directive Team Leader, Water Team\tDataset shows the targeted waterbodies in cycle 2 (2015-2021) for the Water Framework Directive.  These are the waterbodies that NRW has committed to improve a WFD boundary class for this second reporting cycle.  The data is in 4 layers; Target Catchments Rivers - NRW_DS121508 – this is the targeted river waterbodies catchments Target Waterbodies Groundwater - NRW_DS121509 – this is the targeted groundwater bodies Target Waterbodies Rivers - NRW_DS121510 – this is the targeted rivers as a line dataset Target Waterbodies Lakes - NRW_DS121511 – this is the targeted lakes wabter bodies\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18492\tNRW_DS121511\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.044Z\t276\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive - Cycle 2 Target Waterbodies Lakes\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353131\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Target Lakes Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tRhian Thomas, Water Framework Directive Team Leader, Water Team\tDataset shows the targeted waterbodies in cycle 2 (2015-2021) for the Water Framework Directive.  These are the waterbodies that NRW has committed to improve a WFD boundary class for this second reporting cycle.  The data is in 4 layers; Target Catchments Rivers - NRW_DS121508 – this is the targeted river waterbodies catchments Target Waterbodies Groundwater - NRW_DS121509 – this is the targeted groundwater bodies Target Waterbodies Rivers - NRW_DS121510 – this is the targeted rivers as a line dataset Target Waterbodies Lakes - NRW_DS121511 – this is the targeted lakes wabter bodies\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18489\tNRW_DS121548\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.905Z\t608\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveys for Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch National Nature Reserve (NNR) / Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and the Afon Penrhos floodplain and for Geyer\u0027s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Cors Geirch NNR in 2017\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353438\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tMetadata Contact: Data Services  Internal Custodian: Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist\tA survey was undertaken in October 2017 to determine the eastern distribution limits of Desmoulin\u0027s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on the Afon Penrhos floodplain following a previous survey covering the western part of the site in 2016. Virtually all potentially suitable habitat was surveyed. Although most new survey areas proved negative, an additional 0.5ha of V. moulinsiana habitat was identified, this being contiguous with the occupied habitat block lying to the immediate west surveyed in 2016. Areas of habitat not supporting the snail were judged to be too dry and/or too acidic to support the snail. It is calculated that the minimum total area of the Afon Penrhos floodplain occupied by V. moulinsiana is 11.07ha, with a possible maximum of 13.2ha if a number of adjoining but potentially suitable un-surveyed areas also support the snail. The Afon Penrhos population is not only the largest of three in Wales, but also probably one of the largest in mainland Britain. The significance of the population here is further enhanced by recent considerable population losses at several Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in southern England and on the nearby Corsydd Llyn SAC (Cors Geirch National Nature Reserve (NNR) / Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)). Now that the probable full extent of the population on Afon Penrhos is known, it is recommended that the site should be notified as a SSSI to reduce any risk of loss or damage due to changes in site management.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:04:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18486\tNRW_DS121555\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.650Z\t10\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Natural Processes Constraints\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353535\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\\tVictoria Greest\tThis appears to be an unnecessary entry as there is no x drive layer called \"Natural Processes Constraints\".  Possibly it is a duplication of Woodland constraints dataset (NRW_DS121359) or Natural Processes woodland (NRW_DS121556)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18490\tNRW_DS121556\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.928Z\t12\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Natural Processes Woodlands\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353536\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Natural Processes woodland.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18487\tNRW_DS121564\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.658Z\t284\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFlood mitigation woodland opportunities for Dyfi, Rhonndda and Tawe Area Trails\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset, due to the nature of the licence of the underlying data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353634\tX:\\Resource Management\\Flood Mitigation Woodland Opportunities\\Dyfi Catchment.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Flood Mitigation Woodland Opportunities\\Rhondda Catchment.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Flood Mitigation Woodland Opportunities\\Tawe Catchment.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Patrick Green (patrick.green@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk)  Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team  This dataset was done under contract and the external contractor was Forest Research: Huw Thomas huw.thomas1@forestry.gsi.gov.uk       Information from Huw Thomas from Forest Research the contractors of this dataset:  I’ve made some enquires and the only dataset which may present a problem would be the NSRI soils data and any dataset we created using the NSRI soil data so anything which selected areas on HOST class for example. In this case, Maps 16 to18. FR and FC are permitted to make use of any soil and soil derived data to meet the objectives of our agency so we can make the data visible via online map browsers and supply printed images but we cannot share the data unless the recipients have the same licence with the NSRI as we do (which the Environment Agency has). If NRW has the same licence agreements as the EA that will be fine also. 3rd parties can not have the spatial data unless they have a licence with NSRI or pay NSRI for the use of your data. However they can view the data.\tThis is a derived spatial dataset created under and Natural Resources Wales (NRW) contract by Forest Research. The creation of this dataset was ommissioned by NRW for three Area Trails, Dyfi, Rhonndda and Tawe, as part of the work related to the Sustainable Management of Natural Resources. This dataset outlines opportunities for woodland creation to help mitigate flooding in the three catchments; Dyfi, Rhonndda and Tawe.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18488\tNRW_DS121565\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.906Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey data from the 2017 Cwm Idwal National Nature Reserve (NNR) Surveillance - vegetation response to grazing removal, 9th round of surveillance.\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353635\tData is held on the NRW DMS within the Protected Sites, Eryri SSSI location.  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landmanps1/psg/ersn/Monitoring%20%20Surveillance/Surveillance/Cwm%20Idwal%20-%20Post%20grazing%20exclusion%20surveillance/2017\tInternal Custodian: Philip Oliver, Environment Officer, Anglesey \u0026amp; Arfon Environment Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Philip Oliver, Environment Officer, Anglesey \u0026amp; Arfon Environment Team\tThis is the data behind the ninth surveillance undertaken to report on the effect of stock exclusion since 1999 in the Cwm Idwal National Nature Reserve (NNR). This surveillance of vegetation response is the first over a five yearly period and the fourth since the recording methodology was changed in 2006. During this surveillance round, both the revised methodology and some elements of the original methodology were used enabling comparison further back than 2006 and interpretation of the changes to date in terms of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). Field data has been gathered in 2017 for 11 of the original (1999) 30 survey plots to feed into this ongoing surveillance program. The data gathered, records the frequency of plant species, selected species groups and other variables (bare ground, exposed peat, bare rock, litter, and sheep/goat droppings) from the five \u0027fixed\u0027 quadrats within each of the eleven surveillance plots covered by the 2006 baseline. The data gathered is primarily intended for use in comparing vegetation change with previous rounds of this surveillance program and is not intended for any other purpose. The data is in the form of an Excel workbook with sheets setting out: - plot locations within the NNR - quadrat locations within plots (5 quadrats per plot) - plot vegetation heights - individual quadrat species and other variable (e.g. bare ground, litter, droppings) presence. - individual quadrat, species\u0027 % cover values - individual cell recordings (twenty, 25x25cm cells in each quadrat) for species present. - Landscape photography location and framing information - Plot and quadrat photography location and framing information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18482\tNRW_DS121566\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.235Z\t713\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCarmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Atlantic Salt Meadows Monitoring (2017)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353636\tGIS Data: Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Team_SouthWest\\Lily_Pauls\\Lily_Pauls.gdb\\SaltmarshSurveys\\all_saltmarsh_points2_text\tInternal custodian: Lily Pauls, Marine Team, SW Ops, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tThis dataset consists of an excel spreadsheet containing the sampling point data for Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Saltmarsh 2012 condition assessment survey. The survey covered saltmarsh in the Burry Inlet, Loughor and Three Rivers. Data recorded included saltmarsh plant species, % cover, negative indicators and sward heights. The GIS layer shows the location of the points referred to within the Excel spreadsheet.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18485\tNRW_DS121588\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.391Z\t608\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status and distribution of the scarab beetles Rhysothorax rufa and Onthophagus nuchicornis on Welsh dunes in 2017\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353838\t[A]  Report is held in the DMS report Archive [B]  Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tInternal Custodian: Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team\tThe Endangered Red Dune Crawler Rhysothorax rufa has only been recorded from a handful of dune systems in England and Wales, and has not been recorded in England for over fifteen years. In Wales, it is known from Kenfig Burrows, where a single adult was found on 20th July 1994, and from Merthyr Mawr Warren, where single adults were recorded in June 1981 and 21st May 2006. Adults feed on detritus in sand. A survey in 2017 failed to find the beetle at either location. Current management at both Kenfig Burrows and Merthyr Mawr Warren is considered sympathetic for the conservation of this species.  The Vulnerable Dillwyn\u0027s Dung Beetle Onthophagus nuchicornis was formerly widespread across England and Wales. It has declined dramatically in England and is now known from just five of 30 previous locations, where it is recorded infrequently and often as single individuals. In Wales, it is restricted to coastal sand dune systems and there are records from fifteen sites, from Merthyr Mawr Warren in the south-east to Morfa Harlech in the north-west. Larvae can be found in the dung of a range of mammals but are probably dependent upon that of large herbivores such as cattle, ponies and sheep. A survey in 2017 of the known sites, utilising both hand-searching and baited pitfall traps, found Onthophagus nuchicornis at seven localities - Kenfig Burrows (975 adults), Merthyr Mawr Warren (36), Morfa Harlech (1), Oxwich Burrows (4), Pembrey Burrows (32), Pennard Burrows (4) and Whiteford Burrows (5). The population on Kenfig Burrows is the strongest in the UK. Elsewhere, absence and low population levels are strongly correlated to cessation of grazing and the quantity and quality of available dung. Management, including grazing regimes, are considered sympathetic for the conservation of this species at three sites (Kenfig Burrows, Merthyr Mawr Warren and Whiteford Burrows), although there is room for improvement. Increased access for cattle to mobile dunes and disturbed areas of open sand would undoubtedly benefit Onthophagus nuchicornis. Management recommendations are made for all currently-occupied sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-05-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:01:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18480\tNRW_DS121589\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.209Z\t282\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring Wetland Invertebrates on Rhos Goch National Nature Reserve, Radnorshire (2018)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353839\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tInternal Custodian: Mike Howe Invertebrate Ecologist  and  Rhys Jenkins   Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tThis work presents the findings of wetland invertebrate monitoring work undertaken in 2017 at Rhos Goch National Nature Reserve, Radnorshire. Previous surveys have identified the four wetland habitats of bare mud, ground layer, open water and wetland flora as key wetland invertebrate assemblage features. Previously in 2007, six monitoring stations were set up and timed searches undertaken using a range of collecting methods, with the aim of developing standardised future monitoring. Target wetland invertebrate species were then chosen for each key habitat. For the reserve to be in favourable condition for its wetland invertebrate assemblage, any monitoring would have to record at least 50% of the target taxa.   This work follows on from invertebrate monitoring undertaken in 2007 and 2012. A total of 438 records of 226 invertebrate species were recorded during the current 2017 monitoring. Of the 115 target species previously recorded, only 26 species (23%) were taken in 2017, marking a steady decline over the 10 year monitoring period (68 species [59%] in 2007; 37 species [32%] in 2012). This shows that all of the four key wetland habitat features are in unfavourable condition and the percentages of the target wetland species are continuing to decline.  It is recommended that the levels of grazing need to be increased to provide bare mud and open water habitat, and that scrub management should continue to keep succession in check.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-08-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18481\tNRW_DS121590\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.212Z\t455\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) G East Felling Plan\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353930\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA G East Felling Plan.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of felling proposed by the Developer for the wind farm development. As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18484\tNRW_DS121591\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.377Z\t465\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA comparison of the dune slack invertebrate faunas of Newborough Warren, Morfa Dyffryn and Morfa Harlech (2015)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353931\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tInternal Custodian: Mike Howe Invertebrate Ecologist  Data Custodian: Data Services Team\tThis dataset are records from comparison surveys of invertebrate species from Newborough Warren where sand dune restoration occurred, and Morfa Dyffryn and Morfa Harlech where natural sand dune habitat occurs. Following management works on Newborough Warren National Nature Reserve in 2013 to create pioneer dune slacks and other early-successional habitats, invertebrate surveys had demonstrated a rapid dispersal/colonisation by seven of sixteen beetle species which Natural Resources Wales regards as requiring these conditions. Additional excavations in March 2015 provided an opportunity to find how quickly this colonisation occurred. Within two months, three pioneer dune slack beetles - Bembidion pallidipenne, Bledius subniger and Dyschirius politus - were recorded in pitfall traps set in this area, all of which had previously been difficult to locate on the dune system. As it was suspected that the colonising fauna of the excavated slacks had its origin in the estuaries to the north and south of Newborough Warren, surveys for Bledius beetles were undertaken in the Cefni and Braint estuaries by mapping the casts thrown up by their activities. Similar surveys were undertaken along the Afon Artro at Morfa Dyffryn and the Glaslyn and Dwyryd estuaries north of Morfa Harlech. It revealed that the Bledius colonies occurred in limited areas and seemed to be associated with sand newly deposited by wind or water and of a low organic content. Diatoms were found abundantly in the intestines of the Bledius beetles.  Pioneer slack habitat is at a premium on all Welsh dune systems other than Morfa Dyffryn where mobile dunes with drifting sand help to create pioneer slack as waves of sand move in front of the prevailing wind. This 2015 survey compared the invertebrate faunas of these naturally-occurring slacks with the artificially-engineered slacks on Newborough Warren. The opportunity was also taken to sample two slacks on Morfa Harlech, a well-vegetated slack on Morfa Dyffryn and two unexcavated slacks on Newborough Warren. The survey was made at 20 sites with five pitfall traps at each and the total data set consisted of 100,205 individual invertebrates spread across 505 species. The taxa used in this survey consisted of beetles, spiders, harvesters, millipedes, centipedes, woodlice and a few orthopteroid insects. This study has divided a previous definition of pioneer slack on Morfa Dyffryn into pioneer slack (damp, almost bare, yellow sand at the foot of an eroding dune) and post-pioneer slack (upwind of pioneer slacks and more vegetated, with bare sand having a black crust). Whilst there are biases with pitfall trapping and some limitations on drawing conclusions from analyses, pitfall data was examined by Principal Components Analysis (PCA) after splitting the data into 13 sub-sets based on taxonomic groupings. The ground cover of the vegetation was assessed in the three slacks sampled on Morfa Dyffryn to determine the differences between pioneer and post-pioneer slacks, with PCA separating them into two distinct clusters. The analysis also groups the excavated slacks on Newborough Warren close to the Morfa Dyffryn pioneer slack. This is particularly so where the data set is presented in the form of histograms of the numbers of selected species across the 20 sites.  An analysis of the sixteen beetle species regarded by NRW as being associated with pioneer slacks, using information from this study and other sources (published literature, species records in the NBN Atlas) suggests that several are often if not exclusively associated with the post-pioneer slack habitat. The pioneer slack beetle fauna is more properly restricted to four species - Bembidion pallidipenne, Bledius fergussoni, Bledius subniger and Dyschirius politus - which are also found in the estuarine habitat at the edge of the salt marsh.  A small survey of the tiger beetle Cicindela maritima found it breeding in numbers in the damp sand of the pioneer slack ...\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-05-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:01:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18477\tNRW_DS121593\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.075Z\t867\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) A Proposed Turbines\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353933\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA A Proposed Turbines.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of proposed turbine locations by the Developer for the windfarm development As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:19:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18483\tNRW_DS121594\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.243Z\t608\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) F Proposed Turbines\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353934\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA F Proposed Turbines.lyr\tCustodians:  James Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of proposed turbine locations by the Developer for the windfarm development As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:18:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18479\tNRW_DS121595\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.014Z\t951\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) G West Proposed Turbines\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353935\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA G West Proposed Turbines.lyr\tCustodians: James Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of proposed turbine locations by the Developer for the windfarm development As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:20:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18478\tNRW_DS121596\t2024-05-28T12:50:12.948Z\t661\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) G East Proposed Turbines\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353936\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA G East Proposed Turbines.lyr\tCustodians: James Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of proposed turbine locations by the Developer for the wind farm development As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:57.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18476\tNRW_DS121599\t2024-05-28T12:50:12.804Z\t1299\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353939\tX:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\ X:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive.lyr\tMark Squire, Team Leader Water Policy \u0026amp; Directives Implementation (mark.squire@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk)       Metadata contact: Mark Squire\t\u0027Urban waste water\u0027 is defined in the Urban Waster Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) as the mixture of domestic waste water from kitchens, bathrooms and toilets, the waste water from industries discharging to sewers, and rainwater run-off from roads and other impermeable surfaces such as roofs, pavements and roads draining to sewers. Urban waste water is often referred to as ‘sewage’. The UWWTR  is intended to protect the water environment for the animals and plants that live in and around water, as well as for recreational purposes and use as a resource for drinking water, sanitation, industry and commerce.   Under the UWWTR further sensitive areas have been identified because further treatment than secondary or equivalent treatment is necessary to meet other EC Directives such as the Water Framework Directive. Areas that come under as sensitive areas include Bathing Waters, Shellfish Waters and waters that are or soon to be Eutrophic.  This entry refers to a series of spatial datasets that can used in Urban Waste Water management.  In 2023 the dataset was updated and renamed from the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD). Please see the child metadata entries for more information on these updates.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-08-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/5375/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_uwwtr\t2025-11-12T11:42:38.167Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18525\tNRW_DS121612\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.530Z\t721\tUnassessed\tEditable\tStatutory Plant Health Notices\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363132\tX:\\Incidents\\Forest Health\\Statutory Plant Health Notices.lyr   lyr   (this one is for people who just view the data – like a normal GIS user)  X:\\Editing\\Statutory Plant Health Notices EDIT.lyr   (this one is for people who can have access and edit the sites a bit like a working area… you cannot view the data in the editing folder if you cannot edit it (only the GIS team can view this data))\tInternal Data Custodian: Callum Stone       Data contains:  Personal Data Legal concerns This data contains information that may be deemed as personal sensitive information, ie legally binding Statutory Notices.\tPhytophthora ramorum (P. ramorum) is a fungus-like pathogen called a water mould. It causes extensive damage and death to a wide range of trees and other plants. This spatial data is owned by Natural Resources Wales and shows the spatial extent of sites served with a Statutory Notice (destruction or containment) under the Plant Health (Forestry) Order 2005.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T16:10:39.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18516\tNRW_DS121625\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.404Z\t969\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales Grassland Fungi Assessment 2017 - 2018\t© CNC/NRW, BIS, Cofnod, WWBIC, SeWBReC, British Mycological Society NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363235\tEvidence \u0026gt; Species \u0026gt; Fungi \u0026gt; Fungus Habitats \u0026gt; Grassland Fungi assessment 2017-18 David Mitchel  DMS copy https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/fun/Fungus%20Habitats/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Ffun%2FFungus%20Habitats%2FGrassland%20Fungi%20assessment%202017%2D18%20David%20Mitchel\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000A00E94777D591649A9E40A217F4AC093\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Sam Bosanquet        • Third Party Data (records from the British Mycological Society and LERCs) • Data that is release could result in environmental harm  (species records that could result in a site owner destroying a potentially important grassland) • Personal Data (names of recorders)\tGrassland fungus data for Wales compiled, updated and checked by David Mitchel in 2017-18. Species names are standardised; records are assigned to sites, subsites and metasites with standard names; the diversity of species on sites, subsites and metasites is given for each grassland fungal genus, and for the whole \u0027CHEGD\u0027 assemblage.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-02-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18533\tNRW_DS121626\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.540Z\t621\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) B Felling Plan\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363236\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA B Felling Plan.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of felling proposed by the Developer for the wind farm development. As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:20:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18528\tNRW_DS121627\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.616Z\t444\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) B Proposed Turbines\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363237\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA B Proposed Turbines.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of proposed turbine locations by the Developer for the windfarm development As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:20:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18530\tNRW_DS121629\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.503Z\t582\tValid\tEditable\tSource Protection Zones (SPZ) Locations\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363239\tx:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SPZ Source Protection Zones Location.lyr\tDave Jones, Senior Specialist Advisor Geoscience\tSPZs are defined around large and public potable groundwater abstraction sites. The purpose of SPZs is to provide additional protection to safeguard drinking water quality through constraining the proximity of an activity that may impact upon a drinking water abstraction. This is part of an initial screening process in assessing impacts to groundwater resources.   Zones around location sites are defined by groundwater travel time to an abstraction. This is determined through applying groundwater flow models run at the location of abstractions, inputting parameters such as flow direction, geology type, rainfall and hydrological boundaries. SPZs provide a visual representation of the increased risks as you get closer to the abstraction.   The following subdivisions are defined within SPZs:   Zone 1: (Inner Protection Zone) - This zone is defined by a travel time of 50-days or less from any point within the zone at, or below, the water table. Additionally, the zone has as a minimum a 50-metre radius. It is based principally on biological decay criteria and is designed to protect against the transmission of toxic chemicals and water-borne disease.   Zone 2: (Outer Protection Zone) - This zone is defined by the 400-day travel time from a point below the water table. Additionally this zone has a minimum radius of 250 or 500 metres, depending on the size of the abstraction. The travel time is derived from consideration of the minimum time required to provide delay, dilution and attenuation of slowly degrading pollutants.   Zone 3: (Total catchment) - This zone is defined as the total area needed to support the abstraction or discharge from the protected groundwater source.   A further Zone 4, or ‘Zone of Special Interest’ was previously defined for some groundwater sources. These zones highlighted areas (mainly on non-aquifers) where known local conditions meant that potentially polluting activities could impact on a groundwater source even though the area is outside the normal catchment of that source. In future this zone will be incorporated into one of the other zones (1, 2 or 3), whichever is appropriate in the particular case.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18529\tNRW_DS121633\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.538Z\t737\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMultibeam Acoustic Data Index\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363333\tX:\\Physical\\Geological\\Marine\\Marine Acoustic Data\\Multibeam acoustic data index.lyr.   On CCW M drive it is stored as M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Hydrographic\\Marine_Acoustic_data\\multibeam_data\\acoustic_data_index.tab.It is also archived as  project 379, media 1390.\tDave Tavner Marine Mapping Scientist, Evidence Analysis  GIS and Spatial data team\tThis is a spatial dataset detailing the location of multibeam data Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to. This was done as a collation exercise to produce a spatial (GIS) index of all multibeam data held by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The Index shows the loaction of surveys and points to where the raw data and/or processed data is held.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-03-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:59:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18532\tNRW_DS121646\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.567Z\t270\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHydroelectric Power (HEP) - Sensitive Bryophytes, Lichens and Ferns\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363436\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Bryophyte_Lichen_HEP_Sensitive.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tSites considered to be sensitive to Hydroelectric Power (HEP) abstraction by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) because of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) bryophyte features, the presence of desiccation-sensitive bryophytes, the presence of rare or scarce riverine lichens, or the presence of Killarney Fern gametophyte. This layer was designed to aid consultation between Environment Agency Wales (EAW) and CCW.  As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18519\tNRW_DS121648\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.335Z\t614\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Habitat Survey in Wales: Analysis for Area Statements 2018\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363438\tThe excel spreadsheet is saved on the DMS: Evidence \u0026gt; Freshwater Ecology \u0026gt; Area Based Statements \u0026gt; River Habitat Survey Contract for Area Based Statements https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/fe/Area%20Based%20Statements/River%20Habitat%20Survey%20Contract%20for%20Area%20Based%20Statements  The Access database is saved on Sue Hearn\u0027s desktop until such as time as it can be moved to the Azure File Storage Solution.\tInternal Data Custodian(s):  Internal: Sue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, Knowledge Strategy and Planning  Metadata Point of Contact: Internal: Sue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, Knowledge Strategy and Planning\tData collation and indices derived from 704 River Habitat Survey sites in Wales surveyed 2007-8. This dataset comprises of an excel spreadsheet of basic attribute data plus calculated indices for Welsh statement areas including Habitat Modification score, Habitat Quality Assessment score and River Habitat Quality score, plus an interactive access database of above.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18534\tNRW_DS121653\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.627Z\t1164\tUnassessed\tEditable\tElectricity Powerlines on Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Land\tUnredacted Data © CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Redacted Data © CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363533\tx:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\Electricity Powerlines on NRW Land.lyr\tGIS Technicians in 4 Forest Districts, National Forest Planning Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains electricity powerlines on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any utility data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured electricity powerline information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-09-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:53.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18524\tNRW_DS121654\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.485Z\t331\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGas Pipelines on Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Land\tUnredacted Data  © CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Redacted Data  © CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363534\tx:\\Utilities\\Gas\\Gas Pipelines on NRW Land.lyr\tGIS Technicians in 4 Forest Districts, National Forest Planning Team, NRW\tThis dataset contains gas pipelines on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any utility data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured overhead telephone line information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-07-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:49.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18531\tNRW_DS121655\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.498Z\t393\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Mowing One Side\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363535\tx:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Mowing One Side.lyr\tKeith Jones, Spatial Evidence Manager, Evidence Knowledge and Advice\tWatercourses within the IDD that are flailed (Mowed) one side due to access restrictions and environmental reasons. Produced to aid the production of the works programme and to ensure watercourses are managed in agreement with stakeholders.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-07-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:53.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18527\tNRW_DS121656\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.541Z\t622\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Watercourses with overhead powerlines\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363536\tx:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Watercourses with OH Power lines.lyr\tKeith Jones, Spatial Evidence Manager, Evidence Knowledge and Advice\tContains the watercourses within the Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels IDD that have overhead powerlines above or near them.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-07-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:36.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18520\tNRW_DS121657\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.446Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMunitions Marine Licence Applications\tAccess to this data may be restricted under relevant legislation. NRW may NOT publish or disseminate the data. Requests should be referred to NRW\u0027s Access to Information Officer.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363537\tX:\\Editing  Restricted use\tShelley Vince, Senior Permitting Officer (shelly.vince@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk).  Custodianship believed to have moved to Joseph Thomas.\tThe records contain coordinate data associated with current marine licence applications in Wales. This data allows the locations of licence applications to be mapped to assess their impacts on designated sites and combined effects with other proposals.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18535\tNRW_DS121668\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.632Z\t1012\tValid\tEditable\tWINS Area Statement Opportunity Maps\tSee the use constraints for each individual dataset part of the WINS project as it will vary depending on the data used in its creation. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363638\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\\tRussell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk)\tThe Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS Project (previously known as SCCAN), showed a range of national opportunities to manage natural resources more sustainably in the SoNaRR report. This current phase has looked at a more local level to provide input to the Area Statement process. The result is a comprehensive map atlas of opportunity, demand and constraint maps, with accompanying spatial data, to facilitate broad, cross-sectorial discussions on sustainable management of natural resources (SMNR).   SCCAN aims to promote more holistic management of natural resources through spatial mapping of opportunity areas for land management interventions. The mapping focusses on priorities of the Welsh Government Natural Resources Policy (2017) arising from the Environment (Wales) Act (2016), which puts an emphasis on taking a place-based approach and delivering nature-based solutions.   The five policy themes considered by the project: Ecological Resilience and Connectivity; Marine and Freshwater Water Quality; Natural Flood Management; Woodland Planting (for various objectives); Urban and Peri-Urban Green Infrastructure.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-01-20\tpublication\thttps://data.envsys.co.uk/geoserver/ONS_habitats/wms?\thttps://smnr-nrw.hub.arcgis.com/apps/c7770d2881394c899123bae210afe370/explore\t2025-12-01T17:20:57.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18517\tNRW_DS121669\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.321Z\t320\tValid\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Agricultural Management Opportunities\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363639\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Agricultural Management Opportunities.lyr\tRussell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.    What the map shows:   The map shows areas where agricultural management measures could contribute positively to natural flood management. Measures considered are the planting or widening of existing hedgerows (all existing hedgerows are displayed in this map), as well as retention of winter stubbles. Hard engineering solutions are not considered in the map set for natural flood management.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:10:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18523\tNRW_DS121670\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.503Z\t293\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Bog Restoration\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363730\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Bog Restoration.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.  What the map shows: This map shows opportunities for bog restoration. The main opportunity for bog restoration is the restoration of existing bog habitat currently in degraded condition. Additionally, peat soils under coniferous plantations are highlighted, which can be a potential opportunity, particularly if the peat soil is in good condition. Existing bog habitat is shown for reference. Hard engineering solutions to flooding are not considered.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:09:09.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18518\tNRW_DS121672\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.358Z\t333\tValid\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Catchment Zones\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363732\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Catchment Zones.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)   This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.    What the map shows:  The map shows the area split into seven separate catchment zones, in which different types of NFM intervention can be used to achieve benefits.   The catchment zones are:  floodplains in valley bottom areas; floodplains in the mid-reaches; gentle and steeply sloping mid-reaches; floodplains in the headwaters regions, as well as gently and steeply sloping headwater regions.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\thttps://smnr-nrw.hub.arcgis.com/apps/c7770d2881394c899123bae210afe370/explore\t2025-12-01T17:23:44.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18522\tNRW_DS121673\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.448Z\t321\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Enhancing Surface Roughness\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363733\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Enhancing Surface Roughness.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.  What the map shows: This map shows opportunities for enhancing surface roughness, for example by relaxing grazing pressure, restoring semi-natural habitat, increasing species diversity or through complete change in land use (e.g. woodland planting). Priority is given to areas that are well connected to the hydrological environment and/or currently have a low surface roughness. The opportunity spaces shown in the map are the areas that remain after the constraints have been applied. Habitats of high conservation value and high economic value have been considered a constraint, as in these areas it is unlikely to be possible or desirable to increase the surface roughness. The opportunity space on this map shows opportunities that remain after all constraints have been applied.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:04:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18521\tNRW_DS121674\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.405Z\t666\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Constraints for Enhancing Surface Roughness\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363734\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Constraints for Enhancing Surface Roughness.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.  What the map shows: The maps shows the number of constraints to enhancing surface roughness for NFM.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:14:24.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18526\tNRW_DS121676\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.537Z\t293\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Catchment Woodland Planting\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363736\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Catchment Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.  What the map shows: This map shows the opportunity space for planting catchment woodland for NFM benefits. It is important to note that most effective use of these opportunity spaces would be to plant deep-rooting species, particularly broadleaved species, in order to fully realise the NFM potential.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:10:17.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18515\tNRW_DS121677\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.814Z\t409\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Constraints for Catchment Woodland Planting\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363737\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Constraints for Catchment Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report. This map shows the constraints preventing catchment woodland planting. The constraints map gives an indication of the number of reasons for not planting woodland for NFM in an area. The following types of constraints have been considered: biophysical, practical, preservation of exisiting urban areas, conservation, cultural, economic and legislative.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:03:12.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18514\tNRW_DS121698\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.694Z\t522\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Riparian Woodland Planting\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363938\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Riparian Woodland Planting .lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets.  What this map shows: This map shows the opportunity space for planting riparian woodland for NFM benefits. It is important to note that most effective use of these opportunity spaces would be to plant deep-rooting species, particularly broadleaved species, in order to fully realise the NFM potential.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:12:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18512\tNRW_DS121699\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.757Z\t333\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Constraints for Riparian Woodland Planting\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363939\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Constraints for Riparian Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. What the map shows: This map shows the combined constraints to planting riparian woodland for NFM benefits.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:08:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18513\tNRW_DS121700\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.760Z\t416\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Floodplain Woodland Planting\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373030\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Floodplain Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report. What the map shows: This map shows the opportunity space for planting floodplain woodland to deliver NFM benefits. It is important to note that most effective use of these opportunity spaces would be to plant deep-rooting species, particularly broadleaved species, in order to fully realise NFM potential.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:18:24.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18509\tNRW_DS121701\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.491Z\t412\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Constraints for Floodplain Woodland Planting\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373031\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Constraints for Floodplain Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:20:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18508\tNRW_DS121703\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.492Z\t298\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Combined Woodland Planting\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373033\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Combined Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.  What the map shows: This map shows all types of opportunities for woodland planting that are likely to deliver NFM benefits. The combined layer is based on the constrained input layers of three types of woodland planting: Riparian/Drainage channel shelter belts, floodplain woodland, woodland throughout the wider catchment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:01:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18511\tNRW_DS121705\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.708Z\t576\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Demand for Natural Flood Management\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373035\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Demand for Natural Flood Management.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. What the map shows: The map shows catchments feeding into urban areas at elevated risk from fluvial flooding; these are demand areas for natural flood management.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:06:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18510\tNRW_DS121710\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.507Z\t330\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Urban Green Infrastructure: Demand for Urban Greenspace - Noise Pollution\t© CNC/NRW. © Office of National Statistics (ONS). © Welsh Government. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373130\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Urban Green Infrastructure\\SCCAN Demand for Urban Greenspace - Noise Pollution.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows demand areas for greenspace based on locations affected by noise pollution, with noise pollution in decibel sound level contours. Along a colour gradient from dark blue to dark brown the demand increases, based on a combination of three factors: the intensity of noise pollution problem, population density and deprivation.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:05:34.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18507\tNRW_DS121712\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.286Z\t684\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Urban Green Infrastructure: Demand for Urban Greenspace - Air Pollution\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © Office for National Statistics (ONS). © DEFRA. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373132\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Urban Green Infrastructure\\SCCAN Demand for Urban Greenspace - Air Pollution.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.  What the map shows: The map shows demand areas for greenspace based on locations with air quality issues, with air quality being based on annual average PM2.5. Along a colour gradient from dark green to dark brown the demand increases, based on a combination of three factors: the intensity of air pollution problem, population density and deprivation.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:15:48.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18506\tNRW_DS121713\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.243Z\t778\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Urban Green Infrastructure: Demand for Urban Greenspace - Urban Accessible Greenspace\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373133\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Urban Green Infrastructure\\SCCAN Demand for Urban Greenspace - Urban Accessible Greenspace.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows the catchment areas for accessible greenspaces, highlighting greenspaces that are likely to have a more ‘natural’ feel. It also shows urban areas that do not currently have access to greenspace (demand), and these have been prioritised according to the number of people and the overall deprivation in the area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-06-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:13:14.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18546\tNRW_DS121714\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.530Z\t479\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Urban Green Infrastructure: Demand for Urban Greenspace - Risk of Pluvial Flooding\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © Office for National Statistics (ONS). Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373134\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Urban Green Infrastructure\\SCCAN Demand for Urban Greenspace - Risk of Pluvial Flooding.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.  What the map shows: The two maps of pluvial flooding show:  [A] the risk of standing water accumulating during heavy rainfall on a bright colour ramp from green to purple, and areas at risk of strong overland flow on a darker colour ramp from green to purple, with purple areas being at highest risk in both cases. The separation into standing water and overland flow can help to target intervention – the dark purple area connected to a bright purple area shows where the water flooding the light purple area originates and, hence, where interventions could be located to disrupt flow paths. [B] the demand based on population density and the Welsh index of multiple deprivation on a gradient from light to dark brown, with darker areas having a higher demand. A purple semi-transparent overly indicates the areas at highest risk from the left-hand map to make the maps spatially comparable.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:56:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18551\tNRW_DS121715\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.673Z\t266\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Woodland Planting: Opportunities for Woodland Planting for Carbon Sequestration\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373135\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Opportunities for Woodland Planting for Carbon Sequestration.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows areas where woodland could be planted and deliver carbon sequestration benefits.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:57:51.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18552\tNRW_DS121716\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.688Z\t310\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Woodland Planting: Constraints for Woodland Planting for Carbon Sequestration\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains SCIMAP Information © 2016 Durham University. All rights reserved. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373136\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Constraints for Woodland Planting for Carbon Sequestration.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows the number of constraints an area has to woodland planting. The constraints map gives an indication for the number of reasons for not planting woodland in an area. The following types of constraints for woodland planting have been considered: biophysical, practical, preservation of existing urban areas, conservation, cultural, economic and legislative.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:45:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18549\tNRW_DS121717\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.669Z\t615\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Woodland Planting: Opportunities for Woodland Planting for Rapid Turnover Forest\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373137\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Opportunities for Woodland Planting for Rapid Turnover Forest.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows areas where rapid turnover woodland could be planted for the biomass and timber industries.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:51:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18555\tNRW_DS121718\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.927Z\t309\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Woodland Planting: Constraints for Woodland Planting for Rapid Turnover Forest\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373138\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Constraints for Woodland Planting for Rapid Turnover Forest.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows the number of constraints an area has to woodland planting to where rapid turnover woodland could be planted for the biomass and timber industries. The constraints map gives an indication for the number of reasons for not planting woodland in an area. The following types of constraints for woodland planting have been considered: biophysical, practical, preservation of existing urban areas, conservation, cultural, economic and legislative.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:39:23.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18548\tNRW_DS121719\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.663Z\t452\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Woodland Planting: Opportunities for Community Woodlands\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373139\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Opportunities for Community Woodlands.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows planting opportunities for community woodlands, as well as already existing accessible and inaccessible woodlands within 2km of urban areas. Each of these three categories is separated into three demand classes: areas where many children are present; where the overall deprivation level is high; and where these two factors coincide, e.g. many children live in areas of high deprivation.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:41:30.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18547\tNRW_DS121720\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.570Z\t434\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Woodland Planting: Opportunities for Planting Large/Medium/Small Woodlands\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373230\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Opportunities for Planting Large Medium \u0026amp; Small Woodlands.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: This map shows areas that are suitable for woodland planting subdivided into woodland size categories, identifying opportunities to plant large, medium or small-scale woodlands, based on the number of land owners.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:44:33.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18553\tNRW_DS121721\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.731Z\t956\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Water Quality: Opportunities for Reducing Faecal Coliform Pollution\t© CNC/NRW All rights reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373231\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Opportunities for Reducing Faecal Coliform Pollution.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows areas where management action could be taken to reduce the risk for faecal coliforms entering into coastal and transitional waterbodies, as well as lakes and rivers. Management opportunities considered are fencing and/or hedges to prevent direct access to water courses by livestock, and woodland planting to form riparian or drainage channel shelter belts. For all opportunity types, those falling into upslope areas of sites with a known problem with faecal coliforms are considered priority opportunities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:52:56.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18550\tNRW_DS121722\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.690Z\t642\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Water Quality: Constraints for Reducing Faecal Coliform Pollution\t© CNC/NRW All rights reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373232\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Constraints for Reducing Faecal Coliform Pollution.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows constraints to intervention types for faecal coliforms entering the water environment. The number of constraints is combined across all land management intervention types, so an areas with one constraint for one type of action, and two constraints for another type of action will show as having three constraints.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:50:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18554\tNRW_DS121723\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.856Z\t319\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Water Quality: Opportunities for Reducing Sedimentation\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © 2021 Cranfield University. All rights reserved. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373233\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Opportunities Reducing Sedimentation.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows areas where management action could be taken to reduce sedimentation in coastal and transitional waterbodies, as well as lakes and rivers. Management opportunities considered for reduction of sedimentation are habitat restoration or conversion in areas at high erosion risk; riparian buffer strips; hedgerow planting; cover crops or grass on tillage land\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:47:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18544\tNRW_DS121724\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.240Z\t347\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Water Quality: Constraints for Reducing Sedimentation\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains SCIMAP Information © 2016 Durham University. All rights reserved. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373234\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Constraints for Reducing Sedimentation.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.  What the map shows: This map shows constraints to intervention types for reduction of sediment entering the water environment. The number of constraints is combined across all land management intervention types, so an area with one constraint for one type of action, and two constraints for another type of action will show as having three constraints.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:46:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18545\tNRW_DS121725\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.211Z\t398\tValid\tEditable\tWINS Water Quality: Opportunities for Reducing Nutrient Enrichment\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373235\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Opportunities for Reducing Nutrient Enrichment .lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.    What the map shows:  The map shows areas where management action could be taken to reduce nutrient enrichment in coastal and transitional waterbodies, as well as lakes and rivers.   Management opportunities considered for reduction of nutrient enrichment are the restoration of land well-connected to the local water environment; hedgerow planting in proximity to agricultural land likely to be a diffuse source of nutrients; planting riparian woodland or wooded shelterbelts along drainage channels.   For each opportunity type, prioritisation is based the number of downslope waterbodies that are known to have issues with nutrient enrichment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-19T10:49:32.433Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18543\tNRW_DS121726\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.232Z\t388\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Water Quality: Constraints for Reducing Nutrient Enrichment\tTo be added as part of an Open data assessment\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373236\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Constraints for Reducing Nutrient Enrichment.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: This map shows constraints to intervention types for reduction of excess nutrients from diffuse sources entering the water environment. The number of constraints is combined across all land management intervention types, so an area with one constraint for one type of action, and two constraints for another type of action will show as having three constraints.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-22T16:40:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18542\tNRW_DS121727\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.169Z\t723\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBullhead (Cottus gobio) population condition assessment for three Welsh SAC rivers: Afonydd Cleddau, River Usk \u0026amp; River Wye. Habitats Directive reporting cycle 3 2013 – 2018\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373237\ttop copy of data held in BIOSYS  Data also stored on W drive (H Garrett)  Report stored on the DMS as Evidence Report No. 266 -  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/fm/Running%20Waters%20Habitats%20Directive%20Data%20Cycle%203/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Ffm%2FRunning%20Waters%20Habitats%20Directive%20Data%20Cycle%203%2FAll%20SAC%20rivers%20Bullhead\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000EE02D3CE2ED1AE4085983FEDF88CC2E5\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B90B85581%2DA33A%2D4F0D%2DB9D4%2DBCB28FC39C08%7D\tHeather Garrett (heather.garrett@cyfoenaturiolcymru.gov.uk) (main author of report) Paul Hyatt, Freshwater monitoring co-ordinator, Senior Monitoring Officer Paul Greest,  Technical Specialist Fisheries.\tBy-catch records of adult \u0026amp; juvenile bullheads from other fish surveys and invertebrate kick sampling sites\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:13:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18541\tNRW_DS121728\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.138Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAbandoned coal mine discharges\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373238\tx:\\Industrial\\Mines\\Abandoned Coal Mine Discharge.lyr\tHaf Peskett, Technical Officer Groundwater and Contaminated Land, Geoscience / Knowledge Strategy and Planning\tPoint location abandoned coal minewater discharges recorded by former Environement Agency Wales based on NGR. Recorded as excel data, converted to point shape files on GIS. Data is used to locate coal minewater discarges across Wales.  Data has not been updated for a number of years and won’t be up to date.   The data is generally for internal use only. Some of the data may have been provided by the coal authority.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:17:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18539\tNRW_DS121731\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.127Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironmental Impact Assessment\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders   NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373331\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\EIA\\Environmental Impact Assessments.lyr\tLilley-Owen, Suzanne, Permitting Team Leader Forestry, Permitting Services (Forestry)\tAreas showing the extent of EIA forestry projects for which we have given our opinion.  The data takes the form of a polygon digitised by protected sites map.   The following attributes are recorded:  Property Name; Grid Reference; Project Type, Ref number. It was captured as a decision making tool, it shows where the site is and whether there are any adjacency issues which may affect our opinion.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-08-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:58:38.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18540\tNRW_DS121783\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.124Z\t698\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCarmarthen Bay common scoter (Melanitta nigra) data from the winter 2016 - 2017\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373833\tY:\\Marine\\Marine_Ornithology\\SPAs \u0026amp; SSSIs\\SPAs\\Camarthen bay SPA\\Camarthen Bay.gdb\tCustodian and metadata point of contact: Matthew Murphy, Senior Marine Ornithologist\tThis is data of common scoter locations collected by contractors APEM from aerial survey in the winter of 2016 to 2017. Carmarthen Bay Special Protection Area (SPA) was the first fully marine SPA in the UK; it includes areas of sea, coast and estuary that support a wide range of different marine habitats and wildlife. The SPA was designated in June 2003 solely for wintering common scoter (Melanitta nigra) and is the most important individual wintering site in Britain and Ireland for this species. To properly manage and report on the condition of the SPA, accurate population estimates are needed.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18537\tNRW_DS121810\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.051Z\t882\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS ONS Broad Habitats - Wales\t© CNC/NRW. Contains Office for National Statistics information © ONS. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383130\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\ONS Broad habitats\\ONS Broad habitats.lyr   ONS Broad Habitat report and statistics spreadsheet stored on the DMS. (https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/nrm/enpb/Project%20Charters/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fadvice%2Fnrm%2Fenpb%2FProject%20Charters%2FA14%20%2D%20Natural%20Resource%20Evidence%20Framework%2FSCCAN%2FONS%20Broad%20Habitats\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000267983946555B041947C4D4E8269E2AF\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BB4208ECF%2D3BE5%2D4868%2DAA0B%2DE40E8633D58A%7D)\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk\tThis map shows the broad habitats throughout Wales based on the Office of National Statistics broad habitats classes. It has been created as part of the project GIS for Area Statements (GISfAS) to support land cover ecosystem accounts by providing information on current economic value and change in land cover. GISfAS is part of a suite of projects also including SoNaRR (State of National Resources Reporting) and SAfAS (Spatial Analysis for Area Statements), all of which are facilitating management of natural resources and ecosystem services within Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:17:10.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17302\tNRW_DS12182\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.399Z\t644\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) G West Felling Plan\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3132313832\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA G West Felling Plan.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of felling proposed by the developer for the wind farm development  As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18538\tNRW_DS121844\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.060Z\t567\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe current status and distribution of the beetles Anthonomus conspersus, Aulacobaris lepidii and Thinobius newberyi on selected Welsh Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in 2018\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383434\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database - Recorder 6\tCustodian: Dr. Mike Howe - Invertebrate Ecologist  Contractor: Adrain Fowles, his home address is the same as his business address - contact Mike Howe for more information\tThe three species selected for this contract are all Individually Qualifying Features on their respective SSSIs, yet they had not been recorded on those SSSIs for at least twenty years. There is no evidence that Anthonomus conspersus or Aulacobaris lepidii had been looked for in the intervening period and although there was some survey effort in 2004 which could have yielded records of Thinobius newberyi, this is a difficult species to find. With habitat conditions for all three ostensibly remaining suitable there was no reason to suppose that any of their populations had become extinct.  Over such a long period, however, confirmation of their continued presence is clearly desirable.  The rowan weevil A. conspersus was found to be strongly established at Coed Boch y rhaeadr in Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SSSI, with adults recorded throughout the wood and on the adjacent ffridd. Historically the area was probably managed as pasture woodland and numerous ancient rowan trees are scattered throughout the more densely wooded parts of the site, as well as the open hillside. A detailed survey of the tree resource was not possible in the time available, but a rough estimate suggests that there are more than 300 trees present. A map of the denser concentrations is provided. For at least 20 years grazing stock has been fenced out of the wood as part of an agri-environment scheme and this has led to an increase in birch scrub on the lower slopes. At present there are sufficient clearings to provide suitable conditions for A. conspersus but consideration should be given to some management to retain these open spaces in the wood.  The yellow crucifer weevil A. lepidii was recorded twice on the Dee Meanders in Afon Dyfrdwy (River Dee) SSSI in the 1990s but has not been seen since. Five sites were selected for survey from aerial photographs where exposed sand and gravel was obvious on the riverbanks, as A. lepidii breeds in the roots of crucifers growing in sandy sediments. A further two sites were visited that had been identified as containing potential habitat during a previous survey. The species was not found during any of these surveys and vegetational succession had taken place at both of the recommended sites such that little or no exposed sand was seen. Hostplants of the weevil were generally scarce on the remaining sites and at four of the five trampling by livestock was noticeable, with the hostplant crucifers being heavily grazed. It is probable that A. lepidii still occurs along this stretch of river but the disturbance to the shingle bars is a concern and consideration should be given to fencing some of them to give protection to the shingle invertebrate fauna.  The shingle rove beetle T. newberyi is a feature of Gro Ty\u0027n yr Helyg SSSI on the Ystwyth and Rheidol Shingles \u0026amp; Backwaters SSSI on the Rheidol. At the original site on the Afon Ystwyth, Gro Ty’n-yr-helyg, the river has altered course to remove most of the former bar. Suitable sediment is deposited further downstream, but this is much smaller in extent than previously. On the Afon Rheidol, the original site of records in the 1990s is no longer suitable as dense willow carr has become established over the whole area. Efforts to reach other shingle bars in the floodplain were frustrated by deep water or dense scrub and the only bar investigated, at Abercwmdolau, had a high silt load in the sediment, whereas clean sand or fine gravel are thought to be required by the species. Hand-searching and excavation on both rivers failed to discover any examples of the target species, but this is a very difficult species to find and its absence from this survey should not be taken as evidence of extinction. The current state of the Rheidol is worrying but future flood events may rework sediments to create new bars. On the Ystywth, potential sites up and downstream from Ty’n-yr-helyg should be investigated to assess their potential for shingle invertebrates.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-11-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:17:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18536\tNRW_DS121852\t2024-05-28T12:51:14.789Z\t1486\tValid\tEditable\tNational Coastal Erosion Risk Management (NCERM)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383532\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Mgt Plan .....lyr  (There are 4 layers) see below  X:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Mgt Plan Gt Orme to England.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Mgt Plan Severn.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Mgt Plan Swansea Carmarthen Bay.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Mgt Plan West Wales.lyr\tLouise Pennington, Senior Specialist, Strategic Planning and Investment, Flood Risk Strategy, Sustainable Communities Group\tThe National Coastal Erosion Risk Management (NCERM) map shows the spatial NCERM coastal baseline. This baseline is split to \u0027frontages\u0027. These are defined as lengths of coast with consistent characteristics based on the cliff behaviour characteristics and the defence characteristics. It is intended as an up-to-date and reliable benchmark dataset showing erosion extents and rates for three periods from a base date of 2005:   - Short Term (0 - 20yr i.e. 2005 to 2025);  - Medium Term (20 - 50yr i.e. 2025 to 2055); and  - Long Term (50 - 100yr i.e. 2055 to 2105).    The data extents are based on 50%ile confidence levels, whilst the data attributes highlight the 5-95%ile confidence range, of erosion predictions for the following two scenarios (NB All distances are cumulative over time and given in metres):  - No Active Intervention Policy Scenario; and  - With the implementation of Shoreline Management Plan 2 Policies.    Defence type and SMP policies for each of the three periods described above are included.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-06-29\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/4142/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_ncerm\t2025-11-12T11:44:26.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18575\tNRW_DS121868\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.665Z\t491\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCoastal Flood Boundaries (CFB) Gauge, Surge, Swell data UK Datasets 2011\t© CNC/NRW, EA, SEPA Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. © Environment Agency copyright and/or database right 2015. All rights reserved. © SEPA\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383638\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB confidence intervals.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Estuary boundaries.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB extreme sea levels.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Gauge data UK.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Surge shapes.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Surge shapes UK.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Swell waves.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Swell waves UK.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis GIS dataset and supporting information providing design / extreme sea level and typical surge information around the coastline of the UK under present day conditions. The data within Wales is owned by Natural Resources Wales, England\u0027s data is owned by Environment Agency, and the Scottish data is owned by SEPA.   This dataset has now been superseded by NRW_DS123552, Coastal Flood Boundaries (CFB) publication 2018.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Environment Agency (EA)-Scottish Environmental Protection Agency (SEPA)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:44.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18570\tNRW_DS121888\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.442Z\t632\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRegulatory Woodland Officer Areas\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383838\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Regulatory Woodland Officer Areas.lyr\tCallum Stone, Team Leader Forest Regulation and Tree Plant Health\tMap of Wales split into 6 sections (South West, South East, South Central, Mid, North West, North East) with the officer responsible for each. Purpose: Staff information\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-03\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18581\tNRW_DS121916\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.831Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSeagrass Area\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393136\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seagrass Areas\tMetadata Contact: Geospatial Services Internal Custodian: Kathryn Borrowdale, Marine Team SW Opps\tWater Framework Directive seagrass area data. This data was mapped by tracking the seagrass bed using a hand held GPS device either by foot or by hovercraft\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-09-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:10:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18577\tNRW_DS121942\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.842Z\t957\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAir Photos (Geomatics)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393432\tX:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Geomatics air photos  for data related to 2009 - 2017\tKeith Jones - GIS and Spatial Data team\tThis data collation was commissioned to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) as an enduring service by Environment Agency (EA) using Geomatics, the surveying arm to the EA.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18584\tNRW_DS122027\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.920Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNationally Important Biotopes: Intertidal Habitats\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution Statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.  Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right [2009]\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303237\tx:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Nationaly_Important_Biotopes\\Intertidal  Habitats 2009.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team  Internal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tNationally Important Biotopes is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of intertidal nationally important biotopes across Wales. A biotope is defined as the combination of abiotive habitat and its associated community of species. It can be define at a variety of scales (with related corresponding defress of similarity) and should be a regularly occuring association to justify its inclusion within a classification system. Biotopes becoming nationally important when they become or are generally geographically restricted on a UK wide basis. Highly rated examples of nationally and internationally important biotopes should be notified features within Site of Special Scienitific Interest (SSSIs). In the UK Biotopes types include chalk biotopes, soft, piddock bored biotopes, extremely exposed fucoid biotopes, Tide-swept algal biotopes, sand-influence rock biotopes, extreme shelter biotopes, muddy gravel biotopes, rockpool biotopes, under-boulder biotopes, cave and overhang biotopes, sublittoral fringe surge gully biotopes and seagrass biotopes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:32:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18572\tNRW_DS122028\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.515Z\t970\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTerrestrial Broad Habitats Wales\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303238\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Broad Habitat Combined BAP Habitats.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Broad Habitat Combined BAP Habitats Point.lyr\tGIS and Spatail Data Team  Liz Howe, Jon Rothwell, Stuart Smith, Pete Jones\tBroad habitats are a spatial datasets derived from Phase 1 Terrestrial habitat and species surveys conducted across Wales. Board habitats classifies / defines habitats as ecologically integrated units at a landscape scale, rather than seeing habitats as simply distinct types of vegetation. These broad habitats were classified in responsible to the convention on Biological Diversity. In Wales there are 36 broad habitats and the terrestrial habitats are as follows: Broad leaved and yes, planted coniferous woodland, native pine woodland, lowland wood pastures and parkland, boundary features, arable, improved grassland, unimproved neutral grassland, acid grassland, calcareous grassland, lowland heathland, grazing marsh, fens carr marsh swamp and reed bed, lowland raised bog, standing open water, rivers and streams, canals, montane (alpine and subalpine types) upland heathland and blanket bog.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-07-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18574\tNRW_DS122093\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.516Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTerrestrial Mammals\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303933\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\tLiz Halliwell GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis spatial dataset details the extent and location of a variety of terrestrial mammals across Wales.\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18571\tNRW_DS122094\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.255Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Non Native Species Spatial Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303934\tx:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\  NB MORE UP TO DATE DATA IS NOW AVAILABLE AT THIS LOCATION\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNB THIS DATA SET HAS BEEN REPLACED BY A MORE UP TO DATE DATA SET (116352) N.B. 116352 is not a replacement as it specifically documents data exyracted  This is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location on marine non native species across Wales. These have been split out into invasive, low risk, other and potential. This data was collected from a variety of ad hoc surveys conducted by Natural Resource Wales staff or contractors.\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:23:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18580\tNRW_DS122098\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.914Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInvasive Non Native Species Records obtained from the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Atlas\t© CNC/NRW NRW, Amphibian and Reptiles Conservation Trust, Biodiversity Information Service for Powys and Brecon Beacon National Park, Biological Records Centre, Botanical Society of the British Isles, Bristol Regional Enivronmental Records Centre, Conchological Society of Great Britain and Ireland, Environment Agency, Marine Conservation Society, Marine Biological Association, Merseyside BioBank, Nature Locator, Pond Conservation, Shire Group of Internal Drainage Boards, Shropshire Ecological Data Network, West Wales Biodiversity Information Centre and the Worcestershire Biological Records Centre.    NRW data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. NRW does NOT grant permission to disseminate or re-use any data that is not labelled as NRW ownership.  Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303938\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Invasive Non Native Species.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jennie Jones, Specialist Advisor, Non-Native Species  GIS and Spatial Data Team       This dataset needs updating with this search... https://wales-species-inns.nbnatlas.org/?\u0026amp;fq\u003dlistMembership_m_s:%22NRW Sustainable Management of Natural Resources (SMNR)%22#tabs-1\tThis data collation contains a variety of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) data and 3rd party owned data and has been obtained from the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Atlas.\tVarious including volunteer recorders-Various-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:48.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18582\tNRW_DS122099\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.919Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNon Native Species: New Zealand mud snail (Potamopyrgus antipodarum)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303939\tx:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Other Potamopyrgus Antipodarum.lyr\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Other Potamopyrgus Antipodarum.lyr\tThe is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of the New Zealand mud snail Potamopyrgus Antipodarum. Potamopyrgus antipodarum is an aquatic snail of the family Hydrobiidae.\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18579\tNRW_DS122101\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.917Z\t573\tValid\tEditable\tHabitats Directive: Article 17 Terrestrial Habitats Cycle 1 (2007)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313031\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2007\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.   Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset collated as part of the Habitats Directive reporting cycle 1 for Article 17 terrestrial habitats. Article 17 required a report to be sent to the European Commision every 6 years follwoing an agreed format. JNCC collate theses on behalf of the UK Country Agencies, and this data was sent to JNCC for the collation.   Article 17 reports are an assessment of conservation status of the habitat and species targetted by the directive. The assessment is made based on information on status and trends of species populations or habitats and on information on main pressures and threats. An important component of the Article 17 report is a map of habitat or species distribution mapped in 10x10 km grid.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T13:55:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18583\tNRW_DS122102\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.860Z\t405\tValid\tEditable\tHabitats Directive: Article 17 Terrestrial Habitats Cycle 2 (2012)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313032\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2012\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.  Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset collated as part of the Habitats Directive reporting cycle 2 for Article 17 terrestrial habitats. Article 17 required a report to be sent to the European Commision every 6 years follwoing an agreed format. JNCC collate theses on behalf of the UK Country Agencies, and this data was sent to JNCC for the collation. Article 17 reports are an assessment of conservation status of the habitat and species targetted by the directive. The assessment is made based on information on status and trends of species populations or habitats and on information on main pressures and threats. An important component of the Article 17 report is a map of habitat or species distribution mapped in 10x10 km grid.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-12-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:11:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18585\tNRW_DS122146\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.980Z\t688\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS) Sampling Points\t© CNC/NRW and Environment Agency Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.  © Environment Agency.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313436\t[A]x:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\River Habitat Survey.lyr  [B] Original data held on Wiski-Kieco  Update from Mel Lacan, August 2021: \\When we have KiECo RHS up and running fully (and calculating HQA Class) then I would like to update this layer and make it more widely available with some comms around RHS intranet page to be produced (so not ready yet).\"\tInternal Custodian: Melissa Lacan, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, SE Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  GIS and Spatial Data Team\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS) is Natural Resources Wales standard for collecting data on the physical character and quality of river habitats across the Wales. This is a spatail dataset detailing the location of sampling points from around Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Environment Agency (EA)\t2009-09-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-19T15:56:59.417Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18573\tNRW_DS122188\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.515Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGlastir Woodland Management Networks\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313838\tx:\\Biological\\Glastir Woodland Management Networks\\Woodland Primary Cores.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Glastir Woodland Management Networks\\Woodland Primary Networks.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Glastir Woodland Management Networks\\Woodland Secondary Cores.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Glastir Woodland Management Networks\\Woodland Secondary Networks.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Glastir Woodland Management Networks\\Woodland Tertiary Networks.lyr\tJim Latham is the internal contact who knows about this dataset.  GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThese were created by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of assess agricultural woodland planting and habitat management teams. This dataset was derived from Phase 1 and created by Forest Research as part of a contract for Countryside Council for Wales. This dataset is now static, but has been and is used for guiding the Glastir Woodland Managemnet Schemes. These datasets have been used to assess where planting woodladn could occur, with those areas closer to the primary core networks scoring higher than those near the tertiary networks. This helped to identify opportunities for planting.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:29:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18568\tNRW_DS122189\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.390Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGlastir Woodland Contract Managers Area\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313839\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Glastir Woodland Contract Managers Area.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team - Keith Harri Jones\tadmin boundary set up by NRW for the woodland contract managers NRW owned - subject to change with OD\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:29:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18569\tNRW_DS122191\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.300Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForestry Local Area Managers\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313931\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\FCW Local Area Managers.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis dataset orginated from Forestry Commission Wales - still being used but in need of an update. This is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Forestry local area team boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for foresty managed teams.  This dataset defines the forestry team boundaries and the 14 team locations within those areas.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18566\tNRW_DS122192\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.163Z\t8\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHydrological Tools Datasets\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313932\tx:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro Rasters.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_Adjoint_Catchments.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_Catchments.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_Drainage_Lines.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_DRN.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_DRN_Nodes.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_Extent.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Terrain_5m_Terrain.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydrological Models.lyr\tBen Anscombe,  GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a collation of datasets used from hydrological modelling within Natural Resources Wales. This collation is a mixture of terrain, raster and vector data which allows more advanced modelling to occur around the hydrology of a catchment and watershed.   These datasets were created by Natural Resources Wales for use in the following tools:   Generic Hydro Tools: Used to model the path of raindrops as part of watershed analyses.  Hydropower Tools: used for hydro developments to model the effects of water abstractions and input on flow speed and directions, and therefore the possible effects this might have on species that are effected by these changes to the watercourse.   This helps users build a 3D profile and therefore model different scenarios.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18578\tNRW_DS122202\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.839Z\t954\tValid\tEditable\tWales\u0027 grey seal photo-identification database: EIRPHOT (1992-2016)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Those wishing to publish papers and academic research based on the data, should cite the report as per the information warning\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323032\tReports and outputs held in the DMS.  Database archived as project 480, media 1557.  Listed on the MS Access database plan to move to new NRW MS access storage/transactional area or MUP.\tTom Stringell, Senior Marine Mammal Ecologist\tThis dataset is a catalogue of seal images that were collected as part of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW) seal monitoring and evidence work.   Images were also provided by volunteers, partners, organisations and members of the public through collaboration with CCW/NRW. The images are of heads, necks and flanks of grey seals (mostly female) photographed at seal haul out sites around the Celtic and Irish Seas from 1992 to 2016 and show the pelage markings of seals that can be individually recognised/identified from subsequent photographic encounters – photo-identification (Capture, Mark, Recapture).   The database contains over 17,000 photographic images of more than 9,000 grey seals. Photo-ID allows individual seals to be captured through time and space and analyses of the data provide insights into site fidelity and associations, movements and connectivity, and longevity/survival.  This entry supersedes metadata entry 115548 which was created in 2013.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-10-30\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2609\t2025-11-12T11:46:10.030Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18567\tNRW_DS122205\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.277Z\t810\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNewborough Warren and Llanddwyn Island Wind data (2018-present)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323035\tData can be downloaded from: www.minimet.co.uk.  NRW users will need to contact Suzie Jackson for Website login details and FTP login details.    Data can be accessed from this location on DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mcps/Evidence%20%20Data/Wind%20data/Wind%20Data%202017-2023?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1   An NRW Evidence report is in prep (as of May 2023) to describe this dataset, link to be added when available.\tInternal Custodian: Suzie Jackson, Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes AWMA, Marine and Coastal Policy and Planning       The actual location of the Weather Station has been removed for safety purposes.  Location is 53.136004, -4.4135866 (Pilots Cottages).  The data has been confirmed as Open data and added to the NRW Open data list.\tWind data collected at Llanddwyn Island, Newborough. Data is available from January 2018 to present. The data provides daily 30 minute instantaneous averaged wind speed and direction.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:14:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18576\tNRW_DS122291\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.688Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tArticle 17 Terrestrial Habitat Features 2018\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323931\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2018\tInternal Custodian 1: Heather Lewis, Lead Specialist Advisor -Coastal Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Internal Custodian 2: Julie Creer, Lead Specialist Advisor – Coastal Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tData collected for Article 17, Habitats Directive reporting 2018. Collection of Point and polygon datasets. This is mapped to standards required by Article 17. The data has been produced through manual survey, referencing relevant documentation and third party data.  101 files, containing distribution and extent data for Article 17, Habitats Directive 2018 reporting:   Art17 2018 H1210 Annual Vegetation of Drift Lines 10km.lyr A rt17 2018 H1210 Annual Vegetation of Drift Lines.lyr  Art17 2018 H1220 Perennial Vegetation Stony Banks.lyr  Art17 2018 H1230 Vegetated Sea Cliffs 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H1230 Vegetated sea cliffs.lyr  Art17 2018 H1310 Salicornia 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H1310 Salicornia.lyr  Art17 2018 H1330 Atlantic Salt Meadow.lyr  Art17 2018 H1330 Atlantic Salt Meadows 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H1420 Mediterranea Saltmarsh Scrub.lyr  Art17 2018 H1420 Mediterranean Saltmarsh Scrub10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H2110 Embryonic Shifting Dunes 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H2110 Embryonic Shifting Dunes.lyr  Art17 2018 H2120 Shifting Dunes with Marram 10km.lyr A rt17 2018 H2120 Shifting Dunes with Marram.lyr  Art17 2018 H2130 Fixed Dunes with Herbaceous Veg 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H2130 Fixed Dunes with Herbaceous Veg.lyr  Art17 2018 H2150 Decalcified Fixed Dunes 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H2150 Decalcified Fixed Dunes.lyr  Art17 2018 H2170 Dunes with Salix.lyr  Art17 2018 H2170 Dunes with Salix 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H2190 Humid Dune 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H2190 Humid dunes.lyr  Art17 2018 H3130 Oligotrophic to Mesotrophic Standing Waters 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H3130 Oligotrophic to Mesotrophic Standing Waters.lyr  Art17 2018 H3140 Hard Oligo Mesotrophic Water Point.lyr  Art17 2018 H3140 Hard Oligo Mesotrophic Waters with Chara Calcium 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H3150 Natural Eutrophic Lakes 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H3150 Natural Eutrophic Lakes Point.lyr  Art17 2018 H3160 Natural Dystrophic Lakes 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H3180 Turloughs 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H3260 Watercourses with Ranunculion Rivers 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H4010 Wet Heath with Cross Leaved Heath 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H4030 Dry Heaths 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H4060 Alpine \u0026amp; Subalpine Heaths 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H5130 Juniper on Heaths or Calcareous Grasslands 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H6130 Calaminarian Grassland 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H6130 Calaminarian Grassland.lyr  Art17 2018 H6130 Calaminarian Grasslands point.lyr  Art172018 H6150 Siliceous Alpine and Boreal Grasslands 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H6170 Alpine \u0026amp; Subalpine Calcareous Grasslands 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H6210 Semi Natural Dry Grasslands 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H6210 Seminaturaldry grasslands.lyr  Art17 2018 H6410 Molinia Meadows 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H6430 Hydrophilious Tall Herb Communities 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H6510 Lowland Hay Meadows 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7110 Active Raised Bogs 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7110 Active Raised Bogs.lyr  Art17 2018 H7120 Degraded Raised Bog 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7130 Blanket Bog 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7140 Very Wet Mires 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7150 Depressions on Peat Substrates 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7210 Calcareous Fen Calcium Rich Fen 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7220Petrifying and Hardwater Springs Depositing Lime 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7230 Alkaline Fen Calcium Rich Springwater 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7230 Alkaline Fen Calcium Rich Springwater.lyr Art17 2018 H7240 Alpine Pioneer Formations 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H8110 Siliceous and Acidic Scree 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H8120 Calcareous and Calcshist Screes Base Rich Scree 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H8210 Calcareous Rocky Slopes 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H8220 Siliceous Rocky Slopes 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H8240 Limestone Pavements 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H8310 Caves Not Open to the Public 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H9120 Beech Forests on Acid Soils 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H9130 Beech Forests on Neutral to Rich Soils 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H9180 Tilio AcerionMixed Woodland 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H91A0 Western Acidic Oak Woodland 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H91D0 Bog Woodland 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H91E0 Alluvial Forest Alder10km.lyr ...\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-12-19\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:13:32.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18565\tNRW_DS122293\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.096Z\t703\tValid\tEditable\tHabitats Directive: Article 17 Terrestrial Habitats Cycle 3 (2018)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323933\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2018\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.  Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset collated as part of the Habitats Directive reporting cycle 3 for Article 17 terrestrial habitats. Article 17 required a report to be sent to the European Commission every 6 years following an agreed format. JNCC collate theses on behalf of the UK Country Agencies, and this data was sent to JNCC for the collation.   Article 17 reports are an assessment of conservation status of the habitat and species targeted by the directive. The assessment is made based on information on status and trends of species populations or habitats and on information on main pressures and threats. An important component of the Article 17 report is a map of habitat or species distribution mapped in 10x10 km grid.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-02-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:43:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18562\tNRW_DS122295\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.876Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status of the blackfly Simulium morsitans on the Afon Teifi at Cors Caron National Nature Reserve - 2018\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323935\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID)\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThe Afon Teifi at Cors Caron supports the only recently recorded UK population of the blackfly Simulium morsitans Edwards (Diptera: Simuliidae). The true extent of its UK distribution and status remain uncertain although Simuliidae are increasingly identified to species level during routine river quality monitoring.   This survey shows the results of samples and observations of simuliid larvae and pupae collected in early August 2018 and compares the results with a similar survey undertaken in 2008.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:05:00.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18561\tNRW_DS122304\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.849Z\t8\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTerrestrial Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) 1km Distribution Maps for Priority Habitats\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232333034\tAll these distrubutions start with: X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\LBAP_Priority_Habitat_\tInternal Custodian Pete Jones but Stuart Smith is also very helpful.  Metadata: GIS Team\t*** This data is no longer accessible, therefore this metadata is under review 07/08/2024 ****  Priority Habitats of Wales 1km squared distribution maps show the area totals for each habitat between 2002 and 2003. Priority habitats of Wales is mainly based on the combined results of the Lowland Habitat Survey of Wales, and the Welsh Upland Vegetation Survey undertaken by the Nature Conservancy Council and subsequently the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) between 1979 and 1997. This data are the results from these surveys which have been compiled and provides information on habitat extent for individual 1 km squares across the entire land of Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:59:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18563\tNRW_DS122387\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.864Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Powysland English Watercourses\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232333837\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\PowysLand\\IDD Powsyland English Watercourses.lyr\tSpatial Data Team, Louise Harris\tWatercourses within the English section of Powysland Internal Drainage Board now administered by Hoople. These watercourses drain into Powysland IDD watercourses within Wales. Mapped in linear form in this vector dataset. Data collected and mapped for the purpose of water level management, flood defence maintenance and planning operations/work programming.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:27:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18564\tNRW_DS122401\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.874Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAn assessment of the current condition of the saproxylic invertebrate assemblage at Castell y Waun a’i Barcdir/Chirk Castle and Parkland SSSI in 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343031\tData is stored in Recorder 6 as part of the Welsh Invertebrate Database\t\tMike Howe\u0027s evidence reports:   Alexander, K.N.A. 2019. An assessment of the current condition of the saproxylic invertebrate assemblage at Castell y Waun a’i Barcdir/Chirk Castle and Parkland SSSI in 2018. NRW Evidence Report No: 317, XXpp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18560\tNRW_DS122417\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.869Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343137\t\t\tAlexander, K.N.A. 2019. Saproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2018. NRW Evidence Report No: 320, xxpp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.  Contact Mike Howe / Rob Bacon for metadata details\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18559\tNRW_DS122418\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.864Z\t1546\tValid\tEditable\tEnvironment (Wales) Act Section 7 Terrestrial Habitats of Principal Importance\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343138\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\\tPete Jones is the custodian of this - although Stuart Smith is also a really good contact.  GIS and Spatial Data Team deal with the updates\tUnder the Environment (Wales) Act (2016), Section 7 requires biodiversity lists to be produced. These lists include types of habitats which are of \"Principal Importance\" for the purpose of maintain and enhancing biodiversity in relation to Wales. This list supersedes the duty in Section 42 of the NERC Act 2006. This dataset details the extent and location of those terrestrial habitats classed of \"Principal Importance\" in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-06-17\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3604/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_terrestrial_sections_7_habitats\t2025-12-19T12:09:03.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18557\tNRW_DS122445\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.555Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tScarce Yellow Sally stonefly Isogenus nubecula, River Dee 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343435\tdata should be entered into Recorder 6 - Mike to confirm R6 SurveyID\t\tthis metadata relates to:  Davy-Bowker, J., Mauvisseau, Q., Hammett M., Sweet, M. \u0026amp; Troth, C. 2019. A survey of the River Dee for the Scarce Yellow Sally stonefly Isogenus nubecula in spring 2018. NRW Evidence Report No. 362  Contact Mike Howe for metadata proforma\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:23:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18558\tNRW_DS122446\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.580Z\t1032\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Ecological Data held in WISKI (KiEco)\t© CNC/NRW Sensitive data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.  Non-sensitive information may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright.   It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343436\tThis module sits within WISKI - KiECO\tMetadata contact: Data Services Team  Data custodians and SME’s (Subject Matter Experts) Mel Lacan (RHS), Bianca Bacchi (Marine), Paul Hyatt (Fish) and Graham Rutt (Freshwater Ecology)\tSome of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) core ecology datasets are held in the WISKI Kisters Ecology (KiEco). KiEco provides a central repository for some of NRW\u0027s ecological monitoring data. The database holdings include data on fish species, River Habitat Surveys, invasive species, marine benthic species and habitat monitoring, and freshwater ecology. KiEco was developed in NRW to take over the required functions of the legacy Environment Agency Wales (EAW) databases, such as Biosys National Fish Population Database (NFPD) and Ecosys.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:49.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18556\tNRW_DS122451\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.464Z\t766\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Blocks\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343531\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Blocks.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Primary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan Secondary Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tWithin each Operational Area, Woodland is initially divided into Forest blocks, these are usually a specific number per woodland blocks. Sometimes very large areas of woodland are broken down into several blocks for management purposes. These geographic blocks are used to identify distinct groups of woodland within an operational area. Planning Staff use this block information to produce production forecasts at block Level. In 2004 Blocks were renamed Design Plan Units, but Blocks numbers are still used in Forester.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:27:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18597\tNRW_DS122453\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.432Z\t604\tValid\tEditable\tEnvironment (Wales) Act Section 7 and OSPAR: Marine Habitats\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Derived in part from data provided under licence by the British Geological Survey © NERC The non restricted (published) data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Sensitive data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release and may be requested. Sensitive data can be requested and may be provided under licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343533\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\tGIS and Spatial Data Team  Internal Custodian: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team       There are two data layers  Oyster Beds Points and Polygones that are not included in the open data download.\tUnder the Environment (Wales) Act (2016), Section 7 requires biodiversity lists to be produced. These lists include types of habitats which are of \u0027Principal Importance\u0027 for the purpose of maintain and enhancing biodiversity in relation to Wales. This list supersedes the duty in Section 42 of the NERC Act 2006. In addition to this marine habitats can be protected under the OSPAR Convention, which has established a list of \u0027threatened and/or declining species and habitats\u0027 in the North-East Atlantic.   This spatial dataset details the extent and location of those marine habitats classed of \u0027Principal Importance\u0027 under Section 7 and are regarded as \u0027threatened or declining\u0027 under OSPAR in Wales.   Habitats include:  Carbonate Reefs, Estuarine Rock, Honeycomb Worm Sabellaria alveolata, Intertidal Underboulder, Mud habitats in deep water, Musculus Discors Green crenella Beds, Blue Mussel Beds (listed under OSPAR aswell), Fragile Sponge and Anthozoans (listed under OSPAR aswell), Horse Mussel Modiolus Bed (listed under OSPAR aswell), Intertidal Mudflats (listed under OSPAR aswell), Maerl Beds live and dead (listed under OSPAR aswell), Sabellaria spinulosa reef (listed under OSPAR aswell), Saline Lagoons (listed under OSPAR aswell), Saltmarsh,  Seagrass Beds (listed under OSPAR aswell), Seapens And Burrowing Megafauna (listed under OSPAR aswell), Peat Clay Exposures (listed under OSPAR aswell), Sheltered Muddy Gravel (listed under OSPAR aswell), Subtidal Mix Mud Sediments (listed under OSPAR aswell), Tide Swept Channels (listed under OSPAR aswell), Oyster Beds (OSPAR only).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/884/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:MarineBAPOSPARHabitats\t2025-12-01T11:35:21.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18611\tNRW_DS122454\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.800Z\t952\tValid\tEditable\tEnvironment (Wales) Act Section 7 and OSPAR: Marine Species\t© CNC/NRW 2007. Published and non- restricted data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343534\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\Section 7 Species\tInternal Custodian 1: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Internal Custodian 2: Dave Tavner, Specialist Advisor – Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Mapping, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tUnder the Environment (Wales) Act (2016), Section 7 requires biodiversity lists to be produced. These lists include types of species which are of \u0027Principal Importance\u0027 for the purpose of maintain and enhancing biodiversity in relation to Wales. This list supersedes the duty in Section 42 of the NERC Act 2006. In addition to this marine species can be protected under the OSPAR Convention.    The OSPAR convention has established a list of \u0027threatened and/or declining species and habitats\u0027 in the North-East Atlantic. This spatial dataset details the location of those marine species classed of \u0027Principal Importance\u0027 under Section 7 and are regarded as \u0027threatened or declining\u0027 under OSPAR in Wales. These species include: Alkmaria Romijni tentacled lagoon worm, Anotrichium barbatum bearded red seaweed, Cruoria Cruoriiformis, Edwardsia Timida burrowing anemone, Eunicella Verrucosa pink sea fan,  Grateloupia Dermocorynus montagnei, Haliclystus auricula stalked jellyfish, Hippocampus sp., Lithothamnion coralloides Coral maerl, Lucernariopsis Campanulata stalked jellyfish, Arctica Islandica Ocean Quahog, Ostrea Edulis, Padina Pavonica, Palinurus Elephas Crawfish, Phymatolithon calcareum common maerl and Tenellia Adspersa.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-07-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/885/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:MarineBAPSpeciesInWales\t2025-12-19T09:54:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18596\tNRW_DS122455\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.421Z\t759\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Management Areas\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343535\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Management Area.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Primary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan Secondary: Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThe Management Layer is the actual area of land NRW manages, this can be larger than the legal boundary, this is because we manage land that is not our own such as extra land or Partnership land.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-01-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18613\tNRW_DS122456\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.815Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Quarries\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343536\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest quarries.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset contains Quarries on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any quarry data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured quarry information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-12-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18603\tNRW_DS122460\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.519Z\t897\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: River Macroinvertebrates\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343630\tData Held in KiEco.   A copy of the latest upload file is held on the Z:Drive - Z:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\Data Mgmt\\Data Services Data Publishing Area\\NBN Data\\NRW_DS122460_RiverMacroInvertebrates\tInternal Custodian:  Jennifer Owen Senior Officer, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer • Environmental Assessment and Advice,  Or Mel Lacan Senior Officer, Environmental Assessment • South East EAAT\tFreshwater macroinvertebrates are used as an indicator of water quality and river flow requirements due to the relative immobility and abundance. This dataset is made up of the species abundance values from the freshwater river macroinvertebrates surveys conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of river water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  A geospatial version of this data is available in NRW_DS161278.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-03-13-2025-09-02\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr2116\t2025-12-03T10:14:02.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18614\tNRW_DS122461\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.876Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Mine Area\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343631\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest mine area.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset contains Mine Areas on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any Mine Area data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured Mine Area information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-12-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18604\tNRW_DS122463\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.521Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Mineshafts\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343633\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest mineshafts.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset contains Mineshafts on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any Mineshafts data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured Mineshafts information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-12-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:32:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18615\tNRW_DS122464\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.908Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Third Party Access\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343634\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest third party access.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tThis dataset contains Third Party Access on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any Third Party Access data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured Third Party Access information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-12-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18599\tNRW_DS122469\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.424Z\t885\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey of the invertebrate fauna of Marford Quarry in 2018, with a particular focus on aculeates (bees, wasps and ants).\t© CNC/NRW and North Wales Wildlife Trust. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343639\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive. Data is due to be entered into the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID) held in Recorder 6\tDr Mike Howe, Lead specialist advisor; terrestrial habitats and species       Data is due to be entered into the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID) held in Recorder 6 by Mike Howe, but this is not yet complete. There is no anticipated ate for input at this time.\tFollowing management work in Marford Quarry to increase the amount of bare sand habitat for psammophilic insects, a survey in 2018 recorded 118 species of aculeate bees, wasps and ants including 35 species new to the site.   The cumulative aculeate list now stands at 171 species, with 81 bees, 80 wasps and 10 ants, 80 wasps, making Marford Quarry one of the most important site in Wales for this taxonomic group. The survey also recorded four new psammophilic aculeates - Anoplius infuscatus, Arachnospila wesmaeli, Coelioxys conoidea and Coelioxys inermis - and the psammophilic ground beetle Harpalus tardus at its only Welsh inland locality. The overall total for the quarry now stands at 31 psammophilic species including 26 aculeates, two beetles and three flies. Six new dead-wood nesting aculeates were found – Ancistrocerus gazella, Ancistrocerus scoticus, Megachile ligniseca, Psenulus pallipes, Stigmus solskyi and the Hornet Vespa crabro – resulting in a cumulative total of 43 species (42 aculeates and one beetle).   Both the psammophilic faunal assemblage and the dead-wood assemblage are considered to be currently in favourable condition although there is an urgent need to provide more deadwood habitat which is at a premium. It is also important to maintain early-successional sandy habitats by a programme of regular excavation work.   The finding of the solitary wasp Gorytes laticinctus in Marford Quarry is arguably the most significant record in the 2018 survey given its UK distribution and indicates a major range extension. Other important records were made of other insects including the tachinid flies Freraea gagatea (new to Wales), Gymnosoma rotundatum and Wagneria gagatea (both at 2nd Welsh localities) and Leucostoma simplex (new to north Wales), the Alder Leaf Beetle Agelastica alni (otherwise known from Fagl Lane Quarry), and the parasitic wasps Sphecophaga vesparum and Haltichella rufipes (both new to Wales).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-North Wales Wildlife Trust\t2019-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:31:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18605\tNRW_DS122565\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.743Z\t484\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDune Rejuvenation Trials - Newboorough Warren \u0026amp; Dunes NNR, Kenfig Dunes NNR, Merthyr Mawr Dunes NNR - Assessment 2016\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232353635\tArchived as Project 481, Media 1558 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media1501to1600\\Media1558)\tInternal Custodian: Emmer Litt, Specialist Advisor - Marine and Coastal Physical Processes, All Wales Advice Team       This entry is related to metadata 114691, 115843, 115776, 115840\tTrial dune rejuvenation works have been undertaken at Kenfig, Merthyr Mawr and Newborough during the winters of 2011-12 to 2014-15, with a hiatus in 2013-14 when work was undertaken only at Merthyr Mawr Warren. The data related to a geomorphological assessment of the effectiveness of these rejuvenation works which will inform the planning of further works and includes UAV data, GPS data, LIDAR DEMs and GIS outputs.  The accompanying report provides a summary of changes in the extent of bare sand and mobile dunes at each site, discusses the factors which have resulted in differences in the impact between and within sites, and comments on the relative merits of the methods which have been used to monitor geomorphological change. Proposals are also made for further intervention works at each of the sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:33:09.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18609\tNRW_DS122580\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.745Z\t150\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Lake Macro-invertebrates Taxa Data\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232353830\tData Held in Wiski-KiEco - a PowerBI Report has been created to export this data\tInternal Custodian: Jennifer Owen, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, NW Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team          Pending finalisation of extract process for NBN / data input into WISKI\tFreshwater marco-invertebrates are used as an indicator of water quality requirements due to the relative immobility and abundance. This dataset is made up of the species abundance values from the freshwater lake macro-invertebrates surveys conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of lake water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-01-01-2025-09-02\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr3315\t2025-12-01T17:30:28.059Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18612\tNRW_DS122590\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.797Z\t339\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey of Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) at Welsh dredge disposal sites:  Milford Haven and the Severn Estuary 2018\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232353930\tDMS\tBen Wray\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) contracted Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd. (Marine EcoSol) to analyse species and habitats surveyed in several dredge disposal sites around Wales (Milford Haven, the Severn Estuary, Swansea Bay Outer and Holyhead Deed), and undertake a data and literature review to discuss the potential for marine Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) transfer via dredge disposal pathways. The purpose of the surveys was to obtain information to inform decision making to minimise further spread of non-native species from dredge disposal.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd\t2018-09-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:50.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18606\tNRW_DS122600\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.767Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNDF Key hotspots of biodiversity \u0026amp; ecological connectivity\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232363030\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\Key hotspots of biodiversity \u0026amp; ecological connectivity.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tThis data shows nationally important ecological networks, where connectivity is high across a range of habitats between biodiversity hotspots. High connectivity means that ecosystems within these areas are relatively resilient to environmental changes, such as climate change, and also have the capacity to help build ecosystem resilience more widely.  It is important to recognise these ecologically important area, both with a view to maintaining the existing habitats that contribute to the networks and also identify opportunities to improve them through targeted action such as habitat restoration and improved landscape management. Whilst the map gives a national overview, it is based on more detailed data of habitat networks that need to be considered for practical implementation, as the finer scale features in the lowland areas are not visible at the national scale.  Maps of ecological networks provide a practical means of considering connectivity alongside development.  Under the Environment (Wales) Act 2016 public authorities across Wales have a duty to promote ecosystem resilience in the exercise of their functions. This layer can used to help inform this work, by demonstrating which broad areas contain important and diverse habitat networks that are likely to benefit from careful management in order to maintain and enhance habitat connectivity and therefore the resilience of the habitats they support. This layer shows summary data at a national level which is made up of more detailed maps that are available to be considered at whatever scale is appropriate.  Ecological connectivity operates at the national as well as local level. This map considers the broadest scale and presents a national framework of ecologically resilient networks and the most important biodiversity hotspots. This map could be used at a strategic level to identify areas which are likely to benefit from careful management to maintain and enhance ecological connectivity and therefore the resilience of the habitats they support. Implementation on the ground is likely to require much finer scale information, as at a local level other networks and ecological hotspots not visible at a national scale will high importance.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:47:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18600\tNRW_DS122690\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.414Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGeomatics AP index\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232363930\tx:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Air photos index\\Geomatics AP index 2018.lyr x:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Air photos index\\Geomatics AP index 2021.lyr\tKeith Jones\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-23T09:26:40.000Z\tKeith Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18593\tNRW_DS122707\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.875Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNDF Hotspots for generating ecosystem servcies that deliver public goods\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373037\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\Hotspots for generating ecosystem services that deliver public goods.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tThis dataset shows areas in Wales that provide a large number of ecosystem service benefits. The services selected to be shown in this map were chosen because of their relevance to development planning and because they may not be immediately apparent in the same way as for example, agricultural productivity. The following services were chosen; flood regulation, water quality, vegetation carbon, soil carbon and landscape aesthetics. This dataset is a useful high level representation of significant areas for ecosystem service generation.  When interpreting this data it needs to be borne in mind that areas that are not highlighted may still make an important contribution to ecosystem services that were not included in the analysis such as agriculture.  The ecosystem services used to create this dataset deal with a number of key issues that have been identified as important in planning policy, including understanding environmental risks, addressing landscape change and adapting to the effects of climate change.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:46:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18610\tNRW_DS122717\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.802Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRecreation Places to Go Promoted Routes\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and database right. Not all routes are for publication. Some are jointly managed routes with charities/external parties. Data is in the process of continual update and may require a status check with the Data and Information Advisor (Recreation) and/or the site managers to confirm data is to a required accuracy depending on use requirements. Data should not be converted to .gpx files  Copyright statements need to reference OS copyright as some of the data has been digitised using the OS basemapping as a guide.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373137\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Recreation Places to Go Promoted Routes.lyr\tHeather Crump, Data and Information Advisor (Recreation), Estate Recreation Planning Team\tVectors (lines) depicting waymarked/promoted recreation routes managed or co-managed by NRW. The ‘ROUTE_DESCRIPTION’ field should be queried in order to delineate the date a route was last updated/confirmed and by whom. Information includes route name, length, direction, grade, type and the NNR or forest the route is listed against. Dataset is a resource for promotional material and visualisation rather than management. Management data associated with recreation routes is stored separately in the Forester database.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-19\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:47:09.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18589\tNRW_DS122722\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.835Z\t367\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRecreation Places to Go\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373232\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Recreation Places to Go.lyr\tCustodian: Heather Crump, Advisor (2) Recreation Content, Estates Recreation Planning Team\tVectors (points) depicting the location of managed or co-managed sites by NRW which are listed on the Recreation Places to Go external map browser.  Information includes site name, grid reference, subtitle and facilities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-04-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:16:25.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18607\tNRW_DS122760\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.739Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNDF Hotspots of resilient ecosystem service provision\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373630\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\Hotspots of resilient ecosystem service provision.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tThis dataset shows an overview of where areas with hotspots of ecological networks, important for maintaining biodiversity into the future, spatially coincide with areas delivering a number of key ecosystem services form the list considered.   The highest values are areas that are delivering a wide range of ecosystem services and are also significant in their provision of biodiversity and ecological networks. These areas therefore are hotspots for nature and areas which should be carefully managed to enhance their ecological and ecosystem service functions.  This dataset deals with a number of key issues that have been identified as important in planning policy, including understanding risks, addressing landscape change and adapting to the effects of climate change. Under the environment Act (Wales) 2016 public authorities across Wales also have a duty to promote ecosystem resilience in the exercise of their functions.  This dataset can help to inform this work at both a national and local scale by identifying the areas that are important habitat networks which also provide a range of ecosystem services.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18608\tNRW_DS122762\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.751Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNDF Greenspace Provision in Main Urban Areas\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373632\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\NDF Greenspace Provision in Main Urban Areas.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tThis dataset shows areas where access to greenspace is limited, either because very little greenspace is available in the area to because access to greenspace is difficult. The larger the circle the more greenspace is available in the area, relative to the size of settlement. Accessibility of greenspace is shown in colour, with green meaning that greenspace is easily accessible and red that most people need to walk far to reach it.   This dataset can be sued to identify areas with a significant shortfall in the provision and accessibility of greenspace. While addressing these types of issues is arguably best done at the local planning level, this dataset gives a national overview in areas where greenspace provision is an issue, this information could be used to steer development away from existing greenspace and/or identify areas where greenspace provision could usefully be improved.   This dataset shows greenspaces and all areas that are within 400m walking distance of at least one greenspace, It also shows how high the demand for greenspace is in areas that fall outside the catchments for these greenspaces.  NRW has a number of other datasets that can be used to refine this information further for use at the local planning level.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:47:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18602\tNRW_DS122763\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.414Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNDF Opportunities to generate multiple benefits through woodland creation\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373633\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\NDF Opportunities to develop multiple benefits through woodland creation.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tThis dataset shows how many ecosystem service benefits a woodland could generate if plated at any given location. In some areas a single woodland might generate four or more ecosystem services, making the site a prime opportunity area. It is important to consider that in some cases a woodland that is only an opportunity for one or two ecosystem service can be very beneficial, if the demand for that service is very high at the site of interest. Services considered in this dataset are: opportunities that would enhance the woodland ecological network, natural flood management, water quality, provide recreation benefits or carbon sequestration.  This datasets gives an overview of how the opportunities for woodland planting are distributed nationally, which aids in achieving Wales\u0027 planting target of 10,000ha by 2020. Only opportunitites are shown that, if realised, would generate large (over 500ha) or moderately sized (over 5ha) woodlands based on the large/medium/small woodland map produced under GIS for Area Statements.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:47:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18594\tNRW_DS122765\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.853Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Chironomid Pupal Exuvial Technique (CPET)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373635\tData Held in KiEco - a PowerBI Report has been created to export this data.\tInternal Custodian:  Harriet Robinson - Data Services Team.  Tim Jones - Enivironmental Reporting Team  SMEs Graham Rutt and Mel Lacan       Pending finalisation of extract process for NBN / data input into WISKI\tChironomid are an insect family that comprises of midges. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) conducts Chironomid Pupal Exuvial Technique (CPET) surveys in lakes, rivers and canals as they provide a robust assessment of the anthropogenic impacts acting on large water bodies with minimal sampling effort due to the passive nature of its sampling strategy. This dataset details the Chironomid species records from the CPET sampling of lakes, rivers and canals conducted by NRW across Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18598\tNRW_DS122766\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.409Z\t1397\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHydrometry and Telemetry Data held in WISKI (WISKI 7)\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset in full.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373636\tAll data is stored in WISKI in the WISKI 7 Also known as WISKI Core or the Hydro and Telemetry module\tInternal Custodian:  Alison Hanson - Hydrometry and Telemetry Team Leader\tWISKI 7 holds core hydrometry and telemetry data owned by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and third parties. This data includes; River Level and Flows, Rainfall, Groundwater levels, and water temperature.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:17:58.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18601\tNRW_DS122909\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.441Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNDF Opportunities to generate multiple benefits through wetland creation\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393039\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\NDF Opportunities to generate multiple benefits through wetland creation.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tThe dataset shows how many ecosystem service benefits a wetland habitat could generate if created at a given location. In some areas, a single wetland habitat may generate multiple ecosystem services, making this site a prime opportunity area. However, it is important to recognise that in some cases a wetland that only delivers a single ecosystem service can still be beneficial, if the demand for that service is high at the site of interest. Services that were considered in this map are: opportunities to enhance the wetland network, restoration priorities for functional peat and opportunities to generate natural flood management.  This dataset gives and overview how the opportunities for wetland habitat creation are distributed nationally, which aids in identifying important opportunity spaces. Looking at the national distribution of restoration opportunities also helps to highlight that not all areas of Wales are the same, with some areas being much more suited to restoration of one broad habitat than the other.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:46:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18587\tNRW_DS122910\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.779Z\t344\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHigh tide roosts for waterbird features in Welsh Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs) 2018/19\t© CNC/NRW and British Trust for Ornithology (BTO)  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. © British Trust for Ornithology (BTO)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393130\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Wildfowl and waders High tide roosts.lyr  Future data to be available to NRW through BTO/RSPB/JNCC Wetland Bird Survey (WeBS).\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External contact: niall.burton@bto.org        Spreadsheet names were listed but have been removed as this record is focused on the .lyr GIS layer.\tThis dataset provides detailed information on the location, extent and composition of high tide waterbird roosts within Welsh protected sites (Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs)). Metadata included in the GIS shapefiles include details of: species using the roost, months in which the roost is used, tides on which the roost is used, habitat and human activities that occur in the associated Wetland Bird Survey (WeBS) count sector.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-11-14\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18591\tNRW_DS122911\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.876Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNDF Opportunities to generate multiple benefits through grassland/heathland creation\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393131\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\NDF Opportunities to generate multiple benefits through grassland_heathland creation.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tThis dataset shows how many ecosystem service benefits a woodland/grassland type habitat could generate, if created at any given location. In some areas, a single grassland/heathland type habitat might generate multiple ecosystem services, making this site a prime opportunity area. However, it is important to recognise that in some cases a grassland/ heathland that will only deliver a single ecosystem services can still be beneficial, if the demand for that services is high at the site of interest. Services that were considered for this map are: Opportunities to enhance the grassland/heathland network, accessible greenspace for recreation benefits, and surface roughness for natural flood management.  This dataset gives an overview of how the opportunities for grassland/heathland type habitat creation are distributed nationally, which aids in identifying important opportunity spaces. Looking at the national distribution of restoration opportunities also helps to highlight that not all areas of Wales are the same, with some areas being much more suited to restoration of one broad habitat than the other.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:47:12.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18590\tNRW_DS122914\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.835Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNew birds\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393134\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Birds\\New birds.lyr\t\t\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:27:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18588\tNRW_DS122915\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.780Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNew birds point\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393135\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Birds\\New birds point.lyr\t\t\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18592\tNRW_DS122916\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.832Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNightjar\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393136\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Birds\\Nightjar.lyr\t\t\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18595\tNRW_DS122919\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.875Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBarn Owl Sites Dec 2003\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393139\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Birds\\Barn Owl Sites Dec 2003.lyr\t\t\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18586\tNRW_DS122922\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.284Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCrayfish All Data\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393232\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Crayfish All Data.lyr\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species, Terrestrial Species Team\t\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:56.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18637\tNRW_DS122923\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.648Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCrayfish WFD catchments\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393233\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Crayfish WFD catchments.lyr\tNatasha Lough\t\t\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18640\tNRW_DS122948\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.845Z\t486\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales Trails Management System\t© CNC/NRW ©Local Authorities/National Park Authority NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393438\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Furniture Lines.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Furniture Points.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Issue Lines.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Issue Points.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Issue Polygons.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Route Lines.lyr\tContact:  Quentin Grimely Access Delivery Team, Sustainable Communities Gruffudd Owen, Wales Coast Path Officer (North)       The data is overwritten as it is updated along the path - so start date may not relfect the date on the dataset.  Natural Resources Wales (NRW) grant fund Local Authorities to collect this data. They input the data into the CAMs system which is hosted. NRW then collate the data on behalf of the grant funded local authorities and supply it back to them as a whole dataset via a Web Mapping Service.  At the moment we arent clear over the ownership of the data, becuase it is grant aided we think that the data is owned by the council but then given to NRW for use.\tThis dataset is related to the condition of the Wales Coast Path and National Trails in Wales (with the exception of the Pembrokeshire Coast Path, the data for which is held by Pembrokeshire Coast National Park Authority). It contains information relating to trail furniture and problems / issues encountered along each respective trail.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:49.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18632\tNRW_DS122967\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.647Z\t219\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey of distribution and extent of Didemnum vexillum in Holyhead Harbour after Storm Emma October 2018\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393637\tMarine Recorder Spreadsheet DM.  Document: EVID-301-367 Location:  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mnns/Monitoring/Didemnum/Holyhead%20Incident%20March%202018/D%20vex%20surveys/20181207%20didemnum%20vexillum%20storm%20emma%20survey%20data.xlsx?d\u003dwa1abdda14eec415d9647c66bc054e58e   Evidence \u0026gt; Marine \u0026gt; marine Non native Species \u0026gt; Holyhead Incident March 2018 \u0026gt; D vex Survey  Images available on https://photos.app.goo.gl/w6VvJd6eoL3YTJBk8 for download\tInternal Custodian: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External contractors: Survey carried out for NRW by CloudBase Productions Ltd (Rohan Holt) with sub-contractor surveyors Kate Northen (Blue-C-Ecology), Nick Owen, Dan Gill, Liz Morris (Marine Ecological Solutions) with funds direct from WG\tThis dataset details the presence and absence of Didemnun vexillum (an invasive non-native Carpet Sea Squirt) around North West Anglesey after March 2018, when Storm Emma destroyed Holyhead marina. Live Didemnum vexillum, previously found to be established on the marina structures and nearby boat hulls and moorings, was presumed to be distributed on marina debris around NW Anglesey. Shore and diving surveys were conducted in October 2018 in the marina area and where the bulk of floating material was washed ashore on the NW of Anglesey coast early in March 2018. D vexillum was only located on the various marina structures that survived the storm and on boat hulls and moorings in the areas where it had been found on previous surveys.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18643\tNRW_DS122973\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.822Z\t731\tValid\tEditable\tRiver Wye catchment (Wales) species rich grassland creation opportunity maps\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: © CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the Centre for Ecology and Hydrology © NERC (CEH). Soil data © Cranfield University (NSRI)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393733\tX:\\Biological\\Floodplain Habitat Restoration\\River Wye catchment (Wales) opportunity\tStuart Smith - Grassland Ecologist.\tA detailed GIS analysis of land use, Phase 1 habitats, National Vegetation Classification (NVC) community data, soils and designated sites for the River Wye catchment within the NRW mid-Wales operational area boundary was undertaken.   The potential hydrological suitability of each soil type for species-rich floodplain vegetation was assessed usng HOST classes. A trial of GIS-based Multi-Criteria Decision Analysis was undertaken to identify potential sites for restoration of species rich floodplain grassland within the Wye catchment.  Target NVC communities for restoration were selected (MG4, MG5, MG8 and M22-26) and criteria likely to affect restoration success identified. GIS vector datasets relevant to each criterion were then assembled or created, and assigned a suitability score. A weighted analysis was then undertaken for each of the target communities resulting in a series of maps, one for each plant community type, indicating areas suitable for restoration.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-07-21\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2646\t2025-05-07T09:21:50.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18638\tNRW_DS123018\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.816Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tActivity Centres\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303138\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Activity Centres.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18645\tNRW_DS123035\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.822Z\t937\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Lakes Survey and Monitoring\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303335\tReports are storted in Library Data is stored in various places, some is in KiEco - LEAFPAC spreadsheets are on the DMS Evidence \u0026gt; Freshwater Ecology \u0026gt;  Internal Research \u0026gt; Lake Leafpacs data and spreadsheets Older data is in the Lakes Database.\tInternal Data Custodian:  Tristan Hatton-Ellis\tThis is a compilation of lake survey and monitoring data carried out by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and it’s predecessor bodies Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Environment Agency Wales (EAW) since 1992. It consists mainly of aquatic plant (macrophyte) surveys supported by water chemistry and dissolved oxygen, temperature and Secchi depth data. The datasets cover a wide range of lakes in Wales focussing on protected sites (Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) lakes) and on larger waterbodies monitored for Water Framework Directive (WFD). A selection of lakes that are not protected but likely to be of biodiversity importance are also included.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18641\tNRW_DS123046\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.846Z\t1219\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Road Access\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303436\tX:\\Physical\\Forestry\\Forest Road Access.lyr\tPeter van-Velzen, Planning Manager, Wales Harvesting \u0026amp; Marketing\tThe point theme contains the access points or gateways into forest or woodland managed by Natural Resources Wales, providing access to the forest roads network.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:39:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18635\tNRW_DS123207\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.646Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOpportunistic Macroalgae Areas\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323037\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Macroalgae Areas\tMetadata Contact: Geospatial Services Internal Custodian: Kathryn Borrowdale, Marine Team SW Opps\tWater Framework Directive opportunistic macroalgae area data for the following waterbodies from 2007 onwards; Afan, Alaw, Artro, Braint, Cefni, Clwyd,  Conwy, Dyfi, Dysynni, Erch, Foryd, Ffraw, Glaslyn, Gwaun, Holyhead Bay, Holyhead Straits, Loughor, Mawddach, Menai Strait, MHI, MHO, Neath, Nyfer Ogmore, Swansea and Teifi. This data was mapped in a desk based study using aerial imagery.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-09-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18644\tNRW_DS123208\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.804Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOpportunistic Macroalgae Quadrats\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323038\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Macroalgae Quadrats\tMetadata Contact: Geospatial Services Internal Custodian: Kathryn Borrowdale, Marine Team SW Opps\tWater Framework Directive opportunistic macroalgae quadrats. This data shows the positions of the quadrats that were taken for the following waterbodies from 2007 onwards; Alaw, Artro, Braint, Dyfi, Erch, Ffraw, Foryd, Holyhead Straits, MHI, MHO, Nyfer and Teifi. Species density and biomass information for these quadrats can be found on KiECO.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-09-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18634\tNRW_DS123209\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.647Z\t21\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSeagrass Quadrats\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323039\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seagrass Quadrats\tMetadata Contact: Geospatial Services Internal Custodian: Kathryn Borrowdale, Marine Team SW Opps\tWater Framework Directive seagrass quadrat data. This data shows the positions of the quadrats that were taken. Species and density information for the quadrats is available on KiECO.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-09-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18642\tNRW_DS123215\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.817Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironmentally Sensitive Permanent Grassland\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323135\tx:\\Biological\\Environmentally Sensitive Permanent Grassland.lyr\tIwan Hughes\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:39:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18633\tNRW_DS123216\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.626Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTBLFish\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323136\tx:\\Biological\\Freshwater Fisheries\\Dee Bullhead\\TBLFish font point.lyr , x:\\Biological\\Freshwater Fisheries\\Dee Bullhead\\TBLFish point.lyr\tTristan Hatton-Ellis, Lead Specialist Advisor, Freshwater Habitats and Species, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:38.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18630\tNRW_DS123219\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.429Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSubtidal with Dee and Severn\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323139\tx:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries habitat and sensitivity\\Habitat\\Subtidal with Dee and Severn  15AUG2012.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries habitat and sensitivity\\Habitat\\Subtidal with Dee and Severn 9FEB2012.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\t\t\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18639\tNRW_DS123220\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.647Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal sensitivity\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323230\tx:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries habitat and sensitivity\\Intertidal sensitivity\\Dee Estuary.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries habitat and sensitivity\\Intertidal sensitivity\\Severn Estuary.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\t\t\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18629\tNRW_DS123221\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.288Z\t12\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMostyn channel buoys\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323231\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine Reports\\Mostyn\\Mostyn channel buoys point.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine Reports\\Mostyn\\Mostyn channel buoys line.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Port of Mostyn Casework\\Mostyn Channel Buoy Line.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Port of Mostyn Casework\\Mostyn Channel Buoys.lyr\tData originally supplied by Paul Brazier, due to role changes, now managed within the Marine Area Advice and Management Team, DPAS and Marine Services\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18631\tNRW_DS123223\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.441Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater fish sites\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323233\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Freshwater Fish Cleddau Sites.lyr       x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Freshwater Fish Lamprey West.lyr      x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Freshwater Fish Teifi Sites.lyr          x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Freshwater Fish Tywi Sites.lyr        x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Freshwater Fish West Cleddau Sites.lyr\tTristan Hatton-Ellis, Lead Specialist Advisor, Freshwater Habitats and Species, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group\t\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:46.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18627\tNRW_DS123225\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.226Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMalltraeth cockle survey\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323235\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\WG_Malltraeth_cockle_survey\\All Stations WGS84.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\WG_Malltraeth_cockle_survey\\District Low Water Marks.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\WG_Malltraeth_cockle_survey\\Most Accurate District Coastline.lyr\t\t\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18636\tNRW_DS123226\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.621Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGreat crested newt habitat suitability\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323236\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\\Great crested newt habitat suitability (EQSS).lyr  x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\\Great crested newt habitat suitability.lyr\tJonathan Rothwell\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18619\tNRW_DS123227\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.737Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatterjack font\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323237\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\\Natter_Jack_font_point.lyr\tLiz Halliwell\t\t\t2015-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18624\tNRW_DS123228\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.953Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInvertebrates South\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323238\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Invertebrates\\Invertebrates South points.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Invertebrates\\Invertebrates South rectangle.lyr\tJonathan Rothwell\t\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18621\tNRW_DS123234\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.582Z\t12\tValid\tEditable\tDrinking Water Protected Areas Lakes and River catchments 2021\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323334\tx:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Drinking Water Protected Areas Lakes.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Drinking Water Protected Areas River Catchments.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn, Specialist Advisor WQ\tAny waterbodies in Wales that are used to provide drinking water are designated as drinking water protected areas (DrWPAs). The designation aims to protect the water and prevent any deterioration in quality that could increase the required treatment of water to meet Drinking Water Standards. Drinking Water Protected Areas are required to be identified under the Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2027-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:58:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18623\tNRW_DS123236\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.953Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW Valleys Regional Park\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323336\tx:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Valleys Regional Park.lyr\tPeter Cloke\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18622\tNRW_DS123237\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.851Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDrinking Water Safeguard Zones 2020\t© CNC/NRW and DCWW This dataset is derived from a third-party dataset owned by DCWW called DCWW DRINKING WATER CATCHMENTS.  A data share agreement for this layer is being negotiated with DCWW. DCWW have allowed NRW to share this data for INTERNAL USE only.  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323337\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\Drinking Water Safeguard Zones 2020.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn Suzanne.Lawn@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tAny waterbodies in Wales that are used to provide drinking water are designated as drinking water protected areas (DrWPAs). The designation aims to protect the water and prevent any deterioration in quality that could increase the required treatment of water to meet Drinking Water Standards. Drinking Water Protected Areas are required to be identified under the Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017. Within these drinking water protected areas we work with water companies to identify raw water sources that are ‘at risk’ of deterioration which would result in the need for additional treatment. These areas are degsinated as Safeguard Zones where additional measures can be used to address any factors that may reduce the water quality. The SgZ have been identified based on DCWW monitoring and risk assessments and are not limited to the DWPA but based on the wider Water Company identified catchment areas.  A catchment is the term used to describe the area of land where water drains or collects. After rain, water flows over the land, through soil and rock, into streams and eventually the main river. The boundary of a catchment follows natural slopes, hills or mountains within a landscape and determines if water flows into one river or another or subsequently into a reservoir.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Dwr Cymru Welsh Water\t2025-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18625\tNRW_DS123239\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.961Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNon EC Bathing Waters Classification\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323339\tX:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\Non EC Bathing Waters Classification.lyr   Original data is held in WISKI - KwiQM (see metadata entry116227)\tHelena Sykes?\t\t\t2021-11-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18626\tNRW_DS123240\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.062Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNon EC Bathing Waters Historic Compliance 1988 to 2014\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323430\tX:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\Non EC Bathing Waters Historic Compliance 1988 to 2014.lyr\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18620\tNRW_DS123241\t2024-05-28T12:53:16.554Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDangerous Substances Directive\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-use