is shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Tenants Connecting Cables. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:02:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18877\tNRW_DS124684\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.533Z\t627\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Turbine Foundation\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363834\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Turbine Foundation.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Turbine Foundation. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:00:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18879\tNRW_DS124685\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.493Z\t633\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Turbine Location\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363835\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Turbine Location.lyr\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Turbine Location.lyr\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Turbine Location. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:00:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18881\tNRW_DS124687\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.514Z\t900\tValid\tEditable\tCommunities at Risk Register 2019\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. The unrestricted data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of the Open Government Licence. The restricted data will only be provided on request, on a case-by-case basis and under provision of a separate NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363837\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe Communities at Risk Register (CaRR) has been developed to provide an objective means of identifying risk and prioritising flood risk management activities at a Wales-wide, community level. It applies a standard methodology across all flood sources to calculate a theoretical ‘danger score’ that allows comparative risks to be quantified and ranked (from High to Low).    The CaRR comprises a spreadsheet that identifies and ranks individual communities for,  1. a natural, ‘undefended’ scenario, and  2. a managed scenario (based on the presence of defences and flood warning).   It is complemented by a GIS layer which defines the individual Community polygons and is attributed with danger scores, community rankings and other metric information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-01-18\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3596/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_communities_at_risk_register\t2025-12-19T14:47:50.317Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18885\tNRW_DS124688\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.530Z\t402\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCaRR 2019 - Addresses at risk\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363838\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\CaRR 2019.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis). Metadata Point of Contact: Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThis dataset may have been superseded by the dataset know as Communities at Risk Register (Metadata record NRW_DS124687).  A review of the Communities at Risk Register data is due in 2025.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:49:21.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18883\tNRW_DS124689\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.530Z\t21\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCaRR 2019 - Wales CaRR Town Areas\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363839\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\CaRR 2019.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis). Metadata Point of Contact: Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThis dataset may have been superseded by the dataset know as Communities at Risk Register (Metadata record NRW_DS124687).  A review of the Communities at Risk Register data is due in 2025.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:48:48.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18880\tNRW_DS124692\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.540Z\t1218\tValid\tEditable\tNational Receptor Dataset (NRD) 2018\t© Natural Resources Wales. All rights reserved. © Ordnance Survey AC0000849444 © Middlesex University, Flood Hazard Research Centre. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking re-use also require a licence from Ordnance Survey for the derived OS data and from Middlesex University for the derived Multi-Coloured Manual (MCM) data. NRW hold an MCM code licence that includes re-use by NRW as well as all Welsh Local Authorities and Welsh Government. Other seeking re-use must purchase a licence from Middlesex University.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363932\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Receptors Database (NRD) 2018.lyr  x:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Receptors Database (NRD) 2018 from OSMM.lyr   Spreadsheet: 2018s0011 - FRAW Project Report - Appendix T03 - NRD2018_Exclusions_v2.xlsx (also knows as AddressBase_products_classification_scheme_nrd2018_AEP7.xlsx), this spreadsheet is required for anyone who wants tocompare the 2018 NDR with previous versions of the data\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group\tThe National Receptor Dataset (NRD) is a collection of risk receptors, typically residential and commercial property, intended for use in flood and coastal erosion risk management. It is a spatial dataset produced from Ordnance Survey (OS) AddressBase® Premium (ABP) and OS MasterMap® (MM) data with information on property type, floor area and Flood Hazard Research Centre’s Multi-Coloured Manual (MCM) codes attributed.     The version of the NRD described here is the NRD 2018 created as part of the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) project. The NRD 2018 builds on, and supersedes the previous NRD 2014 (and NRD 2011 before this).   Production of the dataset was streamlined as part of the FRAW project whilst maintaining conceptual consistency. Several additional data attributes have been added to facilitate flood damage calculations in the impacts analysis element of the FRAW project. Consequently, the NRD 2018 comprises two parts – a base NRD which has a very similar structure to NRD 2014, and an additional attribute table with extra information useful for impacts analysis and economic damage calculations.    Several standard NRD and non-standard attributes have been updated to support the national assessment of flood risk.  Some aspects of this are bespoke for Wales and may differ to the NRD datasets used in England.  Further details of this are included in the project report, but the main changes are:     - MCM code – addition of a caravan and bungalow code   - Settlement name – name of settlement to aid understanding   - Ground level – based on analysis from from the DTM   - Property value – creation of a residential property value    - Property classification – attribute based on MCM codes and JBA analysis    - Population – required for basic reporting of flood metrics and for Danger score calculation within NRW’s Communities at Risk Register, or ‘CaRR’   - SFVI_CAT2a – to identify communities that are more vulnerable to the adverse health and social effects associated with floods   - Individual property protection   - Warning and alert areas   - Defended areas and SoP\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-08-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:50:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18884\tNRW_DS124694\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.510Z\t405\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAn assessment of the condition of the Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior at Whiteford Burrows NNR, October 2018\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363934\tReport to be stored in DMS Reports Archive Data to be stored in the Wales Invertebrate Database (WID) in Recorder 6  -\tMike Howe       Metadata to be supplied and data location of raw spreadsheets to be confirmed.  Date to be entered into Recorder 6 - 15/01/2019\tSingle day assessment visit was undertaken in October 2018. which allowed an overview site-survey, combined with off-site bulk sample analysis, to assess most of the sand dune/salt marsh transition zone and allowing the field assessment of the snail at 11 sites, which also involved the removal of bulk vegetation samples for off-site determination of snail presence. The results revealed V. angustior to be present at seven sites including several in the northern transition zone, an area in which the snail had not been recorded previously and which had been overlooked for study since the 1993 survey. The 2018 results demonstrated the presence of the snail over a linear distance of 1.9km extending the previously recorded range by 1.2 km, a 171% increase\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T16:21:08.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18867\tNRW_DS124695\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.406Z\t668\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAnchoring, mooring and launching spatial distribution in Wales\t© CNC/NRW and [insert other owner(s)] Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. This may change depending on individual case and third-party licences conditions\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363935\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Anchoring Mooring and Launching\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor -Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tAnchoring, mooring and launching of recreational vessel has the potential to damage benthic marine habitats. The impact and significance of these activities depend on a number of factors including the intensity of the activity and the sensitivity of underlying habitats. Defining the specific locations in which these activities occur is integral to determining the potential for interactions with sensitive features. The data collection has been commissioned under the Wales non-licensable activities project and provides a robust dataset from a wide range of sources on the location of anchoring, mooring and launching sites in Wales from the high-water mark to 12nm offshore.  The collated data was sent to a range of knowledgeable marine stakeholders for consultation to ensure that the polygons accurately represent the anchoring, mooring and launching areas and that no additional areas had been missed.  A range of intensity metrics were used to allocate a value of intensity to each polygon for anchoring, mooring and launching.  This includes extracting values from AIS data from the Royal Yachting Association, proximity to sailing clubs and centres and consultation with stakeholders.   The dataset comprises 4 GIS layers; • Anchoring • Mooring • Launching • No-anchoring\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-01-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18874\tNRW_DS124706\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.499Z\t8\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW Flood Technical Services areas\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373036\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Flood Technical Services areas.lyr\tLucy Edwards\tMap of Wales split into 2 sections, each section covers the area of responsibility for each of the Flood technical services team area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18876\tNRW_DS124730\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.518Z\t340\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBaseline Project – Pressures Contract\t© NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373330\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/sites/smar/stratplan/tsg/Programmes/Terrestrial%20Transition%20Planning/Task%2002%20-%20Baseline/Baseline%20Project%20-%20Pressures%20contract/Contract%20final%20products?csf\u003d1\t\tDevelopment and application of a methodology to assess the anthropogenic pressures acting upon independently qualifying features of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18871\tNRW_DS124732\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.519Z\t742\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOtter Road Mortality Survey (ORMS)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders. NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. The redacted data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the unredacted or sensitive data.  Attribution Statement: The dataset contains Natural Resource Wales Information © Natural Resources Wales and database right.  All rights reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373332\tX Drive GIS Layer: X:\\Biological\\Otter_Road_Mortality_Survey_1971_To_2021.lyr  DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/mam/Cardiff%20University%20Otter%20Project/ORMS%20Access%20Database%20Export%20-%20v3.xlsx?d\u003dw1bbd568441bc42ab93330890c936c318 MS PowerBI: https://app.powerbi.com/groups/20b3821f-8d6d-445e-a3b2-928cd79199d9/reports/5f564b08-4cf7-4b0c-aa3b-0a726901cc9e?ctid\u003d8865ef0f-acde-487c-bf17-5cb50375d757 ArcGIS Online:https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/518736dfc090454f83bbd905bf732ff3 MS Access: L:\\EAW\\Legacy Databases\\OtterRoadMortality\\ORMDb.mdb Paper files: Basement office in boxes on shelves behind Janet Buckles old desk\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Internal Custodian: Hilary Foster - Specialist Advisor: Freshwater Habitats and Species, Freshwater Habitats \u0026amp; Species Team Internal Contact: Olly Ishmael, Advisor 2,  Geospatial Services (Formerly Janet Buckles - Retired)\tThe Otter Road Mortality Survey (ORMS) database was initiated after the UK Otter Biodiversity Action Plan (which arose from the Rio Summit of 1992) identified that otter road deaths were a significant factor causing loss or decline in otter populations in the UK. The database was initiated in association with a report commissioned to identify blackspots in Wales (written by Geoff Liles and published in March 2000).  Members of the Public who spot dead otters and report them were originally asked to complete a paper form to provide observational details. These were subsequently recorded in the ORMS Access database.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-07-14\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:55.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18878\tNRW_DS124733\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.522Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Coppice Mawr, Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2019\tTo be added\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373333\tData to be entered into Recorder 6 (Survey ID to be added)\tMike Howe\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:02:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18866\tNRW_DS124734\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.267Z\t682\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveys for Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch SSSI, Geyer’s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Cors Geirch SSSI \u0026amp; Cors Erddreiniog SSSI, 2019\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373334\tAdded to Recorder 6 on the Welsh Invertebrate Database\tMike Howe\tGeyer’s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri has been recorded from three sites in Wales. However, surveys of Cors Erddreiniog SSSI in 2016 and of Cors Geirch SSSI in 2016 and 2017 failed to locate the snail. It was feared that V. geyeri had been lost from both sites but, as small populations of this tiny snail can easily be overlooked, it was felt premature to declare the snail as extinct and additional surveys were therefore undertaken at both sites in October 2019.   The survey focussed upon two areas of potentially suitable habitat that had been recently surveyed;  (1) an area of horse-grazed ‘Carex-lawn’ within the National Nature Reserve where V. geyeri had been reported in 1998 and  (2) a series of base-rich flushes lying towards the western end of the National Nature Reserve last surveyed in 2016.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T10:09:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18869\tNRW_DS124735\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.521Z\t722\tUnassessed\tEditable\tStatus of Dillwyn’s Dung Beetle Onthophagus nuchicornis, Morfa Harlech 2019\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373335\tRecorder 6 - WID\tMike Howe\tDillwyn’s Dung Beetle Onthophagus nuchicornis was formerly local but widespread across England and Wales. It has declined dramatically in England and since 2000 is extant at just five of the forty known locations. In Wales, this species has fared better but is restricted to grazed coastal sand dune systems. Historically known from fifteen sites, it was still present at half of these in 2017. The beetle feeds on the dung of a range of mammals but is probably dependent for breeding on medium to large herbivore dung such as cattle, ponies and sheep.    A survey in 2017 of all the known sites in Wales found Onthophagus nuchicornis at seven localities – Kenfig Burrows (975 adults), Merthyr Mawr Warren (36), Morfa Harlech (1), Oxwich Burrows (4), Pembrey Burrows (32), Pennard Burrows (4) and Whiteford Burrows (5). The authors regard the populations at Kenfig Burrows and Methyr Mawr to be the best in the UK. Elsewhere, absence and low population levels are strongly correlated to the cessation of grazing and the quantity and quality of available dung on sand.  Following the 2017 survey, a more intensive survey of Morfa Harlech, which represents a northern outlier for the beetle, was undertaken in 2019 to determine the status of Onthophagus nuchicornis at this site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T10:16:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18865\tNRW_DS124736\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.885Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tArachnids (spiders \u0026amp; harvestmen) data for Morfa Dyffryn National Nature Reserve (NNR) 2017-2019\tTo Be Added\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373336\tData to be added to Recorder 6 - WID (Survey ID to be added)\tMike Howe\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:49:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18882\tNRW_DS124737\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.533Z\t8\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSeascape and visual sensitivity to Offshore wind farms in Wales: Low and Medium effect buffers\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373337\tTo be stored on the X drive.  Has been emailed to Keith Jones 03/0382020 My understanding is that the data is only partially loaded onto the X drive now (as per email from John Briggs in Nov 2020), Low and Medium buffer layers have been uploaded but not Seascape visual sensitivity layer. All layers are otherwise stored in https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/advice/mar/ind/Impact%20Assessment/OWF%20-%20Visual%20Impacts%20Assessment/Seascapes%20contract%202018/GIS%20files?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1  X:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascape and visual sensitivity to Offshore Windfarms\\Buffers for Low magnitude of effect.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascape and visual sensitivity to Offshore Windfarms\\Buffers for Medium magnitude of effect.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Lucie Skates (Lead Specialist Advisor Marine Planning Implementation - Sustainable Places Land \u0026amp; Sea) and John Briggs (Specialist Advisor, Landscape - Planning, Landscape, Energy,De-carbonisation)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Lucie Skates\tThese GIS files were produced as part of a project looking at seascape and visual sensitivity to future offshore windfarm development off the coast of Wales.   The aim of the project was to improve the evidence base and update our advice to reflect the likely scale of new offshore windfarm developments. The project had three stages which looked at the following topics:   Stage 1 - The relationship between distance of offshore turbine away from a sensitive receptor and magnitude of visual effects  Stage 2 - Offshore wind farm siting and design guidelines in relation to seascapes  Stage 3 – Visual sensitivity of the marine settings of Wales’s Designated Landscapes to offshore wind farms.   As part of stage 1, GIS maps of the Welsh marine area were produced which showed the proposed buffers for offshore windfarms with low and medium magnitude visual effects. As part of stage 3, the seascape was divided into 15 zones and a summary of the sensitivity of each zone to offshore windfarm development provided. A GIS map shows the location and boundaries of these zones.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:26:30.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18873\tNRW_DS124741\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.454Z\t444\tValid\tEditable\tCarbon sequestration potential of marine habitats\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Derived in part from data provided under Licence by the British Geological Survey © NERC. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373431\tY:\\SharedProjects\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Karen_Robinson\\Blue carbon project\tInternal Data Custodian:  Karen Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor - Marine Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.\tBlue carbon is the carbon stored and sequestered by marine habitats. Related terms can be defined as follows:  • A carbon sink is a natural or artificial reservoir that accumulates and stores carbon. • A carbon store encompasses the carbon stored in the water column, surface sediments and floral and faunal biomass. This storage may be short or long-term.  Where carbon is stored in the long-term, it can be considered to be sequestered.  In order to estimate the blue carbon potential of Welsh waters, and thus allow a greater understanding of how Welsh marine ecosystems contribute to offsetting the release of carbon through human activities, the following steps have been undertaken for this study:  • The best and most up to date available spatial datalayers for blue carbon habitats have been identified and combined so that approximate Welsh coverage could be determined for each. • The relevant literature has been reviewed to obtain the most relevant carbon storage and sequestration values which would then be applied to Welsh marine habitats. • A dedicated numerical model has been interrogated to obtain (1) water column carbon estimates (including plankton biomass); (2) estimates on air-sea flux of Carbon Dioxide (CO2) (i.e. how much CO2 enters the water column and is converted into various forms of carbon), as well as (3) estimates of carbon flux across the Welsh marine boundary (i.e. how much carbon leaves Welsh waters and gets transported to adjacent shelf and deeper waters). • Riverine inputs of carbon have also been estimated based on literature values and average river discharges supplied by NRW hydrologists. • Carbon values have been calculated and put into context with terrestrial (‘green carbon’) rates, as well as Welsh CO2 emissions.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:02:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18861\tNRW_DS124750\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.814Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSouthern Damselfly Coenagrion mercuriale Habitat Creation and Restoration, Pembrokeshire from 2020 onwards\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373530\tData to to provided\tData Custodian:  Mike Howe\tData is likley to be a CD with QGIS files showing the location of all recommended and potential habitat restoration areas Needs to be transformed and placed on the X drive  *****************report number to be provided******************************\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:52:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18863\tNRW_DS124753\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.885Z\t33\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNational Flood Asset Dataset for Wales: NRW flood Assets held in AMX-Flood\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373533\tAMX\tRichard Kelland - now moved to new role\tmetadata to be provided by custodian.   AMX flood asset data store contains data that has been split into three types according to data owner (NRW, EA, others)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:14:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18864\tNRW_DS124755\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.886Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEC Bathing Waters Classification\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373535\tX:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\EC Bathing Waters Classification.lyr    Original data is held in WISKI - KwiQM (see metadata entry116227)\tHelena Sykes?\t\t\t2021-11-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18862\tNRW_DS124756\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.814Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) Landscape Rhos Glyn-yr-helyg (2019)\tto be added\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373536\tRecorder 6 - WID\tMike Howe\tMetadata to be supplied (data will be added to the WID in Recorder 6) *************GIS layer may be supplied by Butterfly conservation for updated map of Wales - this would be new third party data********** and could potentially replace or add to data held under X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Invertebrates\\Insects\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18859\tNRW_DS124759\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.644Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Adaptability Habitat Recovery Period\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373539\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThe rate at which a habitat can recover back to a steady, stable state from a disturbance event is an inherent property of ecosystem resilience. Habitats that have an inherent ability to re-establish quickly following disturbance have a higher likelihood of persisting because they can re-establish rapidly and before the next disturbance event occurs. For example, wet woodlands can recover relatively quickly from flood events (providing this flooding stays within the range of historic limits), whilst ancient woodlands may take many hundreds of years to re-create if lost. The effect that recovery periods may have on ecosystem resilience is explored through this factor.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T10:17:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18860\tNRW_DS124760\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.686Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Adaptability Overall\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373630\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset shows resilience based on adaptability, using a range of factors contributing to this attribute.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T10:18:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18857\tNRW_DS124762\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.472Z\t819\tValid\tEditable\tSSSI NH3 Critical Levels\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution Statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373632\tx:\\Resource Management\\Air Quality\\SSSI NH3 Critical Levels.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species  Metadata Point of Contact: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species\tThis layer provides a Critical Level (CLe) for ammonia against which impacts on a SSSI should be assessed. The value is the lowest CLe for a feature on that site. The layer gives the SSSI name, SSSI code and ammonia Critical Level. The ammonia CLe can be: 1: a 1µg/m3 NH3 Critical Level indicates a SSSI has N-sensitive bryophytes/lichens as a feature or has bryophytes/lichens as key components of a habitat feature of that SSSI: 3: a 3µg/m3 NH3 Critical Level indicates a SSSI has N-sensitive vegetation, or has invertebrate features that are dependent on N-sensitive vegetation. NS: all features on a site are considered not to be particularly sensitive to ammonia; this does not mean that other aspects of a development will not have negative impacts on a SSSI, for example through water pollution or disturbance.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-08-03\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3583/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_sssi_nh3_cl\t2025-12-19T10:33:12.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18858\tNRW_DS124763\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.621Z\t777\tValid\tEditable\tNH3 Sensitive Ancient Woodlands\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution Statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373633\tX:\\Resource Management\\Air Quality\\NH3 sensitive Ancient Woodlands.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tThis layer identifies areas of Ancient Woodland and Parkland from which NRW hold recent, auditable records of N-sensitive lichens. Not all lichens are sensitive to increased ammonia: the 62 species used to identify N-sensitive Ancient Woodland and Parkland are particularly sensitive and are important in the woodland/parkland ecosystem. Any area identified as sensitive in this GIS layer should be assessed using a 1µg/m3 NH3 Critical Level.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-08-03\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3513/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_n_sensitive_wood\t2025-05-02T15:16:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18856\tNRW_DS124764\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.386Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Condition INNS\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373634\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tInvasive non-native species (INNS) can pose a threat to ecosystem resilience throughout Wales.  The output shows relative resilience as high for squares where very few unique INNS were recorded.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:02:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18905\tNRW_DS124765\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.155Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Condition Overall\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373635\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset shows resilience based on condition, using a range of factors contributing to this attribute.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18909\tNRW_DS124766\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.125Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Condition PosPlantIndicators\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373636\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tFor many habitats, specific species associated with the habitat are known to only occur if the habitat is in good overall condition. These species can be considered indicators of good habitat condition.    This factor shows on a relative scale the diversity of positive plant indicator species, associated with a given habitat, are present within that habitat group for each 1 km reporting square.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18908\tNRW_DS124767\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.193Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Condition PressureOnAir Acidification\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373637\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tAir is one of the most important supporting features of ecosystem resilience. Clean air affects every habitat, from freshwater to forests and upland habitats.  On a relative scale from 0 to 100, the output shows which areas are the most resilient to an increase in air pollution, i.e. areas where an increase in air pollutant emissions is less likely to have an immediate effect on ecosystem condition. In interpreting this dataset, it is important to note that values are based on emission of pollutants, not on concentration of pollutants in the air.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:08:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18900\tNRW_DS124769\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.148Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Condition PressureOnAir OzoneAmmonia\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373639\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tAir is one of the most important supporting features of ecosystem resilience. Clean air affects every habitat, from freshwater to forests and upland habitats.     On a relative scale from 0 to 100, the output shows which areas are the most resilient to an increase in air pollution, i.e. areas where an increase in air pollutant emissions is less likely to have an immediate effect on ecosystem condition. In interpreting this dataset, it is important to note that values are based on emission of pollutants, not on concentration of pollutants in the air.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:04:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18904\tNRW_DS124770\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.184Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Condition SACs\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373730\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tDue to European reporting requirements, SACs are amongst the best monitored conservation designations in Wales, making them one of the few areas with direct measurements of condition acquired through field work. In field assessments of habitat condition are one of, if not the, most valuable contribution to the attribute Condition.    The output shows the percentage of SAC features in favourable condition within the area; areas outside of SACs take a NoData value.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T10:19:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18907\tNRW_DS124771\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.142Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Condition SoilErosionRisk\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373731\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tSoil is one of the most fundamental factors underlying ecosystem health.  The output shows erosion risk on a relative scale from 0 to 100 from a purely soil-centric perspective; the values express how resilient the soil would be without landcover, and important consideration when considering the soils; resilience to land use change. Areas with peaty soils show as NoData areas in this factor, as the erodibility of peat is driven by the condition of the landcover to such an extent that the DEFRA methodology was not applicable to these soil types.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:08:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18896\tNRW_DS124772\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.134Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Condition WaterQuality\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373732\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tWater is a supporting abiotic component of functioning habitats. The quality of the water available is not only intrinsically important for the flora and fauna living directly within water ways, but also has a wider effect on the habitats drawing from these water sources. For this project, river water quality was chosen as a factor, as rivers permeate throughout Wales, thereby having a large influence on many ecosystems.  The output shows the relative resilience of each reporting square based on the condition of the freshwater available to the area through rivers. As this data was originally collected on a catchment-by-catchment basis, gradients between catchment boundaries are quite sharp, with intermediate values only occurring where one reporting square overlaps multiple river waterbody catchments.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:04:48.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18915\tNRW_DS124773\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.654Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Connectivity EcologicalNetworks\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373733\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset shows the number of ecological networks represented within each reporting square. In some squares, 5 out of 6 ecological networks are present, generating areas that act as major nodes between various habitat networks across Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:08:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18910\tNRW_DS124774\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.196Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Connectivity FeatureLength\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373734\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tConnective features in this instance refers to habitat types that are typically long and narrow. These habitats may act as corridors or stepping stones, facilitating species movement through oftentimes heavily modified and otherwise impermeable landscapes.    The output shows the relative quantity of connective features present within each square.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:08:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18902\tNRW_DS124775\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.186Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Connectivity Overall\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373735\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset shows resilience based on connectivity, using a range of factors contributing to this attribute.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:08:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18913\tNRW_DS124776\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.167Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Diversity SemiNaturalHabitats\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373736\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tHabitat diversity forms an important component of expected resilience, as the variety of available ecological niches in the area means that the area is likely to harbour greater species and genetic diversity.  The output shows the diversity of semi-natural habitats on a relative scale from 0 to 100 within each reporting square. In the interpretation of these outputs, it is important to consider that only diverse habitat mosaics will show with the highest values, whilst areas dominated by few classes will show with low values, regardless of whether or not they are semi-natural.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:08:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18897\tNRW_DS124777\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.894Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Diversity SSSIs Proportion\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373737\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tSites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) are areas of land notified for their special scientific interest. They support an important range of biological, geological and physiographical features. The most important areas for habitat and species conservation, at both national and international levels, lie within them, and all are considered of national importance for nature conservation.    The proportion of land within SSSIs in each 1 km reporting square is used as factor for the Diversity factor.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:04:46.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18912\tNRW_DS124778\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.212Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Diversity Topographic\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373738\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tTopography is one of the main biophysical drivers behind habitat establishment.  The output expresses on a relative scale how topographically diverse the landscape within each of the reporting squares is. Areas with higher values can be expected to contain habitats at different elevation levels, encompassing areas with shallow slopes, as well as steeply rising land. Furthermore, areas with the highest values are expected to have slopes facing in different cardinal directions, affecting the amount and timing of sunlight they receive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18898\tNRW_DS124779\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.154Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Ecosystem Resilience Overall\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373739\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset shows current relative resilience across Wales, based on five attributes (Diveristy, Extent, Condition, Connectivity, Adaptability).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:05:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18906\tNRW_DS124780\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.070Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Extent Overall\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373830\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset shows resilience based on extent, using a range of factors contributing to this attribute.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18901\tNRW_DS124781\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.155Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Extent PatchDynamics\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373831\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tHabitat resilience does not only depend on the amount of semi-natural habitat available, but also on the spatial configuration of this habitat.  The output shows on a relative scale from 0 to 100 how patchy the distribution of the semi-natural habitat within the reporting square is (one large vs several small).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:05:32.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18903\tNRW_DS124782\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.167Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Extent SemiNaturalHabitats\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373832\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tSemi-natural habitats with their natural and diverse assemblage of flora and fauna are inherently more resilient than habitats modified strongly by human management, making this an important factor to include for the attribute Extent.  The output shows the proportion of semi-natural habitats in the reporting square.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18914\tNRW_DS124783\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.640Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Diversity Overall\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373833\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset shows resilience based on diversity, using a range of factors contributing to this attribute.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:30.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18911\tNRW_DS124784\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.193Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Connectivity River Modifications\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373834\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis dataset shows the resilience value derived from the amount of hard engineering affecting rivers within the reporting squares. These values are based on the expected quantity of physical modifications, but do not take into account the type of modification and how strongly it impacts upon the river’s capacity to act as connective feature.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T10:22:58.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18899\tNRW_DS124786\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.959Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAll Wales Slope 5m2 (degrees)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373836\tX:\\Basemapping\\Height\\All Wales Slope 5m2 (degrees).lyr\tPaul Challender, Lead Specialist - Industry and Waste Regulation, Industry Regulation NE\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:23:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18895\tNRW_DS124788\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.213Z\t8\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDeterioration of Natura 2000 Sites in Wales due to Coastal Squeeze Losses\tTo be confirmed\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373838\tSpreadsheets- DMS (record numbers to be confirmed) GIS data - X drive - location to be confirmed  Currently zipped in the DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:u:/r/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mcps/Evidence%20NRW%20projects/Article%206%20-2%20Deterioration%20due%20to%20Coastal%20Squeeze%20Project/Data_20190710.zip?csf\u003d1  And copy (to be deleted) in  K:\\Archive\\Temp_forArchive\\Report 355\\Data\tInternal Contact: Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine and Coastal Physical Process and Coastal Management, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tThe data resource includes NRW Evidence Report 355 plus associated Excel and GIS files. The project has calculated potential coastal squeeze losses under a range of different No Active Intervention and Managed Realignment management Scenarios, climate change predictions and Shoreline Management Plan Epochs. The study covers the Pen Llŷn a’r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation. The excel files contain coastal squeeze calculations which are summarised within the report, and GIS files used to undertake analysis and produce maps provided within the evidence report.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18892\tNRW_DS124789\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.196Z\t442\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGreen Flag Awards Boundary data\t© CNC/NRW and Keep Wales Tidy NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373839\tGIS Corporate Data Store: X-Layer_Files\\ Recreation and Access\\Green Flag\\Green Flag sites poly.lyr\tPeter Frost - Advisor -  Planning, Landscape, Energy, Decarbonisation\tThe boundaries of all sites that held a Green Flag or Green Flag Community Award in the 2019-2020 Award Year.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T14:48:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18893\tNRW_DS124790\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.196Z\t1014\tValid\tEditable\tNational Flood Hazard Maps - Present Day\t© CNC/NRW. Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the UK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology © UKCEH, © EA and Getmapping Plc and Bluesky International Limited (2015). Defra, Met Office and DARD Rivers Agency © Crown copyright. © Cranfield University. © James Hutton Institute. Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right. Land and Property Services © Crown copyright and database right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373930\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Rivers\\Flood Hazard River Depth.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Rivers\\Flood Hazard Rivers Extent.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Rivers\\Flood Hazard Rivers Hazard.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Rivers\\Flood Hazard Rivers Velocity.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses Depth.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses Depth.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses Hazard.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses Velocity.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from the Sea\\Flood Hazard from the Sea Depth.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from the Sea\\Flood Hazard from the Sea Extent.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from the Sea\\Flood Hazard from the Sea Hazard.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh / GIS and Spatial Data Team   Metadata Point of Contact: Mark Pugh / Simon James / Dave Tarrant       Surface Water Catchments (subset dataset NRW_DS124811): Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from Environment Agency © EA and Getmapping Plc and Bluesky International Limited [2015].\tThe National Flood Hazard Maps for Present day,  are based on generalised modelling only. The maps have been published to comply with the Flood Risk Regulations (2009) and the EU Directive (2007/60/EC), and used to inform the creation of Flood Risk Management plans across Wales. These maps have no official status for Planning or Insurance purposes, therefore the public and professionals are advised to use the information contained within the Flood Risk Assessment Wales Map, the Development Advice Map/ Flood Map for Planning (as appropriate) for these purposes as these will be more up-to-date and incorporate improved modelling information.   The National Flood Hazard Maps for Present Day have been created for 3 sources of flooding, namely,   1. Flooding from Rivers  2. Flooding from the Sea  3. Flooding from Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourse    The Maps show flood depth, velocity (speed and direction), hazard and extent for High, Medium and Low risk scenarios without raised flood defences.    For Rivers and Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses, High risk is up to 1 in 30year; Medium risk is between 1 in 30 and 1 in 100year; and Low risk is between 1 in 100year and 1 in 1,000year Annual Exceedance Probability (AEP).    For the Sea, High risk is up to 1 in 30 year; Medium risk is between 1 in 30 to 1 in 200 year; and Low risk 1 in 200 year to 1 in 1000 year.    The Flooding from Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses dataset is a direct replacement for the earlier Updated Flood Map for Surface Water (uFMfSW), 2013 and is based on the modelling of Surface Water Catchments.  Details of the modelling work to produce the new National Flood Hazard Maps are available in the supporting Information sheets.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-01\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2649\t2025-12-19T12:11:27.247Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18894\tNRW_DS124791\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.203Z\t744\tValid\tEditable\tNational Flood Risk Maps 2019\t© CNC/NRW. Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the UK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology © UKCEH, © EA and Getmapping Plc and Bluesky International Limited (2015). Defra, Met Office and DARD Rivers Agency © Crown copyright. © Cranfield University. © James Hutton Institute. Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right. Land and Property Services © Crown copyright and database right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373931\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Risk from Rivers.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Risk from Surface Water and Small Watercourses.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Risk from the Sea.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Risk CaRR.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh / GIS and Spatial Data Team   Metadata Point of Contact: Mark Pugh / Simon James / Dave Tarrant\tThe National Flood Hazard and Risk Maps are based on generalised modelling only. The maps have been published to comply with the Flood Risk Regulations (2009) and the EU Directive (2007/60/EC), and used to inform the creation of Flood Risk Management plans across Wales. These maps have no official status for Planning or Insurance purposes, therefore the public and professionals are advised to use the information contained within the Flood Risk Assessment Wales Map and Development Advice Map/ Flood Map for Planning (as appropriate) for these purposes as these will be more up-to-date and incorporate improved modelling information.   The National Flood Risk Maps have been created for 3 sources of flooding, namely,  1. Flooding from Rivers 2. Flooding from the Sea 3. Flooding from Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourse  The maps show Risk to a range of receptors, grouped in categories of People, Economic and Environment. Risk information is aggregated and displayed at a community scale.  For Rivers and Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses, High risk is up to 1 in 30year; Medium risk is between 1 in 30 and 1 in 100year; and Low risk is between 1 in 100year and 1 in 1,000year Annual Exceedance Probability (AEP).  For the Sea, High risk is up to 1 in 30 year; Medium risk is between 1 in 30 to 1 in 200 year; and Low risk 1 in 200 year to 1 in 1000 year.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-06-29\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/888/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:NationalFloodRiskMaps\t2025-03-07T15:59:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18890\tNRW_DS124793\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.044Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUnchecked Rain Gauge Data from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Gauges\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373933\tWiski H\u0026amp;T\tLiam Perrot and Alison Hanson\tThis dataset covers monitoring data that is updated on our systems on a minimum of a daily update cycle. This is increased during times of flooding etc. Measurements of the height (m) of water in a river, lake or coastal site taken using automatic field devices, are given usually every 15 minutes. Information is available for NRW river monitoring sites throughout Wales, as well as some reservoirs and coastal sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:05:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18891\tNRW_DS124794\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.036Z\t986\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Protected Areas in Welsh waters\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373934\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\tNatasha Lough.  Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning\tThe Marine protected area (MPA) network is a subset of existing protected sites that have marine features. Sites include: SACs, SPAs, Ramsars, SSSIs and MCZs.     What features are considered marine is determined through expert groups of SNCBs led by JNCC. The list of MPA network features are hosted by JNCC https://hub.jncc.gov.uk/assets/8ee15786-510b-44e4-819e-e6681a1abd96.     The need for a coherent and well managed MPA network is underpinned by the legislation Marine and Coastal access act and MFSD (UK Marine Strategy). This has been driven by our commitment to OSPAR (Oslo Paris convention) and CBD (Convention on biological diversity). NRW are the custodians of MPAs within 12nm (Welsh territorial waters), sites that are found in out offshore waters are hosted by JNCC and can be downloaded from their website https://jncc.gov.uk/mpa-mapper/.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-06-09\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3601/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_marine_protected_areas_in_welsh_waters\t2025-06-10T15:46:54.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18889\tNRW_DS124795\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.056Z\t391\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) bryophyte surveys\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373935\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE bryophyte surveys.lyr  Spreadsheet version also available in DMS LAND-75481194-986\tToni Henwood, Team Member 2, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South WalesSands of LIFE\tLocations of notable bryophyte species recorded during pre-intervention surveys for the Sands of LIFE project at Tywyn Aberffraw, Newborough, Morfa Harlech, Pembrey Coast, Kenfig and Merthyr Mawr. Data was collected between September and November 2019.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-06-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18888\tNRW_DS124796\t2024-05-28T12:57:55.993Z\t783\tUnassessed\tEditable\tScarce Yellow Sally stonefly Isogenus nubecula, River Dee at Bangor-on-Dee 2020\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373936\tStored on the WID in recorder 6\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tIn 2018, a survey commissioned by Natural Resources Wales across the full former range of I. nubecula confirmed that it occurred at five sites on the River Dee, two of which (Bangor-on-Dee and Pickhill Meadows) appeared to support strong populations. In 2020, Natural Resources Wales commissioned a second survey to confirm the continued presence of Scarce Yellow Sally, and six nymphs were found at Bangor-on-Dee on 24th March 2020\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T14:50:50.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18887\tNRW_DS124799\t2024-05-28T12:57:55.976Z\t680\tValid\tEditable\tCurrent Relative Value of ecosystem resilience (CuRVe)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373939\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJohnathan Rothwell -  Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping\tCuRVe is a series of interconnected web-based maps that together form an atlas. It is a tool that can help users to explore how ecosystem resilience varies across Wales and understand the underlying reasons for this variation. It can be used to support and improve decision making about the use of, and investment in, the sustainable management of natural resources. CuRVe stands for Current Relative Value of ecosystem resilience. The Atlas is the first step in an experimental project to reveal relative values and spatial patterns of ecosystem resilience at a landscape scale for Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-06-22\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/1110/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:CuRVe\t2025-05-07T15:49:06.127Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18886\tNRW_DS124802\t2024-05-28T12:57:55.770Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW Fisheries Teams\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383032\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Fisheries Teams.lyr\tGary Eccleston, Advisor 2, Geospatial Knowledge, Information Management\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Fisheries Teams boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for operational Fisheries Teams post Organisation Design, July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries, incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-07-25\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18934\tNRW_DS124803\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.510Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW Forestry Operations Teams\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383033\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Forestry Operations Teams.lyr\tGary Eccleston, Advisor 2, Geospatial Knowledge, Information Management\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Fisheries Teams boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for Forestry operations Teams post Organisation Design, July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries, incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-01-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-05T13:06:28.000Z\tGary Eccleston\t-\tPrivate record\n18929\tNRW_DS124804\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.583Z\t522\tValid\tEditable\tDistribution of the spider Baryphyma gowerense on Cors Bodeilio, Cors Erddreiniog, Cors Geirch and Cors Goch 2019\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383034\tData stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder.\tMike Howe\tIn order to improve our knowledge of the ecology and distribution of the Vulnerable and Nationally Rare money spider Baryphyma gowerense, survey work was conducted on Cors Bodeilio, Cors Erddreiniog, Cors Goch and Cors Geirch National Nature Reserves (NNRs) between June and August 2019.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-06-01\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr1212\t2025-12-19T16:16:10.645Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18939\tNRW_DS124805\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.850Z\t710\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAlien Taxa Environmental and Socio-economic impact classification\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383035\tData held in the DMS at the following locations:  EICAT spreadsheet: EVID-363697614-2272  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mnns/EMFF%20INNS%20Project/EXTERNAL%20CONTRACTS/Marine%20Biological%20Association%20-%20Impacts%20of%20INNS/MBA%20final%20report/MBA_EICAT_042020.xlsx?d\u003dw1d3200d4e3824d73a6c21125ab1de07d    SEICAT Spreadsheet: EVID-363697614-2271: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mnns/EMFF%20INNS%20Project/EXTERNAL%20CONTRACTS/Marine%20Biological%20Association%20-%20Impacts%20of%20INNS/MBA%20final%20report/MBA_SEICAT_assessment_042020.xls?d\u003dw11cbbbd4221242d2be394c94583c320f\tInternal Custodian: Chloe Powell-Jennings, Senior Advisor (EMFF Biosecurity Project Officer), Southwest Operations Internal Custodian: Katherine Griffith, Senior Marine Advisor, Marine Area Management \u0026amp; Advice Team\tThere are two excel spreadsheets which accompany the report titled ‘Assessing the impact of key Marine Invasive Non-Native Species on Welsh Marine Protected Area habitat features, fisheries and aquaculture’. These are the Environmental Impact Classification of Alien Taxa (EICAT) and the Socio-economic Impact Classification of Alien Taxa (SEICAT) which summarise and provide additional information data for the report.  The purpose of the spreadsheets (along with the accompanying report) is to assess potential impacts of 16 invasive non-native species on Welsh Marine Protected Area features, commercial fisheries and aquaculture to address knowledge gaps for biosecurity planning.   The EICAT Excel workbook consists of a summary matrix for the 16 invasive non-native species providing a summary score for likelihood of establishment and an aggregated (worst-case) score for impact on each of the 41 Marine Protected Area features assessed by the project.    The SEICAT Excel workbook consists of a summary matrix for the 16 invasive non-native species providing a summary impact score for direct impacts on fisheries and aquaculture via interactions which affect deployment and operation of gear and installations and impacts on target or cultivated species. It does not consider indirect impacts or ancillary activities (for example, impacts on gaining access or impacts on bait).    A number of general limitations regarding the impact assessments should be considered in their application:  •The impact assessments are generic and NOT site-specific. They are based on the likely effects of an INNS pressure on a ‘hypothetical’ MPA feature/ biotope based on general characteristics of these features. •The impact assessments are general assessments that indicate the likely effects of a given INNS (likely to arise from one or more impact pathways) on MPA features or aquaculture and fisheries operations as represented in Wales. •The assessments have attempted where possible to take account of spatial or temporal scales.  •The significance of impacts arising from pressures also needs to take account of the scale of the features. •There are limitations in the scientific evidence on the biology and ecology of MPA features and their responses to INNS on which the impact assessments have been based. •The EICAT impact assessment methodology takes account of the level of impact and the likely recovery to differentiate between the two most severe impact ranks (massive and major). Eradication of INNS is difficult and recovery will generally only occur where management measures are implemented. There is the potential, therefore for all major impacts to become massive. •As a general rule, where the impact is ranked as higher, the need for management measures is greater. •A rank of ‘Minor or Minimal Concern does not mean that no impact is possible from a particular ‘INNS x feature’ combination, only that a limited impact was judged to be likely based on the available evidence.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-09-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:19:12.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18944\tNRW_DS124806\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.908Z\t337\tValid\tEditable\tBait digging areas 2019/2020\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383036\tShapefile of digging X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Mapping bait digging\\Bait digging areas (2019-2020).lyr  Layer file for the drone photos X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Drone footage\\Intertidal Drone Photos 2019-2020.lyr  The PDFs and with processing notes and word documents are saved in DMS Advice\u0026gt;Marine\u0026gt;MPA Non-Licensable Activities\u0026gt;WNLA Phase 3 Bait Digging\u0026gt;Final Report and products July 2020    Digital terrain model outputs showing bait digging areas - Z:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Bait Digging DSMs 2019-20\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team                                                         This dataset has been listed as sensitive because it shows the location of areas that may be targeted by bait diggers. Project Officer has identified this as data that should be kept confidential however areas suitable for bait digging are not listed on the sensitive species guidance. But the data should still be licensed and restricted under EIR. Data should not be proactively published.\tThe data has been collected under a contract run under the Wales non-licensable activities project.    Bait digging and collection of living resources was identified as an activity which is causing damage to the Marine Protection Area (MPA) network in Wales.  The data obtained from this contract provides NRW with evidence which will help inform discussions around future management of this activity.    A GIS layer was created through interpretation of the visible evidence of bait digging from the aerial images (NRW_DS124825 Bait digging areas aerial imagery 2019/2020).  The GIS layer was created by digitising around areas identified as impacted by bait digging. In addition the layer displays site name, confidence in interpretation, the age of the evidence (old/new) and digging intensity (low, medium and high) along with who captured the data, the date and image date.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-07-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-16T15:13:19.298Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18936\tNRW_DS124807\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.866Z\t952\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) Pathways Heatmaps for Wales 2020\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © RYA. © Crown Estate. © ABPmer. © Cefas. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383037\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Marine INNS Pathway Heatmaps\tInternal Custodian: Chloe Powell-Jennings, Senior Advisor (EMFF Biosecurity Project Officer), Southwest Operations.   Internal Custodian: Katherine Griffith, Senior Marine Advisor, Marine Area Management \u0026amp; Advice Team\tHeatmaps (geodatabases) were created for invasive non-native species pathways in Welsh waters including commercial shipping, recreational boating, aquaculture, fishing and offshore activities. Heatmaps were produced at both  2km x 2km and 5km x 5km resolutions and have associated symbology layers. An overall heatmap was also produced to highlight key areas which are likely most at risk from invasion of invasive non-native species. This assessment provides a valuable insight into hotspots related to each potential pathway of introduction, the areas where the main areas of risk overlap for the different pathways and their overlap with protected areas. The outputs from this assessment can form the basis to underpin biosecurity planning.       It should be noted, however, that this was a national scale mapping exercise based only on freely available datasets, which themselves have limitations and possible inaccuracies due to incomplete coverage, both temporally and spatially. The scorings applied to the input datasets and weightings applied to the introduction pathways are also based on available literature and expert judgement.  Site-specific activities and conditions will always be an important consideration, as well as temporal variations.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-08-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3602/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_marine_inns_pathways_heatmaps_for_wales_2020\t2025-12-03T17:20:52.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18942\tNRW_DS124808\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.856Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW Industry Regulation Teams\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383038\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Industry Regulation Teams.lyr\tSophie Lewis, Team Member, Customer Management/Customer Hub\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Fisheries Teams boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for Industry regulation Teams post Organisation Design, July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries, incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-07-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18927\tNRW_DS124809\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.673Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW Integrated Engineering Teams\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383039\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Integrated Engineering Teams.lyr\tMike Thomson, Specialist Advisor, Incident ManagementIncident and Emergencies, Flood and Operational Risk Management\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Integrated Engineering Teams boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for Integrated engineering Teams post Organisation Design, July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries, incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-07-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18945\tNRW_DS124810\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.864Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW Waste Regulation Teams\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383130\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Waste Regulation Teams.lyr\tSophie Lewis, Team Member, Customer Management/Customer Hub\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Waste regulation Team boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for Integrated engineering Teams post Organisation Design, July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries, incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-07-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18941\tNRW_DS124811\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.873Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurface Water Catchments\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.    Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.  Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from Environment Agency © EA and Getmapping Plc and Bluesky International Limited [2015].\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383131\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses\\Surface water catchments.lyr\tSimon James Flood Risk Analysis (FCRM) David Tarrant Flood Risk Analysis (FCRM) Mark Pugh\tThe datasets shows surface water catchment areas.  The data was produced to create the National Hazard Maps for Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses (2019) as part of the National Flood Hazard Maps (2019)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-07-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:14:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18933\tNRW_DS124813\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.639Z\t395\tValid\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Invertebrate Surveys\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383133\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Invertbrate points May.lyr X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Invertbrate points July.lyr X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Invertbrate polygons    https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landman/Protected-Sites/so-life/D1%20Physical%20Monitoring/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;clickparams\u003deyAiWC1BcHBOYW1lIiA6ICJNaWNyb3NvZnQgT3V0bG9vayIsICJYLUFwcFZlcnNpb24iIDogIjE2LjAuMTgyMjcuMjAyMjIiLCAiT1MiIDogIldpbmRvd3MiIH0%3D\u0026amp;CID\u003df14f7ca1%2D20e6%2D0000%2D415a%2D9e4a3862147a\u0026amp;cidOR\u003dSPO\u0026amp;RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Flandman%2FProtected%2DSites%2Fso%2Dlife%2FD1%20Physical%20Monitoring%2F00%20SoLIFE%20Data%20Archive%2FInvertebrates\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000DC881C721DC6C9439F0968E82C157F72  Final combined dataset for post and pre surveys in Darwin Core Format found here - Z:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\Data Mgmt\\Data Services Data Publishing Area\\NBN Data\\NRW_DS124813_SoLIFE Inverts\tInternal Custodian: Kathryn Hewitt, Team Leader, Sands of LIFE Project\tThis report presents the results of surveys carried out for the Sands of LIFE project to understand the impact of dune slack rejuvenation and other sand dune restoration work on the invertebrate fauna. Dune slacks are known to support a range of rare and specialist invertebrate species that are expected to benefit from the works.  Pitfall trapping, timed sweep netting and visual searches were undertaken across seven project sites: Merthyr Mawr, Kenfig, Whiteford Burrows, Pembrey Burrows, Morfa Harlech, Newborough and Tywyn Aberffraw. Morfa Dinlle was also surveyed but only in 2019. Surveys were carried out in May, July and August in three separate years, 2019, 2021 and 2022. At each project site survey plots were identified in 2019 at locations where future conservation management interventions were planned, as well as nearby control areas.   The data includes invertebrates (Coleoptera, Diptera, Araneae, Opiliones, Hymenoptera, Hemiptera (Heteroptera) identified to species level together with their frequency for each survey plot in each project site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-07-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-18T09:43:57.695Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18932\tNRW_DS124814\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.653Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Pierce, Alcove \u0026amp; Piercefield Woods SSSI, Coppice Mawr WGWE and Dingestow Court in SE Wales 2019\t***To be defined - likley to be classed as Open data****\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383134\tTo be entered into Recorder 6 - contact Mike Howe for R6 SurveyID\tMike Howe\t****Metadata to be supplied by Mike Howe************  Have asked Mke if should be combined with Metadata entry 124733, which seems like it could be same of similiar dataset.  Saproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Coppice Mawr, Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2019\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18943\tNRW_DS124815\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.897Z\t992\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Sand Lizard surveys\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383135\t[A] X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Sand Lizards 2019.lyr  [B] Spreadsheet version in DMS LAND-75481194-1033 and a copy provided to LERC (Cofnod)\tInternal Custodian: Toni Henwood, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South Wales, Sands of LIFE Project Internal Custodian: Kathryn Hewitt, Team Leader, Sands of LIFE Project\tLocations of lizard sightings during pre-intervention surveys for the Sands of LIFE project at Tywyn Aberffraw and Newborough Warren. Data was collected between April and October 2019.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-07-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:17:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18940\tNRW_DS124817\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.857Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWFD Rocky Shore Site Summary\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383137\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Rocky Shore\\WFD Rocky Shore Site Summary.lyr\tKate Borrowdale Swyddog Asesu Amgylcheddol Morol / Marine Environmental Assessment Officer Tîm Asesu ac Adrodd Monitro Morol / Marine Monitoring Assessment and Reporting Team\tWater Framework Directive rocky shore site summary spatial data. Monitoring locations and years when monitoring has taken place are included in the data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-08-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:38.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18928\tNRW_DS124820\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.667Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW North Assistant Flood Incident Duty Officer Areas\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383230\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW North Assistant Flood Incident Duty Officer Areas.lyr\tEirian Gilford, Warning \u0026amp; Informing Team Leader, NRW Gwyn Moseley, Warning \u0026amp; Informing Advisor 2, NRW\tAreas covered by the various Assisant Flood Incident Duty Officers within North Operations, with regards to flood incident management and operational response.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-08-14\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18935\tNRW_DS124825\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.694Z\t611\tValid\tEditable\tBait digging areas aerial imagery 2019/2020\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383235\tX:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Drone footage\\Intertidal Drone Photos 2019-2020.lyr\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe data has been collected under a contract run under the Wales non-licensable activities project.  Bait digging and collection of living resources was identified as an activity which is causing damage to the Marine Protection Area (MPA) network in Wales.    The data obtained from this contract provides NRW with evidence which will help inform discussions around future management of this activity.    The data contains 15 aerial images (.tiff format) of 12 separate priority intertidal sites in Wales which were suspected of having bait digging occurring.  The images were captured between October 2019 and March 2020 with a drone.  They display visual evidence of bait digging that can be picked up by sediment disturbance and patterning which indicates digging activity.    From these aerial images, a GIS layer (Bait Digging Areas 2019_2020 NRW_DS124806) was created through interpretation of the visible evidence of bait digging from the aerial images.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-07-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-16T15:11:06.219Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18937\tNRW_DS124827\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.886Z\t12\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Transect and Quadrat points\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383237\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh\tKate Borrowdale Swyddog Asesu Amgylcheddol Morol / Marine Environmental Assessment Officer Tîm Asesu ac Adrodd Monitro Morol / Marine Monitoring Assessment and Reporting Team\tWater Framework Directive saltmarsh monitoring. The points in these layers show the location of the saltmarsh transects (landward, seaward and zone transitions) and saltmarsh quadrats and also additional species (if spatial data was recorded).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-09-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18931\tNRW_DS124828\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.673Z\t765\tValid\tEditable\tIndustrial and Commercial Waste Survey Wales 2018\t© CNC/NRW Non -sensitive data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383238\tDMS\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Marcus Fogarty, Project Manager  Metadata Point of Contact:  Marcus Fogarty, Project Manager\tThe purpose of this study was to produce information on the quantities, origins (by industry sector and geographic region), and fate (management method) of industrial \u0026amp; commercial (I and C) wastes generated by businesses in Wales in 2018.  This information is required by Welsh Government for a variety of reasons including:  reporting on waste generation in compliance with the EU Waste Statistics Regulations 2002; informing the development of national waste policy; monitoring progress against national waste prevention and recycling targets; informing waste planners and the regulator; and providing data to the waste management industry to inform investment decisions.  The precision for the total waste generated is +/- 4.54% at 90% confidence.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-01-05\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2647\t2025-05-07T09:08:01.094Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18926\tNRW_DS124830\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.547Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNitrate Vulnerable Zones (NVZ) Groundwater Points\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383330\tX:\\EU Directives\\Nitrates Directive\\NVZ Groundwater Points.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:13:42.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18938\tNRW_DS124831\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.657Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNitrate Vulnerable Zones (NVZ) Shallow Sandy Soil\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383331\tX:\\EU Directives\\Nitrates Directive\\NVZ Shallow Sandy Soil.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:17:12.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18930\tNRW_DS124832\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.571Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNitrate Vulnerable Zones (NVZ) Surface Water Points\tData may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383332\tX:\\EU Directives\\Nitrates Directive\\NVZ Surfacewater Points.lyr\t\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:42.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18925\tNRW_DS124833\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.819Z\t9\tValid\tEditable\tThe Freshwater and Terrestrial Protected Sites Baseline Evaluation Project Results\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383333\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/en-gb/ourwork/Pages/Baseline-Evaluation-results-fuel-calls-for-partnership-approach-to-a-nature-rich-future-for-Wales.aspx\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dylan Lloyd, (Thomas Wright, Sue Byrne ), Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning.\tThe Freshwater and Terrestrial Protected Sites Baseline evaluation project (henceforth known as the ‘Baseline evaluation project’) uses existing evidence to derive, where possible, ‘indicative’ feature condition assessments across the range of freshwater and terrestrial features on protected sites (SAC, SPA, SSSI) in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-12-08\tpublication\tnull\thttps://naturalresources.wales/evidence-and-data/research-and-reports/protected-sites-baseline-assessment-2020/?lang\u003den\t2025-12-04T10:40:46.145Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n18923\tNRW_DS124838\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.699Z\t826\tValid\tEditable\tWater Resource Availability\t© CNC/NRW. Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology © NERC (CEH). Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383338\tX:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Resource Availability Wales.lyr\tEmma Amos Water Resource Planning Team\tThe Water Resource Availability data is based on a nationally consistent method and provides a national picture of Water Resource Availability for each Water Framework Directive Cycle 2 water body. Local Water Resource Availability for abstraction licensing is provided in our Abstraction Licensing Strategies (ALS) published on our website.  ALS outline the implications for abstraction licensing of the different water resource availability colours.  This national picture of Water Resource Availability may be different to local assessments in ALS particularly for regulated rivers and rivers designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) where local requirements may mean that results have been overridden.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-07-06\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3478/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wrgis_resource_availability\t2025-05-08T16:31:27.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18921\tNRW_DS124839\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.571Z\t20\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Risk Assessments for Cycle 3\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383339\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Risk Assessments\\2019\tInternal Data Custodian(s): David Allen, Monitoring Analysis Team Leader, NRW Metadata Point of Contact: David Allen, Monitoring Analysis Team Leader, NRW\tWe have reviewed water quality data and information on the types and magnitude of pressures affecting water bodies across Walest to assess how susceptible they are to those pressures and, in particular, estimate the likelihood of water bodies failing to meet their environmental quality objectives in the future, or deteriorating from their current condition. The methodology for each risk assessment was tailored to the specific pressure, but in general, it was an assessment of the scale of the pressure and the sensitivity of the water body.   The risk assessments are available as maps for each individual pressure, including: chemicals and metals, dissolved inorganic nitrogen, phosphates, dissolved oxygen and ammonia, faecal indicator organisms, acidification, abstraction and flow, invasive non-native species and sediment. They are valid until 2027. We will use the risk assessments to identify where more data is and prioritise our monitoring strategy, and support the development of local and national programmes of measures, including for pressures where Water Framework Directive classification data is missing.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:55:18.869Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18924\tNRW_DS124841\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.720Z\t786\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW)\t© CNC/NRW and UKCEH. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383431\tX\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Risk Assessment Wales\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tFlood Risk Assessment Wales provides a national assessment of risk flooding from Rivers, the Sea and Surface Water and Small Watercourses. The assessment takes into account flood defences and combines new, national-scale modelling with detailed local-scale models to categories risk into 3 bands, labelled ‘High’, ‘Medium’ and ‘Low’ risk.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-11-26\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/889/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:FloodRiskAssessmentWales\t2025-11-28T10:36:01.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18922\tNRW_DS124842\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.661Z\t924\tValid\tEditable\tRisk Levels Under Review\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383432\tCentral GIS Store X:\\Layer_Files\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tMark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)   Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe Risk Level Under Review identifies locations where model data incorporated to the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) map is under review and potentially liable to change within the next 6 months.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-11-28\tpublication\tnull\thttps://naturalresources.wales/flooding/check-your-flood-risk-by-postcode/?lang\u003den\t2025-12-19T12:14:04.101Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18919\tNRW_DS124843\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.439Z\t1455\tValid\tEditable\tAreas Benefiting From Flood Defences\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383433\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Areas Benefiting From Flood Defences\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe Areas Benefitting from Flood Defences show areas in Wales that benefit from flood defences that protect against flooding from Rivers and the Sea.  The areas shown benefit from different levels of flood protection and this is recognised in the risk classification shown in the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) map.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/594/?ows_service\u003dwms\tnull\t2025-12-01T11:29:04.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18920\tNRW_DS124844\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.572Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Fen Orchid Monitoring 2020\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383434\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Fen Orchid locations 2020.lyr    X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Slack boundaries Fen Orchid 2020.lyr    Species data held in DMS as document LAND-75481194-1312  (SOL-JBAU-LL-XX-DB-EN-0001-S3-PO1-Species_records.xls) Full DMS File path: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landman/Protected-Sites/so-life/D1%20Physical%20Monitoring/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Flandman%2FProtected%2DSites%2Fso%2Dlife%2FD1%20Physical%20Monitoring%2FKenfig%20Fen%20Orchid%20monitoring\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000DC881C721DC6C9439F0968E82C157F72\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BC00F4132%2D066F%2D4E23%2D8D53%2D597F2DDD6C62%7D\tInternal Custodian: Toni Henwood, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South Wales, Sands of LIFE Project Internal Custodian: Kathryn Hewitt, Team Leader, Sands of LIFE Project\tLocation and phenology of fen orchids in January 2016 North slack and Lpost Extension Scrape as recorded in August 2020. Boundaries of the slacks are also included. Species abundance data is also recorded in separate spreadsheets.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:35:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18918\tNRW_DS124845\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.360Z\t1016\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Risk Areas (Cycle 2)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383435\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe Cycle 2 Flood Risk Areas are those communities in Wales where most significant flood risks exist across all sources of flood risk. They were identified for the purpose of the second Preliminary Flood Risk Assessment under the Flood Risk Regulations 2009.   The threshold for communities at significant risk of flooding was set by Welsh Government with input from Natural Resources Wales and the Welsh Local Government Association (representing Lead Local Flood Authorities).    The Cycle 2 Flood Risk Areas were identified in December 2018 and replace the Cycle 1 Flood Risk Areas.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-06-29\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3359/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_flood_risk_areas_cycle_2\t2025-12-19T10:39:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18917\tNRW_DS124850\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.303Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFCW Woodland Officer Areas\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383530\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\FCW Woodland Officer Areas.lyr\tSuzanne Lilley-Owen, Permitting Team Leader Forestry, Permitting Services (Forestry)\tMap of Wales split into 8 sections, each section covers the area of responsibility for each of the Regulatory Woodland Officers. The attributes are the name, phone numbers, email address for the area covered. Purpose: Staff information\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18916\tNRW_DS124851\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.254Z\t1088\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Tree Cover 2018\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383531\tX:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\\tJohn Briggs\tThis study updates the data on Urban Tree Canopy Cover in Wales, highlighting changes in the extent, location and provision of urban tree cover since 2013. This spatial dataset relates to Phase 4 and consists of 2 individual layers, being Points (individual trees) and polygons (groups of trees).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-01-13\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/1109/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:UrbanTreeCover2018\t2025-05-20T08:53:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18974\tNRW_DS124858\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.490Z\t380\tValid\tEditable\tDistribution data for spider Mioxena blanda at Newborough Warren – Ynys Llanddwyn (SSSI) 2019-2020\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383538\tStored on the WID in Recorder 6\tMike Howe\tA survey to improve our understanding of the ecology and distribution of the Nationally Rare money spider Mioxena blanda was conducted at Newborough Warren – Ynys Llanddwyn SSSI. The survey ran between 28th September 2019 and 7th March 2020, employing a variety of survey techniques: terrestrial and aerial pitfall traps, vacuum sampling, grubbing, sieving, rock turning and hand sampling.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:01:51.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18963\tNRW_DS124859\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.221Z\t785\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring the Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae at Dale \u0026amp; South Marloes Coast SSSI in Pembrokeshire in 2020 and a survey for further populations on nearby beaches\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383539\tData held on the WID in Recorded 6\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThe Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae is found in Wales only within Dale \u0026amp; South Marloes Coast SSSI where it was first recorded in 1999 and again surveyed in 2006. The 2020 survey found that suitable habitat exists along approximately just 262 metres at Marloes Sands and 40 metres at West Dale Bay. A total of 287 adults (male and female) and nymphs of Scaly Cricket was recorded in 2020, indicating that breeding populations exist at both Marloes Sands and West Dale Bay. However, Scaly Cricket was found at just four monitoring plots at Marloes Sands and suitable habitat was present in just six plots.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T14:58:11.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18970\tNRW_DS124861\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.486Z\t420\tValid\tEditable\tIndicative feature condition assessments and reasons for failure for Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) habitats in Wales.\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains British Geological Survey Data © NERC. Data is available on request and may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383631\tY:\\Marine\\Marine and Coastal Policy and Planning Team\\MPA CIP Mapping\\FINAL Welsh SAC Designated Habitat Features Condition and Reasons for Failure.gdb  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine SAC features\\Indicative Assessments 2018.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine SAC features\\Indicative Assessments 2018\\Features.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine SAC features\\Indicative Assessments 2018\\Sites.lyr   Further spreadsheet located in the DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/mar/MPAImpProg/ProgDocs/Features_Sites_Reasons_for_failure_final.csv?d\u003dwdf33cfb0e9e74aedabbc8318ff0b5c78\tInternal Contact: Harriet Robinson, Specialist Advisor - Marine Systems, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tThis is a spatial dataset detailing the location of Special Area of Conservation (SAC) habitat features, their condition and reasons for failure where applicable.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-11-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T15:19:35.444Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18964\tNRW_DS124863\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.208Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW People and Places teams\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383633\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW People and Places teams.lyr\tMike Thomson, Specialist Advisor, Incident ManagementIncident and Emergencies, Flood and Operational Risk Management\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales People and Places team boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for People and Places Teams post Organisation Design, July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries, incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-06-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:48.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18971\tNRW_DS124865\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.425Z\t681\tValid\tEditable\tCompliance Assessment of Welsh River SACs against Phosphorus Targets\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383635\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Compliance with NRW River SAC Phosphorus Targets 2017-19 by Catchment Waterbodies X:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Phosphorus Sensitive SAC Freshwater Catchments\tTristan Hatton-Ellis\tRevisions to Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) monitoring guidance have required Natural Resources Wales (NRW) to review its conservation objectives for river Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales, notably with respect to phosphorus where targets have been substantially tightened.   This data was created to identify compliance with the revised phosphorus targets for Welsh river SACs. Phosphorus concentration data were extracted from the NRW water quality database (KiWQM), for a three-year period from January 2017 to December 2019 for all sample points within water bodies in the nine SACs designated for one or more river features. These were Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn; Afon Eden – Cors Goch Trawsfynydd; Meirionnydd Oakwoods (Afon Glaslyn); Afon Teifi; Afon Tywi; Afonydd Cleddau; River Dee \u0026amp; Llyn Tegid; River Usk; and River Wye. In total, 125 water bodies (river sections) were in scope.   Data were excluded where (i) detection limits were inadequate to assess compliance with the target or (ii) where the sample exceeded the recommended holding time of three days prior to analysis. Outliers in the data were identified and, if they were considered likely to be causing a failure on their own, data from other nutrient pollution indicators (biochemical oxygen demand, nitrate and ammonia) were checked to corroborate possible pollution events.   The data consists of two products: 1. An Excel spreadsheet summarising results and 2. An ArcGIS layer consisting of the WFD catchment area water bodies, clipped to the Welsh border and excluding water bodies not overlapping with river SAC boundaries.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-02-22\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/1113/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:ComplianceAssessmentOfWelshRiverSacsAgainstPhosphorusTargets\t2025-02-04T10:17:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18965\tNRW_DS124867\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.311Z\t11\tUnassessed\tEditable\tExtended Phase 1 Habitat Survey of the Gwent Levels Sea Wall October 2020\t© CNC/NRW.  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383637\tAvailable from NRW Library. DMS location: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/manbus/ManagingRegionsAndGroups/nrmu/Work%20Planning/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fmanbus%2FManagingRegionsAndGroups%2Fnrmu%2FWork%20Planning%2FProjects%2FSea%20Wall%20Biodiversity%20Project%2FSea%20Wall%20Botanical%20Survey%20%2D%20Map%20Files%20and%20Photos\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC854CF9F65FC344AFC2099255D45C99\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B21D5C4CC%2D0AD6%2D4D1D%2D8624%2DC4E9BB1EBEA0%7D\tInternal Contact: Catrin Grimstead, Senior Officer, Environment Team (Usk)\tThis GIS dataset maps an Extended Phase 1 Habitat Survey of the Gwent Levels Sea Wall between Cardiff and Chepstow completed in summer 2020. Red Data Book (RDB) species, invasive species, notable arable weeds and other notable species are also recorded. Habitats have also been assigned provisional NVC communities. Suggested management prescriptions to improve the value of the sea wall for Shrill Carder Bee, and general biodiversity are described and an evaluation of ‘landscape potential’ for Shrill Carder Bee is given.   There are five datasets:  SeaWall_Photo_point.lyr,   SeaWall_Summary.lyr,   SeaWall_Phase1.lyr,   SeaWall_Phase1_polyline.lyr,   SeaWall_LandscapeHabitatPotential.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Governance Licence and available for public use, see Limitation on Public Access and Use Constraints\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:36:03.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18967\tNRW_DS124868\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.349Z\t705\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales Water Vole Survey Data 2020\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383638\tDMS:  South West Wales Spreadsheet: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7B2F3B52BD-437F-4F34-A211-AF30841ABDAC%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dSurvey%20Records%20database%20SOUTH%20WEST%20final.xlsx  South East Wales Spreadsheet: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7BB492C1C2-7793-44A4-B1AB-CDAFB061BC1F%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dVOL5420%20Survey%20Records%20SE%20Wales%20Results.xlsx  North West Wales Spreadsheet: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7B1D52AED3-6E00-4411-A0C2-BC4BBD4D9B4B%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dWater%20Vole%20Survey%20Records%20North-West%20Area.xlsx  Mid East Wales Spreadsheet https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7B652E603B-A969-44EC-A40F-2F2EBFD435CC%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dSurvey%20Records%20MWT%202020-09.xlsx\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Internal Custodian: Jonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat \u0026amp; Resilience Mapping, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tThe Water Vole is now Wales fastest declining Mammal and has been classified as \u0027Endangered\u0027 in Wales in 2020 with the species disappearing from sites where it was previously present. Surveys were carried out in 2020 to assess the presence of Water Voles in areas which had previously recorded positive and negative sightings of the Water Voles across Wales. To accompany this the presence of Mink and Mink control were also recorded as the predator is a major cause in the decline of Water Vole in Wales.   The surveys were carried out across 4 regions in Wales - North West, Mid East, South East and South West which were then divided into separate sites. The Data is in a spreadsheet format to accompany four separate Natural Resources Wales Evidence Report on the 2020 Water Vole Surveys for Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:25:56.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18969\tNRW_DS124869\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.435Z\t935\tValid\tEditable\tWater Resource Reliability\t© CNC/NRW. Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology © NERC (CEH). Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose Attribution Statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology © NERC (CEH). Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383639\tX:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Resource Reliability Wales.lyr\tEmma Amos -   Specialist Advisor Water Resources\tThe Resource Reliability data indicates the percentage of time additional consumptive abstraction may be available and what this means for abstraction licensing. The Resource Reliability data is based on a nationally consistent method. Results have been overridden to reflect local assessments in our Abstraction Licensing Strategies (ALS) published on our website. ALS outline the implications for abstraction licensing of resource reliability.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-07-21\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3479/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_water_resource_reliability\t2025-02-04T10:38:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18959\tNRW_DS124871\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.068Z\t333\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGrey Seal Census for North Wales 2017\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383731\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mm/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7BF756400B-FB4D-4155-8CC5-BAAFB9FF1B70%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dOEL_NRW_SLP0817_DTA_FULL.xlsx\u0026amp;wdLOR\u003dc7EB90253-6835-4F64-B333-E6F032772D01\u0026amp;_DSL\u003d1\u0026amp;action\u003ddefault\u0026amp;mobileredirect\u003dtrue\tTom Stringell (Lead specialist Advisor: Marine Species)\tFull-ground based survey of Grey Seals undertaken between Aberystwyth and the Dee Estuary aimed to identify potential and active pupping sites throughout the North Wales and to estimate pup production for the 2017 season. All active and newly identified potential pupping sites were surveyed in 7 to 10 day cycles by boat, sea kayak and on foot. A drone was used to supplement pup counts and to quantify sampling error at The Skerries. A total of 126 potential and previously recorded pupping sites along the North Wales coast were defined\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-08-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:13:58.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18956\tNRW_DS124874\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.767Z\t10\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales National Forest\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution Statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383734\t\tPrimary Metadata contact: National Forest for Wales Team\tExtent of the National Forest as of the end of the first phase, 2021. Boundaries derived from Forester data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18968\tNRW_DS124875\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.242Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Export Cable 132kV\tUnredacted Data © CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Redacted Data © CNC/N