tatement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383832\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\Communications Mast.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThese shapefiles relate to the Communications Mast located within Cefn Croes\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-01-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:17:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18954\tNRW_DS124883\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.563Z\t8\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCefn Croes Windfarm - BT Fibre Optic Cable\tUnredacted Data © CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Redacted Data © CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383833\t*X:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\BT Fibre Optic Cable.lyr\t\tThese shapefiles relate to the Cefn Croes Windfarm BT Fibre Optic Cable within Welsh Government Woodland Estate This area forms part of the existing data held for ‘UG Telephone Lines on NRW Land’ – within the Utilities layer of MyMap\t\t2021-01-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18952\tNRW_DS124884\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.563Z\t204\tValid\tEditable\tNRW Woodland Names\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383834\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW Woodland Names.lyr\tData Custodian: Guy Bennett, Specialist Advisor NRW Estate Programming  Data Originator: Geraint Morgan, Specialist Advisor, Forester System\tThis data contains woodland names used across NRW to differentiate different parts of a Forest Block. Each section is part of a wider forest block and will coincide with one of seven NRW Operations Areas, aka Regions. Each polygon can be referred to as a ‘section’.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-01-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-09T14:15:02.061Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18951\tNRW_DS124885\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.568Z\t1137\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales Green Infrastructure Dataset 2021\t© CNC/NRW. © Ordnance Survey. © Welsh Local Authorities. Data may be re-used by Public Sector Geospatial Agreement (PSGA) Members only, under the terms and conditions of the PSGA licence, acknowledging both the source and owners copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. NRW does NOT grant permission to non PSGA members to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383835\tX:\\Social Economic\\Greenspaces\\Wales Green Infrastructure.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Pippa Jones, Geospatial Advisor, NRW  Metadata Point of Contact:  Pippa Jones, Geospatial Advisor, NRW\tThe Wales Green Infrastructure (GI) Data Set shows every bit of Wales including wetlands and water that is not built upon or covered with a man-made surface. It shows areas that are designated for nature conservation (such as Sites of Special Scientific Interest, Local Nature Reserves and local wildlife sites) and areas with a legal right of public access. At a lower level of confidence, the data set can show: a typology of sites (e.g. open space, amenity space etc.); management quality (e.g. Green Flag Awards); and accessibility (e.g. public parks, country parks etc). It shows where green space exists near where people live, if it is likely to be natural, and whether people are likely to be able to get into it.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-09-16\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:39:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18953\tNRW_DS124889\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.574Z\t848\tValid\tEditable\tPublic Service Boards\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution Statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383839\tX:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Public Service Boards.lyr\tData Custodian: Oliver Ishmael, Geospatial Advisor\tThis dataset shows the boundary of Wales\u0027 19 Public Service Boards (PSB). The PSB improve joint working across all public services in each local authority area in Wales. Each board must carry out a well-being assessment and publish an annual local well-being plan.   The plan sets out how they will meet their responsibilities under the Well being of Future Generations (Wales) Act. Further Information: https://gov.wales/public-services-boards https://www.futuregenerations.wales/work/public-service-boards/\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-07-21\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3511/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_public_service_boards\t2025-05-09T10:43:50.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18950\tNRW_DS124900\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.556Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Great Crested Newt\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393030\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Aberfraw Ponds - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Aberfraw Terrestrial - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Aberfraw Terrestrial Refuges - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Harlech Ponds - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Harlech Terrestrial - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Kenfig Ponds - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Kenfig Terrestrial - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Newborough Ponds - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Newborough Terrestrial - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Newborough Terrestrial Refuges - Great Crested Newt.lyr\tToni Henwood, Team Member 2, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South Wales Sands of LIFE\tResults of pond surveys to look for Great Crested Newt breeding. Results of habitat assessment for areas of suitable terrestrial habitat for Great Crested Newt\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-02-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18949\tNRW_DS124902\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.478Z\t260\tValid\tEditable\tOtter Survey of Wales 2015 - 2018\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393032\tDMS - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/Otter Data/Distribution %26 Population/2017 Otter Survey Wales/2017 Survey Only/Master data of NRW 2020 Otter Survey and hydrology contract_FINAL.csv?d\u003dw3c7e9be636d045acb28103d6f7cd0d0f\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1  EVID-285-2145\tRebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Mammals, Biodiversity and Ecosystem Resilience Group\tThis data is an Excel spreadsheet detailing the 6th Otter Survey of Wales results and hydrology analysis. The data was collected by expert surveyors and volunteers to enable analysis of otter presence across Wales.     The data has been used for the 6th Otter Survey of Wales but may be useful for other research purposes.  Data collected in 1991, 2002 and 2009-2010 is described separately (see metadata entry 116334).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-09-14\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-09T14:09:44.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18955\tNRW_DS124903\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.559Z\t1889\tValid\tEditable\tNRW Open Water Quality Archive\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393033\tKiWQM\tMartin Stephens\tNRW’s water quality archive provides data from samples collected around Wales, including coastal, estuary, rivers, lakes, ponds, canals, sewage discharges, trade discharges, pollution investigation points and waste sites.   NRW holds data goes back to 1962 whereas the open data product contains data from 2000 onwards. The open data product is extracted from NRW’s Water Quality database (KiWQM) and updated every month.   Some field measurements are taken in situ at the sampling station, whereas some samples are analysed in laboratories to measure aspects of the water quality. Once samples are taken, they must be processed and verified before they are added to the water quality archive. Therefore, there is a delay between samples being taken and the data being made available. Data may also be subject to change after publication, but the archive will be updated each month to include corrections.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-23\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_water_quality_archive_stations\t2026-02-24T15:26:48.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18948\tNRW_DS124905\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.185Z\t305\tValid\tEditable\tCommunity Flood Plans\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate sensitive data. Non-sensitive data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose and to take note of any information warnings and limitations provided.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393035\tTop Copy: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/waterman/frm/aware/Results%20%20Reporting/Reports/CFP%20Update%20Live.xls?d\u003dw671f401b42de404f81b240fe7cf67f69                                                                       A GIS version of the data is available at: X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Community Flood Plans.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Hannah Davies, Team Member 5, Community Engagement and Resilience (Flood) Kelly McLauchlan, Lead Specialist Advisor, Community Engagement and Resilience (Flood)         Although the data contains personal data, this is in the top copy and should have been redacted from the GIS layer.  Providers have consented to provide their personal data.\tA community flood plan is an account of information and actions, agreed by communities at risk of flooding across Wales. The aim and content of the plan depends on the needs and motivations of the community.   Community flood plans are volunteer and community led.   Data presented is a snapshot and only based on data disclosed to NRW, rather than live information managed by NRW.    This data is collected during the annual maintenance of community flood plans across Wales, at the start of financial year. The data is collected by Operation Warning \u0026amp; Informing teams and collated by the EPP Community Engagement and Resilience Teams.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-06-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-08T11:12:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18947\tNRW_DS124913\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.229Z\t1169\tValid\tEditable\tTranquillity and Place: Dark Skies\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © Earth Observation Group, Payne Institute for Public Policy. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393133\tX:\\Physical\\Light Pollution\\Night lights.lyr  Data also includes a set of maps produced in JPEG format and an Excel spreadsheet containing the zonal statistics analysis.\tJill Bullen - Lead Specialist Advisor, Landscape\tDark skies can affect our experience of nature, landscapes and greenspaces and can benefit our health and wellbeing and local wildlife.   The Dark Sky and Light Pollution Dataset for Wales has been created to identify the strategic and local resource in remote, rural, peri-urban and urban areas for use as an evidence base to inform policy intent, practice and provision for benefits to benefits to wellbeing.    The dataset is based upon satellite imagery taken at 1:30 A.M in December 2019 by the United States National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) Suomi National Polar-orbiting satellite with the Visible Infrared Imaging Radiometer Suite (VIIRS) capturing visible and infrared imagery used in this dataset and later processed in 2021.    A set of maps have been created to display the dark skies data against the following geographical boundaries:   - Local Authority areas   - Future Wales boundaries    - Designated landscapes (AONBs and National Parks)   - NRW Operational Areas   - National Landscape Character Areas   - LANDMAP Visual \u0026amp; Sensory aspect areas\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-04-04\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/5877\t2025-11-28T15:33:08.015Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18946\tNRW_DS124914\t2024-05-28T12:59:08.999Z\t801\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ) zooplankton survey\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393134\tT:\\shared drive\\data\\biological data\\plankton.    The data series has been submitted for archive at DASSH and may be onward published as open data to the NBN, EMODnet or eur/OBIS and will be added to regulally.                              2021 data has been added to the NBN by DASSH on behlaf of NRW\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Burton - Marine Environmental Assessment Officer  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Burton - Marine Environmental Assessment Officer\tTime series of consistent plankton samples taken using a Zooplankton net (200um mesh) from a single point on the North side of Skomer island. The net is hauled vertically from 40m and the methods used are designed to replicate the methods used by the Plymouth Marine Laboratory at the L4 plankton sampling station. Taxa are enumerated and recorded to various levels of taxonomic resolution depending on the group. Different taxonomists have been used throughout the time series but the data has been truncated to a standard species list.  As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  In 2023, data from 2021 Survey was made available through the NBNAtlas by the DASSH Data Archive Centre on behalf of NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-03-24\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr2929\t2026-01-09T15:18:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18993\tNRW_DS124915\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.096Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) National Vegetation Classification Survey Data\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393135\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Boundaries.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Mospoly.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Photographs.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Polygons.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Quadrats.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Target Notes.lyr\tToni Henwood, Team Member 2, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South Wales Sands of LIFE\tData from a National Vegetetation Classificaiotn survey of 10 sand dune sites including shapefiles of NVC polygons, vegetation mosaics, target notes, quadrats, photograph locations and overall survey boundary. Data collected in 2019 based on field survey of entire sites with field maps digitised to OS Mastermap and aerial photographs.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-03-25\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:23:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18997\tNRW_DS124916\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.114Z\t830\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Marine Phytoplankton - Geospatial\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393136\tData is stored in WISKI  Geospatial Layers located in -  X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Marine Phytoplankton Taxa Detail.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Marine Phytoplankton Site Detail.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Marine Phytoplankton Sample Detail.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Bianca Bacchi, Senior Marine Monitoring Officer  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer\tThis data collates all the Phytoplankton samples collected within Wales from 2007 onwards by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales that are held within NRWs KiEco Database. This methodology is based on the principle that additional nutrients (especially nitrogen) can alter the amount of phytoplankton in transitional and coastal waters. Persistent and high counts of phytoplankton taxa are also seen as a measure of disturbance in the phytoplankton community. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring for legislative requirements and to meet organisational drivers. In addition, sites may be sampled for investigative work, or on an ad hoc basis.  Phytoplankton samples are taken in situ and are usually sent to a specialist marine contractor for Species Identity and abundance (two labs have been used in this time period; CEFAS 2007 – 2009 \u0026amp; APEM 2010 – onwards). Phytoplankton samples are analysed (identified and counted) using the Utermöhl method. Further information is available from the UK-TAG WFD website.  For many of our marine survey techniques we record an NGR at the site because this acts as a central/representative location around which we collect lots of samples. An NGR is then also recorded against each of the samples to give a more accurate sampling location.  A Darwin Core version of this data which is published to the NBN Atlas is available in NRW_DS161379.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-24\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6723/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_marine_phytoplankton\t2026-02-25T10:35:37.250Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n19003\tNRW_DS124922\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.473Z\t900\tValid\tEditable\tRumney Great Wharf to River Usk Drone Survey 2021\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393232\tZ:Drive Location: Archive/ Land Mgmt/ Protected Sites/ Rumney Great Wharf to River Usk Drone Survey\tSarah Revill - Senior Marine Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) wish to establish a topographical baseline and aerial imagery of the foreshore and surrounds to help inform the consideration of possible nature-based solutions at this location. The purpose of the survey was to collect topographic data of the foreshore, along the 12km frontage that stretches between Cardiff in the west and Newport to the east. Vertical images were taken for the photogrammetric reconstruction. Flights were also undertaken to capture oblique imagery looking back at the coastline. A key constraint of this survey was being unable to take GPS points out in the mudflats. To overcome this, a RTK capable drone was used, which reduces the reliance on ground control points by ensuring that the geolocation for each image is significantly more accurate than on a standard drone.  The survey was carried out over a period of 6 days between the 3rd and 12th of February 2021.  Flights were planned to ensure lowest possible tides. In total, 19 flights were completed to collect the aerial data, taking a combined 285 minutes (4.75 hours). Survey deliverables comprised ASCII Grid, Orthophotos and Obliques\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-17T11:38:23.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18991\tNRW_DS124923\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.954Z\t1150\tValid\tEditable\tRestoring marine and coastal habitats in Wales: identifying spatial opportunities and benefits\tOutput data: © CNC/NRW. Unrestricted data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Input data: © various. Data may be re-used under the terms of the 3rd Party Data licence made available with the data (see lineage). It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for purpose and it is advised that you obtain the latest copy from the provider.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393233\tMain GIS Data Layers are located in:   • X:\\Biological\\Habitat Restoration\\Marine Habitat   Further Mapping Packages for the report are stored in:   • Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Restoration\\ABPMer Restoration opportunities GIS data\\March\\Geodatabase\\Report_figure_mxd\tKate Griffith - Specialist Advisor Marine Sustainable Management of Natural Resources\tIn order to better understand opportunities to build resilience in the Welsh marine environment, a report was commissioned by Natural Resources Wales to identify potential spatial opportunities to restore valuable marine habitats.     The report created spatial products (Arc Geo database) for: areas in the floodplain that might be suitable for the creation of mudflat and saltmarsh through managed realignment and areas where the right conditions might exist for native oyster habitat and horse mussel bed restoration. The horse mussel data was provided from JNCC. The report also addresses restoration potential for Sabellaria alveolata reefs although no spatial data was created for this habitat and seagrass habitat although this was provided as a map image from JNCC.    These spatial Data layers should be considered as initial aides to identifying potential locations or areas but come with several limitations which have been discussed in the report. Any restoration projects should undergo further site validation and local engagement before proceeding with any specific site.    The following GIS datasets were created for this:  Saltmarsh_Mudflat_Opportunities_Opportunities_Floodplains_SMPpolicy,  Native_Oyster_Opportunities,   Wave_Height_Grid_ABPmerSeastates,  Current_Speed_Grid_ABPmerSeastates,  Historic_Saltmarsh_Extents,  S_alveolata_records_historic_and_current,  O_edulis_records_historic_and_current,  M_modiolus_records_historic_and_current,  Potential_Beneficial_Use_Sources.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-07-21\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3599/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_restoring_marine_and_coastal_habitats_in_wales_identifying_spatial_opportunities_and_benefits\t2025-12-03T16:09:36.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18996\tNRW_DS124925\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.073Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOperational Small Scale Hydro Projects\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393235\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Small Scale Hydro Projects.lyr\tDave Farmery, Senior Officer, Energy Delivery Manager, NRW Enid Carrick, Project Support Officer, NRW\tThis shapefile relates to Small Scale Hydro Projects that are sited on Natural Resources Wales managed estate and facilitated by the Energy Delivery Team Data received from Project Manager and supporting officers during construction phase\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-04-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:26:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19005\tNRW_DS124928\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.757Z\t778\tValid\tEditable\tWelsh Lowland Raised Bog Inventory\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393238\tX drive layer file: X:\\Physical\\Peat\\Welsh Lowland Raised Bog Inventory.lyr  DMS Word document explaining the attribute domains: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/peatl/Evidence Analysis Projects/Welsh Lowland Raised Bog Inventory/Welsh Lowland Raised Bog Inventory ArcGIS data structure.docx?d\u003dw7065453cc6ca43d887e02948ebf77083\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\tKathryn Birch- Specialist Advisor Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThis dataset maps the distribution of Lowland Raised Bogs within Wales, giving details on their size, elevation, conservation status, context, and whether they have been surveyed. It was compiled using data from the Phase 2 Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales (2004-2020), and aerial photographs.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-07-20\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3480/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_welsh_lowland_raised_bog_inventory\t2025-05-09T11:05:06.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18998\tNRW_DS124929\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.051Z\t532\tValid\tEditable\tVisible Settings of Designated Landscapes in Wales\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393239\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Visible settings of landscapes in Wales.lyr\tJohn Briggs, Specialist Advisor: Landscape, NRW\tNature of data: showing the extent of areas visible from a range of landscape-scale polygons and view points.    Purpose of data: To assist strategic site choices when planning visually prominent development types. A web page with general introduction and links to map images produced from the data is available from Natural resources Wales. This is new data output, believed to be seminal for Wales.    Resolution and extent: The visibility mapping uses a 30m resolution open-source digital terrain model for the whole of Wales, out to maximum viewing distance of 35km, including overlaps to/from England where relevant. The 30m digital terrain model was sourced from the Shuttle Radar Topography Mission (Van Zyl, 2001) and processed using the Quantum GIS plugin developed by Cuckovic (2016). See page 23 of technical report (via the link above) for more detail.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-02-19\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/4340\t2025-11-28T15:50:54.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18992\tNRW_DS124932\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.047Z\t413\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCrymlyn Bog SSSI; Rapid appraisal of Himalayan balsam Impatiens glandulifera distribution: 2020 – GIS dataset\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393332\tX:\\Biological\\LIFE\\Crymlyn Impatiens glandulifera 2020.lyr  X:\\Biological\\LIFE\\Crymlyn Impatiens Survey Route 2020.lyr\tJohn Ratcliffe - Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tA rapid survey of Himalayan balsam Impatiens glandulifera on Crymlyn Bog SSSI and Pant y Sais SSSI was undertaken 7-9 Sept 2020 using free-survey method. Observations were made from the defined route and estimates of extent marked on a 1:10000 scale field map and later transcribed to ArcGIS.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-06-03\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:27:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18989\tNRW_DS124933\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.806Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Framework: Pilot projects to prove concept and approach – GIS dataset\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393333\tY:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Upland Framework\\Eryri Upland Options.shp Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Upland Framework\\Mynydd Du Trial Upland Options.shp Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Upland Framework\\Eryri Upland Options.lyr   Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Upland Framework\\Mynydd Du Trial Upland Options.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): John Ratcliffe, Specialist Adviser, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team.  Metadata Point of Contact: John Ratcliffe, Specialist Adviser, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team.\tIn a pilot project, two maps of upland areas; Eryri in North Wales and Mynydd Du in West Wales, were created to indicate the preferred habitat cover over the long-term. This was undertaken using available habitat and land cover data (Phase 1 / Habitat Survey of Wales, Phase 2 / National Vegetation Survey, unified peatland data, aerial imagery, Environmental Record Centre species data, BSBI vascular plant data, RSPB data etc.) consideration of designated features and conservation objectives for protected sites, along with local expert knowledge. In the case of Eryri, extensive local expert knowledge was relied on; in contrast in the case of Mynydd Du, consultation with graziers on the Common Land led to a suggested approach, covering part of the site, for further consultation with Commoners. Both maps are recommendations for future habitat but should be used as a starting point, in discussion with other interests, to determine the practical objectives for particular parcels of land.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-06-02\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:40:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18990\tNRW_DS124939\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.826Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClocaenog Forest Windfarm 33 kV Cables\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393339\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\33KV Cables.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team       To be reviewed by Data Services\tThis dataset shows the Clocaenog windfarm 33 kV cables. 33kV cables are the six underground electric cable circuits, coming from the wind turbine power systems. They terminate at the sub-station on site. This is the final true data received from the developer (RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:24:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19000\tNRW_DS124942\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.124Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClocaenog Forest Windfarm Turbines\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393432\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\Turbine Building Line.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows Clocaenog Forest Windfarm (as built) wind turbine building lines. This shows all 27 turbines on the Clocaenog Forest Windfarm. Each Vestas wind turbine is 3.6MW in power. This is the final true data received from the developer (RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:27:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19002\tNRW_DS124943\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.255Z\t587\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClocaenog Forest Windfarm Substation\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393433\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\Substation.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows Clocaenog Forest Windfarm Substation Location. The location of the on-site substation. This is where transformers change the 33kV input from the wind turbines to a 132kV rating for use in the National Grid. This the final true data received from the developer ((RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:27:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18994\tNRW_DS124948\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.038Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClocaenog Forest Windfarm Turbines Point Location\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393438\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\Turbines.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows Clocaenog Forest Windfarm (as built) wind turbine point locations. This shows all 27 turbines on the Clocaenog Forest Windfarm. Each Vestas wind turbine is 3.6MW in power. This is the final true data received from the developer (RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:27:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18995\tNRW_DS124949\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.106Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClocaenog Forest Electrical Marker Posts\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393439\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\Electrical Marker Post.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis dataset shows Clocaenog Forest Windfarm (as built) electrical marker post locations. This shows all 382 electrical marker posts on the Clocaenog Forest Windfarm. Electrical marker posts are located along the line of the 33kV cables, for operational health and safety ground truthing. This is the final true data received from the developer (RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:24:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18988\tNRW_DS124950\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.723Z\t314\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClocaenog Forest Windfarm Site Boundary\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393530\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\Development Boundary.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tThis shapefile shows Clocaenog Forest Windfarm Construction Site Boundary. The boundary that was granted by the Local Planning Authority and was used as the Construction Design Management Regulations 2015 (CDM 2015) area. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:13:58.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18999\tNRW_DS124992\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.023Z\t1160\tValid\tEditable\tDrinking Water Protected Areas for Groundwater in Wales\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393932\tx:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Groundwater Drinking Water Protected Areas.lyr\tDave Jones - Senior Specialist Advisor Geoscience\tAll groundwater bodies in Wales are designated as drinking water protected areas (DrWPAs). This aims to protect groundwater from over-abstraction and to prevent deterioration in groundwater quality that could increase the treatment of drinking water. Drinking Water Protected Areas are required to be identified under the Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-04-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19001\tNRW_DS124997\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.218Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLocations where Coastal Adaptations measures may affect Coastal Access over the lifetime of the Shoreline Management Plans\tTo be added\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393937\tNRW File Storage Z:DRIVE - Archive - Access Recreation \u0026amp; Education - Access and Recreation Programmes and Projs - Wales Coast Path \u0026amp; National Trails\tNicola Rimington - Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine and Coastal Physical Process and Coastal Management\tThe purpose of the survey was to determine which coastal defences and coastal footpaths could be affected by the implementation of a Shoreline Management Plan policy unit classified as No Active Intervention or Managed Realignment. For the affected areas of footpaths the objectives of the survey were to record the total lengths of footpath affected, the Shoreline Management Plan policy unit and Local Authority area the footpaths are located and when the coastal footpaths could be affected.  This data from this survey contains the following outputs, •A spreadsheet of all affected footpaths which are listed by a unique path identifier as well as the associated costal coastal defence identifier and each intersecting policy unit. This also includes the The local authority in which the section of path is located and the length (in metres) of path is also included. •GIS shapefiles of potentially affected footpaths identified in this study. •GIS shapefiles of coastal defences and the ‘affected zone’ of potential influence of defences used in the study.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:49:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18986\tNRW_DS125005\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.684Z\t400\tValid\tEditable\tVisitor count data at a selection of NRW sites\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303035\tLineTop: ‘Z:\\Archive\\Access Recreation \u0026amp; Education\\Recreation Data \u0026amp; Knowledge\\LineTop\\CSV Archive\\Data Transfer Prep\\For_Chambers’. Grafana: data is downloaded as CSV from the Grafana portal.  GIS Data - X:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Visitor counters.lyr\tDavid Liddy, Specialist Advisor Recreational Safety and Heather Crump.\tFor the purpose of monitoring its visitor centres, National Nature Reserves (NNR) and bike trails, Natural Resources Wales contracted Linetop LTD to provide data and reporting on visitor numbers.   Reports are produced quarterly and provide a breakdown of visitor numbers in each site and reserve.  This data includes:   Site cards - which provide details of counter placements and specification.   Raw Count data - raw counts from sensors.  Reports - summary data provided as MS Word documents at regular intervals.  This metadata entry also covers the Grafana Chambers Electronics downloadable visitor counter data which is time stamp and number count data, which is affectively counting the same thing as the Linetop data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-09-01-2025-07-23\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18987\tNRW_DS125074\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.702Z\t1222\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Map for Planning (FMfP) TAN15 Defended Zones\t© CNC/NRW and UKCEH. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303734\tGIS Corporate Data Store X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe Flood Map for Planning (FMfP) includes climate change information to show how this will affect flood risk extents for rivers, the sea and surface water and small watercourses over the next century. It shows the potential extent of flooding assuming no defences are in place.  It also indicates areas at risk from coastal erosion, areas that have flooded in the past and key information on the location of flood defence infrastructure.     The FMfP can be considered as the best available information on flood risk and will replace the Development Advice Map for planning purposes when the Welsh Government implements the revised TAN15.  Although it currently has no official status for planning purposes it is a useful data source when considering flood risks.  The coming into force of the new TAN 15 and the Flood Map for Planning has been suspended due to a further consultation on the TAN.  Technical advice note (TAN) 15: development, flooding and coastal erosion - https://www.gov.wales/technical-advice-note-tan-15-development-flooding-and-coastal-erosion  The FMfP introduces a new layer called the TAN15 Defended Zone.  This shows areas that benefit from risk management authority owned flood defence infrastructure, that have a minimum, Present Day level of protection of:     • 1% (1 in 100) annual event probability (AEP) for rivers, or   • 0.5% (1 in 200) annual event probability (AEP) for the sea.     Flood defences built after 1 January 2016 must also include design freeboard and an allowance for climate change.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/2065/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_TAN15_DEFENDED_ZONES\t2025-12-01T11:32:30.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19004\tNRW_DS125075\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.662Z\t502\tValid\tEditable\tAberdovey Sand Dune drone survey 2021\t© CNC/NRW and © STRI Ltd. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303735\tx:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Drone footage\\Aberdovey Sand Dunes 32WDF18RA AGC 2021.lyr\tKatrina Jenkinson, Senior Officer (Ecologist), Dwyfor and Meirionnydd Environment Team\tThis drone survey data was collected as part of a Section 16 Management Agreement (Environment (Wales) Act 2016) between Aberdovey Golf Club and NRW. The aim was to have baseline data on the sand dune line and produce an up-to-date accurate map of the current coastline.    The survey will be repeated at the end of this agreement / start of a renewal.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-06-16\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-09T13:26:24.883Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18984\tNRW_DS125076\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.396Z\t1767\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Map for Planning Flood Zones 2 and 3\t© CNC/NRW and UKCEH. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303736\tGIS Corporate Data Store X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe Flood Map for Planning (FMfP) includes climate change information to show how this will affect flood risk extents for rivers, the sea and surface water and small watercourses over the next century. It shows the potential extent of flooding assuming no defences are in place.  It also indicates areas at risk from coastal erosion, areas that have flooded in the past and key information on the location of flood defence infrastructure.     The FMfP can be considered as the best available information on flood risk and will replace the Development Advice Map for planning purposes when the Welsh Government implements the revised TAN15.  Although it currently has no official status for planning purposes it is a useful data source when considering flood risks.  The coming into force of the new TAN 15 and the Flood Map for Planning has been suspended due to a further consultation on the TAN.  Technical advice note (TAN) 15: development, flooding and coastal erosion - https://www.gov.wales/technical-advice-note-tan-15-development-flooding-and-coastal-erosion   The Flood Zones within the FMfP shows the undefended risk of flooding from Rivers, the Sea and from Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses.    Flood Zone 3 displays the extent of flooding from:  • rivers with a 1% (1 in 100) chance or greater of happening in any given year, including an allowance for climate change.  • the sea with a 0.5% (1 in 200) chance or greater of happening in any given year, including an allowance for climate change.  • Surface water \u0026amp; small watercourses with a 1% (1 in 100) chance or greater of happening in any given year, including an allowance for climate change.     Flood Zone 2 displays the extent of flooding from:  • Rivers with less than 1% (1 in 100) but greater than or equal to 0.1% (1 in 1,000) chance of happening in any given year, including an allowance for climate change.  • the Sea with less than 0.5% (1 in 200) but greater than or equal to 0.1% (1 in 1,000) chance of flooding in any given year, including an allowance for climate change.  • Surface water \u0026amp; small watercourses with less than 1% (1 in 100) but greater than or equal to 0.1% (1 in 1,000) chance of happening in any given year, including an allowance for climate change.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-11-26\tpublication\tnull\thttps://flood-map-for-planning.naturalresources.wales/\t2025-11-28T10:35:09.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18982\tNRW_DS125080\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.209Z\t850\tValid\tEditable\tNRW Local Model Manager\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303830\tGIS Corporate Data Store X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe ‘NRW Local Model Manager’ shows the location of NRW’s detailed, local flood models which can be requested via datadistribution@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/2069/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_MODEL_MANAGER\t2025-12-01T11:30:38.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18985\tNRW_DS125083\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.387Z\t949\tValid\tEditable\tMaterials Facilities Reporting\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303833\tWaste Returns Database\tInternal Data Custodian(s): John Fry, Waste Policy, EPP Metadata Point of Contact: waste.returns@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tThis displays the quarterly Operator reported, input and output sampling data that qualifying Materials Facilities are required to provide by the regulations. This excludes additional information reported by sites that is not required to be sampled.   Data prior to 2020 was previously published for England and Wales on the WRAP Portal.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-05-07\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2648\t2025-12-19T10:45:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18983\tNRW_DS125112\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.388Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Fen Orchid Monitoring 2021\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313132\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Fen Orchid locations 2021.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Toni Henwood, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South Wales, Sands of LIFE Project Internal Custodian: Kathryn Hewitt, Team Leader, Sands of LIFE Project\tLocation and phenology of fen orchids in January 2016 North slack and Lpost Extension Scrape as recorded in August 2021. Data also available Excel spreadsheet\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-06-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:38.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18979\tNRW_DS125154\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.068Z\t321\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAn invertebrate survey of Llangennith Moors 2018\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313534\tData stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder.\tMike Howe Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tLlangennith Moors grazing marsh lies at the extreme western end of the Gower Peninsula. Before this survey, the invertebrate interest of the site was little known, with the only previous survey having been carried out in July 1991 which had a focus upon Diptera. Due to the minimal invertebrate knowledge of the site and relative absence of ditch maintenance there since the 1990s, Natural Resources Wales commissioned a survey of ditch and fen invertebrates and a search for two whorl snails of European importance, the Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior (which is found on the nearby Whiteford Burrows and Oxwich Burrows) and Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana, which is found in similar habitat on the south Llŷn coast in North Wales. The surveys were aimed at determining the current invertebrate interest of Llangennith Moors following a sea wall breach and subsequent managed retreat at Cwm Ivy Marsh and the loss of grazing marsh to saltmarsh. The Llangennith surveys were undertaken simultaneously by two surveyors in October 2018.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-12-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T15:20:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18980\tNRW_DS125158\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.217Z\t129\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Non-Native Marine Species\t© CNC/NRW and various third-party recorders NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313538\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Non Native Species All Wales Marine July 2024.lyr\tDave Tavmer: Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Mapping\tThis dataset comprises data on marine non-native species in Wales.  The purpose it to gain an up to date understanding of the distribution of marine non-native species to inform their spread and inform casework advice. The data used to build this GIS layer comes from existing relevant locations where data is held and has been amalgamated into one location in ESRI format. It contains data from the 1930s up until 2023.  No new surveys were carried out for this dataset.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-11-26\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/7212/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_marine_inns\t2026-01-19T11:19:01.096Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18981\tNRW_DS125168\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.228Z\t301\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNDF Preassessed areas for wind energy\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313638\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\NDF Preassessed areas for wind energy.lyr\tOlwen Maidment\tAreas with a presumption in favour of large-scale wind energy development.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-09T11:38:29.083Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18978\tNRW_DS125172\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.053Z\t863\tValid\tEditable\tAssessing the vulnerability of Annex I marine habitats to climate change in Wales (2021)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313732\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability Assessment\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dr. Karen Robinson  Metadata Point of Contact:  Dr. Karen Robinson\tThis project assessed the potential impact of climate change pressures on SACs and their Annex 1 features in Wales under two different climate change emission scenarios from 2025 – 2100. The outputs from the project were a report, GIS layers showing vulnerability levels for features within each site (based on their component biotopes) and a series of excel workbooks summarising vulnerabilities of each site and feature by pressure type.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-12-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T15:25:45.549Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18977\tNRW_DS125181\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.054Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tScallop Fisheries Orders (closed and unrestricted areas)\t© CNC/NRW and ....... Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.  May contain data owned by WG.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313831\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries Orders\\Scallop order closed areas.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries Orders\\Cardigan Bay Unrestricted Scallop Area.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries Orders\\Cardigan Bay Unrestricted Scallop Area Points.lyr\tData Custodian: Alexander Scorey Possibly Colin Charman.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18976\tNRW_DS125183\t2024-05-28T12:59:48.892Z\t15\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) UAV Survey 2020\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313833\tX:\\Basemapping\\LiDAR\\SoLIFE File Name: Sands of Life LIDAR – Orthophotography.lyr\tSands of LIFE Team, NRW\tUnmanned aerial vehicle (drone) survey of the Sand of LIFE (SoLIFE) sand dunes sites at the Gower, Newborough Warren, Pembrey Coast, Kenfig and Merthyr Mawr. Survey data includes high resolution aerial imagery and photogrammetry derived Digital Elevation Models.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:18:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19023\tNRW_DS125188\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.478Z\t458\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPolitical Boundaries of Wales\t© Ordnance Survey Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution Statement:  You must acknowledge the copyright and the source of the data, as follows:  Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right [year] If you use Code-Point Open data, you must also display the following: Contains Royal Mail data © Royal Mail copyright and Database right [year] / Contains National Statistics data © Crown copyright and database right [year]\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313838\tNRWGDBV.GISDATA.WALES X:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Wales.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Olly Ishmael\tPolitical boundary of Wales\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:01.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19020\tNRW_DS125193\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.490Z\t639\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMynydd Du Vegetation Map 2019\t© CNC/NRW, © Ordnance Survey and © JNCC. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313933\tENVI format classified image at  Z:\\Archive\\Sampling Monitoring \u0026amp; Analysis Reporting\\Remote Sensing\\Mynydd_Du\\updated_map\\Mynydd Du Vegetation Map 2019.hdr and .dat\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Philip Jutson, Monitoring Analysis Team  Metadata Point of Contact:  Philip Jutson, Monitoring Analysis Team\tThe extent of land cover classes on the common lands of Mynydd Du in the western end of the Brecon Beacons National Park derived from satellite imagery.  The classes include dominant vegetation type, rock outcrop and scree, road and lake.  The land cover classes follow the scheme used by the  Meithrin Mynydd 1997 survey but with adjustments where some classes could not be separated.  The dataset was derived from Sentinel 2 satellite imagery from 2019 with a pixel size of 10m by 10m on the ground.  The goal was to extend vegetation mapping of the west end of Mynydd Du from 2018 carried out by NRW\u0027s Environmental Assessment Officer and so provide up-to-date vegetation mapping of upland grassland, moor and mire in an area actively managed by commoners for sheep grazing in collaboration with the BBNPA.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T16:18:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19030\tNRW_DS125195\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.411Z\t497\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwaterbodies Cycle 3\t© CNC/NRW and British Geological Society (BGS). Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313935\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Groundwater Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwaterbodies Cycle 3 (2021-2027) is a spatial (polygon) dataset which has been created for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027…all water which is below the surface of the ground in the saturation zone and in direct contact with the ground or subsoil\u0027.   For the purposes of reporting under the WFD, a groundwater body represents a distinct body of groundwater flow with a coherent flow unit including recharge and discharge areas with little flow across the boundaries. These reflect hydrogeological characteristics containing information on flow and stage properties, recharge and vulnerability to pollution. This has been undertaken through defining aquifers into different types and broken into catchment units at Catchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) scale.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4460/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_groundwater_c3_baseline_classification\t2025-05-09T11:07:12.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19017\tNRW_DS125196\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.482Z\t574\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Surface Water Transfer Cycle 3\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313936\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Surface Water Transfers Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tThis dataset is a GIS layer identifying the Surface Water Transfer (SWT) waterbodies managed under the Water Framework Directive and any related programmes. This includes SWT which are reported to Europe as artificial rivers. These waterbodies are represented by their centreline, and can be linked to other Water Framework Directive (WFD) data using the unique water body ID (WB_ID). WFD River, canal and SWT Waterbodies Cycle 3, is a subset extracted from the Natural Resources Wales Detailed River Network, with attributes removed and merged on the WB_ID.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4469/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_surface_water_transfers_c3_baseline_classification\t2025-05-12T10:55:07.681Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19024\tNRW_DS125197\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.477Z\t857\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 3\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313937\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Lakes Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 3 is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing Water Framework Directive (WFD) attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Article 2, clause 5 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...a body of standing inland surface water\u0027.   There is data on the physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives that can be linked to waterbodies by their unique identifiers. Artificial and modified lake waterbodies are included within this dataset, however, generally only lakes above \u0026gt; 50 hectares were assessed under the WFD except for lakes in protected areas, where a minimum of 5.0 ha was used. Lakes below this threshold are not included within this dataset unless allocated as Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). Each waterbody has been assigned \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027, which is a unique identifier that enables a link to WFD attributes.   These data apply to Cycle 3 of the Water Framework Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4463/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_lakes_c3_baseline_classification\t2025-05-12T11:25:06.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19035\tNRW_DS125198\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.897Z\t941\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbodies Cycle 3\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313938\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbodies Cycle 3 are a spatial (polygon) dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. \u0027WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 3\u0027 is a subset extracted from the NRW\u0027s Detailed River Network (DRN).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-12-10\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4467/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_river_waterbodies_c3_baseline_classification\t2025-12-17T13:12:25.248Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19019\tNRW_DS125199\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.481Z\t516\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 3\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313939\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Coastal Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 7 of the WFD defines coastal waterbodies as \u0027...a surface water on the landward side of a line, every point of which is at a distance of one nautical mile on the seaward side from the nearest point of the baseline from which the breadth of territorial waters is measured, extending where appropriate up to the outer limit of transitional waters\u0027.   Coastal waters were defined by territorial waters 1 nautical mile from the Mean High Water coastline taken directly from OS 1:50K MeridianTM 2. The delineation between coastal and estuarine waters was delineated by the Environment Agency defined transitional waterbodies. Waterbodies are also split and assigned to River Basin Districts. Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives.   This dataset covers the layer for Cycle 3 of the Water framework Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4458/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_coastal_c3_baseline_classification\t2025-05-12T12:40:08.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19029\tNRW_DS125200\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.429Z\t947\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 3\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323030\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 3.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\t\u0027Water Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional Waterbodies\u0027 is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 6 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...bodies of surface water in the vicinity of river mouths which are partly saline in character as a result of their proximity to coastal waters but are substantially influenced by freshwater flows\u0027.   Transitional waterbodies were defined from Mean High Water boundaries, taken directly from OS 1:50K MeridianTM 2, and NRW estuarine boundaries defined for the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD). Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives. These data apply to Cycle 3 of the Water Framework Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4468/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_transitional_c3_baseline_classification\t2025-05-12T12:54:46.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19032\tNRW_DS125201\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.582Z\t936\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 3\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323031\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 3 are a polygon dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Catchments are defined as an area of land from which all surface run-off flows through a series of streams, rivers and, possibly, lakes to a particular point in the water course such as a river confluence. Since rivers are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to the WFD river waterbody to which it relates.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-12-10\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4470/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_river_waterbody_catchments_c3_baseline_classification\t2025-12-17T13:11:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19031\tNRW_DS125202\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.448Z\t625\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDuty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Land Management Incidents\t© CNC/NRW  Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323032\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Duty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Land Management Incidents.lyr\tSally Davies - Incident Management Team NRW\tNRW currently has Duty Tactical Manager (DTM) rotas covering the North, South East and South West areas of Wales. This dataset shows the areas of DTM responsibility for Land Management incidents (i.e. incidents on the NRW estate).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:43.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19027\tNRW_DS125203\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.433Z\t263\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDuty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Flood Incidents\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323033\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Duty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Flood Incidents.lyr \"\tSally Davies - Incident Management Team\tNRW currently has Duty Tactical Manager (DTM) rotas covering the North, South East and South West areas of Wales. This dataset shows the areas of DTM responsibility for Flood incidents.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-02-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19028\tNRW_DS125204\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.431Z\t259\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDuty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Non-Flood and Non-Land Management Incidents\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323034\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Duty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Non-Flood and Non-Land Management Incidents.lyr\tSally Davies - Incident Management Team\tNRW currently has Duty Tactical Manager (DTM) rotas covering the North, South East and South West areas of Wales. This dataset shows the areas of DTM responsibility for non-Flood or Land Management incidents (e.g. pollution incidents, office closures etc).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-02-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19018\tNRW_DS125213\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.482Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGlastir Woodland Creation agreements/contract data and related schemes 2022-2027\t© Welsh Government NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323133\tX:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Glastir Woodland Creation 11 EoI Boundaries.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Woodland Creation Planning Pilot Scheme (WCPPS).lyr\tGary.kingsbury@gov.wales RPW IT Data Management Metadata Point of Contact: Gary.kingsbury@gov.wales\tEach set of agreement/contract data contains 3 elements (shapefiles). There will be many sets of data delivered to NRW when they become available. 1. EoI (Expresion of Interest) – Welsh Government (WG) constructed from whole Land Parcel Identification Layer (LPIS) polygons covering the land where the applicant wants to construct planting compartments 2. Planting compartment (polygons) created by independent Forestry Planners – need to be within the EoI 3. Fences (polylines) - independent Forestry Planners – need to be within the EoI Once checked and confirmed correct by NRW – the data will be used in WG systems to create payments for Glastir Woodland Creation.\tWelsh Government\t2022-03-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:47:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19022\tNRW_DS125214\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.425Z\t19\tValid\tEditable\tAfon Iwrch Sea Trout Survey Project 1984-1994 (paper data records)\t© CNC/NRW Subject to a full assessment, data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323134\tOffsite storage - Original Thomas archive code: B0008972 Restore code: EAWC00000000916\tUnknown custodian – could be fisheries team/lab team/EEAT?\tSeries of 7 folders containing field notes relating to Sea Trout smolt.  Sea Trout Trap Smolt visit record sheets, contains information such as information about the actual trap, physical data (e.g. condition, temperature, pH), biological data (no. of brown and sea trout, species name, length, markings, sex stage).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1984-01-01\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:02:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19034\tNRW_DS125215\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.614Z\t889\tValid\tEditable\tLarge Raised Reservoir register (Locations and undertakers)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323135\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Reservoirs\tMatthew O\u0027Brien\tThis dataset contains details of Large Raised Reservoirs in Wales.     Natural Resources Wales (NRW) collects and maintains data on all reservoirs designed or capable of holding more than 10,000 cubic metres of water above the natural level of any part of the land adjoining them defined as \u0027large raised reservoirs\u0027 under the Reservoirs Act 1975. Two types of reservoir are maintained within the register:   - Impounding; or  - Non-Impounding.    This dataset is an extract from NRW’s Cronfeydd/Reservoir Database. It contains information on the location, undertakers and engineers of the reservoirs.    \u0027Undertaker\u0027 is the legal term used to identify who manages and controls the use of the reservoir and is responsible for maintaining compliance with the law. The undertaker is the person, people or company that uses a reservoir for a particular purpose. If there is no use, the owners or lessees are the undertakers. The undertaker may be different from the owner. If several people or organisations manage and control the reservoir, there can be more than one undertaker.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3505/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_large_raised_reservoirs_location_and_undertakers\t2026-02-23T11:48:11.100Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19033\tNRW_DS125216\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.482Z\t1393\tValid\tEditable\tLarge Raised Reservoir (Registered information)\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323136\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Reservoirs\tMatthew O\u0027Brien, Senior Specialist Advisor, Reservoir Safety Group\tThis dataset contains details of Large Raised Reservoirs.    Natural Resources Wales (NRW) collects and maintains data on reservoirs designed or capable of holding more than 10,000 cubic metres of water above the natural level of any part of the land adjoining them defined as \u0027large raised reservoirs\u0027 under the Reservoirs Act 1975. Two types of reservoir are maintained within the register:    - Impounding; or  - Non-Impounding.    This dataset is an extract from NRW’s Cronfeydd/Reservoir Database. It contains registered information about the physical characteristics and inspection regimes of the dam.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3514/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_large_raised_reservoirs_registered_information\t2026-02-23T11:48:55.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19025\tNRW_DS125219\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.477Z\t585\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Canal Waterbodies Cycle 3\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323139\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Canals Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tThis dataset is a GIS layer identifying the canal waterbodies managed under the Water Framework Directive and any related programmes. This includes Surface Water Transfer (SWT) waterbodies which are reported to Europe as artificial rivers. These waterbodies are represented by their centreline, and can be linked to other Water Framework Directive (WFD) data using the unique water body ID (WB_ID). WFD River, canal and SWT Waterbodies Cycle 3, is a subset extracted from the Natural Resources Wales Detailed River Network, with attributes removed and merged on the WB_ID.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4459/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_canals_c3_baseline_classification\t2025-05-12T13:09:16.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19016\tNRW_DS125226\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.362Z\t532\tValid\tEditable\tHabitats Directive: Article 17 Terrestrial Habitat Features\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323236\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2007,   X:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2012,   X:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2018,\tDave Johnston Team Leader (on secondment), Environmental Reporting.   Geralene Mills, Team Leader,  Environmental Reporting.\tThe Habitats Directive Article 17 reporting maps are a snapshot of the most up to date spatial data for features listed on the various Annexes of the Directive at the time of reporting. They represent the current known extent and location of features in Wales. The latest 10km2 habitat distribution maps submitted as part of the official Article 17 reporting can be obtained from the JNCC: European reporting | JNCC - Adviser to Government on Nature Conservation.   The maps are reviewed and updated every 6 years as part of the Habitats Directive reporting process. (Since EU exit now replaced by Regulation 9a of the Conservation and Species Regulations 2017).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-01-03\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3594/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_terrestrial_art_17_habitats\t2025-12-03T17:26:02.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19026\tNRW_DS125234\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.471Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRed Zones\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323334\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Red zones.lyr\tPeter van Velzen\tThe polygon theme called “RedZones” is a subjective user interpretation (based on experience) showing forest areas with high public useage. This theme is used in the Land Management Service Plan to determine which work sites require additional mitigation- and safety measures to safeguard the public.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-03-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:47:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19021\tNRW_DS125236\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.425Z\t172\tValid\tEditable\tUwch-Conwy Drone Survey October 2020\tData may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323336\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\Uwch-Conwy GIS layers: 1. UwchConwy_dsm 2. UwchConwy_Orthomosaic 3. UwchConwy_water 4. UwchConwy _dsm_contours_50cm 5. UwchConwywatershed_stream_segments 6. UwchConwy_hag_line 7. UwchConwy_watershed_basins 8. UwchConwy_site_boundaries 9. UwchConwy_peat 10. UwchConwy_hag GylcheddFlyby.mp4\tEsther Clews, Team Leader Peatland Data, National Peatland Action Programme, South East Wales Ops\tThe 10 sites were flown between the 10th and 16th September 2020 over the Uwch-Conwy peatlands.   All sites were surveyed using an RGB vertical camera, then the data processed to produce digital surface models (DSM) and orthomosaics.    The DSMs were processed to generate catchment, flow and contour vector data sets to assist with the peatbog restoration planning.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:52:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19014\tNRW_DS125237\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.727Z\t1276\tValid\tEditable\tSpecial Landscape Areas Wales\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. © Welsh Local Authorities. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323337\tx:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Special Landscape Areas.lyr\tJill Bullen, Lead Specialist Advisor: Landscape, Strategic Planning Group.\tSpecial Landscape Areas (SLAs) are a non-statutory designation applied by a local authority to define areas of high landscape importance within their administrative boundary.  This data resource is a collation of local authority derived SLAs and is intended to help users identify whether a location of interest is in an SLA (or not), the boundaries and extent of an SLA and the SLA reference or name to help sourcing the additional information and relevant policies. It is correct as of April 2022.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-01-13\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4176/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_special_landscape_area\t2025-05-09T16:09:12.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19015\tNRW_DS125257\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.726Z\t444\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Opportunity Catchments Layer Final 2021 - Cycle 3\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323537\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Opportunity Catchments Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Helen Haider, SMNR Advisor (Water), NRW\tThe Opportunity Catchment layer shows the boundaries and locations of Wales’ ten Opportunity Catchments.  The Opportunity Catchments form part of the third cycle River Basin Management Plans (2021-2027).  The layer shows the ten Opportunity Catchments in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4466/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_opportunity_catchments_c3\t2025-05-12T10:03:00.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19011\tNRW_DS125258\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.658Z\t1116\tValid\tEditable\tWales Construction and Demolition waste arisings survey\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323538\thttps://naturalresourceswales.sharefile.eu/share/view/s65d2984bde9a4d5aabf47af3a0d7ef4a/fo74f4e3-fe88-43d1-a02d-7c9a96c06426\tInternal Data Custodian(s): John Fry, Project Executive, NRW  Metadata Point of Contact:  John Fry, Project Executive, NRW\tThe purpose of the study was to provide information on the types, quantities, origins (by Construction and Demolition sector and geographic region), and fate (management method) of Construction and Demolition (C\u0026amp;D) wastes generated by businesses and the public sector in Wales in 2019. This is a stand-alone survey and supersedes the data from the last survey for the 2012 calendar year.   This information is required for a variety of reasons including:     • statutory reporting on waste generation in compliance with Part 2 of Schedule 1 of the Waste (England and Wales) Regulations 2011 and, up until 2020, the EU Waste Statistics Regulations 2002;  • informing the development of national waste policy;  • monitoring progress against national waste prevention and recycling targets;  • informing waste planners and the regulator;  • supporting investment decisions such as the selection of the type and scale of new waste management facilities required by local authorities and the waste industry;  • updating the evidence base for the State of Natural Resources Report Wales assessment (Natural Resources Wales 2021), and;  • providing businesses with a better understanding of their current waste practices and for developing sustainable waste management support strategies by Welsh Government for improving environmental performance of businesses.    The precision for the total waste generated in Wales was +/- 16.7% at 90% confidence.  Results from all surveys are subject to limitations with respect to the quality of estimates produced. Whilst these limitations do not alter the results or the statistical data, they should be borne in mind by users of the data.  Information on waste management in the report is generally reliable but the accuracy of the results is limited to information available to surveyed producers on the final destinations of their waste. The accuracy is limited owing to the complexities of waste management routes and difficulties linking final fate back to source.    Further information, including on the survey confidence, is contained in the published survey report. The detailed survey technical appendices are also available on request.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-05-19\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2938\t2025-02-04T10:16:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19013\tNRW_DS125264\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.727Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBenthic Core Sites\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323634\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Benthic Cores\\Nyfer 2021 Benthic Quadrats.lyr  X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Benthic Cores\\Teifi 2021 Benthic Quadrats.lyr\tKathrynBorrowdale\tWater Framework Directive benthic core data. This data shows the positions of the cores that were taken. Species and partical size information for the cores are available on KiECO.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-05-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19012\tNRW_DS125266\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.655Z\t815\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Defence Structures\t© CNC/NRW \u0026amp; © Welsh Local Authorities. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323636\tZ:\\Archive\\Water Management\\Flood Risk Mgmt\\FRA\\Maps\\FD Locations and X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Defence Locations\tBen Hext - lead specialist advisor Flood Risk Analysis\tThe National Flood Asset Database records flood infrastructure in Wales managed by Lead Local Flood Authorities (LLFAs) and Natural Resources Wales (NRW). It includes many embankments, walls, flood gates, culverts, and inlet screens (which catch debris), but not all flood infrastructure maintained or owned by these parties. This infrastructure has been constructed to help manage the risk of flooding from rivers and the sea. They do not remove the risk of flooding and can be overtopped, blocked, or fail in extreme weather conditions.   Three datasets are available as open data:  1. Flood defence Locations. These show the linear alignment of flood defence asset that have been built to protect against flooding from rivers and the sea. It includes the type, length, maintainer, and an ID. These defence locations are also shown in Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW), Flood Map for Planning (FMfP) and Find flood defence structures near you maps. They may relate to an Area Benefiting from Flood Defence (FRAW) and TAN15 zone (FMfP).     2. Culverts (Polyline); These show an indicative location of underground culverts for general guidance only. Attributes include maintainer, owner, length and ID. Due to the underground nature and history of many culverts, the locations may not be accurate, complete, or up to date.     3. Culvert openings and Screens (Points). These show an indicative location of culvert openings and screens (often referred to as Trash or Debris Screens). Attributes include maintainer, owner and ID. Locations and details may not be accurate, complete or up to date.     We will regularly update the information when new and improved data becomes available. If you have any queries or concerns about location, maintenance, condition, or records of flood assets, check with the owner or maintainer.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/4126/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_flood_defence_structures\t2025-12-01T11:33:24.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19009\tNRW_DS125268\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.382Z\t16\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Transect Lines\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323638\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh\tKathryn Borrowdale\tWater Framework Directive saltmarsh monitoring. The lines in these layers show the location of the saltmarsh transects surveyed.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-09-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:12.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19008\tNRW_DS125279\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.366Z\t927\tValid\tEditable\tLANDMAP Cultural Landscape Services\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323739\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap\\LANDMAP Cultural Landscape.lyr\tJill Bullen - Lead Specialist Advisor Landscapes\tLANDMAP is a complete All-Wales GIS based landscape resource where landscape characteristics, qualities and influences on the landscape are recorded and evaluated into a nationally consistent data set. LANDMAP comprises five spatially related datasets known as the Geological Landscape, Landscape Habitats, Landscape Habitats, the Historic Landscape and the Cultural Landscape Services.    The LANDMAP Cultural Landscape Services layer provides information relating to cultural services and the connections between landscape, the natural environment and place. Including natural settings, aesthetic appreciation, tranquillity, cultural heritage, inspiration and spiritual and identity and sense of place. The existing Visual \u0026amp; Sensory boundaries are used as context, and all data is calculated to these boundaries.   Cultural Landscape Services replaces the LANDMAP Cultural Landscape dataset.  This new dataset (which supersedes NRW_DS108654) will focus mapping on some key cultural services particularly linked to landscape but will in time include richer information in the surveys. This will be a significant step forward in delivering reliable, usable data that can be built upon and continually improved.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-07-22\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/2475/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_LANDMAP_Cultural_Landscape_Services\t2025-12-03T16:19:02.099Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19007\tNRW_DS125285\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.362Z\t968\tValid\tEditable\tFirebreaks in NRW South Central Region\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323835\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Firebreaks in NRW South Central Region.lyr\tGeraint Price Senior Officer Forest Operations – South Central (+ Rebekah Davies – Technical Support Officer Forest Operations)\tDataset showing the location of firebreaks created and managed by Natural Resources Wales operational staff in the Welsh Government Woodland Estate. The data covers the NRW South Central Operational Region.  Data was collected so firebreak locations can be shared with South Wales Fire and Rescue Service, to aid in the tackling wildfires.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-03-24\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4510/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_sc_firebreaks\t2025-10-24T08:51:21.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19010\tNRW_DS125288\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.401Z\t497\tValid\tEditable\tBait digging evidence database\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323838\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/advice/mar/MPANLICACT/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7B8803200A-1E1E-4EA3-A959-6791FB8FBD5D%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dR3772_BaitDiggingEvidenceDatabase_100222.xlsx\u0026amp;action\u003ddefault\u0026amp;mobileredirect\u003dtrue\u0026amp;DefaultItemOpen\u003d1\u0026amp;CID\u003dE7498CFC-2686-4650-98FC-FBFA5BFF8B67\u0026amp;wdLOR\u003dcE61ECA1C-CF4D-43B9-ACF3-CADEB8A6DAE6\tLaura Grant, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe bait digging evidence database (an Excel spreadsheet) contains the detailed information which forms the rationale for the report produced by ABPmer entitled ‘West, V., Robbins, K., Frost, N., Hull, S. 2022. Bait Digging Management Options. NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 602, 74pp, NRW, Bangor’.     It displays information about each of the eleven bait digging sites in Wales in order to inform potential management measures.  This includes information such as sediment type, exposure, area of the site, intensity of digging, extent of Section 7 muddy gravels, recovery times of sediment, protected sites present, site access and proximity of potential displacement sites.  It also contains the intensity scoring, which uses proportion of the site used for bait digging and intensity of digging to rank and prioritise the sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-08-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T09:57:15.457Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n19006\tNRW_DS125294\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.257Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBala Sluices Water Level Records\t© CNC/NRW Data Re-use conditions to be determined (tbc- full open data assessment to br done when digitised)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323934\t2 off-site storage archive boxes - number tbc\tDebbie Evans/Rob Bissell Water Resources Team  off-site storage box ref: EAW/BALASLU/1 and  EAW/BALASLU/2 Ta\tBala Sluices Water Level Records\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T15:15:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19048\tNRW_DS125297\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.618Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDetermining Confidence in the 2018 Indicative Condition Assessments\tTo be added\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323937\tData currently stored on DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/mar/mar-wq/Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fadvice%2Fmar%2Fmar%2Dwq%2FEvidence%2FNutrient%20SAC%20project\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200078914C818B753241BAFF4899DE5F2F8F\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1FDF1E04%2D17DE%2D4770%2DBA8B%2D3EDC8394EB22%7D\t\tIn 2018 Indicative Condition Assessments were undertaken to examine the feature condition of Marine Protected Areas. Given the time available at the time, the process for assigning condition was limited. Thus, given the potential for marine to be taken into the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) river project, whereby permits may be refused or reviewed due to water quality failures within SACs, we need to determine our confidence in those failures assigned in 2018. The dataset here is that used to support the generation of confidence metrics. It comes in Excel files and GIS shapefiles.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-07-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T16:45:24.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19054\tNRW_DS125305\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.650Z\t1956\tValid\tEditable\tTranquillity and Place: Visually Tranquil Areas\t© CNC/NRW and British Geological Society Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333035\tX:Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquility and Place\tJill Bullen - Specialist Advisor Agriculture\tA nationally consistent terrestrial Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place resource that identifies visually tranquil areas for use as an evidence base to inform policy intent, practice and provision for well-being benefits.   Storymap link https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/865c1876d9f64280a3dfc6e2769a46a5    Tranquillity is associated with the degree to which places and ecosystems deliver a state of quiet, calm, peace and well-being. This can be described as a relative abundance, perception or experience of nature, natural landscapes and features and/or a relative freedom from unwanted visual disturbance, signs of human influence and artificial noise.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-04-29\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/4003\t2025-04-30T15:25:26.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19049\tNRW_DS125308\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.623Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPowys Woodland Survey 1982-85/86\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333038\toff-site archive storage (add number)\tData Services team (OpenData inbox)       Data Services team opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tPowys County Council set up a woodland project in 1981 to discover more about the semi-natural broadleaved woodland of the County. The Powys Woodland project survey ran from 1982 to 1985 and completed an ecological classification of semi-natural woods in the county, recognising fifteen woodland site types in a sample of four hundred and thirty woods surveyed either by the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) between 1970-80 or by local Nature Trusts between 1980-82.  The work explores how much exists, its type and distribution, how much change has occurred  and some possible reasons for the survival of long-established woods. The approach was ecological to find out the amount of hardwood timber left, the irreplacable assets of the country.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1985-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T16:24:07.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19052\tNRW_DS125313\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.643Z\t859\tValid\tEditable\tWales River Restoration Plans\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333133\tX:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\River Restoration Fisheries Activity Lines.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\River Restoration Fisheries Activity Points.r X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\River Restoration Line.lyr\tRhodri Powell - Specialist Advisor, River Restoration Programme, Freshwater Ecosystems and Fisheries Management\tCollection of river restoration plans presented in geodatabases identifying opportunities for river restoration within applicable waterbody.   Individual river restoration plans commissioned by Natural Resources Wales, delivered by external consultant.    Plans are made for the following rivers:  Rhymney Tywi Roath Brook Pembroke River Pelenna Nant Y Fendrod Nant Glandulas Ely/ Nant Dowlais Dulas Cynon Clywedog Afonydd Cleddau Afon Wen\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-11-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:58.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19050\tNRW_DS125315\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.607Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWoodland Surveys in Dyfed/Powys 1988 using the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) Cooke \u0026amp;Saunders - ( Paper data records)\ttbc but likely to be open data provided no landowner details are present\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333135\tpaper data currently in MYF post room floor 1\tData Services team opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk (or James Latham although he has moved post and no longer the custodian)       Data Services team (OpenData@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk)\tThis survey covers 156 woods and 2200 ha throughout Dyfed and the districts of Radnor and Montgomery in Powys. Many private and public landowners granted permission to survey their wooodland. Survey results were recorded on standard NVC record cards and a 300-500 word report on each site was written.  Phase II surveys were undertaken in selected woods using standard procedures. Woodland communities were described using the National Vegetation Classification NVC.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1988-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-22T16:23:57.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19047\tNRW_DS125323\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.592Z\t1442\tValid\tEditable\tRiver Wye Special Area of Conservation Sonde Data\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333233\tZ:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\Data Mgmt\\Sonde Data\\Wye\\Processed\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Johnston, Environmental Reporting Team Leader (Temporary reassignment, please contact Geralene Mills)  NRW  Metadata Point of Contact:  Dave Johnston, Environmental Reporting Team Leader, NRW\tA number of automatic monitors are in place in the River Wye Special Area of Conservation. Data is collected for seven water quality parameters at 15 minute intervals on a semi-permanent basis to assess the state of the river and changes over time.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-03-24\tpublication\tnull\thttps://smnr-nrw.hub.arcgis.com/apps/98b26568c38f48c797ddc9a198dea7d0/explore\t2025-12-19T10:47:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19055\tNRW_DS125325\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.677Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMetal Mine Interventions in Wales\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333235\tX:\\Industrial\\Mines\\Metal Mine Interventions in Wales.lyr\tSophia Lennie\tMetal Mine Interventions in Wales records the locations of structures and desk studies completed by the Metal Mine Programme. These structures range from flow gauging station to surface water management to capping works. The data is collected to display where remediation works and desk studies on metal mine sites have been done. It should be noted that the dataset is collected in 2022. As the Metal Mine Programme continues, new assets may be created in the future.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:47:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19051\tNRW_DS125326\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.588Z\t14\tValid\tEditable\tMetal Mine Impacted Waterbodies in Wales\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333236\tX:\\Industrial\\Mines\\Metal Mine Impacted Waterbodies in Wales.lyr\tDave Johnston\tThe Metal Mine Impacted Waterbodies in Wales contains waterbodies and its lengths which have been affected by metal mine, either through direct or diffuse pollution. The data is collected to show the extent of downstream impacts of metal mine pollution. It should be noted that this dataset is collected in 2022. As remediation works continue, the number and length of waterbodies impacted may change.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-07-09\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/7628/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_mine_impacted_wbs\t2025-07-09T10:52:55.000Z\tAndrew Jeffery\t-\tPrivate record\n19053\tNRW_DS125330\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.674Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tVisitor safety zones\t© CNC/NRW  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and database right. Note buffering around linear and point features mean that area calculations cannot be achieved accurately. These should not be undertaken. Printing of maps should be conducted on the appropriate visitor safety zonation template and printed maps are only accurate as to the date they were printed. Site managers must contact the data custodian to ensure this data aligns with their public safety risk assessments. To edit or print a map of the zones, email the Data and Content Advisor (Recreation) to ensure the correct template and formatting are used. Data does not include ExEAW land as yet.  PRoW is a NRW representation only. The NRW estate is z ned according to VSG risk categories as illustrated on the map. Although these zones appear with hard boundaries, they are indicative only and care must be exercised if zooming in closely and zones are not sharply defined regarding the application of control measures.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333330\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Visitor Safety Zones.lyr\tDavid Liddy, Specialist Advisor Recreational Safety, NRW Heather Crump, Data and Content Advisor (Recreation), NRW\tThe NRW estate zoned according to Visitor Safety Group risk categories. Although these zones appear with hard boundaries, they are indicative only and care must be exercised if zooming in closely and zones are not sharply defined regarding the application of control measures. Public rights of way infoamtion is a NRW representation only. These maps should be used to compliment public safety risk assessments. Reliance is upon site managers to contact the data custodian to keep this data layer current. To edit or print a map of the zones, email the Data and Content Advisor (Recreation) to ensure the correct template and formatting are used.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-09-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:46:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19044\tNRW_DS125331\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.262Z\t680\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGenetic ancestry of introduced populations of sand lizard (Lacerta agilis) in Wales.\tData may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333331\tData is held within science report 596 in NRW library - Data outputs associated with this project are archived in the Supercomputing Wales system “Hawk”, physically located at Cardiff University, with additional local back-up copies stored in the Environment Centre Wales, Bangor University on server–based storage at Natural Resources Wales.\tMatthew Ellis, Senior Advisor Casework Team NRW  Sam Dyer, Specialist advisor: Terrestrial habitats and species\tThe purpose of this investigation was to use a state-of-the-art whole genome sequencing approach to test the three most plausible hypotheses for the origin of the Gower population of Lacerta agilis. Assessing the origin and ancestry of Gower sand lizards is a fundamental step in assessing the need and desirability for conservation action to preserve the population, or, in the case of an introduced population of a non-native lineage, to consider the case for removal.  The date is presented in a report format with tables of DNA sequencing results presented in table and graphs\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-09-16\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:54:35.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19046\tNRW_DS125332\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.438Z\t733\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCurlew GPS Data: Curlew Movements\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333332\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/birds/Taxa/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbirds%2FTaxa%2FBreeding%20Waders%20%2D%20Curlew%2FCurlew%20GPS%20tagging%20contract\tPatrick Lindley - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tTo improve our understanding of how Curlew use structurally different landscapes in the uplands and lowlands and under different land management regimes, in order to aid Natural Resources Wales’ work in agri-environmental policy design.  Data is collected and transferred to an Excel spreadsheet.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-09-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-22T16:20:31.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19043\tNRW_DS125335\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.325Z\t273\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Operational Catchments Cycle 3 2022\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333335\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Operational Catchments Cycle 3 2022.lyr\tRob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting Team\tWFD Operational Catchments’ is a polygon Shapefile dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD).  Operational catchments are the unit of geography that group river water bodies into smaller sub-catchments.    These units support the management and understanding of the river network at a local scale.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4464/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_operational_catchments_c3\t2025-05-12T10:40:01.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19040\tNRW_DS125344\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.277Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWye Valley Woodlands Project 1850 -1980 - distribution of woodland species vascular plants and bryophytes - Paper Survey records\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333434\tOff site storage Box CCW/SERBC/209\t?\tPaper survey records relating to the Wye Valley Woodland vascular species and bryophytes survey.  This is a collation of existing records and new records. The survey was done in two parts.  Records compiled by Stephanie Taylor 1979 and in 1978. 1980 NCC commissioned Ian Bolt to collect records for additional sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1980-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:42.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19045\tNRW_DS125346\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.325Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClwyd Pond Survey 1979 (paper survey records)\tTo be added\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333436\tOff site archive storage, ref: CCW-NRM/R-53\t\tThis is a paper format survey of pond species and habitat of the surrounding area which was undertaken during 1979. Photos of the ponds are also included.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T16:39:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19039\tNRW_DS125350\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.238Z\t16\tInvalid\tEditable\tLimestone grassland survey 1987 (paper data records)\tTBC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333530\toff-site storage, ref CCW/NRM/R-53\t\tPaper survey sheets of a 1987 survey of limestone grassland in grid reference area SJ and SH\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:56:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19041\tNRW_DS125353\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.288Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDenbigh Grassland survey 1979/1980\tTBC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333533\toff-site storage, ref: CCW/NRM/R-53\tData Services Team\tThis is a grassland survey of Denbigh area in paper format undertaken during 1979 and 1980\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:58:52.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19042\tNRW_DS125355\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.283Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGwent Grassland Survey\tTo be added\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333535\toff-site storage ref: CCW/SERBC/209 / CCW/NRM/R-53\tData Services Team\tGwent Grassland Survey 1983\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T16:37:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19038\tNRW_DS125356\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.082Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGwent Meadows Survey 1987\ttbc\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333536\toff-site storage ref: CCW/SERBC/209\t\tPaper data shetts of Gwent Meadows Survey 1987\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:17:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19037\tNRW_DS125358\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.081Z\t948\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Fish Monitoring Data\t© CNC/NRW. NRW owned data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. NRW does NOT grant permission to disseminate or re-use any data that is not labelled as NRW ownership. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333538\tWISKI KIECO\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Paul Hyatt\tFreshwater fish are used as an indicator of stock status, water and habitat quality, artificial obstacles to migration and river flow requirements due to specific life-cycle requirements. This dataset is made up of the species count and length values from the freshwater river fish surveys conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of fish in their natural environment for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  For the geospatial version of this data please see NRW_DS125743 \"KiEco: Freshwater Fish - Geospatial\".\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-11-17-2025-01-16\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr3164\t2025-12-03T10:08:57.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19036\tNRW_DS125359\t2024-05-28T13:00:55.922Z\t366\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNature and Us / Natur am Ni Phase 1 Survey Responses\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. Redacted data (personal data removed) may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333539\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/manbus/PMO/V2050/Phase%201%20outputs/EDITED_Nature%20and%20Us%20survey%20responses_English%20Edited%20Copy.xlsx?web\u003d1     (Secure DMS Location)\tSerinde van Wijk, Lead Specialist Advisor Nature and Us,\tThis data was collected as part of the Nature and Us national conversation between March and May 2022. As part of this compain, an online survey was available for any one in Wales to complete that sought to address five research outcomes: • what people identify as the critical issue for the future • if things went well, being optimistic but realistic, what would be a desirable  outcome • if things went wrong, what factors would people worry about • what people think about different scenarios of the future • what people are willing to change. The survey asked individuals about what concerned them about the environment, what action should be taken to help address climate change and the nature crises in Wales, what actions they would like to prioritise and what changes they would like to see. A total of 3,069 survey responses was received which are included in this dataset.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Governance Licence and available for public use, see Limitation on Public Access and Use Constraints\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-07-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-22T16:28:56.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19078\tNRW_DS125360\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.237Z\t699\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUnofficial Mountain Bike Trails (2021/22 Snapshot)\tContains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and database right  © CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333630\tZ:\\Processing\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Management\\1 - Geospatial\\SM\\Unofficial Mountain Bike Trails (2021_2 Snapshot) - Heather Crump\\Unofficial Mountain Bike Trails (2021_2 Snapshot).gdb\tHeather Crump - Advisor (2) Recreation Content\tThis \u0027Wild MTB trails\u0027 data was collected by contractors and covers NRW managed land. The survey began with Strava Heat Maps and other user driven websites and was validated on site. It looks at the busiest trails and sites and covers most, but certainly not all of the forests or all of the trails. It represents the 2021/2 snapshot. Data includes trail routes (line data) and hazard points (point data) associated with a set of photographs stored on the DMS. Data regarding rider numbers was manually written down trail by trail. THIS DATA IS FOR INTERNAL USE ONLY.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-09-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19082\tNRW_DS125362\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.248Z\t582\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) At Risk and Failing Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 3 2022\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333632\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD At Risk Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 3 2022.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Failing Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 3 2022.lyr\tData Custodian: Rob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment\tAt Risk Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 3 2022 and Failing Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 3 2022 are spatial (polygon shapefile) datasets containing attributes that have been collated as defined, for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD).   To implement the guidance on managing forests in order to minimise adverse impacts in acid-sensitive areas, two categories of acid sensitivity have been identified. These are defined as \u0027failing due to acidification\u0027 and \u0027at risk of failing due to acidification\u0027.    Water bodies identified as currently \u0027failing\u0027 have been classified through the Water Framework Directive monitoring as being of less than \u0027good\u0027 status and the cause of that failure has been attributed to acidification. The \u0027at risk of failing\u0027 water bodies have been identified, via the findings of the Welsh acidification risk assessment, as being at risk of failing to achieve \u0027good\u0027 status by 2027, due to acid deposition. The risk assessment combines chemical and biological data and an assessment of the environment\u0027s ability to neutralise the predicted acid pollutants.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-08-05\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_wfd_cycle_3_classifications\t2025-09-04T10:50:17.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19083\tNRW_DS125363\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.056Z\t14\tValid\tEditable\tAncient Woodland Inventory for Wales 2004 (AWI2004)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333633\tX:\\Physical\\Forestry\\Ancient Woodland Inventory 2004.lyr Paper copies of the tracings are stored in Maes y Ffynnon, Floor 1 Post room (Jim Latham\u0027s cabinets)\tChris Tucker, Specialist Advisor: Technical Forestry (Resilience)\tIn the 1980s, the Nature Conservancy Council instigated the Ancient Woodland Inventory (AWI). In Wales, the mapping baseline used was the 1st editions of the Ordnance Survey (OS) 1” maps published in the 1830s. The 1980s version of the inventory was compiled manually, using tracings from paper maps. It contained a significant number of boundary discrepancies. In the late 1990s, paper maps were converted to digital format. Work to reconcile boundaries with digital OS boundaries developed the first digital version of the inventory, made available in 2004. This version is referred to as the AWI 2004.    Also see the metadata entry for Ancient Woodland Inventory, dataset number NRW_DS116442 https://metadata.naturalresources.wales/geonetwork/srv/api/records/NRW_DS116442?language\u003deng\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:24:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19085\tNRW_DS125370\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.379Z\t925\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDevelopment and implementation of a monitoring project for two rare spiders (Heliophanus dampfi and Lasaeola prona) on bogs within Dyfi SSSI\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333730\tStored on the WID in Recorder 6\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThis project surveyed the arachnid (spider, harvestman and pseudoscorpion) fauna of five bogs within Dyfi SSSI - Cors Fochno, Ynys Greigiog, Tynohir, Gogarth Bog and Ynys-hir - during 2021 and early 2022.   The primary aim was to develop new monitoring protocols, based on vacuum sampling, for the two Nationally Rare spiders Heliophanus dampfi (IUCN Vulnerable) and Lasaeola prona (IUCN Endangered). No monitoring with the development of targets for these two species is known from other UK sites. Both species are qualifying features of Dyfi SSSI.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T15:31:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19075\tNRW_DS125371\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.238Z\t811\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status and distribution of Lilljeborg’s Whorl Snail Vertigo lilljeborgi at Pont Croesor on the Afon Glaslyn in 2022\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333731\tWelsh Invertebrate Database held in Recorder 6\tMike Howe - NRW Invertebrate Specialist\tResults of a monitoring survey in August 2022 for the Near Threatened Lilljeborg’s Whorl Snail Vertigo lilljeborgi within Glaslyn SSSI are presented. The area surveyed is north of Pont Croesor on the Afon Glaslyn. Judged to be in Unfavourable condition after the last survey in July 2008 (Willing, 2009), the present survey over two days in August repeated the previous survey. The wetland habitats remain visually very similar to those described in 2008 and weather and river level conditions were similar.  All 8 sites were revisited and sampled by visual search, beating vegetation, and collecting bulk samples of decaying plant matter. A 9th site was sampled near Site 7, the point at which V. lilljeborgi was last recorded in 2008. A suction sampler was used in 5 additional locations between sites on the east bank. A total of 12 adults of V. lilljeborgi was found at Site 7 and Site 8, confirming that the species survives on both sides of the Afon Glaslyn, although it is still apparently restricted to small areas. Two juvenile Vertigo were also found at Site 6. These could not be unambiguously identified as V. lilljeborgi, owing to the presence of another species, Vertigo antivertigo, nearby. All Vertigo specimens were found in large bulk samples that had been dried and sieved. No Vertigo were observed in the field, emphasising the importance of multiple sampling methods.   Using the Conservation Objective Lower Limit of 10 individuals set by Willing (2009), V. lilljeborgi is judged to be in Favourable condition within Glaslyn SSSI in August 2022. Nonetheless, the population only just exceeds the Lower Limit, and remains small and potentially vulnerable. Further sampling in the areas around Pont Croesor could help determine whether V. lilljeborgi is more widespread on the Afon Glaslyn floodplain.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T15:39:09.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19076\tNRW_DS125375\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.253Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOcean Literacy Survey of Wales 2022\tData may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333735\t\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Kathryn Hughes, Senior Advisor: Marine Area Statement   Metadata Point of Contact: Kathryn Hughes\tThis data provides the evidence for the report “Survey on Ocean Literacy in Wales”. The survey was commissioned as part of the project “Understanding Ocean Literacy and Ocean Climate-related Behaviour Change in the UK” by Defra in collaboration with the Ocean Conservation Trust, Marine Scotland and Natural Resources Wales. The main aim of the survey was to better understand the extent and current level of Ocean Literacy in the England and Wales. The survey defines ‘Ocean Literacy’ as an understanding of the ocean’s influence on a person and their influence on the ocean. The survey explored different dimensions of ‘Ocean Literacy’: including information on public awareness, knowledge, attitudes, communication, activism and behaviours related to the marine environment. The survey also explored barriers to promoting Ocean Literacy in the population.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-05-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19081\tNRW_DS125376\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.239Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClwyd Woodland Survey\tto be decided\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333736\toff-site storage box ref: CCW/NRW/R51 \u0026amp;52\t\tClwyd woodlland survey of 1981 and preliminary survey of 1979\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19080\tNRW_DS125377\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.183Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPembrokeshire Rare Plant Register proformas (Paper Records)\tFor Internal reference use only\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333737\tOff site storage CCW/WRPB/33  Some info to Julian Woodman?\tJulian Woodman, off-site storage reference number CCW/WRPB/33\tCopies of handwritten Rare Plant Register Index proformas, information includes species name and locality, coverage, associates and conservation status.  Although all original information supplied to BSBI to collate, CCW and subsequently NRW retained a copy of the paper proformas for internal use.  Information was collected by NRW staff and therefore is not classed as third party data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Botanical Society Of The British Isles (BSBI)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19077\tNRW_DS125378\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.252Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNCC Powys Local Woodland surveys up to 1976/77 (Paper records)\tto be determined once digitised\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333738\toff-site archive storage. box no: CCW/SERN/149\t\tPaper record cards - Woodland surveys for Powys - SO 05-08, 14-18, 25, 26/36, 27/37 and SN 97 grid squares\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19071\tNRW_DS125379\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.095Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForestry Comission Woodland Survey  - Gwent 1987 (paper records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333739\toff-site archive box FCW/FELL/5\topendata@naturalresources.gov.uk\tIn 1987 NCC Woodland survey cards were completed for FC broad-leafed woodland likely to be semi-natural .  Area: Ebbw area of Gwent.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19084\tNRW_DS125380\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.143Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDerelict Land Habitat \u0026amp; Species Survey 1979 (paper data records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333830\toff-site storage box ref CCW/NRM/R-52\tOpenData@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tSurvey Site reports for SJ grid ref area and Anglesey\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19074\tNRW_DS125381\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.214Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSouth Wales Open Water Survey 1981 (paper data records)\ttbc\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333831\tOffsite archive storage box no CCW/SERC/219\tOpendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       Opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tSurvey of 94 permanent standing open water sites in Gwent 1981- areas greter than once acre in site, ST and SO grid references\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19073\tNRW_DS125385\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.238Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNant Irfon NNR Plant Species Survey 1974 and 1991/2\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333835\toffisite storage -box number CCW/SERN/149\tNo know custodian,contact Opendata@naturalreosueceswales.gov.uk to discuss       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNant Irfon NNR Plant Species Quadrat Survey 1974 and 1991/2\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:43:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19072\tNRW_DS125389\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.090Z\t873\tValid\tEditable\tHolyhead Marina Ultrasound Trial Footage 2020-2021\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333839\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Holyhead ultrasound trial footage 2020-2021\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor – Marine Ecology, NRW\tVideo (MP4) taken by a Natural Resources Wales owned Underwater Remotely Operated Vehicle (Chasing Innovation M2) at Holyhead marina. This was part of the baseline survey and monitoring of effectiveness of the ultrasound trial attempted in Holyhead marina to eradicate Didemnum vexillum. Video stills (JPG) were captured from the footage.     The data was collected between October 2020 and January 2021.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-11-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T10:13:53.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n19066\tNRW_DS125390\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.489Z\t680\tValid\tEditable\tHolyhead Harbour Carpet Sea Squirt Survey Data November 2020\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333930\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Holyhead Harbour D. vex survey data November 2020\tInternal Data Custodian(s):  Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology\tThis is the raw data from a dive survey of Holyhead harbour in November 2020, which focussed on mapping the distribution of the invasive non-native species Carpet seasquirt, Didemnum vexillum.  It contains;  • Video (MP4) and image files (JPG and ARW) of underwater habitats and species taken during the surveys  • Drone aerial images and videos of the current layout of the pontoons and mooring buoys (JPG and DNG and MP4).  • An Excel spreadsheet of sites surveyed    • Data recording forms – PDF copies of dive log sheets and recording forms    This data accompanies; Holt, R.H.F. 2018. Didemnum vexillum: survey of distribution and extent in Holyhead Harbour – November 2020. NRW Evidence report no. 538. 29 pp.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-11-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T10:15:19.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n19069\tNRW_DS125391\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.791Z\t347\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey of the caddisfly Limnephilus tauricus at Cors Erddreiniog National Nature Reserve (NNR) in 2022\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333931\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID) – Natur Am Byth Surveys.\tMike Howe\tA survey of Cors Erddreiniog National Nature Reserve (NNR) was carried out in order to update knowledge of the status and distribution of the very rare caddisfly Limnephilus tauricus – a species known only from this reserve in Wales. The survey also aimed to locate the even rarer caddisfly Limnephilus pati, which hasn’t been previously recorded at the reserve (or in Wales) but is present in similar habitats within the same flight period.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-12-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T15:40:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19067\tNRW_DS125392\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.645Z\t9\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Fixed Point Botanical Monitoring Survey Data\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333932\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Fixed Point Quadrat Boundaries.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Fixed Point Quadrat Centres.lyr\tToni Henwood\tData from the fixed point quadrats to monitoring the effects of SoLIFE project actions\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-08-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:33:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19079\tNRW_DS125393\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.237Z\t633\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Sand Dune Soil Baseline Monitoring 2023\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333933\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Soils Slacks.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Soils Transects.lyr\tToni Henwood\tThis survey focuses on baseline, pre-intervention monitoring in relation to two of the planned intervention types: notch creation and dune slack re-profiling. The monitoring covers six sites in Wales: Tywyn Aberffraw, Newborough, and Morfa Harlech in north Wales, and Kenfig, Merthyr Mawr and Pembrey Burrows in south Wales.  At each site, baseline monitoring was set up for the planned interventions and for control locations where no intervention will take place, in order to clearly show the effect of the restoration measures.  For both intervention types, soil profiles were taken at each monitoring location. Soil profiles were recorded to a depth of 50 cm and soil samples were taken to a depth of 20 cm. In addition, the baseline monitoring for the notches involved setting up sand-rain collectors at transects away from the proposed notch and control locations, downwind in the direction of the prevailing winds. Each transect started at 50 m distance from the inland end of the notch, and then at two further points (100 and 200 m). Soil samples were analysed for bulk density, electrical conductivity, pH, organic matter content by loss on ignition (LOI), calcium carbonate content, carbon (C) and nitrogen (N) content.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-12-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:39:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19068\tNRW_DS125394\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.670Z\t824\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonk\u0027s Trod and Hengae Drone Survey 2022\t© CNC/NRW   Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333934\tZ:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\GIS \u0026amp; Spatial Data\\Drone data\tMichael Sneade - Advisor 2, Natura 2000 Sustainable Management\tGIS layers from Monks Trod and Hengae survey areas: • full site orthomosaic • tiled orthomosaic • full site digital surface model • tiled digital surface model  Used to survey the following: Ascertaining a baseline status of the highway. Seeing how far the traffic \"drifts\" from the highway.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-12-02\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:14:01.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19070\tNRW_DS125395\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.852Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Grassland survey 1986 Mid \u0026amp; South Glamorgan (paper data records)\ttbc when digistised\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333935\toff-site archive storage ref: CCWRF43501 \u0026amp; CCWRF43502\tData Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tPhase 2 Grassland survey of SN, SO, SS and ST  Mid\u0026amp;South Glamorgan\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:56.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19061\tNRW_DS125396\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.299Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGwent Level Plant Species Survey 1991 (Paper Records )\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333936\toff-site paper storage CCW/SERC/1463  - RF24454\tnot known stored in off-site archive box ref: CCW/SERC/143 (RF24454)\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19063\tNRW_DS125397\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.398Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRare Plant Species survey of Wales 1987-88 (paper records)\tto be deciced when digitised\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333937\tOff site storage box ref:  CCW/WRAB/025\tstored in offsite archive box ref: CCW/WRAB/025,  internal contact  Data Services team\tSurveys of South Wales including Dyfed/Powys and West Glamorgan/Llanelli. Reference copies of species found by 10km grid squares, not known where originals are kept\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19062\tNRW_DS125398\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.408Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPowys Woodlands Old Maps and woodland survey (Paper records)\ttbc\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333938\toff-site archive ref CCW/SERN/30\tOpendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tpaper copies of old maps of Powys Woodlands and woodland species, no year mentioned\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19060\tNRW_DS125399\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.234Z\t10\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecies \u0026amp; Habitats survey data by grid square - paper data records\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333939\toff-site archive storage ref:  CCW/GRID/SH/1, CCW/GRID/SH/2,  CCW/GRID/SJ/1,  CCW/GRID/SJ/2,  CCW/GRID/SN/1,  CCW/GRID/SN/2,  CCW/GRID/SN/3,  CCW/GRID/SN/4,  CCW/GRID/SN/5,  CCW/GRID/SN/6,  CCW/WRAB/027- is also SN CCC/ tbc by Jill- is also SN CCW/GRID/SO/1,  CCW/GRID/SO/2,  CCW/GRID/SO/3,  CCW/GRID/SS/1,  CCW/GRID/ST/1\tData Services Team opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       Opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tThis is a collation of mixed habitat \u0026amp; species data (Heath/Mires, Grasslands,  Mixed Habitats , Heath/Upland, Wetlands and Open Water  Bird, Bats, Invertebrates, lower plants, Woodlands etc.) for the following grid squares: SH, SJ, SN, SS,ST, SO\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T09:50:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19059\tNRW_DS125400\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.177Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHabitat Maps for designated sites (NNRs \u0026amp; SSSIs) - paper records\ttbc\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343030\toff-site storage references: fill in once received from JILL Records team\topendata@naturalresources.gov.uk       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tThis is a collation of habitat maps and a mixture of copies and originals from 1970s\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19065\tNRW_DS125401\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.387Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHeathland data for Pembrokeshire (paper records)\ttbc\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343031\toff-site archive storage ref: CCW/WRPB/005\topendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tThese are the various paper data survey records: 1. 1987/88 Skomer general vegetation and seacliff communities data 2. 1979 Pembrokeshire Heathland survey field notes (species recording cards) 3. 2 files of collated and original information related to all Heathland sites in Pembrokeshire file 1 all sites, file 2 Dowrog Common detailed file\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19064\tNRW_DS125404\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.409Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIthon river valley survey (paper records)\ttbc\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343034\toff-site archive box ref: CCW/SERNB/41\topendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tThis is a paper record survey of 1980 of the river bank and aquatic habitat\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:43:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19058\tNRW_DS125407\t2024-05-28T13:01:42.975Z\t420\tValid\tEditable\tElan Valley Drone Survey 2022\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343037\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\Elan Valley - for combined .lyr  files (raw data: Z:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\GIS \u0026amp; Spatial Data\\From Y drive\\Peatlands\\Elan Valley)\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Esther Clews, Specialist Advisor, Data and GIS – National Peatland Action Programme\tAir Survey of 10 upland peatland sites in Mid Wales flown in January and March 2022. The 10 sites were flown between the 12th January and 25th March 2022.   All sites were surveyed using an RGB vertical camera, then the data processed to produce digital surface models (DSM) and orthomosaics. The DSMs were processed to generate catchment, flow and contour vector data sets to assist with the peatbog restoration planning.   Drone details - eBee RTK unmanned aircraft system (UAS) by Sensefly. The eBee is an electric fixed wing Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV), weight 1.1 kg, with a maximum speed of 110 km/h and a maximum wind capability of 12 m/s.   Camera - Sensefly S.O.D.A. RGB camera: 1″ Sensor, RGB lens F/2.8-11, 10.6 mm, resolution 5,472 x 3,648 px (3:2), Exposure compensation ±2.0, Global Shutter 1/30 – 1/2000s, Auto White balance, ISO range 125-6400.   Flight path - GIS ESRI Shape or kml files provided by the client were loaded into the UAS to provide survey area coverage parameters.   Data resolution - The UAV is programmed to fly the areas at a height that obtains an image resolution of between 3-4 cm/pixel, with an image overlap of 75%. The exact resolution is dependent on the ruggedness of the terrain; the resolution is set by the lowest resolution area of the orthomosaic.   Data capture - Once the orthomosaic sand DSMs had been created, the DSMs were processed to generate catchments, flow lines and 0.5m interval contour layers. This was carried out using QGIS and GRASS. The orthomosaics were then used to manually digitise peat and hag patchwork areas, hags, bare peat/water.   The 10 sites fell into the following categories:  • No visible peat bog areas or stabilised hags and gulleys.  • Signs of historic peatbog degradation, but currently stable/vegetated – vegetated hags and gulleys  • Visibly eroding peatbog areas – exposed bare peat, visible eroding hags and gulleys\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-12-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-07T15:05:49.730Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19057\tNRW_DS125432\t2024-05-28T13:01:42.910Z\t354\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDynamic Dunescapes National Vegetation Classification Survey Date\t© CNC/NRW   Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343332\tPlanned for X:Drive with suggested filepath but needs confirming  X:\\Biological\\Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Boundaries.lyr  X:\\Biological\\ Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Mospoly.lyr  X:\\Biological\\ Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Photographs.lyr  X:\\Biological\\ Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Polygons.lyr  X:\\Biological\\ Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Quadrats.lyr  X:\\Biological\\ Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Target Notes.lyr\tJulie Creer - Lead Specialist Advisor: Coastal Habitats\tData from a National Vegetation Classification survey of 10 sand dune sites including shapefiles of NVC polygons, vegetation mosaics, target notes, quadrats, photograph locations and overall survey boundary. Data collected in 2020 and 2021 based on field survey of entire sites with field maps digitised to OS Mastermap and aerial photographs.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-12-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19056\tNRW_DS125436\t2024-05-28T13:01:42.865Z\t212\tValid\tEditable\tTerrestrial Priority Habitat Surveys\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343336\tX:\\Biological\\Priority Habitat Site Surveys (Terrestrial)\\Priority Habitat Mapping Vegetation.lyr X:\\Biological\\Priority Habitat Site Surveys (Terrestrial)\\Priority Habitat Mapping Mospoly.lyr X:\\Biological\\Priority Habitat Site Surveys (Terrestrial)\\Priority Habitat Mapping Notes.lyr X:\\Biological\\Priority Habitat Site Surveys (Terrestrial)\\Priority Habitat Mapping photos.lyr X:\\Biological\\Priority Habitat Site Surveys (Terrestrial)\\Priority Habitat Mapping Quadrats.lyr X:\\Biological\\Priority Habitat Site Surveys (Terrestrial)\\Priority Habitat Mapping Sites.lyr  Associated survey reports will be saved in different places on the DMS depending on the location or type of site surveyed\tKathryn Birch, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Habitats\tThis dataset contains Priority Habitat Surveys of individual sites (not Wales-wide coverage), focusing on terrestrial habitats listed under Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2016, known as Priority Habitats. It consists of detailed surveys of plant communities in mixed habitat context at a coarser scale than Phase 2 (National Vegetation Classification) habitat surveys.    The primary objective of these surveys is to identify and map areas of Priority Habitat of nature conservation importance, which can then feed into existing Wales-wide Priority Habitat extent and distribution data. The survey data can be used to identify areas of important habitat to inform schemes and projects such as woodland creation schemes, developments, or agricultural improvement, and can inform conservation management and restoration work to improve habitat condition and connectivity, and enhance biodiversity.    The dataset is not the product of a co-ordinated Survey Programme like the Lowland Peatland and Lowland Grassland Surveys of Wales, but is instead designed to be a single place to store ad-hoc site surveys of mixed Priority Habitat, under a common data structure, so they can be easily analysed and shared. Only surveys that have been extensively validated and verified by NRW vegetation and cartographic specialists will be added to the dataset.    This dataset is not to be confused with layer files derived from the ‘Environment (Wales) Act Section 7 Terrestrial Habitats of Principal Importance’ dataset, which is largely based on the Phase 1 Terrestrial Habitat Survey (1987-1997). These layer files are split into Wales-wide coverage for individual habitats, for example ‘Priority Habitat Blanket Bog.lyr.\u0027 See metadata NRW_DS122418 for more information.    Habitat composition may change over time, for example due to successional trends, land management or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type or species in the digital data is therefore no guarantee of it still being extant on the ground.  Individual site reports for most sites surveyed are also available upon request.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-01-12-2025-10-30\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-03T13:02:43.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19108\tNRW_DS125437\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.957Z\t821\tValid\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343337\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Tidal Flaps.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Watercourses.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Pumping Stations.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Boundaries.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Sluices.lyr ,\tJames West, Team Leader, Integrated Engineering NW\tDrainage districts are typically found in low-Iying land where boundaries are determined by physical, not, political attributes. Districts vary in nature and size, from the mainly agricultural (such as Conwy, Llanfrothen and Towyn) to the semi-urban (such as the Gwent Levels).    Drainage districts are at risk from various sources of flooding. Without flood risk and water level management, drainage districts would be unsuitable to live in and would not protect and provide for the variety of agricultural land, utilities and transport networks that have developed within their boundaries.     This dataset shows the location of the boundaries of IDD\u0027s within Wales including the maintained watercourses, tidal flaps, pumping stations and for for Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge Levels \u0026amp; Lower Wye and Powysland it also shows locations of sluices.     Current Drainage Districts as of 2022:   Afon Ganol Ardudwy Marsh,  Borth Bog,   Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge Levels \u0026amp; Lower Wye (Gwent Levels),  Dysynni Valley,    Glaslyn - Pensyflog,    Harlech \u0026amp; Maentwrog,    Llanfrothen,    Malltraeth Marsh,    Mawddach \u0026amp; Wnion,    Powysland,    River Conwy,    Towyn.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-01-17\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/4185/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_internal_drainage_district\t2025-05-09T16:06:49.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19112\tNRW_DS125446\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.980Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Hydrology and Dipwells\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343436\t\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19110\tNRW_DS125448\t2024-05-28T13:02:41.037Z\t488\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey and assessment of the saproxylic invertebrate fauna of Gregynog National Nature Reserve, Powys - 2022\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343438\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID) in Recorder 6\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThe Gregynog Hall Estate comprises about 300ha of ancient wood pasture habitats within the old county of Montgomeryshire. The 15ha of Great Wood were designated a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) for its rich epiphytic lichen communities in 1978 although it later proved to also have significant interest for invertebrates and especially those species associated with the decaying wood (saproxylic) habitats provided by the abundance of veteran trees. The SSSI was extended in 2011 and the whole estate declared a National Nature Reserve in 2012.   A survey of the saproxylic invertebrates of the wider NNR was commissioned by Natural Resources Wales in 2022. This is the first baseline survey of the fauna, previous research comprising exploratory surveys only. The aims of the survey were to determine the current condition of the saproxylic invertebrate fauna and to identify any threats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T15:42:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19104\tNRW_DS125451\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.796Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForester Management Features\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343531\tForrester database\tGeraint Morgan - Specialist Advisor, Forester System\tThese datasets show the location of features within the national forest estate that are capture and stored for forestry management purposes such as for planning, safety and access. This record covers the following features within the forest estate boundaries:  Crossing points - these will generally be electricity or telecom pylons. Cattle Grid, Gates and Barrier Areas of open water - these will generally be fairly natural and permanent in character and not be created by dams Walls water courses - these will be natural water courses which will usually be permanent.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T15:43:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19101\tNRW_DS125452\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.888Z\t1376\tValid\tEditable\tAuthorised Landfill Sites Points\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343532\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Waste\\Historic Landfill Sites Points.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Gareth E Lewis - SUW Advisor Wales, Knowledge Strategy and Planning\tThis dataset shows the location of landfill sites which are currently authorised by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under The Environmental Permitting (England and Wales) Regulations 2010. This data is a subset of information from NRWs permiting database. The dataset includes waste and installation permits that fall into the following category’s.   • A1: Co-Disposal Landfill Site;  • A2: Other Landfill Site taking Special Waste;  • A4: Household, Commercial and Industrial Waste Landfill;  • A5: Landfill taking Non-Biodegradable Wastes;  • A6: Landfill taking other wastes;  • A7: Industrial Waste Landfill (Factory cartilage);  • 5.2 A(1) a): Waste Landfilling; \u0026gt;10T/D with capacity\u0026gt;25,000T excluding inert waste;  • 5.2 A(1) b): Waste Landfilling;  • Any other Landfill to which the 2002 regulations apply;  • L04: Non Hazardous Landfill;  • and L05: Inert Landfill.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/6939/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_authorised_landfill_sites_pnt\t2026-02-23T11:01:29.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19096\tNRW_DS125453\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.535Z\t1800\tValid\tEditable\tHistoric Landfill Sites points\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343533\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Waste\\Historic Landfill Sites Points.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Gareth E Lewis - SUW Advisor Wales, Knowledge Strategy and Planning  Also could contact: Tyrone (Ty) Ward - PPC/RSR Officer 1, PPC Team\tThis dataset shows the point location historic landfill sites that are held in NRWs permitting database and have been issues under the EPR. These are landfill sites which have been taken off the Authorised Landfill Sites points dataset when the waste licence status changes to either:  - Licence expired: Some licences issued under the Control of Pollution Act 1974 were time limited and expired on the date specified in the licence.  - Licence Revoked: Whereby the licence has been entirely revoked and is no longer in force; or  - Licence Surrendered: Operator has successfully surrendered the licence which is no longer in force.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/6940/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_historic_landfill_sites_pnt\t2026-02-23T11:02:35.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19103\tNRW_DS125454\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.799Z\t212\tValid\tEditable\tHabitat Map of Wales resurvey pilot – field survey\t© CNC/NRW and OS Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343534\tCurrently in Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Natural_Resources_Planning\\Biodiversity_Fisheries_Geodiversity\\Jon Rothwell\\P1_2022\\Checking\\Final GDB and LYR Files   But getting moved to X:Drive - location not decided yet.\tStuart Smith, Lead Specialist Advisor, NRW and Jon Rothwell, Specialist Advisor, NRW\tPhase 1 survey of 30 tetrads in Carmarthenshire and Neath Port Talbot. Digitised in the field using Qfield (android software a data capture application linked to QGIS).     Data collected as part of the Pilot for Habitat Map of Wales resurvey project.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-01-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T10:51:25.447Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19100\tNRW_DS125456\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.858Z\t734\tUnassessed\tEditable\tReservoir Regulations\t© CNC/NRW   Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343536\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Reservoir Regulation.lyr\tGerallt Richards,  Reservoir Regulation Team\tA layer showing the reservoirs in Wales. Reservoirs with a capacity greater than 10,000 cubic metres are regulated as large raised reservoirs. This layer provides information such as the capacity of the reservoir, dam height and ownership details. Further information can be obtained from the Reservoir Regulation Team.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-02-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19113\tNRW_DS125457\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.958Z\t627\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThreat of landfill sites to Marine Protected Area habitat features in Wales\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under an OGL subject to a full open data assessment.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343537\tX:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\Coastal landfill sites posing a threat to MPA features\tHarriet Robinson - Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning\tThe map package shows the potential overall threat coastal landfill sites in Wales pose to Marine Protected Area (MPA) habitat features under a present day epoch (2005-2025), a medium term epoch (2025-2055) and a long-term epoch (2055-2105).  Overall threat presented in the map package is a combination of the risk of waste being released from the landfill site and the vulnerability of MPA habitat features to waste if released from these sites. The map package ultimately shows the results of a high-level assessment on the potential impacts of coastal landfill sites to MPAs.   The study aimed to provide an initial assessment of this impact to highlight where regional or site-specific investigations should be undertaken to further understand the threat coastal landfill sites pose to MPA features.     The boundaries of the Operational Areas and MPAs are also included in the map package.   Excel files of the attribute tables from the GIS map package are also provided.     More information on the rationale, methods, results and assumptions can be found in the Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Evidence Report 673.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-03-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:47:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19115\tNRW_DS125458\t2024-05-28T13:02:41.128Z\t1558\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Regulations Cycle 3 Classification\tSee the use constraints for each individual Cycle 3 layer, as it will vary depending on the data used in its creation. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343538\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\tData Custodian: Rob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment\tThis dataset shows the boundaries and baseline classification, undertaken in 2021, for The Water Framework Directive (WFD) Cycle 3. It is an update in classification for all water bodies in Wales following on from the cycle 1 and cycle 2 classification rounds.    The Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England \u0026amp; Wales) Regulations 2017 (referred to as the WFD Regulations) provide a framework for managing the water environment in England and Wales. The Directive aims for “good status” of all ground and surface water (rivers, lakes, transitional water, and coastal waters). Under the WFD Regulations, a river basin management plan must be prepared for each river basin district. The plan includes environmental objectives and a summary of the programmes of measures required to achieve those objectives.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-08-05\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/4471/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_wfd_cycle_3_classifications\t2025-10-15T09:43:37.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19107\tNRW_DS125460\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.956Z\t352\tValid\tEditable\tEryri SSSI Bryophyte Survey 2015\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343630\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FEryri%20Bryophyte%20Survey%20BBS%202015\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet - Terrestrial Habitats and Species Specialist Advisor\t18 cwms and two summits in Eryri SSSI were surveyed by 30 bryologists from the British Bryological Society, including 9 national experts, between 7th and 12th June 2015. The survey was coordinated by the Natural Resources Wales non-vascular plant ecologist.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-09-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-06-30T14:24:03.033Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19098\tNRW_DS125462\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.660Z\t721\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey for larvae of the caddis Limnephilus tauricus on Cors Erddreiniog - Winter 2022\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343632\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID)\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThe ‘Natur am Byth!’ partnership unites nine environmental charities and Natural Resources Wales to deliver the country’s largest natural heritage and outreach programme to save species from extinction and reconnect people to nature.  Following the rediscovery of adults of the Vulnerable caddis Limnephilus tauricus on Cors Erddreiniog in July and August 2022, its only Welsh locality, an extensive search of 26 locations for larvae was undertaken. The opportunity was taken to look for the Critically Endangered L. pati O’Connor at the same time as the two species are known to occur together although it has never been recorded from Wales (Wallace, 2016).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T15:45:32.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19111\tNRW_DS125465\t2024-05-28T13:02:41.033Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNorth Wales Marine Observation Scheme (Paper Data)\tIncludes some sensitive marine species. Full restrictions to be added\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343635\tPaper Data stored in off site storage – Combined with CCW/NRB/118\tUnknown. Catalogued by Data Services Team as part of Records and paper data records management project May have value if mobilised\tVolunteer based survey of marine observations, carried in sublittoral zone around Anglesey and Llyn Peninsular\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T11:02:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19109\tNRW_DS125466\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.994Z\t341\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaproxylic invertebrates at Carn Gafallt RSPB Reserve in 2022\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343636\tWID – Natur Am Byth Surveys\tMike Howe\tA survey of the dead wood (saproxylic) invertebrate fauna of the Carn Gafallt Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB) Reserve was undertaken over four days in 2022. This work is part of the Natur am Byth! partnership project, which aims to promote the recovery of a range of key species in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T15:47:15.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19097\tNRW_DS125467\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.631Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNVC Upland Surveys Ceredigion and Powys 1991-1993 (paper records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343637\tOffsite-archive box no. CCW/SERNB/51.\tdata custodian not known; contact Data Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tRaw survey data, field maps \u0026amp; notes, quadrat cards   (potentially ad hoc surveys?)  The various papper record files cover: Claerddu, Elendydd SSSI (Cwmanddwr Common, LLuest Abercarnon, Glascwm \u0026amp; Gladesty Hill SSi, Gamrhiw, Pen y Garreg, Ty Mawr, Great Gors, Upper Cwm Pastures, Nantybeddau, Nant yt Henfran SO67753, Rogesrstone Pasture SO 07123, Dolfawr Pastures, Claerwen, Traedrhiw Drain, Ty Mawr,\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19099\tNRW_DS125471\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.718Z\t333\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey and Monitoring of Epiphytic Lichens around PAWS Sites in Brechfa Forest\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343731\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FPAWS%20Brechfa%20Forest\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet\tThis survey was designed to look at four sections of the forest not previously recorded for lichens. Oak, ash and birch were selected to be surveyed and monitored in PAWS (Planted Ancient Woodland Sites) and AW (Ancient Woodland) areas of the forest.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:34:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19105\tNRW_DS125474\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.797Z\t423\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveying for the Bog Pseudoscorpion Microbisium brevifemoratum on the raised bog at Cors Fochno (Dyfi SSSI)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343734\tData is stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder6\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThis project surveyed Cors Fochno in October/November 2022, using standardised vacuum sampling, for the very rare Bog Pseudoscorpion Microbisium brevifemoratum(Ellingsen, 1903). A total of sixty-two minutes of vacuum sampling was undertaken during the survey at 17 sample points. In total, five specimens of M. brevifemoratum were found at two sample points during the survey. A single adult at SN63849084 (29th October 2022) represents a new location, with a further four adults found at SN64029087 (22nd November 2022) very near to where the species was found in 2021.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T17:26:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19102\tNRW_DS125494\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.797Z\t491\tValid\tEditable\tBryophyte Survey and Assessment of Cwm Rheidol NNR and Coedydd a Cheunant Rheidol SSSI\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343934\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FCoed%20Rheidol%20Bryophyte%20Survey\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tAn updated bryophyte survey of the Cwm Rheidol NNR and Coedydd a Cheunant SSSI. It updates a previous survey undertaken by Averis in 1998.   The bryophyte assemblage was found to be of national conservation interest and is certainly within the top ten sites in Wales for Oceanic Ravine Bryophytes (a species assemblage of national conservation importance under Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2006).    The survey area comprises two sections of the Afon Rheidol, one running north from Devil’s Bridge for approximately 3km and one running west from Devil’s Bridge for approximately 2km.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19114\tNRW_DS125497\t2024-05-28T13:02:41.129Z\t829\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLichen survey of three coastal SSSIs on the Llŷn Peninsula, Gwynedd\t© CNC/NRW  Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343937\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FCoastal%20Lichens%20Lleyn\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet - Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tBaseline survey of coastal lichens covering three SSSI on the western Llŷn Peninsula, Gwynedd; Glannau Aberdaron, Mynydd Penarfynydd and Porth Ceiriad, Porth Neigwl ac Ynysoedd Sant Tudwal. It will involve coverage of all three SSSI but complete coverage is not anticipated given their size and difficult terrain.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-03-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19095\tNRW_DS125498\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.042Z\t678\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveillance of the montane macrolichen Thamnolia vermicularis in Eryri, 2022\t© CNC/NRW   Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343938\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FThamnolia%20vermicularis%20in%20Eryri\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet - Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tResurveys of all four Welsh sites where the snowbed lichen Thamnolia vermicularis has been recorded found no extant colonies. Climate Change, Nitrogen pollution and heavy sheep grazing are implicated in its extinction in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-03-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:17:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19093\tNRW_DS125499\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.896Z\t597\tValid\tEditable\tMapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Spatial Planning in Wales\tData may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343939\tX:\\Planning\\Marine\\Mapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Planning - Birds.lyr,     X:\\Planning\\Marine\\Mapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Planning - Fish.lyr,    X:\\Planning\\Marine\\Mapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Planning - Habitats Benthic Species.lyr,   X:\\Planning\\Marine\\Mapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Planning - Mammals.lyr\tLee Murray - Specialist Advisor Marine Spatial Planning\tEnvironmental Considerations spatial datasets for marine planning. These datasets are derived from multiple datasets indicating the presence of protected features in Welsh waters. The data is presented on 1 km2 hexagonal grids.    Operational phase was considered for all sectors for all feature groups, and includes maintenance activities.  The construction phase was only considered for sectors where potential construction impacts are substantially different from those during operation (tidal stream, wave, tidal range, Floating offshore wind and cabling).  However, for the Bird feature group, construction phase was only considered for tidal range and cabling. The scoring methodology included three main stages:   1. The occurrence score reflects either the presence or absence, or standardized abundance (where abundance data was available) of features (i.e., species, habitats, or protected sites) and were scored from 1 to 3 on a continuous scale where 1 was the lowest density and 3 the highest for each feature.    2. The conservation importance score is an estimate of the level of protection each feature has based on the legislation that confers a feature protection. It is important to note that all the features included in this work are conferred protection under various legislation and all are important considerations in the planning of developments. This step was scored from 1 to 5.     3. An impact score to reflect potential impacts on features based on the likely pressures generated by each sector was applied for the operational pressures of each of the focus sectors and, separate to this, construction pressures for some sectors. Where there was considered to be a lack of evidence then a higher score was applied. The current approach did not assess specific technologies individually but considered the potential pressures that could originate from a sector and their potential to negatively affect each of the features. This step was scored from 1 to 3.   A total score was calculated for each feature in each cell, and these were summed for each 1 km2 hexagonal grid cell, providing a relative ‘environmental considerations’ score for each feature group per sector. The approach taken to the scoring differed slightly between feature groups based on the available evidence\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-10-24\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/5653/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_mecmp\t2025-11-12T13:21:19.028Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n19090\tNRW_DS125500\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.857Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDNA Barcoding Survey of Dune Slack Bryum in Wales\t@CNC/NRW  Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353030\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FDune%20Bryum%20DNA%20Barcoding\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tA survey of dune slack mosses using DNA Barcoding, including sampling of mosses from recently scraped slacks.   The following sites were surveyed: Tywyn Aberffraw Newborough Warren Morfa Dyffryn  Whiteford Burrows Merthyr Mawr Warren Kenfig Burrows\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-03-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:32:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19091\tNRW_DS125502\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.857Z\t298\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGathering Twig Lichen Data for Powys SSSIs to Investigate Ammonia Levels: Second Phase\t@CNC/NRW  Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353032\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2FLichen%20surveys%20to%20investigate%20ammonia%20impacts\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet - Sam.Bosanquet@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tTwig Lichen Surveys on 9 SSSI in Powys, showing that these sites are suffering significant ammonia pollution except in a few upland areas.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-03-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19094\tNRW_DS125504\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.002Z\t778\tValid\tEditable\tInformation for each Carbon Reduction Commitment (CRC) participant\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353034\tExternal Website: Published on data.gov by the EA.  DO NOT ONWARD PUBLISH METADATA TO FindOpenData (Published by the EA so this metadata for NRW internal catalogue only)\tCustodian: Elizabeth Voice, Lead Specialist Officer, Species and Carbon Permitting.                    DO NOT ONWARD PUBLISH DATA as Published by the EA\tThe CRC Order 2013 provides for publication of information on the basis of participants\u0027 annual reports plus details submitted when they registered for the scheme. The CRC Energy Efficiency Scheme (CRC) is a mandatory scheme which aims to improve energy efficiency and cut emissions for large public and private sector energy users across the UK. The scheme aims to encourage organisations to develop energy management strategies that promote a better understanding of energy usage, and lead to improved energy efficiency.   The scheme is split into phases: Phase 1 from 1 April 2010 until 31 March 2014 and Phase 2 from 1 April 2014 until 31 March 2019.    Each year, CRC participants have to monitor their energy consumption and report this information into the CRC Registry, an online IT system, which calculates their carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions. Participants must purchase and surrender \u0027allowances\u0027 to cover these emissions - one allowance for each tonne of CO2.     Further information on the scheme can be found on the CRC pages of GOV.UK.   The ARP format was introduced for the 2012 to 2013 reporting year and replaces the performance league tables (PLT). Removal of the PLT was part of the government’s simplification of the CRC scheme. These annually produced data sets contain the information provided by participants to the CRC Registry including organisational type, energy use and CO2 emissions. The data should be read in combination with the equivalent years ‘CRC Energy Efficiency Scheme: annual report publication which are located on GOV.UK. CRC performance league tables were published for the 2010 to 2011 and 2011 to 2012 compliance years and ranked participants according to their energy efficiency performance against a range of metrics.\tEnvironment Agency (EA)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-03-03\tpublication\tnull\thttps://www.data.gov.uk/dataset/4b709a17-6e98-4021-a207-137ac931bfc3/information-for-each-carbon-reduction-commitment-crc-participant3\t2026-01-09T15:59:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19089\tNRW_DS125505\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.825Z\t913\tValid\tEditable\tRed Squirrel (Sciurus vulgaris) abundance and habitat preference survey data – Anglesey 2021/22\t© CNC/NRW. Requests for this data will be considered by the NRW Terrestrial Ecosystems Groups. If requests are approved, the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353035\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/Red%20Squirrel/Research%20Papers/NRW%20Evidence%20Report%20672%20-survey%20data%20(Red%20Squirrel%20abundance%20and%20habitat%20preference%20-%20Anglesey).xlsx?d\u003dw01227aac5cc5427b85dd2d31e5e2155f\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\tCustodian: Becky Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor  - Terrestrial Mammals, Terrestrial Ecosystems Groups\tProject undertaken to analyse the use of camera traps in determining population abundance of red squirrel in different habitat types on Anglesey.   Two sites were used in this survey; Pentraeth and Newborough,     The data contains trap locations identified by What3 words, and includes details of habitat types and numbers of squirrels caught.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-02-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:04:32.143Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19092\tNRW_DS125506\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.855Z\t315\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLichen Survey of Dinefwr NNR (VC 44 Carmarthenshire): including re-monitoring the 2013 baseline and comments on air pollution and Ash Dieback.\t@CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353036\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FDinefwr%20Lichen%20Surveys\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet - Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tSurvey and accompanying spreadsheet covering monitoring of the epiphytic lichens of Dinefwr Estate SSSI in Carmarthenshire, including assessment impacts of Ash Dieback and ammonia pollution\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-03-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19087\tNRW_DS125508\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.634Z\t179\tValid\tEditable\tMorfa Friog Monitoring Data 2018\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353038\tZ:\\Archive\\Flood\\SPI\\NHCP\\Morfa Friog\\ \\MorfaFriog_Habitats_v2\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Heather Lewis, Lead Specialist Advisor: Coastal Habitats, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team\tMorfa Friog managed coastal realignment site situated on the Mawddach estuary was created when the flood embankment was breached in November 2015.   A methodology was established and baseline data collected during autumn 2018 to monitor the development of the saltmarsh vegetation on the site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-03-16\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19088\tNRW_DS125511\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.597Z\t399\tValid\tEditable\tMonitoring of the Black Bog Ant Formica picea on Rhossili Down SSSI in 2022\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353131\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID)\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThe Black Bog Ant Formica picea is rare in the UK where it is associated with bogs and wet heath at a small number of sites in southern England including the heaths of Dorset and Surrey and the valley bogs in the New Forest, with a single site in Yorkshire. In Wales, it is restricted to Rhossili Down on Gower, Cors Goch Llanllwch in Carmarthenshire and Cors Llanerch in Pembrokeshire.   It was first recorded on Rhossili Down in 1913 and subsequently refound in 1996. A survey in July 1997 found 49 active nests along four east to west-oriented transects across the bog and flushed wet heath habitats to the east of the central ridge, with a further 23 active nests elsewhere on the site. It was estimated that between 840 and 4970 active nests were present across the site, representing the largest population in the UK.   The current survey in August 2022 repeated the 1997 work, with a count of active nests along the four transects.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-05-02T14:48:23.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19086\tNRW_DS125516\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.545Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCeredigion Coastal Rare Plant Survey 1975 \u0026amp; Ceredigion Rare Plants\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353136\toff-site archive ref: CCW/WRAB/024\tData Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       Data Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tThis is a survey of rare plants in Ceredigion coastal areas. Most of the 1700 species of British vascular plants can be found on the coast, the greater part of them on the more terrestrial coastal habitats on sand dunes, shingle beaches or cliffs. Also included is a register and survey of Ceredigion rare plants.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:06:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19139\tNRW_DS125517\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.379Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey of hedges and their woody species in Ceredigion 1974-1979\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353137\tCCW/WRAB/026\tData Services Opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       Opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk Data Services Team\tA survey of the woody species and physical attributes of 2223 30 m sample lenths of 752 hedges in Ceredigion took plce between 1974-1979. Most hedges ocurred on banks. Lane hedges were in the best condition; the hedge conditions and proportion managed declined significantly with altitude. As each hedge in this survey has been mapped, there is the potential for the survey to be repeated in the future.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19132\tNRW_DS125518\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.234Z\t9\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNCC Biological survey of common land in Wales 1989-1994 (paper data records)\tTBC - will be open data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353138\t5 boxes of paper data are currently held in the comms room F13, under the table.  Labelled as , PP19, PP20, PP21, PP22, PP23.  These are to be prepared to go to TNA (work to be carried out with Jill), Box PP23 my need some info to the disposed of as mainly supporting info or rough maps\tPossibly Buddug Jones or Stuart Smith\tIn 1986 the Countryside Commission published the Report of the Common Land Forum which resulted in the possibility of new legislation on common land. The Nature Conservancy Council subsequently commissioned the Biological Survey of Common Land in order to assess the implications for conservation agencies.   Work on the Survey started in November 1987 and continued until December 1994. It was undertaken by Rural Surveys Research Unit at Aberystwyth University, and following the split of NCC into English Nature, Scottish Natural Heritage and the Countryside Council for Wales in 1991, the three national bodies continued to fund the research.    The aim of the survey was to record detailed information about wildlife in each of the common land areas in order to produce informed management schemes. These schemes would be operated by local management associations, consisting of representatives from all interested parties.\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW)-Nature Conservancy Council (NCC)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-03-30\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T11:59:41.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n19137\tNRW_DS125521\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.366Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Survey of Selected Grassland Sites in Ceredigion 1987\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353231\toffi-site archive box ref: CCW/WRAB/023\tData Services Team opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk, off-site archive box ref:CCW/WRAB/023        Data Services team Opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tThe objective of the filed work were: Determine the nature and distribution of plant comunities within the site. Collect detailed records from a representative sample of plant communities. Note the distribution and abundance within the site of any plant species known to be rare or uncommon in Ceredigion\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:42.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19145\tNRW_DS125522\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.421Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey of Broadleaved Woodlands in Ceredigion 1978\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353232\toff-site archive box ref: CCW/WRAB/023\tData Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk, Off-site archive box ref: CCW/WRAB/023       Data Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNCC Survey to study the extent and state of semi-natural broadleaved woodland in Ceredigion (SN grdi squares)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19142\tNRW_DS125523\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.376Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAfon Teifi Data Collection 1989 (paper  data records)\ttbc - likely to be open data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353233\tData to be sent to off-site storage. Box numbers: box 1 of 2 is CCW/WRAB/48 and box 2 of 2 is CCW/WRAB/49\tData to be stored off site.  Mobilisation would be in form of scanning as raw data may be available elsewhere.  Would only be considered if originals are found, otherwise may be disposed of\tData Collection created ahead of proposed notification of the site as a NCR.  The aim of the project was to collate a wide range of scientific information already available from NCC and others to aid the designation of the site.  data was collated from local regional NCC files, FBA, Welsh Water, RSBP, Otter Haven Trust, UWIST, Dyfed Wildlife Trust.  The data consists of 8 volumes.  Volumes 7 is a Report and Bibliography Volume 1: Vegetation main river Volume 2: Vegetation tributaries Volume 3: Vegetation tributaries report Volume 4:Inverts, fish Volume 5: Birds Volume 6: mammals Volume 7: Final report and tributaries Volume 8: Maps\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:43:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19140\tNRW_DS125526\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.377Z\t469\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Distribution of the Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior on Whiteford Burrows \u0026amp; Cwm Ivy Marsh - September 2021\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353236\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID) in Recorder 6\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tWhiteford Burrows is a dune system lying on the north-west coast of the Gower Peninsula and is part of the much larger Carmarthen Bay Dunes/Twyni Bae Caerfyrddin SAC. The Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior, listed on the EC Habitats \u0026amp; Species Directive Annex IIa, was first found on Whiteford Burrows in May 1983 - a new live record for the species not only in Wales but also in western Britain. Previous V. angustior studies have been carried out at this location between 1983 and 2018. It was suggested that further surveys be undertaken at the far north-east of the transition zone, throughout the main body of the dune system (focussing upon areas in damp hollows and on the margins of dune slacks where the water table is near the surface) and with a survey of the Cwm Ivy Marsh marine/terrestrial transition zone recently formed with the area now subject to tidal inflow.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T16:01:04.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19143\tNRW_DS125529\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.416Z\t438\tValid\tEditable\tSaproxylic Invertebrates Survey Data at Gilfach Radnorshire Wildlife Trust Reserve in 2022\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353239\tWID held on Recorder 6 and wll be published as part of the NBN dataset\tMike Howe\tA survey of the dead wood (saproxylic) invertebrate fauna of the Gilfach Nature Reserve, which is owned and managed by the Radnorshire Wildlife Trust (RWT), was undertaken over four man-days in 2022. This work is part of the Natur am Byth! Partnership project, which aims to promote the recovery of selected key species in Wales. The Gilfach survey involved both a range of hand-searching techniques and the installation of five vane traps that were operated between May and September. The survey has shown Gilfach to support a diverse invertebrate fauna that includes a number of scarce species.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T15:28:09.886Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19138\tNRW_DS125531\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.371Z\t1162\tValid\tEditable\tTranquillity \u0026amp; Place Wales Sound Environment Part 1 2023\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444 and Derived in Part from BGS Digital Data under Licence Number 2013/062. British Geological Society. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353331\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquillity and Place\\Sound Environment Part 1 2023\tJill Bullen - Lead Specialist Advisor: Landscape\tThe nationally consistent terrestrial Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place resource identifies the strategic and local resource in remote, rural, peri-urban and urban areas for use as an evidence base to inform policy intent, practice and provision for well-being benefits. There are 6 themes.    Tranquillity is associated with the degree to which places and ecosystems deliver a state of quiet, calm, peace and well-being. This can be described as a relative abundance, perception or experience of nature, natural landscapes and features and/or a relative freedom from unwanted visual disturbance, signs of human influence and artificial noise.    Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Sound Environment Part I delivers Theme 4, mapping sound environments where natural sounds are more prominent than noise and are appropriate to context. It includes sounds other than road and railway noise.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-04-04\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/5344\t2025-04-07T09:59:03.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19141\tNRW_DS125532\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.379Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveys for Large Mason Bee Osmia xanthomelana on Llŷn Peninsula in 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353332\tLink to Associated Evidence Report - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/kdim/rl/NRW%20Publications/Natur%20Am%20Byth%20Reports/Natur%20am%20Byth!%20Report%20No.%20007%20-%20Osmia%20xanthomelana%20report%20April%202023%20redacted.pdf  Data is contained within the Welsh Invertebrate Database - Natur Am Byth\tMike Howe\tCreated as part of the development phase of the Natur am Byth project this field survey was taken at two sites (Porth Neigwl and Porth Ceiriad) located on the Llyn Peninsula where previous records have been made of the Large Mason Bee.\t\t2023-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19131\tNRW_DS125534\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.099Z\t428\tValid\tEditable\tA survey for Short-necked Oil Beetle Meloe brevicollis at Castlemartin Range SSSI\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353334\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID)\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tFollowing the recording of an adult at Linney Head in June 2015, a survey was conducted at Castlemartin Range SSSI in Pembrokeshire in 2022 to clarify the  conservation status of the Nationally Rare Short-necked Oil Beetle Meloe brevicollis. A single dead adult and numerous triungulins were found at Pen-y-holt Bay confirming that Short-necked Oil Beetle is both present and breeding within the SSSI. This is believed to be the first record of wild larvae of Short-necked Oil Beetle in Wales and suggests that triungulin surveys can be a successful tool in identifying ‘new’ locations for the beetle at times when adults are less active.  Short-necked Oil Beetle exhibits a restricted distribution at Castlemartin Range SSSI, increasing its vulnerability to local and national extinction. At present, Pen-y-holt Bay is the only location within the SSSI supporting recent records of the beetle (and, indeed, the only breeding evidence). The conservation of this population is therefore of upmost importance.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:03:35.345Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19144\tNRW_DS125543\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.304Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey of rare coastal species of the moss genus Bryum in Wales in September 2015\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353433\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FDune%20Bryum%20survey%202015\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tA report on the monitoring of rare Bryum mosses on Welsh sand dunes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T17:25:08.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19135\tNRW_DS125549\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.337Z\t710\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Catchments Cycle 3\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353439\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Coastal Catchments Cycle 3.lyr\tRobert Blacklidge - Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Catchments Cycle 3 are a polygon dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Catchments are defined as an area of land from which all surface run-off flows through a series of streams, rivers and, possibly, lakes to a particular point in the water course such as a river confluence. It is a layer of the undesignated small coastal catchments and is used alongside the Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 3 dataset to produce outputs showing all the river catchments together.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-05-04\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/4471/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_wfd_cycle_3_classifications\t2025-12-19T14:12:36.561Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19136\tNRW_DS125552\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.336Z\t617\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring the Gipsywort Weevil Datonychus arquatus on Pembrey Air Weapons Range in 2022\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353532\tData is stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database stored on Recorder6\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThe Black Bog Ant Formica picea is rare in the UK where it is associated with bogs and wet heath at a small number of sites in southern England including the heaths of Dorset and Surrey and the valley bogs in the New Forest, with a single site in Yorkshire. In Wales, it is restricted to Rhossili Down on Gower, Cors Goch Llanllwch in Carmarthenshire and Cors Llanerch in Pembrokeshire. It was first recorded on Rhossili Down in 1913 and subsequently refound in 1996. A survey in July 1997 found 49 active nests along four east to west-oriented transects across the bog and flushed wet heath habitats to the east of the central ridge, with a further 23 active nests elsewhere on the site. It was estimated thatbetween 840 and 4970 active nests were present across the site, representing the largest population in the UK. The current survey in August 2022 repeated the 1997 work, with a count of active nests along the four transects. A total of 47 active nests was recorded, two less than in 1997, although numbers along each transect varied\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T17:27:57.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19127\tNRW_DS125554\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.954Z\t71\tValid\tEditable\tWelsh Dune Fungi: Data Collation, Evaluation and Conservation Priorities\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353534\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/fun/Fungus%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Ffun%2FFungus%20Evidence%2F0%20Fungi%20Evidence%20Report%20Data%2FWelsh%20Dune%20Fungi%20Data%20Collation\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200083EEAA977243FD40886539B9E121A6E5\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B3D1F5A48%2D0066%2D4C30%2DAF53%2D7534D1DCD95A%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tA report and data spreadsheet covering analysis of historic data on dune fungi, which subsequently led to the development of a scoring system for SSSI selection.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T16:06:12.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19128\tNRW_DS125556\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.960Z\t80\tValid\tEditable\tThe effects of topography and rainfall on the distribution of Oceanic/Atlantic bryophytes in Wales – revised 2015\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353536\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FAtlantic%20Bryophytes%20vs%20Topography%20and%20Rainfall\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tA review of studies around the effects of rainfall and waterfall flows on oceanic bryophyte distribution.  Data associated with this report is held within the report only.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T16:12:24.800Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19133\tNRW_DS125561\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.246Z\t1698\tValid\tEditable\tModelled Distributions and Abundance of Cetaceans and Seabirds of Wales and Surrounding Waters\t© CNC/NRW. Published data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. NRW does not grant permission to re-use or disseminate the underlying third party data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353631\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Maximum Numbers,  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Numbers by Month,   X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\Maximum Numbers,   X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\Numbers by Decade,   X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\Numbers by Month.\tTom Stringell - Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Species - For Mammals.   Matthew Murphy - Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Species - For Seabirds\tThe data resource is a package of GIS shapefiles of modelled maximum and monthly (mean) densities of cetacean and seabird species presented in Natural Resources Wales’ Evidence Report No 646: Modelled Distributions and Abundance of Cetaceans and Seabirds in Wales and Surrounding Waters. Refer to report for further information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-10-17\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/5304/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_marine_atlas_2023\t2025-11-12T13:28:49.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n19129\tNRW_DS125562\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.940Z\t463\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLichen survey of parts of Gwydyr Forest, Glyn Lledr, Gwynedd\t© CNC/NRW    Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353632\tStored in the DMS - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FPAWS%20Glyn%20Lledr\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet\tA survey of epiphytic lichens in seven areas of the Gwydyr Forest along the Glyn Lledr valley, including recommendations for forest management.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:43:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19134\tNRW_DS125570\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.299Z\t466\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status and distribution of the Strandline Beetle Eurynebria complanata on Whiteford Burrows, Pembrey Burrows, Laugharne \u0026amp; Pendine Burrows and habitat assessment of Baglan Burrows \u0026amp; Frainslake Sands in 2022\tContains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353730\tData held on the WID in Recorded 6 - folder titled Natur Am Byth\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tFollowing surveys in 2016 which identified healthy populations were established on the three largest beaches at Pendine-Laugharne this survey of E. complanata was designed to inform future management of strandline habitats, also to assess habitat condition of two potential reintroduction sites at Frainslake and Crymlyn.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-05-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T16:14:07.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19130\tNRW_DS125572\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.084Z\t1348\tValid\tEditable\tPriority Ecological Networks\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353732\tX:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Priority Ecological Networks\tJon Rothwell - Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping\tPriority Ecological Networks (PENs) in the terrestrial environment are versions of the all-Wales habitat networks that show areas of connectivity between Protected Sites, and as such provide a framework to inform the location of action to build functional resilient ecological networks based on our most important places for biodiversity.  Typical actions would be habitat improvement, restoration, or creation, located within the boundaries of a PEN, or situated at its margins to consolidate, extend, or link different PENs. These PENs supersede the GIS layers that were previously known as ‘Level 2 networks’, and are available for:   Native woodland; Semi-natural grassland; Heathland; Bog; Fen and Sand Dune.   All these priority networks are also represented as a ‘PEN Hotspot’ map, which is a simple count of all available networks within a given 1km square for an ‘at a glance’ view across Wales.    In addition, the ‘native woodland’ and ‘semi-natural grassland’ PENs have both received updates in the form of additional networks to  accommodate our latest protected sites series and survey information and expand beyond what was originally available. Heathland, Bog and Fen have been merged from their originally separate upland and lowland versions into singular datasets and a previously unreleased ‘sand dune’ network is also now included.  A Freshwater PEN is planned.   At sea, an ecologically coherent network of marine protected areas (MPAs) has been established which, when measures are in place to bring about good condition, will form the marine Resilient Ecological Network.   Connectivity within the MPA Network is inherently good, however some of the features (species and habitats for which the sites are designated) are known to be in unfavourable condition.    The Marine Priority Ecological Network (MPEN) map shows the full extent of the MPA network, highlighting the sites where one or more features are known to be in unfavourable condition. In the highlighted areas, action is needed to deliver measures that restore features, reduce pressures to enable their recovery, or gather evidence needed to support effective management.  NRW continues to gather and assess information about the condition of habitats and species across the MPA network.  Future iterations of the MPEN will incorporate new evidence and understanding as it becomes available.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-09-03\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/4785/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_priority_ecological_networks\t2025-09-05T11:24:48.016Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19126\tNRW_DS125590\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.916Z\t606\tUnassessed\tEditable\tImportant areas for ponds in Wales\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353930\tCurrently in -   Being moved to the X Drive under River Restoration Layers\tTristan Hatton-Ellis - Lead Specialist Advisor: Freshwater Habitats and Species\tImportant Areas for Ponds in Wales created as part of the Wales River Restoration Project and derived from UK Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology UK Lakes Portal.  https://eip.ceh.ac.uk/apps/lakes/\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-06-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:07:21.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19124\tNRW_DS125591\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.583Z\t330\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Restoration Project - Protected Area Lake Catchments\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353931\tCurrently Stored at Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Tristan_Hatton-Ellis\\Lakes_Catchments\\Lakes Catchments.gdb\\Protected_Area_Lake_Catchments   # Being relocated to X:Drive under river restoration\tTristan Hatton-Ellis - Lead Specialist Advisor: Freshwater Habitats and Species\tDervied from Freshwater Habitats Trust Project (Important Areas for Ponds (IAPs) - Freshwater Habitats TrustFreshwater Habitats Trust). The Important Areas for Ponds (IAP) concept was proposed and developed by Freshwater Habitats Trust to raise awareness of geographic regions that support ponds of national or international biodiversity importance.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-06-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:06:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19125\tNRW_DS125601\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.547Z\t882\tValid\tEditable\tNutrients Review 2023\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363031\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tOn the 1st April 2021, The Water Resources (Control of Agricultural Pollution) (Wales) Regulations 2021 came into force. The regulations revoke and replace the Nitrate Pollution Prevention (Wales) Regulations 2013. The objective of the new regulations is to reduce losses of pollutants from agriculture to the environment by setting rules for certain farming practices.  With the implementation of the new Regulations, at WG’s request, NRW appointed consultants (Arup) to undertake a review of nutrients in water bodies across Wales to develop a national scale understanding of the current baseline nutrient water quality and assess potential nutrient risks. The nutrient review covers both nitrate and phosphate, which are considered to be the key nutrients contributing to poor water quality.   The review has assessed the following water bodies in Wales:  · Groundwater; · Surface water (rivers);  · Lakes; and  · Transitional and Coastal (TraC) waters.    The methodology used in the review broadly followed the previous method used for the 2017 NVZ review (NRW_DS116240) but has been adapted to incorporate the assessment of phosphate. The methodologies represent a consistent and practical approach to identification of potentially polluted waters across Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T13:30:11.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19122\tNRW_DS125602\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.447Z\t996\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Review - Risk Maps\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. Subject to a full assessment, a redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363032\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Surface Water Phosphate Risk Map.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Surface Water Nitrate Risk Map.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Groundwater Phosphate Risk Map.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Groundwater Nitrate Risk Map.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tSurface water and groundwater risk maps created by combining current and future interpolated nitrate and phosphate surface water and concentrations and the nitrate and phosphate leachate concentrations. Each component was assessed using a scoring and weighting system, and the scores were combined to generate a national risk map for nitrate at a scale of 1 km2.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:32:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19123\tNRW_DS125603\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.475Z\t364\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Review - Surface and Sub-Surface Phosphate and Nitrogen Loads\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. Subject to a full assessment, a redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363033\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Subsurface Load Nitrate.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Subsurface Load Phosphorus.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Surface Load Phosphorus.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Surface Load Nitrate.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tDataset shows the predicted surface and sub-surface phosphorus and Nitrogen loads in transitional, surface water, lake catchment and coastal catchments.  based on a land use/land cover and associated loading rates (Kg/5m grid/yr).  Estimated amount of phosphorus and nitrates for each 5m grid square that enters the soil by subsurface flow.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:07:18.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19121\tNRW_DS125604\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.311Z\t597\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrients Review - Predicted Nitrogen and Phosphorus Source Appointments\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. Subject to a full assessment, a redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363034\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Nutrient Source Apportionment.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tTotal phosphorus and nitrogen export from t catchments by surface and subsurface flow (kg/yr). Relative contribution of each source to the overall phosphorus and nitrogen load within the transitional, surface water, lake catchment and coastal catchment water bodies.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:32:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19120\tNRW_DS125605\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.283Z\t231\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrients Review - Phosphorus and Nitrogen Source Appointment Percentages\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. Subject to a full assessment, a redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363035\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Nitrogen and Phosphorus Source Apportionment by Land Use Type (%).lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tRelative contribution to total nitrogen and phosphorus load from each land use type in the transitional, surface water, coastal and  lake catchment (%)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-10T10:59:03.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19119\tNRW_DS125606\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.133Z\t422\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Review - Leachate Concentrations\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. Subject to a full assessment, a redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363036\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Nutrient Leachate Concentrations.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tThis dataset forms part of the NRW Nutrient Review of 2023. This layer shows the predicted nitrate and phosphate concentration based on total surface and subsurface nitrogen load  divided by hydrologically effective rainfall for   Surface Water Catchments (mg/l) Transitional Catchments (mg/l) Coastal Catchments (mg/l\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:17:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19118\tNRW_DS125607\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.113Z\t353\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Review - Phosphorus and Nitrate Loading From Consented Discharges\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. Subject to a full assessment, a redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363037\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Nitrogen and Phosphorus Loading from Consented Discharges.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tEstimated phosphorus and nitrate loading from consented discharges for sites with a numerical limit on phosphorus (kg/yr)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:32:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19117\tNRW_DS125608\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.092Z\t627\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Review - Groundwater and Surface Water Locations Scoped in and Out of Nitrate and Phosphate Assessments\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. Subject to a full assessment, a redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363038\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Groundwater Quality Monitoring Locations.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Surface Water Monitoring Locations.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tThis dataset shows NRW Nutrient Review 2023 surface water and groundwater monitoring sites excluded and includes from the nitrate and phosphate water quality assessment following data processing and application of data rules.  For the included data The current (2019) and predicted (2031) at each location is provided along with the method used to assess the concentrations.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:39:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19116\tNRW_DS125609\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.072Z\t563\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Review - Groundwater Interpolated Future and Current Data\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. Subject to a full assessment, a redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363039\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Groundwater Quality Interpolation.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tFuture (2031) and current (2019) predicted groundwater quality for phosphate and nitrate to understand spatial patterns in the groundwater dataset and predict phosphate and nitrate concentrations at unmonitored locations across Wales\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:14:02.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19175\tNRW_DS125610\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.969Z\t493\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Review - Lake Mean Totals and Monitoring Locations\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. Subject to a full assessment, a redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363130\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Lake Monitoring Locations.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tLake mean total nitrogen and phosphate concentrations for monitoring sites between 2014 and 2019.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:17:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19176\tNRW_DS125611\t2024-05-28T13:04:35.897Z\t305\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Review - Transitional and Coastal Monitoring Locations\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. Subject to a full assessment, a redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363131\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Transitional Monitoring Locations.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Coastal Monitoring Locations.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Coastal Water Monitoring Point Current (Winter 2019_2020) DIN WFD classification.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Transitional Water Monitoring Point Current (Winter 2019_2020) DIN WFD classification.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tThis dataset shows NRW coastal and transitional water monitoring sites where dissolved inorganic nitrogen included in assessment following data processing and application of data rules.  Current (Winter 2019/2020) dissolved organic nitrogen WFD classification for each coastal and transitional water monitoring point to understand spatial patterns in the coastal water dataset.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:17:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19162\tNRW_DS125620\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.468Z\t27\tValid\tEditable\tNational Forest for Wales\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under under terms of an open government licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363230\tx:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest for Wales.lyr\tStacey Delbridge, Team Leader, National Forest for Wales\tThis dataset shows the extent of the National Forest for Wales. The National Forest for Wales includes a variety of diverse woodlands with a range of ages, sizes, settings and benefits. All National Forest sites are recognised as good quality, well managed woodlands. Some excel in providing access or engagement with their community, some are recognised for the sustainable way they are managed, others focus on the benefits they have for nature. All play their part in helping address the nature and climate emergencies and providing a long-lasting woodland resource for Wales.  This dataset is periodically updated as more forest gets incorporated into the national forest through the status scheme assessment process. Data relating to the Welsh Government Woodland Estates sites put forward for National Forest for Wales Status will be gathered using existing data sources. Other land included from maps supplied by applicants is digitised from aerial photography and OS basemaps and verified using land registry documents. All boundaries and areas of sites are shown only for indicative purposes, are subject to change at any time, and should not be taken as legally binding or definitive in any way.  The National Forest for Wales team are responsible for enabling woodland site owners to become part of the National Forest for Wales network. The extent of Prospective National Forest for Wales sites will be mapped as polygon data, metadata will be gathered with relevant site details in order to inform whether they meet the outcomes necessary.     The extent of ownership will be determined through discussion with the site owner and when a site comes into the National Forest for Wales scheme this will be verified using their Land registry documents.    Contacts and events will also be logged as point data so that we have a record of the extent of engagement across Wales about the National Forest including relevant funding streams that the team can signpost to.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-07-09-2025-08-01\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/6676/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_national_forest\t2025-08-22T13:29:02.006Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n19177\tNRW_DS125626\t2024-05-28T13:04:35.898Z\t782\tValid\tEditable\tEnvironmental Permits for disposal of waste sheep dip to land\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363236\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Waste\\Environmental Permits for disposal of waste sheep dip to land.lyr\tDave Jones - Senior Specialist Advisor Geoscience\tThis dataset provide details of permit details as required under the Environmental Permit Regulations issued for the disposal of waste sheep dip to land. The data comes from Natural Resources Wales Public Register. The published datasets only includes permits with an \u0027Effective\u0027 status; revoked, expired or surrendered permits are not included.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/5250/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_sheep_dip_permit\t2026-02-23T11:47:14.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19159\tNRW_DS125627\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.526Z\t393\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone Management Zones\t© CNC/NRW Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363237\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\\Skomer MCZ Management Zones\tKate Lock, Specialist (Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer) - kate.lock@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk  Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer - mark.burton@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThis dataset contains multiple layers used to recreate a map of Skomer Marine Conservation Zones with byelaws and codes of conduct zones for: vessel speeds, indicative seasonal breeding zones for seals and seabirds, sensitive areas for dolphins and porpoises, no anchoring areas and anchoring areas.  This dataset contains the following layers:  Skomer Inshore Protection Zone Skomer Nearshore Protection Zone Skomer Seasonal Breeding Zones Seals Skomer Seasonal Breeding Zones Seabirds Skomer No Anchoring Areas Skomer Anchoring Areas Skomer Sensitive Areas for Dolphins and Porpoises\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-07-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:50:44.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19168\tNRW_DS125630\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.800Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPembrokeshire Sea Cliff Vegetation Survey 1974-1975 - (paper data records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363330\tAdd in here the off-site storage location/box number\t\tSea cliff plant community data, based on Dr A.J.C Malloch (Lancaster University) survey methodology.  Pembrokeshire coast was split into sections and plan communities described for each section.  Work carried by NCC.  Likely to be a small portion of a larger survey (the rest of the survey data is currently unaccounted for).  Areas of the Pembrokeshire coast split into Units 304-358, 361-400, 401-409.  May be related to NCC report (Malloch, 1971)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19173\tNRW_DS125637\t2024-05-28T13:04:11.020Z\t758\tValid\tEditable\tAssessing Welsh Aquaculture Activities\tData may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363337\tBiotopes (DMS file: ADVI-1932381820-54280):    https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/advice/mar/fish/Projects/AWAA%20Project/Task%2005%20-%20Interactions%20spreadsheet/FINAL_interactions_spreadsheet_biotopes_16062023.xlsx?d\u003dw20239fd02510433d8693a44239bae8c8\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1    Species (DMS file: ADVI-1932381820-54281):    https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/advice/mar/fish/Projects/AWAA%20Project/Task%2005%20-%20Interactions%20spreadsheet/FINAL_interactions_spreadsheet_species_16062023.xlsx?d\u003dwea1e3c443ae2413297114697497538af\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\tColin Charman - Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries\tThe two AWAA Interactions Spreadsheets in Excel present the potential sensitivity of biotopes (components of habitats) and species (designated within MPAs, SSSIs or included within the Environment (Wales) Act 2016 Section 7 list) to the pressures associated with different aquaculture activities. The Biotopes Interactions Spreadsheet contains the information presented in the AWAA Mapping Tool.   The AWAA Mapping Tool displays the sensitivity of marine biotopes in Welsh Waters to the potential pressures associated with 11 different aquaculture activities. The Tool allows users such as developers, regulators and advisors to view the potential sensitivity of biotopes around Wales or within the vicinity of a proposed aquaculture development to the potential pressures associated with that activity.   Biotope locations have been sourced from the Marine Recorder (2020) and Intertidal Phase 1 Habitat Survey (2022). The sensitivities of the biotopes are sourced from the Marine Life Information Network (MarLIN) as part of the Marine Evidence base Sensitivity Assessment (MarESA) (Tyler-Walers et al., 2022).  Full details of data sources and acknowledgements can be found on the ‘Attributions’ button.   The data viewer was created by ABPmer Ltd for Natural Resources Wales.   The AWAA Project is supported by the EU through Welsh Ministers and is fully funded by the European Maritime and Fisheries Fund  Story Map can be found here: https://smnr-nrw.hub.arcgis.com/apps/73894fae28f64ed0b9b7344dcc667493/explore\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-11-21\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/5767\t2025-11-12T13:27:35.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n19169\tNRW_DS125640\t2024-05-28T13:04:11.020Z\t273\tValid\tEditable\tFindings of drop down video and stills imagery from an area of drumlins off Northwest Anglesey, July 2019\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensur\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363430\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\North Anglesey Drumlin Survey 2019\tLaura Grant, Specialist Advisor – Marine Ecology\tThis data contains the raw products of a seabed survey of an area of drumlins off north west Anglesey.  The images and video was taken by a Natural Resources Wales owned drop down camera system to provide a better understanding of the seabed structure and biological communities at the survey sites and to investigate the presence of non-native species. The data contains Video (.asf) (and still images (.JPG) The data was collected on the 11th July 2019. The a copy of the MR snapshot produced after data analysis by the contractor is also supplied here.  The analysis of the video and stills imagery followed guidelines provided by NRW, Marine EcoSol’s in-house standard operating procedures, and JNCC/NMBAQC recommendations. The video camera showed a swath of seabed approximately 0.3m wide, while still images showed an area estimated as about 0.13m2. All images were reviewed using appropriate high-quality imaging equipment capable of playing and displaying full HD video at 1920p x 1080p resolution.     The video and set of stills from each tow were reviewed prior to detailed assessment and recording, and the boundaries between biotopes or biotope complexes noted by recording the time and positional coordinates at which major changes in seabed and habitat type occurred. The species and substratum in each habitat ‘sample’ (tow section) was then recorded, and biotopes assigned.  Discrete habitat tow sections or ‘samples’ as defined above were determined using  a combination of video and stills images. The lower quality video imagery was then linked to the ‘sample’ in terms of data recording using the time stamp on the video playback. Higher quality stills images were used to identify and quantify substrates and taxa, with abundances pooled to assess the abundance of the taxa over each sample.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-07-03\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:31:01.048Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n19174\tNRW_DS125641\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.969Z\t317\tValid\tEditable\tEmissions of radioactive substances from non-nuclear sites - OSPAR Report Data\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363431\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/reporting/sr/Industrial%20Emissions/Non-nuclear%20Radioactive%20Substances\tToby Griffiths, Radioactivity and Industry Policy  Karl Lombardi, Radioactivity and Industry Policy\tHolders of an RSR (Radioactive Substances Regulation) open source permit, including disposal to the environment, are required to report annually to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) emissions of certain non-nuclear radioactive substances.   This emissions data is collated as part of a UK dataset and is reported to the OSPAR Commission. OSPAR is the mechanism by which 15 Governments and the EU cooperate to protect the marine environment of the North-East Atlantic.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-10-24\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/5648\t2025-12-19T10:22:48.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19167\tNRW_DS125642\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.720Z\t747\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDee Conservancy, Dee Estuary (Navigation)\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363432\tX:\\Resource Management\\Dee Conservancy\tInternal Data Custodian: Dawn Beech, Senior Dee Conservancy Advisor, Marine Management and Advice Team, Marine Ops.  Dawn.beech@cyfoethnaturiolcymr.gov.uk Tel: 077468 742713\tMaritime map data relating to the Dee Conservancy.  Showing Natural Resources Wales’ jurisdiction as the Statutory Harbour and Local Lighthouse Authority for the Dee Conservancy, including location and names of aids to navigation (buoys and markers) managed by Natural Resources Wales.  The data also shows the extent of the Port of Mostyn Harbour and the Mostyn Operational Area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-07-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T12:03:51.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19161\tNRW_DS125685\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.595Z\t1339\tValid\tEditable\tCardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipients responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363835\tX:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Barry and Cardiff Site Boundaries.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Barry Hedgerow Survey Results.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Barry Hedgerows.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Cardiff and Caerphilly Hedherows.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Cardiff and Caerphilly Survey Results.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Invasive Species Target Notes.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Photo Points.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Veteran Trees.lyr\tLiz Hancocks - Senior Officer, People and Places\tIn order to further an understanding of and improve connectivity in the landscape Natural Resources Wales commissioned a survey of the hedgerow network around Cardiff in two areas to the North and South West of Cardiff. A total of 696 hedgerows were surveyed in November and December 2022 using a bespoke survey pro forma based primarily on the DEFRA hedgerow survey handbook.  This dataset compromises of a range of maps that are based on analysis of aerial imagery using AI.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-10-04-2024-08-01\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6860/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_cardiff_hedgerows\t2025-12-19T10:41:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19155\tNRW_DS125697\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.468Z\t1074\tValid\tEditable\tSlope for Landspreading\tContains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and database right. All rights reserved. Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from © Environment Agency.  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363937\tX:\\Basemapping\\Height\\Slope for landspreading.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): To be determined.   Metadata Point of Contact: Paul Challender, Lead Specialist Industry \u0026amp; Waste Regulation\tThe Slope to Landspreading layer is a dataset derived from height data. It provides a colourised visual representation of pertinent slope angles for the regulation of landspreading activities.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-11-24\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/5844\t2025-03-07T15:43:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19160\tNRW_DS125698\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.409Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tStanner Rocks National Nature Reserve meteorological data (paper data records)\t© CNC/NRW Subject to a full assessment, data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363938\tPaper records.  Note 16 November 2023 - Donna has kept these paper records in the comms room in Bangor for now just in case she finds more to add to the box. The paper data didn’t come from archives so there is no old number. She will give it a few months before transferring, when she does she\u0027ll get a number from Jill and pass it on.\tDylan Lloyd, Lead Specialist Advisor, Monitoring Strategy and Planning Donna Muirhead, Specialist Advisor, Data Services\tHourly and daily meteorological data from Stanner Rocks National Nature Reserve (NNR) was recorded from 1976 to 1979. It included solar, temperate (c), rainfall (mms) and hours of sunshine.   The data was captured in association with work of the Nature Conservancy Council.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1979-10-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:43:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19165\tNRW_DS125699\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.650Z\t1060\tValid\tEditable\tRed Squirrel Focal Sites Wales\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363939\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Squirrel Buffer Zone April 2023.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Squirrel Buffer Zone December 2010.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Squirrel Focal Site April 2023.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Squirrel Focal Site December 2010.lyr\tChris Tucker - Specialist Advisor: Technical Forestry (Resilience)\tThis spatial dataset comprises the boundaries of Red Squirrel Focal sites in Wales. This includes the core areas and the buffers.    There are three red squirrel focal sites in Wales identified by the Wales Squirrel Forum (WSF) as part of the Wales Red Squirrel Conservation Plan 2009.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-07-03\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:04.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19166\tNRW_DS125704\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.656Z\t670\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLichen Survey of Baron Hill Park SSSI\t© CNC/NRW   Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373034\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FBaron%20Hill%20Park\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet\tA survey of epiphytic lichens on parkland trees at Baron Hill Park SSSI\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:17:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19170\tNRW_DS125707\t2024-05-28T13:04:11.020Z\t18\tValid\tEditable\tCompliance Assessment of Welsh River Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) Against Water Quality Targets Associated Data (2017-2019)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373037\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/fe/Internal%20Research/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2Ffe%2FInternal%20Research%2FCondition%20assessments%2FSAC%20River%20Water%20Quality%20Compliance%202022\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120003484E0523A301C478CE56BB727C2A9A2\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BB2119901%2DA148%2D4F26%2D93EE%2D937B564A9233%7D\tHilary Foster - Specialist Advisor: Freshwater Habitats and Species\tThese data identify the compliance of Welsh river Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) regarding targets for seven water quality attributes: Dissolved Oxygen, Biochemical Oxygen Demand, Total Ammonia, Unionised Ammonia, Trophic Diatom Index, pH and Acid Neutralising Capacity. The targets and methods of assessment are set at a UK level by the Joint Nature Conservation Committee as published in the Common Standards Guidance for Rivers 2016, and form part of the conservation objectives for the riverine SAC features.  There are nine SACs in Wales with riverine features (known in their respective Core Management Plans as): • River Dee and Bala Lake  • Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn  • Coedydd Derw a Safleoedd Ystlumod Meirion (Afon Glaslyn)  • Afon Eden - Cors Goch Trawsfynydd • Afon Teifi  • Afonydd Cleddau  • Afon Tywi  • River Usk  • River Wye   For the purposes of water quality reporting, Natural Resources Wales (NRW) divides rivers into units called water bodies. A total of 119 (of a possible 127) water bodies, each with a single sampling point, were assessed for one or more attribute.   Water quality data collected by NRW’s routine surveillance monitoring programme for a three-year period (1st of January 2017 – 31st December 2019) were extracted from the NRW water quality database (KiWQM) to assess compliance with targets.    Data were not available for all attributes for all water bodies due to the risk-based design of NRW’s monitoring programmes. The majority of water bodies were assessed for more than one attribute. All data were subject to robust quality assurance processes. The data consists of two products: 1. An Excel spreadsheet summarising results and 2. GIS layers consisting of the WFD catchment area water bodies, clipped to the Welsh border and excluding water bodies not overlapping with river SAC boundaries.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-01-11\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/5941/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_sac_rivers_project\t2025-03-05T15:46:56.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n19163\tNRW_DS125721\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.609Z\t3810\tValid\tEditable\tNational Receptor Dataset (NRD) 2023 and 2025\t© Natural Resources Wales. All rights reserved. © Ordnance Survey AC0000849444 © Middlesex University, Flood Hazard Research Centre. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking re-use also require a licence from Ordnance Survey for the derived OS data and from Middlesex University for the derived Multi-Coloured Manual (MCM) data. NRW hold an MCM code licence that includes re-use by NRW as well as all Welsh Local Authorities and Welsh Government. Other seeking re-use must purchase a licence from Middlesex University.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373231\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Risk Management National Receptors Database 2023 (NRD 2023).lyr X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Risk Management National Receptors Database 2025 (NRD 2025).lyr \u0026amp; X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Risk Management National Receptors Database 2025 from OSMM.lyr\tData Custodian: Dave Tarrant, Lead Specialist Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group\tThe National Receptor Dataset (NRD) is a collection of risk receptors, typically residential and commercial property, intended for use in flood and coastal erosion risk management. It is a spatial dataset produced from Ordnance Survey (OS) AddressBase® Premium (ABP) and OS MasterMap® (MM) data with information on property type, floor area and Flood Hazard Research Centre’s Multi-Coloured Manual (MCM) codes attributed.    The versions of the NRD described here for the NRD 2023 \u0026amp; 2025 created as part of the Flood Risk Assessment Wales Data Management (FRAW DM) process. These datasets of NRD builds on the same methodology as NRD 2018, with a refresh of input datasets.    NRD 2023 \u0026amp; 2025 comprises of two layers, they have been created from the OS AddressBase Premium dataset and from OSMM, which is created from OS MasterMap.  Both datasets should be viewed together, to give a complete view of the NRD datasets.      List of improvements from NRD 2018 to NRD 2023 \u0026amp; 2025 include:  1. Main input datasets have been updated, including; OS ABP, OS MM, NRW Flood Alert and Warning Areas, NRW Flood Warning Uptake, Defended Areas.  2. Over 52,000 Static Caravans are now included in the OS MM datasets, therefore these have now been included in the NRD 2023 \u0026amp; 2025 from OSMM Layer.  3. The building footprint threshold within the NRD 2023 \u0026amp; 2025 from OSMM layer has been increased from 30m² to 60m².  This is to remove unwanted features like garages being classified as “Residential” dwelling.  4. Ordnance Survey have significantly improved their classification of properties across Wales.  For example, end of terrace houses in some location were called “semi-detached” previously, these have now been correctly assigned as “end of terrace” in NRD 2023 \u0026amp; 2025.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-09-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T10:20:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19172\tNRW_DS125722\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.910Z\t1154\tValid\tEditable\tKieco Marine Fish - Geospatial\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373232\tData is held in KiEco stored on the WISKI database - an FME process creates a GIS layer which are stored here:  X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Marine Fish Individually Measured.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Marine Fish Site Details.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Marine Fish Total Counts.lyr\tPaul Hyatt, Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer), Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting\tMarine fish are used as an indicator of stock status, water and habitat quality. This dataset is made up of the species count and length values from the fish surveys conducted across Wales marine environment by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of fish in their natural environment for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  This metadata was created for the geospatial data for KiEco Marine Fish - for species data that is downloadable from the NBN please see NRW_DS125741\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-12-01-2026-02-24\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6211/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_marine_fish_surveys\t2026-02-25T10:29:06.242Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19153\tNRW_DS125723\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.109Z\t669\tValid\tEditable\tImportant Curlew Areas (ICAs) in Wales Map\t© CNC/NRW, © OS, © RSPB, © Denbighshire County Council, © Brecon Beacons National Park,  © Game \u0026amp; Wildlife Conservation Trust, © Elan Valley Trust, © Welsh Ornithological Society. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373233\tY:\\Knowledge Strategy Planning\\Natural Resource Planning\\Biodiversity Fisheries Geodiversity\\Jon Rothwell\\Curlew 2021\\Final ICA.GDB\tData Custodian: Bethan Beech, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Species Recovery, Biodiversity and Ecosystem Resilience Group\tThe 12 Important Curlew Area (ICA) boundaries have been drawn up by the members of Gylfinir Cymru / Curlew Wales. Glyfinir Cymru / Curlew Wales is a joint working group between organisations that are committed to Curlew recovery in Wales, including representatives from the government, conservation, farming, and game management sectors.    Some of the original hand drawn maps were passed to British Trust for Ornithology (BTO) to draw, then passed to NRW to finalise. The others were sent to NRW to check. If they were correct they were uploaded to the Gylfinir Cymru / Curlew Wales web site. If they were incorrect, they were sent back to the originator.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Curlew Wales\t2024-02-14\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/5944/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_ica_wales\t2025-02-04T10:10:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19164\tNRW_DS125726\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.626Z\t420\tValid\tEditable\tEryri snowbed bryophyte monitoring 2023\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373236\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FEryri%20Snowbed%20Bryophytes%202023\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet : Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tA survey of snowbed bryophytes in two areas of Eryri along with setting up of monitoring plots for those species.  The two sites were Ffynnon Llyffant on Carnedd Llewelyn in the Carneddau and Pant Ifan on Glyder Fawr in the Glyderau. These sites were selected for survey as they were found by previous surveys to be the most important sites for snowbed bryophytes in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-11-16\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-03T09:31:58.725Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19158\tNRW_DS125727\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.465Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBontddu Hydropower bryophyte monitoring 2023\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373237\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2FBryophyte%20Hydropower%20Monitoring\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet\tMonitoring of bryophytes within plots set up before the operation of the Bontddu Hydropower commenced 10 years ago and following an initial monitoring round 3 years after operation commenced\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19171\tNRW_DS125728\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.962Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAlgae Raw Data Sample Forms - North Wales (paper data records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373238\tPaper data records\tJenny Owen, Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer), Analysis and Reporting\tThese paper data records are analysis forms of ad-hoc incident algal samples. They were taken in the area of North Wales covered by Environment Agency Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:56:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19156\tNRW_DS125730\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.444Z\t979\tValid\tEditable\tNational Peatland Action Programme (NPAP) Funded Peatland Restoration Activities 2020-2023 – GIS Dataset\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373330\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\NPAP Erosion Control 2020 24.lyr X:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\NPAP Grazing 2020 24.lyr X:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\NPAP Hydrological Management 2020 24.lyr X:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\NPAP Total Activity Footprint 2020 24.lyr X:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\NPAP Tree Management 2020 24.lyr X:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\NPAP Vegetation Management 2020 24.lyr\tAlice Whittle - NPAPA Data and GIS Officer\tThe total activities footprint for NPAP funded restoration work from programme inception in 2020 to 2023.   This layer covers both NPAP direct delivery restoration work and restoration delivered by partners across several NPAP grant streams: Strategically Allocated Funds (SAF), Section 16 Land Agreements and Competitive Grants.  The activities footprint is formatted as a shapefile layer, comprised of polygons representing restoration activities across Wales  Restoration Activity Footprint  This dataset shows the total area of peatland restoration across Wales up to present day. The data is presented as a polygon shapefile, with the unit of measurement being hectares.  Restoration Activities  This dataset shows the total area, measured in hectares, of peatland restoration that carried out across Wales up to the present date. The data is presented as a polygon shapefile that captures restoration activities. Restoration activities have been divided into five broad groups: Hydrological Management, Erosion Control, Tree Management, Vegetation Management and Grazing.  Hydrological Management aims to reverse the impact of harmful activities that have resulted in adverse changes to hydrological regimes.  Erosion Control aims to reduce the effect of erosional features such as haggs and gullies. These features expose areas of bare peat which are prone to further degradation if left uncontrolled.  Tree Management aims to reduce the impact of trees on peatland. Most Tree Management techniques for peatland restoration involve conifer plantation, there are also activities addressing the presence of broadleaf trees.   Vegetation Management aims to control the dominance of small numbers of abundant species (e.g. Molinia, Calluna) to ensure the recovery and persistence of mire vegetation.  Grazing activities aim to restore and manage peatlands through modifying grazing regimes.  The data is broken down into polygons with columns detailing the broad activity types, sites, delivery organisation, start and end dates of restoration activity, and the financial year it occurred in.  Further information on the structure of the dataset can be found in the\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-03-01-2024-08-22\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6258/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_npap_resto_activity\t2025-10-10T07:51:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19152\tNRW_DS125733\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.127Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe current status of the spiders Callilepis nocturna, Dipoena erythropus, Erigone dentigera, Gongylidiellum murcidum, Lathys stigmatisata, Maro lepidus and Marpissa radiata on selected Welsh SSSIs in 2021 and 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373333\t\tMichael Howe\tMike Howe To supply\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19154\tNRW_DS125734\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.380Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status and distribution of Broom-tip Chesias rufata at Morfa Harlech and Harlech Forest in 2023\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373334\t\tMike Howe\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19151\tNRW_DS125735\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.115Z\t279\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTimber Transport Forum Draft Road Categorisation\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. Subject to a full assessment, a redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373335\tX:\\Transport\\Timber Transport Forum\\Timber Transport Forum All Roads.lyr\tRob Marsh - Senior Officer, Forest Planning Mid\tShapefile (lines) derived from OS Open Roads dataset, for the purpose of categorising the status of roads for timber haulage. The Timber Transport Forum is developing this dataset with a view to improving communication and consultation around timber haulage. The present dataset is draft / in development, and road categorisation is currently in place for small areas of Wales only.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-02-29\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19149\tNRW_DS125736\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.097Z\t104\tValid\tEditable\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of selected areas of the Dee Estuary - 2022\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373336\tY:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Coastal Habitats\\Dee NVC 2022\\Dee_saltmarsh_NVC2022.gdb\tData Custodian: Heather Lewis, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team, EPP\tThe report and GIS dataset present the results of a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey carried out in 2022 on selected areas of saltmarsh habitat and transitional vegetation in the Dee Estuary Special Area of Conservation (SAC).     Seven distinct areas of saltmarsh were assessed, from the marshes between accreting dunes at Gronant, southwards through Point of Ayr, Greenfield Flushing Lagoon, Bagillt Marshes, Flint Marshes, and Connah’s Quay.  Marshes to the most southerly areas included in the survey referred to as the Dee River Marshes which extend southwards to the Jubilee Lift Bridge at Queensferry.  A field based judgement was carried out on community assignment supported by quadrat samples and target notes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-11-27\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T14:23:26.962Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19148\tNRW_DS125740\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.112Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe condition of the aquatic element of the grazing levels invertebrate assemblages on Nash \u0026amp; Goldcliff SSSI and Newport Wetlands SSSI on the Gwent Levels in 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373430\tData to be loaded to the WID on recorder6\tMike Howe\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:09.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19157\tNRW_DS125741\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.465Z\t409\tValid\tEditable\tKieco Marine Fish\t© CNC/NRW.NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373431\tData Held in KiECO database and and extract published to the NBNAtlas.\tPaul Hyatt, Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer), Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting\tMarine fish are used as an indicator of stock status, water and habitat quality. This dataset is made up of the species count and length values from the fish surveys conducted across Wales marine environment by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of fish in their natural environment for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Governance Licence and available for public use, see Limitation on Public Access and Use Constraints\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-12-01-2024-06-11\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr3163\t2026-01-19T16:11:18.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19150\tNRW_DS125742\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.026Z\t810\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Diatoms - Geospatial\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373432\tX:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Diatom Sample Metrics.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Diatom Site Details.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Diatom Taxa.lyr\tJennifer Owen Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer) Analysis and Reporting\tFreshwater diatoms (phytobenthos) are free-living microscopic plants that form a biofilm on submerged substrates such as rock/stone or large plants. They are used as an indicator of water quality because their silica shells are easy to identify under the microscope.   This dataset is made up of the relative abundance of different species within the sample from freshwater diatom surveys conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of river water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  A version of this data is available in the Darwin Core format as a CSV - Please see metadata here - NRW_DS123303\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-24\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6009/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_freshwater_diatom_survey\t2026-02-25T10:18:34.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19147\tNRW_DS125743\t2024-05-28T13:04:09.754Z\t958\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Fish Monitoring Data - Geospatial\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373433\tX/Monitoring/Kieco\tPaul Hyatt Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer), Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting\tFreshwater fish are used as an indicator of stock status, water and habitat quality, artificial obstacles to migration and river flow requirements due to specific life-cycle requirements.   This dataset is made up of the species count and length values from the freshwater river fish surveys conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of fish in their natural environment for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  For the non-geospatial delimited data format please see NRW_DS125358 \"KiEco Freshwater Ecology: Fish Monitoring Data\".\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-24\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6244/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_freshwater_fish_surveys\t2026-02-25T10:24:25.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19146\tNRW_DS161271\t2024-05-28T13:04:09.317Z\t532\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Constraints for Bog Restoration\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323731\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Constraints for Bog Restoration.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report. What the map shows: This map shows the number of constraints to bog restoration for natural flood management. Constraints to hard engineering solutions are not considered.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:21:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19276\tNRW_DS161272\t2024-06-28T08:33:41.858Z\t644\tValid\tEditable\tDrinking Water Protected Area\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323732\tx:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Groundwater Drinking Water Protected Areas.lyr x:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Drinking Water Protected Areas Lakes.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Drinking Water Protected Areas River Catchments.lyr\tDave Jones, Suzanne Lawn\tAny water bodies in Wales used to provide drinking water are designated as Drinking Water Protected Areas (DrWPAs). The designation aims to protect the water and prevent any deterioration in quality that could increase the required treatment of water to meet Drinking Water Standards.   Drinking Water Protected Areas need to be identified under the Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017.   This data shows the designated Drinking Water Protected Areas in Wales and is split into two separate layers for Surface Water Drinking Water Protected Areas and one for Groundwater Drinking Water Protected Areas. This aims to protect from over-abstraction and prevent deterioration in water quality that could increase the treatment of drinking water.   Within these Drinking Water Protected Areas, we work with water companies to identify raw water sources that are \u0027at risk\u0027 of deterioration, which would result in the need for additional treatment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-07-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6859/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_dwpa\t2025-11-10T16:47:12.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19283\tNRW_DS161273\t2024-07-02T08:35:13.664Z\t963\tValid\tEditable\t3.0 Otter TEST Data Discovery *** This is not a real dataset *** FULL FIELD EXAMPLE\tUSE STATEMENT TEST: © CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323733\tINTERNAL LOCATION TEST:   Archived as Project 360, Media #1590.   GIS layer:  X:\\Layer_Files\\Biology\\Species\\Example_MEDIN_Compliant_Full_Field.lyr.\tData Custodians \u0026amp; SME:  John Smith, Otter specialist.   Geospatial Custodian: Joe Bloggs, Geospatial Officer.\tTHIS IS NOT REAL DATA.      • This is NRW\u0027s test dataset to ensure staff can refer back to a fully valid record to help themselves solve validation errors • It is also to ensure all elements are displaying and exporting to multiple XML schemas.  The Metadata is written to be compliant with the NRW, MEDIN and GEMINI Standard.   • It may temporarily be in public view when checking view formats for the public but will be removed later.  We have not addressed the issue with using mean sea level as a vertical datum.  It is recommneded that to be inline with MEDIN statdanrt Vertical min and max extents are converted to Ordnance Datum Newlyn Vertical Datum - we are waiting on encoding advice from MEDIN\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2020-12-16-2023-02-03-2024-06-30\tcreation-publication-revision\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/414/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_MAIN_RIVERS\t2026-02-24T12:19:55.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19284\tNRW_DS161274\t2024-07-05T07:56:03.299Z\t1207\tValid\tEditable\tTranquillity and Place Wales Sound Environment Part 2 2024\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444 and Derived in Part from BGS Digital Data under Licence Number 2013/062. British Geological Society. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323734\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquillity and Place\\Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Sound Environment Indicator T4-2-A Low Flying Military Aircraft.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquillity and Place\\Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Sound Environment Road \u0026amp; Rail.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquillity and Place\\Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Sound Environment Combined.lyr\tJill Bullen - Lead Specialist Advisor: Landscape\tThe nationally consistent terrestrial Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place resource identifies the strategic and local resource in remote, rural, peri-urban and urban areas for use as an evidence base to inform policy intent, practice and provision for well-being benefits. There are 6 themes.   Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Sound Environment Part II delivers Theme 4, where natural sounds may be expected to be more prominent than noise and are appropriate to context building upon Part I to incorporate Welsh Government Strategic Noise Mapping for Wales 2022 Road and Rail.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-05-22-2025-04-04\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/sign-in/?next\u003d/documents/6760\t2025-04-07T10:04:27.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19317\tNRW_DS161276\t2024-07-15T09:05:20.167Z\t561\tValid\tEditable\tDigital grain sizing of Welsh shingle beaches\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323736\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mcps/Evidence%20%20External%20Research/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2fteams%2fevidence%2fMarine-Evidence%2fmcps%2fEvidence++External+Research%2fShingleSourcesData\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000686B1A13B2902E43B637F5C8EB33B608\u0026amp;View\u003d%7b4CA932F0-0F0A-47A2-B5BF-FD4C292536A3%7d\u0026amp;xsdata\%3d\u0026amp;sdata\u003dOWhpVEVIbWZjM2lWNCtSRlA0OFZjTDNadFFicHVtN0NXVEovQUU3cVU0WT0%3d\tIain Fairley, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team, NRW.\tThis dataset consists of particle size analysis of five shingle beaches around the coast of Wales. The five beaches are (from north to south): Pensarn; Morfa Dinlle; Borth; Amroth; Leys Beach (Aberthaw).   Particle size information was collected using digital grain size analysis. Both an excel file containing the particle size analysis, images of grain size histograms and the raw digital data are provided.  The data was collected to enable assessment of suitable quarry sources of shingle for beach renourishment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-04-11\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T14:59:19.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19318\tNRW_DS161277\t2024-07-15T12:55:52.058Z\t674\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Lake Macroinvertebrates - Geospatial\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323737\tData Held in Wiski-KiEco - a FME Report has been created to export this data to the GIS.  X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Lake Macro-invertebrates Sample Metric.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Lake Macro-invertebrates Site Detail.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Lake Macro-invertebrates Taxa.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jennifer Owen, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, NW Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tFreshwater macroinvertebrates are used as an indicator of water quality and Lake flow requirements due to the relative immobility and abundance.   This dataset contains site, sample, metric and taxa information from freshwater Lake macroinvertebrate surveys carried out  across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales since 1997 and onwards. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of Lake water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business needs.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-24\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6713/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_lake_macroinvertebrates\t2026-02-25T10:26:56.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19320\tNRW_DS161278\t2024-07-15T14:26:12.215Z\t1201\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: River Macroinvertebrates - Geospatial\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323738\tData Held in KiEco - a FME Report has been created to export this data to the GIS.   X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macro-invertebrates Taxa.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macro-invertebrates Site Detail.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macro-invertebrates Sample Metric.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Jennifer Owen Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer) Analysis and Reporting\tFreshwater macroinvertebrates are used as an indicator of water quality and river flow requirements due to the relative immobility and abundance.   This dataset contains site, sample, metric and taxa information from freshwater river macroinvertebrate surveys carried out  across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales since 1978 and onwards. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of river water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  A version of this data is available in a Darwin Core format, please see NRW_DS122460 for more information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-24\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6703/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_river_macroinvertebrates\t2026-02-25T10:37:39.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19331\tNRW_DS161279\t2024-07-16T11:39:04.105Z\t483\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: River Macrophytes - Geospatial\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323739\tData Held in KiEco - a FME Report has been created to export this data to the GIS.   X:drive locations   X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macrophyte Sample Metric.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macrophyte Site Detail.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macrophyte Taxa.lyr\tJennifer Owen - Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer), Analysis and Reporting\tMacrophytes (water plants visible to the naked eye) can indicate the impact of pressures such as increased nutrients, flow pressures, channel alterations etc. The species present and their abundance give an indication as to the health of a river.   This dataset is made up of  site information, sample metrics and taxa details for each survey conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales since 1993. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of river water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-24\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6715/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_river_macrophyte\t2026-02-25T10:39:01.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19396\tNRW_DS161281\t2024-08-01T08:13:34.915Z\t19\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecies License Mitigation Sites 1994\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323831\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\tIwan G Hughes Species Permitting Team Leader\tThe database lists the spatial locations of protected species mitigation sites throughout Wales. These sites were proposed by applicants for licences to take or disturb protected species or damage or destroy their breeding sites , resting places or places of shelter for the purposes of demonstrating their applications would not be detrimental to the maintenance of the favourable conservation status (FCS) of the applicable local population of protected species in the long term. The majority of protected species mitigation sites have been for European protected species, and consequently the majority of the sites are to facilitate the conservation of bats, hazel dormouse and great crested newt The information has been collated by (a) The scanning of plans illustrating the location of mitigations as part of licence applications to the Welsh Government (WG), Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Natural Resources Wales (NRW); and (b) The submission of Geographical Information System (GIS) shape files as a condition of licences issued by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under the Conservation of Habitats and Species Regulations 2017 (as amended) As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\t\t2024-08-01\tcreation\tnull\t\t2024-11-19T10:16:27.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19426\tNRW_DS161282\t2024-08-12T11:35:12.357Z\t834\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts Cycle 3\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD River Basin Districts Cycle3.lyr\tInternal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts (RBD) is a spatial dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027…the area of land and sea, made up of one or more neighbouring river basins together with their associated groundwaters and coastal waters…\u0027. River Basin Districts have been delineated by using River Catchments as \"building blocks\" that have been aggregated together within a GIS, ensuring that WFD rivers do not intersect boundaries. Coastal and transitional waterbodies are also merged and assigned to a river basin district. River Catchments were delineated through use of flow data and a digital terrain model run through a hydrological model.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4465/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_river_basin_districts_c3\t2024-08-20T14:53:49.447Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19430\tNRW_DS161283\t2024-08-13T14:34:56.920Z\t39\tInvalid\tEditable\tWales Integrated Natural Beauty Mapping 2024 – GIS dataset\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323833\tWaiting to be loaded on X:Drive (13/08/2024)\tAlex Hardie-Brown\tThe Wales Integrated Natural Beauty Mapping is part of the Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Designated Landscape Programme, which receives funding from the Welsh Government. This project was initiated to create a consistent applicable methodology and evidence base for informing landscape interventions across Wales. As the Welsh government\u0027s designating authority for landscape and statutory advisor on landscape matters, NRW has developed this mapping tool using the recognized natural beauty criteria outlined in NRW’s Procedural Guidance 2022.  The mapping tool aims: -To map natural beauty across Wales -To integrate landscape analysis with nature recovery, resilience to climate change and well-being -To identify areas to focus detailed analysis for future landscape interventions\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-10-10T12:59:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19445\tNRW_DS161284\t2024-08-19T09:23:40.090Z\t53\tInvalid\tEditable\tUnderstanding the likely scale of deterioration of Marine Protected Area features due to coastal squeeze: Part 2\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323834\tY:\\Marine\\Coastal Squeeze geospatial files awaiting to be assessed and moved to X:Drive Spreadsheets found in DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/mar/MNN22-25/CSMPA/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fadvice%2Fmar%2FMNN22%2D25%2FCSMPA%2FFinal%20Data\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BFEAFDBF3%2DFEA3%2D4A4D%2DA512%2DE1FB3CF2337E%7D\tNicola Rimington - Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine and Coastal Physical Process and Coastal Management\tTo complete the coastal squeeze assessments a series of geospatial layers and accompanying spreadsheets were created as part of a project to understand the likely scale of deterioration of Marine Protected Area features due to coastal squeeze  Coastal squeeze and natural squeeze  was calculated for habitats at a national scale for the whole of the Welsh coastline, as well as for smaller delineations of the coastline. The potential scale of habitat deterioration due to coastal squeeze was then reported:  · Nationally; · Against individual MPAs; and · Against MPA designations amalgamated across Wales: o SACs; o SSSIs; o SPAs; and o Ramsar sites.  Coastal squeeze has been assessed for three timeframes (epochs): · 2025 to 2055 (30 years) – Equivalent to SMP2 medium-term epoch; · 2055 to 2105 (50 years) – Equivalent to SMP2 long-term epoch; and · 2105 to 2155 (50 years) – New long-term epoch.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:17:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19447\tNRW_DS161285\t2024-08-19T11:08:35.712Z\t1048\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Management Catchments Cycle 3\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323835\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Management Catchments Cycle 3.lyr\tRobert Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS\tWFD Management Catchments Cycle 3 is a dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Management catchments are the unit of geography for which action plans are drafted in implementing the WFD. This process was conducted by using expert judgement in consultation. WFD Management Catchments have an action plan published that relates to all waterbodies that fall within its boundaries.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4462/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_management_catchments_c3\t2025-03-21T10:58:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19464\tNRW_DS161288\t2024-08-27T08:40:25.194Z\t1776\tValid\tEditable\tNational Flood Hazard Maps - Climate Change\t© CNC/NRW. Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from the UK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology © UKCEH, © EA and Getmapping Plc and Bluesky International Limited (2015). Defra, Met Office and DARD Rivers Agency © Crown copyright. © Cranfield University. © James Hutton Institute. Contains OS data © Crown copyright and database right. Land and Property Services © Crown copyright and database right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323838\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh / GIS and Spatial Data Team   Metadata Point of Contact: Mark Pugh / Simon James / Dave Tarrant       Surface Water Catchments (subset dataset NRW_DS124811): Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from Environment Agency © EA and Getmapping Plc and Bluesky International Limited [2015].\tThe National Flood Hazard Climate Change maps are based on generalised modelling only.  These maps use the same base information as the Present Day Hazard Maps, but include an allowance for Climate Change (2120).  These maps have no official status for Planning or Insurance purposes, therefore the public and professionals are advised to use the information contained within the Flood Risk Assessment Wales Map, the Development Advice Map / Flood Map for planning (as appropriate) for these purposes as these will be more up-to-date and incorporate improved modelling information.    The National Flood Hazard Map for Climate Change have been created for 3 sources of flooding, namely, 1. Flooding from Rivers 2. Flooding from the Sea 3. Flooding from Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses  The Maps show flood depth, velocity (speed), hazard and extent for High, Medium and Low risk scenarios with raised flood defences.    For Rivers and Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses, High risk is up to 1 in 30year plus climate change uplift (CC); Medium risk is between 1 in 30 plus CC and 1 in 100year plus CC and Low risk is between 1 in 100 year plus CC and 1 in 1,000year plus CC Annual Exceedance Probability (AEP).  For the Sea, High risk is up to 1 in 30 year plus CC; Medium risk is between 1 in 30 plus CC to 1 in 200 year plus CC; and Low risk 1 in 200 year plus CC to 1 in 1000 year plus CC.  The Climate Change uplift values use the “Central” estimate for the year 2120 from the Welsh Government guidance available at the time of modelling (2018).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-12-16\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/7106\t2025-12-19T12:10:08.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19465\tNRW_DS161289\t2024-08-27T10:08:44.721Z\t295\tValid\tEditable\tWinter Gull Survey\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323839\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mneb/Liaison%20%20BTO/Winter%20Gull%20survey%20WiNGs/BTO%20WiNGs%20data?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\tMatthew Murphy: Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Species\tThese data were collected as part of the Winter Gull Survey (WinGS) project which is a UK wide survey coordinated by the BTO and funded by the BTO, Defra and country nature conservation bodies. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) is the owner of the Welsh part of the data. The project aims to produce revised wintering population estimates for non-breeding gulls in the UK and its constituent countries, and also to assess numbers of each species in autumn.   Information will be provided for all gull species, but with a focus on Black-headed Gull, Common Gull, Mediterranean Gull Ichthyaetus melanocephalus, Lesser Black-backed Gull, Herring Gull and Great Black-backed Gull as the six most numerous and widespread species that occur in the UK during the non-breeding seasons. Mediterranean Gulls have been added as an additional target species for WinGS, recognising their increased use of UK inland and coastal waters across breeding and non-breeding seasons.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-08-27\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-28T15:25:17.235Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19489\tNRW_DS161291\t2024-09-03T08:28:35.393Z\t916\tValid\tEditable\tNorth Wales Groundwater Archive Database\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323931\tZ:\\Archive\\Water Mgmt\\Groundwater\\Groundwater Archive (LTD)\tKay Roberts - Specialist Advisor, Hydrogeology\tData from groundwater sources (borehole, wells and springs) from across North Wales and the Lower Dee in England, but predominantly in the Clwyd and Lower Dee catchments.  Data includes borehole construction and geology logs (records), test pumping data, ad hoc groundwater level and quality data, surveying details (for elevation), maps, data analysis and other correspondence and notes on the site.  Data collection started in the 1970s and continued until 2014 (when the officer colleting the data retired).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-09-03\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2024-12-20T14:29:10.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19490\tNRW_DS161292\t2024-09-03T10:01:29.031Z\t747\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Gull Surveys 2021\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323932\tStored on the DMS - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mneb/Liaison%20%20SMP/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;clickparams\u003deyAiWC1BcHBOYW1lIiA6ICJNaWNyb3NvZnQgT3V0bG9vayIsICJYLUFwcFZlcnNpb24iIDogIjE2LjAuMTc3MjYuMjAyMDYiLCAiT1MiIDogIldpbmRvd3MiIH0%3D\u0026amp;CID\u003d6ae24ca1%2D20b6%2D0000%2D1b3d%2Db9a684ddd4c9\u0026amp;cidOR\u003dSPO\u0026amp;RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FMarine%2DEvidence%2Fmneb%2FLiaison%20%20SMP%2FUK%20Wide%20Seabird%20Census%20%2D%20Urban%20Gulls\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120005ECAE68515E2714E8923C04099DEA67D\tMatthew Murphy: Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine Species\tFollowing evidence of widespread decline in the productivity of Herring Gulls and less black backed gulls this survey compromises of a ground based survey and aerial survey of gulls to estimate the number of black-back  and herring gulls breeding in urban environments in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-09-03-2024-11-19\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-28T15:20:04.317Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19568\tNRW_DS161296\t2024-09-20T10:05:57.369Z\t425\tValid\tEditable\tThe Status and Distribution of Broom-tip Chesias rufata at Morfa Harlech and Harlech Forest in 2023\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\tData is stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database which is stored on the Recorder 6 Database\tDr Michael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tA survey for Broom-tip Chesias rufata at Morfa Harlech   The aims of the current survey were to map the distribution of the larval foodplant on Morfa Harlech and Harlech Forest and to assess the distribution of Broom-tip by searching for larvae. This would provide a truer reflection of the exact distribution of the moth rather than recording the more mobile adult stage. Surveys were undertaken from July to September to avoid confusion with larvae of the Streak moth Chesias legatella which have a similar appearance and also feed on Broom in May and June.and Harlech Forest, the key locality for the moth in north Wales, from July to early September 2023.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-12-01\tcreation\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr1212\t2025-02-19T16:38:38.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19602\tNRW_DS161297\t2024-09-26T10:07:35.491Z\t26\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForester Conservation\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.th the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323937\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Conservation.lyr\tArpana Chunilal, Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support, Evidence, Policy and Permitting\tThis layer is a copy of the Master Conservation Sites layer; where the site location is sensitive it is obscured by the application of the internal obfuscation value set in the Master Conservation Sites layer. It is a read only layer on ForesterWeb which is available to all users. The layer contains details of locations, species, and inspection records for all conservation sites on the National Forest Estate. The individual records are recorded as points, polygons, and lines. It is collected to create a record of all species on the Welsh Government Woodland Estate, but also to be used for constraints in coupe planning, and for habitat restoration projects.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-09-26\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-21T10:48:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19603\tNRW_DS161298\t2024-09-26T10:46:42.933Z\t31\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForester Heritage\t© CNC/NRW and various third-party recorders. NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323938\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Heritage.lyr\tArpana Chunilal - Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support\tThis layer holds information about heritage features on or near to the National Forest Estate. It is made up of data recorded by NRW staff and data imported from third parties. The records are recorded as points, polygons, and lines. It is collected to create a record of all the heritage features on the  Welsh Government Woodland Estate so they can be managed and improved if possible. It is also used for constraints in coupe planning, and for heritage feature restoration projects.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-09-26\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:48.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19604\tNRW_DS161299\t2024-09-26T11:14:05.358Z\t26\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForester Other Trails\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323939\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Other Trails.lyr\tArpana Chunilal, Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support, Evidence, Policy and Permitting.\tThis layer holds additional trail data which is NOT held in the Rec-Walking, Rec-Mountain biking, and Rec- Miscellaneous routes. It is collected to create a record of trails that are not assigned any recreational status and do not fall into any other categories of layers i.e. public right of way, bridal ways, etc. The attributes only contain the name of the trail and it has no other identifying features. It is spatially represented as lines only. It is used for constrains in coupe planning.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-09-26\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:45.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19605\tNRW_DS161300\t2024-09-26T19:56:08.426Z\t24\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForester Planting Plan\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333030\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Planting Plan.lyr\tArpana Chunilal - Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support, Evidence, Policy and Permitting\tThis layer holds additional restock information that is separate from the ‘Restock Areas’ layer. The records shows specific tree species that are to be planted. The data is spatially records as polygons. The data is used for coupe planning and to update the sub-compartment database.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-09-26\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:02.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19606\tNRW_DS161301\t2024-09-26T20:06:00.894Z\t24\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForester Recreational Walking\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333031\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Recreational Walking.lyr\tArpana Chunilal - Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support, Evidence, Policy and Permitting\tThis layer holds recreational walking routes across the Welsh Government Woodland Estate. The data collected shows a range of suitable walking routes that range from Forest trails to Heritage trails. The data is spatially recorded as lines only and it is used as a constrains layer in coupe planning.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-09-26\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:44.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19607\tNRW_DS161302\t2024-09-26T20:10:55.211Z\t25\tValid\tEditable\tForester Rides\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333032\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Rides.lyr\tArpana Chunilal - Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support\tThis layer holds information about ride features on the Welsh Government Woodland Estate. Rides are a network of established tracks across the woodland developed to aid timber extractions. It is collected to form a record of all the rides on the Welsh Government Woodland Estate. The data is spatially recorded as lines only. It is used by operational staff to identify  existing rides so they can be utilised in operations.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-09-26\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:14:05.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19608\tNRW_DS161303\t2024-09-26T20:14:47.404Z\t26\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForester Watercourses\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333033\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Watercourses.lyr\tArpana Chunilal - Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support, Evidence, Policy and Permitting\tThis layer is a copy of the detailed river networks layer. It also contains additional features added in by NRW staff. The quality of the data is satisfactory as it is not quality controlled and it is not monitored. It is used for constrains in coupe planning.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Cadw. Welsh Historic Monuments\t2024-09-26\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19625\tNRW_DS161305\t2024-10-04T07:36:33.264Z\t249\tValid\tEditable\tSurvey of the Black Bog Ant Formica picea on Cors Goch, Llanllwch SSSI in 2024\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333035\tData Stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database in Recorder6\tMichael Howe - Michael.Howe@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tThis survey was commissioned to determine the current status and distribution of Black Bog Ant on Cors Goch Llanllwch south of the railway line in August 2024 by undertaking a count of solaria.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-03-11\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-11T15:59:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19626\tNRW_DS161306\t2024-10-04T07:40:30.750Z\t345\tValid\tEditable\tSurveys for additional populations of Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae in Pembrokeshire and west Carmarthenshire in 2023 and 2024\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333036\tData is stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder6\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThe current two-year survey visited a further eleven sites in Pembrokeshire and one in west Carmarthenshire. A single Scaly Cricket nymph was found in a pitfall trap at Monkstone Beach near Tenby in October 2023. An additional seven nymphs were recorded at the same site in October 2024, again in pitfall traps, suggesting that a resident population is present. This represents the 4th known locality in Wales, all of which are in Pembrokeshire, and the 6th in the UK. The current survey suggests that there are few suitable beaches for Scaly Cricket and that, even where suitable habitat is present, it is not always utilised by the species which seems to have a very restricted distribution. The survey further highlights the importance of the Marloes Sands population as it is clearly the largest in Wales. Populations at Marloes Sands, West Dale Bay and Monkstone Beach remain very vulnerable to habitat loss due to coastal erosion and sea-level rise.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-03-11\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-11T11:31:52.232Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19628\tNRW_DS161307\t2024-10-04T09:12:46.217Z\t18\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecies in Peril in Wales 2024\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333037\tData is a spreadsheet collection of a range of species and link will be supplied by Mike Howe\tMichael Howe\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-21T10:52:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19632\tNRW_DS161310\t2024-10-08T11:01:46.644Z\t22\tValid\tEditable\tLocal Seascape Character Areas\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333130\tThe working master file is stored at Y:\\Landscape\\Seascapes_LSCA\\LSCA.gdb - waiting to be loaded to X:Drive\tJohn Briggs - Specialist Advisor: Landscape\tA single, all-Wales, geospatial database, showing Local Seascape Character Areas (LSCA). Spatial extent is to 12 nautical miles out to sea (coincident with the National Wales Marine Plan for Inshore Waters boundary). In addition, coastal land areas are included (seascape being about the connections between land and sea). Land areas vary in distance from the coast line. They include the whole extent of NRW’s Flood Map for Planning Extreme Tidal Inundation, plus a range of land of a coastal type, such as cliffs and slopes, dune systems, ports and coastal settlements, and a wider coastal setting area. Area boundaries reflect changes in character, which may in practice be a broad zone of transition, and so should not be taken as a sudden change in character.   Boundaries in the marine area are highly generalised (to 1:250,000 accuracy), reflecting available evidence, and are informed by a variety of different baseline datasets and field observations. Boundary lines inland are similarly generalised (but to 1:25,000 accuracy), reflecting the greater availability of more specific spatial evidence. Inland boundaries follow identifiable features, such as edges of built up areas, changes in land cover or land use, field patterns, and changes in topographic pattern. Boundary lines are freshly drawn rather than being ‘snapped’ to baseline datasets, and any apparent exception is unintended.  Attribute table data includes: area geographical name and number; various typologies; from which source assessment the area is derived; to which National Marine Character Area the LSCA relates; and some evaluations related to different factors. There are various URLs to link to published written profiles for areas, where they exist. (These extend to several pages per area, and are available for about 70% of the LSCA). NRW manages and publishes the dataset ‘once for Wales’ to promote easier access for users, than the formerly fragmented datasets arising from different assessments in different areas.  In addition, our LSCAs attribute each area to a National Marine Character Area, in effect creating a nesting hierarchy between the two scales of assessment. LSCA introduces a new nationally consistent area numbering system to achieve this. Although baseline data has not been copied directly, a number of baseline datasets have informed the characterisation, both for identifying area boundaries and to inform the written profile documents. These include: UKHO rastor charts, Ordnance Survey 1:25,000 scale rastor map; Lidar mapping of land; NRW Flood map for Planning; Slope for Land-spreading data; BGS Seabed sediment data; Landcover Map 2020; NRW Inter-tidal survey; LANDMAP Visual and Sensory layer.   Other datasets consulted included: MMO Land-sea inter-visibility; Key variations in Marine Habitats, Wave Energy and Water Depth (a dataset created for the National Marine Character Areas preparation); Seabed sediments; Land with sea views and Sea visibility from land. Some fields in the LSCA attribute table are blank at the time of writing. This reflects limited resources and it is intended to fill out all blanks in due course with further work and updates (no timescale). The attribute table and polygons at least provide a common structure into which future data can be added. It is often 3rd party work that uses the datasets that results in new data arising, which are in turn be fed back into the LSCA master dataset.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-02-13\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T10:24:41.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19667\tNRW_DS161312\t2024-10-17T14:31:22.261Z\t30\tValid\tEditable\tNRW Fisheries Enforcement Teams\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333132\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Fisheries Enforcement Teams.lyr\tJeremy Goddard, Team Leader Industry \u0026amp; Waste Regulation\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Fisheries Enforcement Team boundaries. The purpose of this data is to capture and define boundaries to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities. It will be used to ensure enquiries and incidents etc, are passed to the correct team.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-10-17\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T13:09:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19712\tNRW_DS161313\t2024-11-04T09:03:58.060Z\t280\tValid\tEditable\tA survey to determine the status of the Depressed River Mussel Pseudanodonta complanata at eight monitoring stations within the River Wye (Lower Wye) SSSI in 2022\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333133\tData stored in the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder6\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tData from a survey of the River Wye taken over a 3 day period from August 17th - 19th 2022. Sampling methodology followed that established in the previous survey in 2007, with eight monitoring sites visited from Backney Common (north of Ross-on-Wye) downstream to Bigsweir covering a 50km stretch of the River Wye. At each site, 60-minute visual surveys were undertaken in the river using a bathyscope to spot mussels buried in sediments or dislodged from them using a pronged rake. The number of mussels located during that period were counted, measured, photographed and then returned to the river.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-11-01\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-10T15:52:42.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19716\tNRW_DS161314\t2024-11-04T11:54:35.896Z\t257\tValid\tEditable\tThe status and distribution of the Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae at Freshwater West in 2023\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\tData held in the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder 6\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tFollowing the discovery of three nymphs of the Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae on the shingle beach at Freshwater West in Pembrokeshire in August and September 2022, a survey was undertaken in 2023 to determine its current status and distribution.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-02-01\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:56.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19741\tNRW_DS161315\t2024-11-11T10:12:22.984Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForester Acquired and Disposed rights\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333135\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\tArpana Chunilal\t1. Acquired rights Acquired rights is a key editable layer that contains data on acquired rights on the Welsh Government Woodland Estate (WGWE). This is a sensitive dataset as it contains transaction references,names, and titles. The attributes are linked to a spatial shape that shows the extent of the acquired right(s). When any rights are disposed of they are amended on this layer and they automatically appear in the ‘Disposed Rights’ layer. This data is collected and digitised by NRW staff and it is quality controlled by the estate surveyors.  2. Disposed rights  Disposed rights is linked to Acquired Rights and all data within is derived from Acquired Rights. The layer is browse only, thus data can only be viewed and not amended.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-08-23\tcreation\tnull\t\t2026-01-12T17:04:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19777\tNRW_DS161317\t2024-11-13T17:03:28.863Z\t664\tValid\tEditable\tClocaenog Forest Red Squirrel Abundancy data 2023\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the targeted survey data. The non-targeted data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333137\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/mam/Red Squirrel/Clocaenog/Population Abundance Contract 2023_2024/Data Associated with Abundancy report/Detections_original_survey_29-10-24.csv  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/mam/Red%20Squirrel/Clocaenog/Population%20Abundance%20Contract%202023_2024/Data%20Associated%20with%20Abundancy%20report/Detections_targetted_survey_29-10-24.csv\tRebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Mammals\tThis study involved the assessment of red squirrel occupancy and habitat preferences within Clocaenog Forest. A stratified random sampling strategy was employed, along with a ‘targeted’ survey to maximise red squirrel detections.  This data consists of spreadsheets detailing red squirrel detections via trail cameras.   One spreadsheet is the original, non-targeted/random approach, the other is the targeted approach.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-11-25\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-29T14:22:33.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19781\tNRW_DS161318\t2024-11-18T15:23:24.503Z\t419\tValid\tEditable\tWest Anglesey fragile sponge and anthozoan communities dive survey, 2023\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333138\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\2023 Anglesey Sponge and Anthozoan\tNatasha Lough - Specialist Advisor, Marine Ecology\tThis resource contains data from surveys of fragile sponge and anthozoan (FSAN) communities off North and West Anglesey, collected during dive surveys in April-May 2023. Divers were tasked with re-surveying up to 17 sites previously surveyed as far back as 1996 to determine the continued presence of reported FSAN communities, namely of the biotope CR.HCR.XFa.ByErSp.DysAct (mixed turf of bryozoans and erect sponges with Dysidea fragilis and Actinothoe sphyrodeta on tide-swept wave exposed circalittoral rock. This data was collected as FSAN communities are listed under Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2016 and this area around Anglesey has been proposed as an Area of Search (AoS) in the Marine Protected Area (MPA) Network Completion Programme by Welsh Government. The data comprises of:  • 3GB of images (covering each of the 20 survey sites) • 4GB of videos taken during dive surveys • A Survey Photo Catalogue (Excel spreadsheet), with details of each photo and video (e.g. filename, subject, notable species, biotope) • A Master metadata Excel spreadsheet\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-11-18\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-17T11:48:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19820\tNRW_DS161319\t2024-11-29T12:10:24.175Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFurther studies to assess the distribution of the Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior on Oxwich Burrows, Nicholaston Burrows, Whiteford Burrows, Three Cliffs Bay \u0026amp; Swansea Dunes in September 2023\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333139\tRecorder 6\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tMike Howe to supply\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-27T09:14:03.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19851\tNRW_DS161320\t2024-12-02T15:17:22.567Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Survey of Lichens on Pine Snags at Cors y Llyn National Nature Reserve\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333230\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data/Snag%20Lichens%20at%20Cors%20y%20Llyn/NRW%20Evidence%20Report%20761%20-%20Cors%20y%20Llyn%20pine%20snag%20lichen%20survey%202023%20data.xlsx?d\u003dwfe1b9fba22b64297ab5b2b787fa4cda2\tSam Bosanquet - Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tCors y Llyn National Nature Reserve in Powys was surveyed for lignicolous lichens on dead Scots Pine Pinus sylvestris snags on three dates in November 2023. Lichens were systematically recorded on 24 Scots Pine Pinus sylvestris snags and on one birch Betula snag, with additional casual records made on other Scots Pines, birches, and Heather Calluna vulgaris. Forty-six lichen species, 10 lichenicolous fungi and 2 non-lichenised fungi were recorded during the survey, of which 33 species were new site records.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-02-01\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:27.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19870\tNRW_DS161321\t2024-12-05T09:58:58.182Z\t327\tValid\tEditable\tCwm Ivy National Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey 2022\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333231\tY:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Coastal Habitats\\Cwm Ivy NVC 2022\\WyndrushWild_CwmIvy_NVC_2022.shp\tHeather Lewis, Lead Specialist Advisor, Coastal Habitats, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team, EPP\tThe report and this GIS dataset present the results of a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey carried out in 2022 on selected areas of saltmarsh habitat and transitional vegetation at Cwm Ivy Marsh, North Gower.   Cwm Ivy is a saltmarsh creation site and the vegetation survey confirms the establishment of saltmarsh over the majority of the site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-01-25\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-01-09T09:22:17.891Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19967\tNRW_DS161323\t2024-12-23T14:25:17.508Z\t764\tValid\tEditable\tLlyn Horse Mussel Multibeam Echosounder Survey data 2024\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333233\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Marine acoustic bathymetric data\\Multibeam\\Modiolus 2024\\H23034_Hydrofix_2024_Deliverables\tHarriet Lincoln, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Projects Team\tThis data is from the 2024 Llyn Horse Mussel (Modiolus modiolus) Multibeam Echosounder Survey (MBES).   The work was carried out by Binnies/Hydrofix funded by Nature Networks as part of an investigation into the decline observed at the reef. Both reefs were mapped to International Hydrographic order (IHO) 1a or higher.   The data was processed and analysed to produce bathymetric surfaces; backscatter mosaics; a seabed classification, including the extents of the mussel beds; and measurements of the mussel bioherms.  The results from the 2024 survey were then compared to previous datasets, provided by NRW, from 2005, 2008 and 2015, all of which were analysed and reprocessed.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-12-17\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-24T14:09:18.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20028\tNRW_DS161324\t2025-01-08T09:23:11.062Z\t147\tValid\tEditable\tNational Environment Programme - 2024 Price Review Final Determination\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333234\tX:\\Utilities\\Water\\Price Review 2024 National Environment Programme\tRuth Johnston \u0026amp; Inoka Manatunga - Integrated Water Planning Team\tThe Price Review 2024 (PR24) Final Determination (FD) National Environment Programme (NEP) dataset is a Wales-wide spatial point dataset that identifies the environmental legislative obligations on water companies operating in or benefitting from Wales’s natural resources. These include five water companies; Dwr Cymru Welsh Water, Hafren Dyfrdwy, United Utilities, Severn Trent Water and South Staffordshire and Cambridgeshire Water.   Delivery of the identified actions occurs in the Asset Management Plan (AMP) period. The PR24 NEP will be delivered in AMP8 from 1 April 2025 to 31 March 2030. These actions are intended to ensure compliance with new and changed environmental obligations relevant to the water companies.   This dataset is published reflecting the version of the NEP used in Ofwat’s final determination for PR24. It is correct as of December 2024. During its delivery in AMP8 the NEP actions may be subject to change.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-01-08-2025-02-04\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/7217/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_national_environment_plan\t2025-11-25T17:13:52.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20084\tNRW_DS161327\t2025-01-20T11:05:24.317Z\t468\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of Life (SoLIFE)\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333237\tMuch of the data resources are stored in the DMS here - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landman/Protected-Sites/so-life/D1%20Physical%20Monitoring/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;clickparams\u003deyAiWC1BcHBOYW1lIiA6ICJNaWNyb3NvZnQgT3V0bG9vayIsICJYLUFwcFZlcnNpb24iIDogIjE2LjAuMTgyMjcuMjAyMjIiLCAiT1MiIDogIldpbmRvd3MiIH0%3D\u0026amp;CID\u003dec5979a1%2D702b%2D0000%2D415a%2D9285ebb2dfae\u0026amp;cidOR\u003dSPO\u0026amp;RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Flandman%2FProtected%2DSites%2Fso%2Dlife%2FD1%20Physical%20Monitoring%2F00%20SoLIFE%20Data%20Archive   Data available in a GIS format is found here - X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life  Users should see individual Sands Of Life Datasets for specific locations\tKathryn Hewitt - Lead Specialist Advisor, Sustainable Farming Scheme\tSands of LIFE was a major conservation project to rejuvenate sand dunes across Wales which ran to June 2024.  The project’s aims were to recreate natural movement in the dunes and revitalise habitats which are home to some of our rarest wildlife.  A range of datasets were created as part of this project which included data for Great Crested Newts, Sand Lizard, Fen Orchid\u0027s, Invertebrates and Bryophytes. For more information on data created from this project please see child metadata entries. This data includes survey results pre-intervention and post-intervention.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-01-20\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-01-20T11:37:47.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n20113\tNRW_DS161328\t2025-01-21T13:43:48.040Z\t233\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE LiDAR Survey\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333238\tX:\\Basemapping\\LiDAR\\SoLIFE\\\tKathryn Hewitt - Lead Specialist Advisor, Sustainable Farming Scheme\tSands of LIFE is a major conservation project to rejuvenate 2400 ha of sand dunes across Wales, by reprofiling dunes, creating bare sand to promote mobility, removing scrub, and other actions.   The Sands of LIFE project commissioned a LiDAR survey of sand dune areas both before and after completion of large-scale sand dune restoration works. The aim was to create an accurate and up-to-date baseline picture of the dune morphology and determine geomorphological changes and sand movement away from the footprint of interventions.    Sands of LIFE specified the provision of aerial LiDAR across the entire area of 10 project sites on the Welsh Coast, split over four Special Areas of Conservation (SACs)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-01-14\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n20114\tNRW_DS161329\t2025-01-21T13:45:15.493Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tData collected for: Sands of LIFE Socio-Economic Impact Assessment (post-intervention)\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333239\t\t\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-21T10:38:27.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20116\tNRW_DS161330\t2025-01-22T10:27:04.920Z\t220\tValid\tEditable\tCommunities at Risk Register 2024 – Present Day (CaRR)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333330\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe Communities at Risk Register (CaRR) has been developed to provide an objective means of identifying risk and prioritising flood risk management activities at a Wales-wide, community level. It applies a standard methodology across all flood sources to calculate a theoretical ‘danger score’ that allows comparative risks to be quantified and ranked (from High to Low).    The CaRR comprises a spreadsheet that identifies and ranks individual communities for,  1. a natural, ‘undefended’ scenario, and  2. a managed scenario (based on the presence of defences and flood warning).   It is complemented by a GIS layer which defines the individual Community polygons and is attributed with danger scores, community rankings and other metric information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:46:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20132\tNRW_DS161331\t2025-01-24T15:37:12.049Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClimate Vulnerability Assessment (CVA) for Ramsar Sites\tAccess restrictions on this data have not been determined. It may contain sensitive information and therefore may be restricted under legislation. NRW may provide access to this dataset on request, however NRW may not release, publish or disseminate the data or derived products freely without further assessment.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333331\tX:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\CVA RAMSAR.lyr\tContact Clive Walmsley, Senior Specialist Advisor, Climate Change and Decarbonisation, SPG for further information about the data.  Data originally produced and provided by Jon Rothwell, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-01-27T10:16:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n20190\tNRW_DS161333\t2025-02-04T10:51:51.583Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBaseline Monitoring of Peatland Invertebrate on the Hafod Elwy - Cord Goch Peatland Restoration Site 2024\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333333\t\tBethan Beech\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-07-08T09:17:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20191\tNRW_DS161334\t2025-02-04T13:53:20.139Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGrizzled Skipper Survey of Wrexham Industrial Estate 2024\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333334\t\tBethan Beech\tWaiting for metadata from custodian\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-07-08T09:17:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20192\tNRW_DS161335\t2025-02-04T13:57:20.684Z\t685\tValid\tEditable\tTranquillity \u0026amp; Place Theme 6 full resource - connecting sound and visually tranquil places\t© CNC/NRW and British Geological Society Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333335\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquillity and Place  Associated Spreadsheet can be found here - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/Landscape/tranq/TP/1.%20Tranquillity%20%26%20Place%20Theme%206%20full%20resource%20Final/Theme%206%20Stats.xlsx?d\u003dw666689ff9a0e4776a5e11cd4b9090e98\tJill Bullen - Lead Specialist Advisor, Landscape\tThe Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place resource was developed using mapped ‘themes’. You can view information and data relating to the Tranquillity and Place themes on the Tranquillity and Place Story Map. - https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/865c1876d9f64280a3dfc6e2769a46a5  • Theme 1 - relative abundance, perception or experience of nature, natural landscapes and greenspaces. • Theme 2 - relative freedom from intrusive visual disturbance and human influence. • Theme 3 - relative dark skies.  • Theme 4 – sound environments where natural sounds are more prominent than noise and are appropriate to context.  • Theme 5 - visually tranquil places (Themes 1, 2 and 3) • Theme 6 – the full tranquillity and place resource (Themes 1, 2, 3 and 4). Theme 6 full resource identifies the strategic and local resource in remote, rural, peri-urban and urban areas for use as an evidence base to inform policy intent, practice and provision for well-being benefits.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-02-19\tcreation\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/7420\t2025-05-14T08:22:43.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20240\tNRW_DS161336\t2025-02-12T11:13:46.570Z\t13\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPre-assessed Areas for Wind Energy\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333336\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pre-assessed areas for wind energy.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tAreas with a presumption in favour of large-scale wind energy development. Data highlights whether the area is suitable for wind, solar or both.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-02-12\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-21T10:52:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20252\tNRW_DS161337\t2025-02-12T15:01:18.392Z\t791\tValid\tEditable\tMonitoring of the Atlantic Woodland Bryophyte Assemblage in Coed Graig Uchaf SSSI\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333337\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FCoed%20Graig%20Uchaf%20Bryophyte%20Monitoring%202024\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam, Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tCoed Graig Uchaf SSSI (32.5 ha) was designated in 1987 and was selected as an example of a woodland with a rich oceanic bryophyte flora.  The first comprehensive bryophyte survey of the site was undertaken during 2014, when baseline monitoring data was collected. The aim of this 2024 study was to undertake the first re-monitoring of the Atlantic woodland bryophyte assemblage and assess what changes may have occurred  This dataset shows bryophyte presence data from 10x10m grid squares in Coed Graig Uchaf SSSI.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-02-11\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-17T10:40:42.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20279\tNRW_DS161339\t2025-02-17T17:15:05.609Z\t623\tValid\tEditable\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS) and Plant Community survey data of the River Wye SAC 2024\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333339\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/fm/CARWC4/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Ffm%2FCARWC4%2FWye%20SAC%20RHS%20and%20macrophyte%20contract%2FRHS%20Macrophyte%20Report%20and%20Data\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200042A0D276A0ABB34DACF1ADA432CBE918  Z:\\Archive\\Sampling Monitoring \u0026amp; Analysis Reporting\\Freshwater Monitoring\\River Habitat Survey (RHS) and Plant Community surveys of the River Wye SAC (Wales) carried out in 2024  No additional open data assessment were undertaken as will be covered by the published kieco datasets\tSuzanne Hearn, Freshwater Ecosystems and Fisheries Management Team Leader\tIn 2024, a River Habitat Survey (RHS), Simplified Phase 1 survey and LEAFPAC aquatic macrophyte survey was carried out on the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC). This was to contribute to the condition assessment of features in the River Wye SAC as well as provide information to support the management and restoration of protected sites and the delivery of River Basin Management Plans.   Twenty-two monitoring sites out of thirty-five possible sites were surveyed in the River Wye catchment between 5th August and 28th September 2024.   At each site the following surveys were conducted:  • River Habitat Surveys (RHS), to assess the quality of physical habitat, supported by  • Simplified Phase 1 survey, and • Aquatic plant surveys using the rivers LEAFPACS method, along with  • Field observations of habitat quality to assist with interpretation of the data.  The principal outputs of this project are digital copies of field records including photographs, access and parking, along with a description of each site.  These surveys were conducted on the Welsh section of the River Wye SAC.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-02-13\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-11T12:25:24.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20280\tNRW_DS161340\t2025-02-18T10:42:24.354Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status of the Grizzled Skipper Pyrgus malvae at Dinham Meadows SSSI and the wider Mod Caerwent Training Area in 2023 \u0026amp; 2024\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333430\t\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tWaiting for report completion\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-21T10:48:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20281\tNRW_DS161341\t2025-02-18T10:44:03.876Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey for the water beetles Haliplus variegatus, Hydrochus ignicollis, Hydrovatus clypealis and Limnebius aluta on selected Anglesey wetlands in 2024\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333431\t\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tWaiting for report completion\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-21T10:48:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20310\tNRW_DS161343\t2025-02-24T09:57:18.679Z\t504\tValid\tEditable\tCompliance Assessment of Welsh River Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) Against Water Quality Targets (2020 - 2023)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333433\tX:\\Resource Management\\Compliance Assessment Welsh River SACs\\Water Quality Targets (2020-2023)\tSuzanne Hearn, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems \u0026amp; Fisheries Management\tThese data identify the compliance of Welsh river Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) regarding targets for eight water quality attributes: Phosphorus, Dissolved Oxygen, Biochemical Oxygen Demand, Total Ammonia, Unionised Ammonia, Trophic Diatom Index, pH and Acid Neutralising Capacity.   The targets and methods of assessment are set at a UK level by the Joint Nature Conservation Committee as published in the Common Standards Guidance for Rivers 2016, and form part of the conservation objectives for the riverine SAC features.   There are nine SACs in Wales with riverine features (known in their respective Core Management Plans as):   • River Dee and Bala Lake  • Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn  • Coedydd Derw a Safleoedd Ystlumod Meirion (Afon Glaslyn)  • Afon Eden - Cors Goch Trawsfynydd  • Afon Teifi  • Afonydd Cleddau  • Afon Tywi  • River Usk  • River Wye   For the purposes of water quality reporting, Natural Resources Wales (NRW) divides rivers into units called water bodies. A total of 119 (of a possible 127) water bodies, each with a single sampling point, were assessed for one or more attribute. The River Dee and Wye were assessed in the Welsh part of the SAC only.   Water quality data collected by NRW’s routine surveillance monitoring programme for a four-year period (1st January 2020 – 31st December 2023) were extracted from the NRW water quality database (KiWQM) to assess compliance with targets.   Data was not available for all attributes for all water bodies due to the risk-based design of NRW’s monitoring programmes. For attributes/waterbodies with insufficient data to determine compliance over the period 2020-2023, but where a result was produced in the previous assessment (using 2017-2019 data), the previous result has been rolled forward.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-02-21-2025-10-09\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/7376/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_sac_river_wqt_2020_23\t2025-10-13T16:11:38.091Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20332\tNRW_DS161344\t2025-02-25T14:51:01.372Z\t482\tValid\tEditable\tNorth Wales Operations Flooded Properties\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333434\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flooded Properties North.lyr\tGwenno Talfryn - Advisor 1, Warning \u0026amp; Informing\tThis data is compiled from all reports of property flooding from Main Rivers and the Sea in North Wales from 1993 that we have within our records, and reports from North Powys from 2019 onwards.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-04-09-2025-10-13\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-15T09:14:20.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n20334\tNRW_DS161345\t2025-02-26T09:04:07.534Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAn insect survey of Ynyslas Dunes in July 2024, with a focus on the Pantaloon Bee Dasypoda hirtipes\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333435\t\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tMike Howe to Provide\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-21T10:52:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20356\tNRW_DS161346\t2025-03-05T09:37:35.539Z\t420\tValid\tEditable\tGwent Levels - Resilient Ecological Networks\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333436\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\RENs\tLiz Hancooks - Senior Officer, People \u0026amp; Places Richard Thomas - Senior Officer, People \u0026amp; Places\tResilient Ecological Networks (RENs), are networks of habitat linking protected sites and other biodiversity hotspots across the wider landscape, providing maximum benefit for biodiversity and human well-being.  Such networks have existing or potential for healthy resilient ecosystems, which provide a range of important ecosystem services, as well as allowing the movement of species across landscapes in response to climate change and disruptive or adverse events. The maintenance, enhancement and re-creation of RENs are vital for nature recovery.  RENs are composed of Core Zones (including protected sites such as SSSIs) and Nature Recovery Zones. These mapped together form an applied opportunity map for a Network.  The current layers available are the mapped products of the Gwent Levels Nature Network, which define resilient ecological networks in this area: Core and Nature Recovery Zone.  As well as this set of priority networks, it is useful to also be able to view these layers alongside other open datasets available which are the: - Sites of Special Scientific Interest NRW_DS98776 - Priority Ecological Networks NRW_DS125572  The output of the project has identified 42 core zones covering 18,979 hectares (ha) and 86 nature recovery zones covering 2,214 ha as two GIS layers. These broadly comprise freshwater, fen, bog, semi-natural grassland, and native woodland ecosystems. The Core Zone layer is based on sites designated for nature conservation in Wales (SAC, SSSI, NNRs, SINC, Priority habitats as identified in Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act, and Ancient Woodland database).   These layers highlight areas of opportunity and serve as an evidence base to support action planning, while remaining flexible and not prescriptive in terms of specific conservation measures.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-04-09\tcreation\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/7650/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_gwent_levels_ren\t2025-12-23T10:18:23.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20373\tNRW_DS161347\t2025-03-06T16:49:33.383Z\t627\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Regulations Cycle 3 Classification (interim 2024)\tData may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333437\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\tData Custodian: Rob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment\tThe Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England \u0026amp; Wales) Regulations 2017 (referred to as the WFD Regulations) provide a framework for managing the water environment in England and Wales. The Directive aims for “good status” of all ground and surface water (rivers, lakes, transitional water, and coastal waters). Under the WFD Regulations, a river basin management plan must be prepared for each river basin district. The plan includes environmental objectives and a summary of the programmes of measures required to achieve those objectives.  This dataset shows the boundaries and interim classification, undertaken in 2024, for The Water Framework Directive (WFD) Cycle 3. It is an update in classification for all water bodies in Wales following on from the Cycle 1, Cycle 2, and 2021 Cycle 3 baseline classification rounds.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-10-09-2025-03-06\tpublication-creation\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/7410/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_wfd_c3_2024_interim\t2026-01-20T10:03:18.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20398\tNRW_DS161348\t2025-03-11T12:07:01.017Z\t454\tValid\tEditable\tEryri Montane Lake Bryophyte Survey 2024\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333438\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FEryri%20Lake%20Bryophytes%202024\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\u0026amp;InitialTabId\u003dRibbon%2ERead\u0026amp;VisibilityContext\u003dWSSTabPersistence\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: non-vascular plants\tRocks along the shores of montane lakes in Eryri have been found to be an important bryophyte habitat, supporting a range of rare and threatened bryophytes that grow only in this habitat.  Due to the limited niche of the species of this habitat, and in some cases very small populations, they are especially vulnerable to disturbance and a single disturbance event could lead to irreparable damage to or even loss of the Welsh population of some species. Therefore, it is of high importance to fully document the bryophyte flora of the shores of these montane lakes and identify rocks that support rare and important bryophytes and are vulnerable to disturbance.   Seven montane lakes within the Eryri SSSI were surveyed in September 2024, spread between the three main massifs of Yr Wyddfa, Glyderau and Carneddau.   Lakes were prioritised for survey based on the quality and quantity of recent bryophyte records. The full shore of each lake was walked and the locations of any rare or otherwise notable bryophytes encountered were recorded.   Important boulders were identified and documented, with any damage present noted. A full species list was recorded from the shore of each lake.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-02-25-2025-02-25\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-20T10:01:06.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20402\tNRW_DS161349\t2025-03-12T14:45:01.614Z\t413\tValid\tEditable\tGweunydd Nant y Twyn SSSI - National Vegetation Classification (NVC) Grassland Survey (2024)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333439\tX/Biological/Phase 2 habitat mapping/Phase 2 lowland grassland  Y:\\Protected_Sites_Monitoring\\SC\\All_Area\\Nant_y_Twyn_2024_pSSSI\\Nant_y_Twyn_2024.gdb  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landmanps2/pscaer/NewPS/Forms/NRW%20Protected%20Sites%20Document%20Set/docsethomepage.aspx?ID\u003d2\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120D52000824C7CCC16790D469B8E16E1874A147100BA418224CB5E96458B8094535919D4B50074DD1354BE7A6941919D304C42B925BF\u0026amp;List\u003ddb63e9b1-3802-45a0-989c-2c3fc8864e14\u0026amp;RootFolder\u003d%2fteams%2flandmanps2%2fpscaer%2fNewPS%2fGweunydd+Nant+y+Twyn+SSSI\u0026amp;RecSrc\u003d%2fteams%2flandmanps2%2fpscaer%2fNewPS%2fGweunydd+Nant+y+Twyn+SSSI\u0026amp;InitialTabId\u003dRibbon.Read\u0026amp;VisibilityContext\u003dWSSTabPersistence\u0026amp;xsdata\%3d\u0026amp;sdata\u003dNXVoTCtwQnA4d0J5UkJuTklodlo3dTR4S3BJei8zUUQzWmMwdmFobiszVT0%3d\u0026amp;clickparams\u003deyAiWC1BcHBOYW1lIiA6ICJNaWNyb3NvZnQgT3V0bG9vayIsICJYLUFwcFZlcnNpb24iIDogIjE2LjAuMTgzMjQuMjAyNDAiLCAiT1MiIDogIldpbmRvd3MiIH0%3D#InplviewHasha1022bbe-43e8-4819-8a85-12eeae834e73\u003dFolderCTID%3D0x0120D52000824C7CCC16790D469B8E16E1874A147100BA418224CB5E96458B8094535919D4B50074DD1354BE7A6941919D304C42B925BF-RecSrc%3D%252Fteams%252Flandmanps2%252Fpscaer%252FNewPS%252FGweunydd%2520Nant%2520y%2520Twyn%2520SSSI-InitialTabId%3DRibbon%252ERead-VisibilityContext%3DWSSTabPersistence-WebPartID%3D%7BA1022BBE--43E8--4819--8A85--12EEAE834E73%7D-FilterField1%3DTitle-FilterValue1%3DPhase%25202%2520Survey%252C%2520Maps%2520and%2520Report%25202024\tSophie Higgett, Natura 2000 Sustainable Management Advisor - Newport, Blaenau Gwent \u0026amp; Caerphilly Environment Team.  Sophie has now left NRW, she advised to contact the team with any queries: et.newportcaerphilly\u0026amp;blaenaugwent@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tA standard Phase 2 NVC survey was undertaken at Gweunydd Nant y Twyn as part of the Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) designation process.  Gweunydd Nant y Twyn consists of a cluster of small fields, with tall, wooded hedgerows. The site is of particular importance for bryophytes.  The site comprises the original Phase 2 site survey site of Mockridge et al. 1998, which has now been resurveyed (2024) and extended to include several contiguous small fields, and a further extension centred on 5 small nearby fields, approximately 1/3rd of a km to the north, linked by a block of woodland and pasture. The total area of the Phase 2 survey site is now 39.9ha.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-03-06\tcreation\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3600/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_phase_2_grassland\t2025-09-22T15:25:34.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20433\tNRW_DS161350\t2025-03-19T09:41:02.437Z\t535\tValid\tEditable\tInland Fisheries River Restoration Strategic Allocation of Funds\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333530\tX:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\Inland Fisheries River Restoration Strategic Allocation of Funds\tRhodri Powell, Specialist Advisor River Restoration, Freshwater Ecosystems and Fisheries Management.  There is no requirement for Data Map Wales but the data is to be included in an AGOL application.\tThis data is two layers consisting of; Habitat Restoration and Other Remedial delivery associated with the Inland Fisheries Habitat Restoration grant.   Delivery is carried out by River Trusts and summary data is collated by Afonydd Cymru.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-03-18\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-24T14:43:07.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n20509\tNRW_DS161352\t2025-03-31T14:53:23.287Z\t275\tValid\tEditable\tAn Evaluation of the Current Use of Farmed vs Wild-caught Ragworm bait in Wales - Survey Results\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333532\tQuestionnaire Results stored here - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/advice/mar/MNN22-25/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7B7170031E-0717-4C32-886C-54DE0ABCCE4B%7D\u0026amp;action\u003dedit\u0026amp;source\u003dhttps%3A%2F%2Fcyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com%2Fteams%2Fadvice%2Fmar%2FMNN22-25%2FBaitCollectn%2FForms%2FNRW%2520View.aspx%3Fcsf%3D1%26web%3D1%26CID%3D466c16f7%252Dc54f%252D4285%252Dbe1e%252Ded89e9fa3bf1%26RootFolder%3D%252Fteams%252Fadvice%252Fmar%252FMNN22%252D25%252FBaitCollectn%252FCode%2520of%2520Conduct%2520and%2520Farmed%2520Bait%2520Workstream%26FolderCTID%3D0x012000911566D4BD76D243A66C042306FD1A40%23InplviewHash9effeb0a-6b03-4b90-aca0-0063f964c6b2%3DPaged%253DTRUE-p_SortBehavior%253D0-p_FileLeafRef%253DChris%252520Smith%252520%25252d%252520Dragon%252520Baits%25252emsg-p_ID%253D327-FolderCTID%253D0x012000911566D4BD76D243A66C042306FD1A40-PageFirstRow%253D31\tDewi R Evans - Specialist Officer, Intertidal Management\tThis is the survey outputs of a study evaluating the current use of farmed vs wild-caught ragworm bait in Wales, identifying any barriers to the use of farmed ragworm bait, and, with those barriers in mind, recommending measures that can be taken to encourage the use of farmed ragworm bait.   The data is an excel spreadsheet detailing questionnaire results which were undertaken by Bangor University under commision by Natural Resources Wales\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-04-01\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-05-14T08:34:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n20533\tNRW_DS161353\t2025-04-04T08:43:01.493Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommon Standards Monitoring of nationally important cave invertebrate assemblages in Ogof Draenen and Ogof Ffynnon Ddu, 2023 \u0026amp; 2024\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333533\tData will be stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder 6\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tTo be completed when report is finalised.\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-07-08T09:17:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20547\tNRW_DS161354\t2025-04-08T12:38:59.712Z\t271\tValid\tEditable\tRapid Evidence Assessment of the Impacts of Organic Manures on the Water Quality of Rivers\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333534\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/waterman/gs/wg/SACWQP/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fwaterman%2Fgs%2Fwg%2FSACWQP%2FPhase%202%20products%2FTechnical%20Products%2FWorkstream%204%2FTask%203%20Evidence%20Needs%2FRapid%20Evidence%20Assessment%2FProduct%203%20%2D%20Final%20Report%20%28Mar%202024%29\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000289C178199C7C343A0E69569780F56CD\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BF267B241%2DA6BD%2D465C%2D9E90%2D12B892E2F18D%7D\tTimothy Jones. Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting\tThe principal aim of this study was to improve Natural Resources Wales’ understanding of how organic manures, particularly from the poultry sector, can impact the nutrient status of rivers, with a focus on phosphorus (P). The methodology used to produce this Rapid Evidence Assessment (REA) included a systematic review of relevant literature to establish an evidence base, which was used to address primary and secondary Research Questions (RQs). The scope of the study was restricted to answering the RQs only, and, as a REA, the literature searches and critical appraisal of the literature were not as comprehensive as a full systemic review.  This metadata is created for the finalised evidence base created from evidence reviews and research questions.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-04-04\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-09T11:04:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n20569\tNRW_DS161355\t2025-04-11T07:53:51.837Z\t500\tValid\tEditable\tWelsh Acoustic Marine Mammal Survey (WAMMS)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333535\tData will be stored on the Crown Estate Marine Data Exchange Portal.\tAlex Zalewski - Marine Ecology Advisor\tThe Welsh Acoustic Marine Mammal Survey (WAMMS)  aim was to develop an effective and robust method for measuring underwater noise and the occurrence of cetaceans (whales, dolphins and porpoises) in marine protected areas (MPAs) around the Welsh coastline. To test and demonstrate these monitoring methods, an 18-month pilot study was undertaken as part of the project in the North Anglesey Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC). As well as illustrating how monitoring could be carried out elsewhere in Welsh marine waters, this pilot study provided baseline data on ambient noise and marine mammal detections at the site.  Three monitoring locations were selected within the North Anglesey Marine SAC: one at Holyhead deep, two off Point Lynas and one situated 1.7km off the coast and the other 5.2 km, at the boundary of the SAC.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-04-11-2025-04-11\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T13:51:42.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20570\tNRW_DS161356\t2025-04-11T11:25:56.162Z\t131\tValid\tEditable\tImpacts of bait collection on Welsh Marine Protected Areas (MPAs): Bait Digging\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333536\tMarine Recorder Online (eb56c392-90f5-45ae-8ecd-c57dc47e3639).  Associated data held in DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/advice/mar/MNN22-25/BaitCollectn/Outputs/ABPmer%20survey%20raw%20data?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;e\u003d0X2cxO\tAlex Zalewski, Marine Ecology Advisor\tSurvey to assess the impacts of three bait collection methods on Welsh MPAs - bait digging (Sites : Angle Bay, Four Mile Bridge, Penmon - Beaumaris), boulder turning (Penmon-Beaumaris, West of Great Orme), crab shelters (Deganwy, Bangor Pier). Samples taken from control and impact sites. Infaunal cores, sediment cores, benthic megafauna (i.e. razor clam, cockle) quadrat, worm cast count, seagrass coverage, boulder epifauna, boulder turning classification.  This metadata covers the results of the bait digging monitoring at Angle Bay, Four Mile Bridge and Penmon-Beaumaris.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-04-11-2025-12-17\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2026-01-14T10:01:34.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n20802\tNRW_DS161359\t2025-05-16T12:53:31.642Z\t328\tValid\tEditable\tNational Peatland Action Programme (NPAP) - Water Vole and Otter Records\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333539\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\NPAP - Water Vole and Otter Records.lyr  Copy of NBN data in Darwin Core format - Z:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\Data Mgmt\\Data Services Data Publishing Area\\NBN Data\\NRW_DS161359_NPAP_VOLE_OTTER\tRobert Bacon, Senior Specialist Officer, National Action Programme for Peatlands\tThis data shows evidence of water vole and otter activity in point data form. Surveys have been undertaken by our contractor to inform National Peatland Action Programme approaches to peatland restoration. No limiting information.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-05-19-2025-07-15\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr3553\t2025-11-18T17:08:05.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20811\tNRW_DS161360\t2025-05-20T10:56:09.693Z\t347\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Protected Areas Compensation Habitat\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333630\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Compensatory Habitats.lyr\tKirsten Ramsay, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThis data is a map of the extent of marine areas in Welsh waters that have been identified as compensatory habitat within Welsh waters and along Welsh coastlines under Regulation 68 (Compensatory measures) of The Conservation of Habitats and Species Regulations 2017.   These areas are afforded the same protection and management requirements as the SAC Natura 2000 designated sites.   This also includes areas delivered by the National Habitat Creation Programme.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-05-20-2025-08-06\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/7679/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_mpa_compensation\t2025-11-18T17:17:20.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n20859\tNRW_DS161361\t2025-06-10T07:47:26.054Z\t133\tValid\tEditable\tAerial survey of seals in Wales 2023\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence, provided it is done so acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and recognising SMRU as the data originator. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333631\tDMS Document: EVID-307-1112  Wales Seal counts 2023.xlsx https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mm/Evidence%20Base%20%20Contracts/Seal%20Monitoring%20and%20Research/aerial%20survey/Wales%20seal%20counts%202023.xlsx?d\u003dw5e051d8b6eb94caaba39d3e53957789e  GIS layer: X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Seals\\Wales seal counts 2023.lyr\tThomas Stringell, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine Species, Natural Resources Planning/Marine and Freshwater Ecosytem Group\tThis data is counts of seals hauled out during aerial surveys of the coast of Wales in August 2023.   Data consists of date and time, site number, photographic frame start and end, grid reference, number of seals and geographic region.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-06-10-2026-01-22\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/8373/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wales_seal_count_2023\t2026-02-04T15:29:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n20931\tNRW_DS161363\t2025-06-17T08:57:46.070Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDrinking Water Safeguard Zones 2023\t© CNC/NRW and DCWW This dataset is derived from a third-party dataset owned by DCWW called DCWW DRINKING WATER CATCHMENTS. A data share agreement for this layer is being negotiated with DCWW. DCWW have allowed NRW to share this data for INTERNAL USE only. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333633\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\Drinking Water Safeguard Zones 2023.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn Suzanne.Lawn@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tAny waterbodies in Wales that are used to provide drinking water are designated as drinking water protected areas (DrWPAs). The designation aims to protect the water and prevent any deterioration in quality that could increase the required treatment of water to meet Drinking Water Standards. Drinking Water Protected Areas are required to be identified under the Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017. Within these drinking water protected areas we work with water companies to identify raw water sources that are ‘at risk’ of deterioration which would result in the need for additional treatment. These areas are degsinated as Safeguard Zones where additional measures can be used to address any factors that may reduce the water quality. The SgZ have been identified based on DCWW monitoring and risk assessments and are not limited to the DWPA but based on the wider Water Company identified catchment areas.  A catchment is the term used to describe the area of land where water drains or collects. After rain, water flows over the land, through soil and rock, into streams and eventually the main river. The boundary of a catchment follows natural slopes, hills or mountains within a landscape and determines if water flows into one river or another or subsequently into a reservoir.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Dwr Cymru Welsh Water\t2025-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:20.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20967\tNRW_DS161364\t2025-06-24T10:47:22.312Z\t405\tValid\tEditable\tThe potential reintroduction of Dicranum Undulatum to Cors Fochno: an assessment of future climate suitability\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333634\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FDicranum%20undulatum%20Climate%20Modelling\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Bryophytes, Lichens and Fungi, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tDicranum undulatum is categorised as ‘Endangered’ on the Red Data List for Wales, and is considered to be of principal importance for conservation under Section 7 of The Environment (Wales) Act 2016. Due to the widespread loss and degradation of peatlands, it has undergone a large decline in Britain, and the only surviving population in Wales occurs in Denbighshire, on Whixall and Bettisfield Mosses. The species was known from Cors Fochno, where it was last seen in 1967. Reasons for the extinction are uncertain, but former fires may be partly to blame. Following significant efforts to improve the habitat condition of the bog, including the elimination of fires and large-scale hydrological restoration, a recent assessment concluded that parts of the site appear to contain favourable habitat for D. undulatum and that reintroduction may be a possibility.   The aim of this study is to investigate whether a suitable macroclimate for the species is likely to occur at Cors Fochno in the medium-term.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-06-24\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T15:14:51.258Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20968\tNRW_DS161365\t2025-06-24T13:24:50.634Z\t330\tValid\tEditable\tMonitoring of the Atlantic woodland bryophyte assemblage of Coed Crafnant, Meirionnydd\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333635\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FCoed%20Crafnant%20Bryophyte%20Monitoring%202025\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Bryophytes, Lichens \u0026amp; Fungi, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tCoed Crafnant is a nature reserve owned and managed by the North Wales Wildlife Trust, comprising part of that woodland plus the contiguous Coed Dolbebin. Almost all of the oceanic woodland habitat of the reserve is contained within Rhinog SSSI and Meirionnydd Oakwoods SAC.   The first comprehensive bryophyte survey of the reserve was undertaken during January 2014, when baseline monitoring data was collected. A further survey was undertaken in May 2022, which recorded areas of the reserve not covered in 2014, but did not repeat the species grid-mapping exercise of 2014 due to time constraints. This 2025 study aimed to repeat the grid-mapping in order to assess what changes have occurred within the Atlantic woodland bryophyte assemblage.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-06-24\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-03T10:35:04.120Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n21005\tNRW_DS161366\t2025-06-30T13:55:17.204Z\t471\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Marine Benthic Invertebrates - Geospatial\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333636\tX/monitoring/Kieco\tBianca Bacchi\tThis data collates all the benthic invertebrate samples collected within Wales from 1988 onwards by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales under the Water Framework Directive (WFD) and Clean Seas Environment Monitoring Program (CSEMP) sampling programs. The dataset contains the presence, and abundance, of benthic invertebrate species at specific marine monitoring points held within the KiECO database.  The data was collected using either a  Day Grab (0.1 m2) or Hammon Grab (0.1m2) for subtidal samples and either 3 x 0.01m2 cores (Core Diameter 11.28cm) or a Box Corer (0.1m2) for intertidal surveys. Data are laboratory assessed and quality assured following the National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control (NMBAQC) scheme.  For many of our marine survey techniques we record an NGR at the site because this acts as a central/representative location around which we collect lots of samples. An NGR is then also recorded against each of the samples to give a more accurate sampling location.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-07-10-2026-02-24\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6727/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_marine_macroinvertebrates\t2026-02-25T10:31:02.342Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n21006\tNRW_DS161367\t2025-07-01T09:00:33.690Z\t219\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwater Cycle 3 - Chemical and Quantitative\t© CNC/NRW and British Geological Society (BGS). Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333637\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Groundwater Cycle 3 2021 Chemical.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Groundwater Cycle 3 2021 Quantitative.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwater Cycle 3 Chemical and Quantitative datasets are a subset of Water Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwaterbodies Cycle 3, which is already published on DataMapWales.  Article 2, clause 2 of the WFD defines groundwaterbodies as \u0027…all water which is below the surface of the ground in the saturation zone and in direct contact with the ground or subsoil\u0027.   For the purposes of reporting under the WFD, a groundwater body represents a distinct body of groundwater flow with a coherent flow unit including recharge and discharge areas with little flow across the boundaries. These reflect hydrogeological characteristics containing information on flow and stage properties, recharge and vulnerability to pollution. This has been undertaken through defining aquifers into different types and broken into catchment units at Catchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) scale.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-19\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:20.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n21017\tNRW_DS161368\t2025-07-03T08:05:19.466Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGeomorphological Surveys to Support the Upper Wye Restoration Project\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333638\tY:\\Geomorphology South\\Upper Wye Restoration\\OUTPUTS \u0026amp; Y:\\Geomorphology South\\Upper Wye Restoration\\OUTPUTS_rnd2\tDale Cox - Specialist Advisor, Geomorphology\tThis dataset contains GIS outputs from geomorphological assessments conducted by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) in 2023–2024 for the Upper Wye Catchment Restoration Project, funded under the Nature and Climate Emergency (NACE) programme. The surveys recorded physical river characteristics to identify restoration opportunities, focusing on erosion, barriers, bank protection, and floodplain conditions. Data were collected through desk-based studies, field surveys, and analysis, and include reach surveys (polygon) and point-based features with assigned priority scores. Interpretation should consider survey limitations, including temporal changes and site-specific variability. Full methodology and prioritisation criteria are detailed in the Geomorphological Surveys of the Ithon and Aran Rivers for the Upper Wye Restoration Project report.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-07-03\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:20.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n21166\tNRW_DS161369\t2025-08-01T08:17:18.303Z\t10\tValid\tEditable\tBroadscale coastal rollback constraints and opportunity\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333639\tY:\\Marine\\Rollback_from_Coastal_squeeze\\Rollback_data_report_859.gdb  awaiting X:Drive load\tIain Fairley - Specialist advisor: marine and coastal physical science\tThis dataset is an ArcGIS geodatabase which provides layers showing lengths of coastline where there are constraints to or opportunities for coastal habitat rollback. The three habitats considered are: saltmarsh, sand dune and shingle.  Lengths are also given for constraints and opportunity within urban areas.   The data was developed from a previous project on coastal squeeze. It provides a high level assessment of coastal rollback to inform future, more detailed, work. The study is detailed in: Fairley, I., 2025. A high-level appraisal of areas of constraint and areas of opportunity for coastal habitat roll-back. NRW Evidence Report No: 859, 71pp.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-08-01\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-08-05T14:48:38.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n21189\tNRW_DS161370\t2025-08-06T08:02:27.330Z\t113\tValid\tEditable\tNutrient Sensitive Marine SAC Freshwater Catchments\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Crown Copyright: Welsh Government. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333730\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine SAC freshwater catchments\\Nutrient sensitive Marine SAC freshwater catchments.lyr\tLucie Skates, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine Planning Implementation\tThe Nutrient sensitive Marine SAC freshwater catchments layer depicts the surface water freshwater catchments that discharge to Marine SAC’s supporting habitat features particularly sensitive to nutrient enrichment.   Catchment polygons are split by Special Area of Conservation and receiving waterbody (Water Framework Directive Regulations, Transitional or Coastal waterbody).  Please also see metadata number NRW_DS161371 \"Marine SAC freshwater catchments requiring nitrogen neutrality\" which is related.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-07-31\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-24T13:27:16.321Z\tMatthew Wareham\t-\tPrivate record\n21190\tNRW_DS161371\t2025-08-06T08:22:07.851Z\t656\tValid\tEditable\tMarine SAC Freshwater Catchments Requiring Nitrogen Neutrality\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Crown Copyright: Welsh Government. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333731\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine SAC freshwater catchments\\Marine SAC freshwater catchments requiring nitrogen neutrality.lyr\tLucie Skates, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine Planning Implementation\tThe Marine SAC Freshwater Catchments Requiring Nitrogen Neutrality layer is a subset of the Nutrient Sensitive Marine SAC Freshwater Catchments layer.  It only shows the three freshwater catchments that require nitrogen neutrality based on NRW’s advice letter of 25th July 2025 and maybe subject to future changes.  Please also see metadata entry NRW_DS161370 \"Nutrient Sensitive Marine SAC Freshwater Catchments\".\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-07-31-2025-09-08\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/7750/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_marice_sac_req_nit_neut\t2025-09-09T15:13:56.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n21202\tNRW_DS161372\t2025-08-07T11:53:17.098Z\t249\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Marine Ecology: Marine Benthic Invertebrates\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333732\tRaw Data extracted from WISKI and final outputs can be found here - Z:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\Data Mgmt\\Open Data Extractions\\NBN Extractions\\NRW_DS xxxxxx Marine Benthic Invertebrates\tBianca Bacchi\tThis data collates all the benthic invertebrate samples collected within Wales from 1988 onwards by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales under the Water Framework Directive (WFD) and Clean Seas Environment Monitoring Program (CSEMP) sampling programs. The dataset contains the presence, and abundance, of benthic invertebrate species at specific marine monitoring points held within the KiECO database.  The data was collected using either a Day Grab (0.1 m2) or Hammon Grab (0.1m2) for subtidal samples and either 3 x 0.01m2 cores (Core Diameter 11.28cm) or a Box Corer (0.1m2) for intertidal surveys. Data are laboratory assessed and quality assured following the National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control (NMBAQC) scheme.  For many of our marine survey techniques we record an NGR at the site because this acts as a central/representative location around which we collect lots of samples. An NGR is then also recorded against each of the samples to give a more accurate sampling location.  For the geospatial version of this see NRW_DS161366.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-08-07\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-06T10:32:56.422Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n21204\tNRW_DS161373\t2025-08-07T12:09:51.063Z\t250\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2021 - 2026 Data for New National Park Proposals\tPlease review the individual child datasets for any constraints\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333733\tGIS Locations are available in the child metadata records for exact locations.\tAlex Hardie-Brown - Geospatial Analysis Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales is the statutory designating authority for new National Parks and National Landscapes in Wales.  Natural Resources Wales has been commissioned by Welsh Government to assess the case for a new National Park focussed on the existing Clwydian Range and Dee Valley National Landscape.  During the designation and investigation into this numerous datasets have been created in various formats and users should look to child metadata records listed below.  NRW_DS161374 - Proposed Boundary for a New National Park September 2025 NRW_DS161375 - Area of Search for a New National Park in Wales - June 2023  NRW_DS161376 - Candidate Area for a New National Park in Wales October 2024 NRW_DS161377- Evaluation Areas for a New National Park in Wales September 2024 NRW_DS161378 - Area Evaluated for Natural Beauty and Recreation - September 2024\t\t2025-08-07\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-10-31T15:35:00.473Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n21205\tNRW_DS161374\t2025-08-07T13:49:44.982Z\t419\tValid\tEditable\tProposed Boundary for a New National Park September 2025\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333734\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Proposed National Park.lyr\tAlex Hardie-Brown - Geospatial Analysis Advisor\tA GIS polygon dataset showing the Proposed Boundary for a new National Park in Wales. This is the fifth dataset in a series that supports the new National Park in Wales project. This is the proposed boundary for a new National Park in Wales for statutory consultation purposes in late 2025. It is the summation of the evidence gathering, evaluation and public consultation project steps. OS MasterMap, SSSI, National Park, National Landscape, Wales England Border and Ancient Woodland boundaries alongside Ordnance Survey base mapping have been used to create this boundary.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-09-07\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n21206\tNRW_DS161375\t2025-08-07T13:56:52.979Z\t245\tValid\tEditable\tArea of Search for a New National Park in Wales - June 2023\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333735\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Proposed National Park\\Area of Search for a new National Park in Wales - June 2023.lyr\tAlex Hardie-Brown, Geospatial Analysis Advisor\tThis dataset is a GIS polygon showing the boundary of the Area of Search for a new National Park in Wales. This dataset is the first of several that contribute to the evolution of the National Park project.   The boundary was drawn to an adequate level of detail to provide the basis for evidence gathering. The boundary was guided by a range of datasets and Ordnance Survey base mapping.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-08-06\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-02T14:38:21.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n21207\tNRW_DS161376\t2025-08-07T13:57:34.915Z\t227\tValid\tEditable\tCandidate Area for a New National Park in Wales October 2024\t© CNC/NRW, © Ordnance Survey AC0000849444, © Sustrans Cymru, © Crown Copyright: Welsh Government. © Local Authority\u0027s © Cadw. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333736\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Proposed National Park\\Candidate Area for a New National Park in Wales - October 2024.lyr\tAlex Hardie-Brown - Geospatial Analysis Advisor\tA GIS dataset showing the boundary of the Candidate Area for a new National Park in Wales. This is the fourth data set in a series that supports the new National Park in Wales project. The Candidate Area is considered to meet the Statutory Criteria by the Gillespies Evaluation report and formed the basis of public consultation in Oct- Dec 2024.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-08-07\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:52:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n21209\tNRW_DS161377\t2025-08-07T14:19:25.040Z\t208\tValid\tEditable\tEvaluation Areas for a New National Park in Wales September 2024\t© CNC/NRW, © Ordnance Survey AC0000849444, © Sustrans Cymru, © Crown Copyright: Welsh Government. © Local Authority\u0027s © Cadw. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333737\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Proposed National Park\\Evaluation Areas for a new National Park in Wales - September 2024.lyr\tAlex Hardie-Brown - Geospatial Analysis Advisor\tA GIS dataset showing the boundaries of the Evaluation Areas for a new National Park in Wales. This is the third data set in a series that supports the new National Park in Wales project.   The Evaluation Areas are subdivisions of the Area Evaluated for Natural Beauty and Recreation. The identification of the Evaluation Areas is primarily based on the LANDMAP Visual and Sensory aspect layer and the associated level 3 typology e.g. Hills, Lower Plateau \u0026amp; Scarp Slopes. As such, nearly all Evaluation Area boundaries within the Area of Search follow the exact Visual and Sensory aspect layer boundaries. In most instances, however, the EAs are formed of several aspect areas. In addition to the LANDMAP data, the identification of Evaluation Areas has considered the indicative boundaries of the National Character Areas, and at a local level, the mapping of local landscape character areas from a range of published landscape character assessments. As far as possible, the Evaluation Area mapping has also considered broad patterns of aspect area values.   Where feasible, the mapping of the Evaluation Areas reflects the boundary of the National Landscape. Although where this splits an aspect area, subdivisions have been made where a relatively extensive area of non-National Landscape exists.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-08-07\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:52:09.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n21210\tNRW_DS161378\t2025-08-07T14:32:40.330Z\t136\tUnassessed\tEditable\tArea Evaluated for Natural Beauty and Recreation - September 2024\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333738\tAwaiting X:Drive data load\tAlex Hardie-Brown, Geospatial Analysis Advisor\tThis dataset is a GIS polygon showing the boundary of the Area Evaluated for Natural Beauty and Recreation, to support the new National Park in Wales project.  This is the second data set in a series that supports the new National Park in Wales project.  It is the area that consultants used to focus their assessment work.    The boundary was informed by LANDMAP, other environmental data and Ordnance Survey base mapping. It was drawn to a sufficient level of detail for assessment work purposes.    Please be aware that the boundary has been drawn at a scale to show the general outline rather a precise boundary. Therefore, this dataset should not be used for analysis on a small scale to avoid inaccurate results and impact any interpretation.     It is important to note that the Area Evaluated for Natural Beauty and Recreation is not the final boundary and could be subject to change. It has no legal status and is not a material consideration.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-08-06\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-09-05T10:57:12.193Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n21211\tNRW_DS161379\t2025-08-08T09:17:57.767Z\t286\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Marine Ecology: Marine Phytoplankton - Taxa Data\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333739\tData is stored on WISKI database.   Copies of Darwin Core Species Data is stored here - Z:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\Data Mgmt\\Data Services Data Publishing Area\\NBN Data\\NRW_DS161379_MarinePhytoplankton\\Marine Phyt Darwin Core.zip\tBianca Bacchi, Senior Marine Monitoring Officer\tThis data collates all the Phytoplankton samples collected within Wales from 2007 onwards by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales that are held within NRWs KiEco Database. This methodology is based on the principle that additional nutrients (especially nitrogen) can alter the amount of phytoplankton in transitional and coastal waters. Persistent and high counts of phytoplankton taxa are also seen as a measure of disturbance in the phytoplankton community. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring for legislative requirements and to meet organisational drivers. In addition, sites may be sampled for investigative work, or on an ad hoc basis.  Phytoplankton samples are taken in situ and are usually sent to a specialist marine contractor for Species Identity and abundance (two labs have been used in this time period; CEFAS 2007 – 2009 \u0026amp; APEM 2010 – onwards). Phytoplankton samples are analysed (identified and counted) using the Utermöhl method. Further information is available from the UK-TAG WFD website.  For many of our marine survey techniques we record an NGR at the site because this acts as a central/representative location around which we collect lots of samples. An NGR is then also recorded against each of the samples to give a more accurate sampling location.  A geospatial version of this data is available in NRW_DS124916\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-08-05-2025-09-08\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr3643\t2025-10-29T14:11:11.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n21271\tNRW_DS161380\t2025-08-19T18:00:43.723Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey for Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior at MoD Pendine in September 2024\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333830\t\t\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-08-19T18:00:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n21272\tNRW_DS161381\t2025-08-20T07:45:43.078Z\t356\tValid\tEditable\tNature and Climate Emergencies (NaCE) Water Quality Capital\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333831\tX:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\Water Quality Capital 2022-2023.lyr X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\Water Quality Capital 2023-2024.lyr X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\Water Quality Capital 2024-2025.lyr\tRhodri Powell - Specialist Advisor, River Restoration Programme\tThis dataset contains delivery data collected from project managers to monitor the progress and benefits of projects completed under the Water Capital Programme. The data records project level information for reporting and evaluation purposes and is intended to support programme monitoring. It should be noted that the dataset reflects information reported by project managers and may be subject to limitations in accuracy or completeness.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-01-19\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-23T12:34:18.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n21282\tNRW_DS161382\t2025-08-21T12:42:15.655Z\t39\tValid\tEditable\tMapping the Role of Coastal Habitats in Mitigating Flood Risk\t© CNC/NRW. © Middlesex University, Flood Hazard Research Centre. © JNCC. © Crown Copyright: Welsh Government. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333832\tExcel file on the DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/coast/ICMP/NBS/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2Fcoast%2FICMP%2FNBS%2FCoastal%20habitats%20%26%20flood%20risk%20summary%20project%2FSpatial%20mapping%20project%20ABP%20Mer%20%26%20Eftec%2FFinal%20documents\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120009401B6C442DD114B981063BE9AE8D618\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B29BB2E32%2D370E%2D46F5%2DADF6%2DE7700E86C3FC%7D GIS data on the X Drive: X:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Habitats Flood Risk Benefits\tIain Fairley, Specialist Advisor: Marine and Coastal Physical Science\tThis data resource contains four GIS layers and a MS Excel workbook that make up the deliverables associated with NRW evidence report 935.   The four GIS layers are: coastal units (Coastal_Units_25062025), dunes habitats per coastal unit (CU_Dunes_22052025), saltmarsh habitats per coastal unit (CU_Saltmarsh_22052025) and shingle habitats per coastal unit (CU_Shingle_29052025).   Coastal Units (CUs) were defined by grouping deliverables from a Coastal Squeeze project (Accommodation Spaces, their associated Assessment Units and Foreshore Areas) together. The coastal units were the unit of assessment for determining the flood risk benefit of coastal habitats around the Welsh coastline.    The sand dune, shingle, and saltmarsh habitats were clipped to each CU and their Functional Value assessed. These are provided in the relevant layers.   The excel file (NCA workbook of flood protection from coastal habitats in Wales_Final) provides a natural capital assessment of the various habitats, which feeds into the values in the coastal unit layer.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-08-18\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-20T10:37:22.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n21284\tNRW_DS161383\t2025-08-21T15:30:17.278Z\t23\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTarenig Welsh Government Woodland Estate Water Management Strategy\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333833\tX:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Catch Pits.lyr X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Clear Catch Pit X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Clinometer Result X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Culvert X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Culvert 1 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Culvert Replacement X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Deposited Roadside Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Eradicate INNS X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Excavated Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Forest Drains X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Forest Roads X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig General Details X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Improve Placement of Roadside Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig INNS X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Photos X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Relief Culverts X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Remove Excavated Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Riparian Zone X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sediment Sink X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sediment Source X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Watercourses X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig WMS Opportunity Delivery Plan X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Bank Lowering X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Bund X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Extra Channels Identified on Site X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Flow Accumulator X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Flow Accumulator Generated Channels X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Forest Drains 1 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Large Woody Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Large Woody Material Placement X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig River Network X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Road Drain X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Scrapes X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Areas Accessed X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Increase Riparian Vegetation X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Lack of Riparian Vegetation X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig NRW Identified Areas of Interest X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Observed Forest Roads X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 1 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 2 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 3 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 4 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 5 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 6 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 7 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 8 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 9 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 10 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 11 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 12\tFrancesca Sanchez, Freshwater Habitat and Species Specialist Advisor\tJBA Consulting was commissioned by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) to undertake a study of a Welsh Government Woodland Estate (WGWE) block, Tarenig in Powys, to investigate existing geomorphological and ecological functioning across the study area in order to identify a range of Integrated Catchment Management interventions.   JBA have developed a Water Management Strategy (WMS) and corresponding Delivery Plan (DP) for Tarenig Forest, presented in an evidence report and accompanying GIS outputs.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-08-19\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-24T15:04:23.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n21287\tNRW_DS161384\t2025-08-22T09:37:08.656Z\t26\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAberhirnant Welsh Government Woodland Estate Water Management Strategy\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333834\tX:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Add Bales or Geomembrane X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Add Boulder X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Add Catchpit X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Add Heather Bundles X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Bridges X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Catch Pit X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Clear Catchpit X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Clinometer Results X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Culvert X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Culvert Replacement X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Deposited Roadside Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Eradicate INNS X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Excavated Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Forestry Team Identified Priority Sites X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant INNS X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Improve Placement of Roadside Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Install Root Wads X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Relief Culvert X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Remove Excavated Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Sediment Sink X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Sediment Source X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant WMS Opportunity Delivery Plan X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Add Large Woody Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Bank Lowering X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Bank Modification X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Embankment X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Extra Channels Identified on Site X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Flow Accumulator Channels X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Forest Drains X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Geomembrane X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Large Woody Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Micro Bund X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant River Network X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Road Drain X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Agricultural Management Pressures X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Areas Accessed X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Increase Riparian Vegetation X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Lack of Riparian Vegetation X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Road Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Sub Catchment 1 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Sub Catchment 2 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Sub Catchment 3 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Sub Catchment 4 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Sub Catchment 5\tFrancesca Sanchez, Freshwater Habitat and Species Specialist Advisor\tJBA Consulting was commissioned by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) to undertake a study of a Welsh Government Woodland Estate (WGWE) block, Aberhirnant in Gwynedd, to investigate existing geomorphological and ecological functioning across the study area in order to identify a range of Integrated Catchment Management interventions.   JBA have developed a Water Management Strategy (WMS) and corresponding Delivery Plan (DP) for Aberhirnant Forest, presented in an evidence report and accompanying GIS outputs.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-08-19\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-22T15:49:39.000Z\tGary Eccleston\t-\tPrivate record\n21304\tNRW_DS161385\t2025-08-26T14:58:47.863Z\t141\tValid\tEditable\tCommunities at Risk Register 2025 – Present Day (CaRR)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333835\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tThe Communities at Risk Register (CaRR) has been developed to provide an objective means of identifying risk and prioritising flood risk management activities at a Wales-wide, community level. It applies a standard methodology across all flood sources to calculate a theoretical ‘danger score’ that allows comparative risks to be quantified and ranked (from High to Low).    The CaRR comprises a spreadsheet that identifies and ranks individual communities for,  1. a natural, ‘undefended’ scenario, and  2. a managed scenario (based on the presence of defences and flood warning).   It is complemented by a GIS layer which defines the individual Community polygons and is attributed with danger scores, community rankings and other metric information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-08-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-23T09:39:57.246Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n21306\tNRW_DS161386\t2025-08-28T12:43:47.563Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey to determine the status and distribution of Geyer\u0027s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Waun Eurad SSSI, its last known Welsh locality, in November 2021\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333836\t\tMichael Howe\tWaiting for final metadata report from Michael Howe\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-08-28T12:44:27.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n21308\tNRW_DS161387\t2025-09-02T12:44:16.223Z\t265\tValid\tEditable\tRiver Restoration Habitats Index\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333837\tX:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\River Restoration Plans.lyr  \tRhodri Powell - Specialist Advisor, River Restoration Programme\tThe resource is a summary of existing NRW \u0027River restoration plans\u0027, with detail of the Water Framework Directive waterbody ID and name with internal links to view the plans. This will be a feature layer to display where the existing restoration plans are in Wales. There are no limits or caveats to the use or interpretation of the data.  Metadata for the restoration plans in detail can be found at NRW_DS125313.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-09-02-2025-10-30\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/8060/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_rrp_index\t2025-11-19T10:13:14.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n21310\tNRW_DS161388\t2025-09-02T12:59:14.627Z\t206\tValid\tEditable\tFisheries Habitat Restoration Plans\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333838\tX:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\Fisheries Habitat Restoration Plans\tRhodri Powell - Specialist Advisor, River Restoration Programme\tThis is a GIS layer that displays where all the NRW \u0027Fisheries Habitat Restoration Plans\u0027 are across Wales. All the information has been extracted from within the plans and it is presented as a feature layer.   These plans were created to serve as a tool to generate work programmes for individual rivers trusts and are linked to the Inland Fisheries Habitat Restoration Grant. Since they were commissioned, the trusts have added to their own knowledge base from further surveys they have carried out.   There are no limits or caveats regarding the use or interpretation of the redacted data.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-09-02-2025-10-06\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/7891/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_fhrp_catchments\t2025-11-19T10:15:49.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n21312\tNRW_DS161389\t2025-09-03T10:06:40.035Z\t275\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSustainable Fisheries Programmes - Post 2011\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333839\tAwaiting X:Drive Load\tRhodri Powell - Specialist Advisor, River Restoration Programme\tThis is a feature layer displaying the works completed by NRW since 2011 to now, reported by the Fisheries Officers. All data has been extracted from the \u0027SFP Project Master Ledger\u0027 and work type has been categorised into \u0027habitat restoration\u0027 type, according to the River Restoration Portal categories.  a layer containing data on pre 2011 programmes can be found in NRW_DS161390\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-09-03\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-09-03T13:28:34.193Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n21313\tNRW_DS161390\t2025-09-03T12:28:33.318Z\t184\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSustainable Fisheries Programmes - Pre 2011\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333930\tWaiting X:Drive load\tRhodri Powell - Specialist Advisor, River Restoration Programme\tThis feature layer will display what project works have been completed across Wales before 2011 in accordance with the Sustainable Fisheries Programme.   There is also a post 2011 dataset - NRW_DS161389. The two datasets were split due to a difference in data quality and standard.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-09-03\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-09-03T13:28:22.836Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n21427\tNRW_DS161393\t2025-09-19T11:10:02.131Z\t373\tValid\tEditable\tSites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) Review Data 2025\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333933\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/Regulatory/psdp/sdsg/SSSI%20Guidance%20and%20Advice/SSSI%20Review%20(Deep%20Dive)/Data%20and%20evidence/Data%20Archive/SSSI%20Review%20Data%20Archive.xlsx?d\u003dwf02c76c3205b4f45bbabd37176c3336e\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\tHolly York, Specialist Advisor Protected Sites Programme\tThe SSSI review data (an excel spreadsheet) contains detailed information which forms the basis of tables and charts presented in the report \u0027Edwards C., York H. 2025. Sites of Special Scientific Interest: a Review of the Current Series in Wales. NRW Evidence Report No: 878, 82pp, Natural Resources Wales\u0027.   It includes data about each notified SSSI in Wales (at the point of February 2025), such as the site type, the area and the length. It contains information on the notified features (species, habitats, geology) and the SSSI\u0027s impacted by habitat fragmentation.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-09-17\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T17:23:06.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n21428\tNRW_DS161394\t2025-09-19T11:38:59.456Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tData related to the construction of the A55 Conwy Tunnel 1984 - 1991 (paper data records)\t© CNC/NRW Subject to a full assessment, data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333934\tPaper records\tHuw Jones, Environment Team North Wales\tThese paper data records are in relation to the construction of the A55 Conwy tunnel 1984 - 1991 and include:   - Conwy Estuary Beach Survey data  - Sediment data  - Welsh Water data and surveys  - Beach analysis  These records are to be reviewed in 10 years.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-09-19\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-09-19T11:51:12.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n21434\tNRW_DS161395\t2025-09-23T14:20:52.060Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey for the Foaming Fern-borer Sawfly Blasticotoma filiceti on Cors Graianog SSSI in August 2025\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333935\tWID\tMichael Howe\tTo be submitted by M.Howe when report is done\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-10-10T07:50:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n21500\tNRW_DS161396\t2025-10-02T15:24:51.364Z\t141\tValid\tEditable\tLowland Grassland NRW Commissioned Surveys\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333936\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Other Grassland\\PH2 Other Grassland Sites.lyr X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Other Grassland\\PH2 Other Grassland Veg.lyr X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Other Grassland\\PH2 Other Grassland Mospolys.lyr X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Other Grassland\\PH2 Other Grassland Notes point.lyr X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Other Grassland\\PH2 Other Grassland Quadrats.lyr X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Other Grassland\\PH2_Other_Grassland_Mosaic.dbf\tStuart Smith, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThese data are from detailed surveys commissioned by NRW of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation across Wales, usually using the National Vegetation Classification.   The data are intended as an addition to or an update of existing detailed in-house grassland survey spatial data.  The survey locations and sites may vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha, to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-08-14-2025-12-08\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-20T10:32:33.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n21675\tNRW_DS161397\t2025-10-16T15:24:09.846Z\t20\tValid\tEditable\tWales Fluvial Flow Database (WFFD)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333937\tOriginal data is stored with FRAW (2019) project deliverables. Contact National Flood Risk Analysis team for details.  Copy of core deliverables can be accessed through X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Hydrology\\Wales Fluvial Flow Database WFFD 2019.lyr\tSimon James, Lead Specialist Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group\tRiver peak flow estimates and hydrographs used in Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW, 2019) and covering watercourses with a catchment area greater than 3km2.  Estimates created at regular intervals along a watercourse and stored in an ESRI Arc file geodatabase. At each interval (node) a variety of information is provided that includes:   - catchment descriptors extracted from FEH.   - donor observation gauges used within the flow estimation calculations.   - the donor observation gauges used within the flow estimation calculations.   - the time to peak and storm duration for the hydrographs and the calculated peak flows and hydrographs for each return period (both from the statistical and hybrid methodologies).   - information about the quality control checks carried out on each point, a decision log outlining any changes taken on each WFFD point before use in the FRAW project, and the final modelled inflow polygons used in the FRAW fluvial hydraulic models.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-10-16\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-23T14:34:25.000Z\tMatthew Wareham\t-\tPrivate record\n21703\tNRW_DS161398\t2025-10-21T14:13:54.013Z\t294\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBryophyte Lichen and Fungus GIS Data\tData may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333938\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Bryophyte Lichen HEP Sensitive.lyr,  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Bryophytes Oceanic Scoring Species.lyr, X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Grassland Fungi Site Diversity.lyr, X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Lichens Woodland Index SOWI URI Pinhead.lyr\tSam Bosanquet: Specialist Advisor: Bryophytes, Lichens \u0026amp; Fungi\tPoint data showing the location of conservation important mosses, liverworts, lichens and fungi across Wales.  The lichen records relate to species growing on trees; the fungi are species found in grasslands; the bryophytes are mostly found in rainforest habitats.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-10-21\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-10-22T11:22:38.024Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n21750\tNRW_DS161399\t2025-10-24T13:59:32.424Z\t155\tValid\tEditable\tReview of Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales MG5 data with great burnet\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333939\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/lg/Projects/FMP%20-%20Review%20of%20MG5%20Data%202025\tStuart Smith, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThis project reviewed data from the Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales against the new MG4 subcommunities to determine if any stands of MG5 containing great burnet could be reclassified as drier expressions of MG4 under the new definitions and, if appropriate, to update the existing inventory of MG4 to help inform future maintenance and restoration of the community in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-10-24\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T13:52:40.688Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n21851\tNRW_DS161402\t2025-11-04T15:21:50.120Z\t21\tValid\tEditable\tSalmonid Fry and Parr Density data\t© CNC/NRW. NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343032\tZ:\\Processing\\Sampling_Monitoring_\u0026amp;_Analysis\\Monitoring Analysis\\Richard - Salmonid Stocks Review\\New Code Attempts.    The name of the dataset file is \"FInal_salmon_meta_data.csv\", the R scripts associated with the data should be stored alongside.    This metadata entry is not set for public view because of the comments in the lineage and additional information.\tRichard Lewis, Specialist Advisor Monitoring Analysis\tThis is a dataset of salmonid fry and parr numbers in Welsh rivers from 2000. It includes individual rivers and catchments, temperature data, flood and flow information, and salmon densities during different lifestages.  The data were collated from internal NRW fisheries survey data, externally acquired earth observation daily mean air temperature data, and internal flow data. The data were not mapped, but are collated from NRW\u0027s regular fisheries monitoring. The data were collated with the aim of undertaking Bayesian analysis to see what environmental factors have been impacting salmonid populations within Welsh rivers and whether driving factors could be elucidated.   Unfortunately the project was shelved during \u0027Case for Change\u0027 due to EPP priority realignment. The aim is to have the data stored safely in archive in case the project may be revisited in future.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-10-27\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:46:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n21855\tNRW_DS161403\t2025-11-04T16:36:40.400Z\t132\tValid\tEditable\tTimber Planning System Coupes\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343033\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Timber Planning System Coupes\tGuy Bennett, Estate Programmer\tThis data comes from the Timber Planning System. The shapes are extracted from the TPS database using FME and transferred to the X drive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-10-29\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T10:05:50.108Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n21857\tNRW_DS161404\t2025-11-04T17:03:47.719Z\t18\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRegulatory Woodland Support Officers Area Boundaries\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343034\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Regulatory Woodland Support Officer Areas.lyr\tNick Fackrell, Senior Officer, Forest Regulation and Tree Plant Health, Forestry Regulations and Tree Health\tThis data shows a map of Wales split into three sections (North, Mid, South) with the Regulatory Woodland Support Officer (RWSO) responsible for each area.   RWSO investigate alleged illegal felling reports and carry out technical surveys for Forest Regulations team related to felling licences and environmental impact assessments.\t\t2025-10-31-2025-11-17\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T08:57:09.000Z\tAndrew Williams\t-\tPrivate record\n22021\tNRW_DS161406\t2025-11-12T16:25:49.340Z\t143\tValid\tEditable\tNational Vegetation Classification survey of Welsh coastal shingle sites\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343036\tY:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Coastal Habitats\\Shingle_NVC_2022.gdb\tJulie Creer Lead Specialist Advisor - Coastal Habitats, Evidence and Analysis\tData from a National Vegetation Classification survey and Shingle classification (Sneddon and Randall methodology) of 52 vegetated shingle sites, including shapefiles of NVC polygons, vegetation mosaics, target notes, quadrats, photograph locations and overall survey boundary.   Data collected in 2022 based on field survey of entire sites with field maps digitised to OS Mastermap and aerial photographs.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-11-06\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T12:02:54.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n22036\tNRW_DS161407\t2025-11-12T16:54:24.093Z\t32\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Annex 1 Habitat Maps (Reg9a 2024)\t© CNC/NRW and ??? Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343037\tX:\\Biological\\Reg 9a\\Marine  This metadata form covers all of the marine polygon, point and line Annex 1 habitat datasets produced for Reg9a reporting in 2024 (i.e. multiple feature classes within a gdb). The datasets cover the intertidal, inshore and offshore marine areas across the whole of Wales (up to the mid-line in the Irish Sea).\tKaren Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine Habitats, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning\tThe marine Annex 1 habitat map layers have been created by collating records from numerous surveys, monitoring programmes, casework, expert knowledge and third party providers.   The map layers contain points, polygons or centroids lines depending on the habitat type. The layers are updated when new records are produced as part of the 6-yearly reporting cycle (previously under Article 17 and now Reg9a of the UK Habitats Regulations).   The original surveys and monitoring events from which the records have been extracted vary widely in dates going back many decades in some cases (surveys were undertaken between 1899 - 2024).   This data consists of 15 dataset layers covering known occurrences of Annex 1 marine habitats across the Welsh marine area in the intertidal, inshore sub-tidal (up to 12nm) and offshore subtidal (12nm to the mid-line).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-11-07\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-19T15:11:09.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22155\tNRW_DS161408\t2025-11-20T10:00:49.300Z\t29\tUnassessed\tEditable\tZostera Marina Habitat Suitability\t© CNC/NRW and ? Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343038\tX:\\Biological\\Habitat Restoration\\Marine Habitat\\Zostera marina habitat suitability JNCC model values.lyr X:\\Biological\\Habitat Restoration\\Marine Habitat\\Zostera marina habitat suitability map source.lyr  The detailed processing notes can be found here: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/teams/advice/mar/mar-pap/Marine%20SMNR/Ecosystem%20Resilience/Restoration/Opportunity%20maps/NRW%20Zostera%20marina%20habitat%20suitability%202024v2.docx?d\u003dwef2989cf2dd74ff094b4d8c2886de081\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;e\u003d7HRKkj\tNatasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology\tThis data is the creation of a map showing areas that are suitable for the predominantly subtidal seagrass species Zostera marina are needed to provide advice on where NRW would potentially support restoration or creation projects for Zostera marina.   This map includes a combination of mapped areas of Zostera marina, habitat suitability model outputs and locations of suitable mapped habitat along the coast where the modelled outputs are absent. The data includes outputs from the JNCC Zostera marina habitat suitability model (Castle et al., 2022), Bertelli, C. Zostera habitat suitability (Bertelli et al., 2022), extracted data from JNCC\u0027s UKASH and NRW\u0027s section 7/OSPAR data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-11-19-2025-11-20\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-10T16:24:01.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22182\tNRW_DS161409\t2025-11-25T12:20:15.735Z\t16\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTerrestrial Priority Habitats\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343039\tX:\\Biological\\Priority Habitats\\Terrestrial Priority Habitats\tKathryn Birch, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThis is a spatial dataset of terrestrial Priority Habitats as listed under Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2016.   This dataset is derived from several existing NRW spatial datasets: Phase 1 Terrestrial Habitat Survey (1987-1997), Phase 2 (National Vegetation Classification) surveys covering peatland, grassland, heathland, woodland, saltmarsh and sand dunes, Priority Habitat Site Surveys (terrestrial) and current Ancient Woodland Inventory. These datasets have been amalgamated into a single dataset.   Where there is more than one dataset in an area e.g. Phase 1 and Phase 2, the latest or most accurate dataset has been used to provide the most up-to-date Wales-wide distribution of Priority Habitats.   The data will be updated at regular intervals when new habitat information becomes available. Only survey data that have been extensively validated and verified by NRW habitat and cartographic specialists will be added to the dataset.   Habitat composition may change over time, for example due to successional trends, land management or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type or species in the digital data is therefore no guarantee of it still being extant on the ground. The data does contain ‘Year’ and ‘Data source’ fields so users will know how old the data is and thus how accurate it is likely to be.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-11-24\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-11-25T12:42:21.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22245\tNRW_DS161414\t2025-12-01T14:06:03.638Z\t45\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNative Oyster Potential Restoration Areas\tData may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343134\tRedacted data: X:\\Biological\\Habitat Restoration\\Marine Habitat\\ABPMer and NRW native oyster Ostrea edulis potential habitat.lyr.   Unredacted data:\tNatasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology. Ben Wray, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology\tThe dataset has been developed to focus oyster restoration areas where the conditions are suitable. It has been developed as part of the Wales Native Oyster Action Plan.   The dataset builds upon work carried out by ABPMer for NRW with additional work by NRW. It is a composite dataset of suitable physical parameters and suitable habitat for oysters to live. This includes current energy \u0026lt;130nm-2, water depths of under 80m and subtidal mixed, coarse or macrophyte dominated sediments. Inshore areas were added where the model input layers were too coarse and the areas were considered suitable by NRW specialists.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-01-21\tpublication\tnull\t\t2026-02-24T12:39:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n22246\tNRW_DS161415\t2025-12-01T15:05:21.045Z\t10\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Areas of Uncertainty\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343135\t\tKate Borrowdale, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Analysis and Reporting Team.  Kate has confirmed that NRW own this data. NRW paid Geomatics (the commercial aerial surveying department of the EA) to capture and analyse the aerial imagery, which generates the saltmarsh uncertainty layers. NRW have a long-standing service level agreement with Geomatics and the EA to carry out these surveys.\tThis dataset relates to Water Framework Directive (WFD) saltmarsh monitoring.  It identifies areas where saltmarsh zone types could not be determined through aerial imagery during a desk-based study. These locations require an on-site visit to accurately classify the appropriate saltmarsh zone type.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-02-09\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T16:14:13.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22248\tNRW_DS161416\t2025-12-01T16:14:10.864Z\t124\tValid\tEditable\tNRW Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC sediments survey 2023\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343136\tMarine Recorder Online  [43cadc21-0e19-4f03-a658-6ca40d5b310b].   Also field data held in DMS (SAMP-1270119632-191)  Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting\u0026gt;Marine Monitoring\u0026gt;Intertidal\u0026gt;CB\u0026amp;E SAC\u0026gt;Carm Data\tPaul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer,  Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tIn Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC the Annex I intertidal habitat features include: Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide.  A series of 23 sediment monitoring locations, distributed around the SAC to represent different areas and habitats, were established between 2005 and 2008.    Most locations were initially defined as transects (a line from upper to lower shore) but many have been redefined as clusters of stations (areas of shore).  Between 1 and 6 sampling stations have been established (with fixed positions) within each of those locations, though some have required repositioning due to channel movements.     These stations have been monitored using a combination of in-situ recording and sediment core sampling with laboratory analysis of infaunal composition and sediment granulometry.  Some stations (those at Crofty and Llansteffan) are surveyed and sampled annually, others at intervals of 2 or more years.  This description refered to data collected in 2023.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2024-08-30-2025-10-11\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-03T10:21:28.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n22321\tNRW_DS161472\t2025-12-12T12:47:49.364Z\t149\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDevelopment \u0026amp; Flood Risk - Flood Risk Activity Permits and Exemptions\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343732\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\FRAP\\Flood risk activity permit (FRAP).lyr\tLucy Tyack, Advisor 2, Development \u0026amp; Flood Risk. Daniel Jones, Advisor 2, Development \u0026amp; Flood Risk. Flood Risk Analysis Team.\tThis dataset maps the locations of Flood Risk Activity Permits (FRAPs) that have been granted or are currently being determined.   The data are collected by Development \u0026amp; Flood Risk (DFR) teams from North and South Wales on their own databases. The data is updated monthly.   FRAP reference numbers can be used to locate documents associated with each permit on the DMS. The dataset should be used when dealing with enquiries, applications and reports of activity in or near rivers to see if any activities have been permitted in the area.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-12-11-2026-01-05\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T15:04:32.232Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n22447\tNRW_DS161473\t2025-12-22T14:08:13.269Z\t46\tValid\tEditable\tHabitats Regulations 9A Wales Reporting 2019-2024 Dataset\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. © Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC). Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343733\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/reporting/sr/HabitReg9A/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Freporting%2Fsr%2FHabitReg9A%2F2024%20Data\tDr Rebecca Laidlaw, Specialist Advisor Environmental Reporting\tThis dataset supports Wales’s first country-level assessment under Regulation 9A of the Conservation of Habitats and Species Regulations 2017 (as amended) for the reporting period 2019–2024. It brings together evidence across terrestrial, freshwater, and marine environments for Annex I habitats, Annex II, IV and V species (excluding birds), and all regularly occurring wild bird species in Wales. The resource includes conservation status assessments for habitats and non-bird species, bird population status and trends with associated pressures and conservation measures.  The data were compiled by Natural Resources Wales on behalf of Welsh Government using the UK-wide methodology set out in the JNCC Approach Document, drawing on NRW monitoring programmes, partner agencies, NGOs, research institutions, and citizen science schemes. All data have undergone quality assurance and expert review to meet statutory reporting requirements and provide an evidence base for biodiversity conservation and policy in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-12-05-2026-01-22\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://naturalresources.wales/evidence-and-data/research-and-reports/habitats-regulations-9a-report-for-wales-2019-2024/?lang\u003den\t2026-02-11T15:30:41.211Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n22448\tNRW_DS161474\t2025-12-22T16:42:19.580Z\t17\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTurbot Bank Multibeam Bathymetry Survey 2025\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343734\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Marine acoustic bathymetric data\\Multibeam\\Turbot Bank 2025\\Turbot Bank (Combined).  There is a lot of raw data behind the processed data. The two main datafiles are the floating point geotiffs: \"Z:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Marine acoustic bathymetric data\\Multibeam\\Turbot Bank 2025\\Turbot Bank (Combined)\\20250923 Turbot Bank 0.5m UTM ODN [Bathymetry Model] Floating Point GeoTiff.tif\" and \"Z:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Marine acoustic bathymetric data\\Multibeam\\Turbot Bank 2025\\Turbot Bank (Combined)\\20250923 Turbot Bank 1m UTM [Backscatter Model] GeoTiff.tif\"\tIain Fairley, Specialist Advisor in Marine and Coastal Physical Science\tThis dataset provides bathymetry and backscatter data over the Turbot Bank, a designated sandbank partially within the Pembrokeshire Marine SAC.   The primary data form is geotiffs at 0.5m and 1m resolution, the raw data is also available. The data was collected to understand the current shape of the sandbank.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-12-17\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-20T10:20:12.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22578\tNRW_DS161476\t2026-01-13T09:53:08.653Z\t32\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBeaver Dam Capacity Index\t© CNC/NRW and others (tbc after assessment). Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343736\tX:\\Biological\\Beaver Habitat Modelling\\Beaver Dam Capacity.lyr\tLiz Halliwell, Sustainable Land and Nature Manager, Terrestrial Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) have identified a need for further understanding of beaver habitat and impacts across Wales.  There is a requirement to complete an analysis of river catchments in Wales to assess their suitability for supporting populations of beaver.   A Beaver Dam Capacity model (BDC) has been developed and run across all NRW Water Framework Directive management and operational catchments. BDC classifies reaches from no capacity for dam building to a pervasive capacity for damming. The BDC model estimates the capacity of river systems to support dams at the reach-scale (c.a. 150m). The model also highlights reaches that are more likely to be dammed by beaver and estimates the number of beaver dams that could occur for a catchment at population carrying capacity. As such, this highly detailed tool would provide understanding of where dams are most likely to occur and in what densities, supporting future work on the conflicts and opportunities that might accrue from beaver reintroduction. However, as with the Beaver Habitat Index model (BHI), it is important to remember BDC is a model and for all critical decisions, particularly at the local scale, understanding from modelling results should be supplemented by site visits. It is important to note that the model assumes both reach and catchment population carrying capacity for beaver. Therefore, in reality the maximum number of 251 dams indicated in a category class is unlikely to occur.   Flow conditions display a high degree of temporal variability, short term fluctuations due to rainfall events patterns and seasonal trends will alter the suitability of a channel for damming. I.e. a channel classed as having a rare capacity for damming, might see this capacity increase during drought periods, but conversely reduce to none during the wet/winter season.   Modelling does not consider the resilience of dams. It is likely that dams in small channels with a high BDC will be more resilient than those in a larger channel with a higher stream power. However, BDC does not quantify this. BDC does not consider the exact spatial distribution or configuration of dams, which is also likely to be heavily dependent on beaver population dynamics. BDC reflects the capacity of a given reach to support beaver dams (assuming catchment beaver population carrying capacity) rather than the actual number of dams that are likely to occur. In isolation, BDC cannot predict the likely number of dams in a catchment.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-01-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-09T16:19:56.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22579\tNRW_DS161477\t2026-01-13T11:40:52.454Z\t8\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMapping Marine Protected Area Features at risk from INNS\t© CNC/NRW and © JNCC. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343737\tGIS file to be added to the X Drive. Excel spreadsheet on DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/advice/mar/MNN22-25/MarineINNS/MPA_Features%20at%20risk%20from%20INNS_Report/Final%20Outputs/Annex%201_Habitat%20Correlation.xlsx?d\u003dw276cdd70251b40cdbc3ad8a74a1092df\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\tChloe Powell Jennings - Senior Advisor, Marine Biosecurity Planning\tThis data consists of an Excel spreadsheet and GIS files.  The Excel spreadsheet presents two tables plus a readme tab. The EICAT summary table consists of a summary matrix for 16 INNS species providing a summary score for likelihood of establishment and an aggregated (worst-case) score for impact on each of the 41 Marine Protected Area features assessed by a previous project (Tillin et al., 2020).   A number of general limitations regarding the EICAT habitat assessments were identified by Tillin et al. (2020) and are relevant to the current project. The impact assessments are generic and not site-specific.  The Annex I habitats tab links the MPA features risk assessed by Tillin et al. (2020) to Annex I habitat features. The resulting aligned Annex I habitat features were used in the report to highlight which Annex I habitats are at risk from different marine INNS. For the SACs it is not always clear when habitats are definitely considered part of the Annex I habitat features for the site and Annex I habitat classifications are context dependent in some instances.    The GIS files map the potential impact of 15 assessed INNS on MPA features at a Welsh scale to produce risk maps for each INNS within six evaluated SAC’s. The maps show the areas of highest risk (concern) for the 15 marine INNS in Wales. Limitations of mapping INNS by habitats is that the extent of suitable habitat may be overestimated . Multiple environmental variables and other factors will influence whether INNS reach a site and become established.\t\t2026-01-13\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-13T12:23:07.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22586\tNRW_DS161478\t2026-01-13T16:21:05.128Z\t66\tValid\tEditable\tImpacts of bait collection on Welsh Marine Protected Areas (MPAs)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343738\tAssociated data held in DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/advice/mar/MNN22-25/BaitCollectn/Outputs/ABPmer%20survey%20raw%20data?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;e\u003d0X2cxO .  Marine Recorder Online:  eb56c392-90f5-45ae-8ecd-c57dc47e3639,  ef12b4e8-dae9-403a-9fcf-2cfde55f7e26,  0c19cb20-8e98-48b5-84d5-ac33e21689ed\tAlex Zalewski, Marine Ecology Advisor\tSurvey to assess the impacts of three bait collection methods on Welsh MPAs - bait digging (Sites : Angle Bay, Four Mile Bridge, Penmon - Beaumaris), boulder turning (Penmon-Beaumaris, West of Great Orme), crab shelters (Deganwy, Bangor Pier). Samples taken from control and impact sites. Infaunal cores, sediment cores, benthic megafauna (i.e. razor clam, cockle) quadrat, worm cast count, seagrass coverage, boulder epifauna, boulder turning classification.  Habitat and Species data recorded as part of the assessments are stored in three individual Marine Recorder Online Surveys (bait digging survey, crab shelter survey, boulder turning survey) , each one is decribed in their own individual metadata record (NRW_DS161356, NRW_DS161479 and NRW_DS161480).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-04-11-2025-12-17\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2026-01-14T09:49:52.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n22587\tNRW_DS161479\t2026-01-13T16:24:11.365Z\t70\tValid\tEditable\tImpacts of bait collection on Welsh Marine Protected Areas (MPAs): Crab Shelter\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343739\tMarine Recorder Online (0c19cb20-8e98-48b5-84d5-ac33e21689ed).  Associated data held in DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/advice/mar/MNN22-25/BaitCollectn/Outputs/ABPmer%20survey%20raw%20data?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;e\u003d0X2cxO\tAlex Zalewski, Advisor, Marine Ecology, Nature Networks\tSurvey to assess the impacts of three bait collection methods on Welsh MPAs - bait digging (Sites : Angle Bay, Four Mile Bridge, Penmon - Beaumaris), boulder turning (Penmon-Beaumaris, West of Great Orme) and crab shelters (Deganwy, Bangor Pier). Samples taken from control and impact sites. Infaunal cores, sediment cores, benthic megafauna (i.e. razor clam, cockle) quadrat, worm cast count, seagrass coverage, boulder epifauna, boulder turning classification.  This metadata covers the results of crab shelter monitoring at Bangor Pier and Deganwy.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-03-07-2026-01-15\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2026-01-15T11:41:58.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n22591\tNRW_DS161480\t2026-01-13T16:53:30.799Z\t75\tValid\tEditable\tImpacts of bait collection on Welsh Marine Protected Areas (MPAs): Boulder Turning\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343830\tMarine Recorder Online (ef12b4e8-dae9-403a-9fcf-2cfde55f7e26).   Associated data held in DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/advice/mar/MNN22-25/BaitCollectn/Outputs/ABPmer%20survey%20raw%20data?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;e\u003d0X2cxO\tAlex Zalewski, Advisor, Marine Ecology, Nature Networks\tSurvey to assess the impacts of three bait collection methods on Welsh MPAs - bait digging (Sites : Angle Bay, Four Mile Bridge, Penmon - Beaumaris), boulder turning (Penmon-Beaumaris, West of Great Orme), crab shelters (Deganwy, Bangor Pier). Samples taken from control and impact sites. Infaunal cores, sediment cores, benthic megafauna (i.e. razor clam, cockle) quadrat, worm cast count, seagrass coverage, boulder epifauna, boulder turning classification.  This metadata covers the results of boulder turning monitoring at Penmon-Beaumaris and West of Great Orme.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-03-06-2026-01-15\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2026-01-15T14:31:41.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n22605\tNRW_DS161481\t2026-01-15T16:27:45.590Z\t27\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBeaver Habitat Index Map\t© CNC/NRW and others (tbc after assessment). Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343831\tX:\\Biological\\Beaver Habitat Modelling\\Beaver Habitat Index Great Britain.lyr\tLiz Halliwell, Sustainable Land and Nature Manager, Terrestrial Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) have identified a need for further understanding of beaver habitat and impacts across Wales. There is a requirement to complete an analysis of river catchments in Wales to assess their suitability for supporting populations of beaver.   Beaver habitat suitability is determined primarily by vegetation suitability which has been classified nationally using a Beaver Vegetation Index (BVI) as well as access to water bodies. Together these two factors have been incorporated into a Beaver Habitat Index model (BHI). BHI has been run nationally to develop a high resolution (5m) continuous raster product that can inform local decision making with regard to beaver reintroduction. BHI classifies habitat suitability from 0 (No access to vegetation - not suitable) to 5 (Highly Suitable). BHI has also been resampled at a 1km scale to allow national and regional patterns to be observed. BHI provides a resource for quantifying beaver habitat suitability with national coverage. A high (5m) spatial resolution enables it to have the capacity to inform detailed local decision making. Additionally, the 1 km resolution resampled dataset allows the exploration and visualisation of beaver habitat at regional and national scales.    Examples of BHI presented in results section 3 overlaid on satellite imagery reflect its ability to provide a highly useful classification of beaver habitat based upon a vegetation suitability ranking and access to water (including both river network and waterbodies such as ponds and lakes). However, it is critical to note that BHI is a model rather than an absolute reflection of reality and the below caveats should be considered when using the BHI model outputs.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-01-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-22T14:06:53.000Z\tGary Eccleston\t-\tPrivate record\n22615\tNRW_DS161482\t2026-01-19T14:21:56.755Z\t11\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2025 Survey of Sandhill Rustic Luperina nickerlii gueneei on Newborough Warren\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343832\tWelsh Invertebrate Database, report on the DMS\tDr Michael Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tThis is a report of a survey for sandhill rustic moth on Newborough Warren on Anglesey in August 2025 to determine its current status and distribution. Stands of the larval foodplant, sand couch, were also mapped.   The data is in the form of species records with grid references and annotated maps and aerial images.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-01-15\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-20T10:14:01.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22616\tNRW_DS161483\t2026-01-19T16:04:45.674Z\t9\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey of Welsh Populations of the Grizzled Skipper Pyrgus malvae in 2023 and 2024\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343833\tWID\tDr. Michael Howe, NRW Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Invertebrates\tThe report provides the results of an all-Wales survey of grizzled skipper, focusing on sites with recent records. Although three sites could not be accessed, the butterfly was found to be restricted to seven populations across nine sites.  All populations are small or very small and most are isolated from one another. Good condition habitat is rare.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-01-19\tcreation\tnull\t\t2026-01-20T10:11:28.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22642\tNRW_DS161485\t2026-01-20T17:43:02.811Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLEAFPACS and Luronium natans Survey Results and Photos – Afon Gwyrfai ac Llyn Cwellyn SAC (2025)\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343835\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/fm/CARWC4/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Ffm%2FCARWC4%2FAfon%20Gwyrfai\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B84335AA9%2D0084%2D4378%2D9C43%2DFB696F2B22B6%7D\tFrancesca Sanchez, Specialist Advisor: Freshwater Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales is required to evaluate the conservation status of the Ranunculion river habitat and Luronium natans features using Common Standards Monitoring guidance. The Afon Gwyrfai ac Llyn Cwellyn is one of seven rivers in Wales that are designated for Ranunculion habitat and this report assesses the macrophyte attribute alongside the population attribute of the Luronium natans feature.   Data were collected in September 2025 using LEAFPACS (Lake \u0026amp; Estuary Aquatic Plant Assessment Comprehensive System) surveys and analysed using the two associated automated spreadsheets. Data were collected and assessed on the condition of L. natans using tailored surveys.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-01-20\tcreation\tnull\t\t2026-01-21T09:26:38.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22643\tNRW_DS161486\t2026-01-21T10:02:32.843Z\t9\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Restock Areas\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343836\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Restock Areas.lyr Forestweb System\tGeraint Morgan, Specialist Advisor Forester\tThis layer is generated by the resource planning activity carried out on the Welsh Government Woodland Estate (WGWE). It is used to describe the primary woodland regime and habitats that may be considered for an area of forest, usually after the area has been felled.   The layer is updated on a day to day basis as part of the process of managing the WGWE.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-01-20\tcreation\tnull\t\t2026-02-09T15:34:57.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22656\tNRW_DS161488\t2026-01-23T14:46:46.489Z\t9\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPumping Stations\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343838\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Assets\\Pumping Station.lyr\tGeospatial Services\tPumping Stations\t\t2026-02-17\tcreation\tnull\t\t2026-02-17T12:06:19.000Z\tAndrew Williams\t-\tPrivate record\n22686\tNRW_DS161489\t2026-01-28T15:14:53.988Z\t24\tValid\tEditable\tLlwybr Coedwig Cymru Trail\t© CNC/NRW and OS???? Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343839\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\National Forest for Wales Trail\\National Forest for Wales Trail.lyr\tStacey Delbridge, Team Leader National Forest\tThe data provides the location of the promoted route Llwybr Coedwig Cymru Trail, which is commissioned by Welsh Government and supported by the National Forest for Wales team in NRW.   The data comes from CAMS idox group who work with local authorities to get the raw data on the status of the route and they have broken the data down into route codes to take into account status and local authority. The trail will be marketed on Welsh Government website to promote the new route which will be launched early in March 2026.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-01-27\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-24T10:52:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n22708\tNRW_DS161490\t2026-02-04T09:26:16.395Z\t2\tUnassessed\tNon-editable\tMwdwl Eithin NVC Phase II Survey (part of Mynydd Hireathog)\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343930\tTo be added to the X Drive\tCaroline Bateson, N2K Officer, Conwy Environment Team\tPhase 2 National Vegetation Classification mapping of upland vegetation communities. The mapping has been carried out to NVC sub-community level. The total area surveyed is approximately 376ha in extent, the majority of which is dry heath, acid grassland and blanket bog habitat with associated communities.   The NVC survey report includes sufficient data to identify NVC communities and sub-communities with appropriately scaled mapping (usually 1:5000) except where flush complexes and rare bryophytes occur where 1:2,500 should be used.   The information is compatible GIS data for use with Arc GIS.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-03\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-04T15:26:50.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22709\tNRW_DS161491\t2026-02-04T14:17:21.213Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS) and Plant Community survey data of the River Wye SAC 2024 and 2025\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343931\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/fm/CARWC4/Wye/Surveys/Combined%202024%20%26%202025%20RHS%20Macrophyte%20Survey%20data%20and%20report\tFrancesca Sanchez, Specialist Advisor, Freshwater Habitats and Species\tIn 2024 and 2025, a River Habitat Survey (RHS), Simplified Phase 1 survey and LEAFPAC aquatic macrophyte survey was carried out at 28 survey sites on the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC). This was to contribute to the condition assessment of features in the River Wye SAC as well as provide information to support the management and restoration of protected sites and the delivery of River Basin Management Plans.   Twenty-eight monitoring sites were surveyed in the River Wye catchment between 5th August and 28th September 2024 and between June and July 2025.   At each site the following surveys were conducted:     • River Habitat Surveys (RHS), to assess the quality of physical habitat, supported by   • Simplified Phase 1 survey, and   • Aquatic plant surveys using the rivers LEAFPACS method, along with   • Field observations of habitat quality to assist with interpretation of the data.     The principal outputs of this project are digital copies of field records including photographs, access and parking, along with a description of each site.     These surveys were conducted on the Welsh section of the River Wye SAC.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-01-26\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-11T12:39:42.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22801\tNRW_DS161493\t2026-02-24T11:47:42.528Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tProtected Sites Monitoring Images\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343933\tZ:\\Archive\\Land Mgmt\\Protected Sites\\Terrestrial Monitoring\tCaroline Moscrop, South-east EAAT Team Leader and Virtual Terrestrial Monitoring Team Leader\tDigital images of protected sites in Wales captured by NCC, CCW and NRW. This includes digitised versions of images and associated metadata recorded by the SSSI Photomonitoring Project (NCC, CCW), digital images and metadata collected as of the SAC Monitoring Project (CCW, NRW), and digital images and metadata collected on SSSI and SAC for terrestrial monitoring by NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-16\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-02-24T15:15:12.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22808\tNRW_DS161494\t2026-02-24T16:12:22.921Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Breeding Wader Survey - 1km Squares\t© CNC/NRW. Data may only be re-used externally under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence following an assessment of the data.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343934\tx:\\Biological\\WelshBreedingWaderSurvey26\\ WELSHWADERSURVEY_1KMSQUARES.lyr\tBethan Beech - Welsh Breeding Wader Survey 2026 (project)\tSurvey areas for the Welsh Breeding Wader Survey. These are ranked and prioritised based on criteria set out by the British Trust for Ornithology (BTO) as a previous contract.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2026-02-19\tcreation\tnull\t\t2026-02-26T08:59:58.000Z\tMichael Goodrich\t-\tPrivate record\n22825\tNRW_DS161496\t2026-02-26T14:11:32.416Z\t2\tUnassessed\tNon-editable\tA survey of key insect species on selected SSSIs in South Wales (2025)\tReplace this text with the Use Constraints statement\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343936\t\t\tThis dataset comes from a survey of ten insect species that form qualifying features for several Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) across South Wales. These species include Chalcis sispes, Chrysis fulgida, Macroplax preyssleri, Ochthebius poweri, Odontoscelis fuliginosa, Plateumaris bracata, Sphaerophoria loewi, and Tapinotus sellatus, recorded at SSSIs in Carmarthenshire, Glamorgan, Gwent, and Pembrokeshire.\t\t2026-02-26\tcreation\tnull\t\t2026-02-26T14:19:12.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n17303\tNRW_DS23774\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.535Z\t1058\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID)\t© CNC/NRW 2008 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence or equivalent. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale. At a spatial scale at which the records are considered non-sensitive then there are no use restrictions on this data, including for commercial and research purposes. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.   Part of this dataset is sensitive and restricted. The sensitive data is as follows - Freshwater Pearl Mussel (Margaritifera margaritifera ).  Sensitive data must be kept confidential; general release is not permitted due to risk of harm to the environment or third parties. Sensitive data may only be released under an appropriate NRW Licence. Requests should be referred to NRW\u0027s Access to Information Officer. Data may be released freely if the sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3233373734\t[A] Data stored in Recorder 6 under the Welsh Invertebrate Database. Recorder 6 is currently located on the legacy Countryside Council for Wales drives, accessed through VMware.\tMike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW. (Adrian Fowles who is now retired) Custodian is the Terrestrial Ecosystems Group. Some records are collected from Adrian Fowles, an ex-CCW employee - these datasets are now managed by Mike Howe.         Data entry into the WID is on-going and is being run by Mike Howe. Some entries have their own specific metadata entry, but these are usually related to contractors work. Some records within the WID are classed as either NEGATIVE RESULT, NEEDS CONFIRMING, ERRONEOUS or DUBIOUS under the attribute Recorder Name. This means that the species was absent from survey / sample if the survey was done to purposely find that species. So when conducting an extract of the data it might be worth checking and removing these entries or making sure it is reflected in the NBN Gateway metadata.\tThe purpose of this data capture was to understand the distribution of species in Wales in order to inform conservation decisions.  Collation of around 500,000 invertebrate occurrence records, most of which relate to Wales. This is the primary database used by Natural Resources Wales; incorporates other major invertebrate datasets. Includes terrestrial, freshwater and coastal airbreathing invertebrates but not marine species.  The database also contains records of associated taxa and incidental records of other species, including alga, vascular plants, amphibians, mammals, birds, fungi, reptiles and bryophytes.  Much of the data has been sent to various UK recording schemes, who may also have supplied this data to the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Atlas.  Family groups where data may already have been uploaded to the Atlas includes, Cerambycidae, Ciidae. Mollusca, Aquatic Heteroptera, Brachycera, All butterfly data, Odonata, Ant data, Tipuloidea and Trichoptera.  Mainly Wales, although there are occasional records from elsewhere in the UK. The database has been maintained since 1992. Records date from 1500s to present day.  Data accuracy is reliant on original sources. However, data has been transcribed by experienced invertebrate biologists in order to research incomplete records and minimise errors. Any uncertainties about a particular record, are noted in the database. Systematic verification has been carried out on significant parts of the dataset.  The database is constantly updated.  This dataset comprises of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Atlas.\tVarious including volunteer recorders-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:02:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17354\tNRW_DS23780\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.797Z\t978\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Peatland Invertebrate Survey (WPIS)\t© CNC/NRW 1989 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3233373830\t[A]  Biological Records are held in Recorder 6  - Welsh Peatland Invertebrate Survey CCW0006600000005 tagged as part of the Welsh Invertebrate Database.\tData Custodian: Mike Howe\tThe purpose of this data capture was to understand the distribution of peatland invertebrate species in Wales in order to inform conservation decisions. The Welsh Peatland Invertebrate Survey sampled invertebrates from peatlands throughout Wales during a 3 year period.  The survey assessed the distribution of invertebrates species in peatland, and studied the effects of site management on the assemblage of invertebrates present.  Around 106,000 records of invertebrates were found in peatland habitats in Wales, along with environmental information for each sampling station. All data relating to the WPIS is now held in NRWs Welsh Invertebrate database (WID).  The survey was based on the whole of Wales and a report is available for each of the following vice counties, Anglesey, Brecon, Caernarfon, Carmarthen, Ceredigion, Denbigh (inc. Wem Moss, Shropshire), Glamorgan, Merioneth, Monmouth, Montgomery, Pembroke, Radnor. Data was collected between 1987-1989.  New records have not been added to the WPIS itself as this dataset is now part of the Welsh Invertebrate Database.\tVarious-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1995-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:34:59.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17346\tNRW_DS24148\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.677Z\t377\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey and Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales Brecknock spreadsheets (1991-1995)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234313438\t[A]  This data is archived in the terrestrial archive under: Project Number T005 Media Number T_Media005.    [B]   Location of data in the CCW H Drive:  H:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\PayneC\\2003\\MYeo spreadsheet data   To convert data from old format to xls, they were copied to TRIM (Location of data in Ffynnon: NRWF-15-001292), where they could be viewed and then copied to a new format.  Retention on these temporary files has passed and they have been deleted.  the converted files are retained in the archive\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems / Heathlands Ecologist, Grasslands Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecologist - Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.  Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to inform site selection and develop understanding of the semi-natural vegetation cover of Wales. The upland data covers all the major upland blocks in Wales; the grassland data is restricted to Brecknock sites.  Approximately 60 spreadsheets of Upland data (environmental, habitat type, statutory classifications and grid referenced sites). Data from various times between 1991 to 1995.  Collation and analysis of data collected by the Wales Field Unit Upland Vegetation Survey 1979-1989 and a small number of subsequent upland NVC surveys in the early 1990s.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1995-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17343\tNRW_DS24176\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.521Z\t270\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Monitoring Data\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234313736\t[A] Location is determined with each of the individual survey projects metadata.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, Marine and Freshwater Sciences Group, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis data provides the baseline for the marine monitoring.   Marine baseline monitoring data (initially from collection trials of monitoring procedures) collected at locations in Wales. The trials were to determine the best combination of data collection for monitoring. This metadata refers to the project as a whole however each individual survey may have metadata of its own. The surveys took place over Penllyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation, Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation and Pembrokeshire Islands Special Area of Conservation between 1999 and  2001.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:20.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17358\tNRW_DS24185\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.895Z\t861\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBat Roosts Database - South Wales (BATDATA-WALES) 1982-1997\t© CNC/NRW 1997 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence.  No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale. Part of this dataset is sensitive and restricted. The sensitive data is as follows; location of bat roosts. Sensitive data must be kept confidential; general release is not permitted due to risk of harm to the environment or third parties.  Sensitive data may only be released under an appropriate NRW Licence. Requests should be referred to NRW\u0027s Access to Information Team. Data may be released freely if the sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234313835\t[A] Species Data held in Recorder 6:  CCW-HQ-Terr-Bat Roosts Database - Wales (BATDATA-WALES) CCW0003800000001\tInternal Custodian: Jean Matthews,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW. Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW.\tThis is a former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) regional dataset of bat roosts in South Wales, containing approximately 600 records. Includes the location of roosts, roost type, species records and public enquiries and/or details of visits to roosts by CCW staff or other bat experts. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts. This data has been used to provide information when dealing with development proposals, to support the monitoring and notification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species.  Original data was created by Nature Conservancy Council as part of a UK-wide dataset and Welsh records were transferred to CCW in 1991. This dataset has not been validated and there may be some errors in grid references and place names.\tVarious including volunteer recorders-County bat groups-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1997-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:14:04.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17357\tNRW_DS24227\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.797Z\t788\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Raptor and Schedule 1 Bird Database\t© CNC/NRW 2004.  Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence.  No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.  IMPORTANT: This data contains sensitive species (bird data).  Part of this dataset is sensitive and restricted. The sensitive data is as follows a) Data showing the exact location of nest sites for specified bird species, if shown below 10km sq scale.  b) Data within the database which is owned by other organisations or individuals. No permission has been given to release or publish that data. Sensitive data must be kept confidential; general release is not permitted due to risk of harm to the environment or third parties. Sensitive data may only be released under an appropriate NRW Licence. Requests should be referred to NRW\u0027s Access to Information Officer. Data may be released freely if the sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323237\tThis data is now part of the terrestrial archive. Project T004 Media Number T_Media004.\tRSPB contact is R Thorpe\tConfidential database of records of Welsh raptors, chough and some other non-raptor Wildlife and Countryside Act Schedule 1 birds. Type of data: nest sites, chick counts and grid references (although some locations are coded by the license returnee and only the area the license covers is given, the exact location is unknown.)  This data is strictly confidential due to Protected Schedule 1 status of birds. Selective parts of the data (but not in full or in the same form) are shared with Reg Thorpe, RSPB and Rare Breeding Birds Panel.  This dataset is used for casework, survey, monitoring and site designation across Wales.\tRoyal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)-Rare Breeding Birds Panel-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17353\tNRW_DS24232\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.854Z\t572\tUnassessed\tEditable\tImportant Welsh Bat Roosts Database (BATSITES-WALES) 1946 - 2001\t© CNC/NRW 2001 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence.  No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323332\t[A]  Species data is held in Recorder 6. CCW-HQ-Terr- Important Welsh  Bat Roosts Database (BATSITES-WALES)  CCW0003800000003\tInternal Custodian:  Mammal Ecologist, Bats and Riparian mammals, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset consists of information on important bat roosts from across Wales, mostly related to Greater and Lesser Horseshoe Bats and other rare species. It includes roost location and other site details, and results of repeat monitoring and surveillance. This database was originally created by Nature Conservancy Council as a UK-wide dataset. Welsh records were transferred to Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in 1991. There has been no systematic updating since 1999. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts, to assist CCW\u0027s conservation work. In particular, to provide information when dealing with development proposals, to support in the monitoring and notification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species.\tVarious including volunteer recorders-County bat groups-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:13:56.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17345\tNRW_DS24234\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.609Z\t1312\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOtter Sightings in Wales (OTTERSURV)\t© CNC/NRW  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323334\tSee the Terrestrial Archive:  Project Number T003 Media T_Media003 (File Path: K:\\Archive\\Terrestrial Archive\\T_Media001to100\\T_Media003)\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tA database of otter sightings in Wales. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Regional consultations and for information and reporting at Head Quarters.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-01-16\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17352\tNRW_DS24235\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.735Z\t737\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInventory of British Lakes (UKLakes)\t© CEH / University College London 2004.  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323335\tThis dataset is held by CEH and will be made available through their data portal.\tExternal Custodian: Centre for Ecology and Hydrology - they are the ones that hold the data and manage the dataset.  Internal Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services        15/02/2016 - This data is held by CEH and we contribute and own a stake in it. CEH are going to add it to their data portal - Tristan Hatton Ellis to send us the link when it is live so we can update the metadata - the link should be added to the additional information - or even the Resource Locator.\tThis is an inventory of lakes in Great Britain derived mainly from Geographical Information System (GIS) datasets. It is linked with a risk based prioritisation protocol for standing waters in Great Britain, which will be used in Lake Habitat Action Plans (HAP) and Water Framework Directive (WFD) work. The purpose of this data capture was to develop the required lake typology under the Water Framework Directive. This dataset is held by Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH).\tUniversity College London. Environmental Change Research Centre-Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)-Environment Agency (EA)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)-Scottish Environmental Protection Agency (SEPA)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:20.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17360\tNRW_DS24253\t2024-05-28T12:20:41.108Z\t729\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - 1kmsq Access Database\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323533\tArchived under Project number T006 Media number T_Media006.   Also available on the H Drive: H:\\Science\\Common\\Phase 1\\1 km square database\\Habsup1\\HABSUP.mdb.   The other access databases (Habsup2 , Habsup3) within this H Drive folder: H:\\Science\\Common\\Phase 1\\1 km square database\\  were intermediaries in the creation of the GIS versions 2 and 3 of the 1kmsq data.\tCustodian:  Internal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping \u0026amp; Farmland Ecologist (Clare Burrows?) Ecosystems Team NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe data are derived from a programme of field recording that was begun by the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) in 1979 and continued by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) after restructuring in 1991.   The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently simplified and imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales (see All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - 1Kmsq .    The database was created in 1999 using data from the Phase 1 Habitat Survey of Wales undertaken between 1979 and 1999.  This table was held in Paradox database but then was converted into Access. It lists Phase 1 habitat areas in every 1 km square in Wales. Habitat area measurements are sub-divided within 1 km squares by, Region, District, Survey programme (Terrestrial Phase 1 Survey and upland surveys), Upland and lowland, and, for land covered by the terrestrial Phase 1 survey, statutory and non-statutory at the time of survey.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T12:26:11.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17356\tNRW_DS24258\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.895Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tStanding Waters Database (Lakes)\tThe Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) owns the database design, whilst the contained data is owned by the three country agencies, Scottish Natural Heritage (SNH), CCW and English Nature (EN).   © CCGC/CCW 2004. CCW data may be used for non-commercial and internal business purposes only. It may be interrogated, imported into your own system and merged with other data, providing it is not factually altered. You may duplicate and publish short extracts of raw data and any findings and other information derived from the data, providing CCW is acknowledged. You may not copy the dataset or transfer it to others; additional copies can be obtained from CCW.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323538\tThe NRW copy is held at N:\\DuiganC\\lakesdb\\JNCC.MDB in MFSG.\tSenior Freshwater Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW.\tThe Standing Waters Database mainly contains data on the relative abundance of lake plants.   This data can be used for: 1. Site data storage  2. Identification of rare plant sites  3. Identification of Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitats and Special Areas of Conservation (SACs)  4. Could be used in Local BAPs  5. Classification scheme used forms the basis of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) site selection  6. Provides data for Regional staff if casework arises on a site  7. Information used in SSSI management plans.\tScottish Natural Heritage (SNH)-English Nature (EN)-Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17350\tNRW_DS24261\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.701Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarchlyn Mawr Ozone / NOx monitoring\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323631\tAvailable to Environmental Audit Group staff on the shared drive:  R:\\Current Projects\\ECN\\EcnData\\A_AtmosChem\\Oz_data\\ OZNOX2\tEnvironmental Change Network (ECN) Team, Enviromental Change Group, NRW. Environmental Change Network Team, Environmental Change Group, NRW.\tMonitoring of Ozone and NOx air pollution. Work is funded by NAW. The purpose of this data capture was to collect data on air pollution levels in North Wales, and contribute to the Welsh Air Pollution database which monitors changes in air pollution levels across Wales (all other stations are in South or mid-Wales).\tThe National Assembly for Wales-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:56:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17342\tNRW_DS24262\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.250Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNational Peatland Resource Inventory (NPRI)\t© CNC/NRW 1996. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323632\tUnknown\tData Custodian: Peatland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW. Now falls under Jane Stevens, TSG, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tNational Peatland Resource Inventory (NPRI) was the most comprehensive assessment of the lowland raised bog resource in Great Britain at the time of publishing (1996). The inventory lists all known existing or former lowland raised bog sites. Within the NPRI raised bog is classified according to a number of sub-categories based on land cover. Four classes of active raised bog are defined: natural or near- natural vegetation; degraded vegetation (usually burnt or dry); drained; and revegetated or regenerating cutover.  There is uncertainty about data reliability and source.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1996-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17359\tNRW_DS24263\t2024-05-28T12:20:41.048Z\t414\tValid\tEditable\tInventory of Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Biotope Mapping Survey Sites 1996 - 2005\t© CNC/NRW 2005 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323633\tMaster copies of data is available to MFSG staff: H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\Ph1sites\\IntertidalPhase1SurveySiteInventory.xls\tInternal Custodian:  Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tThis dataset comprises of an Microsoft Excel spreadsheet listing all intertidal sites surveyed by the Intertidal Team as part of the Marine Phase 1 Intertidal Phase 1 Mapping Survey.  It provides a summary of site code, site name, the year surveyed, the extent of the site (grid refs), which Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) guidelines the site was assessed and qualified under, if any, and the original version of the biotope classification the site was surveyed under. The purpose of this data capture was to assist with the management of the of Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Biotope Mapping Survey and to provide manageable areas to assist with the assessment of the intertidal for the potential of designating SSSIs. Data collection started in 1996 and finished in 2005. The site names, codes and boundaries remain constant but the qualifying guidelines may go out of date if further assessments are done.  The spreadsheet should be updated in line with any future assessments.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:33:11.113Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17347\tNRW_DS24264\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.695Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCoppicing Surveilance at Coeddyd Aber National Nature Reserve\t© CNC/NRW. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323634\tUnknown\t\tLarge spreadsheet of plant species against quadrat number and percentage cover values.  Collected as part of coppicing surveillance at Coedydd Aber National Nature Reserve (NNR).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17348\tNRW_DS24267\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.635Z\t479\tValid\tEditable\tSevern Estuary Database Phase 2\t© CNC/NRW and © Natural England 2001. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ English Nature and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323637\tArchived as Project No. 427, Media No. 1464, 1465 (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1464 and K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1465)  Update August 2021 by Nicola Rimington.  Held for archive purposes only.\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tThis data is made up of the results from evaluating the nature conservation resource of the sediment environment in the Severn Estuary and Bristol Channel.   This database was created for the Marine Aggregates Resource Management Planning System (MARMPS), a National Assembly project, in order to create a decision-support tool for the Environment Minister when making decisions on aggregate extraction licences.\tEnglish Nature (EN)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:23:22.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n17344\tNRW_DS24269\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.513Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBird Spreadsheet Data\t© CNC/NRW 2004. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323639\tSian Whitehead\u0027s G:drive.\tSian Whitehead: Ornithologists, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.\tThis is a collation of spreadsheets containing bird data and 1 containing rainfall data, as follows: -  Numbers of lesser black backed gulls occurring on Welsh Islands Numbers of curlew recorded in Welsh tetrads, 1988 - 1991 Summary of surveys undertaken at each Welsh bird SSSI Summary of upland bird data from site surveys in Wales. Summary of bird species surveys in Wales. Pied flycatcher (and other species) breeding data for nest box colony  Enlli-skylark\u0027s bird days\u0027 column: Skylark days on Ynys Enlli Wye rainfall data, 1944 - 1996 Number of raptors only, no site details, for Elenydd. Sian also holds  Numbers of dead and RSPCA recovered sea birds recorded by date  This is in Quattro Pro version 5. Numbers of black grouse breeding on Marine Number of common scoter recorded in Carmarthen Bay, 1977 - 1995 Numbers of lapwing recorded in Welsh tetrads, 1988 - 1991 Number of ring ouzels recorded on Spring and Autumn passage at UK Bird Number of ring ouzel days recorded on Spring and Autumn passage at Number of ring ouzels recorded in Welsh tetrads, 1995 \u0026amp; 1996 Number of pairs of roseate terns in Wales, 1960 -1995 (The file name for each of these surveys is available on Cyprian Payne\u0027s original Access metadatabase.) Number of pairs of artic terns on the Skerries, 1980 -1995 Number of pairs of roseate terns on Ynys Feurig and in UK and Ireland, Number of pairs of gannets on Grassholm, 1850 - 1995 Five spreadsheets containing data from previous spreadsheets plus common Marine date is in format 10 -Sep -95. Numbers of common scoter  recorded Number of hen harrieres recorded at individual locations, 1978 -1986 Raven breeding data for some sites, 1986 - 1992 (data from AVC)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:30.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17355\tNRW_DS24270\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.812Z\t769\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRare Flowering Plant and Fern Data\t© CNC/NRW 2002  Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence or equivalent. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale. At a spatial scale at which the records are considered non-sensitive then there are no use restrictions on this data, including for commercial and research purposes. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323730\t[A]  Species data is held in Recorder 6. [B]  Data held in database: Rec2002 - Higher Plants in Aberystwyth.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tJulian Woodman, Higher Plant Specialist, Terrestrial Species, NRW\tThis dataset is a collation of locality information, conservation status and dates of most recent records for Red Data Book, Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) and Nationally Extinct vascular plants in Wales.  This dataset is separate and different from JNCCs Threatened Plants Database, but there is likely to be considerable duplication of records between them.  The purpose of this data capture was to inform survey, monitoring and conservation management of sites for rare plants. The records span the period 1639 (one record extracted from literature) to 1999.  The majority of records occur after 1950.  Not all dates are given to the precise day or month, most are only known to be within a particular year.\tVarious including volunteer recorders-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17349\tNRW_DS24274\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.677Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tData on RIGS (GD2 Database)\t© CCGC/CCW 2004. CCW data may be used for non-commercial and internal business purposes only. It may be interrogated, imported into your own system and merged with other data, providing it is not factually altered. You may duplicate and publish short extracts of raw data and any findings and other information derived from the data, providing CCW is acknowledged. You may not copy the dataset or transfer it to others; additional copies can be obtained from CCW.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323734\tThe Program is on Tony Rogers PC. And is backed up to C.Payne G:\\drive\\docs\\databases\\geology.  Command to start the program is: AREV gd/x\t\tData on Regionally Important Geological and Geomorphological Sites (RIGS).  Including information on minerals, rocks, and stratigraphic units.  This database was developed from one written to handle records from the National Scheme for Geological Site Documentation (NSGSD) Project in 1991. The Lithostratigraphic table has been developed from data initially provided by the British Geological Society. The main data file contains Site data including all the main descriptive and keyword data for the Site. The Long Record is the main data entry screen containing all the fields that appear on the NSGSD Site recording form.\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17339\tNRW_DS24276\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.983Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCountryside Information System (CIS)\t© CNC/NRW 2004. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323736\t\tLiz Howe, Species Team Leader \u0026amp; Herpetologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW.\tThe Countryside Information System (CIS) is physical and geological data based on 1 km squares. The data is at three levels. Level 1 is \"Summary Statistics\"; Level 2 is \"Land Class Data Sets\"; and Level 3 is \"Data Access Services\".  CCW is no longer a member of CS2000 Advisory Group thus is not eligible to receive free level one and level two data in electronic format.  Consortium members receive all the land cover data sets and full licences operating in perpetuity for internal use of the data.\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17351\tNRW_DS24278\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.743Z\t672\tValid\tEditable\tCoastal Surveillance Unit (CSU) monitoring programme\t© CNC/NRW and Coastal Surveillance Unit Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323738\tArchived as Project No. 256, Media No. 918 (File path: K\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 918)\tInternal Custodian: Gabrielle Wyn, Team Leader, All Wales Marine Advice Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Coastal Surveillance Unit (CSU) monitoring programme is comprised of 10 databases, including those for abundance records for around 1000 coastal species.  The purpose of the monitoring (funded by Shell UK Ltd.) was to detect any changes that might have occurred as a result of a Shell oil pipeline that runs from a terminal at sea off Amlwch across Anglesey to the mainland. Monitoring was carried out over 10 years from 1973 - 1983, with a resurvey in 1993.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-University College Of North Wales. Marine Science Laboratories: Coastal Surveillance Unit\t1993-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:42:27.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17341\tNRW_DS24280\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.250Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWELSHNAMELIST.MDB\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323830\tUnknown\tNatasha Lough, Marine Habitats Biologist, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Science Group, CCW.\tWelsh/English dictionary of marine intertidal species names.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:43:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17340\tNRW_DS24282\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.985Z\t564\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine and Estuarine Fish Database\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323832\tThis data is stored on the DMS: Advice \u0026gt; Marine \u0026gt; Fisheries Science \u0026gt; Reports \u0026gt; Fin Fish \u0026gt; Marine and estuarine fishes of wales Entire Dataset  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/mar/fishsci/Reports/Fin%20fish/Marine%20and%20esturine%20fishes%20of%20wales%20Entire%20Dataset.xls?d\u003dwd944d693e99c4492a4b447f54c33fe9e\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tMarine and estuarine fishes are important components of the coastal environment and their presence/absence is seen as indicative of the state of inshore seas. In view of the conservation significance of marine fishes as a major component of the marine and estuarine environment the conservation agencies of the UK have statutory obligations to examine current conservation requirement of the marine fish fauna and to establish and maintain a monitoring programme by which to review their status. Other literature sources were obtained through the National Lending Library inter-library loan scheme.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2000-12-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17338\tNRW_DS24283\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.943Z\t954\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGreat Crested Newt (Triturus cristatus) Sites in Wales\t© CNC/NRW There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323833\t[A]  Species data held in Recorder 6 CCW - HQ Terr - All Wales Great Crested Newt Sites CCW000480000000C  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tLiz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\tThis datasets contains the location and abundance or presence of Great Crested Newts (Triturus cristatus) across Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to provide information on the distribution and abundance of Great Crested Newt, as it is an Annex II species listed under the EC Habitats Directive. This dataset covers the whole of Wales although the majority of records occur in Ynys Mon and on the borders of east Wales.  The dataset has been subject to validation (checking for inputting errors). Records range in accuracy from 2 to 8 figure grid references. This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Atlas.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:55.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17336\tNRW_DS24288\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.986Z\t404\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Welsh Lowland Peatland Survey (Fenbase 1977 - 1982)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323838\tOriginal paper field records held at Sheffield University.  Some copies within CCW. FENBASE: Location unknown - Access Database Site summaries (Wales Wetland Survey.xls) held by Peter Jones, TSG; Jo Hughes G:drive, TSG; Cyprian Payne N:drive (WETLSURV.XLS) Slides: Unknown\tPeter Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tThis dataset has now been incorporated into, and superseded by the new Phase 2 Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales. The survey work was split into two time periods, with the first Phase 2 lowland peatland surveys being conducted between 1977 and 1982 (this dataset). There was then a break between this and the second tranche of surveys which have occurred from 2004 onwards. These more modern surveys were conducted on sites already covered on the 1997-1982 surveys and on newer identified sites. FenBASE is an ecological database on fens maintained by the Wetland Research Group, University of Sheffield. Pre-NVC survey of 4100 quadrats of vegetation data, collected on paper and held in the FENBASE database. Includes site summaries (known as Wales Wetland Survey). Also includes some slides. FenBASE was created to provide a comprehensive database of information pertaining to the fens within England. FenBASE data were used to evaluate the potential issue of nutrient enrichment of basin fens at the regional level, and provide an overview of mitigation option applicable to those sites at risk from nutrient enrichment. It is also used to provide indication of additional data that would be appropriate to further aid assessment and evaluation.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1982-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:43:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17337\tNRW_DS24289\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.928Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Geological Sites\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323839\tFfynnon.\tRaymond Roberts, Senior Earth Scientist  Geologist/Earth Science Officer, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tAn excel spreadsheet containing the records of over 400 geological sites in Wales. Contains site names, grid references, block name, associated SSSIs, SSSI code and type of geology, county, area  etc.  Purpose of data capture:  Information on the spreadsheet was used to set up the Geological Conservation Review (GCR) database, but has continued to be updated.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:43:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17334\tNRW_DS24290\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.817Z\t351\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhoto Monitoring of Statutory Sites\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323930\tSlides from photomonitoring pre-dating digital photography are archived in approximately 10 filling cabinets in NRW\u0027s archive (Bangor). Several plastic crates and carboard boxes of protected sites monitoring images in a store-room (F29) in Maes y Ffynnon\tInternal Custodian: Tom Harrison, Environmental Assessment Officer, NE EAAT.  (Originally managed by Stuart Matley, contact Monitoring Team, Operational Support Group, NRW)  Update from Tom Harrison 2021 - This dataset needs a custodian at a higher level to take responsibility for it.  Sarah Gorton has moved some of the images from the SSSI photomonitoring dataset from plastic crates into slide hangers in filing cabinets in Maes y Ffynnon.  These have been partly catalogued (to be completed post-COVID).  The other images are still in boxes/crates or their previous filing cabinets in Maes y Ffynnon.\tSystematic photo-monitoring of statutory sites, including data, paper records and photographic images. Steps are being taken to make the data available as a MapInfo layer with pdf links to a scanned paper record and a georeferenced point of where the photos were taken. Most images are from the 1980s onwards however there are some historical images. The files are referenced by Special Site of Scientific Interest (SSSI) name, SSSI code, year and the film number. The purpose of this data capture and archiving is to record and create a collation of the change of statutory sites over time.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17333\tNRW_DS24294\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.751Z\t1714\tValid\tEditable\tRegionally Important Geodiversity Sites (RIGS)\t© AWRG and NRW. AWRG owned and restricted data:- NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly. NRW collated spatial data:- Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323934\tX:\\Physical\\Geological\\RIG Sites.lyr\tRaymond Roberts, Senior Earth Scientist  Geologist/Earth Science Officer, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  External: GeoConservation UK- The Association of UK RIGS Groups\tRegionally Important Geodiversity Sites (RIGS) are non-statutory sites selected to protect the most important places for geology, geomorphology and soils, complementing the network of legally protected Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs).   RIGS are selected for their scientific, educational, historical and aesthetic features. As stated in Planning Policy Wales, Planning Authorities should protect the features and qualities for which RIGS have been designated. The impact of proposed developments will depend on the nature of the RIGS feature, so early consultation with the local RIGS group or NRW is strongly recommended.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Association of UK Rigs Groups\t2022-03-24\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/452/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_RIG_SITES\t2025-05-09T15:57:34.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17332\tNRW_DS24299\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.711Z\t617\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInvertebrate Surveys conducted on Welsh Parklands\t© CNC/NRW, year.   There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323939\t[A]  Data is held in Recorder 6 Welsh Peatland Invertebrate Survey  CCW0006600000005\t\tTHIS HAS NOW BE MERGED INTO VARIOUS SURVEYS WITHIN RECORDER 6 UNDER THE WELSH INVERTEBRATE DATABASE (WID). THIS DATASET WAS ARCHIVED BECAUSE THE DATA WAS NOT EASILY IDENTIFIABLE IN RECORDER 6. THIS DATASET SHOULD NOT BE CONFUSED WITH THE WELSH PEATLAND INVERTEBRATE SURVEYS (WPIS).   This is a biogeographical dataset for Welsh saproxylic arthropods (invertebrates). Data generated during the Nature Conservancy Council. Welsh Parkland Invertebrate Survey (WPIS) of 130 wetland sites in Wales plus Wem Moss in Shropshire. This datasets consists of all records from sites with a suffix (WPIS), or with recorder as Welsh Peatland Invertebrate Survey.  The purpose of this data capture was to understand the distribution of species in Wales in order to inform conservation decisions.  Survey is comprised of two parts: North Wales and South Wales.\tVarious-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1990-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17335\tNRW_DS24303\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.875Z\t106919\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Survey of Great Britain\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333033\tThese are stored in the Maes-y-Ffynnon library (Classmark Research and Survey 17).\tInternal Custodian: Peter Rhind. Coastal Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSalt marshes in Great Britain represent one of a series of habitats confined largely to the coastline. They usually develop on intertidal land within the range of the normal spring tides, where colonisation by halophytic plants i.e. plants which are adapted to high salinity is and able to withstand immersion in seawater, takes place. The extent of the saltmarsh also depends on the amount of sediment available for the position, together with a combination of physical factors such as degree of exposure, tidal range, and the slope of the land. Salt marsh communities may also be found in the splash same along clifftops. However the salt marsh types are not included in this project.   This dataset consists of inventories of sites and vegetation each the major coastal habitats Great Britain specifically salt marshes. Salt marshes were selected for the first study in this series for two reasons. At the time the habitat was rare and not well studied and was one of the most threatened of coastal habitats. Consequently the salt marsh survey of Great Britain was set up in 1981 with the following objectives:  1. To review existing information on the distribution and abundance of salt marshes Great Britain  2. To identify omissions in our knowledge of the resource as a whole; and,  3. To fill the gaps in order to increase our overall knowledge of the salt marsh resource and therefore to produce as complete as possible a description of the resource to a minimum standard.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Nature Conservancy Council (NCC)\t1989-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T16:28:21.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17331\tNRW_DS24304\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.442Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGeological Conservation Review (GCR) Site Database\t© JNCC, 2015. This material contains sensitive data which must be kept confidential. Not for release by NRW.  NRW does NOT grant permission to disseminate or re-use this data. Those seeking such permission must approach the originator directly.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333034\tThe database is held and maintained by JNCC\t\tThe Geological Conservation Review (GCR) was a major initiative launched in 1977 to identify and describe the most important geological sites in Britain. The GCR site database contains details of Geological Conservation Review sites in Wales, such as notification date and priority, unit authority and GCR number.  Purpose of data capture: The GCR was designed to identify those sites of national and international importance needed to show all the key scientific elements of the Earth heritage of Britain.  Methods of data capture: Site evaluation and documentation have resulted in the creation of an inventory of approximately 3500 GCR sites, selected for around 100 categories (the GCR Blocks) encompassing the range of geological and geomorphological features of Britain.  Geographic coverage: England, Scotland and Wales  Temporal coverage: The site evaluation and documentation ocurred between 1977 and 1990. New sites are added to the database when necessary.\tJoint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)\t1990-12-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:43:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17380\tNRW_DS24307\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.044Z\t708\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDormouse Database (1905 - 2004)\t© CNC/NRW, 2004.   Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333037\t[A]  A welsh abstract of the species data is held in Recorder 6 Dormouse Database - NE - Welsh Extract CCW000380000000C\t\tThe database was constructed to hold data arising from the Great Nut Hunt and build a complete and up to date picture of the distribution of the dormouse. This has been used for reporting on the delivery of the Biodiversity Action Plan. England and Wales. The dormouse does not occur in Scotland or Northern Ireland. The dataset has relied largely on contributed data rather than proactive survey, so it suffers from bias towards places that people visited during the Great Nut Hunts. Grid references would normally refer to the centroid of a wood where the dormouse was found, rather than the actual point. Many grid references have not been checked on a GIS to confirm that they fall within the woodland boundary; this is an on-going process and would normally result in an 8-figure (10m resolution) grid reference being used.  This dataset includes the name of recorder, site name, grid reference and type of sighting (for example dead or alive, location of nest).  Records originate from a variety of sources, with the collation held and maintained by Tony Mitchell-Jones at English Nature. It is an incomplete directory of sites, some of which are monitored annually as part of the national monitoring scheme. Only the most recent record for each site is included in this dataset, historical data is held in the original database. The source data also includes comments and the record source. Records use 6 figure British National Grid references (precision unknown), all give the centre of the site.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17383\tNRW_DS24310\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.096Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGeological Conservation Review (GCR) Site Photomonitoring Data\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333130\tHafod Elfyn\u0027s archival store, CCW Headquarters.\tRaymond Roberts, Senior Earth Scientist  Geologist/Earth Science Officer, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tA black and white, fixed point, archival-quality photographic survey of most GCR sites in Wales.  The images were captured to provide a baseline when CCW was created in 1990/1991.  Work was carried out by CCW\u0027s Earth Science Officers between 1991 and 1992, to establish a baseline of site condition for GCR sites in Wales. The negatives and contact prints were specially processed to ensure the longest possible storage life.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:56:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17384\tNRW_DS24311\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.097Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMapinfo Habitats Database on Candidate Special Areas of Conservation (cSACs)\t© CNC/NRW You may reproduce this dataset free of charge for non-commercial and internal business purposes in any format or medium, provided that you do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. To avoid using old information, we recommend that you obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333131\t\tGIS Data Manager, NRW.\tMapinfo habitat databases on 23 candidate Special Areas of Conservation (cSACs) comprising 5 to 20000 records. Used to fulfill requirements of the Habitats Directive to set up protected areas in the marine environment. Collaborations with relevant authorities established operational management schemes on candidate marine SACs, around the United Kingdom, the locations of which are located on the map of projects and special features. Also available on an interactive map on the web.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:09.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17388\tNRW_DS24313\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.210Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) Data for Wales\t© CNC/NRW \u0026amp; JNCC 1992 Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333133\t\tPeter Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) data for Wales only. This data is used as historical records of the floristic data used in compiling the Nation Vegetation Classification (NVC). The quadrat data are also valuable as historical documentation of the floristic composition of geo-referenced localities in Wales about 25 years ago.\tJoint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1992-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:52:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17390\tNRW_DS24316\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.221Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Voles Site Records\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333136\t\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team (Formerly Liz Halliwell) R. Gwynne\tThis database of water vole (Arvicola terrestris) site records (including grid references) is being developed by the North Wales Riparian Mammals Group (Menter Mon, Anglesey) as part of a UK steering group stategy (CCW, EA, Wildlife Trust and others).\tNorth Wales Riparian Mammals Group\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:09.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17386\tNRW_DS24323\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.219Z\t402\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcidification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333233\tStatistical data held on paper and Recorder 6 Geographic data held digitally in MapInfo\tSimon Bareham, Team Leader Environmental Futures, SPG, NRW. (Project Officer at the time)\tThe dataset was collected as part of a specific project and is no longer used by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for reporting purposes. This dataset is to be kept for reference and as part of former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 data archive. The data was collected between 1992 and 1993 and used as part of a specific data modelling exercise. Site specific information about Welsh Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) that are under threat from acidification, with predicted damage under three scenarios of acid rain reduction. 218 sites are involved at the worst level of acidification and 86 sites are involved under the best scenario modelled (out of 845 SSSIs in Wales in total). The data include the area of the site at risk and the communities at risk. In the UK Critical Loads Advisory Group report on Critical and Target Load Maps for Soils in Great Britain the production of soils critical loads maps is explained as follows: The soil information is based on 1km squares of the UK Ordnance Survey national grid and on 1:250000 scale soil maps and supporting databases. The soil maps for Wales was produced and published by the Soil survey and land research centre. The critical load assigned to each 1 km square was the Critical for the soil association what occupied the largest proportion of search square. Each map unit was placed in one of five soil classes. These five soil classes were based on soil mineralogy.  This dataset was created to help describe the \u0027stock at risk\u0027 according to the Critical load exceeding limits (due to sulphur) at a 1 km resolution. The dataset was used to as an assessment of the biological SSSIs within areas where the soil\u0027s critical load for sulphur was predicted to exceed in 2005, when the agreed 60 percent reduction in SO2 from large combustion plants in Europe is implemented. The data was also used to consider the ecological benefits which may accrue if greater emission reduction targets are agreed. A possible 80 percent reduction was examined and compared with the predicted effects of the current scenario.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:24.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17387\tNRW_DS24327\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.200Z\t750\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Sand Dune Data\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333237\tThe site reports are available from NRW\u0027s library.   Biological Data held in Recorder 6 Sand Dune Survey Wales CCW0007600000001  Spreadsheets were stored on the personal drive of NRW\u0027s Coastal Ecologist (Peter Rhind, retired). There is a data set here:- Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\RhindP\\Dune Spreadsheets.  According to Julie Creer this appears to be  \u0027Peter Rhind\u0027s own interrogation of the Phase 2 sand dune survey data (both the UK and Wales), which I suspect he got from the paper versions of survey. Looking at a few of the spreadsheets it looks like he separated out the different sand dune NVC communities and then tabulated which sites had those NVC communities and then added in the quadrat data tables for each site of that NVC type. S, in short, this is his analysis of that data and not the original dataset per se. These are most useful to me, but I’m not sure useful to anyone else?\u0027\tInternal Custodian: Julie Creer, Lead Specialist Advisor – Coastal Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSand Dune National Vegetation Classification (NVC) spreadsheets containing area and DOMIN values for each NVC community. The purpose of this data capture was to characterise the sand dune vegetation of Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1995-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:14:44.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17381\tNRW_DS24328\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.086Z\t1292\tValid\tEditable\tPembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas (PMSA)\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders 1998. NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source. This data is comprised of a mixture of third party owned records and NRW records. NRW policies permits access to and release of this data under the same terms as its own data. Part of this dataset is sensitive and restricted. The sensitive data is as follows: - King Scallop, Pecten maximus (below 10km square). - Queen Scallop, Aequipecten opercularis (below 10km square). - Native Oyster, Ostrea edulis (below 10km square). - Crayfish, Palinurus elephas (below 10km square). - Seahorse, Hippocampus guttulatus (below 10km square). - Short snouted seahorse, Hippocampus hippocampus (below 10km square). - Grey seal, Halichoerus grypus (below 5km square). - All cetaceans, Phocoena phocoena (below 10km square). - Oyster bed habitat IMX.Ost (biotope code) (below 20km square). Sensitive data must be kept confidential; general release is not permitted due to risk of harm to the environment or third parties. Sensitive data may only be released under an appropriate NRW Licence. Requests should be referred to NRW\u0027s Access to Information Officer. Data may be released freely if the sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333238\tData is archived under project no. 109 and 156, and media no. 382 and 394. Species Data is held in Marine Recorder.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas  Some of the paper MNCR forms are held in off-site storage (Box CCW/MNCR/1, Box CCW/MNCR/2, Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tMonica Jones, Data Services Team Leader, EPP, NRW\tThis datasets acts as an atlas of marine species for Pembrokeshire including flora and fauna from the intertidal and sublittoral marine zones, funded by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW).    The purpose of this data capture was to collate and make accessible all historical marine species data for Pembrokeshire between 1899 and 1998. The majority of records are from 1956 to 1998, and this dataset is now classed as static.   This dataset is a collation of data from various CCW and third party sources (both published and unpublished) that cover Pembrokeshire. It is comprised of 79 surveys  (available to download on NBN Gateway, see resource locator). This is a historical collation which is not being updated.    The data includes all records from the Dale Fort Marine Fauna survey plus additional data from subsequent and prior surveys.  Data that are included in the Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNRC) database have been excluded from this dataset to avoid duplication.   This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Marine Recorder which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tVarious including volunteer recorders-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)-Field Studies Council (FSC)\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-11-19T10:23:47.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17389\tNRW_DS24333\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.219Z\t822\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Phase 1 Intertidal Habitat Survey\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release. An un-redacted version can be requested and may be provided under licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333333\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal Data is archived under project no. 204, media no\u0027s 34-35,148,194-195,569,1157-1183,1185,1187-1190 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media etc.)  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tPaul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tThis is a dataset compiled of spatially-referenced survey data obtained from different intertidal communities (biotopes) in Wales. Maps include information on biotope location, species composition, access and photographs points.  Additional information is recorded as target notes.  Further site information, including site descriptions,  geomorphology, uses, impacts, target notes and habitat information is held in an accompanying database  Intertidal mapped data provides an integrated information resource for Local Authority structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.   The maps give important regional information about habitat and biotope distribution. They are used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments, assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), as a basis for the development of sensitivity mapping, and as a baseline for developing monitoring of intertidal features in marine Special Areas of Conservation (SAC). Sites are mapped once and therefore are a \u0027snapshot\u0027 in time.  All data has been subject to a validation process. The data available to the public under an Open Government Licence has some information redacted.  An un-redacted version can be requested and may be provided under NRW licence.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-08-30\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3607/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_intertidal_phase_1_habitat_survey\t2025-11-07T16:06:24.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17377\tNRW_DS24337\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.962Z\t490\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Lesser Horseshoe Bat (Rhinolophus hipposideros) Summer Roost Surveillance Database (1987-2013)\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333337\tSpecies data: Held in Recorder 6 database   - CCW-HQ-Terr-Welsh Lesser Horseshoe Bat Summer Roost Surveillance 1987 to 2006  CCW0003800000002   - CCW-HQ-Terr-Welsh Lesser Horseshoe Bat Summer Roost Surveillance 2006 to 2011  LC0003850000006H\tInternal Contact: Jean Matthews, Mammal Ecologist Bats and Riparian Mammals, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe monitoring of Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros) is required under the EC Habitats Directive and contributes towards the UK Biodiversity Action Plan for the species. This dataset comprises of two summer surveillance collations for Welsh Lesser Horseshoe bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros). The two collations date from 1987 to 2006 and from 2006 to 2011. They contain the results of annual summer surveillance of selected Lesser Horseshoe bat maternity and nursery roost sites. It includes a range of associated environmental data, such as temperature and weather conditions. The purpose of this data capture was to provide standardised information for planning and development control purposes. Data is used to support notification and subsequent monitoring of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). This dataset is now classed as static as no data is currently added to these records. Instead these records are now sent directly to the Bat Conservation Trust as part of their National Bat Monitoring Programme.\tVarious including volunteer recorders-County bat groups-Vincent Wildlife Trust-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:43.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17373\tNRW_DS24341\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.922Z\t628\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCors Fochno Invertebrates Pitfall Trapping Survey\t© CNC/NRW 1996. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333431\t[A]  Data held in Recorder 6 under Welsh Invertebrate Database: - CCW Staff- Adrain Fowles CCW000660000000C  - Cors Fochno Pitfall Survey CCW0006600000GCB.\tInternal Contact: Mike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.   Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset was collected to assess the occurrence of invertebrate species at Cors Fochno Ceredigion between 1987 and 2002.  The species were recorded in order to investigate the effect of a major fire in 1986 on the raised mire invertebrate assemblage. The data will be used for CSM assemblage monitoring.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:23.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17382\tNRW_DS24350\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.035Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Parkland Survey (Phase 1)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333530\t\tMike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, TEG, NRW (Formerly Adrian Fowles, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, CCW)\tDatabase of Phase 1 Welsh Parkland Survey detailing number of over-mature trees and aerial photograph information for 201 parkland, pasture and woodland sites in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:13:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17376\tNRW_DS24352\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.855Z\t347\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUnmanaged Semi-Natural Vegetation in Welsh Uplands\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333532\tG drive of Upland Ecologist, TSG.\tBarbara Jones, Upland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW.\tLists of upland sites (often small enclosures) in Wales fenced off from grazing.  The dataset contains site name, grid reference, habitat type present, grazing species found, grazing pressure, management regime and actions such as burning, brush cutting, monitoring and  publications referenced. Also has a section for fencing and canopy and regeneration species.  The database was commissioned by the Uplands Habitat Working Group in 1998.  Data entry was frozen in 1999.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:13:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17378\tNRW_DS24420\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.047Z\t368\tUnassessed\tEditable\tProtected Sites Data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t© CNC/NRW 2015. NRW data may be used for non-commercial and internal business purposes only. It may be interrogated, imported into your own system and merged with other data, providing it is not factually altered. You may duplicate and publish short extracts of raw data and any findings and other information derived from the data, providing NRW is acknowledged. You may not copy the dataset or transfer it to others; additional copies can be obtained from NRW.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343230\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tSSSI and Species Protection Team Leader, Operational Support Group, CCW\tDataset providing information on Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSIs), Special Areas of Conservation (SACs), Special Protection Areas (SPAs), RAMSAR Sites, Geological Conservation Review Sites (GCRs), Mainre Conservation Zones (MCZs), Local Nature Reserves (LNRs), Marine Nature Reserves (MNRs), National Nature Reserves (NNRs), Biogenetic reserves, Biosphere reserves, National Parks, Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONBs). The information varies depending on the designation.  SSSIs have site description, PDOs, historical data on notifications, site area and central gri reference, cross refrence sites and responsible officers. Other designations have similar information excluding the PDO list which is only relevant to SSSIs.  There are also some Commons with limited data recorded on the system.   The purpose of this data capture was to collate information on protected sites in Wales, and provide historical data on designation dates aiding the reporting process for designations in Wales. The dataset is derived from a pre Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and is now owned, managed and updated by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and is an ongoing system updated as and when changes occur to a desination or when a new site is designated.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:20:23.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17385\tNRW_DS24421\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.138Z\t236\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLand Agency Data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t© CNC/NRW 2015. NRW data may be used for non-commercial and internal business purposes only. It may be interrogated, imported into your own system and merged with other data, providing it is not factually altered. You may duplicate and publish short extracts of raw data and any findings and other information derived from the data, providing NRW is acknowledged. You may not copy the dataset or transfer it to others; additional copies can be obtained from NRW.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343231\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tWest Region Regional Admin Manager, NRW\tManages award and payment process for management agreements on National Nature Reserves (NNRs) and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), including land purchases, and buildings leases.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:14:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17379\tNRW_DS24423\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.986Z\t334\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales Survey Inventory (WALSIN) Data\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343233\tThis dataset is held in the DMS: Knowledge \u0026gt; Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Records Management \u0026gt; CCW Records \u0026gt; Walsin Records  KNOW-55-75   https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/kdim/Records-Management/CCW%20Records/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?id\u003d%2Fteams%2Fkdim%2FRecords%2DManagement%2FCCW%20Records%2FWalsin%20Records\u0026amp;viewid\u003db151fb6e%2Dc7d5%2D4ca4%2Da30d%2D4d258f16d972   This is a legacy CCW dataset which used to be held in the ISIS Database before it was moved to the DMS. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files. [A] Was held in ISIS database under WALSIN ISIS database  [B] Was Held in ISIS (Oracle) database.\tData Custodian: Beth Hall - Library and Information Services Team Leader.  Support Desk Team Leader, Information Group. NRW\tIndex to all environmental surveys of NC/NCC since 1949  and bibliography of all files held by NCC, including archives from approximately 1900 until March 1992. It lists every file note that holds survey information but it does not hold actual data. Every survey has a separate entry on the database consisting of two screens: the first screen contains structured data giving bibliographic, geographic and scientific details for the survey, the second, a summary of the survey\u0027s objectives, methodology, results and conclusions together with notes on the number of sites in the survey, maps and photographs.  Generally the records are limited to one line of information in the objectives, methodology, results and conclusions fields.  WALSIN can only be used for archival storage and retrieval and does not allow new data input.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17370\tNRW_DS24424\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.901Z\t378\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFeatures data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) database\t© CNC/NRW 2014 There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343234\tSafle (Protected Sites) Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.  (Formerly held on ISIS)\tInternal Custodian: Holly York, Advisor 2, Protected Sites (SSSIs), Biodiversity, Ecosystems \u0026amp; Geodiversity\tThis dataset holds information on qualifying and non-qualifying features (species and habitats) on Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs). The purpose of this data capture is to maintain and have access to information on Wales\u0027 qualifying, non-qualifying and notified features (species and habitats)  on SSSIs, SACs and SPAs. Data entry began in 2005 and is ongoing as new sites are designated and existing sites redesignated.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:11:56.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17375\tNRW_DS24425\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.883Z\t8\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring Data Held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database (Archived 2021)\t© CNC/NRW 2015. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343235\tISIS Database. Electronic. Formerly available to NRW staff over intranet and site files. This system has now been closed down and replaced by the protected sites management system. Currently there is no evidence \u0026amp; monitoring module on the system.\tInternal Custodian: Christine Edwards, Lead Specialist Advisor – Protected Sites programme       Metadata was archived 17th August 2021. Dylan Lloyd is the new custodian for this and has highlighted that after the ISIS System was decommissioned monitoring data was moved to the  protected sites baseline review data (124833) and will be used in the future possibly to populate a newer monitoring module when created.\tA collation of ,now archived, which SSSI Features of protected sites in Wales have been monitored. The previous limited monitoring data was incorporated into the Baseline protected sites condition review project data (124833). This will then be imported into the replacement to ISIS i.e. the Protected Sites Management System when the \u0027Evidence and Monitoring Module\u0027 has been built. This new system will then be the repository for the site feature monitoring data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:23:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17372\tNRW_DS24426\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.836Z\t227\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOpen Access held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t© CNC/NRW 2015. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343236\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tRecreation Information Development Officer, Recreation \u0026amp; Access Group, NRW\tThis dataset contains data in relation to the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000, where members of the public and people with a registered interest in land known as mountain, moor, heath or down have consulted Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in relation to parcels of land.  The data covers enquiries about the CRoW Act, land parcels, requests for leaflets, publications and maps, and advice or guidance. The bulk of the data relates to specific land parcels in the 14 A to O consultation areas in Wales (for draft, provisional and conclusive mapping), along with descriptions of the land, decisions related to the land in terms of whether or not it is mountain, moor, heath or down and appeals based on decisions about it\u0027s land status.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:18:42.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17369\tNRW_DS24428\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.840Z\t210\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLicencing held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t© CNC/NRW and various third party recorders NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343238\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tSpecies Protection Officer, Operational Support Group, NRW\tDetails of the species licences requested and granted by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to individuals and organisations. CCW\u0027s Operational Support Group received licence applications from individuals/companies/organisations detailing who is requesting it, for what type of activity, affecting which species, where, etc. The Species Protection Administrator enters the information into the Licensing ISIS (Oracle) database before sending the application to specialists for consultation. The licence is then either issued or not, if issued, details of the licence are added to ISIS.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:16:25.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17366\tNRW_DS24715\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.254Z\t1049\tValid\tEditable\tTerrestrial Phase 1 Habitat Survey\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Non-sensitive data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234373135\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Terrestrial\\Original_Phase_1    Original UVS maps stored NRW Maes y Fynnon.  Copies held in site reports.   Original NVC maps held in site reports.  Ph1 Survey \u0027master maps\u0027 archived at National Library of Wales; raster and vector data and 1km sq table are stored in the Geospatial Central Data Store.\tFor scientific issues; Jim Latham, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team Leader.     For data specification issues \u0026amp; Metadata; Jon Rothwell,  Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping.  Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     For additional information on the \u0027master map\u0027 archive contact Paul Joyner, NLW, Aberystwyth, Ceredigion SY23 3BU; tel 01970 632800\tThis dataset holds comprehensive habitat cover data for the whole of Wales derived from a programme of field recording that was begun by the Wales Field Unit (WFU) of the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) in 1979 and continued by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) after restructuring in 1991.   The purpose of this data capture was to inform site selection, casework and policy decisions. The lowland survey was particularly used to locate grassland sites suitable for more in-depth survey for notification purposes.  Habitat extent data has been derived to inform the Local Biodiversity Action Plan process.   The digitisation was carried out, in the first instance, for the drafting of maps for open country and registered common land as required by the CROW Act 2000. For reporting purposes during the lowland phase of the surveys, Wales was divided up into 15 different areas based on the Nature Conservancy Council\u0027s (NCC) Areas of Search (AoS).  The dataset is unique, in that it holds full census field survey habitat data for the whole of terrestrial Wales. It has been used widely for research, advice and planning both within and outside CCW.     The majority of the fieldwork was carried out \u0027in house\u0027 by closely co-ordinated teams of trained surveyors. Every effort was made to ensure consistency of habitat mapping.  A check on surveyor accuracy during the early stages of the Phase 1 Survey revealed comparatively high repeatability levels (Kerry, S. M., Rimes, C. A., Smith, S. L. N., Williams, A. M. 1994. An assessment of errors in Phase 1 habitat survey in Wales.  CCW report no. 94/4/1, Countryside Council for Wales, Bangor; Stevens, J.P., Blackstock, T.H., Howe, E.A., Stevens, D.P., 2004. Repeatability of Phase 1 habitat survey. J. Environ. Manage. 73, 53-59), and checks on the Upland Vegetation Survey data were carried out between 1994-1996 (Yeo, M.J.M., Blackstock, T.H., 2002.   A vegetation analysis of the pastoral landscapes of upland Wales, UK. J. Veg. Sci. 13, 803-816).    Digital maps were checked for accuracy at three stages: by contractor (Cynefin Environmental), and by CCW (C. Burrows, L. Howe, J. Stevens, J. Rothwell). 1km square table: the data were input and checked by a contractor (C. Copp) and re-checked by CCW (C. Burrows and J. Stevens). For a detailed overview of the background, methods and outputs of the Survey see Blackstock, T. H., Howe, E. A., Stevens, J. P., Burrows, C. R., Jones, P. S. 2010. Habitats of Wales. A comprehensive field survey 1979 -1997.  University of Wales Press, Cardiff.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-08-16\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3597/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_terrestrial_phase_1_habitat_survey\t2025-05-09T15:48:53.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17371\tNRW_DS45523\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.835Z\t380\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPeregrine breeding population surveillance in Pembrokeshire (1981-2013)\t© CNC/NRW and Pembrokeshire Bird Group. 2013. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence.  No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3435353233\tData is stored in Recorder 6 45 VC Pembs: Peregrine breeding sites CCW000080000004.  The data is published on the NBN Gateway within the Stackpole National Nature Reserve Species Inventory and Ad Hoc sightings from around Pembrokeshire (extracted from Recorder 6). See Parent ID.\tInternal Custodian: Paul Culyer - Senior Reserve Manager, Operational Services, South, NRW (Used to be Bob Haycock - Senior Reserve Manager, Stackpole, NRW.)  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis is a non spatial datasets map up of species data from the surveillance of Peregrine (Falco peregrinus) breeding populations around Pembrokeshire, south Wales. The surveys cover both coastal and inland monitoring, and just contains Peregrine data points. There were both local and national reasons for this data collection. Local Purposes: a) To maintain records of Pembrokeshire breeding population of peregrines, to service update/data requirements for local peregrine Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP), in partnership with other members of Pembrokeshire Bird Group.  b) To assess the condition of peregrine as an Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) feature in Castlemartin Cliffs and Dunes SSSI for Castlemartin Range Integrated Land Management Plan. c) To maintain records of concentrations of peregrine relevant to management of recreation (sea cliff-climbing and coasteering)   National Purposes:  a) To contribute to decadal national/international surveys - normally managed by the British Trust for Ornithology (BTO).  b) To contribute to all Wales database maintained by the RSPB, via Wales Raptor Study Group (when requested).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Pembrokeshire Bird Group\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17374\tNRW_DS45632\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.923Z\t710\tUnassessed\tEditable\tYr Wyddfa/Snowdon Terrestrial Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site\t© CNC/NRW and WG 2014. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ Welsh Government and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.   Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence.  No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3435363332\tSee individual metadata entries in the \u0027contains\u0027 field above for more details\tInternal Custodian: Environmental Change Network Project Officer, Operational Support Group, NRW. Environmental Change Network Data Officer, Operational Support Group, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tLong term monitoring of physical, chemical and biological variables on Yr Wyddfa/Snowdon. The monitoring is designed to collect, store, analyse and interpret long-term datasets for physical, chemical and biological variables, which drive or respond to environmental change. The Yr Wyddfa monitoring contributes to the national Environmental Change Network. It is one of 12 terrestrial ECN sites across the UK and is important within the context of the network because it extends its climatic and ecological coverage, as well as the geographical distribution (it\u0027s the only montane site under oceanic influence and has the highest rainfall). The ECN\u0027s objectives are to establish and maintain a network of sites from which to obtain comparable long-term datasets through the monitoring of a range of variables; to integrate and analyse these data to identify natural and man-induced environmental changes, improve understanding of the causes of change, distinguish short-term fluctuations from long-term trends, and predict future changes.  NRW also carries out other monitoring at the site which contributes to a variety of national monitoring initiatives, see lineage fields of the individual metadata entries in the \u0027contains\u0027 field below.\tWelsh Government-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17365\tNRW_DS45660\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.061Z\t1190\tUnassessed\tEditable\tChough Breeding Population Surveillance in Pembrokeshire\t© CNC/NRW and Pembrokeshire Coast National Park 2004. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/ Pembrokeshire Coast National Park and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.  Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence.  No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3435363630\tRaw south Pembrokeshire data are held by NRW at Stackpole office;  Remainder by Pembrokeshire Coast National Park All Wales colour-ringed data are held by Tony Cross/Adrienne Stratford (and by RSPB)\tStackpole office (see contact details above); BTO/NRW annual Schedule 1 Licence reports (location unknown).\tSurveillance of breeding population (including productivity and recruitment) and non-breeding population of Chough (Pyrrhocorax pyrrhocorax) in Pembrokeshire, South Wales. Former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) collect and collate data from the south Pembrokeshire coastline which includes Caldey Island, and the Castlemartin Coast Special Protection Area (SPA).  SPAs are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive, which came into force in April 1979. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive), and for regularly occurring migratory species. Castlemartin SPA is designed for breeding and over wintering choughs. This includes the Pembrokeshire Coastal National Park (PCNP) coordinate records from St Anne\u0027s Head to Cemaes Head and the offshore islands, including Ramsey (SPA) and St David\u0027s Head). In this dataset some records date from early 1960s forming part of the first national survey; recording was established at the main study area in 1978 but most records date from 1982.  There were numerous national and local purposes to this data capture.   National purposes:  - To contribute to decadal national and international surveys of chough and to update JNCC records for reporting under the Birds Directive (SPAs) and Habitats Directive (Special Areas of Conservation (SAC)).   Local purposes  - To assess condition of chough as an Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) feature in Castlemartin Coast SPA; - To maintain up-to-date records of concentrations of chough relevant to management of recreation (sea cliff-climbing and coasteering); - To service update data requirements for local chough Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP), in partnership with other members of a local chough study group, including PCNP Authority who coordinate records for rest of Pembrokeshire.\tPembrokeshire Coast National Park-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:17.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17368\tNRW_DS45924\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.247Z\t14\tUnassessed\tEditable\t1 Otter Survey Wales 2000 to 2004 - ***  FULL FIELD TEST NOT REAL DATA  *** update two - Third Party Dataset\tState the Copy Right and Use Constraints for the Dataset:\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3435393234\tThe location of the data internally: EXAMPLE: Data is archived as project 450, media 1372. (Or) GIS Corporate Data Store: X-Layer_Files\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Ramsar.lyr   Mammal Ecologist office Terrestrial Science Group CCW.\tData Custodian:  Jonathon Rothwell - Terrestrial Mapping Scientist NRW.  Or contact Mammal Ecologists, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.\t*** This metadata entry is NOT REAL data. ***  It is just acting as a test metadata entry for use internally within NRW and as an example for staff who are entering metadata.  Description to be included in the Abstract: - Purpose of data capture. - Geographic Coverage. - Temporal Coverage. - Give context to the data collected.  - Be as detailed as possible.   EXAMPLE: The aim of this data collection was to monitoring for otter spraints, holts and sightings on all major river systems and their tributaries across Wales.Surveying was carried out between April and October between 2000 and 2004. Each river was visited only once each year. The river systems covered included Usk, Tywi and Wye, all of which are designated under the Habitats Directive as Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).\tCoal Authority\t1991-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:43:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17367\tNRW_DS46083\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.239Z\t656\tUnassessed\tEditable\tStoneworts in Wales (Recorder 6 Dataset)\t© CNC/NRW and Third Party Recorders. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.  Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436303833\t[A]  Species Records are available in Recorder 6  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tJulian Woodman, Higher Plant Specialist, Terrestrial Species, NRW\tA collation of all known records of stoneworts in Wales from 1726 up till August 2007. Stoneworts (or charophytes) are a group of algae that grow in fresh and brackish water and have a relatively complex structure. Stoneworts are good indicators of water quality due to their sensitivities towards pollution, including nutrient enrichment acting as a bio-indicator.  Many are of conservation importance. 30 species have been recorded in the UK and 17 of these occur in Wales. Of the latter, four are Red Data Book species, six are Nationally Scarce, and 9 are considered rare in Wales; three are Priority Species in the UK Biodiversity Action Plane (BAP) and are included on the National Assembly of Wales\u0027 List of Species and Habitats of Principal Importance for the Conservation of Biological Diversity (Section 74 list).  All data collated and verified at source by known expert.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various including volunteer recorders\t2007-09-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17364\tNRW_DS46084\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.017Z\t871\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Invertebrate Data for Wales\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436303834\t[A]  Original data is held in Recorder 6 within the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID).  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tMike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\tThis dataset is a collation of all confirmed occurrence records of UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority invertebrate species reported from Wales. The aim of the data was to collate information on UK BAP invertebrates in Wales and inform Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) partners. Data accuracy is reliant on original sources. Spatial references, dates and species identifications have been validated by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) invertebrate experts checking printed lists of records, and literature references have been added where possible.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various including volunteer recorders\t2001-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17363\tNRW_DS46086\t2024-05-28T12:21:21.926Z\t449\tUnassessed\tEditable\tContacts Data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t© CCGC/NRW 2015 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of a NRW Restricted Release Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436303836\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tAndrew Peterken, Designated Sites Project Manager, South and East Region, NRW\tThe Contacts data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) database information of individuals and organisations who do business with the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), who now form part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  The purpose of this data capture was to collate information on Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 contacts.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:12:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17362\tNRW_DS46100\t2024-05-28T12:21:21.883Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLichens of Conservation Concern - Site Data for Wales\t© CNC/NRW and third party recorders Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners.  It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313030\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas  The Threatened Lichen Database is basically defunct now - all data for Threatened Lichens are stored along with those for all other bryophytes by the British Lichen Society and there is no point in NRW storing them separately as that just leads to duplication and errors.\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team (Formerly Alan Hale, Lower Plant Ecologist, CCW)\tA collation of distribution data for 123 species of Red Data Book and National Biodiversity Action Plan lichens. This dataset is historic and is not being updated. New records and corrections are being collated in the Wales Threatened Lichens dataset (see separate metadata entry). The purpose of this data capture as to assist in informing the process of conservation objective setting.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-British Lichen Society-Various including volunteer recorders\t2002-01-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:13:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17361\tNRW_DS46129\t2024-05-28T12:21:21.741Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tArctic-Alpine Plant Monitoring for Effects of Climate Change\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313239\tUnknown\tJonathon Rothwell, Terrestrial Mapping Specialist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  The data was collected orginially by: Alex Turner, H Roberts, and B Jones.\tThis data consists of 6 arctic-alpine species monitoring results. The purpose of this data capture was to determine the long-term effects of climate change on a specific group of arctic-alpine species.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17411\tNRW_DS46133\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.411Z\t631\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra) Survey (1994 - 2002)\t© CNC/NRWand AWCSS Partnership 2002. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW / AWCSS Partnership and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313333\tAssociated data is held as Project No. 19, media 537 - Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media501to600\\Media537\tThe internal data custodians are: Marine Ornithologist, NRW and Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. The metadata point of contact: Data Services Team\tThis dataset comprises of results from an aerial survey of common scoters, Melanitta nigra, (seabirds) in Carmarthen Bay, Cardigan Bay and Liverpool Bay. The data was collected to provide information for Special Protection Areas (SPA) designations and monitoring.   Common Scoter feed on bivalves and other invertebrates such as polychaetes and amphipods which are found in abundance in the shallow waters (less than 10 m) in Carmarthen Bay.\tAll Wales Common Scoter Survey Partnership (AWCSS Partnership)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T15:22:44.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17413\tNRW_DS46136\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.415Z\t693\tValid\tEditable\tSeabird Colony Surveillance along Castlemartin Coastline, South Pembrokeshire\t© CNC/NRW 2001. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313336\tRaw data are held by NRW at Stackpole and by JNCC -  electronic copies annually forwarded to their Seabird data coordinator.\tSenior Reserve Warden - Stackpole, NRW.\tThis dataset contains the results from the surveillance of breeding seabird species including: auks (guillemot, razorbill, puffin), large Larus gulls (herring, lesser black-back, great black-back), kittiwake, shag and fulmar and the surveillance of productivity of kittiwakes.   The overall purposes of the data capture was to contribute to the JNCC-managed Seabird Monitoring Programme.   Local purposes for the data capture were to: - Assess SSSI feature condition (seabird assemblage) in two adjacent Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) - Maintain up-to-date records of regionally high concentrations of seabirds relevent to management of recreation (sea cliff-climbing and coasteering)   - Service update/data requirements for local regional marine pollution contingency plans.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T15:29:28.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17418\tNRW_DS46140\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.589Z\t703\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGreater Horseshoe Bat (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum) breeding productivity surveillance in Pembrokeshire\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release. .\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313430\tStackpole office, NRW\tInternal Custodian:  Paul Culyer, Senior Reserve Manager - Pembrokeshire, Operational Services, South (used to be Bob Haycock, Senior Reserve Warden - Stackpole, NRW.)  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe greater horseshoe bat Rhinolophus ferrumequinum, nursery roosts at Stackpole and Slebech, Pembrokeshire, are principal summer roosts of the West Wales population of the species which ranges through Pembrokeshire, Carmarthenshire, Ceredigion and Gower, West Glamorgan. Monitoring the population is difficult, as for much of the year only a small percentage are accessible through winter surveys of hibernacula. Monitoring the status of the population is therefore only practical at the nursery roosts. This dataset consists of Greater Horseshoe Bat (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum) surveillance results. The surveillance of the breeding productivity of Greater Horseshoe Bat i.e. numbers of young born annually occurs between June and August. The purpose of this data capture was to: -  Provide information and updates to the local Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) -  Assess feature conditions of greater horseshoe bats within Pembrokeshire Bat Sites Special Area of Conservation (SAC), and 3 component Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI).  -  Contribute to the management database of the breeding population records for Greater Horseshoe Bats species recovers programme and the UK BAP species programmes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17406\tNRW_DS46159\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.343Z\t353\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of Horseshoe Bats with Automatic Bat Counters\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313539\tAfter preliminary analysis the data are held in spreadsheets on NRW corporate document store (Ffynnon). H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\EIT\\Staff (ALL)\\Cyprian Payne\\Bats\tStuart Kay or Barnaby Letheren for technical questions Jean Matthews, Mammal Ecologist Bats and Riparian Mammals, NRW.\tAutomated bat counters have been deployed at various important roost sites for Greater and Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum and Rhinolophus hipposideros). These collect roost entrance and exit numbers for the bats as well as some limited environmental data. The purpose of this data capture was to obtain reliable population data, in order to relate the changes in bat population and behaviour to changes in the climate and environment in an attempt to understand the factors that limit the geographical ranges of the 2 species. In Wales, Greater Horseshoe Bats breed at the northern limit of their distribution and Lesser Horseshoe Bats breed up to the north coast of Wales where they are fairly common but they are not found at this latitude in England or the mainland of Europe. The sample roosts are within Special Areas of Conservation. The data gives valuable information on the seasonal activity of these bats which can be directly related to management within and around these key roost sites.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:24.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17415\tNRW_DS46160\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.468Z\t992\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313630\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Lowland grassland   a)  Reports: TSG N:drive and Archive Room.   b)  Recorder 6 database - All surveys related to this are tagged as Phase 2 lowland grassland surveys  - LGSW Brecknock CCW0002600000009     - LGSW Carmarthen \u0026amp; Dinefwr CCW0002600000008     - LGSW Ceredigion CCW0002600000005     - LGSW Clwyd CCW0002600000003     - LGSW East Gwynedd CCW0002600000002     - LGSW Gwent CCW0002600000012     - LGSW Mid \u0026amp; South Glamorgan CCW0002600000011     - LGSW Montgomery CCW0002600000004     - LGSW Preseli \u0026amp; South Pembroke CCW0002600000007     - LGSW Radnor CCW0002600000006     - LGSW West Glamorgan \u0026amp; Llanelli CCW0002600000010     - LGSW West Gwynedd CCW0002600000001     c)  GIS layers  X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Lowland grassland.   Document explaining file structure can be found here:- DMS\\Evidence\\Terrestrial Habitats\\Lowland Grassland Phase II Survey\\All Wales\\Database - digital\\P2 Grassland data structures in ArcGIS. d)  Site packs: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV and on DMS DMS\\Evidence\\Terrestrial Habitats\\Lowland Grassland Phase II Survey\\Grassland Survey Site Packs e)  Some images are stored on Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV\\PHASE II PHOTO AND IMAGE ARCHIVE.   DVDs are in the safe.   This data is now available on the NBN Gateway\tStuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team, NRW  For data held on Recorder 6 - Master Reference Data Team, NRW For spatial datasets - contact Spatial Services Team, NRW\tThis project comprised of a detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation across Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed.   The survey locations and sites vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha, to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.   The datasets which were produced from this project have been extensively validated and verified. However, the main limitation of these datasets is their age. Habitat composition may change over time due to successional trends, agricultural treatments or even planned developments, which means that there is no guarantee that the information within these datasets is still representative on the ground today. However they do provide an important long-term catalogue of data which can be used to monitor changes to the grassland environments overtime.    The dataset contributes to JNCC\u0027s UK Lowland Grassland Database, held by the Lowland Grassland Lead Co-ordination Network at NE in Peterborough.     Where a grassland site has been resurveyed the most recent survey data is in the main Phase2 Grassland Survey datasets and the previous survey(s) have now been put into a folder called Archive [Historic]  GIS layer files within this dataset include: Site boundary (Sites), Vegetation polygons (veg), Mosaic Table, Mosaic Polygons (Mospolys), Quadrats, Photos and Target Note (Notes, points, polygons).    There is a DMS document explaining the GIS layers and data structure called P2 Grassland data structures in ArcGIS.   Survey Reports are also available from NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-12-09\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3600/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_phase_2_grassland\t2025-12-11T10:42:20.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17414\tNRW_DS46197\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.588Z\t351\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCondition monitoring of National Nature Reserves (NNRs) in Wales\t© CNC/NRW 2014. NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.  Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence.  No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313937\tHigh level data are held centrally by NRW Environmental Change Group.  Supporting data are held in Regional NRW offices or by Science Groups.\tCustodian: Dylan Lloyd  or   Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tNational Nature Reserves (NNRs) contain examples of some of the most important natural and semi-natural terrestrial and coastal ecosystems in Great Britain. They are managed to conserve their habitats or to provide special opportunities for scientific study of the habitats communities and species represented within them. In addition they may be managed to provide public recreation that is compatible with their natural heritage interests.  NNRs are declared by the statutory country conservation agencies under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949 and the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981. In some cases NNRs form part of for wholly within Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) or Special Protection Areas (SPAs).  The purpose of this data capture was to assess the condition of SSSI/SAC/SPA features on NNRs in Wales. SAC features are generally monitored to the condition of the feature at the SPA level. Features on designated sites are to be assessed over a period of six years in a rolling monitoring cycle. The first cycle began in 1999, however SAC monitoring cycle began in 2001.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:23.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17408\tNRW_DS46198\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.286Z\t1374\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommon Standards Monitoring (CSM)\t© CCGC/CCW 2004.  CCW data may be used for non-commercial and internal business purposes only. It may be interrogated, imported into your own system and merged with other data, providing it is not factually altered. You may duplicate and publish short extracts of raw data and any findings and other information derived from the data, providing CCW is acknowledged. You may not copy the dataset or transfer it to others; additional copies can be obtained from CCW.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313938\tHigh level data are held centrally by NRW Environmental Change Group.  Supporting data are held in Regional NRW offices or by Science Groups.\tCustodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team.  Metadata: Data Services Team\tOne of the key responsibilities of the statutory nature conservation agencies in the UK, like Natural Resources Wales, is the identification and protection of a series of sites intended to conserve important wildlife and Earth science features. Such sites may be designated under: - National legislation (Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs)), - European Directives (Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs)), or - An international Convention (Ramsar sites).  The scope of monitoring includes coastal, freshwater, lowland grassland, lowland heathland, lowland wetland, marine, upland habitats, woodland, earth science (geodiversity), amphibians, birds, fish, invertebrates, reptiles, marine mammals, terrestrial mammals, vascular plants, bryophytes and lichens. The purpose of this data capture is to assess the condition of features on all designated sites (SSSI, SAC, SPA and Ramsar) based on site-specific performance indicators, and to record aspects of their management, both as an aid to management decisions and for reporting at a variety of scales, including UK through JNCC, and for contributing to UK reports on Conservation Status under the EC Habitats Directive.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2014-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:23.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17409\tNRW_DS46201\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.461Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tImpacts of Atmospheric Nitrogen Deposition in Combination with Variable Grazing Pressure upon Upland Grazing\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436323031\t\t\tThis study was done to provide baseline data on nitrogen deposition. The following was monitored: Pollution, habitats, plant communities and species assemblages. The purpose of this data capture was to aid with the monitoring of Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSI) features, and allow staff to make informed decision on the conservation management of upland sites, especially those in ESA or with management agreements.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:52:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17403\tNRW_DS46211\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.261Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) invertebrate data for Ceredigion\tStaff should consult the NRW metadata entry or data custodian for the data for copyright statement Details will differ depending on individual cases.  Staff should consult the NRW metadata entry or data custodian for the data for restrictions and advice\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436323131\tThe dataset is held on the NBN Gateway only. The full dataset from which it was derived from is held in NRW (see separate metadata entry).\tInternal Custodian: John Bratton, Biological Data Officer Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW, Bangor  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team\tCollation of occurrence records for UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority invertebrate species in Ceredigion. This is a subset of  \u0027UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) invertebrate data for Wales\u0027.  In Ceredigion it covers 7 species of ant, 8 species of beetle, 3 species of butterfly, 1 species of dragonfly, 14 species of moth, 5 species of true fly and 1 mollusc. The purpose of this data capture was to understand the distribution of species in Wales in order to inform conservation decisions.\tVarious including volunteer recorders-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:48.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17419\tNRW_DS46214\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.693Z\t602\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBenthic Marine Data from Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Technical Support (Research and Monitoring) Contracts\t© CNC/NRW 2001 Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436323134\tMFSG Marine Recorder database at http://data.nbn.org.uk/\tInternal Custodian and Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThis dataset is a collection of marine benthic surveys related to former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), and now Natural Resources Wales (NRWs) technical support, research and monitoring contracts.  The purpose of this data capture and collation was to aid coastal zone and sea-use management and to contribute to the identification of important areas of marine natural heritage.  The surveys focussed on benthic habitats, often referred to as \u0027biotopes\u0027, in the intertidal and inshore (typically within 3 nm) subtidal areas. Original copies of all the surveys are now held separately in NRW\u0027s Marine Recorder database.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:21:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17410\tNRW_DS46322\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.435Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGrassholm Northern Gannet (Morus bassanus) Survey (2004)\t© CNC/NRW  All intellectual property rights (including copyrights and database rights) are owned by NRW; except photographs which are jointly owned by NRW / CEH / Chris Perrins.  NRW data is issued with non-transferable licence which permits private or educational use, internal business use, reproduction and publication, subject to conditions. The dataset may not be used for commercial purposes or financial profit or gain, or sold or transferred without special agreement from NRW.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436333232\tphotos and excel key to jpgs are located at N:\\USERS\\MurphyM (was GrayC)\\SPAs \u0026amp; SSSIs\\SPAs\\Grassholm\\Grassholm survey 2004\\  H:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\MurphyM\\SPAs \u0026amp; SSSIs\\SPAs\\Grassholm H:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\MurphyM\\contracts\\Completed projects\\Grassholm  Report on H:TSG/Gray_C/Contract reports/Report 604 Gannets 2004 Will be held JNCC seabird database and NBN\tMatty Murphy, Senior Maritime Ornithologist, Natural Resources Planning/Marine and Freshwater Ecosystem Group, NRW (Formerly Catherine Gray, Ornithologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW)\tThis dataset consists of a count of the breeding northern gannet (Morus bassanus) population on Grassholm in 2004. The purpose of this data capture was to assess population estimates for Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and Special Protection Areas (SPA) monitoring and to update the UK population estimate. The projected aimed to make the first complete survey of this gannetry since 1999 and to: -  assess current numbers, conservation status and number of Apparently Occupied Site (AOS),  -  contribute to the decadal survey of the UK gannet population and the wider north Atlantic gannet colonies, and  -  establish survey protocol for future site condition monitoring.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Perrins-Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17417\tNRW_DS46929\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.596Z\t308\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBiodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) in Wales\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436393239\t[A]  Original data is held within the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID) CCW in Recorder 6  The data is also available as a GIS layeron the X drive (see metadata entry URI. NRW_DS99503)\tMike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, TEG, NRW (Formerly Adrian Fowles, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, Countryside Council for Wales)\tMarsh Fritillary  (Euphydryas aurinia) is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority invertebrate species. This dataset is a collation of all confirmed occurrence records of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) butterfly from Wales. The purpose of this data collation is to inform Local Biodiversity Action Plan  (LBAP) Partners and inform conservation decisions related to Marsh Fritillary butterflies. Data accuracy is reliant on original sources. Spatial references, dates and species identifications have been validated by Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 CCW\u0027s invertebrate experts checking printed lists of records, and literature references have been added where possible.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various including volunteer recorders\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17404\tNRW_DS46954\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.288Z\t303\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAerial Photographs of the Intertidal Areas of Wales 1996 to 2002\t© CNC/NRW, CUCAP, and CR World 2002. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW/CUCAP/CR World and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436393534\tHafod Elfyn Fireproof room. M:/airphotos\tInternal Custodians: Phase 1 Intertidal Team, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services / Spatial Data Team, NRW\tStereoscopic aerial photographs supplied by CUCAP (Cambridge University Centre for Aerial Photography) at scale of 1:10,000, CR World (Liverpool Bay Forum), BKS Surveys Limited, ExeGesIS and the Environment Agency for the coast of Wales.  The aim of this data collation was to map Intertidal Phase 1 communities (biotopes) both in the field and for the production of digital Phase 1 Intertidal maps on the GIS (MapInfo). Also used for monitoring Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) features and intertidal casework. The collation is a complete coverage of the Welsh coast from the Ribble Estuary (England) to the Severn Estuary inclusive.\tCR World GeoInformation Group-Cambridge University. Centre for Aerial Photography (CUCAP)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:22:04.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17412\tNRW_DS46955\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.473Z\t655\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Great Crested Newt (Triturus cristatus) Populations at Halkyn Mountain / Mynydd Helygain Special Area of Conservation (SAC) (2005)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2005. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence.  Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.  Restrictions on publishing this data apply to the following survey.  Various - Historical Newt Surveys at Halkyn Mountain SAC CCW000480000000D.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436393535\t[A]  Associated data is held in the following location; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\27 GCN at Halkyn Mountain SAC   [B]  Species Data held in Recorder 6: CCW - HQ Main Assessment of Great Crested Newts at Halkyn Mountain SAC CCW000480000000E\t\tMonitoring of Great Crested Newts (GCNs) on Halkyn Mountain Special Area of Conservation (SAC), under contract. The monitoring consisted of the following:  a) Survey of 45 ponds, where GCN had previously been recorded, for eggs, larvae and juveniles and other environmental data.   b) Mapping of terrestrial habitat within 250m of pond to assess the value of the habitat for GCNs (total of 183.9ha). c) Collation of existing GCN count data from 1985 to 2005 in known display ponds in the SAC. d) Condition assessment of GCN as a SAC feature.  This dataset forms part of the Wales-wide Common Standards Monitoring. The aim of this data capture was to assess the condition of designated SAC features based on site-specific performance indicators, and to record aspects of their management, both as an aid to management decisions and for reporting at a variety of scales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17405\tNRW_DS47010\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.204Z\t892\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBat Roosts Database - North West Wales (GWYBATS)\t© CNC/NRW 2005 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence.  No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3437303130\tThis data is held and managed by Cofnod - who have the top copy. Shared drive with Dolgellau, Llys-y-bont, HQ.   MapInfo export held on the M Drive.\tInternal Custodian: NR Protected Species Officer Casework, Maes y Fynnon, NRW. Mammal Ecologist Bats and Riparian Mammals, Maes y Fynnon, NRW.  Internal Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW  External Contact: Aisling May  - Rheolwr Data/Data Manager       aisling.may@cofnod.org.uk Cofnod, Intec, Ffordd y Parc, Parc Menai, Bangor, Gwynedd. LL57 4FG.  (01248) 672603\tThis GWYBATS is a Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) regional bat roost site information and lesser horseshoe bat monitoring data for Caernarvonshire, Anglesey and Merionethshire. The original records were derived from BATSITES-WALES and the original Bat Roosts Database - Caernarvonshire and Anglesey (GWYBATS), which were subsequently validated. New records received by the CCW Bangor (Llys y Bont) regional office have since been added. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts, to assist CCW\u0027s conservation work. In particular, to provide information when dealing with development proposals, to support the monitoring and notification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species.\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC)-Various including volunteer recorders-County bat groups-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:14.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17416\tNRW_DS47079\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.577Z\t324\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: European Dry Heath, Northern Atlantic Wet Heath and Blanket Bog at Eryri candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2005)\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright 2005. All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence.  Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3437303739\tData held on the H Drive at;  H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027AMPISAND\u0027 Datasets\\26 Heaths and blanket bogs at Eryri cSAC\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tMonitoring to assess the condition of three European Annex 1 habitats; Dry Heath, Wet Heath and Blanket Bog, at Eryri. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the condition of features on the Eryri cSAC designated site based on site-specific performance indicators, and to record aspects of their management, both as an aid to management decisions and for reporting at a variety of scales. The areas of Eryri targeted for survey lie in four separate compartments, and their approximate locations are around Aber Falls, Llyn Cowlyd, Tryfan and an area to the south and west of Bethesda.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17401\tNRW_DS47082\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.118Z\t743\tUnassessed\tEditable\tStorm Petrel (Hydrobates pelagicus) Census of Skokholm and Skomer Special Protection Area (SPA)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3437303832\tH:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\MurphyM\\contracts\\Completed projects\\Skomer \u0026amp; Skokholm storm petrels  Report on H:\\TSG\\Gray_C\\Contract reports\\\tMatt Murphy, Senior Maritime Ornithologist, Natural resources planning/Marine and freshwater ecosytem group, NRW (Formerly Catherine Gray)\tPopulation estimate of storm petrels Hydrobates pelagicus on Skomer in 2003 and 2004, and Skokholm in 2003. The purpose of this data capture was to create a population estimate to provide a baseline for monitoring. As well as establishment of monitoring plots and methods for future monitoring.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:21.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17420\tNRW_DS64557\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.645Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tImages/Photograph Libraries\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353537\tHQ and Regional offices.\tMonica\u0027s Advice:  This is a collection of images that was held by Linda Williams in CCW (also known as the CCW photo image library and identified as SOAP Ref: 003920 on the ETC).  All images are in the process of transferring to the NRW image library as part of the Image store project.  Have no idea about the hardcopy slides, which probably sit still in regional offices or have been transferred to Jill/Dwyn as part of office moves and paper archive project.  To be honest I am not sure that it needs an entry now as I don’t really consider promotional/nice images to be data and would just delete the entry. Alternatively perhaps change it for a general entry about the content of the NRW Image store in the same way we have a general entry for WISKI content or Marine Recorder content (it could include general information about the archive of slides that Jill/Dwyn currently hold too?).  Bryan Tozer would be best placed to help write a generic entry for the NRW image store (the lineage could include a sentence that described that this now contains what was previously knows as the CCW image library – a set of presentation level images used for promotional purposes). NRW holds so many images, unless they are a specifically data e.g. photo-monitoring time series of site/feature or something that is digitally analysed using software I wouldnt bother with metadata entries for them as they are not data and I don’t think they fall under our remit to give advice on.  If those that are considered data tend to be part of a larger dataset and are captures as part of the metadata for the larger dataset.\tNRW holds a large number of good quality photographs (both in digital and 35mm slide format) of biodiversity, landscape, countryside, marine and environmental subjects. These are held in various sets in HQ and Regional offices.  Purpose of data capture: To represent species and habitats etc found in Wales which may be used for interpretational and promotional purposes, eg. illustrations, slide shows, displays.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17407\tNRW_DS64560\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.414Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW Aerial Photographs of Wales\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353630\t\t\tCCW has commissioned aerial photographs, which are held in various offices.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:52:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17402\tNRW_DS64561\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.137Z\t1235\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAdmiralty Charts (Paper Records)\tNRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353631\tVarious paper maps are available through HQ and Regional offices.\tInternal Custodian: Keith Jones, GIS Data Manager, Information Management Group, CCW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) holds paper copies of Admiralty Charts (maps of sea areas) covering much of the Welsh coastline.\tUnited Kingdom Hydrographic Office (UKHO)\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:58:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17399\tNRW_DS64563\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.579Z\t676\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Image Store\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353633\thttp://imagestore.cyfoethnaturiolcymru.internal/asset-bank/action/viewHome  NB  You need to be on Pulse Secure for the URL in internal location to work.\tInternal Contact: Bryan Tozer, Advisor 2, Master Data Team  Whilst Bryan is the main contact for Image Store - each image and image collation will have an owner in the metadata in image store itself\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) holds a large numbers of photographic images created for the following numerous purposes, including biological recording; habitat recording; land-use recording; recording events and activities; communications and public engagement.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various\t2013-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-10T09:14:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17400\tNRW_DS64572\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.625Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForestry Grant Scheme Recording\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353732\t\t\tPurpose of data capture: To keep a record of FC grant aided schemes\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:52:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17397\tNRW_DS64573\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.421Z\t435\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCasework Data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353733\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.  Reports from the database may be edited to remove information that would reveal the locations of sensitive habitats or species. Individual records may be released with the authority of the D band manager responsible for the content of the record. Data may only be released for the purpose of further analyses with the authority of the casework database manager (Richard Ninnes). Analyses of the data may only be released with the authority of the casework database manager (Richard Ninnes). The database is only accessible directly to NRW staff because it gives access to letters and documents that may contain information in all categories of restricted information.\tRichard Ninnes, North Region Operations Manager, NRW Bryn Jones, Gemma Beyton\tThe casework database records all consultations about development proposals and plans received by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and CCW\u0027s responses from across Wales. The main content is consultations received from other regulatory authorities, including planning authorities, seeking CCW\u0027s advice. The database includes a record of designated sites, protected species and other natural heritage interests affected by proposals.  The database cross references with CCW\u0027s response letters filed on the electronic data and records management system (Ffynnon).  As of January 2010, the casework database does not include the following:  Policy consultations; Section 28 notifications from Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) owners and occupiers (use the consents database); Section 28H and Section 28G notifications (use the consents database); Applications for licences issued by CCW (Use the Licence module of ISIS).  Recording of these will be integrated with the casework database in due course.  The casework database fulfills the following functions: a) A framework for processing consultations. b) A record of documentation for each consultation. c) A consistent approach and response to consultations throughout CCW. d) Management information about consultations\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:15:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17396\tNRW_DS64578\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.335Z\t698\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecies Licence Return Datasets\t© CNC/NRW For Paper Records:  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.  For Species Data: Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353738\tRecorder 6 database Operational Support Group, CCW, Bangor.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tDylan Lloyd, Environmental Surveillance Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW possibly should now be Carys Roberts\tThis dataset was collected for recording and monitoring species which require licences. It is a collation created using licence return forms, and is made up of a number of species including, amphibians and reptiles, dormouse, bats, plants, badgers and otters.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:35:00.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17393\tNRW_DS64583\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.285Z\t273\tUnassessed\tEditable\tConsents and Assents held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353833\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tGill Walker, Data Manager (Sites), Operational Support Group, CCW\tThis dataset contains information on consents and assents requests received by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and CCW\u0027s responses to the requests.   The purpose of this data capture is to maintain database records for all Section 28 and Regulation 48 Consent and Assent requests received by CCW and CCW\u0027s responses. This dataset is now managed by Natural Resources Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:17:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17398\tNRW_DS86828\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.312Z\t370\tValid\tEditable\tSurvey of North Wales and Pembrokeshire tide influenced communities\t© CNC/NRW 2004. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3836383238\t[A] Data is archived as project 79, media 223 and 224 (File paths  K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 223 and K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 224) [B]Species and habitat data held in Marine Recorder (MRCCW80000000002, MRCCW80000000003, MRCCW80000000004, MRCCW80000000005). [C] Paper MNCR forms in off-site storage (Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tAs part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 UNEP Convention on Biological Diversity there have been a number of coordinated initiatives to identify those habitats which may be under threat in the UK, and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity.   The UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) is now one of the main focuses of that work. It provides detailed proposals for a number of species, species groups and habitats requiring urgent conservation action. Tidal Rapids is one of those habitats. Tidal Rapids are a UK Biodiversity Action Plan feature and rocky Reefs are listed as an Annex I feature under the Habitats Directive (1992). These features are of conservation importance and the data collected during this survey is used to help define conservation objectives and inform decisions regarding protection and management.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T15:46:02.367Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17394\tNRW_DS87090\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.335Z\t681\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcross Wales Drop-down Video monitoring 2004-2005\t© CCGC/CCW 2006 Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837303930\t[A]  Also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW HQ as project no. 35, Media 175.   [B]  Fire-proof storage facilities at CCW HQ on optical media. [C]  Species data held in Marine Recorder [MRCCW31600000002]\tData Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Team, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team\tDrop-down video surveys of Special Area of Conservation (SACs) across Wales (Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau, Menai Strait and Conwy Bay, Cardigan Bay and Pembrokeshire).  Work carried out by Seastar Survey under contract to Countryside Council of Wales (CCW).  The data was collected to enable site monitoring to support management and reporting of SACs in Wales. The monitoring of subtidal aspects of SACs will indicate whether the desired condition of the feature of interest for which the site was designated is being achieved. The work will also enable judgements to be made about whether the management of the site is appropriate. Analysis of video recordings from the 2005 survey will be used to produce descriptions of the seabed environment including substrata and faunal communities as well as classifying the different sites into biotopes according to Connor et al. (2004). Survey areas within the SACs focused on north Pen Llyn, Bardsey Island, Tremadog Bay and Moelfre in north Wales, areas off Cardigan Island, Cemaes Head and between Aberaeron and Llangranog (off Newquay) within Cardigan Bay, and Hats and Barrels Reef, Bishops and Clerks, Skokholm Island and the Castlemartin ridges in Pembrokeshire.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:22:52.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17395\tNRW_DS87091\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.226Z\t437\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Monitoring Great Crested Newts at Johnstown SAC and  Deeside and Buckley SAC (2003)\t© CNC/NRW 2004 Data-set Collation There are no access restrictions to this data. NRW may release, publish or disseminate it freely. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.   IPR of historical records NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Those seeking such permission should approach the originator directly.  Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by NRW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence.  Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.  Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence.  No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.  Restrictions apply to the following dataset: Various - Historical Newt Surveys at Johnstown SAC and Deeside \u0026amp; Buckley SAC CCW0004800000009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837303931\t[A]  Associated data is held within the Recorder 6 database. [B]  Data is also held on the H:/ at H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\01 Great crested newt at Johnstown, Deeside \u0026amp; Buckley\t\tDeeside and Buckley and Johnstown Newt Sites candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) in Flintshire; and Johnstown Newt Sites cSAC in Wrexham support some of the most important areas for newts (Triturus cristatus) in Great Britain. The main aim of the current project was to provide monitoring information on Great Crested Newts adults and juveniles and on Great Crested Newts  aquatic and terrestrial habitat within the two cSACs.   This was to appraise the condition of great crested newt populations at Johnstown Newt Sites SAC, and Deeside and Buckley Newt Sites SAC and to provide monitoring data on adults and juveniles and on Great Crested Newts  aquatic and terrestrial habitat.  The dataset consists of:  1) A collation of historical Great Crested Newts surveillance data from known display ponds for the two SACs (then candidate SACs).  Records were mainly taken from Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) paper files, but also from Deeside Urban Wildlife Group (DUWG), Flintshire County Council (FCC), and Wrexham County Borough Council (WCBC).  Individual CCW recorders names are not known. 2) 2003 monitoring data for presence of Great Crested Newts (Jun), and larvae/adults (Aug-Sept).\tFflintshire County Council \u003d Cyngor Sir y Fflint-Wrexham County Borough-Deeside Urban Wildlife Group-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17392\tNRW_DS87108\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.002Z\t371\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGround truthing towed video images from North Pen Llyn horse mussel (Modiolus modiolus) reef\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313038\tFire-proof storage facilities at NRW, Maes y Fynnon, on optical media.  Also maintained on backed-up server based storage as Media 280-282.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) was initially selected for two of the Annex II habitat types listed in the Habitats Directive - reefs and estuaries. Monitoring trials were developed in relation to these two features. During the late 1990\u0027s there was a dynamic period where the development of marine monitoring and surveillance under the Habitats and Species Directive was undertaken. The presence of the horse mussel Modiolus modiolus off the north Pen Llyn is thought to have been known since the early 19th Century. The extent of the biogenic reef feature was considered an important attribute to monitor as part of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC. This dataset consists of digital footage of the horse mussel (Modiolus modiolus) reef collected in 1999. Data was collected to validate the results of an acoustic monitoring survey of the Modiolus modiolus reefs present within the SAC.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17391\tNRW_DS87114\t2024-05-28T12:22:31.952Z\t407\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDrop-down Video Footage and Biotope Identification Data 1999\t© CNC/NRW 2012 Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313134\ta) Fire-proof storage facilities at NRW Maes-y-Ffynnon on optical media. Also maintained on backed-up server based storage as project 56 and 351, Media Nos 275-278, 290-293, 307, 1044-1056,1094,1100,1329 b) Sanderson et al., (Eds.) 2001, section 2.1.2, and data appendix. See related titles field. c) Marine Recorder [MRCCW31600000003] d) Original species and habitats data held in Marine Recorder mdb file in the archivee) Paper MNCR Survey forms held in NRW off-site storage\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team, NRW Metadata Point of contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to create a dataset to help with an evaluation of the deployment of drop-down video for identifying biotopes. Footage collected as an assessment of using drop-down video as part of a site-orientated monitoring technique.  Data was gathered as a result of the development and trial of a remote drop-down video-based monitoring method to measure biotope richness. Data was collected from two sites that presented sufficiently different physical conditions and biological conditions- north Pen Llyn reefs, and Sarn Badrig reef.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:23:31.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17449\tNRW_DS87115\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.211Z\t838\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMenai Strait turbidity and other co-variate surveillance\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313135\tData is archived as project no. 7 and 183, and media no. 132, 231 and 524 (File paths are K:\\Archive\\Media 101to200\\Media 132, K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\231 and K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 524)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThe Menai Strait, separating the Isle of Anglesey from the north coast of Wales is part of the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation. Monitoring of physical parameters in the strait, including water turbidity, has been carried out on a sporadic basis for the past 40 years. These parameters are of importance to the biological communities for which the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay has been designated and help to inform assessments of the condition of those communities and the effectiveness of management measures to conserve them. The observations of physical parameters in the strait have shown large, long-term fluctuations in turbidity. The aim of this project was to establish an automatic system for continuous monitoring of water turbidity, and other relevant physical parameters including temperature, salinity and meteorological variables.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:25:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17444\tNRW_DS87116\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.274Z\t889\tValid\tEditable\tPembrokeshire Marine / Sir Benfro Forol Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Grey Seal (Halichoerus grypus) Monitoring 2005\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313136\tStored as Project No. 60, media 355\tInternal Custodians:  Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II.   For SACs in Wales, the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Grey seals (Halichoerus grypus) are among the rarest seals in the world: the UK population represents about 40 percent of the world population and 95 percent of the EU population. Pembrokeshire Marine / Sir Benfro Forol SAC is home to one of the largest populations of grey seals around the west coast of the UK.   The purpose of this data capture was to record, document and report indicators of the condition of grey seals within the SAC. In addition to this, monitoring is also used to document and report on the impact of human activities, assessment of supporting habitat, and used in the estimations related to the population and structure (age and sex).  The data collected will contribute to an estimate of whole site (SAC) production using indices developed following the 1992-94 seal census.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:18:39.670Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17434\tNRW_DS87117\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.191Z\t337\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSub-surface towed video footage off North Pen Llyn, and subsequent biotope identification data\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313137\tData is archived under project no. 58, media no.\u0027s 279,283-287,294,300-309,543\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset is a collation of around 5 hours of digital video footage. Positional and other associated data are given in Sanderson et al., (Eds.) 2001 (CCW Marine Monitoring Report No.1, section 2.1.3, and data appendix). The purpose of the data capture was to create an evaluation of the deployment of towed video for monitoring biotope richness. Footage collected as an assessment of using towed video as part of a site-orientated monitoring technique. Following the increased commercial availability of small digital video cameras and digital video editing software, data was collected to evaluate the use of these systems in marine monitoring studies. Biotope richness (ie. The number of biotopes present, rather than species diversity within a biotope), was assessed from the recorded video.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:26:21.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17450\tNRW_DS87120\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.420Z\t443\tValid\tEditable\tNon Native Species Data - Sargassum muticum around Wales\t© CNC/NRW 2008. NRW owns the copyright of the collation, but the ownership of the records themselves rests with the originator. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and the copyright of NRW / third party recorders and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313230\t[A]  Data is archived under Project No. 151, media no\u0027s. 230 and 485 (File paths Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media201to300\\Media230 and Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media401to500\\Media485  [B]  Species Data is held in Marine Recorder: 1998-ongoing CCW Sargassum muticum records for Wales MRCCW30000000047\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tThe purpose of this data capture was to collate and monitor the spread of non-native species in Wales. This survey contains a series of records of the non-native marine alga, Sargassum muticum, around Wales. Many of the records were obtained during the intertidal phase 1 survey, while others are anecdotal records from both Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff and various other recorders surveying the shoreline for both work or recreationally. The dataset has been fully validated.  All grid references have been cross-referenced against site name.  The majority of records were recorded by specialists.  The data was collated in order to monitor the spread of non-native species in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:20:25.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17446\tNRW_DS87128\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.275Z\t786\tValid\tEditable\tMenai Strait tidally exposed seabed and shores (2002)\t© CCGC/CCW 2004. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of a CCW RPSI Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313238\t[A] Data is archived under Project No. 79, media No\u0027s 223 and 224 (File paths are  K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 223 and K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 223 [B] Species data stored in Marine Recorder v5 (Survey number: MRCCW80000000002) [C] Paper MNCR forms in off-site storage (Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tAs part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 UNEP Convention on Biological Diversity there have been a number of coordinated initiatives to identify those habitats which may be under threat in the UK, and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity.   The UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) is now one of the main focuses of that work. It provides detailed proposals for a number of species, species groups and habitats requiring urgent conservation action.   In addition to this the Habitats Directive (1992) requires the establishment of a European network of important high-quality conservation sites, Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).  The Habitats Directive identifies Reefs as a major category of habitats that should be considered for inclusion in SACs. Tidal rapids not only forms part of the reef environments classification but is also highlighted as an important site under the UKBAP habitats list. Tidal rapids is used to describe a broad range of high energy environments including deep tidal streams and tide-swept habitats. JNCC’s Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) defined rapids as ‘strong tidal streams resulting from a constriction in the coastline at the entrance to, or within the length of, an enclosed body of water such as a sea loch. Depth is usually shallower than five metres.    The data collected during this survey is being used to help define the conservation objectives for the cSAC reef features in Wales and to inform management of this feature.  One of the actions listed in the Habitat Action Plan for tidal rapids is to, complete survey and recording of the extent, quality and composition of the habitats and communities of tidal rapids.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T17:11:42.912Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17441\tNRW_DS87129\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.207Z\t615\tValid\tEditable\tMilford Haven and Daugleddau Estuary Tidally Exposed Seabed and Shores (2002)\t© CCGC/CCW 2004. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of a CCW RPSI Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313239\t[A] Data is archived under Project No. 79, media No\u0027s 223 and 224    Marine Recorder survey: MRCCW80000000003  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media201to300\\Media223 Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media201to300\\Media224\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW.\tAs part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 UNEP Convention on Biological Diversity there have been a number of coordinated initiatives to identify those habitats which may be under threat in the UK, and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity. The UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) is now one of the main focuses of that work. It provides detailed proposals for a number of species, species groups and habitats requiring urgent conservation action. In addition to this the Habitats Directive (1992) requires the establishment of a European network of important high-quality conservation sites, Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). The Habitats Directive identifies Reefs as a major category of habitats that should be considered for inclusion in SACs. Tidal rapids not only forms part of the reef environments classification but is also highlighted as an important site under the UKBAP habitats list.   Tidal rapids is used to describe a broad range of high energy environments including deep tidal streams and tide-swept habitats. JNCC’s Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) defined rapids as ‘strong tidal streams resulting from a constriction in the coastline at the entrance to, or within the length of, an enclosed body of water such as a sea loch. Depth is usually shallower than five metres. The data collected during this survey is being used to help define the conservation objectives for the cSAC reef features in Wales and to inform management of this feature. One of the actions listed in the Habitat Action Plan for tidal rapids is to, complete survey and recording of the extent, quality and composition of the habitats and communities of tidal rapids.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T15:17:18.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17445\tNRW_DS87132\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.271Z\t675\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNorth West Anglesey tidally exposed seabed and shores (2003-2004)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313332\t[A] Data is archived under Project No. 79, media No.\u0027s 223 and 224 (K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 223 and K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 224) [B] Species data are in Marine Recorder 5 (MRCCW80000000004)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tAs part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 UNEP Convention on Biological Diversity there have been a number of coordinated initiatives to identify those habitats which may be under threat in the UK, and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity. The UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) is now one of the main focuses of that work. It provides detailed proposals for a number of species, species groups and habitats requiring urgent conservation action. In addition to this the Habitats Directive (1992) requires the establishment of a European network of important high-quality conservation sites, Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). The Habitats Directive identifies Reefs as a major category of habitats that should be considered for inclusion in SACs. Tidal rapids not only forms part of the reef environments classification but is also highlighted as an important site under the UKBAP habitats list. Tidal rapids is used to describe a broad range of high energy environments including deep tidal streams and tide-swept habitats. JNCC’s Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) defined rapids as ‘strong tidal streams resulting from a constriction in the coastline at the entrance to, or within the length of, an enclosed body of water such as a sea loch. Depth is usually shallower than five metres. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the features in North West Anglesey to help inform and define conservation objective and decisions regarding their protection and management.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:27:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17447\tNRW_DS87133\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.278Z\t760\tValid\tEditable\tWest Pembrokeshire tidally exposed seabed and shores (2003)\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313333\t[A] Data is archived as Project No. 79, media No.\u0027s 223 and 224 (File paths: K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 223 and K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 224) [B] Raw biological data in Marine recorder survey MRCCW80000000005\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.\tAs part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 UNEP Convention on Biological Diversity there have been a number of coordinated initiatives to identify those habitats which may be under threat in the UK, and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity. The UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) is now one of the main focuses of that work. It provides detailed proposals for a number of species, species groups and habitats requiring urgent conservation action. Tidal Rapids is one of those habitats.    Tidal Rapids are a UK Biodiversity Action Plan feature and rocky Reefs are listed as an Annex I feature under the Habitats Directive (1992). These features are of conservation importance and the data collected during this survey is used to help define conservation objectives and inform decisions regarding protection and management.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T15:24:41.168Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17440\tNRW_DS87141\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.212Z\t361\tValid\tEditable\tResults of a study into the use of airborne remote sensing to identify habitats and biotopes in the Mawddach estuary\t© CCGC/CCW and © Crown copyright [2001]. All rights reserved. Countryside Council for Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by CCW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313431\tData is archived as Project No. 82, Media No.\u0027s 165 and 272.  Data is also held on backed-up server based storage.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tThis dataset contains the results of a study into the use of airborne remote sensing to produce a comprehensive thematic map of biotope and sediment type distribution in the Mawddach estuary. The purpose of this data capture was to be able to report on the condition of the Mawddach estuary a number of provisional attributes have been identified which may require monitoring e.g. the relative proportion of sandy and muddy biotopes and distribution of major communities.  This project investigated the possibility of using remotely sensed data to provide a contextual overview of broad-scale attributes in a monitoring programme.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2001-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T15:25:46.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17433\tNRW_DS87152\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.165Z\t842\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMaritime Supralittoral Lichen Survey of the Portmeirion Promontory, Morfa Harlech National Nature Reserve (NNR), Site of Special Sceintific Importance (SSSI), and Candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC)\t© CNC/NRW 2004. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313532\tData is held as Project No.83, Media 358.\tCustodian: Intertidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tThis dataset is made up of the results of a supralittoral lichen survey on coastal rocks around the Portmeirion headland within the Morfa Harlech. Morfa Harlech is designated as a National Nature Reserve (NNR), Site of Special Sceintific Importance (SSSI), and Candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC). The development and diversity of the supralittoral lichen communities is this area is one of great importance and is of conservation significance in this context. The purpose of this data capture was to produce an inventory of supralittoral lichen species, highlighting and localising any noteworthy taxa and assemblages.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:27:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17448\tNRW_DS87182\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.274Z\t629\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSensitivity and mapping of inshore marine biotopes in the southern Irish Sea (SensMap): Final report\t© CCGC/CCW and © Crown copyright All rights reserved. Countryside Council for Wales, AC0000849444. Third parties seeking to re-use this data require a Licence issued by CCW. It allows re-use, reproduction and dissemination in any format or medium but only for non-commercial purposes, according to the terms and conditions of the Licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313832\tData is archived as Project No. 181, media No\u0027s. 359 and 521 (File paths: K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 359 and K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 521)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tThe SensMap project, funded under the INTERREG programme involved survey and mapping of biotopes in the marine intertidal and subtidal in both Wales and Ireland. The project partners were the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Ecological Consultancy Services Ltd (EcoServe) and the National Parks and Wildlife Service (NPWS) in Ireland now Duchas, the Heritage Service.  This project had three main objectives:  1. To develop an integrated methodology for mapping in the marine intertidal and subtidal which can link both marine and terrestrial maps.  2. To develop a protocol for determining the sensitivity of marine biotopes according to the sensitivity of marine species to a range of natural and man-induced changes including, changes to water quality, oil pollution, fishing and recreational activities, and in the longer term, climate change. 3. To develop a model for a decision-making support system based on sensitivity mapping.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2000-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:28:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17439\tNRW_DS87189\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.175Z\t829\tValid\tEditable\tFood sources for Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra) in Carmarthen Bay\t© CCGC/CCW 2004. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and CCW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313839\tData and Report are archived under Project No. 1, Media 222\tInternal Custodian Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services.\tIn the UK the Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra) is recognised as a nationally threatened species due to its small and declining breeding population. Carmarthen Bay is an internationally important wintering ground for the Common Scoter duck (Melanitta nigra) and is widely regarded as the most important such site in the UK causing the site to be proposed as a Special Protection Area (pSPA) under the EC Birds Directive.   Carmarthen Bay is considered to support peak numbers of 17000 - 24000 birds. The Common Scoter is strongly migratory and often travels considerable distances over land making brief stop-overs on inland waters. Its diet consists predominantly of molluscs, especially during the winter, although it occasionally takes other aquatic invertebrates such as crustaceans (e.g. barnacles and shrimps), worms, echinoderms, isopods, amphipods and insects (e.g. midges and caddis flies) as well as small fish and fish eggs.   An extensive macrofauna sampling survey was undertaken to coincide with an aerial scoter survey. Conservation concerns for the scoter population focus on threats of hunting pressure, breeding habitat disturbance and loss, food chain contaminants, oil spills and the threat to non-breeding habitats represented by offshore developments.   The aim of this project was to investigate the biological and physical factors affecting the winter distribution of common scoter in Carmarthen Bay and to identify what component of the benthos they feed upon.  The identification of such components is crucial to the future management and monitoring of the Carmarthen Bay Special Protection Area and, to some extent, the Special Area of Conservation.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T15:27:57.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17437\tNRW_DS87460\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.175Z\t443\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThreatened Bryophyte Database (TBDB) UK (1965 - 2005)\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. The British Bryological Society currently publishes all unrestricted data on the NBN Gateway.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837343630\tMaster Copy held by British Bryological Society.   Recorder 6 holds some species data: Tycanol: RF36 Bryophyte records - BBS database SR0006010000000C.   An export of welsh data held as MapInfo file on m drive.  NB Update from Sam Bosanquet 2021 - The Threatened Bryophyte Database is basically defunct now - all data for Threatened Bryophytes are stored along with those for all other bryophytes by the British Bryological Society and there is no point in NRW storing them separately as that just leads to duplication and errors.\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team External Custodian: British Bryological Society\tThis metadata is derived from metadata created by British Bryological Society for the NBN gateway. The purpose of this data capture is to service bryophyte conservation by documenting detailed records of threatened species, and tracking the populations of these species through time. It is a \u0027live\u0027 database feeding specialist information to UKBAP Lead Partners and conservation agencies, and to feed information about bryophyte conservation programmes to bryologists. Some biological records have more precise location information than others. Often they are recorded using national grid references, which identify which 10km, 2km, 1km or 100m square they occur in. This table shows how many records there are at these resolutions for this dataset.\tBritish Bryological Society\t2005-08-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17436\tNRW_DS88070\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.164Z\t606\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBat Roosts Database - North East Wales\t© CNC/NRW  Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3838303730\tThis dataset is held and managed by Cofnod. They hold the top copy of the dataset and therefore requests should go directly to them, via the NBN Gateway.  Historically: CCW data was stored in Recorder on the Mold server Rec2002 - Mold. MapInfo layer held on m:/species/North East/Mamamals/Bats/AllBats_NEWales_validated.tab.   Additional MapInfo layers are available for each of the species within the same folder.\tMatthew Ellis - see Distributor above for contact details. External Contact: Aisling May  - Rheolwr Data/Data Manager       aisling.may@cofnod.org.uk Cofnod, Intec, Ffordd y Parc, Parc Menai, Bangor, Gwynedd. LL57 4FG.  (01248) 672603\tThis dataset is the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) regional database of bat roosts for all species of bats found in North East Wales.  This includes the site name, grid reference, species, date and abundance where available. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts, to assist CCW\u0027s conservation work. In particular, to provide information when dealing with development proposals, to support the monitoring and notification of SSSIs, and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species.\tVarious including volunteer recorders-County bat groups-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:42.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17431\tNRW_DS88158\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.055Z\t519\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBat Roosts Database - Montgomeryshire\t© CNC/NRW 2003 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence.  No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3838313538\t[A]  Data is archived as Project No. 175, Media 509  [B]  Copy also held in the Newtown Office database CCW Montgomeryshire General Bat Files WA0001500000001O    [C]  A Master copy held at Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park Biodiversity Information Service (BIS) Local Environmental Records Center\t\tThis dataset contains records for all species of bats recorded in roosts within Montgomeryshire. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts in order to assist Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRWs)  conservation work. In particular, to provide information when dealing with development proposals, to support the monitoring and notification of SSSIs, and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species. This dataset contains records for all species of bats recorded in roosts within Montgomeryshire.  The data also indicates the abundance of each species at each location. Data was extracted from files as part of an exercise carried out by the Local Record Centre on behalf of former Countryside Council for Wales.  A top copy of this data is held by the Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park Biodiversity Information Service (BIS) Local Record Centre. An older copy of the data is held on NRW\u0027s copy of Recorder 6 under CCW Montgomeryshire General Bat Files WA0001500000001O however this has not been updated since 2003.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-07-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T13:30:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17442\tNRW_DS88241\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.213Z\t453\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater Site Visits and Ad-hoc Sightings\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3838323431\t[A]  Species Data held in Recorder 6: -  Freshwater Sites - Occasional Reconnaissance Surveys - CCW CCW0002800000001    -  Freshwater ad-hoc sightings collected by CCW staff - CCW CCW0002800000003      This data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the 17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader of Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tRecords of species and habitat ad hoc sightings collected in the freshwater environment.  This includes, but it not limited to aquatic plants, amphibians and certain easily identified invertebrates.  Data is collected in order to assist with targeted monitoring and site management of freshwater environments across Wales.  Data collection will be on-going during the monitoring and other survey work.  This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:21.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17435\tNRW_DS88663\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.192Z\t324\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBat Roosts Database - Pembrokeshire (1887 - 2011)\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3838363633\t[A]  Paper records are held at the NRW Stackpole / Pembrokeshire Offices.   [B]  Digitised species data held in Recorder 6 45 VC Pembs:  Bats - Hibernacula \u0026amp; day/night roost records  CCW0000800000027\tPaul Culyer, Senior Reserve Manager - Pembrokeshire, Operational Services, South, NRW (Formerly Bob Haycock, NRW Senior Reserve Manager - Stackpole)\tThis dataset comprises of former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 regional bat hibernacula and day/night roost records for Pembrokeshire. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts to assist with the former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) bat conservation work. In particular, to provide information when dealing with development proposals, to support the monitoring and notification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest, and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species across Pembrokeshire. Ad hoc survey work is continuing however the main data was collected between 1887 and 2011.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2011-03-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:45.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17432\tNRW_DS98705\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.128Z\t370\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBiodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Invertebrate Species\t© CNC/NRW 2003.  You may reproduce this dataset free of charge for non-commercial and internal business purposes in any format or medium, provided that you do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. To avoid using old information, we recommend that you obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373035\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestial\\Invertebrates Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\biological\\species\\all_wales\\baps\\invertebrates\\bap_species.tab\tData Custodian: Mike Howe (legacy CCW dataset put together by Adrian Fowles) Senior Invertebrate Ecologist or John Bratton Biological Data Officer. Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons.  This purpose of this data capture was to enable Non Government Organisations (NGOs), Local Authorities (LAs) and Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW) staff to have access to spatial data on BAP Priority invertebrates.  This dataset is a collation of all confirmed occurrence records of UK Biodiversity Action Plan ( BAP ) priority invertebrate species reported from Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17443\tNRW_DS98710\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.260Z\t710\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLocal Biodiveristy Action Plan (LBAP) Areas\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444   Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373130\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Terrestial\\LBAP_areas.lyr\tMetadata and Custodian Contact:  GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the locations of the Local Biodiversity Action Plan Areas.The Local Biodiversity Partnership areas across Wales were used for tragetted species and habitat Action Plans, allowing for a more localised place based approach to monitoring and protecting key areas in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17438\tNRW_DS98725\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.188Z\t980\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW) Public Rights of Way (PROW) Condition Survey\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444 Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence. Those seeking commercial re-use require a commercial licence available from the OS. Attribution Statement: © CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373235\tThere is no spatial dataset related to the condistion status, only the base dataset of PRoW created by CCW and supplmented by Local Authority data.  PRoW base data stored at x:\\Recreation and Access\\Public rights of way\\PROW.lyr\tThis metadata may not be complete for more information please contact Jont Bulbeck, Access Team Leader, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing spatial information related to the Public Rights of Way (PROW) obtained from the PROW condition survey. The core survey assessed the condition of 3,283 km of rights of way within 225 randomly selected survey squares. A sufficient number of survey squares were selected to ensure a geographically representative spread and were randomly selected within highway and national park authority areas. The length of paths surveyed was calculated to provide statistically reliable and representative information about path condition within each authority area.  The purpose of this data capture was to strategically assess the physical condition of public rights of way in Wales to inform Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s now NRW\u0027s) work, including policy advice and guidance to the Welsh Assembly Government and other stakeholders. This can be used for the basis for cost estimates for improving the PROW network in Wales. Condition survey data was deemed very good [a robust sampling methodology and survey and data quality control procedures were employed]. These are detailed in an associated research report.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:02:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17429\tNRW_DS98736\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.468Z\t2424\tValid\tEditable\tAreas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONBs)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373336\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\AONB Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty.lyr  Paper maps are stored in map store Maes y Ffynnon Unitary Local Authorities may also keep a copy of paper map of AONB for their area.\tInternal Custodian: Carole Thornley, Lead Specialist Adviser Landscape, Designated Landscapes Programme\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONBs) statutory landscape designation in Wales. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has a legal duty to maintain and make available AONB boundaries.  Following recommendations from a review commissioned by Welsh Government, in 2024 AONBs were rebranded as National Landscapes. Both names of AONB and National Landscapes are currently in use. Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty remains the legal name for this statutory designation.  AONBs are designated for the purpose of conserving and enhancing the natural beauty and differ from National Parks in that they do not have a recreation remit. AONBs are managed by their constituent Local Authorities.  NRW is the designating authority to create any new AONB in Wales or boundary variations to existing ones, however these must be confirmed by Welsh Ministers. NRWs statutory powers derive from the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act (NPAC) 1949 and Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000. Any new AONBs designated by NRW would now be under powers derived from the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000.  Five AONBs have been designated in Wales: Gower (1956) was the first AONB designated in England and Wales, Llŷn (1957), Anglesey (1967) and Wye Valley (1971), which straddles the border with England. The Clwydian Range AONB was designated in 1985 and extended by the then Countryside Council for Wales (a predecessor organisation to NRW) in 2011.  The data has been held digitally since mid-1990s. The boundary has been transferred to OS MasterMap by NRW and has been subject to joint checking by NRW staff and the relevant Local Authority staff.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/382/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_AONB\t2025-11-04T14:22:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17430\tNRW_DS98737\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.479Z\t1159\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales\t© CNC/NRW NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate the full dataset. A redacted version of the data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373337\tSpatial data available on X Drive: X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales and Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\ Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Phase_2_Peatland [Working versions, and draft data. Moved to X-drive when finished. GIS data also on DataMapWales].     Some Species Data is held in Recorder 6.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas. This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  01/01/2020 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).     [A]  Some associated documents are stored in Terrestrial Science Group folder called PEATSURV on the server (Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\TSG\\ PEATSURV) and Content Manager (Ffynnon).  [B]  Biological Data held in Recorder 6: See Data Format for more information (between 2004 to 2011)  - LPSW Brecknock CCW0007100000005     - LPSW Carmarthen and Dinefwr CCW0007100000006   - LPSW Ceredigion CCW0007100000007    - LPSW Clwyd CCW0007100000008  - LPSW East Gwynedd CCW0007100000009    - LPSW Gwent CCW0007100000010   - LPSW Mid \u0026amp; South Glamorgan CCW0007100000001   - LPSW Montgomeryshire CCW0007100000011    - LPSW Preseli \u0026amp; South Pembrokeshire CCW0007100000012     - LPSW Radnorshire CCW0007100000002    - LPSW West Glamorgan \u0026amp; Llanelli CCW0007100000003   - LPSW West Gwynedd CCW0007100000004     [C] All survey reports and quadrat data in spreadsheets are on DMS DMS\\Evidence\\Terrestrial Habitats\\Peatland Survey https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/ps/SitePages/Home.aspx; species records (quadrat data) https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/ps/Data%20Tables/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx.     [D] Most photos upto 2020 are on Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\TSG\\ PEATSURV , but those from 2021 onwards are in a series of OneDrive folders, as NRW still doesn\u0027t have a workable solution for data photos.\tInternal Custodian: Kathryn Birch, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Habitats, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team\tThis dataset is part of the Phase 2 Habitats surveys conducted across Wales. It consists of a detailed survey of plant communities in lowland peatland vegetation.  The survey work was split into two time periods, with the first Phase 2 lowland peatland surveys being conducted between 1977 and 1982. There was then a break between this and the second tranche of surveys which have occurred from 2004 onwards. These more modern surveys were conducted on sites already covered on the 1997-1982 surveys and on newer identified sites. The surveys were conducted at Phase 2 (plant community) level of herbaceous mire habitats predominantly within the lowland environment of Wales.   Key project outputs include site-based assessments of community type, distribution and ecology and evaluative accounts of vegetation and its condition.   Strategic outputs include Area of Search/regional/all Wales synthesis and analysis of data to inform site selection, conservation and restoration.   This part of the Phase 2 survey just includes species datasets collected by Countryside Council for Wales and Natural Resources Wales in-house expert Phase 2 survey team.  Peer-based quality assurance is carried out within the team and by NRW/CCW\u0027s Peatland Specialist. Regular meetings are held to ensure consistent community and species determination and survey protocol application.      The primary objective of the survey is to characterise and evaluate the vegetation cover of lowland peatlands in Wales to ensure the identification, protection and management of important peatland sites.  All Wales coverage although sites vary in size and complexity.   The survey programme is predominantly focussed on lowland mires, i.e. those below the upper functional limit of agricultural enclosure. A few mires above this limit may be considered where they display essentially lowland characteristics or where survey would provide additional critical information for rare communities. The survey encompasses all lowland Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) supporting at least one independently qualifying peatland feature. The only exceptions to this are (i) the largest of the raised mires (namely Cors Fochno, Cors Caron, and Fenns, Whixall, Bettisfield, Wem and Cadney Mosses), and (ii) any sites for which recent reliable information collected in a manner broadly comparable to that of the current survey is already available. Non-statutory sites are included if they have peatland features considered likely to be of individually qualifying status or if they have a notable mire element. A 0.5 ha threshold for individual or groups of communities has been used to help prioritise sites for survey.  GIS layer files within this dataset include: Site boundary (Sites), Vegetation polygons (veg), Mosaic Table, Mosaic Polygons (Mospolys), Habitat (SSSI) Features (features), Quadrats, Photos and Target Note (Notes, points, polygons and text), Deep peat, Peat depth, Candidate sites.    There is a DMS document explaining the GIS layers and data structure called P2 Peatland data structures in ArcGIS.   Survey Reports are also available from NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-08-04\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3595/?ows_service\u003dwms\t2025-12-19T12:12:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17428\tNRW_DS98740\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.469Z\t1268\tValid\tEditable\tBiogenetic Reserves\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373430\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Biogenetic Reserves.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Rhodri T. Jones, Advisor (2) Protected Sites (International), Biodiversity Policy \u0026amp; Strategy\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the boundaries of Biogenetic Reserves in Wales.  Biogenetic Reserves aim to conserve European flora, fauna and natural areas especially heathlands and dry grasslands that although common in one country, may be scarce in another. In this way a store of genetic material - the genes of plants and animals - is kept for the future, hence the term Biogenetic.  To qualify for this designation, sites in the UK must first be Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) or otherwise designated. The Bern Convention - signed by the UK government in 1982, led to the establishment of this European network of Biogenetic Reserves.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-11-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T15:30:15.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17426\tNRW_DS98741\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.451Z\t1059\tValid\tEditable\tBiosphere Reserves\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373431\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Biospheric Reserves.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Rhodri T. Jones, Advisor (2) Protected Sites (International), Biodiversity Policy \u0026amp; Strategy\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of biosphere reserves in Wales.   The Biosphere Reserve is an international designation made by UNESCO based on nominations made by more than 110 countries. These sites are chosen to conserve examples of areas which are characteristic of the world\u0027s natural regions. They must also be areas where people are important components of everyday life. The biosphere is rapidly changing under the influence of human activity, so meeting their needs whilst conserving the area\u0027s natural processes and wildlife is of the utmost importance.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-04-22\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/391/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_BIOSPHERE\t2025-12-23T10:48:34.243Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17427\tNRW_DS98742\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.452Z\t921\tValid\tEditable\tCountry Parks\t© CNC/NRW 2009. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right 2009. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and copyright as specified, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373432\tX:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Country Parks.lyr\tJont Bulbeck - Team Leader, Outdoor Access \u0026amp; Recreation\tThere are over 250 recognised Country Parks in England and Wales. Most Country Parks were designated in the 1970s, under the Countryside Act 1968 with the support of the former Countryside Commission. In more recent times there has been no specific financial support for Country Parks directly, and fewer have been designated. Most are managed by local authorities, although other organisations and private individuals can also run them.   A Country Park is an area designated for people to visit and enjoy recreation in a countryside environment. The purpose of a Country Park is to provide somewhere for visitors who do not necessarily want to go out into the wider countryside. Visitors can enjoy a public open space with an informal atmosphere, as opposed to a formal park as might be found in an urban area. For this reason Country Parks are usually found close to or on the edge of built-up areas.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-10-03\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/915/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:country_parks\t2025-12-23T10:44:03.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17424\tNRW_DS98745\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.322Z\t676\tValid\tEditable\tHeritage Coast\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373435\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\Heritage Coasts.lyr\tCarole Thornley, Lead Specialist Advisor: Landscape Planning, Landscape and Energy\tThis spatial dataset contains digitised boundaries of heritage coasts in Wales. Heritage Coasts occupy about a third of the Welsh coastline, that is 500 km (300 miles). These sites were set up to protect our coastlines from insensitive developments. Most are defined simply by the coastline between two named points, however some have clearly defined inland boundaries. Their status carries no legal protection, but planning authorities must take the designation into account when making decisions on development.   Management of Heritage Coasts is the remit of the local authority and is generally carried out by Heritage Coast Officers with some practical tasks done by volunteers. Most of the Heritage coastal strips were defined in 1973, 1974 and one in 1984. The descriptions and paper maps associated with this dataset are the legal documents.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/387/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_HERITAGE_COAST\t2025-11-11T15:38:17.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17425\tNRW_DS98746\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.358Z\t1151\tValid\tEditable\tLocal Nature Reserves (LNRs)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373436\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\LNR Local Nature Reserves.lyr\tHolly York\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Local Nature Reserves (LNRs) in Wales. Local Nature Reserves are established and managed by local authorities, following consultation with Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949.   For a site to become an LNR it must have natural features of special interest to the local area, and the authority must either have a legal interest in the land or have an agreement with the owner to manage the land as a reserve.   LNR prove to be useful not only to protect habitats and wildlife but increase people\u0027s awareness of their environment. They are places where children can learn about nature, and they are often situated in or near urban areas.   Please refer to Countryside Act 1949 for reasons for capture of original designation.   With the coming of GIS and its use in government departments and commercial companies mobilising the data in this way, advances the protection of these sites and the efficiency of decision making. The LNRs have been designated over a number of years, from 1970 to the present day, and are on-going. The data has been held digitally since mid 1990s. This data can be subject to change by the local authority at anytime.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-12-15\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/388/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_LNR\t2025-12-16T10:11:37.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17423\tNRW_DS98750\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.256Z\t703\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFormer Marine Nature Reserves (MNRs)\t© CNC/NRW 2010.  All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right 2010. There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and copyright as specified, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373530\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Nature Reserve.lyr\t\tIn 2015 the Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). This spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Marine Conservation Zones (MCZs) former Marine Nature Reserves in Wales. MCZs and MNRs are a way of conserving marine habitats and wildlife and other features, of special importance, along the shore or on the seabed. Skomer, the first and only MNR in Wales, was established in 1990 and is situated around the island of Skomer and the Marloes Peninsula in south west Wales. The Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 empowers NRW to apply to the First Minister for the National Assembly for Wales (NAW) to designate MNR.  MNR may be governed by byelaws, created through discussions and mutual agreements with all concerned, and approved by WAG. MNR make a valuable contribution to the protection of our seas by creating an awareness of the fragility and value of the natural marine heritage. In Wales the MNRs that existed have been superseded by MCZs. The Marine and Coastal Access Act 2009 allows for the creation of MCZs. MCZs protect a range of nationally important marine wildlife, habitats, geology and geomorphology, and can be designated anywhere in English and Welsh territorial and UK offshore waters.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17422\tNRW_DS98751\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.123Z\t821\tValid\tEditable\tNational Nature Reserves (NNRs) Declarations\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373531\tX:\\Resource Management\\NNR Declarations\\NNR Declarations.lyr\tThomas Hellawell, Lead Specialist Advisor Estates\tThis is a spatial dataset which shows National Nature Reserves (NNRs) with their different declarations. NNRs represent the very best examples of our wildlife habitats and geological features and can range in size between five hectares to well over 2,000.   NNRs are declared by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act of 1949, or under the Wildlife and Countryside Act of 1981. They are owned or leased by NRW, or the land is held by an approved body, such as a County Wildlife Trust. Each reserve has a programme of work to manage the site\u0027s special features. All of them are also Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and may provide places for educational projects, research and management trials. Some reserves require permits to gain access to them.    Please refer to National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949 and Wildlife and Countryside Act of 1981, for reasons of creation of the original designation. With the coming of GIS and its use in government departments and commercial companies mobilising the data in this way, advances the protection of these sites and the efficiency of decision making.    This data has been checked by NRW staff. The original, individual, declaration paper maps are the legal documents. It should be pointed out that some of the oldest declaration maps.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-12-08\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_nnr_declarations\t2025-03-07T16:36:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17421\tNRW_DS98759\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.061Z\t1123\tValid\tEditable\tRamsar Sites - Wetlands of International Importance\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373539\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Ramsar.lyr x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\\Marine protected areas Ramsar.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Rhodri T. Jones, Advisor (2) Protected Sites (International), Biodiversity Policy \u0026amp; Strategy\tThe Convention on Wetlands, called the Ramsar Convention, is an intergovernmental treaty that provides the framework for national action and international cooperation for the conservation and wise use of wetlands and their resources. This spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Ramsar sites in Wales. In ratifying the Convention in 1976, the UK government accepted a commitment to promote the conservation of internationally important wetland sites within its territories.  Wetlands are vital for many types of birds particularly waterfowl and Wales have some prime sites that are essential to the survival of many wetland plants and animals. Wetland sites can be areas of marsh, fen, peatland or open water; natural or artificial; permanent or temporary; with water that is fresh, brackish or salty. They can also include shallow areas of sea. All Ramsar sites are also Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). Wetlands of International Importance are identified by Natural Resources Wales (NRW), in collaboration with the UK Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC), and designated by the First Minister for the National Assembly for Wales.    The Ramsars have been designated over a number of years, from 1976 to the present day, and are on-going.  The data has been held digitally since mid1990s. The boundaries have been checked by NRW staff. Due to scale enlargement and different editions of map a certain degree of interpretation was required.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/379/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_RAMSAR\t2025-12-23T10:39:55.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17477\tNRW_DS98766\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.385Z\t1757\tValid\tEditable\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373636\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SAC Special Areas of Conservation.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\cSAC Candidate Special Areas of Conservation.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\\Marine protected areas SAC.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\pSAC Proposed Special Areas of Conservation.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Rhodri T. Jones, Advisor (2) Protected Sites (International), Biodiversity Policy \u0026amp; Strategy\tThis spatial dataset contains digital boundaries of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales. The EC Habitats and Species Directive came into effect in 1992 with the aim of maintaining a rich variety of wildlife by protecting vulnerable habitats, and the plants and animals they support. The Habitat Directive together with the 1979 Birds Directive will lead to the establishment of a series of sites throughout Europe that will be known as \u0027Natura 2000\u0027. This represents the cream of European sites and will ensure their survival for future generations. SACs have been designated over a number of years, from 1993 to the present day, and are on-going.    These sites have been mapped on paper since the mid 1990s and then there was a major review from 2000 with the \u0027SAC Moderation Project\u0027 when the data was captured digitally for the first time.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/380/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_SAC\t2025-12-23T10:35:11.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17462\tNRW_DS98775\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.819Z\t1145\tValid\tEditable\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPA)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373735\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SPA Special Protection Areas.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\\Marine protected areas SPA.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\pSPA Proposed Special Protection Areas.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Rhodri T. Jones, Advisor (2) Protected Sites (International), Biodiversity Policy \u0026amp; Strategy\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of all Spatial Protection Areas (SPAs) in Wales. The EC Birds Directive of 1979 requires member states to establish SPAs to conserve the habitats of two categories of birds:  i) Species which are rare or vulnerable, of which there are forty-eight in the UK.   ii) Some migratory species which visit our shores regularly.    SPAs in Wales are identified by NRW, in conjunction with the UK Joint Nature Conservation Committee, and designated by the First Minister for the Welsh Assembly Government.   These sites are also protected through being Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs). The 1994 Conservation Regulations also provide a means of protecting such areas at sea. SPAs together with SACs will contribute to a European Union network of protected sites to be known as \u0027Natura 2000\u0027 (N2K sites). SPAs have been designated over a number of years, from 1982 to the present day, and are on-going.  The data has been held digitally since the mid1990s.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/381/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_SPA\t2026-01-09T16:41:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17474\tNRW_DS98776\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.313Z\t2028\tValid\tEditable\tSites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373736\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SSSI Sites of Special Scientific Interest.lyr X:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\\Marine protected areas SSSI.lyr\tHolly York\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales. SSSIs cover a wide range of habitats from small fens, bogs and riverside meadows to sand-dunes, woodlands and vast tracts of uplands. Most are in private ownership, although some are owned and managed by local wildlife trusts, or other voluntary conservation bodies.   Notification of an SSSI under the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 has since been amended by the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2001, which brought about numerous changes in the way SSSI are notified managed and protected.   In order to ensure consistent, favourable long-term management of these sites, Natural Resources Wales (NRW) with landowners have prepared management plans for all SSSI in Wales. Local planning authorities are required to consult NRW before allowing any development to proceed that may affect an SSSI. Water, gas and electricity companies must also do the same.   SSSIs have been designated, from 1949 to the present day, and are on-going. The data has been held digitally since the mid-1990s. This data has been checked by relevant NRW staff. Please refer to the designation map as the legal definitive boundary. For large SSSIs that were captured digitally and have been printed on a smaller scale map than OS MasterMap, please refer to the OS MasterMap edition at time of capture to view the definitive boundary.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-11-13\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/386/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_SSSI\t2025-12-23T10:16:09.225Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17470\tNRW_DS98777\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.137Z\t1287\tValid\tEditable\tNational Parks\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373737\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\National Parks.lyr\tCarole Thornley, Lead Specialist Adviser Landscape, Designated Landscapes Programme\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of National Parks, which are statutory landscape designations in Wales. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has a legal duty to maintain and make available National Park boundaries.  National Parks are designated for the purposes of conserving and enhancing the natural beauty, wildlife, and cultural heritage and for the public to enjoy the special qualities of these nationally important landscapes.   NRW is the designating authority for any new National Parks in Wales or boundary variations to existing ones, however these must be confirmed by Welsh Ministers. NRWs statutory powers derive from the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act (NPAC) 1949 and Environment Act 1995. The Environment Act (1995) established National Park Authorities, as independent Special Purpose Local Authorities to administer the National Park area in order to deliver their statutory purposes.   Three National Parks have been designated in Wales: Snowdonia 1951 (variation 1974); Pembrokeshire Coast 1952 (Variation 1989); and Brecon Beacons 1955 (Variation 1966).   The data has been held digitally since the mid-1990s. The boundary has been transferred to OS MasterMap by NRW and has been subject to joint checking by NRW staff and the relevant Park authority staff.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-05-29\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/383/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_NATIONAL_PARK\t2025-12-23T09:58:21.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17472\tNRW_DS98778\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.153Z\t1017\tValid\tEditable\tNational Nature Reserves (NNRs)\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373738\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\NNR National Nature Reserves.lyr\tTom Hellawell, Conservation and Recreation Management Planning Team Leader, Ecosystems Partnership \u0026amp; Planning North\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of National Nature Reserves (NNRs) in Wales. NNRs represent the very best examples of our wildlife habitats and geological features and can range in size between five hectares to well over 2,000.   NNRs are declared by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act of 1949, or under the Wildlife and Countryside Act of 1981. They are owned or leased by NRW, or the land is held by an approved body, such as a County Wildlife Trust.   Each reserve has a programme of work to manage the site\u0027s special features. All of them are also Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and may provide places for educational projects, research and management trials. Some reserves require permits to gain access to them. NNRs are designated over a number of years, beginning in 1954 and on going.   This data has been checked by NRW staff. The original, individual, declaration paper maps are the legal documents.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/384/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_NNR\t2025-12-23T10:14:04.230Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17471\tNRW_DS98785\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.152Z\t734\tUnassessed\tEditable\tProtected sites : Old SSSI 1949 Act\t© CNC/NRW 2006 You may reproduce this dataset free of charge for non-commercial and internal business purposes in any format or medium, provided that you do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. To avoid using old information, we recommend that you obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373835\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SSSI 1949 Act.lyr\t\tThis is a spatial dataset. This data set is SSSI notified under the 1949 W\u0026amp;ATC Act only. They were not re-notified under the 1981 Wildlife act.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2006-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17463\tNRW_DS99287\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.808Z\t988\tValid\tEditable\tGeological Conservation Review (GCR) Site Boundaries\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939323837\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Geological Conservation Review sites.lyr   Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\geology\\CCW\\GCR_Site_Boundaries_Original.TAB   Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\geology\\CCW\\gcrsites_north.TAB Held by JNCC and CCW (in Hafod Elfyn basement registry)\tInternal Custodian: (Raymond Roberts) Regional Earth Science Officers, NRW (info on individual sites) Hannah Brazier Cartography Officer, PKCG, NRW (collation and digitisation of data)  Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThe dataset contains the original site boundaries for all Geological Conservation Review (GCR) Sites in Wales.  The boundaries define the area of a particular GCR Site (usually coincident with the geological feature as defined by Common Standards Monitoring). Proposed, current and denotified boundaries are included. Records originally collected to show the extent of GCR blocks.  These blocks represent important geological or geomorphological topics (e.g. Mineralogy), geological time divisions, stages and series (e.g. Llandeilo), and areas of search (e.g. South Wales and the Mendips).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2021-04-02\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/451/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_GCR_SITES\t2025-12-23T09:55:51.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17479\tNRW_DS99333\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.377Z\t21\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGrazing Marsh Site Boundaries\t© CNC/NRW. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939333333\t[A] X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Coastal_and_Floodplain_Grazing_Marsh.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Claire Burrows, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Internal Custodian: Jonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat \u0026amp; Resilience Mapping, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team.   This metadata may not be complete for more information please contact Clare Burrows.\t*** This data is no longer accessible,  therefore this metadata is under review 02/08/2024 ****  This is a spatial dataset containing the boundaries for grazing marsh and floodplain grasslands areas for Wales. These have been collated from the inventory and review of grazing marsh and floodplain grasslands. The aim of this data capture was to produce an inventory of the priority UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitat grazing marshes and flood plains. Information not previously available due to the composite nature of the habitat. Confidence in the site outlines is good. Verification of the habitat coding was undertaken at the time of digitising but errors have subsequently been found.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2003-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:13:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17476\tNRW_DS99344\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.118Z\t966\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Inventory\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939333434\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_heathland Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\phase_2\\lowland_heathland\tInternal Custodian:  Jane Stevens - TEG, NRW (Used to be Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW.)\tThis is a GIS dataset consisting of a inventory of the phase 2 lowland Heathland inventory.  National Vegetation Classification (NVC) is the basis used for the selection of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and is used to define Annex 1 habitats for the selection of Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) under the Habitats Directive (EU Directive 92/43).  This project comprises of detailed data related to the Phase 2 plant community survey for Lowland Heath across Wales. The project covers dry and maritime heath covered by the heathland section of the NVC and wet heath covered by the section of the NVC. Two non-NVC communities the calcareous grass and heath, and shingle heath are also described.  This project had three main purposes: - To provide an overview of the Welsh lowland heathland resource allowing local conservation programs particularly local Biodiversity Action Plans (BAP) partnerships, to assess and interpret their resource in a Welsh context.  - To identify heathland types which are underrepresented in the SSSIs series.  - To identified gaps in knowledge and highlight areas for further study.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-08-13\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6157/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_ph2_lowland_heathland\t2025-12-23T10:08:18.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17478\tNRW_DS99400\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.296Z\t287\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Photo Points\t© CNC/NRW Data may be re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343030\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Map Photos.lyr  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\phase1_intertidal  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media569 Archived under project no. 204, media 569.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW.\tThis is a static spatial dataset.  Phase 1 level intertidal survey photograph index to complement a seamless layer of communities within the intertidal zone. The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005. All work has been undertaken by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team.   Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2007-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T10:00:19.055Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17473\tNRW_DS99408\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.357Z\t338\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Recorder Sample Point Data (Spatial Export)\t© CNC/NRW 2013 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence. No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343038\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine_Recorder\\Marine_Recorder_Biotopes_WGS84.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine_Recorder\\Marine_Recorder_Species_WGS84.lyr\tMetadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team / GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a spatial export of all welsh sample points held within the Marine Recorder database. Not to be distributed outside NRW without the prior permission of the Master Reference Data Team. This is essentially a collation of data from many different marine biological surveys held within Marine Recorder. It contains all biotope instances and species data within the Wales Territorial Sea (12 nautical miles offshore), plus some instances beyond where relevant to boundaries such as the Dee and Severn estuaries.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2023-04-25\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:09:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17469\tNRW_DS99411\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.050Z\t1072\tValid\tEditable\tMINESCAN\t© CNC/NRW. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343131\tX:\\Industrial\\Mines\\MINESCAN.lyr ;  Minescan spreadsheet currently not located (Jun 2022) but Raymond is looking for it after discussing with Gabi Williams as part of TNA project; might be on old CCW drive??  There is also a report that seems to contain info from the spreadsheet but this is not a CCW science report and only marked as National Museum and Galleries of Wales: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Geodiversity/grr/Mineralogy/MINESCAN%20%20An%20Introduction,%20Bevins%20\u0026amp;%20Mason,%201996.PDF\tRaymond Roberts, Lead Specialist Advisor, Geodiversity Policy, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tThis is a spatial dataset containing point data of mine sites (metaliferous non-metaliferous) in Wales. Data taken from minescan spreadsheet data.  The purpose of this data capture was to form a spatial framework for MINESCAN sites.   In 1996 the Minescan project ran for four years. This was commissioned by the former Countryside Council for Wales and John S. Mason undertook the project as a consultant to the National Museum and Galleries of Wales (NMGW).   The project involved field assessments of the conservation value of over 1000 old mines and other mineralogical sites throughout Wales, and the data obtained permitted an objective ranking of the sites relative to one another. As a primary result, a comprehensive network of SSSI and RIGS sites was identified, representing all aspects of mineralisation and metallogenesis in Wales. These sites have a real chance of survival as future scientific resources.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-06-18-2022-06-22\tpublication-creation\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/7627/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_minescan\t2025-12-23T10:08:30.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17480\tNRW_DS99415\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.324Z\t508\tValid\tEditable\tNational Trails\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343135\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\National Trails (Walked line).lyr\tInternal Custodian: Chris Heaps, Specialist Advisor: Trails and Open Access Gary Eccleston/Geospatial team are responsible for the GIS digitisation and updates.\tNational Trails are long distance walking, cycling and horse riding routes through the best landscapes in Wales. After World War II the desire to keep areas of Britain \u0027special\u0027 and to protect them from post-war development led to the establishment of Long Distance Routes (now called National Trails).  Each Trail has a Trail Partnership made up of the local authority or national park responsible for the path on the ground. There is a dedicated National Trail Officer with responsibility for keeping the Trail up to the high standards set for National Trails. Maintenance work is carried out by the local highway authorities together with landowners often with the help of volunteers.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2025-06-18\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/327/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_NATIONAL_TRAIL\t2025-12-23T09:51:10.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17467\tNRW_DS99419\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.098Z\t484\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Habitat Layer\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right.   Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343139\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase1 Habitat.lyr ,   Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\Lookup_tables\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.      Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing point data showing the location of habitats mapped in mixed habitat polygons during the Upland Vegetation Survey.   The habitat codes have been translated from Ratcliffe and Birkes to Phase 1. Corrections made in 2014 are: missing points added for Cothi Tywi and Mynydd Mallaen and Pwllpeirian, and points removed from single habitat and uniform mixed habitat polygons in Berwyn. This update was done when the voronoi version of the Phase 1 veg layer was created.    The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:13:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17464\tNRW_DS99421\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.820Z\t858\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Sand Dune Survey of Wales\t© CNC/NRW 2004. There are no use restrictions on this data. Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343231\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Sand_dunes Site reports are available from NRW\u0027s library.\tInternal Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing a collation of all mapped sand dune habitats in Wales. Over 40 NVC Phase 2 surveys were carried out on sand dunes across Wales. This dataset is a collation of data from these surveys grouped together for mobilisation purposes and to provide information on a Wales-wide scale. One of the major strategic initiatives of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The purpose of this data capture was to help define the sand dune vegetation resource of Wales. The surveys were initiated in the late 1980\u0027s and continued until the mid \u002790\u0027s. Digitisation of the maps took place in 2004.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2022-06-28-2022-10-21\tpublication-revision\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3593/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_phase_2_sand_dunes\t2025-12-23T09:47:32.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17461\tNRW_DS99437\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.807Z\t686\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPaper Air Photo Index\t© CNC/NRW 2004.  You may reproduce this dataset free of charge for non-commercial and internal business purposes in any format or medium, provided that you do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. To avoid using old information, we recommend that you obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343337\tX:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Air photos index\\Paper air photos in NRW index.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team - Hannah Brazier\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the centre point of each paper air photo, taken for the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) between 1991 and 2002. This data is now managed and held by Natural Resources Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:06:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17460\tNRW_DS99457\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.763Z\t937\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Habitat Survey - Vegetation\t© CNC/NRW 2006 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343537\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Terrestial\\Original_Phase_1\\Phase1 Vegetation.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset. The Upland Habitat Survey GIS dataset is based largely on vegetation maps produced by the Wales Field Unit of the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) using the Ratcliffe and Birks vegetation classification. It also contains National Vegetation Classification (NVC) maps for parts of five upland sites and Phase 1 Survey information for three areas of Common Land. The vegetation layer holds the boundaries and attributes of vegetation polygons.  The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. The GIS dataset was created as part of the remit of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to identify all areas of \u0027open country\u0027 in Wales for the implementation of The Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000. All major Welsh upland sites, and other upland areas not mapped by the terrestrial Phase 1 Survey.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2004-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17465\tNRW_DS99459\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.952Z\t321\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Scattered Vegetation Layer\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343539\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase1 Scattered.lyr ,  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\phase_1\\allwales_ph1\\p1colour_sca.tab\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing point data showing the location of scattered vegetation mapped during the Phase 1 Habitat Survey. The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. This dataset is an all - Wales, electronic dataset created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.  Uniformly distributed scattered vegetation is also captured as a polygon attribute in the vegetation layer.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2019-05-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:07:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17475\tNRW_DS99495\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.178Z\t295\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Habitat Survey - Habitat Features\t© CNC/NRW 2006 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343935\tX:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Level 1 all networks\\Upland Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\phase_1\\upland_survey\\habitat_survey_upland_hab.TAB\tInternal Custodian: Habitat and Ecosystems Resource Analyst, NRW.\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons. The Upland Habitat Survey GIS dataset is based largely on vegetation maps produced by the Wales Field Unit of the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) using the Ratcliffe and Birks vegetation classification. It also contains National Vegetation Classification (NVC) maps for parts of five upland sites and Phase 1 Survey information for three areas of Common Land. The Habitat layer is a point layer that shows the location of different habitats and habitat strings mapped in mosaic polygons on the Ratcliffe and Birks vegetation maps.  The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. The GIS dataset was created as part of the remit of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) to identify all areas of \u0027open country\u0027 in Wales for the implementation of The Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000. The Upland Habitat Survey GIS dataset has been combined with digitised data from the terrestrial Phase 1 survey to form the AllWales Phase 1 GIS dataset. All major Welsh upland sites, and other upland areas not mapped by the terrestrial Phase 1 Survey.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:20.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17466\tNRW_DS99503\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.910Z\t665\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBiodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) in Wales\t© CNC/NRW 2015 There are no use restrictions on this data.  Recipients may re-use, reproduce, disseminate this data free of charge in any format or medium, provided they do so accurately, acknowledging both the source and NRW\u0027s copyright, and do not use it in a misleading context. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose, that dissemination or publishing does not result in duplication, and that it is fairly interpreted. Advice on interpretation should be sought where required. To avoid re-using old data, users should periodically obtain the latest version from the original source.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939353033\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestial\\Invertebrates\\Insects  (multiple datasets held under this file)\tCustodian of WID - Mike Howe and therefore contact for Marsh Frillitary data as well. Custodian of GIS data - GIS and Spatial Team\tMarsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority invertebrate species. This dataset is a spatial collation of all confirmed occurrence records of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) butterfly from Wales.  The purpose of this data collation is to inform Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) Partners and inform conservation decisions related to Marsh Fritillary butterflies.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:47.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17468\tNRW_DS99549\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.021Z\t339\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Target Notes\t© CNC/NRW 2015 Recipients may only re-use sensitive data under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939353439\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\.PHASE1_INTERTIDAL_TARGET_NOTES.lyr  Data is archived as project 204, media 569\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a static spatial dataset. Phase 1 level intertidal habitat and substrata information in the format of target notes (point data) to complement a seamless layer of communities within the intertidal zone.  The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005.  All work has been undertaken by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team.   Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.  The data available to the public under an Open Government Licence has some information redacted\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T16:13:57.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17454\tNRW_DS99603\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.351Z\t325\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales Easements Over Third-Party Land\t© CNC/NRW, 2015. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data in strict confidence under the terms of an appropriate NRW Licence.  No publication or dissemination is permitted, except if sensitive records are removed in advance, or shown only at a coarser scale.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363033\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Easements Over 3rd Party Land current line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Easements Over 3rd Party Land current poly.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Easements Over 3rd Party Land proposed line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Easements Over 3rd Party Land proposed poly.lyr\t\tThis is a spatial dataset showing the easements for NRW over third parties land used for Access and pipelines. Data are both completed and proposed. Includes information on site name, owner, scale captured, expiry date, unique codes. This is to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements and to provide a complete table showing all proposed and completed easements across third party land in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17455\tNRW_DS99611\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.523Z\t365\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales Easements Over NRW Land\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Non-sensitive information may be considered for re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.  In addition those seeking commercial re-use of non-sensitive information require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363131\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Easements Over NRW Land current line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Easements Over NRW Land current poly.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Easements Over NRW Land proposed line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Easements Over NRW Land proposed poly.lyr\tNatural Resources Wales GIS and Spatial Data Team\tThis is a  spatial dataset showing the easements for Natural Resources Wales (NRW) over NRW land used for Access and pipelines. Easements are rights of access across land (can be for people / pipelines).  Data are both completed and proposed. Includes information on site name, owner, scale captured, expiry date, unique codes. This is to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements and to provide a complete table showing all proposed and completed easements across NRW land.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:02.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17458\tNRW_DS99623\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.506Z\t785\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW) Landholdings\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363233\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex CCW Land holdings current.lyr  x:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex CCW land holdings expired.lyr  x:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex CCW land holdings proposed.lyr\tHannah Bazier, Cartographic Officer, GIS Team,NRW Ian Medcalf, GIS Officer, Estates Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset consisting of all land currently owned or purchased, by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) which now forms part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  The dataset holds information such as site name, scale captured and unique codes, amongst others. The purpose of this data capture was to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements and provide a complete table showing all land owned by CCW in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2015-12-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17452\tNRW_DS99635\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.207Z\t662\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Target Notes\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Non sensitive data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence providing it is done so, acknowledging both the source and copyright of the owners. It is the recipient\u0027s responsibility to ensure the data is fit for the intended purpose. The associated target notes do contain personal data which may not be released without permission. There are redacted versions of these sensitive targets note available to the public.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363335\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase1 Target.lyr .   The scanned target notes in pdf format are stored on Ffynnon (CCW legacy DMS - now called Content Manager)\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tDuring the terrestrial Phase 1 Survey of Wales thousands of target note and cover notes were created. Over the last 6 years these notes have all been scanned and a layer file (ARCMAP)  has been created to record the position and reference number of Phase 1 target notes.    The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. The target notes layer was created as a way of collating all the Phase 1 terrestrial target note information for All Wales and to allow the pdf target notes to be easily accessible to all staff. Field survey spanned from 1979 to 1997, target notes were produced between 1989 and 1997 and the scanning and digitisation took place between 2004 and 2010.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2010-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:13:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17457\tNRW_DS99644\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.425Z\t367\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTir Cymen Farms\t© WAG NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363434\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\agrienvironment\\wag_depc\\tir_cymen\\tircymen_farms.tab\tGIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Welsh Government and licensed to NRW for internal use only. The dataset has been supplied by the Welsh Government and shows Tir Cymen Farm locations as a point layer.\tWelsh Government\t2006-08-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:47:56.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17453\tNRW_DS99662\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.412Z\t579\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Biotope Map\t© CNC/NRW All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Recipients may only re-use sensitive data under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence and NRWs guidance on Ecological data exempt from general release.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363632\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Biotopes.lyr    Derived Layer: [requestors should get this information from the original layer] Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\biological\\habitats\\marine\\phase1_intertidal\\mapbiotopes.TAB.   Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media569 Data is also archived as project 204, media 569.\tInternal Custodian:  Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environment Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tThis is a static layer. This dataset contains Phase 1 level intertidal habitat and substrata information as a seamless layer of communities within the intertidal zone. The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005.  All work has been undertaken by former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team.    Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping.  They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.   A complete validation process has been undertaken. The data available to the public under an Open Government Licence has some information redacted. An un-redacted version can be requested and may be provided under NRW licence.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T16:15:37.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17456\tNRW_DS99705\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.431Z\t591\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLettings by Natural Resources Wales\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Non-sensitive information may be considered for re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.  In addition those seeking commercial re-use of non-sensitive information require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939373035\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\Lettings by NRW current.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\Lettings by NRW expired.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\Lettings by NRW proposed.lyr\tNatural Resources Wales GIS Team\tThis is a spatial dataset containing all current, proposed and expired Land Agency Lettings by Natural Resources Wales (NRW). This is generally letting out of our land or giving people licences to use our land for certain activities. It is always money coming into NRW. The dataset contains only polygons and includes information on site name, owner, scale captured, expiry date, unique codes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:38.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17459\tNRW_DS99710\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.490Z\t292\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLettings to Natural Resources Wales\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Non-sensitive information may be considered for re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.  In addition those seeking commercial re-use of non-sensitive information require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939373130\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\Lettings to NRW current.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\Lettings to NRW expired.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\Lettings to NRW proposed.lyr\tNatural Resources Wales GIS Team Hannah Brazier Cartographic Officer, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing all current, proposed and expired Land Agency Lettings to Natural Resources Wales (NRW).This is lease of land and sporting rights to NRW, so we can influence how things are managed across this land. This is always money going out from NRW. The dataset contains only polygons and includes information on site name, owner, scale captured, expiry date, and unique codes.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17451\tNRW_DS99725\t2024-05-28T12:24:08.816Z\t409\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales Land Management Agreements\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Non-sensitive information may be considered for re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.  In addition those seeking commercial re-use of non-sensitive information require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939373235\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Agreement current.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\NRW Agreement expired.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Agreement proposed.lyr   Feature class includes proposed, completed and expired Land Management Agreements\tNatural Resources Wales GIS Team\tThis is a spatial dataset containing land management agreements for Natural Resources Wales (NRW). This layer contains all current (completed / live) land management agreements as well as all currently proposed management agreements (not yet signed) Plus expired agreements. Dataset contains only polygons and includes information on site name, owner, scale captured, expiry date and unique codes. The purpose is to provide accurate legal maps for management agreements and to provide a complete table showing all live management agreements in Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17507\tNRW_DS99807\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.760Z\t588\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales Land Management Plans\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Non-sensitive information may be considered for re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.  In addition those seeking commercial re-use of non-sensitive information require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939383037\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Agreement management proposed poly.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Agreement management proposed line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Agreement management current line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Agreement management current poly.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\NRW Agreement management expired line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\NRW Agreement management expired poly.lyr\tInternal Custodian and metadata contact:  GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW (Pippa Jones / Hannah Brazier)\tThis is a spatial dataset containing all currently proposed, completed and expired Land Management Data. This is the information about what management needs doing within the agreement area, e.g. fences, ditches, areas to be burnt etc. It includes information on site name, owner, scale captured, expiry date, and unique codes. The purpose of this data capture was to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements, and provide a complete table showing all management information for management agreements in Wales ( except text and symbols ).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17506\tNRW_DS99835\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.850Z\t278\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales Land Management Agreement Access\t© CNC/NRW and © Crown copyright.  All rights reserved. Natural Resources Wales, AC0000849444  NRW does NOT grant permission to third parties to re-use or disseminate this dataset. Non-sensitive information may be considered for re-used under provision and in line with the terms of a NRW licence.  In addition those seeking commercial re-use of non-sensitive information require a commercial licence available from the OS.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939383335\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Agreement access proposed.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Agreement access current.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\NRW Agreement access expired.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Hannah Brazier or Pippa Jones, Cartographers, GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW  Metadata contact:  GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tThis is a spatial dataset containing all current, proposed and expired agreement Access Routes and includes information on site name, owner, scale captured, expiry date, unique codes. The purpose of this data capture was to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements. and provide a complete table showing all agreement access routes in Wales. High (with less confidence in some pre 2000 data).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t2013-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n"
          }
        ]
      },
