hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\tx:\\Ownership\\Monitoring Equipment\\FCGB Forest Research Experiment Sites.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17251\tEXT_DS123426\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.912Z\t13\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFire and rescue authorities (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343236\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\tx:\\Resource Management\\Emergency Services\\Fire and rescue authorities.lyr\tData Custodian: Geospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management.   Business Contact: Lucy Edwards, Lead Specialist Advisor, Flood Warning \u0026amp; Informing\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17249\tEXT_DS123459\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.987Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh schools (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343539\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.  There is no information about the ownership of this collated dataset\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\tx:\\Social Economic\\Education\\Welsh schools.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17254\tEXT_DS123460\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.908Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOffice of National Statistics (ONS): Built Up Area by Population Size (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343630\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\tx:\\Social Economic\\Office of National Statistics\\Built Up Area by Population Size.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17247\tEXT_DS123462\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.859Z\t11\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOffice of National Statistics (ONS): Medium super output areas 2011 (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233343632\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\tx:\\Social Economic\\Office of National Statistics\\Census 2011\\Medium super output areas 2011.lyr\tData Custodian: Geospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17248\tEXT_DS123552\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.807Z\t644\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironment Agency: Coastal Flood Boundaries (CFB) publication 2018 - England and Wales (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233353532\tThis metadata record is for AfA product AfA 010. This is part of Coastal Design/Extreme Sea Levels, a GIS dataset and supporting information providing design / extreme sea level and typical surge information around the coastline of the UK, including England, Wales, Scotland, Northern Ireland, Isle of Man and Jersey. The information is relevant under present day (year 2018) conditions and does not account for future changes due to climate change, such as sea level rise. This is a specialist dataset which informs on work commenced around the coast ranging from coastal flood modelling, scheme design, strategic planning and flood risk assessments.\tThis dataset is wholly owned by Environment Agency. Data may be re-used under the terms of the Open Government Licence. Enquirers are advised to obtain a copy from the owner/originator directly.\tFull metadata can be viewed via \u0027Resource Locator’\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2018\\CFB Estuary boundaries.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2018\\CFB Exteam sea levels.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2018\\CFB Exteam sea levels estuary.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2018\\CFB Gauge data.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2018\\CFB Surge shapes.lyr\tBen Hext\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\tThird Party Open\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T16:58:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17246\tEXT_DS123682\t2024-05-28T12:18:10.444Z\t577\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRSPB: UK and Eire 95% Utilisation Distributions in 5% Bands (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233363832\tThis is a spatial data collation wholly owned by RSPB. From 2010 to 2015, the RSPB and partners undertook a series of large-scale seabird tracking studies across the UK during the late incubation / early chick rearing period of the breeding season using cutting-edge GPS tracking technology. For four of the species tracked, there was sufficient data coverage to map their UK-wide at sea distributions using habitat selection models. These four species were European shag Phalacrocorax aristotelis, black-legged kittiwake Rissa tridactyla, common guillemot Uria aalge, and razorbill Alca torda. Habitat selection models were based upon all GPS locations and therefore included coverage of all behaviours (e.g. foraging, commuting, resting etc.). This dataset uses the UK distribution maps of these four species to identify important areas of high seabird density at sea, based on hotspot mapping techniques. Utilisation distributions are 2-dimensional probability distributions that represent the time spent in a specific area and thus the probability of encountering an animal in that location during a future observation period.\tUK-level utilisation distributions (UD) for European shag, Razorbill, Black-legged Kittiwake and Common Guillemot. UDs are provided in a sequence of 5% contours for each species starting at the 5% utilisation distribution and ending at the 95% utilisation distributions. Within the ecological literature the 95% UD is often used to define the 95% home range of a species and the 50% UD is used to define the 50% core range of a species.\tFor further information see the following report:  Cleasby IR, Owen E, Wilson LJ, Bolton M (2018) Combining habitat modelling and hotspot analysis to reveal the location of high density seabird areas across the UK: Technical Report. RSPB Research Report no. 63. RSPB Centre for Conservation Science, RSPB, The Lodge, Sandy, Bedfordshire, SG19 2DL.  https://www.rspb.org.uk/globalassets/downloads/documents/conservation-science/cleasby_owen_wilson_bolton_2018.pdf For data Guidelines see the following link:\t\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Matty Murphy  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Services Team\tRoyal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)\t\t2018-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:24:55.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17294\tEXT_DS124237\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.130Z\t654\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMet Office: Numerical Weather Prediction (NWP) System data (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323337\tNumerical weather prediction (NWP) is the process of obtaining an objective forecast of the future state of the atmosphere by running a computer model.\tThis data was obtained for use with the Met Office model by NRW Air Quality strategic evidence team.  The input NWP data is for internal NRW use only and may not be provided to third parties.  The model may be used for Non-commercial Research use only and therefore any outputs derived from the NWP data using the model may be limited to non-commercial use.\tFull metadata can be viewed via Resource Locator.\t\tJiping Shi, Team Leader Air Quality Modelling and Risk Assessment Team, Integrated Evidence Group\tMet Office\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17297\tEXT_DS124279\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.350Z\t940\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLocal Authorities: Conwy Wildlife Sites (archived 2019) (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323739\tConwy Wildlife Sites, 3rd party data and property of local authority.  Archived 2019, the Sites of Importance for Nature Conservation (SINC) record 121617 is to be used instead.\t\t\tArchived from x:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Conwy Wildlife Sites.lyr  in 2019, retained in geospatial store\tInternal contact; Geospatial team\tConwy County Borough Council\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:24:55.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17283\tEXT_DS124281\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.767Z\t330\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLocal Authorities: Denbigh Wildlife Sites (archived 2019) (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323831\tDenbigh Wildlife Sites, 3rd party data and property of local authority. Archived 2019, the Sites of Importance for Nature Conservation (SINC) record 121617 is to be used instead.\t\t\tArchivd from x:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Denbigh Wildlife Sites.lyr , retained in geospatial store\tInternal contact: Geospatial team\tDenbighshire County Council\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17292\tEXT_DS124282\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.937Z\t607\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLocal Authorities: Flint Wildlife Sites (archived 2019) (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323832\tFlint Wildlife Sites, 3rd party data and property of local authority. Archived 2019, the Sites of Importance for Nature Conservation (SINC) record 121617 is to be used instead.\t\t\tArchived from x:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Flint Wildlife Sites.lyr , retained in geospatial store\tInternal contact; geospatial team\tFflintshire County Council \u003d Cyngor Sir y Fflint\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17287\tEXT_DS124284\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.824Z\t482\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLocal Authorities: Wrexham Wildlife Sites (archived 2019) (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323834\tWrexham Wildlife Sites, 3rd party data and property of local authority. Archived 2019, the Sites of Importance for Nature Conservation (SINC) record 121617 is to be used instead.\t\t\tarchived from x:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Wrexham Wildlife Sites.lyr , retained in geospatial store\tInternal contact: Geospatial team\tWrexham County Borough\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17300\tEXT_DS124285\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.338Z\t284\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTree Health Phytophthora ramorum and Phytophthora kernoviae disease data for England \u0026amp; Wales (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323835\tTree Health Phytophthora ramorum and Phytophthora kernoviae disease data for England \u0026amp; Wales, owned by Fera Science Limited; https://www.fera.co.uk/\t\t\tStored on the Y:/ drive for use by the tree health team only.\tData Custodian: Sam Milner/Andy Wright\tFera Science Limited\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17291\tEXT_DS124288\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.933Z\t619\tUnassessed\tEditable\tESTYN: Education data (3rd party data)\tData addysg Estyn (data 3ydd parti)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323838\tFour spatial layers covering the following;    1) ESTYN Higher Education; Higher Education establishments in Wales, as at January 2014.    2) ESTYN annual report 2011-14; Based on the Local Authorities dataset provided by NRW, this layer shows the Local Authorities ESTYN results for period 2012-2014. Fields Overall Performance and Overall Prospects have been updated based on the ESTYN results downloaded from http://www.estyn.gov.uk/english/annual-report/.   3) ESTYN Further Education; Further Education establishments in Wales, as at January 2014.   4) ESTYN Independent Schools; Independant Schools in Wales, as at January 2014.\t\t\tX:\\Social Economic\\Education\tData custodian ; Geospatial Team. Knowledge Information Management\tEstyn\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17284\tEXT_DS124289\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.767Z\t677\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPublic rights of way (PROW) (3rd party data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323839\tPublic rights of way (PROW) data. This data is related to the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Public Rights of Way (PROW) Condition Survey (98725) and covers the baseline PRoW dataset  INFORMATION WARNING this data is from 2007 and as such may be out of date.It should NOT be used for legal challenges. For anything legal related please contact the Local Authorities for the most up to date version of this dataset.\t\t\tx:\\Recreation and Access\\Public rights of way\\PROW.lyr\tData Custodian: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various Local Authorities\t\t2025-04-23\trevision\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-23T09:25:09.000Z\tGary Eccleston\t-\tPublic record\n17296\tEXT_DS124491\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.339Z\t249\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNHS health data (3rd party data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234343931\tHealth statistics by Upper / Lower Super Output Area for adult acticity, diabetes, heart conditions, weight, hospital clinics, hospitals and mental components.\tSourced from freely available statistics provided on NHS Wales Website (http://www.wales.nhs.uk/healthtopics/conditions/) and joined to SOA layer provided by NHS Wales\tNHS Wales Website (http://www.wales.nhs.uk/healthtopics/conditions/)\tX:\\Social Economic\\Health\tInternal contact; Geospatial team\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17290\tEXT_DS124494\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.846Z\t781\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRSPB marine bird data (3rd Party data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234343934\tSpatial layers for marine bird 95% Utilisation Distributions in 5% Bands for; Black-legged kittiwake, Common Guillemot, European Shag and Razorbill.\t\t\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\tInternal contact; geospatial team\tRoyal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17285\tEXT_DS124512\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.766Z\t491\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThreatened Lichen species (3rd party data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353132\tBritish Lichen society collation of the available records for rare and threatened lichens and lichenicolous fungi in Wales from 1700 to 2012, for purposes of conservation, future monitoring and research. Habitat and substrate information is included where available. Species are included if they are nationally rare or scarce, or have a conservation status other than least concern, data deficient or not evaluated.\tObtained from the British Lichen Society on NBN Atlas wales under a Creative Commons with Attribution 4.0 licence\tRecords and details available from NBN Atlas and British Lichen Society\tThe data is downloaded to a spreadsheet, stored in the DMS: teams\u0026gt;evidence\u0026gt;species\u0026gt;lich\u0026gt;Lichen Information\u0026gt;Lichen Statuses (EVID-357-7).  This data is for internal use only and is not to be disseminated. The data has been collated into a gdb along with other information Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Non-vascular Plants\\Non-vascularPlants.gdb\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tBritish Lichen Society\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17277\tEXT_DS124586\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.388Z\t452\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFlood Hazard Research Centre (FHRC): multi-coloured manual (MCM) codes (3rd party data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353836\tMiddlesex University Flood Hazard Research Centre (FHRC): multi-coloured manual (MCM) codes for use in Natural Receptors Database 2019 only\t\t\tData is held on Flood team DMS  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/teams/kdim/Data-Management/dai/Third%20Party%20Data%20Licenses%20Agreements/Middlesex%20University%20(RE%20MCM%20codes%20for%20NRD%20data)/MCM%20licence%20to%2030%20April%202020.docx?d\u003dw8347a6146492437abf82c4b8117109a0\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\tCustodian: Mark Pugh\tFlood Hazard Research Centre (FHRC)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:23.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17299\tEXT_DS124587\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.347Z\t640\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNational Oceanography Centre (NOC): Class A tide gauge data (3rd party data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353837\tNational Oceanography Centre (NOC) Class A tide gauge data\t\t\tData held in WISKI- Hydro module\tCustodian: Liam Perrot / Andy Wall\tNational Oceanography Centre (NOC)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:14:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17288\tEXT_DS124588\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.833Z\t375\tUnassessed\tEditable\tJNCC: 2013 Article 17 species distribution data (3rd party data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353838\tHaitats regulations article 17 species distribution data by JNCC.\t\t\tData held on DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/reporting/sr/Habitats%20Directive/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx  \"I am working with the Environment Portal team to publish the trends in a Power BI report on the NRW website. All data behind the overall results is already publically available on the JNCC website but in spreadsheet format per feature\".\tInternal Contact: Sheryl Davies, Specialist Advisor - Environmental Reporting, Environmental Reporting Team Internal Contact: Dylan Lloyd, Specialist Advisor - Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tJoint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17295\tEXT_DS124590\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.349Z\t611\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMet Office: Bala, Alwen, Ness Gardens climate data (historic) (3rd party data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353930\tMet Office: Bala, Alwen, Ness Gardens climate data (historic data held only; mutual exchange no longer taking place)\t\t\tCustodian: Andy Wall / Zhong Zhang\tCustodian: Andy Wall / Zhong Zhang\tMet Office\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17293\tEXT_DS124591\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.999Z\t749\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMet Office: Soil Moisture Index (MORECS) Weekly and monthly data (3rd party data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353931\tMet Office Soil Moisture Index (MORECS) Weekly and monthly data\t\t\tData held on DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/waterman/wrmr/ww/Morecs%20Data/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx\tCustodian: Andy Wall/Zhong Zhang\tMet Office\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17286\tEXT_DS124593\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.883Z\t429\tUnassessed\tEditable\tScottish Power: cable records (3rd party data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353933\tOnline Access to GIS cable records database, refreshed every 24 hours\t\t\tWe have an access only licence for one NRW user (John A Williams) to access this data online/as a service\tinternal contact: John A Williams\tScottish Power (SP Power Systems Ltd)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:24:54.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17298\tEXT_DS124594\t2024-05-28T12:19:17.348Z\t427\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNetwork Rail: Network Route data (3rd party data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353934\tRail network route data from Network Rail.\t\t\tonly available to Nick Pritchard through the W drive\tInternal contact: Nick Pritchard\tNetwork Rail\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17289\tEXT_DS124598\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.872Z\t41\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNational Flood Asset Dataset for Wales: Ordinary Water courses flood assets held in AMX–Flood (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234353938\tmetadata to be provided by custodian.   AMX flood asset data store contains data that has been split into three types according to data owner (NRW, EA, others)\t\t\tAMX\tawaiting information from custodian Richard Kelland - now moved to new role\tVarious Local Authorities\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17279\tEXT_DS124611\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.481Z\t607\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironment Agency (EA): Integrated Height Model data (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363131\tData supplied by the Environment Agency.   Data was derived from 2016 NRW and EA LIDAR, with gaps derived from licenced APGB DTM data.\t\t\tX:\\Basemapping\\Height\\EA Integrated Height Model 2016 DTM 02m.lyr  X:\\Basemapping\\Height\\EA Integrated Height Model 2016 DTM 02m Hillshade.lyr\tBen Anscombe\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:24:56.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17282\tEXT_DS124614\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.708Z\t484\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRSPB: Marine Mammal Data (Cetaceans and seals) Holy Island, Anglesey (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363134\tAd-hoc sighting records of cetaceans from Ellins Tower, South Stack Anglesey. All records are taken from the window looking west.  Additional information, such as behaviour, direction of travel are also recorded and provided to SeaWatch Foundation. Ad-hoc sighting records of Grey Seals and total number of pups from North Stack to the far end of The Range also known as Penrhosfeilw.\tData is provided to NRW for internal information only.  No internal or external re-use is permitted.  Request to re-use the data internally need to be directed to the RSPB Data Unit.\thttps://www.rspb.org.uk/our-work/conservation/conservation-and-sustainability/mapping-and-gis/\tCetaceanSightingsRecords2019_RSPB.xlsx. held in DMS as document  EVID-446-321 Seal survey 2019_RSPB.xlsx held in DMS as document EVID-446-320\tTom Stringell, Marine Mammal Specialist\tRoyal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:24:56.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17276\tEXT_DS124645\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.296Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironment Agency: NRW Alternate Class Points WGS84 (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363435\tWater Framework Directive saltmarsh monitoring. Saltmarsh points of uncertainty taken from 2018 aerial imagery. These points required ground-truthing to determine the species present. In coordinate system WGS84 for ease of use on hand held differential GPS device.  This data is Open, but owned by the Environment Agency\tSaltmarsh points of uncertainty were derived from our contractors aerial photography. These points were ground-truthed.   This data contributes to Natural Resources Wales Water Framework Directive Classifications.\t\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh Areas\\NRW Alternate Class Points WGS84.lyr\tMarine Monitoring Officer\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\t\t2018-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17281\tEXT_DS124707\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.718Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMedian line to 12 Nautical Miles (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373037\t\t\t\tx:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Median line to 12 NM.lyr\tGeospatial team, Knowledge Information Management Group\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17280\tEXT_DS124708\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.406Z\t10\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government (WG) Cardigan and Tremadog Bays Annex I boulder/cobble reef towed video, multibeam and sidescan Sept 2019 for Assessing Welsh Fishing Activity (AWFA) (3rd Party Data)\tLlywodraeth Cymru (LlC) Bae Ceredigion a Bae Tremadog Atodiad I Rîff clogfeini/coblau - data camera fideo sy’n cael ei lusgo, system amlbelydr a ‘sganio ochr’ ar gyfer Asesu Gweithgareddau Pysgota yng Nghymru (AWFA) - Medi 2019 (Data 3ydd Parti)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373038\t*************Data provided by WG under an OGL.  NRW intend to analyse the data and store for internal and external use.  Any products will belong entirely to NRW, however raw data remains under WG ownership *******************    Survey aim was to improve confidence and confirm the presence or absence of Annex I boulder and cobble reef (stony reef) within areas of Tremadog and Cardigan Bays where historically demersal fishing was undertaken within SACs. NRW has previous survey evidence of low or medium quality (10-40% cover) stony reef in some of these areas, although confidence in this data varies and therefore needs validation, and where found to be present, reef extent needs confirming and mapping.    Survey output included:   * Raw, processed \u0026amp; georeferenced multibeam (MBES) and sidescan (SS) survey data and metadata to include survey period start and end times, locations and any explanatory notes.  * Raw video and stills image files; ship or USBL GPS logs showing locations of all video transects.   Drop-down video (DDV) survey metadata to include:     DDV tow codes, start and end times (i.e. camera first on and last off seabed); Start and end times/coordinates for areas of boulder/cobble reef;    details of species and habitats visible whist viewing the onboard video feed;  and any explanatory notes that will assist with future use of the imagery data.\tProvided by Welsh Government under Open Government Licence.\t\tto be stored in Central Archive - to be confirmed by Stu Kay/Mark Diggle\tHarry Goudge  - Specialist (Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer)\tWelsh Government\tThird Party Open\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:00:34.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17274\tEXT_DS124723\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.236Z\t622\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government: Multibeam, Drop-Down Video survey and Habitat Maps from St David’s Head, Pembrokeshire and West coast of Anglesey (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373233\tThis dataset is wholly owned by Welsh Government. Natural Resources Wales has access to this data through an Open Government Licence. This dataset is made up of three parts: Multibeam Survey data, detailing the topography of the seafloor; drop down video surveys taken at point of interest within the survey extent, and broad habitat maps detailing the extent and location of habitats, based on the findings from the analysis of the drop-down videos and multibeam data. This project has received funding through the European Maritime and Fisheries Fund, which is funded by the European Union and the Welsh Government.\tAll survey work was completed by ABPmer on behalf of Welsh Government. Two areas were chosen for the survey work off covering two broad geographical areas, St David’s Head, Pembrokeshire; and an area to the west of Anglesey. Surveys were conducted during July and September 2019. Multibeam: When collecting multibeam data, an appropriate overlap was maintained to ensure that 100% coverage is achieved without any data gaps or holes. Appropriate statistical analysis of cross line/ main line intersections was done to assess the quality of the data.\tThis data collection forms part of a wider mapping project, looking and the environmental constraints in Welsh water, helping to identify opportunities for aquaculture, wave energy and tidal energy. With this in mind, during selection of suitable survey locations, early prioritisation was afforded to those areas which would maximise the value of evidence obtained rather than those areas which offered relatively good shelter from prevailing wind conditions. https://gov.wales/sites/default/files/publications/2019-11/sustainable-management-of-marine-natural-resources-part-2.pdf https://gov.wales/marine-planning\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\EMFF SMNR Spatial Approach Data from WG\tContact: Internal:  Lee Murrary Marine Mapping Analyst\tWelsh Government\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17278\tEXT_DS124752\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.366Z\t529\tUnassessed\tEditable\tJNCC: Marine Habitat EUNIS level 3 combined Map (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373532\tIn some cases, a full-coverage map displaying the best available data everywhere at the expense of consistency is required. The production of such a product showing EUNIS level 3 habitats requires integrating EUNIS level 3 seabed habitat data from fine and broad-scale habitat maps. The product aims to create a complete map that presents the best available information on the distribution of EUNIS level 3 habitats at any locations in UK waters. This data product is required for, among other things, assessments of progress towards networks of marine protected areas (MPAs) and marine spatial planning in the UK. The aim was to produce a single map layer displaying the best quality EUNIS level 3 data for any given location. Furthermore, the process for producing the layer needed to be:  • Repeatable;  • Transparent; • Easy to explain and understand;  • Objective;  • Fully documented;  • Appropriate for EUNIS level 3 habitats; and,  • Appropriate for the UK intertidal and subtidal areas   Any location would show only a single value describing the habitat at EUNIS level 3, i.e. no overlapping polygons leading to multiple possible values at a location. The data at any location should be the best available to describe the EUNIS level 3 habitat type (i.e. the most likely to be correct). EUNIS level 3 habitats (Appendix 1) describe physical habitats classified using biologically meaningful parameters, substrate type, and additionally for rock: energy and biological zone. Therefore, a method was created to choose data based on their ability to describe these physical variables.\tThe data used to create the final product can be categorised into: 1. Habitat maps created from field survey data through JNCCs obligations to assess and report on seabed habitats at a UK scale, it retains a catalogue of the seabed habitat maps that exist for the UK. Data from these surveys were collected across a range of spatial, temporal and thematic scales, using a variety of survey.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Broadscale habitats\\JNCC combined map EUNIS level 3.lyr   This is a third party dataset, the data is downloadable from the JNCC website.\tNatasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tJoint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)\t\t2019-09-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:24:55.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17275\tEXT_DS124754\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.316Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNational Flood Asset Dataset for Wales: Environment Agency Flood Assets held in AMX–Flood (Historic legacy data) (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373534\tmetadata to be provided by custodian.   AMX flood asset data store contains data that has been split into three types according to data owner (NRW, EA, others)\t\t\tAMX\tRichard Kelland - now moved posts\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17272\tEXT_DS124757\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.048Z\t652\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDepartment for Transport (DfT) Data (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373537\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) holds Department for Transport data (DfT) for Wales. This includes DfT car parks and DfT National Public Transport Access Nodes (NaPTAN) data.  The static car parks dataset includes co-ordinates of each car park and entrance/exit, car park name, locality and operator, for Wales.   The NaPTAN data is a national dataset of public transport access points  - currently NRW have obtained data relating to Welsh bus stops, Welsh ferry terminals and Welsh railway stations.\tThis data is openly available and can be re-used under the terms and conditions of an Open Government Licence.\tFull metadata can be viewed via Resource Locator.\tx:\\Transport\\Dept of Transport car parks.lyr  X:\\Transport\\DfT Welsh ferry terminals.lyr  X:\\Transport\\Railway\\DfT Welsh railway stations.lyr  X:\\Transport\\Bus Stops.lyr\tGeospatial team\tDepartment for Transport\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:24:57.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17271\tEXT_DS124768\t2024-05-28T12:19:16.047Z\t419\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironment Agency (EA): Area of Interest (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373638\tThis dataset is owned by Enivronment Agency (EA). This is a dataset that is used to define the areas by which NRW extract Water Quality Sampling Data and Water Quality Permits to send to the Environment Agency as part of the Data Exchange Agreement.\t\t\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\EA Area of interest.lyr\tGeospatail Team\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:16:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17273\tEXT_DS124785\t2024-05-28T12:19:15.952Z\t17\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government: Glastir Monitoring and Evaluation Programme (GMEP) data (3rd Party data)\tLlywodraeth Cymru: Data Rhaglen Monitro a Gwerthuso Glastir (GMEP) (data 3ydd parti)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373835\tGlastir is the Welsh Government\u0027s sustainable land management scheme which pays for environmental goods and services aimed at: Combating climate change; Improving water quality and managing water resources; Improving soil quality and management; Halting biodiversity loss; Managing landscapes and historic environment and improving public access to the countryside; Woodland creation and management.  The Glastir Monitoring and Evaluation Programme (GMEP) has been commissioned by the Welsh Government to assess the performance of Glastir and ran from 2013 to 2016.   The data layers are as follows:  • Vegetation plot data  • Topsoil meso-fauna data  • Topsoil particle size distribution  • Landscape and habitat area data  • Landscape point data  • Landscape linear feature data  • Pond quality metrics  • Topsoil physio-chemical properties  • Headwater stream quality metrics  • Birds  • Pollinators\tAn attribution statement to acknowledge the source of the information must be used.  A copyright notice for the Data must be included on all copies of the Data, publications and reports, including but not limited to, use in presentations.  The citation of any relevant key publications, Digital Object Identifiers (DOIs) must be included in full in the reference list of any reports or publications that describe any research in which the Data have been used.  Please note, this is open data because it is at a reduced resolution. The full resolution GMEP data on NRW land is restricted data, please see metadata entry EXT_DS125220.\tFurther information about the GMEP programme can be found at https://gmep.wales/ Further information about each of the individual datasets, including citations, copyright notice and DOIs can be viewed through the CEH data catalogue, where the data is made available https://catalogue.ceh.ac.uk/eidc/documents#term\u003dgmep\u0026amp;page\u003d1\tTo be added - Data to be downloaded from CEH data portal and added to the X drive\tInternal Custodian: Claire Burrows, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team External  Custodian: James Skates - Welsh Government       NRW has just negotiated access to all the data 22/06/2022.  Matt is going to discuss with Barny if we need to keep the NRW land version or whether the full data will replace it or whether we will need metadata entries for all three Reduced resolution Open data downloaded from CEH (THis metadata entry_ - suggest renaming to incl Open in the title and adding to the abstract for each of the entries whether they are open data or restricted data) Full resolution data for NRW land Full resolution data for all Wales\tWelsh Government\tThird Party Open\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:03:17.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17327\tEXT_DS124787\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.499Z\t300\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMet Office: Flood Forecasting Data (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373837\tFlood forecasting data (observed and forecast), including Atmospheric Gridded Best Data. Data is provided to NRW to Inform the NRW Flood Warning service on decision making, including input into flood forecasting models. Also used for event planning, informing communications and scaling up of resources for operational resilience.\tData is received via a service and is for internal NRW use only.\t\tNRWs Flood forecasting Service, currently licensed until 2023\tAndy Wall\tMet Office\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17328\tEXT_DS124812\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.611Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCefas: WaveNet data (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383132\tWaveNet is Cefas’ strategic wave monitoring network for the United Kingdom and provides a single source of real-time wave data from a network of wave buoys located in areas at risk from flooding.  The network has been in operation since 2002, WaveNet collects and processes data from the Cefas-operated Datawell Directional Waverider buoys, tethered at strategic locations around the UK coastline. The WaveNet system also gathers wave data from a variety of third party platforms and programmes (industry and public sector-funded).  Cefas has been contracted to make WaveNet data available  in XML format to UK Coastal Flood Forecasting Service (UKCFF) through the Environment Agency or through the Met Office. Such data are collated through a number of contracts with third parties.\tThe data has been licensed via Data Sharing Agreement for internal use only for flood forecasting purposes or to provide technical support of the forecasting systems used by the UKCFF.\t\t\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tCentre for Environmental Fisheries and Aquatic Sciences (CEFAS)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:07:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17321\tEXT_DS124834\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.309Z\t1231\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales and West Utilities: Gas Data (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383334\tWales and West Utilities gas data showing the location of Gas Pipes across Wales. NRW has access to the following:  Intermediate Pressure Gas Main Pipelines Low Pressure Gas Main Pipelines High Pressure Gas Main Pipelines\tThe data is held in a Vector format and is licenced for NRW internal use and can be licenced to NRW Contractors.  NRW last received a copy of the data on 10 September 2020 and users should check whether newer information is available.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Gas\\Wales and West Intermediate pressure gas main.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Gas\\Wales and West Low pressure gas main.lyr  X:\\Utilities\\Gas\\Wales and West Medium pressure gas main.lyr\t\tWales and West Utilities Limited\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17329\tEXT_DS124837\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.617Z\t678\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales and West Utilities: Major Accident Hazard Pipeline (3rd party data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383337\tWales and West Utilities gas data showing the location of Major Accident Hazard Pipelines across Wales.\tThe data is held in a Vector format and is licenced for NRW internal use and can be licenced to NRW Contractors.  NRW last received a copy of the data on 10 September 2020 and users should check whether newer information is available.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Gas\\Wales and West Major Accident Hazard Pipeline.lyr\t\tWales and West Utilities Limited\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:24:57.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17323\tEXT_DS124854\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.328Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMet Office: Quality Controlled Rainfall Data (3rd party data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383534\tTo be completed\tTo be completed\t\t\tH\u0026amp;T Team\tMet Office\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17318\tEXT_DS124855\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.257Z\t899\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Land Cover Map 2015 (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383535\tLand Cover Map is a UK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH) spatial dataset, which NRW acquires under licence. NRW only has a segment of the data and not the whole of GB. This segment covers, the whole of Wales, cross border catchments and coastal areas around the Bristol Channel.  The dataset is an update from the Land Cover Map 2007 (LCM2007).\tLand Cover Map 2015 (LCM2015) is a parcel-based land cover map for the UK, created by classifying satellite data into 21 land cover classes. The classes are based on the UK Biodiversity Action Plan Broad Habitat definitions. LCM2015 was created by classifying two-date composite images and is based mainly on data from Landsat-8 (30m resolution) supplemented with AWIFS data (60m resolution) as required. LCM2015 updates the 2007 Land Cover Map and uses an updated version of the LCM2007 spatial framework. LCM2015, like LCM2007 before it, is therefore constructed from polygons that reflect real-world boundaries. This increases both its ease of interpretation for users and also its compatibility with other geospatial data sets.   The LCM2015 spatial framework was derived from generalised digital cartography (Ordnance Survey MasterMap topographic layer (OSMM) for GB and Land \u0026amp; Property Services Large-scale Vector for Northern Ireland), refined with rural payment boundary. Other differences in between the LCM2007 and LCM2015 datasets include the removal of Montane, Rough Grassland from the classifications.\tFor further information about this 3rd party dataset see the UK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology Website: https://www.ceh.ac.uk/services/land-cover-map-2015\tX:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2015.lyr\t\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:24:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17316\tEXT_DS124931\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.307Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMet Office Rainfall Amount Annual Average 1961-1990 (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393331\tThis is a spatial datasets for annual average rainfall obtained from the Met Office. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) have obtained this dataset via a bespoke licence from the Met Office. The Met Office is the UK National Meteorological Service set up to research the possibilities of forecasting the weather, and now uses more than 10 million weather observation from across the UK to create advanced atmospheric models to predict upcoming forecasts and longer term climate change scenarios.\tThese maps enable you to view maps of annual averages of rainfall between 1961-1990 for Wales only.\t\tX:\\Physical\\Meteorological\\Met Office Rainfall Amount Annual Average 1961-1990.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tMet Office\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:40:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17320\tEXT_DS124935\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.307Z\t755\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEarthTrack (Living Wales) Ground Validation Points for Land Cover and Land Cover Change (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393335\tThe EARTHTRACK app has been specifically designed to allow a wide range of users to collect ground truth data that then supports a) spatial retrieval and validation of attributes with pre-defined units (e.g., m, %) or categories (e.g., species type) from EO data (by allowing measurement and recording of these on the ground) and b) validation of derived land cover and change classifications obtained from satellite and airborne data.   Earth Track points include near real time and historical records of observations of land cover and change on the ground that are geographically referenced (typically \u0026lt; 3-5 m positional accuracy) with well-defined categories that mirror those mapped at the national level through Living Wales (wales.livingearth.online).  These points have been collected by the EarthTrack mobile application, which has been developed using Open Data Kit software and functionality but is being translated (in 2021/22) to a professional version available to be made available on the app stores and allowing use across major operating systems.  All points are used to validate the land cover and land cover change maps from 2018 onwards and into the future, including in near real time.  The Living Wales 10 m national land cover maps for Wales are constructed from environmental descriptors with predefined units (e.g., m, %) or codes (e.g., for lifeform or leaf type) retrieved or classified respectively primarily from satellite sensor data and according to the according to the United Nations (UN) Food and Agriculture Organisation (FAO) Land Cover Classification System (LCCS) Version 2 taxonomy (Di Gregorio, 2005).  The EarthTrack mobile application exactly replicates how these land cover classes are constructed but at the ground level.    The land cover change information also replicates that of the land cover maps for Wales, which is based on evidence, and uses the Global Change Taxonomy (with an associated glossary). This describes change across global environments according to at least 230 classes, with these defined according to the notation ‘impacts (pressures)’.   These categories are derived from the Drivers-Pressures-States-Impacts-Response framework, where the states define the physical, chemical and/or biological characteristics of an environment.  Impacts are discerned from state changes (either one or many) and result from pressures associated with abiotic or biotic events or processes (e.g., fires, droughts) or anthropogenic activities (e.g., ploughing, timber harvesting) with these driven primarily by climate/weather and economy.  Di Gregorio, A.: Land Cover Classification System (LCCS). Classification concepts and user manual for software version 2. FAO Environment and Natural Resources Series, 8, 190 pp (2005).\tEarthTrack points can be recorded by any individual who has downloaded and installed the EarthTrack mobile application on their mobile device.  Spatial accuracy will depend on the individual device used for recording observations and the reliability of observations on the components used to generate the land cover classification and the impact and pressure categories will depend on the knowledge of the person using EarthTrack.  The data is provided to NRW as open data under a Creative Commons licences that requires an attribution (Living wales 2021) on the data and that any derived data MUST be distributed under a CC-BY-SA licence.\tEarth Track website: https://earthtrack.aber.ac.uk/, which allows for download of the mobile (ODK version) and the ground data points as and when acquired.  Data for Wales with photographs for each point (landscape, up and down) can be viewed by clicking on points on the earthtrack website (select LAND \u0026gt; WALES). Living Wales website:  https://wales.livingearth.online/ gives access to Living Wales land cover and change maps for Wales (go to Geoportal and select year and environmental descriptor). UN FAO LCCS Website:  http://www.fao.org/land-water/land/land-governance/land-resources-planning-toolbox/category/details/en/c/1036361/.\tX:\\Biological\\Living Wales\\Earth Track Points in Wales (LCCS data format).lyr\tHelena Sykes and Philip Jutson - Specialist Advisors, Monitoring and Analysis\tAberystwyth University\t\t2017-04-11\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:50:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17312\tEXT_DS124989\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.941Z\t24\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSmall Sewage Discharge Risk Zone Map England (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393839\tSmall Sewage Discharge map of the UK is a risk map covering the whole of England so it can be used to inform the potential risk of Diffuse Water Pollution for new or existing small discharges to ground for all sensitive SSSIs  Please be aware that the dataset is no longer held within NRW and has not been replaced or superseded.  The Metadata entry is a record of the existence of the dataset in NRW\tSouthampton University created the dataset on behalf of Natural England using a methodology developed by May et al 2016.  This looked at the proximity to surface water, slope and depth to ground water were combined and scored to create the risk map of England.\t\t\tDave Jones\tNatural England\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T13:03:41.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17330\tEXT_DS125070\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.574Z\t864\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCardiff Bay Barrage Fisheries Monitoring Programme (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303730\tThe data sets include fish telemetry data, fish trapping data, rod and line caught fish data, broodstock and juvenile stocking data and returning fish data. All data was collected to assess the impact of Cardiff Barrage on the runs of sea trout and salmon on the rivers Taff and Ely. An Annual Report was produced on a yearly basis from 1990 to 2019. A range of supporting information and data was also produced such as weir information.\tThe project was a statutory requirement under the Cardiff Bay Barrage Act 1993 and carried out the following surveys to determine the impact from the barrage; Radio and acoustic tracking DIDSON - Dual-Frequency Identification Sonar Fish trapping Rod and line caught fish Floy tagging Micro tags (coded wire tags)\t\tZ:\\Archive\\Access Recreation \u0026amp; Education\\Access \u0026amp; Recreation Programmes \u0026amp; Projs\\Cardiff Bay Fisheries Monitoring Project\t\tCardiff Council\tThird Party Open\t2019-12-19\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:05:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17315\tEXT_DS125135\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.199Z\t517\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLocal Environmental Records Centres (LERCS) Wales: All Species Data (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313335\tThe data are collated from species datasets held by the Welsh Local Environmental Records Centres.   All species are included in this dataset. If you want just priority and protected species please refer to metadata entry EXT_DS111486 or Invasive non-native species please refer to metadata entry EXT_DS122097.    The purpose of this data capture was to inform conservation management and decisions.  Data being received from LERC\u0027s does vary between centre. North Wales Environmental Information Centre (COFNOD) data only contains data that has been verified by trusted sources and specialists in the field. South East Wales Biodiversity Records Centre (SEWBReC), West Wales Biodiversity Information Centre (WWBIC) and Biodiversity Information Service for Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park (BIS) data contains all protected and priority records including records that have been unverified.\tData is collated from a range of datasets supplied or digitised by Welsh Local Environmental Records Centres (LERC). Data collated from Protected and Priority species data held by the 4 Welsh LERCs. Data are supplied to LERCs by reputable data suppliers. All data are checked before being entered onto the LERCs database.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Local Record Centres\\LRC undesignated species point.lyr   X:\\Biological\\Local Record Centres\\LRC undesignated species polygon.lyr\tBarnaby Letheren\tCofnod (North Wales Local Record Centre)-WWBIC (West Wales Biodiversity Information Centre)-BIS (Biodiversity Information Service for Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park)-SEWBReC (South East Wales Biodiversity Records Centre)\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:15:11.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17326\tEXT_DS125192\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.508Z\t584\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRWE Seahorse Hippocampus guttulatus records (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313932\tExcel spreadsheet of hippocampus guttulatus Seahorse records from RWE and derived GIS.\tData was provided to NRW from RWE.\t\tThe collated GIS dataset with the RWE records can be currently found here: Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Marine_Habitats_Species\\BAP_species\\Updated_records_20200701.gdb\\Hippocampus_guttalatus_20211207 . It is due to go on the X drive in this location: X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\Section 7 Species  . Spreadsheet: Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Marine_Habitats_Species\\BAP_species\\new_data\\RWE_hippocampus_data_GIS_table_confirmed_20211207.xlsx\tNatasha Lough\tRWE Generation UK\tThird Party Conditional\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:19:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17322\tEXT_DS125220\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.247Z\t19\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government: Glastir Monitoring and Evaluation Programme (GMEP) data on NRW land (3rd Party data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323230\tGlastir is the Welsh Government\u0027s sustainable land management scheme which pays for environmental goods and services aimed at:  Combating climate change Improving water quality and managing water resources Improving soil quality and management Halting biodiversity loss Managing landscapes and historic environment and improving public access to the countryside Woodland creation and management  The Glastir Monitoring and Evaluation Programme (GMEP) has been commissioned by the Welsh Government to assess the performance of Glastir. GMEP was launched at the same time as the Glastir scheme. This provides fast policy feedback allowing for the scheme to be modified to improve efficiency and effectiveness.\tInformation warnings for this data:  The location data cannot be used in any way for compliance or inspection purposes.  The location of the survey squares should not be used as the basis for arranging site visits (to areas outside of your management).  The location of the 1km survey squares or data from within them must not be published or shared in any way which may reveal their location. The most detailed level at which the survey square can be shown is the containing 10km2 Ordnance Survey grid square.\t\t\tFull resolution data for NRW land only.  NRW has just negotiated access to all the data 22/06/2022.  Matt is going to discuss with Barny if we need to keep the NRW land version or whether the full data will replace it or whether we will need metadata entries for all three Reduced resolution data downloaded from CEH Full resolution data for NRW land Full resolution data for all Wales\tWelsh Government\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:21:34.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17324\tEXT_DS125269\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.280Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonmouthshire Public Rights of Way (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323639\tPublic footpaths, byways and bridleways in Monmouthshire. Provided by Monmouthshire council in 2022.\tNo restrictions or warnings\tFull data can be viewed on Monmouthshire council website rights of way map browser\tNRWGDBTP.GISDATA.PROW_MONMOUTHSHIRE_22_09_2021\tThomas Blythe\tMonmouthshire County Council\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:43:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17325\tEXT_DS125289\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.496Z\t263\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEMFF Protecting Our Seas Project (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323839\tIn 2020-21 seabed surveys were carried out by ENVISION for the Welsh Fishermen’s Association - Cymdeithas Pysgotwyr Cymru (WFA-CPC) in two Welsh Special Areas of Conservation (SACs), where interaction between mobile fishing gear and conservation features have been highlighted as a potential issue.   The study supported ongoing work by the Welsh Government and Natural Resources Wales to evaluate interactions between fishing activities and features of Welsh Marine Protected Areas (MPAs).  Surveys were completed in the Severn Estuary/Môr Hafren SAC to explore the location of Sabellaria alveolata reefs, and in Cardigan Bay/Bae Ceredigion SAC to establish the extent and ‘reefiness’ of cobble/boulder habitats.  The data products for the Severn Estuary include the processed geophysical data, processed ARIS (Sonar Camera) data, data logs, GIS files and reports. The data products for Cardigan Bay include processed video data, data logs, GIS files and reports.\tData was provided to NRW from WFA-CPC. WFA-CPC have requested that they are consulted or made aware of the intended use for any re-use or publication of the data, before permission is granted. For a full list of the acceptable re-use of this data, please contact the Data Services Team - opendata@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk.\t\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\EMFF Protecting our Seas project Envision contract outputs\tNatasha Lough\tWelsh Fishermen\u0027s Association - Cymdeithas Pysgotwyr Cymru (WFA-CPC)\tThird Party Open\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:22:56.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17314\tEXT_DS125322\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.117Z\t599\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government: Peatlands of Wales Maps (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333232\tThe Peatlands of Wales Map series provides an updated distribution of Welsh Peatlands (to 2022) based on current evidence sources. The data layers were created on a 50m grid whereby the presence and thickness of peat are elucidated from a range of sources for each 50m grid cell across Wales. A peatland evidence score defines the level of confidence in the presence of peat in any given grid cell, with those cells scoring more than 2 on this scale of 1-10, captured in the ‘Peatlands of Wales’ peat distribution map.    Full details of the sources of data used to build this map as well as the methodologies used to define the level of evidence for:    - the presence of peat - https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:peatlands_of_wales_scg8  - the derivation of the peat evidence score - https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:peatlands_of_wales_evidence_2nss  - estimates of peat thickness (depth) - https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:peatlands_of_wales_thickness   - carbon stock estimates - https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:peatlands_of_wales_carbon_stock  - greenhouse gas emissions - https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:peatlands_of_wales_emmisions.  These are presented in the Peatlands of Wales mapping methodology report.\tFull details of the methodology are provided in the methodology report: Peatlands of Wales mapping methodology report. https://gov.wales/soils-policy-evidence-programme-all-documents https://llyw.cymru/cynhyrchu-map-mawndiroedd-cymru.  Please be aware of the following restrictions with this data: any requests for the underlying raw data should be directed to Welsh Government but NRW is able to use the data for internal use only (and provide to contractors working on our behalf).\tDatamap Wales: https://datamap.gov.wales/  DMW Metadata record: https://datamap.gov.wales/maps/peatlands-of-wales-maps/metadata_detail https://mapdata.llyw.cymru/maps/peatlands-of-wales-maps/metadata_detail\tX drive at: X:\\Physical\\Peat Peatlands of Wales  Peatlands of Wales Thickness Peatlands of Wales Evidence Score Peatlands of Wales Emissions Peatlands of Wales Carbon Stock\tEsther Clews\tWelsh Government\tThird Party Conditional\t2022-04-04\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/maps/peatlands-of-wales-maps/\t2026-01-07T17:26:23.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17319\tEXT_DS125406\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.254Z\t548\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSevern Lower Seagrass Project - Seagrass Aerial Imagery Test 2022 (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343036\tAerial imagery collected by Project Seagrass using the WingtraOne VTOL drone. Imagery gathered as a test to determine feasibility of gathering seagrass extent data.  Dataset – aerial imagery in the form of Raster files destined for ARC GIS.\tProvided to NRW from Project Seagrass (owners).\thttps://www.projectseagrass.org/\t\tAdam Leyshon\tProject Seagrass\tThird Party Conditional\t2022-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:27:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17311\tEXT_DS125512\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.000Z\t606\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPackaging Regulations - Approved Reprocessors and Exporters\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353132\tA public register, including filters such as by Regulator, that produces a pdf report in that shows the details of packaging reprocessors and/or exporters that are accredited by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under the Producer Responsibility (Packaging Wate) Obligation Regulations 1997.  The report is downloadable from the National Packaging Waste Database. This is  UK wide ICT system, dedicated to producer responsibility, that is managed by the Environment Agency (EA) on behalf of the regulators across the UK (NRW, SEPA and NIEA).\tThe report is automatically generated from information held in the National Packaging Waste Database -https://npwd.environment-agency.gov.uk/.\t\tN/A  (not stored by NRW – data held on a stand-alone ICT system - National Packaging Waste Database.)   https://npwd.environment-agency.gov.uk/\tKate Llewellyn - Officer 2 - Once in Ops Waste Regulation\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\tThird Party Open\t2022-10-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:28:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17317\tEXT_DS125513\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.244Z\t284\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPackaging Regulations Approved Schemes\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353133\tProducers are manufacturers, converters, importers, pack-fillers and sellers of packaging, who supply to another part of the chain or to the end user. The Producer Responsibility Obligations (Packaging Waste) Regulations 2007 (as amended) oblige companies to recycle packaging waste to meet national targets. Producers\u0027 recycling obligations are based on packaging handled in the previous year.  Businesses must pass two threshold tests to become obligated producers. They must have a turnover of £2million or above and handle 50 tonnes or more of packaging in a calendar year.  Environment Agency store this data and Public Register of Compliance Schemes Reports are available via the National Packaging Waste Database. https://npwd.environment-agency.gov.uk/PublicRegisterSchemes.aspx?ReturnUrl\u003d%2fPackagingPublicRegisterLinks.aspx%3fReturnUrl%3d%252fdefault.aspx\tThe report is generated from information held in the National Packaging Waste Database.\t\tNot stored by NRW. EA Maintain this dataset https://npwd.environment-agency.gov.uk/PublicRegisterSchemes.aspx?ReturnUrl\u003d%2fPackagingPublicRegisterLinks.aspx%3fReturnUrl%3d%252fdefault.aspx\tKate Llewellyn -  Officer 2 - Once in Ops Waste Regulation\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\t\t2022-10-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:40:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17313\tEXT_DS125568\t2024-05-28T12:19:57.136Z\t41\tInvalid\tEditable\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS): Depth to Groundwater (3rd Party Data) (Archived)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353638\tThis dataset is owned by the British Geological Survey (BGS) who is a provides UK geoscientific data, information and knowledge.  This dataset that displays probable maximum depth in metres to the phreatic water table.  This represents the likely lowest water level under natural conditions, in an open well or borehole drilled into the upper parts of the bedrock.  NRW no longer have valid re-use licence for this data from 30th April 2024 and any locally stored copied should be deleted.\tThis is raster dataset created using gridded interpolation with 50 x 50m pixels holding values of the maximum depth to the water table.  The dataset has been modelled from topography and hydrology based on an assumption that the surface water and groundwater are hydraulically connected.  It doesn\u0027t contain any used observations of groundwater level in wells or boreholes but have been used to validate the accuracy of the data.  Please note additional information is available in the User Guide - please contact Data Services for the document.  Please note NRW no longer hold a licence for this dataset from the 30th April 2024 and any locally stored copied should be deleted.\t\tX:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS Depth to Groundwater.lyr\tMatthew Wareham, Data Services Team\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS)\tThird Party Conditional\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:52:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17306\tEXT_DS125585\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.639Z\t10\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Landcover Crops (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353835\tDeveloped in collaboration with Remote Sensing Applications Consultants Ltd (RSAC), the maps exploit satellite data - Copernicus Sentinel-1 C-band SAR (Synthetic Aperture Radar) and, from 2016 onwards, Sentinel-2 optical data.   The crops from 2016 onwards are winter wheat (including oats), spring wheat, winter barley, spring barley, oilseed rape, field beans, potatoes, sugar beet, maize, and improved grass. Other cereals, root crops, early potatoes, and vegetables are grouped in a class called ‘other’, together with a small number of parcels which could not be classified. From 2020, solar fields (sites with solar panels at ground level) are also included.   UKCEH Land Cover® plus: Crops is based on the Land Cover Map parcel framework. Every parcel which is larger than 2ha and categorised as agricultural land is coded with crop type information. Where parcels contain two or more crops, preliminary processing is applied to subdivide parcels into separate cropping units.\tLand Cover Map is a suite of geospatial land cover datasets (raster and polygon) describing the UK land surface. These were produced at the UK Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology by classifying satellite images.    LCM2021 includes four geospatial datasets each for Great Britain and Northern Ireland:  - 10 m Classified Pixel dataset, classified to create a single mosaic of national cover.  - Land Parcel datasets which are the result of intersecting the 10 m Classified Pixel dataset with the UKCEH Land Parcel Spatial Framework to generate land parcel attributes.  - 25 m Pixel Rasterised Land Parcel datasets: the result of rasterising the Land Parcel dataset into 25 m pixels.  - 1 km summary raster products, summarising dominant land cover class and percentage coverage of each land cover class.\t\tArchived\tMatthew Wareham Data Services\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS)\tThird Party Conditional\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:31:11.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17309\tEXT_DS125586\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.699Z\t10\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Land Cover Plus - Crops (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353836\tDeveloped in collaboration with Remote Sensing Applications Consultants Ltd (RSAC), the maps exploit satellite data - Copernicus Sentinel-1 C-band SAR (Synthetic Aperture Radar) and, from 2016 onwards, Sentinel-2 optical data.   The crops from 2016 onwards are winter wheat (including oats), spring wheat, winter barley, spring barley, oilseed rape, field beans, potatoes, sugar beet, maize, and improved grass. Other cereals, root crops, early potatoes, and vegetables are grouped in a class called ‘other’, together with a small number of parcels which could not be classified. From 2020, solar fields (sites with solar panels at ground level) are also included.  UKCEH Land Cover® plus: Crops is based on the Land Cover Map parcel framework. Every parcel which is larger than 2ha and categorised as agricultural land is coded with crop type information. Where parcels contain two or more crops, preliminary processing is applied to subdivide parcels into separate cropping units.\tLand Cover Map is a suite of geospatial land cover datasets (raster and polygon) describing the UK land surface.. These were produced at the UK Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology by classifying satellite images.  LCM2021 includes four geospatial datasets each for Great Britain and Northern Ireland: - 10 m Classified Pixel dataset, classified to create a single mosaic of national cover. - Land Parcel datasets which are the result of intersecting the 10 m Classified Pixel dataset with the UKCEH Land Parcel Spatial Framework to generate land parcel attributes. - 25 m Pixel Rasterised Land Parcel datasets: the result of rasterising the Land Parcel dataset into 25 m pixels. - 1 km summary raster products, summarising dominant land cover class and percentage coverage of each land cover class.\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T17:33:00.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17310\tEXT_DS125587\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.756Z\t703\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTraws Eryri long distance cycle trail – Cycling UK (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353837\tLong distance off road cycle trail promoted by Cycling UK and supported by NRW. Route runs from Machynlleth to Conwy primarily on existing public rights of way - unclassified council roads, bridleways, tarmac roads and forest roads. Some short sections use private land where an agreement for access is in place with the landowner.\tData downloaded from GPX file of route provided by Cycling UK  For internal NRW use only.\t\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Cycling and walking routes\\Traws Eryri cycle trail.lyr  \"X:\\Recreation and Access\\Cycling and walking routes\\Traws Eryri cycle trail fallback route.lyr \"\tJohn H Taylor - Team Leader, Recreation - NW Wales\tCycling UK\t\t2023-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:24:55.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17304\tEXT_DS125633\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.564Z\t948\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMet Office Wave Height - Hindcast Extract (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363333\tMet Office have developed a wave modelling to develop and maintain wave forecasts of sea state.  These wave models have been used as a tool to predict the sea state and wave height which can then be applied to coastal overtopping and flooding.  The forecast model for Wales has been developed from using three locations around the coastline of Wales (North, South and West).  From these three locations, NRW have collected observed data and the following parameters are collected.  The data stored in the archive consists of the following information:  dir: Wave Mean Direction (degree) hs: Significant Wave Height (m) tm: Mean Period Tm01 (s)  NRW have stored this information for all of the offshore locations in Wales for the period 2006 – 2020.\tMet office Wave Model aims to represent how wind energy and other atmospheric process to determine how it influences the sea surface.  This provides the forecaster information on how the energy is transferred to the sea surface and the formation of waves. Observational data can then be fed into the model and be used to forecast wave height and determine any risk present to coastal communities.\thttps://wavenet.cefas.co.uk/hindcast\t\tNeil Counsell, Specialist Advisor - Flood Forecasting, Flood and Operational Risk Management\tMet Office\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t2020-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:32:04.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17305\tEXT_DS125653\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.534Z\t341\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWoodland Creation Planner Scheme (WCPS) Pre-applications (Third Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363533\tNatural Resources Wales provides a verification service to Welsh Government for Government funding for new woodland creation government grants. Woodland Creation Planner Scheme (WCPS) has introduced a pre-application service to inform if land is suitable.\tSite plans are provided as PDFs, photocopies or as a shapefiles from Welsh Government. These are then georeferenced and digitised by a member of the geospatial team.\tData resource received from public for those applying for WCPS Government Grants for new woodland creation.\tX:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\tCarol Owen Email: Carol.Owen@Naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tWelsh Government\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17308\tEXT_DS125658\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.685Z\t497\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS): GeoScour Open Data (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363538\tThe British Geological Survey (BGS) is a UK provider of geoscientific data, information and knowledge. Natural Resources Wales has downloaded this open data for the Wales extent to use internally in our mapping system.  The BGS GeoScour Open dataset provide a generalised overview of the natural characteristics and properties of catchment and riverine environments for the assessment of river scour in Great Britain.  The dataset comprises two different tiers of geographical information system data containing seven different data layers. Each tier represents a different scale of assessment, from a high-level catchment to subcatchment data. The datasets are polygon (area) layers, which are described using straightforward classifications and enabling an indicative catchment susceptibility assessment.\tTier 1 — catchment level data consists of one dataset: catchment stability (1:625 000 scale), which identifies and describes the relative catchment-scale characteristics in terms of landscape evolution, sediment availability and typical response in flood conditions.  Tier 2 — subcatchment level consists of a suite of six subcatchment datasets (1:250 000 scale) including:  catchment geological runoff potential catchment morphology catchment designated sites catchment flood accommodation catchment urban coverage catchment geological susceptibility layers including: (new in V2) Catchment Average Surface Susceptibility Catchment Best Surface Susceptibility Catchment Worst Surface Susceptibility\thttps://www.bgs.ac.uk/datasets/bgs-geoscour-open/ https://www.bgs.ac.uk/download/bgs-geoscour-user-guide/\tX:\\Physical\\Geological\\BGS Geoscour Tier 1 and BGS Geoscour Tier 2  (7 layers)\tCustodian - GIS Team  Internal Contact - Oliver Lowe\tBritish Geological Survey (BGS)\tThird Party Open\t2022-07-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:33:56.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17307\tEXT_DS125713\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.653Z\t281\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Wales wide marine non-native species distribution data from iRecord (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373133\tThis data contains marine non-native species distribution in Wales.  The data was downloaded from iRecord (https://irecord.org.uk/ which is administered by CEH) using a pre set up query. The list of 102 non-native species of interest was provided to CEH in advance. The boundary is set to include species up to 12nm and includes a small inland buffer. The downloaded data contains data input to iRecord at all verification levels, including species which have not yet been verified.\tThe downloaded data comes with licence conditions - the data can be used internally within NRW and shared with NRW contractors, but it cannot be published.  The ID and source information should be kept with the records at all times.  NRW holds two versions of this data – the original download from iRecord with all fields and a second version which has certain columns redacted (e.g. surveyor and verifier) in order to allow this data to be shared with contractors and to make it more streamlined.  The fields removed are;  ExternalKey Order Family TaxonVersionKey Date interpreted Recorder Determiner Verifier\t\tGIS data is on x drive (both full and redacted copies): X\u0026gt;Biological\u0026gt;Non-Native Species\u0026gt;iRecord  Spreadsheets with both full and redacted copies of the data are located here: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mnns/New%20records/Data%20from%20iRecord  Data licence stored: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mnns/New%20records/Data%20from%20iRecord/TERMS_NRW%20download%20from%20iRecord_2023_08_17.pdf\tLaura Grant, Specialist Advisor – Marine Ecology\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t2023-08-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T17:35:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17301\tEXT_DS125725\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.315Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCrown Estate offshore wind leasing round 5 PDA WGS84\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373235\tIn support of The Crown Estate’s Offshore Leasing Round 5, this data describes a final scenario of Project Development Areas (PDAs) for the leasing round.\tData sourced from: https://opendata-thecrownestate.opendata.arcgis.com/datasets/thecrownestate::offshore-wind-leasing-round-5-project-development-areas-pdas/explore?location\u003d51.137181%2C-3.523377%2C7.93 Reprojected to WGS84 in the Central Data Stor\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Crown Estate offshore wind leasing round 5 PDA\tThe Crown Estate, 1 St James\u0027s Market, London, SW1Y 4AH 020 7851 5000 enquiries@thecrownestate.co.uk http://www.ccw.gov.uk\t\t\t2023-11-14\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:19:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19510\tEXT_DS161293\t2024-09-10T14:39:07.496Z\t16\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Land Cover Map 2022 (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323933\tLCM2022 is the tenth UK land cover map produced by UKCEH (others include LCM1990, 2000, 2007, 2015, 2017, 2018, 2019, 2020, 2021). Land cover is given as 21 UKCEH Land Cover Classes based on Biodiversity Broad Habitats (BAP, see Jackson 2000). LCM2022 was created by classifying Classification Scenes comprising Sentinel-2 Seasonal Composite Images combined with a series of Context Layers designed to reduce spectral confusion. The classification process is automatic, using a technique we call Bootstrap Training, combined with a Random Forest classifier.   NRW has access to the 10m Classified Pixel and the 25 m Pixel Rasterised Parcel Datasets\tThese are the result of rasterising the Land Parcel dataset into 25 m pixels, with the pixel origin matching the origin of the British National Grid (for Great Britain) or the Irish National Grid (for Northern Ireland). Three attributes are carried from the Land Parcel datasets, giving a 3-band raster. Band 1 is the dominant land cover _mode; band 2 is _conf; and band 3 _purity.  The 10 m pixel datasets are produced from multiple classified Classification Scenes that are compiled to create a single mosaic of national cover. The Random Forest classifier derives a probability of membership for each class. The class with the highest is the most likely land cover. The first band of the 10 m pixel dataset is an integer representing the most likely UKCEH Land Cover Class. The second band is the probability that was associated with this result; this gives an indication of the confidence of the classification.  Unlike pixels of the 25 m Rasterised Land Parcel dataset, the 10 m Classified Pixels have not been generalised by combination with the UKCEH Land Parcel Spatial Framework. This preserves intricate features of the landscape such as narrow linear features and small patches of habitat that fall below the 0.5 hectare minimum mappable unit (MMU) of the UKCEH Land Parcel Spatial Framework. We anticipate that this extra detail will be of interest and useful but please note the formal validation exercises were performed against the 25 m Rasterised Land Parcel dataset, not the 10 m Classified Pixels.\thttps://catalogue.ceh.ac.uk/documents/1e09f53f-55de-4960-b2a1-da2a42029e7e\tX:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2022 10m Classified Pixels.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2022 25m Rasterised Parcels.lyr\tGIS Team\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\tThird Party Conditional\t2024-05-20\tcreation\tnull\thttps://catalogue.ceh.ac.uk/documents/a413d1c9-5c2a-4864-a3ff-2b9f764ec32d\t2025-03-21T10:38:25.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19547\tEXT_DS161295\t2024-09-18T09:31:46.974Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCopy of template Template for Dataset Metadata in NRW 1.0 format created at 2024-09-18T10:31:46.974+01:00[Europe/London]\tCopy of template Templed ar gyfer Dataset Metadata mewn fformat NRW 1.0 created at 2024-09-18T10:31:46.974+01:00[Europe/London]\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323935\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-07-08T08:29:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19624\tEXT_DS161304\t2024-10-01T10:46:28.807Z\t532\tInvalid\tEditable\tNational Disused Coal Tip (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333034\tThis dataset has been compiled by Welsh Government and shows the locations and boundaries of disused coal tips across Wales.  The dataset includes information on the potential to impact public safety ratings from A to R as well as source of boundary data, local authority where the tip is located and additional information about the tip.\t\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\National Disused Coal Tips.lyr, X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Disused Coal Tips on NRW Land.lyr\tMary Lane, Specialist Advisor, Land Management \u0026amp; Liabilities, Land Management Team\tWelsh Government\tThird Party Conditional\t2024-06-26-2025-10-16\tcreation-publication\tnull\t\t2025-10-16T13:41:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19629\tEXT_DS161308\t2024-10-04T10:27:37.541Z\t22\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Land Cover Map 2022 (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333038\tLand Cover Map 2022 (LCM2022) is the tenth UK land cover map produced by UKCEH (others include LCM1990, 2000, 2007, 2015, 2017, 2018, 2019, 2020, 2021). Land cover is given as 21 UKCEH Land Cover Classes based on Biodiversity Broad Habitats (BAP, see Jackson 2000).   LCM2022 was created by classifying Classification Scenes comprising Sentinel-2 Seasonal Composite Images combined with a series of Context Layers designed to reduce spectral confusion. The classification process is automatic, using a technique we call Bootstrap Training, combined with a Random Forest classifier.     NRW has access to the 10m Classified Pixel and the 25 m Pixel Rasterised Parcel Datasets\tThese are the result of rasterising the Land Parcel dataset into 25 m pixels, with the pixel origin matching the origin of the British National Grid (for Great Britain) or the Irish National Grid (for Northern Ireland). Three attributes are carried from the Land Parcel datasets, giving a 3-band raster:  - Band 1 is the dominant land cover _mode;  - Band 2 is _conf;  - Band 3 is _purity.    The 10 m pixel datasets are produced from multiple classified Classification Scenes that are compiled to create a single mosaic of national cover. The Random Forest classifier derives a probability of membership for each class. The class with the highest is the most likely land cover.   The first band of the 10 m pixel dataset is an integer representing the most likely UKCEH Land Cover Class. The second band is the probability that was associated with this result; this gives an indication of the confidence of the classification.    Unlike pixels of the 25 m Rasterised Land Parcel dataset, the 10 m Classified Pixels have not been generalised by combination with the UKCEH Land Parcel Spatial Framework. This preserves intricate features of the landscape such as narrow linear features and small patches of habitat that fall below the 0.5 hectare minimum mappable unit (MMU) of the UKCEH Land Parcel Spatial Framework. We anticipate that this extra detail will be of interest and useful but please note the formal validation exercises were performed against the 25 m Rasterised Land Parcel dataset, not the 10 m Classified Pixels.\t\tX:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2022 10m Classified Pixels.lyr X:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2022 25m Rasterised Parcels.lyr\tGeospatial (GIS) Team\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\tThird Party Conditional\t2024-06-24\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-27T09:10:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19630\tEXT_DS161309\t2024-10-04T10:41:54.105Z\t1109\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (UKCEH): Land Cover Map 2023 (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\tLand Cover Map 2023 (LCM2023) is the eleventh UK land cover map produced by UKCEH (others include LCM1990, 2000, 2007, 2015, 2017, 2018, 2019, 2020, 2021, 2022). Land cover is given as 21 UKCEH Land Cover Classes based on Biodiversity Broad Habitats (BAP, see Jackson 2000).   LCM2023 was created by classifying Classification Scenes comprising Sentinel-2 Seasonal Composite Images combined with a series of Context Layers designed to reduce spectral confusion. The classification process is automatic, using a technique we call Bootstrap Training, combined with a Random Forest classifier.    NRW has access to the 10m classified Pixel and the 25m Pixel Rasterised Parcel Datasets\tThe 10 m pixel datasets are produced from multiple classified Classification Scenes that are compiled to create a single mosaic of national cover. The Random Forest classifier derives a probability of membership for each class. The class with the highest is the most likely land cover. The first band of the 10 m pixel dataset is an integer representing the most likely UKCEH Land Cover Class. The second band is the probability that was associated with this result; this gives an indication of the confidence of the classification  These are the result of rasterising the Land Parcel dataset into 25 m pixels, with the pixel origin matching the origin of the British National Grid (for Great Britain) or the Irish National Grid (for Northern Ireland). Three attributes are carried from the Land Parcel datasets, giving a 3-band raster:  - Band 1 is the dominant land cover _mode - Band 2 is _conf - Band 3 _purity\t\tX:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2023 10m Classified Pixels.lyr X:\\Physical\\Landcover Map\\Landcover Map 2023 25m Rasterised Parcels.lyr\tGIS Team\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\tThird Party Conditional\t2024-10-04\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2024-10-17T08:41:15.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19666\tEXT_DS161311\t2024-10-17T09:24:41.780Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCopy of template Template for Dataset Metadata in NRW 1.0 format created at 2024-10-17T10:24:41.78+01:00[Europe/London]\tCopy of template Templed ar gyfer Dataset Metadata mewn fformat NRW 1.0 created at 2024-10-17T10:24:41.78+01:00[Europe/London]\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2024-10-17T09:28:28.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20046\tEXT_DS161326\t2025-01-10T13:52:27.289Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHistoric Floodplain Meadows along the River Wye (3rd Party Date)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333236\tA shapefile comprising a single layer containing polygons each representing a floodplain meadow (FPM) as spatial information with attributes. The shapefile covers a study area comprising part of the catchment of the River Wye. the selected stretch covers the main channel of the Wye from Builth Wells to Llangurig. The study area was defined by a 1km buffer along the River Wye, excluding minor tributaries.\tFloodplain meadows have been identified, mapped and recorded based on the interpretation of multiple sources relating to the historic environment, including Historic Ordnance Survey 25 inch maps;  Ordnance Surveyors’ Drawings made between 1780s and 1840; Tithe maps dating from c.1840; Google Satellite imagery; Lidar. The form of each floodplain meadow record is intentionally simple, comprising a single layer in a flat-file format, so that it can be readily included within third-party workspaces/GIS projects.\t\tWaiting for X:Drive load\tStuart Smith - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tFloodplain Meadows Partnership\tThird Party Open\t2025-01-10\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-01-14T10:02:56.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20189\tEXT_DS161332\t2025-02-04T10:25:43.747Z\t9\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNational Soil Resources Institute (NSRI) : Anglesey 63K Soil Series Map (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333332\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.  National Soil Resources Institute has been incorporated into LandIS, or the Land Information System, which is one of the offerings of the Cranfield Environment Centre (CEC) the largest UK national and international centre for research and development, consultancy and training in soils and their interaction with the atmosphere, land use, geology and water resources.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\tx:\\Physical\\Soils\\NSRI Anglesey 63K Soil Series Map.lyr\tGeospatial Team, Knowledge Information Management\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20306\tEXT_DS161342\t2025-02-21T10:59:41.681Z\t27\tInvalid\tEditable\tGazetteer and Bibliography of the Mines and Quarries of Wales (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333432\tJeremy Wilkinson’s Gazetteer and Bibliography of these mines and quarries. The gazetteer currently has entries for more than 4,670 documented mine, quarry and other site names plus entries for more than 4,300 other sites (unnamed levels, pits, quarries, shafts etc.) identified from historic maps.\tThe Gazetteer and Bibliography was originally compiled over a considerable number of years by Jeremy Wilkinson from Public Record Office and other sources of public record. He used the data and a set of BASIC programs to create a database, initially on a BBC B computer and later on PCs.\t\tY:\\Metal Mines\\NWCRO data\\Mines data.gdb\tDave Johnston - Senior Specialist Advisor, Abandoned Metal Mines\tHendre Coed\tThird Party Open\t2025-02-21\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T13:59:27.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n20472\tEXT_DS161351\t2025-03-25T11:43:47.919Z\t35\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGB1900 Filtered Mines and Quarries (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333531\tThe GB1900 project computerised all the place names and other text on the Second Edition County Series six-inch-to-one-mile maps covering the whole of Great Britain, published by the Ordnance Survey between 1888 and 1914. This dataset is a filtered version containing elements identified as related to mining and quarrying\tThe dataset was prepared from the full GB1900 dataset by the North Wales Cave Rescue Organisation and provided to NRW\t\tY:\\Metal Mines\\NWCRO data\tDave Johnston - Senior Specialist Advisor, Abandoned Metal Mines\tNorth Wales Cave Rescue Operation\tThird Party Open\t2025-03-25\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-05-07T08:22:04.830Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n21545\tEXT_DS161391\t2025-10-08T14:55:56.453Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAfonydd Cymru - All Wales River Restoration Opportunities 2017-2025 (3rd Party Data)\tAfonydd Cymru - Cyfleoedd Adfer Afonydd Cymru Gyfan 2017-2025 (Data 3ydd Parti)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333931\tHabitat restoration and barrier easement opportunities identified on Welsh rivers.  Data were collected by Welsh rivers trusts undertaking walkover surveys of watercourses across Wales.\tSurveys were undertaken in 2020-21 and Afonydd Cymru is not responsible for any findings since then.   Data updates take place regularly to ensure scheme status, progress and final costs are correct.\t\tX:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Welsh River Trust Areas.lyr\tRhodri Powell - Specialist Advisor, River Restoration Programme\tAfonydd Cymru\tThird Party Conditional\t2025-09-18\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-08T15:10:56.175Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n21886\tEXT_DS161405\t2025-11-06T11:42:07.921Z\t9\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBannau Brycheiniog National Park Public Rights of Way (3rd party data)\tHawliau Tramwy Cyhoeddus Parc Cenedlaethol Bannau Brycheiniog (Data trydydd parti)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343035\tGIS data set showing the public rights of way across the national park footpaths, including route number, route length, route location - parish and ward.\tThis data set contains PRoW data across the Bannau Brycheiniog National Park Authority.  The data was collected by the National Park, and the data set created by the National Park.\t\tOne Drive\tGeminie Drinkwater, Senior Officer, Land Management, Waterfalls Country Project\tBrecon Beacons National Park Authority\tThird Party Conditional\t2025-11-03\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-12T14:38:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n22278\tEXT_DS161471\t2025-12-03T15:59:10.303Z\t64\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK CEH Stream Power (3rd Party data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343731\tStream power is a measure of river energy based on specific stream power (W/m2).  Rivers with higher energy have a greater capacity to \u0027self-heal\u0027 and can be restored using passive restoration techniques. In contrast, lower energy rivers require more costly interventionist restoration approaches such as remeandering. Through understanding the energy regime of the river we can design restoration to minimize the work needed and maximize the length of river improved by working with and enhancing the natural processes of the river.\tRiver channels were split up into individual points for every 50 m point.  At each point the stream power was derived from the Slope layer and discharge at QMED.   The river locations were derived from 1:50,000 UK CEH digital river network.      Within the dataset SP is stream power also called total stream power (TSP) and SSP is specific stream power which is the total stream power divided by river wetted width, ie SP per meter of transect. SSP allows comparing rivers of different sizes.\t\tX:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\CEH Stream Power.lyr\tSuzanne Hearn\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\tThird Party Open\t2021-10-07\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-17T11:15:06.000Z\tMatthew Wareham\t-\tPublic record\n22529\tEXT_DS161475\t2026-01-08T13:26:02.318Z\t8\tUnassessed\tNon-editable\t4.0 EXT Test Record *** This is not a real dataset *** FULLFIELD EXAMPLE (3rd party dataset)\t4.0 Cofnod Prawf EXT *** Nid set ddata go iawn yw hon *** ENGHRAIFFT MAES LLAWN (set ddata trydydd parti)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343735\tTHIS IS NOT REAL DATA.  • This is NRW\u0027s test dataset for our reduced metadata schema for third party dataset supplied to NRW for use.  We do not write fully standard compliant metadata, but adhere to a reduced NRW standard.  THis record is intended to ensure staff can refer back to a record that meets this NRW standard to help themselves solve validation errors • It is also to ensure all elements are displaying and exporting to multiple XML schemas. The Metadata is written to be compliant with the NRW EXT Standard • It may temporarily be in public view when checking view formats for the public but will be removed later.  Completing a record to the EXT standard using the template will mean it is not compliant with GEMINI or MEDIN standards and therefpre the internal validation routines will have errors.  If in rare cases where (NRW has been transferred custodianship of the data by the third party for purposes of publishing) and the record needs to be onward published to MEDIN or FindOPenData portaland therefore meet the Gemini or MEDIN standard, then the record will need to be edited and added to in Advanced View.  Example text: Geospatial biological datasets provided by Welsh Government.  It includes: - Welsh Goverment bird data - Welsh Government mammal data - Welsh Government insect data\tData is available to use internally only, however derived products can be produced and published for reporting and statutory duties as long as the source of the data is acknowledged and biological data is subject to the same redcations as NRWs own biological data for any species considered exampt from release.\tFuther information about these datasets can be found on the Welsh Government DataMapWales site\tX:\\Biological\\WelshGovernment\\speciesdata.lyr\tNRW contact is Geospatail Team (Keith Harri Jones) Data is sourced through the Welsh Government Environmental Modelling, Geospatial and Monitoring Branch (Oliver Massey)\tWelsh Government\tThird Party Conditional\t2022-02-01\tcreation\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:seabird_foraging_population_counts\t2026-01-08T16:50:48.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17079\tEXT_DS24229\t2024-05-28T12:10:11.121Z\t293\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBiodiversity Species Audit for Wales (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323239\tA Species Audit produced by Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB) as part of the Biodiversity Action Plan process to inform Local Biodiversity Action Planners of the whereabouts and status of selected species considered of particular importance for Wales. It is hoped that, in the longer term, analysis of these data will help identify other measures to address Species Action Plan targets.  The purpose of this data capture is to create an audit of species data across Wales.\tVarious - information on species distribution was collated from 2000 and 2003 into one database.   July 2000 Dataset This dataset holds a variety of information on Insects (C to H) Insects (L to T) non-insect invertebrates, plants, vertebrates and marine species. A total of 651 species were recorded. The spreadsheets hold the following information, species list, distributions, in relation to Vice Counties across Wales, and profiles. The Profiles hold information on species conservation concern, species, distribution and status across Europe, habitat, threats, current management and conservation suggestions.  July 2003 Dataset The dataset holds a full distribution and species list (841 records) which includes but is not limited to, a variety of terrestrial plants, beetles and lichen data,  across the vice counties in Wales. There is a profile of each species giving conversation concern, species name, distribution and status across Europe, Habitat, threats management and suggested conservation mechanisms.\t\tCD ROM in NRW Maes y Fynnon Library. 2 CD’s available: Library Number:  502.74(429)\tGIS Service Desk Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.\tRoyal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)\t\t2003-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:30:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17071\tEXT_DS24266\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.869Z\t700\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDatabase of Welsh Fens (Fenbase 6) (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323636\tDatabase of Welsh Fens, which was collected as part of a larger inventory of UK Fens data (Fenbase) by the Department of Animal and Plant Science, University of Sheffield. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and therefore Natural Resources Wales (NRW) does not possesses the underlying access code and therefore the data base cannot be altered, except for adding, removing and editing data records.\tEnglish Nature (EN) and Scottish Natural Heritage (SNH) also utilise sections of the inventory relevant to their regions, but these data are not shared or compiled with Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) or with each other.\tThe database contains a significant number of errors.\tCCW VMware: H:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\JonesP\\FENBASE\tGIS Service Desk Peter Jones Peatland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW Kathryn Birch Habitat Survey and Evaluation Ecologist Peatland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\tUniversity Of Sheffield. Dept.Of Animal And Plant Science\t\t1990-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:24.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17080\tEXT_DS24275\t2024-05-28T12:10:11.374Z\t279\tUnassessed\tEditable\tJNCC Conservation of River Macrophyte Database (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323735\tA copy of a Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) database used in the conservation of river macrophytes containing a collation of data on: Site Information, Survey Information, Plants, Physical Data, Fish and Invertebrate data.  The database is managed by the Freshwater Lead Co-ordination Network, the data is owned by individual conservation agencies or even individuals and contains NRWs River Macrophyte data.   The purpose of this data capture was to collate data on a variety of river sites across Wales. The database is not fully populated: some invert and fish sections have few records, though most of the coverage is reasonably comprehensive.\tData for the collation is derived from a variety of sources, including conservation agencies or individuals. It includes data from over 7,000 survey sites. Data have been collected from all over the UK between 1977 and the present day, following the methods of Holmes et al. (1999).  NRW contribute to this database, however JNCC act as distributors by making the information available through the JNCC website as an MS Access database.  The RMD is an ‘active’ database (i.e. survey records are still be added). However, with a new standard method of river plant survey now being adopted by the UK conservation agencies (i.e. the LEAFPACS method), it is likely that less surveys will be added in future, and it may develop into more of a \u0027legacy\u0027 database.  The publically available version of this database has some information redacted.\tFurther information is available via the JNCC website http://archive.jncc.gov.uk/default.aspx?page\u003d5815\tThe master version is available through the JNCC website http://jncc.defra.gov.uk/page-5815. NRW hold a copy in archive on server-based storage as project 293, media 1196.  This is the 2011 version, where restrictions were included and is an unreacted version. NRW have also downloaded a copy of the redacted version for general use\tSenior Freshwater Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Evidence Group, NRW.  Tristan Hatton-Ellis acts as the data(base) manager for the Freshwater Lead Co-ordination Network, which represents JNCC on freshwater issues.   This responsibility may have been passed to Sue Hearn       JNCC have requested permission from all agencies/contributors to make this database available as open data 2016-05-11 (Lynn Heeley is main contact).  In principal NRW have agreed providing the database does not contain personal data or sensitive species.  When confirmed we will need to amend the metadata to OGL licences, requests would be referred to JNCC and we can add the correct resource locator.   Data was released under an OGL in 2016.  Personal data (recorder names) has been redacted.  Information about the original restrictions is held in the ReadMe file in the archive media 1196\tNatural England-Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)-Scottish Natural Heritage (SNH)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:12.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17070\tEXT_DS24320\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.588Z\t249\tInvalid\tEditable\tFuturecoast (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333230\tIn 2000, the Department for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs (DEFRA) and the National Assembly for Wales commissioned Halcrow to develop Futurecoast, a completely new way of predicting shoreline evolution. The analysis it contains gives a better understanding of coastal systems and their characteristics, and it is now being used to create the next generation of Shoreline Management Plans.  The main format is oblique aerial photos taken from helicopter of the entire GB coastline, linked to the Ordnance Survey map.  They were produced by DEFRA in conjunction with Halcro, expressly to assist in the shoreline management planning process.  A \u0027behavioural systems\u0027 approach, such as was adopted by the FUTURECOAST project, involves the identification of the different elements that make up the coastal structure and developing an understanding of how these elements interact on a range of both temporal and spatial scales. In this exercise it is the interaction between the units that is central to determining the behaviour. Feedback invariably plays an important role and changes in energy/sediment inputs that affect one unit can in turn affect other units, which themselves give rise to a change in the level of energy/sediment input.  Whilst the starting point for a behavioural system is the energy and sediment pathways, it is important to identify the causative mechanism as a basis for building a robust means of predicting the response to change. This must take account of variations in sediment supply and forcing parameters, such as tide and wave energy. However, it is also important to look for situations where the system response is to switch to a different state, for example, the catastrophic failure of a spit, or the switching of channels as a consequence of episodic storm events.\tThe main component of the study is an analysis of future shoreline evolution potential for every section of the coast of England and Wales that provides an improved understanding of the coastal systems and their behavioural characteristics.\t\tArchived as Project No. 426, Media No. 1459, 1460, 1461, 1462.\tGIS Service Desk Terrestrial Science Group, NRW External Custodian: Beth Greenaway (020) 72381587 beth.greenaway@defra.gsi.gov.uk\tDEFRA (Department for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs)\t\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:56.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17074\tEXT_DS47100\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.864Z\t289\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEffects of Air Pollution and Climate Change on Welsh Calcicolous Ecosystems (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3437313030\tThis dataset is comprised of the welsh component of a larger dataset of 128 plots spread over 56 calcicolous grassland monitoring sites in the UK. In Wales there are 17 plots over 9 calcicolous grasslands. The sites surveyed were eight National Nature Reserves (NNR\u0027s): Cadair Idris, Craig Cerrig gleisiad, Craig y Cilau, Cwm Idwal, Ogof Ffynnon Ddu, Snowdon, South Gower, Stackpole, and one Site of Species Scientific Interest (SSSI): The Great Orme. The idea behind the work was to create a network of calcicolous grassland sites to look at the effects of air pollution and climate change on these sites.\tVegetation was recorded using the NVC classification system in 36 quadrats at each plot. All vascular plants, bryophytes and macro lichens rooted within the quadrat were recorded as qualitatively present. Cover of individual species was not recorded, the overall frequency of species among the quadrats in each plot providing an indication of quantitative presence and an easily repeatable profile of the grassland composition at that location. For each plot environmental data - altitude, slope, aspect, soil type, surface pH and underlying geology have been recorded. The work was carried out by the Unit of vegetation Science at Lancaster University under the guidance of John Rodwell, an expert in NVC vegetation classification. Sub-set of a UK-wide dataset called Effects of Air pollution and climate change on Calcicolous ecosystems.\tWe think that this project was set up by Defra, with the data being collect by the Institute of Terrestrial Ecology (ITE) now known as Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH). The project looked at air pollution across Wales, and built upon the data collected as part of the International Biological Data, all of which formed the baseline datasets helping initiate the Snowdon Environmental Change Network site.\tPaper reports: Room G4 shelves, Monitoring Team, Environmental Change Group.  Raw data: ECG R:drive.\tGIS Service Desk  Environmental Audit Group, NRW External Contact: Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)       Alternative Title: Effects of Air pollution and climate change on British Calcicolous ecosystems - Welsh sites    Alternative Title: Effeithiau llygredd aer a newid hinsawdd ar ecosystemau calchgar Cymru     Temporal Extent: 1992-12-31 ; 1990-01-01/1992-12-31    Frequency of Update: Not Planned Extent : Wales British National Grid No Spatial Element Data Format Type: Documents Site reports including maps and ground photographs in paper format.  Raw data in reports and on MS Excel file. Originator: Department of the Environment (DOE) (Air and Environmental Quality Division)    Originator: Lancaster University. Unit of Vegetation Science\tDEFRA (Department for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs)\t\t1992-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:55:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17078\tEXT_DS64566\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.911Z\t890\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOrdnance Survey Basemaps (3rd Party Data)\tMapiau sylfaenol yr Arolwg Ordnans\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353636\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) holds copies of Ordnance Survey (OS) basemaps for the whole of Wales, in paper and digital format. NRW obtain much of it\u0027s OS data, initially, as part of the Public Sector Mapping Agreement (PSMA) and from 2020 as part of the Public Sector Geospatial Agreement (PSGA), which is a collective agreement between OS and the government.\tOrdnance Survey Basemaps available to Natural Resources Wales includes spatial datasets related to:  - Grids; which includes, 100km, 10km, 20km, 2km, 4km and 5km  - OS MasterMap; which includes All Raster and Mastermap in colour and greyscale, and MasterMap Areas, Lines and Text for various years.  - OS Products; OSMM Urban Greenspace and water lines  - OS Raster Maps; which includes various scales and raster maps in both greyscale and colour.  - OS Strategic Vector 200k; which includes admin boundaries, transport locations, country boundaries, land use, urban areas, coastal and marine boundaries. (This data is considered an old product, please consult the Geospatial Team for more information on suitable alternatives)  Further information about individual datasets can be found through the OS DataHub\t\tX:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping   X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\Grids   X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS MasterMap   X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS Products  X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS Raster maps  X:\\Basemapping\\Ordnance Survey Mapping\\OS Strategi Vector 200k\tGIS Service Desk\tOrdnance Survey (OS)\t\t2020-07-16\tpublication\tnull\thttps://www.ordnancesurvey.co.uk/customers/public-sector\t2025-07-08T08:29:38.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17076\tEXT_DS87452\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.880Z\t382\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Species and Species of Local Conservation Concern for the Dyfi Biosphere Catchment Area (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837343532\tThe Dyfi Biosphere is the only Biosphere Reserve in Wales and includes the Dyfi Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and Cors Fochno SSSI. This dataset is a collation of species records. Just under 5000 species records within the Dyfi catchment.  Species include all UK Species of Conservation Concern, Red Data Book and Nationally Scarce species, Amber List Birds and other locally important species.  Also recorded is UK and EU legally protected species, Section 74 of the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000 (CRoW) species, UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Species and Red List Birds.  The data was collated on behalf of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to contribute to the Dyfi Biosphere project.  Biosphere Reserves are nominated by national governments and designated under the Man and the Biosphere Programme of UNESCO.\tData collated from a wide variety of sources by Powys and Brecon Beacons Local Record Centre (BIS) on behalf of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The dataset is a collation of species records collected between 1934 and 2006 for Priority Species and between 1957 and 2006 for Species of Local Conservation Concern.  These have been collated from a variety of sources, including, but not limited to: Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), British Lichen Society, Montgomeryshire Wildlife Trust, Montgomeryshire Field Society, British Bryological Society and Montgomeryshire Barn Owl Group. Due to the nature of the species collation, part of this dataset is sensitive and restricted.\t\tMaster copy held by BIS Local Record Centre.   Copy held by Mike Bailey (Aberystwyth/Ynys Las).\tGIS Service Desk  Mike Bailey, Dyfi Reserve, Senior Reserve Manager, NRW\tBiodiversity Information Service (BIS)\t\t2006-05-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17075\tEXT_DS99376\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.916Z\t782\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBat Records from National Trust Properties in Wales (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939333736\tThis is a third party dataset containing records from the National Trust Recorder 2002 species database extracted in March 2007.  These datasets include records of all bat species commonly found in Wales and are split into 5 separate surveys.  The National Trust licenced these datasets for use by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and CCW staff only under their Access Agreement. This Access Agreement has now been outdated. The purpose of this data capture was to conduct bat surveys across National Trust sites in Wales.\tRecords come from a wide variety of sources including Biological Survey Reports, Bat Surveys and ad hoc records. Dataset supplied by National Trust, originally exported from Recorder 3.3 and Recorder 2002.  It may also contain duplicates with datasets already held by CCW.  The dataset contains validity codes for species identification as follows: National Trust Validity codes (A/B/C) were included in Occurrence Comment field for most records (often with further details as appropriate, including roost type, further ID info, etc) This is taken from notes relating to our previous bat database (which was incorporated into Recorder, subsequent records will follow this pattern). A - No question about the ID. In the hand, close inspection or unequivocal view plus bat detection sounds. Plecotus would be in here but not brown long-eared unless positively separated from grey long-eared.  Species of small Myotis (daubentonii, nattereri, mystacinus, brandtii) would not normally be in A unless examined in the hand or very close inspection. B - Includes bat detector records of most species by competent bat workers. In here is long range heterodyne calls where the bat is not seen Nyctalus and Eptesicus. Heterodyne records of noctule were not put in A- so few people positively separate it from Leisler\u0027s (or even serotine). Also in here is brown\\ long-eared that has not been positively separated from \u0027grey\u0027 Daubenton\u0027s records solely from small Myotis zooming around a water body and virtually all \u0027whiskered\u0027 and \u0027Brandt\u0027s\u0027. C- Bat record species not known.\"\t\tA copy of the data is held in Recorder 6. This data in is Recorder 6 under the Third Party Data (copy) tag.\tGIS Service Desk Sam Dyer, Mammal Ecologist, bats and riparian mammals, Terrestrial Species Team, NRW       This data in is Recorder 6 under the Third Party Data (copy) tag.\tNational Trust\t\t2006-06-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:18.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17077\tEXT_DS99475\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.905Z\t736\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government: Tir Gofal Whole Farm Boundary (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343735\tThis is a spatial dataset owned by Welsh Government, detailing the extent and location of Tir Gofal which is a whole farm agri-environment scheme across Wales. This third party dataset is licensed to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for internal use only. Tir Gofal, a whole farm agri-environment scheme for Wales, encourages farmers throughout Wales to maintain and enhance the agricultural landscape and its wildlife and to provide new opportunities for people to visit the countryside. It replaces the previous schemes such as Environmentally Sensitive Areas and Tir Cymen, and takes forward the experience gained in a single whole farm scheme.\tThe dataset has been supplied by the Welsh Government and contains Tir Gofal Farm boundaries.\tThis metadata may not be complete for more information please contact the Welsh Government.\tX:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Whole Farm.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Whole Farm Features Line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Whole Farm Features Points.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Tir Gofal Whole Farm Features Poly.lyr    Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\agrienvironment\\wag_depc\\tir_gofal\\wf_signed\\tg_whole_farm.tab\tGIS Service Desk\tWelsh Government\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:56:38.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17068\tEXT_DS99640\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.481Z\t379\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government: Tir Cymen Areas (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363430\tThis dataset is wholly owned by the Welsh Government and licensed to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for internal use only. Tir Cymen Areas details the extent and location of areas that come under the Tir Cymen agri-environment schemes.\tThe dataset has been supplied by the Welsh Government and contains Tir Cymen Areas taken from the old Wales county boundaries, less any overlapping Environmentally Sensitive Areas.\t\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\agrienvironment\\wag_depc\\tir_cymen\\tircymen_area.tab\tGIS Service Desk\tWelsh Government\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:59:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17073\tEXT_DS99645\t2024-05-28T12:10:10.934Z\t213\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Government: Tir Cymen Open Access Areas (3rd Party Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363435\tThis spatial dataset is owned by the Welsh Government and licensed to NRW for internal use only. Tir Cymen is an agri-environment scheme.\tThe dataset has been supplied by the Welsh Government and contains Tir Cymen open access land in Meirionydd, Dinefwr and Swansea.\t\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\agrienvironment\\wag_depc\\tir_cymen\\tir_cymen_open_access_areas.tab\tGIS Service Desk\tWelsh Government\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19274\tNRW_DS000001\t2024-06-26T11:14:56.464Z\t36\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTemplate for Dataset Metadata in NRW 1.0 format\tTempled ar gyfer Dataset Metadata mewn fformat NRW 1.0\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2024-09-25T15:18:56.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19187\tNRW_DS000002\t2024-05-28T13:15:20.379Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2024-07-31T07:58:26.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19184\tNRW_DS000003\t2024-05-28T13:12:50.305Z\t403\tUnassessed\tEditable\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2024-08-02T13:13:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19186\tNRW_DS000004\t2024-05-28T13:14:30.956Z\t165\tUnassessed\tEditable\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2024-08-02T13:13:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17504\tNRW_DS100031\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.712Z\t556\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLand Agency - Proposed Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Holdings\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030303331\tThis is a GIS dataset containing all land proposed to be owned (purchased) by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW).  The dataset includes information on site name, scale captured, and unique codes. The purpose of this data capture was to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements and provide a complete table showing all land proposed to be owned by NRW in Wales.\tDigitised to OS Products at different scales as appropriate (mainly 1:2500).  Data moves from here to completed_land_holdings.\t\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex CCW land holdings proposed.lyr\tInternal Custodian and Metadata contact:  GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW ( Hannah Brazier or Pippa Jones )\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17505\tNRW_DS100107\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.762Z\t768\tValid\tEditable\tLakes Inventory\tLlynnoedd yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313037\tThis is the Welsh subset of an inventory of standing waters (freshwater lakes and lochs) derived from Ordnance Survey digital map data at a scale of 1:50 000 . The inventory basic physical data such as location, surface area, perimeter and altitude. Catchment areas were computed for water bodies with a surface area larger than 1 ha from a digital terrain model (DTM) using customised routines in a geographical information system (GIS).\tAutomated process carried out under contract by Ordnance Survey.    Some errors noticed in the product along with some discrepancies when compared to other datasets. e.g estuaries being counted as lakes.   This dataset is still available on request however it is no longer published as there are improved alternatives available.\thttps://eip.ceh.ac.uk/apps/lakes/about.html\tX:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Lakes inventory.lyr  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\physical\\hydrology\\lakes\\welsh_lakes.tab\tTristan Hatton-Ellis\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2006-03-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:07:12.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17503\tNRW_DS100124\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.911Z\t23\tValid\tEditable\tGrazing Marsh Phase 1 Habitat Data (Derived from Phase 1 Terrestrial Habitat Survey)\tCorsydd Pori Cynefin Cam 1_Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313234\t*** This data is no longer accessible,  therefore this metadata is under review 02/08/2024 ****  This is a spatial dataset based on the Habitats of Wales Phase 1 data (1979-1997) it shows updated phase 1 polygons for Grazing Marsh and Floodplain Grasslands for Wales extracted from the all Wales Phase 1 digitisation.   This was used to produce an inventory of the priority UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitat grazing marshes and flood plains for all Wales, see metadata entry NRW_DS99333.\tExtracted from the all Wales digitisation of the 1979 to 1997 Phase 1 data. OS Landline was used as a backdrop and polygons were captured individually. The data was clipped against the site boundaries layer (see separate metadata entry).    This dataset supersedes the original grazing marsh phase 1 data taken from the Inventory and Review of Grazing Marsh and Floodplain Grasslands. Due to lack of base-mapping and logistics the original boundaries were traced as contiguous habitat blocks onto tracing paper, then captured by scans using a raster to vector conversion tool, resulting in spatial accuracy implications. This updated dataset has improved significantly on the accuracy of the original.\tTerrestrial Ecosystems Group\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Terrestial\\Original_Phase_1   derived data from at least 4 datasets in this folder). The grazing site boundary data is no longer available in this folder - contact GIST for further information.   Archived under Project 248, Media 900. This includes both Site Boundaries and Phase 1 Habitat Data.\tJon Rothwell?\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:02:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17508\tNRW_DS100170\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.791Z\t373\tUnassessed\tEditable\tStoneworts in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313730\tThis is an excel spreadsheet that is also presented as a spatial dataset.  The datasets contains a collation of all known records of stoneworts in Wales from 1726 up till July 2018. Stoneworts (or charophytes) are a group of algae that grow in fresh and brackish water and have a relatively complex structure. They are sensitive to pollution, including nutrient enrichment, and are therefore good indicators of water quality. Thirty species have been recorded in the UK and seventeen of these occur in Wales. Of the latter, four are Red Data Book species, six are Nationally Scarce, and 9 are considered rare in Wales; three are Priority Species in the UK BAP and are included on the National Assembly of Wales\u0027 List of Species and Habitats of Principal Importance for the Conservation of Biological Diversity (the \\Section 74\\\" list). The purpose of this data capture is to inform understanding of the distribution and status of rare an/or threatened lichen species in Wales. This in turn will facilitate decision-making on SSSI notifications species status assessments etc.\"\tOriginal records captured in the field, with subsequent microscopic confirmation of taxon identification where necessary. Data collation from existing databases and paper records.  Based largely of field work carried out for CCW by N F Stewart. The spreadsheet data was initially compiled in 2002 from data from the Biological Records Centre, survey reports, personal data held by interested botanists and an extensive herbarium and literature search. This was subsequently updated in 2004 and 2007.  An additional 500 records have now been added to the 1500 records.   Information Warning: The spatail datasets needs to be updated to reflect that latest version of the data (Mar2020)\tStewart NF, Hatton-Ellis TW. 2020. A Red List of Stoneworts in Wales. NRW Evidence Report No: 406, 105pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tTop copy as a spreadsheet in the DMS (EVID-359161902-13) https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/stnew/Stonewort%20Information/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fstnew%2FStonewort%20Information%2FStonewort%20Statuses  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestial\\Lower_plants\\Stoneworts.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Stoneworts_Grid.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Snr Habitat Survey \u0026amp; Evaluation \u0026amp; Non-Vascular Plant Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW Internal Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW       The GIS data may need tp be updated from the sreadsheet\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17492\tNRW_DS100178\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.378Z\t1202\tValid\tEditable\tOpen Access - Registered Common Land (CRoW Act)\tMynediad Agored - Tir Comin Cofrestredig\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313738\tThe CRoW Act provides for public access on foot to certain types of land, amends the law relating to public rights of way, increases measures for the management and protection for Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and strengthens wildlife enforcement legislation, and provides for better management of Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONB). Under Section 4 of the CRoW Act it states the following: It shall be the duty of Natural Resources Wales (formerly Countryside Council for Wales) to prepare, in respect of Wales, maps which together show; (a)  all registered common land, and (b)  all open country.   It is a redacted version of the \u0027Common Land (CROW Act) Fully Attributed Dataset\u0027, see separate metadata entry for details.   Digitisation took place in 2014 for all Wales. This dataset does not include Urban Commons, Commons with Deeds for Access, 1899 Commons Act, Provisional Order Confirmation or Village Greens.   When using this data to understand the extent of Open Access , it should be used in conjunction with Open land, Open Country, Registered Common Land, Other Statutory Access Land and Other Dedicated Land from the same years. Contact your local authority for access to and information about Registers of Common Land.-Mae Deddf CRoW yn darparu ar gyfer mynediad cyhoeddus ar droed i fathau penodol o dir, yn diwygio\u0027r gyfraith sy\u0027n ymwneud â hawliau tramwy cyhoeddus, yn cynyddu mesurau ar gyfer rheoli a gwarchod Safleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGA) ac yn cryfhau deddfwriaeth gorfodi bywyd gwyllt, ac yn darparu ar gyfer gwell rheolaeth o Ardaloedd o Harddwch Naturiol Eithriadol (AHNE). O dan Adran 4 Deddf CRoW mae\u0027n datgan y canlynol:  Bydd yn ddyletswydd ar Gyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (Cyngor Cefn Gwlad Cymru gynt) i baratoi, mewn perthynas â Chymru, fapiau sydd gyda\u0027i gilydd yn dangos; (a) yr holl dir comin cofrestredig, a (b) yr holl dir agored.  Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn fersiwn wedi\u0027i golygu o\u0027r Set Ddata Tir Comin (Deddf CGaHT) a Fully Attributed Dataset\u0027, gweler y cofnod metadata ar wahân am fanylion. Cynhaliwyd y digido ar gyfer Cymru gyfan yn 2014. Nid yw\u0027r set data yn cynnwys Tiroedd Comin Trefol, Tiroedd Comin gyda Gweithredoedd ar gyfer Mynediad, Deddf Tiroedd Comin 1899, Gorchymyn Dros Dro ar gyfer Cadarnhau Meysydd Pentref. Wrth ddefnyddio\u0027r data hwn ar gyfer Mynediad Agored, dylid eu defnyddio ar y cyd â Thir Agored, Tir Mynediad Statudol Arall a Choedwigoedd Cyhoeddus.   Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis dataset is based on the original common land paper registers as held by the local authorities, and was originally digitised against OS Land-Line by Landmark Information Group (LIG) for NRW for Open Access mapping work.   Any line shown as not matching the original boundary has been copied from what is shown on the original registers and is not a digitising error.   This dataset is an update from the Open Access 10 year review, which was completed in October 2014. The Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000 (CRoW Act 2000) applies to England and Wales only.\tThe Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000 (CRoW Act 2000) applies to England and Wales only.\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Registered Common Land 2004.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Registered Common Land 2014.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Chris Heaps, Specialist Advisor: Trails and Open Access Gary Eccleston/Geospatial team are responsible for the GIS digitisation and updates.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-04-16\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/395/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_COMMON_LAND_2014\t2026-01-06T16:00:25.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17499\tNRW_DS100182\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.564Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLocal Access Forums\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313832\tThis is a spatial Dataset\tLocal Access Forum Boundaries\tContact the GIS Team -see data supplier details\t\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:59:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17510\tNRW_DS100196\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.901Z\t387\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Vegetation Layer\tCam 1 Daearol Cymru Gyfan - llystyfiant\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030313936\tThis is a spatial dataset containing polygon layers showing the boundaries and attributes of vegetation polygons.   In 2014 the data were cleaned by NRW\u0027s Terrestrial Mapping Specialist, to rectify the line work issues, and the resolution was set to 0.5 m. Polygon attributes include Phase 1 habitat code(s), information on scattered vegetation uniformly distributed across an entire polygon, acid flush present in patches too small to map individually, dominant species codes, habitats in re-vegetated quarry and spoil, whether it was apparent from the OS Landline maps at the time of digitising in 2000-2002 that habitat loss had occurred, name of Upland Vegetation Survey site (or site-complex), whether polygon is upland or lowland, polygon area.   The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. The all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales. About 30 percent of upland polygons are mixed habitat with no information on habitat proportions. A voronoi version of the vegetation layer has been created to help address this (see metadata for All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Vegetation Layer voronoi - GIS dataset).\tThe Field survey spanned from 1979 to 1999. Closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors carried out field recording of habitats onto 1:10,000 scale maps (rarely 1:25,000 during the upland stage). The minimum size of habitat patch recorded lies predominantly within the range 0.1 - 0.25 ha, although smaller areas were mapped in the lowlands.   The survey methods employed during the initial upland stage are given in more detail in a WFU report (Day, A. (1989) Upland Vegetation Survey 1979-1989 Background Methodology and Summary of Data Collected. NCC, Bangor). Methods for the lowland survey followed the Handbook for Phase 1 Habitat Survey with some minor modifications adopted for use in Wales (Howe E.A. and Blackstock T.H. (1991) Supplementary Notes for Phase I Survey in Wales. CCW, Bangor).   All field mapping was carried out by trained and closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors. A study of survey reliability was conducted during the lowland phase (Kerry, Rimes, Smith, Williams. 1994. An Assessment of Errors in Phase I Habitat Survey in Wales. Bangor: Countryside Council for Wales, Report No. 94/4/1). It showed the field mapping to be 83% repeatable (Stevens, Blackstock, Howe, Stevens. 2004. Repeatability of Phase 1 habitat survey. J. Environ. Manage. 73, 53-59).The electronic data have undergone a series of checks for accuracy both by the contractor and subsequently within CCW.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase1 Vegetation.lyr .   Version 3 is on the cloud for use in ArcGIS and version 2 is on M:\\Data\\Live.   Full description can be found in M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\Lookup_tables.\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17498\tNRW_DS100201\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.563Z\t581\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Scans\tCam 1 Daearol - Sganiwyd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030323031\tThis is a spatial dataset of raster images of the hand-coloured Phase 1 Survey 5x5 km square 1:10,000 Ordnance Survey maps. The original maps (\u0027master maps\u0027) are lodged in the National Library of Wales.   The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.\tThe field survey spanned from 1987 to 1997. Closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors carried out field recording of habitats onto 1:10,000 scale maps. The minimum size of habitat patch recorded lies predominantly within the range 0.1-0.25 ha, although smaller areas were mapped in the lowlands. Methods followed the Handbook for Phase 1 Habitat Survey with some minor modifications adopted for use in Wales (Howe E.A. and Blackstock T.H. (1991) Supplementary Notes for Phase I Survey in Wales. CCW, Bangor).   All field mapping was carried out by trained and closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors. A study of survey reliability was conducted during the lowland phase (Kerry, Rimes, Smith, Williams. 1994. An Assessment of Errors in Phase I Habitat Survey in Wales. Bangor: Countryside Council for Wales, Report No. 94/4/1). It showed the field mapping to be 83% repeatable (Stevens, Blackstock, Howe, Stevens. 2004. Repeatability of Phase 1 habitat survey. J. Environ. Manage. 73, 53-59). The electronic data have undergone a series of checks for accuracy both by the contractor and subsequently within CCW.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Scans.lyr\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.   Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:02:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17501\tNRW_DS100202\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.735Z\t315\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - SurveyArea\tCam 1 Daearol - Ardal Arolygu\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030323032\tThis spatial dataset shows the boundaries and survey dates of the 15 different survey areas adopted for reporting purposes during the lowland phase of the All Wales terrestrial Phase 1 habitat survey. The survey areas are based on the Nature Conservancy Council\u0027s 15 Areas of Search. The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level.   An all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.\tThe field survey spanned from 1987 to 1997. Closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors carried out field recording of habitats onto 1:10,000 scale maps. The minimum size of habitat patch recorded lies predominantly within the range 0.1-0.25 ha, although smaller areas were mapped in the lowlands.   Methods for the lowland survey followed the Handbook for Phase 1 Habitat Survey with some minor modifications adopted for use in Wales. All field mapping was carried out by trained and closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors. The electronic data have undergone a series of checks for accuracy both by the contractor and subsequently within CCW.\tA study of survey reliability was conducted during the lowland phase (Kerry, Rimes, Smith, Williams. 1994. An Assessment of Errors in Phase I Habitat Survey in Wales. Bangor: Countryside Council for Wales, Report No. 94/4/1). It showed the field mapping to be 83 percent repeatable (Stevens, Blackstock, Howe, Stevens. 2004. Repeatability of Phase 1 habitat survey. J. Environ. Manage. 73, 53-59).\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Survey area.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Habitat Survey.lyr\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2006-04-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17496\tNRW_DS100237\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.422Z\t590\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Upland Boundary\tCan 1 Daearol - Ffininau\u0027r Ucheldir\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030323337\tThis spatial dataset shows the boundary between upland and lowland that was adopted during the terrestrial Phase 1 Survey and the WFU Upland Vegetation Survey of Wales.   The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.\tThe Field survey spanned from 1979 to 1999. Closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors carried out field recording of habitats onto 1:10,000 scale maps (rarely 1:25,000 during the upland stage). The minimum size of habitat patch recorded lies predominantly within the range 0.1 - 0.25 ha, although smaller areas were mapped in the lowlands. The survey methods employed during the initial upland stage are given in more detail in a WFU report (Day, A. (1989) Upland Vegetation Survey 1979-1989 Background Methodology and Summary of Data Collected. NCC, Bangor). Methods for the lowland survey followed the Handbook for Phase 1 Habitat Survey with some minor modifications adopted for use in Wales (Howe E.A. and Blackstock T.H. (1991) Supplementary Notes for Phase I Survey in Wales. CCW, Bangor).   All field mapping was carried out by trained and closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors. A study of survey reliability was conducted during the lowland phase (Kerry, Rimes, Smith, Williams. 1994. An Assessment of Errors in Phase I Habitat Survey in Wales. Bangor: Countryside Council for Wales, Report No. 94/4/1). It showed the field mapping to be 83% repeatable (Stevens, Blackstock, Howe, Stevens. 2004. Repeatability of Phase 1 habitat survey. J. Environ. Manage. 73, 53-59).The electronic data have undergone a series of checks for accuracy both by the contractor and subsequently within CCW.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Upland boundary.lyr\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2021-07-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:01:56.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17497\tNRW_DS100238\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.434Z\t478\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - 1Kmsq\tCam 1 Daearol - 1 cilomedr sgwar\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030323338\tThis is a spatial dataset made up of point dataset of Phase 1 habitat areas in every 1 km square in Wales.   The data are derived from a programme of field recording that was begun by the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) in 1979 and continued by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) after restructuring in 1991. The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level.   This electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage (see metadata for All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - 1kmsq - Access database). The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.   Habitat areas are summed over regions and districts within 1 km squares. Version 2 is a point layer of 1 km square centroids; habitats are labelled with Phase 1 lettered and alphanumeric codes; a \u0027modbog\u0027 column identifies whether modified bog (E.1.7, E.1.8 and E.4) is derived from blanket bog or raised bog; habitat areas are given for upland, lowland and total. Version 3 was derived to facilitate data disaggregation and reporting. In it areas of heath-grass mosaics (D.5, D.6 and D.7) are split 50:50 between the appropriate heathland and grassland classes; modified blanket bog is labelled E.1.6.1.2 and modified raised bog is labelled E.1.6.2.2; the Phase 1 fen categories are combined as E.3 and modified fen as E.3.1; the saltmarsh categories are combined as H.2.6; limestone pavement is combined with basic rock; intertidal habitats are removed because they were mapped in greater detail by CCW\u0027s Phase 1 Intertidal Survey (1996-2005). Version 3 comprises a non-mappable browser table of habitat areas per 1 km square, a table of 1km square centroids and a table of 1 km square polygons. The three tables link on the grid square field.\tThe Field survey spanned from 1979 to 1999. Closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors carried out field recording of habitats onto 1:10,000 scale maps (rarely 1:25,000 during the upland stage). The minimum size of habitat patch recorded lies predominantly within the range 0.1 - 0.25 ha, although smaller areas were mapped in the lowlands. The survey methods employed during the initial upland stage are given in more detail in a WFU report (Day, A. (1989) Upland Vegetation Survey 1979-1989 Background Methodology and Summary of Data Collected. NCC, Bangor). Methods for the lowland survey followed the Handbook for Phase 1 Habitat Survey with some minor modifications adopted for use in Wales (Howe E.A. and Blackstock T.H. (1991) Supplementary Notes for Phase I Survey in Wales. CCW, Bangor).   All field mapping was carried out by trained and closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors. A study of survey reliability was conducted during the lowland phase (Kerry, Rimes, Smith, Williams. 1994. An Assessment of Errors in Phase I Habitat Survey in Wales. Bangor: Countryside Council for Wales, Report No. 94/4/1). It showed the field mapping to be 83% repeatable (Stevens, Blackstock, Howe, Stevens. 2004. Repeatability of Phase 1 habitat survey. J. Environ. Manage. 73, 53-59).  The electronic data have undergone a series of checks for accuracy both by the contractor and subsequently within CCW.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 1kmsq V2.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 1kmsq V3.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 1kmsq V3 centre point.lyr ,  Legacy CCW Archive - M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\ph_1_database\\Version_3\\Ph1_1kmsq_v3_centroids, data and polygons\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2006-04-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:59:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17495\tNRW_DS100241\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.430Z\t383\tValid\tEditable\tBiodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Sabellaria alveolata Honeycomb Worm\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030323431\tSabellaria alveolata reefs are formed by the honeycomb worm Sabellaria alveolata, a polychaete which constructs tubes in tightly packed masses with a distinctive honeycomb-like appearance. These reefs can be up to 30 or even 50 cm thick and take the form of hummocks, sheets or more massive formations. Reefs are mainly found on the bottom third of the shore, but may reach mean high water of neap tides and extend into the shallow subtidal in places. This is a spatial dataset made up of known locations of the Sabellaria alveolata Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitats across Wales. The data are derived from the Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCWs) intertidal Phase 1 survey, data held by the Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) from the Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) project and from ad-hoc records by individuals. The aim of this data capture digitally is to represent the distribution of the BAP habitat across wales.\tField survey and manual digitising from hand drawn maps and sourced from scientific papers, the confidence is high. Data key Red points indicate records from 1979 to 1997 (PH2) ; Yellow points represent records from 1933.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan\\Marine\\Marine Bap Habitats Sabellaria Alveolata Reef.lyr\tContact: GIS and Spatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-16T15:23:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17494\tNRW_DS100338\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.373Z\t333\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland_Sites (Archived)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333338\tThis is a spatial dataset containing sets of survey site boundaries recorded during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales (1987-2004). The Phase 2 lowland Grassland Survey of Wales was a detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation. The chief aims of the Phase 2 survey were to characterise the semi natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed. Sites varied in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering. The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and 2013 by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  Please see NRW_DS46160 which is metadata for the whole Phase 2 Lowland Grass Surveys.\tSurveyed all forms of semi-natural neutral, acidic and calcareous grassland in lowland Wales, as well as wetter Molinia (purple-moor grass) and Juncus (rush) pasture communities and associated soligenous mires. Community definition follows the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). At each site, homogeneous stands of vegetation were identified, sampled with 2 x 2 m quadrats and mapped onto Ordnance Survey base maps or aerial photographs at 1:5000 scale. The main limitation of the data is their age. Habitat composition may change over time due to successional trends and also through agricultural treatments or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still covers that extent on the ground.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_grassland\\PH2_Lowland_Grassland_sites.lyr\tMetadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Services, NRW Custodian: Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-10-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17491\tNRW_DS100358\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.452Z\t364\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Mosaic Polygons (Archived)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333538\tThis is a spatial dataset of grassland mosaic polygons. These polygons are a gridsquares, coloured in chequerboard fasion according to Vegetation_type, for display purposes only. The records where collected during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales ( 1987 - 2004 ). Digitising this datasets allowed the detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation to be accessible more widely. The purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales, and provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed. Sites vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.  The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). CCW now forms part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  The main limitation of the data are the age of records. Habitat composition may change over time due to successional trends and also through agricultural treatments or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.  Please see NRW_DS46160 which is metadata for the whole Phase 2 Lowland Grass Surveys.\tThe records are from surveys conducted on all forms of semi-natural neutral, acidic and calcareous grassland in lowland Wales, as well as wetter Molinia (purple-moor grass) and Juncus (rush) pasture communities and associated soligenous mires. Community definition follows the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). At each site, homogeneous stands of vegetation were identified, sampled with 2 x 2 m quadrats and mapped onto Ordnance Survey base maps or aerial photographs at 1:5000 scale.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_grassland\\PH2_Lowland_Grassland_mospolys.lyr\tCustodian - Terrestrial Ecosystem Group, NRW Jane Stevens, Habitat and Ecosystem Resource Analyst, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-04-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:50:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17502\tNRW_DS100359\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.737Z\t555\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Photo (Archived)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333539\tThe purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed. This is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons. Photo locations recorded during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales (1987-2004). A detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation. This dataset has all Wales coverage. Sites vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.  The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW).  Please see NRW_DS46160 which is metadata for the whole Phase 2 Lowland Grass Surveys.\tSurveys were carried out on all forms of semi-natural neutral, acidic and calcareous grassland in lowland Wales, as well as wetter Molinia (purple-moor grass) and Juncus (rush) pasture communities and associated soligenous mires. Community definition follows the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).  At each site, homogeneous stands of vegetation were identified, sampled with 2 x 2 m quadrats and mapped onto Ordnance Survey base maps or aerial photographs at 1:5000 scale.  The main limitation of the data is their age. Habitat composition may change over time due to successional trends and also through agricultural treatments or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_grassland\\PH2_Lowland_Grassland_photos.lyr   Images are on DVD in the Data Services Safe.\tMetadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW Custodian: Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:50:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17489\tNRW_DS100389\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.724Z\t278\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Quadrat (Archived)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333839\tThe purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed.  This dataset consists of quadrat locations recorded during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland survey of Wales ( 1987 - 2004 ) and a detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation. Sites across Wales vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.  The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by CCW.  Please see NRW_DS46160 which is metadata for the whole Phase 2 Lowland Grass Surveys.\tSurveys were conducted on all forms of semi-natural neutral, acidic and calcareous grassland in lowland Wales, as well as wetter Molinia (purple-moor grass) and Juncus (rush) pasture communities and associated soligenous mires. Community definition follows the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). At each site, homogeneous stands of vegetation were identified, sampled with 2 x 2 m quadrats and mapped onto Ordnance Survey base maps or aerial photographs at 1:5000 scale. The main limitation of the data is their age. Habitat composition may change over time due to successional trends and also through agricultural treatments or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_grassland\\PH2_Lowland_Grassland_quadrats.lyr\tMetadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW Custodian: Terrestrial Ecosystem Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17500\tNRW_DS100390\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.664Z\t316\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Target Notes (Archived)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333930\tThe purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed.  This is a GIS dataset containing target notes recorded during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales (1987 - 2004). The Welsh Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey was a detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation. Sites vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.  The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW).  Please see NRW_DS46160 which is metadata for the whole Phase 2 Lowland Grass Surveys.\tSurveys were conducted on all forms of semi-natural neutral, acidic and calcareous grassland in lowland Wales, as well as wetter Molinia (purple-moor grass) and Juncus (rush) pasture communities and associated soligenous mires. Community definition follows the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). At each site, homogeneous stands of vegetation were identified, sampled with 2 x 2 m quadrats and mapped onto Ordnance Survey base maps or aerial photographs at 1:5000 scale.  The main limitation of the data is their age. Habitat composition may change over time due to successional trends and also through agricultural treatments or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Terrestial\\Original_Phase_1\\Phase1 Target.lyr\tMetadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Services, NRW Custodian: Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:52:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17509\tNRW_DS100391\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.908Z\t323\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Vegetation (Archived)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030333931\tThe purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed.  This is a GIS dataset containing vegetation types recorded during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland survey of Wales (1987 - 2004). A detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation.  Sites vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha. The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and continued since 1991 by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW).  Please see NRW_DS46160 which is metadata for the whole Phase 2 Lowland Grass Surveys.\tSurveyed all forms of semi-natural neutral, acidic and calcareous grassland in lowland Wales, as well as wetter Molinia (purple-moor grass) and Juncus (rush) pasture communities and associated soligenous mires. Community definition follows the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). At each site, homogeneous stands of vegetation were identified, sampled with 2 x 2 m quadrats and mapped onto Ordnance Survey base maps or aerial photographs at 1:5000 scale.  The main limitation of the data is their age. Habitat composition may change over time due to successional trends and also through agricultural treatments or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Terrestial\\Original_Phase_1\\Phase1 Vegetation.lyr\tMetadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Services, NRW Custodian: Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:52:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17490\tNRW_DS100503\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.733Z\t316\tUnassessed\tEditable\tArea of Search\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030353033\tThis is a spatial dataset consisting of the former Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) categories for surveying and following vice County boundaries.\tBased on unitary authorities\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Areas of search.lyr\tGIS Service Desk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Ordnance Survey\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:59:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17493\tNRW_DS100514\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.425Z\t617\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Zostera marina (Eelgrass) Monitoring\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Monitro gwellt y gamlas Zostera marina\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030353134\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Zostera marina is the only flowering plant within the British Isles that grows and produces seed entirely submerged by seawater.  Zostera populations are highly productive habitats and they provide an important stabilising function for the mobile marine sediments. The maintenance of Zostera populations directly influences the associated algal and invertebrate communities that it supports, which are an important source of food for birds. Zostera marina is one of three seagrass species which are listed as nationally scarce and are included as a key habitat for conservation in the UK Biodiversity Action Plan 1994. The bed at North Haven, Skomer is one of only 4 in Wales and is the deepest. It is completely sub tidal and has been well surveyed.  The purpose of this data capture was: 1.  To map the boundaries of the Zostera marina bed. 2.  To determine and identify changes in its distribution and abundance. 3.  Record conspicuous organisms associated with the Zostera population.\tPermanent markers define the corners of a survey plot of 60 x 60 m2, in North Haven and lead lines marked every 5m are laid for the survey duration.  Within the plot area transects are completed every 5 m.  Every 5 metres along each transects Zostera shoot counts are taken in 6, 25 x 25 cm2 quadrats.  The transects are continued outside the survey plot where Zostera is present. Quadrat counts are completed along these transects at 5 metres until no more Zostera is found.  A boat based GPS unit was used to electronically record the boundary of the Zostera bed as divers with a surface marker buoy (SMB) swam the edge of the Zostera bed.\t\tSkomer MNR, West Region Drive  /shared documents/data/biological data/zostera\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:35:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17487\tNRW_DS100675\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.636Z\t315\tValid\tEditable\tEC Bathing Waters Historic Compliance 1988 to 2013\tCydymffurfiaeth Hanesyddol Dŵr Ymdrochi\u0027r UE 1988 i 2013\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030363735\tThis dataset is an archive of data relevant to the old Bathing Water Directive (76/160/EEC) and won\u0027t be updated now the directive is no longer in force.   For the latest bathing water quality information please see https://environment.data.gov.uk/wales/bathing-waters/index.html.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn archif o ddata sy\u0027n berthnasol i\u0027r hen Gyfarwyddeb Dŵr Ymdrochi (76/160/EEC) ac ni fydd yn cael ei diweddaru gan nad ywr gyfarwyddeb mewn grym mwyach. I gael yr wybodaeth ddiweddaraf am ansawdd dŵr ymdrochi gweler https://environment.data.gov.uk/wales/bathing-waters/index-cy.html. Bob wythnos yn ystod y tymor ymdrochi (15 Mai i 30 Medi 1988-2014), cymerodd Asiantaeth yr Amgylchedd samplau o ddyfroedd ymdrochi arfordirol a mewndirol dynodedig.\tEvery week during the bathing season (15 May to 30 September 1988-2014), the Environment Agency took samples from coastal and inland designated bathing waters. These samples were analysed against the standards laid out in the 1976 European Bathing Water Directive and published online.   This data consists of: bathing water sites, site samples and site sample and annual site compliance from 1988-2014.\tBathing water quality  explorer: https://environment.data.gov.uk/wales/bathing-waters/profiles/index.html\tX:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\EC Bathing Waters Historic Compliance 1988 to 2013.lyr\tMark Squire Reporting team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2015-03-17\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/78/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:EUBathingWaterCompliance\t2025-12-01T16:21:58.364Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17486\tNRW_DS100749\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.579Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Enterprise Holdings\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373439\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Forest Enterprise Holdings and licensed to CCW for internal use only.  The dataset has been supplied by the Forest Enterprise Holdings and contains the legal boundaries of Forestry Enterprise land as at 11/07/2001.\t\tPlease contact the Forestry Commission\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\external_agency\\forestry\\forest_enterprise\\fe_land.TAB\t\tForest Enterprise\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:59:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17488\tNRW_DS100750\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.716Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHabitat Agri-Environment Scheme\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373530\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Welsh Assembly Government and licensed to CCW for internal use only. The dataset has been supplied by the Welsh Assembly Government and, similar to the ESA`s, the Habitat scheme is a part farm Agri-Environment scheme, run by the WAG Agricultural Dept.\t\tPlease contact the Welsh Assembly Government.\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\agrienvironment\\wag_depc\\esa\\habitatbddis.TAB\t\tFarming And Rural Conservation Agency [FRCA]\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:06:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17485\tNRW_DS100751\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.454Z\t315\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntegrated Pollution Prevention Control (IPPC) Sites\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373531\tIn 1996 the EU adopted a set of common rules for permitting and controlling industrial installations in the IPPC Directive. In essence, the IPPC Directive was about minimising pollution from various industrial sources throughout the EU. Operators of industrial installations whose operating activities covered by Annex I of the IPPC Directive were required to obtain an environmental permit from the authorities in the EU countries.  Integrated Pollution Prevention Control (IPPC) Sites is a spatial dataset detail the point location of industrial installations that require a permit under The IPPC Directive. Please note, these points do not indicate the size or area covered by the installations, they represent a general location only. The system of Integrated Pollution Prevention and Control (IPPC) set out in the IPPC Directive applies an integrated environmental approach to the regulation of certain industrial activities. This means that emissions to air, water (including discharges to sewer) and land, plus a range of other environmental effects, must be considered together as part of a bespoke mix of regulated pollution prevention. This bespoke regulation will depend on the type, location and size of the installation. IPPC aims to prevent emissions and waste production and where that is not practicable, reduce them to acceptable levels.\tThis dataset has been extracted from the Permitting and Licences (PALS) database, which forms part of the Customer Relationship Management software. It contains information from both within Wales (Natural Resources Wales owned data) and England (Environment Agency owned data).  INFORMATION WARNING: This dataset has not been updated since 2014. For a more up to date version contact General Enquiries.\tIPPC Directive was replaced with the Industrial Emissions Directive and within the UK these are regulated as EPR Installations under the Environmental Permitting Regulations.\tx:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\Active IPPC Sites.lyr  x:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\IPPC Sites.lyr   Archived Dataset: Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\physical\\hydrology\\environment_agency\\industrial_sites\\ipc_sites.TAB\tGIS Service Desk Data Custodian: Susan Freeman\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17484\tNRW_DS100779\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.536Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRAS Sites\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373739\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Environment Agency Wales and licensed to CCW for internal use only.  The dataset has been supplied by the Environment Agency Wales and contains Sites under Radioactive Substance control (point data)\t\tPlease contact the Environment Agency\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\physical\\hydrology\\environment_agency\\industrial_sites\\ras_authorisations_sites.TAB\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:23.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17483\tNRW_DS100786\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.315Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) - River Biology Spatial Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373836\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Environment Agency Wales and licensed to NRW for internal use only. The dataset has been supplied by the Environment Agency Wales and contains  River quality biology. GQA covering England \u0026amp; Wales displayed in varying thickness according to flow and colour according to Biology (year 2000).\t\t\tx:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\GQA Biology Network.lyr  X:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\GQA Biology Sample Points.lyr\tPlease contact Nick Davies ( Environment Agency)\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:49:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17482\tNRW_DS100791\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.291Z\t697\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSquirrel Survey of Aberhirnant Forest and Woodland surrounding Lake Vyrnwy (1999 - 2000)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030373931\tThis dataset is made up of presence or absence records for red and grey squirrels in Aberhirnant Forest and the woodlands surrounding Lake Vyrnwy.   The purpose of this data capture was to report on the presence, distribution and likely status of red squirrels in Aberhirnant Forest and the Lake Vyrnwy area.  The data is will be used as a basis to assess whether red squirrels have a future in this area, that is, sufficient potential to justify further monitoring and sympathetic forest management. The target sites included conifer plantations of Aberhirnant Forest and the catchment area of Lake Vyrnwy.\tThis original survey was carried our between 1999 and 2000.  A follow up survey was commissioned by CCW in 2001.  An initial survey of 17 grids of hair tubes were set out in Aberhirnant Forest and 2 in the Lake Vyrnwy Plantation in 1999.  Each grid consisted of 20 hair tubes in four rows each of 5 tubes.  Each row was 100 m apart and the tubes were 100 m apart in the rows.  Tubes were fixed to trees and baited.  These were then checked every two weeks and hairs deposited in the tubes were checked by eye and verified under a microscope using the negative staining technique where necessary. Grid references have been estimated from a map.  A total of 1290 records were taken, of which 45 were positive.\tFurther information is available in the report \"Red Squirrel Conservation: Report on the status of red and grey squirrels in the Aberhirnant Forest and woodlands surrounding Lake Vyrnwy FCRA Project 241 squirrel management in Woodlands.\"\t[A] Species data held in Recorder 6\tLiz Hallewell - Mammal Ecologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17481\tNRW_DS100811\t2024-05-28T12:24:44.219Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Discharge ( Revoked )\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030383131\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons.    The dataset has been supplied by the NRW and contains Revoked Water Discharge Consent Licenses point data information.  This is likely to be a duplicate of \"WIMS Revoked Consents - Aug 2016.lyr\" (NRW_DS123341) as the dataset WIMS Revoked Consents.LYR no longer exists on the NRW X drive.\t\tPlease contact Nick Richardson Environment Agency\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Water Abstractions \u0026amp; Discharges\\WIMS Revoked Consents.lyr\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:01:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17528\tNRW_DS100821\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.247Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWoodland Grant Scheme 1\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030383231\tThe Woodland Grant Scheme (WGS) provides incentives for people to create and manage woodlands on sites all over Wales. WGS1 operated between April 1989 and April 1992. This is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects in the form of polygons. The dataset identifies areas approved by grant type within each WGS Scheme. The list below provides a summary of grant type captured: SCHEME BOUNDARY -The boundary of the area(s) included within the scheme. NEW PLANTING APPROVED - Areas within the contract where new planting has been approved under NP, NB, NS, NC, NK, NQ and/or NU. NEW NATURAL REGENERATION APPROVED - Areas within the contract where new natural regeneration has been approved under NN, NL and/or NZ. REPLANTING/REGENERATION/MANAGEMENT - Areas within the contract where replanting, restocking by natural regeneration or management has been approved under RP, RN, RC RG, RQ, EN, MG and/or SM. COMMUNITY WOODLAND CONTRIBUTION - Areas within the contract approved for Community Woodland Contribution. Abbreviations used above: NP New planting, NB New planting with better land supplement, NS New planting accepted into the 1988 set-aside scheme, NC New planting of coppice, NK New planting of coppice with better land supplement, NQ New planting short rotation coppice, NU New planting short rotation coppice with better land supplement, NN New planting by natural regeneration, NL New planting by natural regeneration with better land supp, NZ New planting by natural regeneration with set-aside, RP Restocking by replanting, RC Restocking with coppice (planting not regrowth), RQ Restocking with short rotation coppice (planting not regrowth), RN Restocking by natural regeneration, EN Restocking by existing natural regeneration, MG Management grant, SM Special management grant. This is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects in the form of polygons.   Purpose of data capture: The scheme provided incentives for people to manage woodlands on sites all over Great Britain.  Temporal coverage: June 1988 to June1991.\tThe spatial element of this dataset has been digitised by CGIS, and former FCW using both Laserscan\u0027s LAMPS and ESRI\u0027s Arcview software.  Information is mapped following final approved stage from contract maps supplied by the applicants (normally based on 1:2500 or 1:10,000 OS maps). Spatial data has been digitised against OS raster backdrops using 1:25,000 scale between 1997 and 2000 and 1:10,000 scale between 2000 and 2004\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 1.lyr\tKeith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1992-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:05:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17536\tNRW_DS100822\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.373Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWoodland Grant Scheme 2\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030383232\tThe Woodland Grant Scheme (WGS) provides incentives for people to create and manage woodlands on sites all over Wales. WGS2 operated between April 1992 and September 1994. This is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects in the form of polygonsThe dataset identifies areas approved by grant type within each WGS Scheme. The list below provides a summary of grant type captured: SCHEME BOUNDARY -The boundary of the area(s) included within the scheme. NEW PLANTING APPROVED - Areas within the contract where new planting has been approved under NP, NB, NS, NC, NK, NQ and/or NU. NEW NATURAL REGENERATION APPROVED - Areas within the contract where new natural regeneration has been approved under NN, NL and/or NZ. REPLANTING/REGENERATION/MANAGEMENT - Areas within the contract where replanting, restocking by natural regeneration or management has been approved under RP, RN, RC RG, RQ, EN, MG and/or SM. COMMUNITY WOODLAND CONTRIBUTION - Areas within the contract approved for Community Woodland Contribution. Abbreviations used above: NP New planting, NB New planting with better land supplement, NS New planting accepted into the 1988 set-aside scheme, NC New planting of coppice, NK New planting of coppice with better land supplement, NQ New planting short rotation coppice, NU New planting short rotation coppice with better land supplement, NN New planting by natural regeneration, NL New planting by natural regeneration with better land supp, NZ New planting by natural regeneration with set-aside, RP Restocking by replanting, RC Restocking with coppice (planting not regrowth), RQ Restocking with short rotation coppice (planting not regrowth), RN Restocking by natural regeneration, EN Restocking by existing natural regeneration, MG Management grant, SM Special management grant.\tThe spatial element of this dataset has been digitised by CGIS, former FCW using both Laserscan\u0027s LAMPS and ESRI\u0027s Arcview software.  The textual element has been extracted from the WGS Oracle database using Business Objects and associated with the polygon data.  Information is mapped following final approved stage from contract maps supplied by the applicants (normally based on 1:2500 or 1:10,000 OS maps).  Spatial data has been digitised against OS raster backdrops using 1:25,000 scale between 1997 and 2000, and 1:10,000 scale between 2000 and 2004.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 2.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Services Team ..........Keith Harri Jones\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1994-09-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17535\tNRW_DS100829\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.363Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWoodland Grant Scheme 3\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313030383239\tThe Woodland Grant Scheme (WGS) provides incentives for people to create and manage woodlands on sites all over Wales. The dataset identifies areas approved by grant type within each WGS Scheme. The list below provides a summary of grant type captured:  - Scheme Boundary: The boundary of the area(s) included within the scheme.  - New Planting Approved: Areas within the contract where new planting has been approved under New planting (NP), New planting with better land supplement (NB), New planting accepted into the 1988 set-aside scheme (NS), New planting of coppice (NC), New planting of coppice with better land supplement (NK), New planting short rotation coppice (NQ) and/or New planting short rotation coppice with better land supplement (NU).  - New Natural Regeneration Approved: Areas within the contract where new natural regeneration has been approved under New planting by natural regeneration (NN), New planting by natural regeneration with better land sup (NL) and/or New planting by natural regeneration with set-aside (NZ).  - Replanting/Regeneration/Management: Areas within the contract where replanting, restocking by natural regeneration or management has been approved under Restocking by replanting (RP), Restocking by natural regeneration (RN), Restocking with coppice (planting not regrowth) (RC), RG, Restocking with short rotation coppice (planting not regrowth) (RQ), Restocking by existing natural regeneration (EN), Management grant (MG) and/or Special management grant (SM).  - Community Woodland Contribution - Areas within the contract approved for Community Woodland Contribution.\tThe WGS information was captured using approved contract maps provided by former FC Area offices in Wales. It was captured against 1:25,000 and 1:10,000 raster images using either a digitising table or heads up digitising\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 3.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17538\tNRW_DS101725\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.436Z\t496\tValid\tEditable\tAge and Growth Analysis of Native Oyster Beds (Ostrea edulis) in Wales\tDadansoddiad o oed a thwf gwelyau wystrys (Ostrea edulis) yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031373235\tThe native flat oyster Ostrea edulis is a UK and Welsh Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species, for which a UK action plan exists. Native oysters have a long history of over-exploitation in Wales and elsewhere in the UK. Harvested beds in Wales used to be present off Porthcawl, Swansea Bay and Mumbles, Milford Haven, Cardigan, Pwllheli and the Menai Strait. All of these fisheries declined during the early 1900\u0027s due to intense exploitation, possibly combined with disease and poor recruitment. Significant oyster beds are now restricted to Milford Haven, Swansea Bay and Porthcawl, although at a much reduced scale. The remaining oyster populations in Wales are under threat through increased fishery effort in the existing oyster fishery and from diversification of fishing activities in the fisheries. Dredging activity used in harvesting sublittoral oysters causes physical damage to the seabed and associated marine biodiversity. Oyster beds in Milford Haven occur in the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and form part of the features for which the SAC is designated.    The present work reports on the morphometric characteristics, the age structure, growth and settlement of Ostrea edulis populations from seven sites in Milford Haven in Pembrokeshire (Wales, UK). Information gained from the project will contribute to an ecological understanding of native oyster beds and provide management advice for the remaining native oyster beds in Wales.\tSamples of Ostrea edulis were collected from eight sites in Milford Haven in Pembrokeshire, South Wales (UK) during September 2002. No oysters were found during the survey in Swansea and Porthcawl, South Wales (U.K) (Thomas, 2003). The sites 1a, 1b, 2, 4 and 6 were located in the middle section of the Milford Haven waterway from a non-commercially exploited oyster area, whereas sites 7, 9 and 10 were further upstream and are from a commercially exploited oyster area. Laboratory examination of Oysters recorded shell length (L), width (W) and height (H) and dry weight of flesh calculated in order to perform morphometric analysis.\t\tArchived at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon as Project No. 77, Media 104  (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 101to200\\Media 104) and also copies are held at Pembroke Dock.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2003-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:37:09.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17526\tNRW_DS101779\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.238Z\t591\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Survey of Native Oyster Beds (Ostrea edulis) in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031373739\tThe native flat oyster Ostrea edulis is a UK and Welsh Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species, for which a UK action plan exists. Native oysters have a long history of over-exploitation in Wales and elsewhere in the UK. Oyster fisheries around Wales declined during the early 1900\u0027s due to intense exploitation, possibly combined with disease and poor recruitment. Significant oyster beds are now restricted to only a few locations. The remaining oyster population in Wales are under threat through increased fishery effort in the existing oyster fishery and from diversification of fishing activities in the fisheries.  The purpose of this data capture was to gather data on the distribution and abundance of oysters in locations known to currently support native oyster beds; and, to collect samples of oysters for age analysis and to gather data on the marine biodiversity associated with oyster beds. This dataset also helps towards a further understanding of the ecology of native oysters as well as informing management decisions (part of the UK and Welsh Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP).\tA total of ten sites were visited between 13th and 18th September 2002.  Of these ten sites, seven were located within Milford Haven, one off Porthcawl, and two sites within Swansea Bay. A differential GPS was used to ensure the positional accuracy of each site (at least 5 m). Abundance and distribution of O. edulis was recorded at each site by a pair of divers using a pre-laid transect line up to 50 m in length and a 2 m survey corridor. Each diver surveyed one side of the transect line with oyster counts taken at 10 m intervals. At each 10 m interval the total number of oysters was recorded onto slates and physical data including substrate composition was also recorded. Oysters along survey corridors were collected by a pair of divers. The oysters were placed into hessian sacks which were labelled with total number of oysters, date, site, transect number and distance along transect travelled. The samples were frozen on return to shore and couriered to the University of Wales Bangor for age analysis, see separate metadata entry \u0027Age and growth analysis of native oyster beds (Ostrea edulis) in Wales\u0027. JNCC Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) Phase II methodology was used to evaluate the marine biodiversity associated with native oyster beds. The divers kept a particular look out for any additional UK priority Biodiversity Action Plan species or other rarities, also, any oysters missed during the initial transect survey were collected with a note made of what distance along the transect line they were found. Still photography was undertaken at each site where visibility allowed.  All Diving Operations were run in accordance with the Approved Code of Practice for Scientific and Archaeological Diving Projects and Emu Ltd\u0027s. In-house Method Statement 18 - Operating Procedures and Rules for Scientific Diving within Emu Ltd.\t\t[A] Data is archived as Project No. 285, media no\u0027s 104,111 and 112 (File paths: K:\\Archive\\Media 101-200\\Media 104 etc.) [B] Marine Recorder Survey (MRCCW30000000032)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-03T17:51:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17540\tNRW_DS101780\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.730Z\t283\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA study of the Milford Haven maerl bed (2005)\tAstudiaeth ar wely marl Aberdaugleddau yn 2005\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031373830\tMaerl beds are a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitat and are also included in the biodiversity duty of public bodies introduced by the NERC Act 2006. Maerl is a collective term for several species of red seaweed, with hard, chalky skeletons. It is rock hard and, unlike other seaweeds, it grows as unattached rounded nodules or short, branched shapes on the seabed. Maerl beds are an important habitat for many different types of marine life, which live amongst or are attached to the surface of Maerl, or burrow in the coarse gravel of dead maerl beneath the top living layer. Maerl beds can be of importance to sustainable fisheries, providing nursery grounds for commercial species of fish and shellfish. In Milford Haven the maerl species Phymatolithon calcareum forms the only living maerl bed in Wales. P. calcareum is listed in the EC Habitats Directive Annex Vb. The aim of this project was to collate high quality images of the Milford Haven maerl bed to create a base line for long term monitoring for Biodiversity Action Plan reporting. There is high confidence in this dataset as the surveyors are amongst the most experienced marine biologists in the UK.\tThe first phase of the fieldwork was to map the extent of the maerl bed in Milford Haven and to determine the distribution and abundance of live maerl using drop down video. The second phase of the fieldwork was detailed study at six sites by divers who recorded the frequency of occurrence of macrobiota in a series of quadrats. At four of the sites, core samples were collected for infaunal and sediment particle size analysis. The present study follows on from work completed in May 2004 (RPS, 2004). RPS carried out an exploratory survey of the maerl bed in Milford Haven waterway in the vicinity of Herbrandston jetty on behalf of South Hook Liquid Natural Gas Terminal Company Ltd (South Hook LNG). This was the most comprehensive work on this maerl bed to date but the survey was partial and significant data gaps remained. The present study was designed to use a similar methodology to that used by RPS and to build on the existing data collected to give a better picture of the extent and quality of the maerl within the Milford Haven waterway.\t\tData is archived under project no.145, media no.\u0027s 471-478 Paper MNCR forms stored in off-site storage (Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Biodiversity Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2007-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T10:28:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17521\tNRW_DS101782\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.140Z\t1103\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Wales\tArolwg Coetiroedd Cymru Cam 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031373832\tThe primary aim of the project was to complete woodland community descriptions of all Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSls) in Wales that have semi-natural woodland as a qualifying feature. There was also a need for some survey of woodland on SSSls where woodland is not the outright qualifying feature and some areas of non-SSSI woodland (primarily pSSSI and areas of woodland adjacent to existing SSSls). Sites and areas of woodland to be included in the survey were selected by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). National Vegetation Classification (NVC) field survey methodology followed procedures.    This dataset contains detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 800 woodlands of conservation interest in Wales, predominantly SSSI and pSSSI woodlands.   The purpose of this data capture was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities, to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland SSSI series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites. Phase 2 woodland surveys have been carried out periodically since the late 1980s but a concentrated period of survey on SSSI and pSSSI woodland occurred from 1995 and 2000.  Data was collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by CCW Knowledge Management Group, corrections made by contractors, validated again by CCW Knowledge Management Group, corrections made in-house in conjunction with Woodland Ecologist.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.-Prif nod y prosiect oedd cwblhau disgrifiadau o gymunedau coetir o bob Safle o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGA) yng Nghymru sydd â choetir lled-naturiol fel nodwedd gymhwysol. Roedd hefyd angen rhai arolygon o goetiroedd ar SoDdGAau lle nad coetir yw’r unig nodwedd gymhwysol, ynghyd â rhai ardaloedd o goetiroedd nad ydynt ar SoDdGA (yn bennaf SoDdGA arfaethedig ac ardaloedd o goetir ger SoDdGAau).  Mae’r set ddata hon yn cynnwys data manwl o arolygon Cam 2 o dros 800 o goetiroedd o ddiddordeb cadwraeth yng Nghymru, yn bennaf coetiroedd ar SoDdGAau a SoDdGAau arfaethedig. Pwrpas casglu’r data oedd gwella’r wybodaeth gyffredinol am gymunedau coetiroedd Cymru, darparu disgrifiadau cyson o gymunedau ar draws y gyfres o SoDdGAau coetir fel gwybodaeth sylfaenol ar gyfer cynnal safleoedd, ac asesu statws safleoedd newydd o ran bod yn SoDdGAau. Mae arolygon coetir Cam 2 wedi cael eu cynnal o bryd i’w gilydd ers diwedd yr 1980au ond cafwyd cyfnod dwys o arolygon ar goetiroedd ar SoDdGAau a SoDdGAau arfaethedig rhwng 1995 a 2000. Cafodd data ei gasglu gan bersonél hyfforddedig, ei ddigideiddio gan gontractwyr, ei ddilysu gan Grŵp Rheoli Gwybodaeth Cyngor Cefn Gwlad Cymru, ei gywiro gan gontractwyr, ei ddilysu eto gan Grŵp Rheoli Gwybodaeth Cyngor Cefn Gwlad Cymru, a’i gywiro’n fewnol ar y cyd ag Ecolegydd Coetiroedd.  Defnyddiwyd y fethodoleg safonol ar gyfer arolygon coetir y Dosbarthiad Llystyfiant Cenedlaethol (NVC) er mwyn casglu’r data hwn. Adnabuwyd cymunedau coetir â’r llygad yna’u mapio, a hynny wedi’i ategu gan gwadradau sampl a nodiadau targed. Mae’r set ddata yn gasgliad o arolygon NVC a gomisiynwyd gan Gyngor Cefn Gwlad Cymru.  Cydnabyddiaeth Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tStandard woodland NVC survey methodology was used to collect this data.  Woodland communities were identified by eye and mapped, supported by sample quadrats and target notes.  Dataset is a collation of CCW commissioned NVC surveys.   This dataset was also digitised and made available through Recorder 6. It is one of a series of major Phase 2 habitat surveys, including in-house and commissioned surveys covering lowland grassland, saltmarsh, lowland peatland and lowland heathland.\t\tSpecies data stored in Recorder 6 all surveys are tagged as being Phase 2 Woodland Survey. a)  Woodland Ecologist\u0027s G:drive  b)  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\EIT\\Staff\\Cyprian Payne\\2009\\P2only.zipc)  M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\phase_2\\Woodland    c)  Spatial data available to internal NRW staff on the corporate spatial data store.  GIS layers are stored on the X Drive: X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Woodland  d) copy of the scanned paper maps are available on legacy CCW M drive at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Woodland\\Raster Data  Species data is also stored in Recorder 6 under the Phase 2 Woodland Survey Tag.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas The species part of the dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2000-09-11-2024-01-19\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6158/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_ph2_wood\t2025-12-01T16:34:57.271Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17534\tNRW_DS101803\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.369Z\t654\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater Image Library\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383033\tThis is a catalogue of images sourced from both slides and digital images. Approximately 75 percent are CCW, 10 percent are Head of the Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group or Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader within NRW. Approximately 15 percent of the images are sources externally from volunteers or organisation - information about this is held within an excel document within the image catalogue. These images were captured for numerous purposes including, training, interpretation, monitoring, and stock images for publication.\tPhotography using a digital camera, and some slides.\t\tFreshwater Ecosystems Team Leader. N: and G: drive These images may have been transferred to the NRW Image Store\tA collection of freshwater images compiled and managed by Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader. If this data has been transferred to the image store the internal contact is Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1994-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:17:59.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17532\tNRW_DS101806\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.252Z\t271\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring of King Scallop Pecten maximus\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Monitro cregyn Aberffro Pecten maximus\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383036\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  The scallop population in the Reserve is of conservation value because of its small size and its remoteness from any substantial population.  Scallops were collected commercially and recreationally in the area of the Reserve until 1990.  In 1990 the South Wales Sea Fisheries Committee (SWSFC) introduced a byelaw prohibiting scallop collecting by any means. The population of scallops needs to be monitored in order to assess the effectiveness of this byelaw. Surveys have been carried out since 1979 looking at scallop density and size.  The purpose of this data capture was to estimate the density of scallops within suitable habitats in the Reserve and to assess the age structure in the population.\tThe 1979-82 and 1985 surveys used slightly different methods. In 2000 a standard method was developed suitable for volunteer diving teams and which could be repeated in subsequent surveys. Scallops are collected by divers from a two metre band, one metre either side of 50m transects (100m2). The scallops are brought to the surface, their growth rings measured, shells marked with notches for identification and then returned to the site.  This is repeated for several transects over several sites of suitable habitat.   Survey data is  published annually. Most recent: NRW Evidence report No.196 - Skomer MVZ Scallop Survey 2016.\t\tSkomer MNR, West Region Drive /shared documents/data/scallops\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:37:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17531\tNRW_DS101833\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.196Z\t737\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFluvial Audits of Welsh River Systems\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383333\tGeomorphological assessments that utilised historical information, contemporary field survey and scientific literature resources, have been undertaken for various Welsh river systems of high conservation importance including the Usk, Wye, Dee and Tywi. The purpose of these fluvial audits it to record the morphological features and to identify, and understand the geomorphological processes that influence and control the channel activity, and the morphological quality of the river systems.   Specific aims of each project include (but are not limited to): - Developing an understanding of the dynamic geomorphology of the key Welsh river systems. - Examining changes to the channel planform over time using historical maps and aerial photographs. - Investigating channel and catchment changes using historical records and consultation with Environment Agency (EA) staff, and to develop a time chart which identifies Potentially Destabilising Phenomena (PDP\u0027s). - Identifying the major geomorphological features and processes occurring by undertaking a field survey of present erosion, deposition, bank protection, flow types and sediment sources. - Identifying the key areas of channel instability (geomorphological activity) and determine the possible factors affecting this instability, in particular those contributing to any severe bank erosion evident. - Determining the presence and absence of geomorphological features associated with the SAC species habitats.\tA standard field methodology was used for the, fluvial audit, which consists of: i) a desk study to collate historical data sources which record changes in the catchment, both natural and anthropogenic, which may have disturbed the fluvial system (discharge or sediment supply), and had direct implications on the channel morphology (change in channel planform, long profile and cross sectional geometry) ii) a contemporary field survey which integrated the key components of both a Detailed Catchment Baseline Survey (DCBS) and Fluvial Audit (FA), and emphasised assessment of the controls and extent of erosion and deposition along the channel.  The surveys have investigated the channel morphology, in particular the presence of bank erosion on a reach-by-reach basis and artificial influences on the channel. This, together with historic analysis enables an assessment of the stability or dynamics of the river system over time and whether it is adjusted to the prevailing flow processes and sediment dynamics, or unstable and actively adjusting (eroding). The surveys have also related the presence or absence of morphological features to the condition parameters that are identified as habitat preferences for the Special Areas of Conservation species.\tSee the separate metadata entries Fluvial Audit of the Upper Usk Tributaries, River Dee, Afon Tywi, and the River Wye and tributaries listed below in the Contains Field.\tArchived as Project 257, Media 951:   See the separate metadata entries Fluvial Audit of the Upper Usk Tributaries (URI: 110182)  Fluvial Audit of the River Dee (110183)  Fluvial Audit of the Afon Tywi (URI: 111562)  Fluvial audit of the River Wye and tributaries (URI: 111860).   Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media901to1000\\Media951\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW Sue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, Knowledge Strategy and Planning, NRW (Formerly Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, CCW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2007-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-09-03T09:56:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17533\tNRW_DS101834\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.432Z\t531\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLlyn Tegid Hydroacoustic Surveys and Gwyniad Translocations 2004-2009\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383334\tThe gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) population of Llyn Tegid has been considered for a number of years to be threatened by deteriorating environmental conditions, especially those associated with eutrophication. This dataset contains the results of a project established in 2004 to carry out translocation, hydroacoustic surveys and measurement of environmental variables. The purpose of this data capture was to consider the implications for the status of gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) and its habitat in Llyn Tegid. The objectives of the project include both conservation actions and further research, including the establishment of a refuge population in Llyn Arenig Fawr. Surveys were carried out annually from 2004 - 2009.  Following a first unsuccessful translocation attempt in 2003, three successful translocations have taken place- in early 2005, 2006 and 2007. A full fish population survey of the recipient site of Llyn Arenig Fawr was undertaken in 2009.\tHydroacoustic surveys and measurements of environmental variables- temperature, oxygen profiles and secchi disk readings- have been carried out annually since 2004. (No secchi disk readings for 2008 survey). In 2005 an analysis of historic lake data in the context of gwyniad spawning requirements was also undertaken.  The methodology technique used standardised survey and monitoring protocol for the assessment of whitefish (Bean, 2003, JNCC, 2005) with two minor deviations- rather than using the target strength to fish length relationship of Foote (1987) the relationship given by Love (1971) was used to allow wider comparison. Secondly a vessel speed of 2.0ms-1 was adopted as general target mean speed rather then an absolute upper limit.  Translocation attempts involved seine netting conducted after dark, stripping was performed on the morning after sampling and the volume of eggs was determined. Viable gwyniad were returned alive to the lake. Eggs were transported to Llyn Arenig Fawr and introduced to incubation boxes. Fish population survey at the latter site was undertaken using hydroacoustics and gill netting.\tRivers Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW Data collected under project number FC 73-02-304. The species records which form part of this dataset are available to download from the NBN Gateway as part of a larger packaged species dataset.\t[A]  Data is Archived under Project Number: 29, 154, 194 ,226, 227, 200 Media Numbers 169, 468, 539, 595, 232, 952   [B]  Species Data is also stored in Recorder 6: CCW000800000000D Gwyniad Hydroacoustic and Gill netting survey of Llyn Arenig Fawr 2009 - CCW Science Report 904   [C]  Hardcopies are archived in CCW HQ fireproof safe: stack 05.\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17537\tNRW_DS101837\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.442Z\t735\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLlyn Tegid automatic monitoring station\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383337\tYear-to-year changes in the weather have a major effect on the water quality in many freshwater lakes. For example, periods of heavy rain transport more nutrients from the surrounding land while periods of warm, calm weather during the summer promote the growth of cyanobacteria. Llyn Tegid, in Snowdonia, is a deep lake that periodically develops short-lived blooms of cyanobacteria. An automatic station was installed in Llyn Tegid in October 2006 to monitor its response to extreme variations in the weather. This station is fitted with a range of meteorological sensors, a chain of water temperature sensors to record the mixing characteristics of the lake and optical sensors to monitor the spatial distribution of the algae. This dataset includes raw data on parameters recorded by the station at hourly intervals such as conductivity, barometric pressure, mean chlorophyll a, temperature at varying depths, wind speed and direction, solar radiation etc. The reports include an analysis of this data and a review of technical performance of the station. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the response of Llyn Tegid to variations in the weather and support more detailed studies on the climatic sensitivity of the lake.\tA Lake Dynamics Monitoring Station (LDMS) was installed. The system is based on two components: the Buoy Station deployed on the lake and the Remote Station used to download the acquired data to a remote site. Data is supplied to NRW on an annual basis.\t\tStored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon on optical media.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage.  Archived as Project 195 (Media 540, 541, 596, 953 and 1212),  project 347 (media1324), project 446 (media1487), project 462 (media 1523)\tInternal Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSnowdonia National Park Authority-University of Wales, Aberystwyth-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:42.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17539\tNRW_DS101839\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.684Z\t736\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGeological Conservation Review (GCR) Site Management Reports\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383339\tMulti-page reports produced for each Geological Conservation Review (GCR) site. The reports contain geological and conservation information, comprehensive site photography and local information. The Geological Conservation Review looked at identifying geological sites across the UK, that make up a unique contribution to Britain’s Earth Heritage. The Geological Conservation Review started in 1997 when a more systematic approach to site selection occurred. The objective of the review was to identify those sites needed to show all the key scientific elements of the Earth heritage of Britain.\tSite selection was undertaken between 1977 and 1990 by the Nature Conservancy Council. It covered both the geology and geomorphology of Britain and involved several hundred scientists from high education, government, industry and the voluntary sector. Reports are produced by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Earth Scientists and approximately 350 have been completed out of 450 Welsh GCR sites. Arguably the most comprehensive geological site information CCW holds.\t\tThis data is stored on the DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Geodiversity/gcr/Data%20%20Statistics/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Geodiversity/gcr/SitePages/Home.aspx  reports are held here: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/kdim/rl/CCW%20GCR%20Site%20Management%20Reports/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx\tRaymond Roberts, North Region Earth Science Officer, CCW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:23.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17525\tNRW_DS101840\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.195Z\t521\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring of Nudibranch Species\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Monitro rhywogaethau gwlithod mor\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383430\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Nudibranchs are predators on a wide range of epibenthic species. Most are seasonal and reliant on their prey for food, shelter and a place to lay their eggs. Being near the top of the food chain they can act as an indicator of the health of the communities they rely on.  Nudibranchs are a feature of the Skomer marine Nature Reserve (MNR) for which species diversity and the presence of reare or scarce species are attributes used to assess conservation status.  As top predators they can act as an indicator of the health of the communities they rely on.   Between 1975 and 1991 surveys of nudibranch diversity in the Skomer MNR were conducted as a series of identification courses/workshops at Dale Fort Field Centre and these showed species diversity to be high.  61 species of nudibranchs were recorded (Bunker Picton and Morrow 1993).  The Skomer MNR team complete nudibranch species surveys every four years, these have been completed in 2002, 2006 and 2010. The Skomer MNRs nudibranch population have been identified as being rich and diverse with over 62 species including one nationally rare species and four nationally scarce species. The purposes for this data capture were to: - to produce a species list for the entire Reserve on a 4 yearly basis.  - to record a minimum of 16 common species annually in the Skomer MNR from a check list.\tSurveys are done in spring and late summer to account for variances in species life cycles. Sites from around the whole Reserve are chosen to provide a range of habitat types. The sublittoral habitat found at each site is described briefly and associated nudibranch species recorded as a list for each site. In addition, an overall list of species is compiled for the Skomer MNR and species recorded photographically.\t\t[A] Skomer MNR, West Region Drive /shared documents/data/biological data/nudibranch ,   [B] Species data in Marine Recorder (survey numbers MRCCW30000000052,MRCCW30000000055, MRCCW3000000005C)  NB this data set appears to have been extended to include nudibranch data from 2014 and 2018. There are surveys in Marine Recorder. Metadata may need updating to reflect this.\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-06-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:38:25.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17524\tNRW_DS101841\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.235Z\t323\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Echinus esculentus and other echinoderm species surveys\tEchinus esculantus ac echinodermau eraill yn arolwg GNF Ynys Sgomer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383431\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ).  The purpose of this data capture was to assess the abundance of an important habitat grazer. The dataset includes data related to the abundance data for common sea urchin Echinus esculantus, Spiny starfish Martasterias glacialis, Seven armed starfish Luidia ciliaris and Common sunstar Crossaster papposus.  The data also contains the density of each species and depth band at which recorded and the presence of \\bald urchin disease\\\".  From 2007 the data includes a qualitative assessment of the larvae in plankton samples.\"\tDiver swum transects (30 m) at 4 depth bands (5, 10, 15 and 20 m) at 6 sites around the Reserve. Counts of individuals made for all species. Urchins were measured - Max diameter (mm).\t\tSkomer MNR, West Region Drive /shared documents/data/biological data/urchins.   This dataset is updated 4-yearly.  2003 data also available in Marine Recorder survey number MRCCW16800000005.\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:39:07.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17530\tNRW_DS101842\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.208Z\t310\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Condition Monitoring of Eunicella verrucosa\tCyflwr Eunicella verrucosa - GNF Ynys Sgomer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383432\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Eunicella verrucosa is a feature of the marine nature reserve, it is at the edge of it\u0027s distribution range and is a potential indicator of climate change. Eunicella verrucosa colonies around the Skomer MNR have been photographed and assessed for condition since 1992. Colonies are revisited every year and assessed for: Presence, damage, epi-foulants, entanglement, growth, necrotic tissue and the presence of Tritonia nilsodherni and Simnia patula. Eunicella verrucosa is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species and information is used for BAP reporting.\tPhotographic and field records were taken.\t\tSkomer MNR office, V drive/ shared documents/ data/ biological/seafans.    Data also available in HQ Marine Recorder (survey number MRCCW16800000004)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:39:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17522\tNRW_DS101843\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.131Z\t614\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring of Red Sea Fingers Alcyonium glomeratum\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Monitro bysedd cochion Alcyonium glomeratum\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383433\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the red sea fingers Alcyonium glomeratum colony area and to look for damage and disease. Alcyonium glomeratum (red sea fingers) is a Lusitanian species near to its northern limit of distribution. Colonies are long-lived and possible indicators of climate change. Several colonies have been monitored since 1982. Some colonies are very stable over time and others are variable.\tNorth Wall Stereo: three quadrats (50 x 40 cm) are photographed using stereo photography. At all other sites photographs (mono) are taken using a 50 x 70 cm framer.  Each site follows either a sequence of photos or transects that are prescribed in site relocation proforma.\t\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/alglom ,  Images - Dell1590 C/docs and settings/Monitoring/alglom\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:40:32.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17529\tNRW_DS101844\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.248Z\t508\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Territorial Fish Species Monitoring\tMonitro pysgod tiriogaethol - GNF Ynys Sgomer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383434\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Monitoring of territorial fish is part of an on-going survey was carried out in the Skomer Marine Conservation Zone using volunteer divers to assess the abundance of territorial fish species and the habitat characteristics of these populated areas.  The survey methods were developed through trials in 1997 and the first survey was completed in 2001 this was affected by heavy plankton bloom and therefore repeat survey was completed in 2002.  In 2005 survey reviewed the methods in order to allow improved statistical analysis but allowed comparison of the 2002 data.  The methods were also developed to be suitable for volunteer dive teams.  Seabed and Habitat data were collected for each site using Seasearch methods to support the fish data. The fish data entered is an average for two habitats (10m and 15m) and represents the number of fish per 90 m2.  In 2009 Habitat Descriptions were created using Phase 1 SeaSearch habitat description methods. These surveys were done by NRW and volunteers as in previous year. 2013 data is currently held by the Skomer Island Marine Team (Assistant Marine Conservation Officer, Pembrokeshire Team) and is to be entered into Marine Recorder.\tVisual counts of a fish along a swum transect, distance- 30-50m. Only species from a fixed list are recorded (Labrus bergylta, Labrus mixtus, Ctenolabrus rupestris, Ctenolabrus exoletus, Crenilabrus melops, Thorogobius epphipiatus, Parablennius gattorugine, Pholis gunnellus, Myoxcephualus scorpius and Taurulus bubalis). The transects are swum at various sites around the Reserve and at 5 m depth bands - 5, 10 15 20 and 25 m. BCD Drop down video surveillance has also been used. - 1997, 2001, 2002, 2005, 2013 - abundance data - 2007 - drop down video data There is medium confidence in this dataset as volunteers used to record the data therefore species ID may vary.\t\tSkomer MNR office, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological/FISH.     Dataset is updated every 5 years  2005 and 2009 data also held in Marine Recorder (surveys MRCCW16800000002, MRMCS00500000036)  Data for 2013 held by Assistant Marine Conservation Officer, Pembrokeshire Team.\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2007-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:41:12.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17523\tNRW_DS101852\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.162Z\t301\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring of Parazoanthus axinellae\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Monitro Parazoanthus axinellae\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383532\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. The population of Parazoanthus axinellae (yellow trumpet anemone) is an important feature of Skomer MNR.  P. axinellae is a Lusitanian (south-western) species near to the edge of its range and may act as an indicator of climatic change. Colonies have been photographed from fixed locations since 2001 and colony growth has been monitored. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor P. axinellae colonies for changes in polyp density and colony area.\tDensity estimates: Close-up photographs are taken using a digital camera. The digital camera is mounted on a 20 x 20 cm framer. The number of P. axinellae polyps are counted in each quadrat. Area of the colony: A series of transects are placed through the colonies. Photographs are taken using a 50 x 70cm framer. In 2008 a digital SLR camera replaced the film camera providing high quality images allowing improved photo analysis.  The images are analysed by overlaying a 5 x 5cm grid and recording presence/absence of P. axinellae within the grid squares.\t\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/parazoo\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Internal Custodian: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:41:52.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17527\tNRW_DS101853\t2024-05-28T12:25:26.238Z\t279\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Ross Coral Pentapora foliacea\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Monitro cwrelau rhosliw Pentapora foliacae\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383533\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Colonies of the bryozoan Pentapora foliacea are fragile structures thought to be moderately slow growing, and long lived. They are important microhabitats for mobile species and are regarded as useful indicators of anthropogenic activity such as mobile fishing gear and anchoring. Stereo photographs of colonies have been taken from 1984. Image analysis methods have been tested and currently a morphological class system is used to classify and monitor the colonies. Alongside the basic monitoring various research projects are in progress looking at; the genetics of the species, Anti-microbial zone assays and palaeoclimate using two different techniques: Variation in zooid size and isotopic signature of the carbonate skeleton. The purpose of this data capture was to: - monitor the numbers and growth rate of Ross coral colonies.   - monitor the amount of damage occurring to the colonies.\tPhotographs are taken using a single or stereo camera set up on a frame 50 x 70 cm. Photographs of P. foliacea are taken along marked transects at each site.\t\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/pentapora ,  Images - dell1590 C/doc \u0026amp; settings/MONITORING/pentapora   CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000057)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:42:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17519\tNRW_DS101856\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.701Z\t579\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Subtidal Algal Assemblages\tCasgliadau o algau islanwol - GNF Ynys Sgomer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383536\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. Skomer MNR\u0027s algal communities have been identified as being rich and diverse with 241 species of red, green and brown algae recorded. This represents 34% of the British marine flora and 21% of North Atlantic marine flora including two nationally scarce species, five near their limit of distribution, four species with specialised habitat preferences and five deep water algal species. Skomer MNR has been identified as a Criteria B European IPA (important plant area) for marine algae. The area has a wide range of habitats, including excellent examples of algal communities on bedrock, boulders and cobbles. It has a very high diversity of algae with over 240 species recorded. Rare or threatened species include: Atractophora hypnoides, Sphacelaria mirabilis, Hydrolithon cruciatum and Hinksia ovata. In situ recording and collection is used to survey the algae at various habitats around the Reserve. A herbarium is kept for all notable species found. Methods have varied from year to year. The whole project was reviewed in 2007. There were numerous aims for this project and data capture.  1. To monitor temporal change in algal communities; 2. To monitor algal species of recognised importance; 3. To investigate species diversity using taxonomic distinctness analysis; 4. To establish a monitoring protocol for the future that is both achievable and worthwhile.\tIn situ ID Species lists with ACFOR abundance for 1983, and 1999 Species lists for 2005 Species lists and counts of Laminaria hyperborea and Delesseria sanguinea 2007 Lab ID of collected specimens - pressed and put in herbarium\t\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/algae.    2005 Data also available in HQ Marine Recorder (Survey number MRCCW16800000008)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2007-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:43:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17520\tNRW_DS101857\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.745Z\t373\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring of sponge assemblages\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Monitro casgliadau o sbyngau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383537\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  The sponge communities at Skomer MNR have been identified as rich and diverse with over 80 species. Six are nationally scarce species and eight near the limit of their distribution. Sponges are filter feeders and therefore reliant on water quality which makes them susceptible to changes in sediment deposition. They are therefore useful biotic indicators of changes in suspended sediment and surface sedimentation rates, the cause of which might include dredge spoil dumping. The purpose of this data capture was to: - monitor the sponge assemblages in the MNR;  - identify natural and anthropogenic fluctuations,  - identify the presence of rare, scarce and edge of range species in the MNR.\tTransects: Four fixed transects are located at Thorn Rock. Photographs are taken from fixed positions along the transect using a stereo camera set up on a 50 x 70cm frame. The slides are analysed using a stereo viewer to count the abundance of sponge species and morphology types. Species survey:  In 2003 all sponge species identified in 16, 50 x 70cm quadrats. In 2007 no quadrats were used, survey completed in the general vicinity of the 4 transects with all species being identified if possible. Species photographs were taken in the field and samples taken, where necessary, for spicule preparations.  Seasonal survey from fixed quadrats: In 2005 15 1m2 quadrats were marked out at 3 sites. The quadrats each consist of 25 cells (20 X 20cm), which are photographed using a digital camera.\t\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/sponges ,  Digital images - Dell1590 C/Documents and settings/monitoring/sponges ,   Species data held in Marine Recorder Surveys (MRCCW30900000005 / MRCCW16600000002)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:43:41.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17515\tNRW_DS101860\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.331Z\t404\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Sediment infauna community data\tCymuned isfilod gwaddod GNF Sgomer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383630\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Species abundance data from grab samples within Skomer MNR from 1993 onwards. Complimented by sediment grain size analysis and various oil content measures. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the sediment infauna community within Skomer Marine Nature Reserve.\tSediment samples were taken consisting of: 10 sites - 2 replicates using a 0.1m2 day grab sieved with a 0.5mm sieve.  3rd grab taken for sediment analysis. Ongoing sampling every 4/5 years. The 1993, 1996 and 1998 data contributed to the Pembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas, see separate metadata entry. Additional sampling in 1996 due to Sea Empress oil spill.\t\tSkomer MNR. West Region drive shared document/data/biological/sediment/infauna.     Data updated every 4 years    Data is stored in NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (survey numbers MRCCW10000000058, MRCCW10000000080, MRCCW10000000095, MRCCW30900000002, MRCCW30900000003, MRCCW3000000005F)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-11-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:44:24.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17518\tNRW_DS101862\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.549Z\t545\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Sediment Surface Epifauna Community Surveys\tArfilod arwyneb y gwaddodion - GNF Ynys Sgomer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383632\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  The aim of this data capture was to describe and monitor the sediment surface community within the Skomer Marine Reserve. The project was an investigation into the sediment surface communities from two sites at Skomer MNR.\tSurveys were undertaken during 1994, 2001 and 2004. Survey methodology used diver swum video transects utilising 0.25m2 quadrats. At each site samples were collected from in-situ quadrats and from 0.1m2 suction samples of the top 10 cm of sediment. All species present were recorded and counted. In 2004 CCW re-laid the transects at the two sites and contracted Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring to re-survey the infauna and epifauna using the same in-situ quadrat and suction sampling methodologies as the 2001 survey. Each site consists of a triangular arrangement of leaded ground lines anchored at each corner by various heavy objects. North of the Neck the legs of the triangle are approximately 25m long and at the Low Point site they are approximately 30m long.  Quadrat surveys were carried out with the aim of recording and quantifying the numbers of all conspicuous species of surface fauna and flora.  Recording was carried out in 0.5m x 0.5m (0.25m2) wire quadrats; each quadrat subdivided with strings at 10cm intervals to give 25 x 0.01m2 squares and the quadrats were placed at random positions along the transects. The divers then recorded the abundance (counts of mobile and solitary species, counts of stones colonised by colonial species, percentage cover of foliose algae) of all species seen.Samples of sediment were taken by diver operated suction sampler (c.f. Rostron, 2001) at 5 random locations along the inside of each transect lines. Random positions were determined in advance using a random number generator to give a distance (in metres) along the transect line. The samples were taken from within a 0.1m2 stainless steel box corer that was pushed down into the sediment. Samples were taken to a depth of 20cm below the sediment surface. A sample of the sediment was collected in a plastic pot for granulometric analysis at a random position close to one of the suction samples on each transect.\t\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/sediment/ epifauna.    2001 data also available in Marine Recorder (MRCCW16800000009).   2004 data is also available in Marine Recorder (MRCCW30900000004).\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Master Reference data team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:45:10.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17516\tNRW_DS101864\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.485Z\t561\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Littoral communities Meso scale\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Monitro cymunedau arfordirol Meso scale\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383634\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. Investigation into the intertidal littoral communities around Skomer MNR.  The purpose of this data capture was assess the \"health\" and changes occurring in the intertidal communities and investigating climate change effects.\tThe intertidal communities are surveyed annually using fixed and random quadrats. Fixed quadrats use cell frequency counts to record the community as a whole. All species are recorded along with specific methods for: Limpets, Barnacles, Lichens, cling fish and Trochids.  Random 5cm quadrats are used to record barnacle species ratios. Random 20cm quadrats to record barnacle % cover and limpet abundance. Random 50cm quadrats to record limpet species ratios. Middle shore limpets are measured. Timed searches for Osilinus lineata, Littorina saxatilis and Cling Fish. Extensive photographs are taken as well to provide a visual record of the shores. MarClim methodology is used at 3 of the sites.\t\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared docs/data/biological/littoral ,   Images - Dell1590 C/docs and settings/MONITORING/littoral.  MarClim data is also available on the NBN Gateway.\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:45:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17517\tNRW_DS101866\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.484Z\t298\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Stereo photographic monitoring of rocky reef communities\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Monitro cymunedau riffiau creigiog yn stereoffotograffig\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383636\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Investigating rocky reef communities at Skomer MNR. Very little is known about the community dynamics on sublittoral habitats. The epibenthic communities are one of the most abundant and important features in the Reserve. In order to make decisions about the conservation status of these communities it is vital to have some understanding of how these communities change over time. Therefore the aim of the stereo photographic monitoring is to monitor how species and communities vary over time. Possible measurements include species richness / diversity, growth rates, longevity and competitive interactions over time.\tTwo sites at Skomer MNR (one on the north and one on the south) have been monitored photographically since 1982. Four fixed quadrats are sampled at 10, 15, 20 and 25m BCD using a stereo underwater camera set up. The images provide a consistent time series record going back 1982. In 1982 four sites at North Wall were established at 5m depth intervals between 10 and 25m below chart datum.  In 1984 a series of 11 fixed position quadrats were established at Thorn Rock. A stereo camera set was used fixed onto a 50 x 40cm frame. Samples have been taken at least twice every year (May and Sept) since the start of the project.\t\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/stereo , Skomer MNR office fire proof cabinet 1 draw 4 (bottom)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tBullimore-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:46:25.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17513\tNRW_DS101867\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.160Z\t715\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Meteorological Records Wooltack Point\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383637\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Weather data collected from the Coastguard lookout station on Wooltack point, Pembrokeshire from May 1993 onwards. The continuous weather data can be used for monitoring climate change and factors effecting species within the MNR.\tMay 1993 - 20th Oct 2005: Fairmount EMS1200 weather station mounted on the coastguard hut. Anemometer, wind vane, air temp, humidity, shaded and un-shaded solarimeter, net radiometer, barometric pressure and a tipping bucket rain gauge.  April 2006 (26/04/06) – onwards: A Cambell Scientific ECN compatible weather station with a CR1000 measurement and control system with Switching anemometer, potentiometer wind vane, Temperature and relative humidity probe, 3 temperature probes (air ground and below ground), tipping bucket rain gauge, pyranometer, net radiometer, water content reflectometers and barometric pressure sensor.\t\tData also available for download through the following link; http://environmental-change.ccw.gov.uk/?page_id\u003d513 Data is held locally at the SKomer Office (DELL 709) A data backup is held one the Skomer mnr West Region drive/shared docs/data/weather\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-04-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T13:25:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17512\tNRW_DS101869\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.134Z\t852\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSand dune survey of Great Britain\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383639\tSand dunes are one of a series of terrestrial habitats that in Britain are almost entirely restricted to the coast. The others are saltmarshes, maritime cliffs and grasslands, vegetated shingle and strandlines. This dataset is made up of the results from vegetation surveys covering 49 sand dune systems in Wales. The specific objectives of the sand dune survey are:  - To review existing knowledge of British dune vegetation; - To compile an inventory of the range and extent of dune vegetation throughout Britain; - To allow the national and regional importance of each individual site to be assessed; - To provide vegetation maps and descriptions for each site in sufficient detail to support site specific casework and conservation management, and to act as a baseline for future monitoring.\tVegetation surveys are based on the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) and survey techniques included the mapping of all vegetation types in each site. The area of each vegetation or other land cover polygon was calculated and results aggregated. Mapping covered 6,405 ha, of dune area excluding large extents of afforested sand. Adding afforested dune area, vegetated dune in Wales totalled 8101 ha and this probably represented almost all of the vegetated dune resources of Wales. Field survey also recorded geomorphological characterises of sites and the impact of human activities. This information was used to classify dune systems into types of dune, summarise erosion accretion data and describe human impacts.\t\tSpreadsheet stored on personal drive of NRW\u0027s Coastal Ecologist (Peter Rhind, retired). However, the data is on the X: drive as a GIS layer, so the \u0027spreadsheet\u0027 could be derived from that layer:- X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Sand Dunes.  JNCC report available in CCW Ffynnon: NRW-14-059263\tInternal Contact: Julie Creer, Lead Specialist Advisor – Coastal Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tScottish Natural Heritage (SNH)-Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)\t\t1995-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:00:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17514\tNRW_DS101870\t2024-05-28T12:25:25.098Z\t272\tValid\tEditable\tMaritime Cliff and Slope Inventory for Wales (2005)\tRhestr o Lethrau a Chlogwyni Arforol Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383730\tMaritime cliff and slopes are a feature of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) listed under annex 1 of the EC Habitats Directive, form part of some Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) across Wales, and are a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitat.   This habitat encompasses a variety of vegetation types, ranging from saxicolous lichen communities in the spray zone through to sparsely vegetated cliff-faces and ledges to coastal grassland, heath and scrub on the deeper soils of the cliff-top.   This is a spatial datasets which outlines 1 km squares where maritime cliff or slope habitat is found across Wales.\tThe methodology used was quadrat based surveys.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Maritime_Cliff_and_Slope.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis, Julie Creer, Coastal Ecologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T16:43:47.766Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17511\tNRW_DS101871\t2024-05-28T12:25:24.980Z\t556\tValid\tEditable\tCollated Saltmarsh Survey of Wales (1994 - 2004) (archived data)\tArolwg o Gorsydd Heli Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383731\tThis, now static, dataset was collected to monitoring the progress of saltmarsh development across Wales, between 1994 and 2002.   The dataset comprises of a collation of biological data recorded across different saltmarsh environments.   This dataset is now archived, as more up-to-date information is available about saltmarsh habitats across Wales. However this still forms an important baseline dataset in which to monitor the health and species richness across saltmarshes and overtime. This will help inform conservation management requirements for the important saltmarsh habitats.   The more up-to-date surveys can be found in the following metadata entry: 114725 Priority Marine Habitats of Wales (coastal saltmarsh).\tThe structure of this data layer was updated in 2011 as part of a project to develop a series of priority and protected spatial marine habitat layers.   The survey methodology used to collect this dataset either followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) Phase 2 methodology or intertidal Phase 1 methodology. The habitats were mapped using GIS against either OS maps or Aerial photographs.    The NVC Saltmarsh layer was identified as most accurate of the three layers collated and therefore forms the base of the collation.  All polygons extracted from the Phase 1 Intertidal Saltmarsh data that intersect with the NVC polygons were erased.  Where 2 NVC survey overlap the most recently surveyed is taken as the most accurate.   All polygons from the 1:50,000 OS map that intersect with either the NVC Saltmarsh layer or the extracted Phase 1 Intertidal Saltmarsh were also erased.\t\tData is archived under Project No. 252, media No.\u0027s 905 and 1099\tInternal Custodian: Coastal Ecologist, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T16:56:07.304Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17551\tNRW_DS101896\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.444Z\t339\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Muddy gravels (LMX.Psyllid) in Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383936\tMuddy gravels consist of shores of mixed sediments ranging from muds with gravel and sand components to mixed sediments with pebbles, gravels, sands and mud in even proportions. Mixed sediments which are predominantly muddy tend to support infauna similar to mud and sandy mud shores.  Although sheltered muddy gravel habitats occur principally in estuaries, rias and sea lochs in areas protected from wave action and strong tidal streams, the environmental conditions of the Menai Strait are unusual, in that the water is relatively turbid and although the area is largely sheltered from wave action it experiences extremely strong tidal currents, reaching up to 8 knots in places during spring tides. The muddy gravel LMX.Psyllid biotope has been mapped along the west side of the Menai Straits, with the mixed sediment shores between Beaumaris and Lleiniog being highly productive, rich in animal and plant species. The muddy gravel LMX.Psyllid contains an unusually high number of syllid polychaetes and form a nationally important biotope, uncommon within the UK. The aim of this data capture was to identify the boundary of muddy gravel and muddy gravel with canopy of tideswept Fucus serratus.  - To determine what exactly constitutes the boundary of muddy gravel - To measure the extent of the muddy gravel biotope - To determine a physical damage index for muddy gravels - To take samples at old and recently impacted areas of muddy gravel.  - To characterise the infaunal community and identify the degree and type of change using previous data.\tMarine mud and muddy gravel habitats had previously been sampled by Barnes (2003) and CCW in August 2002.  The survey was undertaken two hours either side of low water on a good spring tide with transects walked from high to low water, perpendicular to the shore along a bearing.  At each sample point (sample station) GPS readings were noted. Information recorded included photograph of sediment surface, sediment type, species found on surface, % cover of fucoids, photograph of profile dug in sediment, species found in profile and infauna detectable by sieving. Sample points were spaced at 25m intervals and sufficient regular transects were sampled to ensure that extent and distribution were described.  Survey dates- 02/08/04-03/08/04, historic data was also used from 2002.\t\tData is archived under project No. 199, media 362\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T13:26:12.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17553\tNRW_DS101897\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.527Z\t298\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales Wetland Survey 1977-1982\tArolwg Gwlyptir Cymru 1977-1982\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031383937\tThis project was a major survey of wetlands in Wales. It was a joint venture between NCC Wales HQ and CST to describe, classify and evaluate the lowland wetlands of Wales on a floristic basis. In all, around 270 wetland sites were surveyed across Wales. The dataset is comprised of sketch maps, quadrat records, proformas  and a report (Ratcliffe \u0026amp; Hattey, 1982). In part, to inform Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) notification.  This dataset is being mobilised in 2023.  Only a redacted version of this data will be available under the Open Governance Licence and available for public use, see Limitation on Public Access and Use Constraints\tMapping typology (pre-NVC) developed survey. Outputs of the Wales Wetland Survey divide into three categories (i) up to 20 quadrat records per site, (ii) a proforma with standard data fields and (iii) sketch maps of the main communities. Anglesey survey maps are more detailed than others. Detailed and reliable surveys were carried out for Anglesey survey. Maps are subsequently more detailed than most in the 1977-1982 Wales Wetland Survey.  There are 7 site packs missing SH44/02 Cors Gyfelog 1983 SH44/09 Cors Graianog 1977 SH56/19 Llyn Cororion SH61/A, B and C Cwm Nancol 1987 SH75/03 Ty\u0027n Mynydd Res\t\tAlso see entry for FenBase (holds the quadrat/species data).  Metadata entry: 24288 * Field maps, Quadrat cards and slides stored in MyF filing cabinet (see Kathryn Birch) * Scanned versions of field maps in DMS:  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/ps/WLPS19771982/Wales%20Wetland%20Survey%201977-1982%20GIS%20Table%20and%20Document%20Tracker.xlsx?d\u003dw890221785d3940d6ad3b6958d613eda5\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1 * Quadrat species data held in FenBase but extraction to accessible system expected by Autumn 2023 * Tracker in DMS:: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/ps/WLPS19771982/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx * GIS table:  X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales\\Wales Lowland Peatland Survey Sites 1977-1987.lyr\tKathryn Birch Or Peter Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  (Formerly, Peter Jones as Peatland Ecologist)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t1982-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-22T16:35:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17555\tNRW_DS101913\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.587Z\t375\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCollation of Images for Use in the Promotion of Marine Biodiversity in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393133\tA significant remit of the Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) process is to raise awareness of biodiversity, both with the public and other stakeholders. Raised awareness leads to better understanding, appreciation and ultimately, the public taking greater responsibility for biodiversity. Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) partnerships in particular, are well placed for this role as they operate on a local scale, able to engage local communities, partners and visitors to an area. Inability to easily access high quality marine images is often a hindrance in this process. The purpose of this project was to collate high quality images of marine BAP habitats and species (both Welsh and local priorities), and also images of general Welsh marine biodiversity.\tImages to illustrate marine biodiversity were sourced in three different ways: - from CCW in-house collections of slides;  - by commissioning new photographs;  - and, from outside sources. For each of these three methods a target list of habitats, species and features was provided by CCW. The slides have been scanned at high resolution (4000 dpi) to produce uncompressed tif files (approx 56 MB each) and jpg files (approx 2 MB each). The tif files are held as archive copies on DVD by NRW.\t\tNRW Pembroke Dock Office, Archived as Project No. 172, Media Nos 502-504. Report is located in the NRW Reports Archive on the DMS.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Team Leader, Pembroke Dock Office, NRW. ( Ex-CCW Aethne Cooke)  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17554\tNRW_DS101925\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.526Z\t246\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales: Digital Photographs\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393235\tThis metadata entry relates to the photographs taken during the grassland surveys of Wales. This links with the The metadata entry \u0027Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Photo _ GIS Dataset\u0027 which details a GIS dataset containing notes about the photo locations.\tAt the onset of the project digital equipment was unavailable and therefore all images were taken on slides however in the last three years of the project digital photographs were taken. A programme of work is in place to transfer the slides into digital format.\t\tMost of the slides are in a filing cabinet in in the office of CCW\u0027s Grassland Ecologist. The digital photos are stored in the restricted access GRASSURV directory on the N:drive of Terrestrial Science Group. H:\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV\\PHASE II PHOTO AND IMAGE ARCHIVE The DVDs are stored in the Marine Fireproof Safe (project T007, Media T_Media007- Media T_Media019)\tStuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, TEG, NRW Jonathon Rothwell, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:08:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17552\tNRW_DS101928\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.528Z\t292\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales: Site packs paper maps and field notes\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393238\tThis dataset contains site packs from over 1070 sites surveyed or resurveyed during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales 1987-2004 and since. The site packs contain, for each site, site summary sheets (general site information and plant community area summations), site descriptions, vegetation maps and plant community (quadrat and stand) data, recorded using the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). The data was collected to provide detailed information for each site, to supplement published summary information of the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales.\tThe data is currently stored in Box Files which are organised by Areas of Search (AoS), of which there are 12. Each site within an AoS has its own site pack. Phase 2 lowland grassland survey is on-going, with some new sites being adopted and older sites being resurvey to supplement information for the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales.\t\tMost of the files are kept in filing cabinets in the office of CCW\u0027s Grassland Ecologist - Stuart Smith, who refers to these on a daily basis.  The electronic copies are held on the restricted access GRASSURV directory on the N:drive of Terrestrial Science Group. Copies of the site packs have also been distributed to the relevant regional offices.  The paper copies are held together in TSG files. The site files and other info on Ffynnon has not been superseded by the Recorder database as the latter only contains the quadrat data and site logistical info (e.g. not maps, descriptions, evaluations). Some files relating to sites in south Glamorgan and Gwent are kept in CCW\u0027s Cardiff office.\tData Custodian: Stuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:08:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17547\tNRW_DS101931\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.146Z\t333\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Infauna of the Usk and Wye and Severn Estuaries 2004\tIsfilod rhynglanwol yn aberoedd Wysg a Gwy 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393331\tAs part of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) intertidal Phase 1 mapping survey. This collection of sediment samples were completed to aid the identification of the biotope of the mud sediments in the two sheltered estuaries of the Usk and Wye, and the more dynamic environment of the Severn estuary.  The data were used to inform the biotope mapping and identification of these estuaries for the CCW intertidal phase 1 mapping survey.\tA surface sample of sediment, approximating to 10 cm x 10 cm, was sieved over a 0.5 mm mesh, in-situ and the retained material preserved in 4% formalin.  The infaunal component was analysed by Emu Ltd in the laboratory.\t\tThe data are archived as Project No 406, Media No 1424.  A copy is also stored at H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Severn\\2004 Usk and Wye Infauna 2004\tIntertidal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-11-11\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:48:05.071Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17549\tNRW_DS101965\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.181Z\t421\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Designated Site Assessment Documents and Data\tDogfennau Asesiadau Safleoedd Dynodedig Rhynglanwol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393635\tData derived from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) intertidal Phase 1 mapping survey have been used to evaluate the relative value of each survey site according to the JNCC intertidal Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) guidelines. The purpose of this data capture was to make recommendations and objectively evaluate the nature conservation value of stretches of the intertidal zone throughout Wales, in order to create new designations for SSSI and provide evidence and identify additional of intertidal features of interest to identify features to existing SSSIs. This in turn helps to develop Site Management Statements and Management Plans for existing SSSI and Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).\tThere are some data gaps in data collected from 1996 to 1999.  All data collected by a well trained survey team.   Intertidal Phase 1 mapping survey techniques were employed. Data derived from the CCW intertidal Phase 1 mapping survey have been used to produce these documents. An assessment was first carried out in 2007 and a subsequent assessment completed June 2010.\tJoint Nature Conservation Committee.  1996.  Guidelines for the selection of biological SSSIs: intertidal marine habitats and saline lagoons.  Peterborough.\tThese reports and data are archived as Project No 270, Media No 569 and 981\tInternal Custodian; Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2010-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:48:26.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17550\tNRW_DS101977\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.250Z\t711\tValid\tEditable\tHABMAP Predictive Habitat Maps of the Irish Sea\tMapiau Rhagfynegol o For Iwerddon (HABMAP)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393737\tThe HABMAP project was set up in response to the need for better spatial awareness of habitat distributions in the Southern Irish Sea. This work produced habitat maps of the seabed using novel predictive modelling techniques. Two main components of the project were the mapping of seabed habitats using data collation and predictive modelling, and the collection of new survey data. This was used to increase the knowledge of Irish Sea habitats and validate the maps produced by predictive modelling. There have be subsequent project developed from this HABMAP project, which includes the re-modelling and larger project area is being covered to include all Welsh waters. The purpose of this data capture is to provide seabed habitat maps that could be used for conservation and management. Project outputs might be used in strategic planning, decision making for offshore developments, sensitivity mapping and mapping essential fish habitats. Confidence of predicted biotope maps was assessed and rated as explained on individual maps produced. The area covered in this project related to the southern Irish Sea.\tThe methods used to undertaken the work included extensive surveys, generally using a combination of accoustic methods (e.g., multi-beam swath bathymetry) and biological survey (e.g. grab sampling, drop-down video). Using this methodology large areas of the maps could be produced including the use of interpolation techniques. The HABMAP project used a multi-parameter predictive tool to develop seabed biotope maps for the southern Irish Sea. This required the collation of existing biological and physical datasets across the whole study area within a Geographical Information System (GIS) and their subsequent use to construct a rule-based biotope prediction model. Data were collated for the following physical parameters: bathymetry, sediment type, temperature, wave stress, tide stress, salinity, bedstate and light attenuation. These data were combined in a GIS to produce a single map with many cells, all of which were associated with values for each of the physical parameters. Data were also collated for seabed biotopes and these points were overlain onto the physical data layer, allowing the relationships between each biotope and the physical environment to be determined. Southern Irish Sea- land-based boundaries include the Welsh coast west of the Dee Estuary in the north and west of the Gower in the south. On the Irish coast the area stretches from around 40 km north of Dublin to approximately 60 km west of Waterford and includes the whole SE coast including the offshore banks as well as parts of the Celtic Sea. Areas surveyed in 2005 - Arklow Bank, Celtic Deep, St George\u0027s Channel, Caernarfon Bay and West Anglesey.\t\tData is archived as project number 274, media number 1014 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1001to1100).   Available on legacy drive: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\HABMAP\\Maps2).   Paper report available from NRW library (see library catalogue/related title field above).    Also available as PDF files on legacy drive: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\HABMAP\\Interreg Report3) HABMAP final report as above;  tif registered files on legacy shared drive: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Scienc\\MFSG\\Users\\HABMAP4).  MapInfo files Intended for CCW internal use only: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Users\\HABMAP\\ .   Image files available to download from HABMAP website (see online resource below).\tInternal Contact: Karen Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tCardiff University-National Museums and Galleries of Wales-Marine Institute-University College Cork-Trinity College Dublin-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:49:31.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17548\tNRW_DS101978\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.151Z\t892\tValid\tEditable\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey of Selected Alder Woods on Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales (JA1)\tArolwg DLlC ar goedydd gwern dethol ar SoDdGA yng Nghymru - data SGDd (JA1)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393738\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of alder woods at eleven sites on ten Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) throughout Wales.    The aim of this survey was to provide a better understanding of the plant communities of Welsh alder woods, a rather neglected habitat.\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned survey contributes to a NVC Woodland Surveys collation. Standard woodland NVC survey methodology was used.  Woodland communities were identified by eye and mapped, supported by sample quadrats and target notes.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Woodland\\PH2_Woodland_Sites.lyr  Archived as Project 209 Media Number 574\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t1996-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-03T13:52:18.693Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n17544\tNRW_DS101979\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.109Z\t639\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Sites (Polygon)\tCam 1 Rhynglanwol Morol: Safleoedd (Polygonau)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393739\tThis is a spatial dataset showing the mapped location of sites. Includes information exported form the Phase 1 Intertidal database and includes selection unit, area, length, dates of visits, digitiser and biotope richness. The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005. All work has been undertaken by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team. Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.\tField survey and manual digitising from hand drawn maps. Confidence in this dataset is high and data capture was undertaken by experienced staff.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Map Sites.lyr .    Archived as project 204, media 569.\tCustodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:51:09.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17546\tNRW_DS101980\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.141Z\t776\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Marine Mud and Muddy Gravel Characterisation in the Menai Strait\tDisgrifio graean lleidiog a llaid morol rhynglanwol yn Y Fenai\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393830\tSeveral rich marine mud and muddy gravel habitats were identified within the Menai Strait by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Marine Intertidal Phase 1 survey team and a sampling programme initiated in order to better characterise the intertidal communities of these habitats.  Marine mud and muddy gravel habitats are an important feature of several candidate Special Area of Conservation (SACs), including the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay cSACs. Species lists for these habitats within the Menai Strait have not been compiled since 1982. This report details sampling work and laboratory analysis undertaken in order to characterise the intertidal communities of these habitats. Several rich marine mud and muddy gravel habitats were identified within the Menai Strait by the CCW Marine Intertidal Phase 1 survey team and a sampling programme initiated in order to better characterise the intertidal communities of these habitats.\tIntertidal core sampling was undertaken using Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) Phase 2 sampling methodologies and recording forms, and a total of 21 samples were collected, for both macrofaunal and granulometric analysis at 7 sites. A total of 182 species and higher taxa groups were recorded across all sites. CCW Marine Intertidal Phase 1 survey team recorded several rich marine mud and muddy gravel habitats within the Menai Strait in 1999 - 2001.\t\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 3, Media No 100  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media100\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:53:40.038Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17545\tNRW_DS101981\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.110Z\t244\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Sites (Line)\tCam 1 Rhynglanwol Morol: Safleoedd (Llinellau)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393831\tThis is a spatial dataset comprising of data for the mean high water mark around the coast of Wales divided into line lengths reflecting the phase 1 survey sites. Information provided includes site ID, site name, name of Special Area of Conservation (SAC) the site is part of, selection unit and length. The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005.   All work has been undertaken by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team. Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.\tField survey and manual digitising from hand drawn maps.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Sites.lyr .   Archived as project 204, media 569.\tInternal Custodian:  Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environment Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T10:06:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17543\tNRW_DS101993\t2024-05-28T12:26:25.018Z\t497\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Underboulder Communities in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004/2005\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393933\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  A specific feature of the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC are the underboulder communities, which are established in the extensive boulder areas within the SAC.  Rich underboulder communities have been recorded and mapped on the rocky intertidal areas of the Menai Strait and an area west of Llandudno.  Underboulder communities are also present where there are tide-swept communities that happen to lie on boulder habitats throughout the Menai Strait.  Sponge dominated communities have been recorded from boulders and cobbles that are nationally uncommon, hosting a rich faunal turf on tide-swept substrata. The associated report combines the surveys and findings undertaking during 2004 (Boyes and Allen, 2004) and 2005 (Mazik et al. 2005). The report combines both datasets and additional analaysis has been completed using both sets of data.  The purpose of this data capture was to  - Provide a reference condition on species present at a number of sites within the SAC, - Evaluate the variability between boulders and between sites and attempt to correlate the variability with physical factors,  - Identify the best methods for measuring the condition of the underboulder features in the SAC in the future and to evaluate the possible methods by which the impact of boulder turning can be assessed and monitored.\tBoulders were chosen along the same shore height (MLW - MSLW) and within close proximity to each other (where possible); boulders were only selected if they fit within a 0.5m x 0.5m quadrat; a GPS point was taken of each boulder; a photograph was taken of the boulder upper surface then a species list taken; the boulder was then turned, photographed and a species list taken from the underside of the boulder; a note was taken of the time spent compiling the species list; Evidence of boulder turning including photographs, an estimation of the number of turned boulders within a 25 m radius; General site description, together with site specific information (to each boulder) including substratum characteristics (underneath and surrounding the boulder), percentage cover of macroalgae and the presence of standing water.  Substratum characteristics included an estimation of the proportion of each major particle size (e.g., boulders, cobbles, gravel, coarse sand, silt), water content and whether or not the sediment was anoxic.  The intertidal Phase 1 mapping survey identified the underboulder communities in the Menai Strait as being important for their species richness and extent from 1999 to 2001. Limitations occurred within in-situ identification of species.\tSee also: DRAFT - Mazik, K., Musk, W.A., Scott, G, Travers, S., Jones, M. and D.P. Brazier.  2008.  Intertidal Monitoring of underboulder communities in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC.  CCW Marine Monitoring report No 36.\tThe report and data collected is archived as Project No 199, Media No 550 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T13:28:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17542\tNRW_DS101994\t2024-05-28T12:26:24.885Z\t453\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Pectenogammarus planicrurus in Cardigan Bay and Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SACs) 2004\tMonitro Rhynglanwol: Pectenogammarus planicrurus yn ACA Bae Ceredigion ac ACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393934\tThere were numerous aims of this data capture. - To establish the presence and extent of Pectenogammarus planicrurus at two sites where it had previously been found.  - To establish whether the amphipod was restricted to a localised habitat at the sites or whether it was present along the entire length of the shoreline, identify and map its presence and extent along the surf zone, estimate its abundance.  - To take representative core samples for enumeration of P. planicrurus and Particle Size Analysis across the full extent of the biotope.  The results of a Pectenogammarus planicrurus monitoring programme carried out at two sites- Pwllheli, Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SACs) and Gilfach yr Halen, Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SACs). P. planicrurus had previously been found at these sites during Phase 1 surveys undertaken by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) so the data collected contributed to an investigation into their continuing existence. P. planicrurus is the only amphipod which is a permanent resident of shingle beaches and is found in the intertidal zone of clean shingle beaches, often in the lee of obstacles such as rocky outcrops and groynes.\tPwllheli: Survey was undertaken by working two hours either side of low water with part of the survey on foot and part using a quad bike (distribution of the amphipod was more extensive than previously thought so use of a quad bike enabled sampling across the full extent of the beach within a single tidal window).  Initial surveying aimed to establish the presence/absence of the species throughout the site and involved randomly chosen sample stations along the surf line (position of each station recorded using dGPS). At each station a shallow trough was dug and the number of P. planicrurus estimated using MNCR SACFOR abundance scales adapted for crustacea. Small amount of sediment was sieved as a secondary check if P. planicrurus not immediately visible.  Sediment particle size and infaunal samples were taken away for analysis.  Gilfach Yr Halen: Survey was undertaken on foot working two hours either side of low water. Sampling stations were located approx every 10m along edge of the surf zone (position of each station recorded using dGPS). Sediment type was noted at each station and where possible a shallow trough was dug to enumerate any P. planicrurus identified. When digging a trough was not possible (on mixed hard substrata) the under cobble/boulder, and fucoid habitats were examined.\t\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 554 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\PectenogammarusH:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2004 IECS\\Pectenogammarus\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T12:55:09.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17541\tNRW_DS101995\t2024-05-28T12:26:24.817Z\t567\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria alveolata in Cardigan Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2004\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393935\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary, tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats).  Honeycomb worms build tubes from sand and shell fragments.  They are found on the lower part of rocky seashores but they also need a sand supply for tube-building. Dense congregations of Sabellaria alveolata tubes form large biogenic reef structures, which can be up to a metre thick and take the form of hummocks, sheets or more massive formations.  The purpose of this data capture was to identify specific survey locations and to measure the extent of Sabellaria alveolata reef along agreed transects, and assess the quality of Sabellaria alveolata reef present and map and make notes on any sightings of wireweed Sargassum muticum.   Confidence in this dataset is high as surveys were undertaken by experienced staff.\tThe four areas of S. alveolata within the Cardigan Bay intertidal area were surveyed in September 2004, working two hours either side of low water. Transect lines were mapped using an ORMTEC GIS412 portable dGPS, with software and logger developed specifically for this type of mapping. This system provides continuous or point logging facilities using the IRLA network, providing a temporal repeatable accuracy of between 1-5m with the data subsequently downloaded onto a PC for input into GIS. In order to achieve repeatability in survey techniques between sites, a working protocol was used.\t\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 548 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2004 IECS\\Sabellaria\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact:  Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T13:34:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17585\tNRW_DS101997\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.809Z\t330\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rocky Reefs in Milford Haven, Pembrokeshire Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2005\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313031393937\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now forming part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. The aim of this data capture was to establish a set of permanent monitoring sites representative of the rocky shore intertidal habitats within the Haven Waterway in order to implement a long-term monitoring programme. The purpose of this data capture and project was to collect and present baseline data on the current status of six sites within the Milford Haven waterway for future comparisons of distribution and abundance of rocky shore organisms.  As part of the Pembrokeshire Marine SAC, monitoring of the Haven waterway is required to provide regular reporting on the condition of the Habitats Directive features within the SAC.\tThis dataset contains the results of a baseline survey of fixed quadrats across six rocky shore sites within the Milford Haven waterway in July 2005. The sites were chosen to represent an environmental gradient along the Haven in terms of salinity and wave exposure to mirror transects established by OPRU in 1995.  However site selection was limited by the need to select sites that had suitable bedrock for permanent marking, reliable and reasonable access and suitable habitat throughout the shore. At each site samples were taken from three different shore heights- upper shore, middle shore and lower shore. Within each shore zone four 1m x 1m quadrats were permanently marked (placed in homogenous areas of inclined rock where possible, avoiding rock pools and large fissures). Photographs were taken of all quadrats and a habitat map produced to facilitate relocation. Within each 1m2 quadrat presence/absence data were recorded for all species using a 25 cell grid. This gave a frequency score of between 0 and 25 for each species. Organisms were identified to species level where possible with specimens being returned to the laboratory for verification where necessary.  Species within the following taxa were aggregated for cell counts: barnacles, limpets, flat winkles, rough winkles, amphipods, Verrucaria spp (except Verrucaria mucosa). Further data were captured at the six sites via limpet counts, limpet population profile, barnacle species abundance, barnacle abundance, collection of small molluscs and crustacea and Ascophyllum nodosum population structure.  The current survey provides baseline data for future comparisons.  Data from previous surveys exist for five of the six sites, however previous monitoring was generally in the form of transect surveys using SACFOR abundance assessment (which has less resolution for detection of population change).  As a consequence, results from these studies are not directly comparable to the data collected from this set of surveys.\t\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 556 (K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters (Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2005 IECS\\BedrockQuadrats Marine Recorder file: MRCCW30200000018 Arcview layers available in Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Pembrokeshire Marine SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T13:35:30.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17572\tNRW_DS102001\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.421Z\t324\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Ruppia maritima in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Spatial Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303031\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Ruppia maritima is a species of aquatic plant known by the common names beaked tasselweed, widgeongrass, ditch-grass and tassel pondweed. Ruppia maritima (beaked tassle weed) is found in suitable habitats all around the British coast and grows in soft sediments in sheltered shallow coastal waters from full salinity to nearly fresh water.  The aim of this survey was to return to an area where Ruppia maritima had previously been identified during a Phase 1 survey carried out by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to determine whether or not the species is still present. Ruppia maritima has previously been identified by CCW in 1999 during a Phase 1 survey of the Afon Tywi. It was found at grid reference SN371122 in a pool on a track in the higher saltmarsh in the middle reaches of the river. This area was revisited and the results of the survey can be found in this dataset.\tThe site and species had previously been identified during a Phase 1 survey carried out by CCW in 1999.  The boundary of the pool was mapped using an ORMTEC GIS412 portable dGPS, with software and logger developed specifically for this type of mapping. This system provides continuous or point logging facilities using the IRLA network, providing a temporal repeatable accuracy of between 1-5m.  The following methodology was used: -  once the site was located, a search for the R. maritima was made. A specimen was taken for formal identification in the laboratory. -  photographs were taken of the pool and its location. -  the extent of the pool was mapped using the dGPS logger.\t\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 551 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\Ruppia maritime\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T14:33:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17582\tNRW_DS102003\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.751Z\t798\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Biotope SLR.FserX.T in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2004\tMonitro Rhynglanwol: Y Biotop SLR.FserX.T yn ACA Bae Caerfyrddin ac Aberoedd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303033\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  SLR.FserX.T is a tide-swept algal biotope typically found on sheltered lower shore boulder, cobble and pebbles on muddy sediments that are subject to enhanced tidal water movement. It is considered nationally uncommon. This dataset contains the presence/absence of the biotope SLR.FserX.T (Fucus serratus with sponges, ascidians and red seaweeds on tide-swept eulittoral mixed substrata).  The purpose of this data capture was to determine the presence/absence of SLR.FserX.T biotope previously identified at Whiteford Point during a CCW Phase 1 survey and to ascertain the condition of the habitat.\tPreviously mapped area was located and a search for the biotope was made at low water by looking for characteristic species. A transect was walked along the tide line at low water within the area with checks made every 10 to 20m along the mapped transect. Mapping was carried out using portable dGPS. The tide-swept algal biotope SLR.FserX.T had previously been identified by CCW during a Phase 1 survey of Whiteford Point.\tReport associated with this data: Boyes, S. 2008 Intertidal Monitoring of the biotope SLR.FserX.T in Carmarthen Bay and  Estuaries SAC. CCW Marine Monitoring report No 38.\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 552 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities on optical media and is maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Fynnon. Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer,  Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T14:47:17.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17584\tNRW_DS102004\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.748Z\t306\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Hydroid-dominated Rockpools in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303034\tHydroid rockpools (LR.H) are, by definition, species poor in comparison to other rockpool biotopes within the UK. LR.H rockpools are shallow mixed substrata pools characterised by the hydroids Obelia longissima and Kirchenpaueria pinnata, ephemeral seaweeds such as Ulva sp. and Enteromorpha spp. and the common periwinkle Littorina littorea. The casts of the lugworm Arenicola marina and the sand mason worm Lanice conchilega can be found in sandier pools. Hydroid rockpools are also associated with mussel beds (SLR.MytX), with Mytilus edulis present in the pools. Barnacles (Semibalanus balanoides and Elminius modestus) and the keel worm Pomatoceros sp. may also be attached to the harder substrata. On the south side of the Burry Inlet, hydroid rockpools have been identified at Whiteford Point during Phase 1 biotope surveys carried out by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in 2000. The rockpools are located at the tip of Whiteford Point, within the vicinity of a cast iron Victorian lighthouse, currently listed as an ancient monument. The purpose of this data capture was to map and assess the current extent and distribution of Hydroid rockpools within the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and evaluate the possible ephemeral nature of Hydroid rockpools by assessing the species richness of the rockpools at Whiteford Point.\tThe study of hydroid-dominated rockpools on a low-lying area of intertidal proved very difficult due to the variable nature of the substratum and of the areas of standing water. This report provides a reference against which the future condition of the pools can be monitored and also identifies some of the more effective means of recording the habitat and associated species. Surveys were carried out by a team of 5 surveyors working three hours either side of low water.  Extent and distribution: A grid format of sample points was surveyed along lines of eastings and northings over the area of rockpools. Sample points were taken every 100m along transects. At each sample point a GPS coordinate and photograph was taken. Physical characteristics (percent sand cover, percent cover of hard substratum and percent cover of standing water) and key species of flora and fauna were recorded within the 4m2 survey area.  Species diversity: A GPS point and photograph were taken for each rockpool and the boundaries of distinct rockpools were mapped using a differential GPS. Characteristics of the pool (dimension, location, distinguishing features) were noted and species present recorded. Additional underboulder species were checked for by turning over at least ten rocks. A note was made of time spent compiling the species list.Teams consisted of 2 experienced intertidal surveyors each, providing reasonable confidence in taxonomic identification. GPS position is expected to be +/- 5 m. In 2012 the species data entered into Marine Recorder was re-structured to meet standard formats. Work carried out under contract to CALM.  Hydroid rockpools have been identified at Whiteford Point during Phase 1 biotope surveys carried out by CCW in 2000. As part of the ongoing monitoring program for CCW, IECS were commissioned to carry out further monitoring at this key site.\tTeams consisted of 2 experienced intertidal surveyors each, providing reasonable confidence in taxonomic identification.  GPS position accuracy is expected to be +/- 5 m. In 2012 the species data entered into Marine Recorder was re-structured to meet standard formats.  Work carried out under contract to CALM.\tThe report and data are archived as Project No 199, Media No 555 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 555) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW headquarters on optical media.   This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters (File path: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05  IECS\\LRH Rockpools.  Marine Recorder file:MRCCW30200000017, MRCCW31600000007  GIS data is here:- Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Carmarthen Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata Point of Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T14:33:39.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17575\tNRW_DS102006\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.504Z\t337\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Ophelia bicornis in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2004\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303036\tThe purpose of this data capture was to determine whether Ophelia bicornis found at two sites within the lower Loughor estuary during Phase 1 surveys carried out by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) were still present.  The polychaete Ophelia bicornis is known from only a few locations in the UK and is therefore of national importance. It is particularly vulnerable to changes in the granulometric composition of the sediment, pollution and trampling. The purpose of this data capture to determine whether O. bicornis found at two sites within the lower Loughor estuary during Phase 1 surveys carried out by CCW were still present.\tOphelia bicornis found at two sites within the lower Loughor estuary during CCW intertidal Phase 1 surveys.  Quadrat sampling was carried out in areas of suitable sediment at 25 m intervals along four 75 m transects spaced 25 m apart. At each sample site a GPS position and photograph were taken and sediment was removed (to a depth of 20cm) from a 50 x 50 cm area and passed through a 0.5 mm sieve. Any animals retained in the sieve were identified and returned to the sediment. Any Ophelia bicornis found were identified and an example preserved and returned to the laboratory for formal identification. All other samples were returned to the sediment. Confidence in this dataset is high. Professional taxonomic experts were used to confirm the identity of polychaetes collected.\t\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199,Media No 553 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Fynnon, on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Fynnon. H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\Ophelia bicornis\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T14:34:18.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17583\tNRW_DS102007\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.739Z\t354\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera noltii) Beds in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2004 and 2005\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303037\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Seagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas, such as lagoons and channels, which are sheltered from significant wave action.  Zostera noltii generally occurs higher on the shore than the other two Zostera species found in the UK and occurs in low/variable salinity areas. Seagrass beds have important biological functions and are of conservation and economic importance; however human activity in the coastal zone (pollution, dredging boat mooring etc) can affect its distribution or even destroy the beds completely.  The work expressed here is both to trial the methodology and to establish a reference condition for the eelgrass beds at Foryd Bay and Traeth Lafan in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. The purpose of this data capture was to determine and map the extent, density and condition of Zostera noltii identified at Traeth Lafan and Foryd bay in previous years, record the presence of epiphytes and ephemeral algal species on the beds, record other conspicuous biota within the Zostera beds, record any damage or disease of the Zostera and develop and test an index of damage.\tBoth sites were surveyed over one tide cycle working three hours either side of low water. Extent of the beds was recorded using portable dGPS to map boundaries. Sampling points at each site were chosen based on a grid system. Transect lines (Foryd Bay \u003d 100 m apart, Traeth Lafan \u003d 50 m apart) were chosen to correspond to specific Ordnance Survey easting lines and sampling was carried out at 25 m (Foryd Bay) or 50 m (Traeth Lafan) intervals along these transect lines.  At each point GPS coordinate and photograph were taken and a 50 cm x 50 cm quadrat was used to record percentage cover of Zostera, cover of Ulva sp., description of substratum, mussel crumble and evidence of disease.  Z. noltii beds were identified at Traeth Lafan and Foryd Bay during the CCW intertidal Phase 1 survey in 2001.  All GPS positions are expected to be +/- 5 metre accuracy.  All surveyors familiar with the attributes to be recorded during these surveys.  Confidence of the accuracy of the data is high.\t\tThe report and data collected is archived as Project No 199, Media No 546 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon, on optical media.   This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\MarineMonitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2004_05 IECS\tIntertidal Ecologist, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T14:34:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17577\tNRW_DS102008\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.533Z\t239\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria alveolata Reefs in Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) (2004/2005)\tMonitro Rhynglanwol: Riffiau Sabellaria alveolata yn ACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau 2004/2005\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303038\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary, tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats). Dense congregations of S. alveolata tubes form large biogenic reef structures, which can be up to a metre thick and take the form of hummocks, sheets or more massive formations. This dataset combines the results of two monitoring events carried out on Sabellaria alveolata reef features within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC during 2004 and 2005.   In 2004 the purpose of this data capture was to identify specific survey locations, measure the extent of S. alveolata reef along agreed transects, assess the quality of S. alveolata reef present.  Map and make notes on any sightings of wireweed Sargassum muticum.   In 2005 the purpose of this data capture was more detailed monitoring of selected reef areas to thoroughly characterise small patches of reef and make an assessment of the impacts of anthropogenic disturbance on reef diversity.\tThe 2004 monitoring was carried out using  a revised methodology with untested field application methodology defined by Allen et al. (2002). In 2005 the four areas of S. alveolata reef within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC intertidal area were surveyed in July and August 2004, working two hours either side of low water. Transect lines were mapped using an ORMTEC GIS412 portable dGPS, with software and logger developed specifically for this type of mapping. This system provides continuous or point logging facilities using the IRLA network, providing a temporal repeatable accuracy of between 1-5m with the data subsequently downloaded onto a PC for input into GIS. In order to achieve repeatability in survey techniques between sites, a working protocol was used.  Sabellaria alveolata reef had previously been mapped along this coastline during Phase 1 surveys carried out by CCW.\t\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 548 (File Path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 548) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maesy Ffynnon on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maesy Ffynnon.(File paths:Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\Sabellaria2004 and Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\Sabellaria2005 Marine Recorder Survey numbers: MRCCW30200000010, MRCCW30200000019\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T14:49:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17576\tNRW_DS102009\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.512Z\t390\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Mawddach Estuary Biotopes in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2004\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303039\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. The Afon Mawddach (Mawddach estuary) is a small, drying, bar-built estuary, and it is one of the best examples of its type in the UK. With its mountainous catchment area, it has some of the lowest nutrient inputs of any estuary in Wales.  For this reason, the Mawddach estuary has been included in the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The Mawddach estuary is predominantly a sandy/sandy mud estuary and contains communities characteristic of these conditions. The Institute of Estuarine and Coastal Studies (IECS) as part of the 2004 intertidal monitoring programme undertook a Phase 1 biotope survey of the Mawddach estuary following previous monitoring at the site. Specific aims of the survey included to carry out a Phase 1 biotope survey along predefined transects noting infaunal species and sediment characteristics, to digitally record photographic evidence along each transect and to assign biotopes to each of the sampling locations. This in turn would allow information to be provided on conservation objectives of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC estuary feature.\tFour transects positioned in the central section of the estuary were resurveyed. The transects were placed, two on each side of the river channel from the saltmarsh at the back of the shore to the river channel and thus traversing the fullest range and zonation of biotopes (from mud to muddy sand to sand). The original stakes, as previously used in the 1999 study, were located and the start position of each transect was noted. Survey techniques used by Wyn and Brazier, 2001 were implemented.  This survey work builds on the CCW phase 1 survey of 1997 and initial trials using the same transects in 1999, as part of a LIFE project to evaluate monitoring methods (Sanderson et al. 2001).\t\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 549 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon on optical media.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\MawddachEstuary\tCustodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-05T15:33:41.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17568\tNRW_DS102010\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.300Z\t732\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera marina) Beds in Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in 2004/2005\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303130\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Seagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas, such as lagoons and channels, which are sheltered from significant wave action. Eelgrass (Zostera marina), is considered to be scarce (present in 16-100 ten km squares) is protected through the EC Habitats Directive and is classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitat.  In particular, seagrass meadows are used as a refuge area by several juvenile commercially important fish species, and similarly provide an important food source for wildfowl such as Brent geese. As a result of their dense root networks, together with a certain degree of wave attenuation and the trapping of sediment particles which further encourages sedimentation, seagrass beds help to stabilise the underlying substratum, thereby playing an important role in terms of coastal defence and the reduction of coastal erosion (Davison and Hughes, 1998). As such, in addition to their biological function and conservation importance, they are also of considerable economic importance. Human activity in the coastal zone (pollution, dredging boat mooring etc) can affect seagrass distribution or even destroy the beds completely.  The purpose of this data capture was to map the current known intertidal extent of the Zostera biotope, develop a survey methodology to determine grass density, record damage to the beds caused by physical impacts, develop an index of disease and record species (epibiota) on the Zostera and adjacent sediment.\tThe extent of the Zostera was mapped on foot by walking around the biotope perimeter recording the boundary position with a portable dGPS system. Representative photographs were taken throughout the survey. The density of the seagrass was measure in 2004. 12 sampling stations of 0.07m2 were chosen at random and blades/leaves within the area counted to calculate blade density per m2. dGPS positions were recorded at each site. In 2005 sample points were chosen based on a grid system with sampling carried out at 25 m intervals along transect lines 50 m apart. A GPS and photograph were taken at each point and a quadrat of 50 cm x 50 cm was used to record Zostera marina cover, cover of Enteromorpha sp and the associated faunal species and the description of substratum. Physical damage was measured using dGPS points taken where any signs of physical damage were observed and notes recorded in situ on extent and type.  Zostera marina beds were mapped during the intertidal phase 1 survey during 1997 and CCW contracted IECS to follow up these records with a monitoring program.\t\tThe report and data is archived as Project No199 Media No 547 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\Z_marina2004H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\Z_marina2005\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T09:27:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17578\tNRW_DS102011\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.509Z\t272\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Muddy Gravel Community (IMX.VsenMtru) in the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303131\tThe results of monitoring the extent and species richness of a distinct area of mixed sediment that lies east of Llanbedrog beach. The muddy gravel community is - IMX.VsenMtru (Venerupis senegalensis and Mya truncata in lower shore or infralittoral muddy gravel), and has a high biodiversity of epifauna, flora and infauna, due to the admixture of cobbles, pebbles, gravel, sand and mud. This provides many different habitat niches. The habitat is located within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) on the lower shore at Llanbedrog. In fully marine conditions on the lower shore, this habitat can be particularly species rich due to the complex nature of the substratum supporting a high diversity of infauna and epifauna, however many factors affect the habitat, physical disturbance, bait digging, organic enrichment, introduction of non-native species etc. The purpose of this data capture was to map the current extent of muddy gravel, define the character of the muddy gravel for in situ recognition, identify current known species composition and identify location for representative core sampling and Particle Size Analysis.\tSurvey conducted on foot, working 2 hours either side of low water. The survey team identified and recorded species in situ where possible, samples were taken of any species that could not be immediately identified in the field for further analysis by experienced IECS benthic staff. Representative photos of the habitat were taken throughout the survey. A suitable location (representative of the overall nature of the biotope) was identified, and the position recorded for comparability of future surveys. At this five replicate core samples, each 10 cm diameter (0.0078 m2), and one sample for PSA were taken to establish infaunal biomass and sediment characteristics (January 2005). Distribution of IMX.VsenMtru previously mapped in the area during Phase 1 survey carried out by CCW in 1996.The extent of muddy gravel was mapped by walking around the biotope perimeter recording the boundary position with a portable dGPS. Notes were recorded in situ with respect to sediment characteristics and features of the biotope.  Infaunal sample does not coincide with epibiota records.  Epibiota recording of moderate confidence.  Positional accuaracy of 1-5 m.\t\tThe report and data collected under CCW contract FC 73-02-291 is archived as Project No 199,Media No 557 (K:\\Archive\\Media 501-600\\Media 557) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon on optical media.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon. (New file path: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\MuddyGravels Marine Recorder file: MRCCW3030000000B\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T09:28:31.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17580\tNRW_DS102026\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.539Z\t382\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Number and Extent of Sea Caves, Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303236\tSubmerged or partially submerged sea caves are included as an Annex I type within the EU Habitats Directive (92/43/EEC). These caves may vary in size and exposure, and thus the communities associated with them vary considerably depending on the structure and extent of the cave system. Sea caves generally provide habitat for colonies of encrusting animal species, but may also have shade-tolerant algal communities at their mouth. Caves on the shore and in the shallow sublittoral zone are frequently subject to conditions of strong wave surge and tend to have floors of coarse sediment, cobbles and boulders. These materials are often highly mobile and scour the cave walls. Caves that are subject to strong wave surge are characterised by communities of mussels Mytilus edulis, barnacles Balanus crenatus, cushion sponges, encrusting bryozoans and colonial ascidians, depending on the degree of water movement and scour at particular points in the cave system. The UK has the most varied and extensive inshore sea caves on the Atlantic coast of Europe. In Wales, sea caves are important within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC and are therefore a qualifying feature, but not a primary reason for the site selection. As such there is a requirement to assess the number and extent of intertidal sea caves within this SAC. Further background information on the surveying of sea caves in Welsh SACs can be found in Bunker and Holt (2003).  Sea caves are important within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC and are a qualifying feature. As such there is a requirement to assess the number and extent of intertidal sea caves within this SAC.  The specific aims of the survey were to identify and map the location of sea caves along the coastline, use digital photography to record their extent and to measure the threat from in-fill by the railway company.\tThe exact location of each cave was mapped using a portable dGPS to give the location at the base of each sea cave. The height and width at the entrance to the cave as well as the depth of each sea cave were estimated in the field to be used for post-survey calculations. Other land marks along the cliff face were also recorded as points of reference for future surveys and all the data was input into GIS. Digital photographs were taken of each cave and a brief description noted. Photographic evidence and relevant field notes were also used to make a post-survey assessment of any impact on the sea caves from the repair and maintenance of the railway running above the headland.\t\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 559 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2004_05 IECS\\SeaCavesFriog\tCustodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T09:29:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17574\tNRW_DS102032\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.492Z\t494\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of LR.Rkp.SwSed Pools in the Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2004\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303332\tRockpools are an important sub-feature of the \u0027Reef\u0027 feature of the Cardigan Bay SAC, in that they provide habitats of high species richness, often with species that are restricted to rockpool habitats only. The nature of rockpools, particularly those with sediment in the bottom, provides additional challenges to developing a repeatable and reliable method for monitoring of these features. This dataset contains the results of a survey investigating the extent, distribution and species richness of LR.Rkp.SwSed (sand scoured) rockpools at Llanina Point, Cei Bach. Rockpools are an important sub-feature of the \u0027Reef\u0027 feature of the Cardigan Bay SAC, in that they provide habitats of high species richness, often with species that are restricted to rockpool habitats only. Taxonomic identification carried out by experienced intertidal surveyors.  Positional accuracy using the dGPS is expected to be 1-5 metres. The aims for the survey of the rockpools at Llanina Point, Cei Bach were to: - map and assess the current extent and distribution of LR.Rkp.SwSed pools within this area - assess the species richness of these rockpools - record any presence of Japanese wireweed Sargassum muticum at the site.\tSurveys were carried out three hours either side of low water. Rockpools were chosen which could be easily relocated for future monitoring. Boundaries of distinct pools were mapped using dGPS, characteristics of the pool were recorded (dimensions, location on shore and any distinguishing features) and photographs were taken. Species present were recorded and any plants or animals which could not be identified in the field were collected for further analysis. Additional underboulder species were checked for by turning over at least ten rocks and a small net was used to catch mobile species. A note was made of time spent compiling the species list. In the case of a single large rockpool a series of sampling points measuring 1m2 were surveyed to gain a representative species list, other species that fell outside the sampling area were also noted. Timed counts were carried out over two minute periods for key characterising species (Patella vulgata , Littorina littorea, Pagurus bernhardus, Nucella lapillus, Gibbula umbilicalis) to give an indication of abundance.  The rockpools at Cei Bach had previously been identified as species rich during Phase 1 survey carried out by CCW in 1996.\tTaxonomic identification carried out by experienced intertidal surveyors. Positional accuracy using the dGPS is expected to be 1-5 metres.\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 555 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW headquarters on optical media.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2004 IECS\\Cei Bach rockpools\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:09:31.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17573\tNRW_DS102033\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.500Z\t427\tValid\tEditable\tCarmarthen Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Intertidal Sediment Flats Monitoring 2005\tMonitro Gwastadeddau Gwaddodion Rhynglanwol ACA Bae Caerfyrddin 2005\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303333\tThe purpose of this data capture was to gain an understanding of the variety and species composition of intertidal communities and their macro-scale distribution within the interest features of the Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The present study aims to revisit the area to undertake representative sampling to be compared to the phase 1 mapping survey undertaken in 1999 and 2001 in order to assess shifts and changes in intertidal habitats within the SAC. As such, the specific objectives of the current survey were therefore to:   - Undertake in-situ biotope recording along pre-defined transects to establish the extent and distribution of intertidal soft sediment biotopes  - Verify field observations with quantitative sampling of the intertidal habitats using core sampling for benthic infauna (and particle size analysis for sedimentary characterisation)  - Compare 2005 findings with baseline phase 1 mapping survey data with the view to detect changes or shifts in biotope distribution  - Assign the 2004 biotope classification.    Between 1999 and 2001, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) undertook a phase 1 Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) biotope mapping survey  of the intertidal areas of the Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The study identified over 80 littoral biotopes covering an area of approximately 130,000 hectares and included several important sub-features such as seagrass Zostera noltii beds, saltmarsh, piddocks in clay, hydroid rockpools, tide-swept algal biotopes, the presence of the polychaete worm Ophelia bicornis and the presence of the tasselweed Ruppia maritima. These habitats were resurveyed in 2004 in order to assess their current distribution and status. In 2005 a further survey was commissioned to assess the macro-scale distribution and community composition of the littoral biotopes in the SAC and determine any significant changes from the phase 1 biotope mosaic.\tSampling was undertaken using transects in order to target a large number of sites, covering a wide range of biotopes. A transect was initially chosen for each of 18 sites which was thought to cover the most number of representative biotopes (Appendix 1) with an additional transect added (Transect 19) during the survey to increase coverage in the \u0027Three Rivers\u0027 area in which the cockle fishery had just reopened. Each transect was located using a Thales Mobile mapper GPS based on positions provided by CCW. Starting at the top of the shore the upper and lower extents of each distinct biotope along the transect was tracked with the GPS (either on foot or using quad bike for more extensive habitats). At each distinct habitat, the substratum and dominant/ characteristic species were identified (in each habitat a surface inspection was complemented by sub-surface infaunal sediment sampling with sediment sieved through a 0.5 mm mesh sieve and species identified). In addition supplementary information was also recorded e.g. surface features, indication of natural or anthropogenic impact impacts recorded (i.e. blown sand, moving drainage channels, vehicle tracks, diggings, etc). This information was then used to assign a biotope code to each habitat.\tA phase 1 biotope mapping survey of the intertidal areas of the Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) has previously been carried out by CCW between 1999 and 2001. See also: Rowe, G.A, (2006). Carmarthen Bay SAC Sediment Flats Monitoring 2005: Intertidal Core Analysis. A report to the Countryside Council for Wales by Emu Ltd, Durley, Southampton. CCW Marine Monitoring Report No: 46. pp.89\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 549 (File path:- K:\\Archive\\Media 501 to 600\\Media 549) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW headquarters on optical media.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. Z:\\Arcive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\LSIB transects Marine Recorder file (Survey No. MRCCW3030000001E) GIS data is here:- Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Carmarthen Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T16:59:27.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17570\tNRW_DS102034\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.380Z\t766\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCarmarthen Bay Intertidal Attributes and Monitoring Techniques - Saltmarsh Boundaries\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303334\tThe Burry Inlet is the large estuary of the River Loughor, which separates the Gower Peninsula from the mainland. The Burry Inlet is a Special Protection Areas (SPA) and Ramsar site and its Atlantic and Mediterranean Saltmeadows have contributed to its designation as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The saltmarsh habitats here correspondingly determine the border of important mudflat and sandflats within the SAC. The area of saltmarsh within the Burry Inlet is regarded the most extensive site of such vegetation in Wales. In 1998, a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey was carried out by Prosser and Wallace (1999) for the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), to provide more detailed information on the distribution of key elements of saltmarsh within the estuary. They mapped an area of 1879 ha of saltmarsh vegetation. As part of the ongoing monitoring program for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), the Institute of Estuarine and Coastal Studies (IECS) were commissioned to carry out further monitoring at key sites in order to provide an understanding of the dynamics of the saltmarsh and mudflat/sandflat habitats. The purpose of the data capture was to map the seaward boundary of saltmarsh at Llanrhidian Marshes between Great Pill and Salthouse Point; map the seaward boundary of saltmarsh between Penrhyn Gwyn and Loughor Bridge and record positions of outlying Spartina anglica tufts at both locations.\tMonitoring took place at three sites within the Burry Inle; Llanrhidian Marshes, Morfa Bacas and Penrhyn Gwyn Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). The surveys at both locations were carried out over one tidal cycle, working 3 hours either side of low water. At Llanrhidian Marshes a quad bike was used to map the saltmarsh expanse between Great Pill and Salthouse Point (7.5 km) in order to cover the distance within one tidal cycle. A continuous boundary track was recorded along the saltmarsh edge with the differential GPS logger (GPS expected to have an accuracy of 1 - 5 metres) and photographs taken at appropriate locations.  At Morfa Bacas and Penrhyn Gwyn SSSI the area between Loughor Bridge and Penrhyn Gwyn was surveyed on foot due to difficult terrain. A continuous boundary track was recorded along the saltmarsh edge with the differential GPS logger and photographs taken at appropriate locations.  In 1998, a NVC survey was carried out by Prosser and Wallace (1999) for the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), to provide more detailed information on the distribution of key elements of saltmarsh within Burry Inlet. Phase 1 biotope survey carried out by CCW in 2000 also defined the seaward boundary of the saltmarsh. IECS have since been commissioned to carry out further monitoring at key sites.\t\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 199, Media No 549.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\Saltmarsh Edge\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:12:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17579\tNRW_DS102035\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.542Z\t597\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCarmarthen Bay Intertidal Attributes and Monitoring Techniques - Monitoring of Piddocks\tTechnegau Monitro a Phriodoleddau Rhynglanwol Bae Caerfyrddin - Monitro Pidogau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303335\tUnder the 1992 EC Habitats and Species Directive, the UK submitted a list of candidate  Special Areas of Conservation (cSACs) to the European Commission (EC). Each cSAC is  selected for one or more of the habitat types and species listed in Annexes I and II of the  Directive. This process was undertaken largely in 1995-1996. The piddock Pholas dactylus occurs in Britain from Kent along the south and south-west coasts including south Wales, Anglesey and Solway (Hill 2004). It has also been recorded from several sites on the east coasts of Yorkshire and Northumbria and southwest Ireland. The surface of the peat or clay bed is characterised by dense algal mats and pools of standing water (JNCC 2004). The presence of piddocks is instantly identified by holes in the substratum.  The aim of this data capture was to map the current known extent and density of boring molluscs, piddocks, across the biotope at Marros Sands and Whiteford Point, and record any mussel spat or seed on the biotope.  Piddocks are bivalves that bore clay and peat exposures. The survey work is largely a trial to determine the best means by which to record the extent of the exposures as well as the populations of piddocks. Sand levels are found to change over time, seasonally, annually and as a result of one off weather events (storms), and thus the areas of populated peat and clay also vary. A non-destructive means of assessing the piddock population is considered.\tTwo sites the Carmarthen Bay SAC, where piddocks had previously been mapped, were chosen; Marros Sands, near Pendine and Whiteford Point. Surveys were carried out 3 hours either side of low water and involved sampling points at 10 m intervals along transect lines chosen to correspond with specific northing lines across the peat and clay exposures. At each sampling point a GPS coordinate and photograph was taken and a 25 cm x 25 cm transect was used to record the following information: Slope of the quadrat, number of live and dead piddocks within the burrows, mussel spat or seed on the surface, cover of Enteromorpha sp, fucoids and associated faunal species (a way of assessing overall habitat quality), description of substratum.  Piddocks had previously been mapped on the north Gower coast during Phase 1 survey.\t\tThe data and report are archived as Project No 199, Media No 558.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW.H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\Piddocks\tData Custodian - Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata point of contact - Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:13:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17581\tNRW_DS102036\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.743Z\t367\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera noltii) Beds in Carmarthen Bay 2004\tMonitro Rhynglanwol: Zostera noltii ym Mae Caerfyrddin yn 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303336\tThe seagrass Zostera is a flowering plant typically found in sheltered areas with a muddy / sandy substratum. Zostera beds are of significant ecological importance in that they support a diverse community of infaunal and epibenthic species, algal and epiphytic species and provide refuge and feeding sites for fish and crustaceans (particularly juveniles). Furthermore, Zostera beds are an important food source for wildfowl (e.g. Brent geese). Zostera beds can be an important factor in coastal defence. The root networks stabilise the sediment, while the plants encourage sedimentation through sediment particle trapping and a certain degree of wave attenuation. This together with their role in supporting commercially valuable fish and crustacean species means that Zostera beds are also of economic importance. However human activity in the coastal zone (pollution, dredging boat mooring etc) can affect its distribution or even destroy the beds completely. The purpose of this data capture was to determine and map the extent, density and condition of Zostera noltii identified in previous years at Llanrhidian Sands and Penrhyn Gwyn in the Burry Inlet, record any damage or disease of the Zostera, record the presence of epiphytes and ephemeral algal species on the beds, record the presence of mussel crumble, and develop an index of damage.\tSites were chosen as broad areas where Zostera noltii had previously been recorded. At each site transect lines (100 m apart) were chosen to correspond with specific northing lines and sampling was carried out at 25 m intervals due west or due east of these transect lines. At each sample position, a GPS coordinate and photograph was taken and a 50 cm x 50 cm quadrat was used for recording percentage cover of Z. noltii, cover of Enteromorpha sp., mussel crumble (small aggregations of mussels, cockles and shell debris) and the associated faunal species (visible on the surface). A brief description of the substratum was also noted and the presence and severity of wasting disease (Labyrinthula zosterae) recorded, based on a scale produced by Burdick et al. (1993). The CCW intertidal phase 1 survey recorded the Zostera noltii in the Burry Inlet during the year 2000.  IECS surveys provide a further understanding of the resource in the Burry Inlet.\t\tThe report and data collected under CCW contract FC 73-02-291 is archived as Project No 199, Media No 546 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 501 to 600\\Media 546) and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at CCW headquarters on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2004_05 IECS\\Zostera Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Carmarthen Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:15:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17569\tNRW_DS102058\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.214Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) - River Chemistry Spatial Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303538\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Environment Agency Wales and licensed to NRW for internal use only.   The dataset has been supplied by the Environment Agency Wales and contains River quality chemistry. GQA covering England \u0026amp; Wales displayed in varying thickness according to flow and colour according to (year 2000) and chemistry.\t\t\tX:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\GQA Chem Network.lyr\tPlease contact Nick Davies ( Environment Agency)\tEnvironment Agency Wales (EA Wales)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:14:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17566\tNRW_DS102082\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.169Z\t440\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSquirrel Collation for Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303832\tRecords presence and absence of red and grey squirrels in Wales.  The collation was created to give a picture of squirrels distributions in Wales and to collect all relevant data in one place 1951 to 2006. The majority of the data is fully validated and verified.\tThe collation was transferred from a collection of 6 reports and existing datasets (mainly in excel format) into Recorder 6.  New datasets will be added to the collation as they become available.  This is a collation of red and grey squirrel records for Wales, collated from CCW contracted reports, joint CCW and Forestry Commission reports and extracted from CCW site files.\t\t[A]  Stored in Recorder 6. -  Squirrel Data - CCW - Central Wales, Report 475  CCW0003800000009  (See Metadata Entry:  102089) -  Squirrel Data - CCW - Central Wales, West area report (56)  CCW000380000000A   (See Metadata Entry:  102112) -  Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Aberhirnant \u0026amp; Lake Vyrnwy, Report 241  CCW0003800000005   (See Metadata Entry:  100791) -  Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Aberhirnant, Report 589  CCW0003800000007   (See Metadata Entry:  102086) -  Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Clocaenog  CCW000380000000B   (See Metadata Entry:  102121) -  Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Cwm Mynach, Report 540  CCW0003800000008   (See Metadata Entry: 102126)  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).\tLiz Halliwell, Mammal Ecologist Woodland and Farmland Mammals, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17567\tNRW_DS102086\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.094Z\t362\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSquirrel survey of Aberhirnant Forest and woodland surrounding Lake Vyrnwy (2001)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303836\tPresence or absence or red and grey squirrels in Aberhirnant Forest and the woodlands surrounding Lake Vyrnwy. This is a follow-up to the \"Squirrel survey of Aberhirnant Forest and woodland surrounding Lake Vyrnwy 1999 - 2000\". The purpose of this data capture is to inform work to remove grey squirrels in the area as well as gather further information about red squirrel populations. The survey locations were conifer plantations of Aberhirnant Forest and the catchment area of Lake Vyrnwy.\tThis is a follow up survey was commissioned by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in 2001 (the original survey was carried our between 1999 and 2000).  An initial survey of 17 grids of hair tubes were set out in Aberhirnant Forest and 2 in the Lake Vyrnwy Plantation in 1999.  Each grid consisted of 20 hair tubes in four rows each of 5 tubes.  Each row was 100 m apart and the tubes were 100 m apart in the rows.  Tubes were fixed to trees and baited.  These were then checked every two weeks and hairs deposited in the tubes were checked by eye and verified under a microscope using the negative staining technique where necessary.  A subsequent survey in 2001 used 2 grids with 30 traps.  Additional tube hair traps were also set.  A total of 120 records were taken, of which 53 were positive.  Grid references are estimated from map.\tMammal Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\t[A] Species Data held in Recorder 6   - Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Aberhirnant, Report 589 CCW0003800000007\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17571\tNRW_DS102089\t2024-05-28T12:26:53.381Z\t380\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSquirrels of Central Wales  (2001)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032303839\tThis dataset contains the records of the presence or absence of red and grey squirrels and associated tree species in four forests, Tywi, Irfon, Crychan and Brechfa, of Central Wales.  The aim of this data capture was to determine the presence and distribution of grey and red squirrel and to make some preliminary conclusions on population viability and recommendations for conservation actions for red squirrel.\tThis is a follow up survey to the collation of historical records of the same area in 2000.  Initial visits were carried out to determine the suitability of the areas for surveys.  Hair tube surveys were carried out using the methodology standard methodologies. On each site, 101 hair tubes were positioned 100 m apart along transects. Tubes were fixed to trees 2 - 3 m above the level of the ground.  Initially the hair was identified visibly but was verified by Sarah Cartmel using a negative staining technique as described by Dagnall et al (1995).  A total of 420 records were taken, of which 376 have positive sightings.  Two additional sightings were also recorded during the field work. Individual tube locations were not recorded.  All grid references are for transect centroids.\tFurther information is available in the report Red Squirrel Survey of Central Wales 2001, CCW Report 475.\t[A]  Recorder 6  Squirrel Data - CCW - Central Wales, Report 475  CCW0003800000009.\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17561\tNRW_DS102112\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.486Z\t628\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSquirrels of Central Wales - Collation of historical records (1950 - 1999)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032313132\tThe red squirrel (Sciurus vulgaris) is Britain\u0027s only native squirrel and has experienced a severe decline in England and Wales over the last 50 years. This decline is linked to the expansion of its larger relative the grey squirrel S. carolinensis, which was introduced into Britain from North America in the late nineteenth century. The exact mechanisms of displacement are not fully understood, but it appears that grey squirrels are better able to utilise food resources in broadleaved woodland, with the result that red squirrels generally disappear from such habitats within about 15 years of the arrival of greys. This dataset is a collation of red squirrel sightings for central Wales.  The purpose of this data capture was to collate all records of red squirrels from central Wales in order to identify remaining areas of refuge for this species. The data was also collated to provide the initial recommendations for conservation actions. Brief habitat assessments of the remaining red squirrels sites were provided, based on forest area, tree species composition and age structure. Forest configuration, adjoining habitat and the presence of large−seeded broadleaves were also considered, to assess the potential threat from grey squirrels. The primary aim of the present study was to collate all recent (post−1990) red squirrel records from central Wales, to identify the remaining red squirrel refugia. Other key objectives were to provide a preliminary habitat assessment of the main sites where red squirrels still occur, and to make initial recommendations for conservation action, as appropriate.\tRecords were collated from Biological Records Centre (BRC), County mammal recorders for Brecknock and Carmarthenshire, CCW, Forest Enterprises, Tilhill Economic Forest, Woodland Management Wales as well as private individuals and local naturalists.\t\tSpecies data held in Recorder 6  Squirrel Data - CCW - Central Wales, West area report (56)   CCW000380000000A\tLiz Halliwell, Mammal ecologist, NRW.  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2000-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17562\tNRW_DS102121\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.517Z\t328\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSquirrels of Clocaenog Conifer Plantation in North Wales (1992-2006)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032313231\tPresence of red and grey squirrels in Clocaenog Forest, based on joint contract run by Forestry Commission Research Agency (FCRA) and Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) (1992-1999) , with subsequent records supplied by Sarah Cartmel (1999-2006).  The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the population ecology of red and grey squirrels and to identify their habitat requirements, and to investigate the relative populations of red and grey squirrels in parts of the forest and whether they are stable or changing.\tSquirrels populations measured using visual counts, trapping and radio tracking.  Food availability was measured with cone transects and canopy photographs.  Additional ad-hoc records also taken.  This dataset also contains negative records.  All grid references have been validated.\tFurther information is available in the report Squirrel Population Dynamics in a Conifer Plantation of North Wales, FRCA Report Y6p/5155 and CCW contract FC-73-03-32.\t[A]  Species data held in Recorder 6 Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Clocaenog CCW000380000000B\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:18:01.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17563\tNRW_DS102126\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.459Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSquirrel Survey of Cwm-Mynach (2001 - 2002)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032313236\tPresence or absence or red and grey squirrels in Cwm-Mynach woods in the Snowdonia National Park.  Also a records of tree species from which the records were taken.  Red squirrels were reported in the Cwm-Mynach woods in 1998 and 1999 and the work was commissioned to establish whether there is a continued presence of red squirrels in this area. Survey took place on conifer plantations to the east of Mynydd Cwm-mynach within the Snowdonia National Park between November 2001 and January 2002. Squirrel activity was low at the time of survey, which may account for the presence of no red squirrels.  Further surveys at a different time of year could be considered.  Records where a positive identification could not be made have been tagged as unidentified.  In some cases the hair was only identified to family level.\tHair tubes were placed in rows in 5 areas of the woods, covering the area of most recent sightings in the centre of the plantation and the sections of the upper and lower plantations.  A full grid pattern could not be established due to access restrictions.  In total 48 tubes were deployed in rows in suitable areas.  Hair was collected from sticky blocks within the hair tubes and identified by eye.  Where this was not possible they were verified under a microscope using a negative staining technique as described by Dagnell et. Al (1995).  144 records were taken, of which 42 have positive results. Grid references for each trap were not recorded. Transect centroid\u0027s were estimated from paper maps.\tFor further information, please see the report Red squirrel survey of Cwm Mynach : November 2001 - January 2002, CCW Report 540.\t[A]  Species data held in Recorder 6 Squirrel Data - CCW - HQ Terr - Cwm Mynach, Report 540 CCW0003800000008\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:14.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17564\tNRW_DS102150\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.802Z\t560\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Biodiversity Action Plan Invertebrate Species Spatial Distribution (archive dataset)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032313530\tThis is an archived reference spatial dataset. It is advised that users gain a more up to date version of this datasets. UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority species were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The original list of UK BAP priority species was created between 1995 and 1999.  In 2007, however, a revised list was produced, following a 2-year review of UK BAP processes and priorities, which included a review of the priority species and habitats lists. The UK list of priority species, however, remains an important reference source and has been used to help draw up statutory lists of priority species in  England,  Scotland, Wales and  Northern Ireland, as required under Section 41 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities Act 2006 (England), Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2016, Section 2(4) of the Nature Conservation (Scotland) Act 2004, and Section 3(1) of the Wildlife and Natural Environment Act (Northern Ireland) 2011. Invertebrate BAP lists of priority species were produced with several important aspects such as the following: - most invertebrates have an annual life cycle and require their specific habitat conditions to be present each year;  - the ecological niches occupied by invertebrates can be highly specialised; - many insect species require different conditions for each stage of their life cycle (egg, larva, pupa, adult); and - many species have limited powers of dispersal.  This invertebrate BAP dataset was originally created in 2004 as a spatial layer for use within Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). This data should be used as reference only as it is out of date.\tThe invertebrate data was extracted from the Recorder 6 version of the Welsh Invertebrate Database and transformed into a MapInfo.tab file. This dataset has now been archived and should be used for reference only. Once the new Section 7 invertebrate species list has been completed as per the Environment (Wales) Act a new more up-to-date spatial layer will be created as an ESRI Feature Class. This will supersede this archived data. The archived data should be kept for internal reference.\t\tM:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Species\\all_wales\\Terrestrial\\BAPs\\invertebrates\tDr Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW Formerly: Adrian Fowles in CCW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:48.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17560\tNRW_DS102203\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.434Z\t1246\tValid\tEditable\tWelsh Lakes Bathymetry Data\tData Bathymetreg Llynnoedd Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032323033\tBathymetric data from Welsh lakes collated from documentary and electronic sources and re-formatted into a consistent and accessible form and structure.    The data includes transformed raw coordinate and depth data to bathymetric surface rasters at 0.5 m interval polygon shapefiles that can be placed into GIS software with volume/depth and area/depth data for the lakes at the same 0.5 m interval.    The data supplied collates existing bathymetric data to produce a series of GIS layers that display detailed bathymetric maps of Welsh lakes.-Data bathymetrig o lynnoedd Cymru wedi\u0027i goladu o ffynonellau dogfennol ac electronig a\u0027i ail-fformatio i ffurf a strwythur cyson a hygyrch. Mae\u0027r data\u0027n cynnwys data cyfesurynnau a dyfnder crai wedi\u0027i drawsnewid i rastrau arwyneb bathymetrig ar ffurf ffeiliau siâp polygon â chyfwng o 0.5m y gellir eu gosod mewn meddalwedd GIS gyda data cyfaint/dyfnder ac arwynebedd/dyfnder ar gyfer y llynnoedd ar yr un cyfwng o 0.5m. Mae\u0027r data a gyflenwir yn coladu data bathymetrig presennol i gynhyrchu haenau GIS sy\u0027n dangos mapiau bathymetrig manwl o holl lynnoedd Cymru.  Casglwyd data o ffynonellau o 1902 ymlaen. Cynhaliwyd gwaith safoni a dadansoddi ystadegol yn 2010. Cafwyd y mesuriadau dyfnder gan ddefnyddio seinydd adlais llaw a systemau ar gychod; cafwyd y lleoliadau daearyddol o fapiau cyfuchliniau llynnoedd gwreiddiol, lleoliadau daearyddol bras ac unedau GPS llaw i gael cyfeirnodau grid cywir. Mae’r hyder yn y set ddata hon yn uchel gan i’r data gael ei goladu a’i ddadansoddi gan weithwyr proffesiynol amgylcheddol profiadol; fodd bynnag, ar gyfer llawer o’r llynnoedd gall y data crai fod yn amherffaith a dylid bod yn ofalus. Mewn rhai achosion nid oes gwybodaeth bathymetrig ar gael (e.e., Llyn Bodlyn, Llyn Ogwen, Llyn Hesgin, Llyn Du) ac mae\u0027r wybodaeth hon ar gael fel data ar gyfer mannau penodol yn unig.  Datganiad priodoli  Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology © UKRI (CEH). © ENSIS-ECRC, University College London\tData was collated from sources from 1902 onwards. Standardisation and statistical analysis was undertaken in 2010. Depth measurements obtained using hand held echo sounder and boat mounted systems, geographic locations obtained from original contour lake maps, approximate geographical locations and handheld GPS units to obtain accurate grid references. The confidence in this dataset is high as data collated and analysed by experienced environmental professionals however for many of the lakes the raw data may be imperfect and should be treated with caution.    In some cases no bathymetric information is available (e.g. Llyn Bodlyn, Llyn Ogwen, Llyn Hesgin, Llyn Bodlyn, Llyn Du) and this information is available as spot data only.  INFORMATION WARNING: This data should not be used for navigation purposes.\t\tArchived in project 297 Media 1207 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1201to1300\\Media1207),  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Welsh lakes bathymetry.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Wales lakes bathymetry points.lyr  Tristan has a copy held on the Y drive Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Monitoring\\Tristan_Hatton_Ellis\\Corporate_data\\CCW_Final_Data\tInternal Custodian:   Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-04-02\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6562/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_wales_lakes_bathy\t2025-12-03T10:46:52.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17565\tNRW_DS102205\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.773Z\t328\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of the Nature Conservation Value of Mussel Crumble in the Burry Inlet and the Effects of Dredging/Hand Gathering Operations\tAsesiad o werth cadwraeth natur gwelyau cregyn gleision wedi’u cysylltu gyda\u0027i gilydd gan edau bysus ym Moryd Byrri ac effeithiau gweithrediadau casglu/treillio gyda llaw.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032323035\tThe aim of this data capture was to identify and quantify the epifauna and infauna of the mussel crumble beds, quantify the impacts of a simulated \u0027dredging\u0027 operation on the fauna of the beds and assess the stability of the beds in relation to dredging activities. Mussel Crumble are beds of mussels, barnacles, mussel and cockle shells, bound together by the mussel byssus thread. This dataset includes sediment particle size data, macrobenthic infaunal data tables, raw data matrices for the epifaunal quadrats. The purpose of this data capture was to identify and quantify the epifauna and infauna of the mussel crumble beds, quantify the impacts of a simulated \u0027dredging\u0027 operation on the fauna of the beds and assess the stability of the beds in relation to dredging activities.\tSuitable mussel crumble was located and it\u0027s position recorded using GPS. The latin square took the form of a single line of 12 sampling squares (3m x 36m).  Six of the latin squares were sampled for infauna and six for epifauna, of each set of six, three were controls and three were \u0027dredged\u0027. The simulated \u0027dredging\u0027 method was based on a previous site visit made by the project officer in the company of  a person who dredges for mussel seed in the Burry Inlet. The \u0027dredged\u0027 squares were sampled before \u0027dredging\u0027 in December, again immediately afterwards and then once in January, The control squares were sampled once in December and once in January.\tMercer, T., Aquatic Environments. 2002.  An assessment of the nature conservation value of mussel crumble in the burry inlet and the effects of dredging / hand gathering operations. Countryside Council for Wales Science Report 526. Bangor.\t[A] Report available from NRW library (Maes Y Ffynnon) and in Ffynnon.  [B] Data is available in Marine Recorder (survey number MRCCW30000000032) [C] Archived in project 307, media1222 (K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:15:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17558\tNRW_DS102209\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.379Z\t966\tValid\tEditable\tLANDMAP Geological Landscape\tTirwedd Ddaearegol LandMap\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032323039\tLANDMAP is a unique national information system, devised by the former Countryside Council for Wales, allowing information about landscape in Wales to be collected and organised into a nationally consistent dataset. The LANDMAP database includes both objective and subjective information and is designed to enable landscape quality to be taken into account in decision making.    This is a geographic dataset showing geological landscape classification for Wales in the form of polygons (Aspect Areas) with large amounts of associated textual information, including General, Description, Evaluation, Recommendations, Tolerance to Change, Area Boundary, Evaluation and Bibliography.    Information is gathered by LANDMAP in order to inform sustainable decision making and to enable landscape to be taken into account during decision making processes. The Geological Landscape dataset is important as geology is often the strongest influence on the landscape, affecting drainage patterns, vegetation cover, the human environment and so on. These effects in turn determine the possibilities for agriculture, water sources, transport networks etc.  2003 to 2008 then updated at regular intervals.   This dataset maps geological, geomorphological and hydrological features of an area. The geographical and topographical characters of areas are noted as are significant active geological and geomorphological processes, and landscape - forming pedological processes.-Mae LandMap yn system wybodaeth genedlaethol unigryw, sy’n caniatáu i wybodaeth am dirwedd yng Nghymru gael ei chasglu a’i threfnu\u0027n i set ddata sy\u0027n gyson yn genedlaethol. Mae cronfa ddata Landmap yn cynnwys gwybodaeth wrthrychol a goddrychol a\u0027i hamcan yw galluogi i ansawdd y dirwedd gael ei ystyried wrth wneud penderfyniadau. Mae’n cynnwys set ddata ddaearegol sy’n dangos dosbarthiad tirwedd daearegol Cymru ar ffurf polygonau (Ardaloedd Agwedd) gyda llawer o wybodaeth destunol gysylltiedig, gan gynnwys Cyffredinol, Disgrifiad, Gwerthusiad, Argymhellion, Goddefgarwch tuag at Newid, Ffin yr Ardal, Gwerthusiad a Llyfryddiaeth. Cesglir gwybodaeth gan LandMap er mwyn llywio\u0027r broses o wneud penderfyniadau cynaliadwy ac er mwyn galluogi ystyried y tirlun yn ystod prosesau gwneud penderfyniadau. Mae\u0027r set ddata Tirwedd Ddaearegol yn bwysig gan mai daeareg yw\u0027r dylanwad cryfaf ar y dirwedd yn aml, gan effeithio ar batrymau draenio, y gorchudd llystyfiant, yr amgylchedd dynol ac yn y blaen. Mae\u0027r effeithiau hyn yn eu tro yn dylanwadu ar y posibiliadau ar gyfer amaethyddiaeth, ffynonellau dŵr, rhwydweithiau trafnidiaeth ac ati. 2003 i 2008 ac yna’u diweddaru\u0027n rheolaidd. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn mapio nodweddion daearegol, geomorffolegol a hydrolegol ardal. Nodir nodweddion daearyddol a thopograffig ardaloedd ynghyd â phrosesau daearegol a geomorffolegol gweithredol sylweddol, a phrosesau priddegol sy’n ffurfio’r dirwedd.  Er bod y storfeydd llwytho i lawr yn cael eu hadnewyddu o bryd i\u0027w gilydd ac y dylent fod yn gyson â\u0027r gwasanaeth gwe, os oes angen gwarant bod y data byw yn cael ei ddefnyddio, dylid defnyddio diweddbwynt WFS neu WMS.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tA LANDMAP Quality Assurance Procedure is in place to ensure that LANDMAP Information meets an agreed standard of good practice and that data is accurate and consistent across Wales. The Geological Landscape dataset is one of five evaluated aspects which make up the LANDMAP information system. The process of  gathering, organising and evaluating data is similar for each and involves:   1) Classifying and mapping distinct Aspect Areas  2) Completing a detailed data capture form for each Aspect Area identified  3) Preparation of an accompanying Technical Report  4) Submission for Quality Assurance assessment\tDetailed methodology and Guidance documents can be found and downloaded from the NRW Website https://naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/business-sectors/planning-and-development/evidence-to-inform-development-planning/landmap-the-welsh-landscape-baseline/?lang\u003den\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap Download\\LandMap Geological Landscape (Boundaries).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap Download\\LandMap Geological Landscape (Classification).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap Download\\LandMap Geological Landscape (Evaluation).lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jill Bullen, Senior Landscape Specialist, Strategic Planning Group Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-07-22\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/2476/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/business-sectors/planning-and-development/evidence-to-inform-development-planning/landmap-the-welsh-landscape-baseline/?lang\u003den\t2025-12-01T17:02:45.299Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17559\tNRW_DS102271\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.330Z\t404\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales: Area of Search (AoS) Reports\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032323731\tAreas of Search (AoS) are geographical areas used in the selection of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs). There are a total of 12 AoS in Wales. For each AoS there are one or two phase 2 lowland grassland AoS reports. This dataset contains the series of these reports, each of which includes a detailed description of the survey undertaken within the area, survey methodology and conservation assessments of each survey site.\tThe 12 Areas of Search in Wales are: Clwyd, Carmarthen and Dinefwr, Ceredigion, Preseli and South Pembrokeshire, Mid and South Glamorgan, West Glamorgan and Llanelli, Gwent, East Gwynedd, West Gwynedd, Brecknock, Radnor, Montgomeryshire\t\tDigital data and reports available on the restricted access GRASSURV directory on the H:\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV  of Terrestrial Science Group.  Paper versions in libraries and held by CCW HQ and district staff.  Reports are found on legacy CCW\u0027s document archive, Report Numbers in Fynnon as follows: NRWF-15-000303      Brecknock 09/27617      Dinefwr and Carmarthen (2 reports) 09/27627      Ceredigion 09/27641      Clwyd NRWF-15-000451      Gwent 09/27672      MId \u0026amp; South Glamorgan 09/89979      Pembrokeshire 09/89950      Radnor 09/89971      West Glamorgan NRWF-15-000271      Anglesey and West Gwynedd (2 reports)\tStuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW Formerly Jane Stevens, Habitat and Ecosystem Resource Analyst, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:56:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17556\tNRW_DS102568\t2024-05-28T12:26:52.158Z\t575\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLakes ( 1:50,000 )\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032353638\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points lines and polygons. Lakes and large ponds in Wales digitised at 1:50,000. The primary objective of this project is to amalgamate  Quattro spreadsheet data extracted from the OS 1:50,000 by the Ordnance Survey and the OS Lakes GIS layer available as a standard GIS dataset to produce a comprehensive GIS Welsh Lakes layer for use within NRW.\tThe spreadsheet and the GIS dataset were checked and any corrections completed before being digitised using the standard format developed ( light blue stippling with outline ) against the 1:50,000  scale base-map\t\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\hydrology\\Lakes\\os_lakes.TAB\t\tOrdnance Survey-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1999-04-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17605\tNRW_DS102597\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.078Z\t243\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGreat Crested Newt (Triturus cristatus) Sites in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313032353937\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons. Listing of great crested newt (Triturus cristatus) sites-extracted from recorder. The purpose of this data capture was to provide information on the distribution and abundance of an Annex II species. Records for the whole of Wales although the majority of records occur in Ynys Mon and on the borders of East Wales. The collection of records began in 1974 and is on-going.\tOriginally held in a Quattro Pro database.  Exported to an MS Excel spreadsheet which is used to update Recorder and MapInfo layer layers.  Some of the data may have been passed to the Herpetological Conservation Trust in Bournemouth for upload to the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway.  Specific methods that were used to obtain the species records are unknown, as they will have varied for each record. This is an extract of all Great Crested Newt records collated from Recorder 6.  The dataset has been subject to validation (checking for inputting errors). Records range in accuracy from 2 to 8 figure grid references.  Some of these 8 figure grid references may have been generated by MapInfo and are not the true observational grid reference.\tAccuracy of dates ranges from exact dates such as 05/04/2001 to simply denoting the year.  e.g. 1996. One record is noted for 1944, however this may be an error within the dataset.  Species data is held within Recorder 6 see metadata record 24283 (under Parent ID) for more information.\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\\Great Crested Newts.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\\Great Crested Newts font point.lyr\tMetadata and data custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:58.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17610\tNRW_DS107789\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.080Z\t310\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDormouse Licence Return Data (1992 - 2006)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037373839\tThis dataset comprises of a collation of records from Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) and Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW) licence returns for work carried out on dormouse sites across Wales. The collation was created to add to the dormouse distribution in Wales, to collect relevant data in once place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available. The dataset does not represent a systematic coverage of survey of Wales. The location of records is generally random, depending on the requirements of the licence holders.\tRecords from licence return forms have been collated by Natural Resources Wales\u0027 and inputted into Recorder 6.  Licence return forms are completed by licence holders as a condition of receiving the licence. Licences are issued to organisations and individuals to permit activities which would otherwise be unlawful, such as deliberately capturing or disturbing dormice, or damaging or destroying their breeding sites and resting places. Licences are generally issued for the purposes of survey, monitoring and research, or for building and other development work.  Records are provided by licence holders, who have very variable recording ability. However, they have been verified by Natural Resources Wales to delete or correct any obvious errors. Some more recent mammal licence return data has been digitised by Cofnod. Cofnod act as data custodian and hold the top copy of this licence return data, not the data held in Recorder 6 which is managed by NRW.\t\t[A] Species data is held in Recorder 6 Dormouse - CCW - HQ Terr - Licence Returns CCW000380000000E\tInternal Custodian: - TEG, NRW -  Master Reference Data Team, NRW  Metadata Contact:  - Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tVarious including volunteer recorders\t\t2006-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17606\tNRW_DS107792\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.107Z\t262\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Shad Monitoring Data for Rivers Wye, Usk and Tywi (2006)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037373932\tThe purpose of this data capture was to monitoring of the Rivers Wye, Usk and Tywi for the presence of Shad.  These Rivers are designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) partly because they support internationally important populations of twait shad.  Allis Shad is also a feature of the sites and a secondary reason for selection as a SAC.  Records of other species of freshwater and estuarine fish are also recorded as part of this dataset. This provides a collation of existing knowledge, habitat and shad data, and provides evidence to design and implement a suitable juvenile monitoring programme for their condition assessment.  There were 5 survey sites along the River Usk, 5 sites along the River Wye and 5 sites along the River Tywi.  Further data is available in paper format in the associated report with historical records of eggs surveys extending back to 1980.  This historical data is collated from the Environment Agency.\tThe collation of data comes from a variety of sources including excel spreadsheets for data collected in 2006, Recorder surveys uploaded in 2007, and spatially mapped sample locations. Juvenile shad seine netting was undertaken in September and October 2005 and July 2006 in accordance with protocols described by Hillman et al (2003). Each sample station was netted multiple times and counts recorded for each. All fish were measured and a sub sample identified to species, using gill raker counts. The confidence in this dataset is high as it was undertaken by experienced staff.\tMonitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW Rivers Ecologist, NRW Further information is available in Countryside Council for Wales Environmental Monitoring Report no.40.\t[A]  Data is archived as project 377, media 1387.   [B]  Species data also available in Recorder 6 - Shad Monitoring, Usk, Wye \u0026amp; Tywi Catchments 2006 - CCW -  Report no.40 CCW0002800000005.\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2007-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17607\tNRW_DS107795\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.107Z\t356\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater Bullhead Survey Data in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037373935\tBullhead is listed under Annex II of the Habitat and Species Directive as a species of community interest and is a feature on the River Wye Afon Gwy and River Usk Afon Wysg Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales. Bullhead numbers have been declining across Europe in recent decades as a result of human activities. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor bullhead populations to assess whether SAC management adequately protects that species.\tRelevant species abundance data was extracted from the overall spreadsheet supplied with the RoC Report. Where quantitative and semi-quantitative surveys were carried out on the same site at the same time then the semi-quantitative data has not been extracted (as this is a subset of the quantitative data). Historical data for 2001 - 2003: Data was collated from the North Wales Fisheries Monitoring Programme of 2001. A limited number of sites (approximately 12) are fished on an annual basis to obtain information and detect trends within the fish populations and catches. Bullhead populations were also recorded as part of the Dee Stock Assessment Programme on a selection of sites in 2004 and as part of work on the Eel Monitoring Programme at 3 sites in the Dee Catchment for 2004.  All sites were surveyed using the electric fishing equipment in accordance with protocols described in the Environment Agency - Electric Fishing Theory and Practice Manual (1988). Two methodologies were employed either quantitative temporal data for sites surveyed annually or semi-quantitative spatial data for sites surveyed every 5 years. Three fishing runs were used for quantitative surveys and one for semi-quantitative surveys.\tThis published packaged dataset is made up of two separate datasets. Please see the following metadata entries for further information: 107797   Bullhead Monitoring for the Dee Catchment, 2004  108557  Bullhead Monitoring for the Usk and Wye Catchments, 2007\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the 17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A] Data is stored in Recorder 6:  - Bullhead Monitoring, Wye \u0026amp; Usk Catchments 2007 - CCW- Report no. 818 CCW000280000000B  - Bullhead Monitoring, Dee Catchment 2001-2004- CCW - RoC Report no. 19 CCW0002800000006\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17614\tNRW_DS107797\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.106Z\t278\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBullhead Monitoring for the Dee Catchment, 2004\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037373937\tBullhead is listed under Annex II of the Habitat and Species Directive as a \"species of community interest\".  Bullhead numbers have been declining across Europe in recent decades as a result of human activities. Bullhead is a designated feature on the River Dee and Bala Lake / Afon Dyfrdwy a Llyn Tegid  Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in Wales.  The purpose of this data capture was to assess bullhead abundances and population structure throughout the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) area. Population data for the fish bullhead (Cottus gobio) collected in the River Dee Catchment since 2001 and additional quantitative and semi-quantitative survey data to assess bullhead abundance from 2004.\tRelevant species abundance data was extracted from the overall spreadsheet supplied with the RoC Report.  Where quantitive and semi-quantitive surveys were carried out on the same site at the same time then the semi-quantitive data has not been extracted (as this is a subset of the quantitive data). Historical data for 2001 - 2003:  Data was collated from the North Wales Fisheries Monitoring Programme of 2001.  A limited number of sites (approx 12) are fished on an annual basis to obtain information and detect trends within the fish populations and catches. Bullhead populations were also recorded as part of the Dee Stock Assessment Programme on a selection of sites in 2004 and as part of work on the Eel Monitoring Programme at 3 sites in the Dee Catchment for 2004.  All sites were surveyed using the electric fishing equipment in accordance with protocols described in the Environment Agency - Electric Fishing Theory and Practice Manual (1988).  Two methodologies were employed either quantitive temporal data for sites surveyed annually or semi-quantitive spatial data for sites surveyed every 5 years.  Three fishing runs were used for quantitive surveys and one for semi-quantitive surveys.\tRivers Ecologist, NRW Further information, including information on population structure is available in CCW Review of Consents Report no. 19.  This dataset has been available to download on the NBN Gateway - See Metadata Unique Resource for full details: 107795\t[A]  Data Archived under Project Number 295 Media Number 1198    [B]  Data is also held in  Recorder 6 CCW0002800000006 Bullhead Monitoring [C]  Dee Catchment 2001-2004- CCW - RoC Report no. 19\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:30:59.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17612\tNRW_DS107825\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.067Z\t605\tValid\tEditable\tInvertebrate Data of Selected Coastal Soft Cliff Sites in Wales (2002 - 2004)\tData anifeiliaid di-asgwrn-cefn o safleoedd clogwyni meddal arfordirol dethol yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037383235\tCoastal soft cliffs have been highlighted as a priority habitat under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan as a component of the Maritime Cliff and Slope.  A total of 6 Grade 1, 7 Grade 2, and 47 Grade 3 species of invertebrates that are restricted to coastal soft cliffs have been identified for Wales.     A 3 year survey, jointly funded by Countryside Council for Wales, National Museums of Liverpool and with logistical support from the National Trust was undertaken between 2002 and 2004 to provide more detailed information on invertebrate fauna of coastal soft cliffs at selected sites throughout Wales.   The purpose of this data capture was to identify current threats and management requirements for soft cliff habitats, and to attempt to rank soft cliff sites on the basis of their cliff fauna across selected sites in Wales.\tAd hoc surveys occurred between 1987 - 2002 and 2004-2005. A total of 26 coastal soft cliff sites were surveyed, 16 of which were sampled intensively (primary sites).  The study generated approximately 15,000 records for more than 2,000 invertebrates species.   Records were collected by hand-sampling techniques, including searching and vacuuming bare ground, wet areas and short vegetation, sweep netting, hand netting and vegetation beating. Static traps were employed at the primary sites.  At each trapping station a combination of 2 yellow water-traps, charged with salt solution and detergent, a grid or transect of 5 pitfall traps, charged with the same solution and a single Malaise trap were used.    The data was provided to Countryside Council for Wales and is to be transferred and made available through Recorder 6.    The 16 intensely sampled \"primary\" sites were spread throughout Wales. On the Llyn Peninsula 2 in Cardiganshire 5 in Pembrokeshire and 4 on the south Gower Coast. The survey was carried out during the month of June and July in 2002 2003 and 2004. Ad hoc surveys occurred between 1987 - 2002 and 2004-2005.     Confidence in this dataset is high as surveys were carried out with experienced surveyors.    Voucher specimens have been retained in perpetuity in the collections of the National Museums of  Liverpool.  Residual samples containing taxa not identified have also been retained.  All samples collected by P.M. Pavett M. Howe J.Cooter I. Cheeseborough P. Boardman and B. Formstone as part of this project were determined by the collectors.\tKnight G.T. and Howe M.A. 2006. The conservation value for invertebrates of selected coastal soft cliff sites in Wales. CCW Contract Science. 761. Countryside Council for Wales and National Museums Liverpool.\t[A] Recorder 6 - Welsh Invertebrate Database Liverpool Museum coastal soft cliff survey CCW000660000000X.\tMike Howe Invertebrate Ecologist Terrestrial Science Group NRW.\tNational Museums Liverpool-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T17:09:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17608\tNRW_DS107902\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.106Z\t412\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring Data for the Rivers Wye and Usk (2007)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037393032\tRiver, Brook and Sea Lamprey are designated features of the River Usk and Wye Special Areas of Conservation (SAC).  Assessments of species composition, distribution, abundance and size composition of larval lamprey (ammocoetes and transformers) populations in these river are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment.  The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of Lampreys in the Usk and Wye Catchments.  Monitoring of the status of lampreys is required to comply with the reporting requirements of the Habitats Directive. 54 Sites on the River Wye (of which 30 contained positive records) and 35 sites in the River Usk (of which 10 contained positive records) were sampled.\tThe sampling strategy for the monitoring programme for sea, river and brook lamprey ammocoetes and transformers followed the protocols developed for the LIFE in UK Rivers project (Harvey and Cowx, 2003).  Electrofishing was carried out with three quantative (using a delimiting, rigid framework) or semi quantative (where the framework deployment was not possible, e.g. marginal areas) samples taken at each site.  The density of both Ammocoetes and Transformers were recorded for optimal (very silty) and sub-optimal (more rocky) habitats.  The source data contains both positive and negative records,  however negative records have not been transferred to Recorder or uploaded to the NBN Gateway.\tMonitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Rivers Ecologist, NRW.  Further information is available from Countryside Council for Wales Environmental Monitoring Report no.41\t[A]  Data is archived under Project 358 Media Number 1339.   [B]  Data is also held in Recorder 6 Lamprey Monitoring, Wye \u0026amp; Usk Catchments 2005 - CCW -  Report no. 41 CCW0002800000007.\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:59.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17609\tNRW_DS107907\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.106Z\t589\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring for the Rivers Tywi, Teifi and Cleddau (2004-2005)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037393037\tRiver, Brook and Sea Lamprey are designated (Annex II) features of the Tywi, Teifi and Cleddau River Special Areas of Conservation.  Assessments of species composition, distribution, abundance and size  of these species in these river are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment. The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of Lampreys in the Tywi, Teifi and Cleddau Special Areas of Conservation.  Monitoring of the status of lampreys is required to comply with the reporting requirements of the Habitats Directive.  A total of 112 sampling sites across the River Tywi, Teifi, Eastern and Western Cleddau were sampled of which 77 have positive records for either River or Brook Lamprey.\tThe sampling strategy for the monitoring programme for sea, river and brook lamprey followed the protocols developed for the LIFE in UK Rivers project (Harvey and Cowx, 2003). Quantitative quadrat surveys were carried out in both optimal (very silty) and sub-optimal (more rocky) habitats.  Electric fishing techniques were used inside a 1m2 quadrat to draw lamprey out of the silt rather than stunning and trapping them within the silt.  Sampling of optimal habitats quadrats was done thee times whilst sub-optimal habitats were sampled once.  The original data contains positive and negative samples.\tRivers Ecologist, NRW. Further information is available from Countryside Council for Wales Review of consents report no. 7.\t[A] Associated data is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 61, Media 356.  [B] Species data available in database called Recorder 6 under the Freshwater. CCW0002800000008 Lamprey Monitoring, Tywi, Teifi \u0026amp; Cleddau Catchments 2004 - CCW - RoC Report no. 7.\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:30:56.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17603\tNRW_DS107949\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.082Z\t366\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Lamprey in the River Dee (2004)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313037393439\tRiver, Brook and Sea Lamprey are a designated (Annex II) feature of the River Dee SAC.  Assessments of species composition, distribution, abundance and size class composition are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment.  The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of Lampreys in the Dee Catchment.  Monitoring of the status of Lampreys is required to comply with the reporting requirements of the Habitats Directive. The dataset is made up of a collation of historical data, recorded during the Environment Agency Wales juvenile salmon surveys from 1985 - 2004 and surveys conducted as part of the Countryside Council for Wales project 2002 - 2004.  Within the historical data, information has not been broken down to a species or genus level and is only recorded at the family level. Historical records collected 1985 - 2002 were sampled by electro-fishing according to protocols laid out by the \\Environment Agency Electric Fishing - Theory and Practice Manual\\\" (Environment Agency 1998). Sampling was either Quantitative or Semi-quantitative or Timed surveys; precise dates are not given and records are assigned a year only.\"\tIn 2002 a semi-quantitative methodology was used, a stretch of river was sampled until either 200 ammocoetes were caught or two hours had elapsed.  In 2003 a quantitative methodology was used employing a rigid quadrat framework with a base of 1m2 according to the protocol developed for the LIFE in UK Rivers project (Harvey and Cowx, 2003) .  The number of ammocoetes were recorded against sizes classes at a 10mm interval.  Sampling from 2004 was also carried out according to protocol developed by Harvey and Coxw and 31 sites were planned to be surveyed between August and October, however due to weather constrains only 3 of these sites were sampled.  Only positive records have been uploaded to Recorder and therefore the NBN Gateway.\tRivers Ecologist, NRW. Further information is available in CCW Review of Consents Report No. 18.  River Dee Catchment. 1985 - 2004.\t[A]  Data is stored as in the data Archive under Project Number 174, 294  Media Number 508, 1197.  [B]  Data is stored in Recorder Lamprey Monitoring, Dee Catchment 2004 - CCW - RoC Report No. 18 CCW0002800000009.   [C]  GIS and Spreadsheet data available.\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17600\tNRW_DS108557\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.993Z\t526\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBullhead Monitoring for the Usk and Wye Catchments 2007\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038353537\tThe bullhead, Cottus gobio, is declining throughout Europe (although still apparently common over its native range in the British Isles) and is listed in Annex II of the EU Habitats Directive as a \\species of community interest\\\". Member states have the duty to designate Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) for such species and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within the SACs. There are six Welsh rivers designated as SACs for bullhead including the Wye and Usk. The Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) (now part of Natural Resources Wales) is responsible for monitoring the condition of bullhead populations within them and this study represents the first systematic quantitative sampling for bullhead in Welsh SACs for this purpose. The bullhead is a feature on the River Wye / Afon Gwy SACs and River Usk / Afon Wysg  SACs in Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor bullhead populations to assess whether Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) management adequately protects that species. This project assessed the population attribute only. No judgement can be made as to whether bullhead is in favourable or unfavourable condition on the Rivers Wye and Usk until the three other mandatory attributes of water quality flow and river morphology have been assessed.  The monitoring was of populations of the freshwater fish Bullhead (Cottus gobio) on the River Usk and Wye SAC\u0027s in south-east Wales.\"\tThe surveys were carried out according to JNCC standard methodology (Cowx and Harvey, 2002) for monitoring bullheads.   Survey areas were established based on Countryside Council for Wales and Environment Agency (EA) data (2006-2007). This survey records the presence and populations of bullhead within these two SACs.  It also records the presence of other freshwater fish, including, eel, lamprey, minnow, salmon, stone loach and trout.  The presents of the invasive species Himalayan / Indian Balsam was also recorded. Comparison between data generated by the present study and those from the 2007 EAW surveys of both SACs suggested that future site condition monitoring should comprise single pass electric fishing but specifically targeted towards bullhead populations (i.e. not as a by product of juvenile salmonid surveys).\tThis dataset has been available to download on the NBN Gateway - See Metadata Unique Resource for full details: 107795 Further information is available in Bullhead Monitoring, Wye and Usk Catchments CCW Contract Science Report No 818.\t[A]  Data is Archived as Project No. 201, Media No. 566.  [B]  CRF Hard Drive.  [C]  Species data available in Recorder 6 CCW000280000000B Bullhead Monitoring, Wye \u0026amp; Usk Catchments 2007 - CCW- Report no. 818.\tCustodian - Master Reference Data Team - species Data is held in Recorder 6. Project Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis / Heather Garret. Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team.  Rivers Ecologist, NRW Area Officer, Llandrindod Wells, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17598\tNRW_DS108573\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.912Z\t395\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Distribution of Salix herbacea (tiny creeping willow) on the Glydeiriau and evaluation of habitat quality\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038353733\tEryri is the most southerly mountain range in Britain with summits of over 914 metres  (3,000 feet). As a consequence, it is also the southern-most station for a number of  Britain\u0027s montane vegetation communities, and also a range of arctic alpine plants. The importance of the montane vegetation in this area has been recognised both nationally through the notification of the three highest mountain ranges as the Eryri Site of Special Interest (SSSI), but also internationally through the designation of this site as the Eryri/Snowdonia Special Area of Conservation (SAC). In addition many of the upland habitats that are present are listed in the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP).  One of the arctic alpine plants which characterises some of Eryri\u0027s most exposed and inhospitable summits and crags is Salix herbacea, the smallest of the British willows. Salix herbacea is a component species of  Carex bigelowii-Racomitrium lanuginosum moss-heath (National Vegetation Classification (NVC) code U10), chasmophytic vegetation of rock crevices and rocky tors, and very rarely a component of flush/spring vegetation including (NVC code M3I) Anthelia julacea-Sphagnum auriculatum spring. The habitat that supports the largest stands of S. herbacea is the moss-heath and this occupies plateaux and ridges from 730-970 metres. Less frequently, it also occurs as a component of chasmophytic communities, usually with very few individuals at each site, and with an altitudinal range of 670 -998 metres.   This dataset is a collation of the results from a survey of the distribution of stands of Salix herbacea on the Glydeiriau, and comparisons between this data-set, and a similar data-set of 1986. The purpose of this data capture was to: -  Assess the condition of those plant communities which support Salix herbacea and where these communities are found to be in unfavourable condition,  -  Assess the causes and impacts that have lead to this condition;   -  Identify any changes in distribution that may have occurred during the period between the capture of data sets in 1986 and 2007 and which might be attributed to climate change;  -  Identify those stands of vegetation which might be expected to be most vulnerable to climate change, and which might be monitored in future years.  All suitable habitat on the Glydeiriau above 600 metres which supported Salix herbacea, included H6150: Siliceous alpine and boreal grasslands on summits, summit ridges, and bwlchs. H8220:  Siliceous rocky slopes with chasmophytic vegetation, particularly on exposed ridges and bwlchs, and high level.\tMethodologies adapted from JNCC (2006) Common Standards Monitoring Guidance for Upland Habitats. During this survey, three methodologies were used for data collection; mapping of stand, the recording of quadrat data, and target notes. Grid references were recorded either through the use of GPS (10 figure grid references), or through the interrogation of field  maps (8 figure grid references).\t\tNRW Upland Ecologist Officer and TSG archive\tThe contractor was Richard Leishman and the contract manager was Barbara Jones Internal Custodian - There is no longer an Upland Ecologist in NRW, so the main contact is Jane Stevens or Andy Jones (in the address book at Robert A Jones). Peter Jones or Stuart Smith could also know where the data is.        Sent an email to Jane Stevens about the location of the data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17611\tNRW_DS108651\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.110Z\t943\tValid\tEditable\tLANDMAP Landscape Habitats\tCynefinoedd Tirwedd LandMap\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363531\tLANDMAP is a unique national information system, devised by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), allowing information about landscape in Wales to be collected and organised into a nationally consistent dataset.   The LANDMAP database includes both objective and subjective information and is designed to enable landscape quality to be taken into account in decision making.    Geographic dataset showing habitat landscape classification for Wales in the form of polygons (Aspect Areas) with large amounts of associated textual information, including General, Description, Evaluation, Recommendations, Area Boundary, Evaluation and Bibliography. A LANDMAP Quality Assurance Procedure is in place to ensure that LANDMAP Information meets an agreed standard of good practice and that data is accurate and consistent across Wales.-Mae LandMap yn system wybodaeth genedlaethol unigryw, sy’n caniatáu i wybodaeth am dirwedd yng Nghymru gael ei gasglu a’i drefnu\u0027n set ddata sy\u0027n gyson yn genedlaethol. Mae cronfa ddata Landmap yn cynnwys gwybodaeth wrthrychol a goddrychol a\u0027i hamcan yw galluogi i ansawdd y dirwedd gael ei ystyried wrth wneud penderfyniadau. Mae’n cynnwys set ddata daearyddol sy’n dangos dosbarthiad cynefinoedd tirwedd Cymru ar ffurf polygonau (Ardaloedd Agwedd) gyda llawer o wybodaeth destunol gysylltiedig, gan gynnwys Cyffredinol, Disgrifiad, Gwerthusiad, Argymhellion, Goddefgarwch tuag at Newid, Ffin yr Ardal, Gwerthusiad a Llyfryddiaeth. Mae Trefn Sicrhau Ansawdd LANDMAP ar waith i sicrhau bod Gwybodaeth LANDMAP yn bodloni’r safon arfer da y cytunwyd arni a bod y data’n gywir ac yn gyson ledled Cymru.  Er bod y storfeydd llwytho i lawr yn cael eu hadnewyddu o bryd i\u0027w gilydd ac y dylent fod yn gyson â\u0027r gwasanaeth gwe, os oes angen gwarant bod y data byw yn cael ei ddefnyddio, dylid defnyddio diweddbwynt WFS neu WMS.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe Landscape Habitat dataset is one of five evaluated aspects which make up the LANDMAP information system. The process of  gathering, organising and evaluating data is similar for each and involves-  1) Classifying and mapping distinct Aspect Areas  2) Completing a detailed data capture form for each Aspect Area identified  3) Preparation of an accompanying Technical Report  4) Submission for Quality Assurance assessment\tDetailed methodology and Guidance documents can be found and downloaded from the NRW Website:    https://naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/business-sectors/planning-and-development/evidence-to-inform-development-planning/landmap-the-welsh-landscape-baseline/?lang\u003den\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Landscape Habitats (Boundaries).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Landscape Habitats (Classification).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Landscape Habitats (Evaluation).lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jill Bullen, Senior Landscape Specialist, Strategic Planning Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-09-17\tpublication\tnull\thttps://naturalresources.wales/evidence-and-data/accessing-our-data/beta-wales-environmental-information-portal/?lang\u003den\t2025-12-19T10:50:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17613\tNRW_DS108652\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.083Z\t585\tValid\tEditable\tLANDMAP Visual Sensory\tSynhwyraidd Gweledol LandMap\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363532\tLANDMAP is a unique national information system, devised by the former Countryside Council for Wales, allowing information about landscape in Wales to be collected and organised into a nationally consistent dataset. The LANDMAP database includes both objective and subjective information and is designed to enable landscape quality to be taken into account in decision making.    Geographic dataset showing visual and sensory landscape classification for Wales in the form of polygons (Aspect Areas) with large amounts of associated textual information, including General, Description, Evaluation, Recommendations, Tolerance to Change, Area Boundary, Evaluation and Assessment.    This dataset maps the landscape as perceived through our senses based on the physical attributes of landform and land cover. The perceived characteristics of the landscape are primarily determined visually, but the senses of hearing, smell and touch are also considered.-Mae LandMap yn system wybodaeth genedlaethol unigryw, sy’n caniatáu i wybodaeth am dirwedd yng Nghymru gael ei gasglu a’i drefnu i mewn i set ddata sy\u0027n gyson yn genedlaethol. Mae cronfa ddata Landmap yn cynnwys gwybodaeth wrthrychol a goddrychol a\u0027i hamcan yw galluogi i ansawdd y dirwedd gael ei ystyried wrth wneud penderfyniadau. Mae’n cynnwys set ddata daearyddol sy’n dangos dosbarthiad gweledol a synhwyraidd tirwedd Cymru ar ffurf polygonau (Ardaloedd Agwedd) gyda llawer o wybodaeth destunol gysylltiedig, gan gynnwys Cyffredinol, Disgrifiad, Gwerthusiad, Argymhellion, Goddefgarwch tuag at Newid, Ffin yr Ardal, Gwerthusiad ac Asesiad. Mae’r set ddata hon yn mapio’r dirwedd fel y’i canfyddir ef drwy ein synhwyrau, yn seiliedig ar nodweddion ffisegol tirffurfiad a gorchudd tir. Mae’r nodweddion a ganfyddir wedi’u seilio ar y gweledol yn bennaf, ond hefyd ystyrir synhwyrau clywed, arogli a chyffwrdd.  Er bod y storfeydd llwytho i lawr yn cael eu hadnewyddu o bryd i\u0027w gilydd ac y dylent fod yn gyson â\u0027r gwasanaeth gwe, os oes angen gwarant bod y data byw yn cael ei ddefnyddio, dylid defnyddio diweddbwynt WFS neu WMS.   Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe Visual and Sensory dataset is one of five evaluated aspects which make up the LANDMAP information system. The process of  gathering, organising and evaluating data is similar for each and involves:  1)  Classifying and mapping distinct Aspect Areas  2)  Completing a detailed data capture form for each Aspect Area identified  3)  Preparation of an accompanying Technical Report  4)  Submission for Quality Assurance assessment    A LANDMAP Quality Assurance Procedure is in place to ensure that LANDMAP Information meets an agreed standard of good practice and that data is accurate and consistent across Wales.\tDetailed methodology and Guidance documents can be found and downloaded from the NRW Website:   https://naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/business-sectors/planning-and-development/evidence-to-inform-development-planning/landmap-the-welsh-landscape-baseline/?lang\u003den\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Visual Sensory (Boundaries).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Visual Sensory (Classification).lyr X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Download\\ LandMap Visual Sensory (Evaluation).lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jill Bullen, Senior Landscape Specialist, Strategic Planning Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-09-20\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/2479/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://naturalresources.wales/evidence-and-data/accessing-our-data/beta-wales-environmental-information-portal/?lang\u003den\t2025-12-19T10:52:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17597\tNRW_DS108653\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.904Z\t1492\tValid\tEditable\tLANDMAP Historic Landscape\tTirwedd Hanesyddol LanMap\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363533\tLANDMAP is a unique national information system, devised by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), allowing information about landscape in Wales to be collected and organised into a nationally consistent dataset. The LANDMAP database includes both objective and subjective information and is designed to enable landscape quality to be taken into account in decision making.    This is a geographic dataset showing historical landscape classification for Wales in the form of polygons (Aspect Areas) with large amounts of associated textual information, including General, Description, Evaluation, Recommendations, Area Boundary, Evaluation and Bibliography. This dataset focuses on archaeological and historical sites and how they relate to each other and to the surrounding landscape. The features mapped include those resulting from past human activity and structurally prominent patterns and features that contribute to the historic character of the present landscape.   Information is gathered by LANDMAP in order to inform sustainable decision making and to enable landscape to be taken into account during decision making processes. Historic Landscape data can be used to assess the speed, direction and effect of change and to provide an understanding of how the landscape has evolved. A LANDMAP Quality Assurance Procedure is in place to ensure that LANDMAP Information meets an agreed standard of good practice and that data is accurate and consistent across Wales.-Mae LandMap yn system wybodaeth genedlaethol unigryw, sy’n caniatáu i wybodaeth am dirwedd yng Nghymru gael ei chasglu a’i threfnu\u0027n set ddata sy\u0027n gyson yn genedlaethol. Mae cronfa ddata Landmap yn cynnwys gwybodaeth wrthrychol a goddrychol a\u0027i hamcan yw galluogi i ansawdd y dirwedd gael ei ystyried wrth wneud penderfyniadau. Mae’n cynnwys set ddata ddaearyddol sy’n dangos dosbarthiad hanesyddol tirwedd Cymru ar ffurf polygonau (Ardaloedd Agwedd) gyda llawer o wybodaeth destunol gysylltiedig, gan gynnwys Cyffredinol, Disgrifiad, Gwerthusiad, Argymhellion, Goddefgarwch tuag at Newid, Ffin yr Ardal, Gwerthusiad a Llyfryddiaeth. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn canolbwyntio ar safleoedd archaeolegol a hanesyddol a’u perthynas â\u0027i gilydd ac â\u0027r dirwedd o’u cwmpas. Mae\u0027r nodweddion a fapiwyd yn cynnwys y rhai sy\u0027n deillio o weithgarwch dynol yn y gorffennol a’r patrymau strwythurol amlwg sy\u0027n cyfrannu at gymeriad hanesyddol y dirwedd bresennol. Cesglir gwybodaeth gan LandMap er mwyn llywio\u0027r broses o wneud penderfyniadau cynaliadwy ac er mwyn galluogi i’r dirwedd gael ei hystyried yn ystod prosesau gwneud penderfyniadau. Gall data Tirwedd Hanesyddol gael ei ddefnyddio i asesu cyflymder, cyfeiriad ac effaith newid a chreu dealltwriaeth o sut mae\u0027r dirwedd wedi esblygu. Mae Trefn Sicrhau Ansawdd LANDMAP ar waith i sicrhau bod Gwybodaeth LANDMAP yn bodloni’r safon arfer da y cytunwyd arni a bod y data’n gywir ac yn gyson ledled Cymru.  Er bod y storfeydd llwytho i lawr yn cael eu hadnewyddu o bryd i\u0027w gilydd ac y dylent fod yn gyson â\u0027r gwasanaeth gwe, os oes angen gwarant bod y data byw yn cael ei ddefnyddio, dylid defnyddio diweddbwynt WFS neu WMS.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe Historic Landscape dataset is one of five evaluated aspects which make up the LANDMAP information system. The process of  gathering, organising and evaluating data is similar for each and involves:  1)  Classifying and mapping distinct Aspect Areas  2)  Completing a detailed data capture form for each Aspect Area identified  3)  Preparation of an accompanying Technical Report  4)  Submission for Quality Assurance assessment\tDetailed methodology and Guidance documents can be found and downloaded from the NRW Website https://naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/business-sectors/planning-and-development/evidence-to-inform-development-planning/landmap-the-welsh-landscape-baseline/?lang\u003den\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap2017\\LANDMAP Historic Landscape.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LandMap Historic Landscape.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jill Bullen, Senior Landscape Specialist, Strategic Planning Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-09-17\tpublication\tnull\thttps://naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/business-sectors/planning-and-development/evidence-to-inform-development-planning/landmap-the-welsh-landscape-baseline/?lang\u003den\t2025-12-19T10:47:30.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17601\tNRW_DS108654\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.946Z\t987\tValid\tEditable\tLANDMAP Cultural Landscape (archived data)\tSet Ddata Tirwedd Ddiwylliannol LANDMAP\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363534\tThis dataset has been superseded by Landmap Cultural Landscape Services (metadata number:125279).  Landmap is a unique national information system, devised by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), allowing information about landscape in Wales to be collected and organised into a nationally consistent dataset.   The Landmap database includes both objective and subjective information and is designed to enable landscape quality to be taken into account in decision making.    It is a geographic dataset showing cultural landscape classification for Wales in the form of polygons (Aspect Areas) with large amounts of associated textual information, including General, Description, Evaluation, Recommendations, Tolerance to Change, Area Boundary, Evaluation and Bibliography.    The dataset records human activity that can be related to landscape, including major influences in art or literature which depicted or described the landscape, as well as more commonplace expressions of culture like farming type, or choice of building materials which creates a distinctive character in an area.   Information is gathered by LANDMAP in order to inform sustainable decision making and to enable landscape to be taken into account during decision making processes. This dataset is important as landscape not only shapes, but is shaped by culture. The dataset will raise awareness and enable people to engage in landscape decision making in Wales.-Mae LANDMAP yn adnodd tirwedd Cymru Gyfan sy’n seiliedig ar GIS lle mae nodweddion tirwedd a dylanwadau ar y dirwedd yn cael eu cofnodi a’u gwerthuso mewn set ddata sy’n gyson yn genedlaethol. Mae LANDMAP yn cynnwys pum set ddata sy\u0027n ymwneud â gofod, sef y Dirwedd Ddaearegol, Cynefinoedd y Dirwedd, Gweledol a Synhwyraidd, y Dirwedd Hanesyddol a\u0027r Gwasanaethau Tirwedd Diwylliannol.  Mae haen Gwasanaethau Tirwedd Diwylliannol LANDMAP yn darparu gwybodaeth yn ymwneud â gwasanaethau diwylliannol a\u0027r cysylltiadau rhwng tirwedd, yr amgylchedd naturiol a lle. Mae’n cynnwys lleoliadau naturiol, gwerthfawrogiad esthetig, llonyddwch, treftadaeth ddiwylliannol, ysbrydoliaeth ac ysbrydolrwydd a hunaniaeth ac ymdeimlad o le. Defnyddir y ffiniau Gweledol a Synhwyraidd presennol fel cyd-destun, a chyfrifir yr holl ddata yn ôl y ffiniau hyn. Mae\u0027r Gwasanaethau Tirwedd Diwylliannol yn disodli set ddata Tirwedd Ddiwylliannol LANDMAP.  Bydd y set ddata newydd hon (sy’n disodli Haen y Dirwedd Ddiwylliannol) yn canolbwyntio ar fapio rhai gwasanaethau diwylliannol allweddol sy’n arbennig o gysylltiedig â thirwedd ond ymhen amser bydd yn cynnwys gwybodaeth gyfoethocach yn yr arolygon. Bydd hwn yn gam sylweddol ymlaen o ran darparu data dibynadwy, ymarferol y gellir adeiladu arno a’i wella’n barhaus.     Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tA LANDMAP Quality Assurance Procedure is in place to ensure that LANDMAP Information meets an agreed standard of good practice and that data is accurate and consistent across Wales. A pdf is available detailing the Quality Assurance Procedure.    The Cultural Landscape dataset is one of five evaluated aspects which make up the LANDMAP information system. The process of  gathering, organising and evaluating data is similar for each and involves:  1) Classifying and mapping distinct Aspect Areas  2) Completing a detailed data capture form for each Aspect Area identified  3) Preparation of an accompanying Technical Report  4) Submission for Quality Assurance assessment\tDetailed methodology and Guidance documents can be found and downloaded from the NRW Website https://naturalresources.wales/planning-and-development/landmap/\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap2017\\LANDMAP Cultural Landscape.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Landmap\\LANDMAP Cultural Landscape.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jill Bullen, Senior Landscape Specialist, Strategic Planning Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2017-09-01\tpublication\tnull\thttps://naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/business-sectors/planning-and-development/evidence-to-inform-development-planning/landmap-the-welsh-landscape-baseline/?lang\u003den\t2025-05-02T15:16:21.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17596\tNRW_DS108658\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.849Z\t739\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAn Assessment of Juniperus communis (Juniper) Sub-species Hemisphaerica in Britain using Molecular Markers\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363538\tThis dataset contains the results of genetic analysis aimed at ascertaining the distinction of subspecies of Juniper comminis (juniper) a morphologically variable dioecious species native to the British Isle. Within Britain the extremes of this variation are recognised by two sub-species ssp. communis and ssp. nana but many intermediate forms are known and two further sub-species are also recognised by some- ssp. depressa and ssp. hemisphaerica. It has been suggested that these sub-species should be considered as varieties of J. communis, however because of the rarity of the ssp. hemisphaerica in Britain further clarification of its taxonomic status was desirable. The ssp. hemisphaerica, while found in mountainous areas of southern Europe and north-west Africa, is confined to two main small populations on low maritime cliffs in the UK. Other population, also consisting of less than 10 individuals, is found on Ramsay Island on the Pembrokeshire coast, south Wales. In addition, 4 other individuals from Wales have been considered to show morphological characters that are indicative of ssp. hemisphaerica, the populations being at Skrinkle Haven (Pembrokeshire) and Holyhead Mountain (Anglesey). The aims of the project were: - to ascertain whether plants named as Juniperus communis ssp. hemisphaerica form a cohesive genetic group distinct from Juniperus communis ssp. communis;  - to assess the extent of clonal growth in British populations of ssp. hemisphaerica. The dataset also confirms the status of a UK BAP species to determine future conservation effort.\tAll available populations/individuals of ssp. hemisphaerica were sampled (22 plants). To place the results into context, ssp. communis from neighbouring localities were sampled where possible as well as other populations from further afield within the UK (50 plants in total) to capture the range of genetic diversity found within this taxon.  DNA was extracted from silica dried leaf material using a Qiagen DNeasy 96 Plant Kit. The genetic techniques used were nuclear microsatellites and chloroplast markers, data analysis was then undertaken.  The contractors are experts in this field and so were chosen for the reliability of their research.\t\tCCW library, TSG archive and Welsh national libraries\tAndy Jones (Robert A Jones) Flowering Plant and Fern Specialist, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:26.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17599\tNRW_DS108672\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.917Z\t393\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCanal sediment and peat chemistry at Crymlyn Bog National Nature Reserve (NNR) (2005)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038363732\tAn investigation found that Crymlyn Brook was a major threat to the integrity of the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) qualifying habitats at Crymlyn Bog as it was a source of nitrogen and phosphorous flowing directly in to the area of low-nutrient status transition mire and quaking bog habitats. It was suggested that the course of the brook should be altered to what it was prior to changes and siltation during the latter half of the twentieth century. This would result in it avoiding the sensitive habitats, instead connecting to Glan-y-Wern canal the main route by which water leaves the mire.  This dataset contains the results of a survey of the possible route for the reinstatement of the Crymlyn Brook relating to the extant vegetation along the route and the degree and depth to which the substratum is contaminated with mineral oil. Also examined were the depth of the Glan-y-Wern canal and the degree to which the sediments in the canal are contaminated with heavy metals and mineral oil. The purpose of this data capture was to provide information on the depth of standing water in the canal, the chemistry of it\u0027s sediments, and various aspects relating to the extent of hydrocarbon pollution within peat lying along the course of a proposed drainage feature.\tSediments were collected and water depth measured at ten sample sites along the Glan-y-Wern canal. A Russian chambered corer was found to be most successful at sampling the bottom sediments, depth of water was measured using a 2.4m, 20mm diameter wooden dowel with graduations and a white wooden circular disk attached to its end. Estimates of the depth of unconsolidated sediment were made at sites accessible by boat. The route of the proposed ditch was surveyed at 21 sample locations at approximately 50m intervals. At each location the grid reference to the nearest metre was taken with a GPS and a simple description of the vegetation was noted (listing dominant species and probable community types). The gross stratigraphy of the peat was described in the field after samples were extracted using a Russian corer down to a depth of 1.5m. The degree of oil contamination was estimated using a six-point scale.  Analytical methods were used to measure  total nitrogen, phosphorous, sulphur, arsenic, diesel range hydrocarbon etc and the solubility of heavy metals was investigated.  Survey initiated in response to a 2004 study which proposed the creation of a link between the functional distal portion of Crymlyn Brook and the Glan Y Wern Canal.\tAssociated Report Title: Headley, AD. (2005)  Investigations of canal sediment and peat chemistry at Crymlyn Bog NNR, South Wales Report No. 721\tThe results are held in the Report located in the NRW library: [exCCW location Ffynnon Reference DCT-10-037447]  Located in Reports Archive: NRW DMS KNOW-1375319549-766\tInternal Custodian:  Peter Jones, Peatland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW Jamie Bevan, Senior Reserves Manager, NNRs/Swansea Neath Port Talbot / Operations SW, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17604\tNRW_DS108704\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.073Z\t449\tValid\tEditable\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey of Woodland Site of Special Scentific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales - (E3)\tArolwg DLlC ar SoDdGA coetiroedd yng Nghymru - (E3)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038373034\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of woodland at 171 sites (E3) on 132 Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) throughout Wales, covering a total of 3806 ha.    The purpose of this data capture was to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland SSSI series as fundamental site-support information.\tCCW commissioned survey contributes to a NVC Woodland Surveys collation. Standard woodland NVC survey methodology was used, which means communities were identified by eye and mapped, supported by sample quadrats and target notes. Data was collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by CCW Knowledge Management Group, corrections made by contractors, validated again by CCW Knowledge Management Group, corrections made in-house in conjunction with Woodland Ecologist.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Woodland\\PH2_Woodland_Sites.lyr  Archived as Project No. 206, Media No. 571\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t1998-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T09:36:47.074Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n17615\tNRW_DS108730\t2024-05-28T12:27:25.105Z\t776\tValid\tEditable\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey of Selected Woodland Proposed Sites of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSIs) in Wales (E4)\tArolwg DLlC ar SoDdGAa coetiroedd dethol yng Nghymru - data SGDd (E4)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038373330\tThe Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) selection criteria for woodlands are based on the National Vegetation Classification (NVC), such that the best areas of each community should be represented within each Area of Search (AoS). NVC information is therefore required for pSSSls.   This dataset comprises of a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of woodland at 13 sites on 12 proposed Sites of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSIs) throughout Wales, covering a total of 305 ha. The purpose of this data capture was to provide consistent survey information to aid assessment of SSSI status and subsequent site-support work.\tStandard woodland NVC survey methodology was used to identify Woodland communities. These communities were mapped by eye and mapped, supported by sample quadrats and target notes. Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by CCW Knowledge Management Group, corrections made by contractors, validated again by CCW Knowledge Management Group, corrections made in-house in conjunction with Woodland Ecologist.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Woodland\\PH2_Woodland_Quadrats.lyr  Archived as Project No.  207, Media No. 572\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t1999-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T09:41:28.352Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n17602\tNRW_DS108734\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.995Z\t364\tValid\tEditable\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey of 5 Woodland Proposed Sites of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSIs) in North East Wales (E5)\tArolwg DLlC ar bump o SoDdGAa coetiroedd yng ngogledd ddwyrain Cymru - (E5)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038373334\tThis dataset consists of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of woodlands on five proposed Sites of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSIs) in North East Wales.   The purpose of this data capture was to provide consistent survey information to aid assessment of SSSI status and subsequent site-support work.\tCCW commissioned survey contributes to a NVC Woodland Surveys collation. Standard woodland NVC survey methodology was used in data collection.  Woodland communities were identified by eye and mapped, supported by sample quadrats and target notes.\t\tArchived under Project No. 208, Media No. 573\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2008-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-03T14:07:05.327Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n17595\tNRW_DS108800\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.080Z\t867\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Saltmarsh Survey of Wales\tArolwg Cam 2 Morfeydd Heli Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383030\tThis National Vegetation Classification (NVC) Phase 2 project and corresponding datasets are a collation of mapped saltmarsh habitats across Wales.  This data was collected to provide an overview of saltmarshes across Wales.  The surveys were conducted between 1990 and 2003. The dataset has been extensively validated and verified.   However, habitat composition may change over time due to successional trends, land management or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type or species in the digital data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.  Coastal saltmarshes in the UK comprise of the upper, vegetated portions of intertidal mudflats, lying approximately between mean high water neap tides and mean high water spring tides.  Saltmarshes are usually restricted to comparatively sheltered locations in five main physiographic situations: in estuaries, in saline lagoons, behind barrier islands, at the heads of sea lochs, and on beach plains.  The development of saltmarsh vegetation is dependent on the presence of intertidal mudflats.  Communities are additionally affected by differences in climate, the particle size of the sediment and, within estuaries, by decreasing salinity in the upper reaches.-Mae\u0027r prosiect Dosbarthiad Llystyfiant Cenedlaethol Cyfnod 2 hwn a\u0027i setiau data cysylltiedig yn gasgliad o gynefinoedd morfa heli sydd wedi\u0027u mapio ledled Cymru. Casglwyd y data hwn i ddarparu trosolwg o forfeydd heli ledled Cymru. Cynhaliwyd yr arolygon rhwng 1990 a 2003. Mae\u0027r set ddata wedi\u0027i chadarnhau a\u0027i dilysu\u0027n drwyadl. Fodd bynnag, gall cyfansoddiad cynefinoedd newid dros amser o ganlyniad i dueddiadau olynol, rheolaeth tir neu hyd yn oed ddatblygiadau arfaethedig. Felly, ni ellir bod yn sicr bod math o lystyfiant neu rywogaeth yn parhau i fodoli am ei fod wedi\u0027i gofnodi yn y data digidol.    Cydnabyddiaeth:   Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right.\tThis dataset is one of a series of major Phase 2 lowland habitat surveys including former Countryside Council for Wales’ (CCW) in-house and commissioned surveys covering grassland, woodland, heathland, peatland and sand dunes. Dataset is a collation of 14 NRW commissioned NVC surveys carried out on saltmarshes across Wales.   Individual surveys utilised standard NVC Phase 2 survey methodology and community definition follows the National Vegetation Classification. Habitats were mapped using GIS against either OS maps or aerial photographs. Target notes and quadrat species lists were compiled.  Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by NRW KMG, corrections made by contractors, validated again by NRW KMG, corrections made in-house in conjunction with relevant salt marsh specialist.  This dataset has contribute towards the monitoring of Special Area of Conservation, which includes: Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries / Bae Caerfyrddin ac Aberoedd; Glannau Môn: Cors heli / Anglesey Coast: Saltmarsh; Pembrokeshire Marine / Sir Benfro Forol; Pen Llyn a`r Sarnau / Lleyn Peninsula and the Sarnau; Dee Estuary / Aber Dyfrdwy and Severn Estuary / Môr Hafren.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Saltmarsh\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM  (was originally managed by Peter Rhind, Coastal Ecologist, ex-CCW).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-08-04\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3605/?access_token\u003di61nd1X0RwdwAgQrGW0sqAsWxBMiwx\u0026amp;ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_phase_2_salt_marsh\t2025-12-19T10:12:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17590\tNRW_DS108805\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.063Z\t329\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Monitoring Sites\tSafleoedd Monitro Morol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383035\tSite location of Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRWs) Marine Monitoring Sites within Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).  This is general location of the sites where marine monitoring takes place and does not show the individual sample locations. It includes the location of monitoring sites for subtidal rock, subtidal sediment, intertidal rock, intertidal sediment, lagoons, marine climate monitoring sites, marine birds and bird food sites, seals and cetacean monitoring sites as well as areas where acoustic and drop down video monitoring takes place.   Information is also available for potential sea cave monitoring sites, however this is not currently a NRW monitoring programme.   The purpose of this data capture was to provide an overview of Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) Marine SAC Monitoring Sites. This is a Wales-wide but concentrated in the SAC and Special Protection Areas (SPAs). However additional sites will be added as they are identified.\tAll points have been plotted from original waypoints recorded on survey, extracted from Marine Monitoring reports or extracted from samples in Marine Recorder.   Several GIS layers have been produced.  1) Point location of survey areas (WalesMonitoringLocations_v5.TAB)  2) Detailed survey locations of the Marine Nature Reserve at Skomer (Skomer monitoring locations.lyr)  3) Line Transects for large scale monitoring, such as for birds and cetaceans (Wales monitoring transects.lyr)  4) Areas within which remotes sensing, such as video or acoustic surveys take place (Wales monitoring areas.lyr)  5) Areas where seal monitoring takes place (Seal monitoring sites.lyr)   All samples are mapped in British National Grid projection, although the native WGS84 grid references are retained in the table where originally recorded.\tFor more information about individual sample location which can be found on http://www.ukdmos.org/.\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Wales monitoring points.lyr  x:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Wales monitoring areas.lyr  x:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Wales monitoring points.lyr  x:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Wales monitoring transects.lyr   Archived as project 345, media 1321.\tMarine Monitoring Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T17:11:34.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17591\tNRW_DS108822\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.054Z\t542\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring invertebrates on Sites of Scientific Interest (SSSIs): Toadflax Leaf Beetle (Chrysolina sanguinolenta) on Poppit Sands, Pembrokeshire\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383232\tPoppit Sands actually fall within two Sites of Scientific Interest (SSSIs), Afon Teifi SSSI in the east and Aberarth − Carreg Wylan SSSI to the west, and the situation is further complicated by the boundaries of these two SSSIs overlapping. The line where the two SSSIs join almost follows a major path running from the car park to the beach 50 metres east of the RNLI buildings. The purpose of this data capture was to map the distribution of common toadflax (Linaria vulgaris) on and around poppit Sands, including number of plants, size of plants and numbers of leaves per plant. Count (zero) of Chrysolina sanguinolenta on and around these plants.  The purpose of this data capture was to determine a method of monitoring the Nationally Notable leaf beetle Chrysolina sanguinolenta, a qualifying SSSI feature here, and carry out Common Standards Monitoring.\tThe methodology used included counts of plants, mapping on aerial photographs, measurements of plant heights, counts of numbers of leaves on plants and photographs. The survey was based upon information in CCW\u0027s database of Welsh invertebrate records.\t\tLibrary and Area Libraries:  H:Drive \\TSG\\ Technical Research Reports\\Fowles_A\tMike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-10-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:14.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17589\tNRW_DS108873\t2024-05-28T12:27:23.989Z\t365\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCockle and Mussel Stock (bird food) Surveys in the Burry Inlet 2005 - 2009\tArolygon ar Gocos a Misglod (bwyd adar) ym Mhorth Tywyn o 2005 - 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383733\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPAs) are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive), and for regularly occurring migratory species. The Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) supports nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders.  It also supports an important cockle fishery. The removal of cockles and the associated disturbance caused by fishing, needs to be balanced with bird food requirements. The aim of the project is to assess the food requirements of wading birds that over-winter in the Burry Inlet Special Protection Areas (SPAs).  Data Collection is repeated annually.  In the future this data may be combined with data gathered on bird foraging behaviour, together with site specific data on bird numbers and used in an individual behaviour based model to predict the food requirements of a bird population.\tThe surveys were coordinated by Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring, who sub-contracted individuals to make up the field survey team.  Over 400 cockle survey stations (on a grid system) were surveyed, using GPS, 0.1m2 quadrats and a sieve of 2 mm mesh.  Cockles retained in the sieve were measured and counted; enabling the production of cockle density maps and estimates of the total cockle populations of each area.  Mussel beds in the Inlet were also surveyed in a similar way, and all mussels over 10 mm length in each 0.1m2 quadrat were measured and counted.  This enabled the estimation of the total mussel populations of each bed.  From 2007, samples of the shellfish were analysed to measure wet weight, ash free dry weight and shell length; providing sufficient data to determine length: weight relationships and thereby calculate estimates of total weight of shellfish in the Inlet. The methodology for the data collection is based on West et al. (2004). The dataset builds on work initiated in 2004 as part of a wider assessment of Cockle and Mussel Stocks across three sites in Wales (The Dee Estuary, Traeth Lafan, Gwynedd and the Burry Inlet).\tData was also collected in 2004 as part of a wider project to look at bird food stocks in several estuaries across Wales. This is detailed in a separate metadata entry (115678: Low Tide Wetland Birds Surveys (WeBS) Counts and Feeding activity of Oystercatch and Knot in the Burry Inlet SPA and Carmarthen Bay SAC (2013-2014)).\tThe data is archived under project 212, media no. 578.\tData custodian Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:22:50.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17592\tNRW_DS108879\t2024-05-28T12:27:23.995Z\t31\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTerrestrial Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitats of Wales (archived 2022)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383739\tThis dataset is the geographic data for Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats. The purpose of data capture was to show the distribution of BAP priority habitats across Wales and allow statistics to be derived.  Survey data initially collected between 1979 - 1997, however this dataset was compiled in February 2003. Derived from Phase 1 Terrestrial Habitat Survey and Other Datasets as required.\tDerived from the combined results of the Lowland Habitat Survey of Wales and the Welsh Upland Vegetation Survey (Phase 1) undertaken by the Nature Conservancy Council and subsequently Countryside Council for Wales between 1979 and 1997 shown as 1 km squares, points or polygons. Data has been compiled as a database which provides information on habitat extent for individual one kilometre squares across the entire land area of Wales. Where appropriate, these data are supplemented by the findings of various plant community surveys. They comprise a strongly contrasting ecological series which cover of semi-natural habitat, as well as heavily urbanised lowland coastal districts with a diverse but often severely fragmented complement of priority habitats; most LBAP areas also include substantial tracts of lowland farmland where improved agricultural grasslands predominate and wildlife habitats are scattered and often marginalised. Equally striking are variations in the extent and distribution of individual priority habitats which are represented in the majority of the Welsh LBAP areas. The confidence in this dataset is good, however the source data is now over 10 years old.  The data is considered archived and is superseded by the dataset Environment (Wales) Act Section 7 Terrestrial Habitats of Principal Importance (NRW_DS122418)\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Archive\\\tPeter Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-11-16\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:17:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17594\tNRW_DS108883\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.091Z\t592\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInvestigation into the Role of the Ancient Woodland Inventory, Historical Maps and Indicator Species in Identifying Ancient Woodland in Wales (2007)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038383833\tThe results of an experimental study involving desk study, field survey and statistical analysis, which investigated whether a more robust basis for identifying ancient woodland in Wales could be developed. Dataset includes species records from 20 study sites, a provisional list of vascular plant ancient woodland indicator species for Wales, broken down by region, results of statistical tests etc. Overall aim was to investigate whether a more robust basis for identifying ancient woodland in Wales can be developed. This included a comparison of the AWI and Epoc 1 County Series OS maps in terms of the identification of possible ancient woodland and a review of the use and reliability of Ancient Woodland Indicator Species in Wales.\tThis study was split into two methodologies desk study and field survey. Desk study: Indicator species lists currently in use to evaluate the nature conservation value and/or antiquity of woodland in Wales were collated. A comparison of the AWI and County Series OS maps was undertaken to consider reasons for omission of woodland from the AWI, quality of information, and ease of use.  Field survey: Field surveys were conducted at woodland sites in Carmarthenshire, Ceredigion, Powys and Glamorgan. Field work was undertaken at 20 study sites. A total of 81 2ha plots were sampled with at least one survey plot selected at each site to represent each of the different categories of woodland present (categories being defined according to whether the woodland appears on the AWI, Epoch 1 County Series maps or on neither of these). Survey concentrated on vascular plant species lists, additional information recorded included NVC types present, evidence of coppicing, presence of veteran trees and historic features. In addition to this statistical analysis tested for floristic differences and differences in frequency and diversity of plant species between categories of woodland.\t\tPaper reports - held in CCW libraries (Regional and HQ- see related titles field) [Ffynnon Reference DCT-12-243383]\tJim Latham, Woodland Ecologist, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:02:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17587\tNRW_DS108947\t2024-05-28T12:27:23.978Z\t612\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHydrological Investigations to Underpin Mire Restoration Work at Cleddon Bog Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), Monmouthshire, SE Wales (2006)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313038393437\tCleddon Bog Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) is located around 9 km south of Monmouth, Monmouthshire SE Wales. The site has a valley-head mire which has developed within two connected valley-head troughs.  The small catchment of which the site forms the upper reaches is a hanging valley system to the River Wye which runs north-south around 1 km to the east. There is a comprehensive body of evidence which shows that the condition of the mire feature at the site is deteriorating. Over the past few decades this has been marked by a decline in the abundance of key mire species at the expense of purple moor-grass and scrub. It is thought that the causes of this deterioration include drying of the mire surface, cause by catchment afforestation and a range of peripheral and internal drainage measures, coupled with the cessation of historical management practices such as grazing and burning. This dataset was collected to assess the current hydrological regime of the site and provide specific detailed recommendations to support a future programme of hydrological restoration.  The objectives of this project were to: - Develop an initial conceptual understanding of the principle water supply mechanisms to the site, with particular reference to valley-flank communities influence by lateral seepage. - Based on the initial conceptual model, undertake a limited programme of field investigation to secure a better understanding of water flow pathways into and within the site.  - Identify a range of hydrological restoration options for the site.\tProject Tasks included: An extensive hand-auger survey of shallow geological deposits and water levels within the site.  The survey positions and related information were levelled to a local site datum through a separate levelling survey.  -  A hydrological feature and hydro-chemical survey of the site, complemented by photo-documentation. -  Analysis and interpretation of a long-term groundwater level dataset. -  Detailed consideration of the influences on site wetness including: 1) quantitative consideration of the date of rainfall under various catchment land-use scenarios, 2) consideration of the influence of peripheral hydrological management features (e.g. drains), and 3) consideration of the hydrological properties of the bog vegetation.  -  Integration and interpretation of the information to develop and unified conceptual understanding of the hydrology of the site, leading to recommendations on future hydrological management.\t\tReport held in Ffynnon reference (DCT-09-011958)\tPete Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group  Senior Conservation Officer and Team Leader Monmouthshire CCW and Peatland Ecologist CCW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-03-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17593\tNRW_DS109024\t2024-05-28T12:27:24.061Z\t309\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAmphibians and Reptiles Licence Return Data  (2001 - 2008)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039303234\tThe collation of amphibian and reptile data from licence returns was created in order to store all relevant data in one location, and mobilise records which may not have otherwise been available to the public. This dataset comprises of records of amphibian and reptile distributions across Wales obtained from former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 and Natural Resources Wales\u0027 licence returns for scheduled amphibian and reptile species. This dataset does not represent a systematic coverage or survey of Wales, but enhances species distribution surveys. The geographic location of individual records is generally random, depending on the requirements of the licence holders. The dataset has been subject to some basic validation to eradicate erroneous outlying records and inputting errors but has not been systematically verified.\tLicence return forms are completed by licence holders as a condition of receiving the licence. Licences are issued to organisations and individuals to permit activities which would otherwise be unlawful, such as deliberately capturing or disturbing scheduled species, or damaging or destroying their breeding sites and resting places. Licences are generally issued for the purposes of survey, monitoring and research, or for building and other development work. Records consist of a mixture of torch, bottle-trap and netting surveys with some translocation of species also recorded. Records from licence return forms were collated into the Recorder 6 database by the former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 Environmental Information Team. This data has been packaged with other species licence return data on Recorder 6 and is available to download via the NBN Gateway (see Parent ID). Some more recent mammal licence return data has been digitised by Cofnod. Cofnod act as data custodian and hold the top copy of this licence return data, not the data held in Recorder 6 which is managed by NRW.\t\tRecorder 6 CCW0007200000002 Amphibian \u0026amp; Reptile Licence Return Data\tInternal Custodian:  Species Protection Team, Maes y Ffynnon/Ffynnon DRMS.  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW / Data Services Team, NRW\tVarious including volunteer recorders-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17588\tNRW_DS109025\t2024-05-28T12:27:23.796Z\t208\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOther Mammals Licence Return Data (1905 - 2007)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039303235\tCollation of records from CCW licence returns for work carried out on scheduled mammal species sites in Wales. The collation was created to add to the picture of protected mammal distributions in Wales, to collect relevant data in one place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available. This dataset includes sightings of Bank Vole, Eurasian Common Shrew, Eurasian Pygmy Shrew, Eurasian Red Squirrel, Eurasian Water Shrew, European Water Vole, Field Vole, Harvest Mouse, Polecat, Wood Mouse and Yellow-necked Mouse.\tLicence return forms are completed by licence holders as a condition of receiving the licence. Licences are issued to organisations and individuals to permit activities which would otherwise by unlawful, such as deliberately capturing or disturbing scheduled species, or damaging or destroying their breeding sites and resting places. Licences are generally issued for the purposes of survey, monitoring and research, or for building and other development work. Location of records is generally random, depending on the requirements of the licence holders. This data has been packaged with other species licence return data on Recorder 6 and is available to download via the NBN Gateway (see Parent ID). Some more recent mammal licence return data has been digitised by Cofnod. Cofnod act as data custodian and hold the top copy of this licence return data, not the data held in Recorder 6 which is managed by NRW.\t\tSpecies Data held in Recorder 6: Other Mammal Licence Returns CCW0008000000006    Paper Records: Dylan Lloyd, Operational Support Group, Maes y Ffynnon.\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various including volunteer recorders\t\t2007-01-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:27.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17586\tNRW_DS109396\t2024-05-28T12:27:23.491Z\t598\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Long-term Viability of Restored Lowland Heathland in Pembrokeshire\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039333936\tThis dataset contains the results of questionnaires and interviews conducted with farmers involved with the management of six restored lowland heathland sites in Pembrokeshire. It includes an assessment of the agricultural value of restored sites versus un-restored sites i.e. had the restoration scheme added value; an assessment of the ecological, environmental and financial implications of a range of long-term management options, and an assessment of the various mechanisms available to support long-term heathland management. The purpose of this data capture was to determine whether the heathland restoration scheme was seen by the agricultural community to have added value to the sites; to determine what levels of support, including financial support and other resources, graziers deemed they required in order to continue to manage the sites in line with nature conservation objectives within the schemes, and to identify any potential future developments which may support or impede the management of heathland.\tSix common land project sites were selected. They were chosen because they were in the process of being, or had been, restored. The sites were chosen to represent a range of current restoration or post-restoration management options. Questionnaires were sent to all the farmers with involvement in the six selected commons for whom contact details were available. These were followed up by one-to-one interviews with the farmers who agreed.\t\tCCW library (Ffynnon Reference: DCT-09-049345) - this just gives the location of the report.\tJane Stevens, TEG (Formerly Jan Sherry CCW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17625\tNRW_DS109473\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.548Z\t551\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSite Dossier for Lichen Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) Features at Pen y Gogarth/Great Orme\u0027s Head SSSI\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039343733\tData collected as part of a data search and lichen survey of Pen Y Gogarth/Great Ormes Head Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). The survey was divided into two parts: Part 1 dealt with the taxa that comprise the lichen flora, the origin of the Orme\u0027s older records and with the people who collected on the Great Orme prior to the current survey. Outputs include information on lichen communities and habitats and tables showing  the conservation status and rarity of each of the records, including those of special importance such as Red listed species.   Part 2 involved developing proformas and establishing Common Standards Monitoring for lichens through trials at the site. Outputs include two indirect monitoring site accounts with a summary of lichen interest, table of notable lichen species and management suggestions; site attribute tables for the two sites, and a map showing the extent of the sites. Also included are direct monitoring proformas for 6 sites with details of location, habitat, abundance, species present etc. The 6 sites monitored in this way are mapped to show their locations.\tPart 1- Study sites were selected mainly on the basis of notable topographic features such as areas of limestone pavement, cliffs etc. A number of unlocalised 10km square records were also searched for. Microscopic identification, chemical spot tests, UV fluorescence, thin-layer chromatography and reference to lichen floras and relevant taxonomic papers were used to identify species whilst referees in Britain and abroad were consulted to rule on critical taxa.  A species list was completed for the site with each taxon given a frequency rating on the DAFOR scale. Specimens resulting from the work have been lodged with the National Museum of Wales, Cardiff. Nomenclature follows Coppins 2002 and information on the conservation status of lichens is based on Woods and Coppins 2003.  Part 2- Trials for establishing a baseline for Common Standards Monitoring of lichens at Pen Y Gogarth/Great Ormes Head SSSI carried out over two days in good weather. Two lichen habitats of considerable importance were chosen- limestone pavements and dry inland limestone cliffs. Two monitoring formats were employed- site attribute tables for indirect lichen habitat monitoring and direct monitoring proformas for easily recognisable notable species or communities.\tData is held within the report.\t[A] CCW library and Ffynnon reference DCT-09-049763 [B] Report copy on DMs\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2007-03-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:29:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17635\tNRW_DS109566\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.064Z\t395\tValid\tEditable\tNon Native Species data - Distribution of Carpet Sea Squirt (Didemnum vexillum) in Holyhead Marina\tDosbarthiad Didemnum vexillum ym Marina Caergybi\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039353636\tDidemnum vexillum is an invasive colonial ascidian that has become established world-wide having been identified in Japan, North America, northern Europe, and New Zealand, following a global expansion since the 1970’s. It forms sheet like colonies on natural and artificial hard substrata as well as benthic organisms including other ascidians and algae. The serious ecological and economic damage experienced in New Zealand and other temperate regions has led to a large investment in on-going research into the biological tolerances and spread of D. vexillum as well as rapid response, monitoring and management following introductions.  In summer 2008 an MSc student from Bangor University carrying out a survey of non native species in marinas in North Wales found a sea squirt in Holyhead Harbour that looked like the invasive non native species D. vexillum. A specimen was sent to a UK expert, who confirmed in November 2008 that it was very likely to be D. vexillum. Specimens were confirmed by Gretchen Lambert (pers. com. University of Washington, USA) as being D. vexillum.   The purpose of this data capture was to assess the distribution and spread of the non-native species and possible threat to native species and biodiversity of the area.\tA rapid assessment diver survey was completed by systematically surveying each of the pontoons.  Data was collected by divers and communicated to the surface where it was mapped. Presence/Absence of Didemnum vexillum was recorded.   Still images and video were also taken at random locations.   Specimen samples were taken at various locations on each of the pontoon for further identification and confirmation by an expert.\t\tGIS Dataset: X:\\Biological\\Non_Native_Species\\Invasive Didemnum Vexillum.lyr  All data is archived under media no 580, 593 \u0026amp; 592 (Project 214). Working copies of the data is held on MFSG shared Drive S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Didemnum vexillum Photos also archived on MMT DVD 17 in the fireproof safe and video archived on the I drive I:\\MMT video Archive\\Handheld Diver video\\2008\\Didemnum vexillum.\tData Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata Custodian: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T17:12:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17642\tNRW_DS109614\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.022Z\t488\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Sea Temperature Recording\tCofnodi tymheredd y mor - GNF Ynys Sgomer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039363134\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. Monitoring of the seabed and temperature at Skomer MNR.  The purpose of this data capture was to:  Provide accurate seawater temperature records for the seabed and the water column.  Record temperature as continuously as possible.  Produce an ongoing long-term data set for the site.\tSea temperature is recorded at seabed (19mbcd) 15m 10m 5m and 1m from the surface. Drop down probes and temperature loggers are used. The Skomer MNR data set runs from July 1992 and is ongoing. Intertidal temperatures have been recorded since Feb 2007. Equipment used Valeport series 600 MKII CTD probe Vemco minilog - seabed logger and Onset \u0027hobo\u0027 pendant loggers. The intertidal seabed data is downloaded every 6 months. Profile data is taken weekly in season (Apr - Oct). Intertidal data is downloaded every 3 months.\t\tSkomer MNR West Region drive/shared documents/data/oceanographic/temperature\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2007-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T10:29:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17639\tNRW_DS109654\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.049Z\t370\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Sea Water Turbidity Recording\tCofnodi cymylogrwydd d\u0027r y mor - GNF Ynys Sgomer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039363534\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Sechi disc readings have been taken since 1992. Samples of suspended sediment have been collected from 2 sites since 1994 and turbidity probes / loggers have been used (with limited success) since 2002. The purpose of this data capture was to provide a long-term record of sediment load in the water column and water clarity for the site.\tSechi disc - visual record of depth before disc is lost from sight. Sechi disc data taken weekly. Passive sediment collectors - samples collected over 2 week period. Analysed for dry weight, organic content, trace metals and grain size.  Passive sediment collectors - samples are frozen and worked up every 4 years. A Seapoint OEM turbidity probe connected to an Idronaut data logger - optical measure of turbidity. YSI 6600 multiparameter sonde with optical turbidity probe replaced the Idronaut in 2007.  Turbidity probe - currently transmitting data to file every 10 mins.\t\tSkomer MNR West Region drive/shared documents/data/oceanographic/sediment\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:23:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17643\tNRW_DS109658\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.057Z\t440\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCollated Zostera Seagrass Bed Habitat Boundaries for Wales\tTerfynau cynefinoedd Zostera ar gyfer Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039363538\tSeagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas, such as lagoons and channels, which are sheltered from significant wave action. The three species of Zostera that occur in the UK, Dwarf eelgrass (Zostera noltii), narrow-leaved eelgrass (Zostera angustifolia), and  eelgrass (Zostera marina),  are all considered to be scarce (present in 16-100 ten km squares) and have some protection through the EC Habitats Directive and is classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitat. Zostera beds is also on the OSPAR List of threatened and/or declining species and habitats. The OSPAR Convention is the Convention for the Protection of the Marine Environment of the North-East Atlantic. This is a collation of Zostera habitats mapped by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Phase 1 Intertidal Survey, Intertidal Monitoring Contracts, Fisheries Sensitivity maps as well as point sources derived from casework site visits, CCWs data holdings in Marine Recorder and Environment Agency Wales (EAW) Water Framework Directive (WFD) Monitoring Results. The purpose of this data capture was to create a spatial representation for Zostera habitat across Wales. This collation was carried out for reporting on the distribution of OSPAR Habitats in Wales.\tThe source data was recorded using Intertidal Phase 1 methodology, where the habitats were mapped using GIS against either OS maps or Aerial photographs. Extents mapped recorded during monitoring or casework were either created from GPS tracks or by digitising around waypoints taken in the field.  Areas collated for the Fisheries sensitivity maps were created by digitising around known data points using background maps or aerial photographs.  Additional beds were digitised from aerial photos. Where areas were mapped by more than one method, the source that provided the largest area was used as this provides an indications of the possible extent of the beds.  All Data collected and digitised by specialists.\thttp://www.magic.gov.uk/\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC OSPAR Seagrass Beds Point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC OSPAR Seagrass Beds Polygon.lyr  Master copy stored on S:\\Users\\lough_n\\OSPAR species and habitats\\Zostera Beds\\Currentlayer_2008 Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\Zostera beds\tMetadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team Custodian: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:25:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17632\tNRW_DS109736\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.056Z\t375\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey of Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373336\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) is the basis used for the selection of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and is used to define Annex 1 habitats for the selection of Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) under the Habitats Directive (EU Directive 92/43).  This project comprises of detailed data related to the Phase 2 plant community survey for Lowland Heath across Wales. The project covers dry and maritime heath covered by the heathland section of the NVC and wet heath covered by the section of the NVC. Two non-NVC communities the calcareous grass and heath, and shingle heath are also described.  This project had three main purposes: - To provide an overview of the Welsh lowland heathland resource allowing local conservation programs particularly local Biodiversity Action Plans (BAP) partnerships, to assess and interpret their resource in a Welsh context.  - To identify heathland types which are underrepresented in the SSSIs series.  - To identified gaps in knowledge and highlight areas for further study.\tA total of 13 surveys were carried out: Anglesey (1994), West Gwynedd (1993), Gwynedd and Anglesey (1994), Pembrokeshire (1995, 1996, 1997), Rivers Ystwyth and Rheidiol, Ceredigion (1995), Gower Commons (1997), Gower, Bridgend, Vale of Glamorgan, Conwy (1998), Powys (1999), Brecknock and Carmarthenshire (2000), Ceredigion (2001) and Gower (2001). The total area of heath surveyed is approximately 4714 ha which is 35% of the total area of lowland and coastal (maritime) heathland recorded in the phase 1 habitats survey of Wales (1979- 1997). This was one of a series of major Phase 2 habitat surveys, including Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in-house and commissioned surveys covering woodland, lowland grassland, saltmarsh and lowland peatland.  The paper maps in the reports are of better quality than the GIS layer and therefore should be referred to as a more accurate source of information. Age of the data is also a limitation as habitat composition may change over time, a record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).  The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type. The species data has been digitised and entered into Recorder 6.\t\t[A] Paper reports for each survey available from CCW library (see CCW library catalogue/related titles field for more information). Gower 2001 survey available as hardcopy in CCW heathland ecologist\u0027s office.  [B] Species data held in Recorder 6 - surveys tagged as Phase 2 Lowland Heathland  [C] GIS data held on X Drive: X-Layer_Files\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_heathland - see GIS metadata in the contains field [D] H:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\SherryJ\\Lheathland\\Survey\\LHS original quadrat data  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tIn the absence of a Heathland Ecologist Liz Howe has suggested that Peter Jones is the best contact; Peter Jones, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW (The old custodian was ex-CCW Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:25.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17645\tNRW_DS109743\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.077Z\t491\tValid\tEditable\tAcross Wales Diving Monitoring Project (2004)\tProsiect Gwaith Monitro Deifio ar draws Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373433\tThe initial 2 year project was a developmental trial for establishing  (i) Methods,  (ii) Recording protocols.  (iii) Deployment strategies,  (iv) Repeatability,  (v) Site marking and relocation,  (vi) Quality Control for site monitoring.   Following on from the initial 2 year project a longer-term project has been established to gather data in order to assess the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) sites for their \u0027favourable conservation status\u0027.  The concept of favourable conservation status is central to the EC Habitats Directive. These SAC sites included five marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) around the Welsh coast though only four of these (Menai Strait and Conwy Bay; Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau; Cardigan Bay; and Pembrokeshire Marine) have \u0027reefs\u0027 listed as Annex I \u0027features\u0027. An ongoing programme of monitoring the reefs in each of these SACs follows the establishment programme.\tA variety of methodologies has been designed for monitoring subtidal reef across the SACs, these include diver quadrats and transects, capture of information on images and digital video and the collection of data from loggers. Detailed information about each of the methodologies can be found in the draft CCW Marine Monitoring Report 25b \u0027Across Wales Diving Monitoring Project Volume 2: Standard operating procedures\u0027.   CCW undertook a programme of surveillance on the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC between 1997 and 2001.  These monitoring trials formed the basis of the Terms of Reference for this programme.  Further information can be found in the Marine Monitoring Handbook (Davie et al. 2001). Metadata for repeatable sites is described separately.  An unrepeated groud-truthing survey was also completed for Cardigan Bay in 2004 in conjunction with the 2004-2005 Across Wales drop-down video survey.  Information about the survey can be found in the CCW Marine Monitoring Report 25a \u0027Across Wales Diving Monitoring Project Volume 1: Site descriptions and results\u0027.\t\tData is stored in Marine Recorder. Data is archived  (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\) as project 54 media 335-353; and projects 215-225, 229, media numbers 581-591 and 598 and in the fireproof safe but currently the data is held by contractors and on CD.  Video data on the Z marine archive drive (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Video), still images on DVD.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Biologist, NRW.  Contact is probably Harry Goudge or Lucie Haines.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T10:30:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17630\tNRW_DS109749\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.049Z\t698\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCockle and Mussel Stock (bird food) Surveys in the Dee, Traeth Lafan and Burry Inlet 2004\tArolygon ar Gocos a Misglod (bwyd adar) yn Afon Dyfrdwy, Traeth Lafan a Porth Tywyn, 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373439\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPAs) are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive), and for regularly occurring migratory species. The Dee Estuary, Traeth Lafan and Burry Inlet SPA support nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders as well as important cockle fisheries. The removal of cockles and the associated disturbance caused by fishing them needs to be balanced with bird food requirements. The aim of the project is to assess the food requirements of wading birds, such as oystercatcher and knot that over-winter in these areas in order to set monitoring targets and to assess the implications of cockle and mussel fishing scenarios to the bird populations. The purpose of this data capture was to  provide data on stock abundance and distribution across the whole of the  survey areas. The data collected and its results will primarily be used for site monitoring and also for casework. Longer term work on the Burry Inlet may combine this data with data gathered on bird foraging behaviour, together with site specific data on bird numbers and used in an individual behaviour based model to predict the food requirements of a bird population.\tThe 2004 cockle and mussel survey was contracted to Aquatic Survey and Monitoring Ltd., who sub-contracted individuals to make up the survey teams in North Wales and South Wales.  Three areas were surveyed - the Dee Estuary, Traeth Lafan and the Burry Inlet.  Over 400 cockle survey stations (on a grid system) were surveyed in each area, using GPS, 0.1m2 quadrats and a sieve of 2 mm mesh.  Cockles retained in the sieve were measured and counted; enabling the production of cockle density maps and estimates of the total cockle populations of each area.  Mussel beds in each survey area were also surveyed in a similar way, and all mussels over 10 mm length in each 0.1m2 quadrat were measured and counted.  This enabled the estimation of the total mussel populations of each bed. The full analysis of the data will be the subject of a later report in CCWs Marine Monitoring Report Series (West et al. 2004).  All data was collected by staff with a background in Biological Sciences using rigid rules laid out in the report methodology.  All data entry was subject to auto-validation and conditional formatting.  A copy of the data was checked regularly by the project manager for errors.  The data was put through a rigorous series of verification procedures to ensure that there were no inconsistencies between the field records, the shellfish measurements and the sub-sample records.  The field notebooks and recording sheets were also supplied to the project manager and numerous records were checked for accuracy of the data entry.\tAll data is published in the CCW Marine Monitoring Report no. 16.\tAssociated data is held in the Marine Archive as Project No. 212, Media 151,577,578\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:26:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17641\tNRW_DS109752\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.065Z\t599\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGround and Aerial Monitoring of Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra) in Carmarthen Bay Spatial Protection Area (SPA) (2002 - 2008)\tMonitro ar y tir ac o\u0027r awyr o\u0027r for-hwyaden ddu Melanitta nigra yn AGA Caerfyrddin, 2002 ymlaen\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373532\tCarmarthen Bay Spatial Protection Area (SPA) was classified as the UK\u0027s first SPA in 2003 on the basis of holding at least 1% (16,000 individuals) of the biogeographic population of Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra). The Common Scoter is strongly migratory and often travels considerable distances over land making brief stop-overs on inland waters. Its diet consists predominantly of molluscs, especially during the winter, although it occasionally takes other aquatic invertebrates such as crustaceans (e.g. barnacles and shrimps), worms, echinoderms, isopods, amphipods and insects (e.g. midges and caddis flies) as well as small fish and fish eggs which are found in abundance in the shallow water in Carmarthen Bay.  Common scoter are also listed as a Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) has supported ground based counts since 1996, following the Sea Empress oil spill.  Aerial surveys have also been employed as scoter often occur out of sight of land.  This dataset is compiled from data collected by The British Trust for Ornithology with support from CCW from 2002 onwards. The purpose of this data capture was to maintain a monitoring program using a combination of ground-based and aerial surveys to assess numbers and distribution of common scoter in Carmarthen Bay and to investigate whether ground-based counts provide an appropriate index of overall numbers of scoter within Carmarthen Bay as well as update the current index.\tThis is a collation of ground and aerial common scoter count surveys. At each site and on each date, the time of count, weather (wind-speed, wind direction, precipitation, cloud cover), sea-state and any disturbance was recorded.  Ground counts are made on 4 dates in the winter. Counts are carried out at three stations along the coast. In some years a fourth station at Rhossili is also used.  At each site weather conditions, sea-state and any disturbance are recorded.  Full sea-scans are then carried out. For each flock, the number of birds, their bearing and distance from shore was recorded.  Aerial surveys are performed using the \u0027distance\u0027 method based on statistical principles of distance sampling .  The surveys are carried out in winter and follow pre-determined transect lines running north-south through the bay.  Transects are spaced 2 km apart to minimise chances of double-counting birds flushed by the plane.   Surveys are carried out by 2 observers using a Partenavia PN68 aircraft, flown at ground speed 200 km/h, altitude 75m.  Surveys cannot be carried out in wind speeds greater than 24 km/h.  Each flock or individual bird was recorded along with time of observation, number and species.  Also distance was recorded to one of four distance bands as described in the standards.This dataset only contains work carried out by the British Trust for Ornithology (BTO) under contract to CCW.\tA sustained program of monitoring scoters in Carmarthen bay has been in place since 1997 (Stewart et al, 1997, Cranswick et al, 1998, WWT 2003).\tData is archived under project no.40, media no. 219 (data for 2002-2004), project No. 162, media 491 (data for 2004-2007) Project No. 165, media no. 494 and Project No. 205,media 570 (data for 2007-2008).\tInternal Custodians:  - Marine Ornithologist, NRW - Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:27:34.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17644\tNRW_DS109756\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.081Z\t315\tUnassessed\tEditable\tExtent and Distribution of Saline Lagoons in Wales (2009)\tMaint a dosbarthiad lagynau hallt yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373536\tCoastal saline lagoons are an unusual and rare habitat in the UK, where they are recognized as a \\key\\\" habitat under the Biodiversity Action Plan. The EC Council Habitats Directive on the conservation of natural habitats and of wild fauna and flora identified coastal lagoons as a priority habitat. Work carried out identified 14 saline or potential saline lagoons and ponds in Wales four of which are identified as of high conservation merit.  The extent of these lagoons have been mapped . A secondary layer showing the extent of other pools that were investigated is also available. The purpose of this digitisation was to provide one collated layer showing the extent and distribution of saline lagoons in Wales. This layer may also be used as the basis for monitoring the extent of saline lagoons and therefore as a contribution to an assessment of their conservation status and to provide one collated layer showing the extent and distribution of saline lagoons in Wales. This layer may also be used as the basis for monitoring the extent of saline lagoons and therefore as a contribution to an assessment of their conservation status. This layer was created in January 2009 based on information provided by CCW Contract Science Report No. 464 (see related titles).\"\tThe lagoon boundaries have been digitised from OS Mastermap (2008) or where the boundaries were not available from COWI-Vexcel aerial photos (2006), flown at 40cm resolution.  The collated dataset topologically was cleaned and checked.  The lagoon boundaries have been digitised from OS Mastermap (2008) or where the boundaries were not available from COWI-Vexcel aerial photos (2006), flown at 40 cm resolution.\t\tH:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working\\Saline Lagoons. H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\XMarine Habitats GIS working\\Saline Lagoons.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:28:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17629\tNRW_DS109760\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.035Z\t747\tValid\tEditable\tAcross Wales Sea Bottom Temperature Time Series Data\tData Cyfres Amser Tymheredd Gwaelod y Mor ar draws Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039373630\tTemperature loggers have been placed at sites across Special Areas of Conservations (SACs) in Wales to measure the bottom sea temperature.  The data is collected continuously but is processed to give the daily mean temperature as well as yearly averages. This data is collected as part of the Across Wales Diving Monitoring Survey.   Additional sea temperature recordings are taken at Skomer Marine Conservation Zone but these are covered in a separate metadata entry.  Water temperature is also recorded in saline lagoons at Cemlyn and Pickleridge.   The data are captured as part of the Across-Wales Diving Survey, designed to gather information on Reef features across the Marine SACs in Wales.  Gathering physical data such as temperature, provides context and background information for the condition assessment of each of the reef features monitored.  For example, biogenic reefs and many of the species that occur around the reefs in Wales may be sensitive to fluctuations in temperature.    The data collected may provide supporting evidence for any recorded changes in species composition on sites, particularly where species are known to be at the edge of their biogeographical range.  All information about the sites is in kept the Temperature loggers site location document.\tThe temperature loggers are part of a wider network of loggers in Wales that include Skomer MNR and the Saline Lagoons.  All data will also be made available through British Oceanographic Data Centre.    All the temperature data is recorded by either Vemco Minilogs, Tiny Talk Temperature or Starr Oddi Salinity and Temperature loggers.  As each logger is deployed a record is kept of which site it has been deployed to and how often the logger has been reused.  No logger is deployed more than a maximum of 5 times.  Loggers are recovered every year (usually in May) but this is dependant on weather conditions and diver availability.  All loggers are placed at a fixed location on the seabed.  These are marked by bolts, pitons or attached to Monitoring Station Markers (e.g. Road Pins \u0027Pyramid\u0027).  The logger records the Date, Time (hh/mm/ss) and Temperature (oC) at 3 hour intervals.  For each logger, metadata is recorded, including the serial number of the logger and the date and times the logger was deployed and recovered.    Temperature Loggers are placed at the following Locations in Wales:   - North Llyn Modiolus (Pyramid) Reef Site 1 (monitoring station marker)  - Pen Cristen, Bardsey Island (piton) (from 2000)  - St Tudwels (East) (\u0027Road Pin\u0027 pyramid) (from 2002)  - Sarn Badrig Pyramid (monitoring station marker)  - Ynys Moelfre (eye bolt)  - Beggars Reach, Milford Haven (eye bolt) (from 2007)  - \u0027Warrior\u0027, Milford Haven (eye bolt)  - Thorn Island, Milford Haven (eye bolt)  - Chapel Rock, Milford Haven (eye bolt)  - Holyhead Marina (from 2009)  - North Llyn (From 2003)  - Sarn Badrig (From 2006)\tConfidence in this data is high. All recordings are automated and all loggers are pre-calibrated.  Gaps do occur within some of the time series datasets where the loggers have been lost or damaged.\t[A]  Data between 2000 and 2015 is archived in media no. 590 and the master spreadsheet held on Ffynnon.    [B]  Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG shared drive at H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Temperature \u0026amp; salinity loggers  The archived data may not be complete, contact the internal contact for more information on the most up-to-date data.\tCharlie Lindenbaum, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T17:29:12.897Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17638\tNRW_DS109800\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.076Z\t300\tValid\tEditable\tNon Native Species Data - Common Slipper Shell (Crepidula fornicata) around Wales\tData crynodeb Crepidula fornicata\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383030\tThe dataset contains a series of records of the non-native slipper limpet Crepidula fornicata, around Wales.   The records have been extracted from the Phase 1 Intertidal survey from target notes and site species lists and collated with ad-hoc records recorded by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff or supplied by third parties, along with records extracted from CCW\u0027s Marine Recorder data holdings. The purpose of this collation is to monitor the spread of non-native marine species across Wales.\tThis is a collation of recorder therefore methods differ depending on the individual source survey, e.g. Phase 1 Intertidal methodologies, Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR), Phase 2 methodologies, Marine monitoring methodologies. This dataset is maintained and added to as an excel spreadsheet by MFSG staff within NRW. A GIS layer is generated from the excel spreadsheet and from Marine Recorder when an update is required.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Invasive Crepidula Fornicata.lyr  Most Current Data: S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Species Records - New\\Non-native records\\AlienCrepidula\\MostCurrentData.\tData Custodian: Intertidal Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T10:32:19.130Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17640\tNRW_DS109802\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.943Z\t635\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Species\tCam 1 Rhynglanwol Morol: Rhywogaethau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383032\tThis is a GIS dataset containing Phase 1 level intertidal species index (point data). The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005. All work has been undertaken by Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team.   During the survey species were recorded in one of three ways:  1) in target notes,   2) in species lists for certain (mainly specialised and nationally important) biotopes found on a site, or  3) in species list for a whole site therefore precision is very variable.   Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.\tField survey and manual digitising derived from hand drawn maps. To create the dataset the data was initially extracted from the Phase 1 survey database in a NBN format (CCWIntertidalSpeciesNBNExtract.ndb) and used to generate this MapInfo layer. It has subsequently been transformed so that it is now compatible with ArcGIS. The species dictionary was used to search the notes field in the TargetNotes table and species records created for these points. Within a survey site, all biotope polygons identified as the same were combined so that an overall width and height and a centre X,Y could be obtained. These were then combined with the SpeciesInBiotope table to obtain best location and precision for each record. The SpeciesFoundAtSite table was used to add any species not already added, in this case the centre of the site and the site extent were used as location and precision.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Species.lyr .   Archived as project 204, media 569.\tCustodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:38:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17633\tNRW_DS109803\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.036Z\t500\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Access Points\tCam 1 Rhynglanwol Morol: Pwyntiau Mynediad\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383033\tThis is a static spatial dataset. The dataset contains details on access points used during the phase 1 intertidal survey, including site ID, OS grid references, access type, status e.g. public/private, contact details of landowners and notes.  Phase 1 intertidal surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.  This particular dataset provides information on access points used by the surveyors to access the various sites across Wales.\tThis data was captured using field surveys and manual digitising from hand drawn maps.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Access points.lyr    Archived as project 204, media 569 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 501-600\\Media 569).\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:39:42.365Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17637\tNRW_DS109804\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.049Z\t253\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Intertidal Area of Wales\tCam 1 Rhynglanwol Morol: Ardal Rynglanwol Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383034\tThis is a static spatial dataset.  The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005. All work has been undertaken by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team. Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications. This particular dataset maps the whole intertidal area of Wales as a polygon to calculate total area.\tField survey and manual digitising from hand drawn maps.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal Data is archived as project 204, media 569 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 569)\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  Internal Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:15:32.124Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17636\tNRW_DS109814\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.051Z\t318\tValid\tEditable\tLower Limit of Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Survey\tTerfyn Isaf Arolwg Cam 1 Rhynglanwol Morol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383134\tThis is a GIS dataset line dataset depicting an approximation of the Lowest Astronomical Tide, through digitising along a combination of the OS 1:50,000 intertidal boundary and the lowest survey points of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) marine intertidal phase 1 mapping survey. The purpose of this data capture was to define the lowest boundary of the intertidal zone, which has relevance when defining Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) boundaries for intertidal features. All SSSI\u0027s designated after the CCW Phase 1 mapping survey use this as their lowest limit.\tDerived from the OS MasterMap intertidal lowest boundary and the CCW marine intertidal Phase 1 mapping survey. The CCW survey provides information on communities within the intertidal zone as a seamless layer. The lowest boundary of the survey, completed during spring tides, approximates to the lowest astronomic tide in most cases, although other factors influence the tide heights such as freshwater input in estuaries and storm surge. The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005. All work has been undertaken by CCW\u0027s in house intertidal survey team.  Production of the Lowest Astronomical Tide (LAT) dataset was completed by CCW cartographic unit.     The combination of OS features (dated pre year 2001) and CCW mapped features (between 1996 and 2005) does not give an actual boundary, but an indication of some of the possibilities of where the boundary may lie. In dynamic locations, such as estuaries and sedimentary embayments, changing sandbanks and channels makes for an unrealistic LAT. The lowest boundary of the CCW survey, completed during spring tides, approximates to the lowest astronomical tide in most cases, although other factores influence the tide height such as freshwater input in estuaries and storm surge.\t\tLegacy CCW Archive (File path: Z\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Hydrographic\\LAT-revised.TAB\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:42:14.311Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17628\tNRW_DS109815\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.862Z\t559\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Climate Change Monitoring (MarClim)\tMonitro Newid Hinsawdd Rhynglanwol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383135\tThe MarClim project was established in 2001 to investigate changes that had occurred in rocky intertidal systems within the last 50 years around the UK. MarClim established a low-cost network of sites covering England, Wales and Scotland which provided subsequent annual updates to track how climate influences the marine biodiversity of the British Isles.   This dataset consists of the Welsh coverage of MarClim surveys only. Since 2005, Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has continued working with the MBA, through a Partnership Agreement. The annual survey in Wales forms part of a longer, thirteen-year continuous UK survey. Geographical coverage includes sites throughout north, mid and southwest Wales for which historical data dating back to the 1950s exist, and additional sites where range extensions have been predicted to occur. Thirty seven sites were surveyed in north Wales and seven sites in south Wales. MarClim has been highlighted in a global scientific assessment of the need for scientific research to be appropriately designed to provide fit-for-purpose information at relevant spatial and temporal scales useful to managers of protected areas, statutory bodies and policymakers.    The main aims at the outset of the MarClim project in 2001 remain as follows: - To use existing historical information and collect new data on intertidal indicator species from the last 50-100 years to develop and test hypotheses on the impact of climatic change on marine biodiversity in Britain and Ireland.  - To forecast future marine community changes on the basis of the Met Office’s Hadley Centre climate change models and the United Kingdom Climate Impacts Partnership’s climate change scenarios. The broad range of species known or likely to be temperature sensitive was covered.  - To establish low-cost, fit-for-purpose, methodologies and networks to provide subsequent regular updates and track how climate influences the marine biodiversity of Britain and Ireland.  - To provide general contextual time series data to support reporting on the success or otherwise of the Marine Strategy Framework Directive, marine aspects of Biodiversity Action Plans, European initiatives including the Habitats, Birds and Water Framework Directives (through appropriate Regulations), and management and monitoring of marine activities and resources, including fisheries and Special Areas of Conservation.  - To evaluate whether the climate indicator species used in this work have a wider contribution to make as part of the sustainability indicators that are needed to underpin the UK sustainable development strategy.  - To disseminate the results widely, and accordingly elucidate the known impact climate has had on marine biodiversity over the last 100 years, and may have in the future.  - To provide a basis for the development of a proposal for European Commission funding to establish a pan-European network with related aims.  - To assess and report on the likely consequences of the predicted changes in response to climate for society, for commercial and non-commercial users of the marine environment and the policies and frameworks that conserve, manage and protect marine biodiversity.\tStandard protocol is used to evaluate populations of a fixed list of species (broad scale survey), population profiles of Phorcus (Osilinus) lineatus, Steromphala (Gibbula) umbilicalis and Patella spp. and photographs of barnacles for analysis of barnacle species proportions. Surveys are carried out by staff from the MBA, NRW HQ and  Skomer staff. Records were based on a fixed list of key species chosen for their relevance to climate change (southern and northern distribution, non-native and, characterising species).  Semi-quantitative SACFOR abundance scores are recorded for a suite of 87 species of invertebrates and macroalgae, including nineteen non-native  species.\tThe MarClim time-series dataset was developed by Mieszkowska, Burrows and Hawkins of the MarClim team as Good Environmental Status Indicators for the MSFD, with the full report published in 2014: http://jncc.defra.gov.uk/page-6813.   The MarClim time-series is being adopted by the INSHORE International Network for the Study of Rocky Shore Ecosystems as the global standard http://rockyinshore.org/about/.    The MarClim project draws on data from A.J. Southward 1950-1987, S.J. Hawkins 1980-2001, Rocky Shore Surveillance Group - R. Bowman, M.A. Kendall and J.R. Lewis 1964-1987, Anglesey Coastal Surveillance Unit - E. Jones CCW 1974- 1984 and R. Herbert 1993- 2001.\tThe reports and data are archived as Project No 262 to 267 and 303, Media No 958 to 962, 964, 1215 and 1409 at CCW headquarters.   A working copy of the data is held at: H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intertidal Climate Change. Since 2013, data and reports are held on the NRW Document Management System\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tMarine Biological Association (MBA)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-03-31-2014-03-31\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-31T09:42:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17627\tNRW_DS109818\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.789Z\t648\tUnassessed\tEditable\tShellfish Stocks, Oystercatcher and Knot (bird) Modelling Data for the Burry Inlet and Three Rivers (2004-2007)\tData rhagfynegol yn ymwneud a chregynbysgod, piod mor a phibyddion yr aber (adar) ar gyfer Porth Tywyn a\u0027r Tair Afon (2004-2007)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383138\tThe Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) supports nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders which feed on the mudflats and sandflats over the winter period. Oystercatcher feed predominantly on cockles, and knot feed on a range of prey including cockle spat.  CCW commissioned a series of studies to determine the food requirements of oystercatcher and knot, to develop conservation objectives, monitoring targets and to assess the implications of cockle and mussel fishing scenarios on the bird populations. The latest of which is published as CCW Marine Monitoring Report no. 65. The purpose of this project was to extend previous modelling to incorporate both the Burry Inlet and Three Rivers in order to determine the inter-reliance of the sites and how environmental change (e.g. changes in shellfishing, habitat loss, changes in human disturbance, climate change and changes in population size) affects foraging animal populations.  This information will be used to help inform management of the SPA.\tAn individual behaviour-based model (IBM) is used to predict the food requirements of a bird population. The model requires information on bird foraging behaviour together with site-specific data on bird numbers, and the size and distribution of cockle and mussel stocks.   The following datasets were collated for the model.   1. Bird population size in the Burry Inlet and Three Rivers 2. Long-term shellfish stocks in the Burry Inlet 3. Detailed shellfish stock estimates in the Burry Inlet and Three Rivers 4. Identification of cockle patches in the Burry Inlet and Three Rivers 5. Shellfish removed by shellfishing in the Burry Inlet and Three Rivers This data was collected under different projects.  The methodologies on how this data was collected is available elsewhere (e.g. Moore, 2009, Banks et al., 2007) The latest version of the model MORPH is used.  MORPH tracks  the foraging location, body condition and ultimate fate of each individual within an animal population. The modelled data builds on work carried out by Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (Dorset) that modelled shellfish stocks, oystercatch and knot for the Dee Estuary, Traeth Lafan and Burry Inlet in.  Previous data is archived in project 158 (media no 103 and 486). This work is now undertaken by Bournemouth University.\t\tAll the data is archived in project 158 and media no. 594. A copy is held on the MFSG shared drive with the report.\tInternal Contact: Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-02-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:41:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17626\tNRW_DS109822\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.573Z\t491\tUnassessed\tEditable\tShellfish Stocks, Oystercatcher and Knot (bird) Modelling Data for the Dee Estuary, Traeth Lafan and the Burry Inlet and Three Rivers, Phase 2 (2003-2004)\tData rhagfynegol yn ymwneud a chregynbysgod, piod mor a phibyddion yr aber (adar) ar gyfer Afon Dyfrdwy, Traeth Lafan a Phorth Tywyn a\u0027r Tair Afon, Cam 1 (2003-2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383232\tThe Dee, Traeth Lafan and Burry Inlet are designated Special Protection Areas (SPAs) for nationally and internationally important populations of wintering wildfowl and waders.  To set conservation objections and assess the sites for Favourable Conservation Status, information is required on numbers of birds using the sites and the food supplies they require to survive the winter and attain adequate body condition for Spring Migration  This is phase 2 of a project that was started in 2002-03 and uses existing and new data to parameterise an individual behaviour-based model to answer key questions relating to target setting, condition monitoring and management of each of the three sites. The purpose of this data capture was to inform the setting of conservation objectives for the SPAs and assess the favourable conservation status of bird populations.\tAn individual behaviour-based model (IBM) is used to predict the food requirements of a bird population. The model requires information on bird foraging behaviour together with site-specific data on bird numbers, and the size and distribution of cockle and mussel stocks.  The following datasets were collated for the model 1. Ash Free Dry Mass analysis data files (excel spreadsheets) 2. Cockle and mussel survey data (excel spreadsheets) 3. Average daily temperatures (text files) Information on cockle and mussel stocks was collected by CCW for the 3 sites for 2003 and 2004 under a separate project. This builds on a scoping study (phase 1) that was undertaken in 2002-2003 that examined existing data to determine what additional data were required for the model and to determine the best methods for stock assessment.  Data from Phase 1 is archived as project no 158 (Media 103).\t\tThe data associated with this project is archived as Project 158 (Media number 486).  A working copy is also held on the MFSG shared drive.\tInternal Custodian: Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2007-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:42:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17631\tNRW_DS109823\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.038Z\t562\tUnassessed\tEditable\tShellfish Stocks, Oystercatcher and Knot (bird) Modelling Data for the Dee Estuary, Traeth Lafan and the Burry Inlet and Three Rivers, Phase 1 (1993-2001)\tData rhagfynegol yn ymwneud a chregynbysgod, piod mor a phibyddion yr aber (adar) ar gyfer Afon Dyfrdwy, Traeth Lafan a Phorth Tywyn a\u0027r Tair Afon, Cam 1 (1993-2001).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383233\tThe Dee, Traeth Lafan and Burry Inlet are designated Special Protection Areas (SPAs) for nationally and internationally important populations of wintering wildfowl and waders. In order to set conservation objectives and assess the sites for Favourable Conservation Status, information is required on numbers of birds using the sites and the food supplies they require to survive the winter and attain adequate body condition for Spring Migration. The three SPAs also support economically important cockle fisheries that may affect oystercatchers directly, by removing some of the food available to them and, indirectly, by displacing them from the best cockle beds to less suitable feeding areas.  This is phase 1 of the project that will assess and use existing data to run an Individual Behaviour-based Model (IBM) to answer key questions relating to target setting, condition monitoring and management of each of the three sites.  There were two main objectives to the project.  The first to assess the power of existing surveys to detect change in the cockle populations at each site and recommend possible improvements.  The second objective was to determine how each site was used by the populations of oystercatchers that roosted there.  The models can be used to establish the target baseline quality habitats in terms of invertebrate populations but also in terms of the ability of the site to support the bird populations for which it was designated and therefore establish parameters for longer term monitoring and to establish whether it is in favourable conservation status.\tThe Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH) developed and tested and an Individual Behaviour-based Model (IBM) that predicts the effects of changes in the food supply and the amount of disturbance on the body condition and the over-winter survival rate of the oystercatchers. The model requires information on bird foraging behaviour together with site-specific data on bird numbers, and the size and distribution of cockle and mussel stocks.  Cockle data was collated from the Environment Agency (EA), North West and North Wales Sea Fisheries Committee (NW and NWSFC), Centre for Environment, Fisheries and Aquaculture Science (CEFAS).  Bird data was collated from the British Trust for Ornithology (BTO).  Other information required to run the model, includes details of ash free weight, tide times, mean daily temperature and information on shellfish disturbance from the fisheries.  Cockle counts: Dee estuary, Traeth Lafan, Burry inlet, Wales  Oystercatcher counts: Dee estuary, Traeth Lafan, Burry Inlet, Alt Estuary, Mersey Estuary, Morecambe Bay, Ribble Estuary Cockle data from the Dee Estuary 1997-2002 Cockle data from Traeth Lafan 1994 - 2000 Cockle data from Burry Inlet 1993 - 2001 Oystercatcher counts (and counts for other bird species) Apr 1986 - Feb 2001 (monthly)\t\tThe data associated with this project is archived as Project 158 (Media number 103)\tInternal Custodian:  Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:42:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17634\tNRW_DS109830\t2024-05-28T12:28:17.053Z\t292\tValid\tEditable\tMarine intertidal Phase 1 Final Report and Analysis Data\tCam 1 Rhynglanwol Morol: Adroddiad Terfynol a Data\u0027r Dadansoddiad\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383330\tThis dataset contains analysis data in the form of excel spreadsheets (survey dates, contacts, recreational popular areas, details of sea defences etc) and MapInfo tables (biotope distribution- including rare, scarce, nationally important and specialised biotopes, biotope richness, mapping of northern and southern species, non native species, LBAP areas, bait digging and boulder turning locations, angling, man made substrata, intertidal designated sites etc). It also contains many images taken during surveying which are stored as JPEGs. These are made up of the results of a 10 year survey of the intertidal zone of Wales including detailed information on a wide variety of features and activities e.g. the distribution of habitats and species, non-native species, man-made substrata, bait collection. It provides comprehensive baseline data for monitoring change or for the identification of locations sensitive to certain anthropogenic impacts - a critical capacity in the context of climate change.    The data also provides information to support a notification programme for intertidal Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), thereby providing a series of intertidal designated sites across Wales.  It also underpins the selection of the internationally important Special Area of Conservation (SAC) site series.  Intertidal mapped data provides an integrated information resource for Local Authority structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications. The maps give important regional information about habitat and biotope distribution. They are used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments, assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), as a basis for the development of sensitivity mapping, and as a baseline for developing monitoring of intertidal features in marine Special Areas of Conservation (SAC).  Sites are mapped once and therefore are a \u0027snapshot\u0027 in time.\tSurvey methodology is based on \u0027Handbook for Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Biotope Mapping Survey\u0027. Biotopes are based on the JNCC report `Marine Nature Conservation Review: marine biotope classification for Britain and Ireland. Volumes 1 and 2. Version 97.06\u0027.   Working at a scale of 1:5,000, field survey is based on using a combination of aerial photographs and Ordnance Survey (OS) maps as a template on to which the substratum and biotopes (habitat and associated biological community) are drawn.   Features and other information that are not map-able are described in target notes, a site description provides general information on the whole site and further information on biotopes and species are recorded on the survey form.  Each survey site is validated by two surveyors who visited the site. A 3 stage validation process is carried out. First validation process includes checking that all survey visit details are correct, all target note information is correct and transferred to the database in the correct fields, all designations are described, all biotopes species lists are transferred to the database and included for the site, all photographs are sequentially labelled. Second validation ensures all spellings and grammar are correct. Third validation process ensures that spatial digital data matches the original paper map and database entries. This includes automating data entry for codes, cross-referencing record id numbers, biotopes, target notes and grid references. Automated checks are carried out on spatial datasets in MapInfo.\t\tData is archived as project 204, media 1423 (File path \u003d K:\\Archive\\Media 1401-1500\\Media 1423)\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2007-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:43:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17623\tNRW_DS109831\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.232Z\t342\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Database\tCam 1 Rhynglanwol Morol: Cronfa Ddata\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383331\tThis is a database of Marine intertidal Phase 1 survey data, which can be queried to provide information on biotope, species distribution, site details, uses and impacts, target notes and conservation interests. Following the completion of the intertidal project in 2007 the database was transferred to Apex software to be made available internally on the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) intranet. The intertidal phase 1 survey was undertaken to provide an integrated information resource for Local Authority structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications. The data collected is also used to provide regional information about habitat and biotope distribution, assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSIs), a basis of sensitivity mapping and in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIAs).    The database itself aims to provide a storage system to effectively store the survey results whilst providing a convenient way to view and manipulate the data collected. Data validated to ensure it\u0027s entered onto the database correctly. The database has been designed to keep data entry errors to a minimum e.g. majority of data entry are taken from drop down menus, percentage values must add up to 100%, many fields are mandatory, and duplicate entries are not allowed.   Each record in the database is checked against the data collection form by the data imputer and by a member of the survey team. A further validation is carried out when the data is cross checked with the digital map to ensure that the database and maps agree.\tSurvey methodology is based on \u0027Handbook for Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Biotope Mapping Survey\u0027. Biotopes are based on the JNCC report `Marine Nature Conservation Review: marine biotope classification for Britain and Ireland. Volumes 1 and 2. Version 97.06\u0027. Working at a scale of 1:5,000, field survey is based on using a combination of aerial photographs and Ordnance Survey (OS) maps as a template on to which the substratum and biotopes (habitat and associated biological community) are drawn. Features and other information that are not map-able are described in target notes, a site description provides general information on the whole site and further information on biotopes and species are recorded on the survey form.  The database is used for storage, display and manipulation of data collected during the survey and can be used to generate site reports. It is also possible to view and query cartographic data held by the GIS from within the database.  The database was developed in Microsoft Access and consists of a front end containing all the tools necessary for the user to input; edit; print and query the data, and the back end, containing only the data itself which is placed on a network server, where it is secure and backed up regularly.\t\tArchived at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\Phase1_Database\\Phase1Acc2k.mdb.   The database is available through the intranet.   Data is also archived as project 204, media 569 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to500\\Media 569 Also under construction on NRW intranet: https://apex.cyfoethnaturiolcymru.internal/apex/f?p\u003d116:1::::::  (see online resource)\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:44:21.204Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17624\tNRW_DS109832\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.276Z\t268\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcross Wales Bays and Banks Subtidal Soft Sediment Surveys\tArolygon ar Waddodion Meddal Islanwol ar draws Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383332\tThe aim of this data capture was to aid in the monitoring and condition assessment of subtidal soft sediment Annex I features in Wales.  Subtidal benthic soft sediment repeat sampling is carried out with Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) across Wales to monitor Large Shallow Inlets and Bays, and Estuaries and Sandbank features. Some of these data are collected in conjunction with the  Water Framework Directive invertebrate monitoring programme so other data from these locations or the surrounding area may be available upon request from the EA. Surveys took place in large shallow inlets and bays (Conwy Bay, Red Wharf Bay,  Cardigan Bay, St Brides Bay, Carmarthen Bay) and designated subtidal sandbank features in Wales. Benthic invertebrate samples are processed by laboratories that are members of the National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control (NMBAQC) scheme and random samples from each survey are also selected for audit by the NMBAQC contractor. Data entry into Marine Recorder database has not been validated.\tMonitoring work is carried out at the same sample station by the Environment Agency and Briggs Marine. Sampling is carried out every three years on in a rolling cycle. Previous projects that this work builds on was carried out by CCW. Data captured: Abundance (and in some cases biomass) of benthic invertebrates (per 0.1m2) and granulometry.  Supporting determinants are: National Grid References, depth of sample relative to chart datum, salinity (if survey is in an estuary) and sample depth (in grab). Digital images are also taken of each sample both in the grab and after sieving and these form part of the audit trail for each sample. All samples are collected using either a Day grab or a Long arm Van Veen grab (both 0.1m2). Benthic invertebrate samples are sieved over a 0.5 mm mesh, some samples have been split into separate 0.5 mm and 1 mm fractions in order to maintain comparability with historic data sets.\tThe data is also available through UKDMOS website; http://www.ukdmos.org/v_ukdmos_edios/result.asp\t[A]  All data is archived under project 240 (media no 763, 910, 1285, 1286, 1330, 1388).   [B]  A working copy is held on H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Subtidal\\Bays and Banks benthic invert data [C]  Associated Spatial Data for sampling locations are held on the X Drive: X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Wales monitoring points.lyr  [D]  Data is currently being entered into Marine Recorder by a contractor – Marine Ecosol\tThe internal data custodian is: Mike Camplin, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist \u0026amp; Marine Monitoring Team Lead, Evidence, Knowledge \u0026amp; Advice The metadata point of contact is: Data Services, NRW\tEnvironment Agency (EA)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:44:03.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17622\tNRW_DS109834\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.199Z\t712\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcross Wales Intertidal Monitoring Survey\tProsiect Gwaith Monitro Deifio ar draws Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383334\tThis dataset comprises of results from intertidal monitoring of Special Area of Conservations (SACs) that contain Wales is undertaken. Work is undertaken to enable reporting on a range of external obligations under Habitats Directive, Water Framework Directive and the inter-agency agreement on Common Standards Monitoring; to harmonise with WFD monitoring in coastal and transitional waters. The work will also be used to inform management decisions on individual sites.  Each sample station will be monitored for one of the following SAC sub-features;   1) Under-boulders   2) Sabellaria reef  (created by the honeycomb worm)  3) Rockpools   4) Characterizing rock or sediment habitat    5) Zostera beds  (seagrass meadows) 6) Nationally rare or scarce species. Work has been carried out from 05/07/2004 and is on-going.    The majority of sample stations are visited every 6 years, although some sites where there are particular pressures are visited annually, for example reef features in Pembrokeshire Marine SAC and Zostera beds at Traeth Lafan in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. Confidence in certain methods and their data is high, whilst the objectives of some surveys are to develop confidence in the method and data.\tRepeat monitoring sample locations were identified from baseline information provided by the Phase 1 Intertidal Mapping Survey. All stations are visited by foot.These sampling stations are available through UKDMOS.  All survey work was undertaken by IECS between 2004 and 2005 and reported by feature.  Surveys carried out in 2007 were undertaken by ASML and reported by SAC.  Monitoring methodologies for each of the sub-features are described in individual reports, available as part of the CCW Marine Monitoring Report series.\tInformation available at: http://www.ukdmos.org/v_ukdmos_edios/search.asp\tData is updated every 6 years  [A]  All data is archived under project 199.  [B]  Data for each feature is archived under separate Media numbers (Please refer to individual metadata entries).   [C]  Repeat sampling station locations is available on the UKDMOS website.   [D]  Species data is available through Marine Recorder.\tInternal custodian: Intertidal Survey Team Leader, MFSG, NRW.   Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:45:34.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17620\tNRW_DS109835\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.059Z\t282\tValid\tEditable\tTremadog Bay Subtidal Macrobenthic Study\tAstudiaeth Facrofenthig Islanwol Bae Tremadog\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383335\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Area of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the \u0027Favourable Conservation Status\u0027 (FCS) of habitats and species as defined in the Directive. In the case of SACs, the features to which FCS applies are the habitats and/or species listed in Annex I and Annex II of the Habitat Directive for which the individual site has been selected. Infaunal monitoring work is required to provide management feedback and enable reporting on the FCS of infaunal aspects of SACs, Special Protection Areas (SPAs: Birds Directive) and Special Sites of Scientific Interest (SSSI).    The aim of this data capture was to gather repeatable data to provide reference conditions and to support the identification of the two characteristics of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau large shallow inlet and bays feature.  Tremadog Bay a large shallow Inlet and Bay within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC.  Two important characteristics of this feature have been identified.  These are:   1. The variety of subtidal sediment communities within the large shallow inlet and bay  2. The species richness and diversity of the sublittoral mixed sediment south west of Pwllheli\tA total of 115 samples were obtained at 39 sites within Tremadog Bay over a 2 year period.  Analysis of samples produced a list of all benthic fauna (infauna and epifauna).  In addition sediment composition was described.  Biotopes were also assigned to each sample based on the classification of Connor et al, 2004.  The sample stations were designed on a grid system, with additional stations created on known mixed sediment areas and additional areas or particular interest.  Grab samples were collected with a 0.1m2 day grab.  2 or more samples were collected at each station, with 6 replicates taken in mixed sediment areas.    A granulometry sample was taken from one of the grabs. Grab samples are sieved on board the survey vessel, through a 0.5mm mesh sieve, Samples with a large amount of coarse material were put through a 1mm mesh sieve first to speed up the process.  The sieved material was then fixed on 10% formalin solution.  Macrofaunal and particle size distribution analysis of the grab samples was completed under contract to EMU Ltd.  Following the macrobenthic analysis, biotopes were assigned to each sampling station.\t\t[A]  The data is archived in Project no. 143 (media 469)- Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media401to500\\Media469. [B]  Species data is also held in Marine Recorder.   [C]  Specimens are stored at EMU.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Habitats Biologist, MFSG, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:45:56.057Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17621\tNRW_DS109859\t2024-05-28T12:28:16.080Z\t1049\tValid\tEditable\tNational Landscape Character Areas (NLCA)\tArdaloedd Cymeriad Tirwedd Cenedlaethol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383539\tThis dataset comprises of boundaries for the 48 regional landscape character areas for Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to geographically distinguish different regions in Wales in terms of landscape identity and what characteristics and qualities make one region different and distinct from another.    These distinctions and policy drivers for this project are described, together with an illustrated narrative, and published on Natural Resources Wales (NRW\u0027s) website. This dataset covers 100% of terrestrial Wales including inter-tidal areas.  Boundary data derived from source data 2007-2008 with the final version being amended October 2008. Some area names were amended in August 2023 to match written profile documents, and to reflect official status change for the names Eryri and Bannau Brycheiniog.\tBoundaries were created from an analysis of some 40+ existing environmental datasets plus a selection of site visits.  Of note, this work includes reference to both the physical environment and human perceptions of it.  Landscape is about the meeting of people and place, not just about place, as such, the boundaries were not derived mechanically from datasets but were also derived using some professional judgement, following site visits and preliminary work on mapping people\u0027s perceptions of how the Welsh landscape divides into \u0027broydd\u0027 (areas with a distinct sense of place that people commonly relate to).     Mapping scale was 1:250,000 - so this dataset has very generalised boundaries.  However, as the object of the exercise was to identify broad regions, concern over whether the boundary runs this side or that side of a road or field are not relevant within the scale of the regions being identified.  For example \u0027Vale of Clwyd\u0027, \u0027Gower\u0027, Montgomeryshire Hills and Vales\u0027.   Instead, boundaries should be assumed to approximate broad zones of transition from one landscape area to another.  Some landscapes presented strong physical features along which to run boundary lines, whereas other landscapes were found to be complex mosaics that gradually changed, or had subtle changes with few obvious single physical lines to follow.   The resources of the project did not allow for detailed resolution of such boundaries and short cuts, typically in the form of following a generalised contour line, have often been used to complete the exercise.  Islands are not included in the GIS dataset available to download, however they are included as contextual features within National Landscape Character Area (NLCA) description profiles and their map images.  Islands are encompassed within the equivalent characterisation of seascapes, being the National Marine Character Areas (NMCA), also available to download on DataMapWales.\tCCW Policy Research Report 07/19 explains the method in more detail. The perception related work (\u0027Heuristic Mapping\u0027) is described in CCW Policy Research report 08/11. This is the first published dataset. Land Use Consultants (LUC) produced a first GIS version dated August 2007, but CCW did not publish this, and it is now superseded by the published version here.\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\National Landscape Character Areas.lyr  Written reports held in paper form in NRW libraries.\tMetadata and Data Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-09-18\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/415/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_LANDSCAPE_CHARACTER_AREAS\t2026-01-12T15:33:54.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17619\tNRW_DS109860\t2024-05-28T12:28:15.984Z\t297\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features of the Braint Estuary 1996\tNodweddion morfa heli Aber Afon Braint\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383630\tThis dataset consists of the results of a National Vegetation Classification as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the Braint Estuary. The purpose of this data capture was to use the National Vegetation Classification to describe and map areas of saltmarsh on the Braint Estuary- put forward as a pSAC as part of the Glannau Mon: Cors Heli/ Anglesey Coast saltmarsh.\tStandard National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey methodology was used to collect this data. Saltmarsh communities were identified by eye based on paced, compass orientated transects and mapped against a NCC commissioned basemap produced by the University of Glasgow. The saltmarsh community identification was supported by sample quadrats. Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by CCW KMG, corrections made by contractors, validated again by CCW KMG, corrections made in-house in conjunction with relevant saltmarsh specialist. CCW commissioned survey which contributes to an NVC Saltmarsh survey collation (see \u0027part of\u0027 field).\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales    Z:\\Archive\\MFSG Data archive\\Media301to400\\Media388 Archived as Project No. 131, media 388\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:47:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17617\tNRW_DS109861\t2024-05-28T12:28:15.881Z\t490\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features at Cefni and Aberffraw 1998\tNodweddion morfa heli Afon Cefni ac Aberffraw\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383631\tThis dataset contains the results of a survey which used the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Anglesey (Cefni and Aberffraw). The purpose of this data capture is to use the National Vegetation Classification to describe and map areas of saltmarsh on the southern shore of Anglesey that were put forward as a potential Special Area of Conservation (pSAC) as part of the Glannau Mon: Cors Heli/ Anglesey Coast saltmarsh.\tStandard NVC survey methodology was used. Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by CCW KMG, corrections made by contractors, validated again by CCW KMG, corrections made in-house in conjunction with relevant salt marsh specialist.  CCW commissioned survey which contributes to an NVC Phase 2 Saltmarsh survey collation (see \u0027part of\u0027 field).\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media201to300\\Media234 Archived as Project 53, media 234\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1999-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:51:21.180Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17618\tNRW_DS109879\t2024-05-28T12:28:15.893Z\t621\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features at Burry Inlet and Loughor Estuary (1998)\tNodweddion morfa heli Porth Tywyn ac Aber Afon Llwchwr\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383739\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Burry Inlet and Loughor Estuary. The area comprises the most extensive site of saltmarsh vegetation in Wales. The purpose of this data capture is to provide detailed information on the distribution of the key elements for which the estuary was submitted for selection as a candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) including the extent of transitional zones, extent of saltmarsh communities and the distribution of the nationally scarce and locally distributed Marsh Mallow, Althaea officinalis. Another aim of the survey was to provide floristic data to enable the separation of the Mediterranean Saltmeadows from their more generally distributed counterparts.\tStandard NVC survey methodology. Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by CCW KMG, corrections made by contractors, validated again by CCW KMG, corrections made in-house in conjunction with relevant salt marsh specialist.  CCW commissioned survey which contributes to an NVC Saltmarsh survey collation (see \u0027part of\u0027 field).\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media201to300\\Media217 Data is held as Project No.52, media 217.\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:54:02.395Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17616\tNRW_DS109880\t2024-05-28T12:28:15.804Z\t262\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features of the Taf, Tywi and Gwendraeth Estuaries 1997\tNodweddion morfa heli Aberoedd Afon Taf, Afon Tywi ac Afon Gwendraeth\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383830\tThis dataset comprises of the results from a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the Taf, Twyi and Gwendraeth Estuaries- the \u0027three rivers complex\u0027. The work was carried out in preparation for notification of the saltmarshes in the complex as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC).\tStandard NVC survey methodology was used in this project. Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by CCW KMG, corrections made by contractors, validated again by CCW KMG, corrections made in-house in conjunction with relevant salt marsh specialist. CCW commissioned survey which contributes to an NVC Saltmarsh survey collation (see \u0027part of\u0027 field).\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media101to200\\Media178 Data is held as Project No. 32, media 178\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1998-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T11:56:19.334Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17675\tNRW_DS109881\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.690Z\t591\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features at Milford Haven 2002\tNodweddion morfa heli Aberdaugleddau 2002\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383831\tThis dataset is the results of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats at Milford Haven. The purpose of this data capture was to survey and map the saltmarsh units within the Milford Haven Waterway within the context of the National Vegetation Classification and to repeat the recording of the permanent quadrats established in 1996 to assess change in the floristics of these areas.    Other purposes include the erection of a series of new permanent quadrats within the eastern section of the estuary as a baseline for the monitoring of future changes, and to record and map the distribution of notable species of the saltmarshes and their associated vegetation. This dataset has been amalgamate with saltmarsh data from across Wales to form the Phase 2 Saltmarsh Survey data for Wales.\tStandard NVC survey methodology. Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Knowledge Management Group (KMG), corrections made by contractors, validated again by CCW KMG, corrections made in-house in conjunction with relevant salt marsh specialist.\t\tArchived as Project 132 Media 389.  X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh  Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW.          Although this dataset is 3rd party, we have decided not to include it as strictly 3rd party, because we were heavily involved in the validation process and we are a partner of the owner.\tMilford Haven Waterway Environmental Surveillance Group [MHWESG]\tNRW Conditional\t2003-03-14\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:16:21.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17673\tNRW_DS109882\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.673Z\t362\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features of the Severn Estuary 1998\tNodweddion morfa heli Aber Afon Hafren\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383832\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the Severn Estuary. The purpose of this data capture was to provide a detailed NVC description of Severn Estuary saltmarsh and an account of distribution and NVC associations of several nationally scarce and one locally scarce species. The survey also provided a preliminary conservation assessment for the Severn Estuary potential Special Area of Conservation (pSAC) and line transects as baseline data for future remote sensing studies.\tStandard NVC survey methodology. Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by NRW, corrections made by contractors, validated again by NRW, corrections made in-house in conjunction with relevant salt marsh specialist. NRW commissioned survey which contributes to an NVC Saltmarsh survey collation (see \u0027part of\u0027 field).\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media201to300\\Media214 Archived as project 130 media 214\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t1999-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:20:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17660\tNRW_DS109883\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.417Z\t384\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features of the Dee and Clwyd Estuaries 2000\tNodweddion morfa heli Aberoedd Afon Dyfrdwy ac Afon Clwyd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383833\tThis dataset contains the results from a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey used as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the Dee and Clwyd Estuaries. The purpose of this dataset was to provide systematic descriptions at the plant community level of saltmarsh and associated habitats in the Dee and Clwyd estuaries. The objective was to provide an essential basis for monitoring, facilitate management planning and improve our ability to evaluate development proposals.\tStandard NVC survey methodology. Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by CCW KMG, corrections made by contractors, validated again by CCW KMG, corrections made in-house in conjunction with relevant salt marsh specialist.  CCW commissioned survey which contributes to an NVC Saltmarsh survey collation (see \u0027part of\u0027 field).\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media101to200\\Media180 Data held as Project No. 129, media 180\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2001-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:21:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17664\tNRW_DS109884\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.372Z\t579\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features at Milford Haven 1996 - Sea Empress Survey\tNodweddion morfa heli Aberdaugleddau 1996 arolwg y Sea Empress\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383834\tThe Sea Empress released approximately 72,000 tonnes of crude oil and about 480 tonnes of heavy fuel oil in the period 15 to 21 Feb 1996 when grounded off Milford Haven and later while at the jetty.   The coastline within Milford Haven was heavily oiled and a major clean-up operation was mounted.  However, to avoid further damage, oil was not cleaned from the saltmarsh. Many of the affected saltmarshes in Milford Haven lie within Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and also form components of candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) under the Habitats Directive.  As part of the response to the oil spill, the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) commissioned a damage assessment survey of impacted saltmarshes in Milford Haven. Carry out an assessment of the possible damage to saltmarsh habitat and the associated scarce species Salicornia pusilla and Limonium humile affected by the Sea Empress oil spillage and to establish a base-line against which future changes may be kept under surveillance - this included mapping the saltmarshes using National Vegetation Classification (NVC) methodology. The survey also aimed to assess the levels of hydrocarbon pollution within the sediments of the affected saltmarshes.\tExamination of NRW colour aerial photographs taken shortly before the spill (1992 to 1995) to assess extent of saltmarsh vegetation at that time to compare with the extent of vegetation mapped after the spill. Consideration of possible impacts of oil known to have been deposited along particular stretches of the shore. Observation on the distribution of oil during fieldwork for the current survey.  However, the fieldwork started eight months after the spill and so some redistribution of oil would have occurred. A phase II NVC style survey of saltmarsh vegetation and the scarce species to provide a baseline for monitoring future change. NRW commissioned survey which contributes to an NVC Saltmarsh survey collation (see \u0027part of\u0027 field). Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by NRW, corrections made by contractors, validated again by NRW, corrections made in-house in conjunction with relevant salt marsh specialist.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Milford Haven 1996 Data also archived as project 47 media numbers 42, 54, 215 at  H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media001to100\\Media042 (report)   , H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media001to100\\Media054  ,    H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media201to300\\Media215\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:26:32.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17663\tNRW_DS109885\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.385Z\t643\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features at Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) - Glaslyn 1993\tNodweddion morfa heli ACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Safnau- Glaslyn 1993\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383835\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau - Glaslyn 1993.  The main objective of the survey was to use the National Vegetation Classification to describe and map the unusual brackish water saltmarsh communities on the seaward edge of the marsh as well as characterising some of the transitional vegetation types and the glycophytic communities on the middle and upper marsh.\tStandard NVC survey methodology. All the homogenous stands of different maritime vegetation were identified and mapped. A 4m2 quadrat was then used to estimate the cover abundance (Domin scale) of all taxa found within selected stands of each vegetation type. Computer software package Vespan II was used to store and handle the quadrat data.  Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by NRW, corrections made by contractors, validated again by NRW, corrections made in-house in conjunction with relevant salt marsh specialist.  NRW commissioned survey which contributes to an NVC Saltmarsh survey collation (see \u0027part of\u0027 field).\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Glaslyn 1993 Field Studies journal available at CCW Headquarters Library, Bangor. (see library catalogue) H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media533 Archived as project 190 media 533\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1994-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:36:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17668\tNRW_DS109886\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.548Z\t305\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaltmarsh features at Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) - Dyfi 1996\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383836\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau - Dyfi 1996. The main objective of the survey was to use the National Vegetation Classification to describe and map the unusual brackish water saltmarsh communities on the seaward edge of the marsh as well as characterising some of the transitional vegetation types and the glycophytic communities on the middle and upper marsh.  Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by NRW, corrections made by contractors, validated again by NRW, corrections made in-house in conjunction with relevant salt marsh specialist.\tStandard NVC survey methodology. All the homogenous stands of different maritime vegetation were identified and mapped. A 4m2 quadrat was then used to estimate the cover abundance (Domin scale) of all taxa found within selected stands of each vegetation type. Computer software package Vespan II was used to store and handle the quadrat data.  NRW commissioned survey which contributes to an NVC Saltmarsh survey collation (see \u0027part of\u0027 field)\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Dyfi 1996 H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media901to1000\\Media902 Archived as project 249 media 902\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1996-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:58.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17662\tNRW_DS109887\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.397Z\t363\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features at Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2003\tNodweddion morfa heli ACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau 2003\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383837\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau.  The purpose of the data capture was to map the location and extent of saltmarsh and associated vegetation types, to classify the stands in relation to the National Vegetation Classification, to apprise the quality of the vegetation and to produce information relevant to the completion of a conservation assessment of the constituent sites.\tStandard NVC survey methodology. Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by CCW KMG, corrections made by contractors, validated again by CCW KMG, corrections made in-house in conjunction with relevant salt marsh specialist. CCW commissioned survey which contributes to an NVC Saltmarsh survey collation (see \u0027part of\u0027 field).\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media201to300\\Media216 Archived as project 39 media number 216\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:40:22.219Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17658\tNRW_DS109888\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.170Z\t368\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features of the Conwy Estuary 2001\tNodweddion morfa heli Aber Afon Conwy\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383838\tThis dataset consists of the results from a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey used as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the North Conwy Estuary.  The principal objective of the survey was to provide a suitable base for future monitoring and also as part of CCW\u0027s commitment to reporting on the suitability of the estuary description as a Site of Special Scientific Importance (SSSI) and inclusion into the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay possible Special Area of Conservation (pSAC).\tStandard saltmarsh NVC survey methodology was used. Saltmarsh communities were identified by eye, supported by aerial photography, and mapped in the field onto 1:2,500 maps using the Ordnance Survey 1:10,000 Land Plan as a base map. Survey took place along transects, and boundaries were fixed using a hand held GPS to an accuracy of +/- 3m. Quadrats were recorded and species lists developed. Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by NRW, corrections made by contractors, validated again by NRW, corrections made in-house in conjunction with relevant salt marsh specialist. NRW commissioned survey contributes to an NVC Saltmarsh survey collation (see \u0027part of\u0027 field).\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Conwy 2001    H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media532 Archived as project 189 media 532\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:41:32.116Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17657\tNRW_DS109889\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.074Z\t237\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features at Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) - Dyfi 1993\tNodweddion morfa heli ACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau- Dyfi 1993\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383839\tUse of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau - Dyfi 1993. Dyfi Junction saltmarsh survey.   The main objective of the survey was to use the National Vegetation Classification to describe and map the unusual brackish water saltmarsh communities as well as characterising some of the transitional vegetation types and the glycophytic communities present.\tStandard NVC survey methodology. All the homogenous stands of different maritime vegetation were identified and mapped. A 4m2 quadrat was then used to estimate the cover abundance (Domin scale) of all taxa found within selected stands of each vegetation type. Computer software package Vespan II was used to store and handle the quadrat data.  Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by NRW, corrections made by contractors, validated again by NRW, corrections made in-house in conjunction with relevant salt marsh specialist. NRW commissioned survey which contributes to an NVC Saltmarsh survey collation.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Dyfi 1993 H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media901to1000\\Media903 Archived as project 250 media 903\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1993-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:44:10.924Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17672\tNRW_DS109891\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.582Z\t385\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcoustic Monitoring of the Extent of the North Llyn Modiolus Reef\tGwaith monitro acwstig ar y riff Modiolus yng Ngogledd Llyn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383931\tBiogenic horse mussel (Modiolus Modiolus) reef is an important feature of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC).  The reef has high biodiversity and is vulnerable to anthropogenic activities, such as benthic fishing.  The mussel reef forms an undulating surface that is significantly different to the surrounding seabed and therefore acoustic monitoring methods can be used to monitor changes in extent and identity areas of potential damage. Work is carried out on the north Llyn horse mussel reef  in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau jointly by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and University of Wales Bangor.  The purpose of this data capture was to: -  Measure extent and temporal changes within the of the Modiolus reef in the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC.   -  Investigate the health of the reef and for signs of anthropogenic activity within the area.   An area approximately 10km by 6km, adjacent to the Pen Llyn coast, south-west of Porth Dinllaen, approximately 1.5km off shore was surveyed.  All data is processed to reduce errors (e.g. positional corrections).  Acoustic data is subject to ground-truthing using drop down videos captured as part of a separate project.  As of April 1st, 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tSide scan-sonar data is captured from either the CCW research vessel (R.V. Pedryn) or the University research vessel (R.V.  Price Madog) using a CMAX CM800 sidescan sonar.  Multibeam has been captured from the University research vessel (R.V.  Prince Madog) in 2008 using a Kongsberg EM 3002 Dual Head multibeam echo sounder.   The reef and surrounding area was also multibeamed as part of the HABMAP project in 2005 on board the Irish Marine Institute\u0027s \u0027Celtic Voyager\u0027 using a simrad EM1002 95KHz multibeam Echosounder.  Concurrently, a sub-bottom profiling system, using a broadband high-resolution \u0027boomer\u0027 was also used in the same area. All data is processed and incorporated into MapInfo GIS. Analysis of the data is written up as unpublished MSc projects at the University of Wales Bangor.  Project is carried out in conjunction with the Across Wales drop-down video project.  Additional projects have been carried out to study the anthropogenic effects discovered at the site.\tPlease see CCW Marine Monitoring Report no.50. 01/07/1998 side-scan sonar monitoring trial . Data is also available online at http://www.ukdmos.org/v_ukdmos_edios/result.asp\tData from monitoring trial and subsequent 1999 surveys archived under media no. 004 and 005 Annual date collection from 2006 archived in project no. 233 (media 602). Analysis of the data from 2006 archived in project no. 234 (media 306)\tInternal Custodian is: Marine Monitoring Officer, MFSG, NRW. Metadata point of contact is: Data Services\tUniversity of Wales Bangor-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:46:44.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17674\tNRW_DS109893\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.688Z\t539\tValid\tEditable\tReview of Side-Scan Sonar Results from North Llyn Modiolus Reef\tAdolygiad o ganlyniadau gwaith monitro sonar yng Ngogledd Llyn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383933\tAn emergency review of side-scan sonar data collected by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in 2007 was required as the images showed marks that were potentially derived from trawling gear.  The area was approximately 10 km by 6 km and approximately 1.5 km offshore, within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and is also protected under Sea Fisheries byelaws that prevent the use of trawls and dredges for the harvesting of molluscan shellfish, except as authorised by the local sea fisheries committee.\tSide-scan images were collected by CCW as part of their annual monitoring of the reef. In addition to sides-scan sonar images, 3 dives were conducted in the area.  Digital high definition video footage from the dives was edited and slowed so they could be examined for evidence of disturbance. Maps of sediment types and waved and tidally induced bed-sheer stress for the locality were provided by the HABMAP project.  This project is based on annual side-scan sonar data collected by CCW as part of ongoing monitoring on the site.  Data for 2007 identified anthropogenic activities on the site that required further study.\tFor further information about the methodology used for acoustic monitoring, please refer to metadata entry \\Acoustic Monitoring of the North Llyn Modiolus reef - NRW_DS109891\\\". \"\tAll data is archived in project no.150 (DV tapes are archived in media 482 \u0026amp; 483, project data is archived in media 484).  The original side-scan sonar data is archived under project 233 (media 602).\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Officer, MFSG, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2007-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:45:16.014Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17659\tNRW_DS109898\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.354Z\t371\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of the Recreational Use and Conservation Value of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383938\tThe coal extraction industry has had a massive impact on the natural environment at a landscape level, the most obvious legacy of the operations being the extensive coal spoil tips apparent across the region. In recent years millions of pounds have been spent on the stabilisation and eventual reclamation of these spoil areas and their redevelopment for housing, new industry or local open spaces. In the past the potential ecological value of the tips has generally been overlooked or understated. However, old coal spoil sites are now recognised as an important habitat for a range of species and the few remaining sites in the Valleys also have cultural and community value, however general awareness of the value of these sites is low. This has resulted in a loss of site value in term of heritage, ecology, landscape and amenity and there was a need to put individual sites into a context. The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), conducted an integrated strategic assessment of the recreational use and conservation value of heathland and associated habitats on the coal spoils of South Wales.  This dataset is a collation of all biological data associated with 25 coal spoil sites in the South Wales valleys. The purpose of this data capture was to conduct a strategic assessment of conservation, recreation and education use and potential of these sites.  Specific aims included initial classification of coal spoil types, collation of species and habitat data to allow an initial assessment of conservation value, assessment of recreational and amenity use and assessment of the potential for enhancements.  The area covered by this work included the South Wales valleys. The current dataset covers Torfaen, Rhondda Cynon Taf, Caerphilly, Blaenau Gwent and Merthyr County Borough Council (CBCs).\tBiodiversity assessment carried out using a desk study methodology to collate data from South East Wales Biological records centre, Wildlife Trust of South and West Wales and other partners.  Recreation and amenity use and potential assessed by carrying out site visits to the 25 sites followed by discussion with the local authority ecologists.\t\ta) MapInfo tables on CD held by CCW HQ library (copy held by Jan Sherry) b) Report and data files held as paper copy and CD by CCW HQ library (copy held by Jan Sherry) c) Excel files on CD held by CCW HQ library (copy held by Jan Sherry)\tLiz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW Formerly Jan Sherry Heathland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17661\tNRW_DS109899\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.467Z\t283\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039383939\tThe coal extraction industry has had a massive impact on the natural environment at a landscape level, the most obvious legacy of the operations being the extensive coal spoil tips apparent across the region. In recent years millions of pounds have been spent on the stabilisation and eventual reclamation of these spoil areas and their redevelopment for housing, new industry or local open spaces. In the past the potential ecological value of the tips has generally been overlooked or understated. However, old coal spoil sites are now recognised as an important habitat for a range of species and the few remaining sites in the Valleys also have cultural and community value, however general awareness of the value of these sites is low. This has resulted in a loss of site value in term of heritage, ecology, landscape and amenity and there was a need to put individual sites into a context. The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), conducted an integrated strategic assessment of the recreational use and conservation value of heathland and associated habitats on the coal spoils of South Wales.  This dataset compiles of the results of an National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of coal spoil habitats on 15 sites in the South Wales valleys. It contains quadrat and species data for heath, grassland, mire and associated habitats and mapped habitat polygons.  The purpose of this data capture was to conduct a strategic assessment of the conservation value of coal spoil in the South Wales valleys to be used to identify important sites which require protection through the planning process and as possible use for designation of sites. It has also been used to aid in identifying action by Local Biodiversity Action Plans (LBAPs).  The area covered includes South Wales valley; Torfaen, Rhondda Cynon Taf, Caerphilly and Merthyr County Borough Council (CBCs).\tThe method of data capture used was quadrat survey using standard NVC methodology on the areas of coal waste selected for survey. Standard former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) GIS mapping of habitats. The 15 sites were chosen from the 25 identified during the 2005 Assessment of the recreational use and conservation value of heathland and associated habitats on the coal spoils of South Wales survey. There is some disagreement regarding NVC communities/species identification and some poor mapping and digitisation in this dataset.\t\ta) Paper report held by CCW HQ library, digital version on CD in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) b) MapInfo layers on CD in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) c) Excel files on CD  in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) d) Photographs on CD  in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry)\tLiz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW (Formerly Jan Sherry Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems/ Heathland Ecologist, NRW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2007-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17671\tNRW_DS109900\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.473Z\t349\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales. Phase II: Assessment of Environmental Parameters\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393030\tThe coal extraction industry has had a massive impact on the natural environment at a landscape level, the most obvious legacy of the operations being the extensive coal spoil tips apparent across the region. In recent years millions of pounds have been spent on the stabilisation and eventual reclamation of these spoil areas and their redevelopment for housing, new industry or local open spaces. In the past the potential ecological value of the tips has generally been overlooked or understated. However, old coal spoil sites are now recognised as an important habitat for a range of species and the few remaining sites in the Valleys also have cultural and community value, however general awareness of the value of these sites is low. This has resulted in a loss of site value in term of heritage, ecology, landscape and amenity and there was a need to put individual sites into a context. The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), conducted an integrated strategic assessment of the recreational use and conservation value of heathland and associated habitats on the coal spoils of South Wales.  This datasets consists of the soil chemical analysis, soil physical analysis and soil biological analysis on 8 sites chosen from the 15 sites surveyed in the Assessment of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales 2006 survey.  The purpose of this data capture was to look at edaphic factors underlying the development of coal spoil communities of high conservation value. Edaphic factor can be defines as an abiotic factor relating to the physical or chemical composition of the soil found in a particular area. For example, very alkaline soil may be an edaphic factor limiting the variety of plants growing in a region. South Wales valley; current dataset covers Torfaen, Rhondda Cynon Taf, Caerphilly and Merthyr County Borough Councils (CBCs).\tStandard soil sampling techniques. Laboratory analysis consisted of standard analyses of chemical, physical and biological properties of soil samples.\t\ta) Paper report held by CCW HQ library, digital version on CD in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) b) MapInfo layer on CD in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) c) Excel files on CD  in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry) d) Photographs on CD  in TSG archive (copy held by Jan Sherry)\tContact: Liz Howe (used to be Jan Sherry Heathland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:38:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17666\tNRW_DS109916\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.462Z\t539\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCoal Spoil Conservation Project\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393136\tThe coal extraction industry has had a massive impact on the natural environment at a landscape level, the most obvious legacy of the operations being the extensive coal spoil tips apparent across the region. In recent years millions of pounds have been spent on the stabilisation and eventual reclamation of these spoil areas and their redevelopment for housing, new industry or local open spaces. In the past the potential ecological value of the tips has generally been overlooked or understated. However, old coal spoil sites are now recognised as an important habitat for a range of species and the few remaining sites in the Valleys also have cultural and community value, however general awareness of the value of these sites is low. This has resulted in a loss of site value in term of heritage, ecology, landscape and amenity and there was a need to put individual sites into a context.  The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), conducted an integrated strategic assessment of the recreational use and conservation value of heathland and associated habitats on the coal spoils of South Wales. This was intended as the first phase of a coal spoil conservation strategy which incorporated the results of additional archaeological and community assessment. The study was limited to the area covered by Torfaen, Merthyr Tydfil, Caerphilly, Rhondda Cynon Taf and Blaenau Gwent County Borough Councils. A Coal Spoil Conservation Strategy was initiated and this dataset includes the results from different phases of the project (see \u0027contains\u0027 field). The aim of this data capture was to provide background data for the delivery of a consistent strategy for local biodiversity action and amenity enhancement. The results of an archaeological and community assessment were also incorporated. The study suggests a classification for types of coal spoil and assesses the ecological value of a large number of sites.\tPhase 1 habitat data, Ordnance Survey maps, aerial photographs and information supplied by the local authorities were assessed. Most detailed records were provided by SEWBREC, the South East Wales Biological Records Centre. Available data is not evenly distributed and recommendations are made for further survey. GIS data and Excel spreadsheets for each site are provided as a separate CD, with the results summarised in this report. Confidence in this data is generally high however some disagreement regarding NVC communities/species identification and poor mapping.\t\tSee individual survey metadata entries in \u0027contains\u0027 field. 109899 Assessment of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales. 109898 Assessment of the recreational use and conservation value of heathland and associated habitats on the coal spoils of South Wales 109900 Assessment of Heathland and Associated Habitats on the Coal Spoils of South Wales. Phase II: Assessment of Environmental Parameters\tLiz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW (Formerly Jan Sherry Heathland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-SEWBReC (South East Wales Biodiversity Records Centre)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17656\tNRW_DS109921\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.103Z\t500\tValid\tEditable\tCoastal Sensitivity Atlas\tAtlas Adnoddau Arfordirol a Morol Sensitif i Lygredd Olew (Cymru) - Set Data SWF\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393231\tThis spatial dataset is an Atlas of Coastal and Marine Resources Sensitive to Oil Pollution in Wales. This data capture aims to highlight sensitive and vulnerable areas across Wales which maybe sensitive to oil pollution. It contains, but is not limited to, information on seabirds, scoter, wetland birds, grey seals, cetaceans, booming plans, protected sites, sand dunes, saline lagoons, sea grass beds and biotopes.  This is intended for use by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) staff, but is a legacy Countryside Council for Wales’ dataset. It should be used when providing environmental advice during an oil spill response operation. It\u0027s primarily intended as a memory jogger during the early stages of operation. It provides information on the location of environmental resources only, background knowledge of the relative sensitivity and vulnerability of the resources is required when providing advice during marine pollution incidence. Covers the coast of Wales and adjacent coasts of England (east coast of Dee estuary to Hoylake and south coast of Bristol Channel to Porlock Bay). 1st edition published March 2003; currently being updated and 2nd edition to be published early 2009.\tThis is a collation of a larger number of datasets that get updated in their own right. Methods of data capture are unique to each of the datasets forming the different layers of the Atlas.  This is the 2nd edition of the atlas (1st edition was completed in 2003). It provides updated information on the original list of resources, some additional resources (including CCW\u0027s advice on use of dispersants) and increased functionality (including a gazetteer to coastal and marine features).    There is no data associated to the Contingency Plan itself. Some wildlife datasets are based on incomplete survey coverage e.g. cetacean data is based largely on sightings by volunteer recorders, not from a comprehensive survey; and scoter data is based primarily on detailed surveys of selected areas. Subjective decisions have been made based upon best available information. Some datasets are not available for the English coasts.\tThis data should be used in conjunction with the MESG Oil Spill Contingency Plan - Fynnon numbers DCT-09-103877, DCT-09-103876. Countryside Council for Wales: Marine Pollution Contingency Plan. Part A and Part B. Volume 1 - Volume 2. The plan should be used in conjunction with the National Contingency Plan for Marine Pollution from Shipping and Offshore Installations (NCP) and any local Standing Environment Group (SEG) Plan.\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Emergencies_Data\\oil_spill\\WalesMarineResourceAtlas. Maes y Fynnon and regional offices. MESG Oil Spill Contigency Plan - Fynnon numbers DCT-09-103877, DCT-09-103876.\tCustodians - GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW Jennie Jones was involve din updating the data, Mike Thomson will be taking over as business lead as hold  role of Duty dispersant officer Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW       This metadata entry need re-writing as the product has been updated and renamed to reflect NRW current role to provide dispersant advice only and that the data has been transferred to ArcGIS from MapInfo and may have been updated in some cases.  The new name is Coastal Sensitivity Atlas (previously known as Atlas of Coastal and Marine Resources Sensitive to Oil Pollution (Wales) - GIS Dataset Jennie Jones has taken a lead on the updating the Atlas, working with Phil Jutson. Mike Thomson (Incidents and Emergencies Training and Systems Manager) will become the business lead as the Dispersant Duty Officer\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2009-01-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:46:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17669\tNRW_DS109980\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.551Z\t888\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCoastal Saline Lagoons and Pools in Wales\tLagwnau a phyllau hallt arfordirol yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393830\tThe Natural History Museum was asked by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to undertake a survey of all potential lagoonal or coastal brackish pond sites in Wales. This dataset presents the results of that survey, conducted during December 1998 as well as the results of the review and proposals for the monitoring and conservation of the coastal saline lagoons in Wales. Lagoons are an unusual and rare habitat, both in the UK (total resource ca 5,000 ha) and elsewhere. The characteristic lagoon community includes species almost or entirely restricted to lagoonal habitats in the United Kingdom, where they represent some of our rarest plants and animals; nine lagoonal species are currently protected under Schedules 5 and 8 of the Wildlife and Countryside Act (1981).  Survey of lagoonal habitats in Wales to identify lagoons and pools and carry out an assessment of condition and monitoring.\tWhile there had been a number of surveys of saline lagoons in England and Scotland (e.g. Bamber, 1997; Covey, 1999), there had been no investigation of the saline lagoon resource of Wales other than the NCC survey in the mid 1980s (e.g. Barnes, 1987). This dataset represents the results of the first complete survey of lagoonal habitats in Wales since then.  A total of 28 potential sites or ponds were assessed, 25 ponds visited, 18 sampled, of which 12 were deemed to be saline lagoons or coastal brackish ponds. At each site the physiography of the lagoon was assessed as were all water inputs (streams, sluices, weirs, etc.) and outflows. The surrounding habitat or human exploitation of the land was noted, as were marginal plants. Salinity was measured using a Reichert salinity refractometer. Semi-quantitative samples were collected from the substratum, the submerged plants and the water column of the lagoons. Sampling was predominantly by 0.5 mm mesh hand net. Fragments of submerged plant material and other hard substrata were also examined for bryozoans and other epizooites. Quantitative samples of the sedimentary benthos were collected from sites conjectured to be saline lagoons and also from para-lagoonal habitats for analytical comparison. At each of the twelve quantitative sample sites, a sample of whole surface sediment was collected for granulometric analysis and for analysis of organic content . Area and depth were measured and sites were photographed. Where found lagoonal cockles were measured to aid interpretation of year classes.\tSenior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, NRW - Bamber, RN., Evans, NJ., Whittall, A. 2000. Survey of potential coastal saline lagoons and pools in Wales, December 1998. Countryside Council for Wales Science Report 377. Bangor.  - Bamber, RN., Evans, NJ., Sanderson, WG., Whittall, A.  Coastal saline lagoons and pools in Wales: Review and proposals. Countryside Council for Wales Science Report 464.\tData is archived as project 12, media 22 and 23 (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 22 and K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 23) Species data held in Marine Recorder (survey number JNCCMNCR40000799) Original survey forms are stored in NRW off-site storage. Fireproof safe - Stack 01, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2000-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:47:41.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17665\tNRW_DS109983\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.434Z\t317\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Turbidity Data and Macroalgal Depth Distributions on a Transect in the Menai Strait\tData am gymylogrwydd dwr a dosbarthiadau dyfnder macroalgaidd ar drawslun yn afon Menai\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393833\tPresentation of data and analysis of patterns of water turbidity in the Menai Strait between 1961 and 1999 as well as depth distribution patterns of macroalgae (Telegraph Cable transect) at intervals from 1956 to 1999. Analysis of any relationship between the depth distribution of macroalgae and water turbidity patterns. The purpose of this data capture was to ascertain changes in turbidity and depth distribution patterns of macroalgae.\tWater Turbidity - Secchi disc readings were taken at Menai Bridge Pier by staff of the Marine Science Laboratories UCNW between 1961 and 1977. The Marine Science Laboratories UCNW recommenced measurements in February 1983.Water clarity (turbidity) measurements were recorded between January 1961 and early 1978.  Recordings were resumed in January 1983, with a hiatus from late 1990 until mid 1992 and relatively sparse data from then on.  No Secchi disc measurements were made between 1979 and 1982.  Algal Depth Distribution - Transects along a disused telegraph cable. Knight-Jones and Nelson-Smith (1956) Hoare and Peattie (1979) and annually since 1983 (NCC). Macroalgal depth distributions were established from the results of a series of surveys of the \u0027Telegraph Cable\u0027 transect line across the Menai Strait.  Surveys on the northern shore of the Strait were made in 1956 (3 stations); 1976 (12 stations); 1983 (12 stations); 1984 (60 stations); 1985-90; 1992; 1996; 1999 (72 stations); although since 1990 data has not been collected systematically from the near shore half of the transect (30 - 40 stations). Calculation of Critical Light Level Depths Z-values calculated Series of surveys carried out across the Menai Strait. Review of original surveys and annual NCC surveys to analyse changes in algal depth distribution and turbidity.\t\tData is archived in the NRW Marine archive as Project No 13, Media numbers 24,31 and 83 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\24 etc etc)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:48:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17667\tNRW_DS109992\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.510Z\t380\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRe-examination of distribution data for the harbour porpoise (Phocoena phocoena) on the North West European Continental Shelf\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393932\tThe Joint Cetacean Database, contributed to by Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC), Sea Watch Foundation (SWF) and Sea Mammal Research Unit (SMRU) contained most, but not all, effort-related cetacean data for North-west European waters up to 1998. Effort-related data from this database have been used to produce maps of cetacean distributions for north-west European waters for a Cetacean Atlas at the scale of 1/4 ICES (International Council for the Exploration of the Sea) rectangles (c. 25 x 25 km - 625 km2) due to be published by the JNCC. Some initial modelling of distribution data for harbour porpoise had not indicated any discrete areas of concentration. However, the spatial analyses conducted to derive species distributions were at too coarse a scale to detect concentrations. The UK has undertaken to re-examine distribution data for harbour porpoise with a view to site selection for this species. During 2001, a number of new effort-related surveys of harbour porpoise distribution were conducted in Welsh waters.  These included:  1) surveys of southern Cardigan Bay with emphasis upon the Special Area of Conservation established for bottlenose dolphins, conducted by Sea Watch Foundation between May and October and funded by EU INTERREG Programme with support from the Countryside Council for Wales (Baines et al., 2002);  2) a parallel acoustic and visual survey of harbour porpoises in South-west Welsh waters with emphasis west of Pembrokeshire, conducted by Chris Pierpoint with support from the International Fund for Animal Welfare (Pierpoint, 2001); surveys of the waters around Bardsey Island and the Llyn Peninsula, North Wales, conducted by Friends of Cardigan Bay and the Whale and Dolphin Conservation Society (WDCS), with support from CCW; and land-based and offshore surveys off the coast of Anglesey, undertaken by Marine Awareness North Wales, also with support from CCW.\tData on porpoise distribution and abundance come from three main sources:  Sea Watch Foundation - opportunistic sightings and effort-related recording;  SAST The Seabirds at Sea Team of the UK Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) - at-sea survey data;  SCANS (Small Cetacean Abundance on the North Sea) -methodology involved line transects to provide each survey block with even coverage of survey effort within the time available and conditions expected.  Data Processing- In order to provide a standardised description of animal distribution, sightings rates are used to describe the perceived density of animals in particular areas. Usually, sightings rates are presented as encounters per km of survey track line, but since many data used here (from the Sea Watch database) were collected by stationary observers from vantage points such as cliff-tops in timed intervals, the number of individuals sighted per unit time was considered to be a more appropriate measure of sightings rate. The only data associated with this project is held within the report.\t\tData is within the Marine archive as Project No. 28 and 169, and Media163 Fireproof safe - Stack 05, Hafod Elfyn, CCW Headquarters, Bangor, Wales.\tMarine and freshwater Evidence Manager, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17670\tNRW_DS109993\t2024-05-28T12:29:03.547Z\t625\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey of Lakes in the Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt Proposed Site of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSI) Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Special Protection Area (SPA)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393933\tThe Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt Proposed Site of Special Scientific Interest (pSSSI) and Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is situated in North Wales in the Snowdonia National Park. The pSSSI/SAC boundary marks the limit of a large upland block located along the eastern fringe of the National Park. The primary reasons for the site qualifying for SAC status are the large areas of blanket bog, a priority habitat under the Habitats Directive, and the upland European dry heath.  Other qualifying features include two Annex I habitat lake types: (1) oligotrophic to mesotrophic standing waters with vegetation of the Littorelletea uniflorae and/or of the Isoeto-Nanojuncetea, and (2), natural dystrophic lakes and ponds. The limnological surveys (surveys of inland water) focus on the collation of site and catchment data and the collection of new data on lake bathymetry, water quality and macrophyte assemblages. A particular focus is an assessment of the current acidification and conservation status of the four lake sites.  A preliminary assessment of whether the lakes are in favourable condition, as defined in the Habitats Directive, is also outlined. The primary aim of this project was to survey four lakes in the Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt pSSSI/SAC in order to build up CCWs datasets on Welsh lakes.  The information collected is required for conservation site assessments, ecological quality judgements for site condition reporting, contributions to site management plans and assistance with casework issues.\tBathymetric Surveys- undertaken at Llynnau Tryweryn, Hesgyn and Hiraethlyn in August 2002. Echo sounding transects were run in a small boat between identifiable points on the shore and marked onto field maps, the transect end point locations were also recorded using a Geographical Positioning System (GPS). This produced a cross sectional profile of the lakebed on a paper chart for each transect. Lake areas, volumes and perimeter lengths calculated using planimetry. Standard physical and morphometric parameters were calculated from the survey data. No further field investigation were required at Llyn Conwy which had sufficient morphometric information from a 1986 survey.  Physico-chemical survey-Water samples collected and temperature measured at the lake outflow. Temperature and dissolved oxygen profiles taken at the deepest part of each lake. Samples for soluble reactive phosphorus (SRP), total phosphorus (TP) and total nitrogen (TN) and filter papers for chlorophyll a were analysed. Acid neutralising capacity (ANC) estimated.  Aquatic macrophyte survey- zonation and maximum growing depth examined using transects, all submerged floating leaved and emergent plants recorded. Plant distribution and abundance assessed and lake type determined.\t\tArchived as Project No. 30, Media 176 Fireproof safe - Stack 05, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Ffynnon,  Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian: Head Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:32:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17653\tNRW_DS109994\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.429Z\t578\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRestoring the Afon Teifi at Cors Caron National Nature Reserve\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313039393934\tThe Afon Teifi is one of the largest rivers in Wales, and is of considerable national and international importance; designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). Approximately 15km from its source, the Teifi runs through the Cors Caron National Nature Reserve (NNR). Within the NNR approximately 1.5km of the Afon Teifi has been altered by straightening and deepening of the river. The northernmost straightening is present on Ordnance Survey maps dating from 1889 whilst the remaining alterations were undertaken in the period 1891 to 1905. The present day aerial photographs show the original meandering channel as sections of open water or vegetated channel. The presence of the original channel has lead to a proposal to restore the river to its former course. A scoping study by the River Restoration Centre has indicated that restoration is a viable option for the Afon Teifi within the NNR. The scheme proposed by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) incorporated an environmental assessment, stakeholder consultation, pre- and post-project monitoring programmes and technical design. This dataset contains the results of the work undertaken to address the scheme\u0027s requirements (Assessment of the current baseline information relating to the existing river morphology and hydrology, the in-channel vegetation and invertebrate fauna, the floodplain and the stakeholder interest). It includes an environmental impact assessment of the proposed restoration and presents a detailed plan of work for the restoration of the Afon Teifi in the NNR with guidance for further research and monitoring.\tLevels survey - A total station with an up to date calibration certificate was deployed on site during November 2004. Temporary wooden benchmarks placed approximately every 100m on the downstream left-hand bank of the current river channel and the old channel to be restored. A permanent benchmark established at the large footbridge downstream of the restoration site. The profile of the current channel, Sections A-D of the original channel, and approximately 10m over the top of the river banks were mapped to provide elevation data along both sides of the river. Sediment cores - For sections of the river to be restored the former river bed profile and depth was determined using a set of cores transverse to the course of the former channel. Cores taken at three sites to investigate the depth of the previous river bed in the old channel sections. Water quality - Water quality tested for pH and conductivity at all the sites where invertebrates were sampled. Invertebrates - Basic invertebrate survey conducted using kick and sweep sampling for two minutes over 1m2 on the channel bed and at the river bank margins. Three drift samples were taken in the main river channel where a drift net was left overnight. Six invertebrate colonisers set out in the emergent vegetation of the old channel during the same period.\t\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media101to200\\Media179 Fireproof safe - Stack 05, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Ffynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian:  Senior Freshwater Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17651\tNRW_DS110015\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.424Z\t768\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh coastal habitats as calving and nursery grounds for the harbour porpoise (Phocoena phocoena)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303135\tThe results of an investigation into strandings data for evidence that harbour porpoises breed and calve in Welsh coastal waters. Harbour porpoise calves are recorded annually off the Welsh coast from mid-May onwards.  However, published survey data is lacking for the region and the distribution and relative abundance of calves have not been reported. This project present data from harbour porpoise strandings on the Welsh coast collected through the collaborative UK and Celtic Marine Mammal Strandings Project, examines the incidence and seasonality of calf and neonate strandings and compare stranding rates in Wales with England, Scotland and Ireland. The only data associated with this project is held within the report. Raw data is held by CSIP.\tData collation and desk study. Marine mammal and turtle strandings in Wales are reported to Marine Environmental Monitoring (MEM) via a dedicated telephone line.  MEM respond to and collect systematic data from all stranding incidents, identify/confirm species, measure body length, sex the carcass, collect tissue samples, and advise on disposal of carcasses.  In addition, animals in fresh, slight or moderate condition are recovered whenever possible placed in cold storage or taken directly for post mortem examination.  All records of cetacean strandings in England and Wales are held by the Natural History Museum, London and have been since 1913.  The present study investigates all strandings of harbour porpoise recorded on the Welsh coast over a 10 year period between January 1989 and January 1999. Identification of maternally dependant and neonate calves- overall body length used to separate stranded animals into approximate age classes. Classify animals of 110cm or less as \u0027calves\u0027. Body length maximum of 90cm to identify calves that were still maternally dependent, i.e. neonates and calves assumed too young to survive alone.\t\tData is held as Project No.65, media 33 Fireproof safe - Stack 01, Hafod Elfyn, CCW Headquarters, Bangor, Wales.\tRob Penrose (CSIP)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17652\tNRW_DS110016\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.394Z\t660\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBreeding Seabird Surveys of South-west Wales Colonies 1996-1998\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303136\tSouth-west Wales has nationally and internationally important populations of nine species of seabirds and a total of thirteen regular breeding species. Following the Sea Empress oil spill at Milford Haven in February 1996, a census of seabird colonies in south-west Wales was initiated in order to assess the impact of the oil spill on population of seabirds breeding in the affected area. This dataset is compiled of the results of breeding seabird monitoring at colonies in south-west Wales in 1996, 1997 and 1998, the three seabird breeding seasons following the Sea Empress oil spill of February 1996.  Data collected in all three years of the project have been compared with data collected in previous years, in order to provide an assessment of the impact of the oil spill on local populations of ten species of breeding seabirds (Fulmar, Cormorant, Shag, Lesser Black-backed Gull, Herring Gull, Great Black-backed Gull, Kittiwake, Guillemot, Razorbill and Puffin).  Data collected for monitoring and comparison purposes to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on local populations of breeding seabirds.\tWhole-colony counts carried out from boats, as well as near-shore colonies counted from land and sea. In all three years of the project census methods and count units used followed the Seabird Monitoring Handbook.  Seabird Colony Register Data Forms and 10km Summary Forms were completed for all colonies in the study area, and Colony Register Forms were updated for existing colonies and compiled for new colonies. Completed forms have been submitted to the Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC). Copies of all forms and annotated maps are provided in a separate appendix, to be held by Natural Resources Wales (NRW). High monitoring effort after Sea Empress oil spill. Impact assessment on breading seabirds.\tThis dataset was collect under contract by The Wildlife Trust West Wales.\tArchived as Project 66, Media 39.\tGIS Service Desk  Senior Reserve Manager, NRW\tWildlife Trust (West Wales)\t\t1996-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17655\tNRW_DS110017\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.492Z\t365\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTemporal variation and monitoring of important lagoonal communities and species in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303137\tThis dataset contains the results of a synthesis, collation and review of knowledge pertaining to coastal saline lagoon sites in Wales and the important species recorded at these lagoons, undertaken by the Natural History Museum on behalf of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The scope was to understand:  - The habitat resource requirements of the species,  - The scales of natural variation in the species and in their habitat resource requirements, - The scales of natural variation in the habitats themselves, and   - To review monitoring and surveillance work in similar habitats and species. This work will allow the development of Standard Operating Procedures for condition monitoring the lagoons.\tData was collated from a variety of reports and synthesised within this report in order to investigate temporal fluctuation in lagoonal communities. The work presented in this report is the third lagoonal study by the Natural History Museum in Wales. Past studies have evaluated potential lagoonal sites and reviewed the extant management related information for them. The only data associated with this project is held within the report.\t\tData is archived as Project No. 9 and Media No. 153\tInternal Contact: Marine Monitoring Ecologist, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17654\tNRW_DS110018\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.577Z\t639\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGeomorphological Response of Welsh Sand Dunes to Sea Level Rise over the Next 100 Years\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303138\tResults of an assessment into the impact of predicted of sea level rise and climate change on Welsh dune systems over the next 100 years, with particular emphasis on Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and certain other dune sites of key nature conservation importance. The main purpose was to assess the impact of predictions of sea level rise and climate change on Welsh dune systems over the next 100 years, with particular emphasis on cSAC sites, certain Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and other important nature conservation sites. The specific objectives of the study were to: -  Estimate future erosion/accretion rates and evolution of the dune systems -  Define the extent of sand dune habitat loss -  Assess the natural and anthropogenic constraints that may limit the ability of the dune systems to readjust -  Identify future management requirements and practices that will ensure the maintenance of the sand dune cSAC resource in Wales in terms of both quantity and quality\tData sources have included: -  Literature reviews -  Maps (current and historical) -  Bathymetric charts (current and some historical) -  Aerial photographs -  Light detection and ranging (LiDAR) data (for selected sites only) -  Beach profile data (for selected sites only) -  Field surveys (selected sites only) -  Discussions with managers and stakeholders -  Data analysis and prediction methods have included: -  Geographical Information System (GIS) data manipulation, largely using MapInfo -  Historical Trend Analysis (HTA) -  Future Change Extrapolation (FCE) based on HTA results -  Future prediction using the Bruun Rule -  Future prediction based on Expert Geomorphological Assessment (EGA)\t\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media101to200\\Media177 Fireproof safe - Stack 05, Hafod Elfyn, NRW - Maes y Ffynnon Data is archived Project Number 31 Media Number 177\tInternal Custodian: Senior Coastal Scientist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:29:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17647\tNRW_DS110019\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.255Z\t760\tUnassessed\tEditable\tField observations on the disturbance of grey seals in North Wales\tArsylwadau maes ar aflonyddu ar forloi llwyd yng Ngogledd Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303139\tMany studies quantify the impacts of non-lethal human disturbance on animal behaviour to which animals respond by deploying what are interpreted as anti-predator strategies. These strategies are recognised as diverting time and energy from behaviours that would have occurred in the absence of human influences. In this report, levels of disturbance to grey seals are measured and these take into account the variety of seal vigilance levels as the prelude to / trigger of gross disturbance. Gross disturbance here denotes the stampeding of a seal or seals into the sea, where they were assembled on land, or where seals \u0027crash dive\u0027 when already in the sea.    The purposes of this data capture were to: -  Record the haul-out patterns of grey seals at Henllwyn Bay on Ynys Enlli/Bardsey when subject to no disturbance caused by people, in order to show examples of normal behaviour. To record examples of disturbance caused by human activity on land, in the sea and in boats, at Henllwyn for comparison with \u0027normal\u0027 / undisturbed behaviour.  -  Describe variables in seal behaviour observed over several tide cycles.   -  Outline some aspects of survey design to best measure disturbance.   -  Outline some site-specific management measures designed to reduce the frequency of disturbance events while also respecting the desires of people to have good views of and insights into the behaviour of grey seals at haul-out sites.   -  Provide a short review of papers and reports on seal disturbance at haul-out sites on the shores of the northeast Atlantic. This will examine management measures that have been or are about to be deployed elsewhere and the ways that such measures are being, or will be, monitored.\tThe natural pattern of grey seal emergence from and return to the sea at Henllwyn, Ynys Enlli/Bardsey was recorded at intervals of 15 minutes through the tide cycle, in order to understand \u0027natural\u0027 behaviour. Incidental notes were made describing seal behaviour and its apparent relationship to disturbance and relaxation. A count of seals in the sea adjacent to the haul-out sites was made at the same time. Simultaneously, instances of human activity at or near the site were recorded at the same interval. The time of arrival and the duration of time stayed at moorings in the bay by boats were recorded. Records were made of wind direction, wind speed and sea state, in order to discover to what extent they might influence site choice around the bay. At Henllwyn events from which gross disturbance resulted were recorded at the time they happened. Some of these events were filmed using a camcorder.\t\tReport and associated data are archived as Project No. 34, Media No\u0027s 76,182-185\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Officer, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:47:27.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17649\tNRW_DS110021\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.375Z\t350\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBaseline Survey of Natura 2000 Standing Water Habitats in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303231\tThe Environment Agency coordinated macrophyte and physico-chemical surveys on over 50 lake sites in England and Wales during 2003, including a number of lake sites within Welsh Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs). In an effort to update existing information on the site condition of these lakes and provide additional information on other Welsh lakes in SACs, former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned surveys on a further 19 lake sites in 2004. This dataset brings together data collected in 2003 and 2004 from 28 Welsh lakes in 11 SACs.  Limnological data collected for the purpose of site condition assessment and to provide a series of lake data reports on each of the 11 specified SACs.\tAquatic Macrophyte Survey- refer to: Joint Nature Conservation Committee (see Common Standards Monitoring Guidance for Standing Waters, JNCC 2005a). The plant surveys consisted of a strandline survey, emergent and marginal survey, shoreline wader survey and boat survey using either a bathyscope or a double-headed rake. Estimates of species abundance converted to a DAFOR scale. Physico-Chemical Survey- Dissolved oxygen and temperature profiles and Secchi disk depth were recorded. Chlorophyll a samples analysed. Chemical determinands analysed. Acid neutralising capacity (ANC) calculated. Bathymetric Surveys and Catchment Mapping produced using a boat mounted Lowrance LMS240, a combined GPS receiver and echo sounder. Diatom Analysis- A sediment core was taken in the summer of 2004 from the deepest part of each lake using a Glew gravity core. Data collected in an effort to update existing information on the site condition of these lakes and to provide additional information on other Welsh lakes in SACs.\t\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media201to300\\Media205 Fireproof safe - Stack 06, Hafod Elyfn, NRW Maes y Ffynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Senior Freshwater Ecologist, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17650\tNRW_DS110022\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.429Z\t780\tValid\tEditable\tZooplankton analysis\tDadansoddi swoplancton\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303232\tPlankton samples were collected from during a sandbank survey at nine sites in four Candidate Special Areas of Conservation (cSAC) using a plankton net positioned above a beam trawl. The zooplankton was analysed my Emu Ltd and found to contain common Irish Sea species.   Classification analysis revealed that four of the sites (Red Wharf Bay, Four Fathom, Helwick and Cardigan) each supported distinct communities based on their faunal composition, and the distinguishing features of each were identified. Samples from the remaining sites did not fall into site-specific groups indicating that the fauna in these areas was less distinct.   Data used to sort and identify the planktonic animals from samples taken as part of the sandbank survey in four SACs along the Welsh coastline.\tThe samples were taken in Jul/Aug 2001 from nine sites (Tripod, Cardigan, Bais, Helwick, Four Fathom, Bastram Shoals, Turbot Bank, Devil\u0027s Ridge and Red Wharf Bay) using a 0.5 mm plankton net suspended above a trawl beam. Planktonic animals (excluding benthic invertebrates and the larvae of decapods) were identified down to the lowest possible taxon using standard techniques. A classification analysis on the loge(x+1) transformed data using the Bray-Curtis similarity measure was performed using PRIMER software to examine inter-relationships between samples. Data collected during a sandbank survey of nine cSACs along the coastline of Wales.\t\tData is held as Project No. 41, media 94 (File path Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 94) Fireproof safe - Stack 03, Hafod Elfyn, CCW Headquarters, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:48:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17648\tNRW_DS110023\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.346Z\t535\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Sandbank Survey: Fish Stomach Content, Fish Epibenthic Survey, and Data Analysis (2001)\tArolwg banciau tywod Cymru 2001:cynnwys boliau pysgod, dadansoddiad o ddata, arolwg epibenthig o bysgod\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303233\tSandbanks are listed as an Annex 1 habitat that requires conservation measures under the European Union Habitats Council of the European Communities (1992). Sandbanks can be categorised either by sediment type or by topography. The different sediments Annex I sandbanks can consist of are sands, gravelly sands and muddy sands. This study was designed to examine the fish and epifaunal biota of sandbanks and formed part of a larger collaborative survey in which the hyperbenthos and infauna were also sampled.  As a result of their unique physical regime, sandbanks may hold their own distinct flora and fauna that is particularly well adapted to such conditions or that uses these habitats in an opportunistic manner (e.g. at slack water). However, the extent to which sandbanks are characterised by a distinct set of taxa remains unknown.\tA total of 11 different sandbanks were chosen as potential seabed features that typically represent sandbank habitats as defined by the interpretation manual of European Habitats (EUR15/2) of which 10 were sampled in the present study (extensions of shallow coastal sediments adjacent to the coastline, distinct sandbanks that are distinct from the surrounding seabed features and sediments). Hyperbenthic, infaunal, epifaunal and fish samples were collected at each site from the RV Prince Madog (34.9 m l.o.a.). At each site, five replicate tows were made with a 2 m beam trawl fitted with a chain matrix and 1 cm square meshed net with a 1 mm codend liner. The 2 m beam trawl was towed at a speed of 1.5 to 2 knots for a standard duration of 5 minutes.  At each site, sediment samples were taken using a Van Veen grab and immediately frozen on board. In the laboratory, particle size fractions, percentage organic matter and calcium carbonate were determined and the data catagorised into the following sediment fractions: mud, muddy sand, sand, silt, carbonates and organic matter.  At three sites (Devil\u0027s Ridge, west of Newquay and Bastram Shoal) plaice Pleuronectes platessa were collected for the purpose of dietary analyses.\tKaiser MJ. 2004. Sandbank survey: fish stomach content, fish epibenthic survey data analysis. Countryside Council for Wales Science Report No. 610. Bangor\t[A] Data is archived as project no. 234, media no. 96 and 745 (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 96 and K:\\Archive\\Media 701to800\\Media 745) [B] A copy is stored on the I drive:I:\\MMT video Archive\\Handheld surface video\\20010000 Sandbanks BS media 745.avi   [C] A clip has been extracted from the video, edited and made available on CCW\u0027s YouTube channel. See online resource below. [D] Species data on Marine Recorder (Survey numbers MRCCW30000000008, MRCCW30000000037, MRCCW30000000038).\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:50:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17646\tNRW_DS110024\t2024-05-28T12:29:02.048Z\t587\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW) Acoustic Habitat Survey (multibeam) of Habitat and Feature Distribution in Pembrokeshire Marine Candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC)\tArolwg Acwstig ar Gynefin ACA Sir Benfro Forol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303234\tPembrokeshire Marine candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) is a large area including open coast and the Milford Haven Waterway that has been identified as being of European marine conservation importance.  Features of this site include marine species and seabed habitats.  The purpose of the Acoustic Habitat Survey was to provide information on the nature and characteristics of the seabed within defined areas of the Pembrokeshire coastline.  The dataset contains the results of a project to gather topographical and seabed characterisation information to improve the knowledge of the site and to aid in future site management and feature monitoring.  Primary focus- areas of topographical distinction such as reefs, other hard substrata and sandbanks. Areas covered in the Pembrokeshire cSAC survey include; Skomer, Grassholm, Hats \u0026amp; Barrels, Welshmans Bay, East side of Skokholm, St Ann\u0027s Head, Milford Approaches and the majority of the Milford Haven estuary upstream as far as Picton Point.\tOnce mobilisation and all calibrations had been conducted, survey work could proceed. The same basic method of survey was used throughout the whole survey area. Throughout the project the vessel was moored in Milford Dock. When leaving the dock, or while alongside the Mackerel pontoon outside the dock entrance, a course check was made on the navigation systems. All transit time was put to full use by conducting survey to and from site. Once all systems were found to be operating correctly a sound velocity profile was observed. The results of the profile were entered into both the multibeam system and the single beam sounder. Once the vessel arrived at the proposed working location for the day another S/V profile was observed. The results were entered into the relevant equipment. Sidescan sonar was required in the Marine Nature Reserve (MNR), now classified as Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). This was observed at the same time as the Multibeam data. As a large proportion of this area was relatively shallow the sidescan was towed behind the vessel on a fairly short tow cable. The offset of the fish behind the Central Reference Point (CRP) of the vessel was applied in the Sidescan data capture system (Octopus 460).\t\tAll data is archived at 102, 392, 515, 516, 517, 518, 519, 520, 954.  Copies are also available in the Pembroke Dock and Skomer offices.  A copy of the georeferenced tifs are also available on the MFSG drive as seamless layers at: S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Pembs Multibeam.   Fireproof safe - Stack 3, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian -  MFSG (NRW) Metadata point of contact - Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:50:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17703\tNRW_DS110026\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.576Z\t555\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDevelopment of a Methodology for the Assessment of Cumulative Effects of Marine Activities Using Liverpool Bay as a Case Study (2002)\tDatblygu methodoleg i asesu effeithiau cronnol gweithgareddau morol gan ddefnyddio Bae Lerpwl fel astudiaeth achos\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303236\tFormer Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and now Natural Resources Wales (NRW), encourages good practice in undertaking Environmental Impact Assessments (EIAs) for developments and activities, including those located in Welsh Territorial waters (up to 12 nm offshore). Experience has shown that EIAs carried out in the marine environment often address impacts of the development in isolation from other similar developments or other developments having similar impacts. A review of environmental statements produced for offshore oil and gas developments found that none addressed cumulative impacts over the projects lifetime or with regards to adjacent existing or proposed developments.  It can therefore be said that cumulative impacts from marine activities have seldom been adequately addressed in EIAs for offshore projects.  The purpose of this data capture is to:  -  Review current understandings of potential cumulative effects from offshore industries and develop a proposed methodology for cumulative effects assessment of offshore industries from a nature conservation and landscape / seascape perspective;   -  Produce a GIS database and analysis tool for offshore Cumulative Effects Assessment (CEA);   -  Identify current and predict likely future cumulative effects from offshore industries in Liverpool Bay on habitats and species of importance; and   -  Identify data gaps, and recommend further data and research requirements for undertaking CEA for offshore activities.\tFollowing a data collation exercise, a vulnerability assessment for cumulative effects was undertaken for species and habitats of nature conservation importance using matrices and Geographical Information System (GIS) software.\t\tData held as Project No. 75, media 80\tData Custodian - Maritime Policy Section Head, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:52:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17699\tNRW_DS110027\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.567Z\t533\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCarmarthen Bay: Evolution of Estuarine Morphology and Consequences for Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Management\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303237\tCarmarthen Bay has a total of four important estuaries comprising the Loughor Estuary and the Taf, Towy and Gwendraeth. Between 16th and 19th century these estuaries lost around 2400 Ha of saltmarsh due to land claim. Between the end of the 19th century and end of the 20th the estuaries gained 1136 Ha. The changes in the saltmarsh environment may impact shellfish, invertebrates and wading birds. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the history of evolution and current trends in the geomorphological development of the estuaries of the then Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC). Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries is now designated fully as an Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for numerous species and habitats including estuaries, mudflats, sand flats and lagoons, which make up 13.7% of the site.\tMethods of data capture: Various information regarding each of the estuaries were obtained including shoreline monitoring profiles, bathymetry contours, mean high water and mean low-water locations extracted from OS Landline data. Physical characteristics of the estuaries were obtained; such as their geology, evolution of coastal barriers, past and present estuary area, tidal regimes, tidal prism, river discharge and tidal flushing. Sediment supply was further investigated as well as historical changes in channel pattern. A cross sectional profile of each estuary was created.\t\t[A] Report is held as Project No. 76, media 93 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 93) [B] Also available on OLIB\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:14.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17705\tNRW_DS110029\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.680Z\t357\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSite Condition Assessments of Welsh Standing Water Features (2003 - 2008)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303239\tThe UK Conservation Agencies have devised a Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) protocol (JNCC, 2005) for the routine monitoring and assessment of protected site condition.  The CSM lake monitoring method is based on previous survey protocols used by the conservation agencies. It aims to gather the maximum amount of information relating to the ecology of each water body in the most efficient way possible.  The purpose of this data capture was to provide an assessment of the current conservation status of water features in Wales.\tBoth reports utilise Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) methods (JNCC, 2005)) for  SSSIs and SAC designated water features. Data collected includes aquatic macrophyte surveys (carried out following the CSM protocol); physico-chemical data; sedimentary diatoms; lake bathymetries; catchment and habitat maps, which was then used to prepare site condition assessments for each of the lakes. The CSM lake monitoring method is based on previous survey protocols used by the conservation agencies. It aims to gather the maximum amount of information relating to the ecology of each water body in the most efficient way possible. The protocol makes use of a combination of shoreline walks to identify marginals, waded transects to describe the shallow water vegetation and boat-based surveys to identify plants in deeper water. In addition, physicochemical data are collected for each lake. The protocol is described in full in JNCC (2005).\t\tArchived as Project No. 84, Media No\u0027s 361 and 604.\tInternal Custodian: Freshwater Ecologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:34:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17704\tNRW_DS110035\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.631Z\t528\tUnassessed\tEditable\tResults of a field survey following the Menai Strait oil spill\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303335\tFormer Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) were informed at 09:13 on Sunday the 25th June 2006 that there was an oil spill in the Menai Strait. Oil had been reported to the Coastguard at around 08:15.  At the time the information available was that there was an oil/diesel slick, of unknown origin, spreading from Caernarfon up to the Swellies. The tide was flooding at this time with high water around 11:00. The level of the incident was thought to be minor. At 10:25 on 25th June CCW regional officers were on the shore close to the Friction Dynamics factory.  The strandline was reported to be contaminated: cobbles splattered with oil. The level of the incident was considered minor/medium at this time. The purpose of this data capture was to assess contamination of species and habitats following the oil spill on the 25th of June 2006.\tOn the morning of the 26th June 2006 a team composed of CCWs Senior Intertidal Ecologist and members of the Marine Monitoring Team were dispatched to further examine the contamination on the shore.  A team of regional marine staff surveyed the shore on the low tide of the same day and observations of a discharge pipe were made by boat on the following day.  A stretch of coastline approximately 1km long was surveyed from just south of the National Watersports Centre at Plas Menai to just south of where the spill was believed to have originated.  Further site visits were made to the same area on 30th June and 10th July.26/06/06- Access to the shore was made at SH 5011 6600 and a stretch of coastline approximately 1km long was surveyed from just south of the National Watersports Centre to just south of where the spill was believed to have originated.  The shore was surveyed for just less than one hour either side of local high water. 27/06/06 -  boat-based observations were made around the source of the discharge from CCWs vessel Pedryn. 30/06/06, 07/07/06 and 08/07/06 - further site visits 10/07/06 - visit to shore at Felinheli where a nationally important biotope, Asc.T (Ascophyllum nodosum, sponges and ascidians on tide-swept mid eulittoral rock) has been recorded\t\tArchived as project number 138 and Media No. 397 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 397)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17694\tNRW_DS110036\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.565Z\t281\tValid\tEditable\tVegetation Survey of Maritime Cliff and Slopes of the Llyn Peninsula\tArolwg o lystyfiant clogwyn a llethrau arforol Penrhyn Llyn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303336\tMaritime cliff and slopes is a priority habitat within the UK Biodiversity Action Plan. Selected maritime sites on the Llyn Peninsula were surveyed, mapped and evaluated in September/October 2000 and the range of National Vegetation Classification (NVC) vegetation types present characterized.  A number of sections of the UK coastline have been nominated as \u0027Vegetated Sea Cliffs of the Atlantic and Baltic Coasts\u0027 candidate Special Areas of Conservation under the EC Habitats Directive. This survey was undertaken as part of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCWs) commitment to undertake research and survey in order to increase knowledge of the extent and quality of this resource. Selected maritime sites on the Llyn Peninsula:  -  Porth Neigwl (Hell\u0027s Mouth) -  Porth Dinllaen (Borth Wen to Penrhyn Nefyn) -  Porth Nefyn (Penrhyn Nefyn to Penrhyn Bodeilias) -  Porth Pistyll (Penrhyn Bodeilias to the lower slopes of Carreg y Llam)\tSurvey carried out during periods of low high tides, to allow the maximum access to the base of the cliffs. The basic recording unit was a 2 m x 2 m quadrat. For each quadrat a full species list, mean height of the vegetation, aspect, and slope were recorded. Where the slope of the cliff was very unstable, or too steep to allow access, quadrat records were made using binoculars. Soil information, grazing records and additional factors relating to disturbance were also recorded. Mapping in the field was carried out on 1:2500 computer-generated maps.\t\tArchived as project number 139, media number 236.\tInternal Custodian:  Julie Creer, Coastal Ecology Specialist, Evidence and Analysis, NRW Heather Lewis, Coastal Ecology Specialist, Evidence \u0026amp; Analysis, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2000-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T14:06:15.077Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17701\tNRW_DS110037\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.591Z\t384\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBroadscale Remote Survey and Mapping of Sublittoral Habitats and Biota of Milford Haven, Pembrokeshire and South Cardigan Bay\tArolwg o bell a mapio graddfa eang cynefinoedd a biota isarforol Aberdaugleddau, Sir Benfro a de Bae Ceredigion\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303337\tThe Broadscale Mapping Project has been devised and funded by a consortium consisting of the Crown Estate, the Countryside Council for Wales, English Nature, Scottish Natural Heritage and the SeaMap research group based at Newcastle University and undertaken by SeaMap. Three trial areas were selected, around Milford Haven, Pembrokeshire and South Cardigan Bay, for developing and testing the methodology, although experience from many other surveys conducted by SeaMap have also contributed to the development of the methodology. The aim of this data collection was to map the seafloor sediments and biota, for habitats and biotope mapping. This was supplemented by maps and supporting data showing the location of ground truth samples and the positions of the acoustic tracks.\tImage processing requires ground truth samples of the biota and habitats from the sea floor and the sampling techniques range from the traditional grab, trawl and dredge to the deployment of remote video. The video is especially useful since it provides a vista of the sea floor which is approximately of the same extent as the area of the seafloor that is covered by one pulse from an echo sounder (the \u0027footprint\u0027).  Image processing is a two-stage process: In the first stage the acoustic data in the immediate vicinity of the ground truth positions are selected and tagged according to the type of habitat or biotope of the ground truth sample. The acoustic data are pooled for each habitat or biotope type and the acoustic characteristic calculated from the mean and standard deviation of the depth and the hardness and roughness values. In this way an acoustic signature is created for each habitat or biotope type.  In the second stage each pixel is matched to the signatures and the likelihood of the pixel belonging to each habitat or biotope calculated. An automated process then selects the most likely category for the pixel. This second stage is, therefore, called a maximum likelihood classification. The methodology relies on the relatively inexpensive acoustic ground discrimination system (AGDS) based on a single beam echo sounder. AGDS analyse the echo from the sounder and gives a measure of the hardness and roughness of the sea floor, as well as depth. These records, in the form of point data, are logged together with time and position as the vessel tracks over the survey area. The point data are used to create continuous digital images for each of the variables through interpolation. This conversion to digital images consisting of rows and columns of pixels enables powerful image processing software, designed primarily for the processing of satellite images, to be applied to the acoustic data.\t\t[A]  Data is Archived as Project 141, Media 77, (related Media numbers include 79, 505 and 506) [B]  Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elyfn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tData Custodians - Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group. Metadata Point of Contact - Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:11:27.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17695\tNRW_DS110038\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.632Z\t794\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey of the Tentacled Lagoon Worm (Alkmaria romijni) at Carew Castle Mill Pond (2006)\tArolwg o fwydyn tentaclog y morlyn (Alkmaria romijni) ym Mhwll Melin Castell Caeriw\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303338\tThe tentacled lagoon worm (Alkmaria romijni) is a nationally scarce protected species found in lagoonal habitats in the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation. In Wales it has only been recorded from two saline lagoon sites in Pembrokeshire: Neyland Weir Pool (Westfield Pill) and Carew Castle Mill Pond.  Elsewhere in the UK it had been recorded from only a handful of sites in lagoons and estuaries along the English Channel and southern North Sea and internationally between Morocco and the Baltic where it is not uncommon. A. romijni is limited to brackish water habitats and threats to these habitats include agricultural run-off and sewage, coastal development and coastal defence as well as pollution from industrial discharges.  There were three main purposes of this data capture:   -  To establish a repeatable baseline survey of the tentacled lagoon worm (Alkmaria romijni) at Carew Castle Mill Pond.  -  To undertake a spatial survey of salinity at the sediment-water interface  -  To note field observations on the integrity of the inflow/outflow system or any activities at the site that might be considered to have an adverse effect on the integrity of the site as a saline lagoonal habitat.\tInitial survey- A sampling pattern of 20 stations distributed evenly across the whole Mill Pond (stations between 45 and 80m apart), was established prior to the survey using Mapinfo GIS software.  The positions derived from the GIS were input into a Garmin handheld GPS in order to enable location of the sampling stations in the field. Once sampling stations had been located , a single grab of the top 20 cm of sediment were taken using a 0.025m2 hand held Ekman grab. Once the grab arrived at the surface, a sample of the water draining out of the grab was put in a pot for later determination of the salinity at the sediment surface.  The grab samples were then sieved through a 0.5 mm mesh until most of the fines had been washed away.  The sieved sample was then placed in a labelled bucket and topped up with water from the Mill Pond. Samples were taken back to the laboratory. The number of Alkmaria romijni worms together with other species found in the samples were counted. The salinity of the water samples collected from the grabs determined using an Atago hand-hand refractometer.  Follow up survey- a sampling technique was adopted following the principles of Adaptive Cluster Sampling (ACS), in which sampling effort is modified in response to observations made during the course of the survey. Initial survey 27 February 2006 follow up survey 15 March 2006.\t\tThe report and data collected under this project is archived as Project 142, Media 467. Data is also available in Marine Recorder\tInternal Custodian: Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:13:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17688\tNRW_DS110039\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.511Z\t305\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Rare Red Alga Anotrichium barbatum: UK Species Action Plan and its Implementation (1998 - 1999)\tYr alga coch prin Anotrichium barbatum: Cynllun Gweithredu\u0027r DU ar gyfer y rhywogaeth a\u0027i weithredu (1998 - 1999)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303339\tThe marine red alga Anotrichium barbatum is one of two rare seaweeds in the UK with a Species Action Plan. The alga was detected in Tremadog Bay in 1998. The site was resurveyed in 1999 and Anotrichium barbatum was again recorded. The dataset contains information on the geographical distribution of the alga and density estimates. In 1997 an Action Plan was prepared for English Nature, but downgraded to a Species Statement because there were no known extant UK populations. The discovery of Anotrichium barbatum in Cardigan Bay in 1998 permitted the completion in 1998 of the Action Plan with target. The minimum objective is to stabilize the species\u0027 status i.e. to maintain the net population size and distribution against baseline figures set at the time of Action Plan publication.\tData was collected using Standard Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) Phase 2 survey habitat recording techniques. There is a high confidence in this dataset. The alga was detected in Tremadog Bay in 1998 using standard MNCR Phase 2 survey methodology (Hiscock 1996). The site was resurveyed in 1999 and Anotrichium barbatum was again recorded. The only data associated with this project is held within Marine Recorder as Reference No. JNCCMNCR40000738.\t\t[A] Archived as project 153, media 251 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 251) [B] Marine Recorder survey (JNCCMNCR40000738).   [C] Paper MNCR forms are held in NRW off-site storage.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2000-02-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:15:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17690\tNRW_DS110042\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.445Z\t452\tUnassessed\tEditable\tModelled biomass, production and species richness of benthic invertebrate communities in Welsh coastal waters\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303432\tSoft-sediment benthic invertebrate communities in the coastal zone of Wales were modelled using a size-based model. Biomass, production and species richness were modelled as a function of local environmental conditions and bottom trawling disturbance. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) contributed to a PhD at the School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University entitled. The socio-economic and ecological implications of inshore fisheries management (project FC 73-02-740). The final part of this PhD includes an evaluation of the economic impacts of different types of fisheries management, including No Take Zones (NTZs) and areas where no fishing with towed gears is permitted.  In order to base this economic analysis on realistic scenarios, data was needed concerning the locations which would most benefit from the protection described.  The state of the benthic community was modelled using bottom trawl data up to 2003 and therefore represents the state in 2003. Predictions for a situation without trawling are not time specific. Values represent a yearly average.  The model that was used was validated for the North Sea and explained 39% of the observed variation there. No data was available for validation of model predictions in the Irish Sea.\tA size-based model of the response of soft-sediment benthic communities to trawling disturbance was used to predict the recovery time of these communities after trawling was used. The model contained 32 state variables, in two faunal groups (soft and hard bodied macrofauna). Growth of the population biomass in each body mass - organism type compartment was modelled by modifying Lotka-Volterra competition equations to give the population biomass flux for a compartment. The interaction between habitat type and trawling effects was modelled by including relationships between growth and mortality and the environment in the model. We included the effect of sediment type on trawling mortality, the effect of bed shear stress on population growth rate, the effects of chlorophyll a content of the sediment on carrying capacity and the effects of sediment erosion on mortality. Recovery in the model can only take place through growth of the local populations, as the model assumes that there is no migration between adjacent areas.\t\tArchived at K:\\Archive\\Media 401to493\\Media493\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW  The PhD was being undertaken by Liz Richardson - former Bangor University\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17698\tNRW_DS110058\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.582Z\t442\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Commercial Shipping Occurring in and around Skomer\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Llongau masnachol yn, ac o amgylch, Ynys Sgomer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303538\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. This dataset consists of records of all the Tankers using St brides bay including details of record numbers, names and movements of vessels. The purpose of this data capture was to document the usage of St Brides Bay as a Tanker anchoring area.  Information may also provide context for any changes in the habitat and species composition in the area.\tField records - activity, name and number of participants are recorded. Daily sampling and records were taken throughout the year.\t\tSkomer MNR office,West Region drive/shared docs/data/commercial/Tankers\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:15:48.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17700\tNRW_DS110059\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.589Z\t607\tValid\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Marine Monitoring Programme Overview\tGwarchodfa Natru Forol (GNF) Ynys Sgomer - Rhaglen Monitro\u0027r Mor\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303539\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ).  Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.   The Skomer Marine Monitoring Team has focused on developing a range of projects to monitor the health of the reserve and investigate the relevant aspects of the species life histories for baseline information.  Large scale surveys developed in the 1970\u0027s and 1980\u0027s provided the foundation for the monitoring.     This metadata details general monitoring on Skomer, each datasets has its own metadata entry.  Monitoring is undertaken to provide an assessment of the condition of features against set targets and will contribute to reporting on the MNR as well as Pembrokeshire Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) within which the reserve lies.  Data is collected around Skomer Island and on the mainland between Musselwick Sands and Gateholm Island within the Marine Nature Reserve.  Data has been collected from 1975 onwards. An extensive review of images has been completed to determine the quantity and quality of information that could be extracted from these images. Species data has been collated from a number of surveys entered into Marine Recorder which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway.\tThe first step of the programme was to map survey sites to enable accurate relocation each year.  New sites continue to be mapped to allow for the expansion of the projects. The sites cover a wide variety of habitats and species from inter-tidal lichens, benthic rock and sediment, mobile species (e.g. seals and territorial fish) and plankton, and therefore a variety of monitoring techniques are used.   Following on from the site mapping, time series data is collected. The majority of the monitoring involves human observation and the development of a series of reliable photographic images.  Sediment communities are sampled by Day grab and suction sampler. The data collected provides information and details of the life histories of particular species over time, the stability of communities, the variations in species abundance and the effect of known events (e.g. the Sea Empress Oil Spill) on species or communities. In addition to biotic data, environmental data is also collected to allow the reasons for fluctuation in biota to be investigated with the aim of separating \u0027natural\u0027 change from anthropogenic change.  Finally commercial and recreational activities are recorded to monitor changes in user activity.\"  Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) for the different monitoring programmes are to be created and stored in the NRW Document Management System (DMS)\tPlease refer to individual metadata entries for more information.  An annual project status report is also published. For an example see Lock, K, Burton, M, Jones, J, Massey, A. (2023) Skomer Marine Conservation Zone,  Annual Report 2022. NRW Evidence Report 657.\t[A]  Species data held in Marine Recorder  [B]  Data held at Skomer Office on   \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\shared documents\\data\\biological data\\ [C]  \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\docs and settings\\Monitoring\\ [D] SOPs held in the DMS   This data is now available on the NBN Atlas  back ups of Video library \u0026amp; CDs in off-site storage: CCW/WRPB/025\tPhil Newman, Senior Marine Conservation Officer, Skomer Marine Conservation Zone\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-11-12T14:15:39.017Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17702\tNRW_DS110060\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.542Z\t377\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Commercial Fishing Activities Occurring in and around Skomer\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Gweithgareddau pysgota masnachol yn, ac o amgylch, Ynys Sgomer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303630\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. The purpose of this data capture was to document the usage of the MNR and to create GIS maps of fishing effort and types of gear used in the reserve. The dataset contains records of all the fishing activity in the reserve.  This includes potting effort, position and length of strings of pots, position of nets, number of boats using the area and number of dredgers in the St Brides area.\tField records were taken on activity, name and number of participants.\t\tSkomer MNR office,West Drive drive/shared docs/data/commercial/potting\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-03-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:10:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17691\tNRW_DS110061\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.533Z\t488\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Scarlet and Gold Cup Coral Balanophyllia regia\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Monitro cwrelau seren aur Balanophyllia regia\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303631\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Cup corals are slow growing filter feeders, which are susceptible to changes in water quality and planktonic food supply.  Scarlet and gold star-coral, Balanophyllia regia, is a Lusitanian species and Skomer MNR is close to the northern edge of its range in the UK. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor Scarlet and Gold cup coral.\tA fixed position quadrat using a 50 x 40 cm framer at Thorn Rock has been photographed since 1985. In 2002 three transects with 51 quadrats were established at the Wick.  Photographs are taken and counts are carried out using GIS techniques (see Burton, Lock and Newman 2002). In 2008 the framer size at the Wick was increased to 50 x 70cm using a digital SLR camera, providing high quality images allowing improved photo analysis.\t\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/cup corals ,  Images - dell1590 C/doc and settings/MONITORING/balanophyllia\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:04:04.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17697\tNRW_DS110062\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.566Z\t735\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Macrophyte Condition Assessment for the Afon Gwyrfai SAC (2007)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303632\tThe Afon Gwyrfai Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Llyn Cwellyn Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) was notified in 2003 and is considered to be one of the best examples in the United Kingdom of an oligotrophic to mesotrophic river with submerged beds of aquatic plants, especially its Callitricho-Batrachion vegetation community.  The purpose of this data capture was to undertake a Condition Assessment of the river macrophyte and river macrophyte-related attributes of the former Countryside Council for Wales CCW\u0027s Management Units 2, 5, 6 and 7 of the Afon Gwyrfai.\tA Common Standards Monitoring Condition Assessment of Afon Gwyrfai SAC river was used to trial a novel \u0027macrophyte mapping\u0027 survey approach which could be used to complement Common Standards Monitoring and to aid and inform management decisions. This macrophyte mapping method involved walking the banks of the 2 km section and recording the channel flora observed and the major shifts in community composition. The aim   of the mapping component was to produce a \u0027banded map\u0027 illustrating these shifts in community composition and structure.\t\tThe report and data collected are archived as Project 198 (Media 545) and are stored in fireproof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Fynnon.  This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage.  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media545\tInternal Custodian: Rivers Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-05-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:11.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17696\tNRW_DS110063\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.589Z\t335\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Devonshire Cup Coral Caryophyllia smithii\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Monitro cwrelau cwpan Dyfnaint Caryophyllia smithii\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303633\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. Cup corals are slow growing filter feeders, which are susceptible to changes in water quality and planktonic food supply. Caryophyllia smithii is a common feature of the sub-littoral benthic community of south-western Britain. Counts of cup corals have been done since 1993 using the images collected from the sponge assemblage project.  The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the population for changes in densities and to look for evidence of recruitment.\tApproximately 70 quadrats have been analysed on a yearly basis since 1993 from photographs taken for the sponge community project at Thorn Rock. Photographs are taken using a 50 x 70 cm framer and counts are carried out using GIS techniques.\t\tSkomer MNR, West Region drive/shared documents/data/biological data/cup corals ,  Images - dell1590 C/doc and settings/MONITORING/cup corals\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:04:44.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17689\tNRW_DS110064\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.523Z\t567\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInitial hydroacoustic mapping of macrophyte communities in the southern end of Llyn Tegid\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303634\tThis dataset is made up of the results of a hydroacoustic survey to map the extent of submerged macrophyte communities and major substrate types in the southern end of Llyn Tegid.  The objectives of the present part of the project were to use hydroacoustics to map the current extent of submerged macrophyte communities and major substrate types in the southern end of Llyn Tegid and to present the findings of such work at the Countryside Council for Wales Marine and Freshwater Workshop 2007. This works was undertaken as a small work package added during the summer of 2007 to a much larger project concerned with the hydroacoustic survey and translocation of the gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus).\tEcho sounding was carried out using a BioSonics DT-X echo sounder with a 200 kHz splitbeam vertical transducer of beam angle 6.5 degrees operating under the controlling software Visual Acquisition Version 5.0.4. Throughout the surveys, data threshold was set at -130 dB, pulse rate at 5 pulses s-1, pulse width at 0.1 ms, and data recorded from a range of 0 m from the transducer. The system was deployed from a 4.8 m inflatable dinghy powered by a 25 horse power petrol outboard engine and moving at a speed of c. 1.1 m s-1, depending on wind conditions. The transducer was positioned c. 0.5 m below the surface of the water. Navigation was accomplished using a Garmin GPSMAP 60CSx Global Positioning System (GPS) with accuracy to less than 10 m, while a JRC Model DGPS212 GPS with accuracy to less than 5 m inputted location data directly to the hydroacoustic system where they were incorporated into the recorded hydroacoustic data files. Prior to the surveys, the hydroacoustic system had been calibrated using a tungsten carbide sphere of target strength (TS) -39.5 dB at a sound velocity of 1470 ms-1. Hydroacoustic surveys were undertaken between c. 20.29 and 21.34 hours on 13 August 2007 using 20 transects, each starting near the 10 m depth contour and running directly inshore to a depth of c. 1 m. Transects varied in length from c. 20 to 226 m depending on local bathymetry and hindrances to navigation in the form of semi-submerged fences or near-surface rocks.\t\tThe report and data collected under CCW contract FC 73-02-304 and is archived as Project 196 (Media 542) and is stored in fireproof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Ffynnon on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage. H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media542\tInternal Custodian:  Rhian Thomas, Team Leader WFD, Water Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tSnowdonia National Park Authority-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-02-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17687\tNRW_DS110067\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.359Z\t281\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Modelling for Llangorse Lake\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303637\tLlangorse Lake is the largest natural eutrophic lake in Wales and has been designated as a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The lake has a history of eutrophication and so it was decided to undertake a nutrient modelling and nutrient budget exercise to determine the size and sources of nutrient loads entering the lake. Llangorse Lake has a history of eutrophication problems believed to have been caused by high nutrient loads entering the lake from the surrounding catchment. This study aimed to determine the size and main sources of those loads. Specific objectives include: -  To review existing data and information on the water quality of Llangorse Lake. -  To conduct a field study to investigate the nutrient budget of the lake. -  To model the nutrient delivery from the catchment to the lake, with particular focus on phosphorus delivery, to give an indication of source apportionment. -  To prepare a draft catchment management plan.\tThe nutrient load to the lake was estimated from nutrient concentrations and flows measured on the inflow streams. Nutrient concentrations were also measured in the outflow to provide an indication of lake water quality. The 8 monitoring sites were chosen to include the main drainage channels into and out of the lake and with sampling points located as close to the lake as practically possible. Water and flow sampling was conducted at fortnightly intervals for one year.  Hydraulic load to the lake was calculated as were nutrient concentration in the lake inflows- specifically phosphorous and nitrate. Phosphorous concentrations in the sediments were collected and analysed.\t\tArchived as project number 202, media number 567 H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media567\tInternal Custodian: Senior Freshwater Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:17:59.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17693\tNRW_DS110068\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.553Z\t340\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Dee Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Comparative Macrophyte Surveys (2008)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303638\tThe Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned ECUS Ltd. to undertake macrophyte survey work on the River Dee Special Area of Conservation (SAC) to inform a larger project aiming to determine the main variability components for macrophyte communities in rivers. The objective of the larger project, a partnership between CCW, Natural England, the Environment Agency (EA), Scottish Environmental Protection Agency (SEPA) and Scotland and Northern Ireland Forum For Environmental Research (SNIFFER), is to develop and test a statistically robust, standardised field sampling method for river macrophytes, to be used by the agencies for assessment of ecological and conservation status of rivers.\tThere are a variety of macrophyte survey methods currently used within the UK, which have been developed over the years for differing purposes and therefore have different emphasises built into their design. One standard method, developed by the NCC for classification of vegetation communities of British Rivers, was updated to become the JNCC Standard Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) methodology and is currently used to by the UK conservation agencies to determine the condition status of Riverine SSSI (Site of Special Scientific Interest) and SACs (Special Area of Conservation). The CSM methodology is based on the survey of 500 m stretches, recording macrophyte species present using a 3 point cover score. It was originally designed primarily as a means of surveying river reaches in order to record the maximum number of species present. Another commonly used survey method is the Mean Trophic Rank (MTR) methodology, developed by the Environment Agency to assess the impact of sewage treatment works on the trophic status of the rivers into which they discharge, as required by the EC Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive. The survey methodology, which uses a 100 m survey section and records macrophyte species using a 9 point cover scale, has been adopted in a slightly modified version as the new LEAFPACS methodology developed by the Environmental Protection Agencies (EA and SEPA) for the purposes of monitoring ecological status of rivers as required by the EU Water Framework Directive (WFD).\t\tThe report and data collected are archived as Project 211 as Media 576 and are stored in fireproof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Ffynnon. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage.\tInternal Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group. NRw  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17684\tNRW_DS110069\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.936Z\t252\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of the Impact of Hydraulic Cockle Dredging on the Macroinvertebrate Faunas of Traeth Lafan (1993-1994)\tAsesiad o effaith cribinio hydrolig am gocos ar ffawna macroinfertebratau Traeth Lafan (1993-1994)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303639\tThis dataset contains the results of a macroinvertebrate and sediment survey conducted post hydraulic cockle dredging around Traeth Lafan, North Wales. The purpose of this data collection was to study the effects of hydraulic suction dredging on benthic invertebrate macrofaunas, substrate conformation and sediment composition on Traeth Lafan. The survey was located at Traeth Lafan, the northern entrance to the Menai Strait (Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC)).  A survey of the macroinvertebrates and sediments of Traeth Lafan, North Wales, was undertaken following extensive hydraulic cockle dredging in autumn 1993. The survey covered the site allocated for dredging and part of the hand gathering area, which was used as \u0027control\u0027, against which changes on the dredged ground could be compared. A follow up survey, using the same sampling grid and rationale, was carried out twelve months later, in November 1994, to ascertain the recovery state of the area after a full season\u0027s recruitment.\tThe survey consisted of a widespread survey using a grid of single sample stations and an intensive survey of four stations with replicate sampling, deployed across the dredged area. Both the single survey grid and one replicate station covered an area of \u0027control\u0027 ground allocated to hand gathering. 0.01m-2 x 0.2 sediment cores were collected at each sampling station and surface evidence of deeper burrowing animals was recorded. A sample was taken of the surface sediment for granulometric analysis, substrate shear strength was measured and the presence of other physical features recorded. Samples were sent to the laboratory for analysis. There is a high confidence in this data, however no spatial reference were provided with data for statistical analysis. It is assumed for the purpose of the metadata that samples were collected using Latitude and Logitude (WGS84).  The post disturbance ecological survey was carried out on 12th December 2003 and 8th January 2004 with the follow up survey undertaken on the 28th and 29th of December 1994.  Confidence in this dataset is high, however no spatial reference were provided with data for statistical analysis. It is assumed for the purpose of the metadata that samples were collected using Latitude and Logitude (WGS84).\tThere is an associated report to accompany this dataset:  P.Allen., CCW. 1995 An assessment of the impact of hydraulic cockle dredging on the macroinvertebrate faunas of Traeth Lafan, North Wales CCW Science Report No. 64. Bangor.\t[A] Data is held as Report  (project?) No.184, media 523 (File path: K\\:Archive\\Media 501-600\\Media 523 [B] Survey data in Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000693)\tMetadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW Internal Contact: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1995-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:06:17.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17692\tNRW_DS110070\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.551Z\t391\tValid\tEditable\tAcross Wales Drop-Down Video Monitoring 2007 - 2010\tGwaith monitor cwymp-gamerau ar draws Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303730\tThe aim of this data capture was to report on the conservation status of the habitats and species within Wales\u0027 marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and enable reporting of their favourable conservation status as well as to inform site management.  Drop-down video surveys of SACs across Wales. Works is carried out by Royal Haskoning under contract to CCW and focuses on Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC and Pembrokeshire Marine SAC.    This project also aims to improve design and implementation of the project, harmonise the work in SACs with Water Framework Directive monitoring in overlapping areas and develop and refine Quality Control and Quality Assurance for drop-down video techniques.\tFollowing methodological and equipment trial (Sanderson et al, 2001; Southeran et al, 2004) drop-down video survey methods were employed extensively in Welsh SACs in 2004 and 2005 (Axelsson et al, 2006). The 2004 survey work focused on Cardigan Bay SAC, while the 2005 survey work focused on Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau, Menai Strait and Conwy Bay, Cardigan Bay and Pembrokeshire Marine.    This data is a continuation of work completed in 2004-2005.  Drop-down video is useful for gathering large volumes of data across wide areas of seabed and often to depths beyond which divers observations can be safely made. Detailed methods and site locations are described in CCW Marine Monitoring Report 24a.   This project also aims to improve design and implementation of the project, harmonise the work in SACs with Water Framework Directive monitoring in overlapping areas and develop and refine Quality Control and Quality Assurance for drop-down video techniques. A subset of data was subject to ring testing using NMBAQC procedures.\tSee metadata NRW_DS87090 for more information about 2004-2005 data. Non-sensitive species data is published and available to download from the NBN Atlas as part of the \"NRW Marine data from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Technical Support (Research \u0026amp; Monitoring) Contracts, Wales\" dataset (NRW_DS116350)\t[A] Data archived as project no. 35, media 1328.   (K:\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1328) - older data is held in media no. 175. [B]  Fire-proof storage facilities at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon on optical media.  [C]  Videos are maintained in the archive within multiple Media folders (see Archive inventory) but an index can be accessed through GIS layers X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine habitat video points\tCharles Lindenbaum, Specialist (Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer), Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Marine Archive: Monica Jones, Data Services Team Leader\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:07:13.188Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17683\tNRW_DS110072\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.970Z\t377\tValid\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Littoral Communities Macro scale\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Monitro cymunedau arfordirol Macro scale\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303732\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.   This data documents gross changes in shores and shore communities by means of viewpoint photography; identify shore or parameters requiring further or specific monitoring. The purpose of this data capture was to document gross scale changes in zonation and beach profiles.\tPhotographic and field records were collated.  More detail will be available in the Marine Monitoring Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) available to request from NRWs internal Document Management System (DMS).\t\tImages -  \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\docs and settings\\Monitoring\\littoral view points ,   SOP - DMS:  (Location to be provided by Helen Carby)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:07:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17686\tNRW_DS110073\t2024-05-28T12:29:34.316Z\t536\tValid\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Recreational Activities Occurring in and around Skomer\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Gweithgareddau hamdden yn, ac o amgylch, Ynys Sgomer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303733\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.    Records of all the recreational activities occurring in and around Skomer MNR. Including; diving, angling, canoe trips, boating activity and any incidents. The purpose of this data capture was to document the usage of the MNR.\tField records - activity, name and number of participants were recorded.  More detail will be available in the Marine Monitoring Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs), available to request from NRWs internal Document Management System (DMS).\t\tImages -  \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\docs and settings\\Monitoring\\littoral view points ,   SOP - DMS:  (Location to be provided by Helen Carby)\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:08:51.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17682\tNRW_DS110074\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.859Z\t639\tValid\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Monitoring Grey Seals Halichoerus grypus\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Monitro morloi llwydion Halichoerus grypus\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303734\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.   Grey seals are a protected species of conservation importance, which live and breed in the Skomer MNR. The West Wales population is the largest in SW Britain and is a feature of the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC).   The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the number and survival rate of seal pups born in the MNR.\tThe pups are recorded from birth through to their first moult using the \"Smith 5-fold classification system\" (Poole 1996). Reason for death is recorded if possible. Additional behavioural observations are recorded for the Island seals (Full method described in \u0027Grey Seal Monitoring Handbook\u0027 Poole 1996).   Regular recording began on Skomer Island in 1974 and surveys have been completed annually since 1983. From 1992 onwards a standard protocol has been adopted to record the pupping success on both the island and the mainland each year. Records of identifying features e.g. scars are recorded.  More detail will be available in the Marine Monitoring Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs), available to request from NRWs internal Document Management System (DMS).\t\tData - Skomer MNR, West Region drive \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\shared documents\\data\\biological data\\seals ,  Images -  \\\\NOA020 - Synology DiskStation\\docs and settings\\Monitoring\\Seals ,   SOP - DMS:  (Location to be provided by Helen Carby) Off-site boxes of the original paper data CCW/WRPB/003 \u0026amp; 004 (1991-1994 data)\tInternal Custodian: Kate Lock, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Internal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:09:27.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17685\tNRW_DS110082\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.971Z\t613\tValid\tEditable\tSpatial Analysis of Intertidal and Subtidal Biotopes in Welsh Waters\tDadansoddiad gofodol o fioteipiau rhynglanwol ac islanwol yn nyfroedd Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303832\tMaps depicting the distribution of marine biotopes are now available for the whole of the Welsh intertidal and most of the seabed in Welsh Territorial Waters.  The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Phase 1 intertidal database provides the former; and the CCW HABMAP database provides the latter.    Using those databases, while taking account of their limitations, CCW wish to know the proportions of each major biotope group that lies within protected areas  - i.e. Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSIs) and Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).  This report provides a spatial analysis of those biotope data. Various data issues mean that these are only very rough calculations; the percentages are not definitive values.\tFor intertidal habitats, the Phase 1 intertidal survey data was used and the percentages of different biotopes at level 2 and level 3 of the 1997 biotope classification within SSSIs and SACs was calculated. For subtidal habitats, the HABMAP project data was used to calculate percentages of different biotopes at levels 2 and 3 of the 2004 biotope classification in SACs.   Project uses data from CCW Phase 1 intertidal survey and the HABMAP dataset.\t\tArchived as project 203, media number 568 on backed up server-based storage at the Countryside Council for Wales (File path is Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media501to600\\Media568)\tInternal Custodian: NRW Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-09-11\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:10:23.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17680\tNRW_DS110095\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.693Z\t564\tValid\tEditable\tSkomer Island National Nature Reserve (NNR) - Resurvey of Lichen Quadrats\tGNG Ynys Sgomer - Ail-arolwg o gwadradau cennau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303935\tThis dataset is made up of the results from a survey of lichen distribution and cover on Skomer Island following the Sea Empress oil spill. Pembrokeshire is of the highest significance for coastal saxicolous and terricolous lichens and Skomer is a key site in Countryside Council for Wales’ (CCW\u0027s) lichen monitoring programme.   After the Sea Empress oil spill SEEEC decided the Skomer lichens should be re-surveyed. Record type, distribution and extent of lichens within the 40 quadrats established on Skomer Island. Compare these records with those from earlier surveys and determine the nature of any changes that have taken place. Interpret any changes as far as possible, with special consideration given to those that may be attributable to oil pollution. Re-mark quadrats by indicating their precise position.\tSkomer was re-visited during the 6th - 12th April 1997. Quadrats were used for counts. The location and quadrat photographs were taken and recorded as in 1991.  Two sizes, 20x30 cm and 10x15 cm with steel wires giving 100 intersection points, were used as defined in Purvis (1992).   All photography was carried out using an Olympus OM-2 camera on a tripod. Monitoring efforts and impact assessment following the Sea Empress oil spill. An initial lichen survey was perfomed by Wolseley and James 1988, in 1991 further quadrats were surveyed by the Natural History Museum and a subsequent re-survey was performed in 1994.\t\tArchived at Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media044.    Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW MyF Maes y Fynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:11:18.114Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17679\tNRW_DS110096\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.675Z\t589\tValid\tEditable\tDevelopment and Field Trials of a Drop-Down Video System (2003)\tDatblygu system fideo gwympo a\u0027i threialu yn y maes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303936\tEnvision Mapping was contracted by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to develop a purpose built drop-down video system suitable for marine monitoring work. Detailed specifications were prepared and the system was manufactured by C-Technics, a specialist underwater video systems manufacturer. The system was trialled during the manufacturing process and modifications made prior to the system being completed. Upon completion the system was commissioned at three locations in Wales. This tested the system in field conditions for both operational suitability and also enabled the system to be modified to best suit the requirements of CCW.\tThe system was deployed in various sea states over a 5 day period, in Tremadog Bay, close to St Tudwal’s Islands, the Menai Strait and Milford Haven. The system was deployed from the RIB Aplysia and the RV Skalmey. The methods used were similar to the procedural and operational guidelines presented in the report, but various configuration of camera position, buoyancy and towing techniques were tried.   The equipment and deployment techniques were refined and adjusted to produce the optimal configuration and operational techniques for various conditions and these methods are presented in the procedural and operational guidelines.   Recommendations are made for methods of data collection, the effects of weather conditions, sea state and tides on the deployment of the system. Because the system is to be used on various vessels, examples of mobilisation configurations are presented.\t\tZ:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media101to200\\Media162\tNRW Marine Monitoring Team, NRW. (original project commissioned by Rohan Holt - Marine and Freshwater Ecosystem Group)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:13:12.078Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17681\tNRW_DS110097\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.782Z\t412\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSediment shore impact assessment- monitoring: strandline fauna\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303937\tThe present study investigates the effects of the Sea Empress oil spill on the invertebrate populations of the strandline on two selected soft sediment shores, Pembrey and Pendine, in Carmarthen Bay, South Wales.  It represents one of four sampling protocols initially proposed by University of Wales, Swansea (March, 1996) and is the protocol selected by CCW (September, 1996).  A comparison can be made between the findings for one of the areas studied (Pembrey) with the results of a previous study on the strandline fauna of the same area (Llewellyn, 1996). Data was used to investigate the effects of the Sea Empress oil spill on the invertebrate populations of strandlines.\tSites were visited during the highest spring tide of the month and the resulting strandline deposit was sampled the following day or as soon as possible afterwards. Due to erosion at Pendine and Pembrey areas were sampled where wave action had made the least physical damage. Eight replicate samples of the strandline were taken on each beach using a 0.25m2 box quadrat which was driven into the sediment to a depth of 15 cm. Surface debris and sand down to a depth of 20 cm was removed, and later sieved to remove the fauna for analysis. The nature of strandline debris was also noted. Increased monitoring efforts and impact assessment following the Sea Empress oil spill. This dataset contributed to the Pembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas, see separate metadata entry. The only data associated with this project is held within the report.\t\t[A] Report is archived as Project No. 14, Media No. 40 (K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 040) [B] Fireproof safe - Stack 01, Hafod Elfyn, NRW-Maes y Fynnon, Bangor, Wales. [C] CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (Survey number:MRCCW10000000078)\tInternal Contact: Master Reference Data Team Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17678\tNRW_DS110098\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.695Z\t308\tValid\tEditable\tAppraisal of Environmental Impact and Recovery Using Laminaria Holdfast Faunas\tArfarnu\u0027r effaith ar yr amgylchedd ac ymadferiad yr amgylchedd gan ddefnyddio ffawna gludafaelion Laminaria\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303938\tThe Sea Empress spill provided an opportunity to test the applicability of Laminaria holdfast faunal communities for detecting the pollution effects of a putative oil contamination gradient, and to assess the immediate response and consequent recovery of components of the holdfast fauna to oil pollution.   Data captured to test the applicability of Laminaria holdfast faunal communities for detecting the pollution effects of a putative oil contamination gradient, and to assess the immediate response and consequent recovery of components of the holdfast fauna to oil pollution.\tFour sites were chosen to reflect potentially differing levels of impact from the oil spill. The maximum length (L), breadth (nearest 0.1 cm) of every holdfast was being measured, in order to calculate the volume of each holdfast using the formula of Jones (1971). Holdfasts were broken up and sieved to collect and identify macrofauna and meiofauna.  The amount of oil in the Laminaria stipes was quantified ( g oil per g algae) fluorometrically using a sycnhronous scanning (23 nm interval) technique, with yellow fuel oil as a standard Warwick and Clarke (1995). Univariate \u0027biodiversity\u0027 measures, termed taxonomic diversity  and taxonomic distinctness used to describe community composition and structure.   The Welsh and North Devon coastal sites include: West Angle, East Angle, Dale Fort, West Blockhouse, Gateholm, Martin\u0027s Haven, Musselwick, St. Brides, Abereiddy, Porth Gain, Aber Draw, Abercastle, Hartland Quay, Welcombe Mouth, Morwenstow, Duckpool.  Monitoring and impact assessment following the Sea Empress oil spill.   This dataset contributed to the Pembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas, see separate metadata entry.\t\tThe report and data are held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 15, media No.41 (pathway: Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media041).   Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW MyF Headquarters, Bangor, Wales.  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder. [MRCCW10000000093]\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team, NRW.   Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1999-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:12:44.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17677\tNRW_DS110099\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.567Z\t324\tValid\tEditable\tStatus and Breeding of Cormorants Phalacrocorax carbo in Wales (1996-1997): The Effect of the Sea Empress Oil Spill\tStatws a nythu mulfrain Phalacrocorax carbo yng Nghymru: effaith gollyngiad olew\u0027r Sea Empress\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130303939\tThe Sea Empress Environmental Evaluation Committee (SEEEC) and the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned various studies of the effects of the oil spill on the wildlife of Pembrokeshire. This dataset contains the results of work carried out to investigate the effects of the spill on Cormorants Phalacrocorax carbo.    In 1996 the project aimed to carry out a survey of the numbers, distribution and breeding performance of cormorants breeding in Wales, including detailed case studies at colonies in the area contaminated by oil in south and west Pembrokeshire.  Monitoring of numbers and breeding performance was continued in 1997 with detailed studies of breeding phenology and breeding success at two South Pembrokeshire colonies (St Margaret\u0027s Island and Green Scar).   Data was used to assess the numbers and distribution of Cormorants breeding in Wales; and to investigate the breeding performance of Cormorants in Wales, including detailed case studies at colonies in the area contaminated by oil in south and west Pembrokeshire.\t1996: Case studies of breeding phenology and breeding success were carried out at four colonies in South Pembrokeshire. The count unit adopted was the apparently occupied nest (aon). A colony was defined as any aggregation of nests separated by at least 500 m from the next nearest aggregation.  Breeding cormorants were counted by single counts timed to coincide with the main incubation or early chick-rearing stages (late May to early June)At colonies in which studies of breeding biology were being conducted repeat counts throughout the breeding period, also recording the position and status of all nests were taken. This technique allows early failures and late starters to be identified and is therefore the most accurate method. Brood size was monitored at 11 colonies in Wales: four in South Pembrokeshire close to the source of the oil spill , three in Cardigan Bay and four in North Wales.  For comparison, data were also collected from three colonies in Devon and one in Caithness.  All were visited on at least one occasion when the young were between four weeks old and fledging.    1997: Survey methodology follows that of Cormorant Breeding Colony Survey ( Sellers 1997). Brood size monitored at 8 colonies and three visits were made to St Margaret\u0027s Island and Green Scar to monitor breeding success.\t\tReports are archived as Project No\u0027s. 17 and 115, and Media No\u0027s. 49 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media049) and 383 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media301to400\\Media383).   Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW- Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.\tInternal Contact: Marine Monitoring Team Leader (was Bill Sanderson at the point of collection, now Lucie Haines), NRW Marine Ornithologist, NRW  (Matty Murphy)  Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:14:08.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17676\tNRW_DS110100\t2024-05-28T12:29:33.409Z\t284\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOrigins, population structure, pathology and diet samples of diver and auk casualties of the Sea Empress oil spill\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313030\tThis dataset provides data on the examination of dead oiled seabirds from southwest Wales collected after the February 1996 Sea Empress oil spill in order to assess impact and damage done by the spill. Data was collected in order to determine origins, pathology and diet of casualties (seabirds affected by the Sea Empress oil spill).\tMeasurements and other data for age/sex structure of samples, condition and possible origins mainly followed Hope Jones et al. (1982a).  All birds were dissected unwashed and the head, left wing and a tarsus were detached and washed before measurement.  All birds were dissected for sexing and to assess condition by scoring subcutaneous and visceral fat, from none (0) to heavy (3) (Hope Jones et al. 1982a). Contents of stomachs and oesophagi were examined for a different sub-sample to that used for pathology.  Contents were examined with a binocular microscope at 10 x magnification and a scanning electron microscope where necessary.  Food remains were compared with reference collections for identification.  Non-food items were also recorded. Increased monitoring effort and impact assessment following the Sea Empress oil spill.\t\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media001to100\\Media055 Data is stored within the report. Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elfyn, CCW Headquarters, Bangor\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy, MFSG, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17733\tNRW_DS110101\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.783Z\t280\tValid\tEditable\tThe Impact of the Sea Empress Oil Spill on the Prosobranch Mollusc Paludinella littorina\tEffaith gollyngiad olew\u0027r Sea Empress ar y molwsg cloriog Paludinella littorina\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313031\tIn March 1996 the Sea Empress Environmental Evaluation Committee (SEEEC) was set up by the government to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on the environment.  This contract was issued as part of the SEEEC monitoring programme designed to evaluate the impact of oil from the Sea Empress on local marine life.   Data collected in order to ascertain whether Paludinella littorina still lived at its known sites at Broad Haven and on Caldey Island after the Sea Empress oil spill and to obtain more information on the extent and density of the populations. The objectives were to:   1.  Ascertain whether Paludinella littorina is still living at its known sites at Broad Haven and on Caldey Island and to obtain more information on the extent and density of the populations.  2.  Attempt an assessment of the impact of oil damage, if any and subsequent recovery, to habitats at known Paludinella littorina sites.  3.  Locate and investigate other sites for Paludinella littorina on Caldey Island.  4.  Evaluate methods for monitoring and recommend a protocol for future monitoring work.\tSurvey work for the present contract was focused at the two known Paludinella localities: Broad Haven near Bosherston, and Caldey Island.    At Broad Haven this involved close examination of a north-west facing wall within a cave where snails had been seen on the previous survey.  Additional surfaces further inside the cave and towards the exterior walls and entrance were also searched.   On Caldey Island two sites investigated in 1994 were revisited and additional sites of potential habitat on the island were examined. During fieldwork photographs were taken of sites including overall views and more focused pictures of habitat. It was not necessary to remove any animals or algae from the field for laboratory investigation.  Increased monitoring efforts and impact assessment following the Sea Empress oil spill.   This dataset contributed to the Pembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas, see separate metadata entry. The species formed the basis of survey work carried out  in 1994 to assess the population at its known Welsh site at Broad Haven.\t\tReports are held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 21 and Media No. 56 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media056).   Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.  NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder [MRCCW10000000089]\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:15:47.384Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17726\tNRW_DS110102\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.485Z\t448\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAutecological study of Thick Top Shell, Osilinus lineatus (Monodonta lineata), in the area affected by the Sea Empress oil spill (1996 and 1997).\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313032\tAfter the oil spill from the Sea Empress investigations were initiated into the biota of many of the habitats affected. One of the species known to be susceptible to oil pollution, that had been studied previously and that would not be adequately investigated by the proposed investigations was the toothed or thick top shell Osilinus lineatus, an animal commonly found on shores in western Britain. Data collected in order to establish the population age and size structure at each study site and allow an assessment of the impact the Sea Empress oil spill has had on these populations of Osilinus linaetus.  As of 1 April 2013, the Countryside Council for Wales, Environment Agency Wales and Forestry Commission Wales became Natural Resources Wales / Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru.\tIn August 1996 six sites were visited in five days to set up sample populations of Osilinus lineatus to investigate the population size, distribution and age structure at sites where such studies had previously been carried out albeit over a decade ago. This first fieldwork in 1996 established that it would be necessary to allow one tide per site and that realistically after the end of August that means one day for each site because of shortening day length. A simple Lincoln index, mark-release-recapture technique was used to estimate the size of the population from which the survey samples were taken. In September 1997 all of the sites were revisited to sample the population, and where reasonably large samples could be found, they were marked and released. Fieldwork in 1997 included additional checks on 1996 recruitment as the growing animals became easier to spot as they changed their habits and moved around into less cryptic micro habitats.  Between 1982 and 1983 a programme of experimental field work was conducted to complement the fixed, interrupted, belt transect monitoring studies of rocky shores in Milford Haven.  The only data associated with this project is held within the report.\t\tThe report is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 23 and Media No. 62 (File path K:\\Media 001to100\\Media 62) Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor,\tMetadata Point of Contact: Data Services Team, NRW Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1999-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17723\tNRW_DS110103\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.495Z\t298\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommon scoter Melanitta nigra monitoring in Carmarthen Bay following the Sea Empress oil spill.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313033\tFollowing the Sea Empress oil spill, a monitoring programme was immediately established by the Countryside Council for Wales to record numbers and distribution of Common Scoters in Carmarthen Bay. Scoters generally prefer feeding in waters less than 10 m deep and previous aerial surveys in Carmarthen Bay have shown that most birds feed inshore of the 10 m depth contour which extends some 6-10 km offshore.  The purpose of this data capture was to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on Common Scoter numbers and distribution in Camarthen Bay.  There is high confidence in this dataset as surveys undertaken by experienced staff members. However no spatial reference were provided with data for statistical analysis. It is assumed for the purpose of the metadata that samples were collected using British National Grid.\tA total of 30 land-based counts were conducted at two-week intervals between 23 February 1996 and 1 April 1997. For monitoring purposes, the bay was divided into 13 sectors and each counted from vantage points collectively covering all coastal areas of the bay. Six aerial surveys of Carmarthen Bay were conducted by RSPB using a high-wing Cessna 185 aircraft flying at 120 m and 90 knots. For compatibility with previous surveys in 1976, aerial surveys followed a circular route of the bay with transect lines some 3-5 km apart. As birds were only visible at distances up to 500 m either side of the aircraft, this survey route provided an index of scoter numbers and distribution rather than a total count. A sustained program of monitoring scoters in Carmarthen bay has been in place since 1997.\t\tReport is held as Project No. 24, media 70.\tInternal Custodian: Patrick Lindley, Maritime Ornithologist, Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17734\tNRW_DS110113\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.788Z\t390\tValid\tEditable\tStatus of Breeding Common Scoter Melanitta nigra nigra in Britain and Ireland in 1996: The Effect of the Sea Empress Oil Spill\tStatws mor-hwyaid duon melanitta nigra nigra oedd yn nythu ym Mhrydain ac Iwerddon yn 1996: effaith gollyngiad olew\u0027r Sea Empress\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313133\tFollowing concerns regarding potential effects of the Sea Empress oil spill in Carmarthen Bay, February 1996, on the British and Irish populations of breeding Common Scoters Melanitta nigra nigra, their status and distribution were determined from a survey in May - June 1996.  The survey was undertaken in five areas (Shetland, the Flow country, West and South Scotland, Islay and Ireland), using methods devised during the first complete British and Irish census of this species in 1995.  Survey effort was concentrated on sites that held Common Scoters in 1995, and on sites with historical records.    The survey visited 223 sites and located 376 birds.  This was 79 fewer birds than the previous year, a 17% decline in numbers (16% in Ireland and 19% in Britain), a 19% decrease in occupied 10-km squares and a 31% decrease in occupied sites, compared to visits made at a similar time in 1995.  It is possible that a decline of this magnitude may be within the range of natural population fluctuations or it could be due to the late arrival of birds after the late spring in 1996.  However, over the last eight years, the number of Common Scoters in the Flow country has declined by 42% from an estimated 48 pairs in 1988 to only 28 in 1996.  Given the Common Scoter\u0027s threatened status in the UK, that the dynamics of the population are unknown, and the potential decline, annual monitoring of both numbers and productivity is a high priority. Data used to assess the potential effects of the Sea Empress oil spill in Carmarthen Bay, February 1996, on the British and Irish populations of breeding Common Scoters Melanitta nigra nigra.\tSites on the Scottish mainland were surveyed from 12-18 May and Islay was surveyed on 7 June.  In Ireland, Loughs Conn/Cullin, Corrib and Arrow were surveyed between 16-19 May, but surveys of Lough Ree were delayed until 22-25 May by poor weather.   During each survey, all open water and the entire mainland and island shorelines were scanned for birds using binoculars or telescopes.  Most observations were land based, but boat surveys were used on larger and complex sites. Clear, calm weather was selected for all field visits and observers recorded the number of birds present, classified into pairs (male and female in continuous close consort), single adult males, single immature males, unaged males, single females and unsexed birds.  To minimise the risk of double counting birds, where possible, surveys were organised to cover adjacent sites simultaneously.    In order to investigate whether there had been any decline in numbers between 1995 and 1996, the numbers of birds present per site were analysed using a generalised linear model with Poisson error distribution and log link function in the GLIM statistical package.  Sites were selected for analysis if birds were present either during the 1996 survey, or during the comparable visit in 1995.  Four explanatory variables were included in the model: sex, area, site, and year.\t\tReport is held as Project No. 25, media 71 (File path: Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media071),   Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW MyF Headquarters, Bangor\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW (At the point of collection was Bill Sanderson, now Lucie Haines) Metadata Contact: Data services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1996-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:16:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17722\tNRW_DS110118\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.533Z\t353\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommon scoter (Melanitta nigra) Monitoring in Carmarthen Bay following the Sea Empress Oil Spill. April 1997 to March 1998.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313138\tThe Common Scoter Melanitta nigra is listed in Annex II/2 of the EC Directive on the Conservation of Wild Birds (79/409/EEC), and occurs on the red list of the UK\u0027s Species of Conservation Concern. Carmarthen Bay, southwest Wales, is by far the most important wintering site in Wales. On 24 February 1996, large amounts of oil from the stricken Sea Empress tanker entered Carmarthen Bay.  Following the oil spill, a monitoring programme was immediately established by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to record numbers and distribution of Common Scoters in Carmarthen Bay. Data collected to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on common scoter populations in Carmarthen Bay, Wales.\tA total of 26 land-based counts were conducted at two-week intervals between 21 March 1997 and 14 March 1998. A similar monitoring strategy to that used during the previous year was implemented to ensure compatibility of data: Carmarthen Bay was divided into 13 sectors and each counted from vantage points collectively covering all coastal areas of the bay. Four aerial surveys of Carmarthen Bay were conducted during 1997/98: on 22 November 1997 and 31 January, 21 February and 14 March 1998.  As in the previous winter, a high-winged Cessna 185 was used, flying at 500-550 ft altitude and at 90-100 knots.  A sustained program of monitoring scoters in Carmarthen bay has been in place since 1997.\t\tReport is held as Project No. 26, media 72\tInternal Custodian: - Patrick Lindley Maritime Ornithologist, NRW - Mike Camplin Team Leader, Specialist Monitoring, NRW.  Metadata contact: - Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1998-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:14:03.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17731\tNRW_DS110119\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.717Z\t321\tValid\tEditable\tSandy Shore Meiofauna of South West Wales-Impact of the Sea Empress Oil Spill\tMeioffawna traethau tywod De Orllewin Cymru - effaith gollyngiad olew\u0027r Sea Empress\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313139\tThe high species diversity of meiofaunal communities imply a complex and intricate ecological web. However, relatively little information has been recorded on the ecological interactions of meiofauna. This is largely due to the practical difficulties involved in the study of these organisms. These difficulties arise from the small body size of meiofauna, their cryptic habitat and the taxonomic difficulties caused by their high species diversity. Various authors have suggested that meiofauna are potentially useful indicators of anthropogenic perturbations of the marine environment (Heip, 1980; Moore and Bett, 1989; Platt and Warwick, 1980; Vincx and Heip, 1991). This data was captured with the aim of assessing the impact of pollution from the Sea Empress oil spill on the structure and composition of meiofaunal communities of intertidal sandy beaches.\tThe study sites consist of twelve intertidal sandy beaches covering an area from St. David\u0027s Head in the north-west to Pendine Sands in the south-east.    Meiofauna: Three replicate cores were collected from each site for faunal analysis.   Physico-chemical samples: Three replicate cores were collected for particle size analysis and assessment of water content.   An additional single core was collected for the analysis of organic carbon. A single sediment core was collected for hydrocarbon analysis.  Three replicate pore water samples were collected at each site for salinity measurements. During November 1994 a range of sandy beaches on the Pembrokeshire coast were sampled as part of a biogeographical study of harpacticoid copepods. These samples provide a baseline data set for the assessment of oil pollution induced changes in the meiofaunal community composition.   This dataset contributed to the Pembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas, see separate metadata entry.\t\tReport and data is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 67, media No. 43 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media043),  Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.   NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder [MRCCW10000000072]\tNRW Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Seems more likely that Kirsten Ramsay or Matt Green would be a suitable contact\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-07-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:17:51.308Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17719\tNRW_DS110120\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.536Z\t637\tValid\tEditable\tThe Abundance and Distribution of Waterfowl Within Milford Haven After the Sea Empress Oil Spill\tToreithrwydd a dosbarthiad adar dr yn Aberdaugleddau ar ol gollyngiad olew\u0027r Sea Empress\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313230\tThis study was put in place to assess whether the oil spill was to impact the nationally important waterfowl populations found in the area of Milford Haven.   Two polluted (Angle Bay and Pembroke River) and two relatively unpolluted \u0027control\u0027 sites (the Carew/Cresswell and Cleddau Rivers) were monitored intensively over the three winters after the oil spill. The four sites have been monitored following standard Wetland Bird Survey (WeBS) methodology to assess the numbers of waterfowl present, and following standard British Trust for Ornithology all-day (across the tidal cycle) count methodology to assess the feeding areas most used.   The data was captured to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on waterfowl populations in the Milford Haven area.   Counts during winter took place between November and March as the estuary is most important for birds during the winter period.  The migration and early autumn periods have been excluded because numbers of birds can fluctuate widely from year to year, for reasons not associated with the estuary.  Each site was counted twice a month with one count on a spring tide and one on a neap tide where possible.  In the first year, only one count was carried out at each site in November. Historical data was used from 1982-1999.\tThis study monitored the use made by waterbirds of intertidal areas in the Cleddau Estuary over three winter seasons, in relation to oiling levels. Four sites were studied:   - a heavily oiled site, Angle Bay;   - a moderately oiled site, Pembroke River, and   - two effectively non-oiled sites, the Carew/Cresswell rivers and the \u0027Upper Cleddau\u0027, where the Eastern and Western Cleddau meet.    The species recorded include divers, grebes, cormorants, herons, wildfowl, waders and auks.  In order to monitor all species comprehensively, an area of open water was also counted at each site. In order to gain a full picture of the use of the four intertidal areas by waterfowl and other birds, counts were carried out across the tidal cycle.  This choice of methodology allows the most important feeding areas to be identified.  Each site was counted once every hour from six hours before to five hours after low tide.  Increased monitoring efforts and impact assessment following the Sea Empress oil spill.\t\tReport and data are held within the Marine Arcive as Project No. 69, Media No\u0027s. 45-47  (File Path: Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media045 ,  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media046 ,  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media047)  Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW MyF Headquarters, Bangor\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy Marine Ornithologist, NRW. /  Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW (now Lucie Haines).  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2000-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:19:31.020Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17729\tNRW_DS110121\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.547Z\t441\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOil contamination of gannets and their nests on Grassholm subsequent to the sea Empress oil spill.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313231\tGrassholm, situated 23 km WNW of the entrance to Milford Haven, supports the worlds third largest gannetry with an estimated population of 30,000 pairs.  Gannet nests are perennial constructions 30-60 cm high, built out of seaweed, grass and items of floating debris collected from the sea.  Almost all gannet nests, therefore, contain man-made materials, often in substantial quantities. Dataset contains results of investigations to assess whether nest material contaminated with oil from the Sea Empress was incorporated into gannet nests on Grassholm and, if so, whether there was any effect on fertility or productivity in contaminated nests. At the time of the Sea Empress incident it is estimated that 80 percent of the adult breeding population of gannets on Grassholm had returned to the colony.  On a ship based survey between the 25-29/2/96, immediately after the spill, only 0.39 percent of 1,279 gannets recorded showed some degree of oiling, and a similar percentage were observed carrying nest material.  The small proportion carrying nest material suggests that at that time they had not started nest building.\tOiling rate of adult gannets within the colony was determined by scoring approximately 1000 birds in a subsection of the colony. Oil contamination was taken as any visible oil spot. Counts were made using binoculars on the 7/4/96, 12/5/95 and 9/4/97 (Bullock/RSPB, 1998). A  measure of productivity  obtained by scoring approximately 1000 nests. Success was defined as the production of a live offspring i.e. that an egg hatched. For the purposes of a \u0027site\u0027 control, ten samples were obtained in May 1996 from nests on St Kilda, the worlds largest gannetry, situated approximately 60 km west of the Outer Hebrides and 700 km NNW of Grassholm. Samples from Grassholm were collected on the 8/11/96.  Nest piles were cut in half vertically and approximately 30-50 cm3 of material removed from the upper 5cm.subset of 14 samples of nest material were selected for this preliminary study, focusing on failed nests on Grassholm (7 samples) in order to determine in the first instance whether PAHs and paraffins would be detectable in the nest material.  Three samples from successful nests on Grassholm were included for comparison, in addition to four samples of nest material from the remote location of St Kilda. Extracts were analysed by GC/MS, using standards to quantify range of PAHs and long-chain hydrocarbons.  Extraction efficiency for each of the analytes was determined by extraction of spiked samples. Increased monitoring effort and impact assessment following the Sea Empress oil spill. The only data associated with this project is held within the report.\t\tReport is held in the Marine Archive as Project No. 70 and Media No. 48.  Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy, Marine Ornithologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1998-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17730\tNRW_DS110124\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.570Z\t485\tValid\tEditable\tCondition of Crustose Coralline Red Algae in Pembrokeshire After the Sea Empress Disaster\tCyflwr algau coch cwrelaidd cramennog yn Sir Benfro ar ol trychineb y Sea Empress\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313234\tFollowing the Sea Empress oil spill observations on the shores of West Angle Bay and Bluck\u0027s Pool and The Pole at Castlemartin showed that some populations of crustose coralline algae had become bleached and white. Collections were made at this time and during the following year to determine the nature of the bleaching and to follow the recovery, or otherwise, of these plants.   The purpose of this data capture was to determine the nature of the bleaching in crustose coralline algae and to follow the recovery, or otherwise, of these plants.    Two Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) were chosen for regular monitoring, the north shore of West Angle Bay 510 41.8\u0027 N 50  06.5\u0027 W and Bluck\u0027s Pool and The Pole, Castlemartin at 510 38\u0027 N  50 03\u0027 W. Further observations and specimens were obtained from Blaise Bullimore on Skomer (510 44\u0027 N 50 17\u0027 W), Manorbier (510  38.5\u0027 N 40 48.35\u0027 W), Worm\u0027s Head, Gower (510 33.5\u0027 N 40 19\u0027 W), Porth Trecastell, Anglesey (53 0 12\u0027 N 40 25.9\u0027 W)  and Bardsey Island (520 45\u0027 N 40 45\u0027W).\tTwo sites (both Sites of Special Scientific Interest) were chosen for regular monitoring during the year from April 1996-April 1997. The sites were the north shore of West Angle Bay and Bluck\u0027s Pool and The Pole, Castlemartin. These sites had been observed during and immediately after the spillage of the oil thus making it possible to add to the results of the actual survey period. Further observations and specimens were obtained from Blaise Bullimore on Skomer. Populations for sampling were chosen mainly on the basis of bleached thalli having been observed there at the time of the Sea Empress disaster. As far as possible the same populations were observed on each visit, but it was not possible to ensure that the same actual plants were observed and the quadrats in the photographs were only placed in approximately the same place each time, they did, however, provide a regular scale for comparison. The quadrat was 25 cm square. Increased monitoring and impact assessment following the Sea Empress oil spill (algal bleaching).\t\tData are held within the Marine Archive as Project No.71, and Media No. 60 (File Path: Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media060) Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.   NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (Survey Number MRCCW10000000074)\tMetadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW  Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:21:18.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17725\tNRW_DS110125\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.488Z\t766\tValid\tEditable\tSea Empress Rocky Shore Impact Assessment/Monitoring: Permanent Transects and Rock Pool Studies\tAsesu effaith y Sea Empress ar draethau creigiog / monitro traethau creigiog: trawsluniau parhaol ac astudiaethau o byllau glan mor\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313235\tThree permanent intertidal transects in the vicinity of the Dale Fort Field Centre were established immediately following the Sea Empress oil spill. Marine communities at these sites and in rock pools at a further site were surveyed at intervals up to fourteen months post spill. Data was collected in order to assess the impact of the Sea Empress oil spill on the rocky shore.\tTransect and rock pool surveys were carried out.    - Transect Studies:  All transects were surveyed using 0.25m2 frame quadrats at 1m vertical height intervals. Where possible, direct counts were made of numbers of individuals of each species present within the quadrat frame.  When direct counting was rendered either impossible, because of the overwhelming numbers of individuals present or inappropriate, because of the growth form of the species; two different approaches were adopted.  For all algae, lichens, small barnacles and colonial animals (e.g. sponges), percentage cover within the quadrat frame was estimated. For Littorina saxatilis (agg.) (rough periwinkles) and  Melarhaphe neritoides (small periwinkle) the quadrat frame was sub-divided to form a smaller quadrat 0.10%  of its total area. Numbers of individuals were counted within this smaller quadrat and the total multiplied by 10 to give an estimate of numbers per 0.25m2.    Transect studies: Castle Beach Bay - Transect1: Grid reference: SM 824051  /  Slip-Pier Beach - Transect 2: Grid reference: SM 822053   /  Monkhaven - Transect 3:  Grid reference: SM 829064    - Rock-pool Studies: Where appropriate, numbers of individuals of each species were counted within each pool. Algae and colonial animals were assayed by estimating percentage cover of each species within the pool.    Rockpool studies: Slip-Pier Beach near Dale Fort: Grid reference: SM 822052\t\tData are held with the Marine Archive as Project No. 72 and Media No. 63 and 64 Fireproof safe - Stack 02, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor.. Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media063 ,  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media064\tContact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1998-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:22:25.408Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17720\tNRW_DS110126\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.536Z\t647\tValid\tEditable\tStudies and Biometric Gut Content Analyses of Common Scoter Melanitta nigra nigra Killed During the Sea Empress Oil Spill\tAstudiaethau a dadansoddiad o gynnwys perfeddion mor-hwyaid duon Melanitta nigra nigra a laddwyd yn ystod damwain olew y Sea Empress\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313236\tCarmarthen Bay is the most important wintering site for Common Scoters in the UK. It is estimated that the Sea Empress oil spill in February 1996 resulted in the deaths of at least 4,500 Common Scoter. This dataset contains the results of a study to describe the population structure, body condition, level of oiling, biometrics, moult, diet and cause of death of Common Scoters killed by the Sea Empress oil spill, and to investigate factors affecting body condition, diet and cause of death. Data captured in order to describe the biometrics and moult of Common Scoters killed in the Sea Empress oil spill, to determine the age structure of killed birds, and to determine the diet of birds through gut contents analysis.  All data is held within the reports.\tAfter retrieval of Common Scoters killed during the Sea Empress oil spill were stored at the National Museum of Wales and catalogued in terms of date and location of recovery, rehabilitation status (i.e. whether or not birds had been supplied from bird hospitals), and condition of corpse (Howlett 1996).   Birds transported to WWT Slimbridge where every specimen was further categorised in terms of the degree of oiling suffered, physical condition, sex and age. The level of oiling was scored from 0 to 4. The amount of fat reserves present was ascertained by dissection and scored from 0 to 4. During the biometrics and moult study, the oesophagus, proventriculus and gizzard of each bird were removed and immediately frozen at -20 degrees C. Gut contents were extracted for analysis at a later date.  As the proportion of Common Scoters with food in the oesophagus or proventriculus was very low ( less than 1% in the preliminary sample), gut contents analysis was restricted to gizzard analyses. Gizzard contents consisted mainly of shell fragments; therefore it was not possible to collect quantitative information on the biomass and relative proportions of different food items. Consequently, each food item was simply identified to species level and the proportion of birds feeding on each food item (% occurrence) calculated.\tHughes, B., Stewart, B., Brown, M., Hearn, R. 1997. Studies of Common scoter Melanitta nigra nigra killed during the Sea Empress oil Spill.  Final Report to SEEEC, CCW Sea Empress Science Report 233.    Hughes, B., Stewart, B., Brown, M., Hearn, R. 1996.  Biometric and Gut Contents Analysis of Common Scoter Melanitta Nigra Nigra.  Preliminary Report to SEEEC, CCW Sea Empress Science Report 233a.\tDraft Reports are held as archive Project No. 73, media 68 (Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media068).    Final Reports are held in the DMS\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Ornothologist (Matty Murphy?) or NRW. Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW (Mike Camplin or Lucie Haines?).  Work was originally commissioned by Bill Sanderson. Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:23:20.041Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17732\tNRW_DS110140\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.757Z\t673\tValid\tEditable\tAssessment of Sublittoral Epibenthic Rock Communities and Species Following the Sea Empress Oil Spill\tAsesiad o gymunedau a rhywogaethau creigiau epibenthig isarforol yn dilyn gollyngiad olew\u0027r Sea Empress\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313430\tThe Sea Empress oil spill in February 1996 affected the water column, seabed and shores of south Pembrokeshire.  Several rocky subtidal sites in and around the entrance to Milford Haven were studied at the time of the spill. This datasets is made up of the results obtained during a subtidal survey of South Pembrokeshire 16 months after the Sea Empress oil spill. The survey aimed to examine the open coast of South Pembrokeshire sites close the entrances of Milford Haven, as far eastwards as Caldey Island.  The objective of this work was to revisit previously surveyed bedrock areas which may be have been subjected to elevated water column hydrocarbon load in order to investigate their community diversity, richness and condition.\tThe sites were investigated using diving techniques and the following tasks were carried out:   - Site and Habitat Descriptions using standard Phase 2 Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) survey techniques.  Diving was carried out using standard SCUBA apparatus.    - Photography of biotopes and close up photographs of selected species.  Underwater photography was achieved using land cameras in underwater housings and a purpose built submersible Nikonos camera.  - Recording of observations which maybe oil pollution related including observations on the presence and abundance of small mobile custacea. Careful observations were made, looking for contamination in any sediments or on rocky surfaces, noting the health of the communities, any signs which may be related to oil pollution e.g. bleaching of algae, dead bivalves and other animals and conspicuous absence of species from the communities.  - Collection samples of the mussel, Mytilus edulis, at sites where it was encountered for hydrocarbon analysis. Collections of specimens including algae were made at each site.  Where Mytilus edulis was encountered, samples were taken and frozen the same day.  These samples were passed on to CEFAS (Centre for Environment, Fisheries and Aquaculture Science) in Burnham-on-Crouch for total hydrocarbon analysis.   When diving in the region of the Milford Haven waterway, the Castlemartin Range and the Manorbier Artillery Range, liaison with the appropriate authorities was undertaken prior to diving.\tRostron, D.M. and Bunker, F.St.P.D (1997). An Assessment of Sublittoral Epibenthic Rock Communities \u0026amp; Species following the Sea Empress Oil Spill. A report to the Countryside Council for Wales from Marine Seen \u0026amp; Sub-Sea Survey.\tArchived at Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media001to100\\Media052 .   Marine Recorder survey JNCCMNCR40000777\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t1997-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:24:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17728\tNRW_DS110145\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.554Z\t522\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGreater Horseshoe Bat (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum) survey of the Castlemartin bat cave following the grounding of the Sea Empress\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313435\tThe Castlemartin coast holds the most important hibernacula for Greater Horseshoe Bats, Rhinolophus ferrumequinum in Wales. Following the oil spill from the Sea Empress, oil was dispersed along and deposited on the Castlemartin coast and concerns were raised about the contamination of Castlemartin bat cave. This dataset contains the results of surveys carried out to check for the presence of oil and bats at the cave. The purpose of this data capture was to determine whether oil pollution from the Sea Empress was adversely affecting the bats.\tBat counts and notes on the oil pollution were taken at the site. It was highlighted that there was increased monitoring effort and impact assessment following the Sea Empress oil spill. The only data held is that within the report itself.\t\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media369\tWest Region Senior Reserve Manager, NRW (The Senior Reserve Managers in NRW include Graham Williams, Hywel Roberts, Emyr Humphreys, Huw Green, Rhodri Dafydd)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17724\tNRW_DS110159\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.450Z\t333\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of Lichen Communities at Stackpole National Nature Reserve (NNR)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313539\tStackpole Warren is noted for its communities of calcicolous terricolous lichens which occupy heavily rabbit-grazed ground with a poor cover of vascular plants. However, available evidence suggests that the amount of open ground and short grazed turf at Stackpole Warren has been decreasing for at least 3 decades. The purpose of this data capture was to examine the terricolous lichen sites at Stackpole Warren for signs of damage which might be attributable to oil pollution from the Sea Empress; to extend monitoring coverage of the terricolous lichen sites at Stackpole National Nature Reserve (NNR); and to assess the success of management in maintaining the notable species and communities.\tCondition monitoring of lichens (lichens examined in the field for signs of damage/unexplained mortality). Quadrat recording-5 lichen quadrats established in 1994 were relocated and full species list made. 107 new lichen quadrats were also established in 1997 at regular intervals along  transects established on a number of the terricolous lichen sites. Photographs were taken of each quadrat. Within each quadrat a list of lichens, vascular plants and bryophytes was made- each assigned a Domin value. The only data associated with this project is held within the report.\t\tArchived at K:\\archive\\Media301to400\\Media372\tInternal Custodian 1: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Internal Custodian 2: Paul Culyer, Senior Officer Land Management, Conservation Land Management Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:14:04.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17727\tNRW_DS110174\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.500Z\t325\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Habitat Video Clips Available Through YouTube\tClipiau fideo CCGC o gynefinoedd morol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313734\tDrop down video and hand held video clips were produced to illustrate the seabed environment and its flora and fauna across Special Areas of Conservations (SACs).   Edited clips have been produced in order to provide access for Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRWs) staff to relevant underwater video.  By disseminating the data through YouTube, it becomes available to external audiences and provides an illustration of NRWs work and to increase awareness of the marine environment in Wales.    These videos were produced and filmed by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) which is now part of NRW. The GIS Dataset shows the location of these video surveys.\tThe data was originally captured as part of the Across Wales Diving Monitoring Survey and the Across Wales Drop Down Video Monitoring survey as a way to assisting with condition assessment of the SACs.  Hand-held video are captured using a housed underwater digital video camera, plus 2 video lights to avoid shadowing.  A Sony SC100E single chip camera using mini DV format tapes was used. Towed video is captured using a Sony VX1000, in a waterproof housing and mounted on a metal frame sledge with two lights mounted either side of the camera.  Laser pointers are mounted 10 cm apart on front of the housing and are used to project two red dots into the filed of view for assessment.   Clips have been edited and cut down to illustrate the best examples of  the habitats. All videos have been edited using Adobe Premier software to produce short clips, approximately 1 minute in length to illustrate a range of marine habitats.\tThe Countryside Council for Wales YouTube channel and content is not supported by Natural Resources Wales\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine_Habitat_videos ,  GIS subset data for video published on YouTube is only available in the Legacy CCW Archive for MapInfo format data: M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\Marine_Habitat_Videos ,  Video files held on NRW You Tube page.\tContact Charles Lindenbaum, Specialist (Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer), Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting for information about dropdown video surveys\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-03-01\tpublication\tnull\thttps://www.youtube.com/@countrysidecouncil/videos\t2025-03-07T16:40:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17721\tNRW_DS110179\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.534Z\t342\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBaseline survey for the Afon Teifi restoration project\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313739\tThe Afon Teifi is the longest river to have its course entirely within Wales. Its conservation value is high, the entire river having Special Area of Conservation (SAC) status. Not far below its source in Teifi Pools, the river traverses the Cors Caron National Nature Reserve, meandering its way between the raised bogs for which the reserve is renowned. Parts of this sinuous course were canalized about a century ago, creating sections of unusually straight and deep channel between steep clay banks. It is proposed to restore these reaches of the river to their original course, turning the canalized sections into new lentic water bodies. This dataset includes the results of a baseline survey conducted in 2006-2007 which focussed on sediments, invertebrates and fish. Sediment sampling was conducted to determine the extent of metal contamination of the material that would be excavated in the course of the restoration. The purpose of this data capture is to contribute to the evidence base that would be essential to completing a fully documented restoration project. Sediment sampling was conducted to determine the extent of metal contamination of the material that would be excavated in the course of the restoration. The risk of metal contamination exists due to historic mining in the upper part of the catchment. Invertebrate sampling aimed to establish the potential for improvement of the river\u0027s conservation value by comparing the invertebrate assemblages of the uncanalized reaches with the canalized sections. It also aimed to establish the conservation value of the existing pond in the original channel, providing both a measure of the potential loss and a benchmark for the development of the replacement ponds in the canalized sections post-restoration. Survey of fish in an upstream uncanalized reach and in two canalized sections aimed to provide a baseline for the assessment of the effect of restoration on the suitability of the river for fish.\tSediment samples: taken in Jan \u002706 as intact cores on transects at 14 points along the former river course. Samples were bulked for analysis of each transect with individual samples retained for further analysis if appropriate. Samples also taken from the existing riverbed at two points above and below where realignment is proposed; sediment recently deposited on river banks (various locations) was also collected. Pond invertebrates: one sample collected on 11/07/06 from each of three sites on the pond by methods recommended in the National Pond Survey Protocol.    River invertebrates: Samples collected from 9 sites on the Teifi on 11-12/04/06 and 18/10/06. The midstream samples consisted of four 30-second kick samples which together included as many varieties of sediment type as were represented at the site. The east and west bank samples collected from the face of the bank by kicking in deeper water or by net sweeping nearer the water surface. Two 15 second samples were collected from each bank at each site. River fish: All the samples collected on 17/07/06. Fish were collected using hand seining and hand netting. Fish sampled were passed to an assistant located on the bank for identification, photographing, and standard length measuring. All fish were returned to the river.\t\tArchived as project number 197 (media number 544) H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media544\tInternal Custodian:  Rivers Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:39:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17735\tNRW_DS110182\t2024-05-28T12:30:03.120Z\t805\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFluvial Audit of the Upper Usk Tributaries\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313832\tThe results of a Fluvial Geomorphical Audit of the Upper Usk tributaries. The Usk system, comprising the River Usk and its upper tributaries, has been designated as a Special Area for Conservation (SAC) and notified as a Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) for its geomorphological and physiographic features and the associated plant and animal communities. It supports internationally important populations of otter, Atlantic salmon, bullhead, brook lamprey, and river lamprey. This survey provides a baseline source for assessing potential habitat suitability by relating the presence or absence of morphological features to the condition parameters that are identified as habitat preferences for the SAC species. The purpose of this data capture is to develop an understanding of the dynamic geomorphology of the Upper Usk system.  The areas surveyed included the Upper Usk system (upstream of Abergavenny), and it\u0027s tributaries which included Hydfer, Crai, Senni, Treweryn, Camlais, Tarell, Cynrig, Menascin, Caerfanell, Cilieni, Eithrim, Bran, Ysgir, Ysgir Fechan, Ysgir Fawr, Honddu, Rhiangoll, Grwyne, Grwyne Fechan, Banw, Grwyne Fawr.\tThe Fluvial Audit (FA) was conducted following the R+D 661 approach, which uses contemporary (field survey) and historic (archive desk study) data collection methods to gain a comprehensive understanding of the river system.\t\tArchived as Project 59, Media 354.   Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media301to400\\Media354 Also archived in CCW legacy GIS drive (M drive) Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\hydrology\\Hydrology_Reports\\fluvial_morphology\tSue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, NRW (Formerly Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, NRW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-09-03T10:05:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17717\tNRW_DS110183\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.422Z\t341\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFluvial Audit of the River Dee\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313833\tThe results of a CCW commissioned Fluvial Geomorphological Audit of the River Dee to record the morphological features and to identify and understand the geomorphological processes which influence and control the channel activity and the morphological quality of the river system.  The River Dee is a dynamic gravel-bed river with evidence of active channel migration in specific locations. The river is of high conservation status being designated a Special Area for Conservation by virtue of its Ranunculus community and in its lower reaches the river channel and its floodplain are designated as a Site of Special Scientific Interest for its morphological and physiographic features.  This survey provides a baseline source for assessing site suitability by relating the presence or absence of morphological features to the condition parameters that are identified as habitat preferences for the components of the Ranunculus community. The purpose of this data capture was to develop an understanding of the dynamic geomorphology of the River Dee. The area used for this study included A 130 km length of the Main River of the River Dee, between the Bala Lake (Llyn Tegid) outflow and the Normal Tide Limit (NTL) at the Chester Weir (SJ 408659).\tThe Fluvial Audit (FA) was conducted following the R+D 661 approach, which uses contemporary (field survey) and historic (archive desk study) data collection methods to gain a comprehensive understanding of the river system.\t\tArchived as Project 161, Media 490.    Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media401to500\\Media490  Also archived in CCW legacy GIS drive (M drive) Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\hydrology\\Hydrology_Reports\\fluvial_morphology\tRhian Thomas, Env Mon Officer, Dadansoddi a Hysbysu, NRW ? Formerly Rhian Thomas in CCW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-09-03T10:06:25.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17716\tNRW_DS110192\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.357Z\t508\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Salinity Records\tParth Cadwraeth Morol (PCM) Sgomer - Cofnodion Halwynedd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313932\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Salinity is recorded from the sea bed and in a water column profile from a single site in the MNR. Records began in Jul 1992 and have continued to date. The purpose of this data capture was to record the oceanographic qualities of the surrounding sea water.\tValeport series 600 MKII CTD probe - profile data - records salinity and conductivity (from 1992 - 2007).  YSI 660 sonde - sea bed (from 2007 onwards).\t\tSkomer MNR, V drive/shared documents/data/oceanographic data/sea water/ salinity\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:32:07.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17718\tNRW_DS110193\t2024-05-28T12:30:02.461Z\t537\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Passive Sediment (Collector) Data\tGNF Ynys Sgomer - Data gwaddodion goddefol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313933\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Passive sediment traps have been deployed at 2 sites intermittently since 1994. Sediment dropping out of the water column is collected into a pot. The sample pots are changed every 2 weeks during the field season and the sediment samples are frozen. The purpose of this data capture was to record the depositional material occurring in the MNR.\tPassive sediment trap - large 110mm pipe with collecting pot at the bottom. Samples are analysed for: Dry weight, organic content, percentage gravel, sand and mud Metal analysis on some samples for: Vanadium, Chromium, Cobalt, Nickel, Copper, Zinc, Arsenic, Selenium, Cadmium, Lead and Antimony.\t\tSkomer MNR, V drive/shared documents/data/oceanographic data/sediment\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton Assistant Marine Conservation Officer - Skomer MNR Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:33:10.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17712\tNRW_DS110194\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.753Z\t585\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCondition monitoring of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130313934\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs) are strictly protected sites designated under the EC Habitats Directive. The Habitats Directive has a list of habitat types and species that are considered to be most in need of conservation at a European level (excluding birds, which are covered under the EC Birds Directive). Once sites have been designated, the country agencies work with owners, occupiers and others to secure their management and protection regime. One element of this work is a programme of habitat and species monitoring across the site. Habitat monitoring includes woodland, uplands, wetlands, lowland grasslands and coastal margins. For species, monitoring covers a range of taxa from invertebrates through to mammals (including bat species) and both higher and lower plant.  This dataset combines the results of SAC monitoring from across Wales. The purpose of this collation is to assess the condition of features (the reason sites are protected) across these specific sites. The condition of features is monitored using site-specific performance indicators. The scope of performance indicators includes habitats and species across the terrestrial, freshwater, marine and coastal environments. This data is also used as an important record for aspects of their management, both as an aid to management decisions and for reporting at a variety of scales, including UK through JNCC, and for contributing to UK reports on Conservation Status under the EC Habitats Directive.\tThe method for data collection across SACs is Common Standards Monitoring, this involves an assessment of feature condition for protected sites. Performance indicators are devised from JNCC generic guidance (http://www.jncc.gov.uk) and modified to reflect local distinctiveness and quantified to reflect the desired state of the feature. Assessment of these performance indicators are made using methods appropriate to the nature and scale of the feature and conclusions drawn depending on whether performance indicators pass or fail. Information relevant to management is also collected. Together the condition assessments and management information provide information to help inform assessments of conservation status. The intention is for every feature on every designated site in the UK to be assessed over a period of six years in a rolling monitoring cycle e.g. 2001 - 2006.\t\tIn summary DMS file and data are stored under the relevant SAC protected site under ‘monitoring’. GIS data is stored under the relevant GIS drive under ‘Evidence_Monitoring’ and split into either North or South Wales depending on location. Other than the top level summary data all field data and records are held and managed by the relevant NRW area.  This has largely been replaced by the baseline dashboard.\tInternal Contact/Custodian: Dylan Lloyd Specialist Advisor - Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team        Dylan Lloyd is supplying some more detailed information on Terrestrial SAC monitoring data to update the entry.  This entry is specifically about terrestrial SAC monitoring for habs directive.  Marine and Freshwater programmes should probably have a separate entry.  Dylan will consider if when data and programme reviewed additional metadata entries need to be added for each Location/feature that is monitored.  This metadata entry is the one that will be linked to from Terrestrial Monitoring SAC reports (reduced to one summary report due to staff workloads)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17713\tNRW_DS110203\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.661Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) - River Nutrients Spatial Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323033\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons. It is an external third party dataset owned by the Environment Agency Wales and licensed to NRW for internal use only.  The dataset has been supplied by the Environment Agency Wales and contains River Nutrient data (nitrate and phosphate) - GQA nitrate and phosphate grades achieved for each stretch in the 10k network.\t\t\tx:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\GQA Phosphate Network.lyr  x:\\Monitoring\\River Quality Assessment Sampling (Historic)\\GQA Nitrate Network.lyr\tPlease contact Jane Roberts ( Environment Agency)\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:23.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17711\tNRW_DS110209\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.657Z\t621\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEstablishment of a Permanent Monitoring Site at Mid Channel Rocks, Milford Haven\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323039\tData from a study undertaken to establish a permanent monitoring site on Mid Channel Rocks, Milford Haven. The work was commissioned following the grounding of the Sea Empress oil tanker on the Mid Channel Rocks, Milford Haven on February 15th 1996.  On the four days following the grounding, the tanker remained in the immediate vicinity of these rocks, grounding and floating free several times.  An estimated 70,000 tonnes of oil were spilled within one nautical mile of the Mid-Channel Rocks.  A considerable volume of dispersant was applied from the air in the nearby area.  This is expected to have led to significantly elevated hydrocarbon levels in the water column, possibly including the more toxic oil fractions where fresh oil was dispersed. The objectives of this project were to establish a permanent sublittoral monitoring site at a rock substrate location close to the source of the spilled oil and which was amongst the most likely to have been subjected to elevated water column hydrocarbon load and to undertake a baseline survey to document epibenthic rock community and species composition, diversity, richness and condition. Data was captured in order to establish a permanent sublittoral monitoring site at a rock substrate location close to the source of the spilled oil and to undertake a baseline survey to document epibenthic rock community and species composition, diversity, richness and condition.\tDivers using standard SCUBA apparatus carried out the survey work. Procedures were carried out under HSE DOW 1981 regulations and JNCC diving rules (Holt, 1993).   Once the study sites were marked out, a variety of data collecting exercises were carried out: Community descriptions were obtained from defined areas using Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) techniques. Particular rock faces were chosen which were typical of the main habitat / community types and careful studies were undertaken to list all conspicuous species present and note abundances. Counts of species in quadrats and also suction samples were undertaken at both the Lower Infralittoral and Circalittoral Study Sites. This project followed immediately on from one which surveyed selected locations along the South Pembrokeshire coast from Milford Haven to Caldey.  Mid Channel Rocks was one of the sites surveyed and the work was carried out with the present monitoring study in mind. The only data associated with this project is held within the report.\t\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media001to100\\Media053 Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elyfn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor, Wales.\tInternal Metadata contact and Custodian: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1998-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:14.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17710\tNRW_DS110210\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.571Z\t266\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEffectiveness of the rehabilitation of oiled birds with reference to the Sea Empress oil spill\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323130\tThis datasets comprises of the results from a review of the rescue and rehabilitation of oiled birds following the Sea Empress oil spill. This data was used in the evaluation of the effectiveness of rehabilitation efforts and suggested management procedures.\tThis study was concentrate in south-west Wales, near to the location of the Sea Empress oil spill. The methods involved collecting birds on the coast of south-west Wales between 22nd Feb and 12th March and counting these. This work formed part of the heightened monitoring efforts following the Sea Empress oil spill.\t\tH:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media001to100\\Media073 Fireproof safe - Stack 02, Hafod Elfyn, NRW Maes y Fynnon, Bangor\tInternal Custodian: Patrick Lindley or Matty Murphy, Marine Ornithologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:38.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17714\tNRW_DS110213\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.656Z\t271\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPolycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbon (PAH) and N-Alkane Residues in Seabird Eggs after the Grounding of the Sea Empress.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323133\tThe grounding of the Sea Empress in February 1996 resulted in large-scale oil pollution of the water around the Pembrokeshire coast and its associated island seabird colonies. The accident occurred at about the time that seabirds were returning to their breeding colonies. Therefore, it was possible that breeding birds might transfer oil directly to their eggs through contact with oiled nesting material, feathers and feet, or oral ingestion of sub-lethal amounts of hydrocarbons and subsequent transfer to yolk. Exposure of adult birds and eggs to crude oil or polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs), which are present in crude oil, can have adverse effects on egg structure and quality, embryo development and hatching success. This dataset contains the results of an investigation into the PAH and n-alkane content of seabird eggs on the coast of Pembrokeshire following the Sea Empress oil spill. The aim of the study was to investigate whether there was any evidence of hydrocarbon contamination of seabird eggs resulting from the Seam Empress spill.\tIncreased monitoring efforts and impact assessment following the Sea Empress oil spill. The only data associated with this project is held within the report. The methods of data capture involved measuring the level of contaminants within the breeding populations. N-alkane and PAH concentrations in the eggs of carious coastal species from oil-contaminated breeding colonies around the Pembrokeshire coast. The levels of contamination were compare with those detected in the eggs of the same or similar species, which were collected from the Isle of May, off the NE coast of Britain. The Isle of May was unaffected by the Sea Empress spill. A sample of weather oil from the Sea Empress was also analysed for n-alkane and PAH levels to obtain a fingerprint of the oil that could be compared with residues detected in eggs.\t\tArchived as project no 93 (media no 368) - File path K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 368 NB The report won\u0027t open in Adobe acrobat or Word.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-10-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17709\tNRW_DS110217\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.588Z\t287\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPolycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbon composition of herbage samples following the Sea Empress oil spill\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323137\tDataset contains the results of an investigation into the Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbon (PAH) composition of grass collected from the South Pembrokeshire coast following the Sea Empress oil spill. Data includes site location and grid references, herbage type, PAH concentrations and n-alkane content. Purpose of this data capture was to determine the concentration of PAH and aliphatic hydrocarbon in herbage samples following the oil spill and detect any patterns/trends.\tGrass was collected from south Pembrokeshire coat in late February / early march 1996 following the grounding of the Sea Empress oil tanker. A second round of sampling took place in May 1997. The Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbons (PAHs) were isolated from the grass and their composition and concentration determined by gas chromatography with mass selective detection (GC-MSD). The aliphatic hydrocarbon composition was determined by gas chromatography with flame ionisation detection (GC-FID). The only data associated with this project is held within the report.\t\tArchived as project no 98 (media no 373) H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media373\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17707\tNRW_DS110218\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.412Z\t327\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEpipelic and epilithic microalgal distribution, density and relative abundance following the Sea Empress oil spill.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323138\tThe ecological impact of a pollution event such as the Sea Empress oil spill is readily observable on macroscopic plants and animals. Although microscopic organisms are not as easily observed and studied they may be a crucial part of the ecosystem structure and function. This dataset contains the result of a study into the effects of oil contamination on epipelic and epilithic microalgal communities in coastal habitats of South West Wales. Sites contaminated by the Sea Empress oil spill were compared to a nearby adjacent uncontaminated site. Purpose of this data capture was to study the effects of oil contamination on the distribution, density and abundance of epipelic and epilithic microscopic algal communities.\tFour collecting sites were established beginning at Abereiddy (not oiled, control) and continuing southerly to Newgale and Popton (lightly to medium oiled) and terminating at West Angle (heavily oiled). Replicate samples were collected at all four sites for the two sampling dates. Sediments (coarse grain material) exposed in the intertidal zome were collected for epipleic algae and exposed rocks were scraped for epilithic algae, seawater samples were collected for nutrient analysis and photographs taken at all sites. Samples were all sent to the laboratory for analysis. Replicate samples collected on 3-4 August and 27-29 September 1996. The only data associated with this project is held within the report.\t\tArchived as project no 111 - media no 385 (File path Z:\\Archive\\Media301to400\\Media385)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1998-12-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17715\tNRW_DS110219\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.660Z\t326\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEffects of the Sea Empress oil spillage on sensitive sessile marine invertebrate communities\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323139\tThe grounding of the Sea Empress at the mouth of Milford Haven in February 1996 resulted in oil polluted shorelines from the south of St Brides Bay to Pendine Sands. This dataset contains the results of an investigation into the effects of the oiling and its clean-up on sensitive sessile intertidal communities of marine invertebrates and the results of an investigation into possible effects of the oil/clean up on the biology of Alcyonidium species at Dale and Angle. The purpose of these investigations were to survey the lower shore sessile fauna on the north side of St Catherine\u0027s Island, Tenby documenting the present composition of the sessile fauna, to assess what changes had occurred since the 1950s and whether any of these might be attributable to the oiling an clean up following the Sea Empress grounding; and investigate the effect of the oil on the reproduction and recruitment of Alcyonidium species (Bryozoa) on Fucus serratus at Dale and Angle in Milford Haven and at control sites in Solva Harbour.\tFive 0.2 m x 0.2 m permanent quadrats were established along a transect extending for 82 m on the north side of St Catherine\u0027s Island. Percentage cover of conspicuous species calculated using image analysis technique, the growth and regression of the dominant ascidian Morchellium argus was calculated as were the varying cover and degree of fragmentation of the sponges Hymeniacidon perleve and Halichondria panacea. Sessile fauna list compiled by examining the shore during spring tides.  In addition to this a survey was done which followed the reproduction and growth of the Alcyonidium spp over the year, inclusive from settlement to reproductive maturity of a cohort. It took area measurements of the Alcyonidium colonies to monitor growth rates and used image analysis package to determine colony areas for growth data.\t\t[A] Archived as project no 107 (media no 380). File path K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 380. [B] Marine Recorder survey MRCCW10000000090\tInternal Custodian and Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1998-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17708\tNRW_DS110223\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.518Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGeneral Quality Assessment (GQA) - River Invertebrate Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323233\tThe GQA River Biology dataset (see separate metadata entry) gives stretches of rivers a grade from A-F depending on the number of invertebrate species caught. This dataset is a more extensive version containing the names of the invertebrates caught for the Rivers Usk and Wye.  This data was captured as part of the Routine Environment Agency England and Wales General Quality Assessment monitoring programme.\tStandard Environment Agency invertebrate kick sampling method.\t\tRhian Thomas, CCW\u0027s Rivers Ecologist holds a copy.\tSue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, NRW (Formerly Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, CCW) Nick Davies, Environment Agency\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17706\tNRW_DS110240\t2024-05-28T12:30:01.199Z\t466\tUnassessed\tEditable\tJuvenile salmonid survey data for Special Area of Conservation (SAC) rivers.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323430\tThe Environment Agency monitors juvenile salmon and trout populations nationally by fishing a limited number of sites on an annual basis to obtain meaningful temporal data for trends in fish populations. A more comprehensive spatial monitoring survey programme is carried out every 5 years to investigate differences between groups of sites within particular river catchments. Other species caught in the surveys are also recorded e.g. bullhead, lampreys, eels etc. This dataset contains juvenile salmon data for the 8 rivers designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) in Wales.  The purpose of this dataset is to obtain temporal data for trends in fish populations and to investigate differences between groups of sites within river catchments.\tA limited number of sites are fished on an annual basis whilst a more comprehensive spatial monitoring survey programme is carried out every 5 years.\t\tLegacy CCW M:drive: M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Species\\all_wales\\Freshwater\\Freshwater_Fish Rhian Thomas, CCW\u0027s Rivers Ecologist holds copies of the spreadsheets.\tInternal custodian: Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, CCW  Metadata contact: Data services Team, NRW\tEnvironment Agency (EA)\t\t2007-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17765\tNRW_DS110241\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.653Z\t601\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMean Trophic Rank Data for rivers in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323431\tThis dataset contains Mean Trophic Rank data for a selection of rivers in Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the trophic status and eutrophication impact on rivers and streams using biotic indices. This is an old methodology and has now been superseded by LEAFPACKS. This dataset used to be supplied to former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) by former Environment Agency Wales (EAW), both of whom now for part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW). This data is now currently held in the BIOSYS database where it can be extracted for internal use.\tMean Trophic Rank, MTR is an assessment of trophic status and eutrophication impact on rivers and streams. The macrophyte flora and physical character of defined lengths (100m) of watercourse are surveyed using a standard checklist. The presence, absence and percentage area covered by each macrophyte are recorded. Each species is allocated a score (species trophic rank, STR) on a scale of 1-10 depending on its tolerance to eutrophication- the higher the score the lower the tolerance to nutrient enrichment. The scores of the species present are integrated as a mean value, weighted according to the relative percentage cover of individual species. The resulting value is the MTR score which increases with decreasing eutrophy. This methodology has now been superseded by LEAFPACKS, and the data is available in NRW\u0027s BIOSYS database.\t\tThe most up-to-date version of the data can be extracted from the BIOSYS database.\tInternal Custodian:  For this extract contact Rhian Thomas, Team Leader WFD, Water Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tEnvironment Agency (EA)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17758\tNRW_DS110245\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.507Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFish Data for Transitional Waters\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323435\tNB FULL DATA FOR WALES IS NOW HELD ON WISKI-KIECO  Abundance data of fish caught in Welsh estuaries during Environment Agency Wales Water Framework Directive monitoring for the Transitional Fish biological quality element in 2007 onwards. For reporting the ecological quality status of Welsh estuaries under the EU Water Framework Directive.\tSeveral survey methods were used in collecting this data: beam trawling, otter trawling, fyke netting and seine netting. The following estuaries are covered: Conwy, Teifi, Nefern, Dee, Milford Haven (Daugleddau), Severn, Foryd. Identification of fish is quality assured under the National Marine Biology Analytical Quality Assurance scheme (NMBAQC) in the form of reverse ring tests where reference collections of fish from each region are sent to Unicomarine for verification. Staff involved in surveys must attend a relevant id course and participate in an annual ring test.\t\tA static archive copy is held on  Z:\\Archive \\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\WFD  Live data is held in the fisheries component of Wiski-Kieco.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-11-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17762\tNRW_DS110271\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.472Z\t371\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring the Health of the North Llyn Modiolus Reef\tMonitro iechyd Riff Modiolus Gogledd Llyn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323731\tModiolus Reefs are a feature of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC).  Information is gathered about the health of the reef through the recording of species present in fixed quadrats.  Live horse mussels are hard to distinguish within the quadrat, however it is known that each live horse mussel supports one colony of the soft coral dead man\u0027s fingers Alcyonium digitatum and therefore these are used to count the number of live horse mussels present. This survey work is carried out as part of the Across Wales Diving Survey. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the health of the reef. This contributes to reporting of favourable conservation status of the SAC feature.  Data will be used in conjunction with acoustic data that looks at the extent of the feature (reef) and potential areas of damage.\tBased on the monitoring trials see Marine Monitoring Report no. 1.  Two sites were set up on the N. Llyn reef in order to monitor the health of the reef.  An additional site is monitored on the Isle of Man as a comparison.  In 2009 several sites of good condition were identified from multibeam and side-scan sonar images for possible additional monitoring.  At these sites stratified random sampling is to be undertaken rather then marking permanent quadrats.At each of the original 2 sites on the Llyn permanent markers are in place that are used to position 0.5m by 0.5m quadrats.  At each site 15 quadrats are placed on the crest of a Modiolus bioherm. Each quadrat is split into 25 cells of 100x100mm.  The quadrat is filmed along each row from left to right using an underwater digital camera that stores footage on mini DV tapes.    Detailed information about the setting up of the sites and standard operating procedures can be found in \u0027CCW Marine Monitoring Report 25b (draft): SOP 3\u0027. Still images are extracted from the video processed and stitched into a mosaic. Live horse mussels are counted using the presence of Alcyonium digitatum as an indicator.  Prior to analysis the quadrats are randomised to reduce any bias from the recorder.  In addition to counting the presence of horse mussels trends in other easily recognizable species can be recorded to provide additional information about the quality and health of the reef.  Other species recorded are live horse mussels common starfish Asterias rubens common brittlestar Ophiothrix fragilis sea urchin Psammechinus miliaris.\t.\tAll data is archived under project no.218, media no. 584. Working copies of the data is held on shared MFSG drive. Video tapes are archived in the fireproof safe.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG Metadata contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:34:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17760\tNRW_DS110284\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.506Z\t699\tUnassessed\tEditable\tVolunteer Diver Seagrass Zostera marina Surveys, Llyn Peninsula and Milford Haven (2008)\tArolygon gan ddeifars gwirfoddol ar wellt y gamlas Zostera marina, Pen Llyn ac Aberdaugleddau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323834\tIn areas where conditions are suitable seagrass beds form and these are considered nationally important in the UK. Seagrasses are thought to help stabilising the seabed in addition to providing organic matter, shelter and food for a variety of fish, birds and invertebrates. Seagrass beds are vital nursery grounds for flatfish, and in some areas also for cephalopods. They are also known to be sensitive to changes in water quality and human disturbance. Seagrass beds have been designated as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan Habitat (underpinned by the Convention of Biological Diversity, Rio Earth Summit 1992), and as a Habitat of Principal Importance for the Conservation of Biological Diversity in Wales (NERC Act 2006: Section 42).   The survey was due to be expanded in 2009 to other areas within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and methodologies refined. 1. Develop methods to look at damage to seagrass beds caused by anchor chains compared to permanent moorings. 2. \u0027Ground-truth\u0027 aerial photographs of Porth Dinllaen and Milford Haven subtidal seagrass beds. 3. Test methods of measuring extent of large seagrass beds using volunteers. 4. Estimate density and measure depth of live seagrass in different parts of the bed. 5. Make note of any unusual feature of the seagrass bed.\tData is captured by using Volunteer divers who are CMAS 2 star qualified or equivalent with a minimum of 60 dives each. Anchor and density of seagrass shoots are captured by placing quadrats at 5 m intervals along a series of transects that are laid from the centre of the anchor point or seagrass bed.  Transects are carried out in a north, south, west and east direction and where time allows also on NE, SW, NW and SE bearings.  The number of shoots is counted in each 30 by 30 cm quadrat.  In addition the condition of shoot and presence of seeds, black spot disease, snake pipefish and Sargassum muticum (and other non-native species), all of which are believed to be good indicators of the condition of the seagrass bed were recorded.  The extent of the beds was also ground-truthed from aerial photographs.  Areas (polygons) of suspected seagrass habitats were identified from the photos.  An acoustic marker was deployed to the centroid of the polygon, divers were then deployed to the suspected outer edge of the seagrass bed and swam to the acoustic marker using a hand held acoustic homing device.  Divers recorded where seagrass started and stopped, changed in seabed types, distance, depth and bearing.  The method was repeated until all 8 cardinal points of the compass were complete (time allowing). Methods were tested at Porth Dinllaen as well as measures of extent, density and damaged from anchoring, whereas work in the Milford Haven concentrated on confirming the existence of seagrass beds and their deepest extent.  All work carried out contributed to reporting on the features Favourable Conservation Status.\t\t[A] Data is archived as project 229, media no. 598 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501-600\\Media 598) [B ] A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG shared drive - [NB this may be out of date information]  [C] Marine Recorder Survey (MRMCS01200000011)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team. Internal Custodian: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Marine Conservation Society (MCS)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:34:55.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17754\tNRW_DS110289\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.369Z\t225\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNewborough Warren and Newborough Forest maps\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130323839\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC) commissioned a survey of Newborough Warren and forest culminating in the production of high quality maps.\tAerial photography flown in 1982 and supplied by the University of Cambridge was used as a basis for mapping. The paper maps produced were scanned and georegistered to produce tif files. Two MapInfo vector layers were produced from the maps- spot heights and contours.\t\tArchived as Project 159, Media 488 and Media 396\tMike Camplin, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist \u0026amp; Marine Monitoring Team Lead, Evidence, Knowledge and Advice - NRW (Used to be Gordon Bell / James Dargie)\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC)\t\t1990-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17749\tNRW_DS110319\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.339Z\t406\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWest Wales Grey Seal Census 1991 to 1994\tCyfrifiad Morloi Llwyd Gorllewin Cymru 1991-1994\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333139\tThe large breeding colonies of grey seals in northern Britain have been censused annually in recent years by the Sea Mammal Research Unit, but the relatively small population in Wales had not been the subject of a population estimate since 1974 and there had never been a comprehensive census of all mainland and island sites sustained through a pupping season.  The purpose of this data capture was to locate and document all grey seal breeding sites during the main pupping season from September to December 1991 and to estimate pup production at these sites. To estimate the total number of cows in the West Wales population, identify \u0027areas\u0027 where seals are subject to disturbance by man and assess the quality of the breeding haul-out environment.\tThe study area comprising the Dyfed coast and offshore islands between Aberystwyth and Caldey Island was divided into 11 sections designated by the letters A to K, at locations with road access to slipways from which the survey vessel could be launched.In each section the coast was surveyed by land and sea between September 1991 and the onset of the 1992 pupping season and all locations considered to be possible grey seal pupping sites were recorded on maps and investigated by landing on the site from the sea. The criteria used for inclusion in the list of potential pupping sites were: -  The presence of a beach above the level of high-water, -  Access for seals from the sea, and -  Relatively low human disturbance.\t\tDataset is archived as Project 137 (media number 393) H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media393\\Report Paper report is available from CCW library (see related titles field) 2 boxes off-site of original paper data: CCW/WRPB/003 - Seal Census ( 1992 - 1994 ), CCW/WRPB/004 - Seal Census ( 1992 - 1994 )\tInternal Custodian: Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1995-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:35:42.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17755\tNRW_DS110320\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.370Z\t311\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAnalysis of seabird blood samples following the Sea Empress Oil Spill.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333230\tThe results of a study that used haematological techniques to investigate the subtle sub-lethal effects of oil on seabirds (razorbills, guillemots and shags) following the Sea Empress oil spill. Data includes summary statistics for the measured blood parameters.  The purpose of this data capture was to investigate subtle sub-lethal effects of oil on seabirds. Blood samples were collected from birds at two sites in Wales- Skomer Island and Middleholm, Pembrokeshire, and one site in Scotland- Isle of May, Firth of Forth.\tBlood samples were collected from birds at a potentially affected site in Pembrokeshire and at control sites in Scotland. Samples were stored at 4 degrees Celsius until processed at the laboratory. Haematological techniques such as red cell counts, haematocrit measurements (proportion of the blood sample that comprises blood cells), and the determination of the proportion of reticulocytes (immature red blood cells) were employed.  The only data associated with this project is held within the report.\t\tArchived as project no 123 (media no 364) H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media364\tInternal Contacts: Matty Murphy, Marine an Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:32.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17763\tNRW_DS110321\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.497Z\t386\tValid\tEditable\tLittoral Monitoring in Permanent Quadrats Established at West Angle Bay and Manorbier 1996-1997\tMonitro\u0027r arfordir mewn cwadratau parhaol a sefydlwyd ym Mae West Angle a Maenorbyr, 1996-1997\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333231\tThe Sea Empress oil tanker ran aground at the entrance to Milford Haven on the 15th of February 1996 and proceeded to spill over 70,000 tonnes of crude oil into the sea. The purpose of data capture of this data capture was to investigate the changes in community structure following the oil spill and identify any differences between the two sites monitored. Two permanent quadrats established prior to the spill at West Angle Bay and Manorbier Bay were both severely impacted however neither were subjected to cleaning. This provided an ideal opportunity for pre and post spill monitoring, the results of which are presented in this dataset.\tPermanent quadrats (50 x 50cm) were established at West Angle Bay on February 1995 and Manorbier Bay in February 1996. The quadrats were marked permanently on the rocks at 0.6m intervals from Chart Datum to the top of the supralittoral zone. All quadrats were photographed and detailed assessments of the abundance of plants and animals were made and recorded on standard data sheets.   In addition, at West Angle bay the numbers, positions and sizes of limpets were recorded whilst at Manorbier numbers of Patella in quadrats were studies and barnacle counts were made. This dataset contributed to the Pembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas, see separate metadata entry.\t\tArchived as project no 126 (media no 367). File path: -Z:|\\Archive\\Media 401to500\\dia301 to 400\\Media367.  Data is also held within Marine Recorder as survey numberMRCCW10000000075.\tInternal Contact: Mike Camplin, Team Leader, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:25:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17756\tNRW_DS110332\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.461Z\t512\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRocky Shore Transect Monitoring in Milford Haven following the Sea Empress Oil Spill\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333332\tThree months before the Sea Empress oil spill, baseline surveys of rocky shore fauna and flora were undertaken at fixed transect sites throughout Milford Haven and the Daugleddau Estuary. Following the spill Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned a full re-survey of the fixed transects in 1996 and 1997. The purpose of this data capture was to identify the effects of the oil spill on the flora and fauna of the rocky shore at Milford Haven through comparisons with baseline surveys and to provide quantitative data on important species for future analysis of recovery. This dataset contains the results of the survey work.\tIn October 1995 Milford Haven Waterway Environmental Monitoring Steering Group (MHWEMSG) commissioned a survey at 31 fixed transect sites. During the first few days of the spill and subsequent months a series of close-up photographs and quantitative data of limpet and barnacle abundances were collected at 11 of the monitoring sites. In 1996 all 31 transects were re-surveyed. The abundance of conspicuous animals, algae and lichens were noted and recorded from 3 m by 30 cm strips. Abundance scales used were the established scales as described in Baker and Wolff (1987). Any points of interest on the shores or relating to the populations observed were noted and sites were photographed for visual comparison purposes. In 1997 the monitoring was continued at ten sites, concentrating on the effects of the oil spill and the recovery from these effects. The methods were modified from that used in 1995 and 1996- the standard programme of work at each site was slightly reduced and additional quantitative recording of limpet, barnacles and fucoid algae was undertaken at selected stations. The only data associated with this project is held within the report.\t\tArchived as project no 101 (media no 376) and project no 108 (media no 381 )  H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media376 H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media381 CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder.\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Marine Vertebrates, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1998-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:43:14.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17750\tNRW_DS110333\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.330Z\t493\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBiotope studies on rocky shores following the Sea Empress oil spill\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333333\tThis dataset consists of the results of a series of broadscale studies on rocky sea shores around the open coast of South Pembrokeshire following the Sea Empress oil spill in February 1996. The dataset includes maps and diagrams of biotopes, list of regional biotopes referred to in survey and target notes. The purpose of this data capture was to help assess the effects of oil on shore biotopes, communities and species over whole shore areas, to provide information on biotopes, habitat, communities or species which will assist in the development of future monitoring or surveillance strategies, and to investigate the role of Phase 1 studies in surveillance and monitoring of rocky intertidal areas. Surveys were concentrated on the open coast of South Pembrokeshire, from West Angle Bay to Manorbier. All survey carried out by experienced surveyors.\tSurveys at the Phase 1 level were carried immediately after the oil spill using adaptations of the methodology defined in the \u0027Handbook for Marine Intertidal Phase 1 and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)Habitat mapping\u0027 Richards et al (1996) and \u0027Phase 1 Littoral Biotope Mapping\u0027 Smith and Bunker (1997). Good quality colour aerial photographs formed the basis of the survey, detailed target notes were recorded within biotopes using either notes or a high quality video camera as a note book. A photographic record of the study was also taken. Further studies were carried out on selected shores at varying intervals until July 1997.\t\t[A] Archived as project no 105, media no 379 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 379) [B] Report available from NRW library\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1998-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:53.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17753\tNRW_DS110334\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.354Z\t239\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEffects of oil and clean-up of oil from the Sea Empress on the nationally rare and scarce vascular plants of Pembrokeshire\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333334\tThis dataset is made up of the results of an investigation into the impact of the Sea Empress oil spillage and its clean-up on populations of some of the rare and scarce vascular plants that were well recorded in Pembrokeshire prior to the pollution incident. The purpose of this data capture was to reassess the population size of selected nationally rare and nationally scarce vascular plants of the Milford Haven waterway and the South Pembrokeshire coast.\tPlants that had potentially been in direct contact with liquid oil or grew in situations where oil contaminated salt spray was likely to have occurred were targeted for resurvey. Plants adjacent to access routes to sites of active beach clean-up were also considered a priority. Field work involved revisiting rare and scarce vascular plants at risk where precise pre-Sea Empress locational and population size data existed. Copies of previously completed population forms were taken into the field and the current extent of the plants checked.  Previous information was incomplete, natural fluctuations in population size from year to year in annual species, lack of precision in many pre-1996 records, limiting survey effort to subjectively chosen selected pant populations may have led to a loss of significant data.\t\tArchived as project no 110 (media no 384). Report No. 289 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 384. Media 379 also mentioned originally in this entry but seems to have no connection.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:53.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17747\tNRW_DS110336\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.312Z\t819\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of golden hair lichen (Teloschistes flavicans) in Pembrokeshire following the Sea Empress oil spill\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333336\tTeloschistes flavicans, the golden haired lichen, is a species formally widespread in the UK, but now mainly found only in South-West England and Wales. It is included as \u0027vulnerable\u0027 in the Red Data Book for British Lichens and is included on schedule 8 of the Wildlife and Countryside Act. It is a species particularly sensitive to sulphur dioxide air pollution. This dataset includes the results of a resurvey of Teloschistes flavicans in South Pembrokeshire following the Sea Empress oil spill in February 1996. A comprehensive survey of all known Teloschistes flavicans sites was undertaken in Pembrokeshire in 1994. It was considered likely that the lichen might be detrimentally affected by air-borne pollutants arising from the Sea Empress oil spill and consequently a resurvey was commissioned.\tPopulations were relocated in all sites using photographs and the 1994 survey. Photographs were taken of location and where possible salient features were photographed and/or marked with yellow enamel paint. Population density of small populations was estimated using a 20x30cm quadrat with 100 intersections and recording number of squares containing Teloschistes. Permanent quadrat locations, 20 x 30cm, were established on Ramsey, Skomer, and Manorbier- quadrat locations marked with yellow enamel paint.  Photographs of situation and quadrat were taken.\t\tArchived as project no 99 (media no 374). Report No. 237 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media301 to 400\\Media374)\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17759\tNRW_DS110337\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.458Z\t414\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInfauna of heavily oiled shores in Milford Haven and Carmarthen Bay following the Sea Empress oil spill\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333337\tThe sediment shores of Pembrokeshire are of many types, from coarse sand, storm beaches to the soft fine muds of the Milford Haven Estuary. Following the grounding of the Sea Empress it became obvious that several sediment shores, particularly in the lower reaches off the Milford Haven, were vulnerable to serious damage as a results of large quantities of crude oil spilt. The physical characteristics of beaches determine to a large extent the types and range of biota present. This dataset includes the results of an assessment of the effects of the Sea Empress oil spill on the infauna of sediment shores in Milford Haven and Carmarthen Bay. The purpose of this data capture was to carry out biological and hydrocarbon analyses to assess the impact of the oil spill incident on sediment infauna.\tPost spill survey at a selection of damaged sites was undertaken as a matter of urgency. Once each survey station had been located in the field, samples for biological analyses (0.01m2 steel corers used to take samples to a depth of 15cm, four replicates per sampling station) and hydrocarbon analyses (samples taken using a solvent washed, stainless steel scoop and transferred into solvent washed aluminium containers) were taken. Initially sites were sampled intensively, once per month, in order to record any abrupt changes in faunal communities related to the pollution event. The time interval was later extended to three months. Biological data were recorded on a spreadsheet and examined using the PRIMER software package designed for the assessment of change in marine communities using various types of statistical analyses. Analysis of total hydrocarbon concentration present was carried out. Previous work was undertaken in 1995 by Rostron, and four stations were also established at West Angle, a site close to the grounded tanker, previously surveyed in 1969 by Withers 1972.\t\tArchived as project no 102 (media no 377). Report No. 244 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 377) Report available in CCW  library, see library catalogue/ \u0027related titles\u0027 field. CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (Survey no.  MRCCW10000000083)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team   Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1998-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17748\tNRW_DS110342\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.330Z\t643\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEcological impacts of shoreline clean-up during the Sea Empress Oil spill\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333432\tThis dataset contains the results of an assessment of the effects of shoreline clean-up activity following the Sea Empress oil spill, concentrating on conspicuous species of rocky shore algae and animals, coastal vegetation and signs of physical disturbance. The Sea Empress ran aground on the 15th of February 1996. Shoreline clean-up activities at over 100 sites around south-west Wales began on the 16th of February and were scaled down gradually from July 1996 through to summer 1997.  The purpose of this data capture was to carry out: -  An assessment of the environmental impacts of each individual clean up technique;  - Compare the impacts with those described from previous spills and experimental studies;  - Make recommendations on the suitability and application of techniques in different situations; - Assess the overall impact of the clean-up activity, in relation to the impacts of the oil pollution itself; and, - Make recommendations for future monitoring and studies of the effects of clean-up activities.\t23 sites across south-west Wales were surveyed. A field programme was developed in the light of information available on the extent of oiling; the condition of the shore biota before, during and immediately after the incident; the clean-up techniques used and the availability of comparable habitats. Early observations involved taking photographs, video and detailed notes at numerous sites. Desk studies were undertaken to review the information collected on the effects of the clean-up activities on the biota of the shores and to collate detailed information about the clean-up activity on each site. Field studies involved two types of sites: General observation sites- general observations on the state of the shore and biological communities in and adjacent to clean up sites were recorded and photographs taken of any evidence of physical or biological damage. Detailed study sites- notes on the degree and type of oiling and the dates and type of clean up applied were taken as were time series photographs and invertebrate abundance measurements.\t\tArchived as project no 122 (media no 363) H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media363 Report available from CCW library (see library catalogue/\u0027related titles\u0027 field)\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17764\tNRW_DS110343\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.509Z\t617\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvival Rate of Rehabilitated Guillemots\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333433\tThe results of a study on the post-release life expectancy and oiled and cleaned guillemots has caused concern for seabird rehabilitation. This lead to considerable concern that seabird rehabilitation programmes are of low conservation value and may even prolong the suffering of the rescued birds. The study aimed to: - Calculate the survival rates of oiled, cleaned and released guillemots, - Compare these with the natural survival rates of guillemots which have not been oil, cleaned and released, - Assess whether the survival of rehabilitated birds has increased in recent years as cleaning techniques have improved, - Critically examine the quality of the available data and make recommendations for future improvements.\tMost of the analysis within the study used only data from birds ringed with \u0027guillemot specials\u0027 made of stronger incoloy, which were introduced in the early 1980s. BTO ring recovery data was used to calculate the survival rates of oiled, cleaned and released guillemots and compare these with the natural survival rates of guillemots which have not been oiled, cleaned and released. An assessment was also made as to whether the survival of rehabilitated birds has increased as cleaning techniques have improved. The only data associated with this project is held within the report.\t\tArchived as project no 96 (media no 371) H:\\MFSG\\Data\\Media301 to 400\\Media371 Report available from CCW library (see library catalogue/\u0027related titles\u0027 field)\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy, Marine Ornithologist  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tBritish Trust for Ornithology (BTO)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17751\tNRW_DS110344\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.343Z\t382\tValid\tEditable\tBroadleaved Woodland in North Wales\tCoetiroedd collddail yng Ngogledd Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333434\tA historically important dataset underpinning woodland site designation in North Wales. 260 woodland sites surveyed across North Wales.  The purpose of this data capture was to survey and evaluate in terms of their nature conservation interest all predominantly broadleaved woods in North Wales using nationally developed standard methods and evaluation criteria. The data collected helped to identify the \u0027best\u0027 examples and assisted in management and protection of the woods by making it possible to view them in context within an overall classification.\tProduced a list of woodlands for potential survey. Followed criteria (include woods over 6ha and broadleaved woodlands containing mainly or entirely native tree species, exclude scattered woodland, areas of scrub, woodlands containing dense rhododendron understorey etc) and made site visits to select woodlands worthy of a detailed survey.  Field survey following the Merlewood Classification system, Bunce (1982). Evaluation of collected data.\t\t1) Raw data- original copies held in CCW North Wales regional offices, photocopies of parts of the dataset held by Jim Latham at CCW Headquarters. 2) Paper reports- held in CCW libraries (Regional and HQ- see related titles field).\tJim Latham, Woodland and Spatial Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1985-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-16T15:26:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17746\tNRW_DS110345\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.173Z\t311\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWoodland Recording Cards\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333435\tApproximately 3,000 recording cards from woodland surveys in Wales. Some are from structured surveys e.g. the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) surveys of woodland Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), whilst others are ad-hoc recording events. The records can include site name, grid references, aspect, slope, altitude, area, species lists, height, cover, age class abundance etc. The purpose of this data capture was to gather compositional and structural information about woodlands; to gain an understanding of their species diversity and conservation importance; to influence site designation and to identify future research and survey needs.\tUsed JNCC standard woodland recording cards from the 1980\u0027s onwards. Earlier records are on a variety of ancestral versions of the standard card.\t\tHeld in box files in Jim Latham\u0027s (woodland ecologist) office.  Relocated to Woodland Tambour Unit in Old Post Room F12.\tInternal Custodian: Jim Latham, Woodland Ecologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW       Includes recently found box for Carmarthen.  Long terms plan is to digitise the habitat maps into GIS, scan the field notes and attach them to the GIS polygon via a hotlink.  Additionally species information should be transcribed to spread sheet for import to corporate biological recording database.  Could be let under data digitising \u0026amp; scanning  call-off contract when one is put in place\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17761\tNRW_DS110347\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.475Z\t845\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironmental Impact Assessment of Borden Chemicals Long Sea Outfall (March 2001)\tAsesiad ar yr Effaith ar yr Amgylchedd o arllwysfa mor hir Borden Chemicals\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130333437\tBorden Chemicals (formerly B.P. Chemicals) is a large chemical works located to the east of Barry, south Wales. Effluent is released into the Bristol Channel via a long sea outfall which discharges approximately 1 km offshore at 51degrees 23.41 N 03degrees 14.49 W. This replaced an old outfall in 1993 that discharged into the intertidal zone to the east of Barry docks at 51degrees 23.67 N 03degrees 14.88 W. The effluent is a complex mix of mostly organic compounds including phenol, toluene, 1,2-dichloroethane, methanol, acrylonitrile and vinyl chloride.  However, the discharge generally meet the levels set by their consent for materials discharged into the waters of the Bristol Channel.   This dataset contains the results of a survey of the benthic environment in the vicinity of the Borden Chemicals long sea outfall, east of Barry in the Bristol Channel. The purpose of this data capture was to collect data to conduct biological analysis as well as sediment samples and physico-chemical analyses to provide information for an Environmental Impact Assessment. As of the 1st April 2013, Natural Resources Wales has taken over the functions currently carried out by the Countryside Council for Wales, Environment Agency Wales and Forestry Commission Wales.\tThe data was entered into Marine Recorder as part of a data collation exercise undertaken by CCW in 2002.  Details of the data coallated project can be found in CCW Science Report no. 515.\t\t[A] A copy of the raw data is archived as part of the Marine Sites: Data collation project as project 27, media 81 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100) [B] Species and habitat data is available in Marine Recorder [MRCCW90000000007]\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:26:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17752\tNRW_DS110433\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.315Z\t316\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW) Upland Framework\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343333\tA framework developed to improve the quality and diversity of habitats on Wales\u0027 statutory upland sites.  The essence of the framework is a map based overview of statutory sites in the Welsh uplands, outlining the future, long-term conservation ideals for each upland statutory site. This dataset is made up of two layers, Upland Framework and the Upland Framework font point. The purpose of this data capture was to develop national and regional conservation priorities by highlighting important features and prioritising their conservation within particular area; and, provide guidance for management planning, including setting long-term objectives and steering management to achieve these objectives on individual sites.\tMaps were put together between 2003 and 2007 using data collected during the 1980\u0027s and 1990\u0027s. The project is ongoing and therefore there may be updates in the future.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Upland Framework Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\Upland_Framework\tCustodian: Liz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW (ex-CCW custodian Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist)  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t2007-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:53.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17757\tNRW_DS110463\t2024-05-28T12:30:31.472Z\t473\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveys of Sea Caves in Welsh Special Areas of Conservation (SAC)\tArolygon o ogofau mor yn Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343633\tInventorial survey as a first step for former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), to develop sensitivity management and monitoring for submerged or partially submerged caves.  Submerged or partially submerged caves are an Annex 1 habitat listed under the Habitats directive. Caves vary in size, from only a few metres to more extensive systems, which may extend hundreds of metres into the rock. There may be tunnels or caverns with one or more entrances, in which vertical and overhanging rock faces provide the principal marine habitat. Sea cave communities vary considerably depending on the structure and extent of the cave system, their degree of submergence and of exposure to sand scour and wave-surge, and their geology. Caves are typically colonised by encrusting animal species but may also support shade-tolerant seaweeds near their entrances. Physical conditions, such as inclination, wave surge, scour and shade, change rapidly from cave entrance to the inner parts of a cave, and this often leads to a marked gradation in the communities present.  Surveys were set up to identify a baseline and trial survey methodologies in order to establish surveillance and monitoring programmes for the feature.  24 caves (and tunnels) were described. They include caves that are wholly Intertidal, wholly subtidal and ones with both littoral and sublittoral elements. They range from caves that extend 1-3 meters into the cliff to ones that extend up to 100 meters into the cliff.  Inventorial survey as a first step for CCW to develop sensitivity management and monitoring for submerged or partially submerged caves.  24 caves across Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC), Cardigan Bay SAC, Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC and Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay SAC.  All measurements were taken to a standard protocol as described in the CCW Marine Monitoring Report no. 6.  Specimens were fixed and preserved.\tPhase 2 survey methods were used to describe habitats and communities. Measurements were taken and diagrams produced to show approximate dimensions of the caves and the distribution of the biotopes within them. The caves were marked with pitons or eye bolts to facilitate future monitoring programmes.  Cave location was determined by GPS.  Video footage was taken in selected caves.\t\tAll data is archived under project 243, media no. 777 including species spreadsheets reformatted for analysis in PRIMER.   Site locations are also available on the m drive and report is available in Ffynnon.  Photographs are available on the Marine Monitoring Team catalogue (CD 21, 22, 24, 44), video grabs available on the Marine Monitoring Team catalogue (CD 40).  Specimens are held by Francis Bunker and will be transferred to the National Museum of Wales in Cardiff. Original paper MNCR forms are held in CCW off-site storage.  (Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:37:18.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17742\tNRW_DS110464\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.702Z\t306\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCemlyn Lagoon Soft Sediment Infauna Survey (2006 - 2012)\tArolwg o isfilod gwaddodion meddal Lagwn Cemlyn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343634\tThe aim of the EC Habitats Directive 92/43/EEC is to promote the maintenance of biodiversity by requiring Member States to take measures to maintain or restore natural habitats and wild species listed on the Annexes to the Directive at a favourable conservation status, introducing robust protection for those habitats and species of European importance. Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) are strictly protected sites designated under the Habitats Directive.   In the UK coastal lagoons show a wide range of geographical and ecological variation; five main sub-types have been identified in the UK, on the basis of their physiography, as meeting the definition of the Annex I habitat type. These are graded from A to D.   - A Outstanding examples of the feature in a European context.  - B Excellent examples of the feature, significantly above the threshold for Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) notification but of somewhat lower value than grade A sites.  - C Examples of the feature which are of at least national importance (i.e. usually above the threshold for SSSI notification on terrestrial sites) but not significantly above this. These features are not the primary reason for SACs being selected.  - D Features of below SSSI quality occurring on SACs. These are non-qualifying features (“non-significant presence”), indicated by a letter D, but this is not a formal global grade.     Cemlyn lagoon on the north coast of Anglesey, north Wales, is considered to be the best example of a saline coastal lagoon in Wales (Graded A) and forms part of the Cemlyn Bay SAC. The lagoon is separated from the sea by a shingle bank with a narrow channel at the western end, across which a sluice system was built in the 1930s. Seawater exchange occurs mainly through the sluice and by percolation through the shingle bank, although in extreme storms coinciding with spring tides waves break over the top of the shingle bank. Cemlyn lagoon supports a relatively diverse set of species, several of which are specific to lagoons, including the bryozoan Conopeum seurati, the lagoon cockle Cerastoderma glaucum and the lagoonal mud-snail Ventrosia ventrosa. Cemlyn lagoon is also the only site in Wales where the lagoonal isopod Idotea chelipes has been recorded. A number of uncommon plant species are found within the lagoon, including the brackish water-crowfoot Ranunculus baudotii and beaked tasselweed Ruppia maritima. The purpose of this data capture is to aid in the monitoring and condition assessment of this Annex I feature. This dataset is a collation of data from subtidal benthic soft sediment sampling carried out within Cemlyn lagoon.\tData capture consisted of benthic invertebrates abundance records taken from samples collected using a mini-Eckman grab (0.025m2) and granulometry readings. All samples Benthic invertebrate samples are sieved over a 0.5 mm mesh. Supporting determinants consist of National Grid References (NGR), recorded using hand-help GPS; salinity and temperature readings, which are continuously recorded at site 2 using a data logger. Infaunal sampling is carried out annually in December. Salinity and temperature are recorded continuously.  Benthic invertebrate samples are processed by laboratories that are members of the National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control (NMBAQC) scheme and random samples from each survey are also selected for audit by Unicomarine.\t\tAll data is archived under project no. 241 and media no. 766.   A working copy is held on S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Lagoons\\Cemlyn Lagoon\tInternal Custodians - Marine monitoring Team, NRW  - Bill Sanderson, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG  Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:29:00.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17744\tNRW_DS110466\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.702Z\t606\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPickleridge Lagoon Soft Sediment Infauna Survey (2006 - 2013)\tArolwg o isfilod gwaddodion meddal Lagwn Pickleridge\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343636\tThis dataset is a collation of data from subtidal benthic soft sediment sampling carried out within Pickleridge lagoon, which is a feature of the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The purpose of this data capture was to aid in the monitoring and condition assessment of this Annex I feature.\tMonitoring work is carried out at 3 fixed sample stations on each sampling visit. Infaunal sampling is carried out annually in Winter. Salinity and temperature are recorded continuously. Benthic invertebrate samples are processed by laboratories that are members of the National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control (NMBAQC) scheme and random samples from each survey are also selected for audit by Unicomarine.  Data captured methods. Abundance of benthic invertebrates (per 0.0044m2) and granulometry. Supporting determinants are: National Grid References recorded using hand held GPS and salinity and temperature which are continuously recorded at middle site using a data logger. Three repeatable stations are sampled (North, middle and South). All samples are collected using a corer (0.0044m2). Benthic invertebrate samples are sieved over a 0.5mm mesh.\t\t[A]  All data is archived under project no. 241,  media no 764.   [B]  A working copy is held on S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Lagoons\\Pickleridge\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Technical Specialist, MFSG. Assisstant Marine Conservation Officer, Skomer. Bill Sanderson, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG.  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:29:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17741\tNRW_DS110467\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.655Z\t711\tValid\tEditable\tMorfa Gwyllt Lagoon Soft Sediment Infauna Survey (2006 - 2012)\tArolwg o isfilod gwaddodion meddal Lagwn Morfa Gwyllt\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343637\tCoastal lagoons are areas of shallow, coastal salt water, wholly or partially separated from the sea by sandbanks, shingle or, less frequently, rocks. Lagoons show a wide range of geographical and ecological variation; five main sub-types have been identified in the UK, on the basis of their physiography, as meeting the definition of the Annex I habitat type. Annex 1 habitat types are those habitats which are listed under the EC Habitats Directive and form part of Special Area of Conservation (SAC) interest features. Morfa Gwyllt lagoon in classed as one of these features within the Pen Llyn a`r Sarnau/ Lleyn Peninsula and the Sarnau SAC. Morfa Gwyllt lagoon is a small percolation lagoon that consists of a depression in a shingle bar across the mouth of the Afon Dysynni in mid Wales. This is the only example of a percolation lagoon in Wales. The substrate is a mosaic of medium sand over/amongst shingle, with muddier patches within the deeper pockets, and scattered larger pebbles.   Three lagoonal specialists have been found at this site: the amphipod Sphaeroma hookeri, the bryozoan Conopeum seurati and the alga Chaetomorpha linum. This dataset is a collation of data from subtidal benthic soft sediment sampling carried out within Morfa Gwyllt lagoon collected to aid the monitoring and condition assessment of this Annex 1 feature.\tMonitoring work is carried out at 10 randomly selected sample stations on each sampling visit. Infaunal sampling if invertebrates (per 0.005m2) and granulometry was carried out at each location whilst the salinity and temperature are recorded continuously.  S  upporting determinants are: National Grid References recorded using hand held GPS, salinity and temperature which are continuously recorded using a data logger. All samples are collected using a corer (0.005m2). Benthic invertebrate samples are sieved over a 0.5 mm mesh. A minimum of 10 random samples are carried out within the lagoon each year.  There is high confidence in this datasets. Benthic invertebrate samples are processed by laboratories that are members of the National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control (NMBAQC) scheme and random samples from each survey are also selected for audit by Unicomarine.\t\tAll data is archived under project no. 241 and  media no 765.   A working copy is held on Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Lagoons\\Morfa Gwyllt\tInternal Custodians:  Marine Technical Specialist, MFSG Marine Monitoring Ecologist, MFSG  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:35:12.399Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17745\tNRW_DS110469\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.701Z\t501\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe condition and conservation of montane heath and summit heath vegetation in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343639\tThis dataset contains the results of an investigation into the relationship between species composition, grazing impacts, nitrogen pollution and nitrogen status of montane heath communities of the Carneddau massif in Snowdonia.  The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the links between species composition, grazing impacts, nitrogen pollution and nitrogen status of montane heath communities on the Cardeddau massif. The main objective was to provide information on the current status of the communities in order to indicate whether overgrazing is likely to be the main factor in their decline and whether a reduction in grazing levels might result in the restoration of these communities in North Wales.\tA total of 37 sites were established along the westernmost section of the Carneddau ridge. They were selected to be representative of the vegetation occurring along this section of the ridge and to include sites thought to be accessible to sheep but away from human trampling influences. Sites were dispersed with a minimum distance of 30 m between plots.  At each site a dung plot was set up and vegetation species composition recorded. At some sites samples of soil and plant tissue were taken for chemical analysis and samples of Racomitrium were collected for nitrate reductase analysis. Data collection involved dung plot monitoring, soil chemistry analysis, plant chemistry analysis, nitrate reductase assay and plant community composition measurements. Data analysis was undertaken to analyse the relationship between species composition, soils and environmental data (altitude, aspect, dung count).\t\tHardcopy of report is available from CCW libraries (see related titles field). PDF is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  (ex-CCW, Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist.) Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T13:30:33.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17743\tNRW_DS110470\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.686Z\t606\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCwm Idwal Surveillance data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343730\tThis dataset currently contains the first year\u0027s recorded data following the establishment of a long-term vegetation surveillance system at Cwm Idwal National Nature Reserve (NNR). An earlier programme of intensive surveillance was undertaken at Cwm Idwal during 1999-2004 in response to the long term exclusion of grazing. This current surveillance project involves the establishment of a new, less intensive surveillance programme based on just eleven of the original plots, which will be repeated at three year intervals. The dataset includes a summary of plot frequency data, summary statistics for each species/feature, digital photographs, general observations on the eleven plots and plot identification sketches. The purpose of this data capture was to inform large-scale restoration projects in other parts of the Welsh uplands and provide information on the effects of such management decisions for the restoration of upland sites.\tThis work was commissioned following an earlier programme of intensive surveillance between 1999 and 2004 (see separate metadata entry entitled \u0027Cwm Idwal vegetation change surveillance project 1999-2004\u0027).  A number of the original landscape photographs have also been replicated, whilst a further series of additional views have also been taken from new vantage-points. All photography employed a Digtial SLR camera of 6.0 megapixels resolution. Eleven plots from the 1999-2004 surveillance projects were chosen either because they were located in transitional zones between vegetation types or in localities where changes were expected or desired. The eleven plots employ the same quadrat locations as those originally used. Presence/absence of selected species were recorded as were occurrences of bare ground, exposed peat, rock, litter, sheep/goat droppings and any other notable feature using a standard recording card. The data from these cards were then entered into excel spreadsheets. At least two photographs were taken of each plot and one of every quadrat recorded. Differential GPS was used to records plot and quadrat positions.\t\t[A]  Hardcopy of report is available from CCW libraries (see related titles field). Word version is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist [B]  Raw data is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist [C]  Digital images on CCWs upland ecologist hardrive and on CD in TSG Archives\tPete Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, NRW  Jane Stevens, Habitat and Ecosystem Resource Analyst, NRW (Formerly: Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist)\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2007-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:53.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17738\tNRW_DS110473\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.523Z\t1253\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCwm Idwal Vegetation Change Surveillance Project 1999-2004\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343733\tThe results of a five year surveillance project carried out in Cwm Idwal National Nature Reserve (NNR), from which grazing has been excluded on a long term experimental basis.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish a detailed monitoring system to determine the long term impact of grazing removal on a range of vegetation communities; and, to determine the nature and direction of vegetation recovery in terms of its structure and composition.\tAttributes recorded for vegetation included percentage cover, mean and maximum height for individual strata, total cover for individual species within quadrats, estimated total cover for bryophytes and lichens. Impacts were recorded in terms of estimated dunging, shoot nibbling and erosion. Photographs were taken throughout the project to assist in the process of plot relocation, to give an impression of the type and condition of vegetation at the time of recording, and to illustrate selected general and specific subjects. Baseline survey of 30 plots was carried out autumn 1998 to early summer 1999. The first repeat survey was carried out in the autumn of 2000 (10 of the 30 plots), second repeat survey took place in 2001 (same 10 plots as in 2000), third repeat survey in 2002 (same 10 plots plus 15 others), fourth repeat survey (same 10 plots as in 2000) and in 2004 all 30 plots were resurveyed at the end of the 5 year cycle.  This surveillance data covers most vegetation communities however scree, chasmophytic and tall herb ledge vegetation are not included- they are being monitored under a separate programme.\t\ta) Hardcopy of final report is available from CCW libraries (see related titles field). Word version is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist. Annual interim reports also exist.  b) Raw data is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist c) Photographs available from CCW\u0027s upland ecologist\tLiz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW (exCCW Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:25.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17739\tNRW_DS110474\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.527Z\t499\tUnassessed\tEditable\tImpact of Grazing and Nitrogen Deposition on the Condition of Racomitrium lanuginosum on the Carneddau Mountains\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343734\tResults of work conducted on the moss heath habitat of the Carneddau massif to establish whether the overall condition of the dominant woolly fragile moss Racomitrium lanuginosum was associated with grazing pressure. The purpose of this data capture was to: - Determine whether the condition and growth of R. lanuginosum (on the basis of tissue chemistry, physiology, and growth rate) differs in areas of differing grazing intensity.  - Investigate the recovery potential of R. lanuginosum under the current pollution climate if grazers were removed.  - Find out whether membrane potassium leakage from R. lanuginosum shoots increases under increased levels of trampling damage and whether the depth of R. lanuginosum is affected by increased levels of trampling damage.\tThe location, altitude and aspect of 31 randomly distributed plots were recorded. Two sample points were set up at each plot. A composite sample of Racomitrium shoots was collected from each sample point for tissue chemistry analysis. The samples were used to analyse the nitrogen assimilation capacity of R. lanuginosum, compare shoot growth between different habitats and measure membrane potassium leakage. Simulated sheep trampling was undertaken to evaluate whether sheep trampling damages moss tissue causing membrane potassium ion leakage.  This work follows on from a study the year before looking at the condition and conservation of montane heath and summit heath vegetation in Wales (CCW Science report 643).\t\ta) Hardcopy of report is available from CCW libraries (see related titles field). Word version is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist b) Raw data is available on hardrive of CCW\u0027s upland ecologist\tPete Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader Jane Stevens, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW Formerly, Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17740\tNRW_DS110476\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.647Z\t588\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Upland Vegetation Surveys of Wales (1999 - 2008)\tCyfnod 2 Arolygon o Ucheldiroedd yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343736\tThis dataset contains both the non-spatial and spatial (GIS) outputs from a vegetation surveys of upland Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales to National Vegetation Classification (NVC) level.   The purpose of this data capture was to provide data on the extent and distribution of vegetation communities in the Welsh uplands for planning, notification and management purposes. Phase 1 level vegetation surveys of around 48 upland sites in Wales.   Each site has a dataset which includes a published report, quadrat data tables, maps (most paper maps have been digitised and are available as MapInfo tables and ESRI Feature Classes) and site descriptions.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys allbynnau gofodol arolygiadau llystyfiant ar gyfer Safleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGA) yn ucheldir Cymru i lefel Dosbarthiad Llystyfiant Cenedlaethol. Diben casglu\u0027r data hwn oedd darparu data ar raddau a dosbarthiad cymunedau llystyfiant yn ucheldir Cymru at ddibenion cynllunio, hysbysu a rheoli. Mae\u0027r set ddata wedi\u0027i chadarnhau a\u0027i dilysu\u0027n drwyadl. Fodd bynnag, gall cyfansoddiad cynefinoedd newid dros amser o ganlyniad i dueddiadau olynol, rheolaeth tir neu hyd yn oed ddatblygiadau arfaethedig. Felly, ni ellir bod yn sicr bod math o lystyfiant neu rywogaeth yn parhau i fodoli am ei fod wedi\u0027i gofnodi yn y data digidol.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tStandard National Vegetation Classification (NVC) methodology using quadrat records to classify vegetation stands.   The data has been digitised into Recorder 6 and also available spatially through a series of ESRI Feature Classes.\t\tNRW\u0027s Upland Ecologist holds a copy of each of the reports; they are also available from the HQ and regional NRW libraries MapInfo tables are stored on former CCW\u0027s m:drive: M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\upland_survey  X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Upland  The NVC data is stored in Recorder under one survey CCW Upland Survey CCW0007400000001. There are separate events for each geographic location.\tInternal Custodian: Jane Stevens, TEG, NRW Formerly Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-02-07\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3608/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_phase_2_upland\t2025-12-19T10:54:03.393Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17737\tNRW_DS110477\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.528Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey of Bannau Brycheiniog/Brecon Beacons Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343737\tGeographic coverage: Brecon Beacons SSSI\t\t\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist\tPete Jones - Terrestrial Ecosystems Team Leader... Used to be Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-09-05T12:36:20.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n17736\tNRW_DS110478\t2024-05-28T12:30:30.229Z\t684\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey of Mynydd Llangatwg and Mynydd Llangyndir\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343738\tThis dataset comprised of the results from a survey of an upland site in and adjacent to the Brecon Beacons National Park, carried out during May 2002 by the Central Environmental Surveys on behalf of the former Countryside Council for Wales. Within the report the survey is divided into two sites. One site includes the area within the boundary of Mynydd Llangatwg Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). The other site includes land to the south-west of the Mynydd Llangatwg SSSI.\tThe sites were visited on a daily basis by a team of four surveyors. Covering a selected area each surveyor visited all the distinguishable vegetation stand within the target area. The aim was to determine the community type, the boundaries with other vegetation and a number of terrain, edaphic and physiognomic parameters, the precise data required usually being dependent on the type of vegetation covered. Terrain features that has to be determined included altitude, slope and aspect of quadrat locations and target notes. Assignment to plant community during the vegetation surveys was based on the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) standard. This raw data then went through a preparation process and were transposed into digital spatial layers.\t\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist.  Raw data and map images are also held within the report.\tPeter Jones, Terrestrial and Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader - NRW (used to be Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17795\tNRW_DS110479\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.561Z\t333\tValid\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey of Cadair Idris National Nature Reserve (NNR)\tArolwg o lystyfiant Gwarchodfa Natur Genedlaethol Cadair Idris\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343739\tThis dataset contains the survey results from the Cadair Idris National Nature Reserve between August and September 1999. The survey was commissioned by former Countryside Council for Wales, in order to map and describe the vegetation of Cadair Idris. The reserve comprises of approximately 450 hectares of the Cadair Idris massif some 6 km south of Dolgellau.\tThe fieldwork was carried out between 29 August and 3 September 1999. Different types of vegetation were distinguished from each other according to variation in flora and structure. These vegetation types were allocated to communities and sub-communities of the National Vegetation Classification.   The National Vegetation Classification (NVC) is a classification of natural and semi−natural vegetation types in the British Isles. The classification works on three levels:  The first is the broad vegetation type denoted by upper−case letters such as H for heath, W for woodland and M for mire.  The second level is the community. There are several within each broad vegetation type and they are denoted by numbers and names such as H18 Vaccinium myrtillus−Deschampsia flexuosa heath.  The final level is the sub-community.   These are divisions within communities. Each sub-community has a lower-case letter and a name, such as H18a Vaccinium myrtillus−Deschampsia flexuosa heath, Hylocomium splendens, Rhytidiadelphus loreus sub-community. NVC communities and sub-communities take their names from between one and three plant species which are characteristic of that NVC type in Great Britain as a whole. These are not necessarily the most common or conspicuous species in every example of the NVC type in question; the assignment of vegetation to an NVC type is based on the total flora.\t\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive \u0026amp; with CCWs upland ecologist Some of this data is also held in Recorder 6 under: CCW Upland Survey CCW0007400000001\tinternal Custodian: Pete Jones, Terrestrial and Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader  Jane Stevens (TEG) Formerly Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2000-02-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-28T16:41:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17792\tNRW_DS110481\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.505Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey of Mynydd Eglwyseg, Denbighshire\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343831\tTemporal coverage: 2000-2001\t\t\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive \u0026amp; with CCWs upland ecologist\tBarbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:09.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17787\tNRW_DS110482\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.297Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey of the Elenydd Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343832\tThe purpose of this survey was to map and describe the vegetation of twelve holdings in Elenydd Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) in mid Wales. These surveys were commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in order to map and describe the vegetation of two areas within Elenydd SSSI. Survey were carried out in August 1998, August 1999 and May 2002.\tDifferent types of vegetation were distinguished from each other according to variation in flora and structure. These vegetation types were allocated to \"communities\" and \"sub-communities\" of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). The National Vegetation Classification (NVC) is a classification of natural and semi-natural vegetation types in the British Isles.\t\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive \u0026amp; with CCWs upland ecologist\tBarbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17781\tNRW_DS110493\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.233Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Surveys of Eryri\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343933\t\t\t\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist\tBarbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17793\tNRW_DS110494\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.527Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey of parts of the Migneint-Dduallt Area\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343934\tThis survey was commissioned by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW). The work was done to map and describe the vegetation of several blocks of upland in the  Migneint-Dduallt area of north-west Wales and to help define the nature and boundaries of the Migneint-Dduallt Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and Special Area for Conservation (SAC). The whole survey area measures 58 square kilometres in area, and has an altitudinal range of 170-854 m. It consists of various types of habitat including level valley floors, steep valley sides, irregularly undulating hills and plateaux, and the high, steep-sided mountains of Arenig Fawr and Moel Llyfnant. The bedrock is a mixture of sedimentary and igneous rocks of Ordovician and Cambrian age, though much of the land is overlain by peat.\tThe fieldwork for this survey was carried out in the periods 13-20th July and 24 August - 9th September 2001. The vegetation was mapped at the scale of 1:10,000 using the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). Representative quadrats were recorded in 56 of the 71 types of vegetation and habitat found in the survey.\t\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist\t(Formerly: Barbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:56:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17794\tNRW_DS110495\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.529Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey of the Pumlumon Sites of Scientific Interest (SSSI)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343935\tThis dataset consists of the results from a vegetation surveys at Pumlumon Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) on the borders of Powys and Ceredigion. In September 2004 a survey of the National Vegetation Classification communities present, together with an assessment of the vegetation condition of four habitat types. The work was carried out by Rigby Jerram Ecological Consultants on behalf of Countryside Council for Wales.\tSurveys were conducted using National Vegetation Classification (NVC) methodology.\t\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist\tBarbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:56:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17791\tNRW_DS110496\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.403Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Surveys of Rhinog Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and Adjacent Land\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343936\tThis data collection was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in order to map, describe and assess the vegetation of the Rhinog Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) in north Wales. Ninety-five types of vegetation and habitat were records, the most extensive being lightly grazed Calluna vulgaris heaths.\tThe vegetation was mapped at the scale of 1:10,000 and categorised using the National Vegetation Classification. Most type of vegetation were sample by 2 m x 2, quadrats in which all species and their percentage cover were recorded. Additional information was recorded about the condition of the heaths and bogs.\t\tHard copies in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copies \u0026amp; electronic copies of reports with CCWs upland ecologist .  Digitised versions of maps for Rhinog SSSI survey also with CCWs upland ecologist\tBarbara Jones, CCW\u0027s Upland Ecologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:56:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17789\tNRW_DS110497\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.338Z\t639\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSea Fishing Atlas of Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343937\tWARNING: This data is outdated due to changes in fishery management and is not recommended for re-use. It could be misleading.  An atlas of sea fishing activities around the coast of Wales. The atlas includes sections on fishing activities, regulations and competent authorities (see \u0027contains\u0027 field for metadata entries for the three different sections). The purpose of this data capture was to outline general fishing patterns, regulations and competent authorities around Wales in order to present a general picture of fishing activity which has previously not been publicly available.\tWARNING: This data is outdated due to changes in fishery management and is not recommended for re-use. It could be misleading.  Maps of fishing activity were produced, covering the period 2000-2005. They have been prepared primarily on the basis of anecdotal comments received from a variety of regulators and individual fishermen, supplemented by information contained in published reports. It is important to note that they are purely indicative in nature and give a general indication of where fishing activity is thought to have occurred over the period 2000 to 2005, although no indication is made regarding the frequency of activity or the general level of effort deployed. While some of the maps are more detailed than others, specifics relating to individual operators are only shown for Several Order sites. A general description is provided alongside each activity map. For further explanation of some technical specifications, both in relation to sea fisheries and marine nature conservation, refer to a separate CCW publication. The maps produced are purely indicative in nature and give a general indication of where fishing activity is thought to have occurred although no indication is made regarding the frequency of activity or the general level of effort deployed.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries_Habitat_and_sensitivity\\Fishing_activity\\ Archived as project 332, media 1291  (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1291\tInternal Custodians: Sea Fisheries Liaison Officer, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:58:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17786\tNRW_DS110498\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.232Z\t83\tValid\tEditable\tSea Fishing Activity in Welsh Waters\tGweithgareddau pysgota mor yn nyfroedd Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343938\tWARNING: This data is outdated due to changes in fishery management and is not recommended for re-use. It could be misleading.    Information has been collated to produce a series of maps of sea fishing activities in Wales complete with regulatory boundaries (12nm, 6nm and 3nm). The purpose of this dataset was to provide spatial information for a range of fishing activities occurring in Welsh waters.   Activities mapped are:   - Beam trawling and scallop dredging  - Mussel, oyster and hydraulic dredging  - Otter trawling  - Light demersal trawls and seines  - Pelagic trawling, drift nets and hook and line fishing  - Static gear fishing - nets and long line  - Static gear fishing - pots  - Hand gathering  - Aquaculture   The dataset includes maps and a text description of each activity.\tWARNING: This data is outdated due to changes in fishery management and is not recommended for re-use. It could be misleading.    The maps of fishing activity have been prepared primarily on the basis of anecdotal comments received by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) from a variety of sources including contractors, relevant authorities, area officers, regulators and individual fisherman. This was supplemented by information contained in published reports.   Maps cover the period 2000-2005.  The maps are purely indicative in nature and give a general indication of where fishing activity is thought to have occurred at the given time, although no indication is made regarding the frequency of activity or the general level of effort deployed.    These layers were improved as part of the \\Distribution of Fisheries Habitats and Fishing Activity project\\\" in 2012 (NRW_DS115270). The layers were changed to the British National Grid projection and clipped to either the mean high water (MHW) or the Lowest Astronomical Tide (LAT). \"\t\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries_Habitat_and_sensitivity\\Fishing_activity\\ Archived as project 332, media 1291 (old version) at K:\\Archive\\Media 1291to1300\\Media 1291 or Project 376, Media 1396 (new version) at K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1396\tInternal Custodian:  Previous custodian who have now left or moved roles: Sea Fisheries Liaison Officer, NRW   Clare Eno and Rowland Sharp, NRW Environmental Policy Group  Current custodian: Internal Contact: Rowland Sharp, Senior Advisor, SW Area Operations (Marine)  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW       Removed from public view on the advice of marine fisheries experts as information is dated and could be misleading.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2012-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T10:28:39.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17778\tNRW_DS110499\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.114Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRegulations Governing Sea Fisheries in Welsh Waters\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130343939\tWARNING: This data is outdated due to changes in fishery management and is not recommended for re-use. It could be misleading.  This dataset contains a collation of all relevant regulations pertaining to Welsh water fisheries. The fisheries regulations are split according to whether they are general to Welsh waters (including relevant EU and UK regulations), Sea Fisheries Committee (SFC) districts or relate to specific locations around the coast. The relevant sections of the byelaws are quoted and correct to the end of 2008. Those fishing activities currently unregulated are also identified and mapped. Another component of the dataset is the Rainbow chart. The purpose of this data capture was to collate all relevant regulations relating to sea fishing activity in Welsh waters and to present the information spatially in the form of maps (most of the regulation information was previously available, primarily within SFC byelaws, but had not previously been mapped). The regulations documented are those that were in force up to December 2008, there may have been changes to some regulations or new regulations created since. For information on the current regulations the relevant organisations should be contacted.\tWARNING: This data is outdated due to changes in fishery management and is not recommended for re-use. It could be misleading.  Fishery regulations including Sea fishery and Environment Agency byelaws and European Union, United Kingdom, and Welsh Assembly Government regulations were collated by contractors. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff created paper maps showing area restricted byelaws relating roughly to the sea fishing activities documented in the Sea Fishing Atlas for Wales (see separate metadata entry - \u0027Sea Fishing activity in Welsh waters\u0027). These maps were digitised by a contractor. This dataset makes up a chapter of the Sea Fishing Atlas for Wales.\t\tArchived as project 332, media 1291 (K:\\Archive\\Media1201to1300\\Media 1291 Available from NRW library  Available on the hard drives of Clare Eno and Rowland Sharp, NRW Environmental Policy Group\tInternal Contact: Rowland Sharp, Senior Advisor, SW Area Operations (Marine)       This is part of a larger dataset the Sea Fishing Atlas of Wales. (which consisted of three parts) The regulations bit is just held in the marine archive and is not available on the X drive.  This is because no-one has ever asked for it and it was a snapshot and I imagine completely out of date so as long as the project and media number are there I don’t think we need a X drive location.    Removed from public view on the advice of marine fisheries experts as information is dated and could be misleading.\tAssociation Of Sea Fisheries Committees-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17780\tNRW_DS110500\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.132Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCompetent Authorities for Fisheries in Welsh Waters\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353030\tWARNING: This data is outdated due to changes in fishery management and is not recommended for re-use. It could be misleading  This dataset contains a collation of tables containing information on the competent authorities i.e. the regulatory bodies with current competence for managing fisheries (towed gears, scallop fishery, static gear- potting, mussel fishery, cockle fishery) within each marine Natura 2000 site in Welsh waters. The purpose of this data capture was to collate all relevant regulations relating to sea fishing activity in Welsh waters. Information including name of European marine site, competent authority, relevant legislation, \u0027permit\u0027 controls and comments collated by contractor. The regulations documented are those that were in force up to December 2008, there may have been changes to some regulations or new regulations created since. For information on the current regulations the relevant organisations should be contacted.\tWARNING: This data is outdated due to changes in fishery management and is not recommended for re-use. It could be misleading  This dataset makes up a chapter of the Sea Fishing Atlas for Wales. See \u0027part of\u0027 field.\t\ta) Report available from NRW library  b) Available on the hard drives of Clare Eno and Rowland Sharp, NRW Environmental Policy Group c) Archived as project 332, media 1291 (File path: K\\Archive\\Media 1200to1300\\Media 1291\tInternal Contact: Colin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine Fisheries, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW       Removed from public view on the advice of marine fisheries experts as information is dated and could be misleading.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17788\tNRW_DS110501\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.297Z\t871\tValid\tEditable\tModelling of Habitat Networks for Welsh Woodlands, Grasslands, Heathlands, Bogs and Fens\tModelu Rhwydweithiau Cynefinoedd ar gyfer coetiroedd, glaswelltiroedd, rhostiroedd, corsydd a ffeniau yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353031\tThere is a growing emphasis on ecological connectivity in planning for effective biodiversity conservation and building ecosystem resilience through Resilient Ecological Networks (RENs). A major part of this is to counteract the negative impacts of habitat fragmentation (reduction in are and increase in isolation).  Modelled habitat networks can provide insights into how landscapes are likely to be functioning and inform the location of actions to improve the connectivity and resilience of protected sites.  This is a spatial dataset consisting of maps of habitat networks originally developed by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in collaboration with Forestry Commission Wales and Forest Research using a functional networks approach, and now managed and progressively developed by NRW.  Patches of habitat and other intervening habitats through which many of their species are able to move are mapped as habitat networks.   Networks have been mapped for habitats including woodland, unimproved grassland, calcareous grassland, marshy grassland, heathland, fens and bogs; in most cases these are divided into upland and lowland versions and in some cases networks that support the highest quality areas of habitat have been selected out as priority layers.   Results are available for the whole of Wales as GIS layers and include three levels of habitat networks; core networks (areas within which species that require extensive habitat and disperse poorly are able to move), focal networks (areas within which species tolerant of smaller habitat patches and with greater dispersal ability are able to move), and local networks (areas within species that can persist within small habitat patches and have very limited dispersal abilities can move).    These habitat networks  also form the basis of ‘Priority Ecological Networks’ (PENs), a subset of the ‘focal’ networks described above which have been selected against our suite of Protected Sites.  Purpose of data capture was to allow the scope and range of potential networks to be rapidly explored. Predicted habitat networks can be used to guide large-scale planning for nature conservation, provide insight into how the landscape is likely to be functioning and prioritise action to improve the connectivity and viability of protected sites.  Layers are arranged in two folders: Level 1, containing all outputs across Wales, and Level 2 which are selected, priority networks, within which action may be targeted to enhance functional networks of the best habitat areas.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn darparu hanes y gwaith ar gysylltedd a mapio blaenoriaethol yng Nghymru, ac mae\u0027n darparu sylfaen ar gyfer mapio cysylltedd. Cyfres o haenau mapio yw\u0027r allbwn, a elwir yn rhwydweithiau craidd, ffocal a lleol. Gyda\u0027i gilydd mae\u0027r rhain yn darparu canllaw i gysylltedd cynefin cyflawn ac mae\u0027n gallu cael ei ddadansoddi mewn nifer o ffyrdd i gynorthwyo gwaith bioamrywiaeth a phrosiectau amgylcheddol yn gyffredinol. Mae mapio ar gael ar gyfer coetir llydanddail, gweundir, glaswelltir heb welliant, ffeniau a chorstiroedd, pob un (heblaw am goetir) mewn fersiynau uwchdir ac iseldir.  Cydnabyddiaeth:   Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\t\u0027Least-cost\u0027 modelling was carried out using Forest Research\u0027s BEETLE (Biological and Environmental Tools for Landscape Ecology) tools, coupled with  Habitat Survey of Wales digital dataset (Phase 1). The model is based on species characteristics, taking into account a minimum habitat area and the relative ecological cost of different habitats.  It relates to ease of movement through different land cover types, expressed in terms of dispersal distance in metres over a 50 year timescale.   In simple terms it draws a \u0027buffer\u0027 around habitat patches, the width of which depends upon the assumed permeability of the intervening habitats. When buffers around patches overlap the habitat patches are considered to be part of the same habitat network with many species potentially able to move between all habitat patches within it.   This project uses data from Phase 1 Habitat Survey of welsh woodlands, grasslands, heathlands and bogs and fens, supplemented with National Forest Inventory data for woodland.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Habitat Networks all Wales\\Lowland ,  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Habitat Networks all Wales\\Upland\tJim Latham, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team Leader\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-06-27\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3592/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_habitat_network\t2025-05-07T08:59:24.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17779\tNRW_DS110502\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.139Z\t414\tValid\tEditable\tA collation of National Vegetation Classification (NVC) data for Woodlands in Wales\tColadiad o ddata\u0027r Dosbarthiad Llystyfiant Cenedlaethol (NVC) ar gyfer Coetiroedd Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353032\tThe National Vegetation Classification (NVC) became established as the standard classification scheme for semi-natural woodlands in Britain, and has wide applications to nature conservation programmes. The NVC is the basis for selection of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and has been adopted as a fundamental tool for planning and creating new native woodlands.   This dataset is a collation of Woodland NVC data from former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) strategic surveys, individual site surveys, records from site visits, interpretation of pre-NVC surveys, and contributions from other bodies.   This purpose of this data capture was to provide a useful platform from which to review current understanding of woodland NVC in Wales, and give a snapshot of the woodland resource at the end of the 20th Century.  The total area surveyed is around 11,500 ha from 802 sites across Wales.   This data collation was also used to identify future research and survey needs. Areas requiring additional survey and any gaps in the understanding of communities could also be highlighted.\tA data collation and analysis project. The collated raw data includes estimates of community area, information on surveyor, survey date, number of quadrat samples, site name, grid reference, how areas have been measured, protected status, and various formulae to translate NVC to habitat Action plan types.   Reliability of records varies but there has been a tendency to be inclusive with records only rejected where there is good evidence that they are unreliable.   Data from surveys carried out between 1985 and 2000.\t\tRaw data spreadsheet and excel spreadsheet on the DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/wood/Woodland%20Types%20and%20Classification/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FTerrestrial%2DHabitats%2Fwood%2FWoodland%20Types%20and%20Classification%2FNVC%2FSurvey%20data%2FSURVEY\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000238FEB7B97F86443BBC09DF6F9B378DE\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BC1284E03%2DE26B%2D453C%2DB036%2DB0055ED6D8F8%7D  MapInfo table located at M:\\GIS_Users\\jim_l\\NVC\\nvc report - this needs to be translated before it is put on the X Drive. Paper reports held in CCW libraries (Regional and HQ - see related titles field).\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2001-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T13:46:17.561Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n17784\tNRW_DS110509\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.233Z\t330\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring and Surveillance of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353039\tThis dataset contains the results from on going monitoring of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) across Wales. One of the key responsibilities of the statutory nature conservation agencies, such as Natural Resources Wales (NRW), in the UK is the identification and protection of a series of sites intended to conserve important wildlife and earth science features. These sites include SACs which are designated as part of the EC Habitats Directive.  Every six years, Member States of the European Union are required to report on implementation of the Habitats Directive, under Article 17 of the Directive. This reporting provides information on: the general implementation of the Directive; and the conservation status of individual habitats and species listed under the Annexes of the Directive.\tEach SAC was designated for it\u0027s presence of habitats or species listed under Annex 1 or 2 of the Habitats Directive. As part of statutory duties listed under Article 17 of the Directive, NRW staff are required to monitor the presence, absence and coverage of these species and habitats and report of these every 6 years. Monitoring data has been collected for the following cycles, 1999 - 2006, 2007 - 2012, 2013 - 2018 (current monitoring cycle). This means there is over 15 years worth of data available on site features.\t\tMarine Monitoring is stored W: Drive  Terrestrial SAC Monitoring Paper Records: A filing cabinet of paper records which have not been digitised and is current stored with Tom Harrison.    Electronic Data: Our electronic data is stored in the following locations: Pre 2013 - On Ffynnon, SAC monitoring data is stored under the Monitoring folder for each SAC, e.g. Protected Sites - North Region - North West - Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn SAC ( SH55.11.100 ) – Monitoring Within each monitoring folder there will be a folder for each monitoring cycle of each feature, e.g. for the Gwyrfai there are folders for “Luronium natans 99 to 06”, “Luronium natans 07 to 12”, “Lutra lutra 99 to 06”, “Lutra Lutra 07 to 12”, etc.  There will also be a folder called “SAC Monitoring reports” which should contain the final SAC monitoring reports for each feature.  Post 2013 where projects were started once the DMS had been set up. This folder system is recreated on DMS for monitoring undertaken in the last 18 months or so.  It is all stored under the Land Management part of DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landman/SitePages/Home.aspx\tInternal Custodians: North Region: Tom Harrison, Environmental Assessment Officer, NE EAAT. South Region: Karen Wilkinson, Environmental Assessment Officer, SE EAAT South Region: Dan Guest, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, SW EAAT South Region: Alex Harding, Team Leader, SW EAAT Used to be: David Allen, Environmental Monitoring Manager, CCW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1999-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:54.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17782\tNRW_DS110510\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.138Z\t772\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCondition Monitoring of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353130\tThe Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) series has developed since 1949 as the suite of sites providing statutory protection for the best examples of the UK\u0027s flora, fauna, or geological or physiographical features. Originally notified under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949, SSSIs were re-notified under the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981. Improved provisions for the protection and management of SSSIs were introduced by the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000. These sites are also used to underpin other national and international nature conservation designations. The SSSIs designation may extend into intertidal areas out to the jurisdictional limit of local authorities. In Wales, the limit is Mean Low Water for SSSIs notified before 2002, and, for more recent notifications, the limit of Lowest Astronomical Tides (LAT), where the features of interest extend down to LAT.  Condition monitoring of SSSIs is essential to determine whether or not they are in satisfactory condition. This dataset contains the results of a single rapid review exercise and a programme of Common Standards Monitoring carried out by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to assess the condition of SSSI features in Wales.  The data from the rapid review monitoring was collected to provide an indicative picture of the condition of a sample of SSSI features. Common Standards Monitoring data is collected and assessed against a set of agreed criteria to provide an indication of the condition in each case. The first cycle of common standards monitoring took place between 2001 and 2006. The first rapid review exercise was carried out in 2003 and was updated in 2006.\tThe first 6 year cycle of Common Standards Monitoring focussed on Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) and Special Protection Area (SPA) features, however as many of these sites are also SSSIs the data could also be used to assess condition for a number of SSSI features. Some specific SSSI monitoring takes place on a limited number of sites. Rapid review monitoring was undertaken based on an assessment of best available information for features (e.g. survey data, field visits).\t\tHigh level data are held centrally by CCW Environmental Change Group.  Supporting data are held in Regional CCW offices or by Science Groups.  Published reports are available from CCWs libraries.\tInternal Custodian: Dylan Lloyd, Specialist Advisor - Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team  (Formerly: David Allen, Team Leader, Monitoring, Strategy and Planning, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:45.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17790\tNRW_DS110511\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.357Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring and Surveillance of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353131\tA variety of monitoring and surveillance projects are carried out on SSSIs across Wales.  Geographic coverage: SSSIs throughout Wales\t\t\t\tInternal Custodian: Dylan Lloyd, Specialist Advisor - Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17785\tNRW_DS110512\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.231Z\t389\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring and Surveillance of National Nature Reserves (NNRs) in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353132\tNational Nature Reserves (NNRs) contain examples of some of the most important natural and semi-natural terrestrial and coastal ecosystems in Great Britain. They are managed to conserve their habitats or to provide special opportunities for scientific study of the habitats communities and species represented within them. In addition they may be managed to provide public recreation that is compatible with their natural heritage interests. NNRs are declared by the statutory country conservation agencies under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949 and the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981.  This dataset contains the monitoring and surveillance of work carried out on National Nature Reserves (NNRs) in areas such as water quality, water level, water chemistry, evapotranspiration, water chemistry, meteorology, soil temperature, atmospheric chemistry, habitats and species. Examples of particular site monitoring include the Coppicing Surveillance at Coedydd Aber or surveillance of vegetation changes following the removal of grazing from Cwm Idwal  (see \u0027contains\u0027 field for more information).\tThe methods used for monitoring of NNR varies depending on the location and type of data collect. It includes but is not limited to, ad hoc sightings, photo monitoring and fixed quadrats techniques. Data is collected by NRW site wardens, monitoring staff, volunteer recorders and contractors.\t\tIndividual NRW NNR warden offices. Data is fed into Countryside Monitoring System (CMS)  which is held on an external server accessed through a network connection. Contact Tom Hellewell about this.   The reserve staff and wardens hold a lot of the data - and also use a lot of the SAC and SSSI monitoring data to inform their site management plans.  September 2021 update - We still ask the NNR managers to update the data in CMSi. This was last done in November 2019. In the past we’ve tried to get it updated every 2 or 3 years – but looking to extend this to every 5 or 6 years as things really weren’t changing much. CMSi is good for at least another 2 years. There are plans to migrate the data to another system and the provisional plan was to do some development work on AMX to allow for this. However, this didn’t make the cut for this years IT development and no guarantee it will for next year either. Also not sure how this is affected by the AMX monitoring module – which I understand has been put on hold.\tInternal Custodian: Thomas Hellawell, Lead Specialist Advisor – Estates, Estate Planning Team Formerly David Allen, Environmental Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1970-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:26.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17773\tNRW_DS110513\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.657Z\t297\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCondition monitoring of Special Protection Areas (SPAs) in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353133\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPAs) are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive, which came into force in April 1979. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive), and for regularly occurring migratory species. Once sites have been designated, the country agencies work with owners, occupiers and others to secure their management and protection regime. One element of this work was a programme of species monitoring across the site. This is a collation of existing monitoring of bird species across SPAs. This work is often undertaken by contractors or partners such as the British Trust for Ornithology (BTO) and the RSPB. The RSPB have previously collated all the data on behalf of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The first round of assessments occurred between 2001-2006. Second round undertaken by funded partnership post within RSPB for assessment period 2007 - 2013.\tThe condition of features is monitored using site-specific performance indicators. The scope of performance indicators includes species across the terrestrial, freshwater, marine and coastal environments. With this in mind various methods of data capture was used depending on the site and type of feature being assessed.\t\tVarious locations.\tInternal Custodian: Dylan Lloyd, Specialist Advisor - Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team  Matty Murphy  - NRW\u0027s main contact for Bird Data\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:42.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17783\tNRW_DS110514\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.231Z\t530\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring and Surveillance of Special Protection Areas (SPAs) in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353134\tThis dataset contains the results from on going monitoring of Special Protection Areas (SPAs) across Wales. SPAs are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive, which came into force in April 1979. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive), and for regularly occurring migratory species. One of the key responsibilities of the statutory nature conservation agencies, such as Natural Resources Wales (NRW), in the UK is the identification and protection of a series of sites intended to conserve important birds. Article 12 under the EC Birds Directive deals with the general reporting obligations of the Member States and Commission. Every three to four years, Member States of the European Union are required to report on implementation of the Birds Directive. This reporting provides information on: the general implementation of the Directive; and the conservation status of individual species listed under the Annexes of the Directive.\tReporting obligations under the Birds Directive, are set out in Article 12, which requires reports on implementation of the Directive every three to four years. The UK has completed twelve reporting rounds in the three year reporting cycle, covering the period from 1983-2019. NRW feed into this work by commenting on the conservation status of birds related to welsh SPAs. Data is collected by NRW through contracting works and conducting census surveys across key SPAs, whilst collating Wales specific data from various other sources. These sources include sightings from volunteer recorders, British Trust of Ornithology (BTO) Wetland Bird Survey (WeBS) and Seabird Monitoring Programme (SMP) coordinated by JNCC.\t\tContact - Patrick Lindley for information about where NRW store their bird monitoring data.\tMatty Murphy - Maritime Ornithologist/ Marine vertebrate team leader, Natural resources planning/Marine and freshwater ecosytem group Patrick Lindley - Maritime Ornithologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW (Formerly David Allen, Environmental Monitoring Manager, CCW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)-British Trust for Ornithology (BTO)\t\t1983-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:06:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17777\tNRW_DS110515\t2024-05-28T12:31:29.057Z\t250\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLicencing Data for Welsh Assembly Government (WAG) and Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Licensing Authorities.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353135\tThe relevant licensing authority depends on the legal purpose of each licence. For licences for which former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is the licensing authority, data includes electronic or hardcopy licence information e.g. historic archive of licence applications, copies of licences, licence return forms and correspondence, and electronic data such as copies of licences.  Where WAG is the licensing authority consultation with CCW occurs and a tracking of activity is kept on an electronic spreadsheet.\tThis data recorded includes information such as reference number of licence application, dates, name of applicant, details of species, CCW specialist involved in consultation, due date of the licence etc. WAG also provides CCW with copies of licence returns which are kept as hardcopies by CCW\u0027s Operational Support Group (OSG).\t\ta) Archives and Dylan Lloyds many filing cabinets (\u0026gt;10) in OSG b) Tracking of activity spreadsheet is currently stored on CCW\u0027s Species Protection forum. Other electronic data is currently stored on Dylan Lloyds user drive, in copies of Recorder or in ISIS.\tDylan Lloyd, Senior Species Protection Officer, Operational Support Group, NRW\tWelsh Government-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17776\tNRW_DS110516\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.984Z\t561\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBat Licence Return Data (1996 - 2009)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353136\tThis former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), who are now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) dataset is a collation of licence return data for work carried out on bat sites in Wales. The collation was created to add to the picture of bat distributions in Wales, to collect relevant data in one place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available. The dataset does not represent a systematic coverage/survey of Wales. Location of records is generally random, depending on the requirements of the licence holders. Records are provided by licence holders, who have very variable recording ability. The dataset has been subject to some basic validation to eradicate erroneous outlying records and inputting errors.\tLicence return forms are completed by licence holders as a condition of receiving the licence. Licences are issued to organisations and individuals to permit activities which would otherwise be unlawful, such as deliberately collecting, capturing or disturbing scheduled species, or damaging or destroying their roosting sites and hibernacula. Some more recent mammal licence return data has been digitised by Cofnod. Cofnod act as data custodian and hold the top copy of this licence return data, not the data held in Recorder 6 which is managed by NRW.\t\tExcel spreadsheet available from Environmental Information Team, CCW.  Paper licence returns stored with Operational Support Group, CCW Species data is held in Recorder 6: Bat Licence Returns CCW0008000000005.\tSenior Species Protection Officer, Operational Support Group, CCW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various including volunteer recorders\t\t2009-07-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:30:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17770\tNRW_DS110517\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.519Z\t298\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBadger Licence Return Data (1997 - 2008)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353137\tThis dataset consists of former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCWs\u0027) collation of licence return data for work carried out affecting badgers and their setts across Wales.  The purpose of this data capture was to: -  add to the picture of  badger distributions in Wales - collect relevant data in one place, and  - mobilise records which may not otherwise be available.   The dataset does not represent a systematic survey coverage  of Wales, and is instead based upon ad hoc records collected from licence returns. Location of records is generally random, depending on the requirements of the licence holders.  Records are provided by licence holders, who have very variable recording ability. The dataset has been subject to some basic validation to eradicate erroneous outlying records and inputting errors.\tLicence return forms are completed by licence holders as a condition of receiving the licence. Licences are issued to organisations and individuals to permit activities which would otherwise be unlawful, such as deliberately collecting, capturing or disturbing scheduled species, or damaging or destroying their setts. Licences are generally issued for the purposes of survey, monitoring and research, or for building and other development work. This data has been packaged with other species licence return data on Recorder 6 and is available to download via the NBN Gateway (see Parent ID). Some more recent mammal licence return data has been digitised by Cofnod. Cofnod act as data custodian and hold the top copy of this licence return data, not the data held in Recorder 6 which is managed by NRW.\t\tExcel spreadsheet available from Environmental Information Team, NRW.  Paper licence returns stored with Operational Support Group, NRW. Some data is stored in Recorder 6\tSenior Species Protection Officer, Operational Support Group, NRW (Adam Cole-King)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various including volunteer recorders\t\t2008-08-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:43.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17775\tNRW_DS110518\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.614Z\t315\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOtter Licence Return Data (1905 - 2008).\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353138\tThis dataset is a former Countryside Council for Wales collation of licence return data for work carried out affecting otters in Wales.The collation was created to add to the picture of otter distributions in Wales, to collect relevant data in one place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available.\tLicence return forms are completed by licence holders as a condition of receiving the licence. Licences are issued to organisations and individuals to permit activities which would otherwise be unlawful, such as deliberately collecting, capturing or disturbing scheduled species, or damaging or destroying their breeding sites and resting places. Licences are generally issued for the purposes of survey, monitoring and research, or for building and other development work. This data has been packaged with other species licence return data on Recorder 6 and is available to download via the NBN Gateway (see Parent ID). Some more recent mammal licence return data has been digitised by Cofnod. Cofnod act as data custodian and hold the top copy of this licence return data, not the data held in Recorder 6 which is managed by NRW.\t\tExcel spreadsheet available from Environmental Information Team, CCW. Paper licence returns stored with Operational Support Group, CCW.  Species data held in Recorder 6: Otter Licence Returns CCW0008000000007\tInternal Custodian, Senior Species Protection Officer, Operational Support Group, CCW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tVarious including volunteer recorders\t\t2008-03-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:18:00.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17771\tNRW_DS110519\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.510Z\t633\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWild Bird Licence Return Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353139\tA collation of licence return data for bird species listed under the EC Directive on the conservation of wild birds and schedule 1 of the Wildlife and Countryside Act. Excludes ringing licences, which are handled by British Trust for Ornithology (BTO). The collation was created to add to the picture of wild birds distributions in Wales, to collect relevant data in one place, and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available.\tLicence return forms are completed by licence holders as a condition of receiving the licence. Licences are issued to organisations and individuals to permit activities which would otherwise be unlawful, such as deliberately collecting, capturing or disturbing scheduled species, or damaging or destroying their nesting sites. Licences are generally issued for the purposes of survey, monitoring and research, or for building and other development work. Records are provided by licence holders, who have very variable recording ability. The RSPB staff responsible for inputting the data have some knowledge and expertise, and are able to weed out any obviously erroneous data. Licences are only issued to surveyors who can demonstrate, through provision of references with their licence application, that they have experience of surveying the relevant bird species.\t\tData is currently held and custodianship rests with the RSPB\tDylan Lloyd, Environmental Surveillance Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW (Formerly Sian Whitehead, Senior Ornithologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various including volunteer recorders\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17769\tNRW_DS110520\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.310Z\t416\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOther Protected Animals Licence Return Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353230\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) collation of licence return data for work carried out in Wales affecting protected animal species. This collation includes invertebrates such as, but not limited to, marsh fritillary, crayfish and fairy shrimp. The collation was created to add to the picture of protected animals distributions in Wales, to collect relevant data in one place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available. The dataset does not represent a systematic coverage/survey of Wales. Location of records is generally random, depending on the requirements of the licence holders.\tLicence return forms are completed by licence holders as a condition of receiving the licence. Licences are issued to organisations and individuals to permit activities which would otherwise be unlawful, such as deliberately collecting, capturing or disturbing scheduled species, or damaging or destroying their breeding sites. Licences are generally issued for the purposes of survey, monitoring and research, or for building and other development work.\t\tPaper licence returns stored with Operational Support Group, CCW\tDylan Lloyd, Environmental Surveillance Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW\tVarious including volunteer recorders-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:32:23.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17774\tNRW_DS110521\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.656Z\t275\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPlant Licence Return Data (2003 - 2006)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130353231\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW) collation of licence return data for work carried out in Wales affecting protected plant species. The collation was created to add to the picture of protected plant distributions in Wales, to collect relevant data in one place and to mobilise records which may not otherwise be available.\tLicence return forms are completed by licence holders as a condition of receiving the licence. Licences are issued to organisations and individuals to permit activities which would otherwise be unlawful, such as deliberately collecting, capturing or disturbing scheduled species, or damaging or destroying their habitat. Licences are generally issued for the purposes of survey, monitoring and research, or for building and other development work. The dataset does not represent a systematic coverage/survey of Wales. Location of records is generally random, depending on the requirements of the licence holders. This data has been packaged with other species licence return data on Recorder 6 and is available to download via the NBN Gateway (see Parent ID).\t\tPaper licence returns stored with Operational Support Group and Andy Jones, CCW.   Species data held on Recorder 6 Plant Licence Returns CCW0008000000003.\tSenior Species Officer, Casework Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various including volunteer recorders\t\t2006-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17772\tNRW_DS110688\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.649Z\t522\tValid\tEditable\tCountryside Council for Wales\u0027 Marine Monitoring Sample Stations for UKDMOS\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130363838\tThe exact location of sampling stations that occur at each of the Marine Monitoring Sites.  The purpose of this data capture is to provide an overview of legacy Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) Marine Monitoring sample stations to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and make these available to a wider audience through a central location.  This will help facilitate a more coordinated approach to Marine Monitoring across the UK and promote the sharing of resources.   Sample stations for a variety of monitoring programmes are available including: Benthic rock and sediment, intertidal rock and sediment, saline lagoons, scoter aerial monitoring, bottlenose dolphins, grey seals, shellfish (bird food) stocks, video and acoustic monitoring. At some locations this is the same as the Marine Monitoring Site location as listed in the separate metadata entry (Marine Monitoring Sites - GIS Dataset) for others it may be a series of samples stations within the sites and for others it may be a larger area that is surveyed remotely.    The Marine Monitoring Sample locations are available online through UK Directory of Marine Observing Systems (UKDMOS).  Included are the following legacy CCW Marine Monitoring programmes;   - Intertidal Monitoring Surveys  - Benthic Subtidal Sediment Monitoring Survey  - Aerial scoter monitoring  - Acoustic Seabed Monitoring of the North Llyn Modiolus reef  - Benthic Rock Monitoring  - Bottlenose Dolphin Monitoring  - Shellfish Stock (bird food) Monitoring  - Skomer Marine Nature Reserve Monitoring  - Across Wales drop-down video monitoring   - Saline lagoon soft sediment survey  - Grey seal monitoring\tAll sample stations have been plotted from original waypoints recorded on survey, extracted from Marine Monitoring reports, extracted from Marine Recorder or created specifically for this project.    This dataset is partially based on the legacy CCW Marine Monitoring Sites dataset.\t\tThis data is archived under Project No. 230, Media No. 599. Raw data provided to BODC for upload to UKDMOS. This data can be accessed via the UKDMOS website.  http://www.ukdmos.org/welcome.asp.\tInternal Custodian - Marine Monitoring Data Officer, MFSG.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\thttp://ukdmos.org/search-directory\t2025-12-01T13:57:09.866Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17768\tNRW_DS110689\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.190Z\t322\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of Yellow Trumpet Anemone Parazoanthus axinellae at Pen Cristen, Bardsey Island\tMonitro\u0027r anemoni clystyrog melyn Parazoanthus axinellae ar Ynys Enlli\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130363839\tYellow trumpet anemone Parazoanthus axinellae is primarily a south-western species, found at Bardsey Island and is close to the northern boundary of its known distribution at this location.  This species has been selected as an attribute for monitoring the condition of reefs in the Special Area of Conservation (SACs).  In 1999 an assessment of appropriate methods was made to look at monitoring the abundance and population structure of Parazoanthus axinellae and the erect branching sponge Axinella dissimilis. The trials focused on comparing different techniques to find the one that was most repeatable and therefore sensitive to population change.  Trials took place at Pen Cristen, on Bardsey Island.  Subsequent monitoring of the Parazoanthus axinellae assemblage has occurred annually at this location when weather conditions allow. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor the numerical abundance, percentage cover and distribution of individuals within the dominant Parazoanthus axinellae assemblage using a quantitative photographic approach.  This will contribute to condition assessment of the reef feature in the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC.\tThe initial data was collected as part of the LIFE monitoring trials. Initial trials compared recording of quadrats by video and by still photography at one station at Pen Cristen.  It was concluded that using still photography produced better, more repeatable results.  Further details can be found in Marine Monitoring Report 2.The following standard procedure is now followed for data capture at Pen Cristen. 3 permanent stations have been set up, Notch face, Wrinkle face and Long face.  These are marked with ring bolts around the perimeter.  Divers string tape measures between the bolts in order to position a quadrat along the edge of the tape measure.  A picture is taken of the sessile community within the quadrat.  The quadrat is then moved to a position along the tape that overlaps the first quadrat by 100mm.  The process is repeated to form a continuous photographic \u0027belt\u0027.  Further details can be found in Marine Monitoring report 25b, SOP 06. Images from each quadrat are analysed and the area covered by Parazoanthus recorded along with the number of polyps.\t\tElectronic data (including maipulated images) is archived under project 219, media no. 800.  Copies of the video data is held on the Imaging drive, the tapes are held in the fireproof safe.  Images are held on the imaging computer and a copy on the MFSG drive.\tMarine Monitoring Officer, MFSG. Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:44:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17767\tNRW_DS110872\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.130Z\t598\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcoustic Monitoring of South Llyn (East of the Tudwels) and Subsequent Infauna Sampling\tGwaith monitro acwstig yn Ne Llyn (i\u0027r dwyrain o Ynys Tudwal) a gwaith dilynol ar samplo\u0027r isfilod\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383732\tA side-scan sonar survey was carried out in Tremadog Bay following reports of possible scallop dredging within the Sea Fisheries Scallop Exclusion Zone.  The acoustic survey was followed up by infaunal sampling to look at the effect of disturbance of the substrata within the area. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate reports of possible scallop dredging within the Exclusion Zone and its effects on biodiversity within the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Exclusion Zone.  Confidence in this dataset is high. The side-scan data is processed to reduce errors (e.g. positional corrections).\tSide scan-sonar data was captured from Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) research vessel (R.V. Pedryn) using a CMAX CM800 side-scan sonar. Two areas were selected for infaunal sampling.  A control site (with no dredge activity) and one area where there was evidence of recent dredge activity.  Infaunal sampling was carried by divers using a 0.1 m2 quadrat. 5 Quadrats were randomly placed in the belly of a dredge scar and 5 within the control site.  Surface material was collected by hand and a diver operated suction sampler was used to remove the sediment within the quadrat to a depth of 20cms using a 0.5mm filter mesh bag.  Large cobbles within the sediment too big for sampler were removed by hand and placed in a large 0.5mm mesh sample bag along with the plastic bag containing surface cobbles and the suction sampler filter bag.  A sediment sample was collected by the diver adjacent to the quadrat using a 1/2litre sealed plastic bucket for granulometry.  The contents of the suction sample filter bag and cobbles from within the sediment were transferred to sample buckets on the surface and fixed in 20% formalin diluted with seawater.  In addition one 2x2 m quadrat was photographed in 40 cm blocks at both the control site and within one dredge scar.  Photos will be stitched together to create a photographic mosaic of the quadrat.\t\t[A]  Electronic data is also archived under project 236, media no 802: (K:\\archive\\Media 801-900\\Media 802)  Copies of edited material are also archived under media 802. [B]  A working copy of data is held on  S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Sidescan\\tudwals 2009.   [C]  Images are held on DVD19 and videos are on the I drive.  [D]  Samples collected were lost: samples that got lost by the couriers (citylink)  then found and destroyed by them. which could be why [E] has not been completed [E]  Species data to be supplied as spreadsheet and entered to Marine Recorder\tInternal Custodian: Charlie Lindenbaum, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer,  Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team    Metadata Contact: Data Services Team       18/01/2016 - HR has contacted Matt Green and Charlie Lindenbaum about the location of the infaunal sampling data as it currently states that this data is held by Hebog. Once located this data should be entered into Marine Recorder, or at least be added to the import list. Reply stated that the samples were lost in the post.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:45:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17766\tNRW_DS110885\t2024-05-28T12:31:28.095Z\t564\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring Survey of the Effect of Parys Mountain Discharge around Amlwch, North Anglesey (2003)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383835\tThe purpose of this data capture was to detect impacts of pumping discharge from the disused copper mines in Parys Mountain.  Intertidal surveys conducted around Amlwch, North Anglesey, during the early part of 2003 to detect impacts of pumping discharge from the disused copper mines in Parys Mountain into the Afon Goch (North) that flows into the sea underneath the Great Lakes plant in Amlwch. In addition there is a undergraduate project that looked at concentrations of copper in Mytilus edulis samples. These projects are both unpublished. The purpose of this data capture was to detect  impacts of pumping discharge from the disused copper mines in Parys Mountain.\tSurveys were carried out on 3 dates prior to discharge of water from the Parys Mountain and again in June following discharge from the mine. Two types of survey were carried out: - Quadrat surveys to record presence and density of mollusc species. 60 randomly placed quadrats of 0.25m2 were recorded at 2 sites from the mid eulittoral zone. Counts of individual species of molluscs were recorded from within each quadrat. - Intertidal species recording mid and lower shores were undertaken in accordance with standard Marine Nature Conservation Review Phase 2 surveying methods (Davies et al, 2001) at the seven sites.  In addition samples of limpets Patella vulgata, mussels, Mytilus edulis and seaweeds, Ascophyllum nodosum, Fucus serratus and Mastocarpus stellatus, from all of the survey sites have been collected for tissue samples that will be analysed for copper concentration before and after discharge. Historical survey of the area conducted in the mid 1970\u0027s.\tSubtidal Team Leader, NRW.\t[A]  This project is archived under project no. 253, media no 907 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 907) [B]  Survey data held in Marine recorder survey: MRCCW3000000002 [C]  Undergraduate project on copper concentrations in mussels available from Kirsten Ramsay (on paper, now send to off sire storage) [D] Paper MNCR forms in off-site storage (Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Contact: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-06-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:02:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17818\tNRW_DS110886\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.140Z\t689\tValid\tEditable\tEpiflora Diversity Within Eelgrass (Zostera marina) Beds on the Welsh Coast\tAmrywiaeth yr arlysiau yn y gwelyau gwellt y gamlas (Zostera marina) ar hyd arfordir Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383836\tSeagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas, such as lagoons and channels, which are sheltered from significant wave action. Eelgrass (Zostera marina), is considered to be scarce (present in 16-100 ten km squares) is protected through the EC Habitats Directive and is classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitat. Zostera beds is also on the OSPAR List of threatened and/or declining species and habitats. The purpose of this data capture was to monitor Zostera marine habitat and condition, and this dataset is a collation results of a survey investigating the epifloral diversity within eelgrass beds around the coast of Wales.\tZostera marina was collected at four sites over a four week period in July and August 2002.  At each site five stations were sampled.  At each station two divers were deployed from a small boat onto a random point within the Zostera bed, which was typically at 2-3 meters depth.  From this point a tape measure was extended in a randomly selected compass direction.  At five set randomly generated distances from the point a 30 by 30 cm quadrat was placed on the substratum.  The depth was recorded and samples of Zostera were collected and taken back to the laboratory for analysis.  The samples were fixed in 1-5% formalin to prevent deterioration.  In the laboratory 10 leaves from each sample were examined for epiflora.\t\tHeld as a Marine Recorder survey: MRCCW30000000036\tCustodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:35:41.225Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17812\tNRW_DS110890\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.909Z\t566\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring the Dynamics of the North Llyn Modiolus Reef\tMonitro dynameg Riff Modiolus Gogledd Llyn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383930\tModiolus Reefs are a feature of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Information is gathered about the dynamics of the reef through the recording of spat/larval settlement.  This is used as an indicator of current/recent influences on the reef and can provide an early warning of potential problems. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the dynamics of the reef. This contributes to reporting of favourable conservation status of the SAC feature. Data will be used in conjunction with acoustic methods which look at the extent of the feature (reef) and photo quadrat methods that look at the health of the feature.\tPost-settlement juvenile \u0027spat\u0027 collectors were attached to the permanent monitoring station at Modiolus site 1.  3 kitchen scouring pads are attached by cable ties onto the pyramid marker at the site. The pads are recovered annually and placed in sealed plastic bags to present loss of juvenile mussels on the ascent back to the surface. Once back in the laboratory the spat collectors were fixed / stored in 4% formalin buffered with seawater.  Upon analysis the spat collector pads were separated into \u0027inner\u0027 and \u0027outer\u0027 faces for settlement and then systematically scanned using a dissecting binocular microscope\t\tAll data is archived under project no.218, media no. 584. Working copies of the data is held on shared MFSG drive\tInternal custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG.  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:46:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17816\tNRW_DS110891\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.943Z\t257\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBritannia Bridge Mussel Survey (1997)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383931\tThe purpose of this data capture was to provide information and data towards the casework requirement relating to the sublittoral mussel bed. Surveys took place around Britannia Bridge in the Menai Straits.\tMethods are core sampling and dive survey using SeaSearch methods and Phase 2 methods.\t\tMarine Recorder survey: MRCCW3000000002F\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-08-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:32:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17824\tNRW_DS110893\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.294Z\t331\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRoxAnn Ground Truthing Video Drops 1997\tGwirio Data Fideo yn y Maes RoxAnn 1997\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383933\tThe purpose of this data capture was to increase the available information of species and habitats, thus extending the area covered by survey within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Results of a survey carried out from the Prince Madog Research Vessel as a joint operation by the University of Wales, Bangor and Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). Using RoxAnn, ground truthing video transects within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC were recorded.\tVideo equipment was towed behind the Prince Madog recording species and limited substratum along each transect. The nature of the video drops made it difficult to accurately record species abundances or substratum percentages. Abundances for species have been assigned from the description given on the video recording forms: S\u003d Any noted as superabundant; A\u003d Any noted as abundant; C\u003d Lots, many patches; F\u003d Quite a lot, patches; O\u003d Sparse, few, sparse patches; R\u003d Very sparse, very few [SACFOR]. Substratum is detailed in the habitat description.\t\tMarine Recorder Survey: JNCCMNCR40000960.  Original MNCR forms are held in CCW off-site storage. Data is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 237, Media No\u0027s. 1, 75 and 109\tInternal Custodian: Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:47:08.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17809\tNRW_DS110894\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.939Z\t420\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Sites: Data Collation\tSafleoedd Morol: Casglu Data\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130383934\tBetween October 2001 and June 2002, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) contracted Sea-Scope to source and collate any Welsh marine biological data which were not in CCW\u0027s possession. The main aim of the Project was to collate extant data, enabling them to be accessible to CCW data management systems and linking to other data collation projects nationally. Priority was to be given to data acquired from Special Areas of Conservation (SACs), particularly those relating to SAC moderation features. A higher priority was to be given to sourcing datasets from areas which had limited representation on the MNCR database, in terms of data already held. Also, datasets collected before 1980, with restricted phyla, or with suspected inaccuracies in species identification were to be given a lower priority.\tData was sought from the following categories- intertidal flora and fauna records, benthic flora, invertebrates and fish, pelagic fish and invertebrates, plankton, cetaceans, seals and environmental data e.g. water temp. Sources of data included universities, museums, wildlife trusts, local authorities, environment agency etc. The datasets acquired were usually in paper form e.g. post grad research reports and were subsequently entered into marine recorder. Work was divided into two parts. Part 1 involved preparing an inventory of all major extant sources of data within Wales that were not held by CCW. Part 2 involved collating the data identified by Part 1 and prioritised accordingly into electronic format- data entry onto Marine Recorder database.\tThe following organisations/individuals were contacted: Universities, Museums, Sea Fisheries Committees, CEFAS, Countryside Council for Wales, English Nature, Joint Nature Conservation Committee, Environment Agency, Local Authorities, Marine SAC Officers, MarLIN, Coastal Wildlife Trusts, other nature conservation NGOs, Water authorities, Environmental consultancies and others. Most datasets acquired were in the form of paper reports (internal research, commissioned research or post-graduate theses), with very little data being available in an electronic format.\ta) archived as Project 27, media no 81 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 81)   b) Marine Recorder - SURVEY NUMBERS NOT RECORDED IN METADATA\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:48:03.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17822\tNRW_DS110926\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.141Z\t415\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of Carbonate Reef Extent and Fish Population at Holden\u0027s Reef (2003 - 2010)\tMonitro poblogaeth y pysgod a maint y riff carbonad yn Riff Holden\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393236\tHolden\u0027s Reef is a small (approximately 40 m diameter), roughly circular reef situated in the northern sector of Cardigan Bay. It is unusual in being formed as a result of a chemical reaction between seawater and the escape of natural gas from the seabed, a phenomenon generally found in much deeper waters.  Holden\u0027s Reef is one of an assemblage of methanogen-precipitated calcium carbonate reefs and was the first of the group to be discovered. This dataset contains the results of a programme of monitoring established to study fish populations and reef extent. It also includes data from an in-house survey carried out at Holden\u0027s Reef in 2003, initiated following the reef\u0027s discovery, and Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) phase 2 surveys carried out in 2005 of \\Little\\\" and \\\"Big\\\" Reef.  Holden\u0027s Reef is monitored for fish abundance and diversity extent and rugosity (the link between rugosity and fish abundance has been seen in several studies). This will contribute to condition assessment of the reef feature in the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC).  Holden\u0027s Reef is situated more than 10 km off shore north west of Barmouth and is approximately 40 m in diameter.  \"\tA site marker (a single road pin) has been established at the centre of the reef in its shallowest part.  An additional marker is located due north from the centre at the edge of the reef. The extent of the reef is measured by divers who swim along a bearing from the central marker to the edge of the reef and the distance recorded.  The distance from the centre to the edge of the reef is measured at 12 bearings 30 degrees apart.  The rugosity of the reef is measured by splitting the reef into an inner and outer zone radiating from the central marker.  The distance between the centre and zone boundary is measured using a taut tape measure.  This distance is then repeated allowing the tape measure to rest along the contours if the reef.  The rugosity is determined to be the ratio between the two measurements.  Fish abundance and diversity is measured by a pair of divers undertaking 3 consecutive timed counts of 5 minute duration.  These counts take place in up to 4 zones that represent different topography and depths on the reef.  To aid recording slates are pre-marked with species likely to be observed.\tDetailed methodologies in use at Holden\u0027s Reef can be found in Marine Monitoring Report 25b (standard operating procedures 11 12 and 13)\t[A]  Data is archived under project 222, media no. 829.  [B]  Copies of the video data is held on the Imaging drive, the tapes are held in the fireproof safe.  [C[  Images are held on the imaging computer and a copy on the MFSG drive.   [D]  A working file is held on the MFSG drive.\tInternal Custodian: Mike Camplin - Marine Monitoring Team, NRW  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:33:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17814\tNRW_DS110931\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.895Z\t239\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDatasets from the South-West Britain Sublittoral Survey\tSetiau data o Arolwg Isarforol De-Orllewin Prydain\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393331\tThe South-west Britain Sublittoral Survey was a three-year project carried out between 1976 and 1979 by the Field Studies Council for the Nature Conservancy Council. Former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) which is now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), holds the data from a selection of surveys that make up the project .These are listed under Alternative Title which also includes: - 1978 SWBSS Gower sublittoral survey (JNCCMNCR10000069) - 1978 SWBSS Skokholm survey (JNCCMNCR10000076) The purpose of this data capture was to provide information on the nearshore sublittoral ecosystems present from Pembrokeshire around to Dorest and to assess the conservation interest of these sites. The area included in the survey is the old county of Pembrokeshire, the south coast of Wales, and the coast of Avon, Somerset, Devon, Cornwall and Dorset.\tThe project consisted of an initial literature review followed by three years of fieldwork, during which around 740 km of coastline at thirteen different sites were surveyed. Sites were selected by inspection of maps and charts to include locations exposed to different environmental conditions.  A recording card was developed for use throughout the survey enabling systematic recording of habitat features. A checklist of conspicuous species was also used along with an abundance scale developed for recording the quantity of species in each separate habitat.\t1977-79 Hiscock PMSA- West Pembrokeshire SWBSS sublittoral survey (MRCCW10000000004) contributed to the Pembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas, see separate metadata entry.\tCCW\u0027s copy of marine recorder (see survey titles and keys in \u0027abstract\u0027) .Paper reports are available from CCW library (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC)\t\t1981-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:49:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17813\tNRW_DS110947\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.891Z\t557\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of Algal Diversity and Richness on the Glacial Moraine of Sarn Badrig\tMonitro cyfoeth ac amrywiaeth algau ar farian rhewlifol Sarn Badrig.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393437\tSarn Badrig (St Patrick\u0027s Causeway) is a belt reef running in a south-westerly direction approximately 18 km out from Mochras Point near Harlech. This reef forms part of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) protected under the EC Habitats Directive. The reef is composed of glacial moraine deposits and is exposed in patches at low water along most of its length.  The reef structure is very mobile and subject to tidal currents, wave action and sand scour and therefore many of the seaweeds found on the reef are annual or perennial species able to survive the scoured conditions.  These include a number of rare species that would be overgrown by species that would normally establish on more stable reef environment. The purpose of this data capture was to map the distribution of substratum types and select biological indices.  Also to assess and determine a suitable methodology to monitor algal richness within a benthic habitat of dynamic substrata.  The site will only be assessed for its algae richness and diversity every 2 years.\tAlgae diversity and richness monitoring at Sarn Badrig is conducted in 2 stages.  Stage 1, habitat mapping and stage 2, algal richness.  The site is marked with a permanent tripod structure and provides a reference point for mapping the surrounding substrata. An area 25 m x 25 m is marked out North East of the tripod marker and at 5 m intervals a quadrat is used to record the substratum (percentage cover of sand, gravel, pebbles and boulder) and biota features (percentage cover of algal turf, presence/absenceof H.siliquosa and Laminaria spp).  Also recorded is how representative the area is of the habitat MIR.HalXK. This process is repeated North West, South West and South East of the marker to form a 50 m x 50 m area with the marker in the centre.  A total of 100 quadrats are completed.  The area that is most representative of the habitat MIR.HalXK is selected for monitoring algal richness.  Its distance and bearing from the pyramid is recorded.  Due to the difficulty of identifying algae species in-situ within a limited time period, different methods of measuring species richness were investigated in 2005.  In 2007, 2 divers each used a 0.25 m quadrat to record algal species, in 10 min intervals up to one hour. The quadrat is used as a tool to focus the search and is moved around the sector by the divers.  Detailed methodologies can be found in Marine Monitoring Report 25b (standard operating procedures 7 and 8 for the 2005 methods). Established as Life Monitoring Trials - CCW report 380, chapter 3.1.1 Contract Science Report 380, followed by contracted Across Wales diving project. Whilst most species were identified in situ, some were gathered for subsequent laboratory analysis using algal identification keys and microscope. Samples of algae were pressed, scanned on a flat-bed colour scanner and incorporated into the CCW herbarium.\t\tAll electronic data is archived under project 221, media no. 587.   Specimens are held in the Marine Monitoring Team Herbarium and scanned versions of these are held on the MFSG drive.   Incidental video is stored on the imaging machine.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-08-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:50:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17808\tNRW_DS110954\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.927Z\t531\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLowland Grassland Areas of Search Re-visit Files\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393534\tThe phase 2 lowland grassland survey of Wales came to an end in 2004; since then various lowland grassland sites within the 12 Welsh Areas of Search have been re-visited on an ad hoc basis. This dataset contains the notes from the re-visits including details of any ecological changes to have taken place since the original survey. The purpose of which was to provide a record of ecological change. Some of the revisit file notes also include information on management and ownership history, records of uncommon species present and redrafted vegetation maps. The dataset includes the photographs taken during the re-visits.\tData collected through re-visiting lowland grassland survey sites.\t\tStored on the restricted access GRASSURV directory on the N:drive of Terrestrial Science Group. H:\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV H:\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV\\PHASE II PHOTO AND IMAGE ARCHIVE\\Areas of Search\tStuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17807\tNRW_DS110956\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.765Z\t353\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales: Species Datasets\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393536\tA detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation. Community definition follows the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). At each site, homogeneous stands of vegetation were identified, sampled with 2 x 2 m quadrats and mapped onto Ordnance Survey base maps or aerial photographs at 1:5000 scale.  This dataset has all Wales coverage with sites varying in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha. The survey was started by Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) and with Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and is now managed by Natural Resources Wales. This dataset contains all the floristic quadrat data and site information from the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales.  The dataset has been extensively validated and verified.  This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway.\tSurveys were conducted on all forms of semi-natural neutral, acidic and calcareous grassland in lowland Wales, as well as wetter Molinia (purple-moor grass) and Juncus (rush) pasture communities and associated soligenous mires. The data was captured in Recorder 6 to keep the data in a more stable environment to secure it for future use, to enable data analysis and to ease the data exchange process.\tAlso See: 116805 Species Data on the NBN Atlas\tThis dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  Biological Species Data is also held in Recorder 6. LGSW Brecknock CCW0002600000009    LGSW Carmarthen \u0026amp; Dinefwr CCW0002600000008    LGSW Ceredigion CCW0002600000005    LGSW Clwyd CCW0002600000003    LGSW East Gwynedd CCW0002600000002    LGSW Gwent CCW0002600000012    LGSW Mid \u0026amp; South Glamorgan CCW0002600000011    LGSW Montgomery CCW0002600000004    LGSW Preseli \u0026amp; South Pembroke CCW0002600000007    LGSW Radnor CCW0002600000006    LGSW West Glamorgan \u0026amp; Llanelli CCW0002600000010    LGSW West Gwynedd CCW0002600000001\tCustodians: Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW.  Data Manager, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW. Excel spreadsheets inputted into Recorder 6 by contractors (Charles Copp and Mike Weideli).  The restricted access GRASSURV directory on the N:drive of Terrestrial Science Group.    Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:15.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17823\tNRW_DS110957\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.088Z\t301\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSublittoral Surveys of Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) Formally a Marine Nature Reserve ( MNR ) Boundaries 1982\tArolygon Isarforol o Ffiniau Gwarchodfa Natur Forol Sgomer 1982\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393537\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. This dataset consists of the results of a survey carried out to describe the types of sublittoral habitats and communities adjacent to the coast in the region of the eastern boundaries of the Skomer Marine Nature Reserve. The purpose of this data capture was provide a basis for assessing the adequacy of the eastern boundaries in relation to sublittoral areas and their relevance in terms of the presence or absence of unusual communities or communities not well represented within other regions of the reserve.\tBroad descriptions of habitats and communities were made based on the recording card developed for the South-West Britain Sublittoral Survey.  The results contributed to the Pembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas (PMSA) - see separate metadata entry and Marine Recorder survey \u00271982 Hiscock PMSA -Skomer MNR boundary sublittoral survey\u0027 (MRCCW10000000061).\t\tPaper report available in NRW\u0027s library collection, see \u0027related titles\u0027 above. Data is stored within JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder as JNCCMNCR10000184. NRW only hold a snapshot of this data.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW External Contact; JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1983-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:50:52.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17815\tNRW_DS110958\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.924Z\t544\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLittoral Survey of Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) Formally a Marine Nature Reserve ( MNR ) 1982\tArolwg Arfordirol o Warchodfa Natur Forol Sgomer 1982\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393538\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. The results of a survey aimed at describing the major littoral habitats and selected special sites, together with their communities, found around Skomer Island and the Marloes peninsula. The purpose of this data capture was to provide information on the range and distribution of littoral habitats and communities to aid in the drafting of the management plan necessary for the establishment of a Statutory Marine Nature Reserve.\t22 sites were selected for survey to be representative of as wide a range of habitats as possible, including bedrock and boulder shores, water surge areas and caves. A site description was noted, species lists were collected and a photographic record of the sites and communities was also made.\tIntertidal Ecologist, NRW JNCC\tPaper report available in NRW\u0027s library collection at Maes-y-Fynnon, see \u0027related titles\u0027 above. Data is stored in JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder as - JNCCMNCR10000160. NRW only hold a snapshot of this data.\tInternal Custodian: Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team External Custodian: JNCC  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1983-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:51:32.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17819\tNRW_DS110960\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.998Z\t360\tValid\tEditable\tSublittoral Survey of Bardsey and the Llyn Peninsula 1983\tArolwg Isarforol o Enlli a Phen Ll\u0027n 1983\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393630\tBardsey Island and the adjacent coast of the Llyn Peninsula was one of seven areas proposed by the Nature Conservancy Council for establishment as a statutory marine nature reserve. This dataset contains the results of a survey carried out in 1983 to describe the range of sublittoral habitats and communities present in nearshore sublittoral areas of Bardsey and the Llyn Peninsula. The purpose of this data capture was to survey and describe the range of habitats and communities present in nearshore sublittoral areas and to assess the scientific interest and make recommendations on the conservation importance of the areas surveyed.\tSurveys were carried out using diving, and access to sites was by inflatable boats. At each site surveyed, a botanist and a zoologist recorded the abundance of conspicuous species in the main habitats encountered. A description of the habitats was made and transcribed on to edge-punched cards. Photographs were also taken to illustrate communities and species present.\t\tPaper report available in NRW\u0027s library collection, see \u0027related titles\u0027 above. Data is stored within JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder as JNCCMNCR10000186. NRW only holds a snapshot of this data - contained in All Wales snapshot.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1984-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:34:50.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17811\tNRW_DS110962\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.866Z\t282\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLittoral Survey of Bardsey and the Llyn Peninsula 1983\tArolwg Arfordirol o Enlli a Phen Ll\u0027n 1983\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313130393632\tBardsey Island and the adjacent coast of the Llyn Peninsula was one of seven area proposed by the Nature Conservancy Council for establishment as a statutory marine nature reserve. The purpose of data capture was to describe the range of habitats, communities and species present on littoral rock within the proposed marine nature reserve and to assess the scientific interest and conservation importance of the areas visited. This dataset contains the results of a survey carried out in 1983 to describe the range of habitats, communities and species present on littoral rock on Bardsey and the Llyn Peninsula.\t25 sites were selected from the maps and charts to ensure that as wide a range of habitats as possible was surveyed in the time available. Systematic surveys were carried out on open-shore communities and records of the abundance of conspicuous species were obtained from the main subzones on the shore. These results enabled the classification of different shore types based on communities present and topographical features. Particular attention was given to the description of other habitats such as rockpools, boulders and caves. Colour transparency photographs were taken of the shores and species present.\t\tPaper report available in NRW\u0027s library collection, see \u0027related titles\u0027 above. Data is stored in JNCC\u0027s copy of  Marine Recorder as - JNCCMNCR10000205\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, NRW External Custodian: JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1984-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T09:54:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17821\tNRW_DS111009\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.122Z\t223\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Biological Survey of Bardsey Island 1977\tArolwg Bioleg Mor o Ynys Enlli 1977\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131303039\tThe results of a survey of the littoral and sub-littoral regions around Bardsey Island carried out in 1977. Bardsey Island was being offered for sale in 1977 therefore this survey was initiated in order to gain knowledge of the littoral and sub-littoral surroundings of the island in case public access to the areas was denied by subsequent owners. The data captured was also used to detect any changes since previous surveys and to investigate whether the shores of Bardsey offered a suitable site for a Marine Nature Reserve.\tLittoral survey- Intensive shore searches at specific sites and collections made along as much of the shoreline as possible to gain a general picture of the floral and faunal distribution. A subjective abundance scale was used to give general relative impression of species density by classifying organisms as \u0027abundant\u0027, \u0027common\u0027, \u0027occasional\u0027 and \u0027rare\u0027. Sub-littoral survey- Before diving, potential sites were surveyed from a dinghy fitted with an inshore echosounder to find sites with a large depth range and varying topography. The positions of sites were fixed and the survey techniques used included- transect lines laid in order to investigate distribution of the prominent species in relation to depth, spot dives of 20 minutes searching time to cover as much of the coast as possible, and a towed underwater sledge to make a preliminary survey of a chosen swim line.\t\tData held within JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000228) Paper report available from CCW\u0027s library (see \u0027related titles\u0027 section above)\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist, NRW External Contact: JNCC  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1982-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:41:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17810\tNRW_DS111011\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.861Z\t343\tValid\tEditable\tMonitoring of Limestone Communities and Sponge Morphology at Ynys Moelfre\tMonitro cymunedau calchfaen a morffoleg sbyngau ar Ynys Moelfre\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131303131\tSponges are considered an important component of benthic communities in temperate waters and the community composition of subtidal limestone biotopes has been identified as a priority feature for the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC).   Monitoring sponge morphology diversity is used as a qualitative estimate of sponge species diversity and changes in morphological composition of sponge assemblages may also provide an indication of changes in environmental conditions.    The purpose of this data capture was to:  -  Identify community composition of subtidal limestone biotopes and their favourable conservation status.   -  Establish a baseline for the community and monitor changes in the diversity of subtidal sponge morphologies.    Changes in sponge species composition may indicate a change within the sessile community as a whole.\tYnys Moelfre, East Anglesey is a monitoring station that lies on the NE side of the Isle of Anglesey on a steep rock and boulder wall.  14 fixed monitoring quadrats (0.25m2) in two rows have been established at Ynys Moelfre.  Divers record in-situ the sponge morphologies from the quadrat on a cell frequency basis.  Sponges have been split into 9 morphological types, each which are relatively straightforward to identify in the field. Species lists for quadrats and/or the site as a whole are taken periodically to place the sponge morphology data collected in context with the sponge species diversity found at the monitoring site.     Detailed methodologies and descriptions of the morphology types can be found in Marine Monitoring Report 25b (standard operating procedures 4). Quality Assurance exercises have allowed the rules and descriptions to be refined over time. 2 additional sites of Ynys Seiriol (Puffin Island) have been investigated for possible future monitoring, although these are not currently active sites.  These sites lie on the north side of the island. Whilst recording is done in situ, still images/video images of the quadrats are taken for quality assurance exercises.  Specimens may also be photographed and taken from outside quadrats for further identification.\t\tAll electronic data is archived under project 226, media no. 898. Quadrat video and incidental video is stored on the imaging machine. A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG shared drive\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2010-06-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:43:09.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17820\tNRW_DS111013\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.007Z\t267\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Photographic Collection\tCasgliad Ffotograffyddol Rhynglanwol Morol Cam 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131303133\tA collection of photographs taken during the Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Mapping Survey of Wales and associated Microsoft Access database for viewing and displaying the images.  Supplementary visual information for the Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Mapping Survey of Wales. The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005.   All work has been undertaken by former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team. Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.\tFilm and digital equipment used to take photographs at a number of survey sites.  Slides scanned and made available digitally.  Site, grid reference, date, photographer, subject matter and biotope recorded within MapInfo point layer (see \u0027Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Photo points- GIS dataset\u0027 metadata entry) and also within the Phase 1 database.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1 Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Map Photos.lyr  for GIS layer showing  where location photos were taken Digital and scanned images stored on MFSG shared drive Z:\\Archive\\legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\Phase1_Database\\IntertidalPhotographs\\Validated\u0027. Pre-digital images stored in the Hafod Elfyn fireproof room.  Digital data is also archived as project 204, media 569 (File path K:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science.\tPaul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:44:06.261Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17817\tNRW_DS111077\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.924Z\t512\tValid\tEditable\tResults of Studies into the Impact of Hydraulic Cockle Dredging on Intertidal Sediment Flat Communities\tCanlyniadau astudiaethau ar yr effaith a gaiff llusgrwydo hydrolig am gocos ar gymunedau gwastadeddau gwaddodion rhynglanwol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131303737\tThis dataset contains the results of experimental studies investigating the impact of hydraulic cockle dredging on intertidal sediment flat communities at Lafan Sands, North Wales, and Blackshaw Flats, Solway Firth during 1989 and 1990. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the implications of hydraulic dredging for marine nature conservation.\tSix sites were established in each area, three to act as controls for the three experimental sites which were dredged during one tidal cycle during June in the case of Lafan Sands, and July for the Blackshaw Flats. The sites were sampled for infaunal and sedimentological analyses on the day before dredging, then one day, one week, three months and six months after dredging. The data were analysed with the aid of a variety of statistical and graphical techniques.\t\tData is held within JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder. NRW only hold a snapshot of this data.  Paper report available from NRW library (see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above)\tCustodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW JNCC  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1990-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:46:05.318Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17806\tNRW_DS111134\t2024-05-28T12:32:24.511Z\t702\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring Tide-Swept Sponge Communities in the Menai Strait, Anglesey and Warrior, Pembrokeshire\tMonitro cymunedau sbwng a ysgubir gan y llanw yn Afon Menai a Sir Benfro\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313334\tSponges are an important component of benthic communities in temperate waters and can become dominant in suitable conditions. Monitoring takes place at 2 sites within the Menai Strait; Coleg Normal and Nelson\u0027s Column and at one site in the Milford Haven (Warrior), Pembrokeshire. Sponge communities in the Menai Strait are dominated by large, relatively fast-growing species, such as Halichondria panicea, Esperiopsis fucorum and Haliclona oculata which thrive in rapid flowing turbid waters. The purpose of this data capture was to record the abundance and \u0027luxuiousness\u0027 of the sponge communities to in order to give an indication of the favourable conservation status of benthic communities within the Special Areas of Conservation (SAC\u0027s).\tMethods at the sites differ due to physical conditions (current and duration of slack water) Transects are marked out at both sites.  At Coleg Nomal and Warrior the depth of the sponge crust along with presence/absence of key sponge species is recorded at 0.5m intervals.  Where erect sponge species are found within 150mm of the transect the species and height of the sponge is noted as well as the circumference.  This is carried out within a \u0027deep\u0027 and \u0027shallow\u0027 zone.  At Nelsons Columns a diver swims the length of the transect filming the seabed and conspicuous species and communities.  The video is used to describe the habitat and specify species.  In addition 0.3x0.3m quadrats are placed at 2m intervals along the transect and the quadrat photographed.  Photos are taken and archived as a record of the community and may be analysed in future. Detailed methodologies and descriptions can be found in Marine Monitoring Report 25b (standard operating procedures 3 and 5). Additional monitoring is carried out at the Warrior site (please see the Pembrokeshire Marine SAC reef monitoring metadata entry).\t\tElectronic data archived under project 217, media number 801.  A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG drive. Streamed video footage is held on the imaging drive.  Mini DV tapes are stored in the fireproof safe.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T10:40:55.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17825\tNRW_DS111139\t2024-05-28T12:32:25.294Z\t312\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of Kelp Depth as an Indication of Turbidity in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Saranac Special Area of Conservation (SAC)\tMonitro dyfnder gwymon fel dynodiad o dyrfedd yn ACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313339\tTurbidity provides an indication of water clarity and water quality.  The depth to which light can penetrate is reduced with increased turbidity, which may be the result of natural processes in coastal and estuarine waters but can also be caused by anthropogenic causes such as coastal construction and dredge spoil dumping.  Curvie (Laminaria hyperborea) is known to be susceptible to the impacts of elevated turbidity levels and therefore changes in L. hyperborea can be an indicator of changes in turbidity.  Grazing of L. hyperborea by the common sea urchin Echinus esculentus may also have an impact of numbers of kelp plant and therefore numbers of this species present must also be taken into consideration. The purpose of this data capture was to use depth measurements for the distribution of kelp Laminaria hyperborea as a monitoring indicator for medium/long-term changes in turbidity level.  Also to measure the overall \u0027luxuriousness\u0027 (biomass) of the algal turf of the rocky reef.  This information will assist with reporting on the favourable conservation status of reef features in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau.\tA transect is established on an unbroken slope of bedrock with a constant gradient.  Permanent stations are not required.  The diver records the depth (corrected for chart datum) and length of the stipe of the deepest kelp plant.  Only established plants i.e. those with a stipe greater than 100mm in length are recorded.  The diver swims directly upslope with a 3m pole, when 3 plants are touching the pole, the details are recorded.  The diver swims upslope again until the pole is touching 6 plants, recording the details.  The process is carried out at 5 transects at least 10m apart.  The number of Common sea urchins Echinus esculentus occurring along the length of the transect are also recorded. Within these 3 zones (lower, middle and upper) algal turf is gathered from a 0.3m x 0.3m quadrat.  Quadrats are repeated as time allows.  In the lab the algae is washed and excess water removed and the wet weight recorded.  The samples are then dried and the ash-free dry weight recorded. Detailed methodologies and descriptions can be found in Marine Monitoring Report 25b (standard operating procedures 10). Although sampling is repeated annually, poor weather conditions have meant that no data has been collected since 14/07/2005.  In future volunteers may be used to assist with data collection.\t\tElectronic data archived under project 220, media number 586. A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG drive. Streamed video footage is held on the imaging drive. Mini DV tapes are stored in the fireproof safe\tInternal Contact: Mike Camplin, Marine Monitoring Team Leader, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T10:42:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17803\tNRW_DS111141\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.952Z\t610\tValid\tEditable\tBenthic Ecology of Carmarthen Bay (1998)\tEcoleg benthig Bae Caerfyrddin (1998)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313431\tThe 1979 European Commission Directive (79/409 EEC) on the Conservation of Wild Birds requires Member States to notify the Commission of Special Protection Areas (SPAs) that are of particular importance to the conservation of wild birds.   For some time there had been an increasing body of evidence that Carmarthen Bay was an important site in Britain for wintering common scoters (Melanitta nigra), one of the species protected by the ‘Birds Directive’. This project was stimulated by concern for the Carmarthen Bay macrofaunal communities, on which the wintering population of the Common scoter depend, following the Sea Empress oil spill of February 1996.   This dataset contains the results of a project on the benthic ecology of Carmarthen Bay undertaken by Andrew Woolmer as part of his PhD to investigate the benthos and Scoter populations of Carmarthen Bay.   The purpose of this data capture was to describe quantitatively the distribution of the macrofaunal communities in Carmarthen Bay to provide accurate baseline data, and to assess the distribution and density of potential prey species of Common scoter in an internationally important over- wintering site.   The project comprised of four main areas of study:  1) Macrofaunal communities of Carmarthen Bay  2) Growth, abundance and spatial models of the Tellinacean bivalve Fabulina fibula  3) Macrofaunal response groups: a multivariate method for isolating indicator species along environmental gradients  4) Temporal variability, structural redundance, and the long term persistence of the Carmarthen Bay Tellina Community\tThe baseline survey of Carmarthen Bay was carried out from 1 - 3 June 1998 using the Bangor University Research Vessel (RV) Prince Madog. This stratified survey consisted of 29 quantitative and 31 qualitative grab stations.    The quantitative grabs allow the accurate description of community type and composition.    The qualitative grabs were taken to identify boundaries between communities and enable more accurate interpolation between them in the construction of a community map.    A further eight qualitative beam trawls were taken to assess populations of large epifauna.  Sampling methods and procedures described here ensure the specimens obtained are in the best possible condition, a prerequisite for precise identification and enumeration.\t\t[A]  Paper report is available from CCW library (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above).  [B]  Report and data are held within the Marine Archive as Media No. 221 [File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 001to100\\Media 221) [C]  Sediment analysis data on CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW30000000004).\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tWoolmer-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2003-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:47:31.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17802\tNRW_DS111142\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.810Z\t598\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSublittoral Monitoring Trials of North Pen Llyn 1998\tTreialon monitro isarforol o ogledd Pen Llŷn 1998\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313432\tThe data was captured in order to assess the suitability of the biotope classification monitoring method trialled. A measure biotope richness as an attribute of north Pen Llyn reefs, was measured over three surveys transects between July and August 1998.  Surveyors were experienced in Phase 2 survey methodology and each record was carefully re-examined by an experienced operator to ensure that data were comparable to those assigned to biotopes in the national classification, therefore were correctly assigned.\tFixed line transects were used in the following ways. A shot line was deployed from which divers reeled-out a 50 m tape from a pole. The pole was designed to measure a fixed transect width and in combination with the fixed 50 m travelled, limit the area surveyed. It was decided that the direction of deployments for the diver based transects would be random and that species and abundances were to be recorded in-situ using standard protocol. Divers were instructed to record the most conspicuous and characterising features of the biotope as opposed to recording all macroscopic organisms. Three broad trial zones were identified and in each zone between 20 and 30 random sampling points were generated using a random number generating program.  There is a high confidence in this dataset. Surveyors were experienced in Phase 2 survey methodology and each record was carefully re-examined by an experienced operator to ensure that data were comparable to those assigned to biotopes in the national classification, therefore were correctly assigned. This trial was a precursor to the Across Wales Diving Monitoring Project (see metadata entry 109743).\t\tPaper report available from NRW library.  Data on NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000772).  MNCR survey forms are held in NRW off-site storage.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:40:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17800\tNRW_DS111144\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.708Z\t286\tValid\tEditable\tResults of a Study into the Use of Transects to Identify the Broad-Scale Attributes of the Mawddach Estuary. (1999 CCW Marine Monitoring Trials)\tCanlyniadau astudiaeth ar ddefnyddio trawsluniau i nodi priodoleddau aber Mawddach\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313434\tThe purpose of this data capture was to investigate the feasibility of using a transect based methodology to determine the proportion of muddy communities and the zonation of muddy communities from upper to lower shore.  The Mawddach is one of the best examples of a small, drying, bar-built estuary in Britain and, with its mountainous catchment area it has some of the lowest nutrient inputs in Wales.   In order to be able to report on the condition of the estuary a number of attributes have been identified which require monitoring.   The aims of this project was to investigate the feasibility of using a transect based methodology to determine the proportion of muddy communities and the zonation of muddy communities from upper to lower shore.\tFour transects were established to cover the majority of saltmarsh and intertidal biotopes present. Groups of core samples were collected at 25 m intervals along the transect. At each sample point sediment surface features and profile were described and photographed and the conspicuous species and morphological characteristics noted. The eight cores collected from each sample station were pooled and the infauna subsequently processed. The fauna present, identified to species level, were recorded on a spreadsheet as were the results of analysis of particle size characteristics of the granulomatric sample. The infaunal and granulometric data were then processed to identify the biotopes.\tSenior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW. This trialling work is a precursor to the intertidal monitoring carried out on the estuary in 2004 (see separate metadata entry) and is part of the LIFE trials, section 3.4.3 of the Monitoring Report. Sanderson, WG., Holt, RHF., Kay, L., yn, G., McMath, AJ. 2001. The establishment of a programme of surveillance and monitoring for judging the condition of the features of Pen Llyn a’r Sarnau cSAC: 1. Progress to March 2001. Countryside Council for Wales Science Report 380. Bangor.\t[A]  Data held in NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000795).   [B]  Paper MNCR forms are held in NRW off-site storage.   [C]  Archived as project 316, media 1240.\tContact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2001-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:50:04.301Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17804\tNRW_DS111145\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.953Z\t360\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of Subtidal Rocky Reef in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC)\tMonitro riff yn ACA Sir Benfro Forol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313435\tThis dataset consists of the results from an integrated monitoring strategy on a site level carried out to provide an overview of the status of reef feature within the Pembrokeshire MarineSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) as a whole.  Work is concentration on tide-swept infralittoral and circalittoral rock. 7 sites across the SAC selected and permanent monitoring quadrat established within each of the biotope complexes.  Within the upper parts of the Milford Haven sites were established at Beggars Reach; Warrior and Dockyard Bank.  Near to the mouth of the Haven; sites were established at Chapel Reef; Thorn Island; and Junkos Reef.  A site was also established within the off-shore islands of The Smalls.\tAt each site quadrats were generally established in rows of 4 quadrats at 2 or 3 depths at each site.  A standard recording methodology was devised to allow comparisons to be made of the  data collected at any site in any year. 0.5m x 0.5m quadrats are attached to the permanent markers.  An initial assessment of the distribution of species within the quadrat as a whole is made. The abundant/dominant species are recorded first followed by the rare and cryptic species using a series of timed cell frequency counts is then undertaken to assess species diversity.  4 successive counting periods of 5 minutes each is taken noting presence of species from within each cell. Counting continues until a total period of 20 minutes. Where species cannot be identified; specimens are collected from outside the quadrat and identified later.  A photo is taken of the species in-situ.  A specimen catalogue for the whole SAC is also collated. Where time allows either a video of the quadrat is made of images of the quadrat are taken.  Still images have been captured from video for each cell and \u0027stitched\u0027 to make a single image of the whole quadrat. Detailed methodologies and descriptions and variations at specific sites can be found in Marine Monitoring Report 25b (standard operating procedures 15). The majority of QA occurs at Thorn Island and allows for a subset of quadrats to be surveyed and compared prior to the data collection exercise.  This allows all surveyors to refresh their skills and ensure species id is consistent. A subset of all quadrats are either photographed or recorded using video and the images archived to allow quadrats to be re-evaluated if necessary. Temperature and/or salinity loggers are also continuously logging at most of these site.  Sponge thickness measures are also taken at Warrior Site.  Analysis of the quadrat data may also be carried out in PRIMER.\t\tElectronic data archived under project 223, media number 899. A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG drive. Mini DV tape is stored in the fireproof safe and streamed material on the Imaging PC.  Still images are stored on the MFSG drive and copies are available on DVD12 and DVD21 in the fireproof safe (until room available in the electronic archive).  Specimen samples are stored in the NRW lab and will be archived with The National Museum of Wales in due course.\tInternal Custodian: Mike Camplin, Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-09-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T10:44:21.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17805\tNRW_DS111146\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.946Z\t281\tValid\tEditable\tResults of a Study into the Use of a Phase 2 Sampling Grid as a Monitoring Strategy for the Mawddach Estuary. (1999 CCW Marine Monitoring trials)\tCanlyniadau astudiaeth ar ddefnyddio grid samplu cam 2 fel strategaeth fonitro yn aber Mawddach\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313436\tThe aim of this survey was to investigate the feasibility of using a phase 2 sampling grid methodology to determine the proportion of muddy and sandy communities and the distribution of major communities within the estuary.  The Mawddach is one of the best examples of a small, drying, bar-built estuary in Britain and, with its mountainous catchment area it has some of the lowest nutrient inputs in Wales. In order to be able to report on the condition of the estuary a number of attributes have been identified which require monitoring.   This dataset contains the results of a survey carried out to trial a sampling strategy and methodology that addresses two of these attributes: - The relative proportion of muddy and sandy communities. - The distribution of major communities within the estuary.\tMapInfo was used to set up an evenly spaced grid of sampling stations across the whole estuary, from high water springs to low water springs.  The grid was scaled to allow for at least five sampling stations on each of the major intertidal biotopes (this done using the 1997 Marine Intertidal Phase 1 map of the estuary). An extra 30 sample stations were added to account for \u0027minor\u0027 biotopes not already covered by the grid. At each sample station (saltmarsh was not sampled) sediment surface features and conspicuous species were noted. Eight cores were collected from each sample station. These were pooled and the infauna subsequently processed. The fauna present, identified to species level, were recorded on a spreadsheet as were the results of analysis of particle size characteristics of the granulomatric sample. The infaunal and granulometrc data were then processed to identify the biotopes.\t\tData held in NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000739). MNCR paper forms are held in NRW off-site storage.Report available from NRW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above) Archived as project 314, media 1238.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2001-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:51:06.176Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17801\tNRW_DS111147\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.800Z\t437\tValid\tEditable\tMacroinvertebrates in the Glaslyn / Dwyryd Estuary\tMacroinfertebratau yn Aber Dwyryd / Glaslyn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313437\tThis dataset contains the results of a macro-invertebrate survey of the Glaslyn/Dwyryd estuary undertaken in 1978 in order to better understand the ability of the estuary to receive wading birds that may be displaced by barrage construction. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the capacity of the estuary to receive wading birds that might be displaced from other estuaries on the Irish Sea flyway by barrage construction. Data capture also helped to obtain data relevant to the management of the adjacent Morfa Harlech National Nature Reserve and enabled collection of data on the marine fauna of the area.\t82 sampling stations were established across the estuary. At each station five core samples were taken. Four were washed through a sieve and the portion retained was preserved in 5% formalin. These were then examined in the laboratory with the animals present counted and identified. The percentage volume of fines was also determined and a measure of organic content taken. The fifth core sample was bagged and preserved for sediment analysis. Salinity measurements of the estuary water were obtained for a high spring and a high neap tide. This forms part of a series of studies on the estuaries of Gwynedd made partly to assess their capacity to receive wading birds.\t\tA copy of the report is available in NRW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above).   The survey data has been entered into JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000633). NRW only has a snapshot of this data.\tInternal Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External Contact: JNCC\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t1978-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:51:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17799\tNRW_DS111148\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.721Z\t336\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMenai Strait Sublittoral Survey 1982 (Paper Data)\tArolwg Isarforol Afon Menai.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313438\tThis dataset consists of the result of a sublittoral survey carried out in the Menai Strait during 1982 to investigate the habitats and communities present. The dataset contains site descriptions (name, location, depths surveyed, site features etc), lists of all algae and animal species observed and habitat data. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the sublittoral ecosystems of the Menai Strait and to determine the range and distribution of habitats and communities present within the Menai Strait and on adjacent open coasts.  Original survey forms are held by NRW, mobilised data is available through the JNCC distributed Marine Recorder snapshot\tSites for the dives were chosen by selecting transects at 1km intervals along the Strait. Dives were usually made at low water to improve visibility and dive duration. Most of the survey recording was done by two divers with the assistance of several divers who provided essential back up acting as dive partners or photographers. Detailed in-situ records were made on the habitat, algal and animal communities found using pre-determined scales of abundance. Specimens were taken when in-situ identification could not be made and collections of algae were taken at most sites.  Checklists were completed as soon as possible after diving and the habitats were recorded on habitat forms. Photographs were taken of the range of habitat types, communities and species observed.\tLumb, C.M., 1983. Menai Strait sublittoral survey 10th April to 19th September 1982. A report to the NCC, iv ,70p. NCC/University College of North Wales Marine Science Laboratories.\tA copy of the report is available in NRW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above). The survey data has been entered into JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000293). NRW only hold a snapshot of this data.  Paper Data and associated additional survey cards stored in off site storage – box no. CCW/NRB/118\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Paper Data: contact Records Team or Data Services Team Electronic Data: Marine Recorder Snapshot distributed by JNCC\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t1983-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T17:25:50.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17798\tNRW_DS111149\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.613Z\t325\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) - Subtidal Monitoring\tMonitro elfennau rhynglanwol Gwarchodfa Natur Forol Ynys Sgomer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313439\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  Data collected during the development and implementation of low budget stereo-photographic monitoring of rocky subtidal epibenthic communities in within Skomer marine reserve. The data includes descriptions of communities present and subsequent changes in these communities over a one year period (1982 - 1983). The Skomer subtidal monitoring project was established as an ongoing project however this dataset includes the results from the first year only. Purpose of this data capture was to locate and permanently mark suitable monitoring stations and to observe and record the topography and epibenthic communities present in order to enable the assessment of the natural fluctuations in subtidal communities.\tStereophotography was chosen as sampling technique due to it being a non-destructive technique which requires minimal manpower and biological expertise. It also provides three dimensional images. Having considered accessibility, seabed topography etc, the north-east coast of Skomer was chosen as a location for establishing the permanent transect. A transect line was run from 30m to 5m and secured to pitons at intervals, marker tags were fixed to the line at 5m depth intervals from 5m to 25m and at these depths 1.5m fixed quadrats were secured with pitons.  The photographs taken were analysed by viewing slides through two stereomicroscopes, using a hand-held commercial stereoslide viewer and examining the prints with photogrammetric apparatus. Each identifiable species or groups of species and biotic or non-biotic substrate is defined as a category, the occurrence of categories in quadrats is recorded quantitatively. The abundance of categories was estimated using two methods- percentage cover of categories was estimated by systematic point counting, and counts of numbers per unit area were made of certain categories.\t\tA copy of the report is available in NRW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above).  The survey data has been entered into JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000156).  NRW only have a snapshot of this data.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1983-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:52:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17797\tNRW_DS111150\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.547Z\t300\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh saxicolous lichen and bryophyte community data from a survey of upland Britain\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313530\tThe British uplands have two groups of lichen species of outstanding conservation interest: 1) species of western oceanic areas and 2) species of calcareous rock at high altitudes. Phytosociological data were collected to describe the terrestrial lichen and bryophyte vegetation of rock or saxicolous habitats within 14 Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) within upland Britain, with special reference to four saxicolous habitats listed in Annex I of the EU Habitats Directive. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds the Welsh data as well as copies of the project report. The work aimed to improve our knowledge of the lichen and bryophyte communities of rock habitats in upland Great Britain, with particular regard to certain habitats, which have importance in nature conservation legislation. The work had the following main objectives: 1. To describe the lower plant assemblages of Annex I rock habitats in selected sites, which are Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). 2. To provide a preliminary classification of the lower plant communities of rock habitats in upland Britain (excluding freshwater habitats).  These objectives were achieved by extensive sampling of rock habitats, using phytosociological methods, within selected SACs. Those visited were, in Wales: Brecon Beacons, Cadair Idris, Eryri and Berwyn and South Clwyd Mountains.\t14 SACs across Britain were surveyed and a number of additional samples were collected from three additional sites for incorporation into the analysis. All types of saxicolous vegetation were regarded as suitable for sampling, except freshwater habitats and stands dominated by vascular plants. Extensive sampling of rock habitats was carried out using standard phytosociological methods as outlined by Rodwell (1991-2000). Data collected were analysed by two-way indicator species analysis implemented by the computer programme TWINSPAN.\tPhytosociology is the branch of science which deals with plant communities, their composition and development, and the relationships between the species within these compositions.\tData is stored on NRW\u0027s upland ecologist\u0027s g:drive. Paper report is available from NRW library (see related titles section above).\tInternal Custodian: Jane Stevens  (used to be: Upland Ecologist, NRW)  Internal Custodian: Data Services Team, NRW\tScottish Natural Heritage (SNH)-Natural England-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17796\tNRW_DS111152\t2024-05-28T12:32:23.489Z\t663\tValid\tEditable\tPembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Sea Salinity Time Series Data\tData Cyfres Amser Halwynedd Mor ACA Sir Benfro Forol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313532\tWithin Pembrokeshire Special Area of Conservation (SAC) an integrated monitoring strategy at a site level is employed to produce a view of the status of each feature within the SAC as a whole. The Data is captured as part of the Across-Wales Diving Survey, designed to gather information on Reef features across the Marine SAC\u0027s in Wales.  In order to ascertain whether observed changes are due to natural factors or anthropogenically created factors, a suite of environmental factors also need to be considered, such as turbidity, temperature, salinity and water chemistry. Salinity loggers have been placed at sites across Pembrokeshire Marine SAC.    The data is collected continuously, although this has not been processed to produce daily averages due to the low confidence in the data. Additional salinity recordings are taken at Skomer MNR but these are covered in a separate metadata entry. Salinity is also recorded in saline lagoons within Wales. Metadata about this data is held in a separate entry. Gathering physical data such as temperature provides context and background information for the condition assessment of each of the reef features monitored.\tAll the Salinity data is recorded by Starr Oddi Salinity and Temperature loggers. As each logger is deployed a record is kept of which site it has been deployed to and how often the logger has been reused. Loggers are recovered every year but this is dependant on weather conditions and diver availability. All loggers are placed at a fixed location on the seabed. These are marked by bolts, pitons or attached to Monitoring Station Markers. The logger records the Date, Time and Temperature (oC) and salinity (psu) at 1 hour intervals. For each logger, metadata is recorded; including the serial number of the logger and the date and times the logger was deployed and recovered. The salinity loggers are part of a wider network of loggers in Wales that include the Skomer MNR and the Saline lagoons loggers as well as a series of temperature loggers that are deployed across Wales.\t\tAll the salinity data is archived under project 225, media 591.  A working copy of the files is stored at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Temperature and salinity loggers\\Salinity Loggers (starr oddi) by year and location.  Metadata about the loggers is stored in Ffynnon.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2009-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:54:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17848\tNRW_DS111154\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.661Z\t8\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Habitats and Species Spatial Layers for Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP), NERC Act, OSPAR (Archived)\tHaenau data OSPAR\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313534\tThis archived collation of datasets has been superseded by Environment (Wales) Act Section 7: Marine Habitats and Species as part of the newer legislative requirements.    These datasets are associated with the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) was published back in 1994, and was the UK Government\u0027s response to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD), which the UK signed up to in 1992 in Rio de Janeiro. The Convention for the Protection of the Marine Environment of the North-East Atlantic (the \u0027OSPAR Convention\u0027) was open for signature at the Ministerial Meeting of the Oslo and Paris Commissions in Paris on 22 September 1992. The new annex on biodiversity and ecosystems was adopted in 1998 to cover non-polluting human activities that can adversely affect the sea. These two conventions have been highly important for identifying statutory lists of priority species and habitats for Wales. Under Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act, 2006 it is required that the a \u0027list of the living organisms and types of habitat which in the Assembly\u0027s opinion are of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\u0027. The UK BAP is one of the main focuses of this work. As part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 CBD, OSPAR Convention and NERC Act there have been a number of initiatives to identify those species and habitats whose UK populations may be under threat and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity.   The list includes all marine UK BAP priorities as well as additional habitats and species to reflect those in need of conservation action in Wales. For each species and habitat data provided it should include a map of the distribution around the Welsh coast and supporting text with information on the ecology, rarity, legislative protection, occurrence in statutory sites and known threats. This dataset is made up of a collation of datasets which have been updated throughout the years. All of these are listed separately under the \u0027Contains\u0027 field below. These datasets related to the marine species and habitats which are of conservation importance for Wales.\tThis data should no longer be used as superseded by Environment (Wales) Act Section 7: Marine Habitats and Species data.  The layers are created by collating records from numerous surveys, monitoring programmes, casework, and third party providers and then combining them using GIS to produce layer containing points, polygons or centroids. The layers are updated when new records are produced. The original surveys and monitoring events from which the records have been extracted vary widely in dates. However surveys were undertaken between the dates of 1899 - 2015.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP Species Archived X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\tInternal Custodian: - Natasha Lough - Marine Habitats Biologist, Knowledge Evidence and Advice,  Kirsten Ramsay - Subtidal Team Leader, Knowledge Evidence and Advice.   NRW  Metadata Contact: - Spatial Data and GIS Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17850\tNRW_DS111157\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.821Z\t6060\tValid\tEditable\tRegional Seascape Units\tUnedau Morweddau Rhanbarthol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313537\tA \u0027seascape\u0027 is an area of sea, coastline and land as perceived by people, whose character results from the actions and interaction of land and sea, by natural and/or human factors.   This dataset is the product of the first detailed assessment of the character of Wales\u0027s seascapes. The purpose of this data capture was to characterise and define the geographical extent of the seascape units of Wales and to assess the sensitivity of each seascape to offshore developments.   The dataset contains a map, images, and descriptive text for each of the 50 seascapes units in Wales. Also included are maps illustrating the national and regional divisions of seascape units, land with sea views, relative visibility of the sea surface from land and tranquillity. There are also maps presenting the results of sensitivity assessments carried out to model the sensitivity of seascape units to offshore wind farm developments, wave farm developments and tidal stream developments. Included in the dataset is a pdf file outlining what exactly the MapInfo files in the dataset show.\tThe assessment was carried out at a regional scale, producing 50 regional seascape units to cover the whole of Wales. Each was described in terms of key characteristics and special qualities as well as being assessed for its sensitivity to offshore wind, wave and tidal stream developments.   The maps were derived from OS and digital terrain modelling data. The data has been thoroughly quality assessed by NRW\u0027s Geographical Information Unit to ensure that it\u0027s technically sound as well as fit for purpose. The project is seminal work and as a result has produced the only dataset of its kind.      INFORMATION WARNING: The 2009 report has since been partly superseded by more recent work by White Consultants (2019) \u0027Seascape and visual sensitivity to offshore wind farms in Wales: Strategic assessment and guidance. Stage 3 - Seascape and visual sensitivity assessment for offshore wind farms. NRW Evidence Report No. 331\u0027.  The data relating to the level of sensitivity or capacity to accommodate offshore wind, wave or tidal stream developments is out of date and should not be used as evidence.  The narrative descriptions in the written report and the and spatial division of the Welsh coastline into 50 regional seascape units may be of continuing assistance in understanding the variety and character of different coastal landscapes in Wales.\t\tX:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes regional units.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes coast hight points.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes coast hight regions.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes index value line.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes merged land units single polys.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes merged land units.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes merged sea units single polys.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes merged sea units.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes regional units.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes unit numbers.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes value index points.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes value weightedscore land units.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascapes value weightedscore line.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Landscape Architect, Environmental Policy Group, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:55:39.144Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17855\tNRW_DS111158\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.917Z\t426\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Nature Conservation Review Intertidal Survey of Rhos Point to New Brighton\tArolwg Rhynglanwol yr Adolygiad Cadwraeth Natur Morol, Pwynt Rhos i New Brighton\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313538\tThe results of intertidal surveys carried out on the flora and fauna of sixteen sites within a survey area along the southern coast of Liverpool Bay. The shores within the survey area were mainly sediment shores however at some sites rocky substratum was present.  The purpose of this data capture was to describe a representative range of intertidal habitats and their associated flora and fauna from locations on the open coast distributed throughout the survey area. The survey was part of the Marine Nature Conservation Review which had the aims of extending our knowledge of British marine ecosystems, identifying sites of nature conservation importance and providing information to support more general measures required to minimise adverse effects of development and pollution.   The sampling area extends from Rhos-on-Sea in the west to New Brighton in the east, (approximately 50 km) and comprises of the majority of the southern coast of Liverpool Bay. All sites were surveyed between the 23rd - 25th of May 1990, and some sites were subject to additional visits on the 17th and 18th of September 1990.\tSites were chosen to provide a representative range of intertidal habitats. At soft sediment sites macrofauna was sampled using four cores of 0.01m2, to a depth of 15 cm. Hand-digging of approximately 1m2 at each site and observations of cast, tubes etc gave information on the larger more sparsely distributed species. Sediment was also taken from each station for granulometric analysis. The survey methods for the hard substrata sites involved taking samples of barnacles from different locations at all sites to check their identity and scrapes of mud encrusting rocky surfaces were also taken in order to identify their flora and fauna. The biological interest and conservation importance in terms of sites, habitats, communities and species was then assessed. This field survey contributed to the Marine Nature Conservation Review of Great Britain which commenced in 1987.\t\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on a copy of JNCC\u0027s Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000240). NRW only hold a snapshot of this data - part of All Wales snapshot)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1991-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T10:54:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17839\tNRW_DS111159\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.464Z\t294\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Reserve Sublittoral Habitats and Communities 1983\tCynefinoedd a chymunedau isarforol Ynys Sgomer 1983\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313539\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ).  This dataset contains the results of the survey of 44 sublittoral survey sites around the Skomer Marine Reserve during 1983. 111 species of plant and 276 animal species were noted (27 species of algae and 27 species of animal were previously unrecorded from the area). The purpose of this data capture was to collect information necessary to provide revised descriptions of habitats and communities for the Skomer Marine Reserve (SMR) and to continue the SMR seaweed survey (first started in 1982) in order to provide information on algal communities around the reserve and to provide quantitative information on seaweed communities.\tThe survey team consisted of diving scientists and diving non-scientists. Sites were primarily chosen on the basis that they had not been covered adequately by previous surveys in the area and that they were within areas to be added to the marine reserve by proposed boundary changes.  Habitat descriptions, brief notes and profile drawings were recorded on specially prepared sheets, and wave and tidal stream exposure were obtained. Plant and animal species were recorded and abundance determined, and at two sites quantitative seaweed surveys were undertaken. Where necessary samples of unidentified plants and animals were taken to the lab for further information. At some sites, photographic records of habitats, communities and species were taken using a Nkionos underwater camera with an electronic flash.\t\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above.  Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR60000117).  NRW only hold a snapshot of this data\tInternal Custodian:  Assistant Marine Conservation Officer - Skomer MNR Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1984-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T09:26:23.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17841\tNRW_DS111160\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.502Z\t236\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey of Mynydd Preseli\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313630\tThis dataset is realated to a draft Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) report entitled \u0027A vegetation survey of Mynydd Preseli, Pembrokeshire 2003-2005\u0027. The quadrat data, which details the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) habitat codes, has not yet been validated.\tThree survey seasons between 2003 and 2005 (most work carried out during 2004).  The survey was undertaken by members of CCW’s Phase II survey team in 2003, 2004 and 2005, with input from A.M. Averis in 2004. No mapping was carried out in August to October 2003, when the focus was on collecting representative quadrat data for the site’s flush communities. The flushed lowlands of the main block of Preseli (CL19 and most of CL43) were mapped in August and September 2004 by S.D.S. Bosanquet, D.K. Reed and A.J. Turner; at the same time, A.M. Averis mapped the drier upper slopes of the site. The 2004 survey area was revisited in early summer 2005 in order to assess whether Carex dioica, C. hostiana or Eriophorum vaginatum had been missed during the previous autumn’s mapping, and this resulted in the reassigning of some stands of marshy grassland vegetation. The southern commons of Gors Fawr and Waun Isaf were mapped by AJT and SDSB respectively in June and July 2005.\t\tH: drive, under Science/TSG/PEATSURV/Upland Survey Preseli H:\\Science\\TSG\\PEATSURV\\Upland Survey Preseli  Legacy information: Draft report is currently held by Alex Turner CCWs ECN Project Officer, once finalised this will be from CCW libraries. The quadrat data is held in an excel spreadsheet, also stored by Alex Turner, whilst the vegetation maps are available as MapInfo tables on CCW Upland Ecologists user drive.\tDataset collected by CCW Staff including Alex Turner, Sam Bosanquet DK Reed AM Averis        Sam Bosanquet said that Alex never finished the Preseli write-up - with the excel spread sheets being in Ffynnon, and the GIS data being somewhere on ArcGIS. However it could still be in MapInfo format.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:53.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17847\tNRW_DS111161\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.557Z\t594\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNant Teyrn Freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313631\tEnvironmental Change Network (ECN) is network of sites spanning the UK where we conduct environmental monitoring using standard methods. There are currently 12 terrestrial sites and 45 freshwater sites, covering a wide range of upland and lowland habitats including moorland, chalk grassland, woods and forests, farmland, small ponds and streams, large rivers and lakes. ECN is the UK member of the International Long-Term Ecological Research Network (ILTER) and of LTER-Europe. The ECN\u0027s objectives are to establish and maintain a network of sites from which to obtain comparable long-term datasets through the monitoring of a range of variables; to integrate and analyse these data to identify natural and man-induced environmental changes, improve understanding of the causes of change, distinguish short-term fluctuations from long-term trends, and predict future changes.  Nant Teyrn is a small upland stream on the out flow from Llyn Teyrn in Yr Wyddfa National Nature Reserve. This freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) site is co-located with Yr Wyddfa/Snowdon terrestrial ECN site.  As part of the ECN project long term monitoring of physical, chemical and biological variables occurs at Nant Teyrn. The monitoring is designed to collect, store, analyse and interpret long-term datasets for physical, chemical and biological variables, which drive or respond to environmental change. The data is subject to a variety of quality control procedures (QA). Each monitoring protocol has a corresponding paper form. The data is entered into an Excel spreadsheet which has background calibration graphs in order to flag any outliers within the data. These outliers can then be double checked against the paper records. Every year the spreadsheet is checked against the paper records, and each line of data within the spreadsheet is assigned a quality code which helps to flag up any problems during data collation e.g. snow in the gauges. The CEH CCU also has its own QA procedures which they follow on receipt of the data.\tStandard protocols which have been developed specifically for ECN are used to collect data (see individual metadata entries in the \u0027contains\u0027 field below for more details). The data is sent to CEH Merlewood where it is checked and transferred into a centralised database managed by the ECN Central Coordinating Unit (CCU) incorporating data from all ECN sites across the UK.\t\tSee individual metadata entries in the \u0027contains\u0027 field above for more details.\tCustodian: Victoria Bowmaker and Alex Turner, ECN Project Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tWelsh Government-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:53.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17846\tNRW_DS111162\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.534Z\t665\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Features of Morfa Uchaf Dyffryn Conwy Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) 1990-1994\tNodweddion morfa heli SoDdGA Morfa Uchaf Dyffryn Conwy\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313632\tNational Vegetation Classification has been used as a basis for community definition in the saltmarsh habitats of the southern Conwy Estuary.  The principal objective of the survey was to provide a suitable basis for Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) designation. Data collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors and CCW staff.\tNo formal publication is associated with this dataset however a series of quadrat cards and floristic tables in paper format in North Region files indicate that NVC methodology was followed.  CCW commissioned survey contributes to an NVC Saltmarsh survey collation (see \u0027part of\u0027 field).  Survey was carried out in three discrete periods: July 1990, July - August 1993 and August 1994 - areas of the site were re-visited, so it is not possible to ascribe a specific date to individual community polygons, most quadrat recording cards indicated a specific date, and this has been retained in the data.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Saltmarsh Amalgamated saltmarsh data for Wales  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media901to1000\\Media904 Archived as Project 251, Media 904. Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Conwy 1990 to 1994 Original paper maps, quadrat cards, and floristic tables are in North Region files (Mold Office)\tInternal Custodian: Heather Lewis - Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice Team,  NRM,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1994-08-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T09:27:14.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17852\tNRW_DS111164\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.918Z\t725\tUnassessed\tEditable\tYr Wyddfa/Snowdon Terrestrial Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site - Physical Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313634\tPhysical data collected at the Environmental Change Network (ECN) terrestrial site - Yr Wyddfa.  The 700ha site incorporates the summit of Yr Wyddfa and includes three additional summits over 800m, it also includes 5 lakes. It is part of Yr Wyddfa National Nature Reserve managed by NRW. Measurements are taken of various meteorological, surface water and soil variables. The purpose of this data capture was to use the observed changes in physical variables to indicate the possible causes and consequences of environmental change in the terrestrial environment. See overarching Yr Wyddfa ECN metadata entry (\u0027part of\u0027 field below) for general ECN objectives.\tMeteorology: both unattended and attended recordings of meteorological data are made at ECN\u0027s sites. Automatic Weather Stations record 13 variables hourly e.g. solar radiation, wind speed, rainfall, soil temperature, and surface wetness. Standard Meteorological Observations are used to measure 14 variables daily e.g. max and min temperatures, soil temperatures, snow depth, sunshine.  The rain sample data collected contributes to the Acid Water Deposition Monitoring network.  Surface water discharge: continuous discharge measurements are taken. The data is sent to CEH Merlewood where it is checked and transferred into a centralised database managed by the ECN Central Coordinating Unit (CCU) incorporating data from all ECN sites across the UK.\t\ta) The excel spreadsheet is stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff. The ECN data is also available from CEH.  b) Paper forms are filed in NRW\u0027s ECN team office c) Paper copies of reports are held in NRW\u0027s library, electronic versions are held by NRW\u0027s ECN team. d) ECN team members m:drives\tInternal Custodian:  Victoria Bowmaker, and Alex Turner, Environmental Change Network Project Officer, NRW  Metadata Contact:  Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tWelsh Government-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:29:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17844\tNRW_DS111165\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.536Z\t490\tUnassessed\tEditable\tYr Wyddfa/Snowdon Terrestrial Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site - Chemical Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313635\tThis dataset is a collation of the chemical data collected at the Environmental Change Network (ECN) terrestrial site - Yr Wyddfa. The 700ha site incorporates the summit of Yr Wyddfa and includes three additional summits over 800 m, it also includes 5 lakes. Measurements taken for chemistry include atmospheric chemistry and precipitation chemistry. As well as the ECN monitoring protocols, other data collected at the site includes the results of ammonia monitoring taking place at Dyffryn Mymbyr, and monitoring of Ozone carried out at Marchlyn Mawr.  The purpose of this data capture is to use the observed changes in chemical variables to indicate the possible causes and consequences of environmental change in the terrestrial environment.\tAtmospheric chemistry:  Passive diffusion tubes are left out for a two-week period in order to measure the concentration of Nitrogen Dioxide (NO2).  Precipitation chemistry:  14 chemical variables are analysed weekly including pH, conductivity, concentrations of Sodium, Potassium, Calcium, Aluminium etc.The ECN data is sent to CEH Merlewood where it is checked and transferred into a centralised database managed by the ECN Central Coordinating Unit (CCU) incorporating data from all ECN sites across the UK. The ammonia data contributes to the UK Ammonia Monitoring Network and the results of the Ozone monitoring are linked to Welsh Air Quality Forum.\t\ta) The excel spreadsheet is stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff.  The ECN data is also available from CEH. b) Paper forms are filed in NRW\u0027s ECN team office c) Paper copies of reports are held in NRW\u0027s library, electronic versions are held by NRW\u0027s ECN team. d) ECN team members m:drives\tVictoria Bowmaker, and Alex Turner, Environmental Change Network Project Officer, NRW.\tWelsh Government-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17836\tNRW_DS111166\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.439Z\t607\tUnassessed\tEditable\tYr Wyddfa/Snowdon Terrestrial Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site - Biological Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313636\tThis dataset consists of biological data collected at the Environmental Change Network (ECN) terrestrial site - Yr Wyddfa / Snowdon North Wales.The 700ha site incorporates the summit of Yr Wyddfa and includes three additional summits over 800 m, it also includes 5 lakes.  Aspects of the vegetation, vertebrates and invertebrates at the site are monitored following ECN protocols. As well as the ECN monitoring protocols, other data collected purely for Natural Resources Wales (NRW) use and include data on the arctic alpine flora and fungi. Phenological information is also collected (information on the timing if things e.g. tadpole hatching). The purpose of this data capture is to collect data on the biodiversity of the site and how it changes over time in order to indicate the possible causes and consequences of environmental change in the terrestrial environment.\tVegetation: Baseline survey carried out on the whole site with species presence related to National Vegetation Classification (NVC). Coarse-grain sampling, 50 random 2 m x 2 m grid plots surveyed every 9 years. Fine-grain sampling, 2 10 m x 10 m plots randomly selected within each NVC type surveyed every three years.  Vertebrates: Annual bird counts by species are undertaken using British Trust for Ornithology (BTO) Breeding Bird Survey methodologies. Bat species and behaviour are mapped on transects. The common frog is also monitored by monitoring spawn development and pond environment.  Invertebrates:  monitoring includes butterfly monitoring, based on the Butterfly Monitoring Scheme protocol; pit fall trap sampling of ground predators and spiders; night counts of moths using the Rothamsted Insect Survey methodology; spittle bug nymph density and colour morph sampling; and tipulid larvae extraction.\tThe data is sent to CEH Merlewood where it is checked and transferred into a centralised database managed by the ECN Central Coordinating Unit (CCU) incorporating data from all ECN sites across the UK. Butterfly data is provided to the UK Butterfly Monitoring Scheme and the phenological information collected is sent to the UK Phenology Network.\ta) The excel spreadsheet is stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff. The ECN data is also available from CEH. b) Paper forms are filed in NRW\u0027s ECN team office c) Paper copies of reports are held in NRW\u0027s library, electronic versions are held by NRW\u0027s ECN team. d) ECN team members m:drives\tInternal Contact: Victoria Bowmaker, or Alex Turner, Environmental Change Network Project Officer, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tWelsh Government-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17851\tNRW_DS111167\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.798Z\t556\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNant Teyrn Freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site - Physical and Chemical data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313637\tEnvironmental Change Network (ECN) is network of sites spanning the UK where we conduct environmental monitoring using standard methods. There are currently 12 terrestrial sites and 45 freshwater sites, covering a wide range of upland and lowland habitats including moorland, chalk grassland, woods and forests, farmland, small ponds and streams, large rivers and lakes. ECN is the UK member of the International Long-Term Ecological Research Network (ILTER) and of LTER-Europe. Nant Teyrn is a small upland stream on the out flow from Llyn Teyrn in Yr Wyddfa National Nature Reserve. This freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) site is co-located with Yr Wyddfa/Snowdon terrestrial ECN site. This dataset consists of the physical and chemical data collected at the ECN freshwater site at Nant Teyrn, Snowdonia. Measurements taken include surface water discharge and surface water chemistry. The physical and chemical variables measured are expected to indicate the possible causes and consequences of environmental change in the aquatic environment.\tSurface water discharge- Continuous stage and discharge measurements recorded using a permanently installed digital logger. Surface water chemistry- Dip samples are analysed for a number of determinands including major ions and heavy metals. Continuous pH, temperature, conductivity and turbidity recording. The data is sent to CEH Merlewood where it is checked and transferred into a centralised database managed by the ECN Central Coordinating Unit (CCU) incorporating data from all ECN sites across the UK.\t\ta) The excel spreadsheet is stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff. The data also gets sent to CEH.  b) Paper forms are filed in NRW\u0027s ECN team office c) Paper copies of reports are held in NRW\u0027s library, electronic versions are held by NRW\u0027s ECN team d) ECN team members m:drives\tCustodian: Victoria Bowmarker, Alex Turner ECN Project Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tWelsh Government-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:29:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17840\tNRW_DS111168\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.513Z\t609\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNant Teyrn Freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site - Biological data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313638\tEnvironmental Change Network (ECN) is network of sites spanning the UK where we conduct environmental monitoring using standard methods. There are currently 12 terrestrial sites and 45 freshwater sites, covering a wide range of upland and lowland habitats including moorland, chalk grassland, woods and forests, farmland, small ponds and streams, large rivers and lakes. ECN is the UK member of the International Long-Term Ecological Research Network (ILTER) and of LTER-Europe.  Nant Teyrn is a small upland stream on the out flow from Llyn Teyrn in Yr Wyddfa National Nature Reserve. This freshwater Environmental Change Network (ECN) site is co-located with Yr Wyddfa/Snowdon terrestrial ECN site. This dataset is made up of the biological data collected at the Environmental Change Network (ECN) freshwater site at Nant Teyrn, Snowdonia. Measurements and observations are made on phytoplankton, aquatic macrophytes, epithilic diatoms and macro-invertebrates.  The biological variables measured are expected to indicate the possible causes and consequences of environmental change in the aquatic environment.\tPhytoplankton- the concentration of phytoplankton chlorophyll is measured weekly. Aquatic macrophytes- species presence, abundance and distribution are recorded annually. Epithilic diatoms - The sampling procedure follows that used for the United Kingdom Acid Waters Monitoring Network and occurs three times per year. Macro-invertebrates - species presence, abundance and deformities are recorded three times per year.\tThe pilot study was undertaken during 2006/2007 (results of this study were used) and monitoring has been continuous since.  The data is sent to CEH Merlewood where it is checked and transferred into a centralised database managed by the ECN Central Coordinating Unit (CCU) incorporating data from all ECN sites across the UK.\ta) The excel spreadsheet is stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff. The data also gets sent to CEH.  b) Paper forms are filed in NRWs ECN team office c) Paper copies of reports are held in NRW\u0027s library, electronic versions held by NRW\u0027s ECN team. d) ECN team members m:drives\tData Custodians: Victoria Bowmaker, or Alex Turner - ECN Project Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tWelsh Government-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17854\tNRW_DS111170\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.895Z\t580\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Sublittoral Habitats and Communities 1984\tCynefinoedd a chymunedau isarforol Ynys Sgomer 1984\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313730\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ).  This dataset contains the results of surveys of habitats and communities from 90 previously unsurveyed or poorly described sites around Skomer Marine Reserve. The dataset includes site locations, grid references, site descriptions and site maps; habitat information; community categories and species distributions.  The purpose of this data capture was to add to the results of past surveys in collecting the information necessary to provide revised descriptions of habitats and communities for the whole reserve. A major aim of the survey was to produce more photographs of species and habitats in the reserve.  The survey spanned the period between the 7th of March and the 6th of September 1984, with the majority of survey work undertaken in the summer months, specifically August.\tThe survey team consisted of diving scientists and diving non-scientists. Dives below 30 m were designated as being deep dives and required special planning. Sites were primarily chosen on the basis that they were unsurveyed deep sites or were of potential interest but not covered/only briefly covered in past surveys. Habitat descriptions, brief notes and profile drawings were recorded on specially prepared sheets (the habitat recording sheets were revised following the 1983 Skomer sublittoral survey- see separate metadata entry). Wave and tidal stream exposure were obtained and plant and animal species were recorded and abundance determined. Where necessary samples of unidentified plants and animals were taken to the lab for further information. At some sites, photographic records of habitats, communities and species were taken using a Nkionos underwater camera with an electronic flash.\t\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above.   Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000161). NRW only hold a snapshot of this data.\tInternal Custodian:  Assistant Marine Conservation Officer - Skomer MNR Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1985-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T09:27:55.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17843\tNRW_DS111172\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.542Z\t388\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland vegetation survey of Bwlch Corog, Ceredigion (2005)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313732\tThis dataset comprises of the results from vegetation survey work carried out at Bwlch Corog. Bwlch Corog is located between two Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs): Pengarreg−gopa a Moel Hyrddod SSSI and Pencreigiau\u0027r Llan SSSI, which are notified for their blanket mire, dry and wet heath vegetation. The survey area can be divided into three main sub−areas: the enclosed fields of Pemprys and Cefn Coch separated by the more upland area of Bwlch Corog. The vegetation of this central area can be regarded as the most semi−natural and the principal vegetation types are M25 Molinia−Potentilla mire, M17 Scirpus−Eriophorum blanket mire, M15 Scirpus− Erica wet heath, H12 Calluna− Vaccinium heath, M6 Carex−Sphagnum mire U4 Festuca− Agrostis−Galium grassland, U5 Nardus−Galium grassland and U6 Juncus−Festuca grassland. There are large expanses of species−poor Molinia−dominated vegetation pointing to a prior history of frequent burning and heavy grazing which probably ceased when the adjacent forestry was planted.\tThe site covers 427.5 ha and rises from 140m to 388m OD on Bwlch Corog itself. The southern part of the site is currently grazed by sheep at a low grazing pressure, while the northern part of the site has been recently cattle grazed. Homogeneous stands of heath, mire, grassland and associated vegetation were identified, sampled and mapped, using the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) as the basis for community definition. Additional forms of vegetation were recognised when floristic composition differed from the descriptions given in the NVC. Field survey work, including mapping and sampling, took 6 person days. Due to shortage of time, quadrats were only recorded in the more extensive and ecologically more interesting vegetation types. Accurate identification of plants encountered during floristic recording was considered to be important, and particular care was taken to distinguish between closely allied species with distinct ecological preferences. Bryophytes and lichens of uncertain determination in the field were sampled and subjected to later microscopic identification.\t\tQuadrat data is held in an excel spreadsheet, stored by Alex Turner, whilst the vegetation maps are available as MapInfo tables on CCW Upland Ecologists user drive. A paper copy of the report is available from CCW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above)\tInternal Custodian:  (used to be Alex Turner) Sam Bosenquet, Snr Habitat Survey \u0026amp; Evaluation \u0026amp; Non-Vascular Plant Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17838\tNRW_DS111173\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.455Z\t325\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSublittoral Monitoring Trials of Sarn Badrig Reef (1997 - 1998)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313733\tThe data was captured in order to assess the suitability of the biotope classification monitoring method trialled. Sublittoral monitoring trials were done to investigate the extent of biotopes on Sarn Badrig reef- the most extensive of three Sarnau reefs within Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC. In total three surveys were been carried out to investigate the biotopes present.  1) A 1998 survey to evaluate the deployment of divers for measuring the extent of the Sarnau reef biotopes.  2) A 1999 survey of Sarn Badrig reef using quadrat methodology.  3) A 1999 transect survey of the reef.\tSurvey 1: A 1998 survey to evaluate the deployment of divers for measuring the extent of the Sarnau reef biotopes.  A shot line was deployed from which divers reeled-out a 50m tape from a pole. The pole was designed to measure a fixed transect width. Two transect locations were chosen to include the full range of broad habitats present. Four broad trial zones were identified and in each, three sampling points were randomly selected. Species and abundances were recorded in-situ using standard protocol.  Survey 2: A 1999 survey of Sarn Badrig reef using quadrat methodology. The initial survey deployed four pairs of divers to map out habitat features of the area surrounding a fixed station. Four quadrats were placed by an expert algologist to represent the range of variability in the community composition within the biotope present. At each, species were recorded by seven diving surveyors, who recorded individual counts, % areas of cover and \u0027cell frequency\u0027. The species data recorded in Marine Recorder only includes the individual counts of species.  Survey 3: A 1999 transect survey of the reef. Stratified random selection of sites were investigated using a `roll-out` transect technique. Three main `sites` were surveyed comprising strings of sampling stations in lines perpendicular to the reef crest: - one on the seaward limit of the reef, one mid-reef and another across the eastern third of the reef. Records were made using Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) abundance scales.\t\tData held in NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (1998 survey- JNCCMNCR40000773, 1999 quadrat survey MRCCW30000000006, 1999 transect survey JNCCMNCR40000776 ). Report available from NRW libraries (see \u0027related titles\u0027 above) MNCR paper forms are held in NRW off-site storage.\tMarine Monitoring Biologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:13:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17853\tNRW_DS111174\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.917Z\t304\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sediment Flats in Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2007\tGwaith monitro rhynglanwol ar wastadeddau gwaddodion yn ACA Y Fenai a Bae Conwy yn 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313734\tThe purpose of this data capture was to establish the reference condition from which to establish conservation objectives; and to be able to take account of natural changes in the appropriate management of the features of the Special Area of Conservation (SAC). In addition to this the data collected to establish sites for long term monitoring and collect baseline/reference data using appropriate methods, in accordance with the conservation objectives.  Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC is designated for five Annex 1 habitats: Sandbanks which are slightly covered by sea water all the time, Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide, Reefs, Large shallow inlets and bays and Submerged or partially submerged sea caves. This dataset includes the results of an intertidal monitoring survey carried out on the sediment flats at Traeth Lafan during September 2007. The intertidal sediment communities across Traeth Lafan are considered to be of particular nature conservation importance and their good condition (Favourable status) is listed as a Conservation Objective of the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. Performance Indicators were still being developed for this feature, so this survey focuses on the richness, distribution and abundance of sediment biotopes.\tPhase 1 surveys of intertidal habitats in the SAC were carried out in the SAC between 1999 and 2003 (Brazier et al. 2007), providing detailed mapping of the intertidal biotopes with some information on characterizing species. A \u0027phase 1\u0027 style methodology adapted from Wyn and Cooke (2001) backed up by a standard core sampling and laboratory analysis.  Existing CCW Phase 1 survey maps were used to define the survey area. The \u0027Grid Maker\u0027 tool in MapInfo was then used to create grids of point across the survey area producing a resulting grid of 106 points with 500m between the grid points. There were three parts to the work: in-situ recording of sediment characteristics (including epibiota, infauna and granulometry) at each of the 106 survey stations; collection of samples from seven stations for laboratory analysis of infauna and sediment granulometry; and mapping the extent of a seagrass (Zostera noltii) bed using the track function of handheld GPS units.  The confidence in this data is high. Work was carried out by a team of 6 experienced marine biologists. The quality and thoroughness of the species records and counts was sometimes reduced when large amounts of coarse sediment (mainly shell material, with coarse sand in some stations) were present in sieve however specimens which could not be identified in the field were later inspected under microscopes at the survey base.\tIntertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW  Moore, J., Aquatic Survey and Monitoring Ltd. 2009.  Across Wale intertidal Sac monitoring Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC September 2007. Countryside Council for Wales Marine Monitoring Report 56. Bangor.\t[A]  Data is held within the marine archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 549 [B]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 56 - MS\\ampersand\\\"CB intertidal monitoring 2007 ASML [C]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\Data [D]  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder [E]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\GIS [F]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\Images\"\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T10:55:39.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17837\tNRW_DS111175\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.431Z\t773\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Tide-Swept Boulder Habitat in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\tGwaith monitro rhynglanwol ar gynefin clogfeini llanwol yn ACA Y Fenai a Bae Conwy yn 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313735\tThere were two purposes for this data capture. Firstly, to establish the reference condition from which to establish conservation objectives; and to be able to take account of natural changes in the appropriate management of the features of the Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Secondly, to repeat survey of sites surveyed in 2005 to further test and develop monitoring methodology and collect monitoring data. Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC is designated for five Annex 1 habitats; Sandbanks which are slightly covered by sea water all the time, Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide, reefs, large shallow inlets and bays; and submerged or partially submerged sea caves. Intertidal mudflats and sandflats in this location include a variety of biotopes, many of which are present on the large sediment flat of Traeth Lafan. Intertidal reefs include a large variety of biotopes, with tide-swept bedrock, boulder and cobble habitats particularly well represented. This dataset includes the results of an intertidal monitoring survey carried out on the tide-swept boulder habitat just south of Britannia Bridge during September 2007.\tThe methodology used was as follows.  5 stations were placed at intervals of 70-100 m along the shore at the Britannia Bridge site with their positions uploaded to handheld GPS. 5 boulders were selected for survey at each station using set criteria. Each boulder was surveyed and results were recorded on a recording form (upper surfaces and under surfaces of the boulders were recorded separately on either side of the form).  Data captured included:  - Digital photographs of the upper and under surface;   - Length and width of the boulder,   - Description of underlying subtrata (including a record of whether or not it was anoxic)  - Assessment of whether the boulder had been turned  - Records of presence or absence of the conspicuous species (aggregate taxa and individual species and genera)  - Estimates of percentage cover for algae and other cover species  - Presence of mobile animals and hydroids etc\tPhase 1 surveys of intertidal habitats in the SAC were carried out in the SAC between 1999 and 2003 (Brazier et al. 2007), providing detailed mapping of the intertidal biotopes with some information on characterizing species. The report associated with this dataset is as follows:  Moore, J. 2009 Across Wales intertidal SAC monitoring Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC September 2007. Countryside Council for Wales Marine Monitoring Report (56). Bangor.\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 552. [A] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 56 - MS\\ampersand\\\"CB intertidal monitoring 2007 ASML [B] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\Data [C] NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder - MRCCW16300000005 [D] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\GIS [E] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\2007 ASML MS\\\"ampersand\\\"CB\\Images\"\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T10:57:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17845\tNRW_DS111176\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.530Z\t312\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rocky Reefs in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313736\tPembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is considered to be one of the best areas in the UK for marine reef habitats, a category within annex 1 of the Habitats Directive. This dataset comprises of the results of re-surveys of the six Milford Haven rocky intertidal transects first established in 2005, and the additional establishment of a similar transect at Nolton Haven in the north of Pembrokeshire.  The purpose of this data capture was to repeat a set of six permanent monitoring sites representative of the rocky shore intertidal habitats within the Haven Waterway and last surveyed in 2005 and to present data on the current status of the sites. This data was also used to establish another site outside the Haven to the north at Nolton Haven.\tFieldwork was carried out during five days of spring tides during 2007.  A number of techniques used within the survey follow the same methodology as currently used within the MarClim monitoring programme and therefore are comparable to data from other sites around the Welsh coastline.  The main surveys conducted were as follows: -  Permanent quadrat recording: Relocated the six monitoring sites. At each site records were taken from upper, middle and lower shore heights, with four 1m x 1m quadrats relocated in each zone. Cell counts were made for conspicuous species. -  Limpet counts and monitoring: Limpet abundance was recorded from 5 random cells within each quadrat. Up to 200 limpets were systematically selected within the middle zone at each site and the longest basal shell length of each was measured to construct population profiles.  -  Barnacle abundance: Percentage cover of barnacles was recorded from 5 random cells within each quadrat  -  Ascophyllum nodosum population structure: 50 Ascophyllum plants were randomly selected from the mid shore zone at Lawrenny Quay. The number of vesicles on the longest unbroken frond of each plant was recorded to provide an estimate of the average age of the plants on the shore.\t\t[A] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 60 - Pembroke Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs [C] NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (Survey No. MRCCW1630000000C) [D] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs\\GIS [E] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs\\Images\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-06T16:03:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17842\tNRW_DS111177\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.525Z\t517\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\tGwaith monitro rhynglanwol ar byllau glan mor yn ACA Sir Benfro Forol yn 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313737\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) was therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.   Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) was selected for eight Habitats Directive Annex I habitat types and five Annex II species. The SAC is considered to be one of the best areas in the UK for marine reef habitats, a category within the Annex I list. High habitat and biological diversity is of great importance throughout the site, particularly the best documented reefs habitat in the Milford Haven ria-estuary.   This dataset includes the results of a survey of intertidal rockpools at Pen-y-Holt on the Castlemartin peninsula.\tA well defined mid shore rock platform was identified from local knowledge. Within this area 10 pools were selected for survey, they all had to be at a similar height on shore, have a surface area of more than 0.5m2, be more than 5 cm deep and not more than 4 times as long as wide. A GPS fix was taken for each.   Photographs were taken of each pool and basic measurement of depth and length were taken. The presence of typical pool sub-habitats and the influence of silt and scour were recorded as were the presence of typical pool taxa. Percentage cover estimates were made for algae and other cover species and presence of mobile animals and hydroids were recorded. A sample of Corallina officinalis algal turf was collected from each pool and from 4 locations in the Milford Haven. These samples were sent to a laboratory for post-survey identification of cryptic fauna. IECS was contracted in 2005 to establish a series of fixed quadrats across the rocky shore sites within the Milford Haven waterway (Hull et al., 2006).   The 2005 survey provides baseline data for this project undertaken in 2007.\t\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 1221. [A]H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 60 - Pembroke Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML[B]H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs[C]  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder[D]H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs\\GIS[E]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2007 ASML Pembs\\Images\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2009-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T09:28:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17849\tNRW_DS111178\t2024-05-28T12:33:16.709Z\t736\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria Reef in Cardigan Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) 2007\tGwaith monitro rhynglanwol ar riff Sabellaria yn ACA Bae Ceredigion yn 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313738\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) was therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.   Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary, tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats).    Cardigan SAC is designated for a number of features, including Bottlenosed dolphins, Sandbanks, Reefs and Sea Caves, and some intertidal features including areas of bedrock and boulder/cobble habitats and many areas are characterised by biogenic reef formed by the honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata.   This dataset includes the results of a survey of the biogenic reef formed by the honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata at two sites within Cardigan Bay SAC. The purpose of this data capture was to establish sites for long term monitoring and collect baseline/reference data using appropriate methods. This is a high quality survey carried out by an experienced team of marine biologists.\tMethodology used was a modified version of the transect survey methodology developed for Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC (2008).   Existing maps of Sabellaria extent were used to define the survey area within which a series of 11 transects were drawn at 50 m intervals approximately perpendicular to the coastline.   Along each transect notes were made on habitat, community and condition/age of Sabellaria reef. Photographs were also taken to illustrate habitat and condition of the reef.   1 or 2 stations were used per transect for quadrat surveys. At each survey station a gridded 0.25m2 quadrat was placed randomly 15 times within a radius of 10m.   Digital photographs were taken of each quadrat and a full survey was carried out on the first 5 quadrats to record % cover of standing water, total Sabellaria and live Sabellaria, key species/taxa, other typical fauna and flora. The other 10 quadrats were used to carry out additional recording of Sabellaria cover.   The extent of the reef was mapped using the track function of handheld GPS units.   Phase 1 surveys of intertidal habitats in the SAC were carried out in the SAC in 1996 and 2003 (Brazier et al. 2007), providing detailed mapping of the intertidal biotopes with some information on characterizing species. Surveys of Sabellaria reefs in the SAC were carried out for CCW in 2004 (Boyes et al. 2008). Additional mapping of Sabellaria reef extent at Aberaeron was carried out by CMACS Ltd. (sub-contracted by Atkins Ltd.) in August 2006 as part of an Environmental Assessment for proposed Aberaeron North Beach Coastal Defence Work (unpublished data provided by Atkins Ltd.).\t\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No, 199, Media No. 548. [A] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 57 - Cardigan Bay Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\Data [C] CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder [D] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\GIS [E] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\Images\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-06-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T13:56:02.365Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17832\tNRW_DS111179\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.970Z\t536\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools in Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\tGwaith monitro rhynglanwol ar byllau glan mor yn ACA Bae Ceredigion yn 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313739\tThe purpose of this data capture was to establish sites for long-term monitoring and collect baseline/reference data using appropriate methods.   Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated primarily for Bottlenose dolphins, but is also designated for Sandbanks, Reefs and Sea Caves. These Reefs and Sea Caves are present within intertidal areas of Cardigan Bay SAC.   Intertidal reefs in Cardigan Bay SAC include areas of bedrock and boulder/cobble habitats and many areas are characterised by biogenic reef formed by the honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria. Many intertidal rockpool biotopes are considered to be important for their biodiversity (JNCC 1996).   Rockpools are also a notable feature of some intertidal reef areas in the SAC. This dataset includes the results of a survey of intertidal rockpools at two sites within Cardigan Bay SAC.\tPhase 1 surveys of intertidal habitats in the SAC were carried out in the SAC in 1996 and 2003, providing detailed mapping of the intertidal biotopes with some information on characterizing species.    Aberporth: A well defined mid shore rock platform was identified from CCW phase 1 data. Within this area 10 pools were selected for survey, they all had to be at a similar height on shore, have a surface area of more than 0.5m2, be more than 5cm deep and not more than 4 times as long as wide. A GPS fix was taken for each. Photographs were taken of each pool and basic measurement of depth and length were taken. The presence of typical pool sub-habitats and the influence of silt and scour were recorded as were the presence of typical pool taxa. Percentage cover estimates were made for algae and other cover species and presence of mobile animals and hydroids were recorded. A sample of Corallina officinalis algal turf was collected from each pool. These samples were sent to a laboratory for post-survey identification of cryptic fauna.    At Cei Bach: Two survey stations were established on the west side of a large shallow pool in the lower shore zone. At each a gridded 0.25m2 a quadrat was placed randomly 5 times within a radius of 10m. Digital photographs were taken of each quadrat. For features recorded at each transect see Aberporth methodology above. A total of two samples of Corallina officinalis algal turf were collected and sent to the laboratory.    There is high confidence in this dataset as survey carried out by an experienced team of marine biologists with good field identification skills.\t\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 555 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 555) [A] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 57 - Cardigan Bay Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\Data [C] NRWs copy of Marine Recorder - MRCCW16300000004 [D] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\GIS [E] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Card Bay\\2007 ASML Card\\Images\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T13:57:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17834\tNRW_DS111180\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.976Z\t330\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools, Caves and Overhangs in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2007\tGwaith monitro rhynglanwol ar byllau glan mor, ogofau a gordoeon yn ACA Bae Caerfyrddin ac Aberoedd yn 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313830\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) was therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features were in favourable conservation status.    There were two purposes for this data capture:    - To provide an inventory and distribution map of selected biotopes present to use as a reference condition for the rocky shore habitat communities in the SAC.  - To describe gross changes in these features since the Phase 1 survey (1999-2001) to give some indication of their stability.    The rocky shore areas that lies within the SAC includes a number of rockpool, overhang and cave features that are considered to be nationally important biotopes and/or Rare or Scarce in Wales and others that are considered to be important for their biodiversity. These potentially species rich rocky habitats were therefore surveyed to provide an inventory of these sub features for future survey work.    The confidence in this data is high as surveys were carried out by a team of experienced marine biologists.\tLocations of recorded rockpools, overhangs and caves in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC were compiled from the CCW Phase 1 database. Records were grouped by geographic area and GPS units and maps were used by the survey teams to navigate to the recorded position of each feature.   If there was no relevant biotope feature present at the recorded position of a Phase 1 survey record but a similar biotope was present in the vicinity, it was assumed that the original record had been inaccurately fixed, so an accurate GPS fix was made and tagged to the original record.   If there was no relevant biotope feature present at the recorded position of a Phase 1 survey record and no similar biotope in the vicinity, it was assumed that the biotope was no longer present, if the surveyors found additional examples of notable rockpools, overhangs or caves, an accurate GPS fix was taken and in-situ records made in the same way as all the other records.   A brief description was recorded of the habitat and communities within each feature e.g. size of feature, its level on the shore, dominant and characteristic species/taxa and other notable features of condition or potential anthropogenic impacts. Photographs were taken to illustrate the feature.    Phase 1 surveys of intertidal habitats in the SAC were carried out in the SAC between 1999 and 2001 (Brazier et al. 2007), providing detailed mapping of the intertidal biotopes with some information on characterizing species.\t\tData is held within the marine archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 555 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 555) [A]  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\MarineMonitoring\\MarineMonitoring Reports\\No 58 - Carm Bay Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B]  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\Data [C]  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder - MRCCW16300000008 [D]  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\GIS [E]   Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\Images\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T13:58:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17831\tNRW_DS111181\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.824Z\t258\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sediment Transects in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\tGwaith monitro rhynglanwol ar drawsluniau gwaddodion yn ACA Bae Caerfyrddin ac Aberoedd yn 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313831\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) was therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.    Between 1999 and 2001, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) undertook a Phase 1 biotope mapping survey of the intertidal areas of the Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC).   This dataset includes the results of surveys of the biotopes present along eight transects around Carmarthen Bay. Thirteen transects had been surveyed with a very similar methodology in 2005.  This survey repeated one of those transects and established seven new transects. The purpose of this data capture was to provide sufficient information to identify the biotopes present and describe the composition of the communities; and to establish sites for long-term monitoring and collect baseline/reference data using appropriate methods.\tA modified version of standard Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) Phase 2 methodology was utilised. 20 transects (13 surveyed initially in 2005 and seven surveyed for the first time in 2007) were pre-selected by CCW to represent the variety of sediment biotopes present. Using handheld GPS the surveyors walked down the transect lines making notes on substrata/habitats and deciding how many biotopes were present. A GPS fix was taken at a station position within each of those biotopes which was considered to best represent the biotope. One or more (max 3) of the sediment stations were selected for quantitative core sampling (usually biotopes most vulnerable to change from anthropogenic activity and which represented a significant portion of the transect). Five core samples were taken from each of the selected sampling stations for macrofauna and granulometry analyses and a site description and photograph were taken at each station. Two in-situ bivalve count methodologies were also trialled opportunistically during the surveys of 4 transects- an estimation of the numbers of Scrobicularia plana and an estimate of the densities of Cerastoderma edule, Macoma balthica and Angulus tenuis.     Between 1999 and 2001, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) undertook a Phase 1 biotope mapping survey (Wyn et al. 2006) of the intertidal areas of the Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC).\t\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 549 [A]H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\MarineMonitoring\\MarineMonitoring Reports\\No 58 - Carm Bay Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\Data [C] CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder  [D] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\GIS [E] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2007 ASML Carm\\Images\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:16:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17835\tNRW_DS111182\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.969Z\t406\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Pectenogammarus planicrurus in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007 - Onwards\tGwaith monitro rhynglanwol ar Pectenogammarus planicrurus yn ACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau yn 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313832\tPectenogammarus planicrurus is the only amphipod which is a permanent resident of shingle beaches and it was found on the steep gravel or pebble beach fronting the promenade at Pwllheli during Phase 1 surveys undertaken by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) during June 1996.  This dataset contains the results of a survey carried out in 2007 to investigate whether P. planicrurus was still present in the area.  Monitoring of this feature is ongoing and is repeated on a yearly basis.   The purpose of this data capture is to confirm the continued presence and monitor the population of this scarce amphipod species at Pwllheli, repeating work carried out in 2004.\tA visit was made to a site in lower shore shingle at Marian-y-de, Pwllheli where populations of the amphipods were known to be present to determine whether or not they were still present. The species is found by finding the appropriate habitat, making a small pool of water in the shingle and then looking for amphipods swimming in the water. This was done at irregular intervals along the shore.  The survey is repeated regularly.\tMercer, T.S. 2013. Intertidal SAC monitoring, Pen Llyn a’r Sarnau SAC July 2012. CCW Marine Monitoring Report No: 102, pp 68 + x, Aquatic Survey \u0026amp; Monitoring Ltd. Bollihope, Co. Durham.\tData is archived within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 554 and Project 410. Media No. 1430.  Marine Monitoring data for each year is also organised in legacy drives  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:40:02.163Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17833\tNRW_DS111183\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.977Z\t689\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Muddy Gravel in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\tGwaith monitro rhynglanwol ar raean lleidiog yn ACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau yn 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313833\tThe purpose of this data capture was to carry out a repeat survey of sites previously surveyed to further test and develop monitoring methodology and collect monitoring data.    In fully marine conditions on the lower shore, sheltered muddy gravel habitats can be particularly species rich due to the complex nature of the substratum supporting a high diversity of infauna and epifauna. Analysis of survey records held on the Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) database suggest that fully saline sheltered muddy gravel communities are scarce in their British distribution, with available descriptions lacking detail and comprehensive data. This has been attributed to the habitats being difficult to survey and monitor, due to the large quantities of coarse material which require sampling and subsequent analysis. Similarly, historical data on the distribution of muddy gravel beds appears limited.   This data set includes results of sediment coring and sampling undertaken at one site in Porthmadog during intertidal monitoring of the muddy gravel in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC).\tFive core samples were taken using a 0.01m2 corer. The cores were taken within a radius of approximately 5 m from the central mark; and randomly placed without reference to the surface features (with eyes closed). Each core was then sieved over a 0.5 mm mesh and the sieve contents transferred to a suitable labelled pot or plastic bag. These samples were then taken back to the field survey base and preserved in 10% formalin.    A sample of the sediment was also taken from each station for granulometry analysis. The sample was usually taken from the side of one of the corer holes, to a depth of at least 5cm, and placed in a labelled plastic bag. Samples for macrofauna and granulometry analyses were given to CCW for later transfer to an analytical laboratory.   The confidence in this data is high as the surveys were carried out by a team of highly experienced intertidal surveyors.\t\tData is held within Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media No. 557 [A] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 59 - PLAS Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\Data [C] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\GIS [D]  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder [E] H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\Images  The report and data collected under NRW contract FC 73-02-342 is archived as Project No 199, Media 549 and is stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Fynnon on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:41:03.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17828\tNRW_DS111184\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.582Z\t346\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Dyfi Estuary in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007\tGwaith monitro rhynglanwol ar aber Afon Dyfi yn ACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau yn 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313834\tThe purpose of this data capture was to establish sites for long-term monitoring and collect baseline and reference data using appropriate methods.    Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary. Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau has representative examples of bar-built estuaries in north-west Wales, and includes the Glaslyn/Dwyryd, Mawddach and Dyfi estuaries.\tThere were three parts to the work:   - In-situ recording of sediment characteristics (including epibiota, infauna and granulometry) at a grid of 106 stations across the whole area of the sediment flats;    - Collection of samples from eleven stations for laboratory analysis of infauna;    - Sediment granulometry and surveys including quadrat recording on three rocky shores.   Modified version of the phase 1 style survey methodology was used. The Grid Maker tool in MapInfo was used to create a grid of 96 stations overlaying existing maps of the Dyfi sediment flats. A single 0.01m2 core sample was taken at each station. This was sieved in a nearby pool and all visible animals were identified and counted. The unrecognisable animals were collected for later inspection under a microscope. Records of conspicuous species and surface sediment features were made from each station within a circle of 1m radius around the core sample position.   In addition to the in-situ recording, core samples were also taken from 11 sites for standard laboratory analysis of macro-infauna and granulometry. Three representative sites along the northern shore were selected for rocky shore surveys. At each, a transect route running down to the shore was divided into zones. The percentage cover of the major species in each zone was recorded, with the presence noted of all other species found. One zone was selected on each shore for sampling with 5 gridded quadrats (0.25 x 0.25 m), with all species present in each quadrat recorded either as percentage cover or as counts. Photographs were taken of each quadrat and each shore zone.    Phase 1 surveys of intertidal habitats in the SAC were carried out between 1999 and 2003 (Brazier et al. 2007), providing detailed mapping of the intertidal biotopes with some information on characterizing species. Confidence in data is high as surveys were carried out by a team of highly experienced intertidal surveyors.\t\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 199, Media 549 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\ Media 549. [A] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\No 59 - PLAS Intertidal Monitoring 2007 ASML [B] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\Data [C] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\GIS [D]  CCW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (Survey No. MRCCW16300000009) [E] Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2007 ASML PLAS\\Images\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2009-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T17:06:37.527Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17829\tNRW_DS111186\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.581Z\t393\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveys of Harbours, Rias and Estuaries in Southern Britain - Loughor Estuary and the Burry Inlet\tArolygon o borthladdoedd, morydiau ac aberoedd yn ne Prydain - Aber Llwchwr a Phorth Tywyn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313836\tThe Nature Conservancy Council commissioned the Field Studies Council to undertake studies of the marine inlets of southern Britain, including harbours, rias and estuaries, in order to gain information on the ecosystems present. The study took place between 1985 and 1988 and covered 24 inlets. This dataset contains the results of a survey of Loughor Estuary (incorporating the Burry Inlet). The purpose of this data capture was to describe areas in terms of their physical attributes; to review the results of previous studies; to review marine resource usage e.g. fisheries, diving activities etc; to catalogue available information and to collect information and photographs on the habitats and species present.\tA review of available information was undertaken before field work commenced. 23 intertidal and 3 subtidal dredge sites were sampled during the survey of Loughor estuary with the sites chosen because of their known nature conservation importance. Both hard substratum and sediment shores were sampled. On hard substratum a systematic description was made of the abundance of species in the main habitats/communities present at different heights on the open shore. Records were also made from unusual habitats e.g. gullies, rockpools etc and if time was short it was this aspect that was prioritised. On sediment shores the habitats present were recorded by making notes of habitat characteristics, using a spade to dig over an area to look for conspicuous fauna, and sieving sediment to find less conspicuous fauna. Core samples were also taken at most sites in for macrofaunal analysis.  To sample sublittoral sediments a fishing boat was hired to do some dredging and the dredge contents were sieved through a 1mm sieve. Notes of depth of water, volume of sediment etc were also made.\t\tPaper report (volume 1) is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000256). NRW only has a snapshot of this data\tData contacts: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1989-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:42:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17830\tNRW_DS111187\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.581Z\t572\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Sediment Survey of Cosheston Pill with Reference to Shelduck Distribution\tArolwg o waddodion rhynglanwol yng Nghilfach Cosheston, gan gyfeirio at ddosbarthiad hwyaid yr eithin\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313837\tCosheston Pill provides an important sanctuary for a number of wildfowl and wader species including redshanks, curlew and shelduck. Previous studies had shown that in Cosheston Pill shelduck appeared to frequent certain areas of the Pill preferentially- this dataset contains the results of an intertidal sediment survey designed to investigate this preference further. The purpose of data capture was to assess whether or not shelduck feeding area preference in Cosheston Pill were related to variation in the distribution of their prey species and therefore related to spatial variations in the characteristics of the sediments.\tFive sample stations were established, three in an area where shelduck were known to congregate and two in areas they avoided. At each station two replicate biology samples and a sample for grain size analysis, organic matter and calcium carbonate determination were collected. All organisms from the sample for biological analysis were identified to the lowest practicable taxon and counted. Statistics were applied to the data to determine the numerically dominant taxa and diversity and dominance indices.\t\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000658)\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW  External Custodian: JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1990-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T15:56:15.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17827\tNRW_DS111189\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.409Z\t421\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveys of Harbours, Rias and Estuaries in Southern Britain - Milford Haven and Daugleddau\tArolygon o borthladdoedd, morydiau ac aberoedd yn ne Prydain - Aberdaugleddau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313839\tThe Nature Conservancy Council commissioned the Field Studies Council to undertake studies of the marine inlets of southern Britain, including harbours, rias and estuaries, in order to gain information on the ecosystems present. The study took place between 1985 and 1988 and covered 24 inlets. This dataset contains the results of a survey of Milford Haven and the estuary of the Rivers Cleddau (Daugleddau estuary). The purpose of this dataset was to describe areas in terms of their physical attributes; to review the results of previous studies; to review marine resource usage e.g. fisheries, diving activites etc; to catalogue available information and, to collect information and photographs on the habitats and species present. The field survey specifically aimed to collect further information on habitats and species where little or no previous work had been carried out, in areas of known or likely nature conservation importance, or where rare or unusual species may be present.\t14 intertidal, 17 subtidal diving and 7 dredge sites were surveyed to investigate four main elements:  1) Epibiota of rocky and sediment shores- a systematic description was made of the abundance of species in the main habitats/communities present at different heights on the open shore. Records were also made from unusual habitats e.g. gullies, rockpools etc. Sketches of site location, site profile and important features were made and photographs taken. 2) Infauna of sediment shores- shore habitats were recorded at each site and abundance of species visible at the surface noted. A general dig for large or widely dispersed species was accompanied by sieving and laboratory analysis to identify the less conspicuous fauna. 3) Epibiota of subtidal rock and sediments- surveys were undertaken from deep to shallow water or offshore to onshore with some spot dives. A drift dive was undertaken at one location. At each survey station the habitat type was described and the abundance of conspicuous species recorded. Photographs were taken species were collected where necessary for identification purposes. 4) Infauna of subtidal sediments-sediment samples were collected at 5 sites for qualitative examination of the macro-infauna. A pipe dredge also collected sediment from each sample site.\t\t[A] Paper reports (volumes 1 and 2) are available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. [B] Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000246). Available on All Wales Snapshot.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Custodian: JNCC    Metadata Contact:Master reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1987-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:43:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17826\tNRW_DS111190\t2024-05-28T12:33:15.367Z\t377\tValid\tEditable\tSublittoral Survey of Sarn Badrig, Sarn-y-Bwch and Cynfelin Patches 1986\tArolwg isarforol o Sarn Badrig, Sarn-y-Bwch a Sarn Cynfelin 1986\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313930\tThe results of a sublittoral survey of the mid Wales sarnau (reefs) carried out in 1986 to more fully describe the sublittoral communities, habitats and species present on Sarn Badrig and the two smaller reefs to the south.  The purpose of this dataset was to describe the sublittoral and sublittoral fringe communities on and around the mid-Wales sarnau (reefs), and to assess their scientific interest and nature conservation importance.\tTwenty sites (15 on Sarn Badrig, 3 on Sarn-y-Bwch and 2 on Cynfelin Patches) were selected to cover a range of depths. At each site, records were made of plants and animals present at each habitat encountered. Abundance scales were used to estimate the abundances of plant and animal species and all the information was later transferred to habitat forms and species checklists. Samples of pebbles and cobbles were collected for later identification of encrusting and cryptic species and a suction sampler was used at several sites to collect sediment infauna for later identification, using a quadrat of 0.075m2 and 10cm deep to collect replicate samples at each site. Photographs of habitats and species present were taken at most sites.\t\tPaper reports are available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000125). NRW only hold a snapshot of this data\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW JNCC  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t1986-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:45:41.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17870\tNRW_DS111195\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.792Z\t514\tValid\tEditable\tGallows Point Sublittoral Survey 1998\tArolwg isarforol o\u0027r Pwynt 1998\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313935\tThe aim of this data collection was to collect baseline data on the habitats and species present in the area adjacent to the proposed marine development. Prior to this site visit there were no survey data from the site. This dataset contains the results of a site visit undertaken by CCW to record baseline data on the habitats and species present at Gallows Point in view of a proposed marina development in the area.\tTwo survey dives were carried out at the site using standard MNCR phase 2 methodologies. There is a high confidence in this data as the survey was undertaken by experienced sublittoral surveyors.\t\tThe data is available on NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000698).  MNCR forms are held in NRW off-site storage.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1998-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:44:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17885\tNRW_DS111196\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.905Z\t541\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRemote Sensing Image Catalogue\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313936\tRemote sensing is any method of collecting information about objects such as lakes, trees, fields and habitats \u0027from a distance\u0027. This dataset contains a collection of around 300 archival images purchased by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to build up a resource of satellite remote sensing images of the Welsh countryside. The images have not all been collected for specific projects, however some tasking has taken place i.e. images requested from a particular satellite or from a particular date. Each image has metadata detailing image identification number, date and time, type of image, location etc.\tData collection is done by instruments carried on satellites.\t\tMost images are kept in locked storage units in CCW\u0027s Maes y Ffynnon and the Environmental Policy group\u0027s work area.  Backups are held on hard drives and stored in a safe. The metadata is stored with the images.\tHelena Skyes  Used to be Alan Brown, Remote Sensing Officer, Environmental Policy Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:02:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17876\tNRW_DS111197\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.871Z\t798\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRemote Sensing Phase 1 Habitat Survey Update\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131313937\tUp-to-date habitat survey of Wales produced by analysis of remote sensing data. The dataset is comprised of contiguous habitat polygons classified into phase 1 categories. It is the product of a large scale remote sensing project aimed at updating the Phase 1 habitat survey carried out in Wales between 1979 and 1999. It was produced in order to have key dataset for Wales which can be used to inform the core work of former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and its partners, and map specialist habitats or interpretations of habitats.  The maps will be checked through field visits and feedback from CCW staff with local knowledge.\tRemote sensing is basically any method of collecting information about objects such as lakes, trees, fields and habitat type \u0027from a distance\u0027 - in this case, predominantly by satellite images. Original source data is predominantly satellite images (see metadata entry \u0027Remote sensing image catalogue\u0027). Other data was derived from aerial photographs and a range of other GIS data sources. A draft map has been produced for the whole of Wales. This has been broken down into 50 projects, eight of which have been refined and re-processed into phase 1 maps (this process will be ongoing subject to funding with the ultimate aim of completing all 50 projects thus having all Wales coverage). Production of the draft map began in 2006 and was completed by the end of the financial year 2007/08, however the training of the systems began in 2004/05. The re-processing of the 50 project areas should be completed over the next two years. A draft map for the whole of Wales has been produced to assess the quality of the general rule base. The rules were fine-tuned one project at a time to get the best local result and to allow quality checking. The images have been combined and analysed to derive an interpretation of the habitat type present. The appearance of habitats varies throughout the year therefore images were compared from different times of year to identify habitats by their characteristic pattern of phenological changes. A draft map of Wales was produced which was then divided into around 50 areas (known as projects) within which it is possible to apply the same interpretation to the colour information form a satellite image. A set of rules were created in a computer programme to interpret the images and determine habitat type present. Ground truth work has also been carried out in the field to calibrate and train the analytical systems to come up with the best interpretation of the habitat type. Maps will be merged using a method known as DS Belief Functions.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Remote sensed Phase 1\\Phase 1 remote sensed.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Senior Remote Sensing Manager, Sustainable Communities, NRW Metadata Custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-11-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:11.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17884\tNRW_DS111205\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.890Z\t398\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveys of Harbours, Rias and Estuaries in Southern Britain - Taf, Tywi and Gwendraeth Estuaries.\tArolygon o borthladdoedd, morydiau ac aberoedd yn ne Prydain - aberoedd Taf, Tywi a Gwendraeth\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323035\tThe Nature Conservancy Council commissioned the Field Studies Council to undertake studies of the marine inlets of southern Britain, including harbours, rias and estuaries, in order to gain information on the ecosystems present. The study took place between 1985 and 1988 and covered 24 inlets. This dataset contains the results of a survey of the Taf, Tywi and Gwendraeth estuaries. The purpose of this data capture was to: - Describe areas in terms of their physical attributes;  - Review the results of previous studies;  - Review marine resource usage e.g. fisheries, diving activities etc; and, - Catalogue available information and to collect information and photographs on the habitats and species present.\t21 sites were surveyed (18 intertidal sites and 3 dredge sites).  On rocky shores a systematic description was made of the abundance of species in the main habitats/communities present at different heights on the open shore. Sketches of the site location and profiles were prepared, photographs were taken and samples were collected for identification where necessary.  On sediment shores shore habitats were recorded and sketch maps prepared. Core samples were also taken for later macrofaunal analysis- at each station four 0.01msq cores were taken in order to describe the species composition of the community. Due to the shallowness of the site subtidal sediments were sampled at low tide using a two gallon galvanised steel bucket scraped through the sediment for a distance of approximately 1m. The bucket contents were then sieved and notes on the sampling depth of water, volume of sediment and grid references were made.\t\tPaper report (volume 1) is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000258)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1989-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T15:56:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17875\tNRW_DS111212\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.865Z\t371\tValid\tEditable\tSurvey of the Littoral Zone of Great Britain\tArolwg o barth rhynglanwol Prydain Fawr\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323132\tBetween 1970 and 1980 NCC contracted a survey on the littoral zone of the coast of Great Britain. Former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds a series of reports prepared under this contract as well as data in Marine Recorder.   The purpose of this data capture was:  -  To record the distribution of habitats and the associated organisms in the littoral zone of Great Britain in order to verify existing information, fill gaps in knowledge, and ensure uniformity in the description.   -  To make a photographic record of the main types of littoral habitats and the associated plant and animals.    -  To develop suitable criteria for the assessment and selection of key areas in terms of their scientific and conservation value and assess the information collected by the criteria and so select key areas, and to zone the rest of the coast in relation to the key areas.\tSurveys were timed wherever possible to coincide with good spring tides in the spring and autumn. Details of the habitat and species found down to Low Water Spring (LWS) were recorded using small cassette tape recorders or notebooks. As wide an area of shore as possible was investigated in order to include micro-habitats such as overhangs, pools and the under surfaces of boulders. Photographs were taken to show the topography of the shore, zonation, details of species assemblages, and the relation of the shore to the coast backing it. The abundance of species was roughly estimated in relation to the normal abundance of that species.   Information on sites and records of species from published papers, reports, and various National recording schemes was also collated. The results of the south west Wales report contributed to the Pembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas (PMSA)- see separate metadata entry and Marine Recorder survey \u00271976-78 Bartrop PMSA - South West Wales littoral zone survey\u0027 (MRCCW10000000035)\tThe survey team consisted of experts from the Scottish Marine Biological Association and the Marine Biological Association. The number of shores that could be visited and the length of time spent at each site was limited by the small survey team (at first four, then six people).\t[A] Paper reports are available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. [B] Data available on JNCCs copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR10000265). NRW only have a snapshot of this data\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External Contact: JNCC  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1980-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:46:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17882\tNRW_DS111220\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.899Z\t319\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCollation of Information on Species and Habitats in the Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) 1975-1991\tCasgliad o wybodaeth am rywogaethau a chynefinoedd Gwarchodfa Natur Forol Ynys Sgomer 1975-1991\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323230\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. This dataset is a collation of previously unpublished information concerning species and habitat records from Skomer MNR and review and assess the conservation importance of faunal species (emphasis on difficult groups of marine infauna e.g. sponges and sea squirts). Some of the information dates from 1975 however most has come from an expedition to Skomer in July 1987 and a series of diving workshops at Dale Fort Field Centre between 1988 and 1991. The data is chiefly from the seas around Skomer but records from nearby areas of the Pembrokeshire coast are also included.\tData from 120 dives at 66 sites is considered.  Site descriptions were recorded on data sheets and in personal log books following dives. During Dale Fort Field Centre courses participants collected specimens (care was taken to separate material from different sites). The identities of the specimens were confirmed by Francis Bunker and Bernard Picton before being entered onto the master lists. Photographs were taken using underwater cameras. This data forms part of the Pembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas.\t\t[A] Paper report is available from NRW Reports Archive on the DMS. [B] Species Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (Survey number: MRCCW10000000033). Snap\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1992-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T15:57:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17872\tNRW_DS111292\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.739Z\t329\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ) Sediment Interface Study 1994-1995\tAstudiaeth o ryngwyneb gwaddodion Ynys Sgomer 1994-1995\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323932\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. A detailed survey of the shores of Skomer Marine Nature Reserve was carried out in 1982. This dataset comprises of the results of surveys at two sites within Skomer MNR during 1994 and 1995 designed to establish monitoring sites and collect baseline survey data on the macrofauna and megafauna of the sediment/water interface in shallow subtidal regions. The purpose of this data capture was to initiate monitoring of the sediment/water interface in shallow subtidal regions taking into account both macrofauna obtained from samples and visible megafauna.\tAt both survey sites three permanent markers were fixed to form a triangle - this provided two transects. At Low Point in-situ studies were carried out along the east side of the easternmost transect, whilst north of the Neck in-situ surveys were carried out on the west side of the westernmost transect. At both sites the same methodology was used- estimation and counting of visible fauna in quadrat areas adjacent to the transect; wide angle photographs taken at consecutive quadrat locations along the transect, and the sampling of a 1msq area. This sampling involved taking 25 close up photographs of the sediment interface and using a purpose built diver operated airlift to remove bottom sediment down to a depth of 10cm. Material collected was filtered and the larger material was retained  for laboratory observation and identification. In the laboratory additional photographs were taken of sorted rock material to illustrate sessile biota present. This dataset also contributes to Pembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas, see separate metadata entry.\t\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above.   Data available on NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000059)\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tSubsea Survey-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1996-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:46:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17883\tNRW_DS111294\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.904Z\t334\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools Following the Sea Empress Oil Spill 1996-97\tGwaith monitro rhynglanwol ar byllau glan mor yn dilyn trychineb olew y Sea Empress\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323934\tFollowing the Sea Empress grounding in Milford Haven on February the 15th 1996, it was decided that a programme of monitoring was required to assess the impact of the oil on the coasts of Pembrokeshire and Carmarthenshire. This dataset contains the results of rockpool monitoring carried out for a year following the spillage to assess community changed caused by the oil spill. The data collected was used to assess community changes caused by the oil spillage. It was thought rockpool monitoring was particular suitable for this purpose because rockpools are diverse in physical characteristics, occur at different height on shores, contain a range of biota, and are relatively easy to re-locate.\t21 rockpools were studied at 10 locations. Surveys involved describing the pools in terms of position on shore, catchment area, level of exposure/shading. Mobile species were counted on approach. Surface dimensions of the pool were sketched and the habitat types within the pool mapped. Photographs were taken to to support the biological and habitat data and a complete species list for each pool was completed. Water samples were taken for chemical determination of hydrocarbon pollution. This dataset contributes to Pembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas, see separate metadata entry.\t\t[A] Paper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. [B] Data available on NRW\u0027s copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000076)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T15:58:31.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17877\tNRW_DS111295\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.876Z\t869\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternational Biological Programme Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323935\tThe International Biological Programme (IBP) was an international research programme set up in the 1960s which aimed to understand the dynamics of whole ecosystems e.g. the energy flow across an ecosystem and the productivity of biological resources. Many sites were set up across the globe as part of the IBP, one of which was in Snowdonia. This dataset contains raw data collected at a site in Snowdonia as part of the IBP. Data collected includes meteorological data, vegetation data flora and fauna data etc. The dataset also contains some earlier data on sheep numbers. Purpose of data capture: The IBP was set up to coordinate large-scale ecological and environmental studies. The main purpose of data capture was to gain a greater understanding of whole ecosystem dynamics and how ecosystem processes may be affected by environmental change. Methods of data capture: Extensive field research was carried out at the site throughout the duration of the IBP. Soil collection, vegetation sampling, meteorological studies and weighing of animals were some of the methods used. Geographic coverage: The main experimental site was above Llyn Llydaw on Snowdon. Temporal coverage: The IBP ran from approximately 1964 to 1974. The main work on Snowdon took place during the late 60s; however there is also data on sheep numbers from the 1950s. Confidence in data: High. Survey work was undertaken by expert scientists.\t\t\tThe hardcopy raw data is to be kept in the monitoring team store room at NRW\u0027s Plas Penrhos campus. The excel spreadsheets are stored on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff.\tAlex Turner, Environmental Change Network Project Officer, Operational Support Group, CCW.\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\t\t1978-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:02:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17879\tNRW_DS111296\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.884Z\t599\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhotographic collection from Yr Wyddfa Environmental Change Network (ECN) site\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323936\tA collection of slides, scans and digital photographs taken at the Environmental Change Network sites on Snowdon. The purpose of this data capture was to keep a photographic record of the site in order to study changes over time.\tPhotographs have been taken from 1995 onwards. Every week two panoramas are taken of the ECN site, one for looking at the number of sheep and the other to note the weather conditions and any obvious changes in vegetation. Photographs are also taken of any survey plots including the arctic alpine plots. When monitoring began at the site photographs were taken on film and kept as slides, most of which have now been scanned. Now all photographs are taken using digital cameras.\t\tDigital images are kept on the user drives of NRW\u0027s ECN team staff, whilst the slides are housed in filing cabinets in NRW\u0027s ECN team office.\tInternal Custodian: Alex Turner, Environmental Change Network Project Officer, Operational Support Group, CCW. Victoria Bowmaker, Environmental Change Network Data Officer, Operational Support Group, CCW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW / Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1995-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:50:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17881\tNRW_DS111297\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.886Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Surveys of the Carneddau (2001-2002)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323937\tThis survey was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales in order to map, describe and assess the vegetation of the western part of the Carneddau. The study forms part of the much larger Eryri Site of Special Scientific Interest Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI).\tThe vegetation was described using the National Vegetation Classification and mapped at the scale of 1:10000. The eastern parts of the Carneddau were surveyed in the summer of 2001 whilst the western parts were surveyed during 2002. Most types of vegetation were sampled by 2mx2m quadrats in which all species and he scale of their percentage cover as DOMIN values were recorded.\t\tHard copy reports are available in CCW libraries and TSG archive.\tBarbara Jones, Upland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:13:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17871\tNRW_DS111298\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.709Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Surveys of Proposed Extensions to the Eryri SAC\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323938\tThis data collation is the results from two studies researching a proposed extension to Eryri Special Area of Conservation (SAC) conducted in 2001 and 2002. The first survey was commissioned by Countryside Council for Wales in order to map and describe the vegetation of six upload areas adjacent to the existing Carneddau and Clydeiriau Site of Special Scientific Interest. The second survey built on the results from the 2001 studies and evaluated these against the requirements for SPA’s.\tIn 2001 four surveyors conducted a vegetation survey in accordance with the National Vegetation Classification standard. In 2002 this data was digitised into separate themes for storing polygon, quadrat and target note data.\t\tHard copy in CCW libraries and TSG archive.  Hard copy \u0026amp; electronic format with CCWs upland ecologist.\tBarbara Jones, Upland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Averis\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:13:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17880\tNRW_DS111299\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.900Z\t771\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHedgerow Database for Angelsey North Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131323939\tThe Hedgerow Database contains data from hedgerow surveys undertaken on Anglesey looking at the structure and composition of the hedgerows.  In 2002 a Hedgerow Survey Handbook was produced setting a standard procedure for local hedgerow surveys in the UK. It was proposed that a series of hedgerow surveys were to be undertaken in Wales using this standard procedure however as yet only the Anglesey survey has taken place due to funding constraints and therefore the database only currently contains data from this one survey. The purpose of this data capture was to build up a detailed body of information on the current status of hedgerows across the UK thus enabling appropriate action to be taken to safeguard the most important hedgerows, and to monitor any changes in the quality and quantity of the hedgerow network over time.\tA hedgerow survey method has been developed under a research contract for Countryside Council For Wales (CCW) producing a clearly defined system of data collection covering the setting of the hedge, associated features, its structure, and woody and ground flora components. The complete methodology guidance can be found in the handbook (see related titles below).\t\tNeeds secure location. Currently on a cd with Claire Burrows\tInternal Custodian: Claire Burrows, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17874\tNRW_DS111300\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.792Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTir Gofal Species Package Maps\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131333030\tThis dataset contains maps of key areas for species that are targeted in Tir Gofal (the Welsh Government\u0027s agri-environmental programme) and information sheets describing the prescriptions for each species.  The maps are designed to be used by Tir Gofal project officers in order to inform them of the distribution of species targeted by Tir Gofal. Data collation and map production exercise carried out during 2007.\tThe maps are maintained by NRW and supplied to the Welsh Government.\t\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Users/clare/TG targeting/final versions\tInternal Custodian: Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tButterfly Conservation-Plantlife-Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17868\tNRW_DS111301\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.669Z\t340\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGrazing Marsh Species Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131333031\tThis dataset is a collation of flora and fauna species records from grazing marshes in Wales. The purpose of this dataset was to collate information on the range of species found in Welsh grazing marshes in order to provide an inventory of the species and their distribution.\tRecords collated from a range of sources e.g. phase 1 survey of grazing marshes, invertebrate database, mammals database etc and entered into spreadsheet/database.\t\t? This data may have been lost\tInternal Custodian: Claire Burrows, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:01:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17873\tNRW_DS111311\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.791Z\t353\tValid\tEditable\tEstablishment of Permanent Quadrats at Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ )\tSefydlu Cwadratau Parhaol ym Mharth Cadwraeth Morol Sgomer (PCS)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131333131\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  A detailed survey of the shores of Skomer Marine Nature Reserve was carried out in 1982.   This dataset contains the results of an exercise carried out in 1992 to repeat some of the littoral transects surveyed in 1982 and establish permanent quadrats at six sites.   The purpose of this data capture was to:   - to establish six permanent littoral monitoring sites at locations surveyed in the 1982 littoral survey.   - survey the sites and where appropriate compare with the results of the 1982 littoral survey.   - photograph the general appearance of the site for relocation purposes and photograph the quadrats at specified intervals along the transects at each site.\tSix sites were selected from those originally surveyed in 1982 as being representative of very exposed, exposed and sheltered shores with north and south facing aspects. Permanent 0.25msq quadrats were marked at 1.2m vertical intervals using a compressed air drill, rawl plugs, brass screws and plastic discs or pitons and tapes, where the rock was too hard to drill. The abundance of animals and plant species were recorded using an abundance scale. Each quadrat was photographed using Kodak 64 or 200 ASA film and a close up taken of central decimetre square for subsequent counts of barnacle densities. Sketch maps were also created.   The six sites were located around the shore of Skomer Marine Nature Reserve. Two transects on relatively sheltered boulder shores in North and South Havens, two on semi-exposed north and south facing shores at South Stream Cliff East and the Lantern and two on extremely exposed sites- south facing shore in the Wick and north facing shore opposite the Garland Stone.   Initial reconnaissance was carried out on 26th July and the pilot transect at South Haven temporarily marked and surveyed on 30th July. All other transects were surveyed, marked and photographed during the period 12-18th of September. It was necessary to return on the 26th and 27th of September to survey quadrats at 0.6m on all transects during the low spring tide.   This data forms part of the Pembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas (URI: 24328).\tAssociated Report Title: Crump, R., 1993  Skomer Marine Nature Reserve littoral monitoring project (permanent quadrats). Countryside Council for Wales Science Report No. 3.\tPaper report is available from CCW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on CCWs copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000068)\tInternal Custodian and Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t1993-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T15:08:05.976Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17878\tNRW_DS111397\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.815Z\t286\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) Subtidal Monitoring Project - Animal Communities on Stones\tProsiect monitro islanwol Gwarchodfa Genedlaethol Forol Sgomer - cymunedau anifeiliaid ar gerrig\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131333937\tThe results of subtidal monitoring undertaken on animal communities found on stones and pebbles at a site within Skomer Marine Nature Reserve. The purpose of this data capture was to observe and record seasonal settlement and colonisation of pebble habitat by marine fauna and record the changes in abundance and where possible the condition of the animals collected over a year.\t6 visits were made to the site at two monthly intervals. During each visit two samples of stones were collected from the monitoring area using a quadrat of 25cm x 25cm. Notes of the abundance of conspicuous species were made in the immediate vicinity of the monitoring site and along a transect. Close up photographs were taken to provide extra details. Surface seawater temperatures were measured by means of a mercury thermometer. In the laboratory each stone was examined individually and attached fauna recorded. Where possible individuals were counted, but for colonial and encrusting species an alternative method was to note the presence of the species on a particular stone then count the number of stones in each sample to provide a measure of percentage occurrence. This data forms part of the Pembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas (see separate metadata entry).\t\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above.   Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000056)\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1988-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:49:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17869\tNRW_DS111406\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.670Z\t400\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Island Eunicella verrucosa Survey 1985\tArolwg Eunicella verrucosa Ynys Sgomer 1985\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343036\tThis dataset is a collation of the results of a survey aimed at studying the population of the broad sea fan Eunicella verrucosa at Skomer Marine Reserve. The dataset also contains data on a number of other species of interest and previously unsurveyed areas of the reserve. The purpose of this data capture was: -  Provide information on the distribution and abundance of E. verrucosa around Skomer and determine habitat preferences.  -  Investigate the size of individual E. verrucosa and the population age structure. -  Study the epibionts of E. verrucosa.  -  Study orientation and growth form of E. verrucosa colonies in a variety of environmental conditions. The survey also aimed to tag and photograph E. verrucosa for future monitoring, record the occurrence and abundance of various species of interest and survey some additional areas around Skomer Marine Reserve for which there was little or no information.\tThis data forms part of the Pembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas (see separate metadata entry). Seven sites around Skomer Marine Nature Reserve were chosen for survey, largely on the basis of information collected during previous surveys. Divers swam offshore from a given point on the coast along a particular compass bearing. The habitat on which each E. verrucosa was found was noted and SMR Habitat Recording Sheets were completed for most sites with profiles drawn. An assessment of E. verrucosa abundance was made by calculating the number recorded per 30 minute dive.  The maximum height and width of individual E. verrucosa was measured to the nearest centimetre and orientation was measured using wrist compasses. E. verrucosa were tagged using plastic electrical cable ties with numbers engraved. These were fixed onto side branches near to the main stem.  A number of target species were searched for and their location was normally shown on profiles and habitat sketches. 3 previously unsurveyed/poorly documented areas were also surveyed. Habitat description and brief notes on conspicuous species were recorded. Plant and animal species were recorded on checklists and abundances determined and photographs were taken. Samples for unidentified plants and animals were taken for later identification.\t\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above. Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000029)\tContact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1986-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:00:08.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17866\tNRW_DS111435\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.230Z\t345\tValid\tEditable\tResurvey of Permanent Quadrats at Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ ) Formally a Marine Nature Reserve ( MNR ) Following the Sea Empress Oil Spill\tAil arolwg cwadratau parhaol yng Ngwarchodfa Natur Forol Sgomer yn dilyn arllwysiad olew y Sea Empress\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343335\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990. The results of a survey carried out to re-examine permanent quadrats and transects established in Skomer Marine Nature Reserve in 1992 (see separate metadata \"Establishment of permanent quadrats at Skomer Marine Nature Reserve\") following the Sea Empress oil spill in February 1996.\tSix permanent transect were visited and a 50 cm x 50 cm square aluminium quadrat strung with cord to divide into 25 10 x 10cm squares was placed accurately on the permanent markers using a reference set of colour photocopies. Each quadrat was then photographed. In each quadrat the abundance of each animal and plant species present was assessed using a seven point abundance scale.   All identification was made in the field ensuring that material was not removed/disturbed. Because of this, identification is given to the nearest taxon.   This data forms part of the Pembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas (24328).\t\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above.Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000092)\tMetadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t1996-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:46:40.492Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17865\tNRW_DS111468\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.917Z\t392\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEffects of the Sea Empress oil spill on seaweed animals of the West Angle tide pools (1996-1997).\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343638\tThe results of a survey investigating the effects of oil pollution on the species richness of mobile fauna living in association with the algal turf in the West Angle Tide Pools. The study compares data collected from the polluted pools at West Angle with data collected from an unpolluted tide pool system in South Devon. The purpose of this data capture was to discover the effects of the Sea Empress oil spill on the fauna of the Coralline alga turf fringing the tide pool system at West Angle Bay, Pembrokeshire.  Samples were collected over a 14 month period beginning in April 1996. Samples were taken in April, June, July and October 1996 and in March and June 1997. Samples were taken from the Devon site for April, June and July 1996.\tSingle samples of weed were collected from the pools by completely clearing an area approximately 10 cm by 10 cm using a paint scraper. Such samples were equivalent in volume to approximately 200 mls of weed. A single sample only was collected on each occasion to minimize the impact of this destructive sampling on the pool system.  The samples were removed to a laboratory where the animals were carefully extracted from the weed, identified and counted. The volume of weed was determined by displacement. The lack of replication affects the reliability of results.\tSee related report title: Effects of the Sea Empress oil spill on seaweed animals of the West Angle tide pools.\tPaper report is available from NRW library, see \u0027related titles\u0027 field above BarCode 14750 Maes y Ffynnon, Bangor. Data available on NRWs copy of Marine Recorder (MRCCW10000000085)\tInternal Custodian and metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tSea Empress Environmental Evaluation Committee (SEEEC)\t\t1997-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:30.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17867\tNRW_DS111487\t2024-05-28T12:34:04.158Z\t566\tValid\tEditable\tVolunteer Diver Seagrass Zostera marina Surveys Porth Dinllaen (2009)\tArolygon gan ddeifars gwirfoddol ar wellt y gamlas Zostera marina, Pen Llyn ac Aberdaugleddau 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343837\tSeagrass is the only marine flowering plant found subtidally in the UK and seagrass beds are considered of National importance.  In 2009 Zostera marina was the focus of former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), volunteer dive surveys at Porth Dinllaen in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) to estimate the extent of the bed. The survey is a continuation of a 2008 pilot, and is due to be re-surveyed before 2012 to start to build up a picture of variation in the extent of the seagrass bed at the site. The main objectives of this project were to: 1. Test methods to measure extent of large seagrass beds using volunteers 2. Estimate density and measure depth of live seagrass in different parts of the bed 3. Make note of any unusual features of the seagrass bed 4. Compare with results and success of 2008 surveys\tData is captured by using volunteer divers who are CMAS 2 star qualified or equivalent with a minimum of 60 dives each. Volunteers estimated the extent of large seagrass beds using rapid unmarked transects analysis, using a GPS surface tracking buoy, noting the time and depth of features of the seagrass bed (eg: where the bed starts or stops, or the position of \u0027mooring scars\u0027). This method was compared with the success when some divers swam towards a fixed underwater acoustic marker (transponder) with a homing device, noting the distance and bearing to the transponder to note the key features. Wherever possible, quadrats were taken at stations along transects and the presence of flowering, seeding and wasting were noted, together with any associated, potentially threatening or BAP species. Survey methods and success were compared to the 2008 survey methods and results, and the technique\u0027s feasibility for future volunteer surveys discussed.\t\tData is archived as project 229, media no. 598.  A working copy of the data is held on the MFSG shared drive.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:50:53.286Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17864\tNRW_DS111488\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.799Z\t383\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Regional Data: West Wales (West Wales Biodiversity Information Centre - WWBIC)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343838\tThis dataset is made up of a collation of data (sensitive species only) derived from Natural Resources Wales (formerly Countryside Council for Wales) regional offices in West Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to mobilise a valuable data resource in order to provide baseline data to inform conservation work, research, site management and decision-making within NRW and externally. Records are for NRW\u0027s West region which includes Carmarthenshire, Pembrokeshire and Ceredigion. Many records are for designated sites and a proportion are from the wider countryside. The majority of records have six figure grid references, some are site centroids.\tThe dataset was created from species records extracted, computerised and collated from paper records held at NRW\u0027s regional offices at Llandeilo, Aberystwyth and Pembroke Dock.  The work was carried out under contract by WWBIC staff; Laura Burnett, Madeline Tasker and Vicky Swann from 2007 to 2010 as part of NRW\u0027s data mobilisation project.  The material from which records were derived included the following: reports, site registers, recording cards, protected site condition assessments, other general survey work and records of casual observations.  Numerous biological recording techniques and survey methodologies were used in the collection of these records.  Some of the records were produced by NRW staff or by qualified contractors working on NRW\u0027s behalf whilst some records have been provided by third parties, or derived from documents, surveys or datasets produced by others, but held in NRW files. Due to the nature of the files and the lack of documentation it has not been possible to determine what proportion of the records within the dataset are NRW owned against third party data. The majority of records were created by NRW staff, or qualified contractors, or other acknowleged experts and are generally of a good standard. These records have not yet been subjected to systematic verification. The computerisation of these records has been subject to some validation. Data should be checked against original sources before use in contentious circumstance.\t\tWest Wales Biodiversity Information Centre  LRC hold the physical data set on their server. If you want extra information about the LERCs contact: Enterprise Data Management Specialist Advisor - Barny Letheren.   X:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to WWBIC.lyr\tWWBIC are the custodians on behalf of NRW Ruth Harding is the main lead with WWBIC  This data will be incoprorated in the GIS layers that NRW also received\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:38.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17863\tNRW_DS111489\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.802Z\t453\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Regional Data: North Wales (North Wales Environmental Information Services - Cofnod)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131343839\tThis dataset is a collation of records derived from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) (formerly Countryside Council for Wales) North Region paper files and entered by staff of Natural Resources Wales onto the Cofnod (North Wales Environmental Information Service) Online Recording System. Records are primarily for the NRW\u0027s North Region which includes the following Local Authority areas: Anglesey, Gwynedd, Conwy, Denbighshire, Flintshire and Wrexham. However there are some records for other areas. Many records are for designated sites (including Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), Special Area of Conservation (SACs), National Nature Reserves (NNRs) etc), however, there are also large numbers of records from the wider countryside. The majority of the records have grid references of at least 100m accuracy (6 figure), however some of these may be derived from site centroids rather than refer to the actual location of the species. The majority of the records were originally created by NRW staff or qualified contractors. They have been entered onto computer by trained Cofnod staff or volunteers using Cofnod\u0027s custom built Data Entry Systems which use standard lists where possible to ensure consistent terminology. Randomised Quality Assurance checks have been carried out on much of the data (apart from that entered directly by NRW staff). All records have been screened to ensure that they have valid dates, species names and grid references. The majority of these records are subject to systematic verification by relevant local experts, and are labelled accordingly (within an additional Attribute field). Individual records have a default verification level of \u0027Assigned\u0027 or \u0027Unassessed\u0027 until they have been looked at by a Verifier, after which they may be re-labelled as \u0027Unconfirmed\u0027 or \u0027Considered Correct by Expert\u0027. Some records known to be from a trusted source are labelled as \u0027Considered Correct by Cofnod\u0027. Records identified by Verifiers as \u0027Known/Probably Incorrect\u0027 will be removed from the NBN Gateway. Further details are given within relevant Cofnod policies. Data should be checked against original sources before use in contentious circumstances.\tThis dataset was originally created from species records extracted, computerised and collated from paper records held at NRW\u0027s Regional office files. The work was mostly carried out by Cofnod Staff Members, between 2007 and 2010, as part of NRW\u0027s Data Mobilisation Project. Offices covered were Dolgellau, Bangor and Mold. Additional records have been captured by Cofnod volunteers and entered directly onto the Cofnod Online Recording System by staff of National Resources Wales. The material from which records were derived included the following: reports, site registers, recording cards, protected site condition assessments, Phase 1 and Phase 2 habitat surveys, other general survey work and records of casual observations. Numerous biological recording techniques and survey methodologies were used in the collection of these records. Some of the records were produced by NRW staff or by qualified contractors working on NRW\u0027s behalf whilst some records have been provided by third parties, or derived from documents, surveys or datasets produced by others, but held in NRW files. Wherever third party records could be readily and practically identified, they have been removed from this dataset.\t\tCofnod LRC hold the physical collation on their server. If you want extra information about the LERCs contact: Enterprise Data Management Specialist Advisor - Barny Letheren.   X:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to COFNOD point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to COFNOD region.lyr\tCofnod is the cutsodian Huw Jones is the main lead with Cofnod This data will be incoprorated in the GIS layers that NRW also received\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17862\tNRW_DS111508\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.793Z\t636\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAccess Maps - Promoted Local Routes\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131353038\tThis dataset is one of a number of MapInfo tables that are used to deliver the Welsh National Access Register (WNAR) project.  The collation and presentation of data on a number of routes and trails throughout Wales in an easy-to-view format enables people to find out more about routes of interest and their accessibility.  The dataset was first created in 2005. It is maintained and added to regularly during the year due to changing partnership information as well as in response to annual agreement partnership checks.   This project helps to deliver the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) \u0027PE04 Enjoying the Outdoors\u0027  programme output \u0027More people from all sectors of society will regularly be using and benefiting from widespread and equitable access to countryside, coast and urban green-space\u0027.  The overall aim of the project was to get more people (1% annual increase) from all sectors of society, regularly participating in open-air recreation and exercise. The objectives from this program output are:  1. Increase availability of high quality recreation resource, including public and other urban green-space, without compromising environmental and cultural interests  2. Provide readily available, targeted information about recreation opportunities and encourage greater awareness of rights and responsibility that will lead to positive behaviour changes  3. Provide a means of finding accommodation alongside the recreational opportunities in discussion with our partners.\tAround 20 partners (e.g. local authorities, National Parks, National Trust) agreed to provide the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) with up to date information about trails and routes that they manage.\t\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Access\\Access_Land\\WNAR\tJill Meyer, Access Information Development Officer, Access Recreation and Communication Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17860\tNRW_DS111509\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.692Z\t594\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAccess Maps - Sites and Reserves\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131353039\tThis dataset is one of a number of GIS tables that are used to deliver the Welsh National Access Register (WNAR) project. The collation and presentation of data on a number of sites throughout Wales in an easy-to-view format enables people to find out more about places of interest and their accessibility. The dataset was first created in 2005. It is maintained and added to regularly during the year due to changing partnership information as well as in response to annual agreement partnership checks.  This ensures we display the information that our partners want to promote as well as keeping broken URL links to a minimum.  This project helps to deliver the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) \u0027PE04 Enjoying the Outdoors\u0027  programme output \u0027More people from all sectors of society will regularly be using and benefiting from widespread and equitable access to countryside, coast and urban green-space\u0027.  The overall aim of the project was to get more people (1% annual increase) from all sectors of society, regularly participating in open-air recreation and exercise. The objectives from this program output are:  1. Increase availability of high quality recreation resource, including public and other urban green-space, without compromising environmental and cultural interests  2. Provide readily available, targeted information about recreation opportunities and encourage greater awareness of rights and responsibility that will lead to positive behaviour changes  3. Provide a means of finding accommodation alongside the recreational opportunities in discussion with our partners.\tAround 35 partners (e.g. RSPB, the Wildlife Trusts, CADW) agreed to provide the former CCW with up to date information about the sites that they manage.\t\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Access\\Access_Land\\WNAR\tInternal Custodian:  Jill Meyer, Access Information Development Officer, Access Recreation and Communication Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tVarious\t\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:02:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17861\tNRW_DS111511\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.681Z\t653\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAccess Maps - Permissive Access in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131353131\tThis dataset contains permissive access land in Wales. This includes land under Tir Gofal agreement, which give access to specified parts of the farm, National Trust land and National Park access agreements and means that the landowner or tenant of the land, has agreed to allow public access for open-air recreation, normally on foot. The purpose of this data capture was to enable people to be confident of their access rights when visiting the Welsh countryside.\tThe Tir Gofal data was supplied to former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now Natural Resources Wales every one to two months by WAG. The data is then manipulated to be in the correct format, stored as a GIS layer.\t\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Access\\Access_Land\\Permissive Access\tInternal Custodian:  Jill Meyer, Access Information Development Officer, Access Recreation and Communication Group, NRW  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\t\t\t2014-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17858\tNRW_DS111562\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.661Z\t699\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFluvial Audit of the Afon Tywi\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131353632\tThe Afon Tywi in Carmarthenshire is a large actively eroding river, designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for its migratory fish, and as a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) for various features including river habitat, river shingle invertebrates and little ringed plover. The river is unusually active in the middle reaches, sometimes moving as much as 50 m in a single year, and the various species for which the river is designated depend on the river remaining active. In order to inform current and future river management, a fluvial audit of the past and present geomorophology of the Afon Tywi was carried out. This consists of a detailed technical report including identification of the most active river sections based on historic maps, a timeline of the river indicating key events such as major floods, and maps showing the geomorphology and sediment character of the river. Advice is also provided on the future sustainable management of the Tywi as an active shingle river. This dataset consists of the results from the Fluvial Audit of the Afon Tywi which describes and spatially maps the past and present fluvial geomorphology of the Tywi catchment. The aim of this data capture was to:  - Develop an understanding of the dynamic geomorphology of the Afon Tywi. Specific aims include: - Describe and map the fluvial geomorphology of the Tywi catchment past and present using a GIS system. - Interpret the data gathered in the context of past and present human activity, including a risk assessment of consented activities. - Provide advice on the future management of the Tywi SAC for use as a management and casework decision-making tool with regards to future bank reinforcement work, flood defence activities, aggregate extractions etc.\tThe project had numerous stages as follows: Stage 1: Catchment Overview - literature study and GIS analysis of the catchment characteristics. Stage 2: Hydrological Assessment. Stage 3: The Fluvial Audit - Map and aerial photograph-based assessment; Field-based assessment; Detailed survey of sediment character; Linking map, field-based and laboratory results; Geomorphic impact of floods; Assessing sediment stores on the Afon Tywi; Impacts of human activity (including information from questionnaire of riparian users); Detailed analysis of case-study reaches. Stage 4: Sensitivity assessment to help evaluate risk - Analysis of sediment balance at the points of gravel extraction; Strategy for assessing the impact of existing and potential gravel extraction. Stage 5: Sediment transport modelling. Stage 6: Risk assessment and management advice.\t\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\hydrology\\Hydrology_Reports\\fluvial_morphology\\Tywi Fluvial AuditData    Archived under Project 257 Media 951.  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media901to1000\\Media951\tInternal Custodians: Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Senior Freshwater Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group Rhian Thomas, Freshwater Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group  Metadata Contact: Data Services for Archived Data or GIS and Spatial Data Team for GIS data locations\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1953-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-09-03T10:00:58.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17859\tNRW_DS111625\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.693Z\t429\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Regional Data: Mid-Wales ( Biodiversity Information Service for Powys and Brecon Beacons - BIS )\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131363235\tAll records collated from species records mobilised from former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) regional files, held at Newtown, Abergavenny and Llandrindod Wells offices. This dataset was created from species records extracted, computerised and collated from paper records held at former CCW\u0027s Regional office files covering the Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park area. The work was carried out under contract by ten Biodiversity Information Service for Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park (BIS) staff members within the NRW offices between 2003 and 2009, as part of CCW Data Mobilisation Project. However, coverage was not complete, as follows:- Newtown: completed 10Km square files and partial completed Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) files, Llandrindod Wells: General files complete. All SSSI files complete Abergavenny: 10km square files complete. SSSI complete. Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and National Nature Reserve (NNR) files not completed Records were entered into Recorder 2002 or Recorder 6 databases.  The majority of records are from Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park or within a 5km buffer. Many records are for designated sites (including SSSIs, SACs, National Nature Reserves etc), however, there are also large numbers of records from the wider countryside.  The grid references entered will be at resolution of original record and could be 2 or 4 figures, however, the majority of the records have 6 figure grid-references. Some grid references may be from site centroids rather than actual location of the species in question or may have been estimated from map. The purpose of this data capture was to create an electronic archive of species data held on CCW regional files and mobilise this data through BIS.  Confidence in data: The source of the original data has not been checked however much of the data originates from CCW staff or other trusted sources. Most of this data has not gone through external verification process but BIS will continue to work with VC recorders to verify records. Recorder has its own validation checks and will not accept data without who, what, where, when present. Some of the grid references entered could be \u0027estimated from map\u0027 if they were not provided. The grid references entered will be at resolution of original record and could be 2 or 4 figures. To avoid duplication each record was checked to see if already in BIS database using MapInfo GIS layer. At this time the operator could check that the grid reference matches location name and is within designated site boundary if appropriate. Operators checked their own entries for accuracy, as resources did not allow for a second operator check. CCW staffs were on hand to help with handwriting or taxonomic issues. Most of the operators on this project originally trained in BIS entering data, and had biological or ecological backgrounds. BIS has recently worked through the surveys to remove outlier records and resolve grid reference issues where possible and will continue to work to remove any duplication.\tNumerous biological recording techniques and survey methodologies were used in the collection of these records. The material from which records were derived included the following: reports, site registers, recording cards, protected site condition assessments, Phase 1 and Phase 2 habitat surveys, other general survey work and records of casual observations. Some of the records were produced by CCW staff or by qualified contractors working on CCW\u0027s behalf whilst some records have been provided by third parties, or derived from documents, surveys or datasets produced by others, but held in CCW files. Due to the nature of the files and the lack of documentation it has not been possible to determine what proportion of the records within the dataset are NRW owned against third party data. Data also includes species records from former CCW Phase 1 target notes for Breconshire, Montgomeryshire and Radnorshire digitised within BIS offices, not under contract. Newtown: completed 10Km square files and partial completed SSSI files, Llandrindod Wells:  General files complete. All SSSI files complete  Abergavenny: 10km square files complete. SSSI complete. SAC and NNR files not completed.\t\tX:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to BIS point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to BIS region.lyr   BIS LERC hold and manage the top copy of some NRW owned species data. Some of this data has its own metadata field (Contains Field) and other data doesn\u0027t. All NRW owned data which is managed by BIS is available to download on the NBN Gateway.   116719 - CCW Montgomeryshire General Bat Files WA0001500000001O\tInternal Custodian: Monica Jones, Team Leader, Data Services External Custodian: BIS  Additional information is available from BIS www.b-i-s.org  Janet Imlach janet@bis.org.uk Unit 4, 6 The Bulwark, Brecon, Powys www.bis.org.uk   Andrew Lucas is the NRW contact with BIS\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:46.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17857\tNRW_DS111647\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.488Z\t325\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAccess Maps - Promoted Regional Routes\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131363437\tThis data set is one of a number of MapInfo tables that are used to deliver the Welsh National Access Register (WNAR) project.  The collation and presentation of data on a number of routes and trails throughout Wales in an easy-to-view format enables people to find out more about places of interest and their accessibility. The dataset was first created in 2005. It is maintained and added to regularly during the year due to changing partnership information as well as in response to annual agreement partnership checks. This project helps to deliver the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) \u0027PE04 Enjoying the Outdoors\u0027  programme output \u0027More people from all sectors of society will regularly be using and benefiting from widespread and equitable access to countryside, coast and urban green-space\u0027.  The overall aim of the project was to get more people (1% annual increase) from all sectors of society, regularly participating in open-air recreation and exercise. 1. Increase availability of high quality recreation resource, including public and other urban green-space, without compromising environmental and cultural interests  2. Provide readily available, targeted information about recreation opportunities and encourage greater awareness of rights and responsibility that will lead to positive behaviour changes  3. Provide a means of finding accommodation alongside the recreational opportunities in discussion with our partners.\tAround 20 partners (e.g. local authorities, National Parks, National Trust) agreed to provide the former CCW with up to date information about trails and routes that they manage.\t\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Access\\Access_Land\\WNAR\tInternal Custodian: Jill Meyer, Access Information Development Officer, Access Recreation and Communication Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tVarious-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:01:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17856\tNRW_DS111769\t2024-05-28T12:34:03.413Z\t439\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSite Actions held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131373639\tThe Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database contains the actions that need to be carried out on sites to achieve appropriate conservation management The purpose of this data capture was to document and share information on who needs to do what, where, in order to get special sites into favourable condition. There are numerous management units involved (7,000 plus), there are many organisations whose work needs to be coordinated, and there are many different types of issues and actions needed. In practice a database is the most efficient way of collection and organising this information. The Actions Database project was initiated in May 2008. Data entry began in November 2008 and is ongoing.\tSites have been divided up into management units based on practical boundaries visible on the ground, often based on ownership boundaries. For each unit, number, name, lead officer, special features, issues, actions needed etc are recorded in the database.  Prioritised Improvement Plans (PIPs) are produced and updated from information held in the database.  These are prioritised, costed action plans for each Natura 2000 site in Wales that are used to help maintain or improve the condition of the designated habitat and species features of the site.  This system was decommissioned and data moved into new NRW systems, such as AMX and CRM. Please contact the Data Custodians for futher information.\t\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.  Can be accessed via extranet by third parties who are responsible for actions. This is a legacy CCW Database and therefore Access maybe limited to a certain number of people.\tChristine Edwards, Strategic Project Manager, Operational Support Group, NRW Access to the database is provided under licence only through Gill Walker, Data Manager Sites       All requests for this data should be referred to Gill Walker.   Metadata will not be set to export to data.gov due to number of restriction son the data and access to the database\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:16:51.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17912\tNRW_DS111788\t2024-05-28T12:34:44.071Z\t618\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPredicting the displacement of common scoter Melanitta nigra from benthic feeding areas due to offshore windfarms\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131373838\tThis study used field observations and surveys combined with an individual-based modelling approach to predict the change in over-wintering mortality rates of common scoter that would result from the displacement of birds from potential feeding habitat through the avoidance of windfarms in Liverpool Bay. The purposes of this data capture were to: -  Develop a model to assist in predicting the effect of offshore windfarms on common scoter due to habitat loss and change. -  Link the non-breeding distribution of common scoter with environmental variables at selected sites -  Identify the characteristics of preferred feeding areas for common scoter within these sites.\tA description of the physical habitat utilised by common scoter using oceanographic modelling: -  Quantification of the spatial and temporal variability in prey -  Observation of the behaviour of birds at sea and their response to disturbance -  Quantification of other forms of disturbance relevant to common scoter -  A review of diving duck literature -  Development of a behavioural model to predict responses to windfarms -  Calibration and validation of the behavioural model -  Predicting the consequences of offshore windfarm developments -  Making recommendations for future research requirements\t\tThis dataset is maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW HQ as Project 188 media numbers 530 \u0026amp; 531.\tInternal Custodian: Matt Murphy, Ornithologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tCollaborative Offshore Windfarm Research Into the Environment (COWRIE)\t\t2006-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:02.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17907\tNRW_DS111793\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.663Z\t441\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommon Scoter (Melanitta nigra) Infaunal Data Collected During Academic Research Projects\tData am for-hwyaid duon a gasglwyd yn ystod prosiectau ymchwil graddedigion\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131373933\tCarmarthen Bay, an extensive, shallow embayment off the south coast of Wales, UK, encompasses a wide range of rich and diverse coastal and marine habitats. Carmarthen Bay is an internationally important wintering ground for the Common Scoter duck (Melanitta nigra) and is widely regarded as the most important such site in the UK causing the site to be proposed as a Special Protection Area (pSPA) under the EC Birds Directive. The Common Scoter is strongly migratory and often travels considerable distances over land making brief stop-overs on inland waters. Its diet consists predominantly of molluscs, especially during the winter, although it occasionally takes other aquatic invertebrates such as crustaceans (e.g. barnacles and shrimps), worms, echinoderms, isopods, amphipods and insects (e.g. midges and caddis flies) as well as small fish and fish eggs. The large concentrations of this species that occur during the moulting period and in winter are highly vulnerable to oil spills, human disturbance and the degradation of food resources as a result of oil exploration. The need for a comprehensive baseline survey of the macro fauna communities of Carmarthen Bay became a priority for government agencies such as the Countryside Council for Wales in the wake of the Sea Empress oil spill February 1996. The long-term presence of certain infaunal species and the stability of the areas populations of the species may be a factor in the importance of the Carmarthen Bay is an internationally important overwintering site for the common scoter.  This dataset consists of an extensive series of surveys of the macrofauna around Carmarthen Bay and was collated to identify environmental and / or biotic conditions relating to areas where scoter number were high or low. The majority of this dataset is data generated as part of a Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) funded PhD for the University of Wales Swansea known as \\Carmarthen Bay infauna scoter project\\\". It also includes data related to a University of Wales Swansea MSc Environmental biology dissertation entitled \u0027Common Scoter Melanitta nigra in their wintering habitats; an investigation into the factors influencing their finer scale distribution\u0027. Data was collected by students with CCW involvement or supervision.\"\tThis is a collation of data from various student projects. The baseline survey of Carmarthen Bay was carried out from the 1st to the 3 June 1998 using the RV Prince Madog (School of Ocean sciences, University of Bangor Wales). This stratified survey consisted of 29 quantitative (three replica grabs sieved through a 0.5 mm mesh) and 31 qualitative (single grabs sieved through a 1.0 mm mesh) stations. These grab samples were also used in sediment analysis and organic content analyses. A further eight qualitative beam trawls were taken to assess populations of large epifauna. To aid the interpretation of the macrofauna data, contour maps illustrating the physical environment the be produced from a large sedimentary and environmental dataset. In addition to this the interpolated grid files which form base sis of the contour plots of hydrographic and sedimentary environment Carmarthen Bay produced using the natural neighbour interpolation model (Voronoi diagram).  These results are all presented in full details in the following PhD Thesis: A.P Woolmer 2003. The benthic ecology of Carmarthen Bay. Unpublished PhD thesis. University of Wales, Swansea / Countryside Council for Wales Marine Monitoring Report No: 10, 374pp.\tThere are three Interim Reports available and the full PhD Thesis from the Natural Resources Wales Library. See Related Titles for more information.\tData is stored as Project No.45 media number 221, and Project 2 media number 3.  The MSc project is archived as project 4 media number 227.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader Metadata Contact: Data Services Team\tUniversity of Wales Swansea. Marine and Environmental Research Group-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:01:23.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17908\tNRW_DS111800\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.619Z\t601\tValid\tEditable\tCommon Scoter Survey (Melanitta nigra) in Liverpool Bay (2000-2001)\tArolwg o for-hwyaid duon ym Mae Lerpwl 2000-2001\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383030\tThe Common Scoter is strongly migratory and often travels considerable distances over land making brief stop-overs on inland waters. It is currently listed under the Birds Directive (Annex 2) Convention on Migratory Species (Bonn Convention Appendix 2) and is featured on the UK red-listed bird of conservation concern (for the breeding population) and a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Species for conservation and is a Schedule 1 species under the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981.    The majority of wintering common scoter recorded around the Welsh coast around Carmarthen Bay, North Cardigan Bay and the North Wales coast, which in combination are thought to hold 30-36,000 birds. A further 10 sites regularly support 1000 or more birds.   These are no known major concentrations away from these 13 sites, although this may be a consequence of the lack of appropriate survey. Large numbers of birds are recorded at several sites in April and May and there are significant post-breeding gatherings at three sites, including Carmarthen Bay, from August onwards. Casual observations suggest that these sites are not moulting grounds for the majority of birds. However there maybe as yet undetected moulting sites further offshore, as is the case in Denmark.    The purpose of this data capture was to record the numbers and distribution of common scoter in the Liverpool Bay; to determine the accuracy of the two survey methods (aerial and land-based) and the correlation between the two; and to provide recommendations on future monitoring of common scoter in Liverpool bay.\tFour aerial surveys were completed, monthly between November 2000 and February 2001. Synchronous land-based counts were made on some dates, although complete coverage from all planned observation points was never achieved. Aerial surveys covered between 702-1142 km2 whilst land-based observations were over an estimated sea area of between 60-120 km2\t\tData is held as Project 44, media number 91 Further work in Liverpool Bay has included the Development of a methodology for the assessment of cumulative effects of marine activities using Liverpool Bay as a case study, held as Media No.80.\tData Custodian: Marine Ornathologist, NRW or Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:51:43.435Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17911\tNRW_DS111813\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.875Z\t1200\tValid\tEditable\tAtlas of the Marine Mammals of Wales\tAtlas Mamaliaid Morol Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383133\tWelsh water support nationally and internationally important populations of marine mammals (whales, dolphins and seals) which use the area at various stages of their life histories such as feeding and breeding. Since the early 1990s the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) has been a major supporter of studies that have recorded the distribution and abundance of marine mammals in Wales.   These studies, often undertaken using dedicated networks of volunteers, have helped to inform conservation objectives for Special Areas of Conservation (SAC), monitoring targets and the assessment of proposed developments and impacts.  This dataset brings together the best available effort-related survey data on marine mammals in Welsh waters and adjacent parts of the Irish Sea, to provide the highest resolution analysis carried out to date of the spatial and temporal variation in their distribution. The Atlas contains distribution maps, including variations both seasonally and over the long terms of the five most common species (harbour porpoise, bottlenose dolphin, short-beaked common dolphin, Risso\u0027s dolphin and minke whale).  The purpose of this data capture is to describe the temporal distribution, spatial distribution and relative abundance of all marine mammal species sighted, detected or stranded in Welsh waters.   This dataset is concentrated on species regularly sighted and is designed to help meet obligations under the EU Habitats Directive. In addition is help former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now Natural Resources Wales (NRW)  advise on marine activities including renewable energy exploitation, fisheries, waterborne recreation and oil and gas exploitation. The study area consists primarily of Welsh territorial seas out to the median line with Ireland and the Isle of Man, and English territorial seas.  It was assumed that data contributed were reliable with respect to species identification and had been checked for errors in date, time and position fields; however factors affecting sight ability, survey characteristics and ecological factors may have affected sighting rates. Survey effort remains inadequate in all but a few small areas.\tAs with almost any data covered in the marine environment, there are variations in, coverage and confidence levels. Great care has been taken in the analysis to minimise errors by. The maps have been carefully interpreted, counting for variations in effort and other factors.  Sixteen groups or survey projects have contributed to the project database that forms the basis of this Atlas. The minimum requirement for data contributed to the project were that they should include associated effort data, including data for effort periods when no sightings were made and that the effort data should include records of sea state. Approximately 216,031 hours of effort are from vessel and aerial surveys and 133,999 hours of land based effort are analysed spanning a 20 year period.    The project database contains 12 species.  In addition the Welsh Strandings Database managed by Marine Environmental Monitoring was reviewed. As well as this grey seal data were sourced from CCW comprising of approximately 3500 individuals.  A grid with resolution of 10\u0027 latitude and 10\u0027 longitude was used, and effort partitioned into segments that could be assigned to a particular grid cell. Effort and sightings formed three separate tables (vessel, aerial and land) and these were then linked to a third table holding data on position and sea area of each cell.  Count rates of animals at the surface were calculated for the 5 regular species. Edition 1 of the Atlas was produced in 2009.  The report and data was subject to a detailed peer review by international scientific experts with the supervision of CCW and JNCC staff.  A second edition of the Atlas was subsequently produced in 2012.  Information Warning: The data is for sightings per hour (relative abundance) absolute densities cannot be derived from the data but relative abundance and distribution may be visualised\t\t[A]  This dataset is archived as Project 200, Media 565 (edition 1) and Media 1317 (edition 2) on backed-up server based storage at NRW, Maes y Fynnon. [B]  The report is available from NRW libraries (see related titles field above) and within the NRW Reports Archive located on the DMS.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2012-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-18T14:33:47.000Z\tnobody nobody\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17896\tNRW_DS111815\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.448Z\t335\tUnassessed\tEditable\tConnectivity of Marine Habitats - A Review of Vulnerable Species (1960 - 2008)\tY Cysylltiad rhwng Cynefinoedd Morol - nodi rhywogaethau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383135\tThe ‘connectivity’ of marine benthic organisms is important as it has potential implications for designation and management of MPAs and is also likely to feed into work on the management of the wider environment through Marine Planning, which will be developed in Welsh waters in the near future. Connectivity is defined here as the exchange of sufficient organisms via dispersal and movement to sustain populations.    Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) contracted Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd. (Marine EcoSol) to undertake the first stages of analysis, primarily to review species that may be vulnerable to low connectivity, or fragmentation, in Welsh waters. The vulnerability to connectivity project (presented in the current report) aimed to identify marine invertebrates, macroalgae, plants and small territorial fish which are theoretically vulnerable to low connectivity and fragmentation, and flag those that might need to be carefully considered when managing the marine environment around Wales. This dataset contains the results of  the \u0027vulnerability to connectivity project\u0027,  a five staged review of existing information which highlighted species that may be vulnerable to poor connectivity, or fragmentation in Welsh Waters based on information easily available in 2009. Connectivity is now generally recognised as an important criterion in the design of networks of Marine Protected Areas. Historical data was reviewed up to November 2008, with the earliest data point recorded in 1960.\tOf over 400 taxa originally considered for assessment, only 192 had sufficient information for full biological assessment. Species\u0027 vulnerability was assessed based on their biological characteristics and was approached in four stages: - A review of information available for each species (from BIOTIC URL  and the scientific literature)  - Development of a method of scoring many species\u0027 theoretical \u0027intrinsic\u0027, or biological connectivity  - Assessment of each species\u0027 specific habitat preference and the availability of this habitat in Wales - Assessment of species\u0027 perceived sensitivity to anthropogenic impacts and environmental change. Confidence in data is high however when interpreting the results, the assumptions made by the methods must be considered. Another consideration is the difficulty in identifying the species of interest; some species may be under-recorded due to mis-identifications or lack of observations.  In addition, it is extremely important to remember that this project did not assess all benthic species found in Welsh waters but only those for which sufficient data was available.\t\tArchived as project number 259, media number 955 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 955)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services\tJoint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:52:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17898\tNRW_DS111816\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.567Z\t572\tValid\tEditable\tMapping Marine Benthic Biodiversity in Wales\tMapio Bioamrywiaeth Fenthig Forol yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383136\tThe UK is committed through international agreements and European obligations to the establishment of an ecologically coherent network of Marine Protected Areas (MPAs) to conserve marine ecosystems and biodiversity. The identification of areas of high biodiversity may be helpful for planning both Marine Protected Areas and for Marine Spatial Planning and can contribute to an ecosystem-based approach to the management of our seas. Furthermore, identifying which areas are most important for biodiversity not only yields benefits for the maintenance of ecosystem structure and functioning but can also enable cost effective prioritisation of areas for marine protection.   Each data layer produced in relation to this project has its own metadata entry. The main objective of the project is to provide maps indicating different levels of biodiversity in Welsh waters (including the intertidal). The data covers the Welsh coastline from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.\tA method was developed specifically for application to the territorial waters of Wales.  This comprised four key stages:  1) data collation and quality assessment; 2) assessment of appropriate scale of grid cells;  3) analysis and generation of biodiversity measures; and  4) validation measures and confidence assessment.  This project builds on previous studies at a UK-wide and regional level (Hiscock and Breckels 2007, Langmead et al. 2008) to develop an approach for identifying marine biodiversity hotspots and apply it to Wales\u0027 sea area.  Confidence rating were applied in the final analysis, based on the quality and quantity of the data used and confidence GIS layers produced.\t\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:54:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17904\tNRW_DS111834\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.587Z\t359\tValid\tEditable\tSt Bride\u0027s Bay Sublittoral Sediment Benthic Survey (2000)\tArolwg benthig o waddodion isarforol ym Mae Sain Ffraid 2000\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383334\tThe purpose of this data capture was to gain an understanding of the benthic sediment ecology of St Brides Bay in order to contribute to the development of conservation objectives for the Pembrokeshire Marine candidate Special Area of Conservation (SAC). During the summer of 2000 Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) investigated St Bride\u0027s Bay using a variety of survey techniques. This dataset contains the results of the analyses of biological data from grab and anchor dredge samples, and examines the community types present by comparison with those listed in the Marine Biotope Classification of Britain and Ireland.\tStandard laboratory procedures were used to produce data spreadsheets showing the distribution of species across the bay in grab and dredge samples, and also sediment characteristics at each sample station.\t\tData is held as Project No. 288, Media No. 1191.  Marine Recorder survey MRCCW30000000002.\tInternal Custodian: West Region Senior Marine Conservation Officer, NRW All other data held by Anne Bunker, West Region.  Metadata custodian: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2000-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T09:09:11.318Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17915\tNRW_DS111860\t2024-05-28T12:34:44.002Z\t859\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFluvial Audit of the River Wye and Tributaries\tArchwiliad afonol o lednentydd afon Gwy\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383630\tThis dataset consists of the results of a Fluvial Geomorphological Audit of the River Wye and it\u0027s Tributaries. The river Wye is one of the largest rivers in England and Wales, and supports a range of rare and threatened habitats and species. The river includes several Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). It is also designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC).   The purpose of this data capture was to characterise the geomorphology of the watercourses surveyed and to identify key areas for monitoring, management and restoration, particularly in relation to the desired physical habitats for the SAC species.   The area covered included 305 km of the River Wye and selected tributaries between Pant Mawr and Hay-on-Wye. Tributaries included Aran, Bachawy, Bachell Brook, Camddwr, Cletwr Brook, Clywedog Brook, Colwyn Brook, Duhonw, Dulas, Dulas Brook, Dulas Llynfi, Edw, Gwenlas Brook, Howey Brook, Ithon, Llaethydy Brook, Llymwynt Brook, Migrams Brook, Mithil Brook, Nant Bwch, Nant yr Offeiriad, rhiwiau Brook, Sgithwen Brook, and Triffrwd.\tThe rivers included in the study were surveyed on foot. For assessment and analytical purposes, the streams were divided into reaches which show similar geomorphological characteristics. Data was collected with respect to the geomorphological features e.g. flow conditions, bed material and erosion. The data was collected with a detailed photographic record and developed onto a Geographical Information System (GIS) that provides access to visual, textual and photographic information based upon Ordnance Survey grid reference locations. Habitat suitability maps were produced for each designated feature.\tThe presence of invasive species was also recorded information for which can be found in the report.\tArchived as Project 149, Media 481.   Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media401to500\\Media481  Also archived in CCW legacy GIS drive (M drive) Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\hydrology\\Hydrology_Reports\\fluvial_morphology\tSue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, Knowledge Strategy and Planning, NRW (Formerly Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, CCW)\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2007-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:30:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17903\tNRW_DS111878\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.715Z\t296\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommunity Composition and Spatial Variation of Epifauna on the Horse Mussel Modiolus Reef (1999)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131383738\tThe purpose of this data capture was to examine spatial variation in epifaunal community composition in order to inform the development of a sensitive and cost-effective future monitoring strategy.  Modiolus modiolus reef communities are comparatively rich in species and because they are also scarce in the UK it was considered important to monitor elements of community composition as an attribute that indicates the condition of these features. This dataset contains the results of in-situ sampling carried out by divers to try to determine the spatial variation in epifaunal community composition on the horse mussel reef off the coast of North Pen Llyn.\tThe following methodologies were used. Epifaunal recording replicate quadrat sampling position markers were deployed at stratified random positions along the axis of 7 reef \u0027crest\u0027 and \u0027trough\u0027 features. 6 rows were sampled by divers \u0027in-situ\u0027 and the 7th row was recorded using video. Divers were instructed to record as many species as possible on their recording slate and \u0027gridded\u0027 quadrats were used to assist in systematic counting or video recording. Each diver recorded the epifauna within a quadrat from as many rows (1-6) as possible in order to spread any errors associated with recording experience and inter-worker variability across rows.\tThis is the trial of methods that were later developed and superceeded by the use of video quadrats, see metadata entry Monitoring the health of the North Llyn Modiolus Reef (OLIB URI 110271\t[A]  Marine Recorder survey JNCCMNCR40000775.  [B]  MNCR forms are held in CCW off-site storage.(Box CCW/MNCR/3) [C]  Report is available from CCW library (see related titles above) this survey relates to section 5.4 of the report.\tNRW Custodian Mike Camplin  Originator: Marine Monitoring Team CCW Bill Sanderson, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, CCW Rohan Holt, Marine Monitoring Biologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, CCW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:46:54.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17906\tNRW_DS111924\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.627Z\t568\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSublittoral Survey of North Pen Llyn 1998\tArolwg Isarforol o Ogledd Pen Llyn 1998\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131393234\tThe aim of this survey was to gather detailed information on reef habitats and their associated communities in the northern part of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau cSAC and to provide a photographic and video record of the habitats and communities surveyed.  The geographic coverage included The North Pen Llyn coast, and concentrated on the section between Penrhyn Mawr and Porth Dinllaen. Surveys took place over a period between the 18th and the 22nd of July 1998.\tA total of 18 sites were surveyed, 16 of which corresponded to a list of 20 sites drawn up by CCW prior to survey. Bad weather resulted in the abandonment of offshore survey so the other two sites surveyed were in the shelter of Porth Dinllaen bay. For each site, descriptions of habitats and communities encountered were obtained by divers using standard MNCR techniques. A variety of biotope and species photographs were taken and a video was taken at each site. Confidence in this dataset is high.\t\tMarine Recorder survey JNCCMNCR40000771. Report is available from NRW library - Maes y Fynnon. Original MNCR forms are maintained at Maes y Fynnon off-site storage.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1999-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:37:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17909\tNRW_DS111945\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.715Z\t345\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBenthos of Cardigan Bay Candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) 1998\tBenthos yACA Bae Ceredigion 1998\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313131393435\tThis survey was intended as a follow up excercise to the RoxAnn ground truthing video drops carried out in 1997.  This dataset comprises of the results of a survey carried out by Ivor Rees to ground truth RoxAnn data collected in Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC).\tA grid of 18 stations across the outer half of the SAC boundary. A 0.1 m Day Grab was used, with full leads and stainless steel buckets. Methodology aimed to take 2 grabs at each station, the first being washed in a 1 mm nylon sieve to be treated as a quantitative sample, the second having a sediment sample removed and then washed as a non quantitative sample. Extra grabs taken where grab failed to bite or where stones in the jaws.\t\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) copy of Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000774)\tThe Custodians are in MFSG, Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW Contact Master Reference Data Team for more information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1998-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:02:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17902\tNRW_DS112020\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.586Z\t775\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommon Land Registers Fully Attributed Dataset\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132303230\tRegistered common land element of Section 4 Map as required by Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000. This dataset contains the Common Land areas of Wales. It is a fully attributed dataset, therefore due to sensitivities in the data cannot be released. Any line shown as not matching the original boundary has been copied from what is shown on the original registers and is not a digitising error.\tThe paper records for common land registers are held by local authorities form a basis to this dataset. This dataset is a faithful copy of the original common land registers as held by the local authorities, and was digitised against OS Land-Line by Landmark Information Group (LIG) for CCW for Open Access mapping work. Licenced by IDeA. Any line shown as not matching the original boundary has been copied from what is shown on the original registers and is not a digitising error.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Common Land Registers 2014.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Common Land Register 2001.lyr\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:46:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17913\tNRW_DS112114\t2024-05-28T12:34:44.094Z\t553\tValid\tEditable\tOpen Access - Dedicated Forests (CRoW Act)\tCoedwigoedd Cyhoeddus Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132313134\tDataset showing forests that comprised the freehold public forest estate dedicated by former Forestry Commission Wales (FCW), now Natural Resources Wales (NRW), under section 16 of the Countryside and Rights of Way (CRoW) Act in 2005. Some forest that was dedicated has since been sold and is now in private ownership.   When using this data to understand the extent of Open Access , it should be used in conjunction with Open land, Open Country, Registered Common Land, Other Statutory Access Land, and Other Dedicated Land from the same years.-Mae Deddf Cefn Gwlad a Hawliau Tramwy yn darparu ar gyfer mynediad cyhoeddus ar droed i fathau penodol o dir, yn diwygio\u0027r gyfraith sy\u0027n ymwneud â hawliau tramwy cyhoeddus, yn cynyddu mesurau ar gyfer rheoli a gwarchod Safleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGA) ac yn cryfhau deddfwriaeth gorfodi bywyd gwyllt, wrth ddarparu ar gyfer gwell rheolaeth ar Ardaloedd o Harddwch Naturiol Eithriadol (AHNE). Ystad Rydd-ddaliad Benodedig y Comisiwn Coedwigaeth, wedi\u0027i thrin at ddibenion cyhoeddi gan yr Arolwg Ordnans ar fapiau Landranger 1:50000. Sylwch, nid yw hon yn set ddata daliadau tir swyddogol y Comisiwn Coedwigaeth. Wrth ddefnyddio\u0027r data hwn ar gyfer Mynediad Agored, dylid ei ddefnyddio ar y cyd â Chefn Gwlad Agored, Tir Mynediad Statudol Arall a Thir Comin Cofrestredig.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tFirst layer provided in 2005 and is updated every 10 years. Land is dedicated in perpetuity. Additions can be made to this dataset on a rolling basis (registered common land and open country is reviewed on a decadal basis, the last review was in 2014).   The Countryside and Rights of Way (CRoW) Act 2000 applies to England and Wales only.\t\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Dedicated Forests 2014.lyr X:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Dedicated Forests 2004.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Gwen Thomas, Advisor (2) Estates Business Systems   Gary Eccleston/Geospatial team are responsible for the GIS digitisation and updates.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-04-16\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_PUBLIC_FOREST_2014\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_PUBLIC_FOREST_2014\t2026-01-12T15:53:26.361Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17899\tNRW_DS112214\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.591Z\t917\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPalaeoecological Study of Llyn Padarn (2009)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132323134\tLlyn Padarn is designated as a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) for several reasons including its populations of Arctic charr and floating water-plantain. However, there have been several concerns of the water quality, owing to the decline in adult charr population and the occurrence of the blue-green algal bloom in 2009. In light of this, it was necessary to assess the recent environmental change that has occurred in the lake.  The aim of this project is to sample and analyse a lake sediment core from Llyn Padarn and relate the findings to chemical and nutrient changes over time and known events in the lake\u0027s history. Palaeolimnological methods offer a means of reconstructing the environmental history of standing waters by studying subfossil fragments of the biota preserved in the lake sediments. The project aims to reconstruct the environmental history of Llyn Padarn over approximately the last century using diatoms, with particular attention to the eutrophication history of the site. The study focuses on diatom remains in sediment cores, and also includes the application of an existing diatom-total phosphorus (TP) model to reconstruct the nutrient history of the lake. In addition to this, X-ray fluorescence analysis was carried out to assess down core changes in chemical composition, with a particular focus on iron, copper and P. The analysis of a dated sediment core collected from Llyn Padarn in 2009 (PADA2) was used to assess environmental change over the last 100-150 years.  The data was also used to reconstruct the environmental history of Llyn Padarn over approximately the last century with particular attention to the eutrophication history of the site. Diatom analysis of only three samples from an undated core taken from the lake in August 2004 showed that the diatom assemblage shifted from species typically associated with nutrient-poor waters towards those associated with more productive systems. This study builds on this earlier work by analysing a new sediment core at a higher resolution and providing a chronology to determine the timing and rates of change. The study feeds into ongoing monitoring at Llyn Padarn to determine the trophic status of the lake and the health of the Arctic charr population.\tCore Collection A single sediment core was collected from the deep basin of the lake using a Glew gravity coring device. The coring location was located by a hand-held echo sounder and recorded by GPS.    Extrusion and core description  The core was extruded in the field at 0.5 cm intervals to a depth of 20 cm and at 1 cm intervals thereafter. Any visible stratigraphic changes were noted and the core was photographed. The percentage dry weight (Percent DW) which gives a measure of the water content of the sediment, percentage loss on ignition (Percent LOI) which gives a measure of the organic matter content, and percentage carbonate were determined in the laboratory on every sample by standard techniques.  Wet density was measured on selected samples by filling and weighing a 2 cm3 brass vial with sediment.  Radiometric Dating Standard reliable methods of establishing a chronology for sediment cores was used for radiometric dating techniques.  Sediment samples from the Llyn Padarn core (coded PADA2) were analysed for 210Pb, 226Ra, 137Cs and 241Am by direct gamma assay in the Bloomsbury Environmental Isotope Facility (BEIF) at University College London using either an ORTEC HPGe GWL series well-type, or an ORTEC HPGe LOAX series planar-type, coaxial low background intrinsic germanium detector. 210Pb was determined via its gamma emissions at 46.5keV, and 226Ra by the 295keV and 352keV gamma rays emitted by its daughter isotope 214Pb following three weeks storage in sealed containers to allow radioactive equilibration. 137Cs and 241Am were measured by their emissions at 662kev and 59.5kev. The absolute efficiencies of the detector were determined using calibrated sources and sediment samples of known activity. Corrections were made for the effect of self-absorption of low energy gamma rays within the sample.\t\tElectronic data is archived as project 269, media no. 980.   The report is available on CCW\u0027s Electronic Documents Management System (Ffynnon: DCT-10-088698)\tSue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, NRW (Formerly Rhian Thomas, Rivers Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, CCW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-05-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:56:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17900\tNRW_DS112225\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.566Z\t640\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBenthic Habitats: Maps of Sensitivity to Fishing Gears (Intertidal and Subtidal)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132323235\tThis dataset consists of intertidal and subtidal maps of the sensitivity of benthic fisheries habitats to fishing gear types within the Welsh Territorial Seas (high water to 12 nm offshore).  Intertidal biotope mapping was carried out in order to determine the distribution and extent of seashore habitats, biological communities and species on the shores of Wales. Subtidal biotopes were predicted in HABMAP in order to act as a guide for marine scientists. The biotopes were converted to fisheries habitats in order to provide a means of assessing the sensitivity of benthic habitats to the effects of fishing gear types. The sensitivity of each fisheries habitat to each fishing gear and access was determined in order to provide a basis for management advice and fisheries regulation.  The intertidal area consists of the whole of the Welsh coastline between the limits of the tidal rise and fall. The subtidal area consists of Welsh water out to 12 nm offshore which are within the Southern Irish Sea i.e. sections around the Severn in the south and the Dee in the north are not included. HABMAP modelling has been re-run to include these areas in the south and north and these biotopes are due to be included in the fisheries habitats maps in 2010.\tIntertidal biotopes were mapped from 1996-2004 and converted to fisheries habitats and sensitivities between 2008 and 2009. Intertidal biotopes were predicted in 2008 - 2009 using seabed survey data collected in 2005 and a variety of other data sources dating back to 1963. Biotopes were converted to fisheries habitats and sensitivities between 2008 and 2009. Intertidal biotope mapping was carried out by a dedicated team of trained staff from Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). Surveyors followed a standard protocol and worked in pairs, with more and less experienced staff working together. Subtidal biotopes were predicted in between 2008 - 2009 using seabed survey data collected in 2005 and a variety of other data sources dating back to 1963. Biotopes were converted to fisheries habitats/sensitivities between  2008 and 2009. Spatial confidence scores were produced alongside the biotopes. These were split into areas of reasonable, low and poor confidence and used for fisheries habitats/sensitivity maps. The conversion from biotope to fisheries habitats was checked using expert judgement. Intertidal and subtidal- the sensitivity level applied to each combination of fisheries habitat and gear type/access was checked using expert judgement.  Both the intertidal and subtidal conversions, from biotope to fisheries habitats, were checked using expert judgement. Fisheries habitats are produced from intertidal and subtidal biotope maps. These data layers will form part of a large project (Distribution of Fisheries Habitats and Fishing Activities to Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) features (Fisheries sensitivity maps and matrices), project 376).\tFisheries habitats will be updated when the intertidal/subtidal biotope maps are updated, dependent on availability of resources. Intertidal biotopes will be updated in the long term (20 yrs) depending on resource availability. Subtidal biotopes are intended to be reviewed and updated via HABMAP as and when resources are available; if a significant amount of new data is collected that will make it a higher priority.\tLegacy CCW Archive (File path is Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Users\\Sue Hearn\\\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team (Was Dr Clare Eno, Senior Fisheries Policy Officer, Envrionmental Policy Group, NRW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:47:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17910\tNRW_DS112227\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.746Z\t379\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal and Subtidal Predicted Benthic Fisheries Habitats Maps  (2009)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132323237\tThis dataset is consists of intertidal and subtidal maps of benthic fisheries habitats within the Welsh Territorial Seas (high water to 12 nm offshore).  Intertidal biotope mapping was carried out in order to determine the distribution and extent of seashore habitats, biological communities and species on the shores of Wales. Subtidal biotopes were predicted in HABMAP in order to act as a guide for marine scientists. The biotopes were converted to fisheries habitats in order to provide a means of assessing the sensitivity of benthic habitats to the effects of fishing gear types. The intertidal area consists of the whole of the welsh coastline between the limits of the tidal rise and fall.  The subtidal area consists of Welsh water out to 12 nm  offshore which are within the Southern Irish Sea  i.e. sections around the Severn in the south and the Dee in the north are not included. HABMAP modelling has been re-run to include these areas in the south and north and these biotopes are due to be included in the fisheries habitats maps in 2010.\tIntertidal surveys and predictive mapping. The intertidal biotope mapping was carried out by dedicated teams of trained staff from former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff.  the intertidal surveyors followed standard protocol and worked in with more and less experienced staff working together.  Intertidal biotopes were mapped as part of CCW\u0027s intertidal field survey of Wales using Phase I methodology.  Intertidal biotopes were mapped from 1996-2004 and converted to fisheries habitats between 2008 and 2009. Subtidal biotopes were predicted as part of the BIOMOR 5 HABMAP I and II project using modelled seabed data. Predictions and conversions into fisheries habitats in 2008 and 2009 using seabed survey data collected in 2005 and a variety of other data sources dating back to 1963. Spatial confidence scores of the subtidal habitat were produced alongside the biotopes. These were split in to areas of reasonable, low and poor confidence.   Both the intertidal and subtidal conversions, from biotope to fisheries habitats, were checked using expert judgement.  Fisheries habitats are produced from intertidal and subtidal biotope maps. These data layers will form part of a large project (Distribution of Fisheries Habitats and Fishing Activities to Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) features (Fisheries sensitivity maps and matrices), project 376).\tFisheries habitats will be updated when the intertidal/subtidal biotope maps are updated, dependent on availability of resources. Intertidal biotopes will be updated in the long term (20 yrs) depending on resource availability. Subtidal biotopes are intended to be reviewed and updated via HABMAP as and when resources are available; if a significant amount of new data is collected that will make it a higher priority. All biotopes were converted to fisheries habitats (grouped according to their response to fishing gear types) by CCW and checked by expert judgement.\tStated as Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Users\\Sue_Hr\\Benthic Fisheries habitats Maps but should have been moved to Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS Users\\Sue_Hr BUT THIS LOCATION DOES NOT APPEAR TO EXIST.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team (Formerly Claire Eno)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:43:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17891\tNRW_DS112289\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.941Z\t590\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Tide-swept Boulder Habitat in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2008\tGwaith monitro rhynglanwol ar gynefin clogfeini llanwol yn ACA Y Fenai a Bae Conwy yn 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132323839\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Rocky reefs (hard substrata) are of considerable conservation importance due the high diversity of the communities they support and, as such, are included under Annex 1 of the EU Habitats Directive (92/43/EEC) (Hill et al. 1998). They are an abundant feature of the UK coastline, especially on western coasts where exposed bedrock predominates. In some areas, the substratum may be composed entirely of bedrock whilst on less steeply shelving / gently shelving shores, large boulders, cobbles, gravel, sand and mud may be present (Hill et al., 1998). Rocky reefs are extremely variable in terms of their structure and the communities they support with reef communities being influenced by the type of rock and the microhabitats available, sediment availability, degree of shelter, shore height, and temperature regime. Communities in exposed areas are usually dominated by sponges, anemones and foliose red algae with ascidians, delicate filamentous algae and fan worms being more common in sheltered areas (JNCC, 2005).\tThe following procedure was employed to compile the species richness of the boulders: a measure of the extent of the underboulder biotope community was attempted with the surveyors hopping in and out of the boat to carry out the survey of extent, boulders were chosen along the same shore height (MLW - MSLW) and within close proximity to each other (where possible), boulders were only selected if they fitted within a 0.5 m x 0.5 m quadrat. These intermediate sized boulders are known to carry the highest diversity, with smaller stones being overturned too often to acquire a stable community whilst larger boulders develop a single-species dominance (Liddiard et al 1989), a GPS point was taken of each boulder, a photograph was taken of the boulder upper surface then a species list taken, The boulder was then turned, photographed and a species list taken from the underside of the boulder.  All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors. Data quality and confidence considered high.\t\tThe data are archived as Project No 199, Media No 550 and is stored on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters.   H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MSCB\\2008 ASML MSCB\tIntertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-06-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:02:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17914\tNRW_DS112290\t2024-05-28T12:34:44.014Z\t387\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sediment Flats in Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2008\tGwaith monitro rhynglanwol ar wastadeddau gwaddodion yn ACA Y Fenai a Bae Conwy yn 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132323930\tMenai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC)  is designated for five Annex 1 habitats: Sandbanks which are slightly covered by sea water all the time, Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide, Reefs, Large shallow inlets and bays and Submerged or partially submerged sea caves. Of these mudflats, sandflats and reefs are the main habitats of interest within intertidal areas of Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. Conservation objectives for these habitats are given in the Regulation 33 advice for the SAC.  The overriding objective of the survey was to establish the reference condition for the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC and develop monitoring stations for recording the level of natural change. This would enable the development of conservation objectives for the appropriate management of the features of the SAC.\tThe Grid Maker tool in the MapInfo GIS package was used to create a grid of 47 stations across the defined survey area, with a north-south distance of 200 m between points and an east-west distance of 330 m. This gave the most appropriate coverage of the flats to enable sampling of the range of biotopes on the Phase 1 map with the manpower available. On site, each survey pair was allocated a section of the grid to survey and GPS units were used to navigate to each station in turn. Where possible, a single 0.01 m2 core sample was taken at each survey station. If the station happened to lie on a rocky outcrop, in a drainage channel or in a pool greater than ~5 cm deep, then a core sample was not taken. The core sample was sieved using a 0.5 mm mesh sieve in a nearby pool. The contents of the sieve were then inspected closely, with the aid of a magnifying glass. All visible animals were identified and counted, to the extent achievable, by the most experienced surveyor in the pair.  Phase 1 surveys of intertidal habitats in the SAC were carried out in the SAC between 1999 and 2003 (Brazier et al. 2007), providing detailed mapping of the intertidal biotopes with some information on characterizing species. Considerable historical data exists on the intertidal habitats and communities present at many sites in the SAC, including Y Foryd, Traeth Lafan and the tide-swept boulder shores of the Menai Strait. Methodological trials and surveys, designed for the purposes of SAC monitoring, have been carried out in recent years. Of particular relevance to this report are the surveys of tide-swept boulder biotopes at sites on the Menai Strait (Boyes and Allen 2004, Mazik et al. 2005) and a report of boulder monitoring stations established by ASML/CCW in 2007 (Moore 2008), and a survey of the Zostera noltii bed in Y Foryd (Boyes, Proctor and Mitchell 2004).  All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  Data quality and confidence considered high.\t\tThe data are archived as Project No 199, Media No 549 and is stored on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters.  [A]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MSCB\\2008 ASML MSCB\tIntertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:03:27.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17897\tNRW_DS112379\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.536Z\t264\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGround Monitoring of Common Scoter Melinitta nigra in Carmarthen Bay 2008 - 2009\tMonitro mor-hwyaid duon Melinitta nigra ar y ddaear ym Mae Caerfyrddin 2008-2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132333739\tCarmarthen Bay Special Protection Area (SPA) was classified as the UK\u0027s first SPA in 2003 on the basis of holding at least 1% (16,000 individuals: Wetlands International 2006) of the biogeographic population of common scoter Melanitta nigra. Common Scoter feed on bivalves and other invertebrates such as polychaetes and amphipods which are found in abundance in the shallow waters (less than 10m) in Carmarthen Bay. Common scoter are also listed as a Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species. Former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), has supported ground based counts since 1996, following the Sea Empress oil spill. The purpose of this data capture was to carry out one scoter count of Carmarthen Bay in January and February 2009. This work feeds in to the condition monitoring and surveillance of Carmarthen Bay SPA and contributes to Common Standards Monitoring.\tCounts were made at 4 locations: Rhossili, Pembrey, Pendine and Amroth.  Rhossili was visited at least once and the other sites at least twice. Methods for counting are outlined in Banks et al (2004, 2007).  All relevant  local conditions were recorded and counts were only taken during suitable weather and visibility conditions and where possible counts coincided with high tide. An experienced counter using high quality binoculars and telescope was employed. However, no spatial reference were provided with data for statistical analysis. It is assumed for the purpose of the metadata that samples were collected using British National Grid. A sustained program of monitoring scoters in Carmarthen bay has been in place since 1997.\t\tData is held in the Marine Archive as project no 272 (media 989).\tInternal Custodian: - Marine Ornithologist, NRW. - Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:57:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17905\tNRW_DS112384\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.567Z\t279\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGround Monitoring of Common Scoter Melinitta nigra in Carmarthen Bay 2009 - 2010\tMonitro mor-hwyaid duon Melinitta nigra ar y ddaear ym Mae Caerfyrddin 2009-2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132333834\tCarmarthen Bay Special Protection Area (SPA) was classified as the UK\u0027s first SPA in 2003 on the basis of holding at least 1% (16,000 individuals: Wetlands International 2006) of the biogeographic population of common scoter Melanitta nigra. Common Scoter feed on bivalves and other invertebrates such as polychaetes and amphipods which are found in abundance in the shallow waters ( less than10m) in Carmarthen Bay. Common scoter are also listed as a BAP species. Purpose of data capture was to collate data to feed into the condition monitoring and surveillance of Carmarthen Bay SPA and contributes to Common Standards Monitoring.\tCounts were made at 4 locations: Rhossili, Pembrey, Pendine and Amroth.  Rhossili was visited at least once and the other sites at least twice. Methods for counting are outlined in Banks et al (2004, 2007).  All relevant local conditions were recorded and counts were only taken during suitable weather and visibility conditions and where possible counts coincided with high tide. An experienced counter using high quality binoculars and telescope was employed. However, no spatial reference were provided with data for statistical analysis. It is assumed for the purpose of the metadata that samples were collected using British National Grid.\tA sustained program of monitoring scoters in Carmarthen bay has been in place since 1997 (Stewart et al, 1997, Cranswick et al, 1998, WWT 2003).\tData is archived under project no 273 (media 990).\tInternal Custodian: - Marine Ornithologist, NRW. - Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T09:57:55.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17894\tNRW_DS112400\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.059Z\t199\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal habitat diversity (neighbourhood analysis)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343030\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Habitat diversity in the intertidal range is measured using biotope richness, biotope distinctness and the number of Biodiversity Action Plan priority features metrics. Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach that analyses data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 1 km is utilised to present these metrics. Biotope information for the Welsh intertidal zone was collated from CCW and is full coverage collected using standardised methods.  Establish intertidal diversity from known data.  The Welsh coastline, from the mean high-water datum to the mean low-water datum covering the intertidal zone.\tSpecies information were collated from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot), the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH).  Biotope codes were matched against EUNIS codes.  Biotopes in 97.06 format were manually matched to 04.05 biotopes prior to EUNIS translation and in some instances where the code could not be accurately matched to the same level, and a higher level code was assigned.  A hexagonal grid was used because they offer the best alignment to complex features, such as the Welsh coastline. The optimal size was determined as 1 km2 for the intertidal region. Metrics used to represent marine biodiversity in Wales are biotope richness, biotope distinctness and the number of priority features.  Each measure was calculated for each broad sampling method type, re-combined by hexagon, and presented as a separate layer on a continuous scale. Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach (analyzing data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one) to identify differences (from that of a non-neighbourhood analysis) that may be due to very localized features or the alignment of the grid itself.  Biotope information for the Welsh intertidal is full coverage and the data was collected using standardised methods, therefore the layer showing biotope diversity can be viewed with a high degree of confidence.\tKirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:01:59.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17901\tNRW_DS112401\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.482Z\t262\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal habitat diversity\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343031\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Habitat diversity in the intertidal range is measured using biotope richness, biotope distinctness and the number of Biodiversity Action Plan priority features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 1 km is utilised to present these metrics. Biotope information for the Welsh intertidal zone was collated from CCW and is full coverage collected using standardised methods.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish intertidal habitat diversity from known data. The Welsh coastline from the mean high water datum to the mean low water datum covering the intertidal zone.\tSpecies information were collated from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot), the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH).  Biotope codes were matched against EUNIS codes.  Biotopes in 97.06 format were manually matched to 04.05 biotopes prior to EUNIS translation and in some instances where the code could not be accurately matched to the same level, and a higher level code was assigned.  A hexagonal grid was used because they offer the best alignment to complex features, such as the Welsh coastline. The optimal size was determined as 1 km2 for the intertidal region. Metrics used to represent marine biodiversity in Wales are biotope richness, biotope distinctness and the number of priority features.  Each measure was calculated for each broad sampling method type, re-combined by hexagon, and presented as a separate layer on a continuous scale.  Biotope information for the Welsh intertidal is full coverage and the data was collected using standardised methods, therefore the layer showing biotope diversity can be viewed with a high degree of confidence.\t\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:56:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17893\tNRW_DS112402\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.951Z\t295\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal Species Diversity Confidence Layer\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343032\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Confidence ratings, based on the quality and quantity of data used in the final species diversity analysis, was calculated for each hexagonal unit to provide users with a view of the underlying data when examining hotspot occurrence. The purpose of this data capture was to establish species diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline from the mean high water datum to the mean low water datum covering the intertidal zone.\tSpecies information was collated from CCW and the National Biodiversity Network Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot from the Joint Nature Conservation Committee, JNCC). MarLIN databases and any additional data holdings within the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH) were also used. Confidence ratings based on the quality and quantity of data used in the final analysis were calculated for each hexagonal unit to provide users with a view of the underlying data when examining hotspot occurrence. A confidence rating was calculated using a three point categorical scale from high to low.  The average quality for each hexagon was calculated by assigning numerical values to each sample (high, medium and low were allocated 3, 2 and 1 respectively) and then the mean for each hexagon was calculated.  The sample counts per hexagon were also aggregated into high, medium and low, using the natural breaks classification.  Once the high, medium and low values were calculated for each hexagon, confidence was calculated using the matrix a confidence matrix (see additional information). An assessment of the suitability of species data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\t\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tContact Data Services for metadata information. Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:08.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17895\tNRW_DS112403\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.160Z\t420\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal species diversity layer (chao2 analysis)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343033\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. The Chao2 estimator was applied to intertidal species data collated from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot), the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH). Chao2 is a technique for extrapolating species richness from limited numbers of samples based on the concept that rare species carry most information about the number of missing species; this was employed to check for artifacts in analyses. The purpose of the data capture was to establish species diversity from known data of the Welsh coastline from the mean high water datum to the mean low water datum covering the intertidal zone.\tSpecies information was collated from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot), the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH). Distinct survey data were imported into a geodatabase.  The full species list was matched against the World Register of Marine Species (WoRMS) and any unrecognised species corrected. The final list was matched against an edited list of candidate Nationally Important Marine Features (NIMF) to identify those recorded in Wales.  An assessment of the suitability of these data for biodiversity assessment was carried out taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy and quality using criteria set out in the ISO 19115 standard for geospatial metadata.  A hexagonal grid was used because they offer the best alignment to complex features, such as the Welsh coastline. The optimal size was determined as 1km2 for the intertidal region. The Chao2 estimator measure was calculated for each broad sampling method type, re-combined by hexagon, and presented as a separate layer on a continuous scale.  Chao2 is a technique for extrapolating species richness from limited numbers of samples based on the concept that rare species carry most information about the number of missing species; this was employed to check for artifacts in analyses.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\t\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956.\tSubtidal Team Leader, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17890\tNRW_DS112404\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.013Z\t249\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal species diversity layer (neighbourhood analysis)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343034\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity in the intertidal range is measured using species richness, average taxonomic distinctness and number of Welsh Nationally Important Marine Features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 1 km is used to present these metrics. Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach that analyses data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one. The purpose of this data capture was to establish intertidal species diversity from known data along the Welsh coastline from the mean high water datum to the mean low water datum covering the intertidal zone.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tSpecies information were collated from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot), the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH). Distinct survey data were imported into a geodatabase.  The full species list was matched against the World Register of Marine Species (WoRMS) and any unrecognised species corrected. The final list was matched against an edited list of candidate Nationally Important Marine Features (NIMF) to identify those recorded in Wales.  An assessment of the suitability of these data for biodiversity assessment was carried out taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy and quality using criteria set out in the ISO 19115 standard for geospatial metadata. A hexagonal grid was used because they offer the best alignment to complex features, such as the Welsh coastline. The optimal size was determined as 1km2 for the intertidal region. Metrics used to represent marine biodiversity in Wales were species richness, average taxonomic distinctness and the number of priority features.  Each measure was calculated for each broad sampling method type, re-combined by hexagon, and presented as a separate layer on a continuous scale.  Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach (analyzing data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one) to identify differences (from that of a non-neighbourhood analysis) that may be due to very localized features or the alignment of the grid itself.\t\tThe data is archived as project 260, Media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team The metadata point of contact is the Data Service Team.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T16:06:55.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17888\tNRW_DS112405\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.597Z\t208\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Intertidal species diversity layer\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343035\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity in the intertidal range is measured using species richness, average taxonomic distinctness and number of Welsh Nationally Important Marine Features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 1 km is used to present these metrics. The purpose of this data capture was to establish intertidal diversity from known data of the Welsh coastline from the mean high water datum to the mean low water datum covering the intertidal zone.\tSpecies information were collated from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot), the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH). Distinct survey data were imported into a geodatabase.  The full species list was matched against the World Register of Marine Species (WoRMS) and any unrecognised species corrected. The final list was matched against an edited list of candidate Nationally Important Marine Features (NIMF) to identify those recorded in Wales.  An assessment of the suitability of these data for biodiversity assessment was carried out taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy and quality using criteria set out in the ISO 19115 standard for geospatial metadata.  A hexagonal grid was used because they offer the best alignment to complex features, such as the Welsh coastline. The optimal size was determined 1km2 for the intertidal region. Metrics used to represent marine biodiversity in Wales were species richness, average taxonomic distinctness and the number of priority features.  Each measure was calculated for each broad sampling method type, re-combined by hexagon, and presented as a separate layer on a continuous scale.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\t\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:34:04.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17892\tNRW_DS112406\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.940Z\t274\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Species and Habitat Diversity Confidence Layer\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343036\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Confidence ratings, based on the quality and quantity of data used in the final species diversity analysis, was calculated for each hexagonal unit to provide users with a view of the underlying data when examining hotspot occurrence. The purpose of this data capture was to establish species diversity from known data for the Welsh coastline taken from the mean high-water datum to 12 nautical miles offshore.\tConfidence ratings based on the quality and quantity of data used in the final analysis were calculated for each hexagonal unit to provide users with a view of the underlying data when examining hotspot occurrence.  A confidence rating was calculated using a three point categorical scale from high to low.  The average quality for each hexagon was calculated by assigning numerical values to each sample (high, medium and low were allocated 3, 2 and 1 respectively) and then the mean for each hexagon was calculated.  The sample counts per hexagon were also aggregated into high, medium and low, using the natural breaks classification.  Once the high, medium and low values were calculated for each hexagon, confidence was calculated using the matrix a confidence matrix (see additional information).  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\t\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Contact Data Services for metadata information.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:11.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17889\tNRW_DS112407\t2024-05-28T12:34:43.013Z\t580\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Species Diversity Layer (Chao2 Analysis)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343037\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. The Chao2 estimator was applied to species data collated from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot), the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH). Chao2 is a technique for extrapolating species richness from limited numbers of samples based on the concept that rare species carry most information about the number of missing species; this was employed to check for artifacts in analyses. The purpose of this data capture was to establish species diversity from known data for the Welsh coastline taken from the mean high water datum to 12 nautical miles offshore.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tDistinct survey data were imported into a geodatabase.  The full species list was matched against the World Register of Marine Species (WoRMS) and any unrecognised species corrected. The final list was matched against an edited list of candidate Nationally Important Marine Features (NIMF) to identify those recorded in Wales.  An assessment of the suitability of these data for biodiversity assessment was carried out taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy and quality using criteria set out in the ISO 19115 standard for geospatial metadata. A hexagonal grid was used because they offer the best alignment to complex features, such as the Welsh coastline. The optimal size was determined as 10km2 for the region. The Chao2 estimator measure was calculated for each broad sampling method type, re-combined by hexagon, and presented as a separate layer on a continuous scale.  Chao2 is a technique for extrapolating species richness from limited numbers of samples based on the concept that rare species carry most information about the number of missing species; this was employed to check for artifacts in analyses.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\t\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of contact is Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:07:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17887\tNRW_DS112408\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.578Z\t528\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Species Diversity Layer (Neighbourhood Analysis)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343038\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity in the range is measured using species richness, average taxonomic distinctness and number of Welsh Nationally Important Marine Features metrics. Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach that analyses data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 10 km is utilised to present these metrics. The purpose of this data capture was to establish diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline, between the mean high-water mark up to 12 nautical miles offshore.   An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tSpecies information was collated from CCW and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot from the Joint Nature Conservation Committee, JNCC). MarLIN databases and any additional data holdings within the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH) were also used. Distinct survey data were imported into a geodatabase.  The full species list was matched against the World Register of Marine Species (WoRMS) and any unrecognised species corrected. The final list was matched against an edited list of candidate Nationally Important Marine Features (NIMF) to identify those recorded in Wales.  An assessment of the suitability of these data for biodiversity assessment was carried out taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy and quality using criteria set out in the ISO 19115 standard for geospatial metadata.   A hexagonal grid was used because they offer the best alignment to complex features, such as the Welsh coastline. The optimal size was determined 10km2 for the region. Metrics used to represent marine biodiversity in Wales were species richness, average taxonomic distinctness and the number of priority features.  Each measure was calculated for each broad sampling method type, re-combined by hexagon, and presented as a separate layer on a continuous scale.  Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach (analyzing data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one) to identify differences (from that of a non-neighbourhood analysis) that may be due to very localized features or the alignment of the grid itself.\t\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of Contact: Master Reference Data Team.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17886\tNRW_DS112409\t2024-05-28T12:34:42.475Z\t294\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Species Diversity Layer\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343039\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity is measured using species richness, average taxonomic distinctness and number of Welsh Nationally Important Marine Features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 10 km is used to present these metrics.    An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses. The purpose of this data capture was to establish species diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline from mean high water datum to 12 nautical miles offshore.\tSpecies information were collated from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot), the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH). Distinct survey data were imported into a geodatabase.  The full species list was matched against the World Register of Marine Species (WoRMS) and any unrecognised species corrected. The final list was matched against an edited list of candidate Nationally Important Marine Features (NIMF) to identify those recorded in Wales.  An assessment of the suitability of these data for biodiversity assessment was carried out taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy and quality using criteria set out in the ISO 19115 standard for geospatial metadata.  A hexagonal grid was used because they offer the best alignment to complex features, such as the Welsh coastline. The optimal size was determined as 10km2 for the region. Metrics used to represent marine biodiversity in Wales were species richness, average taxonomic distinctness and the number of priority features.  Each measure was calculated for each broad sampling method type, re-combined by hexagon, and presented as a separate layer on a continuous scale.  Data were queried and analysed using a normal hexagonal grid.\t\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:57.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17936\tNRW_DS112410\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.870Z\t276\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Subtidal Habitat Diversity Layer (Neighbourhood Analysis)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343130\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Habitat diversity in the range is measured using biotope richness, biotope distinctness and the number of Biodiversity Action Plan priority features metrics. Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach that analyses data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 10 km is utilised to present these metrics. The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline, from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tBiotope information for subtidal habitats was collated from CCW and the National Biodiversity Network Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot from the Joint Nature Conservation Committee, JNCC). MarLIN databases and any additional data holdings within the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH) were also used. Biotope codes were matched against EUNIS codes.  Biotopes in 97.06 format were manually matched to 04.05 biotopes prior to EUNIS translation and in some instances where the code could not be accurately matched to the same level, and a higher level code was assigned. A hexagonal grid was used because they offer the best alignment to complex features, such as the Welsh coastline. The optimal size was determined 20km2 for the subtidal region. The proposed metrics to represent marine biodiversity in Wales were biotope richness, biotope distinctness and the number of priority features.  Each measure was calculated for each broad sampling method type, re-combined by hexagon, and presented as a separate layer on a continuous scale.  Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach (analyzing data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one) to identify differences (from that of a non-neighbourhood analysis) that may be due to very localized features or the alignment of the grid itself.\t\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tThe metadata point of contact is: Data Services Team Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:09.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17940\tNRW_DS112412\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.963Z\t280\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSubtidal Habitat Diversity Layer (2010)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343132\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Habitat diversity in the range is measured using biotope richness, biotope distinctness and the number of Biodiversity Action Plan priority features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 1 km is used to present these metrics.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline, from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tBiotope information for subtidal habitats was collated from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot), the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH).   Biotope codes were matched against EUNIS codes.  Biotopes in 97.06 format were manually matched to 04.05 biotopes prior to EUNIS translation and in some instances where the code could not be accurately matched to the same level, and a higher level code was assigned.  A hexagonal grid was used because they offer the best alignment to complex features, such as the Welsh coastline. The optimal size was determined 20km2 for the subtidal region. Metrics used to represent marine biodiversity in Wales were biotope richness, biotope distinctness and the number of priority features.  Each measure was calculated for each broad sampling method type, re-combined by hexagon, and presented as a separate layer on a continuous scale.\t\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:24:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17930\tNRW_DS112413\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.946Z\t451\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Subtidal Species and Habitat Diversity Confidence Layer\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343133\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Confidence ratings, based on the quality and quantity of data used in the final species diversity analysis, was calculated for each hexagonal unit to provide users with a view of the underlying data when examining hotspot occurrence.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline, from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.    An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tA confidence rating was calculated using a three point categorical scale from high to low.  The average quality for each hexagon was calculated by assigning numerical values to each sample (high, medium and low were allocated 3, 2 and 1 respectively) and then the mean for each hexagon was calculated.  The sample counts per hexagon were also aggregated into high, medium and low, using the natural breaks classification.  Once the high, medium and low values were calculated for each hexagon, confidence was calculated using the matrix a confidence matrix (see additional information).\t\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Point of contact: Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:10.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17935\tNRW_DS112415\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.871Z\t319\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Subtidal Species Diversity Layer (Chao2 Analysis)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343135\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. The Chao2 estimator was applied to species data collated from the CCW and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot), the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH). Chao2 is a technique for extrapolating species richness from limited numbers of samples based on the concept that rare species carry most information about the number of missing species; this was employed to check for artifacts in analyses. The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline, from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tDistinct survey data were imported into a geodatabase.  The full species list was matched against the World Register of Marine Species (WoRMS) and any unrecognised species corrected. The final list was matched against an edited list of candidate Nationally Important Marine Features (NIMF) to identify those recorded in Wales.  An assessment of the suitability of these data for biodiversity assessment was carried out taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy and quality using criteria set out in the ISO 19115 standard for geospatial metadata.  A hexagonal grid was used because they offer the best alignment to complex features, such as the Welsh coastline. The optimal size was determined as 20km2 for the subtidal region. The Chao2 estimator measure was calculated for each broad sampling method type, re-combined by hexagon, and presented as a separate layer on a continuous scale.  Chao2 is a technique for extrapolating species richness from limited numbers of samples based on the concept that rare species carry most information about the number of missing species; this was employed to check for artifacts in analyses.\t\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17926\tNRW_DS112416\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.825Z\t299\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Subtidal species diversity layer (neighbourhood analysis)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343136\tThis layer was produced for CCW as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity in the range is measured using biotope richness, biotope distinctness and the number of Biodiversity Action Plan priority features metrics.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline, from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.   An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tData were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach that analyses data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 20 km is utilised to present these metrics. Species information for the subtidal region was collated from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot), the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH).   Distinct survey data were imported into a geodatabase.  The full species list was matched against the World Register of Marine Species (WoRMS) and any unrecognised species corrected. The final list was matched against an edited list of candidate Nationally Important Marine Features (NIMF) to identify those recorded in Wales.  An assessment of the suitability of these data for biodiversity assessment was carried out taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy and quality using criteria set out in the ISO 19115 standard for geospatial metadata.  A hexagonal grid was used because they offer the best alignment to complex features, such as the Welsh coastline. The optimal size was determined 20km2 for the subtidal region. The metrics used to represent marine biodiversity in Wales were species richness, average taxonomic distinctness and the number of priority features.  Each measure was calculated for each broad sampling method type, re-combined by hexagon, and presented as a separate layer on a continuous scale.  Data were queried and analysed using a neighbourhood approach (analyzing data from the adjacent six hexagons together with the central one) to identify differences (from that of a non-neighbourhood analysis) that may be due to very localized features or the alignment of the grid itself.\t\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17941\tNRW_DS112417\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.962Z\t243\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2010 Countryside Council for Wales, Subtidal species diversity layer\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343137\tThis layer was produced for Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of the \u0027Identifying Marine Biodiversity Hotspots in Wales\u0027 project. Species diversity is measured using species richness, average taxonomic distinctness and number of Welsh Nationally Important Marine Features metrics. A hexagonal grid with a resolution of 20 km is used to present these metrics.  The purpose of this data capture was to establish subtidal diversity from known data around the Welsh coastline, from mean high water to 12 nautical miles offshore.  An assessment of the suitability of data was carried out, taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy. Data were also graded on survey quality using the following three categories with respect to field surveyors: professional and academic; volunteer with expert ID; and volunteer. Derived confidence ratings were recorded in the metadata and low quality data were flagged and removed from subsequent analyses.\tSpecies information were collated from the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway (Marine Recorder Snapshot), the MarLIN databases and the Data Archive for Seabed Species and Habitats data (DASSH).  Distinct survey data were imported into a geodatabase.  The full species list was matched against the World Register of Marine Species (WoRMS) and any unrecognised species corrected. The final list was matched against an edited list of candidate Nationally Important Marine Features (NIMF) to identify those recorded in Wales.  An assessment of the suitability of these data for biodiversity assessment was carried out taking into account the source of the data, its age, spatial, taxonomic and methodological accuracy and quality using criteria set out in the ISO 19115 standard for geospatial metadata.   A hexagonal grid was used because they offer the best alignment to complex features, such as the Welsh coastline. The optimal size was determined 20km2 for the subtidal region. The metrics used to represent marine biodiversity in Wales were species richness, average taxonomic distinctness and the number of priority features.  Each measure was calculated for each broad sampling method type, re-combined by hexagon, and presented as a separate layer on a continuous scale.  Data were queried and analysed using a normal hexagonal grid.\t\tThe data is archived as project 260, media no. 956 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 956)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of Contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17929\tNRW_DS112484\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.898Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGeological Site Condition Reports\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132343834\tShort paper forms used to record condition of geological features/ Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), accompanied by digital photographs. The forms form the basis of the geological site monitoring condition reports on the Monitoring Module of the Features Database.\tThis dataset was created by Area Earth Science Officers using Common Standards Monitoring methodology. Monitoring-related digital images go back to at least 2000 together with accompanying paper recording forms for each monitoring visit. Confidence in data: Each site is visited at a frequency of between 1-5 years depending on the level of perceived threat.\t\tKept by Area Earth Science Officers. Digital photographs stored on the personal computers of the Area Earth Science Officers For instance, Bob Mathews works from home and stores his records on a work laptop that is backed-up on an external hard-drive. Paper records are also available.\tRaymond Roberts, North Region Earth Science Officer, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:56:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17942\tNRW_DS112503\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.970Z\t535\tValid\tEditable\tHabitat Mapping for Conservation and Management of the Southern Irish Sea (HABMAP)\tMapio cynefinoedd ar gyfer gwarchod a rheoli de Mor Iwerddon (HABMAP)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353033\tThe HABMAP project was set up in response to the need for better spatial awareness of habitat distributions in the Southern Irish Sea. This work produced habitat maps of the seabed using novel predictive modelling techniques. This dataset is related to the predictive modelling only.    The HABMAP Extension Project has built on the methods developed during the original project, and has repeated the modelling work using higher resolution / improved input datasets to help increase the accuracy of the predictive map outputs. The modelling work has also been extended to cover all of Welsh waters (previously cut-off at the Interreg funding boundary), notably including the Dee and Severn estuaries.    The purpose of this data capture was to provide seabed habitat maps that could be used for conservation and management. Project outputs might be used in strategic planning, decision making for offshore developments, Marine Protected Area selection, sensitivity mapping and mapping essential fish habitats. However, because of the way the  has been produced, and the fact that some data has been modelled and derived, the maps are not appropriate to act as the sole evidence for any specific planning or regulatory decision or assessment without further supporting studies or evidence.    The project boundaries were as follows: Southern Irish Sea- land-based boundaries include the whole Welsh coast to the English border on the east side of the Dee Estuary in the north, and the whole Severn Estuary and Bristol Channel coastline in the south, extending as far as Morte Point (east of Ifracombe) in England. The southern project boundary then extends offshore (skirting the northern tip of Lundy) across to a point approximate 60km west of Waterford on the Irish coast, including the whole SE Ireland coastline and offshore banks as well as parts of the Celtic Sea. The boundary then extends northwards along the Irish coast to a point approximately 40 km north of Dublin.-Sefydlwyd prosiect HABMAP mewn ymateb i’r angen am well ymwybyddiaeth gofodol o ddosbarthiad cynefinoedd ym Môr De Iwerddon. Mae’r gwaith hwn wedi cynhyrchu mapiau o gynefinoedd gwely’r môr gan ddefnyddio technegau modelau rhagfynegol arbennig. Mae Prosiect Ymestyn HABMAP wedi adeiladu ar y dulliau a ddatblygwyd yn y prosiect gwreiddiol, ac yn ailadrodd y gwaith modelu gan ddefnyddio cydraniad uwch / gwell setiau data mewnbwn er mwyn gwella cywirdeb y mapiau rhagfynegol.  Mae’r gwaith modelu wedi’i ymestyn hefyd i ymdrin â holl ddyfroedd Cymru (pennwyd y ffiniau gwreiddiol yn ôl ffiniau cyllidol Interreg), gan gynnwys aberoedd afonydd Dyfrdwy a Hafren.  Aethpwyd ati i gasglu’r data hwn er mwyn darparu mapiau o gynefinoedd gwely’r môr y gellid eu defnyddio er dibenion cadwraeth a rheoli. Mae’n bosibl defnyddio allbynnau’r prosiect hefyd ym maes cynllunio strategol, gwneud penderfyniadau ynghylch datblygiadau alltraeth, dethol Ardaloedd Morol Dan Warchodaeth, mapio sensitifrwydd a mapio cynefinoedd pysgodfeydd hanfodol. Fodd bynnag, oherwydd y modd y cynhyrchwyd y mapiau, ac oherwydd bod peth data wedi’i fodelu a’i ddeillio, nid yw’n briodol defnyddio’r mapiau fel yr unig dystiolaeth ar gyfer unrhyw gynllunio penodol neu benderfyniad neu asesiad rheoleiddio heb astudiaethau neu dystiolaeth ategol bellach.  Cydnabyddiaeth: Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe HABMAP project used a multi-parameter predictive modelling tool to develop seabed biotope maps for the Southern Irish Sea. Existing biological and physical datasets across the whole study area were collated within a Geographical Information System (GIS) and used to construct a rule-based biotope prediction model. Data were collated for the following physical parameters: bathymetry, sediment type, temperature, wave stress, tide stress, salinity and light attenuation. (NB Bedform data was not included in the extension project modelling work as it was found to be unreliable during the original project). These data were combined in GIS to produce a single map with many cells, each with a value for each of the physical parameters. Data was also collated for seabed biotopes and these points were overlain onto the physical data layer, allowing the relationships between each biotope and the physical environment to be determined. Preference ranges of each physical parameter were then identified for each biotope, leading to the development of predictive rules that could identify other cells with the same physical data ranges. This meant that maps could be produced showing the recorded and predicted distributions for each biotope. A final map was produced which listed all recorded and predicted biotopes in each cell, in order of confidence.\tThe data used to create the model output is knows as Input data parameter maps. These have restricted access. The British Geological Society (BGS) derived seabed sediment map; Temperature data was provided by the Met Office; Bathymetry layer derived from Seazone hydrospatial dataset. Tidal and wave dataset were supplied by ABPmer (for DEFRA data layers project). There is a need to check the conditions of use before dissemination.  The datasets will not be loaded to DataMapWales as individual layers with endpoints as the data is too complex and needs to be viewed as a package.  It will remain as ShareFile link only\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\HABMAP\\   or z:\\Archive\\legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\HABMAP   or Archived as Project 275 and Media 1029 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1001to1100\\Media 1029)\tInternal Contact: Karen Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-ABP Marine Environmental Research Ltd-British Geological Survey (BGS)-Met Office-SeaZone\t\t2017-08-11\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2618\t2026-01-12T15:58:06.551Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17944\tNRW_DS112540\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.970Z\t680\tValid\tEditable\tUnitisation Boundaries\tFfiniau Uno\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353430\tDesignated sites in Wales include, but is not limited to, Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), Special Protection Areas (SPA), Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) and Ramsar sites. These sites have been divided into management units. This dataset contains the boundaries of these units and includes information such as designation type, unit name, unit area, national grid reference and former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) regions.   Digitising began in 2007 and is on going as and when new sites are created or existing sites are altered. The purpose of these management units is to have a coherent plan in order to manage sites in a practical manner to ensure that work is done to get sites and their corresponding features into a favourable condition, as per the requirements of the protected sites.-Mae safleoedd dynodedig yng Nghymru yn cynnwys, ond heb fod yn gyfyngedig i, Safleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGA), Ardaloedd Gwarchodaeth Arbennig (AGA), Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACA) a safleoedd Ramsar. Caiff y safleoedd hyn eu rhannu\u0027n unedau rheoli. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys ffiniau\u0027r unedau hyn, ynghyd â gwybodaeth am y math o ddynodiad, enw\u0027r uned, arwynebedd, cyfeirnod grid cenedlaethol a hen ranbarthau Cyngor Cefn Gwlad Cymru (CCGC). Dechreuodd y gwaith digido yn 2007, ac maen dal i ddigwydd pan gaiff safleoedd newydd eu creu neu eu haddasu. Pwrpas yr unedau rheoli hyn yw cael cynllun cydlynol fel y gellir rheoli safleoedd mewn modd ymarferol er mwyn gwneud yn siŵr fod gwaith yn cael ei wneud i sicrhau bod y safleoedd a\u0027u nodweddion cyfatebol mewn cyflwr ffafriol, yn unol â gofynion y safleoedd gwarchodedig.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tManagement units are generally digitised using OS Mastermap 2006 (exceptions to this are noted in the MapInfo table). The data is then cleaned and entered into Oracle Spatial. When new protected sites are designated, or existing sites are amended, there can be a lag time for when the unitisation layer is updated to reflect the latest changes. Therefore this layers should not be used as the definitive source for showing protected site boundaries.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Unitization Boundaries.lyr\tChristine Edwards, Lead Specialist Advisor: Protected Sites Programme, Protected Sites\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-10-16\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/406/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_UNITIZATION\t2025-12-19T10:49:29.330Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17931\tNRW_DS112544\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.943Z\t379\tValid\tEditable\tDrop-Down Video Survey Location Inventory (1999 - 2010)\tRhestr Lleoliadau Arolygon Fideo Dan y Dŵr (1999 – 2010)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353434\tThis is a spatial dataset to show the location  of drop-down video surveys. This dataset acts as an inventory of dropdown video survey which enables staff to navigate drop-down video archive using and identifying specific geographic location of each video clip. The purpose of this data capture was to allow staff to use and access relevant spatial information.\tGIS layer extracted from Marine Recorder using transect centroid to represent the location of the video drop. Video clips attached using \u0027hotlink\u0027 function within MapInfo.  As the data has been transformed from MapInfo format to ArcGIS format, the hotlink function no longer works, however the pathways to the individual video clips is stored within the Attribute Table.  Please note that the marine video archive server has also changed and all video folders have been moved from W: drive and can now be accessed through  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\  (the rest of the pathway denoted in the attribute table remain as they were).\tDerived products from drop-down video surveys: 1999 Biotope Identification Data, 2004-2005 Across Wales Monitoring, 2007-2010 Across Wales Monitoring creating a user friendly inventory for the data.\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine habitat video points ,  Archived under project 277, media 1057 (pathway: Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1001to1100\\Media1057) Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\Marine_Habitat_Videos\tCharles Lindenbaum, Specialist (Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer), Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting,   Monica Jones, Data Services Team Leader.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:08:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17945\tNRW_DS112565\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.920Z\t460\tValid\tEditable\tOpen Access - Open Country (CRoW Act)\tMynediad Agored - Tir Agored\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353635\tThe CRoW Act provides for public access on foot to certain types of land, amends the law relating to public rights of way, increases measures for the management and protection for Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and strengthens wildlife enforcement legislation, whilst providing for better management of Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONB). Open Country 2014 is digitised to OS MasterMap. This data was part of the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000 (CroW Act). When using this data to understand the extent of Open Access , it should be used in conjunction with Open land, Open Country, Registered Common Land, Other Statutory Access Land and Other Dedicated Land from the same years. Registered Common Land is a faithful copy of Local Authority commons.-Mae Deddf Cefn Gwlad a Hawliau Tramwy yn darparu ar gyfer mynediad cyhoeddus ar droed i fathau penodol o dir, yn diwygio\u0027r gyfraith sy\u0027n ymwneud â hawliau tramwy cyhoeddus, yn cynyddu mesurau ar gyfer rheoli a gwarchod Safleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGA) ac yn cryfhau deddfwriaeth gorfodi bywyd gwyllt, wrth ddarparu ar gyfer gwell rheolaeth ar Ardaloedd o Harddwch Naturiol Eithriadol (AHNE). Mae Tir Agored 2014 wedi ei ddigido i MasterMap AO. Roedd y data hwn yn rhan o Ddeddf Cefn Gwlad a Hawliau Tramwy 2000. Wrth ddefnyddio\u0027r data hwn ar gyfer Mynediad Agored, dylid ei ddefnyddio ar y cyd â Thir Comin Cofrestredig, Tir Mynediad Statudol Arall a Choedwigoedd Cyhoeddus. Mae Tir Comin Cofrestredig yn gopi ffyddlon o dir comin Awdurdodau Lleol.  Cafodd Tir Agored ei ddigido i Landline AO yn wreiddiol gan ddefnyddio data Cam 1 rhwng 2000 a 2001. Yna aeth Tir Agored trwy Raglen Gwella Cywirdeb Lleoliad (PAI) i gyd-fynd â\u0027r cynnyrch MasterMap AO newydd. Yn adolygiad 10 mlynedd y prosiect Mynediad Agored gwnaed newidiadau trwy ymgynghori â\u0027r cyhoedd. Mae\u0027r data hwn yn cael ei reoli a\u0027i gadw gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru yn awr.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tOpen Country was originally digitised to OS Landline using Phase 1 data between 2000 and 2001. Open Country then went through Positional Accuracy Improvement Programme (PAI) to fit the new OS MasterMap product. The 10 Year review of the Open Access project saw amendments applied through public consultation. This data is now managed and held by Natural Resources Wales. The Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000 (CRoW Act 2000) applies to England and Wales only.\t\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Open Country 2014.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Open County 2004.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Chris Heaps, Specialist Advisor: Trails and Open Access Gary Eccleston/Geospatial team are responsible for the GIS digitisation and updates.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-16\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/393/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_OPEN_COUNTRY_2014\t2025-03-19T08:46:24.453Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17938\tNRW_DS112566\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.889Z\t590\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSarn Badrig Reef Sublittoral Survey 1993\tArolwg Isarforol o riff Sarn Badrig 1993\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353636\tThis dataset contains the results of a survey aiming to trial the three main methods used to describe, biotope recognition, biotope mapping, determining species presence, determining species abundance and cost effectiveness.  The three methods trialled were the BIOMAR/Roxanne methodology, the MNCR method and the Seasearch method.\tEach methodology was trialled in the same area of Sarn Badrig Reef.  - The BIOMAR methodology uses drop down video to interpret the acoustic data collected. Much of the Sarn Badrig reef was surveyed using the acoustic Roxanne system, and video was taken at 12 sites. Unfortunately video of only five of the sites survived however brief notes were made on the other sites before the images were lost. The video was used to describe the biotopes and the abundance of recognisable species was noted.  - The Seasearch methodology is a technique designed primarily for use by amateur divers. During this survey sports divers with varying degrees of survey and identification expertise filled in a Seasearch form for each site including records of species recognised. Some species were collected for identification on the surface. - The MNCR methodology has been designed for professional diving marine biologists to obtain detailed habitat and community descriptions. MNCR forms were filled in for 6 sites and algal specimens taken for later examination where necessary. Still photographs were taken at 5 of the sites.\t\tThe MNCR survey results have been entered into Marine Recorder (JNCCMNCR40000498) Paper copies of the report are available in NRW\u0027s library and the original report is held by Lucy Kay in NRW\u0027s North Region.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW North Region Marine Specialist, NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1994-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:19:51.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17939\tNRW_DS112571\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.966Z\t648\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLamprey Habitat Assessement in the River Usk 1999\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132353731\tThe River Usk has been notified as a site of Special Site of Scientific Interest (SSSI) and a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The three lamprey species which occur there are all listed in Annex II of the EU Species and Habitat Directive. The aim of this study was to investigate the status of lampreys in the Usk and to consider matters relevant to their conservation. Specific objectives of the study have been.  -  To define the characteristics of lamprey spawning and nursery habitat -  To obtain and summarise information on the distribution of lampreys and lamprey spawning and nursery habitat in the Usk -  To investigate the possibility of using information from RHS to predict the occurrence or lampreys in the Usk.  -  To make recommendations to assist with the monitoring and conservation of lampreys and their habitat. -  To monitoring the status of lampreys required to comply with the reporting requirements of  the Habitats Directive.\tDistribution of lampreys was determined by electric fishing at 44 sites throughout the main river and selected tributaries, and potential obstructions to upstream migration were assessed. Confidence in this dataset is high.\tRivers Ecologist, NRW.\t[A]  Associated is data is held within the Marine data Archive as project 280, media 1076.  [B]  Biological Data is held in Recorder 6 Lamprey Habitat Assessment in the River Usk 1998 - CCW - Report No. 365 CCW000280000000C\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:44:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17932\tNRW_DS112682\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.908Z\t342\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommon Scoter (Melanitta nigra) Summary Statistics and Distribution Analysis for Carmarthen Bay 1998 - 2010\tMor-hwyaid duon Melanitta nigra: ystadegau cryno a dadansoddiad o\u0027u dosbarthiad ym Mae Caerfyrddin 1998-2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132363832\tCarmarthen Bay, an extensive, shallow embayment off the south coast of Wales, UK, encompasses a wide range of rich and diverse coastal and marine habitats. Carmarthen Bay is an internationally important wintering ground for the Common Scoter duck (Melanitta nigra) and is widely regarded as the most important such site in the UK causing the site to be proposed as a Special Protection Area (pSPA) under the EC Birds Directive. Former Countryside Council for Wales (now part of Natural Resources Wales) have supported ground and aerial based surveys for a 10 year period since the Sea Empress oil spill in 1996. The purpose of this data capture was to collate and synthesise records on common scoter populations and distributions for a 10 year period. Data was collated from old CCW reports. A summary of the data  has been produced to cover the period 1998 to 2010.\tA sustained program of monitoring common scoters in Carmarthen Bay has been in place since 1997.  Data is published in five CCW Marine Monitoring Reports (no. 11, 48, 64, 83, 84). Details Although data was collected using a standard method, data display, format and analysis varied with each data collection contract. Methods used to continue the program of aerial and ground-based survey were broadly similar to those reported in 2002, except in that in 2008 only one aerial survey was conducted and this did not coincide with ground counts.  - A total of 20 survey events were achieved in 2002/03, with a further 23 in 2003/04. - A total of four survey events were achieved in 2004/05; 9 in 2005/06; and 11 in 2006/07. - A total of 3 ground survey events and 1 aerial survey event were achieved in 2007/08. - A total of 2 ground based surveys events took place in 2008/09. - A total of 3 ground base survey events took place in 2009/10. The collation and analysis of this data has allowed the data to be formatted in a standard way and analysed against a standard background grid cell layer to produce statistics for the winter period for aerial counts and monthly statistics for ground counts.  Detailed processing notes are available with the dataset.\t\tSummary data is archived under project 213, media 579 within the Marine Data Archive (W drive).   Source raw data is not archived with this project as the data is already in the archive with the project it was collected under\tData Custodians Marine Ornithologist, NRW or Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:09:21.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17943\tNRW_DS112723\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.968Z\t742\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTir Cymen Farm Management Plans for Wales (Paper Records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132373233\tManagement plans for a number of holdings throughout Wales that are part of the Tir Cymen Farm Scheme. Each plan consists of personal details including the names and address of the owner and tenant of the holding; the type of farm enterprise; the general management practice of the holding including livestock type, use of common land and the growth of crops; the changes in management to conform to Tir Cymen guidelines; schedules of annual management payments and standard capital payments; paper maps showing the extent of the holding and surrounding area, the different habitat types surrounding the holding and the capital works being completed around the holding. The plans also list the whole farm management code which includes listing the archaeological features, land forms and water and wetlands found around the holding. The purpose of data capture was to ensure selected land holdings meet the Tir Cymen Farm Scheme.\tDirect communication with owner/tenant; land surveys; direct observations were recorded.\t\tOff-site storage (contact library team)\tBuddug Jones, Common Land and Agriculture Advisor, Ecosystem Services Team/ Natural Resources Planning, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1993-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:10.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17927\tNRW_DS112726\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.694Z\t696\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTir Cymen Farms - Raw Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132373236\tRaw data to support the management plans for a number of holdings throughout Wales that are part of the Tir Cymen Farm Scheme. The raw data includes macro landscape assessment records taken in each of the areas over a five year period; characterisation records; species plot records; cover plot records; boundary plot records, management maps. The purpose of this data capture was to ensure selected land holdings meet and continue to meet the Tir Cymen Farm Scheme.\tDirect communication with owner/tenant; land surveys; direct observations. Contributes to Tir Cymen Farm Management Plans for Wales.\t\tOff-site storage (contact library team)\tInternal Custodian: Buddug Jones, Common Land and Agriculture Advisor, Ecosystem Services Team/ Natural Resources Planning.  Metadata contact: Information Services Officer, Records and Information Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1996-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:07:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17937\tNRW_DS112727\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.940Z\t437\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Twaite (Alosa fallax) and Allis (Alosa alosa) Shad egg survey of the River Usk (2010-2011)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132373237\tThe River Usk rises in the Black Mountain range in the west of the Brecon Beacons National Park and flows east and then south, to enter the Severn Estuary at Newport.  The river is designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for several features, including salmon, lampreys and shads.  Twaite (Alosa fallax) and allis (Alosa alosa) shad are anadromous members of the Family Clupeidae (Herring).  In Britain, twaite shad is known to spawn in the Rivers Severn, Wye, Usk and Tywi.  However, it is not certain whether a breeding population of allis shad still exists.  The fomer Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), and now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) is responsible for monitoring the condition of shad populations within SACs and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within these designated sites. The JNCC \u0027Common Standards Methodology Guidance for Freshwater Fauna\u0027 (2005) gives a target for the spawning distribution of shad.  It states there should be \u0027no decline in spawning distribution\u0027 assessed by kick sampling during May and June.  There are two main data sources of shad spawning in the River Usk.  The Environment Agency Wales (EAW), now Natural Resources Wales recorded the presence or absence of shad eggs at a number of sites, from 1990 to 2005, although surveys have only been conducted annually since 2003.\tThe data and report outlines the monitoring undertaken by former CCW and EAW staff on the spawning distribution of shad during the spawning seasons of 2010 and 2011.   - 2010  Surveys and evidence of spawning was recorded from the 26th of May to 22nd of June. - 2011  Surveys and evidence of spawning was recorded from the 19th of May to 6th of July. Natural Resources Wales also hold records of visible shad spawning at one location (Usk Town, NGR SO374007) for 5 consecutive years from 1997 to 2001.  The standard Hillman et al. (2003) methodology was amended to record the presence or absence of shad eggs only. A minimum of 10 kick samples were undertaken at each site with the number of eggs found in each kick recorded. If in total 10 shad eggs were recorded the survey was terminated.  However, if less than 10 shad eggs were recorded the kick sampling continued either until over 10 eggs were found or a maximum of 25 kick samples had been undertaken. Confidence in data: High - work carried out by qualified CCW monitoring staff.\tRivers Ecologist, NRW.\t[A]  2010 survey data is held in the Marine Data Archive as Project 281 and Media 1096.  [B]  2011 survey data is held in the Marine Data Archive as Project 326, Media 1283.  [C]  Biological Data is held in Recorder 6: -  Shad Egg Survey River Usk 2010 - CCW - Report no.10/8/1 CCW000280000000E   -  Shad Egg Survey River Usk 2011 - CCW - Report no.11/8/3  CCW0008000000009\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T15:40:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17934\tNRW_DS112739\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.829Z\t13\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Species in Wales (Archived 2008)\tCynefinoedd a Rhywogaethau\u0027r Cynllun Gweithredu Bioamrywiaeth Morol yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132373339\tThe UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) was published back in 1994, and was the UK Government’s response to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD), which the UK signed up to in 1992 in Rio de Janeiro. Similarly under Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act, 2006 it is required that the a \u0027list of the living organisms and types of habitat which in the Assembly\u0027s opinion are of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\u0027. The UK BAP is one of the main focuses of this work.  As part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 CBD and NERC Act there have been a number of initiatives to identify those species and habitats whose UK populations may be under threat and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity.  The list includes all marine UK BAP priorities as well as additional habitats and species to reflect those in need of conservation action in Wales. For each species and habitat data provided it should include a map of the distribution around the Welsh coast and supporting text with information on the ecology, rarity, legislative protection, occurrence in statutory sites and known threats. This data was captured to contribute towards providing all public bodies and Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) partnerships with information on the marine habitats and species included in the Section 42 list. This work included intertidal habitat searches for biotopes in the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Phase 1 dataset, done using 1997 biotope classification codes. Biotopes associated with \u0027Section 42\u0027 habitats were compiled using the 2004 biotope codes. Subtidal habitats were plotted using records of biotopes and/or species from Marine Recorder (Snapshot data dated November 2007).\tThis dataset is an amalgamation of data from two existing Welsh BAP reports- John Moore (CALM) produced a report on the marine BAP species and habitats and Species of Conservation Concern (SoCC) in 2002, then in 2008 Sea-Scope Marine Environmental Consultants provided additional reviews for the species and habitats identified as BAP priorities in 2007.  The data collation included the use of Marine Recorder to query the plot distribution of six chosen species. Habitats were plotted from sources such as CCW Phase 1 spatially (GIS) mapped biotope layers, and target notes. The validity of records were checked. Records that were considered doubtful due to the habitat, geographic location and known inexperience of the recorder, were checked thoroughly and excluded if not corroborated.\tThis dataset has now been updated and superseded by a more up-to-date version of this dataset.  This dataset is kept as an archive only. Users are advised to refer to more recent data NRW_DS111154.\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP Species Archived.     Data is archived under Project No. 231, Media 600.   This dataset has now been updated and superseded by a more up-to-date version of this dataset known as Section 7 species.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Biodiversity Liaison Officer (Ben Wray?), NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17933\tNRW_DS112903\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.834Z\t318\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools in Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2008\tMonitro pyllau glan mor ACA Bae Ceredigion ASML/y Cyngor Cefn Gwlad 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313132393033\tIntertidal surveys provide comprehensive baseline data for monitoring change or for the identification of locations sensitive to certain anthropogenic impacts. The data also provides information to support a notification programme or intertidal Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) thereby providing a series of intertidal designated sites across Wales.  Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation  Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated primarily for Bottlenose dolphins, but is also designated for Sandbanks, Reefs and Sea Caves. Of these Reefs and Sea Caves are present within intertidal areas of Cardigan Bay SAC. Rockpools are also a notable feature of some intertidal reef areas in the SAC. Phase 1 surveys of intertidal habitats in the SAC were carried out in the SAC in 1996 and 2003 (Brazier et al. 2007), providing detailed mapping of the intertidal biotopes with some information on characterizing species. The data represents the results of a survey of intertidal rockpools at Aberporth, one of two sites surveyed during a similar survey undertaken in 2007, within the Cardigan Bay SAC.\tThe 14 rockpools that were identified and surveyed in 2007 were re-located and re-surveyed in May 2008 - 8 on a mid-shore rock platform and 6 on an area of upper shore bedrock. See Moore (2009) for details on the rockpool selection criteria. Photographs (usually 2 from different sides) were first taken of the whole pool, with gridded quadrats (1 m2 or combination of 1 m2 and 0.25 m2) laid over it. Photograph numbers were recorded on the survey form. Basic measurements of depth and length were not taken in 2008 as they were assumed to be the same as 2007. The presence of sediment on the pool bottom was recorded. The influence of silt and scour was estimated on a 5-point scale. The presence of typical pool taxa (\u0027Aggregate taxa\u0027 and \u0027Frequently recorded taxa\u0027 ) were then searched for and recorded if present. Estimates of percentage cover were made for algae and other cover species.  Notable examples of rockpool habitat in the SAC were mapped by the CCW Phase 1 survey (Brazier et al. 2007). Boyes et al. (2009) carried out some survey work in a large shallow rockpool on Cei Bach. A follow-up survey, using in-situ quadrat recording methods, was carried out in the Cei Bach pool in May by ASML and CCW. A methodology for monitoring small discrete rockpools was developed by ASML and CCW in 2007; and monitoring sites at Aberporth were established and surveyed in May 2007 (Moore 2009).\t\tThe data and the report are archived as Project No 199, Media no. 548 and 555.  [A] This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters and stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW headquarters on optical media. [B]  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Cardigan Bay\\2008 ASML Cardigan BayData\tCustodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata point of contact:  Master Reference Data Team.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:09:57.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17928\tNRW_DS113087\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.618Z\t593\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Survey Mapping (phase 1) from Pen-ychain to Criccieth, Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) 2010\tYr arolwg o\u0027r ardal rynglanwol o Ben-ychain i Gastell Cricieth ar arfordir deheuol Pen Llyn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313133303837\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. The purpose of this data capture was to conduct a habitat survey of the intertidal area from Pen-ychain to Criccieth Castle on the south coast of the Llyn Peninsula. This work supported the detailed SAC monitoring program by providing a more up to date baseline survey of the intertidal area. It provided CCW with information to help deal with current and future casework in relation to coastal defence, and reporting particularly regarding BAP habitats and species of conservation concern. One such Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitat are the Sabellaria alveolata reefs which are formed by the honeycomb worm Sabellaria alveolata, a polychaete which constructs tubes in tightly packed masses with a distinctive honeycomb-like appearance. These reefs can be up to 30 or even 50 cm thick and take the form of hummocks, sheets or more massive formations. Reefs are mainly found on the bottom third of the shore, but may reach mean high water of neap tides and extend into the shallow subtidal in places. The current report aims to fill these knowledge gaps for the survey area.  The purpose of this data capture is to update previous intertidal mapping in the area and to specifically provide an updated extent of honeycomb worm reef Sabellaria alveolata. The specific project aims were to identify natural temporal changes that have been witnessed since the original Phase 1 Intertidal Mapping Project in 1996-1997.\tThe fieldwork phase was completed in October 2010; dates chosen due to suitably timed Low Water on spring tides.  The survey was conducted by staff from Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd. and support provided by the Intertidal Ecologist at CCW.  Intertidal habitat mapping methodologies were used as described in Wyn et al (2006) and using the 2004 biotope classification described in Conner et al (2004).  The area was first mapped in July 1996 as part of the 10 year long CCW Phase 1 Intertidal project, that mapped Intertidal habitats for the entire coastline of Wales.\t\tThe data are archived as Project No 289, Media No 1192 [K:\\Archive\\Media 1101-1200\\Media 1192]. Arc LayerY:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Pen Llyn SAC Monitoring.gdb\tPaul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team. Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T10:25:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17923\tNRW_DS113445\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.989Z\t377\tValid\tEditable\tModelling the Predicted Spread of a Carpet Sea Squirt ( Didemnum vexillum ) around the Welsh Coast (2011)\tModelu lledaeniad disgwyliedig Didemnum vexillum o amgylch arfordir Cymru 2011\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313133343435\tDidemnum vexillum is an invasive sea squirt that is not native to UK shores. It was first detected in Europe in 1991 and has since spread to several countries (including France, Ireland and the UK). The species has been located in Wales, Scotland and England and there is concern D. vexillum may have negative impacts on biodiversity and shellfish interests.    Predicting the spread of an invasive species is crucial when assessing possible management actions. The potential impacts of the species on both biodiversity and commercial interests need to be studied and a cost-benefit approach taken to decide on the best course of management for that species.    Geographic Information System (GIS) offers a fast, efficient way to map this predicted spread. The results of this mapping can then be used to focus on areas where D. vexillum may conflict with conservation and commercial interests.\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW) has been investigating and controlling the spread of D. vexillum in Holyhead Marina since 2009.  For this project, all the GIS spatial processing was carried out in ArcMAP 9.3 and used the ArcMAP toolbox along with Hawth\u0027s Tools and ET Geowizzards toolboxes.  Data was used from 2009 - 2011 and a  predictability model used which may have some limitations. In order to predict the potential spread of D. vexillum a simple modelling approach can be used, which uses the species biological limits and current known populations to estimate the positions of future colonies. Whilst this approach quickly produces results, it is limited as the model is not quantitative and can not be adequately tested.\t\tData is archived as Project No. 298, media No. 1208 (File path is Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 1208)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata point of contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-01-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:10:58.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17925\tNRW_DS113460\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.071Z\t537\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2008 CCW / ASML Carmarthen Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Cockle Bed Survey\tArolwg o Wely Cocos ACA Bae Caerfyrddin CCGC / ASML 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313133343630\tThe Habitats Directive requires that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II.  For SACs in Wales, the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) was therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  To do this the former CCW developed a programme of feature condition monitoring.  Aquatic Survey and Monitoring Ltd. (ASML) have been contracted by CCW to develop and manage the monitoring programme for the intertidal features in marine SACs for the period 2006 to 2011; working as a team with CCW HQ and CCW Regional staff.  In Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are Large shallow inlets and bays, estuaries and Mudflats and sandflats not covered by sea water at low tide.  Specific areas of interest include the extensive sediment flats of the 3 rivers (Taf, Tywi and Gwendraeth), Loughor estuary (Burry inlet), open coast sands and notable rocky habitats, particularly in west Carmarthen Bay.  This report and dataset contains the results of the surveys carried out in those areas in June 2007.   The forms part of the monitoring that CCW also developed on intertidal monitoring across Wales during 2004 and 2005.  These surveys were managed and implemented for CCW by the Institute of Estuarine and Coastal Studies (IECS, University of Hull).  These projects focused on a wide range of sensitive habitats such as Zostera, muddy gravels, caves, rockpools, algal-dominated rocky shores, Sabellaria reefs, under-boulders, and various rare habitats and species.  In Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC, IECS surveyed Ruppia maritima (Boyes 2008a), Zostera noltii beds (Mazik \u0026amp; Boyes 2009), the biotope SLR.FserX.T (Boyes 2008b), the polychaete Ophelia bicornis (Mazik 2008), hydroid dominated rockpools (Boyes et al. 2004), saltmarsh boundary (Boyes \u0026amp; Brazier 2008) and the sediment flats across the SAC (Allen et al. 2008).\tRepeat transects were undertaken across the shores of Carmarthen Bay, 3 Rivers and Burry Inlet.  The survey consisted of in-situ recording and the collection of core samples for infauna and particle size analyses.  The work involved working along 3 transects, to repeat work completed in 2005 and 2007.  Sediment cores were collected and analysed from 31 stations, at locations previously sampled in 2005 and 2007.  Additional sediment surveys were completed on Pembrey Sands to account for a known bird feeding area outside of the sheltered inlets.  Descriptive surveys of the biotopes present along 3 transects in the 3 Rivers estuaries (Laugharne, Llansteffan, Gwendraeth), were carried out with a modification of standard MNCR Phase 2 methodology (Hiscock 1996).  The twenty transects were originally pre-selected by CCW to represent the variety of sediment biotopes present around the Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC, whilst those concentrated on in 2008 were specifically to respond to concerns of the condition of the habitats in the estuaries.  Core samples for standard laboratory analysis of macrofauna and granulometry were taken from five or more sediment stations at each location (transect). Core sampling - Three core samples were taken from each of the selected sampling stations, using a 0.01 m2 corer.  The cores were taken within a radius of approximately 5 m from the central mark; and randomly placed without reference to the surface features (with eyes closed!).  Each core was then sieved over a 0.5 mm mesh and the sieve contents transferred to a suitable labelled pot.  These samples were then taken back to the field survey base and preserved in 10% formalin. The work involved working along 3 transects, to repeat work completed in the area in both 2005 and 2007. All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  Data quality and confidence is considered high.\t\tThe data are archived as Project No. 199, Media No. 549 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 549) whilst the report is held within Media No. 1218 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1201-1300\\Media 1218.This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW Maes y Fynnon. [A]  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm Bay\\2008 ASML Carm [B]  Stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Fynnon on optical media.  [C]  GIS data Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Carmarthen Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb [D] Marine Recorder file (survey number MRCCW16300000012)\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Internal Metadata point of contact - Master Reference Data Team  or Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:11:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17920\tNRW_DS113480\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.783Z\t259\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Non-Native Atlas for Wales 2010\tAtlas Anfrodorol Morol y Cyngor Cefn Gwlad ar gyfer Cymru 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313133343830\tThe dataset contains a series of records for each of the non-native marine species currently identified as occurring in Wales. The records have been extracted from Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCWs) Phase 1 Intertidal surveys, Marine Recorder data holdings, National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Gateway web source, MarLIN/DASSH web source, DEFRA Marine Protected Area (MPA) non-native GIS layers, Local Environmental Record Centres (LERC\u0027s ), and other ad-hoc records collated by CCW staff or supplied by third parties. The purpose of this data collation is to monitor the spread of non-native marine species in Wales by creating a current and meaningful atlas using GIS layers for individual layers.\tVersion 1 of the Atlas. This is a collation of records and therefore may be related to varied and specific methodologies associated with individual surveys e.g. Phase 1 Intertidal, Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) Phase II and marine monitoring surveys.  The data was extracted for source datasets and imported to MapInfo as British National Grid (BNG) projection. The majority of records are collected by specialists. Ad-hoc records are verified where possible.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species  Archived under project 286, media no. 1184. Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Species\\all_wales\\Marine\\NonNatives\tCustodian: Marine and Freshwater Group or GIS and Spatial Data Team. Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T10:26:10.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17924\tNRW_DS113767\t2024-05-28T12:35:30.078Z\t427\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFeasibility Study on the Use of Dispersants in the Bristol Channel in the Event of an Oil Pollution Incident (2002)\tAstudiaeth ddichonoldeb ar ddefnyddio gwasgarwyr ym Mor Hafren pe bai damwain olew yn digwydd 2002\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313133373637\tThe approval and use of oil spill dispersants is regulated in UK waters. The Food and Environment Protection Act 1985 requires that a licence be issued for the deposit of any substance or article in the sea or that an exemption be granted under the Deposits in the Sea (Exemptions) Order 1985. The licensing authority for England and Wales is DEFRA (Department of Environment, Food and Rural Affairs (formerly MAFF - Ministry of Agriculture, Fisheries and Food).   Specific permission to use oil spill dispersants in shallow water (defined as areas of sea where the water depth is less than 20 metres, as marked on Admiralty charts, or within 1 nautical mile of such area) must be granted by DEFRA. The role of the EG will be to provide information about the sensitivities of resources in the area to oil pollution, so that DEFRA can make a reasoned decision on dispersant use.   The BCSEG considered many different relevant factors including the oil spill risks, likely spilled oil behaviour, characteristics of the Bristol Channel and the shoreline and marine resources that could be affected by spilled oil. The consequences of dispersing or not dispersing spilled oil were also considered. As a result of these deliberations, the areas of the Bristol Channel and Severn Estuary have been designated as dispersant use or non-use zones.  These zones are marked on maps for ease of reference. These zones are:  - Zone A Where dispersants can be used at any time of the tide  - Zone B Where dispersants can be used (but only with DEFRA\u0027s specific permission) on an outgoing tide from High Water until High Water + 5 hours  - Zone C Where dispersants can be used (but only with DEFRA\u0027s specific permission) on an outgoing tide from High Water until High Water + 2 hours  - Zone D Where dispersants are not to be used at any time .  In the event of oil pollution occurring in the Bristol Channel an Environment Group (EG) will be activated to consider the environmental consequences of the spill and the most suitable ways in which damage can be minimised. The use of oil spill dispersants is a response method that might be used. The Bristol Channel Standing Environment Group (BCSEG) commissioned this report to describe the factors in the Bristol Channel that need to be considered when deciding to use, or not to use, oil spill dispersants.\tThe Bristol Channel has the second largest tidal range in the world and has been the focus of many hydrodynamic studies. A recent study, Bristol Channel Marine Aggregates - Resources and Constraints (Posford Duvivier, 2000) contains comprehensive details of many aspects of the Bristol Channel, including: - Tides (range, flow patterns and residual currents) - Wind (directions and speeds) - Waves (height and direction) - Sediment distributions - Macrofauna distributions  The Severn Estuary / Mor Hafren Shoreline Management Plan (Gifford Associated Consultants, 1998) also contains many detailed maps of many physical characteristics such as seabed sediments. No new studies have been carried out on these aspects for the preparation of the report. Data is unique and irreplaceable. Important operational and decision making data.\t\tData is archived in the NRW data archive as project 299, media no.1209\tContact the Data Services Team Internal Custodian: Marine and Freshwater Evidence Manager, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:14:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17922\tNRW_DS114189\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.811Z\t384\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGround Monitoring of Common Scoter Melinitta nigra in Carmarthen Bay (2010 - 2011)\tMonitro mor-hwyaid duon Melinitta nigra ar y ddaear ym Mae Caerfyrddin 2010 - 2011\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134313839\tCarmarthen Bay Spatial Protection Area (SPA) was classified as the UK\u0027s first SPA in 2003 on the basis of holding at least 1% (16,000 individuals) of the biogeographic population of Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra). The Common Scoter is strongly migratory and often travels considerable distances over land making brief stop-overs on inland waters. Its diet consists predominantly of molluscs, especially during the winter, although it occasionally takes other aquatic invertebrates such as crustaceans (e.g. barnacles and shrimps), worms, echinoderms, isopods, amphipods and insects (e.g. midges and caddis flies) as well as small fish and fish eggs which are found in abundance in the shallow water in Carmarthen Bay. Common scoter are also listed as a Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) has supported ground based counts since 1996, following the Sea Empress oil spill. This work feeds in to the condition monitoring and surveillance of Carmarthen Bay SPA and contributes to Common Standards Monitoring.\tThree ground counts of common scoters (and other species) were made in Carmarthen Bay between December 2010 and February 2011.  These sites were Pembrey Sand Dunes, Dolwen Point near Pendine and Merrifields, Amroth. An additional site at Spaniard Rocks above Burry Holms was visited in January and February to check scoter abundance. Burry Holms was favoured above the count station at Kitchen Point, Rhossili, which has previously been used, as 10,000 common scoter had been recorded from this location on 2nd January 2011. All relevant local conditions were recorded and counts were only taken during suitable weather and visibility conditions and where possible counts coincided with high tide.\tA sustained program of monitoring scoters in Carmarthen bay has been in place since 1997.\tData is archived under project no 301 (media 1211).\tInternal Custodians - Marine Ornithologist, NRW - Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata Contact: - Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T10:26:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17921\tNRW_DS114338\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.812Z\t307\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sediment Transects in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2008\tMonitro rhynglanwol o Waddidion Trawsluniau ym Mae Caerfyrddin ac Aberoedd ACA yn 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134333338\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. The purpose of this data capture was to conduct Intertidal Special Area of Conservation monitoring.  Transect survey of sediment flats in Camarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC in June 2007. In Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are Large shallow inlets and bays, estuaries and Mudflats and sandflats not covered by sea water at low tide. Rockpools, overhangs and caves have also been identified as important elements of the large shallow inlets and bays feature and of Sites of Special Scientific Interest and metadata for these datasets are documented elsewhere.\tDescriptive surveys of the biotopes present along 3 transects in the 3 Rivers estuaries (Laugharne, Llansteffan, Gwendraeth), were carried out with a modification of standard MNCR Phase 2 methodology (Hiscock 1996).  The twenty transects were originally pre-selected by CCW to represent the variety of sediment biotopes present around the Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC (Allen et al 2008; Moore 2009), whilst those concentrated on in 2008 were specifically to respond to concerns of the condition of the habitats in the estuaries.  Core samples for standard laboratory analysis of macrofauna and granulometry were taken from five or more sediment stations at each location (transect).  All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  Data quality and confidence considered high\t\tArchive project 199, media 1218. Data is held within Marine Recorder as No. MRCCW16300000012\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T10:27:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17919\tNRW_DS114339\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.749Z\t268\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools, Caves and Overhangs in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2008\tArolwg rhynglanw o Byllau Glan Mor, Ogofau a Gordoi yn ACA Bae Caerfyrddin a Morydiau 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134333339\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  Rockpools, overhangs and caves have been identified as important elements of the \u0027large shallow inlets and bays\u0027 feature and of Sites of Special Scientific Interest. These potentially species rich rocky habitats were surveyed in Carmarthen Bay to provide an inventory and to fully characterise these sub-features.  The purpose of this data capture was to provide an inventory and distribution map of selected biotopes and species inventory across South Wales.\tA brief description of the habitat and community(s) present within each feature was recorded in situ. These descriptions typically included the size of the feature, its level on the shore, the dominant and characterising species or taxa, and other notable features of condition or potential anthropogenic impacts. Photographs were taken to illustrate each feature. Species lists were taken for each of the features studied.\t\tArchive project 199, media no. 1218 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1218) Species data available in Marine Recorder [MRCCW1630000001A] Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Carmarthen Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T10:28:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17918\tNRW_DS114387\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.626Z\t377\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Open Coast Sediment (Sandflats and Mudflats) in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2009\tMonitro gwaddodion arfordir agored ACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau (gwastadeddau tywod a gwastadeddau llaid) 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134333837\tThe aim of this data capture was to proceed with the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) intertidal monitoring.  Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) developed a programme of intertidal monitoring work across Wales during 2004 and 2005. These surveys were managed and implemented for CCW by the Institute of Estuarine and Coastal Studies (IECS, University of Hull). These projects focused on a wide range of sensitive habitats such as Zostera, muddy gravels, caves, rock pools, algal dominated rocky shores, Sabellaria reefs, under-boulders, and various rare habitats and species.  Aquatic Survey and Monitoring Ltd. (ASML) have been contracted by CCW to continue development and management of the intertidal monitoring programme for each marine SAC for the period 2006 to 2011, working as a team with CCW HQ and CCW Regional staff.\tMacrobenthic core samples were taken from 4 selected beach sites for standard laboratory analysis of the macrobenthic infaunal biotope and the sediment granulometry.  All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors. Data quality and confidence considered high.\t\tThe data and report are archived as Project No 109, Media No 1219 and stored in fire-proof storage facilities at NRW Maes y Fynnon, on optical media. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage.H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2009 ASML PLAS\\\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T10:29:03.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17917\tNRW_DS114388\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.566Z\t521\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria reef in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2009\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134333838\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary, tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats). CCW developed a programme of intertidal monitoring work across Wales during 2004 and 2005. These surveys were managed and implemented for CCW by the Institute of Estuarine and Coastal Studies (IECS, University of Hull). These projects focused on a wide range of sensitive habitats such as Zostera, muddy gravels, caves, rock pools, algal dominated rocky shores, Sabellaria reefs, under-boulders, and various rare habitats and species.   Aquatic Survey and Monitoring Ltd. (ASML) have been contracted by CCW to continue development and management of the intertidal monitoring programme for each marine SAC for the period 2006 to 2011, working as a team with CCW HQ and CCW Regional staff.\tAt each transect location, surveyors walked down the transect using the GPS as described above and made notes on habitat, community and condition / age of the Sabellaria reef. Condition of Sabellaria reef was recorded using a defined 7 point scale. The age of the Sabellaria reef was recorded using a defined 4 point scale. GPS fixes were taken along the transect to mark changes in Sabellaria reef condition and to mark the top and bottom of the transect (often the water line). Photographs were taken to illustrate habitat and condition of the Sabellaria reef.\t\tData is archived under: Project 190, media 1219\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:08.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17916\tNRW_DS114436\t2024-05-28T12:35:29.538Z\t417\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rockpools in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2007 to 2013\tGwaith Monitro Rhynglanwol ar Byllau Glan Mor yn ACA Sir Benfro Forol 2007-2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343336\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. In Pembrokeshire Marine SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features include Reefs. The specific area sub-feature of interest covered here are the rockpools of Pen-y-holt on the wave exposed intertidal limestone reefs of South Pembrokeshire.  The aim of this data capture was to support a report of the Favourable conservation status of SAC features through intertidal monitoring. This data capture is related to studies carried out in these rockpools between 2007 and 2010.\tTen rockpools were studied at Pen-y-holt, five in the upper middle shore and five in the middle shore. The locations of the rockpools were referenced by photographs supplemented by GPS positions and photographs and visited on three sampling occasions in the late summer (August or September) of 2007, 2009 and 2010. Note - No data exists for 2008 as the site was not surveyed due to adverse weather conditions.  The surveys consisted of in situ recording and the collection of samples for identification in the laboratory where required. The same methodology was employed in each rockpool with percentage cover of seaweeds being recorded together with presence or absence of animals. Percentage cover of groups of species termed ‘aggregate taxa’ (e.g. green seaweeds) were also recorded.\t\tThe report and data collected under CCW contract FC 73-02-342 is archived as Project No 199, Media Nos 1221 and is maintained on a backed-up server based storage at NRW . H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2010 ASML Pembs\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-10-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T10:29:41.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17962\tNRW_DS114451\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.674Z\t693\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDiver Observations on Arctic Charr (Salvelinus alpinus) Spawning Grounds in Llyn Padarn Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) - December 2010\tSylwadau\u0027r nofiwr tanddwr ar leoedd epilio\u0027r Torgoch (salvelinus alpinus L.) yn SoDdGA Llyn Padarn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343531\tLlyn Padarn is a large, natural lake located at the foot of Snowdon alongside the village of Llanberis, North Wales. The lake is designated as a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) for its rare, genetically distinct Arctic charr (Salvelinus alpinus) population, the presence of the nationally scarce Annex II aquatic macrophyte, Luronium natans L. (Floating Water-plantain) and the exposure of geologically important Cambrian rock sequences. The Arctic charr population is one of only three naturally occurring populations in Wales and one of the southernmost in the UK. There has been concern in relation to the declining Arctic charr population in Llyn Padarn and the potential impact of the water quality of the lake on the population. This study was undertaken to investigate potential spawning areas in the lake for Arctic charr by a SCUBA diving survey. Knowledge of the location of the spawning grounds of charr in Llyn Padarn is important to inform management of the site for conservation of the species. The purpose of this data capture was to collected information so that any additional spawning grounds (to the one location known) could be identified along with any potentially important spawning sites.\tThe scuba dive survey of the shoreline was then undertaken on the 15th of December 2010 by four divers, operating in pairs. The zodiac inflatable boat was launched from SH57376086 and the lake surveyed anti-clockwise. Generally the shore areas were investigated at 6 sites around the lake to a maximum depth of 8m. The known spawning area in Afon y Bala was also investigated. Areas considered as potential redds and the general substrate were photographed.  The project objects were as follows: 1.  Undertake a scuba dive survey in Llyn Padarn in December 2010. 2. Collect any charr eggs found for subsequent identification. 3. Record any potential charr spawning areas. 4. Record the substrate material at survey locations.  Repeat footage was taken in 2011\tThomas, Rh. \u0026amp; Holt, R 2011  Diver observations on Arctic charr (Salvelinus alpinus L.) spawning grounds in Llyn Padarn SSSI.  CCW Staff Science Report No.  11/8/2. 28pp.\tData is archived within the MFSG data archive as project no. 302 and media no\u0027s 1214 and 1227.   Video is held on the imaging archive at V:\\MMT video Archive\\Tristan Freshwater\\2010 Llyn Padarn Arctic Charr Media1214 Additional footage is archived as  project 335, media 1301 and currently held on:  V:\\MMT video Archive\\Tristan Freshwater\\2011 Llyn Padan (Arctic Char) Media 1301 V:\\MMT video Archive\\Tristan Freshwater\\2011 1221 Llyn Padarn Arctic Charr RH Media 1301\tInternal Custodian: Rivers Ecologist, MFSG, NRW Contact:  Data Services for metadata information\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:15:29.383Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17973\tNRW_DS114452\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.709Z\t710\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRestoration of Native Oyster, Ostrea edulis, in South Wales\tAdfer yr wystrysen Ostrea edulis yn Ne Cymru: Dewisiadau a Dulliau / Restoration of Native Oyster, Ostrea edulis, in South Wales: Options and Approaches\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343532\tNative oyster populations throughout Europe suffered a serious decline in the late 19th and early 20th centuries due predominantly to over fishing and associated habitat loss, but exacerbated by anthropogenic and environmental pressures of pollution, disease and a series of cold winters. Historic accounts place Ostrea edulis at the centre of important local fisheries in South Wales back to the Roman occupation of Britain and describe a fishery supporting 200 vessels landing over 9 million oysters at its peak in the 19th century. Ostrea edulis are found on a wide variety of firm and stable seabed habitats where clean hard surfaces exist for larval settlement but it is likely that more complex habitat forms have historically existed including oyster banks and reefs. The aim of this study was to identify the scope and potential approaches for restoration of native oyster populations and habitats in South Wales. The associated report describes the options for practical approaches for restoration of the native oyster in its natural habitat in South Wales to meet UK and Welsh Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) targets for recovery.\tReview of biological and ecological factors, and mapping of the physical environmental factors combined with a review of historical and current documentation and consultation with local fishermen. Historical records date back to the late 1800\u0027s. Newer records are as recent as 2010. Reference dates for each bed are included within the data. A wide variety of data sources were utilised but an assessment of confidence was made for each location.\t\tData is archived under project no. 306, media no. 1220 (File path is K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1220)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team [previously  -Aethne Cooke (ex CCW), Marine Biodiversity Liaison Officer, NRW] Metadata Contact: Data Services, Evidence Knowledge and Information Management, KSP, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-03-16\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:18:21.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17966\tNRW_DS114453\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.647Z\t382\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Mapping Project (Updating Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Biotope Mapping Survey)\tProsiect Mapio rhynglanwol (yn diweddaru arolwg mapio biotop Rhynglanwol Morol Cyfnod 1)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343533\tThe Phase 1 Intertidal project was completed in 2005 and maps finalised (see metadata record 24333), however since 2005 selected of areas have been re-mapped as up-to-date information was required for casework and other needs. There is a need to incorporate these newly mapped areas with the original Intertidal data layer and make it available as the most up-to-date data source.  A decision was made to only incorporate newly mapped areas or sites and not individual habitats, such as, Sabellaria reefs.   The purpose of this data capture was to update the Phase 1 Intertidal biotope and target note layers.   From 2005 onwards for updates, but the original sites were mapped from April 1996.  The methodology and the maps have undergone quality control independently, but the combined final output has not.  The data available to the public under an Open Government Licence has some information redacted. An un-redacted version can be requested and may be provided under NRW licence.\tSites or areas were mapped using standard Phase 1 methodologies.  Data from these sites is collated with existing data as follows: a) Where the layers overlapped, the original data were cut out and the new data pasted in its place.  This was done by cookie cutting using the newer data.  Nodes were overlaid to ensure that all intersects had the necessary nodes. b) Where there were a number of slivers as a result of the cut and replace, these were removed and the adjacent polygons tidied up to abut properly.  These adjustments were well inside the errors that can be attributed to the survey or digitizing errors (less than metre scale). Data processing notes are maintained for the resulting layers.  The dataset builds on data collected for CCW Marine Intertidal Phase 1 project. metadata for the project can be viewed  through metadata URI 24333.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1 Intertidal H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media1201to1300\\Media1217 Archived under project 305, media 1217. The data is available on the CCW corporate GIS (M) drive.\tInternal Custodians: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Spatial Data and GIS Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2010-10-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:16:26.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17964\tNRW_DS114458\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.645Z\t387\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria alveolata Reefs in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2010\tGwaith Monitro Rhynglanwol - riffiau Sabellaria alveolata yn ACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343538\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary, tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats). Honeycomb worms build tubes from sand and shell fragments. They are found on the lower part of rocky seashores but they also need a sand supply for tube-building. They are found on the lower part of rocky seashores but they also need a sand supply for tube-building.  Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary ones. Of these, Estuaries and their constituent mudflats, sandflats and reefs, large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds, populations of the shingle-dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus, piddocks in clay and lower shore boulder communities and Reefs, particularly biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata are the main habitats of interest within the intertidal areas of the SAC. The purpose of this data capture was to monitoring the extent of Sabellaria alveolata reefs. Field work was carried out during five days of spring tides, 6th to 10th July 2009. Data quality and confidence considered high as all surveys were completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.\tTwo areas of Sabellaria reef were re-surveyed in 2010, having been surveyed by ASML in 2009: Llandanwg and West of Afon Dwyfor. At each quadrat station, surveyors established an origin at the centre of an imaginary 10 m x 10 m grid (i.e. at location (5 m, 5 m)) and then the surveyors proceeded to survey the initial 5 random quadrats in detail. At each transect location, surveyors walked down the transect using the GPS and made notes on habitat, community and condition / age of the Sabellaria reef. Condition of Sabellaria reef was recorded using a defined 7 point scale. The age of the Sabellaria reef was recorded using a defined 4 point scale. GPS fixes were taken along the transect to mark changes in Sabellaria reef condition and to mark the top and bottom of the transect (often the water line). Photographs were taken to illustrate habitat and condition of the Sabellaria reef. A survey by ASML was also undertaken in 2009.\t\tThe data and report are archived as Project No 199, Media No 1228. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW-Maes y Fynnon\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata contact: Master Data Reference Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:19:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17959\tNRW_DS114459\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.627Z\t276\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera marina) Beds in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2010\tGwaith Monitro Rhynglanwol - Zostera marina yn ACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343539\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Seagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas, such as lagoons and channels, which are sheltered from significant wave action. Eelgrass (Zostera marina), is considered to be scarce (present in 16-100 ten km squares) is protected through the EC Habitats Directive and is classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitat. Data is captured to support reporting on conservation status of the Pen Llyn air Sarnau SAC.\tThe Zostera marina beds were mapped using the tracking facility on the GPS receivers, although on 14th July the tide failed to expose more than the uppermost plants in the diffuse bed. Digital photographs were also taken at points of interest during all Z. marina surveys. Whilst the perimeter of the Porth Dinllaen bed was being mapped, several pairs of surveyors walked north/south transects along the bed on the beach. The surveyors assessed quadrats at regular intervals noting Zostera density, substratum type, abundance of core taxa and presence/ absence of lesser taxa. The transects surveyed at Porth Dinllaen were worked using handheld GPS receivers.  All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors. Data quality and confidence considered high. Intertidal SAC monitoring, previous work was also carried out in 2009 and 2008.\t\tThe data and report are archived as Project No 199, Media No 1228. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW, Maes y Fynnon\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:19:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17957\tNRW_DS114460\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.325Z\t838\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Heritage Evidence to Support Strategic Planning for Marine Renewable Energy\tTystiolaeth treftadaeth naturiol i gefnogi cynllunio strategol ar gyfer ynni adnewyddadwy\u0027r mor yng Nghymru.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343630\tThe Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is committed to working with regulators and developers to support the development of marine renewable energy in locations, and using technologies, that avoid significant adverse environmental impacts. The UK government is committed to producing 15 percent of all energy requirements from renewable sources by 2020. With approximately 1.5 percent of UK energy currently sourced from renewables, significant expansion will be needed to meet this target. As far as marine sources of renewable energy are concerned, UK government has to date relied on deployment of offshore wind to contribute to UK renewable energy targets and indications suggest that commercial scale (10 to 100MW) arrays of wave and tidal technologies are expected to commence largely post 2015 (DECC, 2010). CCW have undertaken to produce and share key synthesised evidence relating to Welsh natural heritage interests, in the form of GIS-based data layers, to be taken into account by strategic planning process for marine energy devices (wave and tidal stream).    The baseline layers highlight the breadth of natural heritage interests that need to be taken into account in strategic planning processes.  The interpreted layers communicate the relative levels of risks to Wales\u0027 marine natural heritage from device deployment.  Evidence layers have been produced for Welsh inshore territorial waters, from Mean High Water Spring baseline out to 12nm. The metadata describes the collation exercise, which was undertaken between Mar 2010 and Feb 2011.  Temporal coverage of each of the baseline datasets is documented elsewhere. The environmental effects of marine renewable energy devices are not well understood and information about the marine environment is often limited, so this work is based on the best available information at the time of the study.  This information is considered a snapshot in time and may be kept for archive puposes only.  Newer data products may be available though the WG Marine Planning Portal.\tInformation and data on those environmental receptors most likely to be sensitive to marine renewable energy devices have been collated manipulated (where appropriate) and mapped in a GIS-based format. Baseline and distribution data was collated and mapped for the following receptors; diving seabirds, marine mammals and protected seabed habitats.  Interpreted evidence to produce an assessment of the relative vulnerability of key receptors around Wales was produced for the following; diving seabirds, cetaceans, grey seal, seabed habitats, coastal landscapes and seascapes.  Methodologies used to collect the baseline data is described in metadata for each of the individual projects, however a description of each of these projects, limitations of the data and the methods used to asses vulnerability is included in the CCW Policy Research Report 11/3 that accompanies this data.     Each data layer produced also includes a document detailing this information and a description of the attribute data.\t\tThe data is archived as project No.309 and media No.1230\tInternal Custodian: Marine Spatial Planning, Energy and Infrastructure Team (Strategic Planning Group),\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-06-30-2011-06-30\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:20:32.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17960\tNRW_DS114466\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.664Z\t338\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTy Gwyn Marsh - National Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey Saltmarsh Survey (2000)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343636\tA standard Phase 2 NVC survey was undertaken at Ty Gywn Marsh as part of the Morfa Harlech Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) designation process.\tStandard  National Vegetation Classification (NVC)  survey techniques. The survey was carried out in-house by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff, now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\t\tAssociated data is held at; M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Saltmarsh\\Ty_Gwyn_marsh_2000\tInternal Custodian: NR Conservation Officer-Sites Team, NRW. Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:05:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17968\tNRW_DS114499\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.599Z\t745\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcross Wales Drop-Down Video Monitoring: North Llyn Ground Truthing (2011)\tGwaith monitro cwymp-gamerau fideo ar draws Cymru 2011: Gwirio Gogledd Llyn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134343939\tA drop-down video survey carried out in North Wales. This work was carried out by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and focused on the North Llyn area.  Subsequent analysis of the video taken was undertaken by Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd on behalf of CCW. This work was conducted as part of a ground truthing exercise through which the extent and quality of Modiolus mussel beds could be assessed.\tDrop-down video is useful for gathering large volumes of data across wide areas of seabed and often to depths beyond which diver observations can be safely made.  Amendments to the previous methods include the addition of a HD video camera system manufactured by C-Technics in place of the SD camera previously used. A Cannon EOS 400D digital SLR was also used in a housing manufactured by Rovtech systems.  Photographic stills were taken every 11 seconds along the tows to assist with species and habitat validation.  Following methodological and equipment trial (Sanderson et al, 2001; Southeran et al, 2004) drop-down video survey methods were employed extensively in Welsh Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in 2004 and 2005 (Axelsson et al, 2006). The 2004 survey work focused on Cardigan Bay SAC, while the 2005 survey work focused on Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau, Menai Strait and Conwy Bay, Cardigan Bay and Pembrokeshire Marine.  Between 2007 and 2010 work focused on the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Modiolus reef.  Additional work was carried out in Milford Haven (Crepidula and Seagrass beds) and St Bride\u0027s Bay to look at possible anchor damage.  In 2011 DDV was also carried out around Puffin Island in Anglesey.\tDetailed methods and site locations are described in CCW Marine Monitoring report 24a.\tMaintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW Maes y Fynnon as project no. 313 media no. 1236. Videos are maintained in the archive but can be accessed through GIS layers on the M drive.\tInternal custodian: Marine Monitoring Officer, MFSG, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-06-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:39:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17975\tNRW_DS114500\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.639Z\t282\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcross Wales Drop-Down Video Monitoring: Puffin Island Survey (2011)\tGwaith monitro cwymp-gamerau fideo ar draws Cymru 2011: Arolwg Ynys Seiriol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353030\tThis dataset consists of drop-down video surveys of sites around Puffin Island, Anglesey. Work was carried out by former Countryside Counicl for Wales (CCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) within the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC).  Subsequent analysis of the video taken was undertaken by Marine Ecological Soultions Ltd on behalf of CCW. The project was designed to validate existing HABMAP data of the area surrounding Puffin Island.   Methods of data capture: Drop-down video is useful for gathering large volumes of data across wide areas of seabed and often to depths beyond which divers\u0027 observations can be safely made.  Detailed methods and site locations are described in CCW Marine Monitoring Report 24a.  Amendments to these methods include the addition of a HD video camera system manufactured by C-Tecnics in place of the SD camera previously used. A Canon EOS 400D digital SLR was also used in a housing manufactured by Rovtech Systems.  Photographic stills were taken every 11 seconds along the tows to assist with species and habitat validation.\tFollowing methodological and equipment trial drop-down video survey methods were employed extensively in Welsh Special Area of Conservation (SACs) in 2004 and 2005.  The 2004 survey work focused on Cardigan Bay SAC, while the 2005 survey work focused on Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau, Menai Strait and Conwy Bay, Cardigan Bay and Pembrokeshire Marine.\t\tMaintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW Maes y Fynnon as project no. 313 media no. 1237. Videos are maintained in the archive but can be accessed through GIS layers on the M drive.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, MFSG, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:43:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17963\tNRW_DS114501\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.610Z\t1125\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Marine Benthic Species and Habitat Collation Held in Marine Recorder\tCasgliad CNC o gynefinoedd a rhywogaethau benthig morol ( yn y Marine Recorder )\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353031\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) currently holds and collates current and former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) marine benthic species, biotope, habitat, and particle size analyses data in Marine Recorder.  A snapshot of the data may be made available through the use of the Marine Wizard and is also available on the NBN Gateway. NRW data are merged with snapshots of datasets from across Wales supplied from other data providers (e.g. JNCC, NE, DASSH and Seasearch) using the suite of tools provided with the program and made available for internal use. The purpose of marine data capture through the use of Marine Recorder is to provide access to all marine benthic species and habitats data at NRW through one program.  From 2024, the data will be migrated to the new Marine Recorder Online tool.  A subset of data that is suitable for public use is published through the NBN Atlas.\tAll data held in marine recorder forms part of other surveys and work commissioned at NRW. Data are provided through a variety of in-house and contracted surveys run by CCW. Methodologies for each survey are held within the description of the survey in Marine Recorder and each has its own metadata entry. Some additional data has been entered through data collation exercises and consists of mainly historical data and some third party data. All data is entered by either NRW staff members or NRW contractors. An additional tool, Marine Recorder Wizard, allows staff to interrogate datasets held within Marine Recorder. There is high confidence in these datasets. All NRW data is validated by NRW staff members and an indication of the quality of the survey is recorded within the dataset. The majority of surveys are carried out by experienced marine biologists. Volunteer surveys are checked by NRW staff. Historical data may have lower precision and accuracy and may not be verified.\t\tMarine Recorder is currently available through the legacy CCW VMWare.  A back up is held on the legacy CCW MFSG drive along with copies of the most recent snapshot (H:\\Science\\MFSG\\MarineRecorder)  Copies of the NRW snapshot is also held at JNCC and the NBNDate file is archived at DASSH.  Some paper MNCR forms are held in off site storage  (Box CCW/MNCR/1, Box CCW/MNCR/2. Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Contact / Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW  Internal Data Contact: Marine and Freshwater Evidence Manager, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2017-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:23:47.092Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17974\tNRW_DS114540\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.679Z\t630\tValid\tEditable\tDrop-Down Video of Historical Native Oyster (Ostrea edulis) Habitat in Swansea Bay, South Wales (2010)\tFideo tanddwr o gynefin wystrys (Ostrea edulis) ym Mae Abertawe, De Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353430\tThe native or flat oyster (Ostrea edulis) is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority species, and it is also listed for Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006 as being \u0027of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity in Wales.\u0027   The native oyster has a long history as an important commercial species in Wales and elsewhere in the UK. Significant oyster beds are now restricted to Milford Haven, Swansea Bay/Mumbles and Porthcawl, although at a much reduced scale. The remaining oyster population in Wales is under threat through the spread of the protozoan parasite Bonamia ostreae, continued poor recruitment, and competition from the alien invasive slipper limpet Crepidula fornicata. The situation is exacerbated by the continued operation of a small oyster fishery in Milford Haven and Swansea Bay.   The aim of this project was to gather information on the status of historical native oyster beds off the south Pembrokeshire coast. This information will contribute to our understanding of the status of historical native oyster beds off the south Wales coast and will inform projects to reinstate oyster habitat and stock enhancement. This dataset collates the examination of drop down video of oyster beds. This project used video data was examined to identify broad seabed type and evidence of oysters.\tDrop down video footage recorded from a survey vessel. Survey work was carried out in July 2010, resulting in video footage from 92 survey stations and sonar classification of rocky ground of a further 17 stations where deployment of video equipment was not possible. Subsequent video analysis provided basic seabed classification and recorded other factors relevant to oyster ecology including any presence of slipper limpets (Crepidula fornicata) and silt deposition. Where oyster shells were recorded, these are not confirmed as to whether specimens are live or dead. Baseline survey of seabed. In 2012 CCW had the video footage reanalysed to identify additional species and habitat records. These were entered into the existing Marine Recorder Survey.\tMarine Biodiversity Liaison Officer, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW The following reports are associated with this dataset: - Woolmer, AP. 2011. Drop down video of historical native oyster ostrea edulis habitat in Swansea, south wales CONFIDENTIAL. Countryside Council for Wales Science Report No. 985. Bangor.  - Woolmer, AP. 2011. Drop down video of historical native oyster ostrea edulis habitat in Swansea, south wales REDACTED. Countryside Council for Wales Science Report No. 985. Bangor.\tData is archived at project no. 317, media nos. 1252 and 1397 (File paths (K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1252 and K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1397) Species data in NRW copy of Marine Recorder (Survey Number: MRCCW31000000003)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team (used to be Aethne Cooke)   Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:36:11.418Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17972\tNRW_DS114561\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.683Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecies Names Translation Lists (Vertebrates)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353631\tThis is a collated list of terrestrial vertebrate species names in English, Welsh and scientific Latin.  Purpose of data capture: To provide terminology lists for the Species Dictionary hosted by the Natural History Museum which feeds into the National Biodiversity Network Gateway and for the use of CCW staff.  Methods of data capture: Most of the translations have been created and/or verified by Cymdeithas Edward Llwyd for CCW the records have been extensively cleaned by the Natural History Museum  Confidence in data: All translations have been carefully checked and approved however with the English and Welsh names, as in any language, there will be multiple derivations and regional variations. Also the scientific terminology changes periodically as work on taxonomy progresses.\t\tData are managed by the I-MAT team in PKCG\tCCW intranet (Ffenest), NBN Gateway and Species Dictionary (Natural History Museum)\t\tCymdeithas Edward Llwyd\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:12.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17965\tNRW_DS114562\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.562Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecies Names Translation Lists (Moths and Butterflies)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353632\tThis is a collated list of moth and butterfly species names in English, Welsh and scientific Latin. Purpose of data capture: To provide terminology lists for the Species Dictionary hosted by the Natural History Museum which feeds into the National Biodiversity Network Gateway and for the use of CCW staff. Methods of data capture: Most of the translations have been created and/or verified by Cymdeithas Edward Llwyd for CCW the records have been extensively cleaned by the Natural History Museum Confidence in data: All translations have been carefully checked and approved however with the English and Welsh names, as in any language, there will be multiple derivations and regional variations. Also the scientific terminology changes periodically as work on taxonomy progresses.\t\tData are managed by the I-MAT team in PKCG\tCCW intranet (Ffenest), NBN Gateway and Species Dictionary (Natural History Museum)\t\tCymdeithas Edward Llwyd\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:13:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17970\tNRW_DS114563\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.671Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecies Names Translation Lists (Plants)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353633\tThis is a collated list of terrestrial plant species names in English, Welsh and scientific Latin.  Purpose of data capture: To provide terminology lists for the Species Dictionary hosted by the Natural History Museum which feeds into the National Biodiversity Network Gateway and for the use of CCW staff.  Methods of data capture: Most of the translations have been created and/or verified by Cymdeithas Edward Llwyd for CCW the records have been extensively cleaned by the Natural History Museum  Confidence in data: All translations have been carefully checked and approved however with the English and Welsh names, as in any language, there will be multiple derivations and regional variations. Also the scientific terminology changes periodically as work on taxonomy progresses.\t\tData are managed by the I-MAT team in PKCG\tCCW intranet (Ffenest), NBN Gateway and Species Dictionary (Natural History Museum)\t\tCymdeithas Edward Llwyd\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17967\tNRW_DS114564\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.585Z\t716\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMorphodynamic Modelled Outputs for the Mawddach Estuary\tCanlyniadau ar sail model morffodynamig ar gyfer Aber Mawddach\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353634\tThis report summarises the third stage of a modelling research study undertaken by Bangor University, investigating the hydrodynamic and morphodynamic response of representative Welsh estuaries (which include both past and potential future changes to the estuaries, as well as their present form) to a range of mean and extreme climatic conditions. In particular, coastal flood risk and potential for erosion and accretion has been assessed through the medium of high resolution computational models. The Mawddach estuary lies within the Snowdonia National Park, north Wales, and its landscape and environment represents one of the key natural assets which Wales possesses. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the response of the estuary to future tidal and atmospheric changes. The model has also been used to investigate the response of the estuarine dynamics to potential management strategies such as embankment re-alignment and re-location.\tA mathematical model of the Mawddach Estuary has been developed to reproduce the interactions of tidal and fresh water hydrodynamics within the estuary and surrounding low lying areas. The model is based on a TELEMAC Modelling System which calculates the temporal and spatial variations in hydrodynamics (e.g. elevations, velocities, pressure, density) and sediment transport through a finite-element model grid (an unstructured mesh of nodes connected in a triangular framework) which represents the bathymetry of the area.  Input data to the model includes bathymetric data from Admiralty charts and LIDAR data. Three grids have been produced against which different scenarios can be modelled. 1) 2-D model of the present day Mawddach Estuary 2) Alterations to represent the past and future 3) Alterations to remove the embankments upstream of Penmaenpool The project extends two previous modelling studies in other representative Welsh estuaries, namely the Dyfi Estuary in mid Wales (Robins, 2009a) and the Burry Inlet in south Wales (Robins, 2009b).\t\tData outputs associated with this project are archived as Project No. 318, Media No\u0027s 1253 and 1262 to 1266  (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1253 etc.)\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tCentre for Applied Marine Sciences (CAMS)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:20:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17956\tNRW_DS114565\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.256Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecies Names Translation Lists (Dragonflies)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353635\tThis is a collated list of dragonfly species names in English, Welsh and scientific Latin. Purpose of data capture: To provide terminology lists for the Species Dictionary hosted by the Natural History Museum which feeds into the National Biodiversity Network Gateway and for the use of CCW staff.\tMethods of data capture: Most of the translations have been created and/or verified by Cymdeithas Edward Llwyd for CCW the records have been extensively cleaned by the Natural History Museum. Confidence in data: All translations have been carefully checked and approved however with the English and Welsh names, as in any language, there will be multiple derivations and regional variations. Also the scientific terminology changes periodically as work on taxonomy progresses.\tData are managed by the I-MAT team in PKCG\tCCW intranet (Ffenest), NBN Gateway and Species Dictionary (Natural History Museum)\t\tCymdeithas Edward Llwyd\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:13:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17958\tNRW_DS114567\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.553Z\t284\tValid\tEditable\tDrop-Down Video of Historical Native Oyster (Ostrea edulis) Habitat in Pembrokeshire, South Wales (2010)\tFideo cwymp-gamera wystrysen frodorol hanesyddol (ostrea edulis) yn Sir Benfro, De Cymru -2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353637\tThe native or flat oyster (Ostrea edulis) is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority species, and it is also listed for Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006 as being \u0027of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity in Wales.\u0027   The native oyster has a long history as an important commercial species in Wales and elsewhere in the UK. Significant oyster beds are now restricted to Milford Haven, Swansea Bay/Mumbles and Porthcawl, although at a much reduced scale. The remaining oyster population in Wales is under threat through the spread of the protozoan parasite Bonamia ostreae, continued poor recruitment, and competition from the alien invasive slipper limpet Crepidula fornicata. The situation is exacerbated by the continued operation of a small oyster fishery in Milford Haven and Swansea Bay.   The aim of this project was to gather information on the status of historical native oyster beds off the south Pembrokeshire coast. This information will contribute to our understanding of the status of historical native oyster beds off the south Wales coast and will inform projects to reinstate oyster habitat and stock enhancement.  This dataset collates the examination of drop down video of oyster beds.\tDrop down video footage recorded from a survey vessel. Survey work was carried out in July 2010, resulting in video footage from 92 survey stations and sonar classification of rocky ground of a further 17 stations where deployment of video equipment was not possible. Subsequent video analysis provided basic seabed classification and recorded other factors relevant to oyster ecology including any presence of slipper limpets (Crepidula fornicata) and silt deposition.   Where oyster shells were recorded, these are not confirmed as to whether specimens are live or dead. Baseline survey of seabed. In 2012 CCW had the video footage reanalysed to identify additional species and habitat records. These were entered into the existing Marine Recorder Survey.\tThe following reports are associated with this data: - Woolmer, AP. 2011. Drop down video of historical native oyster ostrea edulis habitat in Pembrokeshire, south Wales REDACTED. Countryside Council for Wales Science Report No. 957. Bangor. - Woolmer, AP. 2011. Drop down video of historical native oyster ostrea edulis habitat in Pembrokeshire, south Wales CONFIDENTIAL. Countryside Council for Wales Science Report No. 957. Bangor.\t[A] MFSG Archive Project no. 320 and Media No. 1268 and 1397 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1268 and K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400) [B] Species data held in Marine Recorder survey number: MRCCW31000000002\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:29:48.971Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17971\tNRW_DS114573\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.650Z\t405\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Algae Sites in Wales: Survey and Inventory of Important and Rare Algae\tSafleoedd algau mor yng Nghymru: Arolwg a Rhestr (algau prin)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353733\tSeveral sites around Wales have been highlighted as being of interest for their marine seaweeds, either as species rich sites or for supporting rare, scarce or Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species. Many of these sites were designated in 2007 as provisional Important Plant Areas for Algae and all the sites are situated within marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) or Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSIs).  This study was designed to provide an inventory of species at selected sites, which were highlighted as the result of previous surveys, and also to recommend methodologies for future monitoring.\tThe sites selected included Rhosneiger, Llanbedrog Low water lagoon, Oyster Bank (Pwllheli), Glanllynnau, Sarn Badrig and Tremadog Bay, and West Angle Bay. - Rhosneigr:  An algae specific species inventory was undertaken at rock pools at different levels across the shore. Viewpoint photography was used to illustrate the overall distribution of Ascophyllum nodosum and other fucoid seaweeds on the rocky shore area. A series of monitoring was also conducted on a population of Cystoseira tamariscifolia and other conspicuous species was undertaken, in an area colonised by Sargassum muticum by recording overall percentage cover.  - Llanbedrog Low water lagoon: An algae specific species inventory was undertaken during a low spring tide. - The Oyster Bank (Pwllheli): A SCUBA diving survey was conducted for the Barbed Twig Weed (Anotrichium barbatum).  An inventory of other species found at the site was made in situ and samples taken for laboratory examination.  This survey was also used for a Phase 2 descriptive of the site. - Glanllynnau: SCUBA diving surveys were conducted in both sites to collect a species inventory of the sublittoral fringe and shallow subtidal area.  - Sarn Badrig and Tremadog Bay: the work carried out related to quality control of ongoing long term monitoring of the subtidal algal communities. A total of 4 dives recording dives were undertaken and the cumulative number of species were recorded over a period of 1 hour for each dive.   - West Angle Bay: An inventory of seaweeds was obtained from 7 habitats selected shore habitats.\tThe project references work carried out by Brodie et al (2007) \" Important Plant Areas for algae: a provisional review of sites and areas of importance for algae in the UK. Plantlife International\" and CCW\u0027s Across Wales Reef Monitoring: Sarn  Badrig and Tremadog Bay).  It also contributes to CCW\u0027s marine intertidal and subtidal survey and monitoring program. The results of the Sarn Badrig and Tremadog Bay are reported in Holt R. Irving R. Stanwell-Smith D. CCW Cross Wales Reef Monitoring. Sarn Badrig and Tremadog Bay 2007 - 2011 (in draft).\tData is archived as project no. 325, media 1282 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1200to1300\\Media 1282)  Species data is also available through Marine Recorder (survey no. MRCCW31200000002)   The herbarium is stored at the National Museum of Wales, Cardiff.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:37:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17961\tNRW_DS114587\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.563Z\t666\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBlack grouse (Tetrao tetrix) National Surveys (Welsh data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353837\tThe male black grouse (Tetrao tetrix) population in Britain was surveyed in 1995/96 and 2005, in order to estimate population size.   The surveys were based on counts of males displaying in the spring. In Wales and Staffordshire, the entire known population was surveyed in 1995. In northern England and Scotland in 1995/96 and 2005, and in Wales in 2005, a stratified random sample of 5 km squares from the breeding range was surveyed. As such the dataset covers around 10% of the UK population overall and does NOT contain all black grouse leks in Britain in the survey years; its aim was to produce a reliable population estimate. Please note that, apart from the 1995 Welsh data, the dataset does NOT contain all leks of black grouse, because only a random subsample of the breeding range was surveyed.  Data have been thoroughly checked for geographical and other errors.\tThe survey covered the complete breeding range in Wales and Staffordshire in 1995, and random stratified 5 km squares selected from the breeding range in northern England and Scotland in both 1995/96 and 2005, and in Wales in 2005. In survey squares, surveyors walked within 500 m of all suitable habitat and counted any displaying males at dawn. Often local knowledge from landowners was used to locate leks. At least one count was made of each lek, within one hour either side of dawn, and if possible in clear, still weather. Display sites were marked on a 1:25 000 map. Single displaying males, and groups of displaying males (leks), recorded more than 500 m, and 200 m apart, respectively, on the same or different visits, were considered as separate display sites. When leks were counted more than once, the peak count was used. Numbers of females observed have been included in the comments field, but these do not represent a population figure due to the usually fleeting and irregular nature of female visits to leks.\tPart of this dataset is sensitive and therefore restricted. Currently records available at 10km at a public resolution, however if access is required at a finer resolution this will have to be issued under licence. See Resource Locator for further information.\tHeld by RSPB Cymru and available by the NBN Gateway\tInternal Custodian: Sian Whitehead, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ornithologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team NRW\tRoyal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17969\tNRW_DS114591\t2024-05-28T12:36:10.634Z\t465\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFarmland Bird Survey North East Wales (2008)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353931\tA systematic regional survey of farmland birds within and around the Bettisfield and Dee Estuary (Welsh side) areas undertaken in 2008. The survey aimed to establish whether corn bunting were still breeding in Wales and to gather information on farmland bird populations in this area.\tFull survey took place in 1km squares with corn bunting records in Wales since 2000 and the area within a 2km buffer of these. A 2km buffer was used to achieve full coverage up to this distance given available resources. The full survey was based on three morning visits: one in each of in each of May, June and July at least two weeks apart following Gilbert et al (1998). The population size was the maximum number of singing (\u003dterritorial) males on any one visit. These data have been gathered by trained field-workers and the data are of a high quality. These data have been mapped and checked for sensitivities and typographical/geographical errors.\t\tHeld by RSPB Cymru and available throught the NBN Gateway\tInternal Custodian: Sian Whitehead, Terrestrial and Freshwater Ornithologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)\t\t2011-06-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:10.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17954\tNRW_DS114594\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.709Z\t554\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRing ouzel (Turdus torquata) national sample survey in the UK in 1999\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353934\tThis is the first co-ordinated census of breeding ring ouzel Turdus torquata across the UK. A random subsample of the UK breeding range was surveyed to arrive at a population estimate, and as such the dataset does NOT contain all breeding locations of ring ouzel in the UK in 1999. Of the tetrads surveyed that were known to be occupied during the 1988-91 breeding bird atlas, 39-43% were unoccupied in 1999. The survey highlights a continuing contraction in range. The survey was run as part of the Statutory Conservation Agencies and RSPB Annual Breeding Birds Scheme (SCARABBS) and was funded jointly by the Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB), the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), English Nature (now Natural England) and Scottish Natural Heritage (SNH). The Ring Ouzel Study Group took part in the survey, and many landowners and site managers allowed access to their land.  The purpose of this data capture was to set a baseline for the future monitoring of breeding ring ouzels and to provide a systematically based estimate of the population size.\tA random sample of tetrads (2 x 2 km squares) was surveyed from the known breeding range of the ring ouzel as defined from the two national breeding bird atlases of 1968-72 and 1988-91. The survey method used tape playback and observation and involved walking four parallel transects in each tetrad.\t\tHeld by RSPB Cymru and available on the NBN Gateway\tInternal Contact: Sian Whitehead, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ornithologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tRoyal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-08-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17955\tNRW_DS114595\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.809Z\t368\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTwite (Carduelis flavirostris) Breeding Season Survey, Wales 2008\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353935\tThis survey updated a survey undertaken in 2002 by Snowdonia National Park and the National Trust. In Wales, twite are restricted primarily to northern Snowdonia around the valley of Nant Ffrancon and Llyn Ogwen, bounded by the Carneddau to the north and east, by Elidir Fawr and Y Garn to the west and by the Glyder range to the south, where breeding has been recorded since 1944, with post breeding flocks of over 150 birds in the recent past (1999 - 2000). This dataset also contains records other species in addition to twite which were recorded during the survey. This survey updated a survey undertaken in 2002 by Snowdonia National Park and the National Trust to assess current status and provide reliable indication of trend in range and abundance between the reference years (2002 - 2008).\tThis survey follows the same method used for the 1999 Statutory Conservation Agencies/RSPB Annual Breeding Bird Scheme (SCARABBS) and is based on Gilbert et al (1998). Three visits were made each to selected 1km squares in two strata 1) stratified random sample of 1km squares; 2) squares identified with historic records. Survey staff walked modified point transects at 200m intervals. Whilst recording was continuous at 100m intervals within suitable breeding habitat, observers made five-minute stops to scan and listen for twite (observers were advised to avoid steeply sloping land). Habitat cover was recorded to the nearest 10%. Supplementary records were also collated. This survey updated a survey undertaken in 2002 by Snowdonia National Park and the National Trust.\t\tHeld by RSPB Cymru and the NBN Gateway\tInternal Contacts: Data Services Team, NRW  Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ornithologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tRoyal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-06-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17952\tNRW_DS114596\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.576Z\t297\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTwite (Carduelis flavirostris) national sample survey in the UK in 1999 (Welsh data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134353936\tThis dataset contains results from the first comprehensive survey of breeding twite in the UK. The aim of this survey was to provide a baseline population estimate of breeding twite in the UK. A random subsample of the UK breeding range was surveyed to arrive at a population estimate, and as such the dataset does NOT contain all breeding locations of twite in the UK in 1999. There were an estimated 10 000 pairs of breeding twite in the UK in 1999, with the range lying between 6000 and 15 000 pairs. The majority, 9948 (95 percent confidence intervals 6293-14 586) pairs, was recorded in Scotland and England. Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) and English Nature (now Natural England) helped fund this survey as part of the Statutory Conservation Agencies/RSPB Annual Breeding Birds Scheme (SCARABBS).\tSupplementary records were collated from Wales and Northern Ireland. Several methods were used to produce population estimates, with bootstrapped confidence intervals.\t\tHeld by RSPB Cymru and the NBN Gateway\tInternal Contacts:  Data Services Team, NRW Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ornithologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)\t\t2008-10-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17953\tNRW_DS114631\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.632Z\t273\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommon Standards Macrophyte Surveys: Welsh Standing Waters 2007 - 2009\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363331\tData collected during the survey of 41 standing water sites in Wales. With the exception of Llyn Bodlyn, all the sites are designated Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), with the majority also being within Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).  These surveys were undertaken as part of a wider project commissioned by the Environment Agency to meet obligations under the Water Framework Directive and provide a measure of site condition assessment for SSSI\u0027s.  The work was commissioned by the Environment Agency and funded in part by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW).  Work was conducted by ENSIS- ECRC. For further information please see \u0027Common Standards Macrophyte Surveys: Welsh Standing Waters 2007 - 2009. Data Summary Report to CCW as part of: Lake Macrophyte \u0026amp; Habitat Surveys for the Water Framework Directive, 2007-10. Contract No: 20457 The Environment Agency\u0027  Work carried out over the course of 3 summers, during 2006 - 2009. Palaeoecological and surface sediment analysis of  these sites incorporating chemical and bathymetric surveys was also conducted and this information is available in an associated report.  Please see \u0027Goldsmith, B., Burgess, A., Bennion, H., Turner, S.D., Appleby, P.G. \u0026amp; Piliposian, G.T. (2010). Palaeoecological and surface.\tSurveys were undertaken using Common Standards Monitoring Guidance (CSM) methods as described in JNCC (2005). Where possible, submerged plants were surveyed using a bathyscope, but a double-headed rake was used in deeper water or where poor water clarity restricted visibility. All survey sections and boat transects were recorded using Global Positioning System (GPS) and backed up with digital photographs. For unknown or taxonomically ambiguous species, vouchers were collected, appropriately preserved and identified in the laboratory. Stoneworts and bladderwort species were sent for identification by Nick Stewart all other voucher determinations were made by Ben Goldsmith. For sites commissioned exclusively by CCW, species were scored as presence or absent for each 10m subsection of the 100 m survey section. The other sites (EA WDF monitoring sites) used the original EA method, which scores the marginal species in a 1-3 abundance scale for the entire section. Frequency data is therefore only presented for marginal species for the CCW sites.\t\tData is archied as project no. 329, media no. 1288\tInternal custodian: Heather Garrett, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tEnvironment Agency (EA)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17951\tNRW_DS114632\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.445Z\t653\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBeach Sediment Particle Size for 10 Welsh Beaches (Collected 2010/2011)\tMaint gronynnau gwaddodion ar gyfer 10 o draethau Cymru (casglwyd 2010/2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363332\tBeaches in Wales are important for several reasons, including as a major \u0027attractor\u0027 of relevance to the tourist industry and local, regional and national economies. The Welsh Coast is vitally important to the tourism industry in Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to provide details of the types and properties of sediment on Welsh beaches to inform the Beach Nourishment in Wales Phase 2 project. 10 beaches were chosen during the Beach Nourishment in Wales Pilot Study project which included both sand and mixed sand/shingle systems located around the coasts of North, West and South Wales and which reflect varying relative importance of touristic / recreational, flood and erosion risk management and nature conservation interests.\tSamples containing little or no gravel were dried and homogenised. Particle size analysis of the gravel fraction was determined by dry sieving at \u0027half phi intervals\u0027 on 300 mm diameter mesh sieves. Particle size analysis of the sand fraction was determined by dry sieving at \u0027quarter phi intervals\u0027 on 200 mm diameter mesh sieves. Where samples contained a significant quantity of mud, samples were initially wet sieved, and particle size analysis of the mud fraction performed by laser diffraction. All data were exported to the GRADISTAT (Blott and Pye, 2001) computer program to calculate the sample statistics. Samples were collected during 2010 and 2011.  The 10 beaches selected in Wales includes Talacre, Abergele-Pensarn, Traeth Crugan, Morfa Dyffryn, Broadwater-Aberdovey, Tenby North Beach, Port Eynon Bay, NW Swansea Bay, Baglan-Aberavon, Porthcawl Data collected by professionals using standard methodologies.\t10 beaches were chosen during the Beach Nourishment in Wales Pilot Study project (contract no 211 MFG 09) (CCW Science Report no 928)   Beach sediment particle size analysis for 10 Welsh beaches for the \u0027Re-building Welsh Beaches to Deliver Multiple Benefits\u0027 (contract no 249 MFSG) (also known as - Beach Nourishment in Wales Phase 2 project).\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 319, Media No. 1267 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1267) HQ library and electronic version held in DMS.\tInternal Custodian: Emmer Litt, Specialist Advisor - Marine and Coastal Physical Processes, All Wales Advice Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:37:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17950\tNRW_DS114672\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.354Z\t729\tUnassessed\tEditable\tApplication of Phase 1 Intertidal Survey Techniques to Monitoring\tDefnyddio technegau arolygon Rhynglanwol Cam 1 wrth fonitro\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363732\tThis dataset is part of the 1999 monitoring trials that became the basis for Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) monitoring in Wales; Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW\u0027s) Marine Monitoring Report No1. \u0027The establishment of a programme of surveillance and monitoring for judging the condition of the features of Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau\". Relating to section 3.4.2  This was important for the notification of protected sites and also for planning conservation management. The purpose of this data capture was to support the designation and conservation planning of protected sites.  The purpose of condition monitoring on the site is essential to provide information which enables judgements to be made about whether the favourable condition of the features of interest for which this site was designated is is being achieved and so unable judgements to be made about the appropriateness of site management. This programme of survey has been stimulated by the EC habitats directive SAC designation requirements because the implementation of the directive requires that appropriate adjacent intertidal land must be notified as a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) before it can be included as part of an SAC. Historically intertidal marine SSSIs have been notified on an ad hoc basis in Wales. However it was recognised a large scale notification of SSSIs linked to the habitats directive would need to be part of a series of sites covered a range of intertidal communities in Wales. In addition to this it was recognised that map data for the intertidal within the SACs would be invaluable for site management including sensitivity mapping and as a basis from which to develop future monitoring.  This dataset forms part of the Intertidal SAC monitoring program.\tThe resolution of Marine Intertidal Phase 1 mapping lies between Phase 1 Terrestrial Mapping and Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) Phase 2 Marine Survey methodologies. During Marine Intertidal Phase 1 mapping, surveyors walk along the shore in order to identify and map the extent and distribution of biotopes. Surveys were undertaken by a range of surveyors using a rapid assessment methodology. This information is recorded onto a copy of an aerial photograph taken at low water. The rough maps produced are transferred onto GIS where the biotope polygons are colour-coded. Biotope identification is carried out in the field and, in addition, species are listed. The technique has been developed to be rapid and emphasis is placed on spatial information. During the present study the development of marine phase 1 survey was modified to suit the monitoring application.  In order to report on the condition of an estuary a number of attributes require monitoring. 1) The relative proportion of muddy and sandy communities. 2) The distribution of  major communities within the estuary. Phase 1 survey techniques were trialled using a 200 m grid.  Some additional sampling locations were added to the grid to take account of rare or otherwise important biotopes not covered by the grid (minor biotopes). Biotope extent and distribution were mapped to aerial photos and GIS by surveyors, sometimes using the GPS locations.\tSee Section 3.4.2 \"Application of Phase 1 intertidal survey techniques to monitoring\"  of The establishment of a programme of surveillance and monitoring for judging the condition of the features of Pen Llŷn a\u0027r Sarnau cSAC: 1. Progress to March 2001. WG Sanderson RHF Holt L Kay G Wyn and AJ McMath March 2001 Contract Science Report: 380.  (CCW Science Report No. 0380 part 2 of 4).\tArchived as project 315, media 1239. The report associated with this data is available in the Reports Archive - NRW\u0027s DMS.\tInternal Custodian : Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:23:15.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17948\tNRW_DS114673\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.346Z\t236\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Shad Egg Survey on the River Wye (2011)\tArolwg o Wyau Herlynod a Gwangod Afon Gwy\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363733\tThe River Wye rises on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains and flows in a generally south-easterly direction to enter the Severn Estuary at Chepstow.  The river is designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the Habitats Directive for several features, including salmon, lampreys and shads.  Twaite (Alosa fallax) and allis (Alosa alosa) shad. In Britain, significant populations of twaite shad are known to spawn in the Rivers Severn, Wye, Usk and Tywi.  However, it is not certain whether a breeding population of allis shad still exists. The population of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and water quality issues. The purpose of this data capture was to make an assessment of Conservation Status by summarising previous data held at the site, conducting a baseline survey and compare these with results from previous datasets.  Along the length of the welsh portion of the River Wye from Builth Wells to Monmouth. The survey was conducted in May, June and July of 2011.\tEnvironment Agency Wales (EAW) and Countryside Council for Wales, recorded egg survey data are available for six years on the River Wye, although widespread sampling data are only available for 1999, 2003 and 2004.  The standard Hillman et al. (2003) methodology was amended to reduce the sampling required at sites where shad eggs were present.  This enabled the JNCC (2005) target to be assessed and many more sites to be surveyed within the time available. Confidence in this dataset is high.\t\t[A]  Data is archived on NRW Marine Archive server as project 330, media 1289. [B]  Species data is available in Recorder 6: Shad Egg Survey River Wye 2011 - CCW - Report no. 11/8/4  CCW0008000000008\tInternal Custodian: Rivers Ecologist, MFEG, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:38:06.806Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17949\tNRW_DS114674\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.368Z\t20\tValid\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales (Archived 2012)\tCynefinoedd Morol a blaenoriaeth yng Nghymru (wedi\u0027i archifo 2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363734\tThe UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) was published back in 1994, and was the UK Government’s response to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD), which the UK signed up to in 1992 in Rio de Janeiro.  Similarly under Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act, 2006 it is required that the a \u0027list of the living organisms and types of habitat which in the Assembly\u0027s opinion are of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\u0027.   Priority marine habitats in Wales are those identified as BAP habitats, Section 42 habitats or OSPAR habitats.  These habitats include: blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.  These datasets have been superseded.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap and Marine Monitoring Data.  Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats.  Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decision made in creating the layers.  A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.   This dataset is kept as an archive only.  Users are advised to refer to more recent data NRW_DS111154 (which is now superseded by NRW_DS122453).\tThis dataset has now been updated and superseded by a more up-to-date version of this dataset known as Marine Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) species in Wales - GIS Dataset (see related titles).\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived.   Data is archived as project 282, media 1098 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1001to1100)\tInternal Custodian: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-20T13:48:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17947\tNRW_DS114688\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.215Z\t608\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBarbastelle (Barbastella barbastellus) Bat Distribution Model (2011)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363838\tThe purpose of this data capture was to use a multidisciplinary approach to fill current knowledge gaps in the ecology and conservation requirements of a rare bat, the barbastelle (Barbastella barbastellus).  Spatial modelling was used to evaluate the environmental parameters that dictate where the species is able to persist in the UK and to identify areas of high conservation value. The purpose of this data capture for Countryside Council for Wales was to assess and predict the distribution of the species around Wales. The models were created and used my Matthew Zeale, a PhD student at the time. The PhD findings from the models indicated that B. barbastellus is highly dependent on large areas of native mature woodland and is limited in its distribution predominantly by summer climate, although a number of other environmental factors also appear to be important. The data collation and survey undertaken by experienced bat biologist. The results were later submitted as part of a PhD Thesis.\tMatthew Zeale (PhD student from Bristol University) investigated the value of using species distribution modelling (Maxent) for facilitating the identification of Barbastella barbastellus maternity colonies within woodland areas. Two models were developed. Firstly B. barbastellus species distribution was modelled at the UK-wide scale to identify suitable regions in which to conduct searches for maternity colonies. A second model was developed to predict the species\u0027 presence or absence within woodlands in a study area in south-west Wales. The predictions made by the latter model were ground-validated by surveying 30 woodlands for B. barbastellus presence using acoustic monitoring and trapping methods. Analysis of the model\u0027s performance showed that it predicted B. barbastellus presence/absence to a high degree of accuracy.\t\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Species\\all_wales\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Bats\\Matt_Zeale_Barbastelle_Model\tInternal Custodian: Jean Matthews or Liz Halliwell - Mammal Ecologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group External Custodian: Bristol University / Matt Zeale  Ownership could be split between Bristol Uni, Matt Zeale and NRW at the moment it says various  Metadata point of Contact: GIS and Spatial Team.\tVarious-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T15:36:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17946\tNRW_DS114691\t2024-05-28T12:36:09.170Z\t513\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMerthyr Mawr Warren: Potential for Dune Reactivation\tTwyni Merthyr Mawr: Y Potensial i Ailfywiogi Twyni\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134363931\tThis dataset is a compilation of work carried out to assess the geomorphological feasibility of sand dune reactivation at Merthyr Mawr Warren, based on a review of background environmental data, air photographs LiDAR data, a site visit and laboratory analysis of a limited number of sediment samples.   The Merthyr Mawr dunefield is a sediment-starved system which is undergoing re-distribution of sandy sediment from the north-western part to the south-eastern part of the system. Photographic evidence indicates that bare sand and active dunes covered a large part of the area between the late 19th century and the late 1960’s, after which time the dunes became progressively stabilized by vegetation. The period of high dune mobility corresponded with a period of coastal erosion. No long-term wind or wave data are available for the area, but it is hypothesized that coastal erosion, frontal dune blowout development and inland dune mobility were encouraged by high average wind and wave energy conditions, and perhaps a higher frequency of severe storms. Conversely, the period of inland dune stability between the 1970’s and the present day was associated with beach accretion and foredune progradation. It is hypothesized that these changes were driven by lower average wind / wave energy conditions and lower frequency of severe storms.   Bare sand and active parabolic dunes have only been maintained in areas where there is high visitor pressure or other anthropogenic disturbance. The purpose of this data capture was to help inform decisions that may result in potential re-mobilisation work, including impact on existing habitats and stakeholder interests.\tThe methodology used included a processing numerous datasets such as  LiDAR (Light Detection And Ranging) and Digital Elevation Model (DEM) background layers date from 2006-2008, base Ordnance Survey (OS) maps date from 1877, 1897, 1934 and 1966, Aerial photography dating from 2009,  Admiralty charts dating from 2009 and Particle Size Analyses (PSA) data collected 7 September 2011. In addition 8 sediment samples were collected in the field from surf ace locations (0 to 10 cm depth). The samples were dry-sieved at \u0027quarter phi\u0027 intervals using a mechanical shaker and the raw data processed using the GRADISTAT software package.\tThe report and data collection/assessment builds on previous feasibility study carried out at Kenfig Burrows in 2001 (CCW Contract Science Report No. 971).\tData is archived as project no. 331, media 1290\tMarine and Coastal Physical Scientist, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:38:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17996\tNRW_DS114713\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.021Z\t352\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessing the Vulnerability of Marine Habitats in Wales to the Impacts of Climate Changes (2011)\tAsesu pa mor fregus yw cynefinoedd y mor yng Nghymru i effeithiau newid hinsawdd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373133\tClimate change is recognised as a major environmental issue and the process of global change and climate will impact upon many aspects of everyday life, presenting a serious threat to the natural environment. In response to the scientific and political consensus of a need to tackle climate change the UK government bought into law the climate change act 2008. This requires a Strategic Climate Change Risk Assessment (SCCRA) to be carried out every five years, the first of which will need to be completed by November 2011.   This project was carried out to develop a methodology to assess the vulnerability of Welsh marine habitats to the impact of climate change and to use this method to undertake an assessment of welsh marine habitats. This assessment helped enable the identification of those habitats most at risk from climate change and it is envisaged that this information will be used to inform management plans and government policy and decision making.\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW) ran a workshop to scope how to assess the vulnerability of marine habitats to climate change in March 2010.  Three main issues were discussed: 1) The concept and methodologies for vulnerability assessment 2) Potential parameters that might  be used in any vulnerability assessment of marine biodiversity around Wales 3) Potential receptors that might be used in any vulnerability assessment of marine biodiversity around Wales  The final session focused in next steps for CCW in taking forward its vulnerability assessment work. 84 habitats were screened at a broad level to identify 15 that would be assessed further.    The 15 habitats were as follows; Horse mussel beds, Littoral mussel beds, Sabellaria Reef, Peat and clay exposures, Kelp with cushion fauna, Fucoids on sheltered marine shores, Kelp and red seaweeds, Echinoderms and crustose communities, Circalittoral mixed sediments, Coastal Saltmarsh, Intertidal mudflats, Littoral seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Sublittoral seagrass beds and Saline lagoons.    The distribution and extent of these habitats were collated and an evaluation was then undertaken to assess vulnerability of two criteria (habitat distribution/extent and habitat quality and/or ecosystem function) to climate change  Details of the methodology are describe in CCW Contract Science Report 969.  Confidence in this dataset is high as surveys were undertaken by experienced staff members. Vulnerability assessment was carried out 2010 / 2011 and vulnerability data; layers finalised in February 2011.\tInformation about the workshop and habitat work are available in CCW Science Report 934 and 969.\tData is archived as project 328, media 1287.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Ecologist, MFEG, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2011-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:39:32.573Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18002\tNRW_DS114722\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.052Z\t825\tValid\tEditable\tSeabirds at Sea Evidence Base\tAdar mor sylfaen tystiolaeth\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373232\tGIS layers showing the abundance and distribution of seabirds in welsh waters.  The datasets consists of the raw data showing observations of all seabirds and derived grids showing the density of flying and sitting species on a 3km grid scale and the survey coverage. The purpose of this data capture was to collate data from ESAS and WWT Consulting databases to produce combined database of birds recorded from surveys of Welsh waters.  Other sources identified were also consulted to produce the most complete representation of  the observed distribution of seabirds.-Haenau SGDd (GIS) yn dangos helaethrwydd a dosbarthiad adar môr yn nyfroedd Cymru. Mae’r set ddata’n cynnwys data crai sy’n dangos arsylwadau’n ymwneud â’r holl adar môr a’r gridiau a grëwyd lle y dangosir dwysedd y rhywogaethau sy’n hedfan ac yn eistedd ar raddfa grid 3 cilometr a chwmpas yr arolwg. Pwrpas y gwaith cipio data hwn oedd coladu data o gronfeydd ESAS a WWT Consulting er mwyn cynhyrchu cronfa ddata gyfunol o adar a gofnodwyd mewn arolygon o ddyfroedd Cymru.  Cydnabyddiaeth:  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe two main sources of seabird distribution and abundance data for welsh waters are held in JNCC\u0027s European Seabirds at Sea (ESAS) database and the Wildfowl and Wetlands (Consulting) Ltd marine aerial surveys database.  A collation for Wales was created by producing a database containing all the fields from both datasets.  This was then populated.  The data was \u0027cleaned\u0027 to remove non-bird objects and data.  A GIS layer was produced and supplied from the database.  Density and abundance maps as well as survey coverage were derived from the database using a 3km by 3km grid created in ArcGIS.    A detailed methodology and the approach taken is described in the WWT Report  \"Collating seabird distribution and abundance data in Welsh waters to support provision of strategic advice on the relative risk of deployment of tidal stream devices\".    Originally the density maps were produced to provide evidence about mobile species and their sensitivity to tidal stream devices (See NRW project 309 media 1230).  This dataset is an update based on the same methodology but includes all gull species which were not required for the renewables work.  The selection of the highest value from the combined JNCC ESAS and WWT datasets for each grid cell is in line with the Precautionary Principle. This may lead to an overestimation of abundance in some grid cells but will reflect the highest potential use of each cell by diving bird species. In addition diving bird density values for each grid cell were standardised according to survey effort so that those grid cells for which there is a very low level of effort may have an overestimation of overall abundance.  It is important to interpret these evidence layers in conjunction with information on the underlying survey effort.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Seabird coverage 3KM WGS84.lyr,  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Seabird WWT ESAS merged flying 3KM WGS84.lyr,  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Seabird WWT ESAS merged sitting 3KM WGS84.lyr,   The source data used to create the grid cell maps is also stored at: X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Seabird WWT SAS OBS merged WGS84.lyr   however this is not covered by this metadata entry as restrictions may apply and only available internally.    Archived data in original MapInfo format is held within the Marine archive as project 333, media no. 1292\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy Marine Ornithologist, MFEG, NRW Metadata contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2010-07-01\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/882/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:SeabirdsAtSea\t2025-05-07T09:03:33.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18001\tNRW_DS114723\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.129Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Blue Mussel Beds (Mytilus edulis) (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373233\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Blue Mussel (Mytilus edulis) beds on sediments only. Blue mussel (Mytilus edulis) beds are small, blue mussels which can form extensive beds, with living and dead mussels, sand and mud bound together by the mussel’s sticky byssus threads. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC OSPAR Blue Mussel Beds Point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC OSPAR Blue Mussel Beds Polygon.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at H:\\Science\\MSFG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Additional Word documents are available as project 282, media 1098.  GIS layers are both point and polygon.\tInternal Custodian:  Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:16:42.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17997\tNRW_DS114724\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.072Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Carbonate Reefs (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373234\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.  The data is related to carbonate reefs only. The reef structure that was identified is known as \u0027Holden\u0027s Reef\u0027 – it is composed of a particular type of calcium carbonate formed by methane gas leaking from the seabed. This, and the associated reefs are the first known example of this habitat in UK inshore waters.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decision made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made. The data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Carbonate Reefs.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working. Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tCustodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Spatial Data and GIS Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17998\tNRW_DS114725\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.070Z\t541\tValid\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Coastal Saltmarsh\tCynefinoedd Morol a blaenoriaeth yng Nghymru (Corsydd Heli Arfordirol)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373235\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.    The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.     The data is related to coastal saltmarsh only. Coastal saltmarshes in the UK comprise the upper, vegetated portions of intertidal mudflats, lying approximately between mean high water neap tides and mean high water spring tides. Saltmarshes are usually restricted to comparatively sheltered locations in five main physiographic situations: in estuaries, in saline lagoons, behind barrier islands, at the heads of sea lochs, and on beach plains. The development of saltmarsh vegetation is dependent on the presence of intertidal mudflats.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Seagrass Beds.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2006-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:40:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17994\tNRW_DS114727\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.023Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Estuarine Rock (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373237\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to estuarine rock only. Estuaries are usually soft, muddy places, so rock and stable boulders in estuaries are rare, and occur mostly in drowned river valleys and fjords.  They form a small proportion of estuarine seascapes but are important because they contribute a lot to the richness of life within estuaries.  The rich and sheltered waters of estuaries provide nursery grounds for fish, and the rocky areas are an important part of these. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC Estuarine Rock Point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\NERC Estuarine Rock Polygon.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17995\tNRW_DS114728\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.017Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Fragile Sponge and Anthozoan (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373238\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to fragile sponge and anthozoan only. Anthozoans are a group of soft animals with feathery tentacles, which includes soft corals, sea-fans, cup corals and anemones.  Members of this group can be found together with branching sponges on steeply sloping bedrock or large boulders in depths from about 50m to just below low tide. These sponge and anthozoan communities require sufficient water movement to bring a ready supply of food but not too much, as they are vulnerable to breakage.  As a result, they are found on shores which are exposed to moderate or strong waves and possibly quite strong tidal currents, but only in areas of those shores where there is some local shelter from the full force of the waves and tides.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Fragile Sponges Anthozoan.lyr    x:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Fragile Sponges Biotopes.lyr    Data is archived as project 282, media 1098 Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.   Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (X drive).  .\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:30.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17993\tNRW_DS114729\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.862Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Honeycomb Worm (Sabellaria) Reefs (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373239\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Honeycomb worm (Sabellaria) reefs only. Sabellaria spinulosa reefs comprise of dense subtidal aggregations of this small, tube-building polychaete worm. Sabellaria spinulosa can act to stabilise cobble, pebble and gravel habitats, providing a consolidated habitat for epibenthic species. They are solid (albeit fragile), massive structures at least several centimetres thick, raised above the surrounding seabed, and persisting for many years. As such, they provide a biogenic habitat that allows many other associated species to become established. The S. spinulosa reef habitats of greatest nature conservation significance are those which occur on predominantly sediment or mixed sediment areas. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Sabellaria Alveolata Reef.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive). Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian:  Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17992\tNRW_DS114730\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.877Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Intertidal Mudflats (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373330\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to intertidal mudflats only. Mudflats are sedimentary intertidal habitats created by deposition in low energy coastal environments, particularly estuaries and other sheltered areas. Their sediment consists mostly of silts and clays with a high organic content. Towards the mouths of estuaries where salinity and wave energy are higher the proportion of sand increases. Mudflats are intimately linked by physical processes to, and may be dependent on, other coastal habitats such as soft cliffs and saltmarshes. They commonly appear in the natural sequence of habitats between subtidal channels and vegetated saltmarshes. In large estuaries they may be several kilometres wide and commonly form the largest part of the intertidal area of estuaries. However, in many places they have been much reduced by land claim. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Intertidal Mudflats.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian:  Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18003\tNRW_DS114731\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.220Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Intertidal Underboulder (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373331\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.    The data is related to Intertidal underboulder communities only. Intertidal underboulder communities are unique environments from the rest of the shoreline due to residing in damp and shaded areas which provide refuge on an otherwise hostile beach. These environments are characterised by sea mats, sponges, sea squirts, barnacles, coat-of-mail shells, tube worms and tufts of pink coralline seaweed encrust the undersurfaces.  Sea slugs, such as the sea-lemon, feed on encrusting sponges and the European cowrie eats sea squirts.  Brittlestars, porcelain crabs (which are actually lobsters) and squat lobsters also cling to the undersides of boulders. The undersurfaces are an important refuge for the eggs of fish, dog whelks and sea slugs. Crabs and fish hide amongst the boulders, and they may be a particularly important shelter for young edible lobsters.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Intertidal Underboulder Points.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Intertidal Underboulder Polygons.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:16:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17987\tNRW_DS114732\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.648Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Maerl (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373332\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to maerl only. Maerl is a collective term for several species of red seaweed, with hard, chalky skeletons. It is rock hard and, unlike other seaweeds, it grows as unattached rounded nodules or short, branched shapes on the seabed. Maerl beds are an important habitat for many different types of marine life, which live amongst or are attached to the surface of Maerl, or burrow in the coarse gravel of dead maerl beneath the top living layer. Maerl beds can be of importance to sustainable fisheries, providing nursery grounds for commercial species of fish and shellfish. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Maerl Beds.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17990\tNRW_DS114733\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.750Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Horse Mussel (Modiolus modiolus) Beds (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373333\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to horse mussel Modiolus modiolus only and was originally collated to assist in the identification of Highly Protected Marine Conservation Zones (HPMCZ\u0027s) in Wales, however it was recognized that the data would have wide uses for conservation in Wales and therefore information about the habitats was as encompassing as possible. The horse mussel Modiolus modiolus forms dense beds at depths of 5-70 m in fully saline, often moderately tide-swept areas off northern and western parts of the British Isles. M. modiolus can occur as relatively small, dense beds of epifaunal mussels carpeting steep rocky surfaces, but is more frequently recessed at least partly into mixed or muddy sediments in a variety of tidal regimes. Extensive expanses of seabed are covered in scattered clumps of semi-recessed M. modiolus on muddy gravels.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Horse Mussel Beds.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18004\tNRW_DS114734\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.352Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Mud Habitats in Deep Water (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373334\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Mud habitats only. Mud habitats in deep water are fairly stable environments typically found below 20 meters where currents are slow and they are not affected by waves. This can be found offshore and in sheltered inshore environments such as sea lochs.  Most of the animals that live here burrow below the surface.  The animal communities vary according to the levels of silt, clay, sand and nutrients found in the mud. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Mud in Deep Water WGS84.lyr      Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tData Custodian:  Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:16:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17989\tNRW_DS114735\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.694Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Musculus discors Habitats (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373335\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Musculus discors habitats only. This biotope typically occurs on the upper faces of moderately exposed, moderately tide-swept bedrock, boulders and cobbles in slightly silty conditions. The mussel Musculus discors occurs in dense mats and occasionally completely coats all available surfaces.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Musculus Discors Beds.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18000\tNRW_DS114736\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.145Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Oyster Beds (Ostrea edulis) (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373336\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Oyster (Ostrea edulis) beds only. Native Oyster (Ostrea edulis) beds occur in shallow water (usually less than about 10 m deep) on fine, muddy sand, native oysters can be found in large numbers.  Where this happens, the oysters themselves and the dead shells amongst them form extensive beds, which become home to many other marine creatures, including crabs, worms, sea snails, sponges, sea urchins, and seaweed.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\OSPAR Oyster Beds Point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\OSPAR Oyster Beds Polygon.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian:  Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1998-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18005\tNRW_DS114737\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.363Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Peat and Clay (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373337\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Peat and Clay only. Seabeds formed of exposed peat or clay, or in some cases both, are uncommon.  Where they do occur, they have been found between the tides as well as fully underwater.  They can be buried by sand or other sediments and then exposed again on a regular basis. These unique and fragile habitats are irreplaceable, as they were formed millions of years ago from ancient lakebeds and forested peatland.  They are threatened by coastal infrastructure development, cable laying, dredging and other activities that disturb the seabed, mussel fisheries and the collection of the piddocks for bait.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Peat and Clay Points.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Peat and Clay Polygons.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:17:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17999\tNRW_DS114738\t2024-05-28T12:37:08.128Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Saline Lagoons (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373338\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Saline Lagoons only. Lagoons in the UK are essentially bodies, natural or artificial, of salinewater partially separated from the adjacent sea. They retain a proportion of their seawater at low tide and may develop as brackish, full saline or hyper-saline water bodies.  Lagoons can contain a variety of substrata, often soft sediments which in turn may support tasselweeds and stoneworts aswell as filamentous green and brown algae. In addition lagoons contain invertebrates rarely found elsewhere. They also provide important habitat for waterfowl, marshland birdsand seabirds. The flora and invertebrate fauna present can be divided into three main components: those that are essentially freshwater in origin, those that are marine/brackish species and those that are more specialist lagoonal species. The presence of certain indigenous and specialist plants and animals make this habitat important to the UK\u0027s overall biodiversity. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Saline Lagoons.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Saline Lagoons 2009.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:46.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17986\tNRW_DS114739\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.596Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Seagrass Beds (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373339\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to seagrass beds only. Seagrasses (also known, for their long thin leaves, as eel grass) are grass-like flowering plants with dark green, long, narrow, ribbon-shaped leaves. They are one of the very few groups of flowering plants that live in the sea. Common eel grass is the only species that occurs below low water mark, and it can form dense underwater lawns.  It grows mainly on sand, but also fine gravel, typically down to a depth of 4 m. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Seagrass Beds.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:48.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17991\tNRW_DS114740\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.876Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Sheltered Muddy Gravel (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373430\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Sheltered Muddy Gravel only. Muddy gravels occur mainly in estuaries, drowned river valleys and sea lochs, in areas protected from wave action and strong tidal streams. They can be found both on the shore and in the shallows.  Which plants and animals occupy the gravels depends on the composition of the seabed and the influence of freshwater. Where the seawater is undiluted, muddy gravel seascapes tend to be dominated by worms, such as delicate peacock worms, with their fan of feathery tentacles, which is used for trapping food from the passing seawater.  Burrowing anemones are found in muddy gravels, as are burrowing bivalves (with their paired, hinged shells), including ridged carpet shells and blunt gapers. Coarse gravel and stones on surface of the muddy gravel provide a hard substrate for seaweeds to attach to.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Sheltered Muddy Gravels.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working. Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-09-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:32.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17988\tNRW_DS114741\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.604Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Subtidal Mixed Muddy Sediments (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373431\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to subtidal mixed sediments only. Subtidal mixed seabeds can have a range of different types of sediment from muddy, gravely sands to mosaics of cobbles and pebbles in or on a sand, gravel or mud seabed. Mixed areas also include seabeds where waves or ribbons of sand form on the surface of a gravel bed.  They may support a wide range of animals, both on and in the sediment.  Animals found here include worms, bivalves (with their paired, hinged shells), starfish and urchins, anemones, sea firs and sea mats.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Subtidal Mixed Muddy Sediment WGS84.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17981\tNRW_DS114742\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.162Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Subtidal Sand and Gravels (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373432\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Subtidal sand and gravels only. Sand and gravel seabeds are widespread.  Those found to the west of the UK include a high proportion of shell fragments, whereas those from the North Sea are mainly formed from rock.  They occur in a range of environmental conditions, from wave-sheltered, enclosed bays and estuaries to highly exposed open coasts. The mix of sand or gravel, and any sand waves or ripples present on the surface of the seabed, depend on factors such as the strength of the waves and tides. The communities of animals that live in this habitat are also determined by the environmental conditions. There tend to be fewer animals where waves and tides are strong, and the communities there will be characterised by rapid burrowers such as bristleworms, tube worms, bivalve shells and shrimp-like creatures, and perhaps by crabs, hermit crabs and sea snails on the surface of the seabed.  Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP_Habitats\\Marine Bap Habitats Subtidal Sands and Gravels BGS.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working.  Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17979\tNRW_DS114743\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.060Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPriority Marine Habitats of Wales: Tideswept Channels (Archived 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373433\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitats cover a wide range of semi-natural habitat types, and were those that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).  The UK BAP habitats and species lists have been used to draw statutory lists of priority habitats as required under Section 42 of the Natural Environmental and Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  The habitats listed as priority habitats include; blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh and Subtidal sands and gravels.   The data is related to Tide-swept channels only. Tide-swept channels occur where the constricted coastline acts as a funnel.  They are found at the entrances to fjords, lochs and lagoons, between individual islands, and between islands and the mainland.  The plentiful supply of food brought in on each tide supports rich and varied communities of marine life. Majority of the records are from the 1960s onwards. This data is still in draft format and has not yet been finalised. All habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap, Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats. Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decisions made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  The habitat layers build on and replaced work started in the CCW BAP Atlas.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived\\Marine Bap Habitats Tidal Swept Channels.lyr   Working copies of the data is held on the MFSG Drive at S:\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Habitats GIS working. Finalised copies will be made available on the corporate GIS drive (m drive).  Data is archived as project 282, media 1098.\tInternal Custodian: Subtidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:14:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17980\tNRW_DS114744\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.159Z\t346\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Marine Species Sightings and Miscellaneous Data (Extracted from Marine Recorder)\tRhywogaethau morol a welwyd a data amrywiol CCGC 2011\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373434\tThe dataset was set up to collate ad-hoc records of species around Wales as seen by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) staff or provided specifically to NRW for its business use.  The purpose of this collation was to record incidental records. These tend to be species of special interest, such as, rare or scarce species or species not considered native in Welsh waters.    Data may include incidental records collected while conducting specific scientific research that are outside of the remit of the research or methodology used. This dataset is a work in progress and is continually updated.  Sightings date from 2007 onwards however older records may be added from files and notes as required. The majority of the recorded by experienced staff or are verified with known experts and staff through the use of photographs, video or specimens.\tAd-hoc sightings therefore no standardised methodology was used.\tOnly non-sensitive data will be available at full resolution via the NBN Atlas.\t[A]  Data is held in Natural Resources Wales copy of Marine Recorder.  Data from the old Marine Recorder (SurveyID MRCCW30000000080, MRCCW3000000005E) is available on the NBN Atlas.  New datasets stored in Marine Recorder Online (MRO) (SurveyID: ba528f7b-3b7f-4bfe-837a-3961dc0b0be6, 8f9ccf45-384e-48ce-9d1a-cb2f62875e4a) is due to be published.\tPaul Brazier Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting.  NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-11-14T11:45:13.658Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17985\tNRW_DS114753\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.243Z\t1277\tValid\tEditable\tMonitoring of a Maerl Bed in the Milford Haven Waterway, Pembrokeshire 2010\tMonitro Gwely Marl yn nyfrffordd Aberdaugleddau, Sir Benfro, 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373533\tMaerl is a collective term for several species of red seaweed, with hard, chalky skeletons. It is rock hard and, unlike other seaweeds, it grows as unattached rounded nodules or short, branched shapes on the seabed.  Like all seaweeds, maerl needs sunlight to grow, and it only occurs to a depth of about 20 m. Maerl can form large beds, when the conditions are right – a fast tidal flow or sufficient wave action to remove fine sediments, but not strong enough to break the brittle maerl branches.  Within these beds, layers of dead maerl build up with a thin layer of pink, living maerl on the top. Maerl beds are classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) priority habitat, listed under the Annex 1 of the Habitats Directive, is on the list of OSPAR\u0027s List of Threatened and/or Declining Species and Habitats (Region 3 - Celtic Sea), and the habitat supports a number of UKBAP priority species, and species of principal important for the purpose of conservation of biodiversity under the Natural Environment and Rural Communities Act 2006. This data contains the results of a monitoring exercise undertaken to assess the status of the maerl bed in the Haven for the reporting purposes, and also to compare with baseline data gathered in 2005 to identify any trends. The information contributes to UK BAP monitoring and reporting obligations and to the management of the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC). This dataset forms part of repeated monitoring of 6 sites within the maerl bed in the Milford Haven waterway. The initial surveys were carried out in 2005.\tThis project was the first repeat of a long term monitoring study (by divers) of the epibiota, infauna and sediment characteristics at six sites within the Milford Haven maerl bed. The following methods were used:  - In situ quadrat cell frequency counts. - In situ cores for Particle Size Analysis. - In situ cores for infaunal analysis. - In situ surface sampling for microscopic identification of epiflora.  The data quality if considered to be high. There is a high confidence in taxonomic skills of contractor and management of sub-contractors with regards to standards in taxonomy throughout the survey. This project was the first repeat of a long term monitoring study (by divers) of the epibiota, infauna and sediment characteristics at six sites within the Milford Haven maerl bed.\t\tData is archived as project 337, media 1303.   Voucher specimens are to be held by the National Museums of Wales, Cardiff.\tMarine Biodiversity Ecologist, NRW. Team Leader - Marine Monitoring, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-12-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:24:00.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17983\tNRW_DS114765\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.152Z\t329\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCockle and Mussel Stock (Bird Food) Surveys in the Burry Inlet 2011\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373635\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPAs) are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive), and for regularly occurring migratory species. The Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) supports nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders. It also supports an important cockle fishery. The removal of cockles and the associated disturbance caused by fishing them needs to be balanced with bird food requirements. The aim of the project is to assess the food requirements of wading birds that over-winter in the Burry Inlet Special Protection Areas (SPAs). Data Collection is repeated annually. The purpose of this data capture was to provide data on stock abundance and distribution across the whole of the Burry Inlet. The data collected and its results will primarily be used for site monitoring and also for casework. In the future this data may be combined with data gathered on bird foraging behaviour, together with site specific data on bird numbers and used in an individual behaviour based model to predict the food requirements of a bird population.\tApproximately 400 cockle survey stations (on a grid system) surveyed, using GPS, 0.1m2 quadrats and a sieve of 2 mm mesh. Cockles retained in the sieve were measured and counted; enabling the production of cockle density maps and estimates of the total cockle populations of each area. Mussel beds in the Inlet were also surveyed in a similar way, and all mussels over 10 mm length in each 0.1m2 quadrat were measured and counted. This enabled the estimation of the total mussel populations of each bed.  All data entry was subject to auto-validation and conditional formatting. Data was also checked by the project manager for errors. The data was put through a rigorous series of verification procedures to ensure that there were no inconsistencies between the field records, the shellfish measurements and the sub-sample records.  Samples of the shellfish were analysed to measure wet weight, ash free dry weight and shell length; providing sufficient data to determine length: weight relationships and thereby calculate estimates of total weight of shellfish in the Inlet.  In addition to the whole Inlet grid of cockle surveys stations (with 250m spacing) a trial was carried out in 2011 to assess the benefits of closer spacing between the stations.  A grid of 49 stations with 100 m spacing was sampled, using the standard sampling methodology, in a sub-area of the north Inlet bed. The methodology for the data collection is based on West et al. (2004). The dataset builds on work initiated in 2004 as part of a wider assessment of Cockle and Mussel Stocks across three sites in Wales (The Dee Estuary, Traeth Lafan, Gwynedd and the Burry Inlet.  Repeat surveys in the Burry were then carried out annually at the same time of year (Sept/Oct) between 2005 and 2009).  There was no survey in 2010.\t\tData is archived as project 341,media 1312\tInternal Custodians:  Maritime Ornithologist, NRW  and   Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17982\tNRW_DS114766\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.163Z\t375\tValid\tEditable\tSurveys of Potential Oystercatcher Prey, Pembrey Sands (2011)\tArolygon o ysglyfaeth posibl piod mor, Traeth Pen-bre, 2011\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373636\tThe Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) supports nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders. The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), are studying the food requirements of oystercatcher and knot, to develop monitoring targets and to assess the implications of shellfish fisheries on the bird populations.  This data was captured to provide data on abundance and distribution of potential oystercatcher prey on Pembrey Sands. The data collected and its results will primarily be used for site monitoring and also for casework. As part of that study, surveys of the cockle and mussel stocks in the Burry Inlet have been carried out every autumn since 2004, using a standard methodology.  Significant populations of oystercatcher also feed in the Three Rivers and along Pembrey Sands and it is known that they are part of the same population that feed in the Burry Inlet SPA.    Data from the British Trust for Ornithology (BTO) on oystercatcher distribution along the Pembrey coast showed that they concentrate in the central section of the Sands and confirmed that they are feeding at low tide.  Sediment fauna that are found along the Pembrey coast include a variety of shellfish (but not cockles and mussels) and a number of large bodied polychaete worms that may be potential prey for oystercatcher.  A survey of potential oystercatcher prey along the Pembrey Sands was carried out in late September 2009.  It showed high densities of the bivalves Donax vittatus and Angulus vittatus and the polychaetes Lanice conchilega and Owenia fusiformis, distributed across the lower and middle shore.  A repeat survey was commissioned by CCW in autumn 2011. In the future this data may be combined with data gathered on bird foraging behaviour, together with site specific data on bird numbers and used in an individual behaviour based model to predict the food requirements of a bird population.\tThe methodology for Pembrey Sands was adapted from that used for the cockle surveys in the Burry Inlet. 94 stations with 250 m spacing were sampled in 2011 using a 0.1m2 quadrat / 2mm mesh sieve.  Bivalves and polychaetes retained in the sieve were measured (length) and counted; enabling the production of distribution / density maps and estimates of the total population for each species. All data was collected by staff with a background in Biological Sciences. All data entry was subject to auto-validation and conditional formatting. Data was also checked by the project manager for errors. The data was put through a rigorous series of verification procedures to ensure that there were no inconsistencies between the field records, the shellfish measurements and the sub-sample records.\t\tData is archived as project 342 and media 1313.\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy, Maritime Ornithologist, NRW. Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata contact:  Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:47:15.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17984\tNRW_DS114771\t2024-05-28T12:37:07.169Z\t502\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcross Wales Marine Non-Native Species Survey around Welsh Marinas\tArolwg o Rywogaethau Anfrodorol ar Draws Cymru 2011\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373731\tThe dataset is part of an Ongoing rapid assessments for presence/absence of marine Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) in Welsh marinas. A sub-section of probable invasive marine species was determined using a mixture of Welsh high-priority species and species previously found by the Marine Aliens 2 Group. The purpose of this data capture was to look at the spread of invasive non-natives in Wales and provide early warnings for new introductions with a possible view to employing eradication techniques.\tAssessments were conducted by visual inspection of the entire marina and associated structures from the surface followed by snorkel surveys where non-natives were observed or where native biota composition suggested that marina habitat was amenable to non-native colonisation. Initial surveys in 2011 were carried out by the Countryside Council for Wales, with subsequent work to be carried out by The School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University under a MoA with CCW.\t\t[A]  Data is archived as project 321 and media 1269.   [B]  Species data is stored in Marine Recorder 2011 CCW/SOS Across Wales Non-Native Species Survey MRCCW31300000002.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Ecologist,  NRW. External Custodian:  Stuart Jenkins, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-07-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:25:15.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17977\tNRW_DS114775\t2024-05-28T12:37:06.807Z\t618\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Marine Education Framework in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373735\tThis project is the first step in the development of a Marine Education Framework in Wales to provide a strategic approach for integrating marine experiences, both inside and outside the classroom, with the Welsh curriculum and subject specifications. The purpose of this data capture was to identify links between Welsh educational policies, subject specifications and curricula and the marine environment.\tData captured through a literature review, web-based consultation and face-to-face consultation. Data is informative, the consultation with teachers is not statistically rigorous.\t\t[A] Data is archived as project 344, media 1320 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1301 to 1400) [B] Reports and documents are also available in Ffynnon.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsty Lindenbaum, Senior Specialist Advisor Marine, Sustainable Places Land \u0026amp; Sea  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:04:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17978\tNRW_DS114776\t2024-05-28T12:37:06.830Z\t370\tUnassessed\tEditable\tVolunteer Resource Packs for Marine Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Species and Habitats\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134373736\tA project designed to develop a series of \"volunteer packs\" to provide guidance for volunteers to take measurements on the \u0027extent\u0027 and \u0027condition\u0027 of 8 Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitats and species as follows; Subtidal seagrass beds;  Mat mussel beds; Crayfish; crawfish or spiny Lobster; Fragile sponge and anthozoans communities on subtidal rock habitats; Intertidal seagrass beds; Honeycomb worm reefs; Peat and clay exposures; Natural blue mussel beds on sediment. This project was create and data captured to allow volunteers to contribute to BAP habiat and species recording by providing robust and scientific data to a described standard.  The project includes a report on the scope and discussion of best methods for volunteers for each habitat and species existing distribution maps and GIS layers for the 8 habitat and species images of each habitat and species draft resource packs and recording forms.\tEach resource pack was developed to stand alone, and contain the following information:  - an introduction to the species or habitat; information on where to find it;  -  guidance on what to record where - how to record it and - useful survey recording forms.\t\tData is archived as project 346, media 1322.   The report is available in Ffynnon. Contract Science Report No 987.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Biodiversity Ecologist, NRW. (Footage taken by Damon-stanwell Smith)  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tCountryside Council for Wales\t\t\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17976\tNRW_DS114807\t2024-05-28T12:37:06.701Z\t549\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Lamprey in the River Dee (2011)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383037\tRiver, Brook and Sea Lamprey are a designated (Annex II) feature of the River Dee Special Area of Conservation. The purpose of this data collection was to assess the conservation status of the Annex II features, including species composition, distribution, abundance and size class composition are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment.  Monitoring was undertaken on the Dee Catchment in 2011.\tIn 2011 monitoring is a continuation of data collected in 2004, which included the capture of historical records from 1985 onwards.  Electrofishing methodologies were used.  Following the LIFE protocol, where possible, surveys were carried out in both optimal and sub-optimal habitat at each site. Habitats were surveyed quantitatively using an enclosed area as a barrier to fish movement. Where possible a 1 m2 quadrat, enclosed with 2 mm fine mesh netting was positioned over the selected habitat at each survey site and then left to settle. At sub optimal sites a complete 1 m2 area of habitat was often not present. In this case several areas of sub optimal habitat were sampled within the 100 m stretch to represent a combined area of 1 m2. Under these circumstances a quadrat was not used to enclose the sample area. Electric fishing was undertaken within the quadrat in such a way as to draw individual lamprey out of the sediment rather than stunning and trapping them in the silt. The anode was energised in short bursts of 20 seconds followed by 5 second gaps. This was carried out over a two minute period. This procedure was classed as a single run, which in optimal habitats was subsequently repeated at least twice within the same quadrat (with a five minute gap between runs). In sub-optimal habitats a single run was carried out at each site.\t\t[A]  Data is archived as project 348, media 1325.   [B]  Species data in Recorder 6 [CCW000800000000A]\tInternal Custodian:  Tristan Hatton Ellis - Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18031\tNRW_DS114817\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.707Z\t317\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Preliminary Description of the Sublittoral Habitats and Associated Biota within the Skomer Marine Conservation Zone ( MCZ )\tDisgrifiad rhagarweiniol o\u0027r cynefinoedd isarforol a\u0027r biota cysylltiedig yng Ngwarchodfa Natur Forol Ynys Sgomer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383137\tIn 2015 Skomer Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). Skomer Marine Nature Reserve (MNR) was designated in 1990.  This dataset consists of the survey results from sublittoral work carried out between 1973 and 1975.  This work was the first baseline sublittoral dive survey of the Skomer Marine Nature Reserve and there is only one copy of the report currently available to NRW.  As a result the digitisation and archiving of this report in a format utilisable to ongoing monitoring work and decision makers was deemed essential. The survey work focussed on the sublittoral environment around Skomer Island Marine Nature Reserve. The locations of the stations were fixed at regular distances throughout the then proposed area of the reserve, at regular depth intervals; forming a series of discontinuously sampled transects of the seabed cliff or slope. This formation of sample stations was chosen to ensure information could be obtained on substrate types and topography; water movement (exposure); and macro-flora and fauna.\tData was extracted from the Hunnam (1976) historical report and entered into Marine Recorder. The survey utilised diving survey (similar to Marine Nature Conservation Review phase 2 dive survey). The original report described 9 habitats and associated species, and assigned these to 100 stations. Stations were roughly digitised from hand drawn maps. Only one or two habitats were assigned per station in the original report although it is suspected from descriptions that more habitats may have been present at some stations than have been digitised, although this could not be confirmed. Where it was uncertain from generic habitat descriptions whether species were present at a specific station, species were marked as \u0027Uncertain\u0027 in Marine Recorder. Note that abundance data was converted to the SACFOR scale wherever possible, but that this is a rough conversion. The original survey was only recorded in paper format but has since been digitised and entered into Marine Recorder.\t\t[A] Data is archived as project 352, media 1331 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1331) [B] Species and habitat data is available in Marine Recorder [MRCCW31700000003]\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1976-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:37:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18035\tNRW_DS114818\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.718Z\t507\tUnassessed\tEditable\tManagement Measures for Self-Propagated Future Recovery of Crawfish, Palinurus elephas, in Welsh Waters.\tMesurau rheoli ar gyfer adfer cimychiaid yr afon, Palinurus elephas, yn nyfroedd Cymru yn y dyfodol trwy hunan-epilio\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383138\tThe crawfish, Palinurus elephas, is a large benthic crustacean found mainly on rocky seabeds from the Azores in the south to Norway in the North and throughout the Mediterranean.  This species is commercially valuable and has been targeted by fisheries for over a century; however, fisheries in Wales and beyond have seen a steep decline in landings since the 1960s which coincided with the introduction of net fisheries for this species.   The crawfish is a priority species for the UK Biodiversity Action Plan and is also an important component of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) designated for rocky reefs.  It has been identified by the National Assembly for Wales as one of the species of \"principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\" in Wales and is listed in the Natural Environment Rural Communities (NERC) Act 2006.  This act requires that steps be taken that are \u0027reasonably practicable to further the conservation of the living organisms\u0027 listed and to \u0027promote the taking by others of such steps\u0027.  It is therefore important to understand the potential impact of fisheries management measures and other management measures on the recovery of this species both in designated areas and within the wider context of Welsh waters as a whole including the potential impact of regulations or management measures in adjacent waters.   The purpose of this data capture was to investigate management measures for the self-propagated future recovery of crawfish Palinurus elephas in Welsh waters. The overall aim of this survey and reporting work was to establish the potential effectiveness of existing and possible management measures (both fishery and nature conservation based) to enable self-propagated recovery of P. elephas populations in Welsh waters within the context of their declined status.  This was to be carried out through a review of relevant literature on this and other crustacean species to determine using the best available data and information the potential benefit of the different management measures.\tIn order to assess the potential effectiveness of existing and potential management measures for the recovery of Welsh populations of P elephas a review of available information has been carried out in the form of a literature review, including grey literature, and through liaison with persons with specific relevant expertise. Where possible direct examples of recovery attributed to different management measures are reported including timescales. Where direct examples are not available, comparisons are drawn and aspects of the biology of P. elephas are interpreted with regards to the specific management measure in question.  Where relevant management measures are applied in adjacent areas with the potential to aid recovery in Welsh waters, these are also included.  The limitations of each management measure in aiding the potential recovery of crawfish populations in Welsh waters are also discussed. This metadata relates to a literature review, therefore there is no associated data other than the references within the final report.\t\tReport archived as project 353, media 1332 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1332)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External Contact: NAFC Marine Centre  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:49:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18033\tNRW_DS114819\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.688Z\t696\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros) and feeding habitat within Coedydd Derw a Safleoedd Ystlumod Meirion / Merionnydd Oak Woods Bat Sites SAC (2006)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383139\tCoedydd Derw a Safloedd Ystlumod Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is a large composite site of international importance for lesser horseshoe bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros) containing more than 15 known maternity roosts and hibernacula. Although lesser horseshoe bat annual count data are collected from roosts within the SAC by the Gwynedd Bat Group and other volunteers, additional information is required regarding the condition of foraging habitat and flight lines surrounding the roosts. For the purposes of this contract, an area covering a radius of two kilometres around each of the ten target roosts was selected for further study. The purpose of this data capture was to establish the condition of the lesser horseshoe bat within the Meirionydd Oakwoods and Bat Sites Special Area of Conservation (SAC).\tA total of 32 sites on potential flight lines, linked to eight target maternity roosts were monitored for bat activity throughout the summer months to establish the accessibility of potential woodland foraging areas. Feeding habitat, flight line condition and bat activity were parameters investigated. Using digital Phase 1 habitat survey data, ten of the survey areas were visited to establish the accuracy of the original data, some of which is over ten years old. Any changes in habitat classes 1-3 were noted and the necessary alterations made to the final maps depicting these potential foraging areas.  Targets were then set  for the area of quality foraging habitat and its connectivity to the target roost to establish the condition of the lesser horseshoe bat population.  Data was used within the project that are collected from roosts within the SAC annually on bat counts by the Gwynedd Bat Group and other volunteers.\t\tAssociated data and reports are held at Maes-y-Ffynnon at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\33 Monitoring selected colonies of LHB and feeding habitat within Meirionnydd Oak Woods SAC.\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:09.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18029\tNRW_DS114836\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.693Z\t873\tValid\tEditable\tCollection Activity in the Menai Strait and Pilot Methods to Assess Frequency of Boulder Turning Activity (2011)\tGweithgareddau casglu yn Y Fenai a dulliau peilot o asesu amlder gweithgareddau troi clogfeini 2011.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383336\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs) are strictly protected sites designated under the EC Habitats Directive. Reporting on the condition of the intertidal features of the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) depends upon an understanding of the features and the threats of damage by regulated and unregulated activities.The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), run an intertidal SAC monitoring program, which is undertaken to evaluate the quality of the biological communities associated with under-boulder and muddy gravel habitats. The biological data must be placed in context with the levels of collecting activity on the shore, specifically bait and winkle collecting.   CCW contracted Marine EcoSol to better assess the intensity of collecting activity in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC, in order to establish the levels that might be considered  acceptable, in terms of their impact on the intertidal (Reef) features. Both muddy gravel and underboulder communities are Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitats, and the 2011 field surveys are within or adjacent to intertidal Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and marine SAC.  The project was to investigate the feasibility of developing rapid visual assessment methods for use by non-specialists and to provide contextual information for status reporting of reef features within the Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay SAC.\tThis builds on work undertaken in 2009 to develop a trial method for the evaluation of levels of unregulated activity of collecting peeler crabs and winkles. Collection effort was rapidly assessed by driving along the length of the Menai Strait from west to east over approximately 3 hours, stopping at 17 sites to record the number of collectors at each site on the same tide. This survey was repeated 6 times between April and September. Methods to assess the impact of any boulder turning were also trialled. Surveyors assigned \u0027turning categories\u0027 to each boulder within 1 m either side of three 20 m transect lines at Y Foryd (a high impacted site) and Y Felinheli (a site of low collector impact).\tA number of improvements to the methodology are suggested in the associated report (CCW Marine Monitoring Report 94).\tArchived as project 355, media 1334. - Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1334\tData Custodian:  Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:51:08.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18018\tNRW_DS114840\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.542Z\t746\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCountryside Survey (Survey data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383430\tCountryside Survey in 2007 was funded by a partnership of government-funded bodies led by the Natural Environment Research Council (NERC) and the Department for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs (Defra).  Countryside Survey is a unique study or ‘audit’ of the natural resources of the UK’s countryside. The Survey has been carried out at discrete intervals since 1978 (2007, 1998, 1990, 1984 and 1978). The Countryside Survey provides scientifically reliable evidence about the state or health of the UK\u0027s countryside today. By comparing present findings with previous surveys it is possible to identify change (and the relative rate of change) in the countryside. This evidence is used to help form policies that influence management of the countryside, both now and in the future.  The purpose of this data capture is to provide a \u0027snap-shot\u0027 of the health of the countryside in the UK and a comparable dataset to track changes.\tThe countryside is sampled and studied using rigorous scientific methods, allowing us to compare results with those from previous surveys. In this way we can record the quantity and quality of change in our landscapes and detect even the most gradual and subtle changes that occur in the UK’s countryside over time.   A selection of 1km squares were surveyed representing a range of habitats across the UK. Different features are recorded on a portable data recorder using bespoke software. Teams of trained field surveyors record a range of information, which includes:  - Mapping Broad and Priority Habitats, as well as linear and point features.  - Recording vegetation plots of different types (29 per square on average).  - Collecting measurements and samples from streams and ponds.  - Collecting soil samples.  - Taking geo-referenced photographs of plots to illustrate their settings.  The 2007 survey is the latest in a series of surveys. Previous dates surveys were carried out are 1998, 1990, 1984 and 1978.\tCEH through the Countryside Survey (http://www.countrysidesurvey.org.uk/)\tData held by the Countryside Survey on their website (http://www.countrysidesurvey.org.uk/home).\tMetadata point of contact: Data Services Custodians: CEH and NERC Contact: Countryside Survey Project Office, Centre for Ecology and Hydrology, Lancaster  Environment Centre, Library Avenue, Bailrigg, Lancaster LA1 4AP\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\t\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:36.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18028\tNRW_DS114859\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.711Z\t371\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Regional Data: South East Wales (South East Wales Biological Records Centre - SEWBReC)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383539\tThis dataset is made up of a collation of species records (excluding sensitive records) derived from Natural Resources Wales (formerly Countryside Council for Wales), South and East Region paper files. The purpose of this data capture was to mobilise a valuable data resource in order to provide baseline data to inform conservation work, research, site management and decision-making within NRW and externally. Records are for the NRW\u0027s South and East Region which includes Blaenau-Gwent, Bridgend, Caerphilly, Cardiff, Merthyr Tydfil, Monmouthshire, Neath-Port Talbot, Newport, Rhondda-Cynon-Taff, Swansea, Torfaen, and The Vale of Glamorgan. There are also Records from other parts of Wales. Many records are for designated sites (including Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), Special Area of Conservation (SACs), National Nature Reserves etc), however, there are also large numbers of records from the wider countryside. The majority of the records have six figure grid-references, but some of these may be from site centroids rather than actual location of the species in question.\tThis dataset was created from species records extracted, computerised and collated from paper records held at NRW\u0027s Regional office files. The work was carried out under contract by SEWBReC Staff Members, Jenny Sneddon, Rebecca Johnson, Lindsay Bamforth, Alice Britt and Elaine Wright, from 2005 to 2008, as part of NRW\u0027s Data Mobilisation Project. Offices covered were Cardiff and Swanesa. The material from which records were derived included the following: reports, site registers, recording cards, protected site condition assessments, Phase 1 and Phase 2 habitat surveys, other general survey work and records of casual observations. Numerous biological recording techniques and survey methodologies were used in the collection of these records. Some of the records were produced by NRW staff or by qualified contractors working on NRW\u0027s behalf whilst some records have been provided by third parties, or derived from documents, surveys or datasets produced by others, but held in NRW files. Due to the nature of the files and the lack of documentation it is impossible to determine what proportion of the records within the dataset are NRW owned against third party data.  The majority of the records were created by NRW staff, or qualified contractors and are generally of a good standard. These records have not been subject to systematic verification. The computerisation of these records has been subject to validation. The spatial reference (grid reference) has been validated. Data should be checked against original sources before use in contentious circumstance.\t\tX:\\Biological\\NRW collated data to LRC\\CCW Regional record to SEWBREC.lyr   SEWBREC hold and manage data on behalf of NRW. NRW still hold ownership over these dataset. All data that SEWBREC hold as a top copy on behalf of NRW is published on the NBN Gateway. In Addition to this NRW hold a copy of the data in Recorder 6 - however this can be out of date. Some of these are listed in the \u0027Contains\u0027 Field and others aren\u0027t.  NRW hold an out of date copy of some of this data in Recorder 6: These are as follows. LC00021500000049  CCW Cosmeston Park SSSI LC0002150000003Z. CCW Craig Pont Rhondda SSSI LC00021500000040   CCW Cwm Taf Fechan Woodland SSSI LC00021500000030   CCW Lesser Horseshoe Bats LC0002150000002U  CCW The River Wye Valley LC0002150000002E  CCW - MOD Caerwent    Also contains the following specific metadata \u003d 116724 - CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework Files 2001 to 2005\tInternal Custodian and Metadata Contact: Karen Wilkinson is the main lead with SewBrec SewBrec are the Custodians This data will be incoprorated in the GIS layers that NRW also received\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:08.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18024\tNRW_DS114860\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.608Z\t642\tValid\tEditable\tColonisation, Growth and Interactions of Carpet Sea Squirt (Didemnum vexillum)\tYmlediad, twf ac ymarweithiad chwistrell for dorfol (Didemnum vexillum)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383630\tThe Carpet Sea Squirt (Didemnum vexillum) is a Invasive Non Native (INN) filter feeding marine invertebrate that forms large colonies carpetting hard structures on which it grows.    Carpet Sea Squirt (Didemnum vexillum) was only confirmed in British waters in 2008. However, there are strong concerns that it will quickly colonise a much wider area as it has been found to do in other countries such as New Zealand and the USA.    After an initial survey to identify the extent of colonisation, an eradication programme was undertaken by the Countryside Council for Wales. A programme of monitoring was initiated to measure the success of the eradication experiment and subsequent recolonisation and a research project undertaken in conjunction with Bangor University.    The purpose of this data capture was to assess patterns of settlement, growth, colonisation and species interactions of Didemnum vexillum within Holyhead Marina from season to season.  Also to look at how different disturbance magnitudes have negative or positive effects on Didemnum vexillum settlement and recruitment.\tTwo experiments were implemented.  Fixed quadrats (0.5x1m2) were set up in 2009.  Disturbance quadrats (30x30cm2) were set up in 2011.    Each cell was recorded using a video camera on a monthly basis.  Still images of each cell were extracted from the video and the images re-sized, stretched and \\stitched\\\" together to give and image of the full quadrat.  Percentage cover of Didemnum vexillum could the be recorded along with cover of 4 key taxonomic groups (solitary ascidians; bivalves; sponges; and macroalgae).    CCW have also established a temperature logger within the vicinity of the research work.  The current research is written up as part of an unpublished Graduate Research project Alun G. (2012).\"\t\tData is archived as projects 323 and 324 and media 1340 and 1341 (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1340 and K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1341)\tStuart Jenkins, Bangor University, School of Ocean Sciences. Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2012-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:35:58.235Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18027\tNRW_DS114892\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.693Z\t466\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEcological Effects of Re-Building Beaches in Wales\tEffeithiau ecolegol ailadeiladu traethau yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134383932\tBeach re-building - also known as beach nourishment, is the process of adding sediment to a beach to make it higher and wider. In an era of sea level rise, beaches are under serious threat - many are already adversely affected by manmade defences that have reduced the natural sediment supply. Beaches are a key natural, social and economic resource for Wales - they play a vital role in sustaining coastal tourism and underpin important nature conservation and landscape sites and acts as \u0027natural\u0027 coastal defences, dissipating wave energy, helping to protect both natural and manmade structures. During 2009/2010 a Steering Group comprising The Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), The Crown Estate, British Marine Aggregates Producers Association (BMAPA), Welsh Government, and the Environment Agency Wales (EAW) commissioned a Pilot Study to begin investigating the issues relating to the use of aggregates (sand, shingle, gravel, etc.) for beach nourishment in Wales, specifically in relation to the potential requirements for the quantity of material that may be needed to maintain Welsh beaches at current and future risk. The study is, in essence, a short data and literature review to establish the current level of knowledge regarding the ecological impacts (both positive and negative) of beach nourishment on the intertidal ecology of Welsh beaches.  The potential impacts of more \u0027traditional\u0027 hard engineering and the use beach control structures are relatively well understood by coastal engineers, while the potential impacts of beach nourishment are less well known by those responsible for all aspects of beach management.  The aim of the study is to look at the ecological effects (including potential benefits or adverse effects) of re-building beaches. This considers the most appropriate places for beach re-building, the methods used (including timing of works) and the suitability of different types of material.\tTen case study beaches were chosen to represent a range of issues around the coast of Wales and to include sandy beaches, sand and shingle beaches, high and low tourist areas and different flood and erosion risks. Using Phase 1 Intertidal maps provided by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) an area on each of the 10 study beaches was identified where beach nourishement might take place.  The areas were reviewed by CCW experts and amendements made.  Additional species and habitats that were considered important were also included in the data study. A literature study was carried out on beach nourishment projects as well as post nourishement studies to look at the sensitivity of the study areas to beach nourishment.  The search was focused using the habitats as identified in each of the study beaches and the potential impact on these. This is a follow up to the 2009/10 beach nourishement pilot study and the Phase 2 project report that looked at the practicalities of rebuilding beaches.\t\tData is archived the marine archive as project 354, media 1333 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1333)\tInternal Custodian: Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Process \u0026amp; Coastal Management, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:26:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18021\tNRW_DS114930\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.611Z\t800\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcoustic Multibeam Echosounder Survey of Turbot Bank Pembrokeshire (March 2012)\tArolwg acwstig ecoseiniwr amlbeledr o Fanc Lleden, Sir Benfro. Mawrth 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393330\tA multibeam echosounder survey was commissioned by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in March 2012 over Turbot Bank, Pembrokeshire. This was completed by EMU Ltd at the end of March 2012 to International Hydrographic Organisation (IHO) Order 1a standards. The data have been verified by the United Kingdom Hydrographic Office (UKHO) and fully tidally corrected to chart datum. The dataset shows the dominant bedform over the bank to be large to very large dunes, with bedrock visible on the eastern edge of the study area. Further data use and analysis by CCW should help to delineate features for mapping purposes, and monitor change over time.  The purpose of this data capture is to obtain bathymetry and backscatter datasets of the study area with 100% coverage.\tFull hydrographic multibeam survey using Applanix POS MV positioning system and RESON 7125 multibeam echosounder. Vessel survey lines were run to obtain 100% seabed coverage.  Dataset commissioned as a one-off and will be come part of the CCW acoustic data archive.  The data have been submitted to the UK Hydrographic Office for verification, and to be made freely available to anyone who requests it as part of the UKHO multibeam bathymetry data archive centre (DAC). This dataset will also be made available by CCW under the pan-Governmental data sharing agreement for multibeam data (See DCT-11-107096).\tFor further information see: Turbot Bank Bathymetric Survey 2012.  Final Report for Countryside Council for Wales.   EMU ref 12/J/02/2053/1340.\tData is archived as Project 362, media 1347.   Data is also available through the UK Hydrographic Office.\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Mapping Scientist, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-06-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:26:55.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18022\tNRW_DS114942\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.642Z\t330\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Piddock Beds in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2009\tGwaith Monitro Rhynglanwol ar Welau Pidogau yn ACA Bae Caerfyrddin ac Aberoedd 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393432\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. This data was captured to support a number of reporting requirements. These were namely reporting on conservation status of the piddock feature; mapping the boundary of the clay and peat exposures; to record the density of the piddock holes; and evaluate the best sampling methods.  Carmarthen Bay SAC was designated for five Annex I intertidal habitat features: Estuaries; Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide; Large shallow inlets and bays; Salicornia and other annuals colonising mud and sand; Atlantic salt meadows (Glauco- Puccinellietalia maritimae). Specific areas of interest include the peat and clay beds burrowed by piddocks and the monitoring aimed to map the boundary of the clay/peat exposures, record the density of the piddock holes and evaluate the best sampling methods.\tThe bed was divided into three blocks (western, eastern, central) and to sample randomly thrown quadrats (0.25 m2) within each block. In the event, it was found that the piddock distribution was clumped along the edges of channels and thus the randomly thrown quadrats were missing most of the piddocks. Because of this, part of the way through the exercise the method was adapted to targeting areas of piddock holes rather than sampling the whole clay exposure. In addition to the quadrat work, a number of timed counts of piddock holes were also carried out, with the intention of making a comparison between the two approaches to help with planning future monitoring work.  Piddock beds were first mapped at Marros Sands near Pendine and Whiteford Point on the north Gower coast during Phase 1 surveys carried out by CCW in 2000.The piddock beds are vulnerable to shifting beach sand, a natural phenomenon. No beds were found at Whiteford Point in 2009, although monitored previously in 2004. Data quality and confidence is considered high as all surveys were completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  Piddock beds were first mapped at Marros Sands near Pendine and Whiteford Point on the north Gower coast during Phase 1 surveys carried out by CCW in 2000.\t\tArchived as project 199 media 1350.  Working copy available as H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm\\2009 ASML Carm\\Data and species data available in Marine Recorder.  Species data also available in Marine Recorder.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:52:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18025\tNRW_DS114943\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.642Z\t398\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera) Beds in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2009\tMonitro Rhynglanwol: Zostera marina yn ACA Bae Caerfyrddin ac Aberoedd yn 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393433\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Seagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas, such as lagoons and channels, which are sheltered from significant wave action. Eelgrass (Zostera marina), is considered to be scarce (present in 16-100 ten km squares) is protected through the EC Habitats Directive and is classed as a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority Habitat. Data is captured to support reporting on conservation status of the piddock feature. Carmarthen Bay SAC was designated for five Annex I intertidal habitat features: Estuaries; Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide; Large shallow inlets and bays; Salicornia and other annuals colonising mud and sand; Atlantic salt meadows (Glauco- Puccinellietalia maritimae). Specific areas of interest include areas of eelgrass Zostera noltii in the Burry Inlet and the monitoring aimed to map the area covered by Zostera at the two sites; sample points on a grid across those areas identified to have Zostera to estimate the cover of surface species.\tThere were two known areas of Zostera noltii - a bed on Llanrhidian Sands and a second at Penrhyn Gwyn. In order to assess the quality of the beds, grids oriented north-south and east-west, with stations spaced at 50 m intervals, were created for each area. The station coordinates were uploaded to handheld GPS units and either two or three surveyors went to each position in turn. At each station a 0.25 m2 quadrat (0.5 m x 0.5 m) was placed randomly 5 times within a radius of approximately 10 m. Random locations were achieved by standing at the station position and throwing the quadrat behind the surveyor. Digital photographs were taken of each quadrat, so that the quadrat filled the frame, and the photograph number was recorded. A count was made of the number of quadrats out of the five in which either Zostera or Spartina was present. In the first quadrat only, the following were recorded: Percentage cover of: Substratum (Mud, sand, gravel, cobble); Standing water; Zostera noltii; Green algae; Mytilus edulis; Spartina anglica; Salicornia. Zostera of less than 5% cover was recorded as One plant, Few plants or Many plants; Cerastoderma edule (None, less than 100/m2, greater than 100/m2); The depth of the black layer in centimetres (measured with a ruler); Other species or features were recorded as Present; The presence of Zostera within a 3 m radius.  Zostera beds were previously mapped in 2004. Data quality and confidence was considered high as all surveys were completed by professionals and experienced marine field surveyors.\t\tArchived as project 199 media 1350.   Working copy available as H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm\\2009 ASML Carm\\Data.  Species data also available through Marine Recorder.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:53:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18023\tNRW_DS114944\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.643Z\t610\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sediment Communities in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2009\tGwaith Monitro Rhynglwnaol ar Gymunedau Gwaddodion yn ACA Bae Caerfyrddin ac Aberoedd 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393434\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  Carmarthen Bay SAC was designated for five Annex I intertidal habitat features: Estuaries; Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide; Large shallow inlets and bays; Salicornia and other annuals colonising mud and sand; Atlantic salt meadows (Glauco- Puccinellietalia maritimae). Specific areas of interest include the range of intertidal sediment communities in the bay and the monitoring aimed to collect core samples along transects established in previous years.  Data is captured to support reporting on conservation status of the piddock feature. All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  Data quality and confidence considered high. Piddocks can be described as a biotope that occurs on circalittoral soft rock, such as soft chalk or clay, most often in moderately exposed tide-swept conditions. As soft chalk and firm clay are often too soft for sessile filter-feeding animals to attach and thrive in large numbers, an extremely impoverished epifauna results on upward-facing surfaces, although vertical faces may be somewhat richer. The rock is sufficiently soft to be bored by bivalves.\tTwo transects were sampled using criteria for the Water Framework infaunal quality index tool. The sampling is currently 3 x replicates at 5 stations in each location. Three replicate cores (0.1 m2) and a sample for Particle Size Analysis were collected at five stations, located by GPS, on each transect. The cores were sieved in situ, using a 0.5 mm sieve, and the species present were recorded. A list was also made of species conspicuous at the sediment surface within a 5 m radius of the sample station.  The percentage cover of standing water, the depth of the black layer, and a brief description of the station was also recorded. Twenty transects were established in 2004 mainly covering sediment biotopes but also including some rocky habitats. The transect survey has only been completed once, although core sampling has been repeated at some of the locations. In 2008, numerous stations were revisited, but since then, only Crofty and Llansteffan, both of which were included in the original survey, have been revisited due to limited resources for collecting samples.\t\tArchived as project 199 media 1350. Working copy available as H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm\\2009 ASML Carm\\Data. Species data also available in Marine Recorder.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:54:14.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18020\tNRW_DS114945\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.590Z\t373\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Saltmarsh Extent in Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) 2009\tGwaith Monitro Rhynglanwol ar Faint y Morfa Heli yn ACA Bae Caerfyrddin ac Aberoedd 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393435\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Carmarthen Bay SAC was designated for five Annex I intertidal habitat features: Estuaries; Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide; Large shallow inlets and bays; Salicornia and other annuals colonising mud and sand; Atlantic salt meadows (Glauco- Puccinellietalia maritimae).   Specific areas of interest include saltmarsh fringing the Burry Inlet.  The Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. Data was captured to, map the 2009 boundary for comparison with previous boundaries; record patches away from the main saltmarsh; take viewpoint photographs to enable a quick comparison of boundaries on future visits.  All surveys completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  Data quality and confidence considered high.\tThe saltmarsh along the northern shore of the River Loughor was mapped between the bridge at Loughor and Penrhyn Gwyn. Three surveyors walked along the edge of the dense marsh (Spartina anglica and Salicornia) with a handheld GPS unit automatically taking a track fix every few seconds (typically 5 seconds). Outlying clumps of marsh were recorded as target notes. Indentations in the saltmarsh boundary were only mapped if believed to be a significant feature (anything over 25 m in width). Small inlets, bumps and kinks were ignored and a resolution of 25 m was mapped with a straight line mapped across the indent. Outlying clumps of marsh were recorded as target notes. Indentations in the saltmarsh boundary were only mapped if believed to be a significant feature (anything over 25 m in width). Small inlets, bumps and kinks were ignored and a resolution of 25 m was mapped with a straight line mapped across the indent.  Mapping the boundary on the Llanrhidian Marshes on the southern shore required a quad bike as the area to be covered was much greater. A small area was mapped by quad bike at Salthouse Point during the survey, following the same protocol as outlined above. The remaining and much more extensive boundary between Salthouse Point and Berges Island was mapped in early March 2010 by M. Farleigh (student in association with CCW). In addition to the mapping, viewpoint photographs from positions recorded by GPS were taken at five points on either side of the estuary.  This survey repeats some work completed in 2004 by IECS (Boyes and Brazier 2008).\t\tArchived as project 199 media 1350.   Working copy available as H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Carm\\2009 ASML Carm\\Data\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:54:48.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18032\tNRW_DS114948\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.690Z\t301\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Boulders Habitat in the Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2010\tGwaith monitro rhynglanwol ar gynefin clogfeini llanwol yn ACA Y Fenai a Bae Conwy yn 2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393438\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. To do this CCW has developed a programme of feature condition monitoring. In Menai Strait \u0026amp; Conwy Bay SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide and Reefs.   The purpose of this data capture was to assist with the reporting on the favourable conservation status of Reef features in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation. This dataset comprises of the results from an intertidal monitoring survey carried out on the tide-swept boulder habitat in the Menai Strait in 2008. Boulders are a reef feature of the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. Boulders at both Britannia Bridge and Felinheli were surveyed. The purpose of this data capture was to assist in the reporting on the favourable conservation status of Reef features in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC. All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors so data quality and confidence is considered high.\tA survey strategy and methodology for the Menai Strait tide-swept boulder sites was developed by ASML and CCW for the 2007 survey and expanded to Felinheli for the 2008 survey. Tide-swept boulder habitats which had been studied in 2005 (both sites) and 2007 (Britannia Bridge only) were surveyed. In-situ recording of all species on a minimum of 5 boulders at each sampling stations was undertaken. Boulders were previously studied at Britannia Bridge in 2005 and 2007 and at Felinheli in 2007.\tDetailed methodology can be found in CCW Marine Monitoring Report 85.\tData is archived as project 199, media 1352 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400) A working copy of the data is available at  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy_CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MSandCB\\2010 ASML MSandCB\\Data Marine Recorder Survey: MRCCW1630000002F ArcGIS layers: Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:55:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18034\tNRW_DS114949\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.710Z\t356\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Biotope SLR.FserX.T in the Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2010\tMonitro Rhynglanwol: Y Biotop SLR.FserX.T yn ACA Y Fenai a Bae Colwyn 2010.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393439\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. This dataset is the results of an intertidal monitoring survey carried out on the tide-swept boulder habitat in the Menai Strait in 2008.  Tide-swept Fucus serratus habitats is a nationally important habitat and although the habitat extent was known, no detailed studies of the communities had been carried out.  Tide-swept habitats were surveyed at two locations to provide a baseline for current condition and to allow a study for potential future impacts of mussel farming in the vicinity.  All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  Data quality and confidence considered high.\tStrategies and methods for the survey of the FserX.T biotope on the shore near Brynsiencyn were developed for the 2009 survey and then repeated and expanded to an additional site in 2010. In situ recording took place at 3 sites within the most tidally exposed area of the F. serratus zone.  Detailed methodologies can be found in CCW Marine Monitoring Report 85.\t\tData is archived as project 199, media 1352.  A working copy of the data is available at  H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MSandCB\\2010 ASML MSandCB\\Data\tInternal custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:56:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18019\tNRW_DS114970\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.522Z\t554\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey in the River Tywi (2011)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393730\tRiver lamprey, Brook lamprey and Sea lamprey are listed in Annex II of the EU Habitats Directive (EU Directive on the Conservation of Natural Habitats of Wild Fauna and Flora 92/43/EEC). There are six rivers in Wales designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) for lamprey, including the Afon Tywi in West Wales. Natural Resources Wales is responsible for monitoring the condition of lamprey species populations within SACs and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within designated sites.  The data, collected and contracted out by former Countryside Council for Wales, is used to assess the population attribute for all three lamprey species.  Sampling was carried out between the 5th October and 22nd November 2011.  A total of 30 sites distributed within the Tywi catchments were selected between Llandovery and Carmarthen.\tField work was conducted by Environment Agency staff using standard electrofishing methodologies.  A condition assessment is made using the JNCC \u0027Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) Guidance for Freshwater Fauna\u0027 . Confidence in this dataset is high.\tRivers Ecologist, NRW Freshwater Monitoring Co-Coordinator, NRW Ffynnon Reference Number: DCT-12-234579  Thomas, RH. and Garrett, H. (2012). Afon Tywi Population Attribute Condition Assessment for Brook, River and Sea Lamprey 2011. CCW Staff Science Report No 11.8/5.\t[A]  Associated data is held within the Marine Archive as Project 356, Media 1335.  [B]  Biological Data is held in Recorder 6 Lamprey Monitoring, Tywi Catchment 2011 - CCW - Staff Science Report 11/8/5 CCW000800000000B.\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:09.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18016\tNRW_DS114978\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.151Z\t676\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDormouse Core Areas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393738\tThis is a spatial dataset held by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) which holds records from the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) dormouse data sourced from areas of work, such as licence returns and dormouse survey work and also The Vincent Wildlife Trust (VWT) dormouse survey data. Data collation completed by PTES under data exchange agreement with PTES. Map has been used as basis for Glastir Targeted Element - core (or mandatory) sites.  Majority of records collected by experienced surveyors, or verified by experienced surveyors (e.g. nut hunt records). Licence returns have not been independently verified.  This dataset is a collation of individual records of dormouse sightings and/or evidence for Wales mostly between 1988 and 2014, although there are a few earlier records. Records originate from a variety of sources, with the collation held and maintained by People\u0027s Trust for Endangered Species (PTES). It is an incomplete directory of sites due to the ad-hoc nature of data collection. Some sites are regularly monitored using nestboxes as part of the National Dormouse Monitoring Programme (NDMP) and these sites are indicated in the attributes and more detailed biometric data are held by PTES who administer the monitoring programme.   Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds a copy of the GIS dataset on their internal GIS data store - however, requestors are advised to obtain the most up-to-date version of the data from PTES. This dataset is also held under the National Dormouse Database (NDD) available to download from the NBN Gateway.\tVarious methods of data capture are used in this dataset. The record type along with the reliability of the data (good, fair or poor) are detailed in the RecordType and RecordTypeReliability attributes. The majority of data are from either Great Nut Hunts. Additional ad-hoc records are collected by PTES via the online recording form at http://surveydata.ptes.org/dormousemonitoring but these are only included in the NDD if they can be verified by a photograph, nut or detailed description by an experienced monitor.\tThe species data is also available to download on the NBN Gateway through the PTES National Dormouse Database (NDD).\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Dormouse_Core_Areas.lyr\tCustodians and metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team Also contact the PTES for the most up to date version of the dataset.\tPeoples Trust for Endangered Species (PTES)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:15.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18011\tNRW_DS114979\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.865Z\t352\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDormouse Outer Areas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313134393739\tThis is a spatial dataset held by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) which holds records from the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) dormouse data sourced from areas of work, such as licence returns and dormouse survey work and also The Vincent Wildlife Trust (VWT) dormouse survey data. This dataset is a collation of dormouse records for Wales from the People\u0027s Trust for Endangered Species (PTES) National Dormouse Database, with a spatial resolution of a 2 km buffer zone. Map has been used as basis for Glastir Targeted Element - core (or mandatory) sites. Majority of records collected by experienced surveyors, or verified by experienced surveyors (e.g. nut hunt records). Licence returns have not been independently verified.  Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds a copy of the GIS dataset on their internal GIS data store - however, requestors are advised to obtain the most up-to-date version of the data from PTES. This dataset is also held under the National Dormouse Database (NDD) available to download from the NBN Gateway.\tVarious methods of data capture are used in this dataset. The record type along with the reliability of the data (good, fair or poor) are detailed in the RecordType and RecordTypeReliability attributes. The majority of data are from either Great Nut Hunts. Additional ad-hoc records are collected by PTES via the online recording form at http://surveydata.ptes.org/dormousemonitoring but these are only included in the NDD if they can be verified by a photograph, nut or detailed description by an experienced monitor.\tThe species data is also available to download on the NBN Gateway through the PTES National Dormouse Database (NDD).\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Dormouse_Outer_Areas.lyr\tMetadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Team  Custodian: GIS and Spatial Team / PTES\tPeoples Trust for Endangered Species (PTES)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:08.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18030\tNRW_DS115034\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.712Z\t440\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCoastal Vegetated Shingle Structures of Wales\tStrwythurau Cerrig Man gyda llystyfiant yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303334\tA survey of Coastal Vegetated Shingle Ridges of Great Britain was commissioned in 1987 by the Nature Conservancy Council.  The project provides a classification of the main shingle plant communities found on stable or semi-stable structures in Great Britain and was published by Joint Nature Conservation Council (JNCC) in four parts.  The data was captured in an attempt to describe the size, location and quality of the Vegetated Shingle Ridges.  The surveys focused on the vegetation of stony banks and did not attempt to map strandline communities; therefore the site boundary is not a true representation of the extent of the Shingle Ridge.  An overall report which combines all data to determine which plant communities are found on shingle at a national level and how these related to existing National Vegetation Classification (NVC) communities, a map and a description of the major plant communities recorded at each of the sites.\tThe first stage of the project identified sites comprising of shingle structures and beaches supported a permanent flora above the strandline.  Each site was surveyed within a framework of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) using techniques recommended for that methodology.  Maps were based on either 1:10,000 maps of the sites or aerial photography if available.  These field maps were used in conjunction with TWINSPAN analysis on plant communities to create specific shingle ridge communities which were mapped onto the final map in detail.  JNCC publish electronic versions of the report on their website.  They also provide a cross tabulation spreadsheet between the Coastal Vegetated Shingle communities defined by Sneddon and Randall (1994) and the NVC Classification. In 2012 Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) created vector GIS layers of the Welsh shingle habitat maps. These form 3 appendices, one for each Wales, England and Scotland.  The data described here only relates to Appendix 1 - Wales.\tSneddon, P., \u0026amp; Randall, R.E. 1993. Coastal Vegetated shingle structures of Great Britain: main report. Peterborough, Joint Nature Conservation Committee. (http://jncc.defra.gov.uk/page-2509)\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1348 Archived as Project 363 and media 1348.   Archived on the Z drive  (not available on x drive) Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_ M_DRIVE:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\VegetatedShingle\\\tCustodians Coastal Scientist, NRW. Marine and Freshwater Data Manager, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1993-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:56:58.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18026\tNRW_DS115035\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.608Z\t412\tUnassessed\tEditable\tVegetated Shingle Habitats\tCynefinoedd Graean a Llystyfiant\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303335\tThis is a spatial dataset showing the boundary of shingle habitats and the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) equivalent code.  A GIS layer was generated in 2012 from paper maps from the 1993 JNCC report by Sneddon and Randall.  The purpose of this data capture is to generate an electronic version of the data and make it easily available for reporting purposes.\tMapping was conducted in Wales between May and June 1989.  Digitisation of the data was carried out between June and August 2012. The paper maps were scanned and geo-referenced to OS Mastermap products.  Polygons were snapped to OS Mastermap where hard boundaries exist.  Where a habitat boundary followed a feature not present on an OS base map then the scanned images was followed.  All areas were calculated from the GIS map. Habitat and site boundary maps were digitised to CCW NVC data standards.  The surveys focused on the vegetation of stony banks and did not attempt to map strandline communities; therefore the site boundary is not a true representation of the extent of the Shingle Ridge. Many of the sites have changed since the paper maps were first produced.  No attempt has been made to re-map the habitats to take account of temporal changes. All data was validated against the original map and topologically cleaned.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping  Archived as Project 363 and media 1348. (File path) K:\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1348 Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_ M_DRIVE:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\VegetatedShingle\\\tInternal Contact: Julie Creer, Lead Specialist Advisor – Coastal Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Internal Custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-08-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:27:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18013\tNRW_DS115036\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.897Z\t252\tUnassessed\tEditable\tVegetated Shingle Site Boundary\tFfin Safle Graean a Llystyfiant\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303336\tA polygon layer showing the boundary of vegetated shingle sites.  A GIS layer was generated in 2012 from paper maps from the 1993 JNCC report by Sneddon and Randall.  The purpose of this data capture is to generate an electronic version of the data and make it easily available for reporting purposes.  The surveys focused on the vegetation of stony banks and did not attempt to map strandline communities; therefore the site boundary is not a true representation of the extent of the Shingle Ridge.\tMapping was conducted in Wales between May and June 1989. Digitisation of the data was carried out between June and August 2012. A Site boundary layer was created by amalgamating all habitat polygons for each individual site. All areas were calculated from the GIS. Habitat and boundary maps were digitised to NRW National Vegetation Classification (NVC) data standards. Many of the sites have changed since the paper maps were first produced.  No attempt has been made to re-map the habitats to take account of temporal changes. All data was validated against the original map and topologically cleaned.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1348 Archived as Project 363 and media 1348.  Needs to be transferred over from Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\VegetatedShingle\\\tInternal Custodian: Monica Jones, Data Services, NRW (Contract Support Officer) Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-08-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T11:28:27.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18012\tNRW_DS115048\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.864Z\t608\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLlyn Tegid Hydroacoustic Surveys and Gwyniad Translocation 2003\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303438\tThe purpose of this project was to develop a generic Whitefish sampling protocol suitable for Llyn Tegid and produce a condition assessment of the population.  A population survey of Gwyniad was recommended to be undertaken after failed attempts to establish a refuge population in 2003. Project to develop a generic protocol for the assessment of whitefish (Coregonus albula and Coregonus lavaretus) populations developed by Scottish Natural Heritage (SNH) to the Gwyniad of Llyn Tegid during July 2003 with the addition of the determination of oxygen and temperature profiles and Secchi depths.  The protocol involved hydroacoustics and limited gill netting.  This datasets contains the results of the gill netting survey only.\tHydroacoustic survey along a discrete parallel design using a BioSonics DT6000 echo sounder.  Gill netting was undertaking using basic and pelagic versions of the Norden survey gill net.  Nets were set at 3 sites inshore together with a basic and pelagic net in the offshore areas.  Oxygen and temperature profiles were taken at one location and secchi disc readings taken at the same site.\tRivers Ecologist, NRW. Associated Report Title: Gwyniad translocation project: Phase One  - a condition assessment of the potential donor population of Llyn Tegid CCW Contract Science Report No. 597 I. Winfield., J. Flectcher., J. James (2003)\t[A]  Data is archived as Project 369, Media 1364.   [B]  Species data is available in Recorder 6 Gwyniad Gill netting survey of Llyn Tegid 2003 - CCW Science Report 597  [CCW000800000000E]\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:14.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18017\tNRW_DS115062\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.432Z\t304\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPredicting the Impacts of Objective 1 Funding on Selected Aquatic Natura 2000 Sites in Wales\tRhagfynegi effeithiau Cyllid Amcan 1 ar safleoedd Natura 2000 dyfrol dethol yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303632\tThe aim of this project was to provide Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) with a clear understanding of where developments funded by the Structural Funds under Objective 1, may potentially impact sites along the Welsh coastline with aquatic Natura 2000 designations under the EU Habitats Directive (92/43/EEC). The project has involved a number of steps including; the identification of objective one structural funding projects likely to impact on the Natura 2000 sites in Wales; the identification of Welsh aquatic maturity thousand sites, and the identification of potential impacts and sensitivities based on the available information contained within Regional Action Plans (RAPS) and Local Action Plans (LAPS) and the list of priorities, measures and projects in each plan. The Objective 1 funded projects have been analysed and grouped into the following categories; fisheries, industrial developments, infrastructure developments, tourism initiatives, farming initiatives, forestry, biodiversity and conservation initiatives, and regional development. As part of this project an interactive GIS tool has been developed which identifies the Welsh aquatic maturity thousand sites within the Objective 1 funded areas and the nature conservation features for which the sites are designated. The GIS tool can be manipulated to indicate the generic impacts resulting from the likely types of Objective 1 funded projects to the features of interest at the site.  This study develops a GIS tool that aims to identify where developments funded under the Structural Funds from Objective 1 may impact Welsh coastlines that occur within Natura 2000 sites.  Although the data was based on the best information available at the time of capture, the base maps (Special Area of Conservation (SAC) features and boundaries) are now considered out-of-date.  Newer sources of data are currently available.\tGIS layers produced that identify SAC features within sites have been continually updated at CCW and are available as SAC feature Maps.  These maps replace those described here.  Baseline GIS layers were produced and supplied by CCW, along with citation descriptions.  It was recognized that not all Natura 2000 feature maps were available at the time of the project.  A summary interactions matrix was developed.  This was then used as a tool to identify potential impacts for each Natura 2000 site.\t\tData is archived as project 359, media 84.   Data is also available on the CCW M drive at the following location M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\marine_reports\\natura_2000\tInternal Custodian:  Marine Spatial Plan Energy and Infrastructure Team, NRW Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:39:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18015\tNRW_DS115064\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.033Z\t503\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCoastal Saltmarsh Monitoring\tMonitro Morfeydd Heli ACA 2006: Dolydd heli\u0027r Iwerydd a Salicornia a phlanhigion unflwydd eraill sy\u0027n cytrefu mewn llaid\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303634\tCoastal saltmarshes in the UK comprise the upper, vegetated portions of intertidal mudflats, lying approximately between mean high water neap tides and mean high water spring tides. The characteristic vegetation consists of a limited number of halophytic (salt tolerant) species adapted to regular immersion by the tides. This project was commissioned by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) primarily to cover the condition monitoring of the Annex I saltmarsh habitats across four Welsh Special Areas of Conservation (SAC). The two habitats concerned are:   - Atlantic salt meadows (referred to as ASM) - Annexe I code 1330. Atlantic salt meadows develop when halophytic vegetation colonises soft intertidal sediments of mud and sand in areas protected from strong wave action. This vegetation forms the middle and upper reaches of saltmarshes, where tidal inundation still occurs but with decreasing frequency and duration. A wide range of community types is represented and the saltmarshes can cover large areas, especially where there has been little or no enclosure on the landward side. The vegetation varies with climate and the frequency and duration of tidal inundation. - Salicornia and other annuals colonising mud (referred to as \u0027Salicornia\u0027) - Annexe I code 1310. This pioneer saltmarsh vegetation colonises intertidal mud and sandflats in areas protected from strong wave action and is an important precursor to the development of more stable saltmarsh vegetation. It develops at the lower reaches of saltmarshes where the vegetation is frequently flooded by the tide, and can also colonise open creek sides, depressions or pans within saltmarshes, as well as disturbed areas of upper saltmarshes.\tThe areas of focus were within Special Areas of Conservation: Glannau Mon Cors Heli SAC, Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC, Pembrokeshire Marine SAC, and, Carmarthen  Bay and Estuaries SAC. Within each SAC a series of transects were established, totalling 39 transects across the four SACs. At each point along the transect presence and absence of a suite of species was used to determine the saltmarsh zone present. Additional information was recorded at each point such as presence of vehicle tracks, bare patches due to poaching damage, anoxic algal mats, Spartina anglica, grazing indicators, pollution damage, turf cutting and rare species. Targets were set for what constituted good condition on a point, transect and SAC level. For some sites estuary specific targets were also set. Surveys were conducted as follows:  - Glannau Mon 28-30 Sept 2006 - Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau 1-6 Oct 2006 - Pembrokeshire Marine 7-11 Oct 2006 - Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries 17-20 Oct 2006 Confidence in this data is High and field work carried out by Central Environment Surveys (CES) under contract to the former Countryside Council for Wales.\t\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A]  Data is archived as project 370, media 1365.   [B]  Species data is also held in Recorder 6 [LC0003850000006Q]\tBianca Bacchi, Senior Env Mon Officer, Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:58:30.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18014\tNRW_DS115065\t2024-05-28T12:37:52.018Z\t339\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Muddy Gravel, Sediments and Rocky Shore in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2008\tArolwg rhynglanw o Raean Lleidiog, Gwaddod a Glan Mor Creigiog y ACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303635\tPen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary ones. The major features of intertidal interest are Estuaries and their constituent mudflats, sandflats and reefs, Large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds, populations of the shingle dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus, piddocks in clay and lower shore boulder communities and Reefs, particularly biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata. Specific areas of interest for are the intertidal sediments and rocky areas in the Glaslyn and Dwyryd Estuaries.  There were two purposes for this data capture. The first was related to sediments. Sediment data was collected to test monitoring methodology, establish sites for long term monitoring and collect baseline and reference data. The second was related to the Data collected on the rocky shore to establish sand levels at key rocky areas - Port Meirion, Ynys Gifftan and Cei Ballast.\tIn situ recording of sediment characteristics were undertaken across the whole area of the sediment flats. This data capture included epibiota, infauna and granulometry sampling. A collection of samples from ten stations were then analysed in the laboratory for infauna and sediment granulometry.  Quadrat recording on three rocky shores was undertaken. One muddy gravel habitat site was visited at Carreg y Defaid but the tide failed to expose enough of the bank to allow a detailed survey. A species list was generated and some specimens were collected for identification.  Data quality and confidence was considered high, as all surveys were completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  The methodology for the in situ grid stations was an adaptation of Phase I survey techniques developed for an earlier survey of the Mawddach Estuary (Wyn and Cooke, 2001). Monitoring was also previously carried out in 2007. All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  Data quality and confidence considered high.\t\tData is archived a project 199, media 249.  Species data is also held in Marine Recorder and on the NBN Gateway.  A working copy of the data is stored at H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2008 ASML PLAS\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T15:59:23.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18010\tNRW_DS115072\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.792Z\t282\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria Reef in Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2008\tMonitro Rhynglanwol: Sabellaria alveolata yn ACA Bae Caerfyrddin 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303732\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata is a sedentary, tube-dwelling polychaete most commonly found on rocky substrata in the lower shore and shallow subtidal zones of marine and estuarine areas. The honeycomb reef worm is a UK BAP Priority Habitat (BAP habitats are now Habitats of Principal Importance/Priority Habitats).  In Cardigan Bay SAC specific areas of interest include the biogenic Sabellaria reef.  Sabellaria reef is considered vulnerable to coastal development, sea defence construction and heavy trampling. The purpose of this data capture was to re-survey the quadrat monitoring sites established in 2007. The descriptive transect surveys are not repeated in 2008. Data quality and confidence is considered high as all survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.\tSurveys of Sabellaria reefs in the SAC were carried out for CCW in 2004 (Boyes et al. 2008). Additional mapping of Sabellaria reef extent at Aberaeron was carried out by CMACS Ltd. (sub-contracted by Atkins Ltd.) In August 2006 as part of an Environmental Assessment for proposed Aberaeron North Beach Coastal Defence Work (unpublished data provided by Atkins Ltd.). In 2007, ASML and CCW further developed Intertidal SAC monitoring methodologies for monitoring the Sabellaria reefs and monitoring sites were established and surveyed in May 2007 (Moore 2009).The extent and measure of the quality of the Sabellaria reef was evaluated using a GPS track and quadrat surveys, respectively. Sabellaria density, physical attributes and other conspicuous species were recorded from replicate quadrat stations to provide a repeatable method of evaluation.\t\tArchived as Project No 199, Media No 548. Species data is also available in Marine Recorder and on the NBN Gateway.  A working copy of the data us stored at H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Cardigan Bay\\2008 ASML Cardigan BayData\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:00:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18008\tNRW_DS115074\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.623Z\t464\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Boulders Habitat in the Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2009\tGwaith monitro rhynglanwol ar gynefin clogfeini llanwol yn ACA Y Fenai a Bae Conwy yn 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303734\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. To do this CCW has developed a programme of feature condition monitoring.In Menai Strait \u0026amp; Conwy Bay SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide and Reefs. Specific areas of interest include areas of tide-swept bedrock and boulders and areas of tide-swept Fucus serratus on mixed sediment along the Menai Strait.  The overriding objective of the survey was to establish the reference condition for the Menai Strait \u0026amp; Conwy Bay SAC, using established monitoring stations (and establishing additional monitoring stations) to describe natural and unnatural changes in the communities. This will enable the development of conservation objectives for the appropriate management of the features of the SAC. This data was collected to build on the surveys conducted in 2005, 2007 and 2008 to further test and develop monitoring methodology and collect monitoring data.\tA survey strategy and methodology for the Menai Strait tide-swept boulder sites was developed by ASML and CCW for the 2007 survey and expanded to Felinheli for the 2008 survey. Tide-swept boulder habitats which had been studied in 2005 (both sites) and 2007 (Britannia Bridge only) were surveyed. In-situ recording of all species on a minimum of 5 boulders at each sampling stations was undertaken. Boulders were previously studied at Britannia Bridge in 2005 and 2007 and at Felinheli in 2007.\t\tData is archived as project 199, media 550.  Species data is also available in Marine Recorder and the NBN Gateway.  A copy of the working data is available at H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2009 ASML MS\u0026amp;CB\\Data\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:01:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18007\tNRW_DS115077\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.556Z\t280\tValid\tEditable\tCollection Activity and Crab Survey in the Menai Strait (2009)\tGweithgarwch casglu ac arolwg crancod yn Afon Menai 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303737\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs) are strictly protected sites designated under the EC Habitats Directive.  In the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide and Reefs. Specific areas of interest include areas of tide-swept bedrock and boulders and areas of tide-swept Fucus serratus on mixed sediment along the Menai Strait. In previous studies it was noted that a portion of the boulders had been turned by bait collectors. Surveys to assess bait collection activity and crab counts were carried out along the length of the shoreline of the Menai Strait using student volunteers. Data quality and confidence considered high.\tThe intertidal zone of each site was divided into approximately 200 m long sections and given \u0027shore block\u0027 codes. In each shore block the amount of collecting activity was observed for 10 minutes and recorded; winkle collectors and peeler collectors were noted as well as any unknown activities. The boulder turning frequency was also recorded for each shore block. In addition, the surveyors worked along the lower-mid shore evaluating the number of turned boulders; 30 boulders were checked in each shore block.   Crab counts were also recorded; juvenile Carcinus maenas, adult Carcinus maenas and juvenile Cancer pagurus.\tThe associated report for this dataset is as follows:  Moore, J., Brazier. P., Hobbs, E. 2009 Intertidal SAC monitoring Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC June 2009. CCW Marine Monitoring Report 82. Bangor.\tData is archived as project 199, media 550.  Species data is also available in Marine Recorder and the NBN Gateway.   A copy of the working data is available at H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2009 ASML MS\u0026amp;CB\\Data\tData Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:53:10.222Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18009\tNRW_DS115081\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.624Z\t580\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Biotope SLR.FserX.T in the Menai Strait and Colwyn Bay Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) 2009\tMonitro Rhynglanwol: Y Biotop SLR.FserX.T yn ACA Y Fenai a Bae Colwyn 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135303831\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  In the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features are mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide, and reefs. Specific areas of interest include areas of tide-swept bedrock and boulders and areas of tide-swept Fucus serratus on mixed sediment along the Menai Strait. The purpose of this in-situ survey was to locate specific tide-swept Fucus serratus zones and measure the width of the zone, percentage of Fucus serratus within the zone and information on epibionts. This in turn provided a baseline for future condition monitoring and helped establish a methodology for intertidal surveying.\tTide-swept Fucus serratus (FserX.T) biotope was surveyed at two locations. A methodology was developed for the survey, focussing on the epibiota present on the Fucus serratus thalli using random quadrat recording.\t\tData is archived as project 199 and media 552.  Species data is also available in Marine Recorder.  A working copy of the data is available at  :\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2009 ASML MS\u0026amp;CB\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:02:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18006\tNRW_DS115123\t2024-05-28T12:37:51.421Z\t258\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveys of Potential Oystercatcher Prey, Pembrey Sands (2009)\tArolygon o ysglyfaeth posibl piod mor, Traeth Pen-bre, 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313233\tThe Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) supports nationally and internationally important populations of wildfowl and waders. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) are studying the food requirements of oystercatcher and knot, to develop monitoring targets and to assess the implications of shellfish fisheries on the bird populations. As part of that study, surveys of the cockle and mussel stocks in the Burry Inlet have been carried out every autumn since 2004, using a standard methodology. Significant populations of oystercatcher also feed in the Three Rivers and along Pembrey Sands and it is known that they are part of the same population that feed in the Burry Inlet SPA, but the information is limited. Data from the British Trust for Ornithology (BTO) on oystercatcher distribution along the Pembrey coast showed that they concentrate in the central section of the Sands and confirmed that they are feeding at low tide. It is known from previous survey work that the sediment fauna found along the Pembrey coast are likely to include a variety of shellfish (but not cockles and mussels), however data is limited. The purpose of this data capture was to provide data on the abundance and distribution of the potential prey species for the oystercatcher along the Pembrey Sands due to limited data availability. This data aimed to determine the potential prey of the oystercatcher in Pembrey Sands, examining the abundance and distribution of the sediment fauna.  Previous findings showed that high numbers of the main prey species of the oyster catchers (cockles and mussels) were not found in the area.\tThe methodology for Pembrey Sands was adapted from that used for the cockle surveys in the Burry Inlet. A total of 97 stations with 250 m spacing were sampled from a grid of 161 stations, using a 0.1m2 quadrat / 2 mm mesh sieve. All sampled stations extended from the mid tide level to below the likely low tide level in Pembrey Sands. Bivalves that were retained in the sieve were identified, counted and their shell lengths were measured with vernier callipers. Additionally counts of polychaetes \u0026gt;2 cm body length were recorded, and tube dwelling and tubeless polychaetes were distinguished. Observations made by four surveyors (experience unknown). The methodology used has been applied to surveys in the Bury Inlet since 2004 and is astandard methodology (Moore, 2009).   Further information can be found in the CCW report \u0027Surveys of potential oystercatcher prey Pembrey Sands 2009\u0027.\t\tData is archived as Project 371, media 1368\tInternal Custodian: Matty Murphy, Maritime Ornithologist, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-10-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:03:48.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18048\tNRW_DS115145\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.442Z\t489\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSeasearch Diver and Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Snorkel Seagrass Zostera Marina Surveys Rhoscolyn 2012\tGwelau gwellt y gamlas Rhoscolyn 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313435\tSeagrass is the only marine flowering plant found subtidally in the UK and seagrass beds are considered of National Importance. In 2012 Seasearch volunteers undertook a one day survey to verify the presence of seagrass beds at Rhoscolyn, Anglesey, North Wales.  Following the successful Seasearch search for seagrass, CCW undertook a snorkel survey to establish the size of the larger bed to the west of the bay. 1. Confirm the presence of seagrass beds at Rhoscolyn. 2. Note estimates of density and depth of live seagrass in different parts of the bed 3. Make note of any unusual features of the seagrass bed\tData is captured by using Seasearch volunteer divers. Countryside Council for Wales staff followed up the initial Seasearch survey with a snorkel visit and using GPS to map the western bed May 2012.  Prior to 2002 there were reports of seagrass washed up at Borthwen Bay near Rhoscolyn.  In 2003 Seasearch attempted to locate the bed, occasional shoots were recorded.  An annual Seasearch skills refresher day was carried out on April 14th, 2012, to confirm the presence and location of a Borthwen seagrass bed and associated species.\tA mini-report was written to summarise the findings:  \"Greenhalgh and Morris (2012). Seagrass beds of Borthwen Bay Rhoscolyn Anglesey 2012. Seasearch Report\". Available online at Seasearch website.\tData is archived as project 375, media 1385 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400\\Media 1385) A working copy is stored at Z:\\Archive\\CCW_H_Drive\\Science\\MFSG\\Users\\Ramsay_K\\rhoscolyn seagrass\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tMarine Conservation Society (MCS)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-09-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:53:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18047\tNRW_DS115165\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.345Z\t426\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Habitat Vulnerability Assessment of the Welsh Coast - Phase 1\tAsesu bregusrwydd cynefinoedd ar hyd Arfordir Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313635\tThe coastline of Wales supports a wide variety of important marine ecological systems, habitats and species. A range of human activities, such as coastal developments and defences can result in the loss of these important coastal and marine habitats. These human activities coupled with future climate change, in particular sea level rise, could have significant impacts upon the natural coastal and marine environments.  Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) require a way to model the vulnerability of coastal habitats to these impacts.  The following metadata describes phase 1 of this project. The purpose of this data capture was to produce a marine habitat vulnerability assessment of the Welsh Coast to consider those habitats/biotopes that are vulnerable to coastal squeeze as a result of coastal management and other natural constraints for the coast of Wales.\tDatasets used in the modelling process include CCW habitat maps (Phase 1 Intertidal and Habmap), previous sensitivity assessments carried out by MarLIN, and details of those habitats listed in Section 42. Other datasets used include Shoreline Management Plant (SMP) Policy Units, information about erosion and sea level rise rates, functional behaviour and character of the coast, existing coastal management and protection amongst others.\tMetadata for each data layer is available with the archived data.\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine habitat vulnerability.lyr   Archived as project 378, media 1389 (K:\\Archive\\Media\\Media 1301-1400\\Media 1389. The database is held in Z:\\Archive\\CCW_H_Drive\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\Ph1sites\\Intertidal Site Assessments\\Intertidal Site Assessment Contract\\ExeGesIS Products\\Assessment Database V10 The database is called: IntertidalPhase1Analysisv10.mde In this folder on the Z:\\ drive are also ArcGIS format maps covering the assessments of biotopes and units, and also summary report tables.\tPaul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:04:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18055\tNRW_DS115166\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.560Z\t578\tValid\tEditable\tHABMAP Acoustic Multibeam Echosounder Survey of Selected Areas of the Southern Irish Sea (Summer 2005)\tArolwg ecoseinydd amlbaladr acwstig o ardaloedd penodedig yn ne Mor Iwerddon ar gyfer y prosiect HABMAP, haf 2005\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313636\tThe HABMAP project was set up in response to the need for better spatial awareness of habitat distributions in the Southern Irish Sea. It was a partnership project between partners in Wales and Ireland, and part funded by INTERREG IIIa European regional development fund.   Data collection included acoustic data, sediment data and seabed imagery. These data were used to classify the seabed and feed into a predictive modelling process to predict benthic habitat.      The multibeam data was collected in five locations throughout the Southern Irish Sea by the R/V Celtic Voyager to Irish National Seabed Survey (INSS) standard. They were then processed, cleaned and classified based on acoustic classes, which were then related to seabed sediment types. Bedforms were also identified using the MBES data.   The Coordinate System used for the multibeam data was UTM Zone 30N. The xyz data was usually processed to a 5 m resolution.   The purpose of this data capture was to obtain multibeam coverage of the five study areas identified to cover a variety of different environments with different physical properties and biological communities. This would be used to link backscatter intensity to physical properties of the seabed. Data depth corrected for tide using POLPRED software which utilises one or more of Proudman Oceanographic Laboratory\u0027s hydrodyamic models, and reduced to chart datum. CARIS HIPS / SIPS software used to create surfaces and gridded datasets by experienced surveyors.\tMultibeam echosounder data acquired using Kongsberg Simrad EM1002 hull mounted on the Research Vessel (RV) Celtic Voyager. Frequency 93kHz (outer +/- 20 degrees) and 98kHz (inner +/- 50 degree swath centered at Nadir) utilising MERLIN software package. Swath ideally kept at maximum of 68 degrees to cover approximately 4.7 times water depth. Line spacing planned to give approximately 20 percent overlap.\t\tData is stored in the CCW / NRW marine data archive as Project 274, Media 1353\tInternal Custodians: - Karen Robinson, Subtidal Marine Ecologist, NRW. - Subtidal Team, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-05-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T11:59:29.386Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18051\tNRW_DS115168\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.536Z\t600\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey in the River Usk (2011)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313638\tRiver lamprey, Brook lamprey and Sea lamprey are listed in Annex II of the EU Habitats Directive (EU Directive on the Conservation of Natural Habitats of Wild Fauna and Flora 92/43/EEC). There are six rivers in Wales designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) for lamprey, including the River Usk in South Wales. The Countryside Council for Wales and now Natural Resources Wales is responsible for monitoring the condition of lamprey species populations within SACs and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within designated sites.  The main aim of this project is to undertake a condition assessment for the population attribute only for brook, river and sea lamprey designated features for the River Usk SAC for the second Habitats Directive monitoring reporting cycle (2007-2012).  A total of 35 sites distributed within the Usk catchment.  Only a few sites had optimal habitats and sub-optimal habitats were dominant throughout.  Sampling was carried out between the 10th October and 16th November 2011.  The data is used to assess the population attribute for all three lamprey species. The results of the four attributes outlined in the Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) Guidance for Freshwater Fauna give an indication of the overall condition assessment.\tWork previously undertaken in 2004/2005.  Field work was conducted by Environment Agency staff using standard electrofishing methodologies.  A condition assessment is made using the JNCC \u0027Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) Guidance for Freshwater Fauna\u0027.  The sampling strategy for the monitoring programme for sea, river and brook lamprey followed the protocols developed for the LIFE in UK Rivers project (Harvey \u0026amp; Cowx, 2003). Quantative quadrat surveys were carried out in both optimal (very silty) and sub-optimal (more rocky) habitats.  Electric fishing techniques were used inside a 1m2 quadrat to draw lamprey out of the silt rather than stunning and trapping them within the silt.  Sampling of optimal habitats quadrats was done thee times whilst sub-optimal habitats were sampled once. The original data contains positive and negative samples.\tRivers Ecologist, NRW Freshwater Monitoring Co-Coordinator, NRW Associated Report (Ffynnon DCT-12-523868) River Usk Population Attribute Condition Assessment for Brook, River and Sea Lamprey 2011. CCW Staff Science Report No. 11/8/6 CCW. Bangor.\t[A]  Data is archived as project 357, media 1336.   [B]  Species data is held within Recorder 6 as Lamprey Monitoring, River Usk 2011 - CCW – Science Report No. 11/8/6 CCW000800000000M.\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:51:09.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18053\tNRW_DS115169\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.565Z\t406\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBottlenose Dolphin and Harbour Porpoise Monitoring in Cardigan Bay and Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation (SAC)\tMonitro Dolffiniaid Trwyn Potel a Llamidyddion yn Ardal Cadwraeth Arbennig Bae Ceredigion ac Ardal Cadwraeth Arbennig Pen Llyn ar Sarnau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313639\tA population of bottlenose dolphins forms a primary interest of Cardigan Bay and it was for this that Cardigan Bay was first selected as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Cardigan Bay is known to host the largest population of semi-resident bottlenose dolphins in the UK. The data collected as part of this research is being used to monitor the population dynamics and behaviour of bottlenose dolphins and harbour porpoises in Cardigan Bay.  Cardigan Bay is known to host the largest population of semi-resident bottlenose dolphins in the UK. This data covers monitoring carried out by the Sea Watch Foundation between 2011-2013 on behalf of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and subsequently Natural Resources Wales (NRW), to monitor bottlenose dolphin and harbour porpoise populations in Cardigan Bay.  The goal of this data collection was to monitor bottlenose dolphin and harbour porpoise populations in Cardigan Bay. The aims were to: - Provide information on the condition of bottlenose dolphins and harbour porpoises in Cardigan Bay including Cardigan Bay and Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation;   - To use photo-identification techniques to evaluate dolphin movements distribution and abundance; - To assess supporting habitats.\tLine-transect surveys in Cardigan Bay took place between July and October 2011 and between April and October in 2012 and 2013, with coverage being particularly good in 2013. A total of 83 line-transect surveys were conducted in Cardigan Bay throughout the study period, amounting to over 10,000 km of effort travelled in favourable conditions (sea states less than 3 Beaufort, low swell, and no rain).  In Cardigan Bay SAC transect lines were divided into inner and outer shore transects. Transect lines were chosen at random. Where possible two primary observers were used to scan with the naked eye and binoculars to investigate potential sightings whilst independent observers were used to view the transect line. Observations of marine mammals were also recorded. Records included a count from scanned sightings along the transect line, general counts off the transect line, casual counts when official searching had ceased and photo identification. Ad libitum surveys were also conducted within Cardigan Bay SAC collecting effort and sightings data and photos for ID purposes.  The entire coastal area from Aberaeron to Cardigan appears to be of particular significance to bottlenose dolphins, especially in the vicinity of New Quay headland, Ynys Lochtyn, Mwnt, Pen Peles and Aberporth. Other centres of activity were found in Tremadog Bay and around the reefs and sandbanks of Sarn Badrig, Sarn-y-Bwch, Sarn Cynfelyn and Patches buoy.  The confidence in this dataset is medium and observations were made by experienced staff and trained volunteers.\tThe associated report is located in Ffynnon, NRW-14-029487 Bottlenose Dolphin and Harbour Porpoise monitoring in Cardigan Bay and Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Areas of Conservation 2011-2013. Feingold., D. Evan., P. Sea Water Foundation Report No. 95\tData is archived under Project No 382  Media No 1393, 1410 (2005-2012) Subsequent data for 2013 Archived as project 441, media 1481 Data for 2014-2016 archived as project 479, media 1556\tInternal Custodian is: Marine Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:05:20.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18049\tNRW_DS115170\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.429Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSea Watch Foundation Welsh Bottlenose Dolphin Photo-Identification Catalogue (1980-onwards)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313730\tLong term studies of individuals through photo-identification (frequently termed photo - ID) of naturally marked cetaceans can reveal much needed information using a technique that is non invasive. It provides information on group structure, site fidelity, movement patterns and population size. In conjunction with other studies, long term photo ID can also enhance descriptions of life history parameters such as age at sexual maturity, calving intervals, reproductive, and total life spans. In order to provide a photo identification catalogue of individual dolphins seen in Welsh Waters to provide further information of dolphin pods in Wales.\tDolphin groups are approached to a distance of 20-50 metres, following the guidelines outlined in the photo-identification licence granted to the Sea Watch Foundation by the Countryside Council for Wales. Photos have been taken using a digital Canon camera equipped with 70-300mm, 18-200mm or 28-300mm zoom lens. Attempts are made to photograph all the individuals in the group including at least one picture of the dorsal fin of each dolphin, and preferably both sides. Photo-Identification sessions last until the dolphins are lost from view, show signs of disturbance, all the dolphins in the group are identified, or the conditions of the licence are reached. Four age categories (adult, juvenile, calf and newborn) are used, based on the size of the animal relative to an adult, the swimming pattern, the skin coloration, presence of foetal folds and proximity to the mother. Group size is determined in the field, but if the photo-identification estimate has resulted in an even larger group size, this latter evaluation is used in the analysis.  All the recognizable dolphin individuals are photographed from Welsh waters and incorporated within a photo-ID catalogue represents all the recognizable dolphin individuals. A customised Access database (designed by Roberto Gramolini) was used to create the Photo-ID Catalogue. This relational database contains tables with information on the encounters (date, time, position, behaviours displayed, group size and composition), on each identified individual (catalogue name, nickname, age, gender, method of gender determination, marking type, first sighting date, number of times sighted, areas where sighted, and relationships with other individuals) and details of the photographs (camera body and lens used, name of photographers, number of pictures taken in each encounter, and number used). The database is linked to an ArcView project from which the encounters can be easily and quickly plotted, either together, or for single individuals.\tThere are two version of the dolphin photo identification catalogue, the first from 2008 (Pesante and Evans, 2008) and the current catalogue which represents all recognizable dolphin individuals up to the end of 2011.\tArchived as project 382, media 1394. All data is held in separate media number #1410 (2005-2012) This data encompasses the catalogued report used for identification held as Media 1394, and the photo-identification catalogue itself including left and right photographs of individuals is held as Media 1410 subsequent data for 2013 Archived as project 441, media 1481 Data for 2014-2016 archived as project 479, media 1556\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW.\tSea Watch Foundation\t\t2012-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18054\tNRW_DS115176\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.483Z\t611\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Eelgrass (Zostera nolitii) Beds at Angle Bay in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) 2008\tGwaith monitro rhynglanwol ar Zostera nolitii ym Mae Angle yn ACA Sir Benfro Forol 2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313736\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. For SACs in Wales Zostera noliti is a sub-feature of the Annex II habitat, mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at high tide. Seagrass beds develop in intertidal and shallow subtidal areas on sands and muds. They may be found in marine inlets and bays but also in other areas, such as lagoons and channels, which are sheltered from significant wave action.   This data was captured to support reporting on conservation status of the Welsh Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) sub-feature eelgrass, zostera noltii.  Pembrokeshire Marine SAC was selected for eight Habitats Directive Annex I habitat types and five Annex II species. The aim of this survey was to initiate eelgrass Zostera noltii monitoring at Angle Bay in Milford Haven.\tThis work contributes on an ongoing programme of intertidal monitoring work across Wales during 2004 and 2005.  The area colonised by Zostera noltii in Angle Bay was mapped using portable GPS units. Based on the map obtained, a grid of 112 sampling stations was created, each being 50 m apart. The percentage cover of seagrass was estimated in 0.25 m2 quadrats placed each station and various other features were also recorded. These included the presence and abundance of cockles, any evidence of bait digging and other biota present. There is a high confidence rating for this dataset as all work undertaken by professional scientific biologist. This work contributes on an ongoing programme of intertidal monitoring work across Wales during 2004/2005.\t\tData is archived as project 199, media 546.   A working copy of the data is held at H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2008 ASML Pembs\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Spatial Data Team, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T12:00:25.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18052\tNRW_DS115180\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.475Z\t259\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSite Condition Assessment of Welsh Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) Standing Water features (2007 - 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313830\tA report and supporting data was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in 2011 to provide an assessment of the current conservation status of 23, mostly oligotrophic Welsh standing waterbodies. These lie within six different Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). The data have been used to generate site-specific condition assessments. The collection of survey data and preparation of the site condition assessments broadly follows the methods detailed in the Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) Guidance (JNCC, 2005). The lakes are assessed every 6 years.  The data contributes the the report \\Site condition assessments of Welsh SAC and SSSI standing water features 2007-2012\\\".\"\tCondition assessments have been prepared for each of the 23 lakes. The Lakes within 6 SAC\u0027s across Wales  were as follows; Eryri/Snowdonia; Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt; Rhinog; Cadair Idris; Elenydd  and Corsydd Môn SAC. Assessments follow common standards guidance (JNCC 2005) but have regard to the site-specific conservation objectives for each site. Required data including aquatic macrophyte surveys and physico-chemical data were collated and are summarised for each lake. Any issues with data confidence are discussed. Trophic scores (TRS and PLEX) have been calculated and are discussed. Reference is made to previous surveys and historic records where available and management and monitoring recommendations are provided.  The data collation is a continuation of work carried out as part of CCW Contract Science Report 705, \\Burgess et al.  (2006) Site Condition Assessments of Welsh SAC and SSSI Standing Water Features\\\". The data for the current assessments may not be perfect but it is the best that has been available for lake monitoring in Wales to-date. The dataset is larger more extensive and generally of higher quality than that available for previous assessment rounds. Furthermore the QA processes involved and use of best practice where possible increases the robustness of the dataset which in turn increases confidence in the status outcomes of the current round of condition assessments.\"\t\tData is archived as project 385, media 1399.\tInternal Custodian: Dr Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Freshwater team leader, CCW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW)\t\t2013-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18043\tNRW_DS115186\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.933Z\t320\tUnassessed\tEditable\tReporting of Marine Protected Areas (MPAs) Contribution to the UK Network\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313836\tSection 124 of the Marine and Coastal Access Act (MCA Act) requires Welsh Government to report on the extent to which they have achieved the objectives in the Act to use conservation sites to contribute to a UK network of Marine Protected Areas (MPAs).  Initial reporting takes place in 2012 and subsequently every 6 years.   The purpose of this data capture was to provide supporting information to the Welsh Government Marine Protected Areas of Wales in both the offshore and inshore marine environment. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) have provided a report and supporting information to WG which provides an assessment of the representative and replication of the suite of Welsh MPAs, as well as a discussion of management and condition evidence to date.  A copy of the data is archived at CCW as a record of the evidence provided for the initial reporting.\tThis was a data collation exercise - please refer to the report for further details of the source data.  Any data layers collated are accompanied by data processing notes. The CCW report is published under the following name \"Welsh Marine Protected Areas: Contribution to the UK Network.  CCW report to Welsh Government to support Government\u0027s Marine Protected Area reporting duty under section 124 of the Marine and Coastal Access Act (2009). November 2012\".\t\tAssociated data is held in the Marine Archive as project 383, media 1395\tInternal Custodian:  Deputy Group Head/Team Leader Marine Ecosystems, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18050\tNRW_DS115188\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.475Z\t296\tValid\tEditable\tInvestigation into Musculus discors Beds in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau (2011)\tYmchwiliad i welau Musculus discors ym Mhen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau 2011\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135313838\tThis survey was carried out as part of a MSc thesis in September 2011. To provide baseline data on M. discors beds around the LlPeninsula, with four specific aims: Establish the population structure for sample beds from around the LlPeninsula in order to investigate population structure and determine bed longevity. Identify the in-fauna/epi-fauna associated with M. discors beds. Investigate the sedimentation that occurs upon M. discors beds. Provide advice to CCW on whether M. discors should be included within a Local Biodiversity Action Plan for Gwynedd.   To determine M. discors population structure and influence on assemblage structure, twenty six, ten centimetre (cm) diameter cores of M. discors, associated fauna and sediment were collected by Seasearch volunteers on SCUBA from four randomly chosen beds in North Wales.were rinsed through a 63 micrometre (sieve with the sediment, M. discors and in-fauna retained. All liquid was retained for later filtration. M. discors were sorted from the sample and length measured to the nearest 0.1 millimetre (mm) using Vernier callipers with epifauna identified and enumerated (Rees et al., 2008). If present, new recruits were sorted from the sample and measured using a calibrated microscope slide (to the nearest 0.1mm).   In cases where there were high numbers of recruits, sub-sampling occurred with the abundance and size of a quarter of the total recruit population determined. All associated fauna were identified to the lowest taxonomic level possible.\tFour sites sampled by diver operated core. A total of 26 cores (10cm diameter) taken. Samples processed in laboratory - sieved over 63 micron mesh. All fauna retained and then identified and counted. Musculus discors lengths measured.  Confidence in this dataset is moderate as an inexperienced surveyor was used with assistance from experienced surveyors.\t\tData is archived a project 372, media 1369  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1369  Species data is available in NRW Marine Recorder [MRCCW31600000008].\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tHopkinson\tOpen Government Licence\t2011-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T09:05:44.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18044\tNRW_DS115216\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.953Z\t450\tValid\tEditable\tAerial Monitoring of Common Scoter Melinitta nigra in Carmarthen Bay 2010 - 2011\tArolwg o\u0027r awyr o AGA Bae Caerfyrddin 2010/11\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323136\tCarmarthen Bay Special Protection Area (SPA) was classified as the UK\u0027s first SPA in 2003 on the basis of holding at least 1% (16,000 individuals: Wetlands International 2006) of the biogeographic population of common scoter Melanitta nigra. Common Scoter feed on bivalves and other invertebrates such as polychaetes and amphipods which are found in abundance in the shallow waters (less than 10 m) in Carmarthen Bay.   Common scoter are also listed as a UK BAP species. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) has supported ground based counts since 1996, following the Sea Empress oil spill. The purpose of this data capture was to collect information regarding the abundance and distribution of common scoters in the Carmarthen Bay SPA.\tA sustained program of monitoring scoters in Carmarthen bay has been in place since 1997 (Stewart et al, 1997, Cranswick et al, 1998, WWT 2003). Data was captured using a visual aerial survey of the Carmarthen Bay SPA on the 6th March 2011, counts were obtained by two surveyors concurrently. The survey used distance sampling technique (assigning birds or rafts of birds to distance bands A-D) with 15 line transects spaced at 2 km intervals. Data was also collected on additional bird and marine mammal species. All staff involved were trained, and confidence levels were provided as part of the report.\t\tData is archived under Project No 301, media 1404\tInternal Custodian:  Maritime Ornithologist, Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-14T12:02:17.496Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18046\tNRW_DS115217\t2024-05-28T12:38:19.266Z\t278\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLlyn Tegid and Llyn Arenig Fawr hydroacoustic and Gwyniad surveys 2012\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323137\tThe gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) population of Llyn Tegid has been considered for a number of years to be threatened by deteriorating environmental conditions, especially those associated with eutrophication. This dataset contains the results of a project investigating the environmental variables. Attemps to translocate a population to Llyn Arenig Fawr have been attempted since 2003. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the status of gwyniad and its habitat in Llyn Tegid (native population) and Llyn Arenig Fawr (introduced population).\tAt Llyn Tegid hydroacoustic surveys and the measurement of associated environmental variables (temperature and oxygen profiles, and secchi disk reading) were conducted. Utilised standardised survey and monitoring protocol for the assessment of whitefish (Bean, 2003, JNCC, 2005) with two minor deviations- rather than using the target strength to fish length relationship of Foote (1987) the relationship given by Love (1971) was used to allow wider comparison. Secondly a vessel speed of 2.0ms-1 was adopted as general target mean speed rather then an absolute upper limit. The above data collection was also undertaken at  Llyn Arenig Fawr including a standardised gill-net survey. This forms part of the continuation of Llyn Tegid Hydroacoustic Surveys and Gwyniad Translocations 2004-2009 Project.\tTeam Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, NRW Ffynnon Reference Number: DCT-13-068971 /  DCT-13-068914   Llyn Tegid Hydroacoustic Survey 2012 I. Winfield., J. Fletcher., and J. James., CCW Contract Science Report No. 1012 Ffynnon Reference Number: DCT-13-068851 /  DCT-13-069046   Llyn Arenig Fawr Gwyniad Survey 2012 Winfield., J. Fletcher., and J. James., CCW Contract Science Report No. 1013\t[A]  Data is archived as project 389, media 1405.  [B]  Species data is held in Recorder 6: - Gwyniad Hydroaccoustic Survey of Llyn Tegid 2012 - CCW Science Report 1012  CCW000800000000G  - Gwyniad Hydroacoustic and Gill Netting Survey of Llyn Arenig Fawr 2012- CCW Science Report 1013 CCW000800000000I.\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-08-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18045\tNRW_DS115243\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.979Z\t292\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMilford Haven Waterway: Benthic and Intertidal Sediment Macrofauna Data Collation 2006\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323433\tThe Milford Haven Waterway is arguably the most intensively studied coastal region of the UK with respect to the soft sediment macrobenthos. These studies have largely been motivated by concern for the environmental effects of the oil industry, since the diversity and composition of the macrobenthos have become one of the mainstays of marine biological effects monitoring. The purpose of this data capture was to: -  Evaluate the existing macrobenthic and environmental data.  -  Undertake new statistical analyses of the data and to interpret the findings of these analyses.  -  Make recommendations for a meaningful and cost effective monitoring programme that might best be employed for regular wide-scale evaluation of biological quality in relation to a range of anthropogenic impacts.\tExisting data obtained from individual surveys in the Milford Haven Waterway were evaluated paying particular attention to survey design and sampling methodology, matching of environmental and macrobenthic data in terms of locations and sampling times, taxonomic quality, presentation and storage of data and data analysis and interpretation. Available data on the macrobenthic assemblages of the Milford Haven Waterway were re-analysed using the most up to date techniques such as univariate methods, distributional techniques and multivariate methods. No spatial reference were provided with data for statistical analysis. Spatial reference would have to be acquired from source data/reports. It is assumed for the purpose of metadata for this collation that samples were collected using Latitude and Longitude (WGS84).\t\tData is archived as project 391, media 1407 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1407)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tMilford Haven Waterway Environmental Surveillance Group [MHWESG]\t\t2006-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18042\tNRW_DS115254\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.893Z\t828\tValid\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) / Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Sidescan Data Collation and Archiving Project\tProsiect archifo a chasglu data \u0027sidescan\u0027 yn y Cyngor Cefn Gwlad/Ysgol Gwyddorau Eigion Prifysgol Bangor\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323534\tAcoustic survey data for Welsh waters is collected by a variety of different organisations including Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the Bangor University School of Ocean Sciences (SOS).   The survey location encompasses the Irish Sea, including Liverpool Bay, Isle of Man and Northern Irish coast.   The majority of data collected from January 1998 onwards is stored in disk format at SOS, and requires a specialised disk reader to be used. In order to make these data more accessible to SOS, CCW and subsequently Natural Resources Wales (NRW), this project was carried out in two parts. The first part translated all the data into a new digital format and also produced GeoTiff images in both low and high resolution. The second part catalogued all the data available in a spreadsheet and mapped it spatially into a GIS based spatial index for easy visualisation and query. The data is now more readily available for use on projects such as for marine monitoring work or habitat mapping. This will give staff an easy to use spatial index of data available to them, as well as additional information such as linked student dissertations or other projects.  Please see the limitations on public access and use constraints section for further information on this dataset.\tRaw sidescan data in CM800 format converted into CM2 format using a data converter, then into Q\u0027mips (.dat) format using MaxView software. Some manual adjustment of brightness and smoothing of navigation data was required as and when necessary in order to result in a suitable output. Geotiff files were then created using the Octopus 461 sidescan processing toolkit. These were then opened in MapInfo GIS and digitised around the boundaries of the data area to produce a polygon layer of data coverage. Additional notes were made from personal communication with Dr Jim Bennell, and from dissertation projects stored at SOS.    Source data collected by CCW and Bangor University School Of Ocean Sciences. The data was collated and indexed between May 2010 and January 2012. All data has been collected by experienced sidescan operators and is accompanied by a navigation data file for spatial referencing. Layback has been applied where the information exists for greater accuracy. Data where problems occur have been noted in the accompanying spreadsheet.\t\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Hydrographic\\Marine_Acoustic_data\\sidescan_data M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Hydrographic\\Marine_Acoustic_data\\sidescan_data\\Sidescan_Index.tab Archived as Project 392, Media 1408.\tGIS Service Desk / Dave Tavner - Marine Mapping Scientist, Evidence Analysis Subtidal Team Leader, MFEG, NRW External Custodian:  Jim Burnell, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-University of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences\tNRW Conditional\t2013-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T17:45:31.939Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18041\tNRW_DS115268\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.870Z\t280\tValid\tEditable\tDistribution of Fisheries Habitats and Fishing Activity Project - Revised Fishing Atlas Activity Layers (Task 2)\tProsiect Dosbarthiad Cynefinoedd Pysgodfeydd a Gweithgareddau Pysgota - haenau gweithgareddau\u0027r Atlas Pysgota (Tasg 2)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323638\tThe objective of the associated project was to summarise how the fisheries habitats developed during the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) habitat sensitivity project intersected with Special Area of Conservation (SACs), SAC features and fishing activity. This sets the fisheries sensitivity work in the context of the Habitats Directive.   As part of the project, the Fishing Atlas activity layers were processed by the toolkit to remove areas where the activity was unlikely to occur due to the habitats present.  The outputs were to be used by CCW to determine whether sensitive habitats were subject to fishing activity.\tFishing activity layers from the Fishing Atlas were processed against intertidal Phase 1 habitat data and HABMAP subtidal habitat data using the Fisheries Habitat toolkit. A lookup table based upon the sensitivity matrices was used to flag and delete habitats over which the activity was unlikely to occur.  The data was created using an automated process to ensure processing was identical for all feature types.   The data was based upon Fishing Atlas activity layers, intertidal phase 1 habitat data and HABMAP subtidal habitat data. Some of this data may be out of date therefore datasets are indicative rather than definitive.\t\tAssociated data is held within the Marine Archive as; Project 376, Media 1396\tData Custodian - Senior Fisheries Advisor, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2012-12-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T10:15:35.307Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18039\tNRW_DS115270\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.776Z\t351\tValid\tEditable\tDistribution of Fisheries Habitats and Fishing Activity Project - Output Layers by Gear Category (Task 4)\tProsiect Dosbarthiad Cynefinoedd Pysgodfeydd a Gweithgareddau Pysgota - haenau data fesul categori offer (Tasg 4)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323730\tThe objective of the associated project was to summarise how the fisheries habitats developed during the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) habitat sensitivity project intersected with Special Area of Conservation (SACs), SAC features and fishing activity. This sets the fisheries sensitivity work in the context of the Habitats Directive.   The outputs for Task 4 included one layer per gear category for each SAC feature, area outside SAC features and 12 nm zone outside SACs over which the activity occurred. The outputs were to be used by CCW to determine whether sensitive habitats were subject to fishing activity and whether these situations occurred within SACs and SAC features. The data could also be used as a baseline for discussions with fishermen.      As of April 1st, 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tSAC feature layers, intertidal Phase 1 habitat data, HABMAP subtidal habitat data and fishing activity layers from the Fishing Atlas were processed using the Fisheries Habitat toolkit to create output layers for each gear category and feature type showing which gear types and habitat types occur within each category and their spatial extent.    The data was created using an automated process to ensure processing was identical for all feature types. The data was based upon Fishing Atlas activity layers, intertidal phase 1 habitat data, HABMAP subtidal habitat data and SAC feature boundaries.   Some of this data may be out of date therefore datasets are indicative rather than definitive.\t\tAssociated data is held in the Marine Archive as Project 376, Media 1396.\tData Custodian: Senior fisheries Advisor, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2012-12-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T10:21:51.058Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18040\tNRW_DS115271\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.793Z\t715\tValid\tEditable\tDistribution of Fisheries Habitats and Fishing Activity Project Outputs\tData prosiect Dosbarthiad Cynefinoedd Pysgodfeydd a Gweithgareddau Pysgota\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323731\tThe objective of the associated project was to summarise how the fisheries habitats developed during the countryside Council for Wales (CCW) habitat sensitivity project intersected with Special Area of Conservation (SACs), SAC features and fishing activity. This sets the fisheries sensitivity work in the context of the Habitats Directive. The data used for this project was in the process of being revised therefore the aim of the project was to produce a toolkit which would allow the analysis to be repeated in future.   The outputs from this project included:  1. MapBasic scripts  2. Set of processed output layers  3. Set of Excel spreadsheet summarising the data  4. Overall report, archive appendix and technical appendix.   The outputs were to be used by CCW to determine whether sensitive habitats were subject to fishing activity and whether these situations occurred within SACs and SAC features. The data could also be used as a baseline for discussions with fishermen.  As of April 1st, 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tSAC feature layers, intertidal Phase 1 habitat data, HABMAP subtidal habitat data and fishing activity layers from the Fishing Atlas were processed using the Fisheries Habitat toolkit to create output layers for each gear category and feature type showing which gear types and habitat types occur within each category and their spatial extent. The outputs were used to create a series of Excel spreadsheets summarising the spatial data. Details of the processes used by the toolkit are given in the Technical Appendix.  The final outputs are modelled and may be re-run by CCW on a regular basis, using the tools provided. The data was created using an automated process to ensure processing was identical for all feature types. The data was based upon Fishing Atlas activity layers, intertidal phase 1 habitat data, HABMAP subtidal habitat data and SAC feature boundaries. Some of this data may be out of date therefore datasets are indicative rather than definitive.\t\tAssociated data is held in the Marine Archive at Project 376, Media 1396   Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1396\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T10:31:02.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18038\tNRW_DS115272\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.815Z\t519\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSensitive Marine Habitats Data Derived from Stereo Baited Remote Underwater Video System (2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323732\tThis dataset was collected using Baited Remote Underwater Video Systems (BRUV). This was the first known study of its kind in temperate waters and involved extensive method development. The BRUV surveys collected information on fish assemblages over three sensitive habitats (kelp, seagrass meadows and horse mussels). Surveys took places in the coastal waters off North Wales (Llyn Peninsula) and South Wales (Pembrokeshire), within Special Areas of Conservation as designated under the EU Habitats Directive. BRUVs were used as a non-destructive and non-invasive method to assess fish communities within Wales. This project was aimed as investigating the fish species assemblages present in each habitat type and determine the age structure of these.\t52 hours of footage were captured and fish assemblages were assess in three sensitive habitats; kelp (Laminaria digitata) , seagrass beds (Zostera marina, a UK BAP Priority Habitat) and horse mussels beds (Modiolus modiolus). BRUV systems were deployed for 2 and 6 hours in order to determine the optimum amount of time the BRUV needed to be deployed temperate waters. Calibrated stereo video cameras were used in order to make accurate length measurements of commercially important fish species. It took an average of 7 minutes for fish to first settle in front of the camera system and a total of 18 families including 15 different species were recorded from the three sensitive habitats. Different fish species assemblages were recorded in each of the different habitats with only poor cod (Trisopterus minutus) and dogfish (Scyliorhinadae) detected in all three habitats.  The following questions were addressed with regards to the methodological approach.  1. Can BRUVs be used to investigate key ecological parameters within fish communities in temperate waters? a. Any problems of using these systems in temperate waters compared to tropical waters? b. Do amendments need to be performed to the systems to allow for sampling around the Welsh coast? c. What is the adequate amount of time a BRUV should be deployed for?   2. Do the specialised software packages produce accurate and reliable results when identifying and measuring the length of taxa in variable visibility? a. Does the shape or bend of a fish affect the length measurement? b. Which length measurement of a taxa produces the most reliable result?   3. Can we assess fish communities in three different types of sensitive habitats? a. Are there species or abundance differences between these habitats?\t\tData is Archived under project 398 media 1415 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1415)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:00.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18037\tNRW_DS115274\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.621Z\t594\tValid\tEditable\tSediment Core Sampling of Seagrass (Zostera) Bed and Sediment Areas in Porth Dinllaen\tSamplo craidd gwaddod o wely Morwellt (zostera) ac ardaloedd gwaddodi ym Mhorth Dinllaen\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323734\tAs part of the management of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC), the SAC Officer has been working with the National Trust, local fishermen, boat owners and others to look at options to reduce the impact of moorings on the seagrass (Zostera marina) bed at Porth Dinllaen, North Wales.  This is a joint project between Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Gwynedd County Council as part of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC. As part of the Porth Dinllaen seagrass project a variety of studies are being undertaken to secure an appropriate baseline to establish a detailed picture of the current condition of the seagrass bed that can be used as a reference point to monitor recovery in the future as the project proceeds.  These were conducted to aid in the management of the seagrass bed (Zostera marina) present, listed as an important feature under Regulation 35 for the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation. This project was a component of a much larger venture which aims to better understand the local ecology and biology of Porth Dinllaen seagrass bed, with an ultimate goal of developing more seagrass friendly moorings and management techniques.   The project has been let in 4 stages: 1) The core and sediment samples and field notes were collected by MES on the 10th October  2) The cores were sent to Thomson Unicomarine for infauna and sediment analysis (By CCW)  3) The species data was sent to MES analysed in PRIMER  (Paid by GCC)  4) The analysed data was entered into Marine Recorder under the PKC data capture contract\tA total of 24 subtidal infaunal sediment cores and 8 sediment particle analysis cores have been collected from three different habitats within the area of the Porth Dinllaen seagrass bed: -mooring scar areas (3 sample locations with 3 replicate infaunal cores and 1 sediment core at each location),  -seagrass bed areas with good seagrass cover (3 sample locations with 3 replicate infaunal cores and 1 sediment core at each location) -sediment areas outside of the seagrass bed and mooring scars (2 sample locations with 3 replicate infaunal cores and 1 sediment core at each location)  In addition to the core data, diver observations of sediment composition, sediment features and characteristics, and epifauna have been collected for each sample location. The aims of these cores were to assess any significant difference between the infaunal communities and sediment profiles of these three habitat types.\t\t[A] Archived as project 386, media 1401 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1401) [B] Marine Recorder (MRCCW31700000008) NBN file and Snapshot  This project follows on from volunteer P. Dinllaen work - carried out under Across Wales Diving, Project 229, media 598, 878 (File paths: K\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 598 and K:\\Archive\\Media 801to900\\Media 878) Also additional work carried out by Gwynedd Country Council (Lucy to send details) that will be archived separately\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T10:34:16.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18036\tNRW_DS115275\t2024-05-28T12:38:18.514Z\t575\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Towed Video Seabed Surveys (2009 - 2011)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323735\tThe primary aim of the survey work and reports by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats, and primarily whether \u0027stony reef\u0027 habitat was present and then to assess the effects of scallop dredging on those habitats. The secondary aim of this data capture was to improve seabed habitat mapping for Cardigan Bay and assign biotopes. Surveys of seabed in Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Bangor University in 2009, 2010 and 2011. Work included side-scan surveys (December 2009) grab sampling of sediments for particle size analysis (December 2009 and June 2010) and drop-down video and stills camera tows (all four surveys). The drop-down imagery dataset comprises: Survey 1 (December 2009): 72 tows (4743 stills); Survey 2 (June 2010): 59 tows (4720 stills); Survey 3 (December 2010): 13 tows (1364 stills); Survey 4 (April 2011): 47 tows (5347 stills). Video tows were approximately 10 minutes in length with still images taken every 10 seconds along the length of the tow. The videos and still images from the first two surveys were analysed by SOS and subsequently converted by Coastal Assessment Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to a format suitable for entry into Marine Recorder including assignment of biotopes. The videos and still images from the last two surveys were analysed by CALM to describe habitats species and biotopes. Grab samples were taken from each site during the first two surveys but only the sediment particle size analyses data from the first survey are included in this dataset. Sidescan survey data are not included with this dataset. The four surveys described were not directly associated with any other programme and no further work is currently planned.  However it is important to note that staff in the SOS are still working on the data collected during this survey and are planning to produce publications from it.\tSurvey platform for drop-down tows and grab sampling was SOS research vessel Prince Madog. Grab sampling was carried out with a Hamond grab and sediment samples were partly processed on-board. As the main aim of the survey was to map areas of rocky reef, the on-board processing only weighed those fractions retained by 5 and 64 mm sieves. However, subsequent laboratory analysis of sub-samples provided particle size data on the finer fractions. Drop-down tows were carried out with a sledge mounted video and stills camera system (models unspecified) which was towed at a speed of approx. 0.5 knots for a period of 10 minutes. Continuous video footage was recorded on DVD and the digital stills camera took a high resolution image every 10 seconds. The field of view of the video camera covered an area of approximately 0.12m2 (width 0.41m x depth 0.30m). Each still image covered an area of 0.13m2 (0.44m x 0.30m). A manual log was kept of the position and time at the start and end of transect while the camera was on the bottom.  Video and stills were analysed by marine biological surveyors to record information on habitat character, conspicuous species and their abundance. Analysis of the images from the first two surveys, by SOS staff, calculated species abundance from the number of stills in which the species was recorded and the area of seabed photographed. Analysis of the images and video from the last two surveys, by CALM staff, estimated abundance using the SACFOR abundance scale). Habitat descriptions and assignment of biotopes from the national biotope classification to each record was later carried out by CALM. Analysis included a QA process to validate and verify the recorded data. The four surveys described were not directly associated with any other programme and no further work is currently planned. However, it is important to note that staff in the SOS are still working on the data collected during this survey, and are planning to produce publications from it.\t\tArchived as Project 334, Media 1293 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1293)  Species data held in Marine Recorder [MRCCW3160000000A, MRCCW3160000000C, MRCCW3160000000D, MRCCW3160000000E]\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences\t\t2011-04-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:01.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18085\tNRW_DS115276\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.974Z\t257\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: European Dry Heath, Northern Atlantic Wet Heath and Blanket Bog monitoring at Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SAC\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323736\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) monitoring is undertaken at the Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SAC in order to assess the condition of the three designated Annex I habitats present. Data for monitoring cycles 2006 and 2012 is available.  The purpose of this data capture is to assess the condition of European Dry Heath, Northern Atlantic Wet Heath and Blanket Bog habitats within the Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SAC.\tFieldwork was carried out in two phases. In the first phase, stands of Blanket Bog (H7130), North Atlantic Wet Heath with Erica tetralix (H4010) and European Dry Heath (H4030) within selected management units were mapped and assigned to a series of discrete condition classes. In the second phase, a series of monitoring plots were established in each of the targeted Annex I habitats. These consisted of regular rectangular grids with equally spaced sampling points. The plots serve the purpose of assessing whether the sampled vegetation currently meets condition targets in relation to a selected set of performance criteria, and also will serve to assist in long-term monitoring of changes in the habitat condition. At each sample point the broad habitat type was established and the condition of the surrounding habitat was assessed (within a 1m or 5m radius) against a series of attributes and attribute targets. Each location was captured using a GPS. This data was converted to MapInfo GIS. Thirteen sample plots were established and monitored in 2005, these were re-monitored with an additional 2 plots in 2012.\t\tAssociated data for the 2006 monitoring report is held at NRW at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\29 European Dry Heath at Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SAC  Electrinic copies: Held on exCCW Document Store - Fynnon: Word Report  DCT-12-519992 Habitat Condition Assessment of the European Dry Heath, Northern Atlantic Wet Heath and Blanket Bog within the Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SAC 2012. Excel Spread Sheet of results: DCT-12-318185 SAC Monitoring 2012 Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt Dry Heath Wet Heath Blanket Bog Results\tData Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:27:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18081\tNRW_DS115277\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.811Z\t724\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater Macrophyte Survey Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323737\tThe habitat \u0027H3260 Water Courses of Plain to Submontane Levels with the Ranunculion fluitantis and Callitricho-batrachion Vegetation\u0027 (henceforth \u0027Ranunculion habitat\u0027) is listed in Annexe I of the EU Habitat Directive as a habitat of European Importance. Four rivers in Wales, the Afon Gwyrfai, Afon Teifi, River Dee and River Wye are considered of European importance for this habitat type. A gap analysis of available data has indicated that there is a shortage of recent information on macrophyte community composition and extent in the Teifi and Upper Wye catchments. Therefore, these catchments have formed the focus of this study and data has been collected and analysed from these areas to determine whether the performance indicators for macrophyte community composition and extent for these rivers pass the required standard for extent and quality. The primary aim of this project is to produce an assessment of the status of the plant community component of the Ranunculion habitat on the Rivers Wye and Teifi. This dataset comprises of a survey entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway. Confidence in this dataset is high, all survey work was undertaken by a team of two experienced macrophyte surveyors.\tField surveys were undertaken within the recognised macrophyte survey season (June-September inclusive).  Survey was undertaken at low flows when visibility through the water column was good and timed to avoid the periods during and immediately following inclement weather conditions such as heavy rain which can affect the quality of macrophyte data collected.  Field surveys followed the LEAFPACS methodology at each survey site. One hundred meter sections of river were surveyed according to this methodology by traversing the channel in a zigzag, recording plants present in all channel habitats that are inundated for \u0026gt;50 % of the year.  Where the channel was very wide (\u0026gt;50 m) or deep (\u0026gt;1 m) and the central channel was devoid of vegetation or could not be accurately recorded due to turbidity or depth, a 5 m wide strip down either side was surveyed. Species were recorded according to the LEAFPACS species list, with voucher specimens taken where necessary to confirm identification. Each species recorded was assigned a cover score for the 100 m according to a nine point scale used in the MTR methodology.\t\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the 17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A]  Data is archived as project 401, media no. 1418.  [B]  Species and abundance data is held within Recorder 6 as Macrophyte Survey of Mid-Wales Rivers 2013- CCW Science Report 1034 No. CCW000800000000K.\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW Data came from the core survey team of Sarah Clarke, Dianne Dobson and Rob Harrison.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18078\tNRW_DS115294\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.763Z\t523\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Shad Egg Survey River Usk and River Wye (2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323934\tThe River Usk and Wye are designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the EC 92/43/EEC Habitats Directive for several features including twaite shad (Alosa fallax) and allis shad (Alosa alosa). Shads are migratory members of the Clupeidae (herring family) that ascend rivers to spawn in late spring. The populations of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and issues surrounding water quality. In the UK, significant populations of shad spawn in the Rivers Severn, Usk, Wye and Tywi.  The River Usk and Wye have been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for shads and other features including salmon, lampreys and bullhead.  The purpose of this data capture was to assist with the assessment of  Favourable Conservation  Status along the Usk and Wye to supplement the main shad spawning distribution condiftion assessment for the Usk (previous surveys conducted in 2010 and 2011) and the Wye (previous surveys were conducted in 2011). 7 sites were chosen along the length of the River Usk and 10 along the length of the River Wye. Both surveys were conducted between high river flows events, and work was carried out by qualified Countryside Council for Wales monitoring staff.\tData is collected according to JNCC published guidance on Common Standards Monitoring of Freshwater Fauns (JNCC, 2005).   The distribution of spawning shad within each river catchments are assessed using a kick sampling technique for eggs conducted in the spring.\tFreshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, NRW. Further information can be found in Countryside Council for Wales Staff Science Report 13/8/3.\t[A]  Data is archived as project 403, media 1420 [B]  Species data is held within Recorder 6 as Shad Egg Survey, River Usk and River Wye, 2012 - CCW - Report No. 13/8/3 CCW000800000000Q.\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18077\tNRW_DS115295\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.791Z\t272\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Function and Restoration of Biogenic Reefs Formed by Sabellaria alveolata\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323935\tThe honeycomb worm Sabellaria alveolata is an example of a species that forms large well-consolidated reef structures. However, as the known area of S. alveolata covers less than 0.5 % of the coastline of the United Kingdom it is considered a fairly rare species. Individuals and reefs are typically found on the low to mid-shore of the intertidal zone, but have also been found in subtidal areas. In sufficient densities, individual tubes of S. alveolata coalesce to form biogenic structures that may reach several kilometres in length. These reefs can provide an important habitat for other benthic vertebrates and invertebrates (La Porta and Nicoletti 2009), as well as offering lesser studied benefits, including sediment entrainment/consolidation and potentially reducing wave energy transfer to the top of the shore. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate the role S. alveolata has on coastal dynamics along with providing new baseline data directed towards the monitoring of the biogenic reefs.\tDetailed field observations were undertaken at three reef sites within Wales, see Geographic Coverage for site area. Each of the sites were photographed using aerial photography. Wave attenuation was assessed using high-resolution pressure sensors capable of measuring water depth at less than 5 mm accuracy in water depths of up to 15 m at a rate of four samples per second. Sensors were deployed at the three sample sites in two transects, one sensor before a reef structure and one sensor up shore, above the reef structure. Two sensors were placed at the same shore heights in an area where reef was absent to act as a control. To supplement wave exposure measurements, two further approaches were also employed. The first was plaster clod dissolution. Shore stability was assessed by observing the movement of labelled cobbles at each site. Two lines of cobbles (n \u003d 10) were lain out at identical shore heights across the on and off-reef areas. These were tagged with dGPS with a residence time of 1 minute that provides a post-processed horizontal accuracy of approximately 30 cm. Cobbles were left for several tidal cycles before being located and re-tagged with the dGPS. The distance moved was calculated using great circle distance calculations in Microsoft Excel. Vortex resuspension tanks were also used within the investigation as well as investigating larval settlement and growth through induced spawning.\t\tArchived as Project 404, Media 1421, 1425, 1426 and 1427\tGIS Service Desk Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist, NRW\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences\t\t2013-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18083\tNRW_DS115296\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.803Z\t313\tValid\tEditable\tDrop-Down Video North Wales Survey 2012\tFideo Tanddwr Arolwg Gogledd Cymru CCGC 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135323936\tDrop down video survey of seabed off the north coast of Pen Llyn and in Tremadog Bay, North Wales, carried out by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in 2012.    The dataset comprises of 26 video tows each of approximately 10 minutes in length with a set of still images that are taken approximately every 10 seconds along the length of the tow.    Videos and stills subsequently analysed by Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring, under contract to CCW, to describe habitats, species and biotopes.   The purpose of this data capture was to improve CCW\u0027s seabed habitat maps in those areas.  4 sites off the north coast of Pen Llyn, between Porth Dinllaen and Porth Colman, and 22 sites distributed around the northern half of Tremadog Bay.\tDrop down video survey was conducted from Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) survey boat Pedryn, an 11.7 m Delta cabined RIB.  The CCW camera system is C-Vision HD with a C-vision mantis HD camera video recorder, a 200 m umbilical cable, a video camera in a waterproof housing mounted on a metal frame sled with two HID lights mounted on either side of the camera.  In addition, a laser scaling device comprising two parallel laser beams, 10 cm apart, provide an indication of size and scale. The auto stills camera is a Seacam Digital still camera (18 mega pixel CMOS chip) with dual Seabeam underwater Strobe lamps.  The surface control unit overlays realtime data such as time, date, depth of camera and position of vessel on the captured video which are recorded to the miniDV recorder.  The camera was  towed for approximately ten minutes at each location, recording continuous video plus still images every 10 seconds.  The sled was towed at 0.5-1.0 knot (ideally) across the seabed either by drifting with the currents or using the drive of the vessel. A manual log was kept of the position and depth at the start and end of transect plus details of substratum type and main taxa observed on the screen during the tow.    Video and stills were subsequently analysed by a experienced marine biological surveyor to record information on habitat character, conspicuous species and their abundance (SACFOR scale), and to assign biotopes from the national biotope classification.  Analysis included a QA process to validate and verify the recorded data.\t\t[A] Associated data is held within NRW\u0027s Marine Archive as; Project 367, Media 1362 (File path is Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1362).    [B] Species data in Marine Recorder survey: MRCCW31600000009\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2012-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T10:38:09.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18071\tNRW_DS115304\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.696Z\t983\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHarvest Mice (Micromys minutus) in Wales - Where Might They Be?\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333034\tThe Harvest mouse (Micromys minutus), Britain’s smallest rodent, is sparsely distributed in Wales. The harvest mouse is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan priority species and is also on the list of Species of Principal Importance in Wales (Section 42, Natural Environment and Rural Communities Act 2006). Thus the species is afforded a high conservation status, yet very little is known about its true distribution across the UK and especially Wales.  This dataset is a collation of records obtained from a report written to question \u0027Harvest mice in Wales - where might they be?\u0027. The work carried out and demonstrated in the associated report (see related titles) was motivated by the lack of data for the harvest mouse in Wales, in an attempt to try and understand whether the species was as sparsely spread as the data would suggest. The results from analysis of the data would act to target harvest mouse survey to areas most likely to discover new populations and confirm existing ones. This dataset is made up of harvest mouse data obtained from the Welsh Local Records Centres, comprising 139 records dating back to 1895, with fewer than 30 records since 2000. Records are scattered across lowland areas in north, west and south Wales, forming obvious clusters in areas such as North Pembrokeshire, Bridgend and Eastern Glamorgan. The purpose of this data capture was to try gain an understanding of the distribution of harvest mouse around Wales. Various data were analysed alongside the harvest mouse records to highlight areas where potential harvest mouse surveys could be targeted. Maps were produced, to highlight sites with the highest habitat ranking around the most recent harvest mouse records.\tData analysis involved the selection of a range of habitats suitable for harvest mouse from Phase 1 Habitat Survey for Wales and National Vegetation Classification (NVC) data. These data were selected within a 10 kilometre radius of the most recent (post 1980) harvest mouse records. An altitudinal threshold was set to remove all harvest mouse suitable habitat above 300 metres. The resultant habitat data were associated with grassland connectivity network data, to create a series of sites which summarised the percentage area of suitable harvest mouse habitat for each site. Analysis was carried out across Wales and identified 479 core grassland connectivity sites and a further 1208 focal grassland connectivity sites, with at least some suitable harvest mouse habitat.  Each site was assigned a ranking based on the amount of combined Phase 1 and NVC harvest mouse suitable habitat.\t\tAssociated data is held at; NRW corporate GIS (M:) drive (File path Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\ )  NB This data location is too imprecise. More detail is required.\tMammal Ecologist, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18084\tNRW_DS115311\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.810Z\t563\tValid\tEditable\tMorphodynamic Modelled Outputs for the Dyfi Estuary\tCanlyniadau ar sail model morffodynamig ar gyfer Aber Afon Dyfi\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333131\tA 4-month study, conducted by Bangor University for the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), to develop a high-resolution morphodynamic model of the Dyfi Estuary and surrounding area.   The purpose of this data capture was to collect data for modelling used to investigate the response of the estuary to future tidal and atmospheric changes. The model has also been used to investigate the response of the estuarine dynamics to potential management strategies. The work leads on from a previous study which developed an initial version of the Dyfi Model (Robins 2009, hereafter denoted the 2009 Dyfi Model). It was possible to improve the model resolution due to ever-increasing computer capabilities and data storage at Bangor University. There is a need for this Dyfi Estuary study to keep track with technological advances in modelling and to improve the accuracy of the simulations, broadening the possible applications of the model to coastal processes and coastal management scenarios.    Further information can be found in CCW Contract science report 1033.\tA mathematical model of the Dyfi Estuary has been developed to reproduce the interactions of tidal and fresh water hydrodynamics and sediment transport within the estuary and surrounding low lying areas. The model is based on a TELEMAC Modelling System which calculates the temporal and spatial variations in hydrodynamics (e.g. elevations, velocities, pressure, density) and sediment transport through a finite-element model grid (an unstructured mesh of nodes connected in a triangular framework) which represents the bathymetry of the area. Input data to the model includes bathymetric data from Admiralty charts and LIDAR data, tidal prediction of sea surface heights, and river flowrate data. Simulations of present-day mean/extreme and future climatic projections have been produced. The project extends three previous modelling studies in other representative Welsh estuaries, namely the Dyfi Estuary, the Burry Inlet, and the Mawddach Estuary (see CCW Science Reports 898a, 898b and 898c).\tRobins, P.E. 2013. Development of a morphodynamic model of the Dyfi Estuary to inform future management decisions: Model update and further validation. CCW Science Report No: 1033, 50 pp., Countryside Council for Wales, Bangor.\tData is archived as project 407, 1428 (File path Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media1401to1500\\Media1428)\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Centre for Applied Marine Sciences (CAMS)\tOpen Government Licence\t2013-06-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T11:00:15.364Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18082\tNRW_DS115312\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.831Z\t569\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Afon Tywi Shad Egg Survey (2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333132\tThe Afon Tywi is designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the EC 92/43/EEC Habitats Directive for several features including twaite shad (Alosa fallax) and allis shad (Alosa alosa). Shads are migratory members of the Clupeidae (herring family) that ascend rivers to spawn in late spring. The populations of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and issues surrounding water quality. In the UK, significant populations of shad spawn in the Rivers Severn, Usk, Wye and Tywi.   Former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), Environment Agency Wales (EAW) and now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) is responsible for monitoring the condition of shad populations within SACs and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within these designated sites. The Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) published generic guidance on \u0027Common Standards Monitoring Guidance for Freshwater Fauna\u0027 (JNCC, 2005) outlining the attributes (mandatory and discretionary) and targets for monitoring freshwater fish. These include targets for shad population, river flow, water quality and river morphology.  The purpose of this data collection was to facilitate the determination of Favourable Conservation status of Shad in the Afon Tywi. Confidence in this dataset is high, as survey were undertaken by experienced staff members.\tCountryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) and Environment Agency Wales (EAW) staff monitored the spawning distribution of shads at 13 sample sites within 60 km of the main river corridor during the spawning season of 2012. The standard methodology was amended to reduce the sampling required at sites where shad eggs were present. This enabled the CSM target to be assessed and many more sites to be surveyed within the available time. The Hillman et al (2003) methodology was modified to a minimum of 10 kick samples were undertaken at each site with the number of eggs found in each kick recorded. If 10 or more shad eggs were recorded after 10 kicks the survey was terminated. However, if fewer than 10 shad eggs were recorded, kick sampling continued either until 10 eggs were found or 25 kick samples had been completed.\tFreshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, NRW. Ffynnon Reference NRW-13-019708  Garrett, H.M., Thomas, Rh. \u0026amp; Hatton-Ellis, T.W. (2012). Afon Tywi SAC shad egg survey 2012. CCW Staff Science Report No. 12/8/4. 37pp, CCW. Bangor.\t[A]  Data is archived as project 408, media 1429.    [B]  Field notes are held in the Llandeilo Office and MFEG Office.   [C]  Species data is held within Recorder 6 as Shad egg Survey Afon Tywi 2012 - CCW - Report No. 12/8/4  CCW000800000000P\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18079\tNRW_DS115314\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.804Z\t296\tValid\tEditable\tInfaunal Sampling of Sediment around the North Llyn Modiolus Bed (Additional Area) 2012\tSamplo isfilod mewn gwaddodion o amgylch Gwely Modiolus Gogledd Llyn (ardal ychwanegol) 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333134\tInfaunal and granulometery samples collected from sediment around the North Llyn modiolus beds.  Sampling was completed to inform decisions on the location of a potential fishing exclusions zone to prevent accidental incursions by fishing gear and smothering by resettlement of sediments suspended by fishing gear operating close to the bed. The work was mainly carried out to collect information about the sediment type in the area surrounding the \u0027new\u0027 Modiolus bed.  The data was also collected to inform discussions over the size of buffer area needed around the Modiolus bed for the towed fishing gears (trawls and dredges) exclusion area.\tThe samples were collected by School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Bangor University (Hilmar Hinz) from the R.V. Prince Madog using a Hamon grab.  The samples were analysed by Hebog Environmental Ltd.\t\t[A] Data is archived as project 402, media 1419.   [B] Species data held in Marine Recorder [survey number:MRCCW3000000006C]\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2013-05-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T11:02:10.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18080\tNRW_DS115332\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.796Z\t363\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Sabellaria alveolata Reefs in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2012\tMonitro Rhynglanwol ar riffiau Sabellaria alveolata yn ACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau yn 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333332\tPen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary ones, listed in Table 1.  Of these, Estuaries and their constituent mudflats, sandflats and reefs, large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds, populations of the shingle-dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus, piddocks in clay and lower shore boulder communities and Reefs, particularly biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata; are the main habitats of interest within the intertidal areas of the SAC.  Conservation objectives for these habitats are given in the Regulation 33 advice for the SAC (CCW 2009). The purpose of this data capture was to continue monitoring the intertidal area within the SAC.\tThe Llandanwg and West Afon Dwyfor Sabellaria reef transects were re-surveyed in 2012, having been surveyed by ASML in 2009 and 2010. Existing 2010 maps of Sabellaria reef in these areas were used to orientate the work area in 2012.  At both Llandanwg and West Afon Dwyfor the survey task was to re-survey the areas surveyed by quadrat in 2009 and 2010.  The Sabellaria reef in the vicinity of the quadrat sites was also mapped from the upper limit of Sabellaria on the shore, to the lower limit of access on the shore, as dictated by the height of the low tide.  The mapped areas were not as large as the 2010 areas, as the tides were not as workable in 2012.  On the shore, the GPS units were used by the survey teams to navigate to the correct, central position for each quadratting area within the \u0027quadrat zone\u0027 described in 2009, within the Sabellaria reef. At each quadrat station, surveyors established an origin at the centre of an imaginary 20 m x 20 m grid (numbers vs letters) i.e. at location 10 \u0027m\u0027 (\u003d x,y) and then the surveyors proceeded to survey the initial 5 random quadrats in detail. Estimates were made of the percentage cover of standing water, total Sabellaria, live Sabellaria and a small selection of key species/taxa.  Other typical fauna and flora (listed on the proforma) were then searched for and recorded.  No estimation of abundance was required for these species (to speed up the recording), but rapid estimates of algal cover and other major cover organisms such as Mytilus edulis were made.\tMercer, T.S. 2013. Intertidal SAC monitoring, Pen Llyn a’r Sarnau SAC July 2012. CCW Marine Monitoring Report No: 102, pp 68 + x, Aquatic Survey \u0026amp; Monitoring Ltd. Bollihope, Co. Durham.\tData is archived as project 410, media 1430 and forms part of archive Project No 199.   This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2012 ASML PLAS.   Marine Recorder Survey MRCCW16300000038\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2012-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T11:03:39.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18076\tNRW_DS115333\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.799Z\t553\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of the Mawddach Estuary Sediments in Pen Llyn ar Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2012\tMonitro Rhynglanwol ar waddodion aber Afon Dyfi yn ACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau yn 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333333\tPen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC)  is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary ones.  Of these, Estuaries and their constituent mudflats, sandflats and reefs, large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds, populations of the shingle-dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus, piddocks in clay and lower shore boulder communities and Reefs, particularly biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata are the main habitats of interest within the intertidal areas of the SAC.  Conservation objectives for these habitats are given in the Regulation 33 advice for the SAC (CCW 2009).\tFour infaunal community sampling transects were set up in 1999 which  run out into the middle of the Mawddach estuary.  These transects run across the sediment flats.  There are two originating from the southern shore and two from the north shore and originally the sediment beyond the lower saltmarsh was sampled at 25 m intervals, to the lower water mark. In order to meet the sampling requirements of the Water Framework Directive (as well as the Habitats Directive), additional infaunal samples were required to provide sufficient sampling of sand, muddy sand and mud habitats.  For this reason, some additional sampling stations were located along transects between the existing transects. At each 25 m station, the sediments were described and photographed.  Once each of the three target zones were identified, a batch of 3 x 0.01 m2 core samples and a granulometry sample were collected from the middle of the zone.  These cores were sieved in the field over a 0.5 mm mesh .All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors. Data quality and confidence considered moderate to high.\tMercer, T.S. 2013. Intertidal SAC monitoring, Pen Llyn a’r Sarnau SAC July 2012. CCW Marine Monitoring Report No: 102, pp 68 + x, Aquatic Survey \u0026amp; Monitoring Ltd. Bollihope, Co. Durham.\t1) Data is archived as project 410, media 1430 (File path Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring) and forms part of archive Project No 199. 2) Marine Recorder Survey Number (MRCCW16300000044). 3) This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2012 ASML PLAS.   4) Y:\\Marine\\Intertidal_SAC_Monitoring\\Pen Llyn SAC Monitoring.gdb\tInternal Contact: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T11:04:58.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18073\tNRW_DS115334\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.743Z\t350\tValid\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Bedrock at Porth Oer in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2012\tMonitro Rhynglanwol ar Graigwelyau ym Mhorth Oer yn ACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau yn 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333334\tPen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated for five Annex 1 habitats and four subsidiary ones. Of these, Estuaries and their constituent mudflats, sandflats and reefs, large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds, populations of the shingle-dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus, piddocks in clay and lower shore boulder communities and Reefs, particularly biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata are the main habitats of interest within the intertidal areas of the SAC.  Conservation objectives for these habitats are given in the Regulation 33 advice for the SAC (CCW 2009).\tConsistent with the aim of this survey, the rocky shore at Porth Oer was reviewed by CCW staff during the afternoon low water of the 22nd July 2012.  Finding it to be a suitable reference site for rocky shores in north Wales, they establish a set of monitoring quadrats in the upper, middle and lower shores.  Quadrat locating screws were fixed in the bedrock and re-locating photographs and notes were taken.  This established 4 x 1 m2 fixed quadrats in each of the upper, middle and lower shore zones.  The quadrats are sited in homogeneous areas of inclined rock (where possible) avoiding rockpools and large fissures.  The quadrats were permanently marked using stainless steel screws and plastic discs to locate at least two corners of the quadrat frame. All survey completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  Data quality and confidence considered moderate to high.\tMercer, T.S. 2013. Intertidal SAC monitoring, Pen Llyn a’r Sarnau SAC July 2012. CCW Marine Monitoring Report No: 102, pp 68 + x, Aquatic Survey \u0026amp; Monitoring Ltd. Bollihope, Co. Durham.\tData is archived as project 410, media 1430 and forms part of archive Project No 199. This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW headquarters. Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2012 ASML PLAS\tPaul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2012-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T11:07:04.061Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18068\tNRW_DS115378\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.672Z\t462\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring for the Afon Cleddau (2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333738\tRiver lamprey (Lampetra fluviatilis), Brook lamprey (Lampetra planeri), and Sea lamprey (Petromyzon marinus) are listed in the Annex II of EU Habitats Directive (EU Directive on the Conservation of Natural Habitats of Wild Fauna and Flora 92/43/EEC) and 6 rivers in Wales are designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) for this feature.  The River Cleddau is one of the westernmost rivers in Britain and can be broadly divided into Eastern and Western arms. Assessments of species composition, distribution, abundance and size class composition are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment. The sampling strategy for the monitoring programme for sea, river and brook lamprey followed the protocols developed for the LIFE in UK Rivers project, was aimed at undertaking a condition assessment for the population of brook, river and sea lamprey designated features for the second Habitats Directive monitoring reporting cycle (2007-2012). 12 sites in the Eastern Cleddau and 13 sites in the Western Cleddau, Pembrokeshire.\tLamprey populations and condition assessment was previously carried out by CCW in 2003 (Harvey and Cowx, 2003) and 2005 (Campbell et al., 2005).  The field survey was conducted in 2012 by Environment Agency staff using the standard electrofishing methodology.  A condition assessment is made using the JNCC Common Standards Monitoring Guidance for Freshwater Fauna. Quantitative quadrat surveys were carried out in both optimal (very silty) and sub-optimal (more rocky) habitats.  Electric fishing techniques were used inside a 1m2 quadrat to draw lamprey out of the silt rather than stunning and trapping them within the silt.  Sampling of optimal habitats quadrats was done thee times whilst sub-optimal habitats were sampled once. The original data contains positive and negative samples. Confidence in this dataset is high.\tFreshwater Monitoring Ecologist, NRW Ffynnon Reference  NRW-13-008997  Afonydd Cleddau Population Attribute Condition Assessment for Brook, River and Sea Lamprey 2012 Garrett. H., Hatton-Ellis. T., Thomas. R. CCW Staff Science Report Number 13/8/1\t[A]  Data is archived as project 415, media 1433. [B]  Species data is held within Recorder 6 as Survey Number CCW000800000000O.\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18069\tNRW_DS115379\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.629Z\t698\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAll Wales Marsh Fritillary ( Euphydryas aurinia ) Habitat Quality Master Map\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135333739\tCompiled MapInfo layer of the condition of  habitat polygons for marsh fritillaries Euphydryas aurinia. The purpose of this data capture is to ascertain the extent and condition of habitat capable of supporting populations of marsh fritillaries across Wales.\tData was collected using field surveys. Collated master map from individual surveys undertaken by CCW/NRW and Local Authorities Generally high quality collected under contract or by CCW/NRW staff, though polygons for Gower and Lleyn are likely to over-estimate the amount of habitat available.\t\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Users\\Adrian_f\\MapInfo Data\\Marsh Fritillary\\Habitat Quality Surveys\\All Wales\\Surveys\\All Wales\\All_Wales_surveys_Jun2013.TAB\tInternal Contact: Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW  ( Mike Howe ) Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team or Spatial Team.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:57:58.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18070\tNRW_DS115409\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.673Z\t755\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring of Rocky Reefs in Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2007-2010\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135343039\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II. For SACs in Wales, the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status.  In Pembrokeshire Marine SAC the relevant Annex I intertidal habitat features include Reefs. The specific area sub-feature of interest covered here are intertidal rocky reefs at six sites in Milford Haven together with one site at Nolton Haven in St Bride’s Bay and another site at Peny-Holt on the Castlemartin peninsula. The purpose of this data capture was to undertake quantitative monitor of the biota colonising sloping emersed bedrock and colonising rockpools.\tDevelopment of a survey strategy and methodology for the fixed quadrats was carried out by ASML and CCW. Field survey equipment provided by ASML and CCW included handheld GPS navigators (various makes and models, all set to British National Grid and OSGB36 datum), digital cameras (various makes and models, all set to high resolution and local time), tape measures and first aid kits. On each sampling occasion, the survey teams were led by surveyors who were very experienced in rocky shore field work and were sometimes assisted by less experienced workers who helped with logistics. At each survey site records were taken from threes shore heights; Upper, Mid and Lower shore. Within each shore zone, the four 1 m x 1 m quadrats were relocated. The quadrats are sited in homogeneous areas of inclined rock (where possible) avoiding rock pools and large fissures. Within each 1 m2 quadrat, presence/absence data were recorded for all conspicuous species using a 25 cell grid (20 cm x 20 cm cells). This gave a frequency score of between 0 and 25 for each species. Organisms were identified to species level where possible with specimens being returned to the laboratory for verification where necessary.\t\tProject 199, media 1436 Species data is held in Marine Recorder: 2007 CCW / ASML Pembrokeshire Marine SAC Rocky Reef Monitoring  MRCCW1630000000C 2008 CCW / ASML Pembrokeshire Marine SAC Rocky Reef Monitoring  MRCCW16300000013 2009 CCW / ASML Pembrokeshire Marine SAC Rocky Reef Monitoring  MRCCW16300000020 2010 CCW / ASML Pembrokeshire Marine SAC Rocky Reef Monitoring  MRCCW1630000002D  The file path for the report: H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Marine Monitoring Reports\\ No 101 - Pembrokeshire Marine 2007-2010 bedrock  The file path for the data and analyses: H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Pembs\\2010 ASML Pembs\\Data H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid Analyses\\Pemb bedrock 2005-\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:15.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18074\tNRW_DS115460\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.689Z\t397\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring Data for River Teifi 2012/2013\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135343630\tThe River Teifi has been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the EU Habitats Directive. The three species of lamprey for which the data was collected are listed under Annex II of the Habitats Directive. Data was collect to determine the habitat condition and suitability of habitats for the lamprey, as well as make recommendations for future surveys.  Data was collected to recognized standards and data presented using a GIS data. The data was collected to determine habitat conditions for lamprey in the Afon Teifi catchment. It involved collating information on age structure, distribution within the catchment, ammocoete density, spawning activity, and barriers to migration.\tLamprey was sampled using electro-fishing techniques.  Inside a 1 m2 area to draw individual lamprey out of the sediment. In total 21 optimal and 27 sub-optimal habitat sites were surveyed and the following information captured: - All Catch - Habitat Data - Length Frequency - Populations Estimates Optimal - Population Estimates Sub-Optimal   30 sites were surveys sites were chosen along Afon Teifi catchment and associated sub-catchments based on their potential as spawning locations for brook, river and sea lamprey. Initial data was collected 8-9th October 2012. Initial plans were changed due to high water flows and the remaining data collected 14th Jan-19th Feb 2013.\tData was collected using methodologies described in Harvey, J.P. and Cowx, I.G. (2003a). Monitoring the River, Brook and Sea Lamprey, Lampetra fluviatilis, L. planeri and Petromyzon marinus. Conserving Natura 2000 River Monitoring Series No. 5, English Nature, Peterborough.\tData is archived as project 422, media 1440\tInternal Custodian: Terrestrial Environmental Evidence Officer, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18067\tNRW_DS115512\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.662Z\t311\tValid\tEditable\tHistoric Aerial Imagery to Monitor Temporal Change in Intertidal Habitats\tDelweddau hanesyddol o\u0027r awyr er mwyn arolygu newid yng nghynefinoedd rhynglanwol tros amser\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353132\tLong-term and broad-scale datasets are essential to any investigation of change in natural communities. This dataset includes the collation of aerial imagery for 44 sites around the Welsh coastline, selected to contain rocky shore, saltmarsh or Sabellaria alveolata across temporal scales providing suitable data for comparisons of change through time, both anthropogenic and biological.   In order for comparisons to present day imagery, current aerial imagery was also obtained in this study. Photographic aerial images containing features of conservation value (specifically rocky shores, Sabellaria alveolata inhabited shores and saltmarsh areas) within Wales were obtained. Historic photographic aerial images that contained features of conservation value (specifically rocky shores, Sabellaria alveolata inhabited shores and saltmarsh areas) within Wales were selected from expert knowledge and Countryside Council for Wales\u0027s collection of habitat maps.    The aim of this project was to collate, digitise and georeference aerial photographic imagery for sites that had multi-year overlap. Accuracies of less than 5 m RMS was obtained (with the exception of Treath Lafan, 1995, and the Dyfi Estuary, 1975). At these saltmarsh locations, there were too few ground control points to achieve this accuracy.\tRocky shores sites included Bull Bay, Cemlyn Bay, Cemaes Bay, Holyhead, Penrhyn (East side of Holyhead bay) and Trearddur Bay. Sabellaria sites included Aberaeron, Mochras and Llanddulas and S. alveolata.    Those images remaining as photographic prints were digitised using an Epson GT-15000 scanner at 1200 dots per inch (dpi). Images in adjacent locations were mosaicked with the image panorama generating software Autopano Giga 2.6, with a root mean square error (RMS) of less than 5 m where possible. Aerial images were then georeferenced in ArcMap10.0, using Ordnance Survey maps where features were readily visible both on the map and on the image. On occasion, images were georeferenced using aerial imagery that was already georeferenced. Accuracies of less than 5 m RMS was obtained. Georeferenced images were then rectified with the ArcMap10 Nearest Neighbour (for discrete data) function as uncompressed TIFFs, before compression to JPEG format with 100% image quality. Image clarity and descriptions were given for individual images. Spatial reference for all data is British National Grid.  Present-day low-altitude imagery was captured with a time-lapse compact Canon camera (D10) with no flash and infinity focus. The camera was attached to either a kite or a helium balloon and led over the sites of interest. The resultant images were mosaicked with Autopano Giga 2.6 achieving the lowest RMS possible and visual clarity. Ground control points were marked during the aerial fly over with a dGPS (Leica GS50 or Magellan MobileMapper 6). On several occasions, dominant habitat types were also walked round in the field providing habitat ground-truthing, logging polylines in the GPS systems. The data from the GPS systems were post-processed using ephemeris data from a fixed station (Morpeth), to achieve accuracies of less than 1.5 m for ground control points and less than 3 m for walk-around data.\tBush, L.E., Davies, A.J. 2013. Historic aerial imagery to monitor temporal change in intertidal habitats. CCW Scientific Reports, Report No: 1047. 103 pp.\tData archived as project 430, media 1467\tPaul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T11:08:59.308Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18075\tNRW_DS115518\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.744Z\t639\tValid\tEditable\tBardsey Seal Counts\tCyfrif Morloi Llwyd Ynys Enlli\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353138\tGrey seals Halichoerus grypus are among the rarest seals in the world: the UK population represents about 40% of the world population and 95% of the EU population. Globally, there are three reproductively-isolated stocks of grey seal: a west Atlantic (northern North American) stock; a Baltic stock; and an East Atlantic stock. The latter extends from Iceland and northern Norway southwards to northern France, with the majority breeding around Great Britain and Ireland.    The extent of connectivity within the Irish Sea grey seal (Halichoerus grypus) population has been investigated every year since 2006 using  photo-identification and bio-logging techniques.  The purpose of this data capture was to survey Bardsey seal population fluctuations. Surveys were undertaken by both trained staff and volunteers.\tSeal counts on Bardsey are taken during a weekly walk round the island during the period where most seals would be hauled out, dry and resting. Seals are differentiated into various stage and gender categories, from youngest to oldest, i.e. white-coat pup; fully moulted pup; youngster; juvenile (female and male); adult male and adult female; and indeterminate (sex of the individual unknown). The seasons are classified as follows: autumn (September to November); winter (December to February; to spring (March to May) and summer (June to August).\t\tArchived within the Marine Archive as Project No. 396, Media No. 1413\tInternal Custodian: Tom Stringell, Marine and Coastal Advice Team, NRW.   RSPB Custodian: Jo Porter\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)\tOpen Government Licence\t2013-01-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T12:16:22.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18066\tNRW_DS115520\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.452Z\t369\tValid\tEditable\tAcross Wales Lagoon Monitoring - Carew Mill Pond (2012)\tMonitro Lagwnau ledled Cymru - Pwll Melin Caeriw 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353230\tThis dataset is a collation of data from subtidal benthic soft sediment sampling carried out within Carew Mill Pond lagoon, which is a feature of the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The data is unpublished. The purpose of this data capture is to aid in the monitoring and condition assessment of this Annex I feature.\tAbundance of benthic invertebrates (per 0.0044m2 core) and granulometry data were captured as part of this project. Supporting determinants are: National Grid References recorded using hand held GPS and salinity and temperature which are continuously recorded using a data logger. All samples are collected using a corer (0.0044m2). Benthic invertebrate samples are sieved over a 0.5 mm mesh. At 3 stations 5 replicate infaunal grab samples are taken as well as 3 granulometry samples from each station.   Time series monitoring of the site began in 2012 however previous site visits occurred in April 2007,  August 2007,  November 2007,  August 2008, May 2009, March 2010 and March 2011.   Benthic invertebrate samples are processed by laboratories that are members of the National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control (NMBAQC) scheme and random samples from each survey are also selected for audit by Unicomarine.\t\tData is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 241 and Media No\u0027s 1337.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting (Matt Green?),  NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2012-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T12:18:10.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18072\tNRW_DS115521\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.738Z\t731\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSwansea Bay and Carmarthen Bay Beach profiles data (Lavernock to St Anns Head Shoreline Management Plan (SMP2))\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353231\tThe purpose of this data capture was to provide a review of the background geological factors and physical processes which is intended to inform the assessment of policy options in the second generation Shoreline Management Plan for the area between St. Ann\u0027s Head and Lavernock Point.   This dataset describes the geological and physical processes occurring at Swansea and Carmarthen Bay. Processes investigated include beach profile, beach topographic survey, LiDAR cross sections, summary characteristics, tide data and weather data. Two related reports were written within the same project Annex A and Annex B, Annex B builds on the earlier review and presents further analysis of data. Particular attention has been given to the estuaries and intervening shorelines in Swansea Bay and Carmarthen Bay. Three sections of open coast associated with the estuaries were also considered:  - The Pendine to Ginst Point barrier complex (including Pendine Burrows and Laugharne Burrows) - The Tywyn Point to Pembrey Burrows barrier complex - The Hills Tor to Whiteford Point barrier complex (including Whiteford Burrows).\tMorphological data from Swansea and Carmarthen Bay was collated from reports and analysed within this report. The Swansea Bay and Carmarthen Bay Shoreline Management Plan II were divided into three Coastal Behaviour Systems (CBS) (1) Bristol Channel North (Penarth to Worm\u0027s Head) (2) Carmarthen Bay (Worms Head to Giltar Point) (3) South Wales Peninsula (Giltar Point to St. David\u0027s Head)  Within these CBS a number of Shoreline Behaviour Units (SBU) were identified (1) Penarth to Nash Point (2) Nash Point to Pothcawl (3) Porthcawl to Mumbles Head (4) Mumbles Head to Worms Head (5) Worms Head to Burry Holms (6) Burry Holms to Pendine (7) Pendine to Giltar Point (8) Giltar Point to Studdock Point  Further analysis was performed within the Annex B report, involving analysis of additional LiDAR data. For the purpose of the study, five estuarine areas were defined within the Carmarthen Bay area and separate analyses performed on each;  - The Loughor estuary (east of a line between Whiteford Point and \u0027The Nose\u0027 Pembrey) - The Taf Estuary (north of a line between Ginst Point and Wharley Point) - The Towy (Tywi) estuary (north of a line between Wharley Point and Tregoning Hill) - The Gwendraeth estuary (east of a line between St Ishmael and Tywyn Point) - The Three Rivers confluence area (south of a line drawn between Tywyn Point and Ginst Point).\tMarine and Coastal Physical Scientist, NRW. Associated Report:  -  K. Pye. 2009, Coastal processes and shoreline behaviour in Swansea Bay and Carmarthen Bay.   -  Annex A2: Morphological Change in Carmarthen Bay and Adjoining Estuaries: Further Analysis (prepared by Professor Ken Pye, March 2010).\tData is archived as project 432, media 1469.\tCeri Seaton       need to understabd the T\u0026amp;C under which this data was provided.  May be for internal use only\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T10:30:17.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18064\tNRW_DS115523\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.022Z\t415\tValid\tEditable\tAcross Wales Lagoon Monitoring - Neyland Wier (Westfield Pill) Pool 2012\tMonitro Lagwnau ledled Cymru - Pwll Cored Neyland (Westfield Pill) 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353233\tThis dataset is a collation of data from subtidal benthic soft sediment sampling carried out within Neyland Wier lagoon, which is a feature of the Pembrokeshire Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC).   The purpose of this data capture was to aid in the monitoring and condition assessment of this Annex I feature\tData captured: Abundance of benthic invertebrates (per 0.0044m2 core) and granulometry.  Supporting determinants are: National Grid References recorded using hand held GPS and salinity and temperature which are continuously recorded using a data logger.    All samples are collected using a corer (0.0044m2). Benthic invertebrate samples are sieved over a 0.5mm mesh. At 3 stations 5 replicate infaunal grab samples are taken as well as 3 granulometry samples from each station.   Although official time series monitoring of the site started in 2012, the site was visited and some data collected on 08 Dec 2010 and 01 Apr 2011.   Benthic invertebrate samples are processed by laboratories that are members of the National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control (NMBAQC) scheme and random samples from each survey are also selected for audit by Unicomarine\tFor further details of analyses see: Analysis of macrofaunal and sediment samples. Analytical Report Report Number: J36221-1 available in NRW Marine Archive.\t[A] Data is archived as Project No. 241, Media No. 1338.    [B] For further details of analyses see: Analysis of macrofaunal and sediment samples. Analytical Report Report Number: J36221-1 available in NRW Marine Archive: Project Number 241 Media Number 1338. [C] Species and PSA data is also available in Marine Recorder [MRCCW3000000007A]\tInternal Custodian: NRW Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team (Matt Green?)  NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2012-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T12:20:01.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18065\tNRW_DS115525\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.016Z\t370\tValid\tEditable\tAcross Wales Drop-Down Video Monitoring: Additional North Llyn Ground Truthing (2012)\tGwaith monitro fideo tanddwr ledled Cymru 2012: Tystiolaeth ychwanegol ar gyfer Gogledd Llŷn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353235\tDrop down video ground-truthing of additional Modiolus bed discovered partially outside the Special Area of Conservation (SAC).  This  follows on from previous ground-truthing carried out in 2011 (Marine Archive project 313). Analysis and Marine Recorder entry to be undertaken later in the year under Data Capture call-off contract.    The purpose of this data capture was to conduct a ground truthing exercise through which the extent and quality of Modiolus mussel beds could be assessed in an area of the coast of the Llyn Peninsula, North Wales.\tThis follows on from previous ground-truthing carried out in 2011 (project 313):  Across Wales drop-down video monitoring, 2012: North Llyn ground truthing.   Drop-down video is useful for gathering large volumes of data across wide areas of seabed and often to depths beyond which diver observations can be safely made. Detailed methods and site location are described in CCW Marine Monitoring report 24a. Amendments to the previous methods include the addition of a HD video camera system manufactured by C-Technics in place of the SD camera previously used. A Cannon EOS 400D digital SLR was also used in a housing manufactured by Rovtech systems. Photographic stills were taken every 11 seconds along the tows to assist with species and habitat validation. GIS vessel tracks exported from Boat GPS.  GIS Start and end points and field notes also supplied.\t\t[A]  Data is held within the Marine Archive as Project No. 366, Media No. 1361 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1301to1400),   [B]  Videos are maintained in the archive  [C]  Marine Recorder Survey  - MRCCW3170000000A\tInternal Contact: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.     NRW.Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2012-06-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T12:21:27.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18063\tNRW_DS115526\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.002Z\t429\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Monitoring Drop-Down Video around North Llyn Special Area of Conservation ( SAC ) 2012\tFideo Tanddwr ar gyfer Monitro\u0027r Môr o amgylch Ardal Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACA) Gogledd Llŷn 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353236\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs) are strictly protected sites designated under the EC Habitats Directive. The Habitats Directive includes a list of habitat types and species that are considered to be most in need of conservation at a European level (excluding birds, which are covered under the EC Birds Directive). Once sites have been designated, the country agencies work with owners, occupiers and others to secure their management and protection regime.    This dataset provides a continuation of data collected from Drop Down Video Monitoring in Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC.  The purpose of this data capture was to inform management decisions within the SACs. QA was undertaken including 5% of tows being re-analysed by a different surveyor and QA of at least 10% of data entry. Analysis and QA undertaken by Marine EcoSol.\tIn June 2012 the Countryside Council for Wales undertook video tows for routine monitoring within the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation. Video was collected using a bespoke C-technics C-Vision Mantis HD video camera towed on a sledge from CCW\u0027s research vessel, Pedryn. Photographic stills (using a Canon 600/DD stills camera) were taken every 8 seconds along to tow to assist with habitat validation and utilised to inform species identification and augment species lists.    Video was analysed for habitats, species and biotopes by Marine EcoSol using standard Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) derived techniques and JNCC Marine Habitat Classification. Data was later entered into Marine Recorder by Marine EcoSol.   GIS vessel tracks were exported from Boat GPS. GIS Start and end points and field notes also supplied.\tThis monitoring is a follow on from data collected between 2004 -  2005 (Across Wales drop-down video monitoring 2004-2005 (NRW_DS87090), and 2007 -  2010 (Across Wales drop-down video monitoring 2007 - 2010 (NRW_DS110070).  Species data is published and available to download from the NBN Atlas as part of the \"NRW Marine data from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Technical Support (Research \u0026amp; Monitoring) Contracts, Wales\" dataset (NRW_DS116350)\tData is Archived as Project No. 365, Media 1360.  (Full pathway: K:\\Archive\\Media1301to1400\\Media1360) All species and habitat data is available in Marine Recorder [ MRCCW31700000005 \u0026amp; MRCCW3170000000B].   This monitoring is a follow on from data collected under archive project No. 35.\tInternal Custodian - CCW Marine Monitoring Ecologist.  Suggested NRW contact is Charles Lindenbaum, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team.   NRW Metadata contact - Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2013-03-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:33:04.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18060\tNRW_DS115528\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.822Z\t700\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDrop Down Video Data Caernarfon Bay, Tremadog Bay, Cardigan Bay and west Anglesey (2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353238\tBangor University School of Ocean Science undertook video surveys during a research programme. This data was provided to former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) so CCW could analyse the video and still images for species and habitat information. This data was then collated and entered into Marine Recorder. The purpose of this data capture and collation was to help build an evidence base on which to create informed management decisions within the marine environment. This data allows for a better Bangor University has given former CCW permission to hold, reuse and release this data as appropriate.\tDrop-down tows were carried out with a sledge mounted video and stills camera system (models unspecified) which was towed at a speed of approximately 0.5 knots for a period of 10 minutes. Continuous video footaage was recorded on DVD and the digital stills camera took a high resolution image every 10 seconds. The field of view of the video camera covered an area of approximately 0.12m2 (width 0.41m x depth 0.30m). Each still image covered an area of 0.13m2 (0.44m x 0.30m). A manual log was kept of the position and time at the start and end of transect while the camera was on the bottom.\tThe contract for the holding, re-using and release of the data by CCW is currently held in the School of Ocean Sciences: contact Hilmar Hinz.\tData is archived as Project No. 387, Media 1402 Working area H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\drop down video\\2012\\SOS data\tData Custodian: Marine Monitoring Ecologist, NRW. Marine Recorder system custodian: Master Reference Data Team Metadata Point of contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-University of Wales Bangor\t\t2012-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:02:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18062\tNRW_DS115537\t2024-05-28T12:39:13.006Z\t338\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring for the River Wye (2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353337\tThe River Wye has been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The data supports a condition assessment for the population of brook, river and sea lamprey (protected under Annex II of the EU Habitats Directive 92/43/EEC) for the second Habitats Directive monitoring report cycle (2007-2012). This dataset combines the survey results from our lamprey surveys conducted by the former Environment Agency Wales (now part of Natural Resources Wales) and Dwr Cymru/Welsh Water. This data collection was required as part of the second Habitats Directive monitoring report cycle (2007-2012). 48 sites were surveyed along the following water courses; Afon Elan, Aran, Bidno, Camddwr-Irfon, Cammarch, Chwerfri, Cledan, Cnyffiad, Duhonw, Dulas Brook, Edw, Garth Dulas - Irfon, Gwenwst, Hafrena, Irfon, Llunfi, Marteg, Nant Gwesyn, Nant y Dernol, Sgithwen bridge, South Dulas - Irfon, Wye.\tAs detailed in the LIFE+ protocol the Environment Agency electrofishing surveys were carried out in both optimal and sub-optimal habitat. Standard electric fishing practice for operators and equipment were followed as developed by the European Standards Committee and detailed in the Environment Agency Electric Fishing Operational Instruction 99308. Data was collated from four fisheries and habitat based surveys to assess species distributions of River, Brook and Sea lamprey. Data collected included population attributes associated with habitat availability, age structure, and distribution within catchment, ammocoete density, and proximity to historic sea lamprey spawning sites. The Environment Agency Wales conducted a survey in 2010 along Afron Irfon. 19 sites were surveyed by Dwr Cymru/Welsh Water in 2012, 12 of which were within the boundary of the River Wye in Wales (the remaining 7 being located in England). 17 sites were surveys in 2012 by the former Environment Agency Wales within the Wye catchment.\tRiver Wye population attribute condition assessment for Brook, River and Sea Lamprey, 2012 CCW Staff Science Report No. 13/8/2.\tArchived as project number 434 Media Number: 1471\tInternal Custodian: Freshwater Monitoring Coordinator, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:14:00.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18061\tNRW_DS115547\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.833Z\t802\tValid\tEditable\tSevern Estuary Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Special Protection Area (SPA): Intertidal Mudflats and Sandflats Condition Assessment 2012\tAsesiad Cyflwr Traethellau Mwd a Thywod Rhynglanwol 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353437\tThe Severn Estuary is the largest example of a coastal plain estuary in the United Kingdom. It has been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the EU Habitats Directive, a Special Protection Area (SPA) under the Birds Directive and as a Ramsar site under the Convention on Wetlands of International Importance.    The primary focus of this study relates to monitoring the intertidal mudflats and sandflats feature of the Severn Estuary SAC by assessing the relevant attributes for the purpose of condition assessment. However, monitoring of prey availability within the Severn Estuary SPA was also a secondary objective.    The survey was undertaken in three blocks between the 13th October and 14th November 2012.\tThe methods employed within this study were largely limited to Phase II sampling that was based on Phase I biotope maps from previous studies. However, if any obviously incorrect biotope boundaries were encountered or if any notable habitats or species were observed during the course of the surveys, then records were appropriately amended. Anthropogenic influences and negative indicators were also recorded where encountered.   A total of 9 littoral mudflat and sandflat sediment biotopes were identified throughout the Severn Estuary SAC and SPA during the course of the study, and their subsequent extent and distribution was mapped using GIS.  At each station;  - five replicate 0.01m2 cores were taken to a depth of 15 cm following ISI, Environment Agency and JNCC guidelines. A photograph of the sediment at the station and the general area was also taken. Macrofauna samples were delivered to APEM Ltd for analysis.  - the depth of the Redox Potential Discontinuity (RPD) layer was determined at each station and a second core taken for sediment granulometry. The sediment samples were delivered to the National Laboratory Service (NLS) at Starcross for PSA.   Previous Phase 1 biotope surveys were carried out on behalf of Natural England in 2005.  Phase I surveys were also carried out on the Welsh shores between 1996 and 2005 on behalf of CCW. The specific methods used during these surveys are unknown as only the GIS map outputs are available.\tNatural England, CCW and Environment Agency Wales (now NRW) jointly funded a joint sediment survey of the Severn estuary in 2012.  The work was done as part of a framework agreement.  CCW (now NRW) are free to supply Welsh related data.\tData archived as Project 435, Media 1472 (File path:  K:\\Archive\\Media1401to1500\\Media1472).   Species and habitat data is available in Marine Recorder through Natural England Snapshot  [MRNE013700000005]\tPaul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer), Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Natural England\t\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T12:26:43.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18059\tNRW_DS115548\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.820Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSeal pelage and Photo ID Monitoring Wales (2005-2013)\tLluniau adnabod blew morloi Cymru at ddibenion arolygu (2005-2013)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353438\tThis dataset is a catalogue of seal images were collected as part of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW) seal monitoring work. Specific seal pelage surveys and images provided by volunteers and members of the public were also collected through NRWs Flicker account. These images were used to monitor behaviour and populations of seals around north Wales.  Photo-ID allowed these pelages to be captured and analysed, which allows a catalogue of individuals to be constructed. This was then used to estimate the population size and movements. By repeating the photo-ID on an annual basis matches can be re-made giving an indication of a seals fidelity to a particular site and associations between individuals.\tImages were obtained if each animal, which consisted of where possible, left and right head profiles, chest, abdomens and flanks. A combination of fields notes and separating images were taken at the same site.  At each site, counts of the number of males, females, juveniles, yearlings and pups were taken.  Suitable images of the Left and Right side of the animal were processed (cropped and colour processed) and matched with the main EIRPhot database. A thumbnail of the image was stored in the database. A spreadsheet was maintained that lists which images in the main catalogue had been processed and matched within the database. Equipment: Boat for deployment and pickup, Digital SLR cameras, Handheld radio (25W radio/aerial/battery - if boat is not staying close by), GPS, charts or maps, Note book and pencil.   This data and metadata entry is replaced by more recent dataset.  Metadata 122202\t\tData is archived as Project No. 395, Media No. 1412  Other information: The majority of the images are created by CCW.  Some images are suppplied to CCW for use within the project by volunteers and other third parties/partners. Images are also supplied to CCW through Flicker.  The ExtractCompare software used as part of this project is held on DELL1407.\tInternal Custodian: Tom Stringell, Senior Marine Mammal ecologist, Natural resources planning/Marine and freshwater ecosystem group,   Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW       This entry will be taken out of public view and a new metadata entry will be created for the latest version of the database.  This entry is retained internally as the data is retained as a snapshot in time.  New metadata entry 122202.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-23T09:38:20.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18058\tNRW_DS115549\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.673Z\t294\tValid\tEditable\tFishMap Môn Detailed Fishing Intensity Levels\tGraddfeydd Dwysedd Pysgota Manwl FishMap Môn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353439\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW), recreational and commercial fishing sectors, and the Welsh Government. The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and combining it with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activity.   Fishing Intensity is described as the effort of a fishing activity in a given area. For example, this could be the number of pots hauled per day per hectare. It is also described as one of three levels, but the actual intensity value is derived differently for each fishing type.  The purpose of this data capture was to examine the spatial extent of fishing activities and the determine the habitats affected by different fishing gear types in an area around Anglesey.   The data consists of output generated for nine different gear types - King Scallop Dredging, Queen Scallop Dredging, Mussel Seed Dredging, Light Otter Trawling, Bottom Set Nets, Potting, Sea Angling and Casual and Professional Hand Gathering. There are multiple outputs for Potting and Sea Angling for commercial and recreational sectors. From the attribute \\_overlaps\\\"  it is possible to determine the number of vessels/ fishers operating in a particular area.   Due to the sensitivity of fishing intensity related data two versions of the FishMap Môn intensity level data were created both a detailed and generalised version. This detailed version of the dataset shows the precise locations of the fishing activities and the data also contains specific information about the fishing activities and so is restricted to the FishMap Môn Team and certain partners - contact the dataset owner for more information. \"\tA series of interviews were conducted with commercial and recreational fisherman, results being completed in an electronic (ESRI Geodatabase) or paper questionnaire.   FishMap Mon was undertaken as a pilot project giving a general indication of where a limited amount of fishing activity is thought to have occurred at a given time, and its expected impact on marine habitats. This fishing effort dataset was a snap shot in time and may not be representative of current fishing practices in the study area or the rest of Wales. There is a low level of confidence in the data and trail methodology.\tRelated, in part, to the Fisheries Sensitivity studies being undertaken in NRW. The generalised version is shown at a coarser scale and is available for release (See Related Titles).\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Intensity\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists).   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2017-05-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T13:44:53.201Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18057\tNRW_DS115551\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.627Z\t594\tValid\tEditable\tFishMap Môn Habitat Sensitivity Confidence\tHyder Sensitifrwydd Cynefin FishMap Môn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353531\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW), the Welsh Government, and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors. Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited, Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers.   The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities.   Habitat Sensitivity Confidence represents the confidence NRW has had in the expert judgement of the Habitat Sensitivity Assessment from a fishing gear type.  The data consists of output generated for nine different gear types - King Scallop Dredging, Queen Scallop Dredging, Mussel Seed Dredging, Light Otter Trawling, Bottom Set Nets, Potting, Sea Angling and Casual and Professional Hand Gathering.  Due to it\u0027s nature as a pilot project NRW no longer makes this data routinely available, alternative fisheries related data may be available on the Welsh Government Marine Planning Portal.\tA series of interviews were conducted with commercial and recreational fisherman, results being completed in an electronic (ESRI Geodatabase) or paper questionnaire. Confidence is recorded as Low, Moderate or High, or Unknown where a particular habitat has not been assigned a Sensitivity to a fishing type.  FishMap Mon was undertaken as a pilot project giving a general indication of where a limited amount of fishing activity is thought to have occurred at a given time, and its expected impact on marine habitats. This fishing effort dataset was a snap shot in time and may not be representative of current fishing practices in the study area or the rest of Wales. There is a low level of confidence in the data and trail methodology.\tRelated, in part, to the Fisheries Sensitivity studies being undertaken in NRW.\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Sensitivity ,   Legacy CCW Archive:  M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\FishMapMon\\FMM_Data\\MapInfo_Tab\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists).  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team,\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2013-12-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:23:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18056\tNRW_DS115553\t2024-05-28T12:39:12.551Z\t741\tValid\tEditable\tFishMap Môn Generalised Fishing Intensity Levels\tGraddfeydd Dwysedd Pysgota Cyffredinoledig FishMap Môn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353533\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW), the Welsh Government, and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors. Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited, Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers.   The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities. The purpose of this data capture was to examine the spatial extent of fishing activities and the determine the habitats affected by different fishing gear types in an area around Anglesey.   Fishing Intensity is described as the effort of a fishing activity in a given area. For example, this could be the number of pots hauled per day per hectare. Fishing intensity is described as one of three levels, but the actual intensity value is derived differently for each fishing type. The data consists of output generated for nine different gear types - King Scallop Dredging, Queen Scallop Dredging, Mussel Seed Dredging, Light Otter Trawling, Bottom Set Nets, Potting, Sea Angling and Casual and Professional Hand Gathering.   There are multiple outputs for Potting and Sea Angling for commercial and recreational sectors From the attribute \\_overlaps\\\"  it is possible to determine the number of vessels/ fishers operating in a particular area. Due to the sensitivity of fishing intensity related data two versions of the FishMap Môn intensity level data were created both a detailed and generalised version. The detailed version is sensitive and therefore has restrictions accompanying the dataset however this generalised version does not.\"  Due to it\u0027s nature as a pilot project NRW no longer makes this data routinely available, alternative fisheries related data may be available on the Welsh Government Marine Planning\tA series of interviews were conducted with commercial and recreational fisherman results being completed in an electronic (ESRI Geodatabase) or paper questionnaire.  FishMap Mon was undertaken as a pilot project giving a general indication of where a limited amount of fishing activity is thought to have occurred at a given time, and its expected impact on marine habitats. This fishing effort dataset was a snap shot in time and may not be representative of current fishing practices in the study area or the rest of Wales. There is a low level of confidence in the data and trail methodology.\tRelated, in part, to the Fisheries Sensitivity studies being undertaken in NRW.\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\FishMapMon\\FMM_Data\\MapInfo_Tab\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists). Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRWCrown Copyright and Database Right.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2013-12-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:09:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18103\tNRW_DS115554\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.045Z\t389\tValid\tEditable\tFishMap Môn Detailed Habitat Sensitivity Levels\tGraddfeydd Sensitifrwydd Cynefin Manwl FishMap Môn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353534\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW), the Welsh Government, and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors. Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited, Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers.   The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities.   This data is related to habitat sensitivity. Habitat Sensitivity is based on the resistance and resilience of a habitat to a particular fishing intensity from a fishing gear type. Sensitivity is recorded as Low, Medium or High. The data consists of output generated for nine different gear types - King Scallop Dredging, Queen Scallop Dredging, Mussel Seed Dredging, Light Otter Trawling, Bottom Set Nets, Potting, Sea Angling and Casual and Professional Hand Gathering. There are multiple outputs for Potting and Sea Angling for commercial and recreational sectors.    The purpose of this data capture was to examine the spatial extent of fishing activities and the determine the habitats affected by different fishing gear types in an area around Anglesey. A 12 nautical mile buffered zone around the Isle of Anglesey / Ynys Mon, extending from Nefyn in the West, to Colwyn Bay in the East.    Due to the sensitivity of fishing intensity related data two versions of the Fishmap Môn habitat sensitivity level data were created, both a detailed and generalised version. This detailed version of the dataset shows the precise locations of the fishing activities, and the data also contains specific information about the fishing activities, and so is restricted to the FishMap Môn Team and certain partners - contact the dataset owner for more information.\tA series of interviews were conducted with commercial and recreational fisherman, results being completed in an electronic (ESRI Geodatabase) or paper questionnaire.  Related, in part, to the Fisheries Sensitivity studies being undertaken in NRW.  FishMap Mon was undertaken as a pilot project giving a general indication of where a limited amount of fishing activity is thought to have occurred at a given time, and its expected impact on marine habitats. This fishing effort dataset was a snap shot in time and may not be representative of current fishing practices in the study area or the rest of Wales. There is a low level of confidence in the data and trail methodology.\tThe generalised version is shown at a coarser scale and is available for release (see metadata URI 115555).\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Habitat\\\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists). Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRWCrown Copyright and Database Right.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2017-05-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18101\tNRW_DS115555\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.911Z\t372\tValid\tEditable\tFishMap Môn Generalised Habitat Sensitivity Levels\tGraddfeydd Sensitifrwydd Cynefin Cyffredinoledig FishMap Môn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353535\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW), the Welsh Government, and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors. Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited, Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers.   The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities.    Habitat Sensitivity is based on the resistance and resilience of a habitat to a particular fishing intensity from a fishing gear type. Sensitivity is recorded as Low, Medium or High. The data consists of output generated for nine different gear types - King Scallop Dredging, Queen Scallop Dredging, Mussel Seed Dredging, Light Otter Trawling, Bottom Set Nets, Potting, Sea Angling and Casual and Professional Hand Gathering. There are multiple outputs for Potting and Sea Angling for commercial and recreational sectors.   Due to the sensitivity of fishing intensity related data two versions of the FishMap Môn habitat sensitivity level data were created, both a detailed and generalised version. The detailed version of the dataset shows the precise locations of the fishing activities, and the data also contains specific information about the fishing activities, and so is restricted to the FishMap Môn Team and certain partners. However this generalised version is shown at a coarser scale and is available for release.  Due to it\u0027s nature as a pilot project NRW no longer makes this data routinely available, alternative fisheries related data may be available on the Welsh Government Marine Planning\tA series of interviews were conducted with commercial and recreational fisherman, results being completed in an electronic (ESRI Geodatabase) or paper questionnaire.  FishMap Mon was undertaken as a pilot project giving a general indication of where a limited amount of fishing activity is thought to have occurred at a given time, and its expected impact on marine habitats. This fishing effort dataset was a snap shot in time and may not be representative of current fishing practices in the study area or the rest of Wales. There is a low level of confidence in the data and trail methodology.\tRelated, in part, to the Fisheries Sensitivity studies being undertaken in NRW.\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\FishMapMon\\FMM_Data\\MapInfo_Tab\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists). Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRWCrown Copyright and Database Right.   Metadata Custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2013-12-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18112\tNRW_DS115556\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.064Z\t673\tValid\tEditable\tFishMap Môn Fishing Activity Combined with Seabed Habitat Sensitivity\tGweithgaredd Pysgota FishMap Mon wedi ei gyfuno a Sensitifrwydd Cynefinoedd Gwely\u0027r Mor\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353536\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW), the Welsh Government, and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors.   Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited, Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers. The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities.    The purpose of this data capture was to examine the spatial extent of fishing activities and the determine the habitats affected by different fishing gear types in an area around Anglesey. Confidence in the data used for habitat types varied depending on the source of the data. The confidence in the Phase 1 habitat data is good, however confidence in subtidal data from the HABMAP program was assigned a confidence level of Very Poor, Poor, Fair and Good.\tThis dataset combines the data from the intertidal and subtidal surveys: - Intertidal Fisheries Habitats  (March 2010) - SUBTIDAL Fisheries Habitats with Dee and Severn 15Aug2012 - Data for Horse Mussel Reefs has also had to be merged in with the Subtidal data, as this is updated: - CCW_horse_mussel_polygon_v2    Each dataset had a different set of attribution, and had to be manipulated to apply a common standard, and also passed through a spatial data cleansing to ensure Quality Assurance of the data prior to use in the FishMap Mon process.  The data represents 31 different habitats, and also a mosaic of rock and sediment. The latter is identified by a dominant habitat in the mosaic, so that appropriate sensitivity scores could be applied.   The data attributes include a Confidence Level, which indicates the confidence in the spatial extent and location of a particular habitat.  For the purposes of FishMap Mon the Confidence levels have been reassigned as Poor, Fair and Good (Very Poor and Poor being merged).    FishMap Mon was undertaken as a pilot project giving a general indication of where a limited amount of fishing activity is thought to have occurred at a given time, and its expected impact on marine habitats. This fishing effort dataset was a snap shot in time and may not be representative of current fishing practices in the study area or the rest of Wales. There is a low level of confidence in the data and trail methodology.  Due to it\u0027s nature as a pilot project NRW no longer makes this data routinely available, alternative fisheries related data may be available on the Welsh Government Marine Planning Portal.\tThis project also relates to, in part, to the Fisheries Sensitivity studies being undertaken in NRW.\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Combined Seabed Habitat Lines.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Combined Seabed Habitat Regions.lyr ,  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\FishMapMon\\FMM_Data\\MapInfo_Tab\tColin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists).  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2013-12-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:59.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18104\tNRW_DS115560\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.035Z\t600\tValid\tEditable\tA Survey of Moorings and their Impact on Seagrass Beds at Porth Dinllaen (2012)\tArolwg Angorfeydd a\u0027u Heffaith ar Wlau Morwellt ym Mhorth Dinllaen, 2012\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353630\tPorth Dinllaen has one of the largest and densest seagrass beds within Wales. As with the majority of the United Kingdom the seagrass bed at Porth Dinllaen is typified by one species, Zostera marina. The seagrass beds at Porth Dinllaen spans over 1 km in length and up to 650 m wide in places, throughout the inner and outer harbour areas. Previous work has found that boat moorings create distinct radial scour patterns within the Z. marina.   As part of the management of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation(SAC), the SAC Officer has been working with the National Trust, Relevant Authorities, local fishermen, boat owners and other stakeholders to look at options to reduce the impact of the moorings on Z. marina at Porth Dinllaen. In 2012 surveys were conducted within the outer harbour using volunteer SCUBA divers.   The aims of the 2012 surveys were to record the mooring number and designs used within Porth Dinllaen outer harbour, and assess how these moorings relate to the density of Z. marina, adjacent Z. marina canopy height, and presence of wasting. The work was carried out under the Ecosystem Resilience and Diversity Fund.\tData was collected using volunteer SCUBA divers. The project relates to work carried out on the site within the same year to look at the sediment infauna and granulometry.\t\tData is located as Project 386, media 1475 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1475)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Gwynedd Council - Cyngor Gwynedd\tOpen Government Licence\t2013-05-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:42:08.141Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18110\tNRW_DS115569\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.044Z\t267\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFisher Towed Survey in Cardigan Bay 2012\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353639\tSurveys of seabed in Cardigan Bay, carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS), Bangor University, in October 2012, as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  The main aim of the survey work was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats, and primarily whether cobble reef habitat was present, as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging.\tDrop-down video tows were carried out with a GoPro video camera attached to a mini-sled towed behind a fishing boat. Continuous video footage was recorded onto MP3 files.  A manual log was kept of the position and time at the start and end of transect while the camera was on the bottom. The tow lengths ranged from 150m to 1206m and seabed consisted primarily of sands and gravels with some pebbles and cobbles.  Videos were analysed by a marine biological surveyor to record information on habitat character, conspicuous species and their abundance.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes. Analysis by CALM staff estimated abundance of all identifiable taxa using the SACFOR abundance scale. Habitat descriptions and assignment of biotopes from the national biotope classification to each record were also recorded.  Analysis included a QA process to validate and verify the recorded data.\tLambert, G.I., Murray L.G., Kaiser M.J., Salomonsen H., Cambie, G. (2013)  Welsh waters scallop survey - Cardigan Bay to Liverpool Bay July-August 2013. Bangor University, Fisheries and Conservation Report No. 30. Bangor University. Albrecht, J. K. (2013). Taxonomic and functional recovery of epifauna after the permanent closure of an area of the Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC), Wales, to a scallop dredge fishery. MSc thesis, Bangor University, Fisheries and Conservation report No. 28, Pp.81\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466.\tGIS Service Desk  External Custodian: Michael Kaiser, School of Ocean Science, Bangor University Formerly: Dr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:45.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18107\tNRW_DS115570\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.039Z\t506\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Liverpool Bay (2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353730\tThis Natural Resources Wales (NRW) owned dataset contains the species data associated with drop down video survey conducted by Bangor University. Surveys of seabed in Liverpool Bay, carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS), Bangor University, in June and October 2012, as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (9 tows) and associated still images collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog, using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was between 8 and 22 km offshore, north east of  Anglesey.  The tow lengths ranged from 100m to 500m and seabed consisted primarily of sands, gravels and pebbles with some areas of cobbles.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes. The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats, as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging.  The aim of the subsequent data capture was to improve Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) seabed habitat mapping for Liverpool Bay and assign biotopes. CCW now forms part of NRW.\tDrop-down video tows were carried out with various combinations of a standard resolution digital video camera, a GoPro video camera and a stills camera attached to a sledge towed behind RV Prince Madog. Standard video was recorded onto .VOB files.  GoPro video footage was recorded onto MP4 files. Stills camera images are .JPG files.  The arrangement of the videos and cameras is described as follows: The middle width of the GoPro videos is about 96cm for the ones that are at an angle (80 at the bottom and \u0026gt;112 at the top) while the ones which face straight down are 80 cm wide. The still camera is a Canon 400D, with field of view of ca. 0.13m2 (44cm by 30cm). The middle width of the videos on the CDs are 41cm. A manual log was kept of the position and time at the start and end of transect while the camera was on the bottom.  Videos and images were analysed by a marine biological surveyor (Jen Jones) to record information on habitat character, conspicuous species and their abundance.  Habitat descriptions and assignment of biotopes from the national biotope classification to each record were also recorded.  Analysis included a QA process to validate and verify the recorded data.  Part of this dataset is sensitive and restricted. The sensitive data is as follows: Pecten maximus and Aequipecten opercularis Sensitive data must be kept confidential; general release is not permitted due to risk of harm to the environment or third parties.\tRelated Titles:  Lambert, G.I., Murray L.G., Kaiser M.J., Salomonsen H., Cambie, G. (2013)  Welsh waters scallop survey - Cardigan Bay to Liverpool Bay July-August 2013. Bangor University, Fisheries and Conservation Report No. 30. Bangor University\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500) Species data held in Marine Recorder (Survey numbers  MRCCW31600000012 and MRCCW31600000013.\tGIS Service Desk  Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Contact: Mike Kaiser or Dr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:29:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18109\tNRW_DS115571\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.961Z\t349\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Cardigan Bay, West of Dyfi (2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353731\tThis dataset consists of the results of a surveys of seabed in Cardigan Bay, carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS), Bangor University, in June 2012, as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (3 tows) and associated still images collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog, using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was approximately 25 km offshore, west of Dyfi.  The tow lengths ranged from 500m to 750m and seabed consisted primarily of sands, gravels and cobble/boulder reef.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes. The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats, as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging. The aim of the subsequent data capture was to improve CCW\u0027s seabed habitat mapping for Cardigan Bay and assign biotopes.\tDrop-down video tows were carried out with various combinations of a standard resolution digital video camera, a GoPro video camera and a stills camera attached to a sledge towed behind RV Prince Madog.  Standard video was recorded onto .VOB files.  GoPro video footage was recorded onto MP4 files. Stills camera images are .JPG files.  The arrangement of the videos and cameras is described below: The middle width of the GoPro videos is about 96 cm for the ones that are at an angle (80 at the bottom and \u0026gt;112 at the top) while the ones which face straight down are 80 cm wide. The still camera is a Canon 400D, with field of view of ca. 0.13m2 (44cm by 30cm). The middle width of the videos on the CDs are 41cm.  A manual log was kept of the position and time at the start and end of transect while the camera was on the bottom. Videos and images were analysed by a marine biological surveyor (Jen Jones) to record information on habitat character, conspicuous species and their abundance.  Habitat descriptions and assignment of biotopes from the national biotope classification to each record were also recorded.  Analysis included a QA process to validate and verify the recorded data. The surveys are part of a 3-year EFF funded project on habitat mapping and effects of fisheries, particularly scallop dredging.\tDr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University. Lambert, G.I., Murray L.G., Kaiser M.J., Salomonsen H., Cambie, G. (2013)  Welsh waters scallop survey - Cardigan Bay to Liverpool Bay July-August 2013. Bangor University, Fisheries and Conservation Report No. 30. Bangor University. Albrecht, J. K. (2013). Taxonomic and functional recovery of epifauna after the permanent closure of an area of the Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC), Wales, to a scallop dredge fishery. MSc thesis, Bangor University, Fisheries and Conservation report No. 28, Pp.81\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1466) Species data held in Marine Recorder (Survey key:MRCCW31600000015)\tGIS Service Desk  Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:23:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18111\tNRW_DS115572\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.038Z\t386\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Cardigan Bay (2013)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353732\tSurveys of seabed in Cardigan Bay, carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS), Bangor University, in July 2013, as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (42 tows (24 in Work Package 1b and 18 in Work Package 2b) and associated still images collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog, using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was between 2 and 22 km offshore of the coast between Cardigan and New Quay.  The tow lengths ranged from 160m to 1800m and seabed consisted primarily of sands, gravels and pebbles with some areas of cobbles and boulders.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes.  The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats, as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging. The aim of the subsequent data capture was to improve CCW\u0027s seabed habitat mapping for Cardigan Bay and assign biotopes.\tDrop-down video tows were carried out with various combinations of a standard resolution digital video camera, a GoPro video camera and a stills camera attached to a sledge towed behind RV Prince Madog. Standard video was recorded onto .VOB files.  GoPro video footage was recorded onto MP4 files. Stills camera images are .JPG files.  The arrangement of the videos and cameras is described below: -  The middle width of the GoPro videos is about 96cm for the ones that are at an angle (80 at the bottom and \u0026gt;112 at the top) while the ones which face straight down are 80 cm wide. The still camera is a Canon 400D, with field of view of ca. 0.13m2 (44cm by 30cm). The middle width of the videos on the CDs are 41cm. -  A manual log was kept of the position and time at the start and end of transect while the camera was on the bottom. -  Videos and images were analysed by a marine biological surveyor (Jen Jones) to record information on habitat character, conspicuous species and their abundance.  Habitat descriptions and assignment of biotopes from the national biotope classification to each record were also recorded.  Analysis included a QA process to validate and verify the recorded data.\tDr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University. Related Titles: Lambert, G.I., Murray L.G., Kaiser M.J., Salomonsen H., Cambie, G. (2013)  Welsh waters scallop survey - Cardigan Bay to Liverpool Bay July-August 2013. Bangor University, Fisheries and Conservation Report No. 30. Bangor University. Albrecht, J. K. (2013). Taxonomic and functional recovery of epifauna after the permanent closure of an area of the Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC), Wales, to a scallop dredge fishery. MSc thesis, Bangor University, Fisheries and Conservation report No. 28, Pp.81\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500) Data is stored in Marine Recorder (survey numbers MRCCW31600000010 and MRCCW31600000011)\tGIS Service Desk  Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team        Part of this dataset is sensitive and restricted. The sensitive data is as follows: Pecten maximus and Aequipecten opercularis. Sensitive data must be kept confidential; general release is not permitted due to risk of harm to the environment or third parties.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:29:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18099\tNRW_DS115574\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.894Z\t362\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Liverpool Bay (2013)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353734\tSurveys of seabed in Liverpool Bay, carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS), Bangor University, in August 2013, as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (9 tows) collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog, using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was between 8 and 27 km offshore, north of Bangor to Colwyn Bay. The tow lengths ranged from 216 m to 611 m and seabed consisted primarily of sands and gravels with some areas of pebbes.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes.  The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats, as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging.\tDrop-down video tows were carried out with various combinations of a standard resolution digital video camera, a GoPro video camera and a stills camera attached to a sledge towed behind RV Prince Madog. Standard video was recorded onto .VOB files.  GoPro video footage was recorded onto MP4 files. No stills images were collected for this survey.  The arrangement of the videos and cameras is described below: The middle width of the GoPro videos is about 96 cm for the ones that are at an angle (80 at the bottom and \u0026gt;112 at the top) while the ones which face straight down are 80 cm wide. The still camera is a Canon 400D, with field of view of ca. 0.13m2 (44cm by 30cm). The middle width of the videos on the CDs are 41cm.  A manual log was kept of the position and time at the start and end of transect while the camera was on the bottom.  Videos and images were analysed by a marine biological surveyor (Jen Jones) to record information on habitat character, conspicuous species and their abundance.  Habitat descriptions and assignment of biotopes from the national biotope classification to each record were also recorded.  Analysis included a QA process to validate and verify the recorded data.  The surveys are part of a 3-year EFF funded project on habitat mapping and effects of fisheries, particularly scallop dredging.  Part of this dataset is sensitive and restricted. The sensitive data is as follows: Pecten maximus and Aequipecten opercularis.\tRelated Title: Lambert, G.I., Murray L.G., Kaiser M.J., Salomonsen H., Cambie, G. (2013)  Welsh waters scallop survey - Cardigan Bay to Liverpool Bay July - August 2013. Bangor University, Fisheries and Conservation Report No. 30. Bangor University.\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1466) Marine Recorder Database:  2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey Liverpool Bay, August MRCCW31600000014\tGIS Service Desk  Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Custodian: (Mike Kaiser) Dr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:29:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18106\tNRW_DS115575\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.030Z\t322\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Caernarfon Bay (2013)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353735\tSurveys of seabed in Caernarfon Bay, carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS), Bangor University, in July 2013, as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (11 tows) and associated still images collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog, using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was between 8 and 25 km offshore, in the centre of Caernarfon Bay.  The tow lengths ranged from 150m to 500m and seabed consisted primarily of sands and gravels with some pebbles and cobbles.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes. The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats, as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging.  The aim of the subsequent data capture was to improve CCW\u0027s seabed habitat mapping for Caernarfon Bay and assign biotopes.\tDrop-down video tows were carried out with various combinations of a standard resolution digital video camera, a GoPro video camera and a stills camera attached to a sledge towed behind RV Prince Madog. Standard video was recorded onto .VOB files.  GoPro video footage was recorded onto MP4 files. Stills camera images are .JPG files.  The arrangement of the videos and cameras is described below: The middle width of the GoPro videos is about 96cm for the ones that are at an angle (80 at the bottom and \u0026gt;112 at the top) while the ones which face straight down are 80 cm wide. The still camera is a Canon 400D, with field of view of ca. 0.13m2 (44cm by 30cm). The middle width of the videos on the CDs are 41 cm. A manual log was kept of the position and time at the start and end of transect while the camera was on the bottom. Videos and images were analysed by a marine biological surveyor (Jen Jones) to record information on habitat character, conspicuous species and their abundance.  Habitat descriptions and assignment of biotopes from the national biotope classification to each record were also recorded.  Analysis included a QA process to validate and verify the recorded data.  The surveys are part of a 3-year EFF funded project on habitat mapping and effects of fisheries, particularly scallop dredging.  Part of this dataset is sensitive and restricted. The sensitive data is as follows: Pecten maximus and Aequipecten opercularis.\tLambert, G.I., Murray L.G., Kaiser M.J., Salomonsen H., Cambie, G. (2013)  Welsh waters scallop survey - Cardigan Bay to Liverpool Bay July-August 2013. Bangor University, Fisheries and Conservation Report No. 30. Bangor University.\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1466) Marine Recorder survey: 2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Caernarfon Bay, July MRCCW31600000016\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Custodian: (Mike Kaiser) Dr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences,  Bangor University.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:11.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18115\tNRW_DS115576\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.103Z\t554\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Tremadog Bay (2013)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353736\tSurveys of seabed in Tremadog Bay, carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS), Bangor University, in July 2013, as part of European Fisheries Fund (EFF) funded research into fisheries and seabed habitats.  This dataset is derived only from drop-down video (1 tow) and associated still images collected by SOS from the RV Prince Madog, using a sledge with combined video cameras and stills camera.  The survey area was about 6 km offshore of Abersoch.  The tow length was 430m and seabed consisted primarily of coarse sand.  The videos were analysed by Coastal Assessment, Liaison and Monitoring (CALM) to describe the seabed habitats and communities and assign them to biotopes. The main aim of the survey work by the School of Ocean Sciences was to assess the characteristics and status of offshore benthic habitats, as part of a study on the effects of scallop dredging.  The aim of the subsequent data capture was to improve CCW\u0027s seabed habitat mapping for Tremadog Bay and assign biotopes. High - experienced surveyors followed by experienced video and stills analysis.\tDrop-down video tows were carried out with a GoPro video camera and a stills camera attached to a sledge towed behind RV Prince Madog. GoPro video footage was recorded onto MP4 files. Stills camera images are .JPG files.  The arrangement of the videos and cameras is described below: The middle width of the GoPro videos is about 96cm for the ones that are at an angle (80 at the bottom and \u0026gt;112 at the top) while the ones which face straight down are 80 cm wide. The still camera is a Canon 400D, with field of view of ca. 0.13m2 (44cm by 30cm). The middle width of the videos on the CDs are 41cm. A manual log was kept of the position and time at the start and end of transect while the camera was on the bottom. Videos and images were analysed by a marine biological surveyor (Jen Jones) to record information on habitat character, conspicuous species and their abundance.  Habitat descriptions and assignment of biotopes from the national biotope classification to each record were also recorded.  Analysis included a QA process to validate and verify the recorded data. The surveys are part of a 3-year EFF funded project on habitat mapping and effects of fisheries, particularly scallop dredging.\t\tArchived as Project 429, Media 1466 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1466. Marine Recorder: 2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Tremadog Bay, July MRCCW31600000017\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Custodian: (Mike Kaiser)  Dr Gwladys Lambert, School of Ocean Sciences,  Bangor University.\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:29:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18105\tNRW_DS115577\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.031Z\t635\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: White Clawed Crayfish in River Wye SAC (2003)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353737\tThe inclusion of the white-clawed crayfish on Annex II of the EU Habitats and Species Directive requires Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to identify Special Areas of Conservation for the species and to ensure that Favourable Conservation Status (FCS) applies. The purpose of this data capture was to undertake monitoring of the white-clawed crayfish and its habitat on the River Wye candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) in order to report on condition as part of the assessment of Favourable Conservation Status for Natura 2000 features.\tCondition monitoring followed the Common Standards approach outlined by the Joint Nature Conservation Committee. Eight monitoring units (Afon Duhonw, Afon Edw, Afon Irfon, Afon Llynfi, Clyro Brook, Dulas Brook [Builth Wells], Nant yr Qffeiriad and Sgithwen Brook) were sampled following the protocol given by Peay (2002; see http://www.english-nature.org.uk/LIFEinUKRivers/monitoring.html). A total of 16 x 500m stretches were selected randomly within each monitoring unit. Starting from the downstream end of the stretch, a 100m sampling site should be selected within each stretch which contains five suitable habitat patches measuring from 1 to 20m2. Within each habitat patch, 10 potential refuges (large cobble or boulder \u0026gt;15cm along longest axis) should be searched and the number of crayfish recorded.  For each crayfish found, a number of attributes were recorded and a detailed record made of the physical attributes of all habitat patches sampled. The white-clawed crayfish is a proposed feature on the River Wye cSAC. Surveys in 1995 and 2002 have helped to determine its status and distribution, and identified the most important tributaries (Rogers and Holdich, 1995; Rogers and Watson, 2003), and this information has enabled the setting of provisional thresholds for condition assessment.\t\tHeld at H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports and Datasets\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW.  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18097\tNRW_DS115578\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.846Z\t388\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: European Dry Heath in Cwm Doethie-Mynydd Mallaen candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2003)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353738\tThe Cwm Doethie-Mynydd Mallaen candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) supports dry heath and sessile oak woodland, both of which are features of international importance. This dataset includes data collected during a survey and monitoring programme of the dry heath feature at Cwm Doethie-Mynydd Mallaen cSAC, undertaken in 2003 and 2004.  The purpose of the contract was to assess the condition, extent and distribution of the dry heath or potential dry heath within the boundary of the cSAC and to monitor the condition of selected plots against a set of performance indicators. The monitoring result would establish the present condition of the feature.\tSurvey methodology consisted of a desk-study to identify potential search areas followed by site survey. The mapping was carried out primarily in October 2003 by R. Birch, with assistance for remote areas from K. Baukham; a small additional area was surveyed in January 2004. Homogeneous stands of heath were mapped in the field onto 1:10,000 scale ortho-rectified aerial photographs. Digital photographs of some stands were taken and these are included as target notes. Target notes were also taken of scattered bracken Pteridium aquilinum; scattered scrub; heavily disturbed areas and rare or notable species. A hand-held GPS was used to mark the locations of each target note. The condition of each stand was described using condition classes provided by CCW.  The course followed in each plot was produced as an overlay in MapInfo (Mynydd Mallaen Dry Heath GPS_route). Each monitoring plot was then confirmed or reassessed as the category of heath mapped at original survey. The field mapping was completed between October 2003 and January 2004, and the data transferred to GIS.  Monitoring plots against performance indicators included the following methodology; Eight monitoring plots across the site within selected vegetation stands were used to undertake sampling points whereby the vegetation was assessed within a 1metre radius against the criteria defining good quality dry heath.\t\tAssociated data is held on the H:/ at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\03 Cwm Doethie European Dry heath\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:32:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18108\tNRW_DS115580\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.968Z\t420\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Northern Atlantic Wet Heath, European Dry Heath and Raised Bog Habitats within Cernydd Carmel candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353830\tThis dataset includes data collected from an assessment of the extent and condition of three Annex I habitats of the EC Habitats Directive at the Cernydd Carmel cSAC; namely North Atlantic Wet Heaths with Erica tetralix, European Dry Heaths (all subtypes) and Active Raised Bogs. Two principle objectives were defined: 1. To carry out an initial condition survey of the Annex I listed habitats using condition classes provided by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff 2. To monitor the condition of the Annex I listed habitats against performance indicator criteria provided by former CCW staff\tCondition monitoring was undertaken within a number of permanent plots placed amongst the three Annex 1 habitat types. Plots were located in areas dominated by the target habitat (or its precursors) and fine-scale mosaics of different broad habitats were in general avoided. Twelve monitoring plots were established, within which 1 m radius sampling points were recorded.  As far as possible, recording of samples was done on a regular grid pattern. All sample points within the plots were located by pacing either 10 m or 20 m intervals, following compass bearings appropriate to the orientation of the plot. At each 1 m radius sample point various performance indicator species/condition classes were recorded on pre-prepared recording sheets. Following the recording of sample points, a brief description of the vegetation of each monitoring plot was made.\t\tAssociated data is held on the H:/ at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\04 Erica Tetralix Cernydd Carmel cSAC\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:04.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18098\tNRW_DS115581\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.909Z\t571\tValid\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the Afon Teifi Catchment Area (2004)\tMonitro ACA: Dyfrgwn yn Nalgylch Afon Teifi\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353831\tThis dataset was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales (Aberystwyth) to investigate the current and potential breeding success of the otter (Lutra lutra) in the Afon Teifi Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and catchment; report on the present condition and conservation status of the species; define conservation objectives; and to design an efficient monitoring methodology for future reporting. The aims of the project are: a) To report on the current and potential distribution and breeding success of the otter in the Afon Teifi cSAC and catchment; b) To produce conservation objectives using data obtained from the project; c) To design an efficient monitoring methodology for future reporting.\tAn assessment of the current distribution of otters within the Teifi catchment is based mainly on the results of the latest Otter Survey of Wales (2004) with additional information from otter sightings and road casualty records. The methodology for this study is based on work carried out for the European Commission\u0027s Life Nature Programme, to investigate the ecological requirements for otter breeding. Information on otter breeding activity is derived from otter road deaths, and from reports of otter cub sightings (collected during visits to householders living close to watercourses). Possible and potential breeding sites were identified from Phase 1 maps, and by walk-over surveys of all possible sites to confirm breeding site potential. A similar method was used to locate potential amphibian feeding sites for otters. To provide an overview of the distribution and juxtaposition of sites (sightings; breeding and feeding sites) rivers are divided into kilometre lengths and each site assigned to the relevant kilometre.\t\tData is held at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\05 Otter in the Afon Teifi\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T07:45:44.054Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18102\tNRW_DS115583\t2024-05-28T12:39:54.025Z\t688\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDistribution of the Invasive Non Native Species (INNS) American slipper limpet Crepidula fornicata in Conwy Estuary (2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353833\tThe Menai Strait and Conwy estuary area is a designated Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in Wales. The Habitats Directive establishes that the management of SACs should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I habitats and Annex II species. Crepidula fornicata, the American slipper limpet is an invasive non native species in Wales. The slipper limpet occurs on a variety of substrates, it is often found attached to the shells of mussels, Mytilus edulis as well as oysters, stones and other shells around the low water mark. This species is found in south Wales, especially the Milford Haven area, with few records north of the Haven. The Menai Strait mussel fishery in north Wales is the largest in the UK. The mussel ‘seed’ is imported from outside the area. The problem with importing the seed from elsewhere is that there is the risk of accidentally introducing the invasive slipper limpet. This was the case in 2006 when C. fornicata escaped into the Menai Strait. The purpose of this data capture was to assess spread of Crepidula fornicate in North Wales by assessing the Conwy estuary.\tIntertidal Sampling The mussel beds were sampled between 8th August and 9th August 2013 during the early morning low tides. 34 random site locations were chosen and at each site 3 x 0.25m squared quadrats were sampled giving a total of 102 quadrat samples. The substratum, mussel percentage, barnacle percentage and presence of C. fornicata were recorded on a proforma recording sheet. Site locations were recorded using the GPS and then imported into the GIS software package, MapInfo. A number of photos were taken on site. Pictures included general location views together with pictures of quadrats and other subjects thought to be relevant at the time of survey.  Subtidal Sampling A 0.1m squared day grab was deployed from Mersey Guardian, an Environment Agency survey vessel, at each site. The day grabs were originally organised for Water Framework Directive monitoring. In some cases however, the grab jaws were not able to shut fully due to the substrate being coarse (cobbles, gravel and sand with many large mussels) and jamming the grab. In these unusable grab samples, hand searching through the material was conducted quickly, specifically looking for C. fornicata. 33 of these grab samples were checked for the invasive species.\t\tData is archived as project 442, media 1482 The file path for the report: H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Non-native species\\Alien Crepidula   The file path for the data and analyses: H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Non-native species\\Alien Crepidula\\Data and GIS  The file path for the archive: W:\\Media1401to1500\\Media1482\tInternal Custodian: Ben Wray, Marine Monitoring Officer, MFSG.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-08-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18114\tNRW_DS115584\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.979Z\t260\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Monitoring Raised Mire Vegetation at Cors Caron candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2004)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353834\tThis dataset was collected at Cors Caron candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC), in partial fulfilment of former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Contract FC 73-05-35 \\Monitoring of Raised Mire Vegetation at Cors Fochno and Cors Caron cSAC\\\". A second report outlines the results of monitoring work undertaken at Cors Fochno. The contract was commissioned to aid the assessment of the extent and condition of three Annex 1 habitats listed in the EC \\\"Habitats Directive\\\" (Council of the European Communities 1992): Degraded raised bogs still capable of natural regeneration; Transition mires and quaking bogs; Depressions on peat substrates of the Rhynchosporion;\"\tTo monitor the Rhynchosporion habitat on the West Bog, a series of three carefully located 100m x 100m plots were established.  A 10 metre sampling interval gave a total of 100 sampling points per plot.  For the degraded raised bog, a total of five 20 m x 20 m plots, sampled at 5 metre intervals gave a total of 25 sample points per plot.  The Transition mire was monitored by three 50 m x 50 m plots, sampled at 10 metre intervals, again equating to a total of 25 sample points per plot. The data collected were then analysed against tables of performance indicator criteria drawn-up by former CCW for the three Annex 1 habitat types. The work follows on from NVC mapping undertaken by Averis (2001).\t\tAssociated data is held within the folloring location within Maes-y-Ffynnon\u0027s IT Sytem; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\06 Raised mire vegetation Cors Caron cSAC\tInternal custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:46.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18113\tNRW_DS115587\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.984Z\t605\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Vertigo geyeri in Waun Eurad and Cors Erddreiniog SACs (2003-2007)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353837\tGeyers whorl Snail, Vertigo geyeri is listed on Annex II of the European Union Habitats and Species Directive, classified in the British Red Data Book as RDB1 (Endangered), and is on the list of priority species of the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP). The purpose of this study was to undertake monitoring of the Vertigo geyeri population at Waun Eurad and Cors Erddreiniog, in order to carry out a condition assessment and to determine the attributes that will be assessed by future monitoring, which is a requirement of the EU Habitats and Species Directive.\tThe monitoring of condition followed the common standards approach outlined by the Joint Nature Conservation Committee, and the methodology and approach was an adaptation of the monitoring protocol developed for a cSAC in northern England. Condition of the snail population and its habitat at Waun Eurad was carried out by measuring environmental variables and snail numbers at intervals along two linear transects (64m and 105m in length). The attributes used to assess Condition were:  - Area of occupancy of Vertigo geyeri habitat in both transects  - Area of occupancy of Vertigo geyeri in both transects - Number of overall positive recordings per number of field samples  - Hydrological field assessment Condition at Cors Erddreiniog was assessed by a survey of the compartments of the site where V. geyeri had been previously recorded or supported suitable habitat, plus a more detailed survey of the area where V. geyeri had been recorded most often. The attributes used to assess Condition were as based on area of occupancy of snail habitat, area of occupancy of snail, number of overall positive recordings per number of field samples, and hydrological field assessment.\t\tAssociated data for 2003 is held within Maes-y-Ffynnon\u0027s IT systems as H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\08 Vertigo Geyeri Cors Waun Eurad \u0027ampisand\u0027 Cors Erddreiniog  Associated data for 2007 monitoring is held at Maes-y-Ffynnon at - H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\42 Vertigo Geyeri Cors Waun Eurad \u0026amp; Cors Erddreiniog\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:03.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18096\tNRW_DS115594\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.845Z\t319\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Vertigo moulinsiana in Cors Geirch, Corsydd Llyn/Lleyn Fens cSAC (2004)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353934\tDesmoulin\u0027s whorl snail, Vertigo moulinsiana, is listed in the British Red Data Book as an RDB3 (Rare) species, is scheduled on Annex II of the European Habitats and Species Directive and is included on the UK Biodiversity Action Plan short-list of priority species. The purpose of this study was to undertake monitoring of the Vertigo moulinsiana population at Cors Geirch in order to determine the thresholds necessary to carry out a condition assessment, which is a requirement of the EU Habitats and Species Directive. The only data held aside from the associated report is photographic images that are also held within the report itself.\tThe monitoring of favourable condition followed the Common Standards approach outlined by the Joint Nature Conservation Committee, as adapted for the LIFE in UK River\u0027s project.  Condition of the snail population and its habitat was carried out by measuring environmental variables and snail numbers at 5m intervals along two linear transects (150m and 40m in length).  The results were logged onto Standard Recording Forms as developed for the LIFE Protocol. The attributes used to assess Condition were:  - Area of occupancy of Vertigo moulinsiana in both transects  - Population density of Vertigo moulinsiana in both transects - Vegetation species class - Ground moisture levels\t\tThe associated data is held on the H:/ at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\09 Vertigo moulinsiana  in Cors Geirch, Corsydd Llyn\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:23:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18092\tNRW_DS115595\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.157Z\t643\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Freshwater Pearl Mussel on the Afon Eden cSAC (2003 and 2011)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135353935\tFreshwater Pearl mussels are protected under national and international legislations. Afon Eden Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is the only site in Wales where pearl mussel forms the primary reason for selection of the Special Area of Conservation. The data forms part of ongoing monitoring of this site. The Afon Eden was surveyed to assess the current population status of Freshwater Pearl Mussel compared to that of a previous survey in 1997. The aim of this project was to undertake monitoring of the freshwater pearl mussel on the Afon Eden candidate (SAC) in order to be able to report on the condition as a requirement of the EC Habitats and Species Directive. There have been two monitoring cycles since the work in 1997, both in 2003 and 2011. The only data held for monitoring undertaken in 2003 are photographs taken of the site.\tThis project follows on from a baseline survey of the Afon Eden in 1997.  Monitoring of the condition for each candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) feature follows the Common Standards approach outlined by the Joint Nature Conservation Committee, as implemented by CCW.  An initial assessment of the LIFE Protocol was carried out based on the results of the 1997 survey and a brief re-survey of selected sections of river.  This assessment principally focused on establishing the mussel population density and distribution.  These collected data were then used to determine whether the protocol was applicable to the Afon Eden.  In 2003 eleven fixed monitoring transects were established within different sections of the river. Transects were repeat monitored in 2011. The protocol recommends that rivers with known mussel populations should be assessed by 50 m long, 1 m wide transects.  The criteria includes a total mussel count, or estimate (if the number is \u0026gt;250); a detailed search of five 1m2 quadrats that includes invasive sampling for juveniles; measurement of 150 randomly chosen individuals; environmental features - substrate, algal cover, bankside vegetation, flow characteristics and other factors (e.g. evidence of pearl fishing, sheep dip etc).\t\tMonitoring data for 2003 and 2011 cycles are held in the Marine Archive as Project No. 336, Media No. 1302.  Paper survey forms for 2011 monitoring are stored in regional registered files (Meirionnydd SH73.5.24)\tFreshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, NRW. Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T13:30:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18100\tNRW_DS115601\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.917Z\t461\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: \u0027European Dry Heath\u0027, \u0027Northern Atlantic Wet Heath with Erica tetralix\u0027 and \u0027Blanket Bog\u0027 at the Cadair Idris candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2004)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363031\tThis dataset details the findings of a two part project to assess the condition of the European dry heath, wet heath and blanket bog habitats within the Cadair Idris candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) . The work was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), and carried out by Central Environmental Surveys (CES) during November 2003.  The purpose of this data capture was to map the dry heath, wet heath and blanket bog habitats, including condition classes of these, within the Cadair Idris cSAC to a uniform specification.\tIn the first phase of the project the targeted vegetation types were mapped according to a uniform set of criteria. Following the completion of the mapping phase, a second phase of the project involved laying a series of permanent plots for monitoring the condition of the vegetation within selected mapping units. The latter also involved taking baseline recordings of these plots. The type, location and size of these monitoring plots was determined during discussions with Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff prior to commencing the second, monitoring phase.  During the mapping phase of the project the vegetation was assessed by dividing each of the three principal (Annex I) habitat categories into sub-categories which highlight the main impacts on vegetation condition, such as bracken invasion, dominance of negative indicators such as Juncus squarrosus, Molinia or Eriophorum vaginatum, or impacts of specific events such as heather burn. The survey region was mapped using these categories.  The mapping phase of the survey was carried out over a three week period between the 11th and the 29th of November 2003 and was undertaken by experienced surveyors.\tThere were existing surveys of parts of the site, ranging from Phase I habitat surveys to a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of the National Nature Reserve (NNR) see metadata entry \u0027Upland vegetation survey of Cadair Idris NNR\u0027. These employed various classifications, and a uniform survey was required to provide specific information designed to the meet the needs of habitat condition assessment and management.\tThe associated data is on the H Drive at;  H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\11 European dry heath, N. Atlantic wet heath at Cadair Idris\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18091\tNRW_DS115607\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.092Z\t629\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Monitoring Raised Mire Vegetation at Cors Fochno candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2004)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363037\tThis dataset has been obtained through monitoring of Raised Mire Vegetation at Cors Fochno candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC).  The primary objectives were to map the extent and then assess the condition of three Annex 1 habitats (of the EC Habitats and Species Directive), namely \u0027Active Raised Bog\u0027 and the \u0027Depressions on peat substrates of the Rhynchosporion\u0027 (Rhynchosporion habitat) that forms part of it, together with \u0027Degraded Raised Bog\u0027. Assumed to be high as undertaken by experienced Surveyors.\tHabitat mapping was undertaken between 10th and 16th September 2003.  Vegetation monitoring comprised the establishment and recording of seven 100 m x 100 m plots situated upon the \u0027Active Raised Bog\u0027 surface; four from within the \u0027core\u0027 area of \u0027Rhynchosporion vegetation\u0027; three outside of it.  A further four plots, 50 m x 50 m in size, were established and recorded from within stands of \u0027Degraded Raised Bog\u0027.\t\tData is held on the H:/ at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18093\tNRW_DS115608\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.174Z\t386\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Petalwort Petalophyllum ralfsii at Y Twyni o Abermenai i Aberffraw cSAC (2004)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363038\tPetalwort Petalophyllum ralfsii is a pale green thalloid liverwort with erect lamellae on its upper surface, that grows in open, damp, calcareous dune slacks, often on low hummocks rather than on the very wet ground, on compacted sandy/muddy bryophyte-rich turf.  The inclusion of the Petallophyllum ralfsii on Annex II of the EU Habitats and Species Directive requires NRW to identify Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) for the species and to ensure that Favourable Conservation Status (FCS) applies. Whilst some background work has enabled the identification of  key areas for Petallophyllum ralfsii on the candidate SAC, further information on its current extent, distribution and habitat associates are required. The purpose of this data capture was to map the distribution, density and habitat characteristics of Petalophyllum ralfsii at Twyni o Abermenai i Aberffraw cSAC.  This will enable the targets within the conservation objective to be confirmed and to report on the condition of the Petalophyllum on the site.\tThe area to be covered by the survey is approximately 146 ha in extent distributed across two dune systems; Newborough Warren, 2319 ha and Aberffraw 368 ha (dune slacks to be visited are indicated on the attached maps).  At each location where Petalophyllum is present:  - The presence of Petalophyllum is to be marked on both a 1:10,000 map and the aerial photographs provided. - Where possible GPS locations with at least 15m precision should also be taken. - An estimate of abundance within each area of a slack that supports Petalophyllum should be made on the following scale: 1-5 per m2, 5-20 per m2 20-50 per m2 and \u0026gt;50 per m2.  - The area over which the Petalophyllum is distributed should be given in m2.  - Sketch maps and photographs should be produced showing the location of the Petalophyllum within the slack.  - A 2x2m quadrat should be established in an area representative of the habitat in which Petalpohyllum is found  and its location documented using photographs and the sketch map. The following data should be recorded within each quadrat:- 1. Vegetation height (cm). 2. % bare ground 3. Presence of an algal crust 4. Presence of standing water 5. The plant species within the quadrat and their DOMIN values 6. The number of Petalophyllum thalli\t\tAssociated data is held on the Z Drive at: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\12 Petallophyllum ralfsii at Tywyni o Abermenai i Aberffraw\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18094\tNRW_DS115610\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.174Z\t435\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the Afon Tywi Catchment Area (2005)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363130\tThe former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned this monitoring to assess the condition and conservation status of the European otter Lutra lutra, within the Afon Tywi Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and catchment. The otter, which has made a significant recovery in numbers from a rapid decline in the 1950\u0027s though to the early 1970\u0027s, is still considered to be rare, and across most of Europe is endangered. Indeed in many countries it is now considered extinct, making the populations in Wales all the more significant.  The results of this project, combined with those of similar projects instigated by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) on other SAC river catchments over the past two years, and in future years, will provide a valuable and unique picture of the otter in Wales.\tA variety of methods were used to gather information about otter breeding activity on the catchment. These might broadly be described under the headings of questionnaires, interviews, record searches, and direct contacts with individuals thought likely to hold relevant information. In addition site inspections were carried out, as well as map searches and inspection of Phase 1 data and aerial photographs. Other records were obtained from agencies such as the Countryside Council for Wales and the Environment Agency.  A definition of a breeding site was set out by Liles in his report for the European Commission\u0027s Life Nature Programme to investigate the ecological requirements for breeding otters (Liles, 2003). This criteria was used and Using the information gathered above, a list of some 112 sites was compiled, and throughout September, October and December 2004, most of these sites were visited and an inspection made of the habitat.  Each river was divided into kilometre lengths in order to provide an easy means of identifying stretches which are of particular importance to otters (sightings, breeding and feeding sites). From this each breeding site or feeding pond were ascribed to a particular kilometre stretch.\t\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at  Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\17 Otter in the Afon Tywi\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:02:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18095\tNRW_DS115614\t2024-05-28T12:39:53.141Z\t303\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Southern Damselfly (Coenagrion mercuriale) on candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (cSAC\u0027s) in Wales (2005)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363134\tFollowing Part 1 of the project to carry out an assessment of favourable condition for Southern Damselfly Coenagrion mercuriale (Boyce, 2004) the author was contracted by the former Countryside Council for Wales to continue with the condition assessment at further sites in Pembrokeshire and elsewhere in Wales on Candidate Special Areas of Conservation. Each site was assessed to examine whether Favourable Condition had been attained. Sites covered were as follows;  -  Pembrokeshire:  Mynydd Preseli, Gweunydd Blaencleddau (SN13), and Waun Fawr (SN03) -  Glamorgan:  Rhossili Down and Sluxton Farm (SS4290) and Cefn Bryn (SS5090) -  Anglesey:  Cors Erddreiniog (SH4683).\tVisits were timed to coincide with the peak flight period of the damselfly. This can vary from mid-June to early August but in most years the bulk of the adult population is on the wing from early July to early August. All male and female Southern damselflies were recorded using an eight figure grid reference generated by using hand held GPS devices. Two \u0027Garmin Etrex\u0027 and one \u0027Magellan GPS315\u0027 device were used. Both are accurate to plus or minus 10 m, but usually accuracy was much better than this (usually plus or minus 3-5 m). Southern damselfly was recorded from both suitable and unsuitable habitats. Using the same hand held devices grid references were generated for habitat deemed to conform to the criteria for favourable habitat condition as detailed within the project specification.\t\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\18 Southern damselfly on cSAC in Wales (part 2)\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18090\tNRW_DS115615\t2024-05-28T12:39:52.932Z\t245\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the River Usk Catchment Area (2005)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363135\tThe Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned this work to assess the condition and conservation status of the European otter Lutra lutra, within the River Usk Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and catchment. The results of this project, combined with those of similar projects instigated by CCW on other SAC river catchments over the past two years, and in future years, will provide a valuable and unique picture of the otter in Wales.\tA variety of methods were used to gather information about otter breeding activity on the catchment. These might broadly be described under the headings of questionnaires, interviews, record searches, and direct contacts with individuals thought likely to hold relevant information. In addition site inspections were carried out, as well as map searches and inspection of Phase 1 data and aerial photographs. Questionnaires were handed out to land owners, members of the public, who were visited on the tributaries during the survey phase of the project and anglers. All individuals, who returned questionnaires, were subsequently interviewed, mainly during personal visits, or as a minimum, by means of telephone discussion. In addition other interviews occurred as a result of site visits. For further data collation approaches were made to the Brecon Beacons National Park, Brecknock Wildlife Trust, Brecknock Otter Group, the Environment Agency, the Forestry Commission, Gwent Wildlife Trust, the Ministry of Defence, Monmouthshire County Council, Newport City Council and the Biodiversity Information Service for Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park. Otter breeding sites were identified on the basis of criteria developed by Liles in his report for the European Commission\u0027s Life Nature Programme to investigate the ecological requirements for breeding otters (Liles, 2003). In addition approaches were also made to several individuals, including the County Mammal Recorder for Monmouthshire. All of the records gathered were used as part of the process of identifying breeding sites and sites which might be used for breeding. Using the information gathered above, a list possible breeding sites was compiled, and throughout September and November 2004, and January 2005, most of these sites were visited and an inspection made of the habitat.\t\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at:- Z\\:Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027AMPISAND\u0027 Datasets\\19 Otter in the River Usk\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:47.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18089\tNRW_DS115616\t2024-05-28T12:39:52.926Z\t820\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Greater Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum) in the Wye Valley (2005)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363136\tThe Wye Valley and Forest of Dean Bats Sites Special Area of Conservation (SAC), is considered one of the best areas in the UK for both Greater Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum) and Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros). The activity patterns of greater horseshoe bats roosting at Newton Court Stable Block Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) were investigated over a ten-day period during August 2004 in order to ascertain key foraging areas, flight paths and night roosting sites. Ten bats, eight lactating females and two juvenile males, were radio-tagged and followed for the duration of the study period. Greena Ecological Consultancy was commissioned by the Countryside Council for Wales to identify and assess the condition of principal flight routes and key foraging areas used by the colony, in order to develop Performance Indicators. A condition survey of the actual and potential flight lines within 500 m of the Newton Court Stable Block was carried out, a paper map and MapInfo file has been compiled allowing future condition monitoring by the lengths of flight features present.\tHorseshoe bats from the Newton Court Stable Block were radio tagged and tracked over a 10-day period. Bats were fitted with 0.57 and 0.7g 173 MHz radio transmitters, manufactured by Biotrack of Dorset, with a specified minimum 10-day battery life. All captured bats were weighed, sexed, measured and examined to ascertain breeding condition or whether they were juveniles. Also where bats were ringed numbers were recorded.  The two fieldworkers used Australis 26K and Sika receivers with Yaggi rigid aerials to track bats and whip omni-directional antennas to search for bats by vehicle. Accurate bearings of bat locations were taken from hand held sighting compasses. The surveyors also used Duet bat detectors on occasion to confirm the presence of non-tagged greater horseshoe bats by listening for their characteristic echolocation calls.  Each time a tagged bat was detected, the following data was recorded: Time or time period, Bat ID number, Bat location bearing/ range of bearings, Initials of surveyor taking a bearing at the same time for triangulation, Surveyor grid reference, Surveyor location description, Signal strength, Comments regarding bat behaviour. Within the confirmed foraging areas habitat categories / features were condition mapped. The condition score indicates many features are present within the precise places bats use and the immediately adjacent habitats. Future condition assessment could then determine if there has been a deterioration in habitat quality (indicated by a reduction in the number of features) or an improvement (shown by an increase in the number of features present). The actual and potential condition areas (with number of feature scores) are included in the habitat maps. Bat count data has been collected for this colony during spring and summer every year since 1991 but additional information was required on habitat and flight corridors around the roost and of the surrounding area.\t\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\20 Greater horseshoe bats in the Wye Valley\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T09:13:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18088\tNRW_DS115617\t2024-05-28T12:39:52.808Z\t682\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: European Dry Heath, North Atlantic Wet Heath and Rhynchosporion Habitats in Preseli SAC (2005)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363137\tThe collection of this dataset was commissioned by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to monitor the condition of the Northern Atlantic wet heath with Erica tetralix, European dry heath and Rhynchosporion habitats within the Special Area of Conservation (SAC). The original aim of the project was to monitor the condition of some of the Annex I habitats for which the site (Preseli SAC) has been designated against performance indicators provided by CCW. These habitats are Northern Atlantic wet heaths with Erica tetralix (H4010), European dry heaths (H4030) and Depressions on peat substrates of the Rhynchosporion (H7150). The monitoring follows on from a detailed habitat survey of the site undertaken jointly by CCW\u0027s Phase II survey team and under contract by earlier in 2005.\tMonitoring was undertaken within a series of 25 sample plots the approximate locations of which were provided by CCW on the basis of the earlier habitat survey. At each sampling point the vegetation was assessed against criteria set out in Performance Indicator tables provided by CCW, which taken together provide a point based definition of desired habitat quality. During September 2004, a team of two surveyors visited the site and set up 5 plots to monitor the condition stands of \u0027Depressions on peat substrates of the Rhynchosporion\u0027. The remaining plots: 7 sampling stands of \u0027European Dry Heath\u0027, 12 sampling typical wet heath and one sampling a site specific type of wet heath rich in Ulex gallii, were set up on a second visit in January 2005. On both occasions sampling points were located using GPS systems.\t\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027AMPISAND\u0027 Datasets\\21 European dry heath within the Preseli SAC\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:25:48.987Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18087\tNRW_DS115618\t2024-05-28T12:39:52.783Z\t273\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Protection Area (SPA) Monitoring: Bird features within Welsh SPAs (2005)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363138\tThis datasets presents a preliminary exploration of the potential use of existing data sets to inform assessments of the condition of Special Protection Area (SPA) features across Wales. The approach adopted for this study was to assess each species/assemblage in the individual SPAs. The most recent list of SPAs in Wales (UK SPA review) contains inconsistencies in the qualifying species/assemblage features when comparisons are made between the Natura 2000 datasheets, the SPA review, site documentation and the SPA review - species documentation.  The aim of this data capture was to: 1 To produce draft performance targets for bird features within Welsh Special Protection Areas, in accordance with the JNCC Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance for birds. 2 To make a preliminary assessment of whether performance targets have been met for the 6 year JNCC report. 3 To identify where existing data are insufficient to report on the performance targets.\tThe only data held is that within the report itself.  This assessment involved four stages: 1 Identification of the features 2 Collation of data on the sites and features to allow baseline values to be confirmed 3 Identification of feature targets in line with Joint Nature Conservancy Council (JNCC) Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance for birds 4 Assessment of current condition of each feature to be made and data deficiencies to be identified.\t\tThe Report, which contains the data, is held on the NRW DMS Under the Reports Archive \u0026gt; CCW Marine Environmental Monitoring Reports: -  KNOW-911039132-34 - CCW EMR 22 - A preliminary assessment of the condition of bird features within Welsh Special Protection Areas (2005)\tAdditional Information Contact: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18086\tNRW_DS115619\t2024-05-28T12:39:52.710Z\t474\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: European Dry Heath and Blanket Bog Habitat at the Berwyn a Mynyddoedd De Clwyd / Berwyn and South Clwyd Mountains candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2005)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363139\tThis dataset presents the findings of an investigation into the condition of key habitats in selected management units within the Berwyn and South Clwyd Mountains candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC). The purpose of this data capture was to determine the extent, distribution and condition of the dry heath and blanket bog habitats.\tA two-phase survey was conducted to collect data on the condition of the Annex I habitat types Blanket Bog (H7130) and European Dry Heath (H4030). In the first phase of the project the targeted vegetation types were mapped, the principal output being a digital map of regions distinguished according to both habitat type and condition class. Following this, a second phase of the project involved setting up a series of baseline plots, expressed as regular rectangular grids with equally spaced sampling stations. These plots serve the purpose of assessing whether the sampled vegetation currently meets condition targets in relation to a selected set of performance criteria, and also will serve to assist in long-term monitoring of changes in the habitat condition.\t\tAssociated data is held on the H Drive at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027AMPISAND\u0027 Datasets\\23 European dry heath at Berwyn \u0027AMPISAND\u0027 S Clwyd mountains\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:33.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18145\tNRW_DS115625\t2024-05-28T12:40:37.374Z\t557\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGrey Seal Census for North Wales 2001\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363235\tThe grey seal, Halichoerus grypus, is relatively uncommon globally with estimates of 300,000 individuals across the north Atlantic in 2000. In the British Isles, the grey seal is more common that other species of seal breeding. This dataset contains the results of a full census of grey seal pup production in North Wales for the 2001 season. The aim of this project was to undertake an appropriate level of survey of grey seals in North Wales, from Aberystwyth to the Dee Estuary, in order to lead to a clearer understanding of the abundance and distribution of grey seals and pup production, and thereby to guide management. The objectives were to: - Locate and document all major sites where grey seal haul out for moulting and breeding,  - Confirm the timing and extent of the breeding season - Locate and document all breeding sites in North Wales - Count the number of pups at each of these sites at regular intervals throughout that breeding season in 2001, in order to estimate pup production - Continue the daily pupping census of Bardsey Island - Take photographs of profiles to identify pelage markings of individuals for possible computer-aided matching in the future - Identify areas where seals are subject to disturbance by humans - Assess the quality of breeding haul-out environment - Advise on appropriate levels of monitoring / surveillance.\tINFORMATION WARNING: The dataset within this dataset are wrong. The dates are incorrectly coded as 2010 instead of 2001. The coasts surveyed lie between Aberystwyth in the south to the Dee estuary in the east and includes the coast of Anglesey. In addition to this the coasts of all island adjacent to these coasts are also included. The coastline was split into six sections, The Penrhyn Llyn, Ynys Enlli, Ynys Mon West, Ynys Mon East, Great Orme and Little Orme, The Dee Esturary. On foot surveys were conducted. Wave ski surveys were conducted to reach offshore environments in a more or less silent way, thereby minimising the prospect of causing disturbance to the seals. Sites which were even further offshore or in particularly exposed areas where tidal races occurred,  were accessed via boat.\t\tArchived as Project 417, Media 1484.\tTom Stringell, Senior Marine Mammal Ecologist, Natural Resources Planning/Marine and Freshwater Ecosystem Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18134\tNRW_DS115626\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.845Z\t381\tValid\tEditable\tModelling the Sensitivity of Seabed Habitats to Combined Fishing Effects around Anglesey, North Wales (2011-2013).\tModelu sensitifrwydd cynefinoedd gwaelod mor i effeithiau pysgota cyfun ogylch Mon (2011-2013).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363236\tA pilot study has been conducted to determine whether the theoretical combination of habitat sensitivity to fishing activity of different gear types. This theory was converted into a model, and run using combined fishing activity data, collected through the FishMap Mon pilot project, to predict the sensitivity of habitats.    The objective of this data capture was to determine whether the data on resistance to fishing activity data for each fisheries habitat developed during the NRW habitat sensitivity project could be applied to the impact of cumulative fishing activity on each habitat.   Specific aims of the modelling project were to:  - Calculate footprint versus resistance curves for each fisheries habitat and fishing activity using the revised activity footprints calculated for the FishMap Mon project  - Develop a methodology to model combined fishing effects  - Test the methodology on Red Wharf Bay and Conwy Bay  - Produce an output layer at 1 km grid resolution  - Produce thematic maps    The source GIS data was collated during the FishMap Mon project using a standard questionnaire and post-processing method. The resistance and intensity definitions were supplied by the FishMap Mon project team. The curves were calculated using an automated process to ensure processing was identical for all habitats and visually verified to ensure a good fit.   This project was a pilot to determine feasibility of the use of such models, therefore the output data is suggestive of the habitat sensitivity.  Due to it\u0027s nature as a pilot project NRW no longer makes this data routinely available, alternative fisheries related data may be available on the Welsh Government Marine Planning Portal.\tIntertidal and subtidal habitats sourced from Phase 1 Biotope Mapping surveys and HABMAP projects were converted into 31 predictive fisheries habitats. Fishing intensity was derived from the density of fishing activity in the waters around Anglesey using data from FishMap Mon. 47 interviews were conducted with commercial fishermen, 26 with chartered boat operators and over 500 non-commercial fishermen (anglers, bait collectors, mussel gatherers etc). Each activity was assigned to a gear category and subsequent calculations of the intensity were completed and maps produced based on these values. Each gear type was assessed separately based on the specific intensities associated with specific gear used.     The output model produced four spatial layers representing the following:  - Maximum resistance value for any activity which occurred within each grid square and therefore shows the most optimistic interpretation of the outputs.   - Minimum resistance value for any activity which occurred within each grid square and therefore shows the most pessimistic interpretation of the outputs.  - Average resistance value for any activity which occurred within each grid square. The average resistance is calculated as a proportional average based upon polygon area and therefore represents the average impact on habitats affected by fishing activity.  - The weighted average resistance value for each grid square. The weighted average resistance is calculated as a proportional average between the average resistance of areas affected by activity and the resistance.\tAssociated Report Title: Lush, C. 2014.  Modelling Sensitivity of Seabed Habitats to Combined Fishing Effects. NRW Evidence Reports. Report No; 03/14, 53pp.   This dataset was derived by implementing the theoretical work associated with: Nisbet K, Robinson LA, Frid CL. 2013. Final report for CCW (June 2013). University of Liverpool.\tArchived as project 444, media 1485\tCustodian:  Was Clare Eno, potentially Colin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries,  Marine and Fisheries Welsh Government or Karen Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Habitats, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group may be able to provide information.   Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-03-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:32.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18130\tNRW_DS115627\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.776Z\t371\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring Invertebrate Features on Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs): Tetragnatha striata on Newport Wetlands National Nature Reserve (NNR) Gwent (2013)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363237\tThe spider genus Tetragnatha is represented in Britain by six species: Tetragnatha extensa, T. montana, T. nigrita, T. obtusa, T. pinicola and T. striata. This dataset concerns Tetragnatha striata, a species which is listed as Nationally Notable B. The Spider Recording Scheme holds 322 British records of Tetragnatha striata. However, in Wales, it has only been recorded from five locations: Cors Goch National Nature Reserves (NNR); Conwy RSPB Reserve; Parc Menai, Bangor; Kenfig NNR; Newport Wetlands NNR. It was first discovered at the Newport Wetlands NNR in 2001, when a population was found inhabiting emergent Phragmites at what is now known as Reedbed-6. The spider constructs a small orb-web on reeds, but is highly cryptic, folding itself along a reed stem when at rest.  The data is used to detail the distribution of this feature across the NNR and proposes a Common Standards Monitoring protocol to enable its condition to be monitored and assessed in the future.\tBoat ditch transects The largely linear nature of the NNR’s open water, together with its dense marginal growth of Phragmites, were key factors in choosing to conduct most of the surveying from a boat (flat bottomed punt). This allowed safe and rapid surveying of the known habitat used by Tetragnatha striata – Phragmites emerging from standing water. Initially each egg-sac’s location was recorded by GPS, and its height above water estimated, but this was found to be impractical due to the number of egg-sacs encountered. Thereafter just total counts of egg-sacs and spiders were made for the lengths of the ditches (determined using GIS). Where multiple egg-sacs were deposited in a row, this information was also captured, but still defined as one \u0027deposition site\u0027. Several spider specimens were collected throughout the survey to ensure other Tetragnatha species were not occupying the same habitat, and to verify that the observed egg-sacs were those of Tetragnatha striata (females collected next to egg-sacs).  Foot survey It was not possible to use the boat at some locations on the NNR, due to the shallow water depth and presence of thick mud. In order to confirm the presence of Tetragnatha striata on the eastern lagoons and the small pool within Reedbed-11 waders were used to walk along sections of emergent reed. At the Old Lagoon and the Southern RSPB Reedbed egg-sac investigations were carried out from the bank using binoculars.  Reed shaking It was found that by grabbing an arm-load of attached reeds and shaking it over the water surface, numerous spiders (including T. striata) would be dislodged and fall onto the water surface, from where they could be collected.\tGallon, R. C. 2014. Monitoring Invertebrate features on SSSIs: Tetragnatha striata on Newport Wetlands NNR, Gwent. NRW Evidence Report Report No: 7, 33pp, Natural Resources Wales, Cardiff\tData held at the Gwent Levels Reserve - location to be confirmed\tKevin Dupe, Reserve Manager Newport Wetlands Team Leader, South East Region, NRW Mike Howe, Invertebrate Specialist, MFSG, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18135\tNRW_DS115628\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.799Z\t656\tUnassessed\tEditable\tConservation Management of Hay Meadows in the Elan Valley\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363238\tThe Elan Valley, in mid-Powys, includes some of the most important and richest examples of unimproved mesotrophic grasslands in Wales. The majority of these are highly species-rich upland-fringe meadows that are now mostly designated as Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) due to their national significance. In order to help safeguard the future of these important sites the Elan Valley Meadow Project was initially set up in 2004 to address both the management of the existing species-rich grasslands and their potential expansion. The project was managed and implemented by a partnership between the Elan Valley Trust, Natural Resources Wales (Natural Resources Wales) (formerly Countryside Council for Wales; CCW) and independent ecological consultants. The primary objective of the project was to provide site-specific and ecologically sustainable management advice to help conserve these important and regionally distinct upland-fringe meadows. Specifically there was a need to determine what levels of nutrient inputs are required to enable reinstatement of traditional hay making practices while still conserving existing high levels of floristic diversity. To this end a long-term replicated field trial was undertaken to determine how a representative group of the Elan Valley meadows respond to different inputs of farmyard manure (FYM) and/or lime in terms of soil chemistry, herbage productivity and changes in species diversity.\tThe experimental plots for investigating the application of varying levels of farmyard manure and lime were initially established during autumn 2004. The experiment consisted of a randomised block design of 6 nutrient application treatments in five replicate blocks (sites). The five sites were established in separate meadow enclosures at three different holdings: two at Hirnant, two at Penglaneinon and one at Rhos yr Hafod. Four of the sites were located within homogeneous stands of species-rich MG5a grassland, while one of the Hirnant sites was sited within an area of less species-rich MG6 grassland undergoing meadow restoration management.\tRelevant NRW Evidence Report: No 8: Conservation Management of species-rich grasslands in the Elan Valley, Radnorshire Hayes, M., Lowther. R\tData outputs associated with this project are archived at HP TRIM Record Number: 09/90051 on server–based storage at Natural Resources Wales. (Ffynnon record number (folder) 09/9005) Relevant NRW Evidence Report: No 8: Conservation Management of species-rich grasslands in the Elan Valley, Radnorshire Hayes, M., Lowther. R   Located on the DMS: KNOW-101-8\tContact: Stuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW [M.J. Hayes \u0026amp; R.A. Lowther - originally collected the data.]  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:38:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18128\tNRW_DS115630\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.698Z\t778\tValid\tEditable\tFishMap Môn Project - Anglesey\tProsiect FishMap Môn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363330\tFishMap Môn is a collaborative project between Natural Resources Wales (NRW), the Welsh Government, and the recreational and commercial fisheries sectors. Project Partners include The North Wales Fisherman\u0027s Cooperative Limited, Bangor Mussel Producers Limited and The Welsh Federation of Sea Anglers.   The project sets out to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping fishing activity and subsequently combining this with information on seabed habitats and their sensitivity to such activities. It was jointly funded 75% / 25% between the European Fisheries Fund and the Welsh Government.   The pilot project focuses on an area around Anglesey in North Wales covering the waters out to 12 nautical miles. The Project area is located between Nefyn to the west and the Great Orme to the east and extends out to the Welsh 12 nautical mile limit. It encompasses an area of approximately 265 kmsq, of which approximately 3% are Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) and 3% are Special Protection Areas (SPAs).   The main aim of the project is to survey commercial, recreational and charter boat fishermen, map the distribution and intensity of fishing activity around Anglesey and combine it with seabed habitat maps and their sensitivities to fishing activity.  The project aimed to deliver the following outputs:  - Mapped fishing activity for a range of commercial and recreational fishing activity in the Project area. Maps have been created following surveys of approximately 50 commercial fishers and 600 recreational fishers.  - The FishMap Môn Guidance Tool; an easy to use web based mapping application allowing stakeholders to view fishing activity data or hypothetical fishing scenarios in combination with seabed habitats and their sensitivity.  - An evaluation of how learning from FishMap Môn could be applied to managing the marine environment in Wales.  - A literature review to explore global case studies where an Ecosystem Approach has been applied to managing the marine environment. The review will identify lessons learnt and how they relate to Wales  - Lessons in stakeholder engagement and collaborative working with the fishing industry The project also aimed to develop a trial method for gathering fishing activity data from recreational and commercial fishing communities; and, to develop and test an indicative, web based guidance tool for the pilot area using GIS Techniques.  Due to it\u0027s nature as a pilot project NRW no longer makes this data routinely available, alternative fisheries related data may be available on the Welsh Government Marine Planning Portal.\tFishMap Môn piloted methods to inform the sustainable management of the Welsh marine environment by collecting and mapping information about fishing activity and combining it with existing information on seabed habitat types and their sensitivity to fishing activities using GIS tools.  Commercial and recreational fishers were surveyed during one-to-one interviews or by postal questionnaires during 2011-2012. Participants mapped the locations of their fishing activity using Admiralty Charts or Ordinance Survey Maps and secondly provided details about the characteristics of their fishing to allow fishing intensity to be determined. All the information was recorded onto GIS.    Detailed and generalised layers were produced due to sensitivity of the information, therefore lower resolution GIS layers were produced.  FishMap Mon was undertaken as a pilot project giving a general indication of where a limited amount of fishing activity is thought to have occurred at a given time, and its expected impact on marine habitats. This fishing effort dataset was a snap shot in time and may not be representative of current fishing practices in the study area or the rest of Wales. There is a low level of confidence in the data and trail methodology.\tSix output layers were produced, each of which have their own metadata (See Contains).\tAssociated data is held at:   X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fishmap_Mon  ,  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\FishMap Mon\\Analysis .   Generalised Data may only be available on the legacy CCW GIS archive (M:\\ drive).   For further details please refer to metadata for each of the individual output layers.\tInternal Custodian:  Colin Charman, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries (FishMap Mon Project Manager role no longer exists).   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:14:04.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18138\tNRW_DS115631\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.780Z\t271\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFine Grain Vegetation Monitoring of Yr Wyddfa / Snowdon ECN Site\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363331\tThe Environmental Change Network (ECN) is the UK\u0027s long-term, integrated environmental monitoring and research programme. It gathers information on the responses to environmental change in physical, chemical and biological systems. This is done through a network of UK monitoring sites from which long-term datasets can be used to identify environmental changes and improve understanding of the causes of change. The purpose of this data capture was to investigate environmental change across the ECN site.\tFieldwork was mostly carried out between 25 September and 30 September with the final two plots recorded on 4 October 2005.  There are 18 permanently marked fine-grain vegetation monitoring plots located at Yr Wyddfa/Snowdon, two in each of nine selected National Vegetation Classification communities found at the site. Plots are 10m x 10m in size and contain 10 randomly selected 40cm x 40cm cells. For each cell, the presence of all higher plants, bryophytes and lichens (except for those growing on wood or rock) was recorded. In addition, the presence of bare soil, rock, open water, leaf litter, dead wood and animal dung was also recorded. Leaf litter was defined as the presence of dead plant material in a thatch layer of variable thickness and extent, often from more than one season. For each plot slope, aspect, slope form and land use were recorded. For each cell a number of environmental variables were recorded. These include the presence of features such as animal tracks, walls, ditches, banks, streams and hedges.\t\tAssociated data is held at H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\28 Fine-Grain Vegetation at Snowdon ECN Site at NRW, Maes y Ffynnon.\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, Maes y Ffynnon, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18141\tNRW_DS115633\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.851Z\t759\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the River Wye SAC and catchment area (2006)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363333\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) monitoring provides an assessment of the current and potential distribution and breeding success of the otter within the Wye SAC and to identify features outside the SAC boundary whose condition may influence the viability of the otter meta-population associated with the River Wye.  Results from the most recent Otter Survey of Wales (Jones, 2004) show that otter signs were found within the Welsh part of the Wye catchment at 92% of sites searched. Whilst these signs are a useful indicator of otter distribution, they shed little light on otter breeding. The ability to monitor breeding activity and breeding sites in the catchment is crucial if the species and its habitats are to be maintained in a Favourable Conservation Status. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the current and potential breeding success of the otter within the Wye catchment.\tThe methodology for this study is based on work carried out for the European Commission\u0027s Life Nature Programme, to investigate the ecological requirements for otter breeding (Liles, 2003). Information on otter breeding activity is derived from a number of sources including: otter road deaths; reports of otter cub sightings; and records of orphan otter cubs. Possible and potential breeding sites were identified from Phase 1 maps, and by walk-over surveys of all possible sites to confirm breeding site potential. A similar method was used to locate potential amphibian feeding sites for otters.  In order to make data management and analyses easier, the river system was divided into three sub-catchments; Lower Wye (Chepstow to Monmouth); Middle Wye (Rhydspence to Builth Wells); and Upper Wye (Builth Wells to its source).\t\tAssociated data is held at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\30 Current \u0026amp; pot distribution, cond \u0026amp; breeding success of the otter in the R Wye SAC\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18140\tNRW_DS115634\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.845Z\t687\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros) in the Wye Valley and Forest of Dean Bat Sites SAC\u0027s (2006)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363334\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) monitoring of Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros) in the Wye Valley, commuting route and foraging areas were investigated. The study considered five nursery roosts and one hibernation roost on the Welsh side of the Wales-England border. The roosts are all subject to regular annual monitoring and it was not a requirement of this study to reproduce the monitoring effort. Primary focus of the project was to identify and verify important foraging habitats for lesser horseshoe bats and to assess the condition of commuting routes between roosts and forage areas.  The purpose of this data capture was to assist in the survey each surveyor was issued with a bat detector and each team had one Pettersson D-240x and one Skye Instruments SBR2100 bat detectors available to them. Detectors were set to a frequency of 108 kHz, the \u0027peak\u0027 echolocation frequency for lesser horseshoe bats.\tWhilst in the field each team was given comparative freedom to assess which routes were being used by the bats to fly between roosts and forage areas. Whilst in the field basic data on air temperature was continually recorded. Other data recorded, onto printed maps, was the direction and time of any lesser horseshoe bat passes. Areas where foraging behaviour could be detected and confirmed were also noted. On average two hours were spent in the field recording as much activity as possible.  The actual assessment of each nursery site was done on the basis of deploying two teams of two surveyors in the field on two different evenings. Only evenings with relatively stable weather conditions were used for survey. One of the survey evenings was spent comparatively close to the roost, whilst the second evening was spent further away, usually between 1.5 km and 3 km of the roost.\t\tAssociated data is held at the following location at NRW - Maes y Ffynnon- H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\31 Monitoring the Welsh colonies of Lesser horseshoe Bats in the Wye Valley.\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Custodian: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18127\tNRW_DS115635\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.710Z\t650\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the Afon Mawddach catchment area (2006)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363335\tThe Afon Mawwdach catchment forms part of two Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) the Afon Eden SAC and the Pen Llyn a Sarnau SAC. Current and potential distribution, and breeding success of the otter were investigated, as well as identifying and mapping potential and actual breeding habitat and feeding ponds and ditches. A major focus of this project is an assessment of the current and potential breeding success of the otter within the catchment, so that conservation objectives can be set, and future trends can be identified.\tThe methodology for this study is based on work carried out for the European Commission\u0027s Life Nature Programme, to investigate the ecological requirements for otter breeding. Information on otter breeding activity is derived from otter road deaths, and from reports of otter cub sightings (collected during visits to householders living close to watercourses). Possible and potential breeding sites were identified from Phase 1 maps, and by walk-over surveys of all possible sites to confirm breeding site potential. A similar method was used to locate potential amphibian feeding sites for otters. The Mawddach system was divided into 4 sub-catchments: Mawddach Estuary; Afon Mawddach (fresh water); Afon Wnion; and Afon Eden.\t\tAssociated data is held at Maes y Ffynnon at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\32 Current \u0026amp; Pot dist, condition of the otter in the Afon Mawddach catchment\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18137\tNRW_DS115636\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.844Z\t537\tValid\tEditable\tThe Feasibility of the Eradication of the Non Native Species Devil\u0027s Tongue Weed (Grateloupia turuturu) in Wales - Milford Haven Survey July 2013\tDichonoldeb dileu Grateloupia turuturu yng Nghymru - arolwg Aberdaugleddau Gorffennaf 2013\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363336\tGrateloupia turuturu, Devil\u0027s tongue weed is a Non Native Species (NNS) now found in the UK. Devil’s tongue weed grows in sheltered areas including harbours and bays, attached to pontoons, harbour walls, shells and stones. It occurs on the lower shore in pools, and subtidally to 7 m.  Due to its tolerance of low salinities it may be found in estuarine areas as well as fully marine waters.    A survey was undertaken to search for and record the presence of G. turuturu within Neyland marina and the surrounding Milford Haven area.  The results showed that 38% of the pontoons and other floating structures surveyed in Neyland marine and wider Milford Haven waterway had a presence of G. turuturu. It is postulated that Neyland marina provides a suitable environment for reproduction of G. turuturu and introduction may have occurred through ship ballast water and/or the import of live shellfish associated with local aquaculture.   The purpose of this survey was to assess the extent of dispersion of G. turuturu in Neyland marina and the wider Milford Haven waterway with the purpose of establishing whether eradication of this species was feasible. Information on the extent, density and dispersion of G. turuturu in the Milford Haven waterway may also influence future management responses to this and other marine non-native species.  Recording the extent of G. turuturu informs future management response and helps decide the feasibility of eradicating the species.\tPontoons and other floating structures were surveyed for the species. This involved people snorkelling around the structures and recording the presence.  22 pontoons at Neyland marina including all of the 184 finger pontoons were surveyed by teams of 2 people. Samples were also taken for further laboratory identification.\tJennings, C. Wray, B. 2014. Surveillance of Grateloupia turuturu in Wales – Milford Haven July 2013. NRW Evidence Report No: 17, 31pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.  Species data is published and available to download from the NBN Atlas as part of the NRW \"Marine Non Native Species records from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Monitoring Research and Ad-hoc Sightings\"(NRW_DS116352)\tArchived as project 445, media 1486. Marine Recorder,  Survey_ID: MRCCW30000000092\tCustodian Contact: Ben Wray Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecology, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Data ServicesTeam\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2014-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T12:29:51.035Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18132\tNRW_DS115638\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.829Z\t259\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater Macrophytes in Nant Teyrn - Freshwater Environmental Change Protocol (2006)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363338\tScott Wilson was commissioned to undertake a macrophyte survey of Nant Teyrn in Snowdonia National Park. The aim of this survey was to provide macrophyte monitoring data on a 100m stretch of Nant Teyrn within the Snowdonia Environmental Change Network (ECN) terrestrial site. The following elements were recorded: ·  Macrophyte species present and their relative abundance with reference to the check list of aquatic macrophytes used in ECN. Macrophytes include any plant observable by the naked eye and nearly always identifiable when observed.This includes all aquatic vascular plants and bryophytes, together with groups of algae which can be seen to be composed predominantly of a single species ·  Species distribution along a river length and zonation at selected sample points. ·  Physical characteristics of the sampling location\tThe macrophyte survey covered a 50 m length of the river immediately upstream of the ECN chemical sample point and 50 metres immediately downstream of the weir. The two 50 m survey sections were measured accurately along the left bank using a tape measure to ensure ease of future replication. Each 50 m length was divided into five 10m units using ranging poles and a field assistant to mark the end points of each unit.  Percentage cover and relative abundance estimates were made for all species of floating and submerged macrophytes contained within the river channel area. All such macrophytes seen submerged or partly submerged at low flow levels were recorded. At the sides of the river all macrophytes growing on parts of the substrata that are likely to be submerged for more than 85% of the time were also included. Percentage cover estimates were made for all macrophyte species in the river channel in each 10 m section of the 100 m length. The following attributes were recorded in each 10 m survey unit as an aid in the interpretation of macrophyte data: Width, Depth, Substrate, Habitats, Shading, Water clarity and Bed stability.\t\tAssociated report and data is held at Maes-Y-Ffynnon at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\34 Freshwater Macrophytes in Nant Teyrn - Freshwater Environmental Change Protocol\tInternal Custodian:  Victoria Bowmaker, ECN Project Officer, NRW Alex Turner, ECN Project Officer, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:34:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18139\tNRW_DS115639\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.762Z\t763\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Otter in the Afon Gwyrfai and the Dyfi Estuary (2006)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363339\tThe Afon Gwyrfai and Dyfi Estuary forms part of two Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) the Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn SAC, and the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau / Llyn Peninsula and the Sarnau SAC. The purpose of this data capture was to report on the current and potential distribution, and breeding success of the otter within the Gwyrfai catchment and Dyfi Estuary area; identify and map actual and potential breeding habitat and feeding ponds and ditches; and, to produce performance indicators to contribute to former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) reporting of the condition and conservation status of the otter.\tThe methodology for this study is based on work carried out for the European Commission\u0027s Life Natura Programme, to investigate the ecological requirements for otter breeding (Liles, 2003). Information on otter breeding activity is derived from otter road deaths, and from reports of otter cub sightings (collected during visits to householders living close to watercourses). Possible and potential breeding sites were identified from Phase 1 maps, and by walk-over surveys of all possible sites to confirm breeding site potential. A similar method was used to locate potential amphibian feeding sites for otters. Data is based on that collected from the Otter Survey of Wales.\t\tAssociated data and reports are held electronically at Maes-y-Ffynnon at;  H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\36 Otters in the Afon Gwyrfai \u0026amp; Dyfi estuary\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18133\tNRW_DS115640\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.821Z\t303\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Woodland Habitat at four SACs in North and Mid Wales (2007)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363430\tThe four Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) surveyed were Coedydd Derw a Safleoedd Ystlumod Meirion/Meirionnydd Oak woods and Bat Sites; Rhinog; Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt and Cwm Doethie Mynydd Mallaen. The Annex 1 habitats surveyed were old sessile oak woods with Ilex and Blechnum (Habitat 91AO); alluvial forests with Alnus glutinosa and Fraxinus excelsior (Alno-Padion, Alnion incanae, Salicion albae) (Habitat 91EO); Tilio-Acerion forests of slopes, screes and ravines (Habitat 9180), and bog woodland (Habitat 91DO). The project was completed in two phases, a survey phase and a monitoring phase. The survey phase of the contract had two main objectives: first, to assess the condition of Annex I woodland habitat within individual management compartments and second, to identify and map hot-spots for lichens and bryophytes. The monitoring phase had a single principal objective: to establish, permanently mark and record 28 sampling plots in areas identified by CCW; these plots will form the basis of long-term monitoring on the sites.\tSurvey phase methodology broadly followed that set out in Kirby et al. (2002) and the Common Standards Monitoring Guidance for Woodland Habitats (JNCC, 2004). Within each management compartment, the condition of the woodland was assessed against a suite of attributes and targets provided by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the stand was assigned to either a favourable or unfavourable condition class. As a rule, at least ten sample points were recorded in woodland blocks over 10 hectares and at least twenty were recorded in blocks over 30 hectares. Once fieldwork for the survey phase was completed, all data was recorded on to Excel spreadsheets. These were hot-linked to MapInfo tables recording stopping points, hot-spots and negative attributes.  On completion of the survey phase of the contract, the draft survey maps were submitted to CCW who used the data to inform the choice of areas in which to establish monitoring plots. The principal objective of the monitoring phase of the contract was to establish, permanently mark and record 28 sampling plots in areas identified by CCW. These plots will form the basis of long-term monitoring on the site and provide more detailed and objective information on the small areas of woodland they encompass than the broader brush survey phase of the contract.\t\tAssociated reports and data are held at Maes-y-Ffynnon at; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\38 Woodland habitats at 4 SACs in North Wales\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2007-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18131\tNRW_DS115641\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.831Z\t415\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Assessing Water Courses of Plain to Montane Levels with the Ranunculion Fluitantis and Callitricho-Batrachion Vegetation of the River Wye SAC\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363431\tThe single habitat listed in Annex I of the Habitats Directive which is a primary reason for selection of the River Wye as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is of Water courses of plain to montane levels with submerged or floating vegetation of the Ranunculion fluitantis and Callitricho-Batrachion (Habitat 3260). The purpose of this data capture was to assess the condition of the Water Courses of Plain to Montane Levels with the Ranunculion Fluitantis and Callitricho-Batrachion Vegetation. The aim of the work was to assess the conservation condition of this habitat. This dataset presents the results of the Common Standards Monitoring Assessment (CSM) of the River Wye and selected tributaries. The assessment was performed based on data collected in 1995 and in 2005. The CSM assessment was based purely on macrophyte survey data as these were the only comparable data available from the 1995 surveys.\tFor each 500 m section a surveyor walks the river ensuring that all macrophytes on the channel and margin are recorded. Each macrophyte is assigned a measure of its percentage cover and of its abundance relative to other taxa. In addition, some physical features are recorded against standard classes and values. A section is typified by running the data through a key (Holmes et al. 1999, page: 104), which will lead to one of the types listed in Appendix C.  Recording Mean Trophic Rank involves a surveyor walking the wetted area of the channel for 100 m, recording the cover of all macrophytes on a five point scale. The abundance of each scoring taxon is then multiplied by the score for the taxon (STR) provided in the manual, all the scores are summed and the total divided by the sum of the cover values to derive an MTR score. A series of thresholds are then given from which the decision is made as to whether or not the discharge is considered to have a significant enough effect that it needs to be modified.  Common Standards Monitoring involves analysis of a number of different data sets including those derived from JNCC macrophyte survey and from River Habitat Survey (RHS). If any of the analyses fail, then the section is deemed to have failed. For this reason, it is reasonable to consider only one element of the river CSM process - the macrophyte element.\t\tAssociated data and reports are held in the NRW DMS Reports Archive \u0026gt; CCW Environmental Monitoring Reports KNOW-911039132-53  CCW EMR 39 - Assessment of the condition of water courses of plain to montane levels with the ranunculion fluitantis and callitricho batrachion vegetation of the River Wye SAC  On the DMS:  KNOW-911039132-53  CCW EMR 39 - Assessment of the condition of water courses of plain to montane levels with the ranunculion fluitantis and callitricho batrachion vegetation of the River Wye SAC Held on the CCW H Drive:  H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\39 Ranunculion of the River Wye SAC\\Report\tInternal custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2007-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18142\tNRW_DS115651\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.848Z\t261\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEuropean Union (EU) Habitats Directive (Council Directive 92/43/EEC) reporting under Article 17 reporting round 2013\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363531\tEvery six years, Member States of the European Union are required (by Article 17 of the Directive) to report on implementation of the Habitats Directive (Council Directive 92/43/EEC). The reporting format set by the European Commission requires a separate analysis for each species and each habitat in each biogeographic region which that country covers. This is the archive version of the databases and supporting evidence which was sent to JNCC added to the archive 15/04/2014. A live version of the databases are stored on a corporate drive and are in use by the relevant specialists. The purpose of this data capture was to meet the Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW\u0027s) obligations under Article 17 of the Habitats Directive. This dataset covers all of Wales and covers the six year reporting period to 2013.  All data was entered by specialists based on expert opinion and supporting data sources.\tDatabase templates were supplied by the  European Commission. These were created in MS Access and consist of a front-end data capture and back-end data store. Species and habitat specialists enter data directly into these along with relevant data in MS Excel spreadsheets and supporting information.  This is the third round of reporting under Article 17 of the Habitats Directive.\t\tAssociated data is held in the Terrestrial Archive: at W:\\Terrestrial Archive\\T_Media001to100\\T_Media002\tInternal Custodian, contact relevant specialists or the Enterprise Data Management Specialist Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18129\tNRW_DS115656\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.826Z\t601\tUnassessed\tEditable\tApproaches for Monitoring the Effectiveness of Tir Gofal in Delivering Benefits to Farmland Birds\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363536\tTir Gofal is the main scheme in Wales, within which some habitat management prescriptions are designed to provide benefits to breeding and wintering farmland birds. The aim of this project was to identify and evaluate methods of monitoring the extent to which this is achieved.  The purpose of this data capture was to assess possible approaches to monitor the effect of Tir Gofal on farmland birds.\tNew data on the abundance and species richness of birds on samples of treatment and control farms in winter were obtained using an area search method modified from previous studies. Similar data for the breeding season were obtained from previous surveys of ESAs in Wales. An evaluation of type I errors rates for given sample size and effect size were carried out by fitting log-linear models to these data. This was done after re-sampling and sub-sampling procedures were used to simulate different sample sizes and effect sizes for a comparative scheme monitoring design.\t\tAssociated data is held at ;  H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\16 Scoping study Tir Gofal delivering benefits to farmland birds\tInternal Custodian:  Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18143\tNRW_DS115678\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.810Z\t324\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLow Tide Wetland Birds Surveys (WeBS) Counts and Feeding activity of Oystercatch and Knot in the Burry Inlet Special Protection Area (SPA) and Carmarthen Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) (2013-2014).\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363738\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPAs) are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive 2009/147/EC, which came into force in April 1979. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive), and for regularly occurring migratory species.  This dataset consists of spatial information detailing Wetland Birds Surveys (WeBS) Low Tide Counts carried out various SPAs in the winter of 2013/2014. These count data details feeding and non-feeding birds. The focus of the study was on Oystercatcher and Knot, however other wetland birds were also included.  The aims of this study were to support further WeBS Low Tide Counts and provide additional information on the feeding behaviour of Oystercatchers and Knots in the Burry Inlet, Pembrey Coast and Three Rivers Estuary during the low tide period.\tWeBS Low Tide Counts were carried out by teams of volunteers co-ordinated by local organisers at the Burry Inlet, Pembrey Coast and Three Rivers Estuary once a month from November 2013 to February 2014. Count sectors matched those used in the previous years surveys and by the WeBS Low Tide Counts scheme. Counts were focused on the priority species of Oystercatcher and Knot but included all waterbird species, and were undertaken during the two hours either side of low tide.  Following the work undertaken in 2006/07, 2007/08, 2008/09 and 2009/10, standard WeBS Low Tide Counts of the Burry Inlet, Pembrey Coast and Three Rivers Estuary were carried out in the winter of 2010/11, 2011/12 and 2012/13.\t\tData archived as project 447, media 1488\tInternal Custodian: Marine Ornithologist Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18126\tNRW_DS115679\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.466Z\t551\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInsect Survey Results for Two Brown Field Sand Quarry Sites, Borras Quarry and Marford Quarry, in North-East Wales (2013)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135363739\tThe dataset contains the results from insect surveys conducted on two sand quarries in north-east Wales, Borras Quarry (162 species recorded) and Marford Quarry (145 species recorded). The data collected from Borras Quarry includes 14 psammophilic, 70 aculeate species. The data collected from Marford quarry includes 8 psammophilic and 69 aculeate species. The aim of this data collection was to identify whether the sand quarries supported suitable conditions for invertebrate species, in order to make recommendations for securing long-term sympathetic management of key sites. The diversity of aculeate species recorded from both quarries to date (135 species in total) ranks these areas as the most species-rich sites in Wales.\tBorras Quarry and Marford Quarry were each visited on several occasions to record the invertebrates present. The data collected focused particularly on aculeate bees, wasps, ants, beetles and flies. Sampling techniques included visual searches, sweep-netting, pitfall-trapping and water-trapping. This data was collected in 2013, further to the previous years work. In 2012, surveys were undertaken at Borras Quarry, Fagl Lane Quarry and Fron Haul Quarry.\tContact: NRW Invertebrate Ecologist Relevant NRW Evidence Report: No 021 Brownfield sites and their value for invertebrates - A survey of selected sand quarries in north-east Wales in 2013: Borras Quarry and Marford Quarry. Formstone. B., Howe. M Located on the DMS: KNOW-101-14 Associated report: Brownfield sites and their value for invertebrates: A survey of selected sand quarries in north-east Wales in 2012. CCW Contract Science. 1020.\t[A]  Recorder 6 database.\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18136\tNRW_DS115702\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.851Z\t611\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring invertebrates by pitfall trapping after excavation of the surface vegetation in two slacks at Newborough Warren, Ynys Llanddwyn Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) in 2013.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373032\tThis dataset holds information regarding the monitoring of excavated dune slacks in Newborough Warren, north Wales. The monitoring consisted on invertebrate surveys focussing on the two excavations sites further inland, as well as an adjacent control site. The dataset hold 12,838 individual invertebrates records made up of 298 species. The aim of the current study is to monitor by pitfall trapping the effects upon selected dune invertebrates of the excavation of the surface vegetation of two slacks and the dunes bordering their NE ends.  Some voucher specimens have been retained and are in the authors\u0027 private collections.\tInvertebrate monitoring was carried out using pitfall traps. There were 5465 effective trap nights spread across 17 sampling sites. All Coleoptera (beetles), Orthoptera (grasshoppers and allies), Diplopoda (millipedes), Chilopoda (centipedes), Isopoda (woodlice), Araneae (spiders) and Opiliones (harvesters) were identified and counted.  Using data from previous surveys, the possible changes in the invertebrate fauna over the last 30 years are discussed in the associated report: Monitoring invertebrates by pitfall trapping after excavation of the surface vegetation in two slacks at Newborough Warren: Ynys Llanddwyn SSSI in 2013. NRW Evidence Report No: 22.\tContact: Invertebrate Ecologist Relevant NRW Evidence Report: No 022 Monitoring invertebrates by pitfall trapping after excavation of the surface vegetation in two slacks at Newborough Warren - Ynys Llanddwyn SSSI in 2013 Loxton. R.\t[A]  All data is currently held in the report, and is being transferred onto Recorder 6. [B]  The final report Located on the DMS: Reports Archive . NRW Evidence Reports KNOW-1206538853-14  NRW Evidence Report No. 022 - Monitoring~ace vegetation in two slacks at Newborough Warren - Ynys Llanddwyn SSSI in 2013\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18144\tNRW_DS115704\t2024-05-28T12:40:37.344Z\t734\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCondition assessment of 8 standing water Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and 24 unprotected lakes across Wales (2012-2014).\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373034\tLake ecosystems are threatened both nationally and globally. Commitments under European legislation and international treaties require the UK to protect and enhance these in a conservation conscience way taking account of the culturally important habitats. This data collection forms part of an ecological and limnological survey. Limnology is the study of inland waters as ecological systems interact with their drainage basins and the atmosphere. The lakes surveyed were as follows:  - Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Lake Surveys: Llynnau Cregennen, Llyn Dinas, Llyn Mair, Llyn Cerig-y-Myllt, Llyn Gwernan, Llyn Gwyddior and Llyn Coch-hwyad.   - Lake Ecological Surveys: Llyn Cadarn, Llyn yr Wyth-Eidion, Llyn Coron, Llyn Goddionduon, Llyn Anafon, Gloyw Lyn, Llyn Cwm Bychan, Llyn Eiddew-mawr, Llandgorse Lake, Llyn Mawr, Cosmoeston Lake, Llyn Eiddwen, Llyn Fanod, Upper Talley Lake, Lower Talley Lake, Llyn Penrhyn.    - Condition Assessment of Eight standing waters in Welsh SSSIs: Pysgodlyn Mawr, Llyn Llech Owain, Llyn Pencarreg, Witchett Pool, Llyn Treflesg, Llyn Cerrig-bach, Llyn Traffwll and Llyn Glasfryn.  Data from this network of lakes, supports an integrated monitoring programme for protected sites, the Water Framework Directive and other legislative and policy driven initiatives. The Welsh Environment Strategy has imposed a target of 95% of all Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) to be in favourable condition by 2015. All the SSSIs assessed in this dataset were classes as being in unfavourable condition. Therefore another objective of this dataset is to provide initial monitor the condition of a series of SSSIs lakes in order to assess the need for improvement or increased monitoring in the future to reach the targets set by the Welsh Environmental Strategy. The data was also collected to fill the gaps in the quality and condition of unprotected sites in Wales, by targeting select lakes of potential biodiversity importance in Wales. This forms part of an on-going strategic survey programme designed by NRW to improve the understanding of the distribution and quality of different lake types in Wales.\tWithout historical water chemistry monitoring data, lake sediments can provide a record of past environmental change. In particular, the siliceous valves of diatoms (microscopic algae) preserved in the sediments can be used as indicators of past limnological conditions, especially lake pH and nutrient concentrations. By identifying the degree of pollution problems, this technique allows judgements on the ecological condition of sites. Palaeolimnology was used to provide additional insights into environmental change at lake sites. This data has a number of associated methods.  The BAP lake surveys. Llynnau Cregennen, Llyn Dinas, Llyn Mair, Llyn Cerig-y-Myllt, Llyn Gwernan, Llyn Gwyddior and Llyn Coch-hwyad. Eight lakes were selected for their potential biodiversity importance base on aerial photography and local knowledge. The sites selected were Llynnau Cregennen (two sites), Llyn Dinas, Llyn Mair, Llyn Cerig-y-Myllt, Llyn Gwernan, Llyn Gwyddior and Llyn Coch-hwyad. Site assessments were based on aquatic macrophyte surveys and mapping, bathymetric survey, physico-chemical measurements, and at 4 sites, palaeolimnological analyses.  Lake Ecological Surveys. The 16 lakes were surveyed to assess the species and abundance of aquatic plants within and around the lake, and to measure water clarity, dissolved oxygen and the temperature within the lakes. At Llyn Goddionduon a sediment core was collected from the deep water and analysed for the possible remains of diatom, in order to infer past environmental conditions and water quality.   Condition Assessment of eight standing waters in Welsh SSSIs. The 8 lake assessments were conducted based on aquatic macrophyte surveys and mapping, bathymetric survey, physic-chemical measurements and at four sites, palaeolimnological analyses.\t\tData outputs associated with this project are archived as Media Number: 1493 Project Number: 449 (Condition Assessment of eight standing waters in Welsh SSSIs) 450 (The BAP lake surveys) 451 (Lake Ecological Surveys)\tInternal Contact: Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader - or Tristan Hatton-Ellis Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:30.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18122\tNRW_DS115773\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.871Z\t1972\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status and distribution of the shrill carder bee Bombus sylvarum in the Kenfig National Nature Reserve (NNR) - Port Talbot (2013).\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373733\tThe shrill carder bee (Bombus sylarum) was once relatively widespread across southern England and lowlands Wales, favouring flower-rich habitats such as sand dunes and established grasslands and heathlands. However, there has been a recent decline in the population. The main purpose of this survey was to provide an up-to-date account of the current status of the species, with an emphasis placed on determining the distribution limits, by re-visiting sites with previous records and surveying sites supporting flower-rich dunes, grasslands and other habitats.  The surveying took place in the Kenfig-Port Talbot area in Glamorgan between late July and early September 2013.  This dataset provides an up-to-date account of the distribution of shrill carder bee (Bombus sylarum) across these sites around South Wales. It is a collection of sightings of shrill carder bee and botanical surveys. The botanical surveys had a particular emphasis on plants associated with the foraging behaviour of shrill carder bees. A total of 17 species of bumblebee, including shrill carder bee, were recorded during the survey with a total of 936 recorded observations of 2122 individual bumblebees.\tWithin each survey area a look and see approach was taken, to apply a flexible approach when efficiently identifying foraging or nesting areas more quickly. Where these foraging or nesting areas were located time counts were undertaken. All shrill carder bees were recorded as a centroid point using GPS and, or positions marked on to aerial images. As well as this all forage plants were noted and a species list recorded of all plants within a 3 m radius of the centroid using the DAFOR scale. These were then recorded at Phase 1 or NVC level where appropriate. All other Bombus species were noted alongside the shrill carder bee, and a photographic record was made of key features within each site.  A total of 88.69 hours were spent carrying out dedicated fieldwork, during which the prescribed survey methods were applied. In addition, a further 14.85 hours of casual observations were carried out in areas with potential habitat totalling 103.54 hours. The data collected as part of this survey was also synchronized with South East Wales Biological Records Centre (SEWBReC).  A survey of the coastal plain and inland lowland areas in central Glamorgan was carried out during late July and early September 2013, when numbers of workers and males were likely to be at their peak. Twelve days of fieldwork were undertaken, with good to fair weather prevailing throughout the period. Surveys targeted known sites, including Kenfig National Nature Reserve (NNR) (including Sker Point/Farm), Margam Moors Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), Margam Burrows, Parc Slip Nature Reserve, Maesteg Golf Course but equal emphasis was also placed on extending into areas previously unsurveyed and a range of potential sites with suitable habitats were systematically surveyed.\tContact: Invertebrate Ecologist  Relevant NRW Evidence Report: No 023 The status and distribution of the shrill carder bee Bombus sylvarum in the Kenfig - Port Talbot area in 2013 Stewart, B., Roberts, P.\t[A]  The data will become available in Recorder 6  [B]  The report located in the DMS Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports  NRW Evidence Report No. 023 - The status~ distribution of the shrill carder bee in the Kenfig - Port Talbot area in 2013.  KNOW-1206538853-15\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18120\tNRW_DS115774\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.849Z\t707\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpatial Conservation Status Modelling of the Great Crested Newt (Triturus cristatus) in South Wales (2013).\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373734\tThis dataset was compiled to build on previous related work conducted in North Wales and Powys. 12 Unitary Authorities in south Wales were assessed in terms of their Favourable Conservation Status for great crested newts (Triturus cristatus) using a habitat modelling approach.  Project specific aims included assessing: i) known distribution, ii) suitable habitat, iii) survey target areas, iv) important populations for metapopulation connectivity, v) pond and habitat creation areas, and, vi) Local Population Targets (LPTs) for each unitary authority in South Wales, to develop a tool kit which can be used by local planners as an indication as to the presence of great crested newts. This tool kit includes spatially-explicit information in the form of GIS-based mapping (layers) and spreadsheets that will inform conservation priorities and action, land-use planning and other things such as derogation licensing.  This project follows on from other recent attempts to describe the status of great crested newts (Triturus cristatus) in the UK and to apply the concept of Favourable Conservation Status (FCS). This is the third and final regional modelling exercise in Wales covering the country\u0027s core great crested newt range (Anglesey, NE Wales, Powys and South Wales between Gower and Monmouthshire). The integrated results will enable the first objective Wales-wide assessment of great crested newt status for the country. MaxEnt model testing produced acceptably high values (bigger than 0.7) for the area under the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve. Due to limited record numbers in individual Unitary Authorities, NRW built and tested the MaxEnt model with records from all 12 Unitary Authorities.\tThis research utilises high resolution (25 m) modelling to develop maps and metrics for great crested newts in 12 South Wales Unitary Authorities. High resolution, 1990-2011 great crested newt records were compiled which after quality control consisted of 149 records representing known populations. Distribution modelling software, MaxEnt, was used along with 24 environmental layers and a subset of 108 spatially filtered records, to produce an estimated probability of presence output. These outputs, and known barriers, were used to derive a resistance surface for calculating Least Cost Path distances and corridors between known populations. Graph theory software, Conefor, was run with these distances to produce probability of connectivity indices (dPC) to highlight important populations for maintaining metapopulation connectivity. To delimit pond creation target areas, we selected low pond density areas within modelled suitable habitat and within an appropriate Unitary Development Plan (UDP) designation. We set local population target figures to mitigate putative historical pond losses in each unitary authority, using a reference date of 1843, local pond loss rates and current pond data. We apportioned these targets to appropriate pond creation areas within cost weighted buffers surrounding known populations, or within corridors between them. The ultimate goal for this data was to develop draft Spatial Conservation Status Strategies for great crested newts in the 12 Unitary Authorities that make up the study area.\tAssociated Report Title: Spatial Conservation Status Modelling of the Great Crested Newt in South Wales (2013) Fletcher DH, Arnell AP, French GCA and Wilkinson JW.  NRW Science Report No.30\tMaxEnt Model outputs are located within the accompaning report: NRW-14-105023 Spatial Conservation Status Modelling of the Great Crested Newt in South Wales. NRW Science Report Series. Report 30.   Raw Data is held and distributed by ARC Trust.  Associated Report Title: Spatial Conservation Status Modelling of the Great Crested Newt in South Wales (2013) Fletcher DH, Arnell AP, French GCA and Wilkinson JW.  NRW Science Report No.30  Ffynnon Number: NRW-14-105023\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tAmphibian and Reptile Conservation-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:30:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18124\tNRW_DS115775\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.870Z\t569\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA geomorphological appraisal and options for dune rejuvenation at Pembrey Burrows (2014).\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373735\tThis dataset consists of the results from a geomorphological assessment of Pembrey Burrows, south Wales. The datasets holds a combination of environmental data, published and unpublished literature, air photography, LiDAR interpretation, site visits and discussions with relevant personnel. The purpose of this data capture and analyses was to provide a geomorphological assessment of Pembrey Burrows sand dunes system, with the aim of creating an informed management policy in the future which could include options for dune rejuvenation.\tMethods of data capture: The dataset compilation involved the following: - A review of relevant environmental data, published and unpublished literature - Analysis of aerial photographs and LiDAR imagery - Analysis of strategic coastal monitoring data - Site visits and discussions with representatives of NRW, Carmarthenshire County Council and Defence Training Estates in January and February 2014 - Laboratory analysis of sand samples collected during site visits.  The report associated with this data is the latest in a series which describe the geomorphological characteristics of major sand dune systems in Wales. All data is included within the report supplied to NRW.\t\tReport location, with datasets included is located on Ffynnon NRW-14-1090223\tInternal Custodian: Invertebrate Ecologist, TEG, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:32:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18123\tNRW_DS115776\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.900Z\t628\tUnassessed\tEditable\tKenfig Dune Rejuvenation Trials Topographic Survey Data (2012 - 2015)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135373736\tThe purpose of this data capture was to assess the topographic mobility of the Kenfig National Nature Reserve sand dunes and to monitor the sand dune rejuvenation.  Kenfig sand dune rejuvenation trails began in December 2012. The dataset consists of points were topographic elevations were determined, many of which were on profile lines which were profile lines. Key features were defined based on the limits. These features included ridge crests, standing water, new vegetation growth and the position of the frontal sand dunes.\tElevation points were determined using Leica RTK GPS SmartRover equipment The ground survey was conducted using a ‘base and rover’ surveying technique.  The first two surveys were carried out using a Leica Viva GPS SmartRover GS15 Receiver mounted on a 2 m pole and a Leica Viva CS15 GNSS Field Controller. The surveys made use of an RTK control station at Mumbles Head. A position for the base station was chosen towards the centre of the site, on a high point to maximise potential for a good quality mobile telephone signal (for GPRS corrections). Two wooden posts were inserted into the ground to act as local temporary benchmarks.  Benchmark 2 being determined, to assess survey accuracy and precision. Accuracy was generally very good with measurements of elevation on average being accurate to better than 10 mm, and measurements of precision on average being better than 7 mm.\tKPAL (2013c) Kenfig Phase 2, Dune Rejuvenation Works Topographic Survey, May 2013. NRW Evidence Report No.92. Kenneth Pye Associates Ltd., Solihull.   KPAL (2014c) Kenfig Phase 2 Dune Restoration Works Topographic Survey, March 2014. NRW Evidence Report No. 97.  Kenneth Pye Associates Ltd., Solihull.  KPAL (2014d) Kenfig Phase 1 Dune Rejuvenation Works Topographic Survey, May 2014. NRW Evidence Report No. 98. Kenneth Pye Associates Ltd., Solihull.\tArchive Project Number: 420  Media number:1438  [Data from 2012 /  2013 surveys]  1490  [Data from 2014 surveys]  1534 [data from 2015 surveys]\tInternal Contact: Marine and Coastal Physical Scientist, Evidence Analysis Group.   Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-10-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18125\tNRW_DS115828\t2024-05-28T12:40:36.041Z\t570\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University SOS Prince Madog Video and Photographic Survey Data for Habitat and Scallop Assessments (2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383238\tSurveys of the seabed in Cardigan Bay, Caernarfon Bay, West Anglesey and Tremadog Bay, were carried out by the School of Ocean Sciences (SOS), Bangor University in 2012. The datasets  comprises of a collation of video tows, photographs and subsequent species and habitat photographic analyses. The main aim of this survey work was to collect information on the characteristics and status of the offshore benthic environment, with specific reference to scallop beds and habitat types, at various locations around Wales.  Confidence in this dataset is high.\tVideo and photographic surveys took place between May and October 2012. Underwater video and still images were collected using a standard definition digital video camera. A High Definition GoPro video camera system was also positioned laterally on the camera sled to provide a wider angle view of the seabed. Photographic stills were taken along each tow to assist with habitat validation and utilized to inform species identification and augment the species list from the video tows. - MRCCW3170000000E: Habitat Survey. Cardigan Bay - 39 video tows collected from circalittorla habitats within Cardigan Bay (6th - 8th October 2012) - MRCCW31700000013: Habitat Survey. Caernarfon Bay - 10 video tows were collected from circalittoral habitats within Caernarfon Bay (5th October 2012). - MRCCW31700000012: Habitat Survey. West Anglesey - 15 tows were undertaken within circalittoral habitats off West Anglesey (16th May 2012). - MRCCW3170000000D: Scallop Survey. Tremadog Bay - 5 video tows were collected from infralittoral habitats with Tremadog Bay (4th June 2012). - MRCCW31700000010: Scallop Survey. Cardigan Bay - 13 video tows were collected from circalittoral habitats with Cardigan Bay (4th June - 8th October 2012).   Videos were analysed for habitats, species and biotopes using standard Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) derived techniques and Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) Marine Habitat Classification. The footage was analysed by Marine EcoSol on behalf of Natural Resources Wales (NRW). Data was later entered into Marine Recorder by Marine EcoSol.\tMarine Recorder File Names: MRCCW3170000000D, MRCCW3170000000E, MRCCW31700000010, MRCCW31700000012,  MRCCW31700000013.\tArchived as Project 387 Media 1402 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1402) Species Data is held in Marine Recorder surveys [MRCCW3170000000D, MRCCW3170000000E, MRCCW31700000010, MRCCW31700000012, MRCCW31700000013].\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Contact: School of Ocean Sciences, Bangor University\tUniversity of Wales Bangor\t\t2012-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:48.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18119\tNRW_DS115829\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.790Z\t374\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUnderwater photographs and video of aquatic flora, habitat structure and water transparency from Llynnau Cregennen.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383239\tThe data supports a condition assessment of Llynnau Cregennen on the northern slope of Cadair Idris above Dolgellau. The dataset supports an assessment of both the water clarity and diversity of aquatic plants in the lake. The dataset is made up of a number of underwater video and photography surveys which were conducted by Marine Ecosol. This data was collected in order to have representative samples, images and video footage of macrophyte and faunal community assemblages within Llynnau Cregennenn.\tDive teams undertook underwater visual census surveys in the form of under photography and video surveys. Both transects and point sampling took place in perseved hot spots for macrophyte speices within Llyannau Cregennen. Under the EU Habitats Directive (92/43/EEC) and the Rio Biodiversity Convention, NRW was obliged to assess Llynnau Cregennen because it had previously been identified as ecologically important.  4 transects and 35 points were surveyed at various locations around Llynnau Cregennen, which is situated on the northern slope of Cadair Idris above Dolgellau.\t\tProject Number 455 Media 1516\tInternal Custodian: Freshwater Team Leader  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-08-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18121\tNRW_DS115830\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.809Z\t339\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Quality of Riverine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) across Wales (2006-2012).\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383330\tThis data collection was required as part of the water quality assessment of various Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) for the monitoring round and report cycle 2006 to 2012. Water quality targets for designated riverine features are specified under the JNCC CSM Guidance. The targets are based on routine water quality monitoring and subsequent reporting. Water quality targets are assessed based on the chemistry, biology, phosphate, suspended solids and un-ionized ammonia. The dataset details the results of water quality target testing for the following rivers and associated SACs: - Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn SAC - Afon Eden and Cors Goch Trawsfynydd SAC - River Dee and Bala Lake / Afon Dyfrdwy a Llyn Tegid SAC - Afon Tywi / River Tywi SAC - Afon Teifi / Afon Teifi - Afonydd Cleddau SAC - River Usk SAC - River Wye SAC - Afon Glaslyn (Meirionnydd Oakwoods SAC)\tThe water quality was tested based on EA General Quality Assessment (GQA) which has 4 classifications; chemistry, biology, nutrients and aesthetics. Each classification is made up of 6 grades from A (very good) to F (bad). The water quality targets can vary depending on the sensitivity of the species or habitat, and whether they are mandatory or discretionary targets. Chemistry Classification: based on dissolved oxygen, biochemical oxygen demand and ammonia.  Sampling: occurred 12 times a year over 3 years Biology Classification: Based on macro-invertebrate communities. Sampling: Kick samples collected in spring and autumn, in one year out of every three. Phosphate Classification: Based on Soluble Reactive Phosphorus (SRP). Sampling: Monthly basis at chemical sampling points. Suspended Solids Classification: Based on the Freshwater Fish Directive. Sampling: Based on annual mean suspended solids. Uni-ionised ammonia Classification: Measured against the target for the river habitat as a 95-percentile.   Acidity Acid sensitive units have been identified and assessed using a risk assessment based on various data sources including the Acid Water Indicator Community (AWICS) invertebrate monitoring tool, UK pH and ANC standards, and critical load exceedance maps.\t\tArchive project number: 448 Archive number 2006-2012\tInternal Contacts: Freshwater Team Leader, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18118\tNRW_DS115834\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.791Z\t298\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAllis (Alosa alosa) and Twaite (Alosa fallax) shad monitoring data sampled from the rivers Wye, Usk and Tywi using Mitochondrial DNA techniques (2013).\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383334\tThe river Wye, Usk and Tywi are, or form part of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs), designed under the EU Habitats Directive (92/43/EEC) for several features including the Twaite and Allis shad. The dataset contains the results from DNA analyses conducted on 226 fish eggs sampled from the river Wye, Usk and Tywi. The aim of the project was to use genetic techniques to address the weaknesses identified in the methods already adopted by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for sampling shad eggs.  The main objectives of analyses were to: - Determine whether the collected eggs belonged to Alosa spp. - Determine the proportion of Twaite and Allis shad using three different genetic markers.\tAnalyses of eggs collected involved DNA barcoding where a short genetic marker, usually the COI gene, of an unknown organism was sequenced using universal primers, to identify the species to which it belongs. This DNA analyses was completed in response to being unable to distinguish between the two Alosa species and reduce the risk of miss identification of fish eggs.\tRelevant NRW Evidence Report: No 001 Monitoring Allis and Twaite shad: Quality assurance and species identification using molecular techniques (2013) Hardouin, E. Andreou, D. Stuart, S. Natural Resources Wales Report.  Located on the DMS:  KNOW-101-3\tProject Number: 436 Media number: 1473\tInternal Contact: Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-11-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18117\tNRW_DS115838\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.555Z\t588\tValid\tEditable\tTowed Video Surveys off the Coastline of the Llyn Peninsula North Wales (2013)\tArolygon fideo llusgedig oddi ar lannau Llyn yng ngogledd Cymru, 2013.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383338\tSurveys of the seabed were carried out using towed dropdown video surveys were taken aboard Lara B and William Stanley, both fishing vessels operating around Llyn Peninsula, Gwynedd, North Wales.   A total of 38 video tows were collected from infralittoral and circalittoral habitats at the following five locations offshore from the Llyn Peninsular North Wales; Llyn-Hells Mouth, Ynys Gwlan, Porth Meudwy, North Llyn Modiolus and North Llyn sea Grass. Where available HD GoPro video footage was used in the analyses, however if this was not possible a lower quality standard definition footage was used. The locations of the video tows were made into GIS files as a spatial representation of the location of the tow surveys. Video surveys were conduct to conduct ground-truthing surveys through the seabed around the Llyn Peninsula, Gwynedd, North Wales.\tDrop-down video tows were carried out onboard small fishing boats using a High Definition (HD) GoPro video camera (approximate field of view is 100cm in the middle of the picture) and a Standard Definition ROVTECH Systems Micro Hi-Res Digital Colour Camera (1/3 inch Sony HAD CCD - 480 TV lines resolution) with 4.3mm wide angle lens (approx. 80 degrees in water) and Mini Seabeam Lights.  A manual log was kept of the location of the start and end of the transects, which were later digitized via GIS files.   The video surveys were analysed for habitats, species and biotopes by Marine EcoSol using standard MNCR derived techniques and JNCC Marine Habitat Classifications. Drop-down video tows were carried out by Salacia Marine whilst onboard small fishing vessels.\tNon-sensitive species data is published and available to download from the NBN Atlas as part of the Data is available to download as part of the \"NRW Marine data from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Technical Support (Research \u0026amp; Monitoring) Contracts, Wales\" dataset (NRW_DS116350)\tMarine Recorder,  Survey Code: MRCCW31700000014.\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2014-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:44:46.033Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18116\tNRW_DS115839\t2024-05-28T12:40:35.464Z\t344\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBangor University School of Ocean Science (SOS) Towed Video Survey in Liverpool Bay; Caernarfon Bay and Cardigan Bay (2012 - 2013)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383339\tIn 2012 and 2013 video tows and photographic surveys were undertaken to ground truth the seabed around Cardigan Bay, Caernarfon Bay and Liverpool Bay. The Bangor University School of Ocean Sciences (SOS) surveyed five sites, two using fishing vessels and three using the Bangor University Research Vessel (RV) Prince Madog. In total sixty video tows were undertaken within circalittoral habitats; six of these had associated photographic stills, of which there were 570.  The purpose of this data capture was to ground truth areas of the seabed around Cardigan Bay, Caernarfon Bay and Liverpool Bay.  Confidence in this dataset is high as data was collected by scientists. Imagery was analysed using standard methods by competent and experienced surveyors.\tSurveys were undertaken by Bangor University Research Vessel (RV) Prince Madog and various fishing vessels.  RV Prince Madog was used to collect videos of the seabed using three cameras: -  A Standard Definition (SD) ROVTECH Systems Ltd. underwater video camera with an approximate field of view of 41cm in the middle of the picture;   -  A High Definition (HD) GoPro video camera with approximate field of view of 70cm at the bottom of the picture and 100cm in the middle;  -  A Canon 400D stills camera with an approximate field of view of 0.13m squared (44cm by 30cm).  Fishing vessels were used ot collect videos of the seabed using two cameras: -  A GoPro HD video camera on a mini-sled towed behind a fishing vessel. The GoPro HD has approximate field of view of 70 cm at the bottom of the picture and 100 cm in the middle.  -  A Standard Definition ROVTECH Systems Micro Hi-Res Digital Colour Camera (1/3 inches  Sony HAD CCD - 480 TV lines resolution) with 4.3 mm wide angle lens (approximately 80 degrees in water) and Mini Seabeam Light.  Imagery was analysed for habitats, species and biotopes by Marine EcoSol using standard Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) derived techniques and Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) Marine Habitat Classification. Photographic still, when taken, were approximately every 11 seconds along the tow to assist with species and habitat validation, but were not analysed independently of the video. Videos and stills were analysed by staff at Marine EcoSol and data was entered into excel spreadsheets and then into Marine Recorder.\t\tArchived as Project 439 Media 1479 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1479)   [C] Marine Recorder data to be entered (data  received 2014-09-31 - to be merged with the main database) MRCCW31700000015 - 2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Liverpool Bay, August. MRCCW31700000016 - 2012 DRAFT Fisher Survey Towed Survey in Cardigan Bay, October. MRCCW31700000017 - 2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Cardigan Bay, July. MRCCW31700000018 - 2013 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey of Caernarfon Bay, July. MRCCW31700000019 - 2012 DRAFT Bangor University SOS Prince Madog Towed Video Survey in Liverpool Bay, October.\tGIS Service Desk Internal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team External Data Custodian: School of Ocean Science - Bangor University\tUniversity of Wales, Bangor. School of Ocean Sciences-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-09-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:20.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18169\tNRW_DS115840\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.718Z\t838\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNewborough Topographic Surveys conducted as part of Sand Dune Rejuvenation Trials (1950-2015)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383430\tThere were three main purposes for this dataset:  -  To provide information on the development of remobilising and monitoring the dune system;  -  Conduct a baseline topographic survey to provide local pre-works and height information as part of the remobilisation and monitoring;  -  Provide temporal comparisons of topographic profiles and mapping the limits of define features  -  Temporal comparison of topographic profiles and mapping limits of define features.  Newborough sand dune system forms part of the Newborough Warren Ynys Llanddwyn Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and the Abermenai to Aberffraw Dunes Special Area of Conservation (SAC). This dataset holds a variety of physical, chemical and historic data related to rejuvenation and monitoring works completed on the dune system. The data includes topographic surveys, sediment sampling, LiDAR surveys, drone aerial photography surveys, historic aerial photos and maps.\tData was captured in a variety of ways; -Topographic surveys with relevant site photos; -Sediment samples; -Historic LiDAR imagery (obtained from NERC -Drones were used for aerial photographic surveys; -Historic aerial and photographic surveys and maps. LiDAR provided by the NERC Airborne Research and Survey Facility. Aerial photography and drone survey conducted by ExeGesis Ltd.  Ground survey (GPRS) and photography by KPAL consultants KPAL (2013d) Newborough Phase 1 Dune Rejuvenation Works Topographic Survey, May 2013. NRW Evidence Report No. 93. Kenneth Pye Associates Ltd., Solihull.  KPAL (2014a) Newborough Phase 1 Dune Rejuvenation Works Topographic Survey, March 2014. NRW Evidence Report No. 95. Kenneth Pye Associates Ltd., Solihull.   KPAL (2015c) Newborough Phase 1 Dune Rejuvenation Works Topographic Survey March 2015. NRW Evidence Report No. 101. Kenneth Pye Associates Ltd., Solihull.   KPAL (2015d) Newborough Dune Rejuvenation Works Topographic Survey March 2015 Phase 2 Site: Zone 1 East. NRW Evidence Report No. 102. Kenneth Pye Associates Ltd., Solihull.   KPAL (2015e) Newborough Dune Rejuvenation Works Topographic Survey March 2015 Phase 2 Site: Zone 1 West. NRW Evidence Report No. 103. Kenneth Pye Associates Ltd., Solihull.     KPAL (2015f) Newborough Phase 3 Dune Rejuvenation Works Topographic Survey March 2015. NRW Evidence Report No. 104. Kenneth Pye Associates Ltd., Solihull.\t\tArchive Project Number: 421  Media number: 1439  [Data from 2013 surveys January and May]  1474  [Data from 2013 surveys August]  1489  [Data from 2014 surveys]   1535 [Data from 2015 Surveys]\tMarine and Coastal Physical Scientist, Evidence Analysis Group. Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-10-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:27.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18167\tNRW_DS115841\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.797Z\t278\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDefining and mapping calaminarian grassland in mid-Wales (2013)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383431\tThe 2012/13 reporting for the status of habitats under the EU Habitats Directive indicated a lack of essential data for H6130 Calaminarian grasslands of the Violetalia, calaminariae  calaminarian grassland. This instigated the collection of data to identify areas of this type of habitat in mid-Wales. This dataset consists of botanical surveys conducted as a preliminary study associated with calaminarian grasslands in mid-Wales. The aim of this data collection was to gather information in order to identify lichen-rich forms of calaminaria grassland in mid-Wales with a view to obtaining information on extent of these areas in the future.\tA total of nine sites were surveyed, which consisted of seven abandoned mines and two contaminated river shingles.   A total of 69 lichen species were recorded, including 15 of nature conservation concern.\tDefining and mapping calaminarian grassland in mid-Wales. NRW Evidence Report No: 024.\t[A]  The data is available on the DMS. Reports Archive \u0026gt;  NRW Evidence Reports.  NRW Evidence Report No. 024 - Defining and mapping calaminarian grassland in mid-Wales 2014   KNOW-1206538853-16 [B]  The data is stored in Ffynnon - Folder NRWF-14-003465 Document NRW14-118779 [C]  The data is also stored on the DMS: Evidence \u0026gt; Terrestrial Habitats \u0026gt; Lowland Grassland \u0026gt; Projects \u0026gt; Calaminarian - definition 2013 Document ID - EVID-271-338\tGrassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:40:04.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18174\tNRW_DS115843\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.756Z\t437\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMerthyr Mawr Topographic Surveys Conducted as part of the Sand Dune Rejuvenation Trials (2012 - 2015).\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383433\tThe purpose of this data capture was to assess the topographic mobility of the Merthyr Mawr National Nature Reserve sand dunes and to monitor the sand dune rejuvenation.  Merthyr Mawr Warren sand dune rejuvenation trails began in December 2012. The dataset consists of points were topographic elevations were determined, many of which were on profile lines which were profile lines. Key features were defined based on the limits. These features included ridge crests, standing water, new vegetation growth and the position of the frontal sand dunes.\tThe report associated with this data provides a brief summary of the results arising from the second in a planned series of topographic monitoring surveys at Merthyr Mawr Warren where dune rejuvenation trials were begun in December 2012.  Elevation points were determined using Leica RTK GPS SmartRover equipment. During each survey ground photographs were taken from a number of fixed points to provide a visual record of changes in the vegetation cover and surface character.  The raw survey data were mathematically ‘corrected’ to allow direct comparison of straight line profiles derived from the October 2009 LiDAR survey and the May 2013 ground survey. The vertical accuracy of the LiDAR has not been quantified directly but is estimated to be better than 10-15 cm.\tKPAL (2013e) Merthyr Mawr Dune Rejuvenation Works Topographic Survey, May 2013. NRW Evidence Report No. 94. Kenneth Pye Associates Ltd., Solihull.  KPAL (2014b) Merthyr Mawr Dune Rejuvenation Works Topographic Survey, March 2014. NRW Evidence Report No. 96. Kenneth Pye Associates Ltd., Solihull.   KPAL (2015a) Merthyr Mawr Dune Rejuvenation Works Topographic Survey, March 2015. NRW Evidence Report No. 99. Kenneth Pye Associates Ltd., Solihull.\tArchive Project Number: 419  Media number: 1437 [Data from 2013 surveys]  1491 [Data from 2014 surveys]  1533 [data from 2015 surveys]\tInternal Contact: Marine and Coastal Physical Scientist, Evidence Analysis Group.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-10-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18162\tNRW_DS115844\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.663Z\t19\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSOS N Llyn and Tremadog Bay Fishers Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383434\t\t\t\tArchive Project: 419  Media: 1437 and 1491 (File paths: K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1437 and K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1491)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18173\tNRW_DS115862\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.909Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBeach Profile Data Swansea Bay \u0026amp; Carmarthen Bay Coastal Autumn 2013\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383632\t\tThis data  was collected by the  Swansea Bay \u0026amp; Carmarthen Bay coastal group, part funded by NRW/legacy bodies.\t\tProject 437 Media 1476 (File path: K\\Archive\\Media  1401to1500\\Media 1476)\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18172\tNRW_DS115863\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.949Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBeach Profile Data - Swansea and Carmarthen Bay 1998- 2013\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383633\tThis data  was collected by the Swansea Bay \u0026amp; Carmarthen Bay coastal group, part funded by NRW/legacy bodies.    It can now be accessed externally via the Wales Coastal Monitoring Centre https://www.wcmc.wales/ We should direct customers to this resource.\t\t\tArchived as Project 438 Media 1477 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media1401to1500\\Media 1477)  The data can now be accessed externally via the Wales Coastal Monitoring Centre https://www.wcmc.wales/\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18165\tNRW_DS115866\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.746Z\t328\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLlyn Tegid Hydroacoustic Surveys (2014)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383636\tThe gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) population of Llyn Tegid has been considered for a number of years to be threatened by deteriorating environmental conditions, especially those associated with eutrophication. This dataset contains the results of a project undertaken in 2014 to carry out a hydroacoustic survey and measurement of environmental variables. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the status of gwyniad and its habitat in Llyn Tegid.\tCarried out hydroacoustic survey and the measurement of associated environmental variables (temperature and oxygen profiles, and secchi disk reading). Utilised standardised survey and monitoring protocol for the assessment of whitefish, with two minor deviations. Rather than using the target strength to fish length relationship of Foote (1987) the relationship given by Love (1971) was used to allow wider comparison. Secondly a vessel speed of 2.0 meters per section was adopted as general target mean speed rather than an absolute upper limit. The objectives of the present project were to collect and analyse hydroacoustic survey and associated environmental data during the summer of 2014, to interpret them with respect to similar data from earlier years, and to consider their implications for the current status of the gwyniad and its habitat in Llyn Tegid.\t\tData is archived as project 458, media 1519\tInternal Contact: Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18159\tNRW_DS115875\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.739Z\t354\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Vegetation Layer - Voronoi\tHaen Llystyfiant Tir Cymru Gyfan - fersiwn Voronoi\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383735\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial polygons showing the boundaries and attributes of vegetation polygons.   A voronoi procedure was applied to the All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Vegetation Layer - GIS dataset to subdivide upland mixed habitat polygons into their component habitats as identified by the All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Habitat Layer - GIS dataset. New polygons derived by the voronoi procedure have Voronoi_UID\u0027s prefixed \u0027v_\u0027. New polygons created from Habitat Layer points mapped with a string of habitats, e.g. D.1.1 + D.2/E.2.1, are attributed \u0027mosaic\u0027 in the Phase 1 Code field, and the component habitats and their proportions are listed in the Mosaic browser table (see metadata for All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Mosaic Layer voronoi - GIS dataset). The two tables link on the Voronoi_UID field. The Phase 2 Tools were used to create a mospolys (mosaic polygons) layer to which the Phase 1 legend can be applied for better visualisation in GIS.    The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales 1km square dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage, and the maps were digitised as part of the mapping of Open Access land. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.   Voronoi was carried out to enable full representation and visualisation of the components of complex mixed-habitat upland polygons in the vector data.\tVoronoi procedure carried out on the cleaned version of the vegetation layer (see metadata for Terrestrial Phase 1 Habitat Survey).   Closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors carried out field recording of habitats onto 1:10,000 scale maps (rarely 1:25,000 during the upland stage). The minimum size of habitat patch recorded lies predominantly within the range 0.1-0.25 ha, although smaller areas were mapped in the lowlands. The survey methods employed during the initial upland stage are given in more detail in a WFU report (Day, A. (1989) Upland Vegetation Survey 1979-1989 Background Methodology and Summary of Data Collected. NCC, Bangor). Methods for the lowland survey followed the Handbook for Phase 1 Habitat Survey with some minor modifications adopted for use in Wales (Howe E.A. and Blackstock T.H. (1991) Supplementary Notes for Phase I Survey in Wales. CCW, Bangor).   The voronoi procedure was carried out in MapInfo to split complex upland mixed habitat polygons into component parts using a tool written in-house by the GIS and Spatial Data Team. All field mapping was carried out by trained and closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors. A study of survey reliability was conducted during the lowland phase (Kerry, Rimes, Smith, Williams. 1994. An Assessment of Errors in Phase I Habitat Survey in Wales. Bangor: Countryside Council for Wales, Report No. 94/4/1). It showed the field mapping to be 83% repeatable (Stevens, Blackstock, Howe, Stevens. 2004. Repeatability of Phase 1 habitat survey. J. Environ. Manage. 73, 53-59).The electronic data have undergone a series of checks for accuracy both by the contractor and subsequently within CCW. The extent of habitats that tend to occur in small patches such as upland flush and upland species-rich ledges may be over-estimated by the voronoi procedure.\tVoronoi maps are constructed from a series of polygons formed around the location of a sample point. Voronoi datasets form a derived mosaic allowing statistically derived polygons to be created from point data.\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Vegetation voronoi.lyr . Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\allwales_ph1 \\phase1_veg_voronoi.tab\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2014-11-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18160\tNRW_DS115876\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.723Z\t623\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Mosaic Layer Voronoi\tHaen Mosaig Tir Cymru Gyfan\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383736\tThis dataset is a GIS browser table that contains information on the habitats present and their proportions and areas in mosaic polygons. This method of dealing with mosaics is based on the approach used for mosaics in Phase 2 vegetation survey GIS datasets.    The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales 1km square dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage, and the maps were digitised as part of the project to map Open Access land.  The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales. Voronoi was carried out to enable full representation and visualisation of the components of complex mixed-habitat upland polygons and the mosaic browser lists habitats in mosaic polygons and gives proportions and area data.   Habitat mixtures were treated as equal splits, e.g. D.1.1 and E.2.1 in the string \u0027D.1.1 + E.2.1\u0027 were allocated proportions 1 : 1, and mosaics were treated as subsidiary divisions, e.g. the components of the string \u0027D.1.1 + D.2/E.2.1\u0027 were allocated proportions 2 : 1 : 1. Habitats in brackets were allocated proportions of 2 percent. The Phase 2 Tools were used to create a mospolys (mosaic polygons) layer to which the Phase 1 legend can be applied for better visualisation in GIS.\tVoronoi procedure carried out on the cleaned version of the vegetation layer, and mosaic browser created from this.   Closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors carried out field recording of habitats onto 1:10,000 scale maps (rarely 1:25,000 during the upland stage). The minimum size of habitat patch recorded lies predominantly within the range 0.1-0.25 ha, although smaller areas were mapped in the lowlands. The survey methods employed during the initial upland stage are given in more detail in a WFU report (Day, A. (1989) Upland Vegetation Survey 1979-1989 Background Methodology and Summary of Data Collected. NCC, Bangor). Methods for the lowland survey followed the Handbook for Phase 1 Habitat Survey with some minor modifications adopted for use in Wales (Howe E.A. and Blackstock T.H. (1991) Supplementary Notes for Phase I Survey in Wales. CCW, Bangor).   The voronoi procedure was carried out in MapInfo to split complex upland mixed habitat polygons into component parts using a tool written in-house by the GIS and Spatial Data Team within NRW.  A study of survey reliability was conducted during the lowland phase (Kerry, Rimes, Smith, Williams. 1994. An Assessment of Errors in Phase I Habitat Survey in Wales. Bangor: Countryside Council for Wales, Report No. 94/4/1). It showed the field mapping to be 83% repeatable (Stevens, Blackstock, Howe, Stevens. 2004. Repeatability of Phase 1 habitat survey. J. Environ. Manage. 73, 53-59).   The electronic data have undergone a series of checks for accuracy both by the contractor and subsequently within CCW. The extent of upland flush and upland species-rich ledges may be over-estimated by the voronoi procedure.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Mosnotes voronoi.lyr ,  X:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Mospolys voronoi.lyr ,  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\allwales_ph1 \\phase1_mosaic.tab\tInternal Custodian: Terrestrial mapping Scientist Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2014-11-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18161\tNRW_DS115877\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.740Z\t463\tUnassessed\tEditable\tQuantifying the Status of Great Crested Newts (Triturus cristatus) in Wales (2014)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383737\tThe Great Crested Newt (Triturus cristatus) is declining in the United Kingdom despite full European-level protection. Reporting on the species status to the EU is carried out at UK level but nature conservation is a devolved function. Knowledge and quantification of the Great Crested Newts status in Wales will therefore forms part of future UK wide reporting.  The purpose of this GIS based dataset was to: (i) Demonstrate the applicability of GIS techniques to quantification of conservation status parameters and predictive mapping; and, (ii) Produce regional, fine-scale spatial outputs (at 25 m resolution) which could be aggregated to create an objective quantification of spatial metrics for the Great Crested Newt across Wales.  Combined model outputs were good to excellent (AUC 0.71 to 0.86) and successfully quantified range (7,312 km squared), population (3,271 occupied ponds) and habitat (2,2170 km squared) parameters, as well as suggesting a proxy quantification for habitat quality (810 high quality ponds). Article 17 reporting is based on quantification of four parameters: range, population, habitat for the species, and future prospects.   Range: The defined range for the great crested newt in Wales is calculated using a convex hull algorithm in the R statistical software. The input data consisted of a collection of all known presence locations. The alpha value used was 8,000, arrived at by trialling several values and selecting the one most representative of the observed core distribution in Wales.  Population: Population is number of populations, equating to number of occupied ponds. To determine this number for Wales, the EQSS threshold for each model was used to determine the areas in which suitable ponds might be found. The suitable habitat was then clipped to the known range of the species in Wales and subsequently used to clip a UK wide pond dataset, extracted from Mastermap.   Habitat for the species: The EQSS outputs were converted into polygons, from which total area in km squared was derived. This area was then clipped to the known range of Great Crested Newts in Wales to provide an estimate of the suitable habitat available to the species within their range.   Future Prospects: It theoretically includes all factors potentially impacting great crested newts at sites and/or larger scales including (but not limited to) legislation, climate or site-specific factors. The UKs latest Article 17 report for the great crested newt lists Future Prospects as unknown. With application of the modelling processes described in the present report to Scotland and regions of England, confidence in habitat and population metrics could be improved to allow a UK-wide assessment of Future Prospects.\tThis dataset combines GIS model outputs from previous studies for Anglesey and North East Wales, Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park, and South Wales (Gower to Monmouthshire) to derive robust, repeatable Great Crested Newt status metrics for the species across its distribution in Wales.   Most Great Crested Newts record data collected by volunteers, but this analysis carried out by professional ecologists so confidence in this dataset is overall high. Fine-scale GIS outputs specifically to inform Great Crested Newt status in Wales were generated from Maximum Entropy Modelling (MaxEnt; also known as Multinomial Logistic Regression) for (i) Anglesey (ii) North East Wales (Flintshire, Denbighshire and Wrexham), (iii) Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park and (iv) South Wales (Gower Peninsula to Monmouthshire). These four areas, delimited by modern Welsh unitary authority boundaries, encompass the overwhelming majority of the natural range of Great Crested Newts in the country. The present study describes the aggregation of model outputs, and the generation of accurate and objective all-Wales status metrics which can be repeated to assess possible status changes in Wales, and could be applied to other regions and countries of the UK. Combining model outputs: The Equal Training Sensitivity and Specificity (EQSS) threshold was used to delineate areas of suitable Great Crested Newt habitat. Final combined outputs were clipped by the boundary of Wales for display and calculation purposes.\t\tA pdf of the report is available in Ffynnon: NRW-15-014399\tContact: Species Team Leader and Herpetologist Associated Report Title:  Quantifying the Status of Great Crested Newts in Wales.French GCA, Wilkinson JW, Fletcher DH, and Arnell AP. 2014. NRW Science Report 31  Ffynnon Number: NRW-15-014399\tAmphibian and Reptile Conservation-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18158\tNRW_DS115880\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.615Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 1 Intertidal Data from Dee\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383830\t\t\t\tData is archived under project 204 media 1185 (File path; K:\\Archive\\Media 1101to1200\\Media 1185)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18175\tNRW_DS115886\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.892Z\t270\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMenai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) muddy gravel survey (2013)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383836\tSeveral rich marine mud and muddy gravel habitats were identified within the Menai Strait by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Marine Intertidal Phase 1 survey team and a sampling programme initiated in order to better characterise the intertidal communities of these habitats. Marine mud and muddy gravel habitats are an important feature of several Special Area of Conservation (SACs), including the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay SACs. The purpose of this data capture was to carry out a repeat survey of sites previously surveyed to further test and develop monitoring methodology and collect monitoring data. In fully marine conditions on the lower shore, sheltered muddy gravel habitats can be particularly species rich due to the complex nature of the substratum supporting a high diversity of infauna and epifauna.\tIn-situ surveys were conducted.\t\tCurrently stored at: he data are archived as Project No 411, Media No xxx and is stored on backed-up server based storage at NRW Maes y Ffynnon, Bangor office.  H:\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MSCB\\2013 ASML MSCB  H:\\Science\\MFSG\\MarineRecorder\\MarineRecorderData\\MRCCW163_JenJones-original\\From Jen Jones 2014\\NBNData CCW163 JJ Pembs \u0026amp; Menai muddy gravel.mdb    Marine Recorder Survey MRCCW16300000031 2013 ASML /NRW Menai Strait \u0026amp; Conwy Bay SAC muddy gravel survey  H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2013 ASML MS\u0026amp;CB\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist NRW  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-06-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18168\tNRW_DS115887\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.868Z\t371\tUnassessed\tEditable\tComprehensive reassessment of Non Native Species in Welsh marinas (Marine Recorder dataset)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383837\tThe dataset is part of an ongoing rapid assessments for presence/absence of marine Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) in Welsh marinas.  Survey undertaken to assess the presence of non-native species in 15 marinas throughout Wales. Funding from obtained by Welsh Government Resilient Ecosystems Fund, which is administered by Natural Resources Wales (NRW). Comprehensive reassessment of non-native species on floating pontoons in Welsh marinas. The purpose of this data capture was to look at the spread of invasive non-natives in Wales and provide early warnings for new introductions with a possible view to employing eradication techniques.  This metadata only covers the data held in NRWs copy of marine recorder.\tA Rapid Assessment Survey was done at each marina. The available floating pontoons were split between the 3 surveyors. Each surveyor spent 1 hour searching their allocated area, examining and scraping pontoon sides, and examining any other substrates accessible from the surface e.g. ropes, buoys, fenders, kelps etc..  All 32 target Non Native Species presences and absences were noted and abundance estimated (using an abbreviated and adapted SACFORN scale where A\u003dSA (Super abundant or abundant), F\u003dCF (common or frequent), R\u003dOR (occasional or rare), N\u003dN (not-present)). Any other potential Non Native Species such as unidentified specimens, those requiring microscopical examination for species confirmation, and specimens to substantiate significant findings were relaxed, preserved and examined later in the laboratory. The majority of species were determined on site. This method focuses primarily on fouling organisms which live attached to solid substrates.  Funding was obtained by Welsh Government Resilient Ecosystems Fund, which is administered by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tThe initial survey took place in 2011 by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) now Natural Resources Wales. See metadata entry 114771 Across Wales Marine Non-Native Species Survey around Welsh Marinas ( extracted from Marine Recorder ).\t[A] Species data is held in Marine Recorder: 2014 NRW Comprehensive reassessment of NNS in Welsh marinas MRCCW32900000002 [B] Copy of the source NBN file currently stored at Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\Science\\MFSG\\MarineRecorder\\MarineRecorderData\\MRCCW329_BishopsGroup_MBA\\MRCCW329_NBNDATA.mdb  [C] A copy of the Marine Recorder species data is included on the NBN Gateway  Archived as project 457 Media 1518 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1501to1600\\Media 1518) A copy of the MEDIN format dataset is lodged at DASSH (likely to be condition of the WG funding).  NRW do not hold a copy in this format.  We are not publishing the metadata to MEDIN as we assume DASSH are.  DASSH identifier DASSHDT00000083\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team       We have not set this metadata entry to harvest to MEDIN portal because NRW only hold a copy of MR data, DASSH have the rest of the data and we assume that they are publishing metadata for the full data outputs. DASSH identifier DASSHDT00000083\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2014-07-03\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T17:26:24.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18156\tNRW_DS115889\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.255Z\t354\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Afon Tywi Shad Egg Survey (2013 and 2014)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383839\tThe Afon Tywi is designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for several features including twaite shad (Alosa fallax) and allis shad (Alosa alosa). Shads are migratory members of the Clupeidae (herring family) that ascend rivers to spawn in late spring. In Britain, significant populations of shad spawn in the Rivers Severn, Usk, Wye and Tywi. The populations of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and issues surrounding water quality. The aim of this data collection was to contribute towards the assessment the condition of the shad population in the Afon Tywi SAC based on 2013 and 2014 egg surveys. The objectives of this study are to: 1)  Conduct a shad egg survey on the Afon Tywi in 2013 and 2014.   2)  Analyse and present the results for spawning distribution of the population attribute only.   3)  Use the data obtained from the genetic analysis of shad egg tissue to give additional context to the above analysis.  2013 Shad eggs were present at seven of 13 survey sites (54 percent). Each of the 12 survey sites were visited once. The egg sample size ranged from 1 to 100 plus eggs. The downstream limit of spawning was Tidal limit (site 16) and the upstream limit was Manordeilo (site 11). A total of 177 plus eggs were recorded. The identification of shad eggs were confirmed through genetic analysis at two of the three selected sample sites.  2014  Shad eggs were recorded at eight of the 14 survey sites (57 percent). Seven of the sample sites were visited twice (54 percent) and four sites were visited three times (29 percent). The 14 sites were surveyed between 16 May and 11 Junes over 6 days by 13 surveyors.   Purpose of data capture:\tThe Hillman method is quantitative which involves counting shad eggs in 25 kick samples per site was used.  In 2013, the Hillman methodology was modified to a minimum of 10 kick samples were undertaken at each site with the number of eggs found in each kick recorded. If 20 or more shad eggs were recorded after 10 kicks the survey was terminated, however, if fewer than 20 shad eggs were recorded, kick sampling continued either until 20 eggs were found or 25 kick samples had been completed.   A standard macroinvertebrate hand net (250 micrometre) was used to collect material dislodged by kicking (or shuffling) upstream of the net for approximately 15 seconds. The net was checked for the presence of shad eggs after each kicking interval and any detritus or channel substrate removed before kicking was resumed. The number of shad eggs were counted after each kick sample and recorded on the field sheet. Sampling started at the downstream end of the potential spawning site to prevent the possible re-recording of eggs dislodged in previous kicks.  If possible, sites where no eggs were found were re-visited at a later date in the spawning season; however, this was dependant on weather conditions and availability of the survey team.  The JNCC have published generic guidance on Common Standards Monitoring Guidance for Freshwater Fauna outlining the attributes (mandatory and discretionary) and targets for monitoring freshwater fish. These include targets for shad population, flow, water quality and river morphology.  Assessments are made on a 6 year cycle. NRW are currently in the third monitoring cycle (2013-2018).  Both allis and twaite shad have been reported as unfavourable overall in the first (2001-2006) and second (2007-2012) reporting cycles.\tFreshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group. Also see associated NRW evidence report: Afon Tywi SAC shad egg surveys 2013 \u0026amp; 2014. Garrett HM, Thomas Rh, \u0026amp; Hatton-Ellis TW.  Report No. NRW Evidence Report No. 48  Date:  February 2015\t[A] Data is archived under Project Number: 459 Media Number: 1520.   [B] Field notes are held in Maes Newydd by the Technical Biodiversity Officer and River Officer, in Maes Ffynnon, under the Marine and Freshwater Ecosystem Group - Evidence and Analysis Team. [C] PDF of the report is held on the DMS Report Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports NRW Evidence Report No. 048 - Afon Tywi SAC shad egg surveys 2013 \u0026amp; 2014  KNOW-1206538853-57\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18157\tNRW_DS115890\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.604Z\t598\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe reproduction and connectivity of Sabellaria alveolata reefs in Wales - MAR4REF (2014)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383930\tThe reef building tube worm Sabellaria alveolata is an important ecosystem engineer on UK shorelines and is subsequently protected as a result of its limited spatial distribution in reef form. To effectively manage and reduce impacts on populations resulting from environmental change, more information is required to understand the distribution and development of S. alveolata reefs. The purpose of this data capture was it collect information to help understand the distribution and development of Sabellaria alveolata reefs in order to effectively manage and reduce impacts of populations resulting from environmental change. The associated report presents the findings from a Welsh Government Resilient Ecosystems Fund funded project lead by Bangor University investigating the reproduction and connectivity of S. alveolata reefs along Welsh coastlines.  Three key sites for data collation, Llanddulas (north Wales), Aberarth (mid-Wales) and Dunraven (south Wales), but expanded to the regional scale for modelling larval movements between major reef sites throughout the Irish Sea. The main research areas included:  1) Laboratory-based experiments to describe larval behaviour in relation to phototaxis and quantification of swimming speed throughout different larval development stages.  2) We assessed adult fecundity and egg sizes from worms collected from all three study sites every month from February-Sept 2014.  3) We measured larval densities in the water column at all three sites during the theorised reproductive period.  4) We produced estimates of population connectivity for different reef sites within Wales and determined major source and sink sites by using a biophysical model to create estimates of larval movement.\tMethodologies included: - Laboratory-based experiments  - Experiments and field surveys looking at phototactic behaviour (the movement of a organism to, or away from a light source) - Plankton netting - Measurements of adult fecundity, egg size and larval densities and produce estimates of population connectivity for different reef sites within Wales.  Survey, laboratory studies and analyses carried out by staff of the Bangor University.\t\tData and Report are Archived as project 460, media 1521 See project 404, media 1421, 1425, 1426, 1427\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-02-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:35:59.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18153\tNRW_DS115891\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.928Z\t353\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAllis (Alosa alosa) and Twaite (Alosa fallax) shad monitoring data sampled from the rivers Wye, Usk and Tywi, using Mitochondrial DNA techniques (2014).\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383931\tPopulations of allis shad (Alosa alosa Lacépède) and twaite shad (Alosa fallax L.) have declined across their geographic distribution over the last century and despite being widespread around the coast of the British Isles, UK spawning populations are largely restricted to the Rivers Severn, Wye, Usk and Tywi which drain into the Bristol Channel. As a result, both species are protected by UK and international legislation. In response to a need to monitor the population status to inform conservation objectives for these species, the former Countryside Council for Wales and Environment Agency Wales, now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) developed a semi-quantitative and cost-effective method to assess recruitment potential by sampling shad eggs. However, this method is unable to distinguish between the two Alosa species, and there is a risk that eggs of other fish species may erroneously be recorded as shad eggs.   The aim of this data collection was to use genetic techniques to address the weaknesses related to the semi-quantitative and cost-effective methods adopted being unable to distinguish between the two Alosa species, and the risks that other eggs related to other fish species being erroneously recorded.  The two objectives were to: (a) Determine whether the collected eggs belonged to Alosa spp.  (b) Determine the proportion of A. fallax and A. alosa using three different genetic markers – mitochondrial DNA and two types of nuclear DNA: nif1-nDNA and six microsatellite loci.  A total of 226 eggs were successfully genotyped. 85% belonged to Alosa spp. demonstrating that a good level of egg identification in the collection team. Non-Alosa eggs were identified as belonging to minnow (Phoxinus phoxinus) and chub (Squalius cephalus). As a result, the extent of putative shad spawning on the Tywi is slightly less than suggested by the egg surveys alone. However, the upper extent of shad spawning on the Wye was confirmed as being Brynwern Bridge, about 9km further upstream than the previous known upper limit at Builth Wells.    Methods of data capture:\tStaff were issued with clear instructions and standard field equipment including pre-labelled 1.5 ml Eppendorf tubes pre-loaded with buffer solution and coolboxes. Up to 20 eggs were collected from each sampling site and placed in ATL (lysing) buffer in pre-labelled 1.5ml Eppendorf tubes (Table 1). Eggs were either immediately couriered by overnight service to the laboratory, or else refrigerated overnight and couriered the following day. During the spawning season (June 2013), Natural Resources Wales (NRW) staff collected 240 individual eggs by kick sampling from 12 sampling sites in the rivers Tywi, Usk and Wye.  Two mitochondrial (mtDNA) haplogroups for the cytochrome B (CytB) gene have been previously identified in Alosa spp.; haplogroup A and haplogroup F are mainly associated with A. alosa and A. fallax respectively. Using the primers described in Alexandrino et al. (2006), a fragment of 401 bp of the (CytB) was successfully amplified. Out of the 226 eggs extracted, 191 samples were amplified using CytB specific primers.\tMonitoring Allis and Twaite Shad: Quality assurance and species Identification using molecular techniques.  Dr E Hardouin, Dr D Andreou and S Stuart Bournemouth University\tData is archived under project number 461 and media number 1522. Report is held on the DMS Report Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports.  NRW Evidence Report No. 053 - Monitoring Allis and Twaite Shad - quality assurance and species identification using molecular techniques  KNOW-1206538853-44\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:29:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18171\tNRW_DS115894\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.775Z\t238\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey of the spider Agroeca dentigera on Dyfi National Nature Reserve, with a particular focus on Ynylas Dunes (2013 to 2014).\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383934\tThe sand dune system at Ynyslas, part of Dyfi National Nature Reserve (NNR), is the only known UK locality for the IUCN Vulnerable liocranid spider Agroeca dentigera. The main purposes of the survey work in 2013 and 2014 was to re-locate the spider, last recorded in 2002 and attempt to define its habitat preferences and ecological requirements by searching the dunes and other potentially suitable habitats within the NNR.  The main purposes of the survey work in 2013 and 2014 was to re-locate the spider, last recorded in 2002 and attempt to define its habitat preferences and ecological requirements by searching the dunes and other potentially suitable habitats within the NNR. The aim of this survey was to determine the current status and distribution of the spider Agroeca dentigera.\tSites were visited in October 2013, April 2014 and October 2014 and surveyed using a combination of hand-searching and mechanical techniques. A total of 136 person hours were given to searching potential habitats. A single mature female of Agroeca dentigera was found on 3rd October 2014 in an area of dense marram within the dune system. In total 94 species of spider were recorded, including Philodromus fallax and Ceratinopsis romana, 11 of these species are thought to be new to Dyfi NNR. An additional 47 other invertebrate species were recorded, 16 of which are thought to be new to Dyfi NNR. Ynyslas Dunes, Dyfi NNR  2013 - 2014  High. Some voucher specimens have been retained and are in the collections in Liverpool Museum.    The 2014 record of Agroeca dentigera was again from dense marram in the dune system and searching of other potential habitats have so far produced negative results. So whilst it is still not possible to elucidate on its habitat preferences it seems likely to be associated with this ecosystem at Dyfi NNR. The efforts required to find the species suggest it occurs at very low densities, making it difficult to recommend how to direct future survey and monitoring protocols. A modified leaf blower was used on one occasion.  The data was provided to NRW and is to be transferred and made available through Recorder 6. Data transfer is currently underway.\t\tData being transferred to Recorder 6.  Report is available through the library. Reports Archive \u0026gt;  NRW Evidence Reports  KNOW-1206538853-47 NRW Evidence Report No. 054 - A survey of the spider Agroeca dentigera on Dyfi NNR, with a particular focus on Ynyslas Dunes in 2013 and 2014\tContact the Invertebrate Ecologist in NRW for additional information.   Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18164\tNRW_DS115896\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.775Z\t347\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring invertebrates by finger searching and pitfall trapping after excavation of the surface vegetation in three slacks at Newborough Warren - Ynys Llanddwyn SSSI (2014).\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383936\tThe purpose of this data capture was to monitor the colonisation of excavated inland dune slacks by specialist pioneer invertebrates on Newborough Warren National Nature Reserve (NNR).  This monitoring data was a continuation of the work undertaken in 2013. All invertebrates captured in the taxa Orthoptera, Heteroptera, Auchenorrhyncha, Coleoptera, Aculeata, Diplopoda, Chilopoda, Araneae and Opiliones were identified and counted. The numbers of the individual species are recorded in tables after being corrected for trapping effort. There was little evidence of any change from 2013 in the fauna on the exposed dune habitat. In the slack habitat exposed by excavation, a marked increase in the abundance of the rove beetle Bledius subniger was revealed by finger searching and mapping the distribution of its casts. Pitfall trapping in Slack 4 also revealed the abundance of the ground beetles Dyschirius impunctipennis and D. politus that are known predators of B. subniger. Another ground beetle, Bembidion pallidipenne, characteristic of embryonic slack habitat, was also abundant. It is tentatively concluded that this fauna has greatly increased, probably from breeding in 2013. Some observations are given to illustrate the bias inherent in pitfall trapping and the danger of too easily comparing abundance between species that are caught in pitfalls. There seemed to be no detectable increase in species of Heterocerus and Dryops. Slack 4 had two distinct areas of slack habitat that had been created by the excavation. In the southern part, there was a higher organic content in the soil and greater plant cover and diversity. This contrasted with the northern part which had little plant cover and a low organic content in the soil. These differences were reflected in a marked difference in the fauna of the two areas. The southern half contained species found in the unexcavated slacks that appear to require some shade but were probably eliminated from the other excavated slacks.\tThe methodology adopted for this survey work was pitfall trapping and visual searches. To date, six of the 16 beetle species recorded from Newborough Warren that are associated with pioneer dune slacks have been recorded from the excavated slacks, including the rove beetles Bledius subniger and Gabrius osseticus, the ground beetles Dyschirius politus and Bembidion pallidipenne, and Dryops nitidulus and Heterocerus flexuosus. The latter was first found here in 2014, with the other five being recorded in both 2013 and 2014. As only Dryops nitidulus has been recorded from the control Slack 1, in 2013, this suggests that these species are colonising the excavated slacks from remnant pockets of suitable habitat within the dune system rather than records representing a legacy of existing populations within the slacks. The data will be provided to NRW to be imported into Recorder 6. This is a very large dataset and it will take time to format it for import. Voucher specimens have been retained by the report author, some of which have been deposited in the collections in Liverpool Museum.\t\tData to be provided and added to Recorder 6.\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18163\tNRW_DS115898\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.714Z\t340\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMacrophyte Survey of Welsh Lakes 2014\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135383938\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) commissioned ECUS Ltd to collect and present ecological data from twenty-five lakes, across eight local authority areas in Wales. The data was needed to support NRW’s integrated monitoring programme for; the Water Framework Directive (WFD); protected sites including Special Areas of Conservation SACs, Special Protection Areas (SPAs) and Site of Special Scientific Importance (SSSIs), as well as other legislative and policy drivers. The lakes surveyed were selected for a number of reasons:  - SAC: Monitoring of a SAC feature (Natura 2000 Site) - SSSI: Monitoring of a Site of Special Scientific Interest feature; - WFD: Water Framework Directive surveillance network site - ND: Nitrates Directive Two notable species that were recorded during the surveys. Floating water-plantain Luronium natans, a European Protected Species, was recorded at seven lakes (Llyn Egnant, Llyn Gynon, Llyn Teifi, Llyn Cerrigllwydion Isaf, Gwnllyn, Llyn Padarn and Llyn Tegid). All of these are known locations for the species. Starry stonewort, Nitellopsis obtusa, is listed as Vulnerable on the UK Red List (JNCC, 2014). Starry stonewort was recorded at Llangorse Lake, making it only the second recorded site in Wales and a new record for this well-studied lake.\tAquatic macrophyte surveys were undertaken at all twenty five lakes, following the JNCC Common Standards Guidance / WFD Leafpacs2 Survey method. LEAFPACS metrics were calculated from the data. Depth profiles of temperature and dissolved oxygen were collected for a single point on each lake and observations on pressures impacting upon the lake were noted. Bathymetric surveys were completed on seven previously unsurveyed lakes and the data analysed using GIS software.\tBaxter, E, and Stewart, N. (2015), Macrophyte Survey of Welsh Lakes for Habitats Directive and Water Framework Directive Monitoring, 2014, Natural Resources Wales, NRW Evidence Report No: 52, 78pp.\tData is archived under Project number 463 Media Number 1524\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:35:58.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18166\tNRW_DS115954\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.795Z\t362\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of the condition of the white-clawed crayfish Austropotamobius pallipes in the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC) (2014).\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393534\tIn October and November 2014, parts of the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC) on the Afon Edw, Nant yr Offeiriad and Sgithwen Brook were surveyed for white-clawed crayfish to assess the condition of both the crayfish populations and crayfish habitat. The work was undertaken to provide some of the data required to assess whether the white-clawed crayfish in the River Wye SAC was in favourable condition.   Whilst further data will need to be collected before the full assessment can be made, the apparent lack of white-clawed crayfish in the Afon Edw, following a mortality in 2006, is worrying given that this has been a very important waterway for this species in the recent past. Whilst crayfish numbers were relatively robust in Nant yr Offeiriad and Sgithwen Brook, populations were mostly confined to the headwaters.  It is important to complete survey work for white-clawed crayfish on the River Wye SAC in 2015 so that the 2014 results can be amalgamated into an overall assessment.\tSampling followed the methodology developed during the 2003 monitoring programme, and included a combination of manual searching and trapping.  The data was provided to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and is to be transferred and made available through Recorder 6 in due course.\t\tData is held within the following report: Rogers D \u0026amp; Watson E 2015. Assessment of the condition of the white-clawed crayfish Austropotamobius pallipes in the River Wye Special Area of Conservation in 2014. NRW Evidence Report No: 74. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:04.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18154\tNRW_DS115976\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.921Z\t421\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntegrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\tCronfa Ddata’r Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393736\tThe Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) is updated continually and consists of several modules, each of which relate to protected sites across Wales. These modules include:  Casework - records of all consultations about development proposals and plans received by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and CCW\u0027s responses;  Consents and Assents - records for all Section 28 and Regulation 48 Consent and Assent requests received by CCW and CCW\u0027s responses;  Contacts - information of individuals and organisations who did business with CCW;   Features - qualifying and non-qualifying features (species and habitats) on Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs);    Land Agency - manages award and payment process for management agreements on NNRs and SSSIs, including land purchases, and buildings leases;  Licencing - details of the species licences granted by CCW to individuals and organisations;  Monitoring data - details about which SSSI Features have been monitored;  Open Access -  records of consultations in relation to the Countryside and Rights of Way (CRoW) Act 2000, in relation to parcels of land known as mountain, moor, heath or down;  Protected Sites - information on protected sites in Wales, and provides historical data on designation dates aiding the reporting process for designations in Wales; Site Actions - documents and shares information on who needs to do what, where, in order to get special conservation sites into favourable condition;  Wales Survey Inventory (WALSIN) - Index to all environmental surveys of NC/NCC since 1949 and bibliography of all files held by NCC, including archives from approximately 1900 until March 1992 (No Actual Data); (See Contains for more information)\tConfidence in the data is variable depending on the module.\tThis system was decommissioned, a copy of the the content was archived and data moved into new NRW systems, such as AMX and CRM.\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tGillian Walker, Data Manager Sites, Terrestrial Management Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW. Christine Edwards, Protected Sites Team Leader, NRW.  Support Desk Team Leader, Information Group, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-23T09:53:09.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18170\tNRW_DS115992\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.735Z\t422\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Sand Lizard  (Lacerta agilis) and Natterjack Toad (Epidalea calamita) Recovery Project 2011-2014\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393932\tThis dataset comprises of field observations of adults, spawn strings and habitat conditions for Natterjack Toads (Epidalea calamita) and Sand Lizards (Lacerta agilis). The data was collected for an assessment of current populations at re-introduction sites.   Natterjack Toad (Epidalea calamita) Natterjack toad breeding activity was recorded in all three sections of Gronant Dunes and Talacre Warren Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) in 2013. The breeding pools were monitored and the data presented to the annual recorders meetings.  A project to establish natterjacks at the Old Colliery site was started in 2009, but the initial translocation was unsuccessful. In 2010, Environment Agency Wales created about 30 pools at the site and further modified them for natterjacks in 2012. Their hydro-periodism was observed during 2013 and the translocation programme will start afresh in 2014.  In 2011 a total of only 168 spawn strings were recorded in Wales, whereas in 2012 was a record year was observed with 454 spawn strings counted. The relatively low spawn count in 2011 can be attributable to the dry weather that shortened the breeding season. In stark contrast to a dry start, 2012 became very wet and the natterjacks had a long and successful breeding season.  The total recorded in 2012 was the highest ever, a reflection of the strengthening population, good weather conditions and an increase in the number of pools at Talacre through the Million Ponds Project funding. The population and breeding range is likely to be at its highest level since the re-introduction project began in 1995.   Sand Lizard (Lacerta agilis) The sand lizard (Lacerta agilis) was formerly known from dune systems in north and west Wales. These known populations were lost due to erosion, development and increased public pressure. Natural Resources Wales (formerly CCW) has continued to provide funds to ascertain the status of the species via site monitoring and provided funding for captive breeding and translocation programmes, to restore the species former range. Monitoring at all of the translocation sites continued though was constrained by poor spring weather in 2013. Positive results of both adults and juveniles have been obtained at all established translocation sites.\tThe majority of the 2013 sand lizard monitoring data has been entered into the ARC and Cofnod databases, though we have not yet received all of the records.  The report collating information on further potential sand lizard translocations remains based on HCT, 2007. Short term \\priority\\\" options included both Newborough Warren National Nature Reserve (NNR) and Aberffraw Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI).\"\t\tRaw Data is held and distributed by ARC Trust.\tContact: Liz Howe Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tAmphibian and Reptile Conservation\t\t2015-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:28.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18155\tNRW_DS115995\t2024-05-28T12:41:32.127Z\t451\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: East Gwynedd, Gower and Glamorgan 1998\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393935\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in East Gwynedd, Gower and Glamorgan. The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around East Gwynedd, Gower and Glamorgan. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology.\tVegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6 Additional sites 1998 (E Gwynedd, Gower, Glamorgan). Prosser \u0026amp; Wallace CCW0007300000001\tMetadata contact: Spatial Data Team, Master Reference Data Team, and Data Services all of which are in Evidence Knowledge and Information Management, NRW  Data Custodian:  Liz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW (The old custodian was ex-CCW Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1998-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18152\tNRW_DS115996\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.927Z\t305\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Anglesey 1994\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393936\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Anglesey. The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Anglesey. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology.\tVegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type.\t\tData is stored in Recorder 6 under CCW0007300000002\tAJ Turner from the North Wales Wildlife Trust was the original contractor.  Metadata contact: Spatial Data Team, Master Reference Data Team, and Data Services all of which are in Evidence Knowledge and Information Management, NRW  Data Custodian:  Liz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW (The old custodian was ex-CCW Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1998-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18149\tNRW_DS115997\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.524Z\t316\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Brecknock and Carmarthen 2000\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393937\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Brecknock and Carmarthen. The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Brecknock and Carmarthen. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology.\tVegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6 CCW0007300000003.\tMetadata contact: Spatial Data Team, Master Reference Data Team, and Data Services all of which are in Evidence Knowledge and Information Management, NRW Data Custodian:  Liz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW (The old custodian was ex-CCW Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2000-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:24.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18148\tNRW_DS115998\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.489Z\t549\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Ceredigion 2001\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393938\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Ceredigion. The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Ceredigion. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology.\tVegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6 CCW0007300000004\tMetadata contact: Spatial Data Team, Master Reference Data Team, and Data Services all of which are in Evidence Knowledge and Information Management, NRW  Data Custodian:  Liz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW (The old custodian was ex-CCW Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18151\tNRW_DS115999\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.476Z\t313\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Gower 1997\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313135393939\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in the Gower, south Wales. The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around the Gower. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology.\tVegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type.\t\t[A] Data stored in Recorder 6 CCW0007300000005.\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1991-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18150\tNRW_DS116000\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.456Z\t288\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Gwynedd 1994\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303030\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Gwynedd. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tThis was one of a series of major Phase 2 habitat surveys, including CCW in-house and commissioned surveys covering woodland, lowland grassland, saltmarsh and lowland peatland. The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type. The paper maps in the reports are of better quality than the GIS layer and therefore should be referred to as a more accurate source of information. Age of the data is also a limitation as habitat composition may change over time, a record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6: CCW0007300000014.\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1994-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18147\tNRW_DS116001\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.328Z\t253\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Gwynedd Supplement 1994 - 1995\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303031\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Gwynedd. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tThis was one of a series of major Phase 2 habitat surveys, including CCW in-house and commissioned surveys covering woodland, lowland grassland, saltmarsh and lowland peatland. The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type. The paper maps in the reports are of better quality than the GIS layer and therefore should be referred to as a more accurate source of information. Age of the data is also a limitation as habitat composition may change over time, a record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6: Gwynedd suppliment 1994-5. Prosser \u0026amp; Wallace. CCW0007300000006\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1995-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18146\tNRW_DS116002\t2024-05-28T12:41:31.157Z\t528\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Pembrokeshire 1995\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303032\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Pembrokeshire. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tThis was one of a series of major Phase 2 habitat surveys, including CCW in-house and commissioned surveys covering woodland, lowland grassland, saltmarsh and lowland peatland. The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type. The paper maps in the reports are of better quality than the GIS layer and therefore should be referred to as a more accurate source of information. Age of the data is also a limitation as habitat composition may change over time, a record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6: CCW0007300000007\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1995-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:42.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18197\tNRW_DS116003\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.613Z\t325\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Pembrokeshire 1996\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303033\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Pembrokeshire. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology.\tVegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6: CCW0007300000008\tMetadata contact: Spatial Data Team, Master Reference Data Team, and Data Services all of which are in Evidence Knowledge and Information Management, NRW Data Custodian:  Liz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW (The old custodian was ex-CCW Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1996-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:28.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18203\tNRW_DS116004\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.678Z\t290\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Pembrokeshire 1997\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303034\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Pembrokeshire. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tThis was one of a series of major Phase 2 habitat surveys, including CCW in-house and commissioned surveys covering woodland, lowland grassland, saltmarsh and lowland peatland. The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type. The paper maps in the reports are of better quality than the GIS layer and therefore should be referred to as a more accurate source of information. Age of the data is also a limitation as habitat composition may change over time, a record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6: CCW0007300000009.\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18189\tNRW_DS116005\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.609Z\t281\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Powys 1999\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303035\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Powys. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tThis was one of a series of major Phase 2 habitat surveys, including CCW in-house and commissioned surveys covering woodland, lowland grassland, saltmarsh and lowland peatland. The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type. The paper maps in the reports are of better quality than the GIS layer and therefore should be referred to as a more accurate source of information. Age of the data is also a limitation as habitat composition may change over time, a record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.\t\tspecies data held on Recorder 6: CCW0007300000010.\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:28.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18201\tNRW_DS116006\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.664Z\t518\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Ramsey 1995\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303036\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Ramsey. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tThis was one of a series of major Phase 2 habitat surveys, including CCW in-house and commissioned surveys covering woodland, lowland grassland, saltmarsh and lowland peatland. The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type. The paper maps in the reports are of better quality than the GIS layer and therefore should be referred to as a more accurate source of information. Age of the data is also a limitation as habitat composition may change over time, a record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6: CCW0007300000015.\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1995-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18190\tNRW_DS116007\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.604Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Rayers and Harding 2001\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303037\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around the Gower. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tThis was one of a series of major Phase 2 habitat surveys, including CCW in-house and commissioned surveys covering woodland, lowland grassland, saltmarsh and lowland peatland. The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type. The paper maps in the reports are of better quality than the GIS layer and therefore should be referred to as a more accurate source of information. Age of the data is also a limitation as habitat composition may change over time, a record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6: CCW0007300000011.\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18191\tNRW_DS116008\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.639Z\t271\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: West Gwynedd 1994\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303038\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around West Gwynedd. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tThis was one of a series of major Phase 2 habitat surveys, including CCW in-house and commissioned surveys covering woodland, lowland grassland, saltmarsh and lowland peatland. The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type. The paper maps in the reports are of better quality than the GIS layer and therefore should be referred to as a more accurate source of information. Age of the data is also a limitation as habitat composition may change over time, a record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6: CCW0007300000012.\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1993-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18204\tNRW_DS116009\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.647Z\t257\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Survey: Ystwyth and Rheidol shingle heaths 1995\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303039\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 plant community data for lowland heathlands in Wales. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The aim of the survey was to characterise the lowland heathland vegetation of around Ystwyth and Rheidol. Utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).\tThis was one of a series of major Phase 2 habitat surveys, including CCW in-house and commissioned surveys covering woodland, lowland grassland, saltmarsh and lowland peatland. The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type. The paper maps in the reports are of better quality than the GIS layer and therefore should be referred to as a more accurate source of information. Age of the data is also a limitation as habitat composition may change over time, a record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6: CCW0007300000013.\tInternal Custodian: Liz Howe, (used to be Jan Sherry) Species Team Leader and Herpetologist, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1995-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18205\tNRW_DS116010\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.996Z\t366\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Carmarthenshire and Llanelli Woodlands (1994 - 2005)\tArolwg Coetir Cam 2 o Goetiroedd Sir Gaerfyrddin a Llanelli (1994 - 2005)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303130\tThis is a dataset containing detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 4607 observations in woodlands of conservation interest across Carmarthenshire and Llanelli woodlands. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales.    The purpose of this work was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities, to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland SSSI series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites.\tDataset is a collation of CCW commissioned NVC surveys. It is one of a series of major Phase 2 habitat surveys, including in-house and commissioned surveys covering lowland grassland, saltmarsh, lowland peatland and lowland heathland. The standard woodland NVC survey methodology was used.     Data was collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by CCW Knowledge Management Group, corrections made by contractors, validated again by CCW Knowledge Management Group, corrections made in-house in conjunction with Woodland Ecologist.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6 database CCW0007500000003 Carmarthenshire and Llanelli Woodlands.\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-06-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T09:47:28.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18192\tNRW_DS116011\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.646Z\t667\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Dinefwr Woodlands 1994\tArolwg Coetir Cam 2 o Goetiroedd Dinefwr 1994\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303131\tThis dataset contains detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 1145 observations in woodlands of conservation interest in and around Carmarthen and Dinefwr woodlands. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales.    The standard woodland NVC survey methodology was used.    The purpose of this work was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities, and to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland SSSI series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites.\tThe dataset is a collation of CCW commissioned NVC surveys. It is one of a series of major Phase 2 habitat surveys, including in-house and commissioned surveys covering lowland grassland, saltmarsh, lowland peatland and lowland heathland.    Data was collected by trained personnel, digitised by contractors, validated by CCW Knowledge Management Group, corrections made by contractors, validated again by CCW Knowledge Management Group, corrections made in-house in conjunction with Woodland Ecologist.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6 database CCW0007500000002.\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1994-09-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T09:55:18.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18195\tNRW_DS116012\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.602Z\t532\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Dinefwr Woodlands (1993 - 1994)\tArolwg Coetir Cam 2 o Goetiroedd Dinefwr (1993 - 1994)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303132\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 7140 observations in woodlands of conservation interest across Dinefwr woodlands. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales.     The purpose of this work was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities, to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites.\tDataset is a collation of CCW commissioned NVC surveys. It is one of a series of major Phase 2 habitat surveys, including in-house and commissioned surveys covering lowland grassland, saltmarsh, lowland peatland and lowland heathland.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6 database CCW0007500000005 ET Dinefwr Woodlands.\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1994-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T09:49:54.012Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18196\tNRW_DS116013\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.664Z\t226\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Welsh Wet Woodlands 1995\tArolwg Coetir Cam 2 o Goetiroedd Gwlyb Cymru 1995\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303133\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 3247 observations in woodlands of conservation interest in across welsh wet woodlands. One of the major strategic initiatives of Countryside Council for Wales.    The standard woodland NVC survey methodology was used.    The purpose of this work was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities, and to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites.\tPart of the data within this dataset doesn\u0027t have a temporal extent assigned to it.   Dataset is a collation of CCW commissioned NVC surveys. It is one of a series of major Phase 2 habitat surveys, including in-house and commissioned surveys covering lowland grassland, saltmarsh, lowland peatland and lowland heathland.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6 database CCW0007500000004 Welsh Wet Woodlands\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1995-09-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T13:25:13.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18188\tNRW_DS116014\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.510Z\t292\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Welsh Woodlands (1996 - 1997)\tArolwg Coetir Cam 2 o Goetiroedd Cymru (1996 - 1997)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136303134\tDataset containing detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 11833 observations in woodlands of conservation interest in across Wales.   The purpose of this work was to improve the overall knowledge of Welsh woodland communities, and to provide consistent community descriptions across the woodland Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) series as fundamental site-support information and to assess SSSI status of new sites.\tSome of the data within this dataset does not have an assigned temporal extent.    Dataset is a collation of CCW commissioned NVC surveys. It is one of a series of major Phase 2 habitat surveys, including in-house and commissioned surveys covering lowland grassland, saltmarsh, lowland peatland and lowland heathland.\t\tData stored in Recorder 6 database CCW0007500000001 Welsh Woodlands.\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t1997-09-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T13:38:57.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18194\tNRW_DS116137\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.662Z\t566\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcoustic multibeam survey of St Bride\u0027s Bay (MBES data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136313337\tThis dataset shows the results of a bathymetric survey of St Brides Bay, Pembrokeshire. This dataset was owned by Environment Agency, however with the formation of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) in 2013, ownership was transferred to NRW.\tThe gridded data was supplied in both BGN and UTM formats.\t\tThe report and data is archived as Project No 399, Media No 1416 (K\\:archive\\Media 1401-1500\\Media 1556) project 399 EA (Environment Agency) Acoustic multibeam survey  of St Bride\u0027s Bay [A] xyz data [B] multibeam tiffs [C] Surpporting information about any processing and source of data in form of emails saved as txt files\tInternal Custodian: Charlie Lindenbaum, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer,  Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW       Data provided to Pete Walker from Mike Camplin (Pete thinks that it came straight from Alison Miles at the EA in 2007). There is no metadata or additional information other than that stored with the data.  Just emails from Charlie Lindenbaum and Lucie Skates.  Don\u0027t think any internal CCW processing was done\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:14.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18200\tNRW_DS116210\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.714Z\t270\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRare and Protected Species Records across Wales (1975 to 2012)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323130\tThe aim of this data collection was to monitor the distribution of rare and protected species across Wales.  This dataset is primarily made up of aquatic and riparian species (marine and freshwater) including invertebrates, plants, algae and fish.  The data includes ad-hoc records from surveys at local offices.  Data collection started in the 1970s onwards.  This dataset does not include records of Freshwater Pearl Mussel, but otherwise includes all Rare and Protected species surveyed by the former Environment Agency Wales, now Natural Resources Wales.\tRare and Protected Species are surveyed and collected through the former Environment Agency\u0027s Wales monitoring activities and from 1970 to 2013. Since 2013 all data collection for Wales was conducted by Natural Resources Wales.  Data are extracted from National Fish Population Database (NFPD).\t\t[A] This data is now available on the NBN Gateway [B] Original data on Wiski-Kieco\tInternal Custodian: Melissa Lacan, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, SE Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:46.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18186\tNRW_DS116211\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.434Z\t548\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNon Native Species Across Wales (1989 to 2012) (Extracted from BIOSYS)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323131\tThe aim of this data capture was to monitor the distribution of non-native species across Wales. Any non-native species present in a sample were recorded when they were of the target group, for example Signal Crayfish (Pacifastacus lenisculus) in a benthic invertebrate sample or Canadian pondweed (Elodea Canadensis) in a plant survey. Other non-native species are recorded when seen at or around monitoring sites. At the time of surveying between 1970s and 2012, non-native species did not have a specific monitoring programme.\tThe primary purpose of this data capture was to monitoring the distribution of aquatic and riparian species.  Whilst these surveys were conducted the location and date of surveys which included non-native species was also recorded. This dataset is split into one whole non native species layerfile and individual species, such as Crayfish Non Native Species.\t\t[A] This data is now available on the NBN Atlas [B] X:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\BIOSYS Invasive non native species.lyr  [C] X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Crayfish Non Native Species.lyr  [D] Original data is on Wiski -Kieco database\tInternal Custodian: Melissa Lacan, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, SE Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18193\tNRW_DS116212\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.634Z\t280\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNative Freshwater Species Surveys Across Wales (1978 to 2019)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323132\tThe purpose of this data capture was to primarily monitor the distribution of freshwater invertebrates.  The data comprises primarily of the Welsh abstract of freshwater invertebrates. It comes from BIOSYS, the national ecology database for freshwater species and is related to the aquatic stages of the invertebrates. Most data have been collected from main rivers as part of an extensive routine monitoring network. The data are restricted to a selection of families which contain one species only.  The records cover the whole of Wales. Sites are recorded at a minimum precision of 100m. The distribution of records is biased towards main rivers and areas with historical water quality issues. This is especially true of older records. The sampling methodologies and taxa identified are the same at all sites.    NB This data set has been expanded to include all freshwater data from 1978 to 2019 at all taxonomic levels. Includes river \u0026amp; lake macroinvertabrates, river \u0026amp; lake diatoms and river macrophytes.\tThis data was collected by ecologists. Most of the data originate from kick samples taken in main rivers and have been collected and analysed using standard sampling methodologies. The data was extracted for Wales from the Environment Wiski Kieco and does not include older records held in paper copy only. Records for families, genera and species from chosen single species families have been extracted and are reported at the species level.    NB This data set has been expanded to include all freshwater data from 1978 to 2019 at all taxonomic levels. Includes river \u0026amp; lake macroinvertabrates, river \u0026amp; lake diatoms and river macrophytes\t\t[A] This data is available on the NBN Atlas.  [B] Original data held on Wiski-Kieco\tInternal Custodian: Melissa Lacan, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, SE Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-03-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:27:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18187\tNRW_DS116215\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.554Z\t341\tUnassessed\tEditable\tConsented Discharges to Controlled Waters (Archived)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323135\tThis data provides details of permit details as required under the Environmental Permit Regulations. Information is held for all permit holders and covers all substances that are controlled. This data is taken from the Permit and Licencing System (PALS)\tData is collated from the Permit and Licencing System (PALS). This dataset is no longer a stand alone dataset and now combined and available as part of the dataset: NRW_DS116329 Consented Discharges to Controlled Waters with Conditions\t\tExtracted post 26/06/2016 from PALS previously extracted from WIMS in Environment Agency Universe. WIMS data is stored in a static dataset on Y:DRive and Sharefile\tHenry Forrest, Permitting Officer, Wales Permitting Centre Cardiff (Water Resources), NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:48.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18198\tNRW_DS116223\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.670Z\t717\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAd hoc North Wales Moth Records 2013 to 2014\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323233\tThis is a third party dataset held by Natural Resources Wales. This dataset was collected as part of a sabbatical taken by Ian Sims. It consists of moth records and sightings obtained between 2013 and 2014 in North Wales. In all over 3113 records of over 11535 moths of 408 species were recorded, including 11 Nationally Notable species, Sandhill Rustic (Luperina nickerlii gueneei), (Na/RDB), Ashworth’s Rustic (Xestia ashworthii) (Na), Sand Dart (Agrotis ripae) (Nb), Anerastia lotella (Nb), Blomer’s Rivulet (Discoloxia blomeri) (Nb), Scarce Water-veneer (Donacaula mucronellus) (Nb), Homoeosoma nebulella (Nb), Marsh Oblique-striped (Hypenodes humidalis) (Nb), Shore Wainscot (Mythimna litoralis) (Nb), Pediasia aridella (Nb) and Pseudopostega crepusculella (Nb). Of the remaining species 69 were classified as  \u0027Local\u0027, 327 as \u0027common\u0027 with one \u0027Immigrant\u0027. 42 BAP species were identified, along with 7 Endangered and 32 Vulnerable species. At least an additional four species were identified to family level only.  This data is now part of Natural Resources Wales\u0027 Welsh Invertebrate Dataset.  This datasets is already available on the NBN Gateway and therefore is not required to be published by Natural Resources Wales.\tSurveys were undertaken almost entirely by light-trapping of adult moths at night, with traps emptied and species identified from the early morning. Individuals which could not be identified quickly in the field were potted up and kept for later identification. No live moths were removed from sites. All identification was visual; no dissection was used at all, although on one or two occasions moths which were found dead were kept as voucher specimens. Photos of some of the more unusual or difficult species were taken and sent to John Harold (Anglesey County Recorder) and/or Andrew Graham (Gwynedd County Recorder) for identification / confirmation. Twelve nights’ light-trapping were undertaken between 5th July and 6th September 2013 and 13th June and 6th September 2014 at five sites in Gwynedd and Anglesey: RSPB Tan yr Allt, RSPB Ystumllyn, RSPB Malltraeth Marsh, Newborough Warren NNR, and Coed Dolgarrog National Nature Reserve. A total of thirteen different traps were used: three 125w Mercury Vapour ‘Robinson’ traps, a 125w Mercury Vapour ‘Skinner’, and nine actinic traps: five 15w, a 25w and three 6w, all (except one 15w ‘Skinner’ type) using the heath-trap design, home-made or otherwise. An additional 53 nights’ trapping were also undertaken on RSPB reserves (Malltraeth Marsh, Valley Wetlands and South Stack) outside of sabbatical time, and these records are included in this report. Records from other non-designated sites (146 nights’ trapping) have been sent to county recorders but are not included here.\tAssociated Report: Sims, I. 2015 Sabbatical 2013 to 2014: Moths of North-West Wales pp.32.\tData is stored in Recorder 6 under the Welsh Invertebrate Database. Recorder survey: Ian Sims sabbatical moth records 2013 - 2014 CCW000800000000Z.\tGIS Service Desk Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist MFSG, NRW External Custodian and Originator: Ian Sims, RSPB North Wales.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-12-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18199\tNRW_DS116225\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.649Z\t791\tValid\tEditable\tSpatial Flood Defences with Standardised Attributes (archived data)\tAmddiffynfeydd Llifogydd Gofodol (gan gynnwys priodoleddau safonedig)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323235\tThis dataset has been superseded by the dataset \"Flood Defence Structures\" (NRW_DS125266).  This dataset shows flood defences that have been built to protect against flooding from rivers and the sea. The defences shown provide different levels of flood protection and this is recognised in the risk classification shown in the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) map.   Flood defences that are not yet shown, and the areas that benefit from them, will be gradually added.    Flood defences do not remove the risk of flooding however and can be overtopped or fail in extreme weather conditions.\tThis dataset Is based on the geographical location of flood defences in Wales. The data is taken from the Asset Management Expert (AMX) database which is regularly updated by NRW local teams.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Defences with standardised attributes.lyr  Stored in AMX but made available on the Y drive\tData Custodian: Mark Pugh- Reservoir Safety Team Metadata Contact - Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:28.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18185\tNRW_DS116226\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.200Z\t1448\tValid\tEditable\tRecorded Flood Outlines (archived data)\tMeintiau Llifogydd wedi\u0027u Cofnodi\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323236\tThis dataset has been archived and has been superceded by  NRW_DS116228 Historic Flood Outlines.  Recorded Flood Outlines contains the individual location outline and approved attributes for records of historic flooding. Please note that these records show flooding to the land and do not necessarily indicate that properties within the historic flood extents were flooded internally.   It is also possible that the pattern of flooding in this area has changed and that this area would now flood under different circumstances. In addition absence of a historic flood event for an area does not mean that the area has never flooded only that we do not currently have records of flooding in this area.-Mae Meintiau Llifogydd wedi\u0027u Cofnodi yn dangos ardaloedd lle ceir cofnodion o lifogydd yn y gorffennol o afonydd, y môr neu ddŵr wyneb. Daw\u0027r cofnodion o nifer o ffynonellau tystiolaeth, gan gynnwys Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru, ei ragflaenyddion neu awdurdodau rheoli risg eraill.  Mae\u0027n bosibl bod y patrwm llifogydd mewn ardal wedi newid a byddai llifogydd yn digwydd ynddi yn awr o dan amgylchiadau gwahanol. Yn ogystal, nid yw absenoldeb maint llifogydd wedi\u0027i gofnodi\u0027n golygu nad yw\u0027r ardal wedi cael llifogydd erioed, dim ond nad oes gennym unrhyw gofnodion o lifogydd yn yr ardal hon ar hyn o bryd.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tSince September 2017 this dataset has not been updated. See Product NRW_DS116228 Historic Flood Outlines.  Information is gathered from staff site surveys and visual data submitted by witnesses to a flood event. The flood outlines and associated information is entered into desktop GIS systems and used to update Floods Geodatabase in the quarterly updates to the floodmap products. Data is edited quarterly by Flood Risk Analysis Teams. GIS \u0026amp; Data teams edit the layers in the Flood Geodatabase\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\ Recorded flood outlines.lyr\tCustodian: Nick Steel, Flood Risk Analysis/FCRM / (Previously) Mark Pugh, Flood \u0026amp; Operational Risk Management, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2019-04-29\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T08:57:28.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18184\tNRW_DS116227\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.180Z\t1084\tValid\tEditable\tBathing Water Quality at Designated Beaches\tAnsawdd Dyfroedd Ymdrochi mewn Traethau Dynodedig\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323237\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) monitors bathing water sites identified under EC Bathing Waters Directive 2006/7/EC in Wales. Samples are taken throughout the bathing season from May to the end of September and analysed for two parameters: Escherichia coli and intestinal enterococci. A classification of bathing water quality, generally based on 4 years of sample results, is produced for each bathing water at the end of each season.-Mae Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru\u0027n monitro safleoedd dŵr nofio yn Nghymru a ganfyddwyd o fewn Cyfarwyddeb Dyfroedd Nofio CE 2006/7. Cymerir samplau trwy gydol y tymor nofio o fis Mai hyd at ddiwedd Medi ac maen nhw\u0027n cael eu dadansoddi am ddau baramedr: Escherichia coli ac enterococci coluddol. Cyflwynir dosbarthiad ansawdd dŵr nofio, fel arfer yn seiliedig ar bedair blynedd o ganlyniadau sampl, ar gyfer pob dŵr nofio ar ddiwedd pob tymor.\tThis dataset consists of 6 tables:   Bathing Water Sites;  Seasonal Info;  Monitoring Results; Abnormal Situations;  Short term Pollution;  Bathing Water Classification.  The data is extracted from the NRW WISKI (KiWQM) System data which holds other information such as environmental monitoring and water quality information. This is a non GIS dataset. For spatial data, please see metadata entries NRW_DS123239 and NRW_DS124755.  The most up to date information on bathing water quality can be found at https://environment.data.gov.uk/wales/bathing-waters/profiles/\t\tExtracted from the WISKI KwiQm database and available on Sharefile.  Originally this data was extracted from WIMS Following transformation from WIMS to WISKI, future data will be extracted from WISKI as follows (2017 - jointly extracted from WIMS \u0026amp; WISKI, 2018 onwards - WISKI only)    There are also 4 GIS layers that are created from the same data in WISKI KwiQM.  These have their own metadata entries:   EC sites: NRW_DS124755 X:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\EC Bathing Waters.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\EC Bathing Waters Classification.lyr  Non-EC sites:  NRW_DS123239 X:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\Non EC Bathing Waters.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\Non EC Bathing Waters Classification.lyr\tCustodian: Helena Sykes Officer, Environmental Information, Environmental Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-02-19\tpublication\tnull\thttps://environment.data.gov.uk/wales/bathing-waters/profiles/\t2025-12-19T10:04:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18182\tNRW_DS116228\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.945Z\t1371\tValid\tEditable\tRecorded Flood Extents\tMeintiau Llifogydd wedi\u0027u Cofnodi\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323238\tRecorded Flood Extents shows areas that have been recorded to have flooded in the past from rivers, the sea or surface water. The records come from a number of evidence sources including Natural Resources Wales, its predecessors or other Risk Management Authorities.    It is possible that the pattern of flooding in an area may have changed and would now flood under different circumstances. In addition, the absence of a recorded flood extent does not mean that the area has never flooded, only that we do not currently have records of flooding in this area.-Mae Meintiau Llifogydd wedi\u0027u Cofnodi yn dangos ardaloedd lle ceir cofnodion o lifogydd yn y gorffennol o afonydd, y môr neu ddŵr wyneb. Daw\u0027r cofnodion o nifer o ffynonellau tystiolaeth, gan gynnwys Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru, ei ragflaenyddion neu awdurdodau rheoli risg eraill.  Mae\u0027n bosibl bod y patrwm llifogydd mewn ardal wedi newid a byddai llifogydd yn digwydd ynddi yn awr o dan amgylchiadau gwahanol. Yn ogystal, nid yw absenoldeb maint llifogydd wedi\u0027i gofnodi\u0027n golygu nad yw\u0027r ardal wedi cael llifogydd erioed, dim ond nad oes gennym unrhyw gofnodion o lifogydd yn yr ardal hon ar hyn o bryd.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe data is updated every three months, but may not change quarter to quarter if there have not been any significant flood events in the preceding period.    Recorded Flood Extents are digitised in ArcGIS from a number of sources including surveyed flood events.  Please note that this map shows flooding to the land and does not necessarily indicate that properties within the Historic Flood Map were flooded internally.    This dataset was updated in May 2017 to include attribution data on the flood events. This was previously made available in the product \"Recorded Flood Outlines\" (NRW_DS116226) which is no longer updated and no longer available.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Historic Floodmap.lyr\tInternal custodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-11-27\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/346/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_HISTORIC_FLOODMAP\t2025-11-27T15:21:29.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18180\tNRW_DS116229\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.822Z\t915\tValid\tEditable\tEmissions Inventory\tRhestr o Ollyngiadau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323239\tThe Emissions Inventory (EI) dataset collects data from regulated industrial activities and includes annual emissions to air and controlled waters, off-site transfers of waste and transfers of pollutants to wastewater.   The NRW EI includes all of the data from Wales that is published on the UK Pollutant Release and Transfer Register (UK PRTR) but also includes further emissions that are under the UK PRTR threshold.   The published dataset has been redacted to not include confidential permits.-Mae\u0027r set ddata Rhestr Allyriadau yn casglu data o weithgareddau diwydiannol a reoleiddir ac mae\u0027n cynnwys allyriadau blynyddol i’r aer a dyfroedd rheoledig, trosglwyddo gwastraff oddi ar safleoedd a throsglwyddo llygryddion i ddŵr gwastraff. Mae Rhestr Allyriadau CNC yn cynnwys yr holl ddata o Gymru a gyhoeddir ar Gofrestr Rhyddhau a Throsglwyddo Llygryddion y DU (UK PRTR) ond mae hefyd yn cynnwys rhai allyriadau pellach sydd o dan drothwy’r UK PRTR.  Mae\u0027r Rhestr Allyriadau yn cynnwys adrodd ar allyriadau blynyddol rhai sylweddau penodol i\u0027r aer, tir a dyfroedd a reolir, a throsglwyddiadau dŵr gwastraff a gwastraff oddi ar safleoedd.  Rhaid i chi adrodd i\u0027r Rhestr Allyriadau os ydych:  yn gweithredu o dan Drwydded Amgylcheddol Rhan A(1) ac wedi cael hysbysiad o dan Reoliad 60 o Reoliadau Trwyddedu Amgylcheddol 2010 yn gweithredu gwaith trin carthion gyda chapasiti o 100,000 neu fwy o unedau cyfwerth â phoblogaeth (PE) yn cael gwared ar wastraff ymbelydrol i aer, dŵr neu garthffosydd sy’n dod o dan drwydded a roddwyd o dan Reoliadau Trwyddedu Amgylcheddol 2010 yn rhedeg chwarel neu fwynglawdd brig gydag arwynebedd o dros 25 hectar, neu fwynglawdd tanddaearol a gwaith cysylltiedig (dim trothwy capasiti). Mae\u0027r set ddata a gyhoeddwyd wedi\u0027i golygu i beidio â chynnwys trwyddedau cyfrinachol.  Datganiad priodoli Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe EI includes reporting on annual emissions of certain substances to air, controlled waters and land, and off-site transfers in wastewater and waste.   You must report to the EI if you:  - operate under a Part A (1) Environmental Permit and have received a notice under Regulation 60 of the Environmental Permitting Regulations 2010  - operate a sewage treatment works with a capacity at, or over, 100,000 population equivalents (PE)  - dispose of radioactive waste to air, water or sewers covered by a permit issued under the Environmental Permitting Regulations 2010  - run an opencast mine or quarry with a surface area over 25 hectares, or an underground mine and related operation (no capacity threshold).\t\tZ:\\Archive\\Sampling Monitoring \u0026amp; Analysis Reporting\\Reporting\\Statutory Reporting\\Industrial Emissions\tCustodian: Amy Healey, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2025-11-17\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/5879\t2025-12-01T17:12:03.061Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18183\tNRW_DS116230\t2024-05-28T12:42:29.199Z\t1705\tValid\tEditable\tSource Protection Zones (SPZ) Merged\tArdaloedd Diogelu Tarddiad Dŵr Cyfunedig\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323330\tSource Protection Zones [Merged] have been created as public facing boundaries where individual groundwater bodies within Source Protection Zones (SPZ) have been dissolved so only the outer boundaries are shown. SPZs are defined around large and public potable groundwater abstraction sites. The purpose of SPZs is to provide additional protection to safeguard drinking water quality through constraining the proximity of an activity that may impact upon a drinking water abstraction.-Mae Ardaloedd Diogelu Tarddiad Dŵr [Cyfunedigl] wedi cael eu creu fel ffiniau ar gyfer y cyhoedd lle mae cyrff dŵr daear unigol mewn Ardaloedd Diogelu Tarddiad Dŵr wedi\u0027u diddymu, felly dim ond y ffiniau allanol a ddangosir.  Mae Ardaloedd Diogelu Tarddiad Dŵr yn cael eu diffinio o amgylch safleoedd tynnu dŵr daear yfadwy mawr a chyhoeddus. Diben Ardaloedd Diogelu Tarddiad Dŵr yw rhoi amddiffyniad ychwanegol i ddiogelu ansawdd dŵr yfed trwy gyfyngu ar agosrwydd gweithgaredd a allai effeithio ar dynnu dŵr yfed.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe following subdivisions are defined within SPZs:     -  Zone 1: (Inner Protection Zone) - This zone is defined by a travel time of 50-days or less from any point within the zone at, or below, the water table. Additionally, the zone has as a minimum a 50-metre radius. It is based principally on biological decay criteria and is designed to protect against the transmission of toxic chemicals and water-borne disease.    -  Zone 2: (Outer Protection Zone) - This zone is defined by the 400-day travel time from a point below the water table. Additionally this zone has a minimum radius of 250 or 500 metres, depending on the size of the abstraction. The travel time is derived from consideration of the minimum time required to provide delay, dilution and attenuation of slowly degrading pollutants.    -  Zone 3: (Total catchment) - This zone is defined as the total area needed to support the abstraction or discharge from the protected groundwater source.    -  A further Zone 4, or \u0027Zone of Special Interest\u0027 was previously defined for some groundwater sources. These zones highlighted areas (mainly on non-aquifers) where known local conditions meant that potentially polluting activities could impact on a groundwater source even though the area is outside the normal catchment of that source. In future this zone will be incorporated into one of the other zones (1, 2 or 3), whichever is appropriate in the particular case.     Large potable groundwater abstraction sites are georeferenced as Source Protection Locations. A groundwater flow model for relevant aquifer is run and classified into zones defined by travel time. Individual Source Protection Zones are merged and dissolved by Zone number stripping out SPZ name. This is for National Security reasons.\t\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SPZ Source Protection Zones Merged.lyr\tDave Jones- Technical Specialist GW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/400/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_Source_Protection_Zones\t2026-01-12T14:39:55.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18181\tNRW_DS116231\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.931Z\t818\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Storage Areas\tArdaloedd Storio Llifogydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323331\tFlood Storage Areas show those areas that act as a balancing reservoir, storage basin or balancing pond. Their purpose is to attenuate an incoming flood peak to a flow level that can be accepted by the downstream channel. It may also delay the timing of a flood peak so that its volume is discharged over a longer time interval.   Flood Storage Areas that are not yet shown will be gradually added as information becomes available.-Mae Ardaloedd Storio Llifogydd yn dangos yr ardaloedd hynny sy\u0027n gweithredu fel cronfa gydbwyso, basn storio dŵr neu gronbwll. Eu diben yw gwanhau brig llifogydd sy\u0027n cyrraedd i lefel lifo y gall gael ei derbyn gan y sianel i lawr yr afon. Gallant oedi amseriad brig llifogydd hefyd er mwyn i\u0027w gyfaint gael ei ryddhau dros gyfnod hirach o amser.  Ychwanegir Ardaloedd Storio Llifogydd na ddangosir eto yn raddol wrth i ragor o wybodaeth ddod ar gael.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThis data is made available spatially by extracting the information from an Asset Management eXpert (AMX) system.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Floodmap Flood Storage Areas.lyr  Data is extracted from Asset Management eXpert (AMX) software.\tInternal Custodian: Wyn Davies, Flood Risk Manager (Asset Management), Flood and Incident Management Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-12-17\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/347/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_FLOODMAP_FLOOD_STORAGE\t2025-05-07T11:03:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18179\tNRW_DS116232\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.602Z\t594\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Map: Flood Zone 3 (archived data)\tMap Llifogydd: Parth Llifogydd 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323332\tThis dataset has been archived and has been superseded by Flood Map for Planning (NRW_DS125076).   The Flood Map shows the areas across Wales that could be affected by flooding from rivers or the sea. It also shows flood defences and the areas that benefit from them.   Flood Map is designed to raise awareness among the public local authorities and other organisations of the likelihood of flooding and to encourage people living and working in areas prone to flooding to find out more and take appropriate action.   The Flood Map includes several layers of information, this layer is: Flood Zone 3, which is NRWs best estimate of the areas of land with a 100 to 1 (or greater) chance of flooding each year from rivers or with a 200 to 1 chance (or greater) of flooding each year from the sea.-Mae\u0027r Map Llifogydd ar gyfer Cynllunio yn cefnogi polisi cynllunio Llywodraeth Cymru ar berygl llifogydd a dyma\u0027r man cychwyn ar gyfer ystyried perygl llifogydd yn y system cynllunio. Mae\u0027n rhoi syniad o ba dir sydd mewn perygl o lifogydd gan afonydd, y môr a dŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bach, gan ystyried effeithiau newid yn yr hinsawdd dros y ganrif nesaf. Y Map Llifogydd ar gyfer Cynllunio yw\u0027r set ddata allweddol i hysbysu a llywio penderfyniadau ynghylch datblygiad yn y dyfodol.  Mae\u0027r Parthau Llifogydd yn y Map Llifogydd ar gyfer Cynllunio yn dangos y peryglon anniffynedig o lifogydd o afonydd, y môr ac o ddŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bach.  Mae Parth Llifogydd 3 yn arddangos rhychwant y llifogydd o:    afonydd gyda siawns o 1% (1 mewn 100) neu fwy o ddigwydd unrhyw flwyddyn, gan gynnwys lwfans ar gyfer newid yn yr hinsawdd.   y môr gyda siawns o 0.5% (1 mewn 200) neu fwy o ddigwydd unrhyw flwyddyn, gan gynnwys lwfans ar gyfer newid yn yr hinsawdd. Dŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bach gyda siawns o 1% (1 mewn 100) neu fwy o ddigwydd unrhyw flwyddyn, gan gynnwys lwfans ar gyfer newid yn yr hinsawdd. Caiff y Map Llifogydd ar gyfer Cynllunio ei arddangos mewn dwy ran, sef golwg sylfaenol a golwg fanwl. Yn yr olwg sylfaenol, dangosir y perygl llifogydd o afonydd a\u0027r môr fel haen wedi\u0027i huno. Yn yr olwg fanwl, caiff y perygl llifogydd ei rannu yn ffynonellau unigol. Yn y ddwy olwg, dangosir y Parthau Llifogydd ar gyfer dŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bach ar wahân.\tFlood zones ignore the presence of the following types of flood defences and structures:   -  Raised walls and embankments  -  Barriers, barrages, and gates  -  Engineered or controlled flood storage areas  -  Pumping stations.   Other infrastructure, including bridges, culverts, engineered channels and bypass channels, and embankments (e.g. railway or road embankments) that are not flood defences, is included in all modelling and mapping of flood zones.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Floodmap Flood Zone 3.lyr\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FRIM, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-10-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18178\tNRW_DS116233\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.601Z\t621\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Defences without Standardised Attributes (archived data)\tAmddiffynfeydd rhag Llifogydd heb Nodweddion Safonol (data wedi\u0027u harchifo)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323333\tThis dataset has been superseded by the dataset \"Flood Defence Structures\" (NRW_DS125266).  This dataset shows flood defences that have been built to protect against flooding from rivers and the sea. The defences shown provide different levels of flood protection and this is recognised in the risk classification shown in the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) map. Flood defences that are not yet shown, and the areas that benefit from them, will be gradually added.   Flood defences do not remove the risk of flooding however and can be overtopped or fail in extreme weather conditions.\tThis data is made available spatially by extracting the information from an Asset Management eXpert (AMX) system.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Floodmap Flood Defences without attributes.lyr Stored in AMX but made available On the Y drive\tMark Pugh, Principal Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group, FIRM\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2019-05-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-24T09:55:52.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18177\tNRW_DS116234\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.459Z\t915\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Map: Areas Benefiting from Flood Defences (archived data)\tMap Llifogydd: Ardaloedd sy’n Elwa ar Amddiffynfeydd Llifogydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323334\tThis dataset has been superceded by the \"Areas Benefitting from Flood Defences\" dataset (NRW_DS124843) which is displayed within the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) map.   The Flood Map is a spatial dataset that shows the areas across Wales that could be affected by flooding from rivers or the sea. It also shows flood defences and the areas that benefit from them.   Flood Map is designed to raise awareness among the public, local authorities and other organisations of the likelihood of flooding and to encourage people living and working in areas prone to flooding to find out more and take appropriate action.   The Flood Map includes several layers of information, this layer is: Areas Benefiting from Flood Defences, which shows those areas that would benefit from the presence of defences in a 1 percent fluvial / 0.5 percent tidal flood event.\tSome flood defences are shown without an attached area benefiting from them.  This may be because we do not know in detail the area that benefits from this flood defence. Or it may be that the defence is, for example, a barrier or flood storage area, in which case the area that benefits from it may be remote from the flood defence structure.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Floodmap Areas Benefitting from Flood Defence.lyr  Extracted from the FloodGeodatabase\tCustodian: Wyn Davies, Flood Risk Manager (Asset Management), Flood and Incident Management, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-12-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T10:55:21.351Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18176\tNRW_DS116235\t2024-05-28T12:42:28.383Z\t754\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Map: Flood Zone 2 (archived data)\tMap Llifogydd: Parth Llifogydd 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323335\tThis dataset has been archived and has been superseded by Flood Map for Planning (NRW_DS125076).  Flood Map shows the areas across Wales that could be affected by flooding from rivers or the sea. It also shows flood defences and the areas that benefit from them.   Flood Map is designed to raise awareness among the public local authorities and other organisations of the likelihood of flooding and to encourage people living and working in areas prone to flooding to find out more and take appropriate action.   The Flood Map includes several layers of information, this layer is: Flood Zone 2, which is NRWs best estimate of the areas of land between Zone 3 and the extent of the flood from rivers or the sea with a 1000 to 1 chance of flooding in any year. It includes those areas defined in Flood Zone 3.\tFlood zones ignore the presence of the following types of flood defences and structures:   -  Raised walls and embankments  -  Barriers, barrages, and gates  -  Engineered or controlled flood storage areas  -  Pumping stations.   Other infrastructure, including bridges, culverts, engineered channels and bypass channels, and embankments (e.g. railway or road embankments) that are not flood defences, is included in all modelling and mapping of flood zones.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Floodmap Flood Zone 2.lyr\tCustodian:  Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-10-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18234\tNRW_DS116236\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.640Z\t1151\tValid\tEditable\tHistoric Landfill Sites\tSafleoedd Tirlenwi Hanesyddol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323336\tThis dataset is now Static and won’t be updated. However, this dataset should still be used for information on permits that were historic before came under EPR regulations.   This dataset should be used in conjunction with the Historic Landfill Sites points dataset (NRW_DS125453), which is the most comprehensive dataset for recently expired, surrendered, or revoked landfill sites.    Historic Landfill Sites is a spatial (polygon) dataset. These are landfill sites which have been taken off the Authorised Landfill Sites when the waste licence status changes to either:   - Licence expired: Some licences issued under the Control of Pollution Act 1974 were time limited and expired on the date specified in the licence.  - Licence Revoked: Whereby the licence has been entirely revoked and is no longer in force; or   - Licence Surrendered: Operator has successfully surrendered the licence which is no longer in force.    Under the Town and Country Planning (General Development Procedure) Order 1995 Local Planning Authorities have to consult with the former Environment Agency Wales, now part of Natural Resources Wales, about all applications they receive to develop land within 250 metres of landfill sites (including any land that has been used as a landfill site within the past 30 years or is likely to be used as one in the near future). The Historic Landfill dataset was created to help fulfil our statutory responsibility to Local Planning Authorities by supplying information on the risks posed by landfill sites for development within 250m.    The data is the most comprehensive and consistent national historic landfill dataset and defines the location of, and provides specific attributes for, known historic (closed) landfill sites, i.e. sites where there is no PPC permit or waste management licence currently in force. This includes sites that existed before the waste licensing regime and sites that have been licensed in the past but where this licence has been revoked, ceased to exist or surrendered and a certificate of completion has been issued.-Mae Safleoedd Tirlenwi Hanesyddol yn set ddata ofodol. Mae\u0027r rhain yn safleoedd tirlenwi sydd wedi cael eu cymryd oddi ar y Safleoedd Tirlenwi Awdurdodedig pan fydd statws y drwydded gwastraff yn newid naill ai i:  - Drwydded wedi darfod: Roedd rhai trwyddedau o dan Ddeddf Rheoli Llygredd 1974 yn gyfyngedig o ran amser ac yn darfod ar ddyddiad a bennwyd yn y drwydded.  - Trwydded wedi\u0027i dirymu: Lle mae trwydded wedi cael ei dirymu\u0027n llwyr ac nid yw mewn grym mwyach; neu  - Trwydded wedi\u0027i hildio: Mae\u0027r gweithredwr wedi ildio\u0027r drwydded yn llwyddiannus ac nad yw mewn grym bellach.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe original boundaries of these sites comes from the paper permits, which are now digitised from a base scale of 1:10,000 and an associated attribute table comprising 34 fields. The polygons and attributes describe where the sites were located, when they were used, who used them and what was deposited. This means there are name and address fields, licensee and operator information, licence issue and surrender dates, first and last input dates, and waste types, together with some historical comments.   Pre 1996 landfill sites data was managed and collated by Local Authorities, after this duty was then transferred to Environment Agency, and was taken over by NRW in 2013.      INFORMATION WARNING: Before 1996 all landfill sites data was managed and collated by Local Authorities. We therefore can\u0027t guarantee the quality and accuracy of individual historic landfill records up to this date.\t\tX:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\Historic Landfill Sites.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Gareth E Lewis - SUW Advisor Wales, Knowledge Strategy and Planning.  Also could contact: Tyrone (Ty) Ward - PPC/RSR Officer 1, PPC Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2024-08-27\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/401/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_Historic_Landfill_Sites\t2025-12-19T11:54:20.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18232\tNRW_DS116237\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.529Z\t1497\tValid\tEditable\tDetailed River Network\tRhwydwaith Afonydd Manwl\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323337\tThe Detailed River Network (DRN) is a large-scale, accurate and fully attributed digital river centreline covering Wales. The DRN is captured from the water features theme of the OS MasterMap topographic layer and built into a network using automated rules. Other input datasets and extensive local staff knowledge has been used to augment the core geometry to incorporate critical spatial detail and attribution, such as flow direction and path, not available from the OS mapping and to verify the accuracy of the centreline itself. The dataset has full-feature network geometry cross-referenced with OS MasterMap following Digital National Framework principles.\tThis dataset has now been superseded by the OS MasterMap Water Network Layer. NRW will no longer be licencing this dataset so it is advised that people requiring this data should go directly to Ordnance Survey to get the more updated Water Network Layer.\t\tX:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Detailed River Network.lyr   DO NOT SET TO EXPORT or publish this data - As this data is old and has been superseded by an OS product (see metadata details)\tKeith Jones Spatial Service Team       DO NOT SET TO EXPORT - As this data is old and has been superseded by an OS product (see metadata details)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t2020-07-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-05-15T09:09:49.234Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18225\tNRW_DS116238\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.309Z\t1164\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Warning Areas\tArdaloedd Rhybuddion Llifogydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323338\tThis spatial Flood Warning Areas dataset are geographical areas where NRW expect flooding to occur and where NRW provide a Flood Warning Service.   Flood Warning Areas generally contain properties that are expected to flood from rivers or the sea. Specifically, Flood Warning Areas define locations within the Flood Warning Service Limit that represent a discrete community at risk of flooding. A discrete community is a recognised and named geographical community, which can be an urban area, a significant suburb of a large city or a village or a hamlet.   The purpose of Flood Warnings is to alert people that flooding is expected and they should take action to protect themselves and their property.   An online viewable version of this dataset is available here https://naturalresources.wales/our-evidence-and-reports/maps/flood-risk-map/?lang\u003den-Mae set ddata ofodol Ardaloedd Rhybudd am Lifogydd CNC yn ardaloedd daearyddol lle mae CNC yn disgwyl i lifogydd ddigwydd a ble mae CNC yn darparu Gwasanaeth Rhybudd am Lifogydd. Yn gyffredinol, yn yr Ardaloedd Rhybudd am Lifogydd mae eiddo y disgwylir iddynt ddioddef o lifogydd o afonydd neu\u0027r môr. Yn benodol, mae Ardaloedd Rhybudd am Lifogydd yn diffinio lleoliadau o fewn Terfyn y Gwasanaeth Rhybudd am Lifogydd ble mae cymuned ar wahân mewn perygl o lifogydd. Mae cymuned ar wahân yn gymuned ddaearyddol gydnabyddedig gydag enw, a gall fod yn ardal drefol, yn faestref sylweddol mewn dinas fawr neu bentref neu bentrefan. Diben Rhybuddion rhag Llifogydd yw rhybuddio pobl bod disgwyl llifogydd ac y dylent gymryd camau i ddiogelu eu hunain a\u0027u heiddo.  Mae fersiwn ar-lein o\u0027r set ddata hon ar gael yma.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tFlood Warnings are issued when flooding is expected to occur, Severe Flood Warnings are issued to similar areas when there is a danger to life or widespread disruption is expected.\tFurther information on our flood warning codes is available on the NRW website. Live flood warnings in force are shown on the NRW website in both tabular and map based formats.  The live information is updated every 15 minutes. It is also available on our Flood Alerts Wales mobile app. Customers can also obtain recorded messages on the current Alerts and Warnings in force and information on the latest flooding situation for the Flood Alert or Flood Warning Area by telephoning Floodline on 0345 9881188 and using the menu options, or by entering a QuickDial code for the respective Alert or Warning Area.\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Warning Areas.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Andy Wall, Flood Risk Manager, NRW  Originators: EAW, NRW (via EA flood warning system as part of managed service)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-11-26\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/349/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_FLOOD_WARNING\t2025-11-27T10:10:26.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18230\tNRW_DS116239\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.339Z\t1410\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Alert Areas\tArdaloedd Rhybuddion Llifogydd Byddwch yn Barod\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323339\tFlood Alert Areas are geographical areas where it is possible for flooding to occur from rivers or the sea. A single Flood Alert Area may cover the floodplain within the Flood Warning Service Limit of multiple catchments of similar characteristics containing a number of Flood Warning Areas. A Flood Alert Area may also match that of a corresponding Flood Warning Area and warn for the possibility of flooding in that area. In some coastal locations a Flood Alert may be issued for spray or overtopping and be defined by a stretch of coastline. Practical and administrative factors may also influence the exact extent of a Flood Alert Area.    An online viewable version of the dataset is available here: https://naturalresources.wales/flooding/check-your-flood-risk-on-a-map-flood-risk-assessment-wales-map/?lang\u003den-Mae Ardaloedd Rhybudd Llifogydd (Flood Alert Areas) yn ardaloedd daearyddol lle mae\u0027n bosibl i lifogydd ddigwydd o afonydd neu\u0027r môr. Gall un Ardal Rhybudd Llifogydd o fewn Terfyn Gwasanaeth Rhybuddio am Lifogydd gynnwys gorlifdir nifer o ddalgylchoedd â nodweddion tebyg sy\u0027n cynnwys nifer o Ardaloedd Rhybuddion am Lifogydd (Flood Warning Areas). Gall Ardal Rhybudd Llifogydd gyd-daro hefyd ag Ardal Rhybuddio am Lifogydd gyfatebol a rhybuddio am y posibilrwydd o lifogydd yn yr ardal honno. Mewn rhai lleoliadau arfordirol gall Rhybudd Llifogydd gael ei gyhoeddi ar gyfer ewyn a thonnau\u0027r môr yn torri dros y lan a chael eu diffinio gan ddarn o arfordir. Gall ffactorau ymarferol a gweinyddol ddylanwadu ar union faint Ardal Rhybudd Llifogydd hefyd.  Mae fersiwn ar-lein o\u0027r set ddata ar gael yma - https://naturalresources.wales/flooding/check-your-flood-risk-on-a-map-flood-risk-assessment-wales-map/?lang\u003dcy  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tA Flood Alert is issued to warn people of the possibility of flooding and encourage them to be alert stay vigilant and make early / low impact preparations for flooding.   Flood Alerts are issued earlier than Flood Warnings to provide advance notice of the possibility of flooding and may be issued when there is less confidence that flooding will occur in a Flood Warning Area.\tFurther information on our flood warning codes is available on the NRW website. Live flood warnings in force are shown on the NRW website in both tabular and map based formats. The live information is updated every 15 minutes. It is also available on our Flood Alerts Wales mobile app. Customers can also obtain recorded messages on the current Alerts and Warnings in force and information on the latest flooding situation for the Flood Alert or Flood Warning Area by telephoning Floodline on 0345 9881188 and using the menu options, or by entering a QuickDial code for the respective Alert or Warning Area.\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Alert Areas.lyr\tCustodian: Andy Wall, Flood \u0026amp; Operational Risk Management\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-11-26\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/348/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_FLOOD_WATCH_AREAS\t2025-11-27T10:34:01.164Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18235\tNRW_DS116240\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.617Z\t1511\tValid\tEditable\tNitrate Vulnerable Zones (NVZ)\tParthau Perygl Nitradau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323430\tNitrate Vulnerable Zones (NVZs) were areas within Wales that contain surface water or groundwater that is susceptible to nitrate pollution from agricultural activities. They were designated in 2013 accordance with the requirements of the European Commission’s Nitrates Directive 91/676/EEC, which aimed to protect water quality across Europe by preventing nitrates from agricultural sources polluting ground and surface waters and by promoting the use of good farming practices. In April 2021 the designated NVZs in Wales were revoked by the introduction of the Water Resources (Control of Agricultural Pollution)(Wales) Regulations with some measures transitioning into law over a period of time. The transition periods do not apply to those farms located in previously designated NVZ  where all the measures within CoAPR apply  immediately. This dataset is relevant until all measures transition into law.-Roedd Parthau Perygl Nitradau (NVZs) yn ardaloedd yng Nghymru sy\u0027n cynnwys dŵr wyneb neu ddŵr daear sy\u0027n agored i lygredd nitradau o weithgareddau amaethyddol. Fe\u0027u dynodwyd yn 2013 yn unol â gofynion Cyfarwyddeb Nitradau\u0027r Comisiwn Ewropeaidd 91/676/EEC, a oedd â\u0027r nod o ddiogelu ansawdd dŵr ledled Ewrop drwy atal nitradau o ffynonellau amaethyddol rhag llygru dyfroedd daear a dŵr wyneb a thrwy hyrwyddo\u0027r defnydd o arferion ffermio da. Ym mis Ebrill 2021 cafodd y Parthau Perygl Nitradau dynodedig yng Nghymru eu dirymu yn sgil cyflwyno’r Rheoliadau Adnoddau Dŵr (Rheoli Llygredd Amaethyddol) (Cymru) gyda rhai mesurau\u0027n cael eu gwneud yn gyfraith dros gyfnod o amser. Nid yw\u0027r cyfnodau pontio yn berthnasol i\u0027r ffermydd hynny sydd wedi\u0027u lleoli mewn Parth Perygl Nitradau a ddynodwyd yn flaenorol lle mae\u0027r holl fesurau o fewn Rheoliadau Adnoddau Dwr (Rheoli Llygredd Amaethyddol) yn berthnasol ar unwaith. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn berthnasol nes bod pob mesur yn cael ei wneud yn gyfraith.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThe Nitrate Vulnerable Zone boundaries were delineated using a Geographical Information System. The first step was to look at the previous NVZ boundaries and compare them with ground water body data from BGS. This information was used to produce a first draft of the new boundaries, which were then clipped to field boundaries and put out to consultation. Following responses from the consultation, amendments were made  to the boundaries which also considered estimated borehole catchments. Next, boundaries were re-adjusted to field boundaries and the mean high water spring tide mark (unless fields fell below the mean high water spring tide mark).\t\tx:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\NVZ Nitrate Vulnerable Zones 2013.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\NVZ Nitrate Vulnerable Zones 2010.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\NVZ Nitrate Vulnerable Zones 2008.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor, Water Quality Policy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-01-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/404/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_Nitrate_Vulnerable_Zones_2013\t2025-05-02T15:16:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18218\tNRW_DS116241\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.176Z\t619\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts Cycle 1\tCylch 1 Ardaloedd Basn Afon y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323431\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts Cycle 1 is a spatial (polygon) dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD).  Article 2, clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...the area of land and sea, made up of one or more neighbouring river basins together with their associated ground waters and coastal waters\u0027.-Mae Ardaloedd Basn Afon y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) ar gyfer Cylch 1 yn cynnwys nodweddion sydd wedi cael eu coladu fel y diffiniwyd ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr. Mae erthygl 2, cymal 2 y WFD yn eu diffinio fel \u0027ardal o dir a môr, sy\u0027n cynnwys un neu fwy o fasnau afon cyfagos ynghyd â\u0027r dyfroedd daear ac arfordirol cysylltiedig\u0027. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cwmpasu\u0027r haen ar gyfer Cylch 1 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr. Datganiad priodoli Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl. Rhai nodweddion o\u0027r wybodaeth hon yn seiliedig ar ddata gofodol digidol a drwyddedwyd oddi wrth y Ganolfan Ecoleg a Hydroleg NERC (CEH). Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans Hawlfraint y Goron a hawl cronfa ddata 2015.\tRiver Basin Districts have been delineated by using River Catchments as \u0027building blocks\u0027 that have been aggregated together within a GIS, ensuring that WFD rivers do not intersect boundaries.  Coastal and transitional waterbodies are also merged and assigned to a river basin district.  River Catchments were delineated through use of flow data and a digital terrain model run through a hydrological model.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD River Basin Districts Cycle 1\tInternal Contacts: Internal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/562/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_RIVERBASINDISTRICT_C1\t2025-03-07T15:52:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18226\tNRW_DS116242\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.357Z\t469\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 1\tCylch 1 Cyrff Dŵr Llyn y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323432\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 1 (2008 -2013) is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing Water Framework Directive (WFD) attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Article 2, clause 5 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...a body of standing inland surface water\u0027. This dataset contains data related to the physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives that can be linked to water bodies by their unique identifiers.-Mae ‘Cyrff Dŵr Llyn y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)’ yn set ddata ofodol sy\u0027n crynhoi priodoleddau WFD sydd wedi\u0027u casglu fel y diffiniwyd ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD). Mae Erthygl 2, cymal 5 y WFD yn diffinio cyrff dŵr llyn fel ‘…corff o ddŵr wyneb llonydd mewndirol’. Gellir cysylltu data ar nodweddion ffisegol, risg, dosbarthiad ac amcanion arfaethedig i\u0027r cyrff dŵr gyda\u0027u dynodwyr unigryw. Mae llynnoedd artiffisial a llynnoedd wedi\u0027u haddasu\u0027n cael eu cynnwys yn y set ddata: fodd bynnag, yn gyffredinol, dim ond llynnoedd dros 50 hectar a aseswyd dan y WFD heblaw am lynnoedd mewn ardaloedd gwarchodedig, lle defnyddiwyd trothwy o 5.0ha. Nid yw llynnoedd llai na\u0027r trothwy hwn yn cael eu cynnwys oni bai bod ganddynt ddynodiad SoDdGA. Mae gan bob corff dŵr ddynodwr unigryw EA_WB_ID sy\u0027n golygu y gellir ei gysylltu â phriodoleddau WFD. Mae\u0027r data\u0027n berthnasol i Gylch 1 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tArtificial and modified lake waterbodies are included within this dataset; however, generally only lakes above \u0026gt; 50 hectares were assessed under the WFD except for lakes in protected areas, where a minimum of 5.0 ha was used. Lakes below this threshold are not included within this dataset unless allocated as Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). Each waterbody has been assigned \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027, which is a unique identifier that enables a link to WFD attributes. These data apply to Cycle 1 of the Water Framework Directive and contains Ordnance Survey (OS) data.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Lake Waterbodies Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/339/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_LAKES\t2025-12-19T11:52:56.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18223\tNRW_DS116243\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.294Z\t1010\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 1\tCylch 1 Cyrff Dŵr Arfordirol y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323433\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the WFD. Article 2, clause 7 of the WFD defines coastal waterbodies as \u0027...a surface water on the landward side of a line, every point of which is at a distance of one nautical mile on the seaward side from the nearest point of the baseline from which the breadth of territorial waters is measured, extending where appropriate up to the outer limit of transitional waters\u0027. Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives.   This dataset covers the layer for Cycle 1 of the Water framework Directive (2008 - 2013). The equivalent layer for Cycle 2 is covered under WFD Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 2.-Mae Cyrff Dŵr Arfordirol y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) yn set ddata ofodol sy\u0027n crynhoi priodoleddau sydd wedi\u0027u casglu fel y diffiniwyd ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD). Mae Cylch 1af Cyrff Dŵr Arfordirol WFD yn diffinio Cyrff Dŵr Arfordirol ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD). Mae Erthygl 2, cymal 7 y WFD yn diffinio cyrff dŵr arfordirol fel \u0027dŵr wyneb ar ochr y tir o linell sydd â phob pwynt ar bellter o un filltir fôr ar ochr y môr o\u0027r pwynt agosaf ar y llinell sylfaen o ble mae maint y dyfroedd tiriogaethol yn cael ei fesur, gan ymestyn, pan fo hynny\u0027n briodol, hyd at ffin allanol y dyfroedd trawsnewidiol\u0027. Diffiniwyd dyfroedd arfordirol fel dyfroedd tiriogaethol 1 filltir forol o\u0027r Penllanw Cymedrig a\u0027r arfordir a gymerwyd yn uniongyrchol or OS 1: 50K MeridianTM 2. Diffiniwyd y newid rhwng dyfroedd arfordirol ac aberol fel cyrff dŵr trosiannol. Gan fod cyrff dŵr yn cael eu priodoli gyda dynodwr unigryw (EA_WB_ID) gellir cysylltu\u0027r set ddata hon yn uniongyrchol â ffynonellau data eraill y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr megis nodweddion ffisegol, risg, dosbarthiad a\u0027r amcanion arfaethedig.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tCoastal waters were defined by territorial waters 1 nautical mile from the Mean High Water coastline taken directly from OS 1:50K Meridian 2. The delineation between coastal and estuarine waters was delineated by the former the Environment Agency Wales defined transitional waterbodies. Waterbodies are also split and assigned to River Basin Districts.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/338/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_COASTAL\t2025-05-02T14:44:50.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18231\tNRW_DS116244\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.331Z\t263\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 1\tCyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) Cyrff Dŵr Trosiannol Cylch 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323434\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional (Estuarine) Waterbodies Cycle 1 (2008-2013) is a polygon dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 6 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...bodies of surface water in the vicinity of river mouths which are partly saline in character as a result of their proximity to coastal waters but are substantially influenced by freshwater flows\u0027. Transitional waterbodies were defined from Mean High Water.-Mae’r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr Cyrff Dŵr (aberol) Trosiannol yn set ddata gofodol sy\u0027n cynnwys priodoleddau a gasglwyd fel y diffiniwyd ar gyfer gweithredu’r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD). Mae Erthygl 2, cymal 6 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr yn eu diffinio fel \u0027... cyrff o dŵr wyneb yng nghyffiniau aberoedd afonydd sy\u0027n rhannol hallt o ganlyniad i\u0027w hagosrwydd i ddyfroedd arfordirol ond sydd hefyd dan ddylanwad sylweddol dŵr croyw sy’n llifo\u0027. Diffiniwyd cyrff dŵr Trosiannol o ffiniau penllanw cymedrig, a gymerwyd yn uniongyrchol o OS 1: 50K MeridianTM 2, a ffiniau aberol a ddiffiniwyd ar gyfer y Gyfarwyddeb Trin Dŵr Gwastraff Trefol (UWWTD). Gan fod cyrff dŵr yn cael eu priodoli â dynodwr unigryw (EA_WB_ID) gellir cysylltu\u0027r set ddata hon yn uniongyrchol â ffynonellau data eraill y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr megis nodweddion ffisegol, risg, dosbarthiad ac amcanion arfaethedig. Mae\u0027r data hwn yn berthnasol i Gylch 1 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tWater boundaries, taken directly from OS 1:50K Meridian 2, and Environment Agency estuarine boundaries defined for the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD). Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives. These data apply to Cycle 1 of the Water Framework Directive. Dataset contains copyright owned by Ordnance Survey.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-04-02\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/337/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_TRANSITIONAL\t2025-05-02T14:50:58.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18222\tNRW_DS116245\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.310Z\t395\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwater bodies Cycle 1\tCyrff dŵr daear Cylch 1 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323435\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwater bodies Cycle 1 is a spatial (polygon) dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD).  Article 2, clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...all water which is below the surface of the ground in the saturation zone and in direct contact with the ground or subsoil\u0027.  For the purposes of reporting under the WFD a groundwater body represent a distinct body of groundwater flow with a coherent flow unit including recharge and discharge areas with little flow across the boundaries.  These reflect hydrogeological characteristics containing information on flow and stage properties, recharge and vulnerability to pollution.  This has been undertaken through defining aquifers into different types and broken into catchment units at Catchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) scale. Since water bodies are attributed with a unique identifier (EA_WB_ID) this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives.-Mae cyrff dŵr daear Cylch 1 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr yn set ddata ofodol (polygon) sy\u0027n cynnwys nodweddion sydd wedi cael eu coladu fel y diffiniwyd ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr. Mae Erthygl 2, Cymal 2 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr yn eu diffinio fel \u0027yr holl ddŵr sy\u0027n is na wyneb y tir yn y parth trwytho ac mewn cyswllt uniongyrchol â\u0027r tir neu isbridd\u0027. At ddibenion hysbysu o dan y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr, mae corff dŵr daear yn cynrychioli corff penodol o lif dŵr daear gydag uned llifo gydlynol, gan gynnwys ardaloedd adlwytho a rhyddhau, gydag ond ychydig o lif ar draws y terfynau. Mae\u0027r rhain yn adlewyrchu nodweddion hydrodaearyddol sy\u0027n ystyried gwybodaeth am nodweddion llif a chamau, adlwytho, a pha mor agored ydynt i lygredd. Mae hyn wedi cael ei gynnal trwy ddiffinio dyfrhaenau o ran teipiau gwahanol a\u0027u rhannu’n unedau dalgylch ar raddfa Strategaethau Rheoli Tynnu Dŵr Dalgylch. Gan fod cyrff dŵr wedi\u0027u penodi gyda rhif adnabod unigryw (EA_WB_ID), mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn gallu cael ei chysylltu\u0027n uniongyrchol â ffynnonnellau data eraill y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr megis nodweddion corfforol, risg, dosbarthiad ac amcanion arfaethedig.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Wedi deillio’n rhannol o ddata digidol ar raddfa 1:50,000 ac 1:250,000 dan ganiatâd Arolwg Daearegol Prydain. © NERC.\tThe primary input dataset is \u00271:625K Classified Aquifer Geology\u0027.  This dataset has been digitised directly from the hard copy \u00271:250K Solid Geology Map\u0027 and classified according to aquifer type and are therefore directly derived from the underlying British Geological Society (BGS) data.  These classifications were verified by the BGS consultation at area level and in some instances 1:50K Solid Geology has been used to define localised boundaries.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Groundwaterbodies Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-10-14\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3582/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_groundwater_bodies_c1\t2025-05-08T07:45:32.522Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18227\tNRW_DS116246\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.353Z\t481\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Monitoring Network Cycle 1\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323436\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) Monitoring Network Cycle 1 (2008-2013) is a point dataset that contains the location of monitoring sites used by Natural Resources Wales in the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). The network consists of numerous monitoring sites used to record various parameters. These data are intended to show the location and extent of the network and does not hold any actual monitored data.\tThe data have abstraction sites rounded to 4 figure grid reference due to concerns over national security.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Monitoring Network Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18233\tNRW_DS116247\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.565Z\t746\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Management Catchments Cycle 1\tCylch 1 Dalgylchoedd Rheoli\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323437\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Management Catchments Cycle 1 is a spatial (polygon) dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the WFD)  Management catchments are the unit of geography for which action plans are drafted in implementing the WFD. WFD Management Catchments have an action plan published that relates to all waterbodies that fall within its boundaries.-Mae Dalgylchoedd Gweithredol y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) ar gyfer Cylch 1 yn set ddata ofodol (polygon) sydd wedi\u0027i chasglu fel y diffiniwyd ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r WFD. Dalgylchoedd gweithredol yw\u0027r uned ddaearyddol y dylunnir cynlluniau gweithredu ar eu cyfer wrth weithredu\u0027r WFD. Mae Dalgylchoedd Gweithredol y WFD wedi cyhoeddi cynllun gweithredu sy\u0027n ymwneud phob corff dŵr sydd yn eu cyffiniau. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys yr haen ar gyfer Cylch 1 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl. Rhai nodweddion o\u0027r wybodaeth hon yn seiliedig ar ddata gofodol digidol a drwyddedwyd oddi wrth y Ganolfan Ecoleg a Hydroleg © NERC (CEH). Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans © Hawlfraint y Goron a hawl cronfa ddata 2015.\tWFD Management Catchments have been delineated by using WFD River Waterbody Catchments [these were delineated through use of flow data and a digital terrain model run through a hydrological model] as \"building blocks\" that have been aggregated together within a GIS ensuring that WFD rivers do not intersect boundaries.  This process was conducted by using expert judgement in consultation.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Management Catchments Cycle 1.lyr\tRob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/559/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_MANAGEMENTCATCHMENT_C1\t2025-05-07T16:01:07.783Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18217\tNRW_DS116248\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.171Z\t985\tValid\tEditable\tSource Protection Zones (SPZ) Individual\tParthau Gwarchod Tarddiad (SPZ) Unigol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323438\tSPZs are defined around large and public potable groundwater abstraction sites. The purpose of SPZs is to provide additional protection to safeguard drinking water quality through constraining the proximity of an activity that may impact upon a drinking water abstraction. This is part of an initial screening process in assessing impacts to groundwater resources.   Zones around location sites are defined by groundwater travel time to an abstraction. This is determined through applying groundwater flow models run at the location of abstractions, inputting parameters such as flow direction, geology type, rainfall and hydrological boundaries. SPZs provide a visual representation of the increased risks as you get closer to the abstraction.   The following subdivisions are defined within SPZs:   Zone 1: (Inner Protection Zone) - This zone is defined by a travel time of 50-days or less from any point within the zone at, or below, the water table. Additionally, the zone has as a minimum a 50-metre radius. It is based principally on biological decay criteria and is designed to protect against the transmission of toxic chemicals and water-borne disease.   Zone 2: (Outer Protection Zone) - This zone is defined by the 400-day travel time from a point below the water table. Additionally this zone has a minimum radius of 250 or 500 metres, depending on the size of the abstraction. The travel time is derived from consideration of the minimum time required to provide delay, dilution and attenuation of slowly degrading pollutants.   Zone 3: (Total catchment) - This zone is defined as the total area needed to support the abstraction or discharge from the protected groundwater source.   A further Zone 4, or ‘Zone of Special Interest’ was previously defined for some groundwater sources. These zones highlighted areas (mainly on non-aquifers) where known local conditions meant that potentially polluting activities could impact on a groundwater source even though the area is outside the normal catchment of that source. In future this zone will be incorporated into one of the other zones (1, 2 or 3), whichever is appropriate in the particular case.\tLarge potable groundwater abstractions are extracted from the National Abstraction Licence Database (NALD) and georeferenced as Source Protection Locations. A groundwater flow model for relevant aquifer is run and classified into zones defined by travel time. Individual Source Protection Zones are merged and dissolved by Zone number stripping out SPZ name. This is for National Security reasons.\t\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SPZ Source Protection Zones Individual.lyr\tDave Jones, Senior Specialist Advisor Geoscience\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t2023-01-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T13:46:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18228\tNRW_DS116249\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.368Z\t876\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAuthorised Landfill Sites\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323439\tThis dataset has now been discontinued and won’t be updated. The permit information may no longer be correct and the boundary may no longer reflect the actual site anymore, as sites may have expanded their perimeter to accommodate additional capacity. Also, this dataset will not show landfills that have been created after 2013 or may contain landfill sites that are now expired, surrendered or revoked.  The authorised landfill site is a spatial (polygon) dataset containing the boundaries of landfill sites which are currently authorised by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under The Environmental Permitting (England and Wales) Regulations 2010. The Environmental Permitting (England and Wales) Regulations provide industry, regulators and others with a single extended permitting and compliance system. Landfill permits were previously authorised by a Waste Management Licence,  or a Pollution Prevention and Control (PCC) Permit, which have subsequently become Environmental  (EPR) Permit, and are recorded within a NRW database. Pre 1996 landfill sites data was managed and collated by Local Authorities, this duty was then transferred to Environment Agency, and was taken over by NRW in 2013.  INFORMATION WARNING: This dataset has now been discontinued and won’t be updated. The permit information may no longer be correct and the and boundary may no longer not reflect the actual site anymore, as sites may have expanded their perimeter to accommodate additional capacity. Also this dataset will not show landfills that have been created after 2013 or may contain landfill sites that are now expired, surrendered or revoked.\tLandfill boundaries come in through via the permits in paper format, and are digitised from OS 1:10 000 Scale Colour Raster if not provided by the operator. Merged into a single layer. Revoked, expired or surrendered licences (not if a change) are identified and removed where relevant by the Waste Team.  The dataset allows NRW to extract data on landfill sites, which can then be filtered based on the following code descriptors. A1: Co-Disposal Landfill Site;  A2: Other Landfill Site taking Special Waste;  A4: Household, Commercial and Industrial Waste Landfill; A5: Landfill taking Non-Biodegradable Wastes;  A6: Landfill taking other wastes;  A7: Industrial Waste Landfill (Factory cartilage);  5.2 A(1) a): Waste Landfilling; \u0026gt;10T/D with capacity\u0026gt;25,000T excluding inert waste;  5.2 A(1) b): Waste Landfilling; Any other Landfill to which the 2002 regulations apply;  L04: Non Hazardous Landfill; and  L05: Inert Landfill. It is important to note that because a site is authorised, it does not necessarily mean it is accepting waste. Landfill sites are only removed from the dataset on a quarterly basis and added to the Historic Landfill site dataset when the waste licence status changes to either: - Licence expired: Some licences issued under the Control of Pollution Act 1974 were time limited and expired on the date specified in the licence. - Licence Revoked: Whereby the licence has been entirely revoked and is no longer in force; or  - Licence Surrendered: Operator has successfully surrendered the licence which is no longer in force.\t\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Waste\\Active Authorised Landfill Boundary.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Gareth E Lewis - SUW Advisor Wales, Knowledge Strategy and Planning  Also could contact: Tyrone (Ty) Ward - PPC/RSR Officer 1, PPC Team  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW       The publish data is a redacted version of the whole dataset - as the non redacted version includes names and addresses.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-04-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:43:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18221\tNRW_DS116250\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.177Z\t730\tValid\tEditable\tReservoir Flood Map Maximum Flood Outline Extent\tMap Llifogydd sy’n Amlinellu Maint Eithaf Llifogydd o Gronfeydd Dŵr\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323530\tData layer showing individual reservoir flood maps for 225 Large Raised Reservoirs including attributed data. Individual reservoirs may have up to 5 flood maps associated with them, based on separate breach locations.   The data shows the maximum extent of flood should the reservoir be breached, although the location of the reservoir can be inferred it is not explicitly shown on the maps.     Reservoir flooding is extremely unlikely to happen. There has been no loss of life in the UK from reservoir flooding since 1925.   All large reservoirs must be inspected and supervised by reservoir panel engineers. As the enforcement authority for the Reservoirs Act 1975 in Wales, we ensure that reservoirs are inspected regularly and essential safety work is carried out.    An online viewable version of this dataset is available here: https://flood-risk-maps.naturalresources.wales/?locale\u003den\tThe Reservoir Flood Map Outline (Extent) shows the largest area that might be flooded if a reservoir were to fail and release the water it holds. Since this is a prediction of a credible worst case scenario, it’s unlikely that any actual flood would be this large.   These data are intended for emergency planning only and are not reliable for large scale flood risk assessments.     Please note that only flood maps for large reservoirs are displayed. Flood maps are not displayed for smaller reservoirs or for reservoirs commissioned after reservoir mapping began in spring 2009.\thttps://flood-risk-maps.naturalresources.wales/?locale\u003den\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Reservoir Maximum Flood Extent.lyr\tData custodian - Phillip Morgan, Team Leader, Reservoir Regulation\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tThird Party Conditional\t2020-10-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T11:49:02.307Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18229\tNRW_DS116251\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.377Z\t1140\tValid\tEditable\tLive Flood Warnings and Alerts Application Programming Interface (API)\tRhyngwyneb Rhaglennu Cymwysiadau Rhybuddion a Rhybuddion Llifogydd Byw (API)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323531\tFlood Warnings are provided by the constant monitoring of stations and forecasting flooding from rivers or the sea:    - Severe Flood Warning: Severe flooding. Danger to life.   - Flood Warning: Flooding is expected. Immediate action required.   - Flood Alert: Flooding is possible. Be prepared.   - Warning no longer in force: Flood warnings and flood alerts that have been removed in the last 24 hours.     Flood Warnings are available on our website and as a Live Feed. The live feed provides a summary of flood warnings (Alert, Warning and Severe) that are sent externally. The Flood Warnings Live Feed provides current status update of every Flood Warning Area in England and Wales. This is updated on the Flood Warning Information System (FWIS) service every minute although it is presented for external users every 15 minutes via the Data Distribution Hub where the XML can be securely downloaded (sFTP feed).-APIs Rhybuddion a Hysbysiadau Llifogydd Byw (Data Agored) – rhestr o’r holl rybuddion sydd mewn grym ar y pryd. Caiff ei ddiweddaru bob 15 munud.\tFlood Warning Duty Area Officers issue an alert if trigger threshold for selected telemetry sites are reached or if a Monitoring Forecasting Duty Officer highlights a forecast flood incident. This is feed through a live feed.\t\tLive feed only: https://api-portal.naturalresources.wales/products/\tAndrew Wall Flood  Flood \u0026amp; Operational Risk Management, National Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2008-06-13\tpublication\tnull\thttps://api-portal.naturalresources.wales/products\t2025-10-24T08:43:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18224\tNRW_DS116252\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.353Z\t884\tValid\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Extents\tHelaethrwydd Morfeydd Heli\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323532\tPolygon data layer showing the extent of Saltmarsh in Coastal and Transitional waters for use by both Flood and Coastal Risk Management and the implementation of Water Framework Directive. At the seaward end of the transect, the final demarcation will be where the saltmarsh vegetation cover has become so sparse it only covers 5% whether it is Upper, Mid, Lower or Pioneer saltmarsh.-Haen data polygon sy\u0027n dangos helaethrwydd morfeydd heli mewn dyfroedd arfordirol a throsiannol ar gyfer defnydd Rheoli Perygl Llifogydd ac Erydu Arfordirol a gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr. Ar ochr y trawslun atfôr, bydd y ffin derfynol lle y mae\u0027r gorchudd planhigion morfa heli mor brin mae ond yn gorchuddio 5%, boed yn forfa heli uchaf, canolig, isaf neu gyntaf.  Mae maint morfa heli wedi cael ei ddehongli o ddelweddu digidol 10cm wrth 10cm o\u0027r awyr. Ffin y forfa heli tua\u0027r tir yw\u0027r pwynt lle y mae\u0027r parthau uchaf yn gorffen a phlanhigion daearol yn cychwyn (wrth droed morglawdd yn aml. Y marc yw lle y mae planhigion morfa heli\u0027n fwy na 5% o\u0027r gymuned sy\u0027n ddaearol yn bennaf.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tSaltmarsh extent has been interpreted from 10 cm by 10 cm digital aerial imagery. The demarcation of the landward extent is the point at which the upper most zones gives way to terrestrial plants (often at the foot of a seawall). The mark is where saltmarsh plants become more than 5% of the predominantly terrestrial community.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Saltmarsh extents.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Bianca Bacchi- Rhymney and Ebbw A\u0026amp;R Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2017-03-01\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/330/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_SALTMARSH_EXTENTS\t2025-12-19T10:08:33.089Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18219\tNRW_DS116253\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.241Z\t1024\tValid\tEditable\tEnvironmental Pollution Incidents\tAchosion o Lygredd Amgylcheddol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323533\tThis dataset contains details pollution incidents reported to Natural Resources Wales (NRW). Only substantiated and closed environmental protection incidents are included. Where these data indicate an incident occurred on a particular site or property no inference should be drawn that the site or property owner necessarily was responsible.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys manylion digwyddiadau o lygredd mae CNC yn cael eu hysbysu amdanynt. Dim ond digwyddiadau diogelu amgylcheddol sydd wedi cael eu cadarnhau ac sydd wedi cau sy\u0027n cael eu cynnwys, lle mae\u0027r lefel o effaith amgylcheddol naill ai yng Nghategori 1 (mawr) neu Gategori 2 (sylweddol) ar o leiaf un cyfrwng (h.y. dŵr, tir neu aer).  Lle mae\u0027r data hwn yn dangos digwyddiad ar safle neu eiddo arbennig, ni ddylid casglu o anghenraid mai\u0027r safle neu\u0027r perchennog oedd yn gyfrifol.  Datganiad priodoli Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tDetails of all pollution incidents reported to NRW are held on the Wales Incident Recording System (WIRS).  Data was stored and collect from 2013-04-01 to 2016-02-29 in National Incident Recording System (NIRS) and from 2016-03-01 to present Wales Incident Recording System (WIRS).   Where these data indicate an incident occurred on a particular site or property no inference should be drawn that the site or property owner necessarily was responsible.   Incidents are categorised as follows:  Category 1: High Major or Major Incident  Category 2: High Minor or Significant Incident  Category 3: Low or Minor Incident\t\tNIRS up to 2016-02-29 WIRS from 2016-03-01 til present\tSean Moore, Incident Communications Centre Team Leader\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-05\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2642\t2026-01-06T10:21:20.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18216\tNRW_DS116254\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.174Z\t357\tValid\tEditable\tNational Receptor Dataset (NRD) 2011\tCronfa Ddata Genedlaethol Derbynyddion (2011)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323534\tThe National Receptor Dataset (NRD) is a collection of risk receptors, typically residential and commercial property, primarily intended for use in flood and coastal erosion risk management.   NRD is a spatial dataset which contains a number of GIS layers categorised into themes of information including; buildings, environment, heritage, transport and utilities.   The NRD includes property points that are Copy Derived from OSMM AddressLayer2 with information on property type, floor area and Flood Hazard Research Centres Multi-Coloured Manual codes attributed.  It was designed to meet the needs of Preliminary Flood Risk Assessments and the Environment Agency\u0027s National Flood Risk Assessment although it can be used for other purposes.\tThe version of the NRD described here is the NRD2011 and was released in November 2011. This dataset is superseded by NRD 2014 and 2018.\tFour technical reports are also available:  352_10 National Receptor Dataset - Guidance for Environment Agency staff and professional partners  353_10 National Receptor Dataset: What it is, what it\u0027s for, and how to access and use it  NRD Update Summary Report v1.1  NRD_PPLv2011_Reconciliation Report v1.3\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Receptors Database (NRD) 2011.lyr   Reports - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/kdim/Data-Management/da/DataArchivingTNA/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?id\u003d%2Fteams%2Fkdim%2FData%2DManagement%2Fda%2FDataArchivingTNA%2FNRD%202011%20reports%2Ezip\u0026amp;parent\u003d%2Fteams%2Fkdim%2FData%2DManagement%2Fda%2FDataArchivingTNA\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2023-11-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18220\tNRW_DS116255\t2024-05-28T12:43:18.179Z\t510\tValid\tEditable\tAreas Affecting Bathing Waters\tArdaloedd sy’n Effeithio ar Dyfroedd Ymdrochi\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323535\tThis spatial dataset comprises of a polygon relating to each site identified under the Bathing Water Directive (76/160/EEC). These sites are drawn as a simple guide to aid the work of Natural Resources Wales and therefore these polygons have no formal status under the Bathing Water Directive.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys polygonau sy\u0027n gysylltiedig â phob safle a nodwyd o dan y Gyfarwyddeb Dŵr Ymdrochi (76/160/EEC); fodd bynnag, nid oes gan y polygonau hyn unrhyw statws ffurfiol dan y Gyfarwyddeb Dŵr Ymdrochi. Lle bo safle\u0027n cyfateb i Ardal Sensitif o dan y Gyfarwyddeb Trin Dŵr Gwastraff Trefol (UWWTD) yng Nghymru a Lloegr, mae polygon yn cael ei ddarparu ar gyfer yr Ardal Sensitif honno. Ar gyfer pob safle arall mae polygon wedi\u0027i lunio fel canllaw syml i gynorthwyo gwaith Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru gyda trwyddedu gollyngiadau. Nid yw\u0027r polygonau hyn yn briodol ar gyfer nodi ardaloedd sy\u0027n addas ar gyfer ymdrochi. Nid yw\u0027r polygonau yn ddiffiniad o helaethrwydd y dŵr ymdrochi o dan y Cyfarwyddebau Dŵr Ymdrochi 76/160/EEC a 2006/7/EC ac ni ddylid eu defnyddio ar gyfer unrhyw ddiffiniad o ardal na helaethrwydd y dŵr ymdrochi.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tWhere a site corresponds to a Sensitive Area under the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD) in Wales, the polygon for that Sensitive Area is provided. For all other sites the polygon has been drawn as a simple guide to aid the work of Natural Resources Wales with permitting of discharges. These polygons are not appropriate for identifying areas suitable for bathing.   The polygons are not a definition of the extent of the bathing water under the Bathing Water Directives 76/160/EEC or 2006/7/EC and should not be used for any definition of the bathing water area or extent.\t\tx:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\Areas Affecting Bathing Waters.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Helena Sykes, Lead Specialist Advisor, Monitoring Analysis, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2017-04-20\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/326/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:Areas_Affecting_Bathing_Waters\t2025-11-06T15:54:34.258Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18215\tNRW_DS116256\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.766Z\t63\tValid\tEditable\tPotential Sites of Hydropower Opportunity\tSafleoedd Posibl ar gyfer Cyfleoedd Ynni Dŵr\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323536\tThis data shows the location of opportunities for hydropower and the basic environmental sensitivity associated with exploiting them in Wales.   Barriers are identified and assessed in this project. The term barriers is used to describe sites with sufficient drop to provide a hydropower opportunity. They are mostly weirs, but could also be other man-made structures or natural features, such as waterfalls. The average maximum power generation capacity on a barrier was 45Kw, with a total potential capacity of 1178Mw.   Environmental sensitivities were assessed. This assessment considered the presence of fish species and whether the site has been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Almost half (46%) of these barriers are classified as highly sensitive, mostly because of the presence of migratory fish species such as salmon and eel. 27% are medium and high sensitivity, and the remainder are unclassified due to a lack of data.   When it is assumed that a new scheme has a fish pass built into it, the environmentally compatible opportunities increase considerably. Given the scale of the project and the data used, the results are not intended to replace any part of an individual site assessment. Instead, the dataset gives national and regional level overviews of the potential opportunities available, their locations, and their relative environmental sensitivity to exploitation.\tThese data are intended to provide a general national overview of the potential hydropower opportunities available, their locations, and their relative environmental sensitivity to exploitation.  At site-level, there will be some error inherent in the results as the map uses a national GIS dataset that is based on various sources. One-third of the sites where older Synthetic Aperture RADAR (SAR) data was used for the height estimate include an error of up to one metre. The remaining two-thirds use ‘Light Detection and Ranging’ (LiDAR), which is accurate to 25cm. This means that the data for an individual site may be inaccurate, but at the national and regional level the error will be averaged out to an extent. There is not a high level of confidence in the power generation calculation. The power category takes account of this uncertainty.   These data are indicative only and are not intended to replace any part of an individual site assessment, which is necessary for a full scheme appraisal.     Barriers were identified by assessing natural and manmade features identified in OS MasterMap - Topography. Height has been assigned to the barriers using 25cm LiDAR data in addition to flow derived from LowFLows Enterprise. Potential power has been calculated using flow and height.  Please note: this data was produced for the purposes of an EA project in 2010 and no updates have been undertaken to it since then.\t\tX:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\River Obstructions.lyr\tGideon Carpenter, Hydrology and Water Resources Management, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t2010-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-03T14:14:07.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18211\tNRW_DS116257\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.465Z\t1125\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRisk of Flooding from Reservoirs\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323537\tNRW is responsible for assuring the safety of the Welsh reservoirs by enforcing the Reservoirs Act 1975. Simplified inundation mapping was carried out nationally by the Environment Agency to provide a baseline assessment of all reservoirs falling within the Act. This work is now carried out by Natural Resources Wales (NRW). Reservoirs Flood Maps – Spatial Data are referred to externally as Risk of Flooding from Reservoirs.\tThe detailed outputs show the potential flood risk if reservoirs were breached.  The models were created at various resolutions ranging from 10 metre cells through to 50 metre squares for locations furthest away from the breach.  These files are not available as a single, discrete file since each reservoir has been modelled individually. It should be noted that these model outputs are for emergency planning purposes and are not intended to reflect the most detailed flood extents.  As such these data show the absolute maximum flood where there is likely to be an impact.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Reservoir Maximum Flood Extent.lyr\tData custodian - Phillip Morgan, Team Leader, Reservoir Regulation\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:33.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18212\tNRW_DS116258\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.494Z\t811\tValid\tEditable\tCatchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) Boundaries\tFfiniau Strategaethau Rheoli Tynnu Dŵr Dalgylchoedd (CAMS)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323538\tCatchment Abstraction Management Strategies (CAMS) assess the amount of water available in each river catchment.   The CAMS Boundaries are an external reference dataset giving an indication of where technical assessments of water abstraction have been undertaken.   CAMS helps to look at the balance between society, the economy and the environment. The technical assessment helps identify where water may be available for future use but also where water resource demands may be impacting the water balance.   The CAMS boundaries should be used on a national (Wales) scale to show the geographical distribution of CAMS and are not suitable for detailed technical assessments.-Mae Ffiniau Cyfeirio Strategaethau Rheoli Tynnu Dŵr Dalgylchoedd yn set ddata allanol ar gyfer cyfeirio sy\u0027n dangos ble mae asesiadau technegol wedi\u0027u cynnal. Mae CAMS o gymorth wrth edrych ar y cydbwysedd rhwng cymdeithas, yr economi a\u0027r amgylchedd. Mae\u0027r asesiad technegol o gymorth wrth nodi lle y gallai dŵr fod ar gael i\u0027w ddefnyddio yn y dyfodol, ond hefyd lle y gallai\u0027r galw am ddŵr fod yn cael effaith ar y cydbwysedd dŵr. Dim ond ar raddfa genedlaethol i ddangos dosbarthiad daearyddol y dylid defnyddio ffiniau CAMS. Nid ydynt yn addas ar gyfer asesiadau technegol manwl.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThis data is created using the approximate indication of where technical assessments of water abstractions have taken place.\tINFORMATION WARNING: These boundaries should not be used for detailed technical assessment or identifying precisely which CAMS permits may fall into. The boundaries may be subject to change based on the Natural Resources Wales technical and catchment understanding.\tX:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Catchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) Boundaries.lyr\tNRW Internal Custodian: Ben Anscombe, Principal Officer, Geographic Information Systems\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-11-22\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/410/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_CAMS_TECH_ASSESSMENT\t2025-12-19T11:36:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18213\tNRW_DS116259\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.531Z\t979\tValid\tEditable\tCoastal Design - Extreme Sea Levels (archived data)\tDyluniad o\u0027r Arfordir – Lefelau Môr Eithafol (data wedi\u0027i archifo)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323539\tThis dataset has now been superseded by data provided by the Environment Agency for England and Wales, please see metadata number EXT_DS123552 - \"Environment Agency: Coastal Flood Boundaries (CFB) publication 2018 - England and Wales (3rd Party Data)\".  This is a GIS dataset and supporting information providing design / extreme sea level and typical surge information around the coastline of Wales under present day conditions. This is a specialist dataset which informs on work commenced around the coast ranging from coastal flood modelling, scheme design, strategic planning and flood risk assessments.   The dataset contains the following layer components:  -  Extreme Sea Level Values: Extreme sea levels for a range of return periods  -  Extreme Sea Level Confidence Level: Confidence intervals providing allowances for uncertainty  -  Estuary Boundaries: Inland estuarine boundaries of the extreme sea levels  -  Surge Shape Locations: Reference to surge shape appropriate for each Class A gauge site and where to apply it  -  Tide Gauge Locations: Location of gauging stations\tFurther documents on the datasets lineage can be found in the data download.  This dataset has now been superseded by data provided by the Environment Agency for England and Wales, please see metadata number 123552, Coastal Flood Boundaries (CFB).\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB confidence intervals.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB Estuary boundaries.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB extreme sea levels.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB Gauge data.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB Surge shapes.lyr\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-11-02\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18210\tNRW_DS116261\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.438Z\t1485\tValid\tEditable\tShoreline Management Plan Policies\tCynllun Rheoli Traethlin\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323631\tA Shoreline Management Plan (SMP) is a large-scale assessment of the risks associated with coastal processes and helps reduce these risks to people and the developed, historic and natural environments. Coastal processes include tidal patterns, wave height, wave direction and the movement of beach and seabed materials.   The SMPs set the strategic policy direction for coastal management and identify the most sustainable approaches to managing the risks to the coast in the short term (Epoch 1 0-20 years), medium term (Epoch 2 20-50 years) and long term (Epoch 3 50-100 years).   A set of preferred policies are identified which are assigned to \u0027policy units\u0027 for each SMP epoch. A \u0027policy unit\u0027 (PU) is a length of shoreline where a separate shoreline management policy applies. PU\u0027s are defined by coastal areas that have similar characteristics in terms of coastal processes and assets at risk, that can be managed efficiently.   Four policy options are available for SMPs:  - Hold the Line (HTL): an aspiration to build or maintain artificial defences so that the current position of the shoreline remains. This can involve maintaining or changing the standard of protection.   - Advance the Line (ATL): by building new defences on the seaward side of the original defences. This is rarely used and is limited to policy units where there is significant land reclamation is considered. There are no policy units in Wales assigned ATL.  - Managed Realignment (MR): by allowing the shoreline to move backwards or forwards naturally but managing the process to direct it in certain areas.  - No Active Intervention (NAI): where there is no planned investment in coastal defences or operations, regardless of whether or not an artificial defence has existed previously.     The coastal management approach for a certain section of coast may change from the status quo where a policy may no longer be practical or acceptable over 100 years. Therefore, a combination of policies may be proposed over the length of the SMP.   The dataset identifies which of the second-generation Shoreline Management Plans are applicable to a particular stretch of the Welsh coastline and the policies assigned to policy units related to that particular area. This dataset is a polyline, spatial data layer for the Welsh coast only.-Asesiad ar raddfa fawr o\u0027r risgiau sy\u0027n gysylltiedig phrosesau arfordirol yw\u0027r Cynllun Rheoli Traethlin, ac mae\u0027n helpu i leihau\u0027r risgiau hyn i bobl a’r amgylchedd datblygedig, hanesyddol a naturiol. Mae prosesau arfordirol yn cynnwys patrymau\u0027r llanw, uchder tonnau, cyfeiriad tonnau, a\u0027r ffordd mae\u0027r traeth a deunyddiau ar wely\u0027r môr yn symud. Mae\u0027r Cynllun Rheoli Traethlin yn pennu cyfeiriad y polisi strategol ar gyfer rheoli arfordiroedd ac yn nodi\u0027r dulliau mwyaf cynaliadwy o reoli\u0027r risgiau i\u0027r arfordir yn y tymor byr (Cyfnod 1: 0-20 mlynedd), y tymor canolig (Cyfnod 2: 20-50 mlynedd) ac yn yr hirdymor (Cyfnod 3: 50-100 mlynedd). Nodir cyfres o bolisïau dewisol, a chânt eu neilltuo i \u0027unedau polisi\u0027 ar gyfer pob cyfnod o\u0027r Cynllun Rheoli Traethlin. \u0027Uned polisi\u0027 yw darn o arfordir y mae polisi rheoli traethlin ar wahân yn berthnasol iddo. Diffinnir unedau polisi yn ôl ardaloedd arfordirol sydd â nodweddion tebyg o ran prosesau arfordirol ac asedau mewn perygl ac y gellir eu rheoli\u0027n effeithlon. Mae pedwar opsiwn polisi ar gael ar gyfer Cynlluniau Rheoli Traethlin: Cynnal y llinell: uchelgais i adeiladu neu i gynnal amddiffynfeydd artiffisial fel bod llinell y draethlin yn cael ei chynnal. Gall hyn gynnwys cynnal neu newid safon yr amddiffyniad. Symud y llinell: drwy adeiladu amddiffynfeydd newydd ar yr ochr o\u0027r amddiffynfeydd gwreiddiol sy\u0027n wynebur môr. Ni ddefnyddir hwn yn aml, ac mae\u0027n gyfyngedig i unedau polisi lle ystyrir adfer darn sylweddol o dir. Nid oes unrhyw unedau polisi yng Nghymru lle neilltuwyd yr opsiwn i symud y llinell. Adlinio a reolir: drwy ganiatáu i\u0027r draethlin symud yn ôl ac ymlaen yn naturiol, ond gan reoli\u0027r broses er mwyn ei chyfeirio mewn mannau penodol. Dim ymyrraeth weithredol: pan nad oes unrhyw gynllun i fuddsoddi mewn amddiffynfeydd na gweithrediadau arfordirol, pa un a oes amddiffynfa artiffisial wedi bodoli yn y gorffennol ai peidio. Gall y dull rheoli arfordirol ar gyfer darn penodol o arfordir newid o\u0027r sefyllfa bresennol pan na fydd polisi o bosibl yn ymarferol mwyach nac yn dderbyniol dros gan mlynedd. Felly, gall cyfuniad o bolisïau gael eu cynnig dros oes y Cynllun Rheoli Traethlin. Mae\u0027r set ddata’n nodi pa Gynlluniau Rheoli Traethlin ail genhedlaeth sy\u0027n berthnasol i ran benodol o arfordir Cymru a\u0027r polisïau a neilltuwyd i unedau polisi sy\u0027n ymwneud â\u0027r ardal benodol honno. Haen aml-linell o ddata gofodol yw\u0027r set ddata hon, sydd ar gyfer arfordir Cymru yn unig.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThis dataset was created to provide a strategic overview map; as a consequence, it was created at a national scale of 1: 250,000.  This means that the definition of the breakpoints and the accuracy to which the SMP lengths reflect the \u0027coastline\u0027 is suitable for strategic level use only. Consideration should be given as to whether it should be replaced by a more accurate representation. More detailed representations of the SMP boundaries may be available at Local/Regional level within the SMP documents.   SMP costing information contained within the GIS layer is at a broad scale and indicative only. This data is not available for all policy units and was inserted as part of the original data. This data layer would not be appropriate to support any detailed costings work, or for identifying planned capital expenditure.   This dataset may contain hyperlinks to websites operated by other parties. We do not control such websites and we take no responsibility for and will not incur any liability in respect of their content. Our inclusion of hyperlinks to such websites does not imply any endorsement of views, statements or information contained in such websites.    INFORMATION WARNING: Users should refer to the SMP2 documents for the definitive policies, if any errors are identified following the publication of this edited data layer in January 2019 please contact opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Management plan (SMP) 2.lyr\tInternal Custodians  Mark Pugh ,  FCRM Louise Pennington FCRM Nich Pritchard, FCRM\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2024-12-17\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/5165/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_shoreline_management_plan_policies\t2025-11-06T15:56:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18214\tNRW_DS116262\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.491Z\t636\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGroundwater Vulnerability (1990 - 2008)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323632\tThis is a spatial dataset of Groundwater Vulnerability between 1990 and 2008 residing as a reference or archived dataset in Natural Resources Wales\u0027 geodatabases. A newer version of this data is now available. This older 1990 - 2008 data consists of two polygon spatial layers available at a scale of 1:100,000.  These are Groundwater Vulnerability Zones, and Groundwater Vulnerability Zones Drift. The data broadly define areas relevant to the protection of groundwater.  The approach considers the vulnerability of the groundwater resources as a whole; and the specific importance of areas which for the catchments main sources of supply. Groundwater resources are assigned a vulnerability class [Groundwater Vulnerability 100K], based on soil type and the underlying geology only (e.g. depth to groundwater is not considered): -  Variably permeable groundwater with low leaching potential -  Variably permeable groundwater with intermediate leaching potential -  Variably permeable groundwater high leaching potential -  Highly permeable groundwater with intermediate leaching potential -  Highly permeable groundwater with high leaching potential -  Highly permeable groundwater with low leaching potential The Groundwater Vulnerability data is intended to be used to indicate where groundwater resources may be vulnerable from activities carried out on the surface land.  Other information, such as depth of groundwater and thickness and type of overlying cover will always be required for a site-specific assessment. An assessment of the vulnerability of groundwaters to diffuse pollution is also included as the Groundwater Vulnerability Drift 100K spatial layer (\u0027Drift\u0027 is transported rock debris overlying the solid bedrock) - it shows the distribution of low permeability drift deposits and should be used in conjunction with Groundwater Vulnerability 100K.\tThese data have been mostly superseded by the Aquifer Designation Maps however, these maps do not provide information on surface soils. Aquifers previously designated as major and minor now become principal and secondary respectively. Users will still need to refer to the Groundwater Vulnerability maps if you are assessing activities on undisturbed natural soils (e.g. agricultural land) and need the soil classes.  In this case you should disregard the old geological classes and combine the soils information with the new aquifer designations.\tThis dataset has subsequently been updated. Please refer to the Groundwater Vulnerability 2018 NRW_DS120494 for the most up to date version of the data: https://libcat.naturalresources.wales/webview/?infile\u003ddetails.glu\u0026amp;loid\u003d120494\u0026amp;rs\u003d674418\u0026amp;hitno\u003d2\tThis dataset is not available on the X drive.  But is stored in NRW\u0027s Geodatabases as a reference or archived dataset. this data can be put on the X Drive if needed, but more appropriate 2018 data is available on the X Drive. Feature Dataset: NRWGDBV.GISDATA.Designations Feature Class: NRWGDBV.GISDATA.GW_VULNERABILITY_ZONE Feature Class: NRWGDBV.GISDATA.GW_VULNERABILITY_ZONE_DRIFT\tCustodian: Dave Jones, Technical Specialist (Groundwater), Geoscience/Knowledge Strategy \u0026amp; Planning, NRW  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-07-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:39:04.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18207\tNRW_DS116263\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.229Z\t1375\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) Sensitive Areas - Eutrophic\tRheoliadau Trin Dŵr Gwastraff Trefol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323633\tThe Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017 (WFD Regulations 2017) requires specification of protected areas for those areas designated as nutrient sensitive areas.   The Urban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) Sensitive Areas Eutrophic layer is a replacement layer of the Welsh only sites previously designated under the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive. It shows the extent of Urban Wastewater Treatment Regulation  (UWWTR) sensitive areas (eutrophic).     The UWWTR describes eutrophication as \u0027the enrichment of water by nutrients, especially compounds of nitrogen and/or phosphorous, causing an accelerated growth of algae and higher forms of plant life to produce an undesirable disturbance to the balance of organisms present in the water and to the quality of the water concerned\u0027.   The UWWTR regulates the collection and treatment of waste water from homes and from industry. In the UK, the Directive is implemented through the Urban Wastewater Treatment Regulations 1994. Under these Regulations, water bodies that are (or may soon become) eutrophic should be designated as sensitive areas by Defra. This applies to still fresh waters, rivers, estuaries and coastal waters.-Diffinnir \u0027dŵr gwastraff trefol\u0027 yn y Rheoliadau Trin Dŵr Gwastraff Trefol (RhTDGT) fel y cymysgedd o ddŵr gwastraff domestig o geginau, ystafelloedd ymolchi a thoiledau, y dŵr gwastraff o ddiwydiannau sy\u0027n gollwng i garthffosydd, a dŵr glaw ffo o ffyrdd ac arwynebau anhydraidd eraill fel toeau, palmentydd a ffyrdd sy\u0027n draenio i garthffosydd. Cyfeirir at ddŵr gwastraff trefol yn aml fel \u0027carthion\u0027. Bwriad y RhTGDT yw diogelu\u0027r amgylchedd dŵr ar gyfer yr anifeiliaid a\u0027r planhigion sy\u0027n byw mewn dŵr ac o\u0027i gwmpas, yn ogystal ag at ddibenion hamdden a\u0027r defnydd ohono fel adnodd ar gyfer dŵr yfed, glanweithdra, diwydiant a masnach. Mae\u0027r cofnod hwn yn cyfeirio at gyfres o setiau data gofodol y gellir eu defnyddio ym maes rheoli Dŵr Gwastraff Trefol.  O dan y RhTGDT, mae ardaloedd sensitif pellach wedi\u0027u nodi oherwydd bod angen triniaeth bellach na thriniaeth eilaidd neu gyfatebol i fodloni Cyfarwyddebau EC eraill megis y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr. Mae\u0027r ardaloedd sy\u0027n dod o dan ardaloedd sensitif yn cynnwys Dyfroedd Ymdrochi, Dyfroedd Pysgod Cregyn a dyfroedd sydd eisoes yn Ewtroffig neu a fydd yn Ewtroffig cyn bo hir.  Dim ond ardaloedd sydd wedi\u0027u dynodi gan Lywodraeth Cymru o dan Reoliadau\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr y mae Dyfroedd Pysgod Cregyn RhTGDT yn eu dangos.  Mae Dyfroedd Ymdrochi RhTGDT yn dangos safleoedd lle mae triniaeth fwy caeth na thriniaeth eilaidd yn angenrheidiol i fodloni gofynion y Gyfarwyddeb Dyfroedd Ymdrochi a dylai Defra eu dynodi\u0027n ardaloedd sensitif  Mae Dyfroedd Ewtroffig RhTGDT yn gyrff dŵr sydd (neu a allai’n fuan droi) yn ewtroffig a dylent gael eu dynodi\u0027n ardaloedd sensitif gan Defra. Mae hyn yn berthnasol i ddyfroedd croyw llonydd, afonydd, aberoedd a dyfroedd arfordirol.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis dataset consists of the following in a single layer:   - rivers currently designated as UWWTR eutrophic sensitive areas  - shows harbours and estuaries currently designated as UWWTR eutrophic sensitive areas.    The new polygons were created by selecting features from OS MasterMap Areas 2020. This was supplemented by features from OS MasterMap Areas 2014 upstream of Ross-on-Wye (that extend beyond the Welsh border). Black Brook was added to the feature for the Sensitive Area known as “River Alyn (including Black Brook and Mold Receiving stream)” which had been missing.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\UWWTD Sensitive Areas - Eutrophic.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Geraint Weber Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-08-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/5375/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_uwwtr\t2025-12-19T11:38:55.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18208\tNRW_DS116264\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.297Z\t1084\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) Sensitive Areas - Bathing Waters\tRheoliadau Trin Dŵr Gwastraff Trefol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323634\tThe Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017 (WFD Regulations 2017) requires specification of protected areas for those areas designated as nutrient sensitive areas.     The Urban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) Sensitive Areas - Bathing Waters layer is a replacement layer of the Welsh only sites previously designated under the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive. It shows the extent of Urban Wastewater Treatment Regulation  (UWWTR) sensitive areas Bathing Waters.     The Urban Wastewater Treatment Regulations regulates the collection and treatment of waste water from homes and from industry. In the UK, the directive is implemented through the Urban Wastewater Treatment regulations 1994. Under these regulations, water bodies where treatment more stringent than secondary is necessary to fulfil the requirements of the Bathing Waters Directive should be designated as sensitive areas by Defra.   This dataset shows areas currently designated as UWWTD bathing water sensitive areas.-Diffinnir \u0027dŵr gwastraff trefol\u0027 yn y Rheoliadau Trin Dŵr Gwastraff Trefol (RhTDGT) fel y cymysgedd o ddŵr gwastraff domestig o geginau, ystafelloedd ymolchi a thoiledau, y dŵr gwastraff o ddiwydiannau sy\u0027n gollwng i garthffosydd, a dŵr glaw ffo o ffyrdd ac arwynebau anhydraidd eraill fel toeau, palmentydd a ffyrdd sy\u0027n draenio i garthffosydd. Cyfeirir at ddŵr gwastraff trefol yn aml fel \u0027carthion\u0027. Bwriad y RhTGDT yw diogelu\u0027r amgylchedd dŵr ar gyfer yr anifeiliaid a\u0027r planhigion sy\u0027n byw mewn dŵr ac o\u0027i gwmpas, yn ogystal ag at ddibenion hamdden a\u0027r defnydd ohono fel adnodd ar gyfer dŵr yfed, glanweithdra, diwydiant a masnach. Mae\u0027r cofnod hwn yn cyfeirio at gyfres o setiau data gofodol y gellir eu defnyddio ym maes rheoli Dŵr Gwastraff Trefol.  O dan y RhTGDT, mae ardaloedd sensitif pellach wedi\u0027u nodi oherwydd bod angen triniaeth bellach na thriniaeth eilaidd neu gyfatebol i fodloni Cyfarwyddebau EC eraill megis y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr. Mae\u0027r ardaloedd sy\u0027n dod o dan ardaloedd sensitif yn cynnwys Dyfroedd Ymdrochi, Dyfroedd Pysgod Cregyn a dyfroedd sydd eisoes yn Ewtroffig neu a fydd yn Ewtroffig cyn bo hir.  Dim ond ardaloedd sydd wedi\u0027u dynodi gan Lywodraeth Cymru o dan Reoliadau\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr y mae Dyfroedd Pysgod Cregyn RhTGDT yn eu dangos.  Mae Dyfroedd Ymdrochi RhTGDT yn dangos safleoedd lle mae triniaeth fwy caeth na thriniaeth eilaidd yn angenrheidiol i fodloni gofynion y Gyfarwyddeb Dyfroedd Ymdrochi a dylai Defra eu dynodi\u0027n ardaloedd sensitif  Mae Dyfroedd Ewtroffig RhTGDT yn gyrff dŵr sydd (neu a allai’n fuan droi) yn ewtroffig a dylent gael eu dynodi\u0027n ardaloedd sensitif gan Defra. Mae hyn yn berthnasol i ddyfroedd croyw llonydd, afonydd, aberoedd a dyfroedd arfordirol.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tGeometry snapped to Detailed River Network in 2011. Aligned to Water Framework Directive waterbodies where possible.\t\tUpdated every 4 years  X:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\UWWTR Sensitive Areas Bathing Waters.lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team Internal Custodian - Geraint Weber\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-08-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/5375/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_uwwtr\t2025-05-07T08:46:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18209\tNRW_DS116265\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.307Z\t597\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD) Sensitive Areas - Nitrates\tArdaloedd Sensitif - Nitradau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323635\tThis dataset is a shapefile showing the extent of Urban Wastewater Treatment Directive (91/271/EEC) (UWWTD) sensitive areas (nitrate).   The Urban Wastewater Treatment Directive (91/271/EEC) regulates the collection and treatment of waste water from homes and from industry. In the UK, the directive is implemented through the Urban Wastewater Treatment regulations 1994. Under these regulations, water bodies that are used as sources for drinking water and which have high nitrate concentrations (as defined by Council Directive 75/440/EEC of 16th June 1975) should be designated as sensitive areas by Defra.   This dataset consists of rivers currently designated as UWWTD nitrate sensitive areas.\tGeometry snapped to Detailed River Network in 2011. Aligned to Water Framework Directive waterbodies where possible.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\UWWTD Sensitive Areas - Nitrates.lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team Internal Custodian - Julie Boswell, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T11:35:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18206\tNRW_DS116266\t2024-05-28T12:43:17.175Z\t815\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Risk Areas (Cycle 1)\tArdaloedd Perygl Llifogydd (Cylch 1)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323636\tThe Cycle 1 Flood Risk Areas are those communities in Wales where most significant flood risks exist from surface water flooding only (from surface water run-off, groundwater and ordinary watercourses). These areas were identified for the purpose of the first Preliminary Flood Risk Assessment under the Flood Risk Regulations 2009 using guidance produced by the Welsh Government to identify areas where flood risk is an issue for greater than 5000 people.   The Flood Risk Areas were first identified in 2011 until being replaced in 2018 by the Cycle 2 Flood Risk Areas.-Ardaloedd Perygl Llifogydd Cylch 1 yw\u0027r cymunedau hynny yng Nghymru lle mae\u0027r peryglon llifogydd mwyaf sylweddol yn bodoli o lifogydd dŵr wyneb yn unig (o ddŵr wyneb ffo, dŵr daear a chyrsiau dŵr cyffredin). Nodwyd yr ardaloedd hyn at ddiben yr Asesiad Cychwynnol o Fygythiad Llifogydd cyntaf o dan Reoliadau Perygl Llifogydd 2009 gan ddefnyddio canllawiau a gynhyrchwyd gan Lywodraeth Cymru i nodi ardaloedd lle mae perygl llifogydd yn broblem i fwy na 5,000 o bobl. Nodwyd yr Ardaloedd Perygl Llifogydd am y tro cyntaf yn 2011 hyd nes iddynt gael eu disodli yn 2018 gan Ardaloedd Perygl Llifogydd Cylch 2..  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThese are based on combining risk to people critical services and commercial and public assets and detailed flood modelling. This data could also be based on any Lead Local Flood Authority (LLFA) information included in their Preliminary Flood Risk Assessment (PFRA).\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Risk Areas.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2017-04-21\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/435/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_FLOOD_RISK_AREAS\t2025-12-19T10:57:08.084Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18256\tNRW_DS116267\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.980Z\t369\tValid\tEditable\tInventory of Closed Mining Waste Facilities\tRhestr o Gyfleusterau Gwastraff Mwyngloddio wedi’u Cau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323637\tThe European Mining Waste Directive (2006/21/EC) requires Member States to create an inventory of closed or abandoned mine waste facilities causing serious environmental impacts, and to make this inventory available to the public. A waste facility means any area designated for the accumulation or deposit of extractive waste.-Yn unol â\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Gwastraff Mwyngloddio Ewropeaidd (2006/21/EC) mae\u0027n ofynnol i aelod wladwriaethau greu rhestr o\u0027r cyfleusterau gwastraff mwyngloddio sydd wedi cau neu wedi\u0027u gadael yn wag ac sy\u0027n achosi effeithiau amgylcheddol difrifol, ac i sicrhau bod y rhestr hon ar gael i\u0027r cyhoedd. Mae cyfleusterau gwastraff yn golygu unrhyw ardal a ddynodwyd ar gyfer crynhoi neu ollwng gwastraff echdynnol.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThis dataset contains water quality data from Water Information Management System (WIMS).\t\tX:\\Industrial\\Mines\\Inventory of Closed Mining Waste Facilities.lyr\tRebecca Favager, Regulation Policy and Approach Manager Philip Goodman, Environment officer (Metal mines project manager), Environment management, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2015-03-18\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/420/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_MINING_WASTE_CLOSED_SITES\t2025-12-19T11:50:14.107Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18249\tNRW_DS116268\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.806Z\t588\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 2\tCyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) Dalgylchoedd Cyrff Dŵr Afon Cylch 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323638\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 2 are a polygon dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive.   Catchments are defined as an area of land from which all surface run-off flows through a series of streams, rivers and, possibly, lakes to a particular point in the water course such as a river confluence. Since rivers are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to the WFD river waterbody to which it relates.  Updated to 2018 version.-Mae\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) Dalgylchoedd Cyrff Dŵr Afon Cylch 2 yn set ddata gofodol sy\u0027n crynhoi priodoleddau a gasglwyd fel y diffiniwyd ar gyfer gweithredu’r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD).  Diffinnir Dalgylchoedd fel ardal o dir lle mae’r holl ddŵr wyneb yn llifo oddi arni trwy gyfres o nentydd, afonydd ac, o bosibl, llynnoedd i bwynt penodol yn y cwrs dŵr megis cydlifiad afonydd. Gan fod afonydd yn cael eu priodoli gyda dynodwr unigryw \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 gellid cysylltu’r set ddata hon yn uniongyrchol â\u0027r cyrff dŵr afon WFD perthnasol.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis dataset was originally created for use by co-deliverers before becoming final for Cycle 2 in 2013. Next map digital elevation model was generalised to 10 m resolution.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD River Waterbodies Catchments Cycle2 2015.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 2 2018 (Interim).lyr\tRob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-08-01\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3463/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_river_waterbody_catchments_c2\t2025-04-02T16:23:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18260\tNRW_DS116269\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.035Z\t825\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbodies Cycle 2\tY Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) Dyfroedd Afon Cylch 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323639\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbodies Cycle 2 are a spatial (polygon) dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. \u0027WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 2\u0027 is a subset extracted from the NRW\u0027s Detailed River Network (DRN).   Updated to 2018 version-Mae’r set ddata gofodol hon yn nodi\u0027r cyrff dŵr afon a reolir dan y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr ac unrhyw raglenni cysylltiedig. Mae WFD Cyrff Dŵr Afon Cylch 2 yn is-set a echdynnwyd o Rwydwaith Afonydd Manwl (RhAM) CNC.  Defnyddiwyd setiau data eraill ynghyd â gwybodaeth leol helaeth staff Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru i ychwanegu at y geometreg graidd i gynnwys manylion a phriodoliad gofodol critigol, megis cyfeiriad llif a llwybr, nad yw ar gael o fapiau OS ac i wirio cywirdeb y llinell ganol. Mae rhannau o\u0027r DRN sy’n perthyn i gorff dŵr penodol wedi’u huno fel bod corff dŵr a ddiffiniwyd yn un cofnod yn y set ddata (yn hytrach na bod mewn cannoedd o gofnodion o fewn y RhAM). Mae priodoleddau RhAM yn darfod ar y pwynt yma ac mae priodoliad penodol i’r corff dŵr wedi’i ychwanegu.  Felly, mae’r geometreg ar gyfer y cyrff dŵr wedi’i gymryd o’r RhAM, ond mae’r priodoliad a nifer y cofnodion yn wahanol iawn.  Nid yw pob adran o’r RhAM yn perthyn i gyrff dŵr diffiniedig ac mae set ddata afonydd terfynol y RhAM yn cynrychioli \u0026lt;20% o\u0027r DRN ar-lein. Mae’r set ddata hefyd yn cynnwys setiau data camlesi a throsglwyddo dŵr wyneb a gofnodwyd fel setiau data ar wahân yn y gorffennol.  Rhybudd Gwybodaeth  Gwelwyd canlyniadau anrheolaidd mewn mannau samplu dŵr croyw trwy Gymru ar gyfer orthoffosffad rhwng Ionawr 2014 a Rhagfyr 2016. Defnyddiwyd y data hwn fel rhan o’r set ddata 3 blynedd a gyfarwyddodd ddosbarthiad WFD 2015 ac a effeithiodd ar ganlyniadau’r ymarfer hwnnw.\t\u0027WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 2\u0027 is a subset extracted from the NRW\u0027s Detailed River Network (DRN). Other input datasets and extensive local Natural Resources Wales staff knowledge has been used to augment the core geometry to incorporate critical spatial detail and attribution, such as flow direction and path, not available from the OS mapping and to verify the accuracy of the centreline itself.   Sections of DRN belonging to a particular waterbody have been merged together so that a defined waterbody is a single record within the dataset (rather than being made up of hundreds of records within the DRN). DRN attributes become obsolete at this point and waterbody specific attribution has been added. Therefore the geometry for the resulting waterbodies is taken from the DRN, but the attribution and number of records is very different. Not all sections of the DRN belong to defined waterbodies and the final WFD river waterbody dataset represents \u0026lt;20% of the online DRN.     Information Warning: Anomalous results were observed at freshwater sampling points throughout Wales for orthophosphate between January 2014 and December 2016. This data was used as part of the 3-year data set which informed the 2015 WFD classification and affected the outcome of that exercise. Please refer to the Supporting Documentation on the DataMapWales website for further information.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Rivers Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 2 2018 (Interim).lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team  Internal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2024-02-14\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/6196/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_river_waterbodies_c2\t2025-05-02T14:50:56.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18253\tNRW_DS116270\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.982Z\t691\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 2\tCylch 2 Cyrff Dŵr Llyn y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323730\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 2 is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing Water Framework Directive (WFD) attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive.   Article 2, clause 5 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...a body of standing inland surface water\u0027. There is data on the physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives that can be linked to waterbodies by their unique identifiers.   Artificial and modified lake waterbodies are included within this dataset, however, generally only lakes above \u0026gt; 50 hectares were assessed under the WFD except for lakes in protected areas, where a minimum of 5.0 ha was used. Lakes below this threshold are not included within this dataset unless allocated as Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI).   Each waterbody has been assigned \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027, which is a unique identifier that enables a link to WFD attributes. These data apply to Cycle 2 of the Water Framework Directive.   Updated to 2018 version.-Mae Cyrff Dŵr Llyn y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) yn set ddata ofodol sy\u0027n crynhoi priodoleddau WFD sydd wedi\u0027u casglu fel y diffiniwyd ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD). Mae Erthygl 2, cymal 5 y WFD yn diffinio cyrff dŵr llyn fel ‘…corff o ddŵr wyneb llonydd mewndirol’. Gellir cysylltu data ar nodweddion ffisegol, risg, dosbarthiad ac amcanion arfaethedig i\u0027r cyrff dŵr gyda\u0027u dynodwyr unigryw. Mae llynnoedd artiffisial a llynnoedd wedi\u0027u haddasu\u0027n cael eu cynnwys yn y set ddata: fodd bynnag, yn gyffredinol, dim ond llynnoedd dros 50 hectar a aseswyd dan y WFD heblaw am lynnoedd mewn ardaloedd gwarchodedig, lle defnyddiwyd trothwy o 5.0ha. Nid yw llynnoedd llai na\u0027r trothwy hwn yn cael eu cynnwys oni bai bod ganddynt ddynodiad SoDdGA. Mae gan bob corff dŵr ddynodwr unigryw EA_WB_ID sy\u0027n golygu y gellir ei gysylltu â phriodoleddau WFD. Mae\u0027r data\u0027n berthnasol i Gylch 2 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis data was created from the UK Lake Inventory dataset which itself took geometry from 1:50 k OS products. Cycle 2 data is currently just slightly adapted from Cycle 1 data with some lakes having been subdivided and some new ones added.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2 X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Lakes Cycle 2 2018 (Interim).lyr\tRob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/331/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_LAKES_C2\t2025-12-19T11:51:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18265\tNRW_DS116271\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.066Z\t935\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 2\tY Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) Ardal Dyfroedd Arfordirol Cylch 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323731\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 7 of the WFD defines coastal waterbodies as \u0027...a surface water on the landward side of a line, every point of which is at a distance of one nautical mile on the seaward side from the nearest point of the baseline from which the breadth of territorial waters is measured, extending where appropriate up to the outer limit of transitional waters\u0027. Coastal waters were defined by territorial waters 1 nautical mile from the Mean High Water coastline taken directly from OS 1:50K MeridianTM 2. The delineation between coastal and estuarine waters was delineated by the Environment Agency defined transitional waterbodies. Waterbodies are also split and assigned to River Basin Districts. Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives. This dataset covers the layer for Cycle 2 of the Water framework Directive.-Mae Cyrff Dŵr Arfordirol Cylch 2 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) yn set ddata ofodol sy\u0027n crynhoi priodoleddau sydd wedi\u0027u casglu fel y diffiniwyd ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD). Mae Cylch 1af Cyrff Dŵr Arfordirol WFD yn diffinio Cyrff Dŵr Arfordirol ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD). Mae Erthygl 2, cymal 7 y WFD yn diffinio cyrff dŵr arfordirol fel \u0027dŵr wyneb ar ochr y tir o linell sydd â phob pwynt ar bellter o un filltir fôr ar ochr y môr o\u0027r pwynt agosaf ar y llinell sylfaen o ble mae maint y dyfroedd tiriogaethol yn cael ei fesur, gan ymestyn, pan fo hynny\u0027n briodol, hyd at ffin allanol y dyfroedd trawsnewidiol\u0027. Caiff dyfroedd arfordirol eu diffinio gan ddyfroedd tiriogaethol 1 filltir forol o\u0027r Penllanw Cymedrig, a\u0027r arfordir a gymerwyd yn uniongyrchol o OS 1: 50K MeridianTM 2. Diffiniwyd y newid rhwng dyfroedd arfordirol ac aberol fel cyrff dŵr trosiannol. Gan fod cyrff dŵr yn cael eu priodoli gyda dynodwr unigryw (EA_WB_ID) gellir cysylltu\u0027r set ddata hon yn uniongyrchol â ffynonellau data eraill y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr megis nodweddion ffisegol, risg, dosbarthiad a\u0027r amcanion arfaethedig.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydde\tThis was created from originally from OS data and territorial waters data as described in the abstract. The Cycle 2 version represents a minor update of the cycle 1 dataset.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team Internal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://mapdata.llyw.cymru/capabilities/layer/431/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://mapdata.llyw.cymru/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_COASTAL_C2\t2025-03-07T15:58:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18250\tNRW_DS116272\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.904Z\t663\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 2\tY Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) Ardal Dyfroedd Trawsnewidiol Cylch 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323732\t\u0027Water Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional Waterbodies\u0027 is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 6 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...bodies of surface water in the vicinity of river mouths which are partly saline in character as a result of their proximity to coastal waters but are substantially influenced by freshwater flows\u0027.   Transitional waterbodies were defined from Mean High Water boundaries, taken directly from OS 1:50K MeridianTM 2, and NRW estuarine boundaries defined for the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD). Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives. These data apply to Cycle 2 of the Water Framework Directive.-Mae Cyrff Dŵr (Aberol) Trosiannol WFD yn set ddata ofodol sy\u0027n cynnwys priodoleddau sydd wedi\u0027u casglu fel y diffiniwyd ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD). Mae Erthygl 2, cymal 6 y WFD yn eu diffinio fel ‘…cyrff dŵr wyneb yng nghyffiniau aberoedd afonydd sy\u0027n rhannol halwynog eu natur gan eu bod yn agos i ddyfroedd arfordirol, ond eu bod dan ddylanwad sylweddol llifoedd dŵr croyw’. Caiff dyfroedd trosiannol eu diffinio gan ffiniau Penllanw Cymedrig, a gymerwyd yn uniongyrchol o OS 1:50K MeridianTM 2, a ffiniau aberol a ddiffiniwyd ar gyfer y Gyfarwyddeb Trin Dŵr Gwastraff Trefol (UWWTD).  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis was created from originally from OS data and territorial waters / coastal waters data as described in the abstract. The Cycle 2 version represents a minor update of the Cycle 1 dataset.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tInternal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/430/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_TRANSITIONAL_C2\t2025-03-07T15:58:48.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18248\tNRW_DS116273\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.789Z\t1006\tValid\tEditable\tRisk of Flooding from Surface Water\tMap Llifogydd a Ddiweddarwyd ar gyfer Pecyn Sylfaenol Dŵr Arwyneb\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323733\tThe surface water flood maps give an indication of the broad areas likely to be at risk of surface water flooding.  This includes flooding that takes place from the surface runoff generated by rainwater (including snow and other precipitation) that: (a) is on the surface of the ground (whether or not it is moving), and (b) has not yet entered a watercourse, drainage system or public sewer.  The Flood Map for Surface Water pick out natural drainage channels, rivers, low areas in floodplains, and flow paths between buildings. But it does not indicate flooding caused by local rainfall.  It does not show flooding that occurs from overflowing watercourses, drainage systems or public sewers caused by catchment-wide rainfall events or river flow.  A national model has been run for 1 in 30, 1 in 100 and 1 in 1000 year rainfall events.  It has been modelled on a 2 metre square grid.  Lead Local Flood Authorities were consulted and where available locally held model outputs have been incorporated into the maps.  This dataset has been superseded by the new National Flood Hazard Maps 2019 - NRW_DS124790\tThe Flood Map for Surface Water is in vector (polygon) format and includes the following component layers:  1. EXTENTS - three layers showing extent of flooding for three separate probabilities : 1 in 30 (high risk), 1 in 100 (medium risk) and 1 in 1000 (low risk) chance of flooding in one year,  2. DEPTHS - three banded layers showing depth of flooding for three separate probabilities: 1 in 30 (high risk), 1 in 100 (medium risk) and 1 in 1000 (low risk) chance of flooding in one year, 3. VELOCITY - three banded layers showing flooding velocity for three separate probabilities: 1 in 30 (high risk), 1 in 100 (medium risk) and 1 in 1000 (low risk) chance of flooding in one year, 4. HAZARDS - is the Hazard rating and is a function of depth and velocity for the particular scenario using the Hazard Rating formula HR \u003d d x (v + 0.5) + DF where HR is hazard rating, d is depth, v is velocity and DF is a debris factor. 5. FLOW DIRECTION (25m) - three banded layers showing flood flow direction (at maximum velocity) for three separate probabilities: 1 in 30, 1 in 100 and 1 in 1000 (created from resampled 25m raster grid) 6. SUITABILITY - one layer showing basic confidence information from the modelling which gives an indication of the scale at which is it generally appropriate to use this information to assess flood risk and parameters used.  This dataset is not suitable for identifying whether an individual property will flood. Because of the way they have been produced and the fact that they are indicative, the maps are not appropriate to act as the sole evidence for any specific planning or regulatory decision or assessment of risk in relation to flooding at any scale without further supporting studies or evidence.\thttps://www.gov.uk/government/uploads/system/uploads/attachment_data/file/297432/LIT_8988_0bf634.pdf\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Surface Water\\Surface Water Flood Risk 30.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Surface Water\\Surface Water Flood Risk 100.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Surface Water\\Surface Water Flood Risk 1000.lyr\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM, NRW Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW       Data will not be added to Lle as the packaged data is too large and complex, will remain as a ShareFile download\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-10-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18259\tNRW_DS116274\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.924Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFlood Map for Surface Water Complex Package\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323734\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:52:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18257\tNRW_DS116275\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.982Z\t437\tValid\tEditable\tRisk of Flooding from Rivers and Seas (RoFRS) Property Flood Likelihood Category (archived data)\tPerygl Llifogydd o Afonydd a’r Môr - Cronfa Ddata Categorïau Tebygolrwydd Effaith Llifogydd ar Eiddo\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323735\tThis dataset is now static. It is therefore no longer updated and is out of date.  This dataset is a product of a national assessment of flood risk for Wales produced using local expertise. This dataset is produced using [Risk of Flooding from Rivers and Sea] which shows the chance of flooding from rivers and/or the sea, based on cells of 50m. Each cell is allocated one of four flood risk categories, taking into account flood defences and their condition.   This dataset uses OS data to assign one of four flood risk categories to each property, based simply on the category allocated to the cell that the property is in. Individual addresses are not provided, but OS referencing is included to enable the data to be linked to address databases.\tThis dataset is now static with no planned updates. Therefore the information contained within will be out of date.   This dataset is supplied on request under an open government licence.    - This dataset is indicative of the scale and distribution of the likelihood of flooding and therefore we advise that viewing at more detailed scales may not be appropriate.  - Because of the way they have been produced and the fact that they are indicative, the maps are not appropriate to act as the sole evidence for any specific planning or regulatory decision or assessment of risk in relation to flooding at any scale without further supporting studies or evidence.  - This data is not raw, factual or measured. It comprises of estimated or modelled results showing expected outcomes based on the data available to us.\t\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116275\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Flood Risk Manager\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-10-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-06-23T14:56:16.595Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18262\tNRW_DS116276\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.060Z\t1037\tValid\tEditable\tRisk of Flooding from Rivers and Seas (RoFRS) (archived data)\tRisg Llifogydd o Afonydd a Moroedd (RoFRS)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323736\tThis dataset has been superceded by the Flood Risk Assessment Wales dataset. It is therefore no longer updated. You can still view your risk of flooding by postcode on our website https://naturalresources.wales/flooding/check-your-flood-risk-by-postcode/?lang\u003den   This is a national assessment of flood risk for Wales produced using local expertise. The dataset shows the chance of flooding from rivers and/or the sea, based on cells of 50 m. Each cell is allocated one of four flood risk categories, taking into account flood defences and their condition. No more detailed resolution is provided.\tSpatial dataset with the floodplain split into 50m x 50m cells and each allocated one of four flood risk likelihood categories.    -  High: each year, there is a chance of flooding of greater than 1 in 30 (3.3 percent). -  Medium: each year, there is a chance of flooding of between 1 in 30 (3.3 percent) and 1 in 100 (1 percent).  -  Low: each year, there is a chance of flooding of between 1 in 100 (1 percent) and 1 in 1000 (0.1 percent).  -  Very Low: each year, there is a chance of flooding of less than 1 in 1000 (0.1 percent).  Cells where the likelihood of flooding is greater than or equal to 1 in 75 (1.3 percent) each year are identified. Each cell has a suitability rating to show at what scale it is generally appropriate to use the data to assess flood risk, and how suitable the data is for a range of different uses.  If data is displayed in map form to others it should be shown with a maximum zoom scale of 1:10,000 and that base mapping of 1:25,000 is used to give context.   Risk of Flooding from Rivers and Seas (RoFRS) results are indicative of the scale and distribution of the likelihood of flooding and therefore we advise that viewing at more detailed scales may not be appropriate.    -  Because of the way they have been produced and the fact that they are indicative, the maps are not appropriate to act as the sole evidence for any specific planning or regulatory decision or assessment of risk in relation to flooding at any scale without further supporting studies or evidence.   -  This data is not raw, factual or measured. It comprises of estimated or modelled results showing expected outcomes based on the data available to us.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Risk of Flooding from Rivers and Sea (RoFRS).lyr\tDatasets custodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, FIRM, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2017-09-20\tpublication\tnull\thttps://naturalresources.wales/evidence-and-data/maps/long-term-flood-risk/?lang\u003den\t2025-06-23T14:47:41.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18251\tNRW_DS116277\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.983Z\t890\tValid\tEditable\tRisk of Flooding from Rivers and Seas (RoFRS) Postcode Flood Likelihood Category (archived data)\tPerygl Llifogydd o Afonydd a’r Môr – Cronfa Ddata Categorïau Llifogydd Côd Post\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323737\tThis dataset is now static. It is therefore no longer updated and is out of date.  This dataset is a product of a national assessment of flood risk for England produced using local expertise. This dataset is produced using [Risk of Flooding from Rivers and Sea] which shows the chance of flooding from rivers and/or the sea, based on cells of 50m. Each cell is allocated one of four flood risk categories, taking into account flood defences and their condition.   This dataset uses OS address data and Royal Mail postcode data to show how many properties are in each of four flood risk categories in each postcode, based simply on the category allocated to the cell that each property is in.\tThis dataset is now static with no planned updates. Therefore the information contained within it will be out of date.   This dataset is supplied on request under an open government licence.    - This dataset is  indicative of the scale and distribution of the likelihood of flooding and therefore we advise that viewing at more detailed scales may not be appropriate.  - Because of the way they have been produced and the fact that they are indicative, the maps are not appropriate to act as the sole evidence for any specific planning or regulatory decision or assessment of risk in relation to flooding at any scale without further supporting studies or evidence.  - This data is not raw, factual or measured. It comprises of estimated or modelled results showing expected outcomes based on the data available to us.\tPlease see documents in the data download\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116277\tDataset custodian: Mark Pugh, Flood Risk Manager\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-06-23T15:00:03.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18255\tNRW_DS116278\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.983Z\t1013\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Operational Catchments Cycle 2\tY Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) Dalgylchoedd Gweithredol Cylch 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323738\tOperational catchments are a way of grouping Water Framework Directive (WFD) waterbodies together at a meaningful scale smaller than the management catchment or River Basin District. The river waterbodies are not always hydrologically linked as they may have been grouped based on pressures and measures. This data set is based on the surface water boundaries of operational catchments and excludes the underlying Groundwater data.-Mae dalgylchoedd gweithredol yn ffordd o grwpio cyrff dŵr y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr gyda\u0027i gilydd ar raddfa ystyrlon, un sy\u0027n llai na dalgylch rheoli neu Ardal Basn Afon. Nid yw cyrff dŵr afonydd bob amser wedi\u0027u cysylltu\u0027n hydrolegol oherwydd efallai eu bod wedi\u0027u grwpio ar sail pwysau a mesurau. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn seiliedig ar ffiniau dŵr wyneb dalgylchoedd gweithredol ac nid yw\u0027n cynnwys y data dŵr daear sylfaenol.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tCreated by merging the Water Framework Directive (WFD) river waterbody catchments the WFD coastal waterbodies and the WFD transitional waterbodies into larger functional groups.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Operational Catchments Cycle2 Draft.lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team Internal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/432/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_OPERATIONAL_CATCHMENTS_C2\t2025-03-07T15:52:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18264\tNRW_DS116279\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.061Z\t1274\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts Cycle 2\tY Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) Ardaloedd Basn Afonydd Cylch 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323739\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts (RBD) is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...the area of land and sea, made up of one or more neighbouring river basins together with their associated groundwaters and coastal waters…\u0027.   River Basin Districts have been delineated by using River Catchments as \\building blocks\\\" that have been aggregated together within a GIS ensuring that WFD rivers do not intersect boundaries. Coastal and transitional waterbodies are also merged and assigned to a river basin district. River Catchments were delineated through use of flow data and a digital terrain model run through a hydrological model. These data apply to Cycle 2 of the Water Framework Directive.\"-Mae Mae Ardaloedd Basn Afon (RBD) y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) yn set ddata ofodol sy\u0027n crynhoi priodoleddau a gasglwyd fel y diffiniwyd ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD). Mae Erthygl 2, cymal 2 o\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr yn eu diffinio fel \u0027... yr ardal o dir a môr sy\u0027n cynnwys un neu fwy o fasnau afonydd cyfagos ynghyd â\u0027u dyfroedd daear cysylltiedig a dyfroedd arfordirol ...\u0027. Mae Ardaloedd Basn Afon wedi\u0027u dyrannu gan ddefnyddio Dalgylchoedd Afonydd fel \"blociau adeiladu\" sydd wedi\u0027u rhoi at ei gilydd mewn GIS, gan sicrhau nad yw afonydd GFfD yn croestorri ffiniau. Mae cyrff dŵr arfordirol a chyrff dŵr trosiannol yn cael eu huno a\u0027u dosbarthu i ardaloedd basn afon hefyd. Amlinellwyd dalgylchoedd afonydd drwy ddefnyddio data llif a model tir digidol wedi\u0027i redeg drwy fodel hydrolegol.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe Cycle 2 RBD boundaries look very similar to the cycle 1 RBD boundaries but are based on the Cycle 2 versions of the river waterbody catchments, transitional and coastal waters. As a result some of the base datasets used for their delineation have changes and they have been declared OpenData.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD River Basin Districts Cycle2 Draft.lyr\tMetadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team Internal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/334/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_RIVER_BASIN_DISTRICTS_C2\t2025-03-07T15:58:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18263\tNRW_DS116280\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.059Z\t798\tValid\tEditable\tNational Receptor Dataset (NRD) 2014\tCronfa Ddata Genedlaethol Derbynyddion (2014)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323830\tThe National Receptor Dataset (NRD) is a collection of risk receptors primarily intended for use in flood and coastal erosion risk management. The NRD includes property points that are Copy Derived from Ordnance Survey (OS) AddressBase Premium with information on property type, floor area and Flood Hazard Research Centres Multi-Coloured Manual codes attributed.  It is designed as the consequence information (particular properties) for risk assessments with flood hazards.   The version of the NRD described here is the NRD2014. This version of the NRD supersedes NRD2011, which was released in November 2011.\t- NRD 2014 is derived from two sources:  OS Addressbase Premium and OS MasterMap Topography.  Updates to both are released every 6 weeks. NRD2014 is therefore a snapshot of a particular moment in time and contains data that was sourced in Winter 2014.   NRD 2014 has not been designed for addressing purposes and we don’t recommend using it for this, mainly because it may not be up-to-date.  We recommend the OS Addressbase Premium instead.   - NRD 2014 has been compiled to provide a general source of receptor information for a range of FCRM activities; you will need to decide whether it is appropriate for your specific application.   For local studies, you will need to check whether any more detailed local information is available, and whether it would be more relevant to the work being undertaken.   You should ensure that you refer to all current guidance on our website or supplied to you when using the Content.   Please note that in NRD2014 not all records are properties; some are parent shells, some are features associated with properties such as CCTV, advertising hoardings and building groupings referred to as property shells by Geoplace.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Receptors Database (NRD) 2014.lyr\tCustodian: Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2020-10-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18254\tNRW_DS116281\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.972Z\t707\tValid\tEditable\tBetter Woodlands for Wales Case Boundaries\tFfiniau Achosion Coetir Gwell i Gymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323831\tBetter Woodlands for Wales (BBW) is the Forestry Commission Wales (FCW) grant scheme designed to meet the Woodland Strategy vision. The vision of the strategy for Wales to be known for its high-quality woodlands that enhance the landscape, are appropriate to local conditions and have a diverse mixture of species and habitats. The BBW scheme has been developed in consultation with woodland owners and their agents as well as our partner organisations. As a result, the new scheme places greater emphasis on good quality woodland management and offers grants specially designed for Welsh woodlands. Grant aid is now based upon an approved long term management plan. This plan must meet the minimum standards under the UK Woodland Assurance Scheme. BWW was closed to new applications in April 2012.-Bu cynllun grant Coetiroedd Gwell i Gymru (BWW) ar waith o 2006 hyd nes y cyflwynwyd cynllun Grant Creu Coetir Glastir yn 2010/11. Roedd BWW yn darparu cymorth i reolwyr tir i gyflawni amcanion polisi coedwigaeth, gan gynnwys creu coetir newydd a rheoli coetiroedd oedd yn bodoli eisoes er mwyn sicrhau amrywiaeth o ganlyniadau amgylcheddol ac amwynder cymdeithasol llesol.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe BWW scheme boundary was captured to OSMM (OS Master Map) from Management Planners paper maps (provided by FCW) or by importing GI spatial data from Management Planners who have their own GIS capabilities.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Better Woods For Wales Boundary.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Better Woods for Wales SCDB.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-11-30\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/328/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_BWW_CASE_BOUNDARIES\t2025-03-07T15:59:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18247\tNRW_DS116283\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.703Z\t399\tValid\tEditable\tWoodland Dedications\tCoetiroedd Penodedig\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323833\tThe Dedication woodland Scheme (Basis I and II) was introduced in 1947 in order to encourage landowners to retain their land in forestry and to introduce good forestry practice. Basis III was introduced in 1974, providing grants for new planting and additional supplements for broadleaves. The Dedication Scheme was closed to new applicants in 1981. Land still under Dedication could continue to be within the scheme but Dedication would terminate on a change of ownership. Dedication schemes without a Plan of Operations and therefore receiving no grant, are deemed to be under Negative Covenant.-Cyflwynwyd y Cynllun Neilltuo (Sail I II) ym 1947 er mwyn annog perchnogion tir i ymrwymo i gadw eu tir mewn coedwigaeth ac i gyflwyno arferion coedwigaeth dda. Cyflwynwyd Sail III ym 1974, gan ddarparu grantiau ar gyfer plannu newydd ac arian ychwanegol ar gyfer coed llydanddail. Caewyd y cynllun i ymgeiswyr newydd ym 1981. Gallai tir oedd eisoes yn y cynllun barhau felly ond byddai\u0027r ymrwymiad yn dod i ben gyda newid perchnogaeth. Mae Cynlluniau Neilltuo sydd heb Gynllun Gweithredu, ac felly ddim yn derbyn grant, yn cael eu hystyried i fod dan Gyfamod Caethiwus. Nodweddion Set Ddata: Disgrifydd : Enw Set Ddata Enw _ Achos: Enw Cynllun Neilltuo Rhif _Achos: Cyfeirif Cynllun Neilltuo Sail: Rhif Sail y Cynllun (I, II neu III) Dyddiad_Cymeradwyo: Dyddiad y daeth y coetir yn Gynllun Gweithredu wedi\u0027i Neilltuo: Cyfnod Cyfamod Cynllun Gweithredu presennol (neu ddiwethaf) : Cynllun o dan Gyfamod ‘Cadarnhaol’ neu ‘Gaethiwus’ Cyf Grid : Cyfeirnod Grid yr eiddo Enw\u0027r _Asiant : Enw\u0027r asiant sy\u0027n gweithredu ar ran y perchennog Cyfanswm_Arwynebedd : Cyfanswm arwynebedd y Cynllun (*Ddim bob amser yn cynnwys OL) Ardal a Reolir : Cyfanswm yr arwynebedd a reolir.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThe spatial element of this dataset has been digitised by CGIS, FCS using both Laserscan\u0027s LAMPS and ESRI\u0027s ArcView software. The textual element has been extracted from an Excel spreadsheet and associated with the polygon data. Information is mapped from contract maps supplied by the applicants (normally based on 1:2500 or 1:10,000 OS maps). Spatial data has been digitised against OS raster backdrops using 1:25,000 scale between 1997 and 2000, and 1:10,000 scale between 2000 and 2004.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Dedications.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-09-26\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/419/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_DEDICATED_WOODLANDS\t2025-03-07T16:51:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18252\tNRW_DS116284\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.981Z\t1211\tValid\tEditable\tFelling Licence Applications\tCeisiadau am Drwyddedau Cwympo Coed\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323834\tAnyone wishing to fell trees must ensure that a licence or permission under a grant scheme has been issued by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) before any felling is carried out or that one of the exceptions apply.  The applicant would normally need to get permission from the NRW to fell growing trees. This is usually given in a Felling Licence or an approval under a Dedication Scheme.   This dataset provides a record of all approved Felling License Applications in Wales and type of license approved.  The data available to the public under an Open Government Licence has personal information redacted.-Dylai unrhyw un sy\u0027n dymuno cwympo coed sicrhau bod trwydded neu ganiatâd o dan gynllun grant wedi\u0027i roi gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) cyn i unrhyw gwympo ddigwydd, neu fod un o\u0027r eithriadau\u0027n berthnasol. Fel arfer, mae angen i\u0027r ymgeisydd gael caniatâd gan CNC i gwympo coed sy\u0027n tyfu. Fel arfer, rhoddir hyn ar ffurf Trwydded Torri Coed neu gymeradwyaeth dan Gynllun Neilltuo. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn gofnod o\u0027r holl geisiadau am Drwyddedau Cwympo Coed a gymeradwywyd yng Nghymru a\u0027r math o drwydded a gymeradwywyd.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThe spatial element of this dataset has been digitised by CGIS, FCW using both Laserscan\u0027s LAMPS and ESRI\u0027s ArcView software. Information is mapped following approval of the licence from agreement maps supplied by the applicants (normally based on 1:2500 or 1:10,000 OS maps). Spatial data has been digitised against OS raster backdrops using 1:25,000 scale between 1997 and 2000, and 1:10,000 scale between 2000 and 2004.\t\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Felling Licences\\Felling Licence Applications.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/397/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_FELLING_LICENCE_APPLICATIONS\t2026-01-12T13:55:33.034Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18246\tNRW_DS116285\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.638Z\t642\tValid\tEditable\tNational Inventory of Woodland and Trees\tRhestr Genedlaethol Coetiroedd a Choed\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323835\tThis dataset was created by Forestry Commission Wales (FCW) and now remains as a static archive of the data. This file contains Forestry Commission\u0027s previous survey, the National Inventory of Woodland and Trees, and includes spatial data and associated metadata for the whole of Great Britain. This dataset shows all areas of woodland over 2ha in Great Britain and the Interpreted Forest Type (IFT) of those woodlands. The dataset includes FC new planting and new Woodland Grant Schemes as of 31st March 2002.-Mae\u0027r data hwn yn cynnwys arolwg blaenorol y Comisiwn Coedwigaeth, y Rhestr Genedlaethol o Goetiroedd a Choed, a data gofodol ar gyfer Prydain Fawr i gyd. Dengys y set ddata bob ardal o goetir dros 2ha ym Mhrydain Fawr a\u0027r math o goedwig yn ôl y dehongliad. Mae\u0027r set ddata\u0027n cynnwys plannu newydd y Comisiwn Coedwigaeth a Chynlluniau Grant Coetir newydd fel ar 31 Mawrth 2002.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tWelsh data is based on interpretation of 1:25000 (some fill in 1:10000) aerial photography (flown 1991-1999) and plotted against OS 1:25000 mapping for the Forestry Commission. Miscellaneous adjustments to original aerial photography interpretation (API) as detected by Survey Foresters Woodland Grant Schemes approx.1992-1999 - digitised from paper maps held by FC. 1995-2002 - WGS Digital data supplied by FC. (Date varies per 100k tile) FC New Planting 1992-1999 - digitised from FC paper maps. 1995-2002 - Digital data supplied by FC. (Date varies per 100k tile).\t\tX:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Inventory of Woodland \u0026amp; Trees (GB) - 2002.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-10-29\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/436/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_NIWT\t2025-02-04T10:45:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18261\tNRW_DS116287\t2024-05-28T12:43:56.049Z\t466\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Recreation Routes\tLlwybrau Hamdden Coedwig CNC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323837\tThe usage, name and grade of the series of recreation segments that link together to form linear recreation features, for example, mountain bike trails.  -  Walking:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment e.g. Nature trail, heritage trail, education trail, forest walk.  -  Cycling:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment e.g. Mountain bike route, road bike, downhill, cross-country, family, rough route.  -  Equestrian:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment e.g. Carriage route, standard. -  Forest Drive:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment e.g. Toll, Toll free.  -  Running:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment.  -  Huskies:  Must be promoted as an NRW forest route and have management activity and investment.  -  Emergency Services:  Must be an agreed access route for emergency services (Fire, ambulance, mountain rescue).  -  Rally:  Must be an approved rally route.  Other Route Types are further split into Route Subtype; Nature Trail, Sculpture Trail, Heritage Trail, Sensory Trail, Education Trail, Play Trail, Downhill, Cross Country, Family, Carriage Route, Toll, Toll Free, Fire, Ambulance, Mountain Rescue, Forest Walk, Trim Trail, All ability Access Trail, Mountain Bike, Road Bike, Standard, Cross Country Ski, Segway, Other. Where appropriate routes are graded; Easy, Moderate, Difficult; Green - Easy, Blue - Moderate, Red - Difficult, Black - Severe, Orange - Bike Park, Forest road or similar.-Defnydd, enw a gradd cyfresi o segmentau hamdden sy\u0027n cysylltu â\u0027i gilydd i ffurfio nodweddion hamdden llinol, er enghraifft, llwybrau beicio mynydd neu gerdded.  - Cerdded - rhaid iddo fod yn cael ei hyrwyddo fel llwybr CNC, a bod ganddo weithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi (e.e. Llwybr natur, llwybr treftadaeth, llwybr addysg, coedwig).  - Beicio - rhaid iddo fod yn cael ei hyrwyddo fel llwybr CNC, a bod ganddo weithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi (llwybr beicio mynydd, beicio ffordd, lawr allt, croes).  - Marchogaeth - rhaid iddo fod yn cael ei hyrwyddo fel llwybr CNC, a bod ganddo weithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi (llwybr gyrru, safonol).  - Llwybr dreifio - rhaid iddo fod yn cael ei hyrwyddo fel llwybr CNC, a bod ganddo weithgarwch rheoli a buddsoddi (e.e. Toll, di-doll).  - Rhedeg - rhaid iddo fod yn cael ei hyrwyddo fel llwybr CNC, a bod ganddo weithgarwch rheoli a buddsoddi.  - Huskies - rhaid iddo fod yn cael ei hyrwyddo fel llwybr CNC, a bod ganddo weithgarwch rheoli a buddsoddi.  - Gwasanaethau Brys - rhaid iddo fod yn llwybr mynediad a gytunwyd ar gyfer y gwasanaethau brys (tân, ambiwlans, achub mynydd).  - Rali - rhaid iddo fod yn llwybr rali wedi\u0027i gymeradwyo.  Mae rhestr lawn o\u0027r gwerthoedd wedi\u0027u codio a ddefnyddir yn y set ddata i\u0027w gweld yma.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe FC\u0027s Forester Recreation extension was rolled out to Forest Districts (FDs) in 2010. Since then FDs have been using this to record new recreation features, and those previously stored locally, to a common standard across GB.  Since the creation of NRW in 2013, NRW have maintained the data in the Forester recreation module.\t\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\NRW Forest Recreation Routes.lyr\tCustodian: Geraint Morgan and Arpana Chunihal, National Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-01-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/343/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_GB_RECREATION_ROUTES\t2025-10-10T07:51:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18258\tNRW_DS116288\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.980Z\t780\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Recreation Areas\tArdaloedd Hamdden Ystâd Coedwigaeth Genedlaethol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323838\tA recreation area feature which is managed and has a physical boundary (e.g. a car park, play area or building). There are a number of Asset Types: -  Vehicle:  Purpose built and installed NRW asset, or regularly used natural feature, which has management activity and investment e.g. Car park, layby.  -  Visitor area:  Purpose built and installed NRW asset, which has management activity and investment e.g. BBQ area, picnic, education, events, arboretum.  -  Sports:  Purpose built and installed NRW asset (or sub-let), which has management activity and investment e.g. Archery, bike park, ropes course, GoApe.  -  Building:  Purpose built and installed NRW asset, which has management activity and investment e.g. Visitor centre, toilet, cycle hire, catering, shelter.  -  Play:  NRW area designated for play activities, which has management activity and investment e.g. Deb building, play area, paddling.  -  Accommodation:  Purpose built and installed NRW asset, which has management activity and investment e.g. Camp site, cabin site, caravan site, wild camp.  - Ponds/Lakes:  Natural or man-made feature, which has management activity and investment (Natural, fishing, model boats, duck flighting). -  Concert:  NRW area designated for concert activities, which has management activity and investment (Stage, backstage, viewing area).   Additionally these asset types are expanded on using a range of Asset Subtypes;  Car Park, Layby, BBQ Area, Picnic, Archery, Bike Park, Water Sports, Visitor Centre, Toilet, Cycle Hire, Information Office, Wildlife Hide, Heritage building, Shelter, Den Building, Play Area - Formal, Play Area - Wild, Paddling, Camp Site, Cabin Site, Caravan site, Natural, Fishing, Model Boars, Duck Flighting, Stage, Back stage, Viewing Area, Woodland Bays, Events, Go Ape, Climbing, Education, Ropes courses, Catering, Forest Classroom, Retail Outlet, Arboretum, Memorial Trees, Wild Camp Area.-Ardal hamdden sy\u0027n cael ei rheoli ac sydd â ffin ffisegol (e.e. maes parcio, lle chwarae neu adeilad). Mae yna nifer o Fathau o Asedau:  - Cerbydau: ased a adeiladwyd i’r diben ac a osodwyd yn ei le gan CNC neu nodwedd naturiol a ddefnyddir yn aml, ac sydd â gweithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi (e.e. Maes parcio, cilfan)  - Ardal ymwelwyr: ased a adeiladwyd i’r diben ac a osodwyd yn ei le gan CNC, ac sydd â gweithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi (e.e. ardal farbeciw, picnic, addysg, digwyddiadau, coedfa)  - Chwaraeon: Ased a adeiladwyd i’r diben ac a osodwyd yn ei le gan CNC (neu sy\u0027n cael ei is-osod), ac sydd â gweithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi (e.e. saethyddiaeth, parc beiciau, cwrs rhaffau, Go Ape)  - Adeilad: ased a adeiladwyd i’r diben ac a osodwyd yn ei le gan CNC, ac sydd â gweithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi. Canolfan ymwelwyr, toiledau, llogi beiciau, arlwyo, lloches.  - Chwarae: ardal CNC a ddynodwyd ar gyfer gweithgareddau chwarae, ac sydd â gweithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi (adeilad cuddfan, ardal chwarae, lle padlo).  - Llety: ased a adeiladwyd i’r diben ac a osodwyd yn ei le gan CNC, ac sydd â gweithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi (e.e. maes gwersylla, safle cabanau, safle carafanau, gwersyll gwyllt.  - Pyllau/llynnoedd: nodwedd naturiol neu wedi\u0027i chreu sydd â gweithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi (naturiol, pysgota, cychod bach, pwll hwyaid).  - Cyngerdd: ardal CNC a ddynodwyd ar gyfer gweithgareddau cyngerdd, sydd â gweithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi (llwyfan, tu cefn llwyfan, ardal wylio).  Ymhelaethir ar y mathau hyn o asedau gan ddefnyddio cyfres o Is-setiau Ased: Maes Parcio, Cilfan, Ardal Farbeciw, Ardal Bicnic, Saethyddiaeth, Parc Beiciau, Chwaraeon Dŵr, Canolfan Ymwelwyr, Toiledau, Llogi Beiciau, Swyddfa Wybodaeth, Cuddfan Bywyd Gwyllt, Adeilad Treftadaeth, Lloches, Adeilad Cuddfan, Ardal Chwarae, Maes Chwarae Ffurfiol, Padlo Gwyllt, Maes Gwersylla, Maes Cabanau, Maes Carafanau, Pysgota Naturiol, Cychod Bach, Pwll Hwyaid, Llwyfan, Tu Cefn Llwyfan, Ardal Wylio, Baeau Coetir, Digwyddiadau, Go Ape, Dringo, Addysg, Cyrsiau Rhaffau, Arlwyo, Ysgol Goedwig, Siop, Coedfa, Coed Coffa, Ardal Gwersyll Gwyllt.  Noder mai prif ddefnydd y setiau data hyn yw ar gyfer rheoli hamdden yn fewnol, felly gofynnwn iddynt beidio â chael eu defnyddio mewn unrhyw gymhwysiad ar y rhyngrwyd neu gyhoeddiad heb gymeradwyaeth gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru ymlaen llaw.  Mae rhestr lawn o\u0027r gwerthoedd wedi\u0027u codio a ddefnyddir yn y set ddata i\u0027w gweld yma.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe FC\u0027s Forester Recreation extension was rolled out to Forest Districts (FDs) in 2010. Since then FDs have been using this to record new recreation features, and those previously stored locally, to a common standard across GB.  Since the creation of NRW in 2013, NRW have maintained the data in the Forester recreation module.\t\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\NRW Forest Recreation Areas.lyr\tCustodian: Geraint Morgan and Arpana Chunihal, National Forest Planning Team     Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-01-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/345/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_GB_RECREATION_AREAS\t2025-10-10T07:51:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18242\tNRW_DS116289\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.181Z\t615\tValid\tEditable\tShoreline Management Plan and Coastal Erosion (archived data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323839\tThis dataset has been archived and is no longer maintained, please see dataset NRW_DS121852 \"National Coastal Erosion Risk Management (NCERM)\" for more up to date information.  The datasets shows the spatial coastal baseline which is split into \u0027frontages\u0027. These are defined as lengths of coast with consistent characteristics based on the cliff behaviour characteristics and the defence characteristics. It is intended as a benchmark dataset showing erosion extents and rates for three periods:    -  Short Term (0 - 20 yr)  -  Medium Term (20 - 50 yr) and  -  Long Term (50 - 100 yr).    Defence type and Shoreline Management Plan (SMP) policies for each of the three periods described above are included.\tWhere this data is used the following information warnings must be passed on with the data.     INFORMATION WARNING: The data and associated information is intended for guidance - it cannot provide details for individual properties. The information considers the predominant risk at the coast, although flooding and erosion processes are often linked, and data on erosion of foreshore features are, in general, not included.   The data describes the upper and lower estimates of erosion risk at a particular location, within which the actual location of the coastline is expected to lie.    The data does not estimate the absolute location of the future coastline.  Details of geologically complex areas, known as \"complex cliffs\" are in general not included within the dataset due to the inherent uncertainties associated with predicting the timing and extent of erosion at these locations.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk/Coastal Erosion.lyr\tLouise Pennington / Mark Pugh: Flood Risk Team.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-12-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:08:18.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18244\tNRW_DS116290\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.349Z\t752\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Legal Boundary\tFfiniau Cyfreithiol Coedwig CNC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323930\tThis layer shows the external legal boundaries of land within Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Forestry ownership. It does not show the internal deed boundaries.  It has been designed to:   - enable the mapping and Geographic Information Units to interrogate and maintain NRW land holdings records,   - provide land ownership data to  staff with GIS,   - produce updated polygons to maintain legal boundary information in the \u0027Forester\u0027 GIS system.-Dengys yr haen hon ffiniau allanol cyfreithiol tir sydd ym mherchnogaeth y Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC). Nid yw\u0027n dangos y ffiniau mewnol ar weithredoedd cyfreithiol. Ei hamcan yw: galluogi\u0027r Unedau Gwybodaeth Mapio Daearyddol i holi a chynnal cofnodion daliadau tir y Comisiwn Coedwigaeth, darparu data perchnogaeth tir i staff Ystadau gyda GIS, cynhyrchu polygonau diweddaru i gadw gwybodaeth am ffiniau cyfreithiol yn system GIS \u0027Forester\u0027 yr Ardal Goedwig.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThis dataset is a product of the Deeds Management System. Spatial information (polygons covering acquisitions and disposals of land areas, NRW accesses, NRW servitudes, legal boundaries) and deeds database information (attributes) are all managed within ARCGIS 10.2.   This file does not have the internal deed boundaries shown and does not have deed attributes attached. This file will be used widely throughout NRW, in various GIS projects where a picture of our land holdings is required.     The Deed Management System application is packaged as an ArcView 8 extension. It links ArcView 8 GIS and ORACLE (a relational database management system, or RDMS).\t\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\NRW Forest Legal Boundary.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Ian Medcalf Secondary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/503/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_FOREST_LEGAL_BOUND\t2026-01-12T14:08:53.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18245\tNRW_DS116291\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.414Z\t745\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Ownership\tPerchnogaeth Coedwig CNC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323931\tThis dataset illustrates the acquisitions that have created the NRW Forestry Estate as we know it today. This information is obtained from the Deed Management System which has been designed for the maintenance and reporting of the NRW land holdings.-Dengys y set ddata hon y caffaeliadau sydd wedi creu Ystâd Goedwig CNC fel yr ydym yn ei hadnabod heddiw.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tEstates, Forest Planning, Mapping and GeoData and ESRI (UK) Ltd have developed the Deed Management Extension. It has been designed to: enable the Mapping and Geographic Information Units to interrogate and maintain the NRW land holdings records, provide land ownership data to Estates staff with GIS, produce update polygons to maintain legal boundary information in NRWs \u0027Forester\u0027 GIS system.   The Deed Management System application is packaged as an ArcMap 9.2 extension. It links ArcMap GIS and ORACLE (a relational database management system, or RDMS). Spatial information (polygons covering acquisitions and disposals of land areas, NRW accesses, NRW servitudes, legal boundaries) and deeds database information (attributes) are all managed within ArcMap 9.2.    Since the creation of NRW in 2013, NRW have maintained the data in the Forester recreation module.\t\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\NRW Forest Ownership.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/329/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_FORESTRY_OWNERSHIP_GB\t2026-01-12T14:12:52.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18243\tNRW_DS116292\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.261Z\t828\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Roads and Planned Forest Roads\tFfyrdd Coedwig CNC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323932\tForest Roads is compiled from Forest road network data managed by Forestry Civil Engineering. The data relates to forest road Classification.\tRoads data is a product of data administered and maintained by Forestry Civil Engineering, exported from the NRW \u0027Forester\u0027 GIS application.   Planned forest roads relates to those which are planned.    Since the creation of NRW in 2013, NRW have maintained the data in the Forester recreation module.\t\tX:\\Transport\\Forest Roads\\NRW Forest Roads.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan   Secondary Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/424/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_FOREST_ROADS\t2026-01-12T14:10:38.344Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18238\tNRW_DS116294\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.110Z\t1254\tValid\tEditable\tTraditional Orchards\tPerllannau Traddodiadol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323934\tThis is a spatial dataset contain the location of Traditional Orchards sites around Wales. Traditional orchards are listed as a UK BAP priority habitats, which are those habitats that were identified as being the most threatened and requiring conservation action under the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP).   Traditional orchards are structurally and ecologically similar to wood-pasture and parkland, with open-grown trees set in herbaceous vegetation, but are generally distinguished from these priority habitat complexes by the following characteristics:   - the species composition of the trees, these being primarily in the family Rosaceae;  - the usually denser arrangement of the trees;  - the small scale of individual habitat patches;  - the wider dispersion and greater frequency of occurrence of habitat patches in the countryside.   Traditional orchards include plantings for nuts, principally hazel nuts, but also walnuts. Management of the trees is the other main feature distinguishing traditional orchards and wood-pasture and parkland. Trees in traditional orchards are, or were, grown for fruit and nut production, usually achieved through activities such as grafting and pruning; whereas timber has been the main product from trees in wood-pastures and parkland, mostly derived from pollarding or selective felling. Grazing or cutting of herbaceous vegetation are integral to orchard management, as they are in wood-pastures and parkland.-Set ddata ofodol yw hon sy\u0027n cynnwys safleoedd perllannau traddodiadol ar hyd a lled Cymru. Caiff perllannau traddodiadol eu rhestru fel cynefinoedd blaenoriaeth Cynllun Gweithredu Bioamrywiaeth y DU, sef y cynefinoedd hynny a nodwyd fel y rhai sy\u0027n wynebu\u0027r bygythiad mwyaf ac sydd angen gweithredu cadwraethol dan Gynllun Gweithredu Bioamrywiaeth y DU. Yn strwythurol ac yn ecolegol mae perllannau traddodiadol yn debyg i borfa goed a pharcdir, gyda choed wedi\u0027u tyfu\u0027n agored yn tyfu mewn llystyfiant llysieuol, ond gan amlaf maent yn wahanol i\u0027r ardaloedd cynefin blaenoriaeth hyn oherwydd y nodweddion canlynol: cyfansoddiad rhywogaethau\u0027r coed, sef yn t teulu Rosaceae yn bennaf; trefniant mwy trwchus y coed fel arfer; clytiau bychain o gynefin unigol; gwasgariad ehangach ac amlder clytiau cynefin yng nghefn gwlad. Mae perllannau traddodiadol yn cynnwys cnau, cnau cyll yn bennaf, a chnau Ffrengig hefyd.  Datganiad priodoli Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tTraditional orchards can easily be distinguished from other wooded habitats based on the preponderance of domestic fruit and nut species: apple, plum, pear, damson, cherry, walnut and cobnut. Only in a very few cases will there be a significant number of other tree species in a traditional orchard, unless the orchard is becoming woodland through neglect. An arbitrary distinction of requiring, say, 50% of trees to be domestic fruit or nut species in an orchard, is rarely likely to be invoked for distinguishing orchards from wood-pasture/parkland.    This dataset is made up of three files - Traditional Orchards Wales data from 2014 and a newer version of the data showing more up-to-date information about the location of Traditional Orchards.\tOrchards are hotspots for biodiversity in the countryside, supporting a wide range of wildlife and containing UK BAP priority habitats and species, as well as an array of Nationally Rare and Nationally Scarce species. The wildlife of orchard sites depends on the mosaic of habitats they encompass, including fruit trees, scrub, hedgerows, hedgerow trees, non-fruit trees within the orchard, the orchard floor habitats, fallen dead wood and associated features such as ponds and streams.\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116294 X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Traditional orchards.lyr   (no longer exists - has it been replaced by X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Priority Habitat Traditional Orchards.lyr )\tInternal Custodian: Claire Burrows, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  [Former Internal Custodians: Hillary Miller (VES) / Jim Latham, Terrestrial Ecosystem Services, NRW]          (I think this dataset is now part of the Section 7 OSPAR habitats, for some reason it has also been published on DMW in its own right and therefore has its own metadata entry. Also see NRW_DS122418).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-04-01\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/350/?ows_service\u003dwms\tnull\t2025-05-07T10:40:26.036Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18239\tNRW_DS116295\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.097Z\t961\tValid\tEditable\tWales Coast Path\tLlwybr Arfordir Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323935\tThis is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of the Wales Coast Path (WCP). The 870 mile long Wales Coast Path was officially opened in May 2012. It runs from the outskirts of Chester to Chepstow and, together with Offa\u0027s Dyke Path, forms a route of over 1000 miles, roughly around the edge of Wales.-Mae hon yn set ddata ofodol sy\u0027n rhoi manylion maint a lleoliad Llwybr Arfordirol Cymru. Agorwyd y Llwybr 870 milltir o hyd yn swyddogol ym mis Mai 2012. Mae\u0027n mynd o gyrion Caer i Gas-gwent ac, ynghyd â Llwybr Clawdd Offa, mae\u0027n ffurfio llwybr o dros 1000 o filltiroedd, o amgylch ymyl Cymru yn fras. Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) sy\u0027n arwain gwaith cydlynu, dosbarthu grant Llywodraeth Cymru, monitro a marchnata\u0027r Llwybr. Caiff ei reoli ar lawr gwlad gan 16 o awdurdodau lleol a dau Barc Cenedlaethol.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) lead on coordination, Welsh Government grant distribution, monitoring and marketing of the Path. It is managed on the ground by 16 local authorities and two National Parks.\tFor further information see:  http://www.walescoastpath.gov.uk/default.aspx?lang\u003den\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Wales Coast Path.lyr\tData Custodian: Quinten Grimley, Coastal Access Project Officer, Access Delivery Team, Sustainable Communities, NRW Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-07-31-2025-05-22\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/333/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WALES_COASTAL_PATH\t2025-11-06T15:59:14.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18241\tNRW_DS116296\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.114Z\t233\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Tree Cover 2006\tRhestr Goed Drefol 2006\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323936\tThis spatial dataset relates to Phase 1 (2006) and consists of 4 individual layers, being Points (individual trees), polygons (groups of trees), urban extents (the extent of the study areas) and land use classifications. The technical report (see Related Titles and lineage) shows what is covered in each Phase (page 25), and provides more detail on the methodology behind the different layers in the dataset in Appendix A2.1 (page 132).-Mae\u0027r set ddata ofodol hon yn gysylltiedig â Cham 1 (2006) ac yn cynnwys pedair haen unigol, sef Pwyntiau (coed unigol), polygonau (grwpiau o goed), categorau tir a meintiau ardaloedd trefol (maint yr ardaloedd astudio). Mae\u0027r adroddiad technegol yn dangos beth sy\u0027n cael ei gynnwys ym mhob Cam, ac maen rhoi mwy o fanylion ar y fethodoleg y tuôl i\u0027r gwahanol haenau yn y set ddata.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis data was captured using desktop analyses of aerial photography from 2006. Methodology is described in NRW (2016) Tree cover in Wales\u0027 towns and cities: understanding canopy cover to better plan and manage our urban trees - Full Report 2016.  Natural Resources Wales.\tNRW, 2016 Tree Cover in Wales’ Towns and Cities Understanding canopy cover to better plan and manage our urban trees, pp.146, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tX:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2006\\Urban Tree Point 2006.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2006\\Urban Tree Poly 2006.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2006\\Urban Trees Land Categories 2006.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\Urban Extent.lyr\tCustodian:  John Briggs, Landscape Architect,   PPG Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team  Used to be Dafydd Fryer, Urban Forest Architect, Knowledge, Strategy and Planning, NRW. No longer works for NRW  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-01-13\tpublication\thttps://mapdata.llyw.cymru/capabilities/layergroup/877/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://mapdata.llyw.cymru/layergroups/inspire-nrw:UrbanTreeCover2006\t2025-02-04T10:17:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18240\tNRW_DS116297\t2024-05-28T12:43:55.109Z\t787\tValid\tEditable\tDee Water Protection Zone\tParth Diogelu Dŵr Dyfrdwy\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323937\tThis dataset shows the location of the Dee Water Protection  Zone (DWPZ). A Water Protection Zone is an area where certain activities (storing or using controlled substances) are banned or restricted to reduce the risk of polluting drinking water. Under the Water Protection Zone (River Dee Catchment) (Procedural and Other Provisions) Regulations 1999, you need consent (permission) to carry out a controlled activity in the DWPZ. The aim is to prevent water pollution arising from activities that cannot be controlled using other permits. This map can be used to whether your site is within the DWPZ.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn dangos lleoliad Parth Diogelu Dŵr Dyfrdwy. Ardal yw Parth Diogelu Dŵr lle y caiff gweithgareddau penodol (storio neu ddefnyddio sylweddau a reolir) eu gwahardd neu eu cyfyngu er mwyn lleihau\u0027r perygl o lygru dŵr yfed. Yn ôl Rheoliadau Parth Diogelu Dŵr (Dalgylch Afon Dyfrdwy) (Darpariaethau Gweithdrefnol a Darpariaethau Eraill) 1999, rydych angen cydsyniad (caniatâd) i gynnal gweithgaredd a reolir ym Mharth Diogelu Dŵr Dyfrdwy. Y nod yw atal llygredd dŵr sy\u0027n deillio o weithgareddau na ellir eu rheoli wrth ddefnyddio trwyddedau eraill. Gellir defnyddio\u0027r map hwn i weld a yw eich safle oddi mewn i Barth Diogelu Dŵr Dyfrdwy ai peidio.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe Water Protection Zone (River Dee Catchment) Designation Order 1999 and accompanying regulations came into force on 21 June 1999 and are regulated by Natural Resource Wales and the Environment Agency.\tFurther information can be found https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/consent-for-a-controlled-activity-within-the-river-dee-water-protection-zone\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Dee water Protection Zone.lyr\tAngela Roberts-Custodian    *Consents relating to the DWPZ are Public Register and currently stored in the DMS and Carole Williams Environment Assistant is looking after these*\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-05-01\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/409/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_DEE_PZ\t2025-11-10T16:45:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18237\tNRW_DS116298\t2024-05-28T12:43:54.927Z\t844\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Operational Areas\tArdaloedd Gweithredol Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323938\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Operational Areas.    This dataset shows the six main operational areas:   - North East Wales  - North West Wales  - Mid Wales  - South East Wales  - South West Wales  - South Wales Central    These are also known as the same areas used for Area Statements.    It is the post OD (Organisational Design) catchment boundaries for the six NRW Operational Areas (or places).-Set ddata ofodol yw hon sy\u0027n cynnwys y polygonau helaethrwydd ar gyfer Ardaloedd Gweithredol Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru.  Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn dangos y saith prif faes gweithredu: - Gogledd-ddwyrain Cymru - Gogledd-orllewin Cymru - Canolbarth Cymru - De-ddwyrain Cymru - Canol De Cymru - Canol De Cymru  - Morol  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThis dataset can be used in conjunction with the NRW Team Boundaries (NRW_DS116299). Was provided as part of Day 1 readiness in preparation for OD go-live date.  In MyMap (internal mapping system) this is layer - Operational Layers/Resource Management Terrestrial/NRW Operational Areas.   This data will be used to ensure enquiries, incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Operational Areas.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Gary Ecclestone, Open Access GIS Officer, GIS and Spatial Information Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2017-04-20\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/341/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_AREAS\t2025-12-19T11:37:44.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18236\tNRW_DS116299\t2024-05-28T12:43:54.738Z\t314\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Team Boundaries (Archived 2019)\tFfiniau Tîm Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136323939\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales team boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for operational managed teams.    This dataset defines the operational team boundaries and the 18 team locations within those areas.    Information warning: This dataset has now been archived as it has been superseded by \u0027Environment Teams, post Organisational Design, July 2019\u0027 NRW_DS123441.\tThis dataset can be used in conjunction with the NRW Operational Areas. (NRW_DS116298)\t\tThis layerfile has been removed from the X: drive. The following dataset supersedes the Team Boundaries layer X:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Environment Teams.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Gary Ecclestone.       This layerfile has been removed from the X: drive. The metadata for Team Boundaries can be archived. The following dataset supersedes the Team Boundaries layer X:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Environment Teams.lyr (metadata 123441)  For this reason this record has been removed from public view.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2019-05-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-05-22T14:23:04.681Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18292\tNRW_DS116300\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.536Z\t734\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Conservation Zones (MCZ)\tParthau Cadwraeth Morol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333030\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Marine Conservation Zones (MCZs) former Marine Nature Reserves in Wales.   MCZs are a way of conserving marine habitats and wildlife and other features, of special importance, along the shore or on the seabed. The Marine and Coastal Access Act 2009 allows for the creation of MCZs. MCZs protect a range of nationally important marine wildlife, habitats, geology and geomorphology, and can be designated anywhere in English and Welsh territorial and UK offshore water.-Yn 2015 newidiwyd dynodiad y Warchodfa Natur Forol (GNF) yn Barth Cadwraeth Morol (PCM). Mae\u0027r set ddata ofodol hon yn cynnwys ffiniau digidol Parthau Cadwraeth Morol, Gwarchodfeydd Natur Morol yn flaenorol, yng Nghymru. Mae\u0027r Parthau Cadwraeth Morol a\u0027r Gwarchodfeydd Natur Morol yn fodd o warchod cynefinoedd morol a nodweddion eraill, sydd o bwysigrwydd arbennig i fywyd gwyllt ar hyd yr arfordir neu ar wely\u0027r môr. Dynodwyd Skomer, y Warchodfa Natur Forol gyntaf a\u0027r unig un yng Nghymru, ym 1990. Mae wedi\u0027i lleoli o gwmpas Ynys Skomer a Phenrhyn Marloes yn ne orllewin Cymru. Mae Deddf Bywyd Gwyllt a Chefn Gwlad 1981 yn rhoi\u0027r hawl i CNC wneud cais i Brif Weinidog Cymru yn gofyn i Gynulliad Cenedlaethol Cymru (CCC) ddynodi GNF. Gall GNF gael ei rheoli gan is-ddeddfau, wedi\u0027u creu drwy drafodaethau a chytundebau rhwng y sefydliadau perthnasol, a\u0027u cymeradwyo gan Lywodraeth Cynulliad Cymru. Mae GNF yn gwneud cyfraniad gwerthfawr i amddiffyn ein moroedd trwy greu ymwybyddiaeth o ba mor fregus a gwerthfawr yw\u0027r dreftadaeth forol naturiol.  Datganiad priodoli   Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThe boundary was first digitised from the designation paper map at 1:10,000 scale. In 2010 it was digitised to OS MasterMap (CCW update 2008) using the description of the boundary from the Byelaws document.   There is no digital data for Highest Astronomical Tides (HAT), which is the landward boundary for a great length, we have adopted a 1.5 m buffer above Mean High Water Mark (MHWM). The Latitude and Longitude positions plotted to WGS84 projection and then the positions converted to British National Grid.   Where the description quotes following MLWOT the boundary has been captured to Mean Low Water Mark (MLWM) as shown on OS MasterMap. Therefore for the definitive boundary please refer to the description in the Byelaws.   With the coming of GIS and its use in government departments and commercial companies mobilising the data in this way, advances the protection of these sites and the efficiency of decision making.\tPlease refer to Countryside Acts 1981, for reasons for capture of original designation of the MNRs.   In Wales the MNRs that existed have been superseded by MCZs.  Please refer to The Marine and Coastal Access Act 2009 allows for the creation of MCZs.\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\MCZ Marine Conservation Zones.lyr\tKirsten Ramsay, Team Leader Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Advice, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/389/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_MNR\t2025-11-13T12:33:47.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18286\tNRW_DS116301\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.543Z\t422\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Tree Cover 2009\tGorchudd Coed Trefol 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333031\tThis spatial dataset relates to Phase 2 (2009) and consists of 4 individual layers, being Points (individual trees), polygons (groups of trees), urban extents (the extent of the study areas) and land use classifications. The technical report (see Related Titles and lineage) shows what is covered in each Phase (page 25), and provides more detail on the methodology behind the different layers in the dataset in Appendix A2.1 (page 132).-Mae’r set ddata ofodol hon yn gysylltiedig â Cham 2 (2009) Gorchudd Coed Trefol ac yn cynnwys 4 lefel unigol, sef Pwyntiau (coed unigol), polygonau (grwpiau o goed), categorïau tir a meintiau ardaloedd trefol (maint yr ardaloedd astudio). Mae’r adroddiad technegol yn dangos beth sy’n cael ei gynnwys ym mhob Cam, ac mae’n rhoi mwy o fanylion ar y fethodoleg y tu ôl i’r gwahanol haenau yn y set ddata.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis data was captured using desktop analyses of aerial photography 2009.  Methodology is described in NRW (2016) Tree cover in Wales\u0027 towns and cities: understanding canopy cover to better plan and manage our urban trees - Full Report 2016.  Natural Resources Wales.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Wales Urban Canopy Cover\\2009\\Urban Trees Point 2009 .lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2009\\Urban Trees Poly 2009.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2009\\Urban Trees Land Categories 2009.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\Urban Extent.lyr\tCustodian:  John Briggs, Landscape Architect, PPG Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team Used to be Dafydd Fryer, Urban Forest Architect, Knowledge, Strategy and Planning, NRW. No longer works for NRW    Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-01-13\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3606/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_urban_tree_cover_2009\t2025-11-19T10:50:32.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18283\tNRW_DS116303\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.405Z\t2451\tValid\tEditable\tNational River Flow Archive (NRFA)\tArchif Genedlaethol Llif Afon\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333033\tThe National River Flow Archive (NRFA) is the UK\u0027s focal point for river flow data and is the primary archive of daily and peak river flows for the United Kingdom. The archive incorporates daily, monthly and flood peak data from over 1500 gauging stations across the UK.   The NRFA holds a wide range of hydrological information to assist in the understanding and interpretation of measured river flows. In addition to time series of gauged river flow, the data centre maintains hydrometric information relating to the gauging stations and the catchments they command and data, quantifying other parts of the hydrological cycle.\tThe NRFA system is used as a way of collating, quality controlling, and archiving hydrometric data from gauging station networks across the UK including the extensive networks operated by the Environment Agency (England), Natural Resources Wales (NRW), the Scottish Environment Protection Agency (SEPA) and the Rivers Agency (Northern Ireland).   NRW owned data is collated internally and sent to Centre for Hydrology and Ecology (CEH) for archive into NFRA.     NRFA data goes through a validation process before it is published and therefore data many not be available for the previous or current year. To get the most up to date version of the Welsh data, not currently available on the NFRA website, customers should contact datadistribution@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\t\tWelsh NRW owned data is currently held in WISKI - and sent to CEH who collate this and put in NRFA  https://nrfa.ceh.ac.uk/data/search\tInternal Custodian of the NRW Welsh Data:  Alison Hanson, Hydrometry and Telemetry.  CEH hold the custodianship over the archived UK data that they publish on the agencies behalf.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-10-31\tpublication\tnull\thttps://nrfa.ceh.ac.uk/data/search\t2025-05-15T11:22:29.984Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18284\tNRW_DS116304\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.478Z\t391\tValid\tEditable\tEnforcement Action against Corporate Entities\tCamau Gorfodi yn erbyn Endidau Corrfforeathol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333034\tConvictions (excluding those under appeal and, for avoidance of doubt, not including acquittals) and formal cautions data, filtered to limit information to corporate entities only. Data available commences from 1/1/2000. Updates available on a quarterly basis.\tData is extracted from information stored in the National Enforcement Database (NEDS) from 01/01/2001 to 31/12/2015 and from 01/01/2016 to present in the Collins Database.\t\tData is extracted from information stored in the National Enforcement Database (NEDS) from 01/01/2001 to 31/12/2015 and from 01/01/2016 to present in the Collins Database.\tCustodian: Helen White Governance / Stuart Kay Master Reference data\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2025-11-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-20T11:54:09.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18293\tNRW_DS116305\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.536Z\t889\tValid\tEditable\tWaste Management Licence Current Exemptions\tEsemptiadau Presennol Trwyddedau Rheoli Gwastraff\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333035\tCurrent Waste Management Licence Exemption data as required by Schedule 3 of the Waste Management Licensing (WML) Regulations 1994 (as amended) lists the activities which are normally exempt from WML in Wales. Circular 26/94 in Wales provides additional information.-Mae data cyfredol Eithriad i Drwydded Rheoli Gwastraff fel sy\u0027n ofynnol gan Atodlen 3 o Reoliadau Trwyddedu Rheoli Gwastraff (WML) 1994 (diwygiwyd) yn rhestru\u0027r gweithgareddau sydd fel arfer wedi eu hesgusodi o WML yng Nghymru. Mae Cylchlythyr 26/94 yng Nghymru yn darparu gwybodaeth ychwanegol. Mae eithriadau nodweddiadol yn cynnwys:  -Glanhau neu orchuddio deunydd pacio gwastraff, cynhwysyddion a thecstilau -Llosgi gwastraff fel tanwydd mewn offer sydd wedi ei eithrio -Llosgi gwastraff fel tanwydd mewn injan -Storio triniaeth wastraff yn ddiogel ar gyfer gwastraff i adfer deunyddiau -Llosgi gwastraff mewn llosgydd wedi ei eithrio mewn man cynhyrchu -Dyddodi archwiliad mwynol -Storio gwastraff Gwastraff Offer Trydanol i\u0027w adfer mewn man arall  Efallai bod rhai gweithgareddau wedi eu heithrio o\u0027r WML ble caiff y gweithgaredd ei reoli o dan drefnau eraill.\tData is extracted from the Customer Relationship Management database. Typical exemptions include:   - Cleaning or coating of waste packaging, containers and textiles  - Burning waste as a fuel in an exempt appliance  - Burning waste as a fuel in an engine  - Secure storage of waste treatment of waste for recovery of materials   - Burning of waste in an exempt incinerator at place of production   - Depositing of mineral exploration waste storing Waste Electrical Equipment for recovery elsewhere   Some activities may be excluded from WML where the activity is controlled under other regimes\t\tData extracted from CRM and is then stored in Sharefile\tCustodian: Helen Florek Oughton - Customer Care Centre Stephen Badman\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2025-11-17\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2635\t2025-11-17T17:26:44.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18289\tNRW_DS116306\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.533Z\t354\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHistorical Dangerous Substances List I and List II\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333036\tData was collated annually until the end of 2011 for the Dangerous Substances Directive (76/464/EEC) on the discharge of dangerous substances to surface waters. The Dangerous Substances Directive was repealed in 2012 following the introduction of the Water Framework Directive (2000/60/EC), which includes monitoring and assessment of a wide range of chemicals in surface waters.  List I covers substances that are particularly toxic or persistent, or may tend to accumulate in the environment. List II covers substances whose effects are less serious. List I includes 20 substances and List II has 67 substances. Every listed dangerous substance has a concentration limit known as an Environmental Quality Standard (EQS). The EQS is usually an upper concentration, with the exception of pH, which has both upper and lower limits. It is set for the receiving watercourse and not the discharge itself. The dangerous substance is not believed to be detrimental to aquatic life at any concentration below its EQS limit. The EQS varies for each substance and can be different for fresh, estuarine or coastal waters.\tData exists from 1995 until 2011 when the Dangerous Substances Directive was repealed. The Historical Dangerous Substances is supplied in a database databases and gives information for both the List I and II substances.\t\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116306\tJulie Boswell / Jane Roberts: Environmental reporting team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-12-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18288\tNRW_DS116307\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.563Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDangerous Substances List II/ Annex 10 Substances under WFD\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333037\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18294\tNRW_DS116308\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.483Z\t1217\tValid\tEditable\tEnvironmental Permitting Regulations - Industrial Sites\tRheoliadau Trwyddedu Amgylcheddol - Safleodd Diwydiannol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333038\tThe Environmental Permitting Regulations, amongst other things, implement the IPPC (Integrated Pollution Prevention and Control) Directive (EC/61/96) in England and Wales.  Facilities covered by this legislation are known as Installations and generally have significant releases to air, land or water, or carry out certain, larger scale, waste management activities.-Rheoliadau Trwyddedu Amgylcheddol, ymysg pethau eraill, sy\u0027n gweithredu Cyfarwyddeb Atal a Rheoli Llygredd mewn ffordd Integredig (EC/61/96) yng Nghymru a Lloegr. Caiff cyfleusterau dan y ddeddfwriaeth hon eu hadnabod fel ‘gweithfeydd’ ac, yn gyffredinol, maent yn gollwng yn sylweddol i aer, tir neu ddŵr, neu\u0027n cynnal gweithgareddau rheoli gwastraff penodol ar raddfa fwy. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys pob safle Diwydiant Proses dan reolaeth y Rheoliadau Trwyddedu Amgylcheddol a rhai gweithgareddau gwastraff mwy. Caiff cyfleusterau gwastraff llai eraill eu hadnabod fel \u0027Gweithrediadau Gwastraff\u0027 (a oedd yn cael eu hadnabod yn flaenorol fel Trwyddedau Rheoli Gwastraff) ac maent yn gynwysedig mewn set ddata ar wahân.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThis dataset covers all Process Industry sites within the EPR regime and some larger waste activities. Other smaller waste facilities are known as \u0027Waste Operations\u0027 (formerly known as Waste Management Licences) and are covered in a separate dataset.    Other related datasets that are available are:  Environmental Permitting Regulations – Waste Sites Authorised Treatment Facilities (End of Life Vehicles) Waste Electrical, Electronic Equipment (WEEE)\t\tPermitting and Licencing  System (PALS)\tData Custodian: Henry Forrest - Permitting Centre Wales, Cardiff\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3494/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_install_permits\t2026-01-12T13:57:35.033Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18280\tNRW_DS116309\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.202Z\t853\tValid\tEditable\tOSPAR River Monitoring Programme\tRhaglen Monitro Afonydd OSPAR\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333039\tThe Convention for the Protection of the Marine Environment of the North-East Atlantic know as the OSPAR Convention 1998 (Oslo Convention 1972 and Paris Convention 1974), is an agreement signed by a number of European countries (including the United Kingdom) to protect the quality of the North East Atlantic.   The objective of the OSPAR Convention is to take all possible steps to prevent and eliminate pollution entering the sea. The OSPAR Convention provides standard methods for estimating the inputs of selected pollutants to the sea by using fixed sampling schedule and a standard input calculation.     In Wales, all principal rivers are sampled monthly (12 times a year) just upstream of their tidal limits. For those rivers carrying the heaviest contaminant loads the sampling frequency maybe increased beyond the minimum of 12. Major trade effluents and sewage effluents to estuaries or coastal waters are also sampled monthly to assess direct discharges to marine waters. Flow values of discharges direct to estuary or sea are sometimes provided by the operators themselves. This is usually part of statutory monitoring arrangements. The aim of these programmes is to assess the level of contamination entering the sea from Wales (the \u0027load\u0027) and to chart the progress in the reduction of this load.   This dataset contains the results and data obtained from these programmes.-Cytundeb a arwyddwyd gan nifer o wledydd Ewropeaidd (gan gynnwys y Deyrnas Unedig) yw Cynhadledd Gwarchod Amgylchedd Forwrol Gogledd-Ddwyrain yr Iwerydd a elwir yn Gynhadledd OSPAR 1998 (\"Oslo Convention 1972 \u0026amp; Paris Convention 1974\") er mwyn gwarchod ansawdd Gogledd-Ddwyrain yr Iwerydd. Amcan y Gynhadledd OSPAR yw cymryd pob cam posib i atal a dileu llygredd rhag mynd i mewn i\u0027r môr. Mae\u0027r Gynhadledd OSPAR yn darparu dulliau safonol o amcangyfrif mewnbwn llygryddion penodol i\u0027r môr gan ddefnyddio amserlen samplu benodol a chyfrifiad mewnbwn safonol. Mae\u0027r prif afonydd i gyd yn cael eu samplu\u0027n fisol (12 gwaith y flwyddyn) i fyny\u0027r afon o\u0027u cyfyngiadau llanw. Ar gyfer yr afonydd hynny sy\u0027n cario\u0027r llwythau difwyno trymaf gall y samplu gynyddu o\u0027r isafswm o 12. Mae\u0027r prif elifiannau masnach a\u0027r elifiannau carthffosiaeth i aberoedd neu ddyfroedd arfordirol hefyd yn cael eu samplu yn fisol i asesu dadlwythiadau uniongyrchol i ddyfroedd morwrol. Pwrpas y rhaglenni hyn yw asesu lefel y difwyno sy\u0027n y môr (y \u0027llwyth\u0027) ac i ddilyn gwelliant yn lleihad y llwyth hwn.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.  Darllenwch y drwydded i ddeall amodau defnyddio\u0027r data.\tThe substances controlled under OSPAR are: Mercury, Cadmium, Copper, Zinc, Lead, polychlorinated biphenyls (PCB), gamma-HCH (Hexachlorocyclohexane), Orthophosphate, Phosphorous (total), Nitrate, Nitrogen (total) and suspended particulate material.   The load of contamination to the sea is measured at over 300 sites around the coast of England and Wales. These sites have been grouped into thirty two coastal zones.   OSPAR data is updated annually approximately 6 months through the year for the previous year. Data held ranges from 1998 to the present. It’s high/low based on twelve annual samples.   This product provides a pair of annual estimates for each sampling point, known as high load, and low load. The difference between the high and low load estimates is owing to the way in which the samples with results at the \u0027limit of detection\u0027 are treated. The limit of detection is the lowest concentration of a substance that can be reliably measured - any real concentration lower than this level, including zero, is reported as being present at \u0027less than\u0027 the limit of detection concentration. Where the substance has been analysed for but the concentration is below the limit of detection, a calculation can be made assuming that it is not present at all (a low load estimate). Alternatively, a calculation can be made by assuming that the substance is present exactly at the limit of detection (a high load estimate). The low load calculation gives an optimistic estimate of the real load, whereas the high load calculation gives a pessimistic estimate. The real load discharged will be somewhere between these two figures.\t\tData is stored in WISKI Database\tCustodian: Claire Popp Environmental Reporting Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-11-01\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2629\t2025-12-19T11:58:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18285\tNRW_DS116310\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.550Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRadioactive Substances Register\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333130\t\t\t\t\tThis data is no longer available for external users; the old EA legacy data is outdated and product is discontinued; no new data is produced due to re-use concerns by Holly Noble’s team.\t\t\t2021-07-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:52:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18281\tNRW_DS116311\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.075Z\t474\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDischarges of Consented Red List Substances\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333131\tThe UK has a list (known as the Red List) of 23 of the most dangerous substances which were selected for priority control under the Integrated Pollution Control legislation (subsequently superseded by the Pollution Prevention and Control and then Environmental Permitting Regulations). This list of substances includes EC List I substances defined under the Dangerous Substances Directive, as well as certain substances listed on EC List 2. There are statutory Environmental Quality Standards (EQSs) in place for their discharge into surface waters. Statutory EQSs for a further 25 substances came into force on 1 April 1998. These deal with substances produced by manufacturing industry, as well as a number of pesticides applied to crops. Dangerous Substances are toxic, do not or are very slow to degrade in water, and are likely to accumulate in living organisms.\tDischarges of Consented Priority Dangerous Substances to water contains: - REDLIST_PERMIT_HOLDERS_FULL:the consent details of companies with consents to discharge priority Dangerous Substances including the type of discharge and where it is discharged; - TBL_FINAL_CONSENTED_REDLIST_DETS: the list of priority Dangerous Substances and the limits consented to be discharged (it does not show the amount of substances actually discharged); - TBL_AREAS:information on the areas associated with consents.   This dataset is no longer a stand alone dataset and now combined and available as part of the dataset: NRW_DS116329 Consented Discharges to Controlled Waters with Conditions\t\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116311\tSimon Bonwick, Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18290\tNRW_DS116312\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.546Z\t775\tValid\tEditable\tHazardous Waste Registrations with Standard Industrial Classification (SIC) Code\tCofrestru Gwastraff Peryglus gyda Chod Dosbarthiad Safonol y Diwydiant\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333132\tThe Hazardous Waste Regulations 2005 require that anyone who produces or holds hazardous waste at any premises in Wales must register the premises with Natural Resources Wales each year, unless the total quantity of hazardous waste is less than 500 kg each year.    There is an online public register that allows searches to be run on registrations which shows the business name, registration reference, address,  postcode, registration start date and registration end date. The dataset contains information about live registrations.-Mae Rheoliadau Gwastraff Peryglus 2005 yn gofyn bod unrhyw un sy\u0027n cynhyrchu neu\u0027n cadw gwastraff peryglus ar unrhyw safle yng Nghymru\u0027n cofrestru\u0027r safle gyda Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru bob blwyddyn, os nad yw cyfanswm y gwastraff peryglus yn llai na 500kg bob blwyddyn. Mae \u0027na gofrestr gyhoeddus ar-lein ble gellir chwilio\u0027r cofrestriadau sy\u0027n dangos enw\u0027r busnes, cyfeirnod y cofrestriad, cyfeiriad, cod post, dyddiad cychwyn y cofrestriad a\u0027r dyddiad y daw\u0027r cofrestriad i ben. Mae\u0027r set ddata\u0027n cynnwys cofrestriadau byw.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThis dataset includes the contact details for each registrant (their primary Standard Industrial Classification (SIC) code identifying business type and number of employees (by category)). Customers can register online, by telephone or on a paper application form.    Data is held in the NRW Customer Relationship Management Database.      SIC (Standard Industrial Classification) codes should be treated with caution. A SIC code is provided by the registrant and indicates its principal area of business. This has not been validated by NRW, and the registrant may also operate in a number of other activities.\thttps://nrwregulatory.naturalresources.wales/HazWaste/PublicRegister/Search\tData is held in the NRW Customer Relationship Management Database.\tCustodian: Stephen Badman Customer Contact Centre\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-11-17\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2622\t2025-11-17T17:52:34.088Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18287\tNRW_DS116313\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.562Z\t651\tUnassessed\tEditable\tReferrals of Red List Discharges to Sewers (Corporate Entities)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333133\tThe Referrals of Red List Discharges to Sewers dataset (extracted from IPCIS) records those companies (and potentially individuals) who have applied to water undertakers for permission to discharge a Red List substance into sewers. The Water Industry Act 1991 (as amended) section 120 (Applications for the discharge of special category effluent) directs sewerage undertakers who have received a notice containing an application for consent to discharge trade effluent from a trade premise into a public sewer (section 119) to refer to the following questions: - whether the discharges to which the notice relates should be prohibited; and - whether, if they are not prohibited, any requirements should be imposed as to the conditions on which they are made. It is this information, stored within IPCIS, that makes up the Referrals of Red List Discharges to Sewers dataset.\tThis dataset used to be known as the Water Industry Act Referrals dataset. The Red List is a list of 23 of the most dangerous substances which were selected for priority control under the Integrated Pollution Control legislation (subsequently superseded by the Pollution Prevention and Control and then Environmental Permitting Regulations). This list of substances includes EC List I substances defined under the Dangerous Substances Directive, as well as certain substances listed on EC List 2.  Welsh Government revoked the requirement for Dwr Cymru to provide annual reports to NRW of any trade effluent consents issued for discharges into sewers, therefore this dataset will not be updated after December 2015. Customers should now go to Dwr Cymru to request this data\t\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116313\tChris Hall (SUW Advisor - Water quality)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-12-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18295\tNRW_DS116314\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.565Z\t899\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGroundwater Level Measurements\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333134\tThis dataset comprises groundwater level time series data taken at borehole monitoring stations located across Wales. Discrete station information is stored for each site including identifier, spatial reference, parameter type and time series type. This dataset contains sites for operational and closed monitoring stations.  Data is collected from NRW borehole monitoring stations that are collated by Area staff normally by either downloading the station \u0027Logger Data\u0027 or manually \u0027Dipping\u0027 to determine borehole water level.\tThis dataset is extract from Natural Resources Wales\u0027 central data store, WISKI 7.\tNRW\u0027s does not supply the whole dataset, requests are dealt with ad hoc only. Therefore we ask customers for the geographical extent they require and include this in the licence as standard.\t\tCustodian: Liam Perrott       This is a large WISKI database dataset; requests are dealt with ad hoc only with usually high extraction costs regarding staff resources. We do not routinely supply this dataset as a whole. Please therefore always ask the customer for the  geographic extent they require and include this in the licence.  Due to national security reason, you must send any raw data extracts you receive from the data custodian password protected. (Please ask custodian or Simon Bonwick/Andrew Jeffery for help with this if required). Please send password and data in a separate email. Otherwise the NGRs must be abbreviated to 4 digits.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18278\tNRW_DS116315\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.939Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSummary Shellfish Directive Assessments\tCrynodeb o Asesiadau Cyfarwyddeb Pysgod Cregyn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333135\tShellfish waters are coastal and brackish waters used for commercial shell fishing. These areas have been designated under the EC Shellfish Waters Directive.  This dataset contains sample results taken within the Designated Shellfish Waters that are assessed for compliance under the standards as defined under Directive (79/923/EEC).  These sites are sampled for water quality and for faecal coliform amongst others and contain both the raw sample results as well as the summary compliance data.  Samples are taken monthly with summary data collated for compliance annually and reported to Defra and the Welsh Government.  Since these data are reported annually the dataset is available for each complete year reported. It is of note that shellfisheries no longer used on a commercial basis are still maintained.\tSamples are taken within designated shellfish waters and assessed for compliance under Directive (79/923/EEC). The sample results are available and compliance summarised.\t\t[A]  Data available in Biosys [B]  Packaged published version available on Sharefile.\tThere is a small risk that this data can be considered commercially confidential but is difficult to assess. However, these data would be made available under an EIR request and as such these would inevitably lead to disclosure where the public interest test has been applied.  For this reason we consider it unnecessary that this slight risk should prevent full access.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-10-25\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T10:36:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18279\tNRW_DS116316\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.947Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSeagrass Taxa and Abundance\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333136\tInformation regarding the presence, and percentage cover, of seagrass species at specific marine monitoring points held within the legacy Environment Agency Wales BIOSYS database. This is now NRW\u0027s store for legacy freshwater EAW data. EAW used this as the main database for storing, manipulating and reporting data from freshwater and marine biological surveys at any taxonomic level. These data represent ground-truthing monitoring for the ecological assessment of seagrasses within transitional and coastal waters of Wales. Seagrass data on BIOSYS are updated as new monitoring data are made available. Monitoring for seagrass occurs June to September with records being updated after the sampling season. The extracted data is a subset of the full dataset and only includes data managed and / or owned by Natural Resources Wales.\tThe presence and percentage cover of seagrass species are monitored at specific monitoring points between June and September. Data is stored in Biological Systems (BIOSYS) - extracted data only includes data managed and / or owned by the Natural Resources Wales.\t\t[A]  Data extracted from Biosys [B] X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seagrass Areas [C] X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seagrass Quadrats\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-06-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T10:29:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18276\tNRW_DS116317\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.866Z\t522\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Rocky Shore Macroalgal Species\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333137\tInformation regarding the presence of macroalgal species on rocky shores monitored for the Water Framework Directive ecological assessment of transitional and coastal waters of Wales. Monitoring follows the Reduced Species List method outlined in the UK Technical Advisory Group method statement. Data are from intertidal rocky shores. The extracted data is a subset of the full dataset and only includes data collected and owned by the Natural Resources Wales.\tThe shore is searched for a set time period and the presence of identified algae, from the WFD Reduced Species List, recorded. Rocky Shore Macroalgal Species data were updated as new monitoring data were made available. Monitoring occurred June to September with records being updated after the sampling season. Data collected by contractor to provide data required to carry out WFD classification of macroalgae on intertidal rocky shores.\t\tBioSys WISKI KiEco\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-06-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18291\tNRW_DS116318\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.549Z\t606\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Benthic Invertebrate Species (extracted from BIOSYS)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333138\tInformation regarding the presence, and abundance, of benthic invertebrate species at specific marine monitoring points held within the Biological Systems (BIOSYS). BIOSYS is a former Environment Agency Wales\u0027 (EAW)) database. This welsh extract of the data is now stored and managed by Natural Resources Wales. BIOSYS was EAW main database for storing, manipulating and reporting data from freshwater and marine biological surveys at any taxonomic level, and Water Framework Directive marine benthic invertebrate database. Data are laboratory assessed and quality assured following the National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control (NMBAQC) scheme. National databases are continually being updated as a result of on-going benthic invertebrate monitoring programmes. The extracted data is a subset of the full dataset and only includes data collected/owned/managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tPresence and abundance of benthic invertebrate species are collected and held in Biological Systems (BIOSYS). Data within the field was collected using Day Grab (0.1 m2), 3x0.01m2 cores and Core (11.28cm diam.).  Data are laboratory assessed and quality assured following the National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control (NMBAQC) scheme. These data only contain data owned and managed by Natural Resources Wales.\t\t[A]  Extracted from BIOSYS Packaged data available: Y:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116318\tBianca Bacchi / Richard West - Senior Env Mon Officer, Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-01-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:33.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18282\tNRW_DS116319\t2024-05-28T12:44:34.126Z\t381\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Species\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333139\tInformation regarding the presence, and percentage cover, of saltmarsh angiosperms (flowering plants) at specific marine monitoring points held within the former Environment Agency Wales\u0027 welsh abstract of the BIOSYS database (our main database for storing, manipulating and reporting data from freshwater and marine biological surveys at any taxonomic level). This data is now managed and held by Natural Resources Wales. These data represent ground-truthing monitoring for the ecological assessment of saltmarsh within transitional and coastal waters of Wales. Welsh saltmarsh data originally held on BIOSYS are now updated as new monitoring data are made available through Natural Resources Wales. Monitoring for saltmarsh occurs June to September with records being updated after the sampling season. The extracted data is a subset of the full dataset and only includes data collected/owned by Natural Resources Wales.\tThe presence and percentage cover of saltmarsh angiosperms are monitored at specific monitoring points between June and September. Data is stored in Biological Systems (BIOSYS) - extracted data only includes data collected/owned by the Natural Resources Wales.\t\t[A]  Extracted from BIOSYS\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-06-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:02.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18277\tNRW_DS116320\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.964Z\t622\tValid\tEditable\tLarge Raised Reservoirs (archived data)\tCyforgronfeydd Dŵr Mawr\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333230\tThis dataset has been discontinued. Please see the datasets which have replaced it:  - Large Raised Reservoir register (Locations and undertakers) NRW_DS125215 and;   - Large Raised Reservoir (Registered information) NRW_DS125216  This dataset contains details of Large Raised Reservoirs. Natural Resource Wales (NRW) collects and maintains data on all reservoirs designed or capable of holding more than 25,000 cubic metres of water above the natural level of any part of the land adjoining them defined as \u0027large raised reservoirs\u0027 under the Reservoirs Act 1975. Two types of reservoir are maintained within the register:   - Impounding (Dammed) or; - Non-Impounding (Pumped/unimpeded)\tThis dataset is an extract from NRW’s Cronfeydd/Reservoir Database. It contains information on the:   - Reservoir Name  - Location  - Grid Reference  - Undertaker Name  - Undertaker Address   \u0027Undertaker\u0027 is the legal term used to identify who manages and controls the use of the reservoir, and is responsible for maintaining compliance with the law. The undertaker is the person, people or company that uses a reservoir for a particular purpose. If there is no use, the owners or lessees are the undertakers. The operator may be different from the owner. If several people or organisations manage and control the reservoir, there can be more than one undertaker.\tThe full register contains details on the type, physical characteristics, inspection details and information on the reservoir undertaker and Panel Engineer overseeing their operation and maintenance. Summaries of certificates and reports are also collated and held for each reservoir but are not part of the electronic database. These details can be requested from Data Distribution.\tData extracted from Cronfeydd Cymru database  Y:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116320\tCustodian: Matthew O\u0027Brien, Reservoir Safety Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2022-05-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T11:55:30.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18274\tNRW_DS116321\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.710Z\t1390\tValid\tEditable\tLicenced Water Abstractions\tTyniadau Dwr\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333231\tThe water abstractions dataset details all sites in Wales covered under the Water Act 2003 where all abstractions of 20 cubic metres or more require an abstraction licence-Mae’r set ddata tynnu dŵr yn nodi manylion pob safle sydd wedi’i gwmpasu gan Ddeddf Dŵr 2003, sy’n peri bod angen trwydded tynnu dŵr ym mhob achos o dynnu 20 metr ciwbig neu fwy o ddŵr.  Mae’r set ddata’n cynnwys manylion pob trwydded tynnu dŵr fyw yng Nghymru. Mae trwyddedau sydd wedi dod i ben neu sydd wedi’u dirymu wedi’u heithrio. Mae’r tabl yn cynnwys manylion yr uchafswm blynyddol a dyddiol o ran tynnu dŵr. Y symiau hyn yw’r uchafswm a ganiateir o dan y drwydded; maent yn rhoi arwydd ynglŷn â maint y gwaith tynnu dŵr. Gall rhai trwyddedau gynnwys amodau cydgasglu neu amodau eraill sy’n cyfyngu ar y gwaith tynnu dŵr; nid yw’r rhain wedi’u cynnwys yn y set ddata.  Rhybudd gwybodaeth  Golygiadau  Mae rhan o’r set ddata hon yn sensitif ac mae wedi’i golygu, a gellir ei hailddefnyddio dim ond yn unol â thelerau trwydded CNC. Gall y set ddata nas golygwyd fod ar gael ar gais drwy anfon Ffurflen Gais Ddata wedi’i llenwi at dîm Dosbarthu Data CNC: datadistribution@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk  Trwyddedau a ddadreoleiddiwyd  Cafodd rhai trwyddedau eu dadreoleiddio ar 31 Mawrth 2005. Mae trwyddedau sydd wedi’u dadreoleiddio yn cynnwys unrhyw waith o dynnu llai nag 20 metr ciwbig o ddŵr y dydd o ffynhonnell dŵr wyneb (e.e. nant neu ddraen) neu o strata tanddaearol (drwy dwll turio neu ffynhonnell), at unrhyw ddiben penodol. Felly, nid oedd modd trwyddedu gwaith tynnu dŵr mwyach os oedd yn perthyn i’r categori hwn, ac fe’i dirymwyd. O ganlyniad, mae’n bosibl bod gwaith tynnu dŵr sydd wedi’i ddadreoleiddio ac achosion eraill o dynnu dŵr heb drwydded yn eich ardal o ddiddordeb, yn arbennig at ddefnydd domestig neu amaethyddol, neu nad yw trwyddedau a ddadreoleiddiwyd a oedd yn hysbys cyn 31 Mawrth 2005 yn weithredol mwyach. Felly, ni fydd yr achosion hyn o dynnu dŵr yn cael eu cynnwys yn y data Gwaith Tynnu Dŵr Trwyddedig a gyflenwyd. Mae rhagor o wybodaeth am yr amserlenni ar gyfer trwyddedu gwaith tynnu dŵr a oedd wedi’i eithrio o’r blaen, ac esemptiadau sy’n weddill, ar gael ar ein gwefan.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe dataset holds details of all live water abstraction licences in Wales. Expired, lapsed and revoked licences are excluded. The table holds details of maximum annual and maximum daily abstraction quantities. The quantities are the maximum permitted under the licence; they give an indication of the size of the abstraction. Some licences may include aggregating conditions or other conditions which restrict the abstraction; these are not included in the dataset.   Data stored from 2013-04-01 to 2016-02-26 in WISKI and from 2016-02-27 to present in PALS.    Some of the data is considered sensitive, a redacted public version of the data is available, requests for the full data will be consider on a case-by-case basis.    Information warning; Deregulated Licences Some licences were deregulated on 31st March 2005. Deregulated licences include any abstraction of less than 20 cubic metres per day from a surface water source (e.g. stream or drain) or from underground strata (via borehole or well) for any particular purpose. Abstractions were therefore no longer licensable if they fell into this category and revoked. It is therefore possible that there are deregulated abstractions and other unlicensed abstractions present in your area of interest, particularly for domestic or agricultural use or that deregulated licences known before 31st March 2005 are now no longer active. So these abstractions will not be included in the supplied Licensed Water Abstraction data. Further information on the timescales for licensing previously exempt abstractions and remaining exemptions can be found on our website.\t\tPermitting and Licencing System (PALS) - also know as Dynamix 365 Data stored from 2013-04-01 to 2016-02-26 in WISKI and from 2016-02-27 to present in PALS (Also know as Dynamix 365)\tData Custodian: Ashley Lansdown, Team Leader, Water Resources Permitting, Future Regulation\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4295/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_water_resource_permits\t2026-01-12T13:53:40.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18273\tNRW_DS116322\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.722Z\t1825\tUnassessed\tEditable\tQuality Controlled Daily Rain Gauge Data from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Gauges\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333232\tThis dataset contains daily rainfall totals, given in mm to 1 decimal place, for all rainfall stations included within this dataset. The list of stations to be defined by the file contents with no separate index of stations. The period for counting rainfall will be in line with the water day which is 24 hour period between 9am and 9am. Stations which do not meet quality control specification, eg missing or invalid data, will be omitted from the published file.\tFiles will only be published when all data for that day have been validated. Typically this will be 3 months after the data was recorded.\t\tHydrometry and Telemetry syste\tAlison Hanson- Hydrometry and telemetry.  Data is provided on request. Metadata- Data services Team       Currently data is only on request as has to be extracted by the H\u0026amp;T team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:26:30.720Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18272\tNRW_DS116323\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.636Z\t961\tValid\tEditable\tWaste Carriers, Brokers and Dealers\tCludwyr, Broceriaid a Delwyr Gwastraff\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333233\tThis dataset contains details of currently permitted waste carriers, brokers and dealers.    Carrier: A person who transports controlled waste in the course of a business or otherwise with a view to profit.   Broker: Waste brokers are people who make arrangements, on behalf of others, to recover or dispose of waste, regardless of whether or not they handle the waste themselves.   Dealer: Waste dealers are people who use an agent to buy then sell wastes, regardless of whether they handle the waste themselves or not.   Historical details are not included.-Mae\u0027r set ddata cludwyr, broceriaid a delwyr gwastraff yn cynnwys manylion y cludwyr, broceriaid a delwyr gwastraff sydd wedi\u0027u trwyddedu ar hyn o bryd yng Nghymru. Nid yw manylion hanesyddol yn cael eu cynnwys. Cludwr yw unigolyn sy\u0027n cludo gwastraff a reolir fel rhan o fusnes neu fel arall gyda\u0027r nod o wneud elw. Broceriaid gwastraff yw pobl sy\u0027n gwneud trefniadau ar ran eraill, i adfer neu waredu gwastraff, ni waeth a ydynt yn ymdrin â\u0027r gwastraff eu hunain neu beidio. Mae delwyr gwastraff yn defnyddio asiant i brynu ac yna gwerthu gwastraff, ni waeth a ydynt yn ymdrin â\u0027r gwastraff eu hunain neu beidio.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tPeople who do not need to register because of a specific exemption in the regulations:   - the operator of certain vessels and vehicles where the activity of waste carriage is for the purpose of a specified marine operation and the activity requires a marine licence or can be carried out under a marine exemption  - any lower tier carrier who does not normally and regularly transport controlled waste   - until after 2013, the existing exemption for carriers who only transport their own waste (unless it is construction and demolition waste) will remain in place.    People who are excluded from the requirement to register. These include:  - Any person who carries controlled wastes but not as part of their business or otherwise for profit   - Ferry operators carrying vehicles that are carrying waste  - Any person carrying waste between different places of the same premises  - Any person carrying waste by air or sea, from a place in Great Britain to any place outside Great Britain  - Any person carrying waste from a country outside of Great Britain to the first point of arrival.   Waste Carriers, Dealers and Brokers are a combined dataset. Operators shift between categories frequently, and so separate datasets could be misleading. Extracting a single type would be extremely time consuming and cost-prohibitive.\t\tData taken from the Customer Relationship Management (CRM) database  Y:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Licenced Data\\NRW_DS116323\tCustodian: Stephen Badman, Customer Contact Centre\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2025-11-17\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2639\t2025-11-17T17:30:00.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18269\tNRW_DS116324\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.326Z\t412\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicators of Water Courses - Biology\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333234\tThe General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicator scheme or GQAHI (previously known as GQA) was the national indicator for water quality in rivers and canals. It was designed to provide an accurate and consistent assessment of the state of water quality and how it changes over time. These assessments were made for Biological, Chemical and Nutrients and undertaken for discrete river stretches. This datasets relates to the biological assessments. The Biology assessment gave an indicator of the overall \u0027health\u0027 of rivers. It describes water quality in terms of 83 groups of macroinvertebrates. Some of these are pollution sensitive so their presence suggested better water quality. A category was assigned to the river stretch where the monitoring point was located:  - A - Very Good: Biology similar to that expected for an average and unpolluted river of this size, type and location. High diversity of groups, usually with several species in each. Rare to find dominance of any one group.  - B - Good: Biology falls a little short of that expected for an unpolluted river. Small reduction in the number of groups that are sensitive to pollution. Moderate increase in the number of individuals in the groups that tolerate pollution. - C - Fairly Good: Biology worse than expected for an unpolluted river. Many sensitive groups absent or number of individuals reduced. Marked rise in numbers of individuals in groups that tolerate pollution.  - D - Fair: Sensitive groups scarce and contains only small numbers of individuals. A range of pollution tolerant groups present, some with high numbers of individuals.  - E - Poor: Biology restricted to pollution tolerant species with some groups dominant in terms of the numbers of individuals. Sensitive groups rare or absent.  - F - Bad: Biology limited to a small number of very tolerant groups (such as worms, midge larvae and leeches), present in very high numbers. In the worst case, there may be no life present. Sample points represent one or many stretches.\tThe assessment was made by carrying out a bi-yearly (Spring/Autumn) three minute active sampling with a pond net and a one-minute visual search for animals living on the surface or attached to rocks or vegetation. The number of taxa present were recorded and the Biological Monitoring Working Party score calculated. These values were then compared with values expected at a site in a similar, but totally unpolluted river. The mathematical model RIVPACS (the River Invertebrate Prediction and Classification System) was used to predict fauna in the absence of pollution.  Issues to Note: Additional attributes are added annually (2008 attributes are represented) with 2009 being the last year of the scheme. In 2007 the GQA river network was reduced to the GQAHI river network. The data described have been amended to be consistent for all years.\t\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116324\tMetadata point of Contact: Data Services Custodian: Jane Roberts-  Environmental Reporting Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18275\tNRW_DS116325\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.723Z\t637\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicators of Water Courses - Chemistry\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333235\tThe General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicator scheme or GQAHI (previously known as GQA) was the national indicator for water quality in rivers and canals. It was designed to provide an accurate and consistent assessment of the state of water quality and how it changes over time. These assessments were made for Biological, Chemical and Nutrients and undertaken for discrete river stretches. This datasets relates to the chemical assessments. In Wales we maintained the full GQA network until 2010 based on 800 sampling sites which provided information for approximately 4700km. Grades of river quality for the chemical GQA, Chemical grade Likely uses and characteristics:en - A: Very good, All abstractions, Very good Salmonid fisheries, Cyprinid fisheries, Natural ecosystems. - B: Good, All abstractions, Salmonid fisheries, Cyprinid fisheries, Ecosystems at or close to natural.  - C: Fairly good, Potable supply after advanced treatment, Other abstractions, Good Cyprinid fisheries, Natural ecosystems, or those corresponding to good Cyprinid fisheries  - D: Fair, Potable supply after advanced treatment, Other abstractions, Fair Cyprinid fisheries, Impacted ecosystems.  - E: Poor Low grade abstraction for industry, Fish absent or sporadically present, vulnerable to pollution, Impoverished ecosystems  - F: Bad, Very polluted rivers which may cause nuisance, Severely restricted ecosystems *Provided other standards are met where the grade is caused by discharges of organic pollution.\tChemistry GQAHI/GQA sites were sampled twelve times a year, the samples being taken at the same spot on each sampling occasion to ensure consistency. In Wales each chemical sample was measured for biochemical oxygen demand (BOD), ammonia and dissolved oxygen (the most common types of organic pollution from sewage treatment works, agriculture and industry). A category was assigned using three years worth of samples for each sampled chemical and assigned a category assessed against chemical standards expressed as percentiles The data collected over three years were used to determine average nutrient concentrations. So the classification for the year 2008 includes the results for 2006 and 2007. Subsequently a category was assigned to each length of river according to the lowest standard achieved by any of the two or three measurements. The Chemistry GQA used in Wales described quality in terms of three chemical measurements that detect the most common types of organic pollution from sewage treatment works, agriculture and industry.  In 2007 the GQA river network was reduced to the GQAHI river network. The assessment was changed to be based on total ammonia and dissolved oxygen only. Biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) was removed from the assessment and all past grades re-calculated. The data described have been amended to be consistent and comparable for all years.\t\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116327\tMetadata point of contact: Data Services Custodian: Jane Roberts\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-07-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18270\tNRW_DS116326\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.228Z\t294\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicators of Water Courses - Nutrients\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333236\tThe General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicator scheme or GQAHI (previously known as GQA) was the national indicator for water quality in rivers and canals. It was designed to provide an accurate and consistent assessment of the state of water quality and how it changes over time. These assessments were made for Biological, Chemical and Nutrients and undertaken for discrete river stretches. This datasets relates to the nutrient assessments. In Wales we maintained the full GQA network until 2010 based on 800 sampling sites which provided information for approximately 4700km. The GQAHI/GQA scheme was designed to provide an accurate and consistent assessment of the state of water quality and how it changes over time. The Nutrients GQA described quality in terms of two nutrients: nitrates (mg NO3 /l) and phosphates (mg P/l) and graded from 1 to 6.\tThe Nutrients GQA described quality in terms of two nutrients: nitrates (mg NO3 /l) and phosphates (mg P/l) and graded from 1 to 6. Grades were allocated for both phosphate and nitrate; they were not combined into a single nutrients grade. There were no set \u0027good\u0027 or \u0027bad\u0027 concentrations for nutrients in rivers in the way that we describe chemical and biological quality. Rivers in different parts of the country have naturally different concentrations of nutrients. ‘Very low’ nutrient concentrations, for example, are not necessarily good or bad; the classifications merely stated that concentrations in this river were very low relative to other rivers. Classification for phosphate Grade limit (mgP/l) Average Description: 0.02 to 0.06 Low \u0026gt;0.06 to 0.1 Moderate \u0026gt;0.1 to 0.2 High \u0026gt;0.2 to 1.0 Very high \u0026gt;1.0 Excessively high Classification for nitrate Grade limit (mg NO3/l) Average Description: 5 to 10 Low \u0026gt;10 to 20 Moderately low \u0026gt;20 to30 Moderate \u0026gt;30 to 40 High \u0026gt;40 Very high.  2009 was the final year of the scheme.\t\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116326\tMetadata point of contact: Data Service team Custodian: Jane Roberts\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Environment Agency Wales\t\t2016-07-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18271\tNRW_DS116327\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.307Z\t778\tValid\tEditable\tHistoric River Quality Objectives\tAmcanion Ansawdd Afon Hynafol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333237\tThe River Quality Objectives (RQO) classification was used for planning water quality improvements until 2006 when the scheme ended. The national RQO classification scheme ceased at the end of 2006, it is now a fixed table which will not be updated. RQO compliance figures are available for 1997-2006.-Roedd y dosbarthiad Amcanion Ansawdd Afon (RQO) yn cael ei ddefnyddio i gynllunio gwelliannau mewn ansawdd dŵr tan 2006 pan orffennodd y cynllun.   Roedd RQOau yn cael eu rhoi i afonydd mawr (yn ddibynnol ar lif yr afon). Roedd samplau cemegol yn cael eu cymryd 12 gwaith y flwyddyn. Byddai unrhyw fethiannau yn ansawdd yr afon, eu rhesymau a\u0027r gweithrediadau i\u0027w rhoi ar waith, yn cael eu storio mewn tablau gwahanol. Daeth y cynllun dosbarthu RQO i ben yn niwedd 2006, ac mae bellach yn dabl sefydlog na ellir ei ddiweddaru.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tRQOs were assigned to all significantly sized rivers (based on river flow). RQOs were based on the River Ecosystem (RE) Classification Scheme which was introduced in 1994 to replace the National Water Council\u0027s methodology. The RE system consists of five classes (1-5) based on the same determinands used in the General Quality Assessment (GQA) chemistry assessment of Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD), ammonia and dissolved oxygen but also includes the additional determinands of (free (or un-ionised) ammonia, pH, hardness, dissolved copper and total zinc) that reflect the requirements of a river ecosystem. Chemical samples were taken 12 times a year. Any river quality failures, their reasons and actions to be taken, were stored in separate tables.\t\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116327\tInternal Contact Dave Johnson (was Jane Roberts)  in the Evidence, Monitoring \u0026amp; Reporting.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-12-20\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2623\t2025-05-02T14:48:18.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18267\tNRW_DS116328\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.187Z\t712\tValid\tEditable\tUK Water Quality Sampling Harmonised Monitoring Scheme Summary Data\tData Crynodol Cynllun Monitro Cytûn Samplu Ansawdd Dŵr y DU\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333238\tThe Historic UK Water Quality Sampling Harmonised Monitoring Scheme (HMS) data contains statistics for a series of water quality sampling sites including annual means, maximum and minimum values for each region for specified determinands from 1975 onwards. The Harmonised Monitoring Scheme (HMS) was established to provide an archive of water quality data for the UK. It is used to provide information for international obligations, including the long-term trends of some determinands and the estimation of riverborne input of selected determinands to the sea.\tThe sampling network includes 230 sites, which are mainly located at the tidal limits of major rivers or at the points of confluence of significant tributaries. The information held within the HMS includes data on: Oxygen and ammonia, Nutrients, List II metals and Pesticides. Annual mean concentrations have been calculated for each HMS site. The data show the annual averages of the site means in each region, with each site given equal weight irrespective of the number of samples taken - an average of all the samples would give a greater weight to the sites at which samples are most frequently taken.   In order to give an indication of the range of values at different sites within each region, figures are also given for the maximum and minimum site mean for each region. The data also includes for the UK average, the 10th percentile and the 90th percentile of the site means for the whole of the UK. For each determin and in each year and the 10-percentile and the 90-percentile of the site means in each region.   The Environment Agency also hold and publish a copy of this dataset.\t\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116328\tInternal Custodian: Julie Boswell- Principal Officer, Information and Environmental Assessment, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS  Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-10-25\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-05-02T14:48:16.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18268\tNRW_DS116329\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.294Z\t842\tValid\tEditable\tConsented Discharges to Controlled Waters with Conditions\tGollyngiadau a Ganiateir I Ddyfoedd a Reolir (gydag amodau)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333239\tThis dataset provide details of permit details as required under the Environmental Permit Regulations. Information is held for all permit holders and covers all substances that are controlled. The data come from Natural Resources Wales public Register.   The data set contains 3 tiers of information:    Tier 1 - Site and General Information relating to the permit operator, discharge address and type. The date of permit issue, effective and revocation. Information where the effluent enters the environment (such as river, coastal, groundwater) for each permit.     Tier 2 - Outlet and release Data on the effluent type e.g. Sewage effluent, Storm Overflow, Trade. The location is supplied for the effluent and the outlet in OS Nation Grid Reference format. Further detail is provided on the permit type and treatment type.    Tier 3 - Determinand /release detail Limits  Further detail is provided on the amount that can be discharged and in which time period in months. Release details/determinands are the substances and numerical limits that make up the effluent. This could include chemical, biological, and physical limits. The permitted limits are included for each determinand /release detail. Data is provided for each effluent and may contain one or more determinands depending on the complexity of the discharge.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn darparu manylion trwyddedu fel sy\u0027n ofynnol gan Reoliadau Trwyddedu Amgylcheddol. Cedwir gwybodaeth am bob deiliaid trwydded ac mae\u0027n cynnwys yr holl sylweddau a reolir. Daw\u0027r data o Gofrestr Gyhoeddus Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru. Mae\u0027r set ddata\u0027n cynnwys tair haen o wybodaeth:  Haen 1 – Safle a chyffredinol  Gwybodaeth yn ymwneud â gweithredwr y drwydded, cyfeiriad gollwng a math. Dyddiad cyflwyno, dod i rym a dirymio\u0027r drwydded. Gwybodaeth ynglŷn â lle mae\u0027r elifion yn mynd i\u0027r amgylchedd (fel afon, arfordir, dŵr daear) ar gyfer pob trwydded.  Haen 2 – Allfa a gollwng  Data ar y math o elifion, e.e. elifiant carthion, gorlif storm, masnach. Caiff lleoliad yr elifiant a\u0027r allfa eu darparu yn fformat Cyfeirnod Grid Cenedlaethol OS. Rhoddir manylion pellach am y math o drwydded a\u0027r math o driniaeth.  Haen 3 – Cyfyngiadau manylion gollwng / penderfynyddion  Darperir gwybodaeth bellach am faint y gellir ei ollwng ac ym mha gyfnod amser mewn misoedd. Manylion gollwng / penderfynyddion yw\u0027r sylweddau a\u0027r cyfyngiadau rhifyddol sy\u0027n cyfrannu at yr elifion. Gallai hyn gynnwys cyfyngiadau cemegol, biolegol a ffisegol. Cynhwysir y cyfyngiadau trwyddedig ar gyfer pob manylyn penderfynydd / gollyngiad. Darperir data ar gyfer pob elifiant a gall gynnwys un penderfynydd neu fwy gan ddibynnu ar gymhlethdod y gollyngiad.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tData was stored in the legacy EA WIMS database from 2013-04-01 to 2016-06-26 before being transitioned into NRW\u0027s Permitting And Licencing System (PALS) database on the 2016/06/27.    This dataset has now incorporated NRW_DS116215 Consented Discharges to Controlled Waters and will also be used to highlight any discharges that have List 1 or 2 substances as detailed in NRW-DS116311 Discharges of Consented List 1 and 2 Substances.\t\tData is extracted from the Permitting and Licencing System (PALS)\tData Custodian: Henry Forrest, Permitting Centre Wales Cardiff\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3544/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_water_quality_permits\t2026-01-12T14:43:24.280Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18266\tNRW_DS116330\t2024-05-28T12:44:33.074Z\t262\tValid\tEditable\tMonitoring of Pesticides and Trace Organics in Water 1992 - 2008\tMonitro Plaladdwyr a Hybrin Organaidd yn Dŵr 1992-2008\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333330\tA pesticide is defined under the Food and Environment Protection Act (1985) as \u0027any substance, preparation or organism prepared or used for destroying any pest\u0027. Pesticides include herbicides, fungicides, insecticides, molluscicides, rodenticides, growth regulators and masonry and timber preservatives. They are not confined to agriculture, but are also used on roads and rail tracks, in homes and gardens, as sheep dips, for the protection of public health, and for many other purposes.   Monitoring for pesticides is guided by statutory requirements to monitor concentrations of specific pesticides listed in certain Directives. We also undertake non-statutory monitoring of pesticides, when investigating known or predicted local problems and pollution incidents. Trace Organics are organic compounds (including pesticides) detectable at low levels.  As with pesticides, our monitoring of these substances is guided by statutory requirements.\tThe dataset contains information on all pesticides and trace organics monitored by Environment Agency Wales. Monitoring frequency varies between sites and years. Monitoring covers fresh and saline surface waters, groundwater, discharges, sediments and biota. Please note: Limits of detection vary between laboratories.   Data is available for monitoring between 1992 and 2008.   The dataset holds each year’s pesticides and trace organics monitoring data. Some sites are identified as being monitored for known or suspected contamination issues, so that they can be excluded for analysis of background levels.  The layout of fields and tables varies from year to year.   Data for 2009 onwards is not available as a standalone dataset.\t\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\tDataset Custodian: Martin Stephens        This dataset is not to be published to data.gov.uk as already published through data.gov.uk by the EA.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2017-10-24\tpublication\tnull\thttps://naturalresourceswales.sharefile.eu/d-sd5506c031384a3bb\t2025-12-23T09:46:33.743Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18307\tNRW_DS116331\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.914Z\t759\tValid\tEditable\tEnvironmental Permitting Regulations - Waste Sites\tRheoliadau Trwyddedu Amgylcheddol - Safleoedd Gwastraff\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333331\tA waste management licence is a legal document issued under the Environmental Protection Act 1990. A licence authorises the treatment, keeping or disposal of waste in or on the land. Once we have issued a licence, neither the activities nor the area of land may be changed unless the licence is modified.-Mae trwydded rheoli gwastraff yn ddogfen gyfreithiol a gyhoeddir dan Ddeddf Diogelu\u0027r Amgylchedd 1990. Mae trwydded yn awdurdodi trin, cadw neu waredu gwastraff yn y tir neu arno. Unwaith i ni gyflwyno trwydded, ni ellir newid y gweithgareddau na\u0027r ardal o dir oni bai bod y drwydded yn cael ei haddasu. Daeth y Rheoliadau Trwyddedu Amgylcheddol, sy\u0027n rheoleiddio safleoedd gwastraff, i rym ar 6 Ebrill 2008 ac maent yn cyfuno nifer o drefnau trwyddedu cynharach.  Datganiad priodoli: Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe Environmental Permitting Regulations, regulating waste sites, came into force on 6 April 2008. The new regime combines a number of earlier permitting / licensing regimes.\tOther related datasets available are:  Pollution Prevention and Control (IPPC) Authorised Treatment Facilities (End of Life Vehicles) Water Quality and Pollution Control (Discharge Consents) Waste Electrical, Electronic Equipment (WEEE)\tThe data is stored from 2013-04-01 to 2015-12-31 in REGIS and  from 2016-01-01 to 2016-07-01 in Permitting And Licencing System.\tData Custodian: Henry Forrest/ Tim Morris - Permitting Centre Wales, Cardiff\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3577/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_waste_permits\t2026-01-12T14:00:42.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18310\tNRW_DS116332\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.929Z\t272\tUnassessed\tEditable\tExtractive Materials Management Statement Summaries – Corporate Entities Only\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333332\tExtractive Materials Management Statements are sent in by operators of mines and quarries to demonstrate that extractive materials produced on site are not extractive waste and therefore do not require a permit under the Environmental Permitting Regulations (EPR) to manage this material as would otherwise be required under the Mining Waste Directive. EMMS are not submitted under any legislative framework, but are submitted as a result of an agreement between ourselves and the mines and quarries sector as a way of stating what materials are not extractive waste due to European Court of Justice rulings that define extractive waste in a more restrictive way that the Directive. The EMMS spreadsheet holds summary details of all EMMS submitted to us since September 2010. Subsequent requests for individual EMM Statements themselves will be treated on an individual basis, and will be checked for relevant confidentialities, in accordance with normal procedures.\tSpecific information is required in an EMMS, to include what type of materials are produced, how the material is incorporated in the final site restoration and an estimate of the total quantity of materials produced during the lifetime of the operation. This can be given by referencing existing information required under other legislation, for example Planning Permission.\t\t[A] Extracted from Extractive Materials Management Statements.\tTim Morris: Waste Permiting       This entry is not added to Lle or Data.Gov to proactively publish it because the data is old and not something we recommend using, unless specifically requested, although it will be available to download from ShareFile as Open Data from the metadata\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-01-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:02:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18313\tNRW_DS116333\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.950Z\t828\tValid\tEditable\tSurface Water Temperature Archive up to 2007\tArchif Tymheredd Arwyneb Dŵr hyd at 2007\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333333\tWater temperature data is collected  for different reasons and in different locations. Time series of surface water temperatures can provide indicators of climate change and associated ecological responses. An archive was created in 2007 as part of a research project, and is a unique collation of Natural Resource Wales and The Environment Agencies water temperature data from more than 30,000 sites across England \u0026amp; Wales. The archive contains water temperature data (up to 2007) and site metadata. The archive contains water temperature data (up to 2007) and site metadata. Most sites have records starting from the 1980s.-Mae data tymheredd dŵr yn cael ei gasglu a\u0027i storio gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru am wahanol resymau ac mewn gwahanol leoliadau. Gall cyfresi amser o dymheredd arwyneb dŵr ddangos newid hinsawdd ac ymatebion ecolegol cysylltiol. Crëwyd archif yn 2007 fel rhan o brosiect ymchwil, ac mae\u0027n goladiad unigryw rhwng data tymheredd Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru ac Asiantaethau\u0027r Amgylchedd o fwy na 30,000 safle ar draws Cymru a Lloegr. Mae\u0027r archif yn cynnwys data tymheredd dŵr (hyd at 2007) a metadata safle. Mae gan y rhan fwyaf o safleoedd gofnodion sy\u0027n cychwyn o\u0027r 1980au.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe water temperature data are available in two main types; spot samples from routine monitoring (e.g. monthly) and high resolution samples (e.g. every 15 minutes). The database was created using Microsoft Access 2003 but has a simple query based front end. As part of the science project about 1 in 10 sites were analysed to assess trends, and images of these graphs are embedded within the archive and linked to sites for information. The archive can be interrogated to find out where water temperature data exist, how frequently sampling occurs and the length of each record. In addition, sites have information about water body type e.g. river, lake or canal.\t\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116333\tInternal Contact: Alison Hanson\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-10-25\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2630\t2025-05-02T14:44:45.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18306\tNRW_DS116334\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.876Z\t806\tValid\tEditable\tOtter Survey Data 1991-2010\tData Arolwg Dyfrgwn 1991-2010\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333334\tThe European otter (Lutra lutra) is widely recognised as an emblem for nature conservation in the UK because it is a top predator and an important biological indicator of the health of our rivers and wetlands. Monitoring the status of the otter therefore gives us a valuable measure of the state of our water and wetland ecosystems. In Wales as in much of the UK, it is a largely nocturnal animal and is rarely observed in the wild. It is however possible to detect its presence by searching for its distinctive droppings (spraints) and footprints.   As part of a scheme to monitor the distribution of otters throughout the UK, five Welsh otter survey were carried out as follows:    - 1st survey: 1977-78 run and funded by NCC.  - 2nd survey: 1984-85 run and funded by Vincent Wildlife Trust  - 3rd survey: 1991 run and funded by Vincent Wildlife Trust  - 4th survey: 2002 run by EAW and the Wildlife Trusts and funded by EAW, CCW and Welsh Water.  - 5th survey: 2009-2010 run by EAW and the Wildlife Trusts, IBAP officers and the Snowdonia Mammal Group.    The aim of these surveys was to monitor the distribution of otters throughout Wales. Sites were selected by accessibility and likelihood of finding signs of otter activity, typically road bridges and the confluences of watercourses. The number of sites visited per hydrometric region varied. No attempt was made to randomise the sampling effort, but there was some site variability due different issues when the surveys were undertaken.     This dataset is made up of two parts; the 3rd and 4th surveys (1991 and 2002), and the 5th survey 2009-2010. The full Welsh for the 1st and 2nd surveys, for 1997-78, 1984-85 is not currently available. The 1991 and 2002 data are currently available on the NBN Gateway (published by JNCC) as redacted open data.   This raw data is currently not available, however more detailed information about each surveying year can be found in the reports available through the library (see related titles). The 2009-2010 otter surveys are available though NRW but will be issued under licence.\t1018 sites have been consistently monitored over the four surveys.    Two field survey techniques are used – full surveys for sites investigated in previous national surveys and spot-checks for all the alternate 50 km squares not covered in previous surveys. Spot checks are made for up to 10 locations within the survey square.   Sites were surveyed for otter spraints (droppings) and footprints, if found these were classed as a positive record / signs.  The survey protocol was to search up to 600 m of one bank of a waterbody from a starting point (grid referenced), either all in one direction or split between two directions. Searching stopped as soon as signs of activity were noted. The number of spraints was recorded.   No otter holts were recorded specifically, however, if present within the dataset these have been removed from the public version and will only be issued under licence. The presence of mink is also recorded but this is only included as part of the more detailed otter survey.\t\tThis data is available through Sharefile as part of a packaged licence product.\tHuw Jones, Team Leader, Natural Resource Management, NRW Janet Buckles, Technical Assistant Biodiversity, Biodiversity, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2020-10-27\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr3416\t2025-12-01T17:16:41.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18317\tNRW_DS116335\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.977Z\t745\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHazardous Waste Interrogator\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333335\tNatural Resources Wales is legally required to monitor all movements of hazardous waste in Wales. Hazardous waste producers are required to register with us and the site where the waste is disposed or recovered is required to inform us of the details of the wastes they receive.  Hazardous waste producer data is commercially confidential. However a summary of the movements is provided in this Hazardous Waste Interrogator. Only high-level waste classification geographical locations (where the waste was produced and where the waste management facility is located) and tonnage is included. Individual site names and producers details are not included.\tThe data for Wales is available from the Environment Agency up until 2013 and is available for download from data.gov.uk. There is a separate data set for each year. Data collected for Wales for the period after 2013 is managed by NRW. INFORMATION WARNING High level summary data. Does not include individual producers’ and site details. Double counting element of the same waste making multiple movements should be noted.\t\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\tTim Morris, Senior Waste Advisor (Hazardous Waste), Waste \u0026amp; Resources Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-07-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18316\tNRW_DS116336\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.977Z\t2243\tValid\tEditable\tWaste Permit Returns Data Interrogator\tHolwr Data Cofnodion Trwyddedau Gwastraff\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333336\tAll operators of regulated waste management facilities have to provide us with details of the quantities and types of waste they deal with i.e. waste received into site and waste sent on from site to other facilities or processes. This data is used to monitor compliance but has historically been used by the EC, DEFRA and local authorities to assist in planning for new waste facilities and for monitoring against statutory targets. We have provided this data in an interrogatable format.-Mae’n rhaid i holl weithredwyr cyfleusterau rheoli gwastraff sy’n cael eu rheoleiddio ddarparu manylion i ni ynghylch y maint a’r math o wastraff y maent yn delio ag ef h.y. faint o wastraff a dderbynnir ar y safle a’r gwastraff a anfonir o’r safle i gyfleusterau neu brosesau eraill. Defnyddir y data hwn i fonitro cydymffurfiad ond yn hanesyddol, fe’i defnyddiwyd gan yr UE, DEFRA ac awdurdodau lleol i gynorthwyo wrth gynllunio ar gyfer cyfleusterau gwastraff newydd ac ar gyfer monitro yn erbyn targedau statudol. Rydym wedi darparu’r data hwn ar ffurf holiadol.  Caiff y set data hon ei gynhyrchu am flwyddyn galendr ac mae’n cynnwys data o safleoedd gwastraff sy’n cael eu rheoleiddio.  Gellir lawrlwytho yr holwyr Data Gwastraff cyn 2014 yn data.gov.uk fel rhan o gasgliadau Asiantaeth yr Amgylchedd.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe dataset is produced for a calendar year and holds the data from regulated waste sites. Waste Data interrogators prior to 2013 can be downloaded from data.gov.uk as part of the Environment Agency collations.    - Operator waste returns are public register information unless a claim for commercial confidentiality has been accepted.  In these cases the data is not provided.   - The operator field shows the operator of the permit at the time of report collation. This may be different from the operator during the period covered by the waste return.   - All tonnage data is reported by site operators. Numbers are based on all correct returns held by NRW at the time of report collation. They do not include waste returns that are outstanding or pending quality assurance action.\t\tWaste Returns database and PALs database\tDataset point of contact: John Fry, Lead Specialist Advisor, Waste Policy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-07-07\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2619\t2025-12-19T10:43:00.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18318\tNRW_DS116337\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.024Z\t516\tValid\tEditable\tHydropower Permits\tTrwyddedau Ynni Dwr\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333337\tPermits issued for Hydropower Generation sites. NRW assesses and licences hydropower schemes for the water they abstract and to protect the local and wider environment; this may require issuing one or a combination of Abstraction, Impoundment and Transfer licences dependant on the scheme/site. This dataset details the permits issued for new, varied or renewed hydropower schemes.-Trwyddedau a gyflwynir ar gyfer safleoedd cynhyrchu ynni dŵr. Mae CNC yn asesu a thrwyddedu cynlluniau ynni dŵr ar gyfer y dŵr a dynnir ganddynt ac i amddiffyn yr amgylchedd lleol a’r amgylchedd ehangach; gall hyn ofyn am gyflwyno un neu gyfuniad o drwyddedau Tynnu Dŵr, Cronni Dŵr a Throsglwyddo Dŵr yn dibynnu ar y cynllun/safle. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys manylion y trwyddedau a gyflwynir ar gyfer cynlluniau ynni dŵr newydd, amrywiol neu adnewyddedig.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tA few, very specific turbine set-ups do not require a licence. These will not appear in this dataset. Data is extracted from NRW\u0027s Permitting and Licencing System.\t\tData Extracted from Permitting And Licencing System (PALS) Data Stored in NALD from 2013-04-01 to 2016-02-26 and then Permitting And Licencing System from 2016-07-01\tData Custodian: Henry Forrest - Permitting Centre Wales, Cardiff\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3512/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_hydropower_permits\t2026-01-12T14:15:38.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18322\tNRW_DS116338\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.321Z\t971\tValid\tEditable\tUK Water Quality Sampling Harmonised Monitoring Scheme Detailed Data\tData Manwl Cynllun Monitro Cytûn Samplo Ansawdd Dŵr y DU\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333338\tThe Historic UK Water Quality Sampling Harmonised Monitoring Scheme (HMS) dataset contains individual determinand results for all sites in the UK Harmonised Monitoring Scheme network from 1974 until 2013. The Harmonised Monitoring Scheme (HMS) was established to provide an archive of water quality data for the UK. It is used to provide information for international obligations, including the long-term trends of some determinands and the estimation of riverborne input of selected determinands to the sea.-Mae cofnodion data Cynllun Hanesyddol Cytûn Monitro Samplu Ansawdd Dŵr y DU (HMS) yn cynnwys canlyniadau penderfynyddion ar gyfer holl safleoedd cynllun Monitro Cytûn y DU o 1974 tan 2013. Sefydlwyd y Cynllun Monitro Cytûn (HMS) er mwyn darparu archif o ddata ansawdd dŵr ar gyfer y DU. Fe\u0027i defnyddir i ddarparu gwybodaeth ar gyfer rhwymedigaethau rhyngwladol, gan gynnwys tueddiadau hirdymor rhai penderfynyddion ac amcangyfrif mewnbwn llif afon rhai penderfynyddion penodol i\u0027r môr.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. All rights reserved © Environment Agency © Scottish Environmental Protection Agency © Northern Ireland Agri-Food and Biosciences Institute.\tThe sampling network includes 230 sites which are mainly located at the tidal limits of major rivers or at the points of confluence of significant tributaries. The information held within the HMS includes data on: Oxygen and ammonia, Nutrients, List II metals and Pesticides.   The Environment Agency also hold and publish a copy of this dataset.\tSummary data for this data set is also available: UK Water Quality Sampling Harmonised Monitoring Scheme Summary Data (116328)\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\tInternal custodian: Mark Squire / (previously) Julie Boswell\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-06-22\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2633\t2025-05-02T15:16:18.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18311\tNRW_DS116339\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.920Z\t828\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333339\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS) is Natural Resources Wales standard for collecting data on the physical character and quality of river habitats across the Wales. This dataset provides survey results carried out from 1994 to 2014.  The bulk of surveys were carried out between 1994 to 1997 and 2006 to 2008. Surveys are still carried out for specific drivers, for example assessing habitat availability and Water Framework Directive.\tRHS is a standard field survey of a 500m stretch of river where data is collected in a replicable manner. At 50m intervals a \u0027spot-check\u0027 is conducted to record specific details about bank and channel physical attributes, man-made modifications, land uses and vegetation structure.  Surveys conducted prior to 2003 should not be compared with surveys conducted after 2003 as survey methodology changed significantly in 2003.\tFor further information on the survey methodology the RHS manual can be downloaded from https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/river-habitat-survey-guidance-manual.\tThis dataset sits within Kieco - WISKI module.   [A]  This data is available on Sharefile and is classed as Open Data and static at the moment, until we can get an update out of WISKI.\tNicola Wiltshire, Environmental Monitoring Officer 1, Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R, NRW Mel Lacan, Senior Environmental Monitoring Officer, Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R, NRW       Notes from 17/07/2018 The RHS data only currently goes up to 2014. When EAW split from EA we got an excel spreadsheet which contains the 1994 - 2004 data, extracted from EA\u0027s Ecosys module. This RHS acts as summary dataset, which is adequate for most people to use, and it includes indices of habitat type. This data is now stored in Keico (WISKI) which contains a lot of data from 2003 onwards. Pre 2003 the methodology was different so it has been hard to get the data into keico, Mel Lacan is hoping that by the end of the year, the data will be entered and they can start reporting again, therefore updating this dataset.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:15.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18323\tNRW_DS116340\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.320Z\t789\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Risk Assessments for Cycle 2\tAsesiadau Risg y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr ar gyfer Cylch 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333430\tThis dataset contains risk assessment data produced to support the cycle 2 river basin management plans. It contains -   Lake water bodies  -   River water bodies  -   Transitional water bodies (estuaries) -   Groundwater bodies -   Coastal water bodies  that are assessed to be at risk of failing WFD Objectives or at risk of deteriorating from current status for a number of pressures. It also contains details of protected areas (Shellfish Waters and Bathing Waters) that are assessed to be at risk of failing their objectives or at risk of deteriorating in the future.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys data asesiad risg a gynhyrchwyd i gefnogi cynlluniau rheoli basn afon Cylch 2. Mae\u0027n cynnwys gwybodaeth am  – Gyrff dŵr llynnoedd  – Cyrff dŵr afonydd  – Cyrff dŵr trawsnewidiol (aberoedd)  – Cyrff dŵr daear  – Cyrff dŵr arfordirol  y dyfernir eu bod mewn perygl o fethu amcanion y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr neu eu bod mewn perygl o ddirywio o\u0027u statws cyfredol oherwydd nifer o bwysau. Mae hefyd yn cynnwys manylion ynghylch ardaloedd gwarchodedig (dyfroedd pysgod cregyn a dyfroedd ymdrochi) y dyfernir eu bod mewn perygl o fethu eu hamcanion neu sydd mewn perygl o ddirywio yn y dyfodol.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThis data was collected to support the cycle 2 river basin management plans.\t\tDMS spreadsheets with the WFD team\tRobert Blacklidge Environmental reporting team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-10-25\tpublication\tnull\thttps://naturalresources.wales/evidence-and-data/research-and-reports/water-reports/river-basin-management-plans-published/?lang\u003den\t2025-05-07T15:34:01.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18320\tNRW_DS116342\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.220Z\t1486\tValid\tEditable\tHistoric Flood Warnings and Flood Alerts\tRhybuddion i Fod yn Barod am Lifogydd a Rhybuddion Llifogydd Hanesyddol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333432\tListing of Severe Flood Warnings, Flood Warnings, Update Flood Warnings and Flood Alerts issued since the Floodline Warnings Direct service went live on January 26th 2006 to the present.   This dataset includes flood warnings issued by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) from 1 April 2013.   Flood Alerts and Warnings are issued for flooding from rivers and the sea.  For Flood Alerts this is generally river catchments or stretches of coastline. For Flood Warnings and Severe Flood Warnings this is specific communities or parts of communities.-Aeth rhestri o Rybuddion Llifogydd Difrifol, Rhybuddion Llifogydd (Flood Warnings), Diweddariadau i Rybuddion Llifogydd a Rhybuddion Llifogydd (Flood Alerts) a gyhoeddwyd ers y gwasanaeth Rhybuddion Llifogydd Uniongyrchol, yn fyw ar Ionawr 26, 2006 hyd heddiw. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys rhybuddion llifogydd a gyhoeddwyd gan Gyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) ers 1 Ebrill 2013. Mae Rhybuddion Llifogydd (Warnings ac Alerts) yn cael eu cyhoeddi ar gyfer llifogydd o afonydd a\u0027r môr. Mae Rhybuddion Llifogydd (Flood Alerts) yn gyffredinol ar gyfer dalgylchoedd afonydd neu rannau o’r arfordir. Mae Rhybuddion Llifogydd (Flood Warnings) a Rhybuddion Llifogydd Difrifol ar gyfer cymunedau penodol neu rannau o gymunedau.  Mae rhybuddion llifogydd (Warnings ac Alerts) i\u0027w gweld yn fyw yma.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThere are three flood warning codes and a notification when warnings are removed. These are:  -  Severe Flood Warning: Severe flooding. Danger to life.   -  Flood Warning: Flooding is expected. Immediate action required  -  Update Flood Warning: an update to a Flood Warning (to cover change in latest situation and local conditions) -  Same as flood warning  -  Flood Alert: Flooding is possible. Be prepared.  -  Warning no longer in force: Flood warnings and flood alerts that have been removed in the last 24 hours.     Please note that from DATE the Flood Alert code replaced the older Flood Watch code. This historic dataset therefore contains Flood Watches rather than Flood Alerts prior to this date.\tLive flood warnings in force are shown on GOV.UK and are available as a separate live feed on the NRW website https://naturalresources.wales/flooding/flood-warnings/?lang\u003den\tEmailed every quarter from EA, but in 2016 the data will come via our own service. Y:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS116342\tAndrew Wall - Flood Forecasting Metadata Contact - Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-12-18\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2644\t2025-12-22T10:38:17.061Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18302\tNRW_DS116343\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.362Z\t934\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Risk Forecast Application Programming Interface (API)\tAPIs Darogan Perygl Llifogydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333433\tThe flood risk forecast is produced by the Flood Forecasting Centre (FFC) on a daily basis. It is issued more frequently when serious flooding is forecast. It provides the indication of the potential for flooding for five days: the day on which it is issued and the subsequent four days ahead.  The forecast highlights flood risk on a county by county basis and includes a short commentary on the situation. It covers flooding from rivers, the sea, surface water and groundwater for Wales and England.  This dataset is produced in partnership with the Met Office Flood Forecasting Centre and the Environment Agency and is intended to complement the Flood Warning Service provided by Natural Resources Wales.-APIs Darogan Perygl Llifogydd (Data Agored) – arwydd o’r perygl llifogydd ar gyfer cyfod o dridiau: y diwrnod y caiff ei gyhoeddi a’r deuddydd canlynol\tThe most recent available flood risk forecast is available as a single JSON data structure. The forecast includes small images showing the regions affected by the forecast risks. These are included in the forecast data as base64 encoded png images, for convenience they are also available as decoded images.    Information Warnings: This flood risk dataset is intended to be used as a statement at a single point in time of the risk of flooding over a five day period. This data should be used for planning and preparation purposes only and not for public advice, instruction or information during a flooding emergency.   Due to the risks of misinformation either in accuracy or timeliness, public authoritative advice in relation to this data should be sought during a flooding emergency from Natural Resources Wales. This is available via Natural Resources Wales website or by telephoning Floodline on 0345 988 11 88.   Live data from the API should be consumed in real time and kept up to date to avoid misinformation. Real Time Commentary in Welsh language is generated by an automated English to Welsh translation tool.  Natural Resources Wales are continually developing this tool and accept no liability for inaccuracies it may contain.  Flood forecasts can also be viewed on Natural Resources Wales Website here:  http://naturalresources.wales/flooding/5-day-flood-forecast\t\tHost by the EA and Made available through our API manager\tMartin Linforth: Flood and coastal risk management\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-10-12\tpublication\tnull\thttps://api-portal.naturalresources.wales/products\t2025-05-13T11:22:03.208Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18321\tNRW_DS116344\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.220Z\t753\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater Gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) Species Survey Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333434\tThe gwyniad (Coregonus lavaretus) population of Llyn Tegid has been considered for a number of years to be threatened by deteriorating environmental conditions, especially those associated with eutrophication. This dataset contains species data from the results of projects established to carry out translocation, hydroacoustic surveys and measurement of environmental variables. This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Atlas. The purpose of this survey work and collation was to develop a generic whitefish sampling protocol suitable for Llyn Tegid and to produce a condition assessment of the population. Data is collected in Llyn Tegid and Llyn Arenig Fawr. All hydroacoustic records are available for site centroid only. Gill netting results are recorded at the site of the cast where information was available.\tHydroacoustic surveys are carried out to detect fish populations. Gill nettings provides information on actual fish species and allows ground-truthing and estimates of the Gwyniad population to be made.\tFor further information see the survey metadata and reports: - 101834  Llyn Tegid Hydroacoustic Surveys and Gwyniad Translocations 2004-2009 - 115217  Llyn Tegid and Llyn Arenig Fawr hydroacoustic and Gwyniad surveys 2012 - 115048  Llyn Tegid Hydroacoustic Surveys and Gwyniad Translocation 2003\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the 17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A] Species data is held in Recorder 6. -  Gwyniad Hydroacoustic and Gill netting survey of Llyn Arenig Fawr 2009 - CCW Science Report 904 CCW000800000000D    -  Gwyniad Hydroacoustic Survey 2003 - 2009 - CCW Science Report 903 - Countryside Council for Wales CCW000800000000C    -  Gwyniad Hydroacoustic and Gill Netting Survey of Llyn Arenig Fawr 2012- CCW Science Report 1013 CCW000800000000I     -  Gwyniad Gill netting survey of Llyn Tegid 2003 - CCW Science Report 597 CCW000800000000E\tTristan Hatton-Ellis, Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18315\tNRW_DS116345\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.966Z\t826\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater River lamprey (Lampetra fluviatilis), Brook lamprey (Lampetra planeri) and Sea lamprey (Petromyzon marinus) Survey Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333435\tRiver lamprey (Lampetra fluviatilis), Brook lamprey (Lampetra planeri), and Sea lamprey (Petromyzon marinus) are designated features of the River Usk and Wye Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). Assessments of species composition, distribution, abundance and size composition of larval lamprey (ammocoetes and transformers) populations in these river are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment. The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of Lampreys in the Usk and Wye Catchments. This collation of lamprey data is made up of data collected by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and the Environment Agency Wales for CCW SAC monitoring reports or CCW Review of Consents reports. The collation includes information about species distribution, composition and abundance for river, brook and sea lampreys which are a designated (Annex II) features within SAC rivers in Wales. The majority of the data was recorded at the genus level and no distinction was made between river and brook lampreys. However for some historical data no distinction was made between sea and river/brook lampreys and these were recorded at the family level. This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Atlas.\tLamprey populations and condition assessments have previously been carried out by CCW in 2003 and 2005. The field survey was conducted in 2012 by Environment Agency staff using the standard electrofishing methodology. A condition assessment is made using the JNCC Common Standards Monitoring Guidance for Freshwater Fauna. Quantitative quadrat surveys were carried out in both optimal (very silty) and sub-optimal (more rocky) habitats. Electric fishing techniques were used inside a 1m2 quadrat to draw lamprey out of the silt rather than stunning and trapping them within the silt. Sampling of optimal habitats quadrats was done three times whilst sub-optimal habitats were sampled once. The original data contains positive and negative samples. Confidence in this dataset is high.\tFor further information see the survey metadata: 107902 SAC Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring data for the Rivers Wye and Usk (2007) 107907 SAC Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring for the Rivers Tywi, Teifi and Cleddau (2004-2005) 107949 SAC Monitoring: Lamprey in the River Dee (2004)  112571 Lamprey Habitat Assessement in the River Usk 1999 115168 SAC Monitoring: Lamprey in the River Usk (2011) 115378 SAC Monitoring: Lamprey Monitoring for the Afon Cleddau (2012) 114807 SAC Monitoring: Lamprey in the River Dee (2011)  114970 SAC Monitoring: Lamprey in the River Tywi (2011)\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the 17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A] Species data held in Recorder 6: - Lamprey Monitoring, Wye and Usk Catchments 2005 - CCW - Report no. 41 CCW0002800000007 - Lamprey Monitoring, Tywi, Teifi and Cleddau Catchments 2004 - CCW - RoC Report no. 7 CCW0002800000008 - Lamprey Monitoring, Dee Catchment 2004 - CCW - RoC Report No. 18 CCW0002800000009 - Lamprey Habitat Assessment in the River Usk 1998 - CCW - Report No. 365 CCW000280000000C - Lamprey Monitoring, River Usk 2011 - CCW – Science Report No. 11/8/6 CCW000800000000M - Lamprey Monitoring, Cleddau Catchments 2012 - CCW - CCW Staff Science Report No. 13/8/1 CCW000800000000O - Lamprey Monitoring, Dee Catchment 2011 - CCW - Contract Science Report 975 CCW0008000000008 - Lamprey Monitoring, Tywi Catchment 2011 - CCW - Staff Science Report 11/8/5 CCW000800000000B\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader of Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:35:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18324\tNRW_DS116346\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.355Z\t887\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater Shad Monitoring Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333436\tThe Rivers Wye, Usk and Tywi are designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) under the EC 92/43/EEC Habitats Directive for several features including twaite shad (Alosa fallax) and allis shad (Alosa alosa). Shads are migratory members of the Clupeidae (herring family) that ascend rivers to spawn in late spring. The populations of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and issues surrounding water quality. In the UK, significant populations of shad spawn in the Rivers Severn, Usk, Wye and Tywi. The River Wye, Usk and Tywi have been designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for shads and other features including salmon, lampreys and bullhead. However, it is not certain whether a breeding population of allis shad still exists. The former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), and now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) is responsible for monitoring the condition of shad populations within SACs and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within these designated sites. The purpose of this data capture was to assist with the assessment of Favourable Conservation Status along the Wye, Usk and Tywi to supplement the main shad spawning distribution condition assessment for these rivers. This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Atlas.  Detailed information about each of the survey data can be found under their individual metadata entries.\tEnvironment Agency Wales (EAW) and Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), recorded shad egg survey data are available for the River Wye Usk and Tywi.  A minimum of 10 kick samples were undertaken at each site with the number of eggs found in each kick recorded. If in total 10 shad eggs were recorded the survey was terminated. However, if less than 10 shad eggs were recorded the kick sampling continued either until over 10 eggs were found or a maximum of 25 kick samples had been undertaken. Confidence in this dataset is high.\tFor further information see the survey metadata:  - 107792  SAC Monitoring: Shad Monitoring data for Rivers Wye, Usk and Tywi (2006)  - 112727  SAC Monitoring: Twaite (Alosa fallax) and Allis (Alosa alosa) Shad egg survey of the River Usk (2010-2011)  - 114673  SAC Monitoring: Shad Egg Survey on the River Wye (2011)   - 115312  SAC Monitoring: Afon Tywi Shad Egg Survey (2012)  - 115294  SAC Monitoring: Shad Egg Survey River Usk and River Wye (2012)\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  [A] Species data is held in Recorder 6. -  Shad Monitoring, Usk, Wye \u0026amp; Tywi Catchments 2006 - CCW - Report no.40 CCW0002800000005. -  Shad Egg Survey River Usk 2010 - CCW - Report no.10/8/1 CCW000280000000E. -  Shad Egg Survey River Usk 2011 - CCW - Report no.11/8/3 CCW0008000000009. -  Shad Egg Survey River Wye 2011 - CCW - Report no. 11/8/4 CCW0008000000008.  -  Shad egg Survey Afon Tywi 2012 - CCW - Report No. 12/8/4 CCW000800000000P. -  Shad Egg Survey, River Usk and River Wye, 2012 - CCW - Report No. 13/8/3 CCW000800000000Q.\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:35:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18309\tNRW_DS116347\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.913Z\t307\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGreat Crested Newt Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333437\tThis dataset comprises of Natural Resources Wales (NRWs) monitoring data for Great Crested Newts on Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales, where Great Crested Newts are a special feature. It also includes records of other amphibians. This dataset forms part of the Wales-wide Common Standards Monitoring. Currently the monitoring covers three sites in North East Wales: -  Johnstown Newt Sites SAC -  Deeside and Buckley Newt Sites SAC -  Halkyn Mountain/Mynydd Helygain SAC The aim of this data capture was to assess the condition of designated SAC features based on site-specific performance indicators, and to record aspects of their management, both as an aid to management decisions and for reporting at a variety of scales.  The reports also include collations of historical records however, these are not included here as they are not owned by NRW and are therefore omitted from this published dataset.\tSlightly different methodologies were used depending on the site. At Johnstown, and Deeside and Buckley Special Areas of Conservation sites were monitored for the presence of Great Crested Newts eggs, larvae and adults. During the initial monitoring in 2003 all ponds were visited once, during June, where visual inspections took place for Great Crested Newts eggs, lying on substrates near the shoreline. Other bird and fish species were also noted. The main assessment in 2003 consisted of site visits to all ponds at least once between August and September to record presence of larvae and eggs using visual observation, bottle trapping, torch surveying and netting. Environmental conditions within the ponds and other birds and fish species were also recorded. At Halkyn Mountain Special Area of Conservation condition assessments were undertaken where 45 ponds were surveyed where Great Crested Newts had previously been recorded, for eggs, larvae, juveniles and other environmental data.\tThere are two datasets which are omitted from the publically available data, as these are restricted and third party owned. Restrictions apply to the following dataset held in Recorder 6:  - Various - Historical Newt Surveys at Johnstown SAC and Deeside and Buckley SAC - Various - Historical Newt Surveys at Halkyn Mountain SAC\t[A]  Species Data held in Recorder 6: - CCW - HQ Terr - Main Assessment of Great Crested Newts at Johnston and Deeside \u0026amp; Buckley SAC CCW000630000000E    - CCW - HQ Terr - Initial Newt Surveys at Johnstown SAC and Deeside \u0026amp; Buckley SAC CCW0004800000001    - CCW - HQ Main Assessment of Great Crested Newts at Halkyn Mountain SAC CCW000480000000E      The following datasets are sensitive and restricted from public access. - Various - Historical Newt Surveys at Johnstown SAC and Deeside \u0026amp; Buckley SAC - Various - Historical Newt Surveys at Halkyn Mountain SAC  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tLiz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW  Johnstown, and Deeside and Buckley Special Areas of Conservation. - Sites were monitored by contractor Jan Wilkinson. At Halkyn Mountain Special Area of Conservation sites were monitored by contractor Young Associates.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:27:08.849Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18325\tNRW_DS116350\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.561Z\t338\tValid\tEditable\tMarine data from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Technical Support (Research and Monitoring) Contracts in Wales\tData morol Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) Cymorth Technegol (Ymchwil a Monitro) - Contractau yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333530\tThis dataset is comprised of a collection of marine species sightings collated since 1993 from marine surveys, technical support contracts and research, and monitoring contracts. This dataset is currently maintained and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW). The surveys focus on benthic habitats (often referred to as \u0027biotopes\u0027) in intertidal, inshore (typically within 3 nm) and subtidal areas. Original copies of all the surveys are now held in NRW\u0027s Marine Recorder database. The purpose of this data capture was to aid coastal zone and sea-use management and to contribute to the identification of areas of important marine natural heritage sites. This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Marine Recorder which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway.  Only a redacted version of this data is published and available under the Open Governance Licence and available for public use, see Limitation on Public Access and Use Constraints\tThis dataset covers the whole of Wales. The individual contract surveys, that this dataset is made up of, were collected according to different methodologies. These include methods described in the Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) and Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) marine monitoring techniques described in CCW Contract Science Report No: 380.\t\t[A] All species data is stored in Marine Recorder   This data is now available on the NBN Gateway\tMonica Jones, Data Services Team Leader, EPP, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-12-23T11:16:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18319\tNRW_DS116351\t2024-05-28T12:45:28.261Z\t265\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSevern Estuary additional samples taken from Phase 1 intertidal surveys (2002)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333531\tThis datasets contains the results of additional sampling taken place during phase 1 intertidal surveys around the Severn Estuary. Marine Nature conservation Review (MNCR) littoral habitat forms were completed, and infauna and Particle Size Analysis (PSA) were collected but never worked up due to resource constraints.\tThis data was collated and entered into Marine Recorder.\t\t[A] All species data is stored in Marine Recorder  2002 CCW Severn Estuary intertidal Phase 1 survey  MRCCW30000000045\tInternal Custodian:  Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environment Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-05-16\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:35.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18312\tNRW_DS116352\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.925Z\t803\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Non Native Species (NNS) records from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Monitoring Research and Ad-hoc Sightings (extracted from Marine Recorder)\tCofnodion o rywogaethau Estron Morol sydd wedi deillio o waith ymchwil monitro Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) ac achosion ad hoc o’u gweld (tynnwyd o Marine Recorder)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136333532\tThis dataset comprises of marine surveys undertaken by the Natural Resources Wales (NRW) (and former Countryside Council for Wales) for Marine non-native species, currently held in Marine Recorder. These datasets have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway. The purpose of this data capture was to collate and monitor the spread of non-native species in Wales. The surveys represent on-going non-native monitoring, sightings and ad-hoc records of invasive and non-native species supplied to NRW or noted outside of other research and monitoring work. This published dataset is kept separate from other species survey data provided by NRW, to allow it to be updated more often. This data will contribute to the information already available in the UK non native species portal.\tIncidental records taken as part of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Phase 1 Intertidal Survey were recorded as part of a timed count. The surveys related to invasive species around Welsh marinas are part of an on-going rapid assessments for presence/absence of marine Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) in Welsh marinas. Assessments were conducted by visual inspection of the entire marina and associated structures from the surface followed by snorkel surveys where non-natives were observed or where native biota composition suggested that marina habitat was amenable to non-native colonisation. Some surveys (such as those monitoring Chinese Mitten Crabs include both ad-hoc recording and fyke netting techniques.\tPlease see the following metadata entries for further information about each survey within this published dataset: -  87120 Non Native Species Data - Sargassum muticum around Wales - 118982 2016 NRW towed video survey North Angelsey INNS \u0026amp; Sabellaria - 114771 Across Wales Marine Non-Native Species Survey around Welsh Marinas ( extracted from Marine Recorder ) - 115636 The Feasibility of the Eradication of the Non Native Species Devil\u0027s Tongue Weed (Grateloupia turuturu) in Wales - Milford Haven Survey July 2013 - 115887 Comprehensive reassessment of Non Native Species in Welsh marinas - 116496/116497 Ad Hoc sightings of Invasive Non Native Species (INNS) chinese mitten crab\tNon Native Species Data - Sargassum muticum around Wales [A] Data is archived under Project No. 151, media no\u0027s. 230 and 485 (File paths: K:\\Archive\\Media201to300\\Media 230 and K:\\Archive\\Media 401to500\\Media 485) [B] Species Data is held in Marine Recorder: 1998-ongoing CCW Sargassum muticum records for Wales MRCCW30000000047  Across Wales Marine Non-Native Species Survey around Welsh Marinas  [C] Data is archived as project 321 and media 1269 (File path K:\\Archive\\Media 1201to1300\\Media 1269) [D] Species data is stored in Marine Recorder 2011 CCW/SOS Across Wales Non-Native Species Survey MRCCW31300000002.  Comprehensive reassessment of Non Native Species in Welsh marinas [E]  Species data is held in Marine Recorder: 2014 NRW Comprehensive reassessment of NNS in Welsh marinas MRCCW32900000002 [F]  Currently stored at Z:\\Achive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_Drive\\Science\\MFSG\\MarineRecorder\\MarineRecorderData\\MRCCW329_BishopsGroup_MBA\\MRCCW329_NBNDATA.mdb   [G] This data is now available on the NBN Atlas [H] Also available as a GSI layers: X:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\tInternal Custodian: Jennie Jones, Officer 2 (Marine Environmental Assessment Officer), Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-06-20\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-11-12T16:22:08.148Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18308\tNRW_DS116441\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.930Z\t656\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Boundaries\tFfiniau Ardaloedd Draenio Mewnol (ADM)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343431\tContains the boundaries of all 14 Internal Drainage District\u0027s within wales. Mapped for the purposes of the management of Flood Defences, Water Levels and Flood Risk. Identifies the area which the Board is responsible for preventing flooding, managing water levels and flood defences. Digitised as polygons.\tContains data for North and South Internal Drainage District\u0027s (IDDs). North IDDs digitised from old paper records which date from the Gwynedd River Authority days. South IDDs digitised from old paper maps.  NRW has submitted a proposal to Welsh Government to alter the boundaries of 8 Internal Drainage Districts. This is pending approval from Welsh Government. The current data we can provide does not include these boundary changes.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Boundaries.lyr\tGIS Service Desk South IDDs - Louise Harris, GIS/Engineering Technician, Spatial Data, KSP Directorate, NRW North IDDs - James West, Advisor- IDB Transition, Flood Risk Strategy, NRW       19/09/2019 Latest info from James West to Data Distribution is that NRW are still waiting for WG to draft an Order confirming the Scheme submitted to them in Feb 2018 to alter the boundaries of 8 (out of 10) IDDs. Until WG do this data will remain unchanged. Metadata will need to be updated when the new boundaries are submitted Contacted James West July 2022.The data on the IDD boundaries Dataset (on the X Drive) is still current. NRW is still awaiting the authorisation of our proposed (very minor) boundary changes we submitted to WG in Feb 2018\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2008-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-23T11:18:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18314\tNRW_DS116442\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.909Z\t1912\tValid\tEditable\tAncient Woodland Inventory\tRhestr Coetiroedd Hynafol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343432\tThis dataset comprises of the boundaries of Ancient Woodland sites in Wales. Each site is categorised as either Ancient Semi Natural Woodland (ASNW), Restored Ancient Woodland Site (RAWS), Plantation on Ancient Woodland Site (PAWS) or Ancient Woodland Site of Unknown Category (AWSU).   Initially all woods greater than 2 ha shown on the Ordnance Survey (OS) 1:25 000 1st Series maps surveyed between 1880 and 1960 were considered. Evidence as to which of these were ancient was provided by presence on the earlier 19th century OS 1st Edition Maps (surveyed 1805 - 1873; scale 1:63 360). The general presumption was that woodland on 1800s maps was ancient, unless there was other evidence that the wood originated between 1600AD and 1800. Supportive indications of ancient status included the wood\u0027s name, its situation in the landscape, and the nature of both the surrounding pattern of enclosure and the pattern of boundaries within the wood. Where available, field survey data such as the presence of indicator species, or other historical maps and documents were also used. In mountainous areas such as Wales the first edition Ordnance Survey 1 inch maps were hard to interpret because the heavy hatching used to indicate steep slopes tended to obscure tree symbols.  The 2021 update to the ancient woodland inventory includes changes to the 2011 version resulting from the provision of additional evidence presented to NRW through public enquiries. This includes private estate maps, tithe maps, management records and photographs. The evidence has been considered by a panel of NRW experts and the inventory amended if the evidence indicates that a site has had another land use for a significant period, i.e. woodland is absent from the site in two consecutive epochs of OS historic maps, or there has been significant ground disturbance to the site, e.g. quarrying, mining or development. Amendments include removal of sites from the inventory, adjustments to boundaries or changes to the ancient woodland category of sites.     The 2011 version remained static until it was replaced by the 2021 version. The 2021 version is being updated at least annually as new evidence becomes available.  An older 2004 version of the dataset also exists - please see metadata for dataset number NRW_DS125363 - Ancient Woodland Inventory for Wales 2004 (AWI2004) https://metadata.naturalresources.wales/geonetwork/srv/api/records/NRW_DS125363?language\u003deng-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn crynhoi ffiniau safleoedd Coetir Hynafol yng Nghymru. Mae pob safle yn cael ei chategoreiddio fel naill ai Coetir Hynafol Lled-Naturiol (ASNW), Safle Coetir Hynafol wedi’i Adfer (RAWS), Planhigfa ar Safle Coetir Hynafol (PAWS) neu Safle Coetir Hynafol Anhysbys (AWSU). Yn gyntaf ystyriwyd pob coetir sy\u0027n fwy na 2 ha a ddangosir ar fapiau Cyfres 1af yr Arolwg Ordnans (OS) 1:25 000 a archwiliwyd rhwng 1880 a 1960. Darparwyd tystiolaeth o ba rai o\u0027r rhain oedd yn hynafol gan eu presenoldeb ar y mapiau cynharaf o’r 19eg ganrif, Mapiau OS Argraffiad 1af (archwiliwyd 1805-1873; graddfa 1:63 360). Y rhagdybiaeth gyffredinol oedd bod coetiroedd ar fapiau o’r 1800au yn hynafol, oni bai bod tystiolaeth arall fod y coed yn tarddu o rhwng 1600 OC a 1800. Roedd arwyddion ychwanegol o statws hynafol yn cynnwys enw\u0027r coetir, ei leoliad yn y dirwedd, a natur y patrwm o amgáu o’i gwmpas a phatrwm ffiniau’r coetir . Lle’r oeddent ar gael, defnyddiwyd data arolwg maes, megis presenoldeb rhywogaethau dangosol, neu fapiau a dogfennau hanesyddol eraill hefyd. Mewn ardaloedd mynyddig fel Cymru roedd mapiau 1 fodfedd argraffiad cyntaf yr Arolwg Ordnans yn anodd eu dehongli gan fod y llinellau trymion a ddefnyddiwyd i nodi llethrau serth yn tueddu i guddio symbolau coed.  Cydnabyddiaeth Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThe areas of ancient woodland with non-native trees planted on them, termed Plantations on Ancient Woodland Sites (PAWS), and those areas of ancient semi-natural woodland (ASNW) were identified and delimited by using the latest Ordnance Survey maps and field survey records. The area of each was measured using a dot grid overlay on flat projections of the 1:25 000 1st series map. The results were presented as a series of reports and maps by administrative regions (counties). As new information has been received from surveys and ad hoc informants, the initial county inventories have been revised by adding or deleting sites, or by changing the classification of the growing stock.   The Ancient Woodland Inventory (AWI) identifies woodlands that have had a continuous woodland cover for centuries. Studies show that these woodlands are typically of higher nature conservation value than those that have developed recently. In the 1980s the former Nature Conservancy Council instigated the AWI, which attempted to list all ancient woodlands in Britain. A baseline date of 1600 AD was adopted because reasonable maps were available from this time (in England, at least). 1600 AD however, was an arbitrary date, and there was no clear ecological cut-off.   The AWI was always considered to be provisional and subject to refinement, and never a definitive listing of the ancient, or the \u0027best\u0027 woods.  Between 2010 and 2011 Forestry Commission Wales (FCW) and Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) commissioned a revision of the AWI in Wales comparing the existing AWI and National Forest Inventory with the Epoch 1: 1st County Series Ordnance Survey maps in terms of identification of ancient woodland. Epoch 1 County Series maps provide a more reliable and comprehensive baseline than the AWI.  Moreover, the Epoch 1 maps incorporate numerous practical advantages over the AWI in terms of their ease of use, accuracy and level of detail.\tThe maps combined with new methodology have provided a more accurate inventory which has resulted in an additional 33,000 hectares being identified as Ancient Woodland of which 5,000 is on the WGWE. Categories of Ancient Woodland identified on the AWI map: Ancient Semi-Natural Woodland (ASNW) – broadleaf woodlands comprising of mainly native tree and shrub species which have been in existence for over 400 years.\tX:\\Physical\\Forestry\\Ancient Woodland Inventory 2011.lyr   X:\\Physical\\Forestry\\Ancient Woodland Inventory 2021.lyr\tCustodian: Chris Tucker, Specialist Advisor: Technical Forestry (Resilience)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-04-09\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/1105/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_ANCIENT_WOODLAND_INVENTORY_2021\t2025-12-19T12:02:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18305\tNRW_DS116443\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.467Z\t408\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPearl Mussel Distribution for Habitats Directive (Council Directive 92/43/EEC) Reporting under Article 17\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343433\tThis dataset comprises of a collation of records of pearl mussels from Welsh rivers.  This dataset is a compilation of numerous records from different sources complied for Habitats Directive Article 17 reporting, related to freshwater pearl mussels Margaritifera margaritifera. Every six years, Member States of the European Union are required (by Article 17 of the Directive) to report on implementation of the Habitats Directive. The reporting format set by the European Commission requires a separate analysis for each species and each habitat in each biogeographic region which that country covers.  This dataset comprises of Environment Agency and Countryside Council for Wales surveys.  The aim of this collation has been to ensure that the 10 km distribution maps on the NBN accurately reflect the known position of this species. Records have been compiled with this in mind and hence no attempt has been made to collate every single record for each 10km square within the designated time period.\tSurveys have been undertaken as part of monitoring programmes and Wales-wide distribution surveys. Records are taken from CCW’s biological database and the Environment Agency (Wales) GIS system and relate to in-house and contracted surveys of Welsh rivers carried out in the past eight years. Surveys have been undertaken as part of monitoring programmes and Wales-wide distribution surveys. The majority of records in this dataset are collated with six or eight figure grid-references, although where surveys have reported on individual river sections then four-figure references may have been used. The dataset represents the distribution of the pearl mussel across Wales during the data capture period.\tMost have an exact date but some are in the following format MM-YYYY or YYYY only.\t[A]  This data is available on NBN Gateway [B] Collation of records extract from numerous databases and ad hoc records.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tMike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18299\tNRW_DS116444\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.363Z\t298\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater Fish ad hoc Records for Habitats Directive (Council Directive 92/43/EEC) reporting under Article 17\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343434\tFreshwater fish species records collected by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff, contractors or members of the public. N.B. this dataset is an interim upload to allow Article 17 reporting to be completed. The data are being entered into Marine Recorder and a regular export will be carried out once this is complete. This export will replace this dataset. There are numerous reasons for this data capture. This dataset is a compilation of numerous records from different sources compiled for Habitats Directive Article 17 reporting, related to freshwater fish species. Every six years, Member States of the European Union are required (by Article 17 of the Directive) to report on implementation of the Habitats Directive. The reporting format set by the European Commission requires a separate analysis for each species and each habitat in each biogeographic region which that country covers.  This dataset is a collation of records that constitutes a gap filling exercise rather than a coherent dataset in itself. Consequently it should not be viewed as being spatially or temporally representative. However, it may be used in combination with other data to make assessments of the range and / or occurrence of relevant species.  We have high confidence in the taxonomic quality of the records.\tThere were numerous methods of data capture including formal site condition assessments, monitoring through surveys, and a compilation of ad hoc records.  This dataset includes records collected by various methods, including hydroacoustic monitoring, netting, electrofishing and visual surveys. Records come from various sources, including members of the public. These records in particular are normally validated with photographic or video evidence. Survey effort varies greatly and the dataset should not be assumed to be statistically representative. National Grid References (NGRs) have been reported to the highest level of accuracy, where available. In most cases this is 6 figures or more.\tAll records are from the period 2000-2012. Most have an exact date but some are in the following format MM-YYYY or YYYY only.\t[A]  This data is available on NBN Atlas [B]  Collation of records extract from numerous databases and ad hoc records.\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:28.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18303\tNRW_DS116445\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.375Z\t772\tValid\tEditable\tEnd of life vehicles - Authorised Treatment Facilities - Public Register\tCerbydau ar Ddiwedd eu Hoes - Cyflesusterau Trin Awdudodedig - Cofrestr Gyhoeddus\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343435\tPermitted End-of-Life Vehicle (ELV) sites that can meet the required standards in the ELV Directive for de-polluting vehicles. Authorised sites can have access to the DVLA Certificate of Destruction (CoD) scheme.-Safleoedd cerbydau sydd wedi dod i ddiwedd eu hoes sy\u0027n gallu bodloni\u0027r safonau gofynnol yn y Gyfarwyddeb Cerbydau sydd wedi dod i Ddiwedd eu Hoes ar gyfer dadlygru cerbydau. Gall safleoedd awdurdodedig gael mynediad at gynllun Tystysgrif Ddinistrio\u0027r DVLA. Nid yw hepgoriad o\u0027r set ddata hon o anghenraid yn golygu nad yw safle\u0027n bodloni safonau\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Cerbydau sydd wedi dod i Ddiwedd eu Hoes ar gyfer Cyfleuster Triniaeth Awdurdodedig. Gallai hepgoriad olygu\u0027r canlynol:  - nid yw gweithredwr y safle am geisio achrediad am fynediad at gynlluniau achredu ffurfiol trwy asesiad Cyfarwyddeb Cerbydau sydd wedi dod i Ddiwedd eu Hoes, neu  - nid yw\u0027r asiantaeth wedi asesu\u0027r safle hwn eto, neu  - mae\u0027n bosibl bod y safle wedi cael ei asesu ac wedi methu â bodloni\u0027r safonau gofynnol er efallai bod y safle\u0027n addas i ddibenion eraill.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tOmission from this dataset does not necessarily mean that a site does not meet the End of Life Vehicle (ELV) Directive standards for an Authorised Treatment Facility (ATF). Omission could mean:   - the Site Operator does not wish to seek accreditation for access to formal accreditation schemes through ELV Directive assessment, or  - the Agency may not yet have assessed this site, or  - the site may have been assessed and failed to meet the required standards even though the site may be fit for other purposes\t\tData is extracted from spread sheets stored in Local Offices\tData Custodian: Lisa McDonald SE Ops / National Waste Audit Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-11-17\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2638\t2025-11-17T17:56:02.206Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18304\tNRW_DS116446\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.469Z\t1517\tValid\tEditable\tRiver Levels Application Programming Interface (API)\tLefelau Afonydd Arlein\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343436\tThis dataset covers monitoring data that is updated on our systems on a minimum of a daily update cycle. This is increased during times of flooding etc. Measurements of the height (m) of water in a river, lake or coastal site taken using automatic field devices, are given usually every 15 minutes. Information is available for NRW river monitoring sites throughout Wales, as well as some reservoirs and coastal sites.-APIs Lefelau Afonydd (Data Agored) – gwybodaeth am lefelau afonydd ar nifer o orsafoedd mesur ar draws Cymru.\tReadings are transferred via telemetry to internal and external. This data may be transferred to these systems or users at different intervals varying, for example, from once per day during normal conditions to several times per day during a flood event. This data is retrieved automatically and is unvalidated.  This data should not be stored for more than 1 calendar year.   NRW cannot guarantee the accuracy or timeliness of the data. It should not be used in applications that require quality controlled data.\t\tTelemetry system River levels online database\tCustodian: Alison Hanson Hydromentry and Telemetry\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-06-30\tpublication\tnull\thttps://api-portal.naturalresources.wales/products\t2025-05-13T11:29:24.436Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18301\tNRW_DS116456\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.375Z\t487\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Watercourses\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343536\tAll former Internal Drainage Board(IDB)/Internal Drainage District(IDD) maintained watercourses also known as Reens, Ditches, Brooks and Drains are mapped in linear form in this vector dataset. Data collected and mapped for the purpose of water level management, flood defence maintenance and planning operations/work programming. Identifies watercourses managed for water voles, priority/critical watercourses which must be maintained and watercourses designated as \\urban\\\". Urban watercourses are those in urban areas which are given priority for Health \u0026amp; Safety and Flood Risk reasons.\"\tNorth IDDs – Digitised from old paper maps dating back to the Gwynedd River Authority days South IDDs - Digitised from old paper maps\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Watercoureses.lyr\tCustodian: Spatial Data Team  South IDDs - Louise Harris North IDDs - James West\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18298\tNRW_DS116457\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.375Z\t252\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Sluices\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343537\tSluices within the Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge, Lower Wye and Powysland Internal Drainage Districts digitised as point features and mapped for the purpose of flood defence, water level management and asset management. Information recorded includes Top, Base, Winter and Summer water levels, status of the sluice e.g. used or disused, if and when its due for conversion to a modern style sluice, sluice type and material and the watercourses the sluices are located on.\tData collection methods unknown. Points digitised using X and Y coordinates.\t\tX;\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Sluices.lyr\tCustodian: Spatial Data Team  South IDDs - Louise Harris North IDDs - James West\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:31:04.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18300\tNRW_DS116458\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.375Z\t275\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Powerlines\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343538\tPowerlines within Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge IDD mapped in linear form in this vector dataset. Data collected and mapped for health and safety reasons and planning of operations on the Gwent Levels. Contains information on the height of powerlines.\tData collected by operators out in the field by manually drawing the information on 1:10000 scale paper maps. The information was then manually digitised\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Powerlines.LYR\tLouise Harris, GIS/Engineering Technician, Spatial Data, KSP Directorate, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:33.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18297\tNRW_DS116459\t2024-05-28T12:45:27.066Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Pipelines\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343539\tPipelines within Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge IDD mapped in point form in this vector dataset. Data collected and mapped for health and safety reasons and for planning operations. Enables operators to identify the location of pipelines and ensure their safety when maintaining watercourses and flood defences.\tData collected by operators out in the field by manually drawing the information on 1:10000 scale paper maps. The information was then manually digitised\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Pipelines.lyr\tCustodian: Spatial Data Team Louise Harris\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18296\tNRW_DS116460\t2024-05-28T12:45:26.883Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Dangerous Corners\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343630\tDangerous corners of watercourses within Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge mapped in linear form in this vector dataset. Shows dangerous corners for flailing works. Data collected and mapped for planning operations and health and safety reasons.\tData collected by operators out in the field by manually drawing the information on 1:10000 scale paper maps. The information was then manually digitised\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL dangerous corners.lyr\tLouise Harris, Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18343\tNRW_DS116461\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.249Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Safe Locations for Plant\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343631\tSafe locations for plant machinery to be left overnight or longer periods across the Gwent levels. Machines have been known to have fuel siphoned from them and faced other vandalism so to reduce this risk of this  safe locations where machinery can be safely left when unattended and not in use have been mapped.\tData collected by operators out in the field -grid coordinates for locations recorded. The information was then manually digitised using the grid coordinates.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Safe Locations for Plant.lyr\tCustodian: Spatial Data Team, Louise Harris\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:47:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18353\tNRW_DS116462\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.472Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNVC Terrestrial Plant Surveys Coastal Ecologist\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343632\tHeather Lewis is filling in the metadata for this survey- Evidence Report 143.\t\t\t\tHeather Lewis, Coastal Ecologist\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:49:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18348\tNRW_DS116463\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.340Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Trash Screens\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343633\tTrash Screens on watercourses within CWL IDD digitised as point features and mapped for the purpose of flood defence and asset management. Trash screens are a type of fencing used to filter out debris in the path of a waterway. They help keep watercourses free of debris and unwanted elements.\tData collected by operators out in the field by recording coordinates for the locations. Points digitised using X and Y coordinates.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Trash Screens.lyr\tLouise Harris, Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:06:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18346\tNRW_DS116464\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.350Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Lower Wye English Watercourses\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343634\tWatercourses within the English section of Lower Wye Internal Drainage Board now administered by Hoople. These watercourses drain into Lower Wye IDD watercourses within Wales. Mapped in linear form in this vector dataset. Data collected and mapped for the purpose of water level management, flood defence maintenance and planning operations/work programming.\tDigitised from old 1:10000 paper maps\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\LowerWye\\IDD Lower Wye English Watercourses.lyr\tSpatial Data Team, Louise Harris\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:49:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18351\tNRW_DS116465\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.411Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Lower Wye English Boundary\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343635\tBoundary of the English section of the Lower Wye IDD. Mapped for the purposes of the management of Flood Defences, Water Levels and Flood Risk. Identifies the area which the Board is responsible for preventing flooding, managing water levels and flood defences.\tDigitised from old 1:10000 scale paper maps\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\LowerWye\\Lower Wye English Boundary.lyr\tSpatial Data Team - Louise Harris\t\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:49:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18355\tNRW_DS116466\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.540Z\t366\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Pumping Stations\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343636\tPumping Stations within Powysland and Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge IDD\u0027s mapped as point features\t\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Pumping Stations.lyr\tLouise Harris, Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:34:57.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18354\tNRW_DS116467\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.548Z\t279\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Land Drainage Consents\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343637\tLand Drainage Consents within Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge, Lower Wye and Powysland IDDs mapped in the form of polygons in this vector dataset. Mapped for the purpose of Flood Risk Management. Enables the identification of previous Land Drainage Consents and aids in assessing new Land Drainage Consents, in particular producing the site specific conditions.\tData manually digitised at 1:2500 scale from location plans submitted by applicants or related planning application site boundaries\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Land Drainage Consents.lyr\tLouise Harris, Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18347\tNRW_DS116471\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.332Z\t20\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Species in Wales (archived 2018)\tCynefinoedd a Rhywogaethau\u0027r Cynllun Gweithredu Bioamrywiaeth Morol yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343731\tThis archived dataset consists of 32 datasets, and has been superseded by Environment (Wales) Act Section 7: Marine Species as part of the newer legislative requirements. This dataset is associated with the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) which was published back in 1994, and was the UK Governments response to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD), which the UK signed up to in 1992 in Rio de Janeiro. Similarly under Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act (applicable to England and Wales) 2006, it is required that a \u0027list of the living organisms and types of habitat which in the Assembly\u0027s opinion are of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\u0027. The NERC Act in Wales, is being superseded by the Environment Act (Wales) 2016. Under Section 7 of the Environment Act (Wales) These datasets will provide a basis on which to create a biodiversity list to meet the requirements of the Environment Act (Wales) set out under Section 6 and 7, which require biodiversity and resilience of ecosystem duty, and biodiversity lists and duty to take steps to maintain and enhance biodiversity are required, respectively. This includes the publishing of a list of living organisms of principal importance in Wales, for the purpose of maintaining and enhancing biodiversity in relation to Wales. These datasets will contribute towards the identification of marine organisms of principal importance in Wales, and will be kept under review in order to keep the list up-to-date as per Section 7 of the Environment Act (Wales).\tThe data collation included the use of Marine Recorder to query the plot distribution species. The validity of records were checked. Records that were considered doubtful due to the geographic location and known inexperience of the recorder, were checked thoroughly and excluded if not corroborated. This data is historical and should be used with caution. There are better more up to date dataset available such as the Atlas of Marine Mammals of Wales (111813). Data layers within this dataset are updated at different times, usually as and when new data is available and collated.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\BAP Species Archived (Consists of 32 layer files)\tInternal Custodian: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tThird Party Conditional\t2015-11-25\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T13:47:18.166Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18350\tNRW_DS116473\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.349Z\t31\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Habitats in Wales (archived 2018)\tCynefinoedd Morol a blaenoriaeth yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343733\tThis archived dataset consists of 37 datasets, and has been superseded by \u0027Environment (Wales) Act Section 7: Marine Habitats\u0027 as part of the newer legislative requirements.   This dataset is associated with the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UK BAP) which was published back in 1994, and was the UK Government\u0027s response to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD), which the UK signed up to in 1992 in Rio de Janeiro. Similarly under Section 42 of the Natural Environment and Rural Communities (NERC) Act, 2006 a \u0027list of the living organisms and types of habitat which in the Assembly\u0027s opinion are of principal importance for the purpose of conserving biodiversity\u0027 is required.   The NERC Act in Wales, is being superseded by the Environment Act (Wales) 2016. This dataset will provided a basis on which to create a biodiversity list to meet the requirements of the Environment Act (Wales) set out under Section 6 and 7, which require biodiversity and resilience of ecosystem duty, and biodiversity lists and duty to take steps to maintain and enhance biodiversity are required, respectively. This includes the publishing of a list of types of habitats of principal importance in Wales, for the purpose of maintaining and enhancing biodiversity in relation to Wales. This dataset captured will contribute towards the identification of marine habitats of principal importance in Wales, and will be kept under review in order to keep the list up-to-date as per Section 7 of the Environment Act (Wales).   These habitats include: blue mussel beds, Fragile sponge and anthozoan communities, Tide swept channels, Horse mussel beds, Maerl beds, Honeycomb worm reefs, Seagrass beds, Native oyster beds, Intertidal boulder communities, Intertidal mudflats, mud habitats in deep water, Sheltered muddy gravels, subtidal mixed muddy sediments, Estuarine rocky habitats, Peat and clay exposures, Carbonate reefs, Musculus discors beds, Saline lagoons, Coastal Saltmarsh, Sabellaria spinulosa (Ross worm) reefs, Seapens and burrowing megafauna communities and Subtidal sands and gravels.\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap and Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats (where both types of data exist – some layers only have point data).   Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decision made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.\tAll habitat records include a confidence measure. Decisions about how these were applied are documented in the processing notes for each habitats. It is worth noting that some of the habitats are both OSPAR and Section 42, but due to slight differences in descriptions, this might not always be the case. Hence there is a true false field saying whether the data conforms the habitat description.\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\Habitats archived  Consists of 37 datasets\tInternal Custodian: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata custodian: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-12-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-17T13:11:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18352\tNRW_DS116480\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.344Z\t683\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMacrophyte Survey of Welsh Lakes 2015\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343830\tThis project set out to collect, process and supply to Natural Resources Wales (NRW), ecological and environmental data from a network of 19 lake sites across Wales, in support of NRW\u0027s integrated monitoring programme for protected sites (Special Areas of Conservation and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)), the Water Framework Directive, Nitrates Directive, Biodiversity Action Plans and other legislative and policy drivers. In particular the surveys are aimed at informing management and restoration of protected sites and facilitating delivery of River Basin Management Plans.  Using standard methods, lakes were surveyed to assess the species and abundance of aquatic plants growing within and directly around the lake and to measure water clarity, dissolved oxygen and temperature within the lakes.  The results of the aquatic plant surveys are suitable for the purposes of assessing site condition for Habitats Directive standing water features and SSSI status. The lake metrics are applicable for the production of ecological quality ratios from which the lakes may be classified in accordance with the requirements of Water Framework Directive (2000/60/EC).\tThe full description of the survey methods used to collect macrophyte data are detailed in the Joint Nature Conservation Committee publication for the Common Standards Monitoring Guidance for Standing Waters (JNCC 2015). In brief, the plant surveys consisted of four components: a strandline survey of species uprooted and washed to the shore; a survey of the emergent and marginal species; a wader survey of the shallow littoral zone to approximately 1.0 m; and a boat survey encompassing species in open water and extending to the point of maximum colonization. Surveys were carried out at each site on up to four discrete 100 m sections of shoreline which were considered representative of the lake and gave good geographical coverage. To reduce disturbance, a maximum of 25% of the shoreline was surveyed, resulting in fewer than four sections being surveyed at smaller lakes. Where lakes had previously been surveyed, transect locations used in the past were re-surveyed to maximise comparability between surveys. These methods were devised to provide quantitative species-abundance data that can be obtained in a pragmatic and repeatable manner. Additional efforts such as sampling drift line flora were made to record other species which did not occur in any of the survey sections. The survey did not aim to produce a complete species list for the lake.  Dissolved oxygen concentration and temperature profiles were taken at the deepest recorded point of each site on the same dates as the macrophyte surveys. Secchi disc depths were recorded at the time of the macrophyte surveys from the deepest point of all lakes and further measurements taken at each survey section at sites where variability in water clarity was observed. Catchment data, land cover and general lake data that are quoted in the text are taken from the original UK Lakes database (Hughes et al. 2004) and the new UK Lakes administered by CEH (CEH 2017).\tGoldsmith, B., Turner, S., Shilland, E. and Goodrich, S. (2015), Ecological Surveys of Welsh Lakes 2015, NRW Evidence Report No. 145, 86 pp, ENSIS Ltd, London.\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Leafpacs calculator files within folder [Evidence \u0026gt; Freshwater Ecology \u0026gt; Internal Research \u0026gt; Lake Leafpacs data and spreadsheets \u0026gt; ENSIS Surveys 2015] in Microsoft Excel format [Lake_name_LEAFPACS 2 0_metriccalculator_Month_Year] and summary information in MS Excel format [Site_metrics_Summary_2015.xls].\tInternal: Tristan Hatton-Ellis,  Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-02-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18349\tNRW_DS116492\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.334Z\t277\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) Habitat Quality on Castlemartin Range Pembrokeshire (2015)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343932\tThe marsh fritillary Euphydryas aurinia is an Annex II European protected species. The butterfly and its habitats are primary features of the Castlemartin Range Special Site of Scientific Interest (SSSI) in south Pembrokeshire. The metapopulation was discovered in 2003, too late to be included in the Limestone Coast of South West Wales Special Area of Conservation (SAC), but it is included as a key SSSI feature in the SAC Core Management Plan (CMP). Marsh fritillaries exhibit metapopulation dynamics, experiencing regular extinctions and colonisations of suitable patches over time. A large network of suitable sites is essential to maintain their populations in the long term. This survey was commissioned to examine potential marsh fritillary habitat on the Range and to determine the total amount and condition of habitat available for the metapopulation. All survey sites were on MOD Castlemartin Training Area, which in turn lies within the Pembrokeshire Coast National Park. The entire survey area is within the Castlemartin Range SSSI, and most of it is included in the Limestone Cliffs of Southwest Wales SAC.\tNRW supplied the contractor with an outline map of the survey area, identifying approximately 360 hectares of grassland and heathland along the Castlemartin coast. These areas were believed to include all vegetation communities with the potential to support marsh fritillaries on the Range. Larval webs were recorded in the course of the habitat surveys. There was insufficient time to do a systematic transect survey, so these are only \u0027casual\u0027 records. All habitat was mapped following the standard NRW methodology. Land was classed into six categories, according to the presence and abundance of devil\u0027s-bit scabious. A tussocky sward structure is usually considered essential for marsh fritillary larvae, to provide shelter, hibernation and spring basking sites. Nearly all Welsh marsh fritillary populations are found in this type of habitat on damp rhos pasture.\t\tSpecies data is stored in Recorder 6 (attached to the citation of the report) Report is available in PDF Format through the Reports Archive on the DMS Spatial Habitat Maps are being produced by Johnathon Rothwell.\tData Custodian: Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, TEG, NRW Jonathon Rothwell, Terrestrial Mapping Scientist  Metadata Contact: Data services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-10-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18342\tNRW_DS116494\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.170Z\t796\tValid\tEditable\tOpen Access - Other Dedicated Land (CRoW Act)\tMynediad Agored - Tir Penodedig Arall\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343934\tThis dataset shows the area of land that has been dedicated under Section 16 of the CRoW Act by organisations/individuals other than Natural Resources Wales (NRW).   When using this data to understand the extent of Open Access, it should be used in conjunction with Open Land, Open Country, Registered Common Land, Other Statutory Access Land and Other Dedicated Land from the same years.-Mae Deddf Cefn Gwlad a Hawliau Tramwy yn darparu ar gyfer mynediad cyhoeddus ar droed i fathau penodol o dir, yn diwygio\u0027r gyfraith sy\u0027n ymwneud â hawliau tramwy cyhoeddus, yn cynyddu mesurau ar gyfer rheoli a gwarchod Safleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGA) ac yn cryfhau deddfwriaeth gorfodi bywyd gwyllt, wrth ddarparu ar gyfer gwell rheolaeth ar Ardaloedd o Harddwch Naturiol Eithriadol (AHNE). Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn dangos yr ardal o dir sydd wedi\u0027i neilltuo dan adran 16 Deddf Cefn Gwlad a Hawliau Tramwy gan sefydliadau/unigolion ac eithrio CNC. Wrth ddefnyddio\u0027r data hwn i ddeall helaethrwydd tir Mynediad Agored dylid gwneud hynny ar y cyd â Thir Agored 2014, Tir Comin Cofrestredig 2014, Tir Mynediad Statudol Arall a Choedwigoedd Penodedig.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis data was created in 2014 by splitting Dedicated Forests 2014 into two. The original Dedicated Forests 2004 (originally named Public Forests), consisted of all dedicated land and not just forests, so it was decided to split forest dedicated land from all other dedicated land to form Dedicated Forests 2014 and Other Dedicated Land 2014. Land is dedicated in perpetuity.   Additions can be made to this dataset on a rolling basis (registered common land and open country is reviewed on a decadal basis, the last review was in 2014). The CRoW Act applies to England and Wales only.\t\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Other Dedicated Land 2014.lyr\tChris Heaps, Specialist Advisor: Trails and Open Access, Open Access Team / Recreation \u0026amp; Partnerships Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-04-16\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/421/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_OTHER_DEDICATED_LAND\t2025-12-19T12:00:41.215Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18340\tNRW_DS116495\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.130Z\t609\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWoodland Grant Scheme (WGS) Walkers Welcome\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343935\tThe former Forestry Commission now Natural Resources Wales, provide the following grants under the Woodland Grant Scheme for woodland owners who welcome the public into their woods: i) Community Woodland Contribution (CWC) ii) Annual Management Grant (AMG) iii) Woodland Improvement Grant (WIG). This dataset shows the locations of the Walkers Welcome areas which are intended to help inform the public of the woodlands where the owner has agreed to general access.\tWalkers Welcome boundaries are derived from Woodland Grant Scheme (WGS) contracts and have been captured against 1:25,000 (pre 2000) and 1:10,000 (post 2000) OS raster backdrops.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\WGS Walkers Welcome.lyr\tCustodian: GIS and Spatial Services Team..........Keith Harris Jones\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T13:30:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18345\tNRW_DS116496\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.145Z\t615\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of Invasive Non Native Species (INNS) Chinese Mitten Crabs (Eriocheir sinensis) on the River Dee 2007 - 2016\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343936\tThe Chinese mitten crab (Eriocheir sinensis) is registered by the Invasive Species Specialist Group (ISSG) of the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) as one of the top 100 invasive species worldwide. Mitten crabs are a catadromous species, migrating between fresh water and marine environments to reproduce. They spend the majority of their adult life in fresh water habitats, where they mature to reproductive size. Depending on mitten crab population density and habitat suitability, juvenile mitten crabs will migrate large distances up river to mature. The Dee Chinese mitten crab project set out to investigate the population of Chinese mitten crab in the Dee catchment and achieve a number of objectives including awareness raising, trialling and evaluating potential methods to monitor and manage the population and make inferences about the current and future status of the species in the Dee catchment.\tMethods included search days organised at various sites along the River Dee. In addition to this, survey work was undertaken onboard boats in 2013. Volunteers also assisted with fyke netting, megalopa sample collection and data retrieval from data loggers positioned along the Dee.\t\tData is held in Marine Recorder - MRCCW3000000009C 2015 NRW Monitoring of Chinese Mitten Crabs (Eriocheir sinensis) on the River Dee (NNS)  Data and associated project details e.g. images also need to be pulled together and put into Project Number is 467 Media number 1540.\tInternal Custodian: Ben Wray, Marine Monitoring Group NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-02-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18344\tNRW_DS116497\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.211Z\t445\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAd Hoc sightings of Invasive Non Native Species (INNS) chinese mitten crab (Eriocheir sinensis) 2013 to 2014\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343937\tThe Chinese mitten crab (Eriocheir sinensis) is registered by the Invasive Species Specialist Group (ISSG) of the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) as one of the top 100 invasive species worldwide. Mitten crabs are a catadromous species, migrating between fresh water and marine environments to reproduce. They spend the majority of their adult life in fresh water habitats, where they mature to reproductive size. Depending on mitten crab population density and habitat suitability, juvenile mitten crabs will migrate large distances up river to mature. The Dee Chinese mitten crab project set out to investigate the population of Chinese mitten crab in the Dee catchment and achieve a number of objectives including awareness raising, trialling and evaluating potential methods to monitor and manage the population and make inferences about the current and future status of the species in the Dee catchment. This dataset contains a subset of the monitoring data comprising of ad hoc sighting records of Chinese mitten crab surveyed between 2013 and 2014.\tThis dataset is made up of ad hoc sightings and therefore methods vary depending on the record.\t\tData is held in Marine Recorder  MRCCW3000000009C 2015  NRW Monitoring of Chinese Mitten Crabs (Eriocheir sinensis) on the River Dee (NNS)   Data and associated project details e.g. images also need to be pulled together and put into Project Number is 467 Media number 1540.\tInternal Custodian: Ben Wray, Marine Monitoring Group NRW Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-02-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:34.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18337\tNRW_DS116498\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.090Z\t622\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Dee and Bala lake Special Area of Conservation (SAC) population attribute condition assessment for brook, river and sea lamprey (2014)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136343938\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) is responsible for monitoring the condition of  lamprey populations within Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and to ensure the maintenance of favourable conservation status within these designated sites. This is in line with Habitats Directive monitoring and reporting on a 5 yearly cycle. The River Dee and Bala lake is designated under the Habitats Directive as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) for several feature including brook, river and sea lamprey (Lampetra planeri, L. fluviatilis and Petromyzon marinus) The populations of all three species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and issues surrounding water quality. The River Dee and Bala lake SAC holds a significant population of these three lamprey species for Wales and Great Britain.   The aim of this data collection was to contribute towards the assessment of the condition of the shad population in the River Dee \u0026amp; Bala lake SAC as part of the Habitats Directive Report Cycles.  This dataset contains data from the following reporting cycles:  Cycle 1: 1999 – 2005,  Cycle 2: 2006 – 2012,  Cycle 3: 2013 – 2018 (data collection is on-going).\tBetween 1999 and  2013 external contractors undertook electric fishing surveys in autumn, usually once in the six year reporting cycle at 30 – 40 sample sites in the catchment. 2013 onwards: NRW has the in-house skills to undertake the electric fishing surveys and so a smaller autumnal annual sampling programme was set up.  Specific details for survey methods are detailed as follows. Lamprey monitoring protocol: 1.  At each site electrofishing should be undertaken over an area judged to be both optimal and sub-optimal lamprey habitat.  2.  Optimal and sub-optimal habitats for ammocoetes should be sampled with 1m2 quadrats in a 100 m stretch of river at each survey site.   3.  A minimum of three quadrat data points is required if possible within each survey site. The number of sampling points should be recorded on the field survey sheet. 4.  All lampreys captured should be counted, measured to the nearest mm and identified as far as possible in the relevant section of the field sheet to species level in the field using the key of Gardiner (2003).  The identification of lamprey ammocoetes and transformers should be as follows: a. Lampetra (river/brook lamprey) ammocoetes b .Petromyzon (sea lamprey) ammocoetes c. River lamprey transformers d. Petromyzon (sea lamprey) transformers\tFor further information see: - Garrett, HM. 2016. River Dee and Bala lake SAC population attribute condition assessment for brook, river and sea lamprey 2014. NRW Evidence Report No 140.  - Campbell, D. \u0026amp; Williams A.E. (2006). Lamprey survey on the River Dee \u0026amp; tributaries.  EAW report.  APEM Reference EA849. - Potter, L. (2005). Review of Dee Migratory Barriers. EAW report no. NEAT/05/10.\t[A] Report Word \u0026amp; pdf format:  Reports and field note are held internally on the DMS using the following pathway Located: Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting (SMAR) \u0026gt; Freshwater Monitoring \u0026gt;  - Running Waters Habitats Directive Data Cycle 1  - Running Waters Habitats Directive Data Cycle 2 - Running Waters Habitats Directive Data Cycle 3. [B]  Field record sheets:  -Dee lamprey annual survey results 2013 – 2018.xlsx SAMP-753-222  [C]  Data:GIS data: Arc GIS Shape files 10.2.2 Spatial data is stored on NRW Y Drive. Location TBC. [D] Species Data in BIOSYS and Recorder 6\tInternal Custodian: Heather Garrett, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning team \u0026amp; Robert Evans, Senior Environmental Monitoring Officer, (A\u0026amp;R), NRW North Ops area team. Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:20:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18338\tNRW_DS116504\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.112Z\t494\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status of Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail (Vertigo moulinsiana) at Rhos Goch National Nature Reserve (NNR) (2015)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353034\tThis dataset is made up of the results of a survey for Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail (Vertigo moulinsiana) in 2015, confirming it\u0027s presences at Rhos Goch National Nature Reserve (NNR) following it discovery at this location in 2007, making this one of only three sites in Wales for the snail. Desmoulin’s whorl snail Vertigo moulinsiana, despite being a small snail, is the largest Vertigo species, with a shell height up to about 2.6 mm. V. moulinsiana is only known from three localities in Wales; Cors Geirch and Afon Penrhos near Pwllheli on the Llŷn peninsula, and Rhos Goch National Nature Reserve near Hay-on-Wye in Radnorshire. All of the Welsh V. moulinsiana sites are isolated and do not closely resemble the lake and river-side base-rich fens typically associated with the snail at most of its English sites.   The conservation importance of the species has meant its inclusion in various schedules and Red Data lists. It was categorised as Rare (category 3) in the UK Red Data Books, and more recently has been classed as Vulnerable in the recent IUCN-based UK Red List status review. The species is listed on Annex IIa of the European Community Habitats and Species Directive (92/43/EEC) and is also a Welsh Section 42 Species of Principal Importance (replacing the UK BAP priority species in 2006). The purpose of this data capture was to survey for V. moulinsiana, focussing on areas where there was known presence in 2007, and later a wider search for potentially suitable habitat within the NNR and adjoining Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI).\tTray beating was undertaken in dry weather conditions, using a gridded white beating tray measuring approximately 25cm X 33cm was used. Tray beating is a well established technique of beating herbaceous fen vegetation onto a gridded white plastic tray. At selected locations, this allowed approximate V. moulinsiana numbers per unit area to be estimated (6 trays being approximately equivalent to 0.5 m2).  In addition to this: - Dominant vegetation presence was recorded, noting particularly ‘+’ and ‘-‘ V. moulinsiana ‘suitability indicators’ (e.g. Carex sp, Glyceria maxima as ‘+’ indicators and Epilobium sp and Urtica dioica as ‘-‘);  - Degree of site shading by overhead or over-hanging trees and bushes was recorded as a simple % canopy cover where appropriate, as shading can negatively affect the suitability of sites for V. moulinsiana;  - Other potentially important site environmental and management details were recorded e.g. grazing and/or ground poaching, recent cutting, human trampling, pheasant release pens. Other mollusc species present were noted. At four locations, bulk samples of vegetation were removed (chiefly in the open fen area lying on the western margins of the site;  - Where located V. moulinsiana numbers were counted per 6-tray samples and then converted into approximate numbers m-2 with numbers of adult and juvenile snails recorded separately. GPS 12 fig references and digital images were recorded for each main sample point. In addition to these, tray beating was carried out as the surveyor walked around the site to try and locate V. mulinsiana ‘pockets’ that might otherwise be overlooked.\tWilling, M.J. 2016 The status of Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana at Rhos Goch NNR in 2015. NRW Evidence Report No: 157, 20pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tA] The final report in Microsoft Word and Adobe PDF formats. [B] Species records, which are held on the NRW Recorder 6 database CCW000800000001J NRW Contracted Invertebrate Research.\tData Custodian: Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystem Services, NRW Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW  This survey was done by a contactor: MJ Willings on behalf of NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18341\tNRW_DS116505\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.145Z\t352\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver habitat condition assessment using 2013 and 2014 River Habitat Survey (RHS)  data and Common Standards Monitoring guidance for River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC).\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353035\tThis dataset contains the results of a condition assessment of the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC) physical river habitat quality using River Habitat Survey (RHS) and LEAFPAC (river macrophyte) survey data 2012 to 2016. Former Environment Agency RHS database contains welsh data which is now held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  NRW is responsible for monitoring the condition of the river habitat quality on SAC rivers designated for the Ranunculion feature; more formally known as \"Water courses of plain to montane levels with the Ranunculion fluitantis and Callitricho-Batrachion vegetation” (EU habitat code 3260). The RHS \u0026amp; LEAFPAC surveys are conducted as part of the Water Framework Directive (WFD) water body classifications. In addition NRW is required under the Habitats Directive to evaluate the conservation status of this river habitat. Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance recommends that some of this data can be used to assess the following physical habitat attributes: Habitat Modification Score; Riparian zone vegetation naturalness; Large woody debris; Fine sediment; Negative indicators: alien / locally absent species. The Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance sets out eight river habitat quality assessment attributes for the condition of this SAC feature. Specific details for RHS survey methods are detailed in The River Habitat Survey manual produced by the Environment Agency in 2003. Details for the LEAFPAC macrophyte survey method are found in Surveying freshwater macrophytes in rivers Operational instruction 131-07 produced by the Environment Agency in 2008.\tThe data is extracted from BIOSYS the Environment Agency RHS database.\tGarrett HM \u0026amp; Thomas RH. (2016). River habitat condition assessment using 2013 \u0026amp; 2014 RHS data \u0026amp; CSM standards for River Wye SAC. NRW Evidence Report No. 141. 29pp. NRW, Bangor.  Thomas, Rh. 2014. CSM Habitat Structure Assessment for the Habitats Directive Second Monitoring Cycle (2007-2012). Updated version 2 (28/02/14). NRW unpublished guidance. Thomas, Rh. (2013). CSM Habitat Structure Assessment for the Habitats Directive Second Monitoring Cycle (2007-2012).  CCW Internal Note.\t[A] Reports and database exports held internally on the DMS using the following pathway: Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting (SMAR) \u0026gt; freshwater monitoring Running waters Habitats Directive data Cycle 3.  [B] Spatial data currently on W drive Heather Garrett Wye RHS but waiting for guidance to move to Y drive. [C] Species data held on BIOSYS \u0026amp; Environment Agency RHS database.\tInternal Custodian: Heather Garret, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team, NRW Melissa Lacan, Senior Env Monitoring Officer (Biology), Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R   Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-03-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18339\tNRW_DS116508\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.134Z\t407\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Monitoring Pen Llyn a’r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2013\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353038\tPen Llyn a’r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) is designated for its estuaries and their constituent mudflats, sandflats and reefs; large shallow inlets and bays with intertidal Zostera beds; populations of the shingle-dwelling amphipod Pectenogammarus, piddocks in intertidal clay and lower shore boulder communities and reefs, particularly the biogenic reefs of Sabellaria alveolata.  These features are monitored by ASML on behalf of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), which has a statutory duty to produce advice under Regulation 35 (formerly Regulation 33) of the Habitats Regulations 1994. This dataset is a compilation of a number of intertidal research and monitoring programmes conducted in 2013.  This included, intertidal surveying of the honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata at West Afon Dwyfor and Llandanwg; the surveying of the population status of Pectenogammarus populations (LGS.Pec), a scarce, shingle shore specific amphipod found near Pwllheli; sediment analyses of biotopes of the Dyfi and Glaslyn/Dwyryd estuaries were conducted, and subsequently the Glaslyn/Dwyryd infaunal samples were analysed; and finally a species list was created using fixed monitoring quadrats in the upper, mid and lower shore at Porth Oer, the results of which were compared to 2012 survey results.\tA number of surveying techniques were used: - Intertidal reefs, characterised by biogenic reef formed by the honeycomb reef worm Sabellaria alveolata were surveyed at West Afon Dwyfor and Llandanwg with multiple quantitative quadrats.   - Pectenogammarus populations (LGS.Pec), a scarce, shingle shore specific amphipod, was surveyed near Pwllheli and its abundance compared to previous years. - The sediment biotopes of the Dyfi \u0026amp; Glaslyn/Dwyryd estuaries were surveyed both qualitatively and quantitatively respectively.   - The Dyfi sediment biotopes were assessed qualitatively in situ and quantitatively.   - The Glaslyn/Dwyryd quantitative infaunal samples were analysed and a range of univariate community statistics were calculated. - The fixed monitoring quadrats in the upper, mid and lower shore stations at Porth Oer were surveyed and compared to 2012’s results.  A species list was produced for the fixed monitoring quadrats.\tMercer, T.S. 2016.  Intertidal monitoring, Pen Llyn a’r Sarnau SAC August 2013.  NRW Evidence Report No. 58, pp 68 + x, Aquatic Survey \u0026amp; Monitoring Ltd. Harehope Quarry, Co. Durham.\tThe data are archived as The Project Number is 411, Media number 1525   This archive is also maintained on backed-up server based storage at NRW headquarters.   H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid PLAS\\2013 ASML PLAS\tInternal Custodian - Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:36.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18336\tNRW_DS116511\t2024-05-28T12:46:38.016Z\t523\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMacrofaunal Community Monitoring on Saline Lagoons across Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353131\tShallow coastal lagoons are designated as a priority Annex I Habitat under the EC Habitats Directive. This dataset includes the infaunal community and species populations and sediment grain distribution data for five Welsh lagoons. Infaunal communities of three lagoons (Cemlyn, Anglesey; Pickleridge, Pembrokeshire; and Morfa Gwyllt, Gwynedd) have been annually monitored since 2006 and two lagoons (Neyland and Carew, Pembrokeshire) have been monitored since 2011. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the ecological health of invertebrate communities and population dynamics across lagoon environments, in order to infer the health and functionality of the ecosystems at each lagoon.\tAnnual quantitative macrofauna and sediment sampling was conducted using a hand held core or Ekman grab for surveys conducted at Cemlyn, Morfa Gwyllt and Pickleridge between 2006 and 2013. In recent years, infauna and sediment winter sampling has also been undertaken at Neyland Weir Pool and Carew Mill Pond (2011, 2012 and 2013). At all lagoons other than Morfa Gwyllt, a fixed sampling station design were chosen to capture the range of potential benthic variation that might occur due to point sources of seawater and freshwater input and to minimize the risk of widespread disturbance caused by destructive sampling. At the smaller Morfa Gwyllt site no potential input gradients were likely and so a randomised sample plot was selected. All samples were sieved over a 0.5 mm mesh in situ, preserved in 4 percent formalin solution and then transferred to a laboratory where biota were sorted and enumerated to the lowest possible taxonomic level.\tSamples were analysed by Fugro Marine (formally ERT Ltd) for all years except 2012, where APEM ltd undertook the analysis. Both contractors undertook work in line with National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control guidance.   For further information see: Green, M \u0026amp; Camplin, M (2013) Saline lagoons: An Across Wales Macrofaunal Community Monitoring Report. NRW Evidence Report Series. Report Number ? Natural Resources Wales, Bangor, Gwynedd, Wales.\tData is currently being entered into Marine Recorder by a contractor – Marine Ecosol\tInternal Custodian: Mike Camplin, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist \u0026amp; Marine Monitoring Team Lead, Evidence, Knowledge \u0026amp; Advice  The data was entered into Marine Recorder by Harry Godge from Marine Ecosol.  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team - NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18334\tNRW_DS116512\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.680Z\t720\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMacrofaunal Community Monitoring on Inlets across Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353132\tLarge shallow inlets and bays are habitat complexes which comprise an interdependent mosaic of subtidal and intertidal habitats. Several of these habitat types (1140 Mudflats and sandflats not covered by sea water at low tide, 1110 Sandbanks which are slightly covered by sea water all the time and 1170 Reefs) are listed as Annex I habitats in their own right.   In Wales inlets occur in a number of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). This includes Carmarthen Bay and Estuary SAC which is located off the south Wales, Pembrokeshire Marine SAC which is located around Pembrokeshire, south Wales; and Pen Llyn a’r Sarnau SAC which is located on the south side of the Llyn Peninsula north-west Wales.  The purpose of this data capture was to examine the indication of anthropogenic change on macro-faunal communities around shallow inlets and bays. This dataset comprises of infaunal species counts, sediment grain size and chemical compositions.\tQuantitative sediment sampling was undertaken at within the Milford Haven and Three Rivers inlets using a using a 0.1 m2 Day Grab. Five replicates grabs were taken at each station for macro faunal sampling; at the majority of locations sediment was sampled directly from the grab used to sample macro fauna with a core. Also a separate grab was taken to sample the sediment Chemistry. Metals were sampled with a plastic scoop and plastic container, whereas Organics were sampled with a metal scoop and acid washed glass container.\t\tData is currently being entered into Marine Recorder by a contractor – Marine Ecosol\tInternal Custodian:  Mike Camplin, Senior Marine Monitoring Ecologist \u0026amp; Marine Monitoring Team Lead, Evidence, Knowledge \u0026amp; Advice  Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:27.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18335\tNRW_DS116513\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.685Z\t771\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Status of the Snowdon Beetle Chrysolina cerealis on Y Wyddfa (2015)\tStatws Chwilen yr Wyddfa Chrysolina cerealis ar Yr Wyddfa (2015)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353133\tThe Snowdon Beetle Chrysolina cerealis is restricted in the United Kingdom to Snowdon (Yr Wyddfa), although it was also found in Cwm Idwal until 1980. As recent records of the beetle have been few, this survey was undertaken in an attempt to determine its status and distribution. A total of five beetles (four larvae and one adult) were found following 63.5 hours of hand searching on and around thyme plants (the only recorded foodplant of the beetle in the UK) and stone-turning near patches of thyme from June to September 2015, with all specimens found in Clogwyn Coch in September. No beetles were found in Cwm du\u0027r Arddu, its traditional hotspot, despite exhaustive searches.   Further surveys were also conducted in 2016 please see -  National Museums Liverpool 2017. The status of the Snowdon Beetle Chrysolina cerealis on Yr Wyddfa in 2016. NRW Evidence Report No: 193, 18pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.  - Metadata for 2016 Survey here -  NRW_DS117097\tSampling techniques included visual searches and stone turning, undertaken by competent surveyors.\tWorld Museum Liverpool 2016. The status of the Snowdon beetle Chrysolina cerealis on Y Wyddfa in 2015. NRW Evidence Report No: 160, 23pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database in the Recorder 6\tDr Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-23T11:04:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18333\tNRW_DS116518\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.499Z\t632\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring Menai Strait and Conwy Bay (2012): Evaluating Intertidal Seagrass Beds – Field Survey Versus Remote Sensing\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353138\tSeagrass beds are of special interest in Wales as features under the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 (as amended), a Section 42 habitat under the NERC Act (2006), a subfeature of Special Areas of Conservation under the Habitats Regulations and is a biological quality element (marine angiosperms) under the Water Framework Directive.  As such, it is crucial that a reliable method of evaluating the quality (extent, density and species richness) of the seagrass beds is used.  Traditionally, field observations are made, and records of the presence and extent derived from mapping using a GPS.  Until recent years, the resolution of aerial photographs, taken from aircraft, has not been high enough to differentiate seagrass beds, unless they are particularly dense, with clearly defined edges.  Many of the intertidal seagrass beds in Wales are patchy and of low density (5-50% cover) and cannot be identified reliably from 1:5,000 aerial photographs. This study uses an unmanned aerial vehicle to take higher resolution images, by flying lower, along with GPS positions, allowing for the many images to be stitched together and be orthorectified.  The two methods are compared, each one proving to have certain benefits and short-comings.\tThese data are part of an annual program of monitoring of these particular seagrass beds from 2008 to 2012.\tBrazier, D. P. 2013.  Evaluating intertidal seagrass Zostera noltii beds –field survey vs remote sensing. CCW Marine Monitoring Report No: 103, pp 16 + v, Countryside Council for Wales, Bangor.\tArchived as project 410, media 1541.  Locally held version of data at H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2012 CCW (to be cleared up)  H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Marine_Habitats_and_Species\\Marine Monitoring\\Intid MS\u0026amp;CB\\2012 CCW Zostera\\ Archive: Data stored as Project 410, media 1541 W:\\Media1501to1600\\Media1541\\2012ZosteraProject\\Data\\\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW       Paul Brazier is completing a metadata proforma.  unlikely that the data will have any restrictions.  Contracted ortho-rectified images\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18332\tNRW_DS116519\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.526Z\t703\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Tidal Flaps\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353139\tThis dataset shows the location of tidal flaps across the Gwent Levels from Cardiff to Caldicot. Tidal Flaps are sea doors that allow water out of the reen/watercourse network when the tide is out and close when the tide is in to stop sea water from coming in to the reen/watercourse network.\tUnknown\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Tidal Flaps\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-01-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18331\tNRW_DS116527\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.412Z\t661\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey of calaminarian grassland in Mid-Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353237\tThe aims of this project were to refine guidance on calaminarian habitat definition for mid-Wales, and to provide data and information on the extent of the different forms. The purpose of this data capture was to assess calaminarian grassland habitat types, condition and extent for each site, and to gather information on the distribution and frequency of metallophytes and other notable species. This was done by detailed survey of the plants and lichens of the calaminarian grasslands on 27 abandoned mine sites in mid-Wales, 26 in Ceredigion and 1 in Montgomeryshire, with mapping of the compartments in GIS. A wide variation in the character of calaminarian habitat was observed, including grass-dominated examples, metallophyte-rich heathland and largely-bare highly-contaminated land.  Some of the vegetation was considered distinctive in both a British and European context. A total of 90 vascular plants, 38 bryophytes, 227 lichens and 15 lichenicolous fungi were recorded, including many species of conservation concern. Categories of calaminarian habitat types in mid Wales are presented, to facilitate future mapping of the habitat.\tVegetation compartments (including rock and bare ground) were mapped on site by walkover survey, using a satellite image to identify boundaries.  Those that appeared to have been influenced by heavy metals were surveyed for all vascular plant, lichen and bryophyte taxa by recording those present in three representative areas of each compartment, supplemented by a more thorough search for metallophytes and other taxa indicative of habitat quality. Abundance was recorded using the DAFOR scale.\tSurvey of calaminarian grassland in mid-Wales March 2016 NRW Evidence report 061.\tThe Excel sheets are on the DMS in Evidence\u0026gt;Terrestrial Habitats\u0026gt;Lowland Grassland\u0026gt;Calaminarian – survey 2014 (marked FINAL).  The ARC data is more difficult as they are currently on Stuart Smiths Y drive but I assume will need moving to a more permanent and accessible location (ie X Drive).\tInternal Custodian:  Stuart Smith Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-03-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18328\tNRW_DS116536\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.291Z\t475\tValid\tEditable\tOpen Access - Other Statutory Access Land (CRoW Act)\tMynediad Agored - Tir Mynediad Statudol Arall\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353336\tThe CRoW Act provides for public access on foot to certain types of land, amends the law relating to public rights of way, increases measures for the management and protection for Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and strengthens wildlife enforcement legislation, and provides for better management of Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONB).   Under Section 4 of the CRoW Act it states the following: It shall be the duty of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW now forms part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW))  to prepare, in respect of Wales, maps which together show; (a) all registered common land, and (b) all open country.    This dataset contains all Common Land with a higher right of access (Urban Commons, Commons with Deeds for Access, 1899 Commons Act) for areas of Wales, and Elan Valley Estate. This dataset does not include the Registered Common Land dataset.   Digitisation took place in 2014 for all Wales.   When using this data for Open Access, it should be used in conjunction with Open Country, Registered Common Land and Public Forests from the same years. Contact your local authority for access to and information about Registers of Common Land.-Mae Deddf Cefn Gwlad a Hawliau Tramwy yn darparu ar gyfer mynediad cyhoeddus ar droed i fathau penodol o dir, yn diwygio\u0027r gyfraith sy\u0027n ymwneud hawliau tramwy cyhoeddus, yn cynyddu mesurau ar gyfer rheoli a gwarchod Safleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGA) ac yn cryfhau deddfwriaeth gorfodi bywyd gwyllt, ac yn darparu ar gyfer gwell rheolaeth ar Ardaloedd o Harddwch Naturiol Eithriadol (AHNE). O dan Adran 4 Deddf Cefn Gwlad a Hawliau Tramwy mae\u0027n datgan y canlynol: Bydd yn ddyletswydd ar Gyngor Cefn Gwlad Cymru i baratoi, mewn perthynas â Chymru, mapiau sydd gyda\u0027i gilydd yn dangos; (a) yr holl dir comin cofrestredig, a (b) yr holl dir agored. Mae Cyngor Cefn Gwlad Cymru yn rhan o Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) erbyn hyn. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys yr holl Dir Comin gyda hawl mynediad uwch (Tiroedd Comin Trefol, Tiroedd Comin â Gweithredoedd ar gyfer Mynediad, Deddf Tir Comin 1899) ar gyfer ardaloedd yng Nghymru, a Stad Cwm Elan. Nid yw\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys Tir Comin Cofrestredig. Digwyddodd y digido yn 2014 ar gyfer Cymru gyfan. Wrth ddefnyddio\u0027r data hwn ar gyfer Mynediad Agored, dylid ei ddefnyddio ar y cyd â Thir Agored, Tir Comin Cofrestredig a Choedwigoedd Cyhoeddus.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis dataset is based on the original common land paper registers as held by the local authorities, and was originally digitised against OS Land-Line by Landmark Information Group (LIG) for NRW for Open Access mapping work. Any line shown as not matching the original boundary has been copied from what is shown on the original registers and is not a digitising error.   This dataset is an update from the recent Open Access 10 year review, with Elan Valley Estate being added to this dataset in 2014. For the 10 year review, each Local Authority was contacted for information on de-registered, new or change of use Commons, and updated Access data as appropriate.\tThe Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000 (CRoW Act 2000) applies to England and Wales only.\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Other Statutory Access Land 2014.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\CROW\\Other Statutory Access Land 2004.lyr\tChris Heaps, Specialist Advisor: Trails and Open Access, Open Access Team / Recreation \u0026amp; Partnerships Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-04-16\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/394/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_OTHER_STATUTORY_LAND_2014\t2025-12-19T11:59:32.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18329\tNRW_DS116538\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.399Z\t608\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Usk Special Area of Conservation (SAC) habitat structure condition assessment 2013 - 2015\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353338\tThis dataset consists of the condition assessment of the River Usk Special Area of Conservation (SAC) physical river habitat quality assessed using River Habitat Survey (RHS) and LEAFPAC (river macrophyte) survey data between 2012 and 2016. NRW is responsible for monitoring the condition of the river habitat quality on SAC rivers designated for the Ranunculion feature; more formally known as “Water courses of plain to montane levels with the Ranunculion fluitantis and Callitricho-Batrachion vegetation” (EU habitat code 3260). Habitats Directive and NRW is  required to evaluate the conservation status of this river habitat. The Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance (JNCC, 2014) sets out eight river habitat quality assessment attributes for the condition of this SAC feature. Specific details for RHS survey methods are detailed in Environment Agency. 2003. The River Habitat Survey manual. Details for the LEAFPAC macrophyte survey method are found in Environment Agency. 2008. Surveying freshwater macrophytes in rivers. Operational instruction 131-07.\tThe River Habitat Survey (extracted from Biosys) and LEAFPAC surveys are conducted as part of the Water Framework Directive (WFD) water bodies classification. Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance recommends that some of this data can be used to assess the following physical habitat attributes: - Habitat Modification Score - Riparian zone vegetation naturalness - Large woody debris - Fine sediment - Negative indicators: alien / locally absent species\tSpecific details for RHS survey methods are detailed in Environment Agency. 2003. The River Habitat Survey manual. Details for the LEAFPAC macrophyte survey method are found in Environment Agency. 2008. Surveying freshwater macrophytes in rivers. Operational instruction 131-07.  Thomas, Rh. 2014. CSM Habitat Structure Assessment for the Habitats Directive Second Monitoring Cycle (2007-2012). Updated version 2 (28/02/14). NRW unpub guidance. Thomas, Rh. (2013). CSM Habitat Structure Assessment for the Habitats Directive Second Monitoring Cycle (2007-2012).  CCW Internal Note.\t[A] Reports and database exports held internally on the DMS using the following pathway: Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting (SMAR) \u0026gt; freshwater monitoring Running waters Habitats Directive data Cycle 3.  [B] Spatial data currently on W drive Heather Garrett Wye RHS but waiting for guidance to move to Y drive. [C] Species data held on BIOSYS \u0026amp; Environment Agency RHS database.\tData Custodian:  Heather Garrett, Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning Team Melissa Lacan, Senior Env Monitoring Officer (Biology) Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R   Internal Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-03-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:15.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18330\tNRW_DS116580\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.411Z\t548\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPotential Accessible Natural Greenspaces for Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136353830\tThis dataset consists of polygon areas showing the potential accessible natural greenspaces for the entire of Wales, input by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) with the assistance of Unitary Authorities and Planning areas and their original Urban Greenspace data. This was mapped against Mastermap 2009 mostly but amendments will be ongoing and will be mapped against the latest version of Mastermap that NRW holds at the time of digitisation.  This data will be used by Unitary Authorities and Planning Areas, and NRW staff to help make informed decisions on how best to change/create urban and rural areas, incorporating greenspaces for the health and wellbeing of the community they are surrounded by. This will also impact on the Welsh Index of Multiple Depravation and the Good for People toolkit.  Warning: The Column titled \u0027Status\u0027 is to be used when running any form of statistics as this is the column that says whether the polygon has been checked by the Local Authority, \u0027Draft\u0027 means it has not been checked and should be ignored for any statistical analysis, \u0027No Ver LA\u0027 Is to signify that the Local Authorities don\u0027t know anything about that parcel of land and should be used cautiously in any statistical analysis. \u0027Completed\u0027 are the polygons the Local Authorities have checked and are happy that the information is correct to the best of their knowledge.\tThis dataset was captured using OS Master Map categories to identify potential greenspaces.\tGreenspaces Toolkit CD\tX:\\Social Economic\\Greenspaces\\Potential Greenspaces.lyr    Pete Frost’s Greenspaces Toolkit CD (https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/are/commu/gi/_layouts/15/WopiFrame.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d{71B1EAFE-DA54-4340-980B-6E3AE44FCA6C}\u0026amp;file\u003dGreenspace%20Toolkit%20version%202%202011.DOC\u0026amp;action\u003ddefault\u0026amp;DefaultItemOpen\u003d1)\tInternal Contacts: Pete Frost, Senior Urban Advisor, NRW  Metadata; Pippa Jones, GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18327\tNRW_DS116698\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.298Z\t1712\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAquifer Designation\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136363938\tAquifer designation consists of two datasets.  These are NRW Aquifer topology superficial [Aquifer Designation Superficial Deposits] and NRW Aquifer topology bedrock [Aquifer Designation Bedrock Geology]. Aquifer designations identify the potential of the geological strata to provide water that can be abstract and have been defined through the assessment of the underlying geology. The designations are attributed directly to British Geological Society (BGS) 1:50K. Bedrock geology (formerly known as \u0027solid\u0027 geology by BGS) is a term used for the main mass of rocks forming the Earth and present everywhere, whether exposed at the surface in outcrops or concealed beneath superficial deposits or water. Geology less than 1.8 million years in age are referred to as Superficial Deposits. Superficial deposits are the youngest geological deposits formed during the most recent period of geological time, the Quaternary, which extends back about 1.8 million years from the present. They rest on older deposits or rocks referred to as Bedrock.\tThe aquifer designations are: Principal Aquifers: geology that exhibit high irregular and/or fracture permeability. They usually provide a high level of water storage. They may support water supply and/or river base flow on a strategic scale; Secondary Aquifers A: permeable strata capable of supporting water supplies at a local rather than strategic scale and in some cases forming an important source of base flow to rivers; Secondary Aquifer B:  predominantly lower permeability strata which may in part have the ability to store and yield limited amounts of groundwater by virtue of localised features such as fissures, thin permeable horizons and weathering; Secondary Undifferentiated: In cases where it has not been possible to attribute either category A or B to a rock type; Unproductive Strata:  these are geological strata with low permeability that have negligible significance for water supply or river base flow. Unknown: Lakes and Landslips.\t\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Aquifer topology superficial.lyr  X:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Aquifer topology bedrock.lyr\tDave Jones-  Groundwater Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-British Geological Survey (BGS)\t\t2021-07-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18326\tNRW_DS116720\t2024-05-28T12:46:37.115Z\t521\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) North Wales Bat Roosts\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373230\tThis dataset comprises of bat records from North Wales, including monitoring data from known bat roosts and some incidental bat rescue records. This dataset is now held and managed by Cofnod, North Wale Environmental Information Service.\tThis dataset was derived from two electronic datasets held originally by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), Bat Roosts Database - Caernarvonshire and Anglesey, and Bat Roosts Database - North East Wales. To compile this dataset, bat records from paper files, held in CCW regional offices were compared with those held electronically within the two datasets.  In addition to this Bat Roost Database - Merionethshire was used, however it was discovered that this dataset was a duplicate of the original Bat Roosts Database - Caernarvonshire and Anglesey. Not all electronic records were checked but any which were found during a systematic sift through the regional files. Existing electronic records were improved by adding missing data which was present in their original paper source and by adding a reference to the file. For all records which were located in the regional files, their geographic location was checked on Ordnance Survey maps using GIS and, where necessary, moved to its correct or more accurate position. Further improvements were made to the dataset by comparison with an earlier version of the Bat Roosts Database - Caernarvonshire and Anglesey (GWYBATS) during early 2013.\tCofnod now hold the top copy of this dataset.\tThis dataset is managed by Cofnod on behalf of NRW - it is published on the NBN Gateway.\tMetadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW  External Contacts and Data Custodian: Aisling May  - Rheolwr Data/Data Manager       aisling.may@cofnod.org.uk Cofnod, Intec, Ffordd y Parc, Parc Menai, Bangor, Gwynedd. LL57 4FG.  (01248) 672603 www.cofnod.org.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2007-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18379\tNRW_DS116722\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.507Z\t716\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Mospolys Layer voronoi\tCam 1 Daearol Cymru Gyfan - Haenen Mospolys voronoi\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373232\tThis is a spatial dataset of Phase 1 mosaic polygons. The polygons are a grid of squares coloured in chequerboard fashion according to Phase1_Code and in proportion to their extent to enable the visualisation of mosaics in a GIS. The mosaic polygons were created by the NRW Phase 2 Tools from the All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Vegetation Layer - Voronoi - GIS dataset and the All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Mosaic Layer Voronoi - GIS dataset.   The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales 1km square dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage, and the maps were digitised as part of the project to map Open Access land. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales. Voronoi was carried out to enable full representation and visualisation of the components of complex mixed-habitat upland polygons.\tVoronoi procedure carried out on the cleaned version of the vegetation layer, and mosaic browser created from this.    Closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors carried out field recording of habitats onto 1:10,000 scale maps (rarely 1:25,000 during the upland stage). The minimum size of habitat patch recorded lies predominantly within the range 0.1-0.25 ha, although smaller areas were mapped in the lowlands. The survey methods employed during the initial upland stage are given in more detail in a WFU report (Day, A. (1989) Upland Vegetation Survey 1979-1989 Background Methodology and Summary of Data Collected. NCC, Bangor). Methods for the lowland survey followed the Handbook for Phase 1 Habitat Survey with some minor modifications adopted for use in Wales (Howe E.A. and Blackstock T.H. (1991) Supplementary Notes for Phase I Survey in Wales. CCW, Bangor).   The voronoi procedure was carried out in MapInfo to split complex upland mixed habitat polygons into component parts using a tool written in-house by the GIS and Spatial Data Team within NRW. A study of survey reliability was conducted during the lowland phase (Kerry, Rimes, Smith, Williams. 1994. An Assessment of Errors in Phase I Habitat Survey in Wales. Bangor: Countryside Council for Wales, Report No. 94/4/1). It showed the field mapping to be 83% repeatable (Stevens, Blackstock, Howe, Stevens. 2004. Repeatability of Phase 1 habitat survey. J. Environ. Manage. 73, 53-59). The electronic data have undergone a series of checks for accuracy both by the contractor and subsequently within CCW. The extent of upland flush and upland species-rich ledges may be over-estimated by the voronoi procedure.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase 1 Mospolys voronoi.lyr\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t1997-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T13:48:12.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18384\tNRW_DS116723\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.471Z\t656\tValid\tEditable\tWelsh Permitted Waste Operations\tGwaith Gwastraff a Ganiateir yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373233\tThe EU Waste Framework Directive (2008/98EC) provides the legislative framework for the collection, transport, recovery and disposal of waste. The directive requires all Member States to take the necessary measures to ensure waste is recovered or disposed of without endangering human health or causing harm to the environment and includes permitting, registration and inspection requirement.   Article 28 of the Waste Framework Directive specifically requires Member States to publicly make available any data relating to waste facilities.   Natural Resources Wales (NRW) issue waste permits across Wales to anyone who deals with waste of any kind. This dataset contains details, obtained from the returned permits, of the waste facilities located across Wales. These waste sites are categorised into groups with each sub-type of waste site being linked to the overarching category.-Mae Cyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Gwastraff yr Uned Ewropeaidd (2008/98/EC) yn darparu\u0027r fframwaith deddfwriaethol ar gyfer casglu, cludo, adfer a gwaredu gwastraff. Mae\u0027r gyfarwyddeb yn mynnu bod yr holl aelod-wladwriaethau\u0027n cymryd y camau angenrheidiol i sicrhau bod gwastraff yn cael ei adfer neu ei waredu heb beryglu iechyd dynol na pheri niwed i\u0027r amgylchedd ac mae\u0027n cynnwys trwyddedu, cofrestru a gofynion arolygu. Mae Erthygl 28 o\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Gwastraff yn galw\u0027n benodol ar aelod-wladwriaethau i sicrhau bod unrhyw ddata cysylltiedig â chyfleusterau gwastraff ar gael yn gyhoeddus. Mae Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru yn rhoi trwyddedau gwastraff ledled Cymru i unrhyw un sy\u0027n ymwneud â gwastraff o unrhyw fath. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys manylion o\u0027r cyfleusterau gwastraff sydd wedi\u0027u lleoli ledled Cymru a gasglwyd o\u0027r trwyddedau a ddychwelwyd. Mae\u0027r safleoedd gwastraff hyn wedi\u0027u dosbarthu\u0027n grwpiau gyda phob is-fath o safle gwastraff yn cael ei gysylltu â\u0027r categori trosfwaol.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tAs part of the permit application, organisations or individuals are required to submit information regarding the name of the applicant, type of waste facility and the capacity required. These are then extracted from Permitting and Licencing (PALS), part of the Customer Relationship Management (CRM) system. Within the dataset the number of Planning Authorities (25) differs to the number of Local Authorities (22) as the 3 National Parks are only included in the Planning Authorities attribute.\t\tCustomer Relationship Management (CRM)  Permitting and Licencing (PALS).\tData point of Contact: Tim Morris Waste Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-08-31\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2637\t2025-05-16T11:27:58.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18382\tNRW_DS116724\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.533Z\t274\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) (Cardiff) Bat Casework Files 2001 to 2011\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373234\tThis dataset comprises of bat records extracted from casework files related to the Cardiff area. The top copy of this dataset is held and managed by South East Wales Biodiversity Records Centre (SEWBReC), who manage the data on behalf of Natural Resources Wales who continue to own the data.\tThis dataset was created from species records extracted, computerised and collated from paper records held at NRW\u0027s Regional office files specifically related to casework from south Wales. The data capture and mobilisation was done by SEWBReC Staff Members on behalf of Natural Resources Wales.\t\tThis data is held and managed by SEWBREC - NRW own the data but SEWBREC manage it on our behalf as a top copy.  A copy of the data is also available in Recorder 6 but currently only from 2001 to 2005 (12/08/2016) We have received a whole update including all years - however this has not yet been put into Recorder 6. Instead at the moment this is on the NBN Gateway as part of the sensitive data that SEWBREC hold (see parent ID). LC0002150000001R CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework File 2001 LC0002150000001Z CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework File 2002 LC00021500000021 CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework File 2003 LC00021500000028 CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework File 2004 LC0002150000002L CCW (Cardiff) Bat Casework File 2005\tContact: Data Services Team, NRW   External Contact:  David Slade Senior IT and Records Officer,  South East Wales Biodiversity Records Centre,  13 St Andrews Crescent, Cardiff CF10 3DB Tel: 029 2064 1110 Fax: 0843 2652714  Email: david.slade@sewbrec.org.uk www.sewbrec.org.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:23.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18369\tNRW_DS116725\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.399Z\t573\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater pearl mussel (Margaritifera margaritifera) larval encystment of host fish species on the Afon Eden – Cors Goch Trawsfynydd Special Area of Conservation (SAC)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373235\tThe freshwater pearl mussel (Margaritifera margaritifera) is protected under several tiers of national and international legislation; EU Habitats and Species Directive Annexes II and V, UK Biodiversity Action Plan list of priority species, UK Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 Schedule 5, Bern Convention Appendix 3, IUCN Red Data List as Critically Endangered. The Afon Eden - Cors Goch Trawsfynydd is the only Special Area of Conservation (SAC) site in Wales where the freshwater pearl mussel Margaritifera margaritifera forms the primary reason for selection of a site. Each year during early summer and when the water temperature is suitable, the female adult mussel releases larvae (glochidia) into the river where they are either ingested or inhaled by host salmonid fish. The species of host fish is specific to each pearl mussel population and prior to these encystment surveys it had been assumed that Atlantic salmon Salmo salar were the host for the Afon Eden population. The results of four annual surveys for gill encystment has proven that brown trout (including sea trout) Salmo trutta is the host species for the Afon Eden population. This dataset contains the results of extent of encystment during electrofishing surveys for salmonid species.\tThe survey methodology consisted of annual electrofishing salmonids on Afon Eden and checking gills for glochidia encystment. Appendix in final summary report gives survey locations and should be kept confidential. Report contributes in EU LIFE Pearls in Peril project.\tH Garrett (2016) Freshwater pearl mussel larval encystment of host fish species on the Afon Eden – Cors Goch Trawsfynydd SAC . NRW Evidence Report 164.\tData held on a site by site basis on word and excel documents on DMS.\tInternal Contact; Heather Garret, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, Monitoring Strategy and Planning Team, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18370\tNRW_DS116726\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.473Z\t600\tUnassessed\tEditable\tStackpole National Nature Reserve Species Inventory and Ad Hoc sightings from around Pembrokeshire (extracted from Recorder 6)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373236\tThis dataset contains species data from across Pembrokeshire and specifically Stackpole National Nature Reserve (NNR). The data was collected as part of Special Area of Conservation and Sites of Special Scientific Interest monitoring. It includes data from Stackpole, Castlemartin, Broomhill Burrows, Corsydd Llangloffan and Freshwater East.\tA variety of methods were used to collect this data. Some was extracted from reports, whilst other include monitoring work and ad hoc species sightings. This data was first held on individual stand alone Recorder 3 databases, but were then centralised and put onto Recorder 6. Since this has happened, now updates have occurred in Recorder 6 and the newer NNR monitoring data is held in the Stackpole Officer in spreadsheet and word document formats.\t\tThis data is held in Recorder 6 under the survey tag Stackpole. In addition to this there is also a lot of data held in the Stackpole Office. These are kept with the NNR managers on hard drives and have not been entered into Recorder 6 due to technical difficulties. They are held in spreadsheets, word documents as individual records and ad hoc sightings.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas.   This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).\tPaul Culyer, Senior Reserve Manager - Pembrokeshire, Operational Services, South, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18373\tNRW_DS116727\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.508Z\t405\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTycanol National Nature Reserve (NNR) Species Inventory\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373237\tThis dataset is made up of species sightings from Tycanol National Nature Reserve (NNR). The purpose of this data capture was to collate together species sightings for Ty Canol in order to create a species inventory for the site.\tVarious methods were used to create the inventory, from monitoring work to ad hoc sightings. This data was first held on individual stand alone Recorder 3 databases, but were then centralised and put onto Recorder 6. Since this has happened, now updates have occurred in Recorder 6 and the newer NNR monitoring data is held in the Tycanol Officer in spreadsheet and word document formats.\t\tAll this data is available in Recorder 6 under the Tycanol Tag.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tPaul Culyer, Senior Reserve Manager - Pembrokeshire, Operational Services, South, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18385\tNRW_DS116742\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.487Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCoastwatch Habitat Maps\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373432\tThis dataset consists of paper records which have been boxed and sent to offsite storage. The records relate to the Coastwatch project started during the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) days. The surveys were related to how people use the coast in terms of resources and recreation. The leaflet details some information related to the surveys as follows: Take part in Coastwatch the largest coastal survey of Great Britain ever planned. If you are interested in the coast or concerned about its protection you can help increase our knowledge of our coastal heritage.... to do the job properly we first need to know the location and size of all the different types of coastal habitat such as rocky and sandy shores saltmarshes and sand dunes. We also want to find out which human pressures are affecting these habitats and where they are concentrated.  This data is currently not known to be digitised. It has been superseded by the Phase 1 Intertidal work but still provides and important historic record of the coastline around Wales. The majority of records cover Wales however there are some ad hoc records which are located elsewhere.\tVolunteers annotated maps by hand detailing habitat types. As part of the survey the volunteers had to answer standard questionnaires which contained the following information: site names, grid references, county, recorders names and addresses, date, day, times of low water, height of low water, survey start time and end time, weather conditions, general comments.  There were also questions related to the general presence of certain categories.  - Use of living resources detailing the presence of animal graxing, e.g. rabbit deer or live stock, the presence of seaweed, reeds, angling, bait digging, shellfish collections, picking of glasswort, lawn turf cutting and collecting of shellfish (mussels, limpets, winkles, cockles, clams, crabs, shrimp).  - Use of non-living resources related to the beach, sand dunes, quarries, and mines.  - Cultivation of living resources, fish farming, molluscs cultivation. Types of coastal protection, groynes - Habitats used by MoD - Shipping and navigation related to wrecks, moorings, location of beached boats, piers and jetting numbers, presence of litter, hazardous cargo - Waste disposal, type of litter dumped or tipped, industrial waste effluents, sewage outfalls. - Education and scientific, number of people working on educational scientific studies, biological specimens being collected, geological specimens being collected. Recreation, access to the shore, car parking, camping / caravan sights, beach huts, tidal swimming pools, marinas, and golf courses. General activities being undertaken by members of the public.  Once this questionnaire was filled in the Coastwatch Habitat map was coloured in by hand as per the key provided detailing the location of habitats within their given area. A survey record sheet was filled in giving more detail about the habitats, such as area of habitat. Within some of these details volunteers have also supplied photographs.\tThe information within the boxes are held together using a folded over questionnaire containing information related to each site.\tArchived as Project 470, Media 1547 (File path: K\\:Archive\\Media1501to1600\\Media1547)\tInternal Custodian: Gabrielle Wyn, Team Leader, All Wales Marine Advice Team (Gabe Wyn advised that should be kept as permanent record)  Gabe Wyn advised that should be kept as permanent record\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:01:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18377\tNRW_DS116744\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.519Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIndexing Paper Record Cards for ad hoc Marine Species Sightings around Wales (Paper Records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373434\tThis is a paper records dataset, which is currently stored with the Data Services Team. These paper recording cards were put together by W.E Jones and are made up of numerous records collated from various reports related to the flora and fauna around Wales. Some of these records have been taken from reports and put into the Marine Recorder, but the paper records contain further information and records which have not be digitised. Some of these data may already be available in JNCC\u0027s copy of Marine Records, for example 1982 Littoral hard substrata of Menai Strait (see Parent ID for further information).\tThe records cards hold information on the species (might be old names which are now out of date and will need updating if digitised), Date, Locatlity name e.g. River Ogweh Estuary, Depth codes, general comments on location, abundance, size, habitat and breeding status and the observer name or initials.   Data Digitisation Procedure:  This dataset needs to be digitised into a excel spreadsheet before individual records can be assessed to see if they have been digitised in within other surveys. Once all records have been entered, and given a broad grid reference or grid square, unless a specific one is specified on the recording card. In addition to this species names need to be checked in WoRMS or Natural History Museum species dictionary for any updates or changes. These should be changed as appropriate in a new column of the spreadsheet. Once the paper records have all been entered into a spreadsheet, they can then be assessed based on the priorities for digitisation e.g. priority species or historic records. These can then be digitised into Marine Recorder, avoiding duplication with other surveys already in Marine Recorder. It is also noted that there are a lot of lichen data records which could be also be in Recorder 6. Each record needs to be checked, to see if there is duplication by specifically looking at year, species, location and any other parameters available.\tJones, W.E. 1983. Littoral hard substrata of the Menai Strait. (Contractor: University College of North Wales, Marine Science Laboratories, Menai Bridge.) Nature Conservancy Council, CSD Report No. 486.  Jones, W.E. 1983. The distribution of marine life around Anglesey. Porcupine Newsletter, 2, 165-168.\tIn Data Services Room in cardboard box labelled EIT Biol Data - Do No Throw Archived as Project 471, Media 1548\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:09:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18372\tNRW_DS116767\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.370Z\t420\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Wales: Site packs, paper maps and field notes\tCam 2 Arolwg Coetiroedd Cymru: Pecynnau safle, mapiau papur a nodiadau maes\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373637\tThis dataset contains site packs for sites surveyed or resurveyed during the Phase 2 Woodland Survey of Wales. The site packs contain site summary sheets, description maps, quadrat data for each site and standard data from National Vegetation Classification (NVC). The summary sheets include general site information, site descriptions, vegetation maps and plant community data.   The data was collected to provide supplementary information for the Phase 2 Woodland Survey of Wales.\tThe data is currently stored in Box Files which are organised by areas of search . Each site within this area of search has its own site pack. Monitoring for Phase 2 woodland surveys is on-going, with some new sites being adopted and older sites being resurvey for supplementary information for the Phase 2 Woodland Survey of Wales.\t\tThese paper records are currently held in Maes y Ffynnon - contact James (Jim) Latham for more information.  Likely to be held in Woodland Tambour Unit in Old Post Room F12 Scans of the maps are held as tifs on Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_M_DRIVE\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_2\\Woodland\\Raster Data\tJames (Jim) Latham - Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team / David Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2021-08-25\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-29T13:37:45.929Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18383\tNRW_DS116782\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.568Z\t827\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAd Hoc Bat Records from around Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373832\tThis dataset combines the bat records Natural Resources Wales (NRW) holds as a top copy within their copy of Recorder 6. It includes Important Welsh Bat Roosts Database (BATSITES-WALES), bat roosts data from Pembrokeshire hibernacula and day / night roost records, Welsh Lesser Horseshoe Bat summer roost surveillance database, and some regional records from Pembrokeshire and south Wales (BATDATA-WALES). This dataset gives an indication of the abundance of each species at each location, and gives the annual summer surveillance records for selected nursery roost sites.  The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts, to assist NRW in conservation work. In particular, to provide information when dealing with development proposals, to support the monitoring and notification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species.\tData was generated from visits to roosts by staff and volunteers as well as enquiries received by former Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) or Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). Data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW). Some validation for this dataset is still required.\tRecords held in BATSITES-WALES, will also be found (without monitoring data) in BATDATA-WALES and some will also appear in Welsh Horseshoe Bat Summer Roost Surveillance Database.\tData is stored in Recorder 6.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tLiz Halliwell, Mammal Ecologist Woodland and Farmland Mammals, Species Team, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-09-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18380\tNRW_DS116788\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.434Z\t587\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Acid Sensitive Waterbodies Cycle 2\tCylch 2 Cyrff Dŵr Sensitif i Asid y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373838\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Acid Sensitive Waterbodies Cycle 2 (2014 - 2019) is a spatial (polygon shapefile) dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined, for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). To implement the guidance on managing forests in order to minimise adverse impacts in acid-sensitive areas, two categories of acid sensitivity have been identified. These are defined as \u0027failing due to acidification\u0027 and \u0027at risk of failing due to acidification\u0027.   Water bodies identified as currently \u0027failing\u0027 have been classified through the Water Framework Directive monitoring as being of less than \u0027good\u0027 status and the cause of that failure has been attributed to acidification. The \u0027at risk of failing\u0027 water bodies have been identified, via the findings of the Welsh acidification risk assessment, as being at risk of failing to achieve \u0027good\u0027 status by 2027, due to acid deposition. The risk assessment combines chemical and biological data and an assessment of the environment\u0027s ability to neutralise the predicted acid pollutants.-Er mwyn gweithredu canllawiau ar reoli coedwigoedd er mwyn lleihau effithiau andwyol mewn ardaloedd sy\u0027n sensitif i asid, clustnodwyd dau gategori o sensitifedd i asid. Cânt eu diffinio fel ‘methu oherwydd asideiddio’ a ‘risg o fethu oherwydd asideiddio’.  Mae cyrff dŵr a glustnodir fel rhai sy\u0027n ‘methu’ ar hyn o bryd wedi\u0027u gwirio o ganlyniad i fynd ati i fonitro eu hasidedd. Clustnodwyd cyrff dŵr ‘mewn perygl o fethu’, drwy ganfyddiadau asesiad risg asideiddio Cymru asesiad risg asideiddio Cymru, fel rhai mewn perygl o fethu o ganlyniad i allyriadau yn 2027. Mae\u0027r asesiad risg yn cyfuno data cemegol a biolegol ac yn asesiad o allu\u0027r amgylchedd i niwtraleiddio\u0027r llygryddion asid a ragwelir.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThis dataset identifies the surface waterbodies, defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive, that are failing or at risk of failing due to acidification. This dataset covers the layer for Cycle 2 of the Water Framework Directive.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 2 2016.lyr\tRob Blacklidge- Reporting Team WFD Specialist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-11-01\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/396/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_ACID_SENSITIVE_WB_CYCLE2_2016\t2025-05-08T08:00:56.214Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18368\tNRW_DS116790\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.363Z\t353\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Artificial Waterbodies Canals Cycle 1\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373930\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Artificial Waterbodies Canals Cycle 1 (2008 - 2013) is a spatial (polyline) dataset containing Water Framework Directive (WFD) attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive.  As stated within Article 2.8 of the WFD \u0027artificial water body\u0027 is defined as a body of surface water created by human activity.  Canals are therefore reported as a discrete, artificial waterbody dataset. Each waterbody has been assigned \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027, which is a unique identifier that enables a link to WFD attributes.\tThese data have been delineated primarily using geography digitised from Ordnance Survey Background mapping and the British Waterways Canal Network GIS layer.  These two data sources were validated against one another to derive a final network containing only artificial canals and omitting canalised rivers.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Artificial Waterbodies Canals Cycle 1.lyr\tInternal Contacts: Internal custodian - Rob Blackridge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .  Metadata contact - GIS and Spatial Information Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-09-02\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18378\tNRW_DS116792\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.310Z\t714\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwaterbodies Cycle 2\tCylch 2 Cyrff Dŵr Daear y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373932\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwaterbodies Cycle 2 (2014-2019) is a spatial (polygon) dataset which has been created for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027…all water which is below the surface of the ground in the saturation zone and in direct contact with the ground or subsoil\u0027.   For the purposes of reporting under the WFD a groundwater body represent a distinct body of groundwater flow with a coherent flow unit including recharge and discharge areas with little flow across the boundaries. These reflect hydrogeological characteristics containing information on flow and stage properties, recharge and vulnerability to pollution. This has been undertaken through defining aquifers into different types and broken into catchment units at Catchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) scale.-Mae cyrff dŵr daear Cylch 2 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (2014-2019) yn set ddata ofodol (polygon) sydd wedi\u0027i chreu ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr. Mae Erthygl 2, Cymal 2 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr yn eu diffinio fel \u0027…yr holl ddŵr sydd o dan wyneb y ddaear yn y parth trwytho ac mewn cyswllt uniongyrchol â\u0027r tir neu\u0027r isbridd\u0027. At ddibenion hysbysu o dan y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr, mae corff dŵr daear yn cynrychioli corff penodol o lif dŵr daear gydag uned lifo gydlynol, gan gynnwys ardaloedd adlwytho a rhyddhau, gydag ond ychydig o lif ar draws y terfynau. Mae\u0027r rhain yn adlewyrchu nodweddion hydroddaearegol sy\u0027n ystyried gwybodaeth am nodweddion llif a cham, adlwytho, a pha mor agored ydynt i lygredd. Mae hyn wedi cael ei gyflawni drwy ddiffinio dyfrhaenau fel teipiau gwahanol a\u0027u rhannu\u0027n unedau dalgylch ar raddfa Strategaethau Rheoli Tynnu Dŵr Dalgylch.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.Wedi deillion rhannol o ddata digidol ar raddfa 1:50,000 ac 1:250,000 dan ganiatd Arolwg Daearegol Prydain. ©NERC.\tThe primary input dataset is \u00271:625K Classified Aquifer Geology\u0027. This dataset has been digitised directly from the hard copy \u00271:250K Solid Geology Map\u0027 and classified according to aquifer type and are therefore directly derived from the underlying BGS data. These classifications were verified by the British Geology Survey (BGS).   These data have gone out for consultation at Area level and in some instances 1:50K Solid Geology has been used to define localised boundaries.   Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Groundwater Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tRob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2021-09-09\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/416/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_GROUNDWATER_C2\t2025-12-19T12:03:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18381\tNRW_DS116796\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.510Z\t422\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInvertebrates Recorded on Exposed Riverine Sediments at Llanelltyd on the Afon Mawddach in 2016.\tCreaduriaid Di-asgwrn-cefn a Gofnodwyd ar Waddodion Afonol Agored yn Llanelltud ar Afon Mawddach yn 2016\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373936\tIn order to alleviate flooding problems on the Afon Mawddach at Llanelltyd, Natural Resources Wales is considering the removal of gravel from shingle bars immediately upstream and downstream of the bridge. Data was collected to evaluate the current significance of these bars for Exposed Riverine Sediments invertebrates and to indicate the possible impact of gravel removal on the Exposed Riverine Sediments fauna.    This dataset holds specialist invertebrate species of Exposed Riverine Sediments recorded on three dates in July and September 2016. Comparisons are made with a similar survey carried out on the Tan-lan bar in 2003 and Exposed Riverine Sediment Quality Indices are presented.   Unseasonal floods during August and September may have limited the range of species, but it would also appear that changes to the topography of the Tan-lan (upstream) sediment bar may also be responsible for limiting the area of habitat. The current survey did not yield sufficient species to allow a reliable index to be calculated but there is no reason to suppose that the Llanelltyd shingle bars are no longer important to the Exposed Riverine Sediments assemblage on the river.\tSampling techniques included visual searches, splash-sampling and excavation sampling. Invertebrate identifications were by a competent taxonomist.\tFowles, A.P. (2016), The importance of exposed riverine sediments at Llanelltyd on the Afon Mawddach for invertebrates in 2016, NRW Evidence Report No: 182, 21pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tData is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database in Recorder 6 Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports\tOriginator: NRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (contractor: Adrian Fowles)       The contact (address) details of the Originator, Adrian Fowles, are withheld as personal data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-12-31\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-12-24T09:27:30.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18374\tNRW_DS116797\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.393Z\t205\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Status of the Gipsywort Weevil Datonychus Arquatus on Pembrey Air Weapons Range in 2016.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136373937\tThe gipsywort weevil Datonychus arquatus, currently classified in Britain as Red Data Book: Indeterminate, is known in the British Isles only from Pembrey Air Weapons Range (SN3604) where it was first discovered in 1997. It is an Independently Qualifying Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) Feature of the Arfordir Pen-bre/ Pembrey Coast SSSI and a Conservation Objective has been developed (Boyce 2007). This contract sought to repeat the monitoring of 2006 to assess the current Condition of the SSSI Feature.   Three Stations were identified in 2006 as supporting an abundance of the foodplant, gipsywort Lycopus europaeus, and the targets for Favourable Condition require assessment of each of these Stations. However, all of the fen vegetation in Stations 1 \u0026amp; 2 had been mown in the week prior to survey, which would have caused the loss of any sub-populations of D. arquatus in these areas had they been present. Survey of the adjacent grazing enclosure revealed an additional area of suitable habitat and this was assessed in addition to Station 3. A total of seven adult arquatus was recorded - five in Station 3 and two in the newly-discovered area, here designated as Station 4. Ten large patches of foodplant were recorded in total, but in four of these the plants were either too short or the area of the patch was insufficient. The loss of Stations 1 \u0026amp; 2 meant that an outcome of Unfavourable: Declining was inevitable and Station 4 is not big enough to compensate for the loss of habitat in those Stations.   Management recommendations are given for each Station, which include the need to allow fen vegetation to recover in Stations 1 \u0026amp; 2 by stopping the current practice of mowing. In Stations 3 \u0026amp; 4 regular small-scale management of scrub encroachment will be needed to ensure that gipsywort patches are not lost to succession. Light grazing should be continued in the enclosure alongside Station 2 to help maintain open fen conditions. A visit was also made to the nearby Ffrwd Farm reserve of the Wildlife Trust of South \u0026amp; West Wales to sample gipsywort populations there for D. arquatus. Here cattle grazing is sufficiently high to restrict gipsywort to protected situations underneath established willow carr etc., although other parts of the reserve without grazing stock, which weren\u0027t surveyed on this visit, may have healthier populations of the foodplant. No examples of D. arquatus were found but further surveys are recommended.\tSampling techniques included hand and visual searches. Invertebrate identifications were by a competent taxonomist.\tFowles, A.P. (2016), The status of the gipsywort weevil Datonychus arquatus on Pembrey Air Weapons Range in 2016, NRW Evidence Report No. 180 25pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tReport is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports Data is stored in the Welsh Invertebrate Database in Recorder 6\tOriginator: NRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (contractor: Adrian Fowles)       The contact (address) details of the Originator, Adrian Fowles, are withheld as personal data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18366\tNRW_DS116802\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.016Z\t890\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Canals Cycle 2\tCylch 2 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr ar gyfer cyrff dŵr camlesi\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383032\tThis dataset is a GIS layer identifying the canal waterbodies managed under the Water Framework Directive and any related programmes. This includes canals which are reported to Europe as artificial rivers. These waterbodies are represented by their centreline, and can be linked to other WFD data using the unique water body ID (WB_ID).   \u0027WFD River, canal and SWT Waterbodies Cycle 2\u0027 is a subset extracted from the Natural Resources Wales Detailed River Network, with attributes removed and merged on the WB_ID.   Updated to 2018 version.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn haen System Gwybodaeth Ddaearyddol sy\u0027n adnabod y cyrff dŵr camlas a reolir o dan y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr ac unrhyw rhaglenni perthnasol. Mae hyn yn cynnwys camlesi sy\u0027n cael eu hysbysu i Ewrop fel afonydd artiffisial. Mae\u0027r cyrff dŵr hyn yn cael eu cynrychioli gan eu llinell ganol, a gellir eu cysylltu at ddata\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr gan ddefnyddio rhif adnabod unigryw y corff dŵr (WB_ID). Mae \u0027Cylch 2 Cyrff Dŵr Afonydd, Camlesi a Throsglwyddiadau Dŵr Wyneb y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr\u0027 yn is-set a dynnwyd o Rwydwaith Afonydd Manwl Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru, gyda nodweddion wedi\u0027u tynnu a\u0027u cyfuno ar y WB_ID.  Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cael ei diweddaru bob chwe mlynedd. Mae hwn yn ddiweddariad o gyrff dŵr artiffisial Cylch 1 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr sydd ar gael o dan drwydded.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis is an update of WFD artificial waterbodies Cycle 1. This dataset is updated every 6 years.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle 2\\WFD Canals Cycle 2 2015.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Canals Cycle 2 2018 (Interim).lyr\tRob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS.      N.B. The cycle 1 dataset is not available on DataMapWales as unlike Cycle 2 data, cycle 1 data is derived from Third Party Data (Canals trust)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/429/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_CANALS_C2_LIVE\t2025-12-19T12:04:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18376\tNRW_DS116803\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.413Z\t894\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Surface Water Cycle 2\tCylch 2 Cyrff Dŵr Trosglwyddiadau Dŵr Wyneb y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383033\tThis dataset is a GIS layer identifying the Surface Water Transfer (SWT) waterbodies managed under the Water Framework Directive and any related programmes. This includes SWT which are reported to Europe as artificial rivers. These waterbodies are represented by their centreline, and can be linked to other Water Framework Directive (WFD) data using the unique water body ID (WB_ID). WFD River, canal and SWT Waterbodies Cycle 2, is a subset extracted from the Natural Resources Wales Detailed River Network, with attributes removed and merged on the WB_ID.  Updated to 2018 version-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys haen System Gwybodaeth Ddaearyddol sy\u0027n adnabod y cyrff dŵr Trosglwyddiad Dŵr Wyneb a reolir o dan y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr ac unrhyw raglenni cysylltiedig. Mae hyn yn cynnwys Trosglwyddiadau Dŵr Wyneb yr hysbysir Ewrop amdanynt fel afonydd artiffisial. Mae\u0027r cyrff dŵr hyn yn cael eu cynrychioli gan eu llinell ganol, a gellir eu cysylltu â data arall y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr gan ddefnyddio rhif adnabod unigryw y corff dŵr (WB_ID). Mae Cylch 2 Cyrff Dŵr Afonydd, Camlesi a Throsglwyddiadau Dŵr Wyneb y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr yn is-set a dynnwyd o Rwydwaith Afonydd Manwl Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru, gyda nodweddion wedi\u0027u tynnu a\u0027u cyfuno ar y WB_ID. Mae hwn yn ddiweddariad o gyrff dŵr artiffisial Cylch 1 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cael ei diweddaru bob chwe mlynedd. Datganiad priodoli Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis is an update of WFD artificial waterbodies Cycle 1. This dataset is updated every 6 years.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Surface Water Cycle 2 2015.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Surface Water Transfers Cycle 2 2018 (Interim).lyr\tRobert Blacklidge, Principal Officer IEA, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/427/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_SURFACE_WATER_C2_LIVE\t2025-05-07T15:28:32.313Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18375\tNRW_DS116805\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.389Z\t1175\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Species Data on the NBN Atlas\tData Rhywogaethau CNC ar Borth y Rhwydwaith Bioamrywiaeth Cenedlaethol (NBN Atlas)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383035\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) species data held on the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Atlas. This includes marine, terrestrial and freshwater species records for a variety of taxonomic groups. Data was collected to support core NRW functions including statutory and non-statutory reporting, condition monitoring and decision making. The NBN data portal is the main route for the publishing NRW species data. Details of each NRW species data can be viewed through the NBN Atlas.\tSpecies data is extracted from NRWs biological databases including; Recorder 6, Marine Recorder and KiEco (previously held in BioSys). The data held on the NBN Atlas is made up of a number of datasets, further information about each of these datasets can be found through the Atlas.  All records are made available to the public at a resolution of 100m under an Open Government Licence (OGL), apart from those deemed environmentally sensitive, which can be made available on request via the NBN Atlas request process.\t\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas  Top copies of data held within corporate store e.g. Recorder 6, Marine Recorder, BioSys, WISKI.   Extracted version in NBN Exchange format held Y:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\NBN Data.  Uploads from legacy CCW held on the H drive (to me moved to the NRW archive storage area)\tJohn Beattie Advisor 2 - Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-08-01-2025-02-06\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-03-03T16:48:00.459Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18371\tNRW_DS116807\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.499Z\t947\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Licences\tTrwyddedau Morol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383037\tThe records contain coordinate data associated with current marine licence applications in Wales. The location of disposal sites is also available. This data allows the locations of licence applications to be mapped to assess their impacts on designated sites and combined effects with other proposals.   Data related to historic and expired marine licence applications are held as a separate dataset (NRW_DS119099).-Mae\u0027r cofnodion hyn yn cynnwys data cyfesurynnol sy\u0027n gysylltiedig â cheisiadau presennol am drwyddedau morol yng Nghymru. Mae lleoliad safleoedd gwaredu ar gael hefyd. Mae\u0027r data\u0027n galluogi i leoliadau ceisiadau am drwyddedau gael eu mapio i asesu eu heffeithiau ar safleoedd dynodedig ac effeithiau cyfunol gyda chynigion eraill.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tNatural Resources Wales took over responsibility for issuing marine licences from the Welsh Government in 2013.  NRW do not hold all the accompanying documents for records pre 2013, created by Welsh Government.    This dataset has some information redacted due to National Security concerns.\t\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licence applications line WGS84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licence applications points WGS84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licence applications polys WGS84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licence disposal sites WGS84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Grab Sample Applications.lyr    The restricted munitions data is stored on restricted editing area of the X drive.  X:\\Editing Restricted\tEmmer Litt\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-11\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/874/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:MarineLicences\t2026-01-12T12:09:36.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18367\tNRW_DS116814\t2024-05-28T12:47:21.360Z\t2129\tValid\tEditable\tLIDAR terrain and surfaces models Wales\tModelau tir ac arwynebau LIDAR Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383134\tThis dataset shows the extent of data in each individual tile that went into the merging process to create the LIDAR Composite DSM \u0026amp; DTM.   Light Detection and Ranging (LIDAR) is an airborne mapping technique, which uses a laser to measure the distance between the aircraft and the ground. Up to 100,000 measurements per second are made of the ground, allowing highly detailed terrain models to be generated at spatial resolutions of between 25cm and 2 metres.   The Natural Resources Wales composite dataset contains digital elevation data derived from surveys carried out over several years and covers approximately 70% of Wales. We have made available 25cm, 50cm, 1m and 2m datasets, supplied as terrain models (a representation of the ground level) or surface models (a representation of object heights such as vehicles, buildings and vegetation).   In addition to the height information, georeferenced, coloured, shaded relief images at the same resolution as the input LiDAR data grids are available.\tThis dataset shows the extent of data in each individual tile that went into the merging process to create the LIDAR Composite DSM \u0026amp; DTM.\t\tX:\\Basemapping\\Height\\LiDAR\\Current LiDAR\tBen Anscombe / Louise Harris (Spatial Services Team).\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2017-01-01\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/6988/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_lidar_tile_catalogue_archive\t2025-05-22T14:15:37.390Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18364\tNRW_DS116819\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.849Z\t406\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAfon Teifi Special Area of Conservation (SAC) population attribute condition assessment for brook, river and sea lamprey population 2014 . Habitats Directive third reporting cycle 2013 – 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383139\tRiver lamprey (Lampetra fluviatilis), Brook lamprey (Lampetra planeri), and Sea lamprey (Petromyzon marinus) are designated features of the Afon Teifi Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Assessments of species composition, distribution, abundance and size composition of larval lamprey (ammocoetes and transformers) populations in these river are of particular interest as an indication of Condition Assessment. The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of Lampreys in the Teifi catchments. This collation of lamprey data is made up of data collected by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW),  Environment Agency Wales and Natural Resources Wales. The collation includes information about species distribution, composition and abundance for river, brook and sea lampreys which are a designated (Annex II) features within SAC rivers in Wales. The majority of the data was recorded at the genus level and no distinction was made between river and brook lampreys. However for some historical data no distinction was made between sea and river/brook lampreys and these were recorded at the family level.\tLamprey populations and condition assessments have previously been carried out by CCW in 2005 and 2013. The field survey was conducted in 2014 by Natural Resources Wales staff using the standard electrofishing methodology. A condition assessment is made using the JNCC Common Standards Monitoring Guidance for Freshwater Fauna (2016). Quantitative quadrat surveys were carried out in both optimal (very silty) and sub-optimal (more rocky) habitats. Electric fishing techniques were used inside a 1m2 quadrat to draw lamprey out of the silt rather than stunning and trapping them within the silt. Sampling of optimal habitats quadrats was done three times whilst sub-optimal habitats were sampled once. The original data contains positive and negative samples. Confidence in this dataset is high.\t\t- Data is stored in WISKI / KiECO  - Analysis: Workbook Teifi lamprey 2014.xlsx DMS ref: SAMP-753-136  - Report: Afon Teifi SAC population attribute condition assessment for brook, river and sea lamprey population 2014. Habitats Directive third reporting cycle 2013 – 2018. Report No. 106.\tData Custodian(s): Emma Keenan, Environmental Monitoring Officer Fisheries  Metadata Point of Contact:  Heather Garrett, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18365\tNRW_DS116820\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.848Z\t452\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBullhead (Cottus gobio) population condition assessment for River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Habitats Directive reporting cycle 3 2013 – 2018.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383230\tBullhead (Cottus gobio) is a designated feature of the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Assessments of bullhead distribution, population demographic and density in this river is of particular interest as an indication of condition assessment. The data was collected to help assess the conservation status of bullhead in the River Wye catchments. This collation of bullhead data is made up of by-catch records collected by Natural Resources Wales during salmonids, eel and coarse fish surveys. In addition a small number of by-catch records are also made during river macroinvertebrate and shad egg kick sampling surveys. The collation includes information about species distribution, size and abundance for bullhead which are a designated (Annex II) features within SAC rivers in Wales. The first 50 fish are measured and the remainder of the catch at a survey point are only counted.\tBullhead by-catch records were collected during juvenile salmonids, eel \u0026amp; coarse fish surveys conducted using electrofishing techniques. This data was previously held by Environment Agency (EA) in their National Fish Population Database (NFPD), but the Welsh data has now been transferred into Natural Resources Wales (NRW’s) WISKI / KiECO database.  Shad egg data collection was done using standard kick sampling techniques. These are currently held in paper record format and spreadsheets.\t\tOriginal data is stored in KiECO / WISKI with the exception of the shad data Analysis (when completed)  for each bullhead SAC river is held in a separate spreadsheet on DMS.  Wye: SAMP-753-344\tData Custodian(s): Emma Keenan, Environmental Monitoring Officer Fisheries  Metadata Point of Contact:  Heather Garrett, Freshwater Monitoring Coordinator\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:12:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18359\tNRW_DS116821\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.613Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine and Coastal Recreation Audit 2009\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383231\tIn 2009 an audit was undertaken of existing information and knowledge on marine and coastal recreation by Land Use Consultants on behalf of the Countryside Council for Wales.  The study was undertaken in two stages: - Identify, analyse and map existing data on recreational activities, including an validation with key bodies and robustness test - Review existing promotional material and guidance on recreational activities in relation to their compatibility with marine protected areas. The study looked water-based activities, Intertidal activities, and land-based activities.  The data described here is a series of spatial datasets.\tData was collated from several key sources - Ruiz (2005) – date was gathered on divers, sea-kayakers and wildlife trips through use of a questionnaire. - Coastal access database – data was extracted from an Environment Agency Wales database (no longer in existence) as a GIS layer, the data was based on personal perceptions and was not verified. - Natural Facilities Database – data was extracted from a 2007 Countryside Council for Wales/Sports Council jointly owned database (managed and maintained by Sport Council Wales) as a GIS layer. Data is based on 6 figure grid references and was partially verified. - Climbing locations provided by the British Mountaineering Council (BMC) - Important Bird Area adapted from data provided by the RSPB - Nature of coastline data which was derived from the NRW Phases 1 Habitat mapping (2009).  NRW only holds a static copy of the data from 2009 and therefore may not truly reflect current situation. It is recommended that you contact relevant data providers for the most up to date information available.\tLand Use Consultants (2009) Marine and Coastal Recreation Audit.  CCW Policy Research Report No. 09/02.   Ruis, A (2009) Spatial Distribution and Economic Valuation of Non-Extractive Recreational Activities Related to Marine biodiversity along the Welsh Coast\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\MCRA H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media1501to1600\\Media1552 Archived as project 475, media 1552.\tData: Kirsty Lindenbaum, Marine Resource Management Advisor or  Mary Lewis, Marine Strategic Plan Energy and Advice Team Leader Metadata point of contact: Monica Jones.  Original data i.e.  Natural Facilities database - contact is Sue Williams, Coastal Access Database is within Jont Bullbeck\u0027s team, Richard Deering is relevant contact, although database no longer exists other than what is available as GIS layers       The datasets have not gone through the Open Data Process and therefore should possible not be classed as open data as yet.  However it is unlikely  that there are any restriction.  The Coastal Access database does not appear to have used an OS admin boundary dataset as the background as it is too generalised for high water.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-10-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:50:59.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18361\tNRW_DS116826\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.699Z\t1309\tValid\tEditable\tHistoric LIDAR Archive\tArchif catalog teils LiDAR CNC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383236\tLight Detection and Ranging (LIDAR) is an airborne mapping technique, which uses a laser to measure the distance between the aircraft and the ground. Up to 100,000 measurements per second are made of the ground, allowing highly detailed terrain models to be generated at spatial resolutions of between 25cm and 2 metres. The Natural Resources Wales composite dataset contains digital elevation data derived from surveys carried out over several years and covers approximately 70% of Wales. We are making available 25cm, 50cm, 1m and 2m datasets, supplied as terrain models (a representation of the ground level) or surface models (a representation of object heights such as vehicles, buildings and vegetation). In addition to the height information, geo-referenced, coloured, shaded relief images at the same resolution as the input LiDAR data grids are available.-Catalog teils o Fodelau Tir Digidol (DTM) a Modelau Arwyneb Digidol (DSM) LiDAR Hanesyddol yng Nghymru a ddarperir gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru gyda hyperddolenni lawrlwytho.  Mae\u0027r data ar gael, yn dibynnu ar amser a lleoliad, mewn cydraniadau rhwng 0.25 a 2m. Mewn rhai lleoliadau bydd mwy nag un deilsen, yn cyfateb i ddyddiadau cipio hanesyddol lluosog.  Ar gyfer data LiDAR a gipiwyd yn gynharach, rhwng 1998 a 2002, weithiau dim ond y DSM sydd ar gael.  Darperir dull amgen o gyrchu\u0027r data trwy fap gwe dros dro sydd ar gael yma.   I lawrlwytho data DSM neu DTM gan ddefnyddio\u0027r map hwn, cliciwch ar y deilsen o ddiddordeb ac, yn y ffenestr naid, ar y ddolen(dolennau) sydd wedi\u0027u nodi fel \"More Info\".   Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl..\tThis dataset shows the extent of data in each individual tile that went into the merging process to create the LIDAR Composite DSM \u0026amp; DTM. This dataset refers to a catalogue of the histoically flown Lidar Surveys the most recent LiDAR surveys are avaialbe through the \"LIDAR terrain and surfaces models Wales\".\t\tx:\\Basemapping\\LiDAR\\Historic LiDAR\tBen Anscombe / Louise Harris (Spatial services team)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-01-01-2024-06-27\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/6988/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_lidar_tile_catalogue_archive\t2025-03-03T16:38:02.306Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18362\tNRW_DS116829\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.705Z\t460\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of the Condition of the White-Clawed Crayfish Austropotamobius pallipes in the River Wye Special Area of Conservation in 2014-2016\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383239\tBetween October 2014 and September 2016 work was undertaken to provide the data required to assess whether the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC) was in favourable condition for the white-clawed crayfish Austropotamobius pallipes. The Afon Edw, Nant yr Offeiriad, Sgithwen Brook, Dulas Brook (Builth Road), Clyro Brook and Afon Llynfi were surveyed to assess the condition of both crayfish populations and crayfish habitat. A further two rivers (Afon Duhonw and Afon Irfon) make up the full complement of the Wye SAC tributaries but no crayfish were found in these two rivers in 2003 and there are no subsequent records, so these were not surveyed during the current survey and it is assumed that no white-clawed crayfish are present in these two rivers for this assessment.  Sampling followed the methodology developed during the 2003 monitoring programme (Rogers \u0026amp; Watson 2004) and included a combination of manual searching and trapping. During the survey, white-clawed crayfish were only found in three of the eight tributaries of the River Wye SAC: Nant yr Offeiriad, Sgithwen Brook and Clyro Brook (although the latter is not included as part of the SAC, it is included within the condition assessment). Whilst crayfish numbers were relatively robust in Nant yr Offeiriad and Sgithwen Brook, populations have now become confined to the headwaters. In 2016, Clyro Brook still supports a thriving population as it did in 2003, and this now appears to be one of the most important populations in the Wye because there is no diminution in numbers or extent as in the Sgithwen and Offeiriad. In fact, both numbers and extent have increased in the Clyro. The lack of white-clawed crayfish in the Afon Edw, following a mortality in 2006, is worrying given that this has been a very important waterway for this species in the recent past. Also, there appeared to be no crayfish left in Dulas Brook (Builth Road) in 2015 despite supporting a good population in downstream reaches in 2003.  Using the provisional conservation objectives as part of the assessment of Favourable Conservation Status for Natura 2000 features, the 2014 - 2016 survey shows the River Wye SAC does not achieve Favourable Conservation Status. Despite abundant suitable habitat (Attribute 3) and a lack of disease and the absence of aliens (Attribute 4), the density of crayfish throughout is not high enough (Attribute 1) and the distribution throughout the monitoring units is not sufficient (Attribute 2) for the River Wye SAC to achieve Favourable Status. In 2013-14, the Natural Resources Wales\u0027 Cynrig Fish Culture Unit implanted approximately 3000 crayfish into Afon Chwefru, a tributary of the Irfon. As part of the present work, an additional survey of a 1km stretch in two 500 metre sections at the introduction sites was included but no crayfish were found. Recommendations include a review of protection for surviving white-clawed crayfish populations and further survey work to verify the presence/absence of native and alien crayfish throughout the main River Wye and all tributaries and sub-tributaries. Given the importance of Clyro Brook for crayfish, it is recommended that this tributary is notified as part of the Wye Site of Special Scientific Interest and Wye SAC. [This report provides an amalgamation of the 2014, 2015 and 2016 crayfish survey results, and supersedes the 2014 and 2015 survey reports (Rogers \u0026amp; Watson, 2015 and 2016)].\tSampling techniques included a combination of manual searching and trapping, and were undertaken by competent surveyors.\tRogers, D. \u0026amp; Watson, E. (2017), Assessment of the condition of the white-clawed crayfish Austropotamobius pallipes in the River Wye Special Area of Conservation in 2014-2016, NRW Report No. 187, 163pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database in Recorder 6\tOriginator: NRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (contractor: David Rogers Associates)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:36.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18363\tNRW_DS116830\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.762Z\t993\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Status and Distribution of the Strandline Beetle Eurynebria complanata on Whiteford Burrows, Cefn Sidan, Laugharne \u0026amp; Pendine Burrows and Frainslake Sands, Castlemartin in 2016.\tStatws a Dosbarthiad y Chwilen Draeth Eurynebria complanata ar Dwyni Whiteford, Cefn Sidan, Twyni Talacharn a Phentywyn a Thraeth Frainslake, Castellmartin yn 2016.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383330\tThe Strandline Beetle Eurynebria complanata was recently classified as Endangered following a decline that has led to an apparent retreat in its Great Britain range to a few beaches in Carmarthen Bay. Even here, recent counts have indicated that the population might be at a critically low level. The beetle is an indicator of the health of strandline communities, which include a range of rare and scarce species likely to be similarly threatened.   The main aim of this study was to survey sites in South Wales in 2016 in order to determine the current status of E. complanata and inform management of strandline habitats. Repeat visits to key sites were undertaken to assess spatial and temporal changes.   Strandline Beetle was found at just four sites, all in Carmarthen Bay, with peak counts in June of 328 adults on Whiteford Burrows, 211 adults on Cefn Sidan, 288 adults on Laugharne-Pendine Burrows and one adult on Broughton Burrows, providing a total Welsh count of 828 beetles.\tSampling techniques included visual searches, undertaken by competent surveyors.\tStewart, B., (2017), The status and distribution of the Strandline Beetle Eurynebria complanata on Whiteford Burrows, Cefn Sidan, Laugharne \u0026amp; Pendine Burrows and Frainslake Sands, Castlemartin in 2016, NRW Report No. 189 80pp., Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database in Recorder 6\tOriginator: NRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (contractor: Barry Stewart Associates)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-23T11:23:30.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18360\tNRW_DS116836\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.640Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOtter Distribution Maps of Wales (1977-1980)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383336\tHand drawn maps showing the extent of rivers where otters are distributed.  Appears to be repeat surveys of 15 Rivers (with some additional sub-sites/tributaries).  Survey carried out 1977-1978, 1979-1980, 1980.\tThe 19977-78 and 1979-80 maps appear to be identical so it is difficult to see if these are a duplicate or a repeat survey.  Also a third set of maps that also appear to be a duplicate. This is a part of a larger collation of historic paper record of otter extent\t\tPaper format only - Held in in Evidence \u0026amp; Analysis files.   Maps are to be scanned and stored in DMS\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team       The paper maps will be scanned in-house (Worked carried out by Rhonwen Williams for the Terrestrial Mammal Group).  Long-term it would be useful to digitise the data into GIS either in-house or by offering to a third party (e.g. otter group, LERC).  The metadata is to remain as internal view only until available in an electronic format.  To check that this is not the same as the data described by EA as AFA224\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18358\tNRW_DS116837\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.324Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPaper Data: NCC Topographical/ Terrain Survey of Sites around Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383337\tto be supplied  3 boxes of paper surveys. Contains hand annotated maps of transect lines, and print out of information such as feature (trig point, fence etc..)grid ref, bearing, distance (3 boxes).  This was field surveys carried out by the Cartography team in the early 1990\u0027s to validated and identify features and reference points that were used to accurately map the boundary of designated sites.  These are to be stored in off-site storage long-term in case the boundaries are queried\t\t\tCurrently in the Shelves in Old post Room F12.  To be sent to off-site storage, Hannah/Jill to supply the archive reference\tKeith Jones, Spatial Information Team Hannah Brazier, Spatial Information Team       Long-term the data could be scanned and disposed off subject to custodian agreement. Entry should not be included in WebView as data is not suitable for public supply\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:06:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18357\tNRW_DS116847\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.273Z\t721\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBiodiversity Action Reporting System (BARS) Archived Action Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313136383437\tAn archive of NRWs (and legacy bodies) actions, boundaries and project data, downloaded December 2016, following the closure of the Biodiversity Action Reporting System.  Biodiversity actions are recorded as GIS point and polygon data with related information held in spreadsheets.  BARS was originally created to support the UK Biodiversity Action Plan (UKBAP) and allowed organisations, companies and partnerships to manage and share their own Biodiversity Action Plans.  The UKBAP closed in 2011 and the last version of BARS was launched in April 2012 to collate biodiversity actions in a standardised form and capture geographic locations, enabling spatial analysis and reporting of activity.  The UK BARS partnership (DEFRA, Natural England, Northern Ireland Environment Agency, Scottish Natural Heritage, Wales Biodiversity Partnership, and Joint Nature Conservation Committee) took the difficult decision to discontinue support for BARS in November 2016.\tThe actions were recorded by the various BARS project officers in an online map viewer. In many cases, there are multiple actions per polygon (hence duplicated polygon data). The GIS data should be rated as poor quality and needs cleaning.  Data Repatriation: Data supplied to BARS is archived securely by JNCC.  All organisations were offered an extract of their own action and project data (including spatial locations) to download and reuse in their own systems.   From March 2017, this data will be downloadable by all interested parties direct from the JNCC website.\tArchived material includes GIS datasets (BARS_Orgs_Actions_Point.shp, BARS_Orgs_Actions_Polygon.shp), spreadsheets (ActionList.csv, ActionProgresList.csv, CandidateActionList.csv, ProjectActionList, ProjectCandidateActionList.csv, ProjectList.csv, ProjectProgresList.csv), 2 2 Case study PDFs and an Explanatory Word document / Readme.txt\tAn archive of the full data is stored as project T008, T_Media020. Copies of relevant spreadsheet will be stored in the DMS\tData and metadata: Terrestrial Mapping Specialist, Jonathan Rothewell Data and System: Specialist Advisor Enterprise Data Management, Barnaby Letheren       The GIS data should be rated as poor quality and will need cleaning up if it is to be made available as a corporate layer\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:36.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18356\tNRW_DS117097\t2024-05-28T12:47:20.195Z\t298\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Status of the Snowdon Beetle Chrysolina cerealis on Yr Wyddfa (2016)\tStatws Chwilen yr Wyddfa Chrysolina cerealis ar Yr Wyddfa (2016)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137303937\tThe Snowdon Beetle Chrysolina cerealis is restricted in the United Kingdom to Snowdon (Yr Wyddfa), although it was also found in Cwm Idwal until 1980. As recent records of the beetle have been few, a survey was undertaken in 2015 (NRW_DS116513) in an attempt to determine its status and distribution. A total of five beetles (four larvae and one adult) was found following 63.5 hours of hand searching on and around thyme plants (the only recorded foodplant of the beetle in the UK) and stone-turning near patches of thyme from June to September 2015, with all specimens found in Clogwyn Coch in September. No beetles were found in Cwm du\u0027r Arddu, its traditional hotspot, despite exhaustive searches.   A continuation of the survey from May to October 2016 located ten adults and one larva following 69 hours of search, with all adults recorded in Clogwyn Coch and the larva found under a stone in Cwm Glas Bach. The 2016 survey reinforces the suggestion that the beetle may be retreating to higher altitudes, perhaps as a consequence of climate change or other factors. Over the two year period, 16 beetles (11 adults and 5 larvae) have been found after more than 130 hours of search. As in 2015, no beetles were found in Cwm Idwal.\tSampling techniques included visual searches and stone turning, undertaken by competent surveyors.\tNational Museums Liverpool 2017. The status of the Snowdon Beetle Chrysolina cerealis on Yr Wyddfa in 2016. NRW Evidence Report No: 193, 18pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database in the Recorder 6\tOriginator: NRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-12-24T09:27:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18403\tNRW_DS117501\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.189Z\t937\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Woodland sub-compartment data\tData Is-adran Coetiroedd CNC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137353031\tThe NRW Woodland Sub-compartment Database (SCDB) is to be our authoritative data source, giving us information for recording, monitoring, analysis and reporting which supports decision-making on the whole of the NRW woodland estate.   Information from the SCDB is used by NRW, wider government, industry and the public for economic, environmental and social forest-related decision-making. Furthermore, it supports forest related national policy development and government initiatives, and helps us meet our national and international forest-related reporting responsibilities.     The Inventory SCDB, will help provide a definitive measure of trends in extent, structure, composition, health, status, use, and management of NRW land holdings. We require this to meet national and international commitments, to report on the sustainable management of forests as well as to help us through the process of business and Forest Design Planning. As well as helping with the above, the SCDB helps us address detailed requests from industry, government, non-government organisations and the public for information on our estate.   NRW\u0027s growing national and international responsibilities and the requirements for monitoring and reporting on a range of forest statistics have highlighted the technical challenges we face in providing consistent, national level data.-Mae\u0027r Gronfa Ddata Is-adran yn ddisgrifiad ffisegol o\u0027r tir mae CNC yn ei reoli. Y Gronfa Ddata Is-adran yw ein ffynhonnell ddata awdurdodedig, sy\u0027n rhoi gwybodaeth i ni ar gyfer cofnodi, monitro, dadansoddi a hysbysu. Trwy hyn, mae\u0027n cefnogi\u0027r gwaith o wneud penderfyniadau cyffredinol am ystad goetir CNC. Defnyddir gwybodaeth o\u0027r rhestr gan CNC, y llywodraeth ehangach, diwydiant a\u0027r cyhoedd er mwyn gwneud penderfyniadau economaidd, amgylcheddol a chymdeithasol sy\u0027n ymwneud â choedwigoedd. At hynny, mae\u0027n cefnogi datblygiad polisi cenedlaethol a mentrau llywodraeth perthnasol sy\u0027n ymwneud â choedwigoedd, ac yn ein helpu i gyflawni ein dyletswyddau cenedlaethol a rhyngwladol o ran adrodd am faterion sy\u0027n ymwneud â choedwigoedd.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe Sub-Compartment Database (SCDB) contains a physical description of the land that the NRW manages on behalf of the public. It was originally a database of individual site or sub-compartment records which contained both crop and site information.   The SCDB is not just about trees, 70% of the land that we manage is of a forest nature, yet 30% by definition is not. This non-forest land covers, farmland, open mountaintops, heathland, estuarine and riparian habitats.     Sub compartments are areas that are similar in land use, species or habitat composition, yield class, age, condition, thinning history etc. to be treated as a single unit. They will generally be contiguous in nature and will not be split by roads, rivers, open space etc. Distinct boundaries are required, and these will often change as crops are felled, thinned, replanted and resurveyed. In some parts of the country foresters used historical and topographical features to delineate sub-compartment boundaries, such as hedges, walls and escarpments. In other areas no account of the history and topography of the site was taken, with field boundaries, hedges, walls, streams etc. being subsumed into the sub-compartment.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest SubCompartments.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan Secondary Metadata contact: Keith Harri Jones\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/504/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_PRODUCTISED_SCDB_LLE\t2026-01-12T14:31:31.086Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18405\tNRW_DS117519\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.325Z\t628\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Tree Cover 2013\tRhestr Goed Drefol 2013\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137353139\tThis spatial dataset relates to Phase 3 (2013) and consists of 3 individual layers, being Points (individual trees), polygons (groups of trees) and urban extents (the extent of the study areas). The technical report (see Related Titles and lineage) shows what is covered in each Phase (page 25), and provides more detail on the methodology behind the different layers in the dataset in Appendix A2.1 (page 132).-Mae\u0027r set ddata ofodol hon yn gysylltiedig Cham 3 (2013) Urban Tree Cover ac yn cynnwys 3 lefel unigol, sef Pwyntiau (coed unigol), polygonau (grwpiau o goed) a maint ardaloedd trefol (maint yr ardaloedd astudio). Yn wahanol i Gam 1 a Cham 2 ni chafodd dosbarthiadau defnydd tir eu creu ar gyfer cam 3. Mae\u0027r adroddiad technegol yn dangos beth sy\u0027n cael ei gynnwys ym mhob Cam, ac mae\u0027n rhoi mwy o fanylion am y fethodoleg y tu ôl i\u0027r gwahanol haenau yn y set ddata.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis data was captured using desktop analyses of aerial photography 2013.  Methodology is described in: NRW (2016) Tree cover in Wales\u0027 towns and cities: understanding canopy cover to better plan and manage our urban trees - Full Report 2016.  Natural Resources Wales.\tFryer D 2016 Urban Tree Cover in Wales’ Towns and Cities :Understanding canopy cover to better plan and manage our urban trees - 146pp Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tX:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2013\\Urban Trees Point 2013.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2013\\Urban Trees Poly 2013.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\2009\\Urban Trees Land Categories 2009.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\Urban Extent 2013.lyr\tCustodian:  John Briggs, Landscape Architect, PPG Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-01-17\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/875/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:UrbanTreeCover2013Polygons\t2025-05-02T15:21:26.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18406\tNRW_DS117601\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.327Z\t366\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpatial Action Plan for Great Crested Newts in Wrexham, A Manual for Achieving Favourable Conservation Status.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137363031\tThis report draws on a series of previous research reports commissioned by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and its predecessor body on determining the conservation status of great crested newts, Triturus cristatus, in north Wales. The Welsh Government is obligated to maintain or restore the favourable conservation status of great crested newts within Wales and this report aims to provide practical guidance on how this can be achieved within the County Borough of Wrexham. It is one of a series of three such reports, the other two covering the counties of Flintshire and Anglesey. Due to its legal status, the great crested newt is the focus of considerable conservation effort from a variety of bodies. This document aims to provide an overarching strategy to guide developers, conservation NGO\u0027s and local and national government in their efforts in order to achieve the greatest conservation benefit for great crested newts and ensure a unified approach to achieving Favourable Conservation Status (FCS) across the county. To this end, the report is accompanied by a number of GIS layers which will be of particular practical use to those engaged in great crested newt conservation across Wrexham.  The report presents a scoring method by which the Current Conservation Status (CCS) of great crested newts can be determined at multiple geographical scales from a single pond to the entire county. It then uses predictive modelling based on geographical, distributional, climatic and historical data to produce habitat suitability maps and to derive survey targets and pond creation targets to enable FCS to be achieved within the county. These techniques can be applied in various land use and planning scenarios and the report provides practical guidance on identifying areas of particularly high or low value for great crested newts, assessing the impacts of proposed developments, designing effective mitigation and the implications of agri-environment schemes for great crested newts.   During the modelling process, a total of 2,593 extant ponds were identified within the County Borough of Wrexham. With a view to redressing historic loss (37% of ponds having been lost since the 1843 mapping data), FCS was considered in the light of the level of occupancy that would have occurred at the time of the original mapping relative to current levels of occupancy and Habitat Suitability of ponds to the species (Habitat Suitability Index (HSI) scores). Historically we estimate there would have been 4,116 ponds in Wrexham and a corresponding 640 ponds occupied by great crested newts and 988 ponds with an HSI score \u0026gt;0.7. We propose that the 640 occupied ponds should be used to provide a target level for great crested newts (Favourable Reference Value for Range and Habitat sufficiency). This is further qualified by requiring these ponds have an HSI \u0026gt;0.7 to ensure habitat quality, has at least a medium population, are found at a density of at least 4 per km2 (to ensure population viability) and has a minor (or lower) level of threat (to address the species\u0027 future prospects). The Current Status of the species in Wrexham is estimated as having 403 ponds occupied by great crested newts.   The approach to identifying CCS and FCS laid out within this document is one of a number which are in the process of being developed across the UK and Europe. Whilst there is currently no single standard approach which can be applied uniformly across a range of species and habitats, one may emerge in the future. With the recent increase in studies looking at European Protected Species and with developing technologies (such as remote sensing and environmental DNA analysis) we may expect that new approaches to status assessment of great crested newts will develop in the near future. We would encourage efforts to learn from this work to further develop practical tools and necessary policy measures to aid the conservation of both for this and other species. However, this d ...\tIt is practically impossible to achieve complete survey coverage of great crested newts within Wrexham and this presents difficulties when making informed decisions regarding their conservation, such as how FCS can be achieved. One approach to filling the significant gaps in our knowledge of great crested newts within the area is to use a predictive model. Over recent years, Amphibian and Reptile Conservation (ARC), in conjunction with Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and its predecessor body, have developed such a model for great crested newts in north Wales, including Wrexham. Previous versions of the model are documented by Amphibian \u0026amp; Reptile Conservation et al (2010) and Arnell \u0026amp; Wilkinson (2011; 2013).  The model underpinning this spatial plan is based on Arnell \u0026amp; Wilkinson (2013). They compiled high resolution, recent (1990-2011) great crested newt records and then used the distribution modelling software, MaxEnt (Elith et al 2006), along with environmental layers to produce an estimated probability of presence output for great crested newts in northeast Wales at a 25m resolution across the study area. The model used 19 bioclimatic variables, the effect of land cover was incorporated using the Land Cover Map 2007, pond density was extracted from Ordnance Survey Mastermap and Slope and Aspect layers were derived from elevation data using GIS software. Full methodological details of the model construction are provided within Arnell \u0026amp; Wilkinson (2013). This model has subsequently been modified to include flood risk data as although floodplains often contain many ponds, these are typically unsuitable for great crested newts as they often contain predatory fish. This had caused the earlier versions to overestimate the suitability of floodplains.\tRussell, L. Starnes, T. and Wilkinson, J. (2017), Spatial Action Plan for Great Crested Newts in Wrexham, A Manual for Achieving Favourable Conservation Status, NRW Science Report Series. Report 77 pp69, NRW, Bangor.\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr E.A.Howe (Contractor: Amphibian and Reptile Conservation Trust)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:04.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18408\tNRW_DS117602\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.342Z\t1103\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironmental Incident data held in Wales Incident Recording System (WIRS)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137363032\tDetails of all environmental incidents that are reported to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) via the environmental incident hotline or by internal NRW staff via the online reporting form are held on the Wales Incident Recording System (WIRS). WIRS in one of NRW\u0027s centralised storage databases. It hold information related to but not restricted too, incidents such as; environmental pollution (land, water and air); illegal dumping of waste (including fly-tipping); damage, destruction and removal of sensitive habitats and species; and incidents relating to the increased risk of flooding. Approximately 9000 calls reporting incidents in Wales are received and processed each year. A risk based approach is used to assess response and is proportionate to our ability to take action to prevent or mitigate impact to the environment; NRW\u0027s role within the incident: and NRW\u0027s professional reputation in influencing the greater public interest. Response time to all major and significant incidents is within 2 hours during office hours and 4 hours outside office hours. Other incidents are assessed based on the risk based approach.  As of 2016-03-01, data is recorded and stored on the Wales Incident Recording System (WIRS). Prior to this, between 2013-04-01 to 2016-02-29 data was stored on the National Incident Recording System (NIRS). The system allows user to manage and track the progress of incidents.  All pertinent details relating to an incident should be recorded as soon as possible. The data recorded over time relating to incidents can be used to provide management information, determine trends and highlight areas of concern, amongst other uses.\tOnce data for an individual incident in complete, that data is locked and can’t be amended.  It can however be extracted  in detail/summary formats to be included in reports The system forces users to enter many mandatory fields, though given the wide range on incident types, internal validation is difficult. Reports can be generated to highlight any potential data quality issues  Incidents are recorded at a national scale and include information covering: date and time; location; reporter details, key dates and times relating to incident management; pollutants, premises, cause, incident categorisation; and action taken. Incidents are categorised on an impact scale of:  Category 1 - major, serious, persistent and or extensive impact or effect on the environment, people and/or property;  Category 2 - significant impact or effect on the environment, people and/or property;  Category 3 - minor or minimal impact or effect on the environment, people and/or property;  Category 4 - no impact.\t\tData is stored in WIRS. WIRS is stored on its own server.\tInternal Data Custodian(s): John Farnhill, Data Quality Officer, NRW  Metadata Point of Contact:  Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:00:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18414\tNRW_DS117691\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.328Z\t759\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey for the Blue Ground Beetle Carabus intricatus at Coed Maesmelin and other Woodlands in the Neath Valley, Glamorgan (2016)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313137363931\tThe chance finding of several adult Blue Ground Beetles Carabus intricatus in a garden on the edge of Neath in 2012 and 2013 led to the discovery of a population in the nearby Coed Maesmelin Woodland Trust reserve in January 2015 during a NRW-funded survey. Three adults were recorded, and recommendations were made to improve the quality of the woodland habitat for the beetle by re-introducing grazing and cutting back bramble to provide more open conditions.  Survey work in February 2016 focussed on finding additional populations of the beetle in woodlands within close proximity to Coed Maesmelin. A total of eight sites were visited during this second phase of survey work but no beetles were found, although some areas in the upper Mellte and Nedd valleys and at Craig Gwladys Country Park appeared to have highly suitable habitat. Coed Maesmelin was also re-visited, with just a single adult being found here on 4th February 2016. Bramble growth appeared to be even stronger than in the previous year and given the absence of other records from the Neath area, implementation of a programme of remedial management that aims to improve the habitat quality here is required urgently.\tSampling techniques included visual searches, undertaken by competent surveyors.\tBoyce, D.C. \u0026amp; Walters, J.M., (2016). Survey for the Blue Ground Beetle Carabus intricatus at Coed Maesmelin and other woodlands in the Neath Valley, Glamorgan, NRW Evidence Report No: 184, 26pp., Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\t[A] Report is held in the DMS report Archive [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (David Boyce)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:26:15.397Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18412\tNRW_DS118108\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.345Z\t403\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInvestigating the Behaviour and Ecology of the Glutinous Snail Myxas glutinosa in Field Conditions in Llyn Tegid (2016)\tYmchwilio Ymddygiad ac Ecoleg y Falwen Ludiog Myxas glutinosa mewn Amodau allan yn y Maes yn Llyn Tegid (2016)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138313038\tThe Glutinous Snail Myxas glutinosa is an aquatic species restricted to a single known population in Britain located in Llyn Tegid.  Following on from successful captive breeding experiments and their revelations, proposals were made to use the knowledge acquired to begin field experiments that might lead to improved understanding of Myxas natural ecology and appropriate management procedures.  The data and associat report comprises the instigation of that work.   The experiments proposed are a tentative method to probe into the life of Myxas and answer the following questions:   Does Myxas migrate up and down the shore as lake levels recede and advance?   Do changes in water level benefit or harm the snail population or are they benign?   What substrates favour Myxas?  What physicochemical differences occur across Llyn Tegid and what effect do they have on Myxas?  Would Myxas benefit from the placement of refuges at particular sites around the lake and if so, where and of what material?   Is it possible to manage habitats with simple methods that benefit Myxas and could be employed to return Myxas to its former range?    The first stage has been completed with the installation of bespoke concrete refuges and a brief second inspection of these has already begun to provide some insight into the life of wild Myxas in the lake and an opportunity to begin mapping areas of the lake bed where Myxas occurs.\tSurveys were by visual searches and stone-turning. Artificial refugia have been installed at various points within the lake.\tHughes, I. (2017), Instigation of actions to investigate the behaviour and ecology of the glutinous snail Myxas glutinosa in field conditions in Llyn Tegid in 2016, NRW Evidence Report No 199: 41pp., Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\t[A] Report is held in the DMS report Archive  [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Ian Hughes)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr1212\t2025-12-23T14:30:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18413\tNRW_DS118342\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.321Z\t380\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Status of the Caddis Adicella filicornis in Wales (2016)\tCyflwr Pryf Gwellt Adicella filicornis yng Nghymru (2016)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138333432\tA survey for the Endangered caddis Adicella filicornis (Trichoptera) in 2016 found pupal cases at all four Welsh sites with modern records, and a single adult. Populations at Coed Dol-goch, Halton Wood, Prisk Wood and Wilderness Wood appear to be small and the habitat used by the caddis - very small permanent streams on quite steep slopes close to their origin from groundwater springs - is also highly localised and very fragile. Only Coed Dol-goch supports a reasonable area of habitat with several stations being occupied.   None of the sites has any statutory protection, although Prisk Wood is owned by the Gwent Wildlife Trust and Coed Dol-goch is a community woodland. Consideration should be given to notifying one, if not all, sites as Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) for Adicella filicornis.   Prisk Wood and Wilderness Wood also support the only Welsh populations of the rare cranefly Ellipteroides alboscutellatus, a species associated with tufa-depositing seepages. Further surveys for Adicella filicornis at all four occupied sites are required to determine its status and distribution and to identify threats to its habitat.\tSurveys were by visual searches for adults, larvae and pupae\tWallace, ID \u0026amp; Wallace, B., (2017), The status of the caddis Adicella filicornis in Wales in 2016, NRW Evidence Report No: 200, 28pp., Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\t[A] Report is held in the DMS report Archive [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Dr Ian Wallace)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr1212\t2025-12-24T09:23:56.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18407\tNRW_DS118344\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.317Z\t626\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Survey for the Caddis Limnephilus tauricus on Cors Erddreiniog, Anglesey (2016)\tArolwg o Bryfed y Gwellt Limnephilus tauricus ar Gors Erddreiniog, Ynys Môn (2016)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138333434\tA survey of Cors Erddreiniog National Nature Reserve (NNR) from July to September 2016 failed to find the caddis Limnephilus tauricus (classified as Vulnerable in the UK) at its only Welsh locality. First found on the reserve in 2006 in a small area of permanently wet, gently-flowing, spring-fed fen supporting an extensive bed of Yellow Flag Iris pseudacorus, small numbers of adults were also found at the same station in 2007 and 2008. No larvae have been found to date. Wider searches of apparently suitable habitat on Cors Erddreiniog in 2008 and 2016 proved fruitless. Whilst the vegetation at the original station looked very similar to the situation in 2006-08, the water flowing through the site was less clear and appeared to contain more iron flocculence. Further searches of Cors Erddreiniog NNR are required to determine the current status of Limnephilus tauricus.\tSurveys were by visual searches, light-trapping and torching at night.\tWallace, ID, Wallace, B, Wallace, GJ \u0026amp; Wallace, MC., (2017), A survey for the caddis Limnephilus tauricus on Cors Erddreiniog, Anglesey in 2016, NRW Evidence Report No: 201, 6pp., Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\t[A] Report is held in the DMS report Archive  [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Dr Ian Wallace)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr1212\t2025-12-24T09:26:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18410\tNRW_DS118345\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.344Z\t343\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Survey for the Sandhill Rustic Luperina nickerlii gueneei on Newborough Warren National Nature Reserve (2016)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138333435\tA total of 149 adults of the Sandhill Rustic Luperina nickerlii gueneei were recorded on Newborough Warren between 29th August and 2nd September 2016. This included 121 adults on saltmarsh and dune edge at Penllyn Pysgod and Braich Abermenai, and 28 adults on Cefni Dunes \u0026amp; saltmarsh. Numbers indicate robust populations at these two sites. They have survived the 2013-14 winter storms which eroded large areas of the fore dunes on Newborough Warren and may have responded to developing Sand Couch habitat following the event. The total count contrasts sharply with the previous survey in 2010 which recorded just twelve adults. However, seaward-facing populations in front of Newborough Forest at Ro Bach and Traeth Llanddwyn, and at Gwddw Llanddwyn on Ynys Llanddwyn, where the moth was recorded in small numbers in 2010, seem to have been lost. It is suggested that areas of suitable breeding habitat are mapped as they develop to determine their extent, and that the Sandhill Rustic population is monitored at regular intervals, depending upon the frequency of severe winter storms.\tSurveys were by visual searches and torching at night.\tWallace, ID, Wallace, B \u0026amp; Wallace, MC., (2017), A survey for the Sandhill Rustic Luperina nickerlii gueneei on Newborough Warren National Nature Reserve in 2016, NRW Evidence Report No: 202, 26pp., Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\t[A] Report is held in the DMS report Archive  [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Dr Ian Wallace)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:23:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18415\tNRW_DS118482\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.435Z\t640\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAfon Teifi and Afon Eden Condition Assessment (2016)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138343832\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS) was carried out in 2016 at 27 sample sites on the Afon Teifi and six sample sites on the Afon Eden. The data was collected to feed into condition assessment of the Afon Teifi and Afon Eden according to Common Standards Monitoring guidance for Rivers for the following physical river habitat quality attributes: Channel planform, Habitat Modification Score, Bank vegetation naturalness, Riparian zone vegetation naturalness, Large woody debris, In-channel structures, Fine sediment, Siltation, Negative indicators: alien/locally absent. species\tNRW is responsible for monitoring the condition of the river habitat quality on Special Area of Conservation (SAC) rivers designated for the Ranunculion feature; more formally known as “Water courses of plain to montane levels with the Ranunculion fluitantis and Callitricho-Batrachion vegetation” (EU habitat code 3260). NRW is required to evaluate the conservation status of this river habitat. The Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance (JNCC, 2016) sets out eight river habitat quality assessment attributes for the condition of this SAC feature. Specific details for RHS survey methods are detailed in the River Habitat Survey Manual (EA, 2003).\tBaxter, E., McKenzie, S., Jones, C., Jones, D. and Metcalfe, P. (2017), Condition assessment using 2016 River Habitat Survey data and Common Standards Monitoring guidance for the Afon Teifi and Afon Eden – Cors Goch Trawsfynydd SACs, NRW Evidence Report No: 192, 89 pp, NRW, Bangor.   EA. 2003. River Habitat Survey in Britain and Ireland. Field Survey Guidance Manual: 2003 Version. Environment Agency. JNCC. 2016. Common Standards Monitoring Guidance for Rivers. Version September 2016 (Updated from January 2014), Peterborough: Joint Nature Conservation Committee.\tData is stored in WISKI\tDr Sue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, NRW (Ecus Ltd) Melanie Lacan, Senior Environmental Monitoring Officer, Rhymney \u0026amp; Ebbw A\u0026amp;R, NRW, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:59:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18411\tNRW_DS118494\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.333Z\t509\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAfon Eden - Cors Goch Trawsfynydd Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Freshwater Pearl Mussel Population Condition Assessment Report - Habitats Directive Reporting Cycle 3 (2013 - 2018)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138343934\tFreshwater pearl mussel counts were carried out in specific monitoring sections and transects on the Afon Eden. The methodology followed protocol devised for the Afon Eden in 2003. The data was collected to feed into condition assessment of the pearl mussel population on the Afon Eden according to Common Standards Monitoring guidance for Freshwater fauna for the following attributes: Spatial extent, Population density, Age structure, Dead shells, Suitable habitat\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) is responsible for monitoring the condition of the river habitat quality on Special Area of Conservation (SAC) rivers designated for the Freshwater Pearl Mussel feature (Margaritifera margaritifera, EU habitat code 1029). NRW is required to evaluate the conservation status of this river species. The Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance (Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC), 2015) sets out four population sub-attributes for the condition of this SAC feature.\tHearn, SM. (2017), Afon Eden – Cors Goch Trawsfynydd SAC Freshwater Pearl Mussel Population Condition Assessment Report. Habitats Directive Reporting Cycle 3 2013 – 2018. NRW Evidence Report No: 169 30pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor. JNCC. 2015. Common Standards Monitoring Guidance for Freshwater fauna. Version October 2015. (Updated from August 2005). Peterborough: Joint Nature Conservation Committee.  Killeen IJ. 2004. Monitoring of the Freshwater Pearl Mussel on the Afon Eden Candidate Special Area of Conservation. CCW Environmental Monitoring Report No. 10. Bangor: CCW.\tReport is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports\tNRW Rivers Ecologist: Sue Hearn (Heather Garrett, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, NRW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:23:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18404\tNRW_DS118585\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.223Z\t464\tValid\tEditable\tHistoric General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicators of Water Courses\tPrif Ddangoswyr Hanesyddol Asesiad Ansawdd Cyffredinol o Gyrsiau Dŵr\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138353835\tThe General Quality Assessment (GQA) Headline Indicator scheme or GQAHI (previously known as GQA) was the national indicator for water quality in rivers and canals. It was designed to provide an accurate and consistent assessment of the state of water quality and how it changes over time. These assessments were made for Biological, Chemical and Nutrients and undertaken for discrete river stretches.  This is now a static dataset and 2009 was the final year of the scheme.-Y cynllun Prif Ddangosydd Asesiad Ansawdd Cyffredinol oedd y dangosydd cenedlaethol ar gyfer ansawdd dŵr mewn afonydd a chamlesi. Cafodd ei gynllunio i ddarparu asesiad cywir a chyson o gyflwr ansawdd dŵr a sut mae\u0027n newid dros amser. Roedd yr asesiadau hyn ar gyfer olion biolegol, cemegol a maetholion ac fe\u0027u cynhaliwyd ar gyfer darnau afon ar wahân. Mae hwn bellach yn set ddata statig a 2009 oedd blwyddyn derfynol y cynllun.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThis is an over arching metadata record for the Biological, Chemical and Nutrient datasets. See the specific metadata for each dataset for further information.\t\tY:\\Knowledge_and_Information_Management\\NRW Data Publishing Area\\Open Data\\NRW_DS118585\tMetadata point of Contact: Data Services Custodian: Jane Roberts-  Environmental Reporting Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-03-21\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2640\t2025-05-02T14:50:59.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18397\tNRW_DS118643\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.059Z\t823\tValid\tEditable\tTranquil Areas Wales\tMapiau o ardaloedd tawel gwledig yng Nghymru 2009\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138363433\tThis data collation is made up of two datasets showing tranquil areas across Wales for 1997 and 2009. A map of the land of Wales, graded to show levels of Tranquillity.  It results from combining several underlying layers of data, covering different factors that either contribute to tranquillity or detract from tranquillity. Produced to provide a strategic baseline for monitoring change in tranquillity in Wales to inform strategic planning and policy-making.   Tranquillity is an aspect of the Welsh Government\u0027s Noise Action Plan (in response to the European Noise Directive) and is one of the qualitative measures of natural resources benefits provided by landscapes, as used in SoNaRR 2016.  An accompanying technical report details the methodology used and provides map images of underlying data layers and the resulting Tranquil Areas Map.   Caveats:  1. The resolution of some layers that informed the map were coarse, meaning that those parts of the data are unsuitable for using at a detailed scale.   2. Tranquillity was not formally defined in the accompanying technical report.  Welsh Government has more recently defined the term and Natural Resources Wales adopted this definition for SoNaRR 2016.    Tranquillity is is an untroubled state, which is peaceful, calm and free from unwanted disturbances. This can refer to a state of mind or a particular environment.  The 2009 dataset repeats more or less the same methodology used for a similar study in 1997.  This allows change over time to be reported. Other projects in other parts of the UK using data at better resolution and with more sophisticated analysis means that the Wales 1997 and 2009 studies are regarded as crude.  However to date the 2009 study is the most recent Tranquil Areas map available for Wales.-Map o dir Cymru, gyda graddau gwahanol yn dangos lefelau Llonyddwch. Mae\u0027n deillio o gyfuno sawl haen sylfaenol o ddata sy\u0027n cwmpasu gwahanol ffactorau sydd naill ai\u0027n cyfrannu at lonyddwch neu\u0027n lleihau llonyddwch. Cynhyrchwyd y map i gynnig llinell sylfaen strategol ar gyfer monitro newid mewn llonyddwch yng Nghymru fel y gellir llywio cynllunio strategol a gwaith llunio polisïau. Mae llonyddwch yn agwedd ar Gynllun Gweithredu ynghylch Sŵn Llywodraeth Cymru (mewn ymateb i\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Sŵn Ewropeaidd) ac mae\u0027n un o\u0027r mesurau ansoddol sy\u0027n berthnasol i fuddion adnoddau naturiol a ddarperir gan dirweddau. Datganiad priodoli Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tOther studies use different method approaches.  Notable other studies are:   - Scottish Natural Heritage.  Mapping Wild Land Areas.  Ongoing.  http://www.snh.gov.uk/protecting-scotlands-nature/looking-after-landscapes/landscape-policy-and-guidance/wild-land/    - CPRE (Campaign for Protection of Rural England).  Tranquil Places.  Ongoing.   http://www.cpre.org.uk/what-we-do/countryside/tranquil-places    - Natural Resources Wales. Accessible Natural Greenspaces Toolkit. (includes consideration of value to people of relative tranquillity in urban areas). Refered to here but currently not published. (NRW Contact: Senior Urban Advisor).  http://naturalresources.wales/people-and-communities/green-spaces/local-green-spaces/?lang\u003den   - Land Use Consultants. 2009. Wales Tranquil Areas Map 2009.  CCW Policy research Report No. 09/11. 71pp.  Natural Resources Wales, Bangor. - ASH Consulting Group. 1997. Tranquil Areas Wales. CCW Policy Research Report No. 97/14.\t\tPublished Dataset:  X:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas 2009.lyr   Other data related to the published data can be found in the following Folder Level: x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas  e.g. x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas 1997.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas A roads zone B.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas A roads zone C.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas active mineral zone B.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas active mineral zone C.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas airports zone B.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Tranquil Areas\\Tranquil Areas airports zone C.lyr\tLead Specialist Advisor: Landscape - Jill Bullen Planning Landscape Energy and Climate Change Team, Environmental Policy and Permitting Department.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-05-04\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/878/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:TranquilAreasWales\t2025-05-08T10:17:24.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18409\tNRW_DS118712\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.306Z\t398\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpatial Action Plan for Great Crested Newts in Anglesey, A Manual for Achieving Favourable Conservation Status.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138373132\tThis report draws on a series of previous research reports commissioned by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and its predecessor body on determining the conservation status of great crested newts, Triturus cristatus, in north Wales. The Welsh Government is obligated to maintain or restore the favourable conservation status of great crested newts within Wales and this report aims to provide practical guidance on how this can be achieved within the County of Anglesey. It is one of a series of three such reports, the other two covering the counties of Flintshire and Wrexham. Due to its legal status, the great crested newt is the focus of considerable conservation effort from a variety of bodies. This document aims to provide an overarching strategy to guide developers, conservation NGO\u0027s and local and national government in their efforts in order to achieve the greatest conservation benefit for great crested newts and ensure a unified approach to achieving Favourable Conservation Status (FCS) across the county. To this end, the report is accompanied by a number of GIS layers which will be of particular practical use to those engaged in great crested newt conservation across Anglesey.  The report presents a scoring method by which the Current Conservation Status (CCS) of great crested newts can be determined at multiple geographical scales from a single pond to the entire county. It then uses predictive modelling based on geographical, distributional, climatic and historical data to produce habitat suitability maps and to derive survey targets and pond creation targets to enable FCS to be achieved within the county. These techniques can be applied in various land use and planning scenarios and the report provides practical guidance on identifying areas of particularly high or low value for great crested newts, assessing the impacts of proposed developments, designing effective mitigation and the implications of agri-environment schemes for great crested newts.    During the modelling process, a total of 2,146 extant ponds were identified within the County of Anglesey.  With a view to redressing historic loss (37% of ponds having been lost since the 1843 mapping data), FCS was considered in the light of the level of occupancy that would have occurred at the time of the original mapping relative to current levels of occupancy and Habitat Suitability of ponds to the species (Habitat Suitability Index (HSI) scores). Historically we estimate there would have been 2,983 ponds in Anglesey and a corresponding 464 ponds occupied by great crested newts and 716 ponds with an HSI score \u0026gt;0.7.  We propose that the 464 occupied ponds should be used to provide a target level for great crested newts (Favourable Reference Value for Range and Habitat sufficiency).  This is further qualified by requiring these ponds have an HSI \u0026gt;0.7 to ensure habitat quality, has at least a medium population, are found at a density of at least 4 per km2 (to ensure population viability) and has a minor (or lower) level of threat (to address the species\u0027 future prospects). The Current Status of the species in Anglesey is estimated as having 334 ponds occupied by great crested newts. Using the proposed scoring system, FCS in Anglesey requires 464 occupied ponds which form part of medium populations, in high pond density areas and that have an HSI of \u0026gt;0.7: the minimum score to achieve these criteria will be 16 for each pond.  To achieve FCS the score would need to exceed is 464 x 16 \u003d 8,352. With an estimated 334 ponds being occupied and if all of the ponds are in favourable condition (i.e. would score 16), then the Current Conservation Status score \u003d 5,344.  The approach to identifying CCS and FCS laid out within this document is one of a number which are in the process of being developed across the UK and Europe. Whilst there is currently no single standard approach which can be applied uniformly across a range of species and habitats, one may emerge in the ...\tIt is practically impossible to achieve complete survey coverage of great crested newts within Anglesey and this presents difficulties when making informed decisions regarding their conservation, such as how FCS can be achieved. One approach to filling the significant gaps in our knowledge of great crested newts within the area is to use a predictive model. Over recent years, Amphibian and Reptile Conservation (ARC), in conjunction with Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and its predecessor body, have developed such a model for great crested newts in north Wales, including Anglesey. Previous versions of the model are documented by Amphibian \u0026amp; Reptile Conservation et al (2010) and Arnell \u0026amp; Wilkinson (2011; 2013).  The model underpinning this spatial plan is based on Arnell \u0026amp; Wilkinson (2013). They compiled high resolution, recent (1990-2011) great crested newt records and then used the distribution modelling software, MaxEnt (Elith et al 2006), along with environmental layers to produce an estimated probability of presence output for great crested newts in northeast Wales at a 25m resolution across the study area. The model used 19 bioclimatic variables, the effect of land cover was incorporated using the Land Cover Map 2007, pond density was extracted from Ordnance Survey Mastermap and Slope and Aspect layers were derived from elevation data using GIS software. Full methodological details of the model construction are provided within Arnell \u0026amp; Wilkinson (2013). This model has subsequently been modified to include flood risk data as although floodplains often contain many ponds, these are typically unsuitable for great crested newts as they often contain predatory fish. This had caused the earlier versions to overestimate the suitability of floodplains.\tRussell, L. Starnes, T. and Wilkinson, J. (2017), Spatial Action Plan for Great Crested Newts in Anglesey, A Manual for Achieving Favourable Conservation Status, NRW Science Report Series. Report 76 pp 69, NRW, Bangor.\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr E.A.Howe (Contractor: Amphibian and Reptile Conservation Trust)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18401\tNRW_DS118724\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.093Z\t406\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Survey for Geyer’s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Cors Erddreiniog SSSI and Cors Geirch SSSI (2016)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138373234\tA survey for Geyer\u0027s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Cors Erddreiniog Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)/ National Nature Reserve (NNR) and Cors Geirch SSSI/NNR in October 2016 failed to locate the snail. Survey locations were based upon previous records dating from 1985 to 2007 on Cors Erddreiniog and 1996 and 2008 on Cors Geirch. Losses from some former strongholds on Cors Erddreiniog are probably due to a lack of grazing, leading to the development of blanketing tussocks of Black Bog-rush Schoenus nigricans and Blunt-flowered Rush Juncus subnodulosus which have shaded out the open \u0027Carex-lawn\u0027 habitat required by the snail. This is most pronounced on Nant Isaf spring field (Compartment 13c) which has not been grazed for over 20 months. In other compartments, snail loss may be a consequence of over-grazing, or periods of under-grazing followed by cattle poaching of the remaining open areas. Similarly, Vertigo geyeri losses from known locations on Cors Geirch appear to be due to the development of a rank \u0027tussocky\u0027 sward that has reduced or eliminated the lightly-grazed \u0027Carex-lawn\u0027 habitat as a result of under-grazing. Voucher specimens from 2005 have been confirmed by the author of this report but no vouchers of the four specimens recorded in 2008 have been seen.       As small populations of Vertigo geyeri can be easily overlooked, it is premature to declare the snail as extinct on Cors Erddreiniog and Cors Geirch. Additionally, areas which previously supported very small numbers of snails and not sampled in 2016 need to be surveyed soon. More regular surveys are required in order to advise on appropriate grazing regimes if Vertigo geyeri is found to be \u0027hanging on\u0027 on either site. If this is the case, then the development of simplified and affordable surveillance routines is suggested.   Even in the apparent absence of Vertigo geyeri, it is vital that areas which previously supported the snail are managed appropriately. Grazing should be re-instated on Nant Isaf spring field as a matter of great urgency, and the current over-grazing of Compartment 9 on Cors Erddreiniog should be relaxed. On Cors Geirch, the area where Vertigo geyeri was first found in 1996 and again in 1998 and 2005 is too rank and grazing levels need to be increased to open up the seepages and the general sward.\tSurveys were by the collection of samples of suitable vegetation for drying and seiving.\tWilling, MJ., (2017), A survey for Geyer’s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Cors Erddreiniog SSSI and Cors Geirch SSSI in 2016, NRW Evidence Report No. 209, 47pp., Natural Resource Wales, Bangor.\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information \u0026gt; Report Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Dr Martin Willing)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:19:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18399\tNRW_DS118733\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.065Z\t1240\tValid\tEditable\tDevelopment Advice Maps (DAM)\tMapiau Cyngor ar Ddatblygu (DAM)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138373333\tThe Development Advice Map (DAM) shows areas at risk of flooding from rivers and the sea for the purposes of land-use planning. The DAM supports Planning Policy Wales and Technical Advice Note (TAN) 15 to guide new development away from areas at risk of flooding wherever possible. Together, they form a precautionary framework to guide planning decisions. The DAM should be considered as a trigger for identifying which development proposals may need to undertake a more detailed assessment of flooding risks and consequences in line with the policy advice set out in TAN15.    Zone C (the 1000yr extreme flood outline) was last updated in January 2020. No further updates are planned to the DAM and you are advised to contact Natural Resources Wales (NRW) about the availability of more up-to-date information.    Zone B (areas known to have flooded in the past) was originally published in 2004 and revised in 2017.  When using the DAM the preview defaults to Zone B only.  You will need to use the legend control to switch on the other zones.    NRW has developed a new Flood Map for Planning (FMfP) which was published in September 2021.  This indicates undefended flood extents over the next 100yrs taking into account the impacts of climate change. This will replace the DAM in June 2023.  Although the Flood Map for Planning is not referred to in current planning policy, it can be considered as the most up to date information on flooding risks.     Due to technical Issues the Web Services and Map Browser for the Development Advice Map are currently not available. If you wish to browse the data see the NRW Flood Risk Viewer https://maps.cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk/Html5Viewer/Index.html?configBase\u003dhttps://maps.cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk/Geocortex/Essentials/REST/sites/Flood_Risk/viewers/Flood_Risk/virtualdirectory/Resources/Config/Default\u0026amp;layerTheme\u003d2-Mae’r Mapiau Cyngor ar Ddatblygu (DAM) yn dangos ardaloedd mewn perygl o lifogydd at bwrpas cynllunio defnydd tir. Dylid defnyddio’r DAM law yn llaw â Pholisi Cynllunio Cymru a TAN 15 i wyro datblygiadau newydd i ffwrdd o ardaloedd mewn risg gymaint â phosib. Gyda’i gilydd, maent yn cynnig fframwaith rhagofalus i lywio penderfyniadau cynllunio. Nid yw’r mapiau a ddangosir yma wedi’u dylunio ar gyfer archwiliadau tu hwnt iI raddfa o 1:25,000 ac fe ddylid eu trin fel sbardun i ddilyn cyngor polisi yn TAN15.  Mae’r mapiau yn seiliedig ar amlinellau llifogydd eithafol Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (Parth C) a data Daeareg Arwynebol Arolwg Daearegol Prydain 10k (Parth B).  O fis Mawrth 2017 ymlaen bydd Parth C yn cael ei ddiweddaru’n chwarterol i alinio â diweddariadau amlinellau llifogydd eithafol CNC. Cafodd data Parth B ei gyhoeddi yn wreiddiol yn 2004, a’i adolygu yn 2017.  Nodwch y diffygion rhagolwg i Barth B yn unig. Defnyddiwch rheolaeth chwedl i newid ar barthau eraill. Data Diweddarwyd ddiwethaf Ebrill 2017.  Oherwydd problemau technegol nid yw Gwasanaethau’r We a’r Porwr Mapiau ar gyfer y Map Cyngor ar Ddatblygu ar gael ar hyn o bryd. Os dymunwch bori’r data gweler y Syllwr Perygl Llifogydd CNC yma.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Derived in part from 1:50,000 scale BGS Digital Data under Licence Number 2013/062. British Geological society. ©NERC\tIn 2004, Zone B coverage was limited to the number of available 50k tiles. In 2017, coverage has increased considerably and now covers approximately 90% of Wales.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\DAM Zone A.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\DAM Zone B.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\DAM Zone C1.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\DAM Zone C2.lyr\tMark Pugh\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-06-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3603/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_development_advice_map\t2025-05-07T15:07:37.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18396\tNRW_DS118757\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.812Z\t647\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpatial Action Plan for Great Crested Newts in Flintshire, A Manual for Achieving Favourable Conservation Status.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138373537\tThis report draws on a series of previous research reports commissioned by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and its predecessor body on determining the conservation status of great crested newts, Triturus cristatus, in north Wales. The Welsh Government is obligated to maintain or restore the favourable conservation status of great crested newts within Wales and this report aims to provide practical guidance on how this can be achieved within the County of Flintshire. It is one of a series of three such reports, the other two covering the counties of Anglesey and Wrexham. Due to its legal status, the great crested newt is the focus of considerable conservation effort from a variety of bodies. This document aims to provide an overarching strategy to guide developers, conservation NGO\u0027s and local and national government in their efforts in order to achieve the greatest conservation benefit for great crested newts and ensure a unified approach to achieving Favourable Conservation Status (FCS) across the county. To this end, the report is accompanied by a number of GIS layers which will be of particular practical use to those engaged in great crested newt conservation across Flintshire.  The report presents a scoring method by which the Current Conservation Status (CCS) of great crested newts can be determined at multiple geographical scales from a single pond to the entire county. It then uses predictive modelling based on geographical, distributional, climatic and historical data to produce habitat suitability maps and to derive survey targets and pond creation targets to enable FCS to be achieved within the county. These techniques can be applied in various land use and planning scenarios and the report provides practical guidance on identifying areas of particularly high or low value for great crested newts, assessing the impacts of proposed developments, designing effective mitigation and the implications of agri-environment schemes for great crested newts.   During the modelling process, a total of 1,116 extant ponds were identified within the County of Flintshire.  With a view to redressing historic loss (37% of ponds having been lost since the 1843 mapping data), FCS was considered in the light of the level of occupancy that would have occurred at the time of the original mapping relative to current levels of occupancy and Habitat Suitability of ponds to the species (HSI scores). Historically we estimate there would have been 1,659 ponds in Flintshire and a corresponding 258 ponds occupied by great crested newts and 398 ponds with an HSI score \u0026gt;0.7.  We propose that the 258 occupied ponds should be used to provide a target level for great crested newts (FRV for Range and Habitat sufficiency).  This is further qualified by requiring these ponds have an HSI \u0026gt;0.7 to ensure habitat quality, has at least a medium population, are found at a density of at least 4 per km2 (to ensure population viability) and has a minor (or lower) level of threat (to address the species’ future prospects). The Current Status of the species in Flintshire is estimated as having 174 ponds occupied by great crested newts. Using the propose scoring system, FCS in Flintshire requires 258 occupied ponds which form part of medium populations, in high pond density areas and that have an HSI of \u0026gt;0.7: the minimum score to achieve these criteria will be 16 for each pond.  To achieve FCS the score would need to exceed is 258 x 16 \u003d 4,128. With an estimated 174 ponds being occupied and if all of the ponds are in favourable condition (i.e. would score 16), then the Current Conservation Status score \u003d 2,748.  The approach to identifying CCS and FCS laid out within this document is one of a number which are in the process of being developed across the UK and Europe. Whilst there is currently no single standard approach which can be applied uniformly across a range of species and habitats, one may emerge in the future. With the recent increase in stud ...\tIt is practically impossible to achieve complete survey coverage of great crested newts within Flintshire and this presents difficulties when making informed decisions regarding their conservation, such as how FCS can be achieved. One approach to filling the significant gaps in our knowledge of great crested newts within the area is to use a predictive model. Over recent years, Amphibian and Reptile Conservation (ARC), in conjunction with Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and its predecessor body, have developed such a model for great crested newts in north Wales, including Flintshire. Previous versions of the model are documented by Amphibian \u0026amp; Reptile Conservation et al (2010) and Arnell \u0026amp; Wilkinson (2011; 2013).  The model underpinning this spatial plan is based on Arnell \u0026amp; Wilkinson (2013). They compiled high resolution, recent (1990-2011) great crested newt records and then used the distribution modelling software, MaxEnt (Elith et al 2006), along with environmental layers to produce an estimated probability of presence output for great crested newts in northeast Wales at a 25m resolution across the study area. The model used 19 bioclimatic variables, the effect of land cover was incorporated using the Land Cover Map 2007, pond density was extracted from Ordnance Survey Mastermap and Slope and Aspect layers were derived from elevation data using GIS software. Full methodological details of the model construction are provided within Arnell \u0026amp; Wilkinson (2013). This model has subsequently been modified to include flood risk data as although floodplains often contain many ponds, these are typically unsuitable for great crested newts as they often contain predatory fish. This had caused the earlier versions to overestimate the suitability of floodplains.\tRussell, L. Starnes, T. and Wilkinson, J. (2017), Spatial Action Plan for Great Crested Newts in Flintshire, A Manual for Achieving Favourable Conservation Status, NRW Science Report Series. Report 78 pp 69, NRW, Bangor.\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Amphibians\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr E.A.Howe (Contractor: Amphibian and Reptile Conservation Trust)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18400\tNRW_DS118898\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.054Z\t334\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Survey for Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch National Nature Reserve / Site of Special Scientific Interest and the Afon Penrhos Floodplain (2016)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138383938\tA survey for Desmoulin\u0027s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch National Nature Reserve (NNR)/ Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and the Afon Penrhos floodplain was undertaken in October 2016 to determine its current status and distribution. Sampled locations were based upon the results of previous surveys at Cors Geirch (1999-2008) and Afon Penrhos (2002-2008).   The population at Cors Geirch was found to have declined considerably, with a complete loss of the snail from former stronghold areas and just 17 shells recorded from two small areas at the southern end of the site. Assessments in 2003 and 2008 had considered the population to be in favourable condition. With an eight-year monitoring hiatus, the causes of the sharp decline are unclear but are likely to include the spread of uncut and un-grazed rank, fen vegetation combined with encroaching birch and sallow. Dense vegetation growth at the site was such that the former ditch-lines, used as features for site monitoring, were almost completely obscured and difficult to reach.  At Afon Penrhos, the snail was found in large numbers in short, mechanically-cut and lightly horse-grazed \u0027fen-meadow\u0027; tall uncut fen supported significantly lower numbers. Elsewhere in the UK, the snail is typically associated with tall fen. Vertigo moulinsiana was recorded on about 19 acres (8 hectares) of the site, but population boundaries were not established on the eastern and south-east borders of the surveyed area. It is estimated that a further 75 acres (30 hectares) of potentially-suitable habitat exists which should be surveyed to determine if it supports the snail. The Afon Penrhos population is not only the largest in Wales, but also probably one of the largest in mainland Britain. The significance of the population here is further enhanced by recent considerable population losses at several Special Areas of Conservation(SAC) in southern England. Once the full extent of the population on Afon Penrhos is determined, it is recommended that the site should be notified as a SSSI to reduce any risk of loss or damage due to changes in site management.  In light of the snail\u0027s association with short \u0027fen-meadow\u0027 at Afon Penrhos, it is recommended that rank areas which previously supported Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch are opened up to provide short fen habitat to assist the recovery of the remnant population.\tSurveys were by visual searches and the collection of samples of suitable vegetation for drying and sieving.\tWilling, MJ., (2017), A survey for Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch NNR/SSSI and the Afon Penrhos floodplain in 2016, NRW Evidence Report No: 210, 36pp., Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information \u0026gt; Report Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tNRW Invertebrate Ecologist: Dr Mike Howe (Dr Martin Willing)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:13:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18402\tNRW_DS118975\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.113Z\t564\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMapping the Extent of Calaminarian Grassland at Mwyngloddfa Cwmystwyth SSSI 2016/2017\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138393735\tThe project aims to map the extent of the Annex 1 type \u0027H6130 Calaminarian grasslands of the Violetalia calaminariae\u0027 (Calaminarian grassland) on Mwyngloddfa Cwmystwyth Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and the adjacent area of Elenydd SSSI in Ceredigion.  The habitat here includes examples where Calaminarian grassland is characterised largely by the presence of metallophyte lichen species, although metallophyte bryophytes and vascular plants also indicate some forms of the community here. This survey built on previous work (Simkin, 2014) that sought to produce a more detailed categorisation of the habitat in mid Wales.  This established that different forms of the habitat existed which were variously typified by metallophyte-rich spoil, grassland and heathland, as well as areas that were largely denuded of vegetation (bare, highly contaminated spoil).\tVegetation (including rock and bare ground) was mapped on site by walkover survey onto copies of aerial photographs. Areas that appeared to have been influenced by heavy metals were surveyed, focussed on metallophytes species. Calaminarian grassland was mapped in line with the categories defined by Simkin (2014). All observations were made by experienced surveyors following standard methodologies.\tForster Brown C., Chambers S.P. 2017. Mapping the extent of calaminarian grassland at Mwyngloddfa Cwmystwyth SSSI. NRW Evidence Report No: 203, 29pp, NRW, Bangor. Lichen records are also held in the British Lichen Society database and will be available on the NBN Atlas.\tY drive:  Shared_Projects\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Phase 2 Grassland\\Phase 2 Grassland.gdb\\Cwmystwyth_veg GIS data stored at above location, rest of the information is stored DMS\u0026gt;Evidence\u0026gt;Terrestrial Habitats\u0026gt;Lowlands Grassland\u0026gt;Projects\u0026gt;Cwm Ystwyth calaminarian survey 2017\tStuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18398\tNRW_DS118981\t2024-05-28T12:47:57.054Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Rhiannon Modiolus Survey 2015: Video and Still Image Analysis\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138393831\tModiolus modiolus (Horse Mussel) reef is an OSPAR threatened and declining habitat and a component of Annex I Reef.  In Wales two Modiolus reefs have been well described to date off the North Lleyn Peninsula. In 2008 the EIR Grid East West Interconnector Project conducted a survey approximately 10nm North of Anglesey and identified an area of seabed with an acoustic signature indicative of biogenic Modiolus modiolus reef. The presence of reef was later confirmed by drop down video survey. In 2012 surveys for the proposed Rhiannon Offshore Windfarm in the same location also identified several areas with an acoustic signal indicative of Modiolus reef.  NRW undertook some survey work in October 2015 to collect video and still images from areas identified as potential Modiolus reef by the two previous commercial projects in welsh waters. This report provides a detailed description of the method, a summary of the results, and some initial conclusions from the study\t\t\tArchived as Project 476, Media 1553 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1501-1600\\Media 1553) and as Marine Recorder Survey MRCCW31600000018 Currently all project data stored at V:\\MMT video Archive\\Drop video\\2015\\Rhiannon Modiolus DDV until moved to archive (this appears to have happened).\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:56:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18392\tNRW_DS118982\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.413Z\t708\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAnglesey Invasive Species, Non-native Species and Sabellaria spinulosa Survey 2016\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313138393832\tOne of the aims of this survey was to collect data on Invasive Non-Native Species particularly in relation to Holyhead Deep.  This disposal site receives dredged disposal material mainly from Holyhead Port, which is in close proximity to Holyhead Marina where there are known records of non-native species. Sabellaria spinulosa (Ross Worm) reef is designated by the Oslo/Paris Convention (for the Protection of the Marine Environment of the North-East Atlantic) as an threatened and declining habitat and a component of Annex I Reef.  Individuals and crusts of Sabellaria spinulosa are recorded commonly around Wales but the presence of reef has rarely been confirmed.  Previous commercial surveys off north and west Anglesey have indicated the presence of Sabellaria spinulosa reef.  This survey, therefore, also aimed to verify these records and give an indication of extent of the habitat.  The principal objective was to undertake a taxonomic analysis of video and still images from survey work undertaken around north and west Anglesey in October 2016.  The survey area extended from Point Lynas west to Church Bay and out to sites on the Skerries, West Anglesey Reef and Holyhead Deep. The collection of underwater video using a high definition digital drop-down camera and stills for this survey was undertaken by the coastal survey vessel RV Mersey Guardian in October 2016.  The video dataset encompassed 25 tows, comprising over eight hours of video in total.  A total of 3759 still images taken at approximately seven second intervals were collected simultaneously with the video tows. The entire video/ set of stills from each tow were reviewed prior to detailed assessment and recording, and the boundaries between habitats (biotopes) or habitat complexes noted.  The species and substratum in each tow section or \u0027sample\u0027 was then recorded, and habitats assigned. A total of 35 samples were derived across the 25 survey stations, with Ross Worm reef recorded in 34 of the 35 samples.  Substantial reef over 10cm high was found at nine survey stations with the most diverse Ross worm reef habitat occurring on a stony reef of cobbles among pebbles off Wylfa.   No non-native species were recorded during the survey.  In particular there was concern that the Carpet Squirt (Didemnum vexillum) might occur at some of the sites receiving dredge material from Holyhead Port but it was not identified in any of the imagery. This species has recently been reported in natural habitats off the north Kent coast. Relatively little litter was seen in the video, mostly fragments of monofilament , pieces of plastic and cloth.  Some toppled Ross worm reef was noted at single sites off Cemlyn and Church Bay. Two of the three sites in Holyhead Deep receiving dredge material from Holyhead Port appeared to be siltier than the near shore survey locations.\tThe survey area extended from Point Lynas west to Church Bay and out to sites on the Skerries, West Anglesey Reef and Holyhead Deep.   The camera equipment used for the survey was a sledge mounted C-Tecnics High definition CT3009 camera providing full 1080i HD recording (1920 x 1080).  This was obliquely mounted on a sledge accompanied by two  C-Tecnics CT4004 LED Lamps each of approximately 1100 lumens, accompanied by two C-Tecnics Laser lights (CT4005 - subsea Laser Modules) and two lasers set 10cm apart to provide a visual scale on the resulting video footage.  The video unit had its own integrated depth sensor.  The video feed was relayed to the surface unit via a 200m umbilical where realtime GPS positional data and other information were over-laid on the video footage and recorded on the surface unit\u0027s computer hard drive.  The drop down sledge was also equipped with a RovTech Seacam 18megapixel auto stills camera with an 18 mm lens, twin strobes and battery pack.  This was a self-contained system that was set to take pictures at predefined intervals (for this study the stills camera interval varied from 6 to 10 seconds).  The camera was set to start recording before the sledge was placed in the water and continued to take images at the predefined interval until it was brought back on board.  Images were then downloaded between each camera tow and saved to a computer hard drive.  The drop down video survey method followed the MESH (Mapping European Seabed Habitats) standard protocols (Coggan et al., 2007) as closely as possible.  Drops were carried out during a neap tide and where possible during the period either side of slack water. Even on a neap tide, given the depth of some of the survey locations and the fast running currents in the area, it was difficult to position the video tow so that it passed exactly through the survey location, although every effort was made to get as close as possible.\tBaldock, L. and Goudge, H. (2017), North Anglesey INNS and Sabellaria video and still image analysis, NRW Evidence Report 206 pp 61, NRW, Bangor.\tStores as Project 477, Media 1554 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media 1501-1600\\Media 1554) [A] Report in PDF Format - held in the DMS (DMS: Evidence\u0026gt;Marine\u0026gt;projects\u0026gt;Contracts\u0026gt;HD INNS and Sabellaria survey October 2016 [B] Raw video files in .ASF format - held in Media 1554 [C] Still images in .JPG format - held in Media 1554  [D] Survey logs as .XLSM  and .TXT format - held in Media 1554  [E] Track GPS data in Arc .SHP and MapInfo .TAB format - held in Media 1554  [G] Marine Recorder Survey [MRCCW32600000002] - held in Media 1554 [H] Field Notes, scanned and stored as .PDF format - held in Media 1554 [H] Field Notes, scanned and stored as .PDF format - held in Media 1554\tInternal Custodian: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor -Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18395\tNRW_DS119005\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.514Z\t639\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Licensing: Band 1 Sensitive Areas\tTrwyddedu Morol: Band 1 Ardaloedd Sensitif\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303035\tNRW agreed, identified and documented which habitats and SAC features are sensitive to a range of Marine Licencing Band 1 activities.   This dataset consists of two layers detailing information about sensitive habitats.  These datasets constitute polygons derived from NRW held BAP (Section 7) / OSPAR layers, Regulation 35 SAC habitat layers and Phase 1 intertidal survey.  All the habitats included in these layers were deemed to be sensitive to Marine Licencing Band 1 activities.     The data includes records of protected or sensitive habitats, collected as part of baseline survey, monitoring programmes and ad-hoc records.  A third layer is derived from the Special Protection Area (SPA) designated sites dataset and identifies SPA sensitive to Marine Licencing Band 1 Activities.    The publicly available dataset has some environmentally sensitive information redacted.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitation on Public Access and Use Constraints. An un-redacted version can be requested and may be provided under a NRW licence.-Cytunodd CNC, gan nodi a dogfennu, pa gynefinoedd a nodweddion ACA sy’n sensitif i weithgareddau Trwyddedu Morol Band 1 amrywiol. Mae\u0027r set ddata yn cynnwys 2 haen sy\u0027n rhoi gwybodaeth am gynefinoedd sensitif. Mae’r setiau data hyn yn cynnwys polygonau sy’n deillio o haenau Cynllun Gweithredu Bioamrywiaeth (Adran 7) / OSPAR sydd gan CNC, haenau cynefin ACA Rheoliad 35 ac arolwg rhynglanwol Cam 1. Ystyriwyd bod yr holl gynefinoedd yn yr haenau hyn yn sensitif i weithgareddau Trwyddedu Morol Band 1. Mae’r data’n cynnwys cofnodion o gynefinoedd gwarchodedig neu sensitif, a gasglwyd fel rhan o arolwg llinell sylfaen, rhaglenni monitro a chofnodion ad-hoc. Mae trydedd haen yn deillio o set ddata safleoedd dynodedig Ardal Gwarchodaeth Arbennig (SPA) ac yn nodi AGA sy\u0027n sensitif i Weithgareddau Trwyddedu Morol Band 1..  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThese sensitive habitats were selected from existing GIS layers.  Point data was given a 50m buffer.  The polygons were dissolved and then separately merged together to create both the two sensitive habitat data layers.  The table structure was altered so it only included a small number of fields and additional fields were added.    The SPA layer was derived from the NRW SPA Designated site dataset (NRW_DS98775).  The SPAs which were relevant to Band 1 Activities were selected out of this layer and the table structure amended to add relevant information.\t\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licensing Band 1 access sensitivity habitats.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licensing Band 1 construction.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licensing Band 1 SPA sensitivity.lyr  for the coprporate versions  Working Version - updated regularly by the Marine Team Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Laura_Grant\\Low_impact_activities\\Final shapefile copies for MLT use\\\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor -Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.                                                              Data will be updated 6 monthly.  The subtidal team will retain an archive, dated version as previous version as we may need to go back to any layers which were issued to marine licence applicants in the past in case there is a dispute.               Update from Laura Grant April 2025: The published dataset hasn\u0027t been updated since 2018. The schedule for 6 month updates was probably overoptimistic when this was said.  However they do intend to update it sometime this year (2025) and they are also hoping eventually to set up something in FME to improve the update process as it is taking a long time to do this manually.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-01-03\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/881/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:MarineLicensingBand1SensitiveAreas\t2025-12-23T10:26:48.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18393\tNRW_DS119019\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.418Z\t423\tValid\tEditable\tThe Wales Outdoor Recreation Survey\tArolwg Hamdden Awyr Agored Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303139\tThe Welsh Outdoor Recreation Survey (WORS) provides information on participation in a wide range of outdoor activities, from climbing to picnics, which take place in all areas from local parks to mountains and the sea. It also covers people\u0027s attitudes towards biodiversity and details any actions they might have taken to help protect the environment. It is the largest survey of public engagement with the environment by people living in Wales.-Mae Arolwg Hamdden Awyr Agored Cymru (WORS) yn rhoi gwybodaeth am gyfranogiad mewn amrediad eang o weithgareddau awyr agored, o ddringo i fynd am bicnic, sy’n digwydd ym mhob lleoliad, o barciau lleol i fynyddoedd a’r môr. Mae hefyd yn cynnwys agweddau pobl tuag at fioamrywiaeth ac mae’n rhoi manylion unrhyw gamau gweithredu y gall y byddant wedi eu cymryd i helpu i ddiogelu’r amgylchedd.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tOver 6000 people were interviewed over a full 12 month period. It includes people from all ages and backgrounds, who live in both the countryside and in towns. Interviews were conducted on every day of the week, and all weeks of the year, to take account of any season differences in participation. The survey is undertaken every three years, starting in 2008 followed by 2011 and 2014.\t\tNRW website and folder in the DMS  https://naturalresources.wales/media/4757/wales-outdoor-recreation-survey-2014-final-report.pdf\tSue Williams: Head of social Sciences.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-05-19\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2641\t2025-05-02T14:48:17.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18394\tNRW_DS119026\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.452Z\t635\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironment Agency Wales (EAW) Landholdings\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303236\tThis is a spatial dataset for internal use only, containing information on areas of land in which former Environment Agency Wales (EAW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) have an interest or a right to manage. This includes freehold and leasehold land, EAW land that is let out and land which has been disposed of, access agreements, agreements, deeds of grants, easements, fishing rights, honoraria, lettings, licenses, sub leases, and wayleaves. This provides an indicative representation land managed by EAW in Wales. The  dataset contains only polygons which includes information on site name and unique codes. Content varies for each parcel of land but often includes personal details (which are regarded as sensitive) and basic details of agreements.  The purpose of the data is to provide location and boundary information primarily for use by the estates department.  This is not an exhaustive list of all of the assets that ex-EAW own/maintain or have an interest in.\tThis dataset has been produced to assist Natural Resources Wales (NRW) employees in day to day land formally managed by EAW. The purpose of this data capture was to provide accurate legal maps all land owned and managed by EAW in Wales however since the transfer of this data to NRW it has become apparent that not all deeds have been captured and not every polygon has legal documents to support our ownership/management of that parcel of land.  In addition to this the dataset was inherited from Environment Agency by NRW so no information is available on the source, quality, collection or processing methods. The data has been digitised to OS Products at different scales as appropriate to the parcel of land. It contains polygons of disposed (sold) land so cannot be used to show an accurate picture of Ex-EA land ownership without manipulation of the data.\t\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex EAW Landholdings.lyr\tIan Medcalf, GIS Officer, NRW William Patterson, Assistant Surveyor National Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-10-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:01:34.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18391\tNRW_DS119037\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.412Z\t431\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Landholdings\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303337\tThis dataset is made up of separate spatial layers from all three legacy bodies, Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), Environment Agency Wales (EAW) and Forestry Commissions Wales (FCW), all of whom now make up Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  Dataset contains only polygons and includes information on site name, scale captured, unique codes. This provides accurate legal maps for management agreements, and provides a complete table showing all land owned by NRW in Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to provide an accurate digital legal maps for management agreements and provide a complete compilation of land owned or managed by NRW.\tThis spatial layer is not an amalgamation of all three legacy bodies spatial layers yet, due to concerns in regarding the accuracy of the underlying data. Therefore currently these datasets are separate. There are plans in the future to clean the underlying data and create one NRW layer.   Please refer to the individual metadata entries for further information about each dataset (CCW landholdings - metadata number: 99623, EAW landholdings - metadata number: 119036, FCW landholdings - metadata number: 119163)\t\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex CCW Land holdings current.lyr  X:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex EAW Landholdings.lyr  X:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex FCW Landholdings.lyr\tKeith Jones, Natural Resources Wales (NRW) GIS TEAM Jonathan Rothwell, Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:50.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18390\tNRW_DS119078\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.411Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFull-coverage European Nature Information System (EUNIS) level 3 layer integrating maps from surveys and broad-scale models\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303738\tThe European Environment Agency (EEA) developed a classification scheme for habitats as part of its European Nature information System (EUNIS) system for managing species, site and habitat information. As part of the classification for EUNIS data there are different levels. Level 1 splits off marine habitats from coastal and terrestrial habitats. Level 2 uses the biological zone and the presence / absence of rock as classification criteria so A1 is littoral rock and other hard substrata while A5 is Sublittoral sediment.  Level 3 introduces depth into the classification and energy for hard substrata, and splits the softer substrata by different sediment types, so A1.1 \u003d High-energy littoral rock while A5.4 \u003d Sublittoral mixed sediments. Some divisions in the classification are determined by biology here, such as biogenic reef and large seaweed or plant dominated sediments. References to specific taxa, rather than those above are first introduced at Level 4, where major epifaunal/epifloral taxa are used to discriminate rocky habitats. At Level 5, discrimination is based on both physical and biological characteristics of the habitats. Level 6, the highest discriminant level in EUNIS, frequently describes notable variations in community structure of level 5 habitats. The purpose of this data capture was to create a spatial layer that contains the best available information on European Nature Information System (EUNIS) Level 3 habitats at any position in UK waters. This dataset combines field surveys, polygon habitat data and board-scale predictive maps from the EUSeaMap model.\tThis dataset is a compilation of over 200 individual habitat maps. Areas of overlap have been removed using a 3-step confidence assessment to provide a flattened map suitable for area analyses. These maps include maps from bespoke field surveys and broad scale predictive maps. Maps from bespoke field surveys are put together as part of JNCC’s obligations to assess and report on benthic habitats at a UK scale, it retains copies of the majority of the seabed habitat maps that exist for the UK. These maps were produced at a variety of spatial scales and describe habitats and biotopes at different levels of detail within the EUNIS habitat classification; they have also been produced using a variety of methods. In total, 209 survey-derived maps were combined in this present process with the map from the EUSeaMap habitat model.  Broad-scale predictive map: the EUSeaMap project produced a seabed habitat map of the entire UK marine area, including the extended continental shelf area, as part of a map covering the wider North Sea and Celtic Sea (Cameron and Askew, 2011); the project also produced equivalent maps for the Western Mediterranean and the Baltic Sea. It was produced by overlaying full-coverage datasets of the physical variables substrate type, current energy, wave energy, depth and light attenuation, matching the conditions at each location to the corresponding definitions of each EUNIS habitat type. This process generates a map that is consistent across large areas in terms of spatial scale and methods used; however, it is usually at a coarser spatial scale (‘pixel’ resolution of ~250m) than survey-derived maps and does not use all of the information available in all locations in the interest of consistency. EUSeaMap currently represents the most recent, up-to-date broad-scale predictive EUNIS habitat map for UK waters as it has been updated more recently than its predecessor UKSeaMap 2010 (McBreen et al., 2011).\tFor additional information please see the following external links: http://jncc.defra.gov.uk/pdf/20140311_combinedEUNISL3mapMethod__v1.0.pdf http://jncc.defra.gov.uk/pdf/20140311_InformationSheet_combinedEUNISL3map_v1.pdf\tLocation no longer exists (changed since entry creation) Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Marine_Habitats_Species\\Broadscale habitats\\JNCC combined map\\Version 20160427\\MPA_Network_Output_Welsh.gdb\\CombinedEUNIS_CP2_Welsh_all_attr_WGS84  Data available via Lle Portal\tInternal Custodian: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External Contact: Graeme.Duncan@jncc.gov.uk\tVarious\t\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:29:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18389\tNRW_DS119099\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.342Z\t700\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Licence Archive\tArchif Trwyddedau Morol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139303939\tThe records contain coordinate data associated with historic and expired marine licence applications in Wales. The data allows the location of applications to be mapped to assess any in combination effects with other proposals.   Data related to current marine licence applications are held as a separate dataset (NRW_DS116807)-Mae\u0027r cofnod yn cynnwys data sy\u0027n gysylltiedig â cheisiadau hanesyddol am drwyddedau morol yng Nghymru, a rhai sydd wedi dod i ben. Mae\u0027r data\u0027n galluogi i leoliad ceisiadau gael eu mapio fel y gellir asesu unrhyw effeithiau cyfunol gyda chynigion eraill.  Datganiad priodoli Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe Marine Licensing Team as part of Natural Resources Wales issue Marine Licences on behalf of the Licensing Authority, Welsh Government.\t\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine licence archive lines WGS84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine licence archive points WSG84.lyr  X:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine licence archive poly WGS84.lyr  Corporate Version Y:\\Marine_Marine Licence apps_Marine Licence apps.gdb Working Version - updated regularly by Marine Team\tEmmer Litt\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2026-01-11\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/879/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:MarineLicenceArchive\t2026-01-12T12:16:36.022Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18387\tNRW_DS119100\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.263Z\t470\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Article 17 Reporting Habitat Features\tNodweddion Cynefinoedd Erthygl 17 Morol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313030\tReporting under Article 17 of the EU Habitats Directive requires member states to report on progress towards achieving favourable conservation status for habitats and species of Community Importance. The assessment of conservation status does not only relate to that component of the habitat area or species population to be found in Special Areas of Conservation, but to the totality of the habitats and species throughout the United Kingdom.   The results of NRW\u0027s feature monitoring work and other evidence collected (survey) or collated (other data sources) on feature extent feed in to the process of mapping and reporting under Article 17.   JNCC coordinate and provide UK-level feature reports under Article 17; England, Wales, Northern Ireland and Scotland submit country-level data and information to inform these reports.   This dataset only consists of the Welsh component of the Article 17 mapping.  This dataset is due to be replaced by the Marine Annex 1 Habitat Maps (Reg9a 2024) data (NRW_DS161407). The new data will be published on DataMapWales once it has gone through an Open Data Risk Assessment.-Mae mapiau Erthygl 17 y Gyfarwyddeb Cynefinoedd yn gipolwg ar y data gofodol diweddaraf ar gyfer nodweddion a restrir yn amrywiol Atodiadau\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb ar adeg y cofnodi. Maent yn cynrychioli helaethrwydd/lleoliad a statws hysbys presennol y nodweddion y tu mewn a\u0027r tu allan i Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig. Caiff mapiau Erthygl 17 eu hadolygu a\u0027u diweddaru bob 6 blynedd fel rhan o broses adrodd y Gyfarwyddeb Cynefinoedd.  Datganiad priodoli   Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Yn deillion rhannol o ddata a ddarparwyd o dan drwydded gan Arolwg Daearegol Prydain © NERC\tThe Habitats Directive Article 17 reporting maps are a snapshot of the most up to date spatial data for features listed on the various Annexes of the Directive at the time of reporting. They represent the current known extent / location and status of features both inside and outside of SACs.   Article 17 maps are reviewed and updated every 6 years as part of the Habitats Directive reporting process (with possible interim updates occurring in line with JNCC’s Article 17 mapping programme).     The layers are created by collating records from numerous surveys, monitoring programmes, expert knowledge and third party providers and then combining them using GIS to produce layers containing points, polygons or lines. The layers are updated when new records are produced.   The original surveys and monitoring events from which the records have been extracted vary widely in dates going back many decades in some cases, with confidence varying significantly.   Consists of 18 dataset layers covering Annex 1 marine features. This dataset is updated approximately every 6 years.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Marine  Consists of 18 layers held within the same location.  These three datasets have been updated in 2018. X:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Marine\\Art17 Subtidal Reef Point.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Marine\\Art17 Subtidal Reef Polygon.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Marine\\Art17 Welsh Sandbanks.lyr  Data is also available via the WG web mapping portal \"DataMapWales\". JNCC also have a UK version of the composite Article 17 datasets that is available from their website.\tInternal Contact: Karen Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor – Marine Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-02-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/883/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:MarineArt17Features\t2025-11-19T15:10:50.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18388\tNRW_DS119101\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.272Z\t859\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Features\tNodweddion Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACA) Morol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313031\tThe Conservation of Habitats and Species Regulations 2010 provide for the designation and protection of \u0027European sites\u0027, the protection of \u0027European protected species\u0027, and the adaptation of planning and other controls for the protection of European Sites.   This dataset is made up of 43 datasets showing the extent of features for which our marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) are designated and forms part of the advice packages Natural Resources Wales (NRW) prepares for our marine SACs under Regulation 37 (formerly Regulation 33 and 35) of the Conservation of Habitats and Species Regulations. They show the presence of (extent) of features within our marine SACs at the time of site designation against which statutory advice can be given. They also act as a baseline for feature extent against which changes can be measured during reporting under Article 17 of the Habitats Directive.-Mae mapiau nodweddion Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACA) Rheoliad 37 (33 a 35 yn flaenorol) yn rhoi\u0027r llinell sylfaen ddangosol ar gyfer helaethrwydd a statws (h.y. statws a/neu hyder “Pendant“ neu “Botensial“ nodwedd) ar gyfer nodweddion ACA adeg dynodi\u0027r safle. Dyma\u0027r mapiau y dylid eu defnyddio i asesu effeithiau posibl cynlluniau a phrosiectau, ac ar gyfer cyngor yn ymwneud gwaith achos. Maent yn llinell sylfaen y gellir ei defnyddio hefyd i fesur newidiadau mewn maint wrth lunio adroddiadau ar gyfer y Gyfarwyddeb Cynefinoedd.  Datganiad priodoli   Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn deillio\u0027n rhannol o Ddata Digidol BGS graddfa 1:50,000 o dan Rif Trwydded 2013/062. Cymdeithas Ddaearegol Prydain. ©NERC.Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThe Regulation 37 SAC datasets provide the indicative baseline extent and status of SAC features at the time of site designation. They should be used to assess potential impacts of plans and projects and for casework advice. They also form the baseline against which we measure changes in extent during Habitats Directive reporting.   Features include: sandbanks which are slightly covered by seawater all the time, estuaries, mudflats and sandflats not covered by sea water at low tide, coastal lagoons, large shallow inlets and bays, reefs, Salicornia and other annuals colonising mud and sand, Atlantic salt meadows (Glauco-Puccinellietalia maritimae), and submerged or partially submerged sea caves.     The layers are created by collating records from numerous surveys, monitoring programmes, expert knowledge, and third party providers and then combining them using GIS to produce layers containing points, polygons or lines. The layers are updated when new evidence to inform feature extent becomes available.   The original datasets surveys and monitoring events from which the records have been extracted vary widely in dates, going back many decades in some cases. However surveys were undertaken between the dates of 1899 - 2015. This dataset is updated approximately every 6 years.\tDescriptions, ecological characteristics and status of Marine SAC features for the UK are published by the Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine SAC features\\SAC features X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine SAC features\\SAC Features Archive   There are 43 datasets\tKaren Robinson- Marine Ecologist - EVIAN       This metadata entry covers both the live data and the archive. Although current regulation is Reg 37, marine team have not updated any of the exiting layers.  Therefore the latest data is Reg 35 in the X drive data and is supplied in response to request for this dataset.  It is classed as Reg 37 data and a copy may be renamed to separate between the two.  Each layer will be updated as new evidence becomes available. The published data is technically the dataset generated for Reg35 but is the most recent information to inform Reg 37\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-04-17\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/886/?ows_service\u003dwms\tnull\t2025-11-13T16:40:53.389Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18386\tNRW_DS119124\t2024-05-28T12:47:56.049Z\t1158\tValid\tEditable\tNational Marine Character Areas (NMCA)\tArdaloedd Cymeriad Morol Cenedlaethol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313234\tSeascapes, like landscapes, reflect the relationship between people and place and the part it plays in forming the setting to our everyday lives. Marine Character Areas highlight the key natural, cultural and perceptual influences that make the character of each seascape distinct and unique. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) were commissioned on behalf of Welsh Government to identify the character of Wales’ seascapes at a broad-scale. We divided our inshore waters into 29 Marine Character Areas. This work provides the strategic spatial evidence on seascapes to inform the Wales National Marine Plan, which is being prepared by Welsh Government. Each area profile includes a description of the key characteristics, including their natural, cultural and perceptual influences, as well as land - sea inter-visibility maps.-Yn yr un modd â thirweddau, mae morweddau\u0027n adlewyrchur berthynas rhwng pobl a lleoedd a\u0027u cyfraniad at ffurfio cyd-destun ein bywydau bob dydd. Mae Ardaloedd Cymeriad Morol yn tynnu sylw at ddylanwadau naturiol, diwylliannol a chanfyddiadol hollbwysig sy\u0027n gwneud cymeriad pob morwedd yn arbennig ac yn unigryw. Comisiynwyd CNC ar ran Llywodraeth Cymru i bennu cymeriad tirweddau Cymru ar raddfa fras. Rhannwyd ein dyfroedd mewndirol yn 29 o Ardaloedd Cymeriad Morol. Mae\u0027r gwaith hwn yn rhoi tystiolaeth ofodol strategol ynghylch morweddau i lywio Cynllun Morol Cenedlaethol Cymru, sy\u0027n cael ei baratoi gan Lywodraeth Cymru. Mae proffil pob ardal yn cynnwys disgrifiad o\u0027r nodweddion allweddol, gan gynnwys eu dylanwadau naturiol, diwylliannol a chanfyddiadol, yn ogystal â mapiau rhyngweledol o\u0027r tir a\u0027r môr.  LUC, 2015. National Seascape Assessment for Wales. Adroddiad Tystiolaeth CNC 80. 78pp, Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru, Bangor (Cymru)  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata\tThis work was carried out under contract to LUC. Character assessment involves desk-based stages and field verification, considering the variety and combination of natural, cultural and perceptual influences that make one place distinct and recognisable from another. In all cases the UK Hydrographic Office’s Marine Charts and Ordnance Survey basemapping (for where the MCAs meet the coast) were used as the primary reference points to guide the boundary drawing process, which was digitised in GIS at a scale of 1:250,000.\thttps://naturalresources.wales/evidence-and-data/maps/marine-character-areas/?lang\u003den  LUC, 2015. National Seascape Assessment for Wales. NRW Evidence Report 80.  78pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor (Wales)\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\National Marine Character Areas.lyr\tJohn Briggs- Landscape Architect, PPG.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-01-30\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/390/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_MARINE_CHARACTER_AREAS\t2025-11-13T16:44:13.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18436\tNRW_DS119125\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.190Z\t254\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Shad Egg Survey on the River Wye (2017)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313235\tThe River Wye rises on Plynlimon in the Cambrian Mountains and flows in a generally south-easterly direction to enter the Severn Estuary at Chepstow. The river is designated as a Special Area of Conservation (SAC) under the Habitats Directive for several features, including salmon, lampreys and shads: Twaite (Alosa fallax) and Allis (Alosa alosa) shad. In Britain, significant populations of Twaite shad are known to spawn in the Rivers Severn, Wye, Usk and Tywi. The population of both species are threatened in Europe mainly due to obstructions to migration and water quality issues. The purpose of this data capture was to make an assessment of Conservation Status by conducting egg deposition surveys to assess spawning extent and compare these with results from previous datasets. Surveys carried out along the length of the Welsh portion of the River Wye from Builth Wells to Monmouth. The survey was conducted in May and June of 2013, 2015 \u0026amp; 2016.\tData is collected according to Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) published guidance on Common Standards Monitoring for Freshwater Fauna (JNCC, 2015). The distribution of spawning shad within each river catchments are assessed using a kick sampling technique for eggs conducted in the spring.\tGarrett, HM., (2017), River Wye SAC Allis \u0026amp; Twaite shad population condition assessment, Reporting cycle 2013 to 2018, 23 pp., Natural Resources Wales, Dolgellau. Unpub.\tDMS \u0026gt; Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting \u0026gt; Freshwater Monitoring \u0026gt; Running Waters Habitat Directive 3rd reporting cycle \u0026gt; Shad Fish Species - 3rd Reporting Cycle \u0026gt; Wye DMS \u0026gt; Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting \u0026gt; Freshwater Monitoring \u0026gt; Running Waters Habitats Directive Data Cycle 3 \u0026gt; Wye Shad \u0026gt; Workbook Wye shad egg surveys 2013 onwards.xlsx\tNRW Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator: Heather Garrett.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:16.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18441\tNRW_DS119129\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.315Z\t597\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGrey Seal Halichoerus grypus Phenology at Skomer and Marloes Peninsula\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313239\tThe grey seal (Halichoerus grypus) is listed in Annex II of the EU Habitats Directive and is a qualifying feature of three Special Areas of Conservation in Wales. Pembrokeshire colonies represent the largest breeding population in southwest Britain. The aim of this study was to provide evidence on the status of grey seal pup numbers around the Island of Skomer and the Marloes Peninsula, Pembrokeshire, based on over 30 years of survey data collected from 1983-2015. This report quantifies recent population growth, analyses the timing of the pupping season and makes recommendations for the future of this survey.  Logistical constraints at the end of the observing season required data modelling to estimate both the mid-point and length of the pupping season.\tTrained staff have conducted over 30 annual surveys from 1983-2015 of grey seal pupping sites. Individual records were kept of each pup’s progress from birth to moult following a standardized protocol. Details included a unique numerical pup identifier, the location (beach name) and date of first observation, and a qualitative assessment of the developmental stage (1-5) of the pup. Sites were visited once a week and pups marked with a unique pattern to help monitor each individual.  Records were maintained in field note books and transferred to recording sheets and Excel spread-sheets. Annual reports have been produced, from which the data in this study have been collated.  Using R, spatially aggregated counts of pup numbers were analysed and trends in population abundances were quantified by fitting a Generalised Additive Model to the time series of annual pup counts.\tSkomer seal report: Bull JC, Börger L, Franconi N, Banga R, Lock KM, Morris CW, Newman PB, Stringell TB. (2017), Temporal trends and phenology in grey seal (Halichoerus grypus) pup counts at Skomer, Wales, NRW Evidence Report No: 217, 23pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.  Marloes seal report: Bull JC, Börger L, Banga R, Franconi N, Lock KM, Morris CW, Newman PB, Stringell TB. (2016), Temporal trends and phenology in grey seal (Halichoerus grypus) pup counts at Marloes Peninsula, Wales, NRW Evidence Report No: 155, 23pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tData is held in DMS: Evidence \u0026gt; Marine \u0026gt;Marine Mammals \u0026gt; Evidence Base / Contracts \u0026gt; Seal Monitoring and Research \u0026gt; Grey Seal phenology reports Referenced in Marine Archive as project 478, media 1555. • Marloes Peninsula census data.xlsx (EVID-307-372) • phenology basic analysis marloes.R (EDIV-307-371) • Data_Skomer.xls (EVID-307-375)  and metadata Data_Skomer_Notes.xls (EVID-307-377) • Skomer_daily_births.csv (EVID-307-376) • Skomer analysis (EVID-307-373)\tOriginator: NRW Senior Marine Mammal Ecologist: Tom Stringell (contractor: James Bull, Dept. Biosciences, Swansea University)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18433\tNRW_DS119132\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.201Z\t928\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Management Catchments Cycle 2\tCylch 2 Dalgylch Rheoli’r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313332\tWFD Management Catchments Cycle 2 is a dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD).   Management catchments are the unit of geography for which action plans are drafted in implementing the WFD. This process was conducted by using expert judgement in consultation.   WFD Management Catchments have an action plan published that relates to all waterbodies that fall within its boundaries. These data apply to Cycle 2 of the Water Framework Directive-Mae Cylch 2 Dalgylchoedd Rheoli\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr yn set ddata a gesglir ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD). Uned o ddaearyddiaeth yw dalgylchoedd rheoli y caiff cynlluniau gweithredu eu llunio ar eu cyfer wrth weithredu\u0027r WFD. Cynhaliwyd y broses hon trwy ddefnyddio barn arbenigol mewn ymgynghoriad. Mae gan Ddalgylchoedd Rheoli WFD gynllun gweithredu cyhoeddedig sy\u0027n ymwneud â phob corff dŵr o fewn ei ffiniau. Mae\u0027r data\u0027n ymwneud â Chylch 2 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tWFD Management Catchments have been delineated by using WFD River Waterbody Catchments [these were delineated through use of flow data and a digital terrain model run through a hydrological model] as “building blocks” that have been aggregated together within a GIS, ensuring that WFD rivers do not intersect boundaries. This process was conducted by using expert judgement in consultation.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Management Catchment Cycle2.lyr\tCustodian: Robert Blacklidge, Environmental Reporting Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/428/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_MGT_CATCHMENTS_C2\t2025-12-19T12:05:33.437Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18444\tNRW_DS119133\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.398Z\t1158\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 1\tDalgylchoedd Cyrff Dŵr Afonol y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr – Cylchred 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313333\tThe \u0027WFD River Waterbody Catchments\u0027 are a polygon Shapefile dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Catchments are defined as an area of land from which all surface run-off flows through a series of streams, rivers and, possibly, lakes to a particular point in the water course such as a river confluence. Delineation of the river catchment boundaries primarily utilised the CEH Flow Grid hydrological model run with CEH Integrated Hydrological Digital Terrain Model (IHDTM) data so as to determine water drainage into river stretches. Upstream catchment size was calculated within an ESRI GIS environment using the extension Arc Hydro.-Mae \u0027Dalgylchoedd Cyrff Dŵr Afon y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr\u0027 yn set ddata Shapefile polygon yw sydd wedi\u0027i chasglu fel y diffiniwyd ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr. Caiff dalgylchoedd eu diffinio fel darn o dir lle y mae\u0027r holl dŵr ffo arwyneb yn llifo trwy gyfres o nentydd, afonydd ac, o bosibl, llynnoedd i bwynt penodol yn y cwrs dŵr, megis cydlifiad afon. Roedd ffiniau dalgylchoedd afonydd yn cael eu dynodi trwy ddefnyddio model hydrolegol Grid Llif CEH yn bennaf, ochr yn ochr â data Model Tir Digidol Hydrolegol Integredig CEH, er mwyn pennu faint o ddŵr oedd yn draenio i mewn i hydoedd afonydd. Cyfrifwyd maint y dalgylch i fyny\u0027r afon o fewn amgylchedd GIS ESRI gan ddefnyddio\u0027r estyniad Arc Hydro.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl. Rhai nodweddion o\u0027r wybodaeth hon yn seiliedig ar ddata gofodol digidol a drwyddedwyd oddi wrth y Ganolfan Ecoleg a Hydroleg © NERC (CEH). Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans © Hawlfraint y Goron a hawl cronfa ddata 2015.\tDelineation of the river catchment boundaries primarily utilised the CEH Flow Grid hydrological model run with CEH Integrated Hydrological Digital Terrain Model (IHDTM) data so as to determine water drainage into river stretches. Upstream catchment size was calculated within an ESRI GIS environment using the extension Arc Hydro.\t\tX:\\EU directives\\WFD\\Cycle 1\\river catchments.lyr\tData custodian: Rob Blacklidge, Environmental reporting team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/560/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_RIVERCATCHMENT_C1\t2025-03-07T15:52:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18445\tNRW_DS119134\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.456Z\t1106\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbodies Cycle 1\tCyrff Dŵr Afonydd y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr Cylchred 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313334\tWFD River Waterbodies is a polyline Shapefile dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). The river polylines were defined by using of the Environment Agency - General Quality Assessment (GQA) River Stretches dataset this is copied directly from the CEH 1:50K River Network with some additional stretches added in by the Environment Agency. Additional stretches were added to cover all stretches designated under the Fresh Water Fish Directive, all stretches designated within riverine SSSIs, and ensuring that all riverine abstractions which contributed to the designation of a Drinking Water Protected Area were sited on a designated river waterbody. In addition to this, some stretches were added where the upstream catchment size was \u0026gt; 10km2, but no other river stretch was designated (10km2 was the original cut-off catchment size form defining a waterbody under the WFD).   The resultant WFD river waterbody datasets is a sub-set of the CEH network, including only stretches that meet any of the criteria outlined above.WFD River waterbodies share the same \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 as their river waterbody catchment allowing these 2 datasets to be linked. All river waterbodies are associated with a waterbody catchment. Not all catchments have a designated WFD river waterbody within them. These data apply to Cycle 1 of the Water Framework Directive. The equivalent layer for Cycle 2 is covered by WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 2 AfA292.-Mae Cyrff Dŵr Afonydd y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) yn set ddata Shapefile aml-linell, sydd wedi\u0027i chasglu fel y diffiniwyd ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD). Diffiniwyd aml-linellau\u0027r afonydd gan ddefnyddio set ddata Hydoedd Afonydd Asesiad Ansawdd Cyffredinol Asiantaeth yr Amgylchedd. Caiff y data hwn ei gopo\u0027n uniongyrchol o Rwydwaith Afonydd CEH 1:50K, gyda rhai hydoedd ychwanegol wedi\u0027u hychwanegu gan Asiantaeth yr Amgylchedd. Ychwanegwyd mwy o hydoedd er mwyn cynnwys yr holl hydoedd a ddynodwyd o dan y Gyfarwyddeb Pysgod Dŵr Croyw a phob hyd a ddynodwyd o fewn SoDdGA afonol, ac er mwyn sicrhau bod yr holl safleoedd tynnu dŵr afonol a oedd yn cyfrannu at ddynodi Ardal Warchodedig Dŵr Yfed wedi\u0027u lleoli ar gorff dŵr afonol dynodedig. Yn ogystal â hyn, ychwanegwyd rhai hydoedd lle roedd maint y dalgylch i fyny\u0027r afon yn 10km2, ond lle nad oedd hyd afonol arall wedi\u0027i ddynodi (10km2 oedd maint mwyaf dalgylch a ddiffiniwyd fel corff dŵr o dan y WFD yn wreiddiol). Is-set o\u0027r rhwydwaith CEH yw setiau data cyrff dŵr afonol canlyniadol y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr, sydd dim ond yn cynnwys yr hydoedd sy\u0027n bodloni unrhyw un o\u0027r meini prawf a amlinellwyd uchod. Mae cyrff dŵr afonol y WFD yn rhannu\u0027r un \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 \u0027u dalgylch corff dŵr afonol, sy\u0027n caniatu i\u0027r ddwy set ddata hyn gael eu cysylltu. Mae pob corff dŵr afonol yn gysylltiedig dalgylch corff dŵr. Nid oes gan bob dalgylch gorff dŵr afonol dynodedig o dan y WFD oddi mewn iddynt. Mae\u0027r data hwn yn berthnasol i Gylchred 1 o\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr. Caiff yr haen gyfwerth ar gyfer Cylchred 2 ei chwmpasu gan Gylchred 2 Cyrff Dŵr Afonol y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl. Rhai nodweddion o\u0027r wybodaeth hon yn seiliedig ar ddata gofodol digidol a drwyddedwyd oddi wrth y Ganolfan Ecoleg a Hydroleg © NERC (CEH). Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans © Hawlfraint y Goron a hawl cronfa ddata 2015.\tThe resultant WFD river waterbody datasets is a sub-set of the CEH network, including only stretches that meet any of the criteria outlined above.  Not all catchments have a designated WFD river waterbody within them. These data apply to Cycle 1 of the Water Framework Directive.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\WFD Rivers Waterbodies Cycle 1.lyr\tData Custodian: Rob Blacklidge, Environmental Reporting Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-12-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/561/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WFD_RIVERWATERBODIES_C1\t2025-02-04T10:16:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18439\tNRW_DS119140\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.160Z\t330\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMacrophyte Survey of Welsh Lakes 2016\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313430\tThis project set out to collect, process and supply to Natural Resources Wales (NRW), ecological and environmental data from a network of 14 lake sites across Wales, in support of NRW\u0027s integrated monitoring programme for protected sites (Special Areas of Conservation and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)), the Water Framework Directive, Nitrates Directive, Biodiversity Action Plans and other legislative and policy drivers. In particular the surveys are aimed at informing management and restoration of protected sites and facilitating delivery of River Basin Management Plans.  Using standard methods, lakes were surveyed to assess the species and abundance of aquatic plants growing within and directly around the lake and to measure water clarity, dissolved oxygen and temperature within the lakes.  The results of the aquatic plant surveys are suitable for the purposes of assessing site condition for Habitats Directive standing water features and SSSI status. The lake metrics are applicable for the production of ecological quality ratios from which the lakes may be classified in accordance with the requirements of Water Framework Directive (2000/60/EC).\tThe full description of the survey methods used to collect macrophyte data are detailed in the Joint Nature Conservation Committee publication for the CSM guidance for standing waters (JNCC 2015). In brief, the plant surveys consisted of four components: a strandline survey of species uprooted and washed to the shore; a survey of the emergent and marginal species; a wader survey of the shallow littoral zone to approximately 1.0 m; and a boat survey encompassing species in open water and extending to the point of maximum colonization. Surveys were carried out at each site on up to four discrete 100 m sections of shoreline which were considered representative of the lake and gave good geographical coverage. To reduce disturbance, a maximum of 25% of the shoreline was surveyed, resulting in fewer than four sections being surveyed at smaller lakes. Where lakes had previously been surveyed, transect locations used in the past were re-surveyed to maximise comparability between surveys. These methods were devised to provide quantitative species-abundance data that can be obtained in a pragmatic and repeatable manner. Additional efforts such as sampling drift line flora were made to record other species which did not occur in any of the survey sections. The survey did not aim to produce a complete species list for the lake.  Dissolved oxygen concentration and temperature profiles were taken at the deepest recorded point of each site on the same dates as the macrophyte surveys. Secchi disc depths were recorded at the time of the macrophyte surveys from the deepest point of all lakes and further measurements taken at each survey section at sites where variability in water clarity was observed. Catchment data, land cover and general lake data that are quoted in the text are taken from the original UK Lakes database (Hughes et al. 2004) and the new UK Lakes administered by CEH (CEH 2017).\tShilland EM, Goldsmith BJ, Hatton-Ellis TW. (2017), Ecological Surveys of Welsh Lakes 2016, NRW Evidence Report No 204. 73 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\t[A] Report is held in the DMS \u0026gt; Knowledge, Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Reports Archive \u0026gt; NRW Evidence Reports [B] Leafpacs calculator files within folder [Evidence \u0026gt; Freshwater Ecology \u0026gt; Internal Research \u0026gt; Lake Leafpacs data and spreadsheets \u0026gt; ENSIS Surveys 2016] in Microsoft Excel format [Lake_name_LEAFPACS 2 0_metriccalculator_Month_Year] and summary information in MS Excel format [Site_metrics_Summary_2015.xls]\tNRW Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems Tristan Hatton-Ellis\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18438\tNRW_DS119141\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.220Z\t378\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Recreation points\tPwyntiau Hamdden Coedwig CNC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313431\tThis is a dataset of physical recreation feature on the ground best depicted as point features. These features a divided into eight Categories.   - Signage - NRW branded signs, information and navigational aids, which have management activity and investment (e.g. trailhead, waymarker, information board)  - Built feature - Purpose built and installed NRW asset, which has management activity and investment (e.g. Gate, stile, bin, barrier)  - Forest furniture - Purpose built and installed NRW asset, which has management activity and investment (e.g. Bench, seat, picnic table). Exercise - Purpose built and installed FC asset, which has management activity and investment (e.g. Activity point).  - Play equipment - Purpose built and installed NRW asset, which has management activity and investment (e.g. Play component, structure).  - Viewpoint - A feature which has management activity and investment. Art feature - Purpose built and installed NRW asset or agreed feature, which has management activity and investment (e.g. Art work, sculpture).  - Forest entrance - Regularly used feature to gain access to the forest/NRW estate (e.g. Car park or layby access point).  - Miscellaneous - Features which do not fall into one of the existing feature types, but required to be captured for management purposes.  Within each Category there are a number of Asset types and Asset Subtypes.-Y ffordd orau o ddangos nodwedd hamdden ffisegol ar y ddaear yw fel pwynt. Mae\u0027r nodweddion hyn yn rhannu\u0027n wyth Categori:  - Arwyddion - arwyddion brand CNC, gwybodaeth a chymhorthion canfod y ffordd, sydd â gweithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi (e.e. arwydd llwybr, cyfeirbwynt, bwrdd gwybodaeth)  - Nodwedd adeiledig - ased a adeiladwyd i’r diben ac a osodwyd yn ei le gan CNC, ac sydd â gweithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi (e.e. llidiart, camfa, bin, rhwystr)  - Dodrefn coedwig - ased a adeiladwyd i’r diben ac a osodwyd yn ei le gan CNC, ac sydd â gweithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi (e.e. mainc, sedd, bwrdd picnic)  - Ymarfer corff - ased a adeiladwyd i’r diben ac a osodwyd yn ei le gan CNC, ac sydd â gweithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi (e.e. pwynt gweithgaredd).  - Offer chwarae - ased a adeiladwyd i’r diben ac a osodwyd yn ei le gan CNC, ac sydd â gweithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi (e.e. Cydran chwarae, strwythur).  - Golygfannau - Nodwedd sydd â gweithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi.  - Nodwedd gelf - ased a adeiladwyd i’r diben ac a osodwyd yn ei le gan CNC ac sydd â gweithgaredd rheoli a buddsoddi (e.e. gwaith celf, cerflun).  - Mynedfa i goedwig -. Nodwedd a ddefnyddir yn rheolaidd i gael mynediad i’r goedwig / Ystad CNC (e.e. maes parcio neu bwynt mynediad mewn cilfan).  - Amrywiol - Nodweddion nad ydynt yn perthyn i un o\u0027r mathau nodwedd sy’n bodoli eisoes, ond y mae\u0027n rhaid eu nodi at ddibenion rheoli.  O fewn pob categori mae nifer o fathau o asedau ac isdeipiau asedau.  Mae rhestr lawn o\u0027r gwerthoedd wedi\u0027u codio a ddefnyddir yn y set ddata i\u0027w gweld yma.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe FC\u0027s Forester Recreation extension was rolled out to Forest Districts (FDs) in 2010. Since then FDs have been using this to record new recreation features, and those previously stored locally, to a common standard across GB.  Since the creation of NRW in 2013, NRW have maintained the data in the Forester recreation module.\t\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Designated Areas\\NRW Forest Recreation Points.lyr\tCustodian: Geraint Morgan and Arpana Chunihal National Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-01-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/344/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_GB_RECREATION_POINTS\t2025-10-10T07:51:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18426\tNRW_DS119153\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.002Z\t948\tValid\tEditable\tTransfrontier Shipment (TFS) of Waste and Refuse Derived Fuel (RDF) (Wales)\tCludo ar Draws Ffiniau (gwastraff) - Tanwydd yn Deillio o Sbwriel (Cymru)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313533\tTransfrontier Shipment (TFS) of waste and Refuse Derived Fuel (RDF) waste export data (monthly tonnage weights) from Wales to non-UK countries, since November 2014. TFS or International Waste Shipments are terms used for exporting and importing waste. The Transfrontier Shipment of Waste regulations govern the movement of recyclable materials across borders either by road, rail, ait or sea. The European Community first introduced a directive on control procedures for the shipment of hazardous waste in 1984, when it became apparent that hazardous waste from developed countries was being exported and dumped in developing countries. RDF is produced from domestic and business waste, which includes biodegradable material as well as plastics.-Mae’r set ddata hon yn cynnwys gwybodaeth am ddata ‘tanwydd sydd wedi deillio o sbwriel’ allforio gwastraff RDF (pwysau tunelledd misol) o Gymru i wledydd tu allan i’r DU, ers Tachwedd 2014. Mae’r ffigurau pwysau tunelledd yn seiliedig ar fanylion a gyflwynwyd gan safleoedd allforio a derbyn / prosesu ar gyfer pob llwyth a gludwyd / allforiwyd ar gyfer adfer ynni y tu allan i’r DU. Dydy’r ffigurau pwysau a gyflenwyd heb eu gwirio gan CNC.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tTonnage weight figures based on returns (mandatory) made by exporting, receiving and processing sites for each waste load shipped or exported for energy recovery outside the UK. Weight figures supplied are not verified by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).      Information warning: Please note, the information in this report may have some outstanding return data due to the lag in processing of waste loads. The whole dataset for a given year may not be complete until the end of the following year i.e. the data for 2024 will be complete and published around the end of 2025.\t\tDMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/Regulatory/Permitting/International%20Waste%20Shipments/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2FRegulatory%2FPermitting%2FInternational%20Waste%20Shipments%2FTFS%20DATA%20%28external%20customers%20only%29%2FRDF%20DATA%2FRDF%20data%20%2DPublished\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000C2B903A34CC62A40BBDD00FD337BE4EC\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B6DF30B29%2D753D%2D47A5%2DA1DF%2D4DEAC78C0A06%7D\tMark Rhodes: Senior Environment Officer: Waste\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-11-18\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2643\t2025-11-18T15:28:15.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18443\tNRW_DS119155\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.371Z\t457\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales Waste Information\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313535\tThis is a summary of the types and quantities of waste handled by permitted waste management facilities in Wales.\tPermitted waste management facilities in Wales are required to make returns (which must be quarterly or annually) to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) detailing the amounts and types of materials handled, treated or disposed of.   Summarising returns data provides information on the material which was deposited (or otherwise dealt with) at permitted waste management facilities.\t\tData is stored in WISKI and also on the DMS - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/reporting/was/SWMA/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Irina Nicholas, Environmental Reporting team, NRW John Fry, SUW Advisor Wales, NRW  Metadata Point of Contact:  Irina Nicholas, Environmental Reporting team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:14:02.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18428\tNRW_DS119161\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.083Z\t1281\tValid\tEditable\tMain Rivers\tPrif Afonydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313631\tThe statutory main river map is a 1:10,000 scale spatial (polyline) dataset that defines statutory watercourses designated by Natural Resources Wales as main rivers. The data is for Wales only. \u0027Main rivers\u0027 are usually larger streams and rivers, but some of them are small watercourses of significance. They include certain structures that control or regulate the flow of water in, into or out of the channel.-Set ddata ofodol (aml-linell) ar raddfa 1:10,000 yw\u0027r map prif afonydd statudol sy\u0027n diffinio\u0027r cyrsiau dŵr statudol a ddynodir gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru fel prif afonydd. Mae\u0027r data ar gyfer Cymru yn unig. Nentydd mwy ac afonydd ye \u0027prif afonydd\u0027 fel arfer, ond mae rhai ohonynt yn gyrsiau dŵr sylweddol. Maent yn cynnwys strwythurau sy\u0027n rheoli neu\u0027n rheoleiddio llif y dŵr i mewn neu allan o\u0027r sianel.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tNatural Resources Wales has permissive powers, but not a duty, to carry out maintenance, improvement or construction work on designated main rivers.   Natural Resources Wales has powers to regulate the activities of others affecting rivers and their flood plains under the Environmental Permitting Regulations, the Water Resources Act 1991 and our land drainage byelaws.   All other watercourses are \u0027ordinary watercourses\u0027. On these watercourses the Lead Local flood Authority or, if within an Internal Drainage District, the Internal Drainage Board have similar permissive powers to maintain and improve. They also have powers to regulate works under the provisions of the Land Drainage Act 1991 and local byelaws.   Natural Resources Wales undertakes the role of the Internal Drainage Board in Wales for all Internal Drainage Districts.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Main Rivers.lyr\tRoss Akers/ Mark Pugh       Have based start date on the date that EA have recorded in their metadata on data.gov\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-01-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/414/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_MAIN_RIVERS\t2025-05-15T09:24:04.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18430\tNRW_DS119162\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.194Z\t993\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDrinking Water Protected Areas Safeguard Zones - Groundwater\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313632\tDrinking water safeguard zones are designated areas in which the use of certain substances must be carefully managed to prevent the pollution of raw water sources that are used to provide drinking water.   For at risk drinking water protected areas we may establish Safeguard Zones. These non-statutory Safeguard Zones are areas where activities can impact adversely on the quality of water abstracted in the drinking water protected area. Action to address pollution is targeted in these zones so that extra treatment of raw water can be avoided.   Safeguard Zones are a joint initiative between Natural Resources Wales and water companies. Safeguard Zones are one of the main tools for delivering the Drinking Water Protected Area objectives of the Water Framework Directive.\tFor groundwater, Safeguard Zones are based on existing Source Protection Zone 1 and Source Protection Zone 2 areas.  Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and database right. All rights reserved.  Derived from BGS digital data under licence from British Geological Survey  © NERC.\tFurther information on groundwater protection in Wales can be found on Gov.UK - https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/protect-groundwater-and-prevent-groundwater-pollution/protect-groundwater-and-prevent-groundwater-pollution  Download a redacted version of the data covering England and Wales on data.gov.uk: https://data.gov.uk/dataset/drinking-water-safeguard-zones-groundwater\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Non-statutory\\NRW Drinking Water Safeguard Zones (Groundwater).lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Jones - Technical Specialist Groundwater and Contaminated Land dave.jones@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:34:59.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18431\tNRW_DS119163\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.236Z\t392\tValid\tEditable\tForestry Commission Wales (FCW) Landholdings\tDaliadau Tir Comisiwn Coedwigaeth Cymru (CCC)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313633\tThis is a spatial dataset for internal use only, containing information on areas of land in which former Forestry Commission Wales (FCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) have a right to manage. This provides an indicative representation of land owned by the former FCW in Wales.   The dataset contains only polygons which includes information on site name and unique codes. Content varies for each parcel of land but often includes personal details which are regarded as sensitive.    The purpose of the data is to provide location and boundary information primarily for use by the estates department.\tThis dataset has been produced to assist Natural Resources Wales (NRW) employees in day to day management of land formally managed by FCW. The purpose of this data capture was to provide accurate legal maps all land owned by FCW in Wales. In addition to this the dataset was inherited from Forestry Commission Wales by NRW so no information is available on the source, quality, collection or processing methods.   Digitally scanned from paper and transparencies by Forestry Commission GB in order to produce a digital dataset of Forestry Commission Wales (FCW) land ownership in ESRI Shapefile format.    A redacted dataset is available for external use.  Please see metadata entry no. 116290 (Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Legal Boundary)\t\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex FCW Landholdings.lyr\tData Custodian: Ian Medcalf GIS Officer, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t2000-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-17T10:44:34.385Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18432\tNRW_DS119168\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.191Z\t320\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhytophthora Working Target Sites\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313638\tPhytophthora ramorum (P. ramorum) is a fungus-like pathogen called a water mould. This is working data for the management of Phytophthora ramorum surveillance.  This is a spatial dataset which is generally updated weekly from information held by Forest Research on Natural Resources Wales (NRWs) behalf, as part of their Service Level Agreement. The data is very rough and is to be used internally, primarily by the Tree Health Team. This is not to be released externally and the use of internally without understanding of the terminology may result in misinformation.\tData is collected from surveys (Aerial and field). The quality cannot be assured and therefore staff are required to contact the Tree Health Team if they wanted to use this data. It is used by the Tree Health Team with the Statutory Actions associated with Phytophthora ramorum.  It is also used by the Forest Planning teams to assess the impact of the surveillance.\t\tX:\\Incidents\\Forest Health\\Phytophthora Working Target Sites.lyr   Also available on the Y Drive: Y:\\Tree Health\\P ramorum\\FRWTS\\   where the tree health officers access the most up to date data. They also archive the older versions of this data in this folder as well.\tInternal Custodian: Callum Stone       Contains personal information in the comments fields which is why we cannot just give it out.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-07-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:59:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18440\tNRW_DS119169\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.317Z\t534\tUnassessed\tEditable\tConstruction and Demolition waste survey Wales 2012\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313639\tThe purpose of this data collection was to produce information on the quantities, origins (by industry sector and geographic region), and fate (management method) of Construction and Demolition (C and D) wastes generated by businesses in Wales in 2012. This information is required by Welsh Government for a variety of reasons including: reporting on waste generation in compliance with the EU Waste Statistics Regulations 2002; informing the development of national waste policy; monitoring progress against national waste prevention and recycling targets; informing waste planners and the regulator; and providing data to the waste management industry to inform investment decisions.  The precision for the total waste generated was +/- 33.2% at 90% confidence.\tData was collected from 457 business sites between July 2013 and January 2014 throughout Wales of differing sector and size, and the data was grossed up using population data to regional and national level in Wales. Office for National Statistics data showed that there were 11,355 business units in Wales in 2012 with 1% in demolition, 39% in construction sectors, 11% in civil engineering and 49% in General Building. Of these business units, 58% had less than three employees and 0.5% had one hundred or more employees. Comparison with previous survey results should not be made because: The definition of waste has changed considerably since the previous survey, excluding a large amount of material which was previously recorded as waste; The recession impacted significantly on the construction sector, reducing output and therefore waste generation; There were no major infrastructure projects accounting for very large quantities of waste in 2012. This contrasts with 2005/06 when three of the five largest waste producers were involved in major projects. This illustrates the difficulty in comparing data from single years; The geographical data for 2012 is reported based on the location of the construction/demolition site as opposed to the location of the business office. This reflects the fact that C and D waste generally needs to be managed close to the site of production.\tRelated Titles: J. Fry. 2016 Survey of Construction and Demolition Waste Generated in Wales 2012. NRW Report.\tData:  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/Regulatory/wasters/waar/CD%202012%20Survey/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx   Report: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/Regulatory/wasters/waar/CD%202012%20Survey/Wales%20Waste%20Survey%20Report%20-%20CD%20Final.pdf\tJohn Fry, Waste Advisor John.Fry@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk       John Fry: john.fry@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:29:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18442\tNRW_DS119170\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.239Z\t244\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIndustrial and Commercial waste survey Wales 2012\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313730\tThe purpose of this study was to produce information on the quantities, origins (by industry sector and geographic region), and fate (management method) of industrial and commercial (I and C) wastes generated by businesses in Wales in 2012. This information is required by Welsh Government for a variety of reasons including: reporting on waste generation in compliance with the EU Waste Statistics Regulations 2002; informing the development of national waste policy; monitoring progress against national waste prevention and recycling targets; informing waste planners and the regulator; and providing data to the waste management industry to inform investment decisions.  The precision for the total waste generated was +/- 7.9% at 90% confidence.\tData was collected from 1,540 business sites of differing sectors and sizes throughout Wales between July 2013 and December 2013. The data was grossed up using population data to regional and national level in Wales. Office for National Statistics data showed that there were 85,695 industrial and commercial business units in Wales in 2012 with 7% in industrial sectors and 93% in commerce. Of these businesses, 63% had fewer than five employees and 2% had one hundred or more employees. The methodology used in this survey was comparable with the previous I and C surveys completed in Wales, of which the most recent provided data for the 2007 calendar year.\tRelated Titles: J. Fry. 2016 Survey of Industrial and Commercial Waste Generated in Wales 2012. NRW Report.\tData and Report Link:  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/Regulatory/wasters/waar/IC%202012%20Survey/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx\tJohn Fry, Waste Advisor john.frycyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18434\tNRW_DS119181\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.225Z\t321\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: Shad Egg Survey on the River Usk (2013 - 2018)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139313831\tThis report assesses the spawning extent population attribute of Allis and Twaite shad on the River Usk Special Area of Conservation (SAC), using a kick sampling monitoring method for shad eggs at routine sampling points using an Natural Resources Wales (NRW) standard protocol (Garrett, 2015) during the months of May - June in 2013, 2015 and 2016. This is one of the population attributes for condition assessment from the Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) guidance for freshwater features (Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC), 2015). The two remaining population attributes are never assessed by NRW because of the invasive techniques for sampling juvenile and adult life stages of these two species. The environmental attribute targets for water quality, flow, river habitat etc will be assessed when sufficient data has been collated and is available.   Overall the shad spawning distribution target was not met on the River Usk SAC in the third reporting cycle. This preliminary assessment is classed as a Fail. For freshwater species and habitats the overall assessment of the feature as \u0027Favourable\u0027 or \u0027Unfavourable\u0027 (JNCC, 2017) incorporates both abiotic and biotic attributes. This overall assessment will be made when in addition to the population attributes, the environmental attributes (water quality, flow, river habitat, barriers to migration etc.) for the SAC river have also been assessed. This analysis will be documented in a summary table for each SAC river and each of its features.\tData is collected according to Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) published guidance on Common Standards Monitoring for Freshwater Fauna (JNCC, 2015). The distribution of spawning shad within each river catchments are assessed using a kick sampling technique for eggs conducted in the spring.\tGarrett, HM., (2017), River Usk SAC Allis \u0026amp; Twaite shad population condition assessment, Reporting cycle 2013 to 2018, 23 pp., Natural Resources Wales, Dolgellau. Unpub.\tDMS \u0026gt; Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting \u0026gt; Freshwater Monitoring \u0026gt; Running Waters Habitat Directive 3rd reporting cycle \u0026gt; Shad Fish Species - 3rd Reporting Cycle \u0026gt; Usk. DMS \u0026gt; Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting \u0026gt; Freshwater Monitoring \u0026gt; Running Waters Habitats Directive Data Cycle 3 \u0026gt; Usk Shad \u0026gt; Workbook Usk shad cycle 3 2013 onwards.xlsx\tNRW Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator: Heather Garrett.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18437\tNRW_DS119221\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.209Z\t621\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAfforested Deep Peat\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323231\tAreas in Wales where deep peat sites have been restored to forest. This dataset comprises the total afforested deep peat, including modified deep peat, in Wales and is categorised as Broadleaved or predominantly broadleaved, Conifer or Predominantly Conifer and Transition. The datasets gives information on soil type along with the species (broadleaved and conifer) replanted or whether the transformation of the site is still in transition.  Peatland in pristine or good condition provides a range of critical ecosystem services, including biodiversity, carbon storage and sequestration, regulation of stream base flows, water runoff and downstream flood peaks and nutrient regulation and retention. Peatlands are also sinks and sources of several natural greenhouse gases, particularly carbon dioxide (CO2) and methane (CH4). More than 75% of deep peat soils in Wales are covered in semi-natural vegetation. Most of this is upland blanket bog, with significant amounts of fen and flush and, locally, lowland raised bog. These are all UK Biodiversity Action Plan priority habitats with UK and Welsh targets for habitat management and restoration.  In order to deliver the habitat restoration targets set out in the Welsh Environmental Strategy, there is a need to progressively restore semi-natural habitat in afforested areas. Restoration measures should seek to better integrate the key objectives of nature conservation, soil carbon protection, carbon sequestration and water quality.\tThis dataset was created by intersecting deep and modified peat from the Wales Total Peatland layer with Interpreted Forest Types from the National Forest Inventory. This gives a dataset of all afforested deep peat and modified deep peat in Wales and can be visualised by Broadleaved or predominantly broadleaved, Conifer or predominantly conifer and Transition (mostly felled, ground under preparation or young trees).\tForest Research report: \u0027A Strategic Assessment of the Afforested Peat Resource in Wales\u0027\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\Afforested Deep Peat.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-10-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:46:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18429\tNRW_DS119234\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.069Z\t929\tUnassessed\tEditable\tKnow Your River - Salmon and Sea Trout Catchment Summary\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323334\tThe Know Your River documents give a brief overview of Salmon and Sea trout stocks on major rivers throughout Wales. Documents contain the following data: Rod Catches - data is graphed. Rod catch data is collected through anglers. All salmon and sea trout rod catch should be recorded by rod license holders. This data is used to estimate overall fish runs on catchments. Conservation objectives - data is graphed. This uses the rod catch data to estimate the spawning stock on a catchment. This is then compared against what we believe is the stock level needed for a healthy population on that catchment. This data influences byelaws for catchments e.g rivers not reaching conservation limit can have 100% catch and release imposed on them. Juvenile salmonid data - Any electro-fishing surveys carried out on a catchment are recorded on a map using the National Fisheries Classification Scheme to highlight the quality of the site. This data is collected due to the National Fisheries Monitoring Programme.\tRod catch - data collected from salmon and sea trout anglers through rod license sales, possible under reporting Juvenile data - sites fished through the National Fisheries Monitoring Programme, all sites electro-fished, data uploaded to WISKI\t\tMost up to date reports should be on the NRW website - https://naturalresources.wales/evidence-and-data/research-and-reports/terrestrial-and-freshwater-species-reports/know-your-rivers-salmon-and-sea-trout-catchment-summaries/?lang\u003den  Internal NE/NW docs  are held - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/anass/fr/Salmon%20action%20plans/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx  SW/MID KYR - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/anass/fr/Salmon%20action%20plans/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Fanass%2Ffr%2FSalmon%20action%20plans%2FSW%20Know%20Your%20Rivers\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120006992F85D1E299A439F90D9D45A521C69\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B8EF66B5A%2DB381%2D4FD9%2DA72E%2D8B9041DFF0D5%7D  SC/SE - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/anass/fr/Salmon%20action%20plans/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Fanass%2Ffr%2FSalmon%20action%20plans%2FSouth%20East%20Know%20Your%20Rivers\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120006992F85D1E299A439F90D9D45A521C69\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B8EF66B5A%2DB381%2D4FD9%2DA72E%2D8B9041DFF0D5%7D\tInternal Contact: Robert Evans, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, NW Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team   Metadata Point of Contact:  Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2009-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18427\tNRW_DS119251\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.039Z\t888\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Water Quality Data Archive held in WISKI (KiWQM)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323531\tWater quality data is held in the WISKI Kisters Water Quality Module (KiWQM). The Water Quality Archive provides a central repository for all data relating to water quality measurements that have since the 1960\u0027s been collected from over 22,000 sampling points, with 2,500 active today. Samples are taken from coastal or estuarine waters, rivers, lakes, ponds, canals or groundwater. Additional data has been provided by third parties as public record. The data measures a wide range of water quality parameters, such as nutrients, metals and physio-chemical determinants for both archived and processed data and these are used to assess discharge monitoring against discharge permits, investigation of pollution incidents or environmental monitoring. Whilst this data is used to meet the requirements of The Water Framework Directive and Bathing Water Quality assessments, the system is currently unable to record anything about compliance.\tThe Water Quality Archive, replaces the previous Environment Agency (EA) WIMS and MIDAS systems. Repeat samples are taken from 2,500 fixed sampling points around Wales (and more than 22,000 historical sample points), the results of which were analysed by the Environment Agency\u0027s National Laboratory in Llanelli until 2016, and since then by Natural Resource Wales Analytical Services facilities based at Swansea University. Additionally, analyses are also completed in the field with handheld probes measuring common parameters such as temperature and pH.as well as derived directly within the KiWQM software from previously recorded parameters, as is the case for Dissolved Oxygen, Organophosphate and Total Oxidised Nitrogen.\t\tWISKI Database - Kister Water Quality Module\tNRW Water Quality Data Analyst: Martin Stephens       In the near future refineries and steelworks data (under PPC regime) will be added. Simon Webb will clearly mark these as third party on KiWQM as these must not be shared with customers; they are classed as commercially confidential/sensitive. Samples provided by welsh water as part of legislative requirements.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18435\tNRW_DS119255\t2024-05-28T12:48:36.248Z\t377\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBottlenose Dolphin Monitoring in Cardigan Bay 2014-2016\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323535\tThis dataset contains the results of bottlenose dolphin monitoring work conducted by the Sea Watch Foundation between 2014 and 2016 on behalf of Natural Resources Wales. The semi-resident population of bottlenose dolphins found in Cardigan Bay is one of two major coastal populations in the UK. The species is the primary feature of Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC), and also a qualifying feature of Pen Llŷn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC in northern Cardigan Bay, designated under the EC Habitats Directive. The primary objective of this contract was to monitor the bottlenose dolphin population of Cardigan Bay including both Cardigan Bay SAC and the wider Cardigan Bay area.\tLine transect and dedicated non-line transect surveys were conducted in Cardigan Bay, 2014- 2016, although coverage of the survey area in 2014 was not complete and no estimate is available. This built upon earlier systematic surveys that had been undertaken by the Sea Watch Foundation since 2001 in Cardigan Bay SAC, and since 2005 in northern Cardigan Bay. Opportunistic observations from wildlife tour boats were used to supplement life history and behavioural information.\t\tData is archived as Project 479, Media 1556:  K:\\archive\\Media 1501 -1600\\Media 1556\tCharlie Lindenbaum, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer,  Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-12-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T10:33:42.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18425\tNRW_DS119263\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.611Z\t418\tValid\tEditable\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SAC) and Special Protection Areas (SPA) Monitoring Programme Results 2013-2018\tCanlyniadau Rhaglen Monitro Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACA) ac Ardaloedd Gwarchodaeth Arbennig (AGA)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323633\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs) are protected and designated sites under the EC Habitats and Birds Directives. The Directives list habitat types and species that are considered to be most in need of conservation at a European level. An obligation for this is a programme of species and habitat monitoring across the site.   This dataset shows the results of NRWs\u0027 feature condition monitoring programme on the SACs and SPAs in Wales and contains the base data used to populate one of NRWs\u0027 corporate performance indicators. The data is updated from the active condition monitoring programme on an annual basis.    The publication of indicative condition assessments for marine features in Wales in January 2018 supersedes the information for marine features currently in this data layer. The indicative condition assessments are NRWs formal advice on condition of its European marine sites and should be used. Information on the assessments and the reports themselves can be downloaded from the following link:  https://naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/environmental-topics/wildlife-and-biodiversity/find-protected-areas-of-land-and-seas/indicative-feature-condition-assessments-for-european-marine-sites-ems/?lang\u003den  Subsequent updates of the SAC and SPA table may provide updated assessments for a few marine features and as such any marine feature assessment dated after May 2017 should be considered to take precedence.-Mae Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACA) ac Ardaloedd Gwarchodaeth Arbennig (AGA) yn safleoedd dynodedig sydd wedi eu diogelu dan Gyfarwyddiaethau Cynefinoedd ac Adar y Gymuned Ewropeaidd. Mae’r Cyfarwyddiaethau yn rhestru mathau o gynefinoedd a rhywogaethau yr ystyrir eu bod angen eu gwarchod fwyaf ar lefel Ewropeaidd. Mewn achos o’r fath mae’n orfodol cael rhaglen sy’n monitro rhywogaethau a chynefin ym mhob rhan o’r safle. Mae’r set ddata hon yn dangos canlyniadau rhaglen fonitro cyflwr nodweddion CNC ar yr ACAoedd a’r AGAoedd ac mae’n cynnwys y data sylfaen a ddefnyddir i boblogi un o ddangosyddion perfformiad corfforaethol CNC. Defnyddir y rhaglen fonitro weithredol i ddiweddaru’r data’n flynyddol.  Mae cyhoeddi asesiadau cyflwr dangosol ar gyfer nodweddion morol yng Nghymru ym mis Ionawr 2018 yn disodli’r wybodaeth ar gyfer nodweddion morol yn yr haen ddata hon. Asesiadau cyflwr dangosol 2018 yw cyngor ffurfiol CNC ar gyflwr ei safleoedd morol Ewropeaidd ac felly dylid defnyddio canlyniadau’r asesiad hwn. Gellir lawrlwytho gwybodaeth ar yr asesiadau a’r adroddiadau eu hunain o’r dolenni canlynol: https://naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/environmental-topics/wildlife-and-biodiversity/find-protected-areas-of-land-and-seas/indicative-feature-condition-assessments-for-european-marine-sites-ems/?lang\u003dcy  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe dataset contains a summary of the condition monitoring results for notified (A-C grade) features of designated SAC and SPA sites in Wales. This data is redacted to meet the NRW guidance on ecological data and information exempt from general release under the EIR/FOI regulations in order to make it publicly available.    Please note the following about the data:   * The data are downloaded from the NRW internal SAC and SPA monitoring programme results spreadsheet annually and while significant changes to the results since that date are not anticipated it is worth noting that there can in some cases be a significant delay between the feature being monitored (the date shown in the spreadsheet) and the condition assessment being completed and reported.   * Condition assessments for the three Killarney fern features are not linked to individual SACs as the species is considered to be sensitive and the locations confidential.  * While some ad hoc monitoring of SSSI and NNR features is undertaken this is not currently part of a coordinated programme and is not held centrally.\tEC Birds Directive (http://jncc.defra.gov.uk/page-1373) EC Habitats Directive (http://jncc.defra.gov.uk/page-1374)\tDMS\tNRW Monitoring and Surveillance Advisor: Daniel Guest. NRW Environmental Surveillance Officer: Dylan Lloyd.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-05-15\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2645\t2025-05-15T09:31:41.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18420\tNRW_DS119266\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.431Z\t1337\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) Sensitive Area - Shellfish Waters\tRheoliadau Trin Dŵr Gwastraff Trefol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323636\tThe Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017 (WFD Regulations 2017) requires specification of protected areas for those areas designated as nutrient sensitive areas.     UWWTR Sensitive Areas Shellfish Waters layer has been renamed from the UWWTD Sensitive Areas Shellfish Waters and updated to only show areas which are designated as Shellfish Waters under the WFD Regulations following Welsh Government review.    This is a spatial dataset showing the extent of the Urban Wastewater Treatment Directive (UWWTD) sensitive areas (Shellfish Water) in Wales.   The Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD) (91/271/EEC) is designed to reduce the pollution of freshwater, estuarine and coastal waters by domestic sewage and industrial wastewater collectively known as urban waste water. These water have been identified as a sensitive because further treatment than secondary or equivalent treatment is necessary to meet other EC Directives such as the Water Framework Directive.-Diffinnir \u0027dŵr gwastraff trefol\u0027 yn y Rheoliadau Trin Dŵr Gwastraff Trefol (RhTDGT) fel y cymysgedd o ddŵr gwastraff domestig o geginau, ystafelloedd ymolchi a thoiledau, y dŵr gwastraff o ddiwydiannau sy\u0027n gollwng i garthffosydd, a dŵr glaw ffo o ffyrdd ac arwynebau anhydraidd eraill fel toeau, palmentydd a ffyrdd sy\u0027n draenio i garthffosydd. Cyfeirir at ddŵr gwastraff trefol yn aml fel \u0027carthion\u0027. Bwriad y RhTGDT yw diogelu\u0027r amgylchedd dŵr ar gyfer yr anifeiliaid a\u0027r planhigion sy\u0027n byw mewn dŵr ac o\u0027i gwmpas, yn ogystal ag at ddibenion hamdden a\u0027r defnydd ohono fel adnodd ar gyfer dŵr yfed, glanweithdra, diwydiant a masnach. Mae\u0027r cofnod hwn yn cyfeirio at gyfres o setiau data gofodol y gellir eu defnyddio ym maes rheoli Dŵr Gwastraff Trefol.  O dan y RhTGDT, mae ardaloedd sensitif pellach wedi\u0027u nodi oherwydd bod angen triniaeth bellach na thriniaeth eilaidd neu gyfatebol i fodloni Cyfarwyddebau EC eraill megis y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr. Mae\u0027r ardaloedd sy\u0027n dod o dan ardaloedd sensitif yn cynnwys Dyfroedd Ymdrochi, Dyfroedd Pysgod Cregyn a dyfroedd sydd eisoes yn Ewtroffig neu a fydd yn Ewtroffig cyn bo hir.  Dim ond ardaloedd sydd wedi\u0027u dynodi gan Lywodraeth Cymru o dan Reoliadau\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr y mae Dyfroedd Pysgod Cregyn RhTGDT yn eu dangos.  Mae Dyfroedd Ymdrochi RhTGDT yn dangos safleoedd lle mae triniaeth fwy caeth na thriniaeth eilaidd yn angenrheidiol i fodloni gofynion y Gyfarwyddeb Dyfroedd Ymdrochi a dylai Defra eu dynodi\u0027n ardaloedd sensitif  Mae Dyfroedd Ewtroffig RhTGDT yn gyrff dŵr sydd (neu a allai’n fuan droi) yn ewtroffig a dylent gael eu dynodi\u0027n ardaloedd sensitif gan Defra. Mae hyn yn berthnasol i ddyfroedd croyw llonydd, afonydd, aberoedd a dyfroedd arfordirol.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis dataset shows areas currently designated as UWWTR Sensitive Area Shellfish Waters in Wales.  The intention is to review and update the data every 4 years.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\UWWTR Sensitive Areas Shellfish Waters.lyr\tGeraint Weber - Team Leader Water Quality\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-08-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/5375/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_uwwtr\t2026-01-09T16:51:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18417\tNRW_DS119267\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.447Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSeal Sites in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323637\tThis dataset shows the location of seal sites in West Wales. The original version was based upon those identified in the West Wales Grey Seal Census 1991-1994 and Grey Seal census for North Wales 2004. See the base reports for further information. This data has subsequently been updated to include sightings in 2006 (see reports listed in the lineage for further information).  The original data was derived from the two reports (from 1995 and 2004 as detailed in the lineage) to meet a need to provide information on the location of seal sites that were included in CCW\u0027s marine monitoring program.  The purpose of this was to provide information to the UKDMOS catalogue.  NRWs collated data has been superseded by a collation undertaken by Welsh Government and published in 2022 on DataMapWales\tInformation Warning: This dataset is old with changes in distribution and use by seals likely to have changed and therefore should be used with caution.   The most recent grey seal census in North Wales (between Aberystwyth and the Dee Estuary) was carried out in 2017. The report for this survey is currently in preparation and will become available in 2019.  This base layer was created to identify all known seal sites, a derived layer was then created to illustrate just those sites where monitoring was ongoing or that was currently suspended.  As seals were previously on the CCW sensitive features list the information was then redacted to 5km square resolution and provided to UKDMOS.   A newer and expanded version of this data was created by NRW -  \u0027Seal haul out sites.lyr\u0027 This is an amalgamation of data from 4 sources: 1) Strong et al, 2006;  2) TCE, 2006;  3) Wescott, S.M. \u0026amp; Stringell, T.B (2004); 4) Bains, M.E., Earl, S.J., Pierpoint, C.J.L. \u0026amp; Poole, J. (1995);  However, WG have commissioned an update as part of their Sustainable Management of Marine Natural Resources project to include data captured in 2020 by NRW and should be considered the most recent relevant dataset.\t\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seal sites.lyr  X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seal haul out sites.lyr   The above are now considered old data and should be archived in favour of data published by WG on DataMapWales\tTom Stringell       This base layer was created to identify all know seal sites, a derived layer was then created to illustrate just those sites where monitoring was ongoing or that was currently suspended  X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seal Monitoring sites.lyr.  As seals were previously on the CCW sensitive features list the information was then redacted to 5km square resolution and provided to UKDMOS.  This redacted layer was also made available to WG Marine Monitoring Portal  ***to be removed when/if metadata is made publicly available**** This base layer was created to identify all known seal sites, a derived layer was then created to illustrate just those sites where monitoring was ongoing or that was currently suspended. As seals were previously on the CCW sensitive features list the information was then redacted to 5km square resolution and provided to UKDMOS. This redacted layer was also made available to WG Marine Monitoring Portal\t\t\t2021-07-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18418\tNRW_DS119270\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.360Z\t958\tValid\tEditable\tForest Management Coupes\tLlennyrch Rheoli Coedwigoedd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323730\tThis dataset shows management coupes. These are areas of intended management and may not be implemented when indicated or at all. The feature description field indicates the probable silvicultural system in use and the year field when that action will take place or be completed by.  A related layer is restocking coupes which show what happens to the area after management has taken place-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn dangos llennyrch rheoli. Mae\u0027r rhain yn ardaloedd rheoli bwriadedig, ond mae\u0027n bosibl na chânt eu gweithredu fel y\u0027i nodir os o gwbl. Mae\u0027r disgrifiad o\u0027r nodwedd yn nodi\u0027r system goedamaeth debygol sy\u0027n cael ei defnyddio a\u0027r flwyddyn yn nodi pryd y bydd y gweithredu hwnnw\u0027n digwydd neu’n cael ei gwblhau erbyn.  Haen berthnasol yw llennyrch ailstocio sy\u0027n dangos yr hyn sy\u0027n digwydd i\u0027r ardal ar ôl iddi gael ei rheoli  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe data is a product of data administered and maintained by Forestry Planners, exported from the NRW \u0027Forester\u0027 GIS application on a quarterly basis. Since the creation of NRW in 2013, NRW have maintained the data in the Forester recreation module.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Management Coupes.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan  Secondary Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2024-02-29\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3515/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_forest_management_coupes\t2025-11-14T13:44:51.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18424\tNRW_DS119273\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.514Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Forest Bridges\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323733\tNational Forest Estate Bridges are managed by Forestry Civil Engineering in one of NRW\u0027s Forester GIS modules. This data set comprises location and category of construction.\tForests Roads is a product of data administered and maintained by Forestry Civil Engineering, exported from the NRW \u0027Forester\u0027 GIS application.    Since the creation of NRW in 2013, NRW have maintained the data in the Forester recreation module.\t\tX:\\Transport\\Bridges\\NRW Forest Bridges.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan    Secondary Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-06-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:00:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18422\tNRW_DS119281\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.448Z\t1157\tValid\tEditable\tForest Restock Coupes\tLlennyrch Ailstocio Coedwigoedd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323831\tThis dataset shows restocking coupes. These are areas of intended management and may not be implemented when indicated or at all. The attribution indicates the probable habitat or species and the year field when those will take place or be completed by. A related layer is management coupes which show what management is intended prior to restocking-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn dangos llennyrch ailstocio. Mae\u0027r rhain yn ardaloedd rheoli bwriadedig, ond mae\u0027n bosibl na chânt eu gweithredu fel y\u0027i nodir os o gwbl. Mae\u0027r priodoliad yn nodi\u0027r cynefinoedd neu rywogaethau tebygol a\u0027r flwyddyn y bydd y rhain yn digwydd neu\u0027n cael eu cwblhau erbyn.    Haen berthnasol yw llennyrch rheoli sy\u0027n dangos pa gamau rheoli y bwriedir eu cymryd cyn ailstocio  Cydnabyddiaeth Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe data is a product of data administered and maintained by Forestry Planners, exported from the NRW \u0027Forester\u0027 GIS application on a quarterly basis. Since the creation of NRW in 2013, NRW have maintained the data in the Forester recreation module.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Restock Coupes.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Restock 2nd rotation.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan Secondary Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-06-30-2024-02-29\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3503/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_forest_restock_coupes\t2025-03-07T15:59:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18419\tNRW_DS119284\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.413Z\t618\tValid\tEditable\tGood for People Toolkit\tPecyn Cymorth Da ar gyfer Pobl\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323834\tThis is not a specific dataset, but rather the Good for People Toolkit is a spatial modelling framework developed to aid the bringing together and combining of data on a modelling platform producing key evidence for use by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).   This metadata entry can be supplied alongside data outputs although more specific metadata may be written if required for specific outputs.   Outputs from the model could be in the form of spreadsheets, spatial layers or maps, but all require some form of interpretation and knowledge for them to be understood by users.     About the Model: In 2014, NRW commissioned AECOM to develop a GIS based toolkit that would help to inform decisions around the use and targeting of NRW resources in order to best deliver priorities and outcomes that are \"Good for People\".   The Good for People GIS Toolkit is a spatial tool designed to help the Good for People programmes and the associated Enabling Plans: Outdoor Access and Recreation; Communities and Regeneration; and Education and Skills.   The toolkit addresses 18 research questions specified by NRW in relation to these plans by allowing data to be viewed in combination. These cover:  Q01 - Deprivation;  Q02 - Physical and Mental Ill-health;  Q03 - Unemployment;  Q04 - Education;  Q05 - Community Facilities;  Q06 - ESTYN (annual inspection report for individual education settings and for educational authorities);  Q07 - NEET and Pre-NEET (Not in Education Employment or Training);  Q08 - Access to Natural Green Space;  Q09 - Urban Tree Canopy Cover;  Q10 - Air Quality;  Q11 - Flooding (criteria not mapped at time);  Q12 - Access to NRW Land;  Q13 - Recreation Provision;  Q14 - Crime and Anti-Social Behaviour;  Q15 - Tourism;  Q16 - Active Travel;  Q17 - Slope;  Q18 - NRW and Partner Managed Land.   The toolkit can be used in a number of different ways. It has been designed to allow users to select the data layers that are most relevant to their purposes. Users can be confident that as an NRW-wide tool all NRW managers will be using the same evidence in a visual format so that plans for different areas can be compared fairly. The Good for People GIS Toolkit should be used where possible to support strategic and operational decision making within NRW in particular to strengthen decisions involving the needs of local communities and the benefits they derive from using and engaging with the natural environment.\tThe Good for People Toolkit model was developed by AECOM to support the deliver of evidence for the Good for People Programmes. It has been developed through non-technical consultation with different areas of the organisation, helping to define the evidence required by certain departments.   Internal work involved identifying and sourcing what data was available and finding out what staff wanted to use the model for, and develop realistic and achievable questions which needed to be answered. The technical building of the model was completed by AECOM with these requirements in mind. It has been designed to allow users to select the data layers that are most relevant to their purposes. Users can be confident that as an NRW-wide tool, all NRW managers will be using the same evidence in a visual format so that plans for different trial areas can be compared fairly.   There are a number of limitations to how the toolkit can be used, which should be taken into consideration:  \u0026gt; The toolkit cannot make decisions. The user will need to use their own knowledge, experience and \u0027ground-truthing\u0027  in order to interpret the data.   \u0026gt; There are limitations in the data and availability of suitable indicators. While the toolkit uses best available data and indicators there are gaps.   \u0026gt; There is variation in the geographical scale at which the data is available. Most of the data use is at Lower Super Output Area (LSOA) level, but some is less specific and only at, for example, local authority level.  \u0026gt; The toolkit does not provide information about individuals or households. It provides information about small areas.   \u0026gt; When looking at maps using LSOA-Level data on deprivation users need to be careful not to think that a large LSOAs mean there are lots of deprived people living there.  \u0026gt; It has been designed to allow NRW to add in new and updated data layers . However, choices made at the toolkit design stage limit this flexibility.\tPlease see Good for People GIS Toolkit Non-technical User Guide for further details on this and the tools itself, which includes case studies, further information about the limitations and instructions for use.\tV:\\Good For People GIS Toolkit Documentation: V:\\Good For People GIS Toolkit\\Documentation Project Files: V:\\Good For People GIS Toolkit\\project_files Info also available on the DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/are/commu/sp/Guidance/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?InitialTabId\u003dRibbon%2EDocument\u0026amp;VisibilityContext\u003dWSSTabPersistence\tMetadata / Custodian Contact: Ben Anscombe GIS and Spatial Data Team. KIM have the technical responsibility for the tool.  Ros Owen Recreation, Health and Wellbeing Team Leader  is also a key contact as she worked on gathering organisational requirements.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2014-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T13:30:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18421\tNRW_DS119289\t2024-05-28T12:48:35.414Z\t878\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD) Sewage Treatment Works\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323839\tSpatial dataset containing wastewater treatment plants monitored and reported under the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD) in Wales. These plants serve population equivalent (PE) greater than 2000 if discharging to freshwater or greater than 10000 if discharging to coastal/transitional waters. Details include load, treatment types and performance.\tData as reported to the European Commission under Article 15 of the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive. The original sources of data are the NRW Water Information Management System (WIMS) archive and data supplied by Water Companies.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\UWWTD Sewage Treatment Works.lyr\tMark Squire, Team Leader Water Policy \u0026amp; Directives Implementation\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2012-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:17:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18416\tNRW_DS119291\t2024-05-28T12:48:34.967Z\t395\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTotal Peatland\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323931\tThis dataset comprises the total peatland for Wales. The peat is categorised as Deep Peaty Soil, Modified Deep Peaty Soil, Shallow Peaty Soil and Soil with Peaty Pockets.\tPeatland in pristine or good condition provides a range of critical ecosystem services, including biodiversity, carbon storage and sequestration, regulation of stream base flows, water runoff and downstream flood peaks and nutrient regulation and retention. Peatlands are also sinks and sources of several natural greenhouse gases, particularly carbon dioxide (CO2) and methane (CH4). More than 75% of deep peat soils in Wales are covered in semi-natural vegetation. Most of this is upland blanket bog, with significant amounts of fen and flush and, locally, lowland raised bog. These are all UK Biodiversity Action Plan priority habitats with UK and Welsh targets for habitat management and restoration.  The National Peat Map for Wales was produced by Forest Research on behalf of Forestry Commission Wales and also involved CCW and EAW. The data shows the total extent of deep peat, modified deep peat, shallow peaty soils and soils with peaty pockets. The extent of deep peat is based on soil (NSRI), drift geology (BGS) and peatland habitat mapping (CCW Phase 1 survey), with the latter being taken as evidence of deep peat when NSRI/BGS is lacking. The data was further improved by making use of recently digitised soil mapping for Welsh forests from the original Forestry Commission soil survey records. The extent of available FC soil survey information across Wales is in total 74,985 ha that have been surveyed.\tForest Research report \u0027A Strategic Assessment of the Afforested Peat Resource in Wales\u0027\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\Total Peatland.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-10-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18460\tNRW_DS119292\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.088Z\t275\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPeat Top Ten Sites\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323932\tThis dataset comprises the National Assessment top 10 sites for potential peat restoration.  Peatland in pristine or good condition provides a range of critical ecosystem services, including biodiversity, carbon storage and sequestration, regulation of stream base flows, water runoff and downstream flood peaks and nutrient regulation and retention. Peatlands are also sinks and sources of several natural greenhouse gases, particularly carbon dioxide (CO2) and methane (CH4). More than 75% of deep peat soils in Wales are covered in semi-natural vegetation. Most of this is upland blanket bog, with significant amounts of fen and flush and, locally, lowland raised bog. These are all UK Biodiversity Action Plan priority habitats with UK and Welsh targets for habitat management and restoration.   In order to deliver the habitat restoration targets set out in the Welsh Environmental Strategy, there is a need to progressively restore semi-natural habitat in afforested areas. Restoration measures should seek to better integrate the key objectives of nature conservation, soil carbon protection, carbon sequestration and water quality.\tA national GIS assessment was carried out by Forest Research to identify potential restoration areas in Wales. Five criteria were used in this assessment: peat current suitability, peat hydro suitability, peat green house gas suitability, peat ecology suitability and peat climate suitability. From these five criteria, a combined score was generated and a dataset created of sites which fall into the top 25%. A 100m buffer was added to each site and the ten largest amalgamated areas were identified as top 10 sites for potential restoration.\tForest Research Report \u0027A Strategic Assessment of the Afforested Peat Resource in Wales\u0027\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\Peat Top Ten Sites.lyr\tCustodian: Keith Harri Jones, GIS and Spatial Services Team  Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-10-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:50.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18470\tNRW_DS119295\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.356Z\t790\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Pipelines on NRW Land\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323935\tThis dataset contains water pipelines on NRW land. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any utility data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured water pipeline information\tThe information is usually gathered on site by contract managers who walk the site to ensure all water pipeline information is available in contract mapper. Generally on screen capture from paper mapping, but some captured using GPS, validated by GIS Technicians and Operational Staff.  Information Warning: There is no guarantee of the completeness and accuracy of the data and therefore should not be used as the sole source of evidence for any work.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Water\\Water Pipelines on NRW Land.lyr\tGIS Technicians in 4 Forest Districts, National Forest Planning Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:50.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18467\tNRW_DS119296\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.323Z\t521\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUnderground Telephone Lines on Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Land\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323936\tThis dataset contains Underground Telephone Lines on NRW land. Currently the data covers forestry areas but in future will expand to all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any utility data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured underground telephone line information.\tThe information is usually gathered on site by contract managers who walk the site to ensure all underground telephone line information is available in contract mapper. Generally on screen capture from paper mapping, but some captured using GPS, validated by GIS Technicians and Operational Staff.   Information Warning: There is no guarantee of the completeness and accuracy of the data and therefore should not be used as the sole source of evidence for any work.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Telecoms\\UG Telephone Lines on NRW Land.lyr\tCustodian: GIS Technicians in 4 Forest Districts, National Forest Planning Team Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:13:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18463\tNRW_DS119297\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.083Z\t290\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOver Head Telephone Lines on Natural Resource Wales (NRW) Land\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323937\tThis dataset contains Overhead Telephone Lines on Natural Resource Wales (NRW) land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any utility data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured overhead telephone line information.\tThe information is currently gathered on site by contract managers who walk the site to ensure all overhead telephone line information is available in contract mapper. Generally on screen capture from paper mapping, but some captured using GPS, validated by GIS Technicians and Operational Staff.  Information Warning: There is no guarantee of the completeness and accuracy of the data and therefore should not be used as the sole source of evidence for any work.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Telecoms\\OH Telephone Lines on NRW Land.lyr\tGIS Technicians in 4 Forest Districts, National Forest Planning Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T13:30:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18464\tNRW_DS119299\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.196Z\t342\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSection 42 Bryophytes Register\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139323939\tA set of records of bryophytes listed on Section 42 of the NERC Act, localised as accurately as possible and with the most recent record for a site listed rather than a full set of records from each site.\tRecords were collated by Sam Bosanquet (NRW) on behalf of Plant Link Cymru, using data from NBN and direct discussion with many of the original recorders to elucidate locality details.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Bryophytes S42 points.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-11-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:02:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18468\tNRW_DS119300\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.325Z\t699\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThreatened Bryophytes Species\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333030\tA set of records of bryophytes listed as threatened in GB and/or Wales, localised as accurately as possible, with records of less than 100m resolution excluded (these are in another layer on Y:).\tRecords were collated by Sam Bosanquet (NRW), using data from NBN (British Bryological Society) and direct discussion with many of the original recorders to elucidate locality details.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Bryophytes Threatened Species 2015.lyr   The spreadsheet for this dataset is available on the DMS: Evidence \u0026gt; Species \u0026gt; Bryophytes \u0026gt; Bryophyte Statuses https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Information/Bryophyte%20Statuses\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-11-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18461\tNRW_DS119301\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.090Z\t555\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSection 42 Fungi Register\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333031\tA set of records of fungi listed on Section 42 of the NERC Act, localised as accurately as possible, with imprecise records excluded.\tRecords were collated by Gareth Griffith (Aberystwyth University) on behalf of Plant Link Cymru, using data from the NBN Atlas and direct discussion with many of the original recorders to elucidate locality details.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Fungi S42 Inventory 2014.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Fungi S42 point.lyr   These excluded records are on the Y Drive  The spatial data have been created from this excel document: Evidence \u0026gt; Species \u0026gt; Fungi \u0026gt; Fungus Information \u0026gt; Fungus Statuses  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/fun/Fungus%20Information/Fungus%20Statuses/Section%2042%20draft%20sites%20inventory%20fungi%20ex%20UWA.XLS\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-11-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:34:58.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18462\tNRW_DS119302\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.190Z\t388\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSection 42 Lichens Register\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333032\tA set of records of lichens listed on Section 42 of the NERC Act, localised as accurately as possible, with imprecise records excluded.\tRecords were collated by Ray Woods (formerly of CCW and Plantlife) on behalf of Plant Link Cymru, using data from NBN and direct discussion with many of the original recorders to elucidate locality details.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Lichens S42 Inventory 2014.lyr   Excluded records these are on another layer on Y:\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-11-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18474\tNRW_DS119303\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.389Z\t435\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThreatened Lichen Species\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333033\tA set of records of lichens listed as threatened in GB and/or Wales, localised as accurately as possible, with records of less than 100m resolution excluded.\tRecords were collated by Sam Bosanquet (NRW), using data from NBN (British Lichen Society) and direct discussion with many of the original recorders to elucidate locality details.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Lichens Threatened Species 2015.lyr   The excel spreadsheet associated with this file is saved in the following location on the DMS: Evidence \u0026gt; Species \u0026gt; Lichens \u0026gt; Lichen Information \u0026gt; Lichen Statuses \u0026gt; Wales threatened lichens 2015 all records https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Information/Lichen%20Statuses/Wales%20Threatened%20Lichens%202015%20all%20records.xlsx\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-11-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:46.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18475\tNRW_DS119362\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.490Z\t551\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPowys Intensive Livestock Units 2004 2017\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333632\tA comprehensive dataset of the locations of intensive livestock units (poultry and pigs) in Powys, operational since 2004.  This is an ongoing, working dataset and will be updated periodically to reflect any new developments across Powys. For internal staff use only.  See also Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2008 2017 - NRW_DS119363 and Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2017 only - NRW_DS119364.\tData set sourced from conservation officer in the Radnorshire NRM team to collaborate all the information on intensive livestock units across Powys to help inform decision making for NRM teams assessing environmental impacts of proposed developments. There is no guarantee that the data within it reflects 100% of the livestock units in question – it is merely a \u0027guide\u0027 and should only be used as such.\tInformation sourced from the Powys planning portal, NRW planning consultations and NRW permitting data.\tX:\\Physical\\Agricultural\\Powys intensive livestock units 2004 2017.lyr\tRachel Jarvis - conservation officer in the Radnorshire NRM team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-11-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:47:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18466\tNRW_DS119363\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.324Z\t323\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPowys Intensive Livestock Units 2008 2017\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333633\tA comprehensive dataset of the locations of intensive livestock units (poultry and pigs) in Powys, operational since 2004.  This is an ongoing, working dataset and will be updated periodically to reflect any new developments across Powys. For internal staff use only.  See also Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2004 2017 - NRW_DS119362 and Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2017 only - NRW_DS119364.\tData set sourced from conservation officer in the Radnorshire NRM team to collaborate all the information on intensive livestock units across Powys to help inform decision making for NRM teams assessing environmental impacts of proposed developments. There is no guarantee that the data within it reflects 100% of the livestock units in question – it is merely a \u0027guide\u0027 and should only be used as such.\tInformation sourced from the Powys planning portal, NRW planning consultations and NRW permitting data.\tX:\\Physical\\Agricultural\\Powys intensive livestock units 2008 2017.lyr\tRachel Jarvis - conservation officer in the Radnorshire NRM team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-11-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:47:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18472\tNRW_DS119364\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.388Z\t484\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPowys Intensive Livestock Units 2017 only\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139333634\tA comprehensive dataset of the locations of intensive livestock units (poultry and pigs) in Powys, operational since 2004.  This is an ongoing, working dataset and will be updated periodically to reflect any new developments across Powys. For internal staff use only.  See also Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2004 2017 - NRW_DS119362 and Powys Intensive Livestock Units 2008 2017 - NRW_DS119363.\tData set sourced from conservation officer in the Radnorshire NRM team to collaborate all the information on intensive livestock units across Powys to help inform decision making for NRM teams assessing environmental impacts of proposed developments. There is no guarantee that the data within it reflects 100% of the livestock units in question – it is merely a \u0027guide\u0027 and should only be used as such.\tInformation sourced from the Powys planning portal, NRW planning consultations and NRW permitting data.\tX:\\Physical\\Agricultural\\Powys intensive livestock units 2017 onwards.lyr\tRachel Jarvis - conservation officer in the Radnorshire NRM team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-07-02\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:47:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18469\tNRW_DS119974\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.200Z\t513\tValid\tEditable\tForest Priority Catchments 2016\tDalgylchoedd Coedwig â Blaenoriaeth 2016\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313139393734\tThe dataset comprises a number of river catchments based on Water Framework Directive water body boundaries which have been identified by NRW as areas within which forestry environmental improvement works will be prioritised to reduce the impact of forestry on the water environment.-The dataset comprises a number of river catchments based on Water Framework Directive water body boundaries which have been identified by NRW as areas within which forestry environmental improvement works will be prioritised to reduce the impact of forestry on the water environment.  The catchments were identified as a priority based upon their failure to achieve good or better status, the identification of forestry as a contributing reason for that failure and at least 30% of the area of the catchment being under closed canopy woodland cover.  Attribution statement Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved.\tThe catchments were identified as a priority based upon their failure to achieve good or better status, the identification of forestry as a contributing reason for that failure and at least 30% of the area of the catchment being under closed canopy woodland cover.      This data set was created using the Environment Agency (EA) Water Framework Directive (WFD) Priority Catchment Boundaries, published May 3 2012. The Forestry Commission Wales National Forest Inventory (NFI) forest cover types was also used. The NFI was unioned with the EA priority catchments, to gain the information in the ‘Raw Data’ worksheet tab.  The Results were than analysed with pivot tables and are shown in the \u0027Results\u0027 tab.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW Forestry Priority Catchments 2016.lyr\tCustodian: Dave Johnston, Team Leader Environmental Reporting Metadata Point of Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-08-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3484/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_forest_priority_catchments_2016\t2025-03-07T15:58:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18473\tNRW_DS120006\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.321Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tKiWQM Sampling Stations\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230303036\tThe KiWQM Sampling Stations GIS layer provides a route of discovery for the spatial element of all data relating to water quality measurements that have taken place in and around Wales. It is a record of locations where water sampling has taken place.  Formerly this data was held as 2 layers; Open stations (Metadata # 120746) and Closed stations (Metadata # 120747). Information for sampling locations for stations in England are documented in a separate metadata entry: 120732 (Environment Agency (EA): KiWQM English Stations (3rd Party Data))\tSince the 1960\u0027s data has been collected from almost 23,000 sampling points, with circa 2,500 active today. Samples are taken from coastal or estuarine waters, rivers, lakes, ponds, canals or groundwater.  Much of the archive we now hold was created by our predecessor bodies, including Environment Agency Wales.  Additional data has been provided by third parties such as Dwr Cymru/ Welsh Water and County Councils. All data currently held is considered to be public record. The data held at the stations measures a wide range of water quality parameters, such as nutrients, metals and physio-chemical determinants. These data are used to assess discharge monitoring against discharge permits, compliance with National and International legislation, investigation of pollution incidents or environmental monitoring.\t\tData is stored in WISKI New layer on GIS X:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\KiWQM NRW Stations.lyr\tData custodian is Martin Stephens  Simon Webb, Stuart Kay       OLLY - i can confirm that this relates to X:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\KiWQM NRW Stations.lyr GARY needs to confirm if this entry, it didn\u0027t seem to be related to a specific dataset, but potentially does now.  X:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\KiWQM NRW Stations.lyr???? MJ  - I don\u0027t think the the access and use is correct in this entry as only third party data may be restricted, should look at the access and use constraint statements for the KiWQM overarching dataset  - GIS team to check\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-03-03\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18459\tNRW_DS120044\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.909Z\t402\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCondition assessment of the Glutinous Snail Myxas glutinosa in Llyn Tegid in 2014\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230303434\tAs part of the Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Common Standards Monitoring programme, this report documents the results of monitoring for the Glutinous Snail Myxas glutinosa during 2014 at Llyn Tegid using the Conservation Objective produced for this snail (Willing, 2004, 2006, 2010). Llyn Tegid was visited in late October 2014 and, over two days, eight survey-stations situated around the lake were monitored (those used on nine previous occasions since 1999) for the presence of the snail (and associated molluscs) using the sampling procedures as detailed in the Conservation Objective. M. glutinosa was found at all 7 sites where it had previously been recorded in numbers that, using Condition Assessment standards, allows the status of the snail to be judged as in a Favourable Condition in the lake in 2014. The population has been in Favourable Condition since standardised monitoring first took place in 2002. The habitat at all of the sites was also judged to be in ‘good condition’ in 2014. Results from surveys undertaken since April 2000 are also included in the report. Following status monitoring work a live sample of M. glutinosa was taken under licence from NRW from the lake survey to allow captive observation of the snails to be undertaken and to investigate the possibility of captive breeding of the snail. Initial observation of the captive Myxas has revealed that the snails emerge from beneath stones in darkness and feed upon encrusting algae both on stones and glass tank sides. Myxas bred in captivity at the end of the experiment.\tSurveys were by visual searches and stone-turning. Survey work was undertaken under Wildlife and Countryside Act (WCA) licence from NRW.\t\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive  Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database - Recorder 6\tCustodian: Mike Howe, invertebrate ecologist NRW.  External Contact / Originator - was put here because it looks as if this might be his home address as well - therefore in Responsible Organisation Martin Willing is C/O NRW. Dr Martin J Willing 14 Goodwood Close MIDHURST West Sussex GU29 9JG\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:56.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18458\tNRW_DS120063\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.849Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNorth West Anglesey Modiolus – Drop Down Video Survey Sept 2009 \u0026amp; 2010\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230303633\tThe area to the North and West of the Isle of Anglesey (North Wales, UK) is one where horse mussel Modiolus modiolus beds are thought to occur. In the past, several surveys have recorded aggregations of Modiolus in this area but in general the density and extent of Modiolus has not been accurately mapped. Modiolus beds are important because they are part of the Habitats Directive Annex I Reef habitat, are on the OSPAR list of threatened and declining habitats and are a Biodiversity Action Plan habitat. Modiolus beds appear to be extremely sensitive to physical disturbance and are thought to have declined in extent and quality. A collaborative project between the Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) and the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) (now Natural Resources Wales) began in 2009 to investigate the possible presence of Modiolus modiolus beds and other reef habitat at two locations to the north and west of Anglesey. These locations were chosen based on historical evidence of the presence of the horse mussel Modiolus modiolus. Area NWA1-North is between 32 and 38km north of Holyhead and has an area of almost 14km2. It is characterised by a deep trench running ENE through the study area, which is approximately 105m at its deepest point, which shallows towards the north and south to depths of approximately 45 and 60m respectively. Area NWA2-West is located between 23 and 28km WNW of Holyhead and is almost 20km2 in area. The shallowest point is in the SE of the study area where there is a plateau at approximately 50m depth, which then drops off towards the NW to the base of a slope at approximately 90m deep.   The two areas were surveyed using a Multibeam Echosounder and drop down video. The Centre for Environment, Fisheries and Aquaculture Science (CEFAS) carried out a Multibeam Echosounder survey in July 2009 and the resulting data was interpreted for bedrock, quaternary bedforms, sediments and habitat features by CEFAS. Four sites in water deeper than 100m were surveyed using a stills camera by the Centre for Marine and Coastal Studies in June 2010. CCW then surveyed selected areas of interest using a drop down video during September 2009 and January 2010. The video footage was collected to identify Annex 1 Horse Mussel (Modiolus) reef habitat by Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd, who developed methods by which to score areas of potential reef to confirm presence of live Modiolus reef, and where possible quantify live Modiolus. 9 video transects were analysed. First they were split into rough sections of different habitat. Areas of potential Modiolus reef were identified, scored for Modiolus and Alcyonium (a known proxy for live Modiolus in the right habitat), and then JNCC’s Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) style habitat surveys were conducted. MNCR style habitat surveys were also undertaken on sections of habitat which were either flat, unconsolidated reefs of dead Modiolus shell, or habitats where no Modiolus were present. All habitats longer than 1 minute in length were further divided into 1 minute subsections (to keep the surveyors’ attention and increase the accuracy of scoring large numbers of Alcyonium).    In total 158 subsections of habitat were analysed (92 scored for Modiolus; 66 subsections for MNCR phase 2 style habitat survey only). 2 areas of certain live Modiolus reef were identified and confirmed, whilst another 7 subsections of habitat showed signs of live Modiolus that may be within areas of potential reef.  This could be due to the ‘banded’ habitats identified, where unconsolidated Modiolus shell gravel, embedded lag cobbles and pebbles, and live Modiolus reef were identified in ‘bands’ across a possibly gradually undulating seabed. 10% of the one minute sections were resurveyed by different surveyors for Quality Assurance (QA of counts and MNCR). As much of the video scoring could be considered ‘semi-quantitative’ and partially subjective, QA did not aim to fi ...\tMethod of data capture: Towed video sledge Purpose of data capture: To identify Annex 1 Modiolus Reef offshore from North West Anglesey Confidence in data: Good\tThe following information from analysis of the videos has been entered onto the Marine Recorder database: seabed type, species, species abundance, biotope.  Additional information was obtained from JNCC multibeam data - see metadata entry EXT_DS115167. Ramsay, K., Walker, P., Bayley, D., Golding, N., Robson, L. 2021. North West Anglesey Survey of Possible Horse Mussel Bed Areas (2009 \u0026amp; 2010). NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 575, 122pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\t[A] The data is stored in the marine archive  as project 268, media 965-979 (videos), media 1307 \u0026amp; 1308 (CMACS video and image grabs), 1309 (analysis and outputs). File path \u003d K:\\Archive\\ Media 900 -1000\\Media 965 etc. etc. [B] The report is still in draft but a copy is in the DMS  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/proj/_layouts/15/DocIdRedir.aspx?ID\u003dEVID-201-24 [C] Marine Recorder surveys : [see MRCCW31900000002 and JNCC survey MRCON02100000003 in the snapshot only)\tInternal Contact: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team        Distributor contact details: Miss Liz Morris Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd, 41 High Street, Menai Bridge, Anglesey, LL59 5EF 07868 705017 or 01248 712277 liz@marine-ecosol.com www.marine-ecosol.com  Analysis was undertaken on the following CCW-held video files: 20090925Tape1NWAngleseyModiolusSurface.avi 20090925Tape2NWAngleseyModiolusSurface.avi 20090928Tape3NWAngleseyModiolusSurface.avi  Data supplied by the contractor, Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd. are: 2 Modiolus_CCW_Metadata.xls \u003d the metadata taken on the day of survey, together with summary details of ALL habitat and subsection divisions 3 Modiolus Data Entry.xls \u003d ALL raw results / data scored by the contractor. Columns highlighted in red are definite Modiolus reef; highlighted in cream are areas of potential reef; text in red are QA duplicate scores 4 Summary of NW Anglesey Modiolus Reef only Sept09.xls \u003d this excel table summarises the areas of confirmed and potential Modiolus reef only.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-06-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:32.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18471\tNRW_DS120116\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.307Z\t564\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Lamprey Species Survey 2013 – 2015\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230313136\tRiver Wye SAC lamprey species population condition assessment using revised Common Standards Monitoring attribute targets (JNCC, 2015). Analysis of lamprey larvae surveys conducted using electrofishing technique and an analysis of population spatial extent, density and age structure for the sea lamprey (Petromyzon marinus), river lamprey (Lampetra fluviatilis) \u0026amp; brook lamprey (Lampetra planeri).\tData extracted from KiECO, GIS maps created \u0026amp; report issued.\tGarrett HM. 2017. River Wye SAC lamprey population condition assessment. Monitoring Round 2013 to 2018.  SAC monitoring summary note. Pp.24. Natural Resources Wales. Dolgellau.\tKiECO database (WISKI)  Some data is also available on the DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/fm/Running%20Waters%20Habitats%20Directive%20Data%20Cycle%203/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Ffm%2FRunning%20Waters%20Habitats%20Directive%20Data%20Cycle%203%2FWye%20Lamprey\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000EE02D3CE2ED1AE4085983FEDF88CC2E5\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B90B85581%2DA33A%2D4F0D%2DB9D4%2DBCB28FC39C08%7D\tSophie Gott, Fisheries Technical Specialist, NRW Heather Garrett, Freshwater Monitoring Co-ordinator, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:46.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18465\tNRW_DS120146\t2024-05-28T12:49:36.289Z\t502\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Priority Catchments 2013\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230313436\tThe dataset comprises a number of river catchments based on Water Framework Directive water body boundaries which have been identified by NRW as areas within which forestry environmental improvement works will be prioritised to reduce the impact of forestry on the water environment.\tThe catchments were identified as a priority based upon their failure to achieve good or better status, the identification of forestry as a contributing reason for that failure and at least 30% of the area of the catchment being under closed canopy woodland cover.  This data set was created using the Environment Agency (EA) Water Framework Directive (WFD) Priority Catchment Boundaries, published May 3 2012. The Forestry Commission Wales National Forest Inventory (NFI) forest cover types was also used. The NFI was unioned with the EA priority catchments, to gain the information in the ‘Raw Data’ worksheet tab.  The Results were than analysed with pivot tables and are shown in the ‘Results’ tab.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle1\\NRW Forestry Priority Catchments 2013.lyr\tCustodian: Dave Johnston, Team Leader Environmental Reporting Metadata Point of Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18456\tNRW_DS120294\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.482Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBadger Licences\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230323934\tThe dataset includes details of Badger licences issued from 2002 to date.  Information includes the licence number, applicant, location, activity and dates of validity.\tData collated via desk top exercise extracting data from paper files, Ffynnon and NRW DMS.  Data extracted to excel spreadsheet and updated on a regular basis.\t\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Species and habitat\\Badger licences.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Iwan G. Hughes, Team Leader, Species \u0026amp; Carbon Permitting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-06-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18457\tNRW_DS120494\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.799Z\t996\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGroundwater Vulnerability (2018)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230343934\tGroundwater vulnerability maps reflect improvements in data mapping and understanding of the factors affecting vulnerability. The maps show the vulnerability of groundwater at a location based on the hydrological, geological, hydrogeological and soil properties within a 1 kilometre square grid. The maps provide a separate assessment of the vulnerability of groundwater in overlying \u0027superficial\u0027, rocks, and those that comprise the underlying \u0027bedrock\u0027. The main aim of the maps is as a high level screening tool to give NRW, planners, consultants and other users an indication of whether a proposed development or activity is likely to be acceptable (e.g. located in an area of low vulnerability or over unproductive strata) or of potential concern (e.g. located in an area of high vulnerability). The maps can also be used to inform and target environmental management and incident response so that preventative and/or remedial actions can be taken as early as possible to protect groundwater.  Two main rock types are recognised: - Superficial (drift): permeable unconsolidated (loose) deposits, such as sands and gravels.  - Bedrock: solid permeable formations such as sandstone, chalk and limestone.  The vulnerability of these two rock types may differ due to their structure and location. Where both types are present, the maps display the most vulnerable category of the two.\tFor the first time the maps provide a separate assessment of the vulnerability of groundwater in overlying (or \u0027superficial\u0027) rocks and those that comprise the underlying \u0027bedrock\u0027. Previously only bedrock was assessed. Some rock types have been reclassified from non-aquifers to secondary aquifers in recognition of their importance for local water supplies and baseflow to rivers. The maps identify areas where solution features that enable rapid movement of a contaminant may be present and show where NRW  hold local information that would affect vulnerability in that area. These changes have resulted in an increase in the area of land that is considered to require protection and an increase in the area that is more vulnerable.\tThe aquifer designation data used in the new maps is based on geological mapping provided by the British Geological Survey. The maps also include Soil Map data licensed from Cranfield University.\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Simplified Groundwater Vulnerability.lyr  X:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Combined Groundwater Vulnerability.lyr   Associated layerfiles with this metadata are: Groundwater Vulnerability Groundwater Vulnerability Local Information Groundwater Vulnerability Solid Rock Risk\tDave Jones\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-British Geological Survey (BGS)\t\t2021-07-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18454\tNRW_DS120507\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.228Z\t1000\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) A Felling Plan\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230353037\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of felling proposed by the Developer for the wind farm development. As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tNot supplied\t\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA A Felling Plan.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:19:58.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18449\tNRW_DS120730\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.086Z\t601\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) F Felling Plan\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230373330\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of felling proposed by the Developer for the wind farm development.\tNot supplied\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA F Felling Plan.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:20:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18452\tNRW_DS120746\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.164Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tKiWQM Open Stations\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230373436\tThe KiWQM Sampling Stations GIS layer provides a route of discovery for the spatial element of all data relating to water quality measurements that have taken place in and around Wales. It is a record of locations where water sampling has taken place.  ***Replaced by metadata entry 120006**** which has created a combined open and closed station dataset\tSince the 1960\u0027s data has been collected from almost 23,000 sampling points, with circa 2,500 active today. Samples are taken from coastal or estuarine waters, rivers, lakes, ponds, canals or groundwater. Much of the archive we now hold was created by our predecessor bodies, including Environment Agency Wales. Additional data has been provided by third parties such as Dwr Cymru/ Welsh Water and County Councils. All data currently held is considered to be public record. The data held at the stations measures a wide range of water quality parameters, such as nutrients, metals and physio-chemical determinants. These data are used to assess discharge monitoring against discharge permits, compliance with National and International legislation, investigation of pollution incidents or environmental monitoring. This data has also been used to create a spatial dataset: KiWQM Open Stations\t\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\KiWQM NRW Open Stations.lyr  Source data is stored in WISKI\tSimon Webb, Stuart Kay - now Martin Stephens?       MJ  - I don\u0027t think the the access and use is correct in this entry as only third party data may be restricted, should look at the access and use constraint statements for the KiWQM overarching dataset  - GIS team to check\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-01-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18455\tNRW_DS120747\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.208Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tKiWQM Closed Stations\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313230373437\tThe KiWQM Sampling Stations GIS layer provides a route of discovery for the spatial element of all data relating to water quality measurements that have taken place in and around Wales. It is a record of locations where water sampling has taken place.  ***Replaced by metadata entry 120006**** which has created a combined open and closed station dataset\tSince the 1960\u0027s data has been collected from almost 23,000 sampling points, with circa 2,500 active today. Samples are taken from coastal or estuarine waters, rivers, lakes, ponds, canals or groundwater. Much of the archive we now hold was created by our predecessor bodies, including Environment Agency Wales.  Additional data has been provided by third parties such as Dwr Cymru/ Welsh Water and County Councils. All data currently held is considered to be public record. The data held at the stations measures a wide range of water quality parameters, such as nutrients, metals and physio-chemical determinants. These data are used to assess discharge monitoring against discharge permits, compliance with National and International legislation, investigation of pollution incidents or environmental monitoring. This data has also been used to create a spatial file - KiWQM Closed Stations.\t\tx:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\KiWQM NRW Closed Stations.lyr  Source data is stored in WISKI\tSimon Webb, Stuart Kay       MJ  - I don\u0027t think the the access and use is correct in this entry as only third party data may be restricted, should look at the access and use constraint statements for the KiWQM overarching dataset  - GIS team to check\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-01-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18453\tNRW_DS121120\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.203Z\t353\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring Invertebrate features on Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs): assessing and reviewing the status of the troglobitic spider Porrhomma rosenhaueri in Ogof y Ci and Lesser Garth Cave, South Wales in 2017\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231313230\tThis dataset was collected to assess whether Porrhomma rosenhaueri was still present in the Ogof y Ci cave system and if so, to consider the viability of establishing a Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) protocol for monitoring the population within the cave; and to revisit the CSM established for the Lesser Garth Cave to collate further data on the condition of both the Porrhomma rosenhaueri population and the ecological condition of the cave.     The cave-dwelling spider Porrhomma rosenhaueri (L. Koch) is unique to the British fauna as it is considered to be the only species of paleotroglobitic spider present, and whilst the cavernicolous fauna of the UK has undoubtedly been under-recorded, sufficient work has occurred to suggest that Porrhomma rosenhaueri is genuinely rare.The spider has a very limited distribution in the UK and is only known from two sites in South Wales, Ogof y Ci near Merthyr Tydfil and Lesser Garth Cave near Cardiff.   The Ogof y Ci population was first discovered in 1971 but since this period no recorded survey work on the spider has taken place and the status of the spider within the cave was unknown. The Lesser Garth Cave population was discovered in 1979 during an investigation of the cave for the cave dwelling isopod Proasellus cavaticus. The cave has since been surveyed for the spider on a number of occasions, initially in 1997 as part of an environmental impact assessment, and more recently in 2009 in order to establish a Common Standards Monitoring (CSM) protocol for the longer-term monitoring of the spider population in the cave.\tSurveys were by visual searches. The survey of Ogof y Ci, along with other accessible sites within the Nant Glais Caves SSSI, was the primary objective of the survey work. Two site visits were carried out in September and October 2017 to assess biospeleology of the Nant Glais SSSI and to survey Ogof y Ci for Porrhomma rosenhaueri.   The spider was found to be still present within the Ogof y Ci cave complex, the first record since 1971, with a total of seven live spiders found during the site survey providing sufficient information to suggest a future monitoring protocol for the cave.     The repeat of the CSM protocol for the Lesser Garth Cave occurred during October 2017 and gave comparable numbers of spiders (15 adults and subadults, and up to 47 webs) and their distribution within the cave to the 2009 survey work suggesting a stable population within the cave. Overall, this survey work demonstrates that Porrhomma rosenhaueri is still present at both of its known locations, and that both cave sites support important cave invertebrate habitats within the UK.\t\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tData Custodian: Mike Howe Invertebrate Ecologist Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T09:32:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18451\tNRW_DS121121\t2024-05-28T12:49:35.095Z\t322\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status and distribution of the ground beetle Harpalus melancholicus at Stackpole Warren in 2017\tStatws a dosbarthiad y chwilen ddaear Harpalus melancholicus yng Nghwningar Ystagbwll yn 2017\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231313231\tStackpole Warren supports the only known British population of the Endangered ground beetle Harpalus melancholicus, the Stackpole Seed-eater. This data collection was commissioned to attempt to determine its current status and distribution on Stackpole Warren.    This survey has done much to characterise the habitat occupied by H. melancholicus, with adults recorded only from two small patches of habitat where the toe of a vegetated sand-dune encroaches over outcropping limestone. The survey has also confirmed that H. melancholicus is a seed-eater, with Lesser Hawkbit Leontodon saxatilis and probably thyme Thymus sp. as food items. It is hypothesised that H. melancholicus is ecologically highly specialised, with a patchy occurrence at Stackpole Warren, but that additional occupied habitat patches remain to be discovered. Alternative hypotheses are also presented.\tSurveys were by visual searches in daytime and at night. Diurnal and nocturnal ground-searching was carried out on 22nd to 23rd August 2017. Eight to ten individuals of H. melancholicus were observed, all at night. This is noteworthy for a species otherwise known at Stackpole Warren from only four individuals recorded between 1992 and 2015.\tTelfer, M.G. 2018. The status and distribution of the ground beetle Harpalus melancholicus at Stackpole Warren in 2017. NRW Evidence Report No: 247, 26 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database (Recorder 6)\tData Custodian: Mike Howe Invertebrate Ecologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T09:30:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18450\tNRW_DS121231\t2024-05-28T12:49:34.919Z\t785\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323331\tThe project created a toolbox of mapped data and methods which enable operational staff in Wales to identify potential locations for Working with Natural Processes (WWNP).    Data has been produced for each intervention covered by the project. The final outputs include the following datasets:    • Floodplain Woodland Planting Potential:   • Riparian Woodland Planting Potential   • Wider Catchment Woodland  • Floodplain Reconnection Potential   • Runoff Attenuation Features 3.3% AEP   • Runoff Attenuation Features 1% AEP  • Woodland Constraints\tFurther information on the Working with Natural Processes project, including a mapping user guide, can be found in the reports published here: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/working-with-natural-processes-to-reduce-flood-risk  JBA Consulting carried out the contract.\tSee individual metadata entries for information about each data layer and link to published location\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-03-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18448\tNRW_DS121238\t2024-05-28T12:49:34.832Z\t770\tValid\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Floodplain Reconnection Potential\tPotensial Ail-gysylltu Gorlifdiroedd WWNP - Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323338\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) Floodplain Reconnection Potential is our best estimate of locations where it may be possible to establish reconnection between a watercourse and its natural floodplain, especially during high flows. The dataset is designed to support signposting of areas where there is currently poor connectivity such that flood waters are constrained to the channel and flood waves may therefore propagate downstream rapidly.-Gweithio gyda Potensial Plannu Coetir ar Orlifdir Prosesau Naturiol (WWNP) yw ein hamcangyfrif gorau o leoliadau lle gall plannu coed ar y gorlifdir fod yn bosibl, ac yn effeithiol i wanhau llifogydd. Mae\u0027r set ddata wedi\u0027i chynllunio i ddangos ardaloedd o orlifdir nad ydynt eisoes yn goediog.  Mae\u0027r wybodaeth a ddarperir yn seiliedig yn bennaf ar ddata wedi\u0027i fodelu a data cyfyngiadau agored, ac felly mae\u0027n ddangosol yn hytrach na phenodol. Efallai bod gwaith adeiladu neu ddefnydd tir mwy diweddar yn y lleoliadau a nodwyd nag sy\u0027n cael ei nodi yn y data sydd ar gael. Mae\u0027n bwysig nodi nad yw perchnogaeth tir a newid i berygl llifogydd wedi cael eu hystyried, ac efallai y bydd angen modelu effeithiau unrhyw blannu sylweddol.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe dataset is based upon the Risk of Flooding from Rivers and Sea probability maps, and identifies areas of low and very low probability that are close to a watercourse, but which do not contain residential property or key services.   The areas may contain non-residential property so it is important to consider this and recent buildings or defences when considering floodplain reconnection. Locations identified may have more recent building or land use than available data indicates. It is important to note that land ownership and change to flood risk have not been considered, and it may be necessary to model the impacts of significant reconnection.    Further information on the Working with Natural Processes project, including a mapping user guide, can be found in the reports published here: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/working-with-natural-processes-to-reduce-flood-risk  JBA Consulting carried out the contract.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Floodplain Reconnection Potential .lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-03-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/510/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WWNP_FLOODPLAIN_WOODLAND_POTENTIAL\t2025-05-02T15:08:21.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18447\tNRW_DS121241\t2024-05-28T12:49:34.811Z\t860\tValid\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Floodplain Woodland Planting Potential\tPotensial Plannu Coetiroedd ar Orlifdir WWN - Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323431\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) Floodplain Woodland Planting Potential is our best estimate of locations where tree planting on the floodplain may be possible, and effective to attenuate flooding.   The dataset is designed to support signposting of areas of floodplain not already wooded. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) also has access to some Environment Agency data which we are licenced to have as part of our data exchange agreement.-Gweithio gyda Potensial Plannu Coetir ar Orlifdir Prosesau Naturiol (WWNP) yw ein hamcangyfrif gorau o leoliadau lle gall plannu coed ar y gorlifdir fod yn bosibl, ac yn effeithiol i wanhau llifogydd. Mae\u0027r set ddata wedi\u0027i chynllunio i ddangos ardaloedd o orlifdir nad ydynt eisoes yn goediog.  Mae\u0027r wybodaeth a ddarperir yn seiliedig yn bennaf ar ddata wedi\u0027i fodelu a data cyfyngiadau agored, ac felly mae\u0027n ddangosol yn hytrach na phenodol. Efallai bod gwaith adeiladu neu ddefnydd tir mwy diweddar yn y lleoliadau a nodwyd nag sy\u0027n cael ei nodi yn y data sydd ar gael. Mae\u0027n bwysig nodi nad yw perchnogaeth tir a newid i berygl llifogydd wedi cael eu hystyried, ac efallai y bydd angen modelu effeithiau unrhyw blannu sylweddol.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe dataset is based upon fluvial Flood Zone 2 of the Flood Map for Planning. A set of open access constraints data was used to erase areas which contained existing woodland, watercourses, peat, roads, rail and urban locations. The information provided is largely based on modelled data and open constraints data, and is therefore indicative rather than specific. Locations identified may have more recent building or land use than available data indicates.   It is important to note that land ownership and change to flood risk have not been considered, and it may be necessary to model the impacts of significant planting.  Further information on the Working with Natural Processes project, including a mapping user guide, can be found in the reports published here: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/working-with-natural-processes-to-reduce-flood-risk. JBA Consulting carried out the contract.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Floodplain Woodland Planting Potential.lyr X:\\Resource Management\\Flood Mitigation Woodland Opportunities\\EA Potential Floodplain Woodland.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Environment Agency (EA)\t\t2018-03-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/510/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WWNP_FLOODPLAIN_WOODLAND_POTENTIAL\t2025-03-07T16:51:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18446\tNRW_DS121294\t2024-05-28T12:49:34.763Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEcological Surveys of Welsh Lakes 2017\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323934\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) is repsonsible for the management and monitoring of the freshwater environment in Wales including protecting sites designated under UK and European legislation and enivronmental montoring for the Water Framework (WFD) and Nitrates Directive. This includes montioring of lakes. This project set out to collect, process and supply to NRW ecological and environmental data from a network of 16 lake sites across Wales, in support of NRW\u0027s integrated monitoring programme for protected sites (Special Area of Conservation and Sites of Special Scientific Interest), the Water Framework Directive, Nitrates Directive, Biodiversity Action Plans and other legislative and policy drivers. In particular, the surveys are aimed at informing management and restoration of protected sites and facilitating delivery of River Basin Management Plans.\tUsing standard methods, lakes were surveyed to assess the species and abundance of aquatic plants growing within and directly around the lake make to measure water clarity, dissolved oxygen and temperature within the lakes. The 16 lakes included: Hanmer Mere, Llynnau Mymbyr, Llyn Fach, Llyn Maelog, Llanbwchllyn Reservoir, Llyn Llygeirian, Bosherston Lily Ponds - Eastern Arm, Bosherston Lily Ponds - Central Arm, Bosterton Lily Ponds - Western Arm and Central Lake, Llyn Penrhyn, Llyn Llywenan, Llangorse Lake, Llyn Tegid / Bala Lake, Llwyn-On Reservoir, Llyn Mwyngill (Tal-y-Llyn Lake) and Llyn Gwynant.\t\tAsk Tristan Hatton Ellis, or see report as alot of the data is in there: Ecological Surveys of Welsh Lakes 2017 NRW Evidence Report No. 257\tTristan Hatton Ellis\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18502\tNRW_DS121295\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.259Z\t267\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2017\tArolwg Infertebrat Saproxylic o Ardal Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACA) Coetiroedd Dyffryn Gwy yn 2017\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323935\tThis dataset was collected to add to our current knowledge of the saproxylic invertebrate fauna found across the Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC). Despite the designation remarkably little is known about the saproxylic invertebrate fauna of the SAC. It is known to support significant populations of a number of nationally rare and threatened species, but until now only limited investigation of the fauna had been carried out.   The two outstanding species known from the SAC are the lime bark beetle Ernoporus tiliae and Cosnard\u0027s net-winged beetle Erotides cosnardi, both Species of Principal Importance in England but not included on Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2016.     These works will be the first phase of an intended larger body of work planned for the coming years aimed at producing a comprehensive inventory of invertebrate interest across the Wye Valley Woodlands. This inventory in the longer term will aid the process of drawing up management guidelines as a basis for the sustainable management of the woodlands within the Wye Valley Woodlands SAC and potentially the wider landscape.   These preliminary surveys have demonstrated the great potential interest of the Wye Valley Woodlands SAC for saproxylic invertebrates and provide ample justification for continued survey, as resources permit. They suggest that it is the extent of the woodland in the SAC that is a key factor, although individual areas may have more localised special interest for particular rare and threatened species. The importance of veteran trees is emphasised as well as the value of individual open-grown trees. These are significant as they suggest a modified approach to saproxylic conservation within the SAC.\tSurveys were by visual searches of key saproxyic habitats as well as sweeping by net and targeted beating.   The ultimate objective is to compile a provisional inventory of woodland saproxylic invertebrate fauna across the Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) that make up the Welsh side of the Wye Valley Woodland SAC. Where possible recording effort was focussed on the identification of any saproxylic species of national or local conservation importance.   Three of the SSSI\u0027s – Fiddlers Elbow; Pierce, Alcove and Piercefield Woods; and Blackcliff/Wyndcliff - were explored during autumn 2017 and notes made on the saproxylic fauna. Using hand-searching techniques, a total of 39 saproxylic species of invertebrate were found, as well as species associated with other habitats; seven of the species found have Nationally Scarce status. Some of these are best known in Wales from Monmouthshire: Diplocoelus fagi, Ernoporicus caucasicus and Platystomos albinus. Although the species lists are currently too small for analysis using the Saproxylic Quality Index (SQI), trial analysis suggests that the SQI values for the two larger sites - Pierce, Alcove and Piercefield Woods; and Blackcliff/Wyndcliff – are comparable with some of the best sites in the UK for representation of rarer saproxylic species.\t\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tData Custodian: Mike Howe\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:07:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18500\tNRW_DS121298\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.352Z\t287\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status and distribution of the ground beetle Leistus montanus in 2017 at its historic sites in Wales.\tStatws a dosbarthiad y chwilen ddaear Leistus montanus yn 2017 yn ei safleoedd hanesyddol yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231323938\tThe aim of the 2017 data collection was to determine the current status and distribution of Leistus montanus in Wales by visiting all known locations and searching under stones on scree slopes. Once regularly recorded in the montane areas of Scotland and the Lake District, recent records of the IUCN Endangered ground beetle Leistus montanus, a species associated with high altitude scree, are confined to the Langdale Pikes in 2008 and Skiddaw in 2016, both in Cumbria. In Wales, it was historically recorded on Cadair Idris in 1829 and 1968, from Y Lliwedd on Snowdon (Yr Wyddfa) in 1965 and 1968, and from Carnedd y Ddelw on the Carneddau in 1971. The only recent record was thought to be from Eglwyseg Rocks in 2007.\tSurveys were by visual searches and stone turning.  A total of 96 person hours between May and October located five adult beetles including two on Cadair Idris and three from Y Lliwedd. A single adult of Leistus spinibarbis was recorded on Eglwyseg Rocks, prompting an examination of the voucher specimen from 2007 which also proved to be L. spinibarbis. No beetles were found at Carnedd y Ddelw where the habitat appears unsuitable, with little or no scree, suggesting that the 1971 record may also be erroneous. L. montanus therefore appears to be restricted in Wales to Cadair Idris and Y Lliwedd, both of which support extensive scree beds, although surveys of other sites with suitable habitat would be needed to confirm this.\t\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tData Custodian: Mike Howe  Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T10:03:34.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18499\tNRW_DS121347\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.340Z\t532\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Development Boundary\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333437\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of the proposed windfarm development area as defined by the Developer. As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot supplied\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy Development Boundary.lyr\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:47:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18503\tNRW_DS121357\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.183Z\t389\tValid\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Riparian Woodland Potential\tPotensial Coetir ar Lannau Afon WWNP - Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333537\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) Riparian Woodland Potential is our best estimate of locations where tree planting may be possible on smaller floodplains close to flow pathways, and effective to attenuate flooding. The dataset is designed to support signposting of riparian areas not already wooded. The dataset is based upon a 50m buffer of available OS Open Data river networks.   A set of open access constraints data was used to erase areas which contained existing woodland, watercourses, peat, roads, rail and urban locations.-Gweithio gyda Potensial Coetir ar Lannau Afon Prosesau Naturiol (WWNP) yw ein hamcangyfrif gorau o leoliadau lle gall plannu coed fod yn bosib ar orlifdiroedd llai yn agos at lwybrau llif, ac yn effeithiol i wanhau llifogydd. Mae\u0027r set ddata wedi\u0027i chynllunio i gefnogi dangos ardaloedd ar lannau afonydd nad ydynt eisoes yn rhai coediog. Mae\u0027r set ddata yn seiliedig ar fyffer 50m o Ddata Agored OS sydd ar gael ar rwydweithiau afon. Defnyddiwyd set o ddata cyfyngiadau mynediad agored i ddileu ardaloedd oedd yn cynnwys coetiroedd presennol, cyrsiau dŵr, mawn, ffyrdd, rheilffyrdd a lleoliadau trefol.  Mae\u0027r wybodaeth a ddarperir yn seiliedig yn bennaf ar ddata agored, ac mae\u0027n arwyddol yn hytrach na phenodol. Efallai bod gwaith adeiladu neu ddefnydd tir mwy diweddar yn y lleoliadau a nodwyd nag sy’n cael ei nodi yn y data sydd ar gael. Mae\u0027n bwysig nodi nad yw perchnogaeth tir a newid i berygl llifogydd wedi cael eu hystyried, ac efallai y bydd angen modelu effeithiau unrhyw blannu sylweddol.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThe dataset is based upon a 50m buffer of available OS Open Data river networks. A set of open access constraints data was used to erase areas which contained existing woodland, watercourses, peat, roads, rail and urban locations.    The information provided is largely based on open data, and is indicative rather than specific. Locations identified may have more recent building or land use than available data indicates. It is important to note that land ownership and change to flood risk have not been considered, and it may be necessary to model the impacts of significant planting.    Further information on the Working with Natural Processes project, including a mapping user guide, can be found in the reports published here: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/working-with-natural-processes-to-reduce-flood-risk  JBA Consulting carried out the contract.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Riparian Woodland Planting Potential.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-03-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/509/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WWNP_RIPERIAN_WOODLAND_POTENTIAL\t2025-03-07T16:51:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18501\tNRW_DS121359\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.326Z\t371\tValid\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Woodland Constraints\tCyfyngiadau Coetiroedd WWNP - Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333539\tThis dataset comprises a range of land cover types where the woodland potential layers have been masked, owing to existing woodland, watercourses, peat, roads, rail and urban areas. Other constraints data, such as protected habitats and high grade agricultural land, should also be considered.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys amrywiaeth o fathau o diroedd lle mae haenau potensial y coetir wedi\u0027u cuddio, oherwydd coetiroedd presennol, cyrsiau dŵr, mawn, ffyrdd, rheilffyrdd ac ardaloedd trefol. Dylid ystyried data cyfyngiadau eraill, fel cynefinoedd a ddiogelir a thir amaethyddol gradd uchel hefyd.  Efallai bod newid defnydd tir mwy diweddar yn y lleoliadau a nodwyd nag oedd ar gael adeg cyhoeddi.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tLocations identified may have more recent land use change than available at the time of publication.   Further information on the Working with Natural Processes project, including a mapping user guide, can be found in the reports published here: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/working-with-natural-processes-to-reduce-flood-risk  JBA Consulting carried out the contract.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Natural Processes woodland.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-06-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3581/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wwnp_woodland_constraints\t2025-05-14T08:23:00.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18494\tNRW_DS121361\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.263Z\t381\tValid\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Wider Catchment Woodland Potential\tPotensial Coetir Dalgylch Ehangach WWNP - Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333631\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) Wider Catchment Woodland Potential is our best estimate of locations where there are slowly permeable soils, where scrub and tree planting may be most effective to increase infiltration and hydrological losses. The dataset is designed to support signposting of areas not already wooded.-Gweithio gyda Potensial Coetir Dalgylch Ehangach Prosesau Naturiol (WWNP) yw ein hamcangyfrif gorau o leoliadau lle mae priddoedd athraidd araf, lle gall plannu prysgwydd a choed fod yn fwyaf effeithiol er mwyn cynyddu ymdreiddiad a cholledion hydrolegol. Mae\u0027r set ddata wedi\u0027i chynllunio i ddangos ardaloedd nad ydynt eisoes yn goediog.  Efallai bod gwaith adeiladu neu ddefnydd tir mwy diweddar yn y lleoliadau a nodwyd nag sy\u0027n cael ei nodi yn y data sydd ar gael. Mae\u0027n bwysig nodi nad yw perchnogaeth tir a newid i berygl llifogydd wedi cael eu hystyried, ac efallai y bydd angen modelu effeithiau unrhyw blannu sylweddol.  Datganiad priodoli   Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn deillio\u0027n rhannol o Ddata Digidol BGS graddfa 1:50,000 o dan Rif Trwydded 2013/062. Cymdeithas Ddaearegol Prydain. ©NERC\tThe dataset is based upon the 1:50k BGS geology survey, and relies upon identifying drift and bedrock geologies that are characteristic of slowly permeable soils. A set of open access constraints data was used to erase areas which contained existing woodland, watercourses, peat, roads, rail and urban locations.      The information provided is largely based on a 100m gridded version of the BGS 1:50k superficial and bedrock data, along with open constraints data, and is indicative rather than specific. Locations identified may have more recent building or land use than available data indicates. It is important to note that land ownership and change to flood risk have not been considered, and it may be necessary to model the impacts of significant planting.      As indicated above, this dataset is based on the British Geological Survey (BGS): 50k Onshore Geology Data. This BGS data has squares missing where BGS have not surveyed the area. Where the BGS data did not exist at certain locations, we were unable to create the modelled WwNP data.   Any missing squares are an expected lack of data, rather than an error.  Further information on the Working with Natural Processes project, including a mapping user guide, can be found in the reports published here: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/working-with-natural-processes-to-reduce-flood-risk  JBA Consulting carried out the contract.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Wider Catchment Woodland Potential.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-03-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/508/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WWNP_WIDER_CATCHMENT_POTENTIAL\t2025-05-02T15:16:23.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18504\tNRW_DS121362\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.242Z\t545\tValid\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Runoff Attenuation Features 1% AEP\tNodweddion Arafu Dŵr Ffo 1% AEP WWNP - Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333632\tRunoff Attenuation Features Potential is our best estimate of locations of high flow accumulation across the land surface or in smaller channels, where it may be possible to temporarily store water and attenuate flooding during high flows. The dataset is designed to support signposting of areas where to target enhanced storage. It is based upon the Risk of Flooding from Surface Water datasets and identifies areas of high flow accumulations for the 1% Annual Exceedance Probability surface water maps. The areas of ponding or accumulation are between 100 and 5000 metres squared, and have been tagged where they fall on an area of slope steeper than 6% as gully blocking opportunities. All the potential areas have been constrained so that they are not in urban areas or on roads, rails or canals.-Potensial Nodweddion Arafu Dŵr Ffo yw ein hamcangyfrif gorau o leoliadau lle mae llif uchel yn cronni ar draws yr arwynebedd tir neu mewn sianelau llai, lle gall fod yn bosibl storio dŵr dros dro a gwanhau llifogydd yn ystod llifoedd uchel. Mae\u0027r set ddata wedi\u0027i chynllunio i ddangos ardaloedd lle gellir targedu storio gwell. Mae\u0027n seiliedig ar y setiau data Perygl Llifogydd o Ddŵr Arwyneb ac mae\u0027n nodi ardaloedd o grynoadau llif uchel ar gyfer y mapiau dŵr arwyneb Tebygolrwydd Gormodiant Blynyddol 1%.  Nid yw\u0027r data\u0027n darparu gwybodaeth am ddyluniad, a all fod angen ystyried materion fel draenio rhwng digwyddiadau llifogydd. Mae\u0027n bwysig nodi nad yw perchenogaeth tir a newid i berygl llifogydd wedi cael eu hystyried. Efallai bod gwaith adeiladu neu ddefnydd tir mwy diweddar yn y lleoliadau a nodwyd nag sydd ar gael.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe data does not does not provide information on design, which may need to consider issues such as drain-down between flood events. It is important to note that land ownership and change to flood risk have not been considered. Locations identified may have more recent building or land use than available.    Further information on the Working with Natural Processes project, including a mapping user guide, can be found in the reports published here: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/working-with-natural-processes-to-reduce-flood-risk  JBA Consulting carried out the contract.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Runoff Attenuation Features 1% AEP.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-03-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/507/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WWWP_RUNOFF_ANTEN_1PC\t2025-05-08T07:47:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18497\tNRW_DS121363\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.305Z\t595\tValid\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Runoff Attenuation Features 3.3% AEP\tNodweddion Arafu Dŵr Ffo 3.3% AEP WWNP - Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333633\tRunoff Attenuation Features Potential is our best estimate of locations of high flow accumulation across the land surface or in smaller channels, where it may be possible to temporarily store water and attenuate flooding during high flows.  The dataset is designed to support signposting of areas to target enhanced storage. It is based upon the Risk of Flooding from Surface Water datasets and identifies areas of high flow accumulations for the 3.3% Annual Exceedance Probability surface water maps. The areas of ponding or accumulation are between 100 and 5000 metres squared, and have been tagged where they fall on an area of slope steeper than 6% as gully blocking opportunities.   All the potential areas have been constrained so that they are not in urban areas or on roads, rails or canals.-Potensial Nodweddion Arafu Dŵr Ffo yw ein hamcangyfrif gorau o leoliadau lle mae llif uchel yn cronni ar draws yr arwynebedd tir neu mewn sianelau llai, lle gall fod yn bosibl storio dŵr dros dro a gwanhau llifogydd yn ystod llifoedd uchel. Mae\u0027r set ddata wedi\u0027i chynllunio i gefnogi dangos ardaloedd lle gellir targedu storio gwell. Mae\u0027n seiliedig ar y setiau data Perygl Llifogydd o Ddŵr Arwyneb ac mae\u0027n nodi ardaloedd o grynoadau llif uchel ar gyfer y mapiau dŵr arwyneb Tebygolrwydd Gormodiant Blynyddol 3.3%.  Nid yw\u0027r data\u0027n darparu gwybodaeth am ddyluniad, a all fod angen ystyried materion fel draenio rhwng digwyddiadau llifogydd. Mae\u0027n bwysig nodi nad yw perchenogaeth tir a newid i berygl llifogydd wedi cael eu hystyried. Efallai bod gwaith adeiladu neu ddefnydd tir mwy diweddar yn y lleoliadau a nodwyd nag sydd ar gael.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe data does not does not provide information on design, which may need to consider issues such as drain-down between flood events. It is important to note that land ownership and change to flood risk have not been considered. Locations identified may have more recent building or land use than available.    Further information on the Working with Natural Processes project, including a mapping user guide, can be found in the reports published here: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/working-with-natural-processes-to-reduce-flood-risk  JBA Consulting carried out the contract.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Runoff Attenuation Features 3% AEP.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-03-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/506/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_WWWP_RUNOFF_ANTEN_3PC\t2025-05-07T08:50:04.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18505\tNRW_DS121391\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.350Z\t367\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessing Sensitivity of Welsh Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs) Features to Unregulated and Partially Regulated Activities\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333931\tThis dataset is associated with the outputs from \u0027Assessing Sensitivity of Welsh Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Special Protection Areas (SPA) Features to Unregulated and Partially Regulated Activities\u0027 contract which is a pilot project part of a wider programme of work. The data outputs are a series of spreadsheets which accompany a report and are split to mirror the outputs of 3 Work Packages.  The data which constitutes these spreadsheets is composed of work by consultants ABPmer alongside existing data obtained from a Natural England led contract and the Marine Biological Association. WP1_Activity_Pressure matrix_070318: Contains definitions of activities used in the study, whether there is evidence to support a link between each activity and pressure, whether the pressures associated with an activity and a level that needs to be considered further, a review of the current pressure benchmarks and an assessment of the activity-pressure against the benchmarks to determine the likelihood of an activity exceeding the benchmark pressure. WP2_Benthic_Activity_Pressure_Feature_070318: Contains the pressure-feature relationship for each activity (for biotopes) and the accompanying sensitivity scores. WP2_Benthic_Sensitivity_Summary_070318: Gives the maximum sensitivity of the activity - feature (biotope) combination. WP2_Mobile_Activity_Pressure_Feature_070318: Contains the pressure-feature relationship for each activity (for mobile species) and the accompanying sensitivity scores. WP2_Mobile_Sensitivity_Summary_070318: Gives the maximum sensitivity of the activity - feature (mobile species) combination. WP3_All_Sites_Features_Max_Sensitivity_070318: Contains the sensitivity scores for each activity vs annex 1 habitat / mobile species (within each relevant Welsh SAC /SPA)\tThe approach to defining feature sensitivity to unregulated or partially regulated activities was divided into a number of key tasks:  - Definition of activities: A total of 41 unregulated or partially regulated activities have been considered within this project. - Establishing the evidence base for activity – pressure relationship: A fixed list of pressures has been derived within OSPAR to ensure consistency in the way pressures are assessed and their impact on biodiversity evaluated. A list of 39 pressures were considered as part of this project. - Assessing the suitability of the pressure benchmarks which underpin existing feature-pressure sensitivity assessments: The aim of this task was to assess whether the activities included in this study were likely to result in a particular pressure at the specified benchmark. - Assessing sensitivities of SAC and SPA features to the pressures arising from each activity; The next stage in the process involved determining which marine habitat and mobile features are considered sensitive to pressures that may occur at a level of concern from each activity. - Populating sensitivity matrices from Welsh Marine Protected Areas based on component designated features: The sensitivity information complied for each activity was the applied to the features within each of the Welsh Marine Protected Areas.\tThis contract was run by Natural Resources Wales and carried out by ABPmer (appointed consultants).  This contract is the first stage in a programme of work to assess and where necessary manage ‘unregulated’ or ‘partially unregulated’ activities where they impact on the condition of Welsh MPAs. This contract focusses on determining the sensitivities of features of SACs and SPAs to certain activities determined as ‘Unregulated’ or ‘Partially regulated’.\tDMS Evidence\u0026gt;Marine\u0026gt;Projects\u0026gt;Contracts\u0026gt;Assessing sensitivities of Welsh MPA features to unregulated activities\tInternal Data Custodian: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor -Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team   Metadata Contact: Data Services Team        Extra info from Laura Grant: There are a couple of reasons why we don’t want to let the public to get hold of this data at the moment. It is part of an interim project and it needs further work before publication.  If the spreadsheets were released now then they could be taken out of context.  Some of the spreadsheets contain data supplied by Natural England.  NE state ‘: We are currently exploring charging for certain advice on operations products, particularly where we have used contracts to conduct some of the assessments as a way of recouping some of this cost. With this in mind, it is very important that this information is not shared any wider than yourselves.  Some of the spreadsheets contain data supplied by MarLIN.  There is ongoing discussion as to who owns the data and MarLIN currently don’t want the information released in the form it appears in these spreadsheets.  DO NOT SET FOR EXPORT.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-03-16\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18498\tNRW_DS121392\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.327Z\t567\tValid\tEditable\tWoodland Grant Scheme\tCynllun Grant Coetir\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333932\tThe Woodland Grant Scheme (WGS) provided incentives for people to create and manage woodlands on sites all over Wales. The scheme was run in 3 phases with the last phase ending in 2006.   Woodland Grant Scheme 1 operated between April 1989 and April 1992.  Woodland Grant Scheme 2 operated between April 1992 and September 1994 Woodland Grant Scheme 3 operated between September 1994 and April 2006  Spatial data has been digitised against OS raster backdrops  by the former Forestry Commission wales.-Darparodd y Cynllun Grant Coetir (WGS) gymhellion i bobl greu a rheoli coetiroedd ar safleoedd ledled Cymru. Cynhaliwyd y cynllun mewn 3 cham gyda’r cam olaf yn dod i ben yn 2006. Mae’r cofnod hwn yn cwmpasu’r holl gynlluniau, mae metadata manwl ar gyfer data pob un o’r cynlluniau i’w weld mewn cofnodion ar wahân yng nghatalog Metadata CNC (metadata URI 100821, 100822, 100829).  Bu Cynllun Grant Coetir 1 ar waith rhwng Ebrill 1989 ac Ebrill 1992. Gweithredodd Cynllun Grant Coetir 2 rhwng Ebrill 1992 a Medi 1994. Roedd Cynllun Grant Coetir 3 ar waith rhwng Medi 1994 ac Ebrill 2006.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tSpatial data has been digitised against OS raster backdrops  by the former Forestry Commission wales. This metadata covers all of the schemes, detailed metadata for data for each of the schemes can be found in separate entries on the NRW Metadata catalogue (metadata URI 100821, 100822, 100829).\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 1.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 2.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Woodland Grant Scheme 3.lyr\tKeith Harri Jones- Spatial team Leader\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-04-01\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:WoodlandGrantScheme\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:WoodlandGrantScheme\t2025-03-04T08:46:29.431Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18496\tNRW_DS121398\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.252Z\t1138\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Coastal Flood Forecasting (UKCFF) Tide Gauge Network\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231333938\tThe UK Coastal Flood Forecasting (UKCFF) is a partnership formed by the Environment Agency (EA), Scottish Environment Protection Agency (SEPA) Department for Agriculture and Rivers Agency Northern Ireland (RAN) and Natural Resources Wales (NRW). Through the partnership the authorities commission and deliver the capability to monitoring and forecasting of coastal conditions that underpin coastal flood forecasting and flood risk modelling throughout the UK.   The network includes 43 gauges most of which are related through the national levelling network to Ordnance Datum Newlyn. The whole network is owned by the Environment Agency who operates the network on behalf of the UKCFF partners. Data are collected, processed and archived centrally to provide long time series of reliable and accurate sea levels. The British Oceanographic Data Centre (BODC) act as Data Archive Centre for this data.\tData from the UKCFF goes into WISKI7 Hydro and Telemetry. Although NRW receives data from several sites within the network, only one site goes into our central data store. This data relates to Mumbles Head only. Data is made available in near real-time with measurements reported every 15 minutes.  The measurements provide mean sea level within each 15-minute window and are reported relative to local datum (m) and relative to Ordnance Datum at Newlyn (mAOD).  BODC has special responsibility for the remote monitoring and retrieval of sea level data from the network. Daily checks are kept on the performance of the gauges and the data are downloaded weekly. These areas then routinely processed, and quality controlled prior to being made available for scientific use.   The data can also be accessed through the BODC website or through an Environment Agency API. Note that for convenience the website displays times in local time (i.e. BST during the summer) whereas the underlying data from the API is given in GMT.\t\tAlthough NRW is supplied the whole dataset, the Mumbles head data is the only data held in WISKI 7 Hydro and telemetry.\tAndy Wall Alison Hanson Liam Perrott, Technical Specialist Hydrometry       Not to publish to data.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Scottish Environment Protection Agency (SEPA)-Environment Agency (EA)-Department of Agriculture, Environment and Rural Affairs\t\t2018-03-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:56.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18491\tNRW_DS121508\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.935Z\t269\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive Cycle 2Target Catchments Rivers\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353038\tDataset shows the targeted waterbodies in cycle 2 (2015-2021) for the Water Framework Directive.  These are the waterbodies that NRW has committed to improve a WFD boundary class for this second reporting cycle.  The data is in 4 layers;  Target Catchments Rivers - NRW_DS121508 – this is the targeted river waterbodies catchments Target Waterbodies Groundwater - NRW_DS121509 – this is the targeted groundwater bodies Target Waterbodies Rivers - NRW_DS121510 – this is the targeted rivers as a line dataset Target Waterbodies Lakes - NRW_DS121511 – this is the targeted lakes waterbodies\tData is based on the cycle 2 WFD layers for rivers, lakes and groundwater – a subset of waterbodies were chosen by NRW to be targeted for improvement in the WFD 2nd cycle.\t\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Target Rivers Catchments Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tRhian Thomas, Water Framework Directive Team Leader, Water Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18495\tNRW_DS121509\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.255Z\t314\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive Cycle 2 - Target Waterbodies Groundwater\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353039\tDataset shows the targeted waterbodies in cycle 2 (2015-2021) for the Water Framework Directive.  These are the waterbodies that NRW has committed to improve a WFD boundary class for this second reporting cycle.  The data is in 4 layers; Target Catchments Rivers - NRW_DS121508 – this is the targeted river waterbodies catchments Target Waterbodies Groundwater - NRW_DS121509 – this is the targeted groundwater bodies Target Waterbodies Rivers - NRW_DS121510 – this is the targeted rivers as a line dataset Target Waterbodies Lakes - NRW_DS121511 – this is the targeted lakes wabter bodies\tData is based on the cycle 2 WFD layers for rivers, lakes and groundwater – a subset of waterbodies were chosen by NRW to be targeted for improvement in the WFD 2nd cycle.\t\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Target Groundwater Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tRhian Thomas, Water Framework Directive Team Leader, Water Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:05.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18493\tNRW_DS121510\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.037Z\t322\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive Cycle 2 - Target Waterbodies Rivers\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353130\tDataset shows the targeted waterbodies in cycle 2 (2015-2021) for the Water Framework Directive.  These are the waterbodies that NRW has committed to improve a WFD boundary class for this second reporting cycle.  The data is in 4 layers; Target Catchments Rivers - NRW_DS121508 – this is the targeted river waterbodies catchments Target Waterbodies Groundwater - NRW_DS121509 – this is the targeted groundwater bodies Target Waterbodies Rivers - NRW_DS121510 – this is the targeted rivers as a line dataset Target Waterbodies Lakes - NRW_DS121511 – this is the targeted lakes wabter bodies\tData is based on the cycle 2 WFD layers for rivers, lakes and groundwater – a subset of waterbodies were chosen by NRW to be targeted for improvement in the WFD 2nd cycle.\t\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Target River Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tRhian Thomas, Water Framework Directive Team Leader, Water Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18492\tNRW_DS121511\t2024-05-28T12:50:14.044Z\t250\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive - Cycle 2 Target Waterbodies Lakes\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353131\tDataset shows the targeted waterbodies in cycle 2 (2015-2021) for the Water Framework Directive.  These are the waterbodies that NRW has committed to improve a WFD boundary class for this second reporting cycle.  The data is in 4 layers; Target Catchments Rivers - NRW_DS121508 – this is the targeted river waterbodies catchments Target Waterbodies Groundwater - NRW_DS121509 – this is the targeted groundwater bodies Target Waterbodies Rivers - NRW_DS121510 – this is the targeted rivers as a line dataset Target Waterbodies Lakes - NRW_DS121511 – this is the targeted lakes wabter bodies\tData is based on the cycle 2 WFD layers for rivers, lakes and groundwater – a subset of waterbodies were chosen by NRW to be targeted for improvement in the WFD 2nd cycle.\t\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Target Lakes Waterbodies Cycle 2 2015.lyr\tRhian Thomas, Water Framework Directive Team Leader, Water Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18489\tNRW_DS121548\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.905Z\t545\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveys for Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch National Nature Reserve (NNR) / Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and the Afon Penrhos floodplain and for Geyer\u0027s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Cors Geirch NNR in 2017\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353438\tA survey was undertaken in October 2017 to determine the eastern distribution limits of Desmoulin\u0027s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on the Afon Penrhos floodplain following a previous survey covering the western part of the site in 2016. Virtually all potentially suitable habitat was surveyed. Although most new survey areas proved negative, an additional 0.5ha of V. moulinsiana habitat was identified, this being contiguous with the occupied habitat block lying to the immediate west surveyed in 2016. Areas of habitat not supporting the snail were judged to be too dry and/or too acidic to support the snail. It is calculated that the minimum total area of the Afon Penrhos floodplain occupied by V. moulinsiana is 11.07ha, with a possible maximum of 13.2ha if a number of adjoining but potentially suitable un-surveyed areas also support the snail. The Afon Penrhos population is not only the largest of three in Wales, but also probably one of the largest in mainland Britain. The significance of the population here is further enhanced by recent considerable population losses at several Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in southern England and on the nearby Corsydd Llyn SAC (Cors Geirch National Nature Reserve (NNR) / Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)). Now that the probable full extent of the population on Afon Penrhos is known, it is recommended that the site should be notified as a SSSI to reduce any risk of loss or damage due to changes in site management.\tSurveys were by visual searches and the collection of vegetation samples.\tWilling, M.J. 2018. Surveys for Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch NNR/SSSI and the Afon Penrhos floodplain \u0026amp; for Geyer’s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Cors Geirch NNR in 2017. NRW Evidence Report No. 258, 23pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tMetadata Contact: Data Services  Internal Custodian: Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:04:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18486\tNRW_DS121555\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.650Z\t10\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Natural Processes Constraints\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353535\tThis appears to be an unnecessary entry as there is no x drive layer called \"Natural Processes Constraints\".  Possibly it is a duplication of Woodland constraints dataset (NRW_DS121359) or Natural Processes woodland (NRW_DS121556)\t\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\\tVictoria Greest\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18490\tNRW_DS121556\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.928Z\t12\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWorking with Natural Processes (WWNP) in Wales: Natural Processes Woodlands\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353536\t\t\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Working with Natural Processes\\Natural Processes woodland.lyr\tVictoria Greest, Advisor, Flood Risk Strategy\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18487\tNRW_DS121564\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.658Z\t248\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFlood mitigation woodland opportunities for Dyfi, Rhonndda and Tawe Area Trails\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353634\tThis is a derived spatial dataset created under and Natural Resources Wales (NRW) contract by Forest Research. The creation of this dataset was ommissioned by NRW for three Area Trails, Dyfi, Rhonndda and Tawe, as part of the work related to the Sustainable Management of Natural Resources. This dataset outlines opportunities for woodland creation to help mitigate flooding in the three catchments; Dyfi, Rhonndda and Tawe.\tThe opportunity areas for woodland creation were identified from data associated with soils, geology, slope, aspect and existing vegetation cover, as well as areas of avoid based on specific sensitivities such as important existing habitats. Forest Research (the contractor) created the spatial derived dataset from number of third party datasets.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Flood Mitigation Woodland Opportunities\\Dyfi Catchment.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Flood Mitigation Woodland Opportunities\\Rhondda Catchment.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Flood Mitigation Woodland Opportunities\\Tawe Catchment.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Patrick Green (patrick.green@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk)  Metadata point of contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team  This dataset was done under contract and the external contractor was Forest Research: Huw Thomas huw.thomas1@forestry.gsi.gov.uk       Information from Huw Thomas from Forest Research the contractors of this dataset:  I’ve made some enquires and the only dataset which may present a problem would be the NSRI soils data and any dataset we created using the NSRI soil data so anything which selected areas on HOST class for example. In this case, Maps 16 to18. FR and FC are permitted to make use of any soil and soil derived data to meet the objectives of our agency so we can make the data visible via online map browsers and supply printed images but we cannot share the data unless the recipients have the same licence with the NSRI as we do (which the Environment Agency has). If NRW has the same licence agreements as the EA that will be fine also. 3rd parties can not have the spatial data unless they have a licence with NSRI or pay NSRI for the use of your data. However they can view the data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18488\tNRW_DS121565\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.906Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey data from the 2017 Cwm Idwal National Nature Reserve (NNR) Surveillance - vegetation response to grazing removal, 9th round of surveillance.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353635\tThis is the data behind the ninth surveillance undertaken to report on the effect of stock exclusion since 1999 in the Cwm Idwal National Nature Reserve (NNR). This surveillance of vegetation response is the first over a five yearly period and the fourth since the recording methodology was changed in 2006. During this surveillance round, both the revised methodology and some elements of the original methodology were used enabling comparison further back than 2006 and interpretation of the changes to date in terms of the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). Field data has been gathered in 2017 for 11 of the original (1999) 30 survey plots to feed into this ongoing surveillance program. The data gathered, records the frequency of plant species, selected species groups and other variables (bare ground, exposed peat, bare rock, litter, and sheep/goat droppings) from the five \u0027fixed\u0027 quadrats within each of the eleven surveillance plots covered by the 2006 baseline. The data gathered is primarily intended for use in comparing vegetation change with previous rounds of this surveillance program and is not intended for any other purpose. The data is in the form of an Excel workbook with sheets setting out: - plot locations within the NNR - quadrat locations within plots (5 quadrats per plot) - plot vegetation heights - individual quadrat species and other variable (e.g. bare ground, litter, droppings) presence. - individual quadrat, species\u0027 % cover values - individual cell recordings (twenty, 25x25cm cells in each quadrat) for species present. - Landscape photography location and framing information - Plot and quadrat photography location and framing information.\tThe data is primarily intended for use in comparing vegetation change with previous rounds of this repeat surveillance at Cwm Idwal, and is not intended for any other purpose.\tThe report associated with this data is:  A.J. Turner. 2017 Cwm Idwal Surveillance Report 2017. NRW Evidence Report No: 238, 118pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tData is held on the NRW DMS within the Protected Sites, Eryri SSSI location.  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landmanps1/psg/ersn/Monitoring%20%20Surveillance/Surveillance/Cwm%20Idwal%20-%20Post%20grazing%20exclusion%20surveillance/2017\tInternal Custodian: Philip Oliver, Environment Officer, Anglesey \u0026amp; Arfon Environment Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Philip Oliver, Environment Officer, Anglesey \u0026amp; Arfon Environment Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18482\tNRW_DS121566\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.235Z\t654\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCarmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Atlantic Salt Meadows Monitoring (2017)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353636\tThis dataset consists of an excel spreadsheet containing the sampling point data for Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Saltmarsh 2012 condition assessment survey. The survey covered saltmarsh in the Burry Inlet, Loughor and Three Rivers. Data recorded included saltmarsh plant species, % cover, negative indicators and sward heights. The GIS layer shows the location of the points referred to within the Excel spreadsheet.\tData was collected using Common Standards Monitoring as far as possible. All data was used for the following report \u0027Pauls, L. 2017. Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC Atlantic Salt Meadows Monitoring Report. NRW Evidence Report 222.\u0027\t\tGIS Data: Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Team_SouthWest\\Lily_Pauls\\Lily_Pauls.gdb\\SaltmarshSurveys\\all_saltmarsh_points2_text\tInternal custodian: Lily Pauls, Marine Team, SW Ops, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18485\tNRW_DS121588\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.391Z\t541\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status and distribution of the scarab beetles Rhysothorax rufa and Onthophagus nuchicornis on Welsh dunes in 2017\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353838\tThe Endangered Red Dune Crawler Rhysothorax rufa has only been recorded from a handful of dune systems in England and Wales, and has not been recorded in England for over fifteen years. In Wales, it is known from Kenfig Burrows, where a single adult was found on 20th July 1994, and from Merthyr Mawr Warren, where single adults were recorded in June 1981 and 21st May 2006. Adults feed on detritus in sand. A survey in 2017 failed to find the beetle at either location. Current management at both Kenfig Burrows and Merthyr Mawr Warren is considered sympathetic for the conservation of this species.  The Vulnerable Dillwyn\u0027s Dung Beetle Onthophagus nuchicornis was formerly widespread across England and Wales. It has declined dramatically in England and is now known from just five of 30 previous locations, where it is recorded infrequently and often as single individuals. In Wales, it is restricted to coastal sand dune systems and there are records from fifteen sites, from Merthyr Mawr Warren in the south-east to Morfa Harlech in the north-west. Larvae can be found in the dung of a range of mammals but are probably dependent upon that of large herbivores such as cattle, ponies and sheep. A survey in 2017 of the known sites, utilising both hand-searching and baited pitfall traps, found Onthophagus nuchicornis at seven localities - Kenfig Burrows (975 adults), Merthyr Mawr Warren (36), Morfa Harlech (1), Oxwich Burrows (4), Pembrey Burrows (32), Pennard Burrows (4) and Whiteford Burrows (5). The population on Kenfig Burrows is the strongest in the UK. Elsewhere, absence and low population levels are strongly correlated to cessation of grazing and the quantity and quality of available dung. Management, including grazing regimes, are considered sympathetic for the conservation of this species at three sites (Kenfig Burrows, Merthyr Mawr Warren and Whiteford Burrows), although there is room for improvement. Increased access for cattle to mobile dunes and disturbed areas of open sand would undoubtedly benefit Onthophagus nuchicornis. Management recommendations are made for all currently-occupied sites.\tSurveys were by visual searches and baited pitfall traps.\t\t[A]  Report is held in the DMS report Archive [B]  Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tInternal Custodian: Mike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-05-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:01:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18480\tNRW_DS121589\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.209Z\t235\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring Wetland Invertebrates on Rhos Goch National Nature Reserve, Radnorshire (2018)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353839\tThis work presents the findings of wetland invertebrate monitoring work undertaken in 2017 at Rhos Goch National Nature Reserve, Radnorshire. Previous surveys have identified the four wetland habitats of bare mud, ground layer, open water and wetland flora as key wetland invertebrate assemblage features. Previously in 2007, six monitoring stations were set up and timed searches undertaken using a range of collecting methods, with the aim of developing standardised future monitoring. Target wetland invertebrate species were then chosen for each key habitat. For the reserve to be in favourable condition for its wetland invertebrate assemblage, any monitoring would have to record at least 50% of the target taxa.   This work follows on from invertebrate monitoring undertaken in 2007 and 2012. A total of 438 records of 226 invertebrate species were recorded during the current 2017 monitoring. Of the 115 target species previously recorded, only 26 species (23%) were taken in 2017, marking a steady decline over the 10 year monitoring period (68 species [59%] in 2007; 37 species [32%] in 2012). This shows that all of the four key wetland habitat features are in unfavourable condition and the percentages of the target wetland species are continuing to decline.  It is recommended that the levels of grazing need to be increased to provide bare mud and open water habitat, and that scrub management should continue to keep succession in check.\tSurveys were by visual searches, sweep netting and water treading/sieving.\t\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tInternal Custodian: Mike Howe Invertebrate Ecologist  and  Rhys Jenkins   Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-08-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18481\tNRW_DS121590\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.212Z\t415\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) G East Felling Plan\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353930\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of felling proposed by the Developer for the wind farm development. As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tNot supplied\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA G East Felling Plan.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18484\tNRW_DS121591\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.377Z\t399\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA comparison of the dune slack invertebrate faunas of Newborough Warren, Morfa Dyffryn and Morfa Harlech (2015)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353931\tThis dataset are records from comparison surveys of invertebrate species from Newborough Warren where sand dune restoration occurred, and Morfa Dyffryn and Morfa Harlech where natural sand dune habitat occurs. Following management works on Newborough Warren National Nature Reserve in 2013 to create pioneer dune slacks and other early-successional habitats, invertebrate surveys had demonstrated a rapid dispersal/colonisation by seven of sixteen beetle species which Natural Resources Wales regards as requiring these conditions. Additional excavations in March 2015 provided an opportunity to find how quickly this colonisation occurred. Within two months, three pioneer dune slack beetles - Bembidion pallidipenne, Bledius subniger and Dyschirius politus - were recorded in pitfall traps set in this area, all of which had previously been difficult to locate on the dune system. As it was suspected that the colonising fauna of the excavated slacks had its origin in the estuaries to the north and south of Newborough Warren, surveys for Bledius beetles were undertaken in the Cefni and Braint estuaries by mapping the casts thrown up by their activities. Similar surveys were undertaken along the Afon Artro at Morfa Dyffryn and the Glaslyn and Dwyryd estuaries north of Morfa Harlech. It revealed that the Bledius colonies occurred in limited areas and seemed to be associated with sand newly deposited by wind or water and of a low organic content. Diatoms were found abundantly in the intestines of the Bledius beetles.  Pioneer slack habitat is at a premium on all Welsh dune systems other than Morfa Dyffryn where mobile dunes with drifting sand help to create pioneer slack as waves of sand move in front of the prevailing wind. This 2015 survey compared the invertebrate faunas of these naturally-occurring slacks with the artificially-engineered slacks on Newborough Warren. The opportunity was also taken to sample two slacks on Morfa Harlech, a well-vegetated slack on Morfa Dyffryn and two unexcavated slacks on Newborough Warren. The survey was made at 20 sites with five pitfall traps at each and the total data set consisted of 100,205 individual invertebrates spread across 505 species. The taxa used in this survey consisted of beetles, spiders, harvesters, millipedes, centipedes, woodlice and a few orthopteroid insects. This study has divided a previous definition of pioneer slack on Morfa Dyffryn into pioneer slack (damp, almost bare, yellow sand at the foot of an eroding dune) and post-pioneer slack (upwind of pioneer slacks and more vegetated, with bare sand having a black crust). Whilst there are biases with pitfall trapping and some limitations on drawing conclusions from analyses, pitfall data was examined by Principal Components Analysis (PCA) after splitting the data into 13 sub-sets based on taxonomic groupings. The ground cover of the vegetation was assessed in the three slacks sampled on Morfa Dyffryn to determine the differences between pioneer and post-pioneer slacks, with PCA separating them into two distinct clusters. The analysis also groups the excavated slacks on Newborough Warren close to the Morfa Dyffryn pioneer slack. This is particularly so where the data set is presented in the form of histograms of the numbers of selected species across the 20 sites.  An analysis of the sixteen beetle species regarded by NRW as being associated with pioneer slacks, using information from this study and other sources (published literature, species records in the NBN Atlas) suggests that several are often if not exclusively associated with the post-pioneer slack habitat. The pioneer slack beetle fauna is more properly restricted to four species - Bembidion pallidipenne, Bledius fergussoni, Bledius subniger and Dyschirius politus - which are also found in the estuarine habitat at the edge of the salt marsh.  A small survey of the tiger beetle Cicindela maritima found it breeding in numbers in the damp sand of the pioneer slack ...\tSurveys were by visual searches and pitfall traps.\t\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database\tInternal Custodian: Mike Howe Invertebrate Ecologist  Data Custodian: Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-05-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:01:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18477\tNRW_DS121593\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.075Z\t776\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) A Proposed Turbines\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353933\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of proposed turbine locations by the Developer for the windfarm development As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot supplied\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA A Proposed Turbines.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:19:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18483\tNRW_DS121594\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.243Z\t541\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) F Proposed Turbines\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353934\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of proposed turbine locations by the Developer for the windfarm development As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot supplied\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA F Proposed Turbines.lyr\tCustodians:  James Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:18:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18479\tNRW_DS121595\t2024-05-28T12:50:13.014Z\t858\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) G West Proposed Turbines\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353935\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of proposed turbine locations by the Developer for the windfarm development As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot supplied\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA G West Proposed Turbines.lyr\tCustodians: James Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:20:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18478\tNRW_DS121596\t2024-05-28T12:50:12.948Z\t600\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) G East Proposed Turbines\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353936\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of proposed turbine locations by the Developer for the wind farm development As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot supplied\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA G East Proposed Turbines.lyr\tCustodians: James Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:57.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18476\tNRW_DS121599\t2024-05-28T12:50:12.804Z\t1166\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Waste Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR)\tRheoliadau Trin Dŵr Gwastraff Trefol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231353939\t\u0027Urban waste water\u0027 is defined in the Urban Waster Water Treatment Regulations (UWWTR) as the mixture of domestic waste water from kitchens, bathrooms and toilets, the waste water from industries discharging to sewers, and rainwater run-off from roads and other impermeable surfaces such as roofs, pavements and roads draining to sewers. Urban waste water is often referred to as ‘sewage’. The UWWTR  is intended to protect the water environment for the animals and plants that live in and around water, as well as for recreational purposes and use as a resource for drinking water, sanitation, industry and commerce.   Under the UWWTR further sensitive areas have been identified because further treatment than secondary or equivalent treatment is necessary to meet other EC Directives such as the Water Framework Directive. Areas that come under as sensitive areas include Bathing Waters, Shellfish Waters and waters that are or soon to be Eutrophic.  This entry refers to a series of spatial datasets that can used in Urban Waste Water management.  In 2023 the dataset was updated and renamed from the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD). Please see the child metadata entries for more information on these updates.-Diffinnir \u0027dŵr gwastraff trefol\u0027 yn y Rheoliadau Trin Dŵr Gwastraff Trefol (RhTDGT) fel y cymysgedd o ddŵr gwastraff domestig o geginau, ystafelloedd ymolchi a thoiledau, y dŵr gwastraff o ddiwydiannau sy\u0027n gollwng i garthffosydd, a dŵr glaw ffo o ffyrdd ac arwynebau anhydraidd eraill fel toeau, palmentydd a ffyrdd sy\u0027n draenio i garthffosydd. Cyfeirir at ddŵr gwastraff trefol yn aml fel \u0027carthion\u0027. Bwriad y RhTGDT yw diogelu\u0027r amgylchedd dŵr ar gyfer yr anifeiliaid a\u0027r planhigion sy\u0027n byw mewn dŵr ac o\u0027i gwmpas, yn ogystal ag at ddibenion hamdden a\u0027r defnydd ohono fel adnodd ar gyfer dŵr yfed, glanweithdra, diwydiant a masnach. Mae\u0027r cofnod hwn yn cyfeirio at gyfres o setiau data gofodol y gellir eu defnyddio ym maes rheoli Dŵr Gwastraff Trefol.  O dan y RhTGDT, mae ardaloedd sensitif pellach wedi\u0027u nodi oherwydd bod angen triniaeth bellach na thriniaeth eilaidd neu gyfatebol i fodloni Cyfarwyddebau EC eraill megis y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr. Mae\u0027r ardaloedd sy\u0027n dod o dan ardaloedd sensitif yn cynnwys Dyfroedd Ymdrochi, Dyfroedd Pysgod Cregyn a dyfroedd sydd eisoes yn Ewtroffig neu a fydd yn Ewtroffig cyn bo hir.  Dim ond ardaloedd sydd wedi\u0027u dynodi gan Lywodraeth Cymru o dan Reoliadau\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr y mae Dyfroedd Pysgod Cregyn RhTGDT yn eu dangos.  Mae Dyfroedd Ymdrochi RhTGDT yn dangos safleoedd lle mae triniaeth fwy caeth na thriniaeth eilaidd yn angenrheidiol i fodloni gofynion y Gyfarwyddeb Dyfroedd Ymdrochi a dylai Defra eu dynodi\u0027n ardaloedd sensitif  Mae Dyfroedd Ewtroffig RhTGDT yn gyrff dŵr sydd (neu a allai’n fuan droi) yn ewtroffig a dylent gael eu dynodi\u0027n ardaloedd sensitif gan Defra. Mae hyn yn berthnasol i ddyfroedd croyw llonydd, afonydd, aberoedd a dyfroedd arfordirol.\tThe Directive was adopted on 21 May 1991. EU member states are required to implement the Directive through their national legislation.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\ X:\\EU Directives\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive\\Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive.lyr\tMark Squire, Team Leader Water Policy \u0026amp; Directives Implementation (mark.squire@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk)       Metadata contact: Mark Squire\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-08-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/5375/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_uwwtr\t2025-11-12T11:42:38.167Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18525\tNRW_DS121612\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.530Z\t634\tUnassessed\tEditable\tStatutory Plant Health Notices\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363132\tPhytophthora ramorum (P. ramorum) is a fungus-like pathogen called a water mould. It causes extensive damage and death to a wide range of trees and other plants. This spatial data is owned by Natural Resources Wales and shows the spatial extent of sites served with a Statutory Notice (destruction or containment) under the Plant Health (Forestry) Order 2005.\tThe boundary data may be based on OS data for physical features such as roads, rivers or derived from aerial photos.\t\tX:\\Incidents\\Forest Health\\Statutory Plant Health Notices.lyr   lyr   (this one is for people who just view the data – like a normal GIS user)  X:\\Editing\\Statutory Plant Health Notices EDIT.lyr   (this one is for people who can have access and edit the sites a bit like a working area… you cannot view the data in the editing folder if you cannot edit it (only the GIS team can view this data))\tInternal Data Custodian: Callum Stone       Data contains:  Personal Data Legal concerns This data contains information that may be deemed as personal sensitive information, ie legally binding Statutory Notices.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t2010-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T16:10:39.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18516\tNRW_DS121625\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.404Z\t868\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales Grassland Fungi Assessment 2017 - 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363235\tGrassland fungus data for Wales compiled, updated and checked by David Mitchel in 2017-18. Species names are standardised; records are assigned to sites, subsites and metasites with standard names; the diversity of species on sites, subsites and metasites is given for each grassland fungal genus, and for the whole \u0027CHEGD\u0027 assemblage.\tRecords include data from the British Mycological Society, NRW contract surveys, individual mycologists and the Local Environmental Records Centres, all of whom gave permission for the inclusion of their records in the database. Data are held in an Access database, with an Excel report allowing easier viewing of the data; there is also an explanatory Word document.\t\tEvidence \u0026gt; Species \u0026gt; Fungi \u0026gt; Fungus Habitats \u0026gt; Grassland Fungi assessment 2017-18 David Mitchel  DMS copy https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/fun/Fungus%20Habitats/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Ffun%2FFungus%20Habitats%2FGrassland%20Fungi%20assessment%202017%2D18%20David%20Mitchel\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000A00E94777D591649A9E40A217F4AC093\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Sam Bosanquet        • Third Party Data (records from the British Mycological Society and LERCs) • Data that is release could result in environmental harm  (species records that could result in a site owner destroying a potentially important grassland) • Personal Data (names of recorders)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-02-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18533\tNRW_DS121626\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.540Z\t546\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) B Felling Plan\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363236\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of felling proposed by the Developer for the wind farm development. As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tNot supplied\t\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA B Felling Plan.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:20:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18528\tNRW_DS121627\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.616Z\t391\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) B Proposed Turbines\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363237\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of proposed turbine locations by the Developer for the windfarm development As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot supplied\t\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA B Proposed Turbines.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:20:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18530\tNRW_DS121629\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.503Z\t525\tValid\tEditable\tSource Protection Zones (SPZ) Locations\tLleoliadau Parthau Gwarchod Tarddiad (SPZ)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363239\tSPZs are defined around large and public potable groundwater abstraction sites. The purpose of SPZs is to provide additional protection to safeguard drinking water quality through constraining the proximity of an activity that may impact upon a drinking water abstraction. This is part of an initial screening process in assessing impacts to groundwater resources.   Zones around location sites are defined by groundwater travel time to an abstraction. This is determined through applying groundwater flow models run at the location of abstractions, inputting parameters such as flow direction, geology type, rainfall and hydrological boundaries. SPZs provide a visual representation of the increased risks as you get closer to the abstraction.   The following subdivisions are defined within SPZs:   Zone 1: (Inner Protection Zone) - This zone is defined by a travel time of 50-days or less from any point within the zone at, or below, the water table. Additionally, the zone has as a minimum a 50-metre radius. It is based principally on biological decay criteria and is designed to protect against the transmission of toxic chemicals and water-borne disease.   Zone 2: (Outer Protection Zone) - This zone is defined by the 400-day travel time from a point below the water table. Additionally this zone has a minimum radius of 250 or 500 metres, depending on the size of the abstraction. The travel time is derived from consideration of the minimum time required to provide delay, dilution and attenuation of slowly degrading pollutants.   Zone 3: (Total catchment) - This zone is defined as the total area needed to support the abstraction or discharge from the protected groundwater source.   A further Zone 4, or ‘Zone of Special Interest’ was previously defined for some groundwater sources. These zones highlighted areas (mainly on non-aquifers) where known local conditions meant that potentially polluting activities could impact on a groundwater source even though the area is outside the normal catchment of that source. In future this zone will be incorporated into one of the other zones (1, 2 or 3), whichever is appropriate in the particular case.\tLarge potable groundwater abstractions are extracted from the National Abstraction Licence Database (NALD) and georeferenced as Source Protection Locations. A groundwater flow model for relevant aquifer is run and classified into zones defined by travel time. Individual Source Protection Zones are merged and dissolved by Zone number stripping out SPZ name. This is for National Security reasons.\t\tx:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SPZ Source Protection Zones Location.lyr\tDave Jones, Senior Specialist Advisor Geoscience\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t2017-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18529\tNRW_DS121633\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.538Z\t657\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMultibeam Acoustic Data Index\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363333\tThis is a spatial dataset detailing the location of multibeam data Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has access to. This was done as a collation exercise to produce a spatial (GIS) index of all multibeam data held by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The Index shows the loaction of surveys and points to where the raw data and/or processed data is held.\tThis spatial dataset was made by staff from Countryside Council for Wales.\t\tX:\\Physical\\Geological\\Marine\\Marine Acoustic Data\\Multibeam acoustic data index.lyr.   On CCW M drive it is stored as M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Hydrographic\\Marine_Acoustic_data\\multibeam_data\\acoustic_data_index.tab.It is also archived as  project 379, media 1390.\tDave Tavner Marine Mapping Scientist, Evidence Analysis  GIS and Spatial data team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-03-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:59:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18532\tNRW_DS121646\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.567Z\t233\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHydroelectric Power (HEP) - Sensitive Bryophytes, Lichens and Ferns\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363436\tSites considered to be sensitive to Hydroelectric Power (HEP) abstraction by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) because of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) bryophyte features, the presence of desiccation-sensitive bryophytes, the presence of rare or scarce riverine lichens, or the presence of Killarney Fern gametophyte. This layer was designed to aid consultation between Environment Agency Wales (EAW) and CCW.  As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\tData were collated by Sam Bosanquet (CCW Staff Member) from British Bryological Society, British Lichen Society and Botanical Society of Britain and Ireland records; boundaries were digitised roughly around sensitive areas, but entire Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI)  were included where appropriate.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Bryophyte_Lichen_HEP_Sensitive.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18519\tNRW_DS121648\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.335Z\t546\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Habitat Survey in Wales: Analysis for Area Statements 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363438\tData collation and indices derived from 704 River Habitat Survey sites in Wales surveyed 2007-8. This dataset comprises of an excel spreadsheet of basic attribute data plus calculated indices for Welsh statement areas including Habitat Modification score, Habitat Quality Assessment score and River Habitat Quality score, plus an interactive access database of above.\tData collation and analysis carried out by experts in River Habitat Survey (RHS) data collection and analysis at Bournemouth University and the River Restoration Centre.\t\tThe excel spreadsheet is saved on the DMS: Evidence \u0026gt; Freshwater Ecology \u0026gt; Area Based Statements \u0026gt; River Habitat Survey Contract for Area Based Statements https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/fe/Area%20Based%20Statements/River%20Habitat%20Survey%20Contract%20for%20Area%20Based%20Statements  The Access database is saved on Sue Hearn\u0027s desktop until such as time as it can be moved to the Azure File Storage Solution.\tInternal Data Custodian(s):  Internal: Sue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, Knowledge Strategy and Planning  Metadata Point of Contact: Internal: Sue Hearn, Rivers Ecologist, Knowledge Strategy and Planning\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2008-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18534\tNRW_DS121653\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.627Z\t1063\tUnassessed\tEditable\tElectricity Powerlines on Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Land\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363533\tThis dataset contains electricity powerlines on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any utility data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured electricity powerline information.\tThe information is currently gathered on site by contract managers who walk the site to ensure all electricity powerline information is available in contract mapper. Generally on screen capture from paper mapping, but some captured using GPS, validated by GIS Technicians and Operational Staff.  Information Warning: There is no guarantee of the completeness and accuracy of the data and therefore should not be used as the sole source of evidence.\t\tx:\\Utilities\\Electricity\\Electricity Powerlines on NRW Land.lyr\tGIS Technicians in 4 Forest Districts, National Forest Planning Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-09-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:53.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18524\tNRW_DS121654\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.485Z\t292\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGas Pipelines on Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Land\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363534\tThis dataset contains gas pipelines on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any utility data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured overhead telephone line information.\tThe information is currently gathered on site by contract managers who walk the site to ensure all gas pipeline information is available in contract mapper. Generally on screen capture from paper mapping, but some captured using GPS, validated by GIS Technicians and Operational Staff.  Information Warning: There is no guarantee of the completeness and accuracy of the data and therefore should not be used as the sole source of evidence for any work.\t\tx:\\Utilities\\Gas\\Gas Pipelines on NRW Land.lyr\tGIS Technicians in 4 Forest Districts, National Forest Planning Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-07-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:49.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18531\tNRW_DS121655\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.498Z\t342\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Mowing One Side\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363535\tWatercourses within the IDD that are flailed (Mowed) one side due to access restrictions and environmental reasons. Produced to aid the production of the works programme and to ensure watercourses are managed in agreement with stakeholders.\tData collected by operators out in the field by manually drawing the information on 1:10000 scale paper maps. The information was then manually digitised\t\tx:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Mowing One Side.lyr\tKeith Jones, Spatial Evidence Manager, Evidence Knowledge and Advice\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-07-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:53.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18527\tNRW_DS121656\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.541Z\t552\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels (CWL) Watercourses with overhead powerlines\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363536\tContains the watercourses within the Caldicot and Wentlooge Levels IDD that have overhead powerlines above or near them.\tData collected by operators out in the field by manually drawing the information on 1:10000 scale paper maps. The information was then manually digitised\t\tx:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot_Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Watercourses with OH Power lines.lyr\tKeith Jones, Spatial Evidence Manager, Evidence Knowledge and Advice\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-07-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:36.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18520\tNRW_DS121657\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.446Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMunitions Marine Licence Applications\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363537\tThe records contain coordinate data associated with current marine licence applications in Wales. This data allows the locations of licence applications to be mapped to assess their impacts on designated sites and combined effects with other proposals.\tNatural Resources Wales took over responsibility for issuing marine licences from the Welsh Government in 2013.  NRW do not hold all the accompanying documents for records pre 2013, created by Welsh Government.  This dataset has some information redacted due to National Security concerns.\t\tX:\\Editing  Restricted use\tShelley Vince, Senior Permitting Officer (shelly.vince@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk).  Custodianship believed to have moved to Joseph Thomas.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18535\tNRW_DS121668\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.632Z\t901\tValid\tEditable\tWINS Area Statement Opportunity Maps\tMapiau Cyfleoedd Datganiadau Ardal\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363638\tThe Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS Project (previously known as SCCAN), showed a range of national opportunities to manage natural resources more sustainably in the SoNaRR report. This current phase has looked at a more local level to provide input to the Area Statement process. The result is a comprehensive map atlas of opportunity, demand and constraint maps, with accompanying spatial data, to facilitate broad, cross-sectorial discussions on sustainable management of natural resources (SMNR).   SCCAN aims to promote more holistic management of natural resources through spatial mapping of opportunity areas for land management interventions. The mapping focusses on priorities of the Welsh Government Natural Resources Policy (2017) arising from the Environment (Wales) Act (2016), which puts an emphasis on taking a place-based approach and delivering nature-based solutions.   The five policy themes considered by the project: Ecological Resilience and Connectivity; Marine and Freshwater Water Quality; Natural Flood Management; Woodland Planting (for various objectives); Urban and Peri-Urban Green Infrastructure.\tThe maps and models have been developed using SCCAN/SENCE. They form part of the evidence base for the Area Statements and show where the best places are to undertake action, to enhance the environment, to promote well-being and support biodiversity net gain.   By using the maps together with other data and local knowledge good decisions can be reached at both the local area scale and the whole Wales scale on the Sustainable Management of Natural Resources (SMNR).   The maps show modelled opportunity spaces for a selected land management intervention; they are intended to be considered as a source of evidence rather than a definitive instruction as to the most appropriate land management choice in a particular location.   The maps should be used in conjunction with other existing sources of evidence (both spatial and non-spatial) and can be supplemented with new spatial data.   The maps have been produced on the strategic scale using national datasets to provide consistency between the maps for each Area. This means that before action is taken at a local scale the maps should be reviewed with stakeholders, and ground-truthed.   If more detailed, local-scale maps are needed then these can be produced following the methodology outlined in the technical report and generic framework, substituting or supplementing more detailed local data.\tThe opportunity maps can be viewed through the following link https://mapmanltd.maps.arcgis.com/apps/MapSeries/index.html?appid\u003df8741b82f4974486ae3ad0ddd8285692, which also includes a Web Mapping Service.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\\tRussell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-01-20\tpublication\thttps://data.envsys.co.uk/geoserver/ONS_habitats/wms?\thttps://smnr-nrw.hub.arcgis.com/apps/c7770d2881394c899123bae210afe370/explore\t2025-12-01T17:20:57.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18517\tNRW_DS121669\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.321Z\t274\tValid\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Agricultural Management Opportunities\tRheolaeth Naturiol o Lifogydd: Cyfleoedd ar gyfer Rheoli Amaethyddol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363639\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.    What the map shows:   The map shows areas where agricultural management measures could contribute positively to natural flood management. Measures considered are the planting or widening of existing hedgerows (all existing hedgerows are displayed in this map), as well as retention of winter stubbles. Hard engineering solutions are not considered in the map set for natural flood management.\tHow the map has been created:    The outer boundary of any parcel of land managed agriculturally has been considered as an opportunity for working with hedgerows, either the widening of existing ones or the planting of new ones, on condition that the land is judged to be biophysically able to support the growth of hedges and if the slope of the land is less than 11 degrees. The slope threshold acknowledges that at particularly steep slopes, an individual hedgerow will not have a noticeable effect on the speed and quantity of overland flow, and hence not deliver natural flood management benefits. If the area is known to be characterised by stone walls, this has been acknowledged as a sensitivity to hedgerow planting. Any area with arable land has been considered as an opportunity area for the retention of winter stubbles, if the area has a slope over 7 degrees; this is an approximate slope threshold for when the land will start to contribute noticeably to overland flow due to infiltration occurring too slowly. Any opportunities falling within 500m of rivers where considered to be priority opportunities.    This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations:  IACS data could be used to identify agricultural land more reliably and prioritise opportunities around grazing land managed with high stocking densities (addressing higher soil compaction and less infiltration) National Phase 1\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Agricultural Management Opportunities.lyr\tRussell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:10:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18523\tNRW_DS121670\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.503Z\t244\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Bog Restoration\tRheolaeth Naturiol o Lifogydd: Cyfleoedd ar gyfer Adfer Corsydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363730\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.  What the map shows: This map shows opportunities for bog restoration. The main opportunity for bog restoration is the restoration of existing bog habitat currently in degraded condition. Additionally, peat soils under coniferous plantations are highlighted, which can be a potential opportunity, particularly if the peat soil is in good condition. Existing bog habitat is shown for reference. Hard engineering solutions to flooding are not considered.\tHow the map has been created: Degraded and existing bogs have been identified from the national Phase 1 habitat map, whilst areas of conifers on peat have been taken from the afforested peat project carried out by NRW. Catchment zones (as mapped for this project) and the predicted viability of peat under a climate change scenario were considered practical constraints for opportunities to restore bog habitats for NFM benefits, while opportunities falling onto common land have been highlighted as requiring additional consideration.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: National Phase 1 dataset is likely out of date in many places.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Bog Restoration.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:09:09.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18518\tNRW_DS121672\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.358Z\t299\tValid\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Catchment Zones\tRheolaeth Naturiol o Lifogydd: Parthau Dalgylch\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363732\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.    What the map shows:  The map shows the area split into seven separate catchment zones, in which different types of NFM intervention can be used to achieve benefits.   The catchment zones are:  floodplains in valley bottom areas; floodplains in the mid-reaches; gentle and steeply sloping mid-reaches; floodplains in the headwaters regions, as well as gently and steeply sloping headwater regions.\tHow the map has been created:   For this map, the valley bottom floodplains have been defined as falling within flood zone 3 and having an elevation of less than 100m. The headwaters region has been defined as having an elevation of over 300m; the region is considered to be headwaters floodplain if the area also falls in flood zone 3; steep headwaters, if the slope is over 11°;  and as gentle headwaters, if it is less than 11°. The area that is neither valley bottom nor head waters region forms the mid-reaches, with areas falling into flood zone 3 forming the mid-reaches floodplains and slope of 11° again forming the threshold between gentle and steep mid-reaches.    This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations:  This dataset shows a rough approximation of catchment zones to help target NFM measures; through more detailed hydrological modelling, more detailed catchment zones could be derived.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Catchment Zones.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)   This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\thttps://smnr-nrw.hub.arcgis.com/apps/c7770d2881394c899123bae210afe370/explore\t2025-12-01T17:23:44.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18522\tNRW_DS121673\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.448Z\t277\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Enhancing Surface Roughness\tRheolaeth Naturiol o Lifogydd: Cyfleoedd ar gyfer Gwella Garwedd Arwyneb\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363733\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.  What the map shows: This map shows opportunities for enhancing surface roughness, for example by relaxing grazing pressure, restoring semi-natural habitat, increasing species diversity or through complete change in land use (e.g. woodland planting). Priority is given to areas that are well connected to the hydrological environment and/or currently have a low surface roughness. The opportunity spaces shown in the map are the areas that remain after the constraints have been applied. Habitats of high conservation value and high economic value have been considered a constraint, as in these areas it is unlikely to be possible or desirable to increase the surface roughness. The opportunity space on this map shows opportunities that remain after all constraints have been applied.\tHow the map has been created: The national Phase 1 habitat map was used to identify habitats where surface roughness could be enhanced through habitat restoration or where introducing a different type of habitat could enhance surface roughness. The network index, an output from the Scimap (Durham University, 2016) fine sediment risk model expressing how well connected an area of land is to the network of rivers and drainage channels, was used to identify areas where enhanced surface roughness would be disproportionally beneficial. Similarly, Phase 1 habitats have been used to approximate the current roughness of every piece of land. Opportunities were prioritised based on where the existing land cover class typically has low surface roughness, and/or the area was well connected to the wider water environment. Constraints for this map were based on the Phase 1 habitat map, showing where land of existing high value for biodiversity and land of high economic importance exist. Additionally, existing urban areas and areas within 500m of the coastline were excluded from the opportunity space, as measures taken this close to the coast are unlikely to deliver noticeable NFM benefits.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: National Phase 1 data is likely out of date in many places. Surface roughness could be derived from more direct measures than habitat scoring  Instead of using a coastal buffer, ideally all areas would be masked out that do not have any features downslope of them.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Enhancing Surface Roughness.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:04:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18521\tNRW_DS121674\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.405Z\t599\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Constraints for Enhancing Surface Roughness\tRheolaeth Naturiol o Lifogydd: Cyfyngiadau ar Wella Garwedd Arwyneb\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363734\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.  What the map shows: The maps shows the number of constraints to enhancing surface roughness for NFM.\tHow the map has been created: The following types of constraints were considered for enhancing surface roughness: Habitat constrain: Existing land cover that is suitable for conversion to another habitat type (high biodiversity-value habitats) or enhanced through restoration of degraded habitat. Coast proximity: Areas within 500m of the coast where considered too close to the coast for the area to have the potential to deliver NFM benefits. Existing urban areas (roads and buildings) are unsuitable for conversion to a habitat type with high surface roughness.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: National Phase 1 data is likely out of date in many places. Only limited inferences of habitat are possible by assessing Phase 1 habitat codes. Surface roughness could be derived from more direct measures than habitat scoring Instead of using a coastal buffer, ideally all areas would be masked out that do not have any urban features dowqnslope of them.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Constraints for Enhancing Surface Roughness.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:14:24.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18526\tNRW_DS121676\t2024-05-28T12:50:50.537Z\t251\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Catchment Woodland Planting\tRheolaeth Naturiol o Lifogydd: Cyfleoedd ar gyfer Plannu Coetir Dalgylch\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363736\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report.  What the map shows: This map shows the opportunity space for planting catchment woodland for NFM benefits. It is important to note that most effective use of these opportunity spaces would be to plant deep-rooting species, particularly broadleaved species, in order to fully realise the NFM potential.\tHow the map has been created: For all maps, the suitability for woodland planting has been established using ALC data split into different attributes, as well as the current land cover. At this stage all areas were identified where it is biophysically possible to plant trees.  The location of artificially drained land was modelled to identify areas where NFM measures, if implemented, could take a longer time period to reap benefits as compared to establishing woodland on land that does not have extensive under-drainage. Within the remaining opportunity space, sensitivities were identified. These are areas within which woodland planting is possible, but special considerations apply, such as for example the species composition in an acid sensitive catchment, and potential presence of high-biodiversity value Ffridd habitat.  Additionally, constraints were applied to the opportunity space; at this stage areas that are not suitable for planting trees are removed from the opportunity space. Several categories of constraint were considered, including biophysical, legislative and conservation constraints. Areas within which the planting of woodland is unlikely to deliver NFM benefits, e.g. areas within 500m of the coast, and areas on soil types that are not slowly permeable, are also excluded at this stage. The combined constraints for catchment woodland planting are shown in a separate map.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: National Phase 1 dataset is likely out of date in many places. Data on land drainage have been modelled, and is likely to over-estimate and under-estimate land drainage in different locations. Use of 500m coastal buffer to prevent coastal areas being mapped as opportunity space is a rough approximation of a complex problem. Some locations within this 500m buffer may in fact be suitable for woodland planting for mediating downslope flood risk.  The extent of potential Fridd habitat has been modelled using the Phase 1 habitat data. It is likely to over-estimate and under-estimate the Ffridd cover in different locations.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Catchment Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:10:17.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18515\tNRW_DS121677\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.814Z\t365\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Constraints for Catchment Woodland Planting\tRheolaeth Naturiol o Lifogydd: Cyfyngiadau ar Blannu Coetir Dalgylch\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363737\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report. This map shows the constraints preventing catchment woodland planting. The constraints map gives an indication of the number of reasons for not planting woodland for NFM in an area. The following types of constraints have been considered: biophysical, practical, preservation of exisiting urban areas, conservation, cultural, economic and legislative.\tThis dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\t-\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Constraints for Catchment Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:03:12.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18514\tNRW_DS121698\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.694Z\t463\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Riparian Woodland Planting\tRheolaeth Naturiol o Lifogydd: Cyfleoedd ar gyfer Plannu Coetir y Glannau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363938\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets.  What this map shows: This map shows the opportunity space for planting riparian woodland for NFM benefits. It is important to note that most effective use of these opportunity spaces would be to plant deep-rooting species, particularly broadleaved species, in order to fully realise the NFM potential.\tHow the map has been created: The suitability for woodland planting has been established using ALC data split into different attributes (soil depth, climate, stoniness, slope, drought and wetness), as well as the current land cover; these datasets were used to identify where it is biophysically possible to plant trees. To identify opportunities for new woodland adjacent to waterways, areas within 50m of rivers or 30m of drainage channels were considered; within this opportunity space, sensitivities were identified and constraints applied. The constraints to riparian planting are presented in a separate map and included areas within 500m of the coast, areas that are scheduled ancient monuments, and areas on climate change-resilient peat. The opportunity space for riparian woodland is less constrained than the opportunities for floodplain woodland or wider catchment woodland. This is because the scale of riparian planting is much smaller, and can often be achieved without changing the overall existing land use or character of an area. For example, it may be possible to carry out riparian planting on existing agricultural land, within certain designated sites (if the site is big enough and planting would be compatible with the site conservation objectives).  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: National Phase 1 dataset is likely out of date in many places. Taking the first 500m from the coastline out of the opportunity space is a rough approximation of a complex problem. Ideally, all areas would be taken out that do not have areas at risk of flooding downslope from them or are too close to the area at risk of flooding to have a noticeable effect on the extent of river swelling The extent of Fridd has been modelled; therefore, it can neither be guaranteed that all Fridd will be included, nor that all land found to likely contain Fridd actually contains Fridd of high biodiversity value.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Riparian Woodland Planting .lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:12:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18512\tNRW_DS121699\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.757Z\t291\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Constraints for Riparian Woodland Planting\tRheolaeth Naturiol o Lifogydd: Cyfyngiadau ar Blannu Coetir y Glannau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231363939\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. What the map shows: This map shows the combined constraints to planting riparian woodland for NFM benefits.\tThe constraints map gives an indication of the number of reasons for not planting woodland for NFM in an area. The following types of constraints for woodland planting have been considered: biophysical, practical, preservation of existing urban areas, conservation and cultural.\tThis dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Constraints for Riparian Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:08:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18513\tNRW_DS121700\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.760Z\t362\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Floodplain Woodland Planting\tRheolaeth Naturiol o Lifogydd: Cyfleoedd ar gyfer Plannu Coetir Gorlifdir\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373030\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report. What the map shows: This map shows the opportunity space for planting floodplain woodland to deliver NFM benefits. It is important to note that most effective use of these opportunity spaces would be to plant deep-rooting species, particularly broadleaved species, in order to fully realise NFM potential.\tHow the map has been created: The suitability for woodland planting has been established using ALC data split into different attributes (soil depth, climate, stoniness, slope, drought and wetness), as well as the current land cover; these datasets were used to identify where it is biophysically possible to plant trees. Only areas falling within floodzone 3 (proxy for floodplains) were considered. Areas likely to flood more frequently, i.e. those falling within floodzone 2, were considered as priority opportunities. Areas where it is not possible or desirable to plant floodplain woodland were identified (the constraints) and removed from the opportunity space. This included best and most versatile land (highest agricultural grades), designated sites, existing urban areas, and climate change-resilient peat. The floodplain woodland constraints are shown on a separate map. Within the remaining opportunity space, areas with sensitivities to woodland planting were identified. Sensitivities included grade 3a agricultural land (moderate agricultural grade), existing peat resource that is not expected to be resilient to climate change, acid sensitive catchments, scheduled ancient monuments, and common land.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: National Phase 1 dataset is likely out of date in many places. Taking the first 500m from the coastline out of the opportunity space is a rough approximation of a complex problem. Ideally, all areas would be taken out that do not have areas at risk of flooding downslope from them or are too close to the area at risk of flooding to have a noticeable effect on the extent of river swelling.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Floodplain Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:18:24.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18509\tNRW_DS121701\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.491Z\t368\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Constraints for Floodplain Woodland Planting\tNRW_DS121701\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373031\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.\tThis dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: National Phase 1 dataset is likely out of date in many places. Taking the first 500m from the coastline out of the opportunity space is a rough approximation of a complex problem. Ideally, all areas would be taken out that do not have areas at risk of flooding downslope from them or are too close to the area at risk of flooding to have a noticeable effect on the extent of river swelling.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Constraints for Floodplain Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:20:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18508\tNRW_DS121703\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.492Z\t258\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Opportunities for Combined Woodland Planting\tRheolaeth Naturiol o Lifogydd: Cyfleoedd ar gyfer Plannu Coetir Cyfunol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373033\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN)  to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.  What the map shows: This map shows all types of opportunities for woodland planting that are likely to deliver NFM benefits. The combined layer is based on the constrained input layers of three types of woodland planting: Riparian/Drainage channel shelter belts, floodplain woodland, woodland throughout the wider catchment.\tHow the map has been created: The map has been created by combining the opportunity spaces identified for riparian/drainage channel planting, floodplain woodland, and woodland in the wider catchment.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: All constraints listed for the input layers in their map specific sections apply.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Opportunities for Combined Woodland Planting.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:01:38.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18511\tNRW_DS121705\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.708Z\t520\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Demand for Natural Flood Management\tRheolaeth Naturiol o Lifogydd: Galw am Reolaeth Naturiol o Lifogydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373035\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. What the map shows: The map shows catchments feeding into urban areas at elevated risk from fluvial flooding; these are demand areas for natural flood management.\tHow the map has been created: The map is based on the F_MAX score in the Community Risk Register. Any areas with a score above 84 (the median of scores throughout Wales) has been considered as a demand area. Next, upslope areas feeding into the demand areas were identified: all Water Framework Directive river catchments feeding into the demand areas were identified and designated as the overall demand areas for catchment scale NFM interventions.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Demand for Natural Flood Management.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:06:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18510\tNRW_DS121710\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.507Z\t287\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Urban Green Infrastructure: Demand for Urban Greenspace - Noise Pollution\tSeilwaith Gwyrdd Trefol: Galw am Fannau Gwyrdd Trefol -Llygredd Sŵn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373130\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows demand areas for greenspace based on locations affected by noise pollution, with noise pollution in decibel sound level contours. Along a colour gradient from dark blue to dark brown the demand increases, based on a combination of three factors: the intensity of noise pollution problem, population density and deprivation.\tHow the map has been created: The map has been created using monitoring results reporting on noise in 5-decibel “A-weighted” contour bands for noise from roads and noise from industry. For each of these datasets, areas falling within the \u0026gt;\u003d75 dB class are considered to have the highest demand for greenspace to shelter from noise pollution, whilst areas falling within the 70-74.9 dB bracket are considered to have some demand. The two datasets were then combined, with any area falling into the “high” bracket for at least one of the two noise datasets being considered to overall have a high demand, and each area not having any “high” demand and “some” demand from at least one of the input dataset considered to overall have some demand. The remaining areas have no demand for noise management. This demand space has been combined with data on population density and WIMD to create an overall demand score for noise pollution measures. For the WIMD, the two most deprived classes within (Rank 1 to 382) were considered to have the highest priority, with ranks 383 to 573 having some priority. Any less deprived areas are not considered to be a priority based on deprivation. In a similar manner, population density taken from the latest census data has been split into 5 classes for Wales using Jenks natural breaks. The two densest populated classes are forming the highest priority, the medium class has some priority, and less densely populated areas are not considered a priority based on population density.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: Noise pollution caveats listed on DataMapWales apply to the input data, and hence also to the outputs based on it\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Urban Green Infrastructure\\SCCAN Demand for Urban Greenspace - Noise Pollution.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:05:34.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18507\tNRW_DS121712\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.286Z\t601\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Urban Green Infrastructure: Demand for Urban Greenspace - Air Pollution\tSeilwaith Gwyrdd Trefol: Galw am Fannau Gwyrdd Trefol - Llygredd Aer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373132\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.  What the map shows: The map shows demand areas for greenspace based on locations with air quality issues, with air quality being based on annual average PM2.5. Along a colour gradient from dark green to dark brown the demand increases, based on a combination of three factors: the intensity of air pollution problem, population density and deprivation.\tHow the map has been created: The map has been created using monitoring results reporting on PM2.5 throughout Wales as an annual average. Areas with PM2.5 greater than 8 µg/m3 were considered as having the worst air quality (e.g. highest demand), with areas at between 6 µg/m3 and 8 µg/m3 are considered to have a moderate demand; all areas with lower levels of air pollution are not considered as demand space. This demand space has been combined with data on population density and WIMD to create an overall demand score for air pollution measures. For the WIMD, the two most deprived classes within (Rank 1 to 382) where considered to have the highest priority, with ranks 383 to 573 having some priority. Any less deprived areas are not considered to be a priority based on deprivation. In a similar manner, population density taken from the latest census data has been split into 5 classes for Wales using Jenks natural breaks. The two densest populated classes are forming the highest priority, the medium class has some priority, and less densely populated areas are not considered a priority based on population density.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: Air quality data accessible for this project reported on annual averages only. However, guidelines on air quality also consider the number of times per year that a critical, health-relevant cut-off value is exceeded. The addition of exceedance values could extend the demand space.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Urban Green Infrastructure\\SCCAN Demand for Urban Greenspace - Air Pollution.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:15:48.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18506\tNRW_DS121713\t2024-05-28T12:50:49.243Z\t694\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Urban Green Infrastructure: Demand for Urban Greenspace - Urban Accessible Greenspace\tSeilwaith Gwyrdd Trefol: Galw am Fannau Gwyrdd Trefol - Mannau Gwyrdd Hygyrch Trefo\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373133\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows the catchment areas for accessible greenspaces, highlighting greenspaces that are likely to have a more ‘natural’ feel. It also shows urban areas that do not currently have access to greenspace (demand), and these have been prioritised according to the number of people and the overall deprivation in the area.\tHow the map has been created: All greenspaces were identified using MasterMap and OS Open Greenspace data. Phase 1 data was also used to identify beaches within MasterMap. Only polygons larger than 0.5ha were considered. For beaches, only polygons that intersected the Bathing Waters at Designated Beaches dataset were used, in order to identify beaches that are truly accessible.  Proxy access points to the greenspaces were then defined by intersecting the greenspaces with the ITN and PROW network. For beaches, the beach polygons were first buffered by 20m to identify potential access points. For each access point, a route network was calculated along available routes up to a distance of 400m using the ITN and PROW network, establishing the actual routes people could use to access the greenspace. Catchment areas were then produced based on creation of concave hulls, establishing the total area reachable within a 400m travel distance from the greenspace. SINC data collected by NRW from local authorities and the Green Flag Park dataset were used to identify catchments belonging to greenspaces that are likely to be in particularly good/natural condition.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: See GIS for Area Statements Map Atlas for further information\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Urban Green Infrastructure\\SCCAN Demand for Urban Greenspace - Urban Accessible Greenspace.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2019-06-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:13:14.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18546\tNRW_DS121714\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.530Z\t431\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Urban Green Infrastructure: Demand for Urban Greenspace - Risk of Pluvial Flooding\tSeilwaith Gwyrdd Trefol: - Galw am Fannau Gwyrdd Trefol - Perygl o Lifogydd Glawol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373134\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.  What the map shows: The two maps of pluvial flooding show:  [A] the risk of standing water accumulating during heavy rainfall on a bright colour ramp from green to purple, and areas at risk of strong overland flow on a darker colour ramp from green to purple, with purple areas being at highest risk in both cases. The separation into standing water and overland flow can help to target intervention – the dark purple area connected to a bright purple area shows where the water flooding the light purple area originates and, hence, where interventions could be located to disrupt flow paths. [B] the demand based on population density and the Welsh index of multiple deprivation on a gradient from light to dark brown, with darker areas having a higher demand. A purple semi-transparent overly indicates the areas at highest risk from the left-hand map to make the maps spatially comparable.\tHow the map has been created: In order to create the map, a DSM for the whole of Wales was derived from Nextmap DTM and MasterMap, with MasterMap features being assigned an average height, which was then added onto the DTM. The hydrology of the derived DSM was analysed using SciMap (Durham University, 2016). SciMap was originally designed to model erosion risk throughout the landscape. However, one of the intermediate outputs produced, the network index or connectivity, can be used to approximate urban flood risk, when using a DSM model instead of a DTM model as input. The network index expresses how likely a parcel of land is to pass material (in this case, water) on to another area. The areas with a particularly high network index are at the most risk from pluvial flooding. To separate between standing water and overland flow, slope information was used, with areas on slopes less than 3° being considered as at risk from standing water, whilst areas on steeper slopes are considered to be at risk from overland flow. To determine the demand, population density was considered, with the census data being split into 5 classes using Jenks Natural breaks; the highest two classes are high priority areas, the medium class a low priority area. Additionally, the Welsh Index of Multiple Deprivation was used, in accordance with classes used on wimd.wales.gov.uk. The 2 most deprived classes (rank 1 to 382) are high priority, the medium class (rank 383 to 573) low priority.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: For urban areas, once the separation of streets and buildings becomes highly relevant, a higher resolution than the 10m pixel sized used for the strategic scale mapping carried out for this project becomes desirable Urban drainage systems are not considered in this mapping; this could lead to some areas modelled to accumulate standing water actually being at lower risk than they are projected to be desirable. Urban drainage systems are not considered in this mapping; this could lead to over-estimation of the standing water risk in some ares.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Urban Green Infrastructure\\SCCAN Demand for Urban Greenspace - Risk of Pluvial Flooding.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:56:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18551\tNRW_DS121715\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.673Z\t229\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Woodland Planting: Opportunities for Woodland Planting for Carbon Sequestration\tPlannu Coetir: Cyfleoedd ar gyfer Plannu Coetiroedd ar gyfer Dal a Storio Carbon\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373135\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows areas where woodland could be planted and deliver carbon sequestration benefits.\tHow the map has been created: To create this map, biophysical suitability for woodland planting was identified using ALC attribute data. As carbon sequestration will be more rapid if trees grow faster, areas where the biophysical conditions are good enough to facilitate fast growth of trees were prioritised. Within this opportunity space, sensitivities such as acid sensitive catchments were identified. Constraints, e.g. areas where the planting of woodland is not suitable due to existing land use and legislation, were then applied to define the final opportunity space.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: National Phase 1 dataset is likely out of date in many places. The model does not consider ffridd areas as a sensitivity or constraint.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Opportunities for Woodland Planting for Carbon Sequestration.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:57:51.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18552\tNRW_DS121716\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.688Z\t270\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Woodland Planting: Constraints for Woodland Planting for Carbon Sequestration\tPlannu Coetir: Cyfyngiadau ar Blannu Coetiroedd ar gyfer Dal a Storio Carbon\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373136\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows the number of constraints an area has to woodland planting. The constraints map gives an indication for the number of reasons for not planting woodland in an area. The following types of constraints for woodland planting have been considered: biophysical, practical, preservation of existing urban areas, conservation, cultural, economic and legislative.\tThis dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Constraints for Woodland Planting for Carbon Sequestration.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:45:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18549\tNRW_DS121717\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.669Z\t563\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Woodland Planting: Opportunities for Woodland Planting for Rapid Turnover Forest\tPlannu Coetir: Cyfleoedd ar gyfer Plannu Coetiroedd ar gyfer Coedwig Trosiant Cyflym\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373137\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows areas where rapid turnover woodland could be planted for the biomass and timber industries.\tHow the map has been created: To create this map, biophysical suitability for woodland planting was identified using ALC attribute data. As this type of woodland is planted with the intention of being worked, shallower slopes (which make working the plantation easier), are considered priority opportunities. Areas at high elevations and/or on aspects that face into the prevailing wind direction are given a reduced priority, as the wood produced in such plantations can be of lesser quality due to stunted and uneven growth. Within the resulting opportunity space, sensitivities were identified (e.g. acid sensitive catchments). Constraints (e.g. areas where woodland planting is not suitable due to existing land use and legislation) were then applied.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: National Phase 1 dataset is likely out of date in many places. The model does not consider ffridd areas as a sensitivity or constraint.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Opportunities for Woodland Planting for Rapid Turnover Forest.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:51:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18555\tNRW_DS121718\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.927Z\t253\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Woodland Planting: Constraints for Woodland Planting for Rapid Turnover Forest\tPlannu Coetir: Cyfyngiadau ar Blannu Coetiroedd ar gyfer Coedwig Trosiant Cyflym\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373138\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows the number of constraints an area has to woodland planting to where rapid turnover woodland could be planted for the biomass and timber industries. The constraints map gives an indication for the number of reasons for not planting woodland in an area. The following types of constraints for woodland planting have been considered: biophysical, practical, preservation of existing urban areas, conservation, cultural, economic and legislative.\tThis dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: National Phase 1 dataset is likely out of date in many places.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Constraints for Woodland Planting for Rapid Turnover Forest.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:39:23.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18548\tNRW_DS121719\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.663Z\t383\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Woodland Planting: Opportunities for Community Woodlands\tPlannu Coetir: Cyfleoedd ar gyfer Coetiroedd Cymunedol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373139\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows planting opportunities for community woodlands, as well as already existing accessible and inaccessible woodlands within 2km of urban areas. Each of these three categories is separated into three demand classes: areas where many children are present; where the overall deprivation level is high; and where these two factors coincide, e.g. many children live in areas of high deprivation.\tHow the map has been created: The map has been created by identifying urban areas and identifying sites where many children live and/or where overall deprivation is high. Sites that allow for woodland planting have been identified following the methodology used for identifying opportunities for carbon sequestration. Out of this opportunity space, only opportunities covering an area of over 1ha and falling within 2 km of urban areas were retained. The remaining opportunities were categorised based on the demand type identified for the area.  Demand was determined as illustrated in the table below.  Additionally, areas of existing woodland falling within the urban perimeter were highlighted separately, and categorised as either accessible or not accessible. Woodland areas were selected from the Phase 1 dataset and classed as accessible if they had been mapped as such by the Woods for People project.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: Woodland accessibility is based completely on areas mapped by Wood for People polygon. National Phase 1 dataset is likely out of date in many areas. All limitations from the carbon sequestration may apply. The map does not identify sensitivities to woodland planting, such as common land; the opportunity areas with sensitivities are shown in the carbon sequestration map, which should be consulted alongside this map to identify sensitivities to woodland planting. Some processing was carried out in the shapefile format; in some cases, areas that do not touch in shapefile format may appear in the rasterised output as if they were touching.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Opportunities for Community Woodlands.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:41:30.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18547\tNRW_DS121720\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.570Z\t387\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Woodland Planting: Opportunities for Planting Large/Medium/Small Woodlands\tPlannu Coetir: Cyfleoedd ar gyfer Plannu Coetiroedd Mawr/Canolig/Bychain\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373230\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: This map shows areas that are suitable for woodland planting subdivided into woodland size categories, identifying opportunities to plant large, medium or small-scale woodlands, based on the number of land owners.\tHow the map has been created: The opportunity space for woodland planting was defined using the same methodology used to define opportunities for carbon sequestration. To incorporate information on the number of land owners, LPIS CRN codes were used. Connected areas of woodland opportunity space were classed as opportunity spaces for large (over 500ha), medium (between 5ha and 500ha) and small (less than 5ha) woodlands. Within each resulting area the number of individual CRNs were counted to give an approximation of the number of land owners that would need to be consulted to plant woodland on their land. The strength of the opportunity was scored based on the number of land owners that would need to be involved, and the likelihood of reaching agreement with increasing numbers of landowners.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: Phase 1 data is likely out of date in many areas.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Woodland Planting\\SCCAN Opportunities for Planting Large Medium \u0026amp; Small Woodlands.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:44:33.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18553\tNRW_DS121721\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.731Z\t869\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Water Quality: Opportunities for Reducing Faecal Coliform Pollution\tAnsawdd Dŵr: Cyfleoedd ar gyfer Lleihau Colifformau Ysgarthol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373231\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows areas where management action could be taken to reduce the risk for faecal coliforms entering into coastal and transitional waterbodies, as well as lakes and rivers. Management opportunities considered are fencing and/or hedges to prevent direct access to water courses by livestock, and woodland planting to form riparian or drainage channel shelter belts. For all opportunity types, those falling into upslope areas of sites with a known problem with faecal coliforms are considered priority opportunities.\tHow the map has been created: The map has been created by identifying the overall opportunity space for each management option under consideration, and then combining them into a single layer summarising the opportunities to reduce the risk of faecal coliforms entering the water column. The opportunities for the individual management types have been identified by the following process: Identify land likely to be used for grazing that is directly adjacent to water courses, including minor drainage channels. In these areas, there is an opportunity for fencing and/or planting of hedgerows to prevent direct access to the river by livestock. Within the land adjacent to watercourses identified above, identify land biophysically suitable for tree planting, and where there are no other constraints to woodland planting. In these areas, wooded shelter belts or riparian woodland can be established.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: National Phase 1 dataset is likely out of date in many places The model does not consider ground water The model does not consider urban areas or sewerage infrastructure as sources of faecal coliforms The model looks at intercepting the transport of faecal coliforms into waterbodies, but does not consider opportunities to reduce input of faecal coliforms at point source.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Opportunities for Reducing Faecal Coliform Pollution.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:52:56.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18550\tNRW_DS121722\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.690Z\t566\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Water Quality: Constraints for Reducing Faecal Coliform Pollution\tAnsawdd Dŵr: Cyfyngiadau ar Leihau Colifformau Ysgarthol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373232\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows constraints to intervention types for faecal coliforms entering the water environment. The number of constraints is combined across all land management intervention types, so an areas with one constraint for one type of action, and two constraints for another type of action will show as having three constraints.\tHow the map has been created: To create the constraints heatmap for faecal coliform management, the number of different constraints coinciding in any one area has been summed for each land management intervention type.  The individual constraints considered were: hedges/fencing, drainage channel shelter belts and riparian woodland planting.\tData gaps and limitations: National Phase 1 dataset is likely out of date in many places. The model does not consider groundwater The model does not consider urban areas of sewerage infrastructure as source of faecal coliforms. The model looks at intercepting the transport of faecal coliforms into waterbodies, but does not consider opportunities to reduce input of faecal coliforms at point source.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Constraints for Reducing Faecal Coliform Pollution.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:50:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18554\tNRW_DS121723\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.856Z\t276\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Water Quality: Opportunities for Reducing Sedimentation\tAnsawdd Dŵr: Cyfleoedd ar gyfer Lleihau Gwaddodi\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373233\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: The map shows areas where management action could be taken to reduce sedimentation in coastal and transitional waterbodies, as well as lakes and rivers. Management opportunities considered for reduction of sedimentation are habitat restoration or conversion in areas at high erosion risk; riparian buffer strips; hedgerow planting; cover crops or grass on tillage land\tHow the map has been created: The map has been created by identifying the overall opportunity space for each management option under consideration, and then combining them into a single layer summarising the opportunities to reduce sedimentation. After combining the opportunity spaces for the individual opportunity types, prioritisation was applied based on areas of demand. The individual opportunity layers were created by: Identifying areas with high erosion risk where habitat conversion or restoration could reduce the erodibility of the land; Identifying areas that are biophysically suitable for tree planting, not affected by any constraints, located within 50m of rivers and in an area of high erosion risk; identifying arable land at high erosion risk that does not already have hedges planted along field boundaries.; Identifying all arable land on steep slopes; For prioritisation, areas were identified that are located upslope of waterbodies that have “sediment” given as reason for not achieving good status in Water Framework Directive monitoring.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: National Phase 1 dataset is likely out of date in many places. The model does not consider point sources of sediment input; it looks at intercepting the transport of sediment into waterbodies\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Opportunities Reducing Sedimentation.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:47:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18544\tNRW_DS121724\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.240Z\t312\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Water Quality: Constraints for Reducing Sedimentation\tAnsawdd Dŵr: Cyfyngiadau ar Leihau Gwaddodi\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373234\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.  What the map shows: This map shows constraints to intervention types for reduction of sediment entering the water environment. The number of constraints is combined across all land management intervention types, so an area with one constraint for one type of action, and two constraints for another type of action will show as having three constraints.\tHow the map has been created: To create the constraints heatmap for sedimentation management, the number of different constraints coinciding in any one area has been summed for each land management intervention type; e.g. an area with two constraints to riparian planting and one constraint to hedgerow planting would show as having three constraints in total. The individual constraints considered were: Habitat restoration, riparian woodland planting, hedgerow planting and cover crops in Autumn/grass on tillage land.  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: National Phase 1 dataset is likely out of date in many places. The model does not consider point sources of sediment input; it looks at intercepting the transport of sediment into waterbodies.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Constraints for Reducing Sedimentation.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:46:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18545\tNRW_DS121725\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.211Z\t345\tValid\tEditable\tWINS Water Quality: Opportunities for Reducing Nutrient Enrichment\tAnsawdd Dŵr: Cyfleoedd ar gyfer Lleihau Gorfaethu\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373235\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Bases Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.    What the map shows:  The map shows areas where management action could be taken to reduce nutrient enrichment in coastal and transitional waterbodies, as well as lakes and rivers.   Management opportunities considered for reduction of nutrient enrichment are the restoration of land well-connected to the local water environment; hedgerow planting in proximity to agricultural land likely to be a diffuse source of nutrients; planting riparian woodland or wooded shelterbelts along drainage channels.   For each opportunity type, prioritisation is based the number of downslope waterbodies that are known to have issues with nutrient enrichment.\tHow the map has been created:  The map has been created by identifying the overall opportunity space for each land management option under consideration and then combining and prioritising them into a single layer summarising the opportunities to reduce nutrient enrichment.   The opportunities for the individual management types have been identified by:  Identifying land that currently does not have much potential to slow down overland flow and which could reach a better state to do so. Where this type of land is well connected to the water environment, an opportunity exists to weaken this connection through increasing surface roughness (thereby reducing overland flow), for example via habitat restoration or reducing grazing pressure.   Identifying arable land that is well connected to the water environment. These arable areas are opportunity sites for working with hedgerows. Identifying land that is biophysically suitable for tree planting, is not affected by any constraints, and located within proximity to either rivers or drainage channels as well as agricultural land. These are opportunity areas for riparian planting or the creation of wooded shelterbelts around drainage channels.   For prioritisation, coastal and transitional waterbodies, as well as rivers and lakes, with a nutrient enrichment problem were identified and their upslope areas calculated. Areas were prioritised according to the number of downstream waterbodies with known issues with nutrient inputs; areas feeding into multiple at-risk waterbodies were ranked a higher priority than areas feeding into a single waterbody.     This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations:  National Phase 1 dataset is likely out of date in many places.   The model does not consider ground water.   The model does not consider point sources of nutrients such as sewer overflows.   The model does not consider opportunities to reduce fertiliser input into agricultural fields\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Opportunities for Reducing Nutrient Enrichment .lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-19T10:49:32.433Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18543\tNRW_DS121726\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.232Z\t339\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Water Quality: Constraints for Reducing Nutrient Enrichment\tAnsawdd Dŵr: Cyfyngiadau ar Leihau Gorfaethu\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373236\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas.   What the map shows: This map shows constraints to intervention types for reduction of excess nutrients from diffuse sources entering the water environment. The number of constraints is combined across all land management intervention types, so an area with one constraint for one type of action, and two constraints for another type of action will show as having three constraints.\tHow the map has been created:  To create the constraints heatmap for sedimentation management, the number of different constraints coinciding in any one area has been summed for each land management intervention type; e.g. an area with two constraints to riparian planting and one constraint to hedgerow planting would show as having three constraints in total.   The individual constraints considered were:  habitat restoration/management change, hedgerow planting, drainage channel shelter belts and riparian woodland planting.    This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tData gaps and limitations: National Phase 1 dataset is likely out of date in many places. The model does not consider ground water. The model does not consider point sources of nutrients such as sewer overflows.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Water Quality\\SCCAN Constraints for Reducing Nutrient Enrichment.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk) This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-22T16:40:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18542\tNRW_DS121727\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.169Z\t641\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBullhead (Cottus gobio) population condition assessment for three Welsh SAC rivers: Afonydd Cleddau, River Usk \u0026amp; River Wye. Habitats Directive reporting cycle 3 2013 – 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373237\tBy-catch records of adult \u0026amp; juvenile bullheads from other fish surveys and invertebrate kick sampling sites\t\tEvidence Report No. 266\ttop copy of data held in BIOSYS  Data also stored on W drive (H Garrett)  Report stored on the DMS as Evidence Report No. 266 -  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/fm/Running%20Waters%20Habitats%20Directive%20Data%20Cycle%203/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Ffm%2FRunning%20Waters%20Habitats%20Directive%20Data%20Cycle%203%2FAll%20SAC%20rivers%20Bullhead\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000EE02D3CE2ED1AE4085983FEDF88CC2E5\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B90B85581%2DA33A%2D4F0D%2DB9D4%2DBCB28FC39C08%7D\tHeather Garrett (heather.garrett@cyfoenaturiolcymru.gov.uk) (main author of report) Paul Hyatt, Freshwater monitoring co-ordinator, Senior Monitoring Officer Paul Greest,  Technical Specialist Fisheries.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:13:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18541\tNRW_DS121728\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.138Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAbandoned coal mine discharges\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373238\tPoint location abandoned coal minewater discharges recorded by former Environement Agency Wales based on NGR. Recorded as excel data, converted to point shape files on GIS. Data is used to locate coal minewater discarges across Wales.  Data has not been updated for a number of years and won’t be up to date.   The data is generally for internal use only. Some of the data may have been provided by the coal authority.\t\t\tx:\\Industrial\\Mines\\Abandoned Coal Mine Discharge.lyr\tHaf Peskett, Technical Officer Groundwater and Contaminated Land, Geoscience / Knowledge Strategy and Planning\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:17:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18539\tNRW_DS121731\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.127Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironmental Impact Assessment\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373331\tAreas showing the extent of EIA forestry projects for which we have given our opinion.  The data takes the form of a polygon digitised by protected sites map.   The following attributes are recorded:  Property Name; Grid Reference; Project Type, Ref number. It was captured as a decision making tool, it shows where the site is and whether there are any adjacency issues which may affect our opinion.\tSource material is the map that is supplied by the customer with the Opinion enquiry form.  Attributes are taken off the opinion form.\t\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\EIA\\Environmental Impact Assessments.lyr\tLilley-Owen, Suzanne, Permitting Team Leader Forestry, Permitting Services (Forestry)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-08-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:58:38.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18540\tNRW_DS121783\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.124Z\t632\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCarmarthen Bay common scoter (Melanitta nigra) data from the winter 2016 - 2017\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231373833\tThis is data of common scoter locations collected by contractors APEM from aerial survey in the winter of 2016 to 2017. Carmarthen Bay Special Protection Area (SPA) was the first fully marine SPA in the UK; it includes areas of sea, coast and estuary that support a wide range of different marine habitats and wildlife. The SPA was designated in June 2003 solely for wintering common scoter (Melanitta nigra) and is the most important individual wintering site in Britain and Ireland for this species. To properly manage and report on the condition of the SPA, accurate population estimates are needed.\tTwo high resolution aerial digital surveys were conducted of Carmarthen Bay SPA, one in December 2016 and one in February 2017. The aerial survey design was based on an irregular grid with 2 cm Ground Sampling Distance (GSD) resolution, designed to deliver approximately 22% coverage of the SPA and a high statistical modelling precision. Up-to-date ‘procedural guidelines’ and site-specific ‘standard operating procedures’ were developed and adhered to during the aerial digital surveys. The target species was common scoter, but other species present in large numbers during both aerial digital surveys included large gulls, small gulls and waders. Several marine mammals (including porpoises and / or dolphin species and phocids) were also recorded.  Aerial digital survey results from December 2016 produced a population estimate of 35,211 (with lower and upper 95% confidence limits of 19,283 and 66,875 respectively). The February 2017 aerial digital survey results produced a population estimate of 36,314 (with lower and upper 95% confidence limits of 19,360 and 74,701 respectively). The February 2017 population estimate likely represents the peak abundance estimate for common scoter utilising Carmarthen Bay SPA during 2016/17. Common scoters were assessed to be in ‘Favourable Condition’ as the latest five-year mean peak far exceeded the SPA’s designated total of 16,946 individuals. As these aerial digital surveys covered the whole of Carmarthen Bay SPA, they do not only provide an estimated population size which is repeatable and comparable to previous/further surveys, but also the opportunity to analyse spatio-temporal distribution and trends.\t\tY:\\Marine\\Marine_Ornithology\\SPAs \u0026amp; SSSIs\\SPAs\\Camarthen bay SPA\\Camarthen Bay.gdb\tCustodian and metadata point of contact: Matthew Murphy, Senior Marine Ornithologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18537\tNRW_DS121810\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.051Z\t790\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS ONS Broad Habitats - Wales\tSYG Cynefinoedd Eang - Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383130\tThis map shows the broad habitats throughout Wales based on the Office of National Statistics broad habitats classes. It has been created as part of the project GIS for Area Statements (GISfAS) to support land cover ecosystem accounts by providing information on current economic value and change in land cover. GISfAS is part of a suite of projects also including SoNaRR (State of National Resources Reporting) and SAfAS (Spatial Analysis for Area Statements), all of which are facilitating management of natural resources and ecosystem services within Wales.\tThe map was created using remote sensing methodology combined with knowledge of the previous field classification; only the rare habitats have been systematically checked. Because of the methods used some classes of habitats have higher certainty than others. Due to time constraints no attempt was made to take accuracy and certainty into account when translating the Phase 1 classes into Broad Habitats.\tData Limitations The Phase 1 habitat map of Wales is based on data which are ten years old  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by Environment Systems Ltd.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\ONS Broad habitats\\ONS Broad habitats.lyr   ONS Broad Habitat report and statistics spreadsheet stored on the DMS. (https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/nrm/enpb/Project%20Charters/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fadvice%2Fnrm%2Fenpb%2FProject%20Charters%2FA14%20%2D%20Natural%20Resource%20Evidence%20Framework%2FSCCAN%2FONS%20Broad%20Habitats\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000267983946555B041947C4D4E8269E2AF\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BB4208ECF%2D3BE5%2D4868%2DAA0B%2DE40E8633D58A%7D)\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T10:17:10.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17302\tNRW_DS12182\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.399Z\t580\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWind Energy Strategic Search Areas (SSA) G West Felling Plan\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3132313832\tThe captured data defines the spatial area of felling proposed by the developer for the wind farm development  As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot supplied\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Wind Energy SSA G West Felling Plan.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18538\tNRW_DS121844\t2024-05-28T12:51:15.060Z\t498\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe current status and distribution of the beetles Anthonomus conspersus, Aulacobaris lepidii and Thinobius newberyi on selected Welsh Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383434\tThe three species selected for this contract are all Individually Qualifying Features on their respective SSSIs, yet they had not been recorded on those SSSIs for at least twenty years. There is no evidence that Anthonomus conspersus or Aulacobaris lepidii had been looked for in the intervening period and although there was some survey effort in 2004 which could have yielded records of Thinobius newberyi, this is a difficult species to find. With habitat conditions for all three ostensibly remaining suitable there was no reason to suppose that any of their populations had become extinct.  Over such a long period, however, confirmation of their continued presence is clearly desirable.  The rowan weevil A. conspersus was found to be strongly established at Coed Boch y rhaeadr in Migneint-Arenig-Dduallt SSSI, with adults recorded throughout the wood and on the adjacent ffridd. Historically the area was probably managed as pasture woodland and numerous ancient rowan trees are scattered throughout the more densely wooded parts of the site, as well as the open hillside. A detailed survey of the tree resource was not possible in the time available, but a rough estimate suggests that there are more than 300 trees present. A map of the denser concentrations is provided. For at least 20 years grazing stock has been fenced out of the wood as part of an agri-environment scheme and this has led to an increase in birch scrub on the lower slopes. At present there are sufficient clearings to provide suitable conditions for A. conspersus but consideration should be given to some management to retain these open spaces in the wood.  The yellow crucifer weevil A. lepidii was recorded twice on the Dee Meanders in Afon Dyfrdwy (River Dee) SSSI in the 1990s but has not been seen since. Five sites were selected for survey from aerial photographs where exposed sand and gravel was obvious on the riverbanks, as A. lepidii breeds in the roots of crucifers growing in sandy sediments. A further two sites were visited that had been identified as containing potential habitat during a previous survey. The species was not found during any of these surveys and vegetational succession had taken place at both of the recommended sites such that little or no exposed sand was seen. Hostplants of the weevil were generally scarce on the remaining sites and at four of the five trampling by livestock was noticeable, with the hostplant crucifers being heavily grazed. It is probable that A. lepidii still occurs along this stretch of river but the disturbance to the shingle bars is a concern and consideration should be given to fencing some of them to give protection to the shingle invertebrate fauna.  The shingle rove beetle T. newberyi is a feature of Gro Ty\u0027n yr Helyg SSSI on the Ystwyth and Rheidol Shingles \u0026amp; Backwaters SSSI on the Rheidol. At the original site on the Afon Ystwyth, Gro Ty’n-yr-helyg, the river has altered course to remove most of the former bar. Suitable sediment is deposited further downstream, but this is much smaller in extent than previously. On the Afon Rheidol, the original site of records in the 1990s is no longer suitable as dense willow carr has become established over the whole area. Efforts to reach other shingle bars in the floodplain were frustrated by deep water or dense scrub and the only bar investigated, at Abercwmdolau, had a high silt load in the sediment, whereas clean sand or fine gravel are thought to be required by the species. Hand-searching and excavation on both rivers failed to discover any examples of the target species, but this is a very difficult species to find and its absence from this survey should not be taken as evidence of extinction. The current state of the Rheidol is worrying but future flood events may rework sediments to create new bars. On the Ystywth, potential sites up and downstream from Ty’n-yr-helyg should be investigated to assess their potential for shingle invertebrates.\tSurvey techniques for the three beetle species involved visual searches, hand-searches and substrate excavations.\t\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive Data is stored in Welsh Invertebrate Database - Recorder 6\tCustodian: Dr. Mike Howe - Invertebrate Ecologist  Contractor: Adrain Fowles, his home address is the same as his business address - contact Mike Howe for more information\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-11-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:17:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18536\tNRW_DS121852\t2024-05-28T12:51:14.789Z\t1325\tValid\tEditable\tNational Coastal Erosion Risk Management (NCERM)\tRheoli Risg Erydiad Arfordirol Cenedlaethol (NCERM)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383532\tThe National Coastal Erosion Risk Management (NCERM) map shows the spatial NCERM coastal baseline. This baseline is split to \u0027frontages\u0027. These are defined as lengths of coast with consistent characteristics based on the cliff behaviour characteristics and the defence characteristics. It is intended as an up-to-date and reliable benchmark dataset showing erosion extents and rates for three periods from a base date of 2005:   - Short Term (0 - 20yr i.e. 2005 to 2025);  - Medium Term (20 - 50yr i.e. 2025 to 2055); and  - Long Term (50 - 100yr i.e. 2055 to 2105).    The data extents are based on 50%ile confidence levels, whilst the data attributes highlight the 5-95%ile confidence range, of erosion predictions for the following two scenarios (NB All distances are cumulative over time and given in metres):  - No Active Intervention Policy Scenario; and  - With the implementation of Shoreline Management Plan 2 Policies.    Defence type and SMP policies for each of the three periods described above are included.-Mae\u0027r map Rheoli Risg Erydiad Arfordirol Cenedlaethol (NCERM) yn dangos gwaelodlin arfordirol ofodol NCERM. Mae\u0027r waelodlin hon wedi\u0027i rhannu’n ‘ffryntiadau’. Diffinnir y rhain fel darnau o arfordir sydd â nodweddion cyson yn seiliedig ar nodweddion ymddygiad clogwyni a\u0027r nodweddion amddiffyn. Fe\u0027i bwriedir fel set ddata meincnodi gyfredol a dibynadwy sy\u0027n dangos graddfa a chyfraddau erydiad ar gyfer tri chyfnod o un dyddiad sylfaenol, sef 2005:  Tymor Byr (0 – 20 mlynedd h.y. 2005 hyd 2025); Tymor Canolig (20 – 50 mlynedd h.y. 2025 hyd 2055); a Tymor Hir (50 – 100 mlynedd h.y. 2055 hyd 2105). Mae’r data ar raddfa’r erydiad yn seiliedig ar lefelau hyder o 50 canradd, ac mae gwerthoedd ar gael ar lefelau hyder o 5-95 canradd ar gyfer rhagfynegiadau erydiad yn y ddau senario isod (Noder – mae’r holl bellterau’n gronnus dros amser ac wedi’u nodi mewn metrau).  Heb unrhyw bolisi ymyrraeth weithredol; a Gan weithredu Polisïau Cynllun Rheoli Traethlin 2. Mae polisïau math Amddiffyn a CRhT ar gyfer pob un o\u0027r tri chyfnod a ddisgrifir uchod wedi\u0027u cynnwys.  Mae\u0027r data a\u0027r wybodaeth gysylltiedig wedi\u0027u bwriadu at ddibenion cyfarwyddyd - ni all y rhain ddarparu manylion ar gyfer eiddo unigol. Mae gwybodaethNCERM yn ystyried y prif risg yn yr arfordir, er bod prosesau llifogydd ac erydiad yn aml yn gysylltiedig, ac nid yw data ar erydu nodweddion y blaendraeth yn cael eu cynnwys yn gyffredinol.  Mae\u0027r data yn disgrifio\u0027r amcangyfrifon uchaf ac isaf o risg erydu mewn lleoliad penodol y disgwylir i leoliad gwirioneddol yr arfordir orwedd ynddo. Nid yw\u0027r data yn amcangyfrif lleoliad absoliwt arfordir y dyfodol. Yn gyffredinol, nid yw manylion ardaloedd daearegol cymhleth, a elwir yn \"glogwyni cymhleth\", wedi\u0027u cynnwys yn gyffredinol yn y set ddata oherwydd yr ansicrwydd cynhenid sy\u0027n gysylltiedig â rhagfynegi amseriad a maint yr erydiad yn y lleoliadau hyn.\tThe data and associated information is intended for guidance purposes - it cannot provide details for individual properties. The NCERM information considers the predominant risk at the coast, although flooding and erosion processes are often linked, and data on erosion of foreshore features are, in general, not included.    The data describes the upper and lower estimates of erosion risk at a particular location, within which the actual location of the coastline is expected to lie.  The data does not estimate the absolute location of the future coastline.  Details of geologically complex areas, known as \u0027complex cliffs\u0027 are, in general, not included within the dataset due to the inherent uncertainties associated with predicting the timing and extent of erosion at these locations.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Mgt Plan .....lyr  (There are 4 layers) see below  X:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Mgt Plan Gt Orme to England.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Mgt Plan Severn.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Mgt Plan Swansea Carmarthen Bay.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Erosion Risk\\Shoreline Mgt Plan West Wales.lyr\tLouise Pennington, Senior Specialist, Strategic Planning and Investment, Flood Risk Strategy, Sustainable Communities Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-06-29\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/4142/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_ncerm\t2025-11-12T11:44:26.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18575\tNRW_DS121868\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.665Z\t426\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCoastal Flood Boundaries (CFB) Gauge, Surge, Swell data UK Datasets 2011\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383638\tThis GIS dataset and supporting information providing design / extreme sea level and typical surge information around the coastline of the UK under present day conditions. The data within Wales is owned by Natural Resources Wales, England\u0027s data is owned by Environment Agency, and the Scottish data is owned by SEPA.   This dataset has now been superseded by NRW_DS123552, Coastal Flood Boundaries (CFB) publication 2018.\tNRW has access to UK wide data for: - Gauges - Surge Shapes - Swell Waves\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB confidence intervals.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Estuary boundaries.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB extreme sea levels.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Gauge data UK.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Surge shapes.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Surge shapes UK.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Swell waves.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Modelling and Mapping\\CFB 2011\\CFB Swell waves UK.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Environment Agency (EA)-Scottish Environmental Protection Agency (SEPA)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:44.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18570\tNRW_DS121888\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.442Z\t568\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRegulatory Woodland Officer Areas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231383838\tMap of Wales split into 6 sections (South West, South East, South Central, Mid, North West, North East) with the officer responsible for each. Purpose: Staff information\tSource material: changes are updated via the GIS system.\t\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Regulatory Woodland Officer Areas.lyr\tCallum Stone, Team Leader Forest Regulation and Tree Plant Health\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-04-03\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18581\tNRW_DS121916\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.831Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSeagrass Area\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393136\tWater Framework Directive seagrass area data. This data was mapped by tracking the seagrass bed using a hand held GPS device either by foot or by hovercraft\tSeagrass area is mapped by tracking around the edge of the seagrass bed with a hand held GPS device either on foot or by using a hovercraft. Quadrats are taken within the seagrass beds, these provide species and density data.  This data contributes to Natural Resources Wales Water Framework Directive classifications.\t\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seagrass Areas\tMetadata Contact: Geospatial Services Internal Custodian: Kathryn Borrowdale, Marine Team SW Opps\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-09-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:10:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18577\tNRW_DS121942\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.842Z\t859\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAir Photos (Geomatics)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313231393432\tThis data collation was commissioned to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) as an enduring service by Environment Agency (EA) using Geomatics, the surveying arm to the EA.\tThe data coverage is principally concentrated to the estuarine regions around Wales, with the occasional coverage on inland areas around south Wales.  NRW has access to the following types of Geomatics Air Photos:  - NRI: Near Infra-red - RGB: Red Green Blue\t\tX:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Geomatics air photos  for data related to 2009 - 2017\tKeith Jones - GIS and Spatial Data team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18584\tNRW_DS122027\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.920Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNationally Important Biotopes: Intertidal Habitats\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303237\tNationally Important Biotopes is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of intertidal nationally important biotopes across Wales. A biotope is defined as the combination of abiotive habitat and its associated community of species. It can be define at a variety of scales (with related corresponding defress of similarity) and should be a regularly occuring association to justify its inclusion within a classification system. Biotopes becoming nationally important when they become or are generally geographically restricted on a UK wide basis. Highly rated examples of nationally and internationally important biotopes should be notified features within Site of Special Scienitific Interest (SSSIs). In the UK Biotopes types include chalk biotopes, soft, piddock bored biotopes, extremely exposed fucoid biotopes, Tide-swept algal biotopes, sand-influence rock biotopes, extreme shelter biotopes, muddy gravel biotopes, rockpool biotopes, under-boulder biotopes, cave and overhang biotopes, sublittoral fringe surge gully biotopes and seagrass biotopes.\tThis dataset is dervied from phase 1 marine intertidal surveys. It includes the extent and location of biotopes based on their UK biotope codes.\t\tx:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Marine\\Nationaly_Important_Biotopes\\Intertidal  Habitats 2009.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team  Internal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:32:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18572\tNRW_DS122028\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.515Z\t861\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTerrestrial Broad Habitats Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303238\tBroad habitats are a spatial datasets derived from Phase 1 Terrestrial habitat and species surveys conducted across Wales. Board habitats classifies / defines habitats as ecologically integrated units at a landscape scale, rather than seeing habitats as simply distinct types of vegetation. These broad habitats were classified in responsible to the convention on Biological Diversity. In Wales there are 36 broad habitats and the terrestrial habitats are as follows: Broad leaved and yes, planted coniferous woodland, native pine woodland, lowland wood pastures and parkland, boundary features, arable, improved grassland, unimproved neutral grassland, acid grassland, calcareous grassland, lowland heathland, grazing marsh, fens carr marsh swamp and reed bed, lowland raised bog, standing open water, rivers and streams, canals, montane (alpine and subalpine types) upland heathland and blanket bog.\tThis dataset is derived from the phase 1 terrestrial habitat surveys. With Board Classifications of habitats, two main criteria were used across the UK, these being; - a workable number of habitat types to ensure the process remain feasible - simplicity - the definitions should be easily understood, unambiguous and recognisable by a broad range of people. These broad habitats were later broken down into Section 7 Habitats of Principal Importance (see metadata 122418)\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Broad Habitat Combined BAP Habitats.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Broad Habitat Combined BAP Habitats Point.lyr\tGIS and Spatail Data Team  Liz Howe, Jon Rothwell, Stuart Smith, Pete Jones\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-07-10\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18574\tNRW_DS122093\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.516Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTerrestrial Mammals\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303933\tThis spatial dataset details the extent and location of a variety of terrestrial mammals across Wales.\tTerrestrial animals within this dataset include: - Bats - Brown Hare - Badger - Dormouse - Hedgehods - Otter - Polecat - Squirrels - Watervole\t\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\tLiz Halliwell GIS and Spatial Data Team\t\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18571\tNRW_DS122094\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.255Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Non Native Species Spatial Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303934\tNB THIS DATA SET HAS BEEN REPLACED BY A MORE UP TO DATE DATA SET (116352) N.B. 116352 is not a replacement as it specifically documents data exyracted  This is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location on marine non native species across Wales. These have been split out into invasive, low risk, other and potential. This data was collected from a variety of ad hoc surveys conducted by Natural Resource Wales staff or contractors.\tNRW has data available for the following species: - Invasive species include Botrylloides Violaceus, Crassostrea Gigas, Crepidula Fornicata, Didemnum Vexillum, Elminius Modestus, Eriocheir Sinensis and Sargassum Muticum.  - Low Risk species include Corella Eumyota and Rhithropanopeus Harrisii  - Other species include Bugula Neritina, Ficopomatus Enigmaticus, Anotrichium Furcellatum, Antithamnionella Spirographidis, Antithamnionella Ternifolia, Asparagopsis Armata, Balanus Amphitrite, Bonnemasisonia Hamifera, Botrylloides CF Diegense, Caprella Mutica, Codium Fragile Subsp  Atlanticum, Colpomenia Peregrina, Dikerogammarus Villosus, Feldmannophycus Okamurae, Goniadella Gracilis,  Grateloupia Turuturu, Haliplanella Lineata, Heterosiphonia Japonica, Mercenaria Mercenaria, Mya Arenaria,  Mylilopsis Leucophaeta, Mytilicola Intestinalis,  Mytilus Galloprovincialis, Perophora Japonica, Petricola Pholadiformis,  Polysiphonia Harveyi, Solieria Chordalis, Spartina Anglica and Tiostrea Lutaria  - Potential Risk species include Codium Fragile Subsp Tomentosoides,  Corophium Sextonae, Styela Clava and Tricellaria Inoptiata\t\tx:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\  NB MORE UP TO DATE DATA IS NOW AVAILABLE AT THIS LOCATION\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\t\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:23:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18580\tNRW_DS122098\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.914Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInvasive Non Native Species Records obtained from the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Atlas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303938\tThis data collation contains a variety of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) data and 3rd party owned data and has been obtained from the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Atlas.\tThis data collation includes records from across Wales and into England.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Invasive Non Native Species.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jennie Jones, Specialist Advisor, Non-Native Species  GIS and Spatial Data Team       This dataset needs updating with this search... https://wales-species-inns.nbnatlas.org/?\u0026amp;fq\u003dlistMembership_m_s:%22NRW Sustainable Management of Natural Resources (SMNR)%22#tabs-1\tVarious including volunteer recorders-Various-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2012-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:48.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18582\tNRW_DS122099\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.919Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNon Native Species: New Zealand mud snail (Potamopyrgus antipodarum)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232303939\tThe is a spatial dataset detailing the extent and location of the New Zealand mud snail Potamopyrgus Antipodarum. Potamopyrgus antipodarum is an aquatic snail of the family Hydrobiidae.\t\t\tx:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Other Potamopyrgus Antipodarum.lyr\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Other Potamopyrgus Antipodarum.lyr\t\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18579\tNRW_DS122101\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.917Z\t520\tValid\tEditable\tHabitats Directive: Article 17 Terrestrial Habitats Cycle 1 (2007)\tNodweddion Cynefinoedd Daearyddol Erthygl 17 cylch 1 (2007)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313031\tThis is a spatial dataset collated as part of the Habitats Directive reporting cycle 1 for Article 17 terrestrial habitats. Article 17 required a report to be sent to the European Commision every 6 years follwoing an agreed format. JNCC collate theses on behalf of the UK Country Agencies, and this data was sent to JNCC for the collation.   Article 17 reports are an assessment of conservation status of the habitat and species targetted by the directive. The assessment is made based on information on status and trends of species populations or habitats and on information on main pressures and threats. An important component of the Article 17 report is a map of habitat or species distribution mapped in 10x10 km grid.\tThe data has been derived from Phase 1 and 2 habitat surveys as well as other surveys. It consists of numerous terrestrial habitat extents for:  - Northern Atlantic wet heaths  - European dry heaths  - Apline and boreal heaths  - Calaminarian grasslands  - Siliceous alpine and boreal grasslands  - semi natural dry grasslands on calcareous substrates  - species rich nardus grassland  - Molinia meadows  - hydrophilous tall herb fringe communities  - lowland hay meadows - active raised bogs  - degraded raised bogs  - blanket bogs  - transition mires  - Rhynchosporion  - calcareous fens with Cladium  - Petrifying springs with tufa   Alkaline fens  - Alpine pioneer formations  - Siliceous scree  - Calcareous and calcshist screes  - Calcareous rocky slopes  - Siliceous rocky slopes  - limestone pavements  - Atlantic acidophilous beech forest  - asperulo Fagetum beech forests  - Tilio Acerion forests  - Old sessile oak woods  - bog woodland  - Alluvial forests  - taxus baccata woods.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2007\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.   Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T13:55:40.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18583\tNRW_DS122102\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.860Z\t355\tValid\tEditable\tHabitats Directive: Article 17 Terrestrial Habitats Cycle 2 (2012)\tNodweddion Cynefinoedd Daearyddol Erthygl 17 cylch 2 (2012)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313032\tThis is a spatial dataset collated as part of the Habitats Directive reporting cycle 2 for Article 17 terrestrial habitats. Article 17 required a report to be sent to the European Commision every 6 years follwoing an agreed format. JNCC collate theses on behalf of the UK Country Agencies, and this data was sent to JNCC for the collation. Article 17 reports are an assessment of conservation status of the habitat and species targetted by the directive. The assessment is made based on information on status and trends of species populations or habitats and on information on main pressures and threats. An important component of the Article 17 report is a map of habitat or species distribution mapped in 10x10 km grid.\tHabitats included in this data collation include 10x10 km grid squares for the following: - Northern Atlantic wet heaths  - European dry heaths  - Apline and boreal heaths  - Calaminarian grasslands  - Siliceous alpine and boreal grasslands  - semi natural dry grasslands on calcareous substrates  - species rich nardus grassland  - Molinia meadows  - hydrophilous tall herb fringe communities  - lowland hay meadows  - active raised bogs  - degraded raised bogs  - blanket bogs  - transition mires  - Rhynchosporion  - calcareous fens with Cladium  - Petrifying springs with tufa  - Alkaline fens  - Alpine pioneer formations  - Siliceous scree  - Calcareous and calcshist screes  - Calcareous rocky slopes  - Siliceous rocky slopes  - limestone pavements  - Atlantic acidophilous beech forest  - asperulo Fagetum beech forests  - Tilio Acerion forests  - Old sessile oak woods  - bog woodland  -  Alluvial forests  - taxus baccata woods.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2012\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.  Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-12-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:11:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18585\tNRW_DS122146\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.980Z\t619\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS) Sampling Points\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313436\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS) is Natural Resources Wales standard for collecting data on the physical character and quality of river habitats across the Wales. This is a spatail dataset detailing the location of sampling points from around Wales.\tThis datasets covers the whole of Wales and also into England where river catchments are applicable to NRW. This is a static dataset that has not been updated since 2013 however is due an update once database developments have occurred.\t\t[A]x:\\Monitoring\\Sampling Points\\River Habitat Survey.lyr  [B] Original data held on Wiski-Kieco  Update from Mel Lacan, August 2021: \\When we have KiECo RHS up and running fully (and calculating HQA Class) then I would like to update this layer and make it more widely available with some comms around RHS intranet page to be produced (so not ready yet).\"\tInternal Custodian: Melissa Lacan, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, SE Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team  GIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Environment Agency (EA)\t\t2009-09-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-19T15:56:59.417Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18573\tNRW_DS122188\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.515Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGlastir Woodland Management Networks\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313838\tThese were created by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) as part of assess agricultural woodland planting and habitat management teams. This dataset was derived from Phase 1 and created by Forest Research as part of a contract for Countryside Council for Wales. This dataset is now static, but has been and is used for guiding the Glastir Woodland Managemnet Schemes. These datasets have been used to assess where planting woodladn could occur, with those areas closer to the primary core networks scoring higher than those near the tertiary networks. This helped to identify opportunities for planting.\tThis datas should be used as reference only. There are now datasets which superceded these and should used instead, such as Habitat Networks.\t\tx:\\Biological\\Glastir Woodland Management Networks\\Woodland Primary Cores.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Glastir Woodland Management Networks\\Woodland Primary Networks.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Glastir Woodland Management Networks\\Woodland Secondary Cores.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Glastir Woodland Management Networks\\Woodland Secondary Networks.lyr  x:\\Biological\\Glastir Woodland Management Networks\\Woodland Tertiary Networks.lyr\tJim Latham is the internal contact who knows about this dataset.  GIS and Spatial Data Team\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:29:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18568\tNRW_DS122189\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.390Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGlastir Woodland Contract Managers Area\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313839\tadmin boundary set up by NRW for the woodland contract managers NRW owned - subject to change with OD\t\t\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Glastir Woodland Contract Managers Area.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team - Keith Harri Jones\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:29:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18569\tNRW_DS122191\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.300Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForestry Local Area Managers\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313931\tThis dataset orginated from Forestry Commission Wales - still being used but in need of an update. This is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Forestry local area team boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for foresty managed teams.  This dataset defines the forestry team boundaries and the 14 team locations within those areas.\tThis dataset can be used in conjunction with the NRW Operational Areas. (NRW_DS116298)\t\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\FCW Local Area Managers.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-10-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18566\tNRW_DS122192\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.163Z\t8\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHydrological Tools Datasets\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232313932\tThis is a collation of datasets used from hydrological modelling within Natural Resources Wales. This collation is a mixture of terrain, raster and vector data which allows more advanced modelling to occur around the hydrology of a catchment and watershed.   These datasets were created by Natural Resources Wales for use in the following tools:   Generic Hydro Tools: Used to model the path of raindrops as part of watershed analyses.  Hydropower Tools: used for hydro developments to model the effects of water abstractions and input on flow speed and directions, and therefore the possible effects this might have on species that are effected by these changes to the watercourse.   This helps users build a 3D profile and therefore model different scenarios.\tSome of these datasets are derived from the Detailed River Network.    The datasets include:  Hydro Rasters Adjoint Catchments Tools,  Catchments Drainage Lines,  DRN,  DRN Nodes, Extent 5m,  Terrain Hydrological Models.  These layers were created specifically for the NRW Hydro tool, and not suitable for general processing in ArcMap.\t\tx:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro Rasters.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_Adjoint_Catchments.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_Catchments.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_Drainage_Lines.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_DRN.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_DRN_Nodes.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Hydro_Tools_Extent.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydro Tools\\Terrain_5m_Terrain.lyr  x:\\Physical\\Hydrological\\Hydrological Models.lyr\tBen Anscombe,  GIS and Spatial Data Team\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18578\tNRW_DS122202\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.839Z\t853\tValid\tEditable\tWales\u0027 grey seal photo-identification database: EIRPHOT (1992-2016)\tCronfa ddata adnabod lluniau morloi llwydion Cymru: EIRPHOT\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323032\tThis dataset is a catalogue of seal images that were collected as part of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW) seal monitoring and evidence work.   Images were also provided by volunteers, partners, organisations and members of the public through collaboration with CCW/NRW. The images are of heads, necks and flanks of grey seals (mostly female) photographed at seal haul out sites around the Celtic and Irish Seas from 1992 to 2016 and show the pelage markings of seals that can be individually recognised/identified from subsequent photographic encounters – photo-identification (Capture, Mark, Recapture).   The database contains over 17,000 photographic images of more than 9,000 grey seals. Photo-ID allows individual seals to be captured through time and space and analyses of the data provide insights into site fidelity and associations, movements and connectivity, and longevity/survival.  This entry supersedes metadata entry 115548 which was created in 2013.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn gatalog o ddelweddau morloi a gasglwyd fel rhan o waith monitro morloi a thystiolaeth Cyngor Cefn Gwlad Cymru a Chyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC). Darparwyd delweddau hefyd gan wirfoddolwyr, partneriaid, sefydliadau ac aelodau\u0027r cyhoedd trwy gydweithrediad â\u0027r  Cyngor Cefn Gwlad/CNC. Mae\u0027r delweddau\u0027n dangos pennau, gyddfau ac ystlysau morloi llwyd (benywod yn bennaf) y tynnwyd ffotograffau ohonynt mewn safleoedd glanio morloi o amgylch y Môr Celtaidd a Môr Iwerddon rhwng 1992 a 2016 ac maent yn dangos marciau croen morloi y gellir eu hadnabod yn unigol o gyfarfyddiadau ffotograffig dilynol - adnabod lluniau (Dal, Nodi, Ail-ddal). Mae\u0027r gronfa ddata yn cynnwys dros 17,000 o ddelweddau ffotograffig o fwy na 9,000 o forloi llwyd. Mae Adnabod trwy luniau yn caniatáu dal morloi unigol drwy amser a gofod ac mae dadansoddiadau o\u0027r data\u0027n rhoi mewnwelediadau i ffyddlondeb a chysylltiadau safle, symudiadau a chysylltedd, a hirhoedledd/goroesiad.  Adroddiad Tystiolaeth CNC 280 mewn cyfeiriadau: Langley I, Rosas da Costa Oliver T, Hiby L, Morris CW, Stringell TB, Pomeroy P 2018. EIRPHOT: A critical assessment of Wales’ grey seal (Halichoerus grypus) photo-identification database. Adroddiad Cyfres Adroddiadau Tystiolaeth CNC Rhif: 280, 94tud, Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru, Bangor  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © a Hawl Cronfa Ddata Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru. Cedwir pob hawl\tThe EIRPHOT database consists of 35,724 pattern extracts, from 17,056 images across 3,273 sampling occasions collected between 1992 and 2016 at 280 locations around the British Isles, with a focus on the Irish and Celtic Seas.   The EIRPHOT database uses Microsoft Access to store photo-ID data. The Sightings table stores metadata on each sampling occasion including the date and location. This is linked to the Encounter table which lists each encounter with a seal, its age, sex, whether it was with a pup, any scars or injuries, and here it is assigned an ID. This table is linked to the Image table which records the names of images taken for each encounter. And finally this is linked to the Cells table, which lists the details of the pattern extracts that are available from the images. Pattern extracts from images entered into EIRPHOT were compared using ExtractCompare software. Capture histories were produced using Spatially Explicit Capture-Recapture software. Outputs in the form of Excel spreadsheets where each row represents a uniquely identifiable seal, each column represents a time period during which the individual has been photographed and matched to a previous image, and values in each spreadsheet cell are the location code in which the animal was seen first during that period.      Information Warning: any published outputs or research based on these data should use the citation for NRW Evidence Report 280 in references: Langley I, Rosas da Costa Oliver T, Hiby L, Morris CW, Stringell TB, Pomeroy P 2018. EIRPHOT: A critical assessment of Wales’ grey seal (Halichoerus grypus) photo-identification database. NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 280, 94pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor. NRW cannot provide advice or support for use or installation of the database.\tLangley I, Rosas da Costa Oliver T, Hiby L, Morris CW, Stringell TB, Pomeroy P 2018. EIRPHOT: A critical assessment of Wales’ grey seal (Halichoerus grypus) photo-identification database. NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 280, 94pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.  Further information about photo-identification software used can be found at http://conservationresearch.org.uk/\tReports and outputs held in the DMS.  Database archived as project 480, media 1557.  Listed on the MS Access database plan to move to new NRW MS access storage/transactional area or MUP.\tTom Stringell, Senior Marine Mammal Ecologist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-10-30\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2609\t2025-11-12T11:46:10.030Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18567\tNRW_DS122205\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.277Z\t703\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNewborough Warren and Llanddwyn Island Wind data (2018-present)\tData Gwynt Tywyn Niwbwrch ac Ynys Llanddwyn (2018-presennol)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323035\tWind data collected at Llanddwyn Island, Newborough. Data is available from January 2018 to present. The data provides daily 30 minute instantaneous averaged wind speed and direction.\tData is collected using a MiniMet Automatic Weather Station, Serial Number 47076 provided by Sky Instruments Ltd.  The DataHog2 datalogger is logging wind speed and wind direction every minute and the GPRS modem is automatically sending the data to the server by ftp. Power is provided by a 30 watt solar panel and incorporated  solar panel regulator.    Information Warning: During October 2018, the wind sensor was masked for filming purposes. The data in this period is therefore advised to be treated with caution. There are also data gaps between 1/8/2020 and 16/11/2021; and between 14/12/2021 and 26/12/2021.\tInstrumentation from Skye Instruments Ltd.(http://www.skyeinstruments.com/minimet-automatic-weather-station/)\tData can be downloaded from: www.minimet.co.uk.  NRW users will need to contact Suzie Jackson for Website login details and FTP login details.    Data can be accessed from this location on DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mcps/Evidence%20%20Data/Wind%20data/Wind%20Data%202017-2023?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1   An NRW Evidence report is in prep (as of May 2023) to describe this dataset, link to be added when available.\tInternal Custodian: Suzie Jackson, Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes AWMA, Marine and Coastal Policy and Planning       The actual location of the Weather Station has been removed for safety purposes.  Location is 53.136004, -4.4135866 (Pilots Cottages).  The data has been confirmed as Open data and added to the NRW Open data list.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:14:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18576\tNRW_DS122291\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.688Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tArticle 17 Terrestrial Habitat Features 2018\tNodweddion Cynefinoedd Daearyddol Erthygl 17\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323931\tData collected for Article 17, Habitats Directive reporting 2018. Collection of Point and polygon datasets. This is mapped to standards required by Article 17. The data has been produced through manual survey, referencing relevant documentation and third party data.  101 files, containing distribution and extent data for Article 17, Habitats Directive 2018 reporting:   Art17 2018 H1210 Annual Vegetation of Drift Lines 10km.lyr A rt17 2018 H1210 Annual Vegetation of Drift Lines.lyr  Art17 2018 H1220 Perennial Vegetation Stony Banks.lyr  Art17 2018 H1230 Vegetated Sea Cliffs 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H1230 Vegetated sea cliffs.lyr  Art17 2018 H1310 Salicornia 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H1310 Salicornia.lyr  Art17 2018 H1330 Atlantic Salt Meadow.lyr  Art17 2018 H1330 Atlantic Salt Meadows 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H1420 Mediterranea Saltmarsh Scrub.lyr  Art17 2018 H1420 Mediterranean Saltmarsh Scrub10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H2110 Embryonic Shifting Dunes 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H2110 Embryonic Shifting Dunes.lyr  Art17 2018 H2120 Shifting Dunes with Marram 10km.lyr A rt17 2018 H2120 Shifting Dunes with Marram.lyr  Art17 2018 H2130 Fixed Dunes with Herbaceous Veg 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H2130 Fixed Dunes with Herbaceous Veg.lyr  Art17 2018 H2150 Decalcified Fixed Dunes 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H2150 Decalcified Fixed Dunes.lyr  Art17 2018 H2170 Dunes with Salix.lyr  Art17 2018 H2170 Dunes with Salix 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H2190 Humid Dune 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H2190 Humid dunes.lyr  Art17 2018 H3130 Oligotrophic to Mesotrophic Standing Waters 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H3130 Oligotrophic to Mesotrophic Standing Waters.lyr  Art17 2018 H3140 Hard Oligo Mesotrophic Water Point.lyr  Art17 2018 H3140 Hard Oligo Mesotrophic Waters with Chara Calcium 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H3150 Natural Eutrophic Lakes 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H3150 Natural Eutrophic Lakes Point.lyr  Art17 2018 H3160 Natural Dystrophic Lakes 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H3180 Turloughs 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H3260 Watercourses with Ranunculion Rivers 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H4010 Wet Heath with Cross Leaved Heath 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H4030 Dry Heaths 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H4060 Alpine \u0026amp; Subalpine Heaths 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H5130 Juniper on Heaths or Calcareous Grasslands 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H6130 Calaminarian Grassland 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H6130 Calaminarian Grassland.lyr  Art17 2018 H6130 Calaminarian Grasslands point.lyr  Art172018 H6150 Siliceous Alpine and Boreal Grasslands 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H6170 Alpine \u0026amp; Subalpine Calcareous Grasslands 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H6210 Semi Natural Dry Grasslands 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H6210 Seminaturaldry grasslands.lyr  Art17 2018 H6410 Molinia Meadows 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H6430 Hydrophilious Tall Herb Communities 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H6510 Lowland Hay Meadows 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7110 Active Raised Bogs 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7110 Active Raised Bogs.lyr  Art17 2018 H7120 Degraded Raised Bog 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7130 Blanket Bog 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7140 Very Wet Mires 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7150 Depressions on Peat Substrates 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7210 Calcareous Fen Calcium Rich Fen 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7220Petrifying and Hardwater Springs Depositing Lime 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7230 Alkaline Fen Calcium Rich Springwater 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H7230 Alkaline Fen Calcium Rich Springwater.lyr Art17 2018 H7240 Alpine Pioneer Formations 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H8110 Siliceous and Acidic Scree 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H8120 Calcareous and Calcshist Screes Base Rich Scree 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H8210 Calcareous Rocky Slopes 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H8220 Siliceous Rocky Slopes 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H8240 Limestone Pavements 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H8310 Caves Not Open to the Public 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H9120 Beech Forests on Acid Soils 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H9130 Beech Forests on Neutral to Rich Soils 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H9180 Tilio AcerionMixed Woodland 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H91A0 Western Acidic Oak Woodland 10km.lyr  Art17 2018 H91D0 Bog Woodland 10km.lyr Art17 2018 H91E0 Alluvial Forest Alder10km.lyr ...\t\t\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2018\tInternal Custodian 1: Heather Lewis, Lead Specialist Advisor -Coastal Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Internal Custodian 2: Julie Creer, Lead Specialist Advisor – Coastal Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-12-19\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:13:32.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18565\tNRW_DS122293\t2024-05-28T12:52:09.096Z\t631\tValid\tEditable\tHabitats Directive: Article 17 Terrestrial Habitats Cycle 3 (2018)\tNodweddion Cynefinoedd Daearyddol Erthygl 17 cylch 3 (2018)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323933\tThis is a spatial dataset collated as part of the Habitats Directive reporting cycle 3 for Article 17 terrestrial habitats. Article 17 required a report to be sent to the European Commission every 6 years following an agreed format. JNCC collate theses on behalf of the UK Country Agencies, and this data was sent to JNCC for the collation.   Article 17 reports are an assessment of conservation status of the habitat and species targeted by the directive. The assessment is made based on information on status and trends of species populations or habitats and on information on main pressures and threats. An important component of the Article 17 report is a map of habitat or species distribution mapped in 10x10 km grid.\t\t\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2018\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.  Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-02-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:43:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18562\tNRW_DS122295\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.876Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status of the blackfly Simulium morsitans on the Afon Teifi at Cors Caron National Nature Reserve - 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232323935\tThe Afon Teifi at Cors Caron supports the only recently recorded UK population of the blackfly Simulium morsitans Edwards (Diptera: Simuliidae). The true extent of its UK distribution and status remain uncertain although Simuliidae are increasingly identified to species level during routine river quality monitoring.   This survey shows the results of samples and observations of simuliid larvae and pupae collected in early August 2018 and compares the results with a similar survey undertaken in 2008.\tThis 2018 Cors Caron survey on the Afon Teifi investigated the current status and distribution of the aquatic stages of S. morsitans. The 2018 survey re-visited and sampled simuliids from the same river zones, providing direct comparisons with the July 2008 survey results.\tBass, J.A.B. 2019. The 2018 status of the blackfly Simulium morsitans on the Afon Teifi at Cors Caron National Nature Reserve. NRW Evidence Report No: 316, 20pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID)\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2019-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:05:00.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18561\tNRW_DS122304\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.849Z\t8\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTerrestrial Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) 1km Distribution Maps for Priority Habitats\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232333034\t*** This data is no longer accessible, therefore this metadata is under review 07/08/2024 ****  Priority Habitats of Wales 1km squared distribution maps show the area totals for each habitat between 2002 and 2003. Priority habitats of Wales is mainly based on the combined results of the Lowland Habitat Survey of Wales, and the Welsh Upland Vegetation Survey undertaken by the Nature Conservancy Council and subsequently the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) between 1979 and 1997. This data are the results from these surveys which have been compiled and provides information on habitat extent for individual 1 km squares across the entire land of Wales.\tThis dataset was compiled using the results from the Phase 1 survey, Lowland habitat Survey of Wales and the Welsh Upland Vegetation Surveys. Where appropriate, these data are supplemented by the findings of carious plant community surveys.\t\tAll these distrubutions start with: X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\LBAP_Priority_Habitat_\tInternal Custodian Pete Jones but Stuart Smith is also very helpful.  Metadata: GIS Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:59:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18563\tNRW_DS122387\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.864Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD) Powysland English Watercourses\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232333837\tWatercourses within the English section of Powysland Internal Drainage Board now administered by Hoople. These watercourses drain into Powysland IDD watercourses within Wales. Mapped in linear form in this vector dataset. Data collected and mapped for the purpose of water level management, flood defence maintenance and planning operations/work programming.\tDigitised from old 1:10000 paper maps\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\PowysLand\\IDD Powsyland English Watercourses.lyr\tSpatial Data Team, Louise Harris\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:27:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18564\tNRW_DS122401\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.874Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAn assessment of the current condition of the saproxylic invertebrate assemblage at Castell y Waun a’i Barcdir/Chirk Castle and Parkland SSSI in 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343031\tMike Howe\u0027s evidence reports:   Alexander, K.N.A. 2019. An assessment of the current condition of the saproxylic invertebrate assemblage at Castell y Waun a’i Barcdir/Chirk Castle and Parkland SSSI in 2018. NRW Evidence Report No: 317, XXpp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\t\t\tData is stored in Recorder 6 as part of the Welsh Invertebrate Database\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18560\tNRW_DS122417\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.869Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343137\tAlexander, K.N.A. 2019. Saproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC) in 2018. NRW Evidence Report No: 320, xxpp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.  Contact Mike Howe / Rob Bacon for metadata details\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18559\tNRW_DS122418\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.864Z\t1408\tValid\tEditable\tEnvironment (Wales) Act Section 7 Terrestrial Habitats of Principal Importance\tDeddf yr Amgylchedd (Cymru) Adran 7 - Cynefinoedd â Blaenoriaeth\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343138\tUnder the Environment (Wales) Act (2016), Section 7 requires biodiversity lists to be produced. These lists include types of habitats which are of \"Principal Importance\" for the purpose of maintain and enhancing biodiversity in relation to Wales. This list supersedes the duty in Section 42 of the NERC Act 2006. This dataset details the extent and location of those terrestrial habitats classed of \"Principal Importance\" in Wales.-O dan Ddeddf yr Amgylchedd (Cymru) (2016), mae Adran 7 yn ei gwneud hi\u0027n ofynnol i lunio rhestrau bioamrywiaeth. Mae\u0027r rhestrau hyn yn cynnwys mathau o gynefinoedd sy’n \"bwysig iawn\" at ddibenion cynnal a gwella bioamrywiaeth mewn perthynas â Chymru. Mae\u0027r rhestr hon yn disodli\u0027r ddyletswydd yn Adran 42 Deddf yr Amgylchedd Naturiol a Chymunedau Gwledig 2006. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn nodi maint a lleoliad y cynefinoedd daearol hynny a nodir fel rhai \"pwysig iawn\" yng Nghymru.  Crëwyd y set ddata hon gan ddefnyddio Cynllun Gweithredu Lleol ar Fioamrywiaeth Cynefinoedd â Blaenoriaeth yng Nghymru a Chynefinoedd Eang, sy\u0027n deillio o arolygon cynefinoedd a rhywogaethau daearol Cam 1 a gynhaliwyd ledled Cymru. Yn 2019, roedd y rhestr dros dro o gynefinoedd pwysig iawn union yr un peth â\u0027r rhestr flaenorol o dan Adran 42 Deddf yr Amgylchedd Naturiol a Chymunedau Gwledig.  Cydnabyddiaeth Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis dataset was created using the terrestrial Priority Habitats of Wales Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) and Broad Habitats both of which are derived from Phase 1 Terrestrial habitat and species surveys conducted across Wales. In 2019 the interim list of habitats of principal importance was exactly the same as the previous list under Section 42 of the NERC Act; Priority Habitats of Wales Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) and was under review in consultation with Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  The current representation of Priority Habitats in the Environment (Wales) Act Section 7 Terrestrial Habitats of Principal Importance catalogue entry on DataMapWales is incomplete. This is because of an ongoing review of the data. We anticipate releasing the complete updated version of the data towards the end of 2025.  If you require a specific Priority Habitat, please contact opendata@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk, and we may be able to provide you with the relevant data, if it’s in a condition to do so. This will be with the caveat that the data is draft and may change.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\\tPete Jones is the custodian of this - although Stuart Smith is also a really good contact.  GIS and Spatial Data Team deal with the updates\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-06-17\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3604/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_terrestrial_sections_7_habitats\t2025-12-19T12:09:03.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18557\tNRW_DS122445\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.555Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tScarce Yellow Sally stonefly Isogenus nubecula, River Dee 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343435\tthis metadata relates to:  Davy-Bowker, J., Mauvisseau, Q., Hammett M., Sweet, M. \u0026amp; Troth, C. 2019. A survey of the River Dee for the Scarce Yellow Sally stonefly Isogenus nubecula in spring 2018. NRW Evidence Report No. 362  Contact Mike Howe for metadata proforma\t\tDavy-Bowker, J., Mauvisseau, Q., Hammett M., Sweet, M. \u0026amp; Troth, C. 2019. A survey of the River Dee for the Scarce Yellow Sally stonefly Isogenus nubecula in spring 2018. NRW Evidence Report No. 362\tdata should be entered into Recorder 6 - Mike to confirm R6 SurveyID\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:23:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18558\tNRW_DS122446\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.580Z\t918\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Ecological Data held in WISKI (KiEco)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343436\tSome of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) core ecology datasets are held in the WISKI Kisters Ecology (KiEco). KiEco provides a central repository for some of NRW\u0027s ecological monitoring data. The database holdings include data on fish species, River Habitat Surveys, invasive species, marine benthic species and habitat monitoring, and freshwater ecology. KiEco was developed in NRW to take over the required functions of the legacy Environment Agency Wales (EAW) databases, such as Biosys National Fish Population Database (NFPD) and Ecosys.\tMuch of the data collection for KiEco follows prescribed methodologies. Some of the data is also recorded as ad hoc sightings and additional information collected during monitoring activities. Within KiEco the standard core elements include information on sampling the biological sampling, including geographical hierarchies and observation values, taxonomic tree information and associated metadata.\tNon sensitive biodiversity information will be made available on the NBN Atlas\tThis module sits within WISKI - KiECO\tMetadata contact: Data Services Team  Data custodians and SME’s (Subject Matter Experts) Mel Lacan (RHS), Bianca Bacchi (Marine), Paul Hyatt (Fish) and Graham Rutt (Freshwater Ecology)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:49.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18556\tNRW_DS122451\t2024-05-28T12:52:08.464Z\t695\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Blocks\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343531\tWithin each Operational Area, Woodland is initially divided into Forest blocks, these are usually a specific number per woodland blocks. Sometimes very large areas of woodland are broken down into several blocks for management purposes. These geographic blocks are used to identify distinct groups of woodland within an operational area. Planning Staff use this block information to produce production forecasts at block Level. In 2004 Blocks were renamed Design Plan Units, but Blocks numbers are still used in Forester.\tThe data is a product of data administered and maintained by Forestry Planners, exported from the NRW \u0027Forester\u0027 GIS application on a quarterly basis. Since the creation of NRW in 2013, NRW have maintained the data in the Forester recreation module.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Blocks.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Primary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan Secondary Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-06-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:27:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18597\tNRW_DS122453\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.432Z\t523\tValid\tEditable\tEnvironment (Wales) Act Section 7 and OSPAR: Marine Habitats\tDeddf yr Amgylchedd (Cymru) Adran 7 ac OSPAR: Cynefinoedd Morol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343533\tUnder the Environment (Wales) Act (2016), Section 7 requires biodiversity lists to be produced. These lists include types of habitats which are of \u0027Principal Importance\u0027 for the purpose of maintain and enhancing biodiversity in relation to Wales. This list supersedes the duty in Section 42 of the NERC Act 2006. In addition to this marine habitats can be protected under the OSPAR Convention, which has established a list of \u0027threatened and/or declining species and habitats\u0027 in the North-East Atlantic.   This spatial dataset details the extent and location of those marine habitats classed of \u0027Principal Importance\u0027 under Section 7 and are regarded as \u0027threatened or declining\u0027 under OSPAR in Wales.   Habitats include:  Carbonate Reefs, Estuarine Rock, Honeycomb Worm Sabellaria alveolata, Intertidal Underboulder, Mud habitats in deep water, Musculus Discors Green crenella Beds, Blue Mussel Beds (listed under OSPAR aswell), Fragile Sponge and Anthozoans (listed under OSPAR aswell), Horse Mussel Modiolus Bed (listed under OSPAR aswell), Intertidal Mudflats (listed under OSPAR aswell), Maerl Beds live and dead (listed under OSPAR aswell), Sabellaria spinulosa reef (listed under OSPAR aswell), Saline Lagoons (listed under OSPAR aswell), Saltmarsh,  Seagrass Beds (listed under OSPAR aswell), Seapens And Burrowing Megafauna (listed under OSPAR aswell), Peat Clay Exposures (listed under OSPAR aswell), Sheltered Muddy Gravel (listed under OSPAR aswell), Subtidal Mix Mud Sediments (listed under OSPAR aswell), Tide Swept Channels (listed under OSPAR aswell), Oyster Beds (OSPAR only).-O dan Ddeddf yr Amgylchedd (Cymru) (2016), mae Adran 7 yn ei gwneud yn ofynnol i restrau bioamrywiaeth gael eu cynhyrchu. Mae\u0027r rhestrau hyn yn cynnwys mathau o gynefinoedd sydd yn \"Bwysig Iawn\" at ddiben cynnal a gwella bioamrywiaeth mewn perthynas â Chymru. Mae\u0027r rhestr hon yn disodli\u0027r ddyletswydd yn Adran 42 o Deddf yr Amgylchedd Naturiol a Chymunedau Gwledig 2006. Yn ogystal â hyn, gellir gwarchod cynefinoedd morol o dan Gonfensiwn OSPAR, sydd wedi sefydlu rhestr o \"rywogaethau a chynefinoedd dan fygythiad a/neu sy\u0027n dirywio\" yng Ngogledd-ddwyrain yr Iwerydd. Mae’r set ddata ofodol hon yn manylu ar helaethrwydd a lleoliad y cynefinoedd morol hynny sydd wedi’u dosbarthu fel “Pwysig Iawn” o dan Adran 7 ac sy’n cael eu hystyried yn rhai “dan fygythiad neu’n dirywio” o dan OSPAR yng Nghymru. Ymhlith y cynefinoedd mae: Creigresi Carbonad, Creigiau Morydol, Mwydyn Crwybr Sabellaria alveolata, Clogfeini Rhynglanwol, Cynefinoedd mwd mewn dyfroedd dyfnion, Gwelyau Misglod Gwyrddion Musculus Discors, Gwelyau Cregyn Gleision (a restrir o dan OSPAR hefyd), Sbwng Bregus ac Anthosoaid (a restrir o dan OSPAR hefyd), Gwely Marchfisglod Modiolus (a restrir o dan OSPAR hefyd), Fflatiau Llaid Rhynglanwol (a restrir o dan OSPAR hefyd), Gwelyau Maerl byw a marw (a restrir o dan OSPAR hefyd), riff Sabellaria spinulosa (a restrir o dan OSPAR hefyd), Lagwnau Heli (a restrir o dan OSPAR hefyd), Morfa Heli, Gwelyau Morwellt (a restrir o dan OSPAR hefyd), Megaffawna Seapens a Thyrchu (a restrir o dan OSPAR hefyd), Datguddiadau Clai Mawn (a restrir o dan OSPAR hefyd), Graean Mwdlyd Cysgodol (a restrir o dan OSPAR hefyd), Gwaddodion Mwd Cymysgedd Islanwol (a restrir o dan OSPAR hefyd), Sianelau wedi’u Hysgubo gan y Llanw (a restrir o dan OSPAR hefyd), Gwelyau Wystrys (OSPAR yn unig).  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Yn deillio\u0027n rhannol o ddata a ddarparwyd o dan drwydded gan Arolwg Daearegol Prydain © NERC\tThe data was collated from a wide range of sources, including but not limited to Marine Recorder, Phase 1 Intertidal, HabMap and Marine Monitoring Data. Habitats layers were produced as both polygon and point data for many of the habitats (where both types of data exist – some layers only have point data). Each layer is accompanied by processing notes detailing data sources and all decision made in creating the layers. A standard template was also produced and documented for attributing the GIS layers and general assumptions that could be made.  Data layers within this dataset are updated at different times, usually as and when new data is available and collated.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\tGIS and Spatial Data Team  Internal Custodian: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team       There are two data layers  Oyster Beds Points and Polygones that are not included in the open data download.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/884/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:MarineBAPOSPARHabitats\t2025-12-01T11:35:21.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18611\tNRW_DS122454\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.800Z\t864\tValid\tEditable\tEnvironment (Wales) Act Section 7 and OSPAR: Marine Species\tDeddf yr Amgylchedd (Cymru) Adran 7 ac OSPAR: Rhywogaethau Morol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343534\tUnder the Environment (Wales) Act (2016), Section 7 requires biodiversity lists to be produced. These lists include types of species which are of \u0027Principal Importance\u0027 for the purpose of maintain and enhancing biodiversity in relation to Wales. This list supersedes the duty in Section 42 of the NERC Act 2006. In addition to this marine species can be protected under the OSPAR Convention.    The OSPAR convention has established a list of \u0027threatened and/or declining species and habitats\u0027 in the North-East Atlantic. This spatial dataset details the location of those marine species classed of \u0027Principal Importance\u0027 under Section 7 and are regarded as \u0027threatened or declining\u0027 under OSPAR in Wales. These species include: Alkmaria Romijni tentacled lagoon worm, Anotrichium barbatum bearded red seaweed, Cruoria Cruoriiformis, Edwardsia Timida burrowing anemone, Eunicella Verrucosa pink sea fan,  Grateloupia Dermocorynus montagnei, Haliclystus auricula stalked jellyfish, Hippocampus sp., Lithothamnion coralloides Coral maerl, Lucernariopsis Campanulata stalked jellyfish, Arctica Islandica Ocean Quahog, Ostrea Edulis, Padina Pavonica, Palinurus Elephas Crawfish, Phymatolithon calcareum common maerl and Tenellia Adspersa.-O dan Ddeddf yr Amgylchedd (Cymru) (2016), mae Adran 7 yn ei gwneud yn ofynnol i restrau bioamrywiaeth gael eu cynhyrchu. Mae\u0027r rhestrau hyn yn cynnwys mathau o rywogaethau sydd yn \"Bwysig Iawn\" er mwyn cynnal a gwella bioamrywiaeth mewn perthynas â Chymru. Mae\u0027r rhestr hon yn disodli\u0027r ddyletswydd yn Adran 42 o Deddf yr Amgylchedd Naturiol a Chymunedau Gwledig 2006. Yn ogystal â hyn, gellir gwarchod rhywogaeth forol o dan Gonfensiwn OSPAR, sydd wedi sefydlu rhestr o \"rywogaethau a chynefinoedd dan fygythiad a/neu sy\u0027n dirywio\" yng Ngogledd-ddwyrain yr Iwerydd. Mae\u0027r set ddata ofodol hon yn manylu ar leoliad y rhywogaethau morol hynny sydd wedi\u0027u dosbarthu fel \"Pwysig Iawn\" o dan Adran 7 ac sy\u0027n cael eu hystyried yn rhai \"dan fygythiad neu\u0027n dirywio\" o dan OSPAR yng Nghymru. Mae\u0027r rhywogaethau hyn yn cynnwys: mwydyn lagŵn tentaclaidd Alkmaria Romijni, gwymon coch barfog Anotrichium barbatum, Cruoria Cruoriiformis, Anemoni tyrchu Edwardsia Timida, môr-wyntyll binc Eunicella Verrucosa, Grateloupia Dermocorynus montagnei, Sglefren fôr goesog Haliclystus auricula, Hippocampus sp., Maerl cwrel Lithothamnion coralloides, Sglefren fôr goesog Lucernariopsis Campanulata, Cragen Forwyn Fwyaf Arctica Islandica, Ostrea Edulis, Padina Pavonica, Cimwch coch Palinurus Elephas, maerl cyffredin Phymatolithon calcareum a Tenellia Adspersa.  Datganiad priodoli   Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl\tThe data collation included the use of Marine Recorder to query the plot distribution species. The validity of records were checked. Records that were considered doubtful due to the geographic location and known inexperience of the recorder, were checked thoroughly and excluded if not corroborated. Data layers within this dataset are updated at different times, usually as and when new data is available and collated.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Section 7 OSPAR\\Marine\\Section 7 Species\tInternal Custodian 1: Natasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Internal Custodian 2: Dave Tavner, Specialist Advisor – Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Mapping, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-07-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/885/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:MarineBAPSpeciesInWales\t2025-12-19T09:54:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18596\tNRW_DS122455\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.421Z\t657\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Management Areas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343535\tThe Management Layer is the actual area of land NRW manages, this can be larger than the legal boundary, this is because we manage land that is not our own such as extra land or Partnership land.\tThe data is a product of data administered and maintained by Forestry Planners, exported from the NRW \u0027Forester\u0027 GIS application on a quarterly basis. Since the creation of NRW in 2013, NRW have maintained the data in the Forester recreation module.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest Management Area.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Primary Metadata contact: Arpana Chunilal / Geraint Morgan Secondary: Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-01-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18613\tNRW_DS122456\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.815Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Quarries\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343536\tThis dataset contains Quarries on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any quarry data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured quarry information.\tThe information is currently gathered on site by contract managers who walk the site to ensure all quarry information is available in contract mapper. Generally on screen capture from paper mapping, but some captured using GPS, validated by GIS Technicians and Operational Staff.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest quarries.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-12-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18603\tNRW_DS122460\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.519Z\t768\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: River Macroinvertebrates\tKiECO Ecoleg Dŵr Croyw Macroinfertebratau afonydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343630\tFreshwater macroinvertebrates are used as an indicator of water quality and river flow requirements due to the relative immobility and abundance. This dataset is made up of the species abundance values from the freshwater river macroinvertebrates surveys conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of river water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  A geospatial version of this data is available in NRW_DS161278.\tA strict methodology is applied when sampling for freshwater river macroinvertebrates. There is a core network of sites which are monitored on a three-yearly rolling programme. These sites are located where risk assessment has shown significant risk of failure of WFD ecological quality standards. In addition sites may be sampled for investigative work, or on an ad hoc basis. Sampling areas are located with a larger survey area and must be representative of the overall survey area. A three-minute kick-sample is the most commonly used sampling method, although where necessary alternative methods are used, for example in deeper water or for investigative work. Once samples are collected they are sent NRW ecology laboratories for identification using standard methodologies, employing a strict Analytical Quality Control (AQC) procedure.   Once the data is collated in the central database, it has then been extracted and QA\u0027d before going onto the NBN Atlas. It is important to note that this dataset will include some sampling events from recent months where sampling has been undertaken but laboratory analysis has not been completed, therefore the full complement of taxa for that sampling event will not yet be uploaded. These records will be completed in future data uploads from NRW to the NBN Atlas. This dataset also includes records for some non-macroinvertebrate species, such as birds and fish, that were either observed or collected during sampling. Any non-target organisms collected during sampling were immediately returned to the river.\t\tData Held in KiEco.   A copy of the latest upload file is held on the Z:Drive - Z:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\Data Mgmt\\Data Services Data Publishing Area\\NBN Data\\NRW_DS122460_RiverMacroInvertebrates\tInternal Custodian:  Jennifer Owen Senior Officer, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer • Environmental Assessment and Advice,  Or Mel Lacan Senior Officer, Environmental Assessment • South East EAAT\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2019-03-13-2025-09-02\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr2116\t2025-12-03T10:14:02.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18614\tNRW_DS122461\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.876Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Mine Area\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343631\tThis dataset contains Mine Areas on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any Mine Area data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured Mine Area information.\tThe information is currently gathered on site by contract managers who walk the site to ensure all Mine Area information is available in contract mapper. Generally on screen capture from paper mapping, but some captured using GPS, validated by GIS Technicians and Operational Staff.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest mine area.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-12-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18604\tNRW_DS122463\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.521Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Mineshafts\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343633\tThis dataset contains Mineshafts on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any Mineshafts data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured Mineshafts information.\tThe information is currently gathered on site by contract managers who walk the site to ensure all Mine Area information is available in contract mapper. Generally on screen capture from paper mapping, but some captured using GPS, validated by GIS Technicians and Operational Staff.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest mineshafts.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-12-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:32:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18615\tNRW_DS122464\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.908Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Third Party Access\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343634\tThis dataset contains Third Party Access on NRW land. The data currently covers forestry areas but in future will expand to cover all operational areas. The data is collected locally by NRW operational staff to supplement any Third Party Access data available to NRW. The data is used in operational work using the Contract Mapper Module to ensure that when operational work is carried out by contractors they are aware of any locally captured Third Party Access information.\tThe information is currently gathered on site by contract managers who walk the site to ensure all Mine Area information is available in contract mapper. Generally on screen capture from paper mapping, but some captured using GPS, validated by GIS Technicians and Operational Staff.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forest third party access.lyr\tNational Forest Planning Team   Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Services Team , NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2018-12-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18599\tNRW_DS122469\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.424Z\t771\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey of the invertebrate fauna of Marford Quarry in 2018, with a particular focus on aculeates (bees, wasps and ants).\tArolwg o ffawna infertebrat Chwarel Marford yn 2018, gan ganolbwyntio\u0027n benodol ar golynogion (gwenyn, gwenyn meirch a morgrug).\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232343639\tFollowing management work in Marford Quarry to increase the amount of bare sand habitat for psammophilic insects, a survey in 2018 recorded 118 species of aculeate bees, wasps and ants including 35 species new to the site.   The cumulative aculeate list now stands at 171 species, with 81 bees, 80 wasps and 10 ants, 80 wasps, making Marford Quarry one of the most important site in Wales for this taxonomic group. The survey also recorded four new psammophilic aculeates - Anoplius infuscatus, Arachnospila wesmaeli, Coelioxys conoidea and Coelioxys inermis - and the psammophilic ground beetle Harpalus tardus at its only Welsh inland locality. The overall total for the quarry now stands at 31 psammophilic species including 26 aculeates, two beetles and three flies. Six new dead-wood nesting aculeates were found – Ancistrocerus gazella, Ancistrocerus scoticus, Megachile ligniseca, Psenulus pallipes, Stigmus solskyi and the Hornet Vespa crabro – resulting in a cumulative total of 43 species (42 aculeates and one beetle).   Both the psammophilic faunal assemblage and the dead-wood assemblage are considered to be currently in favourable condition although there is an urgent need to provide more deadwood habitat which is at a premium. It is also important to maintain early-successional sandy habitats by a programme of regular excavation work.   The finding of the solitary wasp Gorytes laticinctus in Marford Quarry is arguably the most significant record in the 2018 survey given its UK distribution and indicates a major range extension. Other important records were made of other insects including the tachinid flies Freraea gagatea (new to Wales), Gymnosoma rotundatum and Wagneria gagatea (both at 2nd Welsh localities) and Leucostoma simplex (new to north Wales), the Alder Leaf Beetle Agelastica alni (otherwise known from Fagl Lane Quarry), and the parasitic wasps Sphecophaga vesparum and Haltichella rufipes (both new to Wales).\tSurvey techniques included visual searches, sweep netting and pitfall trapping.\tFormstone, J.B. 2019. A survey of the invertebrate fauna of Marford Quarry in 2018, with a particular focus on aculeates (bees, wasps and ants). NRW Evidence Report No: 328, 46pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tReport is held in the DMS report Archive. Data is due to be entered into the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID) held in Recorder 6\tDr Mike Howe, Lead specialist advisor; terrestrial habitats and species       Data is due to be entered into the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID) held in Recorder 6 by Mike Howe, but this is not yet complete. There is no anticipated ate for input at this time.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-North Wales Wildlife Trust\tOpen Government Licence\t2019-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:31:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18605\tNRW_DS122565\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.743Z\t435\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDune Rejuvenation Trials - Newboorough Warren \u0026amp; Dunes NNR, Kenfig Dunes NNR, Merthyr Mawr Dunes NNR - Assessment 2016\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232353635\tTrial dune rejuvenation works have been undertaken at Kenfig, Merthyr Mawr and Newborough during the winters of 2011-12 to 2014-15, with a hiatus in 2013-14 when work was undertaken only at Merthyr Mawr Warren. The data related to a geomorphological assessment of the effectiveness of these rejuvenation works which will inform the planning of further works and includes UAV data, GPS data, LIDAR DEMs and GIS outputs.  The accompanying report provides a summary of changes in the extent of bare sand and mobile dunes at each site, discusses the factors which have resulted in differences in the impact between and within sites, and comments on the relative merits of the methods which have been used to monitor geomorphological change. Proposals are also made for further intervention works at each of the sites.\tThe assessment is based on: * Pre-works targets and objectives;  * Initial accomplishments in terms of bare sand area created by the works;  * Post-works RTK-GPS topographic survey data;  * Pre-works and post-works LiDAR survey data;  * Pre-works and post-works conventional aerial photographs;   * Post-works UAV survey photography and photogrammetric data;  * Pre-and post-works ground photographs taken during survey visits;  * Wind, rainfall and temperature data for nearby weather stations.\tFull information is held in the following report: Pye, K; Blott, S. 2016. Dune Rejuvenation Trials Overview Report. Natural Resources Wales Evidence Report Series Report No: 296, 140pp, Natural Resources Wale\tArchived as Project 481, Media 1558 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media1501to1600\\Media1558)\tInternal Custodian: Emmer Litt, Specialist Advisor - Marine and Coastal Physical Processes, All Wales Advice Team       This entry is related to metadata 114691, 115843, 115776, 115840\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-08-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:33:09.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18609\tNRW_DS122580\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.745Z\t117\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Lake Macro-invertebrates Taxa Data\tKiECO Ecoleg Dŵr Croyw Macro-infertebratau Llynnoedd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232353830\tFreshwater marco-invertebrates are used as an indicator of water quality requirements due to the relative immobility and abundance. This dataset is made up of the species abundance values from the freshwater lake macro-invertebrates surveys conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of lake water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tA strict methodology is applied when sampling for freshwater lake macro-invertebrates. A three-minute kick-sample is the most commonly used method for sampling freshwater macro-invertebrates. However, where necessary alternative methods are used, for example in deeper water or for investigative work. Once samples are collected they are sent to the laboratory for Analytical Quality Control (AQC). Once the data is collated in the central database, it has then been extracted and QA\u0027d before going onto the NBN Atlas.\t\tData Held in Wiski-KiEco - a PowerBI Report has been created to export this data\tInternal Custodian: Jennifer Owen, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, NW Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team          Pending finalisation of extract process for NBN / data input into WISKI\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-01-01-2025-09-02\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr3315\t2025-12-01T17:30:28.059Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18612\tNRW_DS122590\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.797Z\t297\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey of Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) at Welsh dredge disposal sites:  Milford Haven and the Severn Estuary 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232353930\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) contracted Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd. (Marine EcoSol) to analyse species and habitats surveyed in several dredge disposal sites around Wales (Milford Haven, the Severn Estuary, Swansea Bay Outer and Holyhead Deed), and undertake a data and literature review to discuss the potential for marine Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) transfer via dredge disposal pathways. The purpose of the surveys was to obtain information to inform decision making to minimise further spread of non-native species from dredge disposal.\tTowed underwater imagery was collected from dredge disposal sites Milford Haven 2 and 3 comprising video and stills. Grab and trawl samples were collected from Merkur Bouy, Cardiff Grounds and Newport.  Marine EcoSol was contracted to analyse towed underwater imagery from the Milford Haven surveys. Video and stills imagery from Milford Haven were analysed for substrates and species (including any INNS), and their habitats classified using the JNCC Marine Habitat Classification system. Photographs of the epibenthic species collected from the Severn Estuary surveys were also re-checked for INNS. All data were entered Marine Recorder v5.4 under the survey name \u00272018 NRW INNS Dredge Disposal Survey\u0027. Imagery was of varying quality, and small epibenthic trawks and grabs possibly insufficient to pick up the presence of INNS within dredge disposal sites. Methodological limitations are discussed within the associated report, Baldock et al. (2019).\tSee Baldock, L.,  Moore, A., Wray, B.,  Evans, S. and E. Morris-Webb. 2019. Survey of Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) at Welsh dredge disposal sites: Milford Haven and the Severn Estuary 2018. NRW Report.\tDMS\tBen Wray\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Marine Ecological Solutions Ltd\t\t2018-09-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:50.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18606\tNRW_DS122600\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.767Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNDF Key hotspots of biodiversity \u0026amp; ecological connectivity\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232363030\tThis data shows nationally important ecological networks, where connectivity is high across a range of habitats between biodiversity hotspots. High connectivity means that ecosystems within these areas are relatively resilient to environmental changes, such as climate change, and also have the capacity to help build ecosystem resilience more widely.  It is important to recognise these ecologically important area, both with a view to maintaining the existing habitats that contribute to the networks and also identify opportunities to improve them through targeted action such as habitat restoration and improved landscape management. Whilst the map gives a national overview, it is based on more detailed data of habitat networks that need to be considered for practical implementation, as the finer scale features in the lowland areas are not visible at the national scale.  Maps of ecological networks provide a practical means of considering connectivity alongside development.  Under the Environment (Wales) Act 2016 public authorities across Wales have a duty to promote ecosystem resilience in the exercise of their functions. This layer can used to help inform this work, by demonstrating which broad areas contain important and diverse habitat networks that are likely to benefit from careful management in order to maintain and enhance habitat connectivity and therefore the resilience of the habitats they support. This layer shows summary data at a national level which is made up of more detailed maps that are available to be considered at whatever scale is appropriate.  Ecological connectivity operates at the national as well as local level. This map considers the broadest scale and presents a national framework of ecologically resilient networks and the most important biodiversity hotspots. This map could be used at a strategic level to identify areas which are likely to benefit from careful management to maintain and enhance ecological connectivity and therefore the resilience of the habitats they support. Implementation on the ground is likely to require much finer scale information, as at a local level other networks and ecological hotspots not visible at a national scale will high importance.\tThis layer is created using data that is consistent across the whole of Wales to ensure no one area is either undervalued or overvalued due to varying levels of detail available from differing local or regional authorities and interested third parties. This means that the data can be viewed at national, regional and local scales to review the existing ecosystem service delivery and identify areas where studies can be undertaken to support strategic development planning. Furthermore, the opportunity analysis conducted also uses the same whole Wales data ensuring that attention is given to the primary hotspots and filtered into more detail within the planning process at the regional and local scales.  .\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\Key hotspots of biodiversity \u0026amp; ecological connectivity.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:47:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18600\tNRW_DS122690\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.414Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGeomatics AP index\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232363930\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\tx:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Air photos index\\Geomatics AP index 2018.lyr x:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Air photos index\\Geomatics AP index 2021.lyr\tKeith Jones\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-23T09:26:40.000Z\tKeith Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18593\tNRW_DS122707\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.875Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNDF Hotspots for generating ecosystem servcies that deliver public goods\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373037\tThis dataset shows areas in Wales that provide a large number of ecosystem service benefits. The services selected to be shown in this map were chosen because of their relevance to development planning and because they may not be immediately apparent in the same way as for example, agricultural productivity. The following services were chosen; flood regulation, water quality, vegetation carbon, soil carbon and landscape aesthetics. This dataset is a useful high level representation of significant areas for ecosystem service generation.  When interpreting this data it needs to be borne in mind that areas that are not highlighted may still make an important contribution to ecosystem services that were not included in the analysis such as agriculture.  The ecosystem services used to create this dataset deal with a number of key issues that have been identified as important in planning policy, including understanding environmental risks, addressing landscape change and adapting to the effects of climate change.\tThis data is important at a national scale, as it identifies ecosystem service hotspots, which are likely to have limited capacity for land development.  For local planning decisions, more detailed information is available from NRW on ecosystem service provision. This includes the SoNaRR report which contains the individual ecosystem service maps that were used to create this combined stock dataset as well as more detailed maps that were created to inform the Area Statements.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\Hotspots for generating ecosystem services that deliver public goods.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:46:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18610\tNRW_DS122717\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.802Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRecreation Places to Go Promoted Routes\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373137\tVectors (lines) depicting waymarked/promoted recreation routes managed or co-managed by NRW. The ‘ROUTE_DESCRIPTION’ field should be queried in order to delineate the date a route was last updated/confirmed and by whom. Information includes route name, length, direction, grade, type and the NNR or forest the route is listed against. Dataset is a resource for promotional material and visualisation rather than management. Management data associated with recreation routes is stored separately in the Forester database.\tOriginal data extracted from Forester and updated by the Data and Information Advisor (Recreation) with the managers for the routes/sites. Digitised on an OS backdrop (either 1:10 000 or 1:25 000) with the use of aerial photography from the X: drive to improve accuracy where required. If the data has been solely produced using a GPS (.gpx), this will be noted in the description field. Most datasets have been digitised on GIS rather than on the ground. In some occasions, .gpx files are used to check or improve the accuracy of already digitised routes. There is no automated update procedure for this data and it is not directly linked to the Forester database at present.  At least 25 000, majority 10 000. Zoomed to at least 3000 when digitising but accurate to 1: 10 000 OS mapping. Only applicable to routes listed as ‘confirmed’. Attributes say when the route was checked with the site manager or by GPX.\t\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Recreation Places to Go Promoted Routes.lyr\tHeather Crump, Data and Information Advisor (Recreation), Estate Recreation Planning Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-04-19\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:47:09.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18589\tNRW_DS122722\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.835Z\t328\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRecreation Places to Go\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373232\tVectors (points) depicting the location of managed or co-managed sites by NRW which are listed on the Recreation Places to Go external map browser.  Information includes site name, grid reference, subtitle and facilities.\tDigitised to reflect the grid reference for the site entrance, carpark or representative location provided by the site manager and/or the recreation webpages. Grid references, site names and subtitles should reflect the information on the NRW website.\t\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Recreation Places to Go.lyr\tCustodian: Heather Crump, Advisor (2) Recreation Content, Estates Recreation Planning Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2019-04-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:16:25.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18607\tNRW_DS122760\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.739Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNDF Hotspots of resilient ecosystem service provision\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373630\tThis dataset shows an overview of where areas with hotspots of ecological networks, important for maintaining biodiversity into the future, spatially coincide with areas delivering a number of key ecosystem services form the list considered.   The highest values are areas that are delivering a wide range of ecosystem services and are also significant in their provision of biodiversity and ecological networks. These areas therefore are hotspots for nature and areas which should be carefully managed to enhance their ecological and ecosystem service functions.  This dataset deals with a number of key issues that have been identified as important in planning policy, including understanding risks, addressing landscape change and adapting to the effects of climate change. Under the environment Act (Wales) 2016 public authorities across Wales also have a duty to promote ecosystem resilience in the exercise of their functions.  This dataset can help to inform this work at both a national and local scale by identifying the areas that are important habitat networks which also provide a range of ecosystem services.\tFor local planning decisions more detailed information is available from NRW on ecosystem service provisions, this includes the SoNaRR report which contains the individual ecosystem service maps that were used to create this combined stock dataset as well as more detailed maps that were created to inform the Area Statements.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\Hotspots of resilient ecosystem service provision.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18608\tNRW_DS122762\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.751Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNDF Greenspace Provision in Main Urban Areas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373632\tThis dataset shows areas where access to greenspace is limited, either because very little greenspace is available in the area to because access to greenspace is difficult. The larger the circle the more greenspace is available in the area, relative to the size of settlement. Accessibility of greenspace is shown in colour, with green meaning that greenspace is easily accessible and red that most people need to walk far to reach it.   This dataset can be sued to identify areas with a significant shortfall in the provision and accessibility of greenspace. While addressing these types of issues is arguably best done at the local planning level, this dataset gives a national overview in areas where greenspace provision is an issue, this information could be used to steer development away from existing greenspace and/or identify areas where greenspace provision could usefully be improved.   This dataset shows greenspaces and all areas that are within 400m walking distance of at least one greenspace, It also shows how high the demand for greenspace is in areas that fall outside the catchments for these greenspaces.  NRW has a number of other datasets that can be used to refine this information further for use at the local planning level.\tOS strategy data was used to identify urban areas, by selecting all urban areas that were classified as ‘large’ in the dataset. The polygons used in this dataset tend to overclassify, i.e. the polygons are larger than the actual urban areas. A number of the polygons also appear to contain non-residential sites. Greenspaces were identified from OS MasterMap and may include areas that are greenspace, but inaccessible to the public. Further refinement and/or checking of the data on the ground may therefore be required before the data can be used to inform local decision making.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\NDF Greenspace Provision in Main Urban Areas.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:47:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18602\tNRW_DS122763\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.414Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNDF Opportunities to generate multiple benefits through woodland creation\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373633\tThis dataset shows how many ecosystem service benefits a woodland could generate if plated at any given location. In some areas a single woodland might generate four or more ecosystem services, making the site a prime opportunity area. It is important to consider that in some cases a woodland that is only an opportunity for one or two ecosystem service can be very beneficial, if the demand for that service is very high at the site of interest. Services considered in this dataset are: opportunities that would enhance the woodland ecological network, natural flood management, water quality, provide recreation benefits or carbon sequestration.  This datasets gives an overview of how the opportunities for woodland planting are distributed nationally, which aids in achieving Wales\u0027 planting target of 10,000ha by 2020. Only opportunitites are shown that, if realised, would generate large (over 500ha) or moderately sized (over 5ha) woodlands based on the large/medium/small woodland map produced under GIS for Area Statements.\tThis dataset can be queried to show, for any location, which ecosystem service the woodland would deliver. It can also be sued to show only areas where, for example, the woodland would likely generate natural flood management and how many additional benefits the woodland might benefit. The project GIS for Area Statements created local scale maps for many of the ecosystem services in question. For some services local scale maps exists that have not been incorporated into this national scale map, as their area coverage is too small scale for national consideration. These include woodland planting to aid with urban noise or air pollution. These maps have been put forward as candidates for the draft of the Welsh Govt National Development Framework.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\NDF Opportunities to develop multiple benefits through woodland creation.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:47:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18594\tNRW_DS122765\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.853Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Chironomid Pupal Exuvial Technique (CPET)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373635\tChironomid are an insect family that comprises of midges. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) conducts Chironomid Pupal Exuvial Technique (CPET) surveys in lakes, rivers and canals as they provide a robust assessment of the anthropogenic impacts acting on large water bodies with minimal sampling effort due to the passive nature of its sampling strategy. This dataset details the Chironomid species records from the CPET sampling of lakes, rivers and canals conducted by NRW across Wales.\tPupae of all aquatic species of Chironomidae rise to the water surface for adult emergence. Discarded pupal exuviae (skins) float on the water surface due to trapped air and a wax-layered cuticle. At standing water sites these exuviae are blown by the wind and gathered at the leeward shore. Therefore, collecting chironomid pupal exuviae from one location is representative of recent adult emergence over a wider expanse of water. In rivers and canals the directional flow of the water will give an indication of where to sample passively-drifting pupal exuviae. Samples at all sites are taken between April and October, evenly spaced throughout the sampling period. Once the samples are collected they are then analysed within the laboratory and the results are entered into KiEco.\t\tData Held in KiEco - a PowerBI Report has been created to export this data.\tInternal Custodian:  Harriet Robinson - Data Services Team.  Tim Jones - Enivironmental Reporting Team  SMEs Graham Rutt and Mel Lacan       Pending finalisation of extract process for NBN / data input into WISKI\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18598\tNRW_DS122766\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.409Z\t1213\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHydrometry and Telemetry Data held in WISKI (WISKI 7)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232373636\tWISKI 7 holds core hydrometry and telemetry data owned by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and third parties. This data includes; River Level and Flows, Rainfall, Groundwater levels, and water temperature.\tData from the telemetry systems that record river level and flows, precipitation, groundwater levels and water temperature collect data from 100s of sites across Wales. These data are then transferred into WISKI 7 and quality controlled by specialists within the hydro and telemetry teams.\t\tAll data is stored in WISKI in the WISKI 7 Also known as WISKI Core or the Hydro and Telemetry module\tInternal Custodian:  Alison Hanson - Hydrometry and Telemetry Team Leader\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:17:58.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18601\tNRW_DS122909\t2024-05-28T12:52:44.441Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNDF Opportunities to generate multiple benefits through wetland creation\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393039\tThe dataset shows how many ecosystem service benefits a wetland habitat could generate if created at a given location. In some areas, a single wetland habitat may generate multiple ecosystem services, making this site a prime opportunity area. However, it is important to recognise that in some cases a wetland that only delivers a single ecosystem service can still be beneficial, if the demand for that service is high at the site of interest. Services that were considered in this map are: opportunities to enhance the wetland network, restoration priorities for functional peat and opportunities to generate natural flood management.  This dataset gives and overview how the opportunities for wetland habitat creation are distributed nationally, which aids in identifying important opportunity spaces. Looking at the national distribution of restoration opportunities also helps to highlight that not all areas of Wales are the same, with some areas being much more suited to restoration of one broad habitat than the other.\tThis dataset can be queried to show,  for  any  location,  which  ecosystem  services the creation of wetland habitat would deliver. Similarly, the dataset can be used to show only areas where extending the wetland network would generate specific ecosystem services, such as natural flood management, and how many additional benefits the wetland might generate. The GIS for Area Statements project created local scale maps for many of the ecosystem services considered for this map. In the case of network opportunities, these maps are broken down further, to show how the opportunity is situated in relation to the existing network.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\NDF Opportunities to generate multiple benefits through wetland creation.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:46:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18587\tNRW_DS122910\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.779Z\t303\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHigh tide roosts for waterbird features in Welsh Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs) 2018/19\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393130\tThis dataset provides detailed information on the location, extent and composition of high tide waterbird roosts within Welsh protected sites (Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs)). Metadata included in the GIS shapefiles include details of: species using the roost, months in which the roost is used, tides on which the roost is used, habitat and human activities that occur in the associated Wetland Bird Survey (WeBS) count sector.\tData have been collected through a pilot online mapping tool developed within the WeBS online data entry portal of the BTO/RSPB/JNCC Wetland Bird Survey (WeBS). It is intended that this development will enable the accumulation of comprehensive data on waterbird roosts within Welsh protected sites (SPAs/SSSIs) over time.   Information warning;   This data was commissioned by NRW for the pilot project and is not updated. NRW recommends that for an up to date copy, contact the British Trust for Ornithology.\tDetails of the BTO/RSPB/JNCC Wetland Bird Survey (WeBS) and the WeBS online data entry portal can be found at: https://www.bto.org/volunteer-surveys/webs\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Wildfowl and waders High tide roosts.lyr  Future data to be available to NRW through BTO/RSPB/JNCC Wetland Bird Survey (WeBS).\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External contact: niall.burton@bto.org        Spreadsheet names were listed but have been removed as this record is focused on the .lyr GIS layer.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-11-14\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18591\tNRW_DS122911\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.876Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNDF Opportunities to generate multiple benefits through grassland/heathland creation\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393131\tThis dataset shows how many ecosystem service benefits a woodland/grassland type habitat could generate, if created at any given location. In some areas, a single grassland/heathland type habitat might generate multiple ecosystem services, making this site a prime opportunity area. However, it is important to recognise that in some cases a grassland/ heathland that will only deliver a single ecosystem services can still be beneficial, if the demand for that services is high at the site of interest. Services that were considered for this map are: Opportunities to enhance the grassland/heathland network, accessible greenspace for recreation benefits, and surface roughness for natural flood management.  This dataset gives an overview of how the opportunities for grassland/heathland type habitat creation are distributed nationally, which aids in identifying important opportunity spaces. Looking at the national distribution of restoration opportunities also helps to highlight that not all areas of Wales are the same, with some areas being much more suited to restoration of one broad habitat than the other.\tThis dataset can be queried to show, for any location, which ecosystem service(s) the creation of  grassland/ heathland would deliver. Similarly, the dataset can be used to show only areas where extending the grassland/ heathland network would generate specific ecosystem services, such as natural flood management, and how many additional benefits the grassland/heathland might generate.   The GIS for Area Statements project created local scale maps for many of the ecosystem services in question, which can be used alongside this map to inform decisions on the ground.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\National Development Framework\\NDF Opportunities to generate multiple benefits through grassland_heathland creation.lyr\tCustodian: Russell Elliot Metadata Point of contact: GIS Spatial Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:47:12.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18590\tNRW_DS122914\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.835Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNew birds\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393134\t\t\t\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Birds\\New birds.lyr\t\t\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:27:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18588\tNRW_DS122915\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.780Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNew birds point\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393135\t\t\t\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Birds\\New birds point.lyr\t\t\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18592\tNRW_DS122916\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.832Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNightjar\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393136\t\t\t\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Birds\\Nightjar.lyr\t\t\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18595\tNRW_DS122919\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.875Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBarn Owl Sites Dec 2003\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393139\t\t\t\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Birds\\Barn Owl Sites Dec 2003.lyr\t\t\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18586\tNRW_DS122922\t2024-05-28T12:52:43.284Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCrayfish All Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393232\t\t\t\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Crayfish All Data.lyr\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species, Terrestrial Species Team\t\t\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:56.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18637\tNRW_DS122923\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.648Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCrayfish WFD catchments\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393233\t\t\t\tx:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Freshwater\\Crayfish WFD catchments.lyr\tNatasha Lough\t\t\t2014-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18640\tNRW_DS122948\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.845Z\t428\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales Trails Management System\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393438\tThis dataset is related to the condition of the Wales Coast Path and National Trails in Wales (with the exception of the Pembrokeshire Coast Path, the data for which is held by Pembrokeshire Coast National Park Authority). It contains information relating to trail furniture and problems / issues encountered along each respective trail.\tData is collected by Local Authority and Natural Resources Wales (NRW) surveyors in the field on an annual basis. Data are entered into the externally hosted Countryside Asset Management System (CAMs) database. NRW then collate the data on behalf of the Local Authorities and supply it back to them as a whole dataset via a Web Feature Service (WFS).\t\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Furniture Lines.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Furniture Points.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Issue Lines.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Issue Points.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Issue Polygons.lyr  X:\\Recreation and Access\\Wales Coast Path\\Route Lines.lyr\tContact:  Quentin Grimely Access Delivery Team, Sustainable Communities Gruffudd Owen, Wales Coast Path Officer (North)       The data is overwritten as it is updated along the path - so start date may not relfect the date on the dataset.  Natural Resources Wales (NRW) grant fund Local Authorities to collect this data. They input the data into the CAMs system which is hosted. NRW then collate the data on behalf of the grant funded local authorities and supply it back to them as a whole dataset via a Web Mapping Service.  At the moment we arent clear over the ownership of the data, becuase it is grant aided we think that the data is owned by the council but then given to NRW for use.\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:06:49.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18632\tNRW_DS122967\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.647Z\t187\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey of distribution and extent of Didemnum vexillum in Holyhead Harbour after Storm Emma October 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393637\tThis dataset details the presence and absence of Didemnun vexillum (an invasive non-native Carpet Sea Squirt) around North West Anglesey after March 2018, when Storm Emma destroyed Holyhead marina. Live Didemnum vexillum, previously found to be established on the marina structures and nearby boat hulls and moorings, was presumed to be distributed on marina debris around NW Anglesey. Shore and diving surveys were conducted in October 2018 in the marina area and where the bulk of floating material was washed ashore on the NW of Anglesey coast early in March 2018. D vexillum was only located on the various marina structures that survived the storm and on boat hulls and moorings in the areas where it had been found on previous surveys.\tIntertidal areas were investigated on foot with particular care taken to inspect marina debris, underboulders, overhangs and shaded vertical surfaces where didemnids are most likely to occur. Subtidal structures and natural features investigated by diving, concentrating on the types of habitat where D vexillum had been found previously: vertical and overhanging shaded surfaces of floating pontoons, boats and mooring buoys and chains. Fixed man-made and natural habitats where didemnids are known to occur were also investigated including harbour walls, jetty pilings, rip-rap and naturally occurring boulders. The intertidal survey covered 9 areas on the NW coast of Anglesey plus Porth y Felin and Scimitar beach in Holyhead New Harbour. Diving surveys were carried out on 58 locations in the New and Old Harbour areas to include the remains of the marina, boats and moorings in the New Harbour and structures, walls, pilings and natural rocky features in the New and Old Harbours. Presence / Absence data was collected for any occurrence of D vexillum with notes made of approximate abundance and the notable presence of any other non-native species.\tHolt, R.H.F. 2018. Didemnum vexillum: survey of distribution and extent in Holyhead Harbour after storm Emma, October 2018. NRW Evidence report no. 306.\tMarine Recorder Spreadsheet DM.  Document: EVID-301-367 Location:  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mnns/Monitoring/Didemnum/Holyhead%20Incident%20March%202018/D%20vex%20surveys/20181207%20didemnum%20vexillum%20storm%20emma%20survey%20data.xlsx?d\u003dwa1abdda14eec415d9647c66bc054e58e   Evidence \u0026gt; Marine \u0026gt; marine Non native Species \u0026gt; Holyhead Incident March 2018 \u0026gt; D vex Survey  Images available on https://photos.app.goo.gl/w6VvJd6eoL3YTJBk8 for download\tInternal Custodian: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  External contractors: Survey carried out for NRW by CloudBase Productions Ltd (Rohan Holt) with sub-contractor surveyors Kate Northen (Blue-C-Ecology), Nick Owen, Dan Gill, Liz Morris (Marine Ecological Solutions) with funds direct from WG\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-07-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18643\tNRW_DS122973\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.822Z\t653\tValid\tEditable\tRiver Wye catchment (Wales) species rich grassland creation opportunity maps\tMapiau ynglŷn â chyfleoedd i greu glaswelltir llawn rhywogaethau yn nalgylch Afon Gwy (Cymru)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313232393733\tA detailed GIS analysis of land use, Phase 1 habitats, National Vegetation Classification (NVC) community data, soils and designated sites for the River Wye catchment within the NRW mid-Wales operational area boundary was undertaken.   The potential hydrological suitability of each soil type for species-rich floodplain vegetation was assessed usng HOST classes. A trial of GIS-based Multi-Criteria Decision Analysis was undertaken to identify potential sites for restoration of species rich floodplain grassland within the Wye catchment.  Target NVC communities for restoration were selected (MG4, MG5, MG8 and M22-26) and criteria likely to affect restoration success identified. GIS vector datasets relevant to each criterion were then assembled or created, and assigned a suitability score. A weighted analysis was then undertaken for each of the target communities resulting in a series of maps, one for each plant community type, indicating areas suitable for restoration.\tThe maps are based on 18 different datasets, listed in Appendix 1 of the report. All other data sources used are available freely through the references provided in Appendix 1 of the associated report.\t2018. Gowing D., Lawson C., Smith  S. Natural capital and ecosystem service from flood plain land uses in Wales. NRW Evidence Report No. 265. November 2018\tX:\\Biological\\Floodplain Habitat Restoration\\River Wye catchment (Wales) opportunity\tStuart Smith - Grassland Ecologist.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-07-21\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2646\t2025-05-07T09:21:50.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18638\tNRW_DS123018\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.816Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tActivity Centres\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303138\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Activity Centres.lyr\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18645\tNRW_DS123035\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.822Z\t836\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Lakes Survey and Monitoring\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303335\tThis is a compilation of lake survey and monitoring data carried out by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and it’s predecessor bodies Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Environment Agency Wales (EAW) since 1992. It consists mainly of aquatic plant (macrophyte) surveys supported by water chemistry and dissolved oxygen, temperature and Secchi depth data. The datasets cover a wide range of lakes in Wales focussing on protected sites (Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) lakes) and on larger waterbodies monitored for Water Framework Directive (WFD). A selection of lakes that are not protected but likely to be of biodiversity importance are also included.\tEarlier surveys used a mapping approach but since around 2004 a standardised transect based method developed for common standards monitoring and Water Framework Directive (WFD) lakes monitoring has been used (the LEAFPACS Survey method). All of the data collected can be used for WFD classification and common standards monitoring conditions assessments and in most cases this has already been done in the relevant reports.   Individual lakes have been surveyed at varying frequencies generally ranging from every 3 years to every 12 years as is the case of protected sites or WFD waterbodies, other lakes have usually only received a one off survey.\t\tReports are storted in Library Data is stored in various places, some is in KiEco - LEAFPAC spreadsheets are on the DMS Evidence \u0026gt; Freshwater Ecology \u0026gt;  Internal Research \u0026gt; Lake Leafpacs data and spreadsheets Older data is in the Lakes Database.\tInternal Data Custodian:  Tristan Hatton-Ellis\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18641\tNRW_DS123046\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.846Z\t1092\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Road Access\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233303436\tThe point theme contains the access points or gateways into forest or woodland managed by Natural Resources Wales, providing access to the forest roads network.\tA point theme generated from end points of Forest Roads snapped to the nearest public road. Only forest trunk roads and spur roads were used to generate the point theme, excluding tracks.  These access points are not verified on the ground and may therefore not always be a true access.\tRelated to the Forest Roads dataset\tX:\\Physical\\Forestry\\Forest Road Access.lyr\tPeter van-Velzen, Planning Manager, Wales Harvesting \u0026amp; Marketing\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:39:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18635\tNRW_DS123207\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.646Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOpportunistic Macroalgae Areas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323037\tWater Framework Directive opportunistic macroalgae area data for the following waterbodies from 2007 onwards; Afan, Alaw, Artro, Braint, Cefni, Clwyd,  Conwy, Dyfi, Dysynni, Erch, Foryd, Ffraw, Glaslyn, Gwaun, Holyhead Bay, Holyhead Straits, Loughor, Mawddach, Menai Strait, MHI, MHO, Neath, Nyfer Ogmore, Swansea and Teifi. This data was mapped in a desk based study using aerial imagery.\tOpportunistic Macroalgae area is mapped using aerial photography from our contractors. The data is ground truthed using quadrats to monitor density and biomass of macroalgae.  This data contributes to Natural Resources Wales Water Framework Directive classifications.\t\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Macroalgae Areas\tMetadata Contact: Geospatial Services Internal Custodian: Kathryn Borrowdale, Marine Team SW Opps\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-09-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18644\tNRW_DS123208\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.804Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOpportunistic Macroalgae Quadrats\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323038\tWater Framework Directive opportunistic macroalgae quadrats. This data shows the positions of the quadrats that were taken for the following waterbodies from 2007 onwards; Alaw, Artro, Braint, Dyfi, Erch, Ffraw, Foryd, Holyhead Straits, MHI, MHO, Nyfer and Teifi. Species density and biomass information for these quadrats can be found on KiECO.\tQuadrat locations are created randomly on GIS software. Survey officers find the quadrat locations in the field using a hand held GPS and take a fix of this position. This data is the position taken by survey officers in the field. Species density and biomass information for these quadrats can be found on KiECO.  This data contributes to Natural Resources Wales Water Framework Directive classifications\t\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Macroalgae Quadrats\tMetadata Contact: Geospatial Services Internal Custodian: Kathryn Borrowdale, Marine Team SW Opps\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-09-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:03:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18634\tNRW_DS123209\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.647Z\t21\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSeagrass Quadrats\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323039\tWater Framework Directive seagrass quadrat data. This data shows the positions of the quadrats that were taken. Species and density information for the quadrats is available on KiECO.\tQuadrat locations are created randomly on GIS software. Survey officers find the quadrat locations in the field using a hand held GPS and take a fix of this position. This data is the position taken by survey officers in the field. Species and density information for the quadrats is available on KiECO.  This data contributes to Natural Resources Wales Water Framework Directive classifications\t\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Seagrass Quadrats\tMetadata Contact: Geospatial Services Internal Custodian: Kathryn Borrowdale, Marine Team SW Opps\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-09-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18642\tNRW_DS123215\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.817Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEnvironmentally Sensitive Permanent Grassland\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323135\t\t\t\tx:\\Biological\\Environmentally Sensitive Permanent Grassland.lyr\tIwan Hughes\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:39:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18633\tNRW_DS123216\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.626Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTBLFish\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233323136\t\t\t\tx:\\Biological\\Freshwater Fisheries\\Dee Bullhead\\TBLFish font point.lyr , x:\\Biological\\Freshwater Fisheries\\Dee Bullhead\\TBLFish point.lyr\tTristan Hatton-Ellis, Lead Specialist Advisor, Freshwater Habitats and Species, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Group\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:38.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18630\tNRW_DS123219\t2024-05-28T12:53:17.429Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSubtidal with Dee and Severn\t\t4f4c