 Day 1 readiness program, in preparation for OD implementation. Wholly derived from; \u0027Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH): Hydrological and Catchment Areas (3rd Party Data)\u0027\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW FRM Operational Areas.lyr\tData custodian; Gary Eccleston, Knowledge \u0026amp; Information Management, NRW Metadata point of contact; Spatial team, NRW Business contact ; Dave Tarrant, Flood Risk Analysis Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\t\t2020-05-25\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:16.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18813\tNRW_DS123529\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.841Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWaste Electrical and Electronic Equipment Approved Authorised Treatment Facility and Approved Exporter\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233353239\tPublic Register showing details of reprocessors and exporters of Waste Electrical and Electronic (WEEE) approved under the Waste Electrical and Electronic Regulations. A Spreadsheet showing the details of companies in Wales currently approved as an  Approved Authorised Treatment Facility (AATF) or Approved Exporter (AE) available on NRWs Online Public register. Only public register data specified in the WEEE Regulations are included.\tOnly data for the current year data is made availalble on the public register.\t\tlist of current approvals on Public Register Copies of all historic reports are on the DMS.\tJohn Davies (weee@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:02:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18818\tNRW_DS123530\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.946Z\t662\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Flows Estimate Data\tData Amcangyfrif Llifau Llifogydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233353330\tPeak flood flow and/or flood hydrograph estimates for one or more locations for one or more design events, usually calculated using gauged data, Flood Estimation Handbook Statistical and/or Rainfall Runoff methods.\tThis data is not raw, factual or measured. It comprises of estimated or modelled results showing expected outcomes based on the data available to us.\t\tWinFap Model run on request by the flood team\tGlenda Tudor-Ward, Specialist Advisor, Hydrology \u0026amp; Water Resources, Hydrology \u0026amp; Water Resources Management\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\tNRW Conditional\t2023-05-05\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:52:12.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18808\tNRW_DS123553\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.553Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCamping and Caravan Sites\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233353533\t\t\t\tx:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Camping and Caravan Sites.lyr\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:02:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18822\tNRW_DS123582\t2024-05-28T12:56:22.028Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Habitat Vulnerability\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233353832\t\t\t\tx:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine habitat vulnerability.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Abrasion and physical disturbance.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Decrease in emergence regime.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Decrease in suspended sediment.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Decrease in turbidity.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Decrease in water flow rate.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Decrease in wave exposure.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Increase in emergence regime.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Increase in suspended sediment.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Increase in turbidity.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Increase in water flow rate.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Increase in wave exposure.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Overall Biotope Vulnerability V2.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Overall Biotope Vulnerability.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Smothering.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP1\\EP1 Substratum loss.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Abrasion and physical disturbance.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Decrease in Emergence Regime.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Decrease in Suspended Sediment.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Decrease in Turbidity.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Decrease in Water Flow Rate.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Decrease in Wave Exposure.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Increase in Emergence Regime.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Increase in Suspended Sediment.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Increase in Turbidity.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Increase in Water Flow Rate.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Increase in Wave Exposure.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Overall Biotope Vulnerability V2.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Overall Biotope Vulnerability.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Smothering.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP2\\EP2 Substratum Loss.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP3\\EP3 Abrasion and Physical Disturbance.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP3\\EP3 Decrease in Emergence Regime.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP3\\EP3 Decrease in Suspended Sediment.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability\\EP3\\EP3 Decrease in Turbidity.lyr  x:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\Marine Habitat Vulnera\tPaul Brazier\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:14:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18814\tNRW_DS123644\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.917Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Licence Disposal Sites\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233363434\tHistoric and current designated sites for disposal of dredge material at sea.\tDisposal sites in the past have been designated by UK government agencies. These sites are now designated in Wales by Natural Resources Wales. Disposal sites are designated based industry requirements taking into account proximately to certain activities, such as ports which are dredged on a regular basis and ecological conditions as well as other parameters. They vary in size and location. Some have evolved over time and sites are designated for different reasons. Designations depends on certain factors including (but not limited to) the type of material being dropped, and the physical processes occurring in those area.\tThis data should be used only in WGS84 as circular sites will appear elliptical in other projections. A full UK Disposal Site Layer can be downloaded from Cefas: http://data.cefas.co.uk/#/View/407\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Marine\\Marine Licence disposal sites WGS84.lyr\tJoseph Thomas\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-10-14\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-14T08:09:43.000Z\tAndrew Williams\t-\tPrivate record\n18823\tNRW_DS123773\t2024-05-28T12:56:22.175Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIntertidal Biotope Map\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233373733\t\t\t\t\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:07:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18809\tNRW_DS123857\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.821Z\t690\tValid\tEditable\tGeneral licences - excluded areas where specific licences are required\tTrwyddedau cyffredinol sy’n ddilys o 1 Ionawr 2024 - ardaloedd wedi\u0027u heithrio\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233383537\tThis spatial dataset shows the areas which are excluded from General licences 001, 002 and 004 issued on 1st January 2024 by Natural Resources Wales to permit the killing of certain species of wild birds. Individuals wishing to kill wild birds within these areas will need to apply to Natural Resources Wales for a specific licence, using the application form which can be found on the Natural Resources website.-Mae\u0027r set ddata ofodol hon yn dangos yr ardaloedd sydd wedi\u0027u heithrio o drwyddedau Cyffredinol 001,002 a 004 a gyhoeddwyd ar 1 Ionawr 2024 gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru i ganiatáu lladd rhai adar gwyllt. Bydd angen i unigolion sy\u0027n dymuno lladd adar gwyllt o fewn y dynodiadau hyn wneud cais i Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru am drwydded benodol, gan ddefnyddio’r ffurflen gais sydd i\u0027w gweld ar wefan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru.\tThis is derived data from SSSI\u0027s, SPA\u0027s and SAC\u0027s as follows: All SPAs;  Mammalian SACs;  Mammalian SSSI;  Bird SSSI, where disturbance is deemed an issue.\t\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Areas where bespoke licence required.lyr\tIwan G Hughes, Team Leader, Species and Carbon Permitting, Technical Services and Customer Care\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2024-01-10\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/568/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_Bespoke_licence_areas\t2025-05-02T14:48:15.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18807\tNRW_DS123892\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.558Z\t324\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales: Species Datasets\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233383932\tThis dataset was part of the Phase 2 Habitats surveys conducted across Wales. It consists of a detailed survey of plant communities in lowland peatland vegetation. The survey was conducted at Phase 2 (plant community) level of herbaceous mire habitats predominantly within the lowland environment of Wales. Key project outputs include site-based assessments of community type, distribution and ecology and evaluative accounts of vegetation and its condition. Strategic outputs include Area of Search/regional/all Wales synthesis and analysis of data to inform site selection, conservation and restoration. This part of the Phase 2 survey includes species datasets collected by Countryside Council for Wales and Natural Resources Wales in-house expert Phase 2 survey team. Peer-based quality assurance is carried out within the team and by CCW\u0027s Peatland Specialist. Regular meetings are held to ensure consistent community and species determination and survey protocol application.\tPhase 2 vegetation mapping and collection of quadrat data on plant community composition. Additional information is collected on notable species, environmental factors, condition, management requirements and conservation evaluation. See \u0027Manual for lowland peatland survey, P. Jones et al., 2007 (in prep.) for more information. Written site accounts are available for each surveyed site. These greatly assist in interpreting the raw data in each case. Characterise and evaluate the vegetation cover of lowland peatlands in Wales based upon a programme of plant (sub-) community level sampling and mapping. All Wales, underpinned by criteria to aid site selection. Surveying is carried out between June and September starting in 2004 and due to finish in 2011.\tThis non-spatial species data is held in Recorder 6 which dates between 2004 and 2011, and non-sensitive species records are available to download via the NBN Atlas (see Resource Locator).\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas. This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).  Update from Kathryn Birch - September 2021 \"2004-2011 species records on NBN Atlas. There is now a lot more species data (2012 to 2021) that needs to go onto NBN Atlas. I haven\u0027t had time to find out how this needs to be done. Probably something to sort out in 2022 when the Peatland survey project ends\".\tInternal Custodian: Kathryn Birch, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:35:01.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18810\tNRW_DS123895\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.802Z\t334\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Woodland Survey of Wales: Species Datasets\tCam 2 Arolwg Coetir Cymru: Setiau Data Rhywogaethau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233383935\tThis dataset contains species records from detailed Phase 2 survey data from over 800 woodlands of conservation interest in Wales, predominantly Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSls) and pSSSI woodlands.   Phase 2 woodland surveys have been carried out periodically since the late 1980s but a concentrated period of survey on SSSI and pSSSI woodland occurred from 1995 and 2000.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tStandard woodland National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey methodology was used to collect this data. Woodland communities were identified by eye and mapped, supported by sample quadrats and target notes.\tThis non-spatial species data is held in Recorder 6 which dates between the late 1980s and 2000, and non-sensitive species records are available to download via the NBN Atlas (see Resource Locator).\tSpecies data is also stored in Recorder 6 under the Phase 2 Woodland Survey Tag. This data is now available on the NBN Atlas. The species part of the dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy system).\tDavid Reed, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats - Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-02-18\tcreation\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-11-25T11:46:33.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18817\tNRW_DS123954\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.745Z\t842\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) meteorological data 2017 to 2023 (Morfa Harlech, Merthyr Mawr, Whiteford)\tData meteorolegol 2017 ‘Twyni Byw’ i’w cyflwyno (Morfa Harlech, Merthyr Mawr, Whiteford)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233393534\tMeteorological data collected at Morfa Harlech, Merthyr Mawr and Whiteford from February 2017 to February 2023. The data provides wind speed and direction, temperature, rain and pressure at 15 minute intervals.\tInstrumentation;  Console type; Davis Vantage Pro II.  Remote Display Unit/s: Skylink-pro Data Loggers/Software: GPRS Modem Field sensors: Wind Speed/Direction, temperature:, humidity, barometric pressure, rainfall, solar radiation, UV  Data availability is as follows:  Morfa Harlech  21/02/2017 – 03/11/2017; 15/11/2017 – 01/12/2017; 18/12/2017 – 04/10/2018; 15/11/2018 – 17/01/2019; 13/02/2019 – 20/02/2023. Whiteford 22/02/2017 – 12/03/2018; 14/03/2018 – 23/09/2018; 09/10/2018 – 21/10/2018; 19/11/2018 – 20/04/2020; 22/04/2020 – 12/02/2022; 14/02/2022 – 19/02/2023. Merthyr Mawr 21/02/2017 – 14/04/2017; 16/04/2017 – 20/04/2017; 22/04/2017 – 24/04/2017; 16/04/2017 – 26/10/2017; 28/10/2017 – 28/11/2017; 20/02/2018 – 11/03/2018; 13/03/2018 – 29/07/2018; 20/07/2019 – 10/04/2020; 10/02/2021 – 20/01/2022; 22/01/2022 – 21/10/2022.\tSkyview website; https://skylink-pro.com/index.php\tData is available on DMS here https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mcps/Evidence%20%20Data/Wind%20data/Wind%20Data%202017-2023?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1   An NRW Evidence report is in prep (as of May 2023) to describe this dataset, link to be added when available.\tInternal Custodian: Iain Fairley, Specialist Advisor: Marine and Coastal Physical Science, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-02-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:23:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18806\tNRW_DS123982\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.454Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Lake Phytoplankton\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233393832\tPhytoplankton form the base of many food webs in lakes. This method uses the principle that an increase in nutrients (particularly phosphorus) leads to an increases in phytoplankton biomass and a change in the taxonomic composition, often leading to an increased occurrence of cyanobacteria (blue-green algae.   Only the collection data is held on the KIECO database.\tSpecies data and counts are not held on the KIECO database.\t\tData Held in KiEco - a PowerBI Report has been created to export this data\tInternal Custodian: Harriet Robinson / Holly Self - Data Services Team.  Internal Custodian: Jennifer Owen, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, NW Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team        Pending finalisation of extract process for NBN / data input into WISKI\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18819\tNRW_DS123983\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.928Z\t562\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: River Macrophytes\tKiECO Ecoleg Dŵr Croyw Macroffytau Afonydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233393833\tMacrophytes (water plants visible to the naked eye) can indicate the impact of pressures such as increased nutrients, flow pressures, channel alterations etc. The species present and their abundance give an indication as to the health of a river. This dataset is made up of species lists and their percentage cover value for each survey conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of river water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.\tA strict methodology is applied when sampling for freshwater macrophytes. There is a core network of sites which are monitoring on a three-year rolling programme. These sites are added to for investigative work, or on an ad hoc basis. Sampling areas are located with a larger survey area and must be representative of the overall survey area. A standard cover-class method is the most commonly used method for sampling freshwater macrophytes. Natural Resources Wales collect and analyse in excess of 100 surveys from rivers every year. Macrophyte species are identified on site and confirmed in the laboratory if necessary.\t\tData Held in KiEco - a PowerBI Report has been created to export this data\tAdvisor - Data Services Team.  Graham Rutt  SMEs       Pending finalisation of extract process for NBN / data input into WISKI Data sent to NBN Feb 2020\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-03-09\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr2172\t2025-02-04T11:14:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18805\tNRW_DS123984\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.252Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Incidental Records\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313233393834\tIncidental records are used to record species of importance (may be rare, invasive, of local value) which were not within the confines of the original survey method or were outside the range (spatially) of the original survey.\t\t\tData Held in KiEco - a PowerBI Report has been created to export this data\tInternal Custodian: Jennifer Owen, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, NW Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team       Pending finalisation of extract process for NBN / data input into WISKI\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18804\tNRW_DS124099\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.030Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRoad verges Designated RVNR\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234303939\t\t\t\tx:\\Biological\\Road verges Designated RVNR.lyr\tIsobella Griffiths\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18797\tNRW_DS124101\t2024-05-28T12:56:20.983Z\t146\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForest Research Peat Restoration Climate Suitability (3rd party data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234313031\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\tx:\\Physical\\Peat\\FR Peat Restoration Climate Suitability.lyr\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-16T08:35:01.062Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18800\tNRW_DS124164\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.030Z\t714\tValid\tEditable\tEighteen years of capture-mark-recapture (CMR) data of grey seal pups, Halichoerus grypus, from Pembrokeshire, Wales\tDeunaw mlynedd o ddata dal-nodi-ail-ddal (CMR) morloi llwyd, Halichoerus grypus, ifainc o Sir Benfro\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234313634\tEighteen years (1954-1972) of capture-mark-recapture data of 245 grey seal pups marked with identification tags in Wales.  The data was obtained from paper files held by NRW and supplied originally West Wales Field Society and includes information on the location and number of animals marked in each year including sex and age estimations.-Deunaw mlynedd (1954-1972) o ddata dal-marcio-ail-ddal 245 o forloi llwyd ifainc wedi\u0027u nodi â thagiau adnabod yng Nghymru.  Cafwyd y data o ffeiliau papur a gedwir gan CNC ac a gyflenwyd yn wreiddiol gan Gymdeithas Maes Gorllewin Cymru ac mae\u0027n cynnwys gwybodaeth am leoliad a nifer yr anifeiliaid a farciwyd bob blwyddyn gan gynnwys amcangyfrifon rhyw ac oedran.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © a Hawl Cronfa Ddata Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data gwirfoddolwyr © Cymdeithas Sŵolegol Llundain a © Cymdeithas Maes Gorllewin Cymru.\tSeal pups were marked on beaches around the coast of Pembrokeshire covering over 100 km of National Park coastline.  Seals were captured and marked with rings during the pupping season and were captured opportunistically. From detailed descriptive accounts for each individual, information of each seal’s natal colony, condition, and censorship, as well as an estimated of their date of birth and calculation of the duration of time between mark and recapture was derived.  From original text descriptions of mark locations geolocated accurate positional information in a latitude-longitude format was also derived.  Data collected in 1952 and 1953 were not documented in the paper files. However, a summary of records for 1952-1953 can be found in Hewer (1955).\tKay, W. P., Börger, L., Stringell, T. B., Mortlock, E., Lock, K., Alexander, M., Bull, J. C. (Year) Eighteen years of capture-mark-recapture data of grey seal pups, Halichoerus grypus, from Pembrokeshire, Wales, United Kingdom.\tCopies of the 2 spreadsheets, scanned paper files, detailed metadata and copy of the paper submitted (which ws not  accepted for publicatuion in the end) are stored in the DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mm/Data Publications  Presentations/Seal Data/Seal Ringing Records Skomer.xlsx?d\u003dw26b1116ca7c44a05a578350158932094    Original paper files were given to presentation to Pembrokeshire Archive on 20/1/23 (by Kate Lock)\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Tom Stringell – electronic data   Metadata Point of Contact:  Tom Stringell       This data accompanies a scientific paper to be published via the Journal of ecology.  Data to be published as part of the paper.  We need to  confirm if the data will be held on the JoE platform or whether we need to put a copy on Lle to link to. All the raw records are third party data but NRW has received permission verbally from a rep of the now obsolete West Wales Field Society Malcolm Cullen.  They have requested the paper data be lodged at the Pembrokeshire Achive and therefore become public information.  detailed metadata (required for the JoE) explains the data futher and is stored in the DMS.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Zoological Society London (ZSL)\tOpen Government Licence\t2019-10-28\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2611\t2025-11-12T11:52:06.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18798\tNRW_DS124197\t2024-05-28T12:56:21.014Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEA Environment Management Teams\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234313937\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\EA Environment Management Teams.lyr\t\t\t\t2019-05-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18799\tNRW_DS124283\t2024-05-28T12:56:20.997Z\t577\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Catchments Cycle 2\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234323833\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Catchments Cycle 2 are a polygon dataset collated and defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Catchments are defined as an area of land from which all surface run-off flows through a series of streams, rivers and, possibly, lakes to a particular point in the water course such as a river confluence. It is a layer of the undesignated small coastal catchments and is used alongside the Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 2 dataset to produce outputs showing all the river catchments together.\tThis dataset was originally created for use by co-deliverers before becoming final for Cycle 2 in 2013.\t\tx:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle2\\WFD Coastal Catchments Cycle 2.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-04-25\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:20:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18802\tNRW_DS124436\t2024-05-28T12:56:20.965Z\t622\tValid\tEditable\tAsh Lichen Alert\tRhybuddion Cennau Ynn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234343336\tThese data identify 10x10m, 100x100m and 1000x1000m areas where a Threatened Lichen has been recorded on Ash. The dataset is designed to allow land managers to rapidly identify whether they need to consider rare lichens when working with Ash trees, especially if they are considering felling a tree because of Ash Dieback.-Mae\u0027r data hyn yn nodi ardaloedd 10x10m, 100x100m a 1000x1000m lle cofnodwyd Cen dan Fygythiad ar Ynn. Dyluniwyd y set ddata i ganiatáu i reolwyr tir nodi\u0027n gyflym a oes angen iddynt ystyried cen prin wrth weithio gyda choed Ynn, yn enwedig os ydynt yn ystyried cwympo coeden oherwydd lladdwr yr ynn (Hymenoscyphus pseudoalbidus).  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tMost data are from BLS Rare and Threatened Lichen Records: Wales (British Lichen Society; CC-BY); a minority are from NRW Regional Data: North Wales (Cofnod - North Wales Environmental Information Service; OGL),  Natural Resources Wales Regional Data: Mid-Wales (BIS for Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park; OGL), NRW Regional Data: all taxa (sensitive species only), West Wales (West Wales Biodiversity Information Centre; OGL) and NRW Regional Data: South East Wales Non-sensitive species (South East Wales Biodiversity Records Centre; OGL).  Key  - Red shows where a rare lichen is growing on an ash tree: these are the most important localities for action to prevent damage to a rare lichen.  - Pink shows where a rare lichen that often grows on ash has been recorded, but where the record does not specify the host tree. Consultation with Natural Resources Wales is advisable in these cases, especially in SSSI, because the rare lichens may be growing on ash.  - Yellow shows where a rare lichen that often grows on ash has been recorded on other host trees.\t\tX:\\Incidents\\Forest Health\\Ash lichens alert.lyr X:\\Incidents\\Forest Health\\Ash lichens alert 1km.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-07-29\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3598/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_ash_lichen_alerts\t2025-07-18T14:57:38.314Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18801\tNRW_DS124618\t2024-05-28T12:56:20.896Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW Water Quality analytical flow and Quality Control nformation held in LIMS (Laborartory Information Management System)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363138\tTo be added when supplied from Data Custodian  Scheduling, collection analysis and reporting of all routine monitoring and pollution response samples.  This includes scheduling and analysis of NRW sample as well as a commercial service for third parties (e.g. Utility companies and Environmental Agencies).  The Results of the Water Quality samples are exported to WISKI (see metadata entry NRW_DS119251)\t\t\tLIMS - Swansea Laboratory\tAndrew Wesley, ICT and Data Specialist, Service Delivery\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:52:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18803\tNRW_DS124625\t2024-05-28T12:56:20.914Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tArachnids (spiders \u0026amp; harvestmen) data for Morfa Harlech National Nature Reserve (NNR) 2017-2019\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363235\tMorfa Harlech NNR is a large sand dune system on the west coast of North Wales. In order to improve knowledge of the arachnid fauna of the NNR, four survey visits were made to the reserve in 2017, 2018 and 2019. Arachnids were surveyed from 28 different sample points across the site to gain a broad insight into the fauna. Sampling was largely undertaken using vacuum samplers (G-vac 2-stroke and E-vac Lithium-ion battery powered machines), with hand searching employed in areas with more mobile sand.\t\tGallon, R.C. 2019. A survey of the arachnids (spiders \u0026amp; harvestmen) of Morfa Harlech National Nature Reserve, 2017-2019. NRW Evidence Report Report No: 380, 54pp, Natural Resources Wales, Cardiff.\tRecorder 6?\tMike Howe       Waiting for Mike Howe to provide information about the dataset\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:52:30.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18796\tNRW_DS124626\t2024-05-28T12:56:20.671Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW North Flood Incident Duty Officer Areas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363236\tAreas covered by the various Flood Incident Duty Officers, with regards to flood incident management and operational response.\tAreas are based on agreed NRW administrative boundaries, and may be subject to change in future.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW North Flood Incident Duty Officer Areas.lyr\tEirian Gilford, Warning \u0026amp; Informing Team Leader, NRW Gwyn Moseley, Warning \u0026amp; Informing Advisor 2, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-08-14\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:59:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18846\tNRW_DS124627\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.072Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW North Flood Warning Duty Officer Areas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363237\tAreas covered by the various Flood Warning Duty Officers, with regards to flood monitoring, forecasting and dissemination of alerts/warnings\tAreas are based on agreed NRW administrative boundaries, and may be subject to change in future\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW North Flood Warning Duty Officer Areas.lyr\tEirian Gilford, Warning \u0026amp; Informing Team Leader, NRW Gwyn Moseley, Warning \u0026amp; Informing Advisor 2, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-08-14\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:59:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18848\tNRW_DS124629\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.071Z\t261\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAculeate Hymenoptera and Diptera assemblages at Dyfi National Nature Reserve – Ynyslas Dunes in 2019\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363239\tA survey of the aculeate bees \u0026amp; wasps and flies (Diptera) of Ynyslas Dunes was undertaken in 2019 in an attempt to determine the current condition of this site for these taxonomic groups. A total of 57 aculeate species was recorded, 17 of which were to the dunes resulting in a cumulative total of 119 species now known from the site. Only 40 of the 102 previously-recorded aculeates were refound in 2019. 22 of the 38 aculeate species identified as part of the Ynyslas Dunes dune invertebrate assemblage (currently consisting of 141 invertebrate species) were recorded duringthe current survey, and four recognized dune-dependent species were added to the site list. Lasioglossum xanthopus was recorded from a 2nd Welsh locality and Osmia bicolor was recorded new to VC46, both representing significant range expansions\tFive days were spent on Ynyslas Dunes between 5th May and 2nd August 2019, visiting when suitable warm and sunny conditions were forecast. On each day, at least two entomologists undertook survey work. For recording purposes, the site was divided into three compartments duplicating the recording areas used on the previous surveys.  Specimens were Obtained by hand searching, sweep netting and pan traps set out in various locations within each compartment. Locations of nest aggregations and significant species were recorded using GPS\tJones, N. \u0026amp; Cheeseborough, I. 2019. Survey of the aculeate Hymenoptera and Diptera assemblages at Dyfi National Nature Reserve – Ynyslas Dunes in 2019. NRW Evidence Report No: 381, xxpp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tWelsh Invertebrate Database - Recorder 6\tMike Howe       Mike Howe to supply full metadata\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2019-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:28:11.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18847\tNRW_DS124635\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.186Z\t607\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAculeate Hymenoptera and Diptera assemblages at Morfa Dyffryn NNR and Morfa Harlech NNR, 2018 and 2019\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363335\tA survey of the aculeate bees \u0026amp; wasps and flies (Diptera) of Morfa Dyffryn and Morfa Harlech dunes was undertaken in 2018 and 2019 in an attempt to determine the current condition of the two sites for these taxonomic groups.    At Morfa Dyffryn, 51 aculeate Hymenoptera species were recorded during the survey including one European IUCN Vulnerable bee Colletes fodiens, two Nationally Rarewasps, one Nationally Rare bee, three Nationally Scarce wasps and two Nationally Scarce bees. 32 aculeate Hymenoptera species of the Welsh sand dune invertebrate assemblage were recorded, but seven previously-recorded species were not found.  Three aculeate Hymenoptera species new to the site list of sand dune assemblage species were recorded, including the Nationally Scarce spider wasp Arachnospila wesmaeli. 110 Diptera (true-flies) species were recorded, including 32 recognised as part of the sand dune invertebrate assemblage. Nine dune-dependent fly species were added to the site list, and nine Nationally Scarce flies were recorded of which two were new to Morfa Dyffryn. The overall totals for dune-dependent aculeate Hymenoptera and Diptera now stand at 39 and 44 species respectively.    At Morfa Harlech, 57 aculeate Hymenoptera species were recorded during the survey including one European IUCN Vulnerable bee Colletes fodiens, one Nationally Rare wasp, one Nationally Rare bee, four Nationally Scarce wasps and three Nationally Scarce bees. 29 aculeate Hymenoptera species of the Welsh sand dune invertebrate assemblage were recorded, but ten previously-recorded species were not found. One Nationally Scarce species recognized as part of the dune  assemblage was recorded new to the site list. 128 Diptera (true-flies) species were recorded (including two species recorded in 2017), including 28 recognised as part of the sand dune invertebrate assemblage. Eight dune-dependent fly species were added to the site list, including Six Nationally Scarce and one “Data Deficient” fly.  The overall totals for dune-dependent aculeate Hymenoptera and Diptera now  stand at 38 and 41 species respectively.\tSix days were spent on Morfa Dyffryn and five days were spent on Morfa Harlech between 9th May 2018 and 25th August 2019, visiting when suitable warm and sunny conditions were forecast. On each day, at least two entomologists undertook survey  work.   Both sites are extensive and it was not possible to explore the whole area of either site in a single day, so the survey attempted to sample from key areas for the dune assemblage of aculeate Hymenoptera and Diptera. The approach taken was to focus searches in a variety of situations including dune slacks, particularly around edges of slacks and on raised areas within slacks\tJones, N. \u0026amp; Cheeseborough, I. 2019. Survey of aculeate Hymenoptera and Diptera assemblages at Morfa Dyffryn NNR and Morfa Harlech NNR, 2018 and 2019. NRW Evidence Report No: 382, 42pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tRecorder 6 - Welsh Invertebrate Database\tDr Mike Howe, Lead specialist advisor; terrestrial habitats and species        Mike Howe to Supply full metadata\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2019-12-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T10:07:26.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18854\tNRW_DS124649\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.285Z\t517\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm 33 kV Cables\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363439\tThis dataset shows the Pen y Cymoedd windfarm 33 kV cables. 33kV cables are internal electric cabling come from the wind turbine power systems. They terminate at the sub-station on site. This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tGIS data provided to NRW for the PyC Windfarm project was acquired from various different sources/contractors as discussed below, but essentially is all derived from GPS surveys undertaken at various stages of the construction process:  1. Site Civils (turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, JBBB, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used, and the data was processed, and final drawings issued by Grontmij on behalf of the Principal Contractor in or around July 2018. Drawings were issued as AutoCAD dwg and converted to ArcGIS shape files to OSGB36 coordinate system/projection. 2. On-site cable arrays (33kV cabling): this was installed by subcontractor PSUK and the locations were surveyed at installation using a survey grade GPS, although there was some question about the accuracy of some sections (which may have been surveyed with a lower accuracy hand-held GPS). 3. The Grid Connection Route and Sub-Station Infrastructure: surveyed by grid connection Contractor ABB using survey grade GPS, although some discrepancies were identified when compared to civils as-built drawings.  Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\33KV Cables.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-10-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:00:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18853\tNRW_DS124650\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.273Z\t191\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Access Roads\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363530\tThis dataset shows Pen y Cymoedd Access Roads. The access roads shows forest roads owned and maintained by NRW on the Welsh Government Woodland Estate. This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tGIS data provided to NRW for the PyC Windfarm project was acquired from various different sources/contractors as discussed below, but essentially is all derived from GPS surveys undertaken at various stages of the construction process:  1. Site Civils (turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, JBBB, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used, and the data was processed, and final drawings issued by Grontmij on behalf of the Principal Contractor in or around July 2018. Drawings were issued as AutoCAD dwg and converted to ArcGIS shape files to OSGB36 coordinate system/projection. 2. On-site cable arrays (33kV cabling): this was installed by subcontractor PSUK and the locations were surveyed at installation using a survey grade GPS, although there was some question about the accuracy of some sections (which may have been surveyed with a lower accuracy hand-held GPS). 3. The Grid Connection Route and Sub-Station Infrastructure: surveyed by grid connection Contractor ABB using survey grade GPS, although some discrepancies were identified when compared to civils as-built drawings.  Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Access Roads.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Deliivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-10-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:56:01.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18839\tNRW_DS124651\t2024-05-28T12:56:50.988Z\t247\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Demised Areas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363531\tThis dataset shows Pen y Cymoedd Demised Areas. Demised areas are areas of land ownership that have been transferred by lease over to the leaseholder (Vatenfall), this includes turbine spur roads, substation, turbine bases etc. Vattenfall are therefore fully responsible for the demised areas, this includes maintaining, repairing and granting access to or across these areas. No works can take place in these areas without full consent from Vattenfall. This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tGIS data provided to NRW for the PyC Windfarm project was acquired from various different sources/contractors as discussed below, but essentially is all derived from GPS surveys undertaken at various stages of the construction process:  1. Site Civils (turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, JBBB, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used, and the data was processed, and final drawings issued by Grontmij on behalf of the Principal Contractor in or around July 2018. Drawings were issued as AutoCAD dwg and converted to ArcGIS shape files to OSGB36 coordinate system/projection. 2. On-site cable arrays (33kV cabling): this was installed by subcontractor PSUK and the locations were surveyed at installation using a survey grade GPS, although there was some question about the accuracy of some sections (which may have been surveyed with a lower accuracy hand-held GPS). 3. The Grid Connection Route and Sub-Station Infrastructure: surveyed by grid connection Contractor ABB using survey grade GPS, although some discrepancies were identified when compared to civils as-built drawings.  Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Demised Areas.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Deliivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-10-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:13:58.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18844\tNRW_DS124652\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.185Z\t283\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Export Cable\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363532\tThis dataset shows the Pen y Cymoedd Export Cable (132 kV).  Export cables relate to the 132Kv cable that leaves the Substation once it has been transformed from 33kV. This then runs down to the sub-station in Rhigos where it connects to the National Grid. This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tGIS data provided to NRW for the PyC Windfarm project was acquired from various different sources/contractors as discussed below, but essentially is all derived from GPS surveys undertaken at various stages of the construction process:  1. Site Civils (turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, JBBB, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used, and the data was processed, and final drawings issued by Grontmij on behalf of the Principal Contractor in or around July 2018. Drawings were issued as AutoCAD dwg and converted to ArcGIS shape files to OSGB36 coordinate system/projection. 2. On-site cable arrays (33kV cabling): this was installed by subcontractor PSUK and the locations were surveyed at installation using a survey grade GPS, although there was some question about the accuracy of some sections (which may have been surveyed with a lower accuracy hand-held GPS). 3. The Grid Connection Route and Sub-Station Infrastructure: surveyed by grid connection Contractor ABB using survey grade GPS, although some discrepancies were identified when compared to civils as-built drawings.  Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Export Cable.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Deliivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-10-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:49:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18849\tNRW_DS124653\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.171Z\t268\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Low Voltage Cables\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363533\tThis dataset shows Pen y Cymoedd Live Cables.  This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tGIS data provided to NRW for the PyC Windfarm project was acquired from various different sources/contractors as discussed below, but essentially is all derived from GPS surveys undertaken at various stages of the construction process:  1. Site Civils (turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, JBBB, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used, and the data was processed, and final drawings issued by Grontmij on behalf of the Principal Contractor in or around July 2018. Drawings were issued as AutoCAD dwg and converted to ArcGIS shape files to OSGB36 coordinate system/projection. 2. On-site cable arrays (33kV cabling): this was installed by subcontractor PSUK and the locations were surveyed at installation using a survey grade GPS, although there was some question about the accuracy of some sections (which may have been surveyed with a lower accuracy hand-held GPS). 3. The Grid Connection Route and Sub-Station Infrastructure: surveyed by grid connection Contractor ABB using survey grade GPS, although some discrepancies were identified when compared to civils as-built drawings.  Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Low Voltage Cables.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Deliivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-10-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:55:59.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18841\tNRW_DS124654\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.048Z\t826\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Spur Roads\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363534\tThis dataset shows Pen y Cymoedd Spur Roads. Spur roads are all roads that are used to access the wind turbine power systems. These roads tend to divert from the original forest road system and in places they enter the demised areas. This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tGIS data provided to NRW for the PyC Windfarm project was acquired from various different sources/contractors as discussed below, but essentially is all derived from GPS surveys undertaken at various stages of the construction process:  1. Site Civils (turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, JBBB, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used, and the data was processed, and final drawings issued by Grontmij on behalf of the Principal Contractor in or around July 2018. Drawings were issued as AutoCAD dwg and converted to ArcGIS shape files to OSGB36 coordinate system/projection. 2. On-site cable arrays (33kV cabling): this was installed by subcontractor PSUK and the locations were surveyed at installation using a survey grade GPS, although there was some question about the accuracy of some sections (which may have been surveyed with a lower accuracy hand-held GPS). 3. The Grid Connection Route and Sub-Station Infrastructure: surveyed by grid connection Contractor ABB using survey grade GPS, although some discrepancies were identified when compared to civils as-built drawings.  Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Spur Roads.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Deliivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-10-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:13:58.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18840\tNRW_DS124655\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.017Z\t454\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Substation\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363535\tThis dataset shows Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm Substation Location. The location of the on-site substation. This is where transformers change the 33kV input from the wind turbines to a 132kV rating for use in the National Grid. This the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tGIS data provided to NRW for the PyC Windfarm project was acquired from various different sources/contractors as discussed below, but essentially is all derived from GPS surveys undertaken at various stages of the construction process:  1. Site Civils (turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, JBBB, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used, and the data was processed, and final drawings issued by Grontmij on behalf of the Principal Contractor in or around July 2018. Drawings were issued as AutoCAD dwg and converted to ArcGIS shape files to OSGB36 coordinate system/projection. 2. On-site cable arrays (33kV cabling): this was installed by subcontractor PSUK and the locations were surveyed at installation using a survey grade GPS, although there was some question about the accuracy of some sections (which may have been surveyed with a lower accuracy hand-held GPS). 3. The Grid Connection Route and Sub-Station Infrastructure: surveyed by grid connection Contractor ABB using survey grade GPS, although some discrepancies were identified when compared to civils as-built drawings.  Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Substation.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Deliivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-12-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:55:58.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18845\tNRW_DS124656\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.186Z\t821\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Turbines\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363536\tThis dataset shows Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm (as built) wind turbine locations. This shows all 76 turbines on the Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm. Each turbine is 2MW in power. This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tGIS data provided to NRW for the PyC Windfarm project was acquired from various different sources/contractors as discussed below, but essentially is all derived from GPS surveys undertaken at various stages of the construction process:  1. Site Civils (turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, JBBB, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used, and the data was processed, and final drawings issued by Grontmij on behalf of the Principal Contractor in or around July 2018. Drawings were issued as AutoCAD dwg and converted to ArcGIS shape files to OSGB36 coordinate system/projection. 2. On-site cable arrays (33kV cabling): this was installed by subcontractor PSUK and the locations were surveyed at installation using a survey grade GPS, although there was some question about the accuracy of some sections (which may have been surveyed with a lower accuracy hand-held GPS). 3. The Grid Connection Route and Sub-Station Infrastructure: surveyed by grid connection Contractor ABB using survey grade GPS, although some discrepancies were identified when compared to civils as-built drawings.  Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Turbines.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Deliivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-10-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:13:58.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18851\tNRW_DS124658\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.147Z\t293\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Permanent Masts\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363538\tThis dataset shows Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm (as built) Permanent Mast locations. A measurement tower or met mast is a free standing tower or a removed mast, which carries measuring instruments with meteorological instruments such as thermometers and instruments to measure wind speed. This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tGIS data provided to NRW for the PyC Windfarm project was acquired from various different sources/contractors as discussed below, but essentially is all derived from GPS surveys undertaken at various stages of the construction process:  1. Site Civils (turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, JBBB, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used, and the data was processed, and final drawings issued by Grontmij on behalf of the Principal Contractor in or around July 2018. Drawings were issued as AutoCAD dwg and converted to ArcGIS shape files to OSGB36 coordinate system/projection. 2. On-site cable arrays (33kV cabling): this was installed by subcontractor PSUK and the locations were surveyed at installation using a survey grade GPS, although there was some question about the accuracy of some sections (which may have been surveyed with a lower accuracy hand-held GPS). 3. The Grid Connection Route and Sub-Station Infrastructure: surveyed by grid connection Contractor ABB using survey grade GPS, although some discrepancies were identified when compared to civils as-built drawings.  Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Permanent Masts.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-10-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:56:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18855\tNRW_DS124659\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.307Z\t282\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Main Access\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363539\tThis dataset shows the Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm Main Access. The Main Access shows the agreed access route used by Vattenfall and their contractors/visitors to get to the Vattenfall control centre (the HWB). The control centre is in the vicinity of the substation.  This is the final true data received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tGIS data provided to NRW for the PyC Windfarm project was acquired from various different sources/contractors as discussed below, but essentially is all derived from GPS surveys undertaken at various stages of the construction process:  1. Site Civils (turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, JBBB, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used, and the data was processed, and final drawings issued by Grontmij on behalf of the Principal Contractor in or around July 2018. Drawings were issued as AutoCAD dwg and converted to ArcGIS shape files to OSGB36 coordinate system/projection. 2. On-site cable arrays (33kV cabling): this was installed by subcontractor PSUK and the locations were surveyed at installation using a survey grade GPS, although there was some question about the accuracy of some sections (which may have been surveyed with a lower accuracy hand-held GPS). 3. The Grid Connection Route and Sub-Station Infrastructure: surveyed by grid connection Contractor ABB using survey grade GPS, although some discrepancies were identified when compared to civils as-built drawings.  Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Main Access.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-10-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:00:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18838\tNRW_DS124661\t2024-05-28T12:56:50.844Z\t243\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPen y Cymoedd Windfarm Site Boundary\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363631\tThis shapefile shows Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm Construction Site Boundary. The boundary that was granted by the Local Planning Authority and was used as the Construction Design Management (CDM) area. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Vattenfall) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tGIS data provided to NRW for the PyC Windfarm project was acquired from various different sources/contractors as discussed below, but essentially is all derived from GPS surveys undertaken at various stages of the construction process:  1. Site Civils (turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, JBBB, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used, and the data was processed, and final drawings issued by Grontmij on behalf of the Principal Contractor in or around July 2018. Drawings were issued as AutoCAD dwg and converted to ArcGIS shape files to OSGB36 coordinate system/projection. 2. On-site cable arrays (33kV cabling): this was installed by subcontractor PSUK and the locations were surveyed at installation using a survey grade GPS, although there was some question about the accuracy of some sections (which may have been surveyed with a lower accuracy hand-held GPS). 3. The Grid Connection Route and Sub-Station Infrastructure: surveyed by grid connection Contractor ABB using survey grade GPS, although some discrepancies were identified when compared to civils as-built drawings.  Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pen y Cymoedd Windfarm\\Site Boundary.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-10-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:14:01.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18843\tNRW_DS124663\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.053Z\t1391\tValid\tEditable\tRemaining Landfill Void in Wales\tGofod Tirlenwi Gwag sy’n Weddill yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363633\tPermitted landfill operators have a condition in their permits to report the remaining landfill void (capacity) of their sites at the end of the calendar year. This information although used for compliance purposes is also used by the EC, Government, Local Authorities and other interested parties for statutory reporting and waste planning purposes. Data is provided in cubic metres and collated into a national dataset.  There are only around 20 operational landfills in Wales. Operators can claim commercial confidentiality for their data at time of submission. To date, no operator has claimed commercial confidentiality.-Mewn trwyddedau gweithredwyr tirlenwi a ganiateir ceir amod eu bod yn rhoi gwybod faint o wagle tirlenwi (capasiti) sy\u0027n weddill yn eu safleoedd ar ddiwedd y flwyddyn galendr. Mae’r wybodaeth hon yn cael ei defnyddio at ddibenion cydymffurfio ond hefyd gan y CE, y Llywodraeth, Awdurdodau Lleol a phartïon eraill â diddordeb, at ddibenion adrodd statudol a chynllunio gwastraff. Darperir data mewn metrau ciwbig a\u0027i gydgasglu mewn set ddata genedlaethol. Dim ond tua 20 safle tirlenwi gweithredol sydd yng Nghymru.  Mae\u0027r amcangyfrifon hyn yn seiliedig ar y gwagle diweddaraf a gofnodwyd gan safleoedd tirlenwi gweithredol a gallant fod yn gysylltiedig ag amcangyfrifon blaenorol lle nad yw data\u0027r blynyddoedd cyfredol ar gael. Nid yw ansawdd amcangyfrifon a ddarparwyd gan weithredwyr tirlenwi wedi\u0027u gwirio. Mae\u0027r ffigurau a ddarparwyd gan weithredwyr safleoedd tirlenwi wedi\u0027u haddasu gan CNC i gyfrif am golli gwagle \u0027gwaredu gwastraff\u0027 gwirioneddol oherwydd gofynion peirianneg a gorchuddio (tua 25%).  Nid yw CNC wedi cynhyrchu amcangyfrifon ar gyfer gwagle tirlenwi ar gyfer pob blwyddyn. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn rhoi cipolwg ar y capasiti amcangyfrifedig diweddaraf ac mae’n gyfyngedig. Mae gennym y ffydd mwyaf yn set ddata 2018 gan fod amcangyfrifon diweddaraf y gweithredwyr wedi\u0027u haddasu i ystyried colli capasiti oherwydd gofynion gorchuddio a pheirianneg. Ni chafodd y fethodoleg amgen hon ei chwblhau ar gyfer amcangyfrifon blynyddol blaenorol o wagle tirlenwi oedd yn weddill yng Nghymru. Bwriedir mabwysiadu\u0027r dull amgen hwn ar gyfer diweddariadau yn y dyfodol.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThese estimations are based on the latest reported void space by operational landfills and may relate to previous estimations where the current years data is not available. Estimations provided by landfill operators have not been quality assured. Figures provided by landfill operators have been adjusted by NRW to account for loss of actual ‘waste disposal’ void through engineering and cover requirements (approximately 25%).    NRW has not produced landfill void estimates for all years. This dataset is a snapshot of the latest estimated capacity and has its limitations. We have most confidence in the 2018 dataset because the latest operator estimates have been adjusted to take into consideration loss of capacity owing to cover and engineering requirements. This improved methodology was not completed for previous annual estimates of remaining landfill void in Wales. It is planned to adopt this improved method for future updates.\t\tOriginal data is held in Documents and collated into CRM (PALS) and provided as an csv export.  Extraction is undertaken annually and placed in Citrix ShareFile.\tJohn Fry Lead Specialist Advisor, Waste Policy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2024-12-06\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/3249\t2025-12-19T12:08:49.251Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18852\tNRW_DS124667\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.171Z\t232\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Armoured Crossing Points\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363637\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Armoured Crossing Points. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tThese are the final shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.  Contact the Energy Delivery Team if further information is required\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Armoured Crossing Points.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:13:59.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18850\tNRW_DS124668\t2024-05-28T12:56:51.186Z\t260\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Blade Pad\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363638\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Blade Pad. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tThese are the final shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.  Contact the Energy Delivery Team if further information is required\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Blade Pad.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:13:57.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18837\tNRW_DS124669\t2024-05-28T12:56:50.842Z\t781\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Bridges\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363639\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Bridges. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tThese are the final shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.  Contact the Energy Delivery Team if further information is required\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Bridges.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t20