nformation is required\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Tenants Connecting Cables.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:02:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18877\tNRW_DS124684\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.533Z\t568\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Turbine Foundation\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363834\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Turbine Foundation. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tThese are the final shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.  Contact the Energy Delivery Team if further information is required\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Turbine Foundation.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Dafydd Davies Project Support Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:00:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18879\tNRW_DS124685\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.493Z\t557\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBrechfa Forest West Windfarm - Turbine Location\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363835\tThis shapefile shows Brechfa Forest West windfarm Turbine Location. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tThese are the final shapefiles received from the developer (Innogy) signifying the end of the construction phase.  Contact the Energy Delivery Team if further information is required\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Turbine Location.lyr\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Brechfa Forest West Windfarm\\Turbine Location.lyr\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:00:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18881\tNRW_DS124687\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.514Z\t815\tValid\tEditable\tCommunities at Risk Register 2019\tCofrestr Cymunedau mewn Perygl 2019 (CaRR)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363837\tThe Communities at Risk Register (CaRR) has been developed to provide an objective means of identifying risk and prioritising flood risk management activities at a Wales-wide, community level. It applies a standard methodology across all flood sources to calculate a theoretical ‘danger score’ that allows comparative risks to be quantified and ranked (from High to Low).    The CaRR comprises a spreadsheet that identifies and ranks individual communities for,  1. a natural, ‘undefended’ scenario, and  2. a managed scenario (based on the presence of defences and flood warning).   It is complemented by a GIS layer which defines the individual Community polygons and is attributed with danger scores, community rankings and other metric information.-Cafodd Cofrestr Cymunedau mewn Perygl ei datblygu i ddarparu dull gwrthrychol o nodi perygl a blaenoriaethu gweithgareddau rheoli perygl llifogydd ar lefel gymunedol Cymru gyfan. Mae’n defnyddio methodoleg safonol ar draws yr holl ffynonellau llifogydd i gyfrifo ‘sgôr perygl’ ddamcaniaethol sy’n caniatáu mesur a rhestru peryglon cymharol yn ôl safle (o Uchel i Isel).  Mae’r Gofrestr Cymunedau mewn Perygl (CaRR) 2019 bellach wedi’I ddisodli gan y Gofrestr Cymunedau mewn Perygl (CaRR) 2024 – Cyfredol a gellir mynd ato yma: https://mapdata.llyw.cymru/layers/geonode:nrw_car2024_present_day  Mae’r CaRR yn cynnwys taenlen sy’n nodi ac yn rhestru safle cymunedau unigol gyda golwg ar:  1. senario ‘ddiamddiffyn’ naturiol, a  2. senario liniarol (seiliedig ar bresenoldeb amddiffynfeydd a rhybudd llifogydd).  Mae’n cael ei ategu gan haen GIS sy’n diffinio’r polygonau cymunedol unigol a’i briodoli gan sgorau perygl, rhestrau safle cymunedol a gwybodaeth fetrig arall.  Mae gwybodaeth bellach ar gael yn y metadata - https://metadata.naturalresources.wales/geonetwork/srv/eng/catalog.search#/metadata/NRW_DS124687  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThe CaRR has been created using national-scale model data developed as part of the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) project.  The CaRR ranks communities based on a theoretical danger score which should be considered alongside other factors (such as history of flooding) when prioritising activities.  The methodology incorporates the ‘Depth in Property’ approach to counting the number of properties at flood risk from different sources.\tA brief summary of objectives and methodology is available in the Information Sheets.\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-01-18\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3596/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_communities_at_risk_register\t2025-12-19T14:47:50.317Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18885\tNRW_DS124688\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.530Z\t359\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCaRR 2019 - Addresses at risk\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363838\tThis dataset may have been superseded by the dataset know as Communities at Risk Register (Metadata record NRW_DS124687).  A review of the Communities at Risk Register data is due in 2025.\t\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\CaRR 2019.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis). Metadata Point of Contact: Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:49:21.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18883\tNRW_DS124689\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.530Z\t20\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCaRR 2019 - Wales CaRR Town Areas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363839\tThis dataset may have been superseded by the dataset know as Communities at Risk Register (Metadata record NRW_DS124687).  A review of the Communities at Risk Register data is due in 2025.\t\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\CaRR 2019.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis). Metadata Point of Contact: Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:48:48.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18880\tNRW_DS124692\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.540Z\t1080\tValid\tEditable\tNational Receptor Dataset (NRD) 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363932\tThe National Receptor Dataset (NRD) is a collection of risk receptors, typically residential and commercial property, intended for use in flood and coastal erosion risk management. It is a spatial dataset produced from Ordnance Survey (OS) AddressBase® Premium (ABP) and OS MasterMap® (MM) data with information on property type, floor area and Flood Hazard Research Centre’s Multi-Coloured Manual (MCM) codes attributed.     The version of the NRD described here is the NRD 2018 created as part of the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) project. The NRD 2018 builds on, and supersedes the previous NRD 2014 (and NRD 2011 before this).   Production of the dataset was streamlined as part of the FRAW project whilst maintaining conceptual consistency. Several additional data attributes have been added to facilitate flood damage calculations in the impacts analysis element of the FRAW project. Consequently, the NRD 2018 comprises two parts – a base NRD which has a very similar structure to NRD 2014, and an additional attribute table with extra information useful for impacts analysis and economic damage calculations.    Several standard NRD and non-standard attributes have been updated to support the national assessment of flood risk.  Some aspects of this are bespoke for Wales and may differ to the NRD datasets used in England.  Further details of this are included in the project report, but the main changes are:     - MCM code – addition of a caravan and bungalow code   - Settlement name – name of settlement to aid understanding   - Ground level – based on analysis from from the DTM   - Property value – creation of a residential property value    - Property classification – attribute based on MCM codes and JBA analysis    - Population – required for basic reporting of flood metrics and for Danger score calculation within NRW’s Communities at Risk Register, or ‘CaRR’   - SFVI_CAT2a – to identify communities that are more vulnerable to the adverse health and social effects associated with floods   - Individual property protection   - Warning and alert areas   - Defended areas and SoP\tNRD 2018 is derived from two sources: OS ABP and OS MM. Updates to both are released every 6 weeks. NRD2018 is therefore a snapshot of a particular moment in time and contains data that was sourced at the beginning of 2018 (for full details refer to Table Q-5 of FRAW Project Report).   NRD 2018 has not been designed for addressing purposes and we don’t recommend using it for this, mainly because it may not be up-to-date. We recommend the OS ABP instead.   NRD 2018 has been compiled to provide a general source of receptor information for FRAW and a range of other FCRM activities; you will need to decide whether it is appropriate for your specific application.   For local studies, you will need to check whether any more detailed local information is available, and whether it would be more relevant to the work being undertaken.   Please note that in NRD 2018 not all records are for properties and may represent other features such as car parks, playing fields and electricity sub-stations. For further details refer to 2018s0011 - FRAW Project Report - Appendix T03 - NRD2018_Exclusions_v2.xlsx. and associated FRAW Project Reports.\tAdditional reference material available comprising:  - NRD Information Sheet  - 2018s0011 - FRAW Project Report - Appendix T03 - NRD2018_Exclusions_v2.xlsx  - FRAW Project Report Appendix Q – Creation of the National Receptor Dataset (NRD)    Information Warning: In order to correlate the 2018 spatial data with the the 2014 spatial data, users will need to reference the additional spreadsheet 2018s0011 - FRAW Project Report - Appendix T03 - NRD2018_Exclusions_v2.\tx:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Receptors Database (NRD) 2018.lyr  x:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Receptors Database (NRD) 2018 from OSMM.lyr   Spreadsheet: 2018s0011 - FRAW Project Report - Appendix T03 - NRD2018_Exclusions_v2.xlsx (also knows as AddressBase_products_classification_scheme_nrd2018_AEP7.xlsx), this spreadsheet is required for anyone who wants tocompare the 2018 NDR with previous versions of the data\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh, Principal Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2019-08-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:50:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18884\tNRW_DS124694\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.510Z\t355\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAn assessment of the condition of the Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior at Whiteford Burrows NNR, October 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363934\tSingle day assessment visit was undertaken in October 2018. which allowed an overview site-survey, combined with off-site bulk sample analysis, to assess most of the sand dune/salt marsh transition zone and allowing the field assessment of the snail at 11 sites, which also involved the removal of bulk vegetation samples for off-site determination of snail presence. The results revealed V. angustior to be present at seven sites including several in the northern transition zone, an area in which the snail had not been recorded previously and which had been overlooked for study since the 1993 survey. The 2018 results demonstrated the presence of the snail over a linear distance of 1.9km extending the previously recorded range by 1.2 km, a 171% increase\tSite survey involved:  1-visual examination of vegetation and ground litter  2-beating vegetation onto a white gridded tray and examining debris with a X6 magnifier;  3-shaking dry moss and low growing fresh and dead plant material through a two tier 2mm and 0.5mm sieve stack\tWilling, M.J. 2020. An assessment of the condition of the Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior at Whiteford Burrows NNR, October 2018. NRW Evidence Report No. 395\tReport to be stored in DMS Reports Archive Data to be stored in the Wales Invertebrate Database (WID) in Recorder 6  -\tMike Howe       Metadata to be supplied and data location of raw spreadsheets to be confirmed.  Date to be entered into Recorder 6 - 15/01/2019\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T16:21:08.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18867\tNRW_DS124695\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.406Z\t603\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAnchoring, mooring and launching spatial distribution in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234363935\tAnchoring, mooring and launching of recreational vessel has the potential to damage benthic marine habitats. The impact and significance of these activities depend on a number of factors including the intensity of the activity and the sensitivity of underlying habitats. Defining the specific locations in which these activities occur is integral to determining the potential for interactions with sensitive features. The data collection has been commissioned under the Wales non-licensable activities project and provides a robust dataset from a wide range of sources on the location of anchoring, mooring and launching sites in Wales from the high-water mark to 12nm offshore.  The collated data was sent to a range of knowledgeable marine stakeholders for consultation to ensure that the polygons accurately represent the anchoring, mooring and launching areas and that no additional areas had been missed.  A range of intensity metrics were used to allocate a value of intensity to each polygon for anchoring, mooring and launching.  This includes extracting values from AIS data from the Royal Yachting Association, proximity to sailing clubs and centres and consultation with stakeholders.   The dataset comprises 4 GIS layers; • Anchoring • Mooring • Launching • No-anchoring\tThis GIS dataset has been compiled by consultants APEM ltd. from a number of different sources, including;  • The Crown Estate   • Large scale hydrographic charts • NRW datasets  • Defra  • OceanWise: Marine Vector Charts (UK Hydrographic Office s-57) • United Kingdom Hydrographic Office (UKHO) • Boatlaunch website • The RYA UK Coastal Atlas of Recreational Boating (Version 2)  A stepwise approach was taken by APEM for the data collection and amalgamation required for production of each of the four layers; anchoring, mooring, launching and no-anchoring.   In each case, the GIS data sources were transformed into the same projection (WGS84).  The Arc catalogue plugin X-ray was used to rearrange the table structure to match the preferred table structure (with the addition of an “Additional notes column” for mooring and launching.\t\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Anchoring Mooring and Launching\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor -Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-01-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18874\tNRW_DS124706\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.499Z\t8\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW Flood Technical Services areas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373036\tMap of Wales split into 2 sections, each section covers the area of responsibility for each of the Flood technical services team area.\tSource material: changes are updated via the GIS system\t\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Flood Technical Services areas.lyr\tLucy Edwards\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-10-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18876\tNRW_DS124730\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.518Z\t297\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBaseline Project – Pressures Contract\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373330\tDevelopment and application of a methodology to assess the anthropogenic pressures acting upon independently qualifying features of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales.\t1. To identify all the key anthropogenic pressures acting on the independently qualifying features in Welsh SSSIs and the key sources of evidence related to each anthropogenic pressure. 2. To link the anthropogenic pressures  acting upon independently qualifying features to individual management units. 3. To consider the severity of the anthropogenic pressures identified. 4. An exploratory exercise that may be subject to further internal review, re-assessment and further quality assurance as methods and technical understanding develops.\t\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/sites/smar/stratplan/tsg/Programmes/Terrestrial%20Transition%20Planning/Task%2002%20-%20Baseline/Baseline%20Project%20-%20Pressures%20contract/Contract%20final%20products?csf\u003d1\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18871\tNRW_DS124732\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.519Z\t654\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOtter Road Mortality Survey (ORMS)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373332\tThe Otter Road Mortality Survey (ORMS) database was initiated after the UK Otter Biodiversity Action Plan (which arose from the Rio Summit of 1992) identified that otter road deaths were a significant factor causing loss or decline in otter populations in the UK. The database was initiated in association with a report commissioned to identify blackspots in Wales (written by Geoff Liles and published in March 2000).  Members of the Public who spot dead otters and report them were originally asked to complete a paper form to provide observational details. These were subsequently recorded in the ORMS Access database.\tThis is data extracted from a MS Access database that is being decommissioned and turned into a Geodatabase and GIS web form interface.  Data consists of: 1. MS Access Database - to be decommissioned and archived.  MS access database reference number 05391 2. Paper survey forms including hand drawn sketches - to be sent to off-site storage (and potentially digitised at a later date) 3. Replacement Geodatabase and associated .lyr files to be made available on GIS  While the original data has been preserved, an ArcGIS Online application is currently in development and will permit the Public to submit their own sightings via a GIS form that will continue to develop our understanding. Both datasets (old and new) can be combined on request.\t\tX Drive GIS Layer: X:\\Biological\\Otter_Road_Mortality_Survey_1971_To_2021.lyr  DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/mam/Cardiff%20University%20Otter%20Project/ORMS%20Access%20Database%20Export%20-%20v3.xlsx?d\u003dw1bbd568441bc42ab93330890c936c318 MS PowerBI: https://app.powerbi.com/groups/20b3821f-8d6d-445e-a3b2-928cd79199d9/reports/5f564b08-4cf7-4b0c-aa3b-0a726901cc9e?ctid\u003d8865ef0f-acde-487c-bf17-5cb50375d757 ArcGIS Online:https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/518736dfc090454f83bbd905bf732ff3 MS Access: L:\\EAW\\Legacy Databases\\OtterRoadMortality\\ORMDb.mdb Paper files: Basement office in boxes on shelves behind Janet Buckles old desk\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Internal Custodian: Hilary Foster - Specialist Advisor: Freshwater Habitats and Species, Freshwater Habitats \u0026amp; Species Team Internal Contact: Olly Ishmael, Advisor 2,  Geospatial Services (Formerly Janet Buckles - Retired)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-07-14\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:55.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18878\tNRW_DS124733\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.522Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Coppice Mawr, Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2019\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373333\t\t\tBoyce, D. 2020. Coppice Mawr saproxylic invertebrate survey 2019. NRW Evidence Report No. 403\tData to be entered into Recorder 6 (Survey ID to be added)\tMike Howe\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:02:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18866\tNRW_DS124734\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.267Z\t624\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveys for Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch SSSI, Geyer’s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Cors Geirch SSSI \u0026amp; Cors Erddreiniog SSSI, 2019\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373334\tGeyer’s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri has been recorded from three sites in Wales. However, surveys of Cors Erddreiniog SSSI in 2016 and of Cors Geirch SSSI in 2016 and 2017 failed to locate the snail. It was feared that V. geyeri had been lost from both sites but, as small populations of this tiny snail can easily be overlooked, it was felt premature to declare the snail as extinct and additional surveys were therefore undertaken at both sites in October 2019.   The survey focussed upon two areas of potentially suitable habitat that had been recently surveyed;  (1) an area of horse-grazed ‘Carex-lawn’ within the National Nature Reserve where V. geyeri had been reported in 1998 and  (2) a series of base-rich flushes lying towards the western end of the National Nature Reserve last surveyed in 2016.\tSurveys for Vertigo geyeri were undertaken on Cors Erddreiniog on 14th and 16th October 2019 and on Cors Geirch on 15th October 2019. Sampling relied primarily upon the well-established technique of selecting potentially  suitable habitat and then removing sample material for later laboratory processing as this tiny snail is typically very difficult to locate in the field.\tWilling, M. J. 2020. Surveys for Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana on Cors Geirch SSSI and for Geyer’s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Cors Geirch SSSI \u0026amp; Cors Erddreiniog SSSI in 2019. NRW Evidence Report No. 404.\tAdded to Recorder 6 on the Welsh Invertebrate Database\tMike Howe\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T10:09:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18869\tNRW_DS124735\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.521Z\t652\tUnassessed\tEditable\tStatus of Dillwyn’s Dung Beetle Onthophagus nuchicornis, Morfa Harlech 2019\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373335\tDillwyn’s Dung Beetle Onthophagus nuchicornis was formerly local but widespread across England and Wales. It has declined dramatically in England and since 2000 is extant at just five of the forty known locations. In Wales, this species has fared better but is restricted to grazed coastal sand dune systems. Historically known from fifteen sites, it was still present at half of these in 2017. The beetle feeds on the dung of a range of mammals but is probably dependent for breeding on medium to large herbivore dung such as cattle, ponies and sheep.    A survey in 2017 of all the known sites in Wales found Onthophagus nuchicornis at seven localities – Kenfig Burrows (975 adults), Merthyr Mawr Warren (36), Morfa Harlech (1), Oxwich Burrows (4), Pembrey Burrows (32), Pennard Burrows (4) and Whiteford Burrows (5). The authors regard the populations at Kenfig Burrows and Methyr Mawr to be the best in the UK. Elsewhere, absence and low population levels are strongly correlated to the cessation of grazing and the quantity and quality of available dung on sand.  Following the 2017 survey, a more intensive survey of Morfa Harlech, which represents a northern outlier for the beetle, was undertaken in 2019 to determine the status of Onthophagus nuchicornis at this site.\tThe aim of the survey was to determine the current status and distribution of the dung beetle Onthophagus nuchicornis on Morfa Harlech dunes and to recommend appropriate management strategies to encourage the conservation of O. nuchicornis.  Field work was carried out between 15th and 20th July 2019. An extensive survey across the NNR and SSSI areas of Morfa Harlech was undertaken. Weather  conditions were favourable throughout the survey period, except for last day when it rained heavily. A range of sampling techniques were used during the survey, these were: a visual search, dung sieving and baited pitfall traps.\tMann, C.M. \u0026amp; Mann, D.J. 2020. The status of Dillwyn’s Dung Beetle Onthophagus nuchicornis on Morfa Harlech in 2019. NRW Evidence Report No: 421, 29pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tRecorder 6 - WID\tMike Howe\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T10:16:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18865\tNRW_DS124736\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.885Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tArachnids (spiders \u0026amp; harvestmen) data for Morfa Dyffryn National Nature Reserve (NNR) 2017-2019\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373336\t\t\tGallon, R.C. 2020. A survey of the arachnids (spiders \u0026amp; harvestmen) of Morfa Dyffryn National Nature Reserve, 2017–2019. NRW Evidence Report Report No: 422, 50pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tData to be added to Recorder 6 - WID (Survey ID to be added)\tMike Howe\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:49:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18882\tNRW_DS124737\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.533Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSeascape and visual sensitivity to Offshore wind farms in Wales: Low and Medium effect buffers\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373337\tThese GIS files were produced as part of a project looking at seascape and visual sensitivity to future offshore windfarm development off the coast of Wales.   The aim of the project was to improve the evidence base and update our advice to reflect the likely scale of new offshore windfarm developments. The project had three stages which looked at the following topics:   Stage 1 - The relationship between distance of offshore turbine away from a sensitive receptor and magnitude of visual effects  Stage 2 - Offshore wind farm siting and design guidelines in relation to seascapes  Stage 3 – Visual sensitivity of the marine settings of Wales’s Designated Landscapes to offshore wind farms.   As part of stage 1, GIS maps of the Welsh marine area were produced which showed the proposed buffers for offshore windfarms with low and medium magnitude visual effects. As part of stage 3, the seascape was divided into 15 zones and a summary of the sensitivity of each zone to offshore windfarm development provided. A GIS map shows the location and boundaries of these zones.\tInformation Warning - all data should only be used in conjunction with NRW Evidence Reports 315, 330, 331 that provide context to the data supplied.\tWhite, S. Michaels, S. King, H. 2019. Seascape and visual sensitivity to offshore wind farms in Wales: Strategic assessment and guidance. Stage 1. NRW Evidence Series. Report No: 315, 94pp, NRW, Bangor.    White, S. Michaels, S. King, H. 2019. Seascape and visual sensitivity to offshore wind farms in Wales: Strategic assessment and guidance. Stage 2. NRW Evidence Series. Report No: 330, 29pp, NRW, Bangor.    White, S. Michaels, S. King, H. 2019. Seascape and visual sensitivity to offshore wind farms in Wales: Strategic assessment and guidance. Stage 3.  NRW Evidence Series. Report No: 331, 96pp, NRW, Bangor.\tTo be stored on the X drive.  Has been emailed to Keith Jones 03/0382020 My understanding is that the data is only partially loaded onto the X drive now (as per email from John Briggs in Nov 2020), Low and Medium buffer layers have been uploaded but not Seascape visual sensitivity layer. All layers are otherwise stored in https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/advice/mar/ind/Impact%20Assessment/OWF%20-%20Visual%20Impacts%20Assessment/Seascapes%20contract%202018/GIS%20files?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1  X:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascape and visual sensitivity to Offshore Windfarms\\Buffers for Low magnitude of effect.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Seascapes\\Seascape and visual sensitivity to Offshore Windfarms\\Buffers for Medium magnitude of effect.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Lucie Skates (Lead Specialist Advisor Marine Planning Implementation - Sustainable Places Land \u0026amp; Sea) and John Briggs (Specialist Advisor, Landscape - Planning, Landscape, Energy,De-carbonisation)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Lucie Skates\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:26:30.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18873\tNRW_DS124741\t2024-05-28T12:57:23.454Z\t400\tValid\tEditable\tCarbon sequestration potential of marine habitats\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373431\tBlue carbon is the carbon stored and sequestered by marine habitats. Related terms can be defined as follows:  • A carbon sink is a natural or artificial reservoir that accumulates and stores carbon. • A carbon store encompasses the carbon stored in the water column, surface sediments and floral and faunal biomass. This storage may be short or long-term.  Where carbon is stored in the long-term, it can be considered to be sequestered.  In order to estimate the blue carbon potential of Welsh waters, and thus allow a greater understanding of how Welsh marine ecosystems contribute to offsetting the release of carbon through human activities, the following steps have been undertaken for this study:  • The best and most up to date available spatial datalayers for blue carbon habitats have been identified and combined so that approximate Welsh coverage could be determined for each. • The relevant literature has been reviewed to obtain the most relevant carbon storage and sequestration values which would then be applied to Welsh marine habitats. • A dedicated numerical model has been interrogated to obtain (1) water column carbon estimates (including plankton biomass); (2) estimates on air-sea flux of Carbon Dioxide (CO2) (i.e. how much CO2 enters the water column and is converted into various forms of carbon), as well as (3) estimates of carbon flux across the Welsh marine boundary (i.e. how much carbon leaves Welsh waters and gets transported to adjacent shelf and deeper waters). • Riverine inputs of carbon have also been estimated based on literature values and average river discharges supplied by NRW hydrologists. • Carbon values have been calculated and put into context with terrestrial (‘green carbon’) rates, as well as Welsh CO2 emissions.\tThis data was created in a number of stages:  • Up-to-date information was collated on the carbon storage and sequestration potential (and rates) of relevant / mappable potential sinks in Welsh waters through a literature and data review.  • This information has then been used to parameterize a simple method which draws on datalayers to derive the spatial distribution of the relevant habitats. The information on storage and sequestration potential has been cross-checked against a basic carbon budget model for Welsh waters that draws on the outputs from a sophisticated numerical model and wider sources. • The derived carbon sequestration values have also been monetised and set into a Welsh context.   Full methodologies and data processing is listed in NRW Evidence Report 428 (Appendix B). There is also a detailed data processing spreadsheet which accompanies the data.  Limitations to the data have been noted, for example in relation to carbon fluxes into and out of the Welsh marine area, uncertainties in habitat distribution and in relation to some sequestration rates.\tHull, S., Armstrong, S., Pearson, Z., Kay, S. 2020. Estimating the Carbon Sink Potential of the Welsh Marine Environment. NRW, NRW Evidence Report Series Report No 428: Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tY:\\SharedProjects\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Karen_Robinson\\Blue carbon project\tInternal Data Custodian:  Karen Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor - Marine Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:02:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18861\tNRW_DS124750\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.814Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSouthern Damselfly Coenagrion mercuriale Habitat Creation and Restoration, Pembrokeshire from 2020 onwards\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373530\tData is likley to be a CD with QGIS files showing the location of all recommended and potential habitat restoration areas Needs to be transformed and placed on the X drive  *****************report number to be provided******************************\t\tJon Hudson Ecological Consultancy. 2020. Habitat Creation and Restoration Work Plan for Southern Damselfly Coenagrion mercuriale in Pembrokeshire from 2020 onwards. NRW Evidence Report No: 4XX, XXpp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tData to to provided\tData Custodian:  Mike Howe\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:52:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18863\tNRW_DS124753\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.885Z\t32\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNational Flood Asset Dataset for Wales: NRW flood Assets held in AMX-Flood\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373533\tmetadata to be provided by custodian.   AMX flood asset data store contains data that has been split into three types according to data owner (NRW, EA, others)\t\t\tAMX\tRichard Kelland - now moved to new role\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:14:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18864\tNRW_DS124755\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.886Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tEC Bathing Waters Classification\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373535\t\tExtracted from WISKI - KwiQM\tThe same information is also available for EC sites\tX:\\EU Directives\\Bathing Waters Directive\\EC Bathing Waters Classification.lyr    Original data is held in WISKI - KwiQM (see metadata entry116227)\tHelena Sykes?\t\t\t2021-11-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18862\tNRW_DS124756\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.814Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAssessment of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) Landscape Rhos Glyn-yr-helyg (2019)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373536\tMetadata to be supplied (data will be added to the WID in Recorder 6) *************GIS layer may be supplied by Butterfly conservation for updated map of Wales - this would be new third party data********** and could potentially replace or add to data held under X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Invertebrates\\Insects\t\tButterfly Conservation Wales. 2020. Marsh Fritillary Rhos Glyn-yr-helyg Landscape Assessment 2019. NRW Evidence Report No: 4XX, XXpp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tRecorder 6 - WID\tMike Howe\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18859\tNRW_DS124759\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.644Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Adaptability Habitat Recovery Period\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373539\tThe rate at which a habitat can recover back to a steady, stable state from a disturbance event is an inherent property of ecosystem resilience. Habitats that have an inherent ability to re-establish quickly following disturbance have a higher likelihood of persisting because they can re-establish rapidly and before the next disturbance event occurs. For example, wet woodlands can recover relatively quickly from flood events (providing this flooding stays within the range of historic limits), whilst ancient woodlands may take many hundreds of years to re-create if lost. The effect that recovery periods may have on ecosystem resilience is explored through this factor.\tFor this factor, all Phase 1 habitats were scored with regards to the expected time taken to reach their desirable species composition after a disturbance event cleared the area completely. This was considered the most objective way to compare the successional behaviour of different habitats, as it gives all habitats a similar starting point: Bare land that has previously contained the habitat in question. The values derived be assessing the Phase 1 data thusly were than averaged to the 1 km reporting squares.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-04-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T10:17:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18860\tNRW_DS124760\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.686Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Adaptability Overall\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373630\tThis dataset shows resilience based on adaptability, using a range of factors contributing to this attribute.\tTo combine the individual factors into attributes, the relevant attribute columns from all factors were joined to the reporting squares. The average is calculated as the sum of all non-NoData factors divided by the count of all non-NoData factors.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-04-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T10:18:46.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18857\tNRW_DS124762\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.472Z\t720\tValid\tEditable\tSSSI NH3 Critical Levels\tdordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGAau)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373632\tThis layer provides a Critical Level (CLe) for ammonia against which impacts on a SSSI should be assessed. The value is the lowest CLe for a feature on that site. The layer gives the SSSI name, SSSI code and ammonia Critical Level. The ammonia CLe can be: 1: a 1µg/m3 NH3 Critical Level indicates a SSSI has N-sensitive bryophytes/lichens as a feature or has bryophytes/lichens as key components of a habitat feature of that SSSI: 3: a 3µg/m3 NH3 Critical Level indicates a SSSI has N-sensitive vegetation, or has invertebrate features that are dependent on N-sensitive vegetation. NS: all features on a site are considered not to be particularly sensitive to ammonia; this does not mean that other aspects of a development will not have negative impacts on a SSSI, for example through water pollution or disturbance.-Mae\u0027r haen hon yn dangos y lefel gritigol (CLe) ar gyfer amonia y dylid asesu effeithiau ar SoDdGA yn ei herbyn. Y gwerth a nodir yw\u0027r gwerth CLe isaf ar gyfer nodwedd ar y safle hwnnw. Mae\u0027r haen yn cynnwys enw\u0027r SoDdGA, cod y SoDdGA a\u0027r lefel gritigol ar gyfer amonia. Gall y CLe ar gyfer amonia fod yn: 1: mae lefel gritigol NH3 o 1µg/m3 yn dangos bod bryoffytau/cennau sy\u0027n sensitif i nitrogen yn nodweddiadol o\u0027r SoDdGA, neu fod bryoffytau/cennau\u0027n gydrannau allweddol o nodwedd cynefin y SoDdGA hwnnw; 3: mae lefel gritigol NH3 o 3µg/m3 yn dangos bod gan SoDdGA lystyfiant sy\u0027n sensitif i nitrogen, neu nodweddion infertebrat y maent yn ddibynnol ar lystyfiant sy\u0027n sensitif i nitrogen; NS: ystyrir bod yr holl nodweddion ar safle heb fod yn enwedig o sensitif i amonia; nid yw hyn yn golygu na fydd agweddau eraill ar ddatblygiad yn cael effaith negyddol ar SoDdGA, er enghraifft drwy lygredd dŵr neu darfu.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThe dataset is based on the SSSI GIS Dataset (metadata number NRW_DS98776) with Critical Levels determined by the Terrestrial Ecosystems and Species Team.\t\tx:\\Resource Management\\Air Quality\\SSSI NH3 Critical Levels.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species  Metadata Point of Contact: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-08-03\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3583/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_sssi_nh3_cl\t2025-12-19T10:33:12.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18858\tNRW_DS124763\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.621Z\t684\tValid\tEditable\tNH3 Sensitive Ancient Woodlands\tCoetiroedd Hynafol Sensitif o ran NH3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373633\tThis layer identifies areas of Ancient Woodland and Parkland from which NRW hold recent, auditable records of N-sensitive lichens. Not all lichens are sensitive to increased ammonia: the 62 species used to identify N-sensitive Ancient Woodland and Parkland are particularly sensitive and are important in the woodland/parkland ecosystem. Any area identified as sensitive in this GIS layer should be assessed using a 1µg/m3 NH3 Critical Level.-Mae\u0027r haen hon yn nodi ardaloedd o goetir hynafol a pharcdir y mae CNC yn cadw cofnodion diweddar ohonynt, y mae modd eu harchwilio, ar gennau sy\u0027n sensitif i nitrogen. Nid yw pob math o gen yn sensitif i gynnydd mewn amonia: mae\u0027r 62 o rywogaethau a ddefnyddiwyd i nodi ardaloedd o goetir hynafol a pharcdir yn enwedig o sensitif i nitrogen, ac maent yn rhan bwysig o ecosystem y coetir/parcdir. Dylid asesu unrhyw ardal y nodwyd ei bod yn sensitif yn yr haen Systemau Gwybodaeth Ddaearyddol hon gan ddefnyddio lefel gritigol NH3 o 1µg/m3  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThe dataset is based on the Ancient Woodland Inventory 2011 (https://metadata.naturalresources.wales/geonetwork/srv/eng/catalog.search#/metadata/EXT_DS116442) and Cadw: Historic Parks and Gardens (https://metadata.naturalresources.wales/geonetwork/srv/eng/catalog.search#/metadata/EXT_DS111947), with polygons selected if they contain a recent record of one of 62 N-sensitive lichens using lichen data from the British Lichen Society.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Air Quality\\NH3 sensitive Ancient Woodlands.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata Point of Contact: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-08-03\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3513/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_n_sensitive_wood\t2025-05-02T15:16:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18856\tNRW_DS124764\t2024-05-28T12:57:22.386Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Condition INNS\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373634\tInvasive non-native species (INNS) can pose a threat to ecosystem resilience throughout Wales.  The output shows relative resilience as high for squares where very few unique INNS were recorded.\tFor this factor, a dataset created by NRW was used. This dataset was created by combining all records of INNS and counting the number of unique species per reporting square. This project has then been normalised to a relative square, with the highest count occurring in the input dataset translating to 100.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:02:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18905\tNRW_DS124765\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.155Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Condition Overall\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373635\tThis dataset shows resilience based on condition, using a range of factors contributing to this attribute.\tTo combine the individual factors into attributes, the relevant attribute columns from all factors were joined to the reporting squares. The average is calculated as the sum of all non-NoData factors divided by the count of all non-NoData factors.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18909\tNRW_DS124766\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.125Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Condition PosPlantIndicators\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373636\tFor many habitats, specific species associated with the habitat are known to only occur if the habitat is in good overall condition. These species can be considered indicators of good habitat condition.    This factor shows on a relative scale the diversity of positive plant indicator species, associated with a given habitat, are present within that habitat group for each 1 km reporting square.\tDue to the limitations inherently associated with most types of species monitoring, this project utilised data produced by CEH (Maskell et al., 2016). Detailed field work obtained through the Countryside Survey was used to facilitate interpolation of the data collected for the sample squares across the whole of Wales using various environmental factors. Details on the methodology used to create this data can be obtained from the CEH website (Maskell et al., 2016).   For this project, the data was normalised to a relative scale from 0 to 100, with the highest value in the original dataset having been translated to 100. To strengthen the focus of the CEH dataset on individual habitats, the Phase 1 habitat data for Wales was grouped into broad habitats (using the broad habitat classes used for the CEH work), and values scaled within these groups, so that the highest CEH value found within each habitat group translated to 100 in the final dataset, and the lowest CEH value found translated to 0.   Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18908\tNRW_DS124767\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.193Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Condition PressureOnAir Acidification\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373637\tAir is one of the most important supporting features of ecosystem resilience. Clean air affects every habitat, from freshwater to forests and upland habitats.  On a relative scale from 0 to 100, the output shows which areas are the most resilient to an increase in air pollution, i.e. areas where an increase in air pollutant emissions is less likely to have an immediate effect on ecosystem condition. In interpreting this dataset, it is important to note that values are based on emission of pollutants, not on concentration of pollutants in the air.\tAmmonia, nitrogen oxides, and sulphur dioxide were selected as gases contributing to acidification (Eurostat, 2017) and emission data for these gases obtained from the National Atmospheric Emissions Inventory (NAEI). Emission values were converted to a relative scale from 0 to 100, based on the ranges used in the NAEI web viewer (NAEI, 2020). The values were then averaged for a combined estimate of emission of pollutants contributing to acidification and inverted to give a resilience estimate, with areas with low emissions being considered of high resilience.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:08:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18900\tNRW_DS124769\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.148Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Condition PressureOnAir OzoneAmmonia\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373639\tAir is one of the most important supporting features of ecosystem resilience. Clean air affects every habitat, from freshwater to forests and upland habitats.     On a relative scale from 0 to 100, the output shows which areas are the most resilient to an increase in air pollution, i.e. areas where an increase in air pollutant emissions is less likely to have an immediate effect on ecosystem condition. In interpreting this dataset, it is important to note that values are based on emission of pollutants, not on concentration of pollutants in the air.\tThe gases included for this factor were ammonia and nitrous oxide (Eurostat, 2017) and emission data for these gases obtained from the National Atmospheric Emissions Inventory (NAEI). Emission values were converted to a relative scale from 0 to 100, based on the ranges used in the NAEI web viewer (NAEI, 2020). The values were then averaged for a combined estimate of emission of these pollutants and inverted to give a resilience estimate, with areas with low emissions being considered of high resilience.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:04:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18904\tNRW_DS124770\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.184Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Condition SACs\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373730\tDue to European reporting requirements, SACs are amongst the best monitored conservation designations in Wales, making them one of the few areas with direct measurements of condition acquired through field work. In field assessments of habitat condition are one of, if not the, most valuable contribution to the attribute Condition.    The output shows the percentage of SAC features in favourable condition within the area; areas outside of SACs take a NoData value.\tTo maximise the number of SACs with assessment, all assessment rounds recorded in NRWs SAC condition spreadsheet (1st round, 2nd round, 3rd round, and most recent assessment) were consolidated, using the latest possible assessment available for each feature (e.g. if no assessment was recorded under most recent assessment, the 3rd round was used, if this was also not assessed, the 2nd round was used, etc.). These feature assessments needed to be summarised to the site level, as most SACs are designated for multiple features of conservation importance that are not recorded spatially. For this project, SAC condition summaries were generated by calculating the percentage of features associated with the site that are in favourable condition. For this calculation, features without assessment were treated as non-existent. The site condition, expressed as percentage of favourable features, was then joined to the spatial mapping of SACs and summarised to the reporting squares by calculating an area weighted average of the favourable percentages of all SACs falling within the reporting square.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T10:19:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18907\tNRW_DS124771\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.142Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Condition SoilErosionRisk\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373731\tSoil is one of the most fundamental factors underlying ecosystem health.  The output shows erosion risk on a relative scale from 0 to 100 from a purely soil-centric perspective; the values express how resilient the soil would be without landcover, and important consideration when considering the soils; resilience to land use change. Areas with peaty soils show as NoData areas in this factor, as the erodibility of peat is driven by the condition of the landcover to such an extent that the DEFRA methodology was not applicable to these soil types.\tIn line with a methodology developed by DEFRA (DEFRA, 2005) erosion risk is considered as being driven by the texture of the soil itself and the slope the soil is located on. The individual texture classes given in the soils of wales dataset were grouped into the texture classes used in the DEFRA methodology. Via overlie analysis with slope, derived from SRTM data, erosion risk groups were assigned and then averaged out over the one km reporting squares.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:08:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18896\tNRW_DS124772\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.134Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Condition WaterQuality\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373732\tWater is a supporting abiotic component of functioning habitats. The quality of the water available is not only intrinsically important for the flora and fauna living directly within water ways, but also has a wider effect on the habitats drawing from these water sources. For this project, river water quality was chosen as a factor, as rivers permeate throughout Wales, thereby having a large influence on many ecosystems.  The output shows the relative resilience of each reporting square based on the condition of the freshwater available to the area through rivers. As this data was originally collected on a catchment-by-catchment basis, gradients between catchment boundaries are quite sharp, with intermediate values only occurring where one reporting square overlaps multiple river waterbody catchments.\tFrom the Water Framework Directive (WFD) values were joined to the river waterbody catchment polygons using the waterbody ID contained both within the spreadsheet and the attribute table associated with the polygons. Catchments not considered as river water body catchments within the WFD were assigned their status based on the coastal/transitional waterbody condition assessment. The assessment of the overall status in this can return either of 5 values: Bad, poor, moderate, good, or high. These were ranked in order of relative ecological resilience, with catchments of value bad being assigned a value of 0, through to catchments of value high being assigned a value of 4. Using these values, the waterbody catchments were rasterised to 10m pixels, sharing the x and y extents of the reporting squares shapefile to ensure an exact overlap between reporting square boundaries and pixel boundaries. The raster generated through this process contained 10,000 pixels within each reporting square. The reporting square was then assigned one value using zonal statistics to identify the mean of the values of all pixels contained within. This mean was then normalised to a scale from 0 to 100, assigning squares with all pixels representing bad condition a value of 0, and squares with all pixels representing high condition a value of 100.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:04:48.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18915\tNRW_DS124773\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.654Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Connectivity EcologicalNetworks\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373733\tThis dataset shows the number of ecological networks represented within each reporting square. In some squares, 5 out of 6 ecological networks are present, generating areas that act as major nodes between various habitat networks across Wales.\tFor this factor, habitat types included in NRWs Habitat Networks mapping were considered (bog, fen, grassland, heathland, woodland, and sand dunes), including the draft sand dune map. For all habitat types, priority, core, focal, and local networks were considered, for both upland and lowland. To generate counts, all individual network files for each habitat were combined and dissolved, to generate one map showing all areas that fell within at least one network type for the habitat (e.g. one overall bog network map, etc.). Within each reporting square, the number of these overall habitat network maps falling within the square were counted to generate a total count of the ecological networks represented within the square.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:08:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18910\tNRW_DS124774\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.196Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Connectivity FeatureLength\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373734\tConnective features in this instance refers to habitat types that are typically long and narrow. These habitats may act as corridors or stepping stones, facilitating species movement through oftentimes heavily modified and otherwise impermeable landscapes.    The output shows the relative quantity of connective features present within each square.\tThe connective features considered for this factor were hedgerows and rivers. Rivers were taken from the Water Framework Directive, while hedges (Phase 1 habitat code: J2.1) were extracted from the updated Phase 1 habitat map for Wales (2009 ). To obtain a relative value of the amount of river network within the square, the rivers, expressed as polylines, were intersected with the reporting squares and dissolved. This was then used to obtain one set of river network within each reporting square, the total length of which was calculated. These absolute length values were normalised to relative values between 0 and 100 using a translation where a square without river would obtain a value of 0, whilst the square with the largest amount of river network would be given a value of 100.  The same process was used to generated relative values for hedgerows, working with hedge area, rather than length, as the input data consisted of polygons, rather than polylines.   To combine the relative values for hedges and rivers, for each reporting square the average between the derived values for hedges and rivers was calculated, in order to maintain a scale from 0 to 100.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:08:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18902\tNRW_DS124775\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.186Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Connectivity Overall\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373735\tThis dataset shows resilience based on connectivity, using a range of factors contributing to this attribute.\tTo combine the individual factors into attributes, the relevant attribute columns from all factors were joined to the reporting squares. The average is calculated as the sum of all non-NoData factors divided by the count of all non-NoData factors.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:08:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18913\tNRW_DS124776\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.167Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Diversity SemiNaturalHabitats\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373736\tHabitat diversity forms an important component of expected resilience, as the variety of available ecological niches in the area means that the area is likely to harbour greater species and genetic diversity.  The output shows the diversity of semi-natural habitats on a relative scale from 0 to 100 within each reporting square. In the interpretation of these outputs, it is important to consider that only diverse habitat mosaics will show with the highest values, whilst areas dominated by few classes will show with low values, regardless of whether or not they are semi-natural.\tFor this dataset, the Phase 1 classes present in the Phase 1 habitat map were assessed as being either semi-natural or not. All non-semi-natural habitats were treated as one class, whilst all semi-natural habitats were treated individually. The counts of pixels for different habitat classes within each reporting square were then used to calculate the Shannon-Wiener diversity index within the reporting square, whereby each pixel was treated as one sample point. These values were then normalised to a scale from 0 to 100.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:08:50.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18897\tNRW_DS124777\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.894Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Diversity SSSIs Proportion\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373737\tSites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) are areas of land notified for their special scientific interest. They support an important range of biological, geological and physiographical features. The most important areas for habitat and species conservation, at both national and international levels, lie within them, and all are considered of national importance for nature conservation.    The proportion of land within SSSIs in each 1 km reporting square is used as factor for the Diversity factor.\tFor the 1km reporting, the proportion of land designated as SSSI was calculated and is reported as percentage (0 – 100) integer value. This in turn was calculated from overlay analysis between the reporting units and the SSSI outlines.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:04:46.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18912\tNRW_DS124778\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.212Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Diversity Topographic\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373738\tTopography is one of the main biophysical drivers behind habitat establishment.  The output expresses on a relative scale how topographically diverse the landscape within each of the reporting squares is. Areas with higher values can be expected to contain habitats at different elevation levels, encompassing areas with shallow slopes, as well as steeply rising land. Furthermore, areas with the highest values are expected to have slopes facing in different cardinal directions, affecting the amount and timing of sunlight they receive.\tThis factor is based on a measure of topographic diversity generated under the project GIS for Area Statements (GISfAS). The topographic diversity layer is based on three measures of topography, slope, aspect, and elevation. For slope and elevation, for each pixel the standard deviation was calculated twice within a moving window of 30m and 510m, respectively. Aspect was converted into 16 cardinal directions (points north, south, east, and west) plus “flat” (with all areas with less than 3 degrees of slope being considered flat) and the number of different cardinal directions within the moving window was counted.   Each of these three layers was then normalised to a scale from 0 (least diverse) to 100 (most diverse). For slope and elevation, the highest standard deviation that occurred in the raster was translated to 100; 0 remained as 0 on the normalised scale. For aspect, the highest possible count, 17 (16 cardinal directions plus flat) translated to 100. However, for aspect, a count of 1 translated to 0 on the normalised scale, as this is the lowest value that could theoretically occur (e.g. within the whole window only one cardinal direction occurs). The three rasters were then added, creating an output raster in which the highest theoretically possible topographic diversity would be indicated by a value of 300, the lowest by a value of 0.  For this project, topographic diversity at landscape and local scale were averaged, to create an overall proxy for topographic diversity at a range of scales.  In order to summarise the topographic diversity into the project’s reporting squares, zonal statistics were used to average the values of all raster pixels falling within the reporting square. The resulting cell values where then normalised to relative values, with the reporting square with the highest value being assigned a relative value of 100 (equation: cell_value * 100 / max_diversity_value).  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additi\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18898\tNRW_DS124779\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.154Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Ecosystem Resilience Overall\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373739\tThis dataset shows current relative resilience across Wales, based on five attributes (Diveristy, Extent, Condition, Connectivity, Adaptability).\tTo create this dataset, the values from the five attributes were averaged.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:05:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18906\tNRW_DS124780\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.070Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Extent Overall\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373830\tThis dataset shows resilience based on extent, using a range of factors contributing to this attribute.\tTo combine the individual factors into attributes, the relevant attribute columns from all factors were joined to the reporting squares. The average is calculated as the sum of all non-NoData factors divided by the count of all non-NoData factors.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18901\tNRW_DS124781\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.155Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Extent PatchDynamics\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373831\tHabitat resilience does not only depend on the amount of semi-natural habitat available, but also on the spatial configuration of this habitat.  The output shows on a relative scale from 0 to 100 how patchy the distribution of the semi-natural habitat within the reporting square is (one large vs several small).\tFor this factor, the Phase 1 habitat data was used in its scored form, with all classes being considered as either semi-natural or not semi-natural. For each reporting square, the aggregation index as given by Hong et al. (2000) was calculated using the associated SAGA module. The aggregation index analyses landscape composition at the raster level, and compares the number of edges shared between pixels of the target class (in this case semi-natural) to the maximum number of edges that could theoretically be shared given the count of target pixels. Using this method, areas with a patchy distribution of the target class are assigned a low aggregation index, whilst areas with one large, evenly shaped patch are assigned a high aggregation index.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:05:32.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18903\tNRW_DS124782\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.167Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Extent SemiNaturalHabitats\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373832\tSemi-natural habitats with their natural and diverse assemblage of flora and fauna are inherently more resilient than habitats modified strongly by human management, making this an important factor to include for the attribute Extent.  The output shows the proportion of semi-natural habitats in the reporting square.\tFor this factor, the updated Phase 1 data generated for this project was classified into semi-natural and non-semi-natural. As the Phase 1 data update was processed at raster level, the proportion of semi-natural habitat within each reporting square was calculated as the proportion of non-NoData pixels taking the value semi-natural (ignoring NoData pixels ensures that coastal areas are assigned appropriate values).  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18914\tNRW_DS124783\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.640Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Diversity Overall\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373833\tThis dataset shows resilience based on diversity, using a range of factors contributing to this attribute.\tTo combine the individual factors into attributes, the relevant attribute columns from all factors were joined to the reporting squares. The average is calculated as the sum of all non-NoData factors divided by the count of all non-NoData factors.  Full methodology details can be found in the project report (see additional information source).\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:09:30.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18911\tNRW_DS124784\t2024-05-28T12:57:57.193Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCuRVe Connectivity River Modifications\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373834\tThis dataset shows the resilience value derived from the amount of hard engineering affecting rivers within the reporting squares. These values are based on the expected quantity of physical modifications, but do not take into account the type of modification and how strongly it impacts upon the river’s capacity to act as connective feature.\tThis factor drew from two data sources: The Water Framework Directive (WFD) and the DEFRA dataset on hydropower opportunities.   From the WFD data, all river waterbody catchments listed as Confirmed or Probable for Physical modifications in the Reasons for Not Achieving Good Status (RNAG) spreadsheet were considered to be affected by hard engineering and assigned a low resilience value. Any catchments listed within the Heavily Modified Water Bodies (HMWB) dataset were added to this selection and also assigned a low resilience value (0). This created a binary dataset, with a low resilience value (0) for catchments with modified water bodies, and a value of 100 for all catchments not included in the RNAG or HMWB. This was not considered the best possible representation of on the ground conditions for this project, particularly as in some cases catchments affected by a barrier further downstream are listed as affected catchments, whilst the catchment containing the barrier is not (as the water condition in the catchment is not affected to the extent to change the overall assessment of the catchment).   Therefore, known locations of physical barriers (artificial only) from the DEFRA dataset on opportunities for hydropower were assessed separately on a reporting square basis. For this assessment, all artificial barriers (e.g. weirs) from the DEFRA dataset were given a score based on their ecological sensitivity assessment (generated by DEFRA based on the sensitivity of species observed in the waterbody to physical barriers). Points with high sensitivity where given a value of 3, moderate a value of 2, and low a value of 1. The values of all points falling within a reporting square were added up to generate a risk score, which was then inverted and normalised to an relative scale from 0 to 100 to generate relative resilience values. The resilience values based on the WFD physical modifications and those based on the DEFRA hydropower opportunities were then averaged to generat\tNaumann, E.-K., Medcalf, K., 2020. Current relative value (CuRVe) map atlas for ecosystem resilience in Wales. NRW Natural Resources Wales report series, Report No: 415, 88pp, Publisher, Place of publication.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience MappingTerrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T10:22:58.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18899\tNRW_DS124786\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.959Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAll Wales Slope 5m2 (degrees)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373836\t\t\t\tX:\\Basemapping\\Height\\All Wales Slope 5m2 (degrees).lyr\tPaul Challender, Lead Specialist - Industry and Waste Regulation, Industry Regulation NE\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:23:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18895\tNRW_DS124788\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.213Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDeterioration of Natura 2000 Sites in Wales due to Coastal Squeeze Losses\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373838\tThe data resource includes NRW Evidence Report 355 plus associated Excel and GIS files. The project has calculated potential coastal squeeze losses under a range of different No Active Intervention and Managed Realignment management Scenarios, climate change predictions and Shoreline Management Plan Epochs. The study covers the Pen Llŷn a’r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation. The excel files contain coastal squeeze calculations which are summarised within the report, and GIS files used to undertake analysis and produce maps provided within the evidence report.\tData is for Internal NRW use Only.\tRoberts, H., Le Vieux, A. and Brooks, A. 2019. Advice on the Scale of Deterioration of Natura 2000 Sites due to Coastal Squeeze Losses: Pen Llŷn a’r Sarnau SAC. NRW Report No: 355, 81, Natural Resources Wales, Cardiff.\tSpreadsheets- DMS (record numbers to be confirmed) GIS data - X drive - location to be confirmed  Currently zipped in the DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:u:/r/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mcps/Evidence%20NRW%20projects/Article%206%20-2%20Deterioration%20due%20to%20Coastal%20Squeeze%20Project/Data_20190710.zip?csf\u003d1  And copy (to be deleted) in  K:\\Archive\\Temp_forArchive\\Report 355\\Data\tInternal Contact: Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine and Coastal Physical Process and Coastal Management, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18892\tNRW_DS124789\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.196Z\t395\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGreen Flag Awards Boundary data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373839\tThe boundaries of all sites that held a Green Flag or Green Flag Community Award in the 2019-2020 Award Year.\tAll files include the same sets of table attributes in GIS, and those are:  •Naming [green space / site name]  •Origin [general dataset origin, if any, or general basis for any site digitisation]  •Precision [general levels of how well a site is mapped – anything below “Exact” means the site polygon has either been altered, combined, clipped, or in some other way modified from any listed “official/original” dataset [e.g. OS Open Greenspace]  •NOTES [general explanation if needed]  Final notes: 1: Remaining GIS topology errors with OS Mastermap are not able to be resolved, as the contractor only had permission to view data, not otherwise directly edit data or “snap” new data to existing polygons.   2: 97% of all Green Flag sites have been mapped to the defined levels of precision.  The remaining 3% of Green Flag sites have best guess boundaries e.g. because they cover a “town centre” or other area with no natural boundary.\thttps://www.keepwalestidy.cymru/Pages/Category/greenflag\tGIS Corporate Data Store: X-Layer_Files\\ Recreation and Access\\Green Flag\\Green Flag sites poly.lyr\tPeter Frost - Advisor -  Planning, Landscape, Energy, Decarbonisation\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T14:48:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18893\tNRW_DS124790\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.196Z\t872\tValid\tEditable\tNational Flood Hazard Maps - Present Day\tMapiau Perygl Llifogydd Cenedlaethol - Y Presennol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373930\tThe National Flood Hazard Maps for Present day,  are based on generalised modelling only. The maps have been published to comply with the Flood Risk Regulations (2009) and the EU Directive (2007/60/EC), and used to inform the creation of Flood Risk Management plans across Wales. These maps have no official status for Planning or Insurance purposes, therefore the public and professionals are advised to use the information contained within the Flood Risk Assessment Wales Map, the Development Advice Map/ Flood Map for Planning (as appropriate) for these purposes as these will be more up-to-date and incorporate improved modelling information.   The National Flood Hazard Maps for Present Day have been created for 3 sources of flooding, namely,   1. Flooding from Rivers  2. Flooding from the Sea  3. Flooding from Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourse    The Maps show flood depth, velocity (speed and direction), hazard and extent for High, Medium and Low risk scenarios without raised flood defences.    For Rivers and Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses, High risk is up to 1 in 30year; Medium risk is between 1 in 30 and 1 in 100year; and Low risk is between 1 in 100year and 1 in 1,000year Annual Exceedance Probability (AEP).    For the Sea, High risk is up to 1 in 30 year; Medium risk is between 1 in 30 to 1 in 200 year; and Low risk 1 in 200 year to 1 in 1000 year.    The Flooding from Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses dataset is a direct replacement for the earlier Updated Flood Map for Surface Water (uFMfSW), 2013 and is based on the modelling of Surface Water Catchments.  Details of the modelling work to produce the new National Flood Hazard Maps are available in the supporting Information sheets.-Mae\u0027r Mapiau Perygl Llifogydd Cenedlaethol ar gyfer y Presennol yn seiliedig ar fodelu cyffredinol yn unig. Cyhoeddwyd y mapiau i gydymffurfio â Rheoliadau Perygl Llifogydd (2009) a Chyfarwyddeb yr UE (2007/60/EC) a chawsant eu defnyddio i gyfarwyddo’r gwaith o greu cynlluniau Rheoli Perygl Llifogydd ledled Cymru. Nid oes gan y mapiau hyn unrhyw statws swyddogol at ddibenion cynllunio neu yswiriant, felly cynghorir y cyhoedd a gweithwyr proffesiynol i ddefnyddio\u0027r wybodaeth sydd i’w chael yn Map Asesu Perygl Llifogydd Cymru, y Map Cyngor Datblygu/Map Llifogydd ar gyfer Cynllunio (fel y bo\u0027n briodol) at y dibenion hyn gan y bydd y rhain yn fwy diweddar ac yn cynnwys gwybodaeth fodelu well.  Mae\u0027r Mapiau Perygl Llifogydd Cenedlaethol ar gyfer y Presennol wedi cael eu creu ar gyfer tair ffynhonnell o lifogydd, sef:  Llifogydd o afonydd Llifogydd o\u0027r môr Llifogydd o ddŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bychain Mae\u0027r mapiau\u0027n dangos dyfnder y llifogydd, cyflymder (cyflymder a chyfeiriad), peryglon ac ehangder ar gyfer sefyllfaoedd perygl uchel, canolig ac isel heb amddiffynfeydd rhag llifogydd uwch.  Ar gyfer afonydd a dŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bychain, risg uchel yw Tebygolrwydd Gormodiant Blynyddol (AEP) o hyd at 1 mewn 30 o flynyddoedd, risg ganolig yw AEP o rhwng 1 a 30 ac 1 mewn 100 o flynyddoedd, a risg isel yw AEP o rhwng 1 mewn 100 o flynyddoedd ac 1 mewn 1,000 o flynyddoedd.  Ar gyfer y môr, risg uchel yw AEP o hyd at 1 mewn 30 o flynyddoedd, risg ganolig yw AEP o rhwng 1 mewn 30 ac 1 mewn 200 o flynyddoedd, a risg isel yw AEP o rhwng 1 mewn 200 o flynyddoedd ac 1 mewn 1,000 o flynyddoedd.  Mae\u0027r set ddata Llifogydd o Ddŵr Wyneb a Chyrsiau Dŵr Bychain wedi disodli set ddata gynharach, y Map Llifogydd Diweddaredig ar gyfer Dŵr Wyneb (2013), yn uniongyrchol ac mae\u0027n seiliedig ar fodelu dalgylchoedd dŵr wyneb.  Mae manylion y gwaith modelu i lunio’r Mapiau Peryglon Llifogydd Cenedlaethol newydd ar gael yn y taflenni gwybodaeth ategol.  Cydnabyddiaethau  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru gwybodaeth © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Mae rhai nodweddion o\u0027r wybodaeth hon yn seiliedig ar ddata gofodol digidol wedi\u0027i drwyddedu gan Ganolfan Ecoleg a Hydroleg y DU © UKCEH, Asiantaeth yr Amgylchedd © EA a Getmapping Plc a Bluesky International Limited [2015]. DEFRA, y Swyddfa Dywydd ac Asiantaeth Afonydd yr Adran Amaethyddiaeth a Datblygu Gwledig © Hawlfraint y Goron. © Prifysgol Cranfield. © Sefydliad James Hutton. Yn cynnwys data\u0027r AO © Hawlfraint y Goron a hawl cronfa ddata. Gwasanaethau Tir ac Eiddo © Hawlfraint y Goron a hawl cronfa ddata.  Darllenwch y drwydded i ddeall amodau defnyddio\u0027r data.\tThe datasets have been created as part of the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) 2018 project. The modelling has been undertaken on the Environment Agency Integrated Height Model (EA IHM) 2016, with input rainfall and flow estimates from the Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology (CEH). Sea level estimates have been taken from the Coastal Flood Boundary Project (CFB) 2018.\tMore information on the FRAW 2018 project can be found in the final project report JBA Consulting  2019  Flood Risk Assessment Wales Project Report.  Report to Natural Resources Wales.  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/waterman/frm/ran/FRAW/2019/2018s0011%20-%20FRAW%20Project%20Report%20-%20And%20Appendices.pdf\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Rivers\\Flood Hazard River Depth.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Rivers\\Flood Hazard Rivers Extent.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Rivers\\Flood Hazard Rivers Hazard.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Rivers\\Flood Hazard Rivers Velocity.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses Depth.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses Depth.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses Hazard.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses Velocity.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from the Sea\\Flood Hazard from the Sea Depth.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from the Sea\\Flood Hazard from the Sea Extent.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from the Sea\\Flood Hazard from the Sea Hazard.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh / GIS and Spatial Data Team   Metadata Point of Contact: Mark Pugh / Simon James / Dave Tarrant       Surface Water Catchments (subset dataset NRW_DS124811): Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from Environment Agency © EA and Getmapping Plc and Bluesky International Limited [2015].\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-10-01\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2649\t2025-12-19T12:11:27.247Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18894\tNRW_DS124791\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.203Z\t656\tValid\tEditable\tNational Flood Risk Maps 2019\tMapiau Perygl Llifogydd Cenedlaethol 2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373931\tThe National Flood Hazard and Risk Maps are based on generalised modelling only. The maps have been published to comply with the Flood Risk Regulations (2009) and the EU Directive (2007/60/EC), and used to inform the creation of Flood Risk Management plans across Wales. These maps have no official status for Planning or Insurance purposes, therefore the public and professionals are advised to use the information contained within the Flood Risk Assessment Wales Map and Development Advice Map/ Flood Map for Planning (as appropriate) for these purposes as these will be more up-to-date and incorporate improved modelling information.   The National Flood Risk Maps have been created for 3 sources of flooding, namely,  1. Flooding from Rivers 2. Flooding from the Sea 3. Flooding from Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourse  The maps show Risk to a range of receptors, grouped in categories of People, Economic and Environment. Risk information is aggregated and displayed at a community scale.  For Rivers and Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses, High risk is up to 1 in 30year; Medium risk is between 1 in 30 and 1 in 100year; and Low risk is between 1 in 100year and 1 in 1,000year Annual Exceedance Probability (AEP).  For the Sea, High risk is up to 1 in 30 year; Medium risk is between 1 in 30 to 1 in 200 year; and Low risk 1 in 200 year to 1 in 1000 year.-Mapiau Perygl Llifogydd Cenedlaethol  Mae\u0027r Mapiau Peryglon Llifogydd a Risg Cenedlaethol yn seiliedig ar fodelu cyffredinol yn unig. Cyhoeddwyd y mapiau i gydymffurfio â Rheoliadau Perygl Llifogydd (2009) a Chyfarwyddeb yr UE (2007/60/EC) a chawsant eu defnyddio i gyfarwyddo’r gwaith o greu cynlluniau Rheoli Perygl Llifogydd ledled Cymru. Nid oes gan y mapiau hyn unrhyw statws swyddogol at ddibenion cynllunio neu yswiriant, felly cynghorir y cyhoedd a gweithwyr proffesiynol i ddefnyddio\u0027r wybodaeth sydd i’w chael yn Map Asesu Perygl Llifogydd Cymru, y Map Cyngor Datblygu/Map Llifogydd ar gyfer Cynllunio (fel y bo\u0027n briodol) at y dibenion hyn gan y bydd y rhain yn fwy diweddar ac yn cynnwys gwybodaeth fodelu well.  Mae\u0027r Mapiau Perygl Llifogydd Cenedlaethol wedi cael eu creu ar gyfer tair ffynhonnell o lifogydd, sef:  Llifogydd o afonydd Llifogydd o\u0027r môr Llifogydd o ddŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bach Mae\u0027r mapiau\u0027n dangos y perygl mewn perthynas ag amrywiaeth o dderbynyddion, wedi\u0027u grwpio yn y categorïau Pobl, Economaidd a\u0027r Amgylchedd. Caiff gwybodaeth am risg ei chydgasglu a\u0027i harddangos ar raddfa cymuned.  Ar gyfer afonydd a dŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bach, risg uchel yw Tebygolrwydd Gormodiant Blynyddol (AEP) o hyd at 1 mewn 30 o flynyddoedd, risg ganolig yw AEP o rhwng 1 a 30 ac 1 mewn 100 o flynyddoedd, a risg isel yw AEP o rhwng 1 mewn 100 o flynyddoedd ac 1 mewn 1,000 o flynyddoedd.  Ar gyfer y môr, risg uchel yw AEP o hyd at 1 mewn 30 o flynyddoedd, risg ganolig yw AEP o rhwng 1 mewn 30 ac 1 mewn 200 o flynyddoedd, a risg isel yw AEP o rhwng 1 mewn 200 o flynyddoedd ac 1 mewn 1,000 o flynyddoedd.  Datganiad priodoliau  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru gwybodaeth © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Mae rhai nodweddion o\u0027r wybodaeth hon yn seiliedig ar ddata gofodol digidol wedi\u0027i drwyddedu gan Ganolfan Ecoleg a Hydroleg y DU © UKCEH, Asiantaeth yr Amgylchedd © EA a Getmapping Plc a Bluesky International Limited [2015]. DEFRA, y Swyddfa Dywydd ac Asiantaeth Afonydd yr Adran Amaethyddiaeth a Datblygu Gwledig © Hawlfraint y Goron. © Prifysgol Cranfield. © Sefydliad James Hutton. Yn cynnwys data\u0027r AO © Hawlfraint y Goron a hawl cronfa ddata. Gwasanaethau Tir ac Eiddo © Hawlfraint y Goron a hawl cronfa ddata.\tThe risk information has been generated from the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) Economic Toolset (v2.0).  The economic toolset used the flood hazard information, to establish the impact on a range of receptors, including: People, property, roads, railways and Environmental datasets.\tMore information on the FRAW 2018 project can be found in the final project report JBA Consulting  2019  Flood Risk Assessment Wales Project Report.  Report to Natural Resources Wales.  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/waterman/frm/ran/FRAW/2019/2018s0011%20-%20FRAW%20Project%20Report%20-%20And%20Appendices.pdf\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Risk from Rivers.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Risk from Surface Water and Small Watercourses.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Risk from the Sea.lyr  X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Risk CaRR.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh / GIS and Spatial Data Team   Metadata Point of Contact: Mark Pugh / Simon James / Dave Tarrant\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-06-29\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/888/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:NationalFloodRiskMaps\t2025-03-07T15:59:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18890\tNRW_DS124793\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.044Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUnchecked Rain Gauge Data from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Gauges\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373933\tThis dataset covers monitoring data that is updated on our systems on a minimum of a daily update cycle. This is increased during times of flooding etc. Measurements of the height (m) of water in a river, lake or coastal site taken using automatic field devices, are given usually every 15 minutes. Information is available for NRW river monitoring sites throughout Wales, as well as some reservoirs and coastal sites.\tReadings are transferred via telemetry to internal and external. This data may be transferred to these systems or users at different intervals varying, for example, from once per day during normal conditions to several times per day during a flood event. This data is retrieved automatically and is unvalidated.  This data should not be stored for more than 1 calendar year. NRW cannot guarantee the accuracy or timeliness of the data. It should not be used in applications that require quality controlled data.\t\tWiski H\u0026amp;T\tLiam Perrot and Alison Hanson\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:05:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18891\tNRW_DS124794\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.036Z\t882\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Protected Areas in Welsh waters\tArdaloedd Morol Gwarchodedig yn Nyfroedd Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373934\tThe Marine protected area (MPA) network is a subset of existing protected sites that have marine features. Sites include: SACs, SPAs, Ramsars, SSSIs and MCZs.     What features are considered marine is determined through expert groups of SNCBs led by JNCC. The list of MPA network features are hosted by JNCC https://hub.jncc.gov.uk/assets/8ee15786-510b-44e4-819e-e6681a1abd96.     The need for a coherent and well managed MPA network is underpinned by the legislation Marine and Coastal access act and MFSD (UK Marine Strategy). This has been driven by our commitment to OSPAR (Oslo Paris convention) and CBD (Convention on biological diversity). NRW are the custodians of MPAs within 12nm (Welsh territorial waters), sites that are found in out offshore waters are hosted by JNCC and can be downloaded from their website https://jncc.gov.uk/mpa-mapper/.-Mae\u0027r Rhwydwaith Ardaloedd Morol Gwarchodedig yn is-set o safleoedd gwarchodedig presennol sydd â nodweddion morol. Mae\u0027r safleoedd yn cynnwys: Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig, Ardaloedd Gwarchodaeth Arbennig, safleoedd Ramsar, Safleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig a Pharthau Cadwraeth Morol.  Pennir pa nodweddion sy\u0027n cael eu hystyried yn forol drwy grwpiau arbenigol o Gyrff Cadwraeth Natur Statudol dan arweiniad y Cydbwyllgor Cadwraeth Natur. Caiff rhestr o nodweddion y Rhwydwaith Ardaloedd Morol Gwarchodedig ei chynnal gan y Cydbwyllgor Cadwraeth Natur: https://hub.jncc.gov.uk/assets/8ee15786-510b-44e4-819e-e6681a1abd96.  Datganiad priodoli:  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru – Hawlfraint a Hawl Gronfa Ddata Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded yr Arolwg Ordnans: AC0000849444. Hawlfraint a hawl Gronfa Ddata y Goron.\tInformation extracted from protected sites (SACs, SPAs, Ramsars, SSSIs and MCZs) to create this data.\t\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\tNatasha Lough.  Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-06-09\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3601/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_marine_protected_areas_in_welsh_waters\t2025-06-10T15:46:54.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18889\tNRW_DS124795\t2024-05-28T12:57:56.056Z\t353\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) bryophyte surveys\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373935\tLocations of notable bryophyte species recorded during pre-intervention surveys for the Sands of LIFE project at Tywyn Aberffraw, Newborough, Morfa Harlech, Pembrey Coast, Kenfig and Merthyr Mawr. Data was collected between September and November 2019.\tObservational field data recorded with handheld GPS accurate to at least 5m.\tCallaghan, D, 2020. Sands of LIFE: Bryophyte Survey. NRW Evidence Report No. 423, 28pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE bryophyte surveys.lyr  Spreadsheet version also available in DMS LAND-75481194-986\tToni Henwood, Team Member 2, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South WalesSands of LIFE\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-06-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18888\tNRW_DS124796\t2024-05-28T12:57:55.993Z\t653\tUnassessed\tEditable\tScarce Yellow Sally stonefly Isogenus nubecula, River Dee at Bangor-on-Dee 2020\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373936\tIn 2018, a survey commissioned by Natural Resources Wales across the full former range of I. nubecula confirmed that it occurred at five sites on the River Dee, two of which (Bangor-on-Dee and Pickhill Meadows) appeared to support strong populations. In 2020, Natural Resources Wales commissioned a second survey to confirm the continued presence of Scarce Yellow Sally, and six nymphs were found at Bangor-on-Dee on 24th March 2020\tThe River Dee is hard to kick sample if the river level is too high. Regular reference was therefore made to both the Met Office website for current and long-range weather forecasts and to online data from the Manley Hall flow gauging station on the River Dee located just to the south of Manley House, near Erbistock\tDavy-Bowker J., Hammett M. \u0026amp; Farr A. 2020. A survey of the River Dee at Bangor-on-Dee for the Scarce Yellow Sally stonefly Isogenus nubecula in spring 2020. NRW Evidence Report No: 462, 11pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tStored on the WID in recorder 6\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T14:50:50.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18887\tNRW_DS124799\t2024-05-28T12:57:55.976Z\t599\tValid\tEditable\tCurrent Relative Value of ecosystem resilience (CuRVe)\tGwerth Cymharol Cyfredol (Current Relative Value) gwydnwch ecosystemau (CuRVe)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234373939\tCuRVe is a series of interconnected web-based maps that together form an atlas. It is a tool that can help users to explore how ecosystem resilience varies across Wales and understand the underlying reasons for this variation. It can be used to support and improve decision making about the use of, and investment in, the sustainable management of natural resources. CuRVe stands for Current Relative Value of ecosystem resilience. The Atlas is the first step in an experimental project to reveal relative values and spatial patterns of ecosystem resilience at a landscape scale for Wales.-Mae CuRVe yn gyfres o fapiau rhyng-gysylltiedig ar y we sydd, gyda\u0027i gilydd, yn ffurfio atlas. Mae\u0027n offeryn sy\u0027n gallu helpu defnyddwyr i archwilio sut y mae gwydnwch ecosystemau yn amrywio ledled Cymru a deall y rhesymau sylfaenol dros yr amrywiaeth hwn. Gellir ei ddefnyddio i gefnogi a gwella\u0027r broses o wneud penderfyniadau ynghylch rheoli adnoddau naturiol mewn modd cynaliadwy, a’r buddsoddiad ynddo. Mae CuRVe yn golygu Gwerth Cymharol Cyfredol (Current Relative Value) gwydnwch ecosystemau. Yr Atlas yw\u0027r cam cyntaf mewn prosiect arbrofol i ddatgelu gwerthoedd cymharol a phatrymau gofodol gwydnwch ecosystemau ar raddfa tirwedd ar gyfer Cymru. Mae\u0027r offeryn yn dod â mathau gwahanol o ddata gofodol am ein hecosystemau at ei gilydd er mwyn darparu mewnwelediadau i wydnwch ecosystemau. Gall defnyddwyr weld gwybodaeth am wydnwch cyffredinol ecosystemau mewn ardal benodol a gwybodaeth am briodoleddau ecosystem fel amrywiaeth, maint, cysylltedd a chyflwr sy\u0027n sail i\u0027r gwydnwch hwn. Mae\u0027r offeryn yn caniatáu i ddefnyddwyr ddewis y raddfa ofodol berthnasol sydd o ddiddordeb iddynt, gan amrywio o raddfa grid 1km i raddfa ar hyd y wlad. Attribution statement: Contains Natural Resources Wales information © Natural Resources Wales and Database Right. All rights Reserved. Contains Ordnance Survey Data. Ordnance Survey Licence number AC0000849444. Crown Copyright and Database Right. Contains data supplied by Natural Environment Research Council.\tThe tool brings together different types of spatial data about our ecosystems to provide insights into ecosystem resilience. Users can view information about overall ecosystem resilience of a given area and information about ecosystem attributes like diversity, extent, connectivity and condition that underpin this resilience.  The tool allows users to choose the relevant spatial scale which interests them ranging from 1 km grid scale up to countrywide.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\CuRVe\tJohnathan Rothwell -  Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-06-22\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/1110/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:CuRVe\t2025-05-07T15:49:06.127Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18886\tNRW_DS124802\t2024-05-28T12:57:55.770Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW Fisheries Teams\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383032\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Fisheries Teams boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for operational Fisheries Teams post Organisation Design, July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries, incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tData retrieved via e-mail. Initially an inbox was created called ‘SME Project’ where an e-mail was sent out to team leaders asking for information. After the initial e-mail had been sent, most of the outgoing e-mails were then sent out from my personal e-mail address. Information stored on an excel spreadsheet which is based on the DMS for internal staff to view.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Fisheries Teams.lyr\tGary Eccleston, Advisor 2, Geospatial Knowledge, Information Management\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-07-25\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18934\tNRW_DS124803\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.510Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW Forestry Operations Teams\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383033\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Fisheries Teams boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for Forestry operations Teams post Organisation Design, July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries, incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tData retrieved via e-mail. Initially an inbox was created called ‘SME Project’ where an e-mail was sent out to team leaders asking for information. After the initial e-mail had been sent, most of the outgoing e-mails were then sent out from my personal e-mail address. Information stored on an excel spreadsheet which is based on the DMS for internal staff to view.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Forestry Operations Teams.lyr\tGary Eccleston, Advisor 2, Geospatial Knowledge, Information Management\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2026-01-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-05T13:06:28.000Z\tGary Eccleston\t-\tPrivate record\n18929\tNRW_DS124804\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.583Z\t454\tValid\tEditable\tDistribution of the spider Baryphyma gowerense on Cors Bodeilio, Cors Erddreiniog, Cors Geirch and Cors Goch 2019\tDosbarthiad y pry cop Baryphyma gowerense ar Gors Bodeilio, Cors Erddreiniog, Cors Geirch a Chors Goch 2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383034\tIn order to improve our knowledge of the ecology and distribution of the Vulnerable and Nationally Rare money spider Baryphyma gowerense, survey work was conducted on Cors Bodeilio, Cors Erddreiniog, Cors Goch and Cors Geirch National Nature Reserves (NNRs) between June and August 2019.\tSurvey work was conducted between 23rd June 2019 and 2nd August 2019. Two survey days were undertaken at Cors Bodeilio (23rd June and 13th July), Cors Erddreiniog (29th June and 20th July) and Cors Goch (12th July and 2nd August), with a single day spent at Cors Geirch (10th July) and an afternoon (28th June) at Newborough Warren, Afon Braint saltmarsh.  Sampling locations were initially selected in habitats close to previous site records for Baryphyma gowerense (in the case of Cors Bodeilio, Cors Erddreiniog and Cors Geirch where the species had been recorded from previously). However, site selection was later refined once the habitat preference of B. gowerense was discovered during the survey. Thereafter, attention was focused towards blocks of NVC plant community M13b (Schoenus nigricans – Juncus subnodulosus mire; Erica tetralix variant), particularly those supporting robust, dense stands of Schoenus nigricans.  To standardise sampling, a Stihl petrol G-vac was used to undertake three, 2-minute long, vacuum sampling sessions at each sample location. After each 2- minute vacuum, the contents of the G-vac’s sample bag were passed through a sieve onto a sorting tray and a representative sample of spiders collected. All “red \u0026amp; black” Linyphiidae were retained to maximise the chances of collecting B. gowerense.\tGallon, R.C. 2019. The status and distribution of the spider Baryphyma gowerense on Cors Bodeilio, Cors Erddreiniog, Cors Geirch and Cors Goch in 2019. NRW Evidence Report Report No: 466, Xpp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tData stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder.\tMike Howe\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-06-01\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr1212\t2025-12-19T16:16:10.645Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18939\tNRW_DS124805\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.850Z\t631\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAlien Taxa Environmental and Socio-economic impact classification\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383035\tThere are two excel spreadsheets which accompany the report titled ‘Assessing the impact of key Marine Invasive Non-Native Species on Welsh Marine Protected Area habitat features, fisheries and aquaculture’. These are the Environmental Impact Classification of Alien Taxa (EICAT) and the Socio-economic Impact Classification of Alien Taxa (SEICAT) which summarise and provide additional information data for the report.  The purpose of the spreadsheets (along with the accompanying report) is to assess potential impacts of 16 invasive non-native species on Welsh Marine Protected Area features, commercial fisheries and aquaculture to address knowledge gaps for biosecurity planning.   The EICAT Excel workbook consists of a summary matrix for the 16 invasive non-native species providing a summary score for likelihood of establishment and an aggregated (worst-case) score for impact on each of the 41 Marine Protected Area features assessed by the project.    The SEICAT Excel workbook consists of a summary matrix for the 16 invasive non-native species providing a summary impact score for direct impacts on fisheries and aquaculture via interactions which affect deployment and operation of gear and installations and impacts on target or cultivated species. It does not consider indirect impacts or ancillary activities (for example, impacts on gaining access or impacts on bait).    A number of general limitations regarding the impact assessments should be considered in their application:  •The impact assessments are generic and NOT site-specific. They are based on the likely effects of an INNS pressure on a ‘hypothetical’ MPA feature/ biotope based on general characteristics of these features. •The impact assessments are general assessments that indicate the likely effects of a given INNS (likely to arise from one or more impact pathways) on MPA features or aquaculture and fisheries operations as represented in Wales. •The assessments have attempted where possible to take account of spatial or temporal scales.  •The significance of impacts arising from pressures also needs to take account of the scale of the features. •There are limitations in the scientific evidence on the biology and ecology of MPA features and their responses to INNS on which the impact assessments have been based. •The EICAT impact assessment methodology takes account of the level of impact and the likely recovery to differentiate between the two most severe impact ranks (massive and major). Eradication of INNS is difficult and recovery will generally only occur where management measures are implemented. There is the potential, therefore for all major impacts to become massive. •As a general rule, where the impact is ranked as higher, the need for management measures is greater. •A rank of ‘Minor or Minimal Concern does not mean that no impact is possible from a particular ‘INNS x feature’ combination, only that a limited impact was judged to be likely based on the available evidence.\tEICAT spreadsheet: The impact assessment criteria and impact categories were adopted from the Environmental Impact Classification of Alien Taxa (EICAT) project (IUCN 2019), as these are well established, peer-reviewed and supported internationally by experts. The impact categories in this project were applied to species that were either key characterising, functional or structuring species within habitats that comprise the MPA features or that were target species for fisheries and aquaculture.  In order to capture habitat feature impacts, the project team added qualifiers to the EICAT definitions for habitat level impacts.   SEICAT spreadsheet: An approach to assess the socio economic impacts of non-native species on human welfare was proposed by Bacher et al., (2018). This approach assesses the impact on human capabilities. INNS can impact people’ opportunities through changes in environmental factors, economic settings or social context. For the current project assessments of socio-economic impact focused on aquaculture and commercial fishing operations and target species which largely maps to the category of material and immaterial assets. Other impacts on human capabilities were noted where evidence was found, for example the sharp shells of Crassostrea gigas may impact on access to fishing grounds and impact collection of bait and other target species.   Key fishery and aquaculture operations were identified from work by Hall et al., (2008) based on the habitat types and fishing techniques known to occur, or to have recently been pursued, in Wales at the time of the study (January 2007) and ABPmer (2016). Target species were identified from UK Sea Fisheries Statistics (MMO 2018).\tTillin, H.M., Kessel, C., Sewell, J., Wood, C.A., Bishop, J.D.D. 2020. Assessing the impact of key Marine Invasive Non-Native Species on Welsh MPA habitat features, fisheries and aquaculture. NRW Evidence Report. Report No: 454, 260pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tData held in the DMS at the following locations:  EICAT spreadsheet: EVID-363697614-2272  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mnns/EMFF%20INNS%20Project/EXTERNAL%20CONTRACTS/Marine%20Biological%20Association%20-%20Impacts%20of%20INNS/MBA%20final%20report/MBA_EICAT_042020.xlsx?d\u003dw1d3200d4e3824d73a6c21125ab1de07d    SEICAT Spreadsheet: EVID-363697614-2271: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mnns/EMFF%20INNS%20Project/EXTERNAL%20CONTRACTS/Marine%20Biological%20Association%20-%20Impacts%20of%20INNS/MBA%20final%20report/MBA_SEICAT_assessment_042020.xls?d\u003dw11cbbbd4221242d2be394c94583c320f\tInternal Custodian: Chloe Powell-Jennings, Senior Advisor (EMFF Biosecurity Project Officer), Southwest Operations Internal Custodian: Katherine Griffith, Senior Marine Advisor, Marine Area Management \u0026amp; Advice Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-09-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:19:12.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18944\tNRW_DS124806\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.908Z\t303\tValid\tEditable\tBait digging areas 2019/2020\tArdaloedd cloddio am abwyd 2019/2020\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383036\tThe data has been collected under a contract run under the Wales non-licensable activities project.    Bait digging and collection of living resources was identified as an activity which is causing damage to the Marine Protection Area (MPA) network in Wales.  The data obtained from this contract provides NRW with evidence which will help inform discussions around future management of this activity.    A GIS layer was created through interpretation of the visible evidence of bait digging from the aerial images (NRW_DS124825 Bait digging areas aerial imagery 2019/2020).  The GIS layer was created by digitising around areas identified as impacted by bait digging. In addition the layer displays site name, confidence in interpretation, the age of the evidence (old/new) and digging intensity (low, medium and high) along with who captured the data, the date and image date.\tTo produce the GIS layer (BaitDiggingAreas_2019_2020), a virtual raster was created for each site using the aerial imagery as tiles.  A 100m grid was applied over each site to ensure all areas of the site were examined.  Aerial imagery was inspected at 1:250 scale.  A scale of 1:500 was used to review areas where evidence of bait digging was not expected, and squares checked off when reviewed.    The site surveyor and the digitiser discussed mapping of polygons in the areas visited by foot.  The digitiser established what could be identified.    It should be acknowledged that this layer has been created using the contractor’s  interpretation of imagery (along with some ground truthing) and is not likely to represent a complete picture of damage at these sites.  It also represents a snapshot in time.\tPerrins, J., Lush, M., Taylor, T., Holt, R and Bunker, F. 2020. Investigating the location and intensity of bait digging in Wales. NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 449, 170pp, NRW, Bangor.\tShapefile of digging X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Mapping bait digging\\Bait digging areas (2019-2020).lyr  Layer file for the drone photos X:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Drone footage\\Intertidal Drone Photos 2019-2020.lyr  The PDFs and with processing notes and word documents are saved in DMS Advice\u0026gt;Marine\u0026gt;MPA Non-Licensable Activities\u0026gt;WNLA Phase 3 Bait Digging\u0026gt;Final Report and products July 2020    Digital terrain model outputs showing bait digging areas - Z:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Bait Digging DSMs 2019-20\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team                                                         This dataset has been listed as sensitive because it shows the location of areas that may be targeted by bait diggers. Project Officer has identified this as data that should be kept confidential however areas suitable for bait digging are not listed on the sensitive species guidance. But the data should still be licensed and restricted under EIR. Data should not be proactively published.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2020-07-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-16T15:13:19.298Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18936\tNRW_DS124807\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.866Z\t873\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Invasive Non-Native Species (INNS) Pathways Heatmaps for Wales 2020\tMapiau Gwres o Lwybrau Rhywogaethau Estron Goresgynnol Morol ar gyfer Cymru 2020\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383037\tHeatmaps (geodatabases) were created for invasive non-native species pathways in Welsh waters including commercial shipping, recreational boating, aquaculture, fishing and offshore activities. Heatmaps were produced at both  2km x 2km and 5km x 5km resolutions and have associated symbology layers. An overall heatmap was also produced to highlight key areas which are likely most at risk from invasion of invasive non-native species. This assessment provides a valuable insight into hotspots related to each potential pathway of introduction, the areas where the main areas of risk overlap for the different pathways and their overlap with protected areas. The outputs from this assessment can form the basis to underpin biosecurity planning.       It should be noted, however, that this was a national scale mapping exercise based only on freely available datasets, which themselves have limitations and possible inaccuracies due to incomplete coverage, both temporally and spatially. The scorings applied to the input datasets and weightings applied to the introduction pathways are also based on available literature and expert judgement.  Site-specific activities and conditions will always be an important consideration, as well as temporal variations.-Crëwyd mapiau gwres (geo-gronfeydd data) ar gyfer llwybrau rhywogaethau estron goresgynnol yn nyfroedd Cymru gan gynnwys llongau masnachol, cychod hamdden, dyframaethu, pysgota a gweithgareddau ar y môr. Cynhyrchwyd mapiau gwres ar raddfa 2km x 2km a 5km x 5km ac mae ganddynt haenau symbolaidd cysylltiedig. Cynhyrchwyd map gwres cyffredinol hefyd i dynnu sylw at ardaloedd allweddol sy\u0027n fwyaf tebygol o gael eu goresgyn gan rywogaethau estron goresgynnol. Mae\u0027r asesiad hwn yn rhoi trosolwg gwerthfawr o fannau problemus sy\u0027n gysylltiedig â phob llwybr cyflwyno posibl, y lleoedd lle mae\u0027r prif ardaloedd risg yn gorgyffwrdd ar gyfer y gwahanol lwybrau a\u0027r mannau lle maent yn gorgyffwrdd ag ardaloedd gwarchodedig. Gall allbynnau\u0027r asesiad hwn fod yn sail i gynlluniau bioddiogelwch.  Dylid nodi, fodd bynnag, mai ymarfer mapio ar raddfa genedlaethol oedd hwn yn seiliedig ar setiau data sydd ar gael am ddim ac yn rhwydd yn unig. Mae gan y rhain gyfyngiadau ac anghywirdebau posibl oherwydd cofnodion anghyflawn, yn amserol ac yn ofodol. Mae\u0027r sgoriau a gymhwysir i’r setiau data a ddefnyddiwyd a\u0027r pwysoliadau a gymhwysir i\u0027r llwybrau cyflwyno hefyd yn seiliedig ar y llenyddiaeth sydd ar gael a barn arbenigol. Bydd gweithgareddau ac amodau safle-benodol bob amser yn ystyriaeth bwysig, yn ogystal ag amrywiadau amserol.  Cydnabyddiaeth: Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Y Gymdeithas Hwylio Frenhinol (RYA) (h) 2022. (h) Ystâd y Goron. Hawlfraint (h) Cedwir pob hawl. ABP Marine Environmental Research Ltd 2022. (h) Canolfan Gwyddorau\u0027r Amgylchedd, Pysgodfeydd a Dyframaethu (Cefas).\tThe approach applied in this study, used data acquired from readily available, open licence sources where possible. A hexagonal grid, clipped to the extent of the Welsh marine planning area, with cell size of 5 km2 was created in ArcGIS and this was spatially joined to input data layers, representative of the introduction pathways, to create heatmaps. A series of heatmaps were created for each of the introduction pathways, using a scaled scoring method. A final combined heatmap of all introduction pathways was also created by applying weightings to the different pathways. Figures of all heatmaps were created, as well as figures of designated sites overlying the final combined heatmap to show sites at potentially greater risk from the introduction of marine INNS.     The Celtic Sea (including Welsh and Irish waters) has approximately two to three new introductions of non-indigenous species every year (European Environment Agency (EEA), 2015; 2019a). Identifying coastal areas where invasive non-native species (INNS) are more likely to be introduced is a valuable tool which will help to focus management efforts and identify sensitive areas which may be at a higher risk. Mapping the intensity of introduction pathway activities can aid the identification of these higher risk areas.     This associated report provides an update to the work carried out by Tidbury et al. in 2014, which considered the potential for the introduction of INNS in coastal areas of UK and Ireland using a 50 km x 50 km grid. This study focuses only on Welsh waters and at a higher spatial resolution than the original study.\tDewey, N., Pack, K, Williamson, D, Walsh, A. 2020. Welsh Marine Invasive Non-Native Pathways Assessment NRW Evidence Report No: 459, 105pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Marine INNS Pathway Heatmaps\tInternal Custodian: Chloe Powell-Jennings, Senior Advisor (EMFF Biosecurity Project Officer), Southwest Operations.   Internal Custodian: Katherine Griffith, Senior Marine Advisor, Marine Area Management \u0026amp; Advice Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-08-12\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3602/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_marine_inns_pathways_heatmaps_for_wales_2020\t2025-12-03T17:20:52.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18942\tNRW_DS124808\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.856Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW Industry Regulation Teams\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383038\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Fisheries Teams boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for Industry regulation Teams post Organisation Design, July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries, incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tData retrieved via e-mail. Initially an inbox was created called ‘SME Project’ where an e-mail was sent out to team leaders asking for information. After the initial e-mail had been sent, most of the outgoing e-mails were then sent out from my personal e-mail address. Information stored on an excel spreadsheet which is based on the DMS for internal staff to view.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Industry Regulation Teams.lyr\tSophie Lewis, Team Member, Customer Management/Customer Hub\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-07-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18927\tNRW_DS124809\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.673Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW Integrated Engineering Teams\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383039\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Integrated Engineering Teams boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for Integrated engineering Teams post Organisation Design, July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries, incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tData retrieved via e-mail. Initially an inbox was created called ‘SME Project’ where an e-mail was sent out to team leaders asking for information. After the initial e-mail had been sent, most of the outgoing e-mails were then sent out from my personal e-mail address. Information stored on an excel spreadsheet which is based on the DMS for internal staff to view.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Integrated Engineering Teams.lyr\tMike Thomson, Specialist Advisor, Incident ManagementIncident and Emergencies, Flood and Operational Risk Management\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-07-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:08.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18945\tNRW_DS124810\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.864Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW Waste Regulation Teams\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383130\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Waste regulation Team boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for Integrated engineering Teams post Organisation Design, July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries, incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tData retrieved via e-mail. Initially an inbox was created called ‘SME Project’ where an e-mail was sent out to team leaders asking for information. After the initial e-mail had been sent, most of the outgoing e-mails were then sent out from my personal e-mail address. Information stored on an excel spreadsheet which is based on the DMS for internal staff to view.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Waste Regulation Teams.lyr\tSophie Lewis, Team Member, Customer Management/Customer Hub\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-07-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18941\tNRW_DS124811\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.873Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurface Water Catchments\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383131\tThe datasets shows surface water catchment areas.  The data was produced to create the National Hazard Maps for Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses (2019) as part of the National Flood Hazard Maps (2019)\tSurface Water Catchment boundaries are created from a hydrological routing analysis (using the SCALGO software package) of a coarsened (20m resolution) and filled Digital Terrain Model (Environment Agency Integrated Height Model, EA IHM, 2016).\tMore information is available in the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) Project Report and Appendices.   JBA Consulting  2019  Flood Risk Assessment Wales Project Report.  Report to Natural Resources Wales.     https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/teams/waterman/frm/ran/FRAW/2019/2018s0011%20-%20FRAW%20Project%20Report%20-%20And%20Appendices.pdf?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\Flood Hazard from Surface Water and Small Watercourses\\Surface water catchments.lyr\tSimon James Flood Risk Analysis (FCRM) David Tarrant Flood Risk Analysis (FCRM) Mark Pugh\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-07-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:14:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18933\tNRW_DS124813\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.639Z\t336\tValid\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Invertebrate Surveys\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383133\tThis report presents the results of surveys carried out for the Sands of LIFE project to understand the impact of dune slack rejuvenation and other sand dune restoration work on the invertebrate fauna. Dune slacks are known to support a range of rare and specialist invertebrate species that are expected to benefit from the works.  Pitfall trapping, timed sweep netting and visual searches were undertaken across seven project sites: Merthyr Mawr, Kenfig, Whiteford Burrows, Pembrey Burrows, Morfa Harlech, Newborough and Tywyn Aberffraw. Morfa Dinlle was also surveyed but only in 2019. Surveys were carried out in May, July and August in three separate years, 2019, 2021 and 2022. At each project site survey plots were identified in 2019 at locations where future conservation management interventions were planned, as well as nearby control areas.   The data includes invertebrates (Coleoptera, Diptera, Araneae, Opiliones, Hymenoptera, Hemiptera (Heteroptera) identified to species level together with their frequency for each survey plot in each project site.\tData was collected using pitfall trapping, sweep netting and visual searching in intervention (dune slack scrape and rabbit management) and control survey plots. Samples were sorted to order and identified by specialist entomologists.\tC.M. Benefer, E. Mayhead, R. Beaumont 2020. Sands of LIFE: Pre-intervention invertebrate monitoring. NRW Evidence Report Series, Report No: 457, 56pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.  Beaumont R. 2024. Sands of LIFE Post-intervention invertebrate monitoring. NRW Sands  of LIFE Evidence Report Series, Report No: 820, 89pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Invertbrate points May.lyr X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Invertbrate points July.lyr X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Invertbrate polygons    https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landman/Protected-Sites/so-life/D1%20Physical%20Monitoring/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;clickparams\u003deyAiWC1BcHBOYW1lIiA6ICJNaWNyb3NvZnQgT3V0bG9vayIsICJYLUFwcFZlcnNpb24iIDogIjE2LjAuMTgyMjcuMjAyMjIiLCAiT1MiIDogIldpbmRvd3MiIH0%3D\u0026amp;CID\u003df14f7ca1%2D20e6%2D0000%2D415a%2D9e4a3862147a\u0026amp;cidOR\u003dSPO\u0026amp;RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Flandman%2FProtected%2DSites%2Fso%2Dlife%2FD1%20Physical%20Monitoring%2F00%20SoLIFE%20Data%20Archive%2FInvertebrates\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000DC881C721DC6C9439F0968E82C157F72  Final combined dataset for post and pre surveys in Darwin Core Format found here - Z:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\Data Mgmt\\Data Services Data Publishing Area\\NBN Data\\NRW_DS124813_SoLIFE Inverts\tInternal Custodian: Kathryn Hewitt, Team Leader, Sands of LIFE Project\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-07-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-18T09:43:57.695Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18932\tNRW_DS124814\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.653Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Pierce, Alcove \u0026amp; Piercefield Woods SSSI, Coppice Mawr WGWE and Dingestow Court in SE Wales 2019\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383134\t****Metadata to be supplied by Mike Howe************  Have asked Mke if should be combined with Metadata entry 124733, which seems like it could be same of similiar dataset.  Saproxylic Invertebrate Survey of Coppice Mawr, Wye Valley Woodlands Special Area of Conservation (SAC) 2019\t\tAlexander, K.N.A. 2020. Saproxylic invertebrates collected in flight interception traps at Pierce, Alcove \u0026amp; Piercefield Woods SSSI, Coppice Mawr WGWE and Dingestow Court in the South East Wales landscape in 2019. NRW Evidence Report No: 452, pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tTo be entered into Recorder 6 - contact Mike Howe for R6 SurveyID\tMike Howe\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18943\tNRW_DS124815\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.897Z\t897\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Sand Lizard surveys\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383135\tLocations of lizard sightings during pre-intervention surveys for the Sands of LIFE project at Tywyn Aberffraw and Newborough Warren. Data was collected between April and October 2019.\tObservational data on reptile presence following standard survey methods. Locations recorded using handheld GPS accurate to at least 5m.\tGleed-Owen, C, 2020. Pre-intervention sand lizard surveys at Tywyn Aberffraw and Newborough Warren for Sands of LIFE. NRW Evidence Report No. 424 , 24pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\t[A] X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Sand Lizards 2019.lyr  [B] Spreadsheet version in DMS LAND-75481194-1033 and a copy provided to LERC (Cofnod)\tInternal Custodian: Toni Henwood, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South Wales, Sands of LIFE Project Internal Custodian: Kathryn Hewitt, Team Leader, Sands of LIFE Project\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-07-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:17:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18940\tNRW_DS124817\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.857Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWFD Rocky Shore Site Summary\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383137\tWater Framework Directive rocky shore site summary spatial data. Monitoring locations and years when monitoring has taken place are included in the data.\tA change in survey methodology occurred in 2018.\tThis data contributes to Natural Resources Wales Water Framework Directive classifications\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Rocky Shore\\WFD Rocky Shore Site Summary.lyr\tKate Borrowdale Swyddog Asesu Amgylcheddol Morol / Marine Environmental Assessment Officer Tîm Asesu ac Adrodd Monitro Morol / Marine Monitoring Assessment and Reporting Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-08-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:38.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18928\tNRW_DS124820\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.667Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW North Assistant Flood Incident Duty Officer Areas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383230\tAreas covered by the various Assisant Flood Incident Duty Officers within North Operations, with regards to flood incident management and operational response.\tAreas are based on agreed NRW administrative boundaries, and may be subject to change in future.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW North Assistant Flood Incident Duty Officer Areas.lyr\tEirian Gilford, Warning \u0026amp; Informing Team Leader, NRW Gwyn Moseley, Warning \u0026amp; Informing Advisor 2, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-08-14\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18935\tNRW_DS124825\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.694Z\t548\tValid\tEditable\tBait digging areas aerial imagery 2019/2020\tDelweddau awyr o ardaloedd cloddio abwyd 2019/2020\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383235\tThe data has been collected under a contract run under the Wales non-licensable activities project.  Bait digging and collection of living resources was identified as an activity which is causing damage to the Marine Protection Area (MPA) network in Wales.    The data obtained from this contract provides NRW with evidence which will help inform discussions around future management of this activity.    The data contains 15 aerial images (.tiff format) of 12 separate priority intertidal sites in Wales which were suspected of having bait digging occurring.  The images were captured between October 2019 and March 2020 with a drone.  They display visual evidence of bait digging that can be picked up by sediment disturbance and patterning which indicates digging activity.    From these aerial images, a GIS layer (Bait Digging Areas 2019_2020 NRW_DS124806) was created through interpretation of the visible evidence of bait digging from the aerial images.\tAerial images (.tiff) were collected using drones, mainly a Sensefly eBee and some additional capture with a DJI Phantom 4 Pro Plus) at approx. 120m, 1 pixel equating to approx. 3cm on the ground. Flight plans were designed using eMotion3 software and uploaded to the drone. The images captured were processed using Pix4D Mapper.\tPerrins, J., Lush, M., Taylor, T., Holt, R and Bunker, F. 2020. Investigating the location and intensity of bait digging in Wales. NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 449, 170pp, NRW, Bangor.\tX:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Drone footage\\Intertidal Drone Photos 2019-2020.lyr\tInternal Contact: Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor - Marine Ecologist, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-07-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-16T15:11:06.219Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18937\tNRW_DS124827\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.886Z\t12\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Transect and Quadrat points\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383237\tWater Framework Directive saltmarsh monitoring. The points in these layers show the location of the saltmarsh transects (landward, seaward and zone transitions) and saltmarsh quadrats and also additional species (if spatial data was recorded).\tThe saltmarsh transects and quadrats are surveyed on foot following WFD methodology. Spatial data is recorded using a hand held GPS device. Where the seaward point cannot be taken on foot, the point is derived from aerial imagery.\tThis data contributes to Natural Resources Wales Water Framework Directive Classifications.\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh\tKate Borrowdale Swyddog Asesu Amgylcheddol Morol / Marine Environmental Assessment Officer Tîm Asesu ac Adrodd Monitro Morol / Marine Monitoring Assessment and Reporting Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-09-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18931\tNRW_DS124828\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.673Z\t702\tValid\tEditable\tIndustrial and Commercial Waste Survey Wales 2018\tArolwg Gwastraff Diwydiannol a Masnachol Cymru 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383238\tThe purpose of this study was to produce information on the quantities, origins (by industry sector and geographic region), and fate (management method) of industrial \u0026amp; commercial (I and C) wastes generated by businesses in Wales in 2018.  This information is required by Welsh Government for a variety of reasons including:  reporting on waste generation in compliance with the EU Waste Statistics Regulations 2002; informing the development of national waste policy; monitoring progress against national waste prevention and recycling targets; informing waste planners and the regulator; and providing data to the waste management industry to inform investment decisions.  The precision for the total waste generated is +/- 4.54% at 90% confidence.-Diben yr astudiaeth hon oedd cynhyrchu gwybodaeth am symiau, tarddiadau (yn ôl sector diwydiant a rhanbarth daearyddol), a thynged (dull rheoli) gwastraff Diwydiannol a Masnachol a gynhyrchwyd gan fusnesau yng Nghymru yn 2018.  Mae’r wybodaeth hon yn ofynnol gan Lywodraeth Cymru am nifer o resymau gan gynnwys:  -          adrodd ar gynhyrchu gwastraff yn unol â Rheoliadau Ystadegau Gwastraff yr UE 2002;  -          cyfarwyddo datblygiad polisi gwastraff cenedlaethol;  -          monitro cynnydd yn erbyn targedau atal ac ailgylchu gwastraff cenedlaethol;  -          hysbysu cynllunwyr gwastraff a\u0027r rheoleiddiwr; a  -          darparu data i\u0027r diwydiant rheoli gwastraff i gyfarwyddo penderfyniadau buddsoddi.  Casglwyd data o sampl gynrychioliadol o 1,755 safle busnes o wahanol sectorau a meintiau ledled Cymru rhwng mis Ebrill 2019 a mis Hydref 2019. Cafodd y data ei grosio gan ddefnyddio data poblogaeth safleoedd busnes y Swyddfa Ystadegau Gwladol (SYG) i lefel ranbarthol a chenedlaethol yng Nghymru. Roedd y fethodoleg a ddefnyddiwyd yn yr arolwg hwn yn gymharol ar y cyfan â’r arolygon Diwydiannol a Masnachol blaenorol a gwblhawyd yng Nghymru, y darparodd y rhai mwyaf diweddar ohonynt ddata ar gyfer blwyddyn galendr 2012.  Y manwl gywirdeb ar gyfer cyfanswm y gwastraff a gynhyrchwyd yw +/- 4.54% gyda hyder o 90%.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tData was collected from a representative sample of 1,755 business sites of differing sectors and sizes throughout Wales between April 2019 and October 2019. The data was grossed up using Office for National Statistics (ONS) business site population data to regional and national level in Wales. The ONS data showed that there were 97,999 Industrial and Commercial business sites in Wales in 2018 with 7% in Industrial sectors and 93% in Commercial sectors. Of these business sites, 65% had fewer than five employees and 2% had one hundred or more employees. The methodology used in this survey was mainly comparable with the previous IandC surveys completed in Wales, of which the most recent provided data for the 2012 calendar year.\t\tDMS\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Marcus Fogarty, Project Manager  Metadata Point of Contact:  Marcus Fogarty, Project Manager\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-01-05\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2647\t2025-05-07T09:08:01.094Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18926\tNRW_DS124830\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.547Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNitrate Vulnerable Zones (NVZ) Groundwater Points\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383330\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Nitrates Directive\\NVZ Groundwater Points.lyr\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:13:42.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18938\tNRW_DS124831\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.657Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNitrate Vulnerable Zones (NVZ) Shallow Sandy Soil\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383331\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Nitrates Directive\\NVZ Shallow Sandy Soil.lyr\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:17:12.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18930\tNRW_DS124832\t2024-05-28T12:58:34.571Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNitrate Vulnerable Zones (NVZ) Surface Water Points\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383332\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Nitrates Directive\\NVZ Surfacewater Points.lyr\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:42.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18925\tNRW_DS124833\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.819Z\t9\tValid\tEditable\tThe Freshwater and Terrestrial Protected Sites Baseline Evaluation Project Results\tCanlyniadau Prosiect Gwerthuso Sylfaen Safleoedd Gwarchodedig Daearol a Dŵr Croyw\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383333\tThe Freshwater and Terrestrial Protected Sites Baseline evaluation project (henceforth known as the ‘Baseline evaluation project’) uses existing evidence to derive, where possible, ‘indicative’ feature condition assessments across the range of freshwater and terrestrial features on protected sites (SAC, SPA, SSSI) in Wales.\t1. A trial exercise that will focus solely on one operational area to gather indicative condition assessments regarding the state of 26 features.   2. Establishing a what we learned log after the trial period has finished to determine what we can bring forward to the main exercise.   3. A main exercise where each operational area (including Freshwater and Earth Science) are asked to provide indicative condition assessments on the state of their features across that particular operational area. Monthly updates on progress will be issued.   4. Collating all of the data received together after the main exercise has been completed.  5. The Outputs of this exercise were published internally and externally.\t\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/en-gb/ourwork/Pages/Baseline-Evaluation-results-fuel-calls-for-partnership-approach-to-a-nature-rich-future-for-Wales.aspx\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dylan Lloyd, (Thomas Wright, Sue Byrne ), Monitoring Strategy \u0026amp; Planning.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-12-08\tpublication\tnull\thttps://naturalresources.wales/evidence-and-data/research-and-reports/protected-sites-baseline-assessment-2020/?lang\u003den\t2025-12-04T10:40:46.145Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n18923\tNRW_DS124838\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.699Z\t747\tValid\tEditable\tWater Resource Availability\tData ar Argaeledd Adnoddau Dŵr\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383338\tThe Water Resource Availability data is based on a nationally consistent method and provides a national picture of Water Resource Availability for each Water Framework Directive Cycle 2 water body. Local Water Resource Availability for abstraction licensing is provided in our Abstraction Licensing Strategies (ALS) published on our website.  ALS outline the implications for abstraction licensing of the different water resource availability colours.  This national picture of Water Resource Availability may be different to local assessments in ALS particularly for regulated rivers and rivers designated as Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) where local requirements may mean that results have been overridden.-Seiliwyd y data Argaeledd Adnoddau Dŵr ar ddull cenedlaethol cyson ac maent yn rhoi darlun o Argaeledd Adnoddau Dŵr ar gyfer pob corff dŵr Cylch 2 o dan y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr. Darperir gwybodaeth am Argaeledd Adnoddau Dŵr Lleol at ddibenion trwyddedu tynnu dŵr yn ein Strategaethau Trwyddedu Tynnu Dŵr a gyhoeddir ar ein gwefan. Mae’r Strategaethau Trwyddedu Tynnu Dŵr yn nodi goblygiadau’r gwahanol liwiau argaeledd adnoddau dŵr ar drwyddedu tynnu dŵr.  Gallai’r darlun cenedlaethol o Argaeledd Adnoddau Dŵr fod yn wahanol i’r asesiadau lleol a wneir mewn Strategaethau Trwyddedu Tynnu Dŵr, yn enwedig felly ar gyfer afonydd wedi’u rheoleiddio ac afonydd a ddynodwyd yn Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig, lle gallai’r gofynion lleol olygu bod y canlyniadau wedi’u diystyru.  Disgrifiadau Argaeledd Adnoddau Dŵr  -          Gwyrdd: Dŵr ar gael i’w drwyddedu  -          Melyn: Dŵr cyfyngedig ar gael i’w drwyddedu  -          Coch: Nid yw’r dŵr ar gael i’w drwyddedu  -          Llwyd: Cyrff dŵr wedi’u haddasu’n sylweddol a/neu ddalgylchoedd sy’n gollwng llawer o ddŵr  Nid data crai mo’r rhain, ac nid ydynt yn ffeithiol nac yn fesuredig. Amcangyfrifon neu ganlyniadau wedi’u modelu ydynt, sy’n dangos y canlyniadau y gellir eu disgwyl ar sail y data sydd ar gael inni.   Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl. Rhai nodweddion o\u0027r wybodaeth hon yn seiliedig ar ddata gofodol digidol a drwyddedwyd oddi wrth y Ganolfan Ecoleg a Hydroleg © NERC (CEH). Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans © Hawlfraint y Goron a hawl cronfa ddata 2015.\tEach water body is colour coded as follows:  Green - Water available for licensing  Yellow - Restricted water available for licensing  Red - Water not available for licensing  Grey - Heavily Modified Waterbodies (and /or discharge rich water bodies).   This data is not raw, factual or measured. It comprises of estimated or modelled results showing expected outcomes based on the data available to us.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Resource Availability Wales.lyr\tEmma Amos Water Resource Planning Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-07-06\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3478/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wrgis_resource_availability\t2025-05-08T16:31:27.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18921\tNRW_DS124839\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.571Z\t20\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Risk Assessments for Cycle 3\tY Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr – Asesiadau Risg, Cylch 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383339\tWe have reviewed water quality data and information on the types and magnitude of pressures affecting water bodies across Walest to assess how susceptible they are to those pressures and, in particular, estimate the likelihood of water bodies failing to meet their environmental quality objectives in the future, or deteriorating from their current condition. The methodology for each risk assessment was tailored to the specific pressure, but in general, it was an assessment of the scale of the pressure and the sensitivity of the water body.   The risk assessments are available as maps for each individual pressure, including: chemicals and metals, dissolved inorganic nitrogen, phosphates, dissolved oxygen and ammonia, faecal indicator organisms, acidification, abstraction and flow, invasive non-native species and sediment. They are valid until 2027. We will use the risk assessments to identify where more data is and prioritise our monitoring strategy, and support the development of local and national programmes of measures, including for pressures where Water Framework Directive classification data is missing.\tThis data was collected to support the cycle 3 river basin management plans. The risk assessments originally compilled in 2014 for cycle 2 and are valid to 2027. The assessments for chemicals and metals, dissolved inorganic nitrogen, phosphates, dissolved oxygen and ammonia were revised in 2019.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Risk Assessments\\2019\tInternal Data Custodian(s): David Allen, Monitoring Analysis Team Leader, NRW Metadata Point of Contact: David Allen, Monitoring Analysis Team Leader, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-10-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:55:18.869Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18924\tNRW_DS124841\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.720Z\t652\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW)\tAsesiad Perygl Llifogydd Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383431\tFlood Risk Assessment Wales provides a national assessment of risk flooding from Rivers, the Sea and Surface Water and Small Watercourses. The assessment takes into account flood defences and combines new, national-scale modelling with detailed local-scale models to categories risk into 3 bands, labelled ‘High’, ‘Medium’ and ‘Low’ risk.-Mae Asesiad Perygl Llifogydd Cymru yn darparu asesiad cenedlaethol o berygl llifogydd o afonydd, y môr a dŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bychain (gan ddisodli set ddata Perygl Llifogydd o Afonydd a\u0027r Môr).  Mae\u0027r asesiad yn ystyried amddiffynfeydd rhag llifogydd ac yn cyfuno gwaith modelu newydd ar raddfa genedlaethol â modelau manwl ar raddfa leol i greu tri band o ddosbarthiad risg o dan y labeli \u0027Uchel\u0027, \u0027Canolig\u0027 ac \u0027Isel\u0027.  Ar gyfer Afonydd a Dŵr Wyneb a Chyrsiau Dŵr Bach:  Mae risg ‘Uchel’ yn golygu bod siawns o lifogydd o fwy nag 1 mewn 30 (3.3%) bob blwyddyn yn yr ardal hon. Mae risg ‘Ganolig’ yn golygu bod siawns o lifogydd o rhwng 1 mewn 100 (1%) ac 1 mewn 30 (3.3%) bob blwyddyn mewn ardal. Mae risg ‘Isel’ yn golygu bod siawns o lifogydd o rhwng 1 mewn 1000 (0.1%) ac 1 mewn 100 (1%) bob blwyddyn mewn ardal. Ar gyfer y Môr:  Mae risg ‘Uchel’ yn golygu bod siawns o lifogydd o fwy nag 1 mewn 30 (3.3%) bob blwyddyn yn yr ardal hon. Mae risg ‘Ganolig’ yn golygu bod siawns o lifogydd o rhwng 1 mewn 200 (0.5%) ac 1 mewn 30 (3.3%) bob blwyddyn mewn ardal. Mae risg ‘Isel’ yn golygu bod siawns o lifogydd o rhwng 1 mewn 1000 (0.1%) ac 1 mewn 200 (0.5%) bob blwyddyn mewn ardal. Mae\u0027r asesiad risg yn ystyried effaith amddiffynfeydd rhag llifogydd pan fo gennym wybodaeth. Mae amddiffynfeydd rhag llifogydd yn lleihau llifogydd, ond nid ydynt yn atal y siawns o lifogydd yn gyfan gwbl oherwydd gall dŵr fynd drostynt neu gallant fethu.  Mae gwybodaeth perygl llifogydd ar gyfer pob ffynhonnell wedi\u0027i chreu fel \u0027croen nionyn\u0027, h.y. heb unrhyw ddata sy\u0027n gorgyffwrdd rhwng bandiau risg unigol. Dylid cyfuno\u0027r bandiau risg hyn a\u0027u harddangos fel un haen ar gyfer pob ffynhonnell llifogydd.  Os dangosir data ar ffurf mapiau i eraill, dylid defnyddio graddfeydd map priodol o ystyried natur ddangosol y modelu.  Dylid ei ddangos gyda graddfa chwyddo mwyaf o 1:5,000 a defnyddio mapio sylfaen o 1:10,000 i roi cyd-destun.  Mae\u0027r mapio llifogydd yn seiliedig ar ardaloedd sydd mewn perygl o ddioddef llifogydd ac nid yw\u0027n benodol ar gyfer eiddo unigol. Nid yw CNC yn cadw gwybodaeth am adeiladwaith eiddo unigol a allai effeithio ar ba un ai y gallai llifogydd o ddyfnder penodol fynd i mewn i eiddo ac achosi difrod. Mae ein gwybodaeth fel y cyfryw wedi\u0027i chynhyrchu i nodi ardaloedd cyffredinol a allai fod mewn perygl o ddioddef llifogydd ar gyfer ymchwiliad pellach.  Cydraniad gofodol y data:  Modelu ar raddfa genedlaethol a wnaed gan ddefnyddio cydraniad grid o 2m.  Modelu ar raddfa leol a wnaed gan ddefnyddio gwahanol gydraniadau, yn nodweddiadol rhwng 2m – 10m.  Mae rhagor o wybodaeth ar gael yn adroddiad prosiect FRAW (2019) a thudalen we CNC Deall eich canlyniadau perygl llifogydd (https://naturalresources.wales/flooding/managing-flood-risk/understanding-your-flood-risk-results?lang\u003dcy ) i gael mwy o wybodaeth am ein data perygl llifogydd a sut y dylid ei ddefnyddio.  Mae\u0027r Lefel Perygl dan Adolygiad yn nodi lleoliadau lle mae ein hasesiad risg o dan adolygiad dros y chwe mis nesaf oherwydd argaeledd gwybodaeth newydd o fodelau ar raddfa leol.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Mae rhai nodweddion o\u0027r wybodaeth hon yn seiliedig ar ddata gofodol digidol wedi\u0027i drwyddedu gan Ganolfan Ecoleg a Hydroleg y DU © UKCEH DEFRA, y Swyddfa Dywydd ac Asiantaeth Afonydd yr Adran Amaethyddiaeth a Datblygu Gwledig © Hawlfraint y Goron. © Prifysgol Cranfield. © Sefydliad James Hutton. Yn cynnwys data OS © Hawlfraint y Goron a hawl cronfa ddata.\tFor Rivers and Surface Water and Small Watercourses:  - ‘High’ risk means that each year, this area has a chance of flooding of greater than 1 in 30 (3.3%)  - ‘Medium’ risk means that each year, an area has a chance of flooding of between 1 in 100 (1%) and 1 in 30 (3.3%).  - ‘Low’ risk means that each year, an area has a chance of flooding of between 1 in 1000 (0.1%) and 1 in 100 (1%).    For the Sea:  - ‘High’ risk means that each year, this area has a chance of flooding of greater than 1 in 30 (3.3%)  - ‘Medium’ risk means that each year, an area has a chance of flooding of between 1 in 200 (0.5%) and 1 in 30 (3.3%).  - ‘Low’ risk means that each year, an area has a chance of flooding of between 1 in 1000 (0.1%) and 1 in 200 (0.5%)    The risk assessment takes into account the effect of flood defences where we have information. Flood defences reduce, but do not completely stop the chance of flooding as they can be overtopped or fail.   Flood risk information for each source has been created as ‘onion-skins’, i.e. with no overlapping data between individual risk bands. These risk bands should be combined and displayed a single layer for each flood source.   If data is displayed in map form to others, appropriate map scales should be used given the indicative nature of the modelling.  It should be shown with a maximum zoom scale of 1:5,000 and that base mapping of 1:10,000 is used to give context.    The flood mapping is based on areas at risk of flooding and is not specific to individual properties. NRW doesn’t hold information about an individual property’s construction that might affect whether flooding of a certain depth could enter a property and cause damage. As such, our information has been produced to identify general areas potentially at risk of flooding for further investigation.    Spatial resolution of the data:  National scale modelling undertaken at 2m grid resolution. Local scale modelling undertaken at different resolutions, typically between 2m – 10m.\tMore information is available in the FRAW (2019) project report and NRWs Understanding your flood risk results webpage (https://naturalresources.wales/flooding/managing-flood-risk/understanding-your-flood-risk-results?lang\u003den) for more information on our flood risk data and how it should be used.\tX\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Risk Assessment Wales\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-11-26\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/889/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:FloodRiskAssessmentWales\t2025-11-28T10:36:01.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18922\tNRW_DS124842\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.661Z\t807\tValid\tEditable\tRisk Levels Under Review\tLefelau Risg sy\u0027n cael eu Hadolygu\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383432\tThe Risk Level Under Review identifies locations where model data incorporated to the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) map is under review and potentially liable to change within the next 6 months.-Mae\u0027r Lefel Perygl dan Adolygiad yn nodi lleoliadau lle mae ein hasesiad risg o dan adolygiad dros y chwe mis nesaf oherwydd argaeledd gwybodaeth newydd o fodelau ar raddfa leol.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Mae rhai nodweddion o\u0027r wybodaeth hon yn seiliedig ar ddata gofodol digidol wedi\u0027i drwyddedu gan Ganolfan Ecoleg a Hydroleg y DU © UKCEH DEFRA, y Swyddfa Dywydd ac Asiantaeth Afonydd yr Adran Amaethyddiaeth a Datblygu Gwledig © Hawlfraint y Goron. © Prifysgol Cranfield. © Sefydliad James Hutton. Yn cynnwys data OS © Hawlfraint y Goron a hawl cronfa ddata.\tRisk Level Under Review is based on the availability of approved local-scale model data to update flood risk information displayed in the FRAW map.\tContact our Flood Risk Analysis team via enquiries@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk for more information.\tCentral GIS Store X:\\Layer_Files\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tMark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)   Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-11-28\tpublication\tnull\thttps://naturalresources.wales/flooding/check-your-flood-risk-by-postcode/?lang\u003den\t2025-12-19T12:14:04.101Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18919\tNRW_DS124843\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.439Z\t1310\tValid\tEditable\tAreas Benefiting From Flood Defences\tArdaloedd sy’n Elwa ar Amddiffynfeydd rhag Llifogydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383433\tThe Areas Benefitting from Flood Defences show areas in Wales that benefit from flood defences that protect against flooding from Rivers and the Sea.  The areas shown benefit from different levels of flood protection and this is recognised in the risk classification shown in the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) map.-Mae Ardaloedd sy\u0027n Elwa ar Amddiffynfeydd rhag Llifogydd yn dangos ardaloedd Cymru sy\u0027n elwa ar amddiffynfeydd rhag llifogydd sy\u0027n amddiffyn rhag llifogydd o afonydd a\u0027r môr. Mae\u0027r ardaloedd a ddangosir yn elwa ar lefelau gwahanol o amddiffyniad rhag llifogydd a chydnabyddir hyn yn y dosbarthiad risg a ddangosir ar fap Asesiad Perygl Llifogydd Cymru.  Mae\u0027r ffin allanol ar gyfer yr Ardaloedd sy\u0027n Elwa ar Amddiffynfeydd rhag Llifogydd wedi\u0027i gosod ar y terfyn lle ceir risg isel o lifogydd o afonydd neu\u0027r môr (h.y. yr ardal sydd siawns o 0.01%, neu 1 mewn 1,000, o gael llifogydd mewn unrhyw flwyddyn).  Nid yw\u0027n benodol i eiddo ac mae\u0027n dangos y budd ar gyfer ardal gyffredinol.  Mae\u0027r wybodaeth hon yn wahanol i\u0027r Map Llifogydd: Ardaloedd sy\u0027n Elwa ar Amddiffynfeydd rhag Llifogydd sydd wedi ymddangos cyn hyn, a ddangosodd dim ond y budd a ddarperir os bydd llifogydd afon gyda siawns o 1% (1 mewn 100) o ddigwydd bob blwyddyn, neu lifogydd o\u0027r môr gyda siawns o 0.5% (1 mewn 200) o ddigwydd bob blwyddyn. Mae gwybodaeth bellach ar gael yn y metadata.  Mae gwybodaeth bellach ar gael yn y metadata.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe outer boundary for the Areas Benefitting from Flood Defences is fixed to the low risk flood extent for flooding from Rivers or the Sea (i.e. the area with a 0.1%, or 1 in 1,000, chance of flooding in any year).    The data is not property specific and shows the benefit for a general area.   This information is different to the previous Flood Map: Areas Benefitting from Flood Defences which only showed the benefit provided in the event of a river flood with a 1% (1 in 100) chance of happening each year, or a flood from the sea with a 0.5% (1 in 200) chance of happening each year.    Not all flood defences and areas that benefit from a flood defence are shown. These will gradually be added as information becomes available.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Areas Benefiting From Flood Defences\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/594/?ows_service\u003dwms\tnull\t2025-12-01T11:29:04.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18920\tNRW_DS124844\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.572Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Fen Orchid Monitoring 2020\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383434\tLocation and phenology of fen orchids in January 2016 North slack and Lpost Extension Scrape as recorded in August 2020. Boundaries of the slacks are also included. Species abundance data is also recorded in separate spreadsheets.\tBoundaries based on NRW Kenfig slack boundary shapefiles.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Fen Orchid locations 2020.lyr    X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Slack boundaries Fen Orchid 2020.lyr    Species data held in DMS as document LAND-75481194-1312  (SOL-JBAU-LL-XX-DB-EN-0001-S3-PO1-Species_records.xls) Full DMS File path: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landman/Protected-Sites/so-life/D1%20Physical%20Monitoring/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Flandman%2FProtected%2DSites%2Fso%2Dlife%2FD1%20Physical%20Monitoring%2FKenfig%20Fen%20Orchid%20monitoring\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000DC881C721DC6C9439F0968E82C157F72\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BC00F4132%2D066F%2D4E23%2D8D53%2D597F2DDD6C62%7D\tInternal Custodian: Toni Henwood, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South Wales, Sands of LIFE Project Internal Custodian: Kathryn Hewitt, Team Leader, Sands of LIFE Project\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-10-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:35:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18918\tNRW_DS124845\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.360Z\t859\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Risk Areas (Cycle 2)\tArdaloedd Perygl Llifogydd (Cylch 2)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383435\tThe Cycle 2 Flood Risk Areas are those communities in Wales where most significant flood risks exist across all sources of flood risk. They were identified for the purpose of the second Preliminary Flood Risk Assessment under the Flood Risk Regulations 2009.   The threshold for communities at significant risk of flooding was set by Welsh Government with input from Natural Resources Wales and the Welsh Local Government Association (representing Lead Local Flood Authorities).    The Cycle 2 Flood Risk Areas were identified in December 2018 and replace the Cycle 1 Flood Risk Areas.-Ardaloedd Perygl Llifogydd Cylch 2 yw\u0027r cymunedau hynny yng Nghymru lle mae\u0027r peryglon llifogydd mwyaf sylweddol yn bodoli ar draws pob ffynhonnell perygl llifogydd. Cawson nhw eu nodi at ddiben yr ail Asesiad Cychwynnol o’r Bygythiad Llifogydd o dan Reoliadau Perygl Llifogydd 2009. Pennwyd y trothwy ar gyfer cymunedau sydd mewn perygl sylweddol o lifogydd gan Lywodraeth Cymru gyda mewnbwn gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chymdeithas Llywodraeth Leol Cymru (sy’n cynrychioli\u0027r Awdurdodau Llifogydd Lleol Arweiniol).  Nodwyd Ardaloedd Perygl Llifogydd Cylch 2 ym mis Rhagfyr 2018 ac maent yn disodli Ardaloedd Perygl Llifogydd Cylch 1.  Am wybodaeth ychwanegol am Ardaloedd Perygl Llifogydd, ewch i adroddiadau’r Asesiad Cychwynnol o’r Bygythiad Llifogydd sydd ar gael ar wefan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (a welir yma).\tThe Cycle 2 Flood Risk Areas have been identified using the Communities at Risk Register (CaRR). Communities in Wales have been ranked from high to low and those considered to be at most significant risk (from any source) selected. The threshold for communities at significant risk was set by The Minister for Environment with input from Natural Resources Wales and the Welsh Local Government Association (representing Lead Local Flood Authorities).\tFor additional information refer to the Preliminary Flood Risk Assessment reports available on the NRW website.  https://naturalresourceswales.gov.uk/evidence-and-data/research-and-reports/reports-evidence-and-data-on-flooding/preliminary-flood-risk-assessment/?lang\u003den\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-06-29\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3359/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_flood_risk_areas_cycle_2\t2025-12-19T10:39:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18917\tNRW_DS124850\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.303Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFCW Woodland Officer Areas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383530\tMap of Wales split into 8 sections, each section covers the area of responsibility for each of the Regulatory Woodland Officers. The attributes are the name, phone numbers, email address for the area covered. Purpose: Staff information\tSource material: changes are updated via the GIS system.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\FCW Woodland Officer Areas.lyr\tSuzanne Lilley-Owen, Permitting Team Leader Forestry, Permitting Services (Forestry)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-10-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18916\tNRW_DS124851\t2024-05-28T12:58:33.254Z\t989\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Tree Cover 2018\tGorchudd Coed Trefol 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383531\tThis study updates the data on Urban Tree Canopy Cover in Wales, highlighting changes in the extent, location and provision of urban tree cover since 2013. This spatial dataset relates to Phase 4 and consists of 2 individual layers, being Points (individual trees) and polygons (groups of trees).-Mae’r set ddata ofodol hon yn gysylltiedig â Cham 4 (2018) Urban Tree Cover ac yn cynnwys 3 lefel unigol, sef Pwyntiau (coed unigol), polygonau (grwpiau o goed) a meintiau ardaloedd trefol (maint yr ardaloedd astudio). Yn wahanol i Gam 1 a Cham 2 ni chafodd dosbarthiadau defnydd tir eu creu ar gyfer cam 3 a 4. Mae’r adroddiad technegol yn dangos beth sy’n cael ei gynnwys ym mhob Cam, ac mae’n rhoi mwy o fanylion ar y fethodoleg y tu ôl i’r gwahanol haenau yn y set ddata. Cydnabyddiaeth Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis data was captured using desktop analyses of aerial photography 2018. Methodology is described in NRW (2020) Tree Cover in Wales’ Towns and Cities: update 2020: Updated information to help us understand canopy cover to better plan and manage our urban trees.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Urban Tree Canopy Cover\\\tJohn Briggs\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-01-13\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/1109/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:UrbanTreeCover2018\t2025-05-20T08:53:12.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18974\tNRW_DS124858\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.490Z\t339\tValid\tEditable\tDistribution data for spider Mioxena blanda at Newborough Warren – Ynys Llanddwyn (SSSI) 2019-2020\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383538\tA survey to improve our understanding of the ecology and distribution of the Nationally Rare money spider Mioxena blanda was conducted at Newborough Warren – Ynys Llanddwyn SSSI. The survey ran between 28th September 2019 and 7th March 2020, employing a variety of survey techniques: terrestrial and aerial pitfall traps, vacuum sampling, grubbing, sieving, rock turning and hand sampling.\tSix pitfall trapping stations were established on the SSSI: three along the Malltraeth Cob, two in Newborough Forest and one on the Newborough Dunes. At each of these trapping stations both aerial and terrestrial pitfall traps were deployed.\tGallon, R.C. 2020. The status and distribution of the spider Mioxena blanda on Newborough Warren – Ynys Llanddwyn SSSI in 2019-2020. NRW Evidence Report No. 495\tStored on the WID in Recorder 6\tMike Howe\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:01:51.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18963\tNRW_DS124859\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.221Z\t711\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring the Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae at Dale \u0026amp; South Marloes Coast SSSI in Pembrokeshire in 2020 and a survey for further populations on nearby beaches\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383539\tThe Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae is found in Wales only within Dale \u0026amp; South Marloes Coast SSSI where it was first recorded in 1999 and again surveyed in 2006. The 2020 survey found that suitable habitat exists along approximately just 262 metres at Marloes Sands and 40 metres at West Dale Bay. A total of 287 adults (male and female) and nymphs of Scaly Cricket was recorded in 2020, indicating that breeding populations exist at both Marloes Sands and West Dale Bay. However, Scaly Cricket was found at just four monitoring plots at Marloes Sands and suitable habitat was present in just six plots.\tMonitoring in September 2020 took place at the known sites for Scaly Cricket on Marloes Sands and West Dale Bay and involved hand searching (stone turning) and counting the numbers of adults and nymphs seen. Monitoring covered approximately 2.0km of Marloes Sands and 0.25km of West Dale Bay.\tJon Hudson Ecological Consultancy. 2020. Monitoring the Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae at Dale \u0026amp; South Marloes Coast SSSI in Pembrokeshire in 2020 and a survey for further populations on nearby beaches. NRW Evidence Report No: 496, 23pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tData held on the WID in Recorded 6\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T14:58:11.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18970\tNRW_DS124861\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.486Z\t362\tValid\tEditable\tIndicative feature condition assessments and reasons for failure for Marine Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) habitats in Wales.\tAsesiadau cyflwr nodweddion dangosol a rhesymau dros fethiant ar gyfer cynefinoedd Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACA) Morol yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383631\tThis is a spatial dataset detailing the location of Special Area of Conservation (SAC) habitat features, their condition and reasons for failure where applicable.\tThe information was created in GIS using information from Natural Resources Wales’ Indicative condition assessments and Article 17 habitats.  Indicative condition assessments: Definition and use: The term ‘indicative condition assessment’ describes the use of readily available evidence and expert judgement in an intensive, collective workshop process to provide an indication of feature condition at the site level. The confidence rating associated with the assessments is an integral part of the indicative assessment. Confidence levels for feature assessments should therefore always be quoted alongside the indicative condition result, together with NRW’s definition of ‘indicative condition assessment’.\tFor full information on Indicative Site Condition Assessment see:   https://naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/environmental-topics/wildlife-and-biodiversity/protected-areas-of-land-and-seas/indicative-feature-condition-assessments-for-european-marine-sites-ems/?lang\u003den\tY:\\Marine\\Marine and Coastal Policy and Planning Team\\MPA CIP Mapping\\FINAL Welsh SAC Designated Habitat Features Condition and Reasons for Failure.gdb  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine SAC features\\Indicative Assessments 2018.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine SAC features\\Indicative Assessments 2018\\Features.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine SAC features\\Indicative Assessments 2018\\Sites.lyr   Further spreadsheet located in the DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/mar/MPAImpProg/ProgDocs/Features_Sites_Reasons_for_failure_final.csv?d\u003dwdf33cfb0e9e74aedabbc8318ff0b5c78\tInternal Contact: Harriet Robinson, Specialist Advisor - Marine Systems, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-11-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T15:19:35.444Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18964\tNRW_DS124863\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.208Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW People and Places teams\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383633\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales People and Places team boundaries. The purpose of this data capture is to define boundaries for People and Places Teams post Organisation Design, July 2019. Will be used to ensure enquiries, incidents etc are passed to the correct team and to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities.\tData retrieved via e-mail. Initially an inbox was created called ‘SME Project’ where an e-mail was sent out to team leaders asking for information. After the initial e-mail had been sent, most of the outgoing e-mails were then sent out from my personal e-mail address. Information stored on an excel spreadsheet which is based on the DMS for internal staff to view.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW People and Places teams.lyr\tMike Thomson, Specialist Advisor, Incident ManagementIncident and Emergencies, Flood and Operational Risk Management\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-06-24\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:48.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18971\tNRW_DS124865\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.425Z\t609\tValid\tEditable\tCompliance Assessment of Welsh River SACs against Phosphorus Targets\tAsesiad o gydymffurfiaeth Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig afonydd Cymru yn erbyn targedau ffosfforws\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383635\tRevisions to Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) monitoring guidance have required Natural Resources Wales (NRW) to review its conservation objectives for river Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales, notably with respect to phosphorus where targets have been substantially tightened.   This data was created to identify compliance with the revised phosphorus targets for Welsh river SACs. Phosphorus concentration data were extracted from the NRW water quality database (KiWQM), for a three-year period from January 2017 to December 2019 for all sample points within water bodies in the nine SACs designated for one or more river features. These were Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn; Afon Eden – Cors Goch Trawsfynydd; Meirionnydd Oakwoods (Afon Glaslyn); Afon Teifi; Afon Tywi; Afonydd Cleddau; River Dee \u0026amp; Llyn Tegid; River Usk; and River Wye. In total, 125 water bodies (river sections) were in scope.   Data were excluded where (i) detection limits were inadequate to assess compliance with the target or (ii) where the sample exceeded the recommended holding time of three days prior to analysis. Outliers in the data were identified and, if they were considered likely to be causing a failure on their own, data from other nutrient pollution indicators (biochemical oxygen demand, nitrate and ammonia) were checked to corroborate possible pollution events.   The data consists of two products: 1. An Excel spreadsheet summarising results and 2. An ArcGIS layer consisting of the WFD catchment area water bodies, clipped to the Welsh border and excluding water bodies not overlapping with river SAC boundaries.-Mae diwygiadau i ganllawiau monitro\u0027r Cydbwyllgor Cadwraeth Natur wedi’i gwneud yn ofynnol i Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) adolygu ei amcanion cadwraeth ar gyfer Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig sy’n seiliedig ar afonydd yng Nghymru, yn enwedig o ran ffosfforws, lle mae\u0027r targedau wedi\u0027u tynhau\u0027n sylweddol. Crëwyd y data hwn i nodi cydymffurfiaeth â\u0027r targedau ffosfforws diwygiedig ar gyfer Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig sy’n seiliedig ar afonydd yng Nghymru. Tynnwyd data crynodiad ffosfforws o gronfa ddata ansawdd dŵr CNC am gyfnod o dair blynedd rhwng Ionawr 2017 a Rhagfyr 2019 ar gyfer yr holl bwyntiau sampl o fewn cyrff dŵr yn y naw Ardal Cadwraeth Arbennig a ddynodwyd ar gyfer un neu fwy o nodweddion afon. Datganiad priodoli Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\t1. Summarised from NRW KiWQM database;  2. WFD water bodies catchment layer on X drive. 3. The data was updated in June 2022 to correct mistakes in the original version\tHatton-Ellis TW, Jones TG. 2020. Compliance Assessment of Welsh River SACs against Phosphorus Targets. NRW Evidence Report No: 489, 95pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Compliance with NRW River SAC Phosphorus Targets 2017-19 by Catchment Waterbodies X:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Phosphorus Sensitive SAC Freshwater Catchments\tTristan Hatton-Ellis\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-02-22\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/1113/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:ComplianceAssessmentOfWelshRiverSacsAgainstPhosphorusTargets\t2025-02-04T10:17:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18965\tNRW_DS124867\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.311Z\t11\tUnassessed\tEditable\tExtended Phase 1 Habitat Survey of the Gwent Levels Sea Wall October 2020\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383637\tThis GIS dataset maps an Extended Phase 1 Habitat Survey of the Gwent Levels Sea Wall between Cardiff and Chepstow completed in summer 2020. Red Data Book (RDB) species, invasive species, notable arable weeds and other notable species are also recorded. Habitats have also been assigned provisional NVC communities. Suggested management prescriptions to improve the value of the sea wall for Shrill Carder Bee, and general biodiversity are described and an evaluation of ‘landscape potential’ for Shrill Carder Bee is given.   There are five datasets:  SeaWall_Photo_point.lyr,   SeaWall_Summary.lyr,   SeaWall_Phase1.lyr,   SeaWall_Phase1_polyline.lyr,   SeaWall_LandscapeHabitatPotential.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Governance Licence and available for public use, see Limitation on Public Access and Use Constraints\tStandard Phase 1 survey methodology has been followed. NVC data are based on professional judgement rather than detailed survey. Perceived management issues and suggested management prescriptions are those of the contracted surveyors. Landscape potential scores are the judgement of the surveyors and not based on any standard methodology. Data collected are relevant to, and limited by, the time of survey (June-August 2020).\tThis dataset should be used in conjunction with the associated survey report which describes the methods used and data collected: Pickard, M. \u0026amp; Stewart, B. (2020). Extended Phase 1 Habitat Survey of the Gwent Levels Sea Wall (Cardiff to Chepstow). NRW Evidence Report No:497. Phase 1 Habitat Survey followed: Institute of Environmental Assessment (1995). Guidelines for Baseline Ecological Assessment. Chapman and Hall, London.\tAvailable from NRW Library. DMS location: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/manbus/ManagingRegionsAndGroups/nrmu/Work%20Planning/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fmanbus%2FManagingRegionsAndGroups%2Fnrmu%2FWork%20Planning%2FProjects%2FSea%20Wall%20Biodiversity%20Project%2FSea%20Wall%20Botanical%20Survey%20%2D%20Map%20Files%20and%20Photos\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC854CF9F65FC344AFC2099255D45C99\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B21D5C4CC%2D0AD6%2D4D1D%2D8624%2DC4E9BB1EBEA0%7D\tInternal Contact: Catrin Grimstead, Senior Officer, Environment Team (Usk)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:36:03.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18967\tNRW_DS124868\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.349Z\t612\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales Water Vole Survey Data 2020\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383638\tThe Water Vole is now Wales fastest declining Mammal and has been classified as \u0027Endangered\u0027 in Wales in 2020 with the species disappearing from sites where it was previously present. Surveys were carried out in 2020 to assess the presence of Water Voles in areas which had previously recorded positive and negative sightings of the Water Voles across Wales. To accompany this the presence of Mink and Mink control were also recorded as the predator is a major cause in the decline of Water Vole in Wales.   The surveys were carried out across 4 regions in Wales - North West, Mid East, South East and South West which were then divided into separate sites. The Data is in a spreadsheet format to accompany four separate Natural Resources Wales Evidence Report on the 2020 Water Vole Surveys for Wales.\tSurveys were carried out in September and October 2020 when populations of Water Vole would be expected to be at their peak with young animals dispersing. This was undertaken in appropriate weather conditions and avoiding periods during, or immediately after spate conditions which could have removed important field signs. While any burrows and feeding remains were to be recorded, in this case it was the presence of latrines which was to be taken as the definitive proof of water vole presence. Surveyors were also asked to determine whether Mink were present and to comment on any known Mink control taking place.   A spot-check survey methodology was instructed to determine presence/absence in previously surveyed areas. 1km squares. Surveyors recordednot only the presence/absence of water voles, but also the habitat and surrounding land use. The location of each site was determined with a 10 figure Grid Reference taken from the approximate centre of the site.  An in-depth survey was not required for this contract.\t1. NRW Evidence Report 501 Water Vole Surveys September 2020 _ Mid East area. NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 501, 14 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor. 2. NRW Water Vole Survey Report North West Area 2020 NRW Report 500. NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 500, 45 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor. 3. Water Vole Wales Vital Nature 2020 South-East Area NRW 498 NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 498, 22 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor. 4. Water Vole Survey Report South West Wales NRW 499 NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 499, 13 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tDMS:  South West Wales Spreadsheet: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7B2F3B52BD-437F-4F34-A211-AF30841ABDAC%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dSurvey%20Records%20database%20SOUTH%20WEST%20final.xlsx  South East Wales Spreadsheet: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7BB492C1C2-7793-44A4-B1AB-CDAFB061BC1F%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dVOL5420%20Survey%20Records%20SE%20Wales%20Results.xlsx  North West Wales Spreadsheet: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7B1D52AED3-6E00-4411-A0C2-BC4BBD4D9B4B%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dWater%20Vole%20Survey%20Records%20North-West%20Area.xlsx  Mid East Wales Spreadsheet https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7B652E603B-A969-44EC-A40F-2F2EBFD435CC%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dSurvey%20Records%20MWT%202020-09.xlsx\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Internal Custodian: Jonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat \u0026amp; Resilience Mapping, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:25:56.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18969\tNRW_DS124869\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.435Z\t842\tValid\tEditable\tWater Resource Reliability\tData ar Ddibynadwyedd Adnoddau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383639\tThe Resource Reliability data indicates the percentage of time additional consumptive abstraction may be available and what this means for abstraction licensing. The Resource Reliability data is based on a nationally consistent method. Results have been overridden to reflect local assessments in our Abstraction Licensing Strategies (ALS) published on our website. ALS outline the implications for abstraction licensing of resource reliability.-Mae’r data Dibynadwyedd Adnoddau’n dangos y ganran o amser y gallai fod modd tynnu mwy o ddŵr yn ddarfodedigol, a goblygiadau hynny ar drwyddedu tynnu dŵr. Seiliwyd y data Dibynadwyedd Adnoddau ar ddull cenedlaethol cyson. Diystyrwyd canlyniadau yn unol â’r asesiadau lleol yn ein Strategaethau Trwyddedu Tynnu Dŵr a gyhoeddir ar ein gwefan. Mae’r Strategaethau Trwyddedu Tynnu Dŵr yn nodi goblygiadau argaeledd adnoddau ar drwyddedu tynnu dŵr.  Cyflwynir y data yn y categoriau canlynol i ddangos pa ganran o’r amser y gallai adnodd darfodedigol ychwanegol fod ar gael  -       Tynnu dŵr darfodedigol ar gael llai na 30% o’r amser.  -       Tynnu dŵr darfodedigol ar gael o leiaf 30% o’r amser.  -       Tynnu dŵr darfodedigol ar gael o leiaf 50% o’r amser.  -       Tynnu dŵr darfodedigol ar gael o leiaf 70% o’r amser.  -       Tynnu dŵr darfodedigol ar gael o leiaf 95% o’r amser.  -       Heb ei asesu.  Nid data crai mo’r rhain, ac nid ydynt yn ffeithiol nac yn fesuredig. Amcangyfrifon neu ganlyniadau wedi’u modelu ydynt, sy’n dangos y canlyniadau y gellir eu disgwyl ar sail y data sydd ar gael inni.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl. Rhai nodweddion o\u0027r wybodaeth hon yn seiliedig ar ddata gofodol digidol a drwyddedwyd oddi wrth y Ganolfan Ecoleg a Hydroleg © NERC (CEH). Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans © Hawlfraint y Goron a hawl cronfa ddata 2015.\tThe data shows  the percentage of the time additional consumptive resource may be available is broken down in to the following categories.  Consumptive abstraction available less than 30% of the time.  Consumptive abstraction available at least 30% of the time.  Consumptive abstraction available at least 50% of the time.  Consumptive abstraction available at least 70% of the time.  Consumptive abstraction available at least 95% of the time.  Not assessed.  This data is not raw, factual or measured. It comprises of estimated or modelled results showing expected outcomes based on the data available to us.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Resource Reliability Wales.lyr\tEmma Amos -   Specialist Advisor Water Resources\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-07-21\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3479/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_water_resource_reliability\t2025-02-04T10:38:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18959\tNRW_DS124871\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.068Z\t295\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGrey Seal Census for North Wales 2017\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383731\tFull-ground based survey of Grey Seals undertaken between Aberystwyth and the Dee Estuary aimed to identify potential and active pupping sites throughout the North Wales and to estimate pup production for the 2017 season. All active and newly identified potential pupping sites were surveyed in 7 to 10 day cycles by boat, sea kayak and on foot. A drone was used to supplement pup counts and to quantify sampling error at The Skerries. A total of 126 potential and previously recorded pupping sites along the North Wales coast were defined\tSurveys were largely conducted using coastal walkover surveys and vessel-based surveys. A trial survey using an unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) was also conducted to assess the efficacy of this method in comparison to the principal methods used (Ocean Ecology, 2018a)\tRobinson G.J., Clarke L.J., Banga R., Griffin R.A., Porter J., Morris C.W., Lindenbaum C.P. \u0026amp; Stringell T.B. (2023). Grey Seal (Halichoerus grypus) Pup Production and Distribution in North Wales during 2017. NRW Evidence Report No. 293. 66pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mm/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7BF756400B-FB4D-4155-8CC5-BAAFB9FF1B70%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dOEL_NRW_SLP0817_DTA_FULL.xlsx\u0026amp;wdLOR\u003dc7EB90253-6835-4F64-B333-E6F032772D01\u0026amp;_DSL\u003d1\u0026amp;action\u003ddefault\u0026amp;mobileredirect\u003dtrue\tTom Stringell (Lead specialist Advisor: Marine Species)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-08-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:13:58.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18956\tNRW_DS124874\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.767Z\t10\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales National Forest\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383734\tExtent of the National Forest as of the end of the first phase, 2021. Boundaries derived from Forester data.\tFor specific legal boundaries, this dataset should not be used as it is derived data – please see Forester legal boundary data.\t\t\tPrimary Metadata contact: National Forest for Wales Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18968\tNRW_DS124875\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.242Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Export Cable 132kV\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383735\tThese shapefiles relate to the Cefn Croes Windfarm Export Cable – 132kV within Welsh Government Woodland Estate This area forms part of the existing data held for ‘Electricity Powerlines on NRW Land’ – Legend: ‘Overhead’ within the Utilities layer of MyMap.\tThis information was initially gathered by a TSU Survey Contact the Energy Delivery Team if further information is required.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\Export Cable.lyr\tDave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-01-25\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:18:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18973\tNRW_DS124876\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.507Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Underground Cable 33kV\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383736\tThese shapefiles relate to Cefn Croes Windfarm Underground Cables – 33kV This area forms part of the existing data held for ‘Electricity Powerlines on NRW Land’ – Legend: ‘Underground’ within the Utilities layer of MyMap.\tThis information was initially received from the developer on site at the time of construction, signifying underground electricity cables connecting wind turbines to the substation. Contact the Energy Delivery Team if further information is required.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\33KV Cables.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-01-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:24:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18962\tNRW_DS124877\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.069Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Wooden Bridge\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383737\tThese shapefiles relate to the Wooden Bridge within Cefn Croes.\tThis information signifies the location of a wooden bridge. Contact the Energy Delivery Team if further information is required.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\Wooden Bridges.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-01-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:17:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18960\tNRW_DS124878\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.048Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Wind Turbines\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383738\tThese shapefiles relate to the location of wind turbines within Cefn Croes windfarm. Data initially received from Developer.\tThis information was initially received from the developer on site at the time of construction, signifying wind turbine locations. Contact the Energy Delivery Team if further information is required.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\Turbines.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-01-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:17:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18958\tNRW_DS124879\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.133Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Sub Station \u0026amp; Control Building\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383739\tThese shapefiles relate to the Cefn Croes Sub-Station + Control Building. Data initially received from Developer.\tThis information was initially received from the developer on site at the time of construction, signifying the location of the Sub-Station and Control Building. Contact the Energy Delivery Team if further information is required.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\Substation and Control Building.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-01-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:17:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18957\tNRW_DS124880\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.065Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Site Boundary\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383830\tThese shapefiles relate to the Cefn Croes Windfarm Site Boundary as per Lease Agreement dated 20.11.2003. Data initially received from Developer. This area forms part of the existing data held for ‘Wind Energy Development Boundary’ within the Utilities layer of MyMap.\tThis information was initially received from the developer on site at the time of construction, signifying the site boundary. Contact the Energy Delivery Team if further information is required.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\Site Boundary.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-01-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:46:56.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18966\tNRW_DS124881\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.206Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Developer Access\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383831\tThese shapefiles relate to Developer access to Cefn Croes Windfarm for construction, maintenance and decommissioning of site as per Lease Agreement dated 20.11.2003.\tThis information signifyies the approved Develoepr access as per Lease Agreement dated 20.11.2003 . Contact the Energy Delivery Team if further information is required.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\Developer Access to Site.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-01-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:24:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18961\tNRW_DS124882\t2024-05-28T12:59:10.065Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCefn Croes Windfarm - Communications Mast\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383832\tThese shapefiles relate to the Communications Mast located within Cefn Croes\tThis information signifies the location of the Communications Mast . Contact the Energy Delivery Team if further information is required.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\Communications Mast.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Farmery Senior Officer Energy Delivery Manager, NRW dave.farmery@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk Enid Carrick Project Support Officer, NRW enid.carrick@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-01-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:17:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18954\tNRW_DS124883\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.563Z\t8\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCefn Croes Windfarm - BT Fibre Optic Cable\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383833\tThese shapefiles relate to the Cefn Croes Windfarm BT Fibre Optic Cable within Welsh Government Woodland Estate This area forms part of the existing data held for ‘UG Telephone Lines on NRW Land’ – within the Utilities layer of MyMap\tThis information was initially received from the developer on site at the time of construction, signifying the location of the under ground BT cable to the Control Building. Contact the Energy Delivery Team if further information is required.\t\t*X:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Cefn Croes Windfarm\\BT Fibre Optic Cable.lyr\t\t\t\t2021-01-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18952\tNRW_DS124884\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.563Z\t169\tValid\tEditable\tNRW Woodland Names\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383834\tThis data contains woodland names used across NRW to differentiate different parts of a Forest Block. Each section is part of a wider forest block and will coincide with one of seven NRW Operations Areas, aka Regions. Each polygon can be referred to as a ‘section’.\tThe section names are attributed through local knowledge. The region and block identifiers were assigned as attribute data through a ‘spatial join’. The ID for the region is identical to the CRM identifier used in the Timber Sale System.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW Woodland Names.lyr\tData Custodian: Guy Bennett, Specialist Advisor NRW Estate Programming  Data Originator: Geraint Morgan, Specialist Advisor, Forester System\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t2021-01-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-09T14:15:02.061Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18951\tNRW_DS124885\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.568Z\t977\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales Green Infrastructure Dataset 2021\tSet Ddata Seilwaith Gwyrdd Cymru 2021\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383835\tThe Wales Green Infrastructure (GI) Data Set shows every bit of Wales including wetlands and water that is not built upon or covered with a man-made surface. It shows areas that are designated for nature conservation (such as Sites of Special Scientific Interest, Local Nature Reserves and local wildlife sites) and areas with a legal right of public access. At a lower level of confidence, the data set can show: a typology of sites (e.g. open space, amenity space etc.); management quality (e.g. Green Flag Awards); and accessibility (e.g. public parks, country parks etc). It shows where green space exists near where people live, if it is likely to be natural, and whether people are likely to be able to get into it.\tThis data set has no link to the legal definition of Green Infrastructure or Green Infrastructure Assessments described in Planning Policy Wales Edition 10 onwards. It is built from data from publicly available sources, local authorities and some third parties, and it is based on mapping done by the Ordnance Survey.   However:   - It doesn’t show all the green space that people could use to get their daily dose of nature; - It hasn’t been checked by visiting the green spaces to see if they have been mapped correctly; and it doesn’t show that site is fit for purpose or adequate for community need.  Data from local authorities shows places that are locally important for contact with nature, and spaces which are open to the public, like municipal parks. This means the data set can be used to get a more detailed picture of where people can actually go to experience nature near to where they live. The data set also uses the Ordnance Survey Open Greenspace Mapping product to supplement local authority data, bearing in mind that this only covers “urban” areas, and has some significant errors so it needs handling carefully.     INFORMATION WARNING: every data set is out of date the moment the “Save” command is used because land use changes all the time. Data sets are not reality – they are expert interpretations and they can only indicate what might be there on the ground. This data set should be used to select areas for further investigation and no decision should ever be taken without checking to see that the data set shows what actually exists.\tTechnical Advice Note 16 of Planning Policy Wales (TAN 16) http://gov.wales/docs/desh/policy/090206tan16en.pdf contains the definitions of the open space classification used in this data set.\tX:\\Social Economic\\Greenspaces\\Wales Green Infrastructure.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Pippa Jones, Geospatial Advisor, NRW  Metadata Point of Contact:  Pippa Jones, Geospatial Advisor, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tThird Party Conditional\t2020-09-16\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:39:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18953\tNRW_DS124889\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.574Z\t766\tValid\tEditable\tPublic Service Boards\tByrddau Gwasanaethau Cyhoeddus\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234383839\tThis dataset shows the boundary of Wales\u0027 19 Public Service Boards (PSB). The PSB improve joint working across all public services in each local authority area in Wales. Each board must carry out a well-being assessment and publish an annual local well-being plan.   The plan sets out how they will meet their responsibilities under the Well being of Future Generations (Wales) Act. Further Information: https://gov.wales/public-services-boards https://www.futuregenerations.wales/work/public-service-boards/-Mae’r set data hwn yn dangos ffiniau’r 19 Bwrdd Gwasanaethau Cyhoeddus yng Nghymru. Mae’r Byrddau Gwasanaethau Cyhoeddus yn gwella’r cydweithio ar draws yr holl wasanaethau cyhoeddus ym mhob ardal awdurdod lleol yng Nghymru. Mae’n rhaid i bob bwrdd gynnal asesiad llesiant a chyhoeddi cynllun llesiant blynyddol. Bydd y cynllun yn amlinellu sut y byddant yn cyflawni eu cyfrifoldebau dan Ddeddf Llesiant Cenedlaethau’r Dyfodol (Cymru).  Gwybodaeth bellach:  https://llyw.cymru/byrddau-gwasanaethau-cyhoeddus  https://www.futuregenerations.wales/cy/work/public-service-boards/  Cafodd nifer o bolygonau cyfagos eu diddymu i greu set data yn dangos y 19 Bwrdd Gwasanaethau Cyhoeddus. Cafodd y parau canlynol o Awdurdodau Unedol eu diddymu:  Conwy a Sir Ddinbych  Ynys Môn a Gwynedd  Merthyr Tudful a Rhondda Cynon Taf (a chafodd y nodwedd hon ei hail enwi’n Cwm Taf)  Datganiad Cydnabyddiaeth:  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tSeveral neighbouring polygons were dissolved to create a dataset showing the 19 Public Service Boards. The following pairs of Unitary Authorities were dissolved: Conwy \u0026amp; Denbighshire Anglesey \u0026amp; Gwynedd. Myrythr Tedfil \u0026amp; Rhondda Cynnon Taf (and this feature was renamed Cwm Taf)\thttps://gov.wales/public-services-boards\tX:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Public Service Boards.lyr\tData Custodian: Oliver Ishmael, Geospatial Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-07-21\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3511/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_public_service_boards\t2025-05-09T10:43:50.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18950\tNRW_DS124900\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.556Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Great Crested Newt\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393030\tResults of pond surveys to look for Great Crested Newt breeding. Results of habitat assessment for areas of suitable terrestrial habitat for Great Crested Newt\tObservational field data recorded with handheld GPS accurate to at least 5m.\tPullen, J.E., Heathcote, S.J., 2020. Sands of LIFE – Great Crested Newt survey report. NRW Evidence Report No. 425, 76pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Aberfraw Ponds - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Aberfraw Terrestrial - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Aberfraw Terrestrial Refuges - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Harlech Ponds - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Harlech Terrestrial - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Kenfig Ponds - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Kenfig Terrestrial - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Newborough Ponds - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Newborough Terrestrial - Great Crested Newt.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Newborough Terrestrial Refuges - Great Crested Newt.lyr\tToni Henwood, Team Member 2, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South Wales Sands of LIFE\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-02-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18949\tNRW_DS124902\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.478Z\t216\tValid\tEditable\tOtter Survey of Wales 2015 - 2018\tArolwg Dyfrgwn Cymru 2015 - 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393032\tThis data is an Excel spreadsheet detailing the 6th Otter Survey of Wales results and hydrology analysis. The data was collected by expert surveyors and volunteers to enable analysis of otter presence across Wales.     The data has been used for the 6th Otter Survey of Wales but may be useful for other research purposes.  Data collected in 1991, 2002 and 2009-2010 is described separately (see metadata entry 116334).\tThe data was collected by trained ecologists and volunteers between 2015 and 2018 using standard otter survey methods. Analysis of the expertise of surveyors has been undertaken and concludes no detriment to overall results.\t\tDMS - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/Otter Data/Distribution %26 Population/2017 Otter Survey Wales/2017 Survey Only/Master data of NRW 2020 Otter Survey and hydrology contract_FINAL.csv?d\u003dw3c7e9be636d045acb28103d6f7cd0d0f\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1  EVID-285-2145\tRebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Mammals, Biodiversity and Ecosystem Resilience Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2021-09-14\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-09T14:09:44.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18955\tNRW_DS124903\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.559Z\t1671\tValid\tEditable\tNRW Open Water Quality Archive\tArchif Ansawdd Dŵr CNC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393033\tNRW’s water quality archive provides data from samples collected around Wales, including coastal, estuary, rivers, lakes, ponds, canals, sewage discharges, trade discharges, pollution investigation points and waste sites.   NRW holds data goes back to 1962 whereas the open data product contains data from 2000 onwards. The open data product is extracted from NRW’s Water Quality database (KiWQM) and updated every month.   Some field measurements are taken in situ at the sampling station, whereas some samples are analysed in laboratories to measure aspects of the water quality. Once samples are taken, they must be processed and verified before they are added to the water quality archive. Therefore, there is a delay between samples being taken and the data being made available. Data may also be subject to change after publication, but the archive will be updated each month to include corrections.-Mae archif ansawdd dŵr CNC yn cadw data o samplau a gasglwyd ledled Cymru, gan gynnwys data arfordirol, data o aberoedd, afonydd, llynnoedd, pyllau a chamlesi, gollyngiadau carthffosiaeth a masnachol, mannau archwilio llygredd, a safleoedd gwastraff.   Cymerir mesuriadau maes yn y man a\u0027r lle yn yr orsaf samplu, ond dadansoddir y rhan fwyaf o baramedrau mewn labordai i fesur agweddau ar ansawdd dŵr. Unwaith y bydd samplau wedi\u0027u dadansoddi, rhaid i\u0027r canlyniadau gael eu prosesu a\u0027u gwirio cyn eu hychwanegu at yr archif ansawdd dŵr. Felly, mae oedi rhwng cymryd samplau a sicrhau bod y data ar gael. Gall data hefyd newid ar ôl ei gyhoeddi, ond bydd yr archif yn cael ei ddiweddaru bob mis i gynnwys cywiriadau.  Mae archif ansawdd dŵr CNC yn set ddata fawr sy’n cynnwys rhywfaint o ddata a allai fod yn sensitif ac felly mae rhywfaint o wybodaeth wedi’i golygu. Mae\u0027r prif faterion sensitif yn ymwneud â dŵr daear, cyflenwadau dŵr yfed, data trydydd parti a samplau ymchwilio.  Mae\u0027r haen GIS yn dangos lleoliadau\u0027r safleoedd, y blynyddoedd y cawsant eu samplu a Dalgylch Rheoli y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr y maent wedi\u0027u lleoli ynddo. Gellir defnyddio hyn i helpu i nodi\u0027r ffeiliau blwyddyn sy\u0027n cynnwys y data sydd ei angen arnoch o\u0027r ddolen isod.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data’r Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif trwydded yr Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a hawl cronfa ddata.\tNRW\u0027s water quality archive is a large dataset containing some potentially sensitive data and therefore some information has been redacted. The main sensitivities are around groundwaters, potable water supplies, 3rd party data and formal investigation samples.  Information Warnings about the quality of the data are flagged against the specific rows within the datasets.\tFor additional help please see our FAQs here: https://naturalresourceswales.sharefile.eu/share/view/s08e447c637cd47549592d271873d8466/fob796e4-390d-4158-8d9a-8feddc72ae34\tKiWQM\tMartin Stephens\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-11\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_water_quality_archive_stations\t2026-01-12T12:19:22.142Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18948\tNRW_DS124905\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.185Z\t249\tValid\tEditable\tCommunity Flood Plans\tCynlluniau llifogydd cymunedol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393035\tA community flood plan is an account of information and actions, agreed by communities at risk of flooding across Wales. The aim and content of the plan depends on the needs and motivations of the community.   Community flood plans are volunteer and community led.   Data presented is a snapshot and only based on data disclosed to NRW, rather than live information managed by NRW.    This data is collected during the annual maintenance of community flood plans across Wales, at the start of financial year. The data is collected by Operation Warning \u0026amp; Informing teams and collated by the EPP Community Engagement and Resilience Teams.\tThe point represented is a random point within that community and does not correlate to specific information and this is not data we hold.   Date completed: Date the plan is finalised, rather than inception date. Typically there would be a large time difference between inception and completion, but inception dates are not known for all plans.   Properties: Number of properties covered within the flood plan.   People_Est: Estimated number of people covered within the flood plan, this value is based on a 2.35 multiplier against each property.   Volunteers: Given number reported to NRW as volunteering as part of plan. This is not the number of volunteers that NRW has direct contact with. This figure is likely to be unreliable, as snapshot from given part of the year.   RD_Upload: Confirmation of whether the flood plan is uploaded to Resilience Direct by NRW.    Information Warning: The GIS dataset is likely to be incomplete or contain inaccuracies as it is a snapshot of the data and may have redactions applied.\t\tTop Copy: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/waterman/frm/aware/Results%20%20Reporting/Reports/CFP%20Update%20Live.xls?d\u003dw671f401b42de404f81b240fe7cf67f69                                                                       A GIS version of the data is available at: X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Community Flood Plans.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Hannah Davies, Team Member 5, Community Engagement and Resilience (Flood) Kelly McLauchlan, Lead Specialist Advisor, Community Engagement and Resilience (Flood)         Although the data contains personal data, this is in the top copy and should have been redacted from the GIS layer.  Providers have consented to provide their personal data.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2022-06-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-08T11:12:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18947\tNRW_DS124913\t2024-05-28T12:59:09.229Z\t1044\tValid\tEditable\tTranquillity and Place: Dark Skies\tol Llonyddwch a Lle – Awyr Dywyll\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393133\tDark skies can affect our experience of nature, landscapes and greenspaces and can benefit our health and wellbeing and local wildlife.   The Dark Sky and Light Pollution Dataset for Wales has been created to identify the strategic and local resource in remote, rural, peri-urban and urban areas for use as an evidence base to inform policy intent, practice and provision for benefits to benefits to wellbeing.    The dataset is based upon satellite imagery taken at 1:30 A.M in December 2019 by the United States National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) Suomi National Polar-orbiting satellite with the Visible Infrared Imaging Radiometer Suite (VIIRS) capturing visible and infrared imagery used in this dataset and later processed in 2021.    A set of maps have been created to display the dark skies data against the following geographical boundaries:   - Local Authority areas   - Future Wales boundaries    - Designated landscapes (AONBs and National Parks)   - NRW Operational Areas   - National Landscape Character Areas   - LANDMAP Visual \u0026amp; Sensory aspect areas-Gall awyr dywyll effeithio ar ein profiad o fyd natur, tirweddau a mannau gwyrdd a gall fod o fudd i’n hiechyd a’n lles a’n bywyd gwyllt lleol. Mae Set Ddata Awyr Dywyll a Llygredd Golau Cymru wedi’i chreu i nodi’r adnoddau strategol a lleol mewn ardaloedd anghysbell, gwledig, ardaloedd trefol a’r ardaloedd o amgylch trefi i’w defnyddio fel sylfaen dystiolaeth i gyfarwyddo bwriad, gweithrediad a darpariaeth polisi ar gyfer buddion llesiant.  Mae\u0027r set ddata yn seiliedig ar ddelweddau lloeren a gymerwyd am 1:30 AM ym mis Rhagfyr 2019 gan loeren Partneriaeth Suomi National Polar-orbiting a’r United States National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) gyda\u0027r VIIRS (Visible Infrared Imaging Radiometer Suite) sy’n cipio delweddau gweladwy ac isgoch a ddefnyddir yn y set ddata hon ac a broseswyd yn ddiweddarach yn 2021.  Mae set o fapiau wedi\u0027u creu i ddangos data\u0027r awyr dywyll yn erbyn y ffiniau daearyddol canlynol:  Ardal Awdurdod Lleol Ffiniau Cymru\u0027r Dyfodol Tirweddau dynodedig (Ardaloedd o Harddwch Naturiol Eithriadol a Pharciau Cenedlaethol) Ardaloedd Gweithredol CNC Ardaloedd Cymeriad Tirwedd Cenedlaethol Ardaloedd agwedd Gweledol a Synhwyraidd LANDMAP   Dolen MapStori:  https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/da8aadb1816a4e61b8ccb0e8416bccaf  Ap gwe Awyr Dywyll a Llygredd Golau yng Nghymru:  https://luc.maps.arcgis.com/apps/dashboards/index.html#/1cd6ba8a1d7d4a62aff635cfcbaf4aec  Datganiad Priodoli:  Mae’n cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Natural Resources Wales a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Earth Observation Group information © Earth Observation Group, Payne Institute for Public Policy.\tData was captured by the United States National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) Suomi National Polar-orbiting satellite with the Visible Infrared Imaging Radiometer Suite (VIIRS) capturing visible and infrared imagery used in this dataset.\tGreen C, Manson D, Chamberlain K 2021. Tranquillity and Place – Dark Skies. NRW Report No: 514, 70pp\tX:\\Physical\\Light Pollution\\Night lights.lyr  Data also includes a set of maps produced in JPEG format and an Excel spreadsheet containing the zonal statistics analysis.\tJill Bullen - Lead Specialist Advisor, Landscape\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-04-04\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/5877\t2025-11-28T15:33:08.015Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18946\tNRW_DS124914\t2024-05-28T12:59:08.999Z\t729\tValid\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Skomer Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ) zooplankton survey\tArolwg plancton Parth Cadwraeth Morol Sgomer, Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393134\tTime series of consistent plankton samples taken using a Zooplankton net (200um mesh) from a single point on the North side of Skomer island. The net is hauled vertically from 40m and the methods used are designed to replicate the methods used by the Plymouth Marine Laboratory at the L4 plankton sampling station. Taxa are enumerated and recorded to various levels of taxonomic resolution depending on the group. Different taxonomists have been used throughout the time series but the data has been truncated to a standard species list.  As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  In 2023, data from 2021 Survey was made available through the NBNAtlas by the DASSH Data Archive Centre on behalf of NRW.\t2008 \u0026amp; 2009: A plankton samples were collected using a 63µm mesh plankton net trawled at less than 2 knots for 500m. The analysis was completed by Plymouth Marine Laboratory.   2010 -2012: A 200µm mesh WP2 net (UNESCO 1968) pulled on a vertical haul from 35- 40m depth at 0.2m / sec (3.5 minute haul). The sample is collected in the ‘cod-end’ bottle and this is preserved in 4% formalin. Two samples are taken at each sampling event. Both replicates are identified and enumerated by Andrea McEvoy, Plymouth Marine Laboratory. The window in the cod-end is of mesh 400µm.   2013 - onwards: A 200µm mesh WP2 net (UNESCO 1968 – see data entry) pulled on a vertical haul from 35- 40m depth at 0.2m / sec (3.5 minute haul). The sample is collected in the ‘cod-end’ bottle and this is preserved in 4% formalin. Two samples are taken at each sampling event. One replicate from each time point has been identified and enumerated by Dave Conway, Marine Biological Association.  The window in the cod-end is of mesh 200µm.\tAndrea McEvoy, Mark Burton, Paul Somerfield and Angus Atkinson 2015 - PosterCost-effective method for establishing an ecological baseline of the zooplankton at Skomer Marine Nature Reserve\tT:\\shared drive\\data\\biological data\\plankton.    The data series has been submitted for archive at DASSH and may be onward published as open data to the NBN, EMODnet or eur/OBIS and will be added to regulally.                              2021 data has been added to the NBN by DASSH on behlaf of NRW\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Burton - Marine Environmental Assessment Officer  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Burton - Marine Environmental Assessment Officer\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2021-03-24\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr2929\t2026-01-09T15:18:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18993\tNRW_DS124915\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.096Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) National Vegetation Classification Survey Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393135\tData from a National Vegetetation Classificaiotn survey of 10 sand dune sites including shapefiles of NVC polygons, vegetation mosaics, target notes, quadrats, photograph locations and overall survey boundary. Data collected in 2019 based on field survey of entire sites with field maps digitised to OS Mastermap and aerial photographs.\tData collected from field survey. Full methods and limitations are reported in SoLIFE NVC project reports.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Boundaries.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Mospoly.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Photographs.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Polygons.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Quadrats.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE NVC Target Notes.lyr\tToni Henwood, Team Member 2, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South Wales Sands of LIFE\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-03-25\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:23:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18997\tNRW_DS124916\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.114Z\t749\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Marine Phytoplankton - Geospatial\tData Phytoplancton Cyrff Dŵr Newidiol ac Arfordirol y Cyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393136\tThis data collates all the Phytoplankton samples collected within Wales from 2007 onwards by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales that are held within NRWs KiEco Database. This methodology is based on the principle that additional nutrients (especially nitrogen) can alter the amount of phytoplankton in transitional and coastal waters. Persistent and high counts of phytoplankton taxa are also seen as a measure of disturbance in the phytoplankton community. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring for legislative requirements and to meet organisational drivers. In addition, sites may be sampled for investigative work, or on an ad hoc basis.  Phytoplankton samples are taken in situ and are usually sent to a specialist marine contractor for Species Identity and abundance (two labs have been used in this time period; CEFAS 2007 – 2009 \u0026amp; APEM 2010 – onwards). Phytoplankton samples are analysed (identified and counted) using the Utermöhl method. Further information is available from the UK-TAG WFD website.  For many of our marine survey techniques we record an NGR at the site because this acts as a central/representative location around which we collect lots of samples. An NGR is then also recorded against each of the samples to give a more accurate sampling location.  A Darwin Core version of this data which is published to the NBN Atlas is available in NRW_DS161379.\tSurveys are carried out by a range of survey methods, the data from one survey is not necessarily directly comparable to another. The user is recommended to check the survey method used in the data provided.  We do our best to avoid data quality problems, but as these datasets reflect the data we hold, they may contain errors  The data is provided as a set of related data tables due to the quantity and structure of the data. A basic understanding of relational data is assumed.  The change in analytical lab in 2010 resulted in the new laboratory reporting increased abundances of species because of the more high-powered microscopes available. There were also inconsistencies in recording Phaeocystis as either cell counts or colony counts.\thttps://www.wfduk.org/resources/tags/transitional-coastal-water-159/tags/phytoplankton-68\tData is stored in WISKI  Geospatial Layers located in -  X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Marine Phytoplankton Taxa Detail.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Marine Phytoplankton Site Detail.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Marine Phytoplankton Sample Detail.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Bianca Bacchi, Senior Marine Monitoring Officer  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-06\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6723/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_marine_phytoplankton\t2026-01-08T10:35:07.310Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n19003\tNRW_DS124922\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.473Z\t804\tValid\tEditable\tRumney Great Wharf to River Usk Drone Survey 2021\tArolwg Drôn Cei Mawr Rhymni i Afon Wysg\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393232\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) wish to establish a topographical baseline and aerial imagery of the foreshore and surrounds to help inform the consideration of possible nature-based solutions at this location. The purpose of the survey was to collect topographic data of the foreshore, along the 12km frontage that stretches between Cardiff in the west and Newport to the east. Vertical images were taken for the photogrammetric reconstruction. Flights were also undertaken to capture oblique imagery looking back at the coastline. A key constraint of this survey was being unable to take GPS points out in the mudflats. To overcome this, a RTK capable drone was used, which reduces the reliance on ground control points by ensuring that the geolocation for each image is significantly more accurate than on a standard drone.  The survey was carried out over a period of 6 days between the 3rd and 12th of February 2021.  Flights were planned to ensure lowest possible tides. In total, 19 flights were completed to collect the aerial data, taking a combined 285 minutes (4.75 hours). Survey deliverables comprised ASCII Grid, Orthophotos and Obliques\tThe following survey equipment was used for the survey:  • Trimble GNSS Rover  • DJI Phantom 4 RTK (x2)  The DJI Phantom 4 RTK was selected as it has a built in RTK GNSS receiver. This enables a higher accuracy of data capture, particularly in areas where ground control points are not possible, such as the mudflats of this survey. A Trimble GNSS Rover connected to the VRS network receiving RTK corrections was used to collect control points for this survey. The GNSS Rover collects points to a level of 1-2cm accuracy depending on atmospheric conditions. A total of 87 Ground control points were collected for use in the processing of this data set. Where safe to do so, control points were taken at the interface between the salt marsh and the mud.  A key constraint of this survey was being unable to take GPS points out in the mudflats. To overcome this, we selected a RTK capable drone, which reduces the reliance on ground control points by ensuring that the geolocation for each image is significantly more accurate than on a standard drone.   In total, 19 flights were required to complete the survey. These were undertaken from the coastal path and on the salt marsh where possible. Automated flight plans were used to ensure consistent data coverage. These flights were flown in a ‘lawnmower’ pattern with an overlap of 70% and sidelap of 80%, ensuing high quality outputs in the post processing.  Flights were conducted at the maximum altitude of 120m. A total of 5,942 vertical images were taken for the photogrammetric reconstruction. Flights were also undertaken to capture oblique imagery looking back at the coastline.\tArmstrong, S., Whitehead, P., Scott, C., Hull, S., 2021. Rumney Great Wharf Saltmarsh Restoration / Enhancement Feasibility and Preferred Option Studies. Produced by ABPmer for NRW. NRW Evidence Report No: 528, 96pp, NRW, Cardiff\tZ:Drive Location: Archive/ Land Mgmt/ Protected Sites/ Rumney Great Wharf to River Usk Drone Survey\tSarah Revill - Senior Marine Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-17T11:38:23.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18991\tNRW_DS124923\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.954Z\t1010\tValid\tEditable\tRestoring marine and coastal habitats in Wales: identifying spatial opportunities and benefits\tAdfer cynefinoedd morol ac arfordirol yng Nghymru: adnabod cyfleoedd a manteision gofodol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393233\tIn order to better understand opportunities to build resilience in the Welsh marine environment, a report was commissioned by Natural Resources Wales to identify potential spatial opportunities to restore valuable marine habitats.     The report created spatial products (Arc Geo database) for: areas in the floodplain that might be suitable for the creation of mudflat and saltmarsh through managed realignment and areas where the right conditions might exist for native oyster habitat and horse mussel bed restoration. The horse mussel data was provided from JNCC. The report also addresses restoration potential for Sabellaria alveolata reefs although no spatial data was created for this habitat and seagrass habitat although this was provided as a map image from JNCC.    These spatial Data layers should be considered as initial aides to identifying potential locations or areas but come with several limitations which have been discussed in the report. Any restoration projects should undergo further site validation and local engagement before proceeding with any specific site.    The following GIS datasets were created for this:  Saltmarsh_Mudflat_Opportunities_Opportunities_Floodplains_SMPpolicy,  Native_Oyster_Opportunities,   Wave_Height_Grid_ABPmerSeastates,  Current_Speed_Grid_ABPmerSeastates,  Historic_Saltmarsh_Extents,  S_alveolata_records_historic_and_current,  O_edulis_records_historic_and_current,  M_modiolus_records_historic_and_current,  Potential_Beneficial_Use_Sources.-Er mwyn deall yn well y cyfleoedd i feithrin cydnerthedd yn yr amgylchedd morol yng Nghymru, comisiynodd Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru adroddiad i adnabod cyfleoedd gofodol posibl i adfer cynefinoedd morol gwerthfawr.  Creodd yr adroddiad gynhyrchion gofodol (cronfa ddata Arc Geo) ar gyfer: ardaloedd yn y gorlifdir a allai fod yn addas ar gyfer creu fflatiau llaid a morfeydd heli drwy eu hailalinio mewn ffordd reoledig, ac ardaloedd ble gallai’r amgylchiadau fod yn gywir ar gyfer cynefin wystrys brodorol ac adfer gwelyau cregyn dilyw. Darparwyd y data ar gregyn dilyw gan JNCC. Mae’r adroddiad hefyd yn ystyried y potensial ar gyfer adfer riffiau Sabellaria alveolata er na chrëwyd data gofodol ar gyfer y cynefin hwn a chynefin morwellt, ond darparwyd hyn fel delwedd map gan JNCC.  Dylid ystyried yr haenau data gofodol hyn fel cymorth cychwynnol ar gyfer adnabod lleoliadau neu ardaloedd posib, ond mae llawer o gyfyngiadau iddynt sydd wedi’u trafod yn yr adroddiad. Ar gyfer unrhyw brosiectau adfer, dylid gwneud asesiadau pellach ar gyfer dilysu’r safle ac ymgysylltu’n lleol cyn symud ymlaen gydag unrhyw safle penodol.  Defnyddiwyd amrywiaeth o ddata ffynhonnell a’i gyfuno i greu detholiad o Haenau GIS sydd wedi’u rhestru yn y crynodeb, er mwyn adnabod cyfleoedd gofodol ar gyfer adfer cynefinoedd a rhywogaethau morol ac arfordirol.  Datganiad priodoli: Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tA variety of source data was used and merged to create a selection of GIS Layers listed in the abstract to identify spatial opportunities for restoration of marine and coastal habitats and species.     •NRW Flood Zone 3 •NRW SMP  •OS Foreshore - https://www.ordnancesurvey.co.uk/business-government/products/vectormap-district  •EA SMP - https://data.gov.uk/dataset/0c492f70-8d54-42d9-ba2c-23cd2e513737/shoreline-management-plan-mapping  •OS MHWS - https://www.ordnancesurvey.co.uk/business-government/products/terrain-50  •EMODnet Bathymetry - https://portal.emodnet-bathymetry.eu/  •Habmap Bathymetry (estuaries only)  •Kinetic Energy / current speed - Developed by ABPmer   •JNCC Combined habitat map - http://gis.ices.dk/geonetwork/srv/eng/catalog.search#/metadata/f7d5a168-0097-4437-944e-cc63111d15c6  •Welsh National Marine Plan Area: https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:wnmp_areas  •Records for each species were downloaded from Obis (https://obis.org/) and NBN Atlas (https://nbnatlas.org/).  •Lle disposal sites dataset\tArmstrong, S., Pearson, Z., Williamson, D., Frost, N., Scott, C. 2021. Restoring marine and coastal habitats in Wales: identifying spatial opportunities and benefits. NRW Report No: 554 NRW, Cardiff.\tMain GIS Data Layers are located in:   • X:\\Biological\\Habitat Restoration\\Marine Habitat   Further Mapping Packages for the report are stored in:   • Y:\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Restoration\\ABPMer Restoration opportunities GIS data\\March\\Geodatabase\\Report_figure_mxd\tKate Griffith - Specialist Advisor Marine Sustainable Management of Natural Resources\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-07-21\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3599/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_restoring_marine_and_coastal_habitats_in_wales_identifying_spatial_opportunities_and_benefits\t2025-12-03T16:09:36.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n18996\tNRW_DS124925\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.073Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOperational Small Scale Hydro Projects\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393235\tThis shapefile relates to Small Scale Hydro Projects that are sited on Natural Resources Wales managed estate and facilitated by the Energy Delivery Team Data received from Project Manager and supporting officers during construction phase\tThis information was initially received from the Project Manager and supporting officers at the time of construction, signifying intake locations. Contact the Energy Delivery Team, if further information is required\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Small Scale Hydro Projects.lyr\tDave Farmery, Senior Officer, Energy Delivery Manager, NRW Enid Carrick, Project Support Officer, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-04-20\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:26:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19005\tNRW_DS124928\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.757Z\t708\tValid\tEditable\tWelsh Lowland Raised Bog Inventory\tRhestr Cyforgorsydd Iseldir Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393238\tThis dataset maps the distribution of Lowland Raised Bogs within Wales, giving details on their size, elevation, conservation status, context, and whether they have been surveyed. It was compiled using data from the Phase 2 Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales (2004-2020), and aerial photographs.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn mapio dosbarthiad Cyforgorsydd Iseldir yng Nghymru, gan roi manylion am eu maint, eu huchder, eu statws cadwraethol, eu cyd-destun, a pha un ai ydynt wedi cael eu harolygu ai peidio. Cafodd ei llunio gan ddefnyddio data o Arolwg Mawndiroedd Iseldir Cam 2 Cymru (2004-2020), a ffotograffau o\u0027r awyr.  Mae’r set ddata hon yn cynnwys data safleoedd gofodol (ArcGIS Feature class), data nad yw\u0027n ofodol (taenlen Excel) ac adroddiad cysylltiedig (Jones P.S. \u0026amp; Birch K.S., sydd wrthi’n cael ei baratoi).  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis dataset contains spatial sites data (ArcGIS Feature class) and non-spatial Excel spreadsheet and accompanying report (Jones P.S . \u0026amp; Birch K.S. (In Prep).\t\tX drive layer file: X:\\Physical\\Peat\\Welsh Lowland Raised Bog Inventory.lyr  DMS Word document explaining the attribute domains: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/peatl/Evidence Analysis Projects/Welsh Lowland Raised Bog Inventory/Welsh Lowland Raised Bog Inventory ArcGIS data structure.docx?d\u003dw7065453cc6ca43d887e02948ebf77083\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\tKathryn Birch- Specialist Advisor Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-07-20\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3480/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_welsh_lowland_raised_bog_inventory\t2025-05-09T11:05:06.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18998\tNRW_DS124929\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.051Z\t455\tValid\tEditable\tVisible Settings of Designated Landscapes in Wales\tLleoliadau Gweladwy Tirweddau Dynodedig yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393239\tNature of data: showing the extent of areas visible from a range of landscape-scale polygons and view points.    Purpose of data: To assist strategic site choices when planning visually prominent development types. A web page with general introduction and links to map images produced from the data is available from Natural resources Wales. This is new data output, believed to be seminal for Wales.    Resolution and extent: The visibility mapping uses a 30m resolution open-source digital terrain model for the whole of Wales, out to maximum viewing distance of 35km, including overlaps to/from England where relevant. The 30m digital terrain model was sourced from the Shuttle Radar Topography Mission (Van Zyl, 2001) and processed using the Quantum GIS plugin developed by Cuckovic (2016). See page 23 of technical report (via the link above) for more detail.\tTypes of data: Data comprises GIS files, a selection of thematic map images created from the GIS, a technical report and user guidance. Output types are (1) zone of theoretical visibility (ZTV); (2) height of object before it is visible (HOBV). Geographical units from which these calculations were run are (a) Key iconic view points to or from nationally designated landscapes – 200 points showing ZTV and HOBV; (b) The visible settings of nationally designated landscapes – 9 large polygons in or affecting Wales, showing ZTV, HOBV; and (c) The visible settings of LANDMAP Visual and Sensory Aspect Areas, 1920 polygons showing ZTV only. Additional derived maps arising from the data for (b) are: ‘heatmaps’ indicating relative concentrations of visibility, and a combined national map showing the pattern of coverage of where there is visibility from a designated landscape.    Caveats on use: The data is designed for strategic planning and site selection use only. It is quantitative data on visibility and does not itself indicate a qualitative measure of visual sensitivity. It does not replace the need for (i) more detailed site and development-specific zone of theoretical visibility mapping evidence and (ii) landscape and visual impact assessment, as part of a development consenting process.\tHodges, C.J., Batten, H., Roberts, O., Dowding, D. 2021. Mapping the visible settings of Designated Landscapes in Wales. NRW Evidence Report No: 522, 81pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Visible settings of landscapes in Wales.lyr\tJohn Briggs, Specialist Advisor: Landscape, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-02-19\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/4340\t2025-11-28T15:50:54.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18992\tNRW_DS124932\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.047Z\t363\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCrymlyn Bog SSSI; Rapid appraisal of Himalayan balsam Impatiens glandulifera distribution: 2020 – GIS dataset\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393332\tA rapid survey of Himalayan balsam Impatiens glandulifera on Crymlyn Bog SSSI and Pant y Sais SSSI was undertaken 7-9 Sept 2020 using free-survey method. Observations were made from the defined route and estimates of extent marked on a 1:10000 scale field map and later transcribed to ArcGIS.\tData collected by field observations; some very scattered occurrences may be missing especially in dense vegetation, though the pattern of spread from key points was apparent and it was generally possible to trace these pathways in the field. Data was checked against local knowledge of National Nature Reserve site manager\tRatcliffe J B (2021) Crymlyn Bog SSSI; Rapid appraisal of Himalayan balsam Impatiens glandulifera distribution. Natural Resouces Wales Evidence Report No. 555\tX:\\Biological\\LIFE\\Crymlyn Impatiens glandulifera 2020.lyr  X:\\Biological\\LIFE\\Crymlyn Impatiens Survey Route 2020.lyr\tJohn Ratcliffe - Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-06-03\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:27:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18989\tNRW_DS124933\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.806Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Framework: Pilot projects to prove concept and approach – GIS dataset\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393333\tIn a pilot project, two maps of upland areas; Eryri in North Wales and Mynydd Du in West Wales, were created to indicate the preferred habitat cover over the long-term. This was undertaken using available habitat and land cover data (Phase 1 / Habitat Survey of Wales, Phase 2 / National Vegetation Survey, unified peatland data, aerial imagery, Environmental Record Centre species data, BSBI vascular plant data, RSPB data etc.) consideration of designated features and conservation objectives for protected sites, along with local expert knowledge. In the case of Eryri, extensive local expert knowledge was relied on; in contrast in the case of Mynydd Du, consultation with graziers on the Common Land led to a suggested approach, covering part of the site, for further consultation with Commoners. Both maps are recommendations for future habitat but should be used as a starting point, in discussion with other interests, to determine the practical objectives for particular parcels of land.\tData issues and limitations are discussed in the project report (Sherry 2021). Phase 1 (Habitat Survey of Wales; Blackstock T.H., Howe, E.A. Stevens, J.P., Burrows C.R. and Jones P.S. 2010. Habitats of Wales. A Comprehensive Field Survey, 1979-1997. University of Wales Press, Cardiff) data forms the baseline generally but is somewhat dated (1979-1986) and subject to spatial error due to lack of orthrectification of aerial images at that time. An earlier attempt (Jones B. 2007. A framework to set conservation objectives and achieve favourable condition in Welsh Upland SSSIs. Unpublished CCW Report. Countryside Council for Wales, Bangor (partially updated 2016 by J. Sherry and J. Rothwell)) was used as guidance (within protected sites) but was extended beyond protected sites to the Upland boundary (defined in Blackstock et al 2010) and the future habitat categories were simplified to reflect the uncertainty of outcomes. The use of expert local knowledge and judgement in projecting future desirable outcomes is critical to this process but also permits alternative interpretation as other factors (economic, social, practical etc.) may dictate.\tSherry, J. 2021. Development of an Upland Ecosystem Option in Wales: Pilot projects to prove concept and approach. NRW [Report Series] Report No: 557, 50pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tY:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Upland Framework\\Eryri Upland Options.shp Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Upland Framework\\Mynydd Du Trial Upland Options.shp Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Upland Framework\\Eryri Upland Options.lyr   Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Upland Framework\\Mynydd Du Trial Upland Options.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): John Ratcliffe, Specialist Adviser, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team.  Metadata Point of Contact: John Ratcliffe, Specialist Adviser, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-06-02\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:40:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18990\tNRW_DS124939\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.826Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClocaenog Forest Windfarm 33 kV Cables\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393339\tThis dataset shows the Clocaenog windfarm 33 kV cables. 33kV cables are the six underground electric cable circuits, coming from the wind turbine power systems. They terminate at the sub-station on site. This is the final true data received from the developer (RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tGIS data provided to NRW for the Clocaenog Forest Windfarm project is all derived from GPS surveys undertaken at various stages of the construction process (June 2018 to September 2020):  1. Site Civils (e.g. turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, Jones Bros Civil Engineering UK LTD, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used. 2. On-site cable arrays (33kV cabling): this was installed by subcontractor Powersystems UK and the locations were surveyed at installation using a survey grade GPS. Drawings were issued as AutoCAD DWG and converted to ArcGIS shape files to OSGB 1936 / British National Grid coordinate system/projection. Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\33KV Cables.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team       To be reviewed by Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:24:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19000\tNRW_DS124942\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.124Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClocaenog Forest Windfarm Turbines\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393432\tThis dataset shows Clocaenog Forest Windfarm (as built) wind turbine building lines. This shows all 27 turbines on the Clocaenog Forest Windfarm. Each Vestas wind turbine is 3.6MW in power. This is the final true data received from the developer (RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tGIS data provided to NRW for the Clocaenog Forest Windfarm project is all derived from GPS surveys undertaken at various stages of the construction process (June 2018 to September 2020):  Site Civils (e.g. turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, Jones Bros Civil Engineering UK LTD, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used.   Drawings were issued as AutoCAD dwg and converted to ArcGIS shape files to OSGB 1936 / British National Grid coordinate system/projection.  Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\Turbine Building Line.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:27:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19002\tNRW_DS124943\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.255Z\t503\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClocaenog Forest Windfarm Substation\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393433\tThis dataset shows Clocaenog Forest Windfarm Substation Location. The location of the on-site substation. This is where transformers change the 33kV input from the wind turbines to a 132kV rating for use in the National Grid. This the final true data received from the developer ((RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tGIS data provided to NRW for the Clocaenog Forest Windfarm project is all derived from GPS surveys undertaken at various stages of the construction process (June 2018 to September 2020):  1. Site Civils (e.g. turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, Jones Bros Civil Engineering UK LTD, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used.  2. Dual transformer 132kV substation to enable the 96MW of power generated from the wind turbines to be exported to the National Grid: this was installed by subcontractor Powersystems UK and the locations were surveyed at installation using a survey grade GPS. Drawings were issued as AutoCAD dwg and converted to ArcGIS shape files to OSGB 1936 / British National Grid coordinate system/projection. Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\Substation.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:27:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n18994\tNRW_DS124948\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.038Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClocaenog Forest Windfarm Turbines Point Location\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393438\tThis dataset shows Clocaenog Forest Windfarm (as built) wind turbine point locations. This shows all 27 turbines on the Clocaenog Forest Windfarm. Each Vestas wind turbine is 3.6MW in power. This is the final true data received from the developer (RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tGIS data provided to NRW for the Clocaenog Forest Windfarm project is all derived from GPS surveys undertaken at various stages of the construction process (June 2018 to September 2020):  Site Civils (e.g. turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, Jones Bros Civil Engineering UK LTD, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used.  Drawings were issued as AutoCAD dwg and converted to ArcGIS shape files to OSGB 1936 / British National Grid coordinate system/projection.  This dataset was created by NRW to show the centre point of each Turbine Building Line to improve user display ability.  Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\Turbines.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:27:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18995\tNRW_DS124949\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.106Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClocaenog Forest Electrical Marker Posts\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393439\tThis dataset shows Clocaenog Forest Windfarm (as built) electrical marker post locations. This shows all 382 electrical marker posts on the Clocaenog Forest Windfarm. Electrical marker posts are located along the line of the 33kV cables, for operational health and safety ground truthing. This is the final true data received from the developer (RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\tGIS data provided to NRW for the Clocaenog Forest Windfarm project (Deleted Text Here) is all derived from GPS surveys undertaken at various stages of the construction process (June 2018 to September 2020):  Site Civils (e.g. turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, Jones Bros Civil Engineering UK LTD, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used.   Drawings were issued as AutoCAD dwg and converted to ArcGIS shape files to OSGB 1936 / British National Grid coordinate system/projection.  Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\Electrical Marker Post.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:24:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18988\tNRW_DS124950\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.723Z\t279\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClocaenog Forest Windfarm Site Boundary\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393530\tThis shapefile shows Clocaenog Forest Windfarm Construction Site Boundary. The boundary that was granted by the Local Planning Authority and was used as the Construction Design Management Regulations 2015 (CDM 2015) area. These are the final true shapefiles received from the developer (RWE Renewables UK Limited) signifying the end of the construction phase.\t1. Site Civils (e.g. turbine locations, track edge, crane hard-standings, culverts, coupe crossing points and all general civils detail etc. etc.): acquired by the Principal Contractor, Jones Bros Civil Engineering UK LTD, as part of the final \u0027As-built\u0027 survey undertaken post construction. A dual-frequency survey grade GPS was used.  2. On-site cable arrays (33kV cabling): this was installed by subcontractor Powersystems UK and the locations were surveyed at installation using a survey grade GPS.  3. Dual transformer 132kV substation to enable the 96MW of power generated from the wind turbines to be exported to the National Grid: this was installed by subcontractor Powersystems UK and the locations were surveyed at installation using a survey grade GPS  Whilst all surveys are theoretically accurate to about 0.5m (when related to OSGB/OSTN2) some uncertainties were identified: given the size of the site, the complex terrain, and the degradation of GPS in dense forestry, an overall accuracy of +/- 2m should be assumed.\t\tx:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Clocaenog Forest Windfarm\\Development Boundary.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian: Daniel Brown Senior Officer, Energy Delivery  Metadata Point of Contact: Geospatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2017-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:13:58.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18999\tNRW_DS124992\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.023Z\t1028\tValid\tEditable\tDrinking Water Protected Areas for Groundwater in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393932\tAll groundwater bodies in Wales are designated as drinking water protected areas (DrWPAs). This aims to protect groundwater from over-abstraction and to prevent deterioration in groundwater quality that could increase the treatment of drinking water. Drinking Water Protected Areas are required to be identified under the Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017.\tSourced from Groundwater Bodies which are available in WFD Groundwaterbodies Cycle 2 2015\t\tx:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Groundwater Drinking Water Protected Areas.lyr\tDave Jones - Senior Specialist Advisor Geoscience\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-04-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19001\tNRW_DS124997\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.218Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLocations where Coastal Adaptations measures may affect Coastal Access over the lifetime of the Shoreline Management Plans\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313234393937\tThe purpose of the survey was to determine which coastal defences and coastal footpaths could be affected by the implementation of a Shoreline Management Plan policy unit classified as No Active Intervention or Managed Realignment. For the affected areas of footpaths the objectives of the survey were to record the total lengths of footpath affected, the Shoreline Management Plan policy unit and Local Authority area the footpaths are located and when the coastal footpaths could be affected.  This data from this survey contains the following outputs, •A spreadsheet of all affected footpaths which are listed by a unique path identifier as well as the associated costal coastal defence identifier and each intersecting policy unit. This also includes the The local authority in which the section of path is located and the length (in metres) of path is also included. •GIS shapefiles of potentially affected footpaths identified in this study. •GIS shapefiles of coastal defences and the ‘affected zone’ of potential influence of defences used in the study.\tThe main processing steps for the creation of this data is as follows but for more detailed lineage please use - Dewey, N., Lockhart, E., Ringwood, O. 2020. Advice on locations where Coastal Adaptation measures may Affect Coastal access over the lifetime of the Shoreline Management Plans. NRW Evidence Report No: 435, 123 pp, NRW, Cardiff.  Public Right of Way and Wales Coast Path data from Natural Resources Wales were merged together to create a single footpath data layer. A coastal defences per Shoreline Management Plan (SMP) policy unit data layer was created using Natural Resources Wales Coastal assets data and SMP2 Policies sourced from DataMapWales. The next step was to identify the affected sections of footpaths. This included  capturing information about the SMP policy unit, specific SMP policy (Manged Realignment or No Active Intervention) and affected coastal defence as an attribute to the output. The final step was to add information about the Local Authority where the affected footpaths were located. This was created by using the Local Authority Layers located within DataMapWales. The final affected footpaths data layer with all required attributes (defence ID, SMP policy, policy unit, footpath ID, length of footpath and Local Authority) was exported to Excel to form the final spreadsheet deliverable.\tDewey, N., Lockhart, E., Ringwood, O. 2020. Advice on locations where Coastal Adaptation measures may Affect Coastal access over the lifetime of the Shoreline Management Plans. NRW Evidence Report No: 435, 123 pp, NRW, Cardiff.\tNRW File Storage Z:DRIVE - Archive - Access Recreation \u0026amp; Education - Access and Recreation Programmes and Projs - Wales Coast Path \u0026amp; National Trails\tNicola Rimington - Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine and Coastal Physical Process and Coastal Management\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:49:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n18986\tNRW_DS125005\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.684Z\t292\tValid\tEditable\tVisitor count data at a selection of NRW sites\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303035\tFor the purpose of monitoring its visitor centres, National Nature Reserves (NNR) and bike trails, Natural Resources Wales contracted Linetop LTD to provide data and reporting on visitor numbers.   Reports are produced quarterly and provide a breakdown of visitor numbers in each site and reserve.  This data includes:   Site cards - which provide details of counter placements and specification.   Raw Count data - raw counts from sensors.  Reports - summary data provided as MS Word documents at regular intervals.  This metadata entry also covers the Grafana Chambers Electronics downloadable visitor counter data which is time stamp and number count data, which is affectively counting the same thing as the Linetop data.\tData is collected in a variety of methods, further details on the types of counters used and their locations can be found within the Site Cards.    Issues such as data logger failures can occur so Raw Data should be used alongside the reports which identify these issues.  Each report has a summary of any technical issues such as counter malfunctions/vandalism.\t\tLineTop: ‘Z:\\Archive\\Access Recreation \u0026amp; Education\\Recreation Data \u0026amp; Knowledge\\LineTop\\CSV Archive\\Data Transfer Prep\\For_Chambers’. Grafana: data is downloaded as CSV from the Grafana portal.  GIS Data - X:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Visitor counters.lyr\tDavid Liddy, Specialist Advisor Recreational Safety and Heather Crump.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2021-09-01-2025-07-23\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:25.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18987\tNRW_DS125074\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.702Z\t1073\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Map for Planning (FMfP) TAN15 Defended Zones\tMap Llifogydd ar gyfer Cynllunio Parthau a Amddiffynnir TAN15\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303734\tThe Flood Map for Planning (FMfP) includes climate change information to show how this will affect flood risk extents for rivers, the sea and surface water and small watercourses over the next century. It shows the potential extent of flooding assuming no defences are in place.  It also indicates areas at risk from coastal erosion, areas that have flooded in the past and key information on the location of flood defence infrastructure.     The FMfP can be considered as the best available information on flood risk and will replace the Development Advice Map for planning purposes when the Welsh Government implements the revised TAN15.  Although it currently has no official status for planning purposes it is a useful data source when considering flood risks.  The coming into force of the new TAN 15 and the Flood Map for Planning has been suspended due to a further consultation on the TAN.  Technical advice note (TAN) 15: development, flooding and coastal erosion - https://www.gov.wales/technical-advice-note-tan-15-development-flooding-and-coastal-erosion  The FMfP introduces a new layer called the TAN15 Defended Zone.  This shows areas that benefit from risk management authority owned flood defence infrastructure, that have a minimum, Present Day level of protection of:     • 1% (1 in 100) annual event probability (AEP) for rivers, or   • 0.5% (1 in 200) annual event probability (AEP) for the sea.     Flood defences built after 1 January 2016 must also include design freeboard and an allowance for climate change.-Mae\u0027r Map Llifogydd ar gyfer Cynllunio yn cefnogi polisi cynllunio Llywodraeth Cymru ar berygl llifogydd a dyma\u0027r man cychwyn ar gyfer ystyriaeth o berygl llifogydd yn y system cynllunio. Mae\u0027n rhoi syniad o ba dir sydd mewn perygl o lifogydd gan afonydd, y môr ac o ddŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bach, gan ystyried effeithiau newid yn yr hinsawdd dros y ganrif nesaf. Y Map Llifogydd ar gyfer Cynllunio yw\u0027r set ddata allweddol i hysbysu a llywio penderfyniadau ynghylch datblygiad yn y dyfodol.  Mae\u0027r Parth a Amddiffynnir TAN15 yn dangos lleoliadau sy\u0027n elwa ar seilwaith amddiffyn rhag llifogydd sylweddol a sydd wedi\u0027u dosbarthu i derfyn Parth Llifogydd 2 (1 mewn 1000 ynghyd â\u0027r newid yn yr hinsawdd).  Mae Parthau a Amddiffynnir TAN15 wedi eu creu ar gyfer yr ardaloedd y tu ôl i amddiffynfeydd llifogydd sy\u0027n cael eu rheoli a\u0027u cynnal a\u0027u cadw gan Awdurdodau Rheoli Risg sydd ag isafswm Safon Amddiffyn Bresennol o:  1% (1 mewn 100) tebygolrwydd digwyddiad blynyddol (AEP) ar gyfer afonydd, neu 0.5% (1 mewn 200) tebygolrwydd digwyddiad blynyddol (AEP) ar gyfer y môr Caiff Parthau a Amddiffynnir TAN15 eu creu ar gyfer ffynonellau unigol o lifogydd (h.y. afonydd neu\u0027r môr).  Nid oes Parthau a Amddiffynnir TAN15 ar gyfer dŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bach.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Mae rhai nodweddion o\u0027r wybodaeth hon yn seiliedig ar ddata gofodol digidol wedi\u0027i drwyddedu gan Ganolfan Ecoleg a Hydroleg y DU © UKCEH DEFRA, y Swyddfa Dywydd ac Asiantaeth Afonydd yr Adran Amaethyddiaeth a Datblygu Gwledig © Hawlfraint y Goron. © Prifysgol Cranfield. © Sefydliad James Hutton. Yn cynnwys data OS © Hawlfraint y Goron a hawl cronfa ddata.\tTAN15 Defended Zones are created for individual sources of flooding (i.e. Rivers or Sea). There are no TAN15 Defended Zones for Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses.\t\tGIS Corporate Data Store X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/2065/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_TAN15_DEFENDED_ZONES\t2025-12-01T11:32:30.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19004\tNRW_DS125075\t2024-05-28T12:59:50.662Z\t445\tValid\tEditable\tAberdovey Sand Dune drone survey 2021\tArolwg drôn Twyni Tywod Aberdyfi 2021\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303735\tThis drone survey data was collected as part of a Section 16 Management Agreement (Environment (Wales) Act 2016) between Aberdovey Golf Club and NRW. The aim was to have baseline data on the sand dune line and produce an up-to-date accurate map of the current coastline.    The survey will be repeated at the end of this agreement / start of a renewal.\tA site visit and drone flights was undertaken on 25th and 26th May 2021. The survey collected aerial imagery of the dunes.  The drone survey was carried out using a DJI Mavic 2, with the Pix 4D mapping software. A total of 2466 images were taken on the site visit and inputted into Pix 4D software in order to stitch together the images. A number of ground GPS control points were also taken in order to ensure images produced were accurately mapped.\t\tx:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Drone footage\\Aberdovey Sand Dunes 32WDF18RA AGC 2021.lyr\tKatrina Jenkinson, Senior Officer (Ecologist), Dwyfor and Meirionnydd Environment Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-06-16\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-09T13:26:24.883Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n18984\tNRW_DS125076\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.396Z\t1570\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Map for Planning Flood Zones 2 and 3\tMap Llifogydd ar gyfer Cynllunio Parthau Llifogydd 2 a 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303736\tThe Flood Map for Planning (FMfP) includes climate change information to show how this will affect flood risk extents for rivers, the sea and surface water and small watercourses over the next century. It shows the potential extent of flooding assuming no defences are in place.  It also indicates areas at risk from coastal erosion, areas that have flooded in the past and key information on the location of flood defence infrastructure.     The FMfP can be considered as the best available information on flood risk and will replace the Development Advice Map for planning purposes when the Welsh Government implements the revised TAN15.  Although it currently has no official status for planning purposes it is a useful data source when considering flood risks.  The coming into force of the new TAN 15 and the Flood Map for Planning has been suspended due to a further consultation on the TAN.  Technical advice note (TAN) 15: development, flooding and coastal erosion - https://www.gov.wales/technical-advice-note-tan-15-development-flooding-and-coastal-erosion   The Flood Zones within the FMfP shows the undefended risk of flooding from Rivers, the Sea and from Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses.    Flood Zone 3 displays the extent of flooding from:  • rivers with a 1% (1 in 100) chance or greater of happening in any given year, including an allowance for climate change.  • the sea with a 0.5% (1 in 200) chance or greater of happening in any given year, including an allowance for climate change.  • Surface water \u0026amp; small watercourses with a 1% (1 in 100) chance or greater of happening in any given year, including an allowance for climate change.     Flood Zone 2 displays the extent of flooding from:  • Rivers with less than 1% (1 in 100) but greater than or equal to 0.1% (1 in 1,000) chance of happening in any given year, including an allowance for climate change.  • the Sea with less than 0.5% (1 in 200) but greater than or equal to 0.1% (1 in 1,000) chance of flooding in any given year, including an allowance for climate change.  • Surface water \u0026amp; small watercourses with less than 1% (1 in 100) but greater than or equal to 0.1% (1 in 1,000) chance of happening in any given year, including an allowance for climate change.-Mae\u0027r Map Llifogydd ar gyfer Cynllunio yn cefnogi polisi cynllunio Llywodraeth Cymru ar berygl llifogydd a dyma\u0027r man cychwyn ar gyfer ystyried perygl llifogydd yn y system cynllunio. Mae\u0027n rhoi syniad o ba dir sydd mewn perygl o lifogydd gan afonydd, y môr a dŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bach, gan ystyried effeithiau newid yn yr hinsawdd dros y ganrif nesaf. Y Map Llifogydd ar gyfer Cynllunio yw\u0027r set ddata allweddol i hysbysu a llywio penderfyniadau ynghylch datblygiad yn y dyfodol.  Mae\u0027r Parthau Llifogydd yn y Map Llifogydd ar gyfer Cynllunio yn dangos y peryglon anniffynedig o lifogydd o afonydd, y môr ac o ddŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bach.  Mae Parth Llifogydd 3 yn arddangos rhychwant y llifogydd o:    afonydd gyda siawns o 1% (1 mewn 100) neu fwy o ddigwydd unrhyw flwyddyn, gan gynnwys lwfans ar gyfer newid yn yr hinsawdd.   y môr gyda siawns o 0.5% (1 mewn 200) neu fwy o ddigwydd unrhyw flwyddyn, gan gynnwys lwfans ar gyfer newid yn yr hinsawdd. Dŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bach gyda siawns o 1% (1 mewn 100) neu fwy o ddigwydd unrhyw flwyddyn, gan gynnwys lwfans ar gyfer newid yn yr hinsawdd.    Mae Parth Llifogydd 2 yn arddangos rhychwant y llifogydd o:    afonydd gyda siawns llai nag 1% (1 mewn 100) ond mwy na 0.1% (1 mewn 1,000) neu\u0027n gyfartal o ddigwydd unrhyw flwyddyn, gan gynnwys lwfans ar gyfer newid yn yr hinsawdd. y môr gyda siawns llai na 0.5% (1 mewn 200) ond mwy na 0.1% (1 mewn 1,000) neu\u0027n gyfartal o lifogydd unrhyw flwyddyn, gan gynnwys lwfans ar gyfer newid yn yr hinsawdd. dŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bach gyda siawns llai nag 1% (1 mewn 100) ond mwy na 0.1% (1 mewn 1,000) neu\u0027n gyfartal o ddigwydd unrhyw flwyddyn, gan gynnwys lwfans ar gyfer newid yn yr hinsawdd.      Caiff y Map Llifogydd ar gyfer Cynllunio ei arddangos mewn dwy ran, sef golwg sylfaenol a golwg fanwl. Yn yr olwg sylfaenol, dangosir y perygl llifogydd o afonydd a\u0027r môr fel haen wedi\u0027i huno. Yn yr olwg fanwl, caiff y perygl llifogydd ei rannu yn ffynonellau unigol. Yn y ddwy olwg, dangosir y Parthau Llifogydd ar gyfer dŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bach ar wahân.     Cydnabyddiaeth:  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Mae rhai nodweddion o\u0027r wybodaeth hon yn seiliedig ar ddata gofodol digidol wedi\u0027i drwyddedu gan Ganolfan Ecoleg a Hydroleg y DU © UKCEH DEFRA, y Swyddfa Dywydd ac Asiantaeth Afonydd yr Adran Amaethyddiaeth a Datblygu Gwledig © Hawlfraint y Goron. © Prifysgol Cranfield. © Sefydliad James Hutton. Yn cynnwys data OS © Hawlfraint y Goron a hawl cronfa ddata.\tThe FMfP is displayed in two parts, a Basic View and a Detailed View. In the Basic View the risk of flooding from Rivers and the Sea is shown as a merged layer. In the Detailed View flood risk is separated into individual sources. In both views, Flood Zones for Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses are shown separately.\tMore information about the national-scale modelling created as part of the FRAW project is available. Information on the local model data incorporated to the Flood Zones is available from the relevant Flood Risk Analysis teams.\tGIS Corporate Data Store X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-11-26\tpublication\tnull\thttps://flood-map-for-planning.naturalresources.wales/\t2025-11-28T10:35:09.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18982\tNRW_DS125080\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.209Z\t762\tValid\tEditable\tNRW Local Model Manager\tRheolwr Modelau Lleol CNC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303830\tThe ‘NRW Local Model Manager’ shows the location of NRW’s detailed, local flood models which can be requested via datadistribution@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk.-Mae ‘Rheolwr Modelau Lleol CNC’ yn dangos lleoliad modelau llifogydd lleol manwl CNC y gellir gwneud cais amdanynt drwy gysylltu ag datadistribution@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk  Mae Rheolwr Modelau Lleol CNC yn cael ei ddiweddaru ddwywaith y flwyddyn i ddangos fersiwn ddiweddaraf yr ardaloedd a gynrychiolir gan fodelau llifogydd CNC. E-bostiwch ymholiadau@cyfoethnaturiol.cymru i weld a oes data modelu ychwanegol ar gael nad yw’n cael ei arddangos yn Rheolwr Modelau Lleol CNC.  Mae’n rhaid i’r derbynnydd wirio’r holl wybodaeth sy’n cael ei darparu gan CNC a hynny CYN ei defnyddio mewn Asesiad Canlyniadau Llifogydd (FCA) neu astudiaeth fodelu.  Cyfrifoldeb defnyddiwr y data yn unig yw sicrhau ei fod yn addas i’r diben ar gyfer y defnydd terfynol a fwriedir.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tNRW Local Model Manager is updated biannually to show up-to-date coverage of NRW’s flood models. Email datadistribution@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk to see if additional model data is available that is not displayed in the NRW Local Model Manager.    All information supplied by NRW needs to be verified by the recipient PRIOR to using in a Flood Consequences Assessment (FCA) or modelling study.    It is the sole responsibility of the user of the data to ensure it is fit for purpose for the intended end use.\t\tGIS Corporate Data Store X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/2069/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_MODEL_MANAGER\t2025-12-01T11:30:38.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18985\tNRW_DS125083\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.387Z\t864\tValid\tEditable\tMaterials Facilities Reporting\tAdrodd ar Weithgareddau Cyfleusterau Deunyddiau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235303833\tThis displays the quarterly Operator reported, input and output sampling data that qualifying Materials Facilities are required to provide by the regulations. This excludes additional information reported by sites that is not required to be sampled.   Data prior to 2020 was previously published for England and Wales on the WRAP Portal.-Mae hwn yn arddangos y data samplu mewnbwn ac allbwn chwarterol a adroddir gan weithredwyr, y mae\u0027n ofynnol i gyfleusterau deunyddiau cymwys ei ddarparu o dan y rheoliadau. Nid yw hyn yn cynnwys gwybodaeth ychwanegol a adroddir gan safleoedd nad yw\u0027n ofynnol iddi gael ei samplu. Câi data ar gyfer Cymru a Lloegr ar gyfer y cyfnod cyn 2020 ei gyhoeddi ar Borth WRAP yn flaenorol.  Data a adroddir gan y gweithredwr ei hun yw hwn. Mae\u0027r rheoliadau yn ei gwneud yn ofynnol i gyfleusterau deunyddiau cymwys ddarparu manylion chwarterol y tunelledd o wastraff cymysg a dderbyniwyd gan bob cyflenwr a\u0027r tunelledd allbwn o bedair ffrwd deunyddiau penodedig. Mae hefyd yn ofynnol i gyfleusterau gymryd samplau o\u0027r deunyddiau mewnbwn ac allbwn a nodi\u0027r ganran gyfartalog o ddeunydd targed, deunydd nad yw’n darged a deunydd na ellir mo’i ailgylchu.  Ar gyfer mewnbynnau gwastraff cymysg, rhaid cymryd samplau ar gyfer pob 125 o dunelli a dderbynnir gan bob cyflenwr (heblaw achosion lle mae\u0027r gwastraff cymysg yn cael ei drosglwyddo i gyfleuster deunyddiau arall er mwyn ei wahanu’n ddeunydd allbwn penodedig). Yn achos deunydd allbwn penodedig, newidiodd yr amlder samplu ar gyfer deunydd allbwn penodedig papur i un sampl fesul pob 60 o dunelli a gynhyrchir, ac yn achos samplu deunydd allbwn penodedig plastig, newidiodd yr amlder i un sampl fesul pob 15 o dunelli a gynhyrchir.  Adroddir yr holl ddata tunelledd gan weithredwyr safleoedd. Mae niferoedd yn seiliedig ar yr holl ffurflenni cywir a gedwir gan CNC ar adeg casglu\u0027r adroddiad. Nid ydynt yn cynnwys ffurflenni gwastraff sy\u0027n weddill neu sy\u0027n aros am weithred sicrhau ansawdd.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThis is self reported data by the operator.  The regulations require qualifying Materials Facilities to provide quarterly details of the mixed waste tonnage received from each supplier and the output tonnage dispatched by 4 specified materials streams.  Facilities are also required to take samples of the input and output materials and identify the average percentage of target, non-target and non-recyclable material.      For mixed waste inputs samples must be taken for every 125 tonnes received from each supplier (except where the mixed waste is being transferred to another MF for separating into specified output material). For specified output material (SOM) the sampling frequency for paper SOM changed to 1 sample for every 60 tonnes produced and for plastic SOM sampling, frequency changed to 1 sample for every 15 tonnes produced.    Best Practice Methodology guidance has been produced by WRAP (Waste and Resources Action Programme) and operators are recommended to follow it.    As the regulator Natural Resources Wales undertakes annual compliance activities which include advising operators on sampling and reporting requirements and assessing compliance with them.  Operator waste returns are public register information unless a claim for commercial confidentiality has been accepted. In these cases the data is not provided.     All tonnage data is reported by site operators. Numbers are based on all correct returns held by NRW at the time of report collation. They do not include waste returns that are outstanding or pending quality assurance action.\tThe Regulations themselves are now part of the Environmental permitting Regs 2016 (Part 2 Schedule 9): https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2016/1154/schedule/9/made  WRAP Portal - https://wrap.org.uk/resources/tool/materials-facility-mf-reporting-portal\tWaste Returns Database\tInternal Data Custodian(s): John Fry, Waste Policy, EPP Metadata Point of Contact: waste.returns@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-05-07\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2648\t2025-12-19T10:45:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n18983\tNRW_DS125112\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.388Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Fen Orchid Monitoring 2021\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313132\tLocation and phenology of fen orchids in January 2016 North slack and Lpost Extension Scrape as recorded in August 2021. Data also available Excel spreadsheet\tBoundaries based on NRW Kenfig slack boundary shapefiles.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Fen Orchid locations 2021.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Toni Henwood, Sands of LIFE Assistant Project Officer South Wales, Sands of LIFE Project Internal Custodian: Kathryn Hewitt, Team Leader, Sands of LIFE Project\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2021-06-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:38.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18979\tNRW_DS125154\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.068Z\t286\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAn invertebrate survey of Llangennith Moors 2018\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313534\tLlangennith Moors grazing marsh lies at the extreme western end of the Gower Peninsula. Before this survey, the invertebrate interest of the site was little known, with the only previous survey having been carried out in July 1991 which had a focus upon Diptera. Due to the minimal invertebrate knowledge of the site and relative absence of ditch maintenance there since the 1990s, Natural Resources Wales commissioned a survey of ditch and fen invertebrates and a search for two whorl snails of European importance, the Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior (which is found on the nearby Whiteford Burrows and Oxwich Burrows) and Desmoulin’s Whorl Snail Vertigo moulinsiana, which is found in similar habitat on the south Llŷn coast in North Wales. The surveys were aimed at determining the current invertebrate interest of Llangennith Moors following a sea wall breach and subsequent managed retreat at Cwm Ivy Marsh and the loss of grazing marsh to saltmarsh. The Llangennith surveys were undertaken simultaneously by two surveyors in October 2018.\tNRW commissioned a survey of the ditch invertebrate fauna in Autumn 2018. Survey work was undertaken by two contractors on 15th and 16th October 2018. The site is divided by the ditch network into several fields. These fields were each assigned a survey unit code, which was used as the basis for recording the invertebrate fauna. Many of the fields supported species-poor, improved, or semi-improved grassland of low ecological interest. The ditch network was mostly composed of ditches that were dried out or completely choked with tall emergent vegetation. As these improved fields and choked ditches had only a relatively low potential to support important wetland invertebrate assemblages, they were not the subject of more detailed follow-up invertebrate survey. Rather, survey effort was targeted towards those areas of the site that had semi-natural fen or marshy grassland and/or more open ditches with permanent standing water.\tWilling, M.J. \u0026amp; Boyce, D.C. 2021. An invertebrate survey of Llangennith Moors in October 2018\tData stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder.\tMike Howe Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2021-12-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T15:20:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n18980\tNRW_DS125158\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.217Z\t42\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Non-Native Marine Species\tRhywogaethau Morol Estron Cymru Gyfan\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313538\tThis dataset comprises data on marine non-native species in Wales.  The purpose it to gain an up to date understanding of the distribution of marine non-native species to inform their spread and inform casework advice. The data used to build this GIS layer comes from existing relevant locations where data is held and has been amalgamated into one location in ESRI format. It contains data from the 1930s up until 2023.  No new surveys were carried out for this dataset.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys data ar rywogaethau estron morol yng Nghymru. Ei diben yw cael dealltwriaeth gyfredol o ddosbarthiad rhywogaethau estron morol er mwyn deall eu lledaeniad a llywio cyngor ar waith achos. Daw\u0027r data a ddefnyddiwyd i adeiladu\u0027r haen GIS hon o leoliadau perthnasol sy\u0027n bodoli eisoes lle cedwir data ac mae wedi\u0027i gyfuno i un lleoliad ar ffurf ESRI. Mae’n cynnwys data o’r 1930au hyd at 2023.  Cafodd setiau data amrywiol yn cynnwys rhywogaethau estron morol eu hintegreiddio i adeiladu\u0027r haen GIS hon. Roedd y rhain yn cynnwys setiau data mewnol CNC gan gynnwys Marine Recorder, KiECO a chofnodion ad-hoc o rywogaethau estron sydd gan CNC. Daeth y setiau data allanol a ddefnyddiwyd o Atlas yr NBN. Ni chynhaliwyd unrhyw arolygon newydd i\u0027w cynnwys yn y set ddata hon. Roedd y dulliau a ddefnyddiwyd i gasglu’r cofnodion o rywogaethau estron morol yn amrywio ac yn cynnwys rhywogaethau estron o arolygon penodol i gofnodion ad-hoc.  Defnyddiwyd rhestr o rywogaethau estron y cytunwyd arnynt fel sail ar gyfer dethol cofnodion rhywogaethau. Defnyddiwyd gwahanol ddulliau i dynnu cofnodion o\u0027r gwahanol ffynonellau ond cafodd rhestr o rywogaethau ei llunio yn Excel a\u0027i mewnforio i ArcGIS i\u0027w hadolygu. Ychwanegwyd meysydd ychwanegol, gan gynnwys un ar gyfer statws y rhywogaethau ar Restr Cymru o Rywogaethau Estron Goresgynnol y Môr y dylid rhoi blaenoriaeth i’w monitro a chadw golwg arnynt.  Ymgymerwyd â phroses i ddileu unrhyw gofnodion dyblyg a nodwyd. Cafodd unrhyw gofnodion yr oedd yn ymddangos eu bod yn fewndirol eu dileu.\tVarious datasets containing marine non-native species were interregated to build this GIS layer.  These included internal NRW datasets including Marine Recorder,  KiECO and ad-hoc records of non-natives NRW hold. External datasets used were from the NBN Atlas. No new surveys were carried out for inclusion in this dataset.  The methods used to collect the marine non-native species records varied and contained non-natives from specific surveys to ad-hoc records.  A list of agreed non-native species was used as a basis on which to extract species records.  Different methods were used to extract records from the different sources  but a list of species was captured in Excel and imported into ArcGIS for review.  Additional fields were added, including one for the species status on the Wales marine INNS Marine Invasive Non-native Species Priority Monitoring and Surveillance List for Wales   A process was undertaken to remove any duplicate records which were noted.  Any records which appeared to be inland were removed.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Non Native Species\\Non Native Species All Wales Marine July 2024.lyr\tDave Tavmer: Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Mapping\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-11-26\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/7212/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_marine_inns\t2026-01-12T10:53:45.311Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18981\tNRW_DS125168\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.228Z\t238\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNDF Preassessed areas for wind energy\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313638\tAreas with a presumption in favour of large-scale wind energy development.\tThis data only appears at a scale of over 100000\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\NDF Preassessed areas for wind energy.lyr\tOlwen Maidment\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-09T11:38:29.083Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n18978\tNRW_DS125172\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.053Z\t790\tValid\tEditable\tAssessing the vulnerability of Annex I marine habitats to climate change in Wales (2021)\tAsesu pa mor agored i niwed yw cynefinoedd morol Atodiad I o ran newid yn yr hinsawdd yng Nghymru (2021)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313732\tThis project assessed the potential impact of climate change pressures on SACs and their Annex 1 features in Wales under two different climate change emission scenarios from 2025 – 2100. The outputs from the project were a report, GIS layers showing vulnerability levels for features within each site (based on their component biotopes) and a series of excel workbooks summarising vulnerabilities of each site and feature by pressure type.\tThe data model outputs generated provide a high-level indication of which Annex I marine features with Welsh Special Areas of Conservation are considered to be most vulnerable to the pressures associated with climate change. This provides an indication of where potential management measures could be best targeted to achieve the greatest benefit to the MPA  network. However, any such measures would need to be set in the context of the degrees of uncertainty associated with the outputs along with wider influences on such features.    A number of limitations in the model were identified that reduce its ability to map potential  Annex I habitat vulnerability. These include the spatial representation of Annex I habitats,  the availability of climate change pressure datasets (and their spatial and temporal resolutions), and uncertainty associated with emissions scenarios and climate projections. Further limitations include the fact that other measures associated with climate change pressures that may be important to vulnerability are not accounted for (e.g. seasonal cues, period/frequency of exposure to pressures).\tOaten J, Brooks A, Frost N, Hull S, Williamson D., Robinson, K., 2021. Assessing the vulnerability of Annex I marine habitats to climate change in Wales. NRW Evidence Report No: 536, 109pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Marine Habitat Vulnerability Assessment\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dr. Karen Robinson  Metadata Point of Contact:  Dr. Karen Robinson\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2021-12-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T15:25:45.549Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n18977\tNRW_DS125181\t2024-05-28T12:59:49.054Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tScallop Fisheries Orders (closed and unrestricted areas)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313831\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries Orders\\Scallop order closed areas.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries Orders\\Cardigan Bay Unrestricted Scallop Area.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Fisheries Orders\\Cardigan Bay Unrestricted Scallop Area Points.lyr\tData Custodian: Alexander Scorey Possibly Colin Charman.\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n18976\tNRW_DS125183\t2024-05-28T12:59:48.892Z\t15\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) UAV Survey 2020\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313833\tUnmanned aerial vehicle (drone) survey of the Sand of LIFE (SoLIFE) sand dunes sites at the Gower, Newborough Warren, Pembrey Coast, Kenfig and Merthyr Mawr. Survey data includes high resolution aerial imagery and photogrammetry derived Digital Elevation Models.\tGround control points: All ground control points (GCP’s) measured using either base and rover or single rover with networked enable corrections (we use Trimble’s catalyst system for 1-2 cm accurate positioning) all GCPs are corrected to OSGB Newlyn co ordinate system. Not all targets used to create the final DSM, some will be used to determine accuracy of the final output. Aerial survey: Aerial flights are carried out by us manually, with target of at least a 60% forward overlap of imagery and a 75% side overlap. Altitude of flights can vary dependant on the area being mapped and the required resolution needed. All imagery is checked on site to determine there is sufficient coverage to properly post process without any gaps. Post processing: Processing of point clouds, orthomosaic imagery and Digital surface models processed using Agisoft photoscan professional. DTM are licence ESRI arcmap/globe/scene users as well as Autodesk AEC collection licence holders. A full report is produced by the software indicating flight math, overlap as well as Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) error for ground\t\tX:\\Basemapping\\LiDAR\\SoLIFE File Name: Sands of Life LIDAR – Orthophotography.lyr\tSands of LIFE Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:18:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19023\tNRW_DS125188\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.478Z\t407\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPolitical Boundaries of Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313838\tPolitical boundary of Wales\t\t\tNRWGDBV.GISDATA.WALES X:\\Basemapping\\Admin Boundaries\\Wales.lyr\tPrimary Metadata contact: Olly Ishmael\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:01.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19020\tNRW_DS125193\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.490Z\t574\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMynydd Du Vegetation Map 2019\tMap Llystyfiant Mynydd Du 2019\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313933\tThe extent of land cover classes on the common lands of Mynydd Du in the western end of the Brecon Beacons National Park derived from satellite imagery.  The classes include dominant vegetation type, rock outcrop and scree, road and lake.  The land cover classes follow the scheme used by the  Meithrin Mynydd 1997 survey but with adjustments where some classes could not be separated.  The dataset was derived from Sentinel 2 satellite imagery from 2019 with a pixel size of 10m by 10m on the ground.  The goal was to extend vegetation mapping of the west end of Mynydd Du from 2018 carried out by NRW\u0027s Environmental Assessment Officer and so provide up-to-date vegetation mapping of upland grassland, moor and mire in an area actively managed by commoners for sheep grazing in collaboration with the BBNPA.\tSentinel 2 imagery from 2019 for winter growth (24 February 2019) and summer growth (29 June 2019) were clipped to the Mynydd Du common land boundary, for Principal Component Analysis.  The first 6 principal components were retained for classification.  Pixels covered by OS MasterMap roads, lakes and mapped areas of Limestone grassland from 1997 were masked out to exclude them from the analysis.  The remainder was classified by Maximum Likelihood Classification using training areas identified manually by expert knowledge instead of field work due to the time of year, COVID-19 restrictions and challenges converting the 2018 mapping into suitable training areas.  Based on: a) Sentinel 2 Analysis Ready Data (ARD) processed by JNCC for DEFRA and taken from the CEDAR archive under an Open Government licence.   b) OS MasterMap roads and lakes on the Mynydd Du Commons. c) The Mynydd Du Common land boundary. d) Limestone grassland areas from the Meithrin Mynydd Survey report of 1997.  Sentinel imagery passed overhead around 11:30 and when combined with the east-west alignment of the ridges of Mynydd Du along with all the steep slopes facing north meant that winter imagery had north facing shadows that had an impact on classification.  Accuracy not fully assessed.\t\tENVI format classified image at  Z:\\Archive\\Sampling Monitoring \u0026amp; Analysis Reporting\\Remote Sensing\\Mynydd_Du\\updated_map\\Mynydd Du Vegetation Map 2019.hdr and .dat\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Philip Jutson, Monitoring Analysis Team  Metadata Point of Contact:  Philip Jutson, Monitoring Analysis Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2021-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T16:18:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19030\tNRW_DS125195\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.411Z\t448\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwaterbodies Cycle 3\tCylch 3 Cyrff Dŵr Daear y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313935\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwaterbodies Cycle 3 (2021-2027) is a spatial (polygon) dataset which has been created for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027…all water which is below the surface of the ground in the saturation zone and in direct contact with the ground or subsoil\u0027.   For the purposes of reporting under the WFD, a groundwater body represents a distinct body of groundwater flow with a coherent flow unit including recharge and discharge areas with little flow across the boundaries. These reflect hydrogeological characteristics containing information on flow and stage properties, recharge and vulnerability to pollution. This has been undertaken through defining aquifers into different types and broken into catchment units at Catchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) scale.\tThe primary input dataset is \u00271:625K Classified Aquifer Geology\u0027. This dataset has been digitised directly from the hard copy \u00271:250K Solid Geology Map\u0027 and classified according to aquifer type and are therefore directly derived from the underlying BGS data. These classifications were verified by the British Geology Survey (BGS). These data have gone out for consultation at Area level and in some instances 1:50K Solid Geology has been used to define localised boundaries. Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Groundwater Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4460/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_groundwater_c3_baseline_classification\t2025-05-09T11:07:12.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19017\tNRW_DS125196\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.482Z\t514\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Surface Water Transfer Cycle 3\tCylch 3 Cyrff Dŵr Wyneb Trosglwyddiadau Dŵr Wyneb y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313936\tThis dataset is a GIS layer identifying the Surface Water Transfer (SWT) waterbodies managed under the Water Framework Directive and any related programmes. This includes SWT which are reported to Europe as artificial rivers. These waterbodies are represented by their centreline, and can be linked to other Water Framework Directive (WFD) data using the unique water body ID (WB_ID). WFD River, canal and SWT Waterbodies Cycle 3, is a subset extracted from the Natural Resources Wales Detailed River Network, with attributes removed and merged on the WB_ID.\tThis is an update of WFD artificial waterbodies Cycle 2. This dataset is updated every 6 years.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Surface Water Transfers Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4469/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_surface_water_transfers_c3_baseline_classification\t2025-05-12T10:55:07.681Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19024\tNRW_DS125197\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.477Z\t782\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 3\tCylch 3 Cyrff Dŵr Llyn y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313937\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Lake Waterbodies Cycle 3 is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing Water Framework Directive (WFD) attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Article 2, clause 5 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...a body of standing inland surface water\u0027.   There is data on the physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives that can be linked to waterbodies by their unique identifiers. Artificial and modified lake waterbodies are included within this dataset, however, generally only lakes above \u0026gt; 50 hectares were assessed under the WFD except for lakes in protected areas, where a minimum of 5.0 ha was used. Lakes below this threshold are not included within this dataset unless allocated as Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). Each waterbody has been assigned \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027, which is a unique identifier that enables a link to WFD attributes.   These data apply to Cycle 3 of the Water Framework Directive.\tThis data was created from the UK Lake Inventory dataset which itself took geometry from 1:50k OS products. Cycle 3 data is currently just slightly adapted from Cycle 2 data with some lakes having been removed and a new one added.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Lakes Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4463/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_lakes_c3_baseline_classification\t2025-05-12T11:25:06.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19035\tNRW_DS125198\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.897Z\t865\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbodies Cycle 3\tCylch 3 Cyrff Dŵr Afon y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313938\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbodies Cycle 3 are a spatial (polygon) dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. \u0027WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 3\u0027 is a subset extracted from the NRW\u0027s Detailed River Network (DRN).\t\u0027WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 3\u0027 is a subset extracted from the NRW\u0027s Detailed River Network (DRN). Other input datasets and extensive local Natural Resources Wales staff knowledge has been used to augment the core geometry to incorporate critical spatial detail and attribution, such as flow direction and path, not available from the OS mapping and to verify the accuracy of the centreline itself.   Sections of DRN belonging to a particular waterbody have been merged together so that a defined waterbody is a single record within the dataset (rather than being made up of hundreds of records within the DRN).   DRN attributes become obsolete at this point and waterbody specific attribution has been added. Therefore the geometry for the resulting waterbodies is taken from the DRN, but the attribution and number of records is very different. Not all sections of the DRN belong to defined waterbodies and the final WFD river waterbody dataset represents \u0026lt;20% of the online DRN.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD River Waterbodies Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-12-10\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4467/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_river_waterbodies_c3_baseline_classification\t2025-12-17T13:12:25.248Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19019\tNRW_DS125199\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.481Z\t467\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies Cycle 3\tCylch 3 Cyrff Dŵr Arfordirol y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235313939\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Waterbodies is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 7 of the WFD defines coastal waterbodies as \u0027...a surface water on the landward side of a line, every point of which is at a distance of one nautical mile on the seaward side from the nearest point of the baseline from which the breadth of territorial waters is measured, extending where appropriate up to the outer limit of transitional waters\u0027.   Coastal waters were defined by territorial waters 1 nautical mile from the Mean High Water coastline taken directly from OS 1:50K MeridianTM 2. The delineation between coastal and estuarine waters was delineated by the Environment Agency defined transitional waterbodies. Waterbodies are also split and assigned to River Basin Districts. Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives.   This dataset covers the layer for Cycle 3 of the Water framework Directive.\tThis was created from originally from OS data and territorial waters data as described in the abstract. The Cycle 3 version represents a minor update of the cycle 2 dataset.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Coastal Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4458/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_coastal_c3_baseline_classification\t2025-05-12T12:40:08.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19029\tNRW_DS125200\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.429Z\t850\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 3\tCylch 3 Cyrff Dŵr Trosiannol y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323030\t\u0027Water Framework Directive (WFD) Transitional Waterbodies\u0027 is a polygon Shapefile dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 6 of the WFD defines them as \u0027...bodies of surface water in the vicinity of river mouths which are partly saline in character as a result of their proximity to coastal waters but are substantially influenced by freshwater flows\u0027.   Transitional waterbodies were defined from Mean High Water boundaries, taken directly from OS 1:50K MeridianTM 2, and NRW estuarine boundaries defined for the Urban Waste Water Treatment Directive (UWWTD). Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives. These data apply to Cycle 3 of the Water Framework Directive.\tThis was created originally from OS data and territorial waters data as described in the abstract. The Cycle 3 version represents a minor update of the cycle 2 dataset.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Transitional Waterbodies Cycle 3.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4468/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_transitional_c3_baseline_classification\t2025-05-12T12:54:46.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19032\tNRW_DS125201\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.582Z\t806\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 3\tCylch 3 Dalgylchoedd Corff Dŵr Afon y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323031\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 3 are a polygon dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Catchments are defined as an area of land from which all surface run-off flows through a series of streams, rivers and, possibly, lakes to a particular point in the water course such as a river confluence. Since rivers are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027EA_WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to the WFD river waterbody to which it relates.\tThis dataset was originally created for use by co-deliverers before becoming final for Cycle 2 in 2013. Next map digital elevation model was generalised to 10 m resolution. Used as the basis to create the Cycle 3 layer.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-12-10\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4470/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_river_waterbody_catchments_c3_baseline_classification\t2025-12-17T13:11:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19031\tNRW_DS125202\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.448Z\t564\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDuty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Land Management Incidents\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323032\tNRW currently has Duty Tactical Manager (DTM) rotas covering the North, South East and South West areas of Wales. This dataset shows the areas of DTM responsibility for Land Management incidents (i.e. incidents on the NRW estate).\tThe boundaries between the areas of responsibility for land management incidents for the North, South East and South West DTMs have been set following the land management team boundaries, but with the Powys teams split between DTMs SW and SE along the flood duty officer area boundary and relevant forestry boundaries.\t\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Duty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Land Management Incidents.lyr\tSally Davies - Incident Management Team NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:43.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19027\tNRW_DS125203\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.433Z\t230\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDuty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Flood Incidents\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323033\tNRW currently has Duty Tactical Manager (DTM) rotas covering the North, South East and South West areas of Wales. This dataset shows the areas of DTM responsibility for Flood incidents.\tThe boundaries between the areas of responsibility for flood incidents for the North, South East and South West DTMs have been set following the flood duty officer areas, with the exception of the Severn, Vernwy and Teme catchment area, which reports to DTM North.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Duty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Flood Incidents.lyr \"\tSally Davies - Incident Management Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-02-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:30:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19028\tNRW_DS125204\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.431Z\t221\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDuty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Non-Flood and Non-Land Management Incidents\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323034\tNRW currently has Duty Tactical Manager (DTM) rotas covering the North, South East and South West areas of Wales. This dataset shows the areas of DTM responsibility for non-Flood or Land Management incidents (e.g. pollution incidents, office closures etc).\tThe boundaries between the areas of responsibility for non-flood incidents for the North, South East and South West DTMs have been set following the Unitary Authority boundaries.\t\tx:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Duty Tactical Manager Area of Responsibility for Non-Flood and Non-Land Management Incidents.lyr\tSally Davies - Incident Management Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-02-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19018\tNRW_DS125213\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.482Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGlastir Woodland Creation agreements/contract data and related schemes 2022-2027\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323133\tEach set of agreement/contract data contains 3 elements (shapefiles). There will be many sets of data delivered to NRW when they become available. 1. EoI (Expresion of Interest) – Welsh Government (WG) constructed from whole Land Parcel Identification Layer (LPIS) polygons covering the land where the applicant wants to construct planting compartments 2. Planting compartment (polygons) created by independent Forestry Planners – need to be within the EoI 3. Fences (polylines) - independent Forestry Planners – need to be within the EoI Once checked and confirmed correct by NRW – the data will be used in WG systems to create payments for Glastir Woodland Creation.\tThe LPIS is created by WG cartogaphers referencing data collected in the field by WG Rural Inspectors. This data is sent to and used by forestry planners to create Glastir planting areas and fence lines. All three are created using heads down digitising with reference to GPS data and data collected using ESRI collector. Data captured using a base of aerial imagery and OS MasterMap topo\tLPIS meta data – please contact Gary.kingsbury@gov.wales\tX:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Glastir Woodland Creation 11 EoI Boundaries.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Welsh Government\\Woodland Creation Planning Pilot Scheme (WCPPS).lyr\tGary.kingsbury@gov.wales RPW IT Data Management Metadata Point of Contact: Gary.kingsbury@gov.wales\tWelsh Government\t\t2022-03-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:47:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19022\tNRW_DS125214\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.425Z\t19\tValid\tEditable\tAfon Iwrch Sea Trout Survey Project 1984-1994 (paper data records)\tProsiect Arolwg Brithyll Môr Afon Iwrch 1984-1994 (cofnodion data papur)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323134\tSeries of 7 folders containing field notes relating to Sea Trout smolt.  Sea Trout Trap Smolt visit record sheets, contains information such as information about the actual trap, physical data (e.g. condition, temperature, pH), biological data (no. of brown and sea trout, species name, length, markings, sex stage).\tRough original hand written field notes.  Possible that data has been entered into legacy systems and therefore transferred to WISKI-KiEco (NFPD).  Field notes to be held until confirmed or disposed of if not requested within 10 years (from 2022).  8 trap sites noted.\t\tOffsite storage - Original Thomas archive code: B0008972 Restore code: EAWC00000000916\tUnknown custodian – could be fisheries team/lab team/EEAT?\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1984-01-01\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:02:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19034\tNRW_DS125215\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.614Z\t756\tValid\tEditable\tLarge Raised Reservoir register (Locations and undertakers)\tCofrestr Cronfeydd Dŵr Dyrchafedig Mawr (Lleoliadau ac ymgymerwyr)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323135\tThis dataset contains details of Large Raised Reservoirs in Wales.     Natural Resources Wales (NRW) collects and maintains data on all reservoirs designed or capable of holding more than 10,000 cubic metres of water above the natural level of any part of the land adjoining them defined as \u0027large raised reservoirs\u0027 under the Reservoirs Act 1975. Two types of reservoir are maintained within the register:   - Impounding; or  - Non-Impounding.    This dataset is an extract from NRW’s Cronfeydd/Reservoir Database. It contains information on the location, undertakers and engineers of the reservoirs.    \u0027Undertaker\u0027 is the legal term used to identify who manages and controls the use of the reservoir and is responsible for maintaining compliance with the law. The undertaker is the person, people or company that uses a reservoir for a particular purpose. If there is no use, the owners or lessees are the undertakers. The undertaker may be different from the owner. If several people or organisations manage and control the reservoir, there can be more than one undertaker.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys manylion cronfeydd dŵr uwch mawr. Mae Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) yn casglu a chynnal data ar bob cronfa ddŵr ddynodedig neu bob cronfa ddŵr sy\u0027n gallu dal dros 10,000 metr ciwbig o ddŵr uwchben lefel naturiol unrhyw ran o\u0027r tir sy\u0027n ffinio arni, sy\u0027n cael ei diffinio fel \u0027cronfa ddŵr uwch mawr\u0027 dan Ddeddf Cronfeydd Dŵr 1975. Mae dau fath o gronfeydd dŵr yn cael eu cynnal o fewn y gofrestr:   - Cronfa sy\u0027n cronni (argaeedig); neu   - Cronfa nad yw\u0027n cronni (pwmpio/dirwystr).  Rhan o Gronfa Ddata Cronfeydd/Reservoir CNC yw’r set ddata hon. Mae\u0027n cynnwys gwybodaeth am leoliad, ymgymerwyr a pheirianwyr y cronfeydd dŵr.  \u0027Ymgymerwr\u0027 (‘Undertaker’ yn Saesneg) yw\u0027r term cyfreithiol a ddefnyddir i nodi pwy sy\u0027n rheoli defnydd y gronfa ddŵr ac sy\u0027n gyfrifol am sicrhau cydymffurfiaeth â\u0027r gyfraith. Yr ymgymerwr yw\u0027r person, y bobl neu\u0027r cwmni sy\u0027n defnyddio cronfa ddŵr at ddiben benodol. Os nad oes defnydd, y perchnogion neu ddeiliaid y les yw\u0027r ymgymerwyr. Gall yr ymgymerwr fod yn wahanol i\u0027r perchennog. Os yw sawl person neu sefydliad yn rheoli\u0027r gronfa ddŵr, gall fod mwy nag un ymgymerwr.  Caiff y data hwn ei gasglu fel rhan o Gofrestr Gyhoeddus Deddf Cronfeydd Dŵr.  Datganiad priodoli: Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThis data is collected as part of the Reservoirs Act Public Register\t\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Reservoirs\tMatthew O\u0027Brien\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3505/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_large_raised_reservoirs_location_and_undertakers\t2026-01-12T14:28:48.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19033\tNRW_DS125216\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.482Z\t1265\tValid\tEditable\tLarge Raised Reservoir (Registered information)\tCronfa Ddŵr Ddyrchafedig Fawr (Gwybodaeth gofrestredig)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323136\tThis dataset contains details of Large Raised Reservoirs.    Natural Resources Wales (NRW) collects and maintains data on reservoirs designed or capable of holding more than 10,000 cubic metres of water above the natural level of any part of the land adjoining them defined as \u0027large raised reservoirs\u0027 under the Reservoirs Act 1975. Two types of reservoir are maintained within the register:    - Impounding; or  - Non-Impounding.    This dataset is an extract from NRW’s Cronfeydd/Reservoir Database. It contains registered information about the physical characteristics and inspection regimes of the dam.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys manylion cronfeydd dŵr uwch mawr. Mae Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) yn casglu a chynnal data ar bob cronfa ddŵr ddynodedig neu bob cronfa ddŵr sy\u0027n gallu dal dros 10,000 metr ciwbig o ddŵr uwchben lefel naturiol unrhyw ran o\u0027r tir sy\u0027n ffinio arni, sy\u0027n cael ei diffinio fel \u0027cronfa ddŵr uwch mawr\u0027 dan Ddeddf Cronfeydd Dŵr 1975. Mae dau fath o gronfeydd dŵr yn cael eu cynnal o fewn y gofrestr:   - Cronfa sy\u0027n cronni (argaeedig); neu   - Cronfa nad yw\u0027n cronni (pwmpio/dirwystr).  Rhan o Gronfa Ddata Cronfeydd/Reservoir CNC yw’r set ddata hon. Mae\u0027n cynnwys gwybodaeth gofrestredig am nodweddion corfforol a chyfundrefnau arolygu\u0027r argae.  Caiff y data hwn ei gasglu fel rhan o Gofrestr Gyhoeddus Deddf Cronfeydd Dŵr.  Datganiad priodoli: Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThis data is collected as part of the Reservoirs Act Public Register\t\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Reservoirs\tMatthew O\u0027Brien, Senior Specialist Advisor, Reservoir Safety Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/3514/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_large_raised_reservoirs_registered_information\t2026-01-12T14:28:06.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19025\tNRW_DS125219\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.477Z\t527\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Canal Waterbodies Cycle 3\tCylch 3 Cyrff Dŵr Camlesi’r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323139\tThis dataset is a GIS layer identifying the canal waterbodies managed under the Water Framework Directive and any related programmes. This includes Surface Water Transfer (SWT) waterbodies which are reported to Europe as artificial rivers. These waterbodies are represented by their centreline, and can be linked to other Water Framework Directive (WFD) data using the unique water body ID (WB_ID). WFD River, canal and SWT Waterbodies Cycle 3, is a subset extracted from the Natural Resources Wales Detailed River Network, with attributes removed and merged on the WB_ID.\tThis is an update of WFD artificial waterbodies Cycle 2. This dataset is updated every 6 years.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Canals Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4459/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_canals_c3_baseline_classification\t2025-05-12T13:09:16.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19016\tNRW_DS125226\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.362Z\t468\tValid\tEditable\tHabitats Directive: Article 17 Terrestrial Habitat Features\tNodweddion Cynefinoedd Daearyddol Erthygl 17\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323236\tThe Habitats Directive Article 17 reporting maps are a snapshot of the most up to date spatial data for features listed on the various Annexes of the Directive at the time of reporting. They represent the current known extent and location of features in Wales. The latest 10km2 habitat distribution maps submitted as part of the official Article 17 reporting can be obtained from the JNCC: European reporting | JNCC - Adviser to Government on Nature Conservation.   The maps are reviewed and updated every 6 years as part of the Habitats Directive reporting process. (Since EU exit now replaced by Regulation 9a of the Conservation and Species Regulations 2017).-Mae mapiau adrodd Erthygl 17 y Gyfarwyddeb Cynefinoedd yn gipolwg o\u0027r data gofodol ar gyfer nodweddion sydd wedi’u rhestru ar wahanol Atodiadau\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb yn ystod y cyfnod adrodd. Maent yn cynrychioli maint a lleoliad hysbys nodweddion yng Nghymru. Gellir cael y mapiau dosbarthu cynefinoedd 10km2 diweddaraf a gyflwynwyd fel rhan o\u0027r adroddiadau Erthygl 17 swyddogol gan y Cyd-bwyllgor Cadwraeth Natur (JNCC): European reporting | JNCC - Adviser to Government on Nature Conservation. Caiff y mapiau eu hadolygu a\u0027u diweddaru bob 6 blynedd fel rhan o broses adrodd y Gyfarwyddeb Cynefinoedd. (Ers ymadael â\u0027r UE, disodlwyd gan Reoliad 9a o Reoliadau Cadwraeth a Rhywogaethau 2017).  Mae\u0027r arolygon gwreiddiol a\u0027r digwyddiadau monitro y mae\u0027r cofnodion wedi\u0027u tynnu ohonynt yn amrywio\u0027n fawr o ran dyddiadau sy\u0027n mynd yn ôl ddegawdau lawer mewn rhai achosion, gyda hyder yn amrywio\u0027n sylweddol.  Cydnabyddiaeth:  Mae’n cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThe original surveys and monitoring events from which the records have been extracted vary widely in dates going back many decades in some cases, with confidence varying significantly. Only the most recent detailed spatial data from the different reporting rounds is published.\tSee Individual metadata entries for cycles 1, 2 and 3\tX:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2007,   X:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2012,   X:\\Biological\\Article 17\\Terrestrial\\2018,\tDave Johnston Team Leader (on secondment), Environmental Reporting.   Geralene Mills, Team Leader,  Environmental Reporting.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-01-03\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3594/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_terrestrial_art_17_habitats\t2025-12-03T17:26:02.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19026\tNRW_DS125234\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.471Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRed Zones\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323334\tThe polygon theme called “RedZones” is a subjective user interpretation (based on experience) showing forest areas with high public useage. This theme is used in the Land Management Service Plan to determine which work sites require additional mitigation- and safety measures to safeguard the public.\tUser interpretation; abstract boundaries that cannot determine any accuracy.\t\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Red zones.lyr\tPeter van Velzen\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-03-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:47:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19021\tNRW_DS125236\t2024-05-28T13:00:28.425Z\t123\tValid\tEditable\tUwch-Conwy Drone Survey October 2020\tArolwg Drôn Uwch-Gonwy Hydref 2020\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323336\tThe 10 sites were flown between the 10th and 16th September 2020 over the Uwch-Conwy peatlands.   All sites were surveyed using an RGB vertical camera, then the data processed to produce digital surface models (DSM) and orthomosaics.    The DSMs were processed to generate catchment, flow and contour vector data sets to assist with the peatbog restoration planning.\tThe accuracy of the resulting orthomosaic and digital surface models is dependent on a range of factors, including:    • The height of the camera above the terrain – this varies depending on the ruggedness of the terrain.   • The contrast and detail in the terrain – featureless and uniform vegetation provides fewer opportunities to match points between photographs resulting in lower accuracy   • Light levels – imagery flown on a bright day will have sharper images, resulting in more accurate pattern matching and thus registration   • The GPS satellite configuration at the time of the survey   • The proximity of the survey area to the GPS base stations used for applying positional corrections.     As a result, the accuracy will vary between the sites and within each site.     The surveys were generally flown in moderate to good light conditions with the GPS positions post-processed using Leica SmartNet corrections and map projection transformations carried out using Ordnance Survey OSTN15 corrections, so the absolute positional accuracy should be better than 10cm in plan and 15cm in height.     For all of the Camera and Drone details, please see accompanying report.\tThe full data report also provides additional site details on the flight path and resolution achieved for each survey. This report should accompany the data. Uwch_Conwy_DroneSurvey_Oct2020_DataReport.pdf    Aerial survey report: Uwch Conwy  Air survey of 10 upland peatbog sites in North Wales  November 2020 Exegenesis Spatial Data Management\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\Uwch-Conwy GIS layers: 1. UwchConwy_dsm 2. UwchConwy_Orthomosaic 3. UwchConwy_water 4. UwchConwy _dsm_contours_50cm 5. UwchConwywatershed_stream_segments 6. UwchConwy_hag_line 7. UwchConwy_watershed_basins 8. UwchConwy_site_boundaries 9. UwchConwy_peat 10. UwchConwy_hag GylcheddFlyby.mp4\tEsther Clews, Team Leader Peatland Data, National Peatland Action Programme, South East Wales Ops\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2020-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:52:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19014\tNRW_DS125237\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.727Z\t1147\tValid\tEditable\tSpecial Landscape Areas Wales\tArdaloedd Tirwedd Arbennig Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323337\tSpecial Landscape Areas (SLAs) are a non-statutory designation applied by a local authority to define areas of high landscape importance within their administrative boundary.  This data resource is a collation of local authority derived SLAs and is intended to help users identify whether a location of interest is in an SLA (or not), the boundaries and extent of an SLA and the SLA reference or name to help sourcing the additional information and relevant policies. It is correct as of April 2022.-Mae Ardal Tirwedd Arbennig (ATA) yn ddynodiad anstatudol a ddefnyddir gan awdurdod lleol i ddiffinio ardaloedd o bwysigrwydd uchel o ran eu tirwedd o fewn eu ffin weinyddol. Mae’r adnodd data hwn yn gasgliad o Ardaloedd Tirwedd Arbennig sy’n deillio o awdurdodau lleol a’i fwriad yw helpu defnyddwyr i nodi pa un a yw lleoliad o ddiddordeb mewn ATA (ai peidio), beth yw ffiniau a maint yr ATA a beth yw cyfeirnod neu enw’r ATA i helpu i ddod o hyd i’r wybodaeth ychwanegol a pholisïau perthnasol. Mae’n gywir ym mis Ebrill 2022.  Datganiad priodoli: Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data OS © Hawlfraint y Goron a hawliau cronfa ddata. Rhif Trwydded yr Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Cafodd data ei ddigideiddio o ddata © pob un o\u0027r 22 Awdurdod Lleol yng Nghymru.\tIndividual local authority SLA GIS data provided to NRW for compilation of an all Wales data resource to share.\t\tx:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Special Landscape Areas.lyr\tJill Bullen, Lead Specialist Advisor: Landscape, Strategic Planning Group.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-01-13\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4176/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_special_landscape_area\t2025-05-09T16:09:12.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19015\tNRW_DS125257\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.726Z\t401\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Opportunity Catchments Layer Final 2021 - Cycle 3\tFersiwn Derfynol Haen Dalgylch Cyfle’r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) 2021 - Cylch 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323537\tThe Opportunity Catchment layer shows the boundaries and locations of Wales’ ten Opportunity Catchments.  The Opportunity Catchments form part of the third cycle River Basin Management Plans (2021-2027).  The layer shows the ten Opportunity Catchments in Wales.\tThe Opportunity Catchment boundaries were created using Water Framework Directive (WFD) (now the WFD Regulations 2017) Cycle 1 waterbody catchment boundaries with small, non reportable waterbodies (e.g. drain to transitional and coastal (TRAC) waterbodies) included where relevant.   Cycle 2 transitional and coastal waterbodies have been included where they have a similar hydrodynamic regime and are subject to similar pressures to terrestrial waterbodies at the seaward extent.  For cross border waterbodies they have been split into two parts one in Wales and England.   A definition query based on the \\Cross_Border\\ field displays only the Opportunity Catchments in Wales.  To see the parts of the Dee and Central Monmouthshire catchments that extend into England remove the definition query.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Opportunity Catchments Cycle 3 2021.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Helen Haider, SMNR Advisor (Water), NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4466/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_opportunity_catchments_c3\t2025-05-12T10:03:00.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19011\tNRW_DS125258\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.658Z\t999\tValid\tEditable\tWales Construction and Demolition waste arisings survey\tArolwg o Sgil-gynhyrchion Gwastraff Adeiladu a Dymchwel yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323538\tThe purpose of the study was to provide information on the types, quantities, origins (by Construction and Demolition sector and geographic region), and fate (management method) of Construction and Demolition (C\u0026amp;D) wastes generated by businesses and the public sector in Wales in 2019. This is a stand-alone survey and supersedes the data from the last survey for the 2012 calendar year.   This information is required for a variety of reasons including:     • statutory reporting on waste generation in compliance with Part 2 of Schedule 1 of the Waste (England and Wales) Regulations 2011 and, up until 2020, the EU Waste Statistics Regulations 2002;  • informing the development of national waste policy;  • monitoring progress against national waste prevention and recycling targets;  • informing waste planners and the regulator;  • supporting investment decisions such as the selection of the type and scale of new waste management facilities required by local authorities and the waste industry;  • updating the evidence base for the State of Natural Resources Report Wales assessment (Natural Resources Wales 2021), and;  • providing businesses with a better understanding of their current waste practices and for developing sustainable waste management support strategies by Welsh Government for improving environmental performance of businesses.    The precision for the total waste generated in Wales was +/- 16.7% at 90% confidence.  Results from all surveys are subject to limitations with respect to the quality of estimates produced. Whilst these limitations do not alter the results or the statistical data, they should be borne in mind by users of the data.  Information on waste management in the report is generally reliable but the accuracy of the results is limited to information available to surveyed producers on the final destinations of their waste. The accuracy is limited owing to the complexities of waste management routes and difficulties linking final fate back to source.    Further information, including on the survey confidence, is contained in the published survey report. The detailed survey technical appendices are also available on request.-Diben yr astudiaeth oedd darparu gwybodaeth am fathau, symiau, tarddiadau (yn ôl y sector Adeiladu a Dymchwel a\u0027r rhanbarth daearyddol), a thynged (dull rheoli) gwastraff Adeiladu a Dymchwel a gynhyrchwyd gan fusnesau a\u0027r sector cyhoeddus yng Nghymru yn 2019. Mae hwn yn arolwg annibynnol ac mae\u0027n disodli\u0027r data o\u0027r arolwg diwethaf ar gyfer blwyddyn galendr 2012. Mae angen y wybodaeth hon am amryw o resymau.  Lefel y manylder ar gyfer cyfanswm y gwastraff a gynhyrchwyd yng Nghymru oedd +/- 16.7% ar hyder o 90%.  Mae cyfyngiadau’n bodoli o ran ansawdd yr amcangyfrifon a gynhyrchir ar gyfer canlyniadau pob arolwg. Er nad yw\u0027r cyfyngiadau hyn yn newid y canlyniadau na\u0027r data ystadegol, dylai defnyddwyr y data eu cadw mewn cof.  Casglwyd data o sampl gynrychioliadol o 508 o safleoedd busnes o wahanol sectorau a meintiau ledled Cymru rhwng mis Ebrill 2021 a mis Medi 2021. Cafodd y data ei grosio gan ddefnyddio data poblogaeth safleoedd busnes y Swyddfa Ystadegau Gwladol (SYG) i lefel ranbarthol a chenedlaethol yng Nghymru.  Roedd y fethodoleg a ddefnyddiwyd i ddatblygu\u0027r matrics samplu a\u0027r gwaith dilynol o grosio canlyniadau\u0027r arolwg yn seiliedig ar arolygon Adeiladu a Dymchwel blaenorol yng Nghymru i sicrhau y gellid cymharu’r canlyniadau. Cafodd dyluniad y matrics samplu ei hun ei newid ychydig o\u0027r hyn a ddefnyddiwyd yn 2012, drwy gyfuno is-sectorau Adeiladu Cyffredinol yn un sector.  Roedd y matrics samplu’n diffinio faint o safleoedd busnes yr oedd angen eu cyrraedd ar gyfer pob bricsen. Fe\u0027i hadolygwyd ran o\u0027r ffordd drwy\u0027r arolwg i ymgorffori anghysondebau o ran sector neu faint cwmnïau a nodwyd yn set ddata wreiddiol y Swyddfa Ystadegau Gwladol (SYG) yn ystod y broses recriwtio, er mwyn adlewyrchu cyfraddau recriwtio gwirioneddol o fewn briciau.  Mae gwybodaeth am reoli gwastraff yn yr adroddiad yn ddibynadwy ar y cyfan ond mae cywirdeb y canlyniadau wedi\u0027i gyfyngu i\u0027r wybodaeth oedd ar gael i gynhyrchwyr a arolygwyd ar gyrchfannau terfynol eu gwastraff. Mae\u0027r cywirdeb yn gyfyngedig oherwydd cymhlethdodau o ran llwybrau rheoli gwastraff ac anawsterau wrth gysylltu tynged derfynol yn ôl i\u0027r ffynhonnell.  Ceir rhagor o wybodaeth, gan gynnwys gwybodaeth o ran hyder yr arolwg, yn adroddiad cyhoeddedig yr arolwg. Mae atodiadau technegol manwl yr arolwg hefyd ar gael ar gais.  Cydnabyddiaeth:   Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.  Darllenwch y drwydded i ddeall amodau defnyddio\u0027r data.\tData was collected from a representative sample of 508 business sites of differing sectors and sizes throughout Wales between April 2021 and September 2021. The data was grossed up using Office for National Statistics (ONS) business site population data to regional and national level in Wales.     The methodology used for development of the sample matrix and subsequent grossing of the survey results was based on previous Wales C\u0026amp;D surveys to ensure comparability of results. The design of the sample matrix itself was changed slightly from that applied in 2012, with the consolidation of General Building subsectors into a single sector.    The sample matrix defined how many business sites needed to be reached for each brick. It was reviewed part way through the survey to incorporate inconsistencies in company size or sector that were identified in the original Office of National Statistics (ONS) dataset during recruitment, in order to reflect actual business recruitment rates within bricks.     Further information on the survey data collection and quality assurance methods is available in the published survey report and the detailed technical appendices are also available on request.\tM.Garrett, G.Armstrong, M.Fogarty, J.Fry, 2019 Wales Construction \u0026amp; Demolition Waste Arisings Survey report.  NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 625, 77pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor - https://cdn.cyfoethnaturiol.cymru/media/694993/cd-2019-survey-web-accessible-final-003.pdf\thttps://naturalresourceswales.sharefile.eu/share/view/s65d2984bde9a4d5aabf47af3a0d7ef4a/fo74f4e3-fe88-43d1-a02d-7c9a96c06426\tInternal Data Custodian(s): John Fry, Project Executive, NRW  Metadata Point of Contact:  John Fry, Project Executive, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-05-19\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/2938\t2025-02-04T10:16:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19013\tNRW_DS125264\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.727Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBenthic Core Sites\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323634\tWater Framework Directive benthic core data. This data shows the positions of the cores that were taken. Species and partical size information for the cores are available on KiECO.\tCore locations are created onGIS software. Survey officers find the core locations in the field using a hand held GPS and take a fix of this position. This data is the position taken by survey officers in the field. Species and partical size information for the cores are available on KiECO.\tThis data contributes to Natural Resources Wales Water Framework Directive classifications. X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Benthic Cores\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Benthic Cores\\Nyfer 2021 Benthic Quadrats.lyr  X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Benthic Cores\\Teifi 2021 Benthic Quadrats.lyr\tKathrynBorrowdale\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-05-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19012\tNRW_DS125266\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.655Z\t709\tValid\tEditable\tFlood Defence Structures\tLleoliadau Amddiffynfeydd rhag Llifogydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323636\tThe National Flood Asset Database records flood infrastructure in Wales managed by Lead Local Flood Authorities (LLFAs) and Natural Resources Wales (NRW). It includes many embankments, walls, flood gates, culverts, and inlet screens (which catch debris), but not all flood infrastructure maintained or owned by these parties. This infrastructure has been constructed to help manage the risk of flooding from rivers and the sea. They do not remove the risk of flooding and can be overtopped, blocked, or fail in extreme weather conditions.   Three datasets are available as open data:  1. Flood defence Locations. These show the linear alignment of flood defence asset that have been built to protect against flooding from rivers and the sea. It includes the type, length, maintainer, and an ID. These defence locations are also shown in Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW), Flood Map for Planning (FMfP) and Find flood defence structures near you maps. They may relate to an Area Benefiting from Flood Defence (FRAW) and TAN15 zone (FMfP).     2. Culverts (Polyline); These show an indicative location of underground culverts for general guidance only. Attributes include maintainer, owner, length and ID. Due to the underground nature and history of many culverts, the locations may not be accurate, complete, or up to date.     3. Culvert openings and Screens (Points). These show an indicative location of culvert openings and screens (often referred to as Trash or Debris Screens). Attributes include maintainer, owner and ID. Locations and details may not be accurate, complete or up to date.     We will regularly update the information when new and improved data becomes available. If you have any queries or concerns about location, maintenance, condition, or records of flood assets, check with the owner or maintainer.-Mae\u0027r Gronfa Ddata Asedau Llifogydd Genedlaethol yn cofnodi\u0027r seilwaith llifogydd yng Nghymru a reolir gan Awdurdodau Llifogydd Lleol Arweiniol a Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC). Mae\u0027n cynnwys llawer o argloddiau, waliau, giatiau llifogydd, cwlferi, a sgriniau mewnfeydd (sy\u0027n dal malurion), ond nid pob seilwaith llifogydd sy\u0027n cael ei gynnal neu sy\u0027n eiddo i\u0027r partïon hyn. Mae\u0027r seilwaith yma wedi\u0027i lunio i helpu i reoli\u0027r risg o lifogydd o afonydd a\u0027r môr. Dydyn nhw ddim yn cael gwared ar y risg o lifogydd a gallant fethu mewn tywydd eithafol. Mae tair set ddata ar gael fel data agored.  Lleoliadau\u0027r amddiffynfeydd rhag llifogydd. Mae\u0027r rhain yn dangos aliniad llinellol asedau atal llifogydd sydd wedi\u0027u hadeiladu i amddiffyn rhag llifogydd o afonydd a\u0027r môr. Mae\u0027n cynnwys y math, yr hyd, y sawl sy’n eu cynnal, a manylion adnabod. Mae\u0027r lleoliadau amddiffynfeydd hyn i\u0027w gweld hefyd yn Asesiad Perygl Llifogydd Cymru, y Map Llifogydd ar gyfer Cynllunio a mapiau Gweld strwythurau atal llifogydd yn agos atoch chi. Efallai y byddant yn ymwneud ag Ardal sy\u0027n Elwa o Amddiffynfa rhag Llifogydd a pharth TAN15. Cwlferi (Polylinell); Mae\u0027r rhain yn dangos lleoliad dangosol cwlferi tanddaearol ar gyfer arweiniad cyffredinol yn unig. Mae\u0027r priodoleddau\u0027n cynnwys y sawl sy’n eu cynnal, y perchennog, yr hyd a manylion adnabod. Oherwydd natur a hanes tanddaearol llawer o gwlferi, efallai na fydd y lleoliadau\u0027n gywir, yn gyflawn, neu’n gyfredol. Agoriadau a Sgriniau Cwlferi (Pwyntiau). Mae\u0027r rhain yn dangos lleoliad dangosol agoriadau a sgriniau cwlferi (y cyfeirir atynt yn aml fel Sgriniau Malurion). Mae\u0027r priodoleddau\u0027n cynnwys y sawl sy’n eu cynnal, y perchennog a manylion adnabod. Efallai na fydd y lleoliadau a’r manylion yn gywir, yn gyflawn neu’n gyfredol. Byddwn yn diweddaru\u0027r wybodaeth yn rheolaidd pan fydd data newydd a gwell ar gael. Os oes gennych unrhyw ymholiadau neu bryderon am leoliad, cyflwr, neu gofnodion o asedau llifogydd, gwiriwch gyda\u0027r perchennog neu\u0027r sawl sy’n eu cynnal.  Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn seiliedig ar leoliad daearyddol asedau llifogydd yng Nghymru ac mae\u0027n deillio o fesurau 6, 7 ac 8 y Strategaeth Llifogydd Genedlaethol sy\u0027n ceisio dod â\u0027r data hwn at ei gilydd a\u0027i wneud yn hygyrch i\u0027r cyhoedd. Mae\u0027r data\u0027n cael ei gymryd o systemau rheoli asedau a reolir gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a\u0027r Awdurdodau Llifogydd Lleol Arweiniol canlynol.  Cyngor Bwrdeistref Sirol Blaenau Gwent  Cyngor Bwrdeistref Sirol Bridgend  Cyngor Bwrdeistref Sirol Caerphilly  Cyngor Caerdydd  Cyngor Sir Caerfyrddin  Cyngor Sir Ceredigion  Cyngor Dinas Casnewydd  Dinas a Sir Abertawe  Cyngor Bwrdeistref Sirol Conwy  Cyngor Bwrdeistref Sirol Sir Ddinbych  Cyngor Gwynedd  Cyngor Sir Ynys Môn  Cyngor Bwrdeistref Sirol Merthyr Tudful  Cyngor Sir y Fflint  Cyngor Sir Fynwy  Cyngor Bwrdeistref Sirol Castell-nedd Port Talbot  Cyngor Sir Penfro  Cyngor Sir Powys  Cyngor Bwrdeistref Sirol Rhondda Cynon Taf  Cyngor Bwrdeistref Sirol Torfaen  Cyngor Bro Morgannwg  Cyngor Bwrdeistref Sirol Wrecsam  Mae lleoliad asedau llifogydd ar gyfer arweiniad cyffredinol yn unig. Nid yw\u0027n dangos pob ased llifogydd nac unrhyw erydiad / amddiffyniad arfordirol a reolir gan y cyrff hyn. Gall rhywfaint o\u0027r data fod yn anghywir neu\u0027n hen. Efallai na fyddai rhai o\u0027r asedau tanddaearol yn hysbys neu wedi eu cofnodi\u0027n gywir. Os oes gennych unrhyw ymholiadau neu bryderon am leoliad, cyflwr neu gofnodion o asedau llifogydd, gwiriwch gyda\u0027r perchennog neu\u0027r sawl sy\u0027n eu cynnal.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. © Awdurdodau Lleol Cymru.\tThis dataset is based on the geographical location of flood assets in Wales and stems from measures 6, 7 and 8 of the National Flood Strategy that aims to bring this data together and make it publicly accessible. The data is taken from asset management systems managed by Natural Resources Wales and the following Lead Local Flood Authorities:    Blaenau Gwent CBC   Bridgend CBC   Caerphilly CBC   Cardiff Council   Carmarthenshire CC   Ceredigion CC   City \u0026amp; County of Swansea  Conwy CBC   Denbighshire CBC   Flintshire CC  Gwynedd Council  Isle of Anglesey CC   Merthyr Tydfil CBC   Monmouthshire CC  Neath-Port Talbot CBC  Newport CC   Pembrokeshire CC  Powys CC   Rhondda Cynon Taff CBC  Torfaen CBC  Vale of Glamorgan Council  Wrexham CBC    The position of flood assets is for general guidance only. It does not show all flood assets or any coastal erosion/ protection managed by these bodies. Some of the data may be inaccurate or out-of-date. Some of the underground assets might not be known or recorded accurately.\t\tZ:\\Archive\\Water Management\\Flood Risk Mgmt\\FRA\\Maps\\FD Locations and X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Defence Locations\tBen Hext - lead specialist advisor Flood Risk Analysis\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/4126/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_flood_defence_structures\t2025-12-01T11:33:24.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19009\tNRW_DS125268\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.382Z\t16\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Transect Lines\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323638\tWater Framework Directive saltmarsh monitoring. The lines in these layers show the location of the saltmarsh transects surveyed.\tThe saltmarsh transects are surveyed on foot following WFD methodology. Spatial data is recorded using a hand held dGPS device. Where the seaward point cannot be taken on foot, the point is derived from aerial imagery.\tThis data contributes to Natural Resources Wales Water Framework Directive Classifications. X:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine\\Saltmarsh\tKathryn Borrowdale\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-09-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:52:12.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19008\tNRW_DS125279\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.366Z\t806\tValid\tEditable\tLANDMAP Cultural Landscape Services\tGwasanaethau Tirwedd Ddiwylliannol LandMap\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323739\tLANDMAP is a complete All-Wales GIS based landscape resource where landscape characteristics, qualities and influences on the landscape are recorded and evaluated into a nationally consistent data set. LANDMAP comprises five spatially related datasets known as the Geological Landscape, Landscape Habitats, Landscape Habitats, the Historic Landscape and the Cultural Landscape Services.    The LANDMAP Cultural Landscape Services layer provides information relating to cultural services and the connections between landscape, the natural environment and place. Including natural settings, aesthetic appreciation, tranquillity, cultural heritage, inspiration and spiritual and identity and sense of place. The existing Visual \u0026amp; Sensory boundaries are used as context, and all data is calculated to these boundaries.   Cultural Landscape Services replaces the LANDMAP Cultural Landscape dataset.  This new dataset (which supersedes NRW_DS108654) will focus mapping on some key cultural services particularly linked to landscape but will in time include richer information in the surveys. This will be a significant step forward in delivering reliable, usable data that can be built upon and continually improved.-Mae LANDMAP yn adnodd tirwedd Cymru Gyfan sy’n seiliedig ar GIS lle mae nodweddion tirwedd a dylanwadau ar y dirwedd yn cael eu cofnodi a’u gwerthuso mewn set ddata sy’n gyson yn genedlaethol. Mae LANDMAP yn cynnwys pum set ddata sy\u0027n ymwneud â gofod, sef y Dirwedd Ddaearegol, Cynefinoedd y Dirwedd, Gweledol a Synhwyraidd, y Dirwedd Hanesyddol a\u0027r Gwasanaethau Tirwedd Diwylliannol.  Mae haen Gwasanaethau Tirwedd Diwylliannol LANDMAP yn darparu gwybodaeth yn ymwneud â gwasanaethau diwylliannol a\u0027r cysylltiadau rhwng tirwedd, yr amgylchedd naturiol a lle. Mae’n cynnwys lleoliadau naturiol, gwerthfawrogiad esthetig, llonyddwch, treftadaeth ddiwylliannol, ysbrydoliaeth ac ysbrydolrwydd a hunaniaeth ac ymdeimlad o le. Defnyddir y ffiniau Gweledol a Synhwyraidd presennol fel cyd-destun, a chyfrifir yr holl ddata yn ôl y ffiniau hyn. Mae\u0027r Gwasanaethau Tirwedd Diwylliannol yn disodli set ddata Tirwedd Ddiwylliannol LANDMAP.  Bydd y set ddata newydd hon (sy’n disodli Haen y Dirwedd Ddiwylliannol) yn canolbwyntio ar fapio rhai gwasanaethau diwylliannol allweddol sy’n arbennig o gysylltiedig â thirwedd ond ymhen amser bydd yn cynnwys gwybodaeth gyfoethocach yn yr arolygon. Bydd hwn yn gam sylweddol ymlaen o ran darparu data dibynadwy, ymarferol y gellir adeiladu arno a’i wella’n barhaus.     Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tAll input data was checked for a selection of data quality issues, namely gaps and overlaps. In cases where such issues were severe enough to have the potential substantially affect results, these were addressed.\tE-K Naumann and Dr K Medcalf 2019 LANDMAP Cultural Landscape Services. NRW Evidence Report No. 336. 51 pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\LandMap\\LANDMAP Cultural Landscape.lyr\tJill Bullen - Lead Specialist Advisor Landscapes\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-07-22\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/2475/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_LANDMAP_Cultural_Landscape_Services\t2025-12-03T16:19:02.099Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19007\tNRW_DS125285\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.362Z\t854\tValid\tEditable\tFirebreaks in NRW South Central Region\tParthau Atal Tân yn Rhanbarth Canol De Cymru CNC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323835\tDataset showing the location of firebreaks created and managed by Natural Resources Wales operational staff in the Welsh Government Woodland Estate. The data covers the NRW South Central Operational Region.  Data was collected so firebreak locations can be shared with South Wales Fire and Rescue Service, to aid in the tackling wildfires.-gweithredol Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru yn Ystad Goetir Llywodraeth Cymru. Mae\u0027r data\u0027n cwmpasu Rhanbarth Gweithredol Canol De Cymru CNC.  Casglwyd data fel y gellir rhannu lleoliadau parthau atal tân â Gwasanaeth Tân ac Achub De Cymru, er mwyn cynorthwyo’r gwaith o fynd i\u0027r afael â thanau gwyllt.  Datganiad priodoli:  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tData was captured on site using a mobile GIS device.  Data is in the form of line data.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Firebreaks in NRW South Central Region.lyr\tGeraint Price Senior Officer Forest Operations – South Central (+ Rebekah Davies – Technical Support Officer Forest Operations)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-03-24\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4510/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_sc_firebreaks\t2025-10-24T08:51:21.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19010\tNRW_DS125288\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.401Z\t415\tValid\tEditable\tBait digging evidence database\tCronfa ddata tystiolaeth cloddio am abwyd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323838\tThe bait digging evidence database (an Excel spreadsheet) contains the detailed information which forms the rationale for the report produced by ABPmer entitled ‘West, V., Robbins, K., Frost, N., Hull, S. 2022. Bait Digging Management Options. NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 602, 74pp, NRW, Bangor’.     It displays information about each of the eleven bait digging sites in Wales in order to inform potential management measures.  This includes information such as sediment type, exposure, area of the site, intensity of digging, extent of Section 7 muddy gravels, recovery times of sediment, protected sites present, site access and proximity of potential displacement sites.  It also contains the intensity scoring, which uses proportion of the site used for bait digging and intensity of digging to rank and prioritise the sites.\tThe bait digging evidence database contains information taken principally from a NRW commissioned report entitled; Perrins, J., Lush, M., Taylor, T., Holt, R and Bunker, F. 2020. Investigating the location and intensity of bait digging in Wales. NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 449, 191pp, NRW, Bangor.  Information has been taken from this report and used to create this summary spreadsheet.      In addition to factual information from the Perrins et al report, ABPmer have used professional judgement to add additional information on site access and proximity of displacement sites.    The spreadsheet also contains additional information from other sources such as protected sites (SACs and SSSIs) and their features which are located at the bait digging sites.    The intensity scoring is also presented which allows the sites to be prioritised and forms the base of the accompanying report.   Data was derived from bait digging intensity at each site (from Perrins et al. 2020). Data were weighted as a proportion of high, medium and low intensity and summed to determine the intensity score. The full method is presented in the accompanying report (R.3772).\tWest, V., Robbins, K., Frost, N., Hull, S. 2022. Bait Digging Management Options. NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 602, 74pp, NRW, Bangor\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/advice/mar/MPANLICACT/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7B8803200A-1E1E-4EA3-A959-6791FB8FBD5D%7D\u0026amp;file\u003dR3772_BaitDiggingEvidenceDatabase_100222.xlsx\u0026amp;action\u003ddefault\u0026amp;mobileredirect\u003dtrue\u0026amp;DefaultItemOpen\u003d1\u0026amp;CID\u003dE7498CFC-2686-4650-98FC-FBFA5BFF8B67\u0026amp;wdLOR\u003dcE61ECA1C-CF4D-43B9-ACF3-CADEB8A6DAE6\tLaura Grant, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-08-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T09:57:15.457Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n19006\tNRW_DS125294\t2024-05-28T13:00:27.257Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBala Sluices Water Level Records\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323934\tBala Sluices Water Level Records\t\t\t2 off-site storage archive boxes - number tbc\tDebbie Evans/Rob Bissell Water Resources Team  off-site storage box ref: EAW/BALASLU/1 and  EAW/BALASLU/2 Ta\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T15:15:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19048\tNRW_DS125297\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.618Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDetermining Confidence in the 2018 Indicative Condition Assessments\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235323937\tIn 2018 Indicative Condition Assessments were undertaken to examine the feature condition of Marine Protected Areas. Given the time available at the time, the process for assigning condition was limited. Thus, given the potential for marine to be taken into the Special Area of Conservation (SAC) river project, whereby permits may be refused or reviewed due to water quality failures within SACs, we need to determine our confidence in those failures assigned in 2018. The dataset here is that used to support the generation of confidence metrics. It comes in Excel files and GIS shapefiles.\tThe original data / source material is as follows: • GIS shapefiles of WFD water bodies, either from our own repository or downloaded off the Environment Agencies catchment explorer.  • GIS shapefiles of Marine SACs and their features • WFD classification results: for 2015 cycle 2 and 2021 cycle 3 (for Wales) and 2015 cycle 2 and 2019 cycle 2 (for England) • Confidence of class for WFD water bodies obtained from the tool leads • Site locations for each WFD element Dissolved Inorganic Nitrogen, phytoplankton and opportunistic macroalgae.  ArcGIS has been used to clip (using the clip tool) WFD data to SAC boundary or SAC feature boundary. The calculate geometry tool was then used to calculate the areas (in km2) of the polygons created. This enabled the percentage of the SAC or SAC feature covered by a WFD water body to be calculated.  For confidence of class (CoC), we have calculated a combined confidence of class. This is so that we can say there is a very certain or quite certain pass (or fail) above (or below) the Good-Moderate boundary. Combined CoC was calculated by adding up the CoC for each status class above or below the Good-Moderate boundary.\tNRW Evidence Report to be added\tData currently stored on DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/mar/mar-wq/Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fadvice%2Fmar%2Fmar%2Dwq%2FEvidence%2FNutrient%20SAC%20project\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200078914C818B753241BAFF4899DE5F2F8F\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1FDF1E04%2D17DE%2D4770%2DBA8B%2D3EDC8394EB22%7D\t\t\t\t2022-07-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19054\tNRW_DS125305\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.650Z\t1753\tValid\tEditable\tTranquillity and Place: Visually Tranquil Areas\tLlonyddwch a Lle: Ardaloedd o Lonyddwch Gweledol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333035\tA nationally consistent terrestrial Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place resource that identifies visually tranquil areas for use as an evidence base to inform policy intent, practice and provision for well-being benefits.   Storymap link https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/865c1876d9f64280a3dfc6e2769a46a5    Tranquillity is associated with the degree to which places and ecosystems deliver a state of quiet, calm, peace and well-being. This can be described as a relative abundance, perception or experience of nature, natural landscapes and features and/or a relative freedom from unwanted visual disturbance, signs of human influence and artificial noise.-Adnodd Llonyddwch a Lle daearol sy’n gyson yn genedlaethol ac sy\u0027n gofnod o ardaloedd o lonyddwch gweledol, i\u0027w ddefnyddio fel sylfaen dystiolaeth i lywio bwriad polisi, ymarfer a darpariaeth ar gyfer buddion lles. Dolen i’r map stori https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/865c1876d9f64280a3dfc6e2769a46a5  Mae llonyddwch yn gysylltiedig â faint mae lleoedd ac ecosystemau yn creu naws o dawelwch, heddwch a lles. Gellir disgrifio hyn fel llawnder, canfyddiad neu brofiad cymharol o fyd natur, tirweddau a nodweddion naturiol a/neu ryddid cymharol rhag aflonyddwch gweledol, arwyddion o ddylanwad dynol a sŵn artiffisial.    Datganiad Priodoli:  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru Cyfoeth © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn deillio yn Rhannol o BGS Digital Data dan Rif Trwydded 2013/062. Cymdeithas Ddaearegol Prydain.\tFull list of datasets used is in evidence report listed under each indicator https://www.lucmaps.co.uk/NRW_TranquillityPlace/Tranquillity%20and%20Place%20Final%20Report.pdf\tGreen C, Manson D, Chamberlain K 2022. Tranquillity and Place. NRW Report No: 569. Natural Resources Wales\tX:Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquility and Place\tJill Bullen - Specialist Advisor Agriculture\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-04-29\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/4003\t2025-04-30T15:25:26.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19049\tNRW_DS125308\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.623Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPowys Woodland Survey 1982-85/86\tArolwg Coedydd Powys\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333038\tPowys County Council set up a woodland project in 1981 to discover more about the semi-natural broadleaved woodland of the County. The Powys Woodland project survey ran from 1982 to 1985 and completed an ecological classification of semi-natural woods in the county, recognising fifteen woodland site types in a sample of four hundred and thirty woods surveyed either by the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) between 1970-80 or by local Nature Trusts between 1980-82.  The work explores how much exists, its type and distribution, how much change has occurred  and some possible reasons for the survival of long-established woods. The approach was ecological to find out the amount of hardwood timber left, the irreplacable assets of the country.\tThe work was undertaken with help from the Nature Conservancy Council, Montogomery Nature Trust and the Hereford and Radnor Nature Trust. Most staff worked under the general supervision of David Humphries, the project manager.\tThe data holdings in NRW might only be the paper records undertaken by NCC staff.\toff-site archive storage (add number)\tData Services team (OpenData inbox)       Data Services team opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t1985-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T16:24:07.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19052\tNRW_DS125313\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.643Z\t770\tValid\tEditable\tWales River Restoration Plans\tCynlluniau Adfer Afonydd Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333133\tCollection of river restoration plans presented in geodatabases identifying opportunities for river restoration within applicable waterbody.   Individual river restoration plans commissioned by Natural Resources Wales, delivered by external consultant.    Plans are made for the following rivers:  Rhymney Tywi Roath Brook Pembroke River Pelenna Nant Y Fendrod Nant Glandulas Ely/ Nant Dowlais Dulas Cynon Clywedog Afonydd Cleddau Afon Wen\tA spatially integrated study has been conducted to gain the understanding necessary to describe system form and behaviour, predict future change and focus efforts on enhancing natural processes with the river catchments.   This study combines desk-based and targeted field-based components, to deliver the geomorphological analysis and ecological interpretation of the physical impacts on the River Clywedog.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\River Restoration Fisheries Activity Lines.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\River Restoration Fisheries Activity Points.r X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\River Restoration Line.lyr\tRhodri Powell - Specialist Advisor, River Restoration Programme, Freshwater Ecosystems and Fisheries Management\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-11-04\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:58.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19050\tNRW_DS125315\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.607Z\t7\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWoodland Surveys in Dyfed/Powys 1988 using the National Vegetation Classification (NVC) Cooke \u0026amp;Saunders - ( Paper data records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333135\tThis survey covers 156 woods and 2200 ha throughout Dyfed and the districts of Radnor and Montgomery in Powys. Many private and public landowners granted permission to survey their wooodland. Survey results were recorded on standard NVC record cards and a 300-500 word report on each site was written.  Phase II surveys were undertaken in selected woods using standard procedures. Woodland communities were described using the National Vegetation Classification NVC.\tInitially a preliminary selection was made from the Inventories of Ancient Woodland using size as a criterion. Semi-natural sites of 10 hectares or more we primarily chosen but this was flexible according to the vice-country. The major geological formations and SSSI status were also taken into account. Information gathered for each site was summarised on a top sheet, to which copies of other records were attached and all of which were filed in 10 km square order for each vice-couinty for easy reference during the survey period.\t\tpaper data currently in MYF post room floor 1\tData Services team opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk (or James Latham although he has moved post and no longer the custodian)       Data Services team (OpenData@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1988-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-22T16:23:57.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19047\tNRW_DS125323\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.592Z\t1275\tValid\tEditable\tRiver Wye Special Area of Conservation Sonde Data\tData Sondiau Ardal Cadwraeth Arbennig Afon Gwy\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333233\tA number of automatic monitors are in place in the River Wye Special Area of Conservation. Data is collected for seven water quality parameters at 15 minute intervals on a semi-permanent basis to assess the state of the river and changes over time.-Mae nifer o ddyfeisiau monitro awtomatig ar waith yn Ardal Cadwraeth Arbennig Afon Gwy. Cesglir data ar gyfer saith paramedr ansawdd dŵr bob 15 munud ar sail lled-barhaol i asesu cyflwr yr afon a newidiadau dros amser.  Cesglir data gan ddyfeisiau monitro awtomatig sydd wedi\u0027u graddnodi. Cesglir y data i helpu Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru i gyflawni ei ddyletswyddau statudol. Fe\u0027i cyhoeddir oherwydd gallai\u0027r wybodaeth fod yn ddefnyddiol i eraill sy\u0027n ymwneud â rheoli dalgylchoedd. Gwneir pob ymdrech i sicrhau cywirdeb y wybodaeth a ddarperir.   Gwneir gwaith glanhau data i ddileu data gwallus, ond nid oes proses sicrhau ansawdd drwyadl.  Datganiad priodoli:   Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tData is collected by calibrated automatic monitors. The data is collected to help Natural Resources Wales fulfil its statutory duties. It is published because the information might be useful to others involved in catchment management. Every effort is made to ensure the accuracy of the information provided The data is subject to data cleansing to remove erroneous data, but is not subject to rigorous quality assurance.\t\tZ:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\Data Mgmt\\Sonde Data\\Wye\\Processed\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Dave Johnston, Environmental Reporting Team Leader (Temporary reassignment, please contact Geralene Mills)  NRW  Metadata Point of Contact:  Dave Johnston, Environmental Reporting Team Leader, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-03-24\tpublication\tnull\thttps://smnr-nrw.hub.arcgis.com/apps/98b26568c38f48c797ddc9a198dea7d0/explore\t2025-12-19T10:47:45.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19055\tNRW_DS125325\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.677Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMetal Mine Interventions in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333235\tMetal Mine Interventions in Wales records the locations of structures and desk studies completed by the Metal Mine Programme. These structures range from flow gauging station to surface water management to capping works. The data is collected to display where remediation works and desk studies on metal mine sites have been done. It should be noted that the dataset is collected in 2022. As the Metal Mine Programme continues, new assets may be created in the future.\tMetal Mine Interventions in Wales: The dataset consists of structures and desk studies completed by the Metal Mine Programme. Through conversation with project managers on each site, precise locations of structures could be collected and displayed. This is checked by the project executive with knowledge on all the site for accuracy. The desk studies used grid references of the mine they are associated with and went through similar process as data for structures on the site.\t\tX:\\Industrial\\Mines\\Metal Mine Interventions in Wales.lyr\tSophia Lennie\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:47:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19051\tNRW_DS125326\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.588Z\t14\tValid\tEditable\tMetal Mine Impacted Waterbodies in Wales\tCyrff Dŵr yr Effeithiwyd arnynt gan Fwyngloddiau Metel\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333236\tThe Metal Mine Impacted Waterbodies in Wales contains waterbodies and its lengths which have been affected by metal mine, either through direct or diffuse pollution. The data is collected to show the extent of downstream impacts of metal mine pollution. It should be noted that this dataset is collected in 2022. As remediation works continue, the number and length of waterbodies impacted may change.\tMetal Mine Impacted Waterbodies in Wales: The dataset is based on Failing Waterbodies Report 2020 by the Coal Authority. The report listed waterbodies and confidence level (high, medium or low) that impacts on them are caused by metal mines. This dataset is checked by the team from Metal Mine Programme to include waterbodies which are considered to be significantly impacted by metal mines.\t\tX:\\Industrial\\Mines\\Metal Mine Impacted Waterbodies in Wales.lyr\tDave Johnston\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-07-09\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/7628/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_mine_impacted_wbs\t2025-07-09T10:52:55.000Z\tAndrew Jeffery\t-\tPrivate record\n19053\tNRW_DS125330\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.674Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tVisitor safety zones\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333330\tThe NRW estate zoned according to Visitor Safety Group risk categories. Although these zones appear with hard boundaries, they are indicative only and care must be exercised if zooming in closely and zones are not sharply defined regarding the application of control measures. Public rights of way infoamtion is a NRW representation only. These maps should be used to compliment public safety risk assessments. Reliance is upon site managers to contact the data custodian to keep this data layer current. To edit or print a map of the zones, email the Data and Content Advisor (Recreation) to ensure the correct template and formatting are used.\tData copied/utilised from the following sources:  - NRW Forest Recreation Areas  - NRW Forest Recreation Points  - NRW Forest Legal boundary1  - Ex CCW Land holdings current  - Lettings to NRW current  - Scheduled Ancient Monuments  - PROW  - National Trails (Walked Line)  - Wales Coast Path  - Regional Trails  - Sustrans National Cycle Network  - NRW Forest Roads - Recreation Places to Go Promoted Routes   Base data has been quality assessed and edited with site managers to align with risk assessments.\tSee https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/are/aar/DataCont/GIS/Methods/Method%20to%20create%20VSG%20zones%20basemapping.docx?d\u003dwd016e6d3034a4e71b036eb3249d747d5 for the method used initially to create the basedata.\tX:\\Recreation and Access\\Recreation\\Visitor Safety Zones.lyr\tDavid Liddy, Specialist Advisor Recreational Safety, NRW Heather Crump, Data and Content Advisor (Recreation), NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-09-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:46:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19044\tNRW_DS125331\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.262Z\t551\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGenetic ancestry of introduced populations of sand lizard (Lacerta agilis) in Wales.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333331\tThe purpose of this investigation was to use a state-of-the-art whole genome sequencing approach to test the three most plausible hypotheses for the origin of the Gower population of Lacerta agilis. Assessing the origin and ancestry of Gower sand lizards is a fundamental step in assessing the need and desirability for conservation action to preserve the population, or, in the case of an introduced population of a non-native lineage, to consider the case for removal.  The date is presented in a report format with tables of DNA sequencing results presented in table and graphs\tThis investigation used 16 buccal swab DNA extractions which had been collected previously as part of a PhD investigation in Bangor University.   DNA extraction was carried out using a Qiagen DNeasy Blood and Tissue Kit. Buccal swabs were processed by cutting half of the spongy tip of the swab and treating it as ‘tissue’ when following the manufacturers protocol. DNA quantification was assessed using a Qubit 3 fluorometer with the dsDNA HS assay kit, following the manufacturer’s protocol. Library preparation, quality control and whole genome sequencing on an Illumina NovaSeq Instrument (2x 150 bp PE reads) to a target 8x coverage was carried out by Novagene Ltd.  Initial data processing involved trimming adapter sequences (synthetic DNA sequences that can occur as part of the sequencing process) from PE reads, requiring a single base overlap between adapter and read (setting “-O 1”), and removing short reads \u0026lt; 30 bp in length (setting -m 30), using the program Cutadapt v. 1.18 (Martin 2011). Overlapping PE read pairs were then identified and merged using the program FLASH v. 1.2.11 (Magoč \u0026amp; Salzberg 2011) using default parameter settings. The merged and unmerged PE reads were then mapped to the reference genome assembly of the sand lizard in separate analyses using the “mem” algorithm of the program bwa v. 0.7.17, using default parameter settings. The program samtools v. 1.3.1 (Li et al. 2009) was then used to filter out unmapped reads (setting “-F 4”), secondary mapped reads (setting “-F 256”) and reads with low mapping quality (setting “-q 30”). Samtools was then used to sort reads by their 5’ mapping position (command “sort”) and remove potential PCR duplicates. Finally, samtools was used to combine the merged and unmerged PE read data into a single file in bam format. Finally, samtools was used to combine the merged and unmerged PE read data into a single file in bam format for further analysis.  Further Genetic Analysis is described fully in report.\tOwens JB, Wilkinson JW, Wüster W, Barlow A, Papadopulos A. 2022. Genetic ancestry of introduced populations of sand lizard (Lacerta agilis) in Wales. NRW Evidence Report. Report No: 596, 30pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tData is held within science report 596 in NRW library - Data outputs associated with this project are archived in the Supercomputing Wales system “Hawk”, physically located at Cardiff University, with additional local back-up copies stored in the Environment Centre Wales, Bangor University on server–based storage at Natural Resources Wales.\tMatthew Ellis, Senior Advisor Casework Team NRW  Sam Dyer, Specialist advisor: Terrestrial habitats and species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-09-16\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:54:35.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19046\tNRW_DS125332\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.438Z\t639\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCurlew GPS Data: Curlew Movements\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333332\tTo improve our understanding of how Curlew use structurally different landscapes in the uplands and lowlands and under different land management regimes, in order to aid Natural Resources Wales’ work in agri-environmental policy design.  Data is collected and transferred to an Excel spreadsheet.\tThe data source is derived from Global Positioning System (GPS) tagged breeding curlew and covers variables such as GPS locations (long and latitude), tagged no., date, time of tagged bird, period tag functioning, battery condition and date and time of GPS location\tTaylor, R., C., Bowgen, K., Burton, N, H., K., \u0026amp; Franks, S, E. 2020. Understanding Welsh breeding Curlew: from local landscape movements through to population estimations and predictions.  NRW Evidence Report (No.485).\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/birds/Taxa/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbirds%2FTaxa%2FBreeding%20Waders%20%2D%20Curlew%2FCurlew%20GPS%20tagging%20contract\tPatrick Lindley - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-09-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-22T16:20:31.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19043\tNRW_DS125335\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.325Z\t243\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Operational Catchments Cycle 3 2022\tCylch 3 Dalgylchoedd Gweithredol y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333335\tWFD Operational Catchments’ is a polygon Shapefile dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD).  Operational catchments are the unit of geography that group river water bodies into smaller sub-catchments.    These units support the management and understanding of the river network at a local scale.\tWFD Operational Catchments have been delineated by using WFD River Waterbody Catchments [these were delineated through use of flow data and a digital terrain model run through a hydrological model] as “building blocks” that have been aggregated together within a GIS, ensuring that WFD rivers do not intersect boundaries.  This process was conducted by using expert judgement in consultation.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Operational Catchments Cycle 3 2022.lyr\tRob Blacklidge - Environment Reporting Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4464/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_operational_catchments_c3\t2025-05-12T10:40:01.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19040\tNRW_DS125344\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.277Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWye Valley Woodlands Project 1850 -1980 - distribution of woodland species vascular plants and bryophytes - Paper Survey records\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333434\tPaper survey records relating to the Wye Valley Woodland vascular species and bryophytes survey.  This is a collation of existing records and new records. The survey was done in two parts.  Records compiled by Stephanie Taylor 1979 and in 1978. 1980 NCC commissioned Ian Bolt to collect records for additional sites.\tOriginal files are arranged by Grid Square, with specific sites surveys in individual files.  Each file is labelled with a file number (those collected by Bolt from 1978 are also labelled with a file number and \u0027Bolt 1978-1980\u0027). It is not clear  if some site files are missing. An alphabetic index is held in the \u0027Wye Valley Project Folder - vascular plants compiled by Stephanie Tyler 1978\u0027 that lists the site and its corresponding site file number, along with a project report. A second report and general project information is held in the \\Bolt Report\\\" folder A third folder holds record cards. \"\tNo published report\tOff site storage Box CCW/SERBC/209\t?\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1980-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:42.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19045\tNRW_DS125346\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.325Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClwyd Pond Survey 1979 (paper survey records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333436\tThis is a paper format survey of pond species and habitat of the surrounding area which was undertaken during 1979. Photos of the ponds are also included.\t\t\tOff site archive storage, ref: CCW-NRM/R-53\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:22:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19039\tNRW_DS125350\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.238Z\t16\tInvalid\tEditable\tLimestone grassland survey 1987 (paper data records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333530\tPaper survey sheets of a 1987 survey of limestone grassland in grid reference area SJ and SH\t\t\toff-site storage, ref CCW/NRM/R-53\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:56:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19041\tNRW_DS125353\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.288Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDenbigh Grassland survey 1979/1980\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333533\tThis is a grassland survey of Denbigh area in paper format undertaken during 1979 and 1980\t\t\toff-site storage, ref: CCW/NRM/R-53\tData Services Team\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:58:52.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19042\tNRW_DS125355\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.283Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGwent Grassland Survey\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333535\tGwent Grassland Survey 1983\tThis survey was undertaken by Gwent County Council and Nature Conservancy Council during 1983\t\toff-site storage ref: CCW/SERBC/209 / CCW/NRM/R-53\tData Services Team\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19038\tNRW_DS125356\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.082Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGwent Meadows Survey 1987\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333536\tPaper data shetts of Gwent Meadows Survey 1987\t\t\toff-site storage ref: CCW/SERBC/209\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:17:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19037\tNRW_DS125358\t2024-05-28T13:00:56.081Z\t776\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Fish Monitoring Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333538\tFreshwater fish are used as an indicator of stock status, water and habitat quality, artificial obstacles to migration and river flow requirements due to specific life-cycle requirements. This dataset is made up of the species count and length values from the freshwater river fish surveys conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of fish in their natural environment for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  For the geospatial version of this data please see NRW_DS125743 \"KiEco: Freshwater Fish - Geospatial\".\tA strict methodology is applied when sampling for freshwater river fish. There is a core network of sites which are monitored on an annual basis, and also catchment-wide surveys on a six-year rolling programme. These sites are located in habitat types suitable for the respective fish species. In addition, sites may be sampled for investigative work, or on an ad hoc basis.   Sampling areas are commonly 50 metres long and the full width of the river and may embrace different flow types and substrate. Electric fishing is the most commonly used sampling method, although alternative methods are available for different strategies, such as netting and trapping.   Once a sample of fish is collected, they are immediately identified in the field, measured and counted and then released alive into the same section of river from where they were captured.    Freshwater fish survey data is available from 1974 to present. A caveat being that historical data are sporadic until 2001. This is because fish survey data collected before 2001 were often saved in local databases and not the national ones (KiEco and formerly NFPD).  Some information has be redacted to make the data openly available. This includes:  Third party data Data collected for ongoing investigations Cancelled or incomplete surveys\t\tWISKI KIECO\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Paul Hyatt\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-11-17-2025-01-16\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr3164\t2025-12-03T10:08:57.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19036\tNRW_DS125359\t2024-05-28T13:00:55.922Z\t312\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNature and Us / Natur am Ni Phase 1 Survey Responses\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333539\tThis data was collected as part of the Nature and Us national conversation between March and May 2022. As part of this compain, an online survey was available for any one in Wales to complete that sought to address five research outcomes: • what people identify as the critical issue for the future • if things went well, being optimistic but realistic, what would be a desirable  outcome • if things went wrong, what factors would people worry about • what people think about different scenarios of the future • what people are willing to change. The survey asked individuals about what concerned them about the environment, what action should be taken to help address climate change and the nature crises in Wales, what actions they would like to prioritise and what changes they would like to see. A total of 3,069 survey responses was received which are included in this dataset.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Governance Licence and available for public use, see Limitation on Public Access and Use Constraints\tThese findings are based upon the views of a non-representative sample of the public, collected through an online involvement process. The limitations of the approach are  acknowledged in the associated report listed in the additional Information Sources.\tNatural Resources Wales 2022 Nature and Us: Phase One Report. Natural Resources Wales 2022 Nature and Us: Phase One Public And Stakeholder Involvement Report\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/manbus/PMO/V2050/Phase%201%20outputs/EDITED_Nature%20and%20Us%20survey%20responses_English%20Edited%20Copy.xlsx?web\u003d1     (Secure DMS Location)\tSerinde van Wijk, Lead Specialist Advisor Nature and Us,\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-07-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-22T16:28:56.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19078\tNRW_DS125360\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.237Z\t625\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUnofficial Mountain Bike Trails (2021/22 Snapshot)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333630\tThis \u0027Wild MTB trails\u0027 data was collected by contractors and covers NRW managed land. The survey began with Strava Heat Maps and other user driven websites and was validated on site. It looks at the busiest trails and sites and covers most, but certainly not all of the forests or all of the trails. It represents the 2021/2 snapshot. Data includes trail routes (line data) and hazard points (point data) associated with a set of photographs stored on the DMS. Data regarding rider numbers was manually written down trail by trail. THIS DATA IS FOR INTERNAL USE ONLY.\tThis data is indicative only, was collected in 2021/22 and should be ground truthed if forest operations are about to take place in the area cover by the trails. Data captured via one of three methods: 1. Field survey using a Garmin Edge 25 GPS with accuracy +/- 3m. GPS was started and stopped at the beginning / end of each trail section 2. Section traced on an OS map layer due to capture error in the field. The resolution will be more coarse on these sections, but the overall accuracy will still be roughly  +/- 10m. 3. Field capture using a Garmin GPS device and imported into Google Earth. Each individual section then digitised separately Rider numbers were written down from Strava.\t\tZ:\\Processing\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Management\\1 - Geospatial\\SM\\Unofficial Mountain Bike Trails (2021_2 Snapshot) - Heather Crump\\Unofficial Mountain Bike Trails (2021_2 Snapshot).gdb\tHeather Crump - Advisor (2) Recreation Content\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-09-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19082\tNRW_DS125362\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.248Z\t529\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) At Risk and Failing Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 3 2022\tY Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr - Cyrff Dŵr Asid-sensitif sy’n Agored i Risg ac yn Methu, Cylch 3 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333632\tAt Risk Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 3 2022 and Failing Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 3 2022 are spatial (polygon shapefile) datasets containing attributes that have been collated as defined, for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD).   To implement the guidance on managing forests in order to minimise adverse impacts in acid-sensitive areas, two categories of acid sensitivity have been identified. These are defined as \u0027failing due to acidification\u0027 and \u0027at risk of failing due to acidification\u0027.    Water bodies identified as currently \u0027failing\u0027 have been classified through the Water Framework Directive monitoring as being of less than \u0027good\u0027 status and the cause of that failure has been attributed to acidification. The \u0027at risk of failing\u0027 water bodies have been identified, via the findings of the Welsh acidification risk assessment, as being at risk of failing to achieve \u0027good\u0027 status by 2027, due to acid deposition. The risk assessment combines chemical and biological data and an assessment of the environment\u0027s ability to neutralise the predicted acid pollutants.-Mae Cyrff Dŵr Sensitif i Asid Mewn Perygl Cylch 3 (2022) a Chyrff Dŵr Sensitif i Asid sy\u0027n Methu Cylch 3 (2022) yn setiau data gofodol (ffeil siâp polygon) sy\u0027n cynnwys priodoleddau sydd wedi\u0027u coladu fel y\u0027u diffinnir, ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr.  Er mwyn gweithredu canllawiau ar reoli coedwigoedd er mwyn lleihau effithiau andwyol mewn ardaloedd sy\u0027n sensitif i asid, clustnodwyd dau gategori o sensitifedd i asid. Cânt eu diffinio fel ‘methu oherwydd asideiddio’ a ‘risg o fethu oherwydd asideiddio’.  Mae cyrff dŵr a nodwyd fel rhai sy\u0027n \u0027methu\u0027 ar hyn o bryd wedi\u0027u dosbarthu trwy fonitro\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr fel rhai o statws is na \u0027da\u0027 ac mae achos y methiant hwnnw wedi\u0027i briodoli i asideiddio. Mae\u0027r cyrff dŵr sydd \u0027mewn perygl o fethu\u0027 wedi\u0027u nodi, trwy ganfyddiadau asesiad risg asideiddio Cymru, fel rhai sydd mewn perygl o fethu â chyflawni statws \u0027da\u0027 erbyn 2027, oherwydd dyddodiad asid. Mae\u0027r asesiad risg yn cyfuno data cemegol a biolegol ac asesiad o allu\u0027r amgylchedd i niwtraleiddio\u0027r llygryddion asid a ragwelir.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThese datasets identify the surface waterbodies, defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive, that are failing or at risk of failing due to acidification. These datasets cover the layers for Cycle 3 of the Water Framework Directive.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD At Risk Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 3 2022.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Failing Acid Sensitive Water Bodies Cycle 3 2022.lyr\tData Custodian: Rob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-08-05\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_wfd_cycle_3_classifications\t2025-09-04T10:50:17.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19083\tNRW_DS125363\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.056Z\t12\tValid\tEditable\tAncient Woodland Inventory for Wales 2004 (AWI2004)\tRhestr o Goetiroedd Hynafol 2004\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333633\tIn the 1980s, the Nature Conservancy Council instigated the Ancient Woodland Inventory (AWI). In Wales, the mapping baseline used was the 1st editions of the Ordnance Survey (OS) 1” maps published in the 1830s. The 1980s version of the inventory was compiled manually, using tracings from paper maps. It contained a significant number of boundary discrepancies. In the late 1990s, paper maps were converted to digital format. Work to reconcile boundaries with digital OS boundaries developed the first digital version of the inventory, made available in 2004. This version is referred to as the AWI 2004.    Also see the metadata entry for Ancient Woodland Inventory, dataset number NRW_DS116442 https://metadata.naturalresources.wales/geonetwork/srv/api/records/NRW_DS116442?language\u003deng\t1st edition Ordnance Survey (OS) 1 inch maps Ancient woodland inventory (1980s) Digital format 1st edition OS 1 inch maps\t\tX:\\Physical\\Forestry\\Ancient Woodland Inventory 2004.lyr Paper copies of the tracings are stored in Maes y Ffynnon, Floor 1 Post room (Jim Latham\u0027s cabinets)\tChris Tucker, Specialist Advisor: Technical Forestry (Resilience)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:24:13.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19085\tNRW_DS125370\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.379Z\t817\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDevelopment and implementation of a monitoring project for two rare spiders (Heliophanus dampfi and Lasaeola prona) on bogs within Dyfi SSSI\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333730\tThis project surveyed the arachnid (spider, harvestman and pseudoscorpion) fauna of five bogs within Dyfi SSSI - Cors Fochno, Ynys Greigiog, Tynohir, Gogarth Bog and Ynys-hir - during 2021 and early 2022.   The primary aim was to develop new monitoring protocols, based on vacuum sampling, for the two Nationally Rare spiders Heliophanus dampfi (IUCN Vulnerable) and Lasaeola prona (IUCN Endangered). No monitoring with the development of targets for these two species is known from other UK sites. Both species are qualifying features of Dyfi SSSI.\tThis survey was conducted between 6th June 2021 and 21st March 2022 across bog sites within Dyfi SSSI. Work was primarily undertaken at Cors Fochno, but visits were also made to Ynys Greigiog, Tynohir, Gogarth Bog and Ynys-hir.  At each site widespread sampling points were made across the area to gain a wider understanding of the arachnid faunas of these bogs: Cors Fochno (45 sample points; Ynys Greigiog (16 sample points); Tynohir (10 sample points); Gogarth Bog (5 sample points); Ynys-hir (11 sample points).    The survey was targeted towards finding Heliophanus dampfi and Lasaeola prona, ensuring sampling was undertaken in areas where previous records were known. However, effort was also made to record these species from new parts of the bogs to gain a better understanding of their site distribution.\tGallon, R.C. 2022. Development and implementation of a monitoring project for two rare spiders (Heliophanus dampfi and Lasaeola prona) on the raised bog – Cors Fochno in the Dyfi National Nature Reserve. NRW Evidence Report No. 648. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tStored on the WID in Recorder 6\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-11-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T15:31:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19075\tNRW_DS125371\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.238Z\t705\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status and distribution of Lilljeborg’s Whorl Snail Vertigo lilljeborgi at Pont Croesor on the Afon Glaslyn in 2022\tStatws a dosbarthiad Malwen Droellog Lilljeborg Vertigo lilljeborgi yn SoDdGA Glaslyn wrth Bont Croesor ar Afon Glaslyn yn 2022.\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333731\tResults of a monitoring survey in August 2022 for the Near Threatened Lilljeborg’s Whorl Snail Vertigo lilljeborgi within Glaslyn SSSI are presented. The area surveyed is north of Pont Croesor on the Afon Glaslyn. Judged to be in Unfavourable condition after the last survey in July 2008 (Willing, 2009), the present survey over two days in August repeated the previous survey. The wetland habitats remain visually very similar to those described in 2008 and weather and river level conditions were similar.  All 8 sites were revisited and sampled by visual search, beating vegetation, and collecting bulk samples of decaying plant matter. A 9th site was sampled near Site 7, the point at which V. lilljeborgi was last recorded in 2008. A suction sampler was used in 5 additional locations between sites on the east bank. A total of 12 adults of V. lilljeborgi was found at Site 7 and Site 8, confirming that the species survives on both sides of the Afon Glaslyn, although it is still apparently restricted to small areas. Two juvenile Vertigo were also found at Site 6. These could not be unambiguously identified as V. lilljeborgi, owing to the presence of another species, Vertigo antivertigo, nearby. All Vertigo specimens were found in large bulk samples that had been dried and sieved. No Vertigo were observed in the field, emphasising the importance of multiple sampling methods.   Using the Conservation Objective Lower Limit of 10 individuals set by Willing (2009), V. lilljeborgi is judged to be in Favourable condition within Glaslyn SSSI in August 2022. Nonetheless, the population only just exceeds the Lower Limit, and remains small and potentially vulnerable. Further sampling in the areas around Pont Croesor could help determine whether V. lilljeborgi is more widespread on the Afon Glaslyn floodplain.\tSampling conducted by by visual search, beating vegetation, and collecting bulk samples of decaying plant matter.\tRowson, B. 2022. The status and distribution of Lilljeborg’s Whorl Snail Vertigo lilljeborgi at Pont Croesor on the Afon Glaslyn in 2022. NRW Evidence Report No. 649. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tWelsh Invertebrate Database held in Recorder 6\tMike Howe - NRW Invertebrate Specialist\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T15:39:09.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19076\tNRW_DS125375\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.253Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOcean Literacy Survey of Wales 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333735\tThis data provides the evidence for the report “Survey on Ocean Literacy in Wales”. The survey was commissioned as part of the project “Understanding Ocean Literacy and Ocean Climate-related Behaviour Change in the UK” by Defra in collaboration with the Ocean Conservation Trust, Marine Scotland and Natural Resources Wales. The main aim of the survey was to better understand the extent and current level of Ocean Literacy in the England and Wales. The survey defines ‘Ocean Literacy’ as an understanding of the ocean’s influence on a person and their influence on the ocean. The survey explored different dimensions of ‘Ocean Literacy’: including information on public awareness, knowledge, attitudes, communication, activism and behaviours related to the marine environment. The survey also explored barriers to promoting Ocean Literacy in the population.\tThe research used an online panel method. Invitations were sent to members of online panels using BMG’s ‘panel blend’ approach which uses simultaneous survey invitations across multiple panels to spread fieldwork. The method is designed to improve the quality of outputs by hedging against the risk of selecting a single panel provider. Quotas were set on age based on the latest Office for National Statistics mid-year estimates as well as whether the area was classified as coastal or non-coastal (based on participant Settlement) to ensure the interviews completed were representative of the population on this basis. These were monitored closely and representative of the population on this basis.  These were monitored closely and any groups that were under-represented were sent further invitations and reminders in order that we achieved as close as possible to the original quotas set. In addition to this all respondents had to have been a permanent resident in Scotland for at least the last 5 years. Additional variables were also monitored during fieldwork to ensure a spread of responses were received: • Region • Gender • Ethnicity • IMD quintiles • ONS Coastal Communities sub-groups • Urban/rural\tAtkinson M., Wenman C., Zachary C. 2022. Ocean Literacy in Wales: Headline Findings Report. NRW Evidence Report. Report No: 633, 53 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\t\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Kathryn Hughes, Senior Advisor: Marine Area Statement   Metadata Point of Contact: Kathryn Hughes\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-05-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19081\tNRW_DS125376\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.239Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClwyd Woodland Survey\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333736\tClwyd woodlland survey of 1981 and preliminary survey of 1979\tCurrently paper data\t\toff-site storage box ref: CCW/NRW/R51 \u0026amp;52\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:10:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19080\tNRW_DS125377\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.183Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPembrokeshire Rare Plant Register proformas (Paper Records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333737\tCopies of handwritten Rare Plant Register Index proformas, information includes species name and locality, coverage, associates and conservation status.  Although all original information supplied to BSBI to collate, CCW and subsequently NRW retained a copy of the paper proformas for internal use.  Information was collected by NRW staff and therefore is not classed as third party data.\tCollected by CCW staff and Vice Country Recorders.  Originals and all species records were supplied to BSBI to manage and custodian.  NRW retains a copy of the proformas on site with the Rare Plant Specialists for reference for as long as Rare Plant specialists requires and then they will be disposed of.\t\tOff site storage CCW/WRPB/33  Some info to Julian Woodman?\tJulian Woodman, off-site storage reference number CCW/WRPB/33\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Botanical Society Of The British Isles (BSBI)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:21.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19077\tNRW_DS125378\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.252Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNCC Powys Local Woodland surveys up to 1976/77 (Paper records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333738\tPaper record cards - Woodland surveys for Powys - SO 05-08, 14-18, 25, 26/36, 27/37 and SN 97 grid squares\t\t\toff-site archive storage. box no: CCW/SERN/149\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19071\tNRW_DS125379\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.095Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForestry Comission Woodland Survey  - Gwent 1987 (paper records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333739\tIn 1987 NCC Woodland survey cards were completed for FC broad-leafed woodland likely to be semi-natural .  Area: Ebbw area of Gwent.\t\t\toff-site archive box FCW/FELL/5\topendata@naturalresources.gov.uk\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19084\tNRW_DS125380\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.143Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDerelict Land Habitat \u0026amp; Species Survey 1979 (paper data records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333830\tSurvey Site reports for SJ grid ref area and Anglesey\t\t\toff-site storage box ref CCW/NRM/R-52\tOpenData@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19074\tNRW_DS125381\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.214Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSouth Wales Open Water Survey 1981 (paper data records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333831\tSurvey of 94 permanent standing open water sites in Gwent 1981- areas greter than once acre in site, ST and SO grid references\t\t\tOffsite archive storage box no CCW/SERC/219\tOpendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       Opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19073\tNRW_DS125385\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.238Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNant Irfon NNR Plant Species Survey 1974 and 1991/2\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333835\tNant Irfon NNR Plant Species Quadrat Survey 1974 and 1991/2\t\t\toffisite storage -box number CCW/SERN/149\tNo know custodian,contact Opendata@naturalreosueceswales.gov.uk to discuss       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:43:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19072\tNRW_DS125389\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.090Z\t788\tValid\tEditable\tHolyhead Marina Ultrasound Trial Footage 2020-2021\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333839\tVideo (MP4) taken by a Natural Resources Wales owned Underwater Remotely Operated Vehicle (Chasing Innovation M2) at Holyhead marina. This was part of the baseline survey and monitoring of effectiveness of the ultrasound trial attempted in Holyhead marina to eradicate Didemnum vexillum. Video stills (JPG) were captured from the footage.     The data was collected between October 2020 and January 2021.\tThe UROV was deployed and operated from the surface by a suitably qualified pilot. Footage was taken in horizontal transect lines along the 6 operational Holyhead Marina pontoons at depths of 0.5 meters and 1.5 meters. The UROV was operated so that it captured footage of the marine growth attached to the pontoon surfaces and allowed for identification of areas where D. vex was present. At times the pontoon surfaces were obscured by heavy algal cover which affected the analysis of the video footage.\t\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Holyhead ultrasound trial footage 2020-2021\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor – Marine Ecology, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-11-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T10:13:53.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n19066\tNRW_DS125390\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.489Z\t599\tValid\tEditable\tHolyhead Harbour Carpet Sea Squirt Survey Data November 2020\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333930\tThis is the raw data from a dive survey of Holyhead harbour in November 2020, which focussed on mapping the distribution of the invasive non-native species Carpet seasquirt, Didemnum vexillum.  It contains;  • Video (MP4) and image files (JPG and ARW) of underwater habitats and species taken during the surveys  • Drone aerial images and videos of the current layout of the pontoons and mooring buoys (JPG and DNG and MP4).  • An Excel spreadsheet of sites surveyed    • Data recording forms – PDF copies of dive log sheets and recording forms    This data accompanies; Holt, R.H.F. 2018. Didemnum vexillum: survey of distribution and extent in Holyhead Harbour – November 2020. NRW Evidence report no. 538. 29 pp.\tVideo and image files – these are taken to represent the full range of different habitats in Holyhead Harbour and show a range of native and non-native species which occur in the marina as part of the survey. These videos and images were taken with underwater camera apparatus.    Drone aerial images and videos of the current layout of the pontoons and mooring buoys – taken with a drone piloted by CloudBase Productions.    An Excel spreadsheet of sites surveyed - ‘Holyhead D vex survey results 20201103-05’ – this is a summary of the locations of the images and sites surveyed which accompanies the report    Data recording forms – PDF copies of dive log sheets and recording forms – these were filled out by the dive contractors during the survey.\tThe data accompanies the following report; Holt, R.H.F. 2018. Didemnum vexillum: survey of distribution and extent in Holyhead Harbour – November 2020. NRW Evidence report no. 538. 29 pp.\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Holyhead Harbour D. vex survey data November 2020\tInternal Data Custodian(s):  Laura Grant, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-11-09\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T10:15:19.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n19069\tNRW_DS125391\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.791Z\t316\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey of the caddisfly Limnephilus tauricus at Cors Erddreiniog National Nature Reserve (NNR) in 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333931\tA survey of Cors Erddreiniog National Nature Reserve (NNR) was carried out in order to update knowledge of the status and distribution of the very rare caddisfly Limnephilus tauricus – a species known only from this reserve in Wales. The survey also aimed to locate the even rarer caddisfly Limnephilus pati, which hasn’t been previously recorded at the reserve (or in Wales) but is present in similar habitats within the same flight period.\tThe survey was conducted by placing light traps at selected locations across the reserve on nine separate occasions between 16th June and 12th August 2022. Since these light traps would also attract moths, a survey of macro- and micro-moths was also conducted at the reserve.\tAron, C.E. \u0026amp; Palin, S. 2022. Survey of the caddisfly Limnephilus tauricus at Cors Erddreiniog NNR in 2022. Natur am Byth! Evidence Report No. 003. 26pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID) – Natur Am Byth Surveys.\tMike Howe\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-12-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T15:40:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19067\tNRW_DS125392\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.645Z\t9\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Fixed Point Botanical Monitoring Survey Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333932\tData from the fixed point quadrats to monitoring the effects of SoLIFE project actions\tData collected from field survey. Full methods and limitations are reported in SoLIFE project reports.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Fixed Point Quadrat Boundaries.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Fixed Point Quadrat Centres.lyr\tToni Henwood\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-08-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:33:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19079\tNRW_DS125393\t2024-05-28T13:01:44.237Z\t583\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Sand Dune Soil Baseline Monitoring 2023\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333933\tThis survey focuses on baseline, pre-intervention monitoring in relation to two of the planned intervention types: notch creation and dune slack re-profiling. The monitoring covers six sites in Wales: Tywyn Aberffraw, Newborough, and Morfa Harlech in north Wales, and Kenfig, Merthyr Mawr and Pembrey Burrows in south Wales.  At each site, baseline monitoring was set up for the planned interventions and for control locations where no intervention will take place, in order to clearly show the effect of the restoration measures.  For both intervention types, soil profiles were taken at each monitoring location. Soil profiles were recorded to a depth of 50 cm and soil samples were taken to a depth of 20 cm. In addition, the baseline monitoring for the notches involved setting up sand-rain collectors at transects away from the proposed notch and control locations, downwind in the direction of the prevailing winds. Each transect started at 50 m distance from the inland end of the notch, and then at two further points (100 and 200 m). Soil samples were analysed for bulk density, electrical conductivity, pH, organic matter content by loss on ignition (LOI), calcium carbonate content, carbon (C) and nitrogen (N) content.\tRaw data collected in the field and subsequent laboratory analysis completed following established protocols, as detailed in full in the accompanying report.\tHolder, A.J., Fitch, A., Robinson, I., Pinder, A., Brentegani, M., Allender, S., van der Schatte Olivier, A. \u0026amp; Jones, L. (2021). Sands of LIFE Sand dune ecological and physical monitoring: Soils report. NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 456, [Enter number of printed pages here]pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tX:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Soils Slacks.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life\\Sand of LIFE Soils Transects.lyr\tToni Henwood\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-12-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:39:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19068\tNRW_DS125394\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.670Z\t743\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonk\u0027s Trod and Hengae Drone Survey 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333934\tGIS layers from Monks Trod and Hengae survey areas: • full site orthomosaic • tiled orthomosaic • full site digital surface model • tiled digital surface model  Used to survey the following: Ascertaining a baseline status of the highway. Seeing how far the traffic \"drifts\" from the highway.\tCollected from drone flights during the middle of September 2022\t\tZ:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\GIS \u0026amp; Spatial Data\\Drone data\tMichael Sneade - Advisor 2, Natura 2000 Sustainable Management\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-12-02\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:14:01.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19070\tNRW_DS125395\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.852Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Grassland survey 1986 Mid \u0026amp; South Glamorgan (paper data records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333935\tPhase 2 Grassland survey of SN, SO, SS and ST  Mid\u0026amp;South Glamorgan\t\t\toff-site archive storage ref: CCWRF43501 \u0026amp; CCWRF43502\tData Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:56.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19061\tNRW_DS125396\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.299Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGwent Level Plant Species Survey 1991 (Paper Records )\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333936\t\t\t\toff-site paper storage CCW/SERC/1463  - RF24454\tnot known stored in off-site archive box ref: CCW/SERC/143 (RF24454)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19063\tNRW_DS125397\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.398Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRare Plant Species survey of Wales 1987-88 (paper records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333937\tSurveys of South Wales including Dyfed/Powys and West Glamorgan/Llanelli. Reference copies of species found by 10km grid squares, not known where originals are kept\t\t\tOff site storage box ref:  CCW/WRAB/025\tstored in offsite archive box ref: CCW/WRAB/025,  internal contact  Data Services team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19062\tNRW_DS125398\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.408Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPowys Woodlands Old Maps and woodland survey (Paper records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333938\tpaper copies of old maps of Powys Woodlands and woodland species, no year mentioned\t\t\toff-site archive ref CCW/SERN/30\tOpendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19060\tNRW_DS125399\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.234Z\t10\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecies \u0026amp; Habitats survey data by grid square - paper data records\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235333939\tThis is a collation of mixed habitat \u0026amp; species data (Heath/Mires, Grasslands,  Mixed Habitats , Heath/Upland, Wetlands and Open Water  Bird, Bats, Invertebrates, lower plants, Woodlands etc.) for the following grid squares: SH, SJ, SN, SS,ST, SO\tData is held in paper format and the collection has been designed to preserve useful data from legacy files that have reached retention.\t\toff-site archive storage ref:  CCW/GRID/SH/1, CCW/GRID/SH/2,  CCW/GRID/SJ/1,  CCW/GRID/SJ/2,  CCW/GRID/SN/1,  CCW/GRID/SN/2,  CCW/GRID/SN/3,  CCW/GRID/SN/4,  CCW/GRID/SN/5,  CCW/GRID/SN/6,  CCW/WRAB/027- is also SN CCC/ tbc by Jill- is also SN CCW/GRID/SO/1,  CCW/GRID/SO/2,  CCW/GRID/SO/3,  CCW/GRID/SS/1,  CCW/GRID/ST/1\tData Services Team opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       Opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T09:50:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19059\tNRW_DS125400\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.177Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHabitat Maps for designated sites (NNRs \u0026amp; SSSIs) - paper records\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343030\tThis is a collation of habitat maps and a mixture of copies and originals from 1970s\tUnknown origin, needs to be assessed by Habitat Specialist\t\toff-site storage references: fill in once received from JILL Records team\topendata@naturalresources.gov.uk       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:27.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19065\tNRW_DS125401\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.387Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHeathland data for Pembrokeshire (paper records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343031\tThese are the various paper data survey records: 1. 1987/88 Skomer general vegetation and seacliff communities data 2. 1979 Pembrokeshire Heathland survey field notes (species recording cards) 3. 2 files of collated and original information related to all Heathland sites in Pembrokeshire file 1 all sites, file 2 Dowrog Common detailed file\t\t\toff-site archive storage ref: CCW/WRPB/005\topendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19064\tNRW_DS125404\t2024-05-28T13:01:43.409Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tIthon river valley survey (paper records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343034\tThis is a paper record survey of 1980 of the river bank and aquatic habitat\t\t\toff-site archive box ref: CCW/SERNB/41\topendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:43:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19058\tNRW_DS125407\t2024-05-28T13:01:42.975Z\t336\tValid\tEditable\tElan Valley Drone Survey 2022\tArolwg Drôn Dyffryn Elan 2022\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343037\tAir Survey of 10 upland peatland sites in Mid Wales flown in January and March 2022. The 10 sites were flown between the 12th January and 25th March 2022.   All sites were surveyed using an RGB vertical camera, then the data processed to produce digital surface models (DSM) and orthomosaics. The DSMs were processed to generate catchment, flow and contour vector data sets to assist with the peatbog restoration planning.   Drone details - eBee RTK unmanned aircraft system (UAS) by Sensefly. The eBee is an electric fixed wing Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV), weight 1.1 kg, with a maximum speed of 110 km/h and a maximum wind capability of 12 m/s.   Camera - Sensefly S.O.D.A. RGB camera: 1″ Sensor, RGB lens F/2.8-11, 10.6 mm, resolution 5,472 x 3,648 px (3:2), Exposure compensation ±2.0, Global Shutter 1/30 – 1/2000s, Auto White balance, ISO range 125-6400.   Flight path - GIS ESRI Shape or kml files provided by the client were loaded into the UAS to provide survey area coverage parameters.   Data resolution - The UAV is programmed to fly the areas at a height that obtains an image resolution of between 3-4 cm/pixel, with an image overlap of 75%. The exact resolution is dependent on the ruggedness of the terrain; the resolution is set by the lowest resolution area of the orthomosaic.   Data capture - Once the orthomosaic sand DSMs had been created, the DSMs were processed to generate catchments, flow lines and 0.5m interval contour layers. This was carried out using QGIS and GRASS. The orthomosaics were then used to manually digitise peat and hag patchwork areas, hags, bare peat/water.   The 10 sites fell into the following categories:  • No visible peat bog areas or stabilised hags and gulleys.  • Signs of historic peatbog degradation, but currently stable/vegetated – vegetated hags and gulleys  • Visibly eroding peatbog areas – exposed bare peat, visible eroding hags and gulleys\tImage processing procedures - The GNSS positions of the images were recorded during the flight. Post-Processed Kinematic (PPK) processing of the image positions was employed to bring the image accuracy to within 10 cm. This was carried out using the UAV control software (eMotion3), with the RINEX correction data from Leica Geosystems base station MACY at: Bro Ddyfi Leisure Centre, Plas Dr, Machynlleth SY20 8ER. The RINEX correction data was provided by HxGN SmartNet. Transformation Conversion from WGS 84, ETRS89 Geodetic to OSGB36/British National Grid was performed using: Ordnance Survey, Grid InQuestII software. The orthomosaic and Digital Surface Model (DSM) were created with Pix4D photogrammetry software, using the provided default settings.   Accuracy - The accuracy of the resulting orthomosaic and digital surface models is dependent on a range of factors, including:  • The height of the camera above the terrain – this varies depending on the ruggedness of the terrain.  • The contrast and detail in the terrain – featureless and uniform vegetation provides fewer opportunities to match points between photographs resulting in lower accuracy • Light levels – imagery flown on a bright day will have sharper images, resulting in more accurate pattern matching and thus registration  • The GPS satellite configuration at the time of the survey  • The proximity of the survey area to the GPS base stations used for applying positional corrections As a result, the accuracy will vary between the sites and within each site. The surveys were generally flown in moderate to good light conditions with the GPS positions post-processed using Leica SmartNet corrections and map projection transformations carried out using Ordnance Survey OSTN15 corrections, so the absolute positional accuracy should be better than 10cm in plan and 15cm in height.\tThe full data report also provides additional site details on the flight path and resolution achieved for each survey. This report should accompany the data. Elan_DroneSurvey_2022_DataReport.pdf    Aerial survey: Elan Valley  Air survey of 10 upland peatland sites   March 2022 Exegenesis Spatial Data Management\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\Elan Valley - for combined .lyr  files (raw data: Z:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\GIS \u0026amp; Spatial Data\\From Y drive\\Peatlands\\Elan Valley)\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Esther Clews, Specialist Advisor, Data and GIS – National Peatland Action Programme\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-12-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-07T15:05:49.730Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19057\tNRW_DS125432\t2024-05-28T13:01:42.910Z\t311\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDynamic Dunescapes National Vegetation Classification Survey Date\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343332\tData from a National Vegetation Classification survey of 10 sand dune sites including shapefiles of NVC polygons, vegetation mosaics, target notes, quadrats, photograph locations and overall survey boundary. Data collected in 2020 and 2021 based on field survey of entire sites with field maps digitised to OS Mastermap and aerial photographs.\tData collected from field survey. Full methods and limitations are reported in Dynamic Dunescapes NVC project report\tNRW Evidence Report No. 659\tPlanned for X:Drive with suggested filepath but needs confirming  X:\\Biological\\Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Boundaries.lyr  X:\\Biological\\ Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Mospoly.lyr  X:\\Biological\\ Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Photographs.lyr  X:\\Biological\\ Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Polygons.lyr  X:\\Biological\\ Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Quadrats.lyr  X:\\Biological\\ Dynamic Dunescapes\\Dynamic Dunescapes NVC Target Notes.lyr\tJulie Creer - Lead Specialist Advisor: Coastal Habitats\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-12-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19056\tNRW_DS125436\t2024-05-28T13:01:42.865Z\t173\tValid\tEditable\tTerrestrial Priority Habitat Surveys\tArolygon Cynefinoedd Daearol â Blaenoriaeth\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343336\tThis dataset contains Priority Habitat Surveys of individual sites (not Wales-wide coverage), focusing on terrestrial habitats listed under Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2016, known as Priority Habitats. It consists of detailed surveys of plant communities in mixed habitat context at a coarser scale than Phase 2 (National Vegetation Classification) habitat surveys.    The primary objective of these surveys is to identify and map areas of Priority Habitat of nature conservation importance, which can then feed into existing Wales-wide Priority Habitat extent and distribution data. The survey data can be used to identify areas of important habitat to inform schemes and projects such as woodland creation schemes, developments, or agricultural improvement, and can inform conservation management and restoration work to improve habitat condition and connectivity, and enhance biodiversity.    The dataset is not the product of a co-ordinated Survey Programme like the Lowland Peatland and Lowland Grassland Surveys of Wales, but is instead designed to be a single place to store ad-hoc site surveys of mixed Priority Habitat, under a common data structure, so they can be easily analysed and shared. Only surveys that have been extensively validated and verified by NRW vegetation and cartographic specialists will be added to the dataset.    This dataset is not to be confused with layer files derived from the ‘Environment (Wales) Act Section 7 Terrestrial Habitats of Principal Importance’ dataset, which is largely based on the Phase 1 Terrestrial Habitat Survey (1987-1997). These layer files are split into Wales-wide coverage for individual habitats, for example ‘Priority Habitat Blanket Bog.lyr.\u0027 See metadata NRW_DS122418 for more information.    Habitat composition may change over time, for example due to successional trends, land management or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type or species in the digital data is therefore no guarantee of it still being extant on the ground.  Individual site reports for most sites surveyed are also available upon request.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys Arolygon Cynefinoedd â Blaenoriaeth o safleoedd unigol (nid yw’n gorchuddio Cymru gyfan), gan ganolbwyntio ar gynefinoedd daearol a restrir o dan Adran 7 Deddf yr Amgylchedd (Cymru) 2016, a elwir yn Gynefinoedd â Blaenoriaeth. Mae\u0027n cynnwys arolygon manwl o gymunedau planhigion yng nghyd-destun cynefinoedd cymysg ar raddfa fwy bras nag arolygon cynefinoedd Cam 2 (Dosbarthiad Llystyfiant Cenedlaethol).  Ni ddylid cymysgu\u0027r set ddata hon â ffeiliau haen sy\u0027n deillio o set ddata \u0027Deddf yr Amgylchedd (Cymru) Adran 7 Cynefinoedd Daearol Pwysig Iawn\u0027, sy\u0027n seiliedig i raddau helaeth ar arolygon Arolwg Cynefinoedd Daearol (1987-1997) Cam 1 ac arolygon Dosbarthiad Llystyfiant Cenedlaethol Cam 2.  Gall cyfansoddiad cynefin newid dros amser, er enghraifft oherwydd tueddiadau olynol, rheoli tir neu hyd yn oed ddatblygiadau wedi\u0027u cynllunio. Felly, nid yw cofnod o fath neu rywogaeth o lystyfiant yn y data digidol yn gwarantu ei fod yn dal i fodoli ar y ddaear. Mae adroddiadau safleoedd unigol ar gyfer y rhan fwyaf o safleoedd a arolygwyd hefyd ar gael ar gais.\tThis dataset contains spatial sites data (ArcGIS Feature class) and non-spatial Excel spreadsheet, photographs and accompanying reports.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Priority Habitat Site Surveys (Terrestrial)\\Priority Habitat Mapping Vegetation.lyr X:\\Biological\\Priority Habitat Site Surveys (Terrestrial)\\Priority Habitat Mapping Mospoly.lyr X:\\Biological\\Priority Habitat Site Surveys (Terrestrial)\\Priority Habitat Mapping Notes.lyr X:\\Biological\\Priority Habitat Site Surveys (Terrestrial)\\Priority Habitat Mapping photos.lyr X:\\Biological\\Priority Habitat Site Surveys (Terrestrial)\\Priority Habitat Mapping Quadrats.lyr X:\\Biological\\Priority Habitat Site Surveys (Terrestrial)\\Priority Habitat Mapping Sites.lyr  Associated survey reports will be saved in different places on the DMS depending on the location or type of site surveyed\tKathryn Birch, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Habitats\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2023-01-12-2025-10-30\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-03T13:02:43.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19108\tNRW_DS125437\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.957Z\t716\tValid\tEditable\tInternal Drainage Districts (IDD)\tArdaloedd Draenio Mewnol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343337\tDrainage districts are typically found in low-Iying land where boundaries are determined by physical, not, political attributes. Districts vary in nature and size, from the mainly agricultural (such as Conwy, Llanfrothen and Towyn) to the semi-urban (such as the Gwent Levels).    Drainage districts are at risk from various sources of flooding. Without flood risk and water level management, drainage districts would be unsuitable to live in and would not protect and provide for the variety of agricultural land, utilities and transport networks that have developed within their boundaries.     This dataset shows the location of the boundaries of IDD\u0027s within Wales including the maintained watercourses, tidal flaps, pumping stations and for for Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge Levels \u0026amp; Lower Wye and Powysland it also shows locations of sluices.     Current Drainage Districts as of 2022:   Afon Ganol Ardudwy Marsh,  Borth Bog,   Caldicot \u0026amp; Wentlooge Levels \u0026amp; Lower Wye (Gwent Levels),  Dysynni Valley,    Glaslyn - Pensyflog,    Harlech \u0026amp; Maentwrog,    Llanfrothen,    Malltraeth Marsh,    Mawddach \u0026amp; Wnion,    Powysland,    River Conwy,    Towyn.-Mae rhanbarthau draenio ar dir isel fel arfer, ac mae eu ffiniau wedi’u pennu gan nodweddion ffisegol, yn hytrach na gwleidyddol. Mae’r rhanbarthau’n amrywio o ran natur a maint. Mae rhai’n rhanbarthau amaethyddol gan mwyaf (fel Conwy, Llanfrothen a Thywyn) a rhai’n lled drefol (fel Gwastadeddau Gwent).  Mae rhanbarthau draenio yn wynebu perygl llifogydd o ffynonellau amrywiol. Heb weithgareddau i reoli lefel y dŵr a pherygl llifogydd, ni fyddai rhanbarthau draenio yn lleoedd addas i fyw ynddyn nhw. Ni fydden nhw chwaith yn diogelu nac yn darparu ar gyfer y tir amaethyddol, y cyfleustodau a’r rhwydweithiau trafnidiaeth amrywiol sydd wedi datblygu o fewn eu ffiniau.  Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn dangos lleoliad ffiniau Ardaloedd Draenio Mewnol yng Nghymru gan gynnwys y cyrsiau dŵr a gynhelir, fflapiau llanw, gorsafoedd pwmpio ac, ar gyfer Gwastadeddau Cil-y-coed a Gwynllŵg a Phowystir, mae hefyd yn dangos lleoliadau llifddorau.  Ardaloedd Draenio Cyfredol 2022:  Afon Ganol Cors Ardudwy Cors Borth Lefelau Cil-y-Coed a Gwynllŵg a Gwy Isaf (Lefelau Gwent) Dyffryn Dysynni Glaslyn - Pensyflog Harlech a Maentwrog Llanfrothen Cors Malltraeth Mawddach ac Wnion Powysland Afon Conwy Towyn  Datganiad priodoli: Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tNorth IDDs – Digitised from old paper maps dating back to the Gwynedd River Authority days. South IDDs - Digitised from old paper maps\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\Caldicot Wentlooge\\IDD CWL Tidal Flaps.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Watercourses.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Pumping Stations.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Boundaries.lyr ,  X:\\Resource Management\\Internal Drainage Boards\\IDD Sluices.lyr ,\tJames West, Team Leader, Integrated Engineering NW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-01-17\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/4185/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_internal_drainage_district\t2025-05-09T16:06:49.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19112\tNRW_DS125446\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.980Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE (SoLIFE) Hydrology and Dipwells\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343436\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19110\tNRW_DS125448\t2024-05-28T13:02:41.037Z\t419\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey and assessment of the saproxylic invertebrate fauna of Gregynog National Nature Reserve, Powys - 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343438\tThe Gregynog Hall Estate comprises about 300ha of ancient wood pasture habitats within the old county of Montgomeryshire. The 15ha of Great Wood were designated a Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) for its rich epiphytic lichen communities in 1978 although it later proved to also have significant interest for invertebrates and especially those species associated with the decaying wood (saproxylic) habitats provided by the abundance of veteran trees. The SSSI was extended in 2011 and the whole estate declared a National Nature Reserve in 2012.   A survey of the saproxylic invertebrates of the wider NNR was commissioned by Natural Resources Wales in 2022. This is the first baseline survey of the fauna, previous research comprising exploratory surveys only. The aims of the survey were to determine the current condition of the saproxylic invertebrate fauna and to identify any threats.\tThe baseline standard for saproxylic invertebrate surveys is for three visits to be carried out across the field season, targeting late spring, high summer and autumn. Given the large extent of the NNR and the desire to operate some flight interception trapping in parallel with field survey each visit involved more than one day.  The first exploratory visit was carried out on 23rd April 2022 and ten flight interception traps positioned on 24th April. The second visit took place on 22nd May and two additional flight traps were positioned. The third visit covered 7th to 9th June and targeted hawthorn blossom as well as emptying and maintaining the traps. The fourth visit was over 11th and 13th August. The final visit was on 18th and 19th November when the traps were removed.    The main techniques used were as follows:  • visual inspection of vegetation and accessible wood-decay for invertebrates;  • spot-sweeping, i.e. targeting observed invertebrates for netting; • general sweep-netting, i.e. un-targeted;  • hand search, e.g. by searching amongst woody debris; • beating foliage and branches of trees, shrubs and dwarf shrubs using beating tray;  • beating blossom on flowering shrubs.  Wherever possible specimens encountered were identified in the field, retaining voucher specimens of the more critical species. Some larvae found amongst decaying wood were retained for rearing through to the more identifiable adult insect. Other specimens were taken away for inspection under microscope.\tAlexander, K.N.A. 2023. A survey and assessment of the saproxylic invertebrate fauna of Gregynog National Nature Reserve, Powys in 2022. NRW Evidence Report No. 671. 61pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID) in Recorder 6\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T15:42:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19104\tNRW_DS125451\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.796Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForester Management Features\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343531\tThese datasets show the location of features within the national forest estate that are capture and stored for forestry management purposes such as for planning, safety and access. This record covers the following features within the forest estate boundaries:  Crossing points - these will generally be electricity or telecom pylons. Cattle Grid, Gates and Barrier Areas of open water - these will generally be fairly natural and permanent in character and not be created by dams Walls water courses - these will be natural water courses which will usually be permanent.\tThese datasets are generally captured on an adhoc basis by operational staff on site visits using paper mapping or GPS. This data is store in NRWs forester application.  These datasets are likely to be incomplete and/or out of date  and should not be relied upon as a source of accurate or up-to-date information. Please use additional sources to verify these datasets.\t\tForrester database\tGeraint Morgan - Specialist Advisor, Forester System\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T15:43:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19101\tNRW_DS125452\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.888Z\t1214\tValid\tEditable\tAuthorised Landfill Sites Points\tPwyntiau Safleoedd Tirlenwi Awdurdodedig\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343532\tThis dataset shows the location of landfill sites which are currently authorised by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under The Environmental Permitting (England and Wales) Regulations 2010. This data is a subset of information from NRWs permiting database. The dataset includes waste and installation permits that fall into the following category’s.   • A1: Co-Disposal Landfill Site;  • A2: Other Landfill Site taking Special Waste;  • A4: Household, Commercial and Industrial Waste Landfill;  • A5: Landfill taking Non-Biodegradable Wastes;  • A6: Landfill taking other wastes;  • A7: Industrial Waste Landfill (Factory cartilage);  • 5.2 A(1) a): Waste Landfilling; \u0026gt;10T/D with capacity\u0026gt;25,000T excluding inert waste;  • 5.2 A(1) b): Waste Landfilling;  • Any other Landfill to which the 2002 regulations apply;  • L04: Non Hazardous Landfill;  • and L05: Inert Landfill.-Mae’r set ddata hon yn dangos lleoliad safleoedd tirlenwi sydd wedi’u hawdurdodi ar hyn o bryd gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) o dan Reoliadau Trwyddedu Amgylcheddol (Cymru a Lloegr) 2010. Mae\u0027r data hyn yn is-set o wybodaeth o gronfa ddata trwyddedau CNC. Mae’r set ddata’n cynnwys trwyddedau gwastraff a gweithfeydd sy’n perthyn i’r categorïau canlynol.   • A1: Safle tirlenwi cydwaredu • A2: Safle tirlenwi arall sy\u0027n derbyn gwastraff arbennig • A4: Safle tirlenwi sy’n derbyn gwastraff cartref, masnachol a diwydiannol • A5: Safle tirlenwi sy\u0027n derbyn gwastraff nad yw\u0027n fioddiraddadwy • A6: Safle tirlenwi sy’n derbyn gwastraff arall • A7: Safle tirlenwi sy’n derbyn gwastraff diwydiannol (cartilag ffatri) • 5.2 A(1) a): Tirlenwi gwastraff: \u0026gt; 10T/D gyda chynhwysedd \u0026gt; 25,000T ac eithrio gwastraff anadweithiol • 5.2 A(1) b): Tirlenwi gwastraff • Unrhyw safle tirlenwi arall y mae rheoliadau 2002 yn berthnasol iddo • L04: Safle tirlenwi ar gyfer gwastraff nad yw’n beryglus • L05: Safle tirlenwi ar gyfer gwastraff anadweithiol  Mae\u0027n bwysig nodi nad yw safle o reidrwydd yn derbyn gwastraff oherwydd ei fod yn safle sydd wedi’i awdurdodi. Dim ond pan fydd statws y drwydded gwastraff yn newid i naill ai ‘wedi dod i ben’, ‘wedi’i dirymu’ neu ‘wedi’i hildio’ y caiff safleoedd tirlenwi eu tynnu o\u0027r set ddata a\u0027u hychwanegu at set ddata safleoedd tirlenwi hanesyddol.\tIt is important to note that because a site is authorised, it does not necessarily mean it is accepting waste. Landfill sites are only removed from the dataset and added to the Historic Landfill site dataset when the waste licence status changes to either Expired, Revoked or Surrendered.\t\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Waste\\Historic Landfill Sites Points.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Gareth E Lewis - SUW Advisor Wales, Knowledge Strategy and Planning\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/6939/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_authorised_landfill_sites_pnt\t2026-01-12T13:42:43.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19096\tNRW_DS125453\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.535Z\t1589\tValid\tEditable\tHistoric Landfill Sites points\tPwyntiau Safleoedd Tirlenwi Hanesyddol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343533\tThis dataset shows the point location historic landfill sites that are held in NRWs permitting database and have been issues under the EPR. These are landfill sites which have been taken off the Authorised Landfill Sites points dataset when the waste licence status changes to either:  - Licence expired: Some licences issued under the Control of Pollution Act 1974 were time limited and expired on the date specified in the licence.  - Licence Revoked: Whereby the licence has been entirely revoked and is no longer in force; or  - Licence Surrendered: Operator has successfully surrendered the licence which is no longer in force.-Mae’r set ddata hon yn dangos lleoliad pwynt safleoedd tirlenwi hanesyddol a gedwir yng nghronfa ddata trwyddedau CNC ac sydd wedi’u cyhoeddi o dan y Rheoliadau Trwyddedu Amgylcheddol. Safleoedd tirlenwi yw’r rhain sydd wedi’u tynnu oddi ar y set ddata pwyntiau safleoedd tirlenwi awdurdodedig pan fydd statws y drwydded gwastraff yn newid i un o’r canlynol:  – Trwydded wedi dod i ben: Roedd terfyn amser ar rai trwyddedau a roddwyd o dan Ddeddf Rheoli Llygredd 1974 a daethant i ben ar y dyddiad a nodir yn y drwydded  – Trwydded wedi\u0027i dirymu: Pan fo’r drwydded wedi’i dirymu’n llwyr ac nad yw bellach mewn grym  – Drwydded wedi\u0027i hildio: Mae’r gweithredwr wedi llwyddo i ildio\u0027r drwydded, nad yw bellach mewn grym  Mae\u0027r data hyn yn is-set o wybodaeth o gronfa ddata trwyddedau CNC. Mae’r set ddata’n cynnwys trwyddedau gwastraff a gweithfeydd sy’n perthyn i’r categorïau canlynol.  A1: Safle tirlenwi cydwaredu A2: Safle tirlenwi arall sy\u0027n derbyn gwastraff arbennig A4: Safle tirlenwi sy’n derbyn gwastraff cartref, masnachol a diwydiannol A5: Safle tirlenwi sy\u0027n derbyn gwastraff nad yw\u0027n fioddiraddadwy A6: Safle tirlenwi sy’n derbyn gwastraff arall A7: Safle tirlenwi sy’n derbyn gwastraff diwydiannol (cartilag ffatri) 5.2 A(1) a): Tirlenwi gwastraff: \u0026gt; 10T/D gyda chynhwysedd \u0026gt; 25,000T ac eithrio gwastraff anadweithiol 5.2 A(1) b): Tirlenwi gwastraff Unrhyw safle tirlenwi arall y mae rheoliadau 2002 yn berthnasol iddo L04: Safle tirlenwi ar gyfer gwastraff nad yw’n beryglus L05: Safle tirlenwi ar gyfer gwastraff anadweithiol RHYBUDD AM YR WYBODAETH: Mae’r set ddata hon yn cynnwys trwyddedau a roddwyd o dan y Rheoliadau Trwyddedu Amgylcheddol yn unig. Cyn 1996, roedd yr holl ddata safleoedd tirlenwi yn cael eu rheoli a’u coladu gan awdurdodau lleol ac nid yw\u0027r data hyn wedi\u0027i gynnwys yn y set ddata hon. Dylid defnyddio\u0027r set ddata hon ar y cyd â set ddata ffiniau safleoedd tirlenwi hanesyddol.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThis data is a subset of information from NRWs permiting database. The dataset includes waste and installation permits that fall into the following category’s.  A1: Co-Disposal Landfill Site; A2: Other Landfill Site taking Special Waste; A4: Household, Commercial and Industrial Waste Landfill; A5: Landfill taking Non-Biodegradable Wastes; A6: Landfill taking other wastes; A7: Industrial Waste Landfill (Factory cartilage); 5.2 A(1) a): Waste Landfilling; \u0026gt;10T/D with capacity\u0026gt;25,000T excluding inert waste; 5.2 A(1) b): Waste Landfilling; Any other Landfill to which the 2002 regulations apply; L04: Non Hazardous Landfill; and L05: Inert Landfill.  INFORMATION WARNING: This dataset only includes permits issued under the EPR. Before 1996 all landfill sites data was managed and collated by Local Authorities and this data is not included in this dataset. This dataset should be used in conjunction with the Historic Landfill Sites boundaries dataset.\t\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Waste\\Historic Landfill Sites Points.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Gareth E Lewis - SUW Advisor Wales, Knowledge Strategy and Planning  Also could contact: Tyrone (Ty) Ward - PPC/RSR Officer 1, PPC Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/6940/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_historic_landfill_sites_pnt\t2026-01-12T13:52:25.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19103\tNRW_DS125454\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.799Z\t96\tValid\tEditable\tHabitat Map of Wales resurvey pilot – field survey\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343534\tPhase 1 survey of 30 tetrads in Carmarthenshire and Neath Port Talbot. Digitised in the field using Qfield (android software a data capture application linked to QGIS).     Data collected as part of the Pilot for Habitat Map of Wales resurvey project.\tData was captured “in the field” using “Qfield” on a Samsung Galaxy active Tab3 tablet running Android. Polygons were digitised by using OS Master Map (OSMM) as a base layer into which Phase 1 survey codes were entered. Data was entered at a whole polygon level with some OSMM polygons split where habitats varied within a polygon. A target note and scattered vegetation layer were also produced, along with a hedgerow condition layer for 10 selected tetrads.  Post data collection, the GIS files were copied to a windows computer and checked and edited as required in QGIS.    To assist planning the survey the following datasets were used:  • Urban and built land from OS Master Map  • Arable and temporary grasslands from WG annual returns  • Existing woodland (c.14% land cover) from the National Forest Inventory  • Recently surveyed sites from NRWs Phase 2 (NVC) databases (approximately 25% of uplands and up to 5% of lowlands)    Other datasets used to enhance and speed up field survey work included:  • An up-to-date spatial map of deep peat  • The original Phase 1 Habitat Map of Wales survey maps   • Recent aerial photography - Bing and Google Maps  • SSSI/SAC boundaries\tHudson, J. 2022. Habitat Map of Wales resurvey pilot – field survey. NRW Report No: 664,18 pp, NRW, Bangor.\tCurrently in Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Natural_Resources_Planning\\Biodiversity_Fisheries_Geodiversity\\Jon Rothwell\\P1_2022\\Checking\\Final GDB and LYR Files   But getting moved to X:Drive - location not decided yet.\tStuart Smith, Lead Specialist Advisor, NRW and Jon Rothwell, Specialist Advisor, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-01-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T10:51:25.447Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19100\tNRW_DS125456\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.858Z\t651\tUnassessed\tEditable\tReservoir Regulations\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343536\tA layer showing the reservoirs in Wales. Reservoirs with a capacity greater than 10,000 cubic metres are regulated as large raised reservoirs. This layer provides information such as the capacity of the reservoir, dam height and ownership details. Further information can be obtained from the Reservoir Regulation Team.\tSource of information is the Reservoirs Database, and is updated by the Reservoirs Regulation Team. Layer is to assist duty officers and NRW staff with queries about reservoirs. Lineage element should be used to document additional information about the background or history of the data resource and can include data quality statements.  The element can include information about: source material; data collection methods; data processing methods; quality control processes and may be useful in determining it\u0027s fitness for purpose.   Any specific information warnings should be added here.   Please note that this differs from Data published on DataMapWales (NRW_DS125216) this layer includes data for smaller reservoirs and also potential sites.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Reservoir Regulation.lyr\tGerallt Richards,  Reservoir Regulation Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-02-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:41.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19113\tNRW_DS125457\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.958Z\t554\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThreat of landfill sites to Marine Protected Area habitat features in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343537\tThe map package shows the potential overall threat coastal landfill sites in Wales pose to Marine Protected Area (MPA) habitat features under a present day epoch (2005-2025), a medium term epoch (2025-2055) and a long-term epoch (2055-2105).  Overall threat presented in the map package is a combination of the risk of waste being released from the landfill site and the vulnerability of MPA habitat features to waste if released from these sites. The map package ultimately shows the results of a high-level assessment on the potential impacts of coastal landfill sites to MPAs.   The study aimed to provide an initial assessment of this impact to highlight where regional or site-specific investigations should be undertaken to further understand the threat coastal landfill sites pose to MPA features.     The boundaries of the Operational Areas and MPAs are also included in the map package.   Excel files of the attribute tables from the GIS map package are also provided.     More information on the rationale, methods, results and assumptions can be found in the Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Evidence Report 673.\tThe coastal landfill sites used in this dataset are derived from Historic Landfill Sites spatial dataset (https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_Historic_Landfill_Sites) and a dataset of currently licenced landfill sites provided by NRW.     Overall threat as determined to be a combination of the risk of waste being released from the landfill site and the vulnerability of MPA habitat features to the waste if released from these sites.   This report and supporting data provides a high-level approach for determining which landfill sites at the coast have potential to pose the greatest threat to MPA features in Wales. Given the high-level approach to this assessment, the aim was to inform where more detailed investigations should be undertaken on specific landfills or regions of potential concern. Given the level of confidence related to some of the project outputs it is not possible to use this information to ascertain accurate site-specific information. Instead, it is recommended that future studies use location specific data to refine the results of this assessment.  For example, data about the local hydrodynamics, local environmental conditions, current management measures in place, exact contents and contamination levels contained within and released from the landfill, and specific habitat/species sensitivity to specific landfill pressures, will aid a better understanding of the local site-specific impacts arising from landfills.    More information on the rationale, methods, results and assumptions can be found in the Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Evidence Report 673\tRobbins, K; Bernard, B; Brooks, A; Frost, N. 2023. Investigating the impact of landfill sites at the coast on Marine Protected Area features in Wales. NRW Evidence Report. Report No: 673, Natural Resources Wales, Cardiff.\tX:\\Industrial\\Pollution and Waste\\Coastal landfill sites posing a threat to MPA features\tHarriet Robinson - Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2023-03-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:47:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19115\tNRW_DS125458\t2024-05-28T13:02:41.128Z\t1379\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Regulations Cycle 3 Classification\tDosbarthiad Cylch 3 Rheoliadau\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343538\tThis dataset shows the boundaries and baseline classification, undertaken in 2021, for The Water Framework Directive (WFD) Cycle 3. It is an update in classification for all water bodies in Wales following on from the cycle 1 and cycle 2 classification rounds.    The Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England \u0026amp; Wales) Regulations 2017 (referred to as the WFD Regulations) provide a framework for managing the water environment in England and Wales. The Directive aims for “good status” of all ground and surface water (rivers, lakes, transitional water, and coastal waters). Under the WFD Regulations, a river basin management plan must be prepared for each river basin district. The plan includes environmental objectives and a summary of the programmes of measures required to achieve those objectives.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn dangos y ffiniau a\u0027r dosbarthiad sylfaen, a wnaed yn 2021, ar gyfer Cylch 3 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD). Mae\u0027n ddiweddariad o ran dosbarthiad ar gyfer pob corff dŵr yng Nghymru yn dilyn rowndiau dosbarthu cylch 1 a chylch 2.  Mae Rheoliadau\u0027r Amgylchedd Dŵr (Y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr) (Cymru a Lloegr) 2017 (y cyfeirir atynt fel Rheoliadau\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr) yn darparu fframwaith ar gyfer rheoli\u0027r amgylchedd dŵr yng Nghymru a Lloegr. Mae’r Gyfarwyddeb yn anelu at “statws da” ar gyfer yr holl ddŵr daear a dŵr wyneb (afonydd, llynnoedd, dŵr trosiannol a dyfroedd arfordirol). O dan Reoliadau\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr, rhaid paratoi cynllun rheoli basn afon ar gyfer pob ardal basn afon. Mae\u0027r cynllun yn cynnwys amcanion amgylcheddol a chrynodeb o\u0027r rhaglenni o fesurau sydd eu hangen i gyflawni\u0027r amcanion hynny.  Mae\u0027r holl gyrff dŵr wedi\u0027u hasesu ac wedi\u0027u cynnwys yn y Cynllun Rheoli Basn Afon lleol (RBMP). Mae CNC yn diweddaru\u0027r dosbarthiad bob 3 blynedd. Bydd dosbarthiad Interim Cylch 3 ar gael yn 2024. Bydd yn cael ei gyhoeddi ar ffurf tabl ar Arsylwi Dyfroedd Cymru. Yna gellir ei gysylltu â chyrff dŵr y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr, os oes angen.  Datganiad priodoli:  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys Data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded yr Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawliau Cronfa Ddata. Yn deillio\u0027n rhannol o ddata digidol graddfa 1:50,000 a 1:250,000 gyda chaniatâd Arolwg Daearegol Prydain. ©NERC.\tAll waterbodies have been assessed and are included within the local River Basin Management Plan (RBMP). NRW updates the classification every 3 years. The Cycle 3 Interim classification is now available, see the metadata record NRW_DS161347 - Water Framework Directive (WFD) Regulations Cycle 3 Classification (Interim 2024).\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\tData Custodian: Rob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-08-05\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/4471/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_wfd_cycle_3_classifications\t2025-10-15T09:43:37.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19107\tNRW_DS125460\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.956Z\t312\tValid\tEditable\tEryri SSSI Bryophyte Survey 2015\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343630\t18 cwms and two summits in Eryri SSSI were surveyed by 30 bryologists from the British Bryological Society, including 9 national experts, between 7th and 12th June 2015. The survey was coordinated by the Natural Resources Wales non-vascular plant ecologist.\tField survey data\tBosanquet SDS \u0026amp; British Bryological Society 2015. Eryri SSSI bryophyte survey 2015. NRW Evidence Report No: 91, 132 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FEryri%20Bryophyte%20Survey%20BBS%202015\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet - Terrestrial Habitats and Species Specialist Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-09-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-06-30T14:24:03.033Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19098\tNRW_DS125462\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.660Z\t657\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey for larvae of the caddis Limnephilus tauricus on Cors Erddreiniog - Winter 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343632\tThe ‘Natur am Byth!’ partnership unites nine environmental charities and Natural Resources Wales to deliver the country’s largest natural heritage and outreach programme to save species from extinction and reconnect people to nature.  Following the rediscovery of adults of the Vulnerable caddis Limnephilus tauricus on Cors Erddreiniog in July and August 2022, its only Welsh locality, an extensive search of 26 locations for larvae was undertaken. The opportunity was taken to look for the Critically Endangered L. pati O’Connor at the same time as the two species are known to occur together although it has never been recorded from Wales (Wallace, 2016).\tThis survey was carried out to locate breeding sites of Limnephilus tauricus on Cors Erddreiniog between 28th November and 4th December 2022. The weather during the sampling period was good, being bright and free from rain and frost. Surveys were undertaken from approximately 1030 to 1630 hours each day.\tWallace, I. \u0026amp; Wallace, B. A survey for larvae of the caddis Limnephilus tauricus on Cors Erddreiniog in winter 2022-23. Natur am Byth! Evidence Report No. 004. 28pp. Buglife \u0026amp; Natural Resources Wales.\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID)\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T15:45:32.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19111\tNRW_DS125465\t2024-05-28T13:02:41.033Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNorth Wales Marine Observation Scheme (Paper Data)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343635\tVolunteer based survey of marine observations, carried in sublittoral zone around Anglesey and Llyn Peninsular\tMay be related to later survey carried out specifically in the Menai Strait with some duplication of records, see metadata entry 111148. Connection unknown\t\tPaper Data stored in off site storage – Combined with CCW/NRB/118\tUnknown. Catalogued by Data Services Team as part of Records and paper data records management project May have value if mobilised\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T11:02:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19109\tNRW_DS125466\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.994Z\t296\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaproxylic invertebrates at Carn Gafallt RSPB Reserve in 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343636\tA survey of the dead wood (saproxylic) invertebrate fauna of the Carn Gafallt Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB) Reserve was undertaken over four days in 2022. This work is part of the Natur am Byth! partnership project, which aims to promote the recovery of a range of key species in Wales.\tThe Carn Gafallt survey involved both a range of hand-searching techniques and the installation of five vane traps that were operated between May and September 2022.\tBoyce, D. C. 2023. Saproxylic invertebrates at Carn Gafallt RSPB Reserve in 2022. Natur am Byth! Evidence Report No. 005. 37pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tWID – Natur Am Byth Surveys\tMike Howe\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T15:47:15.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19097\tNRW_DS125467\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.631Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNVC Upland Surveys Ceredigion and Powys 1991-1993 (paper records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343637\tRaw survey data, field maps \u0026amp; notes, quadrat cards   (potentially ad hoc surveys?)  The various papper record files cover: Claerddu, Elendydd SSSI (Cwmanddwr Common, LLuest Abercarnon, Glascwm \u0026amp; Gladesty Hill SSi, Gamrhiw, Pen y Garreg, Ty Mawr, Great Gors, Upper Cwm Pastures, Nantybeddau, Nant yt Henfran SO67753, Rogesrstone Pasture SO 07123, Dolfawr Pastures, Claerwen, Traedrhiw Drain, Ty Mawr,\t\tNot know if dta has already been mobilised- would require expert to confrim\tOffsite-archive box no. CCW/SERNB/51.\tdata custodian not known; contact Data Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19099\tNRW_DS125471\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.718Z\t293\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey and Monitoring of Epiphytic Lichens around PAWS Sites in Brechfa Forest\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343731\tThis survey was designed to look at four sections of the forest not previously recorded for lichens. Oak, ash and birch were selected to be surveyed and monitored in PAWS (Planted Ancient Woodland Sites) and AW (Ancient Woodland) areas of the forest.\tField Survey where Lichen frequencies were recorded on five of each tree species at each of the four sections.\tDouglass J. Survey and Monitoring of Epiphytic Lichens around PAWS Sites in Brechfa Forest. NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 333 , 67pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FPAWS%20Brechfa%20Forest\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2019-02-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:34:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19105\tNRW_DS125474\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.797Z\t380\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveying for the Bog Pseudoscorpion Microbisium brevifemoratum on the raised bog at Cors Fochno (Dyfi SSSI)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343734\tThis project surveyed Cors Fochno in October/November 2022, using standardised vacuum sampling, for the very rare Bog Pseudoscorpion Microbisium brevifemoratum(Ellingsen, 1903). A total of sixty-two minutes of vacuum sampling was undertaken during the survey at 17 sample points. In total, five specimens of M. brevifemoratum were found at two sample points during the survey. A single adult at SN63849084 (29th October 2022) represents a new location, with a further four adults found at SN64029087 (22nd November 2022) very near to where the species was found in 2021.\tTimed vacuum sampling using a petrol vacuum sampler (Stihl SH56C), as described in Gallon (2022), was the main survey method employed. A total of 62 minutes of vacuum sampling was undertaken during the survey at 17 sample points (Figure 1, Figures 6 to 22). The resulting sieved vacuum samples were examined with x2 magnification glasses to find Microbisium specimens and spiders (Figure 2). A clump of Sphagnum was sieved over a tray in the field at SN63809102 (Figures 3 \u0026amp; 13).Two samples of Sphagnum from SN63989088 (Sample Point 3) and SN64029087(Sample Point 13) were subjected to Tullgren Funnel extraction in the lab (Figures 4 \u0026amp; 5). Here, Sphagnum moss was placed on a wire mesh grid within a large plastic funnel and subjected to a 25W incandescent light bulb from above. This method slowly dries out the moss and any hidden invertebrates are driven downwards, falling through the funnel and into a sample pot of preserving alcohol. As Tullgren Funnels are widely used for surveying pseudoscorpions from leaflitter, straw and moss, it was felt this would be worth trying during this survey.\tGallon, R.C. 2023. Surveying for the Bog Pseudoscorpion Microbisium brevifemoratum on the raised bog at Cors Fochno (Dyfi SSSI). NRW Evidence Report No. 678. 37pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tData is stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder6\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T17:26:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19102\tNRW_DS125494\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.797Z\t398\tValid\tEditable\tBryophyte Survey and Assessment of Cwm Rheidol NNR and Coedydd a Cheunant Rheidol SSSI\tArolwg ac Asesiad Bryoffytau o Warchodfa Natur Genedlaethol Cwm Rheidol a SoDdGA Coedydd a Cheunant Rheidol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343934\tAn updated bryophyte survey of the Cwm Rheidol NNR and Coedydd a Cheunant SSSI. It updates a previous survey undertaken by Averis in 1998.   The bryophyte assemblage was found to be of national conservation interest and is certainly within the top ten sites in Wales for Oceanic Ravine Bryophytes (a species assemblage of national conservation importance under Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2006).    The survey area comprises two sections of the Afon Rheidol, one running north from Devil’s Bridge for approximately 3km and one running west from Devil’s Bridge for approximately 2km.\tWithin the overall survey areas, targeting was selective. Thus, areas previously visited by Averis were prioritised. In addition, many areas were inaccessible due to sheer cliffs or other steep, dangerous terrain.\tForster Brown, C. 2020. Bryophyte survey and assessment of Cwm Rheidol NNR and Coedydd a Cheunant Rheidol SSSI, NRW Evidence Report 356, 36 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FCoed%20Rheidol%20Bryophyte%20Survey\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2020-10-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:07.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19114\tNRW_DS125497\t2024-05-28T13:02:41.129Z\t749\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLichen survey of three coastal SSSIs on the Llŷn Peninsula, Gwynedd\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343937\tBaseline survey of coastal lichens covering three SSSI on the western Llŷn Peninsula, Gwynedd; Glannau Aberdaron, Mynydd Penarfynydd and Porth Ceiriad, Porth Neigwl ac Ynysoedd Sant Tudwal. It will involve coverage of all three SSSI but complete coverage is not anticipated given their size and difficult terrain.\tField Survey Data\tLamacraft, D, SP Chambers. 2023. Lichen survey of three coastal SSSIs on the Llŷn Peninsula, Gwynedd. NRW Evidence Report No: 675, 79pp, Natural Resources Wales.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FCoastal%20Lichens%20Lleyn\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet - Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-03-08\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:17:31.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19095\tNRW_DS125498\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.042Z\t593\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveillance of the montane macrolichen Thamnolia vermicularis in Eryri, 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343938\tResurveys of all four Welsh sites where the snowbed lichen Thamnolia vermicularis has been recorded found no extant colonies. Climate Change, Nitrogen pollution and heavy sheep grazing are implicated in its extinction in Wales.\tField survey data\tTurner, A.J. 2023. Surveillance of the montane macrolichen Thamnolia vermicularis in Eryri, 2022. NRW Evidence Report No: 670, 53pp, Natural Resources Wales.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FThamnolia%20vermicularis%20in%20Eryri\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet - Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-03-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:17:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19093\tNRW_DS125499\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.896Z\t541\tValid\tEditable\tMapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Spatial Planning in Wales\tMapio ystyriaethau amgylcheddol ar gyfer cynllunio gofodol morol yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235343939\tEnvironmental Considerations spatial datasets for marine planning. These datasets are derived from multiple datasets indicating the presence of protected features in Welsh waters. The data is presented on 1 km2 hexagonal grids.    Operational phase was considered for all sectors for all feature groups, and includes maintenance activities.  The construction phase was only considered for sectors where potential construction impacts are substantially different from those during operation (tidal stream, wave, tidal range, Floating offshore wind and cabling).  However, for the Bird feature group, construction phase was only considered for tidal range and cabling. The scoring methodology included three main stages:   1. The occurrence score reflects either the presence or absence, or standardized abundance (where abundance data was available) of features (i.e., species, habitats, or protected sites) and were scored from 1 to 3 on a continuous scale where 1 was the lowest density and 3 the highest for each feature.    2. The conservation importance score is an estimate of the level of protection each feature has based on the legislation that confers a feature protection. It is important to note that all the features included in this work are conferred protection under various legislation and all are important considerations in the planning of developments. This step was scored from 1 to 5.     3. An impact score to reflect potential impacts on features based on the likely pressures generated by each sector was applied for the operational pressures of each of the focus sectors and, separate to this, construction pressures for some sectors. Where there was considered to be a lack of evidence then a higher score was applied. The current approach did not assess specific technologies individually but considered the potential pressures that could originate from a sector and their potential to negatively affect each of the features. This step was scored from 1 to 3.   A total score was calculated for each feature in each cell, and these were summed for each 1 km2 hexagonal grid cell, providing a relative ‘environmental considerations’ score for each feature group per sector. The approach taken to the scoring differed slightly between feature groups based on the available evidence-Mae\u0027r setiau data hyn yn deillio o nifer o setiau data sy\u0027n nodi presenoldeb nodweddion gwarchodedig yn nyfroedd Cymru. Cyflwynir y data ar gridiau hecsagonol 1 km2.  Mae rhagor o fanylion am y fethodoleg wedi\u0027u cynnwys yn yr adroddiad canlynol:  Murray, L.G., Bloomfield, H. a Skates, L. (2023). Mapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Spatial Planning in Wales: Methodology. Adroddiad i Lywodraeth Cymru. Bangor, Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru. 48tt.  Ystyriwyd y cyfnod gweithredol ar gyfer pob sector ar gyfer pob grŵp nodwedd, ac mae\u0027n cynnwys gweithgareddau cynnal a chadw. Dim ond ar gyfer sectorau lle mae effeithiau adeiladu posibl yn wahanol iawn i\u0027r rhai gweithredu (ynni llif y llanw, ynni’r tonnau, ynni amrediad y llanw, ynni gwynt arnofiol ar y môr a cheblau) y cafodd y cam adeiladu ei ystyried. Fodd bynnag, ar gyfer grŵp nodwedd Adar, dim ond ar gyfer amediad llanw a cheblau y cafodd y cam adeiladu ei ystyried. Roedd y fethodoleg sgorio yn cynnwys tri phrif gam:  1.   Mae\u0027r sgôr digwyddiad yn adlewyrchu naill ai presenoldeb neu absenoldeb, neu helaethrwydd safonedig (lle’r oedd data helaethrwydd ar gael) o nodweddion (h.y., rhywogaethau, cynefinoedd, neu safleoedd gwarchodedig) ac fe\u0027u sgoriwyd o 1 i 3 ar raddfa barhaus lle mai 1 oedd y dwysedd isaf a 3 yr uchaf ar gyfer pob nodwedd.  2.  Mae\u0027r sgôr pwysigrwydd cadwraeth yn amcangyfrif o lefel yr amddiffyniad sydd gan bob nodwedd yn seiliedig ar y ddeddfwriaeth sy\u0027n gwarchod nodwedd. Mae\u0027n bwysig nodi bod yr holl nodweddion a gynhwysir yn y gwaith hwn yn cael eu gwarchod o dan ddeddfwriaeth amrywiol ac mae pob un ohonynt yn ystyriaethau pwysig wrth gynllunio datblygiadau. Sgoriwyd y cam hwn o 1 i 5.  3.  Cymhwyswyd sgôr effaith i adlewyrchu effeithiau posibl ar nodweddion yn seiliedig ar y pwysau tebygol a gynhyrchir gan bob sector ar gyfer pwysau gweithredol pob un o\u0027r sectorau ffocws ac, ar wahân i hyn, pwysau adeiladu ar gyfer rhai sectorau. Lle ystyriwyd bod diffyg tystiolaeth, yna cymhwyswyd sgôr uwch. Nid oedd y dull gweithredu presennol yn asesu technolegau penodol yn unigol ond ystyriodd y pwysau posibl a allai ddeillio o sector a\u0027u potensial i effeithio\u0027n negyddol ar bob un o\u0027r nodweddion. Sgoriwyd y cam hwn o 1 i 3.  Cyfrifwyd cyfanswm sgôr ar gyfer pob nodwedd ym mhob cell, a chyfunwyd y rhain ar gyfer pob cell grid hecsagonol 1 km2, gan ddarparu sgôr \u0027ystyriaethau amgylcheddol\u0027 cymharol ar gyfer pob grŵp nodwedd fesul sector. Roedd y dull a ddefnyddiwyd i sgorio ychydig yn wahanol rhwng grwpiau nodwedd yn seiliedig ar y dystiolaeth sydd ar gael.  Datganiad priodoli:   Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data’r Cyd-bwyllgor Cadwraeth Natur, Natural England, Scottish National Heritage, Swyddfa Hydrograffig y DU, RSPB, Ymddiriedolaeth Adareg Prydain, Y Gymdeithas Cadwraeth Morol, Cefas, Llywodraeth Cymru ac Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawliau Cronfa Ddata.\tThe methods used to derive these datasets are based on those used in the Sustainable Management of Marine Natural Resources project: ABPmer, 2020. Sustainable Management of Marine Natural Resources, Ecological Constraints and Opportunities, ABPmer Report No. R.3527. A report produced by ABPmer for Welsh Government.  Datasets and methods have been updated and applied to additional sectors.\tMurray, L.G., Bloomfield, H. and Skates, L. (2023). Mapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Spatial Planning in Wales: Methodology Report. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tX:\\Planning\\Marine\\Mapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Planning - Birds.lyr,     X:\\Planning\\Marine\\Mapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Planning - Fish.lyr,    X:\\Planning\\Marine\\Mapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Planning - Habitats Benthic Species.lyr,   X:\\Planning\\Marine\\Mapping Environmental Considerations for Marine Planning - Mammals.lyr\tLee Murray - Specialist Advisor Marine Spatial Planning\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-10-24\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/5653/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_mecmp\t2025-11-12T13:21:19.028Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n19090\tNRW_DS125500\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.857Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDNA Barcoding Survey of Dune Slack Bryum in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353030\tA survey of dune slack mosses using DNA Barcoding, including sampling of mosses from recently scraped slacks.   The following sites were surveyed: Tywyn Aberffraw Newborough Warren Morfa Dyffryn  Whiteford Burrows Merthyr Mawr Warren Kenfig Burrows\tField survey data, DNA Barcoding\tBosanquet, SDS, J Creer, L Forrest, D Bell, P Hollingsworth. 2023. DNA Barcoding survey of dune slack Bryum in Wales. NRW Evidence Report No: 669. 44 pages.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FDune%20Bryum%20DNA%20Barcoding\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-03-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:32:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19091\tNRW_DS125502\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.857Z\t261\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGathering Twig Lichen Data for Powys SSSIs to Investigate Ammonia Levels: Second Phase\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353032\tTwig Lichen Surveys on 9 SSSI in Powys, showing that these sites are suffering significant ammonia pollution except in a few upland areas.\tField Survey Data\tBacciu, NG \u0026amp; McGill, J. 2023. Gathering twig lichen data for Powys SSSIs to investigate ammonia levels: Second phase. NRW Evidence Report 676, 90 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2FLichen%20surveys%20to%20investigate%20ammonia%20impacts\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet - Sam.Bosanquet@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-03-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:36:13.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19094\tNRW_DS125504\t2024-05-28T13:02:40.002Z\t705\tValid\tEditable\tInformation for each Carbon Reduction Commitment (CRC) participant\tGwybodaeth ar gyfer pob cyfranogwr yn yr Ymrwymiad Lleihau Carbon (CRC)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353034\tThe CRC Order 2013 provides for publication of information on the basis of participants\u0027 annual reports plus details submitted when they registered for the scheme. The CRC Energy Efficiency Scheme (CRC) is a mandatory scheme which aims to improve energy efficiency and cut emissions for large public and private sector energy users across the UK. The scheme aims to encourage organisations to develop energy management strategies that promote a better understanding of energy usage, and lead to improved energy efficiency.   The scheme is split into phases: Phase 1 from 1 April 2010 until 31 March 2014 and Phase 2 from 1 April 2014 until 31 March 2019.    Each year, CRC participants have to monitor their energy consumption and report this information into the CRC Registry, an online IT system, which calculates their carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions. Participants must purchase and surrender \u0027allowances\u0027 to cover these emissions - one allowance for each tonne of CO2.     Further information on the scheme can be found on the CRC pages of GOV.UK.   The ARP format was introduced for the 2012 to 2013 reporting year and replaces the performance league tables (PLT). Removal of the PLT was part of the government’s simplification of the CRC scheme. These annually produced data sets contain the information provided by participants to the CRC Registry including organisational type, energy use and CO2 emissions. The data should be read in combination with the equivalent years ‘CRC Energy Efficiency Scheme: annual report publication which are located on GOV.UK. CRC performance league tables were published for the 2010 to 2011 and 2011 to 2012 compliance years and ranked participants according to their energy efficiency performance against a range of metrics.\tThis dataset replaces ‘Carbon Reduction Commitment Performance Data’ which has been retired.\tMetadata for this dataset is already made available by the EA through Data.gov.uk https://www.data.gov.uk/dataset/4b709a17-6e98-4021-a207-137ac931bfc3/information-for-each-carbon-reduction-commitment-crc-participant#licence-info\tExternal Website: Published on data.gov by the EA.  DO NOT ONWARD PUBLISH METADATA TO FindOpenData (Published by the EA so this metadata for NRW internal catalogue only)\tCustodian: Elizabeth Voice, Lead Specialist Officer, Species and Carbon Permitting.                    DO NOT ONWARD PUBLISH DATA as Published by the EA\tEnvironment Agency (EA)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-03-03\tpublication\tnull\thttps://www.data.gov.uk/dataset/4b709a17-6e98-4021-a207-137ac931bfc3/information-for-each-carbon-reduction-commitment-crc-participant3\t2026-01-09T15:59:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19089\tNRW_DS125505\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.825Z\t817\tValid\tEditable\tRed Squirrel (Sciurus vulgaris) abundance and habitat preference survey data – Anglesey 2021/22\tData arolwg nifer a dewis cynefinoedd y Wiwer Goch (Sciurus vulgaris) – Ynys Môn 2021/22\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353035\tProject undertaken to analyse the use of camera traps in determining population abundance of red squirrel in different habitat types on Anglesey.   Two sites were used in this survey; Pentraeth and Newborough,     The data contains trap locations identified by What3 words, and includes details of habitat types and numbers of squirrels caught.\t50 Sampling locations (20 for Pentraeth and 30 for Newborough) were selected across four stand type categories that would be as central to the coupe as possible and no less than 250 metres away from any other given sampling location (to maximise independence).    This data is considered sensitive, please see the access and use restrictions for further information.\tShannon G, Valle S \u0026amp; Shuttleworth CM. 2022. Using remote activated cameras to estimate relative abundance and habitat preference of red squirrels (Sciurus vulgaris). NRW Evidence Report Series. Report No: 672. 40pp.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/evidence/Species/mam/Red%20Squirrel/Research%20Papers/NRW%20Evidence%20Report%20672%20-survey%20data%20(Red%20Squirrel%20abundance%20and%20habitat%20preference%20-%20Anglesey).xlsx?d\u003dw01227aac5cc5427b85dd2d31e5e2155f\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\tCustodian: Becky Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor  - Terrestrial Mammals, Terrestrial Ecosystems Groups\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2023-02-22\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:04:32.143Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19092\tNRW_DS125506\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.855Z\t275\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLichen Survey of Dinefwr NNR (VC 44 Carmarthenshire): including re-monitoring the 2013 baseline and comments on air pollution and Ash Dieback.\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353036\tSurvey and accompanying spreadsheet covering monitoring of the epiphytic lichens of Dinefwr Estate SSSI in Carmarthenshire, including assessment impacts of Ash Dieback and ammonia pollution\tField survey data\tWindle A, Douglass JR. 2023. Lichen Survey of Dinefwr NNR (VC 44 Carmarthenshire): including re-monitoring the 2013 baseline and comments on air pollution and Ash Dieback. NRW Evidence Report 682, 186 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FDinefwr%20Lichen%20Surveys\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet - Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-03-13\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19087\tNRW_DS125508\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.634Z\t138\tValid\tEditable\tMorfa Friog Monitoring Data 2018\tData Monitro Morfa Friog 2018\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353038\tMorfa Friog managed coastal realignment site situated on the Mawddach estuary was created when the flood embankment was breached in November 2015.   A methodology was established and baseline data collected during autumn 2018 to monitor the development of the saltmarsh vegetation on the site.\tThe data consists of;  • A fixed-wing drone survey of the site to collect high-resolution aerial photography, undertaken on the 5th September 2018.   • The generation of an accurate height model from the collected aerial photography.   • Capture of vegetation communities based on species dominance from the aerial photography and data collected in the field.   • The selection of three transects across the main site.   • Detailed survey of a series of randomly located 2×2 metre quadrats along the transects.   • Photographs of transects quadrats and features of interest.\t\tZ:\\Archive\\Flood\\SPI\\NHCP\\Morfa Friog\\ \\MorfaFriog_Habitats_v2\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Heather Lewis, Lead Specialist Advisor: Coastal Habitats, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-03-16\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19088\tNRW_DS125511\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.597Z\t330\tValid\tEditable\tMonitoring of the Black Bog Ant Formica picea on Rhossili Down SSSI in 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353131\tThe Black Bog Ant Formica picea is rare in the UK where it is associated with bogs and wet heath at a small number of sites in southern England including the heaths of Dorset and Surrey and the valley bogs in the New Forest, with a single site in Yorkshire. In Wales, it is restricted to Rhossili Down on Gower, Cors Goch Llanllwch in Carmarthenshire and Cors Llanerch in Pembrokeshire.   It was first recorded on Rhossili Down in 1913 and subsequently refound in 1996. A survey in July 1997 found 49 active nests along four east to west-oriented transects across the bog and flushed wet heath habitats to the east of the central ridge, with a further 23 active nests elsewhere on the site. It was estimated that between 840 and 4970 active nests were present across the site, representing the largest population in the UK.   The current survey in August 2022 repeated the 1997 work, with a count of active nests along the four transects.\tThe technique used to record Black Bog Ant along the transects was a modified form of that employed in the 1997 survey. For each of the four transects, a strip approximately 10 metres wide centred on the appropriate grid line, was searched intensively following the method used on Cors Goch Llanllwch by Fowles \u0026amp; Hurford (1996). To facilitate recording, the transect was divided into ten-metre sample sections, each of which was assigned a unique sample number, starting at the western end of the transect.  The main difference in methodology between the 1997 survey and the current monitoring was that in 1997, three surveyors walked in a line along each transect, whereas in 2022, over the same number of days, there was just the one surveyor. It would therefore be expected, given the cryptic nature of Black Bog Ant nests, that the earlier study would have produced more records than the latter.\tBoyce, D.C. 2023. Monitoring of the black bog ant Formica picea on Rhossili Down SSSI in 2022. NRW Evidence Report No. 686. 21pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID)\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-05-02T14:48:23.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19086\tNRW_DS125516\t2024-05-28T13:02:39.545Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCeredigion Coastal Rare Plant Survey 1975 \u0026amp; Ceredigion Rare Plants\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353136\tThis is a survey of rare plants in Ceredigion coastal areas. Most of the 1700 species of British vascular plants can be found on the coast, the greater part of them on the more terrestrial coastal habitats on sand dunes, shingle beaches or cliffs. Also included is a register and survey of Ceredigion rare plants.\tThis survey of rare plants in Ceredigion is part of the Coastal Survey of England and Wales planned in 1968 by the Nature Conservancy Council. The overall aim of this survey was to collate and assess information about coastal resources and the special qualities and values which they possess.\t\toff-site archive ref: CCW/WRAB/024\tData Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       Data Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:06:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19139\tNRW_DS125517\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.379Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey of hedges and their woody species in Ceredigion 1974-1979\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353137\tA survey of the woody species and physical attributes of 2223 30 m sample lenths of 752 hedges in Ceredigion took plce between 1974-1979. Most hedges ocurred on banks. Lane hedges were in the best condition; the hedge conditions and proportion managed declined significantly with altitude. As each hedge in this survey has been mapped, there is the potential for the survey to be repeated in the future.\tDating of hedges was done from manuscript estate maps and parish tithe maps of the 1840s in the National Library of Wales and from Ordnance Survey maps\t\tCCW/WRAB/026\tData Services Opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk       Opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk Data Services Team\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19132\tNRW_DS125518\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.234Z\t9\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNCC Biological survey of common land in Wales 1989-1994 (paper data records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353138\tIn 1986 the Countryside Commission published the Report of the Common Land Forum which resulted in the possibility of new legislation on common land. The Nature Conservancy Council subsequently commissioned the Biological Survey of Common Land in order to assess the implications for conservation agencies.   Work on the Survey started in November 1987 and continued until December 1994. It was undertaken by Rural Surveys Research Unit at Aberystwyth University, and following the split of NCC into English Nature, Scottish Natural Heritage and the Countryside Council for Wales in 1991, the three national bodies continued to fund the research.    The aim of the survey was to record detailed information about wildlife in each of the common land areas in order to produce informed management schemes. These schemes would be operated by local management associations, consisting of representatives from all interested parties.\tThe survey was split into four areas of work: to produce boundary maps, vegetation maps and, where possible National Vegetation Classifications; to provide brief descriptions of biological interest of each common (mainly botanical but also to include fauna information if possible); to provide brief descriptions of management practises and recommendations for each site; and to give an overview of the biological characteristics and management of common land at county level.    NRW currently hold copies of each of the reports for Wales.  We also hold original recording forms, slides and images.  These are under review for submission to The National Archives (TNA). There is also a report commissioned by CCW that provides a synthesis of the information\tAitchison, J 1993.  Common land and conservation : biological surveys in England and Wales : a synthesis.  CCW Science Report No. 59\t5 boxes of paper data are currently held in the comms room F13, under the table.  Labelled as , PP19, PP20, PP21, PP22, PP23.  These are to be prepared to go to TNA (work to be carried out with Jill), Box PP23 my need some info to the disposed of as mainly supporting info or rough maps\tPossibly Buddug Jones or Stuart Smith\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW)-Nature Conservancy Council (NCC)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-03-30\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T11:59:41.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n19137\tNRW_DS125521\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.366Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Survey of Selected Grassland Sites in Ceredigion 1987\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353231\tThe objective of the filed work were: Determine the nature and distribution of plant comunities within the site. Collect detailed records from a representative sample of plant communities. Note the distribution and abundance within the site of any plant species known to be rare or uncommon in Ceredigion\t\t\toffi-site archive box ref: CCW/WRAB/023\tData Services Team opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk, off-site archive box ref:CCW/WRAB/023        Data Services team Opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:42.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19145\tNRW_DS125522\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.421Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey of Broadleaved Woodlands in Ceredigion 1978\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353232\tNCC Survey to study the extent and state of semi-natural broadleaved woodland in Ceredigion (SN grdi squares)\t\t\toff-site archive box ref: CCW/WRAB/023\tData Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk, Off-site archive box ref: CCW/WRAB/023       Data Services opendata@naturalresourceswales.gov.uk\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19142\tNRW_DS125523\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.376Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAfon Teifi Data Collection 1989 (paper  data records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353233\tData Collection created ahead of proposed notification of the site as a NCR.  The aim of the project was to collate a wide range of scientific information already available from NCC and others to aid the designation of the site.  data was collated from local regional NCC files, FBA, Welsh Water, RSBP, Otter Haven Trust, UWIST, Dyfed Wildlife Trust.  The data consists of 8 volumes.  Volumes 7 is a Report and Bibliography Volume 1: Vegetation main river Volume 2: Vegetation tributaries Volume 3: Vegetation tributaries report Volume 4:Inverts, fish Volume 5: Birds Volume 6: mammals Volume 7: Final report and tributaries Volume 8: Maps\tAlthough the data is a collation, it is unclear if the original data is still available.  This is a photocopy, however there is no record of the original and the NCC report has not been found.\tWildlife Surveys Wales  (1989) The RiverTeifi Data Collection: Report to NCC by Wildlife Survey (Wales)\tData to be sent to off-site storage. Box numbers: box 1 of 2 is CCW/WRAB/48 and box 2 of 2 is CCW/WRAB/49\tData to be stored off site.  Mobilisation would be in form of scanning as raw data may be available elsewhere.  Would only be considered if originals are found, otherwise may be disposed of\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:43:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19140\tNRW_DS125526\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.377Z\t416\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe Distribution of the Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior on Whiteford Burrows \u0026amp; Cwm Ivy Marsh - September 2021\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353236\tWhiteford Burrows is a dune system lying on the north-west coast of the Gower Peninsula and is part of the much larger Carmarthen Bay Dunes/Twyni Bae Caerfyrddin SAC. The Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior, listed on the EC Habitats \u0026amp; Species Directive Annex IIa, was first found on Whiteford Burrows in May 1983 - a new live record for the species not only in Wales but also in western Britain. Previous V. angustior studies have been carried out at this location between 1983 and 2018. It was suggested that further surveys be undertaken at the far north-east of the transition zone, throughout the main body of the dune system (focussing upon areas in damp hollows and on the margins of dune slacks where the water table is near the surface) and with a survey of the Cwm Ivy Marsh marine/terrestrial transition zone recently formed with the area now subject to tidal inflow.\tSurvey work was undertaken on three days, 27th to 29th September 2021. Survey locations were chosen with a focus on (1) the relatively recently established upper salt marsh transition zone of Cwm Ivy Marsh at the south and (2) areas not previously visited lying at the extreme north and and centre of the dunes and so avoiding the previous focus on the eastern saltmarsh / sand dune transition zone. A total of 29 sites were surveyed. Site survey involved: 1. visual examination of vegetation and ground litter; 2. beating vegetation onto a white gridded tray and examining debris with a X6 magnifier; 3. shaking dry moss and low growing fresh and dead plant material through a two-tier 2mm and 0.5mm sieve stack\tWilling, M.J. 2023. The distribution of the Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior on Whiteford Burrows \u0026amp; Cwm Ivy Marsh in September 2021. NRW Evidence Report No. 698. 47pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID) in Recorder 6\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T16:01:04.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19143\tNRW_DS125529\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.416Z\t375\tValid\tEditable\tSaproxylic Invertebrates Survey Data at Gilfach Radnorshire Wildlife Trust Reserve in 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353239\tA survey of the dead wood (saproxylic) invertebrate fauna of the Gilfach Nature Reserve, which is owned and managed by the Radnorshire Wildlife Trust (RWT), was undertaken over four man-days in 2022. This work is part of the Natur am Byth! Partnership project, which aims to promote the recovery of selected key species in Wales. The Gilfach survey involved both a range of hand-searching techniques and the installation of five vane traps that were operated between May and September. The survey has shown Gilfach to support a diverse invertebrate fauna that includes a number of scarce species.\tFour days’ fieldwork were undertaken at Gilfach over five days in 2022. These visits were on 24th May, 21st June, 29th July, 15th September and 22nd December. The main focus of recording effort has been on dead wood beetles, which make up the largest and most important element of the specialist saproxylic fauna.  Five vane traps were set up at each site during the May visit, with details of the location and siting of these being provided in Table 2 below. In May, beating flowering Hawthorn was a major focus of sampling effort. Additionally, on all visits  much time has been spent beating foliage and dead branches of mature and veteran trees and hand-searching under bark and in heart-rot of veteran trees. During the September visit, the vane traps were emptied and removed and sampling effort focused on hand-searching saproxylic fungi. The December visit aimed to search for northern-distributed saproxylic species that are known to have occurred in the upland oak woods on the fringes of the Cambrian Mountains.\tBoyce, D.C. 2023. Saproxylic invertebrates at Gilfach RWT reserve in 2022. Natur am Byth! Report No. 006. 35pp. Buglife \u0026amp; Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tWID held on Recorder 6 and wll be published as part of the NBN dataset\tMike Howe\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T15:28:09.886Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19138\tNRW_DS125531\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.371Z\t1005\tValid\tEditable\tTranquillity \u0026amp; Place Wales Sound Environment Part 1 2023\tLlonyddwch a Lle: Amgylchedd Sain Cymru Rhan 1 2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353331\tThe nationally consistent terrestrial Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place resource identifies the strategic and local resource in remote, rural, peri-urban and urban areas for use as an evidence base to inform policy intent, practice and provision for well-being benefits. There are 6 themes.    Tranquillity is associated with the degree to which places and ecosystems deliver a state of quiet, calm, peace and well-being. This can be described as a relative abundance, perception or experience of nature, natural landscapes and features and/or a relative freedom from unwanted visual disturbance, signs of human influence and artificial noise.    Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Sound Environment Part I delivers Theme 4, mapping sound environments where natural sounds are more prominent than noise and are appropriate to context. It includes sounds other than road and railway noise.-Mae Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place, adnodd daearol sy’n genedlaethol gyson, yn nodi’r adnodd strategol a lleol mewn ardaloedd anghysbell, gwledig, ardaloedd trefol a’r ardaloedd o amgylch trefi i’w ddefnyddio fel sail tystiolaeth i gyfarwyddo bwriad polisi, arfer a darpariaeth ar gyfer buddion llesiant. Ceir 6 thema.  Mae Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Sound Environment Part I yn cyflwyno Thema 4, mapio amgylcheddau sain lle mae synau naturiol yn fwy amlwg na sŵn ac yn addas i\u0027r cyd-destun. Mae\u0027n cynnwys synau heblaw sŵn ffyrdd a rheilffyrdd.  Cydnabyddiaeth: Hawlfraint © CNC/NRW. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data’r Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif trwydded yr Arolwg Ordnans: AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a hawl cronfa ddata. Yn deillio yn Rhannol o BGS Digital Data dan Rif Trwydded 2013/062. Cymdeithas Ddaearegol Prydain.\tThe rural analysis (50m resolution) covers the entirety of Wales, including those areas covered by the urban analysis, to allow for consistent comparisons between different geographies. The urban analysis (10m resolution) is in addition to this, but only covers the urban areas defined using the extent of the study areas mapped in the Urban Tree Cover data.   Scores use a scale of 0 – 10, sounds that contribute to tranquillity getting a higher score, decreasing with distance from the source. Sounds that detract from tranquillity were given a lower score, increasing with distance from the source. It is important that the Part I results are taken as an indicative whole rather than in isolation. The forthcoming addition of part 2 data on road and rail will be an important addition.\tGreen C, Manson D, Chamberlain K, Grace I 2023. Tranquillity and Place Sound Environment (Part 1). NRW Report No: 660, 100pp, NRW\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquillity and Place\\Sound Environment Part 1 2023\tJill Bullen - Lead Specialist Advisor: Landscape\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-04-04\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/5344\t2025-04-07T09:59:03.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19141\tNRW_DS125532\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.379Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurveys for Large Mason Bee Osmia xanthomelana on Llŷn Peninsula in 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353332\tCreated as part of the development phase of the Natur am Byth project this field survey was taken at two sites (Porth Neigwl and Porth Ceiriad) located on the Llyn Peninsula where previous records have been made of the Large Mason Bee.\tSurveys were conducted over 5 days in May and June 2022 and undertaken in dry/summer conditions where the Large Mason Bee is most active.\tOlds L.T. \u0026amp; Dawson R. 2023. Surveys for Large Mason Bee Osmia xanthomelana on  Llŷn Peninsula in 2022. Natur am Byth! Report No. 007. 38pp. Natural Resources  Wales, Bangor \u0026amp; Buglife.\tLink to Associated Evidence Report - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/kdim/rl/NRW%20Publications/Natur%20Am%20Byth%20Reports/Natur%20am%20Byth!%20Report%20No.%20007%20-%20Osmia%20xanthomelana%20report%20April%202023%20redacted.pdf  Data is contained within the Welsh Invertebrate Database - Natur Am Byth\tMike Howe\t\t\t2023-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19131\tNRW_DS125534\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.099Z\t350\tValid\tEditable\tA survey for Short-necked Oil Beetle Meloe brevicollis at Castlemartin Range SSSI\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353334\tFollowing the recording of an adult at Linney Head in June 2015, a survey was conducted at Castlemartin Range SSSI in Pembrokeshire in 2022 to clarify the  conservation status of the Nationally Rare Short-necked Oil Beetle Meloe brevicollis. A single dead adult and numerous triungulins were found at Pen-y-holt Bay confirming that Short-necked Oil Beetle is both present and breeding within the SSSI. This is believed to be the first record of wild larvae of Short-necked Oil Beetle in Wales and suggests that triungulin surveys can be a successful tool in identifying ‘new’ locations for the beetle at times when adults are less active.  Short-necked Oil Beetle exhibits a restricted distribution at Castlemartin Range SSSI, increasing its vulnerability to local and national extinction. At present, Pen-y-holt Bay is the only location within the SSSI supporting recent records of the beetle (and, indeed, the only breeding evidence). The conservation of this population is therefore of upmost importance.\tThe survey was conducted over three visits to Castlemartin Range SSSI in 2022. The first of these was on 22nd April, the second on 14th May, and the third and final visit on 27th June 2022.   The first visit targeted St Govan’s Head (SR9792) in the east of Castlemartin Range SSSI, while the remaining visits targeted Linney Head (SR8896) in the west and the coastline east of Linney Head to approximately Mount Sion Down (SR9194).\tOlds L.T. 2023. A survey for Short-necked Oil Beetle Meloe brevicollis at Castlemartin Range SSSI in 2022. Natur am Byth! Report No. 008. 17pp. Buglife \u0026amp;  Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID)\tMike Howe, Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:03:35.345Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19144\tNRW_DS125543\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.304Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey of rare coastal species of the moss genus Bryum in Wales in September 2015\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353433\tA report on the monitoring of rare Bryum mosses on Welsh sand dunes.\tField survey data\tHolyoak DT 2015. A survey of rare coastal species of the moss genus Bryum in Wales in September 2015. NRW Evidence Report No: 133, 45 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FDune%20Bryum%20survey%202015\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T17:25:08.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19135\tNRW_DS125549\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.337Z\t631\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Catchments Cycle 3\tY Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr – Dalgylchoed Arfordirol, Cylch 3\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353439\tThe Water Framework Directive (WFD) Coastal Catchments Cycle 3 are a polygon dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive. Catchments are defined as an area of land from which all surface run-off flows through a series of streams, rivers and, possibly, lakes to a particular point in the water course such as a river confluence. It is a layer of the undesignated small coastal catchments and is used alongside the Water Framework Directive (WFD) River Waterbody Catchments Cycle 3 dataset to produce outputs showing all the river catchments together.\tThis dataset was originally created for use by co-deliverers before becoming final for Cycle 2 in 2013. Next map digital elevation model was generalised to 10 m resolution. Used as the basis to create the Cycle 3 layer.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Coastal Catchments Cycle 3.lyr\tRobert Blacklidge - Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-05-04\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/4471/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_wfd_cycle_3_classifications\t2025-12-19T14:12:36.561Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19136\tNRW_DS125552\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.336Z\t563\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring the Gipsywort Weevil Datonychus arquatus on Pembrey Air Weapons Range in 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353532\tThe Black Bog Ant Formica picea is rare in the UK where it is associated with bogs and wet heath at a small number of sites in southern England including the heaths of Dorset and Surrey and the valley bogs in the New Forest, with a single site in Yorkshire. In Wales, it is restricted to Rhossili Down on Gower, Cors Goch Llanllwch in Carmarthenshire and Cors Llanerch in Pembrokeshire. It was first recorded on Rhossili Down in 1913 and subsequently refound in 1996. A survey in July 1997 found 49 active nests along four east to west-oriented transects across the bog and flushed wet heath habitats to the east of the central ridge, with a further 23 active nests elsewhere on the site. It was estimated thatbetween 840 and 4970 active nests were present across the site, representing the largest population in the UK. The current survey in August 2022 repeated the 1997 work, with a count of active nests along the four transects. A total of 47 active nests was recorded, two less than in 1997, although numbers along each transect varied\tThe technique used to record Black Bog Ant along the transects was a modified form of that employed in the 1997 survey. For each of the four transects, a strip approximately 10 metres wide centred on the appropriate grid line, was searched intensively following the method used on Cors Goch Llanllwch by Fowles \u0026amp; Hurford (1996). To facilitate recording, the transect was divided into ten-metre sample sections, each of which was assigned a unique sample number, starting at the western end of the transect.\tBoyce, D.C. 2023. Monitoring of the black bog ant Formica picea on Rhossili Down SSSI in 2022. NRW Evidence Report No. 686. 21pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tData is stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database stored on Recorder6\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T17:27:57.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19127\tNRW_DS125554\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.954Z\t23\tValid\tEditable\tWelsh Dune Fungi: Data Collation, Evaluation and Conservation Priorities\tFfwng Twyni Cymru: Casglu Data, Gwerthuso a Blaenoriaethau Cadwraeth\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353534\tA report and data spreadsheet covering analysis of historic data on dune fungi, which subsequently led to the development of a scoring system for SSSI selection.\tAnalysis of historic survey data.\t\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/fun/Fungus%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Ffun%2FFungus%20Evidence%2F0%20Fungi%20Evidence%20Report%20Data%2FWelsh%20Dune%20Fungi%20Data%20Collation\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200083EEAA977243FD40886539B9E121A6E5\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B3D1F5A48%2D0066%2D4C30%2DAF53%2D7534D1DCD95A%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T16:06:12.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19128\tNRW_DS125556\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.960Z\t19\tValid\tEditable\tThe effects of topography and rainfall on the distribution of Oceanic/Atlantic bryophytes in Wales – revised 2015\tEffeithiau topograffeg a glawiad ar ddosbarthiad bryoffytau Cefnforol/Atlantig yng Nghymru – diwygiwyd 2015\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353536\tA review of studies around the effects of rainfall and waterfall flows on oceanic bryophyte distribution.  Data associated with this report is held within the report only.\tAnalysis of historic survey data.\t\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FAtlantic%20Bryophytes%20vs%20Topography%20and%20Rainfall\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T16:12:24.800Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19133\tNRW_DS125561\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.246Z\t1522\tValid\tEditable\tModelled Distributions and Abundance of Cetaceans and Seabirds of Wales and Surrounding Waters\tDosbarthiad ac amlder a fodelwyd ar gyfer Morfilod ac Adar Morol Cymru a\u0027r Dyfroedd Cyfagos\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353631\tThe data resource is a package of GIS shapefiles of modelled maximum and monthly (mean) densities of cetacean and seabird species presented in Natural Resources Wales’ Evidence Report No 646: Modelled Distributions and Abundance of Cetaceans and Seabirds in Wales and Surrounding Waters. Refer to report for further information.-Mae hon yn set ddata o ddwyseddau mwyaf a misol (cymedrig) a fodelwyd ar gyfer rhywogaethau o forfilod ac adar môr penodol ym Môr Iwerddon a’r Môr Celtaidd o amgylch Cymru. Mae dwyseddau a fodelwyd yn deillio o fodelu rhagfynegol o achosion o weld y creaduriaid a newidynnau amgylcheddol. Mae\u0027r mapiau mwyaf yn cynrychioli\u0027r dwysedd mwyaf fesul cell 2.5km2 o\u0027r set ddata gyfan. Mynegir mapiau misol (adar môr a morfilod) a degawdol (morfilod yn unig) fel dwysedd cymedrig fesul cell 2.5km2.  Gellir lawrlwytho\u0027r data mwyaf a fodelwyd gan ddefnyddio Map Data Cymru. Ar gyfer data misol (ar gyfer adar môr a morfilod) a degawdol (ar gyfer morfilod yn unig) dilynwch y ddolen hon.  Gellir adeiladu tymhorau (ar gyfer adar môr a morfilod) a Tymhorau yn ôl Degawd a Degawdau (ar gyfer morfilod yn unig) drwy gyfuno\u0027r mapiau misol perthnasol yn GIS  Er mwyn cael rhagor o wybodaeth am y setiau data a\u0027r dulliau, gweler adroddiad tystiolaeth CNC yma: 646: Dosbarthiad ac amlder a fodelwyd ar gyfer Morfilod ac Adar Morol Cymru a\u0027r Dyfroedd Cyfagos (cyfoethnaturiol.cymru)  Datganiad priodoli:     Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Set ddata wedi’i chreu gan ddefnyddio data gan y Sea Watch Foundation a\u0027r Rhaglen Ymchwil Ecosystemau Morol.\tModelled densities are derived from  predictive GEE_GLM modelling of sightings and environmental variables. See report for details.   Shapefiles are filed in the following folder structure: Maximum, Months, (seabirds and cetaceans) and Months by Decade (cetaceans only).  Seasons (for seabirds and cetaceans) and Seasons by Decade and Decades (for cetaceans only) can be constructed by combining the relevant monthly maps in GIS  Maximum maps represent the maximum density per 2.5km2 cell from the monthly data layers   Monthly maps are expressed as mean density per 2.5km2 cell.   File names are named as: NRW (Natural Resources Wales)_IRS (Irish Sea)_Species_month (numeric)_projection (WGS84).    Data in the shapefile consists of SP_ID (specific identifier), Species, LOW (5% quantile), FIT (density predicted from GEE-GLM model), and UPP (95% quantile) Quantiles are derived from simulations of the data (see report p32 for details).    The sightings and effort maps illustrate underlying third-party data and cannot be made publicly available. Underlying data can be requested from individual providers.\tEvans, P.G.H. and Waggitt, J.J. 2023. Modelled Distribution and Abundance of Cetaceans and Seabirds in Wales and Surrounding Waters. NRW Evidence Report, Report No: 646, 354 pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Maximum Numbers,  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Birds\\Numbers by Month,   X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\Maximum Numbers,   X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\Numbers by Decade,   X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Marine\\Cetaceans\\Numbers by Month.\tTom Stringell - Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Species - For Mammals.   Matthew Murphy - Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Species - For Seabirds\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-10-17\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/5304/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_marine_atlas_2023\t2025-11-12T13:28:49.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n19129\tNRW_DS125562\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.940Z\t410\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLichen survey of parts of Gwydyr Forest, Glyn Lledr, Gwynedd\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353632\tA survey of epiphytic lichens in seven areas of the Gwydyr Forest along the Glyn Lledr valley, including recommendations for forest management.\tField survey data\tLamacraft, D. 2023. Lichen survey of parts of Gwydyr Forest, Glyn Lledr, Gwynedd. NRW Evidence Report No: 701, Natural Resources Wales\tStored in the DMS - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FPAWS%20Glyn%20Lledr\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:43:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19134\tNRW_DS125570\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.299Z\t381\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status and distribution of the Strandline Beetle Eurynebria complanata on Whiteford Burrows, Pembrey Burrows, Laugharne \u0026amp; Pendine Burrows and habitat assessment of Baglan Burrows \u0026amp; Frainslake Sands in 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353730\tFollowing surveys in 2016 which identified healthy populations were established on the three largest beaches at Pendine-Laugharne this survey of E. complanata was designed to inform future management of strandline habitats, also to assess habitat condition of two potential reintroduction sites at Frainslake and Crymlyn.\tThe survey method was based on monthly counts at two key sites, Whiteford Burrows and Cefn Sidan, the latter being the single beach unit that extends from Pembrey Burrows in the south-east to Tywyn Burrows in the north-west. Additionally, spring and autumn visits were made to Laugharne Burrows \u0026amp; Pendine Burrows\tStewart, B. 2023. The status and distribution of the Strandline Beetle Eurynebria complanata on Whiteford Burrows, Pembrey Burrows, Laugharne \u0026amp; Pendine Burrows and habitat assessment of Baglan Burrows \u0026amp; Frainslake Sands in 2022. Natur am Byth! Report No. 009. 53pp. Buglife \u0026amp; Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tData held on the WID in Recorded 6 - folder titled Natur Am Byth\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-05-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-09T16:14:07.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19130\tNRW_DS125572\t2024-05-28T13:03:39.084Z\t1213\tValid\tEditable\tPriority Ecological Networks\tRhwydweithiau Ecolegol â Blaenoriaeth\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353732\tPriority Ecological Networks (PENs) in the terrestrial environment are versions of the all-Wales habitat networks that show areas of connectivity between Protected Sites, and as such provide a framework to inform the location of action to build functional resilient ecological networks based on our most important places for biodiversity.  Typical actions would be habitat improvement, restoration, or creation, located within the boundaries of a PEN, or situated at its margins to consolidate, extend, or link different PENs. These PENs supersede the GIS layers that were previously known as ‘Level 2 networks’, and are available for:   Native woodland; Semi-natural grassland; Heathland; Bog; Fen and Sand Dune.   All these priority networks are also represented as a ‘PEN Hotspot’ map, which is a simple count of all available networks within a given 1km square for an ‘at a glance’ view across Wales.    In addition, the ‘native woodland’ and ‘semi-natural grassland’ PENs have both received updates in the form of additional networks to  accommodate our latest protected sites series and survey information and expand beyond what was originally available. Heathland, Bog and Fen have been merged from their originally separate upland and lowland versions into singular datasets and a previously unreleased ‘sand dune’ network is also now included.  A Freshwater PEN is planned.   At sea, an ecologically coherent network of marine protected areas (MPAs) has been established which, when measures are in place to bring about good condition, will form the marine Resilient Ecological Network.   Connectivity within the MPA Network is inherently good, however some of the features (species and habitats for which the sites are designated) are known to be in unfavourable condition.    The Marine Priority Ecological Network (MPEN) map shows the full extent of the MPA network, highlighting the sites where one or more features are known to be in unfavourable condition. In the highlighted areas, action is needed to deliver measures that restore features, reduce pressures to enable their recovery, or gather evidence needed to support effective management.  NRW continues to gather and assess information about the condition of habitats and species across the MPA network.  Future iterations of the MPEN will incorporate new evidence and understanding as it becomes available.-Rhwydweithiau Ecolegol â Blaenoriaeth yn yr amgylchedd daearol  Mae Rhwydweithiau Ecolegol â Blaenoriaeth yn yr amgylchedd daearol yn fathau o rwydweithiau cynefinoedd Cymru gyfan sy’n dangos ardaloedd o gysylltedd rhwng safleoedd gwarchodedig, ac felly’n darparu fframwaith i lywio lleoliad gweithredu i adeiladu rhwydweithiau ecolegol gwydn a swyddogaethol yn seiliedig ar ein mannau pwysicaf ar gyfer bioamrywiaeth. Camau gweithredu nodweddiadol fyddai gwella, adfer neu greu cynefinoedd, wedi’u lleoli o fewn ffiniau Rhwydwaith ecolegol â Blaenoriaeth, neu wedi’u lleoli ar ei ymylon, i gydgrynhoi, ymestyn neu gysylltu gwahanol Rwydweithiau Ecolegol â Blaenoriaeth. Mae’r Rhwydweithiau Ecolegol â Blaenoriaeth hyn yn disodli haenau system gwybodaeth ddaearyddol a elwid gynt yn ‘rhwydweithiau Lefel 2’, ac maent ar gael ar gyfer y canlynol:  Coetiroedd brodorol, glaswelltiroedd lled-naturiol, rhostiroedd, corsydd, ffeniau a thwyni tywod  Mae’r holl rwydweithiau blaenoriaeth hyn hefyd yn cael eu cynrychioli fel map ‘Llecynnau Rhwydweithiau Ecolegol â Blaenoriaeth’, sy’n gyfrif syml o’r holl rwydweithiau sydd ar gael o fewn 1km sgwâr penodol i gael ‘cipolwg’ ar draws Cymru.  Yn ogystal, mae Rhwydweithiau Ecolegol â Blaenoriaeth y ‘coetiroedd brodorol’ a’r ‘glaswelltiroedd lled-naturiol’ wedi cael diweddariadau ar ffurf rhwydweithiau ychwanegol i ddarparu ar gyfer ein cyfres ddiweddaraf o safleoedd gwarchodedig a gwybodaeth arolwg ac maent yn ehangu y tu hwnt i’r hyn a oedd ar gael yn wreiddiol. Mae rhostiroedd, corsydd a ffeniau wedi’u huno o’u fersiynau ucheldirol ac iseldirol ar wahân yn wreiddiol yn setiau data unigol ac mae rhwydwaith ‘twyni tywod’ nas cyhoeddwyd o’r blaen hefyd wedi’i gynnwys bellach. Mae Rhwydwaith Ecolegol â Blaenoriaeth dŵr croyw yn yr arfaeth.  Sylwer: Mae’r haenau mapiau unigol hyn yn ddisgrifiad wedi’i fodelu o gysylltedd cyfredol rhwng cynefinoedd ac felly dylid eu hystyried fel rhai disgrifiadol yn hytrach na rhai rhagnodol!  Yn ogystal â’r set hon o rwydweithiau blaenoriaeth, mae’n ddefnyddiol hefyd gallu gweld yr haenau hyn ochr yn ochr â setiau data agored eraill sydd ar gael i’w lawrlwytho o MapDataCymru gan ddefnyddio’r dolenni isod:  Ffiniau Safleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig: Safleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGA) | MapDataCymru (llyw.cymru)  Ffiniau SoDdGA wedi’u clustogi i 300m WOM21 Byffer 100/300m Safle o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGA) / Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACA) | MapDataCymru (llyw.cymru)  Rhwydwaith Ecolegol â Blaenoriaeth yn yr amgylchedd morol  Ar y môr, sefydlwyd rhwydwaith ecolegol gydlynol o Ardaloedd Morol Gwarchodedig a fydd, pan fydd mesurau ar waith i sicrhau cyflwr da, yn ffurfio Rhwydwaith Ecolegol Gwydn y môr.  Mae cysylltedd o fewn rhwydwaith yr Ardaloedd Morol Gwarchodedig yn gynhenid dda, ond mae’n hysbys bod rhai o’r nodweddion (rhywogaethau a chynefinoedd y dynodwyd y safleoedd ar eu cyfer) mewn cyflwr anffafriol (Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru / Asesiadau cyflwr nodweddion dangosol ar gyfer Safleoedd Morol Ewropeaidd).  Mae map y Rhwydwaith Ecolegol â Blaenoriaeth morol yn dangos maint llawn rhwydwaith yr Ardaloedd Morol Gwarchodedig (glas golau), gan amlygu’r safleoedd lle mae’n hysbys bod un neu ragor o nodweddion mewn cyflwr anffafriol (ardal â chroeslinellau).  Yn yr ardaloedd hyn â chroeslinellau, mae angen gweithredu i gyflwyno mesurau sy’n adfer nodweddion, yn lleihau’r pwysau i’w galluogi i wella, neu’n casglu’r dystiolaeth y mae ei hangen i gynorthwyo rheolaeth effeithiol.  Mae CNC yn parhau i gasglu ac asesu gwybodaeth am gyflwr cynefinoedd a rhywogaethau ar draws rhwydwaith yr Ardaloedd Morol Gwarchodedig. Bydd fersiynau o’r Rhwydwaith Ecolegol â Blaenoriaeth morol yn y dyfodol yn ymgorffori tystiolaeth a dealltwriaeth newydd wrth iddynt ddod ar gael.  Ochr yn ochr â’r Rhwydwaith Ecolegol â Blaenoriaeth morol, gallai fod yn ddefnyddiol edrych ar y canlynol:  Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru / Asesiadau cyflwr nodweddion dangosol ar gyfer Safleoedd Morol Ewropeaidd: adroddiadau o asesiadau cyflwr nodweddion dangosol a gynhaliwyd yn 2018. Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru / Cynlluniau Gweithredu Thematig Rhaglen N2K LIFE: sydd yn cynnwys gwybodaeth am bwysau a bygythiadau allweddol sy’n effeithio ar safleoedd a nodweddion o fewn y Rhwydwaith Ecolegol â Blaenoriaeth morol. Mae setiau data agored defnyddiol eraill sydd ar gael i’w lawrlwytho o MapDataCymru yn cynnwys:  Ardaloedd Morol Gwarchodedig yn Nyfroedd Cymru | MapDataCymru (llyw.cymru): mae rhwydwaith yr Ardaloedd Morol Gwarchodedig yn cynnwys y canlynol: Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig, Ardaloedd Gwarchodaeth Arbennig, Safleoedd Ramsar, Safleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig a Pharth Cadwraeth Morol. Nodweddion Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACA) Morol | MapDataCymru (llyw.cymru): maint sylfaenol dangosol a statws nodweddion cynefinoedd Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig ar adeg dynodi safle.  Cydnabyddiaeth:   Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThese individual map layers are a modelled description of current connectivity between habitats and as such should be considered as descriptive, rather than prescriptive!\t\tX:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Priority Ecological Networks\tJon Rothwell - Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-09-03\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/4785/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_priority_ecological_networks\t2025-09-05T11:24:48.016Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19126\tNRW_DS125590\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.916Z\t540\tUnassessed\tEditable\tImportant areas for ponds in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353930\tImportant Areas for Ponds in Wales created as part of the Wales River Restoration Project and derived from UK Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology UK Lakes Portal.  https://eip.ceh.ac.uk/apps/lakes/\tInternal Only and not for external use. Contains CEH Data\t\tCurrently in -   Being moved to the X Drive under River Restoration Layers\tTristan Hatton-Ellis - Lead Specialist Advisor: Freshwater Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-06-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:07:21.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19124\tNRW_DS125591\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.583Z\t296\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRiver Restoration Project - Protected Area Lake Catchments\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235353931\tDervied from Freshwater Habitats Trust Project (Important Areas for Ponds (IAPs) - Freshwater Habitats TrustFreshwater Habitats Trust). The Important Areas for Ponds (IAP) concept was proposed and developed by Freshwater Habitats Trust to raise awareness of geographic regions that support ponds of national or international biodiversity importance.\tDerived Data that is for internal NRW use only.\t\tCurrently Stored at Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Tristan_Hatton-Ellis\\Lakes_Catchments\\Lakes Catchments.gdb\\Protected_Area_Lake_Catchments   # Being relocated to X:Drive under river restoration\tTristan Hatton-Ellis - Lead Specialist Advisor: Freshwater Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-06-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:06:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19125\tNRW_DS125601\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.547Z\t785\tValid\tEditable\tNutrients Review 2023\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363031\tOn the 1st April 2021, The Water Resources (Control of Agricultural Pollution) (Wales) Regulations 2021 came into force. The regulations revoke and replace the Nitrate Pollution Prevention (Wales) Regulations 2013. The objective of the new regulations is to reduce losses of pollutants from agriculture to the environment by setting rules for certain farming practices.  With the implementation of the new Regulations, at WG’s request, NRW appointed consultants (Arup) to undertake a review of nutrients in water bodies across Wales to develop a national scale understanding of the current baseline nutrient water quality and assess potential nutrient risks. The nutrient review covers both nitrate and phosphate, which are considered to be the key nutrients contributing to poor water quality.   The review has assessed the following water bodies in Wales:  · Groundwater; · Surface water (rivers);  · Lakes; and  · Transitional and Coastal (TraC) waters.    The methodology used in the review broadly followed the previous method used for the 2017 NVZ review (NRW_DS116240) but has been adapted to incorporate the assessment of phosphate. The methodologies represent a consistent and practical approach to identification of potentially polluted waters across Wales.\tThe methods used in this review largely follows the 2016 NVZ methodology. We did this to allow the results to be compared with those previous assessment of waters polluted by nutrients from agricultural sources. There are differences and these include;  · Phosphorous has been included in addition to Nitrogen. This gives a better understanding of the potential effects of nutrients in our waters as both are important in the nutrient cycle and both should be considered when looking at measures to reduce the nutrient inputs to our waters.   · Modelling has combined both agricultural and urban land use while previous reviews used them individually (land use type and individual inputs can still be seen separately in the spatial layers on the dashboard).   · More land use types have been used (previous reviews used a maximum 5 land types while this review uses 17).   · The same apportionment has been used across all water types (different methods used previously).  · Due to the anomalous nutrient results affecting some WQ data, orthophosphate and total oxidised nitrogen (TON) monitoring data from 2014 – 2016, that data has been excluded from this review.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T13:30:11.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19122\tNRW_DS125602\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.447Z\t888\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Review - Risk Maps\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363032\tSurface water and groundwater risk maps created by combining current and future interpolated nitrate and phosphate surface water and concentrations and the nitrate and phosphate leachate concentrations. Each component was assessed using a scoring and weighting system, and the scores were combined to generate a national risk map for nitrate at a scale of 1 km2.\tBroadly following the approach used for the 2016 review of groundwater NVZs, a GIS-based risk model was used to determine potential risks to surface and groundwater from phosphate/nitrate. The risk model combines national datasets of current and future phosphate/nitrate surface and groundwater concentrations and the leaching models. Each component is assessed using a scoring and weighting system, and the scores are combined to generate a national risk map for phosphate at a 1 km square grid scale. Areas with higher scores indicate potential risk to groundwater from phosphate. The risk model comprises three components derived using national monitoring and land use datasets. Each component was given a score and weightings were applied to these scores. The weighted scores were then combined to yield an overall risk score indicating the strength of evidence that the surface and groundwater is polluted by phosphate/nitrate from modelled land use diffuse sources. The scores and weighting applied are similar to those used for the 2016 review of groundwater NVZs. The GIS model was used to divide Wales into 1km grid squares. The weighted scores for each of the risk components were assigned to each grid square and summed to provide a total risk score to produce a national risk map for phosphate/nitrate.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Surface Water Phosphate Risk Map.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Surface Water Nitrate Risk Map.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Groundwater Phosphate Risk Map.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Groundwater Nitrate Risk Map.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:32:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19123\tNRW_DS125603\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.475Z\t317\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Review - Surface and Sub-Surface Phosphate and Nitrogen Loads\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363033\tDataset shows the predicted surface and sub-surface phosphorus and Nitrogen loads in transitional, surface water, lake catchment and coastal catchments.  based on a land use/land cover and associated loading rates (Kg/5m grid/yr).  Estimated amount of phosphorus and nitrates for each 5m grid square that enters the soil by subsurface flow.\tThe land use modelling undertaken was based on catchment scale assessments. For each transitional water catchment the InVEST Nutrient Delivery Ratio Model was used to map nutrient sources and their transport to water courses with the land uses, soil type, topography and rainfall and the phosphorus load coefficients from the Farmscoper and Lerner models as inputs. Each catchment was analysed at a 5m grid resolution. The modelling resulted in each 5m grid square being assigned a subsurface or subsurface phosphorus/ntirogen load (kg N/yr).  T\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Subsurface Load Nitrate.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Subsurface Load Phosphorus.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Surface Load Phosphorus.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Surface Load Nitrate.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:07:18.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19121\tNRW_DS125604\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.311Z\t510\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrients Review - Predicted Nitrogen and Phosphorus Source Appointments\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363034\tTotal phosphorus and nitrogen export from t catchments by surface and subsurface flow (kg/yr). Relative contribution of each source to the overall phosphorus and nitrogen load within the transitional, surface water, lake catchment and coastal catchment water bodies.\tThe final output from the modelling provided an estimate of the phosphorus and nitrogen source apportionment for each type ofwater catchment. The source apportionment data maps the sources of nutrients and estimates the relative contribution of each to the overall nutrient load in the water body.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Nutrient Source Apportionment.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:32:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19120\tNRW_DS125605\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.283Z\t197\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrients Review - Phosphorus and Nitrogen Source Appointment Percentages\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363035\tRelative contribution to total nitrogen and phosphorus load from each land use type in the transitional, surface water, coastal and  lake catchment (%)\tAs part of the land use modelling, a separate statistical assessment of land uses within each transitional water catchment was undertaken to provide additional understanding of land use and the phosphorus source potential. For each transitional water catchment, the GIS spatial data included a statistical assessment of the percentage contribution to the total phosphorus and Nitrogen load within the transitional, surface water, lake and coastal water catchment by land use type.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Nitrogen and Phosphorus Source Apportionment by Land Use Type (%).lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:34:58.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19119\tNRW_DS125606\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.133Z\t368\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Review - Leachate Concentrations\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363036\tThis dataset forms part of the NRW Nutrient Review of 2023. This layer shows the predicted nitrate and phosphate concentration based on total surface and subsurface nitrogen load  divided by hydrologically effective rainfall for   Surface Water Catchments (mg/l) Transitional Catchments (mg/l) Coastal Catchments (mg/l\tThe total nitrogen and phosphorus loading (total of surface and subsurface load) were divided by the hydrologically effective rainfall (mm, converted to l/5m grid/yr) to provide an estimate of nitrate concentrations (as mg/l) in runoff from diffuse sources that could potentially leach to waterbodies for comparison with water quality data.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Nutrient Leachate Concentrations.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:17:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19118\tNRW_DS125607\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.113Z\t309\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Review - Phosphorus and Nitrate Loading From Consented Discharges\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363037\tEstimated phosphorus and nitrate loading from consented discharges for sites with a numerical limit on phosphorus (kg/yr)\tData was extracted from NRW’s lists of discharge consents. The lists (a static WIMS dataset from 2016 and a live PALS extract from 2022) were combined, taking the more recent data where there were multiple or conflicting sources of information. As each discharge may have multiple consent lines describing different conditions, the analysis was carried out on the assumption that each consent reference represents one discharge. Duplicate sites were then removed where appropriate. CSOs and storm overflows were removed, as the consents do not generally give a good indication of discharged flows or loadings. An initial list of all discharges with a flow consent was created. This encompassed consents with “Flow” in the description (determined and codes 6425, 3527, 7782, 6426, 9827, 9843, 7435, 37). These were calculated to generate annual flows as follows:  • Dry weather flow consents were multiplied by 1.3 to estimate mean daily flow  • Maximum or total daily flows were multiplied by 0.5 to estimate mean daily flow  • Hourly mean or weekly flows were treated as means for those time periods, and multiplied up to estimate mean daily flow  • Instantaneous maximum flow was multiplied by 0.5 to estimate mean daily flow   Mean daily flow was then multiplied up to estimate total average annual flow in l/yr for each discharge.  Each of these discharges were then checked for nitrogen and phosphorus consents.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Nitrogen and Phosphorus Loading from Consented Discharges.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:32:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19117\tNRW_DS125608\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.092Z\t559\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Review - Groundwater and Surface Water Locations Scoped in and Out of Nitrate and Phosphate Assessments\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363038\tThis dataset shows NRW Nutrient Review 2023 surface water and groundwater monitoring sites excluded and includes from the nitrate and phosphate water quality assessment following data processing and application of data rules.  For the included data The current (2019) and predicted (2031) at each location is provided along with the method used to assess the concentrations.\tScoped out - Starting with the complete set of NRW groundwater monitoring sites, those which did not satisfy all of the following criteria and were not progressed for statistical assessment were saved int this layer. These criteria were: - Site has representative coordinates and is in Wales; -  Site has sufficient data so that a value of phosphate and Nitrate is available on at least 6 separate days for data between 1980-2019 (excluding 2014-2016).  Scoped In - Starting with the complete set of NRW surface water monitoring sites, only those which satisfied the following criteria were progressed for statistical assessment. These criteria were: - Site has representative coordinates and is in Wales; - Site has sufficient data so that a value of TIN can be determined on at least 10 separate days across at least 6 different years for data between 1990-2019 (excluding 2014-2016).\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Groundwater Quality Monitoring Locations.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Surface Water Monitoring Locations.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:39:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19116\tNRW_DS125609\t2024-05-28T13:03:38.072Z\t504\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Review - Groundwater Interpolated Future and Current Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363039\tFuture (2031) and current (2019) predicted groundwater quality for phosphate and nitrate to understand spatial patterns in the groundwater dataset and predict phosphate and nitrate concentrations at unmonitored locations across Wales\tThe current (2019) predicted groundwater quality layer for nitrates and phosphates was made by spatial interpolation of the current (2019) predicted groundwater 95th percentile nitrate and phosphate concentration for each of the scoped in sites. These values were estimates made using one of these statistical approaches based on the amount of data available, and range of years this data was across: - Weibull for the 2017-2019 data; a bootstrapped Theil – Sen using a 1.16 adjustment of the mean concentration for all data; a uniform value based on a 1.16 adjustment of the mean concentration of all data.  The future (2031) predicted groundwater quality layer for nitrates and phosphates was made by spatial interpolation of the future (2031) predicted groundwater 95th percentile nitrate and phosphate concentration for each of the scoped in sites. These values were estimates made using one of these statistical approaches based on the amount of data available, and range of years this data was across: - Weibull for the 2017-2019 data followed by a bootstrapped Theil – Sen for all data; a bootstrapped Theil – Sen using a 1.16 adjustment of the mean concentration for all data; a uniform value based on a 1.16 adjustment of the mean concentration of all data.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Groundwater Quality Interpolation.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:14:02.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19175\tNRW_DS125610\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.969Z\t425\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Review - Lake Mean Totals and Monitoring Locations\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363130\tLake mean total nitrogen and phosphate concentrations for monitoring sites between 2014 and 2019.\tMonitoring points were compared to the monitoring points used for WFD and SAC compliance assessments to get an initial list linking lake sample points to individual lakes. Monitoring points within the dataset were then spatially joined to lakes in the CEH lakes layer. An initial distance buffer of 100 m was applied to monitoring points within this proximity distance of lakes, assumed to be associated with that lake catchment. Sites outside of this range were excluded.  Monitoring sampling point types were assessed to determine whether they should be included within the assessment. Monitoring points on rivers and those draining away from lakes were excluded from the assessment. The total phosphorus and nitrate data was processed using a bespoke Python script to determine lake mean concentrations between 2014 and 2019 for each monitoring location. Monitoring data for the years 2014-2019 was selected for this assessment as a continuation from the years used for the previous assessment.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Lake Monitoring Locations.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:17:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19176\tNRW_DS125611\t2024-05-28T13:04:35.897Z\t258\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNutrient Review - Transitional and Coastal Monitoring Locations\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363131\tThis dataset shows NRW coastal and transitional water monitoring sites where dissolved inorganic nitrogen included in assessment following data processing and application of data rules.  Current (Winter 2019/2020) dissolved organic nitrogen WFD classification for each coastal and transitional water monitoring point to understand spatial patterns in the coastal water dataset.\tTransitional and coastal water quality monitoring data provided by NRW was filtered to include recent winter DIN results only for the period November 2019 to February 2020. Where sites had more than one WFD DIN class (Bad, Poor, Moderate, Good, High) across the period, the lower WFD class was included.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Transitional Monitoring Locations.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Coastal Monitoring Locations.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Coastal Water Monitoring Point Current (Winter 2019_2020) DIN WFD classification.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Nutrients Review\\Transitional Water Monitoring Point Current (Winter 2019_2020) DIN WFD classification.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn - Specialist Advisor: Water Quality Policy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:17:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19162\tNRW_DS125620\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.468Z\t26\tValid\tEditable\tNational Forest for Wales\tCoedwig Genedlaethol Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363230\tThis dataset shows the extent of the National Forest for Wales. The National Forest for Wales includes a variety of diverse woodlands with a range of ages, sizes, settings and benefits. All National Forest sites are recognised as good quality, well managed woodlands. Some excel in providing access or engagement with their community, some are recognised for the sustainable way they are managed, others focus on the benefits they have for nature. All play their part in helping address the nature and climate emergencies and providing a long-lasting woodland resource for Wales.  This dataset is periodically updated as more forest gets incorporated into the national forest through the status scheme assessment process. Data relating to the Welsh Government Woodland Estates sites put forward for National Forest for Wales Status will be gathered using existing data sources. Other land included from maps supplied by applicants is digitised from aerial photography and OS basemaps and verified using land registry documents. All boundaries and areas of sites are shown only for indicative purposes, are subject to change at any time, and should not be taken as legally binding or definitive in any way.  The National Forest for Wales team are responsible for enabling woodland site owners to become part of the National Forest for Wales network. The extent of Prospective National Forest for Wales sites will be mapped as polygon data, metadata will be gathered with relevant site details in order to inform whether they meet the outcomes necessary.     The extent of ownership will be determined through discussion with the site owner and when a site comes into the National Forest for Wales scheme this will be verified using their Land registry documents.    Contacts and events will also be logged as point data so that we have a record of the extent of engagement across Wales about the National Forest including relevant funding streams that the team can signpost to.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn dangos maint Coedwig Genedlaethol Cymru. Mae Coedwig Genedlaethol Cymru yn cynnwys amrywiaeth o goetiroedd amrywiol gydag ystod o oedrannau, meintiau, lleoliadau a buddion. Mae holl safleoedd y Goedwig Genedlaethol wedi’u cydnabod fel coetiroedd o ansawdd da ac sy’n cael eu rheoli\u0027n dda. Mae rhai yn rhagori o ran sicrhau mynediad neu ymgysylltu â\u0027r gymuned leol, mae rhai yn cael eu cydnabod am y ffordd gynaliadwy y cânt eu rheoli, ac eraill yn canolbwyntio ar y buddion i fyd natur. Mae pob un yn chwarae ei ran yn y gwaith o fynd i\u0027r afael â’r argyfyngau natur a hinsawdd ac wrth sicrhau adnodd coetir hirdymor i Gymru.  Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cael ei diweddaru o bryd i\u0027w gilydd wrth i fwy o goedwigoedd gael eu cynnwys yn y goedwig genedlaethol trwy broses asesu\u0027r cynllun statws. Cesglir data sy’n ymwneud â safleoedd Ystad Goetir Llywodraeth Cymru a gyflwynwyd ar gyfer Statws Coedwig Cenedlaethol i Gymru gan ddefnyddio ffynonellau data presennol. Caiff tiroedd eraill a nodir ar fapiau gan ymgeiswyr eu digido gan ddefnyddio awyrluniau a mapiau sylfaen yr Arolwg Ordnans, a\u0027u gwirio gan ddefnyddio dogfennau\u0027r gofrestrfa tir. Dangosir ffiniau ac ardaloedd o safleoedd at ddibenion dangosol yn unig, gallant newid ar unrhyw adeg, ac ni ddylid eu hystyried yn gyfreithiol-rwym nac yn derfynol mewn unrhyw ffordd.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tData relating to the Welsh Government Woodland Estates sites put forward for National Forest for Wales Status will be gathered using existing data sources.\t\tx:\\Physical\\Forestry\\National Forest for Wales.lyr\tStacey Delbridge, Team Leader, National Forest for Wales\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-07-09-2025-08-01\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/6676/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_national_forest\t2025-08-22T13:29:02.006Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n19177\tNRW_DS125626\t2024-05-28T13:04:35.898Z\t694\tValid\tEditable\tEnvironmental Permits for disposal of waste sheep dip to land\tTrwyddedau Amgylcheddol ar gyfer gwaredu gwastraff dip defaid i’r tir\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363236\tThis dataset provide details of permit details as required under the Environmental Permit Regulations issued for the disposal of waste sheep dip to land. The data comes from Natural Resources Wales Public Register. The published datasets only includes permits with an \u0027Effective\u0027 status; revoked, expired or surrendered permits are not included.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn rhoi manylion trwyddedau fel sy\u0027n ofynnol o dan y Rheoliadau Trwyddedau Amgylcheddol a gyhoeddir ar gyfer gwaredu dip defaid gwastraff i’r tir. Daw\u0027r data o Gofrestr Gyhoeddus Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru. Mae\u0027r setiau data a gyhoeddir yn cynnwys trwyddedau sydd â statws \"Effeithiol\" yn unig. Nid yw’n cynnwys trwyddedau sydd wedi\u0027u diddymu, trwyddedau sydd wedi dod i ben neu rai sydd wedi cael eu hildio.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe data is stored in NRW\u0027s Permitting And Licencing System (PALS) database with a process to create and update GIS layers weekly.\t\tx:\\Licensing and Permitting\\Waste\\Environmental Permits for disposal of waste sheep dip to land.lyr\tDave Jones - Senior Specialist Advisor Geoscience\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2026-01-08\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/5250/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_sheep_dip_permit\t2026-01-12T14:34:38.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19159\tNRW_DS125627\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.526Z\t343\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSkomer Marine Conservation Zone Management Zones\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363237\tThis dataset contains multiple layers used to recreate a map of Skomer Marine Conservation Zones with byelaws and codes of conduct zones for: vessel speeds, indicative seasonal breeding zones for seals and seabirds, sensitive areas for dolphins and porpoises, no anchoring areas and anchoring areas.  This dataset contains the following layers:  Skomer Inshore Protection Zone Skomer Nearshore Protection Zone Skomer Seasonal Breeding Zones Seals Skomer Seasonal Breeding Zones Seabirds Skomer No Anchoring Areas Skomer Anchoring Areas Skomer Sensitive Areas for Dolphins and Porpoises\tThe original Skomer MCZ Zone map was georeferenced and then digitised to recreate the layers used in the map. The artwork in the original map was done by Stuart Lloyd Associates.\t\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\\Skomer MCZ Management Zones\tKate Lock, Specialist (Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer) - kate.lock@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk  Mark Burton, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer - mark.burton@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t2023-07-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:50:44.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19168\tNRW_DS125630\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.800Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPembrokeshire Sea Cliff Vegetation Survey 1974-1975 - (paper data records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363330\tSea cliff plant community data, based on Dr A.J.C Malloch (Lancaster University) survey methodology.  Pembrokeshire coast was split into sections and plan communities described for each section.  Work carried by NCC.  Likely to be a small portion of a larger survey (the rest of the survey data is currently unaccounted for).  Areas of the Pembrokeshire coast split into Units 304-358, 361-400, 401-409.  May be related to NCC report (Malloch, 1971)\t\t\tAdd in here the off-site storage location/box number\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:36.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19173\tNRW_DS125637\t2024-05-28T13:04:11.020Z\t661\tValid\tEditable\tAssessing Welsh Aquaculture Activities\tAsesu Gweithgareddau Dyframaethu Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363337\tThe two AWAA Interactions Spreadsheets in Excel present the potential sensitivity of biotopes (components of habitats) and species (designated within MPAs, SSSIs or included within the Environment (Wales) Act 2016 Section 7 list) to the pressures associated with different aquaculture activities. The Biotopes Interactions Spreadsheet contains the information presented in the AWAA Mapping Tool.   The AWAA Mapping Tool displays the sensitivity of marine biotopes in Welsh Waters to the potential pressures associated with 11 different aquaculture activities. The Tool allows users such as developers, regulators and advisors to view the potential sensitivity of biotopes around Wales or within the vicinity of a proposed aquaculture development to the potential pressures associated with that activity.   Biotope locations have been sourced from the Marine Recorder (2020) and Intertidal Phase 1 Habitat Survey (2022). The sensitivities of the biotopes are sourced from the Marine Life Information Network (MarLIN) as part of the Marine Evidence base Sensitivity Assessment (MarESA) (Tyler-Walers et al., 2022).  Full details of data sources and acknowledgements can be found on the ‘Attributions’ button.   The data viewer was created by ABPmer Ltd for Natural Resources Wales.   The AWAA Project is supported by the EU through Welsh Ministers and is fully funded by the European Maritime and Fisheries Fund  Story Map can be found here: https://smnr-nrw.hub.arcgis.com/apps/73894fae28f64ed0b9b7344dcc667493/explore-Mae dwy Daenlen Rhyngweithiadau Asesu Gweithgareddau Dyframaethu Cymru yn Excel yn cyflwyno sensitifrwydd posibl biotopau (cydrannau cynefinoedd) a rhywogaethau (a ddynodwyd o fewn AMGau, SoDdGAau neu sydd wedi’u cynnwys yn rhestr Adran 7 Deddf yr Amgylchedd (Cymru) 2016) i’r pwysau sy’n gysylltiedig â gwahanol weithgareddau dyframaethu. Mae’r Daenlen Rhyngweithiadau Biotopau yn cynnwys yr wybodaeth sydd wedi’i chyflwyno yn yr Offeryn Mapio AWAA. Mae Offeryn Mapio AWAA yn dangos sensitifrwydd biotopau morol yn nyfroedd Cymru i’r pwysau posibl sy’n gysylltiedig ag 11 o wahanol weithgareddau dyframaethu. Mae\u0027r Offeryn yn caniatáu i ddefnyddwyr fel datblygwyr, rheoleiddwyr a chynghorwyr weld pa mor sensitif yw biotopau o amgylch Cymru neu o fewn cyffiniau datblygiad dyframaethu arfaethedig i\u0027r pwysau posibl sy\u0027n gysylltiedig â\u0027r gweithgaredd hwnnw. Mae lleoliadau biotopau wedi cael eu ffynonellu o’r Marine Recorder (2020) ac Arolwg Cynefinoedd Rhynglanwol Cam 1 (2022). Daw sensitifrwydd y biotopau o’r Marine Life Information Network (MarLIN) fel rhan o Marine Evidence base Sensitivity Assessment) (Tyler-Walers et al., 2022).  Cydnabyddiaeth:   Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tBiotope locations have been sourced from the Marine Recorder (2020) and Intertidal Phase 1 Habitat Survey (2022). Biotopes at EUNIS level 1 or 2 have been removed from both datasets.  Marine Recorder: Natural Resources Wales, 2020. Marine Recorder Sample Point Data (Spatial Export).  Intertidal Phase 1 Habitat Survey: Natural Resources Wales, 2022. Intertidal Phase 1 Habitat Survey. (Available from: https://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_intertidal_phase_1_habitat_survey).    The sensitivities of the biotopes are sourced from the Marine Life Information Network (MarLIN) as part of the Marine Evidence base Sensitivity Assessment (MarESA) (Tyler-Walers et al., 2022). Contains data provided by the MarLIN programme (http://www.marlin.ac.uk),    The sensitivities of protected species in Welsh waters are sourced from the Natural England Mobile Species Sensitivity Assessment (2022) and the Marine Evidence base Sensitivity Assessment (MarESA) (Tyler-Walers et al., 2022). Natural England, 2022. Highly Mobile Species Sensitivity Assessment  Data_March2022, dataset.    General AWAA Mapping Tool Rules are provided in the Tool under the ‘Guidance’ button which highlights potential assumptions, things to consider or limitations of the data presented in the Tool. The AWAA Final Report and the AWAA Activity Assessments should be read to understand how the data provided by the AWAA Mapping Tool can be used alongside the other Project Resources including the AWAA Dashboard and Evidence Database. The AWAA Final Report details the methods, assumptions and decisions that have informed the sensitivity of the biotopes.\tABPmer, 2023. Assessing Welsh Aquaculture Activities Project Report. NRW Evidence Report No: 726. , Natural Resources Wales, Cardiff.\tBiotopes (DMS file: ADVI-1932381820-54280):    https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/advice/mar/fish/Projects/AWAA%20Project/Task%2005%20-%20Interactions%20spreadsheet/FINAL_interactions_spreadsheet_biotopes_16062023.xlsx?d\u003dw20239fd02510433d8693a44239bae8c8\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1    Species (DMS file: ADVI-1932381820-54281):    https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/advice/mar/fish/Projects/AWAA%20Project/Task%2005%20-%20Interactions%20spreadsheet/FINAL_interactions_spreadsheet_species_16062023.xlsx?d\u003dwea1e3c443ae2413297114697497538af\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\tColin Charman - Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Fisheries\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-11-21\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/5767\t2025-11-12T13:27:35.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n19169\tNRW_DS125640\t2024-05-28T13:04:11.020Z\t230\tValid\tEditable\tFindings of drop down video and stills imagery from an area of drumlins off Northwest Anglesey, July 2019\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363430\tThis data contains the raw products of a seabed survey of an area of drumlins off north west Anglesey.  The images and video was taken by a Natural Resources Wales owned drop down camera system to provide a better understanding of the seabed structure and biological communities at the survey sites and to investigate the presence of non-native species. The data contains Video (.asf) (and still images (.JPG) The data was collected on the 11th July 2019. The a copy of the MR snapshot produced after data analysis by the contractor is also supplied here.  The analysis of the video and stills imagery followed guidelines provided by NRW, Marine EcoSol’s in-house standard operating procedures, and JNCC/NMBAQC recommendations. The video camera showed a swath of seabed approximately 0.3m wide, while still images showed an area estimated as about 0.13m2. All images were reviewed using appropriate high-quality imaging equipment capable of playing and displaying full HD video at 1920p x 1080p resolution.     The video and set of stills from each tow were reviewed prior to detailed assessment and recording, and the boundaries between biotopes or biotope complexes noted by recording the time and positional coordinates at which major changes in seabed and habitat type occurred. The species and substratum in each habitat ‘sample’ (tow section) was then recorded, and biotopes assigned.  Discrete habitat tow sections or ‘samples’ as defined above were determined using  a combination of video and stills images. The lower quality video imagery was then linked to the ‘sample’ in terms of data recording using the time stamp on the video playback. Higher quality stills images were used to identify and quantify substrates and taxa, with abundances pooled to assess the abundance of the taxa over each sample.\tThe images and video was taken by a  Natural Resources Wales owned high definition camera drop down camera system from the survey vessel Mersey Guardian.  It was deployed at a total of nine locations situated between 5km and 20km off the north west coast of Anglesey in an area of drumlin fields.  Individual tows ranged from 319m to 864m in length most being between 500m and 700m.  Depths ranged from 43m to 65m. Over two hours of video footage were collected together with 998 usable still images. Tow speeds ranged from 0.2knots up to 2.6knots, the faster speeds resulting in poor quality video imagery. In general stills image quality was good.  All data were entered into Marine Recorder v5.7 under the Marine Recorder survey name ‘2019 Draft NRW Northwest Anglesey Drumlin Survey’. Each tow was a survey event, each habitat section a ‘sample’.\tBaldock, L., Morris-Webb, L. 2020. Report on findings of drop down video and stills imagery from an area of drumlins off Northwest Anglesey, July 2019. NRW Evidence Report No: 401, 26pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\North Anglesey Drumlin Survey 2019\tLaura Grant, Specialist Advisor – Marine Ecology\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-07-03\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:31:01.048Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n19174\tNRW_DS125641\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.969Z\t290\tValid\tEditable\tEmissions of radioactive substances from non-nuclear sites - OSPAR Report Data\tAllyriadau sylweddau ymbelydrol o safleoedd anniwclear – Data Adroddiad OSPAR\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363431\tHolders of an RSR (Radioactive Substances Regulation) open source permit, including disposal to the environment, are required to report annually to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) emissions of certain non-nuclear radioactive substances.   This emissions data is collated as part of a UK dataset and is reported to the OSPAR Commission. OSPAR is the mechanism by which 15 Governments and the EU cooperate to protect the marine environment of the North-East Atlantic.\tOSPAR’s non-nuclear reporting covers:    • the oil \u0026amp; gas sector;  • the medical sector;  • universities and research centres;  • the phosphate industry;  • titanium dioxide pigment manufactures;  • primary steel manufacture;  • radiochemical production.    Not all of these sectors are represented in Wales.  The substances required to be reported are:   • Carbon-14  • Chromium-51  • Hydrogen-3  • Iodine-125  • Iodine-131  • Lead-210  • Phosphorus-32  • Polonium-210  • Radium-226  • Radium-228  • Sulphur-35\t\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/reporting/sr/Industrial%20Emissions/Non-nuclear%20Radioactive%20Substances\tToby Griffiths, Radioactivity and Industry Policy  Karl Lombardi, Radioactivity and Industry Policy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-10-24\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/5648\t2025-12-19T10:22:48.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19167\tNRW_DS125642\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.720Z\t671\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDee Conservancy, Dee Estuary (Navigation)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363432\tMaritime map data relating to the Dee Conservancy.  Showing Natural Resources Wales’ jurisdiction as the Statutory Harbour and Local Lighthouse Authority for the Dee Conservancy, including location and names of aids to navigation (buoys and markers) managed by Natural Resources Wales.  The data also shows the extent of the Port of Mostyn Harbour and the Mostyn Operational Area.\tData has been provided by the Dee Conservancy Harbour Master (on contract to Natural Resources Wales), and Natural Resources Wales’ Senior Dee Conservancy Advisor. Source material includes the Dee Conservancy aids to navigation asset inventory, Dee Conservancy Act 1889, the Dee Harbour Revision Order 2023, Mostyn Docks Harbour Revision Order 2016.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Dee Conservancy\tInternal Data Custodian: Dawn Beech, Senior Dee Conservancy Advisor, Marine Management and Advice Team, Marine Ops.  Dawn.beech@cyfoethnaturiolcymr.gov.uk Tel: 077468 742713\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t2023-07-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T12:03:51.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19161\tNRW_DS125685\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.595Z\t1201\tValid\tEditable\tCardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\tArolwg Gwrychoedd Ymylol Caerdydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363835\tIn order to further an understanding of and improve connectivity in the landscape Natural Resources Wales commissioned a survey of the hedgerow network around Cardiff in two areas to the North and South West of Cardiff. A total of 696 hedgerows were surveyed in November and December 2022 using a bespoke survey pro forma based primarily on the DEFRA hedgerow survey handbook.  This dataset compromises of a range of maps that are based on analysis of aerial imagery using AI.-Er mwyn hyrwyddo dealltwriaeth a gwella cysylltedd yn y dirwedd comisiynodd Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru arolwg o\u0027r rhwydwaith gwrychoedd o amgylch Caerdydd mewn dwy ardal i\u0027r Gogledd ac i\u0027r De Orllewin o Gaerdydd. Arolygwyd cyfanswm o 696 o wrychoedd ym mis Tachwedd a mis Rhagfyr 2022 gan ddefnyddio ffurflen arolwg bwrpasol yn seiliedig yn bennaf ar lawlyfr arolygon gwrychoedd DEFRA. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys ystod o fapiau sy\u0027n seiliedig ar ddadansoddi delweddau o\u0027r awyr gan ddefnyddio deallusrwydd artiffisial.  Dechreuodd yr arolygon ym mis Tachwedd 2022 a daethant i ben ym mis Rhagfyr 2022, sydd y tu allan i\u0027r tymor tyfu botanegol. O ganlyniad, cytunwyd na fyddai fflora daear yn cael ei chofnodi yn ystod yr arolygon ac er fod pob ymdrech wedi ei gwneud i arolygu dwy ochr bob gwrych, mewn rhai achosion dim ond o un ochr yr oedd modd casglu data oherwydd diffyg mynediad.  Mae’n cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Natural Resources Wales a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tSurveys commenced in November 2022 and ended in December 2022, which is outside of the botanical growing season. As a result, it was agreed that ground flora would not be recorded during the surveys and although every effort was made to survey both sides of every hedge, in some instances it was only possible to collect data from one side due to lack of access.\tWalsh, T. 2023. Hedgerow Survey of the Rural Fringe of Cardiff. NRW Evidence Reports Report No: 740, 83 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Cardiff\tX:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Barry and Cardiff Site Boundaries.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Barry Hedgerow Survey Results.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Barry Hedgerows.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Cardiff and Caerphilly Hedherows.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Cardiff and Caerphilly Survey Results.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Invasive Species Target Notes.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Photo Points.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Cardiff Fringe Hedgerow Survey\\Veteran Trees.lyr\tLiz Hancocks - Senior Officer, People and Places\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-10-04-2024-08-01\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6860/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_cardiff_hedgerows\t2025-12-19T10:41:06.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19155\tNRW_DS125697\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.468Z\t973\tValid\tEditable\tSlope for Landspreading\tGoleddf ar gyfer Taenu ar y Tir\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363937\tThe Slope to Landspreading layer is a dataset derived from height data. It provides a colourised visual representation of pertinent slope angles for the regulation of landspreading activities.-Set ddata sy’n deillio o ddata uchder yw’r haen Goleddf ar gyfer Taenu ar y Tir. Mae’n rhoi cynrychioliad gweledol mewn lliw o onglau goleddf sy’n berthnasol i reoleiddio gweithgareddau taenu ar y tir.  Datblygwyd yr haen goleddfau GIS yn bennaf gan swyddog Taclo Troseddau Gwastraff Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru yn 2020. Fe’i datblygwyd fel ymyriad rhagofalus i’w ddefnyddio yn ystod y broses o wneud cais i daenu deunyddiau gwastraff ar y tir fel ymyriad rhagofalus mesuradwy.  Mae taenu gwastraff ar y tir yn faes gwaith i CNC sy’n ehangach nag adennill gwastraff. Mae’r un cyfyngiadau o ran y goleddf sy’n berthnasol i daenu deunyddiau gwastraff hefyd yn berthnasol i ddeunyddiau nad ydynt yn wastraff.  Datganiad Priodoli:   Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a hawl cronfa ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Mae rhai o nodweddion y wybodaeth hon yn seiliedig ar ddata gofodol digidol sydd dan drwydded gan © Environment Agency\tThe GIS slope layer was primarily developed by the Natural Resources Wales Tackling Waste Crime officer in 2020. It was developed as an upstream intervention to be utilised during the deployment application process for the spreading of waste materials to land as a measurable upstream intervention.     Landspreading is an area of work for NRW that is broader than waste recovery.  The same restrictions regarding slope that apply to the spreading of waste materials also apply to none-waste materials.    The GIS layer was made available to all NRW officers for compliance work.  The ArcMap slope tool was utilised to create an all Wales layer of slope angles.  The underlying source data used to create this layer was the Environment Agency Integrated Height Model (EA IHM).  Due to landspreading restrictions applied in Standard Rules permits and Welsh Government guidance documents the layer was colourised as below:   • Areas with a slope of 7 degrees or less are transparent  • Areas with a slope greater than 7 degrees but less than 12 degrees are coloured yellow  • Areas with a slope equal to or greater than 12 degrees are coloured red\t\tX:\\Basemapping\\Height\\Slope for landspreading.lyr\tInternal Data Custodian(s): To be determined.   Metadata Point of Contact: Paul Challender, Lead Specialist Industry \u0026amp; Waste Regulation\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-11-24\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/5844\t2025-03-07T15:43:47.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19160\tNRW_DS125698\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.409Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tStanner Rocks National Nature Reserve meteorological data (paper data records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363938\tHourly and daily meteorological data from Stanner Rocks National Nature Reserve (NNR) was recorded from 1976 to 1979. It included solar, temperate (c), rainfall (mms) and hours of sunshine.   The data was captured in association with work of the Nature Conservancy Council.\tThe data was recorded between December 1976 and October 1979.  The station was located 52.2 latitude and 300 metres altitude.\t\tPaper records.  Note 16 November 2023 - Donna has kept these paper records in the comms room in Bangor for now just in case she finds more to add to the box. The paper data didn’t come from archives so there is no old number. She will give it a few months before transferring, when she does she\u0027ll get a number from Jill and pass it on.\tDylan Lloyd, Lead Specialist Advisor, Monitoring Strategy and Planning Donna Muirhead, Specialist Advisor, Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1979-10-15\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:43:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19165\tNRW_DS125699\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.650Z\t942\tValid\tEditable\tRed Squirrel Focal Sites Wales\tSafleoedd Canolbwyntiol Gwiwerod Coch Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235363939\tThis spatial dataset comprises the boundaries of Red Squirrel Focal sites in Wales. This includes the core areas and the buffers.    There are three red squirrel focal sites in Wales identified by the Wales Squirrel Forum (WSF) as part of the Wales Red Squirrel Conservation Plan 2009.\tIdentification of focal sites was determined by red squirrel presence, red squirrel population, the magnitude of grey squirrel threat, woodland type and size, suitability of habitat, landscape defendability, % of large-seeded broadleaves in buffer zone, landowner support, involvement of local community, management potential of the site and management potential of buffer.   Data quality was assessed using age of records and comprehensiveness of survey. Each of the criteria were assessed on a scale of poor to excellent. The WSF decided which sites are robust for the future of red squirrel populations. The 2010 data reflect the focal site boundaries agreed through the above process.   WSF can review and update the focal site boundaries as per the conservation plan.   NB the plan was reviewed in 2018, The Red Squirrel Conservation Plan for Wales, 2018. The 2023 data reflects changes agreed by the WSF.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Squirrel Buffer Zone April 2023.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Squirrel Buffer Zone December 2010.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Squirrel Focal Site April 2023.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Mammals\\Squirrel Focal Site December 2010.lyr\tChris Tucker - Specialist Advisor: Technical Forestry (Resilience)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-07-03\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:04.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19166\tNRW_DS125704\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.656Z\t592\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLichen Survey of Baron Hill Park SSSI\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373034\tA survey of epiphytic lichens on parkland trees at Baron Hill Park SSSI\tField Survey Data\tLamacraft, D. 2023. Lichen survey of Baron Hill Park SSSI. NRW Evidence Report No: 733, Natural Resources Wales\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Flich%2FLichen%20Evidence%2F0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FBaron%20Hill%20Park\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000AC4B9F9FA250F44C9BB6B984A0E01D87\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BD164C541%2DA53B%2D4F5A%2DA3A7%2D7DF81050108B%7D\tSam Bosanquet\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:17:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19170\tNRW_DS125707\t2024-05-28T13:04:11.020Z\t17\tValid\tEditable\tCompliance Assessment of Welsh River Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) Against Water Quality Targets Associated Data (2017-2019)\tAsesiad Cydymffurfiaeth Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACAau) Afonydd Cymru yn erbyn Targedau Ansawdd Dŵr (2017-2019)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373037\tThese data identify the compliance of Welsh river Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) regarding targets for seven water quality attributes: Dissolved Oxygen, Biochemical Oxygen Demand, Total Ammonia, Unionised Ammonia, Trophic Diatom Index, pH and Acid Neutralising Capacity. The targets and methods of assessment are set at a UK level by the Joint Nature Conservation Committee as published in the Common Standards Guidance for Rivers 2016, and form part of the conservation objectives for the riverine SAC features.  There are nine SACs in Wales with riverine features (known in their respective Core Management Plans as): • River Dee and Bala Lake  • Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn  • Coedydd Derw a Safleoedd Ystlumod Meirion (Afon Glaslyn)  • Afon Eden - Cors Goch Trawsfynydd • Afon Teifi  • Afonydd Cleddau  • Afon Tywi  • River Usk  • River Wye   For the purposes of water quality reporting, Natural Resources Wales (NRW) divides rivers into units called water bodies. A total of 119 (of a possible 127) water bodies, each with a single sampling point, were assessed for one or more attribute.   Water quality data collected by NRW’s routine surveillance monitoring programme for a three-year period (1st of January 2017 – 31st December 2019) were extracted from the NRW water quality database (KiWQM) to assess compliance with targets.    Data were not available for all attributes for all water bodies due to the risk-based design of NRW’s monitoring programmes. The majority of water bodies were assessed for more than one attribute. All data were subject to robust quality assurance processes. The data consists of two products: 1. An Excel spreadsheet summarising results and 2. GIS layers consisting of the WFD catchment area water bodies, clipped to the Welsh border and excluding water bodies not overlapping with river SAC boundaries.-Mae’r data hwn yn pennu cydymffurfiaeth Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACA) afonydd Cymru o ran targedau ar gyfer saith nodwedd ansawdd dŵr: Ocsigen Tawdd, Y Galw Biocemegol am Ocsigen, Cyfanswm yr Amonia, Amonia Heb ei Ïoneiddio, Mynegai Diatomau Troffig, pH a’r Gallu i Niwtralu Asidau. Mae’r targedau a’r dulliau asesu wedi’u pennu ar lefel y DU gan y Cyd-bwyllgor Cadwraeth Natur fel y’u cyhoeddwyd yn y ‘Common Standards Guidance for Rivers 2016’, ac maent yn rhan o’r amcanion cadwraeth ar gyfer nodweddion afonol ACAau.   Mae naw ACA yng Nghymru sydd â nodweddion afonol (a elwir yn eu Cynlluniau Rheoli Craidd priodol fel a ganlyn):  Afon Dyfrdwy a Llyn Tegid Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn Coedydd Derw a Safleoedd Ystlumod Meirion (Afon Glaslyn) Afon Eden - Cors Goch Trawsfynydd Afon Teifi Afonydd Cleddau Afon Tywi Afon Wysg Afon Gwy At ddibenion adrodd ar ansawdd dŵr, mae Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) yn rhannu afonydd yn unedau a elwir yn gyrff dŵr. Aseswyd cyfanswm o 119 o gyrff dŵr (allan o 127 posibl), pob un ag un pwynt samplu, ar gyfer un nodwedd neu fwy.  Cafodd data ansawdd dŵr a gasglwyd gan raglen monitro gwyliadwriaeth arferol CNC am gyfnod o dair blynedd (1 Ionawr 2017 - 31 Rhagfyr 2019) ei dynnu o gronfa ddata ansawdd dŵr (KiWQM) CNC er mwyn asesu cydymffurfiaeth â thargedau.   Nid oedd data ar gael yn achos pob nodwedd ar gyfer pob corff dŵr oherwydd cynllun seiliedig ar risg rhaglenni monitro CNC. Aseswyd y rhan fwyaf o gyrff dŵr ar gyfer mwy nag un nodwedd. Roedd yr holl ddata yn destun prosesau sicrhau ansawdd cadarn. Defnyddiwyd methodolegau casglu a dadansoddi data yn unol â chanllawiau safonol CNC.  Mae’r dulliau llawn wedi’u cyflwyno yn yr adroddiad atodol.\tData extracted from NRW KiWQM database. Data collection and analysis methodologies undertaken in line with NRW’s standard guidance.  All data were subject to robust quality assurance processes.  Full methods are presented in the accompanying report.\tFoster H., Jones T.G, Jâms, I.B., Hatton-Ellis T.W. 2024. Compliance Assessment of Welsh River SACs Against Water Quality Targets. NRW Evidence Report No: 729, 106pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/fe/Internal%20Research/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2Ffe%2FInternal%20Research%2FCondition%20assessments%2FSAC%20River%20Water%20Quality%20Compliance%202022\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120003484E0523A301C478CE56BB727C2A9A2\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BB2119901%2DA148%2D4F26%2D93EE%2D937B564A9233%7D\tHilary Foster - Specialist Advisor: Freshwater Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-01-11\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/5941/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_sac_rivers_project\t2025-03-05T15:46:56.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n19163\tNRW_DS125721\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.609Z\t3413\tValid\tEditable\tNational Receptor Dataset (NRD) 2023 and 2025\tSet Ddata Derbynnydd Cenedlaethol (NRD) 2023 a 2025\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373231\tThe National Receptor Dataset (NRD) is a collection of risk receptors, typically residential and commercial property, intended for use in flood and coastal erosion risk management. It is a spatial dataset produced from Ordnance Survey (OS) AddressBase® Premium (ABP) and OS MasterMap® (MM) data with information on property type, floor area and Flood Hazard Research Centre’s Multi-Coloured Manual (MCM) codes attributed.    The versions of the NRD described here for the NRD 2023 \u0026amp; 2025 created as part of the Flood Risk Assessment Wales Data Management (FRAW DM) process. These datasets of NRD builds on the same methodology as NRD 2018, with a refresh of input datasets.    NRD 2023 \u0026amp; 2025 comprises of two layers, they have been created from the OS AddressBase Premium dataset and from OSMM, which is created from OS MasterMap.  Both datasets should be viewed together, to give a complete view of the NRD datasets.      List of improvements from NRD 2018 to NRD 2023 \u0026amp; 2025 include:  1. Main input datasets have been updated, including; OS ABP, OS MM, NRW Flood Alert and Warning Areas, NRW Flood Warning Uptake, Defended Areas.  2. Over 52,000 Static Caravans are now included in the OS MM datasets, therefore these have now been included in the NRD 2023 \u0026amp; 2025 from OSMM Layer.  3. The building footprint threshold within the NRD 2023 \u0026amp; 2025 from OSMM layer has been increased from 30m² to 60m².  This is to remove unwanted features like garages being classified as “Residential” dwelling.  4. Ordnance Survey have significantly improved their classification of properties across Wales.  For example, end of terrace houses in some location were called “semi-detached” previously, these have now been correctly assigned as “end of terrace” in NRD 2023 \u0026amp; 2025.\tThese dataset builds on the same methodology used in NRD2018 and is derived from two sources: OS ABP and OS MM. Updates to both are released every 6 weeks. NRD 2023 \u0026amp; 2025 are a snapshot of a particular moment in time.    Both datasets have not been designed for addressing purposes and we don’t recommend using it for this, mainly because it may not be up-to-date. We recommend the OS ABP instead.   NRD 2023 \u0026amp; 2025 have been compiled to provide a general source of receptor information for FRAW and a range of other FCRM activities; you will need to decide whether it is appropriate for your specific application.   For local studies, you will need to check whether any more detailed local information is available, and whether it would be more relevant to the work being undertaken.   Please note that in NRD 2023 \u0026amp; 2025 not all records are for properties and may represent other features such as car parks, playing fields and electricity sub-stations. For further details refer to NRD 2023 \u0026amp; 2025 External User Guide and previous NRD 2018 documentation, including 2018s0011 - FRAW Project Report - Appendix T03 - NRD2018_Exclusions_v2.xlsx. and associated FRAW Project Reports.\tAdditional reference material available comprising: NRD_2023_EXTERNAL_User_Guide_001 NRD_2018_Reference_Documents It is important to note that both NRD 2023 and NRD 2023 from OS Mastermap should be viewed together, to give a complete view of the NRD 2023 dataset.  NRD_2025_EXTERNAL_User_Guide_001\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Risk Management National Receptors Database 2023 (NRD 2023).lyr X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Risk Management National Receptors Database 2025 (NRD 2025).lyr \u0026amp; X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flood Risk Management National Receptors Database 2025 from OSMM.lyr\tData Custodian: Dave Tarrant, Lead Specialist Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2025-09-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T10:20:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19172\tNRW_DS125722\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.910Z\t1033\tValid\tEditable\tKieco Marine Fish - Geospatial\tData Pysgod Morol CNC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373232\tMarine fish are used as an indicator of stock status, water and habitat quality. This dataset is made up of the species count and length values from the fish surveys conducted across Wales marine environment by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of fish in their natural environment for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  This metadata was created for the geospatial data for KiEco Marine Fish - for species data that is downloadable from the NBN please see NRW_DS125741-Defnyddir pysgod morol fel dangosydd statws stoc ac ansawdd dŵr a chynefin. Mae’r set ddata hon yn cynnwys y cyfrif rhywogaethau a’r gwerthoedd hyd o’r arolygon pysgod a gynhaliwyd ar draws amgylchedd morol Cymru gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru ac Asiantaeth yr Amgylchedd Cymru cyn hynny. Cynhelir yr arolygon hyn fel rhan o waith monitro parhaus o bysgod yn eu hamgylchedd naturiol ar gyfer gofynion deddfwriaethol ac i fodloni ysgogwyr busnes.  Defnyddir methodoleg lem wrth samplu pysgod morol. Mae rhwydwaith craidd o safleoedd sy\u0027n cael eu monitro\u0027n rheolaidd. Mae\u0027r safleoedd hyn wedi\u0027u lleoli mewn mathau o gynefin sy\u0027n addas ar gyfer y rhywogaethau pysgod priodol. Yn ogystal, gellir samplu safleoedd ar gyfer gwaith ymchwiliol neu ar sail ad hoc. Unwaith y bydd sampl o bysgod yn cael ei chasglu, cânt eu hadnabod ar unwaith yn y maes, eu mesur a\u0027u cyfrif, ac yna eu rhyddhau\u0027n fyw o\u0027r man lle cawsant eu dal.  Golygiadau  Mae rhywfaint o wybodaeth wedi’i golygu i sicrhau bod y data ar gael yn agored. Mae hyn yn cynnwys:  Data trydydd parti Data a gasglwyd ar gyfer ymchwiliadau parhaus Arolygon wedi’u canslo neu heb eu cwblhau Amlder Diweddaru  Mae\u0027r data hwn yn cael ei ddiweddaru\u0027n barhaus gyda\u0027r rhan fwyaf o\u0027r newidiadau\u0027n cael eu gwneud yn gynnar yn yr hydref. Yn aml, nid yw data o dymhorau arolygon yn cael ei fewnbynnu ar adeg ei gipio a gall gymryd sawl mis cyn iddo gael ei roi yn y system. Mae\u0027r set ddata allanol yn cael ei diweddaru\u0027n fisol i gynnwys data newydd, a gall hefyd gynnwys cywiriadau i ddata a gyhoeddwyd yn flaenorol oherwydd gwaith sicrhau ansawdd.  Rhybuddion Gwybodaeth  Mae data arolygon pysgod morol ar gael o 1997 hyd heddiw. Cafeat yw bod data hanesyddol yn ysbeidiol tan 2001. Mae hyn oherwydd bod data arolygon pysgod a gasglwyd cyn 2001 yn aml yn cael ei gadw mewn cronfeydd data lleol ac nid y rhai cenedlaethol (KiEco ac NFPD gynt). Mae arolygon pysgod yn cael eu cynnal trwy amrywiaeth o ddulliau arolygu ac nid yw\u0027r data o un arolwg o reidrwydd yn uniongyrchol gymaradwy ag un arall. Argymhellir y defnyddiwr i wirio\u0027r dull arolygu a ddefnyddiwyd yn y data a ddarperir. Gwnawn ein gorau i osgoi problemau ansawdd data, ond gan fod y setiau data hyn yn adlewyrchu’r data sydd gennym, gallant gynnwys gwallau. Darperir y data fel set o dablau data cysylltiedig oherwydd maint a strwythur y data. Rhagdybir dealltwriaeth sylfaenol o wyddor pysgodfeydd a chronfeydd data perthynol. Gwybodaeth bellach  Gweler y ddogfen ganlynol am wybodaeth bellach  Gellir gweld a lawrlwytho data\u0027r tacsonau hefyd o Atlas yr NBN. Mae cael mynediad at ddata\u0027r tacsonau fel hyn yn cynnig nifer o fanteision. Gellir ei weld ochr yn ochr ag ystod eang o dacsonau o arolygon eraill a gynhaliwyd gan unigolion a sefydliadau amrywiol. At hynny, gellir archwilio’r tacsonau drwy eu hierarchaeth dacsonomig lawn drwy integreiddio â Rhestr Rhywogaethau’r DU, sef y gronfa ddata fwyaf cynhwysfawr sydd wedi’i churadu o dacsonomeg ac enwau bywyd gwyllt y DU.  Atlas yr NBN  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tA strict methodology is applied when sampling for marine fish. There is a core network of sites which are regularly monitored. These sites are located in habitat types suitable for the respective fish species. In addition, sites may be sampled for investigative work, or on an ad hoc basis. Once a sample of fish is collected, they are immediately identified in the field, measured and counted and then released alive from where they were captured.  Redactions  Some information has be redacted to make the data openly available. This includes:  - Third party data - Data collected for ongoing investigations - Cancelled or incomplete surveys - Update Frequency  This data is continually update with most changes made in the early autumn. Data from survey seasons is often not enter at the time of capture and can be several months before its is entered into the system. The external dataset is updated monthly to include new data, and may also contain corrections to previously published data due to quality assurance work.  Information Warnings  Marine fish survey data is available from 1997 to present. A caveat being that historical data are sporadic until 2001. This is because fish survey data collected before 2001 were often saved in local databases and not the national ones (KiEco and formerly NFPD).  Fish surveys are carried out by a range of survey methods, the data from one survey is not necessarily directly comparable to another. The user is recommended to check the survey method used in the data provided.  We do our best to avoid data quality problems, but as these datasets reflect the data we hold, they may contain errors The data is provided as a set of related data tables due to the quantity and structure of the data. A basic understanding of fisheries science and relational databases is assumed.\t\tData is held in KiEco stored on the WISKI database - an FME process creates a GIS layer which are stored here:  X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Marine Fish Individually Measured.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Marine Fish Site Details.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Marine Fish Total Counts.lyr\tPaul Hyatt, Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer), Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-12-01-2026-01-06\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6211/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_marine_fish_surveys\t2026-01-08T12:27:44.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19153\tNRW_DS125723\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.109Z\t585\tValid\tEditable\tImportant Curlew Areas (ICAs) in Wales Map\tMap Ardaloedd Gylfinir Pwysig (ICAs) yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373233\tThe 12 Important Curlew Area (ICA) boundaries have been drawn up by the members of Gylfinir Cymru / Curlew Wales. Glyfinir Cymru / Curlew Wales is a joint working group between organisations that are committed to Curlew recovery in Wales, including representatives from the government, conservation, farming, and game management sectors.    Some of the original hand drawn maps were passed to British Trust for Ornithology (BTO) to draw, then passed to NRW to finalise. The others were sent to NRW to check. If they were correct they were uploaded to the Gylfinir Cymru / Curlew Wales web site. If they were incorrect, they were sent back to the originator.-Dr Mae ffiniau 12 Ardal Gylfinir Bwysig (ICA) wedi cael eu llunio gan aelodau Gylfinir Cymru / Curlew Wales. Gweithgor ar y cyd rhwng sefydliadau sydd wedi ymrwymo i adfer y Gylfinir yng Nghymru yw Gylfinir Cymru / Curlew Wales, sy’n cynnwys cynrychiolwyr o sectorau’r llywodraeth, cadwraeth, ffermio a rheoli helwriaeth.    Cafodd rhai o\u0027r mapiau gwreiddiol a dynnwyd â llaw eu trosglwyddo i Ymddiriedolaeth Adareg Prydain (BTO) i’w llunio, yna cawsant eu trosglwyddo i CNC i’w gorffen. Anfonwyd y lleill i CNC yn uniongyrchol i’w gwirio. Os oeddent yn gywir roeddent yn cael eu uwchlwytho i wefan Gylfinir Cymru/Curlew Wales. Os oeddent yn anghywir, byddent yn cael eu hanfon yn ôl i’r unigolyn gwreiddiol.   Mae\u0027r haen Ardaloedd Gylfinir Pwysig (ICA) GIS derfynol yn gyfuniad o\u0027r holl wahanol ffiniau ardal a ddarperir gan bartneriaid prosiect Gylfinir Cymru fel un haen gyffredinol. Mae\u0027r ffiniau a ddarparwyd yn wreiddiol yn gymysgedd bras o rai a luniwyd â llaw ar y system GIS, tra bo rhai eraill yn cael eu matsio’n ofalus â ffiniau gweinyddol/safleoedd gwarchodedig, felly dylai defnyddwyr fod yn ymwybodol nad yw\u0027r holl ddata wedi\u0027i ddal o’r un safon neu eglurdeb/graddfa.  Datganiad priodoli Mae’n cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Natural Resources Wales a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Mae’n cynnwys Data’r Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawliau Cronfa Ddata. © RSPB 2023. © Cyngor Sir Ddinbych. © Hawlfraint Parc Cenedlaethol Bannau Brycheiniog. © 2023 Ymddiriedolaeth Cadwraeth Gêm a Bywyd Gwyllt © Hawlfraint 2023 Ymddiriedolaeth Dyffryn Elan. © Cymdeithas Adaregol Cymru.\tThis map is published on the Gylfinir Cymru / Curlew Wales web site in PDF format.  It has been agreed by the members of Gylfinir Cymru / Curlew Wales that anyone requesting the map may have it as a GIS file.          The final ICA GIS layer is an amalgamation of all the separate area boundaries provided by Gylfinir Cymru / Curlew Wales project partners into one overall layer. The boundaries originally provided are a mixture of broadly hand-digitised ones, with others being carefully snapped to administrative boundaries / protected sites, so users should be aware that not all data has been captured to the same standard or resolution / scale.\thttps://www.curlewwales.org/\tY:\\Knowledge Strategy Planning\\Natural Resource Planning\\Biodiversity Fisheries Geodiversity\\Jon Rothwell\\Curlew 2021\\Final ICA.GDB\tData Custodian: Bethan Beech, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Species Recovery, Biodiversity and Ecosystem Resilience Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Curlew Wales\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-02-14\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/5944/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_ica_wales\t2025-02-04T10:10:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19164\tNRW_DS125726\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.626Z\t335\tValid\tEditable\tEryri snowbed bryophyte monitoring 2023\tMonitro bryoffyt gwely eira Eryri 2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373236\tA survey of snowbed bryophytes in two areas of Eryri along with setting up of monitoring plots for those species.  The two sites were Ffynnon Llyffant on Carnedd Llewelyn in the Carneddau and Pant Ifan on Glyder Fawr in the Glyderau. These sites were selected for survey as they were found by previous surveys to be the most important sites for snowbed bryophytes in Wales.\tThe two survey sites were visited between the 30th of May and 2nd of June 2023. A route was taken through each site to firstly, re-find previously recorded populations of the target species and, secondly, to investigate areas where further populations of these species are likely to occur, particularly where snow is known to lie late in the year. At each population of target species located, precise grid references were recorded, photos were taken and notes were made on their ecology, abundance and general health. Monitoring plots were recorded at a subset of populations to enable future detection of changes in the abundance and habitat of these species.\tHodd, R.L. 2023. Eryri snowbed bryophyte monitoring 2023. NRW Evidence Report No: 739, 84 pp, Natural Resources Wales\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FEryri%20Snowbed%20Bryophytes%202023\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet : Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-11-16\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-03T09:31:58.725Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19158\tNRW_DS125727\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.465Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBontddu Hydropower bryophyte monitoring 2023\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373237\tMonitoring of bryophytes within plots set up before the operation of the Bontddu Hydropower commenced 10 years ago and following an initial monitoring round 3 years after operation commenced\tField Survey Data\tCallaghan, D.A. 2023. Bontddu Hydropower bryophyte monitoring 2023. NRW Evidence Report No: tbc, Natural Resources Wales\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2FBryophyte%20Hydropower%20Monitoring\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19171\tNRW_DS125728\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.962Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAlgae Raw Data Sample Forms - North Wales (paper data records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373238\tThese paper data records are analysis forms of ad-hoc incident algal samples. They were taken in the area of North Wales covered by Environment Agency Wales.\tThe algal samples were taken by the Environment Teams due to a potential bloom or incident. They were analysed microscopically and the taxen and abundance was listed.\t\tPaper data records\tJenny Owen, Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer), Analysis and Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:56:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19156\tNRW_DS125730\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.444Z\t883\tValid\tEditable\tNational Peatland Action Programme (NPAP) Funded Peatland Restoration Activities 2020-2023 – GIS Dataset\tGweithgareddau Adfer Mawndir a Ariannwyd drwy’r Rhaglen Weithredu Genedlaethol ar Fawndiroedd (NPAP) 2020-2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373330\tThe total activities footprint for NPAP funded restoration work from programme inception in 2020 to 2023.   This layer covers both NPAP direct delivery restoration work and restoration delivered by partners across several NPAP grant streams: Strategically Allocated Funds (SAF), Section 16 Land Agreements and Competitive Grants.  The activities footprint is formatted as a shapefile layer, comprised of polygons representing restoration activities across Wales  Restoration Activity Footprint  This dataset shows the total area of peatland restoration across Wales up to present day. The data is presented as a polygon shapefile, with the unit of measurement being hectares.  Restoration Activities  This dataset shows the total area, measured in hectares, of peatland restoration that carried out across Wales up to the present date. The data is presented as a polygon shapefile that captures restoration activities. Restoration activities have been divided into five broad groups: Hydrological Management, Erosion Control, Tree Management, Vegetation Management and Grazing.  Hydrological Management aims to reverse the impact of harmful activities that have resulted in adverse changes to hydrological regimes.  Erosion Control aims to reduce the effect of erosional features such as haggs and gullies. These features expose areas of bare peat which are prone to further degradation if left uncontrolled.  Tree Management aims to reduce the impact of trees on peatland. Most Tree Management techniques for peatland restoration involve conifer plantation, there are also activities addressing the presence of broadleaf trees.   Vegetation Management aims to control the dominance of small numbers of abundant species (e.g. Molinia, Calluna) to ensure the recovery and persistence of mire vegetation.  Grazing activities aim to restore and manage peatlands through modifying grazing regimes.  The data is broken down into polygons with columns detailing the broad activity types, sites, delivery organisation, start and end dates of restoration activity, and the financial year it occurred in.  Further information on the structure of the dataset can be found in the-Ôl Troed Gweithgaredd Adfer  Mae’r set ddata hon yn dangos cyfanswm arwynebedd y mawndiroedd sy’n cael eu hadfer ledled Cymru hyd heddiw. Cyflwynir y data fel ffeil siâp polygon, gyda\u0027r uned fesur yn hectarau.  Gweithgareddau Adfer  Mae’r set ddata hon yn dangos cyfanswm yr arwynebedd, wedi’i fesur mewn hectarau, o waith adfer mawndir a wnaed ledled Cymru hyd heddiw. Cyflwynir y data fel ffeil siâp polygon sy\u0027n cofnodi gweithgareddau adfer. Mae gweithgareddau adfer wedi’u rhannu’n bum grŵp eang: Rheolaeth Hydrolegol, Rheoli Erydiad, Rheoli Coed, Rheoli Llystyfiant a Phori.  Nod Rheolaeth Hydrolegol yw gwrthdroi effaith gweithgareddau niweidiol sydd wedi arwain at newidiadau niweidiol i gyfundrefnau hydrolegol.  Nod Rheoli Erydiad yw lleihau effaith nodweddion erydol fel torlannau mawn a gylïau. Mae’r rhain yn datgelu ardaloedd o fawn moel sy\u0027n dueddol o ddiraddio ymhellach os cânt eu gadael heb eu rheoli.  Nod Rheoli Coed yw lleihau effaith coed ar fawndir. Mae\u0027r rhan fwyaf o dechnegau Rheoli Coed ar gyfer adfer mawndir yn cynnwys planhigfeydd conwydd. Ceir hefyd gweithgareddau sy\u0027n mynd i\u0027r afael â phresenoldeb coed llydanddail.   Nod Rheoli Llystyfiant yw rheoli goruchafiaeth niferoedd bach o rywogaethau toreithiog (e.e. Molinia, Calluna) er mwyn sicrhau adferiad a pharhad llystyfiant corsydd.  Nod gweithgareddau pori yw adfer a rheoli mawndiroedd trwy addasu trefnidadau pori.  Mae\u0027r data wedi\u0027i rannu\u0027n bolygonau gyda cholofnau’n nodi manylion y mathau eang o weithgareddau, safleoedd, sefydliadau darparu, dyddiadau dechrau a gorffen gweithgaredd adfer, a\u0027r flwyddyn ariannol y digwyddodd ynddi.\tData was collected in the form of point, polylines and polygons. NPAP staff and partners were required to submit GIS data to evidence their restoration work. ESRI shapefiles were created using the spatial reference system OSGB 1936, formatted using a template to standardise data collection.\t\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\NPAP Erosion Control 2020 24.lyr X:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\NPAP Grazing 2020 24.lyr X:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\NPAP Hydrological Management 2020 24.lyr X:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\NPAP Total Activity Footprint 2020 24.lyr X:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\NPAP Tree Management 2020 24.lyr X:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\NPAP Vegetation Management 2020 24.lyr\tAlice Whittle - NPAPA Data and GIS Officer\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-03-01-2024-08-22\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6258/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_npap_resto_activity\t2025-10-10T07:51:03.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19152\tNRW_DS125733\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.127Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe current status of the spiders Callilepis nocturna, Dipoena erythropus, Erigone dentigera, Gongylidiellum murcidum, Lathys stigmatisata, Maro lepidus and Marpissa radiata on selected Welsh SSSIs in 2021 and 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373333\tMike Howe To supply\t\t\t\tMichael Howe\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19154\tNRW_DS125734\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.380Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status and distribution of Broom-tip Chesias rufata at Morfa Harlech and Harlech Forest in 2023\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373334\t\t\t\t\tMike Howe\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:38:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19151\tNRW_DS125735\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.115Z\t233\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTimber Transport Forum Draft Road Categorisation\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373335\tShapefile (lines) derived from OS Open Roads dataset, for the purpose of categorising the status of roads for timber haulage. The Timber Transport Forum is developing this dataset with a view to improving communication and consultation around timber haulage. The present dataset is draft / in development, and road categorisation is currently in place for small areas of Wales only.\tBased on OS Open Roads dataset (2023), clipped by Local Authority area.   Original attribute data is retained. Three attribute fields added: County (self explanatory) TTFstatus (for general categorisation) TTF_Restr (to flag roads with additional access restrictions for timber haulage)\t\tX:\\Transport\\Timber Transport Forum\\Timber Transport Forum All Roads.lyr\tRob Marsh - Senior Officer, Forest Planning Mid\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2024-02-29\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:30:17.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19149\tNRW_DS125736\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.097Z\t51\tValid\tEditable\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey of selected areas of the Dee Estuary - 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373336\tThe report and GIS dataset present the results of a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey carried out in 2022 on selected areas of saltmarsh habitat and transitional vegetation in the Dee Estuary Special Area of Conservation (SAC).     Seven distinct areas of saltmarsh were assessed, from the marshes between accreting dunes at Gronant, southwards through Point of Ayr, Greenfield Flushing Lagoon, Bagillt Marshes, Flint Marshes, and Connah’s Quay.  Marshes to the most southerly areas included in the survey referred to as the Dee River Marshes which extend southwards to the Jubilee Lift Bridge at Queensferry.  A field based judgement was carried out on community assignment supported by quadrat samples and target notes.\tThe vegetation was surveyed in August and September 2022 by a team of ecologists who assigned to NVC sub-communities and mapped these communities onto aerial images.  A field based judgement was carried out on community assignment supported by quadrat samples and target notes.\t\tY:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Coastal Habitats\\Dee NVC 2022\\Dee_saltmarsh_NVC2022.gdb\tData Custodian: Heather Lewis, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team, EPP\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-11-27\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T14:23:26.962Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19148\tNRW_DS125740\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.112Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe condition of the aquatic element of the grazing levels invertebrate assemblages on Nash \u0026amp; Goldcliff SSSI and Newport Wetlands SSSI on the Gwent Levels in 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373430\t\t\t\tData to be loaded to the WID on recorder6\tMike Howe\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:09.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n19157\tNRW_DS125741\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.465Z\t349\tUnassessed\tEditable\tKieco Marine Fish\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373431\tMarine fish are used as an indicator of stock status, water and habitat quality. This dataset is made up of the species count and length values from the fish surveys conducted across Wales marine environment by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of fish in their natural environment for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:43:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n19150\tNRW_DS125742\t2024-05-28T13:04:10.026Z\t735\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Diatoms - Geospatial\tKiECO Ecoleg Dŵr Croyw Diatomau\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373432\tFreshwater diatoms (phytobenthos) are free-living microscopic plants that form a biofilm on submerged substrates such as rock/stone or large plants. They are used as an indicator of water quality because their silica shells are easy to identify under the microscope.   This dataset is made up of the relative abundance of different species within the sample from freshwater diatom surveys conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of river water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  A version of this data is available in the Darwin Core format as a CSV - Please see metadata here - NRW_DS123303-Planhigion microsgopig sy’n byw’n rhydd yw diatomau dŵr croyw (ffytobenthos) sy’n ffurfio bioffilm ar swbstradau tanddwr fel creigiau/cerrig neu blanhigion mawr. Fe’u defnyddir fel dangosyddion ansawdd dŵr oherwydd bod eu cregyn silica yn hawdd eu hadnabod o dan ficrosgop. Mae’r set ddata hon yn cynnwys digonedd cymharol y gwahanol rywogaethau o fewn y sampl o arolygon diatomau dŵr croyw a gynhaliwyd ledled Cymru gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a chan Asiantaeth yr Amgylchedd Cymru cyn hynny. Cynhelir yr arolygon hyn fel rhan o waith monitro parhaus ansawdd dŵr afonydd ar gyfer gofynion deddfwriaethol ac i fodloni gyrwyr busnes.\tStrict methodology is applied when sampling for freshwater diatoms. Diatom sampling is carried out as part of the routine freshwater ecology programme for a number of different reasons (WFD, Water Resources, ECN etc). These sites may be added to for investigative work, or on an ad hoc basis. Sampling areas are located with a larger survey area and must be representative of the overall survey area. A stone scrape method is the most commonly used method for sampling freshwater diatoms. However, where necessary alternative methods are used, for example in areas with no suitable substrate. Natural Resources Wales collect and analyse in excess of 350 diatom samples from rivers and lakes every year. Once samples are collected they analysed by NRW freshwater ecologists.  Redactions Some information has be redacted to make the data openly available. This includes  Third party data Data collected for ongoing investigations Cancelled or incomplete surveys Update Frequency  This data is continually updated with the most surveys undertaken in the spring and autumn. Data from survey seasons is often not enter at the time of capture and can be several months before its is entered into the system. The external dataset is updated monthly to include new data, and may also contain corrections to previously published data due to quality assurance work.  Information Warnings We do our best to avoid data quality problems, but as these datasets reflect the data we hold, they may contain errors.  There is currently an error within system which means some TDI values are calculated incorrectly and give negative values. This issue has been identified by our developers and will be corrected in the next system update.  The data is provided as a set of related data tables due to the quantity and structure of the data. A basic understanding of ecology and relational data is assumed.  Data related to pollution incident investigations or protected species information is not released as locational information of these sites may be detrimental to ongoing investigations or species protection.\t\tX:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Diatom Sample Metrics.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Diatom Site Details.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Diatom Taxa.lyr\tJennifer Owen Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer) Analysis and Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-06\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6009/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_freshwater_diatom_survey\t2026-01-08T12:32:46.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19147\tNRW_DS125743\t2024-05-28T13:04:09.754Z\t857\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Fish Monitoring Data - Geospatial\tData Arolwg Pysgod Dŵr Croyw CNC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313235373433\tFreshwater fish are used as an indicator of stock status, water and habitat quality, artificial obstacles to migration and river flow requirements due to specific life-cycle requirements.   This dataset is made up of the species count and length values from the freshwater river fish surveys conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of fish in their natural environment for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  For the non-geospatial delimited data format please see NRW_DS125358 \"KiEco Freshwater Ecology: Fish Monitoring Data\".-Defnyddir pysgod dŵr croyw fel dangosydd statws stoc, ansawdd dŵr a chynefin, rhwystrau artiffisial i fudo, a gofynion llif afonydd oherwydd eu gofynion cylch bywyd penodol. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys y cyfrif rhywogaethau a’r gwerthoedd hyd o’r arolygon o bysgod mewn afonydd dŵr croyw a gynhaliwyd ledled Cymru gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a’r hyn oedd Asiantaeth yr Amgylchedd Cymru. Mae\u0027r arolygon hyn yn cael eu cynnal fel rhan o\u0027r gwaith parhaus o fonitro pysgod yn eu hamgylchedd naturiol ar gyfer gofynion deddfwriaethol ac i fodloni ysgogwyr busnes.\tA strict methodology is applied when sampling for freshwater river fish. There is a core network of sites which are monitored on an annual basis, and also catchment-wide surveys on a six-year rolling programme. These sites are located in habitat types suitable for the respective fish species. In addition, sites may be sampled for investigative work, or on an ad hoc basis. Sampling areas are commonly 50 metres long and the full width of the river and may embrace different flow types and substrate. For Catch Per Unit Effort (CPUE) survey strategy the effort is based on a time measurement.  Electric fishing is the most commonly used sampling method, although alternative methods are available for different strategies, such as netting and trapping. Once a sample of fish is collected, they are immediately identified in the field, measured and counted and then released alive into the same section of river from where they were captured.  For many of our surveys we record an NGR at the site because this acts as a central/representative location around which we collect lots of samples. An NGR is then also recorded against each of the samples to give a more accurate sampling location.  Redactions  Some information has be redacted to make the data openly available. This includes:  Third party data Data collected for ongoing investigations Cancelled or incomplete surveys  Update Frequency  This data is continually update with the most surveys undertaken in the summer. Data from survey seasons is often not enter at the time of capture and can be several months before its is entered into the system. The external dataset is updated monthly to include new data, and may also contain corrections to previously published data due to quality assurance work.  Information Warnings  Freshwater fish survey data is available from 1974 to present. A caveat being that historical data are sporadic until 2001. This is because fish survey data collected before 2001 were often saved in local databases and not the national ones (KiEco and formerly NFPD).  Fish surveys are carried out by a range of survey methods, the data from one survey is not necessarily directly comparable to another. For example, the total fish count may be based on multiple or single run surveys or Catch Per Unit Effort (CPUE) surveys. The user is recommended to check the survey method used in the data provided.  We do our best to avoid data quality problems, but as these datasets reflect the data we hold, they may contain errors.\t\tX/Monitoring/Kieco\tPaul Hyatt Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer), Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-06\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6244/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_freshwater_fish_surveys\t2026-01-08T12:31:10.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19146\tNRW_DS161271\t2024-05-28T13:04:09.317Z\t492\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWINS Natural Flood Management: Constraints for Bog Restoration\tRheolaeth Naturiol o Lifogydd: Cyfyngiadau ar Adfer Corsydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323731\tThis dataset was created for the Welsh Information for Nature Based Solution or WINS project (Previously known as SCCAN) to identify opportunities to carry out land management interventions to increase ecosystem service delivery in a number of key policy areas. There is an accompanying User Guide to assist in the use of these datasets. A full list of datasets used and data limitations can be found in the project technical report. What the map shows: This map shows the number of constraints to bog restoration for natural flood management. Constraints to hard engineering solutions are not considered.\tHow the map has been created: To create the constraints heatmap for bog restoration, the number of different constraints coinciding in any one area has been calculated. The individual constraints considered for bog restoration were: - Areas unlikely to remain suitable for supporting bog habitats under a climate change scenario. - Areas falling outside of the catchment zones \u0027Gentle headwaters\u0027 or \u0027Headwaters floodplains\u0027. In these areas bog restoration is unlikely to have noticeable NFM effects This dataset was produced for Environment Systems Ltd under contract to NRW.\tData gaps and limitations: National Phase 1 dataset is likely out of date in many places.\tX:\\Resource Management\\SCCAN\\Opportunity Mapping\\Natural Flood Management\\SCCAN Constraints for Bog Restoration.lyr\tInternal contact: Russell Elliott, Senior Advisor (russell.elliott@cyfoethanturiolcymru.gov.uk)  This dataset was produced for NRW under contract by: Environment Systems Ltd,  Cefn Llan Science Park,  Aberystwyth, SY23 3AH      Telephone:   01970 626688 Email:  gemma.bell@envsys.co.uk          Website:  www.envsys.co.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2018-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-24T11:21:49.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19276\tNRW_DS161272\t2024-06-28T08:33:41.858Z\t555\tValid\tEditable\tDrinking Water Protected Area\tArdal Warchodedig Dŵr Yfed\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323732\tAny water bodies in Wales used to provide drinking water are designated as Drinking Water Protected Areas (DrWPAs). The designation aims to protect the water and prevent any deterioration in quality that could increase the required treatment of water to meet Drinking Water Standards.   Drinking Water Protected Areas need to be identified under the Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017.   This data shows the designated Drinking Water Protected Areas in Wales and is split into two separate layers for Surface Water Drinking Water Protected Areas and one for Groundwater Drinking Water Protected Areas. This aims to protect from over-abstraction and prevent deterioration in water quality that could increase the treatment of drinking water.   Within these Drinking Water Protected Areas, we work with water companies to identify raw water sources that are \u0027at risk\u0027 of deterioration, which would result in the need for additional treatment.-Ardaloedd Gwarchodedig Dŵr Yfed. Nod y dynodiad yw gwarchod y dŵr ac atal unrhyw ddirywiad yn yr ansawdd, a allai gynyddu\u0027r driniaeth sydd ei hangen ar y dŵr i fodloni’r Safonau Dŵr Yfed. Mae angen nodi Ardaloedd Gwarchodedig Dŵr Yfed o dan Reoliadau’r Amgylchedd Dŵr (Cyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr) (Cymru a Lloegr) 2017. Mae\u0027r data hwn yn dangos yr Ardaloedd Gwarchodedig Dŵr Yfed dynodedig yng Nghymru ac mae wedi\u0027i rannu\u0027n ddwy haen ar wahân ar gyfer Ardaloedd Gwarchodedig Dŵr Yfed - Dŵr Wyneb ac un ar gyfer Ardaloedd Gwarchodedig Dŵr Yfed - Dŵr Daear. Nod hyn yw amddiffyn rhag gordynnu dŵr ac atal dirywiad mewn ansawdd dŵr a allai gynyddu triniaeth dŵr yfed. O fewn yr Ardaloedd Gwarchodedig Dŵr Yfed hyn, rydym yn gweithio gyda chwmnïau dŵr i nodi ffynonellau dŵr crai sydd \u0027mewn perygl\u0027 o ddirywio, a fyddai\u0027n arwain at yr angen am driniaeth ychwanegol.\tSurface Water: The designation may be based on any WFD river or lake waterbody that meets the qualifying criteria as specified in Article 7 of the Water Framework Directive .  Groundwater: All groundwater bodies in Wales are designated as Drinking Water Protected Areas. These are sourced from Groundwater Bodies available in WFD Groundwaterbodies Cycle 2, 2015.\t\tx:\\Resource Management\\Water Resources\\Groundwater Drinking Water Protected Areas.lyr x:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Drinking Water Protected Areas Lakes.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Drinking Water Protected Areas River Catchments.lyr\tDave Jones, Suzanne Lawn\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-07-31\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6859/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_dwpa\t2025-11-10T16:47:12.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19283\tNRW_DS161273\t2024-07-02T08:35:13.664Z\t830\tValid\tEditable\t3.0 Otter TEST Data Discovery *** This is not a real dataset *** FULL FIELD EXAMPLE\tTeitl Cymraeg: 3.0 Otter TEST Data Discovery *** This is not a real dataset *** FULL FIELD EXAMPLE\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323733\tTHIS IS NOT REAL DATA.      • This is NRW\u0027s test dataset to ensure staff can refer back to a fully valid record to help themselves solve validation errors • It is also to ensure all elements are displaying and exporting to multiple XML schemas.  The Metadata is written to be compliant with the NRW, MEDIN and GEMINI Standard.   • It may temporarily be in public view when checking view formats for the public but will be removed later.  We have not addressed the issue with using mean sea level as a vertical datum.  It is recommneded that to be inline with MEDIN statdanrt Vertical min and max extents are converted to Ordnance Datum Newlyn Vertical Datum - we are waiting on encoding advice from MEDIN-NID DATA GO IAWN YW HWN.    Abstract Cymraeg  Datganiad priodoli Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata\tLINEAGE TEST.    Lineage element should be used to document additional information about the background or history of the data resource and can include data quality statements.  The element can include information about: source material; data collection methods; data processing methods; quality control processes and may be useful in determining it\u0027s fitness for purpose.    Any specific information warnings should be added here.\tADDITIONAL INFORMATION TEST.    Countryside Council for Wales. 1993. Taff otter survey 1992 and Tawe and tributaries otter survey 1992. Countryside Council for Wales Science Report Series: Reports No. 7.  312pp Natural Resources Wales.    Any reference to external information that are considered useful, e.g. report title use standard citation (see above for example), Standard Operating Procedure (SOP), project website.  This should NOT be the web site or service where the data is published.\tINTERNAL LOCATION TEST:   Archived as Project 360, Media #1590.   GIS layer:  X:\\Layer_Files\\Biology\\Species\\Example_MEDIN_Compliant_Full_Field.lyr.\tData Custodians \u0026amp; SME:  John Smith, Otter specialist.   Geospatial Custodian: Joe Bloggs, Geospatial Officer.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2020-12-16-2023-02-03-2024-06-30\tcreation-publication-revision\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/414/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_MAIN_RIVERS\t2026-01-12T12:25:02.837Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n19284\tNRW_DS161274\t2024-07-05T07:56:03.299Z\t1045\tValid\tEditable\tTranquillity and Place Wales Sound Environment Part 2 2024\tLlonyddwch a Lle: Amgylchedd Sain Cymru Rhan 2 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323734\tThe nationally consistent terrestrial Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place resource identifies the strategic and local resource in remote, rural, peri-urban and urban areas for use as an evidence base to inform policy intent, practice and provision for well-being benefits. There are 6 themes.   Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Sound Environment Part II delivers Theme 4, where natural sounds may be expected to be more prominent than noise and are appropriate to context building upon Part I to incorporate Welsh Government Strategic Noise Mapping for Wales 2022 Road and Rail.-Mae Llonyddwch a Lle, adnodd daearol sy’n genedlaethol gyson, yn nodi’r adnodd strategol a lleol mewn ardaloedd anghysbell, gwledig, ardaloedd trefol a’r ardaloedd o amgylch trefi i’w ddefnyddio fel sail tystiolaeth i lywio bwriad polisi, arfer a darpariaeth ar gyfer buddion llesiant. Ceir 6 thema.  Mae Llonyddwch a Lle: Amgylchedd Sain Rhan II yn cyflwyno Thema 4, lle gellir disgwyl i synau naturiol fod yn fwy amlwg na sŵn ac yn addas i’r cyd-destun, gan adeiladu ar Ran I i ymgorffori gwaith Mapio Sŵn Strategol Llywodraeth Cymru ar gyfer Cymru 2022: Ffyrdd a Rheilffyrdd.  Cydnabyddiaeth:  © CNC/NRW Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data’r Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif trwydded yr Arolwg Ordnans: AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawliau Cronfa Ddata. Yn deillio\u0027n rhannol o Ddata Digidol BGS o dan Rif Trwydded 2013/062. Cymdeithas Ddaearegol Prydain.\tThe rural analysis (50m resolution) covers the entirety of Wales, including those areas covered by the urban analysis, to allow for consistent comparisons between different geographies. The urban analysis (10m resolution) is in addition to this, but only covers the urban areas defined using the extent of the study areas mapped in the Urban Tree Cover data. Scores use a scale of 0 – 10, sounds that contribute to tranquillity getting a higher score, decreasing with distance from the source. Sounds that detract from tranquillity were given a lower score, increasing with distance from the source.\tGibbs G, Sims J 2024. Tranquillity and Place Sound Environment (Part 2). NRW Report  No: 736, 94pp, Natural Resources Wales.  Green C, Manson D, Chamberlain K, Grace I 2023. Tranquillity and Place Sound Environment (Part 1). NRW Report No: 660, 100pp, Natural Resources Wales  Youdale C Phillips J Trow J, 2023, Round Four Noise Mapping Project Report. 12694A-01-R01-01-F01, 250pp, Noise Consultants Ltd. Welsh Government.\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquillity and Place\\Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Sound Environment Indicator T4-2-A Low Flying Military Aircraft.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquillity and Place\\Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Sound Environment Road \u0026amp; Rail.lyr  X:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquillity and Place\\Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Sound Environment Combined.lyr\tJill Bullen - Lead Specialist Advisor: Landscape\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-05-22-2025-04-04\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/sign-in/?next\u003d/documents/6760\t2025-04-07T10:04:27.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19317\tNRW_DS161276\t2024-07-15T09:05:20.167Z\t510\tValid\tEditable\tDigital grain sizing of Welsh shingle beaches\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323736\tThis dataset consists of particle size analysis of five shingle beaches around the coast of Wales. The five beaches are (from north to south): Pensarn; Morfa Dinlle; Borth; Amroth; Leys Beach (Aberthaw).   Particle size information was collected using digital grain size analysis. Both an excel file containing the particle size analysis, images of grain size histograms and the raw digital data are provided.  The data was collected to enable assessment of suitable quarry sources of shingle for beach renourishment.\tParticle size data collected using digital grain sizing by CMAR (University of Plymouth Enterprise Ltd.) under contract for Natural Resources Wales.  Digital grain sizing method followed Buscombe, D. (2013), Transferable wavelet method for grain-size distribution from images of sediment surfaces and thin sections, and other natural granular patterns. Sedimentology, 60: 1709-1732. https://doi.org/10.1111/sed.12049  At each study site, digital samples were collected from up to 10 cross-shore transects with a maximum of 7 sample points per transect. Each of the transects had a minimum alongshore spacing of 50 m, and each survey point along the transect had a minimum cross-shore spacing of 10 m\tPoate, T., Hamilton, T., Masselink, G., 2024. Evaluation of Welsh coastal shingle and appropriate sources of nourishment material. NRW Evidence Report No: 756. 51 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Cardiff.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mcps/Evidence%20%20External%20Research/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2fteams%2fevidence%2fMarine-Evidence%2fmcps%2fEvidence++External+Research%2fShingleSourcesData\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000686B1A13B2902E43B637F5C8EB33B608\u0026amp;View\u003d%7b4CA932F0-0F0A-47A2-B5BF-FD4C292536A3%7d\u0026amp;xsdata\%3d\u0026amp;sdata\u003dOWhpVEVIbWZjM2lWNCtSRlA0OFZjTDNadFFicHVtN0NXVEovQUU3cVU0WT0%3d\tIain Fairley, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-04-11\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T14:59:19.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19318\tNRW_DS161277\t2024-07-15T12:55:52.058Z\t622\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: Lake Macroinvertebrates - Geospatial\tKiECO Ecoleg Dŵr Croyw Macro-infertebratau Llynnoedd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323737\tFreshwater macroinvertebrates are used as an indicator of water quality and Lake flow requirements due to the relative immobility and abundance.   This dataset contains site, sample, metric and taxa information from freshwater Lake macroinvertebrate surveys carried out  across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales since 1997 and onwards. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of Lake water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business needs.-Defnyddir macroinfertebrata dŵr croyw fel dangosydd o ansawdd dŵr a gofynion llif y Llyn oherwydd eu disymudedd a\u0027u helaethrwydd cymharol. Mae’r set ddata hon yn cynnwys gwybodaeth am safle, sampl, metrigau a thacsonau o arolygon macroinfertebrata llynnoedd dŵr croyw a gynhaliwyd ledled Cymru gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru ac Asiantaeth yr Amgylchedd Cymru gynt ers 1997 ac ymlaen. Cynhelir yr arolygon hyn fel rhan o waith monitro parhaus o ansawdd dŵr llynnoedd ar gyfer gofynion deddfwriaethol ac i fodloni ysgogwyr busnes.  Cesglir data infertebratau llynnoedd gan ddefnyddio gwahanol ddulliau samplu. Mae samplau cic creaduriaid infertebratau llyn yn defnyddio\u0027r un dechneg tair munud ag mewn afonydd ac fe\u0027u defnyddir fel rhan o\u0027r broses y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr i asesu asideiddio mewn llynnoedd. Techneg Cironomid Exuviae (CPET) sy’n asesu poblogaeth Cironomidau yn y llyn i helpu i asesu maetholion ac amodau pH yn y corff dŵr.  Defnyddir methodoleg gaeth wrth samplu am facroinfertebratau llyn. Mae rhwydwaith craidd o safleoedd sy\u0027n cael eu monitro ar raglen dreigl tair blynedd. Mae\u0027r safleoedd hyn wedi\u0027u lleoli lle mae asesiad risg wedi dangos risg sylweddol o fethiant safonau ansawdd ecolegol y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr. Yn ogystal, gellir samplu safleoedd ar gyfer gwaith ymchwiliol neu ar sail ad hoc. Mae ardaloedd samplu wedi\u0027u lleoli gydag ardal arolwg fwy ac mae\u0027n rhaid iddynt fod yn gynrychioliadol o\u0027r ardal arolwg gyffredinol. Unwaith y bydd samplau wedi\u0027u casglu, byddant yn cael eu hanfon at labordai ecoleg CNC i\u0027w hadnabod gan ddefnyddio methodolegau safonol, gan ddefnyddio gweithdrefn Rheoli Ansawdd Dadansoddol llym.  Unwaith y bydd y data wedi\u0027i goladu yn y gronfa ddata ganolog, mae wedi\u0027i echdynnu a\u0027i sicrhau cyn ei gyhoeddi. Mae\u0027n bwysig nodi y bydd y set ddata hon yn cynnwys rhai digwyddiadau samplu o\u0027r misoedd diwethaf lle mae samplu wedi\u0027i wneud ond nad yw dadansoddiad labordy wedi\u0027i gwblhau, felly ni fydd y cyflenwad llawn o dacsonau ar gyfer y digwyddiad samplu hwnnw\u0027n cael ei lanlwytho eto. Bydd y cofnodion hyn yn cael eu cwblhau wrth lanlwytho data yn y dyfodol o CNC i Atlas y Rhwydwaith Bioamrywiaeth Cenedlaethol. Mae\u0027r set ddata hon hefyd yn cynnwys cofnodion ar gyfer rhai rhywogaethau nad ydynt yn macroinfertebratau, megis adar a physgod, a welwyd neu a gasglwyd yn ystod y samplu. Cafodd unrhyw organebau nad oeddent yn darged a gasglwyd yn ystod y samplu eu dychwelyd ar unwaith i\u0027r llyn.  Golygiadau Mae rhywfaint o wybodaeth wedi’i golygu i sicrhau bod y data ar gael yn agored. Mae hyn yn cynnwys:  Data trydydd parti Data a gasglwyd ar gyfer ymchwiliadau parhaus Arolygon wedi’u canslo neu heb eu cwblhau Lleoliad rhywogaethau a warchodir lle gallai rhyddhau manylion eu lleoliad gael effaith andwyol ee misglod perlog yr afon. Amlder Diweddaru Mae\u0027r data hwn yn cael ei ddiweddaru\u0027n barhaus. Cymerir samplau CPET rhwng mis Ebrill a mis Hydref (4 sampl wedi\u0027u gwasgaru\u0027n gyfartal) a chymerir samplau cic ar y llyn yn y gwanwyn yn unig (Mawrth-Mai). Yn aml, nid yw data o dymhorau arolygon yn cael eu mewnbynnu ar adeg eu cipio a gall fod sawl mis cyn iddo gael ei roi yn y system. Mae’r set ddata allanol yn cael ei diweddaru yn fisol i gynnwys data newydd, a gall hefyd gynnwys cywiriadau i ddata a gyhoeddwyd yn flaenorol oherwydd gwaith sicrhau ansawdd.  Rhybuddion Gwybodaeth Gwnawn ein gorau i osgoi problemau ansawdd data, ond gan fod y setiau data hyn yn adlewyrchu’r data sydd gennym, gallant gynnwys gwallau. Darperir y data fel set o dablau data cysylltiedig oherwydd maint a strwythur y data. Tybir bod dealltwriaeth sylfaenol o ecoleg a data perthynol yn bodoli. Nid yw data sy\u0027n ymwneud ag ymchwiliadau i achosion o lygredd neu wybodaeth am rywogaethau a warchodir yn cael ei ryddhau oherwydd gallai gwybodaeth am leoliad y safleoedd hyn fod yn niweidiol i ymchwiliadau parhaus neu warchod rhywogaethau. Nid oes gan bob safle ddata sylfaen amgylcheddol wedi\u0027i restru. Datganiad priodoli Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tLake invertebrate data is collected using different sampling methods. Lake invertebrate kick samples use the same 3-minute technique as in rivers and are used as part of the WFD process to assess acidification within lakes. Chironomid Exuviae Technique (CPET) which assesses the population of Chironomids within the lake to help assess nutrient and pH conditions within the waterbody.  A strict methodology is applied when sampling for freshwater Lake macroinvertebrates. There is a core network of sites which are monitored on a three-yearly rolling programme. These sites are located where risk assessment has shown significant risk of failure of WFD ecological quality standards. In addition sites may be sampled for investigative work, or on an ad hoc basis. Sampling areas are located with a larger survey area and must be representative of the overall survey area. Once samples are collected they are sent NRW ecology laboratories for identification using standard methodologies, employing a strict Analytical Quality Control (AQC) procedure.  Once the data is collated in the central database, it has then been extracted and QA\u0027d before being published. It is important to note that this dataset will include some sampling events from recent months where sampling has been undertaken but laboratory analysis has not been completed, therefore the full complement of taxa for that sampling event will not yet be uploaded. These records will be completed in future data uploads from NRW to the NBN Atlas. This dataset also includes records for some non-macroinvertebrate species, such as birds and fish, that were either observed or collected during sampling. Any non-target organisms collected during sampling were immediately returned to the Lake.\t\tData Held in Wiski-KiEco - a FME Report has been created to export this data to the GIS.  X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Lake Macro-invertebrates Sample Metric.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Lake Macro-invertebrates Site Detail.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater Lake Macro-invertebrates Taxa.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Jennifer Owen, Senior Environmental Assessment Officer, NW Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-06\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6713/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_lake_macroinvertebrates\t2026-01-08T12:29:24.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19320\tNRW_DS161278\t2024-07-15T14:26:12.215Z\t1070\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: River Macroinvertebrates - Geospatial\tKiECO Ecoleg Dŵr Croyw Macroinfertebratau afonydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323738\tFreshwater macroinvertebrates are used as an indicator of water quality and river flow requirements due to the relative immobility and abundance.   This dataset contains site, sample, metric and taxa information from freshwater river macroinvertebrate surveys carried out  across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales since 1978 and onwards. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of river water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.  A version of this data is available in a Darwin Core format, please see NRW_DS122460 for more information.-Defnyddir macroinfertebratau dŵr croyw fel dangosydd o ansawdd dŵr a gofynion llif afonydd oherwydd eu disymudedd cymharol a’u helaethrwydd. Mae’r set ddata hon yn cynnwys gwybodaeth am safle, sampl, metrigau a thacsonau o arolygon macroinfertebratau afonydd dŵr croyw a gynhaliwyd ledled Cymru gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru ac Asiantaeth yr Amgylchedd Cymru gynt ers 1978 ac ymlaen. Cynhelir yr arolygon hyn fel rhan o waith monitro parhaus o ansawdd dŵr afonydd ar gyfer gofynion deddfwriaethol ac i fodloni ysgogwyr busnes.\tA strict methodology is applied when sampling for freshwater river macroinvertebrates. There is a core network of sites which are monitored on a three-yearly rolling programme. These sites are located where risk assessment has shown significant risk of failure of WFD ecological quality standards. In addition sites may be sampled for investigative work, or on an ad hoc basis. Sampling areas are located with a larger survey area and must be representative of the overall survey area. A three-minute kick-sample is the most commonly used sampling method, although where necessary alternative methods are used, for example in deeper water or for investigative work. Once samples are collected they are sent NRW ecology laboratories for identification using standard methodologies, employing a strict Analytical Quality Control (AQC) procedure.  Once the data is collated in the central database, it has then been extracted and QA\u0027d before being published. It is important to note that this dataset will include some sampling events from recent months where sampling has been undertaken but laboratory analysis has not been completed, therefore the full complement of taxa for that sampling event will not yet be uploaded. These records will be completed in future data uploads from NRW to the NBN Atlas. This dataset also includes records for some non-macroinvertebrate species, such as birds and fish, that were either observed or collected during sampling. Any non-target organisms collected during sampling were immediately returned to the river.  Redactions Some information has be redacted to make the data openly available. This includes:  - Third party data - Data collected for ongoing investigations - Cancelled or incomplete surveys - Location of protected species where releasing details of their location could have a detrimental effect e.g. freshwater pearl mussels.  Update Frequency This data is continually updated with the most surveys undertaken in the spring and autumn. Data from survey seasons is often not enter at the time of capture and can be several months before its is entered into the system. The external dataset is updated monthly to include new data, and may also contain corrections to previously published data due to quality assurance work.  Information Warnings We do our best to avoid data quality problems, but as these datasets reflect the data we hold, they may contain errors The data is provided as a set of related data tables due to the quantity and structure of the data. A basic understanding of ecology and relational data is assumed.  Data related to pollution incident investigations or protected species information is not released as locational information of these sites may be detrimental to ongoing investigations or species protection.  Not all sites have environmental base data listed.  Much of the data collected prior to 2002 was recorded using a logarithmic scale 3\u003dA, 33 \u003d B etc.  Prior to 2012, most taxa were only recorded to family level, we moved to mixed-taxon level at this time. For this reason, some early samples may show blanks for some of the indices which required a higher level of taxonomic detail eg. LIFE species.\t\tData Held in KiEco - a FME Report has been created to export this data to the GIS.   X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macro-invertebrates Taxa.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macro-invertebrates Site Detail.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macro-invertebrates Sample Metric.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Jennifer Owen Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer) Analysis and Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-06\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6703/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_river_macroinvertebrates\t2026-01-08T10:33:18.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19331\tNRW_DS161279\t2024-07-16T11:39:04.105Z\t414\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Freshwater Ecology: River Macrophytes - Geospatial\tKiECO Ecoleg Dŵr Croyw Macroffytau Afonydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323739\tMacrophytes (water plants visible to the naked eye) can indicate the impact of pressures such as increased nutrients, flow pressures, channel alterations etc. The species present and their abundance give an indication as to the health of a river.   This dataset is made up of  site information, sample metrics and taxa details for each survey conducted across Wales by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales since 1993. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring of river water quality for legislative requirements and to meet business drivers.-Mae macroffytau (planhigion dŵr sy’n weladwy i’r llygad noeth) yn gallu dynodi effaith pwysau fel mwy o faetholion, pwysau llif, newidiadau sianel ac ati. Mae\u0027r rhywogaethau sy\u0027n bresennol a\u0027u helaethrwydd yn rhoi arwydd i ni am iechyd afon. Mae’r set ddata hon yn cynnwys gwybodaeth safle, metrigau sampl a manylion tacsonau ar gyfer pob arolwg a gynhaliwyd ledled Cymru gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru ac Asiantaeth yr Amgylchedd Cymru gynt ers 1993. Mae\u0027r arolygon hyn yn cael eu cynnal fel rhan o\u0027r gwaith parhaus o fonitro ansawdd dŵr afonydd ar gyfer gofynion deddfwriaethol ac i fodloni ysgogwyr busnes.\tA strict methodology is applied when sampling for freshwater macrophytes. There is a core network of sites which are monitoring on a three-year rolling programme. These sites are added to for investigative work, or on an ad hoc basis. Sampling areas are located with a larger survey area and must be representative of the overall survey area. A standard cover-class method is the most commonly used method for sampling freshwater macrophytes. Macrophyte species are identified on site and confirmed in the laboratory if necessary.  Redactions Some information has be redacted to make the data openly available. This includes: - Third party data - Data collected for ongoing investigations - Cancelled or incomplete surveys - Taxa that falls within NRWs policy on Ecological data exempt from general release.  Update Frequency This data is continually updated with the most surveys undertaken in the summer period (June to September). Data from survey seasons is entered as soon as possible following the surveys. The external dataset is updated monthly to include new data, and may also contain corrections to previously published data due to quality assurance work.  Information Warnings We do our best to avoid data quality problems, but as these datasets reflect the data we hold, they may contain errors The data is provided as a set of related data tables due to the quantity and structure of the data. A basic understanding of ecology and relational data is assumed.  Data related to pollution incident investigations or protected species information is not released as locational information of these sites may be detrimental to ongoing investigations or species protection.\t\tData Held in KiEco - a FME Report has been created to export this data to the GIS.   X:drive locations   X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macrophyte Sample Metric.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macrophyte Site Detail.lyr X:\\Monitoring\\KiEco\\KiEco Freshwater River Macrophyte Taxa.lyr\tJennifer Owen - Senior Officer (Senior Environmental Assessment Officer), Analysis and Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2026-01-06\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6715/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_river_macrophyte\t2026-01-08T10:23:18.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19396\tNRW_DS161281\t2024-08-01T08:13:34.915Z\t18\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecies License Mitigation Sites 1994\tCopy of template Templed ar gyfer Dataset Metadata mewn fformat NRW 1.0 created at 2024-08-01T09:13:34.915+01:00[Europe/London]\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323831\tThe database lists the spatial locations of protected species mitigation sites throughout Wales. These sites were proposed by applicants for licences to take or disturb protected species or damage or destroy their breeding sites , resting places or places of shelter for the purposes of demonstrating their applications would not be detrimental to the maintenance of the favourable conservation status (FCS) of the applicable local population of protected species in the long term. The majority of protected species mitigation sites have been for European protected species, and consequently the majority of the sites are to facilitate the conservation of bats, hazel dormouse and great crested newt The information has been collated by (a) The scanning of plans illustrating the location of mitigations as part of licence applications to the Welsh Government (WG), Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Natural Resources Wales (NRW); and (b) The submission of Geographical Information System (GIS) shape files as a condition of licences issued by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under the Conservation of Habitats and Species Regulations 2017 (as amended) As of April 1st 2013 data is held and managed by Natural Resources Wales (NRW).\t1. As background, applications for protected licences issued under the Conservation of Habitats and Species Regulations 2017 (as amended) [or previous enacted Habitats Regulations] or the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 (as amended) included the submission of dedicated land and/or buildings for long term conservation purposes. 2. In respect of European protected species and applications submitted under the Habitats Regulations, a licence could only be issued if it could be demonstrated that the proposal is not likely to be detrimental to the maintenance of the favourable conservation status of the species concerned in its natural range. One of the criteria of material relevance to the favourable conservation status concerns the long term . Consequently the submission of land specifically dedicated for species mitigation purposes provides an appropriate mechanism for demonstrating appropriate consideration of the long term as a material attribute of conservation status 3. These sites are predominantly include terrestrial habitats (eg woodland grassland and scrub) and in the case of amphibians such as the great crested newt aquatic habitats. In respect of bats, buildings that function as roosts may also be included within the boundaries of land dedicated for long term conservation purposes. These sites may include retained habitats or other features and/or habitats retained or created for mitigation or compensation purposes. These sites are hereafter referred to as mitigation sites and therefore include retained and compensation habitats 4. In 2000 Welsh Government (WG) became responsible for issuing licences for development schemes under the Habitats Directive Article 16 derogation purpose scheme “Preserving public health or public safety or other imperative reasons of overriding public interest, including those of a social or economic nature and beneficial consequences of primary importance for the environment”. In 2013, Natural Resources Wales became the relevant licensing authority in Wales. 5. Applications to both WG and NRW were submitted in paper or subsequently electronic formats. Plans illustrating the locations of mitigation sites were previous submitted as hard copy maps that illustrated the layout and location of mitigation sites . Currently, applications are submitted as electronic documents with PDF’s used to identify the locations of long term compensation sites. 6. No register was developed that illustrated the spatial locations of long term mitigation sites. Location maps are retained in individual site folders in DMS. 7. Owing to no previously maintained spatial register of mitigation sites, there is no corporate data that identifies the locations of these sites and consequently these locations are therefore unlikely to be used for informing future public decision making. Future development management proposals have consequently resulted in the loss or damage to these 8. NRW has now undertaken an internal review of the locations of previous species licence mitigation areas and utilized scanned images to assist with the upload the locations as GIS shape files. 9. NRW is now imposing licence conditions requiring licence holders to submit GIS shape files to NRW for subsequent mobilization to the mitigation site location layer.\tGeographical Information System shape file data provided either as part of application submission or to discharge condition for European protected species licences issued under the provisions of species protection legislation.\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\tIwan G Hughes Species Permitting Team Leader\t\t\t2024-08-01\tcreation\tnull\t\t2024-11-19T10:16:27.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19426\tNRW_DS161282\t2024-08-12T11:35:12.357Z\t742\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts Cycle 3\tCylch 3 Ardaloedd Basn Afon y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) River Basin Districts (RBD) is a spatial dataset containing attributes that have been collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Article 2, clause 2 of the WFD defines them as \u0027…the area of land and sea, made up of one or more neighbouring river basins together with their associated groundwaters and coastal waters…\u0027. River Basin Districts have been delineated by using River Catchments as \"building blocks\" that have been aggregated together within a GIS, ensuring that WFD rivers do not intersect boundaries. Coastal and transitional waterbodies are also merged and assigned to a river basin district. River Catchments were delineated through use of flow data and a digital terrain model run through a hydrological model.-Mae Mae Ardaloedd Basn Afon (RBD) y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD) yn set ddata ofodol sy\u0027n crynhoi priodoleddau a gasglwyd fel y diffiniwyd ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD). Mae Erthygl 2, cymal 2 o\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr yn eu diffinio fel \u0027... yr ardal o dir a môr sy\u0027n cynnwys un neu fwy o fasnau afonydd cyfagos ynghyd â\u0027u dyfroedd daear cysylltiedig a dyfroedd arfordirol ...\u0027. Mae Ardaloedd Basn Afon wedi\u0027u dyrannu gan ddefnyddio Dalgylchoedd Afonydd fel \"blociau adeiladu\" sydd wedi\u0027u rhoi at ei gilydd mewn GIS, gan sicrhau nad yw afonydd GFfD yn croestorri ffiniau. Mae cyrff dŵr arfordirol a chyrff dŵr trosiannol yn cael eu huno a\u0027u dosbarthu i ardaloedd basn afon hefyd. Amlinellwyd dalgylchoedd afonydd drwy ddefnyddio data llif a model tir digidol wedi\u0027i redeg drwy fodel hydrolegol.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe Cycle 3 RBD boundaries look very similar to the cycle 1 and 2 RBD boundaries but are based on the Cycle 3 versions of the river waterbody catchments, transitional and coastal waters. As a result some of the base datasets used for their delineation have changes and they have been declared OpenData.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD River Basin Districts Cycle3.lyr\tInternal Custodian - Rob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4465/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_river_basin_districts_c3\t2024-08-20T14:53:49.447Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19430\tNRW_DS161283\t2024-08-13T14:34:56.920Z\t38\tInvalid\tEditable\tWales Integrated Natural Beauty Mapping 2024 – GIS dataset\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323833\tThe Wales Integrated Natural Beauty Mapping is part of the Natural Resources Wales (NRW) Designated Landscape Programme, which receives funding from the Welsh Government. This project was initiated to create a consistent applicable methodology and evidence base for informing landscape interventions across Wales. As the Welsh government\u0027s designating authority for landscape and statutory advisor on landscape matters, NRW has developed this mapping tool using the recognized natural beauty criteria outlined in NRW’s Procedural Guidance 2022.  The mapping tool aims: -To map natural beauty across Wales -To integrate landscape analysis with nature recovery, resilience to climate change and well-being -To identify areas to focus detailed analysis for future landscape interventions\tThis draft is still subject to further consultation and quality assurance. NRW has been meeting regularly with stakeholders and testing the mapping tool as part of an iterative process. It is important to note that this tool is a strategic, landscape-scale desk tool; it should not be used for site-specific decisions, such as development management or green infrastructure planning in urban areas. Instead, it helps identify regions needing more detailed analysis.  The scores and weightings provided are high-level indicators reflecting variations across the landscape, representing mean averages derived from overlapping datasets. The mapping units combine data from Landmap but do not reflect the resolution of these datasets. A continuous colour gradient illustrates broad variations for each theme (e.g., from high contributors to high detractors of natural beauty). Care should be taken when interpreting the scores and comparing different areas. This tool has been developed using current and accessible data sources, gathered through extensive stakeholder consultation.\t\tWaiting to be loaded on X:Drive (13/08/2024)\tAlex Hardie-Brown\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-10-10T12:59:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19445\tNRW_DS161284\t2024-08-19T09:23:40.090Z\t52\tInvalid\tEditable\tUnderstanding the likely scale of deterioration of Marine Protected Area features due to coastal squeeze: Part 2\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323834\tTo complete the coastal squeeze assessments a series of geospatial layers and accompanying spreadsheets were created as part of a project to understand the likely scale of deterioration of Marine Protected Area features due to coastal squeeze  Coastal squeeze and natural squeeze  was calculated for habitats at a national scale for the whole of the Welsh coastline, as well as for smaller delineations of the coastline. The potential scale of habitat deterioration due to coastal squeeze was then reported:  · Nationally; · Against individual MPAs; and · Against MPA designations amalgamated across Wales: o SACs; o SSSIs; o SPAs; and o Ramsar sites.  Coastal squeeze has been assessed for three timeframes (epochs): · 2025 to 2055 (30 years) – Equivalent to SMP2 medium-term epoch; · 2055 to 2105 (50 years) – Equivalent to SMP2 long-term epoch; and · 2105 to 2155 (50 years) – New long-term epoch.\tThe project applies standard predictive hypsometric analysis to calculate the changes in tidal frame around the Welsh coast as a result of SLR. These tidal frames are calculated from a newly created national Digital Terrain Model (DTM). Present-day habitat maps are then used to provide a more site-specific assessment of the habitats present, and hence how they may change over time. Further information on the habitat datalayers used in the assessment can be found in the Volume 1 Report.  The coastal squeeze and natural squeeze assessments are then undertaken. The quantification of coastal squeeze and natural squeeze is calculated: · Nationally; · Against SMP2 Policy Unit (PU); and · Against individual Assessment Units.  In each case, coastal squeeze and natural squeeze is calculated for defined Habitat Groups (see Section 2.2). An assessment of coastal and natural squeeze across the MPA network has then been undertaken. The potential scale of deterioration of MPAs due to coastal squeeze is reported against:  · Individual MPAs; and · MPA designations amalgamated across Wales: o SACs; o SSSIs; o SPAs; and o Ramsar sites.  The derived data products are vast and complex and to help users assess and interpret the data they should review section 6 of the accompanying report which will highlight frequently encountered observations.\tOaten J, Finch D, Frost N 2024. Understanding the likely scale of deterioration of Marine Protected Area features due to coastal squeeze: Volume 2 – Results \u0026amp; Discussion. NRW Evidence Report No: 789, 110pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tY:\\Marine\\Coastal Squeeze geospatial files awaiting to be assessed and moved to X:Drive Spreadsheets found in DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/mar/MNN22-25/CSMPA/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fadvice%2Fmar%2FMNN22%2D25%2FCSMPA%2FFinal%20Data\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BFEAFDBF3%2DFEA3%2D4A4D%2DA512%2DE1FB3CF2337E%7D\tNicola Rimington - Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine and Coastal Physical Process and Coastal Management\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:17:29.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19447\tNRW_DS161285\t2024-08-19T11:08:35.712Z\t935\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Management Catchments Cycle 3\tCylch 3 Dalgylch Rheoli’r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323835\tWFD Management Catchments Cycle 3 is a dataset collated as defined for the implementation of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). Management catchments are the unit of geography for which action plans are drafted in implementing the WFD. This process was conducted by using expert judgement in consultation. WFD Management Catchments have an action plan published that relates to all waterbodies that fall within its boundaries.-Mae Cylch 3 Dalgylchoedd Rheoli\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr yn set ddata a gesglir ar gyfer gweithredu\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD). Uned o ddaearyddiaeth yw dalgylchoedd rheoli y caiff cynlluniau gweithredu eu llunio ar eu cyfer wrth weithredu\u0027r WFD. Cynhaliwyd y broses hon trwy ddefnyddio barn arbenigol mewn ymgynghoriad. Mae gan Ddalgylchoedd Rheoli WFD gynllun gweithredu cyhoeddedig sy\u0027n ymwneud â phob corff dŵr o fewn ei ffiniau.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tWFD Management Catchments have been delineated by using WFD River Waterbody Catchments [these were delineated through use of flow data and a digital terrain model run through a hydrological model] as “building blocks” that have been aggregated together within a GIS, ensuring that WFD rivers do not intersect boundaries. This process was conducted by using expert judgement in consultation.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Management Catchments Cycle 3.lyr\tRobert Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-04-19\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/4462/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_management_catchments_c3\t2025-03-21T10:58:54.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19464\tNRW_DS161288\t2024-08-27T08:40:25.194Z\t1521\tValid\tEditable\tNational Flood Hazard Maps - Climate Change\tMapiau Perygl Llifogydd Cenedlaethol - Newid Hinsawdd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323838\tThe National Flood Hazard Climate Change maps are based on generalised modelling only.  These maps use the same base information as the Present Day Hazard Maps, but include an allowance for Climate Change (2120).  These maps have no official status for Planning or Insurance purposes, therefore the public and professionals are advised to use the information contained within the Flood Risk Assessment Wales Map, the Development Advice Map / Flood Map for planning (as appropriate) for these purposes as these will be more up-to-date and incorporate improved modelling information.    The National Flood Hazard Map for Climate Change have been created for 3 sources of flooding, namely, 1. Flooding from Rivers 2. Flooding from the Sea 3. Flooding from Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses  The Maps show flood depth, velocity (speed), hazard and extent for High, Medium and Low risk scenarios with raised flood defences.    For Rivers and Surface Water \u0026amp; Small Watercourses, High risk is up to 1 in 30year plus climate change uplift (CC); Medium risk is between 1 in 30 plus CC and 1 in 100year plus CC and Low risk is between 1 in 100 year plus CC and 1 in 1,000year plus CC Annual Exceedance Probability (AEP).  For the Sea, High risk is up to 1 in 30 year plus CC; Medium risk is between 1 in 30 plus CC to 1 in 200 year plus CC; and Low risk 1 in 200 year plus CC to 1 in 1000 year plus CC.  The Climate Change uplift values use the “Central” estimate for the year 2120 from the Welsh Government guidance available at the time of modelling (2018).-Mae\u0027r mapiau Perygl Llifogydd Cenedlaethol Newid Hinsawdd yn seiliedig ar fodelu cyffredinol yn unig.  Mae\u0027r mapiau hyn yn defnyddio\u0027r un wybodaeth sylfaenol â Mapiau Peryglon Heddiw, ond maent yn caniatáu ar gyfer Newid Hinsawdd (2120).  Nid oes gan y mapiau hyn unrhyw  statws swyddogol at ddibenion Cynllunio neu Yswiriant, felly cynghorir y cyhoedd a gweithwyr proffesiynol i ddefnyddio’r wybodaeth sydd wedi’i chynnwys ym Map Asesiad Perygl Llifogydd Cymru, y Map Cyngor Datblygu / Map Llifogydd ar gyfer cynllunio (fel y bo’n briodol) at y dibenion hyn gan y bydd y rhain yn fwy diweddar ac yn ymgorffori gwell gwybodaeth ar gyfer modelu.    Mae’r Map Peryglon Llifogydd Cenedlaethol ar gyfer Newid Hinsawdd wedi’i greu ar gyfer 3 ffynhonnell llifogydd, sef, 1. Llifogydd o afonydd 2. Llifogydd o\u0027r môr 3. Llifogydd o ddŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bychain  Mae\u0027r Mapiau\u0027n dangos dyfnder, cyflymder (buanedd), perygl a maint llifogydd ar gyfer senarios risg uchel, canolig ac isel gydag amddiffynfeydd llifogydd uwch.    Ar gyfer afonydd a dŵr wyneb a chyrsiau dŵr bychain, risg uchel yw hyd at 1 mewn 30 mlynedd ynghyd ag ymgodiad newid hinsawdd (CC); Mae risg ganolig rhwng 1 mewn 30 ynghyd â CC ac 1 mewn 100 mlynedd a mwy o CC ac mae risg isel rhwng 1 mewn 100 mlynedd ynghyd ag CC ac 1 mewn 1,000 o flynyddoedd ynghyd â Thebygolrwydd Gormodiant Blynyddol (AEP) CC.  Ar gyfer y Môr, risg uchel yw hyd at 1 mewn 30 mlynedd ynghyd â CC; risg ganolig yw rhwng 1 mewn 30 plws CC i 1 mewn 200 mlynedd ynghyd â CC; a risg isel yw 1 mewn 200 mlynedd ynghyd â CC i 1 mewn 1000 o flynyddoedd ynghyd â CC.  Mae gwerthoedd ymgodiad Newid Hinsawdd yn defnyddio’r amcangyfrif “Canolog” ar gyfer y flwyddyn 2120 o ganllawiau Llywodraeth Cymru a oedd ar gael adeg y modelu (2018).\tThe datasets have been created as part of the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) 2018 project. The modelling has been undertaken on the Environment Agency Integrated Height Model (EA IHM) 2016, with input rainfall and flow estimates from the Centre for Ecology \u0026amp; Hydrology (CEH). Sea level estimates have been taken from the Coastal Flood Boundary Project (CFB) 2018. Climate Change uplift values taken from the Welsh Government guidance available at the time of modelling (2018).  More information about the maps can be found in a briefing note which is available via the \u0027download data\u0027 link.\tMore information on the FRAW 2018 project can be found in the final project report JBA Consulting  2019  Flood Risk Assessment Wales Project Report.  Report to Natural Resources Wales.  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/waterman/frm/ran/FRAW/2019/2018s0011%20-%20FRAW%20Project%20Report%20-%20And%20Appendices.pdf\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\National Hazard and Risk\\\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh / GIS and Spatial Data Team   Metadata Point of Contact: Mark Pugh / Simon James / Dave Tarrant       Surface Water Catchments (subset dataset NRW_DS124811): Some features of this information are based on digital spatial data licensed from Environment Agency © EA and Getmapping Plc and Bluesky International Limited [2015].\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-12-16\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/7106\t2025-12-19T12:10:08.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19465\tNRW_DS161289\t2024-08-27T10:08:44.721Z\t243\tValid\tEditable\tWinter Gull Survey\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323839\tThese data were collected as part of the Winter Gull Survey (WinGS) project which is a UK wide survey coordinated by the BTO and funded by the BTO, Defra and country nature conservation bodies. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) is the owner of the Welsh part of the data. The project aims to produce revised wintering population estimates for non-breeding gulls in the UK and its constituent countries, and also to assess numbers of each species in autumn.   Information will be provided for all gull species, but with a focus on Black-headed Gull, Common Gull, Mediterranean Gull Ichthyaetus melanocephalus, Lesser Black-backed Gull, Herring Gull and Great Black-backed Gull as the six most numerous and widespread species that occur in the UK during the non-breeding seasons. Mediterranean Gulls have been added as an additional target species for WinGS, recognising their increased use of UK inland and coastal waters across breeding and non-breeding seasons.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tThe data collection is scheduled to run over two winters (2023/24 and 2024/25) with additional data collection due to be carried out in autumn 2024. Analysis and reporting will be carried out after the final round of data collection in January 2025, and   The data in this spreadsheet are raw data collected during the first winter (2023/24) and are unvalidated - data validation will take place during the subsequent stages of the project including data analysis and it is possible that some counts may be amended following this validation process.   It is also important to note that these data are incomplete and do not provide a full estimate of the numbers of each species in Wales. Further surveys are scheduled to be carried out during the second survey winter (2024/25) and the subsequent analysis stage will use the full data set to produce an updated winter population estimate for Wales.\t\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mneb/Liaison%20%20BTO/Winter%20Gull%20survey%20WiNGs/BTO%20WiNGs%20data?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\tMatthew Murphy: Lead Specialist Advisor: Marine Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-08-27\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-28T15:25:17.235Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19489\tNRW_DS161291\t2024-09-03T08:28:35.393Z\t797\tValid\tEditable\tNorth Wales Groundwater Archive Database\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323931\tData from groundwater sources (borehole, wells and springs) from across North Wales and the Lower Dee in England, but predominantly in the Clwyd and Lower Dee catchments.  Data includes borehole construction and geology logs (records), test pumping data, ad hoc groundwater level and quality data, surveying details (for elevation), maps, data analysis and other correspondence and notes on the site.  Data collection started in the 1970s and continued until 2014 (when the officer colleting the data retired).\tData collected as hard copy (paper files) these were scanned into PDF in 2000s. Data would have been recorded from conversation with site owners, site records, planning application, water resources applications and permit applications. Water level data is available for a number of sites digitally in WISKI. It is noted on the individual Site record whether the site is in WISKI and this can be found in WISKI using the site reference/ archive number eg.. SJ 06/009. Data quality will be indicated on the WISKI control bar\t\tZ:\\Archive\\Water Mgmt\\Groundwater\\Groundwater Archive (LTD)\tKay Roberts - Specialist Advisor, Hydrogeology\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2024-09-03\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2024-12-20T14:29:10.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19490\tNRW_DS161292\t2024-09-03T10:01:29.031Z\t671\tValid\tEditable\tUrban Gull Surveys 2021\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323932\tFollowing evidence of widespread decline in the productivity of Herring Gulls and less black backed gulls this survey compromises of a ground based survey and aerial survey of gulls to estimate the number of black-back  and herring gulls breeding in urban environments in Wales.\tA total of 366 1km squares were surveyed from the ground in the 2021 gull breeding season. The squares, as determined by JNCC/NRW, were a stratified random sample based on the number of urban squares, and the ratio of strata, found within each administrative area. Streets within the survey square were walked to count the number of gulls seen associated with urban environment by surveyors with experience in bird surveys and gull identification.  For more details on the survey limitations or methodology then please look at the associated report.\tJ. Clarke 2021 Urban Gull Welsh Surveys 2021. 34pp Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tStored on the DMS - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mneb/Liaison%20%20SMP/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;clickparams\u003deyAiWC1BcHBOYW1lIiA6ICJNaWNyb3NvZnQgT3V0bG9vayIsICJYLUFwcFZlcnNpb24iIDogIjE2LjAuMTc3MjYuMjAyMDYiLCAiT1MiIDogIldpbmRvd3MiIH0%3D\u0026amp;CID\u003d6ae24ca1%2D20b6%2D0000%2D1b3d%2Db9a684ddd4c9\u0026amp;cidOR\u003dSPO\u0026amp;RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FMarine%2DEvidence%2Fmneb%2FLiaison%20%20SMP%2FUK%20Wide%20Seabird%20Census%20%2D%20Urban%20Gulls\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120005ECAE68515E2714E8923C04099DEA67D\tMatthew Murphy: Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-09-03-2024-11-19\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-28T15:20:04.317Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n19537\tNRW_DS161294\t2024-09-17T10:12:47.684Z\t6\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCopy of template Template for Dataset Metadata in NRW 1.0 format created at 2024-09-17T11:12:47.684+01:00[Europe/London]\tCopy of template Templed ar gyfer Dataset Metadata mewn fformat NRW 1.0 created at 2024-09-17T11:12:47.684+01:00[Europe/London]\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323934\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2024-11-19T10:18:52.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19568\tNRW_DS161296\t2024-09-20T10:05:57.369Z\t349\tValid\tEditable\tThe Status and Distribution of Broom-tip Chesias rufata at Morfa Harlech and Harlech Forest in 2023\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\tA survey for Broom-tip Chesias rufata at Morfa Harlech   The aims of the current survey were to map the distribution of the larval foodplant on Morfa Harlech and Harlech Forest and to assess the distribution of Broom-tip by searching for larvae. This would provide a truer reflection of the exact distribution of the moth rather than recording the more mobile adult stage. Surveys were undertaken from July to September to avoid confusion with larvae of the Streak moth Chesias legatella which have a similar appearance and also feed on Broom in May and June.and Harlech Forest, the key locality for the moth in north Wales, from July to early September 2023.\tThe site was visited on seven occasions between 8th July and 4th September 2023 to encompass the period when larvae would be expected to be found. The aim was to beat as many of the Broom bushes as possible on the reserve and this was made easier by the fact that most of them were situated along the edges of the forest rides. The northern ride was revisited on the last two dates as the other areas seemed unproductive, in the hope of recording late larvae.  Each Broom bush was tapped sharply with a stout stick onto a beating tray (purchased from Watkins \u0026amp; Doncaster) held underneath. Large bushes were beaten in more than one place. Insects, especially bugs, would readily fall onto the beating tray and these were then tapped off with the stick. Any larvae, of Broom-tip or other species, would remain attached to the tray and also remain still for a time while they could be examined or photographed. Larvae would be carefully placed back onto Broom bushes after identification.\tAron, C. 2023. The status and distribution of Broom-tip Chesias rufata at Morfa Harlech and Harlech Forest in 2023. NRW Evidence Report No. 745. 13pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tData is stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database which is stored on the Recorder 6 Database\tDr Michael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-12-01\tcreation\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr1212\t2025-02-19T16:38:38.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19602\tNRW_DS161297\t2024-09-26T10:07:35.491Z\t26\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForester Conservation\tCadwraeth Forester\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323937\tThis layer is a copy of the Master Conservation Sites layer; where the site location is sensitive it is obscured by the application of the internal obfuscation value set in the Master Conservation Sites layer. It is a read only layer on ForesterWeb which is available to all users. The layer contains details of locations, species, and inspection records for all conservation sites on the National Forest Estate. The individual records are recorded as points, polygons, and lines. It is collected to create a record of all species on the Welsh Government Woodland Estate, but also to be used for constraints in coupe planning, and for habitat restoration projects.\tThe dataset is managed and updated by a range of users and GIS managers. The data is collected through various individuals, groups, and surveyors. The data is restricted and so access is only given to NRW ForesterWeb users who can see the data without any obfuscation. It contains external and internal data, so there are further restrictions imposed on the external records i.e. they cannot be shared outside of NRW. It is quality controlled by the Conservation and GIS managers.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Conservation.lyr\tArpana Chunilal, Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support, Evidence, Policy and Permitting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2024-09-26\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-21T10:48:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19603\tNRW_DS161298\t2024-09-26T10:46:42.933Z\t29\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForester Heritage\tTreftadaeth Forester\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323938\tThis layer holds information about heritage features on or near to the National Forest Estate. It is made up of data recorded by NRW staff and data imported from third parties. The records are recorded as points, polygons, and lines. It is collected to create a record of all the heritage features on the  Welsh Government Woodland Estate so they can be managed and improved if possible. It is also used for constraints in coupe planning, and for heritage feature restoration projects.\tThe dataset is managed  and updated by a range of users and GIS managers. The data is collected through various individuals, groups, specialised surveyors, and archaeological trusts. It is also available through external organisations such as CADW. The data is mostly collected when Forest Resource Plans are being planned. The internal data is maintained and quality controlled. The external data cannot be amended as NRW is not the owner, thus it only gets updated when new updates are available from CADW.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Heritage.lyr\tArpana Chunilal - Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2024-09-26\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:48.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19604\tNRW_DS161299\t2024-09-26T11:14:05.358Z\t26\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForester Other Trails\tLlwybrau Eraill Forester\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631323939\tThis layer holds additional trail data which is NOT held in the Rec-Walking, Rec-Mountain biking, and Rec- Miscellaneous routes. It is collected to create a record of trails that are not assigned any recreational status and do not fall into any other categories of layers i.e. public right of way, bridal ways, etc. The attributes only contain the name of the trail and it has no other identifying features. It is spatially represented as lines only. It is used for constrains in coupe planning.\tThe data is inputted and managed by operational staff and land management teams. The data is collected in the field through field maps or other devices that support GIS applications. The data is not quality controlled.  For access to this dataset, in the first instance, we would direct users to the \u0027NRW Forest Recreation Routes\u0027 dataset which is already published on DataMapWales.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Other Trails.lyr\tArpana Chunilal, Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support, Evidence, Policy and Permitting.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2024-09-26\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:45.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19605\tNRW_DS161300\t2024-09-26T19:56:08.426Z\t24\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForester Planting Plan\tCynllun Plannu Forester\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333030\tThis layer holds additional restock information that is separate from the ‘Restock Areas’ layer. The records shows specific tree species that are to be planted. The data is spatially records as polygons. The data is used for coupe planning and to update the sub-compartment database.\tThis is a newly introduced layer that became live in the financial year 2024/2025. It does not have any historical records and does not yet have any data quality control.  The data is input and managed by operational staff.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Planting Plan.lyr\tArpana Chunilal - Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support, Evidence, Policy and Permitting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2024-09-26\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:02.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19606\tNRW_DS161301\t2024-09-26T20:06:00.894Z\t24\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForester Recreational Walking\tCerdded Hamddenol Forester\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333031\tThis layer holds recreational walking routes across the Welsh Government Woodland Estate. The data collected shows a range of suitable walking routes that range from Forest trails to Heritage trails. The data is spatially recorded as lines only and it is used as a constrains layer in coupe planning.\tThe dataset is managed and updated by a range of users and GIS managers. The data is collected in the field through field maps or other devices that support GIS applications. It is quality controlled as the dataset is frequently used in coupe planning for constraints.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Recreational Walking.lyr\tArpana Chunilal - Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support, Evidence, Policy and Permitting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2024-09-26\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:44.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19607\tNRW_DS161302\t2024-09-26T20:10:55.211Z\t25\tValid\tEditable\tForester Rides\tReidiau Forester\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333032\tThis layer holds information about ride features on the Welsh Government Woodland Estate. Rides are a network of established tracks across the woodland developed to aid timber extractions. It is collected to form a record of all the rides on the Welsh Government Woodland Estate. The data is spatially recorded as lines only. It is used by operational staff to identify  existing rides so they can be utilised in operations.\tThe dataset is collected and updated by operational staff who utilise it for coupe plans and delivery. The management of the data is also carried out by the operational staff. The quality of the data is good as it is recorded by staff in the field and then digitised on ForesterWeb.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Rides.lyr\tArpana Chunilal - Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2024-09-26\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:14:05.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19608\tNRW_DS161303\t2024-09-26T20:14:47.404Z\t26\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForester Watercourses\tCyrsiau Dŵr Forester\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333033\tThis layer is a copy of the detailed river networks layer. It also contains additional features added in by NRW staff. The quality of the data is satisfactory as it is not quality controlled and it is not monitored. It is used for constrains in coupe planning.\tThe dataset is a copy of the detailed river network layer. It was created so users on the Welsh Government Woodland Estate could amend records and add in their own records, this means that the quality of the data is satisfactory as it is not monitored or checked for data quality. The responsibility of maintaining the data has been left up to the users, so its collection method is not known.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Forester Watercourses.lyr\tArpana Chunilal - Advisor (2) GIS Technical Support, Evidence, Policy and Permitting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Cadw. Welsh Historic Monuments\tNRW Conditional\t2024-09-26\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19625\tNRW_DS161305\t2024-10-04T07:36:33.264Z\t198\tValid\tEditable\tSurvey of the Black Bog Ant Formica picea on Cors Goch, Llanllwch SSSI in 2024\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333035\tThis survey was commissioned to determine the current status and distribution of Black Bog Ant on Cors Goch Llanllwch south of the railway line in August 2024 by undertaking a count of solaria.\tTo assess the number of solaria present, the site was walked over by two surveyors on 20th August 2024 working from east to west and back in “transects” running parallel to the old drainage ditches. The surveyors worked in parallel but kept apart and covered transects roughly 4m wide (2m on each side of the surveyor) to cover as much habitat as  possible whilst ensuring there was no double counting of solaria. Areas of mown, grazed (or naturally shorter, more open vegetation) were used as the main “transect” routes. Areas of taller, more rank vegetation between these were also searched by walking “W walks” through them.\tHudson, J. 2024. Survey of the Black Bog Ant Formica picea on Cors Goch, Llanllwch SSSI in 2024. NRW Evidence Report No. 816. 19pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tData Stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database in Recorder6\tMichael Howe - Michael.Howe@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-03-11\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-11T15:59:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19626\tNRW_DS161306\t2024-10-04T07:40:30.750Z\t268\tValid\tEditable\tSurveys for additional populations of Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae in Pembrokeshire and west Carmarthenshire in 2023 and 2024\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333036\tThe current two-year survey visited a further eleven sites in Pembrokeshire and one in west Carmarthenshire. A single Scaly Cricket nymph was found in a pitfall trap at Monkstone Beach near Tenby in October 2023. An additional seven nymphs were recorded at the same site in October 2024, again in pitfall traps, suggesting that a resident population is present. This represents the 4th known locality in Wales, all of which are in Pembrokeshire, and the 6th in the UK. The current survey suggests that there are few suitable beaches for Scaly Cricket and that, even where suitable habitat is present, it is not always utilised by the species which seems to have a very restricted distribution. The survey further highlights the importance of the Marloes Sands population as it is clearly the largest in Wales. Populations at Marloes Sands, West Dale Bay and Monkstone Beach remain very vulnerable to habitat loss due to coastal erosion and sea-level rise.\tHand Searching Within areas of suitable habitat, large pebbles, cobbles and stones were swiftly removed or lifted by one edge. Large pebbles beneath the lifted stone were then removed to a depth of around 10-20cm. Where the upper layers of the beach deposits have become dry these were removed and the above search methods were applied to the newly exposed, damper layers. Searches were focussed on areas of suitable habitat where strandline deposits were present.  Pitfall Trapping A plastic pint beer glass was sunk into the beach deposits. The bait used was dried cat biscuits and pieces of cooked sausage. Fine shingle was placed around the traps to ensure invertebrates were able to access the traps and a large flat stone was placed on top of the trap. Trap locations were marked by pebble cairns and photographed to aid relocation.\tHudson, J. 2024. Surveys for additional populations of Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae in Pembrokeshire and west Carmarthenshire in 2023 and 2024. NRW Evidence Report No. 817. 38pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tData is stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder6\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-03-11\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-11T11:31:52.232Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19628\tNRW_DS161307\t2024-10-04T09:12:46.217Z\t14\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecies in Peril in Wales 2024\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333037\t\t\t\tData is a spreadsheet collection of a range of species and link will be supplied by Mike Howe\tMichael Howe\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-21T10:52:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19632\tNRW_DS161310\t2024-10-08T11:01:46.644Z\t22\tValid\tEditable\tLocal Seascape Character Areas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333130\tA single, all-Wales, geospatial database, showing Local Seascape Character Areas (LSCA). Spatial extent is to 12 nautical miles out to sea (coincident with the National Wales Marine Plan for Inshore Waters boundary). In addition, coastal land areas are included (seascape being about the connections between land and sea). Land areas vary in distance from the coast line. They include the whole extent of NRW’s Flood Map for Planning Extreme Tidal Inundation, plus a range of land of a coastal type, such as cliffs and slopes, dune systems, ports and coastal settlements, and a wider coastal setting area. Area boundaries reflect changes in character, which may in practice be a broad zone of transition, and so should not be taken as a sudden change in character.   Boundaries in the marine area are highly generalised (to 1:250,000 accuracy), reflecting available evidence, and are informed by a variety of different baseline datasets and field observations. Boundary lines inland are similarly generalised (but to 1:25,000 accuracy), reflecting the greater availability of more specific spatial evidence. Inland boundaries follow identifiable features, such as edges of built up areas, changes in land cover or land use, field patterns, and changes in topographic pattern. Boundary lines are freshly drawn rather than being ‘snapped’ to baseline datasets, and any apparent exception is unintended.  Attribute table data includes: area geographical name and number; various typologies; from which source assessment the area is derived; to which National Marine Character Area the LSCA relates; and some evaluations related to different factors. There are various URLs to link to published written profiles for areas, where they exist. (These extend to several pages per area, and are available for about 70% of the LSCA). NRW manages and publishes the dataset ‘once for Wales’ to promote easier access for users, than the formerly fragmented datasets arising from different assessments in different areas.  In addition, our LSCAs attribute each area to a National Marine Character Area, in effect creating a nesting hierarchy between the two scales of assessment. LSCA introduces a new nationally consistent area numbering system to achieve this. Although baseline data has not been copied directly, a number of baseline datasets have informed the characterisation, both for identifying area boundaries and to inform the written profile documents. These include: UKHO rastor charts, Ordnance Survey 1:25,000 scale rastor map; Lidar mapping of land; NRW Flood map for Planning; Slope for Land-spreading data; BGS Seabed sediment data; Landcover Map 2020; NRW Inter-tidal survey; LANDMAP Visual and Sensory layer.   Other datasets consulted included: MMO Land-sea inter-visibility; Key variations in Marine Habitats, Wave Energy and Water Depth (a dataset created for the National Marine Character Areas preparation); Seabed sediments; Land with sea views and Sea visibility from land. Some fields in the LSCA attribute table are blank at the time of writing. This reflects limited resources and it is intended to fill out all blanks in due course with further work and updates (no timescale). The attribute table and polygons at least provide a common structure into which future data can be added. It is often 3rd party work that uses the datasets that results in new data arising, which are in turn be fed back into the LSCA master dataset.\tNRW’s Local seascape character areas (LSCA) result from gathering the various existing local-scale (“source”) assessments of seascape character around Wales, which were prepared between 2012 and the present. The gathering process also amended existing assessments for national consistency. Also, spatial gaps in existing assessment coverage have been filled with basic data from new desktop analysis and local knowledge to avoid leaving spatial gaps. Such areas await any possible future local assessment to fill out more details.   To date, existing assessments have also included separate written profile documents (of a few pages length per area), have been through a stakeholder engagement process, have been published online, and in most cases have been ‘adopted’ in local planning documents, often as Supplementary Planning Guidance.   Our spatial gap filling has not been through that extended process, but offers a provisional edition of all-Wales coverage, of assistance to users wishing to carry out all-Wales high-level analysis. Our gap filling also helps new projects to save resources for early stage mapping, while helping them to fit in with our national coverage. Seascape characterisation is concerned with identifying what is typical or common-place to find in an area, and as such, draws on a wide variety of baseline and site assessment information, often in a very generalised way. While each source assessment will vary in baseline, a broadly similar method and scale of assessment was followed, enabling our all-Wales LSCA dataset to be created.\tNatural Resources Wales. 2025. Local Seascape Character Areas (LSCA) Technical Report to accompany users of the GIS files.\tThe working master file is stored at Y:\\Landscape\\Seascapes_LSCA\\LSCA.gdb - waiting to be loaded to X:Drive\tJohn Briggs - Specialist Advisor: Landscape\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-02-13\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T10:24:41.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19667\tNRW_DS161312\t2024-10-17T14:31:22.261Z\t30\tValid\tEditable\tNRW Fisheries Enforcement Teams\tCopy of template Templed ar gyfer Dataset Metadata mewn fformat NRW 1.0 created at 2024-10-17T15:31:22.261+01:00[Europe/London]\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333132\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the extent polygons for the Natural Resources Wales Fisheries Enforcement Team boundaries. The purpose of this data is to capture and define boundaries to show geographical areas of teams’ responsibilities. It will be used to ensure enquiries and incidents etc, are passed to the correct team.\tData retrieved via e-mail. Initially an inbox was created called ‘SME Project’ where an e-mail was sent out to team leaders asking for information. After the initial e-mail had been sent, most of the outgoing e-mails were then sent out from my personal e-mail address. Information stored on an excel spreadsheet which is based on the DMS for internal staff to view.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\NRW Fisheries Enforcement Teams.lyr\tJeremy Goddard, Team Leader Industry \u0026amp; Waste Regulation\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2024-10-17\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T13:09:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n19712\tNRW_DS161313\t2024-11-04T09:03:58.060Z\t222\tValid\tEditable\tA survey to determine the status of the Depressed River Mussel Pseudanodonta complanata at eight monitoring stations within the River Wye (Lower Wye) SSSI in 2022\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333133\tData from a survey of the River Wye taken over a 3 day period from August 17th - 19th 2022. Sampling methodology followed that established in the previous survey in 2007, with eight monitoring sites visited from Backney Common (north of Ross-on-Wye) downstream to Bigsweir covering a 50km stretch of the River Wye. At each site, 60-minute visual surveys were undertaken in the river using a bathyscope to spot mussels buried in sediments or dislodged from them using a pronged rake. The number of mussels located during that period were counted, measured, photographed and then returned to the river.\tExperience gained by the contractor during the 2007 P. complanata survey were adopted to take account of the particular behaviours of the mussel in the Lower Wye. These included their relative scarcity, patchy distribution and habit of living deeply buried (often in small groups) in coarse sand between gravel and cobbles. Search technique involved the use of a bathyscope (‘glass-bottomed bucket’) to visually search the riverbed whilst wading in water depths to about 1.2m whilst wearing chest waders and a flotation aid.\tWilling, M.J. 2024. A survey to determine the status of the Depressed River Mussel Pseudanodonta complanata at eight monitoring stations within the River Wye (Lower Wye) SSSI in 2022. NRW Evidence Report No. 846. 47pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tData stored in the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder6\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-11-01\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-10T15:52:42.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19716\tNRW_DS161314\t2024-11-04T11:54:35.896Z\t229\tValid\tEditable\tThe status and distribution of the Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae at Freshwater West in 2023\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-303030303031\tFollowing the discovery of three nymphs of the Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae on the shingle beach at Freshwater West in Pembrokeshire in August and September 2022, a survey was undertaken in 2023 to determine its current status and distribution.\tVisual and pitfalls surveys for Scaly Cricket on Freshwater West were undertaken during 10th to 14th July and 8th to 11th August 2023. All locations of the pitfall traps were recorded and the suitability of habitat assessed.\tBanner, S.E. \u0026amp; Banner, E.R. 2024. The status and distribution of the Scaly Cricket Pseudomogoplistes vicentae at Freshwater West in 2023. NRW Evidence Report No. 772. 31pp. Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tData held in the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder 6\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-02-01\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:56.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19741\tNRW_DS161315\t2024-11-11T10:12:22.984Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForester Acquired and Disposed rights\tCopy of template Templed ar gyfer Dataset Metadata mewn fformat NRW 1.0 created at 2024-11-11T10:12:22.984Z[Europe/London]\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333135\t1. Acquired rights Acquired rights is a key editable layer that contains data on acquired rights on the Welsh Government Woodland Estate (WGWE). This is a sensitive dataset as it contains transaction references,names, and titles. The attributes are linked to a spatial shape that shows the extent of the acquired right(s). When any rights are disposed of they are amended on this layer and they automatically appear in the ‘Disposed Rights’ layer. This data is collected and digitised by NRW staff and it is quality controlled by the estate surveyors.  2. Disposed rights  Disposed rights is linked to Acquired Rights and all data within is derived from Acquired Rights. The layer is browse only, thus data can only be viewed and not amended.\t1. Acquired rights The data is collected by NRW staff and quality controlled by the Estates team/ GIS Manager.  2. Disposed rights The data is collected by NRW staff and quality controlled by the Estates team/ GIS Manager.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\tArpana Chunilal\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2024-08-23\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-27T09:10:54.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19777\tNRW_DS161317\t2024-11-13T17:03:28.863Z\t582\tValid\tEditable\tClocaenog Forest Red Squirrel Abundancy data 2023\tTo be added\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333137\tThis study involved the assessment of red squirrel occupancy and habitat preferences within Clocaenog Forest. A stratified random sampling strategy was employed, along with a ‘targeted’ survey to maximise red squirrel detections.  This data consists of spreadsheets detailing red squirrel detections via trail cameras.   One spreadsheet is the original, non-targeted/random approach, the other is the targeted approach.\tData collected via trail cameras.  Camera-trap surveys were conducted over three months -November 2023 to January 2024 for the non-targeted/random approach. Following the initial camera-trap survey, the targeted survey was undertaken during January and February 2024.\tShannon G, Barton O, Valle S, Shuttleworth CM. 2024. Developing a method for assessing the relative abundance of red squirrels in low-density populations. NRW Evidence Report Series, Report No:795, 22 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/mam/Red Squirrel/Clocaenog/Population Abundance Contract 2023_2024/Data Associated with Abundancy report/Detections_original_survey_29-10-24.csv  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/mam/Red%20Squirrel/Clocaenog/Population%20Abundance%20Contract%202023_2024/Data%20Associated%20with%20Abundancy%20report/Detections_targetted_survey_29-10-24.csv\tRebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Mammals\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2023-11-25\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-29T14:22:33.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19781\tNRW_DS161318\t2024-11-18T15:23:24.503Z\t357\tValid\tEditable\tWest Anglesey fragile sponge and anthozoan communities dive survey, 2023\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333138\tThis resource contains data from surveys of fragile sponge and anthozoan (FSAN) communities off North and West Anglesey, collected during dive surveys in April-May 2023. Divers were tasked with re-surveying up to 17 sites previously surveyed as far back as 1996 to determine the continued presence of reported FSAN communities, namely of the biotope CR.HCR.XFa.ByErSp.DysAct (mixed turf of bryozoans and erect sponges with Dysidea fragilis and Actinothoe sphyrodeta on tide-swept wave exposed circalittoral rock. This data was collected as FSAN communities are listed under Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2016 and this area around Anglesey has been proposed as an Area of Search (AoS) in the Marine Protected Area (MPA) Network Completion Programme by Welsh Government. The data comprises of:  • 3GB of images (covering each of the 20 survey sites) • 4GB of videos taken during dive surveys • A Survey Photo Catalogue (Excel spreadsheet), with details of each photo and video (e.g. filename, subject, notable species, biotope) • A Master metadata Excel spreadsheet\tMNCR style, phase 2 diving surveys. MNCR forms were completed where the habitat was encountered and images and video recorded. Positional fixes of survey sites made by non-differential GPS taken from boat-based GPS system (horizontal accuracy \u0026lt;5m); coordinate reference system WGS84. Data entry was subjected to QA on-site and post-survey by the survey team, and by Matt Doggett/ Kate Northen during reporting.\tNorthen KO, Doggett M, 2023. West Anglesey fragile sponge and anthozoan communities dive survey, 2023: final report. NRW Evidence Report No: 742, 144pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\2023 Anglesey Sponge and Anthozoan\tNatasha Lough - Specialist Advisor, Marine Ecology\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-11-18\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-17T11:48:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n19820\tNRW_DS161319\t2024-11-29T12:10:24.175Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFurther studies to assess the distribution of the Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior on Oxwich Burrows, Nicholaston Burrows, Whiteford Burrows, Three Cliffs Bay \u0026amp; Swansea Dunes in September 2023\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333139\tMike Howe to supply\t\t\tRecorder 6\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-27T09:14:03.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19851\tNRW_DS161320\t2024-12-02T15:17:22.567Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Survey of Lichens on Pine Snags at Cors y Llyn National Nature Reserve\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333230\tCors y Llyn National Nature Reserve in Powys was surveyed for lignicolous lichens on dead Scots Pine Pinus sylvestris snags on three dates in November 2023. Lichens were systematically recorded on 24 Scots Pine Pinus sylvestris snags and on one birch Betula snag, with additional casual records made on other Scots Pines, birches, and Heather Calluna vulgaris. Forty-six lichen species, 10 lichenicolous fungi and 2 non-lichenised fungi were recorded during the survey, of which 33 species were new site records.\tThe survey involved systematic recording of lichens on a representative sample of Scots Pine snags, and casual recording of lichens on other pine snags, living pines, birch trees, and Heather Calluna vulgaris. The diameter at breast height (dbh) of the snags sampled was measured using a 5 m diameter tape. The height of each snag was measured, or estimated if longer than 2 metres. The angle of lean for each snag was measured and the snags were also photographed. The frequency of each lignicolous lichen on the snags sampled was expressed on the DAFOR scale using the following classes:\u0026lt; 5%rare, 6–20% occasional, 21–50% frequent, 51–90%abundant, and 91–100% dominant.\tBacciu, NG \u0026amp; McGill, J. 2024. A survey of lichens on pine snags at Cors y Llyn National Nature Reserve. NRW Evidence Report 761, 39 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/lich/Lichen%20Evidence/0%20Lichen%20Evidence%20Report%20data/Snag%20Lichens%20at%20Cors%20y%20Llyn/NRW%20Evidence%20Report%20761%20-%20Cors%20y%20Llyn%20pine%20snag%20lichen%20survey%202023%20data.xlsx?d\u003dwfe1b9fba22b64297ab5b2b787fa4cda2\tSam Bosanquet - Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2024-02-01\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:38:27.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n19870\tNRW_DS161321\t2024-12-05T09:58:58.182Z\t279\tValid\tEditable\tCwm Ivy National Vegetation Classification (NVC) Survey 2022\tTo be added\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333231\tThe report and this GIS dataset present the results of a National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey carried out in 2022 on selected areas of saltmarsh habitat and transitional vegetation at Cwm Ivy Marsh, North Gower.   Cwm Ivy is a saltmarsh creation site and the vegetation survey confirms the establishment of saltmarsh over the majority of the site.\tThe vegetation was surveyed in August 2022 and the vegetation was assigned to NVC sub-communities and communities mapped onto aerial images. A field based judgement was carried out on community assignment supported by quadrat samples and target notes.\tSutton, M.D. 2023. NVC Survey of Cwm Ivy Marsh, Llanmadoc, Gower, NRW Evidence Report 727, 46pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\tY:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Coastal Habitats\\Cwm Ivy NVC 2022\\WyndrushWild_CwmIvy_NVC_2022.shp\tHeather Lewis, Lead Specialist Advisor, Coastal Habitats, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team, EPP\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2024-01-25\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-01-09T09:22:17.891Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n19967\tNRW_DS161323\t2024-12-23T14:25:17.508Z\t664\tValid\tEditable\tLlyn Horse Mussel Multibeam Echosounder Survey data 2024\tTo be added\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333233\tThis data is from the 2024 Llyn Horse Mussel (Modiolus modiolus) Multibeam Echosounder Survey (MBES).   The work was carried out by Binnies/Hydrofix funded by Nature Networks as part of an investigation into the decline observed at the reef. Both reefs were mapped to International Hydrographic order (IHO) 1a or higher.   The data was processed and analysed to produce bathymetric surfaces; backscatter mosaics; a seabed classification, including the extents of the mussel beds; and measurements of the mussel bioherms.  The results from the 2024 survey were then compared to previous datasets, provided by NRW, from 2005, 2008 and 2015, all of which were analysed and reprocessed.\tThe data collected represents the highest quality data so far made available over the survey areas, with higher data density and thus better resolution than previously achieved.    Post processing of all navigation data has ensured the highest levels of positional accuracy, with cm scale accuracies achieved, while the resulting data far exceeds the IHO 1a survey specifications prescribed.\tWillcock, J. (2024). Pen Llŷn A’r Sarnau SAC Horse Mussel (Modiolus modiolus) reef MBES survey report (2024) and time series analysis (2005-2024). NRW Evidence Report No: 851, 67pp, Natural Resources Wales\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Marine acoustic bathymetric data\\Multibeam\\Modiolus 2024\\H23034_Hydrofix_2024_Deliverables\tHarriet Lincoln, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Projects Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-12-17\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-24T14:09:18.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20028\tNRW_DS161324\t2025-01-08T09:23:11.062Z\t103\tValid\tEditable\tNational Environment Programme - 2024 Price Review Final Determination\tRhaglen Genedlaethol yr Amgylchedd - Penderfyniad Terfynol Adolygiad Prisiau 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333234\tThe Price Review 2024 (PR24) Final Determination (FD) National Environment Programme (NEP) dataset is a Wales-wide spatial point dataset that identifies the environmental legislative obligations on water companies operating in or benefitting from Wales’s natural resources. These include five water companies; Dwr Cymru Welsh Water, Hafren Dyfrdwy, United Utilities, Severn Trent Water and South Staffordshire and Cambridgeshire Water.   Delivery of the identified actions occurs in the Asset Management Plan (AMP) period. The PR24 NEP will be delivered in AMP8 from 1 April 2025 to 31 March 2030. These actions are intended to ensure compliance with new and changed environmental obligations relevant to the water companies.   This dataset is published reflecting the version of the NEP used in Ofwat’s final determination for PR24. It is correct as of December 2024. During its delivery in AMP8 the NEP actions may be subject to change.-Mae set ddata Penderfyniad Terfynol Adolygiad Prisiau 2024 (PR24) Rhaglen Genedlaethol yr Amgylchedd yn set ddata o bwyntiau gofodol Cymru gyfan sy\u0027n nodi rhwymedigaethau deddfwriaethol amgylcheddol y cwmnïau dŵr sy\u0027n gweithredu o fewn adnoddau naturiol Cymru neu sy\u0027n elwa arnynt. Mae\u0027r rhain yn cynnwys pum cwmni dŵr; Dŵr Cymru, Hafren Dyfrdwy, United Utilities, Severn Trent Water a South Staffordshire and Cambridgeshire Water. Cyflawnir y camau gweithredu a nodwyd yn ystod cyfnod y Cynllun Rheoli Asedau (AMP). Bydd PR24 Rhaglen Genedlaethol yr Amgylchedd yn cael ei gyflwyno yng Nghynllun Rheoli Asedau 8 (AMP8) o 1 Ebrill 2025 i 31 Mawrth 2030. Bwriad y camau gweithredu hyn yw sicrhau cydymffurfiaeth â rhwymedigaethau amgylcheddol newydd a rhai sydd wedi newid sy\u0027n berthnasol i\u0027r cwmnïau dŵr. Cyhoeddir y set ddata hon gan adlewyrchu\u0027r fersiwn o Raglen Genedlaethol yr Amgylchedd a ddefnyddiwyd ym mhenderfyniad terfynol Ofwat ar gyfer PR24. Mae\u0027r data hwn yn gywir ym mis Rhagfyr 2024. Yn ystod y cyfnod y bydd yn cael ei gyflwyno yn AMP8 efallai y bydd camau gweithredu Rhaglen Genedlaethol yr Amgylchedd yn cael eu newid.  Mae\u0027r data ar gael ar ffurf GIS gyda thaenlen sy’n cynnwys camau gweithredu penodol, canlyniadau a metrigau cyfatebol pob cwmni dŵr.\tThis dataset is based on data held by NRW including knowledge from staff that has been shared with and confirmed with the relevant water company. It reflects where NRW as the environmental regulator has identified a new or changed requirement on the water company requiring action by that company to fulfil their legislative obligations.   This dataset aims to provide a clear and comprehensive representation of each company\u0027s obligations through spatial analysis as used in Ofwat’s Final Determination for he 2024 Price Review in December 2024.   The primary source for this data is from NRW with input from relevant water companies. The data collection method involves tracking site specific knowledge to establish the new or changed obligations and the to agree what action was required by the relevant water company.\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Water\\Price Review 2024 National Environment Programme\tRuth Johnston \u0026amp; Inoka Manatunga - Integrated Water Planning Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-01-08-2025-02-04\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/7217/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_national_environment_plan\t2025-11-25T17:13:52.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20084\tNRW_DS161327\t2025-01-20T11:05:24.317Z\t429\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of Life (SoLIFE)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333237\tSands of LIFE was a major conservation project to rejuvenate sand dunes across Wales which ran to June 2024.  The project’s aims were to recreate natural movement in the dunes and revitalise habitats which are home to some of our rarest wildlife.  A range of datasets were created as part of this project which included data for Great Crested Newts, Sand Lizard, Fen Orchid\u0027s, Invertebrates and Bryophytes. For more information on data created from this project please see child metadata entries. This data includes survey results pre-intervention and post-intervention.\tFor more detailed information on the lineage and detailed methodologies please see child metadata entries.\thttps://naturalresources.wales/sandsoflife?lang\u003den\tMuch of the data resources are stored in the DMS here - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landman/Protected-Sites/so-life/D1%20Physical%20Monitoring/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;clickparams\u003deyAiWC1BcHBOYW1lIiA6ICJNaWNyb3NvZnQgT3V0bG9vayIsICJYLUFwcFZlcnNpb24iIDogIjE2LjAuMTgyMjcuMjAyMjIiLCAiT1MiIDogIldpbmRvd3MiIH0%3D\u0026amp;CID\u003dec5979a1%2D702b%2D0000%2D415a%2D9285ebb2dfae\u0026amp;cidOR\u003dSPO\u0026amp;RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Flandman%2FProtected%2DSites%2Fso%2Dlife%2FD1%20Physical%20Monitoring%2F00%20SoLIFE%20Data%20Archive   Data available in a GIS format is found here - X:\\Biological\\Sands of Life  Users should see individual Sands Of Life Datasets for specific locations\tKathryn Hewitt - Lead Specialist Advisor, Sustainable Farming Scheme\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-01-20\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-01-20T11:37:47.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n20113\tNRW_DS161328\t2025-01-21T13:43:48.040Z\t176\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSands of LIFE LiDAR Survey\tArolwg LiDAR Twyni Byw\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333238\tSands of LIFE is a major conservation project to rejuvenate 2400 ha of sand dunes across Wales, by reprofiling dunes, creating bare sand to promote mobility, removing scrub, and other actions.   The Sands of LIFE project commissioned a LiDAR survey of sand dune areas both before and after completion of large-scale sand dune restoration works. The aim was to create an accurate and up-to-date baseline picture of the dune morphology and determine geomorphological changes and sand movement away from the footprint of interventions.    Sands of LIFE specified the provision of aerial LiDAR across the entire area of 10 project sites on the Welsh Coast, split over four Special Areas of Conservation (SACs)-Roedd Twyni Byw yn brosiect cadwraeth sylweddol i adnewyddu twyni tywod ar hyd a lled Cymru, a fu’n rhedeg tan fis Mehefin 2024.  Nodau’r prosiect oedd ail-greu symudiad naturiol yn y twyni tywod ac adfywio cynefinoedd sy’n gartref i rai o’n mathau prinnaf o fywyd gwyllt.  Comisiynodd prosiect Twyni Byw arolwg LiDAR o ardaloedd twyni tywod cyn ac ar ôl cwblhau’r gwaith adfer ar raddfa fawr. Y nod oedd llunio darlun cywir a chyfoes o’r llinell sylfaen ar gyfer morffoleg y twyni a phennu newidiadau geomorffolegol a symudiad y tywod oddi wrth ôl troed yr ymyriadau.  Pennodd Twyni Byw y byddai arolygon LiDAR o’r awyr yn cael eu cynnal ar draws ardal gyfan y 10 safle ar Arfordir Cymru, a oedd wedi’u rhannu rhwng pedair Ardal Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACA).  Gwnaed arolygon llinell sylfaen drwy hedfan dros safleoedd y prosiect rhwng mis Gorffennaf 2019 a mis Ionawr 2020 gan gasglu data LiDAR yn llwyddiannus ar wyth pwynt y metr a chyda’r cywirdeb canlynol: RMSE fertigol: 10cm, RMSE llorweddol: 35cm. Cafodd y gwaith arolygu ei ailadrodd ym mis Rhagfyr 2023,  gan gasglu set gyfatebol o ddata LiDAR wedi’r ymyriadau.  Mae rhagor o fanylion am brosiect Twyni Byw i’w cael yma - https://cyfoethnaturiol.cymru/twynibyw\tBaseline flights were flown across the project sites between July 2019 and January 2020 and successfully captured LiDAR data at eight points per meter and to the following accuracies: Vertical RMSE:10cm, Horizontal RMSE:35cm. The survey work was repeated in December 2023, with a matching set of post-intervention LiDAR data captured.\tClay J, Thomas M, Chisholm N. 2024. Sands of LIFE LiDAR survey report. NRW Evidence Report No: 832, 29 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tX:\\Basemapping\\LiDAR\\SoLIFE\\\tKathryn Hewitt - Lead Specialist Advisor, Sustainable Farming Scheme\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-01-14\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n20114\tNRW_DS161329\t2025-01-21T13:45:15.493Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tData collected for: Sands of LIFE Socio-Economic Impact Assessment (post-intervention)\tCopy of template Templed ar gyfer Dataset Metadata mewn fformat NRW 1.0 created at 2025-01-21T13:45:15.493Z[Europe/London]\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333239\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-21T10:38:27.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20116\tNRW_DS161330\t2025-01-22T10:27:04.920Z\t133\tValid\tEditable\tCommunities at Risk Register 2024 – Present Day (CaRR)\tY Gofrestr Cymunedau mewn Perygl (CaRR) 2024 - Cyfredol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333330\tThe Communities at Risk Register (CaRR) has been developed to provide an objective means of identifying risk and prioritising flood risk management activities at a Wales-wide, community level. It applies a standard methodology across all flood sources to calculate a theoretical ‘danger score’ that allows comparative risks to be quantified and ranked (from High to Low).    The CaRR comprises a spreadsheet that identifies and ranks individual communities for,  1. a natural, ‘undefended’ scenario, and  2. a managed scenario (based on the presence of defences and flood warning).   It is complemented by a GIS layer which defines the individual Community polygons and is attributed with danger scores, community rankings and other metric information.\tThe CaRR has been created using national-scale model data developed as part of the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) project.  The CaRR ranks communities based on a theoretical danger score which should be considered alongside other factors (such as history of flooding) when prioritising activities.  The methodology incorporates the ‘Depth in Property’ approach to counting the number of properties at flood risk from different sources.\tA brief summary of objectives and methodology is available in the Information Sheets.\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T14:46:37.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20132\tNRW_DS161331\t2025-01-24T15:37:12.049Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tClimate Vulnerability Assessment (CVA) for Ramsar Sites\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333331\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\tThe Geospatial Services Team do not currently hold any information for this section. If you are the custodian, please contact the team so we can populate this field.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Climate Vulnerability\\CVA RAMSAR.lyr\tContact Clive Walmsley, Senior Specialist Advisor, Climate Change and Decarbonisation, SPG for further information about the data.  Data originally produced and provided by Jon Rothwell, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-01-27T10:16:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n20190\tNRW_DS161333\t2025-02-04T10:51:51.583Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBaseline Monitoring of Peatland Invertebrate on the Hafod Elwy - Cord Goch Peatland Restoration Site 2024\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333333\t\t\t\t\tBethan Beech\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-07-08T09:17:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20191\tNRW_DS161334\t2025-02-04T13:53:20.139Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGrizzled Skipper Survey of Wrexham Industrial Estate 2024\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333334\tWaiting for metadata from custodian\t\t\t\tBethan Beech\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-07-08T09:17:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20192\tNRW_DS161335\t2025-02-04T13:57:20.684Z\t559\tValid\tEditable\tTranquillity \u0026amp; Place Theme 6 full resource - connecting sound and visually tranquil places\tLlonyddwch a Lle Thema 6 adnodd llawn - cysylltu mannau o lonyddwch gweledol a chlywedol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333335\tThe Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place resource was developed using mapped ‘themes’. You can view information and data relating to the Tranquillity and Place themes on the Tranquillity and Place Story Map. - https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/865c1876d9f64280a3dfc6e2769a46a5  • Theme 1 - relative abundance, perception or experience of nature, natural landscapes and greenspaces. • Theme 2 - relative freedom from intrusive visual disturbance and human influence. • Theme 3 - relative dark skies.  • Theme 4 – sound environments where natural sounds are more prominent than noise and are appropriate to context.  • Theme 5 - visually tranquil places (Themes 1, 2 and 3) • Theme 6 – the full tranquillity and place resource (Themes 1, 2, 3 and 4). Theme 6 full resource identifies the strategic and local resource in remote, rural, peri-urban and urban areas for use as an evidence base to inform policy intent, practice and provision for well-being benefits.-Datblygwyd yr adnodd Llonyddwch a Lle gan ddefnyddio \u0027themâu\u0027 wedi\u0027u mapio. Gallwch weld gwybodaeth a data yn ymwneud â themâu Llonyddwch a Lle ar y Map Stori Llonyddwch a Lle. - https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/865c1876d9f64280a3dfc6e2769a46a5  Mae thema 6 adnodd llawn yn nodi’r adnodd strategol a lleol mewn ardaloedd anghysbell, gwledig, ardaloedd trefol a’r ardaloedd o amgylch trefi i’w ddefnyddio fel sail tystiolaeth i lywio bwriad polisi, arfer a darpariaeth ar gyfer buddion llesiant.  Cydnabyddiaeth:  Mae’n cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Natural Resources Wales a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Mae’n cynnwys Data’r Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif trwydded yr Arolwg Ordnans: AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawliau Cronfa Ddata. Data Digidol Cymdeithas Ddaearegol Prydain o dan Rif Trwydded 2013/062. Cymdeithas Ddaearegol Prydain\tThis dataset has been created by combining the previous theme\u0027s of the stufy into a whole dataset.   In summary; the data is divided into 10 categories, from 1 to 10; the lower the value, the less likely an area is to be tranquil, and the higher the value, the more likely it is to be tranquil\tGreen C, Horton K, Gibbs G, Sims J 2025. Tranquillity \u0026amp; Place Theme 6 full resource - connecting sound and visually tranquil places. NRW Report No: 807, 63pp, NRW.\tX:\\Physical\\Landscape\\Tranquillity and Place  Associated Spreadsheet can be found here - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/Landscape/tranq/TP/1.%20Tranquillity%20%26%20Place%20Theme%206%20full%20resource%20Final/Theme%206%20Stats.xlsx?d\u003dw666689ff9a0e4776a5e11cd4b9090e98\tJill Bullen - Lead Specialist Advisor, Landscape\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-02-19\tcreation\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/documents/7420\t2025-05-14T08:22:43.000Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20240\tNRW_DS161336\t2025-02-12T11:13:46.570Z\t12\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPre-assessed Areas for Wind Energy\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333336\tAreas with a presumption in favour of large-scale wind energy development. Data highlights whether the area is suitable for wind, solar or both.\tThis data only appears at a scale of over 100000\t\tX:\\Utilities\\Renewables\\Pre-assessed areas for wind energy.lyr\tJames Laing, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW Enid Carrick, Energy Delivery Programme, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-02-12\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-21T10:52:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20252\tNRW_DS161337\t2025-02-12T15:01:18.392Z\t678\tValid\tEditable\tMonitoring of the Atlantic Woodland Bryophyte Assemblage in Coed Graig Uchaf SSSI\tTo be added\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333337\tCoed Graig Uchaf SSSI (32.5 ha) was designated in 1987 and was selected as an example of a woodland with a rich oceanic bryophyte flora.  The first comprehensive bryophyte survey of the site was undertaken during 2014, when baseline monitoring data was collected. The aim of this 2024 study was to undertake the first re-monitoring of the Atlantic woodland bryophyte assemblage and assess what changes may have occurred  This dataset shows bryophyte presence data from 10x10m grid squares in Coed Graig Uchaf SSSI.\tData was collected in the field following the Bryophyte Grid Mapping Protocol.  A total of 633 OS 10 m grid cells was surveyed during the base-line monitoring in 2014, covering all of the accessible ravine habitat and the best areas of oceanic woodland within the SSSI. During this study in November 2024, all of these grid cells were revisited and a search of each was undertaken for members of the Atlantic woodland bryophyte assemblage and other notable species\tCallaghan, D.A. 2024. Monitoring of the Atlantic woodland bryophyte assemblage in Coed Graig Uchaf SSSI. NRW Evidence Report No: 866, 50pp, Natural Resources Wales.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FCoed%20Graig%20Uchaf%20Bryophyte%20Monitoring%202024\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam, Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-02-11\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-17T10:40:42.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20279\tNRW_DS161339\t2025-02-17T17:15:05.609Z\t515\tValid\tEditable\tRiver Habitat Survey (RHS) and Plant Community survey data of the River Wye SAC 2024\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333339\tIn 2024, a River Habitat Survey (RHS), Simplified Phase 1 survey and LEAFPAC aquatic macrophyte survey was carried out on the River Wye Special Area of Conservation (SAC). This was to contribute to the condition assessment of features in the River Wye SAC as well as provide information to support the management and restoration of protected sites and the delivery of River Basin Management Plans.   Twenty-two monitoring sites out of thirty-five possible sites were surveyed in the River Wye catchment between 5th August and 28th September 2024.   At each site the following surveys were conducted:  • River Habitat Surveys (RHS), to assess the quality of physical habitat, supported by  • Simplified Phase 1 survey, and • Aquatic plant surveys using the rivers LEAFPACS method, along with  • Field observations of habitat quality to assist with interpretation of the data.  The principal outputs of this project are digital copies of field records including photographs, access and parking, along with a description of each site.  These surveys were conducted on the Welsh section of the River Wye SAC.\tThe QA’d RHS and Macrophyte data will be added into the Kieco Ecological Data Management System. Some of the surveys will need to be repeated due to high flows at the time of survey. This information is clearly set out in the report.  Data from this work will be incorporated into, and made available as part of, the existing datasets: River Habitat Survey (RHS) (NRW_DS116339) and Freshwater Ecology: River Macrophytes (NRW_DS161279).\tBywater, J. A. 2024. River Habitat Survey (RHS) and Plant Community surveys of the River Wye SAC (Wales). Report No: 849, 92pp, NRW, Bangor.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/sites/smar/fm/CARWC4/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fsites%2Fsmar%2Ffm%2FCARWC4%2FWye%20SAC%20RHS%20and%20macrophyte%20contract%2FRHS%20Macrophyte%20Report%20and%20Data\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200042A0D276A0ABB34DACF1ADA432CBE918  Z:\\Archive\\Sampling Monitoring \u0026amp; Analysis Reporting\\Freshwater Monitoring\\River Habitat Survey (RHS) and Plant Community surveys of the River Wye SAC (Wales) carried out in 2024  No additional open data assessment were undertaken as will be covered by the published kieco datasets\tSuzanne Hearn, Freshwater Ecosystems and Fisheries Management Team Leader\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-02-13\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-28T14:54:42.055Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20280\tNRW_DS161340\t2025-02-18T10:42:24.354Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThe status of the Grizzled Skipper Pyrgus malvae at Dinham Meadows SSSI and the wider Mod Caerwent Training Area in 2023 \u0026amp; 2024\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333430\tWaiting for report completion\t\t\t\tMike Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-21T10:48:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20281\tNRW_DS161341\t2025-02-18T10:44:03.876Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey for the water beetles Haliplus variegatus, Hydrochus ignicollis, Hydrovatus clypealis and Limnebius aluta on selected Anglesey wetlands in 2024\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333431\tWaiting for report completion\t\t\t\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-21T10:48:40.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20310\tNRW_DS161343\t2025-02-24T09:57:18.679Z\t433\tValid\tEditable\tCompliance Assessment of Welsh River Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) Against Water Quality Targets (2020 - 2023)\tAsesiad Cydymffurfiaeth Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACA) Afonydd Cymru yn erbyn Targedau Ansawdd Dŵr (2020-2023)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333433\tThese data identify the compliance of Welsh river Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) regarding targets for eight water quality attributes: Phosphorus, Dissolved Oxygen, Biochemical Oxygen Demand, Total Ammonia, Unionised Ammonia, Trophic Diatom Index, pH and Acid Neutralising Capacity.   The targets and methods of assessment are set at a UK level by the Joint Nature Conservation Committee as published in the Common Standards Guidance for Rivers 2016, and form part of the conservation objectives for the riverine SAC features.   There are nine SACs in Wales with riverine features (known in their respective Core Management Plans as):   • River Dee and Bala Lake  • Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn  • Coedydd Derw a Safleoedd Ystlumod Meirion (Afon Glaslyn)  • Afon Eden - Cors Goch Trawsfynydd  • Afon Teifi  • Afonydd Cleddau  • Afon Tywi  • River Usk  • River Wye   For the purposes of water quality reporting, Natural Resources Wales (NRW) divides rivers into units called water bodies. A total of 119 (of a possible 127) water bodies, each with a single sampling point, were assessed for one or more attribute. The River Dee and Wye were assessed in the Welsh part of the SAC only.   Water quality data collected by NRW’s routine surveillance monitoring programme for a four-year period (1st January 2020 – 31st December 2023) were extracted from the NRW water quality database (KiWQM) to assess compliance with targets.   Data was not available for all attributes for all water bodies due to the risk-based design of NRW’s monitoring programmes. For attributes/waterbodies with insufficient data to determine compliance over the period 2020-2023, but where a result was produced in the previous assessment (using 2017-2019 data), the previous result has been rolled forward.-Mae’r data hwn yn pennu cydymffurfiaeth Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACA) afonydd Cymru o ran targedau ar gyfer wyth nodwedd ansawdd dŵr: Ffosfforws, Ocsigen Tawdd, Y Galw Biocemegol am Ocsigen, Cyfanswm yr Amonia, Amonia Heb ei Ïoneiddio, Mynegai Diatomau Troffig, pH a’r Gallu i Niwtralu Asidau.   Mae’r targedau a’r dulliau asesu wedi’u pennu ar lefel y DU gan y Cyd-bwyllgor Cadwraeth Natur fel y’u cyhoeddwyd yn y ‘Common Standards Guidance for Rivers 2016’, ac maent yn rhan o’r amcanion cadwraeth ar gyfer nodweddion afonol ACAau.   Mae naw ACA yng Nghymru sydd â nodweddion afonol (a elwir yn eu Cynlluniau Rheoli Craidd priodol fel a ganlyn):  • Afon Dyfrdwy a Llyn Tegid • Afon Gwyrfai a Llyn Cwellyn • Coedydd Derw a Safleoedd Ystlumod Meirion (Afon Glaslyn) • Afon Eden - Cors Goch Trawsfynydd • Afon Teifi • Afonydd Cleddau • Afon Tywi • Afon Wysg • Afon Gwy  At ddibenion adrodd ar ansawdd dŵr, mae Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) yn rhannu afonydd yn unedau a elwir yn gyrff dŵr. Aseswyd cyfanswm o 119 o gyrff dŵr (allan o 127 posibl), pob un ag un pwynt samplu, ar gyfer un nodwedd neu fwy. Aseswyd afon Dyfrdwy ac afon Gwy yn y rhan o’r ACA sydd yng Nghymru yn unig.  Cafodd data ansawdd dŵr a gasglwyd gan raglen monitro gwyliadwriaeth arferol CNC am gyfnod o bedair blynedd (1 Ionawr 2020 – 31 Rhagfyr 2023) ei dynnu o gronfa ddata ansawdd dŵr (KiWQM) CNC er mwyn asesu cydymffurfiaeth â thargedau.   Nid oedd data ar gael ar gyfer pob nodwedd o bob corff dŵr oherwydd cynllun seiliedig ar risg rhaglenni monitro CNC. Ar gyfer nodweddion/cyrff dŵr heb ddigon o ddata i bennu cydymffurfiaeth dros y cyfnod 2020-2023, ond lle cynhyrchwyd canlyniad yn yr asesiad blaenorol (gan ddefnyddio data 2017-2019), mae’r canlyniad blaenorol wedi’i gario ymlaen.  Roedd yr holl ddata yn destun prosesau sicrhau ansawdd cadarn. Defnyddiwyd methodolegau casglu a dadansoddi data yn unol â chanllawiau safonol CNC.  Mae’r dulliau llawn wedi’u cyflwyno yn yr adroddiad atodol.\tAll data was subject to robust quality assurance processes. Data collection and analysis methodologies were undertaken in line with NRW’s standard guidance. Full methods are presented in the accompanying report.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Compliance Assessment Welsh River SACs\\Water Quality Targets (2020-2023)\tSuzanne Hearn, Team Leader Freshwater Ecosystems \u0026amp; Fisheries Management\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-02-21-2025-10-09\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/7376/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_sac_river_wqt_2020_23\t2025-10-13T16:11:38.091Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20332\tNRW_DS161344\t2025-02-25T14:51:01.372Z\t395\tValid\tEditable\tNorth Wales Operations Flooded Properties\tFlooded Properties North.lyr\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333434\tThis data is compiled from all reports of property flooding from Main Rivers and the Sea in North Wales from 1993 that we have within our records, and reports from North Powys from 2019 onwards.\tDepending on the flooding instance it may not have been possible to verify or collect complete data, especially for older reports and reports received through external sources. New reports of property flooding are added as we are made aware of them through our Response Partners, reports from the public, site visits and others. Some reports of property flooding from non-Main River or Tidal sources are also included in the database, but as they do not fall under Natural Resources Wales\u0027 remit these are not included as a default.\t\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Flooded Properties North.lyr\tGwenno Talfryn - Advisor 1, Warning \u0026amp; Informing\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t2025-04-09-2025-10-13\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-15T09:14:20.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n20334\tNRW_DS161345\t2025-02-26T09:04:07.534Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAn insect survey of Ynyslas Dunes in July 2024, with a focus on the Pantaloon Bee Dasypoda hirtipes\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333435\tMike Howe to Provide\t\t\t\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-03-21T10:52:41.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20356\tNRW_DS161346\t2025-03-05T09:37:35.539Z\t355\tValid\tEditable\tGwent Levels - Resilient Ecological Networks\tRhwydwaith Ecolegol Gadarn Gwastadeddau Gwent\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333436\tResilient Ecological Networks (RENs), are networks of habitat linking protected sites and other biodiversity hotspots across the wider landscape, providing maximum benefit for biodiversity and human well-being.  Such networks have existing or potential for healthy resilient ecosystems, which provide a range of important ecosystem services, as well as allowing the movement of species across landscapes in response to climate change and disruptive or adverse events. The maintenance, enhancement and re-creation of RENs are vital for nature recovery.  RENs are composed of Core Zones (including protected sites such as SSSIs) and Nature Recovery Zones. These mapped together form an applied opportunity map for a Network.  The current layers available are the mapped products of the Gwent Levels Nature Network, which define resilient ecological networks in this area: Core and Nature Recovery Zone.  As well as this set of priority networks, it is useful to also be able to view these layers alongside other open datasets available which are the: - Sites of Special Scientific Interest NRW_DS98776 - Priority Ecological Networks NRW_DS125572  The output of the project has identified 42 core zones covering 18,979 hectares (ha) and 86 nature recovery zones covering 2,214 ha as two GIS layers. These broadly comprise freshwater, fen, bog, semi-natural grassland, and native woodland ecosystems. The Core Zone layer is based on sites designated for nature conservation in Wales (SAC, SSSI, NNRs, SINC, Priority habitats as identified in Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act, and Ancient Woodland database).   These layers highlight areas of opportunity and serve as an evidence base to support action planning, while remaining flexible and not prescriptive in terms of specific conservation measures.-Mae\u0027r haenau hyn yn gynnyrch mapio Rhwydwaith Natur Gwastadeddau Gwent, sy\u0027n diffinio rhwydweithiau ecolegol cadarn yn yr ardal hon: Parth Craidd a Pharth Adfer Natur.   Mae allbwn y prosiect wedi nodi 42 parth craidd sy\u0027n cwmpasu 18,979 hectar (ha) ac 86 parth adfer natur sy\u0027n cwmpasu 2,214 ha fel dwy haen GIS. Yn fras, mae\u0027r rhain yn cynnwys ecosystemau dŵr croyw, cors galchog, cors, glaswelltir lled-naturiol ac ecosystemau coetir brodorol.  Mae\u0027r haen Parth Craidd yn seiliedig ar safleoedd a ddynodwyd ar gyfer cadwraeth natur yng Nghymru (ACA, SoDdGA, GNG, SINC, cynefinoedd â blaenoriaeth fel y\u0027u nodir yn Adran 7 o Ddeddf yr Amgylchedd (Cymru) a chronfa ddata Coetir Hynafol).  Mae’n cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Natural Resources Wales a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Mae’n cynnwys Data’r Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif trwydded yr Arolwg Ordnans: AC0000849444. Mae’n cynnwys data sy\u0027n deillio o awdurdodau lleol Caerdydd, Sir Fynwy a Chasnewydd a data a gasglwyd fel rhan o\u0027r Bartneriaeth Lefelau Byw ac Ymddiriedolaeth Natur Gwent\tCore Zone layer is based on sites desginated for nature conservation in Wales (SAC, SSSI, NNRs, SINC and Ancient Woodland database).  Full methodology can be found in the NRW Evidence Report titled in the additional information.\tHancocks, E., Thomas, R., Waters, E. [2025]. Gwent Levels. Mapping Resilient Ecological Network core zones and nature recovery zones. NRW Evidence Reports. Report No: 855, 67 pp, Natural Resources Wales, Cardiff.\tX:\\Biological\\Ecosystem Resilience\\RENs\tLiz Hancooks - Senior Officer, People \u0026amp; Places Richard Thomas - Senior Officer, People \u0026amp; Places\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-04-09\tcreation\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/7650/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_gwent_levels_ren\t2025-12-23T10:18:23.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20373\tNRW_DS161347\t2025-03-06T16:49:33.383Z\t519\tValid\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Regulations Cycle 3 Classification (interim 2024)\tDosbarthiad Cylch 3 Rheoliadau\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (Dros Dro 2024)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333437\tThe Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England \u0026amp; Wales) Regulations 2017 (referred to as the WFD Regulations) provide a framework for managing the water environment in England and Wales. The Directive aims for “good status” of all ground and surface water (rivers, lakes, transitional water, and coastal waters). Under the WFD Regulations, a river basin management plan must be prepared for each river basin district. The plan includes environmental objectives and a summary of the programmes of measures required to achieve those objectives.  This dataset shows the boundaries and interim classification, undertaken in 2024, for The Water Framework Directive (WFD) Cycle 3. It is an update in classification for all water bodies in Wales following on from the Cycle 1, Cycle 2, and 2021 Cycle 3 baseline classification rounds.-Mae Rheoliadau\u0027r Amgylchedd Dŵr (Y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr) (Cymru a Lloegr) 2017 (y cyfeirir atynt fel Rheoliadau\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr) yn darparu fframwaith ar gyfer rheoli\u0027r amgylchedd dŵr yng Nghymru a Lloegr. Mae’r Gyfarwyddeb yn anelu at “statws da” ar gyfer yr holl ddŵr daear a dŵr wyneb (afonydd, llynnoedd, dŵr trosiannol a dyfroedd arfordirol). O dan Reoliadau\u0027r Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr, rhaid paratoi cynllun rheoli basn afon ar gyfer pob ardal basn afon. Mae\u0027r cynllun yn cynnwys amcanion amgylcheddol a chrynodeb o\u0027r rhaglenni o fesurau sydd eu hangen i gyflawni\u0027r amcanion hynny.  Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn dangos y ffiniau a\u0027r dosbarthiad dros dro, a wnaed yn 2024, ar gyfer Cylch 3 y Gyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr (WFD). Mae\u0027n ddiweddariad o ran dosbarthiad ar gyfer pob corff dŵr yng Nghymru yn dilyn rowndiau dosbarthu sylfaenol Cylch 1, Cylch 2 a Chylch 3 yn 2021.  Mae\u0027r holl gyrff dŵr wedi\u0027u hasesu ac wedi\u0027u cynnwys yn y Cynllun Rheoli Basn Afon lleol (RBMP). Mae CNC yn diweddaru\u0027r dosbarthiad bob 3 blynedd.   Sylwer, nid yw\u0027r set ddata yn cynnwys diweddariad Dŵr Daear y tro hwn.   Datganiad priodoli:  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys Data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded yr Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444.\tAll waterbodies have been assessed and are included within the local River Basin Management Plan (RBMP). NRW updates the classification every 3 years.   Please note, the dataset does not include a Groundwater update this time.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\tData Custodian: Rob Blacklidge, Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting, Environment Assessment\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-10-09-2025-03-06\tpublication-creation\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/7410/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_wfd_c3_2024_interim\t2025-10-27T17:35:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20398\tNRW_DS161348\t2025-03-11T12:07:01.017Z\t394\tValid\tEditable\tEryri Montane Lake Bryophyte Survey 2024\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333438\tRocks along the shores of montane lakes in Eryri have been found to be an important bryophyte habitat, supporting a range of rare and threatened bryophytes that grow only in this habitat.  Due to the limited niche of the species of this habitat, and in some cases very small populations, they are especially vulnerable to disturbance and a single disturbance event could lead to irreparable damage to or even loss of the Welsh population of some species. Therefore, it is of high importance to fully document the bryophyte flora of the shores of these montane lakes and identify rocks that support rare and important bryophytes and are vulnerable to disturbance.   Seven montane lakes within the Eryri SSSI were surveyed in September 2024, spread between the three main massifs of Yr Wyddfa, Glyderau and Carneddau.   Lakes were prioritised for survey based on the quality and quantity of recent bryophyte records. The full shore of each lake was walked and the locations of any rare or otherwise notable bryophytes encountered were recorded.   Important boulders were identified and documented, with any damage present noted. A full species list was recorded from the shore of each lake.\tThe field survey was undertaken over the course of five days between the 9th and 13th of September 2024. One site was visited on each day, resulting in the survey of a total of seven lakes, as three lakes are located within Cwm Clogwyn on Yr Wydffa, so could all be surveyed in one day.\tHodd, R.L. 2025. A survey of the bryophytes of the montane lakes of Eryri. NRW Evidence Report No: 879, 96pp, Natural Resources Wales.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FEryri%20Lake%20Bryophytes%202024\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\u0026amp;InitialTabId\u003dRibbon%2ERead\u0026amp;VisibilityContext\u003dWSSTabPersistence\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: non-vascular plants\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-02-25-2025-02-25\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-24T09:36:11.198Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20402\tNRW_DS161349\t2025-03-12T14:45:01.614Z\t338\tValid\tEditable\tGweunydd Nant y Twyn SSSI - National Vegetation Classification (NVC) Grassland Survey (2024)\tSoDdGA Gweunydd Nant y Twyn - Arolwg Dosbarthiad Llystyfiant Cenedlaethol (2024)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333439\tA standard Phase 2 NVC survey was undertaken at Gweunydd Nant y Twyn as part of the Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) designation process.  Gweunydd Nant y Twyn consists of a cluster of small fields, with tall, wooded hedgerows. The site is of particular importance for bryophytes.  The site comprises the original Phase 2 site survey site of Mockridge et al. 1998, which has now been resurveyed (2024) and extended to include several contiguous small fields, and a further extension centred on 5 small nearby fields, approximately 1/3rd of a km to the north, linked by a block of woodland and pasture. The total area of the Phase 2 survey site is now 39.9ha.\tStandard National Vegetation Classification (NVC) survey techniques were used. The survey was carried out in-house by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) staff.  Note, the Phase 2 survey site boundary does not coincide with the Gweunydd Nant y Twyn SSSI boundary, which excludes certain fields of more modified grassland.  Gweunydd Nant y Twyn GIS data has been added to the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey. One of the files contains sensitive species but this has been redacted before being published.\tWestwood, S. \u0026amp; Bosanquet, S. 2025. NRW Lowland Grassland Survey and Site Description of ST 19/17 Gweunydd Nant y Twyn Revisit and New Annex. NRW Report No: 864, 25pp, Natural Resources Wales, Cardiff.\tX/Biological/Phase 2 habitat mapping/Phase 2 lowland grassland  Y:\\Protected_Sites_Monitoring\\SC\\All_Area\\Nant_y_Twyn_2024_pSSSI\\Nant_y_Twyn_2024.gdb  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/landmanps2/pscaer/NewPS/Forms/NRW%20Protected%20Sites%20Document%20Set/docsethomepage.aspx?ID\u003d2\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120D52000824C7CCC16790D469B8E16E1874A147100BA418224CB5E96458B8094535919D4B50074DD1354BE7A6941919D304C42B925BF\u0026amp;List\u003ddb63e9b1-3802-45a0-989c-2c3fc8864e14\u0026amp;RootFolder\u003d%2fteams%2flandmanps2%2fpscaer%2fNewPS%2fGweunydd+Nant+y+Twyn+SSSI\u0026amp;RecSrc\u003d%2fteams%2flandmanps2%2fpscaer%2fNewPS%2fGweunydd+Nant+y+Twyn+SSSI\u0026amp;InitialTabId\u003dRibbon.Read\u0026amp;VisibilityContext\u003dWSSTabPersistence\u0026amp;xsdata\%3d\u0026amp;sdata\u003dNXVoTCtwQnA4d0J5UkJuTklodlo3dTR4S3BJei8zUUQzWmMwdmFobiszVT0%3d\u0026amp;clickparams\u003deyAiWC1BcHBOYW1lIiA6ICJNaWNyb3NvZnQgT3V0bG9vayIsICJYLUFwcFZlcnNpb24iIDogIjE2LjAuMTgzMjQuMjAyNDAiLCAiT1MiIDogIldpbmRvd3MiIH0%3D#InplviewHasha1022bbe-43e8-4819-8a85-12eeae834e73\u003dFolderCTID%3D0x0120D52000824C7CCC16790D469B8E16E1874A147100BA418224CB5E96458B8094535919D4B50074DD1354BE7A6941919D304C42B925BF-RecSrc%3D%252Fteams%252Flandmanps2%252Fpscaer%252FNewPS%252FGweunydd%2520Nant%2520y%2520Twyn%2520SSSI-InitialTabId%3DRibbon%252ERead-VisibilityContext%3DWSSTabPersistence-WebPartID%3D%7BA1022BBE--43E8--4819--8A85--12EEAE834E73%7D-FilterField1%3DTitle-FilterValue1%3DPhase%25202%2520Survey%252C%2520Maps%2520and%2520Report%25202024\tSophie Higgett, Natura 2000 Sustainable Management Advisor - Newport, Blaenau Gwent \u0026amp; Caerphilly Environment Team.  Sophie has now left NRW, she advised to contact the team with any queries: et.newportcaerphilly\u0026amp;blaenaugwent@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-03-06\tcreation\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3600/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_phase_2_grassland\t2025-09-22T15:25:34.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20433\tNRW_DS161350\t2025-03-19T09:41:02.437Z\t460\tValid\tEditable\tInland Fisheries River Restoration Strategic Allocation of Funds\tAdfer Afonydd Pysgodfeydd Mewndirol Dyraniad Strategol o Gronfeydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333530\tThis data is two layers consisting of; Habitat Restoration and Other Remedial delivery associated with the Inland Fisheries Habitat Restoration grant.   Delivery is carried out by River Trusts and summary data is collated by Afonydd Cymru.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tThe underlying data was collected as a result of a series of commissions from Natural Resources Wales (NRW) to Afonydd Cymru (AC) to undertake river habitat surveys to identify riverine habitat pressures on salmon and sea trout. The location comprising of either discrete points or upstream and downstream extents. Subsequently AC and the Welsh rivers trusts have received grant funding (Inland Fisheries Habitat Restoration Grants, funded via the NaCE programme) from NRW to address these pressures, resulting in further data on measures undertaken, and additional pressures being identified.   The data is collated by AC with an iterative quality assurance process between NRW, AC and the individual trusts being applied.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\Inland Fisheries River Restoration Strategic Allocation of Funds\tRhodri Powell, Specialist Advisor River Restoration, Freshwater Ecosystems and Fisheries Management.  There is no requirement for Data Map Wales but the data is to be included in an AGOL application.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-03-18\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-24T14:43:07.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n20509\tNRW_DS161352\t2025-03-31T14:53:23.287Z\t231\tValid\tEditable\tAn Evaluation of the Current Use of Farmed vs Wild-caught Ragworm bait in Wales - Survey Results\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333532\tThis is the survey outputs of a study evaluating the current use of farmed vs wild-caught ragworm bait in Wales, identifying any barriers to the use of farmed ragworm bait, and, with those barriers in mind, recommending measures that can be taken to encourage the use of farmed ragworm bait.   The data is an excel spreadsheet detailing questionnaire results which were undertaken by Bangor University under commision by Natural Resources Wales\tQuestionnaire (online \u0026amp; face-to-face via structured interview facilitating online entry)\tE.S. Morris-Webb, R. Parry, S.R. Jenkin Intertidal Bait Collection Management: Code of Conduct and Farmed Bait (Ragworm) Contract. NRW Evidence Report No. 883. Natural Resources Wales, Cardiff\tQuestionnaire Results stored here - https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/advice/mar/MNN22-25/_layouts/15/Doc.aspx?sourcedoc\u003d%7B7170031E-0717-4C32-886C-54DE0ABCCE4B%7D\u0026amp;action\u003dedit\u0026amp;source\u003dhttps%3A%2F%2Fcyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com%2Fteams%2Fadvice%2Fmar%2FMNN22-25%2FBaitCollectn%2FForms%2FNRW%2520View.aspx%3Fcsf%3D1%26web%3D1%26CID%3D466c16f7%252Dc54f%252D4285%252Dbe1e%252Ded89e9fa3bf1%26RootFolder%3D%252Fteams%252Fadvice%252Fmar%252FMNN22%252D25%252FBaitCollectn%252FCode%2520of%2520Conduct%2520and%2520Farmed%2520Bait%2520Workstream%26FolderCTID%3D0x012000911566D4BD76D243A66C042306FD1A40%23InplviewHash9effeb0a-6b03-4b90-aca0-0063f964c6b2%3DPaged%253DTRUE-p_SortBehavior%253D0-p_FileLeafRef%253DChris%252520Smith%252520%25252d%252520Dragon%252520Baits%25252emsg-p_ID%253D327-FolderCTID%253D0x012000911566D4BD76D243A66C042306FD1A40-PageFirstRow%253D31\tDewi R Evans - Specialist Officer, Intertidal Management\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2025-04-01\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-05-14T08:34:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n20533\tNRW_DS161353\t2025-04-04T08:43:01.493Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommon Standards Monitoring of nationally important cave invertebrate assemblages in Ogof Draenen and Ogof Ffynnon Ddu, 2023 \u0026amp; 2024\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333533\tTo be completed when report is finalised.\t\t\tData will be stored on the Welsh Invertebrate Database on Recorder 6\tMichael Howe - Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-07-08T09:17:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20547\tNRW_DS161354\t2025-04-08T12:38:59.712Z\t207\tValid\tEditable\tRapid Evidence Assessment of the Impacts of Organic Manures on the Water Quality of Rivers\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333534\tThe principal aim of this study was to improve Natural Resources Wales’ understanding of how organic manures, particularly from the poultry sector, can impact the nutrient status of rivers, with a focus on phosphorus (P). The methodology used to produce this Rapid Evidence Assessment (REA) included a systematic review of relevant literature to establish an evidence base, which was used to address primary and secondary Research Questions (RQs). The scope of the study was restricted to answering the RQs only, and, as a REA, the literature searches and critical appraisal of the literature were not as comprehensive as a full systemic review.  This metadata is created for the finalised evidence base created from evidence reviews and research questions.\tAn REA was undertaken following the methods described by Collins et al. (2015). Structured searches of the academic literature were conducted using the search engine Scopus. Search strings were designed based on the adapted PICO framework. Searches were limited to the article title, abstract and keywords and restricted to the last 25 years (1999 – 2023) to ensure the most relevant information was captured.  The geographic scope was defined as countries with the same bioclimatic classification as the UK. The top 80 most relevant studies were downloaded per search term. The top 25 most relevant articles (not limited by publication date) were downloaded for each manure type and search term, and duplicates removed with the original list. A final search was conducted that focused on UK-specific literature, as this is the primary focus of the review, following which the top 20 articles (ordered by relevance) were selected. Additional unstructured searches were conducted using Google Scholar. The relevance of each article was initially reviewed based on the title, and designated a category of ‘relevant’, ‘somewhat relevant’, or ‘not relevant’. Following this, the second stage of the filtering process reviewed the abstracts of those articles deemed ‘relevant’ or ‘somewhat relevant’ at the title filtering stage into ‘relevant’ or ‘not relevant’.\tVallack, H., Hargreaves, A., Gibbs, H. 2025. Rapid Evidence Assesment of the Impacts of Organic Manures on the Water Quality of Rivers. NRW Evidence Report No: 774, 122pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/waterman/gs/wg/SACWQP/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fwaterman%2Fgs%2Fwg%2FSACWQP%2FPhase%202%20products%2FTechnical%20Products%2FWorkstream%204%2FTask%203%20Evidence%20Needs%2FRapid%20Evidence%20Assessment%2FProduct%203%20%2D%20Final%20Report%20%28Mar%202024%29\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x012000289C178199C7C343A0E69569780F56CD\u0026amp;View\u003d%7BF267B241%2DA6BD%2D465C%2D9E90%2D12B892E2F18D%7D\tTimothy Jones. Specialist Advisor, Environmental Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-04-04\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:34:34.147Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n20569\tNRW_DS161355\t2025-04-11T07:53:51.837Z\t372\tValid\tEditable\tWelsh Acoustic Marine Mammal Survey (WAMMS)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333535\tThe Welsh Acoustic Marine Mammal Survey (WAMMS)  aim was to develop an effective and robust method for measuring underwater noise and the occurrence of cetaceans (whales, dolphins and porpoises) in marine protected areas (MPAs) around the Welsh coastline. To test and demonstrate these monitoring methods, an 18-month pilot study was undertaken as part of the project in the North Anglesey Marine Special Area of Conservation (SAC). As well as illustrating how monitoring could be carried out elsewhere in Welsh marine waters, this pilot study provided baseline data on ambient noise and marine mammal detections at the site.  Three monitoring locations were selected within the North Anglesey Marine SAC: one at Holyhead deep, two off Point Lynas and one situated 1.7km off the coast and the other 5.2 km, at the boundary of the SAC.\tAt the three proposed monitoring locations, underwater noise data will be collected using the latest generation of Soundtraps (ST600HF, Ocean Instruments NZ) as well as click detector FPODs (Chelonia). Equipment will be deployed over an 18-month field season (May 2023 – November 2024) to better understand ambient noise levels and cetacean presence in the North Anglesey Marine SAC.   Further details on the methodology report can be seen in the 2023 Welsh Acoustic Marine Mammal Survey (WAMMS) Methodology Report.\tPutland, R., Merchant, N., Veneruso, G. (2023). Welsh Acoustic Marine Mammal Survey (WAMMS) Methodology Report title. Cefas Project Report for Natural Resource Wales, 26 pp.  Merchant, N.D., Putland, R., Roberts, G., Veneruso, G. 2025 Welsh Acoustic Marine Mammal Survey (WAMMS) Final Field Report and Data Analysis. NRW [Report Series] Report No: 860, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tData will be stored on the Crown Estate Marine Data Exchange Portal.\tAlex Zalewski - Marine Ecology Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-04-11-2025-04-11\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T13:51:42.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20570\tNRW_DS161356\t2025-04-11T11:25:56.162Z\t59\tValid\tEditable\tImpacts of bait collection on Welsh Marine Protected Areas (MPAs)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333536\tSurvey to assess the impacts of three bait collection methods on Welsh MPAs - bait digging (Sites : Angle Bay, Four Mile Bridge, Penmon - Beaumaris), boulder turning (Penmon-Beaumaris, West of Great Orme), crab shelters (Deganwy, Bangor Pier). Samples taken from control and impact sites. Infaunal cores, sediment cores, benthic megafauna (i.e. razor clam, cockle) quadrat, worm cast count, seagrass coverage, boulder epifauna, boulder turning classification\tBoulder turning activities were monitored at west of Great Orme, Conwy and Penmon-Beaumaris, Anglesey. The west of Great Orme site falls within the Aber Afon Conwy Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and the Penmon-Beaumaris site is situated in the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation (SAC) and Glannau Penmon-Biwmares SSSI. Both sites are subject to boulder turning which has the potential to impact some designated features within each site.\tGihwala, K.N., West, V.A., Frost, N.J, Robbins, K. 2024. Impacts of bait collection on Welsh Marine Protected Areas: Year two survey report. NRW Evidence Report, Report No: 482, 266pp, ABPmer, Southampton\tMarine Recorder Online (eb56c392-90f5-45ae-8ecd-c57dc47e3639).  Associated data held in DMS https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:f:/r/teams/advice/mar/MNN22-25/BaitCollectn/Outputs/ABPmer%20survey%20raw%20data?csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;e\u003d0X2cxO\tAlex Zalewski, Marine Ecology Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-04-11-2025-12-17\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-12-18T09:18:18.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n20573\tNRW_DS161357\t2025-04-17T08:09:33.799Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA Survey of Welsh populations of the Grizzled Skipper Pyrgus malvae, 2023-2024\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333537\tTo be added once report is completed.\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-07-08T09:26:23.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20802\tNRW_DS161359\t2025-05-16T12:53:31.642Z\t282\tValid\tEditable\tNational Peatland Action Programme (NPAP) - Water Vole and Otter Records\tRhaglen Genedlaethol Gweithredu Mawndiroedd (NPAP) - Cofnodion Llygod y Dŵr a Dyfrgwn\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333539\tThis data shows evidence of water vole and otter activity in point data form. Surveys have been undertaken by our contractor to inform National Peatland Action Programme approaches to peatland restoration. No limiting information.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tThe surveys are typically conducted on an annual basis in association with a water vole licence.  For a redacted version of this dataset, please use the data published on the National Biodiversity Network Atlas. For full access to this dataset a licence will need to be requested.\t\tX:\\Physical\\Peat\\NPAP\\NPAP - Water Vole and Otter Records.lyr  Copy of NBN data in Darwin Core format - Z:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\Data Mgmt\\Data Services Data Publishing Area\\NBN Data\\NRW_DS161359_NPAP_VOLE_OTTER\tRobert Bacon, Senior Specialist Officer, National Action Programme for Peatlands\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-05-19-2025-07-15\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr3553\t2025-11-18T17:08:05.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20811\tNRW_DS161360\t2025-05-20T10:56:09.693Z\t265\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Protected Areas Compensation Habitat\tCynefin Iawndal Ardaloedd Morol Gwarchodedig\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333630\tThis data is a map of the extent of marine areas in Welsh waters that have been identified as compensatory habitat within Welsh waters and along Welsh coastlines under Regulation 68 (Compensatory measures) of The Conservation of Habitats and Species Regulations 2017.   These areas are afforded the same protection and management requirements as the SAC Natura 2000 designated sites.   This also includes areas delivered by the National Habitat Creation Programme.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.-Mae\u0027r data hwn yn fap sy’n dangos yr ardaloedd morol yn nyfroedd Cymru sydd wedi\u0027u nodi fel cynefinoedd cydadferol o fewn dyfroedd Cymru ac ar hyd arfordiroedd Cymru o dan Reoliad 68 (Mesurau cydadferol) Rheoliadau Cadwraeth Cynefinoedd a Rhywogaethau 2017.  Rhoddir yr un gofynion gwarchodaeth a rheoli i\u0027r ardaloedd hyn â safleoedd dynodedig Natura 2000 ACA.  Mae hyn hefyd yn cynnwys ardaloedd a ddarperir gan y Rhaglen Genedlaethol Creu Cynefinoedd.  Mae data wedi\u0027i ddarparu naill ai gan CNC neu gan ddatblygwyr, yn dilyn cytundeb Trwydded Forol ar gyfer cynefin cydadferol.  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tData has been provided either by NRW or by developers, following Marine Licence agreement for compensatory habitat.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Compensatory Habitats.lyr\tKirsten Ramsay, Marine and Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-05-20-2025-08-06\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/7679/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_mpa_compensation\t2025-11-18T17:17:20.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n20859\tNRW_DS161361\t2025-06-10T07:47:26.054Z\t64\tValid\tEditable\tAerial survey of seals in Wales 2023\tArolwg o\u0027r awyr o forloi yng Nghymru 2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333631\tThis data is counts of seals hauled out during aerial surveys of the coast of Wales in August 2023.   Data consists of date and time, site number, photographic frame start and end, grid reference, number of seals and geographic region.\tData was collected by the Sea Mammal Research Unit, University of St Andrews (SMRU) during their oblique aerial photographic surveys of the coast of Wales in August 2023.   Survey followed standard techniques as outlined in Thompson et al. (2019).  INFOMATION WARNING:   When re-using the data described, end users must acknowledge the contribution of the originators (Sea Mammal Research Unit) in collecting the data.    NRW does not authorise the commercial re-use of SMRUs preexisting IPR (e.g. methodologies or know-how) however this does not affect re-use of the resultant data described in this metadata.\tThomson, D. 2025. Census of grey seals (Halichoerus grypus) around Wales during August 2023 using aerial survey. NRW Evidence Report Series Report No: 865, 28pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor\tDMS Document: EVID-307-1112  Wales Seall counts 2023.xlsx https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Marine-Evidence/mm/Evidence%20Base%20%20Contracts/Seal%20Monitoring%20and%20Research/aerial%20survey/Wales%20seal%20counts%202023.xlsx?d\u003dw5e051d8b6eb94caaba39d3e53957789e  GIS layer: To be created and loaded to the X drive\tThomas Stringell, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine Species, Natural Resources Planning/Marine and Freshwater Ecosytem Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-06-10\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T09:47:27.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n20931\tNRW_DS161363\t2025-06-17T08:57:46.070Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDrinking Water Safeguard Zones 2023\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333633\tAny waterbodies in Wales that are used to provide drinking water are designated as drinking water protected areas (DrWPAs). The designation aims to protect the water and prevent any deterioration in quality that could increase the required treatment of water to meet Drinking Water Standards. Drinking Water Protected Areas are required to be identified under the Water Environment (Water Framework Directive) (England and Wales) Regulations 2017. Within these drinking water protected areas we work with water companies to identify raw water sources that are ‘at risk’ of deterioration which would result in the need for additional treatment. These areas are degsinated as Safeguard Zones where additional measures can be used to address any factors that may reduce the water quality. The SgZ have been identified based on DCWW monitoring and risk assessments and are not limited to the DWPA but based on the wider Water Company identified catchment areas.  A catchment is the term used to describe the area of land where water drains or collects. After rain, water flows over the land, through soil and rock, into streams and eventually the main river. The boundary of a catchment follows natural slopes, hills or mountains within a landscape and determines if water flows into one river or another or subsequently into a reservoir.\tThe boundaries in the dataset are reviewed annually and are subject to change.\t\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\Drinking Water Safeguard Zones 2023.lyr\tSuzanne Lawn Suzanne.Lawn@cyfoethnaturiolcymru.gov.uk\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Dwr Cymru Welsh Water\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t2025-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:20.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n20967\tNRW_DS161364\t2025-06-24T10:47:22.312Z\t339\tValid\tEditable\tThe potential reintroduction of Dicranum Undulatum to Cors Fochno: an assessment of future climate suitability\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333634\tDicranum undulatum is categorised as ‘Endangered’ on the Red Data List for Wales, and is considered to be of principal importance for conservation under Section 7 of The Environment (Wales) Act 2016. Due to the widespread loss and degradation of peatlands, it has undergone a large decline in Britain, and the only surviving population in Wales occurs in Denbighshire, on Whixall and Bettisfield Mosses. The species was known from Cors Fochno, where it was last seen in 1967. Reasons for the extinction are uncertain, but former fires may be partly to blame. Following significant efforts to improve the habitat condition of the bog, including the elimination of fires and large-scale hydrological restoration, a recent assessment concluded that parts of the site appear to contain favourable habitat for D. undulatum and that reintroduction may be a possibility.   The aim of this study is to investigate whether a suitable macroclimate for the species is likely to occur at Cors Fochno in the medium-term.\tDistribution data from Global Biodiversity Information Facility (GBIF) and Climate Data from WorldClim v.2.1 were used for this study.  Using generalised linear modelling, two species distribution models of Dicranum undulatum were constructed from the latest bioclimatic variables of WorldClim, one covering Britain and Ireland, and the other covering Europe.\tCallaghan, D.A. 2025. The potential reintroduction of Dicranum undulatum to Cors Fochno: an assessment of future climate suitability. NRW Evidence Report No: 936, xx pp, Natural Resources Wales.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FDicranum%20undulatum%20Climate%20Modelling\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Bryophytes, Lichens and Fungi, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-06-24\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T15:14:51.258Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n20968\tNRW_DS161365\t2025-06-24T13:24:50.634Z\t242\tValid\tEditable\tMonitoring of the Atlantic woodland bryophyte assemblage of Coed Crafnant, Meirionnydd\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333635\tCoed Crafnant is a nature reserve owned and managed by the North Wales Wildlife Trust, comprising part of that woodland plus the contiguous Coed Dolbebin. Almost all of the oceanic woodland habitat of the reserve is contained within Rhinog SSSI and Meirionnydd Oakwoods SAC.   The first comprehensive bryophyte survey of the reserve was undertaken during January 2014, when baseline monitoring data was collected. A further survey was undertaken in May 2022, which recorded areas of the reserve not covered in 2014, but did not repeat the species grid-mapping exercise of 2014 due to time constraints. This 2025 study aimed to repeat the grid-mapping in order to assess what changes have occurred within the Atlantic woodland bryophyte assemblage.\tThis survey was undertaken between 17–20 February 2025 and used field recording and GPS.   In the survey, all of the grid cells surveyed in 2014 were revisited and GPS waypoints were recorded for all colonies of the above four species plus a further seven species of the Atlantic woodland assemblage, including Cololejeunea microscopica, Drepanolejeunea hamatifolia, Harpalejeunea molleri, Plagiochila bifaria, Plagiochila exigua, Plagiochila punctata and Plagiochila spinulosa. The former three species were absent from the surveyed grid cells in 2014, whilst the other species were present in 2014 but were not grid-mapped due to time constraints.   As in 2014, the GPS waypoints were loaded into GIS to produce maps of grid cells occupied by each species and a count of the number of cells occupied.\tCallaghan, D.A. 2025. Monitoring of the Atlantic woodland bryophyte assemblage of Coed Crafnant, Meirionnydd. NRW Evidence Report No: 920, 50 pp, Natural Resources Wales.\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Species/bry/Bryophyte%20Evidence/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2FSpecies%2Fbry%2FBryophyte%20Evidence%2F0%20Bryophyte%20Evidence%20Report%20data%2FCoed%20Crafnant%20Bryophyte%20Monitoring%202025\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x01200090F8CB7E3F3D584CA9A5222F3D74DFA6\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B1B23357D%2D5146%2D4D17%2D8934%2DEC9B23429415%7D\tSam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor: Bryophytes, Lichens \u0026amp; Fungi, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-06-24\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-03T10:35:04.120Z\tnobody nobody\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n21005\tNRW_DS161366\t2025-06-30T13:55:17.204Z\t387\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Marine Benthic Invertebrates - Geospatial\tAnifeiliaid Infertebrata Benthig Morol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333636\tThis data collates all the benthic invertebrate samples collected within Wales from 1988 onwards by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales under the Water Framework Directive (WFD) and Clean Seas Environment Monitoring Program (CSEMP) sampling programs. The dataset contains the presence, and abundance, of benthic invertebrate species at specific marine monitoring points held within the KiECO database.  The data was collected using either a  Day Grab (0.1 m2) or Hammon Grab (0.1m2) for subtidal samples and either 3 x 0.01m2 cores (Core Diameter 11.28cm) or a Box Corer (0.1m2) for intertidal surveys. Data are laboratory assessed and quality assured following the National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control (NMBAQC) scheme.  For many of our marine survey techniques we record an NGR at the site because this acts as a central/representative location around which we collect lots of samples. An NGR is then also recorded against each of the samples to give a more accurate sampling location.-Mae\u0027r data hwn yn coladu\u0027r holl samplau infertebrata benthig a gasglwyd yng Nghymru o 1988 ymlaen gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru ac Asiantaeth yr Amgylchedd Cymru gynt. Gwybodaeth am bresenoldeb a helaethrwydd rhywogaethau infertebrata benthig mewn mannau monitro morol penodol a gedwir o fewn cronfa ddata KiECO.  Casglwyd data o fewn y maes gan ddefnyddio naill ai Day Grab (0.1 m2) neu Hammon Grab (0.1m2) ar gyfer samplau islanwol a naill ai creiddiau 3 x 0.01m2 (diamedr craidd 11.28cm) neu digreiddiwr blwch (0.1m2). Asesir data mewn labordy a sicrheir ansawdd yn dilyn y cynllun Rheoli Ansawdd Dadansoddol Biolegol Morol Cenedlaethol.  Ar gyfer llawer o\u0027n technegau  arolygon morol, rydym yn cofnodi Cyfeirnod Grid Cenedlaethol ar y safle oherwydd mae hwn yn gweithredu fel lleoliad canolog/cynrychioliadol yr ydym yn casglu llawer o samplau o\u0027i gwmpas.  Yna mae Cyfeirnod Grid Cenedlaethol hefyd yn cael ei gofnodi yn erbyn pob un o\u0027r samplau i roi lleoliad samplu mwy cywir.  Golygiadau Mae rhywfaint o wybodaeth wedi’i golygu i sicrhau bod y data ar gael yn agored. Mae hyn yn cynnwys:  Data trydydd parti Data a gasglwyd ar gyfer ymchwiliadau parhaus Arolygon wedi’u canslo neu heb eu cwblhau Amlder Diweddaru Mae\u0027r data hwn yn cael ei ddiweddaru\u0027n barhaus gyda\u0027r rhan fwyaf o\u0027r newidiadau\u0027n cael eu gwneud yn y gwanwyn. Yn aml, nid yw data o dymhorau arolygon yn cael eu mewnbynnu ar adeg eu cipio a gall fod sawl mis cyn iddo gael ei roi yn y system. Mae\u0027r set ddata allanol yn cael ei diweddaru\u0027n fisol i gynnwys data newydd, a gall hefyd gynnwys cywiriadau i ddata a gyhoeddwyd yn flaenorol oherwydd gwaith sicrhau ansawdd.  Rhybuddion Gwybodaeth Mae arolygon yn cael eu cynnal gan ystod o ddulliau arolygu, ac nid yw\u0027r data o un arolwg o reidrwydd yn uniongyrchol gymaradwy ag un arall. Argymhellir bod y defnyddiwr yn gwirio\u0027r dull arolygu a ddefnyddiwyd yn y data a ddarperir. Gwnawn ein gorau i osgoi problemau ansawdd data, ond gan fod y setiau data hyn yn adlewyrchu’r data sydd gennym, gallant gynnwys gwallau. Darperir y data fel set o dablau data cysylltiedig oherwydd maint a strwythur y data. Tybir bod dealltwriaeth sylfaenol o ecoleg a data perthynol yn bodoli. Gwybodaeth bellach Gweler y ddogfen ganlynol am wybodaeth bellach  Cydnabyddiaeth Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThis data is continually update with most changes made in the Spring. Data from survey seasons is often not enter at the time of capture and can be several months before it is entered into the system. The external dataset is updated monthly to include new data, and may also contain corrections to previously published data due to quality assurance work.  Information Warnings Surveys are carried out by a range of survey methods, the data from one survey is not necessarily directly comparable to another. The user is recommended to check the survey method used in the data provided.  We do our best to avoid data quality problems, but as these datasets reflect the data we hold, they may contain errors  The data is provided as a set of related data tables due to the quantity and structure of the data. A basic understanding relational data is assumed.\t\tX/monitoring/Kieco\tBianca Bacchi\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-07-10-2026-01-06\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6727/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_marine_macroinvertebrates\t2026-01-09T14:19:55.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n21006\tNRW_DS161367\t2025-07-01T09:00:33.690Z\t169\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwater Cycle 3 - Chemical and Quantitative\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333637\tWater Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwater Cycle 3 Chemical and Quantitative datasets are a subset of Water Framework Directive (WFD) Groundwaterbodies Cycle 3, which is already published on DataMapWales.  Article 2, clause 2 of the WFD defines groundwaterbodies as \u0027…all water which is below the surface of the ground in the saturation zone and in direct contact with the ground or subsoil\u0027.   For the purposes of reporting under the WFD, a groundwater body represents a distinct body of groundwater flow with a coherent flow unit including recharge and discharge areas with little flow across the boundaries. These reflect hydrogeological characteristics containing information on flow and stage properties, recharge and vulnerability to pollution. This has been undertaken through defining aquifers into different types and broken into catchment units at Catchment Abstraction Management Strategy (CAMS) scale.\tThe primary input dataset is \u00271:625K Classified Aquifer Geology\u0027. This dataset has been digitised directly from the hard copy \u00271:250K Solid Geology Map\u0027 and classified according to aquifer type and are therefore directly derived from the underlying BGS data. These classifications were verified by the British Geology Survey (BGS). These data have gone out for consultation at Area level and in some instances 1:50K Solid Geology has been used to define localised boundaries. Since waterbodies are attributed with a unique identifier \u0027WB_ID\u0027 this dataset can be linked directly to other WFD data sources such as physical characteristics, risk, classification and proposed objectives.\t\tX:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Groundwater Cycle 3 2021 Chemical.lyr  X:\\EU Directives\\Water Framework Directive\\Cycle3\\WFD Groundwater Cycle 3 2021 Quantitative.lyr\tInternal custodian - Rob Blacklidge - Environment Assessment, Reporting and GIS .\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-04-19\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:20.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n21017\tNRW_DS161368\t2025-07-03T08:05:19.466Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGeomorphological Surveys to Support the Upper Wye Restoration Project\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333638\tThis dataset contains GIS outputs from geomorphological assessments conducted by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) in 2023–2024 for the Upper Wye Catchment Restoration Project, funded under the Nature and Climate Emergency (NACE) programme. The surveys recorded physical river characteristics to identify restoration opportunities, focusing on erosion, barriers, bank protection, and floodplain conditions. Data were collected through desk-based studies, field surveys, and analysis, and include reach surveys (polygon) and point-based features with assigned priority scores. Interpretation should consider survey limitations, including temporal changes and site-specific variability. Full methodology and prioritisation criteria are detailed in the Geomorphological Surveys of the Ithon and Aran Rivers for the Upper Wye Restoration Project report.\tData collected from in-person walkover surveys conducted over 2023 and 2024 using a bespoke data collection form created on ArcGIS Survey123.\t\tY:\\Geomorphology South\\Upper Wye Restoration\\OUTPUTS \u0026amp; Y:\\Geomorphology South\\Upper Wye Restoration\\OUTPUTS_rnd2\tDale Cox - Specialist Advisor, Geomorphology\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-07-03\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:20.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n21166\tNRW_DS161369\t2025-08-01T08:17:18.303Z\t8\tValid\tEditable\tBroadscale coastal rollback constraints and opportunity\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333639\tThis dataset is an ArcGIS geodatabase which provides layers showing lengths of coastline where there are constraints to or opportunities for coastal habitat rollback. The three habitats considered are: saltmarsh, sand dune and shingle.  Lengths are also given for constraints and opportunity within urban areas.   The data was developed from a previous project on coastal squeeze. It provides a high level assessment of coastal rollback to inform future, more detailed, work. The study is detailed in: Fairley, I., 2025. A high-level appraisal of areas of constraint and areas of opportunity for coastal habitat roll-back. NRW Evidence Report No: 859, 71pp.\tThis coastal rollback data was derived from data produced as part of a coastal squeeze project (NRW evidence report 789). The methodology used to develop the coastal rollback data is described in detail in NRW evidence report 859.  In brief, areas of rollback opportunity were defined as areas where there was coastal habitat around the high tide line and low lying land behind, with no coastal structures; areas of constraint were defined as areas where there was coastal habitat around the high tide line, low lying land behind but a coastal structure preventing rollback.\tFairley, I., 2025. A high-level appraisal of areas of constraint and areas of opportunity for coastal habitat roll-back. NRW Evidence Report No: 859, 71pp.\tY:\\Marine\\Rollback_from_Coastal_squeeze\\Rollback_data_report_859.gdb  awaiting X:Drive load\tIain Fairley - Specialist advisor: marine and coastal physical science\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-08-01\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-08-05T14:48:38.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n21189\tNRW_DS161370\t2025-08-06T08:02:27.330Z\t113\tValid\tEditable\tNutrient Sensitive Marine SAC Freshwater Catchments\tDalgylchoedd dŵr croyw ACA Morol sy\u0027n sensitif i faetholion\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333730\tThe Nutrient sensitive Marine SAC freshwater catchments layer depicts the surface water freshwater catchments that discharge to Marine SAC’s supporting habitat features particularly sensitive to nutrient enrichment.   Catchment polygons are split by Special Area of Conservation and receiving waterbody (Water Framework Directive Regulations, Transitional or Coastal waterbody).  Please also see metadata number NRW_DS161371 \"Marine SAC freshwater catchments requiring nitrogen neutrality\" which is related.\tThe Nutrient sensitive Marine SAC freshwater catchments layer has been created in QGIS largely using the PCRaster toolset.  Data sources from DataMapWales:  - ‘LIDAR data download’ - comprising Welsh Govt LIDAR 2020-2023  - ‘Wales Boundary’ (High Water Mark),   - ‘Wales National Marine Plan Boundary’ - (Normal Tidal Limit)  - ‘SACs’   - ‘WFD Cycle 2 TraC Waterbodies’.  All Open Government Data sources.  Data Processing: (QGIS largely using PCRaster toolset)   - Native resolution LIDAR Digital Terrain Model (DTM) geotiff data was resampled at 10m x 10m resolution.   - DTM converted to PC raster format.  - DTM clipped to High Water using Wales (High Water Mark) polygon.  - DEM converted to Local Drain Direction (LDD) raster having filled pits in DTM (using ‘LDDcreate’ tool for both tasks)  - LDD raster processed (using ‘pit’ tool) to identify drainage outlets to the DTM boundary, creating an ‘outlets’ raster.  - LDD and Outlets rasters processed (using ‘catchments’ tool) to create upstream catchments flowing to the outlet points, one for each outlet. Created ‘catchments’ raster.  - Converted Catchments raster to vector format.  Tidying up and adding data attributes:  - The Catchments vector layer was clipped using the National Marine Plan boundary (Normal Tidal Limits) to better represent the land/sea interface and create a closer match with WFD Transitional water body boundaries.  - Catchment polygons were assigned values representing the SAC and WFD Transitional or Coastal waterbody they flowed into, or could directly affect (i.e. where adjacent) – created using the SAC and WFD Cycle 2 WB layers.   Adjustment for Marine SAC nutrient use:  - Catchments (polygons) with common combined Waterbody and SAC values were merged.   - The Nutrient sensitive Marine SAC freshwater catchments layer still has some areas of flat coastal drainage that needs further data processing e.g. the boundary between the Dee and the Clywd freshwater catchments, and the boundary between Kenfig SAC and Port Talbot (Swansea Bay coastal WB).\tMarine SAC condition assessments published on NRW website:  https://www.naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/environmental-topics/wildlife-and-biodiversity/protected-areas-of-land-and-seas/condition-assessments-for-welsh-european-marine-sites-ems/?lang\u003den\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine SAC freshwater catchments\\Nutrient sensitive Marine SAC freshwater catchments.lyr\tLucie Skates, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine Planning Implementation\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2025-07-31\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-24T13:27:16.321Z\tMatthew Wareham\t-\tPrivate record\n21190\tNRW_DS161371\t2025-08-06T08:22:07.851Z\t503\tValid\tEditable\tMarine SAC Freshwater Catchments Requiring Nitrogen Neutrality\tDalgylchoedd dŵr croyw ACA morol sydd angen niwtraliaeth nitrogen\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333731\tThe Marine SAC Freshwater Catchments Requiring Nitrogen Neutrality layer is a subset of the Nutrient Sensitive Marine SAC Freshwater Catchments layer.  It only shows the three freshwater catchments that require nitrogen neutrality based on NRW’s advice letter of 25th July 2025 and maybe subject to future changes.  Please also see metadata entry NRW_DS161370 \"Nutrient Sensitive Marine SAC Freshwater Catchments\".\tThe Nutrient sensitive Marine SAC freshwater catchments layer has been created in QGIS largely using the PCRaster toolset.  Data sources from DataMapWales: - ‘LIDAR data download’ - comprising Welsh Government LIDAR 2020-2023 - ‘Wales Boundary’ (High Water Mark), - ‘Wales National Marine Plan Boundary’ - (Normal Tidal Limit) - ‘SACs’ - ‘WFD Cycle 2 TraC Waterbodies’. All Open Government Data sources.  Data Processing: (QGIS largely using PCRaster toolset) - Native resolution LIDAR Digital Terrain Model (DTM) geotiff data was resampled at 10m x 10m resolution. - DTM converted to PC raster format. - DTM clipped to High Water using Wales (High Water Mark) polygon. - DEM converted to Local Drain Direction (LDD) raster having filled pits in DTM (using ‘LDDcreate’ tool for both tasks) - LDD raster processed (using ‘pit’ tool) to identify drainage outlets to the DTM boundary, creating an ‘outlets’ raster. - LDD and Outlets rasters processed (using ‘catchments’ tool) to create upstream catchments flowing to the outlet points, one for each outlet. Created ‘catchments’ raster. - Converted Catchments raster to vector format.  Tidying up and adding data attributes: - The Catchments vector layer was clipped using the National Marine Plan boundary (Normal Tidal Limits) to better represent the land/sea interface and create a closer match with WFD Transitional water body boundaries. - Catchment polygons were assigned values representing the SAC and WFD Transitional or Coastal waterbody they flowed into, or could directly affect (i.e. where adjacent) – created using the SAC and WFD Cycle 2 WB layers.   Adjustment for Marine SAC nutrient use: - Catchments (polygons) with common combined Waterbody and SAC values were merged.  Where there was an obvious deviation between the freshwater catchment vector layer and the existing WFD river catchment layer, resulting from man-made interventions viewable on the map data, the freshwater catchment boundary was adjusted to align with the WFD river catchment water body boundary.\tMarine SAC condition assessments published on NRW website:  https://www.naturalresources.wales/guidance-and-advice/environmental-topics/wildlife-and-biodiversity/protected-areas-of-land-and-seas/condition-assessments-for-welsh-european-marine-sites-ems/?lang\u003den\tX:\\Monitoring\\Marine SAC freshwater catchments\\Marine SAC freshwater catchments requiring nitrogen neutrality.lyr\tLucie Skates, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine Planning Implementation\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-07-31-2025-09-08\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/7750/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_marice_sac_req_nit_neut\t2025-09-09T15:13:56.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n21202\tNRW_DS161372\t2025-08-07T11:53:17.098Z\t184\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Marine Ecology: Marine Benthic Invertebrates\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333732\tThis data collates all the benthic invertebrate samples collected within Wales from 1988 onwards by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales under the Water Framework Directive (WFD) and Clean Seas Environment Monitoring Program (CSEMP) sampling programs. The dataset contains the presence, and abundance, of benthic invertebrate species at specific marine monitoring points held within the KiECO database.  The data was collected using either a Day Grab (0.1 m2) or Hammon Grab (0.1m2) for subtidal samples and either 3 x 0.01m2 cores (Core Diameter 11.28cm) or a Box Corer (0.1m2) for intertidal surveys. Data are laboratory assessed and quality assured following the National Marine Biological Analytical Quality Control (NMBAQC) scheme.  For many of our marine survey techniques we record an NGR at the site because this acts as a central/representative location around which we collect lots of samples. An NGR is then also recorded against each of the samples to give a more accurate sampling location.  For the geospatial version of this see NRW_DS161366.\tThis data is continually update with most changes made in the Spring. Data from survey seasons is often not enter at the time of capture and can be several months before it is entered into the system. The external dataset is updated monthly to include new data, and may also contain corrections to previously published data due to quality assurance work.  Information Warnings Surveys are carried out by a range of survey methods, the data from one survey is not necessarily directly comparable to another. The user is recommended to check the survey method used in the data provided.  We do our best to avoid data quality problems, but as these datasets reflect the data we hold, they may contain errors  The data is provided as a set of related data tables due to the quantity and structure of the data. A basic understanding relational data is assumed.\t\tRaw Data extracted from WISKI and final outputs can be found here - Z:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\Data Mgmt\\Open Data Extractions\\NBN Extractions\\NRW_DS xxxxxx Marine Benthic Invertebrates\tBianca Bacchi\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-08-07\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-06T10:32:56.422Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n21204\tNRW_DS161373\t2025-08-07T12:09:51.063Z\t188\tUnassessed\tEditable\t2021 - 2026 Data for New National Park Proposals\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333733\tNatural Resources Wales is the statutory designating authority for new National Parks and National Landscapes in Wales.  Natural Resources Wales has been commissioned by Welsh Government to assess the case for a new National Park focussed on the existing Clwydian Range and Dee Valley National Landscape.  During the designation and investigation into this numerous datasets have been created in various formats and users should look to child metadata records listed below.  NRW_DS161374 - Proposed Boundary for a New National Park September 2025 NRW_DS161375 - Area of Search for a New National Park in Wales - June 2023  NRW_DS161376 - Candidate Area for a New National Park in Wales October 2024 NRW_DS161377- Evaluation Areas for a New National Park in Wales September 2024 NRW_DS161378 - Area Evaluated for Natural Beauty and Recreation - September 2024\tFor how each dataset related to the new National Park please refer to individual child datasets on how the datasets were created.\t\tGIS Locations are available in the child metadata records for exact locations.\tAlex Hardie-Brown - Geospatial Analysis Advisor\t\t\t2025-08-07\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-10-31T15:35:00.473Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n21205\tNRW_DS161374\t2025-08-07T13:49:44.982Z\t324\tValid\tEditable\tProposed Boundary for a New National Park September 2025\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333734\tA GIS polygon dataset showing the Proposed Boundary for a new National Park in Wales. This is the fifth dataset in a series that supports the new National Park in Wales project. This is the proposed boundary for a new National Park in Wales for statutory consultation purposes in late 2025. It is the summation of the evidence gathering, evaluation and public consultation project steps. OS MasterMap, SSSI, National Park, National Landscape, Wales England Border and Ancient Woodland boundaries alongside Ordnance Survey base mapping have been used to create this boundary.\tNRW protected boundary standards have been applied except for the National Landscape boundary (formerly AONB). This was designated in 1985 and was drawn to different boundary setting conventions. OS MasterMap is mapped at a maximum scale of 1:10,000. Therefore, this dataset should not be used for analysis on a smaller scale than this to avoid inaccurate results and impact any interpretation. It is important to note that the Proposed Boundary for a New National Park in Wales is not the final boundary and could be subject to change. It has no legal status and is not a material consideration.\t\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Proposed National Park.lyr\tAlex Hardie-Brown - Geospatial Analysis Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-09-07\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:51:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n21206\tNRW_DS161375\t2025-08-07T13:56:52.979Z\t189\tValid\tEditable\tArea of Search for a New National Park in Wales - June 2023\tArdal Chwilio am Barc Cenedlaethol Newydd yng Nghymru - Mehefin 2023\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333735\tThis dataset is a GIS polygon showing the boundary of the Area of Search for a new National Park in Wales. This dataset is the first of several that contribute to the evolution of the National Park project.   The boundary was drawn to an adequate level of detail to provide the basis for evidence gathering. The boundary was guided by a range of datasets and Ordnance Survey base mapping.\tPlease be aware that the boundary has been drawn at a scale to show the general outline rather than a precise boundary. Therefore, this dataset should not be used for analysis on a small scale to avoid inaccurate results and impact any interpretation.     It is important to note that the Area of Search for a New National Park in Wales is not the final boundary and could be subject to change. It has no legal status and is not a material consideration.\tPlease refer to the Area of Search Report June 2023 for more information.\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Proposed National Park\\Area of Search for a new National Park in Wales - June 2023.lyr\tAlex Hardie-Brown, Geospatial Analysis Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2025-08-06\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-02T14:38:21.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n21207\tNRW_DS161376\t2025-08-07T13:57:34.915Z\t166\tValid\tEditable\tCandidate Area for a New National Park in Wales October 2024\tArdal Ymgeisydd ar gyfer Parc Cenedlaethol Newydd yng Nghymru Hydref 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333736\tA GIS dataset showing the boundary of the Candidate Area for a new National Park in Wales. This is the fourth data set in a series that supports the new National Park in Wales project. The Candidate Area is considered to meet the Statutory Criteria by the Gillespies Evaluation report and formed the basis of public consultation in Oct- Dec 2024.\tPlease be aware that the boundary has been drawn at a 1:25,000 scale to show the general outline rather a precise boundary. Therefore, this dataset should not be used for analysis on a smaller scale than this to avoid inaccurate results and impact any interpretation. The Candidate Area for a new National Park in Wales is not the final boundary and could be subject to change. It has no legal status and is not a material consideration.\t\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Proposed National Park\\Candidate Area for a New National Park in Wales - October 2024.lyr\tAlex Hardie-Brown - Geospatial Analysis Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2025-08-07\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:52:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n21209\tNRW_DS161377\t2025-08-07T14:19:25.040Z\t155\tValid\tEditable\tEvaluation Areas for a New National Park in Wales September 2024\tArdaloedd Gwerthuso ar gyfer Parc Cenedlaethol Newydd yng Nghymru Medi 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333737\tA GIS dataset showing the boundaries of the Evaluation Areas for a new National Park in Wales. This is the third data set in a series that supports the new National Park in Wales project.   The Evaluation Areas are subdivisions of the Area Evaluated for Natural Beauty and Recreation. The identification of the Evaluation Areas is primarily based on the LANDMAP Visual and Sensory aspect layer and the associated level 3 typology e.g. Hills, Lower Plateau \u0026amp; Scarp Slopes. As such, nearly all Evaluation Area boundaries within the Area of Search follow the exact Visual and Sensory aspect layer boundaries. In most instances, however, the EAs are formed of several aspect areas. In addition to the LANDMAP data, the identification of Evaluation Areas has considered the indicative boundaries of the National Character Areas, and at a local level, the mapping of local landscape character areas from a range of published landscape character assessments. As far as possible, the Evaluation Area mapping has also considered broad patterns of aspect area values.   Where feasible, the mapping of the Evaluation Areas reflects the boundary of the National Landscape. Although where this splits an aspect area, subdivisions have been made where a relatively extensive area of non-National Landscape exists.\tPlease be aware that the boundary has been drawn at a scale to show the general outline rather a precise boundary. Therefore, this dataset should not be used for analysis on a small scale to avoid inaccurate results and impact any interpretation.   It is important to note that the Evaluation Areas for a New National Park in Wales are not the final boundary and could be subject to change. It has no legal status and is not a material consideration.\t\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Proposed National Park\\Evaluation Areas for a new National Park in Wales - September 2024.lyr\tAlex Hardie-Brown - Geospatial Analysis Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2025-08-07\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-10T07:52:09.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n21210\tNRW_DS161378\t2025-08-07T14:32:40.330Z\t101\tUnassessed\tEditable\tArea Evaluated for Natural Beauty and Recreation - September 2024\tArdal wedi\u0027i Gwerthuso am Harddwch Naturiol a Hamdden - Medi 2024\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333738\tThis dataset is a GIS polygon showing the boundary of the Area Evaluated for Natural Beauty and Recreation, to support the new National Park in Wales project.  This is the second data set in a series that supports the new National Park in Wales project.  It is the area that consultants used to focus their assessment work.    The boundary was informed by LANDMAP, other environmental data and Ordnance Survey base mapping. It was drawn to a sufficient level of detail for assessment work purposes.    Please be aware that the boundary has been drawn at a scale to show the general outline rather a precise boundary. Therefore, this dataset should not be used for analysis on a small scale to avoid inaccurate results and impact any interpretation.     It is important to note that the Area Evaluated for Natural Beauty and Recreation is not the final boundary and could be subject to change. It has no legal status and is not a material consideration.\t\tPlease refer to the Gillespies Evaluation Report September 2024 for more information.\tAwaiting X:Drive data load\tAlex Hardie-Brown, Geospatial Analysis Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-08-06\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-09-05T10:57:12.193Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n21211\tNRW_DS161379\t2025-08-08T09:17:57.767Z\t216\tValid\tEditable\tKiEco Marine Ecology: Marine Phytoplankton - Taxa Data\tData Phytoplancton Cyrff Dŵr Newidiol ac Arfordirol y Cyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333739\tThis data collates all the Phytoplankton samples collected within Wales from 2007 onwards by Natural Resources Wales and former Environment Agency Wales that are held within NRWs KiEco Database. This methodology is based on the principle that additional nutrients (especially nitrogen) can alter the amount of phytoplankton in transitional and coastal waters. Persistent and high counts of phytoplankton taxa are also seen as a measure of disturbance in the phytoplankton community. These surveys are conducted as part of on-going monitoring for legislative requirements and to meet organisational drivers. In addition, sites may be sampled for investigative work, or on an ad hoc basis.  Phytoplankton samples are taken in situ and are usually sent to a specialist marine contractor for Species Identity and abundance (two labs have been used in this time period; CEFAS 2007 – 2009 \u0026amp; APEM 2010 – onwards). Phytoplankton samples are analysed (identified and counted) using the Utermöhl method. Further information is available from the UK-TAG WFD website.  For many of our marine survey techniques we record an NGR at the site because this acts as a central/representative location around which we collect lots of samples. An NGR is then also recorded against each of the samples to give a more accurate sampling location.  A geospatial version of this data is available in NRW_DS124916-Mae\u0027r data hwn yn coladu\u0027r holl samplau ffytoplancton a gasglwyd yng Nghymru o 2007 ymlaen gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru ac Asiantaeth yr Amgylchedd Cymru gynt. Mae\u0027r fethodoleg hon yn seiliedig ar yr egwyddor y gall maetholion ychwanegol (yn enwedig nitrogen) newid faint o ffytoplancton sydd mewn dyfroedd trosiannol ac arfordirol. Mae cyfrifon cyson ac uchel o dacsonau ffytoplancton hefyd yn cael eu gweld fel mesur o aflonyddwch yn y gymuned ffytoplancton. Cynhelir yr arolygon hyn fel rhan o waith monitro parhaus ar gyfer gofynion deddfwriaethol ac i fodloni ysgogwyr sefydliadol. Yn ogystal, gellir samplu safleoedd ar gyfer gwaith ymchwiliol neu ar sail ad hoc.  Cymerir samplau ffytoplancton yn y fan a\u0027r lle ac fel arfer cânt eu hanfon at gontractwr morol arbenigol ar gyfer nodi’r rhywogaeth a’i helaethrwydd (defnyddiwyd dau labordy yn y cyfnod hwn; CEFAS 2007 – 2009 ac APEM 2010 – ymlaen). Mae samplau ffytoplancton yn cael eu dadansoddi (eu nodi a\u0027u cyfrif) gan ddefnyddio\u0027r dull Utermöhl.  Ar gyfer llawer o\u0027n technegau  arolygon morol, rydym yn cofnodi Cyfeirnod Grid Cenedlaethol ar y safle oherwydd mae hwn yn gweithredu fel lleoliad canolog/cynrychioliadol yr ydym yn casglu llawer o samplau o\u0027i gwmpas.  Yna mae Cyfeirnod Grid Cenedlaethol hefyd yn cael ei gofnodi yn erbyn pob un o\u0027r samplau i roi lleoliad samplu mwy cywir.\tSurveys are carried out by a range of survey methods, the data from one survey is not necessarily directly comparable to another. The user is recommended to check the survey method used in the data provided.  We do our best to avoid data quality problems, but as these datasets reflect the data we hold, they may contain errors  The data is provided as a set of related data tables due to the quantity and structure of the data. A basic understanding of relational data is assumed.  The change in analytical lab in 2010 resulted in the new laboratory reporting increased abundances of species because of the more high-powered microscopes available. There were also inconsistencies in recording Phaeocystis as either cell counts or colony counts.\t\tData is stored on WISKI database.   Copies of Darwin Core Species Data is stored here - Z:\\Archive\\Knowledge Data \u0026amp; Information Mgmt\\Data Mgmt\\Data Services Data Publishing Area\\NBN Data\\NRW_DS161379_MarinePhytoplankton\\Marine Phyt Darwin Core.zip\tBianca Bacchi, Senior Marine Monitoring Officer\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-08-05-2025-09-08\tcreation-publication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dr3643\t2025-10-29T14:11:11.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n21271\tNRW_DS161380\t2025-08-19T18:00:43.723Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey for Narrow-mouthed Whorl Snail Vertigo angustior at MoD Pendine in September 2024\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333830\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-08-19T18:00:51.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n21272\tNRW_DS161381\t2025-08-20T07:45:43.078Z\t219\tValid\tEditable\tNature and Climate Emergencies (NaCE) Water Quality Capital\tArgyfyngau Natur a Hinsawdd (NaCE) - Cyfalaf Ansawdd Dŵr: 2022 - 2025\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333831\tThis dataset contains delivery data collected from project managers to monitor the progress and benefits of projects completed under the Water Capital Programme. The data records project level information for reporting and evaluation purposes and is intended to support programme monitoring. It should be noted that the dataset reflects information reported by project managers and may be subject to limitations in accuracy or completeness.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.-Mae\u0027r set ddata hon yn cynnwys data cyflawni a gasglwyd gan reolwyr prosiectau i fonitro cynnydd a manteision prosiectau a gwblhawyd o dan y Rhaglen Cyfalaf Dŵr.   Mae\u0027r data\u0027n cofnodi gwybodaeth lefel prosiect at ddibenion adrodd a gwerthuso a\u0027i fwriad yw cefnogi monitro rhaglenni. Dylid nodi bod y set ddata yn adlewyrchu gwybodaeth a adroddwyd gan reolwyr prosiectau ac y gall fod yn destun cyfyngiadau o ran cywirdeb neu gyflawnrwydd.  Mae rhywfaint o wybodaeth wedi\u0027i golygu er mwyn sicrhau bod y data ar gael yn agored, gan gynnwys lleoliad rhywogaethau sensitif.  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Chronfa Ddata Hawl. Cedwir pob hawl.\tThe data was collected from project managers using an Excel spreadsheet. This method allowed flexibility but means the dataset is less robust than later collections undertaken in MS Forms, which enforced stricter entry requirements (e.g., consistent units, standardised interventions, and fewer gaps).  Part of this data is sensitive, a redacted version will be available for the public.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\Water Quality Capital 2022-2023.lyr X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\Water Quality Capital 2023-2024.lyr\tRhodri Powell - Specialist Advisor, River Restoration Programme\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-08-20\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-24T14:46:06.074Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n21282\tNRW_DS161382\t2025-08-21T12:42:15.655Z\t31\tValid\tEditable\tMapping the Role of Coastal Habitats in Mitigating Flood Risk\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333832\tThis data resource contains four GIS layers and a MS Excel workbook that make up the deliverables associated with NRW evidence report 935.   The four GIS layers are: coastal units (Coastal_Units_25062025), dunes habitats per coastal unit (CU_Dunes_22052025), saltmarsh habitats per coastal unit (CU_Saltmarsh_22052025) and shingle habitats per coastal unit (CU_Shingle_29052025).   Coastal Units (CUs) were defined by grouping deliverables from a Coastal Squeeze project (Accommodation Spaces, their associated Assessment Units and Foreshore Areas) together. The coastal units were the unit of assessment for determining the flood risk benefit of coastal habitats around the Welsh coastline.    The sand dune, shingle, and saltmarsh habitats were clipped to each CU and their Functional Value assessed. These are provided in the relevant layers.   The excel file (NCA workbook of flood protection from coastal habitats in Wales_Final) provides a natural capital assessment of the various habitats, which feeds into the values in the coastal unit layer.\tFor detailed methods see NRW evidence report 935 “Spatial mapping of the role of coastal habitats in mitigating flood risk in Wales.”    For each Coastal Unit, the functional value of habitats was determined based on width and height (for dunes and shingle). The estimated length of the coastline fronted by each habitat and the total area of each habitat within the Coastal Unit were obtained and used to calculate an inferred width of each habitat within each Coastal Unit. Heights of the habitats were estimated using the Digital Terrain Model. These values are stored in the three habitat layers. The functional value was used to estimate a percentage reduction in flood damages that could be attributed to each habitat.    For each Coastal Unit, areas at high, medium and low risk from flooding from the sea in the present day were identified using the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) data layer. The key assets (properties and infrastructure, agricultural land, road and rail) within each flood risk zone were identified. The CU deliverable also captures short-term erosion rates, overlap with designated areas, and the presence of a flood defence where applicable.   Also included in the CU are the central estimates of economic evaluation results detailed in eftec’s NCA Workbook deliverable.  Data inputs:   • Coastal Squeeze deliverables: Assessment Unit line, Accommodation Space, Foreshore Area, Digital Terrain Model  • Habitat extents: Sand dune, Saltmarsh, Vegetated shingle, and Littoral coarse sediment extent  • Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) (https://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/inspire-nrw:FloodRiskAssessmentWales)   • National Receptor Dataset (NRD) 2023, NRW (https://metadata.naturalresources.wales/geonetwork/srv/eng/catalog.search#/metadata/NRW_DS125721)   • Predictive Agricultural Land Classification (ALC) Map 2, Welsh Government (https://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-wg:wg_predictive_alc2)   • OS Open Roads, Ordnance Survey (https://osdatahub.os.uk/downloads/open/OpenRoads)   • Rail lines from OS Open Zoomstack, Ordnance Survey (https://osdatahub.os.uk/downloads/open/OpenZoomstack)   • National Coastal Erosion Risk Management (NCERM) (https://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_ncerm)\tBeynon-Davies, C. 2025. A review of the role of coastal and marine habitats in mitigating coastal erosion and flooding in Wales. NRW Evidence Report No: 922, 93pp, NRW, Cardiff.   Fairchild TP, Bennett WG, Smith G, Day B, Skov MW, Möller I, Beaumont N, Karunarathna H, Griffin JN. 2021. Coastal wetlands mitigate storm flooding and associated costs in estuaries. Environmental Research Letters (16) 074034.\tExcel file on the DMS: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/coast/ICMP/NBS/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?RootFolder\u003d%2Fteams%2Fevidence%2Fcoast%2FICMP%2FNBS%2FCoastal%20habitats%20%26%20flood%20risk%20summary%20project%2FSpatial%20mapping%20project%20ABP%20Mer%20%26%20Eftec%2FFinal%20documents\u0026amp;FolderCTID\u003d0x0120009401B6C442DD114B981063BE9AE8D618\u0026amp;View\u003d%7B29BB2E32%2D370E%2D46F5%2DADF6%2DE7700E86C3FC%7D GIS data on the X Drive: X:\\Flood Risk\\Coastal Habitats Flood Risk Benefits\tIain Fairley, Specialist Advisor: Marine and Coastal Physical Science\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2025-08-18\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-25T15:07:46.000Z\tMatthew Wareham\t-\tPrivate record\n21284\tNRW_DS161383\t2025-08-21T15:30:17.278Z\t19\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTarenig Welsh Government Woodland Estate Water Management Strategy\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333833\tJBA Consulting was commissioned by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) to undertake a study of a Welsh Government Woodland Estate (WGWE) block, Tarenig in Powys, to investigate existing geomorphological and ecological functioning across the study area in order to identify a range of Integrated Catchment Management interventions.   JBA have developed a Water Management Strategy (WMS) and corresponding Delivery Plan (DP) for Tarenig Forest, presented in an evidence report and accompanying GIS outputs.\tJBA carried out desk based assessment and spent 5 days onsite to carry out a geomorphological walkover and ecological survey. They used GIS-hydro tools to develop a flow accumulation tool.   Data was QA\u0027d by JBA, Forest Research and the NRW Project Steering Group.\tEvidence Report No: 770\tX:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Catch Pits.lyr X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Clear Catch Pit X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Clinometer Result X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Culvert X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Culvert 1 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Culvert Replacement X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Deposited Roadside Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Eradicate INNS X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Excavated Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Forest Drains X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Forest Roads X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig General Details X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Improve Placement of Roadside Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig INNS X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Photos X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Relief Culverts X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Remove Excavated Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Riparian Zone X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sediment Sink X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sediment Source X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Watercourses X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig WMS Opportunity Delivery Plan X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Bank Lowering X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Bund X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Extra Channels Identified on Site X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Flow Accumulator X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Flow Accumulator Generated Channels X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Forest Drains 1 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Large Woody Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Large Woody Material Placement X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig River Network X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Road Drain X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Scrapes X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Areas Accessed X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Increase Riparian Vegetation X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Lack of Riparian Vegetation X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig NRW Identified Areas of Interest X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Observed Forest Roads X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 1 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 2 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 3 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 4 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 5 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 6 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 7 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 8 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 9 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 10 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 11 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Tarenig Sub Catchment 12\tFrancesca Sanchez, Freshwater Habitat and Species Specialist Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-08-19\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-10-22T15:51:05.000Z\tGary Eccleston\t-\tPrivate record\n21287\tNRW_DS161384\t2025-08-22T09:37:08.656Z\t21\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAberhirnant Welsh Government Woodland Estate Water Management Strategy\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333834\tJBA Consulting was commissioned by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) to undertake a study of a Welsh Government Woodland Estate (WGWE) block, Aberhirnant in Gwynedd, to investigate existing geomorphological and ecological functioning across the study area in order to identify a range of Integrated Catchment Management interventions.   JBA have developed a Water Management Strategy (WMS) and corresponding Delivery Plan (DP) for Aberhirnant Forest, presented in an evidence report and accompanying GIS outputs.\tJBA carried out desk based assessment and spent 5 days onsite to carry out a geomorphological walkover and ecological survey. They used GIS-hydro tools to develop a flow accumulation tool.   Data was QA\u0027d by JBA, Forest Research and the NRW Project Steering Group.\tAssociated with Evidence Report No: 771\tX:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Add Bales or Geomembrane X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Add Boulder X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Add Catchpit X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Add Heather Bundles X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Bridges X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Catch Pit X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Clear Catchpit X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Clinometer Results X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Culvert X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Culvert Replacement X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Deposited Roadside Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Eradicate INNS X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Excavated Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Forestry Team Identified Priority Sites X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant INNS X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Improve Placement of Roadside Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Install Root Wads X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Relief Culvert X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Remove Excavated Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Sediment Sink X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Sediment Source X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant WMS Opportunity Delivery Plan X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Add Large Woody Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Bank Lowering X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Bank Modification X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Embankment X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Extra Channels Identified on Site X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Flow Accumulator Channels X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Forest Drains X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Geomembrane X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Large Woody Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Micro Bund X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant River Network X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Road Drain X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Agricultural Management Pressures X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Areas Accessed X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Increase Riparian Vegetation X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Lack of Riparian Vegetation X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Road Material X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Sub Catchment 1 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Sub Catchment 2 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Sub Catchment 3 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Sub Catchment 4 X:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\WGWE Water Management Strategy\\WGWE Aberhirnant Sub Catchment 5\tFrancesca Sanchez, Freshwater Habitat and Species Specialist Advisor\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-08-19\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-10-22T15:49:39.000Z\tGary Eccleston\t-\tPrivate record\n21304\tNRW_DS161385\t2025-08-26T14:58:47.863Z\t67\tValid\tEditable\tCommunities at Risk Register 2025 – Present Day (CaRR)\tY Gofrestr Cymunedau mewn Perygl (CaRR) 2025\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333835\tThe Communities at Risk Register (CaRR) has been developed to provide an objective means of identifying risk and prioritising flood risk management activities at a Wales-wide, community level. It applies a standard methodology across all flood sources to calculate a theoretical ‘danger score’ that allows comparative risks to be quantified and ranked (from High to Low).    The CaRR comprises a spreadsheet that identifies and ranks individual communities for,  1. a natural, ‘undefended’ scenario, and  2. a managed scenario (based on the presence of defences and flood warning).   It is complemented by a GIS layer which defines the individual Community polygons and is attributed with danger scores, community rankings and other metric information.\tThe CaRR has been created using national-scale model data developed as part of the Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW) project.  The CaRR ranks communities based on a theoretical danger score which should be considered alongside other factors (such as history of flooding) when prioritising activities.  The methodology incorporates the ‘Depth in Property’ approach to counting the number of properties at flood risk from different sources.\tA brief summary of objectives and methodology is available in the Information Sheets.\tX:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\tInternal Data Custodian(s): Mark Pugh (Flood Risk Analysis)  Metadata Point of Contact:  Mark Pugh, Simon James, Dave Tarrant (Flood Risk Analysis)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-08-29\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-23T09:39:57.246Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n21306\tNRW_DS161386\t2025-08-28T12:43:47.563Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSurvey to determine the status and distribution of Geyer\u0027s Whorl Snail Vertigo geyeri on Waun Eurad SSSI, its last known Welsh locality, in November 2021\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333836\tWaiting for final metadata report from Michael Howe\t\t\t\tMichael Howe\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-08-28T12:44:27.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n21308\tNRW_DS161387\t2025-09-02T12:44:16.223Z\t188\tValid\tEditable\tRiver Restoration Habitats Index\tMynegai Cynefinoedd Adfer Afonydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333837\tThe resource is a summary of existing NRW \u0027River restoration plans\u0027, with detail of the Water Framework Directive waterbody ID and name with internal links to view the plans. This will be a feature layer to display where the existing restoration plans are in Wales. There are no limits or caveats to the use or interpretation of the data.  Metadata for the restoration plans in detail can be found at NRW_DS125313.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.-Mae hon yn haen GIS sy\u0027n dangos ble mae holl \u0027Gynlluniau Adfer Afonydd\u0027 CNC ar hyd at lled Cymru. Cafodd y cynlluniau hyn eu creu i helpu i nodi’r pwysau, yr effeithiau a chyfleoedd i adfer y cyrff dŵr yn unol â Chyfarwyddeb Fframwaith Dŵr yr UE.  Mae\u0027r holl wybodaeth wedi\u0027i thynnu o’r cynlluniau, y gellir gofyn amdanynt gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru. Nid oes unrhyw gyfyngiadau na rhybuddion ynghylch defnyddio na dehongli\u0027r data.\tAll the data on the layer has been sourced from the original restoration plans (NRW_DS125313), which are also linked in each entry.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\River Restoration Plans.lyr  \tRhodri Powell - Specialist Advisor, River Restoration Programme\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-09-02-2025-10-30\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/8060/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_rrp_index\t2025-11-19T10:13:14.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n21310\tNRW_DS161388\t2025-09-02T12:59:14.627Z\t152\tValid\tEditable\tFisheries Habitat Restoration Plans\tCynllun adfer cynefinoedd pysgodfeydd\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333838\tThis is a GIS layer that displays where all the NRW \u0027Fisheries Habitat Restoration Plans\u0027 are across Wales. All the information has been extracted from within the plans and it is presented as a feature layer.   These plans were created to serve as a tool to generate work programmes for individual rivers trusts and are linked to the Inland Fisheries Habitat Restoration Grant. Since they were commissioned, the trusts have added to their own knowledge base from further surveys they have carried out.   There are no limits or caveats regarding the use or interpretation of the redacted data.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.-Haen GIS yw hon sy\u0027n dangos ble mae holl \u0027Gynlluniau Adfer Cynefinoedd Pysgodfeydd\u0027 CNC ar hyd a lled Cymru. Crëwyd y cynlluniau hyn i wasanaethu fel offeryn i gynhyrchu rhaglenni gwaith ar gyfer ymddiriedolaethau afonydd unigol ac maent yn gysylltiedig â\u0027r Grant Adfer Cynefinoedd Pysgodfeydd Mewndirol. Ers iddynt gael eu comisiynu, mae\u0027r ymddiriedolaethau wedi ychwanegu at eu cronfa wybodaeth eu hunain o arolygon pellach y maent wedi\u0027u cynnal. Mae\u0027r holl wybodaeth wedi cael ei thynnu o’r cynlluniau ac mae’n cael ei chyflwyno fel haen nodwedd.   Mae\u0027r holl wybodaeth o fewn yr haen wedi\u0027i ffynonellu o\u0027r Cynlluniau Cynefinoedd eu hunain, y gellir gofyn amdanynt gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru. Nid oes unrhyw gyfyngiadau na rhybuddion ynghylch defnyddio na dehongli\u0027r data.\tAll information within the layer has been sourced from the Habitat Plans themself, which are all available either in the online library or the DMS. No new information has been added.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\River Restoration\\Fisheries Habitat Restoration Plans\tRhodri Powell - Specialist Advisor, River Restoration Programme\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-09-02-2025-10-06\tcreation-publication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/7891/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_wfd_fhrp_catchments\t2025-11-19T10:15:49.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n21312\tNRW_DS161389\t2025-09-03T10:06:40.035Z\t198\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSustainable Fisheries Programmes - Post 2011\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333839\tThis is a feature layer displaying the works completed by NRW since 2011 to now, reported by the Fisheries Officers. All data has been extracted from the \u0027SFP Project Master Ledger\u0027 and work type has been categorised into \u0027habitat restoration\u0027 type, according to the River Restoration Portal categories.  a layer containing data on pre 2011 programmes can be found in NRW_DS161390\tAll information in the feature layer has been extracted from the SFP master ledger, with no new information added. The SFP master ledger was populated by the fisheries leads/officers from each region of Wales since 2011.\t\tAwaiting X:Drive Load\tRhodri Powell - Specialist Advisor, River Restoration Programme\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-09-03\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-09-03T13:28:34.193Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n21313\tNRW_DS161390\t2025-09-03T12:28:33.318Z\t128\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSustainable Fisheries Programmes - Pre 2011\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333930\tThis feature layer will display what project works have been completed across Wales before 2011 in accordance with the Sustainable Fisheries Programme.   There is also a post 2011 dataset - NRW_DS161389. The two datasets were split due to a difference in data quality and standard.\tAll data has been derived from a master copy and split between pre and post 2011.\t\tWaiting X:Drive load\tRhodri Powell - Specialist Advisor, River Restoration Programme\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-09-03\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-09-03T13:28:22.836Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n21405\tNRW_DS161392\t2025-09-18T12:44:18.545Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey of Welsh Populations of the Grizzled Skipper Pyrgus malvae in 2023 and 2024\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333932\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-09-18T12:44:32.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPrivate record\n21427\tNRW_DS161393\t2025-09-19T11:10:02.131Z\t236\tValid\tEditable\tSites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) Review Data 2025\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333933\tThe SSSI review data (an excel spreadsheet) contains detailed information which forms the basis of tables and charts presented in the report \u0027Edwards C., York H. 2025. Sites of Special Scientific Interest: a Review of the Current Series in Wales. NRW Evidence Report No: 878, 82pp, Natural Resources Wales\u0027.   It includes data about each notified SSSI in Wales (at the point of February 2025), such as the site type, the area and the length. It contains information on the notified features (species, habitats, geology) and the SSSI\u0027s impacted by habitat fragmentation.\tThe SSSI review data is principally sourced from NRW\u0027s Protected Sites Database and the SSSI GIS spatial layer. It contains additional information produced by analysing this data. The data is based on notified SSSIs as of February 2025.   Full methodology details can be found in the NRW Evidence Report.\tEdwards C., York H. 2025. Sites of Special Scientific Interest: a Review of the Current Series in Wales. NRW Evidence Report No: 878, 82pp, Natural Resources Wales\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:x:/r/teams/Regulatory/psdp/sdsg/SSSI%20Guidance%20and%20Advice/SSSI%20Review%20(Deep%20Dive)/Data%20and%20evidence/Data%20Archive/SSSI%20Review%20Data%20Archive.xlsx?d\u003dwf02c76c3205b4f45bbabd37176c3336e\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\tHolly York, Specialist Advisor Protected Sites Programme\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-09-17\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T17:23:06.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n21428\tNRW_DS161394\t2025-09-19T11:38:59.456Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tData related to the construction of the A55 Conwy Tunnel 1984 - 1991 (paper data records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333934\tThese paper data records are in relation to the construction of the A55 Conwy tunnel 1984 - 1991 and include:   - Conwy Estuary Beach Survey data  - Sediment data  - Welsh Water data and surveys  - Beach analysis  These records are to be reviewed in 10 years.\t\t\tPaper records\tHuw Jones, Environment Team North Wales\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-09-19\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-09-19T11:51:12.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n21434\tNRW_DS161395\t2025-09-23T14:20:52.060Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tA survey for the Foaming Fern-borer Sawfly Blasticotoma filiceti on Cors Graianog SSSI in August 2025\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333935\tTo be submitted by M.Howe when report is done\t\t\tWID\tMichael Howe\t\t\t\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-10-10T07:50:45.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n21500\tNRW_DS161396\t2025-10-02T15:24:51.364Z\t86\tValid\tEditable\tLowland Grassland NRW Commissioned Surveys\tGlaswelltir isel Arolygon a Gomisiynwyd gan CNC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333936\tThese data are from detailed surveys commissioned by NRW of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation across Wales, usually using the National Vegetation Classification.   The data are intended as an addition to or an update of existing detailed in-house grassland survey spatial data.  The survey locations and sites may vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha, to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.\tThe data have been validated and verified. They were produced from surveys which are a ‘snap-shot’ of a site’s plant communities at the time of survey, and it should be noted that habitat composition may change over time due to, for example, ecological successional trends, agricultural treatments or planned developments, which means that there is no guarantee that the information within these datasets is still representative on the ground today. However they do provide an important long-term catalogue of data which can be used to, for example, monitor changes to the grassland environments overtime.\tThere are associated reports and sample data for some surveys.\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Other Grassland\\PH2 Other Grassland Sites.lyr X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Other Grassland\\PH2 Other Grassland Veg.lyr X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Other Grassland\\PH2 Other Grassland Mospolys.lyr X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Other Grassland\\PH2 Other Grassland Notes point.lyr X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Other Grassland\\PH2 Other Grassland Quadrats.lyr X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Other Grassland\\PH2_Other_Grassland_Mosaic.dbf\tStuart Smith, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-08-14-2025-12-08\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-12T14:52:04.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n21675\tNRW_DS161397\t2025-10-16T15:24:09.846Z\t15\tValid\tEditable\tWales Fluvial Flow Database (WFFD)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333937\tRiver peak flow estimates and hydrographs used in Flood Risk Assessment Wales (FRAW, 2019) and covering watercourses with a catchment area greater than 3km2.  Estimates created at regular intervals along a watercourse and stored in an ESRI Arc file geodatabase. At each interval (node) a variety of information is provided that includes:   - catchment descriptors extracted from FEH.   - donor observation gauges used within the flow estimation calculations.   - the donor observation gauges used within the flow estimation calculations.   - the time to peak and storm duration for the hydrographs and the calculated peak flows and hydrographs for each return period (both from the statistical and hybrid methodologies).   - information about the quality control checks carried out on each point, a decision log outlining any changes taken on each WFFD point before use in the FRAW project, and the final modelled inflow polygons used in the FRAW fluvial hydraulic models.\tThe WFFD was created to provide national-scale river peak flow estimates for FRAW. It is based on data and flood estimation methodologies available in 2017. There has been no verification of the estimated peak flows against observed flows from telemetry gauges or local modelling studies.  The WFFD does not provide detailed flow estimates required for local modelling studies and estimates could be refined by more detailed investigation and more recent datasets and methodologies.\t\tOriginal data is stored with FRAW (2019) project deliverables. Contact National Flood Risk Analysis team for details.  Copy of core deliverables can be accessed through X:\\Flood Risk\\Flood Risk Management\\Hydrology\\Wales Fluvial Flow Database WFFD 2019.lyr  Additional information on the source data for the WFFD dataset can be found in another File Geodatabase held in the following location:  Z:\\Archive\\Water Mgmt\\Flood Risk Mgmt\\FRA\\FRAW\\FRAW_2019\\3. Other\\1. Input Data\\1. WFFD\\2. Version 3\tSimon James, Lead Specialist Advisor, Flood Risk Analysis Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2025-10-16\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-24T12:08:06.000Z\tMatthew Wareham\t-\tPrivate record\n21703\tNRW_DS161398\t2025-10-21T14:13:54.013Z\t208\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBryophyte Lichen and Fungus GIS Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333938\tPoint data showing the location of conservation important mosses, liverworts, lichens and fungi across Wales.  The lichen records relate to species growing on trees; the fungi are species found in grasslands; the bryophytes are mostly found in rainforest habitats.\tField records gathered by the British Bryological Society, British Lichen Society and British Mycological Society converted from a spreadsheet to a GIS layer using Grid References.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Bryophyte Lichen HEP Sensitive.lyr,  X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Bryophytes Oceanic Scoring Species.lyr, X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Grassland Fungi Site Diversity.lyr, X:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestrial\\Lower plants\\Lichens Woodland Index SOWI URI Pinhead.lyr\tSam Bosanquet: Specialist Advisor: Bryophytes, Lichens \u0026amp; Fungi\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-10-21\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-10-22T11:22:38.024Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n21750\tNRW_DS161399\t2025-10-24T13:59:32.424Z\t98\tValid\tEditable\tReview of Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales MG5 data with great burnet\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631333939\tThis project reviewed data from the Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales against the new MG4 subcommunities to determine if any stands of MG5 containing great burnet could be reclassified as drier expressions of MG4 under the new definitions and, if appropriate, to update the existing inventory of MG4 to help inform future maintenance and restoration of the community in Wales.\tThe data reviewed in this project was originally collected between 1987 and 2004 by the in-house lowland grassland survey team and is owned by NRW.  The outputs were produced using RMAVIS, an application for assigning vegetation data to NVC communities.\t\thttps://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/lg/Projects/FMP%20-%20Review%20of%20MG5%20Data%202025\tStuart Smith, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-10-24\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T13:52:40.688Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n21851\tNRW_DS161402\t2025-11-04T15:21:50.120Z\t21\tValid\tEditable\tSalmonid Fry and Parr Density data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343032\tThis is a dataset of salmonid fry and parr numbers in Welsh rivers from 2000. It includes individual rivers and catchments, temperature data, flood and flow information, and salmon densities during different lifestages.  The data were collated from internal NRW fisheries survey data, externally acquired earth observation daily mean air temperature data, and internal flow data. The data were not mapped, but are collated from NRW\u0027s regular fisheries monitoring. The data were collated with the aim of undertaking Bayesian analysis to see what environmental factors have been impacting salmonid populations within Welsh rivers and whether driving factors could be elucidated.   Unfortunately the project was shelved during \u0027Case for Change\u0027 due to EPP priority realignment. The aim is to have the data stored safely in archive in case the project may be revisited in future.\tThere are several scripts of R code associated with this data which may be of use.   In terms of data quality control, the project itself was not finished and not internally peer reviewed and as of present should not be used in any communication or other work until such a step has been undertaken. As of now it is exploratory and not fit for any interpretation or further use.\tAn Open Data Assessment has not been done on this data. This is because, as described in the lineage, this data is archived and currently for internal use only.\tZ:\\Processing\\Sampling_Monitoring_\u0026amp;_Analysis\\Monitoring Analysis\\Richard - Salmonid Stocks Review\\New Code Attempts.    The name of the dataset file is \"FInal_salmon_meta_data.csv\", the R scripts associated with the data should be stored alongside.    This metadata entry is not set for public view because of the comments in the lineage and additional information.\tRichard Lewis, Specialist Advisor Monitoring Analysis\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNot Licenced for Re-use\t2025-10-27\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-12T12:46:05.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n21855\tNRW_DS161403\t2025-11-04T16:36:40.400Z\t87\tValid\tEditable\tTimber Planning System Coupes\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343033\tThis data comes from the Timber Planning System. The shapes are extracted from the TPS database using FME and transferred to the X drive.\tThe data come from the Approved Management Coupes and Operational Thinning layer held within ForesterWeb.  These are polygons which are drawn and edited by the Forest Planners and Forest Operatives. The Polygons are then sent to the Timber Planning System from ForesterWeb where they are extracted from the TPS database using FME and transferred to the X drive.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\Timber Planning System Coupes\tGuy Bennett, Estate Programmer\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-10-29\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T10:05:50.108Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n21857\tNRW_DS161404\t2025-11-04T17:03:47.719Z\t18\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRegulatory Woodland Support Officers Area Boundaries\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343034\tThis data shows a map of Wales split into three sections (North, Mid, South) with the Regulatory Woodland Support Officer (RWSO) responsible for each area.   RWSO investigate alleged illegal felling reports and carry out technical surveys for Forest Regulations team related to felling licences and environmental impact assessments.\t\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NRW Operational Areas\\Regulatory Woodland Support Officer Areas.lyr\tNick Fackrell, Senior Officer, Forest Regulation and Tree Plant Health, Forestry Regulations and Tree Health\t\t\t2025-10-31-2025-11-17\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T08:57:09.000Z\tAndrew Williams\t-\tPrivate record\n22021\tNRW_DS161406\t2025-11-12T16:25:49.340Z\t99\tValid\tEditable\tNational Vegetation Classification survey of Welsh coastal shingle sites\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343036\tData from a National Vegetation Classification survey and Shingle classification (Sneddon and Randall methodology) of 52 vegetated shingle sites, including shapefiles of NVC polygons, vegetation mosaics, target notes, quadrats, photograph locations and overall survey boundary.   Data collected in 2022 based on field survey of entire sites with field maps digitised to OS Mastermap and aerial photographs.\tData collected from field survey. Full methods and limitations are reported in National Vegetation Classification survey of coastal shingle sites report.\tHeathcote, S., Finch, R., Harrison, J. 2022. National Vegetation Classification Survey of coastal shingle sites in Wales 2022. NRW Evidence Report Series. Report No. 688, 39pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor.  https://data.jncc.gov.uk/data/aa6b4652-8944-4c24-8f95-148045d140ce/JNCC-Coastal-vegetated-shingle-structures-GB-Main-Report-WEB-1993.pdf   https://data.jncc.gov.uk/data/aa6b4652-8944-4c24-8f95-148045d140ce/JNCC-Coastal-vegetated-shingle-structures-GB-Appendix1-Wales-WEB-1993.pdf\tY:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Coastal Habitats\\Shingle_NVC_2022.gdb\tJulie Creer Lead Specialist Advisor - Coastal Habitats, Evidence and Analysis\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-11-06\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-13T12:02:54.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPublic record\n22036\tNRW_DS161407\t2025-11-12T16:54:24.093Z\t15\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Annex 1 Habitat Maps (Reg9a 2024)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343037\tThe marine Annex 1 habitat map layers have been created by collating records from numerous surveys, monitoring programmes, casework, expert knowledge and third party providers.   The map layers contain points, polygons or centroids lines depending on the habitat type. The layers are updated when new records are produced as part of the 6-yearly reporting cycle (previously under Article 17 and now Reg9a of the UK Habitats Regulations).   The original surveys and monitoring events from which the records have been extracted vary widely in dates going back many decades in some cases (surveys were undertaken between 1899 - 2024).   This data consists of 15 dataset layers covering known occurrences of Annex 1 marine habitats across the Welsh marine area in the intertidal, inshore sub-tidal (up to 12nm) and offshore subtidal (12nm to the mid-line).\tThe marine Annex 1 habitat maps were produced for the 2024 Reg9a reporting process. They are a snapshot of best available knowledge on marine Annex 1 habitat distribution at the time. Confidence levels are included in the maps to help identify areas where feature occurrence is less certain (based on data source and type).\t\tX:\\Biological\\Reg 9a\\Marine  This metadata form covers all of the marine polygon, point and line Annex 1 habitat datasets produced for Reg9a reporting in 2024 (i.e. multiple feature classes within a gdb). The datasets cover the intertidal, inshore and offshore marine areas across the whole of Wales (up to the mid-line in the Irish Sea).\tKaren Robinson, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine Habitats, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group / Natural Resources Planning\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-11-07\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-19T15:11:09.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22155\tNRW_DS161408\t2025-11-20T10:00:49.300Z\t21\tUnassessed\tEditable\tZostera Marina Habitat Suitability\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343038\tThis data is the creation of a map showing areas that are suitable for the predominantly subtidal seagrass species Zostera marina are needed to provide advice on where NRW would potentially support restoration or creation projects for Zostera marina.   This map includes a combination of mapped areas of Zostera marina, habitat suitability model outputs and locations of suitable mapped habitat along the coast where the modelled outputs are absent. The data includes outputs from the JNCC Zostera marina habitat suitability model (Castle et al., 2022), Bertelli, C. Zostera habitat suitability (Bertelli et al., 2022), extracted data from JNCC\u0027s UKASH and NRW\u0027s section 7/OSPAR data.\tThe data is in vector format and should be viewed using the two lyr files: Zostera marina habitat suitability JNCC model values.lyr and Zostera marina habitat suitability map source.lyr.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Habitat Restoration\\Marine Habitat\\Zostera marina habitat suitability JNCC model values.lyr X:\\Biological\\Habitat Restoration\\Marine Habitat\\Zostera marina habitat suitability map source.lyr  The detailed processing notes can be found here: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/teams/advice/mar/mar-pap/Marine%20SMNR/Ecosystem%20Resilience/Restoration/Opportunity%20maps/NRW%20Zostera%20marina%20habitat%20suitability%202024v2.docx?d\u003dwef2989cf2dd74ff094b4d8c2886de081\u0026amp;csf\u003d1\u0026amp;web\u003d1\u0026amp;e\u003d7HRKkj\tNatasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-11-19-2025-11-20\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-20T12:22:45.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22182\tNRW_DS161409\t2025-11-25T12:20:15.735Z\t9\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTerrestrial Priority Habitats\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343039\tThis is a spatial dataset of terrestrial Priority Habitats as listed under Section 7 of the Environment (Wales) Act 2016.   This dataset is derived from several existing NRW spatial datasets: Phase 1 Terrestrial Habitat Survey (1987-1997), Phase 2 (National Vegetation Classification) surveys covering peatland, grassland, heathland, woodland, saltmarsh and sand dunes, Priority Habitat Site Surveys (terrestrial) and current Ancient Woodland Inventory. These datasets have been amalgamated into a single dataset.   Where there is more than one dataset in an area e.g. Phase 1 and Phase 2, the latest or most accurate dataset has been used to provide the most up-to-date Wales-wide distribution of Priority Habitats.   The data will be updated at regular intervals when new habitat information becomes available. Only survey data that have been extensively validated and verified by NRW habitat and cartographic specialists will be added to the dataset.   Habitat composition may change over time, for example due to successional trends, land management or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type or species in the digital data is therefore no guarantee of it still being extant on the ground. The data does contain ‘Year’ and ‘Data source’ fields so users will know how old the data is and thus how accurate it is likely to be.\tThis dataset contains spatial sites data (ArcGIS Feature class) derived from NRW’s Phase 1 Terrestrial Habitat Survey (1987-1997), Phase 2 (National Vegetation Classification) surveys covering peatland, grassland, heathland, woodland, saltmarsh and sand dunes, Priority Habitat Site surveys (terrestrial) and the Ancient Woodland Inventory which have been converted into Priority Habitats.    This dataset will replace the individual habitat layer files derived from the ‘Environment (Wales) Act Section 7 Terrestrial Habitats of Principle Importance’ dataset, which is largely based on the Phase 1 Terrestrial Habitat Survey (1987-1997).   These layer files are split into Wales-wide coverage for individual habitats, for example ‘Priority Habitat Blanket Bog.lyr\u0027. See metadata NRW_DS122418 for more information.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Priority Habitats\\Terrestrial Priority Habitats\tKathryn Birch, Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Habitats and Species\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-11-24\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-11-25T12:42:21.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22245\tNRW_DS161414\t2025-12-01T14:06:03.638Z\t12\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNative Oyster Potential Restoration Areas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343134\tThe dataset has been developed to focus oyster restoration areas where the conditions are suitable. It has been developed as part of the Wales Native Oyster Action Plan.   The dataset builds upon work carried out by ABPMer for NRW with additional work by NRW. It is a composite dataset of suitable physical parameters and suitable habitat for oysters to live. This includes current energy \u0026lt;130nm-2, water depths of under 80m and subtidal mixed, coarse or macrophyte dominated sediments. Inshore areas were added where the model input layers were too coarse and the areas were considered suitable by NRW specialists.\tThis is a composite dataset of suitable physical parameters and suitable habitat for oysters to live. This includes current energy \u0026lt;130nm-2, water depths of under 80m and subtidal mixed, coarse or macrophyte dominated sediments. Inshore areas were added where the model input layers were too coarse and the areas were considered suitable by NRW specialists.   Sources of data include EMODNet (current energy), GEBCO (UK bathymetry), JNCC UKASH combined map.\t\tY:\\Marine\\Marine_Subtidal\\Restoration\\Habitat_suitability_July24.gdb\\Zmar_suitable_habitat_model_mapped_combination_20251119  To be migrated onto the X Drive once finalised.\tNatasha Lough, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology. Ben Wray, Specialist Advisor Marine Ecology\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-11-27\tcreation\tnull\t\t2025-12-02T11:21:42.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22246\tNRW_DS161415\t2025-12-01T15:05:21.045Z\t10\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Areas of Uncertainty\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343135\tThis dataset relates to Water Framework Directive (WFD) saltmarsh monitoring.  It identifies areas where saltmarsh zone types could not be determined through aerial imagery during a desk-based study. These locations require an on-site visit to accurately classify the appropriate saltmarsh zone type.\tThis data collation was commissioned to Natural Resources Wales (NRW) as an enduring service by Environment Agency (EA) using Geomatics, the surveying arm to the EA.\t\t\tKate Borrowdale, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Monitoring, Analysis and Reporting Team.  Kate has confirmed that NRW own this data. NRW paid Geomatics (the commercial aerial surveying department of the EA) to capture and analyse the aerial imagery, which generates the saltmarsh uncertainty layers. NRW have a long-standing service level agreement with Geomatics and the EA to carry out these surveys.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2024-02-09\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-04T16:14:13.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22248\tNRW_DS161416\t2025-12-01T16:14:10.864Z\t71\tValid\tEditable\tNRW Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC sediments survey 2023\tArolwg gwaddodion ACA Bae a Morydau Caerfyrddin 2023 CNC\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343136\tIn Carmarthen Bay and Estuaries SAC the Annex I intertidal habitat features include: Mudflats and sandflats not covered by seawater at low tide.  A series of 23 sediment monitoring locations, distributed around the SAC to represent different areas and habitats, were established between 2005 and 2008.    Most locations were initially defined as transects (a line from upper to lower shore) but many have been redefined as clusters of stations (areas of shore).  Between 1 and 6 sampling stations have been established (with fixed positions) within each of those locations, though some have required repositioning due to channel movements.     These stations have been monitored using a combination of in-situ recording and sediment core sampling with laboratory analysis of infaunal composition and sediment granulometry.  Some stations (those at Crofty and Llansteffan) are surveyed and sampled annually, others at intervals of 2 or more years.  This description refered to data collected in 2023.\tTwo survey methodologies are undertaken :    1) In-situ survey of conspicuous epibiota and their abundance (various data types depending on species) visible on the sediment surface (area within 5m radius from GPS located station coordinates), plus in-situ characterisation of sediment habitat.  Photographs taken of the habitat and representative core samples at each station.    2) Five x 0.01m2 core samples sieved and preserved in formalin for infaunal analysis, plus a sample of sediment taken for granulometry analysis.  Samples (macrofauna and sediments)  analysed by Hull Marine Lab.  Data collated and used to assign biotopes to each station.  The pupose of this data collection is to support long-term monitoring to maintain a baseline of data that can be used for regular assessment of condition of the SAC, to study any changes over time, and to understand the scale of natural temporal fluctuations in those communities and species.  All surveys completed by professional and experienced marine field surveyors.  Data quality and confidence considered moderate to high.\tThe data will be available through MEDIN Data Archive Centre and the NBN Atlas once automatic flow from MRO Is established\tMarine Recorder Online  [43cadc21-0e19-4f03-a658-6ca40d5b310b].   Also field data held in DMS (SAMP-1270119632-191)  Sampling, Monitoring, Analysis \u0026amp; Reporting\u0026gt;Marine Monitoring\u0026gt;Intertidal\u0026gt;CB\u0026amp;E SAC\u0026gt;Carm Data\tPaul Brazier, Senior Specialist, Marine Environmental Assessment Officer,  Marine Monitoring, Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2024-08-30-2025-10-11\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-03T10:21:28.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n22321\tNRW_DS161472\t2025-12-12T12:47:49.364Z\t72\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDevelopment \u0026amp; Flood Risk - Flood Risk Activity Permits and Exemptions\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343732\tThis dataset maps the locations of Flood Risk Activity Permits (FRAPs) that have been granted or are currently being determined.   The data are collected by Development \u0026amp; Flood Risk (DFR) teams from North and South Wales on their own databases. The data is updated monthly.   FRAP reference numbers can be used to locate documents associated with each permit on the DMS. The dataset should be used when dealing with enquiries, applications and reports of activity in or near rivers to see if any activities have been permitted in the area.\tGrid references are provided by applicants, and copied by DFR advisors into the database. The accuracy of locations is therefore not guaranteed.\t\tX:\\Licensing and Permitting\\FRAP\\Flood risk activity permit (FRAP).lyr\tLucy Tyack, Advisor 2, Development \u0026amp; Flood Risk. Daniel Jones, Advisor 2, Development \u0026amp; Flood Risk. Flood Risk Analysis Team.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-12-11-2026-01-05\tcreation-publication\tnull\tnull\t2026-01-07T15:04:32.232Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n22447\tNRW_DS161473\t2025-12-22T14:08:13.269Z\t14\tUnassessed\tEditable\tHabitats Regulations 9A Wales Reporting 2019-2024 Dataset\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343733\tThis dataset supports Wales’s first country-level assessment under Regulation 9A of the Conservation of Habitats and Species Regulations 2017 (as amended) for the reporting period 2019–2024. It brings together evidence across terrestrial, freshwater, and marine environments for Annex I habitats, Annex II, IV and V species (excluding birds), and all regularly occurring wild bird species in Wales. The resource includes conservation status assessments for habitats and non-bird species, bird population status and trends with associated pressures and conservation measures.  The data were compiled by Natural Resources Wales on behalf of Welsh Government using the UK-wide methodology set out in the JNCC Approach Document, drawing on NRW monitoring programmes, partner agencies, NGOs, research institutions, and citizen science schemes. All data have undergone quality assurance and expert review to meet statutory reporting requirements and provide an evidence base for biodiversity conservation and policy in Wales.\tSupporting information spreadsheets for habitat, non-bird species and marine species  These supporting information spreadsheets are a download of the reporting information for Wales, and provide all results for each habitat and species from Wales with audit notes where relevant.  For marine species the spatial scale used is the UK level or Marine Management Units.  - Wales habitats supporting information data - Wales non-bird species supporting information data - UK and Management Unit (MU) marine species supporting information data  For habitats, the reporting sections include:  1. General information; 2. Maps; 3. Biogeographical and marine regions; 4. Range; 5. Area covered by habitat; 6. Structure and functions; 7. Main pressures and threats; 8. Conservation measures; 9. Future prospects; 10. Conservation status conclusions and trend; 11. UK National Site Network (pSCIs, SCIs and SACs) coverage for Annex I habitat types; 12. Complementary information.   For species, the reporting sections include:  1. General information; 2. Maps; 3. Information related to Annex V species; 4. Biogeographical and marine regions; 5. Range; 6. Population; 7. Habitat for the species; 8. Main pressures and threats; 9. Conservation measures; 10. Future prospects; 11. Conservation status conclusions and trend; 12. UK National Site Network (pSCIs, SCIs and SACs) coverage for Annex II species; 13. Complementary information  Summary of conclusion spreadsheets for habitat, non-bird species and marine species  These summary of conclusion spreadsheets provide an overview of the Wales Favourable Conservation Status (FCS) Assessments and short term trends for habitats and non-bird species and marine species.  - Wales habitat conclusions - Wales non-bird species conclusions - UK and MU marine species conclusions  For habitats, the reporting fields include: Conservation Status Overall assessment, Conservation Status Overall trend, parameter conclusions (Range, Area, Structure and Function and Future Prospects), and short term trends (range, area and habitat in good condition).  For species, the reporting fields include: Conservation Status Overall assessment, Conservation Status Overall trend, parameter conclusions (Range, Population, Habitat for Species and Future Prospects), and short term trends (range, population and habitat in good condition).   Birds data: Bird population estimates are presented at UK scale with Wales-specific context. - Wales birds data – Population status and trends - Wales birds data – Birds of Conservation Concern (BoCC) - Wales birds data – IUCN change-population and change-species - Wales birds data – Pressures - Wales birds data – Conservation Measures - Wales birds data – SPA proportion - Wales birds data – Species Action Plans\thttps://naturalresources.wales/evidence-and-data/research-and-reports/habitats-regulations-9a-report-for-wales-2019-2024/?lang\u003den\tTBC\tDr Rebecca Laidlaw, Specialist Advisor Environmental Reporting\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-12-05\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-22T15:39:02.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n22448\tNRW_DS161474\t2025-12-22T16:42:19.580Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTurbot Bank Multibeam Bathymetry Survey 2025\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-313631343734\tThis dataset provides bathymetry and backscatter data over the Turbot Bank, a designated sandbank partially within the Pembrokeshire Marine SAC.   The primary data form is geotiffs at 0.5m and 1m resolution, the raw data is also available. The data was collected to understand the current shape of the sandbank.\tThis data was collected by Bangor University using a Norbit multibeam system attached to the RV Prince Madog.   Data was collected in September 2025.\tSurvey report not yet provided - title will be added in due course\tZ:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Marine acoustic bathymetric data\\Multibeam\\Turbot Bank 2025\\Turbot Bank (Combined).  There is a lot of raw data behind the processed data. The two main datafiles are the floating point geotiffs: \"Z:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Marine acoustic bathymetric data\\Multibeam\\Turbot Bank 2025\\Turbot Bank (Combined)\\20250923 Turbot Bank 0.5m UTM ODN [Bathymetry Model] Floating Point GeoTiff.tif\" and \"Z:\\Archive\\Evidence\\Marine\\Marine acoustic bathymetric data\\Multibeam\\Turbot Bank 2025\\Turbot Bank (Combined)\\20250923 Turbot Bank 1m UTM [Backscatter Model] GeoTiff.tif\"\tIain Fairley, Specialist Advisor in Marine and Coastal Physical Science\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2025-12-17\tcreation\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-23T09:26:32.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n17303\tNRW_DS23774\t2024-05-28T12:19:56.535Z\t902\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Invertebrate Database (WID)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3233373734\tThe purpose of this data capture was to understand the distribution of species in Wales in order to inform conservation decisions.  Collation of around 500,000 invertebrate occurrence records, most of which relate to Wales. This is the primary database used by Natural Resources Wales; incorporates other major invertebrate datasets. Includes terrestrial, freshwater and coastal airbreathing invertebrates but not marine species.  The database also contains records of associated taxa and incidental records of other species, including alga, vascular plants, amphibians, mammals, birds, fungi, reptiles and bryophytes.  Much of the data has been sent to various UK recording schemes, who may also have supplied this data to the National Biodiversity Network (NBN) Atlas.  Family groups where data may already have been uploaded to the Atlas includes, Cerambycidae, Ciidae. Mollusca, Aquatic Heteroptera, Brachycera, All butterfly data, Odonata, Ant data, Tipuloidea and Trichoptera.  Mainly Wales, although there are occasional records from elsewhere in the UK. The database has been maintained since 1992. Records date from 1500s to present day.  Data accuracy is reliant on original sources. However, data has been transcribed by experienced invertebrate biologists in order to research incomplete records and minimise errors. Any uncertainties about a particular record, are noted in the database. Systematic verification has been carried out on significant parts of the dataset.  The database is constantly updated.  This dataset comprises of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Atlas.\tRecords have been derived from a wide variety of sources, including publications, records provided by schemes and societies, records created by Countryside Council for Wales and Natural Resources Wales staff as part of their normal work, records provided by amateur recorders, other inventories, results from structured surveys or monitoring projects etc. There are over 5,000 different recorders listed in the database. This dataset superseded the UK Invertebrate Site Register (UK ISR) in 1991/2, however, UK ISR data for Wales was incorporated into, and used as a basis for the WID. Other significant surveys which have been incorporated into the WID include Welsh Peatland Invertebrate Survey (WPIS) and the Welsh Parkland Invertebrate Survey amongst others. Significant subsets have been derived from WID for specific purposes e.g. Welsh Marsh Fritillaries, UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) invertebrate data for Wales, Invertebrates on National Nature Reserves.  Data accuracy is reliant on original sources. However, data has been transcribed by experienced invertebrate biologists in order to research incomplete records and minimise errors. Any uncertainties about a particular record, are noted in the database, and re. Systematic verification has been carried out on significant parts of the dataset.  The database is constantly updated, and updated biannually on the NBN Atlas.  Any records where there is not enough information to be fully verified are identified and these are not disseminated to external sources, such as the NBN Gateway or Local Records Centres.  All data that is suitable for public release will be made available on the NBN Atlas and given to Local Environmental Record Centres.\tSee separate metadata entries 24341, 115679, 107825, 24299, 23780 for further information about specific surveys.   This dataset include the Invertebrate surveys undertaken in Welsh Parklands which was developed by the Nature Conservancy Council. The purpose of this data capture was to understand the distribution of species in Wales in order to inform conservation decisions. Survey is comprised of two parts: North Wales and South Wales.  Some records within the WID are classed as either NEGATIVE RESULT, NEEDS CONFIRMING, ERRONEOUS or DUBIOUS under the attribute Recorder Name. This means that the species was absent from survey / sample if the survey was done to purposely find that species.\t[A] Data stored in Recorder 6 under the Welsh Invertebrate Database. Recorder 6 is currently located on the legacy Countryside Council for Wales drives, accessed through VMware.\tMike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW. (Adrian Fowles who is now retired) Custodian is the Terrestrial Ecosystems Group. Some records are collected from Adrian Fowles, an ex-CCW employee - these datasets are now managed by Mike Howe.         Data entry into the WID is on-going and is being run by Mike Howe. Some entries have their own specific metadata entry, but these are usually related to contractors work. Some records within the WID are classed as either NEGATIVE RESULT, NEEDS CONFIRMING, ERRONEOUS or DUBIOUS under the attribute Recorder Name. This means that the species was absent from survey / sample if the survey was done to purposely find that species. So when conducting an extract of the data it might be worth checking and removing these entries or making sure it is reflected in the NBN Gateway metadata.\tVarious including volunteer recorders-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:02:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17354\tNRW_DS23780\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.797Z\t867\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Peatland Invertebrate Survey (WPIS)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3233373830\tThe purpose of this data capture was to understand the distribution of peatland invertebrate species in Wales in order to inform conservation decisions. The Welsh Peatland Invertebrate Survey sampled invertebrates from peatlands throughout Wales during a 3 year period.  The survey assessed the distribution of invertebrates species in peatland, and studied the effects of site management on the assemblage of invertebrates present.  Around 106,000 records of invertebrates were found in peatland habitats in Wales, along with environmental information for each sampling station. All data relating to the WPIS is now held in NRWs Welsh Invertebrate database (WID).  The survey was based on the whole of Wales and a report is available for each of the following vice counties, Anglesey, Brecon, Caernarfon, Carmarthen, Ceredigion, Denbigh (inc. Wem Moss, Shropshire), Glamorgan, Merioneth, Monmouth, Montgomery, Pembroke, Radnor. Data was collected between 1987-1989.  New records have not been added to the WPIS itself as this dataset is now part of the Welsh Invertebrate Database.\tWPIS is incorporated within the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID). Sampling methods are described in detail in Holmes et al (1991).  Invertebrates were trapped at 300 tapping stations in 118 study areas.  The number of trapping stations in each site varied from 1-15, depending on the size of the site and the variety of habitats present. Confidence in this dataset is high.  Most of the invertebrates were identified by project members and specimens were lodged with specialists for each group.  Any material not taken by a specialist was lodged with the National Museum of Wales.  Rare and difficult beetle species were checked by Martin Luff and John Owen; snails by Arthur Chater, David Holyoake and Michael Kerney; Spiders by Peter Merrett.  Invertebrates targeted for identification were those for which there was an extensive body of ecological information available.  These were expected to provide the greatest return in analysis of management effects.\tSenior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.\t[A]  Biological Records are held in Recorder 6  - Welsh Peatland Invertebrate Survey CCW0006600000005 tagged as part of the Welsh Invertebrate Database.\tData Custodian: Mike Howe\tVarious-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1995-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:34:59.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17346\tNRW_DS24148\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.677Z\t328\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Vegetation Survey and Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales Brecknock spreadsheets (1991-1995)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234313438\tThe purpose of this data capture was to inform site selection and develop understanding of the semi-natural vegetation cover of Wales. The upland data covers all the major upland blocks in Wales; the grassland data is restricted to Brecknock sites.  Approximately 60 spreadsheets of Upland data (environmental, habitat type, statutory classifications and grid referenced sites). Data from various times between 1991 to 1995.  Collation and analysis of data collected by the Wales Field Unit Upland Vegetation Survey 1979-1989 and a small number of subsequent upland NVC surveys in the early 1990s.\tA Field-based vegetation survey approach was taken to collect the data.  The field survey was carried out in-house by Countryside Council for Wales, by closely co-ordinated teams of highly qualified surveyors. Many upland sites were re-visited between 1994-1996 to check the validity of field records.  The Upland Vegetation Survey maps were subsequently digitised and combined with maps from the companion Phase 1 Survey of the lowlands 1987-1997 to create the all-Wales Phase 1 habitat maps. The Brecknock grassland data forms part of the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales (see metadata entry 110956). The maps from this survey have been fully digitised and all quadrat data is stored in Recorder 6 Habitats Database.\tPublished in: Yeo, M.J.M. and Blackstock, T.H., 2002. A vegetation analysis of the pastoral landscapes of upland Wales, UK. Journal of Vegetation Science 13, 803-816.   Collation and analysis of Brecknock data collected during the Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales 1987-2004.  Published in: Yeo, M.J.M, Blackstock, T.H. and Stevens, D.P., 1998. The use of phyotsociological data in conservation assessment : a case study of lowland grasslands in mid Wales. Biological Conservation 86, 125-138.\t[A]  This data is archived in the terrestrial archive under: Project Number T005 Media Number T_Media005.    [B]   Location of data in the CCW H Drive:  H:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\PayneC\\2003\\MYeo spreadsheet data   To convert data from old format to xls, they were copied to TRIM (Location of data in Ffynnon: NRWF-15-001292), where they could be viewed and then copied to a new format.  Retention on these temporary files has passed and they have been deleted.  the converted files are retained in the archive\tInternal Custodian: Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems / Heathlands Ecologist, Grasslands Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecologist - Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.  Metadata Point of Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1995-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17343\tNRW_DS24176\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.521Z\t242\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Monitoring Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234313736\tThis data provides the baseline for the marine monitoring.   Marine baseline monitoring data (initially from collection trials of monitoring procedures) collected at locations in Wales. The trials were to determine the best combination of data collection for monitoring. This metadata refers to the project as a whole however each individual survey may have metadata of its own. The surveys took place over Penllyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation, Cardigan Bay Special Area of Conservation and Pembrokeshire Islands Special Area of Conservation between 1999 and  2001.\tThe project aimed to test a variety of methods in order to determine the most appropriate therefore methods range from broad scale mapping, drop down video, accoustic, using divers, sidescan sonar and remote sensing. Confidence in this dataset is high.\t\t[A] Location is determined with each of the individual survey projects metadata.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Biologist, Marine and Freshwater Sciences Group, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:20.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17358\tNRW_DS24185\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.895Z\t759\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBat Roosts Database - South Wales (BATDATA-WALES) 1982-1997\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234313835\tThis is a former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) regional dataset of bat roosts in South Wales, containing approximately 600 records. Includes the location of roosts, roost type, species records and public enquiries and/or details of visits to roosts by CCW staff or other bat experts. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts. This data has been used to provide information when dealing with development proposals, to support the monitoring and notification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species.  Original data was created by Nature Conservancy Council as part of a UK-wide dataset and Welsh records were transferred to CCW in 1991. This dataset has not been validated and there may be some errors in grid references and place names.\tThis is a static dataset. Data was generated from visits to roosts by staff and volunteers and/or from enquiries received by NCC or CCW. This dataset only covers south-east Wales. Natural Resources Wales hold the top copy of BATDATA-WALES. Some of these records do appear in other surveys as follows. BATSITES-WALES which fed into BATSITES-UK also appear in BATDATA-WALES, however does not contain the monitoring data. In addition to this Welsh Horseshoe Bat Summer Roost Surveillance Database holds some of the BATDATA-WALES data.\t\t[A] Species Data held in Recorder 6:  CCW-HQ-Terr-Bat Roosts Database - Wales (BATDATA-WALES) CCW0003800000001\tInternal Custodian: Jean Matthews,  Terrestrial Science Group, NRW. Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW.\tVarious including volunteer recorders-County bat groups-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1997-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:14:04.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17357\tNRW_DS24227\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.797Z\t697\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Raptor and Schedule 1 Bird Database\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323237\tConfidential database of records of Welsh raptors, chough and some other non-raptor Wildlife and Countryside Act Schedule 1 birds. Type of data: nest sites, chick counts and grid references (although some locations are coded by the license returnee and only the area the license covers is given, the exact location is unknown.)  This data is strictly confidential due to Protected Schedule 1 status of birds. Selective parts of the data (but not in full or in the same form) are shared with Reg Thorpe, RSPB and Rare Breeding Birds Panel.  This dataset is used for casework, survey, monitoring and site designation across Wales.\tThe database is updated annually with licence returns. The data was supplied as a joint partnership between Natural Resources Wales and RSPB Cymru.\tSenior Ornithologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.\tThis data is now part of the terrestrial archive. Project T004 Media Number T_Media004.\tRSPB contact is R Thorpe\tRoyal Society for the Protection of Birds (RSPB)-Rare Breeding Birds Panel-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:05.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17353\tNRW_DS24232\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.854Z\t501\tUnassessed\tEditable\tImportant Welsh Bat Roosts Database (BATSITES-WALES) 1946 - 2001\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323332\tThis dataset consists of information on important bat roosts from across Wales, mostly related to Greater and Lesser Horseshoe Bats and other rare species. It includes roost location and other site details, and results of repeat monitoring and surveillance. This database was originally created by Nature Conservancy Council as a UK-wide dataset. Welsh records were transferred to Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in 1991. There has been no systematic updating since 1999. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts, to assist CCW\u0027s conservation work. In particular, to provide information when dealing with development proposals, to support in the monitoring and notification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species.\tData was generated from visits to roosts by staff and volunteers and/or from enquiries received by NCC or CCW. This data is collated into BATSITES-UK which is held by former English Nature. Records held in BATSITES-WALES, will also be found (without monitoring data) in BATDATA-WALES and some will also appear in Welsh Horseshoe Bat Summer Roost Surveillance Database. This dataset\u0027s top copy is held and managed by NRW within their legacy CCW Recorder 6 application. There are 3716 records within this now static dataset.\t\t[A]  Species data is held in Recorder 6. CCW-HQ-Terr- Important Welsh  Bat Roosts Database (BATSITES-WALES)  CCW0003800000003\tInternal Custodian:  Mammal Ecologist, Bats and Riparian mammals, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW. Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tVarious including volunteer recorders-County bat groups-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:13:56.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17345\tNRW_DS24234\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.609Z\t1094\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOtter Sightings in Wales (OTTERSURV)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323334\tA database of otter sightings in Wales. Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Regional consultations and for information and reporting at Head Quarters.\tA specific method was adopted water surveying water courses.  Compiled from datasets produced by Regional Species Officers. Updates are send to HQ on a regular basis. The has been archived for reference, for full otter sightings information contact the Local Environmental Records Centres (LERCs).\t\tSee the Terrestrial Archive:  Project Number T003 Media T_Media003 (File Path: K:\\Archive\\Terrestrial Archive\\T_Media001to100\\T_Media003)\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1999-01-16\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17352\tNRW_DS24235\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.735Z\t660\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInventory of British Lakes (UKLakes)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323335\tThis is an inventory of lakes in Great Britain derived mainly from Geographical Information System (GIS) datasets. It is linked with a risk based prioritisation protocol for standing waters in Great Britain, which will be used in Lake Habitat Action Plans (HAP) and Water Framework Directive (WFD) work. The purpose of this data capture was to develop the required lake typology under the Water Framework Directive. This dataset is held by Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH).\tThe inventory of lakes around the UK is compiled from data from Natural Resources Wales (NRW), Environment Agency (EA), Scottish Environment Protection Agency (SEPA) and various other organisations.\t\tThis dataset is held by CEH and will be made available through their data portal.\tExternal Custodian: Centre for Ecology and Hydrology - they are the ones that hold the data and manage the dataset.  Internal Custodian: Tristan Hatton-Ellis, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services        15/02/2016 - This data is held by CEH and we contribute and own a stake in it. CEH are going to add it to their data portal - Tristan Hatton Ellis to send us the link when it is live so we can update the metadata - the link should be added to the additional information - or even the Resource Locator.\tUniversity College London. Environmental Change Research Centre-Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)-Environment Agency (EA)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)-Scottish Environmental Protection Agency (SEPA)\t\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:20.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17360\tNRW_DS24253\t2024-05-28T12:20:41.108Z\t628\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - 1kmsq Access Database\tCam 1 Daearol - 1 cilomedr sgwar - bas data paradox\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323533\tThe data are derived from a programme of field recording that was begun by the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) in 1979 and continued by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) after restructuring in 1991.   The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently simplified and imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales (see All Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - 1Kmsq .    The database was created in 1999 using data from the Phase 1 Habitat Survey of Wales undertaken between 1979 and 1999.  This table was held in Paradox database but then was converted into Access. It lists Phase 1 habitat areas in every 1 km square in Wales. Habitat area measurements are sub-divided within 1 km squares by, Region, District, Survey programme (Terrestrial Phase 1 Survey and upland surveys), Upland and lowland, and, for land covered by the terrestrial Phase 1 survey, statutory and non-statutory at the time of survey.\tClosely co-ordinated teams of surveyors carried out field recording of habitats onto 1:10,000 scale maps (rarely 1:25,000 during the upland stage). The minimum size of habitat patch recorded lies predominantly within the range 0.1-0.25 ha, although smaller areas were mapped in the lowlands. The survey methods employed during the initial upland stage are given in more detail in a WFU report (Day, A. (1989) Upland Vegetation Survey 1979-1989 Background Methodology and Summary of Data Collected. NCC, Bangor). Methods for the lowland survey followed the Handbook for Phase 1 Habitat Survey with some minor modifications adopted for use in Wales (Howe E.A. and Blackstock T.H. (1991) Supplementary Notes for Phase I Survey in Wales. CCW, Bangor).   Charles Copp was contracted by CCW to input records from CCW paper survey forms into Paradox.   All field mapping was carried out by trained and closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors. A study of survey reliability was conducted during the lowland phase (Kerry, Rimes, Smith, Williams. 1994. An Assessment of Errors in Phase I Habitat Survey in Wales. Bangor: Countryside Council for Wales, Report No. 94/4/1). It showed the field mapping to be 83% repeatable (Stevens, Blackstock, Howe, Stevens. 2004. Repeatability of Phase 1 habitat survey. J. Environ. Manage. 73, 53-59).The electronic data have undergone a series of checks for accuracy both by the contractor and subsequently within CCW. Habitat area data from the Upland Vegetation Survey were derived from 1 km square size-class estimates using an iterative process.\t\tArchived under Project number T006 Media number T_Media006.   Also available on the H Drive: H:\\Science\\Common\\Phase 1\\1 km square database\\Habsup1\\HABSUP.mdb.   The other access databases (Habsup2 , Habsup3) within this H Drive folder: H:\\Science\\Common\\Phase 1\\1 km square database\\  were intermediaries in the creation of the GIS versions 2 and 3 of the 1kmsq data.\tCustodian:  Internal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping \u0026amp; Farmland Ecologist (Clare Burrows?) Ecosystems Team NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T12:26:11.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17356\tNRW_DS24258\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.895Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tStanding Waters Database (Lakes)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323538\tThe Standing Waters Database mainly contains data on the relative abundance of lake plants.   This data can be used for: 1. Site data storage  2. Identification of rare plant sites  3. Identification of Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitats and Special Areas of Conservation (SACs)  4. Could be used in Local BAPs  5. Classification scheme used forms the basis of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) site selection  6. Provides data for Regional staff if casework arises on a site  7. Information used in SSSI management plans.\tThe plants were recorded using the DAFOR Scale (Dominant, Abundant, Frequent, Occasional, Rare).\t\tThe NRW copy is held at N:\\DuiganC\\lakesdb\\JNCC.MDB in MFSG.\tSenior Freshwater Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW.\tScottish Natural Heritage (SNH)-English Nature (EN)-Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:24.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17350\tNRW_DS24261\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.701Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarchlyn Mawr Ozone / NOx monitoring\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323631\tMonitoring of Ozone and NOx air pollution. Work is funded by NAW. The purpose of this data capture was to collect data on air pollution levels in North Wales, and contribute to the Welsh Air Pollution database which monitors changes in air pollution levels across Wales (all other stations are in South or mid-Wales).\tStandard protocols are used to collected data. Measurements are integrated at hourly intervals.\t\tAvailable to Environmental Audit Group staff on the shared drive:  R:\\Current Projects\\ECN\\EcnData\\A_AtmosChem\\Oz_data\\ OZNOX2\tEnvironmental Change Network (ECN) Team, Enviromental Change Group, NRW. Environmental Change Network Team, Environmental Change Group, NRW.\tThe National Assembly for Wales-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:56:34.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17342\tNRW_DS24262\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.250Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNational Peatland Resource Inventory (NPRI)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323632\tNational Peatland Resource Inventory (NPRI) was the most comprehensive assessment of the lowland raised bog resource in Great Britain at the time of publishing (1996). The inventory lists all known existing or former lowland raised bog sites. Within the NPRI raised bog is classified according to a number of sub-categories based on land cover. Four classes of active raised bog are defined: natural or near- natural vegetation; degraded vegetation (usually burnt or dry); drained; and revegetated or regenerating cutover.  There is uncertainty about data reliability and source.\t\t\tUnknown\tData Custodian: Peatland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW. Now falls under Jane Stevens, TSG, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1996-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:18.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17359\tNRW_DS24263\t2024-05-28T12:20:41.048Z\t381\tValid\tEditable\tInventory of Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Biotope Mapping Survey Sites 1996 - 2005\tRhestr o Safleoedd Arolygon Mapio Biotopau Cam 1 Rhynglanwol Morol 1996-2005\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323633\tThis dataset comprises of an Microsoft Excel spreadsheet listing all intertidal sites surveyed by the Intertidal Team as part of the Marine Phase 1 Intertidal Phase 1 Mapping Survey.  It provides a summary of site code, site name, the year surveyed, the extent of the site (grid refs), which Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) guidelines the site was assessed and qualified under, if any, and the original version of the biotope classification the site was surveyed under. The purpose of this data capture was to assist with the management of the of Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Biotope Mapping Survey and to provide manageable areas to assist with the assessment of the intertidal for the potential of designating SSSIs. Data collection started in 1996 and finished in 2005. The site names, codes and boundaries remain constant but the qualifying guidelines may go out of date if further assessments are done.  The spreadsheet should be updated in line with any future assessments.\tThe sites were chosen according to \u0027Guidelines for selection of biological SSSIs: intertidal marine habitats and saline lagoons\u0027, JNCC 1996 and  \u0027CCW Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Conservation Assessment Protocol\u0027. The data contributes to CCW\u0027s Marine Intertidal Phase 1 mapping survey. The datasets listed make provide information on the sites used in the survey.\t\tMaster copies of data is available to MFSG staff: H:\\Science\\MFSG\\Projects\\Ph1sites\\IntertidalPhase1SurveySiteInventory.xls\tInternal Custodian:  Intertidal Ecologist, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:33:11.113Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17347\tNRW_DS24264\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.695Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCoppicing Surveilance at Coeddyd Aber National Nature Reserve\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323634\tLarge spreadsheet of plant species against quadrat number and percentage cover values.  Collected as part of coppicing surveillance at Coedydd Aber National Nature Reserve (NNR).\t\tWoodland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\tUnknown\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17348\tNRW_DS24267\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.635Z\t479\tValid\tEditable\tSevern Estuary Database Phase 2\tCronfa Ddata Aber Hafren Cam II\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323637\tThis data is made up of the results from evaluating the nature conservation resource of the sediment environment in the Severn Estuary and Bristol Channel.   This database was created for the Marine Aggregates Resource Management Planning System (MARMPS), a National Assembly project, in order to create a decision-support tool for the Environment Minister when making decisions on aggregate extraction licences.\tThis database is made up of c. 80 data layers produced in the Bristol Channel Marine Aggregates Study (BCMAS) as part of former CCW\u0027s response to the Marine Aggregates Dredging Policy. It was completely reformatted due to a new set of requirements and to conduct a review of the data again.  There is a scoring mechanism (0, 1, 2, 3.  0 \u003d  neutral sensitivity, 3 \u003d highest sensitivity) for features and supporting habitats for each of  the 49 coastal cells in four separate categories: Ramsar, Special Area of Conservation (SAC), Special Protection Area (SPA) and Site of Special Scientific Importance (SSSI). In addition there are tabs to include information on Areas of Oustanding Natural Beauty (AONB), National Nature Reserves (NNR), Marine Nature Reserves (MNR), and Biodiversity Action Plans (BAP) and comments for each cell. There is a summary mechanism which calculates the maximum sensitivity for any cell for all four scored categories (must be maximum, not  average).  Update August 2021 by Nicola Rimington \u0027This database was used to inform WG policy on marine aggregate extraction that was published in 2004 and has since been superseded by the Welsh National Marine Plan 2019. It is worth retaining a copy for reference but there is limited value in sharing it internally or externally given that things have significantly moved on\u0027.\t\tArchived as Project No. 427, Media No. 1464, 1465 (File paths K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1464 and K:\\Archive\\Media 1401to1500\\Media 1465)  Update August 2021 by Nicola Rimington.  Held for archive purposes only.\tInternal Custodian – Nicola Rimington, Lead Specialist Advisor, Marine and Coastal Physical Processes and Coastal Management, Marine and Coastal Policy \u0026amp; Planning Team\tEnglish Nature (EN)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2001-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:23:22.000Z\tRachel Morris\t-\tPrivate record\n17344\tNRW_DS24269\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.513Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBird Spreadsheet Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323639\tThis is a collation of spreadsheets containing bird data and 1 containing rainfall data, as follows: -  Numbers of lesser black backed gulls occurring on Welsh Islands Numbers of curlew recorded in Welsh tetrads, 1988 - 1991 Summary of surveys undertaken at each Welsh bird SSSI Summary of upland bird data from site surveys in Wales. Summary of bird species surveys in Wales. Pied flycatcher (and other species) breeding data for nest box colony  Enlli-skylark\u0027s bird days\u0027 column: Skylark days on Ynys Enlli Wye rainfall data, 1944 - 1996 Number of raptors only, no site details, for Elenydd. Sian also holds  Numbers of dead and RSPCA recovered sea birds recorded by date  This is in Quattro Pro version 5. Numbers of black grouse breeding on Marine Number of common scoter recorded in Carmarthen Bay, 1977 - 1995 Numbers of lapwing recorded in Welsh tetrads, 1988 - 1991 Number of ring ouzels recorded on Spring and Autumn passage at UK Bird Number of ring ouzel days recorded on Spring and Autumn passage at Number of ring ouzels recorded in Welsh tetrads, 1995 \u0026amp; 1996 Number of pairs of roseate terns in Wales, 1960 -1995 (The file name for each of these surveys is available on Cyprian Payne\u0027s original Access metadatabase.) Number of pairs of artic terns on the Skerries, 1980 -1995 Number of pairs of roseate terns on Ynys Feurig and in UK and Ireland, Number of pairs of gannets on Grassholm, 1850 - 1995 Five spreadsheets containing data from previous spreadsheets plus common Marine date is in format 10 -Sep -95. Numbers of common scoter  recorded Number of hen harrieres recorded at individual locations, 1978 -1986 Raven breeding data for some sites, 1986 - 1992 (data from AVC)\tThe data files would need converting into Excel for public use which can lead to formatting problems, making the data difficult to read.\t\tSian Whitehead\u0027s G:drive.\tSian Whitehead: Ornithologists, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:30.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17355\tNRW_DS24270\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.812Z\t681\tUnassessed\tEditable\tRare Flowering Plant and Fern Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323730\tThis dataset is a collation of locality information, conservation status and dates of most recent records for Red Data Book, Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) and Nationally Extinct vascular plants in Wales.  This dataset is separate and different from JNCCs Threatened Plants Database, but there is likely to be considerable duplication of records between them.  The purpose of this data capture was to inform survey, monitoring and conservation management of sites for rare plants. The records span the period 1639 (one record extracted from literature) to 1999.  The majority of records occur after 1950.  Not all dates are given to the precise day or month, most are only known to be within a particular year.\tThe dataset was originally stored in Recorder 6. Although only the basic information of species name, date, location, grid reference, surveyors reference and provenance have been imported to Recorder 6.  The records span the period 1680 (one record extracted from literature) to 1999. The majority of records occur after 1950. Not all dates are given to the precise day or month, most are only known to be within a particular year.\tThe dataset has not been updated since 1999. All the six-figure grid references are verified through fieldwork, either by BSBI recorders, NRW staff (or their NCC predecessors) and the majority of 2 and 4 figure grid references will have a determiner; about 50% of the locality information has been validated by comparing electronic maps of the data on GIS with field notes, sketch maps and other paper records.\t[A]  Species data is held in Recorder 6. [B]  Data held in database: Rec2002 - Higher Plants in Aberystwyth.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tJulian Woodman, Higher Plant Specialist, Terrestrial Species, NRW\tVarious including volunteer recorders-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:39.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17349\tNRW_DS24274\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.677Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tData on RIGS (GD2 Database)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323734\tData on Regionally Important Geological and Geomorphological Sites (RIGS).  Including information on minerals, rocks, and stratigraphic units.  This database was developed from one written to handle records from the National Scheme for Geological Site Documentation (NSGSD) Project in 1991. The Lithostratigraphic table has been developed from data initially provided by the British Geological Society. The main data file contains Site data including all the main descriptive and keyword data for the Site. The Long Record is the main data entry screen containing all the fields that appear on the NSGSD Site recording form.\t\t\tThe Program is on Tony Rogers PC. And is backed up to C.Payne G:\\drive\\docs\\databases\\geology.  Command to start the program is: AREV gd/x\t\t\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:10.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17339\tNRW_DS24276\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.983Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCountryside Information System (CIS)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323736\tThe Countryside Information System (CIS) is physical and geological data based on 1 km squares. The data is at three levels. Level 1 is \"Summary Statistics\"; Level 2 is \"Land Class Data Sets\"; and Level 3 is \"Data Access Services\".  CCW is no longer a member of CS2000 Advisory Group thus is not eligible to receive free level one and level two data in electronic format.  Consortium members receive all the land cover data sets and full licences operating in perpetuity for internal use of the data.\t\tDr Helen Pontier, CEH, Natural Resources and Rural Affairs Science Unit, 1/06b, Temple Quay House, 2 The Square, Temple Quay, Bristol, BS1 6EB, Tel: 0117 372 8705.\t\tLiz Howe, Species Team Leader \u0026amp; Herpetologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW.\tCentre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:45:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17351\tNRW_DS24278\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.743Z\t608\tValid\tEditable\tCoastal Surveillance Unit (CSU) monitoring programme\tRhaglen fonitro\u0027r Uned Wyliadwriaeth Arfordirol (CSU)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323738\tThe Coastal Surveillance Unit (CSU) monitoring programme is comprised of 10 databases, including those for abundance records for around 1000 coastal species.  The purpose of the monitoring (funded by Shell UK Ltd.) was to detect any changes that might have occurred as a result of a Shell oil pipeline that runs from a terminal at sea off Amlwch across Anglesey to the mainland. Monitoring was carried out over 10 years from 1973 - 1983, with a resurvey in 1993.\tSpecies abundance was measured at site transect and quadrat level for each sampling event from 10 transects, mainly from around Anglesey. Data was also derived from fixed quadrats for the fucoids as a group (and Fucus serratus and Fucus spiralis individually), for limpets (Patella species).  Meteorological data (by date and met station), site information and zoning of sites on the shore were data also collected.\t\tArchived as Project No. 256, Media No. 918 (File path: K\\Archive\\Media 901to1000\\Media 918)\tInternal Custodian: Gabrielle Wyn, Team Leader, All Wales Marine Advice Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-University College Of North Wales. Marine Science Laboratories: Coastal Surveillance Unit\t\t1993-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T13:42:27.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17341\tNRW_DS24280\t2024-05-28T12:20:40.250Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWELSHNAMELIST.MDB\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323830\tWelsh/English dictionary of marine intertidal species names.\t\t\tUnknown\tNatasha Lough, Marine Habitats Biologist, Marine \u0026amp; Freshwater Science Group, CCW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:43:20.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17340\tNRW_DS24282\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.985Z\t499\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine and Estuarine Fish Database\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323832\tMarine and estuarine fishes are important components of the coastal environment and their presence/absence is seen as indicative of the state of inshore seas. In view of the conservation significance of marine fishes as a major component of the marine and estuarine environment the conservation agencies of the UK have statutory obligations to examine current conservation requirement of the marine fish fauna and to establish and maintain a monitoring programme by which to review their status. Other literature sources were obtained through the National Lending Library inter-library loan scheme.\tThe species list of marine and estuarine fishes of Wales was compiled after extensive literature searches using the library of the Marine Biological Association with its special publication \u0027The bibliography of the estuaries and costal waters of the British Isles\u0027 its South West Waters database and the Aquatic Sciences and Fisheries Abstract CD-rom retrieval system. The review brings together all major information sources on the marine and estuarine fishes of Wales. The publication date of the Marine and Estuarine Fishes of Wales Report has been used for the End Date of the metadata as no dates of data collection have been made clear within the report. The only data held is that within the report itself.\t\tThis data is stored on the DMS: Advice \u0026gt; Marine \u0026gt; Fisheries Science \u0026gt; Reports \u0026gt; Fin Fish \u0026gt; Marine and estuarine fishes of wales Entire Dataset  https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/advice/mar/fishsci/Reports/Fin%20fish/Marine%20and%20esturine%20fishes%20of%20wales%20Entire%20Dataset.xls?d\u003dwd944d693e99c4492a4b447f54c33fe9e\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2000-12-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:29.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17338\tNRW_DS24283\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.943Z\t839\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGreat Crested Newt (Triturus cristatus) Sites in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323833\tThis datasets contains the location and abundance or presence of Great Crested Newts (Triturus cristatus) across Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to provide information on the distribution and abundance of Great Crested Newt, as it is an Annex II species listed under the EC Habitats Directive. This dataset covers the whole of Wales although the majority of records occur in Ynys Mon and on the borders of east Wales.  The dataset has been subject to validation (checking for inputting errors). Records range in accuracy from 2 to 8 figure grid references. This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Atlas.\tThis dataset is was collated from Quattro Pro and excel spreadsheets and inputted into Recorder 6.  Some of the data may have been passed to the Herpetological Conservation Trust in Bournemouth for upload to the NBN.  A collation of records from a range of sources. One record is noted for 1944. This may be an error within the dataset.\t\t[A]  Species data held in Recorder 6 CCW - HQ Terr - All Wales Great Crested Newt Sites CCW000480000000C  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tLiz Howe, Terrestrial Ecosystem Group Team Leader, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2007-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T09:10:55.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17336\tNRW_DS24288\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.986Z\t367\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhase 2 Welsh Lowland Peatland Survey (Fenbase 1977 - 1982)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323838\tThis dataset has now been incorporated into, and superseded by the new Phase 2 Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales. The survey work was split into two time periods, with the first Phase 2 lowland peatland surveys being conducted between 1977 and 1982 (this dataset). There was then a break between this and the second tranche of surveys which have occurred from 2004 onwards. These more modern surveys were conducted on sites already covered on the 1997-1982 surveys and on newer identified sites. FenBASE is an ecological database on fens maintained by the Wetland Research Group, University of Sheffield. Pre-NVC survey of 4100 quadrats of vegetation data, collected on paper and held in the FENBASE database. Includes site summaries (known as Wales Wetland Survey). Also includes some slides. FenBASE was created to provide a comprehensive database of information pertaining to the fens within England. FenBASE data were used to evaluate the potential issue of nutrient enrichment of basin fens at the regional level, and provide an overview of mitigation option applicable to those sites at risk from nutrient enrichment. It is also used to provide indication of additional data that would be appropriate to further aid assessment and evaluation.\tThe data was initially collected using National Vegetation Classification methodology.\t\tOriginal paper field records held at Sheffield University.  Some copies within CCW. FENBASE: Location unknown - Access Database Site summaries (Wales Wetland Survey.xls) held by Peter Jones, TSG; Jo Hughes G:drive, TSG; Cyprian Payne N:drive (WETLSURV.XLS) Slides: Unknown\tPeter Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1982-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:43:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17337\tNRW_DS24289\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.928Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Geological Sites\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323839\tAn excel spreadsheet containing the records of over 400 geological sites in Wales. Contains site names, grid references, block name, associated SSSIs, SSSI code and type of geology, county, area  etc.  Purpose of data capture:  Information on the spreadsheet was used to set up the Geological Conservation Review (GCR) database, but has continued to be updated.\t\t\tFfynnon.\tRaymond Roberts, Senior Earth Scientist  Geologist/Earth Science Officer, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:43:35.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17334\tNRW_DS24290\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.817Z\t305\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPhoto Monitoring of Statutory Sites\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323930\tSystematic photo-monitoring of statutory sites, including data, paper records and photographic images. Steps are being taken to make the data available as a MapInfo layer with pdf links to a scanned paper record and a georeferenced point of where the photos were taken. Most images are from the 1980s onwards however there are some historical images. The files are referenced by Special Site of Scientific Interest (SSSI) name, SSSI code, year and the film number. The purpose of this data capture and archiving is to record and create a collation of the change of statutory sites over time.\tImages include: - c. 40,000 images from 370 SSSIs - Terrestrial and Marine Photomonitoring  (although the latter is generally administered by the local officer) c. 50 000 images - Some terrestrial species photos (especially lichens) c. 4000 images - Special Area of Conservation (SAC) LIFE Project monitoring c. 1000 images - Donated photographs, including deteriorating historical images.\t\tSlides from photomonitoring pre-dating digital photography are archived in approximately 10 filling cabinets in NRW\u0027s archive (Bangor). Several plastic crates and carboard boxes of protected sites monitoring images in a store-room (F29) in Maes y Ffynnon\tInternal Custodian: Tom Harrison, Environmental Assessment Officer, NE EAAT.  (Originally managed by Stuart Matley, contact Monitoring Team, Operational Support Group, NRW)  Update from Tom Harrison 2021 - This dataset needs a custodian at a higher level to take responsibility for it.  Sarah Gorton has moved some of the images from the SSSI photomonitoring dataset from plastic crates into slide hangers in filing cabinets in Maes y Ffynnon.  These have been partly catalogued (to be completed post-COVID).  The other images are still in boxes/crates or their previous filing cabinets in Maes y Ffynnon.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17333\tNRW_DS24294\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.751Z\t1563\tValid\tEditable\tRegionally Important Geodiversity Sites (RIGS)\tSafleoedd Geoamrywiaeth o Bwys Rhanbarthol (SGBRh)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323934\tRegionally Important Geodiversity Sites (RIGS) are non-statutory sites selected to protect the most important places for geology, geomorphology and soils, complementing the network of legally protected Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs).   RIGS are selected for their scientific, educational, historical and aesthetic features. As stated in Planning Policy Wales, Planning Authorities should protect the features and qualities for which RIGS have been designated. The impact of proposed developments will depend on the nature of the RIGS feature, so early consultation with the local RIGS group or NRW is strongly recommended.-Mae Safleoedd Geoamrywiaeth Rhanbarthol Pwysig (SGBRh) yn safleoedd anstatudol a ddewiswyd i ddiogelu’r mannau pwysicaf o ran daeareg, geomorffoleg, a phriddoedd, sy\u0027n ategu\u0027r rhwydwaith o Safleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGAu) a warchodir yn gyfreithiol. Dewisir safleoedd SGBRh oherwydd eu nodweddion gwyddonol, addysgol, hanesyddol ac esthetig. Fel y nodwyd ym Mholisi Cynllunio Cymru, dylai Awdurdodau Cynllunio ddiogelu\u0027r nodweddion a\u0027r rhinweddau y mae safleoedd SGBRh wedi\u0027u dynodi ar eu cyfer. Bydd effaith datblygiadau arfaethedig yn dibynnu ar natur nodwedd y SGBRh, felly argymhellir yn gryf bod ymgynghoriad cynnar yn cael ei gynnal â\u0027r grŵp safleoedd SGBRh lleol neu CNC.  Datganiad priodoli:   Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tData is produced on an ongoing basis for RIGS in Wales by the constituent RIGS groups.\t\tX:\\Physical\\Geological\\RIG Sites.lyr\tRaymond Roberts, Senior Earth Scientist  Geologist/Earth Science Officer, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  External: GeoConservation UK- The Association of UK RIGS Groups\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Association of UK Rigs Groups\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-03-24\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/452/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_RIG_SITES\t2025-05-09T15:57:34.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17332\tNRW_DS24299\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.711Z\t561\tUnassessed\tEditable\tInvertebrate Surveys conducted on Welsh Parklands\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234323939\tTHIS HAS NOW BE MERGED INTO VARIOUS SURVEYS WITHIN RECORDER 6 UNDER THE WELSH INVERTEBRATE DATABASE (WID). THIS DATASET WAS ARCHIVED BECAUSE THE DATA WAS NOT EASILY IDENTIFIABLE IN RECORDER 6. THIS DATASET SHOULD NOT BE CONFUSED WITH THE WELSH PEATLAND INVERTEBRATE SURVEYS (WPIS).   This is a biogeographical dataset for Welsh saproxylic arthropods (invertebrates). Data generated during the Nature Conservancy Council. Welsh Parkland Invertebrate Survey (WPIS) of 130 wetland sites in Wales plus Wem Moss in Shropshire. This datasets consists of all records from sites with a suffix (WPIS), or with recorder as Welsh Peatland Invertebrate Survey.  The purpose of this data capture was to understand the distribution of species in Wales in order to inform conservation decisions.  Survey is comprised of two parts: North Wales and South Wales.\tThe lichen and invertebrate data for Montgomeryshire sites were sent to English Nature for inclusion on their Parkland database. This data is now incorporated within the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID) within Recorder 6.  The data was created from a contract let by the Nature Conservancy Council.\tSenior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.\t[A]  Data is held in Recorder 6 Welsh Peatland Invertebrate Survey  CCW0006600000005\t\tVarious-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1990-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:28.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17335\tNRW_DS24303\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.875Z\t106834\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSaltmarsh Survey of Great Britain\tArolwg Morfeydd Heli Gwledydd Prydain\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333033\tSalt marshes in Great Britain represent one of a series of habitats confined largely to the coastline. They usually develop on intertidal land within the range of the normal spring tides, where colonisation by halophytic plants i.e. plants which are adapted to high salinity is and able to withstand immersion in seawater, takes place. The extent of the saltmarsh also depends on the amount of sediment available for the position, together with a combination of physical factors such as degree of exposure, tidal range, and the slope of the land. Salt marsh communities may also be found in the splash same along clifftops. However the salt marsh types are not included in this project.   This dataset consists of inventories of sites and vegetation each the major coastal habitats Great Britain specifically salt marshes. Salt marshes were selected for the first study in this series for two reasons. At the time the habitat was rare and not well studied and was one of the most threatened of coastal habitats. Consequently the salt marsh survey of Great Britain was set up in 1981 with the following objectives:  1. To review existing information on the distribution and abundance of salt marshes Great Britain  2. To identify omissions in our knowledge of the resource as a whole; and,  3. To fill the gaps in order to increase our overall knowledge of the salt marsh resource and therefore to produce as complete as possible a description of the resource to a minimum standard.\tThe method employed for some men between phase I and phase II surveys, be more detailed than a phase 1 survey the vegetation types within a map, but not as detailed space to, since no contract records were taken. Many of the problems and assumptions implied in this project arise from this rather broad methodology which was, at the time, the minimum level which it was felt could provide meaningful data on a national. This project excluded inland salt marshes and splash signs on cliffs.  Plant Community mapping categories were made of NVC communities amalgamated into blocks of vegetation which could easily be recognised in the field and were capable of being drawn rapidly and sketch maps.   There are no plans to computerise this data. Site forms included list of vegetation communities and tick boxes for other features.    The survey for Great Britain was commissioned by the Nature Conservancy Council which was replaced by the Countryside Agencies and JNCC in the 1990s. This paper dataset was split up and distributed to each of the Countryside Agencies. CCW took ownership of the Welsh reports and associated data which has now transferred to Natural Resources Wales (JNCC may also hold a copy of the collated data for Great Britain).\tThe seaward limit of the area surveyed may be identified as the lower limit higher plants and is fairly easily defined, although there may be some seasonal problems. Any survey work obviously takes place at one specific point in time and does not easy to judge how much of the area is within the tidal range and is therefore to be included in the definition of Saltmarsh. The reports are made up of a GB Report and 12 regional reports for Wales (per county)\tThese are stored in the Maes-y-Ffynnon library (Classmark Research and Survey 17).\tInternal Custodian: Peter Rhind. Coastal Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Nature Conservancy Council (NCC)\tOpen Government Licence\t1989-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T16:28:21.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPublic record\n17331\tNRW_DS24304\t2024-05-28T12:20:39.442Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGeological Conservation Review (GCR) Site Database\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333034\tThe Geological Conservation Review (GCR) was a major initiative launched in 1977 to identify and describe the most important geological sites in Britain. The GCR site database contains details of Geological Conservation Review sites in Wales, such as notification date and priority, unit authority and GCR number.  Purpose of data capture: The GCR was designed to identify those sites of national and international importance needed to show all the key scientific elements of the Earth heritage of Britain.  Methods of data capture: Site evaluation and documentation have resulted in the creation of an inventory of approximately 3500 GCR sites, selected for around 100 categories (the GCR Blocks) encompassing the range of geological and geomorphological features of Britain.  Geographic coverage: England, Scotland and Wales  Temporal coverage: The site evaluation and documentation ocurred between 1977 and 1990. New sites are added to the database when necessary.\t\t\tThe database is held and maintained by JNCC\t\tJoint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)\t\t1990-12-12\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:43:00.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17380\tNRW_DS24307\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.044Z\t636\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDormouse Database (1905 - 2004)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333037\tThe database was constructed to hold data arising from the Great Nut Hunt and build a complete and up to date picture of the distribution of the dormouse. This has been used for reporting on the delivery of the Biodiversity Action Plan. England and Wales. The dormouse does not occur in Scotland or Northern Ireland. The dataset has relied largely on contributed data rather than proactive survey, so it suffers from bias towards places that people visited during the Great Nut Hunts. Grid references would normally refer to the centroid of a wood where the dormouse was found, rather than the actual point. Many grid references have not been checked on a GIS to confirm that they fall within the woodland boundary; this is an on-going process and would normally result in an 8-figure (10m resolution) grid reference being used.  This dataset includes the name of recorder, site name, grid reference and type of sighting (for example dead or alive, location of nest).  Records originate from a variety of sources, with the collation held and maintained by Tony Mitchell-Jones at English Nature. It is an incomplete directory of sites, some of which are monitored annually as part of the national monitoring scheme. Only the most recent record for each site is included in this dataset, historical data is held in the original database. The source data also includes comments and the record source. Records use 6 figure British National Grid references (precision unknown), all give the centre of the site.\tThe presence of dormice was identified in several different ways, which are recorded in the dataset. These have varying degrees of confidence. * High confidence: Live specimen; dead specimen; confirmed hazel nut; nest in box. * Medium confidence: Unconfirmed hazel nut; nest in vegetation. * Low confidence: Report; other; unknown; unconfirmed nest.  1) Hazel nuts opened by dormice were collected by members of the public in 2 Great Nut Hunts. All opened nuts were checked by Dr P Morris.  2) occasional records submitted by individuals (very few records).  3) Site data from the National Dormouse Monitoring programme. This would normally include data from live animals seen at the site.  Data collection has been limited to records collected since 1990. From that time, collection has been sporadic and related to the Great Nut Hunt and other projects which collected dormouse records (eg hedgerow project). The majority of records are based on the identification of hazel nuts opened in a characteristic way by dormice. These can be found at any time of year although winter is probably the most productive time) and indicate the presence of dormice within the past 12 months or so (depending on the decay rate of nut shells). For this reason, the year of the record is more important than the exact date.\t\t[A]  A welsh abstract of the species data is held in Recorder 6 Dormouse Database - NE - Welsh Extract CCW000380000000C\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-06-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:31:15.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17383\tNRW_DS24310\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.096Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGeological Conservation Review (GCR) Site Photomonitoring Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333130\tA black and white, fixed point, archival-quality photographic survey of most GCR sites in Wales.  The images were captured to provide a baseline when CCW was created in 1990/1991.  Work was carried out by CCW\u0027s Earth Science Officers between 1991 and 1992, to establish a baseline of site condition for GCR sites in Wales. The negatives and contact prints were specially processed to ensure the longest possible storage life.\tA photocopy of each site monitored is included within relevant Site Management Reports (see separate metadata entry).\t\tHafod Elfyn\u0027s archival store, CCW Headquarters.\tRaymond Roberts, Senior Earth Scientist  Geologist/Earth Science Officer, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:56:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17384\tNRW_DS24311\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.097Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMapinfo Habitats Database on Candidate Special Areas of Conservation (cSACs)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333131\tMapinfo habitat databases on 23 candidate Special Areas of Conservation (cSACs) comprising 5 to 20000 records. Used to fulfill requirements of the Habitats Directive to set up protected areas in the marine environment. Collaborations with relevant authorities established operational management schemes on candidate marine SACs, around the United Kingdom, the locations of which are located on the map of projects and special features. Also available on an interactive map on the web.\t\t\t\tGIS Data Manager, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:40:09.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17388\tNRW_DS24313\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.210Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) Data for Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333133\tNational Vegetation Classification (NVC) data for Wales only. This data is used as historical records of the floristic data used in compiling the Nation Vegetation Classification (NVC). The quadrat data are also valuable as historical documentation of the floristic composition of geo-referenced localities in Wales about 25 years ago.\tData is from surveys that took place between 1975 and 1992. The data is collated by the Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC) to create a UK data set. However, the JNCC dataset does not have community codes attached. The data has community codes assigned (retrospectively by John Rodwell, co-ordinator of the NVC, using the MATCH programme) to the quadrats.\t\t\tPeter Jones, Terrestrial \u0026amp; Freshwater Ecosystems Team Leader, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW\tJoint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1992-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:52:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17390\tNRW_DS24316\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.221Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWater Voles Site Records\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333136\tThis database of water vole (Arvicola terrestris) site records (including grid references) is being developed by the North Wales Riparian Mammals Group (Menter Mon, Anglesey) as part of a UK steering group stategy (CCW, EA, Wildlife Trust and others).\t\t\t\tInternal Custodian: Rebecca Clews-Roberts, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitats \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team (Formerly Liz Halliwell) R. Gwynne\tNorth Wales Riparian Mammals Group\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:09.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17386\tNRW_DS24323\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.219Z\t369\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcidification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333233\tThe dataset was collected as part of a specific project and is no longer used by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) for reporting purposes. This dataset is to be kept for reference and as part of former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 data archive. The data was collected between 1992 and 1993 and used as part of a specific data modelling exercise. Site specific information about Welsh Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) that are under threat from acidification, with predicted damage under three scenarios of acid rain reduction. 218 sites are involved at the worst level of acidification and 86 sites are involved under the best scenario modelled (out of 845 SSSIs in Wales in total). The data include the area of the site at risk and the communities at risk. In the UK Critical Loads Advisory Group report on Critical and Target Load Maps for Soils in Great Britain the production of soils critical loads maps is explained as follows: The soil information is based on 1km squares of the UK Ordnance Survey national grid and on 1:250000 scale soil maps and supporting databases. The soil maps for Wales was produced and published by the Soil survey and land research centre. The critical load assigned to each 1 km square was the Critical for the soil association what occupied the largest proportion of search square. Each map unit was placed in one of five soil classes. These five soil classes were based on soil mineralogy.  This dataset was created to help describe the \u0027stock at risk\u0027 according to the Critical load exceeding limits (due to sulphur) at a 1 km resolution. The dataset was used to as an assessment of the biological SSSIs within areas where the soil\u0027s critical load for sulphur was predicted to exceed in 2005, when the agreed 60 percent reduction in SO2 from large combustion plants in Europe is implemented. The data was also used to consider the ecological benefits which may accrue if greater emission reduction targets are agreed. A possible 80 percent reduction was examined and compared with the predicted effects of the current scenario.\tA predictive modelling approach was taken to assess the effects of critical load on SSSIs. Firstly soils were classed based on their mineralogy. Each soil classes were assigned a single critical load value (1-5). Only the top 50 cm of the soil profile was considered. The soils critical load map was combined with a sulphur deposition map. The long range transport of atmospheric pollutants was modelling using simple, single level trajectory approach. SSSIs were then identified and the predictions were made based on 20 km grid squares, and later 1 km grid squares. There is Medium confidence in this dataset as no ground truthing occurred and therefore it was noted in the associated CCW Scientific Report (No. 26) that no directive evidence of site damage can be inferred.\t\tStatistical data held on paper and Recorder 6 Geographic data held digitally in MapInfo\tSimon Bareham, Team Leader Environmental Futures, SPG, NRW. (Project Officer at the time)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:24.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17387\tNRW_DS24327\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.200Z\t677\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Sand Dune Data\tSet data twyni tywod Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333237\tSand Dune National Vegetation Classification (NVC) spreadsheets containing area and DOMIN values for each NVC community. The purpose of this data capture was to characterise the sand dune vegetation of Wales.\tThe field surveys utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). The area information and DOMIN values were then entered into Excel spreadsheets. This dataset forms part of the Phase 2 Sand Dune Vegetation survey of Wales (see separate metadata in \u0027Part of\u0027 field below) which in turn is part of the Sand Dune Vegetation Survey of Great Britain.\t\tThe site reports are available from NRW\u0027s library.   Biological Data held in Recorder 6 Sand Dune Survey Wales CCW0007600000001  Spreadsheets were stored on the personal drive of NRW\u0027s Coastal Ecologist (Peter Rhind, retired). There is a data set here:- Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\RhindP\\Dune Spreadsheets.  According to Julie Creer this appears to be  \u0027Peter Rhind\u0027s own interrogation of the Phase 2 sand dune survey data (both the UK and Wales), which I suspect he got from the paper versions of survey. Looking at a few of the spreadsheets it looks like he separated out the different sand dune NVC communities and then tabulated which sites had those NVC communities and then added in the quadrat data tables for each site of that NVC type. S, in short, this is his analysis of that data and not the original dataset per se. These are most useful to me, but I’m not sure useful to anyone else?\u0027\tInternal Custodian: Julie Creer, Lead Specialist Advisor – Coastal Habitats, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1995-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:14:44.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17381\tNRW_DS24328\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.086Z\t1144\tValid\tEditable\tPembrokeshire Marine Species Atlas (PMSA)\tAtlas o Rywogaethau Morol Sir Benfro (PMSA)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333238\tThis datasets acts as an atlas of marine species for Pembrokeshire including flora and fauna from the intertidal and sublittoral marine zones, funded by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW).    The purpose of this data capture was to collate and make accessible all historical marine species data for Pembrokeshire between 1899 and 1998. The majority of records are from 1956 to 1998, and this dataset is now classed as static.   This dataset is a collation of data from various CCW and third party sources (both published and unpublished) that cover Pembrokeshire. It is comprised of 79 surveys  (available to download on NBN Gateway, see resource locator). This is a historical collation which is not being updated.    The data includes all records from the Dale Fort Marine Fauna survey plus additional data from subsequent and prior surveys.  Data that are included in the Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNRC) database have been excluded from this dataset to avoid duplication.   This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Marine Recorder which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway.  Only a redacted version of this data is available under the Open Government Licence and available for public use, see Limitations on Public Access and Use Constraints.\tVarious survey techniques were employed in order to collect the data see individual survey metadata for more information.    The data is historic and Natural Resources Wales (NRW) cannot guarantee its accuracy or validity. However, those data which were collated by Dale Rostron under contract to CCW (1997) was subject to basic validation, including the checking of all positional information.\t\tData is archived under project no. 109 and 156, and media no. 382 and 394. Species Data is held in Marine Recorder.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas  Some of the paper MNCR forms are held in off-site storage (Box CCW/MNCR/1, Box CCW/MNCR/2, Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tMonica Jones, Data Services Team Leader, EPP, NRW\tVarious including volunteer recorders-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)-Field Studies Council (FSC)\tOpen Government Licence\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\thttps://registry.nbnatlas.org/public/show/dp122\t2025-11-19T10:23:47.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17389\tNRW_DS24333\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.219Z\t730\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Phase 1 Intertidal Habitat Survey\tArolwg Cynefin Rhynglanwol Morol Cam 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333333\tThis is a dataset compiled of spatially-referenced survey data obtained from different intertidal communities (biotopes) in Wales. Maps include information on biotope location, species composition, access and photographs points.  Additional information is recorded as target notes.  Further site information, including site descriptions,  geomorphology, uses, impacts, target notes and habitat information is held in an accompanying database  Intertidal mapped data provides an integrated information resource for Local Authority structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.   The maps give important regional information about habitat and biotope distribution. They are used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments, assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI), as a basis for the development of sensitivity mapping, and as a baseline for developing monitoring of intertidal features in marine Special Areas of Conservation (SAC). Sites are mapped once and therefore are a \u0027snapshot\u0027 in time.  All data has been subject to a validation process. The data available to the public under an Open Government Licence has some information redacted.  An un-redacted version can be requested and may be provided under NRW licence.-Dyma set ddata wedi ei chasglu o ddata arolwg gyda chyfeiriadau gofodol a gafwyd gan wahanol gymunedau rhynglanw (biotopau) yng Nghymru. Mae mapiau\u0027n cynnwys gwybodaeth ar leoliad biotop, lleoliad targed, mynediad a lluniau o leoliadau. Unwaith yn unig mae safleoedd yn cael eu mapio ac felly maent yn \"giplun\" mewn amser. Mae methodoleg yr arolwg yn seiliedig ar \u0027Handbook for Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Biotope Mapping Survey\u0027. Mae biotopau\u0027n seiliedig ar yr adroddiad Adolygiad Cadwraeth Natur Morwrol JNCC. Mae\u0027r data sydd ar gael i\u0027r cyhoedd o dan Drwydded Agored y Llywodraeth yn cynnwys peth gwybodaeth wedi ei golygu. Gellir gwneud cais am fersiwn heb ei olygu ac fe\u0027i darperir o dan drwydded.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tSurvey methodology is based on \u0027Handbook for Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Biotope Mapping Survey\u0027.  Biotopes are based on the JNCC report `Marine Nature Conservation Review: marine biotope classification for Britain and Ireland.  Volumes 1 and 2.  Version 97.06\u0027.  Working at a scale of 1:5,000, field survey is based on using a combination of aerial photographs and Ordnance Survey (OS) maps as a template on to which the substratum and biotopes (habitat and associated biological community) are drawn.  Features and other information that are not map-able are described in target notes, a site description provides general information on the whole site and further information on biotopes and species are recorded on the survey form.    The mapped outputs of this project are static datasets, however some sites have been revisited after the project is finished.  The up-to-date biotope and target note maps is the product that is maintained and made available when requesting this data, metadata detailing changes can be viewed through metadata URI 114453.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal Data is archived under project no. 204, media no\u0027s 34-35,148,194-195,569,1157-1183,1185,1187-1190 (File path: K:\\Archive\\Media etc.)  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tPaul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-08-30\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3607/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_intertidal_phase_1_habitat_survey\t2025-11-07T16:06:24.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17377\tNRW_DS24337\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.962Z\t432\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Lesser Horseshoe Bat (Rhinolophus hipposideros) Summer Roost Surveillance Database (1987-2013)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333337\tThe monitoring of Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros) is required under the EC Habitats Directive and contributes towards the UK Biodiversity Action Plan for the species. This dataset comprises of two summer surveillance collations for Welsh Lesser Horseshoe bats (Rhinolophus hipposideros). The two collations date from 1987 to 2006 and from 2006 to 2011. They contain the results of annual summer surveillance of selected Lesser Horseshoe bat maternity and nursery roost sites. It includes a range of associated environmental data, such as temperature and weather conditions. The purpose of this data capture was to provide standardised information for planning and development control purposes. Data is used to support notification and subsequent monitoring of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) and Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). This dataset is now classed as static as no data is currently added to these records. Instead these records are now sent directly to the Bat Conservation Trust as part of their National Bat Monitoring Programme.\tWales has an internationally important population of Lesser Horseshoe Bats with over 60 sites included throughout Wales. The surveillance enables trends in the populations to be modelled. The surveillance surveys were carried out by over 100 volunteers to UK standard methodology, as used by National Bat Monitoring Programme. Bats were counted at emergence using bat detectors. Count surveys were carried out in June within two ten day periods. There is a high level of confidence in records collected using the standard methodology. The data form includes question on whether each count has been validated. Some recorders do not count within the specified period and their counts are given in the report, but not included in the ranked tables, nor in the data analysis. Roosts in this dataset also appear in BATSITES-WALES and BATDATA-WALES but the data collected forms differing datasets. This dataset covers the whole of Wales and NRW hold and manage the top copy of the data which make up 3538 records (1987-2006 has 1910 records and 2006-2011 has 1628 records).\tThere are a number of factors affecting the number of bats using a roost site and caution is required in interpreting trends for individual sites.\tSpecies data: Held in Recorder 6 database   - CCW-HQ-Terr-Welsh Lesser Horseshoe Bat Summer Roost Surveillance 1987 to 2006  CCW0003800000002   - CCW-HQ-Terr-Welsh Lesser Horseshoe Bat Summer Roost Surveillance 2006 to 2011  LC0003850000006H\tInternal Contact: Jean Matthews, Mammal Ecologist Bats and Riparian Mammals, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tVarious including volunteer recorders-County bat groups-Vincent Wildlife Trust-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:43.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17373\tNRW_DS24341\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.922Z\t570\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCors Fochno Invertebrates Pitfall Trapping Survey\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333431\tThis dataset was collected to assess the occurrence of invertebrate species at Cors Fochno Ceredigion between 1987 and 2002.  The species were recorded in order to investigate the effect of a major fire in 1986 on the raised mire invertebrate assemblage. The data will be used for CSM assemblage monitoring.\tThe methods used were pitfall trapping at individual sampling stations during the Cors Fochno Pitfall Survey.\t\t[A]  Data held in Recorder 6 under Welsh Invertebrate Database: - CCW Staff- Adrain Fowles CCW000660000000C  - Cors Fochno Pitfall Survey CCW0006600000GCB.\tInternal Contact: Mike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.   Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:23.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17382\tNRW_DS24350\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.035Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWelsh Parkland Survey (Phase 1)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333530\tDatabase of Phase 1 Welsh Parkland Survey detailing number of over-mature trees and aerial photograph information for 201 parkland, pasture and woodland sites in Wales.\tThe lichen and invert data for Montgomeryshire sites were sent to English Nature for inclusion on their Parkland database, which is on Recorder 6.\t\t\tMike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, TEG, NRW (Formerly Adrian Fowles, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, CCW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:13:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17376\tNRW_DS24352\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.855Z\t314\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUnmanaged Semi-Natural Vegetation in Welsh Uplands\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234333532\tLists of upland sites (often small enclosures) in Wales fenced off from grazing.  The dataset contains site name, grid reference, habitat type present, grazing species found, grazing pressure, management regime and actions such as burning, brush cutting, monitoring and  publications referenced. Also has a section for fencing and canopy and regeneration species.  The database was commissioned by the Uplands Habitat Working Group in 1998.  Data entry was frozen in 1999.\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) Heathland Ecologist undertook a data collation excercise to bring together information from CCW, National Trust, RSPB etc.\t\tG drive of Upland Ecologist, TSG.\tBarbara Jones, Upland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:13:54.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17378\tNRW_DS24420\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.047Z\t368\tUnassessed\tEditable\tProtected Sites Data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343230\tDataset providing information on Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSIs), Special Areas of Conservation (SACs), Special Protection Areas (SPAs), RAMSAR Sites, Geological Conservation Review Sites (GCRs), Mainre Conservation Zones (MCZs), Local Nature Reserves (LNRs), Marine Nature Reserves (MNRs), National Nature Reserves (NNRs), Biogenetic reserves, Biosphere reserves, National Parks, Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONBs). The information varies depending on the designation.  SSSIs have site description, PDOs, historical data on notifications, site area and central gri reference, cross refrence sites and responsible officers. Other designations have similar information excluding the PDO list which is only relevant to SSSIs.  There are also some Commons with limited data recorded on the system.   The purpose of this data capture was to collate information on protected sites in Wales, and provide historical data on designation dates aiding the reporting process for designations in Wales. The dataset is derived from a pre Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and is now owned, managed and updated by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) and is an ongoing system updated as and when changes occur to a desination or when a new site is designated.\tData on sites is entered by NRW staff within Operational Support Group. Domestic designations are entered by SSSI protection Team.  International designations are entered by the International Sites Team. Confidence in the data is low  in some cases. For example, the area figures are a mix of hand measured and digital measurements, the system still refers to 5 regions as opposed to 3 etc.  This system was decommissioned and data moved into new NRW systems, such as AMX and CRM. Please contact the Data Custodians for futher information.\t\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tSSSI and Species Protection Team Leader, Operational Support Group, CCW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:20:23.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17385\tNRW_DS24421\t2024-05-28T12:21:23.138Z\t236\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLand Agency Data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343231\tManages award and payment process for management agreements on National Nature Reserves (NNRs) and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), including land purchases, and buildings leases.\tCurrent database was populated from original \u0027Coredata\u0027 database in the early 1990s and is data entry is on-going. Land Agency data is entered by a restricted number of NRW staff.  Data entry divided into 4 role types demanded by financial audit. Finance commitments load directly into e-fin. Confidence in data: Confidence in the data is low in some cases.  This system was decommissioned and data moved into new NRW systems, such as AMX and CRM. Please contact the Data Custodians for futher information.\t\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tWest Region Regional Admin Manager, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:14:02.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17379\tNRW_DS24423\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.986Z\t294\tUnassessed\tEditable\tWales Survey Inventory (WALSIN) Data\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343233\tIndex to all environmental surveys of NC/NCC since 1949  and bibliography of all files held by NCC, including archives from approximately 1900 until March 1992. It lists every file note that holds survey information but it does not hold actual data. Every survey has a separate entry on the database consisting of two screens: the first screen contains structured data giving bibliographic, geographic and scientific details for the survey, the second, a summary of the survey\u0027s objectives, methodology, results and conclusions together with notes on the number of sites in the survey, maps and photographs.  Generally the records are limited to one line of information in the objectives, methodology, results and conclusions fields.  WALSIN can only be used for archival storage and retrieval and does not allow new data input.\tThis dataset was compiled when the NCC split into SNH, EN and CCW.  For methods of data capture see WALSIN User Manual. Confidence in data: Files previously held in the Fishguard office and Brecon Becon National Park were not included.  Data was originally catalogued within the CCW Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database but was extracted and moved to NRWs Document Management System as part of the decommission of the ISIS system.\t\tThis dataset is held in the DMS: Knowledge \u0026gt; Data and Information Management \u0026gt; Records Management \u0026gt; CCW Records \u0026gt; Walsin Records  KNOW-55-75   https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/kdim/Records-Management/CCW%20Records/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx?id\u003d%2Fteams%2Fkdim%2FRecords%2DManagement%2FCCW%20Records%2FWalsin%20Records\u0026amp;viewid\u003db151fb6e%2Dc7d5%2D4ca4%2Da30d%2D4d258f16d972   This is a legacy CCW dataset which used to be held in the ISIS Database before it was moved to the DMS. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files. [A] Was held in ISIS database under WALSIN ISIS database  [B] Was Held in ISIS (Oracle) database.\tData Custodian: Beth Hall - Library and Information Services Team Leader.  Support Desk Team Leader, Information Group. NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:11.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17370\tNRW_DS24424\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.901Z\t378\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFeatures data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) database\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343234\tThis dataset holds information on qualifying and non-qualifying features (species and habitats) on Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs). The purpose of this data capture is to maintain and have access to information on Wales\u0027 qualifying, non-qualifying and notified features (species and habitats)  on SSSIs, SACs and SPAs. Data entry began in 2005 and is ongoing as new sites are designated and existing sites redesignated.\tInformation on features is entered into a database by NRW staff. The information is input at notification as a record of the site\u0027s justification for selection.  This system was decommissioned and data moved into new NRW systems, such as AMX and CRM. Please contact the Data Custodians for futher information.\t\tSafle (Protected Sites) Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.  (Formerly held on ISIS)\tInternal Custodian: Holly York, Advisor 2, Protected Sites (SSSIs), Biodiversity, Ecosystems \u0026amp; Geodiversity\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:11:56.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17375\tNRW_DS24425\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.883Z\t8\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring Data Held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database (Archived 2021)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343235\tA collation of ,now archived, which SSSI Features of protected sites in Wales have been monitored. The previous limited monitoring data was incorporated into the Baseline protected sites condition review project data (124833). This will then be imported into the replacement to ISIS i.e. the Protected Sites Management System when the \u0027Evidence and Monitoring Module\u0027 has been built. This new system will then be the repository for the site feature monitoring data.\tThe dataset is derived from a pre Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Natural Resources Wales (NRW) system as and when changes occur to a designations or when a new site is designated. Confidence in data: Confidence in the data is low in some cases.  This system has been decommissioned and the data arachived, please contact the data custodian for more information\t\tISIS Database. Electronic. Formerly available to NRW staff over intranet and site files. This system has now been closed down and replaced by the protected sites management system. Currently there is no evidence \u0026amp; monitoring module on the system.\tInternal Custodian: Christine Edwards, Lead Specialist Advisor – Protected Sites programme       Metadata was archived 17th August 2021. Dylan Lloyd is the new custodian for this and has highlighted that after the ISIS System was decommissioned monitoring data was moved to the  protected sites baseline review data (124833) and will be used in the future possibly to populate a newer monitoring module when created.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:23:28.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17372\tNRW_DS24426\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.836Z\t227\tUnassessed\tEditable\tOpen Access held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343236\tThis dataset contains data in relation to the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000, where members of the public and people with a registered interest in land known as mountain, moor, heath or down have consulted Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in relation to parcels of land.  The data covers enquiries about the CRoW Act, land parcels, requests for leaflets, publications and maps, and advice or guidance. The bulk of the data relates to specific land parcels in the 14 A to O consultation areas in Wales (for draft, provisional and conclusive mapping), along with descriptions of the land, decisions related to the land in terms of whether or not it is mountain, moor, heath or down and appeals based on decisions about it\u0027s land status.\tThis dataset was created to collate information on protected sites in Wales. Data on open access land was entered into an Oracle database by CCW staff based in the 5 regions of Wales as well as Bangor HQ. Confidence in the data is low in some cases.  This system was decommissioned and data moved into new NRW systems, such as AMX and CRM. Please contact the Data Custodians for futher information.\t\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tRecreation Information Development Officer, Recreation \u0026amp; Access Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:18:42.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17369\tNRW_DS24428\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.840Z\t210\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLicencing held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234343238\tDetails of the species licences requested and granted by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to individuals and organisations. CCW\u0027s Operational Support Group received licence applications from individuals/companies/organisations detailing who is requesting it, for what type of activity, affecting which species, where, etc. The Species Protection Administrator enters the information into the Licensing ISIS (Oracle) database before sending the application to specialists for consultation. The licence is then either issued or not, if issued, details of the licence are added to ISIS.\tConfidence in the data is low in some cases.  This system was decommissioned and data moved into new NRW systems, such as AMX and CRM. Please contact the Data Custodians for futher information.\t\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tSpecies Protection Officer, Operational Support Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:16:25.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17366\tNRW_DS24715\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.254Z\t896\tValid\tEditable\tTerrestrial Phase 1 Habitat Survey\tCynefinoedd Cymru: Gorchudd cynefin Cyfnod i digidol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3234373135\tThis dataset holds comprehensive habitat cover data for the whole of Wales derived from a programme of field recording that was begun by the Wales Field Unit (WFU) of the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) in 1979 and continued by the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) after restructuring in 1991.   The purpose of this data capture was to inform site selection, casework and policy decisions. The lowland survey was particularly used to locate grassland sites suitable for more in-depth survey for notification purposes.  Habitat extent data has been derived to inform the Local Biodiversity Action Plan process.   The digitisation was carried out, in the first instance, for the drafting of maps for open country and registered common land as required by the CROW Act 2000. For reporting purposes during the lowland phase of the surveys, Wales was divided up into 15 different areas based on the Nature Conservancy Council\u0027s (NCC) Areas of Search (AoS).  The dataset is unique, in that it holds full census field survey habitat data for the whole of terrestrial Wales. It has been used widely for research, advice and planning both within and outside CCW.     The majority of the fieldwork was carried out \u0027in house\u0027 by closely co-ordinated teams of trained surveyors. Every effort was made to ensure consistency of habitat mapping.  A check on surveyor accuracy during the early stages of the Phase 1 Survey revealed comparatively high repeatability levels (Kerry, S. M., Rimes, C. A., Smith, S. L. N., Williams, A. M. 1994. An assessment of errors in Phase 1 habitat survey in Wales.  CCW report no. 94/4/1, Countryside Council for Wales, Bangor; Stevens, J.P., Blackstock, T.H., Howe, E.A., Stevens, D.P., 2004. Repeatability of Phase 1 habitat survey. J. Environ. Manage. 73, 53-59), and checks on the Upland Vegetation Survey data were carried out between 1994-1996 (Yeo, M.J.M., Blackstock, T.H., 2002.   A vegetation analysis of the pastoral landscapes of upland Wales, UK. J. Veg. Sci. 13, 803-816).    Digital maps were checked for accuracy at three stages: by contractor (Cynefin Environmental), and by CCW (C. Burrows, L. Howe, J. Stevens, J. Rothwell). 1km square table: the data were input and checked by a contractor (C. Copp) and re-checked by CCW (C. Burrows and J. Stevens). For a detailed overview of the background, methods and outputs of the Survey see Blackstock, T. H., Howe, E. A., Stevens, J. P., Burrows, C. R., Jones, P. S. 2010. Habitats of Wales. A comprehensive field survey 1979 -1997.  University of Wales Press, Cardiff.-Mae\u0027r cofnodion data yn cynnwys data cynefin cynhwysfawr am Gymru gyfan sy\u0027n deillio o raglen o gofnodion maes a ddechreuwyd gan Uned Faes Cymru, yn rhan o Gyngor Cadwraeth Natur yn 1979 ac a barhawyd gan Gyngor Cefn Gwlad Cymru yn dilyn ail-strwythuro yn 1991. Pwrpas y data hwn yw darparu gwybodaeth ar benderfyniadau polisi, gwaith achos, dewis safleoedd. Gwnaed y gwaith maes mewn dau brif gam. Arolygwyd y rhan fwyaf o\u0027r uwchdiroedd yn ystod y cam cyntaf (Arolwg Llystyfiant Uwchdiroedd), a cafodd ardaloedd eu dosbarthu i fathau o gynefinoedd gan ddefnyddio dosbarthiadau Ratcliffe a Birks. Arolygwyd gweddill Cymru gan gynnwys yr iseldiroedd a rhai ardaloedd uwchdirol nad arolygwyd cynt yn ystod yr ail gam (y cam iseldirol), a cafodd cynefinoedd eu rhoi yn nosbarth Cam I, gyda rhai addasiadau\u0027n cael eu defnyddio ar gyfer defnydd yng Nghymru.  Mae’r set ddata hon yn cynnwys yr haenau canlynol:  • Voronoi Llystyfiant: Yn dangos ffiniau a phriodoleddau polygonau llystyfiant gydag israniadau polygonau llystyfiant cynefin cymysg yr ucheldir wedi eu hisrannu i’w cynefinoedd cyfansoddol • Cynefin: Data pwyntiau yn dangos lleoliad cynefinoedd a fapiwyd mewn polygonau cynefin cymysg yn ystod yr Arolwg Llystyfiant yr Ucheldir • Voronoi Mospolys: Grid wedi ei liwio mewn patrwm sgwarog yn ôl Phase1_Code ac mewn cyfrannedd â’u gallu i ganiatáu gweld mosaigau • Voronoi Mosnotes: Data pwyntiau i ddisgrifio cyfansoddiad cynefin a wnaed o bolygonau mosäic • Llystyfiant Gwasgaredig: data pwyntiau yn dangos lleoliad llystyfiant gwasgaredig • Nodiadau Targed: cofnod o leoliad a chyfeirnod nodiadau targed Cam 1 • Voronoi Mosäic: Tabl pori i gyflenwi mathau o gynefinoedd, ardaloedd a chyfraneddau ar gyfer polygonau mosäic\tUplands are classified as open upland landscapes coming down as far as the upper limit of enclosure; lowlands and uplands are mutually exclusive.  The fieldwork was conducted in two main stages.     Most upland areas were surveyed during the first stage (the Upland Vegetation Survey) between 1979 and1989, and areas were assigned to habitat types using the Ratcliffe and Birks classification. The survey methods employed during the initial upland phase are given in more detail in a Wales Field Unit report (Day, A. 1989. Upland vegetation survey 1979–1989. Background, methodology and summary of data collected. NCC report no. W91/2, Nature Conservancy Council, Bangor). Some upland regions were surveyed between 1991 and 1999 using the National Vegetation Classification, and small patches were covered between 1982-1992 using the NCC scheme for SSSI recording or the NCC/RSNC classification (NCC/RSNC Habitat Classification and Description, 1984). Mapping was carried out on foot, using mostly 1:10,000 scale aerial photographs for locating habitat polygons (rarely 1:25,000 during the upland stage).    The rest of Wales including all the lowlands and some previously unsurveyed upland areas was covered during the second stage (the lowland phase) between 1987-1997 and habitats assigned to the Phase I classification (Handbook for Phase I habitat survey- a technique for environmental audit, NCC, 1990), with some minor modifications adopted for use in Wales (Supplementary Notes for Phase 1 survey in Wales, Howe E.A. and Blackstock T.H., 1991, CCW, Bangor). Habitats were recorded field-by-field by using public rights of way and obtaining access permission to look more closely at some fields. Habitats were mapped onto 1:10,000 scale 5 x 5 km master-maps. The minimum size of habitat patch recorded lies predominantly within the range 0.1 – 0.25 ha, although smaller areas were mapped in the in lowlands.\tWith such a vast dataset (approximately 481,000 polygons and 434,000 points), it is inevitable that some transcription errors remain. Users detecting significant differences between the digital data and the habitat maps from which they were derived are encouraged to report these to Jonathan Rothwell (from NRW) quoting polygon Unique_ID.    About 30% of the upland polygons are mixed habitat and therefore should not be used to calculate habitat area. A voronoi version of the vegetation layer has been crested to help address this. The 1 km square dataset is the definitive source of habitat area statistics.  Phase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales (Tir Stent site number SH71/2)\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Terrestrial\\Original_Phase_1    Original UVS maps stored NRW Maes y Fynnon.  Copies held in site reports.   Original NVC maps held in site reports.  Ph1 Survey \u0027master maps\u0027 archived at National Library of Wales; raster and vector data and 1km sq table are stored in the Geospatial Central Data Store.\tFor scientific issues; Jim Latham, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team Leader.     For data specification issues \u0026amp; Metadata; Jon Rothwell,  Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping.  Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     For additional information on the \u0027master map\u0027 archive contact Paul Joyner, NLW, Aberystwyth, Ceredigion SY23 3BU; tel 01970 632800\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-08-16\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3597/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_terrestrial_phase_1_habitat_survey\t2025-05-09T15:48:53.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17371\tNRW_DS45523\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.835Z\t345\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPeregrine breeding population surveillance in Pembrokeshire (1981-2013)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3435353233\tThis is a non spatial datasets map up of species data from the surveillance of Peregrine (Falco peregrinus) breeding populations around Pembrokeshire, south Wales. The surveys cover both coastal and inland monitoring, and just contains Peregrine data points. There were both local and national reasons for this data collection. Local Purposes: a) To maintain records of Pembrokeshire breeding population of peregrines, to service update/data requirements for local peregrine Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP), in partnership with other members of Pembrokeshire Bird Group.  b) To assess the condition of peregrine as an Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) feature in Castlemartin Cliffs and Dunes SSSI for Castlemartin Range Integrated Land Management Plan. c) To maintain records of concentrations of peregrine relevant to management of recreation (sea cliff-climbing and coasteering)   National Purposes:  a) To contribute to decadal national/international surveys - normally managed by the British Trust for Ornithology (BTO).  b) To contribute to all Wales database maintained by the RSPB, via Wales Raptor Study Group (when requested).\tThis data is a collated summary records of Pembrokeshire (Vice County 45) breeding peregrine activity. It contains records of territory occupancy and where possible confirmation of breeding outcome at each known territory/nest site. Information based on coordinated counts of sites by members of Pembrokeshire Bird Group, Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff and general public were compiled on spreadsheet and imported into Recorder 6.  Breeding activity and productivity of the population is recorded annually, (including GIS register of nest site locations) from cliff-tops. This dataset is linked to periodic decadal national surveys, e.g. 1981, 1991, 2002.\t\tData is stored in Recorder 6 45 VC Pembs: Peregrine breeding sites CCW000080000004.  The data is published on the NBN Gateway within the Stackpole National Nature Reserve Species Inventory and Ad Hoc sightings from around Pembrokeshire (extracted from Recorder 6). See Parent ID.\tInternal Custodian: Paul Culyer - Senior Reserve Manager, Operational Services, South, NRW (Used to be Bob Haycock - Senior Reserve Manager, Stackpole, NRW.)  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Pembrokeshire Bird Group\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17374\tNRW_DS45632\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.923Z\t634\tUnassessed\tEditable\tYr Wyddfa/Snowdon Terrestrial Environmental Change Network (ECN) Site\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3435363332\tLong term monitoring of physical, chemical and biological variables on Yr Wyddfa/Snowdon. The monitoring is designed to collect, store, analyse and interpret long-term datasets for physical, chemical and biological variables, which drive or respond to environmental change. The Yr Wyddfa monitoring contributes to the national Environmental Change Network. It is one of 12 terrestrial ECN sites across the UK and is important within the context of the network because it extends its climatic and ecological coverage, as well as the geographical distribution (it\u0027s the only montane site under oceanic influence and has the highest rainfall). The ECN\u0027s objectives are to establish and maintain a network of sites from which to obtain comparable long-term datasets through the monitoring of a range of variables; to integrate and analyse these data to identify natural and man-induced environmental changes, improve understanding of the causes of change, distinguish short-term fluctuations from long-term trends, and predict future changes.  NRW also carries out other monitoring at the site which contributes to a variety of national monitoring initiatives, see lineage fields of the individual metadata entries in the \u0027contains\u0027 field below.\tStandard protocols are used to collect data, some of which have been developed specifically for ECN (see individual metadata entries in the contains field below for more details). The data is sent to CEH Merlewood where it is checked and transferred into a centralised database managed by the ECN Central Coordinating Unit (CCU) incorporating data from all ECN sites across the UK. Yr Wyddfa/Snowdon, Gwynedd site: The 700ha site incorporates the summit of Yr Wyddfa and includes three additional summits over 800m, it also includes 5 lakes. It is part of Yr Wyddfa National Nature Reserve managed by CCW.\tThe data is subject to a variety of quality control procedures (QA). Each monitoring protocol has a corresponding paper form which is filled in the field. The data is entered into an Excel spreadsheet which has background calibration graphs in order to flag any outliers within the data. These outliers can then be double checked against the paper records. Every year the spreadsheet is checked against the paper records, and each line of data within the spreadsheet is assigned a quality code which helps to flag up any problems during data collation e.g. snow in gauge. The CEH CCU also have their own QA procedures which they follow on receipt of the data.\tSee individual metadata entries in the \u0027contains\u0027 field above for more details\tInternal Custodian: Environmental Change Network Project Officer, Operational Support Group, NRW. Environmental Change Network Data Officer, Operational Support Group, NRW.  Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tWelsh Government-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:13.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17365\tNRW_DS45660\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.061Z\t1047\tUnassessed\tEditable\tChough Breeding Population Surveillance in Pembrokeshire\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3435363630\tSurveillance of breeding population (including productivity and recruitment) and non-breeding population of Chough (Pyrrhocorax pyrrhocorax) in Pembrokeshire, South Wales. Former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW) collect and collate data from the south Pembrokeshire coastline which includes Caldey Island, and the Castlemartin Coast Special Protection Area (SPA).  SPAs are strictly protected sites classified in accordance with Article 4 of the EC Birds Directive, which came into force in April 1979. They are classified for rare and vulnerable birds (as listed on Annex I of the Directive), and for regularly occurring migratory species. Castlemartin SPA is designed for breeding and over wintering choughs. This includes the Pembrokeshire Coastal National Park (PCNP) coordinate records from St Anne\u0027s Head to Cemaes Head and the offshore islands, including Ramsey (SPA) and St David\u0027s Head). In this dataset some records date from early 1960s forming part of the first national survey; recording was established at the main study area in 1978 but most records date from 1982.  There were numerous national and local purposes to this data capture.   National purposes:  - To contribute to decadal national and international surveys of chough and to update JNCC records for reporting under the Birds Directive (SPAs) and Habitats Directive (Special Areas of Conservation (SAC)).   Local purposes  - To assess condition of chough as an Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) feature in Castlemartin Coast SPA; - To maintain up-to-date records of concentrations of chough relevant to management of recreation (sea cliff-climbing and coasteering); - To service update data requirements for local chough Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP), in partnership with other members of a local chough study group, including PCNP Authority who coordinate records for rest of Pembrokeshire.\tBreeding population is censused annually from cliff-tops and nest site locations recorded in GIS register. Annual estimates of breeding productivity are made. Estimates of recruitment to population are made based on locally managed database of colour-ringed sightings of nestlings within two SPA study areas (Castlemartin and Ramsey Island) and contribution to all Wales chough colour-ringed sightings database. GIS register of feeding and roosting areas for breeding and non-breeding birds is maintained. The dataset is linked to periodic decadal national surveys, e.g. 1982, 1992, 2002.  Also see SCARABBS and RBBP for all Wales monitoring.\t\tRaw south Pembrokeshire data are held by NRW at Stackpole office;  Remainder by Pembrokeshire Coast National Park All Wales colour-ringed data are held by Tony Cross/Adrienne Stratford (and by RSPB)\tStackpole office (see contact details above); BTO/NRW annual Schedule 1 Licence reports (location unknown).\tPembrokeshire Coast National Park-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:30:17.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17368\tNRW_DS45924\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.247Z\t14\tUnassessed\tEditable\t1 Otter Survey Wales 2000 to 2004 - ***  FULL FIELD TEST NOT REAL DATA  *** update two - Third Party Dataset\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3435393234\t*** This metadata entry is NOT REAL data. ***  It is just acting as a test metadata entry for use internally within NRW and as an example for staff who are entering metadata.  Description to be included in the Abstract: - Purpose of data capture. - Geographic Coverage. - Temporal Coverage. - Give context to the data collected.  - Be as detailed as possible.   EXAMPLE: The aim of this data collection was to monitoring for otter spraints, holts and sightings on all major river systems and their tributaries across Wales.Surveying was carried out between April and October between 2000 and 2004. Each river was visited only once each year. The river systems covered included Usk, Tywi and Wye, all of which are designated under the Habitats Directive as Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).\t*** This metadata entry is NOT REAL data. *** It is just acting as a test metadata entry for use internally within NRW and as an example for staff who are entering metadata.  Description to be included in the Lineage: - Methods of data capture. - Confidence in the data (if available). - Any other relevant information. - Be as detailed as possible.   EXAMPLE: 70 walk over transects were conducted over 250 sites across Wales looking for otter spraints, holts and sightings on all major river systems and their tributaries. Each river was visited only once each year, and where time allowed additional searches were carried out.  Contractors work was checked by Regional CCW staff and final data was checked by Specialist CCW staff. Survey areas were established based on records of otter sightings in the Welsh Otter Survey 1985-90, published by Welsh Otter Society.  Records are sent to JNCC annually, to contribute to the UK Otter Survey.\t*** This metadata entry is NOT REAL data. *** It is just acting as a test metadata entry for use internally within NRW and as an example for staff who are entering metadata.\tThe location of the data internally: EXAMPLE: Data is archived as project 450, media 1372. (Or) GIS Corporate Data Store: X-Layer_Files\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Ramsar.lyr   Mammal Ecologist office Terrestrial Science Group CCW.\tData Custodian:  Jonathon Rothwell - Terrestrial Mapping Scientist NRW.  Or contact Mammal Ecologists, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW.\tCoal Authority\t\t1991-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:43:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17367\tNRW_DS46083\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.239Z\t596\tUnassessed\tEditable\tStoneworts in Wales (Recorder 6 Dataset)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436303833\tA collation of all known records of stoneworts in Wales from 1726 up till August 2007. Stoneworts (or charophytes) are a group of algae that grow in fresh and brackish water and have a relatively complex structure. Stoneworts are good indicators of water quality due to their sensitivities towards pollution, including nutrient enrichment acting as a bio-indicator.  Many are of conservation importance. 30 species have been recorded in the UK and 17 of these occur in Wales. Of the latter, four are Red Data Book species, six are Nationally Scarce, and 9 are considered rare in Wales; three are Priority Species in the UK Biodiversity Action Plane (BAP) and are included on the National Assembly of Wales\u0027 List of Species and Habitats of Principal Importance for the Conservation of Biological Diversity (Section 74 list).  All data collated and verified at source by known expert.\tFor each record in the collation the collector or recorder is named and in most cases there is a reference to the original source.  The collation has been updated in 2008.  The data has been derived from a range of sources; mostly from articles in journals, floras and guides, but also from herbarium records, personal observations and commissioned surveys.\tAssociated spatial data is also available. See metadata record 100170 Stoneworts in Wales - GIS Dataset\t[A]  Species Records are available in Recorder 6  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tJulian Woodman, Higher Plant Specialist, Terrestrial Species, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various including volunteer recorders\t\t2007-09-26\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:58:51.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17364\tNRW_DS46084\t2024-05-28T12:21:22.017Z\t779\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Invertebrate Data for Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436303834\tThis dataset is a collation of all confirmed occurrence records of UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority invertebrate species reported from Wales. The aim of the data was to collate information on UK BAP invertebrates in Wales and inform Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) partners. Data accuracy is reliant on original sources. Spatial references, dates and species identifications have been validated by Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) invertebrate experts checking printed lists of records, and literature references have been added where possible.\tThis is a derived sub-set of the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID).  WID is a collation of records from many available sources, including literature records, published surveys and information submitted by naturalists. Records (including negative occurrences where the species has been specifically searched for without success) are noted.\t\t[A]  Original data is held in Recorder 6 within the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID).  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tMike Howe, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various including volunteer recorders\t\t2001-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:19.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17363\tNRW_DS46086\t2024-05-28T12:21:21.926Z\t449\tUnassessed\tEditable\tContacts Data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436303836\tThe Contacts data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) database information of individuals and organisations who do business with the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), who now form part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  The purpose of this data capture was to collate information on Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 contacts.\tData was entered into the system by staff. This system was decommissioned and data moved into new NRW systems, such as AMX and CRM. Please contact the Data Custodians for futher information.\t\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tAndrew Peterken, Designated Sites Project Manager, South and East Region, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:12:35.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17362\tNRW_DS46100\t2024-05-28T12:21:21.883Z\t2\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLichens of Conservation Concern - Site Data for Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313030\tA collation of distribution data for 123 species of Red Data Book and National Biodiversity Action Plan lichens. This dataset is historic and is not being updated. New records and corrections are being collated in the Wales Threatened Lichens dataset (see separate metadata entry). The purpose of this data capture as to assist in informing the process of conservation objective setting.\tThe data has been derived from a wide range of sources e.g. literature, herbarium records, personal observations, records of the British Lichen Society, commissioned surveys and monitoring. The records concentrate on the period since 1960 but a few earlier literature and herbarium records are included where no other more recent records have been located. The majority of the records are from 1980 onwards. In all cases the date given is the date when last reported. Not all dates are given to the precise day or month, most are only known to be within or before a particular year. The dataset is likely to be subject to some degree of error or omission, as validation by checking for transcription errors has been minimal. The dataset is historic and is not being updated.\tTO BE ARCHIVED - THIS DATASET AS BEEN SUPERSEDED BY THE LICHENS THREATENED SPECIES LAYER\tThis data is now available on the NBN Atlas  The Threatened Lichen Database is basically defunct now - all data for Threatened Lichens are stored along with those for all other bryophytes by the British Lichen Society and there is no point in NRW storing them separately as that just leads to duplication and errors.\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team (Formerly Alan Hale, Lower Plant Ecologist, CCW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-British Lichen Society-Various including volunteer recorders\t\t2002-01-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:13:52.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17361\tNRW_DS46129\t2024-05-28T12:21:21.741Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tArctic-Alpine Plant Monitoring for Effects of Climate Change\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313239\tThis data consists of 6 arctic-alpine species monitoring results. The purpose of this data capture was to determine the long-term effects of climate change on a specific group of arctic-alpine species.\tSpecies presence/absence is monitored in a fine grained, subdivided quadrats. Approximately 30 quadrats on 3 sites in Yr Wyddfa National Nature Reserve (NNR).  Species distribution is represented diagrammatically. Baseline data has been collected and will be repeated every 5 years.\t\tUnknown\tJonathon Rothwell, Terrestrial Mapping Specialist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team / Terrestrial Ecosystems Group, NRW  The data was collected orginially by: Alex Turner, H Roberts, and B Jones.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17411\tNRW_DS46133\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.411Z\t571\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra) Survey (1994 - 2002)\tArolwg Mor-hwyaid Duon Cymru gyfan\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313333\tThis dataset comprises of results from an aerial survey of common scoters, Melanitta nigra, (seabirds) in Carmarthen Bay, Cardigan Bay and Liverpool Bay. The data was collected to provide information for Special Protection Areas (SPA) designations and monitoring.   Common Scoter feed on bivalves and other invertebrates such as polychaetes and amphipods which are found in abundance in the shallow waters (less than 10 m) in Carmarthen Bay.\tThis is a collation of both aerial and ground monitoring surveys of the Common scoter over a seven year period.\tSee the following metadata entry for 2010 - 2011 data: Aerial monitoring of common scoter Melinitta nigra in Carmarthen Bay 2010 - 2011. Metadata Resource Identifier 115216\tAssociated data is held as Project No. 19, media 537 - Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media501to600\\Media537\tThe internal data custodians are: Marine Ornithologist, NRW and Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. The metadata point of contact: Data Services Team\tAll Wales Common Scoter Survey Partnership (AWCSS Partnership)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T15:22:44.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17413\tNRW_DS46136\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.415Z\t612\tValid\tEditable\tSeabird Colony Surveillance along Castlemartin Coastline, South Pembrokeshire\tCadw golwg ar gytrefi adar mor ar hyd arfordir Castellmartin, de Penfro\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313336\tThis dataset contains the results from the surveillance of breeding seabird species including: auks (guillemot, razorbill, puffin), large Larus gulls (herring, lesser black-back, great black-back), kittiwake, shag and fulmar and the surveillance of productivity of kittiwakes.   The overall purposes of the data capture was to contribute to the JNCC-managed Seabird Monitoring Programme.   Local purposes for the data capture were to: - Assess SSSI feature condition (seabird assemblage) in two adjacent Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) - Maintain up-to-date records of regionally high concentrations of seabirds relevent to management of recreation (sea cliff-climbing and coasteering)   - Service update/data requirements for local regional marine pollution contingency plans.\tCounts of whole colonies for all species, and recorded/mapped study plots (for auks and kittiwake) from the cliff-top and by boat (standard methodology utilised, following JNCC Seabird Monitoring manual), within colony/sub colony zones in standard JNCC Seabird 2000 Census areas and sites within the UK Seabird colony register.   Approximately 20 kms of Castlemartin limestone coast, including Stackpole SSSI and Castlemartin Cliffs and Dunes SSSI (both coastal areas are within a chough SPA) with occasional surveys of other south Pembs coastal areas between Tenby and Freshwater West (all within other coastal SSSI).   Linked also to periodic national seabird surveys, first one in 1969; next 1985/87; most recent Seabird 2000 (1999-2001).\t\tRaw data are held by NRW at Stackpole and by JNCC -  electronic copies annually forwarded to their Seabird data coordinator.\tSenior Reserve Warden - Stackpole, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T15:29:28.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17418\tNRW_DS46140\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.589Z\t632\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGreater Horseshoe Bat (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum) breeding productivity surveillance in Pembrokeshire\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313430\tThe greater horseshoe bat Rhinolophus ferrumequinum, nursery roosts at Stackpole and Slebech, Pembrokeshire, are principal summer roosts of the West Wales population of the species which ranges through Pembrokeshire, Carmarthenshire, Ceredigion and Gower, West Glamorgan. Monitoring the population is difficult, as for much of the year only a small percentage are accessible through winter surveys of hibernacula. Monitoring the status of the population is therefore only practical at the nursery roosts. This dataset consists of Greater Horseshoe Bat (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum) surveillance results. The surveillance of the breeding productivity of Greater Horseshoe Bat i.e. numbers of young born annually occurs between June and August. The purpose of this data capture was to: -  Provide information and updates to the local Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) -  Assess feature conditions of greater horseshoe bats within Pembrokeshire Bat Sites Special Area of Conservation (SAC), and 3 component Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI).  -  Contribute to the management database of the breeding population records for Greater Horseshoe Bats species recovers programme and the UK BAP species programmes.\tSurveillance included the records of births and babies counted/handled/toe-nail-clipped within nursery roosts. Growth measurements taken and animals sexed.\t\tStackpole office, NRW\tInternal Custodian:  Paul Culyer, Senior Reserve Manager - Pembrokeshire, Operational Services, South (used to be Bob Haycock, Senior Reserve Warden - Stackpole, NRW.)  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17406\tNRW_DS46159\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.343Z\t308\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMonitoring of Horseshoe Bats with Automatic Bat Counters\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313539\tAutomated bat counters have been deployed at various important roost sites for Greater and Lesser Horseshoe Bats (Rhinolophus ferrumequinum and Rhinolophus hipposideros). These collect roost entrance and exit numbers for the bats as well as some limited environmental data. The purpose of this data capture was to obtain reliable population data, in order to relate the changes in bat population and behaviour to changes in the climate and environment in an attempt to understand the factors that limit the geographical ranges of the 2 species. In Wales, Greater Horseshoe Bats breed at the northern limit of their distribution and Lesser Horseshoe Bats breed up to the north coast of Wales where they are fairly common but they are not found at this latitude in England or the mainland of Europe. The sample roosts are within Special Areas of Conservation. The data gives valuable information on the seasonal activity of these bats which can be directly related to management within and around these key roost sites.\tThe flight of all bats in and out of a roost is recorded continuously on a computer (together with some environmental data in the case of some loggers). The bats are detected by infrared beam arrays, mounted in the  roost entrance, together with laptops installed nearby. A bat flying through a roost entrance is detected when a beam is interrupted. There are two parallel arrays so direction can be recorder along with time/date. Raw data is manually processed using a script every few months to produce counts and graphs.\t\tAfter preliminary analysis the data are held in spreadsheets on NRW corporate document store (Ffynnon). H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\EIT\\Staff (ALL)\\Cyprian Payne\\Bats\tStuart Kay or Barnaby Letheren for technical questions Jean Matthews, Mammal Ecologist Bats and Riparian Mammals, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:24.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17415\tNRW_DS46160\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.468Z\t870\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Grassland Survey of Wales\tCyfnod 2 Arolwg o Laswelltir yr Iseldir yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313630\tThis project comprised of a detailed survey of plant communities in semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation across Wales. The purpose of this data capture was to characterise the semi-natural lowland grassland vegetation of Wales and to provide a systematic conservation assessment of the sites surveyed.   The survey locations and sites vary in size and complexity from small uniform hay-meadows of less than 1 ha, to extensive wet-dry pasture systems covering over 100 ha.   The datasets which were produced from this project have been extensively validated and verified. However, the main limitation of these datasets is their age. Habitat composition may change over time due to successional trends, agricultural treatments or even planned developments, which means that there is no guarantee that the information within these datasets is still representative on the ground today. However they do provide an important long-term catalogue of data which can be used to monitor changes to the grassland environments overtime.    The dataset contributes to JNCC\u0027s UK Lowland Grassland Database, held by the Lowland Grassland Lead Co-ordination Network at NE in Peterborough.     Where a grassland site has been resurveyed the most recent survey data is in the main Phase2 Grassland Survey datasets and the previous survey(s) have now been put into a folder called Archive [Historic]  GIS layer files within this dataset include: Site boundary (Sites), Vegetation polygons (veg), Mosaic Table, Mosaic Polygons (Mospolys), Quadrats, Photos and Target Note (Notes, points, polygons).    There is a DMS document explaining the GIS layers and data structure called P2 Grassland data structures in ArcGIS.   Survey Reports are also available from NRW.-Roedd y prosiect hwn yn cynnwys arolwg manwl o gymunedau planhigion yn llystyfiant glaswelltir rhannol naturiol yr iseldir ledled Cymru. Diben casglu\u0027r data hwn oedd nodi llystyfiant glaswelltir rhannol naturiol iseldir Cymru a darparu asesiad cadwraeth systematig o\u0027r safleoedd a arolygwyd. Mae\u0027r setiau data a gynhyrchwyd yn sgil y prosiect hwn wedi\u0027u cadarnhau a\u0027u dilysu\u0027n drwyadl. Fodd bynnag, prif gyfyngiad y setiau data hyn yw eu hoedran. Gall cyfansoddiad cynefinoedd newid dros amser o ganlyniad i dueddiadau olynol, triniaethau amaethyddol neu hyd yn oed ddatblygiadau arfaethedig, sy\u0027n golygu nad oes unrhyw sicrwydd bod y setiau data hyn yn parhau i gynrychioli\u0027r hyn sydd ar y tir heddiw.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThe sites identified for survey were identified mainly from the Phase 1 Habitat Survey.  The areas surveyed included all forms of semi-natural neutral, acidic and calcareous grassland in lowland Wales, as well as wetter Molinia (purple-moor grass) and Juncus (rush) pasture communities and associated soligenous mires. Community definition follows the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). At each site, homogeneous stands of vegetation were identified, sampled with 2 x 2 m quadrats and mapped onto Ordnance Survey base maps or aerial photographs at a 1:5000 scale.\tThis dataset is compiled of numerous GIS layers, images and species datasets. This dataset is one of a series of major Phase 2 lowland habitat surveys including Natural Resources Wales (NRW)/Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) in house and commissioned surveys covering peatland, heathland, woodland and saltmarsh.\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Lowland grassland   a)  Reports: TSG N:drive and Archive Room.   b)  Recorder 6 database - All surveys related to this are tagged as Phase 2 lowland grassland surveys  - LGSW Brecknock CCW0002600000009     - LGSW Carmarthen \u0026amp; Dinefwr CCW0002600000008     - LGSW Ceredigion CCW0002600000005     - LGSW Clwyd CCW0002600000003     - LGSW East Gwynedd CCW0002600000002     - LGSW Gwent CCW0002600000012     - LGSW Mid \u0026amp; South Glamorgan CCW0002600000011     - LGSW Montgomery CCW0002600000004     - LGSW Preseli \u0026amp; South Pembroke CCW0002600000007     - LGSW Radnor CCW0002600000006     - LGSW West Glamorgan \u0026amp; Llanelli CCW0002600000010     - LGSW West Gwynedd CCW0002600000001     c)  GIS layers  X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 2 Lowland grassland.   Document explaining file structure can be found here:- DMS\\Evidence\\Terrestrial Habitats\\Lowland Grassland Phase II Survey\\All Wales\\Database - digital\\P2 Grassland data structures in ArcGIS. d)  Site packs: Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV and on DMS DMS\\Evidence\\Terrestrial Habitats\\Lowland Grassland Phase II Survey\\Grassland Survey Site Packs e)  Some images are stored on Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\TSG\\GRASSURV\\PHASE II PHOTO AND IMAGE ARCHIVE.   DVDs are in the safe.   This data is now available on the NBN Gateway\tStuart Smith, Grassland Ecologist, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team, NRW  For data held on Recorder 6 - Master Reference Data Team, NRW For spatial datasets - contact Spatial Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-12-09\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3600/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_phase_2_grassland\t2025-12-11T10:42:20.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17414\tNRW_DS46197\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.588Z\t313\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCondition monitoring of National Nature Reserves (NNRs) in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313937\tNational Nature Reserves (NNRs) contain examples of some of the most important natural and semi-natural terrestrial and coastal ecosystems in Great Britain. They are managed to conserve their habitats or to provide special opportunities for scientific study of the habitats communities and species represented within them. In addition they may be managed to provide public recreation that is compatible with their natural heritage interests.  NNRs are declared by the statutory country conservation agencies under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949 and the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981. In some cases NNRs form part of for wholly within Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) or Special Protection Areas (SPAs).  The purpose of this data capture was to assess the condition of SSSI/SAC/SPA features on NNRs in Wales. SAC features are generally monitored to the condition of the feature at the SPA level. Features on designated sites are to be assessed over a period of six years in a rolling monitoring cycle. The first cycle began in 1999, however SAC monitoring cycle began in 2001.\tAll protected features on the NNRs are monitored using the same methodology for the specific protected sites it encompasses.  - Condition monitoring of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales: Rapid Assessments on a 6 yearly cycle. - Condition Monitoring of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales: Common standards monitoring consisting of an assessment of feature condition for protected sites based on performance indicators. - Condition monitoring of Special Protection Areas (SPAs) in Wales: With this in mind various methods of data capture was used depending on the site and type of feature being assessed.\t\tHigh level data are held centrally by NRW Environmental Change Group.  Supporting data are held in Regional NRW offices or by Science Groups.\tCustodian: Dylan Lloyd  or   Team Leader Monitoring and Surveillance, NRW Metadata point of contact: Data Services\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:23.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17408\tNRW_DS46198\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.286Z\t1229\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCommon Standards Monitoring (CSM)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436313938\tOne of the key responsibilities of the statutory nature conservation agencies in the UK, like Natural Resources Wales, is the identification and protection of a series of sites intended to conserve important wildlife and Earth science features. Such sites may be designated under: - National legislation (Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs)), - European Directives (Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Special Protection Areas (SPAs)), or - An international Convention (Ramsar sites).  The scope of monitoring includes coastal, freshwater, lowland grassland, lowland heathland, lowland wetland, marine, upland habitats, woodland, earth science (geodiversity), amphibians, birds, fish, invertebrates, reptiles, marine mammals, terrestrial mammals, vascular plants, bryophytes and lichens. The purpose of this data capture is to assess the condition of features on all designated sites (SSSI, SAC, SPA and Ramsar) based on site-specific performance indicators, and to record aspects of their management, both as an aid to management decisions and for reporting at a variety of scales, including UK through JNCC, and for contributing to UK reports on Conservation Status under the EC Habitats Directive.\tThere are many methodologies used in the reporting and monitoring on protected sites, including Wetland Bird Survey (WeBS), intertidal monitoring, seabird monitoring programmes, cetacean monitoring, Statutory Conservation Agency / RSPB Annual Breeding Bird Schemes (SCARABBS). Performance indicators are devised from JNCC generic guidance (http://www.jncc.gov.uk), modified to reflect local distinctiveness and quantified to reflect the desired state of the feature. Assessments of these performance indicators are made using methods appropriate to the nature and scale of the feature and conclusions drawn about condition depending on whether performance indicators pass or fail. Information relevant to management is also collected. Together, the condition assessments and management information provide the basis for an assessment of conservation status.\t\tHigh level data are held centrally by NRW Environmental Change Group.  Supporting data are held in Regional NRW offices or by Science Groups.\tCustodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team.  Metadata: Data Services Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2014-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:23.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17409\tNRW_DS46201\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.461Z\t5\tUnassessed\tEditable\tImpacts of Atmospheric Nitrogen Deposition in Combination with Variable Grazing Pressure upon Upland Grazing\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436323031\tThis study was done to provide baseline data on nitrogen deposition. The following was monitored: Pollution, habitats, plant communities and species assemblages. The purpose of this data capture was to aid with the monitoring of Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSI) features, and allow staff to make informed decision on the conservation management of upland sites, especially those in ESA or with management agreements.\t\t\t\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:52:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17403\tNRW_DS46211\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.261Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) invertebrate data for Ceredigion\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436323131\tCollation of occurrence records for UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority invertebrate species in Ceredigion. This is a subset of  \u0027UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) invertebrate data for Wales\u0027.  In Ceredigion it covers 7 species of ant, 8 species of beetle, 3 species of butterfly, 1 species of dragonfly, 14 species of moth, 5 species of true fly and 1 mollusc. The purpose of this data capture was to understand the distribution of species in Wales in order to inform conservation decisions.\tThe full (all-Wales) dataset consists of all records for these species (including negative occurrences where the species has been specifically searched for without success) that have been collated by CCW staff from available sources, including literature records, published surveys and information submitted by naturalists. This is a subset of  \u0027UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) invertebrate data for Wales\u0027, created by querying the main dataset.  Data accuracy is reliant on original sources. Spatial references, dates and species identifications have been validated by CCW\u0027s invertebrate experts checking printed lists of records, and literature references have been added where possible. The Ceredigion dataset has not been updated since 2002, although the source dataset (i.e. the all-Wales dataset) is updated on an ongoing basis.\t\tThe dataset is held on the NBN Gateway only. The full dataset from which it was derived from is held in NRW (see separate metadata entry).\tInternal Custodian: John Bratton, Biological Data Officer Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, NRW, Bangor  Metadata Contact: Data Services Team\tVarious including volunteer recorders-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-01-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:48.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17419\tNRW_DS46214\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.693Z\t545\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBenthic Marine Data from Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) Technical Support (Research and Monitoring) Contracts\tData Morol Benthig o Gontractau Cefnogaeth Dechnegol (Ymchwil a Monitro) y Cyngor Cefn Gwlad\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436323134\tThis dataset is a collection of marine benthic surveys related to former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), and now Natural Resources Wales (NRWs) technical support, research and monitoring contracts.  The purpose of this data capture and collation was to aid coastal zone and sea-use management and to contribute to the identification of important areas of marine natural heritage.  The surveys focussed on benthic habitats, often referred to as \u0027biotopes\u0027, in the intertidal and inshore (typically within 3 nm) subtidal areas. Original copies of all the surveys are now held separately in NRW\u0027s Marine Recorder database.\tThe contract surveys were collected according to different methodologies, although some were collected according to methods described in the Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) Rational and Methods report. Data was collected by qualified and/or experienced contractors. Some validation and verification has subsequently been carried out by CCW staff, however, the extent of this varied between surveys.\t\tMFSG Marine Recorder database at http://data.nbn.org.uk/\tInternal Custodian and Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:21:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17410\tNRW_DS46322\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.435Z\t4\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGrassholm Northern Gannet (Morus bassanus) Survey (2004)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436333232\tThis dataset consists of a count of the breeding northern gannet (Morus bassanus) population on Grassholm in 2004. The purpose of this data capture was to assess population estimates for Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and Special Protection Areas (SPA) monitoring and to update the UK population estimate. The projected aimed to make the first complete survey of this gannetry since 1999 and to: -  assess current numbers, conservation status and number of Apparently Occupied Site (AOS),  -  contribute to the decadal survey of the UK gannet population and the wider north Atlantic gannet colonies, and  -  establish survey protocol for future site condition monitoring.\tA land count and aerial survey of the Grassholm gannetry was carried out to check colony boundaries and club areas. The mean population estimate was 32,094 apparently occupied sites (AOS).\t\tphotos and excel key to jpgs are located at N:\\USERS\\MurphyM (was GrayC)\\SPAs \u0026amp; SSSIs\\SPAs\\Grassholm\\Grassholm survey 2004\\  H:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\MurphyM\\SPAs \u0026amp; SSSIs\\SPAs\\Grassholm H:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\MurphyM\\contracts\\Completed projects\\Grassholm  Report on H:TSG/Gray_C/Contract reports/Report 604 Gannets 2004 Will be held JNCC seabird database and NBN\tMatty Murphy, Senior Maritime Ornithologist, Natural Resources Planning/Marine and Freshwater Ecosystem Group, NRW (Formerly Catherine Gray, Ornithologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Perrins-Centre for Ecology and Hydrology (CEH)\t\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:14:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17417\tNRW_DS46929\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.596Z\t264\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBiodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436393239\tMarsh Fritillary  (Euphydryas aurinia) is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority invertebrate species. This dataset is a collation of all confirmed occurrence records of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) butterfly from Wales. The purpose of this data collation is to inform Local Biodiversity Action Plan  (LBAP) Partners and inform conservation decisions related to Marsh Fritillary butterflies. Data accuracy is reliant on original sources. Spatial references, dates and species identifications have been validated by Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 CCW\u0027s invertebrate experts checking printed lists of records, and literature references have been added where possible.\tThe dataset is a sub-set of the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID) (See Parent ID). WID is a collation of records from many available sources, including literature records, published surveys and information submitted by naturalists. Records (including negative occurrences where the species has been specifically searched for without success) are noted.  The data is also converted to a GIS datset (see metadata entry: 99503)\t\t[A]  Original data is held within the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID) CCW in Recorder 6  The data is also available as a GIS layeron the X drive (see metadata entry URI. NRW_DS99503)\tMike Howe, Invertebrate Ecologist, TEG, NRW (Formerly Adrian Fowles, Senior Invertebrate Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, Countryside Council for Wales)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various including volunteer recorders\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:37.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17404\tNRW_DS46954\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.288Z\t262\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAerial Photographs of the Intertidal Areas of Wales 1996 to 2002\tLluniau o Ardaloedd Rhynglanw Cymru wedi\u0027u tynnu o\u0027r awyr, 1996 i 2002\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436393534\tStereoscopic aerial photographs supplied by CUCAP (Cambridge University Centre for Aerial Photography) at scale of 1:10,000, CR World (Liverpool Bay Forum), BKS Surveys Limited, ExeGesIS and the Environment Agency for the coast of Wales.  The aim of this data collation was to map Intertidal Phase 1 communities (biotopes) both in the field and for the production of digital Phase 1 Intertidal maps on the GIS (MapInfo). Also used for monitoring Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) and Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) features and intertidal casework. The collation is a complete coverage of the Welsh coast from the Ribble Estuary (England) to the Severn Estuary inclusive.\tPhotos flown on mean low water springs when these occur as close to mid-day as possible on a clear day between 1996 and 2002. Scanned and rectified at 300ppi with the provision of tab files for MapInfo.\t\tHafod Elfyn Fireproof room. M:/airphotos\tInternal Custodians: Phase 1 Intertidal Team, Marine and Freshwater Science Group, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Data Services / Spatial Data Team, NRW\tCR World GeoInformation Group-Cambridge University. Centre for Aerial Photography (CUCAP)-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:22:04.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17412\tNRW_DS46955\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.473Z\t567\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Great Crested Newt (Triturus cristatus) Populations at Halkyn Mountain / Mynydd Helygain Special Area of Conservation (SAC) (2005)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3436393535\tMonitoring of Great Crested Newts (GCNs) on Halkyn Mountain Special Area of Conservation (SAC), under contract. The monitoring consisted of the following:  a) Survey of 45 ponds, where GCN had previously been recorded, for eggs, larvae and juveniles and other environmental data.   b) Mapping of terrestrial habitat within 250m of pond to assess the value of the habitat for GCNs (total of 183.9ha). c) Collation of existing GCN count data from 1985 to 2005 in known display ponds in the SAC. d) Condition assessment of GCN as a SAC feature.  This dataset forms part of the Wales-wide Common Standards Monitoring. The aim of this data capture was to assess the condition of designated SAC features based on site-specific performance indicators, and to record aspects of their management, both as an aid to management decisions and for reporting at a variety of scales.\tVarious methodologies were used in this data collection.  Terrestrial Habitat - Terrestrial habitat for great crested newts was defined by Countryside Council for Wales as being within 250 m of each pond. Areas that fell outside the boundary of the SAC were excluded from the mapping. Using MapInfo Professional v 6.5 GIS software, maps were produced showing a habitat survey zone of 250 m radius around each of the ponds to be surveyed. A 10m x 10m grid was overlaid onto this base map in order to classify the terrestrial habitat at the required resolution.   Pond Assessments - All 45 ponds were revisited on 23rd August 2005 to undertake the pond assessments. Estimates were made of: - The percentage of the pond surface shaded by native macrophytes; - The percentage of the entire pond perimeter shaded to at least 1m from the shore; - The percentage of the southern perimeter shaded; and - Whether the pond was less than 10cm deep at its deepest point.  Larval Surveys - A torchlight survey using a 1 million candle power Clulite torch was undertaken on 23rd August 2005 of all ponds still containing water, in order to determine the presence of larvae/juveniles. Egg searches - Natural and artificial egg laying substrates within 1m of the shore were inspected for the presence of Triturus cristatus eggs. Signs of the presence of fish or waterfowl were also recorded at this time, as well as the presence of any invasive species such as Crassula helmsii, or evidence of potential disturbance or pollution.\tMonitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, Bangor, NRW.\t[A]  Associated data is held in the following location; H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0026amp; Datasets\\27 GCN at Halkyn Mountain SAC   [B]  Species Data held in Recorder 6: CCW - HQ Main Assessment of Great Crested Newts at Halkyn Mountain SAC CCW000480000000E\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:06:31.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17405\tNRW_DS47010\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.204Z\t736\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBat Roosts Database - North West Wales (GWYBATS)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3437303130\tThis GWYBATS is a Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) regional bat roost site information and lesser horseshoe bat monitoring data for Caernarvonshire, Anglesey and Merionethshire. The original records were derived from BATSITES-WALES and the original Bat Roosts Database - Caernarvonshire and Anglesey (GWYBATS), which were subsequently validated. New records received by the CCW Bangor (Llys y Bont) regional office have since been added. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts, to assist CCW\u0027s conservation work. In particular, to provide information when dealing with development proposals, to support the monitoring and notification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs), and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species.\tThis dataset has been derived from Caernarvonshire, Anglesey and Merionethshire vice-counties and Gwynedd County/Unitary authority 1974-1995/8.  Data was generated from visits to roosts by staff and volunteers and/or from enquiries received by NCC or CCW.  The dataset called GWYBATS was compiled using data from BATDATA-WALES and BATSITES-WALES for the Gwynedd Unitary Authority region. Now the top copy of this data is held and managed by Cofnod who publish it on the NBN Gateway on behalf of NRW, who own the dataset.\tCofnod have used this data to improve the Natural Resources Wales North Wales Bat Roosts dataset - See Parent ID for a link to this data and further information.\tThis data is held and managed by Cofnod - who have the top copy. Shared drive with Dolgellau, Llys-y-bont, HQ.   MapInfo export held on the M Drive.\tInternal Custodian: NR Protected Species Officer Casework, Maes y Fynnon, NRW. Mammal Ecologist Bats and Riparian Mammals, Maes y Fynnon, NRW.  Internal Metadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW  External Contact: Aisling May  - Rheolwr Data/Data Manager       aisling.may@cofnod.org.uk Cofnod, Intec, Ffordd y Parc, Parc Menai, Bangor, Gwynedd. LL57 4FG.  (01248) 672603\tNature Conservancy Council (NCC)-Various including volunteer recorders-County bat groups-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T12:26:14.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17416\tNRW_DS47079\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.577Z\t297\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecial Area of Conservation (SAC) Monitoring: European Dry Heath, Northern Atlantic Wet Heath and Blanket Bog at Eryri candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) (2005)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3437303739\tMonitoring to assess the condition of three European Annex 1 habitats; Dry Heath, Wet Heath and Blanket Bog, at Eryri. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the condition of features on the Eryri cSAC designated site based on site-specific performance indicators, and to record aspects of their management, both as an aid to management decisions and for reporting at a variety of scales. The areas of Eryri targeted for survey lie in four separate compartments, and their approximate locations are around Aber Falls, Llyn Cowlyd, Tryfan and an area to the south and west of Bethesda.\tWork comprised of two phases.  Phase 1, the survey phase: Approximately one quarter of the Eryri cSAC was surveyed to assess the extent and condition of the three habitats.  Phase 2, the monitoring phase: Fifteen Sample Plots were subsequently chosen for monitoring, most but not all of these plots lie within the Phase 1 survey area. Seven Dry Heaths and four of each of Blanket Bog and Wet Heath habitats were sampled.  Survey phase:  Areas of habitat were mapped in the field and classified according to habitat type and condition; condition was based upon a set of Performance Indicators (PIs). The survey made use of the Countryside Council for Wales\u0027s National Vegetation Classification (NVC) maps along with recent aerial photography.  Monitoring phase: Monitoring phase: 14 Monitoring Plots were monitored with Sample Points situated within a grid format.  Botanical data and Geographical Position System (GPS) locations were captured using a high accuracy GPS.  Revised Performance Indicators were used.  Contributes to the \u0027Common Standards Monitoring (CSM)\u0027 dataset.\t\tData held on the H Drive at;  H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027AMPISAND\u0027 Datasets\\26 Heaths and blanket bogs at Eryri cSAC\tInternal Custodian: Monitoring and Surveillance Team Leader, NRW. Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-08-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17401\tNRW_DS47082\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.118Z\t665\tUnassessed\tEditable\tStorm Petrel (Hydrobates pelagicus) Census of Skokholm and Skomer Special Protection Area (SPA)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3437303832\tPopulation estimate of storm petrels Hydrobates pelagicus on Skomer in 2003 and 2004, and Skokholm in 2003. The purpose of this data capture was to create a population estimate to provide a baseline for monitoring. As well as establishment of monitoring plots and methods for future monitoring.\tTape playback census was conducted, with repeat visits to a calibration site to calculate response rates. Previous storm petrel surveys have been carried out to different methodologies, but this is the only complete census of both islands using standard tape-playback methods. This is the most reliable census to date, and confidence limits are given for the Skomer estimate. However, data can be influenced by variation in response rates, and further investigation is needed to better understand this variation and associated error. All data is held within the report.\t\tH:\\Science\\TSG\\USERS\\MurphyM\\contracts\\Completed projects\\Skomer \u0026amp; Skokholm storm petrels  Report on H:\\TSG\\Gray_C\\Contract reports\\\tMatt Murphy, Senior Maritime Ornithologist, Natural resources planning/Marine and freshwater ecosytem group, NRW (Formerly Catherine Gray)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2005-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:21.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17420\tNRW_DS64557\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.645Z\t0\tUnassessed\tEditable\tImages/Photograph Libraries\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353537\tNRW holds a large number of good quality photographs (both in digital and 35mm slide format) of biodiversity, landscape, countryside, marine and environmental subjects. These are held in various sets in HQ and Regional offices.  Purpose of data capture: To represent species and habitats etc found in Wales which may be used for interpretational and promotional purposes, eg. illustrations, slide shows, displays.\t\t\tHQ and Regional offices.\tMonica\u0027s Advice:  This is a collection of images that was held by Linda Williams in CCW (also known as the CCW photo image library and identified as SOAP Ref: 003920 on the ETC).  All images are in the process of transferring to the NRW image library as part of the Image store project.  Have no idea about the hardcopy slides, which probably sit still in regional offices or have been transferred to Jill/Dwyn as part of office moves and paper archive project.  To be honest I am not sure that it needs an entry now as I don’t really consider promotional/nice images to be data and would just delete the entry. Alternatively perhaps change it for a general entry about the content of the NRW Image store in the same way we have a general entry for WISKI content or Marine Recorder content (it could include general information about the archive of slides that Jill/Dwyn currently hold too?).  Bryan Tozer would be best placed to help write a generic entry for the NRW image store (the lineage could include a sentence that described that this now contains what was previously knows as the CCW image library – a set of presentation level images used for promotional purposes). NRW holds so many images, unless they are a specifically data e.g. photo-monitoring time series of site/feature or something that is digitally analysed using software I wouldnt bother with metadata entries for them as they are not data and I don’t think they fall under our remit to give advice on.  If those that are considered data tend to be part of a larger dataset and are captures as part of the metadata for the larger dataset.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T12:59:33.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17407\tNRW_DS64560\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.414Z\t3\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNRW Aerial Photographs of Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353630\tCCW has commissioned aerial photographs, which are held in various offices.\t\t\t\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:52:26.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17402\tNRW_DS64561\t2024-05-28T12:22:33.137Z\t1151\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAdmiralty Charts (Paper Records)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353631\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) holds paper copies of Admiralty Charts (maps of sea areas) covering much of the Welsh coastline.\tAdmiralty Charts are used for various marine related work around Wales.\t\tVarious paper maps are available through HQ and Regional offices.\tInternal Custodian: Keith Jones, GIS Data Manager, Information Management Group, CCW\tUnited Kingdom Hydrographic Office (UKHO)\t\t2013-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:58:53.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17399\tNRW_DS64563\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.579Z\t593\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) Image Store\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353633\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW) holds a large numbers of photographic images created for the following numerous purposes, including biological recording; habitat recording; land-use recording; recording events and activities; communications and public engagement.\tImages are captured by NRW staff or contractors on behalf of NRW, and uploaded to the NRW Image Store which was developed by Asset Bank. Each image contains it own metadata related to image properties and NRW keywords. Images from legacy Forestry Commission Wales, Countryside Council for Wales and Environment Agency Wales are also collated in the NRW Image Store.\t\thttp://imagestore.cyfoethnaturiolcymru.internal/asset-bank/action/viewHome  NB  You need to be on Pulse Secure for the URL in internal location to work.\tInternal Contact: Bryan Tozer, Advisor 2, Master Data Team  Whilst Bryan is the main contact for Image Store - each image and image collation will have an owner in the metadata in image store itself\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)-Various\t\t2013-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-10T09:14:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17400\tNRW_DS64572\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.625Z\t1\tUnassessed\tEditable\tForestry Grant Scheme Recording\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353732\tPurpose of data capture: To keep a record of FC grant aided schemes\t\t\t\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T10:52:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17397\tNRW_DS64573\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.421Z\t434\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCasework Data held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353733\tThe casework database records all consultations about development proposals and plans received by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and CCW\u0027s responses from across Wales. The main content is consultations received from other regulatory authorities, including planning authorities, seeking CCW\u0027s advice. The database includes a record of designated sites, protected species and other natural heritage interests affected by proposals.  The database cross references with CCW\u0027s response letters filed on the electronic data and records management system (Ffynnon).  As of January 2010, the casework database does not include the following:  Policy consultations; Section 28 notifications from Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) owners and occupiers (use the consents database); Section 28H and Section 28G notifications (use the consents database); Applications for licences issued by CCW (Use the Licence module of ISIS).  Recording of these will be integrated with the casework database in due course.  The casework database fulfills the following functions: a) A framework for processing consultations. b) A record of documentation for each consultation. c) A consistent approach and response to consultations throughout CCW. d) Management information about consultations\tNRW staff input on receipt of consultations and on dispatch of response letters.  The accuracy of data input if very high.  Data is missing from some secondary fields of the database.  The data is suitable for extracting information without any special knowledge of the database.  As of April 2010, analysis of data and interpretation of analyses relating to the period 2007 to March 2010 requires expert advice from the casework database manager.  High confidence:  Logging of consultations on the database. Records of the consulting authority for each consultation. Correct recording of the statutory purpose of consultations. Accurate recording of the natural heritage interests affected by proposals. Accurate recording of CCW\u0027s response.  Medium confidence: Until April 2010: Recording of licence consultations from the Welsh Assembly Government is not complete for all parts of CCW. The record of designated sites affected by a consultation does not always include all relevant designations. The record of protected species affected by a consultation does not always include all relevant species.  This system was decommissioned and data moved into new NRW systems, such as AMX and CRM. Please contact the Data Custodians for futher information.\t\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.  Reports from the database may be edited to remove information that would reveal the locations of sensitive habitats or species. Individual records may be released with the authority of the D band manager responsible for the content of the record. Data may only be released for the purpose of further analyses with the authority of the casework database manager (Richard Ninnes). Analyses of the data may only be released with the authority of the casework database manager (Richard Ninnes). The database is only accessible directly to NRW staff because it gives access to letters and documents that may contain information in all categories of restricted information.\tRichard Ninnes, North Region Operations Manager, NRW Bryn Jones, Gemma Beyton\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:15:53.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17396\tNRW_DS64578\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.335Z\t590\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSpecies Licence Return Datasets\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353738\tThis dataset was collected for recording and monitoring species which require licences. It is a collation created using licence return forms, and is made up of a number of species including, amphibians and reptiles, dormouse, bats, plants, badgers and otters.\tSpecies data is extracted from returned species licences. Species data could contain sensitive species information and may require a licence. Paper records stored by NRW are all sensitive and restricted. Some of the paper records have been digitised by NRW and put into Recorder 6. This data has been packaged with other species licence return data on Recorder 6 and is available to download via the NBN Gateway (see Parent ID). Some of the data has been digitised by Cofnod, and others remain in hard copy paper format. Some more recent mammal licence return data has been digitised by Cofnod. Cofnod act as data custodian and hold the top copy of this licence return data, not the data held in Recorder 6 which is managed by NRW.\t\tRecorder 6 database Operational Support Group, CCW, Bangor.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas\tDylan Lloyd, Environmental Surveillance Officer, Monitoring \u0026amp; Surveillance Team, NRW possibly should now be Carys Roberts\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:35:00.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17393\tNRW_DS64583\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.285Z\t273\tUnassessed\tEditable\tConsents and Assents held in the Integrated Sites Information System (ISIS) Database\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3634353833\tThis dataset contains information on consents and assents requests received by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and CCW\u0027s responses to the requests.   The purpose of this data capture is to maintain database records for all Section 28 and Regulation 48 Consent and Assent requests received by CCW and CCW\u0027s responses. This dataset is now managed by Natural Resources Wales.\tFormer CCW staff input data on receipt of request and process Consent or Assent. The dataset acts as a collation of potentially damaging operations per Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and provides historical information of activities. It will eventually link to historical Review of Consents data.  This system was decommissioned and data moved into new NRW systems, such as AMX and CRM. Please contact the Data Custodians for futher information.\t\tISIS Database. Electronic. Available to NRW staff over intranet and site files.\tGill Walker, Data Manager (Sites), Operational Support Group, CCW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-10-29T10:17:19.000Z\tMonica Jones\t-\tPrivate record\n17398\tNRW_DS86828\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.312Z\t330\tValid\tEditable\tSurvey of North Wales and Pembrokeshire tide influenced communities\tArolwg o gymunedau dan ddylanwad y llanw yng ngogledd Cymru a Sir Benfro\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3836383238\tAs part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 UNEP Convention on Biological Diversity there have been a number of coordinated initiatives to identify those habitats which may be under threat in the UK, and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity.   The UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) is now one of the main focuses of that work. It provides detailed proposals for a number of species, species groups and habitats requiring urgent conservation action. Tidal Rapids is one of those habitats. Tidal Rapids are a UK Biodiversity Action Plan feature and rocky Reefs are listed as an Annex I feature under the Habitats Directive (1992). These features are of conservation importance and the data collected during this survey is used to help define conservation objectives and inform decisions regarding protection and management.\tMarine biological surveys of seabed habitats and communities in four areas exposed to strong tidal currents around southwest and northwest Wales, were made in summer 2002 and summer 2003.   Survey methods were based on the standard Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) techniques using in-situ identification of conspicuous species and description of habitat and community characteristics.   The survey sites were chosen to represent the range of tide-swept rocky habitats present in each area; particularly concentrating on sites for which where there was no (or very limited) previous survey data. Recent multi-beam survey images aided selection of the sites in the Daugleddau Estuary, Milford Haven and West Pembrokeshire coast.  Subtidal and intertidal components of the fieldwork were carried out separately. Further details of these standard methodologies are given in MNCR Rational and Methods (Hiscock 1996). Each site survey was recorded immediately on standard MNCR recording forms.    Previous surveys have been undertaken in each of the four survey areas, by various parties and include some detail on the tidal rapids and reef systems.  These include surveys carried out by CCW, JNCC, MCS Seasearch and others.\t\t[A] Data is archived as project 79, media 223 and 224 (File paths  K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 223 and K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 224) [B]Species and habitat data held in Marine Recorder (MRCCW80000000002, MRCCW80000000003, MRCCW80000000004, MRCCW80000000005). [C] Paper MNCR forms in off-site storage (Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2004-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T15:46:02.367Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17394\tNRW_DS87090\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.335Z\t603\tUnassessed\tEditable\tAcross Wales Drop-down Video monitoring 2004-2005\tGwaith monitro cwymp-gamerau ar draws Cymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837303930\tDrop-down video surveys of Special Area of Conservation (SACs) across Wales (Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau, Menai Strait and Conwy Bay, Cardigan Bay and Pembrokeshire).  Work carried out by Seastar Survey under contract to Countryside Council of Wales (CCW).  The data was collected to enable site monitoring to support management and reporting of SACs in Wales. The monitoring of subtidal aspects of SACs will indicate whether the desired condition of the feature of interest for which the site was designated is being achieved. The work will also enable judgements to be made about whether the management of the site is appropriate. Analysis of video recordings from the 2005 survey will be used to produce descriptions of the seabed environment including substrata and faunal communities as well as classifying the different sites into biotopes according to Connor et al. (2004). Survey areas within the SACs focused on north Pen Llyn, Bardsey Island, Tremadog Bay and Moelfre in north Wales, areas off Cardigan Island, Cemaes Head and between Aberaeron and Llangranog (off Newquay) within Cardigan Bay, and Hats and Barrels Reef, Bishops and Clerks, Skokholm Island and the Castlemartin ridges in Pembrokeshire.\tDrop down video surveying was conducted at well-defined sites. Surveys were carried out in 2004 and 2005. The 2004 survey was carried over August 23-26, and focused on the Cardigan Bay SAC. 2005 survey work was carried out during the period May to July and focused on the other SACs. Ground truthing was carried out over September 6-10 2004.  Data collection follows trials of the drop-down video monitoring technique (Sanderson et al., 2001; CCW Marine Monitoring report No. 1, CCW Marine Monitoring report No. 15). Future data collection is planned annually, visiting the same sites to form a time-series of comparative data.  In 2010 a subset of videos were re-analysed to provide further habitat and species detail.  In 2012 all video data was reanalysed in April 2012.  Methods and site locations are described in CCW Marine Monitoring Report No. 24a.  A separate report (CCW Marine Monitoring report No, 24b) details exact quality control and standard operating procedures in relation to survey methodologies used. Additional ground truthing was carried out in September 2004 by a team of 6 divers at 22 sites. Species and habitat data entered into Marine Recorder.\t\t[A]  Also maintained on backed-up server based storage at CCW HQ as project no. 35, Media 175.   [B]  Fire-proof storage facilities at CCW HQ on optical media. [C]  Species data held in Marine Recorder [MRCCW31600000002]\tData Custodian:  Marine Monitoring Team, NRW. Metadata point of contact: Master Reference Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-05-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:22:52.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17395\tNRW_DS87091\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.226Z\t376\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSAC Monitoring: Monitoring Great Crested Newts at Johnstown SAC and  Deeside and Buckley SAC (2003)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837303931\tDeeside and Buckley and Johnstown Newt Sites candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC) in Flintshire; and Johnstown Newt Sites cSAC in Wrexham support some of the most important areas for newts (Triturus cristatus) in Great Britain. The main aim of the current project was to provide monitoring information on Great Crested Newts adults and juveniles and on Great Crested Newts  aquatic and terrestrial habitat within the two cSACs.   This was to appraise the condition of great crested newt populations at Johnstown Newt Sites SAC, and Deeside and Buckley Newt Sites SAC and to provide monitoring data on adults and juveniles and on Great Crested Newts  aquatic and terrestrial habitat.  The dataset consists of:  1) A collation of historical Great Crested Newts surveillance data from known display ponds for the two SACs (then candidate SACs).  Records were mainly taken from Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) paper files, but also from Deeside Urban Wildlife Group (DUWG), Flintshire County Council (FCC), and Wrexham County Borough Council (WCBC).  Individual CCW recorders names are not known. 2) 2003 monitoring data for presence of Great Crested Newts (Jun), and larvae/adults (Aug-Sept).\tThe following methodologies were used. 1) Historical data: Mainly torch surveys for adults carried out during the breeding season, but in some cases they include records for larvae as well as additional information such as the presence of fish. 2) a) 2003 monitoring (initial): All ponds visited once in June, to carry out visual inspection for Great Crested Newt eggs on laying substrates near shore. Other bird and fish species noted. 2) b) 2003 monitoring (main assessment): All ponds visited at least once, in most cases twice, during August and September, to record presence of larvae/efts using visual observation, bottle trapping, torch surveying and netting. Environmental conditions within pond and other waterfowl and fish species also noted.    1) Historical data: There was a wide variation in the format and level of detail in the original historical records, and this is reflected in the data collated.  Some surveys grouped the results by site, so that it is not possible to distinguish the records for any one particular pond. The lack of a systematic pond numbering system for some sites also meant that it was sometimes difficult to relate records to particular ponds, although most surveys used the new alphanumeric pond references.  Wherever possible clarification of records through consultation with CCW and partner organisations was sought. 2) 2003 monitoring: Eggs were only found in a small number of ponds, partly because most would have hatched by the time of the survey.\tSpecies Team Leader and Herpetologist, Terrestrial Science Group, NRW. Assistant Monitoring Ecologist, Environmental Change Group, NRW. Full details are available in CCW Environmental Monitoring Report 1: \\An appraisal of the condition of the great crested newt populations of Johnstown newt site cSAC and Deeside and Buckley newt site cSAC\\\".\"\t[A]  Associated data is held within the Recorder 6 database. [B]  Data is also held on the H:/ at H:\\Science\\Operational Support\\Monitoring Team_ECN\\Current Projects\\Environmental Monitoring Reports \u0027ampisand\u0027 Datasets\\01 Great crested newt at Johnstown, Deeside \u0026amp; Buckley\t\tFflintshire County Council \u003d Cyngor Sir y Fflint-Wrexham County Borough-Deeside Urban Wildlife Group-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:24:44.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17392\tNRW_DS87108\t2024-05-28T12:22:32.002Z\t319\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGround truthing towed video images from North Pen Llyn horse mussel (Modiolus modiolus) reef\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313038\tThe Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau Special Area of Conservation (SAC) was initially selected for two of the Annex II habitat types listed in the Habitats Directive - reefs and estuaries. Monitoring trials were developed in relation to these two features. During the late 1990\u0027s there was a dynamic period where the development of marine monitoring and surveillance under the Habitats and Species Directive was undertaken. The presence of the horse mussel Modiolus modiolus off the north Pen Llyn is thought to have been known since the early 19th Century. The extent of the biogenic reef feature was considered an important attribute to monitor as part of the Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau SAC. This dataset consists of digital footage of the horse mussel (Modiolus modiolus) reef collected in 1999. Data was collected to validate the results of an acoustic monitoring survey of the Modiolus modiolus reefs present within the SAC.\tA ground truthed RoxAnn(TM) was used to confirm the presence of Modiolus modiolus reef features see on the side scan sonar images. In 2010 a subset of videos were re-analysed to provide further habitat and species detail.\tSee section 3.3.1  Sanderson, WG. Bennell, J. Rees, EIS, Holt, RHF. 2001. Extent of north Pen Llyn Modiolus modiolus reef - the potential use of acoustic methods. within CCW Marine Monitoring Report No. 1.\tFire-proof storage facilities at NRW, Maes y Fynnon, on optical media.  Also maintained on backed-up server based storage as Media 280-282.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team, NRW  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:28:49.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17391\tNRW_DS87114\t2024-05-28T12:22:31.952Z\t345\tUnassessed\tEditable\tDrop-down Video Footage and Biotope Identification Data 1999\tFideo Tanddwr a Data Adnabod Biotopau 1999\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313134\tThe purpose of this data capture was to create a dataset to help with an evaluation of the deployment of drop-down video for identifying biotopes. Footage collected as an assessment of using drop-down video as part of a site-orientated monitoring technique.  Data was gathered as a result of the development and trial of a remote drop-down video-based monitoring method to measure biotope richness. Data was collected from two sites that presented sufficiently different physical conditions and biological conditions- north Pen Llyn reefs, and Sarn Badrig reef.\tThe method development and trialling carried out during this survey was a precursor to the across Wales drop-down video monitoring work undertaken in 2004-05. The video was subsequently re-analysed in April 2012 by CALM and the data held in Marine Recorder replaced. Drop-down digital video-based monitoring gear, deployed from a 7.5m aluminium RIB. Species and habitat data entered into Marine Recorder. Data collected for primarily for method validation purposes.  See sections 2.1.3 and 3.1.2 of the Marine Monitoring Report.  Analysis of the video for it\u0027s biological and physical attributes was undertaken by Aquascan Ltd in 1999.  The video was subsequently re-analysed in April 2012 by CALM and the data held in Marine Recorder replaced.\t\ta) Fire-proof storage facilities at NRW Maes-y-Ffynnon on optical media. Also maintained on backed-up server based storage as project 56 and 351, Media Nos 275-278, 290-293, 307, 1044-1056,1094,1100,1329 b) Sanderson et al., (Eds.) 2001, section 2.1.2, and data appendix. See related titles field. c) Marine Recorder [MRCCW31600000003] d) Original species and habitats data held in Marine Recorder mdb file in the archivee) Paper MNCR Survey forms held in NRW off-site storage\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team, NRW Metadata Point of contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:23:31.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17449\tNRW_DS87115\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.211Z\t709\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMenai Strait turbidity and other co-variate surveillance\tGwylio cymylogrwydd a chydamrywiannau eraill yn y Fenai\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313135\tThe Menai Strait, separating the Isle of Anglesey from the north coast of Wales is part of the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay Special Area of Conservation. Monitoring of physical parameters in the strait, including water turbidity, has been carried out on a sporadic basis for the past 40 years. These parameters are of importance to the biological communities for which the Menai Strait and Conwy Bay has been designated and help to inform assessments of the condition of those communities and the effectiveness of management measures to conserve them. The observations of physical parameters in the strait have shown large, long-term fluctuations in turbidity. The aim of this project was to establish an automatic system for continuous monitoring of water turbidity, and other relevant physical parameters including temperature, salinity and meteorological variables.\tUsing an automated system for continuous monitoring, water turbidity, temperature and salinity were measured, along with meteorological variables (wind speed and direction, wind gust speed, atmospheric pressure, air temperature, rainfall). A Conductivity Temperature Depth (CTD) sensor, fitted with Optical Backscatter (OBS) sensor measuring backscattered light at 880nm was used for oceanographic measurements. Weather stations in Menai Bridge and Caernarfon airport provided meteorological data. CTD monitoring was carried out at one fixed site at the Low Water Springs mark off the end of the rock jetty on Ynys Faelog in the Menai Strait. Meteorological data came from weather stations in Menai Bridge and Caernarfon airport. Automatically collected data was logged every 5 minutes. The recorded observations were collected from 2003-2005. Instrument calibration data is included, and described in detail in CCW Marine Monitoring Report No. 22. Secchi depth measurements have been made sporadically in the strait for the last 40 years (Lumb, 1989,1990; Birkett and Maggs, 2001, Kratzer et al., 2003). These show that there was a steady decrease in water clarity in the strait between the early 1960\u0027s and the early 1980\u0027s, but that since then, clarity has been improving so that Secchi depths are more or less back to the levels they were 40 years ago.\t\tData is archived as project no. 7 and 183, and media no. 132, 231 and 524 (File paths are K:\\Archive\\Media 101to200\\Media 132, K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\231 and K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 524)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata Contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:25:36.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17444\tNRW_DS87116\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.274Z\t824\tValid\tEditable\tPembrokeshire Marine / Sir Benfro Forol Special Area of Conservation (SAC) Grey Seal (Halichoerus grypus) Monitoring 2005\tMonitro morloi llwyd Ardal Gadwraeth Arbennig mor Sir Benfro 2005\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313136\tThe Habitats Directive establishes that the management of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) should aim to achieve the favourable conservation status of habitat and species features listed within its Annex I and Annex II.   For SACs in Wales, the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), now part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW), is therefore required to report on a regular basis on whether features are in favourable conservation status. Grey seals (Halichoerus grypus) are among the rarest seals in the world: the UK population represents about 40 percent of the world population and 95 percent of the EU population. Pembrokeshire Marine / Sir Benfro Forol SAC is home to one of the largest populations of grey seals around the west coast of the UK.   The purpose of this data capture was to record, document and report indicators of the condition of grey seals within the SAC. In addition to this, monitoring is also used to document and report on the impact of human activities, assessment of supporting habitat, and used in the estimations related to the population and structure (age and sex).  The data collected will contribute to an estimate of whole site (SAC) production using indices developed following the 1992-94 seal census.\tNatal sites were scanned thoroughly and systematically using binoculars (10x40 or 8x 40) from the standard observation points. At each monitoring site, abundance, estimated age, sex and behavioural categories were recorded. Environmental conditions were recorded, including date, time, wind, cloud cover and sea conditions (sea state and swell).  Recent wind, sea and tidal conditions were also recorded as field notes. The 1992-94 West Wales seal Census established a \u0027population\u0027 baseline for the area between Caldey Island in the southeast and Aberystwyth in mid Cardigan Bay.  The pupping data for Skomer Island is the longest running dataset for the area (beginning 1974, then annually since 1983).  The data set for the Skomer MNR as a whole runs from 1991 to the present.   This project will continue the work previously carried out in Pembrokeshire by Strong (1995, 1996, 1998) in an effort to be comparable to the previous work and concurrently address monitoring objectives. Images collected at sites within and outside the key study area, using pelage photography techniques, will be used to evaluate seal movements, abundance estimates and distribution of adults in future work.\t\tStored as Project No. 60, media 355\tInternal Custodians:  Marine Vertebrate Ecologist, NRW Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2006-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:18:39.670Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17434\tNRW_DS87117\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.191Z\t305\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSub-surface towed video footage off North Pen Llyn, and subsequent biotope identification data\tLluniau fideo wedi\u0027u tynnu o dan yr wyneb oddi ar ogledd Pen Llyn, a data adnabod biotopau yn sgil hynny\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313137\tThis dataset is a collation of around 5 hours of digital video footage. Positional and other associated data are given in Sanderson et al., (Eds.) 2001 (CCW Marine Monitoring Report No.1, section 2.1.3, and data appendix). The purpose of the data capture was to create an evaluation of the deployment of towed video for monitoring biotope richness. Footage collected as an assessment of using towed video as part of a site-orientated monitoring technique. Following the increased commercial availability of small digital video cameras and digital video editing software, data was collected to evaluate the use of these systems in marine monitoring studies. Biotope richness (ie. The number of biotopes present, rather than species diversity within a biotope), was assessed from the recorded video.\tTowed sled video-based monitoring method, as described in Connor et al. (1997). Biotopes described in Connor et al., (1997) were identified following reviews of recorded video. In 2010 a subset of videos were re-analysed to provide further habitat and species detail.\t\tData is archived under project no. 58, media no.\u0027s 279,283-287,294,300-309,543\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW.  Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:26:21.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17450\tNRW_DS87120\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.420Z\t395\tValid\tEditable\tNon Native Species Data - Sargassum muticum around Wales\tData crynodeb sargaswm\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313230\tThe purpose of this data capture was to collate and monitor the spread of non-native species in Wales. This survey contains a series of records of the non-native marine alga, Sargassum muticum, around Wales. Many of the records were obtained during the intertidal phase 1 survey, while others are anecdotal records from both Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) staff and various other recorders surveying the shoreline for both work or recreationally. The dataset has been fully validated.  All grid references have been cross-referenced against site name.  The majority of records were recorded by specialists.  The data was collated in order to monitor the spread of non-native species in Wales.\tField observations and incidental records taken as part of the CCW Phase 1 Intertidal Survey. Where records were taken for specialised habitats, such as rock pools, these were recorded as part of a timed count.  This dataset is maintained and added to as an excel spreadsheet by marine staff within Natural Resources Wales. A GIS layer has also been generated from the excel spreadsheet and the majority of records added to Marine Recorder (duplicate or restricted records are not added). When updates are added a copy is archived under media no. 230.\t\t[A]  Data is archived under Project No. 151, media no\u0027s. 230 and 485 (File paths Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media201to300\\Media230 and Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media401to500\\Media485  [B]  Species Data is held in Marine Recorder: 1998-ongoing CCW Sargassum muticum records for Wales MRCCW30000000047\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Master Reference Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2015-11-18\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-12T16:20:25.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17446\tNRW_DS87128\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.275Z\t697\tValid\tEditable\tMenai Strait tidally exposed seabed and shores (2002)\tGwely\u0027r mor a\u0027r glannau a ddinoethir gan y llanw yn y Fenai (2002)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313238\tAs part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 UNEP Convention on Biological Diversity there have been a number of coordinated initiatives to identify those habitats which may be under threat in the UK, and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity.   The UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) is now one of the main focuses of that work. It provides detailed proposals for a number of species, species groups and habitats requiring urgent conservation action.   In addition to this the Habitats Directive (1992) requires the establishment of a European network of important high-quality conservation sites, Special Areas of Conservation (SACs).  The Habitats Directive identifies Reefs as a major category of habitats that should be considered for inclusion in SACs. Tidal rapids not only forms part of the reef environments classification but is also highlighted as an important site under the UKBAP habitats list. Tidal rapids is used to describe a broad range of high energy environments including deep tidal streams and tide-swept habitats. JNCC’s Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) defined rapids as ‘strong tidal streams resulting from a constriction in the coastline at the entrance to, or within the length of, an enclosed body of water such as a sea loch. Depth is usually shallower than five metres.    The data collected during this survey is being used to help define the conservation objectives for the cSAC reef features in Wales and to inform management of this feature.  One of the actions listed in the Habitat Action Plan for tidal rapids is to, complete survey and recording of the extent, quality and composition of the habitats and communities of tidal rapids.\tSurvey methods were based on the standard Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) techniques using in-situ identification of conspicuous species and description of habitat and community characteristics. Subtidal and intertidal components of the fieldwork were carried out separately. Survey data was recorded immediately on standard MNCR recording forms.   Confidence in this dataset is high because it was undertaken by experienced surveyors.   Previous surveys have been undertaken in this areas, by various parties and include some detail of the \u0027Tidal Rapids\u0027 and reef systems.  These include surveys carried out by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), JNCC, Seasearch and various others.\t\t[A] Data is archived under Project No. 79, media No\u0027s 223 and 224 (File paths are  K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 223 and K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 223 [B] Species data stored in Marine Recorder v5 (Survey number: MRCCW80000000002) [C] Paper MNCR forms in off-site storage (Box CCW/MNCR/3)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2004-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T17:11:42.912Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17441\tNRW_DS87129\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.207Z\t547\tValid\tEditable\tMilford Haven and Daugleddau Estuary Tidally Exposed Seabed and Shores (2002)\tGwely\u0027r mor a\u0027r glannau a ddinoethir gan y llanw yn Aberdaugleddau (2002)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313239\tAs part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 UNEP Convention on Biological Diversity there have been a number of coordinated initiatives to identify those habitats which may be under threat in the UK, and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity. The UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) is now one of the main focuses of that work. It provides detailed proposals for a number of species, species groups and habitats requiring urgent conservation action. In addition to this the Habitats Directive (1992) requires the establishment of a European network of important high-quality conservation sites, Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). The Habitats Directive identifies Reefs as a major category of habitats that should be considered for inclusion in SACs. Tidal rapids not only forms part of the reef environments classification but is also highlighted as an important site under the UKBAP habitats list.   Tidal rapids is used to describe a broad range of high energy environments including deep tidal streams and tide-swept habitats. JNCC’s Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) defined rapids as ‘strong tidal streams resulting from a constriction in the coastline at the entrance to, or within the length of, an enclosed body of water such as a sea loch. Depth is usually shallower than five metres. The data collected during this survey is being used to help define the conservation objectives for the cSAC reef features in Wales and to inform management of this feature. One of the actions listed in the Habitat Action Plan for tidal rapids is to, complete survey and recording of the extent, quality and composition of the habitats and communities of tidal rapids.\tMarine biological surveys of seabed habitats and communities in four areas exposed to strong tidal currents around southwest and northwest Wales, were made in summer 2002 and summer 2003. Survey methods were based on the standard Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) techniques using in-situ identification of conspicuous species and description of habitat and community characteristics.   The survey sites were chosen to represent the range of tide-swept rocky habitats present in each area; particularly concentrating on sites for which where there was no (or very limited) previous survey data. Recent multi-beam survey images aided selection of the sites in the Daugleddau Estuary, Milford Haven and West Pembrokeshire coast.  Subtidal and intertidal components of the fieldwork were carried out separately. Further details of these standard methodologies are given in MNCR Rational and Methods (Hiscock 1996). Each site survey was recorded immediately on standard MNCR recording forms. Previous surveys have been undertaken in this areas, by various parties and include some detail of the \u0027Tidal Rapids\u0027 and reef systems.  These include surveys carried out by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW), JNCC, Seasearch and various others.\t\t[A] Data is archived under Project No. 79, media No\u0027s 223 and 224    Marine Recorder survey: MRCCW80000000003  Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media201to300\\Media223 Z:\\Archive\\Marine Monitoring Archive\\Archive\\Media201to300\\Media224\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T15:17:18.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17445\tNRW_DS87132\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.271Z\t611\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNorth West Anglesey tidally exposed seabed and shores (2003-2004)\tGwely\u0027r mor a\u0027r glannau a ddinoethir gan y llanw yng ngogledd-orllewin Mon (2003-2004)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313332\tAs part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 UNEP Convention on Biological Diversity there have been a number of coordinated initiatives to identify those habitats which may be under threat in the UK, and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity. The UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) is now one of the main focuses of that work. It provides detailed proposals for a number of species, species groups and habitats requiring urgent conservation action. In addition to this the Habitats Directive (1992) requires the establishment of a European network of important high-quality conservation sites, Special Areas of Conservation (SACs). The Habitats Directive identifies Reefs as a major category of habitats that should be considered for inclusion in SACs. Tidal rapids not only forms part of the reef environments classification but is also highlighted as an important site under the UKBAP habitats list. Tidal rapids is used to describe a broad range of high energy environments including deep tidal streams and tide-swept habitats. JNCC’s Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) defined rapids as ‘strong tidal streams resulting from a constriction in the coastline at the entrance to, or within the length of, an enclosed body of water such as a sea loch. Depth is usually shallower than five metres. The purpose of this data capture was to assess the features in North West Anglesey to help inform and define conservation objective and decisions regarding their protection and management.\tMarine biological surveys of seabed habitats and communities in four areas exposed to strong tidal currents around southwest and northwest Wales, were made in summer 2002 and summer 2003. Survey methods were based on the standard Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) techniques using in-situ identification of conspicuous species and description of habitat and community characteristics. The survey sites were chosen to represent the range of tide-swept rocky habitats present in each area; particularly concentrating on sites for where there was no (or very limited) previous survey data. Recent multi-beam survey images aided selection of the sites in the Daugleddau Estuary, Milford Haven and West Pembrokeshire coast. Subtidal and intertidal components of the fieldwork were carried out separately. Further details of these standard methodologies are given in MNCR Rational and Methods.  Each site survey was recorded immediately on standard MNCR recording forms.  Confidence in this dataset is high. Previous surveys have been undertaken in this area, by various parties and include some detail of the \u0027Tidal Rapids\u0027 and reef systems. These include surveys carried out by JNCC, some Seasearch surveys and a handful of other smaller surveys.\t\t[A] Data is archived under Project No. 79, media No.\u0027s 223 and 224 (K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 223 and K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 224) [B] Species data are in Marine Recorder 5 (MRCCW80000000004)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:27:01.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17447\tNRW_DS87133\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.278Z\t685\tValid\tEditable\tWest Pembrokeshire tidally exposed seabed and shores (2003)\tGwely\u0027r mor a\u0027r glannau a ddinoethir gan y llanw yng ngorllewin Sir Benfro (2003)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313333\tAs part of the UK\u0027s commitment to the 1992 UNEP Convention on Biological Diversity there have been a number of coordinated initiatives to identify those habitats which may be under threat in the UK, and to establish plans to reduce those threats and thereby maintain biodiversity. The UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) is now one of the main focuses of that work. It provides detailed proposals for a number of species, species groups and habitats requiring urgent conservation action. Tidal Rapids is one of those habitats.    Tidal Rapids are a UK Biodiversity Action Plan feature and rocky Reefs are listed as an Annex I feature under the Habitats Directive (1992). These features are of conservation importance and the data collected during this survey is used to help define conservation objectives and inform decisions regarding protection and management.\tMarine biological surveys of seabed habitats and communities in four areas exposed to strong tidal currents around southwest and northwest Wales, were made in summer 2002 and summer 2003. Survey methods were based on the standard Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) techniques using in-situ identification of conspicuous species and description of habitat and community characteristics.   The survey sites were chosen to represent the range of tide-swept rocky habitats present in each area; particularly concentrating on sites for which where there was no (or very limited) previous survey data. Recent multi-beam survey images aided selection of the sites in the Daugleddau Estuary, Milford Haven and West Pembrokeshire coast. Subtidal and intertidal components of the fieldwork were carried out separately.   Further details of these standard methodologies are given in MNCR Rational and Methods. Each site survey was recorded immediately on standard MNCR recording forms.  Previous surveys have been undertaken this areas, by various parties and include some detail of the \u0027Tidal Rapids\u0027 and reef systems. These include surveys carried out by CCW, JNCC, some Seasearch surveys and other surveys. However, the previously available data are limited and do not fully describe the range of rocky tide-swept features present in each area.\t\t[A] Data is archived as Project No. 79, media No.\u0027s 223 and 224 (File paths: K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 223 and K:\\Archive\\Media 201to300\\Media 224) [B] Raw biological data in Marine recorder survey MRCCW80000000005\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2004-03-05\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T15:24:41.168Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17440\tNRW_DS87141\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.212Z\t329\tValid\tEditable\tResults of a study into the use of airborne remote sensing to identify habitats and biotopes in the Mawddach estuary\tPosibilrwydd defnyddio synhwyro pell o\u0027r awyr i ganfod cynefinoedd a biotopau yn aber Mawddach\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313431\tThis dataset contains the results of a study into the use of airborne remote sensing to produce a comprehensive thematic map of biotope and sediment type distribution in the Mawddach estuary. The purpose of this data capture was to be able to report on the condition of the Mawddach estuary a number of provisional attributes have been identified which may require monitoring e.g. the relative proportion of sandy and muddy biotopes and distribution of major communities.  This project investigated the possibility of using remotely sensed data to provide a contextual overview of broad-scale attributes in a monitoring programme.\tThe survey was carried out over an 80 minute period. Initial surveying was carried out at 2000 feet to obtain a swath of imagery covering the entire width of the estuary. This was followed by a 1000 feet survey along the predetermined flight-lines. True colour video, infra-red video and hyperspectral data were recorded during the entire survey and recording was synchronised to GPS time for all video data. All data acquired during the survey were recorded on analogue video tapes, these required digitising before processing. Data capture operations produced 1) raw colour and infra-red image frames, 2) raw hyperspectral reflectance data, 3) Georeferenced database record of flight line. Processing of this imagery included geometric, radiometric and bi-directional reflectance corrections in order to produce a thematic map of biotope/ sediment/ vegetation distribution.\t\tData is archived as Project No. 82, Media No.\u0027s 165 and 272.  Data is also held on backed-up server based storage.\tInternal Custodian: Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW. Metadata contact: Data Services, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2001-04-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T15:25:46.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17433\tNRW_DS87152\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.165Z\t745\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMaritime Supralittoral Lichen Survey of the Portmeirion Promontory, Morfa Harlech National Nature Reserve (NNR), Site of Special Sceintific Importance (SSSI), and Candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC)\tArolwg o Gennau Uwch-lanwol Arforol Pentir Portmeirion, yACA Pen Llyn a\u0027r Sarnau/SoDdGA/GNG Morfa Harlech\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313532\tThis dataset is made up of the results of a supralittoral lichen survey on coastal rocks around the Portmeirion headland within the Morfa Harlech. Morfa Harlech is designated as a National Nature Reserve (NNR), Site of Special Sceintific Importance (SSSI), and Candidate Special Area of Conservation (cSAC). The development and diversity of the supralittoral lichen communities is this area is one of great importance and is of conservation significance in this context. The purpose of this data capture was to produce an inventory of supralittoral lichen species, highlighting and localising any noteworthy taxa and assemblages.\tObservations were made by experienced surveyors following standard methodology. The coastline was systematically walked and stops made at promising localities. The survey focussed on supralittoral lichen communities, i.e. those occurring at and above mean high water (MHW). Frequently submerged lower shore eu-littoral lichen communities, dominated by filmy Verrucaria species (‘oil lichens’), were not examined in any detail. The upper survey boundary was set at the terrestrial – xeric-supralittoral interface. In practice, because of the low elevation of the shore exposures, this was where cliff-top rock terminated and terrestrial woody and coastal scrub vegetation commenced. An exception was made for one essentially terrestrial dry dwarf shrub heath formation, with coastal character, on the cliff-top a short distance inland from Trwynypenrhyn, which supported an interesting mix of hard upland acid and coastal rock species, including the rare Pertusaria monogona. Notable species and assemblages were logged in the field with a Garmin 12-channel Global Positioning System (GPS) unit, delivering grid references accurate to 1-15m (maximum ten-figure resolution), the performance depending on satellite reception and topographic shadowing. GPS locations form spatial reference points for the location target notes as detailed below.\t\tData is held as Project No.83, Media 358.\tCustodian: Intertidal Team Leader, NRW Metadata contact: Data Services Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-11-30\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:27:43.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17448\tNRW_DS87182\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.274Z\t552\tUnassessed\tEditable\tSensitivity and mapping of inshore marine biotopes in the southern Irish Sea (SensMap): Final report\tSensitifrwydd a mapio biotopau morol y glannau yn rhan ddeheuol Mor Iwerddon (SensMap): Adroddiad terfynol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313832\tThe SensMap project, funded under the INTERREG programme involved survey and mapping of biotopes in the marine intertidal and subtidal in both Wales and Ireland. The project partners were the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and Ecological Consultancy Services Ltd (EcoServe) and the National Parks and Wildlife Service (NPWS) in Ireland now Duchas, the Heritage Service.  This project had three main objectives:  1. To develop an integrated methodology for mapping in the marine intertidal and subtidal which can link both marine and terrestrial maps.  2. To develop a protocol for determining the sensitivity of marine biotopes according to the sensitivity of marine species to a range of natural and man-induced changes including, changes to water quality, oil pollution, fishing and recreational activities, and in the longer term, climate change. 3. To develop a model for a decision-making support system based on sensitivity mapping.\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 biotope mapping was developed by the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and partners over the last four years and this methodology was reassessed, adapted and transferred to Ireland through collaborative fieldwork (Emblow et al., 1998; SensMap Handbook for Marine Intertidal Phase 1 Survey and Mapping). For intertidal mapping Ordnance Survey National Grid basemaps are used whereas for subtidal, information is mapped on to Hydrographic Office Admiralty Charts. Intertidal survey is more detailed and information is gathered at a higher resolution than broadscale subtidal or terrestrial Phase 1 mapping techniques. This is because the intertidal is logistically easier to access than the subtidal benthos. Conversely, because it is more difficult to access intertidal than terrestrial habitats, greater detail is recorded in intertidal Phase 1 compared with the terrestrial equivalent to make best use of time spent in a single visit. Marine intertidal biotope mapping is based on the use of aerial photographs and/or Ordnance Survey maps to map the distribution and extent of Marine Nature Conservation Review (MNCR) biotopes (Connor et al., 1997a and b).\t\tData is archived as Project No. 181, media No\u0027s. 359 and 521 (File paths: K:\\Archive\\Media 301to400\\Media 359 and K:\\Archive\\Media 501to600\\Media 521)\tInternal Custodian: Kirsten Ramsay, Team Leader, Marine \u0026amp; Coastal Ecosystems Team  Metadata Contact: Data Service Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2000-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-10T16:28:22.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17439\tNRW_DS87189\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.175Z\t700\tValid\tEditable\tFood sources for Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra) in Carmarthen Bay\tFfynonellau bwyd i\u0027r for - hwyaden ddu ym Mae Caerfyrddin\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837313839\tIn the UK the Common Scoter (Melanitta nigra) is recognised as a nationally threatened species due to its small and declining breeding population. Carmarthen Bay is an internationally important wintering ground for the Common Scoter duck (Melanitta nigra) and is widely regarded as the most important such site in the UK causing the site to be proposed as a Special Protection Area (pSPA) under the EC Birds Directive.   Carmarthen Bay is considered to support peak numbers of 17000 - 24000 birds. The Common Scoter is strongly migratory and often travels considerable distances over land making brief stop-overs on inland waters. Its diet consists predominantly of molluscs, especially during the winter, although it occasionally takes other aquatic invertebrates such as crustaceans (e.g. barnacles and shrimps), worms, echinoderms, isopods, amphipods and insects (e.g. midges and caddis flies) as well as small fish and fish eggs.   An extensive macrofauna sampling survey was undertaken to coincide with an aerial scoter survey. Conservation concerns for the scoter population focus on threats of hunting pressure, breeding habitat disturbance and loss, food chain contaminants, oil spills and the threat to non-breeding habitats represented by offshore developments.   The aim of this project was to investigate the biological and physical factors affecting the winter distribution of common scoter in Carmarthen Bay and to identify what component of the benthos they feed upon.  The identification of such components is crucial to the future management and monitoring of the Carmarthen Bay Special Protection Area and, to some extent, the Special Area of Conservation.\tGuided by a previous aerial scoter survey on the 25 January 2004, a stratified random sampling strategy was used to sample the benthos in 50 2x2 km grid cells using a Day Grab. In addition a series (8) of 2m beam trawls and 6 intertidal samples were taken to sample potential epifaunal and lower intertidal prey respectively. Biological data were recorded on a suite of epifauna, bivalve and macroinfauna biomass, shell length  and bivalve abundance (count) and individual bivalve taxa abundance.  An aerial survey was undertaken on the 28th February, 2004.\tPrevious work on scoters in the Carmarthen Bay include; Woolmer (2003) report and data from Unpublished PhD Thesis - Media 207;  ABP (2000) Data from Bristol Channel marine Aggregates: Resources and constraints research project (Wales Assembly Government Report)- Media 207; Woolmer et al (2001) Two PhD studentships investigated the benthos and Scoter populations of Carmarthen Bay, in response to concerns stimulated by the consequences of the Sea Empress oil spill of February 1996- Media 221; Smyth (2003) Unpublished MSc Thesis-Media 227.\tData and Report are archived under Project No. 1, Media 222\tInternal Custodian Marine Monitoring Team Leader, NRW Metadata Contact: Data Services.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T15:27:57.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17437\tNRW_DS87460\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.175Z\t396\tUnassessed\tEditable\tThreatened Bryophyte Database (TBDB) UK (1965 - 2005)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3837343630\tThis metadata is derived from metadata created by British Bryological Society for the NBN gateway. The purpose of this data capture is to service bryophyte conservation by documenting detailed records of threatened species, and tracking the populations of these species through time. It is a \u0027live\u0027 database feeding specialist information to UKBAP Lead Partners and conservation agencies, and to feed information about bryophyte conservation programmes to bryologists. Some biological records have more precise location information than others. Often they are recorded using national grid references, which identify which 10km, 2km, 1km or 100m square they occur in. This table shows how many records there are at these resolutions for this dataset.\tThis dataset includes all records provided by Alan Hale from the Threatened Bryophytes in Wales dataset.  These were re-sourced from their original reference rather than simply copied from the Threatened Bryophytes in Wales dataset.  The data was then supplied back to CCW in Recorder format. An export of Welsh data was created which was added to the original Welsh dataset and made into a MapInfo table.  This now contains numerous duplicates.  The most up to date and accurate data is available to download via the British Bryological Society, who publish this data on the NBN Gateway.\t\tMaster Copy held by British Bryological Society.   Recorder 6 holds some species data: Tycanol: RF36 Bryophyte records - BBS database SR0006010000000C.   An export of welsh data held as MapInfo file on m drive.  NB Update from Sam Bosanquet 2021 - The Threatened Bryophyte Database is basically defunct now - all data for Threatened Bryophytes are stored along with those for all other bryophytes by the British Bryological Society and there is no point in NRW storing them separately as that just leads to duplication and errors.\tInternal Custodian: Sam Bosanquet, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team External Custodian: British Bryological Society\tBritish Bryological Society\t\t2005-08-21\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17436\tNRW_DS88070\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.164Z\t547\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBat Roosts Database - North East Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3838303730\tThis dataset is the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) regional database of bat roosts for all species of bats found in North East Wales.  This includes the site name, grid reference, species, date and abundance where available. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts, to assist CCW\u0027s conservation work. In particular, to provide information when dealing with development proposals, to support the monitoring and notification of SSSIs, and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species.\tThis dataset covers the North East Wales, including Flint, Denbighshire, Conway, Wrexham with some occasional records in Powys and Gwynedd. Data was generated from visits to roosts by CCW staff and volunteers following a request, from enquiries from members of the public received by CCW, and/or from specialist groups. The data was collected and entered into MapInfo by regional staff. Data was subsequently validated and transferred to Recorder 2002. All species names and dates have been validated during transfer process to Recorder 2002.  Data recorder names are not stored electronically, although these could be found within the paper files. Since then this data and custodianship of the data has been given to Cofnod to manage as a top copy for NRW. The data ownership remains with NRW, and Cofnod have amalgamated this with GWYBATs for produce the Natural Resources Wales North Wales Bat Roosts dataset available on the NBN Gateway. See the Parent ID for more information and data download link.\t\tThis dataset is held and managed by Cofnod. They hold the top copy of the dataset and therefore requests should go directly to them, via the NBN Gateway.  Historically: CCW data was stored in Recorder on the Mold server Rec2002 - Mold. MapInfo layer held on m:/species/North East/Mamamals/Bats/AllBats_NEWales_validated.tab.   Additional MapInfo layers are available for each of the species within the same folder.\tMatthew Ellis - see Distributor above for contact details. External Contact: Aisling May  - Rheolwr Data/Data Manager       aisling.may@cofnod.org.uk Cofnod, Intec, Ffordd y Parc, Parc Menai, Bangor, Gwynedd. LL57 4FG.  (01248) 672603\tVarious including volunteer recorders-County bat groups-Natural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:48:42.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17431\tNRW_DS88158\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.055Z\t466\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBat Roosts Database - Montgomeryshire\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3838313538\tThis dataset contains records for all species of bats recorded in roosts within Montgomeryshire. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts in order to assist Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRWs)  conservation work. In particular, to provide information when dealing with development proposals, to support the monitoring and notification of SSSIs, and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species. This dataset contains records for all species of bats recorded in roosts within Montgomeryshire.  The data also indicates the abundance of each species at each location. Data was extracted from files as part of an exercise carried out by the Local Record Centre on behalf of former Countryside Council for Wales.  A top copy of this data is held by the Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park Biodiversity Information Service (BIS) Local Record Centre. An older copy of the data is held on NRW\u0027s copy of Recorder 6 under CCW Montgomeryshire General Bat Files WA0001500000001O however this has not been updated since 2003.\tThis datasets has been obtained from former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 Regional site files and visits to bat roosts by Countryside Council for Wales\u0027  staff and volunteers.  The data has been collated in paper format and transferred to Recorder 6 under agreement with the Local Record Centres, Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park Biodiversity Information Service (BIS) .  An electronic copy was then provided back to Natural Resources Wales\u0027 to be held at the local office (Newtown). The dataset has been validated, although one or two outliers do still exist in the dataset.  A top copy of the data is held by Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park Biodiversity Information Service (BIS) and is also available through the NBN Gateway.\tFor additional information about this dataset Contact the Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park Biodiversity Information Service (BIS) Local Record Centre who hold the top copy of this dataset.\t[A]  Data is archived as Project No. 175, Media 509  [B]  Copy also held in the Newtown Office database CCW Montgomeryshire General Bat Files WA0001500000001O    [C]  A Master copy held at Powys and Brecon Beacons National Park Biodiversity Information Service (BIS) Local Environmental Records Center\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-07-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-27T13:30:30.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17442\tNRW_DS88241\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.213Z\t397\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFreshwater Site Visits and Ad-hoc Sightings\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3838323431\tRecords of species and habitat ad hoc sightings collected in the freshwater environment.  This includes, but it not limited to aquatic plants, amphibians and certain easily identified invertebrates.  Data is collected in order to assist with targeted monitoring and site management of freshwater environments across Wales.  Data collection will be on-going during the monitoring and other survey work.  This dataset comprises of a number of surveys entered into Recorder 6 which have been packaged for dissemination onto the NBN Gateway.\tThis dataset consists of ad hoc sightings data collected during structured site visits. Recording is mainly focused on recording plant species using the standard grapnel sampling methodology from a boat.  Additional species are collected as incidental or ad-hoc records during the survey. There is good confidence in this data as species identification was made in the field with any rare or uncertain species verified in the lab or sent to a recognised expert for verification.\t\t[A]  Species Data held in Recorder 6: -  Freshwater Sites - Occasional Reconnaissance Surveys - CCW CCW0002800000001    -  Freshwater ad-hoc sightings collected by CCW staff - CCW CCW0002800000003      This data is now available on the NBN Atlas This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the 17/08/2016 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).\tTristan Hatton Ellis, Team Leader of Freshwater Ecosystems, Marine and Freshwater Ecosystems Group, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2016-08-17\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:21.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17435\tNRW_DS88663\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.192Z\t291\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBat Roosts Database - Pembrokeshire (1887 - 2011)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3838363633\tThis dataset comprises of former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 regional bat hibernacula and day/night roost records for Pembrokeshire. The purpose of this data capture was to record species distribution and details of bat roosts to assist with the former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW) bat conservation work. In particular, to provide information when dealing with development proposals, to support the monitoring and notification of Sites of Special Scientific Interest, and to aid with the monitoring and reporting on the status and trends of protected bat species across Pembrokeshire. Ad hoc survey work is continuing however the main data was collected between 1887 and 2011.\tThe data are derived from bat records sourced from former CCW horseshoe bat, and other species, hibernaculum indexed record cards and various reports, now held at the Natural Resources Wales’ Stackpole office. This dataset excludes horseshoe bat breeding roost adult counts, (i.e. nursery roost adult counts, summary data from automatic counters and productivity records) from across Pembrokeshire. These are covered by other surveys. It also excludes Pembrokeshire Bat Sites and Limestone Coast of South Wales summer and winter bat roost counts and surveillance data. Again these records are covered by other bat surveys. These records have been digitised and entered into the Recorder 6 database. NRW manage and hold the top copy of this dataset which now remains static with 1622 records.\t\t[A]  Paper records are held at the NRW Stackpole / Pembrokeshire Offices.   [B]  Digitised species data held in Recorder 6 45 VC Pembs:  Bats - Hibernacula \u0026amp; day/night roost records  CCW0000800000027\tPaul Culyer, Senior Reserve Manager - Pembrokeshire, Operational Services, South, NRW (Formerly Bob Haycock, NRW Senior Reserve Manager - Stackpole)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2011-03-06\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:45.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17432\tNRW_DS98705\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.128Z\t321\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBiodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Invertebrate Species\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373035\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons.  This purpose of this data capture was to enable Non Government Organisations (NGOs), Local Authorities (LAs) and Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW) staff to have access to spatial data on BAP Priority invertebrates.  This dataset is a collation of all confirmed occurrence records of UK Biodiversity Action Plan ( BAP ) priority invertebrate species reported from Wales.\tThis dataset is an extract from the Recorder 6 database. The data was taken from the Welsh Invertebrate Database.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestial\\Invertebrates Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\biological\\species\\all_wales\\baps\\invertebrates\\bap_species.tab\tData Custodian: Mike Howe (legacy CCW dataset put together by Adrian Fowles) Senior Invertebrate Ecologist or John Bratton Biological Data Officer. Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T17:33:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17443\tNRW_DS98710\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.260Z\t625\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLocal Biodiveristy Action Plan (LBAP) Areas\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373130\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the locations of the Local Biodiversity Action Plan Areas.The Local Biodiversity Partnership areas across Wales were used for tragetted species and habitat Action Plans, allowing for a more localised place based approach to monitoring and protecting key areas in Wales.\tPolygons created from OS Unitary Authorities boundaries and National Parks.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Terrestial\\LBAP_areas.lyr\tMetadata and Custodian Contact:  GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17438\tNRW_DS98725\t2024-05-28T12:23:13.188Z\t803\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW) Public Rights of Way (PROW) Condition Survey\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373235\tThis is a spatial dataset containing spatial information related to the Public Rights of Way (PROW) obtained from the PROW condition survey. The core survey assessed the condition of 3,283 km of rights of way within 225 randomly selected survey squares. A sufficient number of survey squares were selected to ensure a geographically representative spread and were randomly selected within highway and national park authority areas. The length of paths surveyed was calculated to provide statistically reliable and representative information about path condition within each authority area.  The purpose of this data capture was to strategically assess the physical condition of public rights of way in Wales to inform Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s now NRW\u0027s) work, including policy advice and guidance to the Welsh Assembly Government and other stakeholders. This can be used for the basis for cost estimates for improving the PROW network in Wales. Condition survey data was deemed very good [a robust sampling methodology and survey and data quality control procedures were employed]. These are detailed in an associated research report.\tPROW base date was taken from CCW\u0027s (now NRW) existing PROW dataset [which was itself digitised from Local Authorities [LAs] definitive maps during the mid 1990s]. This data was  [partially] supplemented or updated using local authorities own GIS definitive PROW data where this was available. The research report details which authorities provided updated information. Condition survey data was collected by on the ground survey over summer months of 2002 and is only available within the report; PROW data largely based on 1990s data supplemented by updated information from some [but not all LAs] at the time of the survey.  INFORMATION WARNING: This dataset is out of date and should not be used for legal challenges. For anything legal related please contact the Local Authorities for the most up to date version of this dataset.\t\tThere is no spatial dataset related to the condistion status, only the base dataset of PRoW created by CCW and supplmented by Local Authority data.  PRoW base data stored at x:\\Recreation and Access\\Public rights of way\\PROW.lyr\tThis metadata may not be complete for more information please contact Jont Bulbeck, Access Team Leader, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:02:57.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17429\tNRW_DS98736\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.468Z\t1920\tValid\tEditable\tAreas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONBs)\tArdaloedd o Harddwch Naturiol Eithriadol (AHNE)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373336\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty (AONBs) statutory landscape designation in Wales. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has a legal duty to maintain and make available AONB boundaries.  Following recommendations from a review commissioned by Welsh Government, in 2024 AONBs were rebranded as National Landscapes. Both names of AONB and National Landscapes are currently in use. Areas of Outstanding Natural Beauty remains the legal name for this statutory designation.  AONBs are designated for the purpose of conserving and enhancing the natural beauty and differ from National Parks in that they do not have a recreation remit. AONBs are managed by their constituent Local Authorities.  NRW is the designating authority to create any new AONB in Wales or boundary variations to existing ones, however these must be confirmed by Welsh Ministers. NRWs statutory powers derive from the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act (NPAC) 1949 and Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000. Any new AONBs designated by NRW would now be under powers derived from the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000.  Five AONBs have been designated in Wales: Gower (1956) was the first AONB designated in England and Wales, Llŷn (1957), Anglesey (1967) and Wye Valley (1971), which straddles the border with England. The Clwydian Range AONB was designated in 1985 and extended by the then Countryside Council for Wales (a predecessor organisation to NRW) in 2011.  The data has been held digitally since mid-1990s. The boundary has been transferred to OS MasterMap by NRW and has been subject to joint checking by NRW staff and the relevant Local Authority staff.-Mae’r set ddata ofodol hon yn cynnwys ffiniau digidol dynodiad tirwedd statudol Ardaloedd o Harddwch Naturiol Eithriadol (AHNEau) yng Nghymru. Mae gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) ddyletswydd gyfreithiol i gynnal ffiniau AHNEau a sicrhau eu bod ar gael.  Yn dilyn argymhellion o adolygiad a gomisiynwyd gan Lywodraeth Cymru, yn 2024 cafodd AHNE eu hailfrandio fel Tirweddau Cenedlaethol. Mae\u0027r ddau enw, AHNE a Thirweddau Cenedlaethol, yn cael eu defnyddio ar hyn o bryd. Ardaloedd o Harddwch Naturiol Eithriadol yw\u0027r enw cyfreithiol ar y dynodiad statudol hwn o hyd.  Mae AHNEau yn cael eu dynodi at ddiben gwarchod a gwella harddwch naturiol ac maent yn wahanol i Barciau Cenedlaethol gan nad oes ganddynt gylch gwaith hamdden. Rheolir AHNEau gan eu Hawdurdodau Lleol cyfansoddol.  CNC yw’r awdurdod dynodi i greu unrhyw AHNE newydd yng Nghymru neu amrywiadau i ffiniau’r rhai presennol, fodd bynnag rhaid i Weinidogion Cymru gadarnhau’r rhain. Mae pwerau statudol CNC yn deillio o Ddeddf Parciau Cenedlaethol a Mynediad i Gefn Gwlad (NPAC) 1949 a Deddf Cefn Gwlad a Hawliau Tramwy 2000. Byddai unrhyw AHNE newydd a ddynodir gan CNC bellach o dan bwerau sy’n deillio o Ddeddf Cefn Gwlad a Hawliau Tramwy 2000.  Mae pum AHNE wedi’u dynodi yng Nghymru: Gŵyr (1956) oedd yr AHNE gyntaf i gael ei dynodi yng Nghymru a Lloegr, Llŷn (1957), Ynys Môn (1967) a Dyffryn Gwy (1971), sy’n pontio’r ffin â Lloegr. Dynodwyd AHNE Bryniau Clwyd yn 1985 a chafodd ei hymestyn gan Gyngor Cefn Gwlad Cymru fel ydoedd ar y pryd (un o’r sefydliadau a ragflaenodd CNC) yn 2011.  Mae’r data wedi’i gadw’n ddigidol ers canol y 1990au. Mae’r ffin wedi’i throsglwyddo i OS MasterMap gan CNC ac wedi bod yn destun gwirio ar y cyd gan staff CNC a staff yr Awdurdod Lleol perthnasol.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444  . Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tOriginally mapped on paper maps at 1:50,000 or 1:63,360 scales. These are now digitised in ArcGIS.\t\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\AONB Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty.lyr  Paper maps are stored in map store Maes y Ffynnon Unitary Local Authorities may also keep a copy of paper map of AONB for their area.\tInternal Custodian: Carole Thornley, Lead Specialist Adviser Landscape, Designated Landscapes Programme\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/382/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_AONB\t2025-11-04T14:22:29.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17430\tNRW_DS98737\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.479Z\t1037\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales\tCyfnod 2 Arolwg o Fawndiroedd yr Iseldir yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373337\tThis dataset is part of the Phase 2 Habitats surveys conducted across Wales. It consists of a detailed survey of plant communities in lowland peatland vegetation.  The survey work was split into two time periods, with the first Phase 2 lowland peatland surveys being conducted between 1977 and 1982. There was then a break between this and the second tranche of surveys which have occurred from 2004 onwards. These more modern surveys were conducted on sites already covered on the 1997-1982 surveys and on newer identified sites. The surveys were conducted at Phase 2 (plant community) level of herbaceous mire habitats predominantly within the lowland environment of Wales.   Key project outputs include site-based assessments of community type, distribution and ecology and evaluative accounts of vegetation and its condition.   Strategic outputs include Area of Search/regional/all Wales synthesis and analysis of data to inform site selection, conservation and restoration.   This part of the Phase 2 survey just includes species datasets collected by Countryside Council for Wales and Natural Resources Wales in-house expert Phase 2 survey team.  Peer-based quality assurance is carried out within the team and by NRW/CCW\u0027s Peatland Specialist. Regular meetings are held to ensure consistent community and species determination and survey protocol application.      The primary objective of the survey is to characterise and evaluate the vegetation cover of lowland peatlands in Wales to ensure the identification, protection and management of important peatland sites.  All Wales coverage although sites vary in size and complexity.   The survey programme is predominantly focussed on lowland mires, i.e. those below the upper functional limit of agricultural enclosure. A few mires above this limit may be considered where they display essentially lowland characteristics or where survey would provide additional critical information for rare communities. The survey encompasses all lowland Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) supporting at least one independently qualifying peatland feature. The only exceptions to this are (i) the largest of the raised mires (namely Cors Fochno, Cors Caron, and Fenns, Whixall, Bettisfield, Wem and Cadney Mosses), and (ii) any sites for which recent reliable information collected in a manner broadly comparable to that of the current survey is already available. Non-statutory sites are included if they have peatland features considered likely to be of individually qualifying status or if they have a notable mire element. A 0.5 ha threshold for individual or groups of communities has been used to help prioritise sites for survey.  GIS layer files within this dataset include: Site boundary (Sites), Vegetation polygons (veg), Mosaic Table, Mosaic Polygons (Mospolys), Habitat (SSSI) Features (features), Quadrats, Photos and Target Note (Notes, points, polygons and text), Deep peat, Peat depth, Candidate sites.    There is a DMS document explaining the GIS layers and data structure called P2 Peatland data structures in ArcGIS.   Survey Reports are also available from NRW.-Roedd y set ddata hon yn rhan o arolygon cynefinoedd Cyfnod 2 a gynhaliwyd ledled Cymru. Mae\u0027n cynnwys arolwg manwl o gymunedau planhigion yn llystyfiant mawndiroedd yr iseldir. Prif nod yr arolwg yw nodi a gwerthuso\u0027r llystyfiant sydd ym mawndiroedd iseldir Cymru er mwyn sicrhau bod safleoedd mawndir pwysig yn cael eu nodi, eu diogelu a\u0027u rheoli. Mae\u0027r holl set ddata wedi\u0027i chadarnhau a\u0027i dilysu\u0027n drwyadl gan arbenigwyr arolygu llystyfiant Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru. Gall cyfansoddiad cynefinoedd newid dros amser o ganlyniad i dueddiadau olynol, rheolaeth tir neu hyd yn oed ddatblygiadau arfaethedig. Felly, ni ellir bod yn sicr bod math o lystyfiant neu rywogaeth yn parhau i fodoli am ei fod wedi\u0027i gofnodi yn y data digidol.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis dataset contains spatial sites and species data, non-spatial species data and images in JEPG and slide format. Phase 2 vegetation mapping and collection of quadrat data on plant community composition.  Additional information is collected on notable species, peat depth, environmental factors, condition, management requirements and conservation evaluation. See Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales Survey Manual Version 2, S.D.S. Bosanquet et al., 2025 (In Prep.) for more information.   Written site accounts are available for each surveyed site. These greatly assist in interpreting the raw data in each case.  Characterise and evaluate the vegetation cover of lowland peatlands in Wales based upon a programme of plant (sub-) community level sampling and mapping. All Wales, underpinned by criteria to aid site selection.     The current survey programme is the latest in a series of Phase 2 level surveys of major habitat groups in the Welsh lowlands.  These include the recently completed Lowland Grassland Survey, which was undertaken in-house, and commissioned surveys covering lowland heathlands, woodlands, sand dunes and saltmarsh.  The current peatland survey programme follows on an earlier pre-NVC assessment conducted between 1977 and 1982 (the Welsh Lowland Peatland Survey) which is dated and inadequate for modern conservation practice.  This is the concluding part of a strategic series of Phase 2 level survey programmes across Wales.    Entire dataset has been extensively validated and verified by NRW vegetation survey specialists. Habitat composition may change over time due to successional trends, land management or even planned developments. A record of a vegetation type or species in the digital data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground. Bosanquet, S.D.S., Jones P.S., Reed D. K., Birch K.S. \u0026amp; Turner A.J. 2026. NRW Evidence Report No: [XX], [XX]pp, Natural Resources Wales, Bangor In Prep.\tA subset of the non-spatial species data is held in Recorder 6 which dates between 2004 and 2011, and is available to download via the NBN Atlas (see Resource Locator).\tSpatial data available on X Drive: X:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Lowland Peatland Survey of Wales and Y:\\Knowledge_Strategy_Planning\\ Evidence_Knowledge_Advice\\Evidence_Analysis\\Phase_2_Peatland [Working versions, and draft data. Moved to X-drive when finished. GIS data also on DataMapWales].     Some Species Data is held in Recorder 6.  This data is now available on the NBN Atlas. This dataset was last updated NBN Gateway (See Resource Locator) on the  01/01/2020 as no more data of this kind is being entered into Recorder 6 this dataset is now classed as static and therefore no longer needs to be updated regularly. This may change if the data gets moved to an NRW centralised system (from legacy CCW).     [A]  Some associated documents are stored in Terrestrial Science Group folder called PEATSURV on the server (Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\TSG\\ PEATSURV) and Content Manager (Ffynnon).  [B]  Biological Data held in Recorder 6: See Data Format for more information (between 2004 to 2011)  - LPSW Brecknock CCW0007100000005     - LPSW Carmarthen and Dinefwr CCW0007100000006   - LPSW Ceredigion CCW0007100000007    - LPSW Clwyd CCW0007100000008  - LPSW East Gwynedd CCW0007100000009    - LPSW Gwent CCW0007100000010   - LPSW Mid \u0026amp; South Glamorgan CCW0007100000001   - LPSW Montgomeryshire CCW0007100000011    - LPSW Preseli \u0026amp; South Pembrokeshire CCW0007100000012     - LPSW Radnorshire CCW0007100000002    - LPSW West Glamorgan \u0026amp; Llanelli CCW0007100000003   - LPSW West Gwynedd CCW0007100000004     [C] All survey reports and quadrat data in spreadsheets are on DMS DMS\\Evidence\\Terrestrial Habitats\\Peatland Survey https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/ps/SitePages/Home.aspx; species records (quadrat data) https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Terrestrial-Habitats/ps/Data%20Tables/Forms/NRW%20View.aspx.     [D] Most photos upto 2020 are on Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\TSG\\ PEATSURV , but those from 2021 onwards are in a series of OneDrive folders, as NRW still doesn\u0027t have a workable solution for data photos.\tInternal Custodian: Kathryn Birch, Specialist Advisor – Terrestrial Habitats, Terrestrial Ecosystems Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-08-04\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3595/?ows_service\u003dwms\t2025-12-19T12:12:59.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17428\tNRW_DS98740\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.469Z\t1140\tValid\tEditable\tBiogenetic Reserves\tGwarchodfeydd Biogenetig\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373430\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the boundaries of Biogenetic Reserves in Wales.  Biogenetic Reserves aim to conserve European flora, fauna and natural areas especially heathlands and dry grasslands that although common in one country, may be scarce in another. In this way a store of genetic material - the genes of plants and animals - is kept for the future, hence the term Biogenetic.  To qualify for this designation, sites in the UK must first be Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) or otherwise designated. The Bern Convention - signed by the UK government in 1982, led to the establishment of this European network of Biogenetic Reserves.-Mae hon yn set ddata ofodol o ffiniau\u0027r Gwarchodfeydd Biogenetig yng Nghymru. Nod Gwarchodfeydd Biogenetig yw gwarchod fflora, ffawna ac ardaloedd naturiol Ewropeaidd, yn enwedig rhostiroedd a glaswelltiroedd sych, sy\u0027n gyffredin mewn un wlad efallai ond yn brin mewn un arall. Fel hyn mae storfa o ddeunydd genetig - genynnau planhigion ac anifeiliaid - yn cael ei chadw ar gyfer y dyfodol, sef ystyr y term Biogenetig. I fod yn gymwys ar gyfer y dynodiad hwn, rhaid i safleoedd yn y DU fod wedi\u0027u dynodi\u0027n SoDdGA neu ddynodiad arall yn gyntaf. Arweiniodd Confensiwn Bern a lofnodwyd gan lywodraeth y DU ym 1982 at sefydlur rhwydwaith Ewropeaidd hwn o Warchodfeydd Biogenetig.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444 . Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tDigital data captured and digitised to OS MasterMap.\tPlease refer to the Bern Convention, for original designation need.\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Biogenetic Reserves.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Rhodri T. Jones, Advisor (2) Protected Sites (International), Biodiversity Policy \u0026amp; Strategy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2022-11-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T15:30:15.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17426\tNRW_DS98741\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.451Z\t929\tValid\tEditable\tBiosphere Reserves\tGwarchodfeydd Biosffer\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373431\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of biosphere reserves in Wales.   The Biosphere Reserve is an international designation made by UNESCO based on nominations made by more than 110 countries. These sites are chosen to conserve examples of areas which are characteristic of the world\u0027s natural regions. They must also be areas where people are important components of everyday life. The biosphere is rapidly changing under the influence of human activity, so meeting their needs whilst conserving the area\u0027s natural processes and wildlife is of the utmost importance.-Mae\u0027r set ddata ofodol hon yn crynhoi ffiniau\u0027r Gwarchodfeydd Biosfferig yng Nghymru. Mae Gwarchodfa Biosfferig yn ddynodiad gan UNESCO ar sail enwebiadau gan fwy na 110 o wledydd. Dewisir y safleoedd er mwyn gwarchod enghreifftiau o ardaloedd sy\u0027n nodweddiadol o ardaloedd naturiol y byd. Rhaid iddynt fod yn ardaloedd lle mae pobl yn rhan bwysig o fywyd bob dydd hefyd. Mae\u0027r biosffer yn newid yn gyflym dan ddylanwad gweithgaredd dynol, felly maen holl bwysig deall sut i ddiwallu\u0027r anghenion dynol hyn tra\u0027n gwarchod prosesau naturiol a bywyd gwyllt yr ardal yr un pryd.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans  AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThe original boundary was a paper map but later became digital data. The outer edge of the biosphere boundary is made of Community Council boundaries to landward and the seaward edge is snapped to the -10 metre bathymetric contour. The inner two zones follow firstly Sites of Scientific Interest (SSSI) boundaries (Buffer Zone) and then the Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) boundaries (Core Zone). The largest area is called the Transition Zone. This latest modification to the original site boundary was produced digitally.    The data has been checked by Natural Resources Wales\u0027 staff.\tPlease refer to the UNESCO Biosphere designation process .\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Biospheric Reserves.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Rhodri T. Jones, Advisor (2) Protected Sites (International), Biodiversity Policy \u0026amp; Strategy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-04-22\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/391/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_BIOSPHERE\t2025-12-23T10:48:34.243Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17427\tNRW_DS98742\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.452Z\t826\tValid\tEditable\tCountry Parks\tParciau Gwledig\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373432\tThere are over 250 recognised Country Parks in England and Wales. Most Country Parks were designated in the 1970s, under the Countryside Act 1968 with the support of the former Countryside Commission. In more recent times there has been no specific financial support for Country Parks directly, and fewer have been designated. Most are managed by local authorities, although other organisations and private individuals can also run them.   A Country Park is an area designated for people to visit and enjoy recreation in a countryside environment. The purpose of a Country Park is to provide somewhere for visitors who do not necessarily want to go out into the wider countryside. Visitors can enjoy a public open space with an informal atmosphere, as opposed to a formal park as might be found in an urban area. For this reason Country Parks are usually found close to or on the edge of built-up areas.-Dynodwyd y rhan fwyaf o Barciau Gwledig yn y 1970au dan Ddeddf Cefn Gwlad 1968 gyda chefnogaeth y cyn Gomisiwn Cefn Gwlad. Yn fwy diweddar ni fu unrhyw gymorth ariannol penodol ar gyfer Parciau Gwledig yn uniongyrchol, ac mae llai wedi cael eu dynodi. Mae\u0027r mwyafrif yn cael eu rheoli gan awdurdodau lleol, er y gall sefydliadau eraill ac unigolion hefyd eu rhedeg. Mae Parc Gwledig yn ardal a ddynodwyd er mwyn i bobl ymweld a mwynhau gweithgareddau hamdden mewn amgylchedd gefn gwlad. Pwrpas Parc Gwledig yw darparu ardal i ymwelwyr nad ydynt o reidrwydd yn awyddus i fynd allan i\u0027r cefn gwlad ehangach. Gall ymwelwyr fwynhau man agored cyhoeddus gydag awyrgylch anffurfiol, yn hytrach na pharc ffurfiol fel mewn ardal drefol. Am y rheswm yma mae Parciau Gwledig fel arfer yn agos at neu ar gyrion ardaloedd trefol, ac yn anaml yng nghefn gwlad go iawn.  Cydnabyddiaeth  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis dataset was originally put together from maps provided by Unitary Authorities and then digitised by the former Countryside Council for Wales (now part of Natural Resources Wales) staff in the mid 1990s. Later updates were compiled from digital data supplied by Unitary Authorities and pdfs. Digital data was captured to OS MasterMap.\t\tX:\\Designations\\Local Authorities\\Country Parks.lyr\tJont Bulbeck - Team Leader, Outdoor Access \u0026amp; Recreation\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-10-03\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/915/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:country_parks\t2025-12-23T10:44:03.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17424\tNRW_DS98745\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.322Z\t580\tValid\tEditable\tHeritage Coast\tArfordiroedd Treftadaeth\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373435\tThis spatial dataset contains digitised boundaries of heritage coasts in Wales. Heritage Coasts occupy about a third of the Welsh coastline, that is 500 km (300 miles). These sites were set up to protect our coastlines from insensitive developments. Most are defined simply by the coastline between two named points, however some have clearly defined inland boundaries. Their status carries no legal protection, but planning authorities must take the designation into account when making decisions on development.   Management of Heritage Coasts is the remit of the local authority and is generally carried out by Heritage Coast Officers with some practical tasks done by volunteers. Most of the Heritage coastal strips were defined in 1973, 1974 and one in 1984. The descriptions and paper maps associated with this dataset are the legal documents.-Mae\u0027r set ddata ofodol hon yn cynnwys ffiniau arfordiroedd treftadaeth yng Nghymru. Mae tua thraean, sef 500 km (300 milltir), o arfordiroedd Cymru\u0027n Arfordiroedd Treftadaeth. Sefydlwyd y safleoedd hyn er mwyn amddiffyn ein harfordiroedd rhag datblygiadau ansensitif. Diffinnir y mwyafrif ohonynt yn syml gan y morlin rhwng dau bwynt a enwir, fodd bynnag, mae gan rai ffiniau mewndirol amlwg. Nid oes unrhyw amddiffynfa gyfreithiol i\u0027r statws, ond mae\u0027n rhaid i awdurdodau cynllunio ystyried y dynodiad wrth wneud penderfyniadau ar ddatblygu. Mae rheoli Arfordiroedd Treftadaeth yn rhan o gylch gwaith yr awdurdodau lleol sy\u0027n cael ei gyflawni gan Swyddogion Arfordir Treftadaeth gan amlaf, gyda rhai tasgau ymarferol yn cael eu gwneud gan wirfoddolwyr. Dynodwyd y rhan fwyaf o\u0027r stribedi Treftadaeth arfordirol ym 1973, 1974 ac un ym 1984..  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis designation was launched in the seventies to protect our coastlines from insensitive developments. Digitising this data makes it more mobile and helps safeguard these areas.   The data was first digitally captured in 1995 and the latest update in 2010 to OS Mastermap. The data was checked by Natural Resources Wales\u0027 (NRW) staff. NRW inherited the descriptions of the Heritage Coasts designations that lie within Wales from the Countryside Commission. Most are represented by pairs of co-ordinates and just two have boundaries Great Orme (1:10560) and Glamorgan (1:50,000). Using this source the designations were digitised on to OS MasterMap 2006.   For all Heritage Coast sites we have buffered a line 0.6 km from Mean High Water Mark (MHWM) and then given this line a 1 metre width in order to conform to supplying polygon data. This has been done for reasons of cartographic display as well as showing there is no definitive terrestrial area given for the majority of sites.\t\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\Heritage Coasts.lyr\tCarole Thornley, Lead Specialist Advisor: Landscape Planning, Landscape and Energy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/387/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_HERITAGE_COAST\t2025-11-11T15:38:17.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17425\tNRW_DS98746\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.358Z\t1030\tValid\tEditable\tLocal Nature Reserves (LNRs)\tGwarchodfeydd Natur Lleol (GNLl)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373436\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Local Nature Reserves (LNRs) in Wales. Local Nature Reserves are established and managed by local authorities, following consultation with Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949.   For a site to become an LNR it must have natural features of special interest to the local area, and the authority must either have a legal interest in the land or have an agreement with the owner to manage the land as a reserve.   LNR prove to be useful not only to protect habitats and wildlife but increase people\u0027s awareness of their environment. They are places where children can learn about nature, and they are often situated in or near urban areas.   Please refer to Countryside Act 1949 for reasons for capture of original designation.   With the coming of GIS and its use in government departments and commercial companies mobilising the data in this way, advances the protection of these sites and the efficiency of decision making. The LNRs have been designated over a number of years, from 1970 to the present day, and are on-going. The data has been held digitally since mid 1990s. This data can be subject to change by the local authority at anytime.-Mae\u0027r set ddata ofodol hon yn crynhoi ffiniau digidol Gwarchodfeydd Natur Lleol (GNLl) yng Nghymru. Mae gwarchodfeydd Natur Lleol yn cael eu sefydlu a\u0027u rheoli gan awdurdodau lleol, yn dilyn ymgynghoriad â hyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) dan Ddeddf Parciau Cenedlaethol a Mynediad i Gefn Gwlad 1949. Er mwyn i safle gael ei dynodi\u0027n Warchodfa Natur Leol rhaid bod ganddi nodweddion naturiol sydd o ddiddordeb arbennig i\u0027r ardal leol ac mae\u0027n rhaid i\u0027r awdurdod naill ai fod â hawl gyfreithiol ar y tir neu gytundeb gyda\u0027r perchennog i reoli\u0027r tir fel gwarchodfa. Mae GNLl yn fuddiol nid yn unig er mwyn diogelu cynefinoedd a bywyd gwyllt ond hefyd er mwyn cynyddu ymwybyddiaeth pobl o\u0027u hamgylchedd. Maent yn lleoedd lle gall plant ddysgu am natur, ac maent wedi\u0027u lleoli mewn neu gerllaw ardaloedd trefol yn aml. Gweler Deddf Cefn Gwlad 1949 am y rhesymau dros y dynodiad gwreiddiol. Mae dyfodiad y System Gwybodaeth Ddaearyddol (GIS) a\u0027i defnydd gan adrannau\u0027r llywodraeth a chwmnau masnachol i ddefnyddio\u0027r data fel hyn, yn gwella amddiffyniad y safleoedd hyn ac effeithlonrwydd y broses o wneud penderfyniadau. Dynodwyd y Gwarchodfeydd Natur Lleol dros nifer o flynyddoedd, o 1970 hyd heddiw, ac mae hyn yn parhau.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tOriginally produced as paper maps but with the progress to GIS later designations produced directly from digital capture.    As NRW is part of the designation process, Unitary Authorities pass their proposed LNR boundary to NRW either as a paper map or digitally. The boundary is re-digitised to the NRW specification and returned for checking to the source Authority. Once designated the LNR is added to the \u0027Main dataset\u0027.    Digital data captured to OS MasterMap.    If the local Authority wish to make subsequent changes to the boundaries they can make them without re-designation and without them being to NRW specifications.\t\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\LNR Local Nature Reserves.lyr\tHolly York\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-12-15\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/388/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_LNR\t2025-12-16T10:11:37.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17423\tNRW_DS98750\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.256Z\t620\tUnassessed\tEditable\tFormer Marine Nature Reserves (MNRs)\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373530\tIn 2015 the Marine Nature Reserve changed designation to Marine Conservation Zone (MCZ). This spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Marine Conservation Zones (MCZs) former Marine Nature Reserves in Wales. MCZs and MNRs are a way of conserving marine habitats and wildlife and other features, of special importance, along the shore or on the seabed. Skomer, the first and only MNR in Wales, was established in 1990 and is situated around the island of Skomer and the Marloes Peninsula in south west Wales. The Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 empowers NRW to apply to the First Minister for the National Assembly for Wales (NAW) to designate MNR.  MNR may be governed by byelaws, created through discussions and mutual agreements with all concerned, and approved by WAG. MNR make a valuable contribution to the protection of our seas by creating an awareness of the fragility and value of the natural marine heritage. In Wales the MNRs that existed have been superseded by MCZs. The Marine and Coastal Access Act 2009 allows for the creation of MCZs. MCZs protect a range of nationally important marine wildlife, habitats, geology and geomorphology, and can be designated anywhere in English and Welsh territorial and UK offshore waters.\tThe boundary was first digitised from the designation paper map at 1:10,000 scale. In 2010 it was digitised to OS MasterMap (CCW update 2008) using the description of the boundary from the Byelaws document. There is no digital data for Highest Astronomical Tides (HAT), which is the landward boundary for a great length, we have adopted a 1.5 m buffer above Mean High Water Mark (MHWM). The Latitude and Longitude positions plotted to WGS84 projection and then the positions converted to British National Grid. Where the description quotes following MLWOT the boundary has been captured to MLWM as shown on OS MasterMap. Therefore for the definitive boundary please refer to the description in the Byelaws. With the coming of GIS and its use in government departments and commercial companies mobilising the data in this way, advances the protection of these sites and the efficiency of decision making.\tPlease refer to Countryside Acts 1981, for reasons for capture of original designation of the MNRs. In Wales the MNRs that existed have been superseded by MCZs.  Please refer to The Marine and Coastal Access Act 2009 allows for the creation of MCZs.\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Nature Reserve.lyr\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:36:16.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17422\tNRW_DS98751\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.123Z\t672\tValid\tEditable\tNational Nature Reserves (NNRs) Declarations\tGwarchodfeydd Natur Cenedlaethol (GNC) gyda’u datganiadau unigol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373531\tThis is a spatial dataset which shows National Nature Reserves (NNRs) with their different declarations. NNRs represent the very best examples of our wildlife habitats and geological features and can range in size between five hectares to well over 2,000.   NNRs are declared by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act of 1949, or under the Wildlife and Countryside Act of 1981. They are owned or leased by NRW, or the land is held by an approved body, such as a County Wildlife Trust. Each reserve has a programme of work to manage the site\u0027s special features. All of them are also Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and may provide places for educational projects, research and management trials. Some reserves require permits to gain access to them.    Please refer to National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949 and Wildlife and Countryside Act of 1981, for reasons of creation of the original designation. With the coming of GIS and its use in government departments and commercial companies mobilising the data in this way, advances the protection of these sites and the efficiency of decision making.    This data has been checked by NRW staff. The original, individual, declaration paper maps are the legal documents. It should be pointed out that some of the oldest declaration maps.-Mae hon yn set ddata ofodol sy’n dangos Gwarchodfeydd Natur Cenedlaethol gyda’u datganiadau unigol. Mae Gwarchodfeydd Natur Cenedlaethol yn cynrychioli’r enghreifftiau gorau oll o’n cynefinoedd bywyd gwyllt a’n nodweddion daearegol a gallant amrywio o ran eu maint o bum hectar i ymhell dros 2,000. Caiff Gwarchodfeydd eu datgan gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) o dan Ddeddf Parciau Cenedlaethol a Mynediad i Gefn Gwlad 1949, neu o dan Ddeddf Bywyd Gwyllt a Chefn Gwlad 1981. Mae CNC yn berchen arnynt neu’n eu prydlesu, neu mae’r tir yn cael ei ddal gan gorff cymeradwy, megis Ymddiriedolaeth Natur Sirol. Mae gan bob gwarchodfa raglen waith i reoli nodweddion arbennig y safle. Mae pob un ohonynt hefyd yn Safleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGA) a gallant fod yn lleoedd ar gyfer prosiectau addysgol, ymchwil a threialon rheoli. Mae angen trwyddedau i gael mynediad at rai o’r gwarchodfeydd.  Cyfeiriwch at Ddeddf Parciau Cenedlaethol a Mynediad i Gefn Gwlad 1949 a Deddf Bywyd Gwyllt a Chefn Gwlad 1981, am resymau dros greu’r dynodiad gwreiddiol. Mae’r data hwn wedi’i wirio gan staff CNC. Y mapiau datganiad papur unigol gwreiddiol yw’r dogfennau cyfreithiol.   Mae pob  map datganiad unigol sy’n cwmpasu un Warchodfa wedi’i ddigideiddio. Mewn rhai achosion mae Gwarchodfa unigol yn cynnwys un map datganiad, tra bod gan eraill 15 o fapiau datganiad ar wahân a gafodd eu cofrestru dros nifer o flynyddoedd. I ddechrau, cynhyrchwyd y mapiau datganiad unigol ar bapur ar raddfeydd 1:10,000 neu 1:2500 ar argraffiad o fap o’r 1900au cynnar, yna yn ddiweddarach fe’u crëwyd drwy GIS. Trosglwyddwyd y set ddata hon i OSMasterMap (diweddariad CCGC 2006) yn 2008 ac mae’n dal i fynd.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tEach individual declaration map that makes up a single NNR has been digitised. In some cases an individual NNR is made up of one declaration map, whereas others have 15 separate declaration maps that were registered over a number of years.   Initially the individual, declaration maps were produced on paper at 1:10,000 or 1:2500 scales on early 1900s edition of map, then in later years created through GIS. This dataset has been transferred to OSMasterMap (CCW 2006 update) in 2008 and is on going.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\NNR Declarations\\NNR Declarations.lyr\tThomas Hellawell, Lead Specialist Advisor Estates\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2023-12-08\tpublication\tnull\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_nnr_declarations\t2025-03-07T16:36:14.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17421\tNRW_DS98759\t2024-05-28T12:23:12.061Z\t963\tValid\tEditable\tRamsar Sites - Wetlands of International Importance\tGwlyptiroedd Ramsar o bwysigrwydd rhyngwladol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373539\tThe Convention on Wetlands, called the Ramsar Convention, is an intergovernmental treaty that provides the framework for national action and international cooperation for the conservation and wise use of wetlands and their resources. This spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Ramsar sites in Wales. In ratifying the Convention in 1976, the UK government accepted a commitment to promote the conservation of internationally important wetland sites within its territories.  Wetlands are vital for many types of birds particularly waterfowl and Wales have some prime sites that are essential to the survival of many wetland plants and animals. Wetland sites can be areas of marsh, fen, peatland or open water; natural or artificial; permanent or temporary; with water that is fresh, brackish or salty. They can also include shallow areas of sea. All Ramsar sites are also Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI). Wetlands of International Importance are identified by Natural Resources Wales (NRW), in collaboration with the UK Joint Nature Conservation Committee (JNCC), and designated by the First Minister for the National Assembly for Wales.    The Ramsars have been designated over a number of years, from 1976 to the present day, and are on-going.  The data has been held digitally since mid1990s. The boundaries have been checked by NRW staff. Due to scale enlargement and different editions of map a certain degree of interpretation was required.-Mae\u0027r set ddata ofodol hon yn cynnwys ffiniau digidol safleoedd Ramsar yng Nghymru. Wrth gadarnhau\u0027r Confensiwn ym 1976, ymrwymodd llywodraeth y DU i hyrwyddo cadwraeth safleoedd gwlyptir o bwysigrwydd rhyngwladol o fewn ei thiriogaethau. Mae gwlyptiroedd yn hanfodol ar gyfer llawer o fathau o adar yn enwedig adar dŵr, ac mae gan Gymru rai o\u0027r safleoedd gorau sy\u0027n hanfodol i oroesiad llawer o blanhigion ac anifeiliaid y gwlypdir. Gall safleoedd gwlyptir fod yn ardaloedd o gors, ffen, mawndir neu ddŵr agored; naturiol neu artiffisial; parhaol neu dros dro; gyda dŵr ffres, lled hallt neu hallt. Gallant gynnwys ardaloedd o fôr bas hefyd. Mae pob safle Ramsar yn Safle o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGA) hefyd. Nodir Gwlyptiroedd o Bwysigrwydd Rhyngwladol gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC), mewn cydweithrediad â Chyd-bwyllgor Cadwraeth Natur y DU, ac fe\u0027u dynodir gan Brif Weinidog Cynulliad Cenedlaethol Cymru. Mae safleoedd Ramsar wedi\u0027u dynodi dros nifer o flynyddoedd, o 1976 hyd heddiw ac maent yn parhau.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444 Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tOriginally produced as paper maps but with the progress to GIS later designations produced directly from digital capture. The data was derived from the designation paper maps that vary in scale from 1:50,000, 1:25,000 and 1:10,000.  The present data is digitised to OS MasterMap. With the coming of GIS and its use in government departments and commercial companies mobilising the data in this way, advances the protection of these sites and the efficiency of decision making.\tPlease refer to Wetlands of International Importance - Ramsar Convention for reasons for capture of original designation. NRW has also created a subset of this dataset showing only those where relate to the marine enivronment. These are available on the Welsh Government marine planning portal.\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Ramsar.lyr x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\\Marine protected areas Ramsar.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Rhodri T. Jones, Advisor (2) Protected Sites (International), Biodiversity Policy \u0026amp; Strategy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/379/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_RAMSAR\t2025-12-23T10:39:55.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17477\tNRW_DS98766\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.385Z\t1545\tValid\tEditable\tSpecial Areas of Conservation (SACs)\tArdaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACA)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373636\tThis spatial dataset contains digital boundaries of Special Areas of Conservation (SACs) in Wales. The EC Habitats and Species Directive came into effect in 1992 with the aim of maintaining a rich variety of wildlife by protecting vulnerable habitats, and the plants and animals they support. The Habitat Directive together with the 1979 Birds Directive will lead to the establishment of a series of sites throughout Europe that will be known as \u0027Natura 2000\u0027. This represents the cream of European sites and will ensure their survival for future generations. SACs have been designated over a number of years, from 1993 to the present day, and are on-going.    These sites have been mapped on paper since the mid 1990s and then there was a major review from 2000 with the \u0027SAC Moderation Project\u0027 when the data was captured digitally for the first time.-Mae\u0027r set ddata ofodol hon yn cynnwys ffiniau Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACA) a ddynodwyd yng Nghymru. Daeth Cyfarwyddeb Cynefinoedd a Rhywogaethau\u0027r Gymuned Ewropeaidd i rym ym 1992 gyda\u0027r nod o warchod bioamrywiaeth trwy ddiogelu amrywiaeth eang o gynefinoedd a rhywogaethau anifeiliaid a phlanhigion. Mae pob ACA, ynghyd ag Ardaloedd Gwarchodaeth Arbennig (AGA) a ddynodwyd o dan Gyfarwyddeb Adar Gwyllt y Gymuned Ewropeaidd i ddiogelu rhywogaethau adar prin a mudol, yn cynnwys rhwydwaith o safleoedd ar draws yr UE a elwir yn \u0027Natura 2000\u0027. Cynigiwyd y rhan fwyaf o ACA yng Nghymru yng nghanol y 1990au ac fe\u0027u dynodwyd yn ffurfiol yn 2004. Cafodd ACA eu mapio ar bapur yn wreiddiol ond ers tua 2000 mae\u0027r data ffiniau wedi ei gipio\u0027n ddigidol. Mae\u0027r safleoedd ACA a ddangosir yn y set ddata hon wedi cwblhau\u0027r broses gyfreithiol lawn o ddynodi.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tIn 2005 all SACs were re-designated/designated and the number of sites doubled in Wales, so this entire dataset originated as digital data to OS Landline.   In 2008 the whole dataset was updated to OS MasterMap (mostly to CCW 2006 OS update). If any underpinning Sites of Scientific Interest (SSSIs) are re-notified the overlying SAC designation will likewise be put to the latest release by NRW of OS MasterMap.  With the coming of GIS and its use in government departments and commercial companies mobilising the data in this way, advances the protection of these sites and the efficiency of decision making.   This data has been checked by NRW staff. However please refer to the designation map as the legal definitive boundary. Large SACs that have been printed on a smaller scale map than OS Landline, please refer to OS MasterMap edition at time of transposition to view the definitive boundary.\tPlease refer to EC Habitats and Species Directive 1992 for reasons for capture of original designation.  Before Special Areas of Conservation or fully designated they go through a stage of being a cSAC or candidate Special Area of Conservation, these reside in a separate layer. In addition to the NRW has also created a subset of this dataset just showing those which are Marine Special Areas of Conservation, this is available on the marine planning portal.\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SAC Special Areas of Conservation.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\cSAC Candidate Special Areas of Conservation.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\\Marine protected areas SAC.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\pSAC Proposed Special Areas of Conservation.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Rhodri T. Jones, Advisor (2) Protected Sites (International), Biodiversity Policy \u0026amp; Strategy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/380/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_SAC\t2025-12-23T10:35:11.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17462\tNRW_DS98775\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.819Z\t1019\tValid\tEditable\tSpecial Protection Areas (SPA)\tArdaloedd Gwarchodaeth Arbennig (AGA)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373735\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of all Spatial Protection Areas (SPAs) in Wales. The EC Birds Directive of 1979 requires member states to establish SPAs to conserve the habitats of two categories of birds:  i) Species which are rare or vulnerable, of which there are forty-eight in the UK.   ii) Some migratory species which visit our shores regularly.    SPAs in Wales are identified by NRW, in conjunction with the UK Joint Nature Conservation Committee, and designated by the First Minister for the Welsh Assembly Government.   These sites are also protected through being Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs). The 1994 Conservation Regulations also provide a means of protecting such areas at sea. SPAs together with SACs will contribute to a European Union network of protected sites to be known as \u0027Natura 2000\u0027 (N2K sites). SPAs have been designated over a number of years, from 1982 to the present day, and are on-going.  The data has been held digitally since the mid1990s.-Mae\u0027r set ddata ofodol hon yn cynnwys ffiniau digidol yr holl Ardaloedd Gwarchodaeth Gofodol (AGG) yng Nghymru. Yn unol â Chyfarwyddeb Adar 1979 y CE rhaid i aelod wladwriaethau sefydlu AGA i warchod cynefinoedd dau gategori o adar: i) Rhywogaethau sy\u0027n brin neu dan fygythiad - mae pedwar deg wyth ohonynt yn y DU. ii) Rhai rhywogaethau mudol sy\u0027n ymweld â\u0027n glannau\u0027n rheolaidd.  Mae Ardaloedd Gwarchodaeth Arbennig yn cael eu nodi yng Nghymru gan CNC, mewn cydweithrediad â Chydbwyllgor Cadwraeth Natur y DU, a\u0027u dynodi gan Brif Weinidog Llywodraeth Cynulliad Cymru. Mae\u0027r safleoedd hyn yn cael eu gwarchod drwy eu dynodiad yn Safleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGA) hefyd. Mae Rheoliadau Cadwraeth 1994 yn darparu modd o amddiffyn ardaloedd morol o\u0027r fath hefyd. Bydd Ardaloedd Gwarchodaeth Arbennig ynghyd ag Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig yn cyfrannu at rwydwaith yr Undeb Ewropeaidd o safleoedd gwarchodedig sy\u0027n cael eu hadnabod fel \u0027Natura 2000\u0027 (safleoedd N2K). Mae Ardaloedd Gwarchodaeth Arbennig wedi\u0027u dynodi dros nifer o flynyddoedd, o 1982 hyd heddiw, ac maent yn parhau. Mae\u0027r data wedi\u0027i gofnodi\u0027n ddigidol ers canol y 1990au.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tAll SPAs designated before the advent of GIS technology have been converted into digital data from paper maps of various scales. In 2008 the whole dataset was updated to OS MasterMap (to CCW 2006 OS update). This update made reference back to original designation maps and all notational descriptions on these maps e.g. boundary follows Mean High Water Mark and is liable to change. All new or re-designated sites will be put to the latest release by NRW, of OS MasterMap. With the coming of GIS and its use in government departments and commercial companies mobilising the data in this way, advances the protection of these sites and the efficiency of decision making.  This data has been checked by relevant NRW staff. However please refer to the designation map as the legal definitive boundary. For recent, large SPAs that were captured digitally and have been printed on a smaller scale map than OS MasterMap, please refer to OSMasterMap edition at time of capture to view the definitive boundary. Marine SPAs will be the exception as they are shown against Marine Charts, so please refer to description for definitive boundary out to sea.\tPlease refer to the EC Birds Directive of 1979 and 1994 Conservation Regulations for reasons for original designations. RW has also created a subset of this dataset showing only those where relate to the marine enivronment. These are available on the Welsh Government marine planning portal.\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SPA Special Protection Areas.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\\Marine protected areas SPA.lyr  x:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\pSPA Proposed Special Protection Areas.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Rhodri T. Jones, Advisor (2) Protected Sites (International), Biodiversity Policy \u0026amp; Strategy\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/381/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_SPA\t2026-01-09T16:41:16.000Z\tMonica Jones\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17474\tNRW_DS98776\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.313Z\t1759\tValid\tEditable\tSites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs)\tSafleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGA)\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373736\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSIs) in Wales. SSSIs cover a wide range of habitats from small fens, bogs and riverside meadows to sand-dunes, woodlands and vast tracts of uplands. Most are in private ownership, although some are owned and managed by local wildlife trusts, or other voluntary conservation bodies.   Notification of an SSSI under the Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981 has since been amended by the Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2001, which brought about numerous changes in the way SSSI are notified managed and protected.   In order to ensure consistent, favourable long-term management of these sites, Natural Resources Wales (NRW) with landowners have prepared management plans for all SSSI in Wales. Local planning authorities are required to consult NRW before allowing any development to proceed that may affect an SSSI. Water, gas and electricity companies must also do the same.   SSSIs have been designated, from 1949 to the present day, and are on-going. The data has been held digitally since the mid-1990s. This data has been checked by relevant NRW staff. Please refer to the designation map as the legal definitive boundary. For large SSSIs that were captured digitally and have been printed on a smaller scale map than OS MasterMap, please refer to the OS MasterMap edition at time of capture to view the definitive boundary.-Mae\u0027r set ddata ofodol hon yn cynnwys ffiniau Safleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGA) yng Nghymru. Mae SoDdGA yn cwmpasu amrywiaeth eang o gynefinoedd megis ffeniau bach, corsydd, dolydd glan afon, twyni tywod, coetiroedd ac ardaloedd helaeth o ucheldir. Mae\u0027r rhan fwyaf mewn perchnogaeth breifat, er bod rhai yn eiddo i ac yn cael eu rheoli gan ymddiriedolaethau bywyd gwyllt lleol neu gyrff cadwraeth gwirfoddol eraill. Mae dynodiad SoDdGA dan Ddeddf Bywyd Gwyllt a Chefn Gwlad 1981 wedi ei ddiwygio gan Ddeddf Cefn Gwlad a Hawliau Tramwy 2001, a gyflwynodd newidiadau niferus i\u0027r ffordd y mae SoDdGA yn cael eu dynodi, eu rheoli a\u0027u gwarchod. Er mwyn sicrhau bod rheolaeth hirdymor yr ardaloedd hyn yn gyson a ffafriol mae Cyfaeth Naturiol Cymru, ar y cyd thirfeddianwyr, wedi paratoi cynlluniau rheoli ar gyfer pob SoDdGA yng Nghymru. Mae\u0027n ofynnol i awdurdodau cynllunio lleol ymgynghori â CNC cyn caniatáu i unrhyw ddatblygiad fynd rhagddo a allai effeithio ar SoDdGA. Mae\u0027n rhaid i gwmnau dŵr, nwy a thrydan wneud hynny hefyd. Mae SoDdGA wedi cael eu dynodi dros nifer o flynyddoedd, o 1949 hyd heddiw, ac maent yn parhau.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tAll SSSIs designated before GIS technology have been converted into digital data from paper maps of various scales. In 2008 the whole dataset was updated to OS MasterMap (mostly to CCW 2006 OS update). This update referred to the original designation maps and all notational descriptions on these maps e.g. “Only the water under the bridges included in the site” OR “SSSI boundary follows, in part the lines of the Mean High and Low Water Marks; both are liable to change.” All new or re-notified sites will be put to the latest release by NRW of OS MasterMap and this information is recorded in the attribute data under the Last_Edited column. As OSMM is updated by the Ordnance Survey it is important to check where boundaries may subsequently have moved.\tPlease refer to Countryside Acts 1949,1981 and 2001, for reasons for capture of original designation. NRW has also created a subset of this dataset showing only those where relate to the marine environment. These are available on the Welsh Government marine planning portal.\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SSSI Sites of Special Scientific Interest.lyr X:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\Marine Protected Areas\\Marine protected areas SSSI.lyr\tHolly York\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-11-13\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/386/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_SSSI\t2025-12-23T10:16:09.225Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17470\tNRW_DS98777\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.137Z\t1112\tValid\tEditable\tNational Parks\tParciau Cenedlaethol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373737\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of National Parks, which are statutory landscape designations in Wales. Natural Resources Wales (NRW) has a legal duty to maintain and make available National Park boundaries.  National Parks are designated for the purposes of conserving and enhancing the natural beauty, wildlife, and cultural heritage and for the public to enjoy the special qualities of these nationally important landscapes.   NRW is the designating authority for any new National Parks in Wales or boundary variations to existing ones, however these must be confirmed by Welsh Ministers. NRWs statutory powers derive from the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act (NPAC) 1949 and Environment Act 1995. The Environment Act (1995) established National Park Authorities, as independent Special Purpose Local Authorities to administer the National Park area in order to deliver their statutory purposes.   Three National Parks have been designated in Wales: Snowdonia 1951 (variation 1974); Pembrokeshire Coast 1952 (Variation 1989); and Brecon Beacons 1955 (Variation 1966).   The data has been held digitally since the mid-1990s. The boundary has been transferred to OS MasterMap by NRW and has been subject to joint checking by NRW staff and the relevant Park authority staff.-Mae’r set ddata ofodol hon yn cynnwys ffiniau digidol Parciau Cenedlaethol, sy’n ddynodiadau tirwedd statudol yng Nghymru. Mae gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) ddyletswydd gyfreithiol i gynnal ffiniau Parciau Cenedlaethol a sicrhau eu bod ar gael.  Mae Parciau Cenedlaethol wedi’u dynodi at ddibenion gwarchod a gwella harddwch naturiol, bywyd gwyllt, a threftadaeth ddiwylliannol ac er mwyn i’r cyhoedd fwynhau rhinweddau arbennig y tirweddau hyn sydd o bwysigrwydd cenedlaethol.   CNC yw’r awdurdod dynodi ar gyfer unrhyw Barciau Cenedlaethol newydd yng Nghymru neu amrywiadau i ffiniau’r rhai presennol, ond rhaid i Weinidogion Cymru gadarnhau’r rhain. Mae pwerau statudol CNC yn deillio o Ddeddf Parciau Cenedlaethol a Mynediad i Gefn Gwlad (NPAC) 1949 a Deddf yr Amgylchedd 1995. Sefydlodd Deddf yr Amgylchedd (1995) Awdurdodau Parciau Cenedlaethol, fel Awdurdodau Lleol Diben Arbennig annibynnol i weinyddu ardal y Parc Cenedlaethol er mwyn cyflawni eu dibenion statudol.   Mae tri Pharc Cenedlaethol wedi’u dynodi yng Nghymru: Eryri 1951 (amrywiad 1974); Arfordir Penfro 1952 (amrywiad 1989); a Bannau Brycheiniog 1955 (amrywiad 1966).   Mae’r data wedi’i gadw’n ddigidol ers canol y 1990au. Mae’r ffin wedi’i throsglwyddo i OS MasterMap gan CNC ac wedi bod yn destun gwirio ar y cyd gan staff CNC a staff awdurdod y Parc perthnasol.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThe data was first digitised to a smaller scale (1:50,000 and 1:10,000), then it was put to OS Landline and then later to OS MasterMap. This final version has had collaboration between NRW and Park Authorities to give the best interpretation of the original designation.   The digital data was created from an interpretation of the National Park designation maps. These maps varied in scale from 1:63,360 (1 inch to 1 mile) and 1:50,000 scale and were accompanied by a written description of the boundary, however the map over-ruled the description if there was inconsistency.   Where administrative boundaries and roads defined the Park boundary it was a rule that the position of that feature at time of designation remained the geographical position of the boundary. However where rivers or Mean High Water Mark (MHWM) formed the boundary these were digitised according to the base map used at digitisation. Digital data captured to OS MasterMap.\tPlease refer to National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949 for reasons for capture of original designation.\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\National Parks.lyr\tCarole Thornley, Lead Specialist Adviser Landscape, Designated Landscapes Programme\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-05-29\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/383/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_NATIONAL_PARK\t2025-12-23T09:58:21.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17472\tNRW_DS98778\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.153Z\t893\tValid\tEditable\tNational Nature Reserves (NNRs)\tSafleoedd (Ffiniau Terfynol GNC) - Gwarchodfeydd Natur Cenedlaethol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373738\tThis spatial dataset contains the digital boundaries of National Nature Reserves (NNRs) in Wales. NNRs represent the very best examples of our wildlife habitats and geological features and can range in size between five hectares to well over 2,000.   NNRs are declared by Natural Resources Wales (NRW) under the National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act of 1949, or under the Wildlife and Countryside Act of 1981. They are owned or leased by NRW, or the land is held by an approved body, such as a County Wildlife Trust.   Each reserve has a programme of work to manage the site\u0027s special features. All of them are also Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and may provide places for educational projects, research and management trials. Some reserves require permits to gain access to them. NNRs are designated over a number of years, beginning in 1954 and on going.   This data has been checked by NRW staff. The original, individual, declaration paper maps are the legal documents.-Mae\u0027r set ddata ofodol hon yn cynnwys ffiniau Gwarchodfeydd Natur Cenedlaethol (GNC) yng Nghymru. GNC yw\u0027r enghreifftiau gorau o\u0027n cynefinoedd bywyd gwyllt a\u0027n nodweddion daearegol a gallant amrywio o ran maint o bum hectar i dros 2,000. Mae Gwarchodfeydd yn cael eu dynodi gan Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru (CNC) dan Ddeddf Parciau Cenedlaethol a Mynediad i Gefn Gwlad 1949, neu dan Ddeddf Bywyd Gwyllt a Chefn Gwlad 1981. Maent yn eiddo i neu\u0027n cael eu prydlesu gan CNC, neu mae\u0027r tir yn cael ei gadw yn nwylo corff cymeradwy, megis Ymddiriedolaeth Bywyd Gwyllt sirol. Mae gan bob gwarchodfa raglen waith ar gyfer rheoli nodweddion arbennig y safle. Mae pob un ohonynt yn Safle o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGA) hefyd a gallant ddarparu lleoliadau ar gyfer prosiectau addysgol, ymchwil a threialon rheoli. Mae angen caniatád i gael mynediad i rai ohonynt. Dynodir Gwarchodfeydd Natur Cenedlaethol dros sawl blwyddyn, gan ddechrau ym 1954 ac maent yn parhau.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThis National Nature Reserve (NNR) final boundary dataset is compiled from the individual declaration maps that make up a single NNR. In some cases an individual NNR is made up of one declaration map, whereas others have many separate declaration maps that were registered over a number of years. Initially the individual, declaration maps were produced on paper at 1:10,000 or 1:2500 scales, then in later years created through GIS.   This dataset has been transferred to OS MasterMap 2006 in 2008.  With the coming of GIS and its use in government departments and commercial companies mobilising the data in this way, advances the protection of these sites and the efficiency of decision making.\tPlease refer to National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act 1949 and Wildlife and Countryside Act of 1981, for reasons of creation of the original designation.\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\NNR National Nature Reserves.lyr\tTom Hellawell, Conservation and Recreation Management Planning Team Leader, Ecosystems Partnership \u0026amp; Planning North\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-11-28\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/384/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_NNR\t2025-12-23T10:14:04.230Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17471\tNRW_DS98785\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.152Z\t661\tUnassessed\tEditable\tProtected sites : Old SSSI 1949 Act\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3938373835\tThis is a spatial dataset. This data set is SSSI notified under the 1949 W\u0026amp;ATC Act only. They were not re-notified under the 1981 Wildlife act.\t\t\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\SSSI 1949 Act.lyr\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2006-04-27\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:39.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17463\tNRW_DS99287\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.808Z\t839\tValid\tEditable\tGeological Conservation Review (GCR) Site Boundaries\tFfiniau Safleoedd Adolygu Cadwraeth Ddaearegol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939323837\tThe dataset contains the original site boundaries for all Geological Conservation Review (GCR) Sites in Wales.  The boundaries define the area of a particular GCR Site (usually coincident with the geological feature as defined by Common Standards Monitoring). Proposed, current and denotified boundaries are included. Records originally collected to show the extent of GCR blocks.  These blocks represent important geological or geomorphological topics (e.g. Mineralogy), geological time divisions, stages and series (e.g. Llandeilo), and areas of search (e.g. South Wales and the Mendips).-Mae\u0027r set ddata\u0027n cynnwys y ffiniau safle gwreiddiol ar gyfer pob Safle Adolygu Cadwraeth Ddaearegol yng Nghymru. Mae\u0027r ffiniau\u0027n diffinio ardal Safle Adolygu Cadwraeth Ddaearegol penodol (sy\u0027n cyd-fynd fel arfer â \u0027r nodwedd ddaearegol fel y\u0027i diffinnir gan Fonitro Safonau Cyffredin). Mae ffiniau arfaethedig a phresennol a ffiniau lle canslwyd yr hysbysiad wedi\u0027u cynnwys. Casglwyd y cofnodion yn wreiddiol i ddangos maint y blociau o Safleoedd Adolygu Cadwraeth Ddaearegol. Mae\u0027r blociau hyn yn cynrychioli pynciau daearegol neu geomorffolegol pwysig (e.e. mwynoleg), rhaniadau amser, cyfnodau a chyfresi daearegol (e.e. Llandeilo), ac ardaloedd chwilio (e.e. de Cymru a Bryniau Mendip).  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl.\tBoundaries were produced for approximately 450 sites in Wales by the GCR Unit between 1977-1990 and have been produced for each GCR site added to the register after 1990. Original paper maps at various scales have been coverted to OSMM (1:2500 scale) using GIS.  Digitising tablets used if necessary.  Where boundaries have been checked by ESO (Earth Scientist Officers) confidence is fairly high.  Where they have not been checked the confidence is moderate (see final column in attribute data for this information). Some boundaries have necessarily been modified in the course of the Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) notification process and other Natural Resource Wales (NRW) reviews. When the latest versions of the site boundaries have been collated and verified by NRW\u0027s Earth Science Officers the aim is to issue a second dataset- \u0027GCR Site Boundaries (Current)\u0027.\t\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Non-Statutory\\NRW Geological Conservation Review sites.lyr   Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\geology\\CCW\\GCR_Site_Boundaries_Original.TAB   Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Physical\\geology\\CCW\\gcrsites_north.TAB Held by JNCC and CCW (in Hafod Elfyn basement registry)\tInternal Custodian: (Raymond Roberts) Regional Earth Science Officers, NRW (info on individual sites) Hannah Brazier Cartography Officer, PKCG, NRW (collation and digitisation of data)  Metadata Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2021-04-02\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/451/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_GCR_SITES\t2025-12-23T09:55:51.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17479\tNRW_DS99333\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.377Z\t21\tUnassessed\tEditable\tGrazing Marsh Site Boundaries\tFfiniau Safleoedd Corsydd Pori\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939333333\t*** This data is no longer accessible,  therefore this metadata is under review 02/08/2024 ****  This is a spatial dataset containing the boundaries for grazing marsh and floodplain grasslands areas for Wales. These have been collated from the inventory and review of grazing marsh and floodplain grasslands. The aim of this data capture was to produce an inventory of the priority UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) habitat grazing marshes and flood plains. Information not previously available due to the composite nature of the habitat. Confidence in the site outlines is good. Verification of the habitat coding was undertaken at the time of digitising but errors have subsequently been found.\tThis dataset was based on the Habitats of Wales Phase 1 data (1979 - 1997) soil and topographic data.  Boundaries were digitised from contour and habitat maps.\tSee CCW Science Report Number 274 An Inventory and conservation review of coastal grazing marshes and floodplain habitats in Wales. Dargie and Dargie 1998, for full details.\t[A] X:\\Biological\\Biodiversity Action Plan NERC OSPAR\\Terrestrial\\Coastal_and_Floodplain_Grazing_Marsh.lyr\tInternal Custodian: Claire Burrows, Lead Specialist Advisor, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team Internal Custodian: Jonathan Rothwell, Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat \u0026amp; Resilience Mapping, Terrestrial Ecosystems \u0026amp; Species Team.   This metadata may not be complete for more information please contact Clare Burrows.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2003-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:13:40.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPrivate record\n17476\tNRW_DS99344\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.118Z\t868\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Lowland Heathland Inventory\tArolwg Rhostir yr Iseldir Cymru Cam 2\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939333434\tThis is a GIS dataset consisting of a inventory of the phase 2 lowland Heathland inventory.  National Vegetation Classification (NVC) is the basis used for the selection of Sites of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI) and is used to define Annex 1 habitats for the selection of Special Areas of Conservation (SAC) under the Habitats Directive (EU Directive 92/43).  This project comprises of detailed data related to the Phase 2 plant community survey for Lowland Heath across Wales. The project covers dry and maritime heath covered by the heathland section of the NVC and wet heath covered by the section of the NVC. Two non-NVC communities the calcareous grass and heath, and shingle heath are also described.  This project had three main purposes: - To provide an overview of the Welsh lowland heathland resource allowing local conservation programs particularly local Biodiversity Action Plans (BAP) partnerships, to assess and interpret their resource in a Welsh context.  - To identify heathland types which are underrepresented in the SSSIs series.  - To identified gaps in knowledge and highlight areas for further study.-Y Dosbarthiad Llystyfiant Cenedlaethol (NVC) yw’r sail ar gyfer dewis Safleoedd o Ddiddordeb Gwyddonol Arbennig (SoDdGAau) ac fe’i defnyddir i ddiffinio cynefinoedd Atodiad 1 ar gyfer dewis Ardaloedd Cadwraeth Arbennig (ACAau) o dan y Gyfarwyddeb Cynefinoedd (Cyfarwyddeb yr UE 92/43). Mae’r prosiect hwn yn cynnwys data manwl yn ymwneud ag arolwg Cam 2 o gymunedau planhigion ar gyfer Rhostir yr Iseldir ar draws Cymru. Mae’r prosiect yn cwmpasu rhostir sych a morol sy’n dod o dan adran rhostir yr NVC, ynghyd â rhostir gwlyb sy’n dod o dan adran yr NVC. Dwy gymuned nad ydynt yn dod o dan yr NVC ydy glaswellt a rhostir calchaidd, a disgrifir rhostir graean hefyd. Roedd tri phrif ddiben i’r prosiect hwn: - Cynnig trosolwg o adnodd rhostir yr iseldir yng Nghymru gan ganiatáu i raglenni cadwraeth lleol yn enwedig partneriaethau Cynlluniau Gweithredu Bioamrywiaeth lleol, asesu a dehongli eu hadnoddau mewn cyd-destun Cymreig. - Nodi mathau o rostir sydd wedi’u tangynrychioli yn y gyfres o SoDdGAau. - Nodi ble mae’r bylchau mewn gwybodaeth ac amlygu meysydd i’w hastudio ymhellach.\tA total of 13 surveys were carried out: Anglesey (1994), West Gwynedd (1993), Gwynedd and Anglesey (1994), Pembrokeshire (1995, 1996, 1997), Rivers Ystwyth and Rheidiol, Ceredigion (1995), Gower Commons (1997), Gower, Bridgend, Vale of Glamorgan, Conwy (1998), Powys (1999), Brecknock and Carmarthenshire (2000), Ceredigion (2001) and Gower (2001). The total area of heath surveyed is approximately 4714 ha which is 35% of the total area of lowland and coastal (maritime) heathland recorded in the phase 1 habitats survey of Wales (1979- 1997).  The age of the data is a limitation as habitat composition may change over time, a record of a vegetation type in the data is therefore no guarantee that it is still extant on the ground  Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC).  The dataset has been extensively validated and verified however problems were encountered when mapping in field which may have resulted in errors, digitisation was of poor quality and there were occasional problems with identifying community type.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Lowland_heathland Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\phase_2\\lowland_heathland\tInternal Custodian:  Jane Stevens - TEG, NRW (Used to be Jan Sherry, Heathland Ecologist, Terrestrial Science Group, CCW.)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-08-13\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/6157/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_ph2_lowland_heathland\t2025-12-23T10:08:18.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17478\tNRW_DS99400\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.296Z\t257\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Photo Points\tPwyntiau Ffotograffau Cam 1 Morol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343030\tThis is a static spatial dataset.  Phase 1 level intertidal survey photograph index to complement a seamless layer of communities within the intertidal zone. The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005. All work has been undertaken by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team.   Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.\tPhase 1 Intertidal Survey methods were implemented followed by manual digitising from collected data and hand drawn maps.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Map Photos.lyr  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Marine\\phase1_intertidal  H:\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media569 Archived under project no. 204, media 569.\tInternal Custodian: Intertidal Ecologist, NRW. Metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2007-02-28\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-12-19T10:00:19.055Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17473\tNRW_DS99408\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.357Z\t290\tUnassessed\tEditable\tMarine Recorder Sample Point Data (Spatial Export)\tData allfudo Marine Recorder\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343038\tThis is a spatial export of all welsh sample points held within the Marine Recorder database. Not to be distributed outside NRW without the prior permission of the Master Reference Data Team. This is essentially a collation of data from many different marine biological surveys held within Marine Recorder. It contains all biotope instances and species data within the Wales Territorial Sea (12 nautical miles offshore), plus some instances beyond where relevant to boundaries such as the Dee and Severn estuaries.\tCollation of data from various surveys entered into Marine Recorder. Various dependent on individual surveys from which data points are derived.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine_Recorder\\Marine_Recorder_Biotopes_WGS84.lyr  X:\\Biological\\Marine Habitat\\Marine_Recorder\\Marine_Recorder_Species_WGS84.lyr\tMetadata Contact: Master Reference Data Team / GIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2023-04-25\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-07T16:09:47.000Z\tMonica Jones\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17469\tNRW_DS99411\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.050Z\t958\tValid\tEditable\tMINESCAN\tSafleoedd Mwyngloddio\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343131\tThis is a spatial dataset containing point data of mine sites (metaliferous non-metaliferous) in Wales. Data taken from minescan spreadsheet data.  The purpose of this data capture was to form a spatial framework for MINESCAN sites.   In 1996 the Minescan project ran for four years. This was commissioned by the former Countryside Council for Wales and John S. Mason undertook the project as a consultant to the National Museum and Galleries of Wales (NMGW).   The project involved field assessments of the conservation value of over 1000 old mines and other mineralogical sites throughout Wales, and the data obtained permitted an objective ranking of the sites relative to one another. As a primary result, a comprehensive network of SSSI and RIGS sites was identified, representing all aspects of mineralisation and metallogenesis in Wales. These sites have a real chance of survival as future scientific resources.\tMINESCAN Survey by National Museum of Wales for joint CCW/NMGW project. The MINESCAN project ran in the late 1990s and looked at all mine sites across Wales with the aim of evaluating their geoconservation importance. This included a desk study capturing the location of all mine sites which were recorded in a spreadsheet and at some stage a GIS point layer was created.  The sites deemed of conservation importance were listed as SSSI or RIGS value and were progressed in the next part of the project.    Information warning: We are unable to audit the complete dataset to correct any errors. The sites that are showing as located in England and off the Welsh coast are incorrect and should be discounted. Errors in the grid references may have happened at numerous stages and there may not have been accurate grid references for some sites referred to in the literature.\t\tX:\\Industrial\\Mines\\MINESCAN.lyr ;  Minescan spreadsheet currently not located (Jun 2022) but Raymond is looking for it after discussing with Gabi Williams as part of TNA project; might be on old CCW drive??  There is also a report that seems to contain info from the spreadsheet but this is not a CCW science report and only marked as National Museum and Galleries of Wales: https://cyfoethnaturiolcymru.sharepoint.com/teams/evidence/Geodiversity/grr/Mineralogy/MINESCAN%20%20An%20Introduction,%20Bevins%20\u0026amp;%20Mason,%201996.PDF\tRaymond Roberts, Lead Specialist Advisor, Geodiversity Policy, Terrestrial Ecosystems Group\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-06-18-2022-06-22\tpublication-creation\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/7627/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/geonode:nrw_minescan\t2025-12-23T10:08:30.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17480\tNRW_DS99415\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.324Z\t457\tValid\tEditable\tNational Trails\tLlwybrau Cenedlaethol\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343135\tNational Trails are long distance walking, cycling and horse riding routes through the best landscapes in Wales. After World War II the desire to keep areas of Britain \u0027special\u0027 and to protect them from post-war development led to the establishment of Long Distance Routes (now called National Trails).  Each Trail has a Trail Partnership made up of the local authority or national park responsible for the path on the ground. There is a dedicated National Trail Officer with responsibility for keeping the Trail up to the high standards set for National Trails. Maintenance work is carried out by the local highway authorities together with landowners often with the help of volunteers.-Mae Llwybrau Cenedlaethol yn llwybrau cerdded, beicio a marchogaeth hir drwy\u0027r tirweddau gorau yng Nghymru. Ar ôl yr Ail Ryfel Byd, arweiniodd yr awydd i gadw ardaloedd o Brydain yn \"arbennig\" a\u0027u diogelu rhag datblygiad at sefydlu Llwybrau Pellter Hir (a elwir yn Llwybrau Cenedlaethol erbyn hyn). Mae gan bob Llwybr Bartneriaeth Llwybr sef awdurdod lleol neu barc cenedlaethol sy\u0027n gyfrifol am y llwybr ar y ddaear. Mae Swyddog Llwybrau Cenedlaethol penodedig sydd chyfrifoldeb am gadw\u0027r Llwybr yn unol â\u0027r safonau uchel a bennwyd ar gyfer Llwybrau Cenedlaethol. Yr awdurdodau priffyrdd lleol sy\u0027n gwneud y gwaith cynnal a chadw, ynghyd â thirfeddianwyr ac yn aml, gyda chymorth gwirfoddolwyr.  Datganiad priodoli  Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tLine datasets showing the extent of National Trails in Wales were digitised into ArcGIS format. This dataset shows the walked line.\t\tX:\\Designations\\NRW Statutory\\National Trails (Walked line).lyr\tInternal Custodian: Chris Heaps, Specialist Advisor: Trails and Open Access Gary Eccleston/Geospatial team are responsible for the GIS digitisation and updates.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2025-06-18\tpublication\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layer/327/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layers/inspire-nrw:NRW_NATIONAL_TRAIL\t2025-12-23T09:51:10.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI\tPublic record\n17467\tNRW_DS99419\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.098Z\t415\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Habitat Layer\tHaen Cynefinoedd Cam 1 Cymru Gyfan\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343139\tThis is a spatial dataset containing point data showing the location of habitats mapped in mixed habitat polygons during the Upland Vegetation Survey.   The habitat codes have been translated from Ratcliffe and Birkes to Phase 1. Corrections made in 2014 are: missing points added for Cothi Tywi and Mynydd Mallaen and Pwllpeirian, and points removed from single habitat and uniform mixed habitat polygons in Berwyn. This update was done when the voronoi version of the Phase 1 veg layer was created.    The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. An all-Wales electronic dataset was created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.\tField survey for the Upland Vegetation Survey spanned from 1979 to 1989.  Closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors carried out field recording of habitats onto 1:10,000 scale maps (rarely 1:25,000 during the upland stage). The minimum size of habitat patch recorded lies predominantly within the range 0.1-0.25 ha, although smaller areas were mapped in the lowlands. The survey methods employed during the initial upland stage are given in more detail in a WFU report (Day, A. (1989) Upland Vegetation Survey 1979-1989 Background Methodology and Summary of Data Collected. NCC, Bangor).   All field mapping was carried out by trained and closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors. Checks on the Upland Vegetation Survey data were carried out between 1994-1996 (Yeo, M. J. M., Blackstock, T. H. 2002. A vegetation analysis of the pastoral landscapes of upland Wales, UK. Journal of Vegetation Science 13, 803–816). The electronic data have undergone a series of checks for accuracy both by the contractor and subsequently within CCW.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase1 Habitat.lyr ,   Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\Terrestrial\\phase_1\\Lookup_tables\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.      Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2004-03-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:13:43.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17464\tNRW_DS99421\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.820Z\t783\tValid\tEditable\tPhase 2 Sand Dune Survey of Wales\tCyfnod 2 Arolwg o Dwyni Tywod yng Nghymru\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343231\tThis is a spatial dataset containing a collation of all mapped sand dune habitats in Wales. Over 40 NVC Phase 2 surveys were carried out on sand dunes across Wales. This dataset is a collation of data from these surveys grouped together for mobilisation purposes and to provide information on a Wales-wide scale. One of the major strategic initiatives of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW). The purpose of this data capture was to help define the sand dune vegetation resource of Wales. The surveys were initiated in the late 1980\u0027s and continued until the mid \u002790\u0027s. Digitisation of the maps took place in 2004.-Mae hon yn set ddata ofodol sy\u0027n cynnwys casgliad o\u0027r holl gynefinoedd twyni tywod sydd wedi\u0027u mapio yng Nghymru. Cynhaliwyd dros 40 o arolygon Dosbarthiad Llystyfiant Cenedlaethol Cyfnod 2 ar dwyni tywod ledled Cymru. Casgliad o ddata o\u0027r arolygon hyn yw\u0027r set ddata hon, wedi\u0027u cyfuno at ddibenion symudiadau ac i ddarparu gwybodaeth ar raddfa Cymru gyfan. Diben y data hwn a gasglwyd oedd helpu i ddiffinio adnoddau llystyfiant twyni tywod Cymru. Gall cyfansoddiad cynefinoedd newid dros amser o ganlyniad i dueddiadau olynol, rheolaeth tir neu hyd yn oed ddatblygiadau arfaethedig. Felly, ni ellir bod yn sicr bod math o lystyfiant neu rywogaeth yn parhau i fodoli am ei fod wedi\u0027i gofnodi yn y data digidol.  Cydnabyddiaeth Yn cynnwys gwybodaeth Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru © Cyfoeth Naturiol Cymru a Hawl Cronfa Ddata. Cedwir pob hawl. Yn cynnwys data Arolwg Ordnans. Rhif Trwydded Arolwg Ordnans AC0000849444. Hawlfraint y Goron a Hawl Cronfa Ddata.\tThe field surveys utilised standard Phase 2 survey methodology. Vegetation mapping and community definition followed the National Vegetation Classification (NVC). For each site surveyed a vegetation map (scale of 1:7500) was produced which was later digitised.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 2 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_2_Sand_dunes Site reports are available from NRW\u0027s library.\tInternal Contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2022-06-28-2022-10-21\tpublication-revision\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/capabilities/layergroup/3593/?ows_service\u003dwms\thttps://datamap.gov.wales/layergroups/geonode:nrw_phase_2_sand_dunes\t2025-12-23T09:47:32.000Z\tRachel Morris\tGEMINI \u0026 MEDIN\tPublic record\n17461\tNRW_DS99437\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.807Z\t621\tUnassessed\tEditable\tPaper Air Photo Index\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343337\tThis is a spatial dataset containing the centre point of each paper air photo, taken for the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) between 1991 and 2002. This data is now managed and held by Natural Resources Wales.\tAlthough this index belongs to NRW, the air photos themselves belong to the external companies.\t\tX:\\Basemapping\\Air Photos\\Air photos index\\Paper air photos in NRW index.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team - Hannah Brazier\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t\t\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T13:06:02.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17460\tNRW_DS99457\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.763Z\t827\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Habitat Survey - Vegetation\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343537\tThis is a spatial dataset. The Upland Habitat Survey GIS dataset is based largely on vegetation maps produced by the Wales Field Unit of the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) using the Ratcliffe and Birks vegetation classification. It also contains National Vegetation Classification (NVC) maps for parts of five upland sites and Phase 1 Survey information for three areas of Common Land. The vegetation layer holds the boundaries and attributes of vegetation polygons.  The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. The GIS dataset was created as part of the remit of the former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to identify all areas of \u0027open country\u0027 in Wales for the implementation of The Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000. All major Welsh upland sites, and other upland areas not mapped by the terrestrial Phase 1 Survey.\tHabitat mapping was carried out by field survey with the aid of aerial photographs. The hand-drawn maps were subsequently scanned and digitised to Ordnance Survey LandLine. The Upland Habitat Survey GIS dataset has been combined with digitised data from the terrestrial Phase 1 survey to form the AllWales Phase 1 GIS dataset. The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. The GIS dataset was created as part of the remit of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) to identify all areas of \u0027open country\u0027 in Wales for the implementation of The Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000. The Upland Habitat Survey GIS dataset has been combined with digitised data from the terrestrial Phase 1 survey to form the AllWales Phase 1 GIS dataset. All major Welsh upland sites, and other upland areas not mapped by the terrestrial Phase 1 Survey.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Terrestial\\Original_Phase_1\\Phase1 Vegetation.lyr\tGIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2004-03-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17465\tNRW_DS99459\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.952Z\t283\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Scattered Vegetation Layer\tHaen Llystyfiant Gwasgaredig Cam 1 Cymru Gyfan\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343539\tThis is a spatial dataset containing point data showing the location of scattered vegetation mapped during the Phase 1 Habitat Survey. The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. This dataset is an all - Wales, electronic dataset created for the generation of national statistics on habitat coverage. The dataset was subsequently imported into a GIS to enable its disaggregation into various different geographical regions within Wales.  Uniformly distributed scattered vegetation is also captured as a polygon attribute in the vegetation layer.\tThe Field survey spanned from 1979 to 1999. Closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors carried out field recording of habitats onto 1:10,000 scale maps (rarely 1:25,000 during the upland stage). The minimum size of habitat patch recorded lies predominantly within the range 0.1 - 0.25 ha, although smaller areas were mapped in the lowlands. The survey methods employed during the initial upland stage are given in more detail in a WFU report (Day, A. (1989) Upland Vegetation Survey 1979-1989 Background Methodology and Summary of Data Collected. NCC, Bangor). Methods for the lowland survey followed the Handbook for Phase 1 Habitat Survey with some minor modifications adopted for use in Wales (Howe E.A. and Blackstock T.H. (1991) Supplementary Notes for Phase I Survey in Wales. CCW, Bangor).   All field mapping was carried out by trained and closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors. A study of survey reliability was conducted during the lowland phase (Kerry, Rimes, Smith, Williams. 1994. An Assessment of Errors in Phase I Habitat Survey in Wales. Bangor: Countryside Council for Wales, Report No. 94/4/1). It showed the field mapping to be 83% repeatable (Stevens, Blackstock, Howe, Stevens. 2004. Repeatability of Phase 1 habitat survey. J. Environ. Manage. 73, 53-59).The electronic data have undergone a series of checks for accuracy both by the contractor and subsequently within CCW.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase1 Scattered.lyr ,  Legacy CCW Archive M:\\phase_1\\allwales_ph1\\p1colour_sca.tab\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tOpen Government Licence\t2019-05-23\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T09:07:22.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17475\tNRW_DS99495\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.178Z\t258\tUnassessed\tEditable\tUpland Habitat Survey - Habitat Features\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939343935\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons. The Upland Habitat Survey GIS dataset is based largely on vegetation maps produced by the Wales Field Unit of the Nature Conservancy Council (NCC) using the Ratcliffe and Birks vegetation classification. It also contains National Vegetation Classification (NVC) maps for parts of five upland sites and Phase 1 Survey information for three areas of Common Land. The Habitat layer is a point layer that shows the location of different habitats and habitat strings mapped in mosaic polygons on the Ratcliffe and Birks vegetation maps.  The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. The GIS dataset was created as part of the remit of the Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) and now Natural Resources Wales (NRW) to identify all areas of \u0027open country\u0027 in Wales for the implementation of The Countryside and Rights of Way Act 2000. The Upland Habitat Survey GIS dataset has been combined with digitised data from the terrestrial Phase 1 survey to form the AllWales Phase 1 GIS dataset. All major Welsh upland sites, and other upland areas not mapped by the terrestrial Phase 1 Survey.\tHabitat mapping was carried out by field survey with the aid of aerial photographs. The hand-drawn maps were subsequently scanned and digitised to Ordnance Survey LandLine. Field survey spanned from 1979 to 1999.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Habitat Networks\\Level 1 all networks\\Upland Legacy CCW Archive M:\\GIS_Data\\Live\\Biological\\Habitats\\phase_1\\upland_survey\\habitat_survey_upland_hab.TAB\tInternal Custodian: Habitat and Ecosystems Resource Analyst, NRW.\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t1999-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:20.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17466\tNRW_DS99503\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.910Z\t598\tUnassessed\tEditable\tBiodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) in Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939353033\tMarsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) is a UK Biodiversity Action Plan (BAP) Priority invertebrate species. This dataset is a spatial collation of all confirmed occurrence records of Marsh Fritillary (Euphydryas aurinia) butterfly from Wales.  The purpose of this data collation is to inform Local Biodiversity Action Plan (LBAP) Partners and inform conservation decisions related to Marsh Fritillary butterflies.\tThe dataset is a sub-set of the Welsh Invertebrate Database (WID) (See Parent ID 46929). Subset of data from NRWs Welsh Invertebrate Database.   **INFORMATION WARNING**.   This GIS dataset has not been updated, for the latested distribution records please refer to the Welsh Invertebrate Database or Butterfly Conservation\tSubset of data from NRWs Welsh Invertebrate Database\tX:\\Biological\\Species sourced by NRW\\Terrestial\\Invertebrates\\Insects  (multiple datasets held under this file)\tCustodian of WID - Mike Howe and therefore contact for Marsh Frillitary data as well. Custodian of GIS data - GIS and Spatial Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2002-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T10:01:47.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17468\tNRW_DS99549\t2024-05-28T12:24:10.021Z\t298\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Target Notes\tNodyn Targed Morol Cam 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939353439\tThis is a static spatial dataset. Phase 1 level intertidal habitat and substrata information in the format of target notes (point data) to complement a seamless layer of communities within the intertidal zone.  The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005.  All work has been undertaken by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team.   Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Site of Special Scientific Interest (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping. They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.  The data available to the public under an Open Government Licence has some information redacted\tField survey and manual digitising from hand drawn maps.  A complete validation process has been undertaken.\tSome sites have been remapped at a later date and a derived layer is available that merges maptargetnote.tab with newly mapped areas.  Metadata for this layer can be viewed through URI 114453.\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\.PHASE1_INTERTIDAL_TARGET_NOTES.lyr  Data is archived as project 204, media 569\tInternal Custodian: Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environmental Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Advice Team metadata contact: GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T16:13:57.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17454\tNRW_DS99603\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.351Z\t293\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales Easements Over Third-Party Land\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363033\tThis is a spatial dataset showing the easements for NRW over third parties land used for Access and pipelines. Data are both completed and proposed. Includes information on site name, owner, scale captured, expiry date, unique codes. This is to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements and to provide a complete table showing all proposed and completed easements across third party land in Wales.\tDigitised to OS Products at different scales as appropriate (mainly 1:2500). The confidence in the accuracy is High - With less confidence in some pre 2000 data.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Easements Over 3rd Party Land current line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Easements Over 3rd Party Land current poly.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Easements Over 3rd Party Land proposed line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Easements Over 3rd Party Land proposed poly.lyr\t\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T16:51:06.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17455\tNRW_DS99611\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.523Z\t309\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales Easements Over NRW Land\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363131\tThis is a  spatial dataset showing the easements for Natural Resources Wales (NRW) over NRW land used for Access and pipelines. Easements are rights of access across land (can be for people / pipelines).  Data are both completed and proposed. Includes information on site name, owner, scale captured, expiry date, unique codes. This is to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements and to provide a complete table showing all proposed and completed easements across NRW land.\tDigitised to OS Products at different scales as appropriate (mainly 1:2500.) Confidence is High (with less confidence in some pre 2000 data) This data set is separated into four layers reading from two feature classes.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Easements Over NRW Land current line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Easements Over NRW Land current poly.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Easements Over NRW Land proposed line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Easements Over NRW Land proposed poly.lyr\tNatural Resources Wales GIS and Spatial Data Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-04-17T09:58:02.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17458\tNRW_DS99623\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.506Z\t708\tUnassessed\tEditable\tCountryside Council for Wales (CCW) Landholdings\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363233\tThis is a spatial dataset consisting of all land currently owned or purchased, by former Countryside Council for Wales (CCW) which now forms part of Natural Resources Wales (NRW).  The dataset holds information such as site name, scale captured and unique codes, amongst others. The purpose of this data capture was to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements and provide a complete table showing all land owned by CCW in Wales.\tThe dataset was digitised based on former CCW landholdings to OS Products at different scales as appropriate (mainly 1:2500). The boundaries generally taken from Land Registry Maps where available and paper copies held by Land Agents.\t\tX:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex CCW Land holdings current.lyr  x:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex CCW land holdings expired.lyr  x:\\Ownership\\NRW Land\\Ex CCW land holdings proposed.lyr\tHannah Bazier, Cartographic Officer, GIS Team,NRW Ian Medcalf, GIS Officer, Estates Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2015-12-07\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17452\tNRW_DS99635\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.207Z\t573\tValid\tEditable\tAll Wales Phase 1 Terrestrial - Target Notes\tNodiadau Targed Cam 1 Cymru Gyfan\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363335\tDuring the terrestrial Phase 1 Survey of Wales thousands of target note and cover notes were created. Over the last 6 years these notes have all been scanned and a layer file (ARCMAP)  has been created to record the position and reference number of Phase 1 target notes.    The original field surveys were carried out as part of a national habitat survey programme required to implement conservation at a local level. The target notes layer was created as a way of collating all the Phase 1 terrestrial target note information for All Wales and to allow the pdf target notes to be easily accessible to all staff. Field survey spanned from 1979 to 1997, target notes were produced between 1989 and 1997 and the scanning and digitisation took place between 2004 and 2010.\tSee the metadata for \u0027Terrestrial Phase 1 Habitat Survey\u0027 .    The target notes collected as part of the survey were scanned as 2 sided documents with a resolution of 300dpi on a black and white scale (where the quality of the hand written note allows). These documents are stored in DMS, NRW\u0027s Electronic Records and Data Management System. A  layer was created with points marking the location of each target note, these points contain a hotlink which links to the scanned document in DMS. Hotlinks only work in the legacy CCW environment. Scans of the cover note for each 5km grid square were also made and stored in a separate layer called phase1_5k_generalpdfnotes. For each target note that contains sensitive information, such as the site owner\u0027s name, a non-sensitive \u0027related\u0027 version has also been saved in DMS. The senstitive species records are collated in an excel spreadsheet.    All field mapping was carried out by trained and closely co-ordinated teams of surveyors. A study of survey reliability was conducted during the lowland phase (Kerry, Rimes, Smith, Williams. 1994. An Assessment of Errors in Phase I Habitat Survey in Wales. Bangor: Countryside Council for Wales, Report No. 94/4/1). It showed the field mapping to be 83% repeatable (Stevens, Blackstock, Howe, Stevens. 2004. Repeatability of Phase 1 habitat survey. J. Environ. Manage. 73, 53-59).The electronic data have undergone a series of checks for accuracy both by the contractor and subsequently within CCW. There are a small proportion of missing target notes and cover sheets which are documented in the layer file.  Field survey spanned from 1979 to 1997, target notes were produced between 1989 and 1997 and the scanning and digitisation took place between 2004 and 2010.\t\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase 1Terrestrial\\Original Phase 1\\Phase1 Target.lyr .   The scanned target notes in pdf format are stored on Ffynnon (CCW legacy DMS - now called Content Manager)\tInternal Contact: Jonathon Rothwell Specialist Advisor: Terrestrial Habitat and Resilience Mapping. Terrestrial Ecosystems Team.     Metadata Contact: Geospatial Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2010-12-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-02-04T11:13:55.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17457\tNRW_DS99644\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.425Z\t318\tUnassessed\tEditable\tTir Cymen Farms\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363434\tThis is a GIS dataset containing spatial objects such as points, lines, and polygons.  It is an external third party dataset owned by the Welsh Government and licensed to NRW for internal use only. The dataset has been supplied by the Welsh Government and shows Tir Cymen Farm locations as a point layer.\tThe data are taken from the Tir Cymen database.\tThis metadata may not be complete for more information please contact the Welsh Assembly Government\tLegacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\land_management\\agrienvironment\\wag_depc\\tir_cymen\\tircymen_farms.tab\tGIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tWelsh Government\t\t2006-08-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-07T15:47:56.000Z\tAstun Technology Admin\t-\tPublic record\n17453\tNRW_DS99662\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.412Z\t520\tValid\tEditable\tMarine Intertidal Phase 1 Biotope Map\tBiotopau Morol Cam 1\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939363632\tThis is a static layer. This dataset contains Phase 1 level intertidal habitat and substrata information as a seamless layer of communities within the intertidal zone. The intertidal habitat mapping began in 1996 and was completed in 2005.  All work has been undertaken by former Countryside Council for Wales\u0027 (CCW\u0027s) in house intertidal survey team.    Surveys were undertaken to provide an assessment of sites as possible Sites of Special Scientific Importance (SSSI\u0027s) and as a basis for developing sensitivity mapping.  They are also used in the production and evaluation of Environmental Impact Assessments (EIA\u0027s) and as an integrated information resource for local authorities\u0027 structure and development plans on the coast and for the consideration of planning applications.   A complete validation process has been undertaken. The data available to the public under an Open Government Licence has some information redacted. An un-redacted version can be requested and may be provided under NRW licence.\tField survey and manual digitising from hand drawn maps. A separate derived spatial layer was created in 2009 as a subset of this biotope map, showing the nationally important biotopes only. This information is also tagged in the main original layer.   Requestors should refer to the original data when enquiring about nationally important biotopes.\tSome sites have been remapped at a later date and a derived layer is available that merges mapbiotopes.tab with newly mapped areas.  Metadata for this layer can be viewed through URI 114453.\tX:\\Biological\\Phase 1 Habitat Mapping\\Phase_1_Intertidal\\Phase1 Intertidal Biotopes.lyr    Derived Layer: [requestors should get this information from the original layer] Legacy CCW Archive M:\\gis_data\\live\\biological\\habitats\\marine\\phase1_intertidal\\mapbiotopes.TAB.   Z:\\Archive\\Legacy\\CCW_H_DRIVE\\Science\\Knowledge Management\\MFSG Data archive\\Media501to600\\Media569 Data is also archived as project 204, media 569.\tInternal Custodian:  Paul Brazier, Senior Marine Environment Assessment Officer, Marine Environmental Assessment \u0026amp; Reporting Team  NRW Metadata Contact: Master Data Reference Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\tNRW Conditional\t2005-10-31\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-11-11T16:15:37.000Z\tRachel Morris\tMEDIN\tPublic record\n17456\tNRW_DS99705\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.431Z\t541\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLettings by Natural Resources Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939373035\tThis is a spatial dataset containing all current, proposed and expired Land Agency Lettings by Natural Resources Wales (NRW). This is generally letting out of our land or giving people licences to use our land for certain activities. It is always money coming into NRW. The dataset contains only polygons and includes information on site name, owner, scale captured, expiry date, unique codes.\tDigitised to OS Products at different scales as appropriate (mainly 1:2500.) Data moves from the proposed_lettings_by_Ex_CCW dataset to here, and then to the expired_lettings_by_Ex_CCW. Digitised to OS Products at different scales as appropriate (mainly 1:2500.)  Complete and proposed lettings by NRW are available as two separate x drive layers but they have been merged as one feature class for Lettings by NRW.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\Lettings by NRW current.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\Lettings by NRW expired.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\Lettings by NRW proposed.lyr\tNatural Resources Wales GIS Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-03-21T10:52:38.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17459\tNRW_DS99710\t2024-05-28T12:24:09.490Z\t255\tUnassessed\tEditable\tLettings to Natural Resources Wales\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939373130\tThis is a spatial dataset containing all current, proposed and expired Land Agency Lettings to Natural Resources Wales (NRW).This is lease of land and sporting rights to NRW, so we can influence how things are managed across this land. This is always money going out from NRW. The dataset contains only polygons and includes information on site name, owner, scale captured, expiry date, and unique codes.\tDigitised to OS Products at different scales as appropriate (mainly 1:2500.)  Data moves from the proposed_lettings_to_CCW dataset to completed, and then to the expired_lettings_to_CCW. Confidence is high (with less confidence in some pre 2000 data). This data is one feature class from three layers, Lettings to NRW expired, Lettings to NRW proposed and Lettings to NRW completed.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\Lettings to NRW current.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\Lettings to NRW expired.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\Lettings to NRW proposed.lyr\tNatural Resources Wales GIS Team Hannah Brazier Cartographic Officer, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17451\tNRW_DS99725\t2024-05-28T12:24:08.816Z\t363\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales Land Management Agreements\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939373235\tThis is a spatial dataset containing land management agreements for Natural Resources Wales (NRW). This layer contains all current (completed / live) land management agreements as well as all currently proposed management agreements (not yet signed) Plus expired agreements. Dataset contains only polygons and includes information on site name, owner, scale captured, expiry date and unique codes. The purpose is to provide accurate legal maps for management agreements and to provide a complete table showing all live management agreements in Wales.\tDigitised to OS Products at different scales as appropriate (mainly 1:2500.) Data comes from proposed_agreements and will move into expired_agreements. Digitised to OS Products at different scales as appropriate (mainly 1:2500.) the confidence is High (with less confidence in some pre 2000 data) Land Agency Management Agreements (live and completed) and  Land Agency Proposed Management Agreements are available as two separate x drive layers but they  have been merged as one feature class for NRW Land Management Agreements\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Agreement current.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\NRW Agreement expired.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Agreement proposed.lyr   Feature class includes proposed, completed and expired Land Management Agreements\tNatural Resources Wales GIS Team\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-01-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:22.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17507\tNRW_DS99807\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.760Z\t531\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales Land Management Plans\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939383037\tThis is a spatial dataset containing all currently proposed, completed and expired Land Management Data. This is the information about what management needs doing within the agreement area, e.g. fences, ditches, areas to be burnt etc. It includes information on site name, owner, scale captured, expiry date, and unique codes. The purpose of this data capture was to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements, and provide a complete table showing all management information for management agreements in Wales ( except text and symbols ).\tDigitised to OS Products at different scales as appropriate (mainly 1:2500.) Data moves from here to the completed_management_plans dataset.  This dataset is available as six layers, but it is two feature classes.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Agreement management proposed poly.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Agreement management proposed line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Agreement management current line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Agreement management current poly.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\NRW Agreement management expired line.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\NRW Agreement management expired poly.lyr\tInternal Custodian and metadata contact:  GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW (Pippa Jones / Hannah Brazier)\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n17506\tNRW_DS99835\t2024-05-28T12:24:45.850Z\t243\tUnassessed\tEditable\tNatural Resources Wales Land Management Agreement Access\t\t4f4c4942-4343-5764-6473-3939383335\tThis is a spatial dataset containing all current, proposed and expired agreement Access Routes and includes information on site name, owner, scale captured, expiry date, unique codes. The purpose of this data capture was to provide accurate legal maps for Management Agreements. and provide a complete table showing all agreement access routes in Wales. High (with less confidence in some pre 2000 data).\tData moves from Proposed Access to Completed Access on signing, then to expired. Although there are three layers. They are merged as one feature class.\t\tX:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements proposed\\NRW Agreement access proposed.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements current\\NRW Agreement access current.lyr  X:\\Resource Management\\Land Management\\NRW agreements expired\\NRW Agreement access expired.lyr\tInternal Custodian:  Hannah Brazier or Pippa Jones, Cartographers, GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW  Metadata contact:  GIS and Spatial Data Team, NRW\tNatural Resources Wales (NRW)\t\t2013-04-01\tpublication\tnull\tnull\t2025-07-08T09:26:19.000Z\tnobody nobody\t-\tPublic record\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "apps": [],
      "runtimeInfos": {},
      "progressUpdateIntervalMs": 500,
      "jobName": "paragraph_1763135846557_600567333",
      "id": "paragraph_1763135846557_600567333",
      "dateCreated": "2025-11-14 15:57:26.557",
      "dateStarted": "2026-01-12 16:19:57.250",
      "dateFinished": "2026-01-12 16:20:32.346",
      "status": "FINISHED"
